<p><-- p. 1264 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><centered><point26>S.</point26></centered></p>

<p><ent>S</ent><br/
<hw>S</hw> <pr>(<ecr/s)</pr>, <def>the nineteenth letter of the English
alphabet, is a consonant, and is often called a sibilant, in allusion to
its hissing sound. It has two principal sounds; one a mere hissing, as
in <xex>sack</xex>, <xex>this</xex>; the other a vocal hissing (the same
as that of <xex>z</xex>), as in <xex>is</xex>, <xex>wise</xex>. Besides
these it sometimes has the sounds of <xex>sh</xex> and <xex>zh</xex>, as
in <xex>sure</xex>, <xex>measure</xex>. It generally has its hissing
sound at the beginning of words, but in the middle and at the end of
words its sound is determined by usage. In a few words it is silent, as
in <xex>isle</xex>, <xex>d<eacute/bris</xex>. With the letter
<xex>h</xex> it forms the digraph <xex>sh</xex>. See <xex>Guide to
pronunciation</xex>, <sect/<sect/ 255-261.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note> Both the form and the name of the letter S are derived from
the Latin, which got the letter through the Greek from the Phoenician.
The ultimate origin is Egyptian. S is etymologically most nearly related
to <xex>c</xex>, <xex>z</xex>, <xex>t</xex>, and <xex>r</xex>; as, in
i<xex>c</xex>e, OE. i<xex>s</xex>; E. hen<xex>c</xex>e, OE.
henne<xex>s</xex>; E. ra<xex>s</xex>e, ra<xex>z</xex>e;
era<xex>s</xex>e, ra<xex>z</xex>or; tha<xex>t</xex>, G. da<xex>s</xex>;
E. rea<xex>s</xex>on, F. rai<xex>s</xex>on, L. ra<xex>t</xex>io; E.
wa<xex>s</xex>, we<xex>r</xex>e; chai<xex>r</xex>, chai<xex>s</xex>e
(see C, Z, T, and R.).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-s</ent><br/
<hw>-s</hw>. <sn>1.</sn> <ety>[OE. <ets>es</ets>, AS.
<ets>as</ets>.]</ety> <def>The suffix used to form the plural of most
words; as in road<xex>s</xex>, elf<xex>s</xex>, side<xex>s</xex>,
account<xex>s</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[OE. <ets>-s</ets>, for older <ets>-th</ets>, AS.
<ets>-<edh/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The suffix used to form the third person
singular indicative of English verbs; as in fall<xex>s</xex>,
tell<xex>s</xex>, send<xex>s</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An adverbial suffix; as in toward<xex>s</xex>,
need<xex>s</xex>, alway<xex>s</xex>, -- originally the genitive,
possesive, ending. See <er>-'s</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-'s</ent><br/
<hw>-'s</hw> <ety>[OE. <ets>-es</ets>, AS. <ets>-es</ets>.]</ety> The
suffix used to form the possessive singular of nouns; <as>as,
boy<ex>'s</ex>; man<ex>'s</ex></as>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>'s</ent><br/
<hw>'s</hw>. <def>A contraction for <xex>is</xex> or (colloquially) for
<xex>has</xex>.</def> <ldquo/My heart<xex>'s</xex> subdued.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saadh</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"adh</hw> <pr>(s<aum/"<adot/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sadh</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saan</ent><br/
<hw>Saan</hw> <pr>(s<aum/n)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Bushmen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabadilla</ent><br/
<hw>Sab`a*dil"la</hw> <pr>(s<acr/b`<adot/*d<icr/l"l<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>cebadilla</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A Mexican liliaceous plant (<spn>Schoenocaulon officinale</spn>);
also, its seeds, which contain the alkaloid <prod>veratrine</prod>. It
was formerly used in medicine as an emetic and purgative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabaean</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*bae"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sabian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabaeanism</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*bae"an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sabianism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabaism</ent><br/
<ent>Sabaeism</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sa"bae*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sa"ba*ism</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sabianism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabal</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"bal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
genus of palm trees including the palmetto of the Southern United
States.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabaoth</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"a*oth</hw> <pr>(s<acr/b"<asl/*<ocr/th <it>or</it>
s<adot/"b<amac/*<ocr/th; 277)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>tseb<amac/'<omac/th</ets>, pl. of <ets>ts<amac/b<amac/'</ets>, an
army or host, fr. <ets>ts<amac/b<amac/'</ets>, to go forth to
war.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Armies; hosts.</def> <note>[Used twice in
the English Bible, in the phrase <ldquo/The Lord of
Sabaoth.<rdquo/]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Incorrectly, the Sabbath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbat</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"bat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sabbath</er>.]</ety> <def>In mediaeval demonology, the nocturnal
assembly in which demons and sorcerers were thought to celebrate their
orgies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbatarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sab`ba*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sabbatarius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sabbataire</ets>. See
<er>Sabbath</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who regards and keeps the
seventh day of the week as holy, agreeably to the letter of the fourth
commandment in the Decalogue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ There were Christians in the early church who held this
opinion, and certain Christians, esp. the <stype>Seventh-day
Baptists</stype>, hold it now.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A strict observer of the Sabbath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbatarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sab`ba*ta"ri*an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Sabbath, or the tenets of Sabbatarians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbatarianism</ent><br/
<hw>Sab`ba*ta"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The tenets
of Sabbatarians.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Ward (1673).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbath</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"bath</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sabat</ets>, <ets>sabbat</ets>, F. <ets>sabbat</ets>, L.
<ets>sabbatum</ets>, Gr. <grk>sa`bbaton</grk>, fr. Heb.
<ets>shabb<amac/th</ets>, fr. <ets>sh<amac/bath</ets> to rest from
labor. Cf. <er>Sabbat</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A season or day of
rest; one day in seven appointed for rest or worship, the observance of
which was enjoined upon the Jews in the Decalogue, and has been
continued by the Christian church with a transference of the day
observed from the last to the first day of the week, which is called
also <altname>Lord's Day</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Remember the <qex>sabbath</qex> day, to keep it holy.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. xx. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The seventh year, observed among the Israelites as
one of rest and festival.</def> <rj><au>Lev. xxv. 4.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: A time of rest or repose; intermission of
pain, effort, sorrow, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Peaceful sleep out the <qex>sabbath</qex> of the tomb.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sabbath breaker</b></col>, <cd>one who violates the law
of the Sabbath.</cd> -- <col><b>Sabbath breaking</b></col>, <cd>the
violation of the law of the Sabbath.</cd> -- <col><b>Sabbath-day's
journey</b></col>, <cd>a distance of about a mile, which, under
Rabbinical law, the Jews were allowed to travel on the
Sabbath.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Sabbath</er>, <er>Sunday</er>.</syn> <usage>
<xex>Sabbath</xex> is not strictly synonymous with <xex>Sunday</xex>.
<xex>Sabbath</xex> denotes the institution; <xex>Sunday</xex> is the
name of the first day of the week. The <xex>Sabbath</xex> of the Jews is
on <xex>Saturday</xex>, and the <xex>Sabbath</xex> of most Christians on
<xex>Sunday</xex>. In New England, the first day of the week has been
called <ldquo/<xex>the Sabbath</xex>,<rdquo/ to mark it as holy time;
<xex>Sunday</xex> is the word more commonly used, at present, in all
parts of the United States, as it is in England. <ldquo/So if we will be
the children of our heavenly Father, we must be careful to keep the
Christian <xex>Sabbath</xex> day, which is the <xex>Sunday</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Homilies.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbathless</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"bath*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without Sabbath, or
intermission of labor; hence, without respite or rest.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbatical</ent><br/
<ent>Sabbatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sab*bat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sab*bat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>sabbatique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the Sabbath;
resembling the Sabbath; enjoying or bringing an intermission of
labor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sabbatical year</b></col> <fld>(Jewish Antiq.)</fld>,
<cd>every seventh year, in which the Israelites were commanded to suffer
their fields and vineyards to rest, or lie without
tillage.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbatism</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"ba*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sabbatismus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to keep the Sabbath: cf. F.
<ets>sabbatisme</ets>. See <er>Sabbath</er>.]</ety> <def>Intermission of
labor, as upon the Sabbath; rest.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabbaton</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"ba*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sp.
<ets>zapaton</ets>, a large shoe, F. <ets>sabot</ets> a wooden
shoe.]</ety> <def>A round-toed, armed covering for the feet, worn during
a part of the sixteenth century in both military and civil
dress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabean</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*be"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sabian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabeism</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"be*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sabianism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*bel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>sabulum</ets> gravel.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
tubicolous annelids having a circle of plumose gills around the
head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabellian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*bel"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the
doctrines or tenets of Sabellius. See <er>Sabellian</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabellian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*bel"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>A follower of <etsep>Sabellius</etsep>, a presbyter of
Ptolemais in the third century, who maintained that there is but one
person in the Godhead, and that the Son and Holy Spirit are only
different powers, operations, or offices of the one God the
Father.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabellianism</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*bel"li*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>The doctrines or tenets of Sabellius. See <er>Sabellian</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabelloid</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*bel"loid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sabella</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Like, or related to, the genus Sabella.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sa*bel"loid</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabre</ent><br/
<ent>Saber</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sa"ber</hw>, <hw>Sa"bre</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sabre</ets>, G. <ets>s<aum/bel</ets>; of
uncertain origin; cf. Hung. <ets>sz<aacute/blya</ets>, Pol.
<ets>szabla</ets>, Russ. <ets>sabla</ets>, and L. Gr. <grk>zabo`s</grk>
crooked, curved.]</ety> <def>A sword with a broad and heavy blade, thick
at the back, and usually more or less curved like a scimiter; a cavalry
sword.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Saber fish</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Sabre
fish</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the cutlass
fish.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabre</ent><br/
<ent>Saber</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sa"ber</hw>, <hw>Sa"bre</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sabered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or
<conjf>Sabred</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sabering</conjf> or <conjf>Sabring</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>sabrer</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
strike, cut, or kill with a saber; to cut down, as with a
saber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You send troops to <qex>saber</qex> and bayonet us into
submission.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabrebill</ent><br/
<ent>Saberbill</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sa"ber*bill`</hw>, <hw>Sa"bre*bill`</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The curlew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"bi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sabaeus</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>Sabean</asp>, and
<asp>Sabaean</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to Saba
in Arabia, celebrated for producing aromatic plants.</def><-- #err An
error in the original dictionary here: it says "Written also ...
Sabaeanism" PCP --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relating to the religion of Saba, or to the worship
of the heavenly bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"bi*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An adherent of the Sabian
religion; a worshiper of the heavenly bodies.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Sabaean</asp>, and <asp>Sabean</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabianism</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"bi*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The doctrine of
the Sabians; the Sabian religion; that species of idolatry which
consists in worshiping the sun, moon, and stars; heliolatry.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>Sabaeanism</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabicu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sab"i*cu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The very hard
wood of a leguminous West Indian tree (<spn>Lysiloma Sabicu</spn>),
valued for shipbuilding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabine</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"bine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sabinus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the ancient Sabines,
a people of Italy.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Sabine
people.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabine</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>Sabina herba</ets>, fr. <ets>Sabini</ets> the Sabines. Cf.
<er>Savin</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Savin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sable</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>sable</ets>,
F. <ets>zibeline</ets> sable (in sense 4), LL. <ets>sabellum</ets>; cf.
D. <ets>sabel</ets>, Dan. <ets>sabel</ets>, <ets>zobel</ets>, Sw.
<ets>sabel</ets>, <ets>sobel</ets>, G. <ets>zobel</ets>; all fr. Russ.
<ets>s<oacute/bole</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
carnivorous animal of the Weasel family (<spn>Mustela zibellina</spn>)
native of the northern latitudes of Europe, Asia, and America, -- noted
for its fine, soft, and valuable fur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The sable resembles the marten, but has a longer head
and ears. Its fur consists of a soft under wool, with a dense coat of
hair, overtopped by another still longer. It varies greatly in color and
quality according to the locality and the season of the year. The
darkest and most valuable furs are taken in autumn and winter in the
colder parts of Siberia, Russia, and British North America.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The American sable, or marten, was formerly considered a
distinct species (<spn>Mustela Americana</spn>), but it differs very
little from the Asiatic sable, and is now considered only a geographical
variety.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The fur of the sable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A mourning garment; a funeral robe; -- generally in
the plural.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sables</xex> wove by destiny.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>The tincture black; -- represented
by vertical and horizontal lines crossing each other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sable</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the color of the
sable's fur; dark; black; -- used chiefly in poetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Night, <qex>sable</qex> goddess! from her ebon throne,<br/
In rayless majesty, now stretches forth<br/
Her leaden scepter o'er a slumbering world.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sable antelope</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large
South African antelope (<spn>Hippotragus niger</spn>). Both sexes have
long, sharp horns. The adult male is black; the female is dark chestnut
above, white beneath.</cd> -- <col><b>Sable iron</b></col>, <cd>a
superior quality of Russia iron; -- so called because originally stamped
with the figure of a sable.</cd> -- <col><b>Sable mouse</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the lemming.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sable</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sabled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sabling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To render sable or
dark; to drape darkly or in black.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sabled</qex> all in black the shady sky.</q> <rj><qau>G.
Fletcher.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabot</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa`bot"</hw> <pr>(s<adot/`b<omac/")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of wooden shoe worn by the
peasantry in France, Belgium, Sweden, and some other European
countries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A thick, circular disk of wood, to
which the cartridge bag and projectile are attached, in fixed ammunition
for cannon; also, a piece of soft metal attached to a projectile to take
the groove of the rifling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabotage</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa`bo`tage"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Scamped work.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Malicious waste or destruction of an employer's property or injury
to his interests by workmen during labor troubles.</def></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>any surreptitious destruction of property or
obstruction of activity by persons not known to be hostile; -- in war,
such actions carried out behind enemy lines by agents or local
sympathisers of the hostile power.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabotiere</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa`bo"ti<egrave/re</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A kind of freezer for ices.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabre</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"bre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saber</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabretasche</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"bre*tasche`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sabretache</ets>, G. <ets>s<aum/beltasche</ets>;
<ets>s<aum/bel</ets> saber + <ets>tasche</ets> a pocket.]</ety>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A leather case or pocket worn by cavalry at the
left side, suspended from the sword belt.</def> <rj><au>Campbell (Dict.
Mil. Sci.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabrina work</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*bri"na work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A variety of appliqu<eacute/
work for quilts, table covers, etc.</def> <rj><au>Caulfeild & S. (Dict.
of Needlework).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabulose</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"u*lose</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sabulosus</ets>, from <ets>sabulum</ets>, <ets>sabulo</ets>,
sand.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Growing in sandy places.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabulosity</ent><br/
<hw>Sab`u*los"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
being sabulous; sandiness; grittiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sabulous</ent><br/
<hw>Sab"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sabulosus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sandy; gritty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sac</ent><br/
<hw>Sac</hw> <pr>(s<add/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sacs</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sac</ent><br/
<hw>Sac</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sake</er>,
<er>Soc</er>.]</ety> <fld>(O.Eng. Law)</fld> <def>The privilege formerly
enjoyed by the lord of a manor, of holding courts, trying causes, and
imposing fines.</def> <rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sac</ent><br/
<hw>Sac</hw> <pr>(s<acr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>saccus</ets> a sack. See <er>Sack</er> a bag.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>See 2d <er>Sack</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A cavity, bag, or receptacle,
usually containing fluid, and either closed, or opening into another
cavity to the exterior; a sack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacalait</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"a*lait</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A kind of fresh-water bass; the crappie.</def> <mark>[Southern
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacar</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"car</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saker</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccade</ent><br/
<hw>Sac*cade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>A sudden, violent check of a horse by drawing or
twitching the reins on a sudden and with one pull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccate</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>saccatus</ets>, fr. L. <ets>saccus</ets> a sack, bag.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Having the form of a sack or pouch;
furnished with a sack or pouch, as a petal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
<ord>Saccata</ord>, a suborder of ctenophores having two pouches into
which the long tentacles can be retracted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharate</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A salt of saccharic acid.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In a
wider sense, a compound of saccharose, or any similar carbohydrate, with
such bases as the oxides of calcium, barium, or lead; a
sucrate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharic</ent><br/
<hw>Sac*char"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or obtained from, saccharine substances;
specifically, designating an acid obtained, as a white amorphous gummy
mass, by the oxidation of mannite, glucose, sucrose, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacchariferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cha*rif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saccharon</ets> sugar + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Producing
sugar; <as>as, <ex>sacchariferous</ex> canes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharify</ent><br/
<hw>Sac*char"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Saccharified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Saccharifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[L. <ets>saccharon</ets> sugar + <ets>-fy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>saccharifier</ets>.]</ety> <def>To convert into, or to impregnate
with, sugar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharilla</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cha*ril"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of
muslin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cha*rim"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saccharon</ets> sugar + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>saccharim<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for
ascertaining the quantity of saccharine matter in any solution, as the
juice of a plant, or brewers' and distillers' worts.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>saccharometer</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>common saccharimeter</xex> of the brewer is an
hydrometer adapted by its scale to point out the proportion of
saccharine matter in a solution of any specific gravity. The
<xex>polarizing saccharimeter</xex> of the chemist is a complex optical
apparatus, in which polarized light is transmitted through the
saccharine solution, and the proportion of sugar indicated by the
relative deviation of the plane of polarization.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharimetrical</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cha*ri*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to saccharimetry; obtained by saccharimetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1265 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Saccharimetry</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cha*rim"e*try</hw>
<pr>(s<acr/k`k<adot/*r<icr/m"<esl/*tr<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
act, process or method of determining the amount and kind of sugar
present in sirup, molasses, and the like, especially by the employment
of polarizing apparatus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharin</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rin</hw> <pr>(s<acr/k"k<adot/*r<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F., from L. <ets>saccharon</ets> sugar.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A bitter white crystalline substance obtained from the
saccharinates and regarded as the lactone of saccharinic acid; -- so
called because formerly supposed to be isomeric with cane sugar
(<xex>saccharose</xex>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharinate</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*ri*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A salt of saccharinic acid.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
salt of saccharine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharine</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rine</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>saccharin</ets>, fr. L. <ets>saccharon</ets> sugar, Gr. <?/, <?/,
<?/, Skr. <ets><cced/arkara</ets>. Cf. <er>Sugar</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to sugar; having the qualities of sugar; producing sugar;
sweet; <as>as, a <ex>saccharine</ex> taste; <ex>saccharine</ex>
matter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharine</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rine</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A trade name for benzoic sulphinide.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>saccharin</asp>.]</altsp> <-- A synthetic
sweetening agent used (in the form of the sodium salt) as a non-caloric
sweetening agent, to avoid gaining weight or for medical purposes.
Benzoic sulfimide, <chform>C7H5NO3S</chform>. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharinic</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rin"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or derived from, saccharin; specifically,
designating a complex acid not known in the free state but well known in
its salts, which are obtained by boiling dextrose and levulose (invert
sugar) with milk of lime.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharize</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Saccharized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Saccharizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
convert into, or to impregnate with, sugar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharoidal</ent><br/
<ent>Saccharoid</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sac"cha*roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sac`cha*roid"al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>saccharon</ets> sugar
+ <ets>-oid</ets>: cf. F. <ets>saccharo<ium/de</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Resembling sugar, as in taste, appearance, consistency, or
composition; <as>as, <ex>saccharoidal</ex> limestone</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cha*rom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
saccharimeter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharomyces</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sac`cha*ro*my"ces</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ sugar + <?/, <?/, a fungus.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
genus of budding fungi, the various species of which have the power, to
a greater or less extent, or splitting up sugar into alcohol and
carbonic acid. They are the active agents in producing fermentation of
wine, beer, etc. <spn>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</spn> is the yeast of
sedimentary beer. Also called <altname>Torula</altname>.</def><--
Brewers' yeast is Saccharomyces cerevisiae. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharomycetes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sac`cha*ro*my*ce"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A family of fungi consisting of the one genus
Saccharomyces.</def> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharonate</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*ro*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of saccharonic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharone</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sacchar</ets>in + lact<ets>one</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A white crystalline substance,
<chform>C6H8O6</chform>, obtained by the oxidation of saccharin, and
regarded as the lactone of saccharonic acid.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An
oily liquid, <chform>C6H10O2</chform>, obtained by the reduction of
saccharin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cha*ron"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or derived from, saccharone; specifically,
designating an unstable acid which is obtained from saccharone
<sd>(a)</sd> by hydration, and forms a well-known series of
salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharose</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Cane sugar; sucrose; also, in general, any one of the group of
which saccharose, or sucrose proper, is the type. See
<er>Sucrose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharous</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cha*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Saccharine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccharum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sac"cha*rum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Saccharine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of tall
tropical grasses including the sugar cane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccholactate</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cho*lac"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Saccholactic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of
saccholactic acid; -- formerly called also
<altname>saccholate</altname>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <see>See
<er>Mucate</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccholactic</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cho*lac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saccharon</ets> sugar + <ets>lac</ets>, <ets>lactis</ets>,
milk.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating,
an acid now called <xex>mucic acid</xex>; saccholic.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccholic</ent><br/
<hw>Sac*chol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Saccholactic.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacchulmate</ent><br/
<hw>Sac*chul"mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of sacchulmic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacchulmic</ent><br/
<hw>Sac*chul"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sacch</ets>arine + <ets>ulmic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, an acid
obtained as a dark amorphous substance by the long-continued boiling of
sucrose with very dilute sulphuric acid. It resembles humic acid.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sacculmic</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacchulmin</ent><br/
<hw>Sac*chul"min</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An amorphous huminlike substance resembling sacchulmic acid, and
produced together with it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacciferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sac*cif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saccus</ets> a sack + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Bearing a sac.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacciform</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ci*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saccus</ets> a sack + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Having the general form of a sac.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccoglossa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sac`co*glos"sa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. L. <ets>saccus</ets> a sack + Gr. <?/ a tongue.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Pellibranchiata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccular</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a sac;
sacciform.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacculated</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cu*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with
little sacs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccule</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"cule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sacculus</ets>, dim. of <ets>saccus</ets> sack.]</ety> <def>A
little sac; specifically, the sacculus of the ear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacculo-cochlear</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cu*lo-coch"le*ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the sacculus and cochlea of the
ear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacculo-utricular</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`cu*lo-u*tric"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the sacculus and utriculus of the
ear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacculus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sac"cu*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sacculi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., little sack.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A little sac; esp., a part of the membranous
labyrinth of the ear.</def> <see>See the Note under <er>Ear</er>.</see>
<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saccus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sac"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sacci</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a sack.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A sac.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacellum</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*cel"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sacella</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., dim. of
<ets>sacrum</ets> a sacred place.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Rom.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>An unroofed space consecrated to a divinity.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A small monumental chapel in a
church.</def> <rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacerdotal</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`er*do"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sacerdotalis</ets>, fr. <ets>sacerdos</ets>, <ets>-otis</ets>, a
priest, fr. <ets>sacer</ets> holy, sacred: cf. F.
<ets>sacerdotal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to priests, or to
the order of priests; relating to the priesthood; priesty; <as>as,
<ex>sacerdotal</ex> dignity; <ex>sacerdotal</ex>
functions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ascendency of the <qex>sacerdotal</qex> order was long the
ascendency which naturally and properly belongs to intellectual
superiority.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacerdotalism</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`er*do"tal*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The system,
style, spirit, or character, of a priesthood, or sacerdotal order;
devotion to the interests of the sacerdotal order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacerdotally</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`er*do"tal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sacerdotal
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sachel</ent><br/
<hw>Sach"el</hw> <pr>(s<acr/ch"<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small
bag.</def> <see>See <er>Satchel</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sachem</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"chem</hw> <pr>(s<acr/"ch<eit/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A chief
of a tribe of the American Indians; a sagamore.</def> <see>See
<er>Sagamore</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sachemdom</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"chem*dom</hw> <pr>(-d<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
government or jurisdiction of a sachem.</def> <rj><au>Dr. T.
Dwight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sachemship</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"chem*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Office or condition of a
sachem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sachet</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa`chet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., dim. of
<ets>sac</ets>. See <er>Sac</er>.]</ety> <def>A scent bag, or perfume
cushion, to be laid among handkerchiefs, garments, etc., to perfume
them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saciety</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*ci"e*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Satiety.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sack</ent><br/
<hw>Sack</hw> <pr>(s<acr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>seck</ets>, F. <ets>sec</ets> dry (cf. Sp. <ets>seco</ets>, It.
<ets>secco</ets>), from L. <ets>siccus</ets> dry, harsh; perhaps akin to
Gr. <grk>'ischno`s</grk>, Skr. <ets>sikata</ets> sand, Ir.
<ets>sesc</ets> dry, W. <ets>hysp</ets>. Cf. <er>Desiccate</er>.]</ety>
<def>A name formerly given to various dry Spanish wines.</def>
<ldquo/Sherris <xex>sack</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sack posset</b></col>, <cd>a posset made of sack, and
some other ingredients.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sack</ent><br/
<hw>Sack</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sak</ets>, <ets>sek</ets>,
AS. <ets>sacc</ets>, <ets>saecc</ets>, L. <ets>saccus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>sa`kkos</grk> from Heb. <ets>sak</ets>; cf. F. <ets>sac</ets>, from
the Latin. Cf. <er>Sac</er>, <er>Satchel</er>, <er>Sack</er> to
plunder.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A bag for holding and carrying goods of
any kind; a receptacle made of some kind of pliable material, as cloth,
leather, and the like; a large pouch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A measure of varying capacity, according to local
usage and the substance. The American <xex>sack</xex> of salt is 215
pounds; the <xex>sack</xex> of wheat, two bushels.</def>
<rj><au>McElrath.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Perhaps a different word.]</ety> <def>Originally, a
loosely hanging garment for women, worn like a cloak about the
shoulders, and serving as a decorative appendage to the gown; now, an
outer garment with sleeves, worn by women; <as>as, a dressing
<ex>sack</ex></as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sacque</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sack coat; a kind of coat worn by men, and
extending from top to bottom without a cross seam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>See 2d <er>Sac</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- <sn>6.</sn> <def>Bed.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sack bearer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>. <cd>See
<cref>Basket worm</cref>, under <er>Basket</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sack
tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an East Indian tree (<spn>Antiaris
saccidora</spn>) which is cut into lengths, and made into sacks by
turning the bark inside out, and leaving a slice of the wood for a
bottom.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To give the sack to</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>get the sack</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to discharge, or be
discharged, from employment; to jilt, or be jilted.</cd>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> -- <col><b>To hit the sack</b></col>, <cd>to go to
bed.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark></cs> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sack</ent><br/
<hw>Sack</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put in a sack; to
bag; <as>as, to <ex>sack</ex> corn</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bolsters <qex>sacked</qex> in cloth, blue and crimson.</q>
<rj><qau>L. Wallace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bear or carry in a sack upon the back or the
shoulders.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sack</ent><br/
<hw>Sack</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sac</ets> plunder, pillage,
originally, a pack, packet, booty packed up, fr. L. <ets>saccus</ets>.
See <er>Sack</er> a bag.]</ety> <def>The pillage or plunder, as of a
town or city; the storm and plunder of a town; devastation;
ravage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The town was stormed, and delivered up to <qex>sack</qex>, -- by
which phrase is to be understood the perpetration of all those outrages
which the ruthless code of war allowed, in that age, on the persons and
property of the defenseless inhabitants, without regard to sex or
age.</q> <rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sack</ent><br/
<hw>Sack</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sacked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sacking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Sack</er>
pillage.]</ety> <def>To plunder or pillage, as a town or city; to
devastate; to ravage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Romans lay under the apprehensions of seeing their city
<qex>sacked</qex> by a barbarous enemy.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sackage</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of taking
by storm and pillaging; sack.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>H.
Roscoe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sackbut</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"but</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>saquebute</ets>, OF. <ets>saqueboute</ets> a sackbut, earlier, a
sort of hook attached to the end of a lance used by foot soldiers to
unhorse cavalrymen; prop. meaning, pull and push; fr.
<ets>saquier</ets>, <ets>sachier</ets>, to pull, draw (perhaps
originally, to put into a bag or take out from a bag; see <er>Sack</er>
a bag) + <ets>bouter</ets> to push (see <er>Butt</er> to thrust). The
name was given to the musical instrument from its being lengthened and
shortened.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A brass wind instrument, like a
bass trumpet, so contrived that it can be lengthened or shortened
according to the tone required; -- said to be the same as the
trombone.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sagbut</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Moore (Encyc. of Music).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>sackbut</xex> of the Scriptures is supposed to
have been a stringed instrument.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sackcloth</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"cloth`</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Linen or
cotton cloth such as sacks are made of; coarse cloth; anciently, a cloth
or garment worn in mourning, distress, mortification, or
penitence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gird you with <qex>sackcloth</qex>, and mourn before Abner.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Sam. iii. 31.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus with <qex>sackcloth</qex> I invest my woe.</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sackclothed</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"clothed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Clothed in
sackcloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacker</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sacks; one who
takes part in the storm and pillage of a town.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sackful</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sackfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>As much as a sack will
hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sackful</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"ful</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bent on plunder.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacking</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>saeccing</ets>, from
<ets>saecc</ets> sack, bag.]</ety> <def>Stout, coarse cloth of which
sacks, bags, etc., are made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sackless</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sacle<aacute/s</ets>;
<ets>sacu</ets> contention + <ets>le<aacute/s</ets> loose, free
from.]</ety> <def>Quiet; peaceable; harmless; innocent.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sack-winged</ent><br/
<hw>Sack"-winged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a peculiar pouch developed near the front edge of the wing;
-- said of certain bats of the genus <gen>Saccopteryx</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacque</ent><br/
<hw>Sacque</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formed after the
analogy of the French. See 2d <er>Sack</er>.]</ety> <def>Same as 2d
<er>Sack</er>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacral</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"cral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the sacrum; in the region of the sacrum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrament</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ra*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sacramentum</ets> an oath, a sacred thing, a mystery, a sacrament,
fr. <ets>sacrare</ets> to declare as sacred, <ets>sacer</ets> sacred:
cf. F. <ets>sacrement</ets>. See <er>Sacred</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The oath of allegiance taken by Roman soldiers; hence, a sacred
ceremony used to impress an obligation; a solemn oath-taking; an
oath.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll take the <qex>sacrament</qex> on't.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The pledge or token of an oath or solemn covenant; a
sacred thing; a mystery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God sometimes sent a light of fire, and pillar of a cloud . . .
and the <qex>sacrament</qex> of a rainbow, to guide his people through
their portion of sorrows.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>One of the solemn religious
ordinances enjoined by Christ, the head of the Christian church, to be
observed by his followers; hence, specifically, the eucharist; the
Lord's Supper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Sacrament</er>, <er>Eucharist</er>.</syn> --
<usage>Protestants apply the term <xex>sacrament</xex> to baptism and
the Lord's Supper, especially the latter. The R. Cath. and Greek
churches have five other sacraments, viz., confirmation, penance, holy
orders, matrimony, and extreme unction. As <xex>sacrament</xex> denotes
an oath or vow, the word has been applied by way of emphasis to the
Lord's Supper, where the most sacred vows are renewed by the Christian
in commemorating the death of his Redeemer. <xex>Eucharist</xex> denotes
the <xex>giving of thanks</xex>; and this term also has been applied to
the same ordinance, as expressing the grateful remembrance of Christ's
sufferings and death. <ldquo/Some receive the <xex>sacrament</xex> as a
means to procure great graces and blessings; others as an
<xex>eucharist</xex> and an office of thanksgiving for what they have
received.<rdquo/</usage> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrament</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ra*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To bind by an
oath.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Laud.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramental</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sacramentalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sacramental</ets>,
<ets>sacramentel</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a
sacrament or the sacraments; of the nature of a sacrament; sacredly or
solemnly binding; <as>as, <ex>sacramental</ex> rites or
elements</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Bound by a sacrament.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sacramental</qex> host of God's elect.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramental</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men"tal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which relates to a
sacrament.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Morton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramentalism</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men"tal*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
doctrine and use of sacraments; attachment of excessive importance to
sacraments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramentalist</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men"tal*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who holds the
doctrine of the real objective presence of Christ's body and blood in
the holy eucharist.</def> <rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramentally</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men"tal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sacramental
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramentarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sacramentarius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sacramentaire</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A name given in the sixteenth
century to those German reformers who rejected both the Roman and the
Lutheran doctrine of the holy eucharist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who holds extreme opinions regarding the
efficacy of sacraments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramentarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men*ta"ri*an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining a sacrament, or to the sacramentals; sacramental.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the Sacramentarians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramentary</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to a sacrament or the sacraments;
sacramental.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the Sacramentarians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramentary</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ra*men"ta*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sacramentarium</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sacramentaire</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An ancient book of the Roman Catholic Church, written
by Pope Gelasius, and revised, corrected, and abridged by St. Gregory,
in which were contained the rites for Mass, the sacraments, the
dedication of churches, and other ceremonies. There are several ancient
books of the same kind in France and Germany.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Sacramentarian</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Papists, Anabaptists, and <qex>Sacramentaries</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacramentize</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ra*ment*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To
administer the sacraments.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Both to preach and <qex>sacramentize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrarium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*cra"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. <ets>sacer</ets>
sacred.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sort of family chapel in the houses of
the Romans, devoted to a special divinity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The adytum of a temple.</def>
<rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In a Christian church, the sanctuary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrate</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"crate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sacratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sacrare</ets>. See
<er>Sacred</er>.]</ety> <def>To consecrate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacration</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*cra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Consecration.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacre</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"cre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saker</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacre</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"cre</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sacrer</ets>. See
<er>Sacred</er>.]</ety> <def>To consecrate; to make sacred.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacred</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"cred</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Originally p. p. of
OE. <ets>sacren</ets> to consecrate, F. <ets>sacrer</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>sacrare</ets>, fr. <ets>sacer</ets> sacred, holy, cursed. Cf.
<er>Consecrate</er>, <er>Execrate</er>, <er>Saint</er>,
<er>Sexton</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Set apart by solemn religious
ceremony; especially, in a good sense, made holy; set apart to religious
use; consecrated; not profane or common; <as>as, a <ex>sacred</ex>
place; a <ex>sacred</ex> day; <ex>sacred</ex> service</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relating to religion, or to the services of
religion; not secular; religious; <as>as, <ex>sacred</ex>
history</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Smit with the love of <qex>sacred</qex> song.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Designated or exalted by a divine sanction;
possessing the highest title to obedience, honor, reverence, or
veneration; entitled to extreme reverence; venerable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such neighbor nearness to our <qex>sacred</qex> [royal] blood<br/
Should nothing privilege him.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Poet and saint to thee alone were given,<br/
The two most <qex>sacred</qex> names of earth and heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hence, not to be profaned or violated;
inviolable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Secrets of marriage still are <qex>sacred</qex> held.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Consecrated; dedicated; devoted; -- with
<xex>to</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A temple, <qex>sacred</qex> to the queen of love.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Solemnly devoted, in a bad sense, as to evil,
vengeance, curse, or the like; accursed; baleful.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But, to destruction <qex>sacred</qex> and devote.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1266 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Society of the Sacred Heart</b></col> <fld>(R.C.
Ch.)</fld>, <cd>a religious order of women, founded in France in 1800,
and approved in 1826. It was introduced into America in 1817. The
members of the order devote themselves to the higher branches of female
education.</cd> -- <col><b>Sacred baboon</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Hamadryas</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sacred bean</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a seed of the Oriental lotus (<spn>Nelumbo
speciosa</spn> or <spn>Nelumbium speciosum</spn>), a plant resembling a
water lily; also, the plant itself. See <er>Lotus</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sacred beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Scarab</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sacred canon</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Canon</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</cd> -- <col><b>Sacred fish</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of fresh-water
African fishes of the family <fam>Mormyridae</fam>. Several large
species inhabit the Nile and were considered sacred by the ancient
Egyptians; especially <spn>Mormyrus oxyrhynchus</spn>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sacred ibis</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Ibis</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sacred monkey</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any
Asiatic monkey of the genus <gen>Semnopithecus</gen>, regarded as sacred
by the Hindoos; especially, the entellus. See <er>Entellus</er>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The sacred baboon.</cd> See <er>Hamadryas</er>.
<sd>(c)</sd> <cd>The bhunder, or rhesus monkey.</cd> -- <col><b>Sacred
place</b></col> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld>, <cd>the place where a deceased
person is buried.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Holy; divine; hallowed; consecrated; dedicated;
devoted; religious; venerable; reverend.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Sa"cred*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sa"cred*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrifical</ent><br/
<ent>Sacrific</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sacrif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sa*crif"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sacrificus</ets>,
<ets>sacrificalis</ets>. See <er>Sacrifice</er>.]</ety> <def>Employed in
sacrifice.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrificable</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*crif"ic*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being offered in sacrifice.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrificant</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*crif"ic*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sacrificans</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Sacrifice</er>.]</ety> <def>One
who offers a sacrifice.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrificator</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ri*fi*ca`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>A sacrificer; one who offers a sacrifice.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrificatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*crif"ic*a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sacrificatoire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Offering sacrifice.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sherwood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrifice</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ri*fice</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sacrifise</ets>, <ets>sacrifice</ets>, F. <ets>sacrifice</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>sacrificium</ets>; <ets>sacer</ets> sacred + <ets>facere</ets>
to make. See <er>Sacred</er>, and <er>Fact</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The offering of anything to God, or to a god; consecratory
rite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Great pomp, and <qex>sacrifice</qex>, and praises loud,<br/
To Dagon.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything consecrated and offered to God, or to a
divinity; an immolated victim, or an offering of any kind, laid upon an
altar, or otherwise presented in the way of religious thanksgiving,
atonement, or conciliation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moloch, horrid king, besmeared with blood<br/
Of human <qex>sacrifice</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My life, if thou preserv'st my life,<br/
Thy <qex>sacrifice</qex> shall be.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Destruction or surrender of anything for the sake of
something else; devotion of some desirable object in behalf of a higher
object, or to a claim deemed more pressing; hence, also, the thing so
devoted or given up; <as>as, the <ex>sacrifice</ex> of interest to
pleasure, or of pleasure to interest</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sale at a price less than the cost or the actual
value.</def> <mark>[Tradesmen's Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Burnt sacrifice</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Burnt
offering</cref>, under <er>Burnt</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sacrifice
hit</b></col> <fld>(Baseball)</fld>, <cd>in batting, a hit of such a
kind that the batter loses his chance of tallying, but enables one or
more who are on bases to get home or gain a base.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrifice</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ri*fice</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sacrificed</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sacrificing</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Sacrifice</er>, <pos>n.</pos>:
cf. F. <ets>sacrifier</ets>, L. <ets>sacrificare</ets>; <ets>sacer</ets>
sacred, holy + <ets>-ficare</ets> (only in comp.) to make. See
<er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make an offering of; to
consecrate or present to a divinity by way of expiation or propitiation,
or as a token acknowledgment or thanksgiving; to immolate on the altar
of God, in order to atone for sin, to procure favor, or to express
thankfulness; <as>as, to <ex>sacrifice</ex> an ox or a
sheep</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Oft <qex>sacrificing</qex> bullock, lamb, or kid.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to destroy, surrender, or suffer to be lost,
for the sake of obtaining something; to give up in favor of a higher or
more imperative object or duty; to devote, with loss or
suffering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Condemned to <qex>sacrifice</qex> his childish years<br/
To babbling ignorance, and to empty fears.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Baronet had <qex>sacrificed</qex> a large sum . . . for the
sake of . . . making this boy his heir.</q> <rj><qau>G.
Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To destroy; to kill.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To sell at a price less than the cost or the actual
value.</def> <mark>[Tradesmen's Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrifice</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ri*fice</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make offerings to God, or
to a deity, of things consumed on the altar; to offer
sacrifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O teacher, some great mischief hath befallen<br/
To that meek man, who well had <qex>sacrificed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrificer</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ri*fi`cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sacrifices.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrificial</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ri*fi"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to sacrifice or sacrifices; consisting in sacrifice;
performing sacrifice.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sacrificial</xex> rites.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrilege</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ri*lege</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sacril<egrave/ge</ets>, L. <ets>sacrilegium</ets>, from
<ets>sacrilegus</ets> that steals, properly, gathers or picks up, sacred
things; <ets>sacer</ets> sacred + <ets>legere</ets> to gather, pick up.
See <er>Sacred</er>, and <er>Legend</er>.]</ety> <def>The sin or crime
of violating or profaning sacred things; the alienating to laymen, or to
common purposes, what has been appropriated or consecrated to religious
persons or uses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the hid treasures in her sacred tomb<br/
With <qex>sacrilege</qex> to dig.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Families raised upon the ruins of churches, and enriched with the
spoils of <qex>sacrilege</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrilegious</ent><br/
<hw>Sac`ri*le"gious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>sacrilege</ets>: cf. L. <ets>sacrilegus</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Violating sacred things; polluted with sacrilege; involving
sacrilege; profane; impious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Above the reach of <qex>sacrilegious</qex> hands.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sac`ri*le"gious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sac`ri*le"gious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrilegist</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ri*le`gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One guilty of
sacrilege.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacring</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ring</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Sacre</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sacring bell</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Sanctus
bell</cref>, under <er>Sanctus</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrist</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"crist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sacrista</ets>. See <er>Sacristan</er>.]</ety> <def>A sacristan;
also, a person retained in a cathedral to copy out music for the choir,
and take care of the books.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacristan</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ris*tan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sacristain</ets>, LL. <ets>sacrista</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sacer</ets>.
See <er>Sacred</er>, and cf. <er>Sexton</er>.]</ety> <def>An officer of
the church who has the care of the utensils or movables, and of the
church in general; a sexton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacristy</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ris*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sacristies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>sacristie</ets>,
LL. <ets>sacristia</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sacer</ets>. See
<er>Sacred</er>.]</ety> <def>An apartment in a church where the sacred
utensils, vestments, etc., are kept; a vestry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacro-</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"cro-</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A combining
form denoting <xex>connection with</xex>, or <xex>relation to</xex>,
<xex>the sacrum</xex>, as in <xex>sacro</xex>-coccygeal,
<xex>sacro</xex>-iliac, <xex>sacro</xex>sciatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrosanct</ent><br/
<hw>Sac"ro*sanct</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sucrosanctus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sacred; inviolable.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrosciatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sa`cro*sci*at"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to both the sacrum and the hip; <as>as, the
<ex>sacrosciatic</ex> foramina formed by the <ex>sacrosciatic</ex>
ligaments which connect the sacrum and the hip bone</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrovertebral</ent><br/
<hw>Sa`cro*ver"te*bral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the sacrum and that part of
the vertebral column immediately anterior to it; <as>as, the
<ex>sacrovertebral</ex> angle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacrum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"crum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>sacra</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>sacer</ets> sacred, <ets>os sacrum</ets> the lowest bone of the
spine.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>That part of the vertebral column
which is directly connected with, or forms a part of, the
pelvis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It may consist of a single vertebra or of several more
or less consolidated. In man it forms the dorsal, or posterior, wall of
the pelvis, and consists of five united vertebrae, which diminish in
size very rapidly to the posterior extremity, which bears the
coccyx.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sacs</ent><br/
<hw>Sacs</hw> <pr>(s<add/ks)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Sac</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A
tribe of Indians, which, together with the Foxes, formerly occupied the
region about Green Bay, Wisconsin.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Sauks</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sad</ent><br/
<hw>Sad</hw> <pr>(s<acr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Sadder</adjf>
<pr>(s<acr/d"d<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Saddest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sad</ets> sated, tired,
satisfied, firm, steadfast, AS. <ets>saed</ets> satisfied, sated; akin
to D. <ets>zat</ets>, OS. <ets>sad</ets>, G. <ets>satt</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sat</ets>, Icel. <ets>sa<edh/r</ets>, <ets>saddr</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sa<thorn/s</ets>, Lith. <ets>sotus</ets>, L. <ets>sat</ets>,
<ets>satis</ets>, enough, <ets>satur</ets> sated, Gr.
<grk>'a`menai</grk> to satiate, <grk>'a`dnh</grk> enough. Cf.
<er>Assets</er>, <er>Sate</er>, <er>Satiate</er>, <er>Satisfy</er>,
<er>Satire</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Sated; satisfied; weary;
tired.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet of that art they can not waxen <qex>sad</qex>,<br/
For unto them it is a bitter sweet.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Heavy; weighty; ponderous; close; hard.</def>
<mark>[Obs., except in a few phrases; <as>as, <ex>sad</ex>
bread</as>.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His hand, more <qex>sad</qex> than lump of lead.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Chalky lands are naturally cold and <qex>sad</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Dull; grave; dark; somber; -- said of colors.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Sad</xex>-colored clothes.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Woad, or wade, is used by the dyers to lay the foundation of all
<qex>sad</qex> colors.</q> <rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Serious; grave; sober; steadfast; not light or
frivolous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Ripe and <xex>sad</xex>
courage.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lady Catharine, a <qex>sad</qex> and religious woman.</q>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which treaty was wisely handled by <qex>sad</qex> and discrete
counsel of both parties.</q> <rj><qau>Ld. Berners.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Affected with grief or unhappiness; cast down with
affliction; downcast; gloomy; mournful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>First were we <qex>sad</qex>, fearing you would not come;<br/
Now <qex>sadder</qex>, that you come so unprovided.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The angelic guards ascended, mute and <qex>sad</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Afflictive; calamitous; causing sorrow; <as>as, a
<ex>sad</ex> accident; a <ex>sad</ex> misfortune</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Hence, bad; naughty; troublesome; wicked.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Sad</xex> tipsy fellows, both of
them.<rdquo/ <rj><au>I. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ex>Sad</ex> is sometimes used in the formation of
self-explaining compounds; <as>as, <ex>sad</ex>-colored,
<ex>sad</ex>-eyed, <ex>sad</ex>-hearted, <ex>sad</ex>-looking, and the
like</as>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sad bread</b></col>, <cd>heavy bread.</cd> <mark>[Scot. &
Local, U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sorrowful; mournful; gloomy; dejected; depressed;
cheerless; downcast; sedate; serious; grave; grievous; afflictive;
calamitous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sad</ent><br/
<hw>Sad</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make sorrowful; to sadden.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How it <qex>sadded</qex> the minister's spirits!</q> <rj><qau>H.
Peters.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>SAD</ent><br/
<hw>SAD</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Seasonal affective
disorder.</def> <mark>[Acron.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sad"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per.
<ets>sad-dar</ets> the hundred gates or ways; <ets>sad</ets> a hundred +
<ets>dar</ets> door, way.]</ety> <def>A work in the Persian tongue,
being a summary of the Zend-Avesta, or sacred books.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadden</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"den</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Saddened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Saddening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make sad.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To render heavy or cohesive.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Marl is binding, and <qex>saddening</qex> of land is the great
prejudice it doth to clay lands.</q> <rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To make dull- or sad-colored, as cloth.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>To make grave or serious; to make melancholy or
sorrowful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her gloomy presence <qex>saddens</qex> all the scene.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadden</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"den</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become, or be made,
sad.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadder</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sadda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddle</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sadel</ets>,
AS. <ets>sadol</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zadel</ets>, G. <ets>sattel</ets>,
OHG. <ets>satal</ets>, <ets>satul</ets>, Icel.
<ets>s<oum/<edh/ull</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>sadel</ets>; cf. Russ.
<ets>siedlo</ets>; all perh. ultimately from the root of E.
<ets>sit</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A seat for a rider, -- usually
made of leather, padded to span comfortably a horse's back, furnished
with stirrups for the rider's feet to rest in, and fastened in place
with a girth; also, a seat for the rider on a bicycle or
tricycle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A padded part of a harness which is worn on a
horse's back, being fastened in place with a girth. It serves various
purposes, as to keep the breeching in place, carry guides for the reins,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A piece of meat containing a part of the backbone of
an animal with the ribs on each side; <as>as, a <ex>saddle</ex> of
mutton, of venison, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A block of wood, usually fastened
to some spar, and shaped to receive the end of another spar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A part, as a flange, which is
hollowed out to fit upon a convex surface and serve as a means of
attachment or support.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The clitellus of an
earthworm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The threshold of a door, when a
separate piece from the floor or landing; -- so called because it spans
and covers the joint between two floors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Phys. Geog.)</fld> <def>A ridge connected two
higher elevations; a low point in the crest line of a ridge; a
col.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A formation of gold-bearing
quartz occurring along the crest of an anticlinal fold, esp. in
Australia.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saddle bar</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>one the
small iron bars to which the lead panels of a glazed window are
secured.</cd> <au>Oxf. Gloss.</au> -- <col><b>Saddle gall</b></col>
<fld>(Far.)</fld>, <cd>a sore or gall upon a horse's back, made by the
saddle.</cd> -- <col><b>Saddle girth</b></col>, <cd>a band passing round
the body of a horse to hold the saddle in its place.</cd> --
<col><b>saddle horse</b></col>, <cd>a horse suitable or trained for
riding with a saddle.</cd> -- <col><b>Saddle joint</b></col>, <cd>in
sheet-metal roofing, a joint formed by bending up the edge of a sheet
and folding it downward over the turned-up edge of the next sheet.</cd>
-- <col><b>Saddle roof</b></col>, <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a roof having
two gables and one ridge; -- said of such a roof when used in places
where a different form is more common; <as>as, a tower surmounted by a
<ex>saddle roof</ex></as>. Called also <altname>saddleback
roof</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Saddle shell</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any thin plicated bivalve shell of the genera
<gen>Placuna</gen> and <gen>Anomia</gen>; -- so called from its shape.
Called also <altname>saddle oyster</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddle</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Saddled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Saddling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sadelian</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put a saddle upon; to
equip (a beast) for riding.</def> <ldquo/<xex>saddle</xex> my
horse.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Abraham rose up early, . . . and <qex>saddled</qex> his ass.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xxii. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: To fix as a charge or burden upon; to load;
to encumber; <as>as, to <ex>saddle</ex> a town with the expense of
bridges and highways</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddleback</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle*back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Saddle-backed</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saddleback roof</b></col>. <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Saddle roof</cref>, under <er>Saddle</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddleback</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle*back`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything
saddle-backed; esp., a hill or ridge having a concave outline at the
top.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The harp seal.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The great blackbacked gull (<spn>Larus
marinus</spn>).</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The larva of a bombycid moth
(<spn>Empretia stimulea</spn>) which has a large, bright green,
saddle-shaped patch of color on the back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddle-backed</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle-backed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having the outline of the upper part concave like the seat of a
saddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a low back and high neck, as a
horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddlebags</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle*bags</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Bags, usually
of leather, united by straps or a band, formerly much used by horseback
riders to carry small articles, one bag hanging on each side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddlebow</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle*bow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sadelboga</ets>.]</ety> <def>The bow or arch in the front part of a
saddle, or the pieces which form the front.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddlecloth</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle*cloth`</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A cloth
under a saddle, and extending out behind; a housing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddled</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a broad patch of color across the back, like a saddle;
saddle-backed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddler</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes
saddles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A harp seal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddlery</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dler*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
materials for making saddles and harnesses; the articles usually offered
for sale in a saddler's shop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The trade or employment of a saddler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddle-shaped</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle-shaped`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shaped like a
saddle.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Bent
down at the sides so as to give the upper part a rounded form.</def>
<rj><au>Henslow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Bent on each side of a mountain
or ridge, without being broken at top; -- said of strata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saddletree</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"dle*tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The frame of a
saddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For <qex>saddletree</qex> scarce reached had he,<br/
His journey to begin.</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadducaic</ent><br/
<hw>Sad`du*ca"ic</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining
to, or like, the Sadducees; <as>as, <ex>Sadducaic</ex>
reasonings</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadducee</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"du*cee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sadducaei</ets>, p., Gr. <?/, Heb. <ets>Tsadd<umac/k<imac/m</ets>;
-- so called from <ets>Ts<amac/d<omac/k</ets>, the founder of the
sect.]</ety> <def>One of a sect among the ancient Jews, who denied the
resurrection, a future state, and the existence of angels.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sad`du*ce"an</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadducism</ent><br/
<ent>Sadduceeism</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sad"du*cee`ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sad"du*cism</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The tenets of the
Sadducees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadducize</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"du*cize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sadducized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sadducizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
adopt the principles of the Sadducees.</def>
<rj><au>Atterbury.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadh</ent><br/
<hw>Sadh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>s<amac/dhu</ets> perfect, pure.]</ety> <def>A member of a
monotheistic sect of Hindoos. Sadhs resemble the Quakers in many
respects.</def> <rj><au>Balfour (Cyc. of India).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadiron</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"i`ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably
<ets>sad</ets> heavy + <ets>iron</ets>.]</ety> <def>An iron for
smoothing clothes; a flatiron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadly</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Wearily; heavily;
firmly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In go the spears full <qex>sadly</qex> in arest.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Seriously; soberly; gravely.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To tell thee <qex>sadly</qex>, shepherd, without blame<br/
Or our neglect, we lost her as we came.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Grievously; deeply; sorrowfully; miserably.</def>
<ldquo/He <xex>sadly</xex> suffers in their grief.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadness</ent><br/
<hw>Sad"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Heaviness;
firmness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Seriousness; gravity; discretion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her <qex>sadness</qex> and her benignity.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Quality of being sad, or unhappy; gloominess;
sorrowfulness; dejection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dim <qex>sadness</qex> did not spare<br/
That time celestial visages.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sorrow; heaviness; dejection. See
<er>Grief</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sadr</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sadr</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
plant of the genus <gen>Ziziphus</gen> (<spn>Ziziphus lotus</spn>); --
so called by the Arabs of Barbary, who use its berries for food. See
<er>Lotus</er> <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saengerbund</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Saeng"er*bund`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>G.
pl.</it> <plw>Saengerb<uum/nde</plw> <pr>(#)</pr></plu>. <ety>[G.
<ets>s<aum/ngerbund</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Music)</fld> <def>A singers'
union; an association of singers or singing clubs, esp.
German.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saengerfest</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Saeng"er*fest</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>s<aum/ngerfest</ets>.]</ety> <def>A festival of singers; a German
singing festival.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safe</ent><br/
<hw>Safe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Safer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Safest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sauf</ets>, F.
<ets>sauf</ets>, fr. L. <ets>salvus</ets>, akin to <ets>salus</ets>
health, welfare, safety. Cf. <er>Salute</er>, <er>Salvation</er>,
<er>Sage</er> a plant, <er>Save</er>, <er>Salvo</er> an
exception.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Free from harm, injury, or risk;
untouched or unthreatened by danger or injury; unharmed; unhurt; secure;
whole; <as>as, <ex>safe</ex> from disease; <ex>safe</ex> from storms;
<ex>safe</ex> from foes</as>.</def> <ldquo/And ye dwelled
<xex>safe</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>1 Sam. xii. 11.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They escaped all <qex>safe</qex> to land.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xxvii.
44.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Established in a <qex>safe</qex>, unenvied throne.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Conferring safety; securing from harm; not exposing
to danger; confining securely; to be relied upon; not dangerous; <as>as,
a <ex>safe</ex> harbor; a <ex>safe</ex> bridge, etc.</as></def>
<ldquo/The man of <xex>safe</xex> discretion.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The King of heaven hath doomed<br/
This place our dungeon, not our <qex>safe</qex> retreat.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1267 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Incapable of doing harm; no longer dangerous; in
secure care or custody; <as>as, the prisoner is
<ex>safe</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But Banquo's <qex>safe</qex>?<br/
Ay, my good lord, <qex>safe</qex> in a ditch he bides.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Safe hit</b></col> <fld>(Baseball)</fld>, <cd>a hit which
enables the batter to get to first base even if no error is made by the
other side.</cd> <-- <col><b>safe house</b></col>, <cd>a residence where
a person in hiding from the authorities or other persons may stay
without being discovered.</cd> --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Secure; unendangered; sure.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safe</ent><br/
<hw>Safe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place for keeping
things in safety.</def> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A strong and fireproof receptacle (as a movable chest of steel,
etc., or a closet or vault of brickwork) for containing money, valuable
papers, or the like.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A ventilated or
refrigerated chest or closet for securing provisions from noxious
animals or insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safe</ent><br/
<hw>Safe</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To render safe; to make
right.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safe-conduct</ent><br/
<hw>Safe"-con"duct</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Safe</ets> + <ets>conduct</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sauf-conduit</ets>.]</ety> <def>That which gives a safe
passage</def>; either <sd>(a)</sd> <def>a convoy or guard to protect a
person in an enemy's country or a foreign country</def>, or <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>a writing, pass, or warrant of security, given to a person to
enable him to travel with safety.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safe-conduct</ent><br/
<hw>Safe`-con*duct"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To conduct
safely; to give safe-conduct to.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He him by all the bonds of love besought<br/
To <qex>safe-conduct</qex> his love.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safeguard</ent><br/
<hw>Safe"guard`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Safe =
<ets>guard</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sauvegarde</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who, or that which, defends or protects; defense;
protection.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy sword, the <qex>safeguard</qex> of thy brother's throne.</q>
<rj><qau>Granville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A convoy or guard to protect a traveler or
property.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A pass; a passport; a safe-conduct.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safeguard</ent><br/
<hw>Safe"guard`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To guard; to protect.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safe-keeping</ent><br/
<hw>Safe"-keep"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Safe</ets> + <ets>keep</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of keeping
or preserving in safety from injury or from escape; care;
custody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safely</ent><br/
<hw>Safe"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a safe manner; danger, injury,
loss, or evil consequences.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safeness</ent><br/
<hw>Safe"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
safe; freedom from hazard, danger, harm, or loss; safety; security; as
the <xex>safeness</xex> of an experiment, of a journey, or of a
possession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safe-pledge</ent><br/
<hw>Safe"-pledge"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>A surety for the appearance of a person at a given time.</def>
<rj><au>Bracton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safety</ent><br/
<hw>Safe"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sauvet<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The condition or
state of being safe; freedom from danger or hazard; exemption from hurt,
injury, or loss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Up led by thee,<br/
Into the heaven I have presumed,<br/
An earthly guest . . . With like <qex>safety</qex> guided down,<br/
Return me to my native element.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Freedom from whatever exposes one to danger or from
liability to cause danger or harm; safeness; hence, the quality of
making safe or secure, or of giving confidence, justifying trust,
insuring against harm or loss, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Would there were any <qex>safety</qex> in thy sex,<br/
That I might put a thousand sorrows off,<br/
And credit thy repentance!</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Preservation from escape; close custody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Imprison him, . . . <br/
Deliver him to <qex>safety</qex>; and return.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Amer. Football)</fld> <def>the act or result of a
ball-carrier on the offensive team being tackled behind his own goal
line, or the downing of a ball behind the offensive team's own goal line
when it had been carried or propelled behind that goal line by a player
on the offensive team; such a play causes a score of two points to be
awarded to the defensive team; -- it is distinguished from
<contr>touchback</contr>, when the ball is downed behind the goal after
being propelled there or last touched by a player of the defending team.
See <er>Touchdown</er>. Same as <cref>Safety touchdown</cref>,
below.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Short for <er>Safety bicycle</er>.</def>
<mark>[archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>a switch on a firearm that locks the trigger and
prevents the firearm from being discharged unintentionally; -- also
called <altname>safety catch</altname>, <altname>safety lock</altname>,
or <altname>lock</altname>.</def> <mark>[archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safety bicycle</ent><br/
<hw>Safety bicycle</hw>. <def>A bicycle with equal or nearly equal
wheels, usually about 28 inches diameter, driven by pedals connected to
the rear (driving) wheel by a multiplying gear. Since the 1930's this
has been the most common type of bicycle, now simply called
<altname>bicycle</altname>. The older high-wheelers are often referred
to as <contr>bone-rattlers</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safety chain</ent><br/
<hw>Safety chain</hw>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Railroads)</fld> <def>A
normally slack chain for preventing excessive movement between a truck
and a car body in sluing.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An auxiliary watch
chain, secured to the clothes, usually out of sight, to prevent stealing
of the watch.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A chain of sheet metal links with
an elongated hole through each broad end, made up by doubling the first
link on itself, slipping the next link through and doubling, and so
on.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Safety arch</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a
discharging arch.</cd> See under <er>Discharge</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos> --
<col><b>Safety belt</b></col>, <cd>a belt made of some buoyant material,
or which is capable of being inflated, so as to enable a person to float
in water; a life preserver.</cd> -- <col><b>Safety buoy</b></col>, <cd>a
buoy to enable a person to float in water; a safety belt.</cd> --
<col><b>Safety cage</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a cage for an
elevator or mine lift, having appliances to prevent it from dropping if
the lifting rope should break.</cd> -- <col><b>Safety lamp</b></col>.
<fld>(Mining)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Lamp</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Safety
match</b></col>, <cd>a match which can be ignited only on a surface
specially prepared for the purpose.</cd> -- <col><b>Safety
pin</b></col>, <cd>a pin made in the form of a clasp, with a guard
covering its point so that it will not prick the wearer.</cd> --
<col><b>Safety plug</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Fusible plug</cref>, under
<er>Fusible</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Safety switch</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Switch</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Safety touchdown</b></col>
<fld>(Football)</fld>, <cd>the act or result of a player's touching to
the ground behind his own goal line a ball which received its last
impulse from a man on his own side; -- distinguished from
<xex>touchback</xex>. See <er>Touchdown</er>. Same as
<er>safety</er></cd> -- <col><b>Safety tube</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a tube to prevent explosion, or to control
delivery of gases by an automatic valvular connection with the outer
air; especially, a bent funnel tube with bulbs for adding those reagents
which produce unpleasant fumes or violent effervescence.</cd> --
<col><b>Safety valve</b></col>, <cd>a valve which is held shut by a
spring or weight and opens automatically to permit the escape of steam,
or confined gas, water, etc., from a boiler, or other vessel, when the
pressure becomes too great for safety; also, sometimes, a similar valve
opening inward to admit air to a vessel in which the pressure is less
than that of the atmosphere, to prevent collapse.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safflow</ent><br/
<hw>Saf"flow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
safflower.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safflower</ent><br/
<hw>Saf"flow`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>safleur</ets>, <ets>saflor</ets>, for <ets>safran</ets>, influenced
by <ets>fleur</ets> flower. See <er>Saffron</er>, and
<er>Flower</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An annual
composite plant (<spn>Carthamus tinctorius</spn>), the flowers of which
are used as a dyestuff and in making rouge; bastard, or false,
saffron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The dried flowers of the <spn>Carthamus
tinctorius</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A dyestuff from these flowers. See <er>Safranin</er>
<sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Oil of safflower</b></col>, <cd>a purgative oil expressed
from the seeds of the safflower.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saffron</ent><br/
<hw>Saf"fron</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>saffran</ets>, F. <ets>safran</ets>; cf. It. <ets>zafferano</ets>,
Sp. <ets>azafran</ets>, Pg. <ets>a<cced/afr<atil/o</ets>; all fr. Ar. &
Per. <ets>za' far<amac/n</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A bulbous iridaceous plant (<spn>Crocus sativus</spn>) having blue
flowers with large yellow stigmas. See <er>Crocus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The aromatic, pungent, dried stigmas, usually with
part of the stile, of the <spn>Crocus sativus</spn>. Saffron is used in
cookery, and in coloring confectionery, liquors, varnishes, etc., and
was formerly much used in medicine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An orange or deep yellow color, like that of the
stigmas of the <spn>Crocus sativus</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Bastard saffron</b></col>, <col><b>Dyer's
saffron</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Safflower</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Meadow saffron</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a bulbous plant (<spn>Colchichum autumnale</spn>)
of Europe, resembling saffron.</cd> -- <col><b>Saffron wood</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the yellowish wood of a South African tree
(<spn>Elaeodendron croceum</spn>); also, the tree itself.</cd> --
<col><b>Saffron yellow</b></col>, <cd>a shade of yellow like that
obtained from the stigmas of the true saffron (<spn>Crocus
sativus</spn>).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saffron</ent><br/
<hw>Saf"fron</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the color
of the stigmas of saffron flowers; deep orange-yellow; <as>as, a
<ex>saffron</ex> face; a <ex>saffron</ex> streamer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saffron</ent><br/
<hw>Saf"fron</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To give color and flavor to, as
by means of saffron; to spice.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And in Latyn I speak a wordes few,<br/
To <qex>saffron</qex> with my predication.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saffrony</ent><br/
<hw>Saf"fron*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a color
somewhat like saffron; yellowish.</def> <rj><au>Lord
(1630).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safranin</ent><br/
<hw>Saf"ra*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>An orange-red dyestuff extracted from the
saffron.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A red dyestuff
extracted from the safflower, and formerly used in dyeing wool, silk,
and cotton pink and scarlet; -- called also <altname>Spanish
red</altname>, <altname>China lake</altname>, and
<altname>carthamin</altname>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>An orange-red
dyestuff prepared from certain nitro compounds of creosol, and used as a
substitute for the safflower dye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Safranine</ent><br/
<hw>Saf"ra*nine</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called
because used as a substitute for safranin.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An orange-red nitrogenous dyestuff produced artificially by
oxidizing certain aniline derivatives, and used in dyeing silk and wool;
also, any one of the series of which safranine proper is the
type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sag</ent><br/
<hw>Sag</hw> <pr>(s<acr/g)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sagged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sagging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to
Sw. <ets>sacka</ets> to settle, sink down, LG. <ets>sacken</ets>, D.
<ets>zakken</ets>. Cf. <er>Sink</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To sink, in the middle, by its weight or under applied
pressure, below a horizontal line or plane; <as>as, a line or cable
supported by its ends <ex>sags</ex>, though tightly drawn; the floor of
a room <ex>sags</ex></as>; hence, to lean, give way, or settle from a
vertical position; <as>as, a building may <ex>sag</ex> one way or
another; a door <ex>sags</ex> on its hinges</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To lose firmness or elasticity; to sink; to
droop; to flag; to bend; to yield, as the mind or spirits, under the
pressure of care, trouble, doubt, or the like; to be unsettled or
unbalanced.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind I sway by, and the heart I bear,<br/
Shall never <qex>sag</qex> with doubt nor shake with fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To loiter in walking; to idle along; to drag or
droop heavily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sag to leeward</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to
make much leeway by reason of the wind, sea, or current; to drift to
leeward; -- said of a vessel.</cd> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sag</ent><br/
<hw>Sag</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to bend or give way; to
load.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sag</ent><br/
<hw>Sag</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State of sinking or bending;
sagging.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saga</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ga</hw> <pr>(s<amac/"g<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sagas</plw> <pr>(-g<adot/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[Icel., akin to E. <ets>saw</ets> a saying. See <er>Say</er>, and
cf. <er>Saw</er>.]</ety> <def>A Scandinavian legend, or heroic or mythic
tradition, among the Norsemen and kindred people; a northern European
popular historical or religious tale of olden time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And then the blue-eyed Norseman told<br/
A <qex>saga</qex> of the days of old.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagacious</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*ga"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sagax</ets>, <ets>sagacis</ets>, akin to <ets>sagire</ets> to
perceive quickly or keenly, and probably to E. <ets>seek</ets>. See
<er>Seek</er>, and cf. <er>Presage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
quick sense perceptions; keen-scented; skilled in following a
trail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sagacious</qex> of his quarry from so far.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, of quick intellectual perceptions; of keen
penetration and judgment; discerning and judicious; knowing;
far-sighted; shrewd; sage; wise; <as>as, a <ex>sagacious</ex> man; a
<ex>sagacious</ex> remark</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Instinct . . . makes them, many times, <qex>sagacious</qex> above
our apprehension.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Only <qex>sagacious</qex> heads light on these observations, and
reduce them into general propositions.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Shrewd</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sa*ga"cious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sa*ga"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagacity</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*gac"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sagacitas</ets>. See <er>Sagacious</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality of
being sagacious; quickness or acuteness of sense perceptions; keenness
of discernment or penetration with soundness of judgment;
shrewdness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some [brutes] show that nice <qex>sagacity</qex> of smell.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Natural <qex>sagacity</qex> improved by generous education.</q>
<rj><qau>V. Knox.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Penetration; shrewdness; judiciousness.</syn> --
<usage><er>Sagacity</er>, <er>Penetration</er>. <xex>Penetration</xex>
enables us to enter into the depths of an abstruse subject, to detect
motives, plans, etc. <xex>Sagacity</xex> adds to penetration a keen,
practical judgment, which enables one to guard against the designs of
others, and to turn everything to the best possible
advantage.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagamore</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"a*more</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sachem</er>.]</ety> <def>The head of a tribe among the American
Indians; a chief; -- generally used as synonymous with
<xex>sachem</xex>, but some writers distinguished between them, making
the <xex>sachem</xex> a chief of the first rank, and a
<xex>sagamore</xex> one of the second rank.</def> <ldquo/Be it
<xex>sagamore</xex>, sachem, or powwow.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A juice used in medicine.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagapen</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"a*pen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sagapenum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagapenum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sag`a*pe"num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sagapenon</ets>, <ets>sacopenium</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>sagapin</ets>, gomme <ets>sagapin</ets>, sagap<eacute/num, Ar.
<ets>sikb<imac/naj</ets>, Per. <ets>sakb<imac/nah</ets>,
<ets>sikb<imac/nah</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A fetid gum
resin obtained from a species of <gen>Ferula</gen>. It has been used in
hysteria, etc., but is now seldom met with. See also
<er>asafetida</er>.</def> <rj><au>U. S. Disp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagathy</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"a*thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sagatis</ets>: cf. Sp. <ets>sagat<iacute/</ets>,
<ets>saet<iacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>A mixed woven fabric of silk and
cotton, or silk and wool; sayette; also, a light woolen
fabric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sage</ent><br/
<hw>Sage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sauge</ets>, F.
<ets>sauge</ets>, L. <ets>salvia</ets>, from <ets>salvus</ets> saved, in
allusion to its reputed healing virtues. See <er>Safe</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A suffruticose labiate plant
(<spn>Salvia officinalis</spn>) with grayish green foliage, much used in
flavoring meats, etc. The name is often extended to the whole genus, of
which many species are cultivated for ornament, as the scarlet sage, and
Mexican red and blue sage.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
sagebrush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Meadow sage</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
blue-flowered species of <gen>Salvia</gen> (<spn>Salvia pratensis</spn>)
growing in meadows in Europe.</cd> -- <col><b>Sage cheese</b></col>,
<cd>cheese flavored with sage, and colored green by the juice of leaves
of spinach and other plants which are added to the milk.</cd> --
<col><b>Sage cock</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the male of the sage
grouse; in a more general sense, the specific name of the sage
grouse.</cd> -- <col><b>Sage green</b></col>, <cd>of a dull grayish
green color, like the leaves of garden sage.</cd> -- <col><b>Sage
grouse</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a very large American grouse
(<spn>Centrocercus urophasianus</spn>), native of the dry sagebrush
plains of Western North America. Called also <altname>cock of the
plains</altname>. The male is called <stype>sage cock</stype>, and the
female <stype>sage hen</stype>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sage
hare</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Sage rabbit</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a species of hare (<spn>Lepus Nuttalli</spn>
syn. <spn>Lepus artemisia</spn>) which inhabits the arid regions of
<geog>Western North America</geog> and lives among sagebrush. By recent
writers it is considered to be merely a variety of the common
cottontail, or wood rabbit.</cd> -- <col><b>Sage hen</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the female of the sage grouse.</cd> --
<col><b>Sage sparrow</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small sparrow
(<spn>Amphispiza Belli</spn>, var. <varn>Nevadensis</varn>) which
inhabits the dry plains of the Rocky Mountain region, living among
sagebrush.</cd> -- <col><b>Sage thrasher</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a singing bird (<spn>Oroscoptes montanus</spn>) which inhabits the
sagebrush plains of <geog>Western North America</geog>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sage willow</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a species of willow
(<spn>Salix tristis</spn>) forming a low bush with nearly sessile
grayish green leaves.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sage</ent><br/
<hw>Sage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sager</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sagest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[F., fr. L. <ets>sapius</ets> (only
in <ets>nesapius</ets> unwise, foolish), fr. <ets>sapere</ets> to be
wise; perhaps akin to E. <ets>sap</ets>. Cf. <er>Savor</er>,
<er>Sapient</er>, <er>Insipid</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having nice
discernment and powers of judging; prudent; grave; sagacious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All you <qex>sage</qex> counselors, hence!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Proceeding from wisdom; well judged; shrewd; well
adapted to the purpose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Commanders, who, cloaking their fear under show of <qex>sage</qex>
advice, counseled the general to retreat.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Grave; serious; solemn.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/[Great bards] in <xex>sage</xex> and solemn tunes have
sung.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj> <-- the "great bards" was moved
inside the quote for consistency. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Wise; sagacious; sapient; grave; prudent;
judicious.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sage</ent><br/
<hw>Sage</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wise man; a man of gravity and
wisdom; especially, a man venerable for years, and of sound judgment and
prudence; a grave philosopher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At his birth a star,<br/
Unseen before in heaven, proclaims him come,<br/
And guides the Eastern <qex>sages</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagebrush</ent><br/
<hw>Sage"brush`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A low irregular
shrub (<spn>Artemisia tridentata</spn>), of the order
<ord>Compositae</ord>, covering vast tracts of the dry alkaline regions
of the American plains; -- called also <altname>sagebush</altname>, and
<altname>wild sage</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagebrush State</ent><br/
<hw>Sagebrush State</hw>. <def>Nevada; -- a nickname.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagely</ent><br/
<hw>Sage"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sage manner;
wisely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagene</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*gene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>sajene</ets>.]</ety> <def>A Russian measure of length equal to
about seven English feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sageness</ent><br/
<hw>Sage"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being sage; wisdom; sagacity; prudence; gravity.</def>
<rj><au>Ascham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagenite</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"e*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sag<eacute/nite</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sagena</ets> a large net. See
<er>Seine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Acicular rutile occurring
in reticulated forms imbedded in quartz.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagenitic</ent><br/
<hw>Sag`e*nit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Resembling sagenite; -- applied to quartz when containing acicular
crystals of other minerals, most commonly rutile, also tourmaline,
actinolite, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagger</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Seggar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pot or case of fire clay, in
which fine stoneware is inclosed while baking in the kiln; a
seggar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The clay of which such pots or cases are
made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagging</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"ging</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bending or sinking
between the ends of a thing, in consequence of its own, or an imposed,
weight; an arching downward in the middle, as of a ship after straining.
Cf. <er>Hogging</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saginate</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saginatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>saginare</ets> to fat, fr.
<ets>sagina</ets> stuffing.]</ety> <def>To make fat; to pamper.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Many a <xex>saginated</xex> boar.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagination</ent><br/
<hw>Sag`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saginatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of fattening or
pampering.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Topsell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagitta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*git"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., an
arrow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A small
constellation north of Aquila; the Arrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The keystone of an arch.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The distance from a point in a
curve to the chord; also, the versed sine of an arc; -- so called from
its resemblance to an arrow resting on the bow and string.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The larger of the two otoliths,
or ear bones, found in most fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of transparent,
free-swimming marine worms having lateral and caudal fins, and capable
of swimming rapidly. It is the type of the class Chaetognatha.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1268 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Sagittal</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"it*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sagitta</ets> an arrow: cf. F. <ets>sagittal</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to an arrow; resembling an arrow;
furnished with an arrowlike appendage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to
the sagittal suture; in the region of the sagittal suture; rhabdoidal;
<as>as, the <ex>sagittal</ex> furrow, or groove, on the inner surface of
the roof of the skull</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In the mesial plane;
mesial; <as>as, a <ex>sagittal</ex> section of an animal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sagittal suture</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
suture between the two parietal bones in the top of the skull; -- called
also <altname>rabdoidal suture</altname>, and <altname>interparietal
suture</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagittarius</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sag`it*ta"ri*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
literally, an archer, fr. <ets>sagittarius</ets> belonging to an arrow,
fr. <ets>sagitta</ets> an arrow.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The ninth of the twelve signs of the zodiac, which the
sun enters about November 22, marked thus [<Sagittarius/] in almanacs;
the Archer.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A zodiacal constellation,
represented on maps and globes as a centaur shooting an arrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagittary</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"it*ta"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sagittarius</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Myth.)</fld> <def>A
centaur; a fabulous being, half man, half horse, armed with a bow and
quiver.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The Arsenal in Venice; -- so called from having a
figure of an archer over the door.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagittary</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"it*ta*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sagittarius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to, or resembling, an
arrow.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagittate</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"it*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>sagittatus</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sagitta</ets> an arrow.]</ety>
<def>Shaped like an arrowhead; triangular, with the two basal angles
prolonged downward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagittated</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"it*ta`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sagittal;
sagittate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagittocyst</ent><br/
<hw>Sag"it*to*cyst</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sagitta</er>, and <er>Cyst</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
defensive cell containing a minute rodlike structure which may be
expelled. Such cells are found in certain Turbellaria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sago</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"go</hw> <pr>(s<amac/"g<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Malay.
<ets>s<amac/gu</ets>.]</ety> <def>A dry granulated starch imported from
the East Indies, much used for making puddings and as an article of diet
for the sick; also, as starch, for stiffening textile fabrics. It is
prepared from the stems of several East Indian and Malayan palm trees,
but chiefly from the <spn>Metroxylon Sagu</spn>; also from several
cycadaceous plants (<spn>Cycas revoluta</spn>, <spn>Zamia
integrifolia</spn>, etc.).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Portland sago</b></col>, <cd>a kind of sago prepared from
the corms of the cuckoopint (<spn>Arum maculatum</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Sago palm</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A palm
tree which yields sago</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A species of Cycas
(<spn>Cycas revoluta</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Sago spleen</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a morbid condition of the spleen, produced by
amyloid degeneration of the organ, in which a cross section shows
scattered gray translucent bodies looking like grains of
sago.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagoin</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*goin"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sagouin</ets>(formed from the native South American name).]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A marmoset; -- called also
<altname>sagouin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"gum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Saga</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>sagum</ets>,
<ets>sagus</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/. Cf. <er>Say</er> a kind of serge.]</ety>
<fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The military cloak of the Roman
soldiers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"gus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sago</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of palms from which
sago is obtained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sagy</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of sage; seasoned
with sage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saheb</ent><br/
<ent>Sahib</ent><br/
<mhw>\'d8<hw>Sa"hib</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Sa"heb</hw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr></mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar. <ets><cced/<amac/hib</ets>
master, lord, fem. <ets><cced/<amac/hibah</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
respectful title or appellation given to Europeans of rank.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sahibah</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"hi*bah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sahib</er>.]</ety> <def>A lady; mistress.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sahidic</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*hid"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Thebaic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sahlite</ent><br/
<hw>Sah"lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Salite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sahui</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*hui"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A marmoset.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sai</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Pg.
<ets>sahi</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Capuchin</er>, 3
<sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saibling</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sai"bling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dial. G.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A European mountain trout (<spn>Salvelinus
alpinus</spn>); -- called also <altname>Bavarian
charr</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saic</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sa<ium/que</ets>, Turk. <ets>sha<ium/ka</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A kind of ketch very common in the Levant, which
has neither topgallant sail nor mizzen topsail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Said</ent><br/
<hw>Said</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Say</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Said</ent><br/
<hw>Said</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Before-mentioned; already spoken of or
specified; aforesaid; -- used chiefly in legal style.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saiga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sai"ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>saika</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An antelope (<spn>Saiga
Tartarica</spn>) native of the plains of Siberia and Eastern Russia. The
male has erect annulated horns, and tufts of long hair beneath the eyes
and ears.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saikyr</ent><br/
<hw>Sai"kyr</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>Saker</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sail</ent><br/
<hw>Sail</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>seil</ets>, AS.
<ets>segel</ets>, <ets>segl</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zeil</ets>, OHG.
<ets>segal</ets>, G. & Sw. <ets>segel</ets>, Icel. <ets>segl</ets>, Dan.
<ets>seil</ets>. <root/ 153.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An extent of canvas
or other fabric by means of which the wind is made serviceable as a
power for propelling vessels through the water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Behoves him now both <qex>sail</qex> and oar.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything resembling a sail, or regarded as a
sail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A wing; a van.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like an eagle soaring<br/
To weather his broad <qex>sails</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The extended surface of the arm of a
windmill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A sailing vessel; a vessel of any kind; a
craft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense, the plural has usually the same form as
the singular; <as>as, twenty <ex>sail</ex> were in
sight</as>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A passage by a sailing vessel; a journey or
excursion upon the water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Sails are of two general kinds, <stype>fore-and-aft
sails</stype>, and <stype>square sails</stype>. Square sails are always
bent to yards, with their foot lying across the line of the vessel.
Fore-and-aft sails are set upon stays or gaffs with their foot in line
with the keel. A fore-and-aft sail is triangular, or quadrilateral with
the after leech longer than the fore leech. Square sails are
quadrilateral, but not necessarily square. See Phrases under
<er>Fore</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and <er>Square</er>, <pos>a.</pos>; also,
<er>Bark</er>, <er>Brig</er>, <er>Schooner</er>, <er>Ship</er>,
<er>Stay</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sail burton</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a purchase
for hoisting sails aloft for bending.</cd> -- <col><b>Sail
fluke</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the whiff.</cd> -- <col><b>Sail
hook</b></col>, <cd>a small hook used in making sails, to hold the seams
square.</cd> -- <col><b>Sail loft</b></col>, <cd>a loft or room where
sails are cut out and made.</cd> -- <col><b>Sail room</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a room in a vessel where sails are stowed when
not in use.</cd> -- <col><b>Sail yard</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>the yard or spar on which a sail is extended.</cd> --
<col><b>Shoulder-of-mutton sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a
triangular sail of peculiar form. It is chiefly used to set on a boat's
mast.</cd> -- <col><b>To crowd sail</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>Crowd</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To loose sails</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to unfurl or spread sails.</cd> -- <col><b>To
make sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to extend an additional
quantity of sail.</cd> -- <col><b>To set a sail</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to extend or spread a sail to the wind.</cd> --
<col><b>To set sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to unfurl or
spread the sails; hence, to begin a voyage.</cd> -- <col><b>To shorten
sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to reduce the extent of sail, or
take in a part.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike sail</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to lower the sails suddenly, as in saluting, or
in sudden gusts of wind; hence, to acknowledge inferiority; to abate
pretension.</cd> -- <col><b>Under sail</b></col>, <cd>having the sails
spread.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sail</ent><br/
<hw>Sail</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sailed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sailing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>segelian</ets>,
<ets>seglian</ets>. See <er>Sail</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To be impelled or driven forward by the action of wind upon sails,
as a ship on water; to be impelled on a body of water by the action of
steam or other power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move through or on the water; to swim, as a fish
or a water fowl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be conveyed in a vessel on water; to pass by
water; <as>as, they <ex>sailed</ex> from London to
Canton</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To set sail; to begin a voyage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To move smoothly through the air; to glide through
the air without apparent exertion, as a bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As is a winged messenger of heaven, . . .<br/
When he bestrides the lazy pacing clouds,<br/
And <qex>sails</qex> upon the bosom of the air.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sail</ent><br/
<hw>Sail</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pass or move upon,
as in a ship, by means of sails; hence, to move or journey upon (the
water) by means of steam or other force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A thousand ships were manned to <qex>sail</qex> the sea.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fly through; to glide or move smoothly
through.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sublime she <qex>sails</qex><br/
The aerial space, and mounts the wing<egrave/d gales.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To direct or manage the motion of, as a vessel;
<as>as, to <ex>sail</ex> one's own ship</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailable</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
sailed over; navigable; <as>as, a <ex>sailable</ex>
river</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailboat</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"boat`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A boat propelled by a sail or
sails.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailcloth</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"cloth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Duck or canvas
used in making sails.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailer</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
sailor.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A ship or other vessel; -- with qualifying words
descriptive of speed or manner of sailing; <as>as, a heavy
<ex>sailer</ex>; a fast <ex>sailer</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailfish</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"fish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The banner fish, or spikefish
(<gen>Histiophorus</gen>.)</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The basking, or
liver, shark.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The quillback.</def> <-- Illust.
of Sailfish (Histiophorus Americanus) --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailing</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of one who, or that which, sails; the motion of a vessel on water,
impelled by wind or steam; the act of starting on a voyage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The art of managing a vessel;
seamanship; navigation; <as>as, globular <ex>sailing</ex>; oblique
<ex>sailing</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ For the several methods of sailing, see under
<er>Circular</er>, <er>Globular</er>, <er>Oblique</er>,
<er>Parallel</er>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sailing master</b></col> <fld>(U. S. Navy)</fld>,
<cd>formerly, a warrant officer, ranking next below a lieutenant, whose
duties were to navigate the vessel; and under the direction of the
executive officer, to attend to the stowage of the hold, to the cables,
rigging, etc. The grade was merged in that of <xex>master</xex> in
1862.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailless</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of
sails.</def> <rj><au>Pollok.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailmaker</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"mak`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One whose
occupation is to make or repair sails.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sail"mak`ing</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sailor</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who follows the
business of navigating ships or other vessels; one who understands the
practical management of ships; one of the crew of a vessel; a mariner; a
common seaman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Mariner; seaman; seafarer.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sailor's choice</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>An excellent marine food fish (<spn>Diplodus
rhomboides</spn>, syn. <spn>Lagodon rhomboides</spn>) of the
<geog>Southern United States</geog>; -- called also
<altname>porgy</altname>, <altname>squirrel fish</altname>,
<altname>yellowtail</altname>, and <altname>salt-water
bream</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A species of grunt
(<spn>Orthopristis chrysopterus</spn> syn. <spn>Pomadasys
chrysopterus</spn>), an excellent food fish common on the southern
coasts of the United States; -- called also <altname>hogfish</altname>,
and <altname>pigfish</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saily</ent><br/
<hw>Sail"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a sail.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saim</ent><br/
<hw>Saim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>sain</ets>, LL.
<ets>saginum</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sagina</ets> a fattening.]</ety>
<def>Lard; grease.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saimir</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sai*mir"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The squirrel monkey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sain</ent><br/
<hw>Sain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>p. p.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Say</er>, for <ets>sayen</ets></mord>. <def>Said.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sain</ent><br/
<hw>Sain</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <ets>Saint</ets>,
<ets>Sane</ets>.]</ety> <def>To sanctify; to bless so as to protect from
evil influence.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sainfoin</ent><br/
<hw>Sain"foin</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>sain</ets> wholesome (L. <ets>sanus</ets>; see <er>Sane</er>.) +
<ets>foin</ets> hay (L. <ets>faenum</ets>); or perh. fr.
<ets>saint</ets> sacred (L. <ets>sanctus</ets>; see <er>Saint</er>) +
<ets>foin</ets> hay.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
leguminous plant (<spn>Onobrychis sativa</spn>) cultivated for
fodder.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>saintfoin</asp>.]</altsp>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A kind of tick trefoil (<spn>Desmodium
Canadense</spn>).</def> <mark>[Canada]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saint</ent><br/
<hw>Saint</hw> <pr>(s<amac/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>sanctus</ets> sacred, properly p. p. of <ets>sancire</ets> to
render sacred by a religious act, to appoint as sacred; akin to
<ets>sacer</ets> sacred. Cf. <er>Sacred</er>, <er>Sanctity</er>,
<er>Sanctum</er>, <er>Sanctus</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A person
sanctified; a holy or godly person; one eminent for piety and virtue;
any true Christian, as being redeemed and consecrated to God.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Them that are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be
<qex>saints</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>1 Cor. i. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the blessed in heaven.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then shall thy <qex>saints</qex>, unmixed, and from the impure<br/
Far separate, circling thy holy mount,<br/
Unfeigned hallelujahs to thee sing.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>One canonized by the
church.</def> <altsp>[Abbrev. <abbr>St.</abbr>]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saint Andrew's cross</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A cross
shaped like the letter X.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>A low
North American shrub (<spn>Ascyrum Crux-Andreae</spn>, the petals of
which have the form of a Saint Andrew's cross.</cd> <au>Gray.</au> --
<col><b>Saint Anthony's cross</b></col>, <cd>a
<universbold>T</universbold>-shaped cross.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint
Anthony's fire</b></col>, <cd>the erysipelas; -- popularly so called
because it was supposed to have been cured by the intercession of Saint
Anthony.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Anthony's nut</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the groundnut (<spn>Bunium flexuosum</spn>); --
so called because swine feed on it, and St. Anthony was once a
swineherd.</cd> <au>Dr. Prior.</au> -- <col><b>Saint Anthony's
turnip</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the bulbous crowfoot, a favorite
food of swine.</cd> <au>Dr. Prior.</au> -- <col><b>Saint Barnaby's
thistle</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of knapweed
(<spn>Centaurea solstitialis</spn>) flowering on St. Barnabas's Day,
June 11th.</cd> <au>Dr. Prior.</au> -- <col><b>Saint Bernard</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a breed of large, handsome dogs celebrated for
strength and sagacity, formerly bred chiefly at the Hospice of St.
Bernard in Switzerland, but now common in Europe and America. There are
two races, the smooth-haired and the rough-haired.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint
Catharine's flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the plant
love-in-a-mist. See under <er>Love</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint
Cuthbert's beads</b></col> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>, <cd>the fossil joints
of crinoid stems.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Dabeoc's heath</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a heatherlike plant (<spn>Daboecia
polifolia</spn>), named from an Irish saint.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint
Distaff's Day</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Distaff</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Saint Elmo's fire</b></col>, <cd>a luminous, flamelike
appearance, sometimes seen in dark, tempestuous nights, at some
prominent point on a ship, particularly at the masthead and the
yardarms. It has also been observed on land, and is due to the discharge
of electricity from elevated or pointed objects. A single flame is
called a <stype>Helena</stype>, or a <stype>Corposant</stype>; a double,
or twin, flame is called a <stype>Castor and Pollux</stype>, or a
<stype>double Corposant</stype>. It takes its name from St. Elmo, the
patron saint of sailors.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint George's cross</b></col>
<fld>(Her.)</fld>, <cd>a Greek cross gules upon a field argent, the
field being represented by a narrow fimbriation in the ensign, or union
jack, of Great Britain.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint George's ensign</b></col>,
<cd>a red cross on a white field with a union jack in the upper corner
next the mast. It is the distinguishing badge of ships of the royal navy
of England; -- called also <altname>the white ensign</altname>.</cd>
<au>Brande & C.</au> -- <col><b>Saint George's flag</b></col>, <cd>a
smaller flag resembling the ensign, but without the union jack; used as
the sign of the presence and command of an admiral.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark> <au>Brande & C.</au> -- <col><b>Saint Gobain
glass</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a fine variety of soda-lime
plate glass, so called from St. Gobain in France, where it was
manufactured.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Ignatius's bean</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the seed of a tree of the Philippines
(<spn>Strychnos Ignatia</spn>), of properties similar to the nux
vomica.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint James's shell</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a pecten (<spn>Vola Jacobaeus</spn>) worn by
pilgrims to the Holy Land.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint James's-wort</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of ragwort (<spn>Senecio
Jacobaea</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Saint John's bread</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Carob</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint
John's-wort</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any plant of the genus
<gen>Hypericum</gen>, most species of which have yellow flowers; --
called also <altname>John's-wort</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint
Leger</b></col>, <cd>the name of a race for three-year-old horses run
annually in September at Doncaster, England; -- instituted in 1776 by
Col. St. Leger.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Martin's herb</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small tropical American violaceous plant
(<spn>Sauvagesia erecta</spn>). It is very mucilaginous and is used in
medicine.</cd> <-- p. 1269 pr=PCP --> -- <col><b>Saint Martin's
summer</b></col>, <cd>a season of mild, damp weather frequently
prevailing during late autumn in England and the Mediterranean
countries; -- so called from St. Martin's Festival, occurring on
November 11. It corresponds to the Indian summer in America.</cd>
<au>Shak.</au> <au>Whittier.</au> -- <col><b>Saint Patrick's
cross</b></col>. -- <col><b>Saint Patrick's Day</b></col>, <cd>the 17th
of March, anniversary of the death (about 466) of St. Patrick, the
apostle and patron saint of Ireland.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Peter's
fish</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>John Dory</cref>, under
<er>John</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Peter's-wort</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name of several plants, as <spn>Hypericum
Ascyron</spn>, <spn>Hypericum quadrangulum</spn>, <spn>Ascyrum
stans</spn>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Peter's wreath</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a shrubby kind of <gen>Spiraea</gen>
(<spn>Spiraea hypericifolia</spn>), having long slender branches covered
with clusters of small white blossoms in spring.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint's
bell</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Sanctus bell</cref>, under
<er>Sanctus</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Vitus's dance</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>chorea; -- so called from the supposed cures
wrought on intercession to this saint.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saint</ent><br/
<hw>Saint</hw> <pr>(s<amac/nt)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sainted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sainting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make a saint of; to enroll
among the saints by an official act, as of the pope; to canonize; to
give the title or reputation of a saint to (some one).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A large hospital, erected by a shoemaker who has been beatified,
though never <qex>sainted</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To saint it</b></col>, <cd>to act as a saint, or with a
show of piety.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whether the charmer sinner it or <qex>saint it</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Saint</ent><br/
<hw>Saint</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act or live as a saint.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintdom</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"dom</hw> <pr>(-d<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or
character of a saint.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sainted</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consecrated; sacred;
holy; pious.</def> <ldquo/A most <xex>sainted</xex> king.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Amongst the enthroned gods on <qex>sainted</qex> seats.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Entered into heaven; -- a euphemism for
<altname>dead</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintess</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female saint.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Fisher.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sainthood</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"hood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
state of being a saint; the condition of a saint.</def>
<rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The order, or united body, of saints; saints,
considered collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was supposed he felt no call to any expedition that might
endanger the reign of the military <qex>sainthood</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintish</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat saintlike; -- used
ironically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintism</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The character or
quality of saints; also, hypocritical pretense of holiness.</def>
<rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintlike</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a
saint; suiting a saint; becoming a saint; saintly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Glossed over only with a <qex>saintlike</qex> show.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintliness</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
saintly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintly</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Saintlier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Saintliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Like a saint; becoming a holy
person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So dear to Heaven is <qex>saintly</qex> chastity.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>St. Nicholas</ent><br/
<ent>Saint Nicholas</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Saint Nicholas</hw>, <hw>St. Nicholas</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A Dutch saint, who was reputed to bring gifts to
children on Christmas even, giving rise to the modern legend of
<altname>Santa Claus</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>A Visit from St. Nicholas</b></col> <cd>The original name
for a poem by <person>Clement Clarke Moore</person>, popularly called
titled <altname>The Night Before Christmans</altname>. It is a popular
poem with the theme of <person>St. Nicholas</person> (Santa Claus)
coming to bring gifts to children on Christmans eve. See <er>Night
Before Christmas</er> in the vocabulary.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintologist</ent><br/
<hw>Saint*ol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Saint</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets> + <ets>-ist</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>One who writes the lives of saints.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saintship</ent><br/
<hw>Saint"ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The character or qualities of a
saint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saint-Simonian</ent><br/
<hw>Saint`-Si*mo"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A follower
of the <person>Count de <etsep>St. Simon</etsep></person>, who died in
1825, and who maintained that the principle of property held in common,
and the just division of the fruits of common labor among the members of
society, are the true remedy for the social evils which exist.</def>
<rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saint-Simonianism</ent><br/
<hw>Saint`-Si*mo"ni*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
principles, doctrines, or practice of the Saint-Simonians; -- called
also <altname>Saint- Simonism</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saint-Simonism</ent><br/
<hw>Saint-Si"mon*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A system of
socialism in which the state owns all the property and the laborer is
entitled to share according to the quality and amount of his work,
founded by <etsep>Saint Simon</etsep> (1760-1825); -- called also
<altname>Saint- Simonianism</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saith</ent><br/
<hw>Saith</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>3d pers. sing. pres.</pos> of
<er>Say</er>.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saithe</ent><br/
<hw>Saithe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>saoidheam</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The pollock, or
coalfish; -- called also <altname>sillock</altname>.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saiva</ent><br/
<hw>Sai"va</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets><cced/aiva</ets> devoted to <ets>Siva</ets>.]</ety> <def>One of an
important religious sect in India which regards Siva with peculiar
veneration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saivism</ent><br/
<hw>Sai"vism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The worship of
Siva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sajene</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*jene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sagene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sajou</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"jou</hw> <pr>(?; F. <?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sapajou</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sake</ent><br/
<hw>Sake</hw> <pr>(s<amac/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sake</ets> cause, also, lawsuit, fault, AS. <ets>sacu</ets> strife,
a cause or suit at law; akin to D. <ets>zaak</ets> cause, thing, affair,
G. <ets>sache</ets> thing, cause in law, OHG. <ets>sahha</ets>, Icel.
<ets>s<oum/k</ets>, Sw. <ets>sak</ets>, Dan. <ets>sag</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sakj<omac/</ets> strife, AS. <ets>sacan</ets> to contend, strive,
Goth. <ets>sakam</ets>, Icel. <ets>saka</ets> to contend, strive, blame,
OHG. <ets>sahhan</ets>, MHG. <ets>sachen</ets>, to contend, strive,
defend one's right, accuse, charge in a lawsuit, and also to E.
<ets>seek</ets>. Cf. <er>Seek</er>.]</ety> <def>Final cause; end;
purpose of obtaining; cause; motive; reason; interest; concern; account;
regard or respect; -- used chiefly in such phrases as, <xex>for the sake
of</xex>, <xex>for his sake</xex>, <xex>for man's sake</xex>, <xex>for
mercy's sake</xex>, and the like; <as>as, to commit crime for the
<ex>sake</ex> of gain; to go abroad for the <ex>sake</ex> of one's
health</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moved with wrath and shame and ladies' <qex>sake</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will not again curse the ground any more for man's
<qex>sake</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. viii. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Will he draw out,<br/
For anger's <qex>sake</qex>, finite to infinite?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Knowledge is for the <qex>sake</qex> of man, and not man for the
<qex>sake</qex> of knowledge.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>-s</xex> of the possessive case preceding
<xex>sake</xex> is sometimes omitted for euphony; as, for goodness
<xex>sake</xex>. <ldquo/<xex>For conscience sake</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>1
Cor. x. 28.</au> The plural <xex>sakes</xex> is often used with a
possessive plural. <ldquo/For both our <xex>sakes</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Shak.</au></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sake</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ke</hw> <pr>(s<aum/"k<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
traditional alcoholic drink of Japan. It is made from rice.</def>
<altsp>[Also spelled <asp>saki</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saker</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ker</hw> <pr>(s<amac/"k<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sacre</ets> (cf. It. <ets>sagro</ets>, Sp. & Pg. <ets>sacre</ets>),
either fr. L. <ets>sacer</ets> sacred, holy, as a translation of Gr.
<grk>"ie`rax</grk> falcon, from <grk>"iero`s</grk> holy, or more
probably from Ar. <ets><cced/aqr</ets> hawk.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sacar</asp>, <asp>sacre</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A falcon (<spn>Falco sacer</spn>)
native of Southern Europe and Asia, closely resembling the
lanner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The female is called <stype>chargh</stype>, and the male
<stype>charghela</stype>, or <stype>sakeret</stype>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The peregrine falcon.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A small piece of artillery.</def>
<rj><au>Wilhelm.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On the bastions were planted culverins and <qex>sakers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The culverins and <qex>sakers</qex> showing their deadly muzzles
over the rampart.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sakeret</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ker*et</hw> <pr>(s<amac/"k<etil/r*<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>sacret</ets>. See <er>Saker</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The male of the saker <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saki</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ki</hw> <pr>(s<amac/"k<icr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. &
Pg. <ets>saki</ets>; probably from the native name.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of South American
monkeys of the genus <gen>Pithecia</gen>. They have large ears, and a
long hairy tail which is not prehensile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The black saki (<spn>Pithecia satanas</spn>), the
white-headed (<spn>Pithecia leucocephala</spn>), and the red-backed, or
hand-drinking, saki (<spn>Pithecia chiropotes</spn>), are among the
best-known.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saki</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ki</hw> <pr>(s<aum/"k<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
alcoholic drink of Japan. It is made from rice; it is usually spelled
<asp>sake</asp>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sakiyeh</ent><br/
<ent>Sakieh</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sak"i*eh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sak"i*yeh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>} <ety>[Ar. <ets>s<amac/q<imac/ah</ets> canal,
trench.]</ety> <def>A kind of water wheel used in Egypt for raising
water, from wells or pits, in buckets attached to its periphery or to an
endless rope.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sakti</ent><br/
<hw>Sak"ti</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.]</ety>
<fld>(Hind. Myth.)</fld> <def>The divine energy, personified as the wife
of a deity (Brahma, Vishnu, Siva, etc.); the female principle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sal</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal</hw> <pr>(s<add/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>s<amac/l</ets>, Skr. <ets><cced/<amac/la</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An East Indian timber tree (<spn>Shorea
robusta</spn>), much used for building purposes. It is of a light brown
color, close-grained, heavy, and durable.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>saul</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sal</ent><br/
<hw>Sal</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Salt</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem. & Pharm.)</fld> <def>Salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sal absinthii</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an impure potassium carbonate obtained from the ashes
of wormwood (<spn>Artemisia Absinthium</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Sal
acetosellae</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>salt
of sorrel.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal alembroth</b></col>. <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Alembroth</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal
ammoniac</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>ammonium chloride,
<chform>NH4Cl</chform>, a white crystalline volatile substance having a
sharp salty taste, obtained from gas works, from nitrogenous matter,
etc. It is largely employed as a source of ammonia, as a reagent, and as
an expectorant in bronchitis. So called because originally made from the
soot from camel's dung at the temple of Jupiter <etsep>Ammon</etsep> in
Africa. Called also <altname>muriate of ammonia</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sal catharticus</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Med.
Chem.)</fld>, <cd>Epsom salts.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal culinarius</b></col>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>common salt, or sodium
chloride.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal Cyrenaicus</b></col>. <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Sal ammoniac</cref> above.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Sal de duobus</b></col>, <col><b>Sal
duplicatum</b></col></mcol> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>potassium sulphate; -- so called because erroneously supposed to be
composed of two salts, one acid and one alkaline.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal
diureticus</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Med. Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>potassium acetate.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal enixum</b></col>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>acid potassium
sulphate.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal gemmae</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Old Min.)</fld>, <cd>common salt occuring native.</cd> --
<col><b>Sal Jovis</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>salt tin, or stannic chloride; -- the alchemical name of tin being
<etsep>Jove</etsep>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal Martis</b></col>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>green vitriol, or ferrous
sulphate; -- the alchemical name of iron being <etsep>Mars</etsep>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sal microcosmicum</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Microcosmic salt</cref>, under
<er>Microcosmic</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal plumbi</b></col>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sugar of lead.</cd> --
<col><b>Sal prunella</b></col>. <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Prunella salt</cref>, under 1st <er>Prunella</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sal Saturni</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>sugar of lead, or lead acetate; -- the alchemical name of lead being
<etsep>Saturn</etsep>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal sedativus</b></col>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sedative salt, or boric
acid.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal Seignette</b></col> <ety>[F.
<ets>seignette</ets>, <ets>sel de seignette</ets>]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>Rochelle salt.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal
soda</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sodium carbonate. See under
<er>Sodium</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sal vitrioli</b></col> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>white vitriol; zinc sulphate.</cd> --
<col><b>Sal volatile</b></col>. <ety>[NL.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Sal ammoniac</cref>, above.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Spirits of ammonia.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salaam</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*laam"</hw> <pr>(s<adot/*l<aum/m")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Same as <er>Salam</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Finally, Josiah might have made his <qex>salaam</qex> to the
exciseman just as he was folding up that letter.</q> <rj><qau>Prof.
Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salaam</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*laam"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make or perform a
salam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have <qex>salaamed</qex> and kowtowed to him.</q> <rj><qau>H.
James.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salability</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
condition of being salable; salableness.</def> <rj><au>Duke of
Argyll.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salable</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sale</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being sold; fit to be sold;
finding a ready market.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sal"a*ble*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos> -- <wf>Sal"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salacious</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*la"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salax</ets>, <ets>-acis</ets>, fond of leaping, lustful, fr.
<ets>salire</ets> to leap. See <er>Salient</er>.]</ety> <def>Having a
propensity to venery; lustful; lecherous.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sa*la"cious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sa*la"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salacity</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lac"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salacitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>salacit<eacute/</ets>]</ety>
<def>Strong propensity to venery; lust; lecherousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salad</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ad</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l"<ait/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salade</ets>, OIt. <ets>salata</ets>, It. <ets>insalata</ets>, fr.
<ets>salare</ets> to salt, fr. L. <ets>sal</ets> salt. See
<er>Salt</er>, and cf. <er>Slaw</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
preparation of vegetables, as lettuce, celery, water cress, onions,
etc., usually dressed with salt, vinegar, oil, and spice, and eaten for
giving a relish to other food; <as>as, lettuce <ex>salad</ex>; tomato
<ex>salad</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Leaves eaten raw are termed <qex>salad</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A dish composed of chopped meat or fish, esp.
chicken or lobster, mixed with lettuce or other vegetables, and seasoned
with oil, vinegar, mustard, and other condiments; <as>as, chicken
<ex>salad</ex>; lobster <ex>salad</ex></as>.</def><-- mention mayonnaise
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Salad burnet</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the common
burnet (<spn>Poterium Sanguisorba</spn>), sometimes eaten as a salad in
Italy.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salad days</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ad days`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>a period when
a person is young and inexperienced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salade</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A helmet. See
<er>Sallet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salading</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ad*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Vegetables for
salad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salaeratus</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`ae*ra"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saleratus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salagane</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"a*gane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the Chinese
name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The esculent swallow. See under
<er>Esculent</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salal-berry</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"al-ber`ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably of
American Indian origin.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The edible fruit
of the <spn>Gaultheria Shallon</spn>, an ericaceous shrub found from
California northwards. The berries are about the size of a common grape
and of a dark purple color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salam</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*lam</hw> <pr>(s<adot/*l<aum/m")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sal<amac/m</ets> peace, safety.]</ety> <def>A salutation or
compliment of ceremony in the east by word or act; an obeisance,
performed by bowing very low and placing the right palm on the
forehead.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>salaam</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salamander</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"a*man`der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salamandre</ets>, L. <ets>salamandra</ets>, Gr. <?/; cf. Per.
<ets>samander</ets>, <ets>samandel</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of Urodela,
belonging to <gen>Salamandra</gen>, <gen>Amblystoma</gen>,
<gen>Plethodon</gen>, and various allied genera, especially those that
are more or less terrestrial in their habits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The salamanders have, like lizards, an elongated body,
four feet, and a long tail, but are destitute of scales. They are true
Amphibia, related to the frogs. Formerly, it was a superstition that the
salamander could live in fire without harm, and even extinguish it by
the natural coldness of its body.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have maintained that <qex>salamander</qex> of yours with fire
any time this two and thirty years.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whereas it is commonly said that a <qex>salamander</qex>
extinguisheth fire, we have found by experience that on hot coals, it
dieth immediately.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The pouched gopher (<spn>Geomys
tuza</spn>) of the Southern United States.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A culinary utensil of metal with a plate or disk
which is heated, and held over pastry, etc., to brown it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A large poker.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>Solidified material in a furnace
hearth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Giant salamander</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Giant</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Salamander's hair</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>Salamander's wool</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a species of asbestos or mineral flax.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Bacon.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salamandrina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`a*man*dri"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu>pl.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A suborder of
Urodela, comprising salamanders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salamandrine</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`a*man"drine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining
to, or resembling, a salamander; enduring fire.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salamandroid</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`a*man"droid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Salamander</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Like or pertaining to the salamanders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salamandroidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`a*man*droi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Amphibia
including the Salamanders and allied groups; the Urodela.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salamstone</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"am*stone`</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A kind of blue sapphire brought from
Ceylon.</def> <rj><au>Dana.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salangana</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lan"ga*na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
salagane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salaried</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"a*ried</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Receiving a salary;
paid by a salary; having a salary attached; <as>as, a <ex>salaried</ex>
officer; a <ex>salaried</ex> office</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salary</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salarius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Saline</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salary</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Salaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>salaire</ets>, L.
<ets>salarium</ets>, originally, salt money, the money given to the
Roman soldiers for salt, which was a part of their pay, fr.
<ets>salarius</ets> belonging to salt, fr. <ets>sal</ets> salt. See
<er>Salt</er>.]</ety> <def>The recompense or consideration paid, or
stipulated to be paid, to a person at regular intervals for services;
fixed wages, as by the year, quarter, or month; stipend; hire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is hire and <qex>salary</qex>, not revenge.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Recompense for services paid at, or reckoned by, short
intervals, as a day or week, is usually called
<xex>wages</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Stipend; pay; wages; hire; allowance.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salary</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"a*ry</hw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Salaried</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Salarying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To pay, or agree
to pay, a salary to; to attach salary to; <as>as, to <ex>salary</ex> a
clerk; to <ex>salary</ex> a position</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sale</ent><br/
<hw>Sale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 1st
<er>Sallow</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sale</ent><br/
<hw>Sale</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>sala</ets>,
<ets>sal</ets>, akin to E. <ets>sell</ets>. See <er>Sell</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of selling; the transfer of
property, or a contract to transfer the ownership of property, from one
person to another for a valuable consideration, or for a price in
money.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Opportunity of selling; demand; market.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They shall have ready <qex>sale</qex> for them.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Public disposal to the highest bidder, or exposure
of goods in market; auction.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Temple.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bill of sale</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Bill</er>.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Of sale</b></col>, <col><b>On sale</b></col>,
<col><b>For sale</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to be bought or sold; offered to
purchasers; in the market.</cd> -- <col><b>To set to sale</b></col>,
<cd>to offer for sale; to put up for purchase; to make merchandise
of.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saleably</ent><br/
<ent>Saleable</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Sale"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>,
<hw>Sale"a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos>, etc.</mhw> <def>See
<er>Salable</er>, <er>Salably</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saleb</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"eb</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Salep</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salebrosity</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`e*bros"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Roughness or
ruggedness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salebrous</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"e*brous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salebrosus</ets>, fr. <ets>salebra</ets> a rugged road, fr.
<ets>salire</ets> to leap.]</ety> <def>Rough; rugged.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salep</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ep</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l"<ecr/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sahleb</ets>, perhaps a corruption of an Arabic word for fox, one
Ar. name of the orchis signifying literally, fox's testicles: cf. F.
<ets>salep</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>saleb</asp>,
<asp>salop</asp>, and <asp>saloop</asp>.]</altsp> <def>The dried tubers
of various species of <gen>Orchis</gen>, and <gen>Eulophia</gen>. It is
used to make a nutritious beverage by treating the powdered preparation
with hot water.</def> <rj><au>U. S. Disp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saleratus</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`e*ra"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>sal
a<eum/ratus</ets>; -- so called because it is a source of fixed air
(carbon dioxide). See <er>Sal</er>, and and <er>Aerated</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>Aerated salt; a white crystalline substance
having an alkaline taste and reaction, consisting of sodium bicarbonate
(see under <er>Sodium</er>.) It is largely used in cooking, with sour
milk (lactic acid) or cream of tartar as a substitute for yeast. It is
also an ingredient of most baking powders, and is used in the
preparation of effervescing drinks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salesman</ent><br/
<hw>Sales"man</hw> <pr>(s<amac/lz"m<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Salesmen</plw> <pr>(-m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[<ets>Sale</ets> + <ets>man</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who sells
anything; one whose occupation is to sell goods or
merchandise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sales tax</ent><br/
<hw>Sales" tax</hw> <pr>(s<amac/lz"t<acr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sales</ets> + <ets>tax</ets>.]</ety> <def>a tax imposed upon
the retail sale of goods or the sale of services, usually collected by
the seller at the time of purchase; -- it is typically calculated as a
percentage of the price of the object sold, being commonly from 3% to 7%
of the <er>base price</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saleswoman</ent><br/
<hw>Sales"wom`an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Saleswomen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A woman whose
occupation is to sell goods or merchandise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salework</ent><br/
<hw>Sale"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Work or things made
for sale; hence, work done carelessly or slightingly.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"lian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Denoting a tribe of
Franks who established themselves early in the fourth century on the
river Sala [now Yssel]; Salic.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
Salian Frank.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliant</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"li*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Salient</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliaunce</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*aunce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sally</er>.]</ety> <def>Salience; onslaught.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/So fierce <xex>saliaunce</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1270 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Salic</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ic</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salique</ets>, fr. the <ets>Salian</ets> Franks, who, in the fifth
century, formed a body of laws called in Latin <ets>leges
Salicae</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the Salian Franks, or to
the Salic law so called.</def> <altsp>[Also
<asp>salique</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Salic law</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A code of laws
formed by the Salian Franks in the fifth century. By one provision of
this code women were excluded from the inheritance of landed
property.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Specifically, in modern times, a law
supposed to be a special application of the above-mentioned provision,
in accordance with which males alone can inherit the throne. This law
has obtained in France, and at times in other countries of Europe, as
Spain.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`i*ca"ceous</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l`<icr/*k<amac/"sh<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>salix</ets>, <ets>-icis</ets>, the
willow.]</ety> <def>Belonging or relating to the willow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicin</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*cin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salix</ets>, <ets>-icis</ets>, a willow: cf. F.
<ets>salicine</ets>. See <er>Sallow</er> the tree.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A glucoside found in the bark and leaves of
several species of willow (<gen>Salix</gen>) and poplar, and extracted
as a bitter white crystalline substance.</def> <-- <altname>salicyl
alcohol glucoside</altname>, <altname>salicyl alcohol
<beta/-D-glucopyranoside</altname>, <altname>saligenin
<beta/-D-glucopyranoside</altname>, <chform>C13H18O7</chform>. It is
used in biochemistry as a standard substrate for evaluating the potency
of <beta/-glucosidase in enzymatic preparations. It is also an
analgesic. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicyl</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*cyl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Salic</ets>in
+ <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The hypothetical
radical of salicylic acid and of certain related compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicylal</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*cyl`al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Salicyl</ets>ic + <ets>al</ets>dehide.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A thin, fragrant, colorless oil,
<chform>HO.C6H4.CHO</chform>, found in the flowers of meadow sweet
(<gen>Spiraea</gen>), and also obtained by oxidation of salicin,
saligenin, etc. It reddens on exposure. Called also
<altname>salicylol</altname>, <altname>salicylic aldehyde</altname>, and
formerly <altname>salicylous acid</altname> <it>or</it>
<altname>spiroylous acid</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicylate</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*cyl`ate</hw> <pr>(-<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of salicylic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicylic</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`i*cyl"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, derived from, or designating, an acid formerly
obtained by fusing salicin with potassium hydroxide, and now made in
large quantities from phenol (carbolic acid) by the action of carbon
dioxide on heated sodium phenolate. It is a white crystalline substance.
It is used as an antiseptic, and in its salts in the treatment of
rheumatism. Called also <altname>hydroxybenzoic</altname>
<it>acid</it>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicylide</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*cyl`ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Salicyl</ets>ic + anhydr<ets>ide</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white crystalline substance obtained by
dehydration of salicylic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicylite</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*cyl`ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A compound of salicylal; -- named after the analogy of a
salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicylol</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*cyl`ol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Salicyl</ets>ic + L. <ets>ol</ets>eum oil.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Salicylal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salicylous</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lic"y*lous</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or designating, a substance
formerly called <xex>salicylous acid</xex>, and now
<xex>salicylal</xex>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salience</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"li*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Salient</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or condition of
being salient; a leaping; a springing forward; an assaulting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of projecting, or being
projected; projection; protrusion.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliency</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"li*en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
salient; hence, vigor.</def> <ldquo/A fatal lack of poetic
<xex>saliency</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. Morley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salient</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"li*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saliens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>salire</ets> to
leap; cf. F. <ets>saillant</ets>. See <er>Sally</er>, <pos>n.</pos> &
<pos>v. i.</pos>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Moving by leaps or springs;
leaping; bounding; jumping.</def> <ldquo/Frogs and <xex>salient</xex>
animals.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Shooting out or up; springing; projecting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had in himself a <qex>salient</qex>, living spring of generous
and manly action.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, figuratively, forcing itself on the
attention; prominent; conspicuous; noticeable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [Grenville] had neither <qex>salient</qex> traits, nor general
comprehensiveness of mind.</q> <rj><qau>Bancroft.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Math. & Fort.)</fld> <def>Projecting outwardly;
<as>as, a <ex>salient</ex> angle</as>; -- opposed to
<contr>reentering</contr>.</def><-- convex? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Represented in a leaping position;
<as>as, a lion <ex>salient</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Salient angle</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Salient</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, 4.</cd> -- <col><b>Salient polygon</b></col>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a polygon all of whose angles are salient.</cd>
-- <col><b>Salient polyhedron</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a
polyhedron all of whose solid angles are salient.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salient</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"li*ent</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A salient
angle or part; a projection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliently</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"li*ent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a salient
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sal</ets> salt + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Producing, or
impregnated with, salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saliferous rocks</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>the
New Red Sandstone system of some geologists; -- so called because, in
Europe, this formation contains beds of salt. The saliferous beds of New
York State belong largely to the Salina period of the Upper Silurian.
See the <xex>Chart</xex> of <er>Geology</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salifiable</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*fi`a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>salifiable</ets>. See <er>Salify</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Capable of neutralizing an acid to form a salt; -- said of bases;
thus, ammonia is <xex>salifiable</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salification</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>salification</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The act,
process, or result of salifying; the state of being salified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salify</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Salified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Salifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>salifier</ets>; from L. <ets>sal</ets> salt + <ets>-ficare</ets>
(only in comp.) to make. See <er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>To combine or impregnate with a salt.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>To form a salt with; to convert into a salt; <as>as,
to <ex>salify</ex> a base or an acid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saligenin</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lig"e*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sal</ets>icin + <ets>-gen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A phenol alcohol obtained, by the decomposition of salicin, as a
white crystalline substance; -- called also <altname>hydroxy-benzyl
alcohol</altname>.</def> <-- ortho-hydroxybenzyl alcohol, saligenol,
salicyl alcohol. <chform>HOCH2.C6H4.OH</chform> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saligot</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*got</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The water chestnut (<spn>Trapa
natans</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Sal*im"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sal</ets> salt + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for
measuring the amount of salt present in any given solution.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>salometer</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salimetry</ent><br/
<hw>Sal*im"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art or
process of measuring the amount of salt in a substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salina</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*li"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>salinae</ets>, pl., salt works, from <ets>sal</ets> salt. See
<er>Saline</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A salt marsh, or
salt pond, inclosed from the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Salt works.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salina period</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*li"na pe"ri*od</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[So called from
<ets>Salina</ets>, a town in New York.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>The period in which the American Upper Silurian system, containing
the brine-producing rocks of central New York, was formed. See the
<xex>Chart</xex> of <er>Geology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salination</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
washing with salt water.</def> <mark>[R. & Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Greenhill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saline</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"line</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salin</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sal</ets> salt: cf. L. <ets>salinae</ets>
salt works, <ets>salinum</ets> saltcellar. See <er>Salt</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of salt, or containing salt; <as>as,
<ex>saline</ex> particles; <ex>saline</ex> substances; a <ex>saline</ex>
cathartic</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of the quality of salt; salty; <as>as, a
<ex>saline</ex> taste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saline</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"line</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>saline</ets>. See <er>Saline</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>A salt
spring; a place where salt water is collected in the earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saline</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A crude potash obtained from beet-root residues
and other similar sources.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>salin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med. Chem.)</fld> <def>A metallic salt; esp., a
salt of potassium, sodium, lithium, or magnesium, used in
medicine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 3. (Med., Biochemistry) A saline solution, esp. normal saline, or
isotonic saline, used for infusion, to maintain blood pressure. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salineness</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*line"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being salt; saltness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`i*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Saline</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Saliferous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliniform</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lin"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the form
or the qualities of a salt, especially of common salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salinity</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lin"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Salineness.</def>
<rj><au>Carpenter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salinometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`i*nom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Saline</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
salimeter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salinous</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lin"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Saline.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salique</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ique</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Salic.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She fulmined out her scorn of laws <qex>salique</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliretin</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`i*re"tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sali</ets>genin + Gr. <?/ resin.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A yellow amorphous resinoid substance obtained by the action of
dilute acids on saligenin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salisburia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`is*bu"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Named
after R. A. <ets>Salisbury</ets>, an English botanist.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The ginkgo tree (<spn>Ginkgo biloba</spn>, or
<spn>Salisburia adiantifolia</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salite</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salitus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>salire</ets> to salt, fr.
<ets>sal</ets> salt.]</ety> <def>To season with salt; to salt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salite</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from
<ets>Sala</ets>, a town in Sweden.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
massive lamellar variety of pyroxene, of a dingy green color.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sahlite</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saliva</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*li"va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.; cf. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The secretion from the salivary
glands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In man the saliva is a more or less turbid and slighty
viscid fluid, generally of an alkaline reaction, and is secreted by the
parotid, submaxillary, and sublingual glands. In the mouth the saliva is
mixed with the secretion from the buccal glands. The secretions from the
individual salivary glands have their own special characteristics, and
these are not the same in all animals. In man and many animals
<xex>mixed saliva</xex>, i.e., saliva composed of the secretions of all
three of the salivary glands, is an important digestive fluid on account
of the presence of the peculiar enzyme, ptyalin.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salival</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*li"val</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Salivary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salivant</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*vant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salivans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>salivare</ets>. See
<er>Salivate</er>.]</ety> <def>Producing salivation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salivant</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*vant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which produces
salivation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salivary</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*va*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salivarius</ets> slimy, clammy: cf. F. <ets>salivaire</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to saliva; producing or
carrying saliva; <as>as, the <ex>salivary</ex> ferment; the
<ex>salivary</ex> glands; the <ex>salivary</ex> ducts,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salivate</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"i*vate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Salivated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Salivating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>salivatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>salivare</ets> to salivate. See
<er>Saliva</er>.]</ety> <def>To produce an abnormal flow of saliva in;
to produce salivation or ptyalism in, as by the use of
mercury.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To produce saliva, esp. in excess.</def> 2.
To drool. 3. (Fig.) To anticipate keenly with pleasure, as though
salivating at the expectation of a delicious meal. Used often with
<it>over.</it>; as, <ex>salivate over</ex> the prospects of high profits
from an enterprise.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note>Probably influenced by the experiments of Pavlov, who trained
dogs to salivate at the sound of a bell, by previously ringing the bell
immediately prior to feeding them.</note> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salivation</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`i*va"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salivatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>salivation</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The act or process of salivating; an
excessive secretion of saliva, often accompanied with soreness of the
mouth and gums; ptyalism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It may be induced by direct chemical or mechanical
stimulation, as in mastication of some tasteless substance like rubber,
or indirectly by some agent which affects the whole system, as mercury
compounds.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salivous</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*li"vous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salivosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>saliveux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining
to saliva; of the nature of saliva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salix</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"lix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Salices</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., the willow.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A genus of trees or shrubs including
the willow, osier, and the like, growing usually in wet grounds.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A tree or shrub of any kind of willow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sallenders</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"len*ders</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>solandres</ets>, <ets>solandre</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Far.)</fld>
<def>An eruption on the hind leg of a horse.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sellanders</asp>, and <asp>sellenders</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On the inside of the hock, or a little below it, as well as at the
bend of the knee, there is occasionally a scurfy eruption called
<ldquo/mallenders<rdquo/ in the fore leg, and
<ldquo/<qex>sallenders</qex><rdquo/ in the hind leg.</q>
<rj><qau>Youatt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sallet</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"let</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l"l<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salade</ets>, Sp. <ets>celada</ets>, or It. <ets>celata</ets>, fr.
L. (cassis) <ets>caelata</ets>, fr. <ets>caelare</ets>,
<ets>caelatum</ets>, to engrave in relief. So called from the figures
engraved upon it.]</ety> <def>A light kind of helmet, with or without a
visor, introduced during the 15th century.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>salade</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then he must have a <qex>sallet</qex> wherewith his head may be
saved.</q> <rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salleting</ent><br/
<ent>Sallet</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sal"let</hw>, <hw>Sal"let*ing</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Salad.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salliance</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"li*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Salience.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sallow</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"low</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l"l<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>salwe</ets>, AS. <ets>sealh</ets>; akin to OHG. <ets>salaha</ets>,
G. <ets>salweide</ets>, Icel. <ets>selja</ets>, L. <ets>salix</ets>, Ir.
<ets>sail</ets>, <ets>saileach</ets>, Gael. <ets>seileach</ets>, W.
<ets>helyg</ets>, Gr. <grk>"eli`kh</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
willow; willow twigs.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And bend the pliant <qex>sallow</qex> to a shield.</q>
<rj><qau>Fawkes.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sallow</qex> knows the basketmaker's thumb.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name given to certain species of
willow, especially those which do not have flexible shoots, as
<spn>Salix caprea</spn>, <spn>Salix cinerea</spn>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sallow thorn</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a European
thorny shrub (<spn>Hippophae rhamnoides</spn>) much like an Elaeagnus.
The yellow berries are sometimes used for making jelly, and the plant
affords a yellow dye.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sallow</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"low</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sallower</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sallowest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>salu</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>zaluw</ets>, OHG. <ets>salo</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<oum/lr</ets>
yellow.]</ety> <def>Having a yellowish color; of a pale, sickly color,
tinged with yellow; <as>as, a <ex>sallow</ex> skin</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sallow</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"low</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To tinge with sallowness.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>July breathes hot, <qex>sallows</qex> the crispy fields.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sallowish</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"low*ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat sallow.</def>
<rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sallowness</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"low*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
condition of being sallow.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sally</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ly</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l"l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sallied</conjf>
<pr>(-l<icr/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sallying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>saillir</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>salire</ets> to leap, spring, akin to Gr. <grk>"a`llesqai</grk>;
cf. Skr. <ets>s<rsdot/</ets> to go, to flow. Cf. <er>Salient</er>,
<er>Assail</er>, <er>Assault</er>, <er>Exult</er>, <er>Insult</er>,
<er>Saltation</er>, <er>Saltire</er>.]</ety> <def>To leap or rush out;
to burst forth; to issue suddenly; as a body of troops from a fortified
place to attack besiegers; to make a sally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They break the truce, and <qex>sally</qex> out by night.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The foe retires, -- she heads the <qex>sallying</qex> host.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sally</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ly</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sallies</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>saillie</ets>, fr. <ets>saillir</ets>.
See <ets>Sally</ets>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A leaping
forth; a darting; a spring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rushing or bursting forth; a quick issue; a sudden
eruption; specifically, an issuing of troops from a place besieged to
attack the besiegers; a sortie.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sallies</qex> were made by the Spaniards, but they were
beaten in with loss.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An excursion from the usual track; range;
digression; deviation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every one shall know a country better that makes often
<qex>sallies</qex> into it, and traverses it up and down, than he that .
. . goes still round in the same track.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A flight of fancy, liveliness, wit, or the like; a
flashing forth of a quick and active mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The unaffected mirth with which she enjoyed his
<qex>sallies</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Transgression of the limits of soberness or
steadiness; act of levity; wild gayety; frolic; escapade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The excursion was esteemed but a <qex>sally</qex> of youth.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sally port</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Fort.)</fld>
<cd>A postern gate, or a passage underground, from the inner to the
outer works, to afford free egress for troops in a sortie</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Naval)</fld> <cd>A large port on each quarter of a
fireship, for the escape of the men into boats when the train is fired;
a large port in an old-fashioned three-decker or a large modern
ironclad.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sally Lunn</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ly Lunn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[From a woman, <ets>Sally
Lunn</ets>, who is said to have first made the cakes, and sold them in
the streets of Bath, Eng.]</ety> <def>A tea cake slightly sweetened, and
raised with yeast, baked in the form of biscuits or in a thin loaf, and
eaten hot with butter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sallyman</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ly*man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The velella; -- called also <altname>saleeman</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salm</ent><br/
<hw>Salm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Psalm.</def>
<mark>[Obs2E]</mark> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salmagundi</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`ma*gun"di</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salmigondis</ets>, of uncertain origin; perhaps from L.
<ets>salgama condita</ets>, pl.; <ets>salgama</ets> pickles +
<ets>condita</ets> preserved (see <er>Condite</er>); or from the
Countess <ets>Salmagondi</ets>, lady of honor to Maria de Medici, who is
said to have invented it; or cf. It. <ets>salame</ets> salt meat, and F.
<ets>salmis</ets> a ragout.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mixture of chopped
meat and pickled herring, with oil, vinegar, pepper, and onions.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a mixture of various ingredients; an olio or
medley; a potpourri; a miscellany.</def> <rj><au>W.
Irving.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salmi</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"mi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Cookery)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Salmis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salmiac</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"mi*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>salmiac</ets>, G. <ets>salmiak</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>
<def>Sal ammoniac. See under <er>Sal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salmis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`mis"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>A ragout of partly roasted game stewed with
sauce, wine, bread, and condiments suited to provoke appetite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salmon</ent><br/
<hw>Salm"on</hw> <pr>(s<acr/m"<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Salmons</plw> <pr>(-<ucr/nz)</pr> <it>or</it>
(<it>collectively</it>) <plw>Salmon</plw>.</plu> <ety>[OE.
<ets>saumoun</ets>, <ets>salmon</ets>, F. <ets>saumon</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>salmo</ets>, <ets>salmonis</ets>, perhaps from <ets>salire</ets> to
leap. Cf. <er>Sally</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of fishes of the
genus <gen>Salmo</gen> and allied genera. The common salmon (<spn>Salmo
salar</spn>) of Northern Europe and Eastern North America, and the
California salmon, or quinnat, are the most important species. They are
extensively preserved for food. See <er>Quinnat</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- In the following paragraph I have taken the collocation section
out of the note section, left it in the same paragraph, and added the
definition tags. PCP --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The salmons ascend rivers and penetrate to their head
streams to spawn. They are remarkably strong fishes, and will even leap
over considerable falls which lie in the way of their progress. The
common salmon has been known to grow to the weight of seventy-five
pounds; more generally it is from fifteen to twenty-five pounds. Young
salmon are called <xex>parr</xex>, <xex>peal</xex>, <xex>smolt</xex>,
and <xex>grilse</xex>.</note> <cs>Among the true salmons are:
<mcol><col><b>Black salmon</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Lake
salmon</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the namaycush.</cd> -- <col><b>Dog
salmon</b></col>, <cd>a salmon of Western North America
(<spn>Oncorhynchus keta</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Humpbacked
salmon</b></col>, <cd>a Pacific-coast salmon (<spn>Oncorhynchus
gorbuscha</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>King salmon</b></col>, <cd>the
quinnat.</cd> -- <col><b>Landlocked salmon</b></col>, <cd>a variety of
the common salmon (var. <varn>Sebago</varn>), long confined in certain
lakes in consequence of obstructions that prevented it from returning to
the sea. This last is called also <altname>dwarf
salmon</altname>.</cd></cs> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note> Among fishes of other families which are locally and
erroneously called <ex>salmon</ex> are: the pike perch, called
<stype>jack salmon</stype>; the spotted, or southern, squeteague; the
cabrilla, called <stype>kelp salmon</stype>; young pollock, called
<stype>sea salmon</stype>; and the California yellowtail.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A reddish yellow or orange color, like the flesh of
the salmon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Salmon berry</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a large red
raspberry growing from Alaska to California, the fruit of the <spn>Rubus
Nutkanus</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salmon killer</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a stickleback (<spn>Gasterosteus
cataphractus</spn>) of Western North America and Northern Asia.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Salmon ladder</b></col>, <col><b>Salmon
stair</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See <cref>Fish ladder</cref>, under
<er>Fish</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salmon peel</b></col>, <cd>a young
salmon.</cd> -- <col><b>Salmon pipe</b></col>, <cd>a certain device for
catching salmon.</cd> <au>Crabb.</au> -- <col><b>Salmon trout</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The European sea trout (<spn>Salmo
trutta</spn>). It resembles the salmon, but is smaller, and has smaller
and more numerous scales.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The American
namaycush</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>A name that is also applied locally to
the adult black spotted trout (<spn>Salmo purpuratus</spn>), and to the
steel head and other large trout of the Pacific coast.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salmon</ent><br/
<hw>Salm"on</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a reddish yellow or orange
color, like that of the flesh of the salmon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salmonella</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`mo*nel"la</hw>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[After <person>Daniel
E. <etsep>Salmon</etsep></person>, a U. S. pathologist
(1850-1914).]</ety> <def>A genus of gram-negative bacteria that may be
motile or non-motile; they are typically rod-shaped and may be aerobic
or facultatively aerobic. They may be pathogenic for humans and other
animals. Their metabolism is fermentative, and they produce acid and
usually gas from glucose, but they do not metabolize lactose. The type
species is <spn>Salmonella cholerae-suis</spn>, which is found in pigs.
Other species, pathogenic in man, are <spn>Salmonella typhi</spn>
(<spn>Salmonella typhosa</spn>), <spn>Salmonella typhimurium</spn>, and
<spn>Salmonella schotmulleri</spn>, which cause <causes>typhoid
fever</causes>, <causes>food poisoning</causes>, and <causes>enteric
fever</causes>, respectively.</def> <au>Stedman.</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>salmonellosis</ent><br/
<hw>sal`mo*nel*lo"sis</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Infection with bacteria
of the genus <gen>Salmonella</gen>.</def> <au>Stedman.</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1271 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Salmonet</ent><br/
<hw>Salm"on*et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<ets>Samlet</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A salmon of small
size; a samlet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salmonoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"mon*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Salmon</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like, or pertaining
to, the <fam>Salmonidae</fam>, a family of fishes including the trout
and salmon.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>Any fish of the family
<fam>Salmonidae</fam>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salogen</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"o*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sal</ets>
salt + <ets>-gen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A halogen.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salol</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Salicylic</ets> +
<ets>-ol</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white crystalline
substance consisting of phenol salicylate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>salometer</ent><br/
<hw>sa*lom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Salimeter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salometry</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Salimetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa`lon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Saloon</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An apartment for the reception
of company; hence, in the plural, fashionable parties; circles of
fashionable society.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An apartment for the reception and exhibition of
works of art; hence, an annual exhibition of paintings, sculptures,
etc., held in Paris by the Society of French Artists; -- sometimes
called the <altname><col><b>Old Salon</b></col></altname>. <col><b>New
Salon</b></col> is a popular name for an annual exhibition of paintings,
sculptures, etc., held in Paris at the Champs de Mars, by the
Soci<eacute/t<eacute/ Nationale des Beaux-Arts (National Society of Fine
Arts), a body of artists who, in 1890, seceded from the
Soci<eacute/t<eacute/ des Artistes Fran<cced/ais (Society of French
Artists).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saloon</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*loon"</hw> <pr>(s<adot/*l<oomac/n")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salon</ets> (cf. It. <ets>salone</ets>), fr. F. <ets>salle</ets> a
large room, a hall, of German or Dutch origin; cf. OHG. <ets>sal</ets>
house, hall, G. <ets>saal</ets>; akin to AS. <ets>sael</ets>,
<ets>sele</ets>, D. <ets>zaal</ets>, Icel. <ets>salr</ets>, Goth.
<ets>saljan</ets> to dwell, and probably to L. <ets>solum</ets> ground.
Cf. <er>Sole</er> of the foot, <er>Soil</er> ground, earth.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A spacious and elegant apartment for the reception of
company or for works of art; a hall of reception, esp. a hall for public
entertainments or amusements; a large room or parlor; <as>as, the
<ex>saloon</ex> of a steamboat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The gilden <qex>saloons</qex> in which the first magnates of the
realm . . . gave banquets and balls.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Popularly, a public room for specific uses; esp., a
barroom or grogshop; <as>as, a drinking <ex>saloon</ex>; an eating
<ex>saloon</ex>; a dancing <ex>saloon</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We hear of no hells, or low music halls, or low dancing
<qex>saloons</qex> [at Athens.]</q> <rj><qau>J. P.
Mahaffy.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saloop</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*loop"</hw> <pr>(s<adot/*l<oomac/p")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
aromatic drink prepared from sassafras bark and other ingredients, at
one time much used in London.</def> <rj><au>J. Smith (Dict. Econ.
Plants).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saloop bush</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an
Australian shrub (<spn>Rhagodia hastata</spn>) of the Goosefoot family,
used for fodder.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salp</ent><br/
<hw>Salp</hw> <pr>(s<acr/lp)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of <gen>Salpa</gen>, or of the family
<fam>Salpidae</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salpa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal"pa</hw> <pr>(s<acr/l"p<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> L. <plw>Salpae</plw> <pr>(s<acr/l"p<emac/)</pr>, E.
<plw>Salpas</plw> <pr>(s<acr/l"p<adot/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.: cf. L.
<ets>salpa</ets> a kind of stockfish.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
genus of transparent, tubular, free-swimming oceanic tunicates found
abundantly in all the warmer latitudes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Each species exists in two distinct forms, one of which
lives solitary, and produces, by budding from an internal organ, a
series of the other kind. These are united together, side by side, so as
to form a chain, or cluster, often of large size. Each of the
individuals composing the chain carries a single egg, which develops
into the solitary kind.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salpid</ent><br/
<ent>Salpian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sal"pi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sal"pid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A salpa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salpicon</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"pi*con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salpicon</ets>, Sp. <ets>salpicon</ets>.]</ety> <def>Chopped meat,
bread, etc., used to stuff legs of veal or other joints; stuffing;
farce.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salpingitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`pin*gi"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Salpinx</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the salpinx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salpinx</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal"pinx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/, <?/, a trumpet.]</ety> <fld>(Old Anat.)</fld> <def>The Eustachian
tube, or the Fallopian tube.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salsafy</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"sa*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Salsify</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salsamentarious</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`sa*men*ta"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salsamentarius</ets>, fr. <ets>salsamentum</ets> brine, pickled
fish, fr. <ets>salsus</ets> salted, p. p. of <ets>salire</ets> to
salt.]</ety> <def>Salt; salted; saline.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salse</ent><br/
<hw>Salse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A mud
volcano, the water of which is often impregnated with salts, whence the
name.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salsify</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"si*fy</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salsifis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See <cref>Oyster
plant</cref> <sd>(a)</sd>, under <er>Oyster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salso-acid</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"so-ac`id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salsus</ets> salted, salt + <ets>acidus</ets> acid.]</ety>
<def>Having a taste compounded of saltness and acidity; both salt and
acid.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salsoda</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`so"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <cref>Sal
soda</cref>, under <er>Sal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salsola</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal"so*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>salsus</ets> salt, because they contain alkaline salts.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants including the glasswort. See
<er>Glasswort</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>salsuginous</ent><br/
<hw>sal*su"gi*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salsugo</ets>, <ets>-ginis</ets>, saltness, from <ets>salsus</ets>
salted, salt: cf. F. <ets>salsugineux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Growing in brackish places or in salt marshes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salt</ent><br/
<hw>Salt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sealt</ets>;
akin to OS. & OFries. <ets>salt</ets>, D. <ets>zout</ets>, G.
<ets>salz</ets>, Icel., Sw., & Dan. <ets>salt</ets>, L. <ets>sal</ets>,
Gr. <?/, Russ. <ets>sole</ets>, Ir. & Gael. <ets>salann</ets>, W.
<ets>halen</ets>, of unknown origin. Cf. <er>Sal</er>, <er>Salad</er>,
<er>Salary</er>, <er>Saline</er>, <er>Sauce</er>,
<er>Sausage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The chloride of sodium, a
substance used for seasoning food, for the preservation of meat, etc. It
is found native in the earth, and is also produced, by evaporation and
crystallization, from sea water and other water impregnated with saline
particles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, flavor; taste; savor; smack;
seasoning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though we are justices and doctors and churchmen . . . we have
some <qex>salt</qex> of our youth in us.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, also, piquancy; wit; sense; <as>as, Attic
<ex>salt</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A dish for salt at table; a saltcellar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I out and bought some things; among others, a dozen of silver
<qex>salts</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Pepys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A sailor; -- usually qualified by
<xex>old</xex>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Around the door are generally to be seen, laughing and gossiping,
clusters of old <qex>salts</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The neutral compound formed by
the union of an acid and a base; <as>thus, sulphuric acid and iron form
the <ex>salt</ex> sulphate of iron or green vitriol</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Except in case of ammonium salts, accurately speaking,
it is the acid radical which unites with the base or basic radical, with
the elimination of hydrogen, of water, or of analogous compounds as side
products. In the case of diacid and triacid bases, and of dibasic and
tribasic acids, the mutual neutralization may vary in degree, producing
respectively <xex>basic</xex>, <xex>neutral</xex>, or <xex>acid</xex>
salts. See Phrases below.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def><markp>Fig.</markp>: That which preserves from
corruption or error; that which purifies; a corrective; an antiseptic;
also, an allowance or deduction; <as>as, his statements must be taken
with a grain of <ex>salt</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye are the <qex>salt</qex> of the earth.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. v.
13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Any mineral salt used as an
aperient or cathartic, especially Epsom salts, Rochelle salt, or
Glauber's salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Marshes flooded by the tide.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><mcol><col><b>Above the salt</b></col>, <col><b>Below the
salt</b></col></mcol>, <cd>phrases which have survived the old custom,
in the houses of people of rank, of placing a large saltcellar near the
middle of a long table, the places above which were assigned to the
guests of distinction, and those below to dependents, inferiors, and
poor relations. See <er>Saltfoot</er>.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His fashion is not to take knowledge of him that is beneath him in
clothes. He never drinks <qex>below the salt</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Acid salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A
salt derived from an acid which has several replaceable hydrogen atoms
which are only partially exchanged for metallic atoms or basic radicals;
<as>as, acid potassium sulphate is an <ex>acid salt</ex></as>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A salt, whatever its constitution, which merely gives
an acid reaction; <as>thus, copper sulphate, which is composed of a
strong acid united with a weak base, is an <ex>acid salt</ex> in this
sense, though theoretically it is a neutral salt</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>Alkaline salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt which
gives an alkaline reaction, as sodium carbonate.</cd> -- <col><b>Amphid
salt</b></col> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt of the oxy type,
formerly regarded as composed of two oxides, an acid and a basic
oxide.</cd> <mark>[Obsolescent]</mark> -- <col><b>Basic salt</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A salt which contains more of the
basic constituent than is required to neutralize the acid</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An alkaline salt.</cd> -- <col><b>Binary salt</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt of the oxy type conveniently regarded as
composed of two ingredients (analogously to a haloid salt), viz., a
metal and an acid radical.</cd> -- <col><b>Double salt</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt regarded as formed by the union of two
distinct salts, as common alum, potassium aluminium sulphate. See under
<er>Double</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Epsom salts</b></col>. <cd>See in the
Vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Essential salt</b></col> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt obtained by crystallizing plant juices.</cd> --
<col><b>Ethereal salt</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Ethereal</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Glauber's salt</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>Glauber's salts</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See in
Vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Haloid salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>a simple salt of a halogen acid, as sodium chloride.</cd> --
<col><b>Microcosmic salt</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld>. <cd>See under
<er>Microcosmic</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Neutral salt</b></col>.
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A salt in which the acid and base
(in theory) neutralize each other</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A salt which
gives a neutral reaction.</cd> -- <col><b>Oxy salt</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt derived from an oxygen acid.</cd> --
<col><b>Per salt</b></col> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt supposed
to be derived from a peroxide base or analogous compound.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>Permanent salt</b></col>, <cd>a salt
which undergoes no change on exposure to the air.</cd> -- <col><b>Proto
salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt derived from a protoxide
base or analogous compound.</cd> -- <col><b>Rochelle salt</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Rochelle</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of amber</b></col>
<fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>succinic acid.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of
colcothar</b></col> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>green vitriol, or
sulphate of iron.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of hartshorn</b></col>. <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Sal ammoniac, or ammonium chloride</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Ammonium carbonate. Cf. <cref>Spirit of
hartshorn</cref>, under <er>Hartshorn</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of
lemons</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Salt of
sorrel</cref>, below.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of Saturn</b></col> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sugar of lead; lead acetate; -- the alchemical name of
lead being <xex>Saturn</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of
Seignette</b></col>. <cd>Same as <er>Rochelle salt</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Salt of soda</b></col> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sodium
carbonate.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of sorrel</b></col> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld>, <cd>acid potassium oxalate, or potassium quadroxalate,
used as a solvent for ink stains; -- so called because found in the
sorrel, or Oxalis. Also sometimes inaccurately called <altname>salt of
lemon</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of tartar</b></col> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld>, <cd>potassium carbonate; -- so called because formerly
made by heating cream of tartar, or potassium tartrate.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>Salt of Venus</b></col> <fld>(Old
Chem.)</fld>, <cd>blue vitriol; copper sulphate; -- the alchemical name
of copper being <xex>Venus</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of
wisdom</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Alembroth</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sedative
salt</b></col> <fld>(Old Med. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>boric acid.</cd> --
<col><b>Sesqui salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt derived
from a sesquioxide base or analogous compound.</cd> -- <col><b>Spirit of
salt</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Spirit</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sulpho salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt
analogous to an oxy salt, but containing sulphur in place of
oxygen.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Salt</ent><br/
<hw>Salt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Salter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Saltest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>sealt</ets>,
<ets>salt</ets>. See <er>Salt</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or relating to salt; abounding in, or containing, salt; prepared
or preserved with, or tasting of, salt; salted; <as>as, <ex>salt</ex>
beef; <ex>salt</ex> water</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Salt</xex>
tears.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Overflowed with, or growing in, salt water; <as>as,
a <ex>salt</ex> marsh; <ex>salt</ex> grass</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def><markp>Fig.</markp>: Bitter; sharp;
pungent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have a <qex>salt</qex> and sorry rheum offends me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def><markp>Fig.</markp>: Salacious; lecherous;
lustful.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salt</ent><br/
<-- Alphabetically, <col><b>Salt rising</b></col> should come after
<col><b>Salt raker</b></col> within <hw>Salt</hw> <pos>a.</pos>; not before.
PCP --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Salt acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>hydrochloric
acid.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt block</b></col>, <cd>an apparatus for evaporating
brine; a salt factory.</cd> <au>Knight.</au> -- <col><b>Salt bottom</b></col>,
<cd>a flat piece of ground covered with saline efflorescences.</cd>
<mark>[Western U.S.]</mark> <au>Bartlett.</au> -- <col><b>Salt cake</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>the white caked mass, consisting of sodium sulphate,
which is obtained as the product of the first stage in the manufacture of
soda, according to Leblanc's process.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt fish</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Salted fish, especially cod, haddock, and similar fishes
that have been salted and dried for food.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A marine
fish.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt garden</b></col>, <cd>an arrangement for the
natural evaporation of sea water for the production of salt, employing large
shallow basins excavated near the seashore.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt gauge</b>
</col>, <cd>an instrument used to test the strength of brine; a salimeter.
</cd> -- <col><b>Salt horse</b></col>, <cd>salted beef.</cd>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> -- <col><b>Salt junk</b></col>, <cd>hard salt beef for
use at sea.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark> -- <col><b>Salt lick</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Lick</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Salt marsh</b></col>,
<cd>grass land subject to the overflow of salt water.</cd> --
<col><b>Salt-marsh caterpillar</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an American
bombycid moth (<spn>Spilosoma acraea</spn> which is very destructive to the
salt-marsh grasses and to other crops. Called also <altname>woolly
bear</altname>. See Illust. under <er>Moth</er>, <er>Pupa</er>, and
<cref>Woolly bear</cref>, under <er>Woolly</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt-marsh
fleabane</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a strong-scented composite herb
(<spn>Pluchea camphorata</spn>) with rayless purplish heads, growing in salt
marshes.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt-marsh hen</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the clapper rail. See under <er>Rail</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt-marsh
terrapin</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the diamond-back.</cd> --
<col><b>Salt mine</b></col>, <cd>a mine where rock salt is obtained.</cd>
-- <col><b>Salt pan</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A large pan used for making
salt by evaporation; also, a shallow basin in the ground where salt water is
evaporated by the heat of the sun.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf>
<cd>Salt works.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt pit</b></col>, <cd>a pit where salt is
obtained or made.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt rising</b></col>, <cd>a kind of yeast
in which common salt is a principal ingredient.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> --
<col><b>Salt raker</b></col>, <cd>one who collects salt in natural salt
ponds, or inclosures from the sea.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt sedative</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>boracic acid.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<col><b>Salt spring</b></col>, <cd>a spring of salt water.</cd> --
<col><b>Salt tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small leguminous
tree (<spn>Halimodendron argenteum</spn>) growing in the salt plains of
the Caspian region and in Siberia.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt water</b></col>,
<cd>water impregnated with salt, as that of the ocean and of certain seas
and lakes; sometimes, also, tears.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mine eyes are full of tears, I can not see;<br/
And yet <qex>salt water</qex> blinds them not so much<br/
But they can see a sort of traitors here.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Salt-water sailor</b></col>, <cd>an ocean mariner.</cd> --
<col><b>Salt-water tailor</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Bluefish</er>.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Salt</ent><br/
<hw>Salt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Salted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Salting</conjf>.]
</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sprinkle, impregnate, or season with salt; to
preserve with salt or in brine; to supply with salt; <as>as, to <ex>salt</ex>
fish, beef, or pork; to <ex>salt</ex> cattle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fill with salt between the timbers and planks, as a
ship, for the preservation of the timber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To salt a mine</b></col>, <cd>to artfully deposit minerals in
a mine in order to deceive purchasers regarding its value.</cd>
<mark>[Cant]</mark> -- <mcol><col><b>To salt away</b></col>, <col><b>To salt
down</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to prepare with, or pack in, salt for preserving,
as meat, eggs, etc.; hence, colloquially, to save, lay up, or invest sagely,
as money.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salt</ent><br/
<hw>Salt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To deposit salt as a
saline solution; <as>as, the brine begins to <ex>salt</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salt</ent><br/
<hw>Salt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>saltus</ets>, fr.
<ets>salire</ets> to leap.]</ety> <def>The act of leaping or jumping;
a leap.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltant</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>saltans</ets>,
p. pr. of <ets>saltare</ets> to dance, v. intens. fr. <ets>salire</ets>
to leap: cf. F. <ets>sautant</ets>. See <er>Sally</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Leaping; jumping; dancing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>In a leaping position; springing
forward; -- applied especially to the squirrel, weasel, and rat, also
to the cat, greyhound, monkey, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltarella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`ta*rel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saltarello</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltarello</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`ta*rel"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., fr. L.
<ets>saltare</ets> to jump.]</ety> <def>A popular Italian dance in quick
3-4 or 6-8 time, running mostly in triplets, but with a hop step at the
beginning of each measure. See <er>Tarantella</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltate</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Saltant</er>.]</ety> <def>To leap or dance.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltation</ent><br/
<hw>Sal*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saltatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>saltation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A leaping or jumping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Continued his <qex>saltation</qex> without pause.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Beating or palpitation; <as>as, the <ex>saltation</ex>
of the great artery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>An abrupt and marked variation in
the condition or appearance of a species; a sudden modification which may
give rise to new races.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We greatly suspect that nature does make considerable jumps in the
way of variation now and then, and that these <qex>saltations</qex> give
rise to some of the gaps which appear to exist in the series of
known forms.</q> <rj><qau>Huxley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltatoria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`ta*to"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Orthoptera including
grasshoppers, locusts, and crickets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltatorial</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`ta*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Relating to leaping; saltatory; <as>as, <ex>saltatorial</ex>
exercises</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Same as
<er>Saltatorious</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Saltatoria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltatorious</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`ta*to"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
leaping; formed for leaping; saltatory; <as>as, a <ex>saltatorious</ex>
insect or leg</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ta*to"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saltatorius</ets>. See <er>Saltant</er>, and cf.
<er>Saltire</er>.]</ety> <def>Leaping or dancing; having the power of,
or used in, leaping or dancing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saltatory evolution</b></col> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>a
theory of evolution which holds that the transmutation of species is
not always gradual, but that there may come sudden and marked variations.
See <er>Saltation</er>.</cd><-- recently revived as "punctuated equilibrium"
--> -- <col><b>Saltatory spasm</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>an affection
in which pressure of the foot on a floor causes the patient to spring into
the air, so as to make repeated involuntary motions of hopping and jumping.
</cd> <rj><au>J. Ross.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltbush</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"bush`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An Australian plant (<spn>Atriplex nummularia</spn>) of the
Goosefoot family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltcat</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"cat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mixture of salt,
coarse meal, lime, etc., attractive to pigeons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltcellar</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"cel*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>saltsaler</ets>; <ets>salt</ets> + F. <ets>sali<egrave/re</ets>
saltcellar, from L. <ets>sal</ets> salt. See <er>Salt</er>, and cf.
<er>Salary</er>.]</ety> <def>Formerly a large vessel, now a small vessel
of glass or other material, used for holding salt on the
table.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salter</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes, sells,
or applies salt; one who salts meat or fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltern</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"ern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A building or place
where salt is made by boiling or by evaporation; salt works.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltfoot</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A large saltcellar
formerly placed near the center of the table. The superior guests were
seated above the saltfoot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salt-green</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"-green</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sea-green in
color.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltie</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"ie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
European dab.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltier</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"tier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saltire</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltigradae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal`ti*gra"dae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Saltigrade</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tribe of
spiders including those which lie in wait and leap upon their prey; the
leaping spiders; called also <altname>Salticidae</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltigrade</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ti*grade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saltus</ets> a leap + <ets>gradi</ets> to walk, go: cf. F.
<ets>saltigrade</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having feet or
legs formed for leaping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltigrade</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ti*grade</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
<fam>Saltigradae</fam>, a tribe of spiders which leap to seize their
prey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltimbanco</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`tim*ban"co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.,
literally, one who leaps or mounts upon a bench; <ets>saltare</ets> to
leap + <ets>in</ets> in, upon + <ets>banco</ets> a bench.]</ety> <def>A
mountebank; a quack.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>santinbanco</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Saltimbancos</qex>, quacksalvers, and charlatans.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>saltiness</ent><br/
<hw>salt"i*ness</hw> <pr>(s<add/lt"<esl/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of containing salt; salt taste;
<as>as, the <ex>saltiness</ex> of sea water</as>.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> saltiness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salting</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of sprinkling, impregnating, or furnishing, with salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A salt marsh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltire</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"tire</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sautoir</ets>, fr. LL. <ets>saltatorium</ets> a sort of stirrup,
fr. L. <ets>saltatorius</ets> saltatory. See <er>Saltatory</er>,
<er>Sally</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>A St.
Andrew's cross, or cross in the form of an <universbold>X</universbold>,
-- one of the honorable ordinaries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltirewise</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"tire*wise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>In the manner of a saltire; -- said especially of the blazoning of
a shield divided by two lines drawn in the direction of a bend and a
bend sinister, and crossing at the center.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltish</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat salt.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Salt"ish*ly</wf>, adv. -- <wf>Salt"ish*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltless</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of salt;
insipid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltly</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With taste of salt; in a salt
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltmouth</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"mouth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wide-mouthed
bottle with glass stopper for holding chemicals, especially crystallized
salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1272 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Saltness</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"ness</hw> <pr>(s<add/lt"n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being salt, or impregnated with salt; salt taste;
<as>as, the <ex>saltness</ex> of sea water</as>. In the sense of having
salt content, <altname>saltiness</altname> is more commonly
used.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> saltiness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltpetre</ent><br/
<ent>Saltpeter</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Salt`pe"ter</hw>, <hw>Salt`pe"tre</hw>, }</mhw>
<pr>(s<add/lt`p<emac/"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salp<ecir/tre</ets>, NL. <ets>sal petrae</ets>, literally, rock
salt, or stone salt; so called because it exudes from rocks or walls.
See <er>Salt</er>, and <er>Petrify</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Potassium nitrate; niter; a white crystalline substance,
<chform>KNO3</chform>, having a cooling saline taste, obtained by
leaching from certain soils in which it is produced by the process of
nitrification (see <er>Nitrification</er>, 2). It is a strong oxidizer,
is the chief constituent of gunpowder, and is also used as an antiseptic
in curing meat, and in medicine as a diuretic, diaphoretic, and
refrigerant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Chili salpeter</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sodium
nitrate (distinguished from potassium nitrate, or true salpeter), a
white crystalline substance, <chform>NaNO3</chform>, having a cooling,
saline, slightly bitter taste. It is obtained by leaching the soil of
the rainless districts of Chili and Peru. It is deliquescent and cannot
be used in gunpowder, but is employed in the production of nitric acid.
Called also <altname>cubic niter</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Saltpeter
acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>nitric acid; -- sometimes so
called because made from saltpeter.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltpetrous</ent><br/
<hw>Salt`pe"trous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>salp<ecir/treux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to saltpeter, or
partaking of its qualities; impregnated with saltpeter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salt rheum</ent><br/
<hw>Salt" rheum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A popular
name, esp. in the United States, for various cutaneous eruptions,
particularly for those of eczema. See <er>Eczema</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saltwort</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
name given to several plants which grow on the seashore, as the
<spn>Batis maritima</spn>, and the glasswort. See
<er>Glasswort</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Black saltwort</b></col>, <cd>the sea
milkwort.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salty</ent><br/
<hw>Salt"y</hw> <pr>(s<add/lt"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Containing salt; tasting of salt; saltish; <as>as, the
<ex>salty</ex> sea; the potatoes are too <ex>salty</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Interesting and witty; piquant; -- of
discourse.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Racy; sexually suggestive; -- of
discourse.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salubrious</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lu"bri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salubris</ets>, or <ets>saluber</ets>, fr. <ets>salus</ets> health;
akin to <ets>salvus</ets> safe, sound, well. See <er>Safe</er>.]</ety>
<def>Favorable to health; healthful; promoting health; <as>as,
<ex>salubrious</ex> air, water, or climate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Healthful; wholesome; healthy;
salutary.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sa-lu"bri*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sa*lu"bri*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salubrity</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lu"bri*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salubritas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>salubrit<eacute/</ets> See
<er>Salubrious</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality of being salubrious;
favorableness to the preservation of health; salubriousness;
wholesomeness; healthfulness; <as>as, the <ex>salubrity</ex> of the air,
of a country, or a climate</as>.</def> <ldquo/A sweet, dry smell of
<xex>salubrity</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>G. W. Cable.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salue</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lue"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>saluer</ets>. See <er>Salute</er>.]</ety> <def>To salute.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was no <ldquo/good day<rdquo/ and no <qex>saluyng</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salutary</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"u*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salutaris</ets>, from <ets>salus</ets>, <ets>-utis</ets>, health,
safety: cf. F. <ets>salutaire</ets>. See <er>Salubrious</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Wholesome; healthful; promoting health; <as>as,
<ex>salutary</ex> exercise</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Promotive of, or contributing to, some beneficial
purpose; beneficial; advantageous; <as>as, a <ex>salutary</ex>
design</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Wholesome; healthful; salubrious; beneficial;
useful; advantageous; profitable.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sal"u*ta*ri*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-- <wf>Sal"u*ta*ri*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salutation</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`u*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salutatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>salutation</ets>. See
<er>Salute</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of saluting, or paying respect or
reverence, by the customary words or actions; the act of greeting, or
expressing good will or courtesy; also, that which is uttered or done in
saluting or greeting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In all public meetings or private addresses, use those forms of
<qex>salutation</qex>, reverence, and decency usual amongst the most
sober persons.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Greeting; salute; address.</syn> --
<usage><er>Salutation</er>, <er>Greeting</er>, <er>Salute</er>.
<xex>Greeting</xex> is the general word for all manner of expressions of
recognition, agreeable or otherwise, made when persons meet or
communicate with each other. A <xex>greeting</xex> may be hearty and
loving, chilling and offensive, or merely formal, as in the opening
sentence of legal documents. <xex>Salutation</xex> more definitely
implies a wishing well, and is used of expressions at parting as well as
at meeting. It is used especially of uttered expressions of good will.
<xex>Salute</xex>, while formerly and sometimes still in the sense of
either <xex>greeting</xex> or <xex>salutation</xex>, is now used
specifically to denote a conventional demonstration not expressed in
words. The guests received a <xex>greeting</xex> which relieved their
embarrassment, offered their <xex>salutations</xex> in well-chosen
terms, and when they retired, as when they entered, made a deferential
<xex>salute</xex>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Woe unto you, Pharisees! for ye love the uppermost seats in the
synagogues, and <qex>greetings</qex> in the markets.</q> <rj><qau>Luke
xi. 43.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When Elisabeth heard the <qex>salutation</qex> of Mary, the babe
leaped in her womb.</q> <rj><qau>Luke i. 41.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I shall not trouble my reader with the first <qex>salutes</qex> of
our three friends.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salutatorian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lu`ta*to"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The student
who pronounces the salutatory oration at the annual Commencement or like
exercises of a college, -- an honor commonly assigned to that member of
the graduating class who ranks second in scholarship.</def>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salutatorily</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lu"ta*to*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By way of
salutation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salutatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lu"ta*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salutatorius</ets>. See <er>Salute</er>.]</ety> <def>Containing or
expressing salutations; speaking a welcome; greeting; -- applied
especially to the oration which introduces the exercises of the
Commencements, or similar public exhibitions, in American
colleges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salutatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lu"ta*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place for
saluting or greeting; a vestibule; a porch.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(American Colleges)</fld> <def>The salutatory
oration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salute</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Saluted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Saluting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>salutare</ets>,
<ets>salutatum</ets>, from <ets>salus</ets>, <ets>-utis</ets>, health,
safety. See <er>Salubrious</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To address, as
with expressions of kind wishes and courtesy; to greet; to
hail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>salute</qex> you with this kingly title.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to give a sign of good will; to compliment by
an act or ceremony, as a kiss, a bow, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have the prettiest tip of a finger . . . I must take the
freedom to <qex>salute</qex> it.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mil. & Naval)</fld> <def>To honor, as some day,
person, or nation, by a discharge of cannon or small arms, by dipping
colors, by cheers, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To promote the welfare and safety of; to benefit; to
gratify.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/If this <xex>salute</xex> my
blood a jot.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salute</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>salut</ets>. See <er>Salute</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of saluting, or expressing kind wishes or respect;
salutation; greeting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sign, token, or ceremony, expressing good will,
compliment, or respect, as a kiss, a bow, etc.</def>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mil. & Naval)</fld> <def>A token of respect or
honor for some distinguished or official personage, for a foreign vessel
or flag, or for some festival or event, as by presenting arms, by a
discharge of cannon, volleys of small arms, dipping the colors or the
topsails, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saluter</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*lut"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
salutes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salutiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`u*tif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salutifer</ets>; <ets>salus</ets>, <ets>-utis</ets>, health +
<ets>ferre</ets> to bring.]</ety> <def>Bringing health; healthy;
salutary; beneficial; <as>as, <ex>salutiferous</ex> air</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Innumerable powers, all of them <qex>salutiferous</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cudworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Healthful; healthy; salutary;
salubrious.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salutiferously</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`u*tif"er*ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Salutarily.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvability</ent><br/
<hw>Sal`va*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
condition of being salvable; salvableness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the Latin scheme of redemption, <qex>salvability</qex> was not
possible outside the communion of the visible organization.</q>
<rj><qau>A. V. G. Allen.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvable</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"va*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salvare</ets> to save, from <ets>salvus</ets> safe. Cf.
<er>Savable</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being saved; admitting of
salvation.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sal"va*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Sal"va*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvage</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"vage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>salvage</ets>, OF. <ets>salver</ets> to save, F. <ets>sauver</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>salvare</ets>. See <er>Save</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of saving a vessel, goods, or life, from perils of the
sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Salvage</qex> of life from a British ship, or a foreign ship
in British waters, ranks before <qex>salvage</qex> of goods.</q>
<rj><qau>Encyc. Brit.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Maritime Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
compensation allowed to persons who voluntarily assist in saving a ship
or her cargo from peril.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>That part of the
property that survives the peril and is saved.</def> <rj><au>Kent.
Abbot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvage</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"vage</hw>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>Savage.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvation</ent><br/
<hw>Sal*va"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>salvacioun</ets>, <ets>sauvacion</ets>, F. <ets>salvation</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>salvatio</ets>, fr. <ets>salvare</ets> to save. See
<er>Save</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of saving; preservation
or deliverance from destruction, danger, or great calamity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The redemption of man from the
bondage of sin and liability to eternal death, and the conferring on him
of everlasting happiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To earn <qex>salvation</qex> for the sons of men.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Godly sorrow worketh repentance to <qex>salvation</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>2. Cor. vii. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Saving power; that which saves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fear ye not; stand still, and see the <qex>salvation</qex> of the
Lord, which he will show to you to-day.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xiv.
13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Salvation Army</b></col>, <cd>an organization for
prosecuting the work of Christian evangelization, especially among the
degraded populations of cities. It is virtually a new sect founded in
London in 1861 by William Booth. The evangelists, male and female, have
military titles according to rank, that of the chief being
<ldquo/General.<rdquo/ They wear a uniform, and in their phraseology and
mode of work adopt a quasi military style.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvationist</ent><br/
<hw>Sal*va"tion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An evangelist, a member, or
a recruit, of the Salvation Army.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"va*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>salvatorium</ets>, fr. <ets>salvare</ets> to save.]</ety> <def>A
place where things are preserved; a repository.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salve</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal"ve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[L., hail, God
save you, imperat. of <ets>salvere</ets> to be well. Cf. <er>Salvo</er>
a volley.]</ety> <def>Hail!</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salve</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ve</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To say
<ldquo/<xex>Salve</xex><rdquo/ to; to greet; to salute.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By this that stranger knight in presence came,<br/
And goodly <qex>salved</qex> them.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salve</ent><br/
<hw>Salve</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sealf</ets> ointment; akin to LG. <ets>salwe</ets>, D.
<ets>zalve</ets>, <ets>zalf</ets>, OHG. <ets>salba</ets>, Dan.
<ets>salve</ets>, Sw. <ets>salfva</ets>, Goth. <ets>salb<omac/n</ets> to
anoint, and probably to Gr. (Hesychius) <?/ oil, <?/ butter, Skr.
<ets>sarpis</ets> clarified butter. <root/155, 291.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>An adhesive composition or substance to be applied to wounds or
sores; a healing ointment.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A soothing remedy or antidote.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Counsel or consolation we may bring.<br/
<qex>Salve</qex> to thy sores.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Salve bug</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large,
stout isopod crustacean (<spn>Aega psora</spn>), parasitic on the
halibut and codfish, -- used by fishermen in the preparation of a salve.
It becomes about two inches in length.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salve</ent><br/
<hw>Salve</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Salved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Salving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>sealfian</ets> to
anoint. See <er>Salve</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
heal by applications or medicaments; to cure by remedial treatment; to
apply salve to; <as>as, to <ex>salve</ex> a wound</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To heal; to remedy; to cure; to make good; to
soothe, as with an ointment, especially by some device, trick, or
quibble; to gloss over.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But Ebranck <qex>salved</qex> both their infamies<br/
With noble deeds.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What may we do, then, to <qex>salve</qex> this seeming
inconsistence?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- salve one's conscience. salve one's wounded pride --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salve</ent><br/
<hw>Salve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Salvage</er>]</ety> <def>To save, as a ship or goods, from the
perils of the sea.</def> <mark>[Recent]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salver</ent><br/
<hw>Salv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who salves, or
uses salve as a remedy; hence, a quacksalver, or quack.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salver</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Salvage</er>.]</ety> <def>A salvor.</def>
<rj><au>Skeat.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salver</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>salva</ets>
pregustation, the tasting of viands before they are served, salver, fr.
<ets>salvar</ets> to save, to taste, to prove the food or drink of
nobles, from L. <ets>salvare</ets> to save. See <er>Save</er>.]</ety>
<def>A tray or waiter on which anything is presented.</def> <-- now used
mostly in compounds; e.g. tea salver --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salver-shaped</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"ver-shaped`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Tubular, with a spreading border. See
<er>Hypocraterimorphous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sal"vi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., sage.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants including the sage. See
<er>Sage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvific</ent><br/
<hw>Sal*vif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>salficus</ets> saving; <ets>salvus</ets> saved, safe +
<ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety> <def>Tending to save or secure
safety.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvo</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"vo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Salvos</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>salvo jure</ets>,
literally, the right being reserved. See <er>Safe.</er>]</ety> <def>An
exception; a reservation; an excuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They admit many <qex>salvos</qex>, cautions, and reservations.</q>
<rj><qau>Eikon Basilike.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvo</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"vo</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>salve</ets> a discharge of
heavy cannon, a volley, L. <ets>salve</ets> hail, imperat. of
<ets>salvere</ets> to be well, akin to <ets>salvus</ets> well. See
<er>Safe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A concentrated
fire from pieces of artillery, as in endeavoring to make a break in a
fortification; a volley.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A salute paid by a simultaneous, or nearly
simultaneous, firing of a number of cannon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Salvor</ent><br/
<hw>Sal"vor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Salvation</er>, <er>Save</er>]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who
assists in saving a ship or goods at sea, without being under special
obligation to do so.</def> <rj><au>Wheaton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sam</ent><br/
<hw>Sam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>same</ets>.
See <er>Same</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Together.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/All in that city <xex>sam</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>SAM</ent><br/
<hw>SAM</hw> <pr>(s<acr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[acronym]</ety>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>a <er>Surface to Air Missile</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samaj</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*maj"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>sam<amac/j</ets> meeting, assembly, fr. Skr. <ets>sam<amac/ja</ets>
a community.]</ety> <def>A society or congregation; a church or
religious body.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samara</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*ma"ra</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>samara</ets>, <ets>samera</ets>, the seed of the elm.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A dry, indehiscent, usually one-seeded, winged
fruit, as that of the ash, maple, and elm; a key or key fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samare</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"are</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Simar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samaritan</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*mar"i*tan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Samaritanus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Samaria, in
Palestine.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Samaria; also, the language of Samaria.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samarium</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*ma"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. E.
<ets>samar</ets>skite.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A rare metallic
element of doubtful identity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Samarium was discovered, by means of spectrum analysis,
in certain minerals (<xex>samarskite</xex>, <xex>cerite</xex>, etc.), in
which it is associated with other elements of the earthy group. It has
been confounded with the doubtful elements <xex>decipium</xex>,
<xex>philippium</xex>, etc., and is possibly a complex mixture of
elements not as yet clearly identified. Symbol Sm. Provisional atomic
weight 150.2.</note> <-- a true element. Symb. Sa or Sm; At. No. 62; At.
wt. 150.43. Valence 2 or 3. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samaroid</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"a*roid</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Samara</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Resembling a samara, or winged seed vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samarra</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*mar"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Simar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samarskite</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*mar"skite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[After
<ets>Samarski</ets>, a Russian.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A rare
mineral having a velvet-black color and submetallic luster. It is a
niobate of uranium, iron, and the yttrium and cerium metals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sambo</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"bo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>zambo</ets>
bandy-legged, the child of a negro and an Indian; prob. of African
origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A negro; sometimes, the offspring of a
black person and a mulatto; -- formerly used colloquially or with
humorous intent, but now considered offensive or racist by
African-Americans.</def> <mark>[Derogatory and offensive]</mark> <note>A
children's book named <it>Little Black Sambo</it> was at one time
popular in elementary schools, but the book has been removed from
reading lists in many American schools due to the development of a
reduced acceptance of patronizing depictions of negros, as well as the
negative associations of the word itself.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In Central America, an Indian and negro half-breed,
or mixed blood.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sambo</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"bo</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>zambo</ets>,
<ets>sambo</ets>.]</ety> <def>A colloquial or humorous appellation for a
negro; sometimes, the offspring of a black person and a mulatto; a
zambo.</def> <-- deprecatory and impolite --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samboo</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"boo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sambur</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sambucus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sam*bu"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., an elder
tree.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of shrubs and trees; the
elder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sambuke</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"buke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sambuca</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An ancient
stringed instrument used by the Greeks, the particular construction of
which is unknown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sambur</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"bur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>s<amac/mbar</ets>, <ets>s<amac/bar</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An East Indian deer (<spn>Rusa Aristotelis</spn>) having a mane on
its neck. Its antlers have but three prongs. Called also
<altname>gerow</altname>. The name is applied to other species of the
genus <gen>Rusa</gen>, as the Bornean sambur (<spn>Rusa
equina</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Same</ent><br/
<hw>Same</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>same</ets>,
adv.; akin to OS. <ets>sama</ets>, <ets>samo</ets>, adv., OHG.
<ets>sam</ets>, a., <ets>sama</ets>, adv., Icel. <ets>samr</ets>, a.,
Sw. <ets>samme</ets>, <ets>samma</ets>, Dan. <ets>samme</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sama</ets>, Russ. <ets>samuii</ets>, Gr. <?/, Skr. <ets>sama</ets>,
Gr. <?/ like, L. <ets>simul</ets> at the same time, <ets>similis</ets>
like, and E. <ets>some</ets>, a., <ets>-some</ets>. <root/191. Cf.
<er>Anomalous</er>, <er>Assemble</er>, <er>Homeopathy</er>,
<er>Homily</er>, <er>Seem</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, <er>Semi-</er>,
<er>Similar</er>, <er>Some</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not different
or other; not another or others; identical; unchanged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou art the <qex>same</qex>, and thy years shall have no end.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. cii. 27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of like kind, species, sort, dimensions, or the
like; not differing in character or in the quality or qualities
compared; corresponding; not discordant; similar; like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ethereal vigor is in all the <qex>same</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Just mentioned, or just about to be
mentioned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What ye know, the <qex>same</qex> do I know.</q> <rj><qau>Job.
xiii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do but think how well the <qex>same</qex> he spends,<br/
Who spends his blood his country to relieve.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Same</xex> is commonly preceded by <xex>the</xex>,
<xex>this</xex>, or <xex>that</xex> and is often used substantively as
in the citations above. In a comparative use it is followed by
<xex>as</xex> or <xex>with</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bees like the <qex>same</qex> odors as we do.</q>
<rj><qau>Lubbock.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] held the <qex>same</qex> political opinions with his
illustrious friend.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sameliness</ent><br/
<hw>Same"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sameness,
2.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bayne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sameness</ent><br/
<hw>Same"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of being
the same; identity; absence of difference; near resemblance;
correspondence; similarity; <as>as, a <ex>sameness</ex> of person, of
manner, of sound, of appearance, and the like</as>.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>sameness</xex> of the terms.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp.
Horsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, want of variety; tedious monotony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Identity; identicalness; oneness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samette</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*mette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Samite</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"mi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Samius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the island of
Samos.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fill high the cup with <qex>Samian</qex> wine.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Samian earth</b></col>, <cd>a species of clay from Samos,
formerly used in medicine as an astringent.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"mi*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Samos.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samiel</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"mi*el</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Turk.
<ets>sam-yeli</ets>; Ar. <ets>samm</ets> poison + Turk. <ets>yel</ets>
wind. Cf. <er>Simoom</er>.]</ety> <def>A hot and destructive wind that
sometimes blows, in Turkey, from the desert. It is identical with the
<altname>simoom</altname> of Arabia and the <altname>kamsin</altname> of
Syria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samiot</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"mi*ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>samiote</ets>.]</ety> <def>Samian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samisen</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sam"i*sen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Jap.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A Japanese musical instrument with three strings,
resembling a guitar or banjo.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1273 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Samite</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"mite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>samit</ets>,
LL. <ets>samitum</ets>, <ets>examitum</ets>, from LGr. <?/, <?/ woven
with six threads; Gr. <?/ six + <?/ a thread. See <er>Six</er>, and cf.
<er>Dimity</er>.]</ety> <def>A species of silk stuff, or taffeta,
generally interwoven with gold.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In silken <qex>samite</qex> she was light arrayed.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samlet</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Salmonet</er>.]</ety> <def>The parr.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sammier</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"mi*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A machine for
pressing the water from skins in tanning.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samoan</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*mo"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
the Samoan Islands (formerly called Navigators' Islands) in the South
Pacific Ocean, or their inhabitants.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>An inhabitant of the Samoan Islands.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samovar</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"mo*var</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>samovar'</ets>.]</ety> <def>A metal urn used in Russia for making
tea. It is filled with water, which is heated by charcoal placed in a
pipe, with chimney attached, which passes through the urn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Samoyed. 1. A breed of medium-sized sled dogs, originating in
Siberia, of white or cream color. 2. A Uralic language spoken by the
Samoyed people. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samoyedes</ent><br/
<hw>Sam`oy*edes"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Samoyede</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>An ignorant and degraded Turanian tribe which occupies a portion of
Northern Russia and a part of Siberia.</def><-- also
<altname>Samoyeds.</altname> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samp</ent><br/
<hw>Samp</hw> <pr>(s<acr/mp)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Massachusetts
Indian <ets>nas<agrave/ump</ets> unparched meal porridge.]</ety> <def>An
article of food consisting of maize broken or bruised, which is cooked
by boiling, and usually eaten with milk; coarse hominy.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sampan</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sam"pan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A Chinese boat from twelve to fifteen feet long, covered with a
house, and sometimes used as a permanent habitation on the inland
waters.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sanpan</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samphire</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"phire</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
l'herbe de <ets>Saint Pierre</ets>. See <er>Saint</er>, and
<er>Petrel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A fleshy,
suffrutescent, umbelliferous European plant (<spn>Crithmum
maritimum</spn>). It grows among rocks and on cliffs along the seacoast,
and is used for pickles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hangs one that gathers <qex>samphire</qex>, dreadful trade!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The species of glasswort (<spn>Salicornia
herbacea</spn>); -- called in England <altname>marsh
samphire</altname>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A seashore shrub
(<spn>Borrichia arborescens</spn>) of the West Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Golden samphire</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Golden</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sample</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sample</ets>, <ets>asaumple</ets>, OF. <ets>essample</ets>,
<ets>example</ets>, fr. L. <ets>exemplum</ets>. See <er>Example</er>,
and cf. <er>Ensample</er>, <er>Sampler</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Example; pattern.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Spenser.</au>
<ldquo/A <xex>sample</xex> to the youngest.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus he concludes, and every hardy knight<br/
His <qex>sample</qex> followed.</q> <rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A part of anything presented for inspection, or
shown as evidence of the quality of the whole; a specimen; <as>as, goods
are often purchased by <ex>samples</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I design this but for a <qex>sample</qex> of what I hope more
fully to discuss.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Specimen; example. See
<er>Specimen</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sample</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"ple</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make or show
something similar to; to match.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take or to test a sample or samples of; <as>as,
to <ex>sample</ex> sugar, teas, wools, cloths</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sampler</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"pler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Exampler</er>, <er>Exemplar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
makes up samples for inspection; one who examines samples, or by
samples; <as>as, a wool <ex>sampler</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pattern; a specimen; especially, a collection of
needlework patterns, as letters, borders, etc., to be used as samples,
or to display the skill of the worker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Susie dear, bring your <qex>sampler</qex> and Mrs. Schumann will
show you how to make that W you bothered over.</q> <rj><qau>E. E.
Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samshu</ent><br/
<ent>Samshoo</ent><br/
<mhw>\'d8<hw>Sam"shoo</hw>, <hw>Sam"shu</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Chinese <ets>san-shao</ets> thrice fired.]</ety>
<def>A spirituous liquor distilled by the Chinese from the yeasty liquor
in which boiled rice has fermented under pressure.</def> <rj><au>S. W.
Williams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samson</ent><br/
<hw>Sam"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An Israelite of Bible
record (see <au>Judges xiii.</au>), distinguished for his great
strength; hence, a man of extraordinary physical strength.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Samson post</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<cd>A strong post resting on the keelson, and supporting a beam of the
deck; also, a temporary or movable pillar carrying a leading block or
pulley for various purposes.</cd> <au>Brande & C.</au> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>In deep-well boring, the post which supports the walking beam of the
apparatus.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samurai</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"mu*rai`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl. & sing.</pos>
<ety>[Jap.]</ety> <def>In the former feudal system of Japan, the class
or a member of the class, of military retainers of the daimios,
constituting the gentry or lesser nobility. They possessed power of life
and death over the commoners, and wore two swords as their
distinguishing mark. Their special rights and privileges were abolished
with the fall of feudalism in 1871. They were referred to as <ldquo/a
cross between a knight and a gentleman<rdquo/.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanability</ent><br/
<hw>San`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sanable; sanableness; curableness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanable</ent><br/
<hw>San"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>sanare</ets> to heal, fr.
<ets>sanus</ets> sound, healthy. See <er>Sane</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable
of being healed or cured; susceptible of remedy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Remediable; curable; healable.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanableness</ent><br/
<hw>San"a*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
sanable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanation</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanatio</ets>. See <er>Sanable</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of healing
or curing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanative</ent><br/
<hw>San"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sanativus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the power to cure or heal;
healing; tending to heal; sanatory.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>San"a*tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanatorium</ent><br/
<hw>San`a*to"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sanatory</er>.]</ety> <def>An establishment for the treatment of the
sick; a resort for invalids. See <er>Sanitarium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanatory</ent><br/
<hw>San"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sanatorius</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sanare</ets> to heal. See
<er>Sanable</er>.]</ety> <def>Conducive to health; tending to cure;
healing; curative; sanative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sanatory</qex> ordinances for the protection of public
health, such as quarantine, fever hospitals, draining, etc.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sanatory</xex> and <xex>sanitary</xex> should not
be confounded. <xex>Sanatory</xex> signifies <xex>conducive to
health</xex>, while <xex>sanitary</xex> has the more general meaning of
<xex>pertaining to health</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanbenito</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>San`be*ni"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. & Pg.
<ets>sambenito</ets>, contr. from L. <ets>saccus</ets> sack +
<ets>benedictus</ets> blessed.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anciently, a
sackcloth coat worn by penitents on being reconciled to the
church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A garnment or cap, or sometimes both, painted with
flames, figures, etc., and worn by persons who had been examined by the
Inquisition and were brought forth for punishment at the
auto-da-f<eacute/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sancte bell</ent><br/
<ent>Sance-bell</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sance"-bell`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sanc"te bell`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <cref>Sanctus bell</cref>,
under <er>Sanctus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sancho</ent><br/
<hw>San"cho</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., a proper
name.]</ety> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>The nine of trumps in sancho
pedro.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sancho pedro</ent><br/
<hw>Sancho pedro</hw>. <ety>[Sp. <ets>Pedro</ets> Peter.]</ety>
<fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>A variety of auction pitch in which the
nine (<xex>sancho</xex>) and five (<xex>pedro</xex>) of trumps are added
as counting cards at their pip value, and the ten of trumps counts
game.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctificate</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"ti*fi*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanctificatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sanctificare</ets>.]</ety>
<def>To sanctify.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctification</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc`ti*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanctificatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sanctification</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sanctifying or making holy; the state of
being sanctified or made holy;</def> <specif>esp.</specif>
<fld>(Theol.)</fld>, <def>the act of God's grace by which the affections
of men are purified, or alienated from sin and the world, and exalted to
a supreme love to God; also, the state of being thus purified or
sanctified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God hath from the beginning chosen you to salvation through
<qex>sanctification</qex> of the Spirit and belief of the truth.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Thess. ii. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of consecrating, or of setting apart for a
sacred purpose; consecration.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctified</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"ti*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made holy; also,
made to have the air of sanctity; sanctimonious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctifier</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"ti*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sanctifies, or makes holy; specifically, the Holy Spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctify</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"ti*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sanctified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sanctifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>sanctifier</ets>, L. <ets>sanctificare</ets>; <ets>sanctus</ets>
holy + <ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.) to make. See <er>Saint</er>, and
<er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make sacred or holy; to set
apart to a holy or religious use; to consecrate by appropriate rites; to
hallow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God blessed the seventh day and <qex>sanctified</qex> it.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. ii. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moses . . . <qex>sanctified</qex> Aaron and his garments.</q>
<rj><qau>Lev. viii. 30.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make free from sin; to cleanse from moral
corruption and pollution; to purify.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sanctify</qex> them through thy truth.</q> <rj><qau>John
xvii. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make efficient as the means of holiness; to
render productive of holiness or piety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A means which his mercy hath <qex>sanctified</qex> so to me as to
make me repent of that unjust act.</q> <rj><qau>Eikon
Basilike.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To impart or impute sacredness, venerableness,
inviolability, title to reverence and respect, or the like, to; to
secure from violation; to give sanction to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The holy man, amazed at what he saw,<br/
Made haste to <qex>sanctify</qex> the bliss by law.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Truth guards the poet, <qex>sanctifies</qex> the line.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctifyingly</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"ti*fy`ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
manner or degree tending to sanctify or make holy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctiloquent</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc*til"o*quent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanctus</ets> holy + <ets>loquens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>loqui</ets>
to speak.]</ety> <def>Discoursing on heavenly or holy things, or in a
holy manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctimonial</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc`ti*mo"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>sanctimonialis</ets>. ]</ety> <def>Sanctimonious.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctimonious</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc`ti*mo"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sanctimony</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Possessing sanctimony;
holy; sacred; saintly.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Making a show of sanctity; affecting saintliness;
hypocritically devout or pious.</def> <ldquo/Like the
<xex>sanctimonious</xex> pirate.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sanc`ti*mo"ni*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sanc`ti*mo"ni*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctimony</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"ti*mo*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanctimonia</ets>, fr. <ets>sanctus</ets> holy: cf. OF.
<ets>sanctimonie</ets>. See <er>Saint</er>.]</ety> <def>Holiness;
devoutness; scrupulous austerity; sanctity; especially, outward or
artificial saintliness; assumed or pretended holiness; hypocritical
devoutness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her pretense is a pilgrimage; . . . which holy undertaking with
most austere <qex>sanctimony</qex> she accomplished.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanction</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanctio</ets>, from <ets>sancire</ets>, <ets>sanctum</ets> to
render sacred or inviolable, to fix unalterably: cf. F.
<ets>sanction</ets>. See <er>Saint</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Solemn
or ceremonious ratification; an official act of a superior by which he
ratifies and gives validity to the act of some other person or body;
establishment or furtherance of anything by giving authority to it;
confirmation; approbation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The strictest professors of reason have added the
<qex>sanction</qex> of their testimony.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything done or said to enforce the will, law, or
authority of another; <as>as, legal <ex>sanctions</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Ratification; authorization; authority;
countenance; support.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanction</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"tion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sanctioned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sanctioning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To give sanction to; to
ratify; to confirm; to approve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Would have counseled, or even <qex>sanctioned</qex>, such perilous
experiments.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To ratify; confirm; authorize;
countenance.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctionary</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"tion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining
to, or giving, sanction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctitude</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"ti*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanctitudo</ets>.]</ety> <def>Holiness; sacredness; sanctity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctity</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"ti*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sanctities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>sanctitas</ets>,
from <ets>sanctus</ets> holy. See <er>Saint</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The state or quality of being sacred or holy; holiness;
saintliness; moral purity; godliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>sanctity</qex> she made no pretense, and, indeed, narrowly
escaped the imputation of irreligion.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sacredness; solemnity; inviolability; religious
binding force; <as>as, the <ex>sanctity</ex> of an oath</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A saint or holy being.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>About him all the <qex>sanctities</qex> of heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Holiness; godliness; piety; devotion; goodness;
purity; religiousness; sacredness; solemnity. See the Note under
<er>Religion</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctuarize</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"tu*a*rize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To shelter
by means of a sanctuary or sacred privileges.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctuary</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"tu*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sanctuaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE.
<ets>seintuarie</ets>, OF. <ets>saintuaire</ets>, F.
<ets>sanctuaire</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sanctuarium</ets>, from
<ets>sanctus</ets> sacred, holy. See <er>Saint</er>.]</ety> <def>A
sacred place; a consecrated spot; a holy and inviolable site.</def>
Hence, specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The most retired part of the
temple at Jerusalem, called the <xex>Holy of Holies</xex>, in which was
kept the ark of the covenant, and into which no person was permitted to
enter except the high priest, and he only once a year, to intercede for
the people; also, the most sacred part of the tabernacle; also, the
temple at Jerusalem.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The most
sacred part of any religious building, esp. that part of a Christian
church in which the altar is placed.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A house
consecrated to the worship of God; a place where divine service is
performed; a church, temple, or other place of worship.</def>
<sd>(d)</sd> <def>A sacred and inviolable asylum; a place of refuge and
protection; shelter; refuge; protection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These laws, whoever made them, bestowed on temples the privilege
of <qex>sanctuary.</qex></q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The admirable works of painting were made fuel for the fire; but
some relics of it took <qex>sanctuary</qex> under ground, and escaped
the common destiny.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- <col><b>Wildlife sanctuary</b></col>, <cd>a tract of land set
aside by law for the preservation of wildlife, in which no hunting is
permitted.</cd> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctum</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., p. p. of
<ets>sancire</ets> to consecrate.]</ety> <def>A sacred place; hence, a
place of retreat; a room reserved for personal use; <as>as, an editor's
<ex>sanctum</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs>\'d8<col><b>Sanctum sanctorum</b></col> <ety>[L.]</ety>, <cd>the
Holy of Holies; the most holy place, as in the Jewish
temple.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanctus</ent><br/
<hw>Sanc"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanctus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sancire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A part of the Mass, or, in Protestant churches,
a part of the communion service, of which the first words in Latin are
<xex>Sanctus, sanctus, sanctus</xex> [Holy, holy, holy]; -- called also
<altname>Tersanctus</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An anthem composed for these
words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sanctus bell</b></col>, <cd>a small bell usually
suspended in a bell cot at the apex of the nave roof, over the chancel
arch, in mediaeval churches, but a hand bell is now often used; -- so
called because rung at the singing of the <xex>sanctus</xex>, at the
conclusion of the ordinary of the Mass, and again at the elevation of
the host. Called also <altname>Mass bell</altname>, <altname>sacring
bell</altname>, <altname>saints' bell</altname>,
<altname>sance-bell</altname>, <altname>sancte
bell</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sand</ent><br/
<hw>Sand</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sand</ets>;
akin to D. <ets>zand</ets>, G. <ets>sand</ets>, OHG. <ets>sant</ets>,
Icel. <ets>sandr</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>sand</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Fine particles of stone, esp. of siliceous stone, but
not reduced to dust; comminuted stone in the form of loose grains, which
are not coherent when wet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That finer matter, called <qex>sand</qex>, is no other than very
small pebbles.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A single particle of such stone.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The sand in the hourglass; hence, a moment or
interval of time; the term or extent of one's life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sands</qex> are numbered that make up my life.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Tracts of land consisting of sand,
like the deserts of Arabia and Africa; also, extensive tracts of sand
exposed by the ebb of the tide.</def> <ldquo/The Libyan
<xex>sands</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au> <ldquo/The <xex>sands</xex> o'
Dee.<rdquo/ <au>C. Kingsley.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Courage; pluck; grit.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sand badger</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
Japanese badger (<spn>Meles ankuma</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
bag</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A bag filled with sand or earth, used
for various purposes, as in fortification, for ballast, etc.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A long bag filled with sand, used as a club by
assassins.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand ball</b></col>, <cd>soap mixed with
sand, made into a ball for use at the toilet.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
bath</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>A vessel of hot sand
in a laboratory, in which vessels that are to be heated are partially
immersed</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A bath in which the body is immersed in
hot sand.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand bed</b></col>, <cd>a thick layer of sand,
whether deposited naturally or artificially; specifically, a thick layer
of sand into which molten metal is run in casting, or from a reducing
furnace.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand birds</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
collective name for numerous species of limicoline birds, such as the
sandpipers, plovers, tattlers, and many others; -- called also
<altname>shore birds</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand blast</b></col>,
<cd>a process of engraving and cutting glass and other hard substances
by driving sand against them by a steam jet or otherwise; also, the
apparatus used in the process.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand box</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A box with a perforated top or cover, for sprinkling
paper with sand</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A box carried on locomotives,
from which sand runs on the rails in front of the driving wheel, to
prevent slipping.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand-box tree</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a tropical American tree (<spn>Hura
crepitans</spn>). Its fruit is a depressed many-celled woody capsule
which, when completely dry, bursts with a loud report and scatters the
seeds.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand bug</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an
American anomuran crustacean (<spn>Hippa talpoidea</spn>) which burrows
in sandy seabeaches. It is often used as bait by fishermen.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand canal</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a tubular vessel
having a calcareous coating, and connecting the oral ambulacral ring
with the madreporic tubercle. It appears to be excretory in
function.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand cock</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the redshank.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Sand
collar</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Sand
saucer</cref>, below.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand crab</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The lady crab.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>A land crab, or ocypodian.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand crack</b></col>
<fld>(Far.)</fld>, <cd>a crack extending downward from the coronet, in
the wall of a horse's hoof, which often causes lameness.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand cricket</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
several species of large terrestrial crickets of the genus
<gen>Stenophelmatus</gen> and allied genera, native of the sandy plains
of the Western United States.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand cusk</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any ophidioid fish. See <er>Illust.</er> under
<er>Ophidioid</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand dab</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small American flounder (<spn>Limanda
ferruginea</spn>); -- called also <altname>rusty dab</altname>. The name
is also applied locally to other allied species.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
darter</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small etheostomoid fish of
the Ohio valley (<spn>Ammocrypta pellucida</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
dollar</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of
small flat circular sea urchins, which live on sandy bottoms, especially
<spn>Echinarachnius parma</spn> of the American coast.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand drift</b></col>, <cd>drifting sand; also, a mound or bank
of drifted sand.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand eel</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A lant, or launce</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A slender
Pacific Ocean fish of the genus <gen>Gonorhynchus</gen>, having barbels
about the mouth.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand flag</b></col>, <cd>sandstone
which splits up into flagstones.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand flea</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any species of flea which inhabits,
or breeds in, sandy places, especially the common dog flea.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The chigoe</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>Any leaping amphipod
crustacean; a beach flea, or orchestian. See <cref>Beach flea</cref>,
under <er>Beach</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand flood</b></col>, <cd>a vast
body of sand borne along by the wind.</cd> <au>James Bruce.</au> --
<col><b>Sand fluke</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The
sandnecker</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The European smooth dab
(<spn>Pleuronectes microcephalus</spn>); -- called also
<altname>kitt</altname>, <altname>marysole</altname>, <altname>smear
dab</altname>, <altname>town dab</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of
small dipterous flies of the genus <gen>Simulium</gen>, abounding on
sandy shores, especially <spn>Simulium nocivum</spn> of the United
States. They are very troublesome on account of their biting habits.
Called also <altname>no-see-um</altname>, <altname>punky</altname>, and
<altname>midge</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand gall</b></col>.
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Sand pipe</cref>, below.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any species of grass
which grows in sand; especially, a tufted grass (<spn>Triplasis
purpurea</spn>) with numerous bearded joints, and acid awl-shaped
leaves, growing on the Atlantic coast.</cd> <-- p. 1274 pr=PCP --> --
<col><b>Sand grouse</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of many
species of Old World birds belonging to the suborder Pterocletes, and
resembling both grouse and pigeons. Called also <altname>rock
grouse</altname>, <altname>rock pigeon</altname>, and
<altname>ganga</altname>. They mostly belong to the genus
<gen>Pterocles</gen>, as the common Indian species (<spn>Pterocles
exustus</spn>). The large sand grouse (<spn>Pterocles arenarius</spn>),
the painted sand grouse (<spn>Pterocles fasciatus</spn>), and the
pintail sand grouse (<spn>Pterocles alchata</spn>) are also found in
India.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand hill</b></col>, <cd>a hill of sand; a
dune.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand-hill crane</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the American brown crane (<spn>Grus Mexicana</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Sand hopper</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a beach flea; an
orchestian.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand hornet</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a sand wasp.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand lark</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A small lark (<spn>Alaudala raytal</spn>), native of
India</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A small sandpiper, or plover, as the
ringneck, the sanderling, and the common European sandpiper</cd>.
<sd>(c)</sd> <cd>The Australian red-capped dotterel (<spn>Aegialophilus
ruficapillus</spn>); -- called also <altname>red-necked
plover</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand launce</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a lant, or launce.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
lizard</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a common European lizard
(<spn>Lacerta agilis</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Sand martin</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the bank swallow.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
mole</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the coast rat.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand monitor</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large Egyptian
lizard (<spn>Monitor arenarius</spn>) which inhabits dry
localities.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand mouse</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the dunlin.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Sand
myrtle</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Myrtle</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sand partridge</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>either of
two small Asiatic partridges of the genus <gen>Ammoperdix</gen>. The
wings are long and the tarsus is spurless. One species (<spn>Ammoperdix
Heeji</spn>) inhabits Palestine and Arabia. The other species
(<spn>Ammoperdix Bonhami</spn>), inhabiting Central Asia, is called also
<altname>seesee partridge</altname>, and <altname>teehoo</altname>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sand picture</b></col>, <cd>a picture made by putting sand of
different colors on an adhesive surface.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
pike</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The sauger</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The lizard fish.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand pillar</b></col>,
<cd>a sand storm which takes the form of a whirling pillar in its
progress in desert tracts like those of the Sahara and Mongolia.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand pipe</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>a tubular cavity,
from a few inches to several feet in depth, occurring especially in
calcareous rocks, and often filled with gravel, sand, etc.; -- called
also <altname>sand gall</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand pride</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small British lamprey now considered to be the
young of larger species; -- called also <altname>sand
prey</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand pump</b></col>, <cd>in artesian
well boring, a long, slender bucket with a valve at the bottom for
raising sand from the well.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand rat</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the pocket gopher.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
rock</b></col>, <cd>a rock made of cemented sand.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
runner</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the turnstone.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand saucer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the mass of egg
capsules, or oothecae, of any mollusk of the genus <gen>Natica</gen> and
allied genera. It has the shape of a bottomless saucer, and is coated
with fine sand; -- called also <altname>sand collar</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand screw</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an amphipod
crustacean (<spn>Lepidactylis arenarius</spn>), which burrows in the
sandy seabeaches of Europe and America.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
shark</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an American shark
(<spn>Odontaspis littoralis</spn>) found on the sandy coasts of the
Eastern United States; -- called also <altname>gray shark</altname>, and
<altname>dogfish shark</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand skink</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of Old World lizards
belonging to the genus <gen>Seps</gen>; <as>as, the ocellated <ex>sand
skink</ex> (<spn>Seps ocellatus</spn>) of Southern Europe</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sand skipper</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a beach flea, or
orchestian.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand smelt</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a silverside.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand snake</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any one of several species of
harmless burrowing snakes of the genus <gen>Eryx</gen>, native of
Southern Europe, Africa, and Asia, especially <spn>Eryx jaculus</spn> of
India and <spn>Eryx Johnii</spn>, used by snake charmers.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Any innocuous South African snake of the genus
<gen>Psammophis</gen>, especially <spn>Psammophis sibilans</spn>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sand snipe</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
sandpiper.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand star</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>an ophiurioid starfish living on sandy sea bottoms; a brittle
star.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand storm</b></col>, <cd>a cloud of sand driven
violently by the wind.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand sucker</b></col>, <cd>the
sandnecker.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand swallow</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the bank swallow. See under <er>Bank</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
trap</b></col>, <fld>(Golf)</fld> <cd>a shallow pit on a golf course
having a layer of sand in it, usually located near a green, and designed
to function as a hazard, due to the difficulty of hitting balls
effectively from such a position.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand tube</b></col>,
<cd>a tube made of sand.</cd> Especially: <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A tube of
vitrified sand, produced by a stroke of lightning; a fulgurite</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Any tube made of cemented sand.</cd>
<sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>In starfishes, a tube having
calcareous particles in its wall, which connects the oral water tube
with the madreporic plate.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand viper</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Hognose snake</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sand
wasp</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of
hymenopterous insects belonging to the families <fam>Pompilidae</fam>
and <fam>Spheridae</fam>, which dig burrows in sand. The female
provisions the nest with insects or spiders which she paralyzes by
stinging, and which serve as food for her young.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sand</ent><br/
<hw>Sand</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sanded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sanding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sprinkle or cover
with sand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To drive upon the sand.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To bury (oysters) beneath drifting sand or
mud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To mix with sand for purposes of fraud; <as>as, to
<ex>sand</ex> sugar</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 5. To grind down or make smooth by rubbing with an abrasive
object, esp. with sandpaper; to sand down --> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandal</ent><br/
<hw>San"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sendal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sails of silk and ropes of <qex>sandal</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandal</ent><br/
<hw>San"dal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sandalwood.</def> <ldquo/Fans of
<xex>sandal</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandal</ent><br/
<hw>San"dal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sandale</ets>, L.
<ets>sandalium</ets>, Gr. <?/, dim. of <?/, probably from Per.
<ets>sandal</ets>.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A kind of shoe consisting of
a sole strapped to the foot; a protection for the foot, covering its
lower surface, but not its upper.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A kind of
slipper.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>An overshoe with parallel openings
across the instep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandaled</ent><br/
<hw>San"daled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Wearing
sandals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The measured footfalls of his <qex>sandaled</qex> feet.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Made like a sandal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandaliform</ent><br/
<hw>San*dal"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sandal</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Shaped like a sandal or slipper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandalwood</ent><br/
<hw>San"dal*wood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sandal</ets>, <ets>santal</ets>, fr. Ar. <ets><cced/andal</ets>, or
Gr. <grk>sa`ntalon</grk>; both ultimately fr. Skr. <ets>candana</ets>.
Cf. <er>Sanders</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
highly perfumed yellowish heartwood of an East Indian and Polynesian
tree (<spn>Santalum album</spn>), and of several other trees of the same
genus, as the Hawaiian <spn>Santalum Freycinetianum</spn> and
<spn>Santalum pyrularium</spn>, the Australian <spn>Santalum
latifolium</spn>, etc. The name is extended to several other kinds of
fragrant wood.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any tree of the genus
<gen>Santalum</gen>, or a tree which yields sandalwood.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>The red wood of a kind of buckthorn, used in Russia
for dyeing leather (<spn>Rhamnus Dahuricus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>False sandalwood</b></col>, <cd>the fragrant wood of
several trees not of the genus <gen>Santalum</gen>, as <spn>Ximenia
Americana</spn>, <spn>Myoporum tenuifolium</spn> of Tahiti.</cd> --
<col><b>Red sandalwood</b></col>, <cd>a heavy, dark red dyewood, being
the heartwood of two leguminous trees of India (<spn>Pterocarpus
santalinus</spn>, and <spn>Adenanthera pavonina</spn>); -- called also
<altname>red sanderswood</altname>, <altname>sanders</altname> or
<altname>saunders</altname>, and
<altname>rubywood</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandarac</ent><br/
<ent>Sandarach</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>San"da*rach</hw>, <hw>San"da*rac</hw>, }</mhw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sandaraca</ets>, Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Realgar; red sulphide of
arsenic.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot. Chem.)</fld> <def>A white or yellow resin
obtained from a Barbary tree (<spn>Callitris quadrivalvis</spn> or
<spn>Thuya articulata</spn>), and pulverized for pounce; -- probably so
called from a resemblance to the mineral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sandbag</ent><br/
<hw>sand"bag`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bag filled with
sand; small sandbags may be used as a weapon, or larger ones to build
walls or as ballast; <as>as, they kept the flooding river from the area
by buiding a temmporary dike out of <ex>sandbags</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sandbag</ent><br/
<hw>sand"bag`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v.</pos> <def>To treat harshly or
unfairly.</def> <wns>[wns=1]</wns><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hit something or somebody with or as if with a
sandbag.</def> <wns>[wns=2]</wns><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To protect or strengthen with sandbags; stop up;
<as>as, the residents <ex>sandbagged</ex> the beach front</as>.</def>
<wns>[wns=3]</wns><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To thwart (another person's plans) by surreptitious
maneuvers; <as>as, he <ex>sandbagged</ex> my proposal by talking in
private with other members of the committee</as>.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To intimidate or coerce, especially by crude
methods.</def> <mark>[Informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To deceive and take advantage of (a person) by
misrepresenting one's true intentions.</def> <mark>[Informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <fld>(Poker)</fld> <def>To
encourage opponents into betting more by first refraining from betting
while having a strong hand, and only later raising the stakes. In
informal games, certain types of <ex>sandbagging</ex> are
forbidden.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandbagger</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"bag`ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An assaulter
whose weapon is a sand bag. See <cref>Sand bag</cref>, under
<er>Sand</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sand-blind</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"-blind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[For <ets>sam
blind</ets> half blind; AS. <ets>s<amac/m-</ets> half (akin to
<ets>semi-</ets>) + <ets>blind</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having defective
sight; dim-sighted; purblind.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanded</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered or sprinkled
with sand; sandy; barren.</def> <rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Marked with small spots; variegated with spots;
speckled; of a sandy color, as a hound.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Short-sighted.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandemanian</ent><br/
<hw>San`de*ma"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>A follower of Robert <xex>Sandeman</xex>, a Scotch
sectary of the eighteenth century. See <er>Glassite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandemanianism</ent><br/
<hw>San`de*ma"ni*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The faith
or system of the Sandemanians.</def> <rj><au>A. Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanderling</ent><br/
<hw>San"der*ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sand</ets>
+ <ets>-ling</ets>. So called because it obtains its food by searching
the moist sands of the seashore.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small
gray and brown sandpiper (<spn>Calidris arenaria</spn>) very common on
sandy beaches in America, Europe, and Asia. Called also
<altname>curwillet</altname>, <altname>sand lark</altname>,
<altname>stint</altname>, and <altname>ruddy plover</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanders</ent><br/
<hw>San"ders</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sandal</er>.]</ety> <def>An old name of sandalwood, now applied only
to the red sandalwood. See under <er>Sandalwood</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanders-blue</ent><br/
<hw>San"ders-blue"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saunders-blue</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandever</ent><br/
<hw>San"de*ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sandiver</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandfish</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A small marine fish of the Pacific coast of North America
(<spn>Trichodon trichodon</spn>) which buries itself in the
sand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandglass</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"glass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An instrument for
measuring time by the running of sand. See <er>Hourglass</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandhiller</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"hill`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A nickname given
to any <ldquo/poor white<rdquo/ living in the pine woods which cover the
sandy hills in Georgia and South Carolina.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sandy, or of being of a sandy color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandish</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Approaching the nature of sand;
loose; not compact.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandiver</ent><br/
<hw>San"di*ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perh. fr. OF.
<ets>sa<ium/n</ets> grease, fat + <ets>de</ets> of + <ets>verre</ets>
glass (cf. <er>Saim</er>), or fr. F. <ets>sel de verre</ets>
sandiver.]</ety> <def>A whitish substance which is cast up, as a scum,
from the materials of glass in fusion, and, floating on the top, is
skimmed off; -- called also <altname>glass gall</altname>.</def>
<altsp>[Formerly written also <asp>sandever</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandix</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>San"dix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sandix</ets>, <ets>sandyx</ets>, vermilion, or a color like
vermilion, Gr. <?/, <?/.]</ety> <def>A kind of minium, or red lead, made
by calcining carbonate of lead, but inferior to true minium.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sandyx</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sand-lot</ent><br/
<ent>sandlot</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>sand"lot</hw>, <hw>Sand"-lot`</hw></mhw>, <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Lit., of or pert. to a lot or piece of sandy ground, --
hence, pert. to, or characteristic of, the policy or practices of the
socialistic or communistic followers of the Irish agitator Denis
Kearney, who delivered many of his speeches in the open sand lots about
San Francisco; <as>as, the <col><b>sand-lot constitution</b></col> of
California, framed in 1879, under the influence of <ex>sand-lot</ex>
agitation</as>.</def></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def> of or pertaining to a sandlot; -- used especially
in reference to informal games played by children; <as>as,
<ex>sandlot</ex> baseball.</as></def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sandlot</ent><br/
<hw>sand"lot</hw> <pr>(s<acr/nd"l<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
vacant lot, especially one where children play games.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandman</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"man`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mythical person
who makes children sleepy, so that they rub their eyes as if there were
sand in them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandnecker</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"neck`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A European flounder (<spn>Hippoglossoides limandoides</spn>); --
called also <altname>rough dab</altname>, <altname>long fluke</altname>,
<altname>sand fluke</altname>, and <altname>sand
sucker</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandpaper</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"pa`per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Paper covered on
one side with sand glued fast, -- used for smoothing and
polishing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandpaper</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"pa`per</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To smooth or polish with
sandpaper; <as>as, to <ex>sandpaper</ex> a door</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandpiper</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"pi`per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of small limicoline
game birds belonging to <gen>Tringa</gen>, <gen>Actodromas</gen>,
<gen>Ereunetes</gen>, and various allied genera of the family
<fam>Tringidae</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The most important North American species are the
pectoral sandpiper (<spn>Tringa maculata</spn>), called also
<altname>brownback</altname>, <altname>grass snipe</altname>, and
<altname>jacksnipe</altname>; the red-backed, or black-breasted,
sandpiper, or dunlin (<spn>Tringa alpina</spn>); the purple sandpiper
(<spn>Tringa maritima</spn>: the red-breasted sandpiper, or knot
(<spn>Tringa canutus</spn>); the semipalmated sandpiper (<spn>Ereunetes
pusillus</spn>); the spotted sandpiper, or teeter-tail (<spn>Actitis
macularia</spn>); the buff-breasted sandpiper (<spn>Tryngites
subruficollis</spn>), and the Bartramian sandpiper, or upland plover.
See under <er>Upland</er>. Among the European species are the dunlin,
the knot, the ruff, the sanderling, and the common sandpiper
(<spn>Actitis hypoleucus</spn> syn. <spn>Tringoides hypoleucus</spn>),
called also <altname>fiddler</altname>, <altname>peeper</altname>,
<altname>pleeps</altname>, <altname>weet-weet</altname>, and
<altname>summer snipe</altname>. Some of the small plovers and tattlers
are also called <ex>sandpipers</ex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small lamprey eel; the
pride.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Curlew sandpiper</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Curlew</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stilt sandpiper</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Stilt</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandpit</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"pit`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pit or excavation
from which sand is or has been taken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandre</ent><br/
<hw>San"dre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
Russian fish (<spn>Lucioperca sandre</spn>) which yields a valuable oil,
called <xex>sandre oil</xex>, used in the preparation of
caviar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandstone</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A rock made of
sand more or less firmly united. Common or siliceous sandstone consists
mainly of quartz sand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Different names are applied to the various kinds of
sandstone according to their composition; as, <xex>granitic</xex>,
<xex>argillaceous</xex>, <xex>micaceous</xex>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Flexible sandstone</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>the
finer-grained variety of itacolumite, which on account of the scales of
mica in the lamination is quite flexible.</cd> -- <col><b>Red
sandstone</b></col>, <cd>a name given to two extensive series of British
rocks in which red sandstones predominate, one below, and the other
above, the coal measures. These were formerly known as the
<xex>Old</xex> and the <xex>New Red Sandstone</xex> respectively, and
the former name is still retained for the group preceding the Coal and
referred to the Devonian age, but the term <xex>New Red Sandstone</xex>
is now little used, some of the strata being regarded as Permian and the
remained as Triassic. See the <xex>Chart</xex> of
<er>Geology</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandwich</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"wich</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Named from the
Earl of <ets>Sandwich</ets>.]</ety> <def>Two pieces of bread and butter
with a thin slice of meat, cheese, or the like, between them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. Any food composed of two pieces of bread with another food in
between. 3. Any object composed of two layers of one subtance on either
side of a second substance. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandwich</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"wich</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sandwiched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sandwiching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make into a sandwich;
also, figuratively, to insert between portions of something dissimilar;
to form of alternate parts or things, or alternating layers of a
different nature; to interlard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandworm</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of numerous species of annelids which burrow
in the sand of the seashore.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any species of
annelids of the genus <gen>Sabellaria</gen>. They construct firm tubes
of agglutinated sand on rocks and shells, and are sometimes destructive
to oysters.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The chigoe, a species of
flea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandwort</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Arenaria</gen>, low, tufted herbs
(order <ord>Caryophyllaceae</ord>.)</def> <-- "of" missing? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandy</ent><br/
<hw>Sand"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sandier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sandiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>sandig</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of, abounding with, or resembling, sand;
full of sand; covered or sprinkled with sand; <as>as, a <ex>sandy</ex>
desert, road, or soil</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of the color of sand; of a light yellowish red
color; <as>as, <ex>sandy</ex> hair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sandyx</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>San"dyx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Sandix</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sane</ent><br/
<hw>Sane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sanus</ets>; cf.
Gr. <?/, <?/, safe, sound. Cf. <er>Sound</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Being in a healthy condition; not deranged; acting
rationally; -- said of the mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Mentally sound; possessing a rational mind; having
the mental faculties in such condition as to be able to anticipate and
judge of the effect of one's actions in an ordinary maner; -- said of
persons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sound; healthy; underanged; unbroken.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saneness</ent><br/
<hw>Sane"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being sane;
sanity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sang</ent><br/
<hw>Sang</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Sing</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sangu</ent><br/
<ent>Sanga</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>San"ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>San"gu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Abyssinian ox
(<spn>Bos Africanus</spn> syn. <spn>Bibos Africanus</spn>), noted for
the great length of its horns. It has a hump on its back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sangaree</ent><br/
<hw>San`ga*ree"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>sangria</ets>, lit., bleeding, from <ets>sangre</ets>, blood, L.
<ets>sanguis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Wine and water sweetened and spiced, --
a favorite West Indian drink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sang-froid</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sang`-froid"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., cold
blood.]</ety> <def>Freedom from agitation or excitement of mind;
coolness in trying circumstances; indifference; calmness.</def>
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sangiac</ent><br/
<hw>San"gi*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sanjak</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sangreal</ent><br/
<ent>Sangraal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>San`graal"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>San"gre*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Saint</er>, and
<er>Grail</er>.]</ety> <def>See <cref>Holy Grail</cref>, under
<er>Grail</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguiferous</ent><br/
<hw>San*guif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguis</ets> blood + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Conveying blood; <as>as,
<ex>sanguiferous</ex> vessels, <it>i. e.</it>, the arteries, veins,
capillaries</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguification</ent><br/
<hw>San`gui*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sanguification</ets>. See <er>Sanguify</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The production of blood; the conversion of
the products of digestion into blood; hematosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguifier</ent><br/
<hw>San"gui*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A producer of
blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguifluous</ent><br/
<hw>San*guif"lu*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguis</ets> blood + <ets>fluere</ets> to flow.]</ety>
<def>Flowing or running with blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguify</ent><br/
<hw>San"gui*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguis</ets> blood + <ets>-fy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sanguifier</ets>.]</ety> <def>To produce blood from.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguigenous</ent><br/
<hw>San*guig"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguis</ets> + <ets>-genous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Producing blood;
<as>as, <ex>sanguigenous</ex> food</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinaceous</ent><br/
<hw>San`gui*na"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Of a
blood-red color; sanguine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>San`gui*na"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sanguinary</er>, <pos>a.</pos> & <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants of the Poppy family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <spn>Sanguinaria Canadensis</spn>, or bloodroot, is the
only species. It has a perennial rootstock, which sends up a few
roundish lobed leaves and solitary white blossoms in early spring. See
<er>Bloodroot</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The rootstock of the bloodroot, used in medicine as
an emetic, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinarily</ent><br/
<hw>San"gui*na*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
sanguinary manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinariness</ent><br/
<hw>San"gui*na*ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being sanguinary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinary</ent><br/
<hw>San"gui*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguinarius</ets>, fr. <ets>sanguis</ets> blood: cf. F.
<ets>sanguinaire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Attended with much
bloodshed; bloody; murderous; <as>as, a <ex>sanguinary</ex> war,
contest, or battle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We may not propagate religion by wars, or by <qex>sanguinary</qex>
persecutions to force consciences.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Bloodthirsty; cruel; eager to shed blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Passion . . . makes us brutal and <qex>sanguinary</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Broome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Bloody; murderous; bloodthirsty; cruel.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinary</ent><br/
<hw>San"gui*na*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>herba
sanguinaria</ets> an herb that stanches blood: cf. F.
<ets>sanguinaire</ets>. See <er>Sanguinary</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The yarrow.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The Sanguinaria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1275 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Sanguine</ent><br/
<hw>San"guine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sanguin</ets>, L. <ets>sanguineus</ets>, fr. <ets>sanguis</ets>
blood. Cf. <er>Sanguineous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the
color of blood; red.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of his complexion he was <qex>sanguine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like to that <qex>sanguine</qex> flower inscribed with woe.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characterized by abundance and active circulation of
blood; <as>as, a <ex>sanguine</ex> bodily temperament</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Warm; ardent; <as>as, a <ex>sanguine</ex>
temper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Anticipating the best; cheerfully optimistic; not
desponding; confident; full of hope; <as>as, <ex>sanguine</ex> of
success; a <ex>sanguine</ex> disposition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Warm; ardent; lively; confident; hopeful;
optimistic.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguine</ent><br/
<hw>San"guine</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Blood color;
red.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything of a blood-red color, as cloth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>sanguine</qex> and in pes he clad was all.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Bloodstone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Red crayon. See the Note under <er>Crayon</er>,
1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguine</ent><br/
<hw>San"guine</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To stain with blood; to impart
the color of blood to; to ensanguine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguineless</ent><br/
<hw>San"guine*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of blood;
pale.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinely</ent><br/
<hw>San"guine*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sanguine
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I can not speculate quite so <qex>sanguinely</qex> as he does.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguineness</ent><br/
<hw>San"guine*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
sanguine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguineous</ent><br/
<hw>San*guin"e*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguineus</ets>. See <er>Sanguine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Abounding with blood; sanguine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to blood; bloody; constituting
blood.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Blood-red; crimson.</def>
<rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sanguinity</ent><br/
<hw>san*guin"i*ty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
sanguine; sanguineness.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinivorous</ent><br/
<hw>San"gui*niv"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguis + vorare</ets> to devour.]</ety> <def>Subsisting on
blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinolency</ent><br/
<hw>San*guin"o*len*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being sanguinolent, or bloody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguinolent</ent><br/
<hw>San*guin"o*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguinolentus</ets>, from <ets>sanguis</ets> blood: cf. F.
<ets>sanguinolent</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tinged or mingled with blood;
bloody; <as>as, <ex>sanguinolent</ex> sputa</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguisuge</ent><br/
<hw>San"gui*suge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguisuga</ets>; <ets>sanguis</ets> blood + <ets>sugere</ets> to
suck.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A bloodsucker, or leech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanguivorous</ent><br/
<hw>San*guiv"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanguis</ets> blood + <ets>vorare</ets> to devour.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Subsisting upon blood; -- said of certain
blood-sucking bats and other animals. See <er>Vampire</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanhedrim</ent><br/
<ent>Sanhedrin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>San"he*drin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>San"he*drim</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>sanhedr<imac/n</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/; <?/ with + <?/ a seat, fr. <?/
to sit. See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Jewish Antiq.)</fld> <def>the
great council of the Jews, which consisted of seventy members, to whom
the high priest was added. It had jurisdiction of religious
matters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanhedrist</ent><br/
<hw>San"he*drist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A member of the
sanhedrin.</def> <rj><au>Schaeffer (Lange's Com.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanhita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>San"hi*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>samhita</ets>, properly, combination.]</ety> <def>A collection of
vedic hymns, songs, or verses, forming the first part of each
Veda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanicle</ent><br/
<hw>San"i*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>sanare</ets> to heal.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of
the umbelliferous genus <gen>Sanicula</gen>, reputed to have healing
powers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanidine</ent><br/
<hw>San"i*dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/. <?/, a
board. So called in allusion to the tabular crystals.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of orthoclase feldspar common in
certain eruptive rocks, as trachyte; -- called also <altname>glassy
feldspar</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanies</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"ni*es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A thin, serous fluid commonly discharged from
ulcers or foul wounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanious</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saniosus</ets>, fr. <ets>sanies</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sanieux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Pertaining
to sanies, or partaking of its nature and appearance; thin and serous,
with a slight bloody tinge; <as>as, the <ex>sanious</ex> matter of an
ulcer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(med.)</fld> <def>Discharging sanies; <as>as, a
<ex>sanious</ex> ulcer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanitarian</ent><br/
<hw>San`i*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to health, or the laws of health; sanitary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanitarian</ent><br/
<hw>San`i*ta"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An advocate of sanitary
measures; one especially interested or versed in sanitary
measures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanitarist</ent><br/
<hw>San"i*ta*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
sanitarian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanitarium</ent><br/
<hw>San`i*ta"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sanitary</er>.]</ety> <def>A health station or retreat; a
sanatorium.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>sanitarium</xex> for troops.<rdquo/
<rj><au>L. Oliphant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanitary</ent><br/
<hw>San"i*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanitas</ets> health: cf. F. <ets>sanitaire</ets>. See
<er>Sanity</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to health; designed to
secure or preserve health; relating to the preservation or restoration
of health; hygienic; <as>as, <ex>sanitary</ex> regulations</as>. See the
Note under <er>Sanatory</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sanitary Commission</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Commission</er>.</cd></cs> <-- sanitary napkin, an absorbant pad
worn to absorb the menstrual flow of women. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanitation</ent><br/
<hw>San`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
rendering sanitary; the science of sanitary conditions; the preservation
of health; the use of sanitary measures; hygiene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How much <qex>sanitation</qex> has advanced during the last half
century.</q> <rj><qau>H. Hartshorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanity</ent><br/
<hw>San"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sanitas</ets>, from <ets>sanus</ets> sound, healthy. See
<er>Sane</er>.]</ety> <def>The condition or quality of being sane;
soundness of health of body or mind, especially of the mind;
saneness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanjak</ent><br/
<hw>San"jak</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Turk.
<ets>sanj<amac/g</ets>.]</ety> <def>A district or a subvision of a
vilayet.</def> <mark>[Turkey]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>San Jose scale</ent><br/
<hw>San Jo*s<eacute/" scale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A very destructive
scale insect (<spn>Aspidiotus perniciosus</spn>) that infests the apple,
pear, and other fruit trees. So called because first introduced into the
United States at <city><etsep>San Jos<eacute/</etsep></city>,
California.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sank</ent><br/
<hw>Sank</hw> <pr>(s<acr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Sink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sankha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sank"ha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets><cced/ankha</ets> a shell.]</ety> <def>A chank shell
(<spn>Turbinella pyrum</spn>); also, a shell bracelet or necklace made
in India from the chank shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sankhya</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sankh"ya</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Hindu system of
philosophy which refers all things to soul and a rootless germ called
<xex>prakriti</xex>, consisting of three elements, goodness, passion,
and darkness.</def> <rj><au>Whitworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sannop</ent><br/
<hw>San"nop</hw> <pr>(s<acr/n"n<ocr/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sannup</er>.</def> <rj><au>Bancroft.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sannup</ent><br/
<hw>San"nup</hw> <pr>(-n<ucr/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A married male
Indian; a brave; -- correlative of <inv>squaw</inv>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanny</ent><br/
<hw>San"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The sandpiper.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sans</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sans</hw> <pr>(s<aum/n; E. s<acr/nz)</pr>, <pos>prep.</pos>
<ety>[F., from L. <ets>sine</ets> without.]</ety> <def>Without; deprived
or destitute of. Rarely used as an English word.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Sans</xex> fail.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sans</qex> teeth, <qex>sans</qex> eyes, <qex>sans</qex>
taste, <qex>sans</qex> everything.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanscrit</ent><br/
<hw>San"scrit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sanskrit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sans-culotte</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sans`-cu`lotte"</hw> <pr>(F. <?/; E. <?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F., without breeches.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A fellow without
breeches; a ragged fellow; -- a name of reproach given in the first
French revolution to the extreme republican party, who rejected breeches
as an emblem peculiar to the upper classes or aristocracy, and adopted
pantaloons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, an extreme or radical republican; a violent
revolutionist; a Jacobin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sans-culottic</ent><br/
<hw>Sans`-cu*lot"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining
to, or involving, sans-culottism; radical; revolutionary;
Jacobinical.</def> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sans-culottism</ent><br/
<hw>Sans`-cu*lot"tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sans-culottisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Extreme republican principles;
the principles or practice of the <xex>sans-culottes</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanskrit</ent><br/
<hw>San"skrit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>Samsk<rsdot/ta</ets> the Sanskrit language, literally, the perfect,
polished, or classical language, fr. <ets>samsk<rsdot/ta</ets> prepared,
wrought, made, excellent, perfect; <ets>sam</ets> together (akin to E.
<ets>same</ets>) + <ets>k<rsdot/ta</ets> made. See <er>Same</er>,
<er>Create</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Sanscrit</asp>.]</altsp> <def>The ancient language of the Hindoos,
long since obsolete in vernacular use, but preserved to the present day
as the literary and sacred dialect of India. It is nearly allied to the
Persian, and to the principal languages of Europe, classical and modern,
and by its more perfect preservation of the roots and forms of the
primitive language from which they are all descended, is a most
important assistance in determining their history and relations. Cf.
<er>Prakrit</er>, and <er>Veda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanskrit</ent><br/
<hw>San"skrit</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to Sanskrit;
written in Sanskrit; <as>as, a <ex>Sanskrit</ex> dictionary or
inscription</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanskritic</ent><br/
<hw>San*skrit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sanskrit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sanskritist</ent><br/
<hw>San"skrit*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in
Sanskrit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sans-souci</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sans`-sou`ci"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Without care; free and easy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santal</ent><br/
<hw>San"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sant</ets>alum +
piperon<ets>al</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A colorless
crystalline substance, isomeric with piperonal, but having weak acid
properties. It is extracted from sandalwood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santalaceous</ent><br/
<hw>San`ta*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a natural order of plants
(<ord>Santalaceae</ord>), of which the genus <gen>Santalum</gen> is the
type, and which includes the buffalo nut and a few other North American
plants, and many peculiar plants of the southern hemisphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santalic</ent><br/
<hw>San*tal"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or obtained from, sandalwood
(<gen>Santalum</gen>); -- used specifically to designate an acid
obtained as a resinous or red crystalline dyestuff, which is called also
<xex>santalin</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santalin</ent><br/
<hw>San"ta*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>santaline</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Santalic acid. See
<er>Santalic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santalum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>San"ta*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sandalwood</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of trees with
entire opposite leaves and small apetalous flowers. There are less than
a dozen species, occurring from India to Australia and the Pacific
Islands. See <er>Sandalwood</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santees</ent><br/
<hw>San`tees"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Santee</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>One of the seven confederated tribes of Indians belonging to the
Sioux, or Dakotas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santer</ent><br/
<hw>San"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saunter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>San"ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>santon</ets>, augmented fr. <ets>santo</ets> holy, L.
<ets>sanctus</ets>.]</ety> <def>A Turkish saint; a kind of dervish,
regarded by the people as a saint: also, a hermit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santonate</ent><br/
<hw>San"to*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of santonic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santonic</ent><br/
<hw>San*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, an acid (distinct from
santoninic acid) obtained from santonin as a white crystalline
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santonin</ent><br/
<hw>San"to*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>herba
santonica</ets>, a kind of plant, fr. <ets>Santoni</ets> a people of
Aquitania; cf. Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>santonine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white crystalline substance having a bitter
taste, extracted from the buds of levant wormseed and used as an
anthelmintic. It occassions a peculiar temporary color blindness,
causing objects to appear as if seen through a yellow glass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santoninate</ent><br/
<hw>San"to*nin`ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of santoninic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Santoninic</ent><br/
<hw>San`to*nin"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to santonin; -- used specifically to designate an
acid not known in the free state, but obtained in its salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sao</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any marine annelid of the genus <gen>Hyalinaecia</gen>, especially
<spn>Hyalinaecia tubicola</spn> of Europe, which inhabits a transparent
movable tube resembling a quill in color and texture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sap</ent><br/
<hw>Sap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>saep</ets>; akin
to OHG. <ets>saf</ets>, G. <ets>saft</ets>, Icel. <ets>safi</ets>; of
uncertain origin; possibly akin to L. <ets>sapere</ets> to taste, to be
wise, <ets>sapa</ets> must or new wine boiled thick. Cf. <er>Sapid</er>,
<er>Sapient</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The juice of plants of any
kind, especially the ascending and descending juices or circulating
fluid essential to nutrition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The ascending is the <xex>crude</xex> sap, the
assimilation of which takes place in the leaves, when it becomes the
<xex>elaborated</xex> sap suited to the growth of the plant.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The sapwood, or alburnum, of a tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A simpleton; a saphead; a milksop.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sap ball</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any large
fungus of the genus Polyporus. See <er>Polyporus</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sap green</b></col>, <cd>a dull light green pigment prepared
from the juice of the ripe berries of the <spn>Rhamnus
catharticus</spn>, or buckthorn. It is used especially by water-color
artists.</cd> -- <col><b>Sap rot</b></col>, <cd>the dry rot. See under
<er>Dry</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sap sucker</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of several species of small American woodpeckers of the
genus <gen>Sphyrapicus</gen>, especially the yellow-bellied woodpecker
(<spn>Sphyrapicus varius</spn>) of the Eastern United States. They are
so named because they puncture the bark of trees and feed upon the sap.
The name is loosely applied to other woodpeckers.</cd> -- <col><b>Sap
tube</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a vessel that conveys
sap.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sap</ent><br/
<hw>Sap</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sapped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sapping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>saper</ets> (cf. Sp.
<ets>zapar</ets>, It. <ets>zapare</ets>), fr. <ets>sape</ets> a sort of
scythe, LL. <ets>sappa</ets> a sort of mattock.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To subvert by digging or wearing away; to mine; to undermine; to
destroy the foundation of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor safe their dwellings were, for <qex>sapped</qex> by
floods,<br/
Their houses fell upon their household gods.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To pierce with saps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make unstable or infirm; to unsettle; to
weaken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ring out the grief that <qex>saps</qex> the mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sap</ent><br/
<hw>Sap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To proceed by mining,
or by secretly undermining; to execute saps.</def> <rj><au>W. P.
Craighill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Both assaults are carried on by <qex>sapping</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tatler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sap</ent><br/
<hw>Sap</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A narrow ditch or
trench made from the foremost parallel toward the glacis or covert way
of a besieged place by digging under cover of gabions, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sap fagot</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a fascine
about three feet long, used in sapping, to close the crevices between
the gabions before the parapet is made.</cd> -- <col><b>Sap
roller</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a large gabion, six or seven
feet long, filled with fascines, which the sapper sometimes rolls along
before him for protection from the fire of an enemy.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapadillo</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`a*dil"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sapodila</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapajo</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"a*jo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The sapajou.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapajou</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"a*jou</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sapajou</ets>, <ets>sajou</ets>, Braz. <ets>sajuassu</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of South American
monkeys of the genus <gen>Cebus</gen>, having long and prehensile tails.
Some of the species are called also <stype>capuchins</stype>. The bonnet
sapajou (<spn>Cebus subcristatus</spn>), the golden-handed sapajou
(<spn>Cebus chrysopus</spn>), and the white-throated sapajou (<spn>Cebus
hypoleucus</spn>) are well known species. See
<er>Capuchin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapan wood</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*pan" wood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Malay
<ets>sapang</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A dyewood yielded by
<spn>Caesalpinia Sappan</spn>, a thorny leguminous tree of Southern Asia
and the neighboring islands. It is the original Brazil wood.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sappan wood</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapful</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in sap;
sappy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saphead</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A weak-minded,
stupid fellow; a milksop.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saphenous</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*phe"nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
manifest.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Manifest; --
applied to the two principal superficial veins of the lower limb of
man.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of, pertaining to, or in the region of, the
saphenous veins; <as>as, the <ex>saphenous</ex> nerves; the
<ex>saphenous</ex> opening, an opening in the broad fascia of the thigh
through which the internal saphenous vein passes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapid</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sapidus</ets>,
fr. <ets>sapere</ets> to taste: cf. F. <ets>sapide</ets>. See
<er>Sapient</er>, <er>Savor</er>.]</ety> <def>Having the power of
affecting the organs of taste; possessing savor, or flavor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Camels, to make the water <qex>sapid</qex>, do raise the mud with
their feet.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapidity</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*pid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sapidit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
sapid; taste; savor; savoriness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whether one kind of <qex>sapidity</qex> is more effective than
another.</q> <rj><qau>M. S. Lamson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapidness</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"id*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being sapid;
sapidity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When the Israelites fancied the <qex>sapidness</qex> and relish of
the fleshpots, they longed to taste and to return.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapience</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"pi*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sapientia</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sapience</ets>. See
<er>Sapient</er>..]</ety> <def>The quality of being sapient; wisdom;
sageness; knowledge.</def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Woman, if I might sit beside your feet,<br/
And glean your scattered <qex>sapience</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapient</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"pi*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sapiens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sapere</ets> to
taste, to have sense, to know. See <er>Sage</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Wise; sage; discerning; -- often in irony or contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where the <qex>sapient</qex> king<br/
Held dalliance with his fair Egyptian spouse.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sage; sagacious; knowing; wise;
discerning.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapiential</ent><br/
<hw>Sa`pi*en"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sapientialis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having or affording wisdom.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Sa`pi*en"tial*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sapiential</qex> books of the Old [Testament].</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapientious</ent><br/
<hw>Sa`pi*en"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sapiential.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapientize</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"pi*ent*ize</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make sapient.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapiently</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"pi*ent*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sapient
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapindaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`in*da"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to an order of trees and shrubs
(<ord>Sapindaceae</ord>), including the (typical) genus Sapindus, the
maples, the margosa, and about seventy other genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapindus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*pin"dus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>sapo</ets> soap + <ets>Indicus</ets> Indian.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of tropical and subtropical trees with
pinnate leaves and panicled flowers. The fruits of some species are used
instead of soap, and their round black seeds are made into
necklaces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapless</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Destitute
of sap; not juicy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def><markp>Fig.</markp>: Dry; old; husky; withered;
spiritless.</def> <ldquo/A somewhat <xex>sapless</xex> womanhood.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now <qex>sapless</qex> on the verge of death he stands.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sapling</ent><br/
<hw>sap"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A young tree.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapodilla</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`o*dil"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>zapote</ets>, <ets>sapotillo</ets>, <ets>zapotillo</ets>, Mexican
<ets>cochit-zapotl</ets>. Cf. <er>Sapota</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A tall, evergeen, tropical American tree (<spn>Achras
Sapota</spn>); also, its edible fruit, the sapodilla plum.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sapadillo</asp>, <asp>sappadillo</asp>,
<asp>sappodilla</asp>, and <asp>zapotilla</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sapodilla plum</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the fruit
of <spn>Achras Sapota</spn>. It is about the size of an ordinary quince,
having a rough, brittle, dull brown rind, the flesh being of a dirty
yellowish white color, very soft, and deliciously sweet. Called also
<altname>naseberry</altname>. It is eatable only when it begins to be
spotted, and is much used in desserts.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapogenin</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*pog"e*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sapo</ets>nin + <ets>-gen</ets> + <ets>-in</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white crystalline substance obtained by the
decomposition of saponin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`o*na"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sapo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, soap, of Teutonic origin, and akin to
E. <ets>soap</ets>. See <er>Soap</er>.]</ety> <def>Resembling soap;
having the qualities of soap; soapy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Saponaceous</xex> bodies are compounds of an acid
and a base, and are in reality a kind of salt.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1276 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Saponacity</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`o*nac"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being saponaceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponary</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"o*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Saponaceous.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponifiable</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*pon*i*fi`a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
conversion into soap; <as>as, a <ex>saponifiable</ex>
substance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponification</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*pon`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>saponification</ets>. See <er>Saponify</er>.]</ety> <def>The act,
process, or result, of soap making; conversion into soap; specifically
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, the decomposition of fats and other ethereal salts
by alkalies; <as>as, the <ex>saponification</ex> of ethyl
acetate</as>.</def><-- "ethereal salt" = ester --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponifier</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*pon"i*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>That which saponifies; any reagent used to cause
saponification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponify</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*pon"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Saponified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Saponifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>sapo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, soap + <ets>-fy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>saponifier</ets>.]</ety> <def>To convert into soap, as tallow or
any fat; hence <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, to subject to any similar process, as
that which ethereal salts undergo in decomposition; <as>as, to
<ex>saponify</ex> ethyl acetate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponin</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"o*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sapo</ets>,
<ets>-onis</ets> soap: cf. F. <ets>saponine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A poisonous glucoside found in many plants, as
in the root of soapwort (<spn>Saponaria officinalis</spn>), in the bark
of soap bark (<spn>Quillaja saponaria</spn>), etc. It is extracted as a
white amorphous powder, which produces a soapy lather in solution, and
produces a local anaesthesia. It is used as a detergent and for
emulsifying oils. Formerly called also <altname>struthiin</altname>,
<altname>quillaiin</altname>, <altname>senegin</altname>,
<altname>polygalic acid</altname>, etc. By extension, any one of a group
of related bodies of which saponin proper is the type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponite</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"o*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sw.
<ets>saponit</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sapo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>,
soap.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A hydrous silicate of magnesia and
alumina. It occurs in soft, soapy, amorphous masses, filling veins in
serpentine and cavities in trap rock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saponul</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"o*nul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>saponule</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sapo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>,
soap.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>A soapy mixture obtained by
treating an essential oil with an alkali; hence, any similar compound of
an essential oil.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>saponule</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapor</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"por</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Savor</er>.]</ety> <def>Power of affecting the organs of taste;
savor; flavor; taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is some <qex>sapor</qex> in all aliments.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saporific</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`o*rif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sapor</ets> taste + <ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety> <def>Having
the power to produce the sensation of taste; producing taste, flavor, or
relish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saporosity</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`o*ros"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
a body by which it excites the sensation of taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saporous</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saporus</ets> that relishes well, savory, fr. <ets>sapor</ets>
taste.]</ety> <def>Having flavor or taste; yielding a taste.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapota</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*po"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Sp.
<ets>sapote</ets>, <ets>zapote</ets>. See <er>Sapodilla</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The sapodilla.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapotaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`o*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a natural order (<ord>Sapotaceae</ord>) of
(mostly tropical) trees and shrubs, including the star apple, the
Lucuma, or natural marmalade tree, the gutta-percha tree
(<gen>Isonandra</gen>), and the India mahwa, as well as the sapodilla,
or sapota, after which the order is named.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sappan wood</ent><br/
<hw>Sap*pan" wood"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Sapan wood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sappare</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"pare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sappare</ets>; -- so called by Saussure.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Kyanite.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sappar</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapper</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sapeur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who saps; specifically
<fld>(Mil.)</fld>, one who is employed in working at saps, building and
repairing fortifications, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapphic</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"phic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sapphicus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ Sappho.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to Sappho, the Grecian poetess; <as>as,
<ex>Sapphic</ex> odes; <ex>Sapphic</ex> verse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Pros.)</fld> <def>Belonging to, or in the manner
of, Sappho; -- said of a certain kind of verse reputed to have been
invented by Sappho, consisting of five feet, of which the first, fourth,
and fifth are trochees, the second is a spondee, and the third a
dactyl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapphic</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"phic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Pros.)</fld> <def>A Sapphic
verse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapphire</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"phire</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>saphir</ets>, F. <ets>saphir</ets>, L. <ets>sapphirus</ets>, Gr.
<?/, of Oriental origin; cf. Heb. <ets>sapp<imac/r</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Native alumina or aluminium
sesquioxide, <chform>Al2O3</chform>; corundum; esp., the blue
transparent variety of corundum, highly prized as a gem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of rubies, <qex>sapphires</qex>, and of pearl<eacute/s white.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sapphire</xex> occurs in hexagonal crystals and
also in granular and massive forms. The name <xex>sapphire</xex> is
usually restricted to the blue crystals, while the bright red crystals
are called <xex>Oriental rubies</xex> (see under <er>Ruby</er>), the
amethystine variety <xex>Oriental amethyst</xex> (see under
<er>Amethyst</er>), and the dull massive varieties <xex>corundum</xex>
(a name which is also used as a general term to include all varieties).
See <er>Corundum</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The color of the gem; bright blue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any humming bird of the genus
<gen>Hylocharis</gen>, native of South America. The throat and breast
are usually bright blue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Star sapphire</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Asteriated sapphire</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a
kind of sapphire which exhibits asterism.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapphire</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"phire</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or resembling sapphire;
sapphirine; blue.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>sapphire</xex> blaze.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapphirine</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"phir*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Resembling
sapphire; made of sapphire; having the color, or any quality of
sapphire.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sapphirine</xex> degree of hardness.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sappho</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"pho</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sapphic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several
species of brilliant South American humming birds of the genus
<gen>Sappho</gen>, having very bright-colored and deeply forked tails;
-- called also <altname>firetail</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sappiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"pi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
being sappy; juiciness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sappodilla</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`po*dil"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sapodilla</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sappy</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sappier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sappiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From 1st <er>Sap</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding with sap; full of sap; juicy;
succulent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, young, not firm; weak, feeble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When he had passed this weak and <qex>sappy</qex> age.</q>
<rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Weak in intellect.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Abounding in sap; resembling, or
consisting largely of, sapwood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sappy</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sapy</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Cf. L. <ets>sapere</ets> to
taste.]</ety> <def>Musty; tainted.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saprophagan</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*proph"a*gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sapro`s</grk> rotten + <grk>fagei^n</grk> to eat: cf. F.
<ets>saprophage</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a tribe of
beetles which feed upon decaying animal and vegetable substances; a
carrion beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saprophagous</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*proph"a*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Feeding on carrion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saprophyte</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"ro*phyte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sapro`s</grk> rotten + <grk>fyto`n</grk> a plant.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant growing on decayed animal or vegetable
matter, as most fungi and some flowering plants with no green color, as
the Indian pipe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saprophytic</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`ro*phyt"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Feeding or
growing upon decaying animal or vegetable matter; pertaining to a
saprophyte or the saprophytes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saprophytism</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"ro*phyt*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State or fact
of being saprophytic.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapsago</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"sa*go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>schabzieger</ets>; <ets>schaben</ets> to shave, to scrape +
<ets>zieger</ets> a sort of whey.]</ety> <def>A kind of Swiss cheese, of
a greenish color, flavored with melilot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapskull</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"skull`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A saphead.</def>
<mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapucaia</ent><br/
<hw>Sap`u*ca"ia</hw> <pr>(?; Pg. <?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pg.
<ets>sapucaya</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Brazilian tree. See
<er>Lecythis</er>, and <er>Monkey-pot</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sapucaya</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sapucaia nut</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the seed of
the sapucaia; -- called also <altname>paradise
nut</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sapwood</ent><br/
<hw>Sap"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The alburnum, or part of the wood of any exogenous tree next to the
bark, being that portion of the tree through which the sap flows most
freely; -- distinguished from <contr>heartwood</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarabaite</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"a*ba*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>Saraba<ium/tae</ets>, pl.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One
of certain vagrant or heretical Oriental monks in the early
church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saraband</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"a*band</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sarabande</ets>, Sp. <ets>zarabanda</ets>, fr. Per.
<ets>serbend</ets> a song.]</ety> <def>A slow Spanish dance of Saracenic
origin, to an air in triple time; also, the air itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She has brought us the newest <qex>saraband</qex> from the court
of Queen Mab.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saracen</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"a*cen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Saracenus</ets> perhaps fr. Ar. <ets>sharqi</ets>, pl.
<ets>sharqi<imac/n</ets>, Oriental, Eastern, fr. <ets>sharaqa</ets> to
rise, said of the sun: cf. F. <ets>sarrasin</ets>. Cf.
<er>Sarcenet</er>, <er>Sarrasin</er>, <er>Sirocco</er>.]</ety>
<def>Anciently, an Arab; later, a Mussulman; in the Middle Ages, the
common term among Christians in Europe for a Mohammedan hostile to the
crusaders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saracens' consound</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
kind of ragwort (<spn>Senecio Saracenicus</spn>), anciently used to heal
wounds.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saracenical</ent><br/
<ent>Saracenic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar`a*cen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sar`a*cen"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Saracens; <as>as, <ex>Saracenic</ex> architecture</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Saracenic</xex> music.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarasin</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"a*sin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sarrasin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saraswati</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa`ras*wa"ti</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>Sarasvat<imac/</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Hind. Myth.)</fld> <def>The
sakti or wife of Brahma; the Hindoo goddess of learning, music, and
poetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcasm</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"casm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sarcasme</ets>, L. <ets>sarcasmus</ets>, Gr. <grk>sarkasmo`s</grk>,
from <grk>sarka`zein</grk> to tear flesh like dogs, to bite the lips in
rage, to speak bitterly, to sneer, fr. <grk>sa`rx</grk>,
<grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh.]</ety> <def>A keen, reproachful expression; a
satirical remark uttered with some degree of scorn or contempt; a taunt;
a gibe; a cutting jest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sarcasms</qex> of those critics who imagine our art to be
a matter of inspiration.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J. Reynolds.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Satire; irony; ridicule; taunt; gibe.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcasmous</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*cas"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sarcastic.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Sarcasmous</xex> scandal.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hubidras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcastical</ent><br/
<ent>Sarcastic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar*cas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sar*cas"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Expressing, or expressed by,
sarcasm; characterized by, or of the nature of, sarcasm; given to the
use of sarcasm; bitterly satirical; scornfully severe;
taunting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What a fierce and <qex>sarcastic</qex> reprehension would this
have drawn from the friendship of the world!</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcastically</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*cas"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sarcastic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcel</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"cel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>cercel</ets>, F. <ets>cerceau</ets>, L. <ets>circellus</ets>, dim.
of <ets>circulus</ets>. See <er>Circle</er>.]</ety> <def>One of the
outer pinions or feathers of the wing of a bird, esp. of a
hawk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarceled</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"celed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(her.)</fld>
<def>Cut through the middle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcelle</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar`celle"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>querquedula</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The old squaw, or
long-tailed duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcenet</ent><br/
<hw>Sarce"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>sarcenet</ets>; cf. LL. <ets>saracenicum</ets> cloth made by
Saracens. See <er>Saracen</er>.]</ety> <def>A species of fine thin silk
fabric, used for linings, etc.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sarsenet</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou green <qex>sarcenet</qex> flap for a sore eye.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcin</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"cin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Hypoxanthin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*ci"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ of flesh, fr. <grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A genus of bacteria found in various organic
fluids, especially in those those of the stomach, associated with
certain diseases. The individual organisms undergo division along two
perpendicular partitions, so that multiplication takes place in two
directions, giving groups of four cubical cells. Also used adjectively;
<as>as, a <ex>sarcina</ex> micrococcus; a <ex>sarcina</ex>
group</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sarcina form</b></col> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>the tetrad
form seen in the division of a dumb-bell group of micrococci into four;
-- applied particularly to bacteria. See
<er>micrococcus</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcle</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sarcler</ets> to weed, fr. L. <ets>sarculare</ets> to hoe, fr.
<ets>sarculum</ets> hoe.]</ety> <def>To weed, or clear of weeds, with a
hoe.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarco-</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"co-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form from Gr.
<grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, <xex>flesh</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>sarco</ex>phagous, flesh-eating; <ex>sarco</ex>logy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcobasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*cob"a*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sarcobases</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh + <?/ base.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A fruit consisting of many dry indehiscent cells,
which contain but few seeds and cohere about a common style, as in the
mallows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcoblast</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"co*blast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sarco-</ets> + <ets>-blast</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A minute yellowish body present in the interior of certain
rhizopods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcocarp</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"co*carp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sarco-</ets>
+ Gr. <?/ fruit: cf. F. <ets>sarcocarpe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The fleshy part of a stone fruit, situated between the skin, or
epicarp, and the stone, or endocarp, as in a peach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term has also been used to denote any fruit which is
fleshy throughout.</note> <rj><au>M. T. Masters.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcocele</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"co*cele</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh + <?/ tumor: cf. F.
<ets>sarcoc<egrave/le</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Any solid
tumor of the testicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcocolla</ent><br/
<ent>Sarcocol</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar"co*col</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sar`co*col"la</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sarcocolla</ets>, from
Gr. <?/; <grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh + <?/ glue: cf. F.
<ets>sarcocolle</ets>.]</ety> <def>A gum resin obtained from certain
shrubs of Africa (<gen>Penaea</gen>), -- formerly thought to cause
healing of wounds and ulcers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcode</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"code</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ fleshy;
<grk>sa`rx</grk>, flesh + <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form. Cf.
<er>Sarcoid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A name applied by
Dujardin in 1835 to the gelatinous material forming the bodies of the
lowest animals; protoplasm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sarcoderma</ent><br/
<ent>Sarcoderm</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar"co*derm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>sar`co*der"ma</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>sarcoderma</ets>. See
<er>Sarco-</er>, and <er>Derm</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A fleshy covering of a seed, lying between the
external and internal integuments.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
sarcocarp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcodic</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*cod"ic</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to sarcode.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/. See
<er>Sarcode</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Resembling flesh, or
muscle; composed of sarcode.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcolactic</ent><br/
<hw>Sar`co*lac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sarco-</ets> + <ets>lactic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Relating to muscle and milk; <as>as, <ex>sarcolactic
acid</ex></as>. See <cref>Lactic acid</cref>, under
<er>Lactic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcolemma</ent><br/
<hw>Sar`co*lem"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh + <?/ rind, skin.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The very thin transparent and apparently
homogeneous sheath which incloses a striated muscular fiber; the
myolemma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcoline</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"co*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Flesh-colored.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcological</ent><br/
<ent>Sarcologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar`co*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sar`co*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
sarcology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcology</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*col"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sarco-</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sarcologie</ets>.]</ety> <def>That part of anatomy which treats of
the soft parts. It includes myology, angiology, neurology, and
splanchnology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*co"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Sarcomata</plw> <pr>(# <or/ #)</pr>, E. <plw>sarcomas</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/, from <grk>sa`rx</grk>,
<grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A tumor of
fleshy consistence; -- formerly applied to many varieties of tumor, now
restricted to a variety of malignant growth made up of cells resembling
those of fetal development without any proper intercellular
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcomatous</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*com"a*tous</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to sarcoma; resembling
sarcoma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcophaga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*coph"a*ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
neut. pl. See <er>Sarcophagus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
suborder of carnivorous and insectivorous marsupials including the
dasyures and the opossums.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcophaga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*coph"a*ga</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fem. sing. See
<er>Sarcophagus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of Diptera,
including the flesh flies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcophagan</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*coph"a*gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any animal which eats flesh, especially any
carnivorous marsupial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any fly of the genus
Sarcophaga.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcophagous</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*coph"a*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Feeding on flesh; flesh-eating; carnivorous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcophagus</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*coph"a*gus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
L. <plw>Sarcophagi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Sarcophaguses</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>sarkofa`gos</grk>, properly,
eating flesh; <grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh +
<grk>fagei^n</grk> to eat. Cf. <er>Sarcasm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A species of limestone used among the Greeks for making coffins,
which was so called because it consumed within a few weeks the flesh of
bodies deposited in it. It is otherwise called <altname>lapis
Assius</altname>, or <altname>Assian stone</altname>, and is said to
have been found at Assos, a city of Lycia.</def>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A coffin or chest-shaped tomb of the kind of stone
described above; hence, any stone coffin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A stone shaped like a sarcophagus and placed by a
grave as a memorial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcophagy</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*coph"a*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sarkofagi`a</grk>. See <er>Sarcophagus</er>.]</ety> <def>The
practice of eating flesh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcophile</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"co*phile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sacro-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a lover.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A flesh-eating animal, especially any one of the carnivorous
marsupials.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcoptes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*cop"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh + <grk>ko`ptein</grk> to
cut.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of parasitic mites including
the itch mites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcoptid</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*cop"tid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of the genus <gen>Sarcoptes</gen> and related genera of
mites, comprising the itch mites and mange mites.</def> --
<def2><pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the itch
mites.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcorhamphi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar`co*rham"phi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh + <?/ beak.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of raptorial birds comprising the
vultures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcoseptum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar`co*sep"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sarcosepta</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[<ets>Sarco-</ets> + <ets>septum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the mesenteries of an anthozoan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcosin</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"co*sin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A crystalline nitrogenous substance, formed in the
decomposition of creatin (one of the constituents of muscle tissue).
Chemically, it is methyl glycocoll.</def><-- N-Methylglycine, C3H7NO2.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*co"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Abnormal formation of flesh.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Sarcoma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcotic</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*cot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>sarcotique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Producing or
promoting the growth of flesh.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A sarcotic medicine.</def></def2>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarcous</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sa`rx</grk>, <grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Fleshy; -- applied to the minute structural elements, called
<xex>sarcous elements</xex>, or <xex>sarcous disks</xex>, of which
striated muscular fiber is composed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarculation</ent><br/
<hw>Sar`cu*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sarculatio</ets>. See <er>Sarcle</er>.]</ety> <def>A weeding, as
with a hoe or a rake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1277 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Sard</ent><br/
<hw>Sard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sarda</ets>, Gr.
<?/, or <?/ (sc. <?/), i.e., Sardian stone, fr. <?/ Sardian, <?/ Sardes,
the capital of Lydia: cf. F. <ets>sarde</ets>. Cf.
<er>Sardius</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of carnelian,
of a rich reddish yellow or brownish red color. See the Note under
<xex>Chalcedony</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardachate</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"da*chate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sardachates</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sardachate</ets>. See <er>Sard</er>,
and <er>Agate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of agate
containing sard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardel</ent><br/
<ent>Sardan</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar"dan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sar"del</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>sardella</ets>. See
<er>Sardine</er> a fish.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sardine.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardel</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"del</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A precious stone. See
<er>Sardius</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardine</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"dine</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sardine</ets> (cf. Sp. <ets>sardina</ets>, <ets>sarda</ets>, It.
<ets>sardina</ets>, <ets>sardella</ets>), L. <ets>sardina</ets>,
<ets>sarda</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/, <?/; so called from the island of
<etsep>Sardinia</etsep>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one
of several small species of herring which are commonly preserved in
olive oil for food, especially the pilchard, or European sardine
(<spn>Clupea pilchardus</spn>). The California sardine (<spn>Clupea
sagax</spn>) is similar. The American sardines of the Atlantic coast are
mostly the young of the common herring and of the menhaden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardine</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"dine</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sardius</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardinian</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*din"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sardinianus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the island,
kingdom, or people of Sardinia.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
native or inhabitant of Sardinia.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardius</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"di*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sardius</ets>, <ets>lapis sardinus</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/, <?/. See
<er>Sard</er>.]</ety> <def>A precious stone, probably a carnelian, one
of which was set in Aaron's breastplate.</def> <rj><au>Ex. xxviii.
17.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardoin</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"doin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sardoine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Sard;
carnelian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardonian</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*do"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sardonien</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sardonic.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/With <xex>Sardonian</xex> smile.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*don"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sardonique</ets>, L. <ets>sardonius</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/, perhaps
fr. <?/ to grin like a dog, or from a certain plant of
<ets>Sardinia</ets>, Gr. <?/, which was said to screw up the face of the
eater.]</ety> <def>Forced; unnatural; insincere; hence, derisive,
mocking, malignant, or bitterly sarcastic; -- applied only to a laugh,
smile, or some facial semblance of gayety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where strained, <qex>sardonic</qex> smiles are glozing still,<br/
And grief is forced to laugh against her will.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H.
Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The scornful, ferocious, <qex>sardonic</qex> grin of a bloody
ruffian.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sardonic grin</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sardonic laugh</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an old medical term for a
spasmodic affection of the muscles of the face, giving it an appearance
of laughter.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*don"ic</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
resembling, a kind of linen made at Colchis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sardonyx</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"do*nyx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/.
See <er>Sard</er>, and <er>Onyx</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
variety of onyx consisting of sard and white chalcedony in alternate
layers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saree</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"ree</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind. <?/.]</ety>
<def>The principal garment of a Hindoo woman. It consists of a long
piece of cloth, which is wrapped round the middle of the body, a portion
being arranged to hang down in front, and the remainder passed across
the bosom over the left shoulder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sargasso</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*gas"so</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>sargazo</ets> seaweed.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The gulf weed.
See under <er>Gulf</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sargasso Sea</b></col>, <cd>a large tract of the North
Atlantic Ocean where sargasso in great abundance floats on the
surface.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sargassum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*gas"sum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>A genus of algae including the gulf weed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sargo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>sargo</ets>, L. <ets>sargus</ets> a kind of fish.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of sparoid fishes
belonging to <gen>Sargus</gen>, <gen>Pomadasys</gen>, and related
genera; -- called also <altname>sar</altname>, and
<altname>saragu</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sari</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"ri</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Saree</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarigue</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*rigue"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from Braz.
<ets><cced/arigueia</ets>, <ets><cced/arigueira</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small South American opossum (<spn>Didelphys
opossum</spn>), having four white spots on the face.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sark</ent><br/
<hw>Sark</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>serce</ets>,
<ets>syrce</ets>, a shirt; akin to Icel. <ets>serkr</ets>, Sw.
<ets>s<aum/rk</ets>.]</ety> <def>A shirt.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sark</ent><br/
<hw>Sark</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>To cover with
sarking, or thin boards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarkin</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>sa`rx</grk>,
<grk>sa`rkos</grk>, flesh.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>Hypoxanthin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarking</ent><br/
<hw>Sark"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Sark</er>
shirt.]</ety> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>Thin boards for sheathing, as
above the rafters, and under the shingles or slates, and for similar
purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarlyk</ent><br/
<ent>Sarlac</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar"lac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sar"lyk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Mongolian <ets>sarlyk</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The yak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarmatic</ent><br/
<ent>Sarmatian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar*ma"tian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sar*mat"ic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sarmaticus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Sarmatia, or its
inhabitants, the ancestors of the Russians and the Poles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarment</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sarmentum</ets> a twig, fr. <ets>sarpere</ets> to cut off, to trim:
cf. F. <ets>sarment</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A prostrate
filiform stem or runner, as of the strawberry. See
<er>Runner</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarmentaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sar`men*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Bearing sarments, or runners, as the strawberry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarmentose</ent><br/
<hw>Sar`men*tose"</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sarmentosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sarmenteux</ets>. See
<er>Sarment</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Long and
filiform, and almost naked, or having only leaves at the joints where it
strikes root; <as>as, a <ex>sarmentose</ex> stem</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Bearing sarments; sarmentaceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarmentous</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*men"tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Sarmentose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarn</ent><br/
<hw>Sarn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[W. <ets>sarn</ets> a
causeway, paving.]</ety> <def>A pavement or stepping-stone.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarong</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa"rong</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Malay
<ets>s<amac/rung</ets>.]</ety> <def>A sort of petticoat worn by both
sexes in Java and the Malay Archipelago.</def> <rj><au>Balfour (Cyc. of
India)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saros</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ros</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/]</ety> <fld>(Astron)</fld> <def>A Chaldean astronomical period or
cycle, the length of which has been variously estimated from 3,600 years
to 3,600 days, or a little short of 10 years.</def> <rj><au>Brande &
C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- A length of time (6535.82 days, or 18 years 11.32 days, assuming
4 leap years in that interval), after which the eclipses of the sun
repeat their pattern, but are shifted 120<deg/ west.. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarplar</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"plar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>sarplare</ets>. See <er>Sarplier</er>.]</ety> <def>A large bale or
package of wool, containing eighty tods, or 2,240 pounds, in
weight.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarplier</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"plier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>serpilli<egrave/re</ets>; cf. Pr. <ets>sarpelheira</ets>, LL.
<ets>serpelleria</ets>, <ets>serpleria</ets>, Catalan
<ets>sarpallera</ets>, Sp. <ets>arpillera</ets>.]</ety> <def>A coarse
cloth made of hemp, and used for packing goods, etc.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sarpelere</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Tyrwhitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarpo</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"po</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corruption of Sp.
<ets>sapo</ets> a toad.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large toadfish
of the Southern United States and the Gulf of Mexico (<spn>Batrachus
tau</spn>, var. <var>pardus</var>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarracenia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar`ra*ce"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
So named after a Dr. <ets>Sarrazin</ets> of Quebec.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of American perennial herbs growing in
bogs; the American pitcher plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They have hollow pitcher-shaped or tubular leaves, and
solitary flowers with an umbrella-shaped style. <spn>Sarracenia
purpurea</spn>, the sidesaddle flower, is common at the North;
<spn>Sarracenia flava</spn>, <spn>Sarracenia rubra</spn>,
<spn>Sarracenia Drummondii</spn>, <spn>Sarracenia variolaris</spn>, and
<spn>Sarracenia psittacina</spn> are Southern species. All are
insectivorous, catching and drowning insects in their curious leaves.
See <er>Illust</er>. of Sidesaddle flower, under
<er>Sidesaddle</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarrasine</ent><br/
<ent>Sarrasin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sar"ra*sin</hw>, <hw>Sar"ra*sine</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sarrasine</ets>, LL. <ets>saracina</ets>.
See <er>Saracen</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A portcullis, or
herse.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sarasin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarsa</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"sa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sarsaparilla.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sarza</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarsaparilla</ent><br/
<hw>Sar`sa*pa*ril"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>zarzaparrilla</ets>; <ets>zarza</ets> a bramble (perhaps fr. Bisc.
<ets>zartzia</ets>) + <ets>parra</ets> a vine, or <ets>Parillo</ets>, a
physician said to have discovered it.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any plant of several tropical American species of
<gen>Smilax</gen>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The bitter mucilaginous roots
of such plants, used in medicine and in sirups for soda, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is also applied to many other plants and their
roots, especially to the <spn>Aralia nudicaulis</spn>, the wild
sarsaparilla of the United States.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarsaparillin</ent><br/
<hw>Sar`sa*pa*ril"lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Parillin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarse</ent><br/
<hw>Sarse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sas</ets>, OF.
<ets>saas</ets>, LL. <ets>setatium</ets>, fr. L. <ets>seta</ets> a stiff
hair.]</ety> <def>A fine sieve; a searce.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarse</ent><br/
<hw>Sarse</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To sift through a sarse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarsen</ent><br/
<hw>Sar"sen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain;
perhaps for <ets>saracen stone</ets>, i.e., a heathen or pagan stone or
monument.]</ety> <def>One of the large sandstone blocks scattered over
the English chalk downs; -- called also <altname>sarsen stone</altname>,
and <altname>Druid stone</altname>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarsenet</ent><br/
<hw>Sarse"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sarcenet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sart</ent><br/
<hw>Sart</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An assart, or
clearing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sartorial</ent><br/
<hw>Sar*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sartorius</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a tailor
or his work.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our legs skulked under the table as free from <qex>sartorial</qex>
impertinences as those of the noblest savages.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the sartorius
muscle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sartorius</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sar*to"ri*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>sartor</ets> a patcher, tailor, fr. <ets>sarcire</ets>,
<ets>sartum</ets>, to patch, mend.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
muscle of the thigh, called the <xex>tailor's muscle</xex>, which arises
from the hip bone and is inserted just below the knee. So named because
its contraction was supposed to produce the position of the legs assumed
by the tailor in sitting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sarum use</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"rum use`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Ch. of Eng.)</fld> <def>A
liturgy, or <xex>use</xex>, put forth about 1087 by St. Osmund, bishop
of <xex>Sarum</xex>, based on Anglo-Saxon and Norman customs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sash</ent><br/
<hw>Sash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pers. <ets>shast</ets> a
sort of girdle.]</ety> <def>A scarf or band worn about the waist, over
the shoulder, or otherwise; a belt; a girdle, -- worn by women and
children as an ornament; also worn as a badge of distinction by military
officers, members of societies, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sash</ent><br/
<hw>Sash</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To adorn with a sash or
scarf.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sash</ent><br/
<hw>Sash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>ch<acir/ssis</ets> a frame,
sash, fr. <ets>ch<acir/sse</ets> a shrine, reliquary, frame, L.
<ets>capsa</ets>. See <er>Case</er> a box.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
framing in which the panes of glass are set in a glazed window or door,
including the narrow bars between the panes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In a sawmill, the rectangular frame in which the saw
is strained and by which it is carried up and down with a reciprocating
motion; -- also called <altname>gate</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>French sash</b></col>, <cd>a casement swinging on hinges;
-- in distinction from a <xex>vertical sash</xex> sliding up and
down.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sash</ent><br/
<hw>Sash</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sashed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sashing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To furnish with a sash or sashes;
<as>as, to <ex>sash</ex> a door or a window</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sashery</ent><br/
<hw>Sash"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From 1st
<er>Sash</er>.]</ety> <def>A collection of sashes; ornamentation by
means of sashes.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Distinguished by their <qex>sasheries</qex> and insignia.</q>
<rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sashoon</ent><br/
<hw>Sash"oon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymology
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A kind of pad worn on the leg under the
boot.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Nares.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sasin</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"sin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
Indian antelope (<spn>Antilope bezoartica</spn> syn. <spn>Antilope
cervicapra</spn>), noted for its beauty and swiftness. It has long,
spiral, divergent horns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sassabye</ent><br/
<ent>Sassaby</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sas"sa*by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sas"sa*bye</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large
African antelope (<spn>Alcelaphus lunata</spn>), similar to the
hartbeest, but having its horns regularly curved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sassafras</ent><br/
<hw>Sas"sa*fras</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sassafras</ets> (cf. It. <ets>sassafrasso</ets>,
<ets>sassafras</ets>, Sp. <ets>sasafras</ets>, <ets>salsafras</ets>,
<ets>salsifrax</ets>, <ets>salsifragia</ets>, <ets>saxifragia</ets>),
fr. L. <ets>saxifraga</ets> saxifrage. See <er>Saxifrage</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An American tree of the Laurel family
(<spn>Sassafras officinale</spn>); also, the bark of the roots, which
has an aromatic smell and taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Australian sassafras</b></col>, <cd>a lofty tree
(<spn>Doryophora Sassafras</spn>) with aromatic bark and leaves.</cd> --
<col><b>Chilian sassafras</b></col>, <cd>an aromatic tree (<spn>Laurelia
sempervirens</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>New Zealand sassafras</b></col>,
<cd>a similar tree (<spn>Laurelia Novae Zelandiae</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Sassafras nut</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Pichurim bean</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Swamp sassafras</b></col>, <cd>the sweet bay (<spn>Magnolia
glauca</spn>). See <er>Magnolia</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sassanage</ent><br/
<hw>Sas"sa*nage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sarse</er> a sieve.]</ety> <def>Stones left after sifting.</def>
<rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sassarara</ent><br/
<hw>Sas`sa*ra"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perh. a
corruption of <ets>certiorari</ets>, the name of a writ.]</ety> <def>A
word used to emphasize a statement.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Out she shall pack, with a <qex>sassarara</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sasse</ent><br/
<hw>Sasse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>sas</ets>, fr.
F. <ets>sas</ets> the basin of a waterfall.]</ety> <def>A sluice or
lock, as in a river, to make it more navigable.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Pepys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sassenach</ent><br/
<hw>Sas"sen*ach</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>sasunnach</ets>.]</ety> <def>A Saxon; an Englishman; a
Lowlander.</def> <mark>[Celtic]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sassoline</ent><br/
<ent>Sassolin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sas"so*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sas"so*line</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>Sasso</ets>, a town
in Italy: cf. F. <ets>sassolin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Native boric acid, found in saline incrustations on the borders of
hot springs near Sasso, in the territory of Florence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sassorolla</ent><br/
<ent>Sassorol</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sas"so*rol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sas`so*rol"la</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The rock
pigeon. See under <er>Pigeon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sassy bark</ent><br/
<hw>Sas"sy bark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The bark of a
West African leguminous tree (<spn>Erythrophlaeum Guineense</spn>, used
by the natives as an ordeal poison, and also medicinally; -- called also
<altname>mancona bark</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sastra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sas"tra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Shaster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sastrugi</ent><br/
<hw>Sas*tru"gi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Incorrect, but common, var. of
<er>Zastrugi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sat</ent><br/
<hw>Sat</hw> <pr>(s<acr/t)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Sit</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sate</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satan</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"tan</hw> <pr>(s<amac/"t<ait/n, s<acr/t"<ait/n <mark>obs</mark>
)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb. <ets>s<amac/t<amac/n</ets> an
adversary, fr. <ets>s<amac/tan</ets> to be adverse, to persecute: cf.
Gr. <grk>Sata^n</grk>, <grk>Satana^s</grk>, L. <ets>Satan</ets>,
<ets>Satanas</ets>.]</ety> <def>The grand adversary of man; the Devil,
or Prince of darkness; the chief of the fallen angels; the
archfiend.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I beheld <qex>Satan</qex> as lightning fall from heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>Luke x. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satanical</ent><br/
<ent>Satanic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sa*tan"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sa*tan"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>satanique</ets>,
Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Satan; having the qualities of
Satan; resembling Satan; extremely malicious or wicked; devilish;
infernal.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Satanic</xex> strength.<rdquo/
<ldquo/<xex>Satanic</xex> host.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Detest the slander which, with a <qex>Satanic</qex> smile, exults
over the character it has ruined.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. T.
Dwight.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sa*tan"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sa*tan"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satanism</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"tan*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
evil and malicious disposition of Satan; a diabolical spirit.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The worship of Satan.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satanist</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"tan*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A very wicked
person.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Granger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A worshiper of Satan.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satanophany</ent><br/
<hw>Sa`tan*oph"a*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Satan</ets> + Gr. <?/ to appear.]</ety> <def>An incarnation
of Satan; a being possessed by a demon.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>O. A. Brownson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satchel</ent><br/
<hw>Satch"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>sachel</ets>, fr. L. <ets>saccellus</ets>, dim. of
<ets>saccus</ets>. See <er>Sack</er> a bag.]</ety> <def>A little sack or
bag for carrying papers, books, or small articles of wearing apparel; a
hand bag.</def> <altsp>[Spelled also <asp>sachel</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The whining schoolboy with his <qex>satchel</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sate</ent><br/
<hw>Sate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably shortened fr.
<ets>satiate</ets>: cf. L. <ets>satur</ets> full. See
<er>Satiate</er>.]</ety> <def>To satisfy the desire or appetite of; to
satiate; to glut; to surfeit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Crowds of wanderers <qex>sated</qex> with the business and
pleasure of great cities.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sate</ent><br/
<hw>Sate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Sit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But <qex>sate</qex> an equal guest at every board.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sateen</ent><br/
<hw>Sat*een"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Satin</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of dress goods made of cotton or
woolen, with a glossy surface resembling satin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sateless</ent><br/
<hw>Sate"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Insatiable.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satellite</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"el*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>satelles</ets>, <ets>-itis</ets>, an attendant.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>An attendant attached to a prince or other powerful person; hence,
an obsequious dependent.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>satellites</xex> of
power.<rdquo/ <rj><au>I. Disraeli.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A secondary planet which
revolves about another planet; <as>as, the moon is a <ex>satellite</ex>
of the earth</as>. See <cref>Solar system</cref>, under
<er>Solar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Satellite moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
handsome European noctuid moth (<spn>Scopelosoma
satellitia</spn>).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satellite</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"el*lite</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated
near; accompanying; <as>as, the <ex>satellite</ex> veins, those which
accompany the arteries</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satellitious</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`el*li"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to,
or consisting of, satellites.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cheyne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sathanas</ent><br/
<hw>Sath"an*as</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Satanas</ets>. See <er>Satan</er>]</ety> <def>Satan.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer. Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satiate</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ti*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>satiatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>satiare</ets> to satisfy, from
<ets>sat</ets>, <ets>satis</ets>, enough. See <er>Sad</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Sate</er>.]</ety> <def>Filled to satiety;
glutted; sated; -- followed by <xex>with</xex> or <xex>of</xex>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Satiate</xex> of applause.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satiate</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"ti*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Satiated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Satiating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To satisfy
the appetite or desire of; to feed to the full; to furnish enjoyment to,
to the extent of desire; to sate; <as>as, to <ex>satiate</ex> appetite
or sense</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These [smells] rather woo the sense than <qex>satiate</qex>
it.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I may yet survive the malice of my enemies, although they should
be <qex>satiated</qex> with my blood.</q> <rj><qau>Eikon
Basilike.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To full beyond natural desire; to gratify to
repletion or loathing; to surfeit; to glut.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To saturate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir
I. Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To satisfy; sate; suffice; cloy; gorge; overfill;
surfeit; glut.</syn> -- <usage><er>Satiate</er>, <er>Satisfy</er>,
<er>Content</er>. These words differ principally in degree. To
<xex>content</xex> is to make contented, even though every desire or
appetite is not fully gratified. To <xex>satisfy</xex> is to appease
fully the longings of desire. To <xex>satiate</xex> is to fill so
completely that it is not possible to receive or enjoy more; hence, to
overfill; to cause disgust in.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Content</qex> with science in the vale of peace.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His whole felicity is endless strife;<br/
No peace, no <qex>satisfaction</qex>, crowns his life.</q>
<rj><qau>Beaumont.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He may be <qex>satiated</qex>, but not <qex>satisfied</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Norris.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satiation</ent><br/
<hw>Sa`ti*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Satiety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satiety</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*ti"e*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>satietas</ets>, from <ets>satis</ets>, <ets>sat</ets>, enough: cf.
F. <ets>sati<eacute/t<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state of being
satiated or glutted; fullness of gratification, either of the appetite
or of any sensual desire; fullness beyond desire; an excess of
gratification which excites wearisomeness or loathing; repletion;
satiation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In all pleasures there is <qex>satiety</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hakewill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But thy words, with grace divine<br/
Imbued, bring to their sweetness no <qex>satiety</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Repletion; satiation; surfeit;
cloyment.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1278 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Satin</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>satin</ets>
(cf. Pg. <ets>setim</ets>), fr. It. <ets>setino</ets>, from
<ets>seta</ets> silk, L. <ets>saeta</ets>, <ets>seta</ets>, a thick,
stiff hair, a bristle; or possibly ultimately of Chinese origin; cf.
Chin. <ets>sz-t<uum/n</ets>, <ets>sz-twan</ets>. Cf.
<er>Sateen</er>.]</ety> <def>A silk cloth, of a thick, close texture,
and overshot woof, which has a glossy surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cloths of gold and <qex>satins</qex> rich of hue.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Denmark satin</b></col>, <cd>a kind of lasting; a stout
worsted stuff, woven with a satin twill, used for women's shoes.</cd> --
<col><b>Farmer's satin</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Farmer</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Satin bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an Australian bower
bird. Called also <altname>satin grackle</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Satin flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Honesty</er>, 4.</cd> -- <col><b>Satin spar</b></col>.
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A fine fibrous variety of calcite,
having a pearly luster</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A similar variety of
gypsum.</cd> -- <col><b>Satin sparrow</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the shining flycatcher (<spn>Myiagra nitida</spn>) of Tasmania and
Australia. The upper surface of the male is rich blackish green with a
metallic luster.</cd> -- <col><b>Satin stone</b></col>, <cd>satin
spar.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satinet</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`i*net"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>satin</ets>. See <er>Satin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thin
kind of satin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of cloth made of cotton warp and woolen
filling, used chiefly for trousers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satinette</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`i*nette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of a breed of
fancy frilled pigeons allied to the owls and turbits, having the body
white, the shoulders tricolored, and the tail bluish black with a large
white spot on each feather.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satin weave</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"in weave</hw>. <def>A style of weaving producing smooth-faced
fabric in which the warp interlaces with the filling at points
distributed over the surface.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satinwood</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"in*wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The hard, lemon-colored, fragrant wood of an East Indian tree
(<spn>Chloroxylon Swietenia</spn>). It takes a lustrous finish, and is
used in cabinetwork. The name is also given to the wood of a species of
prickly ash (<spn>Xanthoxylum Caribaeum</spn>) growing in Florida and
the West Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satiny</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"in*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like or composed of
satin; glossy; <as>as, to have a <ex>satiny</ex> appearance; a
<ex>satiny</ex> texture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sation</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>satio</ets>,
fr. <ets>serere</ets>, <ets>satum</ets>, to sow.]</ety> <def>A sowing or
planting.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satire</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"ire</hw> <pr>(?; <xex>in Eng. often</xex> <?/; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>satira</ets>, <ets>satura</ets>, fr.
<ets>satura</ets> (sc. <ets>lanx</ets>) a dish filled with various kinds
of fruits, food composed of various ingredients, a mixture, a medley,
fr. <ets>satur</ets> full of food, sated, fr. <ets>sat</ets>,
<ets>satis</ets>, enough: cf. F. <ets>satire</ets>. See <er>Sate</er>,
<er>Sad</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Saturate</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A composition, generally poetical, holding up vice or
folly to reprobation; a keen or severe exposure of what in public or
private morals deserves rebuke; an invective poem; <as>as, the
<ex>Satires</ex> of Juvenal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Keenness and severity of remark; caustic exposure to
reprobation; trenchant wit; sarcasm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Lampoon; sarcasm; irony; ridicule; pasquinade;
burlesque; wit; humor.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satirical</ent><br/
<ent>Satiric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sa*tir"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sa*tir"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>satiricus</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>satirique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
satire; of the nature of satire; <as>as, a <ex>satiric</ex>
style</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Censorious; severe in language; sarcastic;
insulting.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Satirical</xex> rogue.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Cutting; caustic; poignant; sarcastic; ironical;
bitter; reproachful; abusive.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sa*tir"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sa*tir"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satirist</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"ir*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>satiriste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who satirizes; especially, one who
writes satire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mighty <qex>satirist</qex>, who . . . had spread terror
through the Whig ranks.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satirize</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"ir*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Satirized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Satirizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>satiriser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make the object of satire; to
attack with satire; to censure with keenness or severe
sarcasm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is as hard to <qex>satirize</qex> well a man of distinguished
vices, as to praise well a man of distinguished virtues.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satisfaction</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`is*fac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>satisfaccioun</ets>, F. <ets>satisfaction</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>satisfactio</ets>, fr. <ets>satisfacere</ets> to satisfy. See
<er>Satisfy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of satisfying, or the
state of being satisfied; gratification of desire; contentment in
possession and enjoyment; repose of mind resulting from compliance with
its desires or demands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind having a power to suspend the execution and
<qex>satisfaction</qex> of any of its desires.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Settlement of a claim, due, or demand; payment;
indemnification; adequate compensation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We shall make full <qex>satisfaction</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which satisfies or gratifies;
atonement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Die he, or justice must; unless for him<br/
Some other, able, and as willing, pay<br/
The rigid <qex>satisfaction</qex>, death for death.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Contentment; content; gratification; pleasure;
recompense; compensation; amends; remuneration; indemnification;
atonement.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satisfactive</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`is*fac"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Satisfactory.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Satisfactive</qex> discernment of fish.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satisfactory</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`is*fac"to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>satisfactoire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Giving or producing
satisfaction; yielding content; especially, relieving the mind from
doubt or uncertainty, and enabling it to rest with confidence;
sufficient; <as>as, a <ex>satisfactory</ex> account or
explanation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Making amends, indemnification, or recompense;
causing to cease from claims and to rest content; compensating; atoning;
<as>as, to make <ex>satisfactory</ex> compensation, or a
<ex>satisfactory</ex> apology</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A most wise and sufficient means of redemption and salvation, by
the <qex>satisfactory</qex> and meritorious death and obedience of the
incarnate Son of God, Jesus Christ.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Sanderson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sat`is*fac"to*ri*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Sat`is*fac"to*ri*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satisfiable</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"is*fi`a*ble</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
satisfied.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satisfier</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"is*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
satisfies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satisfy</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"is*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Satisfied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Satisfying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>satisfier</ets>; L. <ets>satis</ets> enough + <ets>-ficare</ets>
(in comp.) to make; cf. F. <ets>satisfaire</ets>, L.
<ets>satisfacere</ets>. See <er>Sad</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and
<er>Fact</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In general, to fill up the
measure of a want of (a person or a thing); hence, to gratify fully the
desire of; to make content; to supply to the full, or so far as to give
contentment with what is wished for.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Death shall . . . with us two<br/
Be forced to <qex>satisfy</qex> his ravenous maw.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pay to the extent of claims or deserts; to give
what is due to; <as>as, to <ex>satisfy</ex> a creditor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To answer or discharge, as a claim, debt, legal
demand, or the like; to give compensation for; to pay off; to requite;
<as>as, to <ex>satisfy</ex> a claim or an execution</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To free from doubt, suspense, or uncertainty; to
give assurance to; to set at rest the mind of; to convince; <as>as, to
<ex>satisfy</ex> one's self by inquiry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The standing evidences of the truth of the gospel are in
themselves most firm, solid, and <qex>satisfying</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To satiate; sate; content; grafity; compensate.
See <er>Satiate</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satisfy</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"is*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
give satisfaction; to afford gratification; to leave nothing to be
desired.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make payment or atonement; to atone.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satisfyingly</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"is*fy`ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>So as to
satisfy; satisfactorily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sative</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sativus</ets>, fr. <ets>serere</ets>, satum, to sow.]</ety>
<def>Sown; propagated by seed.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satle</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To
settle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satrap</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"trap</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>satrapes</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. OPers. <ets>khshatrap<amac/van</ets>
ruler: cf. F. <ets>satrape</ets>.]</ety> <def>The governor of a province
in ancient Persia; hence, a petty autocrat despot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satrapal</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"trap*al</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a satrap, or a satrapy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satrapess</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"trap*ess</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female
satrap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satrapical</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*trap"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Satrapal.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satrapy</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"trap*y</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Satrapies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>satrapia</ets>,
<ets>satrapea</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>satrapie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The government or jurisdiction of a satrap; a principality.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satsuma ware</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"su*ma ware"</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>. <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld>
<def>A kind of ornamental hard-glazed pottery made at Satsuma in Kiushu,
one of the Japanese islands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturable</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"u*ra*ble</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saturabilis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>saturable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable
of being saturated; admitting of saturation.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sat`u*ra*bil"i*ty</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturant</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"u*rant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saturans</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Saturate</er>.]</ety>
<def>Impregnating to the full; saturating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturant</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"u*rant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
substance used to neutralize or saturate the affinity of another
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An antacid, as magnesia, used to
correct acidity of the stomach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturate</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"u*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Saturated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Saturating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>saturatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>saturare</ets> to saturate, fr.
<ets>satur</ets> full of food, sated. See <er>Satire</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to become completely penetrated, impregnated,
or soaked; to fill fully; to sate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Innumerable flocks and herds covered that vast expanse of emerald
meadow <qex>saturated</qex> with the moisture of the Atlantic.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fill and <qex>saturate</qex> each kind<br/
With good according to its mind.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To satisfy the affinity of; to
cause to become inert by chemical combination with all that it can hold;
<as>as, to <ex>saturate</ex> phosphorus with chlorine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturate</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"u*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>p. a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saturatus</ets>, p. p.]</ety> <def>Filled to repletion; saturated;
soaked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dries his feathers <qex>saturate</qex> with dew.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sand beneath our feet is <qex>saturate</qex><br/
With blood of martyrs.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturated</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"u*ra`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Filled to repletion; holding by absorption, or in solution, all
that is possible; <as>as, <ex>saturated</ex> garments; a
<ex>saturated</ex> solution of salt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Having its affinity satisfied;
combined with all it can hold; -- said of certain atoms, radicals, or
compounds; <as>thus, methane is a <ex>saturated</ex> compound</as>.
Contrasted with <contr>unsaturated</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A <ex>saturated</ex> compound may exchange certain
ingredients for others, but can not take on more without such
exchange.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saturated color</b></col> <fld>(Optics)</fld>, <cd>a
color not diluted with white; a pure unmixed color, like those of the
spectrum.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturation</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`u*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saturatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>saturation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of saturating, or the state of being saturating; complete
penetration or impregnation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The act, process, or result of
saturating a substance, or of combining it to its fullest
extent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Optics)</fld> <def>Freedom from mixture or dilution
with white; purity; -- said of colors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The degree of <xex>saturation</xex> of a color is its
relative purity, or freedom from admixture with white.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturator</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"u*ra`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who, or that which, saturates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturday</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"ur*day</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>Saterday</ets>, AS. <ets>Saeterdaeg</ets>, <ets>Saeterndaeg</ets>,
<ets>Saeternesdaeg</ets>, literally, Saturn's day, fr. L.
<ets>Saturnus</ets> Saturn + AS. <ets>daeg</ets> day; cf. L. <ets>dies
Saturni</ets>.]</ety> <def>The seventh or last day of the week; the day
following Friday and preceding Sunday.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturity</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*tu"ri*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saturitas</ets>, fr. <ets>satur</ets> full of food, sated.]</ety>
<def>The state of being saturated; fullness of supply.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Warner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturn</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"turn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Saturnus</ets>, literally, the sower, fr. <ets>serere</ets>,
<ets>satum</ets>, to sow. See <er>Season</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Roman Myth.)</fld> <def>One of the elder and principal deities,
the son of Coelus and Terra (Heaven and Earth), and the father of
Jupiter. The corresponding Greek divinity was <grk>Kro`nos</grk>, later
<grk>CHro`nos</grk>, Time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>One of the planets of the solar
system, next in magnitude to Jupiter, but more remote from the sun. Its
diameter is seventy thousand miles, its mean distance from the sun
nearly eight hundred and eighty millions of miles, and its year, or
periodical revolution round the sun, nearly twenty-nine years and a
half. It is surrounded by a remarkable system of rings, and has eight
satellites.</def> <-- more satellites have been discovered. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Alchem.)</fld> <def>The metal lead.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturnalia</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`ur*na"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Saturn</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The
festival of Saturn, celebrated in December, originally during one day,
but afterward during seven days, as a period of unrestrained license and
merriment for all classes, extending even to the slaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: A period or occasion of general license, in
which the passions or vices have riotous indulgence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturnalian</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`ur*na"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to the Saturnalia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of unrestrained and intemperate jollity; riotously
merry; dissolute.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Saturnalian</xex> amusement.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturnian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*tur"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Saturnius</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Roman Myth.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to Saturn, whose age or reign, from the mildness
and wisdom of his government, is called the <xex>golden
age</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: Resembling the golden age; distinguished for
peacefulness, happiness, contentment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Augustus, born to bring <qex>Saturnian</qex> times.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the planet
Saturn; <as>as, the <ex>Saturnian</ex> year</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saturnian verse</b></col> <fld>(Pros.)</fld>, <cd>a meter
employed by early Roman satirists, consisting of three iambics and an
extra syllable followed by three trochees, as in the line: -- Th<ecr/
qu<emac/en | w<acr/s <imac/n | th<ecr/ k<imac/tch | <ecr/n \'d8
<emac/at<icr/ng | br<emac/ad <acr/nd | h<omac/n<ecr/y. </cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturnian</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*tur"ni*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of large handsome moths belonging to
<gen>Saturnia</gen> and allied genera. The luna moth, polyphemus, and
promethea, are examples. They belong to the Silkworn family, and some
are raised for their silk. See <er>Polyphemus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturnicentric</ent><br/
<hw>Sat`urn*i*cen"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Appearing as if seen from the center of the
planet Saturn; relating or referred to Saturn as a center.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturnine</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"ur*nine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Saturnus</ets> the god Saturn, also, the planet Saturn: cf. F.
<ets>saturnin</ets> of or pertaining to lead (Saturn, in old chemistry,
meaning lead), <ets>saturnien</ets> saturnine, saturnian. See
<er>Saturn</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Born under, or influenced by,
the planet Saturn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Heavy; grave; gloomy; dull; -- the opposite of
<ant>mercurial</ant>; <as>as, a <ex>saturnine</ex> person or
temper</as>.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to lead;
characterized by, or resembling, lead, which was formerly called
<xex>Saturn</xex>.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saturnine colic</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>lead
colic.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturnism</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"ur*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Plumbism.</def> <rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saturnist</ent><br/
<hw>Sat"ur*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person of a
dull, grave, gloomy temperament.</def> <rj><au>W. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satyr</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"tyr</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>satyrus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>satyre</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld> <def>A sylvan deity or demigod,
represented as part man and part goat, and characterized by riotous
merriment and lasciviousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rough <qex>Satyrs</qex> danced; and Fauns, with cloven heel,<br/
From the glad sound would not be absent long.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of many species of
butterflies belonging to the family <fam>Nymphalidae</fam>. Their colors
are commonly brown and gray, often with ocelli on the wings. Called also
<altname>meadow browns</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The orang-outang.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satyriasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sat`y*ri"a*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
Gr. <?/. See <er>Satyr</er>.]</ety> <def>Immoderate venereal appetite in
the male.</def> <rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satyrical</ent><br/
<ent>Satyric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sa*tyr"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sa*tyr"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>satyricus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>satyriko`s</grk>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to satyrs;
burlesque; <as>as, <ex>satyric</ex> tragedy</as>.</def> <rj><au>P.
Cyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Satyrion</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa*tyr"i*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>saty`rion</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any one of several
kinds of orchids.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauba ant</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"ba ant`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A South
American ant (<spn>Oecodoma cephalotes</spn>) remarkable for having two
large kinds of workers besides the ordinary ones, and for the immense
size of its formicaries. The sauba ant cuts off leaves of plants and
carries them into its subterranean nests, and thus often does great
damage by defoliating trees and cultivated plants.</def><-- a
leaf-cutting ant --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauce</ent><br/
<hw>Sauce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. OF.
<ets>sausse</ets>, LL. <ets>salsa</ets>, properly, salt pickle, fr. L.
<ets>salsus</ets> salted, salt, p. p. of <ets>salire</ets> to salt, fr.
<ets>sal</ets> salt. See <er>Salt</er>, and cf. <er>Saucer</er>,
<er>Souse</er> pickle, <er>Souse</er> to plunge.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A composition of condiments and appetizing ingredients eaten with
food as a relish; especially, a dressing for meat or fish or for
puddings; <as>as, mint <ex>sauce</ex>; sweet <ex>sauce</ex>,
etc.</as></def> <ldquo/Poignant <xex>sauce</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>High <qex>sauces</qex> and rich spices fetched from the
Indies.</q> <rj><qau>Sir S. Baker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any garden vegetables eaten with meat.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq. U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Forby.
Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Roots, herbs, vine fruits, and salad flowers . . . they dish up
various ways, and find them very delicious <qex>sauce</qex> to their
meats, both roasted and boiled, fresh and salt.</q>
<rj><qau>Beverly.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Stewed or preserved fruit eaten with other food as a
relish; <as>as, apple <ex>sauce</ex>, cranberry <ex>sauce</ex>,
etc.</as></def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> <ldquo/Stewed apple
<xex>sauce</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mrs. Lincoln (Cook Book).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Sauciness; impertinence.</def> <mark>[Low.]</mark>
<rj><au>Haliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To serve one the same sauce</b></col>, <cd>to retaliate
in the same kind.</cd> <mark>[Vulgar]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1279 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Sauce</ent><br/
<hw>Sauce</hw> <pr>(s<add/s)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>saucer</ets>.]</ety> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sauced</conjf> <pr>(s<add/st)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Saucing</conjf> <pr>(s<add/"s<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To accompany with something intended to give a higher relish; to
supply with appetizing condiments; to season; to flavor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to relish anything, as if with a sauce; to
tickle or gratify, as the palate; to please; to stimulate; hence, to
cover, mingle, or dress, as if with sauce; to make an application
to.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Earth, yield me roots;<br/
Who seeks for better of thee, <qex>sauce</qex> his palate<br/
With thy most operant poison!</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make poignant; to give zest, flavor or interest
to; to set off; to vary and render attractive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then fell she to <qex>sauce</qex> her desires with
threatenings.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou sayest his meat was <qex>sauced</qex> with thy
upbraidings.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To treat with bitter, pert, or tart language; to be
impudent or saucy to.</def> <mark>[Colloq. or Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll <qex>sauce</qex> her with bitter words.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauce</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sauce</hw> <pr>(s<omac/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Fine Art)</fld> <def>A soft crayon for use in stump drawing or in
shading with the stump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauce-alone</ent><br/
<hw>Sauce"-a*lone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Jack-by-the-hedge. See under
<er>Jack</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saucebox</ent><br/
<hw>Sauce"box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sauce</er>, and <er>Saucy</er>.]</ety> <def>A saucy, impudent
person; especially, a pert child.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Saucebox, go, meddle with your lady's fan,<br/
And prate not here!</q> <rj><qau>A. Brewer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saucepan</ent><br/
<hw>Sauce"pan`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small pan with a
handle, in which sauce is prepared over a fire; a stewpan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saucer</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sauci<egrave/re</ets>, from <ets>sauce</ets>. See
<er>Sauce</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small pan or vessel in which
sauce was set on a table.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small dish, commonly deeper than a plate, in which
a cup is set at table.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something resembling a saucer in shape.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A flat, shallow caisson for raising
sunken ships.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A shallow socket for the pivot of
a capstan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- <col><b>Flying saucer</b></col>, <cd>a type of Unidentified
Flying Object, having a biconvex discoid shape; such objects are
occasionally reported to have been sighted, but no example of one has
been reliably shown to exist.</cd> They are believed by ufologists to
originate in outer space, but they are generally presumed to be
misinterpretations of ordinary phenomena, illusions or imaginary
objects. Fraudulent photographs purporting to show flying saucers are
published from time to time. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saucily</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"ci*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a saucy manner;
impudently; with impertinent boldness.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauciness</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"ci*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
saucy; that which is saucy; impertinent boldness; contempt of superiors;
impudence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your <qex>sauciness</qex> will jest upon my love.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Impudence; impertinence; rudeness; insolence. See
<er>Impudence</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saucisse</ent><br/
<ent>Saucisson</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Sau`cis`son"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sau`cisse"</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. <ets>saucisse</ets>
sausage. See <er>Sausage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mining or
Gun.)</fld> <def>A long and slender pipe or bag, made of cloth well
pitched, or of leather, filled with powder, and used to communicate fire
to mines, caissons, bomb chests, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A fascine of more than ordinary
length.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saucy</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Saucier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sauciest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Sauce</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Showing impertinent boldness or pertness; transgressing
the rules of decorum; treating superiors with contempt; impudent;
insolent; <as>as, a <ex>saucy</ex> fellow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Am I not protector, <qex>saucy</qex> priest?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expressive of, or characterized by, impudence;
impertinent; <as>as, a <ex>saucy</ex> eye; <ex>saucy</ex>
looks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We then have done you bold and <qex>saucy</qex> wrongs.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Impudent; insolent; impertinent; rude.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauerkraut</ent><br/
<hw>Sauer"kraut`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G., fr.
<ets>sauer</ets> sour + <ets>kraut</ets> herb, cabbage.]</ety>
<def>Cabbage cut fine and allowed to ferment in a brine made of its own
juice with salt, -- a German dish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauf</ent><br/
<hw>Sauf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Safe.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauf</ent><br/
<hw>Sauf</hw>, <pos>conj. & prep.</pos> <def>Save; except.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Sauf</xex> I myself.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saufly</ent><br/
<hw>Sauf"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Safely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauger</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
American fresh-water food fish (<spn>Stizostedion Canadense</spn>); --
called also <altname>gray pike</altname>, <altname>blue pike</altname>,
<altname>hornfish</altname>, <altname>land pike</altname>, <altname>sand
pike</altname>, <altname>pickering</altname>, and
<altname>pickerel</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauh</ent><br/
<ent>Saugh</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Saugh</hw>, <hw>Sauh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp. sing.</pos> of <er>See</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauks</ent><br/
<hw>Sauks</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sacs</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saul</ent><br/
<hw>Saul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Soul.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saul</ent><br/
<hw>Saul</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Sal</er>, the
tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saulie</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"lie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hired mourner at a
funeral.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sault</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sault</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF., F.
<ets>saut</ets>, fr. L. <ets>saltus</ets>. See <er>Salt</er> a
leap.]</ety> <def>A rapid in some rivers; <as>as, the <ex>Sault</ex>
Ste. Marie</as>.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saunders</ent><br/
<hw>Saun"ders</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sandress</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saunders-blue</ent><br/
<hw>Saun"ders-blue`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted fr.
F. <ets>cendres bleues</ets> blue ashes.]</ety> <def>A kind of color
prepared from calcined lapis lazuli; ultramarine; also, a blue prepared
from carbonate of copper.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sanders-blue</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saunter</ent><br/
<hw>Saun"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sauntered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sauntering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Written also
<ets>santer</ets>.]</ety> <ety>[Probably fr. F. <ets>s'aventurer</ets>
to adventure (one's self), through a shortened form
<ets>s'auntrer</ets>. See <er>Adventure</er>, <pos>n. & v.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To wander or walk about idly and in a leisurely or lazy manner; to
lounge; to stroll; to loiter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One could lie under elm trees in a lawn, or <qex>saunter</qex> in
meadows by the side of a stream.</q> <rj><qau>Masson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To loiter; linger; stroll; wander.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saunter</ent><br/
<hw>Saun"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sauntering, or a sauntering
place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That wheel of fops, that <qex>saunter</qex> of the town.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saunterer</ent><br/
<hw>Saun"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
saunters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saur</ent><br/
<hw>Saur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contracted from Gael.
<ets>salachar</ets> filth, nastiness, fr. <ets>salach</ets> nasty, fr.
<ets>sal</ets> filth, refuse.]</ety> <def>Soil; dirt; dirty water; urine
from a cowhouse.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saurel</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"rel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
carangoid fish of the genus <gen>Trachurus</gen>, especially
<spn>Trachurus trachurus</spn>, or <spn>Trachurus saurus</spn>, of
Europe and America, and <spn>Trachurus picturatus</spn> of California.
Called also <altname>skipjack</altname>, and <altname>horse
mackerel</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ a lizard.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Reptilia
formerly established to include the Lacertilia, Crocodilia, Dinosauria,
and other groups. By some writers the name is restricted to the
Lacertilia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saurian</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to, or of the nature of, the Sauria.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Sauria.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saurioid</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"ri*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sauroid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saurobatrachia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau"ro*ba*tra"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Sauria</er>, and <er>Batrachia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Urodela.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saurognathous</ent><br/
<hw>Sau*rog"na*thous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
lizard + <?/ the jaw.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the bones of
the palate arranged as in saurians, the vomer consisting of two lateral
halves, as in the woodpeckers (<fam>Pici</fam>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauroid</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a lizard +
<ets>-oid</ets>: cf. Gr. <?/ lizardlike.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Like or pertaining to the saurians.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Resembling a saurian superficially; <as>as, a <ex>sauroid</ex>
fish</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauroidichnite</ent><br/
<hw>Sau`roid*ich"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sauroid</er>, and <er>Ichnite</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>The fossil track of a saurian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauropoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau*rop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ a lizard + <ets>-poda</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An
extinct order of herbivorous dinosaurs having the feet of a saurian
type, instead of birdlike, as they are in many dinosaurs. It includes
the largest known land animals, belonging to Brontosaurus, Camarasaurus,
and allied genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauropsida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau*rop"si*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a lizard + <?/ appearance.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
comprehensive group of vertebrates, comprising the reptiles and
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauropterygia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau*rop`te*ryg"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a lizard + <?/, <?/, a wing.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Plesiosauria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saururae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau*ru"rae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ a lizard + <?/ a tail.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An
extinct order of birds having a long vertebrated tail with quills along
each side of it. Archaeopteryx is the type. See <er>Archaeopteryx</er>,
and <er>Odontornithes</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saury</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sauries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A slender marine fish (<spn>Scomberesox
saurus</spn>) of Europe and America. It has long, thin, beaklike jaws.
Called also <altname>billfish</altname>, <altname>gowdnook</altname>,
<altname>gawnook</altname>, <altname>skipper</altname>,
<altname>skipjack</altname>, <altname>skopster</altname>,
<altname>lizard fish</altname>, and <altname>Egypt
herring</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sausage</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"sage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>saucisse</ets>, LL. <ets>salcitia</ets>, <ets>salsicia</ets>, fr.
<ets>salsa</ets>. See <er>Sauce</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An article
of food consisting of meat (esp. pork) minced and highly seasoned, and
inclosed in a cylindrical case or skin usually made of the prepared
intestine of some animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A saucisson. See <er>Saucisson</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Wilhelm.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauseflem</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"se*flem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>saus</ets> salt (L. <ets>salsus</ets>) + <ets>flemme</ets>
phlegm.]</ety> <def>Having a red, pimpled face.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sawceflem</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saussurite</ent><br/
<hw>Saus"sur*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. So called
from M. <ets>Saussure</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A tough,
compact mineral, of a white, greenish, or grayish color. It is near
zoisite in composition, and in part, at least, has been produced by the
alteration of feldspar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saute</ent><br/
<ent>Saut</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Saut</hw>, <hw>Saute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>An assault.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saute</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau`te"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sauter</er>.</def> <rj><au>C. Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauter</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau`ter"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[F., properly,
to jump.]</ety> <def>To fry lightly and quickly, as meat, by turning or
tossing it over frequently in a hot pan greased with a little
fat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauter</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Psalter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauterelle</ent><br/
<hw>Sau`te*relle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>An instrument used by masons and others to trace and form
angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauterne</ent><br/
<hw>Sau`terne"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
white wine made in the district of <etsep>Sauterne</etsep>,
France.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sautrie</ent><br/
<hw>Sau"trie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Psaltery.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sauvegarde</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sau`ve*garde"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The monitor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savable</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Save</er>.
Cf. <er>Salvable</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of, or admitting of, being
saved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the person prayed for there ought to be the great disposition
of being in a <qex>savable</qex> condition.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savableness</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"a*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Capability of being
saved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savacioun</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*va"ci*oun`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Salvation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savage</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sauvage</ets>, OF. <ets>salvage</ets>, fr. L. <ets>silvaticus</ets>
belonging to a wood, wild, fr. <ets>silva</ets> a wood. See
<er>Silvan</er>, and cf. <er>Sylvatic</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to the forest; remote from human abodes and cultivation;
in a state of nature; wild; <as>as, a <ex>savage</ex>
wilderness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wild; untamed; uncultivated; <as>as, <ex>savage</ex>
beasts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cornels, and <qex>savage</qex> berries of the wood.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Uncivilized; untaught; unpolished; rude; <as>as,
<ex>savage</ex> life; <ex>savage</ex> manners</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What nation, since the commencement of the Christian era, ever
rose from <qex>savage</qex> to civilized without Christianity?</q>
<rj><qau>E. D. Griffin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Characterized by cruelty; barbarous; fierce;
ferocious; inhuman; brutal; <as>as, a <ex>savage</ex>
spirit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Ferocious; wild; uncultivated; untamed; untaught;
uncivilized; unpolished; rude; brutish; brutal; heathenish; barbarous;
cruel; inhuman; fierce; pitiless; merciless; unmerciful; atrocious. See
<er>Ferocious</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savage</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"age</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A human being in his
native state of rudeness; one who is untaught, uncivilized, or without
cultivation of mind or manners.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A man of extreme, unfeeling, brutal cruelty; a
barbarian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savage</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make
savage.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Its bloodhounds, <qex>savaged</qex> by a cross of wolf.</q>
<rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savagely</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"age*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a savage manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savageness</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"age*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of being
savage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wolves and bears, they say,<br/
Casting their <qex>savageness</qex> aside have done<br/
Like offices of pity.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savagery</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"age*ry</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sauvagerie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of being
savage; savageness; savagism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A like work of primeval <qex>savagery</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Kingsley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An act of cruelty; barbarity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wildest <qex>savagery</qex>, the vilest stroke,<br/
That ever wall-eyed wrath or staring rage<br/
Presented to the tears of soft remorse.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Wild growth, as of plants.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savagism</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"a*gism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
savage; the state of rude, uncivilized men, or of men in their native
wildness and rudeness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savanilla</ent><br/
<hw>Sav`a*nil"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The tarpum.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savanna</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*van"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of American Indian
origin; cf. Sp. <ets>sabana</ets>, F. <ets>savane</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
tract of level land covered with the vegetable growth usually found in a
damp soil and warm climate, -- as grass or reeds, -- but destitute of
trees.</def> <altsp>[Spelt also <asp>savannah</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Savannahs</qex> are clear pieces of land without woods.</q>
<rj><qau>Dampier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Savanna flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a West
Indian name for several climbing apocyneous plants of the genus
<gen>Echites</gen>.</cd> -- <col><b>Savanna sparrow</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an American sparrow (<spn>Ammodramus
sandwichensis</spn> or <spn>Passerculus savanna</spn>) of which several
varieties are found on grassy plains from Alaska to the Eastern United
States.</cd> -- <col><b>Savanna wattle</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>a name of two West Indian trees of the genus
<gen>Citharexylum</gen>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savant</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sa`vant"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Savants</plw> <pr>(F. <?/; E. <?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>savoir</ets> to know, L. <ets>sapere</ets>. See <er>Sage</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>A man of learning; one versed in literature or
science; a person eminent for acquirements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Save</ent><br/
<hw>Save</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sage</er> the
herb.]</ety> <def>The herb sage, or salvia.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Save</ent><br/
<hw>Save</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Saved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Saving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>saven</ets>,
<ets>sauven</ets>, <ets>salven</ets>, OF. <ets>salver</ets>,
<ets>sauver</ets>, F. <ets>sauver</ets>, L. <ets>salvare</ets>, fr.
<ets>salvus</ets> saved, safe. See <er>Safe</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To make safe; to procure the safety of; to preserve
from injury, destruction, or evil of any kind; to rescue from impending
danger; <as>as, to <ex>save</ex> a house from the flames</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God <qex>save</qex> all this fair company.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He cried, saying, Lord, <qex>save</qex> me.</q> <rj><qau>Matt.
xiv. 30.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou hast . . . quitted all to <qex>save</qex><br/
A world from utter loss.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>Specifically, to deliver from
sin and its penalty; to rescue from a state of condemnation and
spiritual death, and bring into a state of spiritual life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Christ Jesus came into the world to <qex>save</qex> sinners.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Tim. i. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To keep from being spent or lost; to secure from
waste or expenditure; to lay up; to reserve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now save a nation, and now <qex>save</qex> a groat.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To rescue from something undesirable or hurtful; to
prevent from doing something; to spare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll <qex>save</qex> you<br/
That labor, sir. All's now done.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To hinder from doing, suffering, or happening; to
obviate the necessity of; to prevent; to spare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Will you not speak to <qex>save</qex> a lady's blush?</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To hold possession or use of; to escape loss
of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Just <qex>saving</qex> the tide, and putting in a stock of
merit.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To save appearances</b></col>, <cd>to preserve a decent
outside; to avoid exposure of a discreditable state of
things.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To preserve; rescue; deliver; protect; spare;
reserve; prevent.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Save</ent><br/
<hw>Save</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To avoid unnecessary expense or
expenditure; to prevent waste; to be economical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Brass ordnance <qex>saveth</qex> in the quantity of the
material.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Save</ent><br/
<hw>Save</hw>, <pos>prep. <or/ conj.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sauf</ets>,
properly adj., safe. See <er>Safe</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Except; excepting; not including; leaving out; deducting;
reserving; saving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Five times received I forty stripes <qex>save</qex> one.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Cor. xi. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Except</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Save</ent><br/
<hw>Save</hw>, <pos>conj.</pos> <def>Except; unless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saveable</ent><br/
<hw>Save"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Savable</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Save-all</ent><br/
<hw>Save"-all`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Save</ets> +
<ets>all</ets>.]</ety> <def>Anything which saves fragments, or prevents
waste or loss.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A device in a
candlestick to hold the ends of candles, so that they be burned.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A small sail sometimes set under
the foot of another sail, to catch the wind that would pass under
it.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> A trough to prevent waste in a paper-making machine.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saveloy</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"e*loy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>cervelas</ets>, It. <ets>cervellata</ets>, fr. <ets>cervello</ets>
brain, L. <ets>cerebellum</ets>, dim. of <ets>cerebrum</ets> brain. See
<er>Cerebral</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of dried sausage.</def>
<rj><au>McElrath.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savely</ent><br/
<hw>Save"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Safely.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savement</ent><br/
<hw>Save"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
saving.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saver</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
saves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savine</ent><br/
<ent>Savin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sav"in</hw>, <hw>Sav"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>saveine</ets>, AS. <ets>safinae</ets>,
<ets>savine</ets>, L. <ets>sabina herba</ets>. Cf.
<er>Sabine</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sabine</asp>.]</altsp>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A coniferous shrub (<spn>Juniperus
Sabina</spn>) of Western Asia, occasionally found also in the northern
parts of the United States and in British America. It is a compact bush,
with dark-colored foliage, and produces small berries having a glaucous
bloom. Its bitter, acrid tops are sometimes used in medicine for gout,
amenorrhoea, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The North American red cedar
(<spn>Juniperus Virginiana</spn>.)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saving</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Preserving; rescuing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is the <qex>saving</qex> strength of his anointed.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xxviii. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Avoiding unnecessary expense or waste; frugal; not
lavish or wasteful; economical; <as>as, a <ex>saving</ex>
cook</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Bringing back in returns or in receipts the sum
expended; incurring no loss, though not gainful; <as>as, a
<ex>saving</ex> bargain; the ship has made a <ex>saving</ex>
voyage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Making reservation or exception; <as>as, a
<ex>saving</ex> clause</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Saving</xex> is often used with a noun to form a
compound adjective; <as>as, labor-<ex>saving</ex>, life-<ex>saving</ex>,
etc.</as></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1280 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Saving</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"ing</hw> <pr>(s<amac/v"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>prep. <or/
conj.</pos>; but properly a <pos>participle</pos>. <def>With the
exception of; except; excepting; also, without disrespect to.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Saving</xex> your reverence.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Saving</xex> your presence.<rdquo/ <au>Burns.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>None of us put off our clothes, <qex>saving</qex> that every one
put them off for washing.</q> <rj><qau>Neh. iv. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth
<qex>saving</qex> he that receiveth it.</q> <rj><qau>Rev. ii.
17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saving</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something kept from
being expended or lost; that which is saved or laid up; <as>as, the
<ex>savings</ex> of years of economy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exception; reservation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Contend not with those that are too strong for us, but still with
a <qex>saving</qex> to honesty.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Savings bank</b></col>, <cd>a bank in which savings or
earnings are deposited and put at interest.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savingly</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a saving
manner; with frugality or parsimony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>So as to be finally saved from eternal
death.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Savingly</qex> born of water and the Spirit.</q>
<rj><qau>Waterland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savingness</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"ing*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality of
being saving; carefulness not to expend money uselessly; frugality;
parsimony.</def> <rj><au>Mrs. H. H. Jackson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tendency to promote salvation.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savior</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"ior</hw> <pr>(s<amac/v"y<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>saveour</ets>, OF. <ets>salveor</ets>, F. <ets>sauveur</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>salvator</ets>, fr. <ets>salvare</ets> to save. See
<er>Save</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>saviour</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who saves, preserves,
or delivers from destruction or danger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically: <xex>The</xex> (or <xex>our</xex>,
<xex>your</xex>, etc.) <xex>Savior</xex>, he who brings salvation to
men; Jesus Christ, the Redeemer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savioress</ent><br/
<hw>Sav"ior*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female savior.</def>
<mark>[Written also saviouress.]</mark> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savor</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>savour</ets>,
<ets>savor</ets>, <ets>savur</ets>, OF. <ets>savor</ets>,
<ets>savour</ets>, F. <ets>saveur</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sapor</ets>, fr.
<ets>sapere</ets> to taste, savor. See <er>Sage</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Sapid</er>, <er>Insipid</er>, <er>Sapor</er>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>savour</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
property of a thing which affects the organs of taste or smell; taste
and odor; flavor; relish; scent; <as>as, the <ex>savor</ex> of an orange
or a rose; an ill <ex>savor</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I smell sweet <qex>savors</qex> and I feel soft things.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, specific flavor or quality; characteristic
property; distinctive temper, tinge, taint, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why is not my life a continual joy, and the <qex>savor</qex> of
heaven perpetually upon my spirit?</q> <rj><qau>Baxter.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Sense of smell; power to scent, or trace by
scent.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Beyond my <xex>savor</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Pleasure; delight; attractiveness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She shall no <qex>savor</qex> have therein but lite.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Taste; flavor; relish; odor; scent;
smell.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savor</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Savored</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Savoring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>savorer</ets>, F.
<ets>savourer</ets>. See <er>Savor</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>savour</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
have a particular smell or taste; -- with <xex>of</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To partake of the quality or nature; to indicate the
presence or influence; to smack; -- with <xex>of</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>savors</qex> not much of distraction.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have rejected everything that <qex>savors</qex> of party.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To use the sense of taste.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By sight, hearing, smelling, tasting or <qex>savoring</qex>, and
feeling.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savor</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To perceive by the
smell or the taste; hence, to perceive; to note.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have the flavor or quality of; to indicate the
presence of.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That cuts us off from hope, and <qex>savors</qex> only<br/
Rancor and pride, impatience and despite.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To taste or smell with pleasure; to delight in; to
relish; to like; to favor.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savorily</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor*i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a savory
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savoriness</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor*i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
savory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savorless</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no savor; destitute of
smell or of taste; insipid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savorly</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Savory.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savorly</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a savory manner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savorous</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor*ous</hw> <pr>(-<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>savoureux</ets>, OF. <ets>saveros</ets>, L. <ets>saporosus</ets>.
Cf. <er>Saporous</er>, and see <er>Savor</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Having a savor; savory.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Rom. of
R.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savory</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vor*y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Savor</er>.]</ety> <def>Pleasing to the organs of taste or
smell.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>savoury</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The chewing flocks<br/
Had ta'en their supper on the <qex>savory</qex> herb.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savory</ent><br/
<hw>Sa"vo*ry</hw> <pr>(s<amac/"v<osl/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>savor<eacute/e</ets>; cf. It. <ets>santoreggia</ets>,
<ets>satureja</ets>, L. <ets>satureia</ets>,]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An aromatic labiate plant (<spn>Satureia hortensis</spn>), much
used in cooking; -- also called <altname>summer savory</altname>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>savoury</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savoy</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*voy"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>chou de
Savoie</ets> cabbage of Savoy.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A variety
of the common cabbage (<spn>Brassica oleracea major</spn>), having
curled leaves, -- much cultivated for winter use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savoyard</ent><br/
<hw>Sav`oy*ard"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
native or inhabitant of Savoy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savvey</ent><br/
<ent>Savvy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sav"vy</hw>, <hw>Sav"vey</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v.
t. & i.</pos> <altsp>[Written also <asp>savey</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>saber</ets> to know, <ets>sabe usted</ets> do you know?]</ety>
<def>To understand; to comprehend; know.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Savvey</ent><br/
<ent>Savvy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sav"vy</hw>, <hw>Sav"vey</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Comprehension; knowledge of affairs; mental grasp; also, practical
know-how; common sense.</def> <mark>[Slang, U. S.]</mark> <def2>also
<pos>adj.</pos> <def>knowledgeable; well-informed; clever; canny;
wise.</def></def2><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw</ent><br/
<hw>Saw</hw> <pr>(s<add/)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>See</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw</ent><br/
<hw>Saw</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sawe</ets>, AS. sagu; akin to
<ets>secgan</ets> to say. See <er>Say</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos> and cf.
<er>Saga</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>Something said; speech; discourse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/To hearken all his <xex>sawe</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A saying; a proverb; a maxim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His champions are the prophets and apostles,<br/
His weapons holy <qex>saws</qex> of sacred writ.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Dictate; command; decree.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Love] rules the creatures by his powerful <qex>saw</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw</ent><br/
<hw>Saw</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sawe</ets>, AS.
<ets>sage</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zaag</ets>, G. <ets>s<aum/ge</ets>,
OHG. <ets>sega</ets>, <ets>saga</ets>, Dan. <ets>sav</ets>, Sw.
<ets>s<aring/g</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<oum/g</ets>, L. <ets>secare</ets> to
cut, <ets>securis</ets> ax, <ets>secula</ets> sickle. Cf.
<er>Scythe</er>, <er>Sickle</er>, <er>Section</er>,
<er>Sedge</er>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for cutting or dividing
substances, as wood, iron, etc., consisting of a thin blade, or plate,
of steel, with a series of sharp teeth on the edge, which remove
successive portions of the material by cutting and tearing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Saw</xex> is frequently used adjectively, or as the
first part of a compound.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Band saw</b></col>, <col><b>Crosscut saw</b></col>,
etc.</mcol> <cd>See under <er>Band</er>, <er>Crosscut</er>, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Circular saw</b></col>, <cd>a disk of steel with saw teeth upon
its periphery, and revolved on an arbor.</cd> -- <col><b>Saw
bench</b></col>, <cd>a bench or table with a flat top for for sawing,
especially with a circular saw which projects above the table.</cd> --
<col><b>Saw file</b></col>, <cd>a three-cornered file, such as is used
for sharpening saw teeth.</cd> -- <col><b>Saw frame</b></col>, <cd>the
frame or sash in a sawmill, in which the saw, or gang of saws, is
held.</cd> -- <col><b>Saw gate</b></col>, <cd>a saw frame.</cd> --
<col><b>Saw gin</b></col>, <cd>the form of cotton gin invented by Eli
Whitney, in which the cotton fibers are drawn, by the teeth of a set of
revolving circular saws, through a wire grating which is too fine for
the seeds to pass.</cd> -- <col><b>Saw grass</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any one of certain cyperaceous plants having the
edges of the leaves set with minute sharp teeth, especially the
<spn>Cladium Mariscus</spn> of Europe, and the <spn>Cladium
effusum</spn> of the Southern United States. Cf. <cref>Razor
grass</cref>, under <er>Razor</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Saw log</b></col>,
<cd>a log of suitable size for sawing into lumber.</cd> -- <col><b>Saw
mandrel</b></col>, <cd>a mandrel on which a circular saw is fastened for
running.</cd> -- <col><b>Saw pit</b></col>, <cd>a pit over which timbor
is sawed by two men, one standing below the timber and the other
above.</cd> <au>Mortimer.</au> -- <col><b>Saw sharpener</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the great titmouse; -- so named from its harsh
call note.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Saw
whetter</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the marsh titmouse (<spn>Parus
palustris</spn>); -- so named from its call note.</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><-- no relation to saw-whet, an owl also named for its note
--> -- <col><b>Scroll saw</b></col>, <cd>a ribbon of steel with saw
teeth upon one edge, stretched in a frame and adapted for sawing curved
outlines; also, a machine in which such a saw is worked by foot or
power.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw</ent><br/
<hw>Saw</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Sawed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sawed</conjf>
<it>or</it> <conjf>Sawn</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sawing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut with a
saw; to separate with a saw; <as>as, to <ex>saw</ex> timber or
marble</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form by cutting with a saw; <as>as, to
<ex>saw</ex> boards or planks, that is, to saw logs or timber into
boards or planks; to <ex>saw</ex> shingles; to <ex>saw</ex> out a
panel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Also used figuratively; <as>as, to <ex>saw</ex> the
air</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw</ent><br/
<hw>Saw</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To use a saw; to
practice sawing; <as>as, a man <ex>saws</ex> well</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cut, as a saw; <as>as, the saw or mill
<ex>saws</ex> fast</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be cut with a saw; <as>as, the timber
<ex>saws</ex> smoothly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawarra nut</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*war"ra nut`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <er>Souari
nut</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawbelly</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"bel`ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The alewife.</def>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawbill</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The merganser.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawbones</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"bones`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A nickname for a
surgeon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawbuck</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"buck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
sawhorse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Colloq., from the Roman X for ten, like the support
of a <ets>sawbuck</ets>.]</ety> <def>a ten-dollar bill; also,
<col><b>double sawbuck</b></col>, a twenty-dollar bill.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawceflem</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"ce*flem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sauseflem</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawder</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A corrupt spelling and
pronunciation of <er>solder</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soft sawder</b></col>, <cd>seductive praise; flattery;
blarney.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawdust</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"dust`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Dust or small
fragments of wood (or of stone, etc.) made by the cutting of a
saw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawer</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"er`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who saws; a
sawyer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawfish</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of several species of elasmobranch fishes of the genus
<gen>Pristis</gen>. They have a sharklike form, but are more nearly
allied to the rays. The flattened and much elongated snout has a row of
stout toothlike structures inserted along each edge, forming a sawlike
organ with which it mutilates or kills its prey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawfly</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"fly`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of hymenopterous insects belonging to
the family <fam>Tenthredinidae</fam>. The female usually has an
ovipositor containing a pair of sawlike organs with which she makes
incisions in the leaves or stems of plants in which to lay the eggs. The
larvae resemble those of Lepidoptera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawhorse</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"horse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of rack,
shaped like a double St. Andrew's cross, on which sticks of wood are
laid for sawing by hand; -- called also <altname>buck</altname>, and
<altname>sawbuck</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawmill</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"mill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mill for sawing,
especially one for sawing timber or lumber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawneb</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"neb`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A merganser.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw palmetto</ent><br/
<hw>Saw" pal*met"to</hw>. <def>See under <er>Palmetto</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw-set</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"-set`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An instrument used
to set or turn the teeth of a saw a little sidewise, that they may make
a kerf somewhat wider than the thickness of the blade, to prevent
friction; -- called also <altname>saw-wrest</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawtooth</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"tooth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An arctic seal (<spn>Lobodon carcinophaga</spn>), having the molars
serrated; -- called also <altname>crab-eating seal</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw-toothed</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"-toothed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a tooth
or teeth like those of a saw; serrate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawtry</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A psaltery.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw-whet</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"-whet`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A small North American owl (<spn>Nyctale Acadica</spn>), destitute
of ear tufts and having feathered toes; -- called also <altname>Acadian
owl</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw-wort</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"-wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the composite genus <gen>Serratula</gen>; -- so named
from the serrated leaves of most of the species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saw-wrest</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"-wrest`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Saw-set</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sawyer</ent><br/
<hw>Saw"yer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Saw</ets> +
<ets>-yer</ets>, as in lawyer. Cf. <er>Sawer</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One whose occupation is to saw timber into planks or boards, or to
saw wood for fuel; a sawer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tree which has fallen into a stream so that its
branches project above the surface, rising and falling with a rocking or
swaying motion in the current.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The bowfin.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sax</ent><br/
<hw>Sax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>seax</ets> a
knife.]</ety> <def>A kind of chopping instrument for trimming the edges
of roofing slates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxatile</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"a*tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saxatilis</ets>, fr. <ets>saxum</ets> a rock: cf. F.
<ets>saxatile</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to rocks; living among
rocks; <as>as, a <ex>saxatile</ex> plant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxhorn</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"horn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A
name given to a numerous family of brass wind instruments with valves,
invented by Antoine Joseph Adolphe <etsep>Sax</etsep> (known as Adolphe
Sax), of Belgium and Paris, and much used in military bands and in
orchestras.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxicava</ent><br/
<hw>Sax`i*ca"va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>saxicavas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Saxicavae</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Saxicavous</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of marine bivalve shells of the
genus <gen>Saxicava</gen>. Some of the species are noted for their power
of boring holes in limestone and similar rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxicavid</ent><br/
<hw>Sax`i*ca"vid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the saxicavas.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A saxicava.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxicavous</ent><br/
<hw>Sax`i*ca"vous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saxum</ets> rock + <ets>cavare</ets> to make hollow, fr.
<ets>cavus</ets> hollow: cf. F. <ets>saxicave</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Boring, or hollowing out, rocks; -- said of
certain mollusks which live in holes which they burrow in
rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxicoline</ent><br/
<hw>Sax*ic"o*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saxum</ets> a rock + <ets>colere</ets> to inhabit.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Stone-inhabiting; pertaining to, or having the
characteristics of, the stonechats.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxicolous</ent><br/
<hw>Sax*ic"o*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Saxicoline</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Growing on
rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxifraga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sax*if"ra*ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
saxifrage. See <er>Saxifrage</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus
of exogenous polypetalous plants, embracing about one hundred and eighty
species. See <er>Saxifrage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxifragaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sax`i*fra*ga"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a natural order of plants
(<ord>Saxifragaceae</ord>) of which saxifrage is the type. The order
includes also the alum root, the hydrangeas, the mock orange, currants
and gooseberries, and many other plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxifragant</ent><br/
<hw>Sax*if"ra*gant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Saxifrage</er>.]</ety> <def>Breaking or destroying stones;
saxifragous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>That
which breaks or destroys stones.</def></def2> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxifrage</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"i*frage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saxifraga</ets>, from <ets>saxifragus</ets> stone-breaking;
<ets>saxum</ets> rock + <ets>frangere</ets> to break: cf. F.
<ets>saxifrage</ets>. See <er>Fracture</er>, and cf. <er>Sassafras</er>,
<er>Saxon</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of the genus
<gen>Saxifraga</gen>, mostly perennial herbs growing in crevices of
rocks in mountainous regions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Burnet saxifrage</b></col>, <cd>a European umbelliferous
plant (<spn>Pimpinella Saxifraga</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Golden
saxifrage</b></col>, <cd>a low half-succulent herb (<spn>Chrysosplenium
oppositifolium</spn>) growing in rivulets in Europe; also,
<spn>Chrysosplenium Americanum</spn>, common in the United States. See
also under <er>Golden</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Meadow
saxifrage</b></col>, or <col><b>Pepper saxifrage</b></col></mcol>.
<cd>See under <er>Meadow</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxifragous</ent><br/
<hw>Sax*if"ra*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saxifragus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>saxifrage</ets>. See
<er>Saxifrage</er>.]</ety> <def>Dissolving stone, especially dissolving
stone in the bladder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxon</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"on</hw> <pr>(s<acr/ks"<ucr/n <it>or</it> -'n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Saxo</ets>, pl. <ets>Saxones</ets>, from the
Saxon national name; cf. AS. pl. <ets>Seaxe</ets>, <ets>Seaxan</ets>,
fr. <ets>seax</ets> a knife, a short sword, a dagger (akin to OHG.
<ets>sahs</ets>, and perhaps to L. <ets>saxum</ets> rock, stone, knives
being originally made of stone); and cf. G. <ets>Sachse</ets>, pl.
<ets>Sachsen</ets>. Cf. <er>Saxifrage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of a nation or people who formerly dwelt in the
northern part of Germany, and who, with other Teutonic tribes, invaded
and conquered England in the fifth and sixth centuries.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Also used in the sense of
<xex>Anglo-Saxon</xex>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A native or inhabitant
of modern Saxony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The language of the Saxons; Anglo-Saxon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Old Saxon</b></col>, <cd>the Saxon of the continent of
Europe in the old form of the language, as shown particularly in the
<ldquo/Heliand<rdquo/, a metrical narration of the gospel history
preserved in manuscripts of the 9th century.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxon</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"on</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the Saxons,
their country, or their language.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Anglo-Saxon.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to Saxony or
its inhabitants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Saxon blue</b></col> <fld>(Dyeing)</fld>, <cd>a deep blue
liquid used in dyeing, and obtained by dissolving indigo in concentrated
sulphuric acid.</cd> <au>Brande & C.</au> -- <col><b>Saxon
green</b></col> <fld>(Dyeing)</fld>, <cd>a green color produced by
dyeing with yellow upon a ground of Saxon blue.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sax*on"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to the
Saxons or Anglo- Saxons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxonism</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"on*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An idiom of the
Saxon or Anglo-Saxon language.</def> <rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxonist</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"on*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in the Saxon
language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxonite</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"on*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>See <cref>Mountain soap</cref>, under <er>Mountain</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxony</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named after the
kingdom of <etsep>Saxony</etsep>, reputed to produce fine wool.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of glossy woolen cloth formerly much
used.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Saxony yarn, or flannel made of it or similar
yarn.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxony yarn</ent><br/
<hw>Saxony yarn</hw>. <def>A fine grade of woolen yarn twisted somewhat
harder and smoother than zephyr yarn.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saxophone</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"o*phone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A.A.J.
<ets>Sax</ets>, the inventor (see <er>Saxhorn</er>) + Gr. <?/
tone.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A wind instrument of brass,
containing a reed, and partaking of the qualities both of a brass
instrument and of a clarinet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sax-tuba</ent><br/
<hw>Sax"-tu`ba</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Saxhorn</er>, and <er>Tube</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A
powerful instrument of brass, curved somewhat like the Roman
<xex>buccina</xex>, or tuba.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1281 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Say</ent><br/
<hw>Say</hw> <pr>(s<amac/)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos>
<mord>of <er>See</er></mord>. <def>Saw.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Say</ent><br/
<hw>Say</hw> <pr>(s<amac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Aphetic form of
<ets>assay</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Trial by sample; assay;
sample; specimen; smack.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If those principal works of God . . . be but certain tastes and
<qex>says</qex>, as it were, of that final benefit.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy tongue some <qex>say</qex> of breeding breathes.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tried quality; temper; proof.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He found a sword of better <qex>say</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Essay; trial; attempt.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To give a say at</b></col>, <cd>to attempt.</cd>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Say</ent><br/
<hw>Say</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To try; to assay.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Say</ent><br/
<hw>Say</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>saie</ets>, F.
<ets>saie</ets>, fr. L. <ets>saga</ets>, equiv. to <ets>sagum</ets>,
<ets>sagus</ets>, a coarse woolen mantle; cf. Gr. <grk>sa`gos</grk>. See
<er>Sagum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of silk or satin.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou <qex>say</qex>, thou serge, nay, thou buckram lord!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A delicate kind of serge, or woolen cloth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His garment neither was of silk nor <qex>say</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Say</ent><br/
<hw>Say</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Said</conjf> <pr>(s<ecr/d)</pr>, contracted from
<xex>sayed</xex>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Saying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>seggen</ets>,
<ets>seyen</ets>, <ets>siggen</ets>, <ets>sayen</ets>, <ets>sayn</ets>,
AS. <ets>secgan</ets>; akin to OS. <ets>seggian</ets>, D.
<ets>zeggen</ets>, LG. <ets>seggen</ets>, OHG. <ets>sag<emac/n</ets>, G.
<ets>sagen</ets>, Icel. <ets>segja</ets>, Sw. <ets>s<aum/ga</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sige</ets>, Lith. <ets>sakyti</ets>; cf. OL. <ets>insece</ets>
tell, relate, Gr. <grk>'e`nnepe</grk> (for <grk>'en-sepe</grk>),
<grk>'e`spete</grk>. Cf. <er>Saga</er>, <er>Saw</er> a saying.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter or express in words; to tell; to speak; to
declare; <as>as, he <ex>said</ex> many wise things</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Arise, and <qex>say</qex> how thou camest here.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To repeat; to rehearse; to recite; to pronounce;
<as>as, to <ex>say</ex> a lesson</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of my instruction hast thou nothing bated<br/
In what thou hadst to <qex>say</qex>?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>After which shall be <qex>said</qex> or sung the following
hymn.</q> <rj><qau>Bk. of Com. Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To announce as a decision or opinion; to state
positively; to assert; hence, to form an opinion upon; to be sure about;
to be determined in mind as to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But what it is, hard is to <qex>say</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To mention or suggest as an estimate, hypothesis, or
approximation; hence, to suppose; -- in the imperative, followed
sometimes by the subjunctive; <as>as, he had, <ex>say</ex> fifty
thousand dollars; the fox had run, <ex>say</ex> ten
miles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Say</qex>, for nonpayment that the debt should double,<br/
Is twenty hundred kisses such a trouble?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>It is said</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>They
say</b></col></mcol>, <cd>it is commonly reported; it is rumored; people
assert or maintain.</cd> -- <col><b>That is to say</b></col>, <cd>that
is; in other words; otherwise.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Say</ent><br/
<hw>Say</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To speak; to express an opinion; to
make answer; to reply.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have <qex>said</qex>; but whether wisely or no, let the forest
judge.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To this argument we shall soon have <qex>said</qex>; for what
concerns it us to hear a husband divulge his household privacies?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Say</ent><br/
<hw>Say</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Say</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>;
cf. <er>Saw</er> a saying.]</ety> <def>A speech; something said; an
expression of opinion; a current story; a maxim or proverb.</def>
<mark>[Archaic or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He no sooner said out his <qex>say</qex>, but up rises a cunning
snap.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That strange palmer's boding <qex>say</qex>,<br/
That fell so ominous and drear<br/
Full on the object of his fear.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sayer</ent><br/
<hw>Say"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who says; an
utterer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mr. Curran was something much better than a <qex>sayer</qex> of
smart sayings.</q> <rj><qau>Jeffrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sayette</ent><br/
<hw>Sa*yette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. Cf. <er>Say</er>
a kind of serge.]</ety> <def>A mixed stuff, called also
<altname>sagathy</altname>. See <er>Sagathy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saying</ent><br/
<hw>Say"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is said; a
declaration; a statement, especially a proverbial one; an aphorism; a
proverb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many are the <qex>sayings</qex> of the wise,<br/
In ancient and in modern books enrolled.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Declaration; speech; adage; maxim; aphorism;
apothegm; saw; proverb; byword.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sayman</ent><br/
<hw>Say"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Say</ets> sample
+ <ets>man</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who assays.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saymaster</ent><br/
<hw>Say"mas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A master of assay;
one who tries or proves.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Great
<xex>saymaster</xex> of state.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Saynd</ent><br/
<-- I have amended "Saynd" to read more like the first "Say". PCP --><hw>Saynd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos> <mord>of <er>Senge</er></mord>, to singe.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>'Sblood</ent><br/
<hw>'Sblood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>An abbreviation of <xex>God's blood</xex>; -- used as an oath.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scab</ent><br/
<hw>Scab</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/b)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>scab</ets>, <ets>scabbe</ets>, <ets>shabbe</ets>; cf. AS. <ets>scaeb</ets>, <ets>sceabb</ets>, <ets>scebb</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>skab</ets>, and also L. <ets>scabies</ets>, fr. <ets>scabere</ets> to scratch, akin to E. <ets>shave</ets>. See <er>Shave</er>, and cf. <er>Shab</er>, <er>Shabby</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An incrustation over a sore, wound, vesicle, or pustule, formed by the drying up of the discharge from the diseased part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The itch in man; also, the scurvy.</def> <mark>[Colloq. or Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The mange, esp. when it appears on sheep.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A disease of potatoes producing pits in their surface, caused by a minute fungus (<spn>Tiburcinia Scabies</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>A slight irregular protuberance which defaces the surface of a casting, caused by the breaking away of a part of the mold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A mean, dirty, paltry fellow.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A nickname for a workman who engages for lower wages than are fixed by the trades unions; also, for one who takes the place of a workman on a strike.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any one of various more or less destructive fungus diseases attacking cultivated plants, and usually forming dark-colored crustlike spots.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scab</ent><br/
<hw>Scab</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scabbed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scabbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To become covered with a scab; <as>as, the wound <ex>scabbed</ex> over</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to take the place of a striking worker.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabbard</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"bard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>scaubert</ets>, <ets>scauberk</ets>, OF. <ets>escaubers</ets>, <ets>escauberz</ets>, pl., scabbards, probably of German or Scan. origin; cf. Icel. <ets>sk<amac/lpr</ets> scabbard, and G. <ets>bergen</ets> to conceal. Cf. <er>Hauberk</er>.]</ety> <def>The case in which the blade of a sword, dagger, etc., is kept; a sheath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor in thy <qex>scabbard</qex> sheathe that famous blade.</q> <rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scabbard fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a long, compressed, silver-colored taenioid fish (<spn>Lepidopus argyreus</spn> syn. <spn>Lepidopus caudatus</spn>), found on the European coasts, and more abundantly about New Zealand, where it is called <altname>frostfish</altname> and considered an excellent food fish.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabbard</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"bard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To put in a scabbard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabbard plane</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"bard plane`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <cref>Scaleboard plane</cref>, under <er>Scaleboard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabbed</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"bed</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding with scabs; diseased with scabs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Mean; paltry; vile; worthless.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabbedness</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"bed*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Scabbiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabbily</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"bi*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scabby manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabbiness</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"bi*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being scabby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabble</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See <er>Scapple</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabby</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Scabbier</adjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Scabbiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Affected with scabs; full of scabs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Diseased with the scab, or mange; mangy.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabies</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sca"bi*es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The itch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabious</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"bi*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scabiosus</ets>, from <ets>scabies</ets> the scab: cf. F. <ets>scabieux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of scabs; rough; itchy; leprous; <as>as, <ex>scabious</ex> eruptions</as>.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabious</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"bi*ous</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>scabieuse</ets>. See <er>Scabious</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Scabiosa</gen>, several of the species of which are common in Europe. They resemble the <fam>Compositae</fam>, and have similar heads of flowers, but the anthers are not connected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sweet scabious</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Mourning bride</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A daisylike plant (<spn>Erigeron annuus</spn>) having a stout branching stem.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabling</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Scapple</er>.]</ety> <def>A fragment or chip of stone.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>scabline</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabredity</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*bred"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scabredo</ets>, fr. <ets>scaber</ets> rough.]</ety> <def>Roughness; ruggedness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabrous</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"brous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scabrosus</ets>, fr. <ets>scaber</ets> rough: cf. F. <ets>scabreux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Rough to the touch, like a file; having small raised dots, scales, or points; scabby; scurfy; scaly.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Harsh; unmusical.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His verse is <qex>scabrous</qex> and hobbling.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabrousness</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"brous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being scabrous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scabwort</ent><br/
<hw>Scab"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Elecampane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scad</ent><br/
<hw>Scad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael. & Ir. <ets>sgadan</ets> a herring.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A small carangoid fish (<spn>Trachurus saurus</spn>) abundant on the European coast, and less common on the American. The name is applied also to several allied species.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The goggler; -- called also <altname>big-eyed scad</altname>. See <er>Goggler</er>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The friar skate.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The cigar fish, or round robin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaffold</ent><br/
<hw>Scaf"fold</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>eschafault</ets>, <ets>eschafaut</ets>, <ets>escafaut</ets>, <ets>escadafaut</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/chafaud</ets>; probably originally the same word as E. & F. <ets>catafalque</ets>, It. <ets>catafalco</ets>. See <er>Catafalque</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A temporary structure of timber, boards, etc., for various purposes, as for supporting workmen and materials in building, for exhibiting a spectacle upon, for holding the spectators at a show, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pardon, gentles all,<br/
The flat, unraised spirits that have dared<br/
On this unworthy <qex>scaffold</qex> to bring forth<br/
So great an object.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, a stage or elevated platform for the execution of a criminal; <as>as, to die on the <ex>scaffold</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That a <qex>scaffold</qex> of execution should grow a scaffold of coronation.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>An accumulation of adherent, partly fused material forming a shelf, or dome-shaped obstruction, above the tuy<egrave/res in a blast furnace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaffold</ent><br/
<hw>Scaf"fold</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To furnish or uphold with a scaffold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaffoldage</ent><br/
<hw>Scaf"fold*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scaffold.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaffolding</ent><br/
<hw>Scaf"fold*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A scaffold; a supporting framework; <as>as, the <ex>scaffolding</ex> of the body</as>.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Materials for building scaffolds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaglia</ent><br/
<hw>Scagl"ia</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>scaglia</ets> a scale, a shell, a chip of marble.]</ety> <def>A reddish variety of limestone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scagliola</ent><br/
<hw>Scagl*io"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>scagliuola</ets>, dim. of <ets>scaglia</ets>. See <er>Scaglia</er>.]</ety> <def>An imitation of any veined and ornamental stone, as marble, formed by a substratum of finely ground gypsum mixed with glue, the surface of which, while soft, is variegated with splinters of marble, spar, granite, etc., and subsequently colored and polished.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scala</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sca"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Scalae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a ladder.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>A machine formerly employed for reducing dislocations of the humerus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A term applied to any one of the three canals of the cochlea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalable</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being scaled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalado</ent><br/
<ent>Scalade</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sca*lade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sca*la"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>See <er>Escalade</er>.</def> <rj><au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalar</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>In the quaternion analysis, a quantity that has magnitude, but not direction; -- distinguished from a <xex>vector</xex>, which has both magnitude and direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sca*la"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., flight of steps.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of marine gastropods of the genus <gen>Scalaria</gen>, or family <fam>Scalaridae</fam>, having elongated spiral turreted shells, with rounded whorls, usually crossed by ribs or varices. The color is generally white or pale. Called also <altname>ladder shell</altname>, and <altname>wentletrap</altname>. See <er>Ptenoglossa</er>, and <er>Wentletrap</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalariform</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*lar"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scalare</ets>, <ets>scalaria</ets>, staircase, ladder + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scalariforme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Resembling a ladder in form or appearance; having transverse bars or markings like the rounds of a ladder; <as>as, the <ex>scalariform</ex> cells and <ex>scalariform</ex> pits in some plants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like or pertaining to a scalaria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalary</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scalaris</ets>, fr. <ets>scalae</ets>, pl. <ets>scala</ets>, staircase, ladder.]</ety> <def>Resembling a ladder; formed with steps.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalawag</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"a*wag</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scamp; a scapegrace.</def> <altsp>[Spelt also <asp>scallawag</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scald</ent><br/
<hw>Scald</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scalded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scalding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>eschalder</ets>, <ets>eschauder</ets>, <ets>escauder</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/chauder</ets>, fr. L. <ets>excaldare</ets>; <ets>ex + caldus</ets>, <ets>calidus</ets>, warm, hot. See <er>Ex</er>, and <er>Caldron</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To burn with hot liquid or steam; to pain or injure by contact with, or immersion in, any hot fluid; <as>as, to <ex>scald</ex> the hand</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mine own tears<br/
Do <qex>scald</qex> like molten lead.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here the blue flames of <qex>scalding</qex> brimstone fall.</q> <rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To expose to a boiling or violent heat over a fire, or in hot water or other liquor; <as>as, to <ex>scald</ex> milk or meat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scald</ent><br/
<hw>Scald</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A burn, or injury to the skin or flesh, by some hot liquid, or by steam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scald</ent><br/
<hw>Scald</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[For <ets>scalled</ets>. See <er>Scall</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Affected with the scab; scabby.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Scurvy; paltry; <as>as, <ex>scald</ex> rhymers</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scald crow</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the hooded crow.</cd> <mark>[Ireland]</mark> -- <col><b>Scald head</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a name popularly given to several diseases of the scalp characterized by pustules (the dried discharge of which forms scales) and by falling out of the hair.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scald</ent><br/
<hw>Scald</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Scurf on the head. See <er>Scall</er>.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scald</ent><br/
<hw>Scald</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/ld <it>or</it> sk<add/ld; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>sk<amac/ld</ets>.]</ety> <def>One of the ancient Scandinavian poets and historiographers; a reciter and singer of heroic poems, eulogies, etc., among the Norsemen; more rarely, a bard of any of the ancient Teutonic tribes.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>skald</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A war song such as was of yore chanted on the field of battle by the <qex>scalds</qex> of the yet heathen Saxons.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalder</ent><br/
<hw>Scald"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Scandinavian poet; a scald.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaldfish</ent><br/
<hw>Scald"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Scald</ets>, a. + <ets>fish</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A European flounder (<spn>Arnoglossus laterna</spn>, or <spn>Psetta arnoglossa</spn>); -- called also <altname>megrim</altname>, and <altname>smooth sole</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaldic</ent><br/
<hw>Scald"ic</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the scalds of the Norsemen; <as>as, <ex>scaldic</ex> poetry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scale</ent><br/
<hw>Scale</hw> <pr>(sk<amac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sc<amac/le</ets>; perhaps influenced by the kindred Icel. <ets>sk<amac/l</ets> balance, dish, akin also to D. <ets>schaal</ets> a scale, bowl, shell, G. <ets>schale</ets>, OHG. <ets>sc<amac/la</ets>, Dan. <ets>skaal</ets> drinking cup, bowl, dish, and perh. to E. <ets>scale</ets> of a fish. Cf. <er>Scale</er> of a fish, <er>Skull</er> the brain case.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The dish of a balance; hence, the balance itself; an instrument or machine for weighing; <as>as, to turn the <ex>scale</ex></as>; -- chiefly used in the plural when applied to the whole instrument or apparatus for weighing. Also used figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Long time in even <qex>scale</qex><br/
The battle hung.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scales</qex> are turned; her kindness weighs no more<br/
Now than my vows.</q> <rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The sign or constellation Libra.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Platform scale</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Platform</er>.</cd></cs> <-- <col><b>tip the scales</b></col>, influence an action so as to change an outcome from one likely result to another. --> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scale</ent><br/
<hw>Scale</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scaled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scaling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To weigh or measure according to
a scale; to measure; also, to grade or vary according to a scale or
system.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Scaling</qex> his present bearing with his past.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To scale a debt, wages, etc.</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>To scale down a debt, wages, etc.</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to
reduce a debt, etc., according to a fixed ratio or scale.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scale</ent><br/
<hw>Scale</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>scealu</ets>,
<ets>scalu</ets>, a shell, parings; akin to D. <ets>schaal</ets>, G.
<ets>schale</ets>, OHG. <ets>scala</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>skal</ets> a
shell, Dan. <ets>skiael</ets> a fish scale, Goth. <ets>skalja</ets>
tile, and E. <ets>shale</ets>, <ets>shell</ets>, and perhaps also to
<ets>scale</ets> of a balance; but perhaps rather fr. OF.
<ets>escale</ets>, <ets>escaile</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/caille</ets>
scale of a fish, and <ets><eacute/cale</ets> shell of beans, pease,
eggs, nuts, of German origin, and akin to Goth. <ets>skalja</ets>, G.
<ets>schale</ets>. See <er>Shale</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>One of the small, thin, membranous, bony or
horny pieces which form the covering of many fishes and reptiles, and
some mammals, belonging to the dermal part of the skeleton, or
dermoskeleton. See <er>Cycloid</er>, <er>Ctenoid</er>, and
<er>Ganoid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fish that, with their fins and shining <qex>scales</qex>,<br/
Glide under the green wave.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, any layer or leaf of metal or other material,
resembling in size and thinness the scale of a fish; <as>as, a
<ex>scale</ex> of iron, of bone, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the small scalelike
structures covering parts of some invertebrates, as those on the wings
of Lepidoptera and on the body of Thysanura; the elytra of certain
annelids. See <er>Lepidoptera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A scale insect. (See
below.)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A small appendage like a
rudimentary leaf, resembling the scales of a fish in form, and often in
arrangement; <as>as, the <ex>scale</ex> of a bud, of a pine cone, and
the like</as>. The name is also given to the chaff on the stems of
ferns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The thin metallic side plate of the handle of a
pocketknife. </def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>An incrustation deposit on the inside of a vessel in
which water is heated, as a steam boiler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>The thin oxide which forms on
the surface of iron forgings. It consists essentially of the magnetic
oxide, <chform>Fe3O4</chform>. Also, a similar coating upon other
metals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Covering scale</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
hydrophyllium.</cd> -- <col><b>Ganoid scale</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Ganoid</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scale
armor</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>armor made of small metallic
scales overlapping, and fastened upon leather or cloth.</cd> --
<col><b>Scale beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the tiger
beetle.</cd> -- <col><b>Scale carp</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
carp having normal scales.</cd> -- <col><b>Scale insect</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of small hemipterous
insects belonging to the family <fam>Coccidae</fam>, in which the
females, when adult, become more or less scalelike in form. They are
found upon the leaves and twigs of various trees and shrubs, and often
do great damage to fruit trees. See <er>Orange scale</er>,under
<er>Orange</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scale moss</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>any leafy-stemmed moss of the order <ord>Hepaticae</ord>; -- so
called from the small imbricated scalelike leaves of most of the
species. See <er>Hepatica</er>, 2, and
<er>Jungermannia</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1282 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scale</ent><br/
<hw>Scale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strip
or clear of scale or scales; <as>as, to <ex>scale</ex> a fish; to
<ex>scale</ex> the inside of a boiler</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take off in thin layers or scales, as tartar from
the teeth; to pare off, as a surface.</def> <ldquo/If all the mountains
were <xex>scaled</xex>, and the earth made even.<rdquo/ <rj><au>T.
Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To scatter; to spread.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Gun.)</fld> <def>To clean, as the inside of a
cannon, by the explosion of a small quantity of powder.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scale</ent><br/
<hw>Scale</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To separate and come
off in thin layers or laminae; <as>as, some sandstone <ex>scales</ex> by
exposure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those that cast their shell are the lobster and crab; the old
skins are found, but the old shells never; so it is likely that they
<qex>scale</qex> off.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To separate; to scatter.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scale</ent><br/
<hw>Scale</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scalae</ets>, pl.,
<ets>scala</ets> staircase, ladder; akin to <ets>scandere</ets> to
climb. See <er>Scan</er>; cf. <er>Escalade</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A ladder; a series of steps; a means of ascending.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, anything graduated, especially when employed
as a measure or rule, or marked by lines at regular intervals.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A mathematical instrument, consisting of
a slip of wood, ivory, or metal, with one or more sets of spaces
graduated and numbered on its surface, for measuring or laying off
distances, etc., as in drawing, plotting, and the like. See <er>Gunter's
scale</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A series of spaces marked by lines,
and representing proportionately larger distances; <as>as, a
<ex>scale</ex> of miles, yards, feet, etc., for a map or
plan</as>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A basis for a numeral system; <as>as,
the decimal <ex>scale</ex>; the binary <ex>scale</ex>, etc.</as></def>
<sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The graduated series of all the
tones, ascending or descending, from the keynote to its octave; --
called also the <altname>gamut</altname>. It may be repeated through any
number of octaves. See <cref>Chromatic scale</cref>, <cref>Diatonic
scale</cref>, <cref>Major scale</cref>, and <cref>Minor scale</cref>,
under <er>Chromatic</er>, <er>Diatonic</er>, <er>Major</er>, and
<er>Minor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Gradation; succession of ascending and descending
steps and degrees; progressive series; scheme of comparative rank or
order; <as>as, a <ex>scale</ex> of being</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a certain <qex>scale</qex> of duties . . . which for want
of studying in right order, all the world is in confusion.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Relative dimensions, without difference in
proportion of parts; size or degree of the parts or components in any
complex thing, compared with other like things; especially, the relative
proportion of the linear dimensions of the parts of a drawing, map,
model, etc., to the dimensions of the corresponding parts of the object
that is represented; <as>as, a map on a <ex>scale</ex> of an inch to a
mile</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scale of chords</b></col>, <cd>a graduated scale on which
are given the lengths of the chords of arcs from 0<deg/ to 90<deg/ in a
circle of given radius, -- used in measuring given angles and in
plotting angles of given numbers of degrees.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scale</ent><br/
<hw>Scale</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It. <ets>scalare</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>scalae</ets>, <ets>scala</ets>. See <er>Scale</er> a
ladder.]</ety> <def>To climb by a ladder, or as if by a ladder; to
ascend by steps or by climbing; to clamber up; <as>as, to <ex>scale</ex>
the wall of a fort</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Oft have I <qex>scaled</qex> the craggy oak.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scale</ent><br/
<hw>Scale</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To lead up by steps; to
ascend.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Satan from hence, now on the lower stair,<br/
That <qex>scaled</qex> by steps of gold to heaven-gate,<br/
Looks down with wonder.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaleback</ent><br/
<hw>Scale"back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of marine annelids of the family
<fam>Polynoidae</fam>, and allies, which have two rows of scales, or
elytra, along the back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalebeam</ent><br/
<hw>Scale"beam`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
lever or beam of a balance; the lever of a platform scale, to which the
poise for weighing is applied.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A weighing apparatus with a sliding weight,
resembling a steelyard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaleboard</ent><br/
<hw>Scale"board`</hw> <pr>(?; <it>commonly</it> <?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[3d <ets>scale</ets> + <ets>board</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A thin slip of wood used to justify a
page.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thin veneer of leaf of wood used for covering the
surface of articles of furniture, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scaleboard plane</b></col>, <cd>a plane for cutting from
a board a wide shaving forming a scaleboard.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaled</ent><br/
<hw>Scaled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered
with scales, or scalelike structures; -- said of a fish, a reptile, a
moth, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Without scales, or with the scales removed; <as>as,
<ex>scaled</ex> herring</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having feathers which in form,
color, or arrangement somewhat resemble scales; <as>as, the
<ex>scaled</ex> dove</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scaled dove</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
American dove of the genus <gen>Scardafella</gen>. Its colored feather
tips resemble scales.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaleless</ent><br/
<hw>Scale"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of
scales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalene</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*lene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scalenus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>scal<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having the sides and
angles unequal; -- said of a triangle.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having
the axis inclined to the base, as a cone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Designating several
triangular muscles called <xex>scalene muscles</xex>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Of or pertaining to the scalene muscles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scalene muscles</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a group
of muscles, usually three on each side in man, extending from the
cervical vertebrae to the first and second ribs.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalene</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*lene"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A triangle
having its sides and angles unequal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalenohedral</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*le`no*he"dral</hw>
<pr>(sk<adot/*l<emac/`n<osl/*h<emac/"dr<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a
scalenohedron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalenohedron</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*le`no*he"dron</hw> <pr>(-dr<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>skalhno`s</grk> uneven + <grk>"e`dra</grk> seat, base.]</ety>
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>A pyramidal form under the rhombohedral
system, inclosed by twelve faces, each a scalene triangle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaler</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, scales; specifically, a dentist's instrument for removing tartar
from the teeth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scale-winged</ent><br/
<hw>Scale"-winged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the wings covered with small scalelike structures, as the
Lepidoptera; scaly-winged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaliness</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
scaly; roughness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaling</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"ing</hw> <pr>(sk<amac/l"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Adapted for removing scales, as from a fish; <as>as, a
<ex>scaling</ex> knife</as>; adapted for removing scale, as from the
interior of a steam boiler; <as>as, a <ex>scaling</ex> hammer, bar,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Serving as an aid in clambering; <as>as, a
<ex>scaling</ex> ladder, used in assaulting a fortified
place</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaliola</ent><br/
<hw>Scal*io"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Scagliola</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scall</ent><br/
<hw>Scall</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skalli</ets>
a bald head. Cf. <er>Scald</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>A scurf or
scabby disease, especially of the scalp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a dry <qex>scall</qex>, even a leprosy upon the head.</q>
<rj><qau>Lev. xiii. 30.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scall</ent><br/
<hw>Scall</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Scabby; scurfy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalled</ent><br/
<hw>Scalled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Scabby; scurfy;
scall.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/With <xex>scalled</xex> brows
black.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scalled head</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Scald head</cref>, under <er>Scald</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scallion</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escalone</ets>, <ets>eschaloingne</ets>, L. <ets>caepa
Ascalonia</ets> onion of Ascalon; <ets>caepa</ets> onion +
<ets>Ascalonius</ets> of Ascalon, fr. <ets>Ascalo</ets> Ascalon, a town
in Palestine. Cf. <er>Shallot</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A kind of small onion (<spn>Allium Ascalonicum</spn>), native of
Palestine; the eschalot, or shallot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any onion which does not <ldquo/bottom out,<rdquo/
but remains with a thick stem like a leek.</def> <rj><au>Amer.
Cyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scallop</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"lop</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escalope</ets> a shell, probably of German or Dutch origin, and
akin to E. <ets>scale of a fish</ets>; cf. D. <ets>schelp</ets> shell.
See <er>Scale</er> of a fish, and cf. <er>Escalop</er>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>scollop</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of marine bivalve
mollusks of the genus Pecten and allied genera of the family
<fam>Pectinidae</fam>. The shell is usually radially ribbed, and the
edge is therefore often undulated in a characteristic manner. The large
adductor muscle of some the species is much used as food. One species
(<spn>Vola Jacobaeus</spn>) occurs on the coast of Palestine, and its
shell was formerly worn by pilgrims as a mark that they had been to the
Holy Land. Called also <altname>fan shell</altname>. See
<er>Pecten</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common edible scallop of the <geog>Eastern United
States</geog> is <spn>Pecten irradians</spn>; the large sea scallop,
also used as food, is <spn>Pecten Clintonius</spn> syn. <spn>Pecten
tenuicostatus</spn>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of series of segments of circles joined at their
extremities, forming a border like the edge or surface of a scallop
shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One of the shells of a scallop; also, a dish
resembling a scallop shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scallop</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"lop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scalloped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scalloping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To mark or cut the
edge or border of into segments of circles, like the edge or surface of
a scallop shell. See <er>Scallop</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>To bake in scallop shells or
dishes; to prepare with crumbs of bread or cracker, and bake. See
<cref>Scalloped oysters</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalloped</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"loped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Furnished with a scallop; made or done with or in a
scallop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the edge or border cut or marked with
segments of circles. See <er>Scallop</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>Baked in a scallop; cooked with
crumbs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scalloped oysters</b></col> <fld>(Cookery)</fld>,
<cd>opened oysters baked in a deep dish with alternate layers of bread
or cracker crumbs, seasoned with pepper, nutmeg, and butter. This was at
first done in scallop shells.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalloper</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"lop*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who fishes for
scallops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalloping</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"lop*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Fishing for
scallops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalp</ent><br/
<hw>Scalp</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/lp)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scallop</er>.]</ety> <def>A bed of oysters or mussels.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalp</ent><br/
<hw>Scalp</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps akin to D. <ets>schelp</ets>
shell. Cf. <er>Scallop</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That part of the
integument of the head which is usually covered with hair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By the bare <qex>scalp</qex> of Robin Hodd's fat friar,<br/
This fellow were a king for our wild faction!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A part of the skin of the head, with the hair
attached, cut or torn off from an enemy by the Indian warriors of North
America, as a token of victory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: The top; the summit.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scalp lock</b></col>, <cd>a long tuft of hair left on the
crown of the head by the warriors of some tribes of American
Indians.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalp</ent><br/
<hw>Scalp</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scalped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scalping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To deprive of the
scalp; to cut or tear the scalp from the head of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>To remove the skin of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We must <qex>scalp</qex> the whole lid [of the eye].</q>
<rj><qau>J. S. Wells.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Milling)</fld> <def>To brush the hairs or fuzz
from, as wheat grains, in the process of high milling.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalp</ent><br/
<hw>Scalp</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a small, quick profit by
slight fluctuations of the market; -- said of brokers who operate in
this way on their own account.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalpel</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"pel</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/l"p<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scalpellum</ets>, dim. of <ets>scalprum</ets> a knife, akin to
<ets>scalpere</ets> to cut, carve, scrape: cf. F.
<ets>scalpel</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>A small knife with a
thin, keen blade, -- used by surgeons, and in dissecting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalper</ent><br/
<hw>Scalper</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/lp"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who, or that which, scalps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>Same as <cref>Scalping
iron</cref>, under <er>Scalping</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A broker who, dealing on his own account, tries to
get a small and quick profit from slight fluctuations of the
market.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A person who buys and sells the unused parts of
railroad tickets.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><--<sn>5.</sn> <def>A person who buys tickets for entertainment or
sports events and sells them at a profit, often at a much higher price.
Also, <altname>ticket scalper</altname>.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark>--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalping</ent><br/
<hw>Scalp"ing</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/lp"<icr/ng)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos>
from <er>Scalp</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scalping iron</b></col> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>, <cd>an
instrument used in scraping foul and carious bones; a raspatory.</cd> --
<col><b>Scalping knife</b></col>, <cd>a knife used by North American
Indians in scalping.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scalpriform</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"pri*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scalprum</ets> chisel, knife + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Shaped like a chisel; <as>as, the
<ex>scalpriform</ex> incisors of rodents</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaly</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered or
abounding with scales; <as>as, a <ex>scaly</ex> fish</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Scaly</xex> crocodile.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resembling scales, laminae, or layers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Mean; low; <as>as, a <ex>scaly</ex>
fellow</as>.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Composed of scales lying over each
other; <as>as, a <ex>scaly</ex> bulb</as>; covered with scales; <as>as,
a <ex>scaly</ex> stem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scaly ant-eater</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
pangolin.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaly-winged</ent><br/
<hw>Scal"y-winged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Scale-winged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamble</ent><br/
<hw>Scam"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scambled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scambling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OD.
<ets>schampelen</ets> to deviate, to slip, <ets>schampen</ets> to go
away, escape, slip, and E. <ets>scamper</ets>,
<ets>shamble</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To move awkwardly; to be
shuffling, irregular, or unsteady; to sprawl; to shamble.</def>
<ldquo/Some <xex>scambling</xex> shifts.<rdquo/ <au>Dr. H. More.</au>
<ldquo/A fine old hall, but a <xex>scambling</xex> house.<rdquo/
<au>Evelyn.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move about pushing and jostling; to be rude and
turbulent; to scramble.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>scambling</xex> and
unquiet time did push it out of . . . question.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamble</ent><br/
<hw>Scam"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To mangle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scambler</ent><br/
<hw>Scam"bler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>1. One who
scambles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bold intruder upon the hospitality of others; a
mealtime visitor.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamblingly</ent><br/
<hw>Scam"bling*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scambling
manner; with turbulence and noise; with bold intrusiveness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scammel</ent><br/
<ent>Scamell</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scam"ell</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>Scam"mel</hw>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The female bar-tailed
godwit.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Whether this is the <xex>scamel</xex> mentioned by
Shakespeare [<ldquo/Tempest,<rdquo/ ii. 2] is not known.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamillus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sca*mil"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scamilli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., originally, a little
bench, dim. of <ets>scamnum</ets> bench, stool.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A sort of second plinth or block, below the
bases of Ionic and Corinthian columns, generally without moldings, and
of smaller size horizontally than the pedestal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scammoniate</ent><br/
<hw>Scam*mo"ni*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made from
scammony; <as>as, a <ex>scammoniate</ex> aperient</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scammony</ent><br/
<hw>Scam"mo*ny</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/m"m<osl/*n<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>scammon<eacute/e</ets>, L. <ets>scammonia</ets>,
<ets>scammonea</ets>, Gr. <grk>skammwni`a</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A species of bindweed or <gen>Convolvulus</gen>
(<spn>Convolvulus Scammonia</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An inspissated sap obtained from the root of the
<spn>Convolvulus Scammonia</spn>, of a blackish gray color, a nauseous
smell like that of old cheese, and a somewhat acrid taste. It is used in
medicine as a cathartic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamp</ent><br/
<hw>Scamp</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/mp)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escamper</ets> to run away, to make one's escape. Originally, one
who runs away, a fugitive, a vagabond. See <er>Scamper</er>.]</ety>
<def>A rascal; a swindler; a rogue.</def> <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamp</ent><br/
<hw>Scamp</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Scamp</er>,<pos>n.</pos>,
or <er>Scant</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and <er>Skimp</er>.]</ety> <def>To
perform in a hasty, neglectful, or imperfect manner; to do
superficially.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A workman is said to <qex>scamp</qex> his work when he does it in
a superficial, dishonest manner.</q> <rj><qau>Wedgwood.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Much of the <qex>scamping</qex> and dawdling complained of is that
of men in establishments of good repute.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Hughes.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scampavia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scam`pa*vi"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>A long, low war galley used by the Neapolitans and Sicilians in the
early part of the nineteenth century.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamper</ent><br/
<hw>Scam"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scampered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scampering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escamper</ets> to escape, to save one's self; L. <ets>ex</ets> from
+ <ets>campus</ets> the field (sc. of battle). See <er>Camp</er>, and
cf. <er>Decamp</er>, <er>Scamp</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Shamble</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To run with speed; to run or move in a
quick, hurried manner; to hasten away.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lady, however, . . . could not help <qex>scampering</qex>
about the room after a mouse.</q> <rj><qau>S. Sharpe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamper</ent><br/
<hw>Scam"per</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scampering; a hasty
flight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scamperer</ent><br/
<hw>Scam"per*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
scampers.</def> <rj><au>Tyndell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scampish</ent><br/
<hw>Scamp"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or like a scamp;
knavish; <as>as, <ex>scampish</ex> conduct</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scan</ent><br/
<hw>Scan</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/n)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scanned</conjf> <pr>(sk<acr/nd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scanning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>scandere</ets>, <ets>scansum</ets>, to climb, to scan, akin to Skr.
<ets>skand</ets> to spring, leap: cf. F. <ets>scander</ets>. Cf.
<er>Ascend</er>, <er>Descend</er>, <er>Scale</er> a ladder.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To mount by steps; to go through with step by
step.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor stayed till she the highest stage had <qex>scand</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Note that "scand" is not given as a hw, a conjf, or an asp. PCP.
It is presumably an older spelling of "scanned". --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically <fld>(Pros.)</fld>, to go through with,
as a verse, marking and distinguishing the feet of which it is composed;
to show, in reading, the metrical structure of; to recite
metrically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To go over and examine point by point; to examine
with care; to look closely at or into; to scrutinize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The actions of men in high stations are all conspicuous, and
liable to be <qex>scanned</qex> and sifted.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To examine quickly, from point to point, in search
of something specific; <as>as, to <ex>scan</ex> an article for mention
of a particular person</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Electronics)</fld> <def>To form an image or an
electronic representation of, by passing a beam of light or electrons
over, and detecting and recording the reflected or transmitted
signal.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandal</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>scandale</ets>, fr. L. <ets>scandalum</ets>, Gr. <?/, a snare laid
for an enemy, a stumbling block, offense, scandal: cf. OE.
<ets>scandle</ets>, OF. <ets>escandle</ets>. See
<er>Slander</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Offense caused or experienced;
reproach or reprobation called forth by what is regarded as wrong,
criminal, heinous, or flagrant: opprobrium or disgrace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, what a <qex>scandal</qex> is it to our crown,<br/
That two such noble peers as ye should jar!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[I] have brought <qex>scandal</qex><br/
To Israel, diffidence of God, and doubt<br/
In feeble hearts.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Reproachful aspersion; opprobrious censure;
defamatory talk, uttered heedlessly or maliciously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You must not put another <qex>scandal</qex> on him.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My known virtue is from <qex>scandal</qex> free.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Equity)</fld> <def>Anything alleged in pleading
which is impertinent, and is reproachful to any person, or which
derogates from the dignity of the court, or is contrary to good
manners.</def> <rj><au>Daniell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Defamation; detraction; slander; calumny;
opprobrium; reproach; shame; disgrace.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1283 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scandal</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
treat opprobriously; to defame; to asperse; to traduce; to
slander.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do fawn on men and hug them hard<br/
And after <qex>scandal</qex> them.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To scandalize; to offend.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Story.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To defame; traduce; reproach; slander;
calumniate; asperse; vilify; disgrace.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandalize</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"dal*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scandalized</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scandalizing</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[F. <ets>scandaliser</ets>, L. <ets>scandalizare</ets>, from Gr.
<grk>skandali`zein</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To offend the feelings
or the conscience of (a person) by some action which is considered
immoral or criminal; to bring shame, disgrace, or reproach
upon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I demand who they are whom we <qex>scandalize</qex> by using
harmless things.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The congregation looked on in silence, the better class
<qex>scandalized</qex>, and the lower orders, some laughing, others
backing the soldier or the minister, as their fancy dictated.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To reproach; to libel; to defame; to
slander.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To tell his tale might be interpreted into <qex>scandalizing</qex>
the order.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandalous</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"dal*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scandaleux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Giving offense to the
conscience or moral feelings; exciting reprobation; calling out
condemnation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing <qex>scandalous</qex> or offensive unto any.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Disgraceful to reputation; bringing shame or infamy;
opprobrious; <as>as, a <ex>scandalous</ex> crime or vice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Defamatory; libelous; <as>as, a <ex>scandalous</ex>
story</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandalously</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"dal*ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a manner
to give offense; shamefully.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His discourse at table was <qex>scandalously</qex> unbecoming the
dignity of his station.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>With a disposition to impute immorality or
wrong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shun their fault, who, <qex>scandalously</qex> nice,<br/
Will needs mistake an author into vice.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandalousness</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"dal*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
scandalous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandalum magnatum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scan"da*lum mag*na"tum`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L., scandal of
magnates.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A defamatory speech or writing
published to the injury of a person of dignity; -- usually abbreviated
<abbr>scan. mag.</abbr></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandent</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scandens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>scandere</ets> to
climb.]</ety> <def>Climbing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Scandent plants may climb either by twining, as the hop,
or by twisted leafstalks, as the clematis, or by tendrils, as the
passion flower, or by rootlets, as the ivy.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandia</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"di*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Scandium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A chemical earth, the
oxide of scandium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandic</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"dic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to scandium; derived from, or containing,
scandium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandinavian</ent><br/
<hw>Scan`di*na"vi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to Scandinavia, that is, Sweden, Norway, and Denmark.</def>
-- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Scandinavia.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scandium</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. So called
because found in <etsep>Scandinavian</etsep> minerals.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A rare metallic element of the boron group,
whose existence was predicted under the provisional name
<altname>ekaboron</altname> by means of the periodic law, and
subsequently discovered by spectrum analysis in certain rare
Scandinavian minerals (<xex>euxenite</xex> and <xex>gadolinite</xex>).
It has not yet been isolated. Symbol Sc. Atomic weight 44.</def><--
Atomic weight 44.96, at. no. 21; valence 3. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scansion</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scansio</ets>, fr. <ets>scandere</ets>, <ets>scansum</ets>, to
climb. See <er>Scan</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Pros.)</fld> <def>The act of
scanning; distinguishing the metrical feet of a verse by emphasis,
pauses, or otherwise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scansores</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scan*so"res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
L. <ets>scandere</ets>, <ets>scansum</ets>, to climb.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An artificial group of birds formerly regarded
as an order. They are distributed among several orders by modern
ornithologists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The toes are in pairs, two before and two behind, by
which they are enabled to cling to, and climb upon, trees, as the
woodpeckers, parrots, cuckoos, and trogons.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scansorial</ent><br/
<hw>Scan*so"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Capable of climbing; <as>as, the woodpecker is a
<ex>scansorial</ex> bird</as>; adapted for climbing; <as>as, a
<ex>scansorial</ex> foot</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining
to the Scansores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scansorial tail</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a tail
in which the feathers are stiff and sharp at the tip, as in the
woodpeckers.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scant</ent><br/
<hw>Scant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Scanter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Scantest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skamt</ets>, neuter of
<ets>skamr</ets>, <ets>skammr</ets>, short; cf. <ets>skamta</ets> to
dole out, to portion.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not full, large, or
plentiful; scarcely sufficient; less than is wanted for the purpose;
scanty; meager; not enough; <as>as, a <ex>scant</ex> allowance of
provisions or water; a <ex>scant</ex> pattern of cloth for a
garment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His sermon was <qex>scant</qex>, in all, a quarter of an hour.</q>
<rj><qau>Ridley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sparing; parsimonious; chary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be somewhat <qex>scanter</qex> of your maiden presence.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See under <er>Scanty</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scant</ent><br/
<hw>Scant</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scanted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scanting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To limit; to
straiten; to treat illiberally; to stint; <as>as, to <ex>scant</ex> one
in provisions; to <ex>scant</ex> ourselves in the use of
necessaries</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where a man hath a great living laid together and where he is
<qex>scanted</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am <qex>scanted</qex> in the pleasure of dwelling on your
actions.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cut short; to make small, narrow, or scanty; to
curtail.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Scant</xex> not my cups.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scant</ent><br/
<hw>Scant</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fail, or become less; to
scantle; <as>as, the wind <ex>scants</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scant</ent><br/
<hw>Scant</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scant manner; with difficulty;
scarcely; hardly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So weak that he was <qex>scant</qex> able to go down the
stairs.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scant</ent><br/
<hw>Scant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Scantness; scarcity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantily</ent><br/
<hw>Scant"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scanty
manner; not fully; not plentifully; sparingly; parsimoniously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His mind was very <qex>scantily</qex> stored with materials.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- scantily clad, wearing almost no clothing. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantiness</ent><br/
<hw>Scant"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or condition of being
scanty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantle</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>scant</ets>, v.]</ety> <def>To be deficient; to fail.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantle</ent><br/
<hw>Scan"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escanteler</ets>, <ets>eschanteler</ets>, to break into contles;
pref. <ets>es-</ets> (L. <ets>ex</ets>) + <ets>cantel</ets>,
<ets>chantel</ets>, corner, side, piece. Confused with E.
<ets>scant</ets>. See <er>Cantle</er>.]</ety> <def>To scant; to be
niggard of; to divide into small pieces; to cut short or down.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All their pay<br/
Must your discretion <qex>scantle</qex>; keep it back.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantlet</ent><br/
<hw>Scant"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>eschantelet</ets> corner.]</ety> <def>A small pattern; a small
quantity.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantling</ent><br/
<hw>Scant"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scant</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Not plentiful; small;
scanty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantling</ent><br/
<hw>Scant"ling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>eschantillon</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/chantillon</ets>, a sample,
pattern, example. In some senses confused with <ets>scant</ets>
insufficient. See <er>Scantle</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A fragment; a bit; a little piece.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A piece or quantity cut for a special purpose; a sample.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such as exceed not this <qex>scantling</qex>; -- to be solace to
the sovereign and harmless to the people.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A pretty <qex>scantling</qex> of his knowledge may taken by his
deferring to be baptized so many years.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A small quantity; a little bit; not much.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Reducing them to narrow <qex>scantlings</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A piece of timber sawed or cut of a small size, as
for studs, rails, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The dimensions of a piece of timber with regard to
its breadth and thickness; hence, the measure or dimensions of
anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A rough draught; a rude sketch or outline.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A frame for casks to lie upon; a trestle.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantly</ent><br/
<hw>Scant"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a scant manner;
not fully or sufficiently; narrowly; penuriously.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Scarcely; hardly; barely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Scantly</qex> they durst their feeble eyes dispread<br/
Upon that town.</q> <rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We hold a tourney here to-morrow morn,<br/
And there is <qex>scantly</qex> time for half the work.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scantness</ent><br/
<hw>Scant"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or condition of
being scant; narrowness; smallness; insufficiency; scantiness.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Scantness</xex> of outward things.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scanty</ent><br/
<hw>Scant"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Scantier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Scantiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Scant</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Lacking amplitude or extent;
narrow; small; not abundant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His dominions were very narrow and <qex>scanty</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now <qex>scantier</qex> limits the proud arch confine.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Somewhat less than is needed; insufficient; scant;
<as>as, a <ex>scanty</ex> supply of words; a <ex>scanty</ex> supply of
bread</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Sparing; niggardly; parsimonious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In illustrating a point of difficulty, be not too
<qex>scanty</qex> of words.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Scant; narrow; small; poor; deficient; meager;
scarce; chary; sparing; parsimonious; penurious; niggardly;
grudging.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scape</ent><br/
<hw>Scape</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scapus</ets>
shaft, stem, stalk; cf. Gr. <?/ a staff: cf. F. <ets>scape</ets>. Cf.
<er>Scepter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A peduncle
rising from the ground or from a subterranean stem, as in the stemless
violets, the bloodroot, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The long basal joint of the
antennae of an insect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The shaft of a
column.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The apophyge of a shaft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scape</ent><br/
<hw>Scape</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scaped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scaping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Aphetic form of
<ets>escape</ets>.]</ety> <def>To escape.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Poetic.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Out of this prison help that we may <qex>scape</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scape</ent><br/
<hw>Scape</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An escape.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I spake of most disastrous chances, . . .<br/
Of hairbreadth <qex>scapes</qex> in the imminent, deadly breach.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Means of escape; evasion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A freak; a slip; a fault; an escapade.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not pardoning so much as the <qex>scapes</qex> of error and
ignorance.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Loose act of vice or lewdness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapegallows</ent><br/
<hw>Scape"gal`lows</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who has
narrowly escaped the gallows for his crimes.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapegoat</ent><br/
<hw>Scape"goat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Scape</ets>
(for <ets>escape</ets>) + <ets>goat</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Jewish Antiq.)</fld> <def>A goat upon whose head were symbolically
placed the sins of the people, after which he was suffered to escape
into the wilderness.</def> <rj><au>Lev. xvi. 10.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a person or thing that is made to bear blame
for others.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapegrace</ent><br/
<hw>Scape"grace`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A graceless,
unprincipled person; one who is wild and reckless.</def>
<rj><au>Beaconsfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapeless</ent><br/
<hw>Scape"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Destitute of a
scape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapement</ent><br/
<hw>Scape"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scape</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, <er>Escapement</er>.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Escapement</er>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scape-wheel</ent><br/
<hw>Scape"-wheel`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Horol.)</fld>
<def>The wheel in an escapement (as of a clock or a watch) into the
teeth of which the pallets play.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphander</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*phan"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
anything hollowed + <?/, <?/, a man: cf. F.
<ets>scaphandre</ets>.]</ety> <def>The case, or impermeable apparel, in
which a diver can work while under water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphism</ent><br/
<hw>Scaph"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>ska`fh</grk> a trough.]</ety> <def>An ancient mode of punishing
criminals among the Persians, by confining the victim in a trough, with
his head and limbs smeared with honey or the like, and exposed to the
sun and to insects until he died.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphite</ent><br/
<hw>Scaph"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scapha</ets> a boat, fr. Gr. <grk>ska`fh</grk> a boat, anything dug
or scooped out, fr. <grk>ska`ptein</grk> to dig.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Any fossil cephalopod shell of the genus
<gen>Scaphites</gen>, belonging to the Ammonite family and having a
chambered boat-shaped shell. Scaphites are found in the Cretaceous
formation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphocephalic</ent><br/
<hw>Scaph`o*ce*phal"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or affected with,
scaphocephaly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphocephaly</ent><br/
<hw>Scaph`o*ceph"a*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>ska`fh</grk> a boat + <grk>kefalh`</grk> head.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A deformed condition of the skull, in which the
vault is narrow, elongated, and more or less boat-shaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphocerite</ent><br/
<hw>Scaph`o*ce"rite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>ska`fh</grk> boat + E. <ets>cerite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A flattened plate or scale attached to the second joint of the
antennae of many Crustacea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphognathite</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*phog"na*thite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>ska`fh</grk> boat + <grk>gna`qos</grk> jaw.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A thin leaflike appendage (the exopodite) of the
second maxilla of decapod crustaceans. It serves as a pumping organ to
draw the water through the gill cavity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphoid</ent><br/
<hw>Scaph"oid</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>ska`fh</grk> a boat + <ets>-oid</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>scapho<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Resembling a
boat in form; boat-shaped.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The
scaphoid bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scaphoid bone</b></col> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>One of the
carpal bones, which articulates with the radius; the radiale</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>One of the tarsal bones; the navicular bone. See under
<er>Navicular</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapholunar</ent><br/
<hw>Scaph`o*lu"nar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Scapho</ets>id + <ets>lunar</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the scaphoid and lunar bones of the
carpus.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The scapholunar
bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scapholunar bone</b></col>, <cd>a bone formed by the
coalescence of the scaphoid and lunar in the carpus of
carnivora.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaphopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sca*phop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <grk>ska`fh</grk> a boat + <ets>-poda</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A class of marine cephalate Mollusca having a
tubular shell open at both ends, a pointed or spadelike foot for
burrowing, and many long, slender, prehensile oral tentacles. It
includes Dentalium, or the tooth shells, and other similar shells.
Called also <altname>Prosopocephala</altname>, and
<altname>Solenoconcha</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapiform</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"pi*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Resembling a scape, or flower stem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapolite</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"o*lite</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/p"<osl/*l<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <?/ a staff, or L. <ets>scapus</ets> a stem, stalk +
<ets>-lite</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scapolite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mon.)</fld>
<def>A grayish white mineral occurring in tetragonal crystals and in
cleavable masses. It is essentially a silicate of alumina and
soda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>scapolite group</xex> includes scapolite
proper, or wernerite, also meionite, dipyre, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapple</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"ple</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/p"p'l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
OF. <ets>eskapeler</ets>, <ets>eschapler</ets>, to cut, hew, LL.
<ets>scapellare</ets>. Cf. <er>Scabble</er>.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To
work roughly, or shape without finishing, as stone before leaving the
quarry.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To dress in any way short of fine
tooling or rubbing, as stone.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapula</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"u*la</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/p"<usl/*l<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> L. <plw>Scapulae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E.
<plw>Scapulas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The principal bone of the shoulder girdle in
mammals; the shoulder blade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the plates from which the
arms of a crinoid arise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapular</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scapulaire</ets>. Cf. <er>Scapulary</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the scapula or the shoulder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scapular arch</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
pectoral arch. See under <er>pectoral</er>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Scapular region</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Scapular
tract</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a definite longitudinal
area over the shoulder and along each side of the back of a bird, from
which the scapular feathers arise.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapular</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"u*lar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a
special group of feathers which arise from each of the scapular regions
and lie along the sides of the back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapulary</ent><br/
<ent>Scapular</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scap"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scap"u*la*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>scapulaire</ets>, LL.
<ets>scapularium</ets>, <ets>scapulare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>scapula</ets>
shoulder blade.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A loose sleeveless vestment falling in front and behind, worn by
certain religious orders and devout persons.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
name given to two pieces of cloth worn under the ordinary garb and over
the shoulders as an act of devotion.</def> <rj><au>Addis &
Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>A bandage passing over the
shoulder to support it, or to retain another bandage in place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapulary</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"u*la*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Scapular</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapulary</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"u*la*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as 2d
and 3d <er>Scapular</er>.</def><-- But 3d Scapular is (R. C. Ch.), not
(Zool.)! PCP --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapulet</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"u*let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>scapula</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A secondary mouth
fold developed at the base of each of the armlike lobes of the manubrium
of many rhizostome medusae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapulo-</ent><br/
<hw>Scap"u*lo-</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <def>A combining form used in
anatomy to indicate <xex>connection with</xex>, or <xex>relation
to</xex>, <xex>the scapula</xex> or <xex>the shoulder</xex>; <as>as, the
<ex>scapulo</ex>-clavicular articulation, the articulation between the
scapula and clavicle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scapus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sca"pus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>See 1st <er>Scape</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scar</ent><br/
<hw>Scar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>escare</ets>,
F. <ets>eschare</ets> an eschar, a dry slough (cf. It. & Sp.
<ets>escara</ets>), L. <ets>eschara</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/ hearth,
fireplace, scab, eschar. Cf. <er>Eschar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
mark in the skin or flesh of an animal, made by a wound or ulcer, and
remaining after the wound or ulcer is healed; a cicatrix; a mark left by
a previous injury; a blemish; a disfigurement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This earth had the beauty of youth, . . . and not a wrinkle,
<qex>scar</qex>, or fracture on all its body.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A mark left upon a stem or branch
by the fall of a leaf, leaflet, or frond, or upon a seed by the
separation of its support.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scar</ent><br/
<hw>Scar</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scarred</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scarring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To mark with a scar or
scars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet I'll not shed her blood;<br/
Nor <qex>scar</qex> that whiter skin of hers than snow.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His cheeks were deeply <qex>scarred</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scar</ent><br/
<hw>Scar</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To form a scar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scar</ent><br/
<hw>Scar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot. <ets>scar</ets>,
<ets>scaur</ets>, Icel. <ets>sker</ets> a skerry, an isolated rock in
the sea; akin to Dan. <ets>skiaer</ets>, Sw. <ets>sk<aum/r</ets>. Cf.
<er>Skerry</er>.]</ety> <def>An isolated or protruding rock; a steep,
rocky eminence; a bare place on the side of a mountain or steep bank of
earth.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>scaur</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O sweet and far, from cliff and <qex>scar</qex>,<br/
The horns of Elfland faintly blowing.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scar</ent><br/
<hw>Scar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scarus</ets>, a kind of fish,
Gr. <grk>ska`ros</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A marine food
fish, the scarus, or parrot fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1284 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scarabee</ent><br/
<ent>Scarab</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scar"ab</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scar"a*bee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scarabaeus</ets>; cf. F.
<ets>scarab<eacute/e</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of lamellicorn beetles of the genus
<gen>Scarabaeus</gen>, or family <fam>Scarabaeidae</fam>, especially the
sacred, or Egyptian, species (<spn>Scarabaeus sacer</spn>, and
<spn>Scarabaeus Egyptiorum</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Egyptian Archaeology, Jewelry)</fld> <def>A
stylized representation of a scarab beetle carved in stone or faience,
or made in baked clay, usually in a conventionalized form in which the
beetle has its legs held closely at its sides, and commonly having an
inscription on the flat underside; -- a symbol of resurrection, used by
the ancient Egyptians as an ornament or a talisman, and in modern times
used in jewelry, usually by engraving the formalized scarab design on
cabuchon stones. Also used attributively; <as>as, a <ex>scarab</ex>
bracelet [a bracelet containing <ex>scarabs</ex>]; a ring with a
<ex>scarab</ex> [the carved stone itself]</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarabaeus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scar`a*bae"us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Scarab</er> in both senses.</def></p>

<p><ent>Scaraboid</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"a*boid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Scarab</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
the family <fam>Scarabaeidae</fam>, an extensive group which includes
the Egyptian scarab, the tumbledung, and many similar lamellicorn
beetles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaraboid</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"a*boid</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A scaraboid
beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaramouch</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"a*mouch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>scaramouche</ets>, It. <ets>scaramuccio</ets>,
<ets>scaramuccia</ets>, originally the name of a celebrated Italian
comedian; cf. It. <ets>scaramuccia</ets>, <ets>scaramuccio</ets>, F.
<ets>escarmouche</ets>, skirmish. Cf. <er>Skirmish</er>.]</ety> <def>A
personage in the old Italian comedy (derived from Spain) characterized
by great boastfulness and poltroonery; hence, a person of like
characteristics; a buffoon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarce</ent><br/
<hw>Scarce</hw> <pr>(sk<acir/rs)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Scarcer</adjf>
<pr>(sk<acir/r"s<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Scarcest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>scars</ets>, OF.
<ets>escars</ets>, <ets>eschars</ets>, LL. <ets>scarpsus</ets>,
<ets>excarpsus</ets>, for L. <ets>excerptus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>excerpere</ets> to pick out, and hence to contract, to shorten;
<ets>ex</ets> (see <er>Ex-</er>) + <ets>carpere</ets>. See
<er>Carpet</er>, and cf. <er>Excerp</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not
plentiful or abundant; in small quantity in proportion to the demand;
not easily to be procured; rare; uncommon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You tell him silver is <qex>scarcer</qex> now in England, and
therefore risen one fifth in value.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scarcest</qex> of all is a Pescennius Niger on a
medallion well preserved.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Scantily supplied (with); deficient (in); -- with
<xex>of</xex>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A region
<xex>scarce</xex> of prey.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Sparing; frugal; parsimonious; stingy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Too <xex>scarce</xex> ne too sparing.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To make one's self scarce</b></col>, <cd>to decamp; to
depart.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Rare; infrequent; deficient. See
<er>Rare</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarcely</ent><br/
<ent>Scarce</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scarce</hw>, <hw>Scarce"ly</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>adv.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>With difficulty; hardly; scantly; barely; but
just.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a <qex>scarce</qex> well-lighted flame.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The eldest <qex>scarcely</qex> five year was of age.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Slowly she sails, and <qex>scarcely</qex> stems the tides.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had <qex>scarcely</qex> finished, when the laborer arrived who
had been sent for my ransom.</q> <rj><qau>W. Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Frugally; penuriously.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarcement</ent><br/
<hw>Scarce"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch. &
Engin.)</fld> <def>An offset where a wall or bank of earth, etc.,
retreats, leaving a shelf or footing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarcity</ent><br/
<ent>Scarceness</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scarce"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scar"ci*ty</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or condition of
being scarce; smallness of quantity in proportion to the wants or
demands; deficiency; lack of plenty; short supply; penury; <as>as, a
<ex>scarcity</ex> of grain; a great <ex>scarcity</ex> of
beauties</as>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>scarcity</qex> of snow would raise a mutiny at Naples.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Praise . . . owes its value to its <qex>scarcity</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The value of an advantage is enhanced by its
<qex>scarceness</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Collier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Deficiency; lack; want; penury; dearth; rareness;
rarity; infrequency.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scard</ent><br/
<hw>Scard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shard or
fragment.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scare</ent><br/
<hw>Scare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scaring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>skerren</ets>,
<ets>skeren</ets>, Icel. <ets>skirra</ets> to bar, prevent,
<ets>skirrask</ets> to shun , shrink from; or fr. OE. <ets>skerre</ets>,
adj., scared, Icel. <ets>skjarr</ets>; both perhaps akin to E.
<ets>sheer</ets> to turn.]</ety> <def>To frighten; to strike with sudden
fear; to alarm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The noise of thy crossbow<br/
Will <qex>scare</qex> the herd, and so my shoot is lost.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To scare away</b></col>, <cd>to drive away by
frightening.</cd> -- <col><b>To scare up</b></col>, <cd>to find by
search, as if by beating for game.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To alarm; frighten; startle; affright;
terrify.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scare</ent><br/
<hw>Scare</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Fright; esp., sudden fright produced
by a trifling cause, or originating in mistake.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarecrow</ent><br/
<hw>Scare"crow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Anything set up to frighten crows or other birds from cornfields;
hence, anything terrifying without danger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>scarecrow</qex> set to frighten fools away.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person clad in rags and tatters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No eye hath seen such <qex>scarecrows</qex>. I'll not march with
them through Coventry, that's flat.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The black tern.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarefire</ent><br/
<hw>Scare"fire`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
alarm of fire.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fire causing alarm.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarf</ent><br/
<hw>Scarf</hw> <pr>(sk<aum/rf)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>skarfr</ets>.]</ety> <def>A cormorant.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarf</ent><br/
<hw>Scarf</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Scarfs</plw>,
rarely <plw>Scarves</plw> <pr>(sk<aum/rvz)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>escharpe</ets> a pilgrim's scrip, or wallet (hanging about the
neck), F. <ets><eacute/charpe</ets> sash, scarf; probably from OHG.
<ets>scharpe</ets> pocket; also (from the French) Dan.
<ets>skiaerf</ets>; Sw. <ets>sk<aum/rp</ets>, Prov. G.
<ets>sch<aum/rfe</ets>, LG. <ets>scherf</ets>, G.
<ets>sch<aum/rpe</ets>; and also AS. <ets>scearf</ets> a fragment;
possibly akin to E. <ets>scrip</ets> a wallet. Cf. <er>Scarp</er> a
scarf.]</ety> <def>An article of dress of a light and decorative
character, worn loosely over the shoulders or about the neck or the
waist; a light shawl or handkerchief for the neck; also, a cravat; a
neckcloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Put on your hood and <qex>scarf</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With care about the banners, <qex>scarves</qex>, and staves.</q>
<rj><qau>R. Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarf</ent><br/
<hw>Scarf</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scarfed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scarfing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw on loosely;
to put on like a scarf.</def> <ldquo/My sea-gown <xex>scarfed</xex>
about me.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To dress with a scarf, or as with a scarf; to cover
with a loose wrapping.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarf</ent><br/
<hw>Scarf</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Sw. <ets>skarfva</ets> to eke
out, to join together, <ets>skarf</ets> a seam, joint; cf. Dan.
<ets>skarre</ets> to joint, to unite timber, Icel. <ets>skara</ets> to
clinch the planks of a boat, G. <ets>scharben</ets> to chop, to cut
small.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To form a scarf on the end or edge of,
as for a joint in timber, metal rods, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To
unite, as two pieces of timber or metal, by a scarf joint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarf</ent><br/
<hw>Scarf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>In a piece
which is to be united to another by a scarf joint, the part of the end
or edge that is tapered off, rabbeted, or notched so as to be thinner
than the rest of the piece.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A scarf
joint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scarf joint</b></col> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A joint made by
overlapping and bolting or locking together the ends of two pieces of
timber that are halved, notched, or cut away so that they will fit each
other and form a lengthened beam of the same size at the junction as
elsewhere</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A joint formed by welding, riveting, or
brazing together the overlapping scarfed ends, or edges, of metal rods,
sheets, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarf weld</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Weld</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarfskin</ent><br/
<hw>Scarf"skin`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Epidermis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarification</ent><br/
<hw>Scar`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scarificatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scarification</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The act of scarifying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarificator</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"i*fi*ca`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scarificateur</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>An instrument,
principally used in cupping, containing several lancets moved
simultaneously by a spring, for making slight incisions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarifier</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"i*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who scarifies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>The instrument used for
scarifying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>An implement for stripping and
loosening the soil, without bringing up a fresh surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have your <qex>scarifiers</qex> to make the ground clean.</q>
<rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarify</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scarified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scarifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>scarifier</ets>, L. <ets>scarificare</ets>, <ets>scarifare</ets>,
fr. Gr. <?/ to scratch up, fr. <?/ a pointed instrument.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To scratch or cut the skin of; esp. <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
to make small incisions in, by means of a lancet or scarificator, so as
to draw blood from the smaller vessels without opening a large
vein.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>To stir the surface soil of, as
a field.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarious</ent><br/
<ent>Scariose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sca"ri*ose</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sca"ri*ous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>scarieux</ets>, NL.
<ets>scariosus</ets>. Cf. <er>Scary</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Thin, dry, membranous, and not green.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarlatina</ent><br/
<hw>Scar`la*ti"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.: cf. F.
<ets>scarlatine</ets>. See <er>Scarlet</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Scarlet fever.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Scar`la*ti"nal</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> -- <wf>Scar*lat"i*nous</wf> <pr>(# <or/
#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarless</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from
scar.</def> <rj><au>Drummond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarlet</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scarlat</ets>, <ets>scarlet</ets>, OF. <ets>escarlate</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/carlate</ets> (cf. Pr. <ets>escarlat</ets>,
<ets>escarlata</ets>, Sp. & Pg. <ets>escarlata</ets>, It.
<ets>scarlatto</ets>, LL. <ets>scarlatum</ets>), from Per.
<ets>sakirl<amac/t</ets>.]</ety> <def>A deep bright red tinged with
orange or yellow, -- of many tints and shades; a vivid or bright red
color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cloth of a scarlet color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All her household are clothed with <qex>scarlet</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xxxi. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarlet</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"let</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the color called scarlet;
<as>as, a <ex>scarlet</ex> cloth or thread</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scarlet admiral</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the red
admiral. See under <er>Red</er>. -- Scarlet bean <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, a
kind of bean (<spn>Phaseolus multiflorus</spn>) having scarlet flowers;
scarlet runner.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarlet fever</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a contagious febrile disease characterized by
inflammation of the fauces and a scarlet rash, appearing usually on the
second day, and ending in desquamation about the sixth or seventh
day.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarlet fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
telescope fish; -- so called from its red color. See under
<er>Telescope</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarlet ibis</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Ibis</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarlet
maple</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the red maple. See
<er>Maple</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarlet mite</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of bright red
carnivorous mites found among grass and moss, especially <spn>Thombidium
holosericeum</spn> and allied species. The young are parasitic upon
spiders and insects.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarlet oak</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a species of oak (<spn>Quercus coccinea</spn>) of
the United States; -- so called from the scarlet color of its leaves in
autumn.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarlet runner</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the scarlet bean.</cd> -- <col><b>Scarlet tanager</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Tanager</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarlet</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"let</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To dye or tinge with
scarlet.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ashy paleness of my cheek<br/
Is <qex>scarleted</qex> in ruddy flakes of wrath.</q>
<rj><qau>Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarmoge</ent><br/
<ent>Scarmage</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scar"mage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scar"moge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slight contest; a skirmish. See
<er>Skirmish</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such cruel game my <qex>scarmoges</qex> disarms.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarn</ent><br/
<hw>Scarn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skarn</ets>;
akin to AS. <ets>scearn</ets>. Cf. <er>Shearn</er>.]</ety>
<def>Dung.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scarn bee</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a dung
beetle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaroid</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"roid</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Scarus</ets> +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
<fam>Scaridae</fam>, a family of marine fishes including the parrot
fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarp</ent><br/
<hw>Scarp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escharpe</ets>. See 2d <er>Scarf</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>A band in the same position as the bend sinister, but only half as
broad as the latter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarp</ent><br/
<hw>Scarp</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Aphetic form of
<er>Escarp</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>The slope of
the ditch nearest the parapet; the escarp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A steep descent or declivity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarp</ent><br/
<hw>Scarp</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scarped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scarping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cut down perpendicularly, or
nearly so; <as>as, to <ex>scarp</ex> the face of a ditch or a
rock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From <qex>scarped</qex> cliff and quarried stone.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sweep ruins from the <qex>scarped</qex> mountain.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarring</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"ring</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scar; a
mark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We find upon the limestone rocks the <qex>scarrings</qex> of the
ancient glacier which brought the bowlder here.</q>
<rj><qau>Tyndall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarry</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bearing scars or marks
of wounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarry</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 4th <er>Scar</er>.]</ety>
<def>Like a scar, or rocky eminence; containing scars.</def>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scarus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sca"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Scar</er> a kind of fish.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
Mediterranean food fish (<spn>Sparisoma scarus</spn>) of excellent
quality and highly valued by the Romans; -- called also <altname>parrot
fish</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scary</ent><br/
<hw>Sca"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>scare</ets> scraggy.]</ety> <def>Barren land having only a thin
coat of grass.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scary</ent><br/
<hw>Scar"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Scare</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Subject to sudden alarm.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. U. S.]</mark> <rj><au>Whittier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Causing fright; alarming.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scasely</ent><br/
<hw>Scase"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Scarcely;
hardly.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Robynson (More's
Utopia)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scat</ent><br/
<hw>Scat</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/t)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>Go away;
begone; away; -- chiefly used in driving off a cat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatt</ent><br/
<ent>Scat</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scat</hw>, <hw>Scatt</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>skattr</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tribute.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/Seizing <xex>scatt</xex> and treasure.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scat</ent><br/
<hw>Scat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shower of rain.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatch</ent><br/
<hw>Scatch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>escache</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of bit for the bridle of a horse;
-- called also <altname>scatchmouth</altname>.</def>
<rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatches</ent><br/
<hw>Scatch"es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>eschaces</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/chasses</ets>, fr. D.
<ets>schaats</ets> a high-heeled shoe, a skate. See <er>Skate</er>, for
the foot.]</ety> <def>Stilts.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scate</ent><br/
<hw>Scate</hw> <pr>(sk<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skate</er>, for the foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatebrous</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"e*brous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scatebra</ets> a gushing up of water, from <ets>scatere</ets> to
bubble, gush.]</ety> <def>Abounding with springs.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scath</ent><br/
<hw>Scath</hw> <pr>(sk<acr/th; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>ska<edh/i</ets>; akin to Dan. <ets>skade</ets>, Sw.
<ets>skada</ets>, AS. <ets>scea<edh/a</ets>, <ets>sca<edh/a</ets>, foe,
injurer, OS. <ets>ska<edh/o</ets>, D. <ets>schade</ets>, harm, injury,
OHG. <ets>scade</ets>, G. <ets>schade</ets>, <ets>schaden</ets>; cf. Gr.
<grk>'askhqh`s</grk> unharmed. Cf. <er>Scathe</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Harm; damage; injury; hurt; waste; misfortune.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>scathe</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But she was somedeal deaf, and that was <qex>skathe</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Great mercy, sure, for to enlarge a thrall,<br/
Whose freedom shall thee turn to greatest <qex>scath</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wherein Rome hath done you any <qex>scath</qex>,<br/
Let him make treble satisfaction.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scath</ent><br/
<ent>Scathe</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scathe</hw> <pr>(sk<amac/<th/; 277)</pr>, <hw>Scath</hw>
<pr>(sk<acr/th; 277)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scathed</conjf> <pr>(sk<amac/<th/d or
sk<acr/tht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scathing</conjf>
<pr>(sk<amac/<th/"<icr/ng or sk<acr/th"-)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>ska<edh/a</ets>; akin to AS. <ets>scea<edh/an</ets>,
<ets>sce<edh/<edh/an</ets>, Dan. <ets>skade</ets>, Sw. <ets>skada</ets>,
D. & G. <ets>schaden</ets>, OHG. <ets>scad<omac/n</ets>, Goth.
<ets>ska<thorn/jan</ets>.]</ety> <def>To do harm to; to injure; to
damage; to waste; to destroy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As when heaven's fire<br/
Hath <qex>scathed</qex> the forest oaks or mountain pines.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Strokes of calamity that <qex>scathe</qex> and scorch the
soul.</q> <rj><qau>W. Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scathful</ent><br/
<hw>Scath"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Harmful; doing
damage; pernicious.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Scath"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scathless</ent><br/
<hw>Scath"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Unharmed.</def> <rj><au>R. L.
Stevenson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He, too, . . . is to be dismissed scathless.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scathly</ent><br/
<hw>Scath"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Injurious; scathful.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatter</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scattered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scattering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scateren</ets>. See <er>Shatter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
strew about; to sprinkle around; to throw down loosely; to deposit or
place here and there, esp. in an open or sparse order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And some are <qex>scattered</qex> all the floor about.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why should my muse enlarge on Libyan swains,<br/
Their <qex>scattered</qex> cottages, and ample plains?</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Teach the glad hours to <qex>scatter</qex>, as they fly,<br/
Soft quiet, gentle love, and endless joy.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to separate in different directions; to
reduce from a close or compact to a loose or broken order; to dissipate;
to disperse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Scatter</qex> and disperse the giddy Goths.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, to frustrate, disappoint, and overthrow;
<as>as, to <ex>scatter</ex> hopes, plans, or the like</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To disperse; dissipate; spread; strew.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatter</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be dispersed or dissipated;
to disperse or separate; <as>as, clouds <ex>scatter</ex> after a
storm</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatter-brain</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter-brain`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A giddy or
thoughtless person; one incapable of concentration or attention.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>scatter-brains</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatter-brained</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter-brained`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Giddy;
thoughtless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scattered</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"tered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Dispersed; dissipated; sprinkled, or loosely spread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Irregular in position; having no
regular order; <as>as, <ex>scattered</ex> leaves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Scat"tered*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Scat"tered*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scattergood</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter*good`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who wastes;
a spendthrift.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scattering</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Going or falling in various
directions; not united or aggregated; divided among many; <as>as,
<ex>scattering</ex> votes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scattering</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act of strewing about;
something scattered.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatteringly</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scattering manner;
dispersedly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scatterling</ent><br/
<hw>Scat"ter*ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Scatter</ets> + <ets>-ling</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who has no
fixed habitation or residence; a vagabond.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Foreign <xex>scatterlings</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaturient</ent><br/
<hw>Sca*tu"ri*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>scaturiens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>scaturire</ets> gush out, from
<ets>scatere</ets> to bubble, gush.]</ety> <def>Gushing forth; full to
overflowing; effusive.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A pen so <qex>scaturient</qex> and unretentive.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaturiginous</ent><br/
<hw>Scat`u*rig"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scaturiginosus</ets>, fr. <ets>scaturigo</ets> gushing water. See
<er>Scaturient</er>.]</ety> <def>Abounding with springs.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaup</ent><br/
<hw>Scaup</hw> <pr>(sk<add/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scalp</er> a bed of oysters or mussels.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
bed or stratum of shellfish; scalp.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><--
Websters omits the "[" before "See" !! PCP --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A scaup duck. See
below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scaup duck</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
several species of northern ducks of the genus <gen>Aythya</gen>, or
<gen>Fuligula</gen>. The adult males are, in large part, black. The
three North American species are: the greater scaup duck (<spn>Aythya
marila</spn>, var. <var>nearctica</var>), called also
<altname>broadbill</altname>, <altname>bluebill</altname>,
<altname>blackhead</altname>, <altname>flock duck</altname>,
<altname>flocking fowl</altname>, and <altname>raft duck</altname>; the
lesser scaup duck (<spn>Aythya affinis</spn>), called also
<altname>little bluebill</altname>, <altname>river broadbill</altname>,
and <altname>shuffler</altname>; the tufted, or ring-necked, scaup duck
(<spn>Aythya collaris</spn>), called also <altname>black jack</altname>,
<altname>ringneck</altname>, <altname>ringbill</altname>,
<altname>ringbill shuffler</altname>, etc. The common European scaup, or
mussel, duck (<spn>Aythya marila</spn>), closely resembles the American
variety.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1285 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scauper</ent><br/
<hw>Scaup"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scalper</er>.]</ety> <def>A tool with a semicircular edge, -- used
by engravers to clear away the spaces between the lines of an
engraving.</def> <rj><au>Fairholt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scaur</ent><br/
<hw>Scaur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A precipitous bank or
rock; a scar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scavage</ent><br/
<hw>Scav"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>scavagium</ets>, fr. AS. <ets>sce<aacute/wian</ets> to look at, to
inspect. See <er>Show</er>.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A toll
or duty formerly exacted of merchant strangers by mayors, sheriffs,
etc., for goods shown or offered for sale within their precincts.</def>
<rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scavenge</ent><br/
<hw>Scav"enge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cleanse, as
streets, from filth.</def> <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to salvage (usable items or material) from discarded
or waste material.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To remove (burned gases) from the cylinder after a
working stroke.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scavenge</ent><br/
<hw>Scav"enge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<fld>(Internal-combustion Engines)</fld> <def>To remove the burned gases
from the cylinder after a working stroke; <as>as, this engine does not
<ex>scavenge</ex> well</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scavenger</ent><br/
<hw>Scav"en*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scavager</ets> an officer with various duties, originally attending
to <ets>scavage</ets>, fr. OE. & E. <ets>scavage</ets>. See
<er>Scavage</er>, <er>Show</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>A person
whose employment is to clean the streets of a city, by scraping or
sweeping, and carrying off the filth. The name is also applied to any
animal which devours refuse, carrion, or anything injurious to
health.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scavenger beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
beetle which feeds on decaying substances, as the carrion beetle.</cd>
-- <col><b>Scavenger crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any crab
which feeds on dead animals, as the spider crab.</cd> --
<col><b>Scavenger's daughter</b></col> <ety>[corrupt. of
<ets>Skevington's daughter</ets>]</ety>, <cd>an instrument of torture
invented by Sir W. <etsep>Skevington</etsep>, which so compressed the
body as to force the blood to flow from the nostrils, and sometimes from
the hands and feet.</cd> <rj><au>Am. Cyc.</au></rj> </cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scavenger hunt</ent><br/
<hw>Scav"en*ger hunt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a game in
which individuals or teams are given a list of items and must go out,
gather them together without purchasing them, and bring them back; the
first person or team to return with the complete list is the winner. The
items are sometimes common but often of a humorous sort.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scavenging</ent><br/
<hw>Scav"eng*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Scavenge</er>.</mord> Hence, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Internal-combustion
Engines)</fld> <def>Act or process of expelling the exhaust gases from
the cylinder by some special means, as, in many four-cycle engines, by
utilizing the momentum of the exhaust gases in a long exhaust
pipe.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scazon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sca"zon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>ska`zwn</grk>, fr. <grk>ska`zein</grk> to limp.]</ety> <fld>(Lat.
Pros.)</fld> <def>A choliamb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scelerat</ent><br/
<hw>Scel"er*at</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sc<eacute/l<eacute/rat</ets> from L. <ets>sceleratus</ets>, p. p.
of <ets>scelerare</ets> to pollute, from <ets>scelus</ets>,
<ets>sceleris</ets>, a crime.]</ety> <def>A villain; a criminal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cheyne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scelestic</ent><br/
<hw>Sce*les"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scelestus</ets>, from <ets>scelus</ets> wickedness.]</ety>
<def>Evil; wicked; atrocious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Scelestic</xex> villainies.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scelet</ent><br/
<hw>Scel"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Skeleton</er>.]</ety> <def>A mummy; a skeleton.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scena</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sce"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A scene in an opera.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>An accompanied dramatic recitative, interspersed with
passages of melody, or followed by a full aria.</def>
<rj><au>Rockstro.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scenario</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sce*na"ri*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>A preliminary sketch of the plot, or main incidents, of an
opera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scenary</ent><br/
<hw>Scen"a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>scaenarius</ets> belonging to the stage.]</ety> <def>Scenery.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scene</ent><br/
<hw>Scene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scaena</ets>,
<ets>scena</ets>, Gr. <grk>skhnh`</grk> a covered place, a tent, a
stage.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The structure on which a spectacle or
play is exhibited; the part of a theater in which the acting is done,
with its adjuncts and decorations; the stage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The decorations and fittings of a stage,
representing the place in which the action is supposed to go on; one of
the slides, or other devices, used to give an appearance of reality to
the action of a play; <as>as, to paint <ex>scenes</ex>; to shift the
<ex>scenes</ex>; to go behind the <ex>scenes</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>So much of a play as passes without change of
locality or time, or important change of character; hence, a subdivision
of an act; a separate portion of a play, subordinate to the act, but
differently determined in different plays; <as>as, an act of four
<ex>scenes</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My dismal <qex>scene</qex> I needs must act alone.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The place, time, circumstance, etc., in which
anything occurs, or in which the action of a story, play, or the like,
is laid; surroundings amid which anything is set before the imagination;
place of occurrence, exhibition, or action.</def> <ldquo/In Troy, there
lies the <xex>scene</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The world is a vast <qex>scene</qex> of strife.</q> <rj><qau>J. M.
Mason.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An assemblage of objects presented to the view at
once; a series of actions and events exhibited in their connection; a
spectacle; a show; an exhibition; a view.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Through what new <qex>scenes</qex> and changes must we pass!</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A landscape, or part of a landscape;
scenery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A sylvan <qex>scene</qex> with various greens was drawn,<br/
Shades on the sides, and in the midst a lawn.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>An exhibition of passionate or strong feeling before
others; often, an artificial or affected action, or course of action,
done for effect; a theatrical display.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Probably no lover of <qex>scenes</qex> would have had very long to
wait for some explosions between parties, both equally ready to take
offense, and careless of giving it.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Behind the scenes</b></col>, <cd>behind the scenery of a
theater; out of the view of the audience, but in sight of the actors,
machinery, etc.; hence, conversant with the hidden motives and agencies
of what appears to public view.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scene</ent><br/
<hw>Scene</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To exhibit as a scene; to make a
scene of; to display.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Abp.
Sancroft.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sceneful</ent><br/
<hw>Scene"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having much
scenery.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sceneman</ent><br/
<hw>Scene"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scenemen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The man who manages the
movable scenes in a theater.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scenery</ent><br/
<hw>Scen"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Assemblage of scenes; the paintings and hangings representing the
scenes of a play; the disposition and arrangement of the scenes in which
the action of a play, poem, etc., is laid; representation of place of
action or occurrence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sum of scenes or views; general aspect, as regards
variety and beauty or the reverse, in a landscape; combination of
natural views, as woods, hills, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Never need an American look beyond his own country for the sublime
and beautiful of natural <qex>scenery</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sceneshifter</ent><br/
<hw>Scene"shift`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who moves
the scenes in a theater; a sceneman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scenical</ent><br/
<ent>Scenic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scen"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scaenicus</ets>,
<ets>scenicus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>sc<eacute/nique</ets>. See
<er>Scene</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to scenery; of the nature
of scenery; theatrical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All these situations communicate a <qex>scenical</qex> animation
to the wild romance, if treated dramatically.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scenograph</ent><br/
<hw>Scen"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scenography</er>.]</ety> <def>A perspective representation or
general view of an object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scenographical</ent><br/
<ent>Scenographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scen`o*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Scen`o*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>sc<eacute/nographique</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to scenography; drawn in perspective.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Scen`o*graph"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scenography</ent><br/
<hw>Sce*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scaenographia</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/ scene, stage +
<grk>gra`fein</grk> to write: cf. F.
<ets>sc<eacute/nographie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art or act of
representing a body on a perspective plane; also, a representation or
description of a body, in all its dimensions, as it appears to the
eye.</def> <rj><au>Greenhill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scent</ent><br/
<hw>Scent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scented</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scenting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Originally <ets>sent</ets>, fr.
F. <ets>sentir</ets> to feel, to smell. See <er>Sense</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To perceive by the olfactory organs; to smell; <as>as,
to <ex>scent</ex> game, as a hound does</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Methinks I <qex>scent</qex> the morning air.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To imbue or fill with odor; to perfume.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Balm from a silver box distilled around,<br/
Shall all bedew the roots, and <qex>scent</qex> the sacred ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scent</ent><br/
<hw>Scent</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To have a smell.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thunderbolts . . . do <qex>scent</qex> strongly of brimstone.</q>
<rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hunt animals by means of the sense of
smell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scent</ent><br/
<hw>Scent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which, issuing from
a body, affects the olfactory organs of animals; odor; smell; <as>as,
the <ex>scent</ex> of an orange, or of a rose; the <ex>scent</ex> of
musk</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With lavish hand diffuses <qex>scents</qex> ambrosial.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, the odor left by an animal on the
ground in passing over it; <as>as, dogs find or lose the
<ex>scent</ex></as>; hence, course of pursuit; track of
discovery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He gained the observations of innumerable ages, and traveled upon
the same <qex>scent</qex> into Ethiopia.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The power of smelling; the sense of smell; <as>as, a
hound of nice <ex>scent</ex>; to divert the <ex>scent</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>I. Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scentful</ent><br/
<hw>Scent"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of
scent or odor; odorous.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>scentful</xex>
nosegay.<rdquo/ <rj><au>W. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of quick or keen smell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scentful</qex> osprey by the rock had fished.</q>
<rj><qau>W. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scentingly</ent><br/
<hw>Scent"ing*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By scent.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scentless</ent><br/
<hw>Scent"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no scent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scentless</qex> and the scented rose.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scepsis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scep"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/ doubt, fr. <?/ to consider: cf. G. <ets>skepsis</ets>. See
<er>Skeptic</er>.]</ety> <def>Skepticism; skeptical philosophy.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Among their products were the system of Locke, the
<qex>scepsis</qex> of Hume, the critical philosophy of Kant.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Martineau.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sceptre</ent><br/
<ent>Scepter</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scep"ter</hw>, <hw>Scep"tre</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sceptre</ets>, L. <ets>sceptrum</ets>, from
Gr. <?/ a staff to lean upon, a scepter; probably akin to E.
<ets>shaft</ets>. See <er>Shaft</er>, and cf. <er>Scape</er> a stem,
shaft.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A staff or baton borne by a sovereign, as
a ceremonial badge or emblem of authority; a royal mace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the king held out Esther the golden <qex>scepter</qex> that
was in his hand.</q> <rj><qau>Esther v. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, royal or imperial power or authority;
sovereignty; <as>as, to assume the <ex>scepter</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scepter</qex> shall not depart from Judah, nor a lawgiver
from between his feet, until Shiloh come.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xlix.
10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sceptre</ent><br/
<ent>Scepter</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scep"ter</hw>, <hw>Scep"tre</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sceptered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>
or <conjf>Sceptred</conjf> (<?/); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sceptering</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Sceptring</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To endow with the scepter, or emblem of
authority; to invest with royal authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To Britain's queen the <qex>sceptered</qex> suppliant bends.</q>
<rj><qau>Tickell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scepterellate</ent><br/
<hw>Scep`ter*el"late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a straight shaft with whorls of spines; -- said of certain
sponge spicules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sceptreless</ent><br/
<ent>Scepterless</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scep"ter*less</hw>, <hw>Scep"tre*less</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no scepter; without authority; powerless;
<as>as, a <ex>scepterless</ex> king</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scepticism</ent><br/
<ent>Sceptical</ent><br/
<ent>Sceptic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scep"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scep"tic*al</hw>,
<hw>Scep"ti*cism</hw>, etc. }</mhw> <def>See <er>Skeptic</er>,
<er>Skeptical</er>, <er>Skepticism</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sceptral</ent><br/
<hw>Scep"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
a scepter; like a scepter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scern</ent><br/
<hw>Scern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To discern; to
perceive.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schade</ent><br/
<hw>Schade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Shade; shadow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ English words now beginning with <xex>sh</xex>, like
<xex>shade</xex>, were formerly often spelled with a <xex>c</xex>
between the <xex>s</xex> and <xex>h</xex>; <xex>as</xex>,
<xex>schade</xex>; <xex>schame</xex>; <xex>schape</xex>;
<xex>schort</xex>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schah</ent><br/
<hw>Schah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shah</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schappe</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schap"pe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G. dial.
(Swiss), waste, impurity.]</ety> <def>A silk yarn or fabric made out of
carded spun silk.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schatchen</ent><br/
<hw>Schat"chen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Yiddish, fr. NHeb.
<ets>shadkh<amac/n</ets>, fr. <ets>sh<amac/dakh</ets> to bring about a
marriage, orig., to persuade.]</ety> <def>A person whose business is
marriage brokage; a marriage broker, esp. among certain Jews.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schediasm</ent><br/
<hw>Sche"di*asm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ an
extempore, fr. <?/ to do offhand, <?/ sudden, fr. <?/ near.]</ety>
<def>Cursory writing on a loose sheet.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schedule</ent><br/
<hw>Sched"ule</hw> <pr>(?; <it>in England commonly</it> ?; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>c<eacute/dule</ets>, formerly also spelt
<ets>schedule</ets>, L. <ets>schedula</ets>, dim. of scheda, scida, a
strip of papyrus bark, a leaf of paper; akin to (or perh. from) Gr. <?/
a tablet, leaf, and to L. <ets>scindere</ets> to cleave, Gr. <?/. See
<er>Schism</er>, and cf. <er>Cedule</er>.]</ety> <def>A written or
printed scroll or sheet of paper; a document; especially, a formal list
or inventory; a list or catalogue annexed to a larger document, as to a
will, a lease, a statute, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. Timetable, esp. a list of times at which a conveyance is
expected to arrive or leave. 3. Program, a list of items which will
occur during an event, usu. with the expected time for each item. 4.
Agenda. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Catalogue; list; inventory. see
<er>List</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schedule</ent><br/
<hw>Sched"ule</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To form into, or place in, a
schedule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheele's green</ent><br/
<hw>Scheele's" green`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[See
<er>Scheelite</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See under
<er>Green</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheelin</ent><br/
<hw>Scheel"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Scheelium.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheelite</ent><br/
<hw>Scheel"ite</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From C.W.
<ets>Scheele</ets>, a Swedish chemist.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Calcium tungstate, a mineral of a white or pale yellowish color and
of the tetragonal system of crystallization.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheelium</ent><br/
<hw>Schee"li*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. From C.W.
<ets>Scheele</ets>, who discovered it.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The metal tungsten.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheik</ent><br/
<hw>Scheik</hw> <pr>(sh<emac/k <it>or</it> sh<amac/k)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Sheik</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schelly</ent><br/
<hw>Schel"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The powan.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schema</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sche"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Schemata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Schemas</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[G. See <er>Scheme</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Kantian
Philos.)</fld> <def>An outline or image universally applicable to a
general conception, under which it is likely to be presented to the
mind; <as>as, five dots in a line are a <ex>schema</ex> of the number
five; a preceding and succeeding event are a <ex>schema</ex> of cause
and effect</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schematic</ent><br/
<hw>Sche*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/
pretended.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a scheme or a
schema.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schematism</ent><br/
<hw>Sche"ma*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sch<eacute/matisme</ets> (cf. L. <ets>schematismos</ets> florid
speech), fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to form. See <er>Scheme</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>Combination of the aspects of
heavenly bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Particular form or disposition of a thing; an
exhibition in outline of any systematic arrangement.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schematist</ent><br/
<hw>Sche"ma*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One given to
forming schemes; a projector; a schemer.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schematize</ent><br/
<hw>Sche"ma*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sch<eacute/matiser</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>To form a scheme or
schemes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheme</ent><br/
<hw>Scheme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>schema</ets> a
rhetorical figure, a shape, figure, manner, Gr. <?/, <?/, form, shape,
outline, plan, fr. <?/, <?/, to have or hold, to hold out, sustain,
check, stop; cf. Skr. <ets>sah</ets> to be victorious, to endure, to
hold out, AS. <ets>sige</ets> victory, G. <ets>sieg</ets>.
<ets>Cf</ets>. <er>Epoch</er>, <er>Hectic</er>, <er>School</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A combination of things connected and adjusted by
design; a system.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The appearance and outward <qex>scheme</qex> of things.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such a <qex>scheme</qex> of things as shall at once take in time
and eternity.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Arguments . . . sufficient to support and demonstrate a whole
<qex>scheme</qex> of moral philosophy.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Edwards.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Revolution came and changed his whole <qex>scheme</qex> of
life.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A plan or theory something to be done; a design; a
project; <as>as, to form a <ex>scheme</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stoical <qex>scheme</qex> of supplying our wants by lopping
off our desires, is like cutting off our feet when we want shoes.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any lineal or mathematical diagram; an
outline.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To draw an exact <qex>scheme</qex> of Constantinople, or a map of
France.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>A representation of the aspects
of the celestial bodies for any moment or at a given event.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A blue silk case, from which was drawn a <qex>scheme</qex> of
nativity.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Plan; project; contrivance; purpose; device;
plot.</syn> -- <usage><er>Scheme</er>, <er>Plan</er>. <xex>Scheme</xex>
and <xex>plan</xex> are subordinate to <xex>design</xex>; they propose
modes of carrying our designs into effect. <xex>Scheme</xex> is the
least definite of the two, and lies more in speculation. A
<xex>plan</xex> is drawn out into details with a view to being carried
into effect. As <xex>schemes</xex> are speculative, they often prove
visionary; hence the opprobrious use of the words <xex>schemer</xex> and
<xex>scheming</xex>. <xex>Plans</xex>, being more practical, are more
frequently carried into effect.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He forms the well-concerted <qex>scheme</qex> of mischief;<br/
'T is fixed, 't is done, and both are doomed to death.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Artists and <qex>plans</qex> relieved my solemn hours;<br/
I founded palaces, and planted bowers.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheme</ent><br/
<hw>Scheme</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Schemed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scheming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make a scheme of; to plan; to
design; to project; to plot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That wickedness which <qex>schemed</qex>, and executed, his
destruction.</q> <rj><qau>G. Stuart.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheme</ent><br/
<hw>Scheme</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To form a scheme or
schemes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schemeful</ent><br/
<hw>Scheme"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of schemes or
plans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schemer</ent><br/
<hw>Schem"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who forms
schemes; a projector; esp., a plotter; an intriguer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Schemers</qex> and confederates in guilt.</q>
<rj><qau>Paley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scheming</ent><br/
<hw>Schem"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Given to forming schemes; artful;
intriguing.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Schem"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schemist</ent><br/
<hw>Schem"ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A schemer.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Waterland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schene</ent><br/
<hw>Schene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>schoenus</ets>, Gr. <?/ a rush, a reed, a land measure: cf. F.
<ets>sch<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Antiq.)</fld> <def>An Egyptian or
Persian measure of length, varying from thirty-two to sixty
stadia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schenkbeer</ent><br/
<hw>Schenk"beer`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>schenkbier</ets>; <ets>schenken</ets> to pour out + <ets>bier</ets>
beer; -- so called because put on draught soon after it is made.]</ety>
<def>A mild German beer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scherbet</ent><br/
<hw>Scher"bet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sherbet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scherif</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scher"if</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sherif</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scherzando</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scher*zan"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>In a playful or sportive manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scherzo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scher"zo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A playful, humorous movement, commonly in 3-4
measure, which often takes the place of the old minuet and trio in a
sonata or a symphony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sche"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/, <?/, to have or hold. See <er>Scheme</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>General state or disposition of the body or mind, or of
one thing with regard to other things; habitude.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Norris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A figure of speech whereby the
mental habitude of an adversary or opponent is feigned for the purpose
of arguing against him.</def> <rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schetical</ent><br/
<ent>Schetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Schet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Schet"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/ holding
back.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the habit of the body;
constitutional.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schiedam</ent><br/
<hw>Schie*dam"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Short for
<ets>Schiedam schnapps</ets>.]</ety> <def>Holland gin made at Schiedam
in the Netherlands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schiller</ent><br/
<hw>Schil"ler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G., play of
colors.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The peculiar bronzelike luster
observed in certain minerals, as hypersthene, schiller spar, etc. It is
due to the presence of minute inclusions in parallel position, and is
sometimes of secondary origin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Schiller spar</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>an altered
variety of enstatite, exhibiting, in certain positions, a bronzelike
luster.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schillerization</ent><br/
<hw>Schil`ler*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The act or process of producing schiller in a
mineral mass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schilling</ent><br/
<hw>Schil"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G. See
<er>Shilling</er>.]</ety> <def>Any one of several small German and Dutch
coins, worth from about one and a half cents to about five
cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schindylesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schin`dy*le"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ a splitting into fragments.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A
form of articulation in which one bone is received into a groove or slit
in another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schirrhus</ent><br/
<hw>Schir"rhus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scirrhus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schism</ent><br/
<hw>Schism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>scisme</ets>,
OF. <ets>cisme</ets>, <ets>scisme</ets>, F. <ets>schisme</ets>, L.
<ets>schisma</ets>, Gr. <grk>schi`sma</grk>, fr. <grk>schi`zein</grk> to
split; akin to L. <ets>scindere</ets>, Skr. <ets>chid</ets>, and prob.
to E. <ets>shed</ets>, v.t. (which see); cf. <er>Rescind</er>,
<er>Schedule</er>, <er>Zest</er>.]</ety> <def>Division or
separation;</def> <specif>specifically</specif> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>,
<def>permanent division or separation in the Christian church; breach of
unity among people of the same religious faith; the offense of seeking
to produce division in a church without justifiable cause.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Set bounds to our passions by reason, to our errors by truth, and
to our <qex>schisms</qex> by charity.</q> <rj><qau>Eikon
Basilike.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1286 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Greek schism</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>the
separation of the Greek and Roman churches.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Great
schism</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Western schism</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <cd>a schism in the Roman church in the latter part
of the 14th century, on account of rival claimants to the papal
throne.</cd> -- <col><b>Schism act</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an
act of the English Parliament requiring all teachers to conform to the
Established Church, -- passed in 1714, repealed in 1719.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schisma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schis"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a split,
separation, Gr. <grk>schi`sma</grk>: cf. F. <ets>schisma</ets>. See
<er>Schism</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anc. Mus.)</fld> <def>An interval equal to
half a comma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schismatic</ent><br/
<hw>Schis*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(s<icr/z*m<acr/t"<icr/k; <it>so nearly all
orthoepists</it>)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>schismaticus</ets>,
Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>schismatique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
schism; implying schism; partaking of the nature of schism; tending to
schism; <as>as, <ex>schismatic</ex> opinions or
proposals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schismatic</ent><br/
<hw>Schis*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
creates or takes part in schism; one who separates from an established
church or religious communion on account of a difference of
opinion.</def> <ldquo/They were popularly classed together as canting
<xex>schismatics</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Heretic; partisan. See
<er>Heretic</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schismatical</ent><br/
<hw>Schis*mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Schismatic</er>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Schis*mat"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Schis*mat"ic*al*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schismatize</ent><br/
<hw>Schis"ma*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Schismatized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Schismatizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>schismatiser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To take part in
schism; to make a breach of communion in the church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schismless</ent><br/
<hw>Schism"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from
schism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schist</ent><br/
<hw>Schist</hw> <pr>(sh<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
divided, divisible, fr. <?/ to divide: cf. F. <ets>schiste</ets>. See
<er>Schism</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Any crystalline rock
having a foliated structure (see <er>Foliation</er>) and hence admitting
of ready division into slabs or slates. The common kinds are <xex>mica
schist</xex>, and <xex>hornblendic schist</xex>, consisting chiefly of
quartz with mica or hornblende and often feldspar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schistaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Schis*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a slate
color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schistic</ent><br/
<hw>Schist"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Schistose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schistous</ent><br/
<ent>Schistose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Schis*tose"</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <hw>Schist*ous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>schisteux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
schist; having the structure of a schist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schistosity</ent><br/
<hw>Schis*tos"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>schistosit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>The
quality or state of being schistose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizo-</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz"o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <?/ to split, cleave.]</ety>
<def>A combining form denoting <xex>division</xex> or
<xex>cleavage</xex>; <as>as, <ex>schizo</ex>genesis, reproduction by
fission or cell division</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizocarp</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz"o*carp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Schizo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A dry fruit which splits at maturity into several closed one-seeded
portions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizocoele</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz"o*coele</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Schizo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ hollow.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Enterocoele</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizocoelous</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz`o*coe"lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or of the nature of, a schizocoele.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizogenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz`o*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Schizo-</ets> + <ets>genesis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Reproduction by fission.</def> <rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizognath</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz"og*nath</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Schizognathous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bird with a
schizognathous palate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizognathae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schi*zog"na*thae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The schizognathous
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizognathism</ent><br/
<hw>Schi*zog"na*thism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The condition of having a schizognathous
palate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizognathous</ent><br/
<hw>Schi*zog"na*thous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Schizo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ the jaw.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the maxillo-palatine bones separate from each other and from
the vomer, which is pointed in front, as in the gulls, snipes, grouse,
and many other birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizomycetes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schiz`o*my*ce"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>,
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ to split + <?/, -<?/, a fungus.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>An order of <phylum>Schizophyta</phylum>,
including the so-called fission fungi, or bacteria. See
<er>Schizophyta</er>, in the Supplement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizonemertea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schiz`o*ne*mer"te*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Schizo-</er>, and <er>Nemertes</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of nemerteans comprising those having a
deep slit along each side of the head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizont</ent><br/
<hw>Schi"zont</hw> <pr>(sk<imac/"z<ocr/nt <it>or</it>
sk<imac/z"<ocr/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/, p.pr.,
cleaving.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>In certain Sporozoa, a cell
formed by the growth of a sporozoite or merozoite (in a cell or
corpuscle of the host) which segment by superficial cleavage, without
encystment or conjugation, into merozoites.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizopelmous</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz`o*pel"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Schizo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>pe`lma</grk> the sole of the
foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the two flexor tendons of
the toes entirely separate, and the <xex>flexor hallucis</xex> going to
the first toe only.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizophyte</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz"o*phyte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Schizo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a plant.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>One of a class of vegetable organisms, in the classification of
Cohn, which includes all of the inferior forms that multiply by fission,
whether they contain chlorophyll or not.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizopod</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz"o*pod</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>one of the Schizopoda. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizopodous</ent><br/
<ent>Schizopod</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Schiz"o*pod</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <hw>Schi*zop"o*dous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
schizopod, or the Schizopoda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schi*zop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>, <ety>[NL.
See <er>Schizo-</er>, and <er>-poda</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of shrimplike Thoracostraca in which each of the
thoracic legs has a long fringed upper branch (exopodite) for
swimming.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schizorhinal</ent><br/
<hw>Schiz`o*rhi"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Schizo-</ets> + <ets>rhinal</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Having the nasal bones separate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the anterior nostrils
prolonged backward in the form of a slit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schlich</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schlich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.; akin to LG.
<ets>slick</ets> mud, D. <ets>slijk</ets>, MHG.
<ets>sl<imac/ch</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>The finer portion
of a crushed ore, as of gold, lead, or tin, separated by the water in
certain wet processes.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>slich</asp>,
<asp>slick</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schmelze</ent><br/
<hw>Schmel"ze</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>schmelz</ets>, <ets>schmelzglas</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of glass
of a red or ruby color, made in Bohemia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schnapps</ent><br/
<hw>Schnapps</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G., a dram of
spirits.]</ety> <def>Holland gin.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schneiderian</ent><br/
<hw>Schnei*de"ri*an</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Discovered or described by C. V.
<xex>Schneider</xex>, a German anatomist of the seventeenth
century.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Schneiderian membrane</b></col>, <cd>the mucous membrane
which lines the nasal chambers; the pituitary membrane.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schnorrer</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schnor"rer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Yiddish, fr.
G. <ets>schnurrer</ets>, fr. <ets>schnurren</ets> to hum, whir, hence,
from the sound of the musical instrument used by strolling beggars, to
beg.]</ety> <def>Among the Jews, a beggar.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoharie grit</ent><br/
<hw>Scho*har"ie grit`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>The
formation belonging to the middle of the three subdivisions of the
Corniferous period in the American Devonian system; -- so called from
<etsep>Schoharie</etsep>, in New York, where it occurs. See the
<xex>Chart</xex> of <er>Geology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholar</ent><br/
<hw>Schol"ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scoler</ets>, AS. <ets>sc<omac/lere</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>scholaris</ets> belonging to a school, fr. <ets>schola</ets> a
school. See <er>School</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who attends a
school; one who learns of a teacher; one under the tuition of a
preceptor; a pupil; a disciple; a learner; a student.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am no breeching <qex>scholar</qex> in the schools.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One engaged in the pursuits of learning; a learned
person; one versed in any branch, or in many branches, of knowledge; a
person of high literary or scientific attainments; a savant.</def>
<rj><au>Shak. Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A man of books.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In English universities, an undergraduate who
belongs to the foundation of a college, and receives support in part
from its revenues.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Pupil; learner; disciple.</syn> --
<usage><er>Scholar</er>, <er>Pupil</er>. <xex>Scholar</xex> refers to
the instruction, and <xex>pupil</xex> to the care and government, of a
teacher. A <xex>scholar</xex> is one who is under instruction; a
<xex>pupil</xex> is one who is under the immediate and personal care of
an instructor; hence we speak of a bright <xex>scholar</xex>, and an
obedient <xex>pupil</xex>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholarity</ent><br/
<hw>Scho*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>scholarit<eacute/</ets>, or LL. <ets>scholaritas</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Scholarship.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholarlike</ent><br/
<hw>Schol"ar*like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Scholarly.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholarly</ent><br/
<hw>Schol"ar*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a scholar, or learned
person; showing the qualities of a scholar; <as>as, a <ex>scholarly</ex>
essay or critique</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
scholarly manner.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholarship</ent><br/
<hw>Schol"ar*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The character and
qualities of a scholar; attainments in science or literature; erudition;
learning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man of my master's . . . great <qex>scholarship</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Literary education.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Any other house of <qex>scholarship</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Maintenance for a scholar; a foundation for the
support of a student.</def> <rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Learning; erudition; knowledge.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholastic</ent><br/
<hw>Scho*las"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scholasticus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to have leisure, to give
lectures, to keep a school, from <?/ leisure, a lecture, a school: cf.
F. <ets>scholastique</ets>, <ets>scolastique</ets>. See
<er>School</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or suiting, a
scholar, a school, or schools; scholarlike; <as>as, <ex>scholastic</ex>
manners or pride; <ex>scholastic</ex> learning</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir
K. Digby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the schoolmen and divines of the
Middle Ages (see <er>Schoolman</er>); <as>as, <ex>scholastic</ex>
divinity or theology; <ex>scholastic</ex> philosophy</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, characterized by excessive subtlety, or
needlessly minute subdivisions; pedantic; formal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholastic</ent><br/
<hw>Scho*las"tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who adheres to
the method or subtleties of the schools.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>See the Note under
<er>Jesuit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholastical</ent><br/
<hw>Scho*las"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos>
<def>Scholastic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholastically</ent><br/
<hw>Scho*las"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scholastic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholasticism</ent><br/
<hw>Scho*las"ti*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The method or
subtleties of the schools of philosophy; scholastic formality;
scholastic doctrines or philosophy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The spirit of the old <qex>scholasticism</qex> . . . spurned
laborious investigation and slow induction.</q> <rj><qau>J. P.
Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholia</ent><br/
<hw>Scho"li*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scholium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholiast</ent><br/
<hw>Scho"li*ast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
a scholium: cf. F. <ets>scoliate</ets>. See <er>Scholium</er>.]</ety>
<def>A maker of scholia; a commentator or annotator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No . . . quotations from Talmudists and <qex>scholiasts</qex> . .
. ever marred the effect of his grave temperate discourses.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholiastic</ent><br/
<hw>Scho`li*as"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a scholiast, or his pursuits.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholiaze</ent><br/
<hw>Scho"li*aze</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <def>To write scholia.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholical</ent><br/
<hw>Schol"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scholicus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/. See <er>School</er>.]</ety>
<def>Scholastic.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hales.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholion</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scho"li*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>A scholium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A judgment which follows immediately from another is sometimes
called a corollary, or consectary . . . One which illustrates the
science where it appears, but is not an integral part of it, is a
<qex>scholion</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Abp. Thomson (Laws of
Thought).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholium</ent><br/
<hw>Scho"li*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Scholia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Scholiums</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/. See
<er>School</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A marginal annotation; an
explanatory remark or comment; specifically, an explanatory comment on
the text of a classic author by an early grammarian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A remark or observation subjoined to a demonstration
or a train of reasoning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholy</ent><br/
<hw>Scho"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scholium.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scholy</ent><br/
<hw>Scho"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To write
scholia; to annotate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>School</ent><br/
<hw>School</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For <ets>shoal</ets> a
crowd; prob. confused with <ets>school</ets> for learning.]</ety> <def>A
shoal; a multitude; <as>as, a <ex>school</ex> of fish</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>School</ent><br/
<hw>School</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>scole</ets>, AS.
<ets>sc<?/lu</ets>, L. <ets>schola</ets>, Gr. <?/ leisure, that in which
leisure is employed, disputation, lecture, a school, probably from the
same root as <?/, the original sense being perhaps, a stopping, a
resting. See <er>Scheme</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place for
learned intercourse and instruction; an institution for learning; an
educational establishment; a place for acquiring knowledge and mental
training; <as>as, the <ex>school</ex> of the prophets</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Disputing daily in the <qex>school</qex> of one Tyrannus.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts xix. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place of primary instruction; an establishment for
the instruction of children; <as>as, a primary <ex>school</ex>; a common
<ex>school</ex>; a grammar <ex>school</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As he sat in the <qex>school</qex> at his primer.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A session of an institution of
instruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How now, Sir Hugh! No <qex>school</qex> to-day?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One of the seminaries for teaching logic,
metaphysics, and theology, which were formed in the Middle Ages, and
which were characterized by academical disputations and subtleties of
reasoning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At Cambridge the philosophy of Descartes was still dominant in the
<qex>schools</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The room or hall in English universities where the
examinations for degrees and honors are held.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An assemblage of scholars; those who attend upon
instruction in a school of any kind; a body of pupils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What is the great community of Christians, but one of the
innumerable <qex>schools</qex> in the vast plan which God has instituted
for the education of various intelligences?</q>
<rj><qau>Buckminster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The disciples or followers of a teacher; those who
hold a common doctrine, or accept the same teachings; a sect or
denomination in philosophy, theology, science, medicine, politics,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let no man be less confident in his faith . . . by reason of any
difference in the several <qex>schools</qex> of Christians.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>The canons, precepts, or body of opinion or
practice, sanctioned by the authority of a particular class or age;
<as>as, he was a gentleman of the old <ex>school</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>  <p><q>His face pale but striking, though not handsome after the <qex>schools</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>A. S. Hardy.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Figuratively, any means of knowledge or discipline;
<as>as, the <ex>school</ex> of experience</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Boarding school</b></col>, <col><b>Common
school</b></col>, <col><b>District school</b></col>, <col><b>Normal
school</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Boarding</er>,
<er>Common</er>, <er>District</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>High
school</b></col>, <cd>a free public school nearest the rank of a
college.</cd> <mark>[U. S.]</mark> -- <col><b>School board</b></col>,
<cd>a corporation established by law in every borough or parish in
England, and elected by the burgesses or ratepayers, with the duty of
providing public school accommodation for all children in their
district.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>School committee</b></col>,
<col><b>School board</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an elected committee of
citizens having charge and care of the public schools in any district,
town, or city, and responsible for control of the money appropriated for
school purposes.</cd> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><-- added "for" after
"responsible", probably omitted inadvertently in original. --> --
<col><b>School days</b></col>, <cd>the period in which youth are sent to
school.</cd> -- <col><b>School district</b></col>, <cd>a division of a
town or city for establishing and conducting schools.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <mcol><col><b>Sunday school</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Sabbath school</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a school held
on Sunday for study of the Bible and for religious instruction; the
pupils, or the teachers and pupils, of such a school,
collectively.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>School</ent><br/
<hw>School</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Schooled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Schooling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To train in an
institution of learning; to educate at a school; to teach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He's gentle, never <qex>schooled</qex>, and yet learned.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To tutor; to chide and admonish; to reprove; to
subject to systematic discipline; to train.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It now remains for you to <qex>school</qex> your child,<br/
And ask why God's Anointed be reviled.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mother, while loving her child with the intensity of a sole
affection, had <qex>schooled</qex> herself to hope for little other
return than the waywardness of an April breeze.</q>
<rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolbook</ent><br/
<hw>School"book`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A book used in
schools for learning lessons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolboy</ent><br/
<hw>School"boy`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A boy belonging
to, or attending, a school.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schooldame</ent><br/
<hw>School"dame`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
schoolmistress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolery</ent><br/
<hw>School"er*y</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Something
taught; precepts; schooling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolfellow</ent><br/
<hw>School"fel`low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One bred at the
same school; an associate in school.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolgirl</ent><br/
<hw>School"girl`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A girl belonging
to, or attending, a school.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolhouse</ent><br/
<hw>School"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A house
appropriated for the use of a school or schools, or for
instruction.</def><-- a building used for schooling, esp. one used as an
elementary school; usu. small, and usu. constructed specifically for
that purpose. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schooling</ent><br/
<hw>School"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Instruction in
school; tuition; education in an institution of learning; act of
teaching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Discipline; reproof; reprimand; <as>as, he gave his
son a good <ex>schooling</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Compensation for instruction; price or reward paid
to an instructor for teaching pupils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schooling</ent><br/
<hw>School"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>School</er> a
shoal.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Collecting or running in schools
or shoals.</def><-- used of fish --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Schooling</qex> species like the herring and menhaden.</q>
<rj><qau>G. B. Goode.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolma'am</ent><br/
<hw>School"ma'am</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
schoolmistress.</def> <mark>[Colloq.U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolmaid</ent><br/
<hw>School"maid`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
schoolgirl.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolman</ent><br/
<hw>School"man`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Schoolmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One versed in the
niceties of academical disputation or of school divinity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>schoolmen</xex> were philosophers and divines
of the Middle Ages, esp. from the 11th century to the Reformation, who
spent much time on points of nice and abstract speculation. They were so
called because they taught in the mediaeval universities and schools of
divinity.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolmaster</ent><br/
<hw>School"mas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
man who presides over and teaches a school; a male teacher of a
school.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let the soldier be abroad if he will; he can do nothing in this
age. There is another personage abroad, -- a person less imposing, -- in
the eyes of some, perhaps, insignificant. The <qex>schoolmaster</qex> is
abroad; and I trust to him, armed with his primer, against the soldier
in full military array.</q> <rj><qau>Brougham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, disciplines and
directs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The law was our <qex>schoolmaster</qex>, to bring us unto
Christ.</q> <rj><qau>Gal. iii. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1287 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Schoolmate</ent><br/
<hw>School"mate`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pupil who
attends the same school as another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolmistress</ent><br/
<hw>School"mis`tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who
governs and teaches a school; a female school-teacher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolroom</ent><br/
<hw>School"room`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A room in which
pupils are taught.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolship</ent><br/
<hw>School"ship`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vessel employed
as a nautical training school, in which naval apprentices receive their
education at the expense of the state, and are trained for service as
sailors. Also, a vessel used as a reform school to which boys are
committed by the courts to be disciplined, and instructed as
mariners.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>School-teacher</ent><br/
<hw>School"-teach`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
teaches or instructs a school.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>School"-teach`ing</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schoolward</ent><br/
<hw>School"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward
school.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schooner</ent><br/
<hw>Schoon"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See the Note below.
Cf. <er>Shun</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Originally, a small,
sharp-built vessel, with two masts and fore-and-aft rig. Sometimes it
carried square topsails on one or both masts and was called a
<stype>topsail schooner</stype>. About 1840, longer vessels with three
masts, fore-and-aft rigged, came into use, and since that time vessels
with four masts and even with six masts, so rigged, are built. Schooners
with more than two masts are designated <xex>three-masted
schooners</xex>, <xex>four-masted schooners</xex>, etc.</def><-- since
early in the 20th century, almost all ocean commerce has been conducted
on motorized ships, and such sailing ships have survived primarily as
historical curiosities, or as pleasure boats. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The first <xex>schooner</xex> ever constructed is said
to have been built in Gloucester, Massachusetts, about the year 1713, by
a Captain Andrew Robinson, and to have received its name from the
following trivial circumstance: When the vessel went off the stocks into
the water, a bystander cried out,<ldquo/O, how she
<xex>scoons</xex>!<rdquo/ Robinson replied, <ldquo/ A <xex>scooner</xex>
let her be;<rdquo/ and, from that time, vessels thus masted and rigged
have gone by this name. The word <xex>scoon</xex> is popularly used in
some parts of New England to denote the act of making stones skip along
the surface of water. The Scottish <xex>scon</xex> means the same thing.
Both words are probably allied to the Icel. <xex>skunda</xex>,
<xex>skynda</xex>, to make haste, hurry, AS. <xex>scunian</xex> to
avoid, shun, Prov. E. <xex>scun</xex>. In the New England records, the
word appears to have been originally written <xex>scooner</xex>. Babson,
in his <ldquo/History of Gloucester,<rdquo/ gives the following extract
from a letter written in that place Sept. 25, 1721, by Dr. Moses Prince,
brother of the Rev. Thomas Prince, the annalist of New England:
<ldquo/This gentleman (Captain Robinson) was first contriver of
<xex>schooners</xex>, and built the first of that sort about eight years
since.<rdquo/</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schooner</ent><br/
<hw>Schoon"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.]</ety> <def>A large goblet or
drinking glass, -- used for lager beer or ale.</def>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schorl</ent><br/
<hw>Schorl</hw> <pr>(sh<ocir/rl)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>sch<oum/rl</ets>; cf. Sw. <ets>sk<oum/rl</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Black tourmaline.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>shorl</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schorlaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Schor*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partaking of
the nature and character of schorl; resembling schorl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schorlous</ent><br/
<hw>Schorl"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Schorlaceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schorly</ent><br/
<hw>Schorl"y</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or
containing, schorl; <as>as, <ex>schorly</ex> granite</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schottische</ent><br/
<ent>Schottish</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Schot"tish</hw>, <hw>Schot"tische</hw> }</mhw>,
<pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>schottish</ets>,
<ets>schotisch</ets> from G. <ets>schottisch</ets> Scottish,
Scotch.]</ety> <def>A Scotch round dance in 2-4 time, similar to the
polka, only slower; also, the music for such a dance; -- not to be
confounded with the <xex><Eacute/cossaise</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schreibersite</ent><br/
<hw>Schrei"bers*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Named after
Carl von <ets>Schreibers</ets>, of Vienna.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A mineral occurring in steel-gray flexible folia. It contains iron,
nickel, and phosphorus, and is found only in meteoric iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schrode</ent><br/
<hw>Schrode</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scrod</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schwann's sheath</ent><br/
<hw>Schwann's" sheath`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[So called from Theodor
<ets>Schwann</ets>, a German anatomist of the 19th century.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The neurilemma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schwann's white substance</ent><br/
<hw>Schwann's white" sub"stance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The substance of the medullary sheath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schwanpan</ent><br/
<hw>Schwan"pan`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Chinese
abacus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schweitzerkaese</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Schweit"zer*k<aum/"se</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>schweizerk<aum/se</ets> Swiss cheese.]</ety> <def>Gruy<egrave/re
cheese.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Schwenkfeldian</ent><br/
<ent>Schwenkfelder</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Schwenk"feld`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Schwenk"feld`i*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A member of a religious sect
founded by Kaspar von Schwenkfeld, a Silesian reformer who disagreed
with Luther, especially on the deification of the body of
Christ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciaenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*ae"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sciaena</ets> a kind of fish (fr. Gr. <?/) +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
<fam>Sciaenidae</fam>, a family of carnivorous marine fishes which
includes the meagre (<spn>Sciaena umbra</spn> or <spn>Sciaena
aquila</spn>), and fish of the <stype>drum</stype> and
<stype>croaker</stype> families. The <stype>croaker</stype> is so called
because it may make a croaking noise by use of its bladder; the
<stype>Atlantic croaker</stype> (<spn>Micropogonias undulatus</spn>,
formerly <spn>Micropogon undulatus</spn>) and the squeteague are a
members of the croaker family, and the <stype>kingfish</stype> is a
drum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciagraph</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"a*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sciagraphy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An old
term for a vertical section of a building; -- called also
<altname>sciagraphy</altname>. See <cref>Vertical section</cref>, under
<er>Section</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Phys.)</fld> <def>A radiograph.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>skiagraph</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciagraphical</ent><br/
<hw>Sci`a*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sciagraphique</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to
sciagraphy.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sci`a*graph"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciagraphy</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*ag"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr.
<?/ drawing in light and shade; <?/ a shadow + <?/ to delineate,
describe: cf. F. <ets>sciagraphie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art
or science of projecting or delineating shadows as they fall in
nature.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Sciagraph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Radiography</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciamachy</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*am"a*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sciomachy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciatherical</ent><br/
<ent>Sciatheric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sci`a*ther"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sci`a*ther"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ a sundial;
<?/ a shadow + <?/ to hunt, to catch.]</ety> <def>Belonging to a
sundial.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sci`a*ther"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms> <rj><au>J. Gregory.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*at"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sciatique</ets>, LL. <ets>sciaticus</ets>, from L.
<ets>ischiadicus</ets>, Gr. <?/. See <er>Ischiadic</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the hip; in the region of,
or affecting, the hip; ischial; ischiatic; <as>as, the <ex>sciatic</ex>
nerve, <ex>sciatic</ex> pains</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*at"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sciatique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Sciatica.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciatica</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*at"i*ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Neuralgia of the sciatic nerve, an affection
characterized by paroxysmal attacks of pain in the buttock, back of the
thigh, or in the leg or foot, following the course of the branches of
the sciatic nerve. The name is also popularly applied to various painful
affections of the hip and the parts adjoining it. See <cref>Ischiadic
passion</cref>, under <er>Ischiadic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciatical</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*at"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Sciatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciatically</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*at"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With, or by means of,
sciatica.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scibboleth</ent><br/
<hw>Scib"bo*leth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Shibboleth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Science</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>scientia</ets>, fr. <ets>sciens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>scire</ets> to know. Cf. <er>Conscience</er>, <er>Conscious</er>,
<er>Nice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Knowledge; knowledge of
principles and causes; ascertained truth of facts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If we conceive God's sight or <qex>science</qex>, before the
creation, to be extended to all and every part of the world, seeing
everything as it is, . . . his <qex>science</qex> or sight from all
eternity lays no necessity on anything to come to pass.</q>
<rj><qau>Hammond.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shakespeare's deep and accurate <qex>science</qex> in mental
philosophy.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Accumulated and established knowledge, which has
been systematized and formulated with reference to the discovery of
general truths or the operation of general laws; knowledge classified
and made available in work, life, or the search for truth;
comprehensive, profound, or philosophical knowledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All this new <qex>science</qex> that men lere [teach].</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Science</qex> is . . . a complement of cognitions, having, in
point of form, the character of logical perfection, and in point of
matter, the character of real truth.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Especially, such knowledge when it relates to the
physical world and its phenomena, the nature, constitution, and forces
of matter, the qualities and functions of living tissues, etc.; --
called also <altname>natural science</altname>, and <altname>physical
science</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Voltaire hardly left a single corner of the field entirely
unexplored in <qex>science</qex>, poetry, history, philosophy.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Morley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Any branch or department of systematized knowledge
considered as a distinct field of investigation or object of study;
<as>as, the <ex>science</ex> of astronomy, of chemistry, or of
mind</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The ancients reckoned seven sciences, namely, grammar,
rhetoric, logic, arithmetic, music, geometry, and astronomy; -- the
first three being included in the <xex>Trivium</xex>, the remaining four
in the <xex>Quadrivium</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Good sense, which only is the gift of Heaven,<br/
And though no <qex>science</qex>, fairly worth the seven.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Art, skill, or expertness, regarded as the result of
knowledge of laws and principles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>science</qex>, coolness, and great strength.</q>
<rj><qau>G. A. Lawrence.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Science</xex> is <xex>applied</xex> or
<xex>pure</xex>. <xex>Applied science</xex> is a knowledge of facts,
events, or phenomena, as explained, accounted for, or produced, by means
of powers, causes, or laws. <xex>Pure science</xex> is the knowledge of
these powers, causes, or laws, considered <xex>apart</xex>, or as pure
from all applications. Both these terms have a similar and special
signification when applied to the science of quantity; as, the
<xex>applied</xex> and <xex>pure mathematics</xex>. <xex>Exact
science</xex> is knowledge so systematized that prediction and
verification, by measurement, experiment, observation, etc., are
possible. The mathematical and physical sciences are called <xex>the
exact sciences</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Comparative sciences</b></col>, <col><b>Inductive
sciences</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Comparative</er>, and
<er>Inductive</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Literature; art; knowledge.</syn> --
<usage><er>Science</er>, <er>Literature</er>, <er>Art</er>.
<xex>Science</xex> is literally <xex>knowledge</xex>, but more usually
denotes a systematic and orderly arrangement of knowledge. In a more
distinctive sense, <xex>science</xex> embraces those branches of
knowledge of which the subject-matter is either ultimate principles, or
facts as explained by principles or laws thus arranged in natural order.
The term <xex>literature</xex> sometimes denotes all compositions not
embraced under <xex>science</xex>, but usually confined to the
<xex>belles-lettres</xex>. [See <er>Literature</er>.] <xex>Art</xex> is
that which depends on practice and skill in performance. <ldquo/In
<xex>science</xex>, <xex>scimus ut sciamus</xex>; in art, <xex>scimus ut
producamus</xex>. And, therefore, <xex>science</xex> and <xex>art</xex>
may be said to be investigations of truth; but one, <xex>science</xex>,
inquires for the sake of knowledge; the other, <xex>art</xex>, for the
sake of production; and hence <xex>science</xex> is more concerned with
the higher truths, <xex>art</xex> with the lower; and <xex>science</xex>
never is engaged, as <xex>art</xex> is, in productive application. And
the most perfect state of <xex>science</xex>, therefore, will be the
most high and accurate inquiry; the perfection of <xex>art</xex> will be
the most apt and efficient system of rules; art always throwing itself
into the form of rules.<rdquo/</usage> <rj><au>Karslake.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Science</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"ence</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to become versed in
science; to make skilled; to instruct.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Francis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>science fiction</ent><br/
<hw>sci"ence fic"tion</hw> <pr>(s<imac/"<eit/ns f<icr/k"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>sci</ets>ence <ets>fi</ets>ction.]</ety> <def>A
genre of fiction in which scientific and technological issues feature
prominently, especially including scenarios in which speculative but
unproven scientific advances are accepted as fact, and usually set at
some time in the future, or in some distant region of the
universe.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scient</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sciens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Knowing; skillful.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cockeram.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scienter</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sci*en"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Knowingly; willfully.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciential</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*en"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>scientialis</ets>, fr. L. <ets>scientia</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Pertaining to, or producing, science.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scientific</ent><br/
<hw>Sci`en*tif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>scientifique</ets>; L. <ets>scientia</ets> science +
<ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
science; used in science; <as>as, <ex>scientific</ex> principles;
<ex>scientific</ex> apparatus; <ex>scientific</ex>
observations</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Agreeing with, or depending on, the rules or
principles of science; <as>as, a <ex>scientific</ex> classification; a
<ex>scientific</ex> arrangement of fossils</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having a knowledge of science, or of a science;
evincing science or systematic knowledge; <as>as, a <ex>scientific</ex>
chemist; a <ex>scientific</ex> reasoner; a <ex>scientific</ex>
argument</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bossuet is as <qex>scientific</qex> in the structure of his
sentences.</q> <rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scientific method</b></col>, <cd>the method employed in
exact science and consisting of: (<it>a</it>) Careful and abundant
observation and experiment. (<it>b</it>) generalization of the results
into formulated <ldquo/Laws<rdquo/ and statements.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scientifical</ent><br/
<hw>Sci`en*tif"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Scientific.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scientifically</ent><br/
<hw>Sci`en*tif"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scientific
manner; according to the rules or principles of science.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is easier to believe than to be <qex>scientifically</qex>
instructed.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scientist</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"en*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One learned in
science; a scientific investigator; one devoted to scientific study; a
savant.</def> <mark>[Recent]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Twenty years ago I ventured to propose one [a name for
the class of men who give their lives to scientific study] which has
been slowly finding its way to general adoption; and the word
<xex>scientist</xex>, though scarcely euphonious, has gradually assumed
its place in our vocabulary.</note> <rj><au>B. A. Gould (Address,
1869).</au></rj> <-- MW10 gives 1834 as first ref. date. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>scifi</ent><br/
<ent>Sci-Fi</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Sci"-Fi"</hw>, <hw>sci"fi"</hw></mhw>
<pr>(s<imac/"f<imac/")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>sci</ets>ence
<ets>fi</ets>ction.]</ety> <def>Science fiction; -- a common shortened
form for the name of the literary genre. See <er>science
fiction</er>.</def> <mark>[informal]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scilicet</ent><br/
<hw>Scil"i*cet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>scire licet</ets> you may know.]</ety> <def>To wit; namely;
videlicet; -- often abbreviated to <abbr>sc.</abbr>, or
<abbr>ss.</abbr></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scillain</ent><br/
<hw>Scil"la*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A glucoside extracted from squill (<spn>Scilla maritima</spn>) as a
light porous substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scillitin</ent><br/
<hw>Scil"li*tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scilitine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A bitter principle
extracted from the bulbs of the squill (<spn>Scilla maritima</spn>), and
probably consisting of a complex mixture of several
substances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scimitar</ent><br/
<ent>Scimiter</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scim"i*ter</hw> , <hw>Scim"i*tar</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>cimeterre</ets>, cf. It.
<ets>scimitarra</ets>, Sp. <ets>cimitarra</ets>; fr. Biscayan
<ets>cimetarra</ets> with a sharp edge; or corrupted from Per.
<ets>shimsh<imac/r</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A saber with a much
curved blade having the edge on the convex side, -- in use among
Mohammedans, esp., the Arabs and Persians.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>cimeter</asp>, and <asp>scymetar</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A long-handled billhook. See
<er>Billhook</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scimiter pods</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
immense curved woody pods of a leguminous woody climbing plant
(<spn>Entada scandens</spn>) growing in tropical India and America. They
contain hard round flattish seeds two inches in diameter, which are made
into boxes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scincoid</ent><br/
<hw>Scin"coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scincus</ets> a kind of lizard (fr. Gr. <?/) + <ets>-oid</ets>. Cf.
<er>Skink</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
family <fam>Scincidae</fam>, or skinks.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A scincoidian.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scincoidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scin*coi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tribe of lizards including
the skinks. See <er>Skink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scincoidian</ent><br/
<hw>Scin*coid"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of lizards of the family
<fam>Scincidae</fam> or tribe Scincoidea. The tongue is not extensile.
The body and tail are covered with overlapping scales, and the toes are
margined.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciniph</ent><br/
<hw>Scin"iph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scinifes</ets>, <ets>cinifes</ets>, or <ets>ciniphes</ets>, pl.,
Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Some kind of stinging or biting insect, as a flea,
a gnat, a sandfly, or the like.</def> <rj><au>Ex. viii. 17 (Douay
version).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scink</ent><br/
<hw>Scink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
skink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scink</ent><br/
<hw>Scink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slunk calf.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scintilla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scin*til"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>A spark; the least particle; an iota; a tittle.</def> <rj><au>R.
North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scintillant</ent><br/
<hw>Scin"til*lant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scintillans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>scintillare</ets> to sparkle. See
<er>Scintillate</er>.]</ety> <def>Emitting sparks, or fine igneous
particles; sparkling.</def> <rj><au>M. Green.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scintillate</ent><br/
<hw>Scin"til*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scintillated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scintillating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>scintillare</ets>, <ets>scintillatum</ets>, from
<ets>scintilla</ets> a spark. Cf. <er>Stencil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To emit sparks, or fine igneous particles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As the electrical globe only <qex>scintillates</qex> when rubbed
against its cushion.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sparkle, as the fixed stars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scintillation</ent><br/
<hw>Scin`til*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scintillatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scintillation</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of scintillating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A spark or flash emitted in scintillating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These <qex>scintillations</qex> are . . . the inflammable
effluences discharged from the bodies collided.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scintillous</ent><br/
<hw>Scin"til*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Scintillant.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scintillously</ent><br/
<hw>Scin"til*lous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scintillant
manner.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciography</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*og"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sciagraphy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciolism</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"o*lism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sciolist</er>.]</ety> <def>The knowledge of a sciolist; superficial
knowledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciolist</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"o*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sciolus</ets>. See <er>Sciolous</er>.]</ety> <def>One who knows
many things superficially; a pretender to science; a
smatterer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These passages in that book were enough to humble the presumption
of our modern <qex>sciolists</qex>, if their pride were not as great as
their ignorance.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A master were lauded and <qex>sciolists</qex> shent.</q>
<rj><qau>R. Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciolistic</ent><br/
<hw>Sci`o*lis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to sciolism, or a sciolist; partaking of sciolism; resembling a
sciolist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciolous</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"o*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scilus</ets>, dim. of <ets>scius</ets> knowing, fr.
<ets>scire</ets> to know. See <er>Science</er>.]</ety> <def>Knowing
superficially or imperfectly.</def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciomachy</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*om"a*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/;
<?/ a shadow + <?/ battle: cf. F. <ets>sciomachie</ets>,
<ets>sciamachie</ets>.]</ety> <def>A fighting with a shadow; a mock
contest; an imaginary or futile combat.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>scimachy</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Cowley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciomancy</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"o*man`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a shadow
+ <ets>-mancy</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sciomance</ets>,
<ets>sciamancie</ets>.]</ety> <def>Divination by means of
shadows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scion</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>cion</ets>,
F. <ets>scion</ets>, probably from <ets>scier</ets> to saw, fr. L.
<ets>secare</ets> to cut. Cf. <er>Section</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A shoot or sprout of a plant; a
sucker.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A piece of a slender branch or twig cut
for grafting.</def> <mark>[Formerly written also cion, and
cyon.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a descendant; an heir; <as>as, a
<ex>scion</ex> of a royal stock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scioptic</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*op"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ shadow +
<?/ belonging to sight: cf. F. <ets>scioptique</ets>. See
<er>Optic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to an
optical arrangement for forming images in a darkened room, usually
called <xex>scioptic ball</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scioptic ball</b></col> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>the lens
of a camera obscura mounted in a wooden ball which fits a socket in a
window shutter so as to be readily turned, like the eye, to different
parts of the landscape.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciopticon</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*op"ti*con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Scioptic</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of magic lantern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scioptics</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*op"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art or process
of exhibiting luminous images, especially those of external objects, in
a darkened room, by arrangements of lenses or mirrors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scioptric</ent><br/
<hw>Sci*op"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>Scioptic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciot</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
island <etsep>Scio</etsep> (Chio or Chios).</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A native or inhabitant of Scio.</def></def2> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Chiot</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciotheric</ent><br/
<hw>Sci`o*ther"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>sciothericon</ets> a sundial. See <er>Sciatheric</er>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to a sundial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sciotheric telescope</b></col> <fld>(Dialing)</fld>,
<cd>an instrument consisting of a horizontal dial, with a telescope
attached to it, used for determining the time, whether of day or
night.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scious</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Knowing; having knowledge.</def>
<ldquo/Brutes may be and are <xex>scious</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1288 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scire facias</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sci`re fa"ci*as</hw> <pr>(s<imac/`r<esl/
f<amac/"sh<icr/*<acr/s)</pr>. <ety>[L., do you cause to know.]</ety>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A judicial writ, founded upon some record, and
requiring the party proceeded against to show cause why the party
bringing it should not have advantage of such record, or (as in the case
of <xex>scire facias</xex> to repeal letters patent) why the record
should not be annulled or vacated.</def> <rj><au>Wharton.
Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scirrhoid</ent><br/
<hw>Scir"rhoid</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/r"roid)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Scirrhus</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling
scirrhus.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scirrhosity</ent><br/
<hw>Scir*rhos"i*ty</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/r*r<ocr/s"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A morbid induration, as of a gland;
state of being scirrhous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scirrhous</ent><br/
<hw>Scir"rhous</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/r"r<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>scirrhosus</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Proceeding from
scirrhus; of the nature of scirrhus; indurated; knotty; <as>as,
<ex>scirrhous</ex> affections; <ex>scirrhous</ex> disease</as>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>skirrhous</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scirrhus</ent><br/
<hw>Scir"rhus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Scirrhi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Scirrhuses</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from L. <ets>scirros</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/,
fr. <?/, <?/, hard.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An
indurated organ or part; especially, an indurated gland.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A cancerous tumor which is hard,
translucent, of a gray or bluish color, and emits a creaking sound when
incised.</def> <altsp>[Sometimes incorrectly written
<asp>schirrus</asp>; written also <asp>skirrhus</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciscitation</ent><br/
<hw>Scis`ci*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sciscitatio</ets>, fr. <ets>sciscitari</ets> to inquire, from
<ets>sciscere</ets> to seek to know, v. incho. from <ets>scire</ets> to
know.]</ety> <def>The act of inquiring; inquiry; demand.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scise</ent><br/
<hw>Scise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scindere</ets>, <ets>scissum</ets>, to cut, split.]</ety> <def>To
cut; to penetrate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wicked steel <qex>scised</qex> deep in his right side.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissel</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scissile</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The clippings of metals made
in various mechanical operations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The slips or plates of metal out of which circular
blanks have been cut for the purpose of coinage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissible</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scindere</ets>, <ets>scissum</ets>, to split.]</ety> <def>Capable
of being cut or divided by a sharp instrument.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissil</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scissel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissile</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scissilis</ets>, fr. <ets>scindere</ets>, <ets>scissum</ets>, to
cut, to split: cf. F. <ets>scissile</ets>. See <er>Schism</er>.]</ety>
<def>Capable of being cut smoothly; scissible.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scission</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scissio</ets>, fr. <ets>scindere</ets>, <ets>scissum</ets>, to cut,
to split: cf. F. <ets>scission</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of dividing
with an instrument having a sharp edge.</def>
<rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissiparity</ent><br/
<hw>Scis`si*par"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scissus</ets> (p. p. of <ets>scindere</ets> to split) +
<ets>parere</ets> to bring forth: cf. F.
<ets>scissiparit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Reproduction by fission.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissor</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cut with
scissors or shears; to prepare with the aid of scissors.</def>
<rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissors</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sors</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sisoures</ets>, OF. <ets>cisoires</ets> (cf. F.
<ets>ciseaux</ets>), probably fr. LL. <ets>cisorium</ets> a cutting
instrument, fr. L. <ets>caedere</ets> to cut. Cf. <er>Chisel</er>,
<er>Concise</er>. The modern spelling is due to a mistaken derivation
from L. <ets>scissor</ets> one who cleaves or divides, fr.
<ets>scindere</ets>, <ets>scissum</ets>, to cut, split.]</ety> <def>A
cutting instrument resembling shears, but smaller, consisting of two
cutting blades with handles, movable on a pin in the center, by which
they are held together. Often called a <altname>pair of
scissors</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Formerly written also
<asp>cisors</asp>, <asp>cizars</asp>, and
<asp>scissars</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scissors grinder</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
European goatsucker.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissorsbill</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sors*bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Skimmer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissorstail</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sors*tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A tyrant flycatcher (<spn>Milvulus forficatus</spn>) of the
Southern United States and Mexico, which has a deeply forked tail. It is
light gray above, white beneath, salmon on the flanks, and fiery red at
the base of the crown feathers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissors-tailed</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sors-tailed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the outer feathers much the longest, the
others decreasing regularly to the median ones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scissure</ent><br/
<hw>Scis"sure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scissura</ets>, from <ets>scindere</ets>, <ets>scissum</ets>, to
cut, split.]</ety> <def>A longitudinal opening in a body, made by
cutting; a cleft; a fissure.</def> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scitamineous</ent><br/
<hw>Scit`a*min"e*ous</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>scitamineosus</ets>, fr. <ets>Scitamineae</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>scitamentum</ets> a delicacy, dainty.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a natural order of plants
(<ord>Scitamineae</ord>), mostly tropical herbs, including the ginger,
Indian shot, banana, and the plants producing turmeric and
arrowroot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciurine</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"u*rine</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sciurien</ets>. See <er>Sciurus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Squirrel family.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A rodent of the Squirrel
family.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciuroid</ent><br/
<hw>Sci"u*roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sciurus</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Resembling the tail of
a squirrel; -- generally said of branches which are close and dense, or
of spikes of grass like barley.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciuromorpha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sci`u*ro*mor"pha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. L. <ets>sciurus</ets> squirrel + Gr. <grk>morfh`</grk> form.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tribe of rodents containing the squirrels and
allied animals, such as the gophers, woodchucks, beavers, and
others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciurus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sci*u"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a squirrel,
Gr. <?/. See <er>Squirrel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus
of rodents comprising the common squirrels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclaff</ent><br/
<hw>Sclaff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sclaffed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sclaffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Orig. uncert.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To scuff or shuffle along.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>To scrape the ground with the sole
of the club, before striking the ball, in making a stroke.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclaff</ent><br/
<hw>Sclaff</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>To scrape (the
club) on the ground, in a stroke, before hitting the ball; also, to make
(a stroke) in that way.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclaff</ent><br/
<hw>Sclaff</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
slight blow; a slap; a soft fall; also, the accompanying
noise.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>The stroke made by one who
sclaffs.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A thin, solid substance, esp. a thin shoe or
slipper.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclaundre</ent><br/
<hw>Sclaun"dre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Slander.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclave</ent><br/
<ent>Sclav</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sclav</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sclave</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Slav</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclavic</ent><br/
<hw>Sclav"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Slavic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclavism</ent><br/
<hw>Sclav"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Slavism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclavonian</ent><br/
<hw>Scla*vo"nian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Slavonian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclavonic</ent><br/
<hw>Scla*von"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Slavonic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclender</ent><br/
<hw>Sclen"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slender.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scleragogy</ent><br/
<hw>Scler"a*go`gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/
hard + <?/ a leading or training.]</ety> <def>Severe discipline.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hacket.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerema</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scle*re"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Induration of
the cellular tissue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sclerema of adults</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Scleroderma</er>.</cd> -- \'d8<col><b>Sclerema neonatorum</b></col>
<pr>(<?/)</pr> <ety>[NL., of the newborn]</ety>, <cd>an affection
characterized by a peculiar hardening and rigidity of the cutaneous and
subcutaneous tissues in the newly born. It is usually fatal. Called also
<altname>skinbound disease</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerenchyma</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*ren"chy*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <ets>-enchyma</ets> as in
<ets>parenchyma</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Vegetable tissue composed of short cells with thickened or hardened
walls, as in nutshells and the gritty parts of a pear. See
<er>Sclerotic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ By recent German writers and their English translators,
this term is used for <altname>liber cells</altname>.</note>
<rj><au>Goodale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The hard calcareous deposit in
the tissues of Anthozoa, constituting the stony corals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerenchymatous</ent><br/
<hw>Scler`en*chym"a*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot. &
Zool.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or composed of, sclerenchyma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerenchyme</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*ren"chyme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sclerenchyma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scleriasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scle*ri"a*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A morbid induration
of the edge of the eyelid.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Induration of any
part, including scleroderma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerite</ent><br/
<hw>Scle"rite</hw> <pr>(skl<emac/"r<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A hard chitinous or calcareous process or
corpuscle, especially a spicule of the Alcyonaria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scleritis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scle*ri"tis</hw> <pr>(skl<esl/*r<imac/"t<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>See <er>Sclerotitis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerobase</ent><br/
<hw>Scler"o*base</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <grk>ba`sis</grk> base.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The calcareous or hornlike coral forming the
central stem or axis of most compound alcyonarians; -- called also
<altname>foot secretion</altname>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Scler`o*ba"sic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scleroderm</ent><br/
<hw>Scler"o*derm</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <grk>de`rma</grk> skin: cf. F.
<ets>scl<eacute/roderme</ets>.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of a tribe of plectognath fishes (<fam>Sclerodermi</fam>)
having the skin covered with hard scales, or plates, as the cowfish and
the trunkfish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the Sclerodermata.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>Hardened, or bony, integument of various
animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scleroderma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scler`o*der"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A disease of adults, characterized by a diffuse
rigidity and hardness of the skin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerodermata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scler`o*der"ma*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The stony corals; the
Madreporaria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerodermous</ent><br/
<ent>Sclerodermic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scler`o*der"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scler`o*der"mous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having the
integument, or skin, hard, or covered with hard plates.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to the Sclerodermata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerodermite</ent><br/
<hw>Scler`o*der"mite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The hard integument of Crustacea.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Sclerenchyma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerogen</ent><br/
<hw>Scler"o*gen</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <ets>-gen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The thickening matter of woody cells; lignin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerogenous</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*rog"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <ets>-genous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Making or secreting a hard substance; becoming hard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scleroid</ent><br/
<hw>Scle"roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having a hard texture, as nutshells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scleroma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scle*ro"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <ets>-oma</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Induration of the tissues. See <er>Sclerema</er>,
<er>Scleroderma</er>, and <er>Sclerosis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerometer</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*rom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument
for determining with accuracy the degree of hardness of a
mineral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerosed</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*rosed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected with
sclerosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scle*ro"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
(<?/<?/, fr. <grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Induration; hardening; especially, that form of
induration produced in an organ by increase of its interstitial
connective tissue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Hardening of the cell wall by
lignification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cerebro-spinal sclerosis</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<cd>an affection in which patches of hardening, produced by increase of
the neuroglia and atrophy of the true nerve tissue, are found scattered
throughout the brain and spinal cord. It is associated with complete or
partial paralysis, a peculiar jerking tremor of the muscles, headache,
and vertigo, and is usually fatal. Formerly referred to as
<altname>multiple sclerosis</altname>, <altname>disseminated
sclerosis</altname>, <it>or</it> <altname>insular sclerosis</altname>,
but now usually called only <altname>multiple sclerosis</altname>, or
<altname>MS</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scleroskeleton</ent><br/
<hw>Scle`ro*skel"e*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + E. <ets>skeleton</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>That part of the skeleton which is developed in
tendons, ligaments, and aponeuroses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerotal</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*ro"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Sclerotic.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The optic capsule; the
sclerotic coat of the eye.</def></def2> <rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerotic</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*rot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard: cf. F. <ets>scl<eacute/rotique</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Hard; firm; indurated; -- applied especially in anatomy
to the firm outer coat of the eyeball, which is often cartilaginous and
sometimes bony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the sclerotic
coat of the eye; sclerotical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Affected with sclerosis;
sclerosed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sclerotic parenchyma</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>sclerenchyma. By some writers a distinction is made, <xex>sclerotic
parenchyma</xex> being applied to tissue composed of cells with the
walls hardened but not thickened, and <xex>sclerenchyma</xex> to tissue
composed of cells with the walls both hardened and
thickened.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerotic</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*rot"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scl<eacute/rotique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
sclerotic coat of the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerotic</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*rot"ic</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining
to, or designating, an acid obtained from ergot or the sclerotium of a
fungus growing on rye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerotical</ent><br/
<hw>Scle*rot"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Sclerotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerotitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scler`o*ti"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sclerotic</er>, and <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the sclerotic coat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerotium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scle*ro"ti*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sclerotia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A hardened body formed by certain fungi, as by the <spn>Claviceps
purpurea</spn>, which produces ergot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The mature or resting stage of a
plasmodium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerotome</ent><br/
<hw>Scler"o*tome</hw> <pr>(skl<ecr/r"<osl/*t<omac/m <it>or</it>
skl<emac/r"<osl/*t<omac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sklhro`s</grk> hard + <grk>te`mnein</grk> to cut.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the bony, cartilaginous, or membranous
partitions which separate the myotomes.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Scler`o*tom"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sclerous</ent><br/
<hw>Scle"rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Hard; indurated; sclerotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoat</ent><br/
<hw>Scoat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To prop; to
scotch.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scobby</ent><br/
<hw>Scob"by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The chaffinch.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scobiform</ent><br/
<hw>Scob"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scobs</ets>, or <ets>scobis</ets>, sawdust, scrapings +
<ets>-form</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scobiforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the
form of, or resembling, sawdust or raspings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scobs</ent><br/
<hw>Scobs</hw>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scobs</ets>, or
<ets>scobis</ets>, fr. <ets>scabere</ets> to scrape.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Raspings of ivory, hartshorn, metals, or other hard
substance.</def> <rj><au>Chambers.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The dross of metals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoff</ent><br/
<hw>Scoff</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scof</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>schof</ets>, OHG.
<ets>scoph</ets>, Icel. <ets>skaup</ets>, and perh. to E.
<ets>shove</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Derision; ridicule; mockery;
derisive or mocking expression of scorn, contempt, or
reproach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With <qex>scoffs</qex>, and scorns, and contumelious taunts.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An object of scorn, mockery, or derision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scoff</qex> of withered age and beardless youth.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoff</ent><br/
<hw>Scoff</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scoffed</conjf> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scoffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Dan. <ets>skuffe</ets> to
deceive, delude, Icel. <ets>skopa</ets> to scoff, OD.
<ets>schoppen</ets>. See <er>Scoff</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To
show insolent ridicule or mockery; to manifest contempt by derisive acts
or language; -- often with <xex>at</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Truth from his lips prevailed with double sway,<br/
And fools who came to <qex>scoff</qex>, remained to pray.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God's better gift they <qex>scoff</qex> at and refuse.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To sneer; mock; gibe; jeer. See
<er>Sneer</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoff</ent><br/
<hw>Scoff</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To treat or address with derision;
to assail scornfully; to mock at.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>scoff</qex> religion is ridiculously proud and
immodest.</q> <rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoffer</ent><br/
<hw>Scoff"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who scoffs.</def>
<rj><au>2 Pet. iii. 3.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoffery</ent><br/
<hw>Scoff"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
scoffing; scoffing conduct; mockery.</def>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoffingly</ent><br/
<hw>Scoff"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scoffing manner.</def>
<rj><au>Broome.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoke</ent><br/
<hw>Scoke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Poke
(<spn>Phytolacca decandra</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolay</ent><br/
<hw>Sco*lay"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scoley</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scold</ent><br/
<hw>Scold</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scolded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scolding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to D. <ets>schelden</ets>,
G. <ets>schelten</ets>, OHG. <ets>sceltan</ets>, Dan.
<ets>skielde</ets>.]</ety> <def>To find fault or rail with rude clamor;
to brawl; to utter harsh, rude, boisterous rebuke; to chide sharply or
coarsely; -- often with <xex>at</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>scold</ex> at a
servant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pardon me, lords, 't is the first time ever<br/
I was forced to <qex>scold</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scold</ent><br/
<hw>Scold</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To chide with rudeness and clamor;
to rate; also, to rebuke or reprove with severity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scold</ent><br/
<hw>Scold</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who scolds, or makes
a practice of scolding; esp., a rude, clamorous woman; a
shrew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She is an irksome, brawling <qex>scold</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A scolding; a brawl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolder</ent><br/
<hw>Scold"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
scolds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The oyster catcher;
-- so called from its shrill cries.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The old
squaw.</def> <mark>[Local U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolding</ent><br/
<hw>Scold"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Scold</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scolding bridle</b></col>, <cd>an iron frame. See
<er>Brank</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoldingly</ent><br/
<hw>Scold"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scolding
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scole</ent><br/
<hw>Scole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>School.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolecida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sco*le"ci*da</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Scolex</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Helminthes</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolecite</ent><br/
<hw>Scol"e*cite</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>skw`lhx</grk>, <grk>-hkos</grk>, a worm, earthworm.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A zeolitic mineral occurring in delicate
radiating groups of white crystals. It is a hydrous silicate of alumina
and lime. Called also <altname>lime mesotype</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolecomorpha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sco*le`co*mor"pha</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Scolex</er>, <er>-morphous</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Scolecida</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolex</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sco"lex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scoleces</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>skw`lhx</grk> worm, grub.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The embryo produced directly from the egg in a metagenetic series,
especially the larva of a tapeworm or other parasitic worm.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the Scolecida.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoley</ent><br/
<hw>Sco*ley"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>escoler</ets> to teach. See <er>School</er>.]</ety> <def>To go to
school; to study.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoliosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sco`li*o"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>skolio`s</grk> crooked.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A lateral
curvature of the spine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolithus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scol"i*thus</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>skw`lhx</grk> a worm + <grk>li`qos</grk> a stone.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A tubular structure found in Potsdam
sandstone, and believed to be the fossil burrow of a marine
worm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scollop</ent><br/
<hw>Scol"lop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scallop</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolopacine</ent><br/
<hw>Scol`o*pa"cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scolopax</ets> a snipe, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the <fam>Scolopacidae</fam>, or Snipe family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolopendra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scol`o*pen"dra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a kind
of multiped, fr. Gr. <?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
genus of venomous myriapods including the centipeds. See
<er>Centiped</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sea fish.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolopendrine</ent><br/
<hw>Scol`o*pen"drine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Like or pertaining to the Scolopendra.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scolytid</ent><br/
<hw>Scol"y*tid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>skoly`ptein</grk> to cut short.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
one of numerous species of small bark-boring beetles of the genus
<gen>Scolytus</gen> and allied genera. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scomber</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scom"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a mackerel,
Gr. <grk>sko`mbros</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
acanthopterygious fishes which includes the common mackerel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scomberoid</ent><br/
<hw>Scom"ber*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scomb<eacute/ro<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>Scombroid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1289 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scombriformes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scom`bri*for"mes</hw>
<pr>(sk<ocr/m`br<icr/*f<ocir/r"m<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of fishes including
the mackerels, tunnies, and allied fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scombroid</ent><br/
<hw>Scom"broid</hw> <pr>(sk<ocr/m"broid)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Scomber</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Like or pertaining to the Mackerel family.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>Any fish of the family <fam>Scombridae</fam>,
of which the mackerel (<gen>Scomber</gen>) is the type, and including
the tuna (<gen>Thunnus</gen> and related genera).</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scomfish</ent><br/
<hw>Scom"fish</hw> <pr>(sk<ocr/m"f<icr/sh <it>or</it> sk<ucr/m"-)</pr>,
<pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To suffocate or stifle; to smother.</def>
<mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scomfit</ent><br/
<hw>Scom"fit</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/m"f<icr/y)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos>
<def>Discomfit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scomm</ent><br/
<hw>Scomm</hw> <pr>(sk<ocr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scomma</ets> a taunt, jeer, scoff, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to mock, scoff
at.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A buffoon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A flout; a jeer; a gibe; a taunt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fotherby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sconce</ent><br/
<hw>Sconce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>schans</ets>,
OD. <ets>schantse</ets>, perhaps from OF. <ets>esconse</ets> a hiding
place, akin to <ets>esconser</ets> to hide, L. <ets>absconsus</ets>,
<pos>p. p.</pos> of <ets>abscondere</ets>. See <er>Abscond</er>, and cf.
<er>Ensconce</er>, <er>Sconce</er> a candlestick.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A fortification, or work for defense; a fort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No <qex>sconce</qex> or fortress of his raising was ever known
either to have been forced, or yielded up, or quitted.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hut for protection and shelter; a stall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One that . . . must raise a <qex>sconce</qex> by the highway and
sell switches.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A piece of armor for the head; headpiece;
helmet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I must get a <qex>sconce</qex> for my head.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: The head; the skull; also, brains; sense;
discretion.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To knock him about the <qex>sconce</qex> with a dirty shovel.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A poll tax; a mulct or fine.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <ety>[OF. <ets>esconse</ets> a dark lantern, properly, a
hiding place. See Etymol. above.]</ety> <def>A protection for a light; a
lantern or cased support for a candle; hence, a fixed hanging or
projecting candlestick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tapers put into lanterns or <qex>sconces</qex> of several-colored,
oiled paper, that the wind might not annoy them.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Golden <qex>sconces</qex> hang not on the walls.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Hence, the circular tube, with a brim, in a
candlestick, into which the candle is inserted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A squinch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A fragment of a floe of ice.</def>
<rj><au>Kane.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <ety>[Perhaps a different word.]</ety> <def>A fixed seat
or shelf.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sconce</ent><br/
<hw>Sconce</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sconced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sconcing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shut up in a
sconce; to imprison; to insconce.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Immure him, <qex>sconce</qex> him, barricade him in 't.</q>
<rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mulct; to fine.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sconcheon</ent><br/
<hw>Scon"cheon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>A squinch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scone</ent><br/
<hw>Scone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A cake, thinner than a
bannock, made of wheat or barley or oat meal.</def> <altsp>[Written
variously, <asp>scon</asp>, <asp>skone</asp>, <asp>skon</asp>,
etc.]</altsp> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoop</ent><br/
<hw>Scoop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>scope</ets>,
of Scand. origin; cf. Sw. <ets>skopa</ets>, akin to D. <ets>schop</ets>
a shovel, G. <ets>sch<uum/ppe</ets>, and also to E. <ets>shove</ets>.
See <er>Shovel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A large ladle; a vessel
with a long handle, used for dipping liquids; a utensil for bailing
boats.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A deep shovel, or any similar implement for digging
out and dipping or shoveling up anything; <as>as, a flour
<ex>scoop</ex>; the <ex>scoop</ex> of a dredging machine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>A spoon-shaped instrument, used
in extracting certain substances or foreign bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A place hollowed out; a basinlike cavity; a
hollow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some had lain in the <qex>scoop</qex> of the rock.</q> <rj><qau>J.
R. Drake.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A sweep; a stroke; a swoop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The act of scooping, or taking with a scoop or
ladle; a motion with a scoop, as in dipping or shoveling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>a quantity sufficient to fill a scoop; -- used
especially for ice cream, dispensed with an ice cream scoop; <as>as, an
ice cream cone with two <ex>scoops</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>an act of reporting (news, research results) before
a rival; also called a <altname>beat</altname>.</def> <mark>[Newspaper
or laboratory cant]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>news or information; <as>as, what's the
<ex>scoop</ex> on John's divorce?</as>.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scoop net</b></col>, <cd>a kind of hand net, used in
fishing; also, a net for sweeping the bottom of a river.</cd> --
<col><b>Scoop wheel</b></col>, <cd>a wheel for raising water, having
scoops or buckets attached to its circumference; a
tympanum.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoop</ent><br/
<hw>Scoop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scooped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scooping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>scopen</ets>. See
<er>Scoop</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take out or up
with, a scoop; to lade out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>scooped</qex> the water from the crystal flood.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To empty by lading; <as>as, to <ex>scoop</ex> a well
dry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make hollow, as a scoop or dish; to excavate; to
dig out; to form by digging or excavation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those carbuncles the Indians will <qex>scoop</qex>, so as to hold
above a pint.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoop</ent><br/
<hw>Scoop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>to report a story first, before (a
rival); to get a scoop, or a beat, on (a rival); -- used commonly in the
passive; <as>as, we were <ex>scooped</ex></as>. Also used in certain
situations in scientific research, when one scientist or team of
scientists reports their results before another who is working on the
same problem.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scooper</ent><br/
<hw>Scoop"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, scoops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The avocet; -- so called because
it scoops up the mud to obtain food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoot</ent><br/
<hw>Scoot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To walk fast; to go
quickly; to run hastily away.</def> <mark>[Colloq. & Humorous, U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoparin</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"pa*rin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A yellow gelatinous or crystalline substance found in broom
(<spn>Cytisus scoparius</spn>) accompanying sparteine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopate</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scopae</ets>, <ets>scopa</ets>, a broom.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the surface closely covered with hairs, like a
brush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-scope</ent><br/
<hw>-scope</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <grk>skopo`s</grk> a watcher,
spy. See <er>Scope</er>.]</ety> <def>A combining form usually signifying
<xex>an instrument for viewing</xex> (with the eye) or
<xex>observing</xex> (in any way); <as>as in micro<ex>scope</ex>,
tele<ex>scope</ex>, alto<ex>scope</ex>,
anemo<ex>scope</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scope</ent><br/
<hw>Scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>scopo</ets>,
L. <ets>scopos</ets> a mark, aim, Gr. <grk>skopo`s</grk>, a watcher,
mark, aim; akin to <?/, <?/ to view, and perh. to E. <ets>spy</ets>. Cf.
<er>Skeptic</er>, <er>Bishop</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That at which
one aims; the thing or end to which the mind directs its view; that
which is purposed to be reached or accomplished; hence, ultimate design,
aim, or purpose; intention; drift; object.</def> <ldquo/Shooting wide,
do miss the marked <xex>scope</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your <qex>scope</qex> is as mine own,<br/
So to enforce or qualify the laws<br/
As to your soul seems good.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scope</qex> of all their pleading against man's
authority, is to overthrow such laws and constitutions in the
church.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Room or opportunity for free outlook or aim; space
for action; amplitude of opportunity; free course or vent; liberty;
range of view, intent, or action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give him line and <qex>scope</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the fate and fortunes of the human race, <qex>scope</qex> is
given to the operation of laws which man must always fail to discern the
reasons of.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Excuse me if I have given too much <qex>scope</qex> to the
reflections which have arisen in my mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An intellectual cultivation of no moderate depth or
<qex>scope</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Extended area.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The
<xex>scopes</xex> of land granted to the first adventurers.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir J. Davies.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Length; extent; sweep; <as>as, <ex>scope</ex> of
cable</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Scope, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To look at for the purpose of
evaluation; usually with <ptcl>out</ptcl>; <as>as, to <ex>scope out</ex>
the area as a camping site</as>.</def> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopeline</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"pe*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Scopeloid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopeloid</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"pe*loid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Scopelus</ets>, typical genus (fr. Gr. <?/ a headland) +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like or pertaining to
fishes of the genus <gen>Scopelus</gen>, or family
<fam>Scopelodae</fam>, which includes many small oceanic fishes, most of
which are phosphorescent.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any fish of the family
<fam>Scopelidae</fam>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sco*pif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scopae</ets>, <ets>scopa</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Bearing a tuft of brushlike hairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopiform</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"pi*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scopae</ets>, <ets>scopa</ets>, a broom + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Having the form of a broom or besom.</def> <ldquo/Zeolite,
stelliform or <xex>scopiform</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Kirwan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopiped</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"pi*ped</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scopae</ets>, <ets>scopa</ets>, a broom + <ets>pes</ets>,
<ets>pedis</ets>, a foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Scopuliped</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoppet</ent><br/
<hw>Scop"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Scoop</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To lade or dip out.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scops owl</ent><br/
<hw>Scops" owl`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[NL. <ets>scops</ets>, fr. Gr.
<?/ the little horned owl.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of small owls of the genus <gen>Scops</gen> having ear
tufts like those of the horned owls, especially the European scops owl
(<spn>Scops giu</spn>), and the American screech owl (<spn>Scops
asio</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoptical</ent><br/
<ent>Scoptic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scop"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scop"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>skwptiko`s</grk>,
from <grk>skw`ptein</grk> to mock, to scoff at.]</ety> <def>Jesting;
jeering; scoffing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Scop"tic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scop"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Scopulas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Scopulae</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>scopulae</ets>, pl. a little
broom.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A peculiar brushlike
organ found on the foot of spiders and used in the construction of the
web.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A special tuft of hairs on the leg of a
bee.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopuliped</ent><br/
<hw>Scop"u*li*ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scopulae</ets>, pl., a little broom (fr. <ets>scopae</ets> a broom)
+ <ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of bee which has on the hind legs a brush of hairs used
for collecting pollen, as the hive bees and bumblebees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scopulous</ent><br/
<hw>Scop"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scopulosus</ets>, fr. <ets>scopulus</ets> a rock, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<def>Full of rocks; rocky.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorbute</ent><br/
<hw>Scor"bute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>scorbutus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scorbut</ets>. See <er>Scurvy</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Scurvy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Purchas.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorbutical</ent><br/
<ent>Scorbutic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scor*bu"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scor*bu"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scorbutique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to scurvy; of the nature of, or resembling, scurvy; diseased with
scurvy; <as>as, a <ex>scorbutic</ex> person; <ex>scorbutic</ex>
complaints or symptoms</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Scor*bu"tic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorbutus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scor*bu"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL. See
<er>Scorbute</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Scurvy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorce</ent><br/
<hw>Scorce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Barter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <see>See <er>Scorse</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorch</ent><br/
<hw>Scorch</hw> <pr>(sk<ocir/rch)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scorched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scorching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scorchen</ets>, probably akin to <ets>scorcnen</ets>; cf. Norw.
<ets>skrokken</ets> shrunk up, <ets>skrekka</ets>,
<ets>skr<oum/kka</ets>, to shrink, to become wrinkled up, dial. Sw.
<ets>skr<aring/kkla</ets> to wrinkle (see <er>Shrug</er>); but perhaps
influenced by OF. <ets>escorchier</ets> to strip the bark from, to flay,
to skin, F. <ets><eacute/corcher</ets>, LL. <ets>excorticare</ets>; L.
<ets>ex</ets> from + <ets>cortex</ets>, <ets>-icis</ets>, bark (cf.
<er>Cork</er>); because the skin falls off when scorched.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To burn superficially; to parch, or shrivel, the
surface of, by heat; to subject to so much heat as changes color and
texture without consuming; <as>as, to <ex>scorch</ex>
linen</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Summer drouth or sing<egrave/d air<br/
Never <qex>scorch</qex> thy tresses fair.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To affect painfully with heat, or as with heat; to
dry up with heat; to affect as by heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lashed by mad rage, and <qex>scorched</qex> by brutal fires.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To burn; to destroy by, or as by, fire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Power was given unto him to <qex>scorch</qex> men with fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Rev. xvi. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fire that <qex>scorches</qex> me to death.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorch</ent><br/
<hw>Scorch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be burnt on the
surface; to be parched; to be dried up.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Scatter a little mungy straw or fern amongst your seedlings, to
prevent the roots from <qex>scorching</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To burn or be burnt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He laid his long forefinger on the scarlet letter, which forthwith
seemed to <qex>scorch</qex> into Hester's breast, as if it had been red
hot.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To ride or drive at great, usually at excessive,
speed; -- applied chiefly to automobilists and bicyclists.
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Scorch"er</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></wordforms></p>

<p><ent>scorcher</ent><br/
<hw>scorch"er</hw> <def>a very hot day.</def>
<mark>[Informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorching</ent><br/
<hw>Scorch"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Burning; parching or
shriveling with heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>sufficiently hot to cause scorching.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Scorch"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Scorch"ing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Score</ent><br/
<hw>Score</hw> <pr>(sk<omac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>scor</ets> twenty, fr. <ets>sceran</ets>, <ets>scieran</ets>, to
shear, cut, divide; or rather the kindred Icel. <ets>skor</ets>
incision, twenty, akin to Dan. <ets>skure</ets> a notch, Sw.
<ets>sk<aring/ra</ets>. See <er>Shear</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
notch or incision; especially, one that is made as a tally mark; hence,
a mark, or line, made for the purpose of account.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whereas, before, our forefathers had no other books but the
<qex>score</qex> and the tally, thou hast caused printing to be
used.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An account or reckoning; account of dues; bill;
hence, indebtedness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He parted well, and paid his <qex>score</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Account; reason; motive; sake; behalf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But left the trade, as many more<br/
Have lately done on the same <qex>score</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You act your kindness in Cydaria's <qex>score</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The number twenty, as being marked off by a special
score or tally; hence, in <pluf>pl.</pluf>, a large number.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Amongst three or four <qex>score</qex> hogsheads.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At length the queen took upon herself to grant patents of monopoly
by <qex>scores</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A distance of twenty yards; -- a term used in
ancient archery and gunnery.</def> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A weight of twenty pounds.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The number of points gained by the contestants, or
either of them, in any game, as in cards or cricket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A line drawn; a groove or furrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The original and entire draught,
or its transcript, of a composition, with the parts for all the
different instruments or voices written on staves one above another, so
that they can be read at a glance; -- so called from the bar, which, in
its early use, was drawn through all the parts.</def> <rj><au>Moore
(Encyc. of Music).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>the grade received on an examination, such as those
given in school or as a qualifying examination for a job or admission to
school; -- it may be expressed as a percentage of answers which are
correct, or as a number or letter; <as>as, a <ex>score</ex> of 98 in a
civil service exam</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>In score</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>having all the
parts arranged and placed in juxtaposition.</cd> <au>Smart.</au> --
<col><b>To quit scores</b></col>, <cd>to settle or balance accounts; to
render an equivalent; to make compensation.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Does not the earth <qex>quit scores</qex> with all the elements in
the noble fruits that issue from it?</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Score</ent><br/
<hw>Score</hw> <pr>(sk<omac/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scored</conjf> <pr>(sk<omac/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scoring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To mark
with lines, scratches, or notches; to cut notches or furrows in; to
notch; to scratch; to furrow; <as>as, to <ex>score</ex> timber for
hewing; to <ex>score</ex> the back with a lash</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let us <qex>score</qex> their backs.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A briar in that tangled wilderness<br/
Had <qex>scored</qex> her white right hand.</q> <rj><qau>M.
Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, to mark with significant lines or
notches, for indicating or keeping account of something; <as>as, to
<ex>score</ex> a tally</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To mark or signify by lines or notches; to keep
record or account of; to set down; to record; to charge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Madam, I know when,<br/
Instead of five, you <qex>scored</qex> me ten.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor need I tallies thy dear love to <qex>score</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To engrave, as upon a shield.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To make a score of, as points, runs, etc., in a
game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To write down in proper order and
arrangement; <as>as, to <ex>score</ex> an overture for an
orchestra</as>. See <er>Score</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 9.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>To mark with parallel lines or
scratches; <as>as, the rocks of New England and the Western States were
<ex>scored</ex> in the drift epoch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Score</ent><br/
<hw>Score</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To keep
the score in a game; to act as scorer.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make or count a point or points, as in a game; to
tally.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To run up a score, or account of dues.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To succeed in finding a partner for sexual
intercourse; to make a sexual conquest.</def> <mark>[slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To purchase drugs illegally.</def>
<mark>[slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorer</ent><br/
<hw>Scor"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, scores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sco"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scoriae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
dung, ordure.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The recrement of metals in fusion,
or the slag rejected after the reduction of metallic ores;
dross.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cellular slaggy lava; volcanic cinders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoriac</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"ri*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Scoriaceous.</def>
<rj><au>E. A. Poe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoriaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sco`ri*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scoriac<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to scoria; like
scoria or the recrement of metals; partaking of the nature of
scoria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorie</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"rie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
young of any gull.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>scaurie</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorification</ent><br/
<hw>Sco`ri*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scorification</ets>. See <er>Scorify</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The act, process, or result of scorifying, or
reducing to a slag; hence, the separation from earthy matter by means of
a slag; <as>as, the <ex>scorification</ex> of ores</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorifier</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"ri*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>One who, or that which, scorifies; specifically, a small flat
bowl-shaped cup used in the first heating in assaying, to remove the
earth and gangue, and to concentrate the gold and silver in a lead
button.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoriform</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"ri*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>In the form of
scoria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorify</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"ri*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scorified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scorifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[<ets>Scoria</ets> + <ets>-fy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>scorifier</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To reduce to scoria
or slag; specifically, in assaying, to fuse so as to separate the gangue
and earthy material, with borax, lead, soda, etc., thus leaving the gold
and silver in a lead button; hence, to separate from, or by means of, a
slag.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorious</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Scoriaceous.</def>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorn</ent><br/
<hw>Scorn</hw> <pr>(sk<ocir/rn)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scorn</ets>, <ets>scarn</ets>, <ets>scharn</ets>, OF.
<ets>escarn</ets>, <ets>escharn</ets>, <ets>eschar</ets>, of German
origin; cf. OHG. <ets>skern</ets> mockery, <ets>skern<omac/n</ets> to
mock; but cf. also OF. <ets>escorner</ets> to mock.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Extreme and lofty contempt; haughty disregard; that disdain which
springs from the opinion of the utter meanness and unworthiness of an
object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Scorn</qex> at first makes after love the more.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And wandered backward as in <qex>scorn</qex>,<br/
To wait an aeon to be born.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An act or expression of extreme contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every sullen frown and bitter <qex>scorn</qex><br/
But fanned the fuel that too fast did burn.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An object of extreme disdain, contempt, or
derision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou makest us a reproach to our neighbors, a <qex>scorn</qex> and
a derision to them that are round about us.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. xliv.
13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To think scorn</b></col>, <cd>to regard as worthy of
scorn or contempt; to disdain.</cd> <ldquo/He <xex>thought scorn</xex>
to lay hands on Mordecai alone.<rdquo/ <au>Esther iii. 6.</au> --
<col><b>To laugh to scorn</b></col>, <cd>to deride; to make a mock of;
to ridicule as contemptible.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Contempt; disdain; derision; contumely; despite;
slight; dishonor; mockery.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorn</ent><br/
<hw>Scorn</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scorned</conjf> <pr>(sk<ocir/rnd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scoring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>scornen</ets>,
<ets>scarnen</ets>, <ets>schornen</ets>, OF. <ets>escarnir</ets>,
<ets>escharnir</ets>. See <er>Scorn</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To hold in extreme contempt; to reject as unworthy of
regard; to despise; to contemn; to disdain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>scorn</qex> thy meat; 't would choke me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This my long sufferance, and my day of grace,<br/
Those who neglect and <qex>scorn</qex> shall never taste.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>scorn</qex> what is in itself contemptible or
disgraceful.</q> <rj><qau>C. J. Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To treat with extreme contempt; to make the object
of insult; to mock; to scoff at; to deride.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His fellow, that lay by his bed's side,<br/
Gan for to laugh, and <qex>scorned</qex> him full fast.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To taunt and <qex>scorn</qex> you thus opprobriously.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To contemn; despise; disdain. See
<er>Contemn</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1290 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scorn</ent><br/
<hw>Scorn</hw> <pr>(sk<ocir/rn)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To scoff; to
mock; to show contumely, derision, or reproach; to act
disdainfully.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He said mine eyes were black and my hair black,<br/
And, now I am remembered, <qex>scorned</qex> at me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorner</ent><br/
<hw>Scorn"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who scorns; a
despiser; a contemner; specifically, a scoffer at religion.</def>
<ldquo/Great <xex>scorners</xex> of death.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Surely he scorneth the <qex>scorners</qex>: but he giveth grace
unto the lowly.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. iii. 34.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scornful</ent><br/
<hw>Scorn"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of
scorn or contempt; contemptuous; disdainful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Scornful</qex> of winter's frost and summer's sun.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dart not <qex>scornful</qex> glances from those eyes.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Treated with scorn; exciting scorn.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scornful</qex> mark of every open eye.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Contemptuous; disdainful; contumelious;
reproachful; insolent.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Scorn"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Scorn"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorny</ent><br/
<hw>Scorn"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Deserving scorn;
paltry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorodite</ent><br/
<hw>Scor"o*dite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>scorodit</ets>; -- so called in allusion to its smell under the
blowpipe, from Gr. <?/ garlic.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
leek-green or brownish mineral occurring in orthorhombic crystals. It is
a hydrous arseniate of iron.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>skorodite</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpaenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Scor*pae"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Scorpaena</ets>, a typical genus (see <er>Scorpene</er>) +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
family <fam>Scorpaenidae</fam>, which includes the scorpene, the
rosefish, the California rockfishes, and many other food fishes.
<altsp>[Written also <asp>scorpaenid</asp>.]</altsp></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpene</ent><br/
<hw>Scor"pene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>scorp<egrave/ne</ets>, fr. L. <ets>scorpaena</ets> a kind of fish,
Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A marine food fish of the genus
<gen>Scorpaena</gen>, as the European hogfish (<spn>Scorpaena
scrofa</spn>), and the California species (<spn>Scorpaena
guttata</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorper</ent><br/
<hw>Scor"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Scauper</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scor"pi*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scorpiones</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A scorpion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The eighth sign of
the zodiac, which the sun enters about the twenty-third day of October,
marked thus [<Scorpio/] in almanacs.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
constellation of the zodiac containing the bright star Antares. It is
drawn on the celestial globe in the figure of a scorpion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpiodea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scor`pi*o"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Scorpiones</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpioidal</ent><br/
<ent>Scorpioid</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scor"pi*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scor`pi*oid"al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/ a scorpion +
<grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the
inflorescence curved or circinate at the end, like a scorpion's
tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpion</ent><br/
<hw>Scor"pi*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>scorpio</ets>, <ets>scorpius</ets>, Gr. <?/, perhaps akin to E.
<ets>sharp</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of pulmonate arachnids of the order
<ord>Scorpiones</ord>, having a suctorial mouth, large claw-bearing
palpi, and a caudal sting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Scorpions have a flattened body, and a long, slender
post-abdomen formed of six movable segments, the last of which
terminates in a curved venomous sting. The venom causes great pain, but
is unattended either with redness or swelling, except in the axillary or
inguinal glands, when an extremity is affected. It is seldom if ever
destructive of life. Scorpions are found widely dispersed in the warm
climates of both the Old and New Worlds.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The pine or gray lizard
(<spn>Sceloporus undulatus</spn>).</def> <mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The scorpene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Script.)</fld> <def>A painful scourge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My father hath chastised you with whips, but I will chastise you
with <qex>scorpions</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>1 Kings xii. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A sign and constellation. See
<er>Scorpio</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Antiq.)</fld> <def>An ancient military engine for
hurling stones and other missiles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Book scorpion</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Book</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>False scorpion</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>False</er>, and <cref>Book
scorpion</cref>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Scorpion bug</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Water scorpion</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Nepa</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scorpion fly</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a neuropterous insect of the genus
<gen>Panorpa</gen>. See <er>Panorpid</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scorpion
grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a plant of the genus
<gen>Myosotis</gen>. <spn>Myosotis palustris</spn> is the
<stype>forget-me-not</stype>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scorpion senna</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a yellow-flowered leguminous shrub
(<spn>Coronilla Emerus</spn>) having a slender joined pod, like a
scorpion's tail. The leaves are said to yield a dye like indigo, and to
be used sometimes to adulterate senna.</cd> -- <col><b>Scorpion
shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any shell of the genus
Pteroceras. See <er>Pteroceras</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scorpion
spiders</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of the
Pedipalpi.</cd> -- <col><b>Scorpion's tail</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>any plant of the leguminous genus <gen>Scorpiurus</gen>, herbs with
a circinately coiled pod; -- also called
<altname>caterpillar</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scorpion's
thorn</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a thorny leguminous plant
(<spn>Genista Scorpius</spn>) of Southern Europe.</cd> -- <col><b>The
Scorpion's Heart</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the star Antares in
the constellation Scorpio.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpiones</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scor`pi*o"nes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of arachnids
comprising the scorpions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpionidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scor`pi*o*nid"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Scorpiones</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorpionwort</ent><br/
<hw>Scor"pi*on*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A leguminous plant (<spn>Ornithopus scorpioides</spn>) of Southern
Europe, having slender curved pods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorse</ent><br/
<hw>Scorse</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It.
<ets>scorsa</ets> a course, and E. <ets>discourse</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Barter; exchange; trade.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And recompensed them with a better <qex>scorse</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorse</ent><br/
<hw>Scorse</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Written also <ets>scourse</ets>,
and <ets>scoss</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To barter or
exchange.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To chase.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scorse</ent><br/
<hw>Scorse</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To deal for the purchase of
anything; to practice barter.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scortatory</ent><br/
<hw>Scor"ta*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scortator</ets> a fornicator, from <ets>scortari</ets> to
fornicate, <ets>scortum</ets> a prostitute.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to
lewdness or fornication; lewd.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scot</ent><br/
<hw>Scot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A name for a horse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scot</ent><br/
<hw>Scot</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L. <ets>Skoti</ets>, pl., AS.
<ets>Scotta</ets>, pl. <ets>Skottas</ets>, <ets>Sceottas</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A native or inhabitant of Scotland; a Scotsman, or
Scotchman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scot</ent><br/
<hw>Scot</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skot</ets>; or OF.
<ets>escot</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/cot</ets>, LL. <ets>scottum</ets>,
<ets>scotum</ets>, from a kindred German word; akin to AS.
<ets>scot</ets>, and E. <ets>shot</ets>, <ets>shoot</ets>; cf. AS.
<ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets> to shoot, to contribute. See <er>Shoot</er>,
and cf. <er>Shot</er>.]</ety> <def>A portion of money assessed or paid;
a tax or contribution; a mulct; a fine; a shot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Scot and lot</b></col>, <cd>formerly, a parish assessment
laid on subjects according to their ability.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<au>Cowell.</au> <cd>Now, a phrase for obligations of every kind
regarded collectivelly.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Experienced men of the world know very well that it is best to pay
<qex>scot and lot</qex> as they go along.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Scotale</ent><br/
<ent>Scotal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scot"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scot"ale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Scot</ets> + <ets>ale</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>The keeping of an alehouse by an officer
of a forest, and drawing people to spend their money for liquor, for
fear of his displeasure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scottish</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Scotland, its
language, or its inhabitants; Scottish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scotch broom</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
<spn>Cytisus scoparius</spn>. See <er>Broom</er>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Scotch dipper</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Scotch
duck</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the bufflehead; -- called
also <altname>Scotch teal</altname>, and
<altname>Scotchman</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scotch fiddle</b></col>,
<cd>the itch.</cd> <mark>[Low]</mark> <au>Sir W. Scott.</au> --
<col><b>Scotch mist</b></col>, <cd>a coarse, dense mist, like fine
rain.</cd> -- <col><b>Scotch nightingale</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the sedge warbler.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Scotch
pebble</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>pebble</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scotch
pine</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Riga fir</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Scotch thistle</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a species of
thistle (<spn>Onopordon acanthium</spn>); -- so called from its being
the national emblem of the Scotch.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The dialect or dialects
of English spoken by the people of Scotland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Collectively, the people of Scotland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scotched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scotching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Prov. E. <ets>scote</ets>
a prop, and Walloon <ets>ascot</ets> a prop, <ets>ascoter</ets> to prop,
F. <ets>accoter</ets>, also Armor. <ets>skoaz</ets> the shoulder,
<ets>skoazia</ets> to shoulder up, to prop, to support, W.
<ets>ysgwydd</ets> a shoulder, <ets>ysgwyddo</ets> to shoulder. Cf.
<er>Scoat</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>scoatch</asp>,
<asp>scoat</asp>.]</altsp> <def>To shoulder up; to prop or block with a
wedge, chock, etc., as a wheel, to prevent its rolling or
slipping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A chock, wedge, prop, or other
support, to prevent slipping; <as>as, a <ex>scotch</ex> for a wheel or a
log on inclined ground</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Probably the same word as
<ets>scutch</ets>; cf. Norw. <ets>skoka</ets>, <ets>skoko</ets>, a
swingle for flax; perhaps akin to E. <ets>shake</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
cut superficially; to wound; to score.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have <qex>scotched</qex> the snake, not killed it.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scotched collops</b></col> <fld>(Cookery)</fld>, <cd>a
dish made of pieces of beef or veal cut thin, or minced, beaten flat,
and stewed with onion and other condiments; -- called also
<altname>Scotch collops</altname>.</cd> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>scotcht collops</asp>.]</altsp></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slight cut or incision; a
score.</def> <rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch-hopper</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch"-hop`per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Hopscotch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotching</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>Dressing
stone with a pick or pointed instrument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotchman</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scotchmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or
inhabitant of Scotland; a Scot; a Scotsman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A piece of wood or stiff hide
placed over shrouds and other rigging to prevent chafe by the running
gear.</def> <rj><au>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch rite</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch rite</hw>. <fld>(Freemasonry)</fld> <def>The ceremonial
observed by one of the <col><b>Masonic systems</b></col>, called in full
the <altname>Ancient and Accepted Scotch Rite</altname>; also, the
system itself, which confers thirty-three degrees, of which the first
three are nearly identical with those of the York rite.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotch terrier</ent><br/
<hw>Scotch terrier</hw>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a breed of small
terriers with long, rough hair.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoter</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Prov. E.
<ets>scote</ets> to plow up.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
several species of northern sea ducks of the genus
<gen>Oidemia</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The European scoters are <styp><spn>Oidemia
nigra</spn></styp>, called also <altname>black duck</altname>,
<altname>black diver</altname>, <altname>surf duck</altname>; and the
velvet, or double, scoter (<styp><spn>Oidemia fusca</spn></styp>). The
common American species are the velvet, or white-winged, scoter
(<spn>Oidemia Deglandi</spn>), called also <altname>velvet
duck</altname>, <altname>white-wing</altname>, <altname>bull
coot</altname>, <altname>white-winged coot</altname>; the black scoter
(<spn>Oidemia Americana</spn>), called also <altname>black
coot</altname>, <altname>butterbill</altname>,
<altname>coppernose</altname>; and the surf scoter, or surf duck
(<spn>Oidemia perspicillata</spn>), called also
<altname>baldpate</altname>, <altname>skunkhead</altname>,
<altname>horsehead</altname>, <altname>patchhead</altname>,
<altname>pishaug</altname>, and <altname>spectacled coot</altname>.
These birds are collectively called also <altname>coots</altname>. The
females and young are called <stype>gray coots</stype>, and <stype>brown
coots</stype>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scot-free</ent><br/
<hw>Scot"-free"</hw> <pr><mark>[?]</mark></pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free
from payment of scot; untaxed; hence, unhurt; clear; safe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do as much for this purpose, and thou shalt pass
<qex>scot-free</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then young Hay escaped <qex>scot-free</qex> to Holland.</q>
<rj><qau>A. Lang.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoth</ent><br/
<hw>Scoth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To clothe or cover
up.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sco"ti*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>skoti`a</grk> darkness, a sunken molding in the base of a pillar,
so called from the dark shadow it casts, from <grk>sko`tos</grk>
darkness.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A concave molding used
especially in classical architecture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotia</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"ti*a</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>Scotland</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O <qex>Scotia</qex>! my dear, my native soil!</q>
<rj><qau>Burns.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotist</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld>
<def>A follower of (Joannes) Duns <etsep>Scotus</etsep>, the Franciscan
scholastic (<it>d.</it> 1308), who maintained certain doctrines in
philosophy and theology, in opposition to the <contr>Thomists</contr>,
or followers of Thomas Aquinas, the Dominican scholastic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotograph</ent><br/
<hw>Scot"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sko`tos</grk> darkness + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
instrument for writing in the dark, or without seeing.</def>
<rj><au>Maunder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sco*to"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Scotomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Scot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>scotomia</ets>, from Gr. <?/ dizziness, fr. <?/ to darken, fr.
<grk>sko`tos</grk> darkness: cf. F. <ets>scotomie</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Dizziness with dimness of sight.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Obscuration of the field of vision
due to the appearance of a dark spot before the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Sco"to*scope</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sko`tos</grk> darkness + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
instrument that discloses objects in the dark or in a faint light.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Pepys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scots</ent><br/
<hw>Scots</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[For older
<ets>Scottis</ets> Scottish. See <er>Scottish</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the Scotch; Scotch; Scottish; <as>as, <ex>Scots</ex> law;
a pound <ex>Scots</ex> (1s. 8d.)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotsman</ent><br/
<hw>Scots"man</hw> <pr>(-m<it>a</it>n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scotchman</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scottering</ent><br/
<hw>Scot"ter*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The burning of a
wad of pease straw at the end of harvest.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotticism</ent><br/
<hw>Scot"ti*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An idiom, or mode
of expression, peculiar to Scotland or Scotchmen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That, in short, in which the <qex>Scotticism</qex> of Scotsmen
most intimately consists, is the habit of emphasis.</q>
<rj><qau>Masson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scotticize</ent><br/
<hw>Scot"ti*cize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to
become like the Scotch; to make Scottish.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scottish</ent><br/
<hw>Scot"tish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Scot</er>
a Scotchman: cf. AS. <ets>Scyttisc</ets>, and E. <er>Scotch</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, <er>Scots</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the inhabitants of Scotland, their country, or their
language; <as>as, <ex>Scottish</ex> industry or economy; a
<ex>Scottish</ex> chief; a <ex>Scottish</ex> dialect</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scottish terrier</ent><br/
<hw>Scot"tish ter"ri*er</hw>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<cref>Scotch terrier</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoundrel</ent><br/
<hw>Scoun"drel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably from
Prov. E. & Scotch <ets>scunner</ets>, <ets>scouner</ets>, to loathe, to
disgust, akin to AS. <ets>scunian</ets> to shun. See
<er>Shun</er>.]</ety> <def>A mean, worthless fellow; a rascal; a
villain; a man without honor or virtue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Go, if your ancient, but ignoble blood<br/
Has crept through <qex>scoundrels</qex> ever since the flood.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoundrel</ent><br/
<hw>Scoun"drel</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Low; base; mean;
unprincipled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoundreldom</ent><br/
<hw>Scoun"drel*dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The domain or
sphere of scoundrels; scoundrels, collectively; the state, ideas, or
practices of scoundrels.</def> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scoundrelism</ent><br/
<hw>Scoun"drel*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The practices
or conduct of a scoundrel; baseness; rascality.</def>
<rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scour</ent><br/
<hw>Scour</hw> <pr>(skour)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scoured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scouring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to LG.
<ets>sch<uum/ren</ets>, D. <ets>schuren</ets>, <ets>schueren</ets>, G.
<ets>scheuern</ets>, Dan. <ets>skure</ets>; Sw. <ets>skura</ets>; all
possibly fr. LL. <ets>escurare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>ex</ets> +
<ets>curare</ets> to take care. Cf. <er>Cure</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To rub hard with something rough, as sand or Bristol brick,
especially for the purpose of cleaning; to clean by friction; to make
clean or bright; to cleanse from grease, dirt, etc., as articles of
dress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To purge; <as>as, to <ex>scour</ex> a
horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To remove by rubbing or cleansing; to sweep along or
off; to carry away or remove, as by a current of water; -- often with
<xex>off</xex> or <xex>away</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[I will] stain my favors in a bloody mask,<br/
Which, washed away, shall <qex>scour</qex> my shame with it.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <ety>[Perhaps a different word; cf. OF.
<ets>escorre</ets>, <ets>escourre</ets>, It. <ets>scorrere</ets>, both
fr. L. <ets>excurrere</ets> to run forth. Cf. <er>Excursion</er>.]</ety>
<def>To pass swiftly over; to brush along; to traverse or search
thoroughly; <as>as, to <ex>scour</ex> the coast</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not so when swift Camilla <qex>scours</qex> the plain.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To cleanse or clear, as by a current of water; to
flush.</def></p>

<p><q>If my neighbor ought to <qex>scour</qex> a ditch.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scouring barrel</b></col>, <cd>a tumbling barrel. See
under <er>Tumbling</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scouring cinder</b></col>
<fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a basic slag, which attacks the lining of a
shaft furnace.</cd> <au>Raymond.</au> -- <col><b>Scouring
rush</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Dutch rush</cref>, under
<er>Dutch</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scouring stock</b></col> <fld>(Woolen
Manuf.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of fulling mill.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scour</ent><br/
<hw>Scour</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To clean anything by
rubbing.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cleanse anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Warm water is softer than cold, for it <qex>scoureth</qex>
better.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be purged freely; to have a diarrhoea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To run swiftly; to rove or range in pursuit or
search of something; to scamper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So four fierce coursers, starting to the race,<br/
<qex>Scour</qex> through the plain, and lengthen every pace.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scour</ent><br/
<hw>Scour</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Diarrhoea or dysentery
among cattle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of scouring.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A place scoured out by running water, as in the bed
of a stream below a fall.</def></p>

<p><q>If you catch the two sole denizens [trout] of a particular
<qex>scour</qex>, you will find another pair installed in their place
to-morrow.</q> <rj><qau>Grant Allen.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scourage</ent><br/
<hw>Scour"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Refuse water
after scouring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scourer</ent><br/
<hw>Scour"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, scours.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rover or footpad; a prowling robber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In those days of highwaymen and <qex>scourers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scourge</ent><br/
<hw>Scourge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>escourg<eacute/e</ets>, fr. L. <ets>excoriata</ets> (sc.
<ets>scutica</ets>) a stripped off (lash or whip), fr.
<ets>excoriare</ets> to strip, to skin. See <er>Excoriate</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A lash; a strap or cord; especially, a lash used to
inflict pain or punishment; an instrument of punishment or discipline; a
whip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Up to coach then goes<br/
The observed maid, takes both the <qex>scourge</qex> and reins.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a means of inflicting punishment, vengeance,
or suffering; an infliction of affliction; a punishment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sharp <qex>scourges</qex> of adversity.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>scourge</qex> for perjury<br/
Can this dark monarchy afford false Clarence?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scourge</ent><br/
<hw>Scourge</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scourged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scourging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From
<er>Scourge</er>, <pos>n.</pos>: cf. OF. <ets>escorgier</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To whip severely; to lash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is it lawful for you to <qex>scourge</qex> a . . . Roman?</q>
<rj><qau>Acts xxii. 25.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To punish with severity; to chastise; to afflict, as
for sins or faults, and with the purpose of correction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whom the Lord loveth he chasteneth, and <qex>scourgeth</qex> every
son whom he receiveth.</q> <rj><qau>Heb. xii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To harass or afflict severely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>scourge</qex> and impoverish the people.</q>
<rj><qau>Brougham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scourger</ent><br/
<hw>Scour"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who scourges or
punishes; one who afflicts severely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The West must own the <qex>scourger</qex> of the world.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scourse</ent><br/
<hw>Scourse</hw> <pr>(sk<omac/rs)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scorse</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scouse</ent><br/
<hw>Scouse</hw> <pr>(skous)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A sailor's dish. Bread <ex>scouse</ex> contains no meat;
lob<ex>scouse</ex> contains meat, etc. See <er>Lobscouse</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scout</ent><br/
<hw>Scout</hw> <pr>(skout)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>sk<umac/ta</ets> a small craft or cutter.]</ety> <def>A swift
sailing boat.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So we took a <qex>scout</qex>, very much pleased with the manner
and conversation of the passengers.</q> <rj><qau>Pepys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scout</ent><br/
<hw>Scout</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>sk<umac/ta</ets> to jut
out. Cf. <er>Scout</er> to reject.]</ety> <def>A projecting rock.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1291 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scout</ent><br/
<hw>Scout</hw> <pr>(skout)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>sk<umac/ta</ets> a taunt; cf. Icel. <ets>sk<umac/ta</ets> to jut
out, <ets>skota</ets> to shove, <ets>skj<omac/ta</ets> to shoot, to
shove. See <er>Shoot</er>.]</ety> <def>To reject with contempt, as
something absurd; to treat with ridicule; to flout; <as>as, to
<ex>scout</ex> an idea or an apology</as>.</def> <ldquo/Flout 'em and
<xex>scout</xex> 'em.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scout</ent><br/
<hw>Scout</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>escoute</ets> scout, spy,
fr. <ets>escouter</ets>, <ets>escolter</ets>, to listen, to hear, F.
<ets><eacute/couter</ets>, fr. L. <ets>auscultare</ets>, to hear with
attention, to listen to. See <er>Auscultation</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A person sent out to gain and bring in tidings; especially, one
employed in war to gain information of the movements and condition of an
enemy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Scouts</qex> each coast light-arm<egrave/d scour,<br/
Each quarter, to descry the distant foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A college student's or undergraduate's servant; --
so called in Oxford, England; at Cambridge called a <xex>gyp</xex>; and
at Dublin, a <xex>skip</xex>.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>A fielder in a game for
practice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act of scouting or reconnoitering.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While the rat is on the <qex>scout</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A boy scout or girl scout (which see,
above).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Scout</er>, <er>Spy</er>.</syn> -- <usage>In
a military sense a <xex>scout</xex> is a soldier who does duty in his
proper uniform, however hazardous his adventure. A <xex>spy</xex> is one
who in disguise penetrates the enemies' lines, or lurks near them, to
obtain information.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scout</ent><br/
<hw>Scout</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scouted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scouting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To observe, watch,
or look for, as a scout; to follow for the purpose of observation, as a
scout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Take more men,<br/
And <qex>scout</qex> him round.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pass over or through, as a scout; to reconnoiter;
<as>as, to <ex>scout</ex> a country</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scout</ent><br/
<hw>Scout</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To go on the business of scouting,
or watching the motions of an enemy; to act as a scout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With obscure wing<br/
<qex>Scout</qex> far and wide into the realm of night.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scovel</ent><br/
<hw>Scov"el</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/v"'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escouve</ets>, <ets>escouvette</ets>, broom, L. <ets>scopae</ets>,
or cf. W. <ets>ysgubell</ets>, dim. of <ets>ysgub</ets> a broom.]</ety>
<def>A mop for sweeping ovens; a malkin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scow</ent><br/
<hw>Scow</hw> <pr>(skou)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>schouw</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A large flat-bottomed
boat, having broad, square ends.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scow</ent><br/
<hw>Scow</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To transport in a scow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scowl</ent><br/
<hw>Scowl</hw> <pr>(skoul)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scowled</conjf> <pr>(skould)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scowling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to Dan.
<ets>skule</ets>; cf. Icel. <ets>skolla</ets> to skulk, LG.
<ets>schulen</ets> to hide one's self, D. <ets>schuilen</ets>, G.
<ets>schielen</ets> to squint, Dan. <ets>skele</ets>, Sw.
<ets>skela</ets>, AS. <ets>sceolh</ets> squinting. Cf.
<er>Skulk</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To wrinkle the brows, as in
frowning or displeasure; to put on a frowning look; to look sour,
sullen, severe, or angry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She <qex>scowled</qex> and frowned with froward countenance.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to look gloomy, dark, or threatening; to
lower.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>scowling</xex> heavens.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scowl</ent><br/
<hw>Scowl</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To look at or repel
with a scowl or a frown.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To express by a scowl; <as>as, to <ex>scowl</ex>
defiance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scowl</ent><br/
<hw>Scowl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The wrinkling of the
brows or face in frowing; the expression of displeasure, sullenness, or
discontent in the countenance; an angry frown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With solemn phiz, and critic <qex>scowl</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Lloyd.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, gloom; dark or threatening aspect.</def>
<rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A ruddy storm, whose <qex>scowl</qex><br/
Made heaven's radiant face look foul.</q>
<rj><qau>Crashaw.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scowlingly</ent><br/
<hw>Scowl"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scowling
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrabbed eggs</ent><br/
<hw>Scrab"bed eggs`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[CF.
<er>Scramble</er>.]</ety> <def>A Lenten dish, composed of eggs boiled
hard, chopped, and seasoned with butter, salt, and pepper.</def>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrabble</ent><br/
<hw>Scrab"ble</hw> <pr>(skr<acr/b"b'l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scrabbled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scrabbling</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of <ets>scrape</ets>. Cf.
<er>Scramble</er>, <er>Scrawl</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To scrape, paw, or scratch with the hands; to proceed by clawing
with the hands and feet; to scramble; <as>as, to <ex>scrabble</ex> up a
cliff or a tree</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now after a while Little-faith came to himself, and getting up
made shift to <qex>scrabble</qex> on his way.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make irregular, crooked, or unmeaning marks; to
scribble; to scrawl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>David . . . <qex>scrabbled</qex> on the doors of the gate.</q>
<rj><qau>1. Sam. xxi. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrabble</ent><br/
<hw>Scrab"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To mark with irregular lines
or letters; to scribble; <as>as, to <ex>scrabble</ex>
paper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrabble</ent><br/
<hw>Scrab"ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of scrabbling; a moving
upon the hands and knees; a scramble; also, a scribble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraber</ent><br/
<hw>Scra"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scrabble</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The Manx
shearwater.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The black guillemot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraffle</ent><br/
<hw>Scraf"fle</hw> <pr>(skr<acr/f"f'l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scramble</er>: cf. OD. <ets>schraeffelen</ets> to scrape.]</ety>
<def>To scramble or struggle; to wrangle; also, to be industrious.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrag</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag</hw> <pr>(skr<acr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. dial. Sw.
<ets>skraka</ets> a great dry tree, a long, lean man, Gael.
<ets>sgreagach</ets> dry, shriveled, rocky. See <er>Shrink</er>, and cf.
<er>Scrog</er>, <er>Shrag</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Something thin, lean, or rough; a bony piece; especially, a bony
neckpiece of meat; hence, humorously or in contempt, the neck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lady MacScrew, who . . . serves up a <qex>scrag</qex> of mutton on
silver.</q> <rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rawboned person.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A ragged, stunted tree or branch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scrag whale</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a North
Atlantic whalebone whale (<spn>Agaphelus gibbosus</spn>). By some it is
considered the young of the right whale.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrag</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scrag</er>.]</ety> <def>To seize, pull, or twist the neck of;
specif., to hang by the neck; to kill by hanging.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></p>

<p><q>An enthusiastic mob will <qex>scrag</qex> me to a certainty the
day war breaks out.</q> <rj><qau>Pall Mall Mag.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scragged</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag"ged</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Rough
with irregular points, or a broken surface; scraggy; <as>as, a
<ex>scragged</ex> backbone</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Lean and rough; scraggy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraggedness</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag"ged*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
scragged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraggily</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag"gi*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scraggy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scragginess</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag"gi*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
scraggy; scraggedness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraggy</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Scragger</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Scraggiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Rough with irregular points; scragged.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>scraggy</xex> rock.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. Philips.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Lean and rough; scragged.</def> <ldquo/His sinewy,
<xex>scraggy</xex> neck.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scragly</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Scraggy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrag-necked</ent><br/
<hw>Scrag"-necked`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
scraggy neck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scram</ent><br/
<hw>Scram</hw> <pr>(skr<acr/m)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scrammed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scramming</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>to leave;
to go away; used mostly as an impolite command to a person to go away
from a specific location.</def> <mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scram</ent><br/
<hw>Scram</hw> <pr>(skr<acr/m)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scrammed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scramming</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>to shut
down (a nuclear reactor) quickly, as in an emergency.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scram</ent><br/
<hw>Scram</hw> <pr>(skr<acr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>the rapid shut
down of a nuclear reactor, as in an emergency.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scramble</ent><br/
<hw>Scram"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scrambled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scrambling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq.
of Prov. E. <ets>scramb</ets> to rake together with the hands, or of
<ets>scramp</ets> to snatch at. cf. <er>Scrabble</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To clamber with hands and knees; to scrabble; <as>as,
to <ex>scramble</ex> up a cliff; to <ex>scramble</ex> over the
rocks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To struggle eagerly with others for something thrown
upon the ground; to go down upon all fours to seize something; to catch
rudely at what is desired.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of other care they little reckoning make,<br/
Than how to <qex>scramble</qex> at the shearer's feast.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scramble</ent><br/
<hw>Scram"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
collect by scrambling; <as>as, to <ex>scramble</ex> up
wealth</as>.</def> <rj><au>Marlowe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To prepare (eggs) as a dish for the table, by
stirring the yolks and whites together while cooking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scramble</ent><br/
<hw>Scram"ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
scrambling, climbing on all fours, or clambering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of jostling and pushing for something
desired; eager and unceremonious struggle for what is thrown or held
out; <as>as, a <ex>scramble</ex> for office</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Scarcity [of money] enhances its price, and increases the
<qex>scramble</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrambled eggs</ent><br/
<hw>Scram"bled eggs</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Eggs of which the whites and
yolks are stirred together while cooking, or eggs beaten slightly, often
with a little milk, and stirred while cooking.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrambler</ent><br/
<hw>Scram"bler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
scrambles; one who climbs on all fours.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A greedy and unceremonious contestant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrambling</ent><br/
<hw>Scram"bling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Confused and
irregular; awkward; scambling.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Scram"bling*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A huge old <qex>scrambling</qex> bedroom.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scranch</ent><br/
<hw>Scranch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scranched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scranching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>schransen</ets> to eat greedily, G. <ets>schranzen</ets>. Cf.
<er>Crunch</er>, <er>Scrunch</er>.]</ety> <def>To grind with the teeth,
and with a crackling sound; to craunch.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scranky</ent><br/
<hw>Scrank"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Thin; lean.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrannel</ent><br/
<hw>Scran"nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scrawny</er>.]</ety> <def>Slight; thin; lean; poor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Grate on their <qex>scrannel</qex> pipes of wretched straw.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scranny</ent><br/
<hw>Scran"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scrannel</er>.]</ety> <def>Thin; lean; meager; scrawny;
scrannel.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrap</ent><br/
<hw>Scrap</hw> <pr>(skr<acr/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scrappe</ets>, fr. Icel. <ets>skrap</ets> trifle, cracking. See
<er>Scrape</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something
scraped off; hence, a small piece; a bit; a fragment; a detached,
incomplete portion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have no materials -- not a <qex>scrap</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, a fragment of something written or
printed; a brief excerpt; an unconnected extract.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The crisp substance that remains
after drying out animal fat; <as>as, pork <ex>scraps</ex></as>.</def><--
original had "trying out". -- apparently a misprint in original --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Same as <cref>Scrap iron</cref>,
below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 5. Manufactured objects or parts useful only for reprocessing,
esp. metal objects. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scrap forgings</b></col>, <cd>forgings made from wrought
iron scrap.</cd> -- <col><b>Scrap iron</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>Cuttings and waste pieces of wrought iron from which bar iron or
forgings can be made; -- called also <altname>wrought-iron
scrap</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Fragments of cast iron or
defective castings suitable for remelting in the foundry; -- called also
<altname>foundry scrap</altname>, or <altname>cast
scrap</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrapbook</ent><br/
<hw>Scrap"book`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A blank book in
which extracts cut from books and papers may be pasted and
kept.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrape</ent><br/
<hw>Scrape</hw> <pr>(skr<amac/p)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scraped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scraping</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Icel. <ets>skrapa</ets>; akin to Sw. <ets>skrapa</ets>, Dan.
<ets>skrabe</ets>, D. <ets>schrapen</ets>, <ets>schrabben</ets>, G.
<ets>schrappen</ets>, and prob. to E. <ets>sharp</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To rub over the surface of (something) with a sharp or
rough instrument; to rub over with something that roughens by removing
portions of the surface; to grate harshly over; to abrade; to make even,
or bring to a required condition or form, by moving the sharp edge of an
instrument breadthwise over the surface with pressure, cutting away
excesses and superfluous parts; to make smooth or clean; <as>as, to
<ex>scrape</ex> a bone with a knife; to <ex>scrape</ex> a metal plate to
an even surface</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To remove by rubbing or scraping (in the sense
above).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will also <qex>scrape</qex> her dust from her, and make her like
the top of a rock.</q> <rj><qau>Ezek. xxvi. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To collect by, or as by, a process of scraping; to
gather in small portions by laborious effort; hence, to acquire
avariciously and save penuriously; -- often followed by
<xex>together</xex> or <xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>scrape</ex> money
together</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The prelatical party complained that, to swell a number the
nonconformists did not choose, but <qex>scrape</qex>, subscribers.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To express disapprobation of, as a play, or to
silence, as a speaker, by drawing the feet back and forth upon the
floor; -- usually with <xex>down</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To scrape acquaintance</b></col>, <cd>to seek
acquaintance otherwise than by an introduction.</cd>
<rj><au>Farquhar.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He tried to <qex>scrape acquaintance</qex> with her, but failed
ignominiously.</q> <rj><qau>G. W. Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrape</ent><br/
<hw>Scrape</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To rub over the
surface of anything with something which roughens or removes it, or
which smooths or cleans it; to rub harshly and noisily along.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To occupy one's self with getting laboriously;
<as>as, he <ex>scraped</ex> and saved until he became rich</as>.</def>
<ldquo/[Spend] their <xex>scraping</xex> fathers' gold.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To play awkwardly and inharmoniously on a violin or
like instrument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To draw back the right foot along the ground or
floor when making a bow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrape</ent><br/
<hw>Scrape</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of scraping;
also, the effect of scraping, as a scratch, or a harsh sound; <as>as, a
noisy <ex>scrape</ex> on the floor; a <ex>scrape</ex> of a
pen</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A drawing back of the right foot when bowing; also,
a bow made with that accompaniment.</def> <rj><au>H.
Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A disagreeable and embarrassing predicament out of
which one can not get without undergoing, as it were, a painful rubbing
or scraping; a perplexity; a difficulty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The too eager pursuit of this his old enemy through thick and thin
has led him into many of these <qex>scrapes</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrapepenny</ent><br/
<hw>Scrape"pen`ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who gathers
and hoards money in trifling sums; a miser.</def><-- = a pinchpenny
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraper</ent><br/
<hw>Scrap"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
instrument with which anything is scraped.</def> Specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>An instrument by which the soles of shoes are cleaned
from mud and the like, by drawing them across it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>An instrument drawn by oxen or horses, used for scraping up earth
in making or repairing roads, digging cellars, canals etc.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>An instrument having two or three
sharp sides or edges, for cleaning the planks, masts, or decks of a
ship.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Lithography)</fld> <def>In the printing
press, a board, or blade, the edge of which is made to rub over the
tympan sheet and thus produce the impression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who scrapes.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>One who plays awkwardly on a violin.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One
who acquires avariciously and saves penuriously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraping</ent><br/
<hw>Scrap"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of scraping; the act or process of making even, or reducing to the
proper form, by means of a scraper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something scraped off; that which is separated from
a substance, or is collected by scraping; <as>as, the <ex>scraping</ex>
of the street</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraping</ent><br/
<hw>Scrap"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling the act of, or the
effect produced by, one who, or that which, scrapes; <as>as, a
<ex>scraping</ex> noise; a <ex>scraping</ex> miser</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Scrap"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrappily</ent><br/
<hw>Scrap"pi*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scrappy
manner; in scraps.</def> <rj><au>Mary Cowden Clarke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrapple</ent><br/
<hw>Scrap"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>scrap</ets>.]</ety> <def>An article of food made by boiling
together bits or scraps of meat, usually pork, and flour or Indian
meal.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrappy</ent><br/
<hw>Scrap"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of scraps;
fragmentary; lacking unity or consistency; <as>as, a <ex>scrappy</ex>
lecture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A dreadfully <qex>scrappy</qex> dinner.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrat</ent><br/
<hw>Scrat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scratten</ets>. Cf. <er>Scratch</er>.]</ety> <def>To scratch.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrat</ent><br/
<hw>Scrat</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To rake; to search.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mir. for Mag.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrat</ent><br/
<hw>Scrat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>scritta</ets> an
hermaphrodite, Ir. <ets>scrut</ets> a scrub, a low, mean person, Gael.
<ets>sgrut</ets>, <ets>sgruit</ets>, an old, shriveled person.]</ety>
<def>An hermaphrodite.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Skinner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratch</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scratched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scratching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>cracchen</ets> (perhaps influenced by OE. <ets>scratten</ets> to
scratch); cf. OHG. <ets>chrazz<omac/n</ets>, G. <ets>kratzen</ets>, OD.
<ets>kratsen</ets>, <ets>kretsen</ets>, D. <ets>krassen</ets>, Sw.
<ets>kratsa</ets> to scrape, <ets>kratta</ets> to rake, to scratch, Dan.
<ets>kradse</ets> to scratch, to scrape, Icel. <ets>krota</ets> to
engrave. Cf. <er>Grate</er> to rub.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To rub and
tear or mark the surface of with something sharp or ragged; to scrape,
roughen, or wound slightly by drawing something pointed or rough across,
as the claws, the nails, a pin, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Small sand-colored stones, so hard as to <qex>scratch</qex>
glass.</q> <rj><qau>Grew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be mindful, when invention fails,<br/
To <qex>scratch</qex> your head, and bite your nails.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To write or draw hastily or awkwardly.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Scratch</xex> out a pamphlet.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cancel by drawing one or more lines through, as
the name of a candidate upon a ballot, or of a horse in a list; hence,
to erase; to efface; -- often with <xex>out</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To dig or excavate with the claws; <as>as, some
animals <ex>scratch</ex> holes, in which they burrow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To scratch a ticket</b></col>, <cd>to cancel one or more
names of candidates on a party ballot; to refuse to vote the party
ticket in its entirety.</cd> <mark>[U. S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratch</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To use the claws or
nails in tearing or in digging; to make scratches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dull, tame things, . . . that will neither bite nor
<qex>scratch</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Billiards)</fld> <def>To score, not by skillful
play but by some fortunate chance of the game.</def> <mark>[Cant, U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratch</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A break in the surface
of a thing made by scratching, or by rubbing with anything pointed or
rough; a slight wound, mark, furrow, or incision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The coarse file . . . makes deep <qex>scratches</qex> in the
work.</q> <rj><qau>Moxon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These nails with <qex>scratches</qex> deform my breast.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God forbid a shallow <qex>scratch</qex> should drive<br/
The prince of Wales from such a field as this.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Pugilistic Matches)</fld> <def>A line across the
prize ring; up to which boxers are brought when they join fight; hence,
test, trial, or proof of courage; <as>as, to bring to the
<ex>scratch</ex>; to come up to the <ex>scratch</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>Minute, but
tender and troublesome, excoriations, covered with scabs, upon the heels
of horses which have been used where it is very wet or muddy.</def>
<rj><au>Law (Farmer's Veter. Adviser).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A kind of wig covering only a portion of the
head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Billiards)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A shot which
scores by chance and not as intended by the player; a fluke.</def>
<mark>[Cant, U. S.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>a shot which results in a
penalty, such as dropping the cue ball in a pocket without hitting
another ball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>In various sports, the line from which the start is
made, except in the case of contestants receiving a distance
handicap.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scratch cradle</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Cratch
cradle</cref>, under <er>Cratch</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scratch
grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a climbing knotweed
(<spn>Polygonum sagittatum</spn>) with a square stem beset with fine
recurved prickles along the angles.</cd> -- <col><b>Scratch
wig</b></col>. <cd>Same as <er>Scratch</er>, 4, above.</cd>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj> -- <col><b>start from scratch</b></col>
<cd>to start (again) from the very beginning; also, to start without
resources.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratch</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made, done, or happening by chance;
arranged with little or no preparation; determined by circumstances;
haphazard; <as>as, a <ex>scratch</ex> team; a <ex>scratch</ex> crew for
a boat race; a <ex>scratch</ex> shot in billiards</as>.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scratch race</b></col>, <cd>one without restrictions
regarding the entrance of competitors; also, one for which the
competitors are chosen by lot.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratchback</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch"back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A toy which
imitates the sound of tearing cloth, -- used by drawing it across the
back of unsuspecting persons.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratchbrush</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch"brush`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stiff wire
brush for cleaning iron castings and other metal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratch coat</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch" coat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The first coat in plastering;
-- called also <cref>scratchwork</cref>. See
<er>Pricking-up</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratcher</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, scratches; specifically <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, any rasorial
bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratch hit</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch" hit</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>a base
hit which was weakly batted, barely allowing the batter to reach first
base safely.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratching</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch"ing</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With the action of
scratching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratch runner</ent><br/
<ent>Scratch player</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scratch player</hw>, <hw>Scratch runner</hw>, etc. }</mhw>
<def>One that starts from the scratch; hence, one of first-rate
ability.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratchweed</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Cleavers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratchwork</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Scratch
coat</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scratchy</ent><br/
<hw>Scratch"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Characterized by
scratches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scraw</ent><br/
<hw>Scraw</hw> <pr>(skr<add/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ir.
<ets>scrath</ets> a turf, <ets>sgraith</ets> a turf, green sod; akin to
Gael. <ets>sgrath</ets>, <ets>sgroth</ets>, the outer skin of anything,
a turf, a green sod.]</ety> <def>A turf.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrawl</ent><br/
<hw>Scrawl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Crawl</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrawl</ent><br/
<hw>Scrawl</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scrawled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scrawling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably corrupted from
<ets>scrabble</ets>.]</ety> <def>To draw or mark awkwardly and
irregularly; to write hastily and carelessly; to scratch; to scribble;
<as>as, to <ex>scrawl</ex> a letter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His name, <qex>scrawled</qex> by himself.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrawl</ent><br/
<hw>Scrawl</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To write unskillfully and
inelegantly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though with a golden pen you <qex>scrawl</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1292 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scrawl</ent><br/
<hw>Scrawl</hw> <pr>(skr<add/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Unskillful or
inelegant writing; that which is unskillfully or inelegantly
written.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The left hand will make such a <qex>scrawl</qex>, that it will not
be legible.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You bid me write no more than a <qex>scrawl</qex> to you.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrawler</ent><br/
<hw>Scrawl"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
scrawls; a hasty, awkward writer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrawny</ent><br/
<hw>Scraw"ny</hw> <pr>(skr<add/"n<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scrannel</er>.]</ety> <def>Meager; thin; rawboned; bony;
scranny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scray</ent><br/
<hw>Scray</hw> <pr>(skr<amac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. W.
<ets>ysgr<aum/en</ets>, <ets>ysgr<aum/ell</ets>, a sea swallow, Armor.
<ets>skrav</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tern; the sea
swallow.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>scraye</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screable</ent><br/
<hw>Scre"a*ble</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/"<adot/*b'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>screare</ets> to hawk, spit out.]</ety> <def>Capable of
being spit out.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screak</ent><br/
<hw>Screak</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/k)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Screaked</conjf>
<pr>(skr<emac/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Screaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Icel. <ets>skraekja</ets>
to screech. Cf. <er>Creak</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, <er>Screech</er>.]</ety>
<def>To utter suddenly a sharp, shrill sound; to screech; to creak, as a
door or wheel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screak</ent><br/
<hw>Screak</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A creaking; a screech; a
shriek.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Bull.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scream</ent><br/
<hw>Scream</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Screamed</conjf>
<pr>(skr<emac/md)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Screaming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skraema</ets> to
scare, terrify; akin to Sw. <ets>skr<aum/ma</ets>, Dan.
<ets>skraemme</ets>. Cf. <er>Screech</er>.]</ety> <def>To cry out with a
shrill voice; to utter a sudden, sharp outcry, or shrill, loud cry, as
in fright or extreme pain; to shriek; to screech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I heard the owl <qex>scream</qex> and the crickets cry.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>scream</qex> thyself as none e'er <qex>screamed</qex>
before.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scream</ent><br/
<hw>Scream</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sharp, shrill cry, uttered
suddenly, as in terror or in pain; a shriek; a screech.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Screams</xex> of horror.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screamer</ent><br/
<hw>Scream"er</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/m"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of three species of South American birds
constituting the family <fam>Anhimidae</fam>, and the suborder
<ord>Palamedeae</ord>. They have two spines on each wing, and the head
is either crested or horned. They are easily tamed, and then serve as
guardians for other poultry. The crested screamers, or chajas, belong to
the genus <gen>Chauna</gen>. The horned screamer, or kamichi, is
<spn>Palamedea cornuta</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something so remarkable as to provoke a scream, as
of joy.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An exclamation mark.</def> <mark>[Printer's
Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screaming</ent><br/
<hw>Scream"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Uttering screams;
shrieking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the nature of a scream; like a scream;
shrill; sharp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fearful matrons raise a <qex>screaming</qex> cry.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scree</ent><br/
<hw>Scree</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pebble; a
stone; also, a heap of stones or rocky d<eacute/bris.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screech</ent><br/
<hw>Screech</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/ch)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Screeched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Screeching</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Also formerly, <ets>scritch</ets>, OE. <ets>skriken</ets>,
<ets>skrichen</ets>, <ets>schriken</ets>, of Scand. origin; cf. Icel.
<ets>skr<ae/kja</ets> to shriek, to screech, <ets>skr<imac/kja</ets> to
titter, Sw. <ets>skrika</ets> to shriek, Dan. <ets>skrige</ets>; also
Gael. <ets>sgreach</ets>, <ets>sgreuch</ets>, W. <ets>ysgrechio</ets>,
Skr. <ets>kharj</ets> to creak. Cf. <er>Shriek</er>, <pos>v.</pos>,
<er>Scream</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>To utter a harsh, shrill cry;
to make a sharp outcry, as in terror or acute pain; to scream; to
shriek.</def> <ldquo/The screech owl, <xex>screeching</xex> loud.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screech</ent><br/
<hw>Screech</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A harsh, shrill cry, as of one in
acute pain or in fright; a shriek; a scream.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Screech bird</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Screech
thrush</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the fieldfare; -- so
called from its harsh cry before rain.</cd> -- <col><b>Screech
rain</b></col>.<cd></cd> -- <col><b>Screech hawk</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the European goatsucker; -- so called from its
note.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Screech owl</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A small American owl (<spn>Scops
asio</spn>), either gray or reddish in color</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The
European barn owl. The name is applied also to other
species.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screechers</ent><br/
<hw>Screech"ers</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The picarian birds, as distinguished from the singing
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screechy</ent><br/
<hw>Screech"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a screech;
shrill and harsh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screed</ent><br/
<hw>Screed</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E., a
shred, the border of a cap. See <er>Shred</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A strip of plaster of the thickness
proposed for the coat, applied to the wall at intervals of four or five
feet, as a guide.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A wooden straightedge used to
lay across the plaster screed, as a limit for the thickness of the
coat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fragment; a portion; a shred.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screed</ent><br/
<hw>Screed</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 1st <er>Screed</er>. For sense
2 cf. also Gael. <ets>sgread</ets> an outcry.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
breach or rent; a breaking forth into a loud, shrill sound; <as>as,
martial <ex>screeds</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An harangue; a long tirade on any subject.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The old carl gae them a <qex>screed</qex> of doctrine; ye might
have heard him a mile down the wind.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screen</ent><br/
<hw>Screen</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scren</ets>, OF. <ets>escrein</ets>, <ets>escran</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/cran</ets>, of uncertain origin; cf. G. <ets>schirm</ets> a
screen, OHG. <ets>scirm</ets>, <ets>scerm</ets> a protection, shield, or
G. <ets>schragen</ets> a trestle, a stack of wood, or G.
<ets>schranne</ets> a railing.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything that
separates or cuts off inconvenience, injury, or danger; that which
shelters or conceals from view; a shield or protection; <as>as, a fire
<ex>screen</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your leavy <qex>screens</qex> throw down.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some ambitious men seem as <qex>screens</qex> to princes in
matters of danger and envy.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A dwarf wall or partition carried
up to a certain height for separation and protection, as in a church, to
separate the aisle from the choir, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A surface, as that afforded by a curtain, sheet,
wall, etc., upon which an image, as a picture, is thrown by a magic
lantern, solar microscope, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A long, coarse riddle or sieve, sometimes a
revolving perforated cylinder, used to separate the coarser from the
finer parts, as of coal, sand, gravel, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>An erection of white canvas or
wood placed on the boundary opposite a batsman to enable him to see ball
better.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>a netting, usu. of metal, contained in a frame, used
mostly in windows or doors to allow in fresh air while excluding
insects.</def> -- <col><b>Screen door</b></col>, <def>a door of which
half or more is composed of a screen.</def> -- <col><b>Screen
window</b></col>, <def>a screen inside a frame, fitted for insertion
into a window frame.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The surface of an electronic device, as a
<partof>television set</partof> or <partof>computer monitor</partof>, on
which a visible image is formed. The screen is frequently the surface of
a cathode-ray tube containing phosphors excited by the electron beam,
but other methods for causing an image to appear on the screen are also
used, as in <partof>flat-panel displays</partof>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>The motion-picture industry; motion pictures.</def>
<ldquo/A star of stage and <ex>screen</ex>.<rdquo/<br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screen</ent><br/
<hw>Screen</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Screened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Screening</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To provide with a shelter or means of concealment; to
separate or cut off from inconvenience, injury, or danger; to shelter;
to protect; to protect by hiding; to conceal; <as>as, fruits
<ex>screened</ex> from cold winds by a forest or hill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They were encouraged and <qex>screened</qex> by some who were in
high commands.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pass, as coal, gravel, ashes, etc., through a
screen in order to separate the coarse from the fine, or the worthless
from the valuable; to sift.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>to examine a group of objects methodically, to
separate them into groups or to select one or more for some
purpose.</def> <specif>As:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To inspect the
qualifications of candidates for a job, to select one or more to be
hired.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Biochem., Med.)</fld> <def>to test a
large number of samples, in order to find those having specific
desirable properties; <as>as, to <ex>screen</ex> plant extracts for
anticancer agents</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screening</ent><br/
<hw>Screen"ing</hw> <pr>(skr<emac/n"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>the process of examining or testing objects methodically to find
those having desirable properties. See <er>screen{3}</er>.</def>
<note>In the pharmaceutical industry, pharmaceutical <ex>screening</ex>
involves testing a large number of samples of substances to find those
having desirable pharmacological activity; those samples which have the
property sought are called <it>active</it> or <it>positive</it> in the
screen. The substances tested may be pure compounds with known
structure, mixtures of pure compounds, or complex mixtures obtained by
extraction from living organisms. There are often additional sets of
test performed on <it>active</it> samples, called
<styp><col><b>counterscreening</b></col></styp> to eliminate those
samples that may also possess undesirable properties. In the case of
<ex>screening</ex> of mixtures from living organisms, a type of
<cref>counterscreening</cref> called <er>dereplication</er> is usually
performed, to determine if the active sample contains a known compound
which has previously been studied.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screenings</ent><br/
<hw>Screen"ings</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>The refuse
left after screening sand, coal, ashes, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screw</ent><br/
<hw>Screw</hw> <pr>(skr<udd/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scrue</ets>, OF. <ets>escroue</ets>, <ets>escroe</ets>, female
screw, F. <ets><eacute/crou</ets>, L. <ets>scrobis</ets> a ditch,
trench, in LL., the hole made by swine in rooting; cf. D.
<ets>schroef</ets> a screw, G. <ets>schraube</ets>, Icel.
<ets>skr<umac/fa</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A cylinder, or a
cylindrical perforation, having a continuous rib, called the
<xex>thread</xex>, winding round it spirally at a constant inclination,
so as to leave a continuous spiral groove between one turn and the next,
-- used chiefly for producing, when revolved, motion or pressure in the
direction of its axis, by the sliding of the threads of the cylinder in
the grooves between the threads of the perforation adapted to it, the
former being distinguished as the <xex>external</xex>, or <xex>male
screw</xex>, or, more usually the <xex>screw</xex>; the latter as the
<xex>internal</xex>, or <xex>female screw</xex>, or, more usually, the
<xex>nut</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The screw, as a mechanical power, is a modification of
the inclined plane, and may be regarded as a right-angled triangle
wrapped round a cylinder, the hypotenuse of the marking the spiral
thread of the screw, its base equaling the circumference of the
cylinder, and its height the <xex>pitch</xex> of the thread.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, a kind of nail with a spiral thread
and a head with a nick to receive the end of the screw-driver. Screws
are much used to hold together pieces of wood or to fasten something; --
called also <altname>wood screws</altname>, and <altname>screw
nails</altname>. See also <cref>Screw bolt</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything shaped or acting like a screw; esp., a form
of wheel for propelling steam vessels. It is placed at the stern, and
furnished with blades having helicoidal surfaces to act against the
water in the manner of a screw. See <cref>Screw propeller</cref>,
below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A steam vesel propelled by a screw instead of
wheels; a screw steamer; a propeller.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An extortioner; a sharp bargainer; a skinflint; a
niggard.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An instructor who examines with great or unnecessary
severity; also, a searching or strict examination of a student by an
instructor.</def> <mark>[Cant, American Colleges]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A small packet of tobacco.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Mayhew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>An unsound or worn-out horse, useful as a hack, and
commonly of good appearance.</def> <rj><au>Ld. Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>A straight line in space with
which a definite linear magnitude termed the <xex>pitch</xex> is
associated (cf. 5th <er>Pitch</er>, 10 <sd>(b)</sd>). It is used to
express the displacement of a rigid body, which may always be made to
consist of a rotation about an axis combined with a translation parallel
to that axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An amphipod crustacean; <as>as,
the skeleton <ex>screw</ex> (<gen>Caprella</gen>)</as>. See <cref>Sand
screw</cref>, under <er>Sand</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Archimedes screw</b></col>, <col><b>Compound
screw</b></col>, <col><b>Foot screw</b></col>, etc.</mcol> <cd>See under
<er>Archimedes</er>, <er>Compound</er>, <er>Foot</er>, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>A screw loose</b></col>, <cd>something out of order, so that
work is not done smoothly; <as>as, there is <ex>a screw loose</ex>
somewhere</as>.</cd> <au>H. Martineau.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>Endless
screw</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>perpetual screw</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>a screw used to give motion to a toothed wheel by the action of its
threads between the teeth of the wheel; -- called also a
<altname>worm</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Lag screw</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Lag</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Micrometer screw</b></col>, <cd>a
screw with fine threads, used for the measurement of very small
spaces.</cd> -- <col><b>Right and left screw</b></col>, <cd>a screw
having threads upon the opposite ends which wind in opposite
directions.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw alley</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Shaft
alley</er>, under <er>Shaft</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw bean</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The curious spirally coiled pod of a
leguminous tree (<spn>Prosopis pubescens</spn>) growing from Texas to
California. It is used for fodder, and ground into meal by the
Indians.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The tree itself. Its heavy hard wood is
used for fuel, for fencing, and for railroad ties.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw
bolt</b></col>, <cd>a bolt having a screw thread on its shank, in
distinction from a <contr>key bolt</contr>. See 1st <er>Bolt</er>,
3.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw box</b></col>, <cd>a device, resembling a die,
for cutting the thread on a wooden screw.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw
dock</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Dock</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw
engine</b></col>, <cd>a marine engine for driving a screw
propeller.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw gear</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Spiral
gear</cref>, under <er>Spiral</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw
jack</b></col>. <cd>Same as <er>Jackscrew</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw
key</b></col>, <cd>a wrench for turning a screw or nut; a spanner
wrench.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw machine</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>One of
a series of machines employed in the manufacture of wood screws.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A machine tool resembling a lathe, having a number of
cutting tools that can be caused to act on the work successively, for
making screws and other turned pieces from metal rods.</cd> --
<col><b>Screw pine</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any plant of the
endogenous genus <gen>Pandanus</gen>, of which there are about fifty
species, natives of tropical lands from Africa to Polynesia; -- named
from the spiral arrangement of the pineapple-like leaves.</cd> --
<col><b>Screw plate</b></col>, <cd>a device for cutting threads on small
screws, consisting of a thin steel plate having a series of perforations
with internal screws forming dies.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw
press</b></col>, <cd>a press in which pressure is exerted by means of a
screw.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw propeller</b></col>, <cd>a screw or spiral
bladed wheel, used in the propulsion of steam vessels; also, a steam
vessel propelled by a screw.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw shell</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a long, slender, spiral gastropod shell,
especially of the genus Turritella and allied genera. See
<er>Turritella</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw steamer</b></col>, <cd>a
steamship propelled by a screw.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw thread</b></col>,
<cd>the spiral rib which forms a screw.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw
stone</b></col> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>, <cd>the fossil stem of an
encrinite.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>any plant of the genus <gen>Helicteres</gen>, consisting of about
thirty species of tropical shrubs, with simple leaves and spirally
twisted, five-celled capsules; -- also called
<altname>twisted-horn</altname>, and <altname>twisty</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Screw valve</b></col>, <cd>a stop valve which is opened or
closed by a screw.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw worm</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the larva of an American fly (<spn>Compsomyia
macellaria</spn>), allied to the blowflies, which sometimes deposits its
eggs in the nostrils, or about wounds, in man and other animals, with
fatal results.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw wrench</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>A wrench for turning a screw.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A wrench with an
adjustable jaw that is moved by a screw.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To put
the screws on</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To put the screw
on</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to use pressure upon, as for the purpose of
extortion; to coerce.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To put under the
screw</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To put under the
screws</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to subject to pressure; to force.</cd> --
<col><b>Wood screw</b></col>, <cd>a metal screw with a sharp thread of
coarse pitch, adapted to holding fast in wood.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screw</ent><br/
<hw>Screw</hw> <pr>(skr<udd/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Screwed</conjf> <pr>(skr<udd/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Screwing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
turn, as a screw; to apply a screw to; to press, fasten, or make firm,
by means of a screw or screws; <as>as, to <ex>screw</ex> a lock on a
door; to <ex>screw</ex> a press</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To force; to squeeze; to press, as by
screws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But <qex>screw</qex> your courage to the sticking place,<br/
And we'll not fail.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence: To practice extortion upon; to oppress by
unreasonable or extortionate exactions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our country landlords, by unmeasurable <qex>screwing</qex> and
racking their tenants, have already reduced the miserable people to a
worse condition than the peasants in France.</q>
<rj><qau>swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To twist; to distort; <as>as, to <ex>screw</ex> his
visage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He screwed his face into a hardened smile.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To examine rigidly, as a student; to subject to a
severe examination.</def> <mark>[Cant, American Colleges]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To screw out</b></col>, <cd>to press out; to extort.</cd>
-- <col><b>To screw up</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>to force; to bring by
violent pressure.</cd> <au>Howell.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>to damage by
unskillful effort; to bungle; to botch; to mess up; <as>as, he
<ex>screwed up</ex> the contract negotiations, and we lost the
deal</as>.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <mark>[intrans.]</mark> <cd>to fail by
unskillful effort, usually causing unpleasant consequences.</cd> --
<col><b>To screw in</b></col>, <cd>to force in by turning or
twisting.</cd></cs> <col><b>Screw around</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>to
act aimlessly or unproductively.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>to commit
adultery; to be sexually promiscuous.</cd> -- <col><b>Screw around
with</b></col>, <cd>to operate or make changes on (a machine or device)
without expert knowledge; to fiddle with.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> .
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screw</ent><br/
<hw>Screw</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To use violent means
in making exactions; to be oppressive or exacting.</def>
<rj><au>Howitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To turn one's self uneasily with a twisting motion;
<as>as, he <ex>screws</ex> about in his chair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>screwball</ent><br/
<hw>screw"ball</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An eccentric or
crazy person; an oddball.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>A baseball pitch that curves
in the direction opposite to that of a curve ball.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>screwball</ent><br/
<hw>screw"ball</hw>, <pos>adj.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Eccentric; zany;
crazy; <as>as, <ex>screwball</ex> antics; a <ex>screwball</ex>
comedy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Ridiculously unsound; improbable or doomed to
failure; -- of plans or ideas; <as>as, a <ex>screwball</ex> plan to
raise cocoanuts in Alaska</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screw-cutting</ent><br/
<hw>Screw"-cut`ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Adapted for
forming a screw by cutting; <as>as, a <ex>screw-cutting</ex>
lathe</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screw-driver</ent><br/
<ent>Screwdriver</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Screw"driv`er</hw>, <hw>Screw"-driv`er</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A tool for turning screws so as to drive them into
their place. It has a thin end which enters the nick in the head of the
screw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screwer</ent><br/
<hw>Screw"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, screws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Screwing</ent><br/
<hw>Screw"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Screw</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Screwing machine</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Screw
machine</cref>, under <er>Screw</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribable</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scribe</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being written, or of being
written upon.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribatious</ent><br/
<hw>Scri*ba"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scribe</er>.]</ety> <def>Skillful in, or fond of, writing.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribbet</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"bet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A painter's
pencil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribble</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scrabble</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Woolen Manuf.)</fld> <def>To card
coarsely; to run through the scribbling machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribble</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scribbled</conjf> <pr>(-b'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scribbling</conjf> <pr>(-bl<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From
<er>Scribe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To write hastily or carelessly,
without regard to correctness or elegance; <as>as, to <ex>scribble</ex>
a letter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fill or cover with careless or worthless
writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribble</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"ble</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To write without care,
elegance, or value; to scrawl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If Maevius <qex>scribble</qex> in Apollo's spite.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribble</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Hasty or careless writing; a
writing of little value; a scrawl; <as>as, a hasty
<ex>scribble</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Neither did I but vacant seasons spend<br/
In this my <qex>scribble</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribblement</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"ble*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
scribble.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Foster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribbler</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"bler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who scribbles;
a petty author; a writer of no reputation; a literary hack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scribbler</qex>, pinched with hunger, writes to dine.</q>
<rj><qau>Granville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribbler</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"bler</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scribbling machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribbling</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"bling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 1st
<er>Scribble</er>.]</ety> <def>The act or process of carding
coarsely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scribbling machine</b></col>, <cd>the machine used for
the first carding of wool or other fiber; -- called also
<altname>scribbler</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribbling</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"bling</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Writing hastily or
poorly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye newspaper witlings! ye pert <qex>scribbling</qex> folks!</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribbling</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"bling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of writing hastily or
idly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribblingly</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"bling*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scribbling
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribe</ent><br/
<hw>Scribe</hw> <pr>(skr<imac/b)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scriba</ets>, fr. <ets>scribere</ets> to write; cf. Gr.
<grk>ska`rifos</grk> a splinter, pencil, style (for writing), E.
<ets>scarify</ets>. Cf. <er>Ascribe</er>, <er>Describe</er>,
<er>Script</er>, <er>Scrivener</er>, <er>Scrutoire</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who writes; a draughtsman; a writer for another;
especially, an offical or public writer; an amanuensis or secretary; a
notary; a copyist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Jewish Hist.)</fld> <def>A writer and doctor of the
law; one skilled in the law and traditions; one who read and explained
the law to the people.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1293 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scribe</ent><br/
<hw>Scribe</hw> <pr>(skr<imac/b)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scribed</conjf>
<pr>(skr<imac/bd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scribing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To write, engrave,
or mark upon; to inscribe.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>To cut (anything) in such a way
as to fit closely to a somewhat irregular surface, as a baseboard to a
floor which is out of level, a board to the curves of a molding, or the
like; -- so called because the workman marks, or <xex>scribes</xex>,
with the compasses the line that he afterwards cuts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To score or mark with compasses or a scribing
iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scribing iron</b></col>, <cd>an iron-pointed instrument
for scribing, or marking, casks and logs.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribe</ent><br/
<hw>Scribe</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a mark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With the separated points of a pair of spring dividers
<qex>scribe</qex> around the edge of the templet.</q> <rj><qau>A. M.
Mayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scriber</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sharp-pointed tool,
used by joiners for drawing lines on stuff; a marking awl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scribism</ent><br/
<hw>Scrib"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The character and
opinions of a Jewish scribe in the time of Christ.</def> <rj><au>F. W.
Robertson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrid</ent><br/
<hw>Scrid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A screed; a shred; a
fragment.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scriggle</ent><br/
<hw>Scrig"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To wriggle.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrim</ent><br/
<hw>Scrim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of
light cotton or linen fabric, often woven in openwork patterns, -- used
for curtains, etc,; -- called also <altname>India
scrim</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Thin canvas glued on the inside of
panels to prevent shrinking, checking, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimer</ent><br/
<hw>Scri"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>escrimeur</ets>. See <er>Skirmish</er>.]</ety> <def>A fencing
master.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimmage</ent><br/
<hw>Scrim"mage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption
of <ets>skirmish</ets>. <ldquo/Sore <xex>scrymmishe</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Ld. Berners.</au>]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>scrummage</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Formerly, a skirmish;
now, a general row or confused fight or struggle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Football)</fld> <def>The struggle in the rush lines
after the ball is put in play.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimp</ent><br/
<hw>Scrimp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scrimped</conjf> <pr>(?; 215)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scrimping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>skrumpe</ets>, G. <ets>schrumpfen</ets>, D. <ets>krimpen</ets>. Cf.
<er>Shrimp</er>, <er>Shrink</er>.]</ety> <def>To make too small or
short; to limit or straiten; to put on short allowance; to scant; to
contract; to shorten; <as>as, to <ex>scrimp</ex> the pattern of a
coat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- To economize. Scrimp and save. Economize and save the money not
spent. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimp</ent><br/
<hw>Scrimp</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Short; scanty; curtailed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimp</ent><br/
<hw>Scrimp</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pinching miser; a niggard.</def>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimping</ent><br/
<hw>Scrimp"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Scrimp</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scrimping bar</b></col>, <cd>a device used in connection
with a calico printing machine for stretching the fabric breadthwise so
that it may be smooth for printing.</cd>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimpingly</ent><br/
<hw>Scrimp"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scrimping
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimpness</ent><br/
<hw>Scrimp"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
scrimp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimption</ent><br/
<hw>Scrimp"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small portion; a
pittance; a little bit.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimshaw</ent><br/
<hw>Scrim"shaw`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To ornament, as
shells, ivory, etc., by engraving, and (usually) rubbing pigments into
the incised lines.</def> <mark>[Sailor's cant. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrimshaw</ent><br/
<hw>Scrim"shaw`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shell, a whale's tooth, or
the like, that is scrimshawed.</def> <mark>[Sailor's cant,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrine</ent><br/
<hw>Scrine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scrinium</ets>
a case for books, letters, etc.: cf. OF. <ets>escrin</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/crin</ets>. See <er>Shrine</er>.]</ety> <def>A chest,
bookcase, or other place, where writings or curiosities are deposited; a
shrine.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But laid them up in immortal <qex>scrine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scringe</ent><br/
<hw>Scringe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scringed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scringing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Cringe</er>.]</ety> <def>To cringe.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrip</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>scrippe</ets>,
probably of Scand. origin; cf. Icel. & OSw. <ets>skreppa</ets>, and also
LL. <ets>scrippum</ets>, OF. <ets>esquerpe</ets>, <ets>escrepe</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/charpe</ets> scarf. Cf. <er>Scrap</er>, <er>Scarf</er> a
piece of dress.]</ety> <def>A small bag; a wallet; a satchel.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And in requital ope his leathern <qex>scrip</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrip</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>script</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A small writing, certificate, or schedule; a piece of
paper containing a writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Call them generally, man by man, according to the
<qex>scrip</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bills of exchange can not pay our debts abroad, till
<qex>scrips</qex> of paper can be made current coin.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A preliminary certificate of a subscription to the
capital of a bank, railroad, or other company, or for a share of other
joint property, or a loan, stating the amount of the subscription and
the date of the payment of the installments; <as>as, insurance
<ex>scrip</ex>, consol <ex>scrip</ex>, etc.</as> When all the
installments are paid, the scrip is exchanged for a bond share
certificate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Paper fractional currency.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrippage</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"page</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The contents of
a scrip, or wallet.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Script</ent><br/
<hw>Script</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>scrit</ets>,
L. <ets>scriptum</ets> something written, fr. <ets>scribere</ets>,
<ets>scriptum</ets> to write: cf. OF. <ets>escript</ets>,
<ets>escrit</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/crit</ets>. See <er>Scribe</er>, and
cf. <er>Scrip</er> a writing.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A writing; a
written document.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>Type made in imitation of
handwriting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An original instrument or
document.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Written characters; style of writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 5. The written document containing the dialogue and action for a
drama; the text of a stage play, movie, or other performance.
Especially, the final form used for the performance itself. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scriptorium</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip*to"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scriptoria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL. See
<er>Scriptory</er>.]</ety> <def>In an abbey or monastery, the room set
apart for writing or copying manuscripts; in general, a room devoted to
writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Writing rooms, or <qex>scriptoria</qex>, where the chief works of
Latin literature . . . were copied and illuminated.</q> <rj><qau>J. R.
Green.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scriptory</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scriptorius</ets>, fr. <ets>scribere</ets>, <ets>scriptum</ets> to
write.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to writing; expressed in writing;
used in writing; <as>as, <ex>scriptory</ex> wills; a <ex>scriptory</ex>
reed</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scriptural</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"tur*al</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Contained in
the Scriptures; according to the Scriptures, or sacred oracles;
biblical; <as>as, a <ex>scriptural</ex> doctrine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scripturalism</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"tur*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being scriptural; literal adherence to the
Scriptures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scripturalist</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"tur*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who adheres literally
to the Scriptures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scripturally</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"tur*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scriptural
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scripturalness</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"tur*al*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
scriptural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scripture</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scriptura</ets>, fr. <ets>scribere</ets>, <ets>scriptum</ets>, to
write: cf. OF. <ets>escripture</ets>, <ets>escriture</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/criture</ets>. See <er>Scribe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Anything written; a writing; a document; an inscription.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have put it in <qex>scripture</qex> and in remembrance.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then the Lord of Manny read the <qex>scripture</qex> on the tomb,
the which was in Latin.</q> <rj><qau>Ld. Berners.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The books of the Old and the New Testament, or of
either of them; the Bible; -- used by way of eminence or distinction,
and chiefly in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is not any action a man ought to do, or to forbear, but the
<qex>Scripture</qex> will give him a clear precept or prohibition for
it.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Compared with the knowledge which the <qex>Scriptures</qex>
contain, every other subject of human inquiry is vanity.</q>
<rj><qau>Buckminster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A passage from the Bible; a text.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The devil can cite <qex>Scripture</qex> for his purpose.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hanging by the twined thread of one doubtful
<qex>Scripture</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scripturian</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip*tu"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
Scripturist.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scripturist</ent><br/
<hw>Scrip"tur*ist</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is
strongly attached to, or versed in, the Scriptures, or who endeavors to
regulate his life by them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Puritan was a <qex>Scripturist</qex>, -- a
<qex>Scripturist</qex> with all his heart, if as yet with imperfect
intelligence . . . he cherished the scheme of looking to the Word of God
as his sole and universal directory.</q>
<rj><qau>Palfrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrit</ent><br/
<hw>Scrit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Script</er>.]</ety> <def>Writing; document; scroll.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Of every <xex>scrit</xex> and bond.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scritch</ent><br/
<hw>Scritch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A screech.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Perhaps it is the owlet's <qex>scritch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrivener</ent><br/
<hw>Scrive"ner</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From older
<ets>scrivein</ets>, OF. <ets>escrivain</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/crivain</ets>, LL. <ets>scribanus</ets>, from L.
<ets>scribere</ets> to write. See <er>Scribe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A professional writer; one whose occupation is to draw contracts or
prepare writings.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The writer better <qex>scrivener</qex> than clerk.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One whose business is to place money at interest; a
broker.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A writing master.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scrivener's palsy</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Writer's
cramp</cref>, under <er>Writer</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrobicula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scro*bic"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Scrobiculae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Scrobiculate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
smooth areas surrounding the tubercles of a sea urchin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrobicular</ent><br/
<hw>Scro*bic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or surrounding, scrobiculae; <as>as,
<ex>scrobicular</ex> tubercles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrobiculated</ent><br/
<ent>Scrobiculate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scro*bic"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scro*bic"u*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scrobiculus</ets>,
dim. of <ets>scrobis</ets> a ditch or trench.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having numerous small, shallow depressions or hollows;
pitted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrode</ent><br/
<ent>Scrod</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scrod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scrode</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A young codfish, especially when cut open on
the back and dressed.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>escrod</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scroddled ware</ent><br/
<hw>Scrod"dled ware`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Mottled pottery made from
scraps of differently colored clays.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrofula</ent><br/
<hw>Scrof"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scrofulae</ets>, fr. <ets>scrofa</ets> a breeding sow, because
swine were supposed to be subject to such a complaint, or by a fanciful
comparison of the glandular swellings to little pigs; perhaps akin to
Gr. <?/ an old sow: cf. F. <ets>scrofules</ets>. Cf.
<er>Scroyle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A constitutional
disease, generally hereditary, especially manifested by chronic
enlargement and cheesy degeneration of the lymphatic glands,
particularly those of the neck, and marked by a tendency to the
development of chronic intractable inflammations of the skin, mucous
membrane, bones, joints, and other parts, and by a diminution in the
power of resistance to disease or injury and the capacity for recovery.
Scrofula is now generally held to be tuberculous in character, and may
develop into general or local tuberculosis (consumption).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrofulide</ent><br/
<hw>Scrof"u*lide</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Any affection of the skin dependent on
scrofula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrofulous</ent><br/
<hw>Scrof"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scrofuleux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pertaining to scrofula,
or partaking of its nature; <as>as, <ex>scrofulous</ex> tumors; a
<ex>scrofulous</ex> habit of body</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Diseased or affected with scrofula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Scrofulous</qex> persons can never be duly nourished.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Scrof"u*lous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Scrof"u*lous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrog</ent><br/
<hw>Scrog</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Scrag</er>, or
Gael. <ets>sgrogag</ets> anything shriveled, from <ets>sgrog</ets> to
compress, shrivel.]</ety> <def>A stunted shrub, bush, or branch.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scroggy</ent><br/
<hw>Scrog"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in scrog;
also, twisted; stunted.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scroll</ent><br/
<hw>Scroll</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A dim. of OE.
<ets>scroue</ets>, <ets>scrowe</ets> (whence E. <ets>escrow</ets>), OF.
<ets>escroe</ets>, <ets>escroue</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/crou</ets> entry
in the jail book, LL. <ets>scroa</ets> scroll, probably of Teutonic
origin; cf. OD. <ets>schroode</ets> a strip, shred, slip of paper, akin
to E. <ets>shred</ets>. Cf. <er>Shred</er>, <er>Escrow</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A roll of paper or parchment; a writing formed into a
roll; a schedule; a list.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The heavens shall be rolled together as a <qex>scroll</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xxxiv. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here is the <qex>scroll</qex> of every man's name.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An ornament formed of undulations
giving off spirals or sprays, usually suggestive of plant form. Roman
architectural ornament is largely of some scroll pattern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A mark or flourish added to a person's signature,
intended to represent a seal, and in some States allowed as a substitute
for a seal.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>Same as <cref>Skew
surface</cref>. See under <er>Skew</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Linen scroll</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Linen</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scroll chuck</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>an adjustable chuck, applicable to a lathe
spindle, for centering and holding work, in which the jaws are adjusted
and tightened simultaneously by turning a disk having in its face a
spiral groove which is entered by teeth on the backs of the jaws.</cd>
-- <col><b>Scroll saw</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Saw</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrolled</ent><br/
<hw>Scrolled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Formed like a scroll;
contained in a scroll; adorned with scrolls; <as>as, <ex>scrolled</ex>
work</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrophularia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scroph`u*la"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. So
called because it was reputed to be a remedy for
<ets>scrofula</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of coarse
herbs having small flowers in panicled cymes; figwort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrophulariaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Scroph`u*la`ri*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a very large natural order of
gamopetalous plants (<ord>Scrophulariaceae</ord>, or
<ord>Scrophularineae</ord>), usually having irregular didynamous flowers
and a two-celled pod. The order includes the mullein, foxglove,
snapdragon, figwort, painted cup, yellow rattle, and some exotic trees,
as the <xex>Paulownia</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrotal</ent><br/
<hw>Scro"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the scrotum; <as>as, <ex>scrotal</ex>
hernia</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrotiform</ent><br/
<hw>Scro"ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scrotum</ets> scrotum + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Purse-shaped;
pouch-shaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrotocele</ent><br/
<hw>Scro"to*cele</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Scrotum</ets> + Gr. <grk>kh`lh</grk> a tumor: cf. F.
<ets>scrotoc<egrave/le</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A rupture or
hernia in the scrotum; scrotal hernia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrotum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scro"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The bag or pouch which contains the testicles;
the cod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrouge</ent><br/
<hw>Scrouge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>To crowd; to squeeze.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrow</ent><br/
<hw>Scrow</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Escrow</er>, <er>Scroll</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
scroll.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Palsgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A clipping from skins; a currier's
cuttings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scroyle</ent><br/
<hw>Scroyle</hw> <pr>(skroil)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>escrouselle</ets> a kind of vermin, <ets>escrouelles</ets>, pl.,
scrofula, F. <ets><eacute/crouelles</ets>, fr. (assumed) LL.
<ets>scrofellae</ets> for L. <ets>scrofulae</ets>. See
<er>Scrofula</er>, and cf. <er>Cruels</er>.]</ety> <def>A mean fellow; a
wretch.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrub</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scrubbed</conjf> <pr>(skr<ucr/bd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Scrubbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>scrobben</ets>, probably of Dutch or Scand. origin; cf. Dan.
<ets>skrubbe</ets>, Sw. <ets>skrubba</ets>, D. <ets>schrobben</ets>, LG.
<ets>schrubben</ets>.]</ety> <def>To rub hard; to wash with rubbing;
usually, to rub with a wet brush, or with something coarse or rough, for
the purpose of cleaning or brightening; <as>as, to <ex>scrub</ex> a
floor, a doorplate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrub</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To rub
anything hard, especially with a wet brush; to scour; hence, to be
diligent and penurious; <as>as, to <ex>scrub</ex> hard for a
living</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrub</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who labors hard and lives meanly; a mean fellow.</def> <ldquo/A sorry
<xex>scrub</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bunyan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We should go there in as proper a manner as possible; nor
altogether like the <qex>scrubs</qex> about us.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something small and mean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A worn-out brush.</def>
<rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A thicket or jungle, often specified by the name of
the prevailing plant; <as>as, oak <ex>scrub</ex>, palmetto
<ex>scrub</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Stock Breeding)</fld> <def>One of the common live
stock of a region of no particular breed or not of pure breed, esp. when
inferior in size, etc.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Vegetation of inferior quality, though sometimes
thick and impenetrable, growing in poor soil or in sand; also, brush; --
called also <altname>scrub brush</altname>. See <er>Brush</er>,
above.</def> <mark>[Australia & South Africa]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Forestry)</fld> <def>A low, straggling tree of
inferior quality.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scrub bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an
Australian passerine bird of the family <fam>Atrichornithidae</fam>, as
<spn>Atrichia clamosa</spn>; -- called also <altname>brush
bird</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scrub oak</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the popular name of several dwarfish species of oak. The scrub oak
of New England and the Middle States is <spn>Quercus ilicifolia</spn>, a
scraggy shrub; that of the Southern States is a small tree (<spn>Quercus
Catesbaei</spn>); that of the Rocky Mountain region is <spn>Quercus
undulata</spn>, var. <varn>Gambelii</varn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Scrub
robin</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an Australian singing bird of
the genus <gen>Drymodes</gen>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrub</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Mean; dirty;
contemptible; scrubby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How solitary, how <qex>scrub</qex>, does this town look!</q>
<rj><qau>Walpole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No little <qex>scrub</qex> joint shall come on my board.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scrub game</b></col>, <cd>a game, as of ball, by
unpracticed players.</cd> -- <col><b>Scrub race</b></col>, <cd>a race
between scrubs, or between untrained animals or
contestants.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrubbed</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub"bed</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b"b<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Dwarfed or stunted; scrubby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrubber</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub"ber</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b"b<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, scrubs; esp., a brush or
machine used in scrubbing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gas Manuf.)</fld> <def>A gas washer. See under
<er>Gas</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Manufacturing)</fld> <def>a device for removing
pollutants from a gas stream, especially for removing sulfur oxides from
processes burning coal or oil.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrubber</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub"ber</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b"b<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>a stunted or emaciated steer.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person who lives in the bush.</def>
<mark>[Australian]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A domesticated animal which has escaped and lives
wild in the bush.</def> <mark>[Australian]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrubboard</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub"board`</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b"b<omac/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A baseboard; a mopboard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrubby</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub"by</hw> <pr>(skr<ucr/b"b<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Scrubbier</adjf>
<pr>(skr<ucr/b"b<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Scrubbiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Of the nature of scrub; small
and mean; stunted in growth; <as>as, a <ex>scrubby</ex> cur</as>.</def>
<ldquo/Dense, <xex>scrubby</xex> woods.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Duke of
Argyll.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrubstone</ent><br/
<hw>Scrub"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A species of
calciferous sandstone.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scruff</ent><br/
<hw>Scruff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Scurf</er>.]</ety> <def>Scurf.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scruff</ent><br/
<hw>Scruff</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Scuff</er>.]</ety> <def>The
nape of the neck; the loose outside skin, as of the back of the
neck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrummage</ent><br/
<hw>Scrum"mage</hw> <pr>(?; 43)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scrimmage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrumptious</ent><br/
<hw>Scrump"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nice; particular;
fastidious; excellent; fine.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrunch</ent><br/
<hw>Scrunch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scranch</er>, <er>Crunch</er>.]</ety> <def>To scranch; to
crunch.</def> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scruple</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scrupulus</ets> a small sharp or pointed stone, the twenty-fourth
part of an ounce, a scruple, uneasiness, doubt, dim. of
<ets>scrupus</ets> a rough or sharp stone, anxiety, uneasiness; perh.
akin to Gr. <?/ the chippings of stone, <?/ a razor, Skr.
<ets>kshura</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scrupule</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
weight of twenty grains; the third part of a dram.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a very small quantity; a particle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will not bate thee a <qex>scruple</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hesitation as to action from the difficulty of
determining what is right or expedient; unwillingness, doubt, or
hesitation proceeding from motives of conscience.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was made miserable by the conflict between his tastes and his
<qex>scruples</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To make scruple</b></col>, <cd>to hesitate from
conscientious motives; to scruple.</cd> <qau>Locke.</qau></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scruple</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ple</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scrupled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scrupling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To be reluctant or
to hesitate, as regards an action, on account of considerations of
conscience or expedience.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are often over-precise, scrupling to say or do those things
which lawfully we may.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men <qex>scruple</qex> at the lawfulness of a set form of divine
worship.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scruple</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ple</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To regard with
suspicion; to hesitate at; to question.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Others long before them . . . <qex>scrupled</qex> more the books
of heretics than of gentiles.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To excite scruples in; to cause to scruple.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Letters which did still <qex>scruple</qex> many of them.</q>
<rj><qau>E. Symmons.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrupler</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"pler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
scruples.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrupulist</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"pu*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scrupler.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrupulize</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"pu*lize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To perplex
with scruples; to regard with scruples.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Montagu.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1294 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scrupulosity</ent><br/
<hw>Scru`pu*los"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(skr<udd/`p<usl/*l<ocr/s"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scrupulositas</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
scrupulous; doubt; doubtfulness respecting decision or action; caution
or tenderness from the fear of doing wrong or offending; nice regard to
exactness and propriety; precision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The first sacrilege is looked on with horror; but when they have
made the breach, their <qex>scrupulosity</qex> soon retires.</q>
<rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Careful, even to <qex>scrupulosity</qex>, . . . to keep their
Sabbath.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrupulous</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"pu*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scrupulosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scrupuleux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Full of scruples; inclined to scruple; nicely doubtful; hesitating
to determine or to act, from a fear of offending or of doing
wrong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Abusing their liberty, to the offense of their weak brethren which
were <qex>scrupulous</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Careful; cautious; exact; nice; <as>as,
<ex>scrupulous</ex> abstinence from labor; <ex>scrupulous</ex>
performance of duties</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Given to making objections; captious.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Equality of two domestic powers<br/
Breed <qex>scrupulous</qex> faction.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Liable to be doubted; doubtful; nice.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The justice of that cause ought to be evident; not obscure, not
<qex>scrupulous</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Cautious; careful; conscientious;
hesitating.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Scru"pu*lous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Scru"pu*lous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutable</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ta*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Discoverable by
scrutiny, inquiry, or critical examination.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutation</ent><br/
<hw>Scru*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scrutatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Search; scrutiny.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutator</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scru*ta"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who scrutinizes; a close examiner or inquirer.</def>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutin de liste</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scru`tin" de liste"</hw> <pr>(skr<usdot/`t<acr/N" d<etil/
l<emac/st)</pr>. <ety>[F., voting by list.]</ety> <def>Voting for a
group of candidates for the same kind of office on one ticket or ballot,
containing a list of them; -- the method, used in France, as from June,
1885, to Feb., 1889, in elections for the Chamber of Deputies, each
elector voting for the candidates for the whole department in which he
lived, as disting. from <col><b>scrutin d'arrondissement</b></col>
<pr>(d<adot/`r<ocir/N`d<emac/s`m<aum/N")</pr>, or voting by each elector
for the candidate or candidates for his own arrondissement
only.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutineer</ent><br/
<hw>Scru`ti*neer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scrutinizer;
specifically, an examiner of votes, as at an election.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutinize</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ti*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scrutinized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scrutinizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From
<er>Scrutiny</er>.]</ety> <def>To examine closely; to inspect or observe
with critical attention; to regard narrowly; <as>as, to
<ex>scrutinize</ex> the measures of administration; to
<ex>scrutinize</ex> the conduct or motives of
individuals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whose votes they were obliged to <qex>scrutinize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ayliffe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those pronounced him youngest who <qex>scrutinized</qex> his face
the closest.</q> <rj><qau>G. W. Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutinize</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ti*nize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make scrutiny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutinizer</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ti*ni`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
scrutinizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutinous</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ti*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Closely
examining, or inquiring; careful; strict.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Scru"ti*nous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutiny</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ti*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scrutinium</ets>, fr. <ets>scrutari</ets> to search carefully,
originally, to search even to the rags, fr. <ets>scruta</ets> trash,
trumpery; perhaps akin to E. <ets>shred</ets>: cf. AS.
<ets>scrudnian</ets> to make scrutiny.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Close
examination; minute inspection; critical observation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They that have designed exactness and deep <qex>scrutiny</qex>
have taken some one part of nature.</q> <rj><qau>Sir M.
Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thenceforth I thought thee worth my nearer view<br/
And narrower <qex>scrutiny</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anc. Church)</fld> <def>An examination of
catechumens, in the last week of Lent, who were to receive baptism on
Easter Day.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Canon Law)</fld> <def>A ticket, or little paper
billet, on which a vote is written.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Parliamentary Practice)</fld> <def>An examination
by a committee of the votes given at an election, for the purpose of
correcting the poll.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutiny</ent><br/
<hw>Scru"ti*ny</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To scrutinize.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scrutoire</ent><br/
<hw>Scru*toire"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escritoire</ets>. See <er>Escritoire</er>.]</ety> <def>A
escritoire; a writing desk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scruze</ent><br/
<hw>Scruze</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Excruciate</er>.]</ety> <def>To squeeze, compress, crush, or
bruise.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Low]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scry</ent><br/
<hw>Scry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To descry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scry</ent><br/
<hw>Scry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Scry</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>A flock of wild fowl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scry</ent><br/
<hw>Scry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>ascrie</ets>, fr.
<ets>ascrien</ets> to cry out, fr. OF. <ets>escrier</ets>, F.
<ets>s'<eacute/crier</ets>. See <er>Ex-</er>, and <er>Cry</er>.]</ety>
<def>A cry or shout.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ld.
Berners.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scud</ent><br/
<hw>Scud</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/d)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Scudded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scudding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Dan. <ets>skyde</ets> to shoot,
shove, push, akin to <ets>skud</ets> shot, gunshot, a shoot, young
bough, and to E. <ets>shoot</ets>. <root/159. See <er>Shoot</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To move swiftly; especially, to move as if driven
forward by something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The first nautilus that <qex>scudded</qex> upon the glassy surface
of warm primeval oceans.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wind was high; the vast white clouds <qex>scudded</qex> over
the blue heaven.</q> <rj><qau>Beaconsfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To be driven swiftly, or to run,
before a gale, with little or no sail spread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scud</ent><br/
<hw>Scud</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pass over quickly.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shenstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scud</ent><br/
<hw>Scud</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of scudding; a
driving along; a rushing with precipitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Loose, vapory clouds driven swiftly by the
wind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Borne on the <qex>scud</qex> of the sea.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scud</qex> was flying fast above us, throwing a veil over
the moon.</q> <rj><qau>Sir S. Baker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A slight, sudden shower.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small flight of larks, or other
birds, less than a flock.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any swimming amphipod
crustacean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Storm scud</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Cloud</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuddle</ent><br/
<hw>Scud"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>scud</ets>: cf. <er>Scuttle</er> to hurry.]</ety> <def>To run
hastily; to hurry; to scuttle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scudo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scudi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It., a crown, a dollar, a
shield, fr. L. <ets>scutum a shield</ets>. Cf. <er>Scute</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Com.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A silver coin, and money of account,
used in Italy and Sicily, varying in value, in different parts, but
worth about 4 shillings sterling, or about 96 cents; also, a gold coin
worth about the same.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A gold coin of Rome, worth
64 shillings 11 pence sterling, or about $ 15.70.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuff</ent><br/
<hw>Scuff</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/f)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>schoft</ets> shoulder, Goth. <ets>skuft</ets> hair of the head. Cf.
<er>Scruff</er>.]</ety> <def>The back part of the neck; the
scruff.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Ld.
Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuff</ent><br/
<hw>Scuff</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scuffed</conjf> <pr>(sk<ucr/ft)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scuffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Scuffle</er>.]</ety>
<def>To walk without lifting the feet; to proceed with a scraping or
dragging movement; to shuffle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuff</ent><br/
<hw>Scuff</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scuffed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scuffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause a blemish
on the surface of, by scraping against an object; <as>as, he
<ex>scuffed</ex> his shoe on the ground</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To scrape with one's foot; <as>as, he
<ex>scuffed</ex> the chair leg with his shoe</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuffle</ent><br/
<hw>Scuf"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scuffled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scuffling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>scuff</ets>, v.i.; cf. Sw. <ets>skuffa</ets> to push, shove,
<ets>skuff</ets> a push, Dan. <ets>skuffe</ets> a drawer, a shovel, and
E. <ets>shuffle</ets>, <ets>shove</ets>. See <er>Shove</er>, and cf.
<er>Shuffle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strive or struggle with a
close grapple; to wrestle in a rough fashion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to strive or contend tumultuously; to
struggle confusedly or at haphazard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A gallant man had rather fight to great disadvantage in the field,
in an orderly way, than <qex>scuffle</qex> with an undisciplined
rabble.</q> <rj><qau>Eikon Basilike.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuffle</ent><br/
<hw>Scuf"fle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A rough, haphazard
struggle, or trial of strength; a disorderly wrestling at close
quarters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a confused contest; a tumultuous struggle for
superiority; a fight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dog leaps upon the serpent, and tears it to pieces; but in the
<qex>scuffle</qex> the cradle happened to be overturned.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A child's pinafore or bib.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A garden hoe.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuffler</ent><br/
<hw>Scuf"fler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
scuffles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An agricultural implement resembling a scarifier,
but usually lighter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scug</ent><br/
<hw>Scug</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/g)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>skygge</ets> to darken, a shade, SW. <ets>skugga</ets> to shade, a
shade, Icel. <ets>skyggja</ets> to shade, <ets>skuggi</ets> a
shade.]</ety> <def>To hide.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scug</ent><br/
<hw>Scug</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place of shelter; the declivity of a
hill.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculker</ent><br/
<ent>Sculk</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sculk</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/lk)</pr>, <hw>Sculk"er</hw>
<pr>(sk<ucr/lk"<etil/r)</pr> }</mhw>. <def>See <er>Skulk</er>,
<er>Skulker</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scull</ent><br/
<hw>Scull</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The skull.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scull</ent><br/
<hw>Scull</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 1st <er>School</er>.]</ety>
<def>A shoal of fish.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scull</ent><br/
<hw>Scull</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of uncertain origin; cf. Icel.
<ets>skola</ets> to wash.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A boat; a cockboat. See <er>Sculler</er>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of a pair of short oars worked by one
person.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A single oar used at the stern in
propelling a boat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common skua gull.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scull</ent><br/
<hw>Scull</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sculled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sculling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To impel (a
boat) with a pair of sculls, or with a single scull or oar worked over
the stern obliquely from side to side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scull</ent><br/
<hw>Scull</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To impel a boat with a scull or
sculls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculler</ent><br/
<hw>Scull"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A boat
rowed by one man with two sculls, or short oars.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who sculls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scullery</ent><br/
<hw>Scul"ler*y</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/l"l<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sculleries</plw>
<pr>(sk<ucr/l"l<etil/r*<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Probably originally, a
place for washing dishes, and for <ets>swillery</ets>, fr. OE.
<ets>swilen</ets> to wash, AS. <ets>swilian</ets> (see <er>Swill</er> to
wash, to drink), but influenced either by Icel. <ets>skola</ets>,
<ets>skyla</ets>, Dan. <ets>skylle</ets>, or by OF. <ets>escuelier</ets>
a place for keeping dishes, fr. <ets>escuele</ets> a dish, F.
<ets><eacute/cuelle</ets>, fr. L. <ets>scutella</ets> a salver, waiter
(cf. <er>Scuttle</er> a basket); or perhaps the English word is
immediately from the OF. <ets>escuelier</ets>; cf. OE.
<ets>squyllare</ets> a dishwasher.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place where
dishes, kettles, and culinary utensils, are cleaned and kept; also, a
room attached to the kitchen, where the coarse work is done; a back
kitchen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, refuse; filth; offal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gauden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scullion</ent><br/
<hw>Scul"lion</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/l"y<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A scallion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scullion</ent><br/
<hw>Scul"lion</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>escouillon</ets> (Cot.)
a dishclout, apparently for <ets>escouvillon</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/couvillon</ets> a swab; cf. also OF. <ets>souillon</ets> a
servant employed for base offices. Cf. <er>Scovel</er>.]</ety> <def>A
servant who cleans pots and kettles, and does other menial services in
the kitchen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The meanest <qex>scullion</qex> that followed his camp.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scullionly</ent><br/
<hw>Scul"lion*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a scullion; base.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculp</ent><br/
<hw>Sculp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sculptor</er>.]</ety> <def>To sculpture; to carve; to engrave.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Humorous.]</mark> <rj><au>Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculpin</ent><br/
<hw>Scul"pin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>skulpin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one
of numerous species of marine cottoid fishes of the genus
<gen>Cottus</gen>, or <gen>Acanthocottus</gen>, having a large head
armed with several sharp spines, and a broad mouth. They are generally
mottled with yellow, brown, and black. Several species are found on the
Atlantic coasts of Europe and America.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A large
cottoid market fish of California (<spn>Scorpaenichthys
marmoratus</spn>); -- called also <altname>bighead</altname>,
<altname>cabezon</altname>, <altname>scorpion</altname>,
<altname>salpa</altname>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The dragonet, or
yellow sculpin, of Europe (<spn>Callionymus lyra</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is also applied to other related California
species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Deep-water sculpin</b></col>, <cd>the sea
raven.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculptile</ent><br/
<hw>Sculp"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sculptilis</ets>. See <ets>Sculptor</ets>.]</ety> <def>Formed by
carving; graven; <as>as, <ex>sculptile</ex> images</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculptor</ent><br/
<hw>Sculp"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sculptor</ets>, fr. <ets>sculpere</ets>, <ets>sculptum</ets>, to
carve; cf. <ets>scalpere</ets> to cut, carve, scratch, and Gr. <?/ to
carve: cf. F. <ets>sculpteur</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
sculptures; one whose occupation is to carve statues, or works of
sculpture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, an artist who designs works of sculpture, his
first studies and his finished model being usually in a plastic
material, from which model the marble is cut, or the bronze is
cast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculptress</ent><br/
<hw>Sculp"tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female
sculptor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculptural</ent><br/
<hw>Sculp"tur*al</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to sculpture.</def> <rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculpture</ent><br/
<hw>Sculp"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sculptura</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sculpture</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The art of carving, cutting, or hewing wood, stone, metal, etc.,
into statues, ornaments, etc., or into figures, as of men, or other
things; hence, the art of producing figures and groups, whether in
plastic or hard materials.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Carved work modeled of, or cut upon, wood, stone,
metal, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There, too, in living <qex>sculpture</qex>, might be seen<br/
The mad affection of the Cretan queen.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculpture</ent><br/
<hw>Sculp"ture</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sculptured</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sculpturing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To form with the chisel on,
in, or from, wood, stone, or metal; to carve; to engrave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sculptured tortoise</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
common North American wood tortoise (<spn>Glyptemys insculpta</spn>).
The shell is marked with strong grooving and ridges which resemble
sculptured figures.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sculpturesque</ent><br/
<hw>Sculp`tur*esque"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>After the
manner of sculpture; resembling, or relating to, sculpture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scum</ent><br/
<hw>Scum</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of Scand. origin;
cf. Dan. & Sw. <ets>skum</ets>, Icel. <ets>sk<umac/m</ets>, LG.
<ets>schum</ets>, D. <ets>schuim</ets>, OHG. <ets>sc<umac/m</ets>, G.
<ets>schaum</ets>; probably from a root meaning, to cover. <root/158.
Cf. <er>Hide</er> skin, <er>Meerschaum</er>, <er>Skim</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>, <er>Sky</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The extraneous matter or impurities which rise to
the surface of liquids in boiling or fermentation, or which form on the
surface by other means; also, the scoria of metals in a molten state;
dross.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some to remove the <qex>scum</qex> as it did rise.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>refuse; recrement; anything vile or
worthless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The great and innocent are insulted by the <qex>scum</qex> and
refuse of the people.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scum</ent><br/
<hw>Scum</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scummed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Scumming</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take
the scum from; to clear off the impure matter from the surface of; to
skim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You that <qex>scum</qex> the molten lead.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden &
Lee.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sweep or range over the surface of.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wandering up and down without certain seat, they lived by
<qex>scumming</qex> those seas and shores as pirates.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scum</ent><br/
<hw>Scum</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To form a scum; to become covered
with scum. Also used figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Life, and the interest of life, have stagnated and
<qex>scummed</qex> over.</q> <rj><qau>A. K. H. Boyd.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scumber</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Discumber</er>.]</ety> <def>To void excrement.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scumber</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"ber</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Dung.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scumble</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scumbled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scumbling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>scum</ets>. <root/ 158.]</ety> <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <def>To cover
lighty, as a painting, or a drawing, with a thin wash of opaque color,
or with color-crayon dust rubbed on with the stump, or to make any
similar additions to the work, so as to produce a softened
effect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scumbling</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"bling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Fine
Arts)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A mode of obtaining a softened effect, in
painting and drawing, by the application of a thin layer of opaque color
to the surface of a painting, or part of the surface, which is too
bright in color, or which requires harmonizing.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>In crayon drawing, the use of the stump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The color so laid on. Also used
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shining above the brown <qex>scumbling</qex> of leafless
orchards.</q> <rj><qau>L. Wallace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scummer</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To scumber.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scummer</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"mer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Excrement; scumber.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scummer</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"mer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>escumoire</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/cumoire</ets>. See <er>Scum</er>, and cf.
<er>Skimmer</er>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for taking off scum; a
skimmer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scumming</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"ming</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The act
of taking off scum.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>That which is scummed off;
skimmings; scum; -- used chiefly in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scummy</ent><br/
<hw>Scum"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered with scum; of
the nature of scum.</def> <rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scunner</ent><br/
<hw>Scun"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Shun</er>.]</ety> <def>To cause to loathe, or feel disgust at.</def>
<mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scunner</ent><br/
<hw>Scun"ner</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To have a feeling of loathing
or disgust; hence, to have dislike, prejudice, or reluctance.</def>
<mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scunner</ent><br/
<hw>Scun"ner</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A feeling of disgust or loathing;
a strong prejudice; abhorrence; <as>as, to take a <ex>scunner</ex>
against some one</as>.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scup</ent><br/
<hw>Scup</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>schop</ets>.]</ety> <def>A swing.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scup</ent><br/
<hw>Scup</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contr. fr. American Indian
<ets>mishc<ugrave/p</ets>, fr. <ets>mishe-kuppi</ets> large,
thick-scaled.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A marine sparoid food fish
(<spn>Stenotomus chrysops</spn>, or <spn>Stenotomus argyrops</spn>),
common on the Atlantic coast of the United States. It appears bright
silvery when swimming in the daytime, but shows broad blackish
transverse bands at night and when dead. Called also
<altname>porgee</altname>, <altname>paugy</altname>,
<altname>porgy</altname>, <altname>scuppaug</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The same names are also applied to a closely allied
Southern species (<spn>Stenotomus Gardeni</spn>).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuppaug</ent><br/
<hw>Scup"paug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contr. fr. Amer.
Indian <ets>mishcuppauog</ets>, pl. of <ets>mishcup</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See 2d <er>Scup</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scupper</ent><br/
<hw>Scup"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escopir</ets>, <ets>escupir</ets>, to spit, perhaps for
<ets>escospir</ets>, L. <ets>ex + conspuere</ets> to spit upon; pref.
<ets>con-</ets> + <ets>spuere</ets> to spit. Cf. <er>Spit</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>An opening cut through the
waterway and bulwarks of a ship, so that water falling on deck may flow
overboard; -- called also <altname>scupper hole</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1295 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scupper hose</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a pipe of
leather, canvas, etc., attached to the mouth of the scuppers, on the
outside of a vessel, to prevent the water from entering.</cd>
<au>Totten.</au> -- <col><b>Scupper nail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>a nail with a very broad head, for securing the edge of the hose to
the scupper.</cd> -- <col><b>Scupper plug</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>a plug to stop a scupper.</cd> <au>Totten.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuppernong</ent><br/
<hw>Scup"per*nong</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/p"p<etil/r*n<ocr/ng)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably of American Indian origin.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An American grape, a form of <spn>Vitis
vulpina</spn>, found in the Southern Atlantic States, and often
cultivated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scur</ent><br/
<hw>Scur</hw> <pr>(sk<ucir/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scour</er> to run.]</ety> <def>To move hastily; to scour.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurf</ent><br/
<hw>Scurf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>scurf</ets>,
<ets>sceorf</ets>, or from Scand.; cf. Sw. <ets>skorf</ets>, Dan.
<ets>skurv</ets>, Icel. <ets>skurfur</ets>, D. <ets>schurft</ets>, G.
<ets>schorf</ets>; all akin to AS. <ets>scurf</ets>, and to AS.
<ets>sceorfan</ets> to scrape, to gnaw, G. <ets>sch<uum/rfen</ets> to
scrape, and probably also to E. <ets>scrape</ets>. Cf.
<er>Scurvy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Thin dry scales or scabs upon
the body; especially, thin scales exfoliated from the cuticle,
particularly of the scalp; dandruff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the foul remains of anything
adherent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>scurf</qex> is worn away of each committed crime.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything like flakes or scales adhering to a
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There stood a hill not far, whose grisly top<br/
Belched fire and rolling smoke; the rest entire<br/
Shone with a glossy <qex>scurf</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Minute membranous scales on the
surface of some leaves, as in the goosefoot.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurff</ent><br/
<hw>Scurff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The bull trout.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurfiness</ent><br/
<hw>Scurf"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Quality or state
of being scurfy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Scurf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurfy</ent><br/
<hw>Scurf"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Scurfier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Scurfiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Having or producing scurf;
covered with scurf; resembling scurf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurrier</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"ri*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
scurries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurrile</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"rile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scurrilis</ets>, fr. <ets>scurra</ets> a bufoon, jester: cf. F.
<ets>scurrile</ets>.]</ety> <def>Such as befits a buffoon or vulgar
jester; grossly opprobrious or loudly jocose in language; scurrilous;
<as>as, <ex>scurrile</ex> taunts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wretched affectation of <qex>scurrile</qex> laughter.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>scurrile</qex> or obscene jest will better advance you at
the court of Charles than your father's ancient name.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurrility</ent><br/
<hw>Scur*ril"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scurrilitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>scurrilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being scurrile or scurrilous;
mean, vile, or obscene jocularity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your reasons . . . have been sharp and sententious, pleasant
without <qex>scurrility</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is scurrile or scurrilous; gross or
obscene language; low buffoonery; vulgar abuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Interrupting prayers and sermons with clamor and
<qex>scurrility</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Scurrilousness; abuse; insolence; vulgarity;
indecency.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurrilous</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"ril*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<ets>Scurrile</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Using the low and indecent
language of the meaner sort of people, or such as only the license of
buffoons can warrant; <as>as, a <ex>scurrilous</ex>
fellow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Containing low indecency or abuse; mean; foul; vile;
obscenely jocular; <as>as, <ex>scurrilous</ex> language</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The absurd and <qex>scurrilous</qex> sermon which had very
unwisely been honored with impeachment.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Opprobrious; abusive; reproachful; insulting;
insolent; offensive; gross; vile; vulgar; low; foul; foul-mouthed;
indecent; scurrile; mean.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Scur"ril*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Scur"ril*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurrit</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"rit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The lesser tern (<spn>Sterna minuta</spn>).</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurry</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Scur</er>,
<er>Skirr</er>.]</ety> <def>To hasten away or along; to move rapidly; to
hurry; <as>as, the rabbit <ex>scurried</ex> away</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurry</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act of scurrying; hurried
movement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurvily</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"vi*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scurvy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurviness</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"vi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being scurvy; vileness; meanness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurvy</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"vy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Scurvier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Scurviest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Scurf</er>; cf.
<er>Scurvy</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered or
affected with scurf or scabs; scabby; scurfy; specifically, diseased
with the scurvy.</def> <ldquo/Whatsoever man . . . be <xex>scurvy</xex>
or scabbed.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Lev. xxi. 18, 20.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Vile; mean; low; vulgar; contemptible.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>scurvy</xex> trick.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ld.
Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>scurvy</qex> custom of taking tobacco.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] spoke spoke such <qex>scurvy</qex> and provoking terms.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scurvy</ent><br/
<hw>Scur"vy</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably from the same source as
<ets>scorbute</ets>, but influenced by <ets>scurf</ets>,
<ets>scurfy</ets>, <ets>scurvy</ets>, adj.; cf. D. <ets>scheurbuik</ets>
scurvy, G. <ets>scharbock</ets>, LL. <ets>scorbutus</ets>. Cf.
<er>Scorbute</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A disease characterized
by livid spots, especially about the thighs and legs, due to
extravasation of blood, and by spongy gums, and bleeding from almost all
the mucous membranes. It is accompanied by paleness, languor,
depression, and general debility. It is occasioned by confinement,
innutritious food, and hard labor, but especially by lack of fresh
vegetable food, or confinement for a long time to a limited range of
food, which is incapable of repairing the waste of the system. It was
formerly prevalent among sailors and soldiers.</def><-- caused by lack
of vitamin C --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scurvy grass</b></col> <ety>[<ets>Scurvy</ets> +
<ets>grass</ets>; or cf. Icel. <ets>skarfa</ets>k<amac/l <ets>scurvy
grass</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>A kind of cress
(<spn>Cochlearia officinalis</spn>) growing along the seacoast of
Northern Europe and in arctic regions. It is a remedy for the scurvy,
and has proved a valuable food to arctic explorers. The name is given
also to other allied species of plants.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scut</ent><br/
<hw>Scut</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>skott</ets> a fox's tail. <root/ 159.]</ety> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<def>The tail of a hare, or of a deer, or other animal whose tail is
short, esp. when carried erect; hence, sometimes, the animal
itself.</def> <ldquo/He ran like a <xex>scut</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Skelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How the Indian hare came to have a long tail, whereas that part in
others attains no higher than a <qex>scut</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My doe with the black <qex>scut</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scutum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutage</ent><br/
<hw>Scu"tage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>scutagium</ets>, from L. <ets>scutum</ets> a shield.]</ety>
<fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld> <def>Shield money; commutation of service for a
sum of money. See <er>Escuage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutal</ent><br/
<hw>Scu"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
shield.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A good example of these <qex>scutal</qex> monstrosities.</q>
<rj><qau>Cussans.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutate</ent><br/
<hw>Scu"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scutatus</ets> armed with a shield, from <ets>scutum</ets> a
shield.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Buckler-shaped; round or nearly
round.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Protected or covered by bony or
horny plates, or large scales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutch</ent><br/
<hw>Scutch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Scutched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scutching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Scotch</er>
to cut slightly.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To beat or whip; to drub.</def>
<mark>[Old or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To separate the woody fiber from (flax, hemp, etc.)
by beating; to swingle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To loosen and dress the fiber of (cotton or silk) by
beating; to free (fibrous substances) from dust by beating and
blowing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scutching machine</b></col>, <cd>a machine used to scutch
cotton, silk, or flax; -- called also <altname>batting
machine</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutch</ent><br/
<hw>Scutch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A wooden instrument used
in scutching flax and hemp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The woody fiber of flax; the refuse of scutched
flax.</def> <ldquo/The smoke of the burning <xex>scutch</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cuthbert Bede.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutcheon</ent><br/
<hw>Scutch"eon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Aphetic form of
<ets>escutcheon</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An escutcheon; an
emblazoned shield.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The corpse lay in state, with all the pomp of
<qex>scutcheons</qex>, wax lights, black hangings, and mutes.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small plate of metal, as the shield around a
keyhole. See <er>Escutcheon</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutcheoned</ent><br/
<hw>Scutch"eoned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Emblazoned on or
as a shield.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Scutcheoned</qex> panes in cloisters old.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutcher</ent><br/
<hw>Scutch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
scutches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An implement or machine for scutching hemp, flax, or
cotton, etc.; a scutch; a scutching machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutch grass</ent><br/
<hw>Scutch" grass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of
pasture grass (<spn>Cynodon Dactylon</spn>). See <er>Bermuda grass</er>:
also <xex>Illustration</xex> in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scute</ent><br/
<hw>Scute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scutum</ets> a
shield, a buckler. See <er>Scudo</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small
shield.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Skelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An old French gold coin of the value of 3s. 4d.
sterling, or about 80 cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A bony scale of a reptile or
fish; a large horny scale on the leg of a bird, or on the belly of a
snake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu*tel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scutellum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu*tel"la</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scutellae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fem. dim. of L.
<ets>scutum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Scutellum</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutellated</ent><br/
<ent>Scutellate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Scu"tel*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Scu"tel*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scutella</ets> a dish,
salver. Cf. <er>Scuttle</er> a basket.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Formed like a plate or salver; composed of
platelike surfaces; <as>as, the <ex>scutellated</ex> bone of a
sturgeon</as>.</def> <rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[See <er>Scutellum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the tarsi covered with broad transverse scales, or scutella;
-- said of certain birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutellation</ent><br/
<hw>Scu`tel*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The entire covering, or mode of arrangement, of scales, as on the
legs and feet of a bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutelliform</ent><br/
<hw>Scu*tel"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scutella</ets> a dish + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Scutellate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the form of a
scutellum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutelliplantar</ent><br/
<hw>Scu*tel`li*plan"tar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scutellus</ets> a shield + <ets>planta</ets> foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having broad scutella on the front, and small
scales on the posterior side, of the tarsus; -- said of certain
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutellum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu*tel"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scutella</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., neut. dim. of L.
<ets>scutum</ets> a shield.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
rounded apothecium having an elevated rim formed of the proper thallus,
the fructification of certain lichens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The third of the
four pieces forming the upper part of a thoracic segment of an insect.
It follows the scutum, and is followed by the small postscutellum; a
scutella. See <er>Thorax</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the
transverse scales on the tarsi and toes of birds; a scutella.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutibranch</ent><br/
<hw>Scu"ti*branch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Scutibranchiate.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the
Scutibranchiata.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutibranchia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu`ti*bran"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Scutibranchiata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutibranchian</ent><br/
<hw>Scu`ti*bran"chi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Scutibranchiata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutibranchiata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu`ti*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Scutum</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of gastropod Mollusca having a heart
with two auricles and one ventricle. The shell may be either spiral or
shieldlike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is now usually regarded as including only the
Rhipidoglossa and the Docoglossa. When originally established, it
included a heterogenous group of mollusks having shieldlike shells, such
as Haliotis, Fissurella, Carinaria, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutibranchiate</ent><br/
<hw>Scu`ti*bran"chi*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the gills protected by a shieldlike
shell; of or pertaining to the Scutibranchiata.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Scutibranchiata.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Scu*tif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scutum</ets> shield + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Carrying a
shield or buckler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutiform</ent><br/
<hw>Scu"ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scutum</ets> shield + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>scutiforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Shield-shaped; scutate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutiger</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu"ti*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>scutum</ets> shield + <ets>gerere</ets> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of chilopod myriapods of the genus
<gen>Scutigera</gen>. They sometimes enter buildings and prey upon
insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutiped</ent><br/
<hw>Scu"ti*ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scutum</ets> a shield + <ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, a foot:
cf. F. <ets>scutip<egrave/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the anterior surface of the tarsus covered with scutella, or
transverse scales, in the form of incomplete bands terminating at a
groove on each side; -- said of certain birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutter</ent><br/
<hw>Scut"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scuttle</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To run quickly; to
scurry; to scuttle.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark></p>

<p><q>A mangy little jackal . . . cocked up his ears and tail, and
<qex>scuttered</qex> across the shallows.</q>
<rj><qau>Kipling.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuttle</ent><br/
<hw>Scut"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>scutel</ets> a dish, platter; cf. Icel. <ets>skutill</ets>; both
fr. L. <ets>scutella</ets>, dim. of <ets>scutra</ets>, <ets>scuta</ets>,
a dish or platter; cf. <ets>scutum</ets> a shield. Cf.
<er>Skillet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A broad, shallow
basket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A wide-mouthed vessel for holding coal: a coal
hod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuttle</ent><br/
<hw>Scut"tle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[For <ets>scuddle</ets>, fr.
<ets>scud</ets>.]</ety> <def>To run with affected precipitation; to
hurry; to bustle; to scuddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With the first dawn of day, old Janet was <qex>scuttling</qex>
about the house to wake the baron.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuttle</ent><br/
<hw>Scut"tle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A quick pace; a short run.</def>
<rj><au>Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuttle</ent><br/
<hw>Scut"tle</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/t"t'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escoutille</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/scoutille</ets>, cf. Sp.
<ets>escotilla</ets>; probably akin to Sp. <ets>escotar</ets> to cut a
thing so as to make it fit, to hollow a garment about the neck, perhaps
originally, to cut a bosom-shaped piece out, and of Teutonic origin; cf.
D. <ets>schoot</ets> lap, bosom, G. <ets>schoss</ets>, Goth.
<ets>skauts</ets> the hem of a garnment. Cf. <er>Sheet</er> an
expanse.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small opening in an outside wall or
covering, furnished with a lid.</def> <specif>Specifically:</specif>
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A small opening or hatchway in the
deck of a ship, large enough to admit a man, and with a lid for covering
it, also, a like hole in the side or bottom of a ship.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>An opening in the roof of a house, with a
lid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The lid or door which covers or closes an opening in
a roof, wall, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Scuttle butt</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Scuttle
cask</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a butt or cask with a
large hole in it, used to contain the fresh water for daily use in a
ship.</cd></cs> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scuttle</ent><br/
<hw>Scut"tle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Scuttled</conjf> <pr>(sk<ucr/t"t'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Scuttling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut a
hole or holes through the bottom, deck, or sides of (as of a ship), for
any purpose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sink by making holes through the bottom of;
<as>as, to <ex>scuttle</ex> a ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>To defeat, frustrate,
abandon, or cause to be abandoned; -- of plans, projects, actions,
hopes; <as>as, the review committee <ex>scuttled</ex> the project due to
lack of funds</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>scuttlebutt</ent><br/
<hw>scut"tle*butt`</hw> <pr>(sk<ucr/t"t'l*b<ucr/t`)</pr> , <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>See <cref>scuttle butt</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A drinking fountain on board a ship or at a naval
station.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The latest gossip; rumors.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scutum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scu"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scuta</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>An oblong shield made of boards or
wickerwork covered with leather, with sometimes an iron rim; -- carried
chiefly by the heavy-armed infantry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A penthouse or
awning.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The second and
largest of the four parts forming the upper surface of a thoracic
segment of an insect. It is preceded by the prescutum and followed by
the scutellum. See the <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Thorax</er>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the two lower valves of the operculum of a
barnacle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scybala</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scyb"a*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>sky`balon</grk> dung.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Hardened
masses of feces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scye</ent><br/
<hw>Scye</hw> <pr>(s<imac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Arm scye, a
cutter's term for the armhole or part of the armhole of the waist of a
garment.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyle</ent><br/
<hw>Scyle</hw> <pr>(s<imac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>scylan</ets> to withdraw or remove.]</ety> <def>To hide; to
secrete; to conceal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scylla</ent><br/
<hw>Scyl"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dangerous rock on
the Italian coast opposite the whirpool Charybdis on the coast of
Sicily, -- both personified in classical literature as ravenous
monsters. The passage between them was formerly considered perilous;
hence, the saying <ldquo/Between Scylla and Charybdis,<rdquo/ signifying
a great peril on either hand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyllaea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scyl*lae"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Scylla</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of oceanic
nudibranchiate mollusks having the small branched gills situated on the
upper side of four fleshy lateral lobes, and on the median caudal
crest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In color and form these mollusks closely imitate the
fronds of sargassum and other floating seaweeds among which they
live.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyllarian</ent><br/
<hw>Scyl*la"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of a family (<fam>Scyllaridae</fam>) of macruran Crustacea,
remarkable for the depressed form of the body, and the broad, flat
antennae. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyllite</ent><br/
<hw>Scyl"lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
white crystalline substance of a sweetish taste, resembling inosite and
metameric with dextrose. It is extracted from the kidney of the dogfish
(of the genus <gen>Scyllium</gen>), the shark, and the skate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scymetar</ent><br/
<hw>Scym"e*tar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scimiter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scypha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scy"pha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scyphae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See <er>Scyphus</er>, 2 <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyphiform</ent><br/
<hw>Scy"phi*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>scyphus</ets> a cup + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Cup-shaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyphistoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scy*phis"to*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Scyphistomata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<plw>Scyphistomae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>sky`fos</grk> a cup + <grk>sto`ma</grk> the mouth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The young attached larva of Discophora in the
stage when it resembles a hydroid, or actinian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyphobranchii</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scy`pho*bran"chi*i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>sky`fos</grk> a cup + <grk>bra`gchion</grk> a
gill.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of fishes including the
blennioid and gobioid fishes, and other related families.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyphomedusae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scy`pho*me*du"sae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>sky`fos</grk> cup + NL.
<ets>medusa</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Acraspeda</er>, or <er>Discophora</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyphophori</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scy*phoph"o*ri</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>sky`fos</grk> a cup + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of fresh-water fishes inhabiting
tropical Africa. They have rudimentary electrical organs on each side of
the tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scyphus</ent><br/
<hw>Scy"phus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scyphi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a cup, Gr.
<grk>sky`fos</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Antiq.)</fld> <def>A kind
of large drinking cup, -- used by Greeks and Romans, esp. by poor
folk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The cup of a
narcissus, or a similar appendage to the corolla in other flowers.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A cup-shaped stem or podetium in lichens. Also called
<altname>scypha</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scythe</ent><br/
<hw>Scythe</hw> <pr>(s<imac/th)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sithe</ets>, AS. s<imac/<edh/e, <ets>sig<edh/e</ets>; akin to Icel.
<ets>sig<edh/r</ets> a sickle, LG. <ets>segd</ets>, <ets>seged</ets>,
<ets>seed</ets>, <ets>seid</ets>, OHG. <ets>segansa</ets> sickle,
scythe, G. <ets>sense</ets> scythe, and to E. <ets>saw</ets> a cutting
instrument. See <er>Saw</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sithe</asp> and <asp>sythe</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
instrument for mowing grass, grain, or the like, by hand, composed of a
long, curving blade, with a sharp edge, made fast to a long handle,
called a <xex>snath</xex>, which is bent into a form convenient for
use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sharp-edged <qex>scythe</qex> shears up the spiring grass.</q>
<rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whatever thing<br/
The <qex>scythe</qex> of Time mows down.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Antiq.)</fld> <def>A scythe-shaped blade attached
to ancient war chariots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1296 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Scythe</ent><br/
<hw>Scythe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cut with a
scythe; to cut off as with a scythe; to mow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Time had not <qex>scythed</qex> all that youth begun.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scythed</ent><br/
<hw>Scythed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Armed with scythes, as
a chariot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Chariots <qex>scythed</qex>,<br/
On thundering axles rolled.</q> <rj><qau>Glover.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scytheman</ent><br/
<hw>Scythe"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Scythemen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who uses a scythe; a
mower.</def> <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scythestone</ent><br/
<hw>Scythe"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stone for
sharpening scythes; a whetstone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scythewhet</ent><br/
<hw>Scythe"whet`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Wilson's thrush; -- so called from its note.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scythian</ent><br/
<hw>Scyth"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Scythia (a name given to the northern part of Asia, and Europe adjoining
to Asia), or its language or inhabitants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Scythian lamb</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Barometz</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scythian</ent><br/
<hw>Scyth"i*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or
inhabitant of Scythia; specifically <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>, one of a
Slavonic race which in early times occupied Eastern Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The language of the Scythians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Scytodermata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Scy`to*der"ma*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a hide + <?/ a skin.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Holothurioidea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sdain</ent><br/
<hw>Sdain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>Disdain.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>'Sdeath</ent><br/
<hw>'Sdeath</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted fr.
<ets>God's death</ets>.]</ety> <def>An exclamation expressive of
impatience or anger.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sdeign</ent><br/
<hw>Sdeign</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To disdain.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But either <qex>sdeigns</qex> with other to partake.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea</ent><br/
<hw>Sea</hw> <pr>(s<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>see</ets>,
AS. <ets>s<aemac/</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zee</ets>, OS. & OHG.
<ets>s<emac/o</ets>, G. <ets>see</ets>, OFries. <ets>se</ets>, Dan.
<ets>s<oum/</ets>, Sw. <ets>sj<oum/</ets>, Icel. <ets>saer</ets>, Goth.
<ets>saiws</ets>, and perhaps to L. <ets>saevus</ets> fierce, savage.
<root/151a.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the larger bodies of salt
water, less than an ocean, found on the earth's surface; a body of salt
water of second rank, generally forming part of, or connecting with, an
ocean or a larger sea; <as>as, the Mediterranean <ex>Sea</ex>; the
<ex>Sea</ex> of Marmora; the North <ex>Sea</ex>; the Carribean
<ex>Sea</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An inland body of water, esp. if large or if salt or
brackish; <as>as, the Caspian <ex>Sea</ex>; <ex>the Sea</ex> of
Aral</as>; sometimes, a small fresh-water lake; <as>as, the <ex>Sea</ex>
of Galilee</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The ocean; the whole body of the salt water which
covers a large part of the globe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I marvel how the fishes live in the <qex>sea</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ambiguous between <qex>sea</qex> and land<br/
The river horse and scaly crocodile.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The swell of the ocean or other body of water in a
high wind; motion or agitation of the water's surface; also, a single
wave; a billow; <as>as, there was a high <ex>sea</ex> after the storm;
the vessel shipped a <ex>sea</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Jewish Antiq.)</fld> <def>A great brazen laver in
the temple at Jerusalem; -- so called from its size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He made a molten <qex>sea</qex> of ten cubits from brim to brim,
round in compass, and five cubits the height thereof.</q> <rj><qau>2
Chron. iv. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Fig.: Anything resembling the sea in vastness;
<as>as, a <ex>sea</ex> of glory</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the space . . . was one <qex>sea</qex> of heads.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sea</xex> is often used in the composition of words
of obvious signification; as, <xex>sea</xex>-bathed,
<xex>sea</xex>-beaten, <xex>sea</xex>-bound, <xex>sea</xex>-bred,
<xex>sea</xex>-circled, <xex>sea</xex>like, <xex>sea</xex>-nursed,
<xex>sea</xex>-tossed, <xex>sea</xex>-walled, <xex>sea</xex>-worn, and
the like. It is also used either adjectively or in combination with
substantives; as, <xex>sea</xex> bird, <xex>sea</xex>-bird, or
<xex>sea</xex>bird, <xex>sea</xex> acorn, or
<xex>sea</xex>-acorn.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At sea</b></col>, <cd>upon the ocean; away from land;
figuratively, without landmarks for guidance; lost; at the mercy of
circumstances.</cd> <ldquo/To say the old man was <xex>at sea</xex>
would be too feeble an expression.<rdquo/ <au>G. W. Cable</au> --
<col><b>At full sea</b></col> <cd>at the height of flood tide; hence, at
the height.</cd> <ldquo/But now God's mercy was <xex>at full
sea</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Jer. Taylor.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>Beyond
seas</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Beyond the sea</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Beyond the seas</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>out of
the state, territory, realm, or country.</cd> <au>Wharton.</au> --
<col><b>Half seas over</b></col>, <cd>half drunk.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <au>Spectator.</au> -- <col><b>Heavy
sea</b></col>, <cd>a sea in which the waves run high.</cd> --
<col><b>Long sea</b></col>, <cd>a sea characterized by the uniform and
steady motion of long and extensive waves.</cd> -- <col><b>Short
sea</b></col>, <cd>a sea in which the waves are short, broken, and
irregular, so as to produce a tumbling or jerking motion.</cd> --
<col><b>To go to sea</b></col>, <cd>to adopt the calling or occupation
of a sailor.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea acorn</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" a"corn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An acorn
barnacle (<gen>Balanus</gen>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea adder</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ad"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The European fifteen-spined stickleback (<spn>Gasterosteus
spinachia</spn>); -- called also <altname>bismore</altname>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The European tanglefish, or pipefish (<spn>Syngnathus
acus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea anchor</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" an"chor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
<cref>Drag sail</cref>, under 4th <er>Drag</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea anemone</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" a*nem"o*ne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one
of numerous species of soft-bodied Anthozoa, belonging to the order
<ord>Actinaria</ord>; an actinian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They have the oral disk surrounded by one or more
circles of simple tapering tentacles, which are often very numerous, and
when expanded somewhat resemble the petals of flowers, with colors
varied and often very beautiful.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea ape</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ape`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The thrasher shark.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sea
otter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea apple</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ap"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The fruit of a
West Indian palm (<spn>Manicaria Plukenetii</spn>), often found floating
in the sea.</def> <rj><au>A. Grisebach.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea arrow</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ar"row</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A squid of
the genus <gen>Ommastrephes</gen>. See <er>Squid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bank</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bank`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>The seashore.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bank or mole to defend against the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-bar</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-bar`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
tern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea barrow</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bar"row</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sea
purse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bass</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bass`</hw>. <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A large marine food fish (<spn>Serranus atrarius</spn> syn.
<spn>Centropristis atrarius</spn>) which abounds on the Atlantic coast
of the <country>United States</country>. It is dark bluish, with black
bands, and more or less varied with small white spots and blotches.
Called also, locally, <altname>blue bass</altname>, <altname>black sea
bass</altname>, <altname>blackfish</altname>,
<altname>bluefish</altname>, and <altname>black perch</altname>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A California food fish (<spn>Cynoscion nobile</spn>);
-- called also <altname>white sea bass</altname>, and <altname>sea
salmon</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bat</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Batfish</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seabeach</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"beach`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A beach lying along
the sea.</def> <ldquo/The bleak <xex>seabeach</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bean</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bean</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Florida bean</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bear</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bear`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any fur seal. See under <er>Fur</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
white bear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seabeard</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"beard`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
green seaweed (<spn>Cladophora rupestris</spn>) growing in dense
tufts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea beast</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" beast`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any large
marine mammal, as a seal, walrus, or cetacean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bird</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bird`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any swimming
bird frequenting the sea; a sea fowl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea blite</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" blite`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant
(<spn>Suaeda maritima</spn>) of the Goosefoot family, growing in salt
marshes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-blubber</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-blub"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A jellyfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaboard</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sea</ets> +
<ets>board</ets>, F. <ets>bord</ets> side.]</ety> <def>The seashore;
seacoast.</def> <rj><au>Ld. Berners.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaboard</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"board`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bordering upon, or being near,
the sea; seaside; seacoast; <as>as, a <ex>seaboard</ex>
town</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaboard</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"board`</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the sea.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaboat</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"boat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<aemac/b<amac/t</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A boat or vessel
adapted to the open sea; hence, a vessel considered with reference to
her power of resisting a storm, or maintaining herself in a heavy sea;
<as>as, a good <ex>sea boat</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A chiton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seabord</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"bord`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seaboard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-bordering</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-bor"der*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bordering on
the sea; situated beside the sea.</def> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-born</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-born`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Born of
the sea; produced by the sea.</def> <ldquo/Neptune and his
<xex>sea-born</xex> niece.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Waller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Born at sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seabound</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"bound`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bounded by the
sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bow</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <cref>Marine rainbow</cref>,
under <er>Rainbow</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea boy</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" boy`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A boy employed on
shipboard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea breach</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" breach`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A breaking or overflow of a
bank or a dike by the sea.</def> <rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bream</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bream`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
several species of sparoid fishes, especially the common European
species (<spn>Pagellus centrodontus</spn>), the Spanish (<spn>Pagellus
Oweni</spn>), and the black sea bream (<spn>Cantharus lineatus</spn>);
-- called also <altname>old wife</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea brief</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" brief`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Same as <er>Sea
letter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea bug</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" bug`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
chiton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-built</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-built`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Built at, in, or
by the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea butterfly</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" but"ter*fly`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
pteropod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea cabbage</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" cab"bage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<cref>Sea kale</cref>, under <er>Kale</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea calf</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" calf`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common
seal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea canary</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ca*na"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[So called from a whistling
sound which it makes.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The beluga, or
white whale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea captain</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" cap"tain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The captain of a vessel that
sails upon the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea card</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" card`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Mariner's card, or
compass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea cat</ent><br/
<ent>Sea catfish</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sea" cat`fish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <hw>Sea" cat`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>. }</mhw> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The wolf
fish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any marine siluroid fish, as
<spn>Aelurichthys marinus</spn>, and <spn>Arinus felis</spn>, of the
eastern coast of the United States. Many species are found on the coasts
of Central and South America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea chart</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" chart`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A chart or map on which the
lines of the shore, islands, shoals, harbors, etc., are
delineated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea chickweed</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" chick"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A fleshy
plant (<spn>Arenaria peploides</spn>) growing in large tufts in the
sands of the northern Atlantic seacoast; -- called also <altname>sea
sandwort</altname>, and <altname>sea purslane</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea clam</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" clam`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of the
large bivalve mollusks found on the open seacoast, especially those of
the family <fam>Mactridae</fam>, as the common American species.
(<spn>Mactra solidissima</spn> or <spn>Spisula solidissima</spn>); --
called also <altname>beach clam</altname>, and <altname>surf
clam</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea coal</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" coal`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Coal brought by sea; -- a name by
which mineral coal was formerly designated in the south of England, in
distinction from <xex>charcoal</xex>, which was brought by
land.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sea-coal facing</b></col> <fld>(Founding)</fld>,
<cd>facing consisting of pulverized bituminous coal.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seacoast</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"coast`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The shore or border
of the land adjacent to the sea or ocean. Also used
adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea cob</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" cob`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
black-backed gull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea cock</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a steamship, a
cock or valve close to the vessel's side, for closing a pipe which
communicates with the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The black-bellied
plover.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A gurnard, as the European red gurnard
(<spn>Trigla pini</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea cocoa</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" co"coa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A magnificent
palm (<spn>Lodoicea Sechellarum</spn>) found only in the Seychelles
Islands. The fruit is an immense two-lobed nut. It was found floating in
the Indian Ocean before the tree was known, and called <xex>sea
cocoanut</xex>, and <xex>double cocoanut</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea colander</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" col"an*der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A large
blackfish seaweed (<spn>Agarum Turneri</spn>), the frond of which is
punctured with many little holes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea colewort</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" cole"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Sea
cabbage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea compass</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" com"pass</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The mariner's compass. See
under <er>Compass</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea coot</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" coot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A scoter
duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea corn</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" corn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A yellow
cylindrical mass of egg capsules of certain species of whelks
(<gen>Buccinum</gen>), which resembles an ear of maize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea cow</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" cow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The mantee.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The dugong.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>The walrus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea crayfish</ent><br/
<ent>Sea crawfish</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sea" craw"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <hw>Sea" cray"fish`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>. }</mhw> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any crustacean of the
genus <gen>Palinurus</gen> and allied genera, as the European spiny
lobster (<spn>Palinurus vulgaris</spn>), which is much used as an
article of food. See <er>Lobster</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea crow</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" crow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The chough.</def> <mark>[Ireland]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
cormorant.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The blackheaded pewit, and other
gulls.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The skua.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>The
razorbill.</def> <mark>[Orkney Islands]</mark> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>The
coot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea cucumber</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" cu"cum*ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any large
holothurian, especially one of those belonging to the genus
<gen>Pentacta</gen>, or <gen>Cucumaria</gen>, as the common American and
European species. (<spn>Pentacta frondosa</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea dace</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" dace`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European
sea perch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea daffodil</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" daf"fo*dil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A European
amaryllidaceous plant (<spn>Pancratium maritimum</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea devil</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" dev`il</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any very large ray, especially any species of the genus
<gen>Manta</gen> or <gen>Cephaloptera</gen>, some of which become more
than twenty feet across and weigh several tons. See also <cref>Ox
ray</cref>, under <er>Ox</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any large
cephalopod, as a large Octopus, or a giant squid
(<gen>Architeuthis</gen>). See <er>Devilfish</er>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>The angler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea dog</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" dog`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
dogfish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The common seal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An old sailor; a salt.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea dotterel</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" dot"ter*el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
turnstone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea dove</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" dove`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The little
auk, or rotche.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea dragon</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" drag"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A dragonet, or sculpin.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
pegasus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea drake</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" drake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The pewit
gull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea duck</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" duck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of ducks which frequent the seacoasts and feed mainly
on fishes and mollusks. The scoters, eiders, old squaw, and ruddy duck
are examples. They may be distinguished by the lobate hind
toe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea eagle</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ea"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of several species of fish-eating eagles of the genus
<gen>Haliaeetus</gen> and allied genera, as the North Pacific sea eagle
(<spn>Haliaeetus pelagicus</spn>), which has white shoulders, head,
rump, and tail; the European white-tailed eagle (<spn>Haliaeetus
albicilla</spn>); and the Indian white-tailed sea eagle, or fishing
eagle (<spn>Polioaetus ichthyaetus</spn>). The bald eagle and the osprey
are also sometimes classed as <ex>sea eagles</ex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The eagle ray. See under
<er>Ray</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-ear</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-ear`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"<emac/r`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of ear-shaped shells of the genus
<gen>Haliotis</gen>. See <er>Abalone</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea eel</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" eel`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" <emac/l`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The conger eel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea egg</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" egg`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" <ecr/g`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A sea urchin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea elephant</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" el"e*phant</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" <emac/"<esl/*f<ait/nt)</pr>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A very large seal (<spn>Macrorhinus
proboscideus</spn>) of the Antarctic seas, much hunted for its oil. It
sometimes attains a length of thirty feet, and is remarkable for the
prolongation of the nose of the adult male into an erectile elastic
proboscis, about a foot in length. Another species of smaller size
(<spn>Macrorhinus angustirostris</spn>) occurs on the coast of Lower
California, but is now nearly extinct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1297 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Sea fan</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" fan"</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" f<acr/n`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any gorgonian which branches in a fanlike form, especially
<spn>Gorgonia flabellum</spn> of Florida and the West Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seafarer</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"far`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sea +
<ets>fare</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who follows the sea as a business; a
mariner; a sailor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seafaring</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"far`ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Following the business of a
mariner; <as>as, a <ex>seafaring</ex> man</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea feather</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" feath"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
gorgonian which branches in a plumelike form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea fennel</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" fen"nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Samphire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea fern</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" fern"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any gorgonian
which branches like a fern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea fight</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" fight`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>An engagement between ships at
sea; a naval battle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea fir</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" fir`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sertularian
hydroid, especially <xex>Sertularia abietina</xex>, which branches like
a miniature fir tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea flower</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" flow"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sea
anemone, or any related anthozoan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea foam</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" foam`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>Foam of sea
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Meerschaum; -- called also
<altname>sea froth</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea fowl</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" fowl`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bird which
habitually frequents the sea, as an auk, gannet, gull, tern, or petrel;
also, all such birds, collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea fox</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" fox`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The thrasher
shark. See <er>Thrasher</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea froth</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" froth`</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>. <def>See <er>Sea foam</er>,
2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-gait</ent><br/
<ent>Sea-gate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sea"-gate`</hw>, <hw>Sea"-gait`</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A long, rolling swell of the sea.</def> <rj><au>Ham. Nav.
Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea gauge</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" gauge`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See under <er>Gauge</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea girkin</ent><br/
<ent>Sea gherkin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sea" gher`kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>Sea"
gir"kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any small
holothurian resembling in form a gherkin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea ginger</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" gin"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A hydroid
coral of the genus <gen>Millepora</gen>, especially <spn>Millepora
alcicornis</spn>, of the <geog>West Indies</geog> and
<state>Florida</state>. So called because it stings the tongue like
ginger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea girdles</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" gir"dles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of
kelp (<spn>Laminaria digitata</spn>) with palmately cleft fronds; --
called also <altname>sea wand</altname>, <altname>seaware</altname>, and
<xex>tangle</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seagirt</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"girt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Surrounded by the
water of the sea or ocean; <as>as, a <ex>seagirt</ex> isle</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea god</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" god`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A marine deity; a fabulous being
supposed to live in, or have dominion over, the sea, or some particular
sea or part of the sea, as Neptune.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea goddess</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" god"dess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A goddess supposed to live in
or reign over the sea, or some part of the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seagoing</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"go`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Going upon the sea;
especially, sailing upon the deep sea; -- used in distinction from
<xex>coasting</xex> or <xex>river</xex>, as applied to
vessels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea goose</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" goose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
phalarope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea gown</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" gown`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A gown or frock with short
sleeves, formerly worn by mariners.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea grape</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" grape`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The gulf weed. See under <er>Gulf</er>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A shrubby plant (<spn>Coccoloba uvifera</spn>) growing
on the sandy shores of tropical America, somewhat resembling the
grapevine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The clusters of
gelatinous egg capsules of a squid (<gen>Loligo</gen>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea grass</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" grass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Eelgrass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea green</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" green`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The green color of sea
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-green</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-green`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a beautiful bluish green
color, like sea water on soundings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea gudgeon</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" gud"geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
European black goby (<spn>Gobius niger</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea gull</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" gull`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any gull
living on the seacoast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seah</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se"ah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Jewish dry measure
containing one third of an ephah.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea hare</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" hare`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
tectibranchiate mollusk of the genus <gen>Aplysia</gen>. See
<er>Aplysia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea hawk</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" hawk`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A jager
gull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea heath</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" heath`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A low
perennial plant (<spn>Frankenia laevis</spn>) resembling heath, growing
along the seashore in Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea hedgehog</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" hedge"hog`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sea
urchin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea hen</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" hen`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common
guillemot; -- applied also to various other sea birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea hog</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" hog`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
porpoise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea holly</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" hol"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An evergeen
seashore plant (<spn>Eryngium maritimum</spn>). See
<er>Eryngium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea holm</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" holm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A small uninhabited
island.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea holm</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" holm`</hw>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Sea holly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea horse</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" horse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>A fabulous creature,
half horse and half fish, represented in classic mythology as driven by
sea dogs or ridden by the Nereids. It is also depicted in heraldry. See
<er>Hippocampus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The walrus.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any fish of the genus Hippocampus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In a passage of Dryden's, the word is supposed to refer
to the hippopotamus.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea hulver</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" hul"ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Sea
holly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-island</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-is`land</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to certain islands along the coast of South Carolina and Georgia;
<as>as, <ex>sea-island</ex> cotton, a superior cotton of long fiber
produced on those islands</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea jelly</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" jel"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A medusa, or
jellyfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seak</ent><br/
<hw>Seak</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Soap prepared for use in
milling cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea kale</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" kale"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See under
<er>Kale</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea king</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" king`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>One of the leaders among the
Norsemen who passed their lives in roving the seas in search of plunder
and adventures; a Norse pirate chief. See the Note under
<er>Viking</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seal</ent><br/
<hw>Seal</hw> <pr>(s<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sele</ets>, AS. <ets>seolh</ets>; akin to OHG. <ets>selah</ets>,
Dan. <ets>sael</ets>, Sw. <ets>sj<aum/l</ets>, Icel.
<ets>selr</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any aquatic carnivorous
mammal of the families <fam>Phocidae</fam> and
<fam>Otariidae</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Seals inhabit seacoasts, and are found principally in
the higher latitudes of both hemispheres. There are numerous species,
bearing such popular names as <stype>sea lion</stype>, <stype>sea
leopard</stype>, <stype>sea bear</stype>, or <stype>ursine seal</stype>,
<stype>fur seal</stype>, and <stype>sea elephant</stype>. The bearded
seal (<spn>Erignathus barbatus</spn>), the hooded seal (<spn>Cystophora
cristata</spn>), and the ringed seal (<spn>Phoca foetida</spn>), are
northern species. See also <cref>Eared seal</cref>, <cref>Harp
seal</cref>, <cref>Monk seal</cref>, and <cref>Fur seal</cref>, under
<er>Eared</er>, <er>Harp</er>, <er>Monk</er>, and <er>Fur</er>. Seals
are much hunted for their skins and fur, and also for their oil, which
in some species is very abundant.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Harbor seal</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the common
seal (<spn>Phoca vitulina</spn>). It inhabits both the North Atlantic
and the North Pacific Ocean, and often ascends rivers; -- called also
<altname>marbled seal</altname>, <altname>native seal</altname>,
<altname>river seal</altname>, <altname>bay seal</altname>,
<altname>land seal</altname>, <altname>sea calf</altname>, <altname>sea
cat</altname>, <altname>sea dog</altname>, <altname>dotard</altname>,
<altname>ranger</altname>, <altname>selchie</altname>,
<altname>tangfish</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seal</ent><br/
<hw>Seal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>seel</ets>, OF.
<ets>seel</ets>, F. <ets>sceau</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sigillum</ets> a
little figure or image, a seal, dim. of <ets>signum</ets> a mark, sign,
figure, or image. See <er>Sign</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Sigil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An engraved or inscribed stamp,
used for marking an impression in wax or other soft substance, to be
attached to a document, or otherwise used by way of authentication or
security.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wax, wafer, or other tenacious substance, set to an
instrument, and impressed or stamped with a seal; <as>as, to give a deed
under hand and <ex>seal</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till thou canst rail the <qex>seal</qex> from off my bond<br/
Thou but offend'st thy lungs to speak so loud.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which seals or fastens; esp., the wax or wafer
placed on a letter or other closed paper, etc., to fasten it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which confirms, ratifies, or makes stable; that
which authenticates; that which secures; assurance.</def> <ldquo/Under
the <xex>seal</xex> of silence.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a red <qex>seal</qex> is the setting sun<br/
On the good and the evil men have done.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An arrangement for preventing the entrance or return
of gas or air into a pipe, by which the open end of the pipe dips
beneath the surface of water or other liquid, or a deep bend or sag in
the pipe is filled with the liquid; a draintrap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Great seal</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Great</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Privy seal</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Privy</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Seal lock</b></col>, <cd>a lock in which
the keyhole is covered by a seal in such a way that the lock can not be
opened without rupturing the seal.</cd> -- <col><b>Seal
manual</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Manual</er>, <pos>a.</pos></cd> --
<col><b>Seal ring</b></col>, <cd>a ring having a seal engraved on it, or
ornamented with a device resembling a seal; a signet ring.</cd>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seal</ent><br/
<hw>Seal</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sealed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sealing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>selen</ets>; cf. OF.
<ets>seeler</ets>, <ets>seieler</ets>, F. <ets>sceller</ets>, LL.
<ets>sigillare</ets>. See <er>Seal</er> a stamp.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To set or affix a seal to; hence, to authenticate; to confirm; to
ratify; to establish; <as>as, to <ex>seal</ex> a deed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And with my hand I <qex>seal</qex> my true heart's love.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mark with a stamp, as an evidence of standard
exactness, legal size, or merchantable quality; <as>as, to <ex>seal</ex>
weights and measures; to <ex>seal</ex> silverware</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fasten with a seal; to attach together with a
wafer, wax, or other substance causing adhesion; <as>as, to
<ex>seal</ex> a letter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hence, to shut close; to keep close; to make fast;
to keep secure or secret.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Seal</qex> up your lips, and give no words but
<ldquo/mum<rdquo/.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To fix, as a piece of iron in a wall, with cement,
plaster, or the like.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To close by means of a seal; <as>as, to
<ex>seal</ex> a drainpipe with water</as>. See 2d <er>Seal</er>,
5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Among the Mormons, to confirm or set apart as a
second or additional wife.</def> <mark>[Utah, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If a man once married desires a second helpmate . . . she is
<qex>sealed</qex> to him under the solemn sanction of the church.</q>
<rj><qau>H. Stansbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seal</ent><br/
<hw>Seal</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To affix one's seal, or a
seal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>seal</qex> unto this bond.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea laces</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" la"ces</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of
seaweed (<spn>Chorda Filum</spn>) having blackish cordlike fronds, often
many feet long.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea lamprey</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" lam"prey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common
lamprey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea language</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" lan"guage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The peculiar language or
phraseology of seamen; sailor's cant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea lark</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" lark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The rock pipit (<spn>Anthus obscurus</spn>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Any one of several small sandpipers and plovers, as the ringed
plover, the turnstone, the dunlin, and the sanderling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea lavender</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" lav"en*der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<cref>Marsh rosemary</cref>, under <er>Marsh</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea lawyer</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" law"yer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The gray
snapper. See under <er>Snapper</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seal-brown</ent><br/
<hw>Seal"-brown`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a rich dark
brown color, like the fur of the fur seal after it is dyed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea legs</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" legs`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Legs able to maintain their
possessor upright in stormy weather at sea, that is, ability to stand or
walk steadily on deck when a vessel is rolling or pitching in a rough
sea.</def> <mark>[Sailor's Cant]</mark> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea lemon</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" lem"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
several species of nudibranchiate mollusks of the genus Doris and allied
genera, having a smooth, thick, convex yellow body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea leopard</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" leop"ard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
several species of spotted seals, especially <spn>Ogmorhinus
leptonyx</spn>, and <spn>Leptonychotes Weddelli</spn>, of the Antarctic
Ocean. The North Pacific sea leopard is the harbor seal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sealer</ent><br/
<hw>Seal"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who seals;
especially, an officer whose duty it is to seal writs or instruments, to
stamp weights and measures, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sealer</ent><br/
<hw>Sealer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mariner or a vessel engaged in the
business of capturing seals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea letter</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" let"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Mar. Law.)</fld> <def>The
customary certificate of national character which neutral merchant
vessels are bound to carry in time of war; a passport for a vessel and
cargo.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea lettuce</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" let"tuce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The green
papery fronds of several seaweeds of the genus <gen>Ulva</gen>,
sometimes used as food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea level</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" lev"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The level of the surface of the
sea; any surface on the same level with the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selch</ent><br/
<ent>Sealgh</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sealgh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Selch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>
}</mhw>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A seal.</def> <mark>[Scotch]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea lily</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" lil"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
crinoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sealing wax</ent><br/
<hw>Seal"ing wax`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A compound of the resinous
materials, pigments, etc., used as a material for seals, as for letters,
documents, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea lion</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" li"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
several large species of seals of the family <fam>Otariidae</fam> native
of the Pacific Ocean, especially the southern sea lion (<spn>Otaria
jubata</spn>) of the South American coast; the northern sea lion
(<spn>Eumetopias Stelleri</spn>) found from California to Japan; and the
black, or California, sea lion (<spn>Zalophus Californianus</spn>),
which is common on the rocks near San Francisco.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea loach</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" loach"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
three-bearded rockling. See <er>Rockling</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea louse</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" louse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of isopod crustaceans of <gen>Cymothoa</gen>,
<gen>Livoneca</gen>, and allied genera, mostly parasites on
fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sealskin</ent><br/
<hw>Seal"skin`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The skin of a seal;
the pelt of a seal prepared for use, esp. of the fur seal; also, a
garment made of this material.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seam</ent><br/
<hw>Seam</hw> <pr>(s<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Saim</er>.]</ety> <def>Grease; tallow; lard.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au> <au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seam</ent><br/
<hw>Seam</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>seem</ets>, <ets>seam</ets>,
AS. <ets>se<aacute/m</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zoom</ets>, OHG.
<ets>soum</ets>, G. <ets>saum</ets>, LG. <ets>soom</ets>, Icel.
<ets>saumr</ets>, Sw. & Dan. <ets>s<oum/m</ets>, and E. <ets>sew</ets>.
<root/ 156. See <er>Sew</er> to fasten with thread.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The fold or line formed by sewing together two pieces of cloth or
leather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a line of junction; a joint; a suture, as on
a ship, a floor, or other structure; the line of union, or joint, of two
boards, planks, metal plates, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Precepts should be so finely wrought together . . . that no coarse
<qex>seam</qex> may discover where they join.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geol. & Mining)</fld> <def>A thin layer or stratum;
a narrow vein between two thicker strata; <as>as, a <ex>seam</ex> of
coal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A line or depression left by a cut or wound; a scar;
a cicatrix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seam blast</b></col>, <cd>a blast made by putting the
powder into seams or cracks of rocks.</cd> -- <col><b>Seam
lace</b></col>, <cd>a lace used by carriage makers to cover seams and
edges; -- called also <altname>seaming lace</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Seam presser</b></col>. <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A
heavy roller to press down newly plowed furrows</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
tailor's sadiron for pressing seams.</cd> <au>Knight.</au> --
<col><b>Seam set</b></col>, <cd>a set for flattering the seams of metal
sheets, leather work, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seam</ent><br/
<hw>Seam</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Seamed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seaming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To form a seam upon
or of; to join by sewing together; to unite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mark with something resembling a seam; to line;
to scar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Seamed</qex> o'er with wounds which his own saber gave.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make the appearance of a seam in, as in knitting
a stocking; hence, to knit with a certain stitch, like that in such
knitting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seam</ent><br/
<hw>Seam</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become ridgy; to crack
open.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Later their lips began to parch and <qex>seam</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>L. Wallace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seam</ent><br/
<hw>Seam</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>se<aacute/m</ets>, LL.
<ets>sauma</ets>, L. <ets>sagma</ets> a packsaddle, fr. Gr. <?/. See
<er>Sumpter</er>.]</ety> <def>A denomination of weight or measure.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The quantity of eight bushels of
grain.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>seam</xex> of oats.<rdquo/ <au>P.
Plowman.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The quantity of 120 pounds of
glass.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-maid</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-maid`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
mermaid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sea nymph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-mail</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-mail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sea</ets> +
(perhaps) <ets>Mall</ets> Mally, for <ets>Mary</ets>; hence, Prov. E.
<ets>mally</ets> a hare.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A gull; the
mew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaman</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Seamen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A merman; the male of the
mermaid.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Not to mention mermaids or
<xex>seamen</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaman</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Seamen</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[AS.
<ets>saeman</ets>.]</ety> <def>One whose occupation is to assist in the
management of ships at sea; a mariner; a sailor; -- applied both to
officers and common mariners, but especially to the latter. Opposed to
<contr>landman</contr>, or <contr>landsman</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Able seaman</b></col>, <cd>a sailor who is practically
conversant with all the duties of common seamanship.</cd> --
<col><b>Ordinary seaman</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Ordinary</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamanlike</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"man*like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having or
showing the skill of a practical seaman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamanship</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"man*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The skill of a good seaman;
the art, or skill in the art, of working a ship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea mantis</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" man"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
squilla.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1298 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Sea marge</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" marge`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Land which borders on the sea;
the seashore.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You are near the <qex>sea marge</qex> of a land teeming with
life.</q> <rj><qau>J. Burroughs.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamark</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"mark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any elevated object
on land which serves as a guide to mariners; a beacon; a landmark
visible from the sea, as a hill, a tree, a steeple, or the like.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea mat</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" mat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bryozoan of
the genus <gen>Flustra</gen> or allied genera which form frondlike
corals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea maw</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" maw`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The sea
mew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamed</ent><br/
<hw>Seamed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Falconry)</fld>
<def>Out of condition; not in good condition; -- said of a
hawk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-mell</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-mell`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The sea mew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea mew</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" mew`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A gull; the
mew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea mile</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" mile`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A geographical mile. See
<er>Mile</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea milkwort</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" milk"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A low,
fleshy perennial herb (<spn>Glaux maritima</spn>) found along northern
seashores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaming</ent><br/
<hw>Seam"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
or process of forming a seam or joint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fishing)</fld> <def>The cord or rope at the margin
of a seine, to which the meshes of the net are attached.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seaming machine</b></col>, <cd>a machine for uniting the
edges of sheet-metal plates by bending them and pinching them
together.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamless</ent><br/
<hw>Seam"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a seam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Christ's <qex>seamless</qex> coat, all of a piece.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea monk</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" monk`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Monk
seal</er>, under <er>Monk</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea monster</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" mon"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any large
sea animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea moss</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" moss`</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
branched marine bryozoan resembling moss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea mouse</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" mouse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A dorsibranchiate annelid, belonging to <gen>Aphrodite</gen> and
allied genera, having long, slender, hairlike setae on the sides.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The dunlin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamster</ent><br/
<hw>Seam"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Seamstress</er>.]</ety> <def>One who sews well, or whose occupation
is to sew.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamstress</ent><br/
<hw>Seam"stress</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From older
<ets>seamster</ets>, properly fem., AS. <ets>se<aacute/mestre</ets>. See
<er>Seam</er>.]</ety> <def>A woman whose occupation is sewing; a
needlewoman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamstressy</ent><br/
<hw>Seam"stress*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The business of
a seamstress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea mud</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" mud`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A rich slimy deposit in salt
marshes and along the seashore, sometimes used as a manure; -- called
also <altname>sea ooze</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seamy</ent><br/
<hw>Seam"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a seam;
containing seams, or showing them.</def> <ldquo/Many a <xex>seamy</xex>
scar.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Everything has its fair, as well as its <qex>seamy</qex>,
side.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sean</ent><br/
<hw>Sean</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seine. See
<er>Seine</er>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seance</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>S<eacute/`ance"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
L. <ets>sedens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sedere</ets> to
sit. See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <def>A session, as of some public body;
especially, a meeting of spiritualists to receive spirit communications,
so called.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea needle</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" nee"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Garfish</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea nettle</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" net`tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A jellyfish, or
medusa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seannachie</ent><br/
<hw>Sean"na*chie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>seanachaidh</ets>.]</ety> <def>A bard among the Highlanders of
Scotland, who preserved and repeated the traditions of the tribes; also,
a genealogist.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sennachy</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea onion</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" on"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The officinal
squill. See <er>Squill</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea ooze</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ooze`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Same as <cref>Sea
mud</cref>.</def> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea orange</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" or"ange</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large
American holothurian (<spn>Lophothuria Fabricii</spn>) having a bright
orange convex body covered with finely granulated scales. Its expanded
tentacles are bright red.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-orb</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-orb`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
globefish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea otter</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ot"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An aquatic
carnivore (<spn>Enhydris lutris</spn> syn. <spn>Enhydris marina</spn>)
found in the <geog>North Pacific Ocean</geog>. Its fur is highly valued,
especially by the Chinese. It is allied to the common otter, but is
larger, with feet more decidedly webbed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sea-otter's cabbage</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
gigantic kelp of the Pacific Ocean (<spn>Nereocystis Lutkeana</spn>).
See <er>Nereocystis</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea owl</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" owl`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" oul`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
lumpfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pad</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pad`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" p<acr/d`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A starfish.</def></p>

<p><ent>Sea parrot</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" par"rot</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" p<acr/r"r<ucr/t)</pr>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The puffin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea partridge</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" par"tridge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
gilthead (<spn>Crenilabrus melops</spn>), a fish of the British
coasts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pass</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A document carried by neutral
merchant vessels in time of war, to show their nationality; a sea letter
or passport. See <er>Passport</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea peach</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" peach`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A beautiful
American ascidian (<spn>Cynthia pyriformis</spn> syn. <spn>Halocynthia
pyriformis</spn>) having the size, form, velvety surface, and color of a
ripe peach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pear</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pear`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" p<acir/r`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A pedunculated ascidian of the genus <gen>Boltonia</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-pen</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-pen"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
pennatula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea perch</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" perch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The European bass (<spn>Roccus lupus</spn> syn. <spn>Labrax
lupus</spn>); -- called also <altname>sea dace</altname>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The cunner.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The sea
bass.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The name is applied also to other species
of fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pheasant</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pheas"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
pintail duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pie</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pie</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" p<imac/`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The oyster catcher, a limicoline bird of the genus
<gen>Haematopus</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pie</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pie`</hw>. <def>A dish of crust or pastry and meat or fish,
etc., cooked together in alternate layers, -- a common food of sailors;
<as>as, a three-decker <ex>sea pie</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seapiece</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"piece`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A picture
representing a scene at sea; a marine picture.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea piet</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pi"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See 1st
<er>Sea pie</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pig</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pig`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
porpoise or dolphin.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A dugong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pigeon</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pi"geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The common guillemot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pike</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pike`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The garfish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A large serranoid food fish
(<spn>Centropomus undecimalis</spn>) found on both coasts of America; --
called also <altname>robalo</altname>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
merluce.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pincushion</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pin`cush`ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A sea purse.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A pentagonal
starfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pink</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pink`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Thrift</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea plover</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" plov"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>The black-bellied
plover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea poker</ent><br/
<ent>Sea poacher</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sea" poach"er</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" p<omac/ch"<etil/r)</pr>.
<hw>Sea" pok"er</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" p<omac/k"<etil/r)</pr>. }</mhw>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The lyrie.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pool</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pool`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A pool of salt water.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea poppy</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pop"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The horn
poppy. See under <er>Horn</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea porcupine</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" por"cu*pine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any fish
of the genus <gen>Diodon</gen>, and allied genera, whose body is covered
with spines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pork</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pork`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An American
compound ascidian (<spn>Amoroecium stellatum</spn>) which forms large
whitish masses resembling salt pork.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaport</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"port`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"p<omac/rt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
port on the seashore, or one accessible for seagoing vessels. Also used
adjectively; <as>as, a <ex>seaport</ex> town</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seapoy</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"poy</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"poy)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sepoy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pudding</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pud"ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any large
holothurian.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea purse</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" purse`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/" p<ucir/rs`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The horny egg case of a skate, and of certain sharks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea purslane</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" purs"lane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See under
<er>Purslane</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pye</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" pye`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See 1st <er>Sea
pie</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea pyot</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" py"ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See 1st
<er>Sea pie</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea quail</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" quail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
turnstone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaquake</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"quake`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"kw<amac/k`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
quaking of the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sere</ent><br/
<ent>Sear</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sear</hw>, <hw>Sere</hw> <pr>(s<emac/r)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>seer</ets>, AS. <ets>se<aacute/r</ets>
(assumed) fr. <ets>se<aacute/rian</ets> to wither; akin to D.
<ets>zoor</ets> dry, LG. <ets>soor</ets>, OHG. <ets>sor<emac/n</ets> to
wither, Gr. <grk>a"y`ein</grk> to parch, to dry, Skr.
<ets><cced/ush</ets> (for <ets>sush</ets>) to dry, to wither, Zend
<ets>hush</ets> to dry. <root/152. Cf. <er>Austere</er>,
<er>Sorrel</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Dry; withered; no longer
green; -- applied to leaves.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have lived long enough; my way of life<br/
Is fall'n into the <qex>sear</qex>, the yellow leaf.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sear</ent><br/
<hw>Sear</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Seared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Searing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>seeren</ets>, AS.
<ets>se<aacute/rian</ets>. See <er>Sear</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To wither; to dry up.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To burn (the surface of) to dryness and hardness; to
cauterize; to expose to a degree of heat such as changes the color or
the hardness and texture of the surface; to scorch; to make callous;
<as>as, to <ex>sear</ex> the skin or flesh</as>. Also used
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'm <qex>seared</qex> with burning steel.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was in vain that the amiable divine tried to give salutary pain
to that <qex>seared</qex> conscience.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The discipline of war, being a discipline in destruction of life,
is a discipline in callousness. Whatever sympathies exist are
<qex>seared</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>H. Spencer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sear</xex> is allied to <xex>scorch</xex> in
signification; but it is applied primarily to animal flesh, and has
special reference to the effect of heat in marking the surface
<xex>hard</xex>. <xex>Scorch</xex> is applied to flesh, cloth, or any
other substance, and has no reference to the effect of
hardness.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sear up</b></col>, <cd>to close by searing.</cd>
<ldquo/Cherish veins of good humor, and <xex>sear</xex> up those of
ill.<rdquo/ <au>Sir W. Temple.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sear</ent><br/
<hw>Sear</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>serre</ets> a grasp,
pressing, fr. L. <ets>sera</ets>. See <er>Serry</er>.]</ety> <def>The
catch in a gunlock by which the hammer is held cocked or half
cocked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sear spring</b></col>, <cd>the spring which causes the
sear to catch in the notches by which the hammer is held.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea rat</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" rat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pirate.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The chimaera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea raven</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ra"ven</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>An American cottoid fish (<spn>Hemitripterus Americanus</spn>)
allied to the sculpins, found on the northern Atlantic coasts.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The cormorant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searce</ent><br/
<hw>Searce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sarse</er>.]</ety> <def>A fine sieve.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searce</ent><br/
<hw>Searce</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To sift; to bolt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searcer</ent><br/
<hw>Sear"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
sifts or bolts.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A searce, or sieve.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Search</ent><br/
<hw>Search</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Searched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Searching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>serchen</ets>, <ets>cerchen</ets>, OF. <ets>cerchier</ets>, F.
<ets>chercher</ets>, L. <ets>circare</ets> to go about, fr. L.
<ets>circum</ets>, <ets>circa</ets>, around. See <er>Circle</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To look over or through, for the purpose of finding
something; to examine; to explore; <as>as, to <ex>search</ex> the
city</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Search</xex> the Scriptures.<rdquo/
<rj><au>John v. 39.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are come to <qex>search</qex> the house.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Search</qex> me, O God, and know my heart.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
cxxxix. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inquire after; to look for; to seek.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will both <qex>search</qex> my sheep, and seek them out.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. xxxiv. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Enough is left besides to <qex>search</qex> and know.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To examine or explore by feeling with an instrument;
to probe; <as>as, to <ex>search</ex> a wound</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To examine; to try; to put to the test.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To search out</b></col>, <cd>to seek till found; to find
by seeking; <as>as, to <ex>search out</ex> truth</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To explore; examine; scrutinize; seek;
investigate; pry into; inquire.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Search</ent><br/
<hw>Search</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To seek; to look for something;
to make inquiry, exploration, or examination; to hunt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Once more <qex>search</qex> with me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It sufficeth that they have once with care sifted the matter, and
<qex>searched</qex> into all the particulars.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Search</ent><br/
<hw>Search</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>cerche</ets>. See
<er>Search</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>The act of seeking or
looking for something; quest; inquiry; pursuit for finding something;
examination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus the orb he roamed<br/
With narrow <qex>search</qex>, and with inspection deep<br/
Considered every creature.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor did my <qex>search</qex> of liberty begin<br/
Till my black hairs were changed upon my chin.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Right of search</b></col> <fld>(Mar. Law)</fld>, <cd>the
right of the lawfully commissioned cruisers of belligerent nations to
examine and search private merchant vessels on the high seas, for the
enemy's property or for articles contraband of war.</cd> --
<col><b>Search warrant</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a warrant legally
issued, authorizing an examination or search of a house, or other place,
for goods stolen, secreted, or concealed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Scrutiny; examination; exploration;
investigation; research; inquiry; quest; pursuit.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searchable</ent><br/
<hw>Search"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
searched.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searchableness</ent><br/
<hw>Search"a*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
searchable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searcher</ent><br/
<hw>Search"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>cercheor</ets> inspector.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which,
searches or examines; a seeker; an inquirer; an examiner; a trier.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Formerly, an officer in London appointed
to examine the bodies of the dead, and report the cause of death.</def>
<au>Graunt.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An officer of the customs whose
business it is to search ships, merchandise, luggage, etc.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>An inspector of leather.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Gun.)</fld> <def>An instrument for
examining the bore of a cannon, to detect cavities.</def> <sd>(e)</sd>
<def>An implement for sampling butter; a butter trier.</def>
<sd>(j)</sd> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An instrument for feeling after
calculi in the bladder, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searching</ent><br/
<hw>Search"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Exploring thoroughly;
scrutinizing; penetrating; trying; <as>as, a <ex>searching</ex>
discourse; a <ex>searching</ex> eye</as>.</def> <ldquo/Piercing,
<xex>searching</xex>, biting, cold.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Search"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Search"ing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searchless</ent><br/
<hw>Search"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Impossible to be searched;
inscrutable; impenetrable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searchlight</ent><br/
<hw>Search"light`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An
apparatus for projecting a powerful beam of light of approximately
parallel rays, usually devised so that it can be swiveled about.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The beam of light projecting by this
apparatus.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searcloth</ent><br/
<hw>Sear"cloth`</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Cerecloth.</def> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searcloth</ent><br/
<hw>Sear"cloth</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cover, as a sore, with
cerecloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seared</ent><br/
<hw>Seared</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Scorched; cauterized;
hence, figuratively, insensible; not susceptible to moral
influences.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>seared</qex> conscience and a remorseless heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Searedness</ent><br/
<hw>Sear"ed*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being seared or callous; insensibility.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea reed</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" reed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The sea-sand
reed. See under <er>Reed</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea risk</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" risk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Risk of injury, destruction, or
loss by the sea, or while at sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea robber</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" rob"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A pirate; a sea
rover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea robin</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" rob"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See under <er>Robin</er>, and
<xex>Illustration</xex> in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea rocket</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" rock"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>.<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See under
<er>Rocket</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea room</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" room`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Room or space
at sea for a vessel to maneuver, drive, or scud, without peril of
running ashore or aground.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea rover</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" rov"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>One that cruises or roves the
sea for plunder; a sea robber; a pirate; also, a piratical
vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-roving</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-rov"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cruising at random on the
ocean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea salmon</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" salm"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A young pollock.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The spotted
squeteague.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>See <er>Sea bass</er>
<sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea salt</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" salt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Common salt, obtained from sea
water by evaporation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea sandpiper</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" sand"pi`per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
purple sandpiper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea sandwort</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" sand"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Sea chickweed</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea saurian</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" sau"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any marine saurian; esp. <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>, the large extinct
species of <gen>Mosasaurus</gen>, <gen>Ichthyosaurus</gen>,
<gen>Plesiosaurus</gen>, and related genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seascape</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"scape</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Landscape</er>.]</ety> <def>A picture representing a scene at sea.
Compare <er>landscape</er>.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1299 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Sea scorpion</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" scor"pi*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A European sculpin (<spn>Cottus scorpius</spn>) having the head
armed with short spines.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The scorpene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea scurf</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" scurf`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bryozoan
which forms rounded or irregular patches of coral on stones, seaweeds,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea serpent</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ser"pent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any marine snake. See <er>Sea snake</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large marine animal of unknown
nature, often reported to have been seen at sea, but never yet
captured.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Many accounts of sea serpents are imaginary or
fictitious; others are greatly exaggerated and distorted by incompetent
observers; but a number have been given by competent and trustworthy
persons, which indicate that several diverse animals have been called
sea serpents. Among these are, apparently, several large snakelike
fishes, as the oar fish, or ribbon fish (<gen>Regalecus</gen>), and huge
conger eels. Other accounts probably refer to the giant squids
(<gen>Architeuthis</gen>). Some of the best accounts seem to describe a
marine saurian, like the fossil Mosasauri, which were large serpentlike
creatures with paddles.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seashell</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"shell`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The shell of any marine mollusk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seashore</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"shore`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
coast of the sea; the land that lies adjacent to the sea or
ocean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>All the ground between the ordinary
high-water and low-water marks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seasick</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"sick`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected with
seasickness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seasickness</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"sick`ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The peculiar sickness,
characterized by nausea and prostration, which is caused by the pitching
or rolling of a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaside</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"side`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The land bordering
on, or adjacent to, the sea; the seashore. Also used
adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea slater</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" slat"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any isopod
crustacean of the genus <gen>Ligia</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea slug</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" slug`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
holothurian.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A nudibranch mollusk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea snail</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" snail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A small fish of the genus <gen>Liparis</gen>, having a ventral
sucker. It lives among stones and seaweeds.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any
small creeping marine gastropod, as the species of <gen>Littorina</gen>,
<gen>Natica</gen>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea snake</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" snake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
many species of venomous aquatic snakes of the family
<fam>Hydrophidae</fam>, having a flattened tail and living entirely in
the sea, especially in the warmer parts of the Indian and Pacific
Oceans. They feed upon fishes, and are mostly of moderate size, but some
species become eight or ten feet long and four inches broad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea snipe</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" snipe`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A sandpiper, as the knot and dunlin.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
bellows fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Season</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sesoun</ets>, F. <ets>saison</ets>, properly, the sowing time, fr.
L. <ets>satio</ets> a sowing, a planting, fr. <ets>serere</ets>,
<ets>satum</ets>, to sow, plant; akin to E. <ets>sow</ets>, v., to
scatter, as seed.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the divisions of the
year, marked by alterations in the length of day and night, or by
distinct conditions of temperature, moisture, etc., caused mainly by the
relative position of the earth with respect to the sun. In the north
temperate zone, four seasons, namely, spring, summer, autumn, and
winter, are generally recognized. Some parts of the world have three
seasons, -- the dry, the rainy, and the cold; other parts have but two,
-- the dry and the rainy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The several <qex>seasons</qex> of the year in their beauty.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a period of time, especially as regards its
fitness for anything contemplated or done; a suitable or convenient
time; proper conjuncture; <as>as, the <ex>season</ex> for planting; the
<ex>season</ex> for rest</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>season</qex>, prime for sweetest scents and airs.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A period of time not very long; a while; a
time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt be blind, not seeing the sun for a
<qex>season</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xiii. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which gives relish; seasoning.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You lack the <qex>season</qex> of all natures, sleep.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In season</b></col>, <cd>in good time, or sufficiently
early for the purpose.</cd> -- <col><b>Out of season</b></col>,
<cd>beyond or out of the proper time or the usual or appointed
time.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Season</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Seasoned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seasoning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To render suitable
or appropriate; to prepare; to fit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is fit and <qex>seasoned</qex> for his passage.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fit for any use by time or habit; to habituate;
to accustom; to inure; to ripen; to mature; <as>as, to <ex>season</ex>
one to a climate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, to prepare by drying or hardening, or removal
of natural juices; <as>as, to <ex>season</ex> timber</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To fit for taste; to render palatable; to give zest
or relish to; to spice; <as>as, to <ex>season</ex> food</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Hence, to fit for enjoyment; to render
agreeable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You <qex>season</qex> still with sports your serious hours.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The proper use of wit is to <qex>season</qex> conversation.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To qualify by admixture; to moderate; to
temper.</def> <ldquo/When mercy <xex>seasons</xex> justice.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To imbue; to tinge or taint.</def> <ldquo/Who by his
tutor being <xex>seasoned</xex> with the love of the truth.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Season</qex> their younger years with prudent and pious
principles.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To copulate with; to impregnate.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Season</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
become mature; to grow fit for use; to become adapted to a
climate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To become dry and hard, by the escape of the natural
juices, or by being penetrated with other substance; <as>as, timber
<ex>seasons</ex> in the sun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To give token; to savor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seasonable</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Occurring in
good time, in due season, or in proper time for the purpose; suitable to
the season; opportune; timely; <as>as, a <ex>seasonable</ex> supply of
rain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mercy is <qex>seasonable</qex> in the time of affliction.</q>
<rj><qau>Ecclus. xxxv. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sea"son*a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Sea"son*a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seasonage</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seasoning.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seasonal</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the seasons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Occurring or being used in a specific season;
<as>as, <ex>seasonal</ex> items for sale</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seasonal dimorphism</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
condition of having two distinct varieties which appear at different
seasons, as certain species of butterflies in which the spring brood
differs from the summer or autumnal brood.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>seasonal affective disorder</ent><br/
<hw>seasonal affective disorder</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
form of mild depression that occurs in winters, associated with
reduction in the amount of sunlight. It is characterized by
oversleeping, irritability, and sometimes overeating. It can be treated
by light therapy and usually disappears with the arrival of
spring.</def> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seasoner</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, seasons, or gives a relish; a seasoning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seasoning</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process
by which anything is seasoned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is added to any species of food, to give
it a higher relish, as salt, spices, etc.; a condiment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, something added to enhance enjoyment or
relieve dullness; <as>as, wit is the <ex>seasoning</ex> of
conversation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Political speculations are of so dry and austere a nature, that
they will not go down with the public without frequent
<qex>seasonings</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seasoning tub</b></col> <fld>(Bakery)</fld>, <cd>a trough
in which dough is set to rise.</cd> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seasonless</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"son*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without succession of the
seasons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea spider</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" spi"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any maioid crab; a spider crab. See <er>Maioid</er>, and
<cref>Spider crab</cref>, under <er>Spider</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Any pycnogonid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea squirt</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" squirt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
ascidian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea star</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" star`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A starfish, or
brittle star.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea surgeon</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" sur"geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A surgeon
fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea swallow</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" swal"low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The common tern.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The storm
petrel.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The gannet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>See <cref>Cornish chough</cref>,
under <er>Chough</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seat</ent><br/
<hw>Seat</hw> <pr>(s<emac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sete</ets>, Icel. <ets>saeti</ets>; akin to Sw.
<ets>s<aum/te</ets>, Dan. <ets>saede</ets>, MHG. <ets>s<amac/ze</ets>,
AS. <ets>set</ets>, <ets>setl</ets>, and E. <ets>sit</ets>. <root/154.
See <er>Sit</er>, and cf. <er>Settle</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The place or thing upon which one sits; hence; anything
made to be sat in or upon, as a chair, bench, stool, saddle, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And Jesus . . . overthrew the tables of the money changers, and
the <qex>seats</qex> of them that sold doves.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. xxi.
12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The place occupied by anything, or where any person
or thing is situated, resides, or abides; a site; an abode, a station; a
post; a situation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where thou dwellest, even where Satan's <qex>seat</qex> is.</q>
<rj><qau>Rev. ii. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that builds a fair house upon an ill <qex>seat</qex> committeth
himself to prison.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>seat</qex> of plenty, content, and tranquillity.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That part of a thing on which a person sits; <as>as,
the <ex>seat</ex> of a chair or saddle; the <ex>seat</ex> of a pair of
pantaloons</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sitting; a right to sit; regular or appropriate
place of sitting; <as>as, a <ex>seat</ex> in a church; a <ex>seat</ex>
for the season in the opera house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Posture, or way of sitting, on horseback.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She had so good a <qex>seat</qex> and hand she might be trusted
with any mount.</q> <rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A part or surface on which
another part or surface rests; <as>as, a valve
<ex>seat</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seat worm</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
pinworm.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seat</ent><br/
<hw>Seat</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Seated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place on a seat;
to cause to sit down; <as>as, to <ex>seat</ex> one's
self</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The guests were no sooner <qex>seated</qex> but they entered into
a warm debate.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to occupy a post, site, situation, or the
like; to station; to establish; to fix; to settle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus high . . . is King Richard <qex>seated</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They had <qex>seated</qex> themselves in New Guiana.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To assign a seat to, or the seats of; to give a
sitting to; <as>as, to <ex>seat</ex> a church, or persons in a
church</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To fix; to set firm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From their foundations, loosening to and fro,<br/
They plucked the <qex>seated</qex> hills.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To settle; to plant with inhabitants; as to
<xex>seat</xex> a country.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>W.
Stith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To put a seat or bottom in; <as>as, to <ex>seat</ex>
a chair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seat</ent><br/
<hw>Seat</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To rest; to lie down.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea tang</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" tang`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of
seaweed; tang; tangle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To their nests of sedge and <qex>sea tang</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea term</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" term`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A term used specifically by
seamen; a nautical word or phrase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea thief</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" thief`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A pirate.</def>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea thongs</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" thongs`</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind
of blackish seaweed (<spn>Himanthalia lorea</spn>) found on the northern
coasts of the Atlantic. It has a thonglike forking process rising from a
top-shaped base.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seating</ent><br/
<hw>Seat"ing</hw> <pr>(s<emac/t"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of providing with a seat or seats; <as>as, the
<ex>seating</ex> of an audience</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of making seats; also, the material for
making seats; <as>as, cane <ex>seating</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea titling</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" tit"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The rock
pipit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seatless</ent><br/
<hw>Seat"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no
seat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea toad</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" toad`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
sculpin.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A toadfish.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
angler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea trout</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" trout`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any one of several species of true trouts which descend rivers and
enter the sea after spawning, as the European bull trout and salmon
trout, and the eastern American spotted trout.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The common squeteague, and the spotted squeteague.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A California fish of the family <fam>Chiridae</fam>,
especially <spn>Hexagrammus decagrammus</spn>; -- called also
<altname>spotted rock trout</altname>. See <cref>Rock trout</cref>,
under <er>Rock</er>.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A California sciaenoid fish
(<spn>Cynoscion nobilis</spn>); -- called also <altname>white sea
bass</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea trumpet</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" trum"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A great blackish seaweed of the Southern Ocean, having a hollow and
expanding stem and a pinnate frond, sometimes twenty feet
long.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any large marine univalve shell
of the genus Triton. See <er>Triton</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea turn</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" turn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A breeze, gale, or mist from the
sea.</def> <rj><au>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea turtle</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" tur"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any one of several very large species of chelonians having the feet
converted into paddles, as the green turtle, hawkbill, loggerhead, and
leatherback. They inhabit all warm seas.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sea
pigeon, or guillemot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea unicorn</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" u"ni*corn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
narwhal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea urchin</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" ur"chin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of echinoderms of the order
<ord>Echinoidea</ord>.</def> <note>When living they are covered with
movable spines which are often long and sharp.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seave</ent><br/
<hw>Seave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>siv</ets>, Sw. <ets>s<aum/f</ets>, Icel. <ets>sef</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A rush.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seavy</ent><br/
<hw>Seav`y</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Overgrown with rushes.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea wall</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" wall`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<ae/weall</ets>.]</ety> <def>A wall, or embankment, to resist
encroachments of the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea-walled</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"-walled`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Surrounded,
bounded, or protected by the sea, as if by a wall.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seawant</ent><br/
<ent>Seawan</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sea"wan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sea"want</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The name used by the Algonquin Indians for
the shell beads which passed among the Indians as money.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ex>Seawan</ex> was of two kinds; <stype>wampum</stype>,
white, and <stype>suckanhock</stype>, black or purple, -- the former
having half the value of the latter. Many writers, however, use the
terms <ex>seawan</ex> and <stype>wampum</stype> indiscriminately.</note>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seawand</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"wand`</hw> . <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See <er>Sea
girdles</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaward</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Directed or situated
toward the sea.</def> <rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Two still clouds . . . sparkled on their <qex>seaward</qex> edges
like a frosted fleece.</q> <rj><qau>G. W. Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaward</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"ward</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the sea.</def>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaware</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"ware`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS.
<ets>s<aemac/w<amac/r</ets> seaweed.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Seaweed; esp., coarse seaweed. See <er>Ware</er>, and <er>Sea
girdles</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaweed</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Popularly, any plant or plants growing in the sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any marine plant of the class
Algae, as kelp, dulse, <gen>Fucus</gen>, <gen>Ulva</gen>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea whip</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" whip`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A gorgonian
having a simple stem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea widgeon</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" wid"geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The scaup duck.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The pintail duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seawife</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"wife`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Seawives</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
European wrasse (<spn>Labrus vetula</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea willow</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" wil"low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A gorgonian
coral with long flexible branches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea wing</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" wing`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A wing shell
(<gen>Avicula</gen>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea withwind</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" with"wind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of
bindweed (<spn>Convolvulus Soldanella</spn>) growing on the seacoast of
Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea wolf</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" wolf`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The wolf fish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The European sea
perch.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The sea elephant.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>A sea lion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea woodcock</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" wood"cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
bar-tailed godwit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea wood louse</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" wood" louse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sea
slater.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea wormwood</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" worm"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A European
species of wormwood (<spn>Artemisia maritima</spn>) growing by the
sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaworthiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"wor`thi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or
quality of being seaworthy, or able to resist the ordinary violence of
wind and weather.</def> <rj><au>Kent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seaworthy</ent><br/
<hw>Sea"wor`thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Fit for a voyage;
worthy of being trusted to transport a cargo with safety; <as>as, a
<ex>seaworthy</ex> ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sea wrack</ent><br/
<hw>Sea" wrack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Wrack</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sebaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ba"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>sebaceus</ets>, from L. <ets>sebum</ets> tallow, grease.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or secreting, fat; composed of
fat; having the appearance of fat; <as>as, the <ex>sebaceous</ex>
secretions of some plants, or the <ex>sebaceous</ex> humor of
animals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sebaceous cyst</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a cyst
formed by distention of a sebaceous gland, due to obstruction of its
excretory duct.</cd> -- <col><b>Sebaceous glands</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>small subcutaneous glands, usually connected
with hair follicles. They secrete an oily semifluid matter, composed in
great part of fat, which softens and lubricates the hair and
skin.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sebacic</ent><br/
<hw>Se*bac"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sebum</ets>
tallow: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/bacique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to fat; derived from, or resembling, fat;
specifically, designating an acid (formerly called also
<altname>sebic</altname>, and <altname>pyroleic</altname>, acid),
obtained by the distillation or saponification of certain oils (as
castor oil) as a white crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sebat</ent><br/
<hw>Se"bat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>sh<ecr/b<amac/t</ets>.]</ety> <def>The eleventh month of the
ancient Hebrew year, approximately corresponding with February.</def>
<rj><au>W. Smith (Bibl. Dict.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sebate</ent><br/
<hw>Se"bate</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"b<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sebacic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sebesten</ent><br/
<hw>Se*bes"ten</hw> <pr>(s<esl/*b<ecr/s"t<ecr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Ar. <ets>sebest<amac/n</ets> the tree: cf. Sp.
<ets>sebesten</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The mucilaginous
drupaceous fruit of two East Indian trees (<spn>Cordia Myxa</spn>, and
<spn>Cordia latifolia</spn>), sometimes used medicinally in pectoral
diseases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the West Indies the name is given to the similar
fruit of <spn>Cordia Sebestana</spn>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1300 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Sebic</ent><br/
<hw>Se"bic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sebacic</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sebiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*bif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sebum</ets> tallow + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Producing vegetable tallow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Producing fat; sebaceous;
<as>as, the <ex>sebiferous</ex>, or sebaceous, glands</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sebiparous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*bip"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sebum</ets> tallow + <ets>parere</ets> to bring forth.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Sebiferous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seborrhea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Seb"or*rhe*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>sebum</ets> tallow + Gr. <?/ to flow.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A morbidly increased discharge of sebaceous matter upon the skin;
stearrhea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secale</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*ca"le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a kind of
grain.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of cereal grasses including
rye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secancy</ent><br/
<hw>Se"can*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Secant</er>.]</ety> <def>A cutting; an intersection; <as>as, the
point of <ex>secancy</ex> of one line by another</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Davies & Peck (Math. Dict.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secant</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cant</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"k<acr/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>secare</ets> to
cut. See <er>Section</er>.]</ety> <def>Cutting; dividing into two parts;
<as>as, a <ex>secant</ex> line</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secant</ent><br/
<hw>Secant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/cante</ets>.
See <er>Secant</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>A line that cuts another; especially, a straight line cutting a
curve in two or more points.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Trig.)</fld> <def>A right line drawn from the
center of a circle through one end of a circular arc, and terminated by
a tangent drawn from the other end; the number expressing the ratio of
this line to the radius of the circle. See <cref>Trigonometrical
function</cref>, under <er>Function</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secco</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sec"co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>Dry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Secco painting</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Painting in secco</b></col></mcol>, <cd>painting on dry plaster,
as distinguished from <contr>fresco painting</contr>, which is on wet or
fresh plaster.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secede</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cede"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Seceded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seceding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>secedere</ets>,
<ets>secessum</ets>; pref <ets>se-</ets> aside + <ets>cedere</ets> to
go, move. See <er>Cede</er>.]</ety> <def>To withdraw from fellowship,
communion, or association; to separate one's self by a solemn act; to
draw off; to retire; especially, to withdraw from a political or
religious body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seceder</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ced"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
secedes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of a numerous body of
Presbyterians in Scotland who seceded from the communion of the
Established Church, about the year 1733, and formed the Secession
Church, so called.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secern</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cern"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Secerned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Secerning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>secernere</ets>. See <er>Secrete</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
separate; to distinguish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Averroes <qex>secerns</qex> a sense of titillation, and a sense of
hunger and thirst.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>To secrete; <as>as, mucus
<ex>secerned</ex> in the nose</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secernent</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cern"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secernens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>Secreting; secretory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secernent</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cern"ent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which promotes
secretion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A vessel in, or by means of,
which the process of secretion takes place; a secreting
vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secernment</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cern"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>The act or process of secreting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secess</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cess"</hw> <pr>(s<esl/*s<ecr/s")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secessus</ets>. See <er>Secede</er>.]</ety> <def>Retirement;
retreat; secession.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secession</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ces"sion</hw> <pr>(s<esl/*s<ecr/sh"<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>secessio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/cession</ets>. See
<er>Secede</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of seceding; separation
from fellowship or association with others, as in a religious or
political organization; withdrawal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(U.S. Hist.)</fld> <def>The withdrawal of a State
from the national Union.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Secession Church</b></col><cd> (in Scotland). See
<er>Seceder</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secessionism</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ces"sion*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The doctrine
or policy of secession; the tenets of secession; the tenets of
secessionists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secessionist</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ces"sion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who upholds
secession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(U.S. Hist.)</fld> <def>One who holds to the belief
that a State has the right to separate from the Union at its
will.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seche</ent><br/
<hw>Seche</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To seek.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sechium</ent><br/
<hw>Se"chi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/chion</ets>; perhaps formed fr. Gr. <?/ cucumber.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The edible fruit of a West Indian plant
(<spn>Sechium edule</spn>) of the Gourd family. It is soft, pear-shaped,
and about four inches long, and contains a single large seed. The root
of the plant resembles a yam, and is used for food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seck</ent><br/
<hw>Seck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sec</ets>,
properly, dry, L. <ets>siccus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Barren; unprofitable.
See <cref>Rent seck</cref>, under <er>Rent</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seckel</ent><br/
<hw>Seck"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
small reddish brown sweet and juicy pear. It originated on a farm near
Philadelphia, afterwards owned by a Mr. <etsep>Seckel</etsep>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secle</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>saeculum</ets>: cf. F. <ets>si<egrave/cle</ets>. See
<er>Secular</er>.]</ety> <def>A century.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seclude</ent><br/
<hw>Se*clude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Secluded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Secluding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>secludere</ets>,
<ets>seclusum</ets>; pref. <ets>se-</ets> aside + <ets>claudere</ets> to
shut. See <er>Close</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
shut up apart from others; to withdraw into, or place in, solitude; to
separate from society or intercourse with others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let Eastern tyrants from the light of heaven<br/
<qex>Seclude</qex> their bosom slaves.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To shut or keep out; to exclude.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Se*clud"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Se*clud"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seclusion</ent><br/
<hw>Se*clu"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Seclude</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of secluding, or the state of
being secluded; separation from society or connection; a withdrawing;
privacy; <as>as, to live in <ex>seclusion</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O blest <qex>seclusion</qex> from a jarring world, which he, thus
occupied, enjoys!</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Solitude; separation; withdrawment; retirement;
privacy. See <er>Solitude</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seclusive</ent><br/
<hw>Se*clu"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to
seclude; keeping in seclusion; secluding; sequestering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Second</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>secundus</ets> second, properly, following, fr. <ets>sequi</ets> to
follow. See <er>Sue</er> to follow, and cf. <er>Secund</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Immediately following the first; next to the first in
order of place or time; hence, occurring again; another;
other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And he slept and dreamed the <qex>second</qex> time.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xli. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Next to the first in value, power, excellence,
dignity, or rank; secondary; subordinate; inferior.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>May the day when we become the <qex>second</qex> people upon earth
. . . be the day of our utter extirpation.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Being of the same kind as another that has preceded;
another, like a prototype; <as>as, a <ex>second</ex> Cato; a
<ex>second</ex> Troy; a <ex>second</ex> deluge</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A Daniel, still say I, a <qex>second</qex> Daniel!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Second Adventist</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Adventist</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Second cousin</b></col>, <cd>the
child of a cousin.</cd> -- <col><b>Second-cut file</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>File</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Second distance</b></col>
<fld>(Art)</fld>, <cd>that part of a picture between the foreground and
the background; -- called also <altname>middle ground</altname>, or
<altname>middle distance</altname>.</cd> <mark>[R.]</mark> --
<col><b>Second estate</b></col> <fld>(Eng.)</fld>, <cd>the House of
Peers.</cd> -- <col><b>Second girl</b></col>, <cd>a female house-servant
who does the lighter work, as chamber work or waiting on table.</cd> --
<col><b>Second intention</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Intention</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Second story</b></col>,
<col><b>Second floor</b></col></mcol>, <cd>in America, the second range
of rooms from the street level. This, in England, is called the
<altname>first floor</altname>, the one beneath being the <contr>ground
floor</contr>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Second thought</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>Second thoughts</b></col></mcol>, <cd>consideration
of a matter following a first impulse or impression;
reconsideration.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On <qex>second thoughts</qex>, gentlemen, I don't wish you had
known him.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Second</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, follows, or comes after; one next and inferior in place,
time, rank, importance, excellence, or power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Man<br/
An angel's <qex>second</qex>, nor his <qex>second</qex> long.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who follows or attends another for his support
and aid; a backer; an assistant; specifically, one who acts as another's
aid in a duel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Being sure enough of <qex>seconds</qex> after the first onset.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Aid; assistance; help.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give <qex>second</qex>, and my love<br/
Is everlasting thine.</q> <rj><qau>J. Fletcher.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>An article of merchandise of a
grade inferior to the best; esp., a coarse or inferior kind of
flour.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <ety>[F. <ets>seconde</ets>. See <er>Second</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>The sixtieth part of a minute of time or of a
minute of space, that is, the <xex>second</xex> regular subdivision of
the degree; <as>as, sound moves about 1,140 English feet in a
<ex>second</ex>; five minutes and ten <ex>seconds</ex> north of this
place.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>In the duodecimal system of mensuration, the twelfth
part of an inch or prime; a line. See <er>Inch</er>, and <er>Prime</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 8.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The interval between
any tone and the tone which is represented on the degree of the staff
next above it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The second part in a concerted
piece; -- often popularly applied to the alto.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Parliamentary Procedure)</fld> <def>A motion in
support of another motion which has been moved in a deliberative body;
<as> a motion without a <ex>second</ex> dies without
discussion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Second hand</b></col>, <cd>the hand which marks the
seconds on the dial of a watch or a clock.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Second</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Seconded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seconding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>seconder</ets>, L.
<ets>secundare</ets>, from <ets>secundus</ets>. See <er>Second</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To follow in the next place; to
succeed; to alternate.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the method of nature, a low valley is immediately
<qex>seconded</qex> with an ambitious hill.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sin is <qex>seconded</qex> with sin.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To follow or attend for the purpose of assisting; to
support; to back; to act as the second of; to assist; to forward; to
encourage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have supplies to <qex>second</qex> our attempt.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In human works though labored on with pain,<br/
A thousand movements scarce one purpose gain;<br/
In God's, one single can its end produce,<br/
Yet serves to <qex>second</qex> too some other use.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Specifically,</specif> <fld>(Parliamentary
Procedure)</fld> <def>to support, as a motion{6} or proposal, by adding
one's voice to that of the mover or proposer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Under common parliamentary rules used by many
organizations, especially legislative bodies, a motion must be
<ex>seconded</ex> in order to come properly before the deliberative body
for discussion. Any motion{6} for which there is no <ex>second{8}</ex>
dies for lack thereof.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secondarily</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond*a*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>In a secondary manner or degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Secondly; in the second place.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God hath set some in the church, first apostles,
<qex>secondarily</qex> prophets, thirdly teachers.</q> <rj><qau>1 Cor.
xii. 28.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secondariness</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond*a*ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
secondary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Full of a girl's sweet sense of <qex>secondariness</qex> to the
object of her love.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs. Oliphant.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secondary</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>secondaire</ets>, L. <ets>secundaire</ets>. See <er>Second</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Succeeding next in order to the
first; of second place, origin, rank, etc.; not primary; subordinate;
not of the first order or rate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wheresoever there is moral right on the one hand, no
<qex>secondary</qex> right can discharge it.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Two are the radical differences; the <qex>secondary</qex>
differences are as four.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Acting by deputation or delegated authority; <as>as,
the work of <ex>secondary</ex> hands</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Possessing some quality, or
having been subject to some operation (as substitution), in the second
degree; <as>as, a <ex>secondary</ex> salt, a <ex>secondary</ex> amine,
etc.</as> Cf. <er>primary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note> A primary amine has the general formula
<chform>R.NH2</chform>; a <ex>secondary</ex> amine has the general
formula <chform>R.NH.R<prime/</chform>, where R and R<prime/ are alkyl
or aryl groups. A primary alcohol has the general formula
<chform>R.CH2.OH</chform>; a <ex>secondary</ex> alcohol has the general
formula <chform>R.CHOH.R<prime/</chform>. Tertiary amines and alcohols
have the general formulas <chform>R.CR<prime/N.R<Prime/</chform> and
<chform>R.CR<prime/OH.R<Prime/</chform>, respectively.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Subsequent in origin; -- said of
minerals produced by alteration or deposition subsequent to the
formation of the original rock mass; also of characters of minerals (as
<ex>secondary</ex> cleavage, etc.) developed by pressure or other
causes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the second joint of
the wing of a bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Dependent or
consequent upon another disease; <as>as, Bright's disease is often
<ex>secondary</ex> to scarlet fever</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Occurring in the second stage of a disease; <as>as, the
<ex>secondary</ex> symptoms of syphilis</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Secondary accent</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Accent</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary
age</b></col>. <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <cd>The Mesozoic age, or age before
the Tertiary. See <er>Mesozoic</er>, and Note under <er>Age</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 8.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary alcohol</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>any one of a series of alcohols which contain
the radical <chform>CH.OH</chform> united with two hydrocarbon radicals.
On oxidation the secondary alcohols form ketones.</cd> --
<col><b>Secondary amputation</b></col> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>, <cd>an
amputation for injury, performed after the constitutional effects of the
injury have subsided.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary axis</b></col>
<fld>(Opt.)</fld>, <cd>any line which passes through the optical center
of a lens but not through the centers of curvature, or, in the case of a
mirror, which passes through the center of curvature but not through the
center of the mirror.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary battery</b></col>.
<fld>(Elec.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Battery</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
4.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary circle</b></col> <fld>(Geom. &
Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a great circle that passes through the poles of
another great circle and is therefore perpendicular to its plane.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Secondary circuit</b></col>, <col><b>Secondary
coil</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>, <cd>a circuit or coil in which
a current is produced by the induction of a current in a neighboring
circuit or coil called the <xex>primary circuit</xex> or
<xex>coil</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary color</b></col>, <cd>a color
formed by mixing any two primary colors in equal proportions.</cd> --
<col><b>Secondary coverts</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the longer
coverts which overlie the basal part of the secondary quills of a
bird.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary crystal</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>,
<cd>a crystal derived from one of the primary forms.</cd> --
<col><b>Secondary current</b></col> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>, <cd>a momentary
current induced in a closed circuit by a current of electricity passing
through the same or a contiguous circuit at the beginning and also at
the end of the passage of the primary current.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary
evidence</b></col>, <cd>that which is admitted upon failure to obtain
the primary or best evidence.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary fever</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a fever coming on in a disease after the
subsidence of the fever with which the disease began, as the fever which
attends the outbreak of the eruption in smallpox.</cd> --
<col><b>Secondary hemorrhage</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>hemorrhage
occuring from a wounded blood vessel at some considerable time after the
original bleeding has ceased.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary
planet</b></col>. <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <cd>See the Note under
<er>Planet</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary qualities</b></col>,
<cd>those qualities of bodies which are not inseparable from them as
such, but are dependent for their development and intensity on the
organism of the percipient, such as color, taste, odor, etc.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Secondary quills</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Secondary
remiges</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the quill feathers
arising from the forearm of a bird and forming a row continuous with the
primaries; -- called also <altname>secondaries</altname>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Secondary rocks</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Secondary
strata</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>those lying between the
Primary, or Paleozoic, and Tertiary (see <cref>Primary rocks</cref>,
under <er>Primary</er>); -- later restricted to strata of the Mesozoic
age, and at present but little used.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary
syphilis</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>the second stage of syphilis,
including the period from the first development of constitutional
symptoms to the time when the bones and the internal organs become
involved.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary tint</b></col>, <cd>any subdued
tint, as gray.</cd> -- <col><b>Secondary union</b></col>
<fld>(Surg.)</fld>, <cd>the union of wounds after suppuration; union by
the second intention.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Second; second-rate; subordinate;
inferior.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secondary</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Secondaries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
occupies a subordinate, inferior, or auxiliary place; a delegate or
deputy; one who is second or next to the chief officer; <as>as, the
<ex>secondary</ex>, or undersheriff of the city of
London</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Old Escalus . . . is thy <qex>secondary</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A secondary
circle.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A satellite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A secondary quill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Second-class</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond-class`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the rank or
degree below the best or highest; inferior; second-rate; <as>as, a
<ex>second-class</ex> house; a <ex>second-class</ex> passage; a
<ex>second-class</ex> citizen</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seconder</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who seconds{3}
or supports what another attempts, affirms, moves, or proposes; <as>as,
the <ex>seconder</ex> of an enterprise or of a motion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secondhand</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond*hand`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not
original or primary; received from another; <as>as, <ex>secondhand</ex>
information</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>They have but a <qex>secondhand</qex> or implicit knowledge.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not new; already or previously possessed or used by
another; <as>as, a <ex>secondhand</ex> book, garment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At second hand</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Hand</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 10.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secondly</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the second place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secondo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*con"do</hw> <pr>(s<esl/*k<ocr/n"d<osl/; It.
s<asl/*k<osl/n"d<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The second part in a concerted piece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Second-rate</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond-rate`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the second
size, rank, quality, or value; <as>as, a <ex>second-rate</ex> ship;
<ex>second-rate</ex> cloth; a <ex>second-rate</ex> champion</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Second-sight</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond-sight`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The power of
discerning what is not visible to the physical eye, or of foreseeing
future events, esp. such as are of a disastrous kind; the capacity of a
seer; prophetic vision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was seized with a fit of <qex>second-sight</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor less availed his optic sleight,<br/
And Scottish gift of <qex>second-sight</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Trumbull.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Second-sighted</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ond-sight`ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the power of
second-sight.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secre</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cre</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Secret;
secretive; faithful to a secret.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be holden stable and <qex>secre</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secre</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cre</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A secret.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secrecy</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cre*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Secrecies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[From
<er>Secret</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or quality of being
hidden; <as>as, his movements were detected in spite of their
<ex>secrecy</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lady Anne,<br/
Whom the king hath in <qex>secrecy</qex> long married.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is concealed; a secret.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Seclusion; privacy; retirement.</def> <ldquo/The
pensive <xex>secrecy</xex> of desert cell.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The quality of being secretive; fidelity to a
secret; forbearance of disclosure or discovery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is not with public as with private prayer; in this, rather
<qex>secrecy</qex> is commanded than outward show.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secrely</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cre*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Secretly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secreness</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cre*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Secrecy; privacy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secret</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>secret</ets>
(cf. Sp. & Pg. <ets>secreto</ets>, It. <ets>secreto</ets>,
<ets>segreto</ets>), fr. L. <ets>secretus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>secernere</ets> to put apart, to separate. See <er>Certain</er>,
and cf. <er>Secrete</er>, <er>Secern</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Hidden; concealed; <as>as, <ex>secret</ex> treasure;
<ex>secret</ex> plans; a <ex>secret</ex> vow</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>secret</qex> things belong unto the Lord our God; but
those things which are revealed belong unto us.</q> <rj><qau>Deut. xxix.
29.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Withdrawn from general intercourse or notice; in
retirement or secrecy; secluded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There, <qex>secret</qex> in her sapphire cell,<br/
He with the Nais wont to dwell.</q> <rj><qau>Fenton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Faithful to a secret; not inclined to divulge or
betray confidence; secretive.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Secret</qex> Romans, that have spoke the word,<br/
And will not palter.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Separate; distinct.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They suppose two other divine hypostases superior thereunto, which
were perfectly <qex>secret</qex> from matter.</q>
<rj><qau>Cudworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Hidden; concealed; secluded; retired; unseen;
unknown; private; obscure; recondite; latent; covert; clandestine;
privy. See <er>Hidden</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secret</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cret</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>secret</ets> (cf. Pr.
<ets>secret</ets>, Sp. & Pg. <ets>secreto</ets>, It. <ets>secreto</ets>,
<ets>segreto</ets>), from L. <ets>secretum</ets>. See <er>Secret</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something studiously concealed; a
thing kept from general knowledge; what is not revealed, or not to be
revealed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To tell our own <qex>secrets</qex> is often folly; to communicate
those of others is treachery.</q> <rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1301 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thing not discovered; what is unknown or
unexplained; a mystery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>secrets</qex> of the deep, all nature's works.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The parts which modesty and
propriety require to be concealed; the genital organs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In secret</b></col>, <cd>in a private place; in privacy
or secrecy; in a state or place not seen; privately.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bread eaten <qex>in secret</qex> is pleasant.</q> <rj><qau>Prov.
ix. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secret</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To keep
secret.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretage</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cret*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
process in which mercury, or some of its salts, is employed to impart
the property of felting to certain kinds of furs.</def>
<rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretarial</ent><br/
<hw>Sec`re*ta"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a secretary; befitting a secretary.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Secretarial</qex>, diplomatic, or other official
training.</q> <rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretariate</ent><br/
<ent>Secretariat</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sec`re*ta"ri*at</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sec`re*ta"ri*ate</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>secr<eacute/tariat</ets>.]</ety> <def>The office of a secretary;
the place where a secretary transacts business, keeps records,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretary</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"re*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Secretaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>secr<eacute/taire</ets> (cf. Pr. <ets>secretari</ets>, Sp. & Pg.
<ets>secretario</ets>, It. <ets>secretario</ets>, <ets>segretario</ets>)
LL. <ets>secretarius</ets>, originally, a confidant, one intrusted with
secrets, from L. <ets>secretum</ets> a secret. See <er>Secret</er>,
<pos>a.</pos> & <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who keeps, or
is intrusted with, secrets.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person employed to write orders, letters,
dispatches, public or private papers, records, and the like; an official
scribe, amanuensis, or writer; one who attends to correspondence, and
transacts other business, for an association, a public body, or an
individual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That which is most of all profitable is acquaintance with the
<qex>secretaries</qex>, and employed men of ambassadors.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An officer of state whose business is to superintend
and manage the affairs of a particular department of government, and who
is usually a member of the cabinet or advisory council of the chief
executive; <as>as, the <ex>secretary</ex> of state, who conducts the
correspondence and attends to the relations of a government with foreign
courts; the <ex>secretary</ex> of the treasury, who manages the
department of finance; the <ex>secretary</ex> of war,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A piece of furniture, with conveniences for writing
and for the arrangement of papers; an escritoire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The secretary bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Secretary bird</b></col>. <ety>[So called in allusion to
the tufts of feathers at the back of its head, which were fancifully
thought to resemble pens stuck behind the ear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>A large long-legged raptorial bird (<spn>Gypogeranus
serpentarius</spn>), native of South Africa, but now naturalized in the
West Indies and some other tropical countries. It has a powerful hooked
beak, a crest of long feathers, and a long tail. It feeds upon reptiles
of various kinds, and is much prized on account of its habit of killing
and devouring snakes of all kinds. Called also <altname>serpent
eater</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See the Note under <er>Clerk</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
4.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretaryship</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"re*ta*ry*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office, or the term
of office, of a secretary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secrete</ent><br/
<hw>Se*crete"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Secreted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Secreting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>secretus</ets>
separated, secret, hidden, p. p. of <ets>secernere</ets>. See
<er>Secret</er>, and cf. <er>Discrete</er>, <er>Discreet</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To deposit in a place of hiding; to hide; to conceal;
<as>as, to <ex>secrete</ex> stolen goods; to <ex>secrete</ex> one's
self</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>To separate from the blood and
elaborate by the process of secretion; to elaborate and emit as a
secretion. See <er>Secretion</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why one set of cells should <qex>secrete</qex> bile, another urea,
and so on, we do not know.</q> <rj><qau>Carpenter.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To conceal; hide. See <er>Conceal</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretion</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cre"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secretio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/cr<eacute/tion</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of secreting or concealing; <as>as, the
<ex>secretion</ex> of dutiable goods</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The act of secreting; the
process by which material is separated from the blood through the agency
of the cells of the various glands and elaborated by the cells into new
substances so as to form the various secretions, as the saliva, bile,
and other digestive fluids. The process varies in the different glands,
and hence are formed the various secretions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Any substance or fluid
secreted, or elaborated and emitted, as the gastric juice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretist</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cret*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dealer in
secrets.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretitious</ent><br/
<hw>Se`cre*ti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Parted by
animal secretion; <as>as, <ex>secretitious</ex> humors</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Floyer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretive</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cret"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to
secrete, or to keep secret or private; <as>as, a <ex>secretive</ex>
disposition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cret"ive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality of
being secretive; disposition or tendency to conceal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Phren.)</fld> <def>The faculty or propensity which
impels to reserve, secrecy, or concealment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretly</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cret*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a secret
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretness</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cret*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or
quality of being secret, hid, or concealed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Secretiveness; concealment.</def>
<rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secreto-motory</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cre`to-mo"to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Causing secretion; -- said of nerves which go
to glands and influence secretion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secretory</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cre"to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/cr<eacute/toire</ets>. See <er>Secrete</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Secreting; performing, or connected with, the
office of secretion; secernent; <as>as, <ex>secretory</ex> vessels,
nerves</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A secretory vessel; a
secernent.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secret service</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cret serv"ice</hw>. <def>The detective service of a government.
In the United States, in time of peace the bureau of secret service is
under the treasury department, and in time of war it aids the war
department in securing information concerning the movements of the
enemy.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sect</ent><br/
<hw>Sect</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/kt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secare</ets>, <ets>sectum</ets>, to cut.]</ety> <def>A cutting; a
scion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sect</ent><br/
<hw>Sect</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/kt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>secte</ets>, L. <ets>secta</ets>, fr. <ets>sequi</ets> to follow;
often confused with L. <ets>secare</ets>, <ets>sectum</ets>, to cut. See
<er>Sue</er> to follow, and cf. <er>Sept</er>, <er>Suit</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Those following a particular leader or
authority, or attached to a certain opinion; a company or set having a
common belief or allegiance distinct from others; in religion, the
believers in a particular creed, or upholders of a particular practice;
especially, in modern times, a party dissenting from an established
church; a denomination; in philosophy, the disciples of a particular
master; a school; in society and the state, an order, rank, class, or
party.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He beareth the sign of poverty,<br/
And in that <qex>sect</qex> our Savior saved all mankind.</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As of the <qex>sect</qex> of which that he was born,<br/
He kept his lay, to which that he was sworn.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The cursed <qex>sect</qex> of that detestable and false prophet
Mohammed.</q> <rj><qau>Fabyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As concerning this <qex>sect</qex> [Christians], we know that
everywhere it is spoken against.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xxviii.
22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectant</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secare</ets>, <ets>sectum</ets>, to cut.]</ety> <def>One of the
portions of space bounded by the three coordinate planes. Specif.
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld>, one of the parts of a crystal into which it is
divided by the axial planes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sec*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to a
sect, or to sects; peculiar to a sect; bigotedly attached to the tenets
and interests of a denomination; <as>as, <ex>sectarian</ex> principles
or prejudices</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sec*ta"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of a sect; a member or
adherent of a special school, denomination, or religious or
philosophical party; one of a party in religion which has separated
itself from established church, or which holds tenets different from
those of the prevailing denomination in a state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Heretic</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectarianism</ent><br/
<hw>Sec*ta"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or character of a sectarian; devotion to the interests of a party;
excess of partisan or denominational zeal; adherence to a separate
church organization.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectarianize</ent><br/
<hw>Sec*ta"ri*an*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To imbue
with sectarian feelings; to subject to the control of a sect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectarism</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ta*rism</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sectarianism.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectarist</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ta*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sectary.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectary</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;<plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sectaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>sectaire</ets>.
See <er>Sect</er>.]</ety> <def>A sectarian; a member or adherent of a
sect; a follower or disciple of some particular teacher in philosophy or
religion; one who separates from an established church; a
dissenter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I never knew that time in England when men of truest religion were
not counted <qex>sectaries</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectator</ent><br/
<hw>Sec*ta"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>sectari</ets>, v. intens. fr. <ets>sequi</ets> to follow. See
<er>Sue</er> to follow.]</ety> <def>A follower; a disciple; an adherent
to a sect.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectile</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sectilis</ets>, fr. <ets>secare</ets>, <ets>sectum</ets>, to cut:
cf. F. <ets>sectile</ets>. See <er>Section</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of
being cut; specifically <fld>(Min.)</fld>, capable of being severed by
the knife with a smooth cut; -- said of minerals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectility</ent><br/
<hw>Sec*til"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or
quality of being sectile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Section</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sectio</ets>, fr. <ets>secare</ets>, <ets>sectum</ets>, to cut;
akin to E. <ets>saw</ets> a cutting instrument: cf. F.
<ets>section</ets>. See <er>Saw</er>, and cf. <er>Scion</er>,
<er>Dissect</er>, <er>Insect</er>, <er>Secant</er>,
<er>Segment</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of cutting, or
separation by cutting; <as>as, the <ex>section</ex> of
bodies</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A part separated from something; a division; a
portion; a slice.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>A distinct part or portion of a book or writing; a
subdivision of a chapter; the division of a law or other writing; a
paragraph; an article; hence, the character <sect/, often used to denote
such a division.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is hardly possible to give a distinct view of his several
arguments in distinct <qex>sections</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A distinct part of a country or people, community,
class, or the like; a part of a territory separated by geographical
lines, or of a people considered as distinct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The extreme <qex>section</qex> of one class consists of bigoted
dotards, the extreme <qex>section</qex> of the other consists of shallow
and reckless empirics.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>One of the portions, of one square mile each, into
which the public lands of the United States are divided; one
thirty-sixth part of a township. These sections are subdivided into
quarter sections for sale under the homestead and preemption
laws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The figure made up of all the
points common to a superficies and a solid which meet, or to two
superficies which meet, or to two lines which meet. In the first case
the section is a superficies, in the second a line, and in the third a
point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld> <def>A division of a genus; a
group of species separated by some distinction from others of the same
genus; -- often indicated by the sign <sect/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A part of a musical period,
composed of one or more phrases. See <er>Phrase</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The description or representation of anything as it
would appear if cut through by any intersecting plane; depiction of what
is beyond a plane passing through, or supposed to pass through, an
object, as a building, a machine, a succession of strata;
profile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In mechanical drawing, as in these Illustrations of a
cannon, a <xex>longitudinal section</xex> (<xex>a</xex>) usually
represents the object as cut through its center lengthwise and
vertically; a <xex>cross</xex> or <xex>transverse section</xex>
(<xex>b</xex>), as cut crosswise and vertically; and a <xex>horizontal
section</xex> (<xex>c</xex>), as cut through its center horizontally.
<xex>Oblique sections</xex> are made at various angles. In architecture,
a <xex>vertical section</xex> is a drawing showing the interior, the
thickness of the walls, etc., as if made on a vertical plane passed
through a building.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Angular sections</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>a
branch of analysis which treats of the relations of sines, tangents,
etc., of arcs to the sines, tangents, etc., of their multiples or of
their parts.</cd> <mark>[R.]</mark> -- <col><b>Conic sections</b></col>.
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Conic</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Section
liner</b></col> <fld>(Drawing)</fld>, <cd>an instrument to aid in
drawing a series of equidistant parallel lines, -- used in representing
sections.</cd> -- <col><b>Thin section</b></col>, <cd>a section or
slice, as of mineral, animal, or vegetable substance, thin enough to be
transparent, and used for study under the microscope.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Part; portion; division.</syn> --
<usage><er>Section</er>, <er>Part</er>. The English more commonly apply
the word <xex>section</xex> to a part or portion of a body of men;
<as>as, a <ex>section</ex> of the clergy, a small <ex>section</ex> of
the Whigs, etc.</as> In the United States this use is less common, but
another use, unknown or but little known in England, is very frequent,
as in the phrases <ldquo/the eastern <xex>section</xex> of our
country,<rdquo/ etc., the same sense being also given to the adjective
<xex>sectional</xex>; as, <xex>sectional</xex> feelings, interests,
etc.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectional</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a section or distinct part of larger body or territory;
local.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>sectional</qex> interests, or party feelings, it is
hoped, will hereafter yield to schemes of ambition.</q>
<rj><qau>Story.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting of sections, or capable of being divided
into sections; <as>as, a <ex>sectional</ex> steam boiler</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectionalism</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tion*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
disproportionate regard for the interests peculiar to a section of the
country; local patriotism, as distinguished from national.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectionality</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tion*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or
quality of being sectional; sectionalism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectionalize</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tion*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To divide
according to geographical sections or local interests.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The principal results of the struggle were to
<qex>sectionalize</qex> parties.</q> <rj><qau>Nicolay & Hay (Life of
Lincoln).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectionally</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tion*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sectional
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectionize</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tion*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To form into
sections.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectism</ent><br/
<hw>Sect"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Devotion to a
sect.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectist</ent><br/
<hw>Sect"ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One devoted to a sect; a
sectary.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectiuncle</ent><br/
<hw>Sect"i*un`cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little or
petty sect.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Some new sect or
<xex>sectiuncle</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. Martineau.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sector</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., properly, a
cutter, fr. <ets>secare</ets>, <ets>sectum</ets>, to cut: cf. F.
<ets>secteur</ets>. See <er>Section</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A part of a circle comprehended between two
radii and the included arc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mathematical instrument, consisting of two rulers
connected at one end by a joint, each arm marked with several scales, as
of equal parts, chords, sines, tangents, etc., one scale of each kind on
each arm, and all on lines radiating from the common center of motion.
The sector is used for plotting, etc., to any scale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An astronomical instrument, the limb of which
embraces a small portion only of a circle, used for measuring
differences of declination too great for the compass of a micrometer.
When it is used for measuring zenith distances of stars, it is called a
<xex>zenith sector</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dip sector</b></col>, <cd>an instrument used for
measuring the dip of the horizon.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sector of a
sphere</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Spherical sector</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>the solid generated by the revolution of the sector of a circle
about one of its radii, or, more rarely, about any straight line drawn
in the plane of the sector through its vertex.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectoral</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"tor*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
a sector; <as>as, a <ex>sectoral</ex> circle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sectorial</ent><br/
<hw>Sec*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Adapted for cutting.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A sectorial,
or carnassial, tooth.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secular</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>secular</ets>, <ets>seculer</ets>. L. <ets>saecularis</ets>, fr.
<ets>saeculum</ets> a race, generation, age, the times, the world;
perhaps akin to E. <ets>soul</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/culier</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Coming or observed
once in an age or a century.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>secular</qex> year was kept but once a century.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to an age, or the progress of ages, or to
a long period of time; accomplished in a long progress of time; <as>as,
<ex>secular</ex> inequality; the <ex>secular</ex> refrigeration of the
globe</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to this present world, or to things
not spiritual or holy; relating to temporal as distinguished from
eternal interests; not immediately or primarily respecting the soul, but
the body; worldly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>New foes arise,<br/
Threatening to bind our souls with <qex>secular</qex> chains.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>Not regular; not bound by
monastic vows or rules; not confined to a monastery, or subject to the
rules of a religious community; <as>as, a <ex>secular</ex>
priest</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He tried to enforce a stricter discipline and greater regard for
morals, both in the religious orders and the <qex>secular</qex>
clergy.</q> <rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Belonging to the laity; lay; not clerical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I speak of folk in <qex>secular</qex> estate.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Secular equation</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the
algebraic or numerical expression of the magnitude of the inequalities
in a planet's motion that remain after the inequalities of a short
period have been allowed for.</cd> -- <col><b>Secular games</b></col>
<fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld>, <cd>games celebrated, at long but irregular
intervals, for three days and nights, with sacrifices, theatrical shows,
combats, sports, and the like.</cd> -- <col><b>Secular music</b></col>,
<cd>any music or songs not adapted to sacred uses.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Secular hymn</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Secular
poem</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a hymn or poem composed for the secular
games, or sung or rehearsed at those games.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secular</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"u*lar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A
secular ecclesiastic, or one not bound by monastic rules.</def>
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A church official whose functions
are confined to the vocal department of the choir.</def>
<rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A layman, as distinguished from a
clergyman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secularism</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"u*lar*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
state or quality of being secular; a secular spirit;
secularity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The tenets or principles of the
secularists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secularist</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"u*lar*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who theoretically rejects
every form of religious faith, and every kind of religious worship, and
accepts only the facts and influences which are derived from the present
life; also, one who believes that education and other matters of civil
policy should be managed without the introduction of a religious
element.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secularity</ent><br/
<hw>Sec`u*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.F.
<ets>s<eacute/cularit<eacute/</ets>, LL. <ets>saecularitas</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Supreme attention to the things of the present life;
worldliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>secularity</qex> of character which makes Christianity and
its principal doctrines distasteful or unintelligible.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secularization</ent><br/
<hw>Sec`u*lar*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/cularisation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of rendering
secular, or the state of being rendered secular; conversion from regular
or monastic to secular; conversion from religious to lay or secular
possession and uses; <as>as, the <ex>secularization</ex> of church
property</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1302 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>Secularize</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"u*lar*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Secularized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Secularizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>s<eacute/culariser</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To convert
from regular or monastic into secular; <as>as, to <ex>secularize</ex> a
priest or a monk</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To convert from spiritual to secular or common use;
<as>as, to <ex>secularize</ex> a church, or church
property</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At the Reformation the abbey was <qex>secularized</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>W. Coxe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make worldly or unspiritual.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Horsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secularly</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"u*lar*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a secular or worldly
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secularness</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"u*lar*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being secular; worldliness; worldly-mindedness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secund</ent><br/
<hw>Se"cund</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secundus</ets> following the course or current of wind or water.
See <er>Second</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Arranged on one side only, as flowers or leaves on a stalk.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secundate</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cun"date</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secundatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>secundare</ets> to direct
favorably.]</ety> <def>To make prosperous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secundation</ent><br/
<hw>Sec`un*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Prosperity.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secundine</ent><br/
<hw>Sec"un*dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>secondine</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
second coat, or integument, of an ovule, lying within the
primine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the ripened seed the primine and secundine are
usually united to form the testa, or outer seed coat. When they remain
distinct the secundine becomes the mesosperm, as in the castor
bean.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>secondines</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
afterbirth, or placenta and membranes; -- generally used in the
plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secundo-geniture</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cun`do-gen"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>secundus</ets> second + <ets>genitura</ets> a begetting,
generation.]</ety> <def>A right of inheritance belonging to a second
son; a property or possession so inherited.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The kingdom of Naples . . . was constituted a
<qex>secundo-geniture</qex> of Spain.</q>
<rj><qau>Bancroft.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Securable</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cur"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
secured.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secure</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cure"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>securus</ets>; pref. <ets>se-</ets> without + <ets>cura</ets> care.
See <er>Cure</er> care, and cf. <er>Sure</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Free from fear, care, or anxiety; easy in mind; not
feeling suspicion or distrust; confident.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But thou, <qex>secure</qex> of soul, unbent with woes.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Overconfident; incautious; careless; -- in a bad
sense.</def> <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Confident in opinion; not entertaining, or not
having reason to entertain, doubt; certain; sure; -- commonly with
<xex>of</xex>; <as>as, <ex>secure</ex> of a welcome</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Confidence then bore thee on, <qex>secure</qex><br/
Either to meet no danger, or to find<br/
Matter of glorious trial.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not exposed to danger; safe; -- applied to persons
and things, and followed by <xex>against</xex> or <xex>from</xex>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Secure</xex> from fortune's blows.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Safe; undisturbed; easy; sure; certain; assured;
confident; careless; heedless; inattentive.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secure</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cure"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Secured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Securing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make safe; to
relieve from apprehensions of, or exposure to, danger; to guard; to
protect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I spread a cloud before the victor's sight,<br/
Sustained the vanquished, and <qex>secured</qex> his flight.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put beyond hazard of losing or of not receiving;
to make certain; to assure; to insure; -- frequently with
<xex>against</xex> or <xex>from</xex>, rarely with <xex>of</xex>;
<as>as, to <ex>secure</ex> a creditor against loss; to <ex>secure</ex> a
debt by a mortgage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It <qex>secures</qex> its possessor of eternal happiness.</q>
<rj><qau>T. Dick.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make fast; to close or confine effectually; to
render incapable of getting loose or escaping; <as>as, to
<ex>secure</ex> a prisoner; to <ex>secure</ex> a door, or the hatches of
a ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To get possession of; to make one's self secure of;
to acquire certainly; <as>as, to <ex>secure</ex> an
estate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Secure arms</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a command
and a position in the manual of arms, used in wet weather, the object
being to guard the firearm from becoming wet. The piece is turned with
the barrel to the front and grasped by the right hand at the lower band,
the muzzle is dropped to the front, and the piece held with the guard
under the right arm, the hand supported against the hip, and the thumb
on the rammer.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Securely</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cure"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a secure manner; without
fear or apprehension; without danger; safely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His daring foe . . . <qex>securely</qex> him defied.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Securement</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cure"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
securing; protection.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Society condemns the <qex>securement</qex> in all cases of
perpetual protection by means of perpetual imprisonment.</q> <rj><qau>C.
A. Ives.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Secureness</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cure"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The condition or quality of
being secure; exemption from fear; want of vigilance;
security.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Securer</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cur"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, secures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Securifera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sec`u*rif"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from L. <ets>securis</ets> an ax + <ets>ferre</ets> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Serrifera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Securiform</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cu"ri*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>securis</ets> an ax or hatchet + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/curiforme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld>
<def>Having the form of an ax or hatchet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Securipalp</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cu"ri*palp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>securis</ets> ax, hatchet + E. <ets>palp</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a family of beetles having the maxillary
palpi terminating in a hatchet-shaped joint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Security</ent><br/
<hw>Se*cu"ri*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Securities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>securitas</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/curit<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Secure</er>, and cf.
<er>Surety</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The condition or quality of
being secure; secureness.</def> <specif>Specifically:</specif>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Freedom from apprehension, anxiety, or care;
confidence of power or safety; hence, assurance; certainty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His trembling hand had lost the ease,<br/
Which marks <qex>security</qex> to please.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>Hence, carelessness; negligence;
heedlessness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He means, my lord, that we are too remiss,<br/
Whilst Bolingbroke, through our <qex>security</qex>,<br/
Grows strong and great in substance and in power.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>Freedom from risk; safety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give up yourself merely to chance and hazard,<br/
From firm <qex>security</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some . . . alleged that we should have no <qex>security</qex> for
our trade.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which secures or makes safe; protection; guard;
defense.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Something given,
deposited, or pledged, to make certain the fulfillment of an obligation,
the performance of a contract, the payment of a debt, or the like;
surety; pledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those who lent him money lent it on no <qex>security</qex> but his
bare word.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>One who becomes surety for another, or engages
himself for the performance of another's obligation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An evidence of debt or of property, as a bond, a
certificate of stock, etc.; <as>as, government
<ex>securities</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Protection; defense; guard; shelter; safety;
certainty; ease; assurance; carelessness; confidence; surety; pledge;
bail.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedan</ent><br/
<hw>Se*dan"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Said to be named from
<ets>Sedan</ets>, in France, where it was first made, and whence it was
introduced into England in the time of King Charles I.]</ety> <def>A
portable chair or covered vehicle for carrying a single person, --
usually borne on poles by two men. Called also <altname>sedan
chair</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedate</ent><br/
<hw>Se*date"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sedatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sedare</ets>, <ets>sedatum</ets>, to
allay, calm, causative of <ets>sedere</ets> to sit. See
<er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <def>Undisturbed by passion or caprice; calm;
tranquil; serene; not passionate or giddy; composed; staid; <as>as, a
<ex>sedate</ex> soul, mind, or temper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Disputation carries away the mind from that calm and
<qex>sedate</qex> temper which is so necessary to contemplate truth.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whatsoever we feel and know<br/
Too <qex>sedate</qex> for outward show.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Settled; composed; calm; quiet; tranquil; still;
serene; unruffled; undisturbed; contemplative; sober; serious.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Se*date"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Se*date"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedation</ent><br/
<hw>Se*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sedatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of calming, or the state of
being calm.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coles.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedative</ent><br/
<hw>Sed"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/datif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to calm, moderate, or
tranquilize</def>; specifically <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <def>allaying
irritability and irritation; assuaging pain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedative</ent><br/
<hw>Sed"a*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A remedy which
allays irritability and irritation, and irritative activity or
pain.</def> <-- ##now usu. referring to tranquilizer or sleeping pill.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedent</ent><br/
<hw>Se"dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sedens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sedere</ets> to sit. See
<er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <def>Sitting; inactive; quiet.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedentarily</ent><br/
<hw>Sed"en*ta*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
sedentary manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedentariness</ent><br/
<hw>Sed"en*ta*ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
sedentary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedentary</ent><br/
<hw>Sed"en*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sedentarius</ets>, fr. <ets>sedere</ets> to sit: cf. F.
<ets>se<eacute/dentaire</ets>. See <er>Sedent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Accustomed to sit much or long; <as>as, a <ex>sedentary</ex>
man</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sedentary</xex>, scholastic sophists.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bp. Warburton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characterized by, or requiring, much sitting;
<as>as, a <ex>sedentary</ex> employment; a <ex>sedentary</ex>
life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Any education that confined itself to <qex>sedentary</qex>
pursuits was essentially imperfect.</q>
<rj><qau>Beaconsfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Inactive; motionless; sluggish; hence, calm;
tranquil.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>sedentary</xex>
earth.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The soul, considered abstractly from its passions, is of a remiss,
<qex>sedentary</qex> nature.</q> <rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Caused by long sitting.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Sedentary</xex> numbness.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Remaining in one place,
especially when firmly attached to some object; <as>as, the oyster is a
<ex>sedentary</ex> mollusk; the barnacles are <ex>sedentary</ex>
crustaceans</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sedentary spider</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>one of
a tribe of spiders which rest motionless until their prey is caught in
their web.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sederunt</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*de"runt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., they sat,
fr. <ets>sedere</ets> to sit.]</ety> <def>A sitting, as of a court or
other body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is pity we have not Burns's own account of that long
<qex>sederunt</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Prof. Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Acts of sederunt</b></col> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld>,
<cd>ordinances of the Court of Session for the ordering of processes and
expediting of justice.</cd> <rj><au>Bell.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedge</ent><br/
<hw>Sedge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>segge</ets>,
AS. <ets>secg</ets>; akin to LG. <ets>segge</ets>; -- probably named
from its bladelike appearance, and akin to L. <ets>secare</ets> to cut,
E. <ets>saw</ets> a cutting instrument; cf. Ir. <ets>seisg</ets>, W.
<ets>hesg</ets>. Cf. <er>Hassock</er>, <er>Saw</er> the
instrument.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of the
genus <gen>Carex</gen>, perennial, endogenous, innutritious herbs, often
growing in dense tufts in marshy places. They have triangular jointless
stems, a spiked inflorescence, and long grasslike leaves which are
usually rough on the margins and midrib. There are several hundred
species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is sometimes given to any other plant of the
order <ord>Cyperaceae</ord>, which includes <gen>Carex</gen>,
<gen>Cyperus</gen>, <gen>Scirpus</gen>, and many other genera of
rushlike plants.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A flock of herons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sedge hen</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the clapper
rail. See under 5th <er>Rail</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sedge
warbler</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small European singing bird
(<spn>Acrocephalus phragmitis</spn>). It often builds its nest among
reeds; -- called also <altname>sedge bird</altname>, <altname>sedge
wren</altname>, <altname>night warbler</altname>, and <altname>Scotch
nightingale</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedged</ent><br/
<hw>Sedged</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made or composed of
sedge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With your <qex>sedged</qex> crowns and ever-harmless looks.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedgy</ent><br/
<hw>Sedg"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Overgrown with
sedge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On the gentle Severn's <qex>sedgy</qex> bank.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedilia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*dil"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Sedile</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <ety>[L. <ets>sedile</ets>
a seat.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Seats in the chancel of a church
near the altar for the officiating clergy during intervals of
service.</def> <rj><au>Hook.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sediment</ent><br/
<hw>Sed"i*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/diment</ets>, L. <ets>sedimentum</ets> a settling, fr.
<ets>sedere</ets> to sit, to settle. See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The matter which subsides to the bottom, from water or
any other liquid; settlings; lees; dregs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>The material of which sedimentary
rocks are formed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedimental</ent><br/
<hw>Sed`i*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sedimentary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedimentary</ent><br/
<hw>Sed`i*men"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/dimentaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
sediment; formed by sediment; containing matter that has
subsided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sedimentary rocks</b></col>. <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Aqueous rocks</cref>, under <er>Aqueous</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedimentation</ent><br/
<hw>Sed`i*men*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
depositing a sediment; specifically <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, the deposition
of the material of which sedimentary rocks are formed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedition</ent><br/
<hw>Se*di"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sedicioun</ets>, OF. <ets>sedition</ets>, F.
<ets>s<eacute/dition</ets>, fr. L. <ets>seditio</ets>, originally, a
going aside; hence, an insurrectionary separation; pref. <ets>se-</ets>,
<ets>sed-</ets>, aside + <ets>itio</ets> a going, fr. <ets>ire</ets>,
<ets>itum</ets>, to go. Cf. <er>Issue</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
raising of commotion in a state, not amounting to insurrection; conduct
tending to treason, but without an overt act; excitement of discontent
against the government, or of resistance to lawful authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In soothing them, we nourish 'gainst our senate<br/
The cockle of rebellion, insolence, <qex>sedition</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Noisy demagogues who had been accused of <qex>sedition</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Dissension; division; schism.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now the works of the flesh are manifest, . . . emulations, wrath,
strife, <qex>seditions</qex>, heresies.</q> <rj><qau>Gal. v. 19,
20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Insurrection; tumult; uproar; riot; rebellion;
revolt. See <er>Insurrection</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seditionary</ent><br/
<hw>Se*di"tion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An inciter or
promoter of sedition.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seditious</ent><br/
<hw>Se*di"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>seditiosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/ditieux</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to sedition; partaking of the nature
of, or tending to excite, sedition; <as>as, <ex>seditious</ex> behavior;
<ex>seditious</ex> strife; <ex>seditious</ex> words</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Disposed to arouse, or take part in, violent
opposition to lawful authority; turbulent; factious; guilty of sedition;
<as>as, <ex>seditious</ex> citizens</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Se*di"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Se*di"tious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedlitz</ent><br/
<hw>Sed"litz</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Seidlitz</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seduce</ent><br/
<hw>Se*duce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Seduced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seducing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>seducere</ets>, <ets>seductum</ets>; pref. <ets>se-</ets> aside +
<ets>ducere</ets> to lead. See <er>Duke</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
draw aside from the path of rectitude and duty in any manner; to entice
to evil; to lead astray; to tempt and lead to iniquity; to
corrupt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For me, the gold of France did not <qex>seduce</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, to induce to engage in sexual
intercourse.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To allure; entice; tempt; attract; mislead;
decoy; inveigle. See <er>Allure</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seducement</ent><br/
<hw>Se*duce"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of seducing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The means employed to seduce, as flattery, promises,
deception, etc.; arts of enticing or corrupting.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seducer</ent><br/
<hw>Se*du"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, seduces. For a female seducer, the term <er>seductress</er> is
also used</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>He whose firm faith no reason could remove,<br/
Will melt before that soft <qex>seducer</qex>, love.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>One who induces
another to engage in sexual intercourse.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seducible</ent><br/
<hw>Se*du"ci*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
seduced; corruptible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seducing</ent><br/
<hw>Se*du"cing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Seductive.</def>
<ldquo/Thy sweet <xex>seducing</xex> charms.<rdquo/ <au>Cowper.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Se*du"cing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seduction</ent><br/
<hw>Se*duc*tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>seductio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/duction</ets>. <ets>See</ets>
<er>Seduce</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of seducing; enticement
to wrong doing; enticement to fail in some duty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
offense of inducing a woman to consent to unlawful sexual intercourse,
by enticements which overcome her scruples; the wrong or crime of
persuading a woman to surrender her chastity.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any successful enticement to
engage in some sexual activity, especially intercourse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which seduces, or is adapted to seduce; means
of leading astray; <as>as, the <ex>seductions</ex> of
wealth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seductive</ent><br/
<hw>Se*duc"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to lead
astray; apt to mislead by flattering appearances; tempting; alluring;
<as>as, a <ex>seductive</ex> offer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This may enable us to understand how <qex>seductive</qex> is the
influence of example.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seductively</ent><br/
<hw>Se*duc"tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a seductive
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seductress</ent><br/
<hw>Se*duc"tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who
seduces; a female seducer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedulity</ent><br/
<hw>Se*du"li*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sedulitas</ets>. See <er>Sedulous</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or
state of being sedulous; diligent and assiduous application; constant
attention; unremitting industry; sedulousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The industrious bee, by his <qex>sedulity</qex> in summer, lives
in honey all the winter.</q> <rj><qau>Feltham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedulous</ent><br/
<hw>Sed"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sedulus</ets>, perhaps from <ets>sedere</ets> to sit, and so akin
to E. <ets>sit</ets>.]</ety> <def>Diligent in application or pursuit;
constant, steady, and persevering in business, or in endeavors to effect
an object; steadily industrious; assiduous; <as>as, the
<ex>sedulous</ex> bee</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What signifies the sound of words in prayer, without the affection
of the heart, and a <qex>sedulous</qex> application of the proper means
that may naturally lead us to such an end?</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Assiduous; diligent; industrious; laborious;
unremitting; untiring; unwearied; persevering.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sed"u*lous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sed"u*lous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sedum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se"dum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>sedere</ets> to sit; so called in allusion to the manner in which
the plants attach themselves to rocks and walls.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants, mostly perennial, having
succulent leaves and cymose flowers; orpine; stonecrop.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>See</ent><br/
<hw>See</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>se</ets>,
<ets>see</ets>, OF. <ets>se</ets>, <ets>sed</ets>, <ets>sied</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>sedes</ets> a seat, or the kindred <ets>sedere</ets> to sit. See
<er>Sit</er>, and cf. <er>Siege</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A seat; a
site; a place where sovereign power is exercised.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jove laughed on Venus from his sovereign <qex>see</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The seat
of episcopal power; a diocese; the jurisdiction of a bishop; <as>as, the
<ex>see</ex> of New York</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The seat of an
archbishop; a province or jurisdiction of an archbishop; <as>as, an
archiepiscopal <ex>see</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The seat,
place, or office of the pope, or Roman pontiff; <as>as, the papal
<ex>see</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The pope or his court at
Rome; <as>as, to appeal to the <ex>see</ex> of Rome</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Apostolic see</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Apostolic</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1303 pr=PCP --></p>

<p><ent>See</ent><br/
<hw>See</hw> <pr>(s<emac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Saw</conjf> <pr>(s<add/)</pr>; <pos>p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Seen</conjf> <pr>(s<emac/n)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Seeing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>seen</ets>,
<ets>sen</ets>, <ets>seon</ets>, AS. <ets>se<oacute/n</ets>; akin to
OFries. <ets>s<imac/a</ets>, D. <ets>zien</ets>, OS. & OHG.
<ets>sehan</ets>, G. <ets>sehen</ets>, Icel. <ets>sj<amac/</ets>, Sw.
<ets>se</ets>, Dan. <ets>see</ets>, Goth. <ets>sa<iacute/hwan</ets>, and
probably to L. <ets>sequi</ets> to follow (and so originally meaning, to
follow with the eyes). Gr. <grk>"e`pesqai</grk>, Skr. <ets>sac</ets>.
Cf. <er>Sight</er>, <er>Sue</er> to follow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
perceive by the eye; to have knowledge of the existence and apparent
qualities of by the organs of sight; to behold; to descry; to
view.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will now turn aside, and <qex>see</qex> this great sight.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. iii. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To perceive by mental vision; to form an idea or
conception of; to note with the mind; to observe; to discern; to
distinguish; to understand; to comprehend; to ascertain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Go, I pray thee, <qex>see</qex> whether it be well with thy
brethren.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xxxvii. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jesus <qex>saw</qex> that he answered discreetly.</q>
<rj><qau>Mark xii. 34.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who's so gross<br/
That <qex>seeth</qex> not this palpable device?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To follow with the eyes, or as with the eyes; to
watch; to regard attentively; to look after.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had a mind to <qex>see</qex> him out, and therefore did not care
for contradicting him.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To have an interview with; especially, to make a
call upon; to visit; <as>as, to go to <ex>see</ex> a
friend</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And Samuel came no more to <qex>see</qex> Saul until the day of
his death.</q> <rj><qau>1 Sam. xv. 35.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To fall in with; to meet or associate with; to have
intercourse or communication with; hence, to have knowledge or
experience of; <as>as, to <ex>see</ex> military service</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Make us glad according to the days wherein thou hast afflicted us,
and the years wherein we have <qex>seen</qex> evil.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. xc.
15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Verily, verily, I say unto you, if a man keep my saying, he shall
never <qex>see</qex> death.</q> <rj><qau>John viii. 51.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Improvement in wisdom and prudence by <qex>seeing</qex> men.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To accompany in person; to escort; to wait upon;
<as>as, to <ex>see</ex> one home; to <ex>see</ex> one aboard the
cars</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>In poker and similar games at cards, to meet (a
bet), or to equal the bet of (a player), by staking the same sum.</def>
<ldquo/I'll <ex>see</ex> you and raise you ten.<rdquo/<br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>God you see</b></col> (<it>or</it> <col><b>God him
see</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>God me see</b></col>, etc.)</mcol>,
<cd>God keep you (him, me, etc.) in his sight; God protect you.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>To see
<plain>(anything)</plain> out</b></col>, <cd>to see (it) to the end; to
be present at, work at, or attend, to the end.</cd> -- <col><b>To see
stars</b></col>, <cd>to see flashes of light, like stars; -- sometimes
the result of concussion of the head.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> --
<col><b>To see <plain>(one)</plain> through</b></col>, <cd>to help,
watch, or guard (one) to the end of a course or an
undertaking.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>See</ent><br/
<hw>See</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To have the power of
sight, or of perceiving by the proper organs; to possess or employ the
sense of vision; <as>as, he <ex>sees</ex> distinctly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whereas I was blind, now I <qex>see</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>John ix.
25.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively: To have intellectual apprehension; to
perceive; to know; to understand; to discern; -- often followed by a
preposition, as <xex>through</xex>, or <xex>into</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For judgment I am come into this world, that they which
<qex>see</qex> not might <qex>see</qex>; and that they which
<qex>see</qex> might be made blind.</q> <rj><qau>John ix.
39.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many sagacious persons will find us out, . . . and <qex>see</qex>
through all our fine pretensions.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be attentive; to take care; to give heed; --
generally with <xex>to</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>see</ex> to the
house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>See</qex> that ye fall not out by the way.</q> <rj><qau>Gen.
xlv. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Let me see</xex>, <xex>Let us see</xex>, are used
to express consideration, or to introduce the particular consideration
of a subject, or some scheme or calculation.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cassio's a proper man, <qex>let me see</qex> now, -<br/
To get his place.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>See</xex> is sometimes used in the imperative for
<xex>look</xex>, or <xex>behold</xex>. <ldquo/<xex>See</xex>.
<xex>see</xex>! upon the banks of Boyne he stands.<rdquo/</note>
<rj><au>Halifax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To see about a thing</b></col>, <cd>to pay attention to
it; to consider it.</cd> -- <col><b>To see on</b></col>, <cd>to look
at.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/She was full more blissful <xex>on
to see</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>To see to</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To look at; to behold; to view</cd>.
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/An altar by Jordan, a great altar <xex>to see
to</xex><rdquo/ <au>Josh. xxii. 10.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To take care
about; to look after; <as>as, <ex>to see to</ex> a
fire</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seecatch</ent><br/
<hw>See"catch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>siekach</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A full-grown male fur
seal.</def> <mark>[Alaska]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seed</ent><br/
<hw>Seed</hw> <pr>(s<emac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Seed</plw> or <plw>Seeds</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE.
<ets>seed</ets>, <ets>sed</ets>, AS. <ets>s<aemac/d</ets>, fr.
<ets>s<amac/wan</ets> to sow; akin to D. <ets>zaad</ets> seed, G.
<ets>saat</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<amac/<edh/</ets>, <ets>sae<edh/i</ets>,
Goth. mana<ets>s<emac/<thorn/s</ets> seed of men, world. See
<er>Sow</er> to scatter seed, and cf. <er>Colza</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A ripened ovule, consisting of an
embryo with one or more integuments, or coverings; <as>as, an apple
<ex>seed</ex>; a currant <ex>seed</ex>.</as> By germination it produces
a new plant.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any small seedlike fruit, though it
may consist of a pericarp, or even a calyx, as well as the seed proper;
<as>as, parsnip <ex>seed</ex>; thistle <ex>seed</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And God said, Let the earth bring forth grass, the herb yielding
<qex>seed</qex>, and the fruit tree yielding fruit after his kind, whose
<qex>seed</qex> is in itself.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. i. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The seed proper has an outer and an inner coat, and
within these the kernel or nucleus. The kernel is either the embryo
alone, or the embryo inclosed in the albumen, which is the material for
the nourishment of the developing embryo. The scar on a seed, left where
the stem parted from it, is called the <xex>hilum</xex>, and the closed
orifice of the ovule, the <xex>micropyle</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The generative fluid of the
male; semen; sperm; -- not used in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That from which anything springs; first principle;
original; source; <as>as, the <ex>seeds</ex> of virtue or
vice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The principle of production.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Praise of great acts he scatters as a <qex>seed</qex>,<br/
<qex>Which may the like in coming ages breed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Progeny; offspring; children; descendants; <as>as,
the <ex>seed</ex> of Abraham; the <ex>seed</ex> of David</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense the word is applied to one person, or to
any number collectively, and admits of the plural form, though rarely
used in the plural.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Race; generation; birth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of mortal <qex>seed</qex> they were not held.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seed bag</b></col> <fld>(Artesian well)</fld>, <cd>a
packing to prevent percolation of water down the bore hole. It consists
of a bag encircling the tubing and filled with flax seed, which swells
when wet and fills the space between the tubing and the sides of the
hole.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed bud</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the germ
or rudiment of the plant in the embryo state; the ovule.</cd> --
<col><b>Seed coat</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the covering of a
seed.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Seed corn</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Seed grain</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>corn or grain
for seed.</cd> -- <col><b>To eat the seed corn</b></col>, <cd>To eat the
corn which should be saved for seed, so as to forestall starvation; -- a
desparate measure, since it only postpones disaster.</cd>
<specif>Hence:</specif> <cd>any desparate action which creates a
disastrous situation in the long-term, done in order to provide
temporary relief.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed down</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the soft hairs on certain seeds, as cotton seed.</cd> --
<col><b>Seed drill</b></col>. <cd>See 6th <er>Drill</er>, 2
<sd>(a)</sd>.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed eater</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any finch of the genera <gen>Sporophila</gen>, and
<gen>Crithagra</gen>. They feed mainly on seeds.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed
gall</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any gall which resembles a seed,
formed on the leaves of various plants, usually by some species of
Phylloxera.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed leaf</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
cotyledon.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed lobe</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
cotyledon; a seed leaf.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed oil</b></col>, <cd>oil
expressed from the seeds of plants.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed
oyster</b></col>, <cd>a young oyster, especially when of a size suitable
for transplantation to a new locality.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed
pearl</b></col>, <cd>a small pearl of little value.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Seed plat</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Seed
plot</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the ground on which seeds are sown, to
produce plants for transplanting; a nursery.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed
stalk</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the stalk of an ovule or seed; a
funicle.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed tick</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>one
of several species of ticks resembling seeds in form and color.</cd> --
<col><b>Seed vessel</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>that part of a
plant which contains the seeds; a pericarp.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed
weevil</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous small
weevils, especially those of the genus <gen>Apion</gen>, which live in
the seeds of various plants.</cd> -- <col><b>Seed wool</b></col>,
<cd>cotton wool not yet cleansed of its seeds.</cd> <mark>[Southern
U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seed</ent><br/
<hw>Seed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sow
seed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To shed the seed.</def>
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To grow to maturity, and to produce seed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many interests have grown up, and <qex>seeded</qex>, and twisted
their roots in the crevices of many wrongs.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seed</ent><br/
<hw>Seed</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Seeded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seeding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sprinkle with
seed; to plant seeds in; to sow; <as>as, to <ex>seed</ex> a
field</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover thinly with something scattered; to
ornament with seedlike decorations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A sable mantle <qex>seeded</qex> with waking eyes.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To seed down</b></col>, <cd>to sow with grass
seed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedbox</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A capsule.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A plant
(<spn>Ludwigia alternifolia</spn>) which has somewhat cubical or
box-shaped capsules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedcake</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"cake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sweet cake or
cooky containing aromatic seeds, as caraway.</def>
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedcod</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"cod`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seedlip.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seeder</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, sows or plants seed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seediness</ent><br/
<hw>Seed`i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being seedy, shabby, or worn out; a state of wretchedness or
exhaustion.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What is called <qex>seediness</qex>, after a debauch, is a plain
proof that nature has been outraged.</q> <rj><qau>J. S.
Blackie.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seed-lac</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"-lac`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A species of lac.
See the Note under <er>Lac</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedless</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without seed or seeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedling</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
plant reared from the seed, as distinguished from one propagated by
layers, buds, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedlop</ent><br/
<ent>Seedlip</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Seed"lip`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Seed"lop`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<aemac/dle<aacute/p</ets>;
<ets>s<aemac/d</ets> seed + <ets>le<aacute/p</ets> basket.]</ety> <def>A
vessel in which a sower carries the seed to be scattered.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedman</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def>See <er>Seedsman</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedness</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Seedtime.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedsman</ent><br/
<hw>Seeds"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Seedsmen</plw> <pr>(-m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sower;
one who sows or scatters seed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>seedsman</qex><br/
Upon the slime and ooze scatters his grain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person who deals in seeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedtime</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"time`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<aemac/dt<imac/ma</ets>.]</ety> <def>The season proper for
sowing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While the earth remaineth, <qex>seedtime</qex> and harvest, and
cold and heat, and summer and winter, and day and night, shall not
cease.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. viii. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seedy</ent><br/
<hw>Seed"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Seedier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Seediest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding with seeds;
bearing seeds; having run to seeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a peculiar flavor supposed to be derived from
the weeds growing among the vines; -- said of certain kinds of French
brandy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Old and worn out; exhausted; spiritless; also, poor
and miserable looking; shabbily clothed; shabby looking; <as>as, he
looked <ex>seedy</ex>; a <ex>seedy</ex> coat</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Little Flanigan here . . . is a little <qex>seedy</qex>, as we say
among us that practice the law.</q> <rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seedy toe</b></col>, <cd>an affection of a horse's foot,
in which a cavity filled with horn powder is formed between the laminae
and the wall of the hoof.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seeing</ent><br/
<hw>See"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>conj.</pos> (but originally a
<pos>present participle</pos>). <def>In view of the fact (that);
considering; taking into account (that); insmuch as; since; because; --
followed by a dependent clause; <as>as, he did well, <ex>seeing</ex>
that he was so young</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wherefore come ye to me, <qex>seeing</qex> ye hate me?</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xxvi. 27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seek</ent><br/
<hw>Seek</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sick.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seek</ent><br/
<hw>Seek</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sought</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seeking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>seken</ets>, AS.
<ets>s<emac/can</ets>, <ets>s<emac/cean</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>s<omac/kian</ets>, LG. <ets>s<oum/ken</ets>, D. <ets>zoeken</ets>,
OHG. <ets>suohhan</ets>, G. <ets>suchen</ets>, Icel. <ets>saekja</ets>,
Sw. <ets>s<oum/ka</ets>, Dan. <ets>s<oum/ge</ets>, Goth.
<ets>s<omac/kjan</ets>, and E. <ets>sake</ets>. Cf. <er>Beseech</er>,
<er>Ransack</er>, <er>Sagacious</er>, <er>Sake</er>,
<er>Soc</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To go in search of; to look for;
to search for; to try to find.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The man saked him, saying, What <qex>seekest</qex> thou? And he
said, I <qex>seek</qex> my brethren.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xxxvii. 15,
16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inquire for; to ask for; to solicit; to
beseech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Others, tempting him, <qex>sought</qex> of him a sign.</q>
<rj><qau>Luke xi. 16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To try to acquire or gain; to strive after; to aim
at; <as>as, to <ex>seek</ex> wealth or fame; to <ex>seek</ex> one's
life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To try to reach or come to; to go to; to resort
to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Seek</qex> not Bethel, nor enter into Gilgal.</q>
<rj><qau>Amos v. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Since great Ulysses <qex>sought</qex> the Phrygian plains.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seek</ent><br/
<hw>Seek</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make search or
inquiry; to endeavor to make discovery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Seek</qex> ye out of the book of the Lord, and read.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xxxiv. 16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To seek</b></col>, <cd>needing to seek or search; hence,
unprepared.</cd> <ldquo/Unpracticed, unprepared, and still <xex>to
seek</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>To
seek after</b></col>, <cd>to make pursuit of; to attempt to find or
take.</cd> -- <col><b>To seek for</b></col>, <cd>to endeavor to
find.</cd> -- <col><b>To seek to</b></col>, <cd>to apply to; to resort
to; to court.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/All the earth <xex>sought
to</xex> Solomon, to hear his wisdom.<rdquo/ <au>1 Kings x. 24.</au> --
<col><b>To seek upon</b></col>, <cd>to make strict inquiry after; to
follow up; to persecute.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>To seek</qex><br/
<qex>Upon</qex> a man and do his soul unrest.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seeker</ent><br/
<hw>Seek"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
seeks; that which is used in seeking or searching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>One of a small heterogeneous sect
of the 17th century, in Great Britain, who professed to be seeking the
true church, ministry, and sacraments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A skeptic [is] ever seeking and never finds, like our new upstart
sect of <qex>Seekers</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bullokar.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seek-no-further</ent><br/
<hw>Seek"-no-fur`ther</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of
choice winter apple, having a subacid taste; -- formerly called
<altname>go-no-further</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seek-sorrow</ent><br/
<hw>Seek"-sor`row</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
contrives to give himself vexation.</def> <mark>[Archaic.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seel</ent><br/
<hw>Seel</hw> <pr>(s<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Seeled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Seeling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>siller</ets>,
<ets>ciller</ets>, fr. <ets>cil</ets> an eyelash, L.
<ets>cilium</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Falconry)</fld> <def>To
close the eyes of (a hawk or other bird) by drawing through the lids
threads which were fastened over the head.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fools climb to fall: fond hopes, like <qex>seeled</qex> doves for
want of better light, mount till they end their flight with falling.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Reading.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to shut or close, as the eyes; to
blind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Come, <qex>seeling</qex> night,<br/
Scarf up the tender eye of pitiful day.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cold death, with a violent fate, his sable eyes did
<qex>seel</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seel</ent><br/
<hw>Seel</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LG. <ets>sielen</ets> to lead
off water, F. <ets>siller</ets> to run ahead, to make headway, E.
<ets>sile</ets>, v.t.]</ety> <def>To incline to one side; to lean; to
roll, as a ship at sea.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seeling</ent><br/
<ent>Seel</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Seel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Seel"ing</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The rolling or agitation of a ship in a storm.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seel</ent><br/
<hw>Seel</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<aemac/l</ets>, from
<ets>s<aemac/l</ets> good, prosperous. See <er>Silly</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Good fortune; favorable opportunity; prosperity.
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/So have I <xex>seel</xex><rdquo/.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Time; season; <as>as, hay <ex>seel</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seelily</ent><br/
<hw>Seel"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a silly
manner.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seely</ent><br/
<hw>Seel"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Silly</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seem</ent><br/
<hw>Seem</hw> <pr>(s<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Seemed</conjf> <pr>(s<emac/md)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Seeming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>semen</ets> to seem, to become, befit, AS. <ets>s<emac/man</ets> to
satisfy, pacify; akin to Icel. <ets>saema</ets> to honor, to bear with,
conform to, <ets>saemr</ets> becoming, fit, <ets>s<omac/ma</ets> to
beseem, to befit, <ets>sama</ets> to beseem, <ets>semja</ets> to
arrange, settle, put right, Goth. <ets>samjan</ets> to please, and to E.
<ets>same</ets>. The sense is probably due to the adj.
<ets>seemly</ets>. <root/191. See <er>Same</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Seemly</er>.]</ety> <def>To appear, or to appear to be; to have a
show or semblance; to present an appearance; to look; to strike one's
apprehension or fancy as being; to be taken as.</def> <ldquo/It now
<xex>seemed</xex> probable.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou picture of what thou <qex>seem'st</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>seemed</qex> well pleased; all <qex>seemed</qex>, but
were not all.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a way which <qex>seemeth</qex> right unto a man; but the
end thereof are the ways of death.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xiv.
12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>It seems</b></col>, <cd>it appears; it is understood as
true; it is said.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A prince of Italy, <qex>it seems</qex>, entertained his mistress
on a great lake.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To appear; look.</syn> -- <usage><er>Seem</er>,
<er>Appear</er>. To <xex>appear</xex> has reference to a thing's being
presented to our view; as, the sun <xex>appears</xex>; to
<xex>seem</xex> is connected with the idea of <xex>semblance</xex>, and
usually implies an inference of our mind as to the probability of a
thing's being so; as, a storm <xex>seems</xex> to be coming. <ldquo/The
story <xex>appears</xex> to be true,<rdquo/ means that the facts, as
presented, go to show its truth; <ldquo/the story <xex>seems</xex> to be
true,<rdquo/ means that it has the semblance of being so, and we infer
that it is true. <ldquo/His first and principal care being to
<xex>appear</xex> unto his people such as he would have them be, and to
be such as he <xex>appeared</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><it>Ham.</it> Ay, madam, it is common.<br/
<it>Queen</it>. If it be,<br/
Why <qex>seems</qex> it so particular with thee?<br/
<it>Ham.</it> <qex>Seems</qex>, madam! Nay, it is; I know not
<ldquo/<qex>seems</qex>.<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seem</ent><br/
<hw>Seem</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To befit; to beseem.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seemer</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who seems; one who
carries or assumes an appearance or semblance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hence shall we see,<br/
If power change purpose, what our <qex>seemers</qex> be.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seeming</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a semblance, whether with
or without reality; apparent; specious; befitting; <as>as,
<ex>seeming</ex> friendship; <ex>seeming</ex> truth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My lord, you have lost a friend indeed;<br/
And I dare swear you borrow not that face<br/
Of <qex>seeming</qex> sorrow, it is sure your own.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seeming</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Appearance; show;
semblance; fair appearance; speciousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These keep<br/
<qex>Seeming</qex> and savor all the winter long.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Apprehension; judgment.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing more clear unto their <qex>seeming</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His persuasive words, impregned<br/
With reason, to her <qex>seeming</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seemingly</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In appearance; in show; in
semblance; apparently; ostensibly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This the father <qex>seemingly</qex> complied with.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seemingness</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"ing*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Semblance; fair appearance;
plausibility.</def> <rj><au>Sir K. Digby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seemless</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Unseemly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seemlily</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"li*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a seemly
manner.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seemliness</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"li*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
seemly: comeliness; propriety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1304 --></p>

<p><ent>Seemly</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"ly</hw> <pr>(s<emac/m"l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos><adjf>Seemlier</adjf>
<pr>(s<emac/m"l<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Seeliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Icel. <ets>s<ae/miligr</ets>, fr.
<ets>s<ae/mr</ets> becoming, fit; akin to <ets>samr</ets> same, E.
<ets>same</ets>; the sense being properly, the same or like, hence,
fitting. See <er>Seem</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>Suited to the
object, occasion, purpose, or character; suitable; fit; becoming;
comely; decorous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had a <qex>seemly</qex> nose.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am a woman, lacking wit<br/
To make a <qex>seemly</qex> answer to such persons.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Suspense of judgment and exercise of charity were safer and
<qex>seemlier</qex> for Christian men than the hot pursuit of these
controversies.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Becoming; fit; suitable; proper; appropriate;
congruous; meet; decent; decorous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seemly</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"ly</hw> <pr>(s<emac/m"l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Seemlier</adjf>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Seemliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>In a decent or suitable manner;
becomingly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Suddenly a men before him stood,<br/
Not rustic as before, but <qex>seemlier</qex> clad,<br/
As one in city or court or place bred.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seemlyhed</ent><br/
<hw>Seem"ly*hed</hw> <pr>(s<emac/m"l<ycr/*h<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[See <er>-hood</er>.]</ety> <def>Comely or decent appearance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Rom. of R. Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seen</ent><br/
<hw>Seen</hw> <pr>(s<emac/n)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>See</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seen</ent><br/
<hw>Seen</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Versed; skilled; accomplished.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Well <qex>seen</qex> in every science that mote be.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Noble Boyle, not less in nature <qex>seen</qex>,<br/
Than his great brother read in states and men.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipe</ent><br/
<ent>Seep</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Seep</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>Sipe</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<imac/pan</ets>
to distill.]</ety> <def>To run or soak through fine pores and
interstices; to ooze.</def> <mark>[Scot. & U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Water <qex>seeps</qex> up through the sidewalks.</q> <rj><qau>G.
W. Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipage</ent><br/
<ent>Seepage</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Seep"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>Sip"age</hw>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Water that seeped or oozed through a porous
soil.</def> <mark>[Scot. & U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seepage</ent><br/
<hw>Seep"age</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process of
seeping; percolation.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a fluid that seeps out of a container; <as>as,
<ex>seepage</ex> from a reservoir</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipy</ent><br/
<ent>Seepy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Seep"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>Sip"y</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Oozy; -- applied to land under cultivation that is
not well drained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seer</ent><br/
<hw>Seer</hw> <pr>(s<emac/r)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sore;
painful.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seer</ent><br/
<hw>Se"er</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sees.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seer</ent><br/
<hw>Seer</hw> <pr>(s<emac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>See</er>.]</ety> <def>A person who foresees events; a prophet.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seeress</ent><br/
<hw>Seer"ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female seer; a
prophetess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seerfish</ent><br/
<hw>Seer"fish`</hw> <pr>(s<emac/r"f<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A scombroid food fish of Madeira (<spn>Cybium
Commersonii</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seerhand</ent><br/
<hw>Seer"hand</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A kind of muslin of a texture between nainsook
and mull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seership</ent><br/
<hw>Seer"ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or quality of a
seer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seersucker</ent><br/
<hw>Seer"suck`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A light fabric,
originally made in the East Indies, of silk and linen, usually having
alternating stripes, and a slightly craped or puckered surface; also, a
cotton fabric of similar appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seerwood</ent><br/
<hw>Seer"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sear</er>.]</ety> <def>Dry wood.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>searwood</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seesaw</ent><br/
<hw>See"saw`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably a
reduplication of <ets>saw</ets>, to express the alternate motion to and
fro, as in the act of sawing.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A play among
children in which they are seated upon the opposite ends of a plank
which is balanced in the middle, and move alternately up and
down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A plank or board adjusted for this play.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A vibratory or reciprocating motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He has been arguing in a circle; there is thus a <qex>seesaw</qex>
between the hypothesis and fact.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Whist.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Crossruff</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seesaw</ent><br/
<hw>See"saw`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Seesawad</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seesawing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To move with a reciprocating
motion; to move backward and forward, or upward and downward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seesaw</ent><br/
<hw>See"saw`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to move backward and
forward in seesaw fashion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>seesaws</qex> himself to and fro.</q> <rj><qau>Ld.
Lytton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seesaw</ent><br/
<hw>See"saw`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Moving up and down, or to and fro;
having a reciprocating motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seet</ent><br/
<hw>Seet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Sit</er></mord>. <def>Sate; sat.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seeth</ent><br/
<hw>Seeth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Seethe</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seethe</ent><br/
<hw>Seethe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Seethed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> (<conjf>Sod</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>,
<mark>obs.</mark>); <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Seethed</conjf>,
<conjf>Sodden</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seething</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sethen</ets>, AS.
<ets>se<oacute/<?/an</ets>; akin to D. <ets>sieden</ets>, OHG.
<ets>siodan</ets>, G. <ets>sieden</ets>, Icel. <ets>sj<?/<?/a</ets>, Sw.
<ets>sjuda</ets>, Dan. <ets>syde</ets>, Goth. <ets>saubs</ets> a burnt
offering. Cf. <er>Sod</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Sodden</er>,
<er>Suds</er>.]</ety> <def>To decoct or prepare for food in hot liquid;
to boil; <as>as, to <ex>seethe</ex> flesh</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>seeth</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Set on the great pot, and <qex>seethe</qex> pottage for the sons
of the prophets.</q> <rj><qau>2 Kings iv. 38.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seethe</ent><br/
<hw>Seethe</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be a state of ebullition or
violent commotion; to be hot; to boil.</def> <rj><au>1 Sam. ii.
13.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A long Pointe, round which the Mississippi used to whirl, and
<qex>seethe</qex>, and foam.</q> <rj><qau>G. W. Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seether</ent><br/
<hw>Seeth"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pot for boiling
things; a boiler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like burnished gold the little <qex>seether</qex> shone.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seg</ent><br/
<hw>Seg</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sedge</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Sedge.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The gladen, and other species of Iris.</def>
<rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seg</ent><br/
<hw>Seg</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably from the root of L.
<ets>secare</ets> to cut.]</ety> <def>A castrated bull.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segar</ent><br/
<hw>Se*gar"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Cigar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seggar</ent><br/
<hw>Seg"gar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>saggard</ets> a seggar, <ets>seggard</ets> a sort of riding
surtout, contr. fr. <ets>safeguard</ets>.]</ety> <def>A case or holder
made of fire clay, in which fine pottery is inclosed while baking in the
kin.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>saggar</asp>, <asp>sagger</asp>,
and <asp>segger</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segge</ent><br/
<hw>Segge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
hedge sparrow.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segment</ent><br/
<hw>Seg"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>segmentum</ets>, fr. <ets>secare</ets> to cut, cut off: cf. F.
<ets>segment</ets>. See <er>Saw</er> a cutting instrument.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the parts into which any body naturally
separates or is divided; a part divided or cut off; a section; a
portion; <as>as, a <ex>segment</ex> of an orange; a <ex>segment</ex> of
a compound or divided leaf.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A part cut off from a figure by a
line or plane; especially, that part of a circle contained between a
chord and an arc of that circle, or so much of the circle as is cut off
by the chord; <as>as, the <ex>segment acb</ex> in the
Illustration</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A piece in the form
of the sector of a circle, or part of a ring; <as>as, the
<ex>segment</ex> of a sectional fly wheel or flywheel rim</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A segment gear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the cells or
division formed by segmentation, as in egg cleavage or in fissiparous
cell formation.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the divisions, rings, or
joints into which many animal bodies are divided; a somite; a metamere;
a somatome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Segment gear</b></col>, <cd>a piece for receiving or
communicating reciprocating motion from or to a cogwheel, consisting of
a sector of a circular gear, or ring, having cogs on the periphery, or
face.</cd> -- <col><b>Segment of a line</b></col>, <cd>the part of a
line contained between two points on it.</cd> -- <col><b>Segment of a
sphere</b></col>, <cd>the part of a sphere cut off by a plane, or
included between two parallel planes.</cd> -- <col><b>Ventral
segment</b></col>. <fld>(Acoustics)</fld> <cd>See <er>Loor</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 5.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segment</ent><br/
<hw>Seg"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>To divide or separate into parts in growth; to undergo
segmentation, or cleavage, as in the segmentation of the ovum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segmental</ent><br/
<hw>Seg*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Relating to, or being, a segment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat. & Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Of or
pertaining to the segments of animals; <as>as, a <ex>segmental</ex>
duct; <ex>segmental</ex> papillae.</as></def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or
pertaining to the segmental organs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Segmental duct</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
primitive duct of the embryonic excretory organs which gives rise to the
Wolffian duct and ureter; the pronephric duct.</cd> -- <col><b>Segmental
organs</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>The embryonic
excretory organs of vertebrates, consisting primarily of the segmental
tubes and segmental ducts</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>The
tubular excretory organs, a pair of which often occur in each of several
segments in annelids. They serve as renal organs, and often, also, as
oviducts and sperm ducts.</cd> -- <col><b>Segmental tubes</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the tubes which primarily open into the
segmental duct, some of which become the urinary tubules of the
adult.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segmentation</ent><br/
<hw>Seg`men*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
process of dividing into segments; specifically <fld>(Biol.)</fld>, a
self-division into segments as a result of growth; cell cleavage; cell
multiplication; endogenous cell formation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Segmentation cavity</b></col> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>the
cavity formed by the arrangement of the cells in segmentation or
cleavage of the ovum; the cavity of the blastosphere. In the gastrula
stage, the segmentation cavity in which the mesoblast is formed lies
between the entoblast and ectoblast. See Illust. of
<er>Invagination</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Segmentation nucleus</b></col>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>the body formed by fusion of the male and female
pronucleus in an impregnated ovum. See the Note under
<er>Pronucleus</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Segmentation of the
ovum</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Egg cleavage</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>the process by which the embryos of all the
higher plants and animals are derived from the germ cell. In the
simplest case, that of small ova destitute of food yolk, the ovum or egg
divides into two similar halves or segments (blastomeres), each of these
again divides into two, and so on, thus giving rise to a mass of cells
(mulberry mass, or <xex>morula</xex>), all equal and similar, from the
growth and development of which the future animal is to be formed. This
constitutes <stype>regular segmentation</stype>. Quite frequently,
however, the equality and regularity of cleavage is interfered with by
the presence of food yolk, from which results <xex>unequal
segmentation</xex>. See <er>Holoblastic</er>, <er>Meroblastic</er>,
<er>Alecithal</er>, <er>Centrolecithal</er>, <er>Ectolecithal</er>, and
<er>Ovum</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Segmentation sphere</b></col>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>the blastosphere, or morula. See
<er>Morula</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segmented</ent><br/
<hw>Seg"ment*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Divided into
segments or joints; articulated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segnity</ent><br/
<ent>Segnitude</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Seg"ni*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Seg"ni*ty</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>segnitas</ets>, fr.
<ets>segnis</ets> slow, sluggish.]</ety> <def>Sluggishness; dullness;
inactivity.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segno</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se"gno</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. See
<er>Sign</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A sign. See <er>Al
segno</er>, and <er>Dal segno</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sego</ent><br/
<hw>Se"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
liliaceous plant (<spn>Calochortus Nuttallii</spn>) of Western North
America, and its edible bulb; -- so called by the Ute Indians and the
Mormons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segregate</ent><br/
<hw>Seg"re*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>segregatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>segregare</ets> to separate; pref.
<ets>se-</ets> aside + <ets>grex</ets>, <ets>gregis</ets>, a flock or
herd. See <er>Gregarious</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Separate;
select.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Separated from others of the same
kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segregate</ent><br/
<hw>Seg"re*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Segregated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Segregating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To separate from
others; to set apart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are still <qex>segregated</qex>, Christians from Christians,
under odious designations.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segregate</ent><br/
<hw>Seg"re*gate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>To
separate from a mass, and collect together about centers or along lines
of fracture, as in the process of crystallization or
solidification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Segregation</ent><br/
<hw>Seg`re*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>segregatio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/gr<eacute/gation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
segregating, or the state of being segregated; separation from others; a
parting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Separation from a mass, and
gathering about centers or into cavities at hand through cohesive
attraction or the crystallizing process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seiches</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Seiches</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Local oscillations in level observed in the case
of some lakes, as Lake Geneva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seid</ent><br/
<hw>Seid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar <ets>seyid</ets>
prince.]</ety> <def>A descendant of Mohammed through his daughter Fatima
and nephew Ali.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seidlitz</ent><br/
<hw>Seid"litz</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Seidlitz, a village in Bohemia.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Sedlitz</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seidlitz powders</b></col>, <cd>effervescing salts,
consisting of two separate powders, one of which contains forty grains
of sodium bicarbonate mixed with two drachms of Rochell<?/ salt
(tartrate of potassium and sodium) and the other contains thirty-five
grains of tartaric acid. The powders are mixed in water, and drunk while
effervescing, as a mild cathartic; -- so called from the resemblance to
the natural water of Seidlitz. Called also <altname>Rochelle
powders</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Seidlitz water</b></col>, <cd>a
natural water from Seidlitz, containing magnesium, sodium, calcium, and
potassium sulphates, with calcium carbonate and a little magnesium
chloride. It is used as an aperient.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seigh</ent><br/
<hw>Seigh</hw> <pr>(s<amac/)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <mord><pos>imp.
sing.</pos> of <er>See</er>.</mord> <def><er>Saw</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seigneurial</ent><br/
<hw>Seign*eu"ri*al</hw> <pr>(s<emac/n*<umac/"r<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. <ets>seigneur</ets>. See
<er>Seignior</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the lord
of a manor; manorial.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Temple.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Vested with large powers; independent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seignior</ent><br/
<hw>Seign"ior</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>seignor</ets>, F. <ets>seigneur</ets>, cf. It. <ets>signore</ets>,
Sp. <ets>se<ntil/or</ets> from an objective case of L. <ets>senier</ets>
elder. See <er>Senior</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A lord; the lord of
a manor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A title of honor or of address in the South of
Europe, corresponding to <xex>Sir</xex> or <xex>Mr</xex>. in
English.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Grand Seignior</b></col>, <cd>the sultan of
Turkey.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seigniorage</ent><br/
<hw>Seign"ior*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>seigneuriage</ets>, OF. <ets>seignorage</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Something claimed or taken by virtue of sovereign prerogative;
specifically, a charge or toll deducted from bullion brought to a mint
to be coined; the difference between the cost of a mass of bullion and
the value as money of the pieces coined from it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If government, however, throws the expense of coinage, as is
reasonable, upon the holders, by making a charge to cover the expense
(which is done by giving back rather less in coin than has been received
in bullion, and is called <ldquo/levying a
<qex>seigniorage</qex><rdquo/), the coin will rise to the extent of the
<qex>seigniorage</qex> above the value of the bullion.</q> <rj><qau>J.
S. Mill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A share of the receipts of a business taken in
payment for the use of a right, as a copyright or a patent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seignioral</ent><br/
<hw>Seign"ior*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a seignior; seigneurial.</def> <ldquo/Kingly or <xex>seignioral</xex>
patronage.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seignioralty</ent><br/
<hw>Seign"ior*al*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The territory
or authority of a seignior, or lord.</def> <rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seigniorial</ent><br/
<hw>Seign*io"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Seigneurial</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seigniorize</ent><br/
<hw>Seign"ior*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To lord it
over.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As proud as he that <qex>seigniorizeth</qex> hell.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seigniory</ent><br/
<hw>Seign"ior*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>seignorie</ets>, OF.
<ets>seigneurie</ets>, F. <ets>seigneurie</ets>; cf. It.
<ets>signoria</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The power or authority of a
lord; dominion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O'Neal never had any <qex>seigniory</qex> over that country but
what by encroachment he got upon the English.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The territory over which a lord holds jurisdiction;
a manor.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>seigneury</asp>, and
<asp>seignory</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seine</ent><br/
<hw>Seine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>seine</ets>, or
AS. <ets>segene</ets>, b<?/th fr. L. <ets>sagena</ets>, Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Fishing.)</fld> <def>A large net, one edge of
which is provided with sinkers, and the other with floats. It hangs
vertically in the water, and when its ends are brought together or drawn
ashore incloses the fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seine boat</b></col>, <cd>a boat specially constructed to
carry and pay out a seine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seiner</ent><br/
<hw>Sein"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who fishes with a
seine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seining</ent><br/
<hw>Sein"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Fishing with a seine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seint</ent><br/
<hw>Seint</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Cincture</er>.]</ety> <def>A girdle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Girt with a <xex>seint</xex> of silk.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seint</ent><br/
<hw>Seint</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A saint.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seintuary</ent><br/
<hw>Sein"tu*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sanctuary.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seirfish</ent><br/
<hw>Seir"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Seerfish</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seirospore</ent><br/
<hw>Sei"ro*spore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a
cord + E. <ets>spore</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of several
spores arranged in a chain as in certain algae of the genus
<gen>Callithamnion</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seise</ent><br/
<hw>Seise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seize</er>.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This is the common spelling in the law phrase <xex>to be
seised of</xex> (an estate).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seisin</ent><br/
<hw>Sei"sin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seizin</er>.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismal</ent><br/
<ent>Seismic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Seis"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Seis"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>seismo`s</grk> an earthquake, from
<grk>sei`ein</grk> to shake.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to an
earthquake; caused by an earthquake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seismic vertical</b></col>, <cd>the point upon the
earth's surface vertically over the center of effort or focal point
whence the earthquake's impulse proceeds, or the vertical line
connecting these two points.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismogram</ent><br/
<hw>Seis"mo*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
earthquake + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>The
trace or record of an earth tremor, made by means of a
seismograph.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismograph</ent><br/
<hw>Seis"mo*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
an earthquake + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An
apparatus for registering the shocks and undulatory motions of
earthquakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismographic</ent><br/
<hw>Seis`mo*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a seismograph; indicated by a seismograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismography</ent><br/
<hw>Seis*mog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
writing about, or a description of, earthquakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The art of registering the shocks and undulatory
movements of earthquakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismological</ent><br/
<hw>Seis`mo*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to seismology.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Seis`mo*log"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismology</ent><br/
<hw>Seis*mol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
an earthquake + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science of
earthquakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismometer</ent><br/
<hw>Seis*mom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
an earthquake + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An
instrument for measuring the direction, duration, and force of
earthquakes and like concussions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismometric</ent><br/
<hw>Seis`mo*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to seismometry, or seismometer; <as>as, <ex>seismometric</ex>
instruments; <ex>seismometric</ex> measurements</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismometry</ent><br/
<hw>Seis*mom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The mensuration
of such phenomena of earthquakes as can be expressed in numbers, or by
their relation to the coordinates of space.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seismoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Seis"mo*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
an earthquake + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A
seismometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seity</ent><br/
<hw>Se"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>se</ets>
one's self.]</ety> <def>Something peculiar to one's self.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seizable</ent><br/
<hw>Seiz"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
seized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seize</ent><br/
<hw>Seize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Seized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Seizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>seisen</ets>,
<ets>saisen</ets>, OF. <ets>seisir</ets>, <ets>saisir</ets>, F.
<ets>saisir</ets>, of Teutonic origin, and akin to E. <ets>set</ets>.
The meaning is properly, to set, put, place, hence, to put in possession
of. See <er>Set</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fall
or rush upon suddenly and lay hold of; to gripe or grasp suddenly; to
reach and grasp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For by no means the high bank he could <qex>seize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Seek you to <qex>seize</qex> and gripe into your hands<br/
The royalties and rights of banished Hereford?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1305 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take possession of by force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At last they <qex>seize</qex><br/
The scepter, and regard not David's sons.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To invade suddenly; to take sudden hold of; to come
upon suddenly; <as>as, a fever <ex>seizes</ex> a patient</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hope and deubt alternate <qex>seize</qex> her seul.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(law)</fld> <def>To take possession of by virtue of
a warrant or other legal authority; <as>as, the sheriff <ex>seized</ex>
the debtor's goods</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To fasten; to fix.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As when a bear hath <qex>seized</qex> her cruel claws<br/
Upon the carcass of some beast too weak.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To grap with the mind; to comprehend fully and
distinctly; <as>as, to <ex>seize</ex> an idea</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To bind or fasten together with a
lashing of small stuff, as yarn or marline; <as>as, to <ex>seize</ex>
ropes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word, by writers on law, is commonly written
<xex>seise</xex>, in the phrase <xex>to be seised of</xex> (an estate),
as also, in composition, <xex>disseise</xex>,
<xex>disseisin</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To be seized of</b></col>, <cd>to have possession, or
right of possession; <as>as, A B was <ex>seized</ex> and possessed
<ex>of</ex> the manor of Dale.</as></cd> <ldquo/Whom age might <xex>see
seized of</xex> what youth made prize.<rdquo/ <au>Chapman.</au> --
<mcol><col><b>To seize on</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To seize
upon</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to fall on and grasp; to take hold on; to
take possession of suddenly and forcibly.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To catch; grasp; clutch; snatch; apprehend;
arrest; take; capture.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seizer</ent><br/
<hw>Seiz"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, seizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seizin</ent><br/
<hw>Sei"zin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>saisine</ets>. See <er>Seize</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Possession; possession of an estate of froehold.
It may be either in <xex>deed</xex> or in <xex>law</xex>; the former
when there is actual possession, the latter when there is a right to
such possession by construction of law. In some of the United States
<xex>seizin</xex> means merely <xex>ownership</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of taking possession.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The thing possessed; property.</def> <rj><au>Sir M.
Halle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Commonly spelt by writers on law
<xex>seisin</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Livery of seizin</b></col>. <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <cd>See
Note under <er>Livery</er>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seizing</ent><br/
<hw>Seiz"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of taking or grasping suddenly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The operation of
fastening together or lashing.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The cord or
lashing used for such fastening.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seizor</ent><br/
<hw>Sei"zor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One
who seizes, or takes possession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seizure</ent><br/
<hw>Sei"zure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of seizing, or the state of being seized; sudden and violent grasp or
gripe; a taking into possession; <as>as, the <ex>seizure</ex> of a
thief, a property, a throne, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Retention within one's grasp or power; hold;
possession; ownership.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Make o'er thy honor by a deed of trust,<br/
And give me <qex>seizure</qex> of the mighty wealth.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is seized, or taken possession of; a
thing laid hold of, or possessed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sejeant</ent><br/
<ent>Sejant</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se"jant</hw>, <hw>Se"jeant</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>s<eacute/ant</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>seoir</ets> to sit, L. <ets>sedere</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>Sitting, as a lion or other beast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sejant rampant</b></col>, <cd>sitting with the forefeet
lifted up.</cd> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sejein</ent><br/
<hw>Se*jein"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sejungere</ets>; pref. <ets>se-</ets> aside + <ets>jungere</ets> to
join. See <er>Join</er>.]</ety> <def>To separate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sejunction</ent><br/
<hw>Se*junc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sejunctio</ets>. See <er>Sejoin</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
disjoining, or the state of being disjoined.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sejungible</ent><br/
<hw>Se*jun"gi*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sejoin</er>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being disjoined.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Pearson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seke</ent><br/
<hw>Seke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sick.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seke</ent><br/
<hw>Seke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To seek.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sekes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se"kes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ a pen, a sacred inclosure, a shrine.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>A place in a pagan temple in which the images of the deities were
inclosed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selachian</ent><br/
<hw>Se*la"chi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Selachii.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selachii</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*la"chi*i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a fish having cartilages instead of bones.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of elasmobranchs including the sharks
and rays; the Plagiostomi. Called also <altname>Selacha</altname>,
<altname>Selache</altname>, and
<altname>Selachoidei</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selachoidei</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sel`a*choi"de*i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Selachii</er>, and <er>-oid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Selachii</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selachostomi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sel`a*chos"to*mi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Selachii</er>, and <er>Stoma</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of ganoid fishes which includes the paddlefish, in which
the mouth is armed with small teeth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selaginella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sel`a*gi*nel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
L. <ets>selago</ets>, <ets>-inis</ets>, a kind of plant.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of cryptogamous plants resembling
Lycopodia, but producing two kinds of spores; also, any plant of this
genus. Many species are cultivated in conservatories.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selah</ent><br/
<hw>Se"lah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>sel<amac/h</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Script.)</fld> <def>A word of
doubtful meaning, occuring frequently in the Psalms; by some, supposed
to signify silence or a pause in the musical performance of the
song.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beyond the fact that <qex>Selah</qex> is a musical term, we know
absolutely nothing about it.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. W. Smith (Bib.
Dict.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selcouth</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"couth</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l"k<oomac/th)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[AS. <ets>selc<umac/<edh/</ets>, <ets>seldc<umac/<edh/</ets>;
<ets>seld</ets> rare + <ets>c<umac/<edh/</ets> known. See
<er>Uncouth</er>.]</ety> <def>Rarely known; unusual; strange.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[She] wondered much at his so <qex>selcouth</qex> case.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seld</ent><br/
<hw>Seld</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/ld)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Seldom</er>.]</ety> <def>Rare; uncommon; unusual.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer. Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seld</ent><br/
<hw>Seld</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Rarely; seldom.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selden</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"den</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l"d<eit/n)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Seldom.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seldom</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"dom</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l"d<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<amorph>[Usually, <pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>More seldom</adjf>
<pr>(m<omac/r" s<ecr/l"d<ucr/m)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Most
seldom</adjf> <pr>(m<omac/st" s<ecr/l"d<ucr/m)</pr>; but sometimes also,
<adjf>Seldomer</adjf> <pr>(s<ecr/l"d<ucr/m*<etil/r)</pr>,
<adjf>Seldomest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>seldan</ets>,
<ets>seldon</ets>, <ets>seldum</ets>, fr. <ets>seld</ets> rare; akin to
OFries. <ets>sielden</ets>, D. <ets>zelden</ets>, G. <ets>selten</ets>,
OHG. <ets>seltan</ets>, Icel. <ets>sjaldan</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sielden</ets>, Sw. <ets>s<aum/llan</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sildaleiks</ets> marvelous.]</ety> <def>Rarely; not often; not
frequently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wisdom and youth are <qex>seldom</qex> joined in one.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seldom</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Rare;
infrequent.</def> <mark>[Archaic.]</mark> <ldquo/A suppressed and
<xex>seldom</xex> anger.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seldomness</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"dom*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Rareness.</def>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seldseen</ent><br/
<hw>Seld"seen`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>seldsiene</ets>.]</ety> <def>Seldom seen.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seldshewn</ent><br/
<hw>Seld"shewn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Seld</ets> +
<ets>shown</ets>.]</ety> <def>Rarely shown or exhibited.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Select</ent><br/
<hw>Se*lect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>selectus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>seligere</ets> to select; pref.
<ets>se-</ets> aside + <ets>levere</ets> to gather. See
<er>Legend</er>.]</ety> <def>Taken from a number by preferance; picked
out as more valuable or exellent than others; of special value or
exellence; nicely chosen; selected; choice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A few <qex>select</qex> spirits had separated from the crowd, and
formed a fit audience round a far greater teacher.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Select</ent><br/
<hw>Se*lect"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Selected</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Selecting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To choose and take from a
number; to take by preference from among others; to pick out; to cull;
<as>as, to <ex>select</ex> the best authors for perusal</as>.</def>
<ldquo/One peculiar nation to <xex>select</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pious chief . . . <br/
A hundred youths from all his train <qex>selects</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selectedly</ent><br/
<hw>Se*lect"ed*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With care and
selection.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selection</ent><br/
<hw>Se*lec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>selectio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/lection</ets>.]</ety> .
<def>The act of selecting, or the state of being selected; choice, by
preference.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is selected; a collection of things
chosen; <as>as, a choice <ex>selection</ex> of books</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Natural selection</b></col>. <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Natural</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selective</ent><br/
<hw>Se*lect"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Selecting; tending
to select.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>selective</qex> providence of the Almighty.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selectman</ent><br/
<hw>Se*lect"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Selectmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One of a board of town
officers chosen annually in the New England States to transact the
general public business of the town, and have a kind of executive
authority. The number is usually from three to seven in each
town.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The system of delegated town action was then, perhaps, the same
which was defined in an <ldquo/order made in 1635 by the inhabitants of
Charlestown at a full meeting for the government of the town, by
<qex>selectmen</qex>;<rdquo/ the name presently extended throughout New
England to municipal governors.</q> <rj><qau>Palfrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selectness</ent><br/
<hw>Se*lect"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
select.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selector</ent><br/
<hw>Se*lect"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>One
who selects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenate</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"e*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of selenic acid; -- formerly called also
<altname>seleniate</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenhydric</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`en*hy"dric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, hydrogen selenide,
<chform>H2Se</chform>, regarded as an acid analogous to sulphydric
acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenic</ent><br/
<hw>Se*len"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/l<eacute/nique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to selenium; derived from, or containing, selenium;
specifically, designating those compounds in which the element has a
higher valence as contrasted with <xex>selenious</xex>
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenide</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"e*nide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A binary compound of selenium, or a compound regarded as binary;
<as>as, ethyl <ex>selenide</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seleniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`e*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Selenium</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>. ]</ety> <def>Containing,
or impregnated with, selenium; <as>as, <ex>seleniferous</ex>
pyrites</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenio-</ent><br/
<hw>Se*le"ni*o-</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A combining
form (also used adjectively) denoting the <sig>presence of
selenium</sig> or <xex>its compounds</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>selenio</ex>-phosphate, a phosphate having selenium in place of all,
or a part, of the oxygen</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenious</ent><br/
<hw>Se*le"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/l<eacute/nieux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or containing, selenium; specifically, designating those
compounds in which the element has a lower valence as contrasted with
<contr>selenic</contr> compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenite</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"e*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of selenious acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenite</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"e*nite</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>selenites</ets>, Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ (sc. <?/<?/<?/), from <grk>selh`nh</grk> the moon. So
called from a fancied resemblance in luster or appearance to the
moon.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of gypsum, occuring in
transparent crystals or crystalline masses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenitical</ent><br/
<ent>Selenitic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sel`e*nit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sel`e*nit"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to selenite; resembling or containing selenite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenium</ent><br/
<hw>Se*le"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>selh`nh</grk> the moon. So called because of its chemical analogy
to <ets>tellurium</ets> (from L. <ets>tellus</ets> the earth), being, as
it were, a companion to it.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A nonmetallic
element of the sulphur group of atomic number 34, analogous to sulphur
in its compounds. It is found in small quantities with sulphur and some
sulphur ores, and obtained in the free state as a dark reddish powder or
crystalline mass, or as a dark metallic-looking substance. It exhibits
under the action of light a remarkable variation in electric
conductivity, and is used in certain electric apparatus. Symbol Se.
Atomic weight 78.96.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seleniuret</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`e*ni"u*ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A selenide.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seleniureted</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`e*ni"u*ret`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Combined with selenium as in a selenide; <as>as,
<ex>seleniureted</ex> hydrogen</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>seleniuretted</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenocentric</ent><br/
<hw>Se*le`no*cen"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>selh`nh</grk> the moon + E. <ets>centric</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>As seen or estimated from the center of the
moon; with the moon central.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenograph</ent><br/
<hw>Se*le"no*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A picture or
delineation of the moon's surface, or of any part of it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenographer</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`e*nog"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled
in selenography.</def> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenographical</ent><br/
<ent>Selenographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sel`e*no*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sel`e*no*graph"i*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/l<eacute/nographique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to selenography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenographist</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`e*nog"ra*phist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
selenographer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenography</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`e*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>selh`nh</grk> the moon + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
science that treats of the physical features of the moon; --
corresponding to <xex>physical geography</xex> in respect to the
earth.</def> <ldquo/Accurate <xex>selenography</xex>, or description of
the moon.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T. Browne. </au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenology</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`e*nol"o*gy</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>selh`nh</grk> the
moonn + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch of astronomy which
treats of the moon.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sel`e*no*log"i*cal</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selenonium</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`e*no"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Selen</ets>ium + sulph<ets>onium</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hypothetical radical of selenium, analogous to
sulphonium.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self</ent><br/
<hw>Self</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/lf)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>self</ets>, <ets>seolf</ets>, <ets>sylf</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>self</ets>, OFries. <ets>self</ets>, D. <ets>zelf</ets>, G.
<ets>selb</ets>, <ets>selber</ets>, <ets>selbst</ets>, Dan.
<ets>selv</ets>. Sw. <ets>sjelf</ets>, Icel. <ets>sj<amac/lfr</ets>,
Goth. <ets>silba</ets>. Cf. <er>Selvage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Same; particular; very; identical.</def> <mark>[Obs., except in the
compound selfsame.]</mark> <ldquo/On these <xex>self</xex> hills.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir. W. Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To shoot another arrow that <qex>self</qex> way<br/
Which you did shoot the first.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At that <qex>self</qex> moment enters Palamon.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having its own or a single nature or character, as
in color, composition, etc., without addition or change; unmixed;
<as>as, a <ex>self</ex> bow, one made from a single piece of wood;
<ex>self</ex> flower or plant, one which is wholly of one color;
<ex>self</ex>-colored</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self</ent><br/
<hw>Self</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Selves</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The individual as the object of
his own reflective consciousness; the man viewed by his own cognition as
the subject of all his mental phenomena, the agent in his own
activities, the subject of his own feelings, and the possessor of
capacities and character; a person as a distinct individual; a being
regarded as having personality.</def> <ldquo/Those who liked their real
<xex>selves</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man's <qex>self</qex> may be the worst fellow to converse with
in the world.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>self</qex>, the I, is recognized in every act of
intelligence as the subject to which that act belongs. It is I that
perceive, I that imagine, I that remember, I that attend, I that
compare, I that feel, I that will, I that am conscious.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, personal interest, or love of private
interest; selfishness; <as>as, <ex>self</ex> is his whole
aim</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Personification; embodiment.</def>
<mark>[Poetic.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She was beauty's <qex>self</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Self</xex> is united to certain personal pronouns
and pronominal adjectives to express emphasis or distinction. Thus, for
emphasis; I <xex>myself</xex> will write; I will examine for
<xex>myself</xex>; thou <xex>thyself</xex> shalt go; thou shalt see for
<xex>thyself</xex>; you <xex>yourself</xex> shall write; you shall see
for <xex>yourself</xex>; he <xex>himself</xex> shall write; he shall
examine for <xex>himself</xex>; she <xex>herself</xex> shall write; she
shall examine for <xex>herself</xex>; the child <xex>itself</xex> shall
be carried; it shall be present <xex>itself</xex>. It is also used
reflexively; as, I abhor <xex>myself</xex>; thou enrichest
<xex>thyself</xex>; he loves <xex>himself</xex>; she admires
<xex>herself</xex>; it pleases <xex>itself</xex>; we walue
<xex>ourselves</xex>; ye hurry <xex>yourselves</xex>; they see
<xex>themselves</xex>. <xex>Himself</xex>, <xex>herself</xex>,
<xex>themselves</xex>, are used in the nominative case, as well as in
the objective. <ldquo/Jesus <xex>himself</xex> baptized not, but his
disciples.<rdquo/ <au>John iv. 2.</au></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ex>self</ex> is used in the formation of innumerable
compounds, usually of obvious signification, in most of which it denotes
either the agent or the object of the action expressed by the word with
which it is joined, or the person in behalf of whom it is performed, or
the person or thing to, for, or towards whom or which a quality,
attribute, or feeling expressed by the following word belongs, is
directed, or is exerted, or from which it proceeds; or it denotes the
subject of, or object affected by, such action, quality, attribute,
feeling, or the like; as, <ex>self</ex>-abandoning,
<ex>self</ex>-abnegation, self-abhorring, <xex>self-absorbed</xex>,
<ex>self</ex>-accusing, <ex>self</ex>-adjusting, <ex>self</ex>-balanced,
<ex>self</ex>-boasting, <ex>self</ex>-canceled, <ex>self</ex>-combating,
<ex>self</ex>-commendation, <ex>self</ex>-condemned,
<ex>self</ex>-conflict, <ex>self</ex>-conquest,
<ex>self</ex>-constituted, <ex>self</ex>-consumed,
<ex>self</ex>-contempt, <ex>self</ex>-controlled,
<ex>self</ex>-deceiving, <ex>self</ex>-denying, <ex>self</ex>-destroyed,
<ex>self</ex>-disclosure, <ex>self</ex>-display, <ex>self</ex>-dominion,
<ex>self</ex>-doomed, <ex>self</ex>-elected, <ex>self</ex>-evolved,
<ex>self</ex>-exalting, <ex>self</ex>-excusing, <ex>self</ex>-exile,
<ex>self</ex>-fed, <ex>self</ex>-fulfillment, <ex>self</ex>-governed,
<ex>self</ex>-harming, <ex>self</ex>-helpless,
<ex>self</ex>-humiliation, <ex>self</ex>-idolized,
<ex>self</ex>-inflicted, <ex>self</ex>-improvement,
<ex>self</ex>-instruction, <ex>self</ex>-invited, <ex>self</ex>-judging,
<ex>self</ex>-justification, <ex>self</ex>-loathing,
<ex>self</ex>-loving, <ex>self</ex>-maintenance, <ex>self</ex>-mastered,
<ex>self</ex>-nourishment, <ex>self</ex>-perfect,
<ex>self</ex>-perpetuation, <ex>self</ex>-pleasing,
<ex>self</ex>-praising, <ex>self</ex>-preserving,
<ex>self</ex>-questioned, <ex>self</ex>-relying,
<ex>self</ex>-restraining, <ex>self</ex>-revelation,
<ex>self</ex>-ruined, <ex>self</ex>-satisfaction, <ex>self</ex>-support,
<ex>self</ex>-sustained, <ex>self</ex>-sustaining,
<ex>self</ex>-tormenting, <ex>self</ex>-troubling, <ex>self</ex>-trust,
<ex>self</ex>-tuition, <ex>self</ex>-upbraiding, <ex>self</ex>-valuing,
<ex>self</ex>-worshiping, and many others.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-abased</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-a*based"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Humbled by
consciousness of inferiority, unworthiness, guilt, or shame.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-abasement</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-a*base"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Degradation of one's self by one's own act.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Humiliation or abasement proceeding from
consciousness of inferiority, guilt, or shame.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-abasing</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-a*bas"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lowering or humbling one's
self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-abhorrence</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ab*hor"rence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Abhorrence
of one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-abnegation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ab`ne*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Self-denial; self-renunciation; self-sacrifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-abuse</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-abuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
abuse of one's own self, powers, or faculties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Self-deception; delusion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Masturbation; onanism; self-pollution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-accused</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-ac*cused"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Accused by
one's self or by one's conscience.</def> <ldquo/Die
<xex>self-accused</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-acting</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-act"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Acting of or by
one's self or by itself; -- said especially of a machine or mechanism
which is made to perform of or for itself what is usually done by human
agency; automatic; <as>as, a <ex>self-acting</ex> feed apparatus; a
<ex>self-acting</ex> mule; a <ex>self-acting</ex> press</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-action</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Action by, or
originating in, one's self or itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-active</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ac"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Acting of one's
self or of itself; acting without depending on other agents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-activity</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ac*tiv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being self-active; self-action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-adjusting</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ad*just"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>Capable of assuming a desired position or
condition with relation to other parts, under varying circumstances,
without requiring to be adjusted by hand; -- said of a piece in
machinery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Self-adjusting bearing</b></col> <fld>(Shafting)</fld>,
<cd>a bearing which is supported in such a manner that it may tip to
accomodate flexure or displacement of the shaft.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-admiration</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ad`mi*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Admiration
of one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-affairs</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-af*fairs"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>One's own
affairs; one's private business.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1306 --></p>

<p><ent>Self-affrighted</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-af*fright"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Frightened
at or by one's self.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-aggrandizement</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ag*gran"dize*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
aggrandizement of one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-annihilated</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-an*ni"hi*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Annihilated by one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-annihilation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-an*ni`hi*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Annihilation by one's own acts; annihilation of one's
desires.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-applause</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ap*plause"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Applause of
one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-applying</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ap*ply"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Applying to
or by one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-approving</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ap*prov"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Approving
one's own action or character by one's own judgment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One <qex>self-approving</qex> hour whole years outweighs<br/
Of stupid starers and of loud huzzas.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-asserting</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-as*sert"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>asserting
one's self, or one's own rights or claims; hence, putting one's self
forward in a confident or assuming manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-assertion</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-as*ser"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
asserting one's self, or one's own rights or claims; the quality of
being self-asserting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-assertive</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-as*sert"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Disposed to
self-assertion; self-asserting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-assumed</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-as*sumed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Assumed by
one's own act, or without authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-assured</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-as*sured`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Assured by or
of one's self; self-reliant; complacent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-banished</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ban"ished</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Exiled
voluntarily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-begotten</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-be*got"ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Begotten by
one's self, or one's own powers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-born</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-born`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Born or produced
by one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-binder</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-bind"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A reaping
machine containing mechanism for binding the grain into
sheaves.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-centred</ent><br/
<ent>Self-centered</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Self`-cen"tered</hw>, <hw>Self`-cen"tred</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Centered in itself, or in
one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There hangs the ball of earth and water mixt,<br/
<qex>Self-centered</qex> and unmoved.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>interested solely or primiarily in oneself, or one's
own welfare; disinclined to consider the interests of others; selfish;
egotistical.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-centring</ent><br/
<ent>Self-centering</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Self`-cen"ter*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Self`-cen"tring</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Centering in one's
self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-centration</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-cen*tra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being self-centered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-charity</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-char"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Self-love.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-color</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-col`or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A color not mixed
or variegated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-colored</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-col"ored</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being of a
single color; -- applied to flowers, animals, and textile
fabrics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-command</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-com*mand"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Control over
one's own feelings, temper, etc.; self-control.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-commune</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-com*mune"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Self-communion.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-communicative</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-com*mu"ni*ca*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Imparting or communicating by its own powers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-communion</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-com*mun"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Communion
with one's self; thoughts about one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-complacency</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-com*pla"cen*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality of being self-complacent.</def> <rj><au>J. Foster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-complacent</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-com*pla"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Satisfied
with one's own character, capacity, and doings;
self-satisfied.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-conceit</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*ceit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Conceit of
one's self; an overweening opinion of one's powers or
endowments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Egotism</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-conceited</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*ceit"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having an overweening
opinion of one's own powers, attainments; vain; conceited.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Self`-con*ceit"ed*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-concern</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*cern"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Concern for
one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-condemnation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con`dem*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Condemnation of one's self by one's own judgment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-confidence</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con"fi*dence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being self-confident; self-reliance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A feeling of <qex>self-confidence</qex> which supported and
sustained him.</q> <rj><qau>Beaconsfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-confident</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con"fi*dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Confident of
one's own strength or powers; relying on one's judgment or ability;
self-reliant.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Self`-con"fi*dent*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-conjugate</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con"ju*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>Having the two things that are conjugate parts
of the same figure; <as>as, <ex>self-conjugate</ex>
triangles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-conscious</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con"scious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Conscious of one's acts or state as belonging to, or originating
in, one's self.</def> <ldquo/My <xex>self-conscious</xex> worth.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Conscious of one's self as an object of the
observation of others; <as>as, the speaker was too
<ex>self-conscious</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-consciousness</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con"scious*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being self-conscious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-considering</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*sid"er*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Considering in one's own mind; deliberating.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-consistency</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*sist"en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being self-consistent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-consistent</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*sist"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consistent
with one's self or with itself; not deviation from the ordinary standard
by which the conduct is guided; logically consistent throughout; having
each part consistent with the rest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-consuming</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*sum"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consuming
one's self or itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-contained</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*tained"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having self-control; reserved; uncommunicative; wholly engrossed in
one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>Having all the essential working
parts connected by a bedplate or framework, or contained in a case,
etc., so that mutual relations of the parts do not depend upon fastening
outside of the machine itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Self-contained steam engine</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A
steam engine having both bearings for the crank shaft attached to the
frame of the engine</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A steam engine and boiler
combined and fastened together; a portable steam engine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-contained Underwater Breathing Apparatus</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*tained" Underwater Breathing Apparatus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A portable device to allow divers to breathe while
under water, consisting of one or two tanks of compressed air which are
strapped onto the back of the diver, and are connected by tubing to a
mouthpiece through which the diver receives the air from the tanks at
rate adjustable by a valve; -- called also <altname>SCUBA</altname>,
<altname>SCUBA gear</altname>, or <altname>SCUBA
apparatus</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-contradiction</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con`tra*dic"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act
of contradicting one's self or itself; repugnancy in conceptions or in
terms; a proposition consisting of two members, one of which contradicts
the other; <as>as, to be and not to be at the same time is a
<ex>self-contradiction</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-contradictory</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con`tra*dict"o*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Contradicting one's self or itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-control</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*trol"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Control of
one's self; restraint exercised over one's self; self-command.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-convicted</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*vict"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Convicted by
one's own consciousness, knowledge, avowal, or acts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-conviction</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-con*vic"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
convicting one's self, or the state of being self-convicted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-created</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-cre*at"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Created by
one's self; not formed or constituted by another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-culture</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-cul"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Culture,
training, or education of one's self by one's own efforts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-deceit</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*ceit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
deceiving one's self, or the state of being self-deceived;
self-deception.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-deceived</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*ceived"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Deceived or
misled respecting one's self by one's own mistake or error.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-deception</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*cep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Self-deceit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-defence</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*fence"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Self-defense</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-defense</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*fense"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
defending one's own person, property, or reputation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In self-defense</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>in
protection of self, -- it being permitted in law to a party on whom a
grave wrong is attempted to resist the wrong, even at the peril of the
life of the assailiant.</cd> <rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-defensive</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*fen"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Defending,
or tending to defend, one's own person, property, or
reputation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-degradation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-deg`ra*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act
of degrading one's self, or the state of being so degraded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-delation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Accusation of
one's self.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-delusion</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*lu"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
deluding one's self, or the state of being thus deluded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-denial</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*ni"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The denial of
one's self; forbearing to gratify one's own desires;
self-sacrifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-denying</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*ny"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Refusing to
gratify one's self; self-sacrificing.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Self`-de*ny"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-dependent</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*pend"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dependent on
one's self; self-depending; self-reliant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-depending</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*pend"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Depending on one's
self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-depraved</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*praved"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Corrupted or
depraved by one's self.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-destroyer</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*stroy"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
destroys himself; a suicide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-destruction</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*struc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
destruction of one's self; self-murder; suicide.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-destructive</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*struc"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Destroying, or tending to destroy, one's self or itself;
rucidal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-determination</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*ter`mi*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Determination by one's self; or, determination of one's acts or
states without the necessitating force of motives; -- applied to the
voluntary or activity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-determining</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*ter"min*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable
of self-determination; <as>as, the <ex>self-determining</ex> power of
will</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-devised</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*vised"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Devised by
one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-devoted</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*vot"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Devoted in
person, or by one's own will.</def> <rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-devotement</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*vote"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Self-devotion.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-devotion</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*vo"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
devoting one's self, or the state of being self-devoted; willingness to
sacrifice one's own advantage or happiness for the sake of others;
self-sacrifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-devouring</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-de*vour"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Devouring
one's self or itself.</def> <rj><au>Danham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-diffusive</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-dif*fu"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having power
to diffuse itself; diffusing itself.</def> <rj><au>Norris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-discipline</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-dis"ci*pline</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Correction
or government of one's self for the sake of improvement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-distrust</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-dis*trust"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Want of
confidence in one' self; diffidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-educated</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ed"u*ca`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Educated by
one's own efforts, without instruction, or without pecuniary assistance
from others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-elective</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-e*lect"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
right of electing one's self, or, as a body, of electing its own
members.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-enjoyment</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-en*joy"ment</hw>, <pr>(?)</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <def>Enjoyment of
one's self; self-satisfaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-esteem</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-es*teem"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The holding a
good opinion of one's self; self-complacency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-estimation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-es`ti*ma"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
estimating one's self; self-esteem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-evidence</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ev"i*dence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being self-evident.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-evident</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ev"i*dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Evident
without proof or reasoning; producing certainty or conviction upon a
bare presentation to the mind; <as>as, a <ex>self-evident</ex>
proposition or truth</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Self`-ev"i*dent*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-evolution</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ev`o*lu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Evolution
of one's self; development by inherent quality or power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-exaltation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ex`al*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
exalting one's self, or the state of being so exalted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-examinant</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ex*am"i*nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
examines himself; one given to self-examination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The humiliated <qex>self-examinant</qex> feels that there is evil
in our nature as well as good.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-examination</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ex*am`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
examination into one's own state, conduct, and motives, particularly in
regard to religious feelings and duties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-excite</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ex*cite"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>To
energize or excite (the field magnets of a dynamo) by induction from the
residual magnetism of its cores, leading all or a part of the current
thus produced through the field-magnet coils.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-existence</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ex*ist"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Inherent
existence; existence possessed by virtue of a being's own nature, and
independent of any other being or cause; -- an attribute peculiar to
God.</def> <rj><au>Blackmore.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-existent</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ex*ist"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Existing of
or by himself, independent of any other being or cause; -- <as>as, God
is the only <ex>self-existent</ex> being</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>self-explaining</ent><br/
<hw>self`-ex*plain"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Explaining
itself; capable of being understood without explanation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-exposure</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ex*po"sure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
exposing one's self; the state of being so exposed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-fertilization</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-fer`ti*li*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The fertilization of a flower by pollen from the
same flower and without outer aid; autogamy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-fertilized</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-fer"ti*lized</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Fertilized by pollen from the same
flower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-glorious</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-glo"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Springing
from vainglory or vanity; vain; boastful.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-government</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-gov"ern*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of governing one's self, or the state of being governed by
one's self; self-control; self-command.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, government of a community, state, or nation
by the joint action of the mass of people constituting such a civil
body; also, the state of being so governed; democratic government;
democracy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is to <qex>self-government</qex>, the great principle of
popular representation and administration, -- the system that lets in
all to participate in the councels that are to assign the good or evil
to all, -- that we may owe what we are and what we hope to be.</q>
<rj><qau>D. Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-gratulation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-grat`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Gratulation of one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-hardening</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-hard"en*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>
<def>Designating, or pert. to, any of various steels that harden when
heated to above a red heat and cooled in air, usually in a blast of cold
air with moderate rapidity, without quenching. Such steels are alloys of
iron and carbon with manganese, tungsten and manganese, chromium,
molybdenum and manganese, etc. They are chiefly used as high-speed
steels.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Self`-hard"en*ed</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-heal</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-heal"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A blue-flowered labiate plant (<spn>Brunella vulgaris</spn>); the
healall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-healing</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-heal"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
power or property of healing itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-help</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-help"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of aiding one's self, without depending on the aid of
others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The right or fact of redressing or
preventing wrongs by one's own action without recourse to legal
proceedings, as in self-defense, distress, abatement of a nuisance,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>developing one's capabilities or solving one's own
problems, especially psychological problems, without the assistance of a
professional, by independent study or by association with other
individuals having similar interests.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-help</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-help"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>of, pertaining to,
or useful for the process of developing one's capabilities or solving
one's problems; <as>as, <ex>self-help</ex> books on writing for
profit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-homicide</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-hom"i*cide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
killing one's self; suicide.</def> <rj><au>Hakewill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfhood</ent><br/
<hw>Self"hood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Existence as a
separate self, or independent person; conscious personality;
individuality.</def> <rj><au>Bib. Sacra.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-ignorance</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ig"no*rance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Ignorance of
one's own character, powers, and limitations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-ignorant</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-ig"no*rant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Ignorant of
one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-imparting</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-im*part"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Imparting by
one's own, or by its own, powers and will.</def>
<rj><au>Norris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-importance</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-im*por"tance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
exaggerated estimate of one's own importance or merit, esp. as
manifested by the conduct or manners; self-conceit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-important</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-im*por"tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having or
manifesting an exaggerated idea of one's own importance or
merit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-imposed</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-im*posed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Voluntarily
taken on one's self; <as>as, <ex>self-imposed</ex> tasks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-imposture</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-im*pos"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Imposture
practiced on one's self; self-deceit.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-indignation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-in`dig*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Indignation at one's own character or actions.</def>
<rj><au>Baxter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-induction</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-in*duc"tion</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>
<def>Induction in a circuit due to the action of one portion of a
current upon an adjacent portion during periods of varying current
strength. The nature of the induction is such as to oppose the action
which produces it.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-indulgence</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-in*dul"gence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Indulgence
of one's appetites, desires, or inclinations; -- the opposite of
<ant>self-restraint</ant>, and <ant>self-denial</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-indulgent</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-in*dul"gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Indulging
one's appetites, desires, etc., freely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-interest</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-in"ter*est</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Private
interest; the interest or advantage of one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-interested</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-in"ter*est*ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Particularly concerned
for one's own interest or happiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-involution</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-in`vo*lu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Involution
in one's self; hence, abstraction of thought; reverie.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfish</ent><br/
<hw>Self"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Caring
supremely or unduly for one's self; regarding one's own comfort,
advantage, etc., in disregard, or at the expense, of those of
others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They judge of things according to their own private appetites and
<qex>selfish</qex> passions.</q> <rj><qau>Cudworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In that throng of <qex>selfish</qex> hearts untrue.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Ethics)</fld> <def>Believing or teaching that the
chief motives of human action are derived from love of self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hobbes and the <qex>selfish</qex> school of philosophers.</q>
<rj><qau>Fleming.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfishly</ent><br/
<hw>Self"ish*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a selfish manner; with
regard to private interest only or chiefly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfishness</ent><br/
<hw>Self"ish*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
selfish; exclusive regard to one's own interest or happiness; that
supreme self-love or self-preference which leads a person to direct his
purposes to the advancement of his own interest, power, or happiness,
without regarding those of others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Selfishness</qex>, -- a vice utterly at variance with the
happiness of him who harbors it, and, as such, condemned by
self-love.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J. Mackintosh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Self-love</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfism</ent><br/
<hw>Self"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Concentration of
one's interests on one's self; self-love; selfishness.</def>
<rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfist</ent><br/
<hw>Self"ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A selfish person.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>I. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-justifier</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-jus"ti*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
excuses or justifies himself.</def> <rj><au>J. M. Mason.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-kindled</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-kin"dled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Kindled of
itself, or without extraneous aid or power.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-knowing</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-know"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Knowing one's self, or one's own character, powers, and
limitations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Knowing of itself, without help from
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-knowledge</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-knowl"edge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Knowledge of
one's self, or of one's own character, powers, limitations,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfless</ent><br/
<hw>Self"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no regard to self;
unselfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lo now, what hearts have men! they never mount<br/
As high as woman in her <qex>selfless</qex> mood.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selflessness</ent><br/
<hw>Self"less*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
selfless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-life</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-life`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Life for one's
self; living solely or chiefly for one's own pleasure or good.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-love</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-love`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The love of one's
self; desire for personal happiness; tendency to seek one's own benefit
or advantage.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Self-love</qex>, the spring of motion, acts the soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Selfishness.</syn> -- <usage><er>Self-love</er>,
<er>Selfishness</er>. The term <xex>self-love</xex> is used in a twofold
sense: 1. It denotes that longing for <xex>good</xex> or for
<xex>well-being</xex> which actuates the breasts of all, entering into
and characterizing every special desire. In this sense it has no moral
quality, being, from the nature of the case, neither good nor evil. 2.
It is applied to a voluntary regard for the gratification of special
desires. In this sense it is morally good or bad according as these
desires are conformed to duty or opposed to it. <xex>Selfishness</xex>
is always voluntary and always wrong, being that regard to our own
interests, gratification, etc., which is sought or indulged at the
expense, and to the injury, of others. <ldquo/So long as
<xex>self-love</xex> does not degenerate into <xex>selfishness</xex>, it
is quite compatible with true benevolence.<rdquo/ <au>Fleming.</au>
<ldquo/Not only is the phrase <xex>self-love</xex> used as synonymous
with the desire of happiness, but it is often confounded with the word
<xex>selfishness</xex>, which certainly, in strict propriety, denotes a
very different disposition of mind.<rdquo/ <au>Slewart.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-luminous</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-lu"mi*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Possessing in
itself the property of emitting light.</def> <rj><au>Sir D.
Brewster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1307 --></p>

<p><ent>Self-made</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-made`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made by one's
self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Self-made man</b></col>, <cd>a man who has risen from
poverty or obscurity by means of his own talents or
energies.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-mettle</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-met`tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Inborn mettle or
courage; one's own temper.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-motion</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-mo"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Motion given by
inherent power, without external impulse; spontaneous or voluntary
motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Matter is not induced with <qex>self-motion</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cheyne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-moved</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-moved"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Moved by inherent
power., without the aid of external impulse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-moving</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-mov"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Moving by
inherent power, without the aid of external impulse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-murder</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-mur"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Suicide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-murderer</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-mur"der*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
suicide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-neglecting</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-neg*lect"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
neglecting of one's self, or of one's own interests.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Self-love, my liege, is not so vile a sin<br/
As <qex>self-neglecting</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfness</ent><br/
<hw>Self"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Selfishness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir. P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-one</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-one"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Secret.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-opinion</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-o*pin"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Opinion,
especially high opinion, of one's self; an overweening estimate of one's
self or of one's own opinion.</def> <rj><au>Collier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-opinioned</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-o*pin"ioned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
high opinion of one's self; opinionated; conceited.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-originating</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-o*rig"i*na`ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Beginning with, or springing from, one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-partiality</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-par`ti*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That
partiality to himself by which a man overrates his own worth when
compared with others.</def> <rj><au>Kames.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-perplexed</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-per*plexed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Perplexed by
doubts originating in one's own mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-posited</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-pos"it*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Disposed or
arranged by an action originating in one's self or in itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These molecular blocks of salt are <qex>self-posited</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tyndall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-positing</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-pos"it*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>The act of disposing or
arranging one's self or itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>self-positing</qex> of the molecules.</q> <rj><qau>R.
Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-possessed</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-pos*sessed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Composed or
tranquil in mind, manner, etc.; undisturbed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-possession</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-pos*ses"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
possession of one's powers; calmness; self-command; presence of mind;
composure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-praise</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-praise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Praise of one's
self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-preservation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-pres`er*va"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
preservation of one's self from destruction or injury.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-propagating</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-prop"a*ga`ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Propagating by one's self or by itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-registering</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-reg"is*ter*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Registering itself; -- said of any instrument so contrived as to
record its own indications of phenomena, whether continuously or at
stated times, as at the maxima and minima of variations; <as>as, a
<ex>self-registering</ex> anemometer or barometer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-regulated</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-reg"u*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Regulated
by one's self or by itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-regulative</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-reg"u*la*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending or
serving to regulate one's self or itself.</def>
<rj><au>Whewell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reliance</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*li"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Reliance on
one's own powers or judgment; self-trust.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reliant</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*li"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Reliant upon
one's self; trusting to one's own powers or judgment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-renunciation</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*nun`ci*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act
of renouncing, or setting aside, one's own wishes, claims, etc.;
self-sacrifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-repellency</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*pel"len*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being self-repelling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-repelling</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*pel"ling</hw>, <pr>(<?/)</pr> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made up of
parts, as molecules or atoms, which mutually repel each other; <as>as,
gases are <ex>self-repelling</ex></as>.</def> <-- gases aren't
self-repelling! Like charges are. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-repetition</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-rep`e*ti"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Repetition
of one's self or of one's acts; the saying or doing what one has already
said or done.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reproach</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*proach"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
reproaching one's self; censure by one's own conscience.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reproached</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*proached"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Reproached
by one's own conscience or judgment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reproaching</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*proach"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Reproaching one's self.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Self`-re*proach"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reproof</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*proof"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
reproving one's self; censure of one's conduct by one's own
judgment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reproved</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*proved"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Reproved by
one's own conscience or one's own sense of guilt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reproving</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*prov"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Reproving
one's self; reproving by consciousness of guilt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reprovingly</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*prov"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a self-reproving
way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-repugnant</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*pug"nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Self-contradictory; inconsistent.</def>
<rj><au>Brougham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-repulsive</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*pul"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Self-repelling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-respect</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*spect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Respect for
one's self; regard for one's character; laudable self-esteem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-restrained</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*strained"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Restrained
by one's self or itself; restrained by one's own power or
will.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-restraint</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-re*straint"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Restraint
over one's self; self-control; self-command.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-reverence</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-rev"er*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A reverent
respect for one's self.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-righteous</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-right"eous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Righteous in
one's own esteem; pharisaic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-righteousness</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-right"eous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being self-righteous; pharisaism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-sacrifice</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-sac"ri*fice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
sacrificing one's self, or one's interest, for others;
self-devotion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-sacrificing</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-sac"ri*fi`cing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Yielding
up one's own interest, feelings, etc; sacrificing one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selfsame</ent><br/
<hw>Self"same</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Self</ets>, a.
+ <ets>same</ets>.]</ety> <def>Precisely the same; the very same;
identical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His servant was healed in the <qex>selfsame</qex> hour.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. viii. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-satisfaction</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-sat`is*fac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being self-satisfied.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-satisfied</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-sat"is*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Satisfied
with one's self or one's actions; self-complacent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-satisfying</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-sat"is*fy`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Giving
satisfaction to one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-seeker</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-seek`er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who seeks only his own
interest, advantage, or pleasure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-seeking</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-seek`ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Seeking one's own interest
or happiness; selfish.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-seeking</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-seek`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or habit of seeking
one's own interest or happiness; selfishness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-slaughter</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-slaugh"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Suicide.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-starter</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-start"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mechanism (usually one
operated by electricity, compressed air, a spring, or an explosive gas),
attached to an internal-combustion engine, as on an automobile, and used
as a means of starting the engine without cranking it by hand; -- called
also a <altname>starter</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-sufficiency</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-suf*fi"cien*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being self-sufficient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-sufficient</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-suf*fi"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Sufficient for one's self without external aid or
cooperation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Neglect of friends can never be proved rational till we prove the
person using it omnipotent and <qex>self-sufficient</qex>, and such as
can never need any mortal assistance.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having an overweening confidence in one's own
abilities or worth; hence, haughty; overbearing.</def> <ldquo/A rash and
<xex>self-sufficient</xex> manner.<rdquo/ <rj><au>I.
Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-sufficing</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-suf*fi"cing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sufficing
for one's self or for itself, without needing external aid;
self-sufficient.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Self`-suf*fi"cing*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au>J. C. Shairp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-suspended</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-sus*pend"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suspended by
one's self or by itself; balanced.</def> <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-suspicious</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-sus*pi"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suspicious
or distrustful of one's self.</def> <rj><au>Baxter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-taught</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-taught`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Taught by one's
own efforts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-tormentor</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-tor*ment"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
torments himself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-torture</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-tor"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
inflicting pain on one's self; pain inflicted on one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-trust</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-trust`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Faith in one's self;
self-reliance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-uned</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-uned"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[E.
<ets>self</ets> + L. <ets>unus</ets> one.]</ety> <def>One with itself;
separate from others.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sylvester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-view</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-view`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A view of one's
self; specifically, carefulness or regard for one's own
interests.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-will</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-will"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>selfwill</ets>.]</ety> <def>One's own will, esp. when opposed to
that of others; obstinacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-willed</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-willed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Governed by
one's own will; not yielding to the wishes of others;
obstinate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-willedness</ent><br/
<hw>Self`-willed"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Obstinacy.</def>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-worship</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-wor`ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The idolizing
of one's self; immoderate self-conceit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Self-wrong</ent><br/
<hw>Self"-wrong`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Wrong done by a
person to himself.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selion</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>seillon</ets> a measure of land, F. <ets>sillon</ets> a ridge,
furrow, LL. <ets>selio</ets> a measure of land.]</ety> <def>A short
piece of land in arable ridges and furrows, of uncertain quantity; also,
a ridge of land lying between two furrows.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seljukian</ent><br/
<hw>Sel*juk"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to <persfn><etsep>Seljuk</etsep></persfn>, a Tartar chief who embraced
Mohammedanism, and began the subjection of Western Asia to that faith
and rule; of or pertaining to the dynasty founded by him, or the empire
maintained by his descendants from the 10th to the 13th century.</def>
<rj><au>J. H. Newman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seljuckian</ent><br/
<hw>Sel*juck"i*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A member of the family of
<persfn><etsep>Seljuk</etsep></persfn>; an adherent of that family, or
subject of its government;</def> (<pluf>pl.</pluf>) <def>the dynasty of
Turkish sultans sprung from Seljuk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sell</ent><br/
<hw>Sell</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Self.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sell</ent><br/
<hw>Sell</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sill.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sell</ent><br/
<hw>Sell</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A cell; a
house.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sell</ent><br/
<hw>Sell</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>selle</ets>, L. <ets>sella</ets>, akin to <ets>sedere</ets> to sit.
See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A saddle for a horse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He left his lofty steed with golden <qex>self</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A throne or lofty seat.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sell</ent><br/
<hw>Sell</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sold</conjf> <pr>(s<omac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Selling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sellen</ets>,
<ets>sillen</ets>, AS. <ets>sellan</ets>, <ets>syllan</ets>, to give, to
deliver; akin to OS. <ets>sellian</ets>, OFries. <ets>sella</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sellen</ets>, Icel. <ets>selja</ets> to hand over, to sell, Sw.
<ets>s<aum/lja</ets> to sell, Dan. <ets>s<ae/lge</ets>, Goth.
<ets>saljan</ets> to offer a sacrifice; all from a noun akin to E.
<ets>sale</ets>. Cf. <er>Sale</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To transfer
to another for an equivalent; to give up for a valuable consideration;
to dispose of in return for something, especially for money. It is the
correlative of <inv>buy</inv>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If thou wilt be perfect, go and <qex>sell</qex> that thou hast,
and give to the poor.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. xix. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am changed; I'll go <qex>sell</qex> all my land.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sell</xex> is corellative to <xex>buy</xex>, as one
party <xex>buys</xex> what the other <xex>sells</xex>. It is
distinguished usually from <xex>exchange</xex> or <xex>barter</xex>, in
which one commodity is given for another; whereas in <xex>selling</xex>
the consideration is usually money, or its representative in current
notes.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a matter of bargain and sale of; to accept a
price or reward for, as for a breach of duty, trust, or the like; to
betray.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You would have <qex>sold</qex> your king to slaughter.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To impose upon; to trick; to deceive; to make a fool
of; to cheat.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sell one's life dearly</b></col>, <cd>to cause much
loss to those who take one's life, as by killing a number of one's
assailants.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To sell</b></col> (anything)
<col><b>out</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to dispose of it wholly or entirely;
as, he had <xex>sold out</xex> his corn, or his interest in a
business.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sell</ent><br/
<hw>Sell</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
practice selling commodities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will buy with you, <qex>sell</qex> with you; . . . but I will
not eat with you.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be sold; <as>as, corn <ex>sells</ex> at a good
price</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sell out</b></col>, <cd>to sell one's whole stock in
trade or one's entire interest in a property or a
business.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sell</ent><br/
<hw>Sell</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An imposition; a cheat; a hoax.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sellenders</ent><br/
<ent>Sellanders</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sel"lan*ders</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sel"len*ders</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Sallenders</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seller</ent><br/
<hw>Sell"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sells.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selters water</ent><br/
<hw>Sel"ters wa"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A mineral water from
<xex>Sellers</xex>, in the district of Nassau, Germany, containing much
free carbonic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seltzer water</ent><br/
<hw>Selt"zer wa"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <er>Selters
water</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seltzo-gene</ent><br/
<hw>Selt"zo-gene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Seltzer</ets> water + the root of Gr. <?/<?/<?/ to be
born.]</ety> <def>A gazogene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selvedge</ent><br/
<ent>Selvage</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sel"vage</hw>, <hw>Sel"vedge</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Self</ets> + <ets>edge</ets>, i. e., its own
proper edge; cf. OD. <ets>selfegge</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
edge of cloth which is woven in such a manner as to prevent
raveling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The edge plate of a lock, through which the bolt
passes.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining.)</fld> <def>A layer of clay or decomposed
rock along the wall of a vein. See <er>Gouge</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
4.</def> <rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selvedged</ent><br/
<ent>Selvaged</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sel"vaged</hw>, <hw>Sel"vedged</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a selvage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selvagee</ent><br/
<hw>Sel`va*gee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A skein or hank of rope yarns wound round with yarns or marline, --
used for stoppers, straps, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selve</ent><br/
<hw>Selve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Self; same.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selves</ent><br/
<hw>Selves</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Self</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sely</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Silly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer. Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semaeostomata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*mae`o*stom"a*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ a military standard + <?/<?/<?/,
<?/<?/<?/, mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
Discophora having large free mouth lobes. It includes
<gen>Aurelia</gen>, and <gen>Pelagia</gen>. Called also
<altname>Semeostoma</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semantical</ent><br/
<ent>Semantic</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Sem*an"tic</hw>, <hw>Sem*an"tic*al</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n. sing. <or/ pl.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>shmantikos</grk> having
meaning, from <grk>sh^ma</grk> a sign.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>pertaining to the meanings of words.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>of or pertaining to semantics.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semantics</ent><br/
<hw>Sem*an"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. sing. <or/ pl.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>shmantikos</grk> having meaning, from <grk>sh^ma</grk> a
sign.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>the study of the meanings of words and of
the sense development of words; -- formerly called
<altname>semasiology</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a doctrine and philosophical approach to language
and its relationship to thought and behavior, developed by Alfred
Korzybski (1879-1950), which holds that the capacity to express ideas
and thereby improve one's interaction with others and one's environment
is enhanced by training in the more critical use of words and other
symbols; -- also called <altname><er>general
semantics</er></altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>the meanings of words as they are used to achieve an
effect; especially, the multiple meanings of words or the multiplicity
of words having the same meaning; -- used in referring to the confusion
that can be caused (intentionally or unintentionally) by multiple
meanings; <as>as, there's no real difference, it's only a matter of
<ex>semantics</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semaphore</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"a*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sh^ma</grk> a sign + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/maphore</ets>.]</ety> <def>A signal telegraph; an
apparatus for giving signals by the disposition of lanterns, flags,
oscillating arms, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semaphorical</ent><br/
<ent>Semaphoric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sem`a*phor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sem`a*phor"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/maphorique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
semaphore, or semaphores; telegraphic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semaphorically</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`a*phor"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By means of a
semaphore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semaphorist</ent><br/
<hw>Se*maph"o*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who manages
or operates a semaphore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semasiology</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ma`si*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>shmasi`a</grk> signification + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Philol.)</fld> <def>The science of meanings or sense development
(of words); the explanation of the development and changes of the
meanings of words; -- more commonly referred to as
<altname>semantics</altname>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Se*ma`si*o*log"ic*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sematic</ent><br/
<hw>Se*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(s<esl/*m<acr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>sh^ma</grk>, <grk>sh`matos</grk>, sign, token.]</ety>
<def>Significant; ominous; serving as a warning of danger; -- applied
esp. to the warning colors or forms of certain animals.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sematology</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`a*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sh^ma</grk>, <grk>sh`matos</grk>, sign + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The doctrine of signs as the expression of thought or reasoning;
the science of indicating thought by signs.</def>
<rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> semantics.</syn>
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sematrope</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"a*trope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sh^ma</grk> sign + <grk>tre`pein</grk> to turn. ]</ety> <def>An
instrument for signaling by reflecting the rays of the sun in different
directions.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semblable</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"bla*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., from
<ets>sembler</ets> to seem, resemble, L. <ets>similare</ets>,
<ets>simulare</ets>. See <er>Simulate</er>.]</ety> <def>Like; similar;
resembling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semblable</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"bla*ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Likeness; representation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semblably</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"bla*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In like manner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semblance</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"blance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Semblable</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Seeming;
appearance; show; figure; form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thier <qex>semblance</qex> kind, and mild their gestures were.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Likeness; resemblance, actual or apparent;
similitude; <as>as, the <ex>semblance</ex> of worth; <ex>semblance</ex>
of virtue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Only <qex>semblances</qex> or imitations of shells.</q>
<rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semblant</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"blant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>semblant</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Like;
resembling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Seeming, rather than real; apparent.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semblant</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"blant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Show;
appearance; figure; semblance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His flatterers made <qex>semblant</qex> of weeping.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The face.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif
(Luke xxiv. 5).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semblative</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"bla*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And all is <qex>semblative</qex> a woman's part.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semble</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sembler</ets>. See <er>Semblable</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To imitate; to make a representation or likeness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where <qex>sembling</qex> art may carve the fair effect.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>It seems; -- chiefly used
impersonally in reports and judgments to express an opinion in reference
to the law on some point not necessary to be decided, and not intended
to be definitely settled in the cause.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semble</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"ble</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like; resembling.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Hudson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sembling</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"bling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Assemble</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The practice of
attracting the males of Lepidoptera or other insects by exposing the
female confined in a cage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is often adopted by collectors in order to procure
specimens of rare species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>seme</ent><br/
<hw>seme</hw> <pr>(s<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sh^ma</grk> a sign.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Linguistics)</fld>
<def>A linguistic sign.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Linguistics)</fld> <def>A basic component of
meaning of a morpheme, especially one which cannot be decomposed into
more basic components; a primitive concept.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seme</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*m<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sem<eacute/</ets>, fr. <ets>semer</ets> to sow.]</ety>
<fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Sprinkled or sown; -- said of field, or a charge,
when strewed or covered with small charges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiography</ent><br/
<ent>Semeiography</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se`mei*og"ra*phy</hw>
<pr>(s<emac/`m<isl/*<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<ycr/)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Se`mi*og"ra*phy</hw>
<pr>(s<emac/`m<icr/*<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<ycr/)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>shmei^on</grk> sign + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A description of the signs of disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiologioal</ent><br/
<ent>Semeiological</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se`mei*o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Se`mi*o*log"io*al</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the science of signs, or the systematic use of signs; <as>as, a
<ex>semeiological</ex> classification of the signs or symptoms of
disease; a <ex>semeiological</ex> arrangement of signs used as
signals.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiology</ent><br/
<ent>Semeiology</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se`mei*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(s<emac/`m<esl/*<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/
<it>or</it> s<emac/`m<isl/*<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Se`mi*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m`<esl/*<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>
}</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>shmei^on</grk> a mark, a sign +
<ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The study of signs as an element of
communication; the analysis of systems of communication; -- also called
<altname>semiotics</altname>.</def> <specif>Specifically:</specif>
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The science of the signs or symptoms
of disease; symptomatology.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The art of using
signs in signaling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiotic</ent><br/
<ent>Semeiotic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se`mei*ot"ic</hw> <pr>(s<emac/`m<esl/*<ocr/t"<icr/k
<it>or</it> s<ecr/`m<esl/*<ocr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Se`mi*ot"ic</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>shmeiwtiko`s</grk>, fr. <grk>shmei^on</grk> a mark, a sign.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating to signs or indications; pertaining to the
language of signs, or to language generally as indicating
thought.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the signs or
symptoms of diseases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiotics</ent><br/
<ent>Semeiotics</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se`mei*ot"ics</hw> <pr>(s<emac/`m<isl/*<ocr/t"<icr/ks)</pr>,
<it>or</it> <hw>Se`mi*ot"ics</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The study of signs as an element of communication; the analysis of
systems of communication; -- also called
<altname>semiology</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a theory of signs and symbols, including as branches
<er>semantics</er>, <er>pragmatics</er> and <er>syntactics</er>.</def>
<au>[RHUD]</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semele</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"e*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Myth.)</fld> <def>A daughter of Cadmus, and
by Zeus mother of Bacchus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semen</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se"men</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"m<ecr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Semina</plw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m"<icr/*n<adot/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., from the root of
<ets>serere</ets>, <ets>satum</ets>, to sow. See <er>Sow</er> to scatter
seed.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The seed of
plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The seed or fecundating fluid
of male animals; sperm. It is a white or whitish viscid fluid secreted
by the testes, characterized by the presence of spermatozoids to which
it owes its generative power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Semen contra</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Semen
cinae</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Semen cynae</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a
strong aromatic, bitter drug, imported from <city>Aleppo</city> and
Barbary, said to consist of the leaves, peduncles, and unexpanded
flowers of various species of <gen>Artemisia</gen>;
wormseed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semeniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`e*nif"er*ous</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m`<esl/*n<icr/f"<etil/r*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Seminiferous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1308 --></p>

<p><ent>Semester</ent><br/
<hw>Se*mes"ter</hw> <pr>(s<esl/*m<ecr/s"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[G., from L. <ets>semestris</ets> half-yearly; <ets>sex</ets> six +
<ets>mensis</ets> a month.]</ety> <def>A period of six months;
especially, a term in a college or university which divides the year
into two terms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>semi</ets>; akin to Gr.
<?/<?/<?/, Skr. <ets>s<amac/mi</ets>-, AS. <ets>s<amac/m</ets>-, and
prob. to E. <ets>same</ets>, from the division into two parts of the
same size. Cf. <er>Hemi-</er>, <er>Sandelend</er>.]</ety> <def>A prefix
signifying <xex>half</xex>, and sometimes <xex>partly</xex> or
<xex>imperfectly</xex>; <as>as, <ex>semi</ex>annual, half yearly;
<ex>semi</ex>transparent, imperfectly transparent</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The prefix <xex>semi</xex> is joined to another word
either with the hyphen or without it. In this book the hyphen is omitted
except before a capital letter; as, <xex>semi</xex>acid,
<xex>semi</xex>aquatic, <xex>semi</xex>-Arian, <xex>semi</xex>axis,
<xex>semi</xex>calcareous.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiacid</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ac"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slightly acid;
subacid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiacidified</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*a*cid"i*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
acidified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiadherent</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ad*her"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Adherent part
way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiamplexicaul</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*am*plex"i*caul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Partially amplexicaul; embracing the stem half
round, as a leaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiangle</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*an`gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>The half of a given, or measuring, angle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiannual</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*an"nu*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Half-yearly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiannually</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*an"nu*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Every half
year.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiannular</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*an"nu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
figure of a half circle; forming a semicircle.</def>
<rj><au>Grew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-Arian</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i-A"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Arian</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>A member of a
branch of the Arians which did not acknowledge the Son to be
consubstantial with the Father, that is, of the same substance, but
admitted him to be of a like substance with the Father, not by nature,
but by a peculiar privilege.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-Arian</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i-A"ri*an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Semi-Arianism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-Arianism</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i-A"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
doctrines or tenets of the Semi-Arians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiaxis</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ax"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>One half of the axis of an ellipse or other figure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semibarbarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*bar*ba"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
barbarous; partially civilized.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One
partly civilized.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semibarbaric</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*bar*bar"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
barbarous or uncivilized; <as>as, <ex>semibarbaric</ex>
display</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semibarbarism</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*bar"ba*rism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being half barbarous or uncivilized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semibarbarous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*bar"ba*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
barbarous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semibreve</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*breve`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>semi-</ets> + <ets>breve</ets>: cf. F. <ets>semi-breve</ets>, It.
<ets>semibreve</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Formerly written
<asp>semibref</asp>.]</altsp> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A note of half the
time or duration of the breve; -- now usually called a <xex>whole
note</xex>. It is the longest note in general use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semibrief</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*brief`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>A semibreve.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semibull</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*bull`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(R.C.Ch.)</fld>
<def>A bull issued by a pope in the period between his election and
coronation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicalcareous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*cal*ca"re*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half or
partially calcareous; <as>as, a <ex>semicalcareous</ex>
plant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicalcined</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*cal*cined"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
calcined; <as>as, <ex>semicalcined</ex> iron</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicastrate</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*cas"trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To deprive
of one testicle.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sem`i*cas*tra"tion</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>,<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicentennial</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*cen*ten"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to half of a century, or a period of fifty years; <as>as, a
<ex>semicentennial</ex> commemoration</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicentennial</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*cen*ten"ni*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fiftieth
anniversary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semichaotic</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*cha*ot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partially
chaotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semichorus</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*cho`rus</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A half
chorus; a passage to be sung by a selected portion of the voices, as the
female voices only, in contrast with the full choir.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-Christianized</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i-Chris"tian*ized</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
Christianized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicircle</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*cir`cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The half of a circle; the part of a circle bounded by
its diameter and half of its circumference.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
semicircumference.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A body in the form of half of a circle, or half of a
circumference.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An instrument for measuring angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicircled</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*cir`cled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Semicircular.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicircular</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*cir"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
form of half of a circle.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Semicircular canals</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>,
<cd>certain canals of the inner ear. See under
<er>Ear</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi circumference</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i cir*cum"fer*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Half of
a circumference.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicirque</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*cirque</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A semicircular
hollow or opening among trees or hills.</def>
<rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicolon</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*co`lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The punctuation
mark [;] indicating a separation between parts or members of a sentence
more distinct than that marked by a comma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicolumn</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*col`umn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A half column; a
column bisected longitudinally, or along its axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicolumnar</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*co*lum"nar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a
semicolumn; flat on one side and round on the other; imperfectly
columnar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicompact</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*com*pact"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half compact;
imperfectly indurated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiconscious</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*con"scious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
conscious; imperfectly conscious.</def> <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicope</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*cope`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A short cope, or
an inferior kind of cope.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi crustaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i crus*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
crustaceous; partially crustaceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicrystalline</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*crys"tal*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Half crystalline; -- said of certain eruptive
rocks composed partly of crystalline, partly of amorphous
matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicubical</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*cu"bic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the square root of the cube of a
quantity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Semicubical parabola</b></col>, <cd>a curve in which the
ordinates are proportional to the square roots of the cubes of the
abscissas.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicupium</ent><br/
<ent>Semicubium</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sem`i*cu"bi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sem`i*cu"pi*um</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL., fr. L. <ets>semi</ets>
half + <ets>cupa</ets> tub, cask.]</ety> <def>A half bath, or one that
covers only the lower extremities and the hips; a sitz-bath; a half
bath, or hip bath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semicylyndrical</ent><br/
<ent>Semicylindric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sem`i*cy*lin"dric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sem`i*cy*lyn"dric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Half cylindrical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semideistical</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*de*is"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
deistical; bordering on deism.</def> <rj><au>S. Miller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidemiquaver</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*dem"i*qua`ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A demisemiquaver; a thirty-second note.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidetached</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*de*tached"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
detached; partly distinct or separate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Semidetached house</b></col>, <cd>one of two tenements
under a single roof, but separated by a party wall.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidiameter</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*di*am"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Half of a diameter; a right line, or the length
of a right line, drawn from the center of a circle, a sphere, or other
curved figure, to its circumference or periphery; a radius.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidiapason</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*di`a*pa"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>An imperfect octave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidiapente</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*di`a*pen"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>An imperfect or diminished fifth.</def>
<rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidiaphaneity</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*di`a*pha*ne"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Half or
imperfect transparency; translucency.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidiaphanous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*di*aph"a*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half or
imperfectly transparent; translucent.</def>
<rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidiatessaron</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*di`a*tes"sa*ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An imperfect or diminished fourth.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-Diesel</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i-Die"sel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Designating an
internal-combustion engine of a type resembling the Diesel engine in
using as fuel heavy oil which is injected in a spray just before the end
of the compression stroke and is fired without electrical ignition. The
fuel is sprayed into an iron box (called a <xex>hot bulb</xex> or
<xex>hot pot</xex>) opening into the combustion chamber, and heated for
ignition by a blast-lamp until the engine is running, when it is,
ordinarily, kept red hot by the heat of combustion.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiditone</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*di"tone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>semi-</ets> + <ets>ditone</ets>: cf. It. <ets>semiditono</ets>. Cf.
<er>Hemiditone</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Mus.)</fld> <def>A lesser third,
having its terms as 6 to 5; a hemiditone.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidiurnal</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*di*ur"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Pertaining to, or accomplished in, half a day, or twelve hours;
occurring twice every day.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or traversed in, six hours, or in
half the time between the rising and setting of a heavenly body; <as>as,
a <ex>semidiurnal</ex> arc</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidome</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*dome`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>A roof or ceiling covering a semicircular room or recess, or one of
nearly that shape, as the apse of a church, a niche, or the like. It is
approximately the quarter of a hollow sphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidouble</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*dou`ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>An office or feast celebrated with less solemnity than the double
ones. See <er>Double</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 8.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semidouble</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*dou"ble</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the
outermost stamens converted into petals, while the inner ones remain
perfect; -- said of a flower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semifable</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*fa`ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is
part fable and part truth; a mixture of truth and fable.</def>
<rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiflexed</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*flexed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
bent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semifloret</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*flo`ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Semifloscule</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semifloscular</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*flos"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Semiflosculous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semifloscule</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*flos`cule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A floscule, or florest, with its corolla prolonged into a
strap-shaped petal; -- called also
<altname>semifloret</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiflosculous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*flos"cu*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having all the florets ligulate, as in the
dandelion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semifluid</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*flu"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Imperfectly
fluid.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A semifluid
substance.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiform</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*form`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A half form; an
imperfect form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiformed</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*formed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half formed;
imperfectly formed; <as>as, <ex>semiformed</ex> crystals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiglutin</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*glu"tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A peptonelike body, insoluble in alcohol, formed by
boiling collagen or gelatin for a long time in water.
<xex>Hemicollin</xex>, a like body, is also formed at the same time, and
differs from semiglutin by being partly soluble in alcohol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semihistorical</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*his*tor"i*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half or
party historical.</def> <rj><au>Sir G. C. Lewis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semihoral</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ho"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Half-hourly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiindurated</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*in"du*ra`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Imperfectly
indurated or hardened.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilapidified</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*la*pid"i*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Imperfectly changed into stone.</def> <rj><au>Kirwan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilens</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*lens`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Opt.)</fld>
<def>The half of a lens divided along a plane passing through its
axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilenticular</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*len*tic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
lenticular or convex; imperfectly resembling a lens.</def>
<rj><au>Kirwan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiligneous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*lig"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half or
partially ligneous, as a stem partly woody and partly
herbaceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiliquid</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*liq"uid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half liquid;
semifluid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiliquidity</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*li*quid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being semiliquid; partial liquidity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilogical</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half logical;
partly logical; said of fallacies.</def> <rj><au>Whately.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilor</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*lor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>similor</ets>, <ets>semilor</ets>.]</ety> <def>A yellowish alloy of
copper and zinc. See <er>Simplor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilunar</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*lu"nar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shaped like a
half moon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Semilunar bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a bone
of the carpus; the lunar. See <er>Lunar</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Semilunar valves</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Sigmoid
valves</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the valves at the
beginning of the aorta and of the pulmonary artery which prevent the
blood from flowing back into the ventricle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilunar</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*lu"nar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
semilunar bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilunary</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*lu"na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Semilunar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilunate</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*lu"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Semilunar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semilune</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*lune`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>The half of a lune.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semimetal</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*met`al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An element possessing metallic properties in an inferior degree and
not malleable, as arsenic, antimony, bismuth, molybdenum, uranium,
etc.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semimetallic</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*me*tal"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a semimetal; possessing metallic properties in
an inferior degree; resembling metal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semimonthly</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*month"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Coming or made
twice in a month; <as>as, <ex>semimonthly</ex> magazine; a
<ex>semimonthly</ex> payment.</as></def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>Something done or made every half month; esp., a semimonthly
periodical.</def></def2> -- <def2><pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a semimonthly
manner; at intervals of half a month.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semimute</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*mute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the faculty
of speech but imperfectly developed or partially lost.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semimute</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*mute`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A semimute
person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminal</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>seminalis</ets>, fr. <ets>semen</ets>, <ets>seminis</ets>, seed,
akin to <ets>serere</ets> to sow: cf. F. <ets>seminal</ets>. See
<er>Sow</er> to scatter seed.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pertaining to,
containing, or consisting of, seed or semen; <as>as, the
<ex>seminal</ex> fluid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Contained in seed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>Holding the first place in a
series of developed results or consequents; serving as a source, or
first principle; giving rise to related ideas or results; germinal;
radical; primary; original; <as>as, <ex>seminal</ex> principles of
generation; <ex>seminal</ex> virtue; a <ex>seminal</ex>
discovery.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The idea of God is, beyond all question or comparison, the one
great <qex>seminal</qex> principle.</q> <rj><qau>Hare.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seminal leaf</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a seed
leaf, or cotyleden.</cd> -- <col><b>Seminal receptacle</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <er>Spermatheca</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminal</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seed.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminality</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*nal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being seminal.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminar</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*nar"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G. See
<er>Seminary</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A group of students
engaged, under the guidance of an instructor, in original research in a
particular line of study, and in the exposition of the results by
theses, lectures, etc.; -- formerly called also
<altname>seminary</altname>, now seldom used in this sense.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminarist</ent><br/
<ent>Seminarian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sem`i*na"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sem"i*na*rist</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/minariste</ets>.]</ety> <def>A member of, or one educated
in, a seminary; specifically, an ecclesiastic educated for the
priesthood in a seminary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminary</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Seminaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>seminarium</ets>, fr. <ets>seminarius</ets> belonging to seed, fr.
<ets>semon</ets>, <ets>seminis</ets>, seed. See <er>Seminal</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A piece of ground where seed is sown for producing
plants for transplantation; a nursery; a seed plat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But if you draw them [seedling] only for the thinning of your
<qex>seminary</qex>, prick them into some empty beds.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the place or original stock whence anything
is brought or produced.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A place of education, as a school of a high grade,
an academy, college, or university.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Seminal state.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir
T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Fig.: A seed bed; a source.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A Roman Catholic priest educated in a foreign
seminary; a seminarist.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminary</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*na*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>seminarius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Belonging to seed; seminal.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminate</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Seminated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Seminating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>seminatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>seminare</ets> to sow, fr.
<ets>semen</ets>, <ets>seminis</ets>, seed.]</ety> <def>To sow; to
spread; to propagate.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Waterhouse.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semination</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>seminatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/mination</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sowing or spreading.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Natural dispersion of seeds.</def>
<rj><au>Martyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semined</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"ined</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Semen</er>.]</ety> <def>Thickly covered or sown, as with
seeds.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>semen</ets>, <ets>semenis</ets>, seed <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Seed-bearing; producing seed; pertaining to, or
connected with, the formation of semen; <as>as, <ex>seminiferous</ex>
cells or vesicles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminifical</ent><br/
<ent>Seminific</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sem`i*nif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sem`i*nif"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>semen</ets>,
<ets>seminis</ets>, seed + <ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Forming or producing seed, or the male
generative product of animals or of plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminification</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ni*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Propagation from seed.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir M.
Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminist</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>A believer in the old theory that the newly created being is formed
by the admixture of the seed of the male with the supposed seed of the
female.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminoles</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*noles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Seminole</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>A tribe of Indians who formerly occupied Florida, where some of
them still remain. They belonged to the Creek Confederation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminose</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*nose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>semen</ets> seed + <ets>glucose</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A carbohydrate of the glucose group found in the thickened
endosperm of certain seeds, and extracted as yellow sirup having a
sweetish-bitter taste.</def> <-- same as D-mannose; C6H12O6, a hexose
opticaly isomeric with glucose. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminude</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*nude"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partially nude;
half naked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seminymph</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*nymph`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The pupa of insects which undergo only a slight change in passing
to the imago state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semioccasionally</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*oc*ca"sion*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Once
in a while; on rare occasions.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiofficial</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*of*fi"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
official; having some official authority or importance; <as>as, a
<ex>semiofficial</ex> statement</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sem`i*of*fi"cial*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiological</ent><br/
<ent>Semiology</ent><br/
<ent>Semiography</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se`mi*og"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Se`mi*ol"o*gy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Se`mi*o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>. <def>Same
as <er>Semeiography</er>, <er>Semeiology</er>,
<er>Semeiological</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiopacous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*o*pa"cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Semiopaque.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiopal</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*o`pal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A variety of opal not possessing opalescence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiopaque</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*o*paque"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half opaque;
only half transparent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiorbicular</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*or*bic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
shape of a half orb or sphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiotic</ent><br/
<hw>Se`mi*ot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Semeiotic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiotics</ent><br/
<hw>Se`mi*ot"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Semeiotics</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semioval</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*o"val</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
oval.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiovate</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*o"vate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
ovate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semioxygenated</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ox"y*gen*a`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Combined
with oxygen only in part.</def> <rj><au>Kirwan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semipagan</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*pa"gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
pagan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semipalmated</ent><br/
<ent>Semipalmate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sem`i*pal"mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sem`i*pal"ma*ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the
anterior toes joined only part way down with a web; half-webbed; <as>as,
a <ex>semipalmate</ex> bird or foot</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiparabola</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*pa*rab"o*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>One branch of a parabola, being terminated at
the principal vertex of the curve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiped</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>semipes</ets>, <ets>semipedis</ets>; pref. <ets>semi-</ets> half +
<ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, a foot.]</ety> <fld>(Pros.)</fld>
<def>A half foot in poetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semipedal</ent><br/
<hw>Se*mip"e*dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Pres.)</fld>
<def>Containing a half foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-Pelagian</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i-Pe*la"gi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>A follower of John Cassianus, a French monk (died
about 448), who modified the doctrines of Pelagius, by denying human
merit, and maintaining the necessity of the Spirit's influence, while,
on the other hand, he rejected the Augustinian doctrines of election,
the inability of man to do good, and the certain perseverance of the
saints.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-Pelagian</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i-Pe*la"gi*an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Semi-Pelagians, or their tenets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1309 --></p>

<p><ent>Semi-Pelagianism</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i-Pe*la"gi*an*ism</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*p<esl/*l<amac/"j<icr/*<ait/n*<icr/z'm)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The doctrines or tenets of the
Semi-Pelagians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semipellucid</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*pel*lu"cid</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*p<ecr/l*l<umac/"s<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Half clear, or imperfectly transparent; <as>as, a
<ex>semipellucid</ex> gem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semipellucidity</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*pel`lu*cid"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*p<ecr/l`l<usl/*s<icr/d"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being imperfectly
transparent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semipenniform</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*pen"ni*form</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*p<ecr/n"n<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Half or partially penniform; <as>as, a
<ex>semipenniform</ex> muscle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semipermanent</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*per"ma*nent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Half or partly
permanent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiperspicuous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*per*spic"u*ous</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*p<etil/r*sp<icr/k"<usl/*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Half transparent; imperfectly clear; semipellucid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiphlogisticated</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*phlo*gis"ti*ca`ted</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*fl<osl/*j<icr/s"t<icr/*k<amac/`t<ecr/d)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>Partially impregnated with
phlogiston.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiplume</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*plume`</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*pl<umac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A feather which has a plumelike web, with the
shaft of an ordinary feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiprecious</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*pre"cious</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*pr<ecr/sh"<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat precious; <as>as, <ex>semiprecious</ex>
stones or metals</as>. Used mostly of gemstones used in jewelry, such as
amethyst, garnet, or iolite, which are sufficiently rare to have
commercial value, but are not considered as <contr>precious</contr>, as
are the diamond, emerald, and ruby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiproof</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*proof`</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m"<icr/*pr<oomac/f`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Half proof; evidence from the testimony of a single
witness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi pupa</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i pu"pa</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*p<umac/"p<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The young of an insect in a stage
between the larva and pupa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiquartile</ent><br/
<ent>Semiquadrate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sem"i*quad`rate</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m"<icr/*kw<ocr/d`r<asl/t)</pr>, <hw>Sem"i*quar`tile</hw>
<pr>(s<ecr/m"<icr/*kw<add/r`t<icr/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>An aspect of the planets when distant from
each other the half of a quadrant, or forty-five degrees, or one sign
and a half.</def> <rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiquaver</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*qua`ver</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m"<icr/*kw<amac/`v<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A note of half the duration of the
quaver; -- now usually called a <altname>sixteenth
note</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiquintile</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*quin`tile</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m"<icr/*kw<icr/n`t<icr/l)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>An aspect of the planets when
distant from each other half of the quintile, or thirty-six
degrees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiradial</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ra"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
radial.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiradial engine</ent><br/
<hw>Semiradial engine</hw>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>See <er>Radial
engine</er>, above.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semirecondite</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*rec"on*dite</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*r<ecr/k"<ocr/n*d<imac/t
<it>or</it> s<ecr/m`<icr/*r<esl/*k<ocr/n"d<icr/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Half hidden or half covered; said of the head of
an insect when half covered by the shield of the thorax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiring</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*ring`</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m"<icr/*r<icr/ng`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>One of the incomplete rings of the upper part of
the bronchial tubes of most birds. The semirings form an essential part
of the syrinx, or musical organ, of singing birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semisavage</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*sav"age</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/m`<icr/*s<acr/v"<asl/j)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Half savage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semisavage</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*sav`age</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is half
savage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semi-Saxon</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i-Sax"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half Saxon; --
specifically applied to the language intermediate between Saxon and
English, belonging to the period 1150-1250.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semisextile</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*sex"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld>
<def>An aspect of the planets when they are distant from each other the
twelfth part of a circle, or thirty degrees.</def>
<rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semisolid</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*sol"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partially
solid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semisoun</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*soun</hw> <pr>(-s<oomac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A half
sound; a low tone.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Soft he cougheth
with a <xex>semisoun</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semispherical</ent><br/
<ent>Semispheric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sem`i*spher"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sem`i*spher"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the figure of a half
sphere.</def> <rj><au>Kirwan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semispheroidal</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*sphe*roid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Formed like
a half spheroid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semisteel</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*steel`</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Puddled
steel.</def> <mark>[U. S. ]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sem"i*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Semitae</plw>.</plu> <ety>[L., a path.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A fasciole of a spatangoid sea urchin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitangent</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*tan`gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>The tangent of half an arc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semite</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One belonging to the
Semitic race. Also used adjectively.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Shemite</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiterete</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*te*rete"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Nat.
Hist.)</fld> <def>Half terete.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitertian</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ter"tian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Having the characteristics of both a tertian and a quotidian
intermittent.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>An intermittent
combining the characteristics of a tertian and a
quotidian.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitic</ent><br/
<hw>Sem*it"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Shem or his descendants; belonging to that division of the Caucasian
race which includes the Arabs, Jews, and related races.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>Shemitic</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Semitic language</b></col>, <cd>a name used to designate
a group of Asiatic and African languages, some living and some dead,
namely: Hebrew and Ph<oe/nician, Aramaic, Assyrian, Arabic, Ethiopic
(Geez and Ampharic).</cd> <rj><au>Encyc. Brit.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitism</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Semitic idiom; a
word of Semitic origin.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Shemitism</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitone</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>semi-</ets> + <ets>tone</ets>. CF. <er>Hemitone</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Half a tone; -- the name commonly applied to the
smaller intervals of the diatonic scale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ There is an impropriety in the use of this word, and
<xex>half step</xex> is now preferred. See <er>Tone</er>.</note>
<rj><au>J. S. Dwight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a semitone; consisting of a semitone, or of semitones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitontine</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ton*tine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(LIfe
Insurance)</fld> <def>Lit., half-tontine; -- used to designate a form of
tontine life insurance. See <er>Tontine insurance</er>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sem`i*ton*tine"</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitransept</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*tran`sept</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>The half of a transept; <as>as, the north <ex>semitransept</ex> of
a church</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitranslucent</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*trans*lu"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slightly
clear; transmitting light in a slight degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitransparency</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*trans*par"en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Imperfect or partial transparency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semitransparent</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*trans*par"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half or
imperfectly transparent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiverticillate</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*ver*tic"il*late</hw>, <pr>(<?/)</pr> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Partially verticillate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semivif</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*vif</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>semivivus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Only half alive.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semivitreous</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*vit"re*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partially
vitreous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semivitrification</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*vit"ri*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or state of being semivitrified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A substance imperfectly vitrified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semivitrified</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*vit"ri*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half or
imperfectly vitrified; partially converted into glass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semivocal</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*vo"cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Phon.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a semivowel; half cocal; imperfectly
sounding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semivowel</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"i*vow`el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Phon.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A sound intermediate between a vowel and a consonant,
or partaking of the nature of both, as in the English <xex>w</xex> and
<xex>y</xex>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sign or letter representing
such a sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semiweekly</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`i*week"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Coming, or made,
or done, once every half week; <as>as, a <ex>semiweekly</ex> newspaper;
a <ex>semiweekly</ex> trip.</as></def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>That
which comes or happens once every half week, esp. a semiweekly
periodical.</def></def2> -- <def2><pos>adv.</pos> <def>At intervals of
half a week each.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semolella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sem`o*lel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Semolina</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semolina</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`o*li"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>semolino</ets>, from <ets>semola</ets> bran, L. <ets>simila</ets>
the finest wheat flour. Cf. <er>Semoule</er>, <er>Simnel</er>.]</ety>
<def>The purified fine, hard parts of durum wheat, derived mostly from
the endosperm, rounded by the attrition of the millstones, -- used in
cookery, such as in the preparation of Italian pasta.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semolino</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sem`o*li"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Semolina</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semoule</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*moule"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Semolina</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempervirent</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`per*vi"rent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>semper</ets> always + <ets>virens</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>virere</ets> to be green.]</ety> <def>Always fresh;
evergreen.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempervive</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"per*vive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>semperviva</ets>, <ets>sempervivum</ets>, fr.
<ets>sempervivus</ets> ever-living; <ets>semper</ets> always +
<ets>vivus</ets> living.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
houseleek.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempervivum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sem`per*vi"vum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of fleshy-leaved plants, of which the
houseleek (<spn>Sempervivum tectorum</spn>) is the commonest
species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempiternal</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`pi*ter"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sempiternus</ets>, fr. <ets>semper</ets> always: cf. F.
<ets>sempiternel</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of neverending duration;
everlasting; endless; having beginning, but no end.</def> <rj><au>Sir M.
Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Without beginning or end; eternal.</def>
<rj><au>Blackmore.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempiterne</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"pi*terne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sempiternal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempiternity</ent><br/
<hw>Sem`pi*ter"ni*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sempiternitas</ets>.]</ety> <def>Future duration without end; the
relation or state of being sempiternal.</def> <rj><au>Sir M.
Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempre</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"pre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[It., fr. L.
<ets>semper</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Always; throughout;
<as>as, <ex>sempre</ex> piano, always soft</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempster</ent><br/
<hw>Semp"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seamster.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempstress</ent><br/
<hw>Semp"stress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
seamstress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Two hundred <qex>sepstress</qex> were employed to make me
shirts.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sempstressy</ent><br/
<hw>Semp"stress*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Seamstressy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semster</ent><br/
<hw>Sem"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seamster.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Semuncia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*mun"ci*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>semi</ets> half + <ets>uncia</ets> ounce.]</ety> <fld>(Rom.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>A Roman coin equivalent to one twenty-fourth part of
a Roman pound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sen</ent><br/
<hw>Sen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Japanese coin, worth
about one half of a cent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sen</ent><br/
<hw>Sen</hw>, <pos>adv., prep., & conj.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Since</er>.]</ety> <def>Since.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senary</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>senarius</ets>, fr. <ets>seni</ets> six each, fr. <ets>sex</ets>
six. See <er>Six</er>.]</ety> <def>Of six; belonging to six; containing
six.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senate</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>senat</ets>,
F. <ets>s<eacute/nat</ets>, fr. L. <ets>senatus</ets>, fr.
<ets>senex</ets>, gen. <ets>senis</ets>, old, an old man. See
<er>Senior</er>, <er>Sir</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An assembly or
council having the highest deliberative and legislative functions.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Anc. Rom.)</fld> <def>A body of elders
appointed or elected from among the nobles of the nation, and having
supreme legislative authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>senate</qex> was thus the medium through which all
affairs of the whole government had to pass.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. W.
Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The upper and less numerous branch of a legislature
in various countries, as in France, in the United States, in most of the
separate States of the United States, and in some Swiss cantons.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>In general, a legislative body; a state council; the
legislative department of government.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The governing body of the Universities of Cambridge
and London.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In some American colleges, a council of elected
students, presided over by the president of the college, to which are
referred cases of discipline and matters of general concern affecting
the students.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Senate chamber</b></col>, <cd>a room where a senate meets
when it transacts business.</cd> -- <col><b>Senate house</b></col>,
<cd>a house where a senate meets when it transacts
business.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senator</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"a*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>senatour</ets>, OF. <ets>senatour</ets>, F.
<ets>s<eacute/nateur</ets>, fr. L. <ets>senator</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A member of a senate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The duke and <qex>senators</qex> of Venice greet you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the United States, each State sends two senators for
a term of six years to the national Congress.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(O.Eng.Law)</fld> <def>A member of the king's
council; a king's councilor.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senatorial</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`a*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/natorial</ets>, or L. <ets>senatorius</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a senator, or a senate; becoming to
a senator, or a senate; <as>as, <ex>senatorial</ex> duties;
<ex>senatorial</ex> dignity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Entitled to elect a senator, or by senators; <as>as,
the <ex>senatorial</ex> districts of a State</as>.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senatorially</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`a*to"ri*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a senatorial
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senatorian</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`a*to"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Senatorial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senatorious</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`a*to"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Senatorial.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senatorship</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"a*tor*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
dignity of a senator.</def> <rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senatusconsult</ent><br/
<hw>Se*na`tus*con*sult"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>senatus consultum</ets>.]</ety> <def>A decree of the Roman
senate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Send</ent><br/
<hw>Send</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sent</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>sendan</ets>; akin to
OS. <ets>sendian</ets>, D. <ets>zenden</ets>, G. <ets>senden</ets>, OHG.
<ets>senten</ets>, Icel. <ets>senda</ets>, Sw. <ets>s<aum/nda</ets>,
Dan. <ets>sende</ets>, Goth. <ets>sandjan</ets>, and to Goth.
<ets>sinp</ets> a time (properly, a going), ga<ets>sinpa</ets>
companion, OHG. <ets>sind</ets> journey, AS. <ets>s<imac/<?/</ets>,
Icel. <ets>sinni</ets> a walk, journey, a time. W. <ets>hynt</ets> a
way, journey, OIr. <ets>s<?/t</ets>. Cf. <er>Sense</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to go in any manner; to dispatch; to
commission or direct to go; <as>as, to <ex>send</ex> a
messenger</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have not <qex>sent</qex> these prophets, yet they ran.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. xxiii. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I proceeded forth and came from God; neither came I of myself, but
he <qex>sent</qex> me.</q> <rj><qau>John viii. 42.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Servants, <qex>sent</qex> on messages, stay out somewhat longer
than the message requires.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give motion to; to cause to be borne or carried;
to procure the going, transmission, or delivery of; <as>as, to
<ex>send</ex> a message</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . <qex>sent</qex> letters by posts on horseback.</q>
<rj><qau>Esther viii. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O <qex>send</qex> out thy light an thy truth; let them lead
me.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. xliii. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To emit; to impel; to cast; to throw; to hurl;
<as>as, to <ex>send</ex> a ball, an arrow, or the like</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to be or to happen; to bestow; to inflict;
to grant; -- sometimes followed by a dependent proposition.</def>
<ldquo/God <xex>send</xex> him well!<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord shall <qex>send</qex> upon thee cursing, vexation, and
rebuke.</q> <rj><qau>Deut. xxviii. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>sendeth</qex> rain on the just and on the unjust.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. v. 45.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God <qex>send</qex> your mission may bring back peace.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Send</ent><br/
<hw>Send</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
dispatch an agent or messenger to convey a message, or to do an
errand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>See ye how this son of a murderer hath <qex>sent</qex> to take
away my head?</q> <rj><qau>2 Kings vi. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To pitch; <as>as, the ship
<ex>sends</ex> forward so violently as to endanger her masts</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To send for</b></col>, <cd>to request or require by
message to come or be brought.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Send</ent><br/
<hw>Send</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The impulse of a
wave by which a vessel is carried bodily.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>scend</asp>.]</altsp> <au>W. C. Russell.</au> <ldquo/The
<xex>send</xex> of the sea<rdquo/. <rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sendal</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>cendal</ets>
(cf. Pr. & Sp. <ets>cendal</ets>, It. <ets>zendale</ets>), LL.
<ets>cendallum</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a fine Indian cloth.]</ety> <def>A
light thin stuff of silk.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>cendal</asp>,
and <asp>sendal</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wore she not a veil of twisted <qex>sendal</qex> embroidered with
silver?</q> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sender</ent><br/
<hw>Send"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sends.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senecas</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"e*cas</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Seneca</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A
tribe of Indians who formerly inhabited a part of Western New York. This
tribe was the most numerous and most warlike of the Five
Nations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seneca grass</b></col><fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>holy grass.
See under <er>Holy</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Seneca eil</b></col>,
<cd>petroleum or naphtha.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Seneca root</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Seneca snakeroot</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the rootstock of an American species of milkworth (<spn>Polygala
Senega</spn>) having an aromatic but bitter taste. It is often used
medicinally as an expectorant and diuretic, and, in large doses, as an
emetic and cathartic.</cd> <altsp>[Written also <asp>Senega root</asp>,
and <asp>Seneka root</asp>.]</altsp></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senecio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*ne"ci*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., groundsel,
lit., an old man. So called in allusion to the hoary appearance of the
pappus.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A very large genus of composite
plants including the groundsel and the golden ragwort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senectitude</ent><br/
<hw>Se*nec"ti*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>senectus</ets> aged, old age, <ets>senex</ets> old.]</ety> <def>Old
age.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Senectitude</xex>, weary of its
toils.<rdquo/ <rj><au>H. Miller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senega</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"e*ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Seneca root.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senegal</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"e*gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Gum senegal. See
under <er>Gum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senegin</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"e*gin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med. Chem.)</fld>
<def>A substance extracted from the rootstock of the <spn>Polygala
Senega</spn> (Seneca root), and probably identical with polygalic
acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senescence</ent><br/
<hw>Se*nes"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Senescent</er>.]</ety> <def>The state of growing old; decay by
time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senescent</ent><br/
<hw>Se*nes"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>senescent</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>senescere</ets> to grow old, incho.
fr. <ets>senere</ets> to be old.]</ety> <def>Growing old; decaying with
the lapse of time.</def> <ldquo/The night was
<xex>senescent</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Poe.</au> <ldquo/With too
<xex>senescent</xex> air.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seneschal</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"es*chal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>seneschal</ets>, LL. <ets>seniscalcus</ets>, of Teutonic origin;
cf. Goth. <ets>sineigs</ets> old, <ets>skalks</ets>, OHG.
<ets>scalch</ets>, AS. <ets>scealc</ets>. Cf. <er>Senior</er>,
<er>Marshal</er>.]</ety> <def>An officer in the houses of princes and
dignitaries, in the Middle Ages, who had the superintendence of feasts
and domestic ceremonies; a steward. Sometimes the seneschal had the
dispensing of justice, and was given high military commands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then marshaled feast<br/
Served up in hall with sewers and <qex>seneschale</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Philip Augustus, by a famous ordinance in 1190, first established
royal courts of justice, held by the officers called baitiffs, or
<qex>seneschals</qex>, who acted as the king's lieutenants in his
demains.</q> <rj><qau>Hallam.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seneschalship</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"es*chal*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office, dignity, or
jurisdiction of a seneschal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senge</ent><br/
<hw>Senge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To singe.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sengreen</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"green</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[AS.
<ets>singr<?/ne</ets>, properly, evergreen, fr. <ets>sin</ets> (in
composition) always + <ets>gr<eum/ne</ets> green; akin to OHG.
<ets>sin-</ets> ever, L. <ets>semper</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The houseleek.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senhor</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*nhor"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pg. Cf.
<er>Se<ntil/or</er>, <er>Senior</er>.]</ety> <def>A Portuguese title of
courtesy corresponding to the Spanish <xex>se<ntil/or</xex> or the
English <it>Mr.</it> or <it>sir</it>; also, a gentleman.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senhora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*nho"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pg. Cf.
<er>Se<ntil/ora</er>.]</ety> <def>A Portuguese title of courtesy given
to a lady; Mrs.; Madam; also, a lady.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senile</ent><br/
<hw>Se"nile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>senilis</ets>, from <ets>senex</ets>, gen. <ets>senis</ets>, old,
an old man: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/nile</ets>. See <er>Senior</er>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to old age; proceeding from, or characteristic of,
old age; affected with the infirmities of old age; <as>as,
<ex>senile</ex> weakness</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Senile</xex> maturity
of judgment.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Senile gangrene</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a form
of gangrene occuring particularly in old people, and caused usually by
insufficient blood supply due to degeneration of the walls of the
smaller arteries.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senility</ent><br/
<hw>Se*nil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/nilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being senile; old age.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senior</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ior</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>senior</ets>,
compar. of <ets>senex</ets>, gen. <ets>senis</ets>, old. See
<er>Sir</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>More advanced than another in age;
prior in age; elder; hence, more advanced in dignity, rank, or office;
superior; <as>as, <ex>senior</ex> member; <ex>senior</ex>
counsel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Belonging to the final year of the regular course in
American colleges, or in professional schools.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senior</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ior</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A person who is older
than another; one more advanced in life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One older in office, or whose entrance upon office
was anterior to that of another; one prior in grade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An aged person; an older.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each village <qex>senior</qex> paused to scan,<br/
And speak the lovely caravan.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One in the fourth or final year of his collegiate
course at an American college; -- originally called <altname>senior
sophister</altname>; also, one in the last year of the course at a
professional schools or at a seminary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seniority</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*ior"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being senior.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seniorize</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ior*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To exercise
authority; to rule; to lord it.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seniory</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ior*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Seniority.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senna</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It. & Sp.
<ets>sena</ets>, Pg. <ets>sene</ets>, F. <ets>s<eacute/n<eacute/</ets>;
all fr. Ar. <ets>san<amac/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The leaves of several leguminous plants of the genus
<gen>Cassia</gen>. (<spn>Cassia acutifolia</spn>, <spn>Cassia
angustifolia</spn>, etc.). They constitute a valuable but nauseous
cathartic medicine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The plants themselves, native to
the East, but now cultivated largely in the south of Europe and in the
West Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bladder senna</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Bladder</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Wild senna</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the <spn>Cassia Marilandica</spn>, growing in the
United States, the leaves of which are used medicinally, like those of
the officinal senna.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1310 --></p>

<p><ent>Sennachy</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"na*chy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seannachie</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sennet</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Properly, a sign
given for the entrance or exit of actors, from OF. <ets>sinet</ets>,
<ets>signet</ets>, dim. of <ets>signe</ets>. See <er>Signet</er>.]</ety>
<def>A signal call on a trumpet or cornet for entrance or exit on the
stage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sennet</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"net</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
barracuda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sennight</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"night</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contr. fr.
<ets>sevennight</ets>.]</ety> <def>The space of seven nights and days; a
week.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>se'nnight</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Archaic.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au> <au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sennit</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"nit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Seven</ets> +
<ets>knit</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A braided
cord or fabric formed by plaiting together rope yarns or other small
stuff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Plaited straw or palm leaves for making
hats.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senocular</ent><br/
<hw>Se*noc"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>seni</ets> six each (fr. <ets>sex</ets> six) + <ets>oculus</ets>
eye.]</ety> <def>Having six eyes.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Derham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senonian</ent><br/
<hw>Se*no"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/nonien</ets>, from the district of
<ets>S<eacute/nonais</ets>, in France.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>In
european geology, a name given to the middle division of the Upper
Cretaceous formation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senor</ent><br/
<ent>Senyor</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*<ntil/or"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. Cf.
<er>Senior</er>.]</ety> <def>A Spanish title of courtesy corresponding
to the English <xex>Mr.</xex> or <xex>Sir</xex>; also, a
gentleman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senora</ent><br/
<ent>Senyora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*<ntil/o"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.]</ety>
<def>A Spanish title of courtesy given to a lady; Mrs.; Madam; also, a
lady.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senorita</ent><br/
<ent>Senyorita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se`<ntil/o*ri"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Sp.]</ety> <def>A Spanish title of courtesy given to a young lady;
Miss; also, a young lady.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sens</ent><br/
<hw>Sens</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Since</er>.]</ety> <def>Since.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensate</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"sate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sensated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sensating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Sensated</er>.]</ety> <def>To feel or apprehend more or less
distinctly through a sense, or the senses; <as>as, to <ex>sensate</ex>
light, or an odor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As those of the one are <qex>sensated</qex> by the ear, so those
of the other are by the eye.</q> <rj><qau>R. Hooke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensated</ent><br/
<ent>Sensate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sen"sate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sen"sa*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sensatus</ets> gifted with sense,
intelligent, fr. <ets>sensus</ets> sense. See <er>Sense</er>.]</ety>
<def>Felt or apprehended through a sense, or the senses.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Baxter.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensation</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sensation</ets>. See <er>Sensate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An impression, or the consciousness of an
impression, made upon the central nervous organ, through the medium of a
sensory or afferent nerve or one of the organs of sense; a feeling, or
state of consciousness, whether agreeable or disagreeable, produced
either by an external object (stimulus), or by some change in the
internal state of the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Perception is only a special kind of knowledge, and
<qex>sensation</qex> a special kind of feeling. . . . Knowledge and
feeling, perception and <qex>sensation</qex>, though always coexistent,
are always in the inverse ratio of each other.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A purely spiritual or psychical affection; agreeable
or disagreeable feelings occasioned by objects that are not corporeal or
material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A state of excited interest or feeling, or that
which causes it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sensation</qex> caused by the appearance of that work is
still remembered by many.</q> <rj><qau>Brougham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Perception.</syn> -- <usage><er>Sensation</er>,
<er>Perseption</er>. The distinction between these words, when used in
mental philosophy, may be thus stated; if I simply smell a rose, I have
a <xex>sensation</xex>; if I refer that smell to the external object
which occasioned it, I have a <xex>perception</xex>. Thus, the former is
mere feeling, without the idea of an object; the latter is the mind's
apprehension of some external object as occasioning that feeling.
<ldquo/<xex>Sensation</xex> properly expresses <xex>that change in the
state of the mind</xex> which is produced by an impression upon an organ
of sense (of which change we can conceive the mind to be conscious,
without any knowledge of external objects). <xex>Perception</xex>, on
the other hand, expresses the <xex>knowledge</xex> or the intimations we
obtain by means of our <xex>sensations</xex> concerning the qualities of
matter, and consequently involves, in every instance, the notion of
<xex>externality</xex>, or <xex>outness</xex>, which it is necessary to
exclude in order to seize the precise import of the word
<xex>sensation</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Fleming.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensational</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*sa"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to sensation; <as>as, <ex>sensational</ex>
nerves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to sensationalism, or the doctrine
that sensation is the sole origin of knowledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Suited or intended to excite temporarily great
interest or emotion; melodramatic; emotional; <as>as,
<ex>sensational</ex> plays or novels; <ex>sensational</ex> preaching;
<ex>sensational</ex> journalism; a <ex>sensational</ex>
report</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensationalism</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*sa"tion*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>The doctrine held by Condillac, and by some
ascribed to Locke, that our ideas originate solely in sensation, and
consist of sensations transformed; sensualism; -- opposed to
<contr>intuitionalism</contr>, and <contr>rationalism</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The practice or methods of sensational writing or
speaking; <as>as, the <ex>sensationalism</ex> of a novel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensationalist</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*sa"tion*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>An advocate of, or believer in, philosophical
sensationalism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who practices sensational writing or
speaking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sense</ent><br/
<hw>Sense</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sensus</ets>,
from <ets>sentire</ets>, <ets>sensum</ets>, to perceive, to feel, from
the same root as E. <ets>send</ets>; cf. OHG. <ets>sin</ets> sense,
mind, <ets>sinnan</ets> to go, to journey, G. <ets>sinnen</ets> to
meditate, to think: cf. F. <ets>sens</ets>. For the change of meaning
cf. <er>See</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos> See <er>Send</er>, and cf.
<er>Assent</er>, <er>Consent</er>, <er>Scent</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>,
<er>Sentence</er>, <er>Sentient</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A faculty, possessed by animals, of
perceiving external objects by means of impressions made upon certain
organs (sensory or sense organs) of the body, or of perceiving changes
in the condition of the body; <as>as, the <ex>senses</ex> of sight,
smell, hearing, taste, and touch</as>. See <cref>Muscular sense</cref>,
under <er>Muscular</er>, and <cref>Temperature sense</cref>, under
<er>Temperature</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let fancy still my <qex>sense</qex> in Lethe steep.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What surmounts the reach<br/
Of human <qex>sense</qex> I shall delineate.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The traitor <qex>Sense</qex> recalls<br/
The soaring soul from rest.</q> <rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Perception by the sensory organs of the body;
sensation; sensibility; feeling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In a living creature, though never so great, the <qex>sense</qex>
and the affects of any one part of the body instantly make a
transcursion through the whole.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Perception through the intellect; apprehension;
recognition; understanding; discernment; appreciation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This Basilius, having the quick <qex>sense</qex> of a lover.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>High disdain from <qex>sense</qex> of injured merit.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Sound perception and reasoning; correct judgment;
good mental capacity; understanding; also, that which is sound, true, or
reasonable; rational meaning.</def> <ldquo/He speaks
<xex>sense</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He raves; his words are loose<br/
As heaps of sand, and scattering wide from <qex>sense</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That which is felt or is held as a sentiment, view,
or opinion; judgment; notion; opinion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I speak my private but impartial <qex>sense</qex><br/
With freedom.</q> <rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The municipal council of the city had ceased to speak the
<qex>sense</qex> of the citizens.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Meaning; import; signification; <as>as, the true
<ex>sense</ex> of words or phrases; the <ex>sense</ex> of a
remark</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So they read in the book in the law of God distinctly, and gave
the <qex>sense</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Neh. viii. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I think 't was in another <qex>sense</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Moral perception or appreciation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some are so hardened in wickedness as to have no <qex>sense</qex>
of the most friendly offices.</q> <rj><qau>L' Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>One of two opposite directions in
which a line, surface, or volume, may be supposed to be described by the
motion of a point, line, or surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Common sense</b></col>, according to Sir W. Hamilton:
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd><ldquo/The complement of those cognitions or
convictions which we receive from nature, which all men possess in
common, and by which they test the truth of knowledge and the morality
of actions.<rdquo/</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd><ldquo/The faculty of first
principles.<rdquo/ These two are the philosophical significations.</cd>
<sd>(c)</sd> <cd><ldquo/Such ordinary complement of intelligence,
that,if a person be deficient therein, he is accounted mad or
foolish.<rdquo/</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>When the substantive is
emphasized: <ldquo/Native practical intelligence, natural prudence,
mother wit, tact in behavior, acuteness in the observation of character,
in contrast to habits of acquired learning or of
speculation.<rdquo/</cd> -- <col><b>Moral sense</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Moral</er>, <sd>(a)</sd>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>The inner
sense</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>The internal
sense</b></col></mcol>, <cd>capacity of the mind to be aware of its own
states; consciousness; reflection.</cd> <ldquo/This source of ideas
every man has wholly in himself, and though it be not sense, as having
nothing to do with external objects, yet it is very like it, and might
properly enough be called <xex>internal sense</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Locke.</au> -- <col><b>Sense capsule</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>,
<cd>one of the cartilaginous or bony cavities which inclose, more or
less completely, the organs of smell, sight, and hearing.</cd> --
<col><b>Sense organ</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>a specially
irritable mechanism by which some one natural force or form of energy is
enabled to excite sensory nerves; as the eye, ear, an end bulb or
tactile corpuscle, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Sense organule</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>one of the modified epithelial cells in or near
which the fibers of the sensory nerves terminate.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Understanding; reason.</syn> --
<usage><er>Sense</er>, <er>Understanding</er>, <er>Reason</er>. Some
philosophers have given a technical signification to these terms, which
may here be stated. <xex>Sense</xex> is the mind's acting in the direct
cognition either of material objects or of its own mental states. In the
first case it is called the <xex>outer</xex>, in the second the
<xex>inner</xex>, sense. <xex>Understanding</xex> is the logical
faculty, <it>i. e.</it>, the power of apprehending under general
conceptions, or the power of classifying, arranging, and making
deductions. <xex>Reason</xex> is the power of apprehending those first
or fundamental truths or principles which are the conditions of all real
and scientific knowledge, and which control the mind in all its
processes of investigation and deduction. These distinctions are given,
not as established, but simply because they often occur in writers of
the present day.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sense</ent><br/
<hw>Sense</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sensed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sensing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To perceive by the senses; to
recognize.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is he sure that objects are not otherwise <qex>sensed</qex> by
others than they are by him?</q> <rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senseful</ent><br/
<hw>Sense"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of sense,
meaning, or reason; reasonable; judicious.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Senseful</xex> speech.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au> <ldquo/Men,
otherwise <xex>senseful</xex> and ingenious.<rdquo/ <au>Norris.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senseless</ent><br/
<hw>Sense"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of, deficient in, or
contrary to, sense; without sensibility or feeling; unconscious; stupid;
foolish; unwise; unreasonable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You blocks, you stones, you worse than <qex>senseless</qex>
things.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ears are <qex>senseless</qex> that should give us hearing.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>senseless</qex> grave feels not your pious sorrows.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They were a <qex>senseless</qex>, stupid race.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They would repent this their <qex>senseless</qex> perverseness
when it would be too late.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sense"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sense"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensibility</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`si*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sensibilities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sensibilit<eacute/</ets>, LL. <ets>sensibilitas</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The quality or state of being
sensible, or capable of sensation; capacity to feel or
perceive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The capacity of emotion or feeling, as distinguished
from the intellect and the will; peculiar susceptibility of impression,
pleasurable or painful; delicacy of feeling; quick emotion or sympathy;
<as>as, <ex>sensibility</ex> to pleasure or pain; <ex>sensibility</ex>
to shame or praise; exquisite <ex>sensibility</ex></as>; -- often used
in the plural.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sensibilities</xex> so fine!<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The true lawgiver ought to have a heart full of
<qex>sensibility</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>sensibilities</qex> seem rather to have been those of
patriotism than of wounded pride.</q> <rj><qau>Marshall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Experience of sensation; actual feeling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This adds greatly to my <qex>sensibility</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That quality of an instrument which makes it
indicate very slight changes of condition; delicacy; <as>as, the
<ex>sensibility</ex> of a balance, or of a thermometer</as>.</def><--
usu. sensitivity --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Taste; susceptibility; feeling. See
<er>Taste</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensible</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>sensibilis</ets>, fr. <ets>sensus</ets> sense.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of being perceived by the senses; apprehensible through the
bodily organs; hence, also, perceptible to the mind; making an
impression upon the sense, reason, or understanding; <as>as,
<xex>sensible</xex> heat; <xex>sensible</xex> resistance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Air is <qex>sensible</qex> to the touch by its motion.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The disgrace was more <qex>sensible</qex> than the pain.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Any very <qex>sensible</qex> effect upon the prices of things.</q>
<rj><qau>A. Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the capacity of receiving impressions from
external objects; capable of perceiving by the instrumentality of the
proper organs; liable to be affected physically or mentally;
impressible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Would your cambric were <qex>sensible</qex> as your finger.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence: Liable to impression from without; easily
affected; having nice perception or acute feeling; sensitive; also,
readily moved or affected by natural agents; delicate; <as>as, a
<ex>sensible</ex> thermometer</as>.</def> <ldquo/With affection wondrous
<xex>sensible</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Perceiving or having perception, either by the
senses or the mind; cognizant; perceiving so clearly as to be convinced;
satisfied; persuaded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [man] can not think at any time, waking or sleeping, without
being <qex>sensible</qex> of it.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are now <qex>sensible</qex> it would have been better to
comply than to refuse.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Having moral perception; capable of being affected
by moral good or evil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Possessing or containing sense or reason; gifted
with, or characterized by, good or common sense; intelligent;
wise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now a <qex>sensible</qex> man, by and by a fool.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sensible note</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sensible tone</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>the major
seventh note of any scale; -- so called because, being but a half step
below the octave, or key tone, and naturally leading up to that, it
makes the ear <xex>sensible</xex> of its approaching sound. Called also
the <altname>leading tone</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sensible
horizon</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Horizon</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.
<sd>(a)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Intelligent; wise.</syn> --
<usage><er>Sensible</er>, <er>Intelligent</er>. We call a man
<xex>sensible</xex> whose judgments and conduct are marked and governed
by sound judgment or good common sense. We call one
<xex>intelligent</xex> who is quick and clear in his understanding,
<it>i. e.</it>, who discriminates readily and nicely in respect to
difficult and important distinctions. The sphere of the
<xex>sensible</xex> man lies in matters of practical concern; of the
<xex>intelligent</xex> man, in subjects of intellectual interest.
<ldquo/I have been tired with accounts from <xex>sensible</xex> men,
furnished with matters of fact which have happened within their own
knowledge.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au> <ldquo/Trace out the numerous
footsteps . . . of a most wise and <xex>intelligent</xex> architect
throughout all this stupendous fabric.<rdquo/
<au>Woodward.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensible</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Sensation; sensibility.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Our temper
changed . . . which must needs remove the <xex>sensible</xex> of
pain.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which impresses itself on the sense; anything
perceptible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Aristotle distinguished <qex>sensibles</qex> into common and
proper.</q> <rj><qau>Krauth-Fleming.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which has sensibility; a sensitive being.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This melancholy extends itself not to men only, but even to
vegetals and <qex>sensibles</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Burton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensibleness</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"si*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being sensible; sensibility; appreciation; capacity of
perception; susceptibility.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>sensibleness</xex> of
the eye.<rdquo/ <au>Sharp.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Sensibleness</xex> and
sorrow for sin.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sensibleness</qex> of the divine presence.</q>
<rj><qau>Hallywell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Intelligence; reasonableness; good sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensibly</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"si*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a sensible
manner; so as to be perceptible to the senses or to the mind;
appreciably; with perception; susceptibly; sensitively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What remains past cure,<br/
Bear not too <qex>sensibly</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>With intelligence or good sense;
judiciously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensifacient</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`si*fa"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sensus</ets> sense + <ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety>
<def>Converting into sensation.</def> <rj><au>Huxley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*sif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sensifer</ets>; <ets>sensus</ets> sense + <ets>ferre</ets> to
bear.]</ety> <def>Exciting sensation; conveying sensation.</def>
<rj><au>Huxley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensific</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*sif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sensificus</ets>; <ets>sensus</ets> sense + <ets>facere</ets> to
make.]</ety> <def>Exciting sensation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensificatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*sif"i*ca*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Susceptible
of, or converting into, sensation; <as>as, the <ex>sensificatory</ex>
part of a nervous system</as>.</def> <rj><au>Huxley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensigenous</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*sig"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sensus</ets> sense + <ets>-genous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Causing or
exciting sensation.</def> <rj><au>Huxley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensism</ent><br/
<hw>Sens"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sensualism</er>, 2 & 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensist</ent><br/
<hw>Sens"ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, in philosophy, holds to
sensism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensitive</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"si*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sensitif</ets>. See <er>Sense</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having
sense of feeling; possessing or exhibiting the capacity of receiving
impressions from external objects; <as>as, a <ex>sensitive</ex>
soul</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having quick and acute sensibility, either to the
action of external objects, or to impressions upon the mind and
feelings; highly susceptible; easily and acutely affected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She was too <qex>sensitive</qex> to abuse and calumny.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>Having a capacity of
being easily affected or moved; <as>as, a <ex>sensitive</ex>
thermometer; <ex>sensitive</ex> scales</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Chem. & Photog.)</fld> <def>Readily affected or changed by certain
appropriate agents; <as>as, silver chloride or bromide, when in contact
with certain organic substances, is extremely <ex>sensitive</ex> to
actinic rays</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Serving to affect the sense; sensible.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A sensitive love of some <qex>sensitive</qex> objects.</q>
<rj><qau>Hammond.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to sensation; depending on
sensation; <as>as, <ex>sensitive</ex> motions; <ex>sensitive</ex>
muscular motions excited by irritation</as>.</def> <rj><au>E.
Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sensitive fern</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an
American fern (<spn>Onoclea sensibilis</spn>), the leaves of which, when
plucked, show a slight tendency to fold together.</cd> --
<col><b>Sensitive flame</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>, <cd>a gas flame
so arranged that under a suitable adjustment of pressure it is
exceedingly sensitive to sounds, being caused to roar, flare, or become
suddenly shortened or extinguished, by slight sounds of the proper
pitch.</cd> -- <col><b>Sensitive joint vetch</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an annual leguminous herb (<spn>Aeschynomene
hispida</spn>), with sensitive foliage.</cd> -- <col><b>Sensitive
paper</b></col>, <cd>paper prepared for photographic purpose by being
rendered sensitive to the effect of light.</cd> -- <col><b>Sensitive
plant</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A leguminous plant
(<spn>Mimosa pudica</spn>, or <spn>Mimosa sensitiva</spn>, and other
allied species), the leaves of which close at the slightest touch.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Any plant showing motions after irritation, as the
sensitive brier (<gen>Schrankia</gen>) of the Southern States, two
common American species of <gen>Cassia</gen> (<spn>Cassia
nictitans</spn>, and <spn>Cassia Chamaecrista</spn>), a kind of sorrel
(<spn>Oxalis sensitiva</spn>), etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1311 --></p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sen"si*tive*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-- <wf>Sen"si*tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensitivity</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`si*tiv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sensitive; -- used chiefly in science and the arts;
<as>as, the <ex>sensitivity</ex> of iodized silver</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sensitivity</qex> and emotivity have also been used as the
scientific term for the capacity of feeling.</q>
<rj><qau>Hickok.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensitize</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"si*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Photog.)</fld>
<def>To render sensitive, or susceptible of being easily acted on by the
actinic rays of the sun; <as>as, <ex>sensitized</ex> paper or
plate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensitizer</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"si*ti`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Photog.)</fld>
<def>An agent that sensitizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sensitizer</qex> should be poured on the middle of the
sheet.</q> <rj><qau>Wilis & Clements (The Platinotype).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensitometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`si*tom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sensitive</er>; <er>-meter</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>An
instrument or apparatus for comparing and grading the sensitiveness of
plates, films, etc., as a screen divided into squares of different
shades or colors, from which a picture is made on the plate to be
tested.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensitory</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"si*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sensory</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensive</ent><br/
<hw>Sens"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having sense or
sensibility; sensitive.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensor</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"sor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sensory; <as>as, the
<ex>sensor</ex> nerves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensorial</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*so"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sensorial</ets>. See <er>Sensorium</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the sensorium; <as>as, <ex>sensorial</ex> faculties,
motions, powers</as>.</def> <rj><au>A. Tucker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensorium</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*so"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Sensoriums</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Sensoria</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. <ets>sentire</ets>, <ets>sensum</ets>,
<ets>to discern or perceive by the senses</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The seat of sensation; the nervous center or
centers to which impressions from the external world must be conveyed
before they can be perceived; the place where external impressions are
localized, and transformed into sensations, prior to being reflected to
other parts of the organism; hence, the whole nervous system, when
animated, so far as it is susceptible of common or special
sensations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensori-volitional</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*so`ri-vo*li"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Concerned both in sensation and volition; --
applied to those nerve fibers which pass to and from the cerebro-spinal
axis, and are respectively concerned in sensation and volition.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensory</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sensories</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sensorium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensory</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"so*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to the sensorium or sensation; <as>as, <ex>sensory</ex>
impulses; -- especially applied to those nerves and nerve fibers which
convey to a nerve center impulses resulting in sensation; also sometimes
loosely employed in the sense of <ex>afferent</ex>, to indicate nerve
fibers which convey impressions of any kind to a nerve
center</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensual</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"su*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sensualis</ets>, from <ets>sensus</ets> sense: cf. F.
<ets>sensuel</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, consisting
in, or affecting, the sense, or bodily organs of perception; relating
to, or concerning, the body, in distinction from the spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pleasing and <qex>sensual</qex> rites and ceremonies.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Far as creation's ample range extends,<br/
The scale of <qex>sensual</qex>, mental powers ascends.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, not spiritual or intellectual; carnal;
fleshly; pertaining to, or consisting in, the gratification of the
senses, or the indulgence of appetites; wordly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These be they who separate themselves, <qex>sensual</qex>, having
not the Spirit.</q> <rj><qau>Jude 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The greatest part of men are such as prefer . . . that good which
is <qex>sensual</qex> before whatsoever is most divine.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Devoted to the pleasures of sense and appetite;
luxurious; voluptuous; lewd; libidinous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No small part of virtue consists in abstaining from that wherein
<qex>sensual</qex> men place their felicity.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Pertaining or peculiar to the philosophical doctrine
of sensualism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensualism</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"su*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sensualisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The condition or
character of one who is sensual; subjection to sensual feelings and
appetite; sensuality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Philos.)</fld> <def>The doctrine that all our
ideas, or the operations of the understanding, not only originate in
sensation, but are transformed sensations, copies or relics of
sensations; sensationalism; sensism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Ethics)</fld> <def>The regarding of the
gratification of the senses as the highest good.</def>
<rj><au>Krauth-Fleming.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensualist</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"su*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. F.
<ets>sensualiste</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is sensual; one
given to the indulgence of the appetites or senses as the means of
happiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who holds to the doctrine of
sensualism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensualistic</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`su*al*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Sensual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Adopting or teaching the doctrines of
sensualism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensuality</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`su*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. F.
<ets>sensualit<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>sensualitas</ets> sensibility,
capacity for sensation.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
sensual; devotedness to the gratification of the bodily appetites; free
indulgence in carnal or sensual pleasures; luxuriousness;
voluptuousness; lewdness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those pampered animals<br/
That rage in savage <qex>sensuality</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They avoid dress, lest they should have affections tainted by any
<qex>sensuality</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensualization</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`su*al*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
sensualizing, or the state of being sensualized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensualize</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"su*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sensualized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sensualizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
make sensual; to subject to the love of sensual pleasure; to debase by
carnal gratifications; to carnalize; <as>as, <ex>sensualized</ex> by
pleasure</as>.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By the neglect of prayer, the thoughts are
<qex>sensualized</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>T. H. Skinner.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensually</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"su*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sensual
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensualness</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"su*al*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sensuality;
fleshliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensuism</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"su*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sensualism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensuosity</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`su*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sensuous; sensuousness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sensuous</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"su*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the senses, or sensible objects; addressing the senses;
suggesting pictures or images of sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To this poetry would be made precedent, as being less subtle and
fine, but more simple, <qex>sensuous</qex>, and passionate.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Highly susceptible to influence through the
senses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sen"su*ous*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sen"su*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sent</ent><br/
<hw>Sent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>See <er>Scent</er>,
<pos>v. & n.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sent</ent><br/
<hw>Sent</hw>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>3d pers. sing. pres.</pos> of
<er>Send</er>, for <xex>sendeth</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sent</ent><br/
<hw>Sent</hw>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of <er>Send</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentence</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"tence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>sententia</ets>, for <ets>sentientia</ets>, from <ets>sentire</ets>
to discern by the senses and the mind, to feel, to think. See
<er>Sense</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf. <er>Sentiensi</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Sense; meaning; significance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tales of best <qex>sentence</qex> and most solace.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The discourse itself, voluble enough, and full of
<qex>sentence</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An opinion; a decision; a
determination; a judgment, especially one of an unfavorable
nature.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My <qex>sentence</qex> is for open war.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That by them [Luther's works] we may pass <qex>sentence</qex> upon
his doctrines.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A philosophical or theological opinion; a dogma;
<as>as, Summary of the <ex>Sentences</ex>; Book of the
<ex>Sentences</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>In civil and admiralty law, the
judgment of a court pronounced in a cause; in criminal and
ecclesiastical courts, a judgment passed on a criminal by a court or
judge; condemnation pronounced by a judicial tribunal; doom. In common
law, the term is exclusively used to denote the judgment in criminal
cases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Received the <qex>sentence</qex> of the law.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A short saying, usually containing moral
instruction; a maxim; an axiom; a saw.</def>
<rj><au>Broome.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A combination of words which is
complete as expressing a thought, and in writing is marked at the close
by a period, or full point. See <er>Proposition</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sentences</xex> are simple or compound. A simple
sentence consists of one subject and one finite verb; as, <ldquo/The
Lord reigns.<rdquo/ A compound sentence contains two or more subjects
and finite verbs, as in this verse: -</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He fills, he bounds, connects, and equals all.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dark sentence</b></col>, <cd>a saying not easily
explained.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A king . . . understanding <qex>dark sentences</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dan. vii. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentence</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"tence</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sentenced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sentencing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
pass or pronounce judgment upon; to doom; to condemn to punishment; to
prescribe the punishment of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nature herself is <qex>sentenced</qex> in your doom.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To decree or announce as a sentence.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To utter sententiously.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentence method</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"tence meth`od</hw>. <fld>(Education)</fld> <def>A method of
teaching reading by giving first attention to phrases and sentences and
later analyzing these into their verbal and alphabetic components; --
contrasted with <contr>alphabet method</contr> and <contr>word
method</contr>. See also <contr>phonics</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentencer</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ten*cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who pronounced
a sentence or condemnation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sentential</ent><br/
<hw>sen*ten"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Comprising sentences; <as>as, a <ex>sentential</ex>
translation</as>.</def> <rj><au>Abp. Newcome.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a sentence, or full period;
<as>as, a <ex>sentential</ex> pause</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sententially</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*ten"tial*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sentential
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sententiarist</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*ten"ti*a*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
sententiary.</def> <rj><au>Barnas Sears (Life of Luther).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sententiary</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*ten"ti*ary</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sententiarius</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who read lectures, or
commented, on the Sentences of Peter Lombard, Bishop of Paris
(1159-1160), a school divine.</def> <rj><au>R. Henry.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sententiosity</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*ten`ti*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being sententious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sententious</ent><br/
<hw>Sen*ten"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>sentenciosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sentencieux</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding with sentences, axioms, and maxims; full of
meaning; terse and energetic in expression; pithy; <as>as, a
<ex>sententious</ex> style or discourse; <ex>sententious</ex>
truth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How he apes his sire,<br/
Ambitiously <qex>sententious</qex>!</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Comprising or representing sentences;
sentential.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Sententious</xex>
marks.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Grew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sen*ten"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sen*ten"tious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentery</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sentry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senteur</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"teur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Scent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentiency</ent><br/
<ent>Sentience</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sen"ti*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sen"ti*en*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sentient</er>,
<er>Sentence</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being sentient;
esp., the quality or state of having sensation.</def> <rj><au>G. H.
Lewes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An example of harmonious action between the intelligence and the
<qex>sentiency</qex> of the mind.</q> <rj><qau>Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentient</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ti*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sentiens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sentire</ets> to
discern or perceive by the senses. See <er>Sense</er>.]</ety>
<def>Having a faculty, or faculties, of sensation and perception.</def>
<specif>Specif.</specif> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <def>especially
sensitive; <as>as, the <ex>sentient</ex> extremities of nerves, which
terminate in the various organs or tissues</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentient</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ti*ent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who has the faculty of
perception; a sentient being.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentiently</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ti*ent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sentient or perceptive
way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentiment</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ti*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sentement</ets>, OF. <ets>sentement</ets>, F. <ets>sentiment</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>sentire</ets> to perceive by the senses and mind, to feel,
to think. See <er>Sentient</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
thought prompted by passion or feeling; a state of mind in view of some
subject; feeling toward or respecting some person or thing; disposition
prompting to action or expression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The word <qex>sentiment</qex>, agreeably to the use made of it by
our best English writers, expresses, in my own opinion very happily,
those complex determinations of the mind which result from the
cooperation of our rational powers and of our moral feelings.</q>
<rj><qau>Stewart.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Alike to council or the assembly came,<br/
With equal souls and <qex>sentiments</qex> the same.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, generally, a decision of the mind formed by
deliberation or reasoning; thought; opinion; notion; judgment; <as>as,
to express one's <ex>sentiments</ex> on a subject</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sentiments</qex> of philosophers about the perception of
external objects.</q> <rj><qau>Reid.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sentiment, as here and elsewhere employed by Reid in the meaning
of opinion (sententia), is not to be imitated.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sentence, or passage, considered as the expression
of a thought; a maxim; a saying; a toast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Sensibility; feeling; tender
susceptibility.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mr. Hume sometimes employs (after the manner of the French
metaphysicians) <qex>sentiment</qex> as synonymous with feeling; a use
of the word quite unprecedented in our tongue.</q>
<rj><qau>Stewart.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Less of <qex>sentiment</qex> than sense.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Thought; opinion; notion; sensibility;
feeling.</syn> -- <usage><er>Sentiment</er>, <er>Opinion</er>,
<er>Feeling</er>. An <xex>opinion</xex> is an intellectual judgment in
respect to any and every kind of truth. <xex>Feeling</xex> describes
those affections of pleasure and pain which spring from the exercise of
our sentient and emotional powers. <xex>Sentiment</xex> (particularly in
the plural) lies between them, denoting <xex>settled opinions</xex> or
principles in regard to subjects which interest the feelings strongly,
and are presented more or less constantly in practical life. Hence, it
is more appropriate to speak of our religious <xex>sentiments</xex> than
<xex>opinions</xex>, unless we mean to exclude all reference to our
feelings. The word <xex>sentiment</xex>, in the singular, leans
ordinarily more to the side of feeling, and denotes a refined
sensibility on subjects affecting the heart. <ldquo/On questions of
feeling, taste, observation, or report, we define our
<xex>sentiments</xex>. On questions of science, argument, or
metaphysical abstraction, we define our <xex>opinions</xex>. The
<xex>sentiments</xex> of the heart. The <xex>opinions</xex> of the mind
. . . There is more of instinct in <xex>sentiment</xex>, and more of
definition in <xex>opinion</xex>. The admiration of a work of art which
results from first impressions is classed with our
<xex>sentiments</xex>; and, when we have accounted to ourselves for the
approbation, it is classed with our <xex>opinions</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>W.
Taylor.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentimental</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`ti*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sentimental</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having, expressing, or
containing a sentiment or sentiments; abounding with moral reflections;
containing a moral reflection; didactic.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nay, ev'n each moral <qex>sentimental</qex> stroke,<br/
Where not the character, but poet, spoke,<br/
He lopped, as foreign to his chaste design,<br/
Nor spared a useless, though a golden line.</q>
<rj><qau>Whitehead.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inclined to sentiment; having an excess of sentiment
or sensibility; indulging the sensibilities for their own sake;
artificially or affectedly tender; -- often in a reproachful
sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sentimental</qex> mind is rather prone to overwrought
feeling and exaggerated tenderness.</q> <rj><qau>Whately.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Addressed or pleasing to the emotions only, usually
to the weaker and the unregulated emotions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Romantic.</syn> -- <usage><er>Sentimental</er>,
<er>Romantic</er>. <xex>Sentimental</xex> usually describes an error or
excess of the sensibilities; <xex>romantic</xex>, a vice of the
imagination. The votary of the former gives indulgence to his
sensibilities for the mere luxury of their excitement; the votary of the
latter allows his imagination to rove for the pleasure of creating
scenes of ideal enjoiment. <ldquo/Perhaps there is no less danger in
works called <xex>sentimental</xex>. They attack the heart more
successfully, because more cautiously.<rdquo/ <au>V. Knox.</au> <ldquo/I
can not but look on an indifferency of mind, as to the good or evil
things of this life, as a mere <xex>romantic</xex> fancy of such who
would be thought to be much wiser than they ever were, or could
be.<rdquo/ <au>Bp. Stillingfleet.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentimentalism</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`ti*men"tal*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sentimentalisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality of being
sentimental; the character or behavior of a sentimentalist;
sentimentality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentimentalist</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`ti*men"tal*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sentimentaliste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who has, or affects,
sentiment or fine feeling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentimentality</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`ti*men*tal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. F.
<ets>sentimentalit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being sentimental.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentimentalize</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`ti*men"tal*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
regard in a sentimental manner; <as>as, to <ex>sentimentalize</ex> a
subject</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentimentalize</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`ti*men"tal*ize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To think or act in a
sentimental manner, or like a sentimentalist; to affect exquisite
sensibility.</def> <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentimentally</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`ti*men"tal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sentimental
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentine</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sentina</ets> bilge water, hold of a ship, dregs: cf. F.
<ets>sentine</ets>.]</ety> <def>A place for dregs and dirt; a sink; a
sewer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentinel</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ti*nel</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sentinelle</ets> (cf.
It. <ets>sentinella</ets>); probably originally, a litle path, the
sentinel's beat,, and a dim. of a word meaning, path; cf. F.
<ets>sente</ets> path. L. <ets>semita</ets>; and OF. <ets>sentine</ets>,
<ets>sentele</ets>, <ets>senteret</ets>, diminutive words. Cf.
<er>Sentry</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who watches or guards;
specifically <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, a soldier set to guard an army, camp, or
other place, from surprise, to observe the approach of danger, and give
notice of it; a sentry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sentinels</qex> who paced the ramparts.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Watch; guard.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/That
princes do keep due <xex>sentinel</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A marine crab (<spn>Podophthalmus
vigil</spn>) native of the Indian Ocean, remarkable for the great length
of its eyestalks; -- called also <altname>sentinel
crab</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentinel</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"ti*nel</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sentineled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Sentinelled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sentineling</conjf> or
<conjf>Sentinelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To watch over
like a sentinel.</def> <ldquo/To <xex>sentinel</xex> enchanted
land.<rdquo/ <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To furnish with a sentinel; to place under the guard
of a sentinel or sentinels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentisection</ent><br/
<hw>Sen`ti*sec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sentire</ets> to feel + E. <ets>section</ets>.]</ety> <def>Painful
vivisection; -- opposed to <contr>callisection</contr>.</def> <rj><au>B.
G. Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sentry</ent><br/
<hw>Sen"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sentires</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Probably from OF.
<ets>senteret</ets> a little patch; cf. F. <ets>sentier</ets> path, and
OF. <ets>sente</ets>. See <er>Sentinel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A soldier placed on guard; a sentinel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Guard; watch, as by a sentinel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here toils, and death, and death's half-brother, sleep,<br/
Forms terrible to view, their <qex>sentry</qex> keep.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sentry box</b></col>, <cd>a small house or box to cover a
sentinel at his post, and shelter him from the weather.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Senza</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sen"za</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prep.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Without; <as>as, <ex>senza</ex> stromenti,
without instruments</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepal</ent><br/
<hw>Se"pal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>sepalum</ets>, formed in imitation of NL. <ets>petalum</ets>,
petal, to denote one of the divisions of the calyx: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/pale</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A leaf or
division of the calyx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ When the calyx consists of but one part, it is said to
be <xex>monosepalous</xex>; when of two parts, it is said to be
<xex>disepalous</xex>; when of a variable and indefinite number of
parts, it is said to be <xex>polysepalous</xex>; when of several parts
united, it is properly called <xex>gamosepalous</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1312 --></p>

<p><ent>Sepaled</ent><br/
<hw>Se"paled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having one or more sepals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepaline</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"al*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Relating to, or having the nature of, sepals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepalody</ent><br/
<hw>Se*pal"o*dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sepal</ets>
+ Gr. <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
metamorphosis of other floral organs into sepals or sepaloid
bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepaloid</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"al*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sepal</ets> +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Like a sepal, or a
division of a calyx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepalous</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having, or relating to, sepals; -- used mostly in composition. See
under <er>Sepal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separability</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`a*ra*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of
being separable or divisible; divisibility; separableness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separable</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*ra*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>separabilis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/parable</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Capable of being separated, disjoined, disunited, or divided;
<as>as, the <ex>separable</ex> parts of plants; qualities not
<ex>separable</ex> from the substance in which they exist.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sep"a*ra*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Sep"a*ra*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trials permit me not to doubt of the <qex>separableness</qex> of a
yellow tincture from gold.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Separated</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Separating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>separatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>separare</ets> to separate; pfref.
<ets>se-</ets> aside + <ets>parare</ets> to make ready, prepare. See
<er>Parade</er>, and cf. <er>Sever</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
disunite; to divide; to disconnect; to sever; to part in any
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From the fine gold I <qex>separate</qex> the alloy.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Separate</qex> thyself, I pray thee, from me.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xiii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who shall <qex>separate</qex> us from the love of Christ?</q>
<rj><qau>Rom. viii. 35.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To come between; to keep apart by occupying the
space between; to lie between; <as>as, the Mediterranean Sea
<ex>separates</ex> Europe and Africa</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To set apart; to select from among others, as for a
special use or service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Separate</qex> me Barnabas and Saul for the work whereunto I
have called thaem.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xiii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Separated flowers</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>flowers which have stamens and pistils in separate flowers;
diclinous flowers.</cd> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*rate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To part; to become disunited;
to be disconnected; to withdraw from one another; <as>as, the family
<ex>separated</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>p. a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>separatus</ets>, p. p. ]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Divided from
another or others; disjoined; disconnected; separated; -- said of things
once connected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him that was <qex>separate</qex> from his brethren.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xlix. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unconnected; not united or associated; distinct; --
said of things that have not been connected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For such an high priest became us, who is holy, harmless,
undefiled, <qex>separate</qex> from sinnere.</q> <rj><qau>Heb. vii.
26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Disunited from the body; disembodied; <as>as, a
<ex>separate</ex> spirit; the <ex>separate</ex> state of
souls</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Separate estate</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an estate
limited to a married woman independent of her husband.</cd> --
<col><b>Separate maintenance</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an
allowance made to a wife by her husband under deed of
separation.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sep"a*rate*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sep"a*rate*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separatical</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`a*rat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to separatism in religion; schismatical.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. T. Dwight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separating</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*ra`ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Designed or
employed to separate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Separating funnel</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
funnel, often globe-shaped, provided with a stopcock for the separate
drawing off of immiscible liquids of different specific
gravities.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separation</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`a*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>separatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/paration</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The act of separating, or the state of being separated, or
separate.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Chemical analysis.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Divorce.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Steam Boilers)</fld>
<def>The operation of removing water from steam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Judicial separation</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a
form of divorce; a separation of man and wife which has the effect of
making each a single person for all legal purposes but without ability
to contract a new marriage.</cd> <au>Mozley & W.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separatism</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*ra*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. F.
<ets>s<eacute/paratisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The character or act of a
separatist; disposition to withdraw from a church; the practice of so
withdrawing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separatist</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`a*ra*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/paratiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who withdraws or
separates himself; especially, one who withdraws from a church to which
he has belonged; a seceder from an established church; a dissenter; a
nonconformist; a schismatic; a sectary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Heavy fines on divines who should preach in any meeting of
<qex>separatist</qex> .</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separatistic</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`a*ra*tis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to separatists; characterizing separatists;
schismatical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separative</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*ra*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>separativus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Causing, or being to cause,
separation.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Separative</xex> virtue of extreme
cold.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separator</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*ra`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who, or that which, separates.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Steam Boilers)</fld> <def>A device for depriving steam of
particles of water mixed with it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mining)</fld>
<def>An apparatus for sorting pulverized ores into grades, or separating
them from gangue.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>An
instrument used for spreading apart the threads of the warp in the loom,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*ra*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Separative.</def> <rj><au>Cheyne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"a*ra*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/paratoire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An apparatus used in separating, as a separating funnel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>A surgical instrument for
separating the pericranium from the cranium.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Separatrix</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`a*ra"trix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>-trices</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>-trixes</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., she that separates.]</ety>
<fld>(Arith.)</fld> <def>The decimal point; the dot placed at the left
of a decimal fraction, to separate it from the whole number which it
follows. The term is sometimes also applied to other marks of
separation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepawn</ent><br/
<hw>Se*pawn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Supawn</er>.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepelible</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"e*li*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sepelibilis</ets>, fr. <ets>sepelire</ets> to bury.]</ety>
<def>Admitting of burial.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepelition</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`e*li"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Burial.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sephardic</ent><br/
<hw>Se*phar"dic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Sephardim</ets>, a name applied to the Spanish Jews, fr.
<etsep>Sephard</etsep>, name of a place where Jews were held in
captivity (<au>Ob. 20</au>).]</ety> <def>Of, pertaining to, or
designating, the Jews (the <xex>Sephardim</xex>, also called
<xex>Spanish</xex> or <xex>Portuguese Jews</xex>) descended from Jewish
families driven from Spain by the Inquisition.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sephardim</ent><br/
<hw>Se*phar"dim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NHeb.; orig.
uncert.]</ety> <def>Jews who are descendants of the former Jews of Spain
and Portugal. They are as a rule darker than the northern Jews, and have
more delicate features.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sephen</ent><br/
<hw>Se"phen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
large sting ray of the genus <gen>Trygon</gen>, especially <spn>Trygon
sephen</spn> of the Indian Ocean and the Red Sea. The skin is an article
of commerce.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepia</ent><br/
<hw>Se"pi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Sepias</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Sepiae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/ the cuttlefish, or squid.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The common European
cuttlefish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A genus comprising the common
cuttlefish and numerous similar species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pigment prepared from the ink, or black secretion,
of the sepia, or cuttlefish. Treated with caustic potash, it has a rich
brown color; and this mixed with a red forms <stype>Roman sepia</stype>.
Cf. <cref>India ink</cref>, under <er>India</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sepia drawing</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Sepia
picture</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a drawing in monochrome, made in sepia
alone, or in sepia with other brown pigments.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepia</ent><br/
<hw>Se"pi*a</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a dark brown color, with a
little red in its composition; also, made of, or done in,
sepia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepic</ent><br/
<hw>Se"pic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
sepia; done in sepia; <as>as, a <ex>sepic</ex> drawing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepidaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`i*da"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Like or pertaining to the cuttlefishes of the genus
Sepia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepiment</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"i*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sepimentum</ets>, <ets>saepimentum</ets>, from <ets>sepire</ets>,
<ets>saepire</ets>, to hedge in.]</ety> <def>Something that separates; a
hedge; a fence.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepiolite</ent><br/
<hw>Se"pi*o*lite`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Septa</ets> + <ets>-lite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Meerschaum. See <er>Meerschaum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepiostare</ent><br/
<hw>Se"pi*o*stare`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sepia</ets> + Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a bone.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The bone or shell of cuttlefish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepon</ent><br/
<hw>Se*pon"</hw> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Supawn</er>.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepose</ent><br/
<hw>Se*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L. pref
<ets>se-</ets> aside + E. <ets>pose</ets>.]</ety> <def>To set
apart.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seposit</ent><br/
<hw>Se*pos"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sepositus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>seponere</ets> to set aside.]</ety>
<def>To set aside; to give up.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seposition</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`o*si"tion</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sepositio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of setting aside, or of giving
up.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepoy</ent><br/
<hw>Se"poy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per.
<ets>sip<amac/h<imac/</ets>, fr. <ets>sip<amac/h</ets> an army. Cf.
<er>Spahi</er>.]</ety> <def>A native of India employed as a soldier in
the service of a European power, esp. of Great Britain; an Oriental
soldier disciplined in the European manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seppuku</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep*pu"ku</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Hara-kiri</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Seppuku</qex>, or hara-kiri, also came into vogue.</q>
<rj><qau>W. E. Griffis.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepsin</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"sin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
putrefaction.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A soluble poison
(ptomaine) present in putrid blood. It is also formed in the
putrefaction of proteid matter in general.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepsis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ putrefaction.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The poisoning of
the system by the introduction of putrescent material into the
blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sept</ent><br/
<hw>Sept</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption of
<ets>sect</ets>, n.]</ety> <def>A clan, tribe, or family, proceeding
from a common progenitor; -- used especially of the ancient clans in
Ireland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The chief, struck by the illustration, asked at once to be
baptized, and all his <qex>sept</qex> followed his example.</q>
<rj><qau>S. Lover.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septaemia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep*tae"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ putrid + <?/<?/<?/ blood.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Septicaemia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septal</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
septum or septa, as of a coral or a shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septane</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>septem</ets>
seven.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See <er>Heptane</er>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septangle</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tan`gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Septi-</ets>
+ <ets>angle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A figure which has
seven angles; a heptagon.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septangular</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tan"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Heptagonal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septarium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep*ta"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;<plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Septaria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>septum</ets>, <ets>saeptum</ets>, an inclosure, a partition, fr.
<ets>sepire</ets>, <ets>saepire</ets>, to inclose.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A flattened concretionary nodule, usually of
limestone, intersected within by cracks which are often filled with
calcite, barite, or other minerals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septum</ets>, <ets>saeptum</ets>, partition.]</ety> <def>Divided by
partition or partitions; having septa; <as>as, a <ex>septate</ex> pod or
shell</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>September</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tem"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>septem</ets> seven, as being the seventh month of the Roman year,
which began with March: cf. F. <ets>septembre</ets>. See
<er>Seven</er>.]</ety> <def>The ninth month of the year, containing
thurty days.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septemberer</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tem"ber*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
Setembrist.</def> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septembrist</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tem"brist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>septembriste</ets>.]</ety> <def>An agent in the massacres in Paris,
committed in patriotic frenzy, on the 22d of September, 1792.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septemfluous</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tem"flu*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>septemfluus</ets>; <ets>septem</ets> seven + <ets>fluere</ets> to
flow.]</ety> <def>Flowing sevenfold; divided into seven streams or
currents.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septempartite</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tem"par*tite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septem</ets> seven + E. <ets>partite</ets>.]</ety> <def>Divided
nearly to the base into seven parts; <as>as, a <ex>septempartite</ex>
leaf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septemtrioun</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tem"tri*oun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Septentrion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septemvir</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep*tem"vir</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
E. <plw>Septemvirs</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Septemviri</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>septemviri</ets>, pl.;
<ets>septem</ets> seven + <ets>viri</ets>, pl. of <ets>vir</ets>
man.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of a board of seven men
associated in some office.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septemvirate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tem"vi*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>septemviratus</ets>.]</ety> <def>The office of septemvir; a
government by septimvirs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septenary</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ten*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septenairus</ets>, from <ets>septeni</ets> seven each,
<ets>septem</ets> seven: cf. F. <ets>sept<eacute/naire</ets>. See
<er>Seven</er>. ]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of, or relating to,
seven; <as>as, a <ex>septenary</ex> number</as>.</def> <rj><au>I.
Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Lasting seven years; continuing seven years.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Septenary</xex> penance.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septenary</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ten*a*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The number seven.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septenate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ten*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septeni</ets> seven each.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having
parts in sevens; heptamerous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septennate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*ten"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>septennat</ets>.]</ety> <def>A period of seven years; <as>as, the
<ex>septennate</ex> during which the President of the French Republic
holds office</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septennial</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*ten"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septennium</ets> a period of seven years; <ets>septem</ets> seven +
<ets>annus</ets> year. See <er>Seven</er>, and <er>Annual</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Lasting or continuing seven years; <as>as,
<ex>septennial</ex> parliaments</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Happening or returning once in every seven years;
<as>as, <ex>septennial</ex> elections in England</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septennially</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*ten"ni*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Once in seven
years.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septentrial</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*ten"tri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Septentrional.</def> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septentrio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep*ten"tri*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Septentrion</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The constellation
Ursa Major.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septentrion</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*ten"tri*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septentrio</ets> the northern regions, the north, fr.
<ets>septentriones</ets> the seven stars near the north pole, called
Charles's Wain, or the Great Bear, also those called the Little Bear;
properly, the seven plow oxen; <ets>septem</ets> seven +
<ets>trio</ets>, orig., a plow ox: cf. F. <ets>septentrion</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The north or northern regions.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Both East West, South and <qex>Septentrioun</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septentrional</ent><br/
<ent>Septentrion</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sep*ten"tri*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sep*ten"tri*on*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>septentrionalis</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>septentrional</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the
north; northern.</def> <ldquo/From cold <xex>septentrion</xex>
blasts.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septentrionality</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*ten`tri*on*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Northerliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septentrionally</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*ten"tri*on*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Northerly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septentrionate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*ten"tri*on*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To tend
or point toward the north; to north.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septette</ent><br/
<ent>Septet</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sep*tet"</hw>, <hw>Sep*tette"</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L. <ets>septem</ets> seven, like
<ets>duet</ets>, from L. <ets>duo</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A set
of seven persons or objects; <as>as, a <ex>septet</ex> of
singers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A musical composition for seven
instruments or seven voices; -- called also
<altname>septuor</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septfoil</ent><br/
<hw>Sept"foil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sept</ets>
seven (L. <ets>septem</ets>) + E. <ets>foil</ets> leaf: cf. L.
<ets>septifolium</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
European herb, the tormentil. See <er>Tormentil</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An ornamental foliation having
seven lobes. Cf. <er>Cinquefoil</er>, <er>Quarterfoil</er>, and
<er>Trefoil</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.Art.)</fld> <def>A typical figure, consisting
of seven equal segments of a circle, used to denote the gifts of the
Holy Chost, the seven sacraments as recognized by the Roman Catholic
Church, etc.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septi-</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ti-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <ety>[L. <ets>septem</ets> seven.]</ety>
<def>A combining form meaning <xex>seven</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>septi</ex>folious, seven-leaved; <ex>septi</ex>-lateral,
seven-sided</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septic</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Septi-</ets> +
<ets>-ic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Of the seventh degree or
order.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Alg.)</fld> <def>A quantic of
the seventh degree.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septical</ent><br/
<ent>Septic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sep"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sep"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>septicus</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/,
fr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ to make putrid: cf. F. <ets>septique</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Having power to promote putrefaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septic</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A substance that promotes
putrefaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septicaemia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep`ti*cae"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/<?/<?/ putrefactive + <?/<?/<?/ blood.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A poisoned condition of the blood produced by the absorption into
it of septic or putrescent material; blood poisoning. It is marked by
chills, fever, prostration, and inflammation of the different serous
membranes and of the lungs, kidneys, and other organs.</def> <-- Now
septicemia. A condition caused by bacterial infection, the effects being
mediated by the release of toxins by bacteria, within the circulation.
(local infection is sepsis) Also septemia, septic fever, hematosepsis,
sapremia --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septically</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tic*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a septic
manner; in a manner tending to promote putrefaction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septicidal</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ti*ci`dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Septum</ets> + L. <ets>caedere</ets> to cut: cf. F.
<ets>septicide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Dividing the
partitions; -- said of a method of dehiscence in which a pod splits
through the partitions and is divided into its component
carpels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septicity</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Septic</er>.]</ety> <def>Tendency to putrefaction; septic
quality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septifarious</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`ti*fa"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septifariam</ets> sevenfold. Cf. <er>Bifarious</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Turned in seven different ways.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Septum</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>septif<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Bearing a
partition; -- said of the valves of a capsule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tif"er*ous</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/ putrefied +
<ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Conveying putrid poison; <as>as, the
virulence of <ex>septiferous</ex> matter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septifluous</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tif"lu*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Septemfluous</er>.]</ety> <def>Flowing in seven streams;
septemfluous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septifolious</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`ti*fo"li*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Septi-</ets> + L. <ets>folium</ets> leaf.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having seven leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septiform</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Septum</ets>
+ <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form of a septum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septifragal</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tif"ra*gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Septum</ets> + L. <ets>frangere</ets>, <ets>fractum</ets>, to
break.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Breaking from the partitions; --
said of a method of dehiscence in which the valves of a pod break away
from the partitions, and these remain attached to the common
axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septilateral</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`ti*lat"er*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Septi-</ets> + <ets>lateral</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having seven
sides; <as>as, a <ex>septilateral</ex> figure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septillion</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*til"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>septilion</ets>, formed fr. L. <ets>septem</ets> seven, in
imitation of <ets>million</ets>.]</ety> <def>According to the French
method of numeration (which is followed also in the United States), the
number expressed by a unit with twenty-four ciphers annexed. According
to the English method, the number expressed by a unit with forty-two
ciphers annexed. See <er>Numeration</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septimole</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ti*mole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septem</ets> seven.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A group of seven
notes to be played in the time of four or six.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septinsular</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*tin"su*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sept</ets>i- + <ets>insular</ets>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of
seven islands; <as>as, the <ex>septinsular</ex> republic of the Ionian
Isles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septisyllable</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ti*syl`la*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Septi-</ets> + <ets>syllable</ets>.]</ety> <def>A word of
seven syllables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septoic</ent><br/
<hw>Sep*to"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septem</ets> seven.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Heptoic</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septomaxillary</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`to*max"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the nasal septum and the
maxilla; situated in the region of these parts.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A small bone between the nasal septum and the
maxilla in many reptiles and amphibians.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septuagenarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`tu*a*ge*na"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person
who is seventy years of age; a septuagenary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septuagenary</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`tu*ag"e*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septuagenarius</ets>, fr. <ets>septuageny</ets> seventy each; akin
to <ets>septuaginta</ets> seventy, <ets>septem</ets> seven. See
<er>Seven</er>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of seventy; also, seventy years
old.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
septuagenarian.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septuagesima</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep`tu*a*ges"i*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. L. <ets>septuagesimus</ets> the seventieth, fr.
<ets>septuaginta</ets> seventy.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The third
Sunday before Lent; -- so called because it is about seventy days before
Easter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1313 --></p>

<p><ent>Septuagesimal</ent><br/
<hw>Sep`tu*a*ges"i*mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting
of seventy days, years, etc.; reckoned by seventies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our abridged and <qex>septuagesimal</qex> age.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septuagint</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tu*a*gint</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>septuaginta</ets> seventy.]</ety> <def>A Greek version of the Old
Testament; -- so called because it was believed to be the work of
seventy (or rather of seventy-two) translators.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The causes which produced it [the Septuagint], the
number and names of the translators, the times at which different
portions were translated, are all uncertain. The only point in which all
agree is that Alexandria was the birthplace of the version. On one other
point there is a near agreement, namely, as to time, that the version
was made, or at least commenced, in the time of the early Ptolemies, in
the first half of the third century b.c.</note> <rj><au>Dr. W. Smith
(Bib. Dict.)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Septuagint chronology</b></col>, <cd>the chronology
founded upon the dates of the Septuagint, which makes 1500 years more
from the creation to Abraham than the Hebrew Bible.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septuary</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tu*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>septem</ets> seven.]</ety> <def>Something composed of seven; a
week.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Ash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septulate</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Dim. fr.
<ets>septum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having imperfect or
spurious septa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septulum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep"tu*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Septula</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., dim. of L.
<ets>septum</ets> septum.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A little
septum; a division between small cavities or parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sep"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Septa</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>septum</ets>,
<ets>saeptum</ets>, an inclosure, hedge, fence, fr. <ets>sepire</ets>,
<ets>saepire</ets>, to hedge in, inclose.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A wall
separating two cavities; a partition; <as>as, the nasal
<ex>septum</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A partition that separates the
cells of a fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the radial
calcareous plates of a coral.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the
transverse partitions dividing the shell of a mollusk, or of a rhizopod,
into several chambers.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>One of the transverse
partitions dividing the body cavity of an annelid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septuor</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tu*or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A septet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septuple</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tu*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>septuplus</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/<?/:cf. F.
<ets>septuple</ets>. Cf. <er>Double</er>, <er>Quadruple</er>.]</ety>
<def>Seven times as much; multiplied by seven; sevenfold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Septuple</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"tu*ple</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Septupled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Septupling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To multiply by
seven; to make sevenfold.</def> <rj><au>Sir J. Herschel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepulchre</ent><br/
<ent>Sepulcher</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sep"ul*cher</hw>, <hw>Sep"ul*chre</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sepulcre</ets>, OF. sepulcre, F.
<ets>s<eacute/pulcre</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sepulcrum</ets>,
<ets>sepulchrum</ets>, fr. <ets>sepelire</ets> to bury.]</ety> <def>The
place in which the dead body of a human being is interred, or a place
set apart for that purpose; a grave; a tomb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stony entrance of this <qex>sepulcher</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The first day of the week cometh Mary Magdalene early, when it was
yet dark, unto the <qex>sepulcher</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>John xx.
1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>A whited sepulcher</b></col>. <cd>Fig.: Any person who is
fair outwardly but unclean or vile within. See <au>Matt. xxiii.
27.</au></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepulchre</ent><br/
<ent>Sepulcher</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sep"ul*cher</hw>, <hw>Sep"ul*chre</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sepulchered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Sepulchred</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sepulchering</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Sepulchring</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<def>To bury; to inter; to entomb; <as>as, obscurely
<ex>sepulchered</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And so <qex>sepulchered</qex> in such pomp dost lie<br/
That kings for such a tomb would wish to die.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepulchral</ent><br/
<hw>Se*pul"chral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sepulcralis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/pulcral</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to burial, to the grave, or to
monuments erected to the memory of the dead; <as>as, a
<ex>sepulchral</ex> stone; a <ex>sepulchral</ex>
inscription.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unnaturally low and grave; hollow in tone; -- said
of sound, especially of the voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This exaggerated dulling of the voice . . . giving what is
commonly called a <qex>sepulchral</qex> tone.</q> <rj><qau>H.
Sweet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sepulture</ent><br/
<hw>Sep"ul*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/pulture</ets>, L. <ets>sepultura</ets>, fr.
<ets>sepelire</ets>, <ets>sepultum</ets>, to bury.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of depositing the dead body of a human being in the grave;
burial; interment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where we may royal <qex>sepulture</qex> prepare.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sepulcher; a grave; a place of burial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Drunkeness that is the horrible <qex>sepulture</qex> of man's
reason.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequacious</ent><br/
<hw>Se*qua"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sequax</ets>, <ets>-acis</ets>, fr. <ets>suquit</ets> to follow.
See <er>Sue</er> to follow. ]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Inclined to follow
a leader; following; attendant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trees uprooted left their place,<br/
<qex>Sequacious</qex> of the lyre.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, ductile; malleable; pliant;
manageable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the greater bodies the forge was easy, the matter being ductile
and <qex>sequacious</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having or observing logical sequence; logically
consistent and rigorous; consecutive in development or transition of
thought.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The scheme of pantheistic omniscience so prevalent among the
<qex>sequacious</qex> thinkers of the day.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Milton was not an extensive or discursive thinker, as Shakespeare
was; for the motions of his mind were slow, solemn, and
<qex>sequacious</qex>, like those of the planets.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequaciousness</ent><br/
<hw>Se*qua"cious*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
sequacious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequacity</ent><br/
<hw>Se*quac"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sequacitas</ets>.]</ety> <def>Quality or state of being sequacious;
sequaciousness.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequel</ent><br/
<hw>Se"quel</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"kw<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sequela</ets>, fr. <ets>sequit</ets> to follow: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/quelle</ets> a following. See <er>Sue</er> to
follow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which follows; a succeeding part;
continuation; <as>as, the <ex>sequel</ex> of a man's advantures or
history</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, let me say no more!<br/
Gather the <qex>sequel</qex> by that went before.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consequence; event; effect; result; <as>as, let the
sun cease, fail, or swerve, and the <ex>sequel</ex> would be
ruin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Conclusion; inference.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Whitgift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequela</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*que"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sequelae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a follower, a result,
from <ets>sequit</ets> to follow.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which,
follows.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An adherent, or a band or
sect of adherents.</def> <ldquo/Coleridge and his
<xex>sequela</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>G. P. Marsh.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>That
which follows as the logical result of reasoning; inference; conclusion;
suggestion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sequelae</qex>, or thoughts suggested by the preceding
aphorisms.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A morbid phenomenon left as the
result of a disease; a disease resulting from another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequence</ent><br/
<hw>Se"quence</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"kw<eit/ns)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/quence</ets>, L. <ets>sequentia</ets>, fr.
<ets>sequens</ets>. See <er>Sequent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
state of being sequent; succession; order of following;
arrangement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How art thou a king<br/
But by fair <qex>sequence</qex> and succession?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sequence</qex> and series of the seasons of the year.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which follows or succeeds as an effect; sequel;
consequence; result.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The inevitable <qex>sequences</qex> of sin and punishment.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Philos.)</fld> <def>Simple succession, or the
coming after in time, without asserting or implying causative energy;
<as>as, the reactions of chemical agents may be conceived as merely
invariable <ex>sequences</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any succession of
chords (or harmonic phrase) rising or falling by the regular diatonic
degrees in the same scale; a succession of similar harmonic steps.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A melodic phrase or passage successively repeated one
tone higher; a rosalia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(R.C.Ch.)</fld> <def>A hymn introduced in the Mass
on certain festival days, and recited or sung immediately before the
gospel, and after the gradual or introit, whence the name.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Fitzpatrick.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Originally the <qex>sequence</qex> was called a Prose, because its
early form was rhythmical prose.</q> <rj><qau>Shipley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Whist)</fld>
<def>Three or more cards of the same suit in immediately consecutive
order of value; as, ace, king, and queen; or knave, ten, nine, and
eight.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Poker)</fld> <def>All five cards, of a
hand, in consecutive order as to value, but not necessarily of the same
suit; when of one suit, it is called a <stype>sequence
flush</stype>.</def> <-- sequence is usu. called a run, and five are now
called straight and straight flush --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>the specific order of any linear arrangement of
items; <as>as, the <ex>sequence</ex> of amino acid residues in a
protein; the <ex>sequence</ex> of instructions in a computer program;
the <ex>sequence</ex> of acts in a variety show</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequence</ent><br/
<hw>Se"quence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Biochem.)</fld>
<def>to determine the sequence of; <as>as, to <ex>sequence</ex> a
protein or a DNA fragment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequent</ent><br/
<hw>Se"quent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sequens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sequi</ets> to
follow. See <er>Sue</er> to follow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Following;
succeeding; in continuance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What to this was <qex>sequent</qex><br/
Thou knowest already.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Following as an effect; consequent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequent</ent><br/
<hw>Se"quent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A follower.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which follows as a result; a
sequence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequential</ent><br/
<hw>Se*quen"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Succeeding or
following in order.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Se*quen"tial*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequester</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ques"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sequestered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sequestering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/questrer</ets>, L. <ets>sequestrare</ets> to give up for
safe keeping, from <ets>sequester</ets> a depositary or trustee in whose
hands the thing contested was placed until the dispute was settled. Cf.
<er>Sequestrate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To
separate from the owner for a time; to take from parties in controversy
and put into the possession of an indifferent person; to seize or take
possession of, as property belonging to another, and hold it till the
profits have paid the demand for which it is taken, or till the owner
has performed the decree of court, or clears himself of contempt; in
international law, to confiscate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Formerly the goods of a defendant in chancery were, in the last
resort, <qex>sequestered</qex> and detained to enforce the decrees of
the court. And now the profits of a benefice are <qex>sequestered</qex>
to pay the debts of ecclesiastics.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause (one) to submit to the process of
sequestration; to deprive (one) of one's estate, property,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was his tailor and his cook, his fine fashions and his French
ragouts, which <qex>sequestered</qex> him.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To set apart; to put aside; to remove; to separate
from other things.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had wholly <qex>sequestered</qex> my civil affairss.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to retire or withdraw into obscurity; to
seclude; to withdraw; -- often used reflexively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When men most <qex>sequester</qex> themselves from action.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A love and desire to <qex>sequester</qex> a man's self for a
higher conversation.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 5. (Chem.) To bind, so as to make [a metal ion] unavailable in
its normal form; -- said of chelating agents, such as EDTA, which, in a
solution, bind tightly to multivalent metal cations, thereby lowering
their effective concentration in solution. Compounds employed
particularly for this purpose are called sequestering agents, or
chelating agents. In biochemistry, sequestration is one means of
reversibly inhibiting enzymes which depend on divalent metal cations
(such as Magnesium) for their activity. Such agents are used, for
example, to help preserve blood for storage and subsequent use in
transfusion. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequester</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ques"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To withdraw; to
retire.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>sequester</qex> out of the world into Atlantic and Utopian
politics.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To renounce (as a widow may) any
concern with the estate of her husband.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequester</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ques"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Sequestration;
separation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A person with whom two or more
contending parties deposit the subject matter of the controversy; one
who mediates between two parties; a mediator; an umpire or
referee.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Sequestrum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequestered</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ques"tered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Retired;
secluded.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sequestered</xex> scenes.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Along the cool, <qex>sequestered</qex> vale of life.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequestrable</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ques"tra*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being sequestered; subject or liable to sequestration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequestral</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ques"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a sequestrum.</def>
<rj><au>Quian.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequestrate</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ques"trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sequestrated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sequestrating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To sequester.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequestration</ent><br/
<hw>Seq`ues*tra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sequestratio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/questration</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Civil & Com. Law)</fld> <def>The act of
separating, or setting aside, a thing in controversy from the possession
of both the parties that contend for it, to be delivered to the one
adjudged entitled to it. It may be voluntary or involuntary.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Chancery)</fld> <def>A prerogative process empowering
certain commissioners to take and hold a defendant's property and
receive the rents and profits thereof, until he clears himself of a
contempt or performs a decree of the court.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<fld>(Eccl. Law)</fld> <def>A kind of execution for a rent, as in the
case of a beneficed clerk, of the profits of a benefice, till he shall
have satisfied some debt established by decree; the gathering up of the
fruits of a benefice during a vacancy, for the use of the next
incumbent; the disposing of the goods, by the ordinary, of one who is
dead, whose estate no man will meddle with.</def> <au>Craig.</au>
<au>Tomlins.</au> <au>Wharton.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Internat.
Law)</fld> <def>The seizure of the property of an individual for the use
of the state; particularly applied to the seizure, by a belligerent
power, of debts due from its subjects to the enemy.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being separated or set aside;
separation; retirement; seclusion from society.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Since Henry Monmouth first began to reign, . . . <br/
This loathsome <qex>sequestration</qex> have I had.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Disunion; disjunction.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 4. (Chem.) the act or process of sequestering (v. t. sense 5)
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequestrator</ent><br/
<hw>Seq"ues*tra`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., one that
hinders or impedes.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One who
sequesters property, or takes the possession of it for a time, to
satisfy a demand out of its rents or profits.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>One to whom the keeping of sequestered property is
committed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequestrum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*ques"trum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sequestra</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sequester</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A portion of dead bone
which becomes separated from the sound portion, as in
necrosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequin</ent><br/
<hw>Se"quin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sequin</ets>,
It. <ets>zecchino</ets>, from <ets>zecca</ets> the mint, fr. Ar.
<ets>sekkah</ets>, <ets>sikkah</ets>, a die, a stamp. Cf.
<er>Zechin</er>.]</ety> <def>An old gold coin of Italy and Turkey. It
was first struck at Venice about the end of the 13th century, and
afterward in the other Italian cities, and by the Levant trade was
introduced into Turkey. It is worth about 9s. 3d. sterling, or about
$2.25. The different kinds vary somewhat in value.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>chequin</asp>, and <asp>zequin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequoia</ent><br/
<hw>Se*quoi"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. So called by
Dr. Endlicher in honor of <ets>Sequoyah</ets>, who invented the Cherokee
alphabet.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of coniferous trees,
consisting of two species, <spn>Sequoia Washingtoniana</spn>, syn.
<spn>Sequoia gigantea</spn>, the <ldquo/big tree<rdquo/ of California,
and <spn>Sequoia sempervirens</spn>, the redwood, both of which attain
an immense height.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sequoiene</ent><br/
<hw>Se*quoi"ene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A hydrocarbon (<chform>C13H10</chform>) obtained in white
fluorescent crystals, in the distillation products of the needles of the
California <ldquo/big tree<rdquo/ (<spn>Sequoia
gigantea</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serac</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>S<eacute/`rac"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. (in the
Alps), orig., a kind of solid cheese.]</ety> <def>A pinnacle of ice
among the crevasses of a glacier; also, one of the blocks into which a
glacier breaks on a steep grade.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seraglio</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ragl"io</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>serraglio</ets>, originally, an inclosure of palisades, afterwards
also, a palace, seraglio (by confusion with Per.
<ets>ser<amac/<ium/</ets>a a palace, an entirely different word), fr.
<ets>serrare</ets> to shut, fr. LL. <ets>serra</ets> a bar for fastening
doors, L. <ets>sera</ets>. See <er>Serry</er>, <er>Series</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An inclosure; a place of separation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I went to the Ghetto, where the Jews dwell as in a suburb, by
themselves. I passed by the piazza Judea, where their
<qex>seraglio</qex> begins.</q> <rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The palace of the Grand Seignior, or Turkish sultan,
at Constantinople, inhabited by the sultan himself, and all the officers
and dependents of his court. In it are also kept the females of the
harem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A harem; a place for keeping wives or concubines;
sometimes, loosely, a place of licentious pleasure; a house of
debauchery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serai</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*ra"i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per.
<ets>ser<amac/<ium/</ets>, or <ets>sar<amac/<ium/</ets>, a palace, a
king's court, a seraglio, an inn. Cf. <er>Caravansary</er>.]</ety>
<def>A palace; a seraglio; also, in the East, a place for the
accommodation of travelers; a caravansary, or rest house.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seralbumen</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`al*bu"men</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
CHem.)</fld> <def>Serum albumin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serang</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*rang"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per.
<ets>sarhang</ets> a commander.]</ety> <def>The boatswain of a Lascar or
East Ondian crew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serape</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*ra"pe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. Amer.
<ets>sarape</ets>.]</ety> <def>A blanket or shawl worn as an outer
garment by the Spanish Americans, as in Mexico.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seraph</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"aph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Seraphs</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, Heb. <plw>Seraphim</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Heb. <ets>ser<amac/phim</ets>, pl.]</ety>
<def>One of an order of celestial beings, each having three pairs of
wings. In ecclesiastical art and in poetry, a seraph is represented as
one of a class of angels.</def> <rj><au>Isa. vi. 2.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As full, as perfect, in vile man that mourns,<br/
As the rapt <qex>seraph</qex> that adores and burns.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seraph moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
numerous species of geometrid moths of the genus <gen>Lobophora</gen>,
having the hind wings deeply bilobed, so that they seem to have six
wings.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seraphical</ent><br/
<ent>Seraphic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se*raph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Se*raph"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/raphique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a seraph;
becoming, or suitable to, a seraph; angelic; sublime; pure;
refined.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Seraphic</xex> arms and trophies.<rdquo/
<au>Milton.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Seraphical</xex> fervor.<rdquo/ <au>Jer.
Taylor.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Se*raph"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Se*raph"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seraphicism</ent><br/
<hw>Se*raph"i*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The character,
quality, or state of a seraph; seraphicalness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seraphim</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"a*phim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The Hebrew plural
of <er>Seraph</er>. Cf. <er>Cherubim</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The double plural form <xex>seraphims</xex> is sometimes
used, as in the King James version of the Bible, <au>Isa. vi. 2 and
6.</au></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1314 --></p>

<p><ent>Seraphina</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`a*phi"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>A seraphine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seraphine</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"a*phine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Seraph</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A wind instrument whose
sounding parts are reeds, consisting of a thin tongue of brass playing
freely through a slot in a plate. It has a case, like a piano, and is
played by means of a similar keybord, the bellows being worked by the
foot. The <xex>melodeon</xex> is a portable variety of this
instrument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serapis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*ra"pis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Myth.)</fld> <def>An Egyptian deity,
at first a symbol of the Nile, and so of fertility; later, one of the
divinities of the lower world. His worship was introduced into Greece
and Rome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seraskier</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ras"kier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Turk., fr. Per.
<ets>ser</ets> head, chief + Ar. <ets>'asker</ets> an army.]</ety>
<def>A general or commander of land forces in the Turkish empire;
especially, the commander-in-chief of minister of war.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seraskierate</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ras"kier*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or
authority of a seraskier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serbonian</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*bo"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to the
lake of Serbonis in Egypt, which by reason of the sand blowing into it
had a deceptive appearance of being solid land, but was a bog.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A gulf profound as that <qex>Serbonian</qex> bog . . . <br/
Where armies whole have sunk.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sere</ent><br/
<hw>Sere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dry; withered. Same as
<er>Sear</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But with its sound it shook the sails<br/
That were so thin and <qex>sere</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sere</ent><br/
<hw>Sere</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>serre</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Claw; talon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serein</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se*rein"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. Cf.
<er>Serenade</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Meteorol.)</fld> <def>A
mist, or very fine rain, which sometimes falls from a clear sky a few
moments after sunset.</def> <rj><au>Tyndall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serenade</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`e*nade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/r<eacute/nade</ets>, It. <ets>serenata</ets>, probably fr.
L. <ets>serenus</ets> serene (cf. <er>Serene</er>), misunderstood as a
derivative fr. L. <ets>serus</ets> late. Cf.
<er>Soir<eacute/e</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Music
sung or performed in the open air at nights; -- usually applied to
musical entertainments given in the open air at night, especially by
gentlemen, in a spirit of gallantry, under the windows of ladies.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A piece of music suitable to be performed at such
times.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serenade</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`e*nade"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Serenaded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Serenading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To entertain with a
serenade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serenade</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`e*nade"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To perform a
serenade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serenader</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`e*nad"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
serenades.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serenate</ent><br/
<ent>Serenata</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ser`e*na"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ser"e*nate</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>serenata</ets>. See
<er>Serenade</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A piece of vocal music,
especially one on an amoreus subject; a serenade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Or <qex>serenate</qex>, which the starved lover sings<br/
To his pround fair.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name <xex>serenata</xex> was given by Italian
composers in the time of Handel, and by Handel himself, to a cantata of
a pastoreal of dramatic character, to a secular ode, etc.; also by
Mozart and others to an orchectral composition, in several movements,
midway between the suite of an earlier period and the modern
symphony.</note> <rj><au>Grove.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serene</ent><br/
<hw>Se*rene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serenus</ets> to grow dry, Gr. <?/<?/<?/ hot, scorching.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Bright; clear; unabscured; <as>as, a <ex>serene</ex>
sky</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The moon <qex>serene</qex> in glory mounts the sky.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Full many a gem of purest ray <qex>serene</qex><br/
The dark unfathomed caves of ocean bear.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Calm; placid; undisturbed; unruffled; <as>as, a
<ex>serene</ex> aspect; a <ex>serene</ex> soul</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In several countries of Europe, <xex>Serene</xex> is
given as a tittle to princes and the members of their families; as, His
<xex>Serene</xex> Highness.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Drop serene</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Amaurosis</er>.</cd> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serene</ent><br/
<hw>Se*rene"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Serenity; clearness;
calmness.</def> <mark>[Poetic.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>serene</xex> of
heaven.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To their master is denied<br/
To share their sweet <qex>serene</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[F. <ets>serein</ets> evening dew or damp. See
<er>Serein</er>.]</ety> <def>Evening air; night chill.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Some <xex>serene</xex> blast me.<rdquo/
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serene</ent><br/
<hw>Se*rene"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>serenare</ets>.]</ety>
<def>To make serene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Heaven and earth, as if contending, vie<br/
To raise his being, and <qex>serene</qex> his soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serenely</ent><br/
<hw>Se*rene"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a serene
manner; clearly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now setting Ph<oe/bus shone <qex>serenely</qex> bright.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>With unruffled temper; coolly; calmly.</def>
<rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sereneness</ent><br/
<hw>Se*rene"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Serenity.</def>
<rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serenitude</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ren"i*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Serenity.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serenity</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ren"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serenuas</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/r<eacute/nit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being serene; clearness and calmness; quietness;
stillness; peace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A general peace and <qex>serenity</qex> newly succeeded a general
trouble.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Calmness of mind; eveness of temper; undisturbed
state; coolness; composure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I can not see how any men should ever transgress those moral rules
with confidence and <qex>serenity</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Serenity</xex> is given as a title to the members
of certain princely families in Europe; as, Your
<xex>Serenity</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serf</ent><br/
<hw>Serf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>serus</ets> servant, slave; akin to <ets>servare</ets> to protect,
preserve, observe, and perhaps originally, a client, a man under one's
protection. Cf. <er>Serve</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>A servant
or slave employed in husbandry, and in some countries attached to the
soil and transferred with it, as formerly in Russia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In England, at least from the reign of Henry II, one only, and
that the inferior species [of villeins], existed . . . But by the
customs of France and Germany, persons in this abject state seem to have
been called <qex>serfs</qex>, and distinguished from
<qex>villeins</qex>, who were only bound to fixed payments and duties in
respect of their lord, though, as it seems, without any legal redress if
injured by him.</q> <rj><qau>Hallam.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Serf</er>, <er>Slave</er>.</syn> <usage> A
<xex>slave</xex> is the absolute property of his master, and may be sold
in any way. A <xex>serf</xex>, according to the strict sense of the
term, is one bound to work on a certain estate, and thus attached to the
soil, and sold with it into the service of whoever purchases the
land.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serfdom</ent><br/
<ent>Serfage</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Serf"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Serf"dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or condition of a serf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serfism</ent><br/
<ent>Serfhood</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Serf"hood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Serf"ism</hw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr></mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Serfage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serge</ent><br/
<hw>Serge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>serge</ets>,
<ets>sarge</ets>, <ets>originally</ets>, <ets>a silken stuff</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>serica</ets>, f. or neut. pl. of <ets>sericus</ets> silken. See
<er>Sericeous</er>, <er>Silk</er>.]</ety> <def>A woolen twilled stuff,
much used as material for clothing for both sexes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Silk serge</b></col>, <cd>a twilled silk fabric used
mostly by tailors for lining parts of gentlemen's coats.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serge</ent><br/
<hw>Serge</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>cierge</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
large wax candle used in the ceremonies of various churches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sergeancy</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"gean*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sergeancies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sergeanty</er>.]</ety> <def>The office of a sergeant;
sergeantship.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>serjeancy</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sergeant</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"geant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sergent</ets>, fr. L. <ets>serviens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p.
pr. of <ets>servire</ets> to serve. See <er>Serve</er>, and cf.
<er>Servant</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>serjeant</asp>. Both
spellings are authorized. In England <asp>serjeant</asp> is usually
preferred, except for military officers. In the United States
<asp>sergeant</asp> is common for civil officers also.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Formerly, in England, an officer nearly answering to
the more modern bailiff of the hundred; also, an officer whose duty was
to attend on the king, and on the lord high steward in court, to arrest
traitors and other offenders. He is now called
<xex>sergeant-at-arms</xex>, and two of these officers, by allowance of
the sovereign, attend on the houses of Parliament (one for each house)
to execute their commands, and another attends the Court
Chancery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sergeant</qex> of the town of Rome them sought.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The magistrates sent the <qex>serjeant</qex>, saying, Let those
men go.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xvi. 35.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This fell <qex>sergeant</qex>, Death,<br/
Is strict in his arrest.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>In a company, battery, or troop, a
noncommissioned officer next in rank above a corporal, whose duty is to
instruct recruits in discipline, to form the ranks, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the United States service, besides the
<xex>sergeants</xex> belonging to the companies there are, in each
regiment, a <xex>sergeant major</xex>, who is the chief noncommissioned
officer, and has important duties as the assistant to the adjutant; a
<xex>quartermaster sergeant</xex>, who assists the quartermaster; a
<xex>color sergeant</xex>, who carries the colors; and a <xex>commissary
sergeant</xex>, who assists in the care and distribution of the stores.
<xex>Ordnance sergeants</xex> have charge of the ammunition at military
posts.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A lawyer of the highest rank,
answering to the <xex>doctor</xex> of the civil law; -- called also
<altname>serjeant at law</altname>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A title sometimes given to the servants of the
sovereign; <as>as, <ex>sergeant</ex> surgeon, that is, a servant, or
attendant, surgeon</as>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The cobia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Drill sergeant</b></col>. <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Drill</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sergeant-at-arms</b></col>, <cd>an
officer of a legislative body, or of a deliberative or judicial
assembly, who executes commands in preserving order and arresting
offenders. See <er>Sergeant</er>, 1.</cd> -- <col><b>Sergeant
major</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <cd>See the Note under
def. 2, above.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>The cow
pilot.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sergeantcy</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"geant*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sergeancy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sergeantry</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"geant*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. OF.
<ets>sergenteric</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Sergeanty</er>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>serjeantry</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sergeantship</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"geant*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of
sergeant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sergeanty</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"geant*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>sergentie</ets>, LL. <ets>sergentia</ets>. See
<er>Sergeant</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld> <def>Tenure of lands of
the crown by an honorary kind of service not due to any lord, but to the
king only.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>serjeanty</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Grand sergeanty</b></col>, <cd>a particular kind of
tenure by which the tenant was bound to do some special honorary service
to the king in person, as to carry his banner, his sword, or the
like.</cd> <au>Tomlins.</au> <au>Cowell.</au> <au>Blackstone.</au> --
<col><b>Petit sergeanty</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Petit</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serial</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a series; consisting of a series; appearing in successive
parts or numbers; <as>as, a <ex>serial</ex> work or
publication</as>.</def> <ldquo/Classification . . . may be more or less
<xex>serial</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>H. Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to rows.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Serial homology</b></col>. <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Homology</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Serial symmetry</b></col>.
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Symmetry</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serial</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ri*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A publication appearing in a
series or succession of part; a tale, or other writing, published in
successive numbers of a periodical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seriality</ent><br/
<hw>Se`ri*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of succession in a series; sequence.</def> <rj><au>H.
Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serially</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ri*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a series, or regular
order; in a serial manner; <as>as, arranged <ex>serially</ex>; published
<ex>serially</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seriate</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ri*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Arranged in a series
or succession; pertaining to a series.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Se"ri*ate*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seriatim</ent><br/
<hw>Se`ri*a"tim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>In regular order; one after the other; severally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seriation</ent><br/
<hw>Se`ri*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Arrangement or position in a series.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sericeous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ri"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sericus</ets> silken, <ets>sericum</ets> Seric stuff, silk, fr.
<ets>Sericus</ets> belonging to the <ets>Seres</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/, a
people of Eastern Asia, the modern Chinese, celebrated for their silken
fabrics. Cf. <er>Silk</er>, <er>Serge</er> a woolen stuff.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to silk; consisting of silk;
silky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Covered with very soft hairs
pressed close to the surface; <as>as, a <ex>sericeous</ex>
leaf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a silklike luster, usually
due to fine, close hairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sericin</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"i*cin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sericus</ets> silken.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A gelatinous
nitrogenous material extracted from crude silk and other similar fiber
by boiling water; -- called also <altname>silk
gelatin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sericite</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"i*cite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sericus</ets> silken.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A kind of
muscovite occuring in silky scales having a fibrous structure. It is
characteristic of sericite schist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sericterium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser`ic*te"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sericeous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A silk gland, as in
the silkworms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sericulture</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"i*cul`ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sericeous</er>, and <er>Culture</er>.]</ety> <def>The raising of
silkworms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serie</ent><br/
<hw>Se"rie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/rie</ets>.]</ety> <def>Series.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seriema</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser`i*e"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Native
name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>either of two large South American
birds related to the cranes, the <altname>cariama</altname> of Southern
Brazil (<spn>Cariama cristata</spn>, formerly <spn>Dicholophus
cristata</spn>) or the <spn>Chunga burmeisteri</spn> of Argentina. They
have an erectile crest and a short, broad bill. They are often
domesticated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Series</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ries</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>series</ets>,
fr. <ets>serere</ets>, <ets>sertum</ets>, to join or bind together; cf.
Gr. <?/<?/<?/ to fasten, Skr. <ets>sarit</ets> thread. Cf.
<er>Assert</er>, <er>Desert</er> a solitude, <er>Exert</er>,
<er>Insert</er>, <er>Seraglio</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A number of
things or events standing or succeeding in order, and connected by a
like relation; sequence; order; course; a succession of things; <as>as,
a continuous <ex>series</ex> of calamitous events</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>During some years his life a <qex>series</qex> of triumphs.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Any comprehensive group of
animals or plants including several subordinate related
groups.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Sometimes a series includes several classes; sometimes
only orders or families; in other cases only species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>In Engler's system of plant
classification, a group of families showing certain structural or
morphological relationships. It corresponds to the
<altname>cohort</altname> of some writers, and to the
<altname>order</altname> of many modern systematists.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>An indefinite number of terms
succeeding one another, each of which is derived from one or more of the
preceding by a fixed law, called the <xex>law</xex> of the series;
<as>as, an arithmetical <ex>series</ex>; a geometrical
<ex>series</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A mode of arranging the separate
parts of a circuit by connecting them successively end to end to form a
single path for the current; -- opposed to <contr>parallel</contr>. The
parts so arranged are said to be <col><b>in series</b></col>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A parcel of rough diamonds of
assorted qualities.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Series dynamo</ent><br/
<hw>Series dynamo</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
series-wound dynamo.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A dynamo running in series
with another or others.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Series motor</ent><br/
<hw>Series motor</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
series-wound motor.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A motor capable of being
used in a series circuit.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Series turns</ent><br/
<hw>Series turns</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The turns in a series
circuit.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Series winding</ent><br/
<hw>Series winding</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A winding in which the
armature coil and the field-magnet coil are in series with the external
circuits; -- opposed to <contr>shunt winding</contr>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Se"ries-wound`</wf>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serigraph</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`i*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sericum</ets> silk + E. <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
autographic device to test the strength of raw silk.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serin</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>serin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A European finch
(<spn>Serinus hortulanus</spn>) closely related to the canary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serine</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"ine</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/r"<emac/n; s<ecr/r"<icr/n; also, less
correctly, s<emac/r"<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sericus</ets> silken.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>one of the
natural <isa>L-amino acids</isa>, obtainable as a white crystalline
nitrogenous substance by the action of dilute sulphuric acid on silk
gelatin. It is found in many proteins, and, having a free primary
hydroxyl group on the side chain, is involved in the catalytic action at
the active site of some enzymes, such as proteases. The IUPAC
abbreviation for serine in protein sequences is <abbr>Ser</abbr>.
Chemically it is <chname>2-amino-3-hydroxy-propanoic acid</chname>
(<chform>C3H7NO3</chform>),
<chform>HO.CH2.CH(NH2).COOH</chform>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serio-comical</ent><br/
<ent>Serio-comic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se`ri*o-com"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Se`ri*o-com"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a mixture of seriousness
and sport; serious and comical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serious</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/rieux</ets>, LL.
<ets>seriosus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Grave in manner or
disposition; earnest; thoughtful; solemn; not light, gay, or
volatile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is always <qex>serious</qex>, yet there is about his manner a
graceful ease.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Really intending what is said; being in earnest; not
jesting or deceiving.</def> <rj><au>Beaconsfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Important; weighty; not trifling; grave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The holy Scriptures bring to our ears the most <qex>serious</qex>
things in the world.</q> <rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hence, giving rise to apprehension; attended with
danger; <as>as, a <ex>serious</ex> injury</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Grave; solemn; earnest; sedate; important;
weighty. See <er>Grave</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Se"ri*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Se"ri*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seriph</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"iph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Type Founding)</fld>
<def>See <er>Ceriph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serjeantcy</ent><br/
<ent>Serjeant</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ser"jeant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ser"jeant*cy</hw>, etc.
}</mhw> <def>See <er>Sergeant</er>, <er>Sergeantcy</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Serjeant-at-arms</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Sergeant-at-arms</cref>, under <er>Sergeant</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermocination</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*moc`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sermocinatio</ets>. See <er>Sermon</er>.]</ety> <def>The making of
speeches or sermons; sermonizing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Peacham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermocinator</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*moc"i*na`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who makes sermons or speeches.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermon</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sermoun</ets>, <ets>sermun</ets>, F. <ets>sermon</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>sermo</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, a speaking, discourse, probably fr.
<ets>serer</ets>, <ets>sertum</ets>, to join, connect; hence, a
connected speech. See <er>Series</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
discourse or address; a talk; a writing; <as>as, the <ex>sermons</ex> of
Chaucer</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, a discourse delivered in public,
usually by a clergyman, for the purpose of religious instruction and
grounded on some text or passage of Scripture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This our life exempt from public haunts<br/
Finds tongues in trees, books in the running brooks,<br/
<qex>Sermons</qex> in stones and good in everything.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His preaching much, but more his practice, wrought,<br/
A living <qex>sermon</qex> of the truths he taught.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, a serious address; a lecture on one's conduct
or duty; an exhortation or reproof; a homily; -- often in a depreciatory
sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermon</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>sermoner</ets>, F.
<ets>sermonner</ets> to lecture one.]</ety> <def>To speak; to discourse;
to compose or deliver a sermon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What needeth it to <qex>sermon</qex> of it more?</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermon</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To discourse to or
of, as in a sermon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To tutor; to lecture.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermoneer</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`mon*eer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
sermonizer.</def> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermoner</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A preacher; a
sermonizer.</def> <mark>[Derogative or Jocose.]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermonet</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`mon*et"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A short
sermon.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sermonette</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermonical</ent><br/
<ent>Sermonic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ser*mon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ser*mon"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like, or appropriate to, a
sermon; grave and didactic.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Conversation
. . . satirical or <xex>sermonic</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Prof. Wilson.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Sermonical</xex> style.<rdquo/ <au>V. Knox.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermoning</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
discoursing; discourse; instruction; preaching.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermonish</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon*ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a sermon.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermonist</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sermonizer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermonize</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sermonized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sermonizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To compose or write a sermon or sermons; to preach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inculcate rigid rules.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chesterfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermonize</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon*ize</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To preach or discourse to;
to affect or influence by means of a sermon or of sermons.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which of us shall sing or <qex>sermonize</qex> the other fast
asleep?</q> <rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sermonizer</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"mon*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sermonizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serolin</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"o*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Ser</ets>um +
L. <ets>ole</ets>um oil.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A peculiar fatty substance found in the blood, probably a mixture
of fats, cholesterin, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A body found in fecal
matter and thought to be formed in the intestines from the cholesterin
of the bile; -- called also <altname>stercorin</altname>, and
<altname>stercolin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1315 --></p>

<p><ent>Seroon</ent><br/
<ent>Seron</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se*ron"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Se*roon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp. <ets>seron</ets> a kind of hamper or
pannier, aug. of <ets>sera</ets> a large pannier or basket.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Ceroon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word as expressing a quantity or weight has no
definite signification.</note> <rj><au>McElrath.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serose</ent><br/
<hw>Se"rose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Serous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serosity</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ros"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>serosit<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Serous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or state of being serous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A thin watery animal fluid, as
synovial fluid and pericardial fluid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serotherapy</ent><br/
<hw>Se`ro*ther"a*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Serum-therapy.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The whey
cure.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serotine</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"o*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/rotine</ets>, fr. L. <ets>serotinus</ets> that comes or
happens late.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European long-eared bat
(<spn>Vesperugo serotinus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serotinous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*rot"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serotinus</ets>, fr. <ets>serus</ets> late.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Appearing or blossoming later in the season than
is customary with allied species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serous</ent><br/
<hw>Se"rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/reux</ets>. See <er>Serum</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Thin; watery; like serum;
<as>as, the <ex>serous</ex> fluids</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or
pertaining to serum; <as>as, the <ex>serous</ex> glands, membranes,
layers</as>. See <er>Serum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Serous membrane</b></col>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Membrane</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrow</ent><br/
<ent>Serow</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ser"ow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sur"row</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The thar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpens</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser"pens</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Serpent</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A constellation
represented as a serpent held by Serpentarius.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpent</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>serpens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets> (sc. <ets>bestia</ets>), fr.
<ets>serpens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>serpere</ets> to creep; akin to Gr.
<?/<?/<?/, Skr. <ets>sarp</ets>, and perhaps to L. <ets>repere</ets>, E.
<ets>reptile</ets>. Cf. <er>Herpes</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any reptile of the order Ophidia; a snake,
especially a large snake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The serpents are mostly long and slender, and move
partly by bending the body into undulations or folds and pressing them
against objects, and partly by using the free edges of their ventral
scales to cling to rough surfaces. Many species glide swiftly over the
ground, some burrow in the earth, others live in trees. A few are
entirely aquatic, and swim rapidly. See <er>Ophidia</er>, and
<er>Fang</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: A subtle, treacherous, malicious
person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A species of firework having a serpentine motion as
it passess through the air or along the ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The constellation
Serpens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A bass wind instrument, of a loud
and coarse tone, formerly much used in military bands, and sometimes
introduced into the orchestra; -- so called from its form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pharaoh's serpent</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>mercuric sulphocyanate, a combustible white substance which in
burning gives off a poisonous vapor and leaves a peculiar brown
voluminous residue which is expelled in a serpentine from. It is
employed as a scientific toy.</cd> -- <col><b>Serpent cucumber</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the long, slender, serpentine fruit of the
cucurbitaceous plant <spn>Trichosanthes colubrina</spn>; also, the plant
itself.</cd> -- <col><b>Serpent eage</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of several species of raptorial birds of the genera
<gen>Circaetus</gen> and <gen>Spilornis</gen>, which prey on serpents.
They inhabit Africa, Southern Europe, and India. The European serpent
eagle is <spn>Circaetus Gallicus</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Serpent
eater</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The secretary
bird</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An Asiatic antelope; the markhoor.</cd> --
<col><b>Serpent fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a fish
(<spn>Cepola rubescens</spn>) with a long, thin, compressed body, and a
band of red running lengthwise.</cd> -- <col><b>Serpent star</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an ophiuran; a brittle star.</cd> --
<col><b>Serpent's tongue</b></col> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>, <cd>the fossil
tooth of a shark; -- so called from its resemblance to a tongue with its
root.</cd> -- <col><b>Serpent withe</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
West Indian climbing plant (<spn>Aristolochia odoratissima</spn>).</cd>
-- <col><b>Tree serpent</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any species of
African serpents belonging to the family
<fam>Dendrophidae</fam>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpent</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pent</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Serpented</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Serpenting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To wind like a serpent; to
crook about; to meander.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The
<xex>serpenting</xex> of the Thames.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpent</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pent</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To wind; to encircle.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser`pen*ta"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L. (sc.
<ets>herba</ets>), fr. <ets>serpens</ets> serpent.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The fibrous aromatic root of the Virginia
snakeroot (<spn>Aristolochia Serpentaria</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentarius</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser`pen*ta"ri*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[NL., fr.
L. <ets>serpens</ets> serpent.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A
constellation on the equator, lying between Scorpio and Hercules; --
called also <altname>Ophiuchus</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentiform</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*pen"ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serpens</ets> a serpent + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the
form of a serpent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentigenous</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`pen*tig"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serpens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, a serpent + <ets>-genous</ets>:
cf. L. <ets>serpentigena</ets>.]</ety> <def>Bred of a serpent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentine</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pen*tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serpentinus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>serpentin</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Resembling a serpent; having the shape or qualities of a serpent;
subtle; winding or turning one way and the other, like a moving serpent;
anfractuous; meandering; sinuous; zigzag; <as>as, <ex>serpentine</ex>
braid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy shape<br/
Like his, and color <qex>serpentine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentine</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pen*tine</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. (for sense 1) F.
<ets>serpentine</ets>, (for sense 2) <ets>serpentin</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A mineral or rock consisting chiefly
of the hydrous silicate of magnesia. It is usually of an obscure green
color, often with a spotted or mottled appearance resembling a serpent's
skin. <xex>Precious</xex>, or <xex>noble</xex>, <xex>serpentine</xex> is
translucent and of a rich oil-green color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Serpentine has been largely produced by the alteration
of other minerals, especially of chrysolite.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Ordnance)</fld> <def>A kind of ancient
cannon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentine</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pen*tine</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To serpentize.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Lyttleton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentinely</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pen*tine*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a serpentine
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentinian</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`pen*tin"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>See 2d <er>Ophite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentinize</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pen*tin*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>To convert (a magnesian silicate) into
serpentine.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Ser`pen*tin`i*za"tion</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentinous</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pen*ti`nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to,
or like, serpentine; <as>as, a rock <ex>serpentinous</ex> in
character</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentize</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pent*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To turn or
bend like a serpent, first in one direction and then in the opposite; to
meander; to wind; to serpentine.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The river runs before the door, and <qex>serpentizes</qex> more
than you can conceive.</q> <rj><qau>Walpole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpentry</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pent*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
winding like a serpent's.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place inhabited or infested by serpents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpent-tongued</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pent-tongued`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a forked tongue, like a
serpent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpet</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sirpus</ets>,
<ets>scirpus</ets>, a rush, bulrush.]</ety> <def>A basket.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpette</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser*pette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A pruning knife with a curved blade.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpiginous</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*pig"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>serpigineux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Creeping; -- said
of lesions which heal over one portion while continuing to advance at
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpigo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser*pi"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL., fr. L.
<ets>serpere</ets> to creep.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A dry, scaly
eruption on the skin; especially, a ringworm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpolet</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"po*let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Wild thyme.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser"pu*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Serpulae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Serpulas</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a little snake. See
<er>Serpent</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous
species of tubicolous annelids of the genus <gen>Serpula</gen> and
allied genera of the family <fam>Serpulidae</fam>. They secrete a
calcareous tube, which is usually irregularly contorted, but is
sometimes spirally coiled. The worm has a wreath of plumelike and often
bright-colored gills around its head, and usually an operculum to close
the aperture of its tube when it retracts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpulidan</ent><br/
<ent>Serpulian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ser*pu"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ser*pu"li*dan</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
serpula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serpulite</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"pu*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fossil serpula
shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serr</ent><br/
<hw>Serr</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>serrer</ets>.
See <er>Serry</er>.]</ety> <def>To crowd, press, or drive
together.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serranoid</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*ra"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Serranus</ets>, a typical genus (fr. L. <ets>serra</ets> a saw) +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any fish of the family
<fam>Serranidae</fam>, which includes the striped bass, the black sea
bass, and many other food fishes.</def> -- <def2><pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
<fam>Serranidae</fam>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrated</ent><br/
<ent>Serrate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ser"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ser"ra*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>serratus</ets>, fr. <ets>serra</ets>
a saw; perhaps akin to <ets>secare</ets> to cut, E. <ets>saw</ets> a
cutting instrument. Cf. <er>Sierra</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Notched
on the edge, like a saw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Beset with teeth pointing forwards
or upwards; <as>as, <ex>serrate</ex> leaves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Doubly serrate</b></col>, <cd>having small serratures
upon the large ones, as the leaves of the elm.</cd> --
<col><b>Serrate-ciliate</b></col>, <cd>having fine hairs, like the
eyelashes, on the serratures; -- said of a leaf.</cd> --
<col><b>Serrate-dentate</b></col>, <cd>having the serratures
toothed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serration</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Condition of being serrate; formation in the shape of a
saw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the teeth in a serrate or serrulate
margin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serratirostral</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*ra`ti*ros"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Serrate</ets> + <ets>rostral</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a toothed bill, like that of a toucan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrator</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*ra"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The ivory gull (<spn>Larus
eburneus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrature</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"ra*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serratura</ets> a sawing, fr. <ets>serrare</ets> to saw.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A notching, like that between the teeth of a saw, in
the edge of anything.</def> <rj><au>Martyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the teeth in a serrated edge; a
serration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serricated</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"ri*ca`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sericeous</er>.]</ety> <def>Covered with fine silky down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serricorn</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"ri*corn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serra</ets> saw + <ets>cornu</ets> horn.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having serrated antenn<?/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serricorn</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"ri*corn</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of a
numerous tribe of beetles (<fam>Serricornia</fam>). The joints of the
antennae are prominent, thus producing a serrate appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serried</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"ried</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Serry</er>.]</ety> <def>Crowded; compact; dense; pressed
together.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor seemed it to relax their <qex>serried</qex> files.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrifera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser*rif"e*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
L. <ets>serra</ets> saw + <ets>ferre</ets> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Hymenoptera comprising the
sawflies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrirostres</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser`ri*ros"tres</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
fr. L. <ets>serra</ets> saw + <ets>rostrum</ets> beak.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Lamellirostres</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrous</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>serra</ets>
a saw.]</ety> <def>Like the teeth off a saw; jagged.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrula</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"ru*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a little
saw.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The red-breasted
merganser.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrulated</ent><br/
<ent>Serrulate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ser"rulate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ser"ru*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>serrula</ets> a little
saw, dim. of <ets>serra</ets> a saw.]</ety> <def>Finely serrate; having
very minute teeth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serrulation</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`ru*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
state of being notched minutely, like a fine saw.</def>
<rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the teeth in a serrulate margin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serry</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Serried</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Serrying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>serrer</ets>, LL.
<ets>serrare</ets>, <ets>serare</ets>, from L. <ets>sera</ets> a bar,
bolt; akin to <ets>serere</ets> to join or bind together. See
<er>Serries</er>.]</ety> <def>To crowd; to press together.</def>
<note>[Now perhaps only in the form <xex>serried</xex>, p. p. or
a.]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sertularia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser`tu*la"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., dim.
fr. L. <ets>serta</ets> a garland.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
genus of delicate branching hydroids having small sessile hydrothecae
along the sides of the branches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sertularian</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`tu*la"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of Sertularia, or of <fam>Sertularidae</fam>, a family
of hydroids having branched chitinous stems and simple sessile
hydrothecae. Also used adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serum</ent><br/
<hw>Se"rum</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"r<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., akin
to Gr. <?/<?/<?/, Skr. <ets>s<amac/ra</ets> curd.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The watery portion of certain
animal fluids, as blood, milk, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A thin
watery fluid, containing more or less albumin, secreted by the serous
membranes of the body, such as the pericardium and peritoneum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Blood serum</b></col>, <cd>the pale yellowish fluid which
exudes from the clot formed in the coagulation of the blood; the liquid
portion of the blood, after removal of the blood corpuscles and the
fibrin.</cd> -- <col><b>Muscle serum</b></col>, <cd>the thin watery
fluid which separates from the muscles after coagulation of the muscle
plasma; the watery portion of the plasma. See <cref>Muscle
plasma</cref>, under <er>Plasma</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Serum
albumin</b></col> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an albuminous body,
closely related to egg albumin, present in nearly all serous fluids;
esp., the albumin of blood serum.</cd> -- <col><b>Serum
globulin</b></col> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>paraglobulin.</cd>
-- <col><b>Serum of milk</b></col> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>the
whey, or fluid portion of milk, remaining after removal of the casein
and fat.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serum-therapy</ent><br/
<hw>Se"rum-ther`a*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The treatment of disease by the injection of blood serum from
immune animals.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servable</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Serve</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being
served.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[L. <ets>servabilis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of
being preserved.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servage</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>servage</ets>.]</ety> <def>Serfage; slavery; servitude.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serval</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"val</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>serval</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An African wild cat
(<spn>Felis serval</spn>) of moderate size; -- called also
<altname>serval cat</altname>. It has rather long legs and a tail of
moderate length. Its color is tawny, with black spots on the body and
rings of black on the tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servaline</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"val*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Related to, or resembling, the serval.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servant</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>servant</ets>, <ets>servaunt</ets>, F. <ets>servant</ets>, a & p.
pr. of <ets>servir</ets> to serve, L. <ets>servire</ets>. See
<er>Serve</er>, and cf. <er>Sergeant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who serves, or does services, voluntarily or on compulsion; a person who
is employed by another for menial offices, or for other labor, and is
subject to his command; a person who labors or exerts himself for the
benefit of another, his master or employer; a subordinate helper.</def>
<ldquo/A yearly hired <xex>servant</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Lev. xxv.
53.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men in office have begun to think themselves mere agents and
<qex>servants</qex> of the appointing power, and not agents of the
government or the country.</q> <rj><qau>D. Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In a legal sense, stewards, factors, bailiffs, and other
agents, are <xex>servants</xex> for the time they are employed in such
character, as they act in subordination to others. So any person may be
legally the servant of another, in whose business, and under whose
order, direction, and control, he is acting for the time being.</note>
<rj><au>Chitty.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One in a state of subjection or bondage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou wast a <qex>servant</qex> in the land of Egypt.</q>
<rj><qau>Deut. v. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A professed lover or suitor; a gallant.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In my time a <qex>servant</qex> was I one.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Servant of servants</b></col>, <cd>one debased to the
lowest condition of servitude.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Your humble
servant</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Your obedient
servant</b></col></mcol>, <cd>phrases of civility formerly often used in
closing a letter, now archaic; -- at one time such phrases were
exaggerated to include <altname>Your most humble, most obedient
servant</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our betters tell us they are <qex>our humble servants</qex>, but
understand us to be their slaves.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servant</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ant</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To subject.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servantess</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ant*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A maidservant.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servantry</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ant*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A body of
servants; servants, collectively.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serve</ent><br/
<hw>Serve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Served</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Serving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>serven</ets>,
<ets>servien</ets>, OF. & F. <ets>servir</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>servire</ets>; akin to <ets>servus</ets> a servant or slave,
<ets>servare</ets> to protect, preserve, observe; cf. Zend
<ets>har</ets> to protect, <ets>haurva</ets> protecting. Cf.
<er>Conserve</er>, <er>Desert</er> merit, <er>Dessert</er>,
<er>Observe</er>, <er>Serf</er>, <er>Sergeant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To work for; to labor in behalf of; to exert one's self
continuously or statedly for the benefit of; to do service for; to be in
the employment of, as an inferior, domestic, serf, slave, hired
assistant, official helper, etc.; specifically, in a religious sense, to
obey and worship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God is my witness, whom I <qex>serve</qex> with my spirit.</q>
<rj><qau>Rom. i. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jacob loved Rachel; and said, I will <qex>serve</qex> thee seven
years for Rachel thy younger daughter.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xxix.
18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No man can <qex>serve</qex> two masters.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. vi.
24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Had I but <qex>served</qex> my God with half the zeal<br/
I <qex>served</qex> my king, he would not in mine age<br/
Have left me naked to mine enemies.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be subordinate to; to act a secondary part under;
to appear as the inferior of; to minister to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bodies bright and greater should not <qex>serve</qex><br/
The less not bright.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be suitor to; to profess love to.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>serve</qex> a lady in his beste wise.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To wait upon; to supply the wants of; to attend;
specifically, to wait upon at table; to attend at meals; to supply with
food; <as>as, to <ex>serve</ex> customers in a shop</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Others, pampered in their shameless pride,<br/
Are <qex>served</qex> in plate and in their chariots ride.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Hence, to bring forward, arrange, deal, or
distribute, as a portion of anything, especially of food prepared for
eating; -- often with <xex>up</xex>; formerly with
<xex>in</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bid them cover the table, <qex>serve</qex> in the meat, and we
will come in to dinner.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some part he roasts, then <qex>serves</qex> it up so dressed.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryde.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1316 --></p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To perform the duties belonging to, or required in
or for; hence, to be of use to; <as>as, a curate may <ex>serve</ex> two
churches; to <ex>serve</ex> one's country</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To contribute or conduce to; to promote; to be
sufficient for; to satisfy; <as>as, to <ex>serve</ex> one's
turn</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Turn it into some advantage, by observing where it can
<qex>serve</qex> another end.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To answer or be (in the place of something) to;
<as>as, a sofa <ex>serves</ex> one for a seat and a
couch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To treat; to behave one's self to; to requite; to
act toward; <as>as, he <ex>served</ex> me very ill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To work; to operate; <as>as, to <ex>serve</ex> the
guns</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To bring to notice,
deliver, or execute, either actually or constructively, in such manner
as the law requires; <as>as, to <ex>serve</ex> a summons</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>To make legal service opon (a person named in a writ,
summons, etc.); <as>as, to <ex>serve</ex> a witness with a
subp<oe/na</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>To pass or spend, as time, esp. time of punishment;
<as>as, to <ex>serve</ex> a term in prison</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>To copulate with; to cover; <as>as, a horse
<ex>serves</ex> a mare</as>; -- said of the male.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Tennis)</fld> <def>To lead off in delivering (the
ball).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To wind spun yarn, or the like,
tightly around (a rope or cable, etc.) so as to protect it from chafing
or from the weather. See under <er>Serving</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><mcol><col><b>To serve an attachment</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>To serve a writ of attachment</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>,
<cd>to levy it on the person or goods by seizure, or to seize.</cd> --
<col><b>To serve an execution</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>to levy it
on a lands, goods, or person, by seizure or taking possession.</cd> --
<col><b>To serve an office</b></col>, <cd>to discharge a public
duty.</cd> -- <col><b>To serve a process</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>,
<cd>in general, to read it, so as to give due notice to the party
concerned, or to leave an attested copy with him or his attorney, or his
usual place of abode.</cd> -- <col><b>To serve a warrant</b></col>,
<cd>to read it, and seize the person against whom it is issued.</cd> --
<col><b>To serve a writ</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>to read it to
the defendant, or to leave an attested copy at his usual place of
abode.</cd> -- <col><b>To serve one out</b></col>, <cd>to retaliate
upon; to requite.</cd> <ldquo/I'll <xex>serve you out</xex> for
this.<rdquo/ <au>C. Kingsley.</au> -- <col><b>To serve one
right</b></col>, <cd>to treat, or cause to befall one, according to his
deserts; -- used commonly of ill deserts; <as>as, it <ex>serves</ex> the
scoundrel <ex>right</ex></as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To serve one's self
of</b></col>, <cd>to avail one's self of; to make use of.</cd> <mark>[A
Gallicism]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>serve myself of</qex> this concession.</q>
<rj><qau>Chillingworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <col><b>To serve out</b></col>, <cd>to distribute; <as>as, <ex>to
serve out</ex> rations</as>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To serve the
time</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To serve the hour</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>to regulate one's actions by the requirements of the time instead of
by one's duty; to be a timeserver.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They think herein we <qex>serve the time</qex>, because thereby we
either hold or seek preferment.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj>
<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To obey; minister to; subserve; promote; aid;
help; assist; benefit; succor.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serve</ent><br/
<hw>Serve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be a
servant or a slave; to be employed in labor or other business for
another; to be in subjection or bondage; to render menial
service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord shall give thee rest . . . from the hard bondage wherein
thou wast made to <qex>serve</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Isa. xiv.
3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To perform domestic offices; to be occupied with
household affairs; to prepare and dish up food, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But Martha . . . said, Lord, dost thou not care that my sister
hath left me to <qex>serve</qex> alone?</q> <rj><qau>Luke x.
40.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be in service; to do duty; to discharge the
requirements of an office or employment. Specifically, to act in the
public service, as a soldier, seaman. etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many . . . who had before been great commanders, but now
<qex>served</qex> as private gentlemen without pay.</q>
<rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be of use; to answer a purpose; to suffice; to
suit; to be convenient or favorable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This little brand will <qex>serve</qex> to light your fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As occasion <qex>serves</qex>, this noble queen<br/
And prince shall follow with a fresh supply.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Tennis)</fld> <def>To lead off in delivering the
ball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Server</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
serves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tray for dishes; a salver.</def>
<rj><au>Randolph.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servian</ent><br/
<hw>Ser"vi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Servia, a kingdom of Southern Europe.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A native or inhabitant of Servia.</def></def2> <-- modern spelling,
Serbia --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Service</ent><br/
<ent>Service</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Serv"ice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Serv"ice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw>. <ety>[Properly, the tree which
bears <ets>serve</ets>, OE. <ets>serves</ets>, pl., service berries, AS.
<ets>syrfe</ets> service tree; akin to L. <ets>sorbus</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name given to several trees and shrubs of the
genus <gen>Pyrus</gen>, as <spn>Pyrus domestica</spn> and <spn>Pyrus
torminalis</spn> of Europe, the various species of mountain ash or rowan
tree, and the American shad bush (see <cref>Shad bush</cref>, under
<er>Shad</er>). They have clusters of small, edible, applelike
berries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Service berry</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the fruit
of any kind of service tree. In British America the name is especially
applied to that of the several species or varieties of the shad bush
(<gen>Amelanchier</gen>.)</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Service</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ice</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>servise</ets>, OF.
<ets>servise</ets>, <ets>service</ets>, F. <ets>service</ets>, from L.
<ets>servitium</ets>. See <er>Serve</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of serving; the occupation of a servant; the performance of labor
for the benefit of another, or at another's command; attendance of an
inferior, hired helper, slave, etc., on a superior, employer, master, or
the like; also, spiritual obedience and love.</def> <ldquo/O God . . .
whose <xex>service</xex> is perfect freedom.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bk. of Com.
Prayer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Madam, I entreat true peace of you,<br/
Which I will purchase with my duteous <qex>service</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God requires no man's <qex>service</qex> upon hard and
unreasonable terms.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The deed of one who serves; labor performed for
another; duty done or required; office.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have served him from the hour of my nativity, . . . and have
nothing at his hands for my <qex>service</qex> but blows.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This poem was the last piece of <qex>service</qex> I did for my
master, King Charles.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To go on the forlorn hope is a <qex>service</qex> of peril; who
will understake it if it be not also a <qex>service</qex> of honor?</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Office of devotion; official religious duty
performed; religious rites appropriate to any event or ceremonial;
<as>as, a burial <ex>service</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The outward <qex>service</qex> of ancient religion, the rites,
ceremonies, and ceremonial vestments of the old law.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hence, a musical composition for use in
churches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Duty performed in, or appropriate to, any office or
charge; official function; hence, specifically, military or naval duty;
performance of the duties of a soldier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When he cometh to experience of <qex>service</qex> abroad . . . ne
maketh a worthy soldier.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Useful office; advantage conferred; that which
promotes interest or happiness; benefit; avail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stork's plea, when taken in a net, was the <qex>service</qex>
she did in picking up venomous creatures.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Profession of respect; acknowledgment of duty
owed.</def> <ldquo/Pray, do my <xex>service</xex> to his majesty.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>The act and manner of bringing food to the persons
who eat it; order of dishes at table; also, a set or number of vessels
ordinarily used at table; <as>as, the <ex>service</ex> was tardy and
awkward; a <ex>service</ex> of plate or glass</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was no extraordinary <qex>service</qex> seen on the
board.</q> <rj><qau>Hakewill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The act of bringing to notice,
either actually or constructively, in such manner as is prescribed by
law; <as>as, the <ex>service</ex> of a subp<oe/na or an
attachment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The materials used for serving a
rope, etc., as spun yarn, small lines, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Tennis)</fld> <def>The act of serving the
ball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>Act of serving or covering. See <er>Serve</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, 13.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Service book</b></col>, <cd>a prayer book or missal.</cd>
-- <col><b>Service line</b></col> <fld>(Tennis)</fld>, <cd>a line
parallel to the net, and at a distance of 21 feet from it.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Service of a writ</b></col>, <col><b>process</b></col>,
etc.</mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>personal delivery or communication of
the writ or process, etc., to the party to be affected by it, so as to
subject him to its operation; the reading of it to the person to whom
notice is intended to be given, or the leaving of an attested copy with
the person or his attorney, or at his usual place of abode.</cd> --
<col><b>Service of an attachment</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the
seizing of the person or goods according to the direction.</cd> --
<col><b>Service of an execution</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the
levying of it upon the goods, estate, or person of the defendant.</cd>
-- <col><b>Service pipe</b></col>, <cd>a pipe connecting mains with a
dwelling, as in gas pipes, and the like.</cd> <au>Tomlinson.</au> --
<col><b>To accept service</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Accept</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To see service</b></col>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>to do duty in the presence of the enemy, or in
actual war.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serviceable</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ice*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Doing service; promoting happiness, interest, advantage, or any
good; useful to any end; adapted to any good end use; beneficial;
advantageous.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Serviceable</xex> to religion and
learning<rdquo/. <au>Atterbury.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Serviceable</xex>
tools.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I know thee well, a <qex>serviceable</qex> villain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Prepared for rendering service; capable of, or fit
for, the performance of duty; hence, active; diligent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Courteous he was, lowly, and <qex>servysable</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bright-hearnessed angels sit in order <qex>serviceable</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Seeing her so sweet and <qex>serviceable</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennnyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Serv"ice*a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Serv"ice*a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serviceage</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ice*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Servitude.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Service hat</ent><br/
<ent>Service cap</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Serv"ice cap</hw> <it>or</it> <hw>Serv"ice hat</hw> }</mhw>.
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A cap or hat worn by officers or enlisted men
when full-dress uniform, or dress uniform, is not worn.</def> <note>In
the United States army the <ex>service cap</ex> is round, about
3<frac12/ inches high, flat-topped, with a visor. The <ex>service
hat</ex> is of soft felt of khaki color, with broad brim and high crown,
creased down the middle.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Service elevator</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ice elevator</hw> <def>an elevator for carrying large or havy
items, or for the use of service people.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Service entrance</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ice entrance</hw> <def>an entrance for use of servants and
those providing services, such as deliveries or repair work.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Service station</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ice station</hw> <def>a business where automobiles may be
serviced and repaired, usually also having a gasoline vending apparatus,
in which case it is also called a <altname>gas station</altname> or
<altname>gasoline station</altname>. Facilities for repair of
automobiles but without a gasoline dispensing function are commonly
called <contr>repair shops</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Service uniform</ent><br/
<hw>Service uniform</hw>. <fld>(Mil. & Nav.)</fld> <def>The uniform
prescribed in regulations for active or routine service, in distinction
from dress, full dress, etc. In the United States army it is of
olive-drab woolen or khaki-colored cotton, with all metal attachments of
dull-finish bronze, with the exceptional of insignia of rank, which are
of gold or silver finish.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servient</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"i*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>serviens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. See
<er>Serve</er>.]</ety> <def>Subordinate.</def> <mark>[Obs. except in
law.]</mark> <rj><au>Dyer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Servient tenement</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Servient estate</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>that on
which the burden of a servitude or an easement is imposed. Cf.
<xex>Dominant estate</xex>, under <er>Dominant</er>.</cd> <rj><au>Gale &
Whately.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serviette</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ser`viette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A table napkin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servile</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>servile</ets>, fr. <ets>servus</ets> a servant or slave: cf. F.
<ets>servile</ets>. See <er>Serve</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a servant or slave; befitting a servant or a slave;
proceeding from dependence; hence, meanly submissive; slavish; mean;
cringing; fawning; <as>as, <ex>servile</ex> flattery; <ex>servile</ex>
fear; <ex>servile</ex> obedience.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She must bend the <qex>servile</qex> knee.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fearing dying pays death <qex>servile</qex> breath.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Held in subjection; dependent; enslaved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Even fortune rules no more, O <qex>servile</qex> land!</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Not belonging to the
original root; <as>as, a <ex>servile</ex> letter</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Not itself sounded, but serving to lengthen the
preceeding vowel, as <xex>e</xex> in <xex>tune</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servile</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>An element
which forms no part of the original root; -- opposed to
<contr>radical</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servilely</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ile*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a servile manner;
slavishly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servileness</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ile*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being servile;
servility.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servility</ent><br/
<hw>Ser*vil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>servilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
servile; servileness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be a queen in bondage is more vile<br/
Than is a slave in base <qex>servility</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serving</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Serve</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Serving board</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a flat
piece of wood used in serving ropes.</cd> -- <col><b>Serving
maid</b></col>, <cd>a female servant; a maidservant.</cd> --
<col><b>Serving mallet</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a wooden
instrument shaped like a mallet, used in serving ropes.</cd> --
<col><b>Serving man</b></col>, <cd>a male servant, or attendant; a
manservant.</cd> -- <col><b>Serving stuff</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>small lines for serving ropes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servite</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>servita</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(R.C.Ch.)</fld> <def>One of the order of
the Religious Servants of the Holy Virgin, founded in Florence in
1223.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servitor</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>servire</ets> to serve: cf. F. <ets>serviteur</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who serves; a servant; an attendant; one who acts
under another; a follower or adherent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your trusty and most valiant <qex>servitor</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Univ. of Oxford, Eng.)</fld> <def>An undergraduate,
partly supported by the college funds, whose duty it formerly was to
wait at table. A <xex>servitor</xex> corresponded to a <xex>sizar</xex>
in Cambridge and Dublin universities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servitorship</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"i*tor*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office, rank, or
condition of a servitor.</def> <rj><au>Boswell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servitude</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"i*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>servitudo</ets>: cf. F. <ets>servitude</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The state of voluntary or compulsory subjection to a master; the
condition of being bound to service; the condition of a slave; slavery;
bondage; hence, a state of slavish dependence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You would have sold your king to slaughter,<br/
His princes and his peers to <qex>servitude</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A splendid <qex>servitude</qex>; . . . for he that rises up early,
and goes to bed late, only to receive addresses, is really as much
abridged in his freedom as he that waits to present one.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Servants, collectively.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>After him a cumbrous train<br/
Of herds and flocks, and numerous <qex>servitude</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A right whereby one thing is
subject to another thing or person for use or convenience, contrary to
the common right.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The object of a <xex>servitude</xex> is either to suffer
something to be done by another, or to omit to do something, with
respect to a thing. The <xex>easements</xex> of the English correspond
in some respects with the <xex>servitudes</xex> of the Roman law. Both
terms are used by common law writers, and often indiscriminately. The
former, however, rather indicates the right enjoyed, and the latter the
burden imposed.</note> <rj><au>Ayliffe. Erskine. E.
Washburn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Penal servitude</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Penal</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Personal servitude</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>that which arises when the use of a thing is
granted as a real right to a particular individual other than the
proprietor.</cd> -- <col><b>Predial servitude</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>that which one estate owes to another estate. When
it related to lands, vineyards, gardens, or the like, it is called
<xex>rural</xex>; when it related to houses and buildings, it is called
<xex>urban</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serviture</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Servants,
collectively.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servitute</ent><br/
<hw>Serv"i*tute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>servitus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Servitude.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servocontrol</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`vo*con*trol"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>control by a <er>servomechanism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a <er>servomechanism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servomechanism</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`vo*mech"an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>an
electronically controlled mechanical or hydraulic device permitting a
large action or strong forces to be controlled by a small electrical
signal.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Servo-motor</ent><br/
<hw>Ser`vo-mo"tor</hw> <pr>(s<etil/r`v<osl/*m<omac/"t<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sometimes erroneously spelt
<ets>serro-motor</ets>.]</ety> <ety>[F. <ets>servo-moteur</ets>. See
<er>Serf</er>; <er>Motor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>
<def>A relay apparatus;</def> <specif>specif.:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>An auxiliary motor, regulated by a hand lever, for quickly and
easily moving the reversing gear of a large marine engine into any
desired position indicated by that of the hand lever, which controls the
valve of the motor.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In a Whitehead torpedo, a
compressed-air motor, for moving the rudders so as to correct deviations
from the course.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a motor which forms part of a
<er>servomechanism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Serye</ent><br/
<hw>Se"rye</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A series.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesame</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"a*me</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sesamum</ets>, <ets>sesama</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/: cf.
F. <ets>s<eacute/same</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Either of two
annual herbaceous plants of the genus <gen>Sesamum</gen> (<spn>Sesamum
Indicum</spn>, and <spn>Sesamum orientale</spn>), from the seeds of
which an oil is expressed; also, the small obovate, flattish seeds of
these plants, sometimes used as food. See <er>Benne</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Open Sesame</b></col>, <cd>the magical command which
opened the door of the robber's den in the Arabian Nights' tale of
<ldquo/<i>The Forty Thieves</i>;<rdquo/ hence, a magical password.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sesame grass</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Gama grass</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesamoid</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"a*moid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/
like sesame; <?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ sesame + <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/samo<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Resembling in
shape the seeds of sesame.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the sesamoid
bones or cartilages; sesamoidal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sesamoid bones</b></col>, <col><b>Sesamoid
cartilages</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>small bones or
cartilages formed in tendons, like the patella and pisiform in
man.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesamoid</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"a*moid</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A sesamoid
bone or cartilage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesamoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`a*moid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Sesamoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesban</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"ban</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. Ar.
<ets>saisab<amac/n</ets>, <ets>seiseb<amac/n</ets>, a kind of tree, fr.
Per. <ets>s<imac/sab<amac/n</ets> seed of cinquefoil.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A leguminous shrub (<spn>Sesbania aculeata</spn>)
which furnishes a fiber used for making ropes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is applied also to the similar plant,
<spn>Sesbania Aegyptiaca</spn>, and other species of the same
genus.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesqui-</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ses`qui-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L., one half more, one and a
half.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A combining form (also used
adjectively) denoting that <xex>three atoms or equivalents</xex> of the
substance to the name of which it is prefixed are combined with
<xex>two</xex> of some other element or radical; <as>as,
<ex>sesqui</ex>bromide, <ex>sesqui</ex>carbonate,
<ex>sesqui</ex>chloride, <ex>sesqui</ex>oxide</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sesquidupli-</xex> is sometimes, but rarely, used
in the same manner to denote the proportions of <xex>two and a
half</xex> to <xex>one</xex>, or rather of <xex>five</xex> to
<xex>two</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquialter</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`qui*al"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sesquialteral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquialtera</ent><br/
<ent>Sesquialter</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ses`qui*al"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Ses`qui*al"ter*a</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>sesquialtera</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A stop on the
organ, containing several ranks of pipes which reenforce some of the
high harmonics of the ground tone, and make the sound more
brilliant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquialterate</ent><br/
<ent>Sesquialteral</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ses`qui*al"ter*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ses`qui*al"ter*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sesquialter</ets> once and a half; <ets>sesqui-</ets> +
<ets>alter</ets> other: cf. F. <ets>sesquialt<eacute/re</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Once and a half times as great as another; having the ratio of one
and a half to one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sesquialteral ratio</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>the
ratio of one and a half to one; <as>thus, 9 and 6 are in a
<ex>sesquialteral ratio</ex></as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquialterous</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`qui*al"ter*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sesquialteral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquibasic</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`qui*ba"sic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + <ets>basic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Containing, or acting as, a base in the proportions of a sesqui
compound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquiduplicate</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`qui*du"pli*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + <ets>duplicate</ets>.]</ety> <def>Twice and a
half as great (as another thing); having the ratio of two and a half to
one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sesquiduplicate ratio</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>,
<cd>the ratio of two and a half to one, or one in which the greater term
contains the lesser twice and a half, as that of 50 to 20.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquioxide</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`qui*ox"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + <ets>oxide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An oxide containing three atoms of oxygen with two atoms (or
radicals) of some other substance; <as>thus, alumina,
<chform>Al2O3</chform> is a <ex>sesquioxide</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquipedalian</ent><br/
<ent>Sesquipedal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ses*quip"e*dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ses`qui*pe*da"li*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + <ets>pedal</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sesquip<eacute/dal</ets>, L. <ets>sesquipedalis</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Measuring or containing a foot and a half; <as>as, a
<ex>sesquipedalian</ex> pygmy</as>; -- sometimes humorously applied to
long words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. having a tendency to use long words. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquipedalism</ent><br/
<ent>Sesquipedalianism</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ses`qui*pe*da"li*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ses*quip"e*dal*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sesquipedality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquipedality</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`qui*pe*dal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or condition of being sesquipedal.</def>
<rj><au>Sterne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The use of sesquipedalian words; style characterized
by the use of long words; sesquipedalism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquiplicate</ent><br/
<hw>Ses*quip"li*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + <ets>plicate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>Subduplicate of the triplicate; -- a term applied to ratios; thus,
<i>a</i> and <i>a</i><prime/ are in the <xex>sesquiplicate</xex> ratio
of <i>b</i> and <i>b</i><prime/, when <i>a</i> is to <i>a</i><prime/ as
the square root of the cube of <i>b</i> is to the square root of the
cube of <i>b</i><prime/, or
<mathex>a:a<prime/::<root/b<exp>3</exp>:<root/b<prime/<exp>3</exp></mathex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The periodic times of the planets are in the
<qex>sesquiplicate</qex> ratio of their mean distances.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
I. Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquisalt</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"qui*salt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + <ets>salt</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt derived from a sesquioxide base, or made up on the
proportions of a sesqui compound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1317 --></p>

<p><ent>Sesquisulphide</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`qui*sul"phide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + <ets>sulphide</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A sulphide, analogous to a sesquioxide,
containing three atoms of sulphur to two of the other ingredient; --
formerly called also <altname>sesquisulphuret</altname>; <as>as,
orpiment, <chform>As2S3</chform> is arsenic
<ex>sesquisulphide</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquitertial</ent><br/
<hw>Ses`qui*ter"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sesquitertian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquitertianal</ent><br/
<ent>Sesquitertian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ses`qui*ter"tian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ses`qui*ter"tian*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + L. <ets>tertianus</ets> belonging to the
third. Cf. <er>Tertian</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Having the
ratio of one and one third to one (as 4 : 3).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesquitone</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"qui*tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sesqui-</ets> + <ets>tone</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>A minor third, or interval of three semitones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sess</ent><br/
<hw>Sess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Aphetic form of
<ets>assess</ets>. See <er>Assess</er>, <er>Cess</er>.]</ety> <def>To
lay a tax upon; to assess.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sess</ent><br/
<hw>Sess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A tax; an assessment. See
<er>Cess</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sessa</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"sa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>Hurry; run.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sessile</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"sile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sessilis</ets> low, dwarf, from <ets>sedere</ets>,
<ets>sessum</ets>, to sit: cf. F. <ets>sessile</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Attached without any sensible projecting support.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Resting directly upon the main
stem or branch, without a petiole or footstalk; <as>as, a
<ex>sessile</ex> leaf or blossom</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Permanently attached; -- said of
the gonophores of certain hydroids which never became
detached.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sessile-eyed</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"sile-eyed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having eyes which are not elevated on a stalk; -- opposed to
<contr>stalk-eyed</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sessile-eyed Crustacea</b></col>, <cd>the
Arthrostraca.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Session</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sessio</ets>, fr. <ets>sedere</ets>, <ets>sessum</ets>, to sit: cf.
F. <ets>session</ets>. See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of sitting, or the state of being seated.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So much his ascension into heaven and his <qex>session</qex> at
the right hand of God do import.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But Viven, gathering somewhat of his mood, . . . <br/
Leaped from her <qex>session</qex> on his lap, and stood.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The actual sitting of a court, council, legislature,
etc., or the actual assembly of the members of such a body, for the
transaction of business.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It's fit this royal <qex>session</qex> do proceed.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, also, the time, period, or term during which
a court, council, legislature, etc., meets daily for business; or, the
space of time between the first meeting and the prorogation or
adjournment; thus, a <xex>session</xex> of Parliaments is opened with a
speech from the throne, and closed by prorogation. The
<xex>session</xex> of a judicial court is called a
<xex>term</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was resolved that the convocation should meet at the beginning
of the next <qex>session</qex> of Parliament.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sessions</xex>, in some of the States, is
particularly used as a title for a court of justices, held for granting
licenses to innkeepers, etc., and for laying out highways, and the like;
it is also the title of several courts of criminal jurisdiction in
England and the United States.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Church session</b></col>, <cd>the lowest court in the
Presbyterian Church, composed of the pastor and a body of elders elected
by the members of a particular church, and having the care of matters
pertaining to the religious interests of that church, as the admission
and dismission of members, discipline, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Court of
Session</b></col>, <cd>the supreme civil court of Scotland.</cd> --
<col><b>Quarter sessions</b></col>. <fld>(Eng.Law)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Quarter</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sessions of the peace</b></col>,
<cd>sittings held by justices of the peace.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sessional</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"sion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a session or sessions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesspool</ent><br/
<hw>Sess"pool`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>suss</ets> hogwash, <ets>soss</ets> a dirty mess, a puddle + E.
<ets>pool</ets> a puddle; cf. Gael. <ets>ses</ets> a coarse mess.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Cesspool</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sesterce</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"terce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sestertius</ets> (sc. <ets>nummus</ets>), fr. <ets>sestertius</ets>
two and a half; <ets>semis</ets> half + <ets>tertius</ets> third: cf. F.
<ets>sesterce</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A Roman coin
or denomination of money, in value the fourth part of a denarius, and
originally containing two asses and a half, afterward four asses, --
equal to about two pence sterling, or four cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The sestertium was equivalent to one thousand sesterces,
equal to <pound/8 17s 1d. sterling, or about $43 (in 1900), before the
reign of Augustus. After his reign its value was about <pound/7 16s. 3d.
sterling. The sesterce was originally coined only in silver, but later
both in silver and brass.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sestet</ent><br/
<hw>Ses*tet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>sestetto</ets>, fr. <ets>sesto</ets> sixth, L. <ets>sextus</ets>,
fr. <ets>sex</ets> six.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A
piece of music composed for six voices or six instruments; a sextet; --
called also <altname>sestuor</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sestett</asp>, <asp>sestette</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Poet.)</fld> <def>The last six lines of a
sonnet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sestetto</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ses*tet"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A sestet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sestine</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sextain</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sestuor</ent><br/
<hw>Ses"tu*or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
sestet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set</ent><br/
<hw>Set</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Set</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Setting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>setten</ets>, AS.
<ets>setton</ets>; akin to OS. <ets>settian</ets>, OFries.
<ets>setta</ets>, D. <ets>zetten</ets>, OHG. <ets>sezzen</ets>, G.
<ets>setzen</ets>, Icel. <ets>setja</ets>, Sw. <ets>s<aum/tta</ets>,
Dan. <ets>s<?/tte</ets>, Goth. <ets>satjan</ets>; causative from the
root of E. <ets>sit</ets>. <root/154. See <er>Sit</er>, and cf.
<er>Seize</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to sit; to make to
assume a specified position or attitude; to give site or place to; to
place; to put; to fix; <as>as, to <ex>set</ex> a house on a stone
foundation; to <ex>set</ex> a book on a shelf; to <ex>set</ex> a dish on
a table; to <ex>set</ex> a chest or trunk on its bottom or on
end</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do <qex>set</qex> my bow in the cloud.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. ix.
13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to attach or affix (something) to something
else, or in or upon a certain place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Set</qex> your affection on things above.</q> <rj><qau>Col.
iii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord <qex>set</qex> a mark upon Cain.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. iv.
15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make to assume specified place, condition, or
occupation; to put in a certain condition or state (described by the
accompanying words); to cause to be.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord thy God will <qex>set</qex> thee on high.</q>
<rj><qau>Deut. xxviii. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am come to <qex>set</qex> a man at variance against his father,
and the daughter against her mother.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. x.
35.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every incident <qex>sets</qex> him thinking.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To fix firmly; to make fast, permanent, or stable;
to render motionless; to give an unchanging place, form, or condition
to.</def> <specif>Specifically:</specif> --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>To cause to stop or stick; to obstruct; to fasten
to a spot; hence, to occasion difficulty to; to embarrass; <as>as, to
<ex>set</ex> a coach in the mud</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They show how hard they are <qex>set</qex> in this particular.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To fix beforehand; to determine; hence, to make
unyielding or obstinate; to render stiff, unpliant, or rigid; <as>as, to
<ex>set</ex> one's countenance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His eyes were <qex>set</qex> by reason of his age.</q> <rj><qau>1
Kings xiv. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On these three objects his heart was <qex>set</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Make my heart as a millstone, <qex>set</qex> my face as a
flint.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To fix in the ground, as a post or a tree; to
plant; <as>as, to <ex>set</ex> pear trees in an orchard</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>To fix, as a precious stone, in a border of metal;
to place in a setting; hence, to place in or amid something which serves
as a setting; <as>as, to <ex>set</ex> glass in a sash</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And him too rich a jewel to be <qex>set</qex><br/
In vulgar metal for a vulgar use.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <def>To render stiff or solid; especially, to convert
into curd; to curdle; <as>as, to <ex>set</ex> milk for
cheese</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To put into a desired position or condition; to
adjust; to regulate; to adapt.</def> Specifically: -- <-- to set a table
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>To put in order in a particular manner; to prepare;
<as>as, to <ex>set</ex> (that is, to hone) a razor; to <ex>set</ex> a
saw</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tables for to <qex>sette</qex>, and beddes make.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To extend and bring into position; to spread;
<as>as, to <ex>set</ex> the sails of a ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To give a pitch to, as a tune; to start by fixing
the keynote; <as>as, to <ex>set</ex> a psalm</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Fielding.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>To reduce from a dislocated or fractured state; to
replace; <as>as, to <ex>set</ex> a broken bone</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <def>To make to agree with some standard; <as>as, to
<ex>set</ex> a watch or a clock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(f)</sd> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>To lower into place and fix
solidly, as the blocks of cut stone in a structure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To stake at play; to wager; to risk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have <qex>set</qex> my life upon a cast,<br/
And I will stand the hazard of the die.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To fit with music; to adapt, as words to notes; to
prepare for singing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Set</qex> thy own songs, and sing them to thy lute.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To determine; to appoint; to assign; to fix; <as>as,
to <ex>set</ex> a time for a meeting; to <ex>set</ex> a price on a
horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To adorn with something infixed or affixed; to stud;
to variegate with objects placed here and there.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>High on their heads, with jewels richly <qex>set</qex>,<br/
Each lady wore a radiant coronet.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pastoral dales thin <qex>set</qex> with modern farms.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To value; to rate; -- with <xex>at</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be you contented, wearing now the garland,<br/
To have a son <qex>set</qex> your decrees at naught.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do not <qex>set</qex> my life at a pin's fee.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To point out the seat or position of, as birds, or
other game; -- said of hunting dogs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>To establish as a rule; to furnish; to prescribe;
to assign; <as>as, to <ex>set</ex> an example; to <ex>set</ex> lessons
to be learned</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>To suit; to become; <as>as, it <ex>sets</ex> him
ill</as>.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>To compose; to arrange in
words, lines, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>set</ex> type; to <ex>set</ex> a
page.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To set abroach</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Abroach</er>.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>To set against</b></col>,
<cd>to oppose; to set in comparison with, or to oppose to, as an
equivalent in exchange; <as>as, <ex>to set</ex> one thing
<ex>against</ex> another</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To set agoing</b></col>,
<cd>to cause to move.</cd> -- <col><b>To set apart</b></col>, <cd>to
separate to a particular use; to separate from the rest; to
reserve.</cd> -- <col><b>To set a saw</b></col>, <cd>to bend each tooth
a little, every alternate one being bent to one side, and the
intermediate ones to the other side, so that the opening made by the saw
may be a little wider than the thickness of the back, to prevent the saw
from sticking.</cd> -- <col><b>To set aside</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To leave out of account; to pass by; to omit; to neglect; to reject;
to annul.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Setting aside</qex> all other considerations, I will endeavor
to know the truth, and yield to that.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To set apart; to reserve; <as>as, <ex>to set
aside</ex> part of one's income</as>.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>Aside</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To set at
defiance</b></col>, <cd>to defy.</cd> -- <col><b>To set at
ease</b></col>, <cd>to quiet; to tranquilize; <as>as, <ex>to set</ex>
the heart <ex>at ease</ex></as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To set at
naught</b></col>, <cd>to undervalue; to contemn; to despise.</cd>
<ldquo/Ye have <xex>set at naught</xex> all my counsel.<rdquo/ <au>Prov.
i. 25.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>To set a trap</b></col> <col><b>To set a
snare</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To set a gin</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>to put it in a proper condition or position to catch prey; hence, to
lay a plan to deceive and draw another into one's power.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>To set at work</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To set to
work</b></col></mcol>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To cause to enter on work or
action, or to direct how tu enter on work.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
apply one's self; -- used reflexively.</cd> -- <col><b>To set
before</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To bring out to view before; to
exhibit.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To propose for choice to; to offer
to.</cd> -- <col><b>To set by</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To set apart
or on one side; to reject.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To attach the value of
(anything) to.</cd> <ldquo/I <xex>set</xex> not a straw <xex>by</xex>
thy dreamings.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>To set by the
compass</b></col>, <cd>to observe and note the bearing or situation of
by the compass.</cd> -- <col><b>To set case</b></col>, <cd>to suppose;
to assume. Cf. <cref>Put case</cref>, under <er>Put</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos></cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>To set
down</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To enter in writing; to
register.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some rules were to be <qex>set down</qex> for the government of
the army.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To fix; to establish; to ordain.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This law we may name eternal, being that order which God . . .
hath <qex>set down</qex> with himself, for himself to do all things
by.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To humiliate.</cd> -- <col><b>To set eyes
on</b></col>, <cd>to see; to behold; to fasten the eyes on.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>To set fire to</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To set on
fire</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to communicate fire to; fig., to inflame; to
enkindle the passions of; to irritate.</cd> -- <col><b>To set
flying</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to hook to halyards, sheets,
etc., instead of extending with rings or the like on a stay; -- said of
a sail.</cd> -- <col><b>To set forth</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To
manifest; to offer or present to view; to exhibt; to display.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To publish; to promulgate; to make appear</cd>.
<au>Waller.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To send out; to prepare and send.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Venetian admiral had a fleet of sixty galleys, <qex>set
forth</qex> by the Venetians.</q> <rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>To set forward</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To cause to
advance.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To promote.</cd> -- <col><b>To set
free</b></col>, <cd>to release from confinement, imprisonment, or
bondage; to liberate; to emancipate.</cd> -- <col><b>To set
in</b></col>, <cd>to put in the way; to begin; to give a start to.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If you please to assist and <qex>set</qex> me <qex>in</qex>, I
will recollect myself.</q> <rj><qau>Collier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>To set in order</b></col>, <cd>to adjust or arrange; to
reduce to method.</cd> <ldquo/The rest will I <xex>set in order</xex>
when I come.<rdquo/ <au>1 Cor. xi. 34.</au> -- <col><b>To set
milk</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To expose it in open dishes in order
that the cream may rise to the surface.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To cause
it to become curdled as by the action of rennet. See 4
<sd>(e)</sd>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To set much by</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>To set little by</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to care much, or little,
for.</cd> -- <col><b>To set of</b></col>, <cd>to value; to set by.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/I <xex>set</xex> not an haw <xex>of</xex> his
proverbs.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>To set off</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To separate from a whole; to assign to a particular
purpose; to portion off; <as>as, <ex>to set off</ex> a portion of an
estate</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To adorn; to decorate; to
embellish</cd>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They . . . <qex>set off</qex> the worst faces with the best
airs.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To give a flattering description of.</cd> --
<col><b>To set off against</b></col>, <cd>to place against as an
equivalent; <as>as, <ex>to set off</ex> one man's services
<ex>against</ex> another's</as>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To set
on</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To set upon</b></col></mcol>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To incite; to instigate.</cd> <ldquo/Thou, traitor,
hast <xex>set on</xex> thy wife to this.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To employ, as in a task.</cd> <ldquo/ <xex>Set on</xex>
thy wife to observe.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To fix upon;
to attach strongly to; as, <xex>to set</xex> one's heart or affections
<xex>on</xex> some object. See definition 2, above.</cd> -- <col><b>To
set one's cap for</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Cap</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>To set one's self against</b></col>,
<cd>to place one's self in a state of enmity or opposition to.</cd> --
<col><b>To set one's teeth</b></col>, <cd>to press them together
tightly.</cd> -- <col><b>To set on foot</b></col>, <cd>to set going; to
put in motion; to start.</cd> -- <col><b>To set out</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To assign; to allot; to mark off; to limit; <as>as,
<ex>to set out</ex> the share of each proprietor or heir of an estate;
<ex>to set out</ex> the widow's thirds</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
publish, as a proclamation</cd>. <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To
adorn; to embellish.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An ugly woman, in rich habit <qex>set out</qex> with jewels,
nothing can become.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To raise, equip, and send forth; to furnish.</cd>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Venetians pretend they could <qex>set out</qex>, in case of
great necessity, thirty men-of-war.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <cd>To show; to display; to recommend; to set
off.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I could <qex>set out</qex> that best side of Luther.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(f)</sd> <cd>To show; to prove.</cd> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/Those very reasons <xex>set out</xex> how heinous his sin
was.<rdquo/ <au>Atterbury.</au> <sd>(g)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>To
recite; to state at large.</cd><-- see under set, v.i.: to begin to act
"he set out to prove" --> -- <col><b>To set over</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To appoint or constitute as supervisor, inspector, ruler, or
commander.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To assign; to transfer; to convey.</cd>
-- <col><b>To set right</b></col>, <cd>to correct; to put in order.</cd>
-- <col><b>To set sail</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Sail</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>To set store by</b></col>,
<cd>to consider valuable.</cd> -- <col><b>To set the fashion</b></col>,
<cd>to determine what shall be the fashion; to establish the mode.</cd>
-- <col><b>To set the teeth on edge</b></col>, <cd>to affect the teeth
with a disagreeable sensation, as when acids are brought in contact with
them.</cd> -- <col><b>To set the watch</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>to place the starboard or port watch on duty.</cd> -- <col><b>To set
to</b></col>, <cd>to attach to; to affix to.</cd> <ldquo/He . . . hath
<xex>set to</xex> his seal that God is true.<rdquo/ <au>John iii.
33.</au> -- <col><b>To set up</b></col>. <fld>(a)</fld> <cd>To erect; to
raise; to elevate; as, <xex>to set up</xex> a building, or a machine;
<xex>to set up</xex> a post, a wall, a pillar.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>Hence, to exalt; to put in power</cd>. <ldquo/I will . . . <xex>set
up</xex> the throne of David over Israel.<rdquo/ <au>2 Sam. iii.
10.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To begin, as a new institution; to institute;
to establish; to found; <as>as, <ex>to set up</ex> a manufactory; to
<ex>set up</ex> a school</as>.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To enable to
commence a new business; <as>as, <ex>to set up</ex> a son in
trade</as></cd>. <sd>(e)</sd> <cd>To place in view; <as>as, <ex>to set
up</ex> a mark</as></cd>. <sd>(f)</sd> <cd>To raise; to utter loudly;
<as>as, <ex>to set up</ex> the voice</as></cd>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll <qex>set up</qex> such a note as she shall hear.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(g)</sd> <cd>To advance; to propose as truth or for reception;
<as>as, <ex>to set up</ex> a new opinion or doctrine</as>.</cd> <au>T.
Burnet.</au> <sd>(h)</sd> <cd>To raise from depression, or to a
sufficient fortune; <as>as, this good fortune quite <ex>set</ex> him
<ex>up</ex></as>.</cd> <sd>(i)</sd> <cd>To intoxicate</cd>.
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <sd>(j)</sd> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <cd>To put in
type; <as>as, <ex>to set up</ex> copy</as>; to arrange in words, lines,
etc., ready for printing; <as>as, <ex>to set up</ex> type</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>To set up the rigging</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to make
it taut by means of tackles.</cd> <au>R. H. Dana, Jr.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Put</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set</ent><br/
<hw>Set</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
pass below the horizon; to go down; to decline; to sink out of sight; to
come to an end.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ere the weary sun <qex>set</qex> in the west.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus this century <qex>sets</qex> with little mirth, and the next
is likely to arise with more mourning.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fit music to words.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To place plants or shoots in the ground; to
plant.</def> <ldquo/To sow dry, and <xex>set</xex> wet.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Old Proverb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be fixed for growth; to strike root; to begin to
germinate or form; <as>as, cuttings <ex>set</ex> well; the fruit has
<ex>set</ex> well (<it>i. e.</it>, not blasted in the
blossom)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To become fixed or rigid; to be fastened.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A gathering and serring of the spirits together to resist, maketh
the teeth to <qex>set</qex> hard one against another.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To congeal; to concrete; to solidify; -- of cements,
glues, gels, concrete, substances polymerizing into plastics,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>That fluid substance in a few minutes begins to
<qex>set</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To have a certain direction in motion; to flow; to
move on; to tend; <as>as, the current <ex>sets</ex> to the north; the
tide <ex>sets</ex> to the windward</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To begin to move; to go out or forth; to start; --
now followed by <xex>out</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king is <qex>set</qex> from London.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To indicate the position of game; -- said of a dog;
<as>as, the dog <ex>sets</ex> well</as>; also, to hunt game by the aid
of a setter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To apply one's self; to undertake earnestly; -- now
followed by <xex>out</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If he <qex>sets</qex> industriously and sincerely to perform the
commands of Christ, he can have no ground of doubting but it shall prove
successful to him.</q> <rj><qau>Hammond.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To fit or suit one; to sit; <as>as, the coat
<ex>sets</ex> well</as>.</def> <note>[Colloquially used, but improperly,
for <ex>sit</ex>.]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The use of the verb <xex>set</xex> for <xex>sit</xex> in
such expressions as, the hen is <xex>setting</xex> on thirteen eggs; a
<xex>setting</xex> hen, etc., although colloquially common, and
sometimes tolerated in serious writing, is not to be
approved.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To set about</b></col>, <cd>to commence; to begin.</cd>
-- <col><b>To set forward</b></col>, <cd>to move or march; to begin to
march; to advance.</cd> -- <col><b>To set forth</b></col>, <cd>to begin
a journey.</cd> -- <col><b>To set in</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To
begin; to enter upon a particular state; <as>as, winter <ex>set in</ex>
early</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To settle one's self; to become
established</cd>. <ldquo/When the weather was <xex>set in</xex> to be
very bad.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To flow toward the
shore; -- said of the tide.</cd> -- <col><b>To set off</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To enter upon a journey; to start.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Typog.)</fld> <cd>To deface or soil the next sheet; -- said of the
ink on a freshly printed sheet, when another sheet comes in contact with
it before it has had time to dry.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To set
on</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To set upon</b></col></mcol>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To begin, as a journey or enterprise; to set
about.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that would seriously <qex>set upon</qex> the search of
truth.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To assault; to make an attack.</cd>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cassio hath here been <qex>set on</qex> in the dark.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>To set out</b></col>, <cd>to begin a journey or course;
<as>as, <ex>to set out</ex> for London, or from London; <ex>to set
out</ex> in business;<ex>to set out</ex> in life or the world</as>.</cd>
-- <col><b>To set to</b></col>, <cd>to apply one's self to.</cd> --
<col><b>To set up</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To begin business or a
scheme of life; <as>as, <ex>to set up</ex> in trade; <ex>to set up</ex>
for one's self</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To profess openly; to make
pretensions</cd>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those men who <qex>set up</qex> for mortality without regard to
religion, are generally but virtuous in part.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><-- p. 1318 --></p>

<p><ent>Set</ent><br/
<hw>Set</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Fixed in
position; immovable; rigid; <as>as, a <ex>set</ex> line; a <ex>set</ex>
countenance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Firm; unchanging; obstinate; <as>as, <ex>set</ex>
opinions or prejudices</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Regular; uniform; formal; <as>as, a <ex>set</ex>
discourse; a <ex>set</ex> battle</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>set</xex>
phrase of peace.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Established; prescribed; <as>as, <ex>set</ex> forms
of prayer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Adjusted; arranged; formed; adapted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Set hammer</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A hammer the head
of which is not tightly fastened upon the handle, but may be
reversed</cd>. <au>Knight.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A hammer with a concave
face which forms a die for shaping anything, as the end of a bolt,
rivet, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Set line</b></col>, <cd>a line to which a
number of baited hooks are attached, and which, supported by floats and
properly secured, may be left unguarded during the absence of the
fisherman.</cd> -- <col><b>Set nut</b></col>, <cd>a jam nut or lock nut.
See under <er>Nut</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Set screw</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a screw, sometimes cupped or printed at one end,
and screwed through one part, as of a machine, tightly upon another
part, to prevent the one from slipping upon the other.</cd> --
<col><b>Set speech</b></col>, <cd>a speech carefully prepared before it
is delivered in public; a formal or methodical speech.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set</ent><br/
<hw>Set</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of setting, as of
the sun or other heavenly body; descent; hence, the close;
termination.</def> <ldquo/Locking at the <xex>set</xex> of day.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The weary sun hath made a golden <qex>set</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is set, placed, or fixed.</def>
Specifically: -- <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A young plant for growth; <as>as, a
<ex>set</ex> of white thorn</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>That which is
staked; a wager; a venture; a stake; hence, a game at venture.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We will in France, by God's grace, play a <qex>set</qex><br/
Shall strike his father's crown into the hazard.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That was but civil war, an equal <qex>set</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>Permanent change of figure in
consequence of excessive strain, as from compression, tension, bending,
twisting, etc.; <as>as, the <ex>set</ex> of a spring</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>A kind of punch used for bending, indenting, or
giving shape to, metal; <as>as, a saw <ex>set</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <fld>(Pile Driving)</fld> <def>A piece placed
temporarily upon the head of a pile when the latter cannot be reached by
the weight, or hammer, except by means of such an intervening
piece.</def> <altsp>[Often incorrectly written
<asp>sett</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(f)</sd> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>A short steel spike used for
driving the head of a nail below the surface. Called also <altname>nail
set</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Perhaps due to confusion with <ets>sect</ets>,
<ets>sept</ets>.]</ety> <def>A number of things of the same kind,
ordinarily used or classed together; a collection of articles which
naturally complement each other, and usually go together; an assortment;
a suit; <as>as, a <ex>set</ex> of chairs, of china, of surgical or
mathematical instruments, of books, etc.</as></def> <altsp>[In this
sense, sometimes incorrectly written <asp>sett</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A number of persons associated by custom, office,
common opinion, quality, or the like; a division; a group; a
clique.</def> <ldquo/Others of our <xex>set</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This falls into different divisions, or <qex>sets</qex>, of
nations connected under particular religions.</q> <rj><qau>R. P.
Ward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Direction or course; <as>as, the <ex>set</ex> of the
wind, or of a current</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>In dancing, the number of persons necessary to
execute a quadrille; also, the series of figures or movements
executed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The deflection of a tooth, or of the teeth, of a
saw, which causes the the saw to cut a kerf, or make an opening, wider
than the blade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A young oyster when first
attached.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Collectively, the crop of young
oysters in any locality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Tennis)</fld> <def>A series of as many games as may
be necessary to enable one side to win six. If at the end of the tenth
game the score is a tie, the set is usually called a <xex>deuce
set</xex>, and decided by an application of the rules for playing off
deuce in a game. See <er>Deuce</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Type Founding)</fld> <def>That dimension of the
body of a type called by printers the
<altname>width</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Textiles)</fld> <def>Any of various standards of
measurement of the fineness of cloth; specif., the number of reeds in
one inch and the number of threads in each reed. The exact meaning
varies according to the location where it is used. Sometimes written
<asp>sett</asp>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>A stone, commonly of granite, shaped like a short
brick and usually somewhat larger than one, used for street paving.
Commonly written <asp>sett</asp>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>Camber of a curved roofing tile.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>The manner, state, or quality of setting or
fitting; fit; <as>as, the <ex>set</ex> of a coat</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <def>Any collection or group of objects considered
together.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dead set</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The act of a setter
dog when it discovers the game, and remains intently fixed in pointing
it out</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A fixed or stationary condition arising
from obstacle or hindrance; a deadlock; as, to be at a <xex>dead
set</xex>.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>A concerted scheme to defraud by
gaming; a determined onset.</cd> -- <col><b>To make a dead
set</b></col>, <cd>to make a determined onset, literally or
figuratively.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Collection; series; group. See
<er>Pair</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set</ent><br/
<hw>Set</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <fld>(Egyptian
Mythology)</fld> <def>An evil beast-headed god with high square ears and
a long snout; his was the brother and murderer of
<persfn>Osiris</persfn>. Called also <altname>Seth</altname></def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Se"ta</hw> <pr>(s<emac/"t<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Setae</plw> <pr>(s<emac/"t<emac/)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L. <ets>seta</ets>, <ets>saeta</ets>, a bristle.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Any slender, more or less rigid,
bristlelike organ or part; as the hairs of a caterpillar, the slender
spines of a crustacean, the hairlike processes of a protozoan, the
bristles or stiff hairs on the leaves of some plants, or the pedicel of
the capsule of a moss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the movable
chitinous spines or hooks of an annelid. They usually arise in clusters
from muscular capsules, and are used in locomotion and for defense. They
are very diverse in form.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the spinelike
feathers at the base of the bill of certain birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(s<esl/*t<amac/"sh<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>seta</ets> a bristle: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/tac<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Set with, or
consisting of, bristles; bristly; <as>as, a stiff, <ex>setaceous</ex>
tail</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Bristelike in form or texture; <as>as, a
<ex>setaceous</ex> feather; a <ex>setaceous</ex> leaf.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setback</ent><br/
<hw>Set"back`</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/t"b<acr/k`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Offset, <pos>n.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A backset; a countercurrent; an eddy.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A reversal of progress in an endeavor; a reverse; a
backset; a check; a repulse; a relapse.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setbolt</ent><br/
<hw>Set"bolt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An iron pin, or bolt, for fitting planks closely
together.</def> <rj><au>Craig.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bolt used for forcing another bolt out of its
hole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set chisel</ent><br/
<hw>Set chisel</hw>. <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A kind of chisel or punch,
variously shaped, with a broad flat end, used for stripping off rivet
heads, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setdown</ent><br/
<hw>Set"down`</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/t"doun`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
humbling of a person by act or words, especially by a disparaging
remark, a retort or a reproof; the retort or the reproof which has such
effect. Also called <altname>put-down</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setee</ent><br/
<hw>Set*ee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
2d <er>Settee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seten</ent><br/
<hw>Set"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>obs. imp. pl.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Sit</er></mord>. <def>Sat.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setewale</ent><br/
<hw>Set"e*wale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Cetewale</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set-fair</ent><br/
<hw>Set"-fair`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>In plastering, a
particularly good troweled surface.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setfoil</ent><br/
<hw>Set"foil`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Septfoil</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seth</ent><br/
<hw>Seth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <fld>(Egyptian
Mythology)</fld> <def>An evil beast-headed god with high square ears and
a long snout; his was the brother and murderer of Osiris. Called also
<altname>Set</altname></def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.6</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sethen</ent><br/
<hw>Seth"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv. & conj.</pos> <def>See
<er>Since</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sethic</ent><br/
<hw>Seth"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sothic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*tif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>seta</ets> a bristle + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Producing,
or having one or more, bristles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setiform</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Seta</ets> +
<ets>-form</ets>: cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/tiforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having
the form or structure of setae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setiger</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ti*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Setigerous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An annelid having
setae; a chaetopod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setigerous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*tig"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Seta</ets>
+ <ets>-gerous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Covered with bristles; having or
bearing a seta or setae; setiferous; <as>as, <ex>setigerous</ex> glands;
a <ex>setigerous</ex> segment of an annelid</as>; specifically
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, tipped with a bristle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setim</ent><br/
<hw>Se"tim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shittim</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>set-in sleeve</ent><br/
<hw>set"-in sleeve`</hw> <pr>(s<ecr/t"<icr/n sl<emac/v`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A sleeve joined to the body of a garment by a seam
starting at the edge of the shoulder and continuing around the armhole.
Contrasted to a <er>raglan sleeve</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setiparous</ent><br/
<hw>Se*tip"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Seta</ets>
+ L. <ets>papere</ets> to produce.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Producing setae; -- said of the organs from which the setae of
annelids arise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setireme</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ti*reme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Seta</ets> +
L. <ets>remus</ets> an oar.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A swimming
leg (of an insect) having a fringe of hairs on the margin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setness</ent><br/
<hw>Set"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being set; formality; obstinacy.</def> <ldquo/The starched
<xex>setness</xex> of a sententious writer.<rdquo/ <rj><au>R.
Masters.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set-off</ent><br/
<hw>Set"-off`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Set</ets> +
<ets>off</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is set off against
another thing; an offset.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do not contemplate such a heroine as a <qex>set-off</qex> to the
many sins imputed to me as committed against woman.</q> <rj><qau>D.
Jerrold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is used to improve the appearance of
anything; a decoration; an ornament.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A counterclaim; a cross debt or
demand; a distinct claim filed or set up by the defendant against the
plaintiff's demand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Set-off</xex> differs from <xex>recoupment</xex>,
as the latter generally grows out of the same matter or contract with
the plaintiff's claim, while the former grows out of distinct matter,
and does not of itself deny the justice of the plaintiff's demand.
<xex>Offset</xex> is sometimes improperly used for the legal term
<xex>set-off</xex>. See <er>Recoupment</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Offset</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>See <er>Offset</er>,
7.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Set-off</er>, <er>Offset</er>.</syn> --
<usage><xex>Offset</xex> originally denoted that which branches off or
projects, as a shoot from a tree, but the term has long been used in
America in the sense of <xex>set-off</xex>. This use is beginning to
obtain in England; though Macaulay uses <xex>set-off</xex>, and so,
perhaps, do a majority of English writers.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seton</ent><br/
<hw>Se"ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>s<eacute/ton</ets> (cf. It. <ets>setone</ets>), from L.
<ets>seta</ets> a thick, stiff hair, a bristle.]</ety> <fld>(Med. &
Far.)</fld> <def>A few silk threads or horsehairs, or a strip of linen
or the like, introduced beneath the skin by a knife or needle, so as to
form an issue; also, the issue so formed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setous</ent><br/
<ent>Setose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Se*tose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Se"tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>setosus</ets>, <ets>saetosus</ets>,
from <ets>seta</ets>, <ets>saeta</ets>, bristle: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/teux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Thickly set with bristles or
bristly hairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setout</ent><br/
<hw>Set"out`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A display, as of
plate, equipage, etc.; that which is displayed.</def>
<mark>[Coloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set-stitched</ent><br/
<hw>Set"-stitched`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stitched
according to a formal pattern.</def> <ldquo/An old
<xex>set-stiched</xex> chair, valanced, and fringed with party-colored
worsted bobs.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sterne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sett</ent><br/
<hw>Sett</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Set</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2 <sd>(e)</sd> and 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settee</ent><br/
<hw>Set*tee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Set</er>;
cf. <er>Settle</er> a seat.]</ety> <def>A long seat with a back, -- made
to accommodate several persons at once.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settee</ent><br/
<hw>Set*tee"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sc<eacute/tie</ets>,
<ets>scitie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A vessel with a very
long, sharp prow, carrying two or three masts with lateen sails, -- used
in the Mediterranean.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>setee</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setter</ent><br/
<hw>Set"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, sets; -- used mostly in composition with a noun, as
type<xex>setter</xex>; or in combination with an adverb, as a
<xex>setter</xex> on (or inciter), a <xex>setter</xex> up, a
<xex>setter</xex> forth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A hunting dog of a special breed
originally derived from a cross between the spaniel and the pointer.
Modern setters are usually trained to indicate the position of game
birds by standing in a fixed position, but originally they indicated it
by sitting or crouching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ There are several distinct varieties of setters; as, the
<xex>Irish</xex>, or <xex>red</xex>, <xex>setter</xex>; the <xex>Gordon
setter</xex>, which is usually red or tan varied with black; and the
<xex>English setter</xex>, which is variously colored, but usually white
and tawny red, with or without black.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who hunts victims for sharpers.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One who adapts words to music in
composition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An adornment; a decoration; -- with
<xex>off</xex>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They come as . . . <qex>setters</qex> off of thy graces.</q>
<rj><qau>Whitlock.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Pottery)</fld> <def>A shallow seggar for
porcelain.</def> <rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setter</ent><br/
<hw>Set"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cut the dewlap (of a cow or
an ox), and to insert a seton, so as to cause an issue.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setterwort</ent><br/
<hw>Set"ter*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The bear's-foot (<spn>Helleborus f<oe/tidus</spn>); -- so called
because the root was used in <xex>settering</xex>, or inserting setons
into the dewlaps of cattle. Called also
<altname>pegroots</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Dr. Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setting</ent><br/
<hw>Set"ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of one who, or that which, sets; <as>as, the <ex>setting</ex> of type,
or of gems; the <ex>setting</ex> of the sun; the <ex>setting</ex>
(hardening) of moist plaster of Paris; the <ex>setting</ex> (set) of a
current.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of marking the position of game, as a setter
does; also, hunting with a setter.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something set in, or inserted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt set in it <qex>settings</qex> of stones.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. xxviii. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That in which something, as a gem, is set; <as>as,
the gold <ex>setting</ex> of a jeweled pin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>the time, place, and circumstances in which an event
(real or fictional) occurs; <as>as, the <ex>setting</ex> of a
novel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Setting coat</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>the
finishing or last coat of plastering on walls or ceilings.</cd> --
<col><b>Setting dog</b></col>, <cd>a setter. See <er>Setter</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Setting pole</b></col>, <cd>a pole,
often iron-pointed, used for pushing boats along in shallow water.</cd>
-- <col><b>Setting rule</b></col>. <fld>(Print.)</fld> <cd>A composing
rule.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setting-up exercise</ent><br/
<hw>Set`ting-up" ex"er*cise</hw>. <def>Any one of a series of gymnastic
exercises used, as in drilling recruits, for the purpose of giving an
erect carriage, supple muscles, and an easy control of the
limbs.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settle</ent><br/
<hw>Set"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>setel</ets>,
<ets>setil</ets>, a seat, AS. <ets>setl</ets>: akin to OHG.
<ets>sezzal</ets>, G. <ets>sessel</ets>, Goth. <ets>sitls</ets>, and E.
<ets>sit</ets>. <root/154. See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
seat of any kind.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Upon the
<xex>settle</xex> of his majesty<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hampole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bench; especially, a bench with a high
back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A place made lower than the rest; a wide step or
platform lower than some other part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And from the bottom upon the ground, even to the lower
<qex>settle</qex>, shall be two cubits, and the breadth one cubit.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. xliii. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Settle bed</b></col>, <cd>a bed convertible into a
seat.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settle</ent><br/
<hw>Set"tle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Settled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Settling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>setlen</ets>, AS. <ets>setlan</ets>. <root/154. See
<er>Settle</er>, <pos>n.</pos> In senses 7, 8, and 9 perhaps confused
with OE. <ets>sahtlen</ets> to reconcile, AS. <ets>sahtlian</ets>, fr.
<ets>saht</ets> reconciliation, <ets>sacon</ets> to contend, dispute.
Cf. <er>Sake</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place in a fixed or
permanent condition; to make firm, steady, or stable; to establish; to
fix; esp., to establish in life; to fix in business, in a home, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And he <qex>settled</qex> his countenance steadfastly upon him,
until he was ashamed.</q> <rj><qau>2 Kings viii. 11. (Rev. Ver.)
</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The father thought the time drew on<br/
Of <qex>setting</qex> in the world his only son.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To establish in the pastoral office; to ordain or
install as pastor or rector of a church, society, or parish; <as>as, to
<ex>settle</ex> a minister</as>.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to be no longer in a disturbed condition;
to render quiet; to still; to calm; to compose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God <qex>settled</qex> then the huge whale-bearing lake.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hoping that sleep might <qex>settle</qex> his brains.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To clear of dregs and impurities by causing them to
sink; to render pure or clear; -- said of a liquid; <as>as, to
<ex>settle</ex> coffee, or the grounds of coffee</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To restore or bring to a smooth, dry, or passable
condition; -- said of the ground, of roads, and the like; <as>as, clear
weather <ex>settles</ex> the roads</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To cause to sink; to lower; to depress; hence, also,
to render close or compact; <as>as, to <ex>settle</ex> the contents of a
barrel or bag by shaking it</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To determine, as something which is exposed to doubt
or question; to free from unscertainty or wavering; to make sure, firm,
or constant; to establish; to compose; to quiet; <as>as, to
<ex>settle</ex> the mind when agitated; to <ex>settle</ex> questions of
law; to <ex>settle</ex> the succession to a throne; to <ex>settle</ex>
an allowance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It will <qex>settle</qex> the wavering, and confirm the
doubtful.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To adjust, as something in discussion; to make up;
to compose; to pacify; <as>as, to <ex>settle</ex> a
quarrel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To adjust, as accounts; to liquidate; to balance;
<as>as, to <ex>settle</ex> an account</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>Hence, to pay; <as>as, to <ex>settle</ex> a
bill</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Abbott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To plant with inhabitants; to colonize; to people;
<as>as, the French first <ex>settled</ex> Canada; the Puritans
<ex>settled</ex> New England; Plymouth was <ex>settled</ex> in
1620.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To settle on</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To
settle upon</b></col></mcol>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>to confer upon by
permanent grant; to assure to.</cd> <ldquo/I . . . have <xex>settled
upon</xex> him a good annuity.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>to choose; to decide on; -- sometimes with the implication that the
choice is not ideal, but the best available.</cd> -- <col><b>To settle
the land</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to cause it to sink, or
appear lower, by receding from it.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To fix; establish; regulate; arrange; compose;
adjust; determine; decide.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settle</ent><br/
<hw>Set"tle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To become fixed or
permanent; to become stationary; to establish one's self or itself; to
assume a lasting form, condition, direction, or the like, in place of a
temporary or changing state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wind came about and <qex>settled</qex> in the west.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Chyle . . . runs through all the intermediate colors until it
<qex>settles</qex> in an intense red.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fix one's residence; to establish a dwelling
place or home; <as>as, the Saxons who <ex>settled</ex> in
Britain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To enter into the married state, or the state of a
householder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As people marry now and <qex>settle</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be established in an employment or profession;
<as>as, to <ex>settle</ex> in the practice of law</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To become firm, dry, and hard, as the ground after
the effects of rain or frost have disappeared; <as>as, the roads
<ex>settled</ex> late in the spring</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To become clear after being turbid or obscure; to
clarify by depositing matter held in suspension; <as>as, the weather
<ex>settled</ex>; wine <ex>settles</ex> by standing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A government, on such occasions, is always thick before it
<qex>settles</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To sink to the bottom; to fall to the bottom, as
dregs of a liquid, or the sediment of a reserveir.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To sink gradually to a lower level; to subside, as
the foundation of a house, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To become calm; to cease from agitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till the fury of his highness <qex>settle</qex>,<br/
Come not before him.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To adjust differences or accounts; to come to an
agreement; <as>as, he has <ex>settled</ex> with his
creditors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To make a jointure for a wife.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He sighs with most success that <qex>settles</qex> well.</q>
<rj><qau>Garth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settledness</ent><br/
<hw>Set"tled*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being settled; confirmed state.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settlement</ent><br/
<hw>Set"tle*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of setting, or the state of being settled.</def> Specifically:
--<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>Establishment in life, in business, condition,
etc.; ordination or installation as pastor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every man living has a design in his head upon wealth power, or
<qex>settlement</qex> in the world.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The act of peopling, or state of being peopled; act
of planting, as a colony; colonization; occupation by settlers; as, the
<xex>settlement</xex> of a new country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1319 --></p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>The act or process of adjusting or determining;
composure of doubts or differences; pacification; liquidation of
accounts; arrangement; adjustment; <as>as, <ex>settlement</ex> of a
controversy, of accounts, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>Bestowal, or giving possession, under legal
sanction; the act of giving or conferring anything in a formal and
permanent manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My flocks, my fields, my woods, my pastures take,<br/
With <qex>settlement</qex> as good as law can make.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A disposition of property for the
benefit of some person or persons, usually through the medium of
trustees, and for the benefit of a wife, children, or other relatives;
jointure granted to a wife, or the act of granting it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which settles, or is settled, established, or
fixed.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>Matter that subsides; settlings; sediment; lees;
dregs.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fuller's earth left a thick <qex>settlement</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A colony newly established; a place or region newly
settled; <as>as, <ex>settlement</ex> in the West</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>That which is bestowed formally and permanently;
the sum secured to a person; especially, a jointure made to a woman at
her marriage; also, in the United States, a sum of money or other
property formerly granted to a pastor in additional to his
salary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The gradual sinking
of a building, whether by the yielding of the ground under the
foundation, or by the compression of the joints or the material.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Fractures or dislocations caused by
settlement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A settled place of abode;
residence; a right growing out of residence; legal residence or
establishment of a person in a particular parish or town, which entitles
him to maintenance if a pauper, and subjects the parish or town to his
support.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone. Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Act of settlement</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld>,
<cd>the statute of 12 and 13 William III, by which the crown was limited
to the present reigning house (the house of Hanover).</cd>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settler</ent><br/
<hw>Set"tler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
settles, becomes fixed, established, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, one who establishes himself in a new
region or a colony; a colonist; a planter; <as>as, the first
<ex>settlers</ex> of New England</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which settles or finishes; hence, a blow, etc.,
which settles or decides a contest.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A vessel, as a tub, in which something, as
pulverized ore suspended in a liquid, is allowed to settle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Settling</ent><br/
<hw>Set"tling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of one who, or that which, settles; the act of establishing one's self,
of colonizing, subsiding, adjusting, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>That which settles at the bottom of
a liquid; lees; dregs; sediment.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Settling day</b></col>, <cd>a day for settling accounts,
as in the stock market.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Set-to</ent><br/
<hw>Set"-to`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A contest in boxing,
in an argument, or the like.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Set"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Setulae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>setula</ets>,
<ets>saetula</ets>, dim. of <ets>seta</ets>, <ets>saeta</ets>,
bristle.]</ety> <def>A small, short hair or bristle; a small
seta.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setule</ent><br/
<hw>Set"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Setula</er>.]</ety> <def>A setula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setulose</ent><br/
<hw>Set"u*lose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having small
bristles or setae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Setwall</ent><br/
<hw>Set"wall`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Cetewale</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant formerly valued
for its restorative qualities (<spn>Valeriana officinalis</spn>, or
<spn>Valeriana Pyrenaica</spn>).</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>setwal</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seven</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>seven</ets>,
<ets>seoven</ets>, <ets>seofen</ets>, AS. <ets>seofon</ets>,
<ets>seofan</ets>, <ets>seofen</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zeven</ets>, OS.,
Goth., & OHG. <ets>sibun</ets>, G. <ets>sieben</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sjau</ets>, <ets>sj<oum/</ets>, Sw. <ets>sju</ets>, Dan.
<ets>syv</ets>, Lith. <ets>septyni</ets>, Russ. <ets>seme</ets>, W.
<ets>saith</ets>, Gael. <ets>seachd</ets>, Ir. <ets>seacht</ets>, L.
<ets>septem</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/, Skr. <ets>saptan</ets>. <root/305. Cf.
<er>Hebdomad</er>, <er>Heptagon</er>, <er>September</er>.]</ety>
<def>One more than six; six and one added; <as>as, <ex>seven</ex> days
make one week</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seven sciences</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Science</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 4.</cd> -- <col><b>Seven
stars</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the Pleiades.</cd> --
<col><b>Seven wonders of the world</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Wonders</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Seven-year apple</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a rubiaceous shrub (<spn>Genipa
clusiifolia</spn>) growing in the West Indies; also, its edible
fruit.</cd> -- <col><b>Seven-year vine</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>a tropical climbing plant (<spn>Ipom<oe/a tuberosa</spn>) related to
the morning-glory.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seven</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The number greater by
one than six; seven units or objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of every beast, and bird, and insect small,<br/
Game <qex>sevens</qex> and pairs.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A symbol representing seven units, as 7, or
vii.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sevenfold</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*fold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Repeated seven
times; having seven thicknesses; increased to seven times the size or
amount.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sevenfold</xex> rage.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sevenfold</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*fold`</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Seven times as much or as
often.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him
<qex>sevenfold</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. iv. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sevennight</ent><br/
<hw>Seven"night</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A week; any period
of seven consecutive days and nights. See <er>Sennight</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sevenscore</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*score`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <def>Seven times
twenty, that is, a hundred and forty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The old Countess of Desmond . . . lived <qex>sevenscore</qex>
years.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seven-shooter</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en-shoot`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A firearm,
esp. a pistol, with seven barrels or chambers for cartridges, or one
capable of firing seven shots without reloading.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventeen</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*teen`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>seventene</ets>, AS. <ets>seofont<ymac/ne</ets>, i. e.,
<ets>seven-ten</ets>. Cf. <er>Seventy</er>.]</ety> <def>One more than
sixteen; ten and seven added; <as>as, <ex>seventeen</ex>
years</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventeen</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*teen`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The number greater
by one than sixteen; the sum of ten and seven; seventeen units or
objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A symbol denoting seventeen units, as 17, or
xvii.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventeenth</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*teenth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Seventeen</er>: cf. AS. <ets>seofonte<oacute/<edh/a</ets>,
<ets>seofonteoge<edh/a</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Next in order
after the sixteenth; coming after sixteen others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In . . . the <qex>seventeenth</qex> day of the month . . . were
all the fountains of the great deep broken up.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. vii.
11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Constituting or being one of seventeen equal parts
into which anything is divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventeenth</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*teenth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
next in order after the sixteenth; one coming after sixteen
others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quotient of a unit divided by seventeen; one of
seventeen equal parts or divisions of one whole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An interval of two octaves and a
third.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventh</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"enth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Seven</er>:
cf. AS. <ets>seofo<edh/a</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Next in order
after the sixth;; coming after six others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On the <qex>seventh</qex> day, God ended his work which he had
made; and he rested on the <qex>seventh</qex> day from all his work
which he had made.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. ii. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Constituting or being one of seven equal parts into
which anything is divided; <as>as, the <ex>seventh</ex>
part</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Seventh day</b></col>, <cd>the seventh day of the week;
Saturday.</cd> -- <col><b>Seventh-day Baptists</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Baptist</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventh</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"enth</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One next in order
after the sixth; one coming after six others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quotient of a unit divided by seven; one of
seven equal parts into which anything is divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An interval embracing
seven diatonic degrees of the scale.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A chord
which includes the interval of a seventh whether major, minor, or
diminished.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seven-thirties</ent><br/
<hw>Sev`en-thir"ties</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>A name
given to three several issues of United States Treasury notes, made
during the Civil War, in denominations of $50 and over, bearing interest
at the rate of seven and three tenths (thirty hundredths) per cent
annually. Within a few years they were all redeemed or funded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventhly</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"enth*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the seventh
place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventieth</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*ti*eth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
hund-<ets>seofontigo<edh/a</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Next in order
after the sixty-ninth; <as>as, a man in the <ex>seventieth</ex> year of
his age</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Constituting or being one of seventy equal
parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventieth</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*ti*eth</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One next in order
after the sixty-ninth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quotient of a unit divided by seventy; one of
seventy equal parts or fractions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventy</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
hund<ets>-seofontig</ets>. See <er>Seven</er>, and <er>Ten</er>, and cf.
<er>Seventeen</er>, <er>Sixty</er>.]</ety> <def>Seven times ten; one
more than sixty-nine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventy</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en*ty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Seventies</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The sum of
seven times ten; seventy units or objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A symbol representing seventy units, as 70, or
lxx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The Seventy</b></col>, <cd>the translators of the Greek
version of the Old Testament called the Septuagint. See
<er>Septuagint</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seventy-four</ent><br/
<hw>Sev`en*ty-four"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A naval vessel carrying seventy-four guns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seven-up</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"en-up`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The game of cards called also
<altname>all fours</altname>, and <altname>old sledge</altname>.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sever</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Severed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Severing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>sevrer</ets>,
<ets>severer</ets>, to separate, F. <ets>sevrer</ets> to wean, fr. L.
<ets>separare</ets>. See <er>Separate</er>, and cf.
<er>Several</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To separate, as one from
another; to cut off from something; to divide; to part in any way,
especially by violence, as by cutting, rending, etc.; <as>as, to
<ex>sever</ex> the head from the body</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The angels shall come forth, and <qex>sever</qex> the wicked from
among the just.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. xiii. 49.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cut or break open or apart; to divide into parts;
to cut through; to disjoin; <as>as, to <ex>sever</ex> the arm or
leg</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our state can not be <qex>severed</qex>; we are one.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To keep distinct or apart; to except; to
exempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>sever</qex> in that day the land of Goshen, in which
my people dwell, that no swarms of flies shall be there.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. viii. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To disunite; to disconnect; to
terminate; <as>as, to <ex>sever</ex> an estate in joint
tenancy</as>.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sever</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To suffer
disjunction; to be parted, or rent asunder; to be separated; to part; to
separate.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a separation or distinction; to
distinguish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord shall <qex>sever</qex> between the cattle of Israel and
the cattle of Egypt.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. ix. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They claimed the right of <qex>severing</qex> in their
challenge.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severable</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
severed.</def> <rj><au>Encyc. Dict.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Several</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF., fr. LL.
<ets>separalis</ets>, fr. L. <ets>separ</ets> separate, different. See
<er>Sever</er>, <er>Separate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Separate;
distinct; particular; single.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each <qex>several</qex> ship a victory did gain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each might his <qex>several</qex> province well command,<br/
Would all but stoop to what they understand.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Diverse; different; various.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Habits and faculties, <qex>several</qex>, and to be
distinguished.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Four <qex>several</qex> armies to the field are led.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Consisting of a number more than two, but not very
many; divers; sundry; <as>as, <ex>several</ex> persons were present when
the event took place</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Several</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*al</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By itself; severally.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every kind of thing is laid up <qex>several</qex> in barns or
storehoudses.</q> <rj><qau>Robynson (More's Utopia).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Several</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Each particular taken
singly; an item; a detail; an individual.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was not time enough to hear . . . <br/
The <qex>severals</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Persons oe objects, more than two, but not very
many.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Several</qex> of them neither rose from any conspicuous
family, nor left any behind them.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An inclosed or separate place; inclosure.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They had their <qex>several</qex> for heathen nations, their
<qex>several</qex> for the people of their own nation.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In several</b></col>, <cd>in a state of separation.</cd>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Where pastures <xex>in several</xex> be.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj> </cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severality</ent><br/
<hw>Sev`er*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Severalities</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>Each particular taken
singly; distinction.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severalize</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
distinguish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severally</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Separately; distinctly;
apart from others; individually.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There must be an auditor to check and revise each
<qex>severally</qex> by itself.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severalty</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*al*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A state of
separation from the rest, or from all others; a holding by individual
right.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Forests which had never been owned in <qex>severalty</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bancroft.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Estate in severalty</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an
estate which the tenant holds in his own right, without being joined in
interest with any other person; -- distinguished from <xex>joint
tenancy</xex>, <xex>coparcenary</xex>, and <xex>common</xex>.</cd>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severance</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of severing, or the state of being severed; partition;
separation.</def> <rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The act of dividing; the singling
or severing of two or more that join, or are joined, in one writ; the
putting in several or separate pleas or answers by two or more
disjointly; the destruction of the unity of interest in a joint
estate.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severe</ent><br/
<hw>Se*vere"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Severer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Severest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>severus</ets>; perhaps akin to Gr. <?/<?/<?/ awe, <?/<?/<?/
revered, holy, solemn, Goth. <ets>swikns</ets> innocent, chaste: cf. F.
<ets>s<eacute/v<egrave/re</ets>. Cf. <er>Asseverate</er>,
<er>Persevere</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Serious in feeling or
manner; sedate; grave; austere; not light, lively, or
cheerful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your looks alter, as your subject does,<br/
From kind to fierce, from wanton to <qex>severe</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Very strict in judgment, discipline, or government;
harsh; not mild or indulgent; rigorous; <as>as, <ex>severe</ex>
criticism; <ex>severe</ex> punishment</as>.</def> <ldquo/Custody
<xex>severe</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Come! you are too <qex>severe</qex> a moraler.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let your zeal, if it must be expressed in anger, be always more
<qex>severe</qex> against thyself than against others.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Rigidly methodical, or adherent to rule or
principle; exactly conformed to a standard; not allowing or employing
unneccessary ornament, amplification, etc.; strict; -- said of style,
argument, etc.</def> <ldquo/Restrained by reason and <xex>severe</xex>
principles.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Latin, a most <qex>severe</qex> and compendious language.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Sharp; afflictive; distressing; violent; extreme;
<as>as, <ex>severe</ex> pain, anguish, fortune; <ex>severe</ex>
cold</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Difficult to be endured; exact; critical; rigorous;
<as>as, a <ex>severe</ex> test</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Strict; grave; austere; stern; morose; rigid;
exact; rigorous; hard; rough; harsh; censorious; tart; acrimonious;
sarcastic; satirical; cutting; biting; keen; bitter; cruel. See
<er>Strict</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Se*vere"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Se*vere"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severity</ent><br/
<hw>Se*ver"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Severities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>severitas</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>s<eacute/v<eacute/rit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality
or state of being severe.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>Gravity or austerity; extreme strictness; rigor;
harshness; <as>as, the <ex>severity</ex> of a reprimand or a reproof;
<ex>severity</ex> of discipline or government; <ex>severity</ex> of
penalties</as>.</def> <ldquo/Strict age, and sour
<xex>severity</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The quality or power of distressing or paining;
extreme degree; extremity; intensity; inclemency; <as>as, the
<ex>severity</ex> of pain or anguish; the <ex>severity</ex> of cold or
heat; the <ex>severity</ex> of the winter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>Harshness; cruel treatment; sharpness of
punishment; <as>as, <ex>severity</ex> practiced on prisoners of
war</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>Exactness; rigorousness; strictness; <as>as, the
<ex>severity</ex> of a test</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Confining myself to the <qex>severity</qex> of truth.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Severy</ent><br/
<hw>Sev"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. corrupted fr.
<ets>ciborium</ets>. <ets>Oxf</ets>. <ets>Gloss</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A bay or compartment of a vaulted ceiling.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>civery</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sevocation</ent><br/
<hw>Sev`o*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sevocare</ets>, <ets>sevocatum</ets>, to call aside.]</ety> <def>A
calling aside.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sevres blue</ent><br/
<hw>S<egrave/"vres blue`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A very light
blue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sevres ware</ent><br/
<hw>S<egrave/"vres ware`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Porcelain manufactured
at <city><etsep>S<egrave/vres</etsep></city>, <country>France</country>,
ecpecially in the national factory situated there.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sew</ent><br/
<hw>Sew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[OE. See <er>Sewer</er>
household officer.]</ety> <def>Juice; gravy; a seasoned dish; a
delicacy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gower.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will not tell of their strange <qex>sewes</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sew</ent><br/
<hw>Sew</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sue</er> to follow.]</ety>
<def>To follow; to pursue; to sue.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer. Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sew</ent><br/
<hw>Sew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Sewed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sewed</conjf>, rarely <conjf>Sewn</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sewing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sewen</ets>, <ets>sowen</ets>, AS. <ets>si<oacute/wian</ets>,
<ets>s<imac/wian</ets>; akin to OHG. <ets>siuwan</ets>, Icel.
<ets>s<?/ja</ets>, Sw. <ets>sy</ets>, Dan. <ets>sye</ets>, Goth.
<ets>siujan</ets>, Lith. <ets>siuti</ets>, Russ, <ets>shite</ets>, L.
<ets>ssuere</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/, Skr. <ets>siv</ets>. <root/156. Cf.
<er>Seam</er> a suture, <er>Suture</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
unite or fasten together by stitches, as with a needle and
thread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No man also <qex>seweth</qex> a piece of new cloth on an old
garment.</q> <rj><qau>Mark ii. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To close or stop by ssewing; -- often with
<xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>sew</ex> up a rip</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To inclose by sewing; -- sometimes with
<xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>sew</ex> money in a bag</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sew</ent><br/
<hw>Sew</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To practice sewing; to work with
needle and thread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sew</ent><br/
<hw>Sew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[<root/151 <it>b.</it>
See <er>Sewer</er> a drain.]</ety> <def>To drain, as a pond, for taking
the fish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewage</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
contents of a sewer or drain; refuse liquids or matter carried off by
sewers</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sewerage, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewe</ent><br/
<hw>Sewe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To perform the duties
of a sewer. See 3d <er>Sewer</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewel</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A scarecrow, generally made of feathers tied to a
string, hung up to prevent deer from breaking into a place.</def>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewellel</ent><br/
<hw>Se*wel"lel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of American Indian
origin.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A peculiar gregarious burrowing
rodent (<spn>Haplodon rufus</spn>), native of the coast region of the
Northwestern United States. It somewhat resembles a muskrat or marmot,
but has only a rudimentary tail. Its head is broad, its eyes are small
and its fur is brownish above, gray beneath. It constitutes the family
<fam>Haplodontidae</fam>. Called also <altname>boomer</altname>,
<altname>showt'l</altname>, and <altname>mountain
beaver</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1320 --></p>

<p><ent>Sewen</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
British trout usually regarded as a variety (var.
<varn>Cambricus</varn>) of the salmon trout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewer</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
sews, or stitches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small tortricid moth whose
larva sews together the edges of a leaf by means of silk; <as>as, the
apple-leaf <ex>sewer</ex> (<spn>Phoxopteris
nubeculana</spn>)</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewer</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>sewiere</ets>, <ets>seuwiere</ets>, ultimately fr. L. <ets>ex</ets>
out + a derivative of <ets>aqua</ets> water; cf. OF. <ets>essevour</ets>
a drain, <ets>essever</ets>, <ets>esseuwer</ets>, <ets>essiaver</ets>,
to cause to flow, to drain, to flow, LL. <ets>exaquatorium</ets> a
channel through which water runs off. Cf. <er>Ewer</er>,
<er>Aquarium</er>.]</ety> <def>A drain or passage to carry off water and
filth under ground; a subterraneous channel, particularly in
cities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewer</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OE. <ets>assewer</ets>, and
<ets>asseour</ets>, OF. <ets>asseour</ets>, F. <ets>asseoir</ets> to
seat, to set, L. <ets>assidere</ets> to sit by; <ets>ad + sedere</ets>
to sit (cf. <er>Sit</er>); or cf. OE. <ets>sew</ets> pottage, sauce,
boiled meat, AS. <ets>se<aacute/w</ets> juice, Skr. <ets>su</ets> to
press out.]</ety> <def>Formerly, an upper servant, or household officer,
who set on and removed the dishes at a feast, and who also brought water
for the hands of the guests.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then the <qex>sewer</qex><br/
Poured water from a great and golden ewer,<br/
That from their hands to a silver caldron ran.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewerage</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"er*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
construction of a sewer or sewers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The system of sewers in a city, town, etc.; the
general drainage of a city or town by means of sewers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The material collected in, and discharged by,
sewers.</def> <altsp>[In this sense <asp>sewage</asp> is preferable and
common.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewin</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>Sewen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewing</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or
occupation of one who sews.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is sewed with the needle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sewing horse</b></col> <fld>(Harness making)</fld>, <cd>a
clamp, operated by the foot, for holding pieces of leather while being
sewed.</cd> -- <col><b>Sewing machine</b></col>, <cd>a machine for
sewing or stitching.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sewing press</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Sewing table</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Bookbinding)</fld>, <cd>a fixture or table having a frame in which
are held the cords to which the back edges of folded sheets are sewed to
form a book.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sewster</ent><br/
<hw>Sew"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seamstress.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sex-</ent><br/
<hw>Sex-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>sex</ets> six. See
<er>Six</er>.]</ety> <def>A combining form meaning <xex>six</xex>;
<as>as, <ex>sex</ex>digitism; <ex>sex</ex>ennial</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sex</ent><br/
<hw>Sex</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sexus</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sexe</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The distinguishing peculiarity
of male or female in both animals and plants; the physical difference
between male and female; the assemblage of properties or qualities by
which male is distinguished from female.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the two divisions of organic beings formed on
the distinction of male and female.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The capability in
plants of fertilizing or of being fertilized; <as>as, staminate and
pistillate flowers are of opposite <ex>sexes</ex></as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the groups founded on this
distinction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The sex</b></col>, <cd>the female sex; women, in
general.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexagenarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sex`a*ge*na"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sexagenary</er>.]</ety> <def>A person who is sixty years
old.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexagenary</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*ag"e*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sexagenarius</ets>, fr. <ets>sexageni</ets> sixty each, akin to
<ets>sexaginta</ets> sixty, <ets>sex</ets> six: cf.
<ets>sexag<eacute/naire</ets>. See <er>Six</er>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining
to, or designating, the number sixty; poceeding by sixties; sixty years
old.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sexagenary arithmetic</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Sexagesimal</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sexagenary scale</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Sexagesimal scale</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>a scale of numbers in which the modulus is
sixty. It is used in treating the divisions of the circle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexagenary</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*ag"e*na*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something
composed of sixty parts or divisions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sexagenarian.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexagesima</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sex`a*ges"i*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fem.
of <ets>sexagesimus</ets> sixtieth, fr. <ets>sexaginta</ets>
sixty.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The second Sunday before Lent; --
so called as being about the sixtieth day before Easter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexagesimal</ent><br/
<hw>Sex`a*ges"i*mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sexag<eacute/simal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to, or founded on,
the number sixty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sexagesimal fractions</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sexagesimal numbers</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Arith. & Alg.)</fld>,
<cd>those fractions whose denominators are some power of sixty; as,
<frac1x60/, <frac1x3600/, <frac1x216000/; -- called also
<altname>astronomical fractions</altname>, because formerly there were
no others used in astronomical calculations.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Sexagesimal arithmetic</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sexagenary arithmetic</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the method of
computing by the sexagenary scale, or by sixties.</cd> --
<col><b>Sexagesimal scale</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>the
sexagenary scale.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexagesimal</ent><br/
<hw>Sex`a*ges"i*mal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sexagesimal
fraction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexangle</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"an`gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sexangulus</ets> sexangular; <ets>sex</ets> six +
<ets>angulus</ets> angle: cf. F. <ets>sexangle</ets>. Cf.
<er>Hexangular</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A hexagon.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexangular</ent><br/
<ent>Sexangled</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sex"an`gled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sex*an"gu*lar</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sexangulaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having six angles; hexagonal.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexangularly</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*an"gu*lar*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Hexagonally.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexavalent</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*av"a*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sexivalent</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexdigitism</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*dig"it*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sex-</ets> + <ets>digit</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state of
having six fingers on a hand, or six toes on a foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexdigitist</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*dig"it*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who has six fingers on a
hand, or six toes on a foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexed</ent><br/
<hw>Sexed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Belonging to sex; having
sex; distinctively male of female; <as>as, the <ex>sexed</ex>
condition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexenary</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"e*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Proceeding by
sixes; sextuple; -- applied especially to a system of arithmetical
computation in which the base is six.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexennial</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*en"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sexennium</ets> a period of six years, <ets>sexennis</ets> of six
years; <ets>sex</ets> six + <ets>annus</ets> a year. See <er>Six</er>,
and <er>Annual</er>.]</ety> <def>Lasting six years, or happening once in
six years.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A sexennial
event.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexennially</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*en"ni*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Once in six
years.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexifid</ent><br/
<ent>Sexfid</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sex"fid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sex"i*fid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sex-</ets> + root of L.
<ets>findere</ets> to split: cf. F. <ets>sexfide</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Six-cleft; <as>as, a <ex>sexfid</ex> calyx or
nectary</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexisyllabic</ent><br/
<hw>Sex`i*syl*lab"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sex-</ets> + <ets>syllabic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having six
syllables.</def> <rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexisyllable</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"i*syl`la*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sex-</ets> + <ets>syllable</ets>.]</ety> <def>A word of six
syllables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexivalent</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*iv"a*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sex-</ets>
+ L. <ets>valens</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Valence</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(hem.)</fld> <def>Hexavalent.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexless</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no
sex.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexlocular</ent><br/
<hw>Sex`loc"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sex-</ets>
+ <ets>locular</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sexloculaire</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having six cells for seeds; six-celled; <as>as, a
<ex>sexlocular</ex> pericarp</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexly</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to
sex.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Should I ascribe any of these things unto myself or my
<qex>sexly</qex> weakness, I were not worthy to live.</q> <rj><qau>Queen
Elizabeth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexradiate</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*ra"di*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sex-</ets>
+ <ets>radiate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having six rays; --
said of certain sponge spicules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sext</ent><br/
<hw>Sext</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sexta</ets>,
fem. of <ets>sextus</ets> sixtt, fr. <ets>sex</ets> six: cf. F.
<ets>sexte</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(R.C.Ch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
office for the sixth canonical hour, being a part of the Breviary.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sixth book of the decretals, added by Pope
Boniface VIII.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextain</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"tain</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sextus</ets> sixth, fr.
<ets>sex</ets> six: cf. It. <ets>sestina</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Pros.)</fld> <def>A stanza of six lines; a sestine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextans</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sex"tans</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Sextant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A
Roman coin, the sixth part of an as.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A constellation on the equator
south of Leo; the Sextant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextant</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sextans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, the sixth part of an as, fr.
<ets>sextus</ets> sixth, <ets>sex</ets> six. See <er>Six</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>The sixth part of a
circle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument for measuring angular distances
between objects, -- used esp. at sea, for ascertaining the latitude and
longitude. It is constructed on the same optical principle as Hadley's
quadrant, but usually of metal, with a nicer graduation, telescopic
sight, and its arc the sixth, and sometimes the third, part of a circle.
See <er>Quadrant</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The constellation
Sextans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Box sextant</b></col>, <cd>a small sextant inclosed in a
cylindrical case to make it more portable.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextary</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sextaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>sextarius</ets>
the sixth part of a measure, weight, etc., fr. <ets>sextus</ets> sixth,
<ets>sex</ets> six.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>An ancient
Roman liquid and dry measure, about equal to an English pint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextary</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>sextonry</ets>.]</ety> <def>A sacristy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextetto</ent><br/
<ent>Sextet</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sex*tet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sex*tet"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Sestet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexteyn</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"teyn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sacristan.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextic</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sextus</ets>
sixth.]</ety> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Of the sixth degree or
order.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Alg.)</fld> <def>A quantic of
the sixth degree.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextile</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sextil</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sextus</ets> the sixth, from
<ets>sex</ets> six. See <er>Six</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld>
<def>Measured by sixty degrees; fixed or indicated by a distance of
sixty degrees.</def> <rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextile</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"tile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. aspect
<ets>sextil</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>The aspect or
position of two planets when distant from each other sixty degrees, or
two signs. This position is marked thus: <8star/.</def>
<rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextillion</ent><br/
<hw>Sex*til"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formed (in
imitation of <ets>million</ets>) fr. L. <ets>sextus</ets> sixth,
<ets>sex</ets> six: cf. F. <ets>sextilion</ets>.]</ety> <def>According
to the method of numeration (which is followed also in the United
States), the number expressed by a unit with twenty-one ciphers annexed.
According to the English method, a million raised to the sixth power, or
the number expressed by a unit with thirty-six ciphers annexed. See
<er>Numeration</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexto</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sextos</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>sextus</ets>
sixth.]</ety> <def>A book consisting of sheets each of which is folded
into six leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextodecimo</ent><br/
<hw>Sex`to*dec"i*mo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sextus-decimus</ets> the sixteenth; <ets>sextus</ets> the sixth
(fr. <ets>sex</ets> six) + <ets>decimus</ets> the tenth, from
<ets>decem</ets> ten. See <er>-mo</er>.]</ety> <def>Having sixteen
leaves to a sheet; of, or equal to, the size of one fold of a sheet of
printing paper when folded so as to make sixteen leaves, or thirty-two
pages; <as>as, a <ex>sextodecimo</ex> volume</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextodecimo</ent><br/
<hw>Sex`to*dec"imo</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sextodecimos</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A book composed of
sheets each of which is folded into sixteen leaves; hence, indicating,
more or less definitely, a size of a book; -- usually written 16mo, or
16<deg/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextolet</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"to*let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A
double triplet; a group of six equal notes played in the time of
four.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexton</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sextein</ets>, contr. fr. <ets>sacristan</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
under officer of a church, whose business is to take care of the church
building and the vessels, vestments, etc., belonging to the church, to
attend on the officiating clergyman, and to perform other duties
pertaining to the church, such as to dig graves, ring the bell,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sexton beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a burying
beetle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextoness</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"ton*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female sexton; a sexton's
wife.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextonry</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"ton*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sextonship.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ld. Bernes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextonship</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"ton*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a sexton.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextry</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sacristy</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sextuple</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"tu*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Formed (in
imitation of <ets>quadruple</ets>) fr. L. <ets>sextus</ets> sixth: cf.
F. <ets>sextuple</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Six times as much;
sixfold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Divisible by six; having six
beats; <as>as, <ex>sixtuple</ex> measure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexual</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"u*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sexualis</ets>, fr. <ets>sexus</ets> sex: cf. F.
<ets>sexuel</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to sex, or the sexes;
distinguishing sex; peculiar to the distinction and office of male or
female; relating to the distinctive genital organs of the sexes;
proceeding from, or based upon, sex; <as>as, <ex>sexual</ex>
characteristics; <ex>sexual</ex> intercourse, connection, or commerce;
<ex>sexual</ex> desire; <ex>sexual</ex> diseases; <ex>sexual</ex>
generation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Sexual dimorphism</b></col> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>the
condition of having one of the sexes existing in two forms, or
varieties, differing in color, size, etc., as in many species of
butterflies which have two kinds of females.</cd> -- <col><b>Sexual
method</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a method of classification
proposed by Linnaeus, founded mainly on difference in number and
position of the stamens and pistils of plants.</cd> -- <col><b>Sexual
selection</b></col> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>, <cd>the selective preference of
one sex for certain characteristics in the other, such as bright colors,
musical notes, etc.; also, the selection which results from certain
individuals of one sex having more opportunities of pairing with the
other sex, on account of greater activity, strength, courage, etc.;
applied likewise to that kind of evolution which results from such
sexual preferences.</cd> <au>Darwin.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In these cases, therefore, natural selection seems to have acted
independently of <qex>sexual selection</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>A. R.
Wallace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Sexualist</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"u*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One who
classifies plants by the sexual method of Linnaeus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexuality</ent><br/
<hw>Sex`u*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being distinguished by sex.</def>
<rj><au>Lindley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexualize</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"u*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To attribute
sex to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sexually</ent><br/
<hw>Sex"u*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sexual manner or
relation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seyh</ent><br/
<ent>Sey</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Seyh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp. sing. & 2d pers. pl.</pos> of
<er>See</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seyen</ent><br/
<ent>Seye</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Seye</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Seyen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp. pl. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>See</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seynd</ent><br/
<hw>Seynd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Senge</er>, to singe.</def> <rj><au> Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Seynt</ent><br/
<hw>Seynt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A gridle. See 1st
<er>Seint</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sforzato</ent><br/
<ent>Sforzando</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Sfor*zan"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Sfor*za"to</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>sforzando</ets>, p.
pr., and <ets>sforzato</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sforzare</ets> to
force.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Forcing or forced; -- a direction
placed over a note, to signify that it must be executed with peculiar
emphasis and force; -- marked <xex>fz</xex> (an abbreviation of
<xex>forzando</xex>), <xex>sf</xex>, <xex>sfz</xex>, or <?/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sfumato</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sfu*ma"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>Having vague outlines, and colors and shades so
mingled as to give a misty appearance; -- said of a painting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sgraffito</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sgraf*fi"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>Scratched; -- said of decorative painting of a
certain style, in which a white overland surface is cut or scratched
through, so as to form the design from a dark ground
underneath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shab</ent><br/
<hw>Shab</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shabbe</ets>,
AS. <ets>sc<?/b</ets>. See <er>Scab</er>.]</ety> <def>The itch in
animals; also, a scab.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shab</ent><br/
<hw>Shab</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shabbed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shabbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Scab</er>, 3.]</ety>
<def>To play mean tricks; to act shabbily.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shab</ent><br/
<hw>Shab</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To scratch; to rub.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Farquhar.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shabbed</ent><br/
<hw>Shab"bed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shabby.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shabbily</ent><br/
<hw>Shab"bi*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shabby
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shabbiness</ent><br/
<hw>Shab"bi*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sghabby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shabble</ent><br/
<ent>Shabble</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shab"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shab"ble</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos><ety>[Cf. D. <ets>sabel</ets>, and G.
<ets>s<aum/bel</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of crooked sword or
hanger.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shabby</ent><br/
<hw>Shab"by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shabbier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shabbiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[See <er>Shab</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, <er>Scabby</er>, and <er>Scab</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Torn or worn to rage; poor; mean; ragged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wearing <qex>shabby</qex> coats and dirty shirts.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Clothed with ragged, much worn, or soiled
garments.</def> <ldquo/The dean was so <xex>shabby</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Mean; paltry; despicable; <as>as, <ex>shabby</ex>
treatment</as>.</def> <ldquo/Very <xex>shabby</xex> fellows.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shabrack</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Shab"rack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Turk.
<ets>tsh<amac/pr<amac/k</ets>, whence F. <ets>chabraque</ets>, G.
<ets>shabracke</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>The saddlecloth or
housing of a cavalry horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shack</ent><br/
<hw>Shack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E., to shake,
to shed. See <er>Shake</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shed or fall, as
corn or grain at harvest.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To feed in stubble, or upon waste corn.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To wander as a vagabond or a tramp.</def>
<mark>[Prev.Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shack</ent><br/
<hw>Shack</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Shack</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <def>a small simple dwelling, usually having only one
room and of flimsy construction; a hut; a shanty; a cabin.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></p>

<p><ent>Shack</ent><br/
<hw>Shack</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Scot. <ets>shag</ets> refuse of
barley or oats.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The grain left after harvest or
gleaning; also, nuts which have fallen to the ground.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Liberty of winter pasturage.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A shiftless fellow; a low, itinerant beggar; a
vagabond; a tramp.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq. U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Forby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the poor old <qex>shacks</qex> about the town found a friend
in Deacon Marble.</q> <rj><qau>H. W. Beecher.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These miserable <qex>shacks</qex> are so low that their occupants
cannot stand erect.</q> <rj><qau>D. C. Worcester.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Common of shack</b></col> <fld>(Eng.Law)</fld>, <cd>the
right of persons occupying lands lying together in the same common field
to turn out their cattle to range in it after harvest.</cd>
<rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shackatory</ent><br/
<hw>Shack"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hound.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shackle</ent><br/
<hw>Shac"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stubble.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Pegge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shackle</ent><br/
<hw>Shac"kle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Generally used in the
plural.]</ety> <ety>[OE. <ets>schakkyll</ets>, <ets>schakle</ets>, AS.
<ets>scacul</ets>, <ets>sceacul</ets>, a shackle, fr. <ets>scacan</ets>
to shake; cf. D. <ets>schakel</ets> a link of a chain, a mesh, Icel.
<ets>sk<oum/kull</ets> the pole of a cart. See <er>Shake</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Something which confines the legs or arms so as to
prevent their free motion; specifically, a ring or band inclosing the
ankle or wrist, and fastened to a similar shackle on the other leg or
arm, or to something else, by a chain or a strap; a gyve; a
fetter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>shackles</qex> empty left; himself escaped clean.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, that which checks or prevents free
action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His very will seems to be in bonds and <qex>shackles</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A fetterlike band worn as an ornament.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Most of the men and women . . . had all earrings made of gold, and
gold <qex>shackles</qex> about their legs and arms.</q>
<rj><qau>Dampier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A link or loop, as in a chain, fitted with a movable
bolt, so that the parts can be separated, or the loop removed; a
clevis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A link for connecting railroad cars; -- called also
<altname>drawlink</altname>, <altname>draglink</altname>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The hinged and curved bar of a padlock, by which it
is hung to the staple.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shackle joint</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a joint
formed by a bony ring passing through a hole in a bone, as at the bases
of spines in some fishes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1321 --></p>

<p><ent>Shackle</ent><br/
<hw>Shac"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shackled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shackling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To tie or
confine the limbs of, so as to prevent free motion; to bind with
shackles; to fetter; to chain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To lead him <qex>shackled</qex>, and exposed to scorn<br/
Of gathering crowds, the Britons' boasted chief.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively: To bind or confine so as to prevent or
embarrass action; to impede; to cumber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shackled</qex> by her devotion to the king, she seldom could
pursue that object.</q> <rj><qau>Walpole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To join by a link or chain, as railroad cars.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shackle bar</b></col>, <cd>the coupling between a
locomotive and its tender.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Shackle
bolt</b></col>, <cd>a shackle.</cd> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shacklock</ent><br/
<hw>Shack"lock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sort of
shackle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shackly</ent><br/
<hw>Shack"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shaky; rickety.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shack up</ent><br/
<hw>Shack" up`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to live together
in a sexual relationship, without being legally married.</def>
<mark>[Slang, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>to live in a cabin, shack, or other crude
dwelling.</def> <mark>[Slang, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shad</ent><br/
<hw>Shad</hw> <pr>(sh<acr/d)</pr>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sceadda</ets> a kind of fish, akin to Prov. G. <ets>schade</ets>;
cf. Ir. & Gael. <ets>sgadan</ets> a herring, W. <ets>ysgadan</ets>
herrings; all perhaps akin to E. <ets>skate</ets> a fish.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of food fishes of the
Herring family. The American species (<spn>Alosa sapidissima</spn>
formerly <spn>Clupea sapidissima</spn>), which is abundant on the
Atlantic coast and ascends the larger rivers in spring to spawn, is an
important market fish. The European allice shad, or alose (<spn>Alosa
alosa</spn> formerly <spn>Clupea alosa</spn>), and the twaite shad
(<spn>Alosa finta</spn> formerly <spn>Clupea finta</spn>), are less
important species.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>chad</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is loosely applied, also, to several other
fishes, as the gizzard shad (see under <er>Gizzard</er>), called also
<altname>mud shad</altname>, <altname>white-eyed shad</altname>, and
<altname>winter shad</altname>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Hardboaded shad</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Yellow-tailed shad</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the menhaden.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Hickory shad</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Tailor
shad</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the <er>mattowacca</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Long-boned shad</b></col>, <cd>one of several species of
important food fishes of the Bermudas and the West Indies, of the genus
<gen>Gerres</gen>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shad bush</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name given to the North American shrubs or
small trees of the rosaceous genus <gen>Amelanchier</gen>
(<spn>Amelanchier Canadensis</spn>, and <spn>Amelanchier
alnifolia</spn>). Their white racemose blossoms open in April or May,
when the shad appear, and the edible berries (pomes) ripen in June or
July, whence they are called <prod>Juneberries</prod>. The plant is also
called <altname>service tree</altname>, and
<altname>Juneberry</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shad frog</b></col>,
<cd>an American spotted frog (<spn>Rana halecina</spn>); -- so called
because it usually appears at the time when the shad begin to run in the
rivers.</cd> -- <col><b>Trout shad</b></col>, <cd>the squeteague.</cd>
-- <col><b>White shad</b></col>, <cd>the common shad.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadbird</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"bird`</hw> <pr>(sh<acr/d"b<etil/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The American, or Wilson's, snipe.
See under <er>Snipe</er>. So called because it appears at the same time
as the shad.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The common European
sandpiper.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadd</ent><br/
<hw>Shadd</hw> <pr>(sh<acr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining.)</fld>
<def>Rounded stones containing tin ore, lying at the surface of the
ground, and indicating a vein.</def> <rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadde</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"de</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>obs. imp.</pos> of
<er>Shed</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaddock</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"dock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Said to be so
called from a Captain <ets>Shaddock</ets>, who first brought this fruit
from the East Indies.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A tree (<spn>Citrus
decumana</spn>) and its fruit, which is a large species of orange; --
called also <altname>forbidden fruit</altname>, and
<altname>pompelmous</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shade</ent><br/
<hw>Shade</hw> <pr>(sh<amac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shade</ets>, <ets>shadewe</ets>, <ets>schadewe</ets>, AS.
<ets>sceadu</ets>, <ets>scead</ets>; akin to OS. <ets>skado</ets>, D.
<ets>schaduw</ets>, OHG. <ets>scato</ets>, (gen. <ets>scatewes</ets>),
G. <ets>schatten</ets>, Goth. <ets>skadus</ets>, Ir. & Gael.
<ets>sgath</ets>, and probably to Gr. <grk>sko`tos</grk> darkness.
<root/162. Cf. <er>Shadow</er>, <er>Shed</er> a hat.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Comparative obscurity owing to interception or interruption of the
rays of light; partial darkness caused by the intervention of something
between the space contemplated and the source of light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Shade</xex> differs from <xex>shadow</xex> as it
implies no particular form or definite limit; whereas a
<xex>shadow</xex> represents in form the object which intercepts the
light. When we speak of the <xex>shade</xex> of a tree, we have no
reference to its form; but when we speak of measuring a pyramid or other
object by its <xex>shadow</xex>, we have reference to its form and
extent.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Darkness; obscurity; -- often in the
plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shades</qex> of night were falling fast.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An obscure place; a spot not exposed to light;
hence, a secluded retreat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let us seek out some desolate <qex>shade</qex>, and there<br/
Weep our sad bosoms empty.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which intercepts, or shelters from, light or
the direct rays of the sun; hence, also, that which protects from heat
or currents of air; a screen; protection; shelter; cover; <as>as, a lamp
<ex>shade</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord is thy <qex>shade</qex> upon thy right hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. cxxi. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sleep under a fresh tree's <qex>shade</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let the arched knife well sharpened now assail the spreading
<qex>shades</qex> of vegetables.</q> <rj><qau>J. Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Shadow.</def> <mark>[Poetic.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Envy will merit, as its <qex>shade</qex>, pursue.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The soul after its separation from the body; -- so
called because the ancients it to be perceptible to the sight, though
not to the touch; a spirit; a ghost; <as>as, the <ex>shades</ex> of
departed heroes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Swift as thought the flitting <qex>shade</qex><br/
Thro' air his momentary journey made.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Painting, Drawing, etc.)</fld> <def>The darker
portion of a picture; a less illuminated part. See Def. 1,
above.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Degree or variation of color, as darker or lighter,
stronger or paler; <as>as, a delicate <ex>shade</ex> of
pink</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>White, red, yellow, blue, with their several degrees, or
<qex>shades</qex> and mixtures, as green only in by the eyes.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A minute difference or variation, as of thought,
belief, expression, etc.; also, the quality or degree of anything which
is distinguished from others similar by slight differences; <as>as, the
<ex>shades</ex> of meaning in synonyms</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>New <qex>shades</qex> and combinations of thought.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every <qex>shade</qex> of religious and political opinion has its
own headquarters.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The Shades</b></col>, <cd>the Nether World; the supposed
abode of souls after leaving the body.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shade</ent><br/
<hw>Shade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shaded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shelter or screen
by intercepting the rays of light; to keep off illumination from.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I went to crop the sylvan scenes,<br/
And <qex>shade</qex> our altars with their leafy greens.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To shelter; to cover from injury; to protect; to
screen; to hide; <as>as, to <ex>shade</ex> one's eyes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ere in our own house I do <qex>shade</qex> my head.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To obscure; to dim the brightness of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou <qex>shad'st</qex><br/
The full blaze of thy beams.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To pain in obscure colors; to darken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To mark with gradations of light or color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To present a shadow or image of; to shadow forth; to
represent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The goddess] in her person cunningly did <qex>shade</qex><br/
That part of Justice which is Equity.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shade</ent><br/
<hw>Shade</hw> <pr>(sh<amac/d)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Shade</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To undergo or exhibit minute
difference or variation, as of color, meaning, expression, etc.; to pass
by slight changes; -- used chiefly with a preposition, as
<xex>into</xex>, <xex>away</xex>, <xex>off</xex>.</def></p>

<p><q>This small group will be most conveniently treated with the
emotional division, into which it <qex>shades</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Edmund
Gurney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadeful</ent><br/
<hw>Shade"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of shade;
shady.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadeless</ent><br/
<hw>Shade"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being without shade; not
shaded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shader</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, shades.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadily</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shady
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadiness</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
shady.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shading</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act or process of
making a shade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That filling up which represents the effect of more
or less darkness, expressing rotundity, projection, etc., in a picture
or a drawing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadoof</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sha*doof"</hw> <pr>(sh<adot/*d<oomac/f")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Ar. <ets>sh<amac/d<umac/f</ets>.]</ety> <def>A machine, resembling
a well sweep, used in Egypt for raising water from the Nile for
irrigation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadow</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"ow</hw> <pr>(sh<acr/d"<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Originally the same word as <ets>shade</ets>. <root/162. See
<er>Shade</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Shade within defined limits;
obscurity or deprivation of light, apparent on a surface, and
representing the form of the body which intercepts the rays of light;
<as>as, the <ex>shadow</ex> of a man, of a tree, or of a tower</as>. See
the Note under <er>Shade</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Darkness; shade; obscurity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Night's sable <qex>shadows</qex> from the ocean rise.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A shaded place; shelter; protection;
security.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In secret <qex>shadow</qex> from the sunny ray,<br/
On a sweet bed of lilies softly laid.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A reflected image, as in a mirror or in water.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That which follows or attends a person or thing like
a shadow; an inseparable companion; hence, an obsequious
follower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sin and her <qex>shadow</qex> Death.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A spirit; a ghost; a shade; a phantom.</def>
<ldquo/Hence, horrible <xex>shadow</xex>!<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>An imperfect and faint representation; adumbration;
indistinct image; dim bodying forth; hence, mystical representation;
type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The law having a <qex>shadow</qex> of good things to come.</q>
<rj><qau>Heb. x. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Types] and <qex>shadows</qex> of that destined seed.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A small degree; a shade.</def> <ldquo/No
variableness, neither <xex>shadow</xex> of turning.<rdquo/ <rj><au>James
i. 17.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>An uninvited guest coming with one who is
invited.</def> <mark>[A Latinism]</mark> <rj><au>Nares.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I must not have my board pastered with <qex>shadows</qex><br/
That under other men's protection break in<br/
Without invitement.</q> <rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shadow of death</b></col>, <cd>darkness or gloom like
that caused by the presence or the impending of death.</cd> <rj><au>Ps.
xxiii. 4.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadow</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"ow</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shadowed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shadowing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shadowen</ets>, AS.
<ets>sceadwian</ets>. See <er>adow</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut off light from; to put in shade; to shade; to
throw a shadow upon; to overspead with obscurity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The warlike elf much wondered at this tree,<br/
So fair and great, that <qex>shadowed</qex> all the ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To conceal; to hide; to screen.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let every soldier hew him down a bough.<br/
And bear't before him; thereby shall we <qex>shadow</qex><br/
The numbers of our host.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To protect; to shelter from danger; to
shroud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shadowing</qex> their right under your wings of war.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To mark with gradations of light or color; to
shade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To represent faintly or imperfectly; to adumbrate;
hence, to represent typically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Augustus is <qex>shadowed</qex> in the person of <AE/neas.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To cloud; to darken; to cast a gloom over.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shadowed</qex> livery of the burnished sun.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why sad?<br/
I must not see the face O love thus <qex>shadowed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To attend as closely as a shadow; to follow and
watch closely, especially in a secret or unobserved manner; <as>as, a
detective <ex>shadows</ex> a criminal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadowiness</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"ow*i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being shadowy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadowing</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"ow*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Shade, or gradation
of light and color; shading.</def> <rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A faint representation; an adumbration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are . . . in savage theology <qex>shadowings</qex>, quaint
or majestic, of the conception of a Supreme Deity.</q>
<rj><qau>Tylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadowish</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"ow*ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shadowy; vague.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadowless</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"ow*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no shadow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadowy</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"ow*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of
shade or shadows; causing shade or shadow.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Shadowy</xex> verdure.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Fenton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>shadowy</qex> desert, unfrequented woods.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, dark; obscure; gloomy; dim.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>shadowy</xex> past.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not brightly luminous; faintly light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The moon . . . with more pleasing light,<br/
<qex>Shadowy</qex> sets off the face things.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Faintly representative; hence, typical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From <qex>shadowy</qex> types to truth, from flesh to spirit.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Unsubstantial; unreal; <as>as, <ex>shadowy</ex>
honor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Milton has brought into his poems two actors of a
<qex>shadowy</qex><br/
and fictitious nature, in the persons of Sin and Death.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shadrach</ent><br/
<hw>Sha"drach</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>
<def>A mass of iron on which the operation of smelting has failed of its
intended effect; -- so called from <xex>Shadrach</xex>, one of the three
Hebrews who came forth unharmed from the fiery furnace of
Nebuchadnezzar. (See <au>Dan. iii. 26, 27.</au>)</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shad-spirit</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"-spir`it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shadbird</er> <sd>(a)</sd></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shad-waiter</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"-wait`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A lake whitefish; the roundfish. See <er>Roundfish</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shady</ent><br/
<hw>Shad"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shadier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shadiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding in shade or
shades; overspread with shade; causing shade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shady</qex> trees cover him with their shadow.</q>
<rj><qau>Job. xl. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And Amaryllis fills the <qex>shady</qex> groves.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sheltered from the glare of light or sultry
heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cast it also that you may have rooms <qex>shady</qex> for summer
and warm for winter.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to shade or darkness;</def>
<specif>hence,</specif> <def>unfit to be seen or known; of questionable
character; unsavory; equivocal; dubious, corrupt, or criminal; <as>as, a
<ex>shady</ex> character</as>; -- of people or activities.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>shady</xex> business.<rdquo/
<rj><au>London Sat. Rev.</au></rj></p>

<p><q><qex>Shady</qex> characters, disreputable, criminal.</q>
<rj><qau>London Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>On the shady side of</b></col>, <cd>on the thither side
of; as, <xex>on the shady side of</xex> fifty; that is, more than
fifty.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <col><b>To keep shady</b></col>,
<cd>to stay in concealment; also, to be reticent.</cd>
<mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaffle</ent><br/
<hw>Shaf"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Shuffle</er>.]</ety> <def>To hobble or limp; to shuffle.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaffler</ent><br/
<hw>Shaf"fler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hobbler; one who
limps; a shuffer.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shafiite</ent><br/
<hw>Sha"fi*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A member of one of
the four sects of the Sunnites, or Orthodox Mohammedans; -- so called
from its founder, Mohammed al-<xex>Shafe<ium/</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaft</ent><br/
<hw>Shaft</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shaft</ets>,
<ets>schaft</ets>, AS. <ets>sceaft</ets>; akin to D. <ets>schacht</ets>,
OHG. <ets>scaft</ets>, G. <ets>schaft</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>skaft</ets>
handle, haft, Icel. <ets>skapt</ets>, and probably to L.
<ets>scapus</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/<?/, a staff. Probably
originally, a shaven or smoothed rod. Cf. <er>Scape</er>,
<er>Scepter</er>, <er>Shave</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The slender,
smooth stem of an arrow; hence, an arrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His sleep, his meat, his drink, is him bereft,<br/
That lean he wax, and dry as is a <qex>shaft</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>shaft</qex> hath three principal parts, the stele [stale],
the feathers, and the head.</q> <rj><qau>Ascham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The long handle of a spear or similar weapon; hence,
the weapon itself; (<mark>Fig.</mark>) anything regarded as a shaft to
be thrown or darted; <as>as, <ex>shafts</ex> of light</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the thunder,<br/
Winged with red lightning and impetuous rage,<br/
Perhaps hath spent his <qex>shafts</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some kinds of literary pursuits . . . have been attacked with all
the <qex>shafts</qex> of ridicule.</q> <rj><qau>V. Knox.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which resembles in some degree the stem or
handle of an arrow or a spear; a long, slender part, especially when
cylindrical.</def> Specifically: (a) <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The trunk,
stem, or stalk of a plant.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The stem or midrib of a feather.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The pole,
or tongue, of a vehicle; also, a thill.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The part
of a candlestick which supports its branches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt make a candlestick of pure gold . . . his
<qex>shaft</qex>, and his branches, his bowls, his knops, and his
flowers, shall be of the same.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xxv. 31.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <def>The handle or helve of certain tools, instruments,
etc., as a hammer, a whip, etc.</def> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>A pole,
especially a Maypole.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Stow.</au>
<sd>(g)</sd> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The body of a column; the
cylindrical pillar between the capital and base (see <xex>Illust.</xex>
of <er>Column</er>). Also, the part of a chimney above the roof. Also,
the spire of a steeple.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark> <au>Gwilt.</au>
<sd>(h)</sd> <def>A column, an obelisk, or other spire-shaped or
columnar monument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bid time and nature gently spare<br/
The <qex>shaft</qex> we raise to thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(i)</sd> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>A rod at the end of a
heddle.</def> <sd>(j)</sd> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A solid or hollow
cylinder or bar, having one or more journals on which it rests and
revolves, and intended to carry one or more wheels or other revolving
parts and to transmit power or motion; <as>as, the <ex>shaft</ex> of a
steam engine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A humming bird (<spn>Thaumastura
cora</spn>) having two of the tail feathers next to the middle ones very
long in the male; -- called also <altname>cora humming
bird</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>schacht</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A well-like excavation in the earth,
perpendicular or nearly so, made for reaching and raising ore, for
raising water, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A long passage for the admission or outlet of air;
an air shaft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The chamber of a blast furnace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Line shaft</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a main shaft
of considerable length, in a shop or factory, usually bearing a number
of pulleys by which machines are driven, commonly by means of
countershafts; -- called also <altname>line</altname>, or <altname>main
line</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shaft alley</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a passage extending from the engine room to the
stern, and containing the propeller shaft.</cd> -- <col><b>Shaft
furnace</b></col> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a furnace, in the form of a
chimney, which is charged at the top and tapped at the
bottom.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shafted</ent><br/
<hw>Shaft"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Furnished with a
shaft, or with shafts; <as>as, a <ex>shafted</ex> arch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Having a shaft; -- applied to a
spear when the head and the shaft are of different tinctures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shafting</ent><br/
<hw>Shaft"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>Shafts,
collectivelly; a system of connected shafts for communicating
motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaftment</ent><br/
<ent>Shaftman</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shaft"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shaft"ment</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sceaftmund</ets>.]</ety> <def>A measure of about six inches.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1322 --></p>

<p><ent>Shag</ent><br/
<hw>Shag</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sceacga</ets> a
bush of hair; akin to Icel. <ets>skegg</ets> the beard, Sw.
<ets>sk<aum/gg</ets>, Dan. <ets>skj<?/g</ets>. Cf. <er>Schock</er> of
hair.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Coarse hair or nap; rough, woolly
hair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>True Witney broadcloth, with its <qex>shag</qex> unshorn.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of cloth having a long, coarse nap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A kind of prepared tobacco cut
fine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of
cormorant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shag</ent><br/
<hw>Shag</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hairy; shaggy.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shag</ent><br/
<hw>Shag</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shagged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shagging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make hairy or shaggy; hence,
to make rough.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shag</qex> the green zone that bounds the boreal skies.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Barlow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- (Sport) To chase, as (a) to chase and return balls hit out of
bounds. (b) (Baseball) to catch fly balls for practise. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shagbark</ent><br/
<hw>Shag"bark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
rough-barked species of hickory (<spn>Carya alba</spn>), its nut. Called
also <altname>shellbark</altname>. See <er>Hickory</er>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The West Indian <spn>Pithecolobium micradenium</spn>,
a legiminous tree with a red coiled-up pod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shagebush</ent><br/
<hw>Shage"bush`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sackbut.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shagged</ent><br/
<hw>Shag"ged</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shaggy; rough.</def>
<au>Milton.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Shag"ged*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<au>Dr. H. More.</au></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shagginess</ent><br/
<hw>Shag"gi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being shaggy; roughness; shaggedness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaggy</ent><br/
<hw>Shag"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shaggier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shaggiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Shag</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Rough with long hair or wool.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>About his shoulders hangs the <qex>shaggy</qex> skin.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rough; rugged; jaggy.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[A rill] that winds unseen beneath the <qex>shaggy</qex> fell.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shag-haired</ent><br/
<hw>Shag"-haired`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having shaggy
hair.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shag-rag</ent><br/
<hw>Shag"-rag`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The unkempt and
ragged part of the community.</def> <mark>[Colloq. or Slang.]</mark>
<rj><au>R. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shagreen</ent><br/
<hw>Sha*green"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
chagrin.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shagreen</ent><br/
<hw>Sha*green"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>chagrin</ets>, It.
<ets>zigrino</ets>, fr. Turk. <ets>saghri</ets> the back of a horse or
other beast of burden, shagreen. Cf. <er>Chagrin</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of untanned leather prepared in Russia and the
East, from the skins of horses, asses, and camels, and grained so as to
be covered with small round granulations. This characteristic surface is
produced by pressing small seeds into the grain or hair side when moist,
and afterward, when dry, scraping off the roughness left between them,
and then, by soaking, causing the portions of the skin which had been
compressed or indented by the seeds to swell up into relief. It is used
for covering small cases and boxes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The skin of various small sharks and other fishes
when having small, rough, bony scales. The dogfishes of the genus
<gen>Scyllium</gen> furnish a large part of that used in the
arts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shagreened</ent><br/
<ent>Shagreen</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sha*green"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sha*greened"</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Made or covered with
the leather called shagreen.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>shagreen</xex> case of
lancets.<rdquo/ <rj><au>T. Hook.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Covered with rough scales or
points like those on shagreen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shah</ent><br/
<hw>Shah</hw> <pr>(sh<aum/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per.
<ets>sh<amac/h</ets> a king, sovereign, prince. Cf. <er>Checkmate</er>,
<er>Chess</er>, <er>Pasha</er>.]</ety> <def>A former title of the
supreme ruler in certain Eastern countries, especially Persia and
Iran.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>schah</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shah Nameh</b></col>. <ety>[Per., Book of Kings.]</ety>
<cd>A celebrated historical poem written by Firdousi, being the most
ancient in the modern Persian language.</cd> <rj><au>Brande &
C.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shahin</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sha*hin"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sh<amac/h<imac/n</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large and
swift Asiatic falcon (<spn>Falco pregrinator</spn>) highly valued in
falconry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaik</ent><br/
<hw>Shaik</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sheik</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shail</ent><br/
<hw>Shail</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS.
<ets>sceolh</ets> squinting, Icel. <ets>skj<amac/gr</ets> wry, oblique,
Dan. <ets>skele</ets> to squint.]</ety> <def>To walk sidewise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheitan</ent><br/
<ent>Shaitan</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Shai"tan</hw>, \'d8<hw>Shei"tan</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sheytan</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Hind. <ets>shait<amac/n</ets>, fr.
Ar. <ets>shai<tsdot/<amac/n</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <mark>Among
Mohammedans</mark>: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An evil spirit; the evil one; the
devil.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of bad disposition; a fiend.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Meteor.)</fld> <def>A dust storm.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shake</ent><br/
<hw>Shake</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>obs. p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shake</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shake</ent><br/
<hw>Shake</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Shook</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shaken</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, (<conjf>Shook</conjf>,
<mark>obs.</mark>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shaken</ets>,
<ets>schaken</ets>, AS. <ets>scacan</ets>, <ets>sceacan</ets>; akin to
Icel. & Sw. <ets>skaka</ets>, OS. <ets>skakan</ets>, to depart, to flee.
<root/161. Cf. <er>Shock</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
cause to move with quick or violent vibrations; to move rapidly one way
and the other; to make to tremble or shiver; to agitate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As a fig tree casteth her untimely figs, when she is
<qex>shaken</qex> of a mighty wind.</q> <rj><qau>Rev. vi.
13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ascend my chariot; guide the rapid wheels<br/
That <qex>shake</qex> heaven's basis.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To move from firmness; to weaken the stability
of; to cause to waver; to impair the resolution of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When his doctrines grew too strong to be <qex>shook</qex> by his
enemies, they persecuted his reputation.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy equal fear that my firm faith and love<br/
Can by his fraud be <qex>shaken</qex> or seduced.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To give a tremulous tone to; to
trill; <as>as, to <ex>shake</ex> a note in music</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To move or remove by agitating; to throw off by a
jolting or vibrating motion; to rid one's self of; -- generally with an
adverb, as <xex>off</xex>, <xex>out</xex>, etc.; <as>as, to
<ex>shake</ex> fruit down from a tree</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shake</qex> off the golden slumber of repose.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'Tis our fast intent<br/
To <qex>shake</qex> all cares and business from our age.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I could scarcely <qex>shake</qex> him out of my company.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shake a cask</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to
knock a cask to pieces and pack the staves.</cd> -- <col><b>To shake
hands</b></col>, <cd>to perform the customary act of civility by
clasping and moving hands, as an expression of greeting, farewell, good
will, agreement, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>To shake out a reef</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to untile the reef points and spread more
canvas.</cd> -- <col><b>To shake the bells</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Bell</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To shake the sails</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to luff up in the wind, causing the sails to
shiver.</cd> <au>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shake</ent><br/
<hw>Shake</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be agitated with a waving or
vibratory motion; to tremble; to shiver; to quake; to totter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Under his burning wheels<br/
The steadfast empyrean <qex>shook</qex> throughout,<br/
All but the throne itself of God.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What danger? Who 's that that <qex>shakes</qex> behind there?</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shaking piece</b></col>, <cd>a name given by butchers to
the piece of beef cut from the under side of the neck.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shake</ent><br/
<hw>Shake</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or
result of shaking; a vacillating or wavering motion; a rapid motion one
way and other; a trembling, quaking, or shivering; agitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The great soldier's honor was composed<br/
Of thicker stuff, which could endure a <qex>shake</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our salutations were very hearty on both sides, consisting of many
kind <qex>shakes</qex> of the hand.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fissure or crack in timber, caused by its being
dried too suddenly.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A fissure in rock or earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A rapid alternation of a principal
tone with another represented on the next degree of the staff above or
below it; a trill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>One of the staves of a hogshead
or barrel taken apart.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A shook of staves and headings.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The redshank; -- so called from
the nodding of its head while on the ground.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>No great shakes</b></col>, <cd>of no great
importance.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark> <au>Byron.</au> -- <col><b>The
shakes</b></col>, <cd>the fever and ague.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shakedown</ent><br/
<hw>Shake"down`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A temporary
substitute for a bed, as one made on the floor or on chairs; -- perhaps
originally from the shaking down of straw for this purpose.</def>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- shake down, v. t. subject to extortion. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shakefork</ent><br/
<hw>Shake"fork`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fork for shaking
hay; a pitchfork.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaken</ent><br/
<hw>Shak"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Caused to
shake; agitated; <as>as, a <ex>shaken</ex> bough</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cracked or checked; split. See <er>Shake</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor is the wood <qex>shaken</qex> or twisted.</q>
<rj><qau>Barroe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Impaired, as by a shock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaker</ent><br/
<hw>Shak"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A person
or thing that shakes, or by means of which something is
shaken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of a religious sect who do not marry, popularly
so called from the movements of the members in dancing, which forms a
part of their worship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The sect originated in England in 1747, and came to the
United States in 1774, under the leadership of Mother Ann Lee. The
Shakers are sometimes nicknamed <xex>Shaking Quakers</xex>, but they
differ from the Quakers in doctrine and practice. They style themselves
the <ldquo/United Society of Believers in Christ's Second
Appearing.<rdquo/ The sect is now confined in the United
States.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A variety of pigeon.</def>
<rj><au>P. J. Selby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shakeress</ent><br/
<hw>Shak"er*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female Shaker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shakerism</ent><br/
<hw>Shak"er*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Doctrines of the
Shakers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shakespearean</ent><br/
<hw>Shake*spear"e*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or in the style of, Shakespeare or his works.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>Shakespearian</asp>, <asp>Shakspearean</asp>,
<asp>Shakspearian</asp>, <asp>Shaksperean</asp>,
<asp>Shaksperian</asp>.etc.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shakiness</ent><br/
<hw>Shak"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
shaky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shakings</ent><br/
<hw>Shak"ings</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>Deck sweepings, refuse of cordage, canvas, etc.</def> <rj><au>Ham.
Nav. Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shako</ent><br/
<hw>Shak"o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hung.
<ets>cs<aacute/k<oacute/</ets>: cf. F. <ets>shako</ets>,
<ets>schako</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of military cap or
headdress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shakudo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Shak"u*do"</hw> <pr>(sh<acr/k"<oocr/*d<omac/")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Jap.]</ety> <def>An alloy of copper, invented by the
Japanese, having a very dark blue color approaching black.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaky</ent><br/
<hw>Shak"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shakier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shakiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Shaking or trembling;
<as>as, a <ex>shaky</ex> spot in a marsh; a <ex>shaky</ex>
hand</as>.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Full of shakes or cracks; cracked; <as>as,
<ex>shaky</ex> timber</as>.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Easily shaken; tottering; unsound; <as>as, a
<ex>shaky</ex> constitution; <ex>shaky</ex> business credit</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shale</ent><br/
<hw>Shale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>scealy</ets>,
<ets>scalu</ets>. See <er>Scalme</er>, and cf. <er>Shell</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A shell or husk; a cod or pod.</def> <ldquo/The green
<xex>shales</xex> of a bean.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[G. <ets>shale</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>A fine-grained sedimentary rock of a thin, laminated, and often
friable, structure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bituminous shale</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Bituminous</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shale</ent><br/
<hw>Shale</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To take off the shell or coat of;
to shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Life, in its upper grades, was bursting its shell, or was
<qex>shaling</qex> off its husk.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shall</ent><br/
<hw>Shall</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & auxiliary.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Should</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[OE. <ets>shal</ets>, <ets>schal</ets>, imp. <ets>sholde</ets>,
<ets>scholde</ets>, AS. <ets>scal</ets>, <ets>sceal</ets>, I am obliged,
imp. <ets>scolde</ets>, <ets>sceolde</ets>, inf. <ets>sculan</ets>; akin
to OS. <ets>skulan</ets>, pres. <ets>skal</ets>, imp. <ets>skolda</ets>,
D. <ets>zullen</ets>, pres. <ets>zal</ets>, imp. <ets>zoude</ets>,
<ets>zou</ets>, OHG. <ets>solan</ets>, <ets>scolan</ets>, pres.
<ets>scal</ets>, <ets>sol</ets>. imp. <ets>scolta</ets>,
<ets>solta</ets>, G. <ets>sollen</ets>, pres. <ets>soll</ets>, imp.
<ets>sollte</ets>, Icel. <ets>skulu</ets>, pres. <ets>skal</ets>, imp.
<ets>skyldi</ets>, SW. <ets>skola</ets>, pres. <ets>skall</ets>, imp.
<ets>skulle</ets>, Dan. <ets>skulle</ets>, pres. <ets>skal</ets>, imp.
<ets>skulde</ets>, Goth. <ets>skulan</ets>, pres. <ets>skal</ets>, imp.
<ets>skulda</ets>, and to AS. <ets>scyld</ets> guilt, G.
<ets>schuld</ets> guilt, fault, debt, and perhaps to L.
<ets>scelus</ets> crime.]</ety> <note>[<xex>Shall</xex> is defective,
having no infinitive, imperative, or participle.]</note> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To owe; to be under obligation for.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/By the faith I <xex>shall</xex> to God<rdquo/ <rj><au>Court of
Love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be obliged; must.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Me athinketh [I am sorry] that I <xex>shall</xex> rehearse it
her.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>As an auxiliary, <xex>shall</xex> indicates a duty
or necessity whose obligation is derived from the person speaking;
<as>as, you <ex>shall</ex> go; he <xex>shall</xex> go</as>; that is, I
order or promise your going. It thus ordinarily expresses, in the second
and third persons, a command, a threat, or a promise. If the auxillary
be emphasized, the command is made more imperative, the promise or that
more positive and sure. It is also employed in the language of prophecy;
<as>as, <ldquo/the day <ex>shall</ex> come when . . . , <rdquo/</as>
since a promise or threat and an authoritative prophecy nearly coincide
in significance. In <xex>shall</xex> with the first person, the
necessity of the action is sometimes implied as residing elsewhere than
in the speaker; <as>as, I <ex>shall</ex> suffer; we <xex>shall</xex>
see</as>; and there is always a less distinct and positive assertion of
his volition than is indicated by <xex>will</xex>. <ldquo/I
<xex>shall</xex> go<rdquo/ implies nearly a simple futurity; more
exactly, a foretelling or an expectation of my going, in which,
naturally enough, a certain degree of plan or intention may be included;
emphasize the <xex>shall</xex>, and the event is described as certain to
occur, and the expression approximates in meaning to our emphatic
<ldquo/I <xex>will</xex> go.<rdquo/ In a question, the relation of
speaker and source of obligation is of course transferred to the person
addressed; as, <ldquo/<xex>Shall</xex> you go?<rdquo/ (answer, <ldquo/I
<xex>shall</xex> go<rdquo/); <ldquo/<xex>Shall</xex> he go?<rdquo/
<it>i. e.</it>, <ldquo/Do you require or promise his going?<rdquo/
(answer, <ldquo/He <xex>shall</xex> go<rdquo/.) The same relation is
transferred to either second or third person in such phrases as
<ldquo/You say, or think, you <xex>shall</xex> go;<rdquo/ <ldquo/He
says, or thinks, he <xex>shall</xex> go.<rdquo/ After a conditional
conjunction (as <xex>if</xex>, whether) <xex>shall</xex> is used in all
persons to express futurity simply; <as>as, if I, you, or he
<ex>shall</ex> say they are right</as>. <xex>Should</xex> is everywhere
used in the same connection and the same senses as <xex>shall</xex>, as
its imperfect. It also expresses duty or moral obligation; <as>as, he
<ex>should</ex> do it whether he will or not</as>. In the early English,
and hence in our English Bible, <ex>shall</ex> is the auxiliary mainly
used, in all the persons, to express simple futurity. (Cf.
<er>Will</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>) <ex>Shall</ex> may be used
elliptically; thus, with an adverb or other word expressive of motion
<xex>go</xex> may be omitted.</def> <ldquo/He to England
<xex>shall</xex> along with you.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Shall</xex> and <xex>will</xex> are often
confounded by inaccurate speakers and writers. Say: I <xex>shall</xex>
be glad to see you. <xex>Shall</xex> I do this? <xex>Shall</xex> I help
you? (not <xex>Will</xex> I do this?) See <er>Will</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shalli</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Challis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallon</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An
evergreen shrub (<spn>Gaultheria Shallon</spn>) of Northwest America;
also, its fruit. See <er>Salal-berry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shalloon</ent><br/
<hw>Shal*loon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>chalon</ets>, from <ets>Ch<acir/lons</ets>, in France, where it was
first made.]</ety> <def>A thin, loosely woven, twilled worsted
stuff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In blue <qex>shalloon</qex> shall Hannibal be clad.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallop</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"lop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>chaloupe</ets>, probably from D. <ets>sloep</ets>. Cf.
<er>Sloop</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A boat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[She] thrust the <qex>shallop</qex> from the floating strand.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <xex>shallop</xex> is applied to boats of all
sizes, from a light canoe up to a large boat with masts and
sails.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallot</ent><br/
<hw>Shal*lot"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>eschalote</ets> (for <ets>escalone</ets>), F.
<ets><eacute/chalote</ets>. See <er>Scallion</er>, and cf.
<er>Eschalot</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A small kind of onion
(<spn>Allium Ascalonicum</spn>) growing in clusters, and ready for
gathering in spring; a scallion, or eschalot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Shallower</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Shallowest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schalowe</ets>, probably originally, sloping or shelving; cf. Icel.
<ets>skj<amac/lgr</ets> wry, squinting, AS. <ets>sceolh</ets>, D. & G.
<ets>scheel</ets>, OHG. <ets>schelah</ets>. Cf. <er>Shelve</er> to
slope, <er>Shoal</er> shallow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not deep; having
little depth; shoal.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Shallow</xex> brooks, and rivers
wide.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not deep in tone.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sound perfecter and not so <qex>shallow</qex> and jarring.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not intellectually deep; not profound; not
penetrating deeply; simple; not wise or knowing; ignorant; superficial;
<as>as, a <ex>shallow</ex> mind; <ex>shallow</ex>
learning</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king was neither so <qex>shallow</qex>, nor so ill advertised,
as not to perceive the intention of the French king.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Deep versed in books, and <qex>shallow</qex> in himself.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place in a body of
water where the water is not deep; a shoal; a flat; a shelf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A swift stream is not heard in the channel, but upon
<qex>shallows</qex> of gravel.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dashed on the <qex>shallows</qex> of the moving sand.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The rudd.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make shallow.</def>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become shallow, as
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow-bodied</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low-bod`ied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>Having a moderate depth of hold; -- said of a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow-brained</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low-brained`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Weak in
intellect; foolish; empty-headed.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow-hearted</ent><br/

<hw>Shal"low-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Incapable of
deep feeling.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallowly</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shallow
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallowness</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
shallow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow-pated</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low-pat`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Shallow-brained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shallow-waisted</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"low-waist`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Having a flush deck, or with only a moderate
depression amidships; -- said of a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shalm</ent><br/
<hw>Shalm</hw> <pr>(sh<add/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shawm</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Knolles.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shalt</ent><br/
<hw>Shalt</hw> <pr>(sh<acr/lt)</pr>, <def><pos>2d per. sing.</pos> of
<er>Shall</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaly</ent><br/
<hw>Shal"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling shale in
structure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sham</ent><br/
<hw>Sham</hw> <pr>(sh<acr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Originally the
same word as <ets>shame</ets>, hence, a disgrace, a trick. See
<er>Shame</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which
deceives expectation; any trick, fraud, or device that deludes and
disappoints; a make-believe; delusion; imposture; humbug.</def> <ldquo/A
mere <xex>sham</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp. Stillingfleet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Believe who will the solemn <qex>sham</qex>, not I.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A false front, or removable ornamental
covering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pillow sham</b></col>, <cd>a covering to be laid on a
pillow.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sham</ent><br/
<hw>Sham</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>False; counterfeit; pretended;
feigned; unreal; <as>as, a <ex>sham</ex> fight</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They scorned the <qex>sham</qex> independence proffered to them by
the Athenians.</q> <rj><qau>Jowett (Thucyd)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sham</ent><br/
<hw>Sham</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shammed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shamming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To trick; to cheat;
to deceive or delude with false pretenses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fooled and <qex>shammed</qex> into a conviction.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To obtrude by fraud or imposition.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We must have a care that we do not . . . <qex>sham</qex> fallacies
upon the world for current reason.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To assume the manner and character of; to imitate;
to ape; to feign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To sham Abram</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To sham
Abraham</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to feign sickness; to malinger. Hence a
malingerer is called, in sailors' cant, <xex>Sham Abram</xex>, or
<xex>Sham Abraham</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sham</ent><br/
<hw>Sham</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make false pretenses; to
deceive; to feign; to impose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wondering . . . whether those who lectured him were such fools as
they professed to be, or were only <qex>shamming</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shama</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sha"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>sh<amac/m<amac/</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A saxicoline
singing bird (<spn>Kittacincla macroura</spn>) of India, noted for the
sweetness and power of its song. In confinement it imitates the notes of
other birds and various animals with accuracy. Its head, neck, back,
breast, and tail are glossy black, the rump white, the under parts
chestnut.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaman</ent><br/
<hw>Sha"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the native
name.]</ety> <def>A priest of Shamanism; a wizard among the
Shamanists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1323 --></p>

<p><ent>Shamanic</ent><br/
<hw>Sha*man"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Shamanism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamanism</ent><br/
<hw>Sha"man*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The type of
religion which once prevalied among all the Ural-Altaic peoples
(Tungusic, Mongol, and Turkish), and which still survives in various
parts of Northern Asia. The Shaman, or wizard priest, deals with good as
well as with evil spirits, especially the good spirits of
ancestors.</def> <rj><au>Encyc. Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamanist</ent><br/
<hw>Sha"man*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An adherent of
Shamanism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamble</ent><br/
<hw>Sham"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schamel</ets> a bench, stool, AS. <ets>scamel</ets>,
<ets>sceamol</ets>, a bench, form, stool, fr. L. <ets>scamellum</ets>,
dim. of <ets>scamnum</ets> a bench, stool.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>One of a succession of niches or platforms, one
above another, to hold ore which is thrown successively from platform to
platform, and thus raised to a higher level.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A place where butcher's meat is
sold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As summer flies are in the <qex>shambles</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A place for slaughtering animals
for meat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To make a <qex>shambles</qex> of the parliament house.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamble</ent><br/
<hw>Sham"ble</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shambled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shambling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OD.
<ets>schampelen</ets> to slip, <ets>schampen</ets> to slip away, escape.
Cf. <er>Scamble</er>, <er>Scamper</er>.]</ety> <def>To walk awkwardly
and unsteadily, as if the knees were weak; to shuffle along.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shambling</ent><br/
<hw>Sham"bling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Characterized by an
awkward, irregular pace; <as>as, a <ex>shambling</ex> trot;
<ex>shambling</ex> legs.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shambling</ent><br/
<hw>Sham"bling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An awkward, irregular
gait.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shame</ent><br/
<hw>Shame</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shame</ets>,
<ets>schame</ets>, AS. <ets>scamu</ets>, <ets>sceamu</ets>; akin to OS.
& OHG. <ets>scama</ets>, G. <ets>scham</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sk<oum/mm</ets>, <ets>shkamm</ets>, Sw. & Dan. <ets>skam</ets>, D.
& G. <ets>schande</ets>, Goth. <ets>skanda</ets> shame, <ets>skaman
sik</ets> to be ashamed; perhaps from a root <ets>skam</ets> meaning to
cover, and akin to the root (<ets>kam</ets>) of G. <ets>hemd</ets>
shirt, E. <ets>chemise</ets>. Cf. <er>Sham</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A painful sensation excited by a consciousness of guilt or
impropriety, or of having done something which injures reputation, or of
the exposure of that which nature or modesty prompts us to
conceal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>HIde, for <qex>shame</qex>,<br/
Romans, your grandsires' images,<br/
That blush at their degenerate progeny.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Have you no modesty, no maiden <qex>shame</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Reproach incurred or suffered; dishonor; ignominy;
derision; contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye have borne the <qex>shame</qex> of the heathen.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. xxxvi. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Honor and <qex>shame</qex> from no condition rise.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And every woe a tear can claim<br/
Except an erring sister's <qex>shame</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The cause or reason of shame; that which brings
reproach, and degrades a person in the estimation of others;
disgrace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O C<?/sar, what a wounding <qex>shame</qex> is this!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Guides who are the <qex>shame</qex> of religion.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The parts which modesty requires to be covered; the
private parts.</def> <rj><au>Isa. xlvii. 3.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>For shame!</b></col> <cd>you should be ashamed; shame on
you!</cd> -- <col><b>To put to shame</b></col>, <cd>to cause to feel
shame; to humiliate; to disgrace.</cd> <ldquo/Let them be driven
backward and <xex>put to shame</xex> that wish me evil.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Ps. xl. 14.</au></rj> </cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shame</ent><br/
<hw>Shame</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shamed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shaming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make ashamed; to
excite in (a person) a comsciousness of guilt or impropriety, or of
conduct derogatory to reputation; to put to shame.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Were there but one righteous in the world, he would . . .
<qex>shame</qex> the world, and not the world him.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover with reproach or ignominy; to dishonor; to
disgrace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And with foul cowardice his carcass <qex>shame</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To mock at; to deride.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye have <qex>shamed</qex> the counsel of the poor.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xiv. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shame</ent><br/
<hw>Shame</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>scamian</ets>,
<ets>sceamian</ets>. See <er>Shame</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To be
ashamed; to feel shame.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do <qex>shame</qex><br/
To think of what a noble strain you are.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamefaced</ent><br/
<hw>Shame"faced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>shamefast</ets>; AS. <ets>scamfaest</ets>. See <er>Shame</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and <er>Fast</er> firm.]</ety> <def>Easily confused or
put out of countenance; diffident; bashful; modest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your <qex>shamefaced</qex> virtue shunned the people's prise.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Shamefaced</xex> was once <xex>shamefast</xex>,
<xex>shamefacedness</xex> was <xex>shamefastness</xex>, like
<xex>steadfast</xex> and <xex>steadfastness</xex>; but the ordinary
manifestations of shame being by the face, have brought it to its
present orthography. <au>Trench.</au></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Shame"faced</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Shame"faced`ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamefast</ent><br/
<hw>Shame"fast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>scamf<ae/st</ets>.]</ety> <def>Modest; shamefaced.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Shame"fast*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Shame"fast*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> See
<er>Shamefaced</er>.</wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shamefast</qex> she was in maiden
<qex>shamefastness</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Conscience] is a blushing <qex>shamefast</qex> spirit.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Modest apparel with <qex>shamefastness</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>1 Tim.
ii. 9 (Rev. Ver.).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shameful</ent><br/
<hw>Shame"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Bringing
shame or disgrace; injurious to reputation; disgraceful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His naval preparations were not more surprising than his quick and
<qex>shameful</qex> retreat.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exciting the feeling of shame in others; indecent;
<as>as, a <ex>shameful</ex> picture; a <ex>shameful</ex>
sight.</as></def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Disgraceful; reproachful; indecent; unbecoming;
degrading; scandalous; ignominious; infamous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Shame"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Shame"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shameless</ent><br/
<hw>Shame"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>scamle<aacute/s</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Destitute of shame;
wanting modesty; brazen-faced; insensible to disgrace.</def> <ldquo/Such
<xex>shameless</xex> bards we have.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shame enough to shame thee, wert thou not
<qex>shameless</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Indicating want of modesty, or sensibility to
disgrace; indecent; <as>as, a <ex>shameless</ex> picture or
poem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Impudent; unblushing; audacious; immodest;
indecent; indelicate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Shame"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Shame"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shame-proof</ent><br/
<hw>Shame"-proof`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Shameless.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamer</ent><br/
<hw>Sham"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, disgraces, or makes ashamed.</def> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shammer</ent><br/
<hw>Sham"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who shams; an
impostor.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shammy</ent><br/
<hw>Sham"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>chamious</ets> a chamois, shammy leather. See
<er>Chamois</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
chamois.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A soft, pliant leather, prepared originally from the
skin of the chamois, but now made also from the skin of the sheep, goat,
kid, deer, and calf. See <er>Shamoying</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>chamois</asp>, <asp>shamoy</asp>, and
<asp>shamois</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamoy</ent><br/
<ent>Shamois</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sham"ois</hw>, <hw>Sham"oy</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Shammy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamoying</ent><br/
<hw>Sha*moy"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Shammy</er>.]</ety> <def>A process used in preparing certain kinds
of leather, which consists in frizzing the skin, and working oil into it
to supply the place of the astringent (tannin, alum, or the like)
ordinarily used in tanning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shampoo</ent><br/
<hw>Sham*poo"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shampooed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shampooing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>ch<amac/mpn<amac/</ets> to press, to squeeze.]</ety>
<altsp>[Writing also <asp>champoo</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
press or knead the whole surface of the body of (a person), and at the
same time to stretch the limbs and joints, in connection with the hot
bath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wash throughly and rub the head of (a person),
with the fingers, using either soap, or a soapy preparation, for the
more thorough cleansing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shampoo</ent><br/
<hw>Sham*poo"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of shampooing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shampooer</ent><br/
<hw>Sham*poo"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
shampoos.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shamrock</ent><br/
<hw>Sham"rock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>seamrog</ets>, <ets>seamar</ets>, trefoil, white clover, white
honeysuckle; akin to Gael. <ets>seamrag</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A trifoliate plant used as a national emblem by the Irish. The
legend is that St. Patrick once plucked a leaf of it for use in
illustrating the doctrine of the trinity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The original plant was probably a kind of wood sorrel
(<spn>Oxalis Acetocella</spn>); but now the name is given to the white
clover (<spn>Trifolium repens</spn>), and the black medic (<spn>Medicago
lupulina</spn>).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shandrydan</ent><br/
<hw>Shan"dry*dan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A jocosely
depreciative name for a vehicle.</def> <mark>[Ireland]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shandygaff</ent><br/
<hw>Shan"dy*gaff</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mixture of
strong beer and ginger beer.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shanghai</ent><br/
<hw>Shang`hai"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shanghaied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shanghaiing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To intoxicate and
ship (a person) as a sailor while in this condition.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>shanghae</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shanghai</ent><br/
<hw>Shang`hai"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A large and tall breed of domestic fowl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shank</ent><br/
<hw>Shank</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Chank</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shank</ent><br/
<hw>Shank</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shanke</ets>,
<ets>schanke</ets>, <ets>schonke</ets>, AS. <ets>scanca</ets>,
<ets>sceanca</ets>, <ets>sconca</ets>, <ets>sceonca</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>schonk</ets> a bone, G. <ets>schenkel</ets> thigh, shank,
<ets>schinken</ets> ham, OHG. <ets>scincha</ets> shank, Dan. & Sw.
<ets>skank</ets>. <root/161. Cf. <er>Skink</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The part of the leg from the knee to the foot; the
shin; the shin bone; also, the whole leg.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His youthful hose, well saved, a world too wide<br/
For his shrunk <qex>shank</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, that part of an instrument, tool, or other
thing, which connects the acting part with a handle or other part, by
which it is held or moved.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>That
part of a key which is between the bow and the part which enters the
wards of the lock.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The middle part of an anchor,
or that part which is between the ring and the arms.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>That part of a hoe, rake, knife, or the like, by which it is
secured to a handle.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A loop forming an eye to a
button.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The space between two channels of
the Doric triglyph.</def> <rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>A large ladle for molten
metal, fitted with long bars for handling it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>The body of a type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Shoemaking)</fld> <def>The part of the sole beneath
the instep connecting the broader front part with the heel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A wading bird with long legs;
<as>as, the green-legged <ex>shank</ex>, or knot; the yellow
<ex>shank</ex>, or tattler</as>; -- called also
<altname>shanks</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Flat-nosed pliers, used by
opticians for nipping off the edges of pieces of glass to make them
round.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shank painter</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a short
rope or chain which holds the shank of an anchor against the side of a
vessel when it is secured for a voyage.</cd> -- <col><b>To ride shank's
mare</b></col>, <cd>to go on foot; to walk.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shank</ent><br/
<hw>Shank</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fall off, as a leaf, flower, or
capsule, on account of disease affecting the supporting footstalk; --
usually followed by <xex>off</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shankbeer</ent><br/
<hw>Shank"beer`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Schenkbeer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shanked</ent><br/
<hw>Shanked</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
shank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shanker</ent><br/
<hw>Shank"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Chancre</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shanny</ent><br/
<hw>Shan"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shannies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European smooth blenny (<spn>Blennius
pholis</spn>). It is olive-green with irregular black spots, and without
appendages on the head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shan't</ent><br/
<hw>Shan't</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A contraction of <xex>shall
not</xex>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shanty</ent><br/
<hw>Shan"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Jaunty; showy.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shanty</ent><br/
<hw>Shan"ty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>;<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Shanties</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Said to be fr. Ir. <ets>sean</ets> old +
<ets>tig</ets>. a house.]</ety> <def>A small, mean dwelling; a rough,
slight building for temporary use; a hut.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shanty</ent><br/
<hw>Shan"ty</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To inhabit a shanty.</def>
<rj><au>S. H. Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shapable</ent><br/
<hw>Shap"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
may be shaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Shapely.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Round and
<xex>shapable</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De Foe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shape</ent><br/
<hw>Shape</hw> <pr>(sh<amac/p)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Shaped</conjf> <pr>(sh<amac/pt)</pr>;
<pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shaped</conjf> or <conjf>Shapen</conjf>
<pr>(sh<amac/p"'n)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shaping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shapen</ets>,
<ets>schapen</ets>, AS. <ets>sceapian</ets>. The p. p. <ets>shapen</ets>
is from the strong verb, AS. <ets>scieppan</ets>, <ets>scyppan</ets>,
<ets>sceppan</ets>, p. p. <ets>sceapen</ets>. See <er>Shape</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To form or create; especially, to
mold or make into a particular form; to give proper form or figure
to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I was <qex>shapen</qex> in iniquity.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. li.
5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Grace <qex>shaped</qex> her limbs, and beauty decked her face.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To adapt to a purpose; to regulate; to adjust; to
direct; <as>as, to <ex>shape</ex> the course of a vessel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To the stream, when neither friends, nor force,<br/
Nor speed nor art avail, he <qex>shapes</qex> his course.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Charmed by their eyes, their manners I acquire,<br/
And <qex>shape</qex> my foolishness to their desire.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To imagine; to conceive; to call forth
(ideas).</def> <mark>[archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Oft my jealousy<br/
<qex>Shapes</qex> faults that are not.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To design; to prepare; to plan; to
arrange.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When <qex>shapen</qex> was all this conspiracy,<br/
From point to point.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shaping machine</b></col>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Shaper</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To shape one's self</b></col>, <cd>to
prepare; to make ready.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will early <qex>shape me</qex> therefor.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shape</ent><br/
<hw>Shape</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To suit; to be
adjusted or conformable.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shape</ent><br/
<hw>Shape</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shap</ets>,
<ets>schap</ets>, AS. <ets>sceap</ets> in <ets>gesceap</ets> creation,
creature, fr. the root of <ets>scieppan</ets>, <ets>scyppan</ets>,
<ets>sceppan</ets>, to shape, to do, to effect; akin to OS.
gi<ets>skeppian</ets>, OFries. <ets>skeppa</ets>, D.
<ets>scheppen</ets>, G. <ets>schaffen</ets>, OHG. <ets>scaffan</ets>,
<ets>scepfen</ets>, <ets>skeffen</ets>, Icer. <ets>skapa</ets>,
<ets>skepja</ets>, Dan. <ets>skabe</ets>, <ets>skaffe</ets>, Sw.
<ets>skapa</ets>, <ets>skaffa</ets>, Goth. ga<ets>skapjan</ets>, and
perhaps to E. <ets>shave</ets>, v. Cf. <er>-ship</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Character or construction of a thing as determining its
external appearance; outward aspect; make; figure; form; guise; <as>as,
the <ex>shape</ex> of a tree; the <ex>shape</ex> of the head; an elegant
<ex>shape</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He beat me grievously, in the <qex>shape</qex> of a woman.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which has form or figure; a figure; an
appearance; a being.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before the gates three sat,<br/
On either side, a formidable <qex>shape</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A model; a pattern; a mold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Form of embodiment, as in words; form, as of thought
or conception; concrete embodiment or example, as of some quality.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Dress for disguise; guise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Look better on this virgin, and consider<br/
This Persian <qex>shape</qex> laid by, and she appearing<br/
In a Greekish dress.</q> <rj><qau>Messinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Iron Manuf.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A rolled or
hammered piece, as a bar, beam, angle iron, etc., having a cross section
different from merchant bar.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A piece which has
been roughly forged nearly to the form it will receive when completely
forged or fitted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To take shape</b></col>, <cd>to assume a definite
form.</cd></cs> <-- in shape, having a good muscle tone; healthy. Get
into shape, to exercise so as to acquire a good muscle tone. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shapeless</ent><br/
<hw>Shape"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of shape or regular
form; wanting symmetry of dimensions; misshapen; -- opposed to
<ant>shapely</ant>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Shape"less*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shapeless</qex> rock, or hanging precipice.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shapeliness</ent><br/
<hw>Shape"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being shapely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shapely</ent><br/
<hw>Shape"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shapelier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shapeliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Well-formed; having
a regular shape; comely; symmetrical.</def> <rj><au>T.
Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Waste sandy valleys, once perplexed with thorn,<br/
The spiry fir and <qex>shapely</qex> box adorn.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where the <qex>shapely</qex> column stood.</q>
<rj><qau>Couper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fit; suitable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shaply</qex> for to be an alderman.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaper</ent><br/
<hw>Shap"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
shapes; <as>as, the <ex>shaper</ex> of one's fortunes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The secret of those old <qex>shapers</qex> died with them.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which shapes; a machine for giving a particular
form or outline to an object.</def> Specifically; <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Metal Working)</fld> <def>A kind of planer in which the tool,
instead of the work, receives a reciprocating motion, usually from a
crank.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Wood Working)</fld> <def>A machine with
a vertically revolving cutter projecting above a flat table top, for
cutting irregular outlines, moldings, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shapoo</ent><br/
<hw>Sha"poo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
oorial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaps</ent><br/
<hw>Shaps</hw> <pr>(sh<acr/ps)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Shortened
fr. <ets>chaparajos</ets>. Cf. <er>Chaps</er>.]</ety>
<def>Chaparajos.</def> <mark>[Western U. S.]</mark></p>

<p><q>A pair of gorgeous buckskin <qex>shaps</qex>, embroidered up the
sides and adorned with innumerable ermine skins.</q> <rj><qau>The
Century.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shard</ent><br/
<hw>Shard</hw> <pr>(sh<aum/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A plant;
chard.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shard</ent><br/
<hw>Shard</hw> <pr>(sh<aum/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sceard</ets>, properly a p. p. from the root of <ets>scearn</ets>
to shear, to cut; akin to D. <ets>schaard</ets> a fragment, G.
<ets>scharte</ets> a notch, Icel. <ets>skar<edh/</ets>. See
<er>Shear</er>, and cf. <er>Sherd</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sheard</asp>, and <asp>sherd</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
piece or fragment of an earthen vessel, or a like brittle substance, as
the shell of an egg or snail.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The precious dish<br/
Broke into <qex>shards</qex> of beauty on the board.</q> <rj><qau>E.
Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The hard wing case of a
beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are his <qex>shards</qex>, and he their beetle.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A gap in a fence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Stanyhurst.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A boundary; a division.</def> <mark>[Obs. &
R.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shard-borne</ent><br/
<hw>Shard"-borne`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Borne on shards
or scaly wing cases.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>shard-borne</xex>
beetle.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharded</ent><br/
<hw>Shard"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having elytra,
as a beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shardy</ent><br/
<hw>Shard"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having, or consisting
of, shards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Share</ent><br/
<hw>Share</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>schar</ets>,
AS. <ets>scear</ets>; akin to OHG. <ets>scaro</ets>, G.
<ets>schar</ets>, pflug<ets>shar</ets>, and E. <ets>shear</ets>, v. See
<er>Shear</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The part (usually an iron or
steel plate) of a plow which cuts the ground at the bottom of a furrow;
a plowshare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The part which opens the ground for the reception of
the seed, in a machine for sowing seed.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Share</ent><br/
<hw>Share</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>share</ets>, AS.
<ets>scearu</ets>, <ets>scaru</ets>, fr. <ets>sceran</ets> to shear,
cut. See <er>Shear</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A certain
quantity; a portion; a part; a division; <as>as, a small <ex>share</ex>
of prudence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, the part allotted or belonging to one,
of any property or interest owned by a number; a portion among others;
an apportioned lot; an allotment; a dividend.</def> <ldquo/My
<xex>share</xex> of fame.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, one of a certain number of equal portions
into which any property or invested capital is divided; <as>as, a ship
owned in ten <ex>shares</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The pubes; the sharebone.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To go shares</b></col>, <cd>to partake; to be equally
concerned.</cd> -- <col><b>Share and share alike</b></col>, <cd>in equal
shares.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Share</ent><br/
<hw>Share</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sharing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To part among two or
more; to distribute in portions; to divide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Suppose I <qex>share</qex> my fortune equally between my children
and a stranger.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1324 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To partake of, use, or experience, with others; to
have a portion of; to take and possess in common; <as>as, to
<ex>share</ex> a shelter with another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>While avarice and rapine share</qex> the land.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cut; to shear; to cleave; to divide.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shared</qex> visage hangs on equal sides.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Share</ent><br/
<hw>Share</hw> <pr>(sh<acir/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To have part;
to receive a portion; to partake, enjoy, or suffer with
others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A right of inheritance gave every one a title to <qex>share</qex>
in the goods of his father.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharebeam</ent><br/
<hw>Share"beam`</hw> <pr>(sh<acir/r"b<emac/m`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The part of the plow to which the share is attached.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharebone</ent><br/
<hw>Share"bone`</hw> <pr>(sh<acir/r"b<omac/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The pubic bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharebroker</ent><br/
<hw>Share"bro`ker</hw> <pr>(sh<acir/r"br<omac/`k<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A broker who deals in railway or other shares and
securities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharecropper</ent><br/
<hw>Share"crop`per</hw> <pr>(sh<acir/r"kr<ocr/p`p<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A farmer who rents a parcel of land from its owner
and pays a portion of the crop as the rent.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shareholder</ent><br/
<hw>Share"hold`er</hw> <pr>(sh<acir/r"h<omac/ld`<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who holds or owns a share or shares in a joint
fund or property.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharer</ent><br/
<hw>Shar"er</hw> <pr>(sh<acir/r"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who shares; a participator; a partaker; also, a divider; a
distributer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharewort</ent><br/
<hw>Share"wort`</hw> <pr>(sh<acir/r"w<ucir/rt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A composite plant (<spn>Aster Tripolium</spn>)
growing along the seacoast of Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shark</ent><br/
<hw>Shark</hw> <pr>(sh<aum/rk)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of uncertain
origin; perhaps through OF. fr. <ets>carcharus</ets> a kind of dogfish,
Gr. <grk>karchari`as</grk>, so called from its sharp teeth, fr.
<grk>ka`rcharos</grk> having sharp or jagged teeth; or perhaps named
from its rapacity (cf. <er>Shark</er>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos>); cf. Corn.
<ets>scarceas</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one
of numerous species of elasmobranch fishes of the order
<ord>Plagiostomi</ord>, found in all seas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Some sharks, as the basking shark and the whale shark,
grow to an enormous size, the former becoming forty feet or more, and
the latter sixty feet or more, in length. Most of them are harmless to
man, but some are exceedingly voracious. The man-eating sharks mostly
belong to the genera <gen>Carcharhinus</gen>, <gen>Carcharodon</gen>,
and related genera. They have several rows of large sharp teeth with
serrated edges, as the great white shark (<spn>Carcharodon
carcharias</spn> <it>or</it> <spn>Carcharodon Rondeleti</spn>) of
tropical seas, and the great blue shark (<spn>Carcharhinus glaucus</spn>
syn. <spn>Prionace glauca</spn>) of all tropical and temperate seas. The
former sometimes becomes thirty-six feet long, and is the most voracious
and dangerous species known. The rare man-eating shark of the United
States coast (<spn>Carcharodon Atwoodi</spn>) is thought by some to be a
variety, or the young, of <spn>Carcharodon carcharias</spn>. The dusky
shark (<spn>Carcharhinus obscurus</spn>) is a common species on the
coast of the United States of moderate size and not dangerous. It feeds
on shellfish and bottom fishes.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note>The original 1913 Webster also mentioned a <ldquo/smaller blue
shark (<spn>C. caudatus</spn>)<rdquo/, but this species could not be
found mentioned on the Web (August 2002). The following is a list of
Atlantic Ocean sharks:<br/
* * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *<br/
Common and Scientific Names of Atlantic Sharks<br/
* * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *<br/
from <ldquo/Our Living Oceans 1995<rdquo/ (published by the National
Printing Office):<br/
NMFS. 1999. Our Living Oceans. Report on the status of U.S. living
marine resources, 1999. U.S. Dep. Commer., NOAA Tech. Memo.
NMFS-F/SPO-41, on-line version,
https://web.archive.org/web/20170513162849/http://spo.nwr.noaa.gov/olo99.htm.<br/
<br/
(the following list is found at at
https://web.archive.org/web/20010614193559/http://spo.nwr.noaa.gov/app5.pdf)
<br/
(1) Pelagic Sharks<br/
<br/
Thresher shark (<spn>Alopias vulpinus</spn>)<br/
Bigeye thresher (<spn>Alopias superciliosus</spn>)<br/
Oceanic whitetip shark (<spn>Carcharhinus longimanus</spn>)<br/
Sevengill shark (<spn>Heptrachias perlo</spn>)<br/
Sixgill shark (<spn>Hexanchus griseus</spn>)<br/
Bigeye sixgill shark (<spn>Hexanchus vitulus</spn>)<br/
Shortfin mako (<spn>Isurus oxyrinchus</spn>)<br/
Longfin mako (<spn>Isurus paucus</spn>)<br/
Porbeagle (<spn>Lamna nasus</spn>)<br/
Blue shark (<spn>Prionace glauca</spn>)<br/
<br/
(2)Large Coastal Sharks<br/
<br/
Sandbar shark (<spn>Carcharhinus plumbeus</spn>)<br/
Reef shark (<spn>Carcharhinus perezi</spn>)<br/
Blacktip shark (<spn>Carcharhinus limbatus</spn>)<br/
Dusky shark (<spn>Carcharhinus obscurus</spn>)<br/
Spinner shark (<spn>Carcharhinus brevipinna</spn>)<br/
Silky shark (<spn>Carcharhinus falciformis</spn>)<br/
Bull shark (<spn>Carcharhinus leucas</spn>)<br/
Bignose shark (<spn>Carcharhinus altimus</spn>)<br/
Galapagos shark (<spn>Carcharhinus galapagensis</spn>)<br/
Night shark (<spn>Carcharhinus signatus</spn>)<br/
White shark (<spn>Carcharodon carcharias</spn>)<br/
Basking shark (<spn>Cetorhinus maximus</spn>)<br/
Tiger shark (<spn>Galeocerdo cuvier</spn>)<br/
Nurse shark (<spn>Ginglymostoma cirratum</spn>)<br/
Lemon shark (<spn>Negaprion brevirostris</spn>)<br/
Ragged-tooth shark (<spn>Odontaspis ferox</spn>)<br/
Whale shark (<spn>Rhincodon typus</spn>)<br/
Scalloped hammerhead (<spn>Sphyrna lewini</spn>)<br/
Great hammerhead (<spn>Sphyrna mokarran</spn>)<br/
Smooth hammerhead (<spn>Sphyrna zygaena</spn>)<br/
<br/
(3) Small Coastal Sharks<br/
<br/
Finetooth shark (<spn>Carcharhinus isodon</spn>)<br/
Blacknose shark (<spn>Carcharhinus acronotus</spn>)<br/
Atlantic sharpnose shark (<spn>Rhizoprionodon erraenovae</spn>)<br/
Caribbean sharpnose shark (<spn>Rhizoprionodon porosus</spn>)<br/
Bonnethead (<spn>Sphyrna tiburo</spn>)<br/
Atlantic angel shark (<spn>Squatina dumeril</spn>)<br/
</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rapacious, artful person; a sharper.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Trickery; fraud; petty rapine; <as>as, to live upon
the <ex>shark</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Basking shark</b></col>, <col><b>Liver
shark</b></col>, <col><b>Nurse shark</b></col>, <col><b>Oil
shark</b></col>, <col><b>Sand shark</b></col>, <col><b>Tiger
shark</b></col>, etc.</mcol> <cd>See under <er>Basking</er>,
<er>Liver</er>, etc. See also <er>Dogfish</er>, <er>Houndfish</er>,
<er>Notidanian</er>, and <er>Tope</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Gray
shark</b></col>, <cd>the sand shark.</cd> -- <col><b>Hammer-headed
shark</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Hammerhead</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Port
Jackson shark</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Cestraciont</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Shark barrow</b></col>, <cd>the eggcase of a shark; a sea
purse.</cd> -- <col><b>Shark ray</b></col>. <cd>Same as <spn>Angel
fish</spn> <sd>(a)</sd>, under <er>Angel</er>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Thrasher shark</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Thresher
shark</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a large, voracious shark. See
<er>Thrasher</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Whale shark</b></col>, <cd>a huge
harmless shark (<spn>Rhinodon typicus</spn>) of the Indian Ocean. It
becomes sixty feet or more in length, but has very small
teeth.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shark</ent><br/
<hw>Shark</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Of uncertain origin; perhaps fr.
<ets>shark</ets>, n., or perhaps related to E. <ets>shear</ets> (as
<ets>hearken</ets> to <ets>hear</ets>), and originally meaning, to clip
off. Cf. <er>Shirk</er>.]</ety> <def>To pick or gather indiscriminately
or covertly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shark</ent><br/
<hw>Shark</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sharked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sharking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To play the petty
thief; to practice fraud or trickery; to swindle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Neither <qex>sharks</qex> for a cup or a reckoning.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To live by shifts and stratagems.</def>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharker</ent><br/
<hw>Shark"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lives by
sharking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharking</ent><br/
<hw>Shark"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Petty rapine; trick; also,
seeking a livelihood by shifts and dishonest devices.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharock</ent><br/
<hw>Shar"ock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An East Indian coin
of the value of 12<frac12/ pence sterling, or about 25 cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sharper</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sharpest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sharp</ets>,
<ets>scharp</ets>, <ets>scarp</ets>, AS. <ets>scearp</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>skarp</ets>, LG. <ets>scharp</ets>, D. <ets>scherp</ets>, G.
<ets>scharf</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>skarp</ets>, Icel. <ets>skarpr</ets>.
Cf. <er>Escarp</er>, <er>Scrape</er>, <er>Scorpion</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Having a very thin edge or fine point; of a nature to
cut or pierce easily; not blunt or dull; keen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He dies upon my scimeter's <qex>sharp</qex> point.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Terminating in a point or edge; not obtuse or
rounded; somewhat pointed or edged; peaked or ridged; <as>as, a
<ex>sharp</ex> hill; <ex>sharp</ex> features.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Affecting the sense as if pointed or cutting, keen,
penetrating, acute: to the taste or smell, pungent, acid, sour, as
ammonia has a <xex>sharp</xex> taste and odor; to the hearing, piercing,
shrill, as a <xex>sharp</xex> sound or voice; to the eye,
instantaneously brilliant, dazzling, as a <xex>sharp</xex>
flash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>High in pitch; acute;
<as>as, a <ex>sharp</ex> note or tone</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Raised a semitone in pitch; <as>as, C <ex>sharp</ex> (C<sharp/),
which is a half step, or semitone, higher than C</as>.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>So high as to be out of tune, or above true pitch;
<as>as, the tone is <ex>sharp</ex>; that instrument is
<ex>sharp</ex></as>. Opposed in all these senses to
<ant>flat</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Very trying to the feelings; piercing; keen; severe;
painful; distressing; <as>as, <ex>sharp</ex> pain, weather; a
<ex>sharp</ex> and frosty air</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sharp</qex> misery had worn him to the bones.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The morning <qex>sharp</qex> and clear.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>sharpest</qex> perils faithful proved.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Cutting in language or import; biting; sarcastic;
cruel; harsh; rigorous; severe; <as>as, a <ex>sharp</ex>
rebuke</as>.</def> <ldquo/That <xex>sharp</xex> look.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To that place the <qex>sharp</qex> Athenian law<br/
Can not pursue us.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be thy words severe,<br/
<qex>Sharp</qex> as merits but the sword forbear.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Of keen perception; quick to discern or distinguish;
having nice discrimination; acute; penetrating; sagacious; clever;
<as>as, a <ex>sharp</ex> eye; <ex>sharp</ex> sight, hearing, or
judgment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing makes men <qex>sharper</qex> . . . than want.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many other things belong to the material world, wherein the
<qex>sharpest</qex> philosophers have never ye<?/ arrived at clear and
distinct ideas.</q> <rj><qau>L. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Eager in pursuit; keen in quest; impatient for
gratification; keen; <as>as, a <ex>sharp</ex> appetite</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Fierce; ardent; fiery; violent; impetuous.</def>
<ldquo/In <xex>sharp</xex> contest of battle.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sharp</qex> assault already is begun.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>Keenly or unduly attentive to one's own interest;
close and exact in dealing; shrewd; <as>as, a <ex>sharp</ex> dealer; a
<ex>sharp</ex> customer.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The necessity of being so <qex>sharp</qex> and exacting.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Composed of hard, angular grains; gritty; <as>as,
<ex>sharp</ex> sand</as>.</def> <rj><au>Moxon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>Steep; precipitous; abrupt; <as>as, a
<ex>sharp</ex> ascent or descent; a <ex>sharp</ex> turn or
curve</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <def>Uttered in a whisper, or
with the breath alone, without voice, as certain consonants, such as
<xex>p</xex>, <xex>k</xex>, <xex>t</xex>, <xex>f</xex>; surd; nonvocal;
aspirated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sharp</xex> is often used in the formation of
self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>sharp</xex>-cornered,
<xex>sharp</xex>-edged, <xex>sharp</xex>-pointed,
<xex>sharp</xex>-tasted, <xex>sharp</xex>-visaged, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sharp practice</b></col>, <cd>the getting of an
advantage, or the attempt to do so, by a tricky expedient.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>To brace sharp</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To sharp
up</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to turn the yards to the
most oblique position possible, that the ship may lie well up to the
wind.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Keen; acute; piercing; penetrating; quick;
sagacious; discerning; shrewd; witty; ingenious; sour; acid; tart;
pungent; acrid; severe; poignant; biting; acrimonious; sarcastic;
cutting; bitter; painful; afflictive; violent; harsh; fierce; ardent;
fiery.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To a point
or edge; piercingly; eagerly; sharply.</def> <rj><au>M.
Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The head [of a spear] full <qex>sharp</qex> yground.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You bite so <qex>sharp</qex> at reasons.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Precisely; exactly; <as>as, we shall start at ten
o'clock <ex>sharp</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Look sharp</b></col>, <cd>attend; be alert.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sharp tool or
weapon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If butchers had but the manners to go to <qex>sharps</qex>,
gentlemen would be contented with a rubber at cuffs.</q>
<rj><qau>Collier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The character
[<sharp/] used to indicate that the note before which it is placed is to
be raised a half step, or semitone, in pitch.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
sharp tone or note.</def> <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A portion of a stream where the water runs very
rapidly.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>C.
Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sewing needle having a very slender point; a
needle of the most pointed of the three grades, <xex>blunts</xex>,
<xex>betweens</xex>, and <xex>sharps</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Same as <er>Middlings</er>,
1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An expert.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sharped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sharping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sharpen.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To raise above the proper pitch;
to elevate the tone of; especially, to raise a half step, or semitone,
above the natural tone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To play tricks in
bargaining; to act the sharper.</def> <rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To sing above the proper
pitch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp-cut</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp-cut`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cut sharply or
definitely, or so as to make a clear, well-defined impression, as the
lines of an engraved plate, and the like; clear-cut; hence, having great
distinctness; well-defined; clear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharpen</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sarpened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sharpening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Sharp</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To make sharp.</def> Specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>To give a keen edge or fine point to; to make sharper;
<as>as, to <ex>sharpen</ex> an ax, or the teeth of a saw</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>To render more quick or acute in perception; to make
more ready or ingenious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The air . . . <qex>sharpened</qex> his visual ray<br/
To objects distant far.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that wrestles with us strengthens our nerves and
<qex>sharpens</qex> our skill.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To make more eager; <as>as, to <ex>sharpen</ex>
men's desires</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Epicurean cooks<br/
<qex>Sharpen</qex> with cloyless sauce his appetite.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>To make more pungent and intense; <as>as, to
<ex>sharpen</ex> a pain or disease</as>.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>To make
biting, sarcastic, or severe.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sharpen</xex> each
word.<rdquo/ <au>E. Smith.</au> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>To render more shrill
or piercing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Inclosures not only preserve sound, but increase and
<qex>sharpen</qex> it.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(g)</sd> <def>To make more tart or acid; to make sour; <as>as,
the rays of the sun <ex>sharpen</ex> vinegar</as>.</def> <sd>(h)</sd>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To raise, as a sound, by means of a sharp; to
apply a sharp to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharpen</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"en</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To grow or become
sharp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharper</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person who bargains
closely, especially, one who cheats in bargains; a swinder; also, a
cheating gamester.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sharpers</qex>, as pikes, prey upon their own kind.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Swindler; cheat; deceiver; trickster; rogue. See
<er>Swindler</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharpie</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"ie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A
long, sharp, flat-bottomed boat, with one or two masts carrying a
triangular sail. They are often called <altname>Fair Haven
sharpies</altname>, after the place on the coast of Connecticut where
they originated.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharpling</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A stickleback.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharply</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sharp manner,; keenly;
acutely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are more <qex>sharply</qex> to be chastised and reformed than
the rude Irish.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The soldiers were <qex>sharply</qex> assailed with wants.</q>
<rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You contract your eye when you would see <qex>sharply</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharpness</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>scearpness</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or condition of being
sharp; keenness; acuteness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharpsaw</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"saw`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The great titmouse; -- so called from its harsh call notes.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp-set</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"-set`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Eager in appetite
or desire of gratification; affected by keen hunger; ravenous; <as>as,
an eagle or a lion <ex>sharp-set</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The town is <qex>sharp-set</qex> on new plays.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharpshooter</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"shoot`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled in
shooting at an object with exactness; a good marksman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharpshooting</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"shoot`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shooting with great
precision and effect; hence, a keen contest of wit or
argument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp-sighted</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"-sight`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having quick
or acute sight; -- used literally and figuratively.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sharp`-sight`ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharptail</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The pintail duck.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The pintail
grouse, or prairie chicken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sharp-witted</ent><br/
<hw>Sharp"-wit`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having an acute
or nicely discerning mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shash</ent><br/
<hw>Shash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sash</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The scarf of a turban.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sash.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shasta</ent><br/
<hw>Shas"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mountain peak, etc.,
in California.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shasta daisy</ent><br/
<hw>Shasta daisy</hw>. <def>A large-flowered garden variety of the oxeye
daisy.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shasta fir</ent><br/
<hw>Shasta fir</hw>. <def>A Californian fir (<spn>Abies
shastensis</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shasta Sam</ent><br/
<hw>Shasta Sam</hw>. <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>A game like
California Jack, except that the pack drawn from is turned face
down.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shastra</ent><br/
<ent>Shaster</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Shas"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Shas"tra</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr. <ets>c<amac/stra</ets> an
order or command, a sacred book, fr. <ets>c<amac/s</ets> to order,
instruct, govern. Cf. <er>Sastra</er>.]</ety> <def>A treatise for
authoritative instruction among the Hindoos; a book of institutes;
especially, a treatise explaining the Vedas.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sastra</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shathmont</ent><br/
<hw>Shath"mont</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
shaftment.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shatter</ent><br/
<hw>Shat"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shattered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shattering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schateren</ets>, <ets>scateren</ets>, to scatter, to dash, AS.
<ets>scateran</ets>; cf. D. <ets>schateren</ets> to crack, to make a
great noise, OD. <ets>schetteren</ets> to scatter, to burst, to crack.
Cf. <er>Scatter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To break at once into many
pieces; to dash, burst, or part violently into fragments; to rend into
splinters; <as>as, an explosion <ex>shatters</ex> a rock or a bomb; too
much steam <ex>shatters</ex> a boiler; an oak is <ex>shattered</ex> by
lightning.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A monarchy was <qex>shattered</qex> to pieces, and divided amongst
revolted subjects.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To disorder; to derange; to render unsound; <as>as,
to be <ex>shattered</ex> in intellect; his constitution was
<ex>shattered</ex>; his hopes were <ex>shattered</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man of a loose, volatile, and <qex>shattered</qex> humor.</q>
<rj><qau>Norris.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To scatter about.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shatter</qex> your leaves before the mellowing year.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shatter</ent><br/
<hw>Shat"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be broken into fragments; to
fall or crumble to pieces by any force applied.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some fragile bodies break but where the force is; some
<qex>shatter</qex> and fly in many places.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shatter</ent><br/
<hw>Shat"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fragment of anything shattered;
-- used chiefly or soley in the phrase <xex>into shatters</xex>; <as>as,
to break a glass into <ex>shatters</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shatter-pated</ent><br/
<ent>Shatter-brained</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shat"ter-brained`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shat"ter-pat`ed</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Disordered or wandering in
intellect; hence, heedless; wild; similar to
<altname>scatter-brained</altname>.</def> <rj><au>J.
Goodman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shattery</ent><br/
<hw>Shat"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Easily breaking
into pieces; not compact; loose of texture; brittle; <as>as,
<ex>shattery</ex> spar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shave</ent><br/
<hw>Shave</hw> <pr>(sh<amac/v)</pr>, <def><pos>obs. p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shave</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His beard was <qex>shave</qex> as nigh as ever he can.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shave</ent><br/
<hw>Shave</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Shaved</conjf> <pr>(sh<amac/vd)</pr>;<pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shaved</conjf> or <conjf>Shaven</conjf> <pr>(sh<amac/v"'n)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Shaving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shaven</ets>, <ets>schaven</ets>, AS. <ets>scafan</ets>,
<ets>sceafan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>schaven</ets>, G.
<ets>schaben</ets>, Icel. <ets>skafa</ets>, Sw. <ets>skafva</ets>, Dan.
<ets>skave</ets>, Goth. <ets>scaban</ets>, Russ. <ets>kopate</ets> to
dig, Gr. <grk>ska`ptein</grk>, and probably to L. <ets>scabere</ets> to
scratch, to scrape. Cf. <er>Scab</er>, <er>Shaft</er>,
<er>Shape</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut or pare off from the
surface of a body with a razor or other edged instrument; to cut off
closely, as with a razor; <as>as, to <ex>shave</ex> the
beard</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make bare or smooth by cutting off closely the
surface, or surface covering, of; especially, to remove the hair from
with a razor or other sharp instrument; to take off the beard or hair
of; <as>as, to <ex>shave</ex> the face or the crown of the head; he
<ex>shaved</ex> himself.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll <qex>shave</qex> your crown for this.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The laborer with the bending scythe is seen<br/
<qex>Shaving</qex> the surface of the waving green.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cut off thin slices from; to cut in thin
slices.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Plants bruised or <qex>shaven</qex> in leaf or root.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To skim along or near the surface of; to pass close
to, or touch lightly, in passing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now <qex>shaves</qex> with level wing the deep.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To strip; to plunder; to fleece.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shave a note</b></col>, <cd>to buy it at a discount
greater than the legal rate of interest, or to deduct in discounting it
more than the legal rate allows.</cd> <mark>[Cant, U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1325 --></p>

<p><ent>Shave</ent><br/
<hw>Shave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To use a razor for
removing the beard; to cut closely; hence, to be hard and severe in a
bargain; to practice extortion; to cheat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shave</ent><br/
<hw>Shave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>scafa</ets>,
sceafa, a sort of knife. See <er>Shave</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A thin slice; a shaving.</def>
<rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A cutting of the beard; the operation of
shaving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An exorbitant discount on a note.</def>
<mark>[Cant, U.S.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A premium paid for an
extension of the time of delivery or payment, or for the right to vary a
stock contract in any particular.</def> <mark>[Cant, U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>N. Biddle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A hand tool consisting of a sharp blade with a
handle at each end; a drawing knife; a spokeshave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The act of passing very near to, so as almost to
graze; <as>as, the bullet missed by a close <ex>shave</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shave grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the scouring
rush. See the Note under <er>Equisetum</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shave
hook</b></col>, <cd>a tool for scraping metals, consisting of a
sharp-edged triangular steel plate attached to a shank and
handle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaveling</ent><br/
<hw>Shave"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A man shaved;
hence, a monk, or other religious; -- used in contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am no longer a <qex>shaveling</qex> than while my frock is on my
back.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaver</ent><br/
<hw>Shav"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
shaves; one whose occupation is to shave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who is close in bargains; a sharper.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who fleeces; a pillager; a plunderer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By these <qex>shavers</qex> the Turks were stripped.</q>
<rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A boy; a lad; a little fellow.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/These unlucky little
<xex>shavers</xex>.<rdquo/ <-- often little shaver -->
<rj><au>Salmagundi.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As I have mentioned at the door to this young <qex>shaver</qex>, I
am on a chase in the name of the king.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A tool or machine for
shaving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>A note shaver</b></col>, <cd>a person who buys notes at a
discount greater than the legal rate of interest.</cd> <mark>[Cant,
U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaving</ent><br/
<hw>Shav"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who, or
that which, shaves; specifically, the act of cutting off the beard with
a razor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is shaved off; a thin slice or strip
pared off with a shave, a knife, a plane, or other cutting
instrument.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Shaving</xex> of silver.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shaving brush</b></col>, <cd>a brush used in lathering
the face preparatory to shaving it.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shaw</ent><br/
<hw>Shaw</hw> <pr>(sh<add/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schawe</ets>, <ets>scha<yogh/e</ets>, thicket, grove, AS.
<ets>scaga</ets>; akin to Dan. <ets>skov</ets>, Sw. <ets>skog</ets>,
Icel. <ets>sk<omac/gr</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thicket; a small
wood or grove.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gaillard he was as goldfinch in the <qex>shaw</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The green <qex>shaws</qex>, the merry green woods.</q>
<rj><qau>Howitt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The leaves and tops of vegetables,
as of potatoes, turnips, etc.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shawfowl</ent><br/
<hw>Shaw"fowl`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot. <ets>schaw</ets>,
<ets>shaw</ets>, show + <ets>fowl</ets>.]</ety> <def>The representation
or image of a fowl made by fowlers to shoot at.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shawl</ent><br/
<hw>Shawl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per. & Hind.
<ets>sh<amac/l</ets>: cf. F. <ets>ch<acir/le</ets>.]</ety> <def>A square
or oblong cloth of wool, cotton, silk, or other textile or netted
fabric, used, especially by women, as a loose covering for the neck and
shoulders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>India shawl</b></col>, <cd>a kind of rich shawl made in
India from the wool of the Cashmere goat. It is woven in pieces, which
are sewed together.</cd> -- <col><b>Shawl goat</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the Cashmere goat.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shawl</ent><br/
<hw>Shawl</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To wrap in a shawl.</def>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shawm</ent><br/
<hw>Shawm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shalmie</ets>,
OF. <ets>chalemie</ets>; cf. F. <ets>chalumeau</ets> shawm,
<ets>chaume</ets> haulm, stalk; all fr. L. <ets>calamus</ets> a reed,
reed pipe. See <er>Haulm</er>, and cf. <er>Calumet</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A wind instrument of music, formerly in use,
supposed to have resembled either the clarinet or the hautboy in
form.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>shalm</asp>,
<asp>shaum</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Otway.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Even from the shrillest <qex>shaum</qex> unto the cornamute.</q>
<rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shawnees</ent><br/
<hw>Shaw`nees"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Shawnee</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>A tribe of North American Indians who occupied Western New York and
part of Ohio, but were driven away and widely dispersed by the
Iroquois.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shay</ent><br/
<hw>Shay</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A chaise.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>She</ent><br/
<hw>She</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>pron.</pos> <nmorph>[<pos>sing.
nom.</pos> <decf>She</decf>; <pos>poss.</pos> <decf>Her</decf>.
<pr>(<?/)</pr> or <decf>Hers</decf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>obj.</pos>
<decf>Her</decf>; <pos>pl. nom.</pos> <decf>They</decf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>poss.</pos> <decf>Their</decf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <decf>Theirs</decf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>obj.</pos> <decf>Them</decf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</nmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>she</ets>, <ets>sche</ets>,
<ets>scheo</ets>, <ets>scho</ets>, AS. <ets>se<oacute/</ets>, fem. of
the definite article, originally a demonstrative pronoun; cf. OS.
<ets>siu</ets>, D. <ets>zij</ets>, G. <ets>sie</ets>, OHG.
<ets>siu</ets>, <ets>s<imac/</ets>, <ets>si</ets>, Icel.
<ets>s<umac/</ets>, <ets>sj<amac/</ets>, Goth. <ets>si</ets> she,
<ets>s<omac/</ets>, fem. article, Russ. <ets>siia</ets>, fem., this, Gr.
<?/, fem. article, Skr. <ets>s<amac/</ets>, <ets>sy<amac/</ets>. The
possessive <ets>her</ets> or <ets>hers</ets>, and the objective
<ets>her</ets>, are from a different root. See <er>Her</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>This or that female; the woman understood or referred
to; the animal of the female sex, or object personified as feminine,
which was spoken of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>She</qex> loved her children best in every wise.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then Sarah denied, . . . for <qex>she</qex> was afraid.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xviii. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A woman; a female; -- used substantively.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lady, you are the cruelest <qex>she</qex> alive.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>She</xex> is used in composition with nouns of
common gender, for <xex>female</xex>, to denote an animal of the female
sex; as, a <xex>she</xex>-bear; a <xex>she</xex>-cat.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheading</ent><br/
<hw>Shead"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From AS.
<ets>sc<amac/dan</ets>, <ets>sce<aacute/dan</ets>, to separate, divide.
See <er>Shed</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>A tithing, or division,
in the Isle of Man, in which there is a coroner, or chief constable. The
island is divided into six sheadings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheaf</ent><br/
<hw>Sheaf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A
sheave.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheaf</ent><br/
<hw>Sheaf</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sheaves</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>sheef</ets>, <ets>shef</ets>,
<ets>schef</ets>, AS. <ets>sce<aacute/f</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>schoof</ets>, OHG. <ets>scoub</ets>, G. <ets>schaub</ets>, Icel.
<ets>skauf</ets> a fox's brush, and E. <ets>shove</ets>. See
<er>Shove</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A quantity of the stalks and
ears of wheat, rye, or other grain, bound together; a bundle of grain or
straw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The reaper fills his greedy hands,<br/
And binds the golden <qex>sheaves</qex> in brittle bands.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any collection of things bound together; a bundle;
specifically, a bundle of arrows sufficient to fill a quiver, or the
allowance of each archer, -- usually twenty-four.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sheaf</qex> of arrows shook and rattled in the case.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheaf</ent><br/
<hw>Sheaf</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To gather and bind into a sheaf;
to make into sheaves; <as>as, to <ex>sheaf</ex> wheat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheaf</ent><br/
<hw>Sheaf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To collect and bind
cut grain, or the like; to make sheaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They that reap must <qex>sheaf</qex> and bind.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheafy</ent><br/
<hw>Sheaf"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or
consisting of, a sheaf or sheaves; resembling a sheaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheal</ent><br/
<hw>Sheal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sheeling</er>.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheal</ent><br/
<hw>Sheal</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To put under a sheal or
shelter.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheal</ent><br/
<hw>Sheal</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Shell</er>.]</ety>
<def>To take the husks or pods off from; to shell; to empty of its
contents, as a husk or a pod.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That's a <qex>shealed</qex> peascod.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheal</ent><br/
<hw>Sheal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shell or pod.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shealing</ent><br/
<hw>Sheal"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The outer husk, pod, or shell, as
of oats, pease, etc.; sheal; shell.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shealing</ent><br/
<hw>Sheal"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Sheeling</er>.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shear</ent><br/
<hw>Shear</hw> <pr>(sh<emac/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Sheared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or
<conjf>Shore</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>;<pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sheared</conjf> or <conjf>Shorn</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Shearing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sheren</ets>, <ets>scheren</ets>, to shear, cut, shave, AS.
<ets>sceran</ets>, <ets>scieran</ets>, <ets>scyran</ets>; akin to D. &
G. <ets>scheren</ets>, Icel. <ets>skera</ets>, Dan. <ets>ski<?/re</ets>,
Gr. <?/<?/<?/. Cf. <er>Jeer</er>, <er>Score</er>, <er>Shard</er>,
<er>Share</er>, <er>Sheer</er> to turn aside.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
cut, clip, or sever anything from with shears or a like instrument;
<as>as, to <ex>shear</ex> sheep; to <ex>shear</ex> cloth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is especially applied to the cutting of wool from
sheep or their skins, and the nap from cloth.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To separate or sever with shears or a similar
instrument; to cut off; to clip (something) from a surface; <as>as, to
<ex>shear</ex> a fleece</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before the golden tresses . . . were <qex>shorn</qex> away.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To reap, as grain.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: To deprive of property; to fleece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>To produce a change of shape in
by a shear. See <er>Shear</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shear</ent><br/
<hw>Shear</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sceara</ets>. See
<er>Shear</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pair of
shears; -- now always used in the plural, but formerly also in the
singular. See <er>Shears</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On his head came razor none, nor <qex>shear</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Short of the wool, and naked from the <qex>shear</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A shearing; -- used in designating the age of
sheep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>After the second shearing, he is a two-<qex>shear</qex> ram; . . .
at the expiration of another year, he is a three-<qex>shear</qex> ram;
the name always taking its date from the time of shearing.</q>
<rj><qau>Youatt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Engin.)</fld> <def>An action, resulting from
applied forces, which tends to cause two contiguous parts of a body to
slide relatively to each other in a direction parallel to their plane of
contact; -- also called <altname>shearing stress</altname>, and
<altname>tangential stress</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A strain, or change of shape, of
an elastic body, consisting of an extension in one direction, an equal
compression in a perpendicular direction, with an unchanged magnitude in
the third direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shear blade</b></col>, <cd>one of the blades of shears or
a shearing machine.</cd> -- <col><b>Shear hulk</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Hulk</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shear steel</b></col>, <cd>a steel
suitable for shears, scythes, and other cutting instruments, prepared
from fagots of blistered steel by repeated heating, rolling, and
tilting, to increase its malleability and fineness of
texture.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shear</ent><br/
<hw>Shear</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To deviate. See
<er>Sheer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Engin.)</fld> <def>To become more or less
completely divided, as a body under the action of forces, by the sliding
of two contiguous parts relatively to each other in a direction parallel
to their plane of contact.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shearbill</ent><br/
<hw>Shear"bill`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The black
skimmer. See <er>Skimmer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheard</ent><br/
<hw>Sheard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shard</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shearer</ent><br/
<hw>Shear"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
shears.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a lamb dumb before his <qex>shearer</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Acts
viii. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A reaper.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shearing</ent><br/
<hw>Shear"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or operation
of clipping with shears or a shearing machine, as the wool from sheep,
or the nap from cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The product of the act or operation of clipping with
shears or a shearing machine; <as>as, the whole <ex>shearing</ex> of a
flock; the <ex>shearings</ex> from cloth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Shearling</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Youatt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act or operation of reaping.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The act or operation of dividing with shears;
<as>as, the <ex>shearing</ex> of metal plates</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The process of preparing shear steel;
tilting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The process of making a vertical
side cutting in working into a face of coal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shearing machine</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A machine
with blades, or rotary disks, for dividing plates or bars of metal</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A machine for shearing cloth.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shearling</ent><br/
<hw>Shear"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sheep but once
sheared.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shearman</ent><br/
<hw>Shear"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shearmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One whose occupation is
to shear cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shearn</ent><br/
<hw>Shearn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>scearn</ets>.
Cf. <er>Scarn</er>.]</ety> <def>Dung; excrement.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also <asp>shern</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shears</ent><br/
<hw>Shears</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Formerly used also
in the singular. See <er>Shear</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A cutting instrument.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>An instrument consisting of two blades, commonly with bevel edges,
connected by a pivot, and working on both sides of the material to be
cut, -- used for cutting cloth and other substances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fate urged the <qex>shears</qex>, and cut the sylph in twain.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A similar instrument the blades of which are
extensions of a curved spring, -- used for shearing sheep or
skins.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A shearing machine; a blade, or a set of
blades, working against a resisting edge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything in the form of shears.</def> Specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A pair of wings.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Spenser.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An apparatus for raising heavy
weights, and especially for stepping and unstepping the lower masts of
ships. It consists of two or more spars or pieces of timber, fastened
together near the top, steadied by a guy or guys, and furnished with the
necessary tackle.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sheers</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>The bedpiece of a machine tool,
upon which a table or slide rest is secured; <as>as, the <ex>shears</ex>
of a lathe or planer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Rotary shears</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Rotary</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shear steel</ent><br/
<hw>Shear steel</hw>. <def>See under <er>Shear</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheartail</ent><br/
<hw>Shear"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The common tern.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any one of
several species of humming birds of the genus <gen>Thaumastura</gen>
having a long forked tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shearwater</ent><br/
<hw>Shear"wa`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Shear</ets>
+ <ets>water</ets>; cf. G. <ets>wassersherer</ets>; -- so called from
its running lightly along the surface of the water.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of long-winged
oceanic birds of the genus <gen>Puffinus</gen> and related genera. They
are allied to the petrels, but are larger. The Manx shearwater
(<spn>Puffinus Anglorum</spn>), the dusky shearwater (<spn>Puffinus
obscurus</spn>), and the greater shearwater (<spn>Puffinus major</spn>),
are well-known species of the North Atlantic. See
<er>Hagdon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheatfish</ent><br/
<hw>Sheat"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. dial. G.
<ets>scheid</ets>, <ets>schaid</ets>, <ets>schaiden</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A European siluroid fish (<spn>Silurus
glanis</spn>) allied to the cat-fishes. It is the largest fresh-water
fish of Europe, sometimes becoming six feet or more in length. See
<er>Siluroid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheath</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schethe</ets>, AS. <ets>sc<aemac/<edh/</ets>,
<ets>sce<aacute/<edh/</ets>, <ets>sc<emac/<edh/</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>sk<emac/<edh/ia</ets>, D. <ets>scheede</ets>, G.
<ets>scheide</ets>, OHG. <ets>sceida</ets>, Sw. <ets>skida</ets>, Dan.
<ets>skede</ets>, Icel. <ets>skei<edh/ir</ets>, pl., and to E.
<ets>shed</ets>, v.t., originally meaning, to separate, to part. See
<er>Shed</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A case for the reception of a
sword, hunting knife, or other long and slender instrument; a
scabbard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dead knight's sword out of his <qex>sheath</qex> he drew.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any sheathlike covering, organ, or part.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The base of a leaf
when sheathing or investing a stem or branch, as in grasses.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the elytra of an
insect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Medullary sheath</b></col>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Medullary</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Primitive sheath</b></col>.
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Neurilemma</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheath
knife</b></col>, <cd>a knife with a fixed blade, carried in a
sheath.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheath of Schwann</b></col>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Schwann's sheath</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheathbill</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath"bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Either one of two species of birds composing the genus
<gen>Chionis</gen>, and family <fam>Chionidae</fam>, native of the
islands of the Antarctic seas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They are related to the gulls and the plovers, but more
nearly to the latter. The base of the bill is covered with a
saddle-shaped horny sheath, and the toes are only slightly webbed. The
plumage of both species is white.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheathe</ent><br/
<hw>Sheathe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sheathed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sheating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Written also
<ets>sheath</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put into a sheath, case,
or scabbard; to inclose or cover with, or as with, a sheath or
case.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The leopard . . . keeps the claws of his fore feet turned up from
the ground, and <qex>sheathed</qex> in the skin of his toes.</q>
<rj><qau>Grew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is in my breast she <qex>sheathes</qex> her dagger now.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fit or furnish, as with a sheath.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To case or cover with something which protects, as
thin boards, sheets of metal, and the like; <as>as, to <ex>sheathe</ex>
a ship with copper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To obtund or blunt, as acrimonious substances, or
sharp particles.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sheathe the sword</b></col>, <cd>to make
peace.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheathed</ent><br/
<hw>Sheathed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Povided
with, or inclosed in, sheath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Invested by a sheath, or
cylindrical membranaceous tube, which is the base of the leaf, as the
stalk or culm in grasses; vaginate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheather</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sheathes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheathfish</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sheatfish</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheathing</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>p. pr. & a.</pos> <mord>from
<er>Sheathe</er>.</mord> <def>Inclosing with a sheath; <as>as, the
<ex>sheathing</ex> leaves of grasses; the <ex>sheathing</ex> stipules of
many polygonaceous plants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheathing</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which sheathes.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The casing or covering of a ship's
bottom and sides; the materials for such covering; <as>as, copper
<ex>sheathing</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The
first covering of boards on the outside wall of a frame house or on a
timber roof; also, the material used for covering; ceiling boards in
general.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheathless</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a sheath
or case for covering; unsheathed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheath-winged</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath"-winged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having elytra, or wing cases, as a beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheathy</ent><br/
<hw>Sheath"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Forming or resembling
a sheath or case.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shea tree</ent><br/
<hw>She"a tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An African
sapotaceous tree (<spn>Bassia Parkii</spn> syn. <spn>Butyrospermum
Parkii</spn>), from the seeds of which a substance resembling butter is
obtained; the African butter tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1326 --></p>

<p><ent>Sheave</ent><br/
<hw>Sheave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to OD.
<ets>schijve</ets> orb, disk, wheel, D. <ets>schiff</ets>, G.
<ets>scheibe</ets>, Icel. <ets>sk<imac/fa</ets> a shaving, slice; cf.
Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a staff. Cf. <er>Shift</er>, <pos>v.</pos>,
<er>Shive</er>.]</ety> <def>A wheel having a groove in the rim for a
rope to work in, and set in a block, mast, or the like; the wheel of a
pulley.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sheave hole</b></col>, <cd>a channel cut in a mast, yard,
rail, or other timber, in which to fix a sheave.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheave</ent><br/
<hw>Sheave</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sheaf</er> of
straw.]</ety> <def>To gather and bind into a sheaf or sheaves; hence, to
collect.</def> <rj><au>Ashmole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheaved</ent><br/
<hw>Sheaved</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made of straw.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shebander</ent><br/
<hw>Sheb"an*der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per.
<ets>sh<amac/hbandar</ets>.]</ety> <def>A harbor master, or ruler of a
port, in the East Indies.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>shebunder</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shebang</ent><br/
<hw>She*bang"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Shebeen</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A jocosely depreciative name
for a dwelling or shop; a primitive dwelling; a shanty.</def>
<mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The structure of an object, process, organization,
or anything viewed as complicated; -- used primarily in the phrase
<col><b>the whole <ex>shebang</ex></b></col>; <as>as, it comes with
unnecessary frills, but you have to buy the whole
<ex>shebang</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[informal]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(computers)</fld> <ety>[Possibly derived from
<ets>shell bang</ets>; the character <it>!</it> is referred to in some
computer contexts as <ets>bang</ets>.]</ety> <def>The character sequence
<it>#!</it>, which frequently begins shell scripts in a Unix
system.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shebeen</ent><br/
<hw>She*been"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of Irish origin;
cf. Ir. <ets>seapa</ets> a shop.]</ety> <def>A low public house;
especially, a place where spirits and other excisable liquors are
illegally and privately sold.</def> <mark>[Ireland]</mark><-- similar to
a speakeasy? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shechinah</ent><br/
<hw>She*chi"nah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shekinah</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shecklaton</ent><br/
<hw>Sheck"la*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Ciclatoun</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of gilt leather. See
<er>Checklaton</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shed</ent><br/
<hw>Shed</hw> <pr>(sh<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[The same word as
<ets>shade</ets>. See <er>Shade</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A slight
or temporary structure built to shade or shelter something; a structure
often open in front; an outbuilding; a hut; <as>as, a wagon
<ex>shed</ex>; a wood <ex>shed</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The first Aletes born in lowly <qex>shed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sheds</qex> of reeds which summer's heat repel.</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Aeronautics)</fld> <def>A covered structure for
housing aircraft; a hangar.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shed</ent><br/
<hw>Shed</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shedding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>scheden</ets>,
<ets>sch<?/den</ets>, to pour, to part, AS. <ets>sc<amac/dan</ets>,
<ets>sce<aacute/dan</ets>, to pert, to separate; akin to OS.
<ets>sk<?/<?/an</ets>, OFries. sk<?/tha, G. <ets>scheiden</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sceidan</ets>, Goth. <ets>skaidan</ets>, and probably to Lith.
<ets>sk<eum/du</ets> I part, separate, L. <ets>scindere</ets> to cleave,
to split, Gr. <?/<?/<?/, Skr. <ets>chid</ets>, and perch. also to L.
<ets>caedere</ets> to cut. <root/159. Cf. <er>Chisel</er>,
<er>Concise</er>, <er>Schism</er>, <er>Sheading</er>, <er>Sheath</er>,
<er>Shide</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To separate; to divide.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Robert of
Brunne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To part with; to throw off or give forth from one's
self; to emit; to diffuse; to cause to emanate or flow; to pour forth or
out; to spill; <as>as, the sun <ex>sheds</ex> light; she <ex>shed</ex>
tears; the clouds <ex>shed</ex> rain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Did Romeo's hand <qex>shed</qex> Tybalt's blood?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Twice seven consenting years have <qex>shed</qex><br/
Their utmost bounty on thy head.</q> <rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To let fall; to throw off, as a natural covering of
hair, feathers, shell; to cast; <as>as, fowls <ex>shed</ex> their
feathers; serpents <ex>shed</ex> their skins; trees <ex>shed</ex>
leaves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to flow off without penetrating; <as>as, a
tight roof, or covering of oiled cloth, <ex>sheeds</ex>
water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To sprinkle; to intersperse; to cover.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Her hair . . . is <xex>shed</xex> with
gray.<rdquo/ <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>To divide, as the warp threads,
so as to form a shed, or passageway, for the shuttle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shed</ent><br/
<hw>Shed</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fall in drops; to
pour.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such a rain down from the welkin <qex>shadde</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To let fall the parts, as seeds or fruit; to throw
off a covering or envelope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>White oats are apt to <qex>shed</qex> most as they lie, and black
as they stand.</q> <rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shed</ent><br/
<hw>Shed</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A parting; a separation; a
division.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They say also that the manner of making the <qex>shed</qex> of
newwedded wives' hair with the iron head of a javelin came up then
likewise.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. North.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of shedding or spilling; -- used only in
composition, as in blood<xex>shed</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which parts, divides, or sheds; -- used in
composition, as in water<xex>shed</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>The passageway between the
threads of the warp through which the shuttle is thrown, having a
sloping top and bottom made by raising and lowering the alternate
threads.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shedder</ent><br/
<hw>Shed"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, sheds; <as>as, a <ex>shedder</ex> of blood; a
<ex>shedder</ex> of tears</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A crab in the act of casting its
shell, or immediately afterwards while still soft; -- applied especially
to the edible crabs, which are most prized while in this
state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shedding</ent><br/
<hw>Shed"ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of shedding, separating, or casting off or out; <as>as, the
<ex>shedding</ex> of blood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is shed, or cast off.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shilfa</ent><br/
<ent>Sheelfa</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sheel"fa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shil"fa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The chaffinch; -- so named
from its call note.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheeling</ent><br/
<hw>Sheel"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>skj<omac/l</ets> a shelter, a cover; akin to Dan. & Sw.
<ets>skjul</ets>.]</ety> <def>A hut or small cottage in an exposed or a
retired place (as on a mountain or at the seaside) such as is used by
shepherds, fishermen, sportsmen, etc.; a summer cottage; also, a
shed.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sheel</asp>, <asp>shealing</asp>,
<asp>sheiling</asp>, etc.]</altsp> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheely</ent><br/
<hw>Sheel"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sheelfa</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheen</ent><br/
<hw>Sheen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sehene</ets>,
AS. <ets>sci<eacute/ne</ets>, <ets>sc<?/ne</ets>, <ets>sc<?/ne</ets>,
splendid, beautiful; akin to OFries. <ets>sk<?/ne</ets>,
<ets>sk<?/ne</ets>, OS. <ets>sc<?/ni</ets>, D. <ets>schoon</ets>, G.
<ets>sch<oum/n</ets>, OHG. <ets>sc<?/ni</ets>, Goth, <ets>skanus</ets>,
and E. <ets>shew</ets>; the original meaning being probably, visible,
worth seeing. It is not akin to E. <ets>shine</ets>. See <er>Shew</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Bright; glittering; radiant; fair; showy;
sheeny.</def> <mark>[R., except in poetry.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This holy maiden, that is so bright and <qex>sheen</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Up rose each warrier bold and brave,<br/
Glistening in filed steel and armor <qex>sheen</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheen</ent><br/
<hw>Sheen</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To shine; to glisten.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This town,<br/
That, <qex>sheening</qex> far, celestial seems to be.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheen</ent><br/
<hw>Sheen</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Brightness; splendor; glitter.</def>
<ldquo/Throned in celestial <xex>sheen</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheenly</ent><br/
<hw>Sheen""ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Brightly.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Mrs. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheeny</ent><br/
<hw>Sheen"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bright; shining;
radiant; sheen.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>sheeny</xex> summer morn.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheep</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shep</ets>, <ets>scheep</ets>, AS. <ets>sc<?/p</ets>,
<ets>sce<aacute/p</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>sk<?/p</ets>, LG. & D.
<ets>schaap</ets>, G. <ets>schaf</ets>, OHG. <ets>sc<amac/f</ets>, Skr.
<ets>ch<amac/ga</ets>. <root/295. Cf. <er>Sheepherd</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
ruminants of the genus <gen>Ovis</gen>, native of the higher mountains
of both hemispheres, but most numerous in Asia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The domestic sheep (<spn>Ovis aries</spn>) varies much
in size, in the length and texture of its wool, the form and size of its
horns, the length of its tail, etc. It was domesticated in prehistoric
ages, and many distinct breeds have been produced; as the merinos,
celebrated for their fine wool; the Cretan sheep, noted for their long
horns; the fat-tailed, or Turkish, sheep, remarkable for the size and
fatness of the tail, which often has to be supported on trucks; the
Southdowns, in which the horns are lacking; and an Asiatic breed which
always has four horns.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A weak, bashful, silly fellow.</def>
<rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Fig.: The people of God, as being
under the government and protection of Christ, the great
Shepherd.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Rocky mountain sheep</b></col>.<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Bighorn</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Maned sheep</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Aoudad</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep
bot</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the larva of the sheep botfly. See
<er>Estrus</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep dog</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a shepherd dog, or collie.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep laurel</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small North American shrub (<spn>Kalmia
angustifolia</spn>) with deep rose-colored flowers in corymbs.</cd> --
<col><b>Sheep pest</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an Australian plant
(<spn>Acaena ovina</spn>) related to the burnet. The fruit is covered
with barbed spines, by which it adheres to the wool of sheep.</cd> --
<col><b>Sheep run</b></col>, <cd>an extensive tract of country where
sheep range and graze.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep's beard</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a cichoraceous herb (<spn>Urospermum
Dalechampii</spn>) of Southern Europe; -- so called from the conspicuous
pappus of the achenes.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep's bit</b></col><cd>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, a European herb (<spn>Jasione montana</spn>) having
much the appearance of scabious.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep pox</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a contagious disease of sheep, characterixed by
the development of vesicles or pocks upon the skin.</cd> --
<col><b>Sheep scabious</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Sheep's bit</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep shears</b></col>, <cd>shears
in which the blades form the two ends of a steel bow, by the elasticity
of which they open as often as pressed together by the hand in cutting;
-- so called because used to cut off the wool of sheep.</cd> --
<col><b>Sheep sorrel</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a prerennial herb
(<spn>Rumex Acetosella</spn>) growing naturally on poor, dry, gravelly
soil. Its leaves have a pleasant acid taste like sorrel.</cd> --
<col><b>Sheep's-wool</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the highest grade
of Florida commercial sponges (<spn>Spongia equina</spn>, variety
<varn>gossypina</varn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep tick</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a wingless parasitic insect (<spn>Melophagus
ovinus</spn>) belonging to the Diptera. It fixes its proboscis in the
skin of the sheep and sucks the blood, leaving a swelling. Called also
<altname>sheep pest</altname>, and <altname>sheep louse</altname>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sheep walk</b></col>, <cd>a pasture for sheep; a sheep
run.</cd> -- <col><b>Wild sheep</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Argali</er>, <er>Mouflon</er>, and <er>Oorial</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepback</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>A rounded knoll of rock resembling the back of a sheep. -- produced
by glacial action. Called also <altname>roche
moutonn<eacute/e</altname>; -- usually in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepberry</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"ber`ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The edible fruit of a small North American tree of the genus
<gen>Viburnum</gen> (<spn>Viburnum Lentago</spn>), having white flowers
in flat cymes; also, the tree itself. Called also
<altname>nannyberry</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepbite</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"bite`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To bite or
nibble like a sheep; hence, to practice petty thefts.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepbiter</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"bit`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who practices
petty thefts.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are political <qex>sheepbiters</qex> as well as pastoral;
betrayers of public trusts as well as of private.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepcote</ent><br/
<ent>Sheepcot</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sheep"cot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sheep"cote`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small inclosure for sheep; a
pen; a fold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheep-faced</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"-faced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Over-bashful;
sheepish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepfold</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"fold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fold or pen for
sheep; a place where sheep are collected or confined.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheep-headed</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"-head`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Silly;
simple-minded; stupid.</def> <rj><au>Taylor (1630)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheephook</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"hook`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hook fastened to
pole, by which shepherds lay hold on the legs or necks of their sheep; a
shepherd's crook.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepish</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
sheep.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Like a sheep; bashful; over-modest; meanly or
foolishly diffident; timorous to excess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wanting change of company, he will, when he comes abroad, be a
<qex>sheepish</qex> or conceited creature.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sheep"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sheep"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepmaster</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"mas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A keeper or
feeder of sheep; also, an owner of sheep.</def> <rj><au>2 Kings iii.
4.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheeprack</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"rack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The starling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheep's-eye</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep's"-eye`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A modest,
diffident look; a loving glance; -- commonly in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I saw her just now give him the languishing eye, as they call it;
. . . of old called the <qex>sheep's-eye</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Wycherley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheep's-foot</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep's-foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A printer's tool
consisting of a metal bar formed into a hammer head at one end and a
claw at the other, -- used as a lever and hammer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepshank</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"shank`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A hitch by which a rope may be temporarily shortened.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepshead</ent><br/
<hw>Sheeps"head`</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called
because of the fancied resemblance of its head and front teeth to those
of a sheep.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large and valuable sparoid
food fish (<spn>Archosargus probatocephalus</spn> syn. <spn>Diplodus
probatocephalus</spn>) found on the Atlantic coast of the United States.
It often weighs from ten to twelve pounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is also locally, in a loose way, applied to
various other fishes, as the butterfish, the fresh-water drumfish, the
parrot fish, the porgy, and the moonfish.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheep-shearer</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"-shear`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
shears, or cuts off the wool from, sheep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheep-shearing</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"-shear`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Act of shearing sheep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A feast at the time of sheep-shearing.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepskin</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"skin`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
skin of a sheep; or, leather prepared from it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A diploma; -- so called because usually written or
printed on parchment prepared from the skin of the sheep.</def>
<mark>[College Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepsplit</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"split`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A split of a
sheepskin; one of the thin sections made by splitting a sheepskin with a
cutting knife or machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheepy</ent><br/
<hw>Sheep"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling sheep;
sheepish.</def> <rj><au>Testament of Love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheer</ent><br/
<hw>Sheer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shere</ets>,
<ets>skere</ets>, pure, bright, Icel. <ets>sk<?/rr</ets>; akin to
<ets>sk<imac/rr</ets>, AS. <ets>sc<imac/r</ets>, OS.
<ets>sk<imac/ri</ets>, MHG. <ets>sch<imac/r</ets>, G. <ets>schier</ets>,
Dan. <ets>sk<?/r</ets>, Sw. <ets>sk<aum/r</ets>, Goth. <ets>skeirs</ets>
clear, and E. <ets>shine</ets>. <root/157. See <er>Shine</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Bright; clear; pure; unmixed.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Sheer</xex> ale.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou <qex>sheer</qex>, immaculate, and silver fountain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Very thin or transparent; -- applied to fabrics;
<as>as, <ex>sheer</ex> muslin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Being only what it seems to be; obvious; simple;
mere; downright; <as>as, <ex>sheer</ex> folly; <ex>sheer</ex>
nonsense</as>.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>sheer</xex> impossibility.<rdquo/
<rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is not a <qex>sheer</qex> advantage to have several strings to
one's bow.</q> <rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Stright up and down; vertical;
prpendicular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sheer</qex> precipice of a thousand feet.</q> <rj><qau>J.
D. Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was at least<br/
Nine roods of <qex>sheer</qex> ascent.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheer</ent><br/
<hw>Sheer</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Clean; quite; at once.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheer</ent><br/
<hw>Sheer</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Shear</er>.]</ety>
<def>To shear.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheer</ent><br/
<hw>Sheer</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sheered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sheering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[D. <ets>sheren</ets> to shear,
cut, withdraw, warp. See <er>Shear</er>.]</ety> <def>To decline or
deviate from the line of the proper course; to turn aside; to swerve;
<as>as, a ship <ex>sheers</ex> from her course; a horse <ex>sheers</ex>
at a bicycle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sheer off</b></col>, <cd>to turn or move aside to a
distance; to move away.</cd> -- <col><b>To sheer up</b></col>, <cd>to
approach obliquely.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheer</ent><br/
<hw>Sheer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The longitudinal upward curvature of the deck,
gunwale, and lines of a vessel, as when viewed from the side.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The position of a vessel riding at single anchor and
swinging clear of it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A turn or change in a course.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give the canoe a <qex>sheer</qex> and get nearer to the shore.</q>
<rj><qau>Cooper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Shears See
<er>Shear</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sheer batten</b></col> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld>, <cd>a
long strip of wood to guide the carpenters in following the sheer
plan.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheer boom</b></col>, <cd>a boom slanting across a
stream to direct floating logs to one side.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheer
hulk</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Shear hulk</cref>, under
<er>Hulk</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sheer plan</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sheer draught</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld>, <cd>a
projection of the lines of a vessel on a vertical longitudinal plane
passing through the middle line of the vessel.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheer
pole</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>an iron rod lashed to the shrouds
just above the dead-eyes and parallel to the ratlines.</cd> --
<col><b>Sheer strake</b></col> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld>, <cd>the strake
under the gunwale on the top side.</cd> <au>Totten.</au> -- <col><b>To
break sheer</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to deviate from sheer, and
risk fouling the anchor.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheerly</ent><br/
<hw>Sheer"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>At once;
absolutely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheerwater</ent><br/
<hw>Sheer"wa`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The shearwater.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheet</ent><br/
<hw>Sheet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shete</ets>,
<ets>schete</ets>, AS. <ets>sc<emac/te</ets>, <ets>sc<ymac/te</ets>, fr.
<ets>sce<aacute/t</ets> a projecting corner, a fold in a garment (akin
to D. <ets>schoot</ets> sheet, bosom, lap, G. <ets>schoss</ets> bosom,
lap, flap of a coat, Icel. <ets>skaut</ets>, Goth. <ets>skauts</ets> the
hem of a garment); originally, that which shoots out, from the root of
AS. <ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets> to shoot. <root/159. See <er>Shoot</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>In general, a large, broad piece of
anything thin, as paper, cloth, etc.; a broad, thin portion of any
substance; an expanded superficies.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A broad piece of cloth, usually linen or cotton, used for wrapping
the body or for a covering; especially, one used as an article of
bedding next to the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He fell into a trance, and saw heaven opened, and a certain vessel
descending unto him, as it had been a great <qex>sheet</qex> knit at the
four corners.</q> <rj><qau>Acts x. 10, 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If I do die before thee, prithee, shroud me<br/
In one of those same <qex>sheets</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A broad piece of paper, whether folded or unfolded,
whether blank or written or printed upon; hence, a letter; a newspaper,
etc.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A single signature of a book or a
pamphlet;</def> in <pluf>pl.</pluf>, <def>the book itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To this the following <qex>sheets</qex> are intended for a full
and distinct answer.</q> <rj><qau>Waterland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>A broad, thinly expanded portion of metal or other
substance; <as>as, a <ex>sheet</ex> of copper, of glass, or the like; a
plate; a leaf</as>.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>A broad expanse of water, or
the like.</def> <ldquo/The two beautiful <xex>sheets</xex> of
water.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>A sail.</def>
<au>Dryden.</au> <sd>(g)</sd> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>An extensive bed
of an eruptive rock intruded between, or overlying, other
strata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[AS. <ets>sce<aacute/ta</ets>. See the Etymology
above.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A rope or chain which
regulates the angle of adjustment of a sail in relation in relation to
the wind; -- usually attached to the lower corner of a sail, or to a
yard or a boom.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The space in
the forward or the after part of a boat where there are no rowers;
<as>as, fore <ex>sheets</ex>; stern <ex>sheets</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sheet</xex> is often used adjectively, or in
combination, to denote that the substance to the name of which it is
prefixed is in the form of sheets, or thin plates or leaves; as,
<xex>sheet</xex> brass, or <xex>sheet</xex>-brass; <xex>sheet</xex>
glass, or <xex>sheet</xex>-glass; <xex>sheet</xex> gold, or
<xex>sheet</xex>-gold; sheet iron, or <xex>sheet</xex>-iron,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>A sheet in the wind</b></col>, <cd>half drunk.</cd>
<mark>[Sailors' Slang]</mark> -- <col><b>Both sheets in the
wind</b></col>, <cd>very drunk.</cd> <mark>[Sailors' Slang]</mark> --
<col><b>In sheets</b></col>, <cd>lying flat or expanded; not folded, or
folded but not bound; -- said especially of printed sheets.</cd> --
<col><b>Sheet bend</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a bend or hitch
used for temporarily fastening a rope to the bight of another rope or to
an eye.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sheet lightning</b></col>, <col><b>Sheet
piling</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Lightning</er>,
<er>Piling</er>, etc.</cd></cs> <-- Three sheets to the wind, very drunk
(now more common than "both sheets in the wind" --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheet</ent><br/
<hw>Sheet</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sheeted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sheeting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To furnish with a
sheet or sheets; to wrap in, or cover with, a sheet, or as with a
sheet.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>sheeted</xex> dead.<rdquo/ <ldquo/When snow
the pasture <xex>sheets</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1327 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To expand, as a sheet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The star shot flew from the welkin blue,<br/
As it fell from the <qex>sheeted</qex> sky.</q> <rj><qau>J. R.
Drake.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sheet home</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to haul
upon a sheet until the sail is as flat, and the clew as near the wind,
as possible.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheet anchor</ent><br/
<hw>Sheet" an"chor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[OE. <ets>scheten</ets> to
shoot, AS. <ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets>; cf. OE. <ets>shoot anchor</ets>.
See <er>Shoot</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A large anchor stowed on shores outside the
waist of a vessel; -- called also <altname>waist anchor</altname>. See
the Note under <er>Anchor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything regarded as a sure support or dependence in
danger; the best hope or refuge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheet cable</ent><br/
<hw>Sheet" ca"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The cable
belonging to the sheet anchor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheet chain</ent><br/
<hw>Sheet" chain"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A chain
sheet cable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheetful</ent><br/
<hw>Sheet"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sheetfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>Enough to fill a sheet;
as much as a sheet can hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheeting</ent><br/
<hw>Sheet"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Cotton or linen cloth
suitable for bed sheets. It is sometimes made of double width.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Hydraul. Engin.)</fld> <def>A lining of planks or
boards (rarely of metal) for protecting an embankment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act or process of forming into sheets, or flat
pieces; also, material made into sheets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheik</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sheik</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sheikh</ets>, <ets>shaykh</ets>, a venerable old man, a chief, fr.
<ets>sh<amac/kha</ets> to grow or be old.]</ety> <def>The head of an
Arab family, or of a clan or a tribe; also, the chief magistrate of an
Arab village. The name is also applied to Mohammedan ecclesiastics of a
high grade.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>scheik</asp>,
<asp>shaik</asp>, <asp>sheikh</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheiling</ent><br/
<ent>Sheil</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sheil</hw> <pr>(sh<emac/l)</pr>, <hw>Sheil"ing</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Sheeling</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shekel</ent><br/
<hw>Shek"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>shegel</ets>, fr. <ets>sh<amac/gal</ets> to weigh.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An ancient weight and coin used by the Jews and by
other nations of the same stock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A common estimate makes the shekel equal in weight to
about 130 grains for gold, 224 grains for silver, and 450 grains for
copper, and the approximate values of the coins are (gold) $5.00,
(silver) 60 cents, and (copper half shekel), one and one half
cents.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A jocose term for
<xex>money</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shekinah</ent><br/
<hw>She*ki"nah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb Talmud
<ets>shek<imac/n<amac/h</ets>, fr. <ets>sh<amac/kan</ets> to
inhabit.]</ety> <def>The visible majesty of the Divine Presence,
especially when resting or dwelling between the cherubim on the mercy
seat, in the Tabernacle, or in the Temple of Solomon; -- a term used in
the Targums and by the later Jews, and adopted by Christians.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>Shechinah</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Dr. W. Smith
(Bib. Dict.)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheld</ent><br/
<hw>Sheld</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE., fr.
<ets>sheld</ets> a shield, probably in allusion to the ornamentation of
shields. See <er>Shield</er>.]</ety> <def>Variegated; spotted; speckled;
piebald.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheldaple</ent><br/
<ent>Sheldafle</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sheld"a*fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sheld"a*ple</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps for <ets>sheld
dapple</ets>. Cf. <er>Sheldrake</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
chaffinch.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sheldapple</asp>, and
<asp>shellapple</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheldfowl</ent><br/
<hw>Sheld"fowl`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The common sheldrake.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheldrake</ent><br/
<hw>Shel"drake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sheld</ets>
+ <ets>drake</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one
of several species of large Old World ducks of the genus
<gen>Tadorna</gen> and allied genera, especially the European and
Asiatic species. (<spn>Tadorna cornuta</spn> syn. <spn>Tadorna
tadorna</spn>), which somewhat resembles a goose in form and habit, but
breeds in burrows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It has the head and neck greenish black, the breast,
sides, and forward part of the back brown, the shoulders and middle of
belly black, the speculum green, and the bill and frontal bright red.
Called also <altname>shelduck</altname>, <altname>shellduck</altname>,
<altname>sheldfowl</altname>, <altname>skeelduck</altname>,
<altname>bergander</altname>, <altname>burrow duck</altname>, and
<altname>links goose</altname>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The Australian sheldrake (<spn>Tadorna radja</spn>) has
the head, neck, breast, flanks, and wing coverts white, the upper part
of the back and a band on the breast deep chestnut, and the back and
tail black. The chestnut sheldrake of Australia (<spn>Casarca
tadornoides</spn>) is varied with black and chestnut, and has a dark
green head and neck. The ruddy sheldrake, or Braminy duck (<spn>Casarca
rutila</spn>), and the white-winged sheldrake (<spn>Casarca
leucoptera</spn>), are related Asiatic species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any one of the American mergansers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is also loosely applied to other ducks, as the
canvasback, and the shoveler.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelduck</ent><br/
<hw>Shel"duck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sheld</ets>
variegated + <ets>duck</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
sheldrake.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>shellduck</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelf</ent><br/
<hw>Shelf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shelves</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>shelfe</ets>,
<ets>schelfe</ets>, AS. <ets>scylfe</ets>; akin to G.
<ets>schelfe</ets>, Icel. <ets>skj<amac/lf</ets>. In senses 2 & 3,
perhaps a different word (cf. <er>Shelve</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>).]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A flat tablet or ledge of any
material set horizontally at a distance from the floor, to hold objects
of use or ornament.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sand bank in the sea, or a rock, or ledge of
rocks, rendering the water shallow, and dangerous to ships.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On the tawny sands and <qex>shelves</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On the secret <qex>shelves</qex> with fury cast.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A stratum lying in a very even
manner; a flat, projecting layer of rock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A piece of timber running the
whole length of a vessel inside the timberheads.</def> <rj><au>D.
Kemp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To lay on the shelf</b></col>, <cd>to lay aside as
unnecessary or useless; to dismiss; to discard.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelfy</ent><br/
<hw>Shelf"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding
in shelves; full of dangerous shallows.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>shelfy</xex>
coast.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Full of strata of rock.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tillable fields are in some places . . . so <qex>shelfy</qex>
that the corn hath much ado to fasten its root.</q>
<rj><qau>Carew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shell</ent><br/
<hw>Shell</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shelle</ets>,
<ets>schelle</ets>, AS. <ets>scell</ets>, <ets>scyll</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>shel</ets>, Icel. <ets>skel</ets>, Goth. <ets>skalja</ets> a tile,
and E. <ets>skill</ets>. Cf. <er>Scale</er> of fishes, <er>Shale</er>,
<er>Skill</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A hard outside covering, as of a
fruit or an animal.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The covering,
or outside part, of a nut; <as>as, a hazelnut <ex>shell</ex></as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A pod.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The hard covering of an
egg.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Think him as a serpent's egg, . . .<br/
And kill him in the <qex>shell</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The hard calcareous or chitinous
external covering of mollusks, crustaceans, and some other
invertebrates. In some mollusks, as the cuttlefishes, it is internal, or
concealed by the mantle. Also, the hard covering of some vertebrates, as
the armadillo, the tortoise, and the like.</def> <sd>(e)</sd>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Hence, by extension, any mollusks having such a
covering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A hollow projectile, of various
shapes, adapted for a mortar or a cannon, and containing an explosive
substance, ignited with a fuse or by percussion, by means of which the
projectile is burst and its fragments scattered. See
<er>Bomb</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The case which holds the powder, or charge of powder
and shot, used with breechloading small arms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Any slight hollow structure; a framework, or
exterior structure, regarded as not complete or filled in; <as>as, the
<ex>shell</ex> of a house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A coarse kind of coffin; also, a thin interior
coffin inclosed in a more substantial one.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An instrument of music, as a lyre, -- the first lyre
having been made, it is said, by drawing strings over a tortoise
shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When Jubal struck the chorded <qex>shell</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>An engraved copper roller used in print
works.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The husks of cacao seeds, a
decoction of which is often used as a substitute for chocolate, cocoa,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The outer frame or case of a
block within which the sheaves revolve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A light boat the frame of which is covered with
thin wood or with paper; <as>as, a racing <ex>shell</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Something similar in form or action to an ordnance
shell;</def> <specif>specif.:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Fireworks)</fld> <def>A case or cartridge containing a charge of
explosive material, which bursts after having been thrown high into the
air. It is often elevated through the agency of a larger firework in
which it is contained.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Oil Wells)</fld> <def>A
torpedo.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>A concave rough cast-iron tool in which a convex
lens is ground to shape.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>A gouge bit or shell bit.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Message shell</b></col>, <cd>a bombshell inside of which
papers may be put, in order to convey messages.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell
bit</b></col>, <cd>a tool shaped like a gouge, used with a brace in
boring wood. See <er>Bit</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell
button</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A button made of shell</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A hollow button made of two pieces, as of metal, one
for the front and the other for the back, -- often covered with cloth,
silk, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell cameo</b></col>, <cd>a cameo cut in
shell instead of stone.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell flower</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Same as <er>Turtlehead</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell
gland</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A glandular organ
in which the rudimentary shell is formed in embryonic mollusks</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A glandular organ which secretes the eggshells of
various worms, crustacea, mollusks, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell
gun</b></col>, <cd>a cannon suitable for throwing shells.</cd> --
<col><b>Shell ibis</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the openbill of
India.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell jacket</b></col>, <cd>an undress military
jacket.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell lime</b></col>, <cd>lime made by burning
the shells of shellfish.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell marl</b></col>
<fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of marl characterized by an abundance of
shells, or fragments of shells.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell meat</b></col>,
<cd>food consisting of shellfish, or testaceous mollusks.</cd>
<au>Fuller.</au> -- <col><b>Shell mound</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Mound</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell of a boiler</b></col>, <cd>the
exterior of a steam boiler, forming a case to contain the water and
steam, often inclosing also flues and the furnace; the barrel of a
cylindrical, or locomotive, boiler.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell
road</b></col>, <cd>a road of which the surface or bed is made of
shells, as oyster shells.</cd> -- <col><b>Shell sand</b></col>,
<cd>minute fragments of shells constituting a considerable part of the
seabeach in some places.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shell</ent><br/
<hw>Shell</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shelled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strip or break
off the shell of; to take out of the shell, pod, etc.; <as>as, to
<ex>shell</ex> nuts or pease; to <ex>shell</ex> oysters.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To separate the kernels of (an ear of Indian corn,
wheat, oats, etc.) from the cob, ear, or husk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To throw shells or bombs upon or into; to bombard;
<as>as, to <ex>shell</ex> a town</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shell out</b></col>, <cd>to distribute freely; to
bring out or pay, as money.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shell</ent><br/
<hw>Shell</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fall off, as a
shell, crust, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cast the shell, or exterior covering; to fall out
of the pod or husk; <as>as, nuts <ex>shell</ex> in
falling</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be disengaged from the ear or husk; <as>as, wheat
or rye <ex>shells</ex> in reaping</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shell-lac</ent><br/
<ent>Shellac</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shel"lac`</hw>, <hw>Shell"-lac`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Shell</ets> + <ets>lac</ets> a resinous
substance; cf. D. <ets>shellak</ets>, G. <ets>schellack</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Lac which has been reduced to a thin crust. See the
Note under 2d <er>Lac</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A solution of shellac{1} in alcohol or other
volatile solvent, used as a varnish.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A phonograph record, made of a material containing
shellac; -- no longer manufactured.</def> <mark>[Obsolescent]</mark>
<au>RHUD</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shellapple</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"ap`ple</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Sheldafle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shellbark</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"bark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A species of hickory (<spn>Carya alba</spn>) whose outer bark is
loose and peeling; a shagbark; also, its nut.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelled</ent><br/
<hw>Shelled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheller</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, shells; <as>as, an oyster <ex>sheller</ex>; a corn
<ex>sheller</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shellfish</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any aquatic animal whose external covering consists of a shell,
either testaceous, as in oysters, clams, and other mollusks, or
crustaceous, as in lobsters and crabs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelling</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Groats; hulled oats.</def>
<rj><au>Simmonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shell-less</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"-less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no shell.</def>
<rj><au>J. Burroughs.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shellproof</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"proof`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
resisting bombs or other shells; bombproof.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shellwork</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Work composed of
shells, or adorned with them.</def> <rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelly</ent><br/
<hw>Shell"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding with shells;
consisting of shells, or of a shell.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>shelly</xex>
shore.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shrinks backward in his <qex>shelly</qex> cave.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelter</ent><br/
<hw>Shel"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OE.
<ets>scheltrun</ets>, <ets>shiltroun</ets>, <ets>schelltrome</ets>,
<ets>scheldtrome</ets>, a guard, squadron, AS. <ets>scildtruma</ets> a
troop of men with shields; <ets>scild</ets> shield + <ets>truma</ets> a
band of men. See <er>Shield</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>That which covers or defends from injury or annoyance; a
protection; a screen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sick and weak the healing plant shall aid,<br/
From storms a <qex>shelter</qex>, and from heat a shade.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who protects; a guardian; a defender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou [God] hast been a <qex>shelter</qex> for me.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
lxi. 3. </qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The state of being covered and protected;
protection; security.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who into <qex>shelter</qex> takes their tender bloom.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shelter tent</b></col>,<cd>a small tent made of pieces of
cotton duck arranged to button together. In field service the soldiers
carry the pieces.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Asylum; refuge; retreat; covert; sanctuary;
protection; defense; security.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelter</ent><br/
<hw>Shel"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sheltered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sheltering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be a
shelter for; to provide with a shelter; to cover from injury or
annoyance; to shield; to protect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those ruins <qex>sheltered</qex> once his sacred head.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have no convents . . . in which such persons may be received
and <qex>sheltered</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To screen or cover from notice; to
disguise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In vain I strove to cheek my growing flame,<br/
Or <qex>shelter</qex> passion under friendship's name.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To betake to cover, or to a safe place; -- used
reflexively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>sheltered</qex> themselves under a rock.</q>
<rj><qau>Abp. Abbot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelter</ent><br/
<hw>Shel"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To take shelter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There oft the Indian herdsman, shunning heat,<br/
<qex>Shelters</qex> in cool.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelterless</ent><br/
<hw>Shel"ter*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of shelter or
protection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now sad and <qex>shelterless</qex> perhaps she lies.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheltery</ent><br/
<hw>Shel"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affording
shelter.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelty</ent><br/
<ent>Sheltie</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shel"tie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shel"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Shetland pony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelve</ent><br/
<hw>Shelve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
furnish with shelves; <as>as, to <ex>shelve</ex> a closet or a
library</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To place on a shelf. Hence: To lay on the shelf; to
put aside; to dismiss from service; to put off indefinitely; <as>as, to
<ex>shelve</ex> an officer; to <ex>shelve</ex> a claim</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelve</ent><br/
<hw>Shelve</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shelved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shelving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Perhapss originally from the
same source as <ets>shallow</ets>, but influenced by <ets>shelf</ets> a
ledge, a platform.]</ety> <def>To incline gradually; to be slopping;
<as>as, the bottom <ex>shelves</ex> from the shore</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelving</ent><br/
<hw>Shelv"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sloping gradually; inclining;
<as>as, a <ex>shelving</ex> shore</as>.</def> <au>Shak.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Shelving</xex> arches.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelving</ent><br/
<hw>Shelv"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of fitting up
shelves; <as>as, the job of <ex>shelving</ex> a closet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of laying on a shelf, or on the shelf;
putting off or aside; <as>as, the <ex>shelving</ex> of a
claim</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Material for shelves; shelves,
collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shelvy</ent><br/
<hw>Shelv"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sloping gradually;
shelving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The shore was <qex>shelving</qex> and shallow.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shemite</ent><br/
<hw>Shem"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A descendant of
Shem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shemitish</ent><br/
<ent>Shemitic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shem*it"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shem"i*tish</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
<xex>Shem</xex>, the son of Noah, or his descendants. See
<er>Semitic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shemitism</ent><br/
<hw>Shem"i*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Semitism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shend</ent><br/
<hw>Shend</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shent</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>scendan</ets> to
disgrace, bring to shame, from <ets>sceand</ets>, <ets>sceond</ets>,
disgrace, dishonor, shame; akin to G. <ets>schande</ets>, Goth.
<ets>skanda</ets>. See <er>Shame</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To injure, mar, spoil, or harm.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Loss of time <xex>shendeth</xex> us.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I fear my body will be <qex>shent</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To blame, reproach, or revile; to degrade, disgrace,
or put to shame.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>R.
Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The famous name of knighthood foully <qex>shend</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She passed the rest as Cynthia doth <qex>shend</qex><br/
The lesser stars.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shendful</ent><br/
<hw>Shend"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destructive;
ruinous; disgraceful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Shend"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms> <rj><au>Fabyan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shendship</ent><br/
<hw>Shend"ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Harm; ruin; also, reproach;
disgrace.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shent</ent><br/
<hw>Shent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>obs. 3d pers. sing. pres.</pos>
of <er>Shend</er>, for <xex>shendeth</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shent</ent><br/
<hw>Shent</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To shend.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheol</ent><br/
<hw>She"ol</hw> <pr>(sh<emac/"<omac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>sh<ecr/<omac/l</ets>.]</ety> <def>The place of departed spirits;
Hades; also, the grave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For thou wilt not leave my soul to <qex>sheol</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xvi. 10. (Rev. Ver.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepen</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stable; a
shippen.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shepne</qex> brenning with the blacke smoke.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepherd</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"herd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schepherde</ets>, <ets>schephirde</ets>, AS.
<ets>sce<aacute/phyrde</ets>; <ets>sce<aacute/p</ets> sheep +
<ets>hyrde</ets>, <ets>hirde</ets>, <ets>heorde</ets>, a herd, a
guardian. See <er>Sheep</er>, and <er>Herd</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A man employed in tending, feeding, and guarding sheep, esp. a
flock grazing at large.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The pastor of a church; one with the religious
guidance of others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shepherd bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
crested screamer. See <er>Screamer</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shepherd
dog</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a breed of dogs used largely for
the herding and care of sheep. There are several kinds, as the collie,
or Scotch shepherd dog, and the English shepherd dog. Called also
<altname>shepherd's dog</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shepherd
dog</b></col>, <cd>a name of Pan.</cd> <au>Keats.</au> --
<col><b>Shepherd kings</b></col>, <cd>the chiefs of a nomadic people who
invaded Egypt from the East in the traditional period, and conquered it,
at least in part. They were expelled after about five hundred years, and
attempts have been made to connect their expulsion with narrative in the
book of Exodus.</cd> -- <col><b>Shepherd's club</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the common mullein. See <er>Mullein</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Shepherd's crook</b></col>, <cd>a long staff having the end
curved so as to form a large hook, -- used by shepherds.</cd> --
<col><b>Shepherd's needle</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the lady's
comb.</cd> -- <col><b>Shepherd's plaid</b></col>, <cd>a kind of woolen
cloth of a checkered black and white pattern.</cd> -- <col><b>Shephered
spider</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a daddy longlegs, or
harvestman.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Shepherd's pouch</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Shepherd's purse</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>an annual cruciferous plant (<spn>Capsella Bursapastoris</spn>)
bearing small white flowers and pouchlike pods.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Shepherd's rod</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Shepherd's
staff</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the small
teasel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepherd</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"herd</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shepherded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shepherding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To tend as a shepherd; to
guard, herd, lead, or drive, as a shepherd.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>White, fleecy clouds . . . </q><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shepherded</qex> by the slow, unwilling wind.</q>
<rj><qau>Shelley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepherdess</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"herd*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who tends sheep;
hence, a rural lass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She put herself into the garb of a <qex>shepherdess</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepherdia</ent><br/
<hw>Shep*her"di*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shepherdias</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. So called from John
<ets>Shepherd</ets>, an English botanist.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A genus of shrubs having silvery scurfy leaves, and belonging to
the same family as Elaeagnus; also, any plant of this genus. See
<cref>Buffalo berry</cref>, under <er>Buffalo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1328 --></p>

<p><ent>Shepherdish</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"herd*ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Resembling a
shepherd; suiting a shepherd; pastoral.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepherdism</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"herd*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pastoral life or
occupation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepherdling</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"herd*ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little
shepherd.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepherdly</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"herd*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling, or
becoming to, a shepherd; pastoral; rustic.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shepster</ent><br/
<hw>Shep"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A seamstress.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Caxton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sherardize</ent><br/
<hw>Sher"ard*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[From
<etsep>Sherard</etsep> Cowper-Coles, the inventor.]</ety>
<fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>To subject to the process of vapor galvanizing
(which see, below).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sherbet</ent><br/
<hw>Sher"bet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sherbet</ets>, <ets>shorbet</ets>, <ets>sharbat</ets>, properly,
one drink or sip, a draught, beverage, from <ets>shariba</ets> to drink.
Cf. <er>Sorbet</er>, <er>Sirup</er>, <er>Shrub</er> a drink.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A refreshing drink, common in the East, made of the
juice of some fruit, diluted, sweetened, and flavored in various ways;
<as>as, orange <ex>sherbet</ex>; lemon <ex>sherbet</ex>; raspberry
<ex>sherbet</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A flavored water ice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A preparation of bicarbonate of soda, tartaric acid,
sugar, etc., variously flavored, for making an effervescing drink; --
called also <altname>sherbet powder</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sherd</ent><br/
<hw>Sherd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fragment; -- now used
only in composition, as in pot<ex>sherd</ex>. See
<er>Shard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The thigh . . . which all in <qex>sherds</qex> it drove.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sherif</ent><br/
<ent>Shereef</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Sher"eef</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Sher"if</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar. <ets>sher<imac/f</ets>
noble, holy, n., a prince.]</ety> <def>A member of an Arab princely
family descended from Mohammed through his son-in-law Ali and daughter
Fatima. The Grand Shereef is the governor of Mecca.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheriat</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sher"i*at</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Turk.
<ets>sher<imac/ 'at</ets>]</ety> <def>The sacred law of the Turkish
empire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheriff</ent><br/
<hw>Sher"iff</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shereve</ets>, AS.
<ets>sc<imac/r-ger<?/fa</ets>; <ets>sc<imac/r</ets> a shire +
<ets>ger<?/fa</ets> a reeve. See Shire, and <er>Reeve</er>, and cf.
<er>Shrievalty</er>.]</ety> <def>The chief officer of a shire or county,
to whom is intrusted the execution of the laws, the serving of judicial
writs and processes, and the preservation of the peace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In England, sheriffs are appointed by the king. In the
United States, sheriffs are elected by the legislature or by the
citizens, or appointed and commissioned by the executive of the State.
The office of sheriff in England is judicial and ministerial. In the
United States, it is mainly ministerial. The sheriff, by himself or his
deputies, executes civil and criminal process throughout the county, has
charge of the jail and prisoners, attends courts, and keeps the peace.
His judicial authority is generally confined to ascertaining damages on
writs of inquiry and the like. <xex>Sheriff</xex>, in Scotland, called
<xex>sheriff depute</xex>, is properly a judge, having also certain
ministerial powers. <xex>Sheriff clerk</xex> is the clerk of the
Sheriff's Court in Scotland. <xex>Sheriff's Court in London</xex> is a
tribunal having cognizance of certain personal actions in that
city.</note> <rj><au>Wharton, Tomlins. Erskine.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheriffwick</ent><br/
<ent>Sheriffship</ent><br/
<ent>Sheriffry</ent><br/
<ent>Sheriffdom</ent><br/
<ent>Sheriffalty</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sher"iff*al*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sher"iff*dom</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sher"iff*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sher"iff*ship</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sher"iff*wick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> }</mhw>
<def>The office or jurisdiction of sheriff. See
<er>Shrievalty</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shern</ent><br/
<hw>Shern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shearn</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sherris</ent><br/
<hw>Sher"ris</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sherry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sherry</ent><br/
<hw>Sher"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from
<ets>Xeres</ets>, a Spanish town near Cadiz, <it>x</it> in Spanish
having been formerly pronounced like <it>sh</it> in English.]</ety>
<def>A Spanish light-colored dry wine, made in Andalusia. As prepared
for commerce it is colored a straw color or a deep amber by mixing with
it cheap wine boiled down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sherry cobbler</b></col>, <cd>a beverage prepared with
sherry wine, water, lemon or orange, sugar, ice, etc., and usually
imbided through a straw or a glass tube.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sherryvallies</ent><br/
<hw>Sher"ry*val`lies</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sp.
<ets>zaraquelles</ets> wide breeches or overalls.]</ety> <def>Trousers
or overalls of thick cloth or leather, buttoned on the outside of each
leg, and generally worn to protect other trousers when riding on
horseback.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shet</ent><br/
<hw>Shet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Shet</conjf>. (<mark>Obs.</mark>
<conjf>Shette</conjf> <pr>(<?/ <or/ <?/)</pr>); <pos>p. pr.</pos>
<conjf>Shet</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shetting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To shut.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shete</ent><br/
<hw>Shete</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To shoot.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sheth</ent><br/
<hw>Sheth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The part of a plow which
projects downward beneath the beam, for holding the share and other
working parts; -- also called <altname>standard</altname>, or
<altname>post</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shetland pony</ent><br/
<hw>Shet"land po"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>One of a small, hardy breed
of horses, with long mane and tail, which originated in the Shetland
Islands; a sheltie.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shew</ent><br/
<hw>Shew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Show</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shew</ent><br/
<hw>Shew</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Show.</def> <mark>[Obs. <plain>except
in</plain> shewbread.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shewbread</ent><br/
<hw>Shew"bread`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See
<er>Showbread</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shewel</ent><br/
<hw>Shew"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scarecrow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Trench.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shewer</ent><br/
<hw>Shew"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who shews. See
<er>Shower</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shewn</ent><br/
<hw>Shewn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shew</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiah</ent><br/
<hw>Shi"ah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Shiite</er>; when used with a plural verb, the Shiites,
collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shibboleth</ent><br/
<hw>Shib"bo*leth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>shibb<omac/leth</ets> an ear of corn, or a stream, a flood.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A word which was made the criterion by which to
distinguish the Ephraimites from the Gileadites. The Ephraimites, not
being able to pronounce <xex>sh</xex>, called the word
<xex>sibboleth</xex>. See <au>Judges xii.</au></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Without reprieve, adjudged to death,<br/
For want of well pronouncing <qex>shibboleth</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><def>Also used in an extended sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>th</qex>, with its twofold value, is . . . the
<qex>shibboleth</qex> of foreigners.</q> <rj><qau>Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the criterion, test, or watchword of a party;
a party cry or pet phrase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shicer</ent><br/
<hw>Shi"cer</hw> <pr>(sh<imac/"s<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob.
fr. G. <ets>scheisser</ets> one who defecates.]</ety>
<fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>An unproductive mine; a duffer.</def>
<mark>[Australia]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shide</ent><br/
<hw>Shide</hw> <pr>(sh<imac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shide</ets>, <ets>schide</ets>, AS. <ets>sc<imac/de</ets>; akin to
OHG. <ets>sc<imac/t</ets>, G. <ets>scheit</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sk<imac/<edh/</ets>, and E. <ets>shed</ets>, v.t.]</ety> <def>A
thin board; a billet of wood; a splinter.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shie</ent><br/
<hw>Shie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See <er>Shy</er>, to
throw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shied</ent><br/
<hw>Shied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiel</ent><br/
<hw>Shiel</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sheeling.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shield</ent><br/
<hw>Shield</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sheld</ets>,
<ets>scheld</ets>, AS. <ets>scield</ets>, <ets>scild</ets>,
<ets>sceld</ets>, <ets>scyld</ets>; akin to OS. <ets>scild</ets>,
OFries. <ets>skeld</ets>, D. & G. <ets>schild</ets>, OHG.
<ets>scilt</ets>, Icel. <ets>skj<oum/ldr</ets>, Sw.
<ets>sk<oum/ld</ets>, Dan. <ets>skiold</ets>, Goth. <ets>skildus</ets>;
of uncertain origin. Cf. <er>Sheldrake</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
broad piece of defensive armor, carried on the arm, -- formerly in
general use in war, for the protection of the body. See
<er>Buckler</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now put your <qex>shields</qex> before your hearts and fight,<br/
With hearts more proof than <qex>shields</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything which protects or defends; defense;
shelter; protection.</def> <ldquo/My council is my
<xex>shield</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Figuratively, one who protects or defends.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fear not, Abram; I am thy <qex>shield</qex>, and thy exceeding
great reward.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xv. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>In lichens, a Hardened cup or disk
surrounded by a rim and containing the fructification, or
asci.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>The escutcheon or field on which
are placed the bearings in coats of arms. Cf.
<er>Lozenge</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mining & Tunneling)</fld> <def>A framework used to
protect workmen in making an adit under ground, and capable of being
pushed along as excavation progresses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A spot resembling, or having the form of, a
shield.</def> <ldquo/Bespotted as with <xex>shields</xex> of red and
black.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A coin, the old French crown, or <eacute/cu, having
on one side the figure of a shield.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shield fern</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any fern of
the genus <gen>Aspidium</gen>, in which the fructifications are covered
with shield-shaped indusia; -- called also <altname>wood
fern</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shield</ent><br/
<hw>Shield</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shielded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shielding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>scidan</ets>,
<ets>scyldan</ets>. <ets>See</ets> <er>Shield</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover with, or as with, a shield; to cover from
danger; to defend; to protect from assault or injury.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shouts of applause ran ringing through the field,<br/
To see the son the vanquished father <qex>shield</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A woman's shape doth <qex>shield</qex> thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To ward off; to keep off or out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They brought with them their usual weeds, fit to <qex>shield</qex>
the cold to which they had been inured.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To avert, as a misfortune; hence, as a supplicatory
exclamation, forbid!</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God <qex>shield</qex> that it should so befall.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God <qex>shield</qex> I should disturb devotion!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shield-bearer</ent><br/
<hw>Shield"-bear`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who, or that which, carries a shield.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any small moth of the genus
<gen>Aspidisca</gen>, whose larva makes a shieldlike covering for itself
out of bits of leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shielddrake</ent><br/
<hw>Shield"drake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A sheldrake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shieldless</ent><br/
<hw>Shield"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of a shield, or of
protection.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Shield"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-- <wf>Shield"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shieldtail</ent><br/
<hw>Shield"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of small burrowing snakes of the family
<fam>Uropeltidae</fam>, native of Ceylon and Southern Asia. They have a
small mouth which can not be dilated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shieling</ent><br/
<hw>Shiel"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hut or shelter for
shepherds of fishers. See <er>Sheeling</er>.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shift</ent><br/
<hw>Shift</hw> <pr>(sh<icr/ft)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shifted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shifting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shiften</ets>,
<ets>schiften</ets>, to divide, change, remove. AS. <ets>sciftan</ets>
to divide; akin to LG. & D. <ets>schiften</ets> to divide, distinguish,
part Icel. <ets>skipta</ets> to divide, to part, to shift, to change,
Dan <ets>skifte</ets>, Sw. <ets>skifta</ets>, and probably to Icel.
<ets>sk<imac/fa</ets> to cut into slices, as n., a slice, and to E.
<ets>shive</ets>, <ets>sheave</ets>, n., <ets>shiver</ets>, n.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To divide; to distribute; to apportion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To which God of his bounty would <qex>shift</qex><br/
Crowns two of flowers well smelling.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To change the place of; to move or remove from one
place to another; <as>as, to <ex>shift</ex> a burden from one shoulder
to another; to <ex>shift</ex> the blame.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hastily he <qex>schifte</qex> him[self].</q> <rj><qau>Piers
Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pare saffron between the two St. Mary's days,<br/
Or set or go <qex>shift</qex> it that knowest the ways.</q>
<rj><qau>Tusser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To change the position of; to alter the bearings of;
to turn; <as>as, to <ex>shift</ex> the helm or sails</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Carrying the oar loose, [they] <qex>shift</qex> it hither and
thither at pleasure.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To exchange for another of the same class; to remove
and to put some similar thing in its place; to change; <as>as, to
<ex>shift</ex> the clothes; to <ex>shift</ex> the scenes.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would advise you to <qex>shift</qex> a shirt.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To change the clothing of; -- used
reflexively.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As it were to ride day and night; and . . . not to have patience
to <qex>shift</qex> me.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To put off or out of the way by some
expedient.</def> <ldquo/I <xex>shifted</xex> him away.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shift off</b></col>, <cd>to delay; to defer; to put
off; to lay aside.</cd> -- <col><b>To shift the scene</b></col>, <cd>to
change the locality or the surroundings, as in a play or a
story.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shift the scene</qex> for half an hour;<br/
Time and place are in thy power.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shift</ent><br/
<hw>Shift</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To divide; to
distribute.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some this, some that, as that him liketh <qex>shift</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a change or changes; to change position; to
move; to veer; to substitute one thing for another; -- used in the
various senses of the transitive verb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sixth age <qex>shifts</qex><br/
Into the lean and slippered pantaloon.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here the Baillie <qex>shifted</qex> and fidgeted about in his
seat.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To resort to expedients for accomplishing a purpose;
to contrive; to manage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men in distress will look to themselves, and leave their
companions to <qex>shift</qex> as well as they can.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To practice indirect or evasive methods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All those schoolmen, though they were exceeding witty, yet better
teach all their followers to <qex>shift</qex>, than to resolve by their
distinctions.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To slip to one side of a ship, so
as to destroy the equilibrum; -- said of ballast or cargo; <as>as, the
cargo <ex>shifted</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shift</ent><br/
<hw>Shift</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>skipti</ets>. See <er>Shift</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of shifting.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The act of putting one thing in the place of another, or of
changing the place of a thing; change; substitution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My going to Oxford was not merely for <qex>shift</qex> of air.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> A turning from one thing to another; hence, an expedient
tried in difficulty; often, an evasion; a trick; a fraud. <ldquo/Reduced
to pitiable <xex>shifts</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I 'll find a thousand <qex>shifts</qex> to get away.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Little souls on little <qex>shifts</qex> rely.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something frequently shifted; especially, a woman's
under-garment; a chemise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The change of one set of workmen for another; hence,
a spell, or turn, of work; also, a set of workmen who work in turn with
other sets; <as>as, a night <ex>shift</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In building, the extent, or arrangement, of the
overlapping of plank, brick, stones, etc., that are placed in courses so
as to break joints.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A breaking off and dislocation
of a seam; a fault.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A change of the position of the
hand on the finger board, in playing the violin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To make shift</b></col>, <cd>to contrive or manage in an
exigency.</cd> <ldquo/I shall <xex>make shift</xex> to go without
him.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They] <qex>made a shift</qex> to keep their own in Ireland.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Shiftable</ent><br/
<hw>Shift"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Admitting of being
shifted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shifter</ent><br/
<hw>Shift"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, shifts; one who plays tricks or practices artifice; a
cozener.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T was such a <qex>shifter</qex> that, if truth were known,<br/
Death was half glad when he had got him down.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>An assistant to the ship's cook
in washing, steeping, and shifting the salt provisions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An arrangement for
shifting a belt sidewise from one pulley to another.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Knitting Mach.)</fld> <def>A wire for changing a loop from one
needle to another, as in narrowing, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(construction, tunneling)</fld> <def>A foreman
responsible for the work on one shift in one area, as in one
heading{4}.</def><br/
[<source>RH</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiftiness</ent><br/
<hw>Shift"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
shifty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Diplomatic <qex>shiftiness</qex> and political versatility.</q>
<rj><qau>J. A. Syminds.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shifting</ent><br/
<hw>Shift"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Changing in place,
position, or direction; varying; variable; fickle; <as>as,
<ex>shifting</ex> winds; <ex>shifting</ex> opinions or
principles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Adapted or used for shifting anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shifting backstays</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>temporary stays that have to be let go whenever the vessel tacks or
jibes.</cd> -- <col><b>Shifting ballast</b></col>, <cd>ballast which may
be moved from one side of a vessel to another as safety requires.</cd>
-- <col><b>Shifting center</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Metacenter</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Shifting locomotive</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Switching
engine</cref>, under <er>Switch</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiftingly</ent><br/
<hw>Shift"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shifting
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiftless</ent><br/
<hw>Shift"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of expedients, or not
using successful expedients; characterized by failure, especially by
failure to provide for one's own support, through negligence or
incapacity; hence, lazy; improvident; thriftless; <as>as, a
<ex>shiftless</ex> fellow; <ex>shiftless</ex> management.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Shift"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Shift"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shifty</ent><br/
<hw>Shift"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of, or ready
with, shifts; fertile in expedients or contrivance.</def>
<rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shifty</qex> and thrifty as old Greek or modern Scot, there
were few things he could not invent, and perhaps nothing he could not
endure.</q> <rj><qau>C. Kingsley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiah</ent><br/
<ent>Shiite</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shi"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shi"ah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar. <ets>sh<imac/'a<imac/</ets>a follower of
the sect of Ali, fr. <ets>sh<imac/'at</ets>, <ets>sh<imac/'ah</ets>, a
multitude following one another in pursuit of the same object, the sect
of Ali, fr. <ets>sh<amac/'a</ets> to follow.]</ety> <def>A member of
that branch of the Mohammedans to which the Persians belong. They reject
the first three caliphs, and consider Ali as being the first and only
rightful successor of Mohammed. They do not acknowledge the Sunna, or
body of traditions respecting Mohammed, as any part of the law, and on
these accounts are treated as heretics by the Sunnites, or orthodox
Mohammedans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shikari</ent><br/
<ent>Shikaree</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shi*ka"ree</hw>, \'d8<hw>Shi*ka"ri</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Hind</ets>.]</ety> <def>A sportsman; esp., a
native hunter.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shilf</ent><br/
<hw>Shilf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. G. <ets>shilf</ets>
sedge.]</ety> <def>Straw.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shill</ent><br/
<hw>Shill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To shell.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shill</ent><br/
<hw>Shill</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Sheal</er>.]</ety>
<def>To put under cover; to sheal.</def> <mark>[Prov.ng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Brockett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shillelah</ent><br/
<ent>Shillalah</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shil*la"lah</hw>, <hw>Shil*le"lah</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>An oaken sapling or cudgel; any cudgel; -- so called
from <xex>Shillelagh</xex>, a place in Ireland of that name famous for
its oaks.</def> <mark>[Irish]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>shillaly</asp>, and <asp>shillely</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shilling</ent><br/
<hw>Shil"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shilling</ets>, <ets>schilling</ets>, AS. <ets>scilling</ets>; akin
to D. <ets>schelling</ets>, OS. & OHG. <ets>scilling</ets>, G.
<ets>schilling</ets>, Sw. & Dan. <ets>skilling</ets>, Icel.
<ets>skillingr</ets>, Goth. <ets>skilliggs</ets>, and perh. to OHG.
<ets>scellan</ets> to sound, G. <ets>schallen</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A silver coin, and money of account, of Great Britain and its
dependencies, equal to twelve pence, or the twentieth part of a pound,
equivalent to about twenty-four cents of the United States
currency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the United States, a denomination of money,
differing in value in different States. It is not now legally
recognized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Many of the States while colonies had issued bills of
credit which had depreciated in different degrees in the different
colonies. Thus, in New England currency (used also in Virginia,
Kentucky, Tennessee, Indiana, Illinois, Missouri, Mississippi, Alabama,
and Florida), after the adoption of the decimal system, the pound in
paper money was worth only $3.333, and the shilling 16<frac23/ cts., or
6s. to $1; in New York currency (also in North Carolina, Ohio, and
Michigan), the pound was worth $2.50, and the shilling 12<frac12/ cts.,
or 8s. to $1; in Pennsylvania currency (also in New Jersey, Delaware,
and Maryland), the pound was worth $2.70, and the shilling 13<frac12/
cts., or 7s. 6d. to $1; and in Georgia currency (also in South
Carolina), the pound was worth $4.20<frac67/, and the shilling
21<frac37/ cts., or 4s 8d. to $1. In many parts of the country . . . the
reckoning by shillings and pence is not yet entirely abandoned.</note>
<rj><au>Am. Cyc. (1890)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1329 --></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The Spanish real, of the value of one eight of a
dollar, or 12<?/ cets; -- formerly so called in New York and some other
States. See Note under 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>York shilling</b></col>. <cd>Same as <er>Shilling</er>,
3.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shilly-shally</ent><br/
<ent>Shill-I-shall-I</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shill"-I-shall`-I</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Shil"ly-shal`ly</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[A reduplication
of <ets>shall I</ets>.]</ety> <def>In an irresolute, undecided, or
hesitating manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am somewhat dainty in making a resolution, because when I make
it, I keep it; I don't stand <qex>shill-I-shall-I</qex> then; if I say
't, I'll do 't.</q> <rj><qau>Congreve.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shilly-shally</ent><br/
<hw>Shil"ly-shal`ly</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To hesitate; to act in
an irresolute manner; hence, to occupy one's self with
trifles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shilly-shally</ent><br/
<hw>Shil"ly-shal`ly</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Irresolution; hesitation;
also, occupation with trifles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>She lost not one of her forty-five minutes in picking and
choosing</qex>, -- <qex>no shilly-shally</qex> in Kate.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiloh</ent><br/
<hw>Shi"loh</hw> <pr>(sh<imac/"l<omac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>sh<imac/l<omac/h</ets>, literally, quiet, rest, fr.
<ets>sh<amac/l<amac/h</ets> to rest.]</ety> <fld>(Script.)</fld> <def>A
word used by Jacob on his deathbed, and interpreted variously, as
<ldquo/the Messiah,<rdquo/ or as the city <ldquo/Shiloh,<rdquo/ or as
<ldquo/Rest.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shily</ent><br/
<hw>Shi"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shyly</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shim</ent><br/
<hw>Shim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of
shallow plow used in tillage to break the ground, and clear it of
weeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A thin piece of metal placed
between two parts to make a fit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shimmer</ent><br/
<hw>Shim"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shimmered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shimmering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schimeren</ets>, AS. <ets>scimerian</ets>; akin to
<ets>sc<imac/mian</ets>, <ets>sc<imac/man</ets>, to glitter, D.
<ets>schemeren</ets>, G. <ets>schimmern</ets>, Dan. <ets>skimre</ets>,
Sw. <ets>skimra</ets>, AS. <ets>sc<imac/ma</ets> a light, brightness,
Icel. <ets>sk<imac/ma</ets>, Goth. <ets>skeima</ets> a torch, a lantern,
and E. <ets>shine</ets>. <root/157. See <er>Shine</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To shine with a tremulous or intermittent light; to
shine faintly; to gleam; to glisten; to glimmer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shimmering</qex> glimpses of a stream.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shimmer</ent><br/
<hw>Shim"mer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A faint, tremulous light; a
gleaming; a glimmer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>TWo silver lamps, fed with perfumed oil, diffused . . . a
trembling twilight-seeming <qex>shimmer</qex> through the quiet
apartment.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shimmering</ent><br/
<hw>Shim"mer*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A gleam or glimmering.</def>
<ldquo/A little <xex>shimmering</xex> of a light.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shimmy</ent><br/
<hw>Shim"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A chemise.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shin</ent><br/
<hw>Shin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shine</ets>,
<ets>schine</ets>, AS. scina; akin to D. <ets>scheen</ets>, OHG.
<ets>scina</ets>, G. <ets>schiene</ets>, <ets>schien</ets>bein, Dan.
<ets>skinne</ets>been, Sw. <ets>sken</ets>ben. Cf.
<er>Chine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The front part of the leg below
the knee; the front edge of the shin bone; the lower part of the leg;
the shank.</def> <ldquo/On his <xex>shin</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Railbroad)</fld> <def>A fish plate for rails.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shin bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
tibia.</cd> -- <col><b>Shin leaf</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
perennial ericaceous herb (<spn>Pyrola elliptica</spn>) with a cluster
of radical leaves and a raceme of greenish white flowers.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shin</ent><br/
<hw>Shin</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shinned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shinning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To climb a mast,
tree, rope, or the like, by embracing it alternately with the arms and
legs, without help of steps, spurs, or the like; -- used with
<xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>shin</ex> up a mast</as>.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <-- now usu. shinny --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To run about borrowing money hastily and
temporarily, as for the payment of one's notes at the bank.</def>
<mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shin</ent><br/
<hw>Shin</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To climb (a pole, etc.) by shinning
up.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shindle</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 2d
<er>Shingle</er>.]</ety> <def>A shingle; also, a slate for
roofing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shindle</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"dle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cover or roof with
shindles.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shindy</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shindies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain; cf.
<er>Shinney</er>, <er>Shinty</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An uproar or
disturbance; a spree; a row; a riot.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hockey; shinney.</def>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A fancy or liking.</def> <mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shine</ent><br/
<hw>Shine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shone</conjf> <pr>(<?/ <or/ <?/; 277)</pr>
(<mark>archaic</mark> <conjf>Shined</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>); <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Shining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shinen</ets>, <ets>schinen</ets>, AS. <ets>sc<imac/nan</ets>; akin
to D. <ets>schijnen</ets>, OFries. <ets>sk<imac/na</ets>, OS. & OHG.
<ets>sc<imac/nan</ets>, G. <ets>scheinen</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sk<imac/na</ets>, Sw. <ets>skina</ets>, Dan. <ets>skinne</ets>,
Goth. <ets>skeinan</ets>, and perh. to Gr. <?/<?/<?/ shadow. <root/157.
Cf. <er>Sheer</er> pure, and <er>Shimmer</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To emit rays of light; to give light; to beam with steady radiance;
to exhibit brightness or splendor; <as>as, the sun <ex>shines</ex> by
day; the moon <ex>shines</ex> by night.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hyperion's quickening fire doth <qex>shine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God, who commanded the light to <qex>shine</qex> out of darkness,
hath <qex>shined</qex> in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge
of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Cghrist.</q> <rj><qau>2 Cor.
iv. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let thine eyes <qex>shine</qex> forth in their full luster.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be bright by reflection of light; to gleam; to be
glossy; <as>as, to <ex>shine</ex> like polished silver</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be effulgent in splendor or beauty.</def>
<ldquo/So proud she <xex>shined</xex> in her princely state.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Once brightest <qex>shined</qex> this child of heat and air.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be eminent, conspicuous, or distinguished; to
exhibit brilliant intellectual powers; <as>as, to <ex>shine</ex> in
courts; to <ex>shine</ex> in conversation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Few are qualified to <qex>shine</qex> in company; but it in most
men's power to be agreeable.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To make the face to shine upon</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>To cause the face to shine upon</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>to be propitious to; to be gracious to.</cd> <rj><au>Num. vi.
25.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shine</ent><br/
<hw>Shine</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to shine, as
a light.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [God] doth not rain wealth, nor <qex>shine</qex> honor and
virtues, upon men equally.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make bright; to cause to shine by reflected
light; <as>as, in hunting, to <ex>shine</ex> the eyes of a deer at night
by throwing a light on them</as>.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shine</ent><br/
<hw>Shine</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of
shining; brightness; luster, gloss; polish; sheen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now sits not girt with taper's holy <qex>shine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fair opening to some court's propitious <qex>shine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The distant <qex>shine</qex> of the celestial city.</q>
<rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sunshine; fair weather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be it fair or foul, or rain or <qex>shine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A liking for a person; a fancy.</def> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Caper; antic; row.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To cut up shines</b></col>, <cd>to play pranks.</cd>
<mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shine</ent><br/
<hw>Shine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sc<imac/n</ets>. See <er>Shine</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Shining; sheen.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiner</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which
shines.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A luminary.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A bright piece of money.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Has she the <qex>shiners</qex>, d' ye think?</q>
<rj><qau>Foote.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- [Colloq.] A bruised eye; a <cref>black eye</cref>. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
small freshwater American cyprinoid fishes, belonging to
<gen>Notropis</gen>, or <gen>Minnilus</gen>, and allied genera; as the
redfin (<spn>Notropis megalops</spn>), and the golden <ex>shiner</ex>
(<spn>Notemigonus chrysoleucus</spn>) of the Eastern United States; also
loosely applied to various other silvery fishes, as the dollar fish, or
horsefish, menhaden, moonfish, sailor's choice, and the sparada.</def>
<sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common Lepisma, or furniture
bug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Blunt-nosed shiner</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
silver moonfish.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiness</ent><br/
<hw>Shi"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shyness</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shingle</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. from Norw.
<ets>singl</ets>, <ets>singling</ets>, coarse gravel, small round
stones.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Round, water-worn, and loose
gravel and pebbles, or a collection of roundish stones, such as are
common on the seashore and elsewhere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shingle</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"gle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shingle</ets>,
<ets>shindle</ets>, fr. L. <ets>scindula</ets>, <ets>scandula</ets>; cf.
<ets>scindere</ets> to cleave, to split, E. <ets>shed</ets>, v. t., Gr.
<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/, shingle, <?/<?/<?/ to slit.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A piece of wood sawed or rived thin and small, with one end thinner
than the other, -- used in covering buildings, especially roofs, the
thick ends of one row overlapping the thin ends of the row
below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I reached St. Asaph, . . . where there is a very poor cathedral
church covered with <qex>shingles</qex> or tiles.</q>
<rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sign for an office or a shop; <as>as, to hang out
one's <ex>shingle</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Jocose, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shingle oak</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of
oak (<spn>Quercus imbricaria</spn>) used in the Western States for
making shingles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shingle</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"gle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shingled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shingling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
cover with shingles; <as>as, to <ex>shingle</ex> a roof</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>shingle</qex> their houses with it.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cut, as hair, so that the ends are evenly exposed
all over the head, as shingles on a roof.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shingle</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"gle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To subject to the process of
shindling, as a mass of iron from the pudding furnace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shingler</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"gler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
shingles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A machine for shingling puddled iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shingles</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"gles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>cengle</ets> a girth, F. <ets>sangle</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>cingulum</ets> a girdle, fr. <ets>cingere</ets> to gird. Cf.
<er>Cincture</er>, <er>Cingle</er>, <er>Surcingle</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A kind of herpes (<spn>Herpes zoster</spn>) which
spreads half way around the body like a girdle, and is usually attended
with violent neuralgic pain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shingling</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"gling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of covering with shingles; shingles, collectively; a covering made of
shingles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metal)</fld> <def>The process of expelling scoriae
and other impurities by hammering and squeezing, in the production of
wrought iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shingling hammer</b></col>, <cd>a ponderous hammer moved
by machinery, used in shingling puddled iron.</cd> -- <col><b>Shingling
mill</b></col>, <cd>a mill or forge where puddled iron is
shingled.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shingly</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"gly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding with
shingle, or gravel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shinhopple</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"hop`ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
hobblebush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shining</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Emitting
light, esp. in a continuous manner; radiant; <as>as, <ex>shining</ex>
lamps</as>; also, bright by the reflection of light; <as>as,
<ex>shining</ex> armor</as>.</def> <ldquo/Fish . . . with their fins and
<xex>shining</xex> scales.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Splendid; illustrious; brilliant; distinguished;
conspicious; <as>as, a <ex>shining</ex> example of
charity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having the surface smooth and polished; -- said of
leaves, the surfaces of shells, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Glistening; bright; radiant; resplendent;
effulgent; lustrous; brilliant; glittering; splendid; illustrious.</syn>
-- <usage><er>Shining</er>, <er>Brilliant</er>, <er>Sparking</er>.
<xex>Shining</xex> describes the steady emission of a strong light, or
the steady reflection of light from a clear or polished surface.
<xex>Brilliant</xex> denotes a shining of great brightness, but with
gleams or flashes. <xex>Sparkling</xex> implies a fitful, intense
shining from radiant points or sparks, by which the eye is dazzled. The
same distinctions obtain when these epithets are figuratively applied. A
man of <xex>shining</xex> talents is made conspicious by possessing
them; if they flash upon the mind with a peculiarly striking effect, we
call them <xex>brilliant</xex>; if his brilliancy is marked by great
vivacity and occasional intensity, he is
<xex>sparkling</xex>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>True paradise . . . inclosed with <qex>shining</qex> rock.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some in a <qex>brilliant</qex> buckle bind her waist,<br/
Some round her neck a circling light display.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>sparkling</qex> blade about his head he blest.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shining</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Emission or reflection of
light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiningness</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"ing*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Brightness.</def> <rj><au>J.
Spence.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shinney</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"ney</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Shindy</er>.]</ety> <def>The game of hockey; -- so called because of
the liability of the players to receive blows on the shin.</def>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shin Shu</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Shin Shu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Jap., lit., true sect.]</ety>
<def>The leading and most progressive Buddhist sect of Japan, resting
its faith rather upon Amida than Gautama Buddha. Rites and ceremonies
are held useless without uprightness.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shintyan</ent><br/
<ent>Shintiyan</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shin"ti*yan</hw> <pr>(sh<icr/n"t<icr/*y<acr/n)</pr>,
<hw>Shin"ty*an</hw> <pr>(sh<icr/n"t<icr/*<acr/n)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos>} <ety>[Ar. <ets>shint<imac/<amac/n</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
kind of wide loose drawers or trousers worn by women in Mohammedan
countries.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><-- shinny. Same as shin, to climb with hands and feet --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shinplaster</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"plas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Formerly, a
jocose term for a bank note greatly depreciated in value; also, for
paper money of a denomination less than a dollar.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shintiism</ent><br/
<ent>Shinto</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shin"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shin"ti*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Chin. <ets>shin</ets> god + <ets>tao</ets>
way, doctrine.]</ety> <def>One of the two great systems of religious
belief in Japan. Its essence is ancestor worship, and sacrifice to dead
heroes.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>Sintu</asp>, and
<asp>Sintuism</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shintoist</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"to*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An adherent of
Shintoism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shinty</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gael.
<ets>sinteag</ets> a skip, a bound.]</ety> <def>A Scotch game resembling
hockey; also, the club used in the game.</def>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiny</ent><br/
<hw>Shin"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shinier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shiniest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Bright; luminous; clear;
unclouded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like distant thunder on a <qex>shiny</qex> day.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-ship</ent><br/
<hw>-ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[OE. <ets>-schipe</ets>, AS.
<ets>-scipe</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>-skipe</ets>, OLG.
<ets>-skepi</ets>, D. <ets>-schap</ets>, OHG. <ets>-scaf</ets>, G.
<ets>-schaft</ets>. Cf. <er>Shape</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and
<er>Landscape</er>.]</ety> <def>A suffix denoting <xex>state</xex>,
<xex>office</xex>, <xex>dignity</xex>, <xex>profession</xex>, or
<xex>art</xex>; as in lord<xex>ship</xex>, friend<xex>ship</xex>,
chancellor<xex>ship</xex>, steward<xex>ship</xex>,
horseman<xex>ship</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ship</ent><br/
<hw>Ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>scipe</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pay; reward.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In withholding or abridging of the <qex>ship</qex> or the hire or
the wages of servants.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ship</ent><br/
<hw>Ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>ship</ets>,
<ets>schip</ets>, AS. <ets>scip</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>skip</ets>,
OS. <ets>scip</ets>, D. <ets>schip</ets>, G. <ets>schiff</ets>, OHG.
<ets>scif</ets>, Dan. <ets>skib</ets>, Sw. <ets>skeep</ets>, Icel. &
Goth. <ets>skip</ets>; of unknown origin. Cf. <er>Equip</er>,
<er>Skiff</er>, <er>Skipper</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any large
seagoing vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a stately <qex>ship</qex> . . . <br/
With all her bravery on, and tackle trim,<br/
Sails filled, and streamers waving.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou, too, sail on, O <qex>Ship</qex> of State!</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, a vessel furnished with a bowsprit and
three masts (a mainmast, a foremast, and a mizzenmast), each of which is
composed of a lower mast, a topmast, and a topgallant mast, and
square-rigged on all masts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- ##?? illustration: Deck plan of a ship --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><caption><xex>l</xex> Port or Larboard Side; <qex>s</qex> Starboard
Side; 1 Roundhouse or Deck House; 2 Tiller; 3 Grating; 4 Wheel; 5 Wheel
Chains; 6 Binnacle; 7 Mizzenmast; 8 Skylight; 9 Capstan; 10 Mainmast; 11
Pumps; 12 Galley or Caboose; 13 Main Hatchway; 14 Windlass; 15 Foremast;
16 Fore Hatchway; 17 Bitts; 18 Bowsprit; 19 Head Rail; 20 Boomkins; 21
Catheads on Port Bow and Starboard Bow; 22 Fore Chains; 23 Main Chains;
24 Mizzen Chains; 25 Stern.</caption><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><--illustration: Outline of a ship --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><caption>1 Fore Royal Stay; 2 Flying Jib Stay; 3 Fore Topgallant
Stay;4 Jib Stay; 5 Fore Topmast Stays; 6 Fore Tacks; 8 Flying
Martingale; 9 Martingale Stay, shackled to Dolphin Striker; 10 Jib Guys;
11 Jumper Guys; 12 Back Ropes; 13 Robstays; 14 Flying Jib Boom; 15
Flying Jib Footropes; 16 Jib Boom; 17 Jib Foottropes; 18 Bowsprit; 19
Fore Truck; 20 Fore Royal Mast; 21 Fore Royal Lift; 22 Fore Royal Yard;
23 Fore Royal Backstays; 24 Fore Royal Braces; 25 Fore Topgallant Mast
and Rigging; 26 Fore Topgallant Lift; 27 Fore Topgallant Yard; 28 Fore
Topgallant Backstays; 29 Fore Topgallant Braces; 30 Fore Topmast and
Rigging; 31 Fore Topsail Lift; 32 Fore Topsail Yard; 33 Fore Topsail
Footropes; 34 Fore Topsail Braces; 35 Fore Yard; 36 Fore Brace; 37 Fore
Lift; 38 Fore Gaff; 39 Fore Trysail Vangs; 40 Fore Topmast Studding-sail
Boom; 41 Foremast and Rigging; 42 Fore Topmast Backstays; 43 Fore
Sheets; 44 Main Truck and Pennant; 45 Main Royal Mast and Backstay; 46
Main Royal Stay; 47 Main Royal Lift; 48 Main Royal Yard; 49 Main Royal
Braces; 50 Main Topgallant Mast and Rigging; 51 Main Topgallant Lift; 52
Main Topgallant Backstays; 53 Main Topgallant Yard; 54 Main Topgallant
Stay; 55 Main Topgallant Braces; 56 Main Topmast and Rigging; 57 Topsail
Lift; 58 Topsail Yard; 59 Topsail Footropes; 60 Topsail Braces; 61
Topmast Stays; 62 Main Topgallant Studding-sail Boom; 63 Main Topmast
Backstay; 64 Main Yard; 65 Main Footropes; 66 Mainmast and Rigging; 67
Main Lift; 68 Main Braces; 69 Main Tacks; 70 Main Sheets; 71 Main
Trysail Gaff; 72 Main Trysail Vangs; 73 Main Stays; 74 Mizzen Truck; 75
Mizzen Royal Mast and Rigging; 76 Mizzen Royal Stay; 77 Mizzen Royal
Lift; 78 Mizzen Royal Yard; 79 Mizzen Royal Braces; 80 Mizzen Topgallant
Mast and Rigging; 81 Mizzen Topgallant Lift; 82 Mizzen Topgallant
Backstays; 83 Mizzen Topgallant Braces; 84 Mizzen Topgallant Yard; 85
Mizzen Topgallant Stay; 86 Mizzen Topmast and Rigging; 87 Mizzen Topmast
Stay; 88 Mizzen Topsail Lift; 89 Mizzen Topmast Backstays; 90 Mizzen
Topsail Braces; 91 Mizzen Topsail Yard; 92 Mizzen Topsail Footropes; 93
Crossjack Yard; 94 Crossjack Footropes; 95 Crossjack Lift; 96 Crossjack
Braces; 97 Mizzenmast and Rigging; 98 Mizzen Stay; 99 Spanker Gaff; 100
Peak Halyards; 101 Spanker Vangs; 102 Spanker Boom; 103 Spanker Boom
Topping Lift; 104 Jacob's Ladder, or Stern Ladder; 105 Spanker Sheet;
106 Cutwater; 107 Starboard Bow; 108 Starboard Beam; 109 Water Line; 110
Starboard Quarter; 111 Rudder.</caption><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1330 --></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A dish or utensil (originally fashioned like the
hull of a ship) used to hold incense.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tyndale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Armed ship</b></col>, <cd>a private ship taken into the
service of the government in time of war, and armed and equipped like a
ship of war.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <au>Brande & C.</au> --
<col><b>General ship</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>General</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Ship biscuit</b></col>, <cd>hard biscuit prepared for use on
shipboard; -- called also <altname>ship bread</altname>. See
<er>Hardtack</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship boy</b></col>, <cd>a boy who
serves in a ship.</cd> <ldquo/Seal up the <xex>ship boy's</xex>
eyes.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>Ship breaker</b></col>, <cd>one
who breaks up vessels when unfit for further use.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship
broker</b></col>, <cd>a mercantile agent employed in buying and selling
ships, procuring cargoes, etc., and generally in transacting the
business of a ship or ships when in port.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship
canal</b></col>, <cd>a canal suitable for the passage of seagoing
vessels.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship carpenter</b></col>, <cd>a carpenter who
works at shipbuilding; a shipwright.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship
chandler</b></col>, <cd>one who deals in cordage, canvas, and other,
furniture of vessels.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship chandlery</b></col>, <cd>the
commodities in which a ship chandler deals; also, the business of a ship
chandler.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship fever</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
form of typhus fever; -- called also <altname>putrid fever</altname>,
<altname>jail fever</altname>, <it>or</it> <altname>hospital
fever</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship joiner</b></col>, <cd>a joiner who
works upon ships.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship letter</b></col>, <cd>a letter
conveyed by a ship not a mail packet.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship
money</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld>, <cd>an imposition formerly
charged on the ports, towns, cities, boroughs, and counties, of England,
for providing and furnishing certain ships for the king's service. The
attempt made by <person>Charles I.</person> to revive and enforce this
tax was resisted by <person>John Hampden</person>, and was one of the
causes which led to the death of <persfn>Charles</persfn>. It was
finally abolished.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship of the line</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Line</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship pendulum</b></col>, <cd>a
pendulum hung amidships to show the extent of the rolling and pitching
of a vessel.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship railway</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>An
inclined railway with a cradelike car, by means of which a ship may be
drawn out of water, as for repairs</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A railway
arranged for the transportation of vessels overland between two water
courses or harbors.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship's company</b></col>, <cd>the
crew of a ship or other vessel.</cd> -- <col><b>Ship's days</b></col>,
<cd>the days allowed a vessel for loading or unloading.</cd> --
<col><b>Ship's husband</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Husband</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Ship's papers</b></col> <fld>(Mar. Law)</fld>, <cd>papers
with which a vessel is required by law to be provided, and the
production of which may be required on certain occasions. Among these
papers are the register, passport or sea letter, charter party, bills of
lading, invoice, log book, muster roll, bill of health, etc.</cd>
<au>Bouvier.</au> <au>Kent.</au> -- <col><b>To make ship</b></col>,
<cd>to embark in a ship or other vessel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ship</ent><br/
<hw>Ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shipped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shipping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put on board of a
ship, or vessel of any kind, for transportation; to send by
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The timber was . . . <qex>shipped</qex> in the bay of Attalia,
from whence it was by sea transported to Pelusium.</q>
<rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>By extension, in commercial usage, to commit to any
conveyance for transportation to a distance; <as>as, to <ex>ship</ex>
freight by railroad</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, to send away; to get rid of.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To engage or secure for service on board of a ship;
<as>as, to <ex>ship</ex> seamen</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To receive on board ship; <as>as, to <ex>ship</ex> a
sea</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To put in its place; <as>as, to <ex>ship</ex> the
tiller or rudder</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ship</ent><br/
<hw>Ship</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To engage to serve on
board of a vessel; <as>as, to <ex>ship</ex> on a
man-of-war</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To embark on a ship.</def> <rj><au>Wyclif (Acts
xxviii. 11)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipboard</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Ship</ets> +
<ets>board.</ets> See <er>Board</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 8]</ety> <def>A
ship's side; hence, by extension, a ship; -- found chiefly in adverbial
phrases; <as>as, on <ex>shipboard</ex>; a
<ex>shipboard</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipbuilder</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"build`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person whose
occupation is to construct ships and other vessels; a naval architect; a
shipwright.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipbuilding</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"build`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Naval architecturel the art
of constructing ships and other vessels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipful</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shipfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>As much or as many as a
ship will hold; enough to fill a ship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipholder</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"hold`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
shipowner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipless</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of ships.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiplet</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little ship.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipload</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"load`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The load, or cargo,
of a ship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipman</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shipmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A seaman, or sailor.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer. R.
Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>About midnight the <qex>shipmen</qex> deemed that they drew near
to some country.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xxvii. 27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shipman's card</b></col>, <cd>the mariner's compass.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipmaster</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"mas`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The captain,
master, or commander of a ship.</def> <rj><au>Jonah i. 6.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipmate</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"mate`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who serves on
board of the same ship with another; a fellow sailor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipment</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
or process of shipping; <as>as, he was engaged in the <ex>shipment</ex>
of coal for London; an active <ex>shipment</ex> of wheat from the
West</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is shipped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The question is, whether the share of M. in the
<qex>shipment</qex> is exempted from condemnation by reason of his
neutral domicle.</q> <rj><qau>Story.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipowner</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"own`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Owner of a ship or
ships.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shippen</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"pen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>scypen</ets>. Cf. <er>Shop</er>, <er>Shepen</er>.]</ety> <def>A
stable; a cowhouse.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipper</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Ship</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and cf. <er>Skipper</er>.]</ety> <def>One who sends goods
from one place to another not in the same city or town, esp. one who
sends goods by water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipping</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating
to ships, their ownership, transfer, or employment; <as>as,
<ex>shiping</ex> concerns</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relating to, or concerned in, the forwarding of
goods; <as>as, a <ex>shipping</ex> clerk</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipping</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"ping</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who,
or of that which, ships; <as>as, the <ex>shipping</ex> of flour to
Liverpool</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The collective body of ships in one place, or
belonging to one port, country, etc.; vessels, generally;
tonnage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Navigation.</def> <ldquo/God send 'em good
<xex>shipping</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shipping articles</b></col>, <cd>articles of agreement
between the captain of a vessel and the seamen on board, in respect to
the amount of wages, length of time for which they are shipping,
etc.</cd> <au>Bouvier.</au> -- <col><b>To take shipping</b></col>,
<cd>to embark; to take ship.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>John vi.
24.</au> <au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipping note</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"ping note</hw>. <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A document used in
shipping goods by sea. In the case of free goods the shipping notes are
the <col><b>receiving note</b></col>, addressed by the shipper to the
chief officer of the vessel, requesting him to receive on board
specified goods, and a receipt for the mate to sign, on receiving whose
signature it is called the <col><b>mate's receipt</b></col>, and is
surrendered by the shipper for the bills of lading.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shippo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ship*po"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Jap.
<ets>shipp<omac/</ets> seven precious things; Chin. <ets>ts'ih</ets>
seven + <ets>pao</ets> gem.]</ety> <fld>(Japanese Art)</fld>
<def>Cloisonn<eacute/ enamel on a background of metal or porcelain; --
also called <altname>shippo yaki</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shippon</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"pon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A cowhouse; a
shippen.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bessy would either do fieldwork, or attend to the cows, the
<qex>shippon</qex>, or churn, or make cheese.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ship railway</ent><br/
<hw>Ship railway</hw>. <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An inclined railway running
into the water with a cradelike car on which a vessel may be drawn out
on land, as for repairs.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A railway on which to
transport vessels overland between bodies of water.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ship-rigged</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"-rigged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>Rigged like a ship, that is, having three masts, each with square
sails.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipshape</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"shape`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Arranged in a
manner befitting a ship; hence, trim; tidy; orderly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Even then she expressed her scorn for the lubbery executioner's
mode of tying a knot, and did it herself in a <qex>shipshape</qex>
orthodox manner.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Keep everything <qex>shipshape</qex>, for I must go</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipshape</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"shape`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shipshape
or seamanlike manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipworm</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any long, slender, worm-shaped bivalve mollusk of <gen>Teredo</gen>
and allied genera. The shipworms burrow in wood, and are destructive to
wooden ships, piles of wharves, etc. See <er>Teredo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipwreck</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"wreck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
breaking in pieces, or shattering, of a ship or other vessel by being
cast ashore or driven against rocks, shoals, etc., by the violence of
the winds and waves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A ship wrecked or destroyed upon the water, or the
parts of such a ship; wreckage.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: Destruction; ruin; irretrievable
loss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Holding faith and a good conscience, which some having put away
concerning faith have made <qex>shipwreck</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>1 Tim. 1.
19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was upon an Indian bill that the late ministry <qex>had made
shipwreck</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>J. Morley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipwreck</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"wreck`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shipwrecked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shipwrecking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To destroy, as a
ship at sea, by running ashore or on rocks or sandbanks, or by the force
of wind and waves in a tempest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shipwrecking</qex> storms and direful thunders break.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to experience shipwreck, as sailors or
passengers. Hence, to cause to suffer some disaster or loss; to destroy
or ruin, as if by shipwreck; to wreck; <as>as, to <ex>shipwreck</ex> a
business</as>.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipwright</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"wright`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One whose
occupation is to construct ships; a builder of ships or other
vessels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shipyard</ent><br/
<hw>Ship"yard`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A yard, place, or
inclosure where ships are built or repaired.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiraz</ent><br/
<hw>Shi*raz"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of Persian
wine; -- so called from the place whence it is brought.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shire</ent><br/
<hw>Shire</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sc<imac/re</ets>, <ets>sc<imac/r</ets>, a division, province,
county. Cf. <er>Sheriff</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A portion of Great
Britain originally under the supervision of an earl; a territorial
division, usually identical with a county, but sometimes limited to a
smaller district; <as>as, Wilt<ex>shire</ex>, York<ex>shire</ex>,
Richmond<ex>shire</ex>, Hallam<ex>shire</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An indefinite number of these hundreds make up a county or
<qex>shire</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A division of a State, embracing several contiguous
townships; a county.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Shire</xex> is commonly added to the specific
designation of a county as a part of its name; as, York<xex>shire</xex>
instead of York <xex>shire</xex>, or the <xex>shire</xex> of York;
Berk<xex>shire</xex> instead of Berks <xex>shire</xex>. Such expressions
as <xex>the county of Yorkshire</xex>, which in a strict sense are
tautological, are used in England. In the United States the composite
word is sometimes the only name of a county; as, <xex>Berkshire
county</xex>, as it is called in Massachusetts, instead of <xex>Berks
county</xex>, as in Pensylvania.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Tyne, Tees, Humber, Wash, Yare, Stour, and Thames separate the
counties of Northumberland, Durham, York<qex>shire</qex>,
Lincoln<qex>shire</qex>, etc.</q> <rj><qau>Encyc. Brit.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Knight of the shire</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Knight</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shire clerk</b></col>, <cd>an officer
of a county court; also, an under sheriff.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Shire mote</b></col> <fld>(Old. Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>the county
court; sheriff's turn, or court.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Cowell.</au> <au>Blackstone.</au> -- <col><b>Shire reeve</b></col>
<fld>(Old Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>the reeve, or bailiff, of a shire; a
sheriff.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au> -- <col><b>Shire town</b></col>, <cd>the
capital town of a county; a county town.</cd> -- <col><b>Shire
wick</b></col>, <cd>a county; a shire.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Holland.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shire horse</ent><br/
<hw>Shire horse</hw>. <def>One of an English breed of heavy draft horses
believed to be descended largely from the horses used in war in the days
of heavy armor. They are the largest of the British draft breeds, and
have long hair on the back of the cannons and fetlocks. Brown or bay
with white on the face and legs is now the commonest color.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirk</ent><br/
<hw>Shirk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shirked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shirking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably the same word as
<ets>shark</ets>. See <er>Shark</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To procure by petty fraud and trickery; to obtain by
mean solicitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You that never heard the call of any vocation, . . . that
<qex>shirk</qex> living from others, but time from Yourselves.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Rainbow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To avoid; to escape; to neglect; -- implying
unfaithfulness or fraud; <as>as, to <ex>shirk</ex> duty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The usual makeshift by which they try to <qex>shirk</qex>
difficulties.</q> <rj><qau>Hare.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirk</ent><br/
<hw>Shirk</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To live by shifts and
fraud; to shark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To evade an obligation; to avoid the performance of
duty, as by running away.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One of the cities <qex>shirked</qex> from the league.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirk</ent><br/
<hw>Shirk</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lives by shifts and tricks;
one who avoids the performance of duty or labor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirker</ent><br/
<hw>Shirk"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who shirks.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirky</ent><br/
<hw>Shirk"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Disposed to
shirk.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirl</ent><br/
<hw>Shirl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shrill.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au> Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirl</ent><br/
<hw>Shirl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Schorl</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirley</ent><br/
<hw>Shir"ley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The bullfinch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirr</ent><br/
<hw>Shirr</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Sewing)</fld> <def>A
series of close parallel runnings which are drawn up so as to make the
material between them set full by gatherings; -- called also
<altname>shirring</altname>, and <altname>gauging</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirred</ent><br/
<hw>Shirred</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Sewing)</fld> <def>Made or gathered into a shirr; <as>as, a
<ex>shirred</ex> bonnet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>Broken into an earthen dish and
baked over the fire; -- said of eggs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirt</ent><br/
<hw>Shirt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>schirte</ets>,
<ets>sherte</ets>, <ets>schurte</ets>; akin to Icel. <ets>skyrta</ets>,
Dan. <ets>skiorte</ets>, Sw. <ets>skjorta</ets>, Dan.
<ets>ski<oum/rt</ets> a petticoat, D. <ets>schort</ets> a petticoat, an
argon, G. <ets>schurz</ets>, <ets>sch<uum/rze</ets>, an argon; all
probably from the root of E. <ets>short</ets>, as being originally a
short garment. See <er>Short</er>, and cf. <er>Skirt</er>.]</ety> <def>A
loose under-garment for the upper part of the body, made of cotton,
linen, or other material; -- formerly used of the under-garment of
either sex, now commonly restricted to that worn by men and
boys.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Several persons in December had nothing over their shoulders but
their <qex>shirts</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She had her <qex>shirts</qex> and girdles of hair.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Fisher.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirt</ent><br/
<hw>Shirt</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shirted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shirting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cover or clothe with a shirt,
or as with a shirt.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirting</ent><br/
<hw>Shirt"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Cloth, specifically cotton cloth,
suitable for making shirts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirtless</ent><br/
<hw>Shirt"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not having or wearing a
shirt.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Shirt"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirt waist</ent><br/
<ent>shirtwaist</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>shirtwaist</hw>, <hw>Shirt waist</hw></mhw>.
<ety>[<ets>shirt</ets> + <ets>waist</ets>; see <er>waist{3}</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A woman's blouse resembling a
men's shirt in cut and style; -- in England called a
<altname>blouse</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a type of woman's dress with a bodice tailored like
a shirt; -- also called <altname>shirt dress</altname>,
<altname>shirtwaist dress</altname>, and
<altname>shirtwaister</altname>.</def> <au>[RHUD]</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shirt-waist suit</ent><br/
<hw>Shirt-waist suit</hw>. <def>A costume consisting of a plain belted
blouse and skirt of the same material.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shistose</ent><br/
<ent>Shist</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shis*tose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>. <def>See <er>Shist</er>, <er>Schistose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shittah tree</ent><br/
<ent>Shittah</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shit"tah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shit"tah tree`</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb. <ets>shitt<amac/h</ets>, pl.
<ets>shitt<imac/m</ets>.]</ety> <def>A tree that furnished the precious
wood of which the ark, tables, altars, boards, etc., of the Jewish
tabernacle were made; -- now believed to have been the wood of the
<spn>Acacia Seyal</spn>, which is hard, fine grained, and yellowish
brown in color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shittim wood</ent><br/
<ent>Shittim</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shit"tim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shit"tim wood`</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The wood of the shittah tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shittle</ent><br/
<hw>Shit"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Shuttle</er>.]</ety> <def>A shuttle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shittle</ent><br/
<hw>Shit"tle</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wavering; unsettled;
inconstant.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shittlecock</ent><br/
<hw>Shit"tle*cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
shuttlecock.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shittleness</ent><br/
<hw>Shit"tle*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Instability;
inconstancy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The vain <qex>shittlenesse</qex> of an unconstant head.</q>
<rj><qau>Baret.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shive</ent><br/
<hw>Shive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sheave</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A slice; <as>as, a <ex>shive</ex>
of bread</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thin piece or fragment; specifically, one of the
scales or pieces of the woody part of flax removed by the operation of
breaking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A thin, flat cork used for stopping a wide-mouthed
bottle; also, a thin wooden bung for casks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiver</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schivere</ets>, fr. <ets>shive</ets>; cf. G. <ets>schifer</ets> a
splinter, slate, OHG. <ets>scivere</ets> a splinter, Dan. & Sw.
<ets>skifer</ets> a slate. See <er>Shive</er>, and cf.
<er>Skever</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the small pieces, or
splinters, into which a brittle thing is broken by sudden violence; --
generally used in the plural.</def> <ldquo/All to <xex>shivers</xex>
dashed.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thin slice; a shive.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>shiver</xex> of their own loaf.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of your soft bread, not but a <qex>shiver</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A variety of blue
slate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A sheave or small wheel in a
pulley.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A small wedge, as for fastening the bolt of a window
shutter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A spindle.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiver</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shivered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shivering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>schiveren</ets>,
<ets>scheveren</ets>; cf. OD. <ets>scheveren</ets>. See <er>Shiver</er>
a fragment.]</ety> <def>To break into many small pieces, or splinters;
to shatter; to dash to pieces by a blow; <as>as, to <ex>shiver</ex> a
glass goblet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the ground<br/
With <qex>shivered</qex> armor strown.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiver</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To separate suddenly into many
small pieces or parts; to be shattered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There <qex>shiver</qex> shafts upon shields thick.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The natural world, should gravity once cease, . . . would
instantly <qex>shiver</qex> into millions of atoms.</q>
<rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiver</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>chiveren</ets>,
<ets>cheveren</ets>; of uncertain origin. This word seems to have been
confused with <ets>shiver</ets> to shatter.]</ety> <def>To tremble; to
vibrate; to quiver; to shake, as from cold or fear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Prometheus is laid<br/
On icy Caucasus to <qex>shiver</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The man that <qex>shivered</qex> on the brink of sin,<br/
Thus steeled and hardened, ventures boldly in.</q>
<rj><qau>Creech.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiver</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To cause to
shake or tremble, as a sail, by steering close to the wind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiver</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of shivering or
trembling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiveringly</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shivering
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shiver-spar</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er-spar`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schiefer-spath</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A variety of
calcite, so called from its slaty structure; -- called also
<altname>slate spar</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shivery</ent><br/
<hw>Shiv"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Tremulous; shivering.</def> <rj><au>Mallet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Easily broken; brittle; shattery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shizoku</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Shi*zo"ku</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos>
<ety>[Jap. <ets>shi-zoku</ets>, fr. Chin. <ets>ch'</ets>
(<ets>chi</ets>) branch, posterity + <ets>tsu</ets> kindered,
class.]</ety> <def>The Japanese warrior gentry or middle class, formerly
called <altname>samurai</altname>; also, any member of this
class.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoad</ent><br/
<hw>Shoad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schutt</ets> rubbish.]</ety> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A train of
vein material mixed with rubbish; fragments of ore which have become
separated by the action of water or the weather, and serve to direct in
the discovery of mines.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>shode</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoading</ent><br/
<hw>Shoad"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The tracing
of veins of metal by shoads.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>shoding</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Pryce.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoal</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>scolu</ets>,
<ets>sceolu</ets>, a company, multitude, crowd, akin to OS.
<ets>skola</ets>; probably originally, a division, and akin to Icel.
<ets>skilja</ets> to part, divide. See <er>Skill</er>, and cf.
<er>School</er>. of fishes.]</ety> <def>A great multitude assembled; a
crowd; a throng; -- said especially of fish; <as>as, a <ex>shoal</ex> of
bass</as>.</def> <ldquo/Great <xex>shoals</xex> of people.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beneath, a <qex>shoal</qex> of silver fishes glides.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoal</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shoaled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shoaling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To assemble in a multitude; to
throng; <as>as, the fishes <ex>shoaled</ex> about the place</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoal</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Shallow</er>; or cf. G.
<ets>scholle</ets> a clod, glebe, OHG. <ets>scollo</ets>,
<ets>scolla</ets>, prob. akin to E. <ets>shoal</ets> a multitude.]</ety>
<def>Having little depth; shallow; <as>as, <ex>shoal</ex>
water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoal</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place where the water
of a sea, lake, river, pond, etc., is shallow; a shallow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The depth of your pond should be six feet; and on the sides some
<qex>shoals</qex> for the fish to lay their span.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wolsey, that once trod the ways of glory,<br/
And sounded all the depths and <qex>shoals</qex> of honor.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sandbank or bar which makes the water
shoal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The god himself with ready trident stands,<br/
And opes the deep, and spreads the moving sands,<br/
Then heaves them off the <qex>shoals</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoal</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become shallow; <as>as, the
color of the water shows where it <ex>shoals</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoal</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to become more shallow;
to come to a more shallow part of; <as>as, a ship <ex>shoals</ex> her
water by advancing into that which is less deep</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Marryat.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoaliness</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being shoaly; little depth of water; shallowness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoaling</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Becoming shallow gradually.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>shoaling</xex> estuary.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Lyell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoaly</ent><br/
<hw>Shoal"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of shoals, or
shallow places.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tossing vessel sailed on <qex>shoaly</qex> ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoar</ent><br/
<hw>Shoar</hw> <pr>(sh<omac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A prop. See 3d
<er>Shore</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoat</ent><br/
<hw>Shoat</hw> <pr>(sh<omac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A young hog.
Same as <er>Shote</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1331 --></p>

<p><ent>Shock</ent><br/
<hw>Shock</hw> <pr>(sh<ocr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schokke</ets>; cf. OD <ets>schocke</ets>, G. <ets>schock</ets> a
heap, quantity, threescore, MHG. <ets>schoc</ets>, Sw. <ets>skok</ets>,
and also G. <ets>hocke</ets> a heap of hay, Lith.
<ets>kugis</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pile or assemblage of
sheaves of grain, as wheat, rye, or the like, set up in a field, the
sheaves varying in number from twelve to sixteen; a stook.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And cause it on <qex>shocks</qex> to be by and by set.</q>
<rj><qau>Tusser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Behind the master walks, builds up the <qex>shocks</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[G. <ets>schock</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Com.)</fld>
<def>A lot consisting of sixty pieces; -- a term applied in some Baltic
ports to loose goods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock</ent><br/
<hw>Shock</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To collect, or make up, into a
shock or shocks; to stook; <as>as, to <ex>shock</ex> rye</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock</ent><br/
<hw>Shock</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be occupied with making
shocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Reap well, scatter not, gather clean that is shorn,<br/
Bind fast, <qex>shock</qex> apace.</q> <rj><qau>Tusser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock</ent><br/
<hw>Shock</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>schok</ets> a bounce,
jolt, or leap, OHG. <ets>scoc</ets> a swing, MHG. <ets>schoc</ets>,
Icel. <ets>skykkjun</ets> tremuously, F. <ets>choc</ets> a shock,
collision, a dashing or striking against, Sp. <ets>choque</ets>, It.
<ets>ciocco</ets> a log. <root/161. Cf. <er>Shock</er> to shake.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A quivering or shaking which is the effect of a blow,
collision, or violent impulse; a blow, impact, or collision; a
concussion; a sudden violent impulse or onset.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These strong, unshaken mounds resist the <qex>shocks</qex><br/
Of tides and seas tempestuous.</q> <rj><qau>Blackmore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He stood the <qex>shock</qex> of a whole host of foes.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sudden agitation of the mind or feelings; a
sensation of pleasure or pain caused by something unexpected or
overpowering; also, a sudden agitating or overpowering event.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>shock</xex> of pleasure.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Talfourd.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A sudden depression of the vital
forces of the entire body, or of a part of it, marking some profound
impression produced upon the nervous system, as by severe injury,
overpowering emotion, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The sudden convulsion or
contraction of the muscles, with the feeling of a concussion, caused by
the discharge, through the animal system, of electricity from a charged
body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Concussion</er>, <er>Shock</er>.</syn>
<usage> Both words signify a sudden violent shaking caused by impact or
colision; but <xex>concussion</xex> is restricted in use to matter,
while <xex>shock</xex> is used also of mental states.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock</ent><br/
<hw>Shock</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shocked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shocking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>schokken</ets>; cf. D.
<ets>schokken</ets>, F. <ets>choquer</ets>, Sp. <ets>chocar</ets>.
<root/161. Cf. <er>Chuck</er> to strike, <er>Jog</er>, <er>Shake</er>,
<er>Shock</er> a striking, <er>Shog</er>, <pos>n.</pos> &
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To give a shock to; to cause to
shake or waver; hence, to strike against suddenly; to encounter with
violence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Come the three corners of the world in arms,<br/
And we shall <qex>shock</qex> them.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I shall never forget the force with which he <qex>shocked</qex> De
Vipont.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strike with surprise, terror, horror, or disgust;
to cause to recoil; <as>as, his violence <ex>shocked</ex> his
associates</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Advise him not to <qex>shock</qex> a father's will.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>To subject to the action of an
electrical discharge so as to cause a more or less violent depression or
commotion of the nervous system.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock</ent><br/
<hw>Shock</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To meet with a shock; to meet in
violent encounter.</def> <ldquo/They saw the moment approach when the
two parties would <xex>shock</xex> together.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock</ent><br/
<hw>Shock</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Shag</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A dog with long hair or shag; --
called also <altname>shockdog</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thick mass of bushy hair; <as>as, a head covered
with a <ex>shock</ex> of sandy hair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock</ent><br/
<hw>Shock</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bushy; shaggy; <as>as, a
<ex>shock</ex> hair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His red <qex>shock</qex> peruke . . . was laid aside.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shockdog</ent><br/
<hw>Shock"dog`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See 7th <er>Shock</er>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock-head</ent><br/
<hw>Shock"-head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Shock-headed.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shock-headed</ent><br/
<hw>Shock"-head`ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a thick and bushy
head of hair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shocking</ent><br/
<hw>Shock"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Causing to shake or tremble, as
by a blow; especially, causing to recoil with horror or disgust;
extremely offensive or disgusting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The grossest and most <qex>shocking</qex> villainies.</q>
<rj><qau>Secker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Shock"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Shock"ing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Shock troops, a highly trained or seasoned group within an army
used to spearhead a strong offensive action. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shod</ent><br/
<hw>Shod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <def>f
<er>Shoe</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoddy</ent><br/
<hw>Shod"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps fr.
<er>Shed</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>; as meaning originally, waste stuff
<ets>shed</ets> or thrown off; cf. dial. <ets>shod</ets> to shed, and E.
<er>Shed</er> a parting, separation, <er>Shode</er> a parting.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A fibrous material obtained by <ldquo/deviling,<rdquo/
or tearing into fibers, refuse woolen goods, old stockings, rags,
druggets, etc. See <er>Mungo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fluffy, fibrous waste from wool carding, worsted
spinning, or weaving of woolens.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A fabric of inferior quality made of, or containing
a large amount of, shoddy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The great quantity of shoddy goods furnished as army
supplies in the late Civil War in the United States gave wide currency
to the word, and it came to be applied to persons who pretend to a
higher position in society than that to which their breeding or worth
entitles them; this term is now (1997) rarely used in that
sense.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoddy fever</ent><br/
<hw>Shoddy fever</hw>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A febrile disease
characterized by dyspnoea and bronchitis caused by inhaling
dust.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoddy</ent><br/
<hw>Shod"dy</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Made wholly or in part
of shoddy; containing shoddy; <as>as, <ex>shoddy</ex> cloth;
<ex>shoddy</ex> blankets</as>; hence, colloquially, not genuine; sham;
pretentious; <as>as, <ex>shoddy</ex> aristocracy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shoddy</qex> inventions designed to bolster up a factitious
pride.</q> <rj><qau>Compton Reade.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>of poor quality or inferior workmanship.</def>
<au>[RHUD]</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>visibly worn or damaged from use; shabby.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoddyism</ent><br/
<hw>Shod"dy*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being shoddy.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <see>See the Note
under <er>Shoddy</er>, <pos>n.</pos></see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shode</ent><br/
<hw>Shode</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sc<amac/de</ets>, fr. <ets>sce<aacute/dan</ets>. See <er>Shed</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The parting of the hair on the
head.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Full straight and even lay his jolly <qex>shode</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The top of the head; the head.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoding</ent><br/
<ent>Shode</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shode</hw>, <hw>Shod"ing</hw> }</mhw>. <def>See
<er>Shoad</er>, <er>Shoading</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoder</ent><br/
<hw>Sho"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A package of gold
beater's skins in which gold is subjected to the second process of
beating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoe</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe</hw> <pr>(sh<oomac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shoes</plw> <pr>(sh<oomac/z)</pr>, <mark>formerly</mark>
<plw>Shoon</plw> <pr>(sh<oomac/n)</pr>, <mark>now
provincial</mark>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>sho</ets>, <ets>scho</ets>, AS.
<ets>sc<omac/h</ets>, <ets>sce<oacute/h</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>sk<omac/</ets>, OS. <ets>sk<omac/h</ets>, D. <ets>schoe</ets>,
<ets>schoen</ets>, G. <ets>schuh</ets>, OHG. <ets>scuoh</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sk<omac/r</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>sko</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sk<omac/hs</ets>; of unknown origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
covering for the human foot, usually made of leather, having a thick and
somewhat stiff sole and a lighter top. It differs from a boot on not
extending so far up the leg.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your hose should be ungartered, . . . your<qex>shoe</qex>
untied.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Spare none but such as go in clouted <qex>shoon</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything resembling a shoe in form, position, or
use.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A plate or rim of iron nailed
to the hoof of an animal to defend it from injury.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A band of iron or steel, or a ship of wood, fastened to the bottom
of the runner of a sleigh, or any vehicle which slides on the
snow.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A drag, or sliding piece of wood or iron,
placed under the wheel of a loaded vehicle, to retard its motion in
going down a hill.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The part of an automobile or
railroad car brake which presses upon the wheel to retard its
motion.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A trough-shaped or
spout-shaped member, put at the bottom of the water leader coming from
the eaves gutter, so as to throw the water off from the building.</def>
<sd>(f)</sd> <fld>(Milling.)</fld> <def>The trough or spout for
conveying the grain from the hopper to the eye of the millstone.</def>
<sd>(g)</sd> <def>An inclined trough in an ore-crushing mill.</def>
<sd>(h)</sd> <def>An iron socket or plate to take the thrust of a strut
or rafter.</def> <sd>(i)</sd> <def>An iron socket to protect the point
of a wooden pile.</def> <sd>(j)</sd> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A plate, or
notched piece, interposed between a moving part and the stationary part
on which it bears, to take the wear and afford means of adjustment; --
called also <altname>slipper</altname>, and
<altname>gib</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Shoe</xex> is often used adjectively, or in
composition; as, <xex>shoe</xex> buckle, or <xex>shoe</xex>-buckle;
<xex>shoe</xex> latchet, or <xex>shoe</xex>-latchet; <xex>shoe</xex>
leathet, or <xex>shoe</xex>-leather; <xex>shoe</xex> string,
<xex>shoe</xex>-string, or <xex>shoe</xex>string.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The outer cover or tread of a pneumatic tire, esp.
for an automobile.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shoe of an anchor</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A small block of wood, convex on the back, with a hole
to receive the point of the anchor fluke, -- used to prevent the anchor
from tearing the planks of the vessel when raised or lowered.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A broad, triangular piece of plank placed upon the
fluke to give it a better hold in soft ground.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoe
block</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a block with two sheaves, one
above the other, and at right angles to each other.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoe
bolt</b></col>, <cd>a bolt with a flaring head, for fastening shoes on
sleigh runners.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoe pac</b></col>, <cd>a kind of
moccasin. See <er>Pac</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoe stone</b></col>, <cd>a
sharpening stone used by shoemakers and other workers in
leather.</cd></cs> <-- brake shoe. the movable portion of a drum brake
on a vehicle which is pressed against the rotating drum to slow or stop
the vehicle by the friction of the brakeshoe against the drum. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoe</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shod</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shoeing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>sc<?/ian</ets>,
<ets>sce<?/ian</ets>. See <er>Shoe</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To furnish with a shoe or shoes; to put a shoe or shoes
on; <as>as, to <ex>shoe</ex> a horse, a sled, an anchor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To protect or ornament with something which serves
the purpose of a shoe; to tip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sharp and small end of the billiard stick, which is
<qex>shod</qex> with brass or silver.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoebill</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A large African wading bird (<spn>Balaeniceps rex</spn>) allied to
the storks and herons, and remarkable for its enormous broad swollen
bill. It inhabits the valley of the White Nile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoeblack</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"black`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who polishes
shoes; same as <er>bootblack</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoefly</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"fly`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Railroading)</fld> <def>A contrivance for throwing the track
temporarily to one side for convenience in filling washouts or effecting
other repairs.</def> <mark>[Cant, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>In some cylinder presses, a
device with long fingers for freeing the sheet from the
cylinder.</def></p>

<p><ent>Shoeing-horn</ent><br/
<ent>Shoehorn</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shoe"horn`</hw>, <hw>Shoe"ing-horn`</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A curved piece of polished
horn, wood, or metal used to facilitate the entrance of the foot into a
shoe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> Figuratively: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Anything by which a
transaction is facilitated; a medium; -- by way of contempt.</def>
<au>Spectator.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Anything which draws on or
allures; an inducement.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoehorn</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"horn</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>to squeeze or force into a
tight-fitting space, with or as though with a shoehorn; -- often used
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoeless</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of shoes.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoelace</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"lace</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a length of cord for tying the
upper parts of a shoe together. Commercial <ex>shoelaces</ex> usually
come in different lengths, and have each end confined in a narrow
plastic tube for convenience in inserting the cords through the holes in
a shoe upper. Also called <altname>shoestring</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoemaker</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"mak`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
whose occupation it is to make shoes and boots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
threadfish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The runner, 12.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoemaking</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"mak`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The business of a
shoemaker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoer</ent><br/
<hw>Sho"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who fits shoes to
the feet; one who furnishes or puts on shoes; <as>as, a <ex>shoer</ex>
of horses</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoestring</ent><br/
<hw>Shoe"string`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>same as
<er>shoelace</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a sum of money, inadequate or barely adequate for
the purpose to which it is put to use; <as>as, to start up an enterprise
on a <ex>shoestring</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shog</ent><br/
<hw>Shog</hw> <pr>(sh<ocr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Shock</er> a striking.]</ety> <def>A shock; a jog; a violent
concussion or impulse.</def> <mark>[R. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shog</ent><br/
<hw>Shog</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To shake; to shock.</def> <mark>[R.
or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shog</ent><br/
<hw>Shog</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. W. <ets>ysgogi</ets> to wag,
to stir. Cf. <er>Jog</er>.]</ety> <def>To jog; to move on.</def>
<mark>[R. or Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoggle</ent><br/
<hw>Shog"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Shog</er>, <er>Joggle</er>.]</ety> <def>To joggle.</def> <mark>[Obs.
or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Pegge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shogun</ent><br/
<hw>Sho"gun</hw> <pr>(sh<omac/"g<oomac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Jap.
military general, from Chin. <ets>tsiang ki<uum/n</ets> commander in
chief.]</ety> <def>A title originally conferred by the Mikado on the
military governor of the eastern provinces of Japan. By gradual
usurpation of power the <ex>Shoguns</ex> (known to foreigners as
<altname>Tycoons</altname>) became finally the virtual rulers of Japan.
The title was abolished in 1867.</def> <altsp>[Written variously,
<asp>Shiogun</asp>, <asp>Shiogoon</asp>, etc.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shogunate</ent><br/
<hw>Sho*gun"ate</hw> <pr>(sh<omac/*g<oomac/n"<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The office or dignity of a Shogun.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Siogoonate</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shola</ent><br/
<hw>Sho"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Sola</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shole</ent><br/
<hw>Shole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A plank fixed beneath an
object, as beneath the rudder of a vessel, to protect it from injury; a
plank on the ground under the end of a shore or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shole</ent><br/
<hw>Shole</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Shoal</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shonde</ent><br/
<hw>Shonde</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sceond</ets>.
Cf. <er>Shend</er>.]</ety> <def>Harm; disgrace; shame.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shone</ent><br/
<hw>Shone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoo</ent><br/
<hw>Shoo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>scheuchen</ets> to scare, drive away.]</ety> <def>Begone; away; --
an expression used in frightening away animals, especially
fowls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shoo-in</ent><br/
<-- <hw>shoo-in</hw>  <def>a candidate who is certain to win easily.</def> --><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shooi</ent><br/
<hw>Sho"oi</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Richardson's skua (<spn>Stercorarius parasiticus</spn>);- so called from its cry.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shook</ent><br/
<hw>Shook</hw> <pr>(sh<oocr/k)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> & <mark>obs.</mark>  or <mark>poet.</mark> <pos>p. p.</pos> of <er>Shake</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shook</ent><br/
<hw>Shook</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Shock</er> a bundle of sheaves.]</ety> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A set of staves and headings sufficient in number for one hogshead, cask, barrel, or the like, trimmed, and bound together in compact form.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A set of boards for a sugar box.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The parts of a piece of house furniture, as a bedstead, packed together.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shook</ent><br/
<hw>Shook</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pack, as staves, in a shook.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoon</ent><br/
<hw>Shoon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of <er>Shoe</er>.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark>  <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They shook the snow from hats and <qex>shoon</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoop</ent><br/
<hw>Shoop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of <er>Shape</er>. Shaped.</def>  <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoot</ent><br/
<hw>Shoot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>chute</ets>. See <er>Chute</er>. Confused with <ets>shoot</ets> to let fly.]</ety> <def>An inclined plane, either artificial or natural, down which timber, coal, etc., are caused to slide; also, a narrow passage, either natural or artificial, in a stream, where the water rushes rapidly; esp., a channel, having a swift current, connecting the ends of a bend in the stream, so as to shorten the course.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>chute</asp>, and <asp>shute</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To take a shoot</b></col>, <cd>to pass through a shoot instead of the main channel; to take the most direct course.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoot</ent><br/
<hw>Shoot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shot</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Shooting</conjf>. The old participle <conjf>Shotten</conjf> is obsolete. See <er>Shotten</er>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shotien</ets>, <ets>schotien</ets>, AS. <ets>scotian</ets>, v. i., <ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>schieten</ets>, G. <ets>schie<?/en</ets>, OHG. <ets>sciozan</ets>, Icel. <ets>skj<?/ta</ets>, Sw. <ets>skjuta</ets>, Dan. <ets>skyde</ets>; cf. Skr. <ets>skund</ets> to jump. <root/159.  Cf. <er>Scot</er> a contribution, <er>Scout</er> to reject, <er>Scud</er>, <er>Scuttle</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, <er>Shot</er>, <er>Sheet</er>, <er>Shut</er>, <er>Shuttle</er>, <er>Skittish</er>, <er>Skittles</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To let fly, or cause to be driven, with force, as an arrow or a bullet; -- followed by a word denoting the missile, as an object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If you please<br/
To shoot an arrow that self way.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To discharge, causing a missile to be driven forth; -- followed by a word denoting the weapon or instrument, as an object; -- often with <xex>off</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>shoot</ex> a gun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The two ends od a bow, <qex>shot</qex> off, fly from one another.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To strike with anything shot; to hit with a missile; often, to kill or wound with a firearm; -- followed by a word denoting the person or thing hit, as an object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When Roger <qex>shot</qex> the hawk hovering over his master's dove house.</q> <rj><qau>A. Tucker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To send out or forth, especially with a rapid or sudden motion; to cast with the hand; to hurl; to discharge; to emit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An honest weaver as ever <qex>shot</qex> shuttle.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A pit into which the dead carts had nightly <qex>shot</qex> corpses by scores.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To push or thrust forward; to project; to protrude; -- often with <xex>out</xex>; <as>as, a plant <ex>shoots</ex> out a bud</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>shoot</qex> out the lip, they shake the head.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. xxii. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beware the secret snake that <qex>shoots</qex> a sting.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>To plane straight; to fit by planing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Two pieces of wood that are <qex>shot</qex>, that is, planed or else pared with a paring chisel.</q> <rj><qau>Moxon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To pass rapidly through, over, or under; <as>as, to <ex>shoot</ex> a rapid or a bridge; to <ex>shoot</ex> a sand bar.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She . . . <qex>shoots</qex> the Stygian sound.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To variegate as if by sprinkling or intermingling; to color in spots or patches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tangled water courses slept,<br/
<qex>Shot</qex> over with purple, and green, and yellow.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To be shot of</b></col>, <cd>to be discharged, cleared, or rid of.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/Are you not glad <xex>to be shot of</xex> him?<rdquo/</cs>
 <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoot</ent><br/
<hw>Shoot</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause an engine or weapon to discharge a missile; -- said of a person or an agent; <as>as, they <ex>shot</ex> at a target; he <ex>shoots</ex> better than he rides</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The archers have . . . <qex>shot</qex> at him.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xlix. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To discharge a missile; -- said of an engine or instrument; <as>as, the gun <ex>shoots</ex> well</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be shot or propelled forcibly; -- said of a missile; to be emitted or driven; to move or extend swiftly, as if propelled; <as>as, a <ex>shooting</ex> star</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There <qex>shot</qex> a streaming lamp along the sky.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To penetrate, as a missile; to dart with a piercing sensation; <as>as, <ex>shooting</ex> pains</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy words <qex>shoot</qex> through my heart.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To feel a quick, darting pain; to throb in pain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These preachers make<br/
His head to <qex>shoot</qex> and ache.</q> <rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To germinate; to bud; to sprout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Onions, as they hang, will <qex>shoot</qex> forth.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But the wild olive <qex>shoots</qex>, and shades the ungrateful plain.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To grow; to advance; <as>as, to <ex>shoot</ex> up rapidly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Well <qex>shot</qex> in years he seemed.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Delightful task! to rear the tender thought,<br/
To teach the young idea how to <qex>shoot</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To change form suddenly; especially, to solidify.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the menstruum be overcharged, metals will <qex>shoot</qex> into crystals.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To protrude; to jut; to project; to extend; <as>as, the land <ex>shoots</ex> into a promontory</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There <qex>shot</qex> up against the dark sky, tall, gaunt, straggling houses.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To move ahead by force of momentum, as a sailing vessel when the helm is put hard alee.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shoot ahead</b></col>, <cd>to pass or move quickly forward; to outstrip others.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoot</ent><br/
<hw>Shoot</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of shooting; the discharge of a missile; a shot; <as>as, the <ex>shoot</ex> of a shuttle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Turkish bow giveth a very forcible <qex>shoot</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One underneath his horse to get a <qex>shoot</qex> doth stalk.</q> <rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A young branch or growth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Superfluous branches and <qex>shoots</qex> of this second spring.</q> <rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A rush of water; a rapid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A vein of ore running in the same general direction as the lode.</def>  <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>A weft thread shot through the shed by the shuttle; a pick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <ety>[Perh. a different word.]</ety> <def>A shoat; a young hog.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shooter</ent><br/
<hw>Shoot"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who shoots, as an archer or a gunner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which shoots.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A firearm; <as>as, a five-<ex>shooter</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A shooting star.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shooting</ent><br/
<hw>Shoot"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who, or that which, shoots; <as>as, the <ex>shooting</ex> of an archery club; the <ex>shooting</ex> of rays of light.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A wounding or killing with a firearm; specifically <fld>(Sporting)</fld>, the killing of game; <as>as, a week of <ex>shooting</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sensation of darting pain; <as>as, a <ex>shooting</ex> in one's head</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shooting</ent><br/
<hw>Shoot"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to shooting; for shooting; darting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shooting board</b></col> <fld>(Joinery)</fld>, <cd>a fixture used in planing or shooting the edge of a board, by means of which the plane is guided and the board held true.</cd> -- <col><b>Shooting box</b></col>, <cd>a small house in the country for use in the shooting season.</cd> <au>Prof. Wilson.</au> -- <col><b>Shooting gallery</b></col>, <cd>a range, usually covered, with targets for practice with firearms.</cd><-- [Slang] a place, often a building or neighborhood, where addicts "shoot up" drugs.  -->  -- <col><b>Shooting iron</b></col>, <cd>a firearm.</cd> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Shooting star</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <cd>A starlike, luminous meteor, that, appearing suddenly, darts quickly across some portion of the sky, and then as suddenly disappears, leaving sometimes, for a few seconds, a luminous train, -- called also <altname>falling star</altname>.</cd> <note><xex>Shooting stars</xex> are small cosmical bodies which encounter the earth in its annual revolution, and which become visible by coming with planetary velocity into the upper regions of the atmosphere. At certain periods, as on the 13th of November and 10th of August, they appear for a few hours in great numbers, apparently diverging from some point in the heavens, such displays being known as <xex>meteoric showers</xex>, or <xex>star showers</xex>. These bodies, before encountering the earth, were moving in orbits closely allied to the orbits of comets. See <er>Leonids</er>, <er>Perseids</er>.</note> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>The American cowslip (<spn>Dodecatheon Meadia</spn>). See under <er>Cowslip</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shooting stick</b></col> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>a tapering piece of wood or iron, used by printers to drive up the quoins in the chase.</cd> <au>Hansard.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1332 --></p>

<p><ent>Shooty</ent><br/
<hw>Shoot"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sprouting or coming up
freely and regularly.</def> <mark>[Prev. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shop</ent><br/
<hw>Shop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Shape</er>. Shaped.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shop</ent><br/
<hw>Shop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shoppe</ets>,
<ets>schoppe</ets>, AS. <ets>sceoppa</ets> a treasury, a storehouse,
stall, booth; akin to <ets>scypen</ets> a shed, LG. <ets>schup</ets> a
shed, G. <ets>schoppen</ets>, <ets>schuppen</ets>, a shed, a coachhouse,
OHG. <ets>scopf</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A building or an
apartment in which goods, wares, drugs, etc., are sold by
retail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From <qex>shop</qex> to <qex>shop</qex><br/
Wandering, and littering with unfolded silks<br/
The polished counter.</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A building in which mechanics or artisans work;
<as>as, a shoe <ex>shop</ex>; a car <ex>shop</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A tailor called me in his <qex>shop</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A person's occupation, business, profession, or the
like, as a subject of attention, interest, conversation, etc.; --
sometimes in deprecation or disapproval; <as>as, to talk <ex>shop</ex>
at a party</as>. Also used attributively, as in <ex>shop
talk</ex>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A place where any industry is carried on; <as>as, a
chemist's <ex>shop</ex></as>;</def> also, <fld>(Slang)</fld>, <def>any
of the various places of business which are commonly called offices, as
of a lawyer, doctor, broker, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Any place of resort, as one's house, a restaurant,
etc.</def> <mark>[Slang, Chiefly Eng.]</mark></p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>the group of workers and the activities controlled
by an administrator; <as>as, to have five people in one's
<ex>shop</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Shop</xex> is often used adjectively or in
composition; as, <xex>shop</xex> rent, or <xex>shop</xex>-rent;
<xex>shop thief</xex>, <xex>or shop</xex>-thief; <xex>shop</xex> window,
or <xex>shop</xex>-window, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To smell of the shop</b></col>, <cd>to indicate too
distinctively one's occupation or profession.</cd> -- <col><b>To talk
shop</b></col>, <cd>to make one's business the topic of social
conversation; also, to use the phrases peculiar to one's
employment.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Store; warehouse. See <er>Store</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shop</ent><br/
<hw>Shop</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shopped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shopping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To visit shops for the purpose
of purchasing goods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was engaged with his mother and some ladies to go
<qex>shopping</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopboard</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bench or board
on which work is performed; a workbench.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopbook</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"book`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A book in which a
tradesman keeps his accounts.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopboy</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"boy`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A boy employed in a
shop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopen</ent><br/
<hw>Sho"pen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos>
of <er>Shape</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopgirl</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"girl`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A girl employed in
a shop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopkeeper</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"keep`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A trader who
sells goods in a shop, or by retail; -- in distinction from one who
sells by wholesale.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoplifter</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"lift`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Shop</ets>
+ <ets>lift.</ets> See <er>Lift</er> to steal.]</ety> <def>One who
steals anything in a shop, or takes goods privately from a shop; one
who, under pretense of buying goods, takes occasion to steal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoplifting</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"lift`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Larceny committed in a shop;
the stealing of anything from a shop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoplike</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"like`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suiting a shop; vulgar.</def>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopmaid</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"maid`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
shopgirl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopman</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shopmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A shopkeeper;
a retailer.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who serves in a shop; a salesman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who works in a shop or a factory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopper</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
shops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoppish</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"pish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
appearance or qualities of a shopkeeper, or shopman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoppy</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding
with shops.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to shops, or one's own shop or
business; <as>as, <ex>shoppy</ex> talk</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Mrs. Gaskell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopshift</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"shift`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The trick of a
shopkeeper; deception.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shop talk</ent><br/
<hw>shop talk</hw>, <def>conversation about a matters pertaining to
person's occupation; -- often used of such discussions when conducted
outside of working hours, as in social gatherings.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopwalker</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"walk`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who walks
about in a shop as an overseer and director. Cf.
<er>Floorwalker</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopwoman</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"wom`an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shopwomen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A woman employed in a
shop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shopworn</ent><br/
<hw>Shop"worn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat worn or
damaged by having been kept for a time in a shop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorage</ent><br/
<hw>Shor"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Duty paid for goods
brought on shore.</def> <rj><au>Grabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shore</ent><br/
<hw>Shore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Shear</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shore</ent><br/
<hw>Shore</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sewer.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shore</ent><br/
<hw>Shore</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>schore</ets>; akin to LG.
<ets>schore</ets>, D. <ets>schoor</ets>, OD. <ets>schoore</ets>, Icel.
<ets>skor<?/a</ets>, and perhaps to E. <ets>shear</ets>, as being a
piece cut off.]</ety> <def>A prop, as a timber, placed as a brace or
support against the side of a building or other structure; a prop placed
beneath anything, as a beam, to prevent it from sinking or
sagging.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>shoar</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shore</ent><br/
<hw>Shore</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shored</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shoring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>schoren</ets>. See
<er>Shore</er> a prop.]</ety> <def>To support by a shore or shores; to
prop; -- usually with <xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>shore</ex> up a
building</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shore</ent><br/
<hw>Shore</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>schore</ets>, AS.
<ets>score</ets>, probably fr. <ets>scieran</ets>, and so meaning
properly, that which is shorn off, edge; akin to OD. <ets>schoore</ets>,
<ets>schoor</ets>. See <er>Shear</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>The
coast or land adjacent to a large body of water, as an ocean, lake, or
large river.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Michael Cassio,<br/
Lieutenant to the warlike Moor Othello,<br/
Is come <qex>shore</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fruitful <qex>shore</qex> of muddy Nile.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In shore</b></col>, <cd>near the shore.</cd>
<au>Marryat.</au> -- <col><b>On shore</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>On</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shore birds</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a collective name for the various limicoline birds found on the
seashore.</cd> -- <col><b>Shore crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any crab found on the beaches, or between tides, especially any one
of various species of grapsoid crabs, as <spn>Heterograpsus nudus</spn>
of California.</cd> -- <col><b>Shore lark</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a small American lark (<spn>Otocoris alpestris</spn>) found in
winter, both on the seacoast and on the Western plains. Its upper parts
are varied with dark brown and light brown. It has a yellow throat,
yellow local streaks, a black crescent on its breast, a black streak
below each eye, and two small black erectile ear tufts. Called also
<altname>horned lark</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shore plover</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large-billed Australian plover (<spn>Esacus
magnirostris</spn>). It lives on the seashore, and feeds on crustaceans,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Shore teetan</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
rock pipit (<spn>Anthus obscurus</spn>).</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shore</ent><br/
<hw>Shore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To set on
shore.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoreless</ent><br/
<hw>Shore"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no shore or coast; of
indefinite or unlimited extent; <as>as, a <ex>shoreless</ex>
ocean</as>.</def> <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoreling</ent><br/
<hw>Shore"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shorling</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorer</ent><br/
<hw>Shor"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, shores or props; a prop; a shore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoreward</ent><br/
<hw>Shore"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the
shore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoring</ent><br/
<hw>Shor"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of supporting
or strengthening with a prop or shore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A system of props; props, collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorlaceous</ent><br/
<ent>Shorl</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Shorl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <hw>Shor*la"ceous</hw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></mhw> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Schorl</er>, <er>Schorlaceous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorling</ent><br/
<hw>Shor"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The skin
of a sheen after the fleece is shorn off, as distinct from the
<xex>morling</xex>, or skin taken from the dead sheep; also, a sheep of
the first year's shearing.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person who is shorn; a shaveling; hence, in
contempt, a priest.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorn</ent><br/
<hw>Shorn</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shear</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short</ent><br/
<hw>Short</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shorter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shortest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>short</ets>,
<ets>schort</ets>, AS. <ets>scort</ets>, <ets>sceort</ets>; akin to OHG.
<ets>scurz</ets>, Icel. <ets>skorta</ets> to be short of, to lack, and
perhaps to E. <ets>shear</ets>, v. t. Cf. <er>Shirt</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Not long; having brief length or linear extension;
<as>as, a <ex>short</ex> distance; a <ex>short</ex> piece of timber; a
<ex>short</ex> flight.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bed is <qex>shorter</qex> than that a man can stretch himself
on it.</q> <rj><qau>Isa. xxviii. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not extended in time; having very limited duration;
not protracted; <as>as, <ex>short</ex> breath</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The life so <qex>short</qex>, the craft so long to learn.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>short</qex> absense I could yield.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Limited in quantity; inadequate; insufficient;
scanty; <as>as, a <ex>short</ex> supply of provisions, or of
water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Insufficiently provided; inadequately supplied;
scantily furnished; lacking; not coming up to a reasonable, or the
ordinary, standard; -- usually with <xex>of</xex>; <as>as, to be
<ex>short</ex> of money</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We shall be <qex>short</qex> in our provision.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Deficient; defective; imperfect; not coming up, as
to a measure or standard; <as>as, an account which is <ex>short</ex> of
the truth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Not distant in time; near at hand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Marinell was sore offended<br/
That his departure thence should be so <qex>short</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He commanded those who were appointed to attend him to be ready by
a <qex>short</qex> day.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Limited in intellectual power or grasp; not
comprehensive; narrow; not tenacious, as memory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their own <qex>short</qex> understandings reach<br/
No farther than the present.</q> <rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Less important, efficacious, or powerful; not equal
or equivalent; less (than); -- with <xex>of</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hardly anything <qex>short</qex> of an invasion could rouse them
again to war.</q> <rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Abrupt; brief; pointed; petulant; <as>as, he gave a
<ex>short</ex> answer to the question</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>Breaking or crumbling readily
in the mouth; crisp; <as>as, <ex>short</ex> pastry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Metal)</fld> <def>Brittle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Metals that are brittle when hot are called
<xex><?/ot-short</xex>; as, cast iron may be <xex>hot-short</xex>, owing
to the presence of sulphur. Those that are brittle when cold are called
<xex>cold-short</xex>; as, cast iron may be <xex>cold-short</xex>, on
account of the presence of phosphorus.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Stock Exchange)</fld> <def>Engaging or engaged to
deliver what is not possessed; <as>as, <ex>short</ex> contracts; to be
<ex>short</ex> of stock. See <ex>The shorts</ex>, under <er>Short</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and <ex>To sell short</ex>, under <er>Short</er>,
<pos>adv.</pos></as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In mercantile transactions, a note or bill is sometimes
made payable <xex>at short sight</xex>, that is, in a little time after
being presented to the payer.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <fld>(Phon.)</fld> <def>Not prolonged, or relatively
less prolonged, in utterance; -- opposed to <ant>long</ant>, and applied
to vowels or to syllables. In English, the long and short of the same
letter are not, in most cases, the long and short of the same sound;
thus, the <xex>i</xex> in <xex>ill</xex> is the short sound, not of
<xex>i</xex> in <xex>isle</xex>, but of <xex>ee</xex> in <xex>eel</xex>,
and the <xex>e</xex> in <xex>pet</xex> is the short sound of
<xex>a</xex> in <xex>pate</xex>, etc. See <er>Quantity</er>, and
<xex>Guide to Pronunciation</xex>, <sect/<sect/22, 30.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Short</xex> is much used with participles to form
numerous self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>short</xex>-armed,
<xex>short</xex>-billed, <xex>short</xex>-fingered,
<xex>short</xex>-haired, <xex>short</xex>-necked,
<xex>short</xex>-sleeved, <xex>short</xex>-tailed,
<xex>short</xex>-winged, <xex>short</xex>-wooled, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At short notice</b></col>, <cd>in a brief time;
promptly.</cd> -- <col><b>Short rib</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>,
<cd>one of the false ribs.</cd> -- <col><b>Short suit</b></col>
<fld>(Whist)</fld>, <cd>any suit having only three cards, or less than
three.</cd> <au>R. A. Proctor.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>To come
short</b></col>, <col><b>To cut short</b></col>, <col><b>To fall
short</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Come</er>, <er>Cut</er>,
etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short</ent><br/
<hw>Short</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A summary
account.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>short</qex> and the long is, our play is preferred.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The part of milled grain sifted out
which is next finer than the bran.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The first remove above bran is <qex>shorts</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Halliwell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Short, inferior hemp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Breeches; shortclothes.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <def>A short sound, syllable, or
vowel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If we compare the nearest conventional <qex>shorts</qex> and longs
in English, as in <ldquo/bit<rdquo/ and <ldquo/beat,<rdquo/
<ldquo/not<rdquo/ and <ldquo/naught,<rdquo/ we find that the short
vowels are generally wide, the long narrow, besides being generally
diphthongic as well. Hence, originally short vowels can be lengthened
and yet kept quite distinct from the original longs.</q> <rj><qau>H.
Sweet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In short</b></col>, <cd>in few words; in brief;
briefly.</cd> -- <col><b>The long and the short</b></col>, <cd>the
whole; a brief summing up.</cd> -- <col><b>The shorts</b></col>
<fld>(Stock Exchange)</fld>, <cd>those who are unsupplied with stocks
which they contracted to deliver.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short</ent><br/
<hw>Short</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a short manner;
briefly; limitedly; abruptly; quickly; <as>as, to stop <ex>short</ex> in
one's course; to turn <ex>short</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was taken up very <qex>short</qex>, and adjudged corrigible for
such presumptuous language.</q> <rj><qau>Howell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sell short</b></col> <fld>(Stock Exchange)</fld>,
<cd>to sell, for future delivery, what the party selling does not own,
but hopes to buy at a lower rate.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short</ent><br/
<hw>Short</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sceortian</ets>.]</ety>
<def>To shorten.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short</ent><br/
<hw>Short</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fail; to decrease.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortage</ent><br/
<hw>Short"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Amount or extent of
deficiency, as determined by some requirement or standard; <as>as, a
<ex>shortage</ex> in money accounts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-breathed</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-breathed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having short-breath, or quick respiration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having short life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortcake</ent><br/
<hw>Short"cake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An unsweetened
breakfast cake shortened with butter or lard, rolled thin, and
baked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short circuit</ent><br/
<hw>Short" cir"cuit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A circuit
formed or closed by a conductor of relatively low resistance because
shorter or of relatively great conductivity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-circuit</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-cir`cuit</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Short-circuited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Short-circuiting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>To
join, as the electrodes of a battery or dynamo or any two points of a
circuit, by a conductor of low resistance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortclothes</ent><br/
<hw>Short"clothes`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Coverings for
the legs of men or boys, consisting of trousers which reach only to the
knees, -- worn with long stockings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortcoming</ent><br/
<hw>Short"com`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
falling, or coming short</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The failure of a
crop, or the like.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Neglect of, or failure in,
performance of duty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-dated</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-dat`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having little
time to run from the date.</def> <ldquo/Thy <xex>short-dated</xex>
life.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorten</ent><br/
<hw>Short"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shortened</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shortening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Short</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make short or shorter in
measure, extent, or time; <as>as, to <ex>shorten</ex> distance; to
<ex>shorten</ex> a road; to <ex>shorten</ex> days of
calamity.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To reduce or diminish in amount, quantity, or
extent; to lessen; to abridge; to curtail; to contract; <as>as, to
<ex>shorten</ex> work, an allowance of food, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here, where the subject is so fruitful, I am <qex>shortened</qex>
by my chain.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make deficient (as to); to deprive; -- with
<xex>of</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Spoiled of his nose, and <qex>shortened</qex> of his ears.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make short or friable, as pastry, with butter,
lard, pot liquor, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shorten a rope</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to
take in the slack of it.</cd> -- <col><b>To shorten sail</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to reduce sail by taking it in.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorten</ent><br/
<hw>Short"en</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become short or shorter;
<as>as, the day <ex>shortens</ex> in northern latitudes from June to
December; a metallic rod <ex>shortens</ex> by cold.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortener</ent><br/
<hw>Short"en*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, shortens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortening</ent><br/
<hw>Short"en*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of making
or becoming short or shorter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>That which renders pastry short
or friable, as butter, lard, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorthand</ent><br/
<hw>Short"hand`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A compendious and
rapid method or writing by substituting characters, abbreviations, or
symbols, for letters, words, etc.; short writing; stenography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-handed</ent><br/
<hw>Short`-hand"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Short of, or lacking the
regular number of, servants or helpers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorthead</ent><br/
<hw>Short"head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sucking whale
less than one year old; -- so called by sailors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shorthorn</ent><br/
<hw>Short"horn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>One of a breed of
large, heavy domestic cattle having short horns. The breed was developed
in England.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-jointed</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-joint`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having short
intervals between the joints; -- said of a plant or an animal,
especially of a horse whose pastern is too short.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-lived</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-lived`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not living or
lasting long; being of short continuance; <as>as, a <ex>short-lived</ex>
race of beings; <ex>short-lived</ex> pleasure; <ex>short-lived</ex>
passion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortly</ent><br/
<hw>Short"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sceortlice</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a short or brief time
or manner; soon; quickly.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I shall grow jealous of you <qex>shortly</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The armies came <qex>shortly</qex> in view of each other.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In few words; briefly; abruptly; curtly; <as>as, to
express ideas more <ex>shortly</ex> in verse than in
prose</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortness</ent><br/
<hw>Short"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
short; want of reach or extension; brevity; deficiency; <as>as, the
<ex>shortness</ex> of a journey; the <ex>shortness</ex> of the days in
winter; the <ex>shortness</ex> of an essay; the <ex>shortness</ex> of
the memory; a <ex>shortness</ex> of provisions; <ex>shortness</ex> of
breath</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortsighted</ent><br/
<hw>Short"sight`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not
able to see far; nearsighted; myopic. See <er>Myopic</er>, and
<er>Myopia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Not able to look far into futurity; unable to
understand things deep; of limited intellect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having little regard for the future;
heedless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Short"sight`ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Short"sight`ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cunning is a kind of <qex>shortsightedness</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-spoken</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-spo`ken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Speaking in a
quick or short manner; hence, gruff; curt.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortstop</ent><br/
<hw>Short"stop`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Baseball)</fld>
<def>The player stationed in the field between the second and third
bases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-waisted</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-waist`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a short
waist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Short-winded</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-wind`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected with
shortness of breath; having a quick, difficult respiration, as dyspnoic
and asthmatic persons.</def> <rj><au>May.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shortwing</ent><br/
<hw>Short"wing`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of several species of small wrenlike Asiatic birds having
short wings and a short tail. They belong to <xex>Brachypterix</xex>,
<xex>Callene</xex>, and allied genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1333 --></p>

<p><ent>Short-witted</ent><br/
<hw>Short"-wit`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having little
wit; not wise; having scanty intellect or judgment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shory</ent><br/
<hw>Shor"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lying near the
shore.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoshones</ent><br/
<hw>Sho*sho"nes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Shoshone</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>A linguistic family or stock of North American Indians, comprising
many tribes, which extends from Montana and Idaho into Mexico. In a
restricted sense the name is applied especially to the Snakes, the most
northern of the tribes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot</ent><br/
<hw>Shot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shoot</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot</ent><br/
<hw>Shot</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Woven in such a way as to produce an
effect of variegation, of changeable tints, or of being figured; <as>as,
<ex>shot</ex> silks</as>. See <er>Shoot</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>,
8.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot</ent><br/
<hw>Shot</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>scot</ets>,
<ets>sceot</ets>, fr. <ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets> to shoot; akin to D.
<ets>sschot</ets>, Icel. <ets>skot</ets>. <root/159. See <er>Scot</er> a
share, <er>Shoot</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Shot</er> a
shooting.]</ety> <def>A share or proportion; a reckoning; a
scot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here no <qex>shots</qex> are where all shares be.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man is never . . . welcome to a place till some certain
<qex>shot</qex> be paid and the hostess say <ldquo/Welcome.<rdquo/</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot</ent><br/
<hw>Shot</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Shot</plw>or
<plw>Shots</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>shot</ets>,
<ets>schot</ets>, AS. <ets>gesceot</ets> a missile; akin to D.
<ets>schot</ets> a shot, shoot, G. <ets>schuss</ets>,
ge<ets>schoss</ets> a missile, Icel. <ets>skot</ets> a throwing, a
javelin, and E. <ets>shoot</ets>, v.t. <root/159. See <er>Shoot</er>,
and cf. <er>Shot</er> a share.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
shooting; discharge of a firearm or other weapon which throws a
missile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He caused twenty <qex>shot</qex> of his greatest cannon to be made
at the king's army.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A missile weapon, particularly a ball or bullet;
specifically, whatever is discharged as a projectile from firearms or
cannon by the force of an explosive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Shot</xex> used in war is of various kinds,
classified according to the material of which it is composed, into
<xex>lead</xex>, <xex>wrought-iron</xex>, and <xex>cast-iron</xex>;
according to form, into <xex>spherical</xex> and <xex>oblong</xex>;
according to structure and modes of operation, into <xex>solid</xex>,
<xex>hollow</xex>, and <xex>case</xex>. See <cref>Bar shot</cref>,
<cref>Chain shot</cref>, etc., under <er>Bar</er>, <er>Chain</er>,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Small globular masses of lead, of various sizes, --
used chiefly as the projectiles in shotguns for killing game; <as>as,
bird <ex>shot</ex>; buck<ex>shot</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The flight of a missile, or the distance which it
is, or can be, thrown; <as>as, the vessel was distant more than a cannon
<ex>shot</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A marksman; one who practices shooting; <as>as, an
excellent <ex>shot</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Fisheries)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A cast of a
net.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The entire throw of nets at one time.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A place or spot for setting nets.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>A single draft or catch of fish made.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Athletics)</fld> <def>A spherical weight, to be
put, or thrown, in competition for distance.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A stroke, throw, or other action to propel a ball or
other game piece in certain games, as in billiards, hockey, basketball,
curling, etc.; also, a move, as in chess.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A guess; conjecture; also, an attempt.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/I'll take a <ex>shot</ex> at
it.<rdquo/<br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shot belt</b></col>, <cd>a belt having a pouch or
compartment for carrying shot.</cd> -- <col><b>Shot cartridge</b></col>,
<cd>a cartridge containing powder and small shot, forming a charge for a
shotgun.</cd> -- <col><b>Shot garland</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>a wooden frame to contain shot, secured to the coamings and ledges
round the hatchways of a ship.</cd> -- <col><b>Shot gauge</b></col>,
<cd>an instrument for measuring the diameter of round shot.</cd>
<au>Totten.</au> -- <col><b>shot hole</b></col>, <cd>a hole made by a
shot or bullet discharged.</cd> -- <col><b>Shot locker</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a strongly framed compartment in the hold of a
vessel, for containing shot.</cd> -- <col><b>Shot of a cable</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the splicing of two or more cables together, or
the whole length of the cables thus united.</cd> -- <col><b>Shot
prop</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a wooden prop covered with tarred
hemp, to stop a hole made by the shot of an enemy in a ship's side.</cd>
-- <col><b>Shot tower</b></col>, <cd>a lofty tower for making shot, by
dropping from its summit melted lead in slender streams. The lead forms
spherical drops which cool in the descent, and are received in water or
other liquid.</cd> -- <col><b>Shot window</b></col>, <cd>a window
projecting from the wall. Ritson, quoted by Halliwell, explains it as a
window that opens and shuts; and Wodrow describes it as a window of
shutters made of timber and a few inches of glass above
them.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot</ent><br/
<hw>Shot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shotted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shotting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To load with shot, as a
gun.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot-clog</ent><br/
<hw>Shot"-clog`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person tolerated
only because he pays the shot, or reckoning, for the rest of the
company, otherwise a mere clog on them.</def> <mark>[Old
Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou common <qex>shot-clog</qex>, gull of all companies.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shote</ent><br/
<hw>Shote</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sce<oacute/ta</ets> a darting fish, a trout, fr.
<ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets>. See <er>Shoot</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A fish resembling the trout.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Garew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Perh. a different word.]</ety> <def>A young hog; a
shoat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot-free</ent><br/
<hw>Shot"-free`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not to be injured
by shot; shot-proof.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot-free</ent><br/
<hw>Shot"-free`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from charge or expense;
hence, unpunished; scot-free.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shotgun</ent><br/
<hw>Shot"gun`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A light,
smooth-bored gun, often double-barreled, especially designed for firing
small shot at short range, and killing small game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot-proof</ent><br/
<hw>Shot"-proof`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Impenetrable by
shot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shots</ent><br/
<hw>Shots</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>The refuse of cattle
taken from a drove.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shot samples</ent><br/
<hw>Shot samples</hw>. <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>Samples taken for assay
from a molten metallic mass pouring a portion into water, to granulate
it.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shotted</ent><br/
<hw>Shot"ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Loaded
with shot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Having a shot attached; <as>as, a
<ex>shotten</ex> suture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shotten</ent><br/
<hw>Shot"ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Properly p. p. of
<ets>shoot</ets>; AS. <ets>scoten</ets>, <ets>sceoten</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having ejected the
spawn; <as>as, a <ex>shotten</ex> herring</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Shot out of its socket; dislocated, as a
bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shough</ent><br/
<hw>Shough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
shockdog.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shough</ent><br/
<hw>Shough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shoo</er>.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Should</ent><br/
<hw>Should</hw> <pr>(sh<oocr/d)</pr>, <pos>imp.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Shall</er></mord>. <ety>[OE. <ets>sholde</ets>, <ets>shulde</ets>,
<ets>scholde</ets>, <ets>schulde</ets>, AS. <ets>scolde</ets>,
<ets>sceolde</ets>. See <er>Shall</er>.]</ety> <def>Used as an auxiliary
verb, to express a conditional or contingent act or state, or as a
supposition of an actual fact; also, to express moral obligation (see
<er>Shall</er>); <it>e. g.</it>: they <xex>should</xex> have come last
week; if I <xex>should</xex> go; I <xex>should</xex> think you could
go.</def> <ldquo/You have done that you <xex>should</xex> be sorry
for.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Ought</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoulder</ent><br/
<hw>Shoul"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shulder</ets>, <ets>shuldre</ets>, <ets>schutder</ets>, AS.
<ets>sculdor</ets>; akin to D. <ets>schoulder</ets>, G.
<ets>schulter</ets>, OHG. <ets>scultarra</ets>, Dan. <ets>skulder</ets>,
Sw. <ets>skuldra</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
joint, or the region of the joint, by which the fore limb is connected
with the body or with the shoulder girdle; the projection formed by the
bones and muscles about that joint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The flesh and muscles connected with the shoulder
joint; the upper part of the back; that part of the human frame on which
it is most easy to carry a heavy burden; -- often used in the
plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then by main force pulled up, and on his <qex>shoulders</qex>
bore<br/
The gates of Azza.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Adown her <qex>shoulders</qex> fell her length of hair.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: That which supports or sustains;
support.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In thy <qex>shoulder</qex> do I build my seat.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which resembles a human shoulder, as any
protuberance or projection from the body of a thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The north western <qex>shoulder</qex> of the mountain.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The upper joint of the fore leg and adjacent parts
of an animal, dressed for market; <as>as, a <ex>shoulder</ex> of
mutton</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>The angle of a bastion included
between the face and flank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>An abrupt projection which forms an abutment on an
object, or limits motion, etc., as the projection around a tenon at the
end of a piece of timber, the part of the top of a type which projects
beyond the base of the raised character, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shoulder belt</b></col>, <cd>a belt that passes across
the shoulder.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoulder blade</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the flat bone of the shoulder, to which the
humerus is articulated; the scapula.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoulder
block</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a block with a projection, or
shoulder, near the upper end, so that it can rest against a spar without
jamming the rope.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoulder clapper</b></col>, <cd>one
who claps another on the shoulder, or who uses great familiarity.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>Shoulder girdle</b></col>.
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Pectoral girdle</cref>, under
<er>Pectoral</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoulder knot</b></col>, <cd>an
ornamental knot of ribbon or lace worn on the shoulder; a kind of
epaulet or braided ornament worn as part of a military uniform.</cd> --
<col><b>Shoulder-of-mutton sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a
triangular sail carried on a boat's mast; -- so called from its
shape.</cd> -- <col><b>Shoulder slip</b></col>, <cd>dislocation of the
shoulder, or of the humerous.</cd> <au>Swift.</au> -- <col><b>Shoulder
strap</b></col>, <cd>a strap worn on or over the shoulder. Specifically
<fld>(Mil. & Naval)</fld>, a narrow strap worn on the shoulder of a
commissioned officer, indicating, by a suitable device, the rank he
holds in the service.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoulder</ent><br/
<hw>Shoul"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shouldered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shouldering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To push
or thrust with the shoulder; to push with violence; to jostle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As they the earth would <qex>shoulder</qex> from her seat.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Around her numberless the rabble flowed,<br/
<qex>Shouldering</qex> each other, crowding for a view.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take upon the shoulder or shoulders; <as>as, to
<ex>shoulder</ex> a basket</as>; hence, to assume the burden or
responsibility of; <as>as, to <ex>shoulder</ex> blame; to
<ex>shoulder</ex> a debt.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As if Hercules<br/
Or burly Atlas <qex>shouldered</qex> up their state.</q>
<rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Right shoulder arms</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a
position in the Manual of Arms which the piece is placed on the right
shoulder, with the lock plate up, and the muzzle elevated and inclined
to the left, and held as in the illustration.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- illustr. of a soldier at right soulder arms. --></p>

<p><ent>Shoulder</ent><br/
<hw>Shoul"der</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To push with the shoulder; to
make one's way, as through a crowd, by using the shoulders; to move
swaying the shoulders from side to side.</def></p>

<p><q>A yoke of the great sulky white bullocks . . . came
<qex>shouldering</qex> along together.</q>
<rj><qau>Kipling.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shouldered</ent><br/
<hw>Shoul"dered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having shoulders;
-- used in composition; <as>as, a broad-<ex>shouldered</ex>
man</as>.</def> <ldquo/He was short-<xex>shouldered</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoulder-shotten</ent><br/
<hw>Shoul"der-shot`ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sprained in
the shoulder, as a horse.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shout</ent><br/
<hw>Shout</hw> <pr>(shout)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shouted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shouting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shouten</ets>, of
unknown origin; perhaps akin to <ets>shoot</ets>; cf. Icel.
<ets>sk<umac/ta</ets>, <ets>sk<umac/ti</ets>, a taunt.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter a sudden and loud outcry, as in joy, triumph,
or exultation, or to attract attention, to animate soldiers,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shouting</qex> of the men and women eke.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>shouted</qex> thrice: what was the last cry for?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To entertain with refreshments or the like
gratuitously; to treat.</def> <mark>[Slang, Australia & U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shout at</b></col>, <cd>to utter shouts at; to deride
or revile with shouts.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shout</ent><br/
<hw>Shout</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter with a shout;
to cry; -- sometimes with <xex>out</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>shout</ex>, or
to <ex>shout</ex> out, a man's name</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To treat with shouts or clamor.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To treat (one) to something; also, to give
(something) by way of treating.</def> <mark>[Slang, Australia & U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shout</ent><br/
<hw>Shout</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A loud burst of voice or
voices; a vehement and sudden outcry, especially of a multitudes
expressing joy, triumph, exultation, or animated courage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Rhodians, seeing the enemy turn their backs, gave a great
<qex>shout</qex> in derision.</q> <rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A gratuitous entertainment, with refreshments or the
like; a treat.</def> <mark>[Slang, Australia & U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shouter</ent><br/
<hw>Shout"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
shouts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shove</ent><br/
<hw>Shove</hw> <pr>(sh<ucr/v)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shoved</conjf> <pr>(sh<ucr/vd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Shoving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shoven</ets>, AS. <ets>scofian</ets>, fr. <ets>sc<umac/fan</ets>;
akin to OFries. <ets>sk<umac/va</ets>, D. <ets>schuiven</ets>, G.
<ets>schieben</ets>, OHG. <ets>scioban</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sk<umac/fa</ets>, <ets>sk<ymac/fa</ets>, Sw. <ets>skuffa</ets>,
Dan. <ets>skuffe</ets>, Goth. af<ets>skiuban</ets> to put away, cast
away; cf. Skr. <ets>kshubh</ets> to become agitated, to quake, Lith.
<ets>skubrus</ets> quick, <ets>skubinti</ets> to hasten. <root/160. Cf.
<er>Sheaf</er> a bundle of stalks, <er>Scoop</er>,
<er>Scuffle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To drive along by the direct
and continuous application of strength; to push; especially, to push (a
body) so as to make it move along the surface of another body; <as>as,
to <ex>shove</ex> a boat on the water; to <ex>shove</ex> a table across
the floor.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To push along, aside, or away, in a careless or rude
manner; to jostle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>shove</qex> away the worthy bidden guest.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He used to <qex>shove</qex> and elbow his fellow servants.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shove</ent><br/
<hw>Shove</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To push or drive
forward; to move onward by pushing or jostling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move off or along by an act pushing, as with an
oar a pole used by one in a boat; sometimes with
<xex>off</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He grasped the oar,<br/
eceived his guests on board, and <qex>shoved</qex> from shore.</q>
<rj><qau>Garth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shove</ent><br/
<hw>Shove</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of shoving; a
forcible push.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I rested . . . and then gave the boat another
<qex>shove</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Thrust</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shove</ent><br/
<hw>Shove</hw>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shove</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovegroat</ent><br/
<ent>Shoveboard</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shove"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shove"groat`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The same as
<er>Shovelboard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovel</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shovele</ets>, <ets>schovele</ets>, AS. <ets>scoft</ets>,
<ets>sceoft</ets>; akin to D. <ets>schoffel</ets>, G.
<ets>schaufel</ets>, OHG. <ets>sc<?/vala</ets>, Dan. <ets>skovl</ets>,
Sw. <ets>skofvel</ets>, <ets>skyffel</ets>, and to E. <ets>shove</ets>.
<root/160. See <er>Shove</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>An implement
consisting of a broad scoop, or more or less hollow blade, with a
handle, used for lifting and throwing earth, coal, grain, or other loose
substances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shovel hat</b></col>, <cd>a broad-brimmed hat, turned up
at the sides, and projecting in front like a shovel, -- worn by some
clergy of the English Church.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> --
<col><b>Shovelspur</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a flat, horny
process on the tarsus of some toads, -- used in burrowing.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam shovel</b></col>, <cd>a machine with a scoop or scoops,
operated by a steam engine, for excavating earth, as in making railway
cuttings.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovel</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shoveled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Shovelled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Shoveling</conjf> or
<conjf>Shovelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take up and
throw with a shovel; <as>as, to <ex>shovel</ex> earth into a heap, or
into a cart, or out of a pit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To gather up as with a shovel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovelard</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el*ard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Shoveler.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovelbill</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el*bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The shoveler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovelboard</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el*board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
board on which a game is played, by pushing or driving pieces of metal
or money to reach certain marks; also, the game itself. Called also
<altname>shuffleboard</altname>, <altname>shoveboard</altname>,
<altname>shovegroat</altname>, <altname>shovelpenny</altname>.</def><--
now usu. shuffleboard. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A game played on board ship in which the aim is to
shove or drive with a cue wooden disks into divisions chalked on the
deck; -- called also <altname>shuffleboard</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoveler</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Also
<ets>shoveller</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which,
shovels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A river duck (<spn>Spatula
clypeata</spn>), native of Europe and America. It has a large bill,
broadest towards the tip. The male is handsomely variegated with green,
blue, brown, black, and white on the body; the head and neck are dark
green. Called also <altname>broadbill</altname>,
<altname>spoonbill</altname>, <altname>shovelbill</altname>, and
<altname>maiden duck</altname>. The Australian shoveler, or shovel-nosed
duck (<spn>Spatula rhynchotis</spn>), is a similar species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovelful</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shovelfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>As much as a shovel
will hold; enough to fill a shovel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovelhead</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el*head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A shark (<spn>Sphryna tiburio</spn>) allied to the hammerhead, and
native of the warmer parts of the Atlantic and Pacific oceans; -- called
also <altname>bonnet shark</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovelnose</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el*nose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The common sand shark. See under <er>Snad</er>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A small California shark (<spn>Heptranchias
maculatus</spn>), which is taken for its oil.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A
Pacific Ocean shark (<spn>Hexanchus corinus</spn>).</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>A ganoid fish of the Sturgeon family (<spn>Scaphirhynchus
platyrhynchus</spn>) of the Mississippi and Ohio rivers; -- called also
<altname>white sturgeon</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shovel-nosed</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"el-nosed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a broad, flat nose; <as>as, the <ex>shovel-nosed</ex> duck,
or shoveler</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shoven</ent><br/
<hw>Shov"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos>
of <er>Shove</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Show</ent><br/
<hw>Show</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Showed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shown</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Showed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Showing</conjf>. <altsp>It is sometimes written
<asp>shew</asp>, <asp>shewed</asp>, <asp>shewn</asp>,
<asp>shewing</asp>.</altsp>]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>schowen</ets>,
<ets>shewen</ets>, <ets>schewen</ets>, <ets>shawen</ets>, AS.
<ets>sce<aacute/wian</ets>, to look, see, view; akin to OS.
<ets>scaw<?/n</ets>, OFries. <ets>skawia</ets>, D. <ets>schouwen</ets>,
OHG. <ets>scouw<?/n</ets>, G. <ets>schauen</ets>, Dan. <ets>skue</ets>,
Sw. <ets>sk<?/da</ets>, Icel. <ets>sko<?/a</ets>, Goth.
us<ets>skawjan</ets> to waken, <ets>skuggwa</ets> a mirror, Icel.
<ets>skuggy</ets> shade, shadow, L. <ets>cavere</ets> to be on one's
guard, Gr. <?/<?/<?/ to mark, perceive, hear, Skr. <ets>kavi</ets> wise.
Cf. <er>Caution</er>, <er>Scavenger</er>, <er>Sheen</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To exhibit or present to view; to place in sight; to
display; -- the thing exhibited being the object, and often with an
indirect object denoting the person or thing seeing or beholding;
<as>as, to <ex>show</ex> a house; <ex>show</ex> your colors; shopkeepers
<ex>show</ex> customers goods (show goods to customers).</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Go thy way, <qex>shew</qex> thyself to the priest.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. viii. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor want we skill or art from whence to raise<br/
Magnificence; and what can heaven <qex>show</qex> more?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exhibit to the mental view; to tell; to disclose;
to reveal; to make known; <as>as, to <ex>show</ex> one's
designs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shew</qex> them the way wherein they must walk.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. xviii. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If it please my father to do thee evil, then I will
<qex>shew</qex> it thee, and send thee away.</q> <rj><qau>1 Sam. xx.
13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Specifically, to make known the way to (a person);
hence, to direct; to guide; to usher; to conduct; <as>as, to
<ex>show</ex> a person into a parlor; to <ex>show</ex> one to the
door</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make apparent or clear, as by evidence,
testimony, or reasoning; to prove; to explain; also, to manifest; to
evince; <as>as, to <ex>show</ex> the truth of a statement; to
<ex>show</ex> the causes of an event</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I 'll <qex>show</qex> my duty by my timely care.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To bestow; to confer; to afford; <as>as, to
<ex>show</ex> favor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shewing</qex> mercy unto thousands of them that love me.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. xx. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To show forth</b></col>, <cd>to manifest; to publish; to
proclaim.</cd> -- <col><b>To show his paces</b></col>, <cd>to exhibit
the gait, speed, or the like; -- said especially of a horse.</cd> --
<col><b>To show off</b></col>, <cd>to exhibit ostentatiously.</cd> --
<col><b>To show up</b></col>, <cd>to expose.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Show</ent><br/
<hw>Show</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>shew</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To exhibit or manifest one's
self or itself; to appear; to look; to be in appearance; to
seem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Just such she <qex>shows</qex> before a rising storm.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All round a hedge upshoots, and <qex>shows</qex><br/
At distance like a little wood.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1334 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have a certain appearance, as well or ill, fit or
unfit; to become or suit; to appear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My lord of York, it better <qex>showed</qex> with you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To show off</b></col>, <cd>to make a show; to display
one's self.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Show</ent><br/
<hw>Show</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formerly written also
<ets>shew</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of showing, or bringing
to view; exposure to sight; exhibition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is shown, or brought to view; that which
is arranged to be seen; a spectacle; an exhibition; <as>as, a traveling
<ex>show</ex>; a cattle <ex>show</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As for triumphs, masks, feasts, and such <qex>shows</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Proud or ostentatious display; parade;
pomp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I envy none their pageantry and <qex>show</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Semblance; likeness; appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He through the midst unmarked,<br/
In <qex>show</qex> plebeian angel militant<br/
Of lowest order, passed.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>False semblance; deceitful appearance;
pretense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beware of the scribes, . . . which devour widows' houses, and for
a <qex>shew</qex> make long prayers.</q> <rj><qau>Luke xx. 46.
47.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A discharge, from the vagina, of
mucus streaked with blood, occurring a short time before
labor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A pale blue flame, at the top of
a candle flame, indicating the presence of fire damp.</def>
<rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Show bill</b></col>, <cd>a broad sheet containing an
advertisement in large letters.</cd> -- <col><b>Show box</b></col>,
<cd>a box containing some object of curiosity carried round as a
show.</cd> -- <col><b>Show card</b></col>, <cd>an advertising placard;
also, a card for displaying samples.</cd> -- <col><b>Show
case</b></col>, <cd>a glazed case, box, or cabinet for displaying and
protecting shopkeepers' wares, articles on exhibition in museums,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Show glass</b></col>, <cd>a glass which displays
objects; a mirror.</cd> -- <col><b>Show of hands</b></col>, <cd>a
raising of hands to indicate judgment; as, the vote was taken by a
<xex>show of hands</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Show stone</b></col>, <cd>a
piece of glass or crystal supposed to have the property of exhibiting
images of persons or things not present, indicating in that way future
events.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showbread</ent><br/
<hw>Show"bread`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Jewish
Antiq.)</fld> <def>Bread of exhibition; loaves to set before God; -- the
term used in translating the various phrases used in the Hebrew and
Greek to designate the loaves of bread which the priest of the week
placed before the Lord on the golden table in the sanctuary. They were
made of fine flour unleavened, and were changed every Sabbath. The
loaves, twelve in number, represented the twelve tribes of Israel. They
were to be eaten by the priests only, and in the Holy Place.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>shewbread</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Mark ii.
26.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shower</ent><br/
<hw>Show"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
shows or exhibits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which shows; a mirror.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shower</ent><br/
<hw>Show"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shour</ets>,
<ets>schour</ets>, AS. <ets>se<?/r</ets>; akin to D. <ets>schoer</ets>,
G. <ets>schauer</ets>, OHG. <ets>sc<?/r</ets>, Icel. <ets>sk<?/r</ets>,
Sw. <ets>skur</ets>, Goth. <ets>sk<?/ra windis</ets> a storm of wind; of
uncertain origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A fall or rain or hail of
short duration; sometimes, but rarely, a like fall of snow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In drought or else <qex>showers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Or wet the thirsty earth with falling <qex>showers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which resembles a shower in falling or passing
through the air copiously and rapidly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With <qex>showers</qex> of stones he drives them far away.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A copious supply bestowed.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He and myself<br/
Have travail'd in the great <qex>shower</qex> of your gifts.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shower bath</b></col>, <cd>a bath in which water is
showered from above, and sometimes from the sides also.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shower</ent><br/
<hw>Show"er</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Showered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Showering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To water with a
shower; to wet copiously with rain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lest it again dissolve and <qex>shower</qex> the earth.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bestow liberally; to distribute or scatter in
abundance; to rain.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Caesar's favor,<br/
That <qex>showers</qex> down greatness on his friends.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shower</ent><br/
<hw>Show"er</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To rain in showers; to fall, as
in a shower or showers.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showerful</ent><br/
<hw>Show"er*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of
showers.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showeriness</ent><br/
<hw>Show"er*i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of
being showery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showerless</ent><br/
<hw>Show"er*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Rainless; free from
showers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showery</ent><br/
<hw>Show"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Raining
in showers; abounding with frequent showers of rain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a shower or showers.</def>
<ldquo/Colors of the <xex>showery</xex> arch.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showily</ent><br/
<hw>Show"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a showy manner;
pompously; with parade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showiness</ent><br/
<hw>Show"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
showy; pompousness; great parade; ostentation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showing</ent><br/
<hw>Show"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Appearance; display;
exhibition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Presentation of facts; statement.</def> <rj><au>J.
S. Mill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showish</ent><br/
<hw>Show"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Showy; ostentatious.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showman</ent><br/
<hw>Show"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Showmen</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who exhibits a show; a
proprietor of a show.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 1 (b) esp. a producer of an entertainment . 2. One who has a
knack for dramatic or entertaining presentation; an entertainer. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shown</ent><br/
<hw>Shown</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Show</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showroom</ent><br/
<hw>Show"room`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A room or apartment
where a show is exhibited.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A room where merchandise is exposed for sale, or
where samples are displayed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Showy</ent><br/
<hw>Show"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Compar</ets>.
<er>Showier</er> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <ets>superl</ets>.
<er>Showiest</er>.]</ety> <def>Making a show; attracting attention;
presenting a marked appearance; ostentatious; gay; gaudy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A present of everything that was rich and <qex>showy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Splendid; gay; gaudy; gorgeous; fine;
magnificent; grand; stately; sumptuous; pompous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrag</ent><br/
<hw>Shrag</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Scrag</er>.]</ety> <def>A twig of a tree cut off.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrag</ent><br/
<hw>Shrag</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To trim, as trees; to lop.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shragger</ent><br/
<hw>Shrag"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lops; one
who trims trees.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Huloet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shram</ent><br/
<hw>Shram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Shrink.]</ety>
<def>To cause to shrink or shrivel with cold; to benumb.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrank</ent><br/
<hw>Shrank</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Shrink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrape</ent><br/
<ent>Shrap</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Shrap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Shrape</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Scrap</er>, and
<er>Scrape</er>.]</ety> <def>A place baited with chaff to entice
birds.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>scrap</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Bedell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrapnel</ent><br/
<hw>Shrap"nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Applied as an
appellation to a kind of shell invented by Gen. H. <xex>Shrapnel</xex>
of the British army.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A shrapnel shell;
shrapnel shells, collectively.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shrapnel shell</b></col> <fld>(Gunnery)</fld>, <cd>a
projectile for a cannon, consisting of a shell filled with bullets and a
small bursting charge to scatter them at any given point while in
flight. See the Note under <er>Case shot</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shred</ent><br/
<hw>Shred</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shrede</ets>,
<ets>schrede</ets>, AS. <ets>scre<aacute/de</ets>; akin to OD.
<ets>schroode</ets>, G. <ets>schrot</ets> a piece cut off, Icel.
<ets>skrjo<edh/r</ets> a shred, and to E. <ets>shroud</ets>. Cf.
<er>Screed</er>, <er>Scroll</er>, <er>Scrutiny</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A long, narrow piece cut or torn off; a strip.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Shreds</xex> of tanned leather.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In general, a fragment; a piece; a particle.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shred</ent><br/
<hw>Shred</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shred</conjf> or <conjf>Shredded</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Shredding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shreden</ets>, <ets>schreden</ets>, AS.
<ets>scre<aacute/dian</ets>; akin to OD. <ets>schrooden</ets>, OHG.
<ets>scr<?/tan</ets>, G. <ets>schroten</ets>. See <er>Shred</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut or tear into small pieces,
particularly narrow and long pieces, as of cloth or leather.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lop; to prune; to trim.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shredcook</ent><br/
<hw>Shred"cook`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The fieldfare; -- so called from its harsh cry before rain.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shredding</ent><br/
<hw>Shred"ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of cutting or tearing into shreds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is cut or torn off; a piece.</def>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shreddy</ent><br/
<hw>Shred"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of
shreds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shredless</ent><br/
<hw>Shred"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no shreds; without a
shred.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And those which waved are <qex>shredless</qex> dust ere now.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrew</ent><br/
<hw>Shrew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shrewe</ets>,
<ets>schrewe</ets>. Cf. <er>Shrewd</er>.]</ety> <def>Wicked;
malicious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrew</ent><br/
<hw>Shrew</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Shrew</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Originally, a brawling, turbulent,
vexatious person of either sex, but now restricted in use to females; a
brawler; a scold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man . . . grudgeth that <qex>shrews</qex> [i. e., bad men] have
prosperity, or else that good men have adversity.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man had got a <qex>shrew</qex> to his wife, and there could be
no quiet in the house for her.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[AS. <ets>scre<aacute/wa</ets>; -- so called because
supposed to be venomous. ]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any small
insectivore of the genus <gen>Sorex</gen> and several allied genera of
the family <fam>Sorecidae</fam>. In form and color they resemble mice,
but they have a longer and more pointed nose. Some of them are the
smallest of all mammals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common European species are the house shrew
(<spn>Crocidura araneus</spn>), and the erd shrew (<spn>Sorex
vulgaris</spn>) (see under <er>Erd</er>.). In the United States several
species of <gen>Sorex</gen> and <gen>Blarina</gen> are common, as the
broadnosed shrew (<spn>Sorex platyrhinus</spn>), Cooper's shrew
(<spn>Sorex Cooperi</spn>), and the short-tailed, or mole, shrew
(<spn>Blarina brevicauda</spn>). Th American water, or marsh, shrew
(<spn>Neosorex palustris</spn>), with fringed feet, is less common. The
common European water shrews are <spn>Crossopus fodiens</spn>, and the
oared shrew (see under <er>Oared</er>).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Earth shrew</b></col>, <cd>any shrewlike burrowing animal
of the family <fam>Centetidae</fam>, as the tendrac.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Elephant shrew</b></col>, <col><b>Jumping shrew</b></col>,
<col><b>Mole shrew</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Elephant</er>,
<er>Jumping</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Musk shrew</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Desman</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>River shrew</b></col>, <cd>an aquatic
West African insectivore (<spn>Potamogale velox</spn>) resembling a
weasel in form and size, but having a large flattened and crested tail
adapted for rapid swimming. It feeds on fishes.</cd> -- <col><b>Shrew
mole</b></col>, <cd>a common large North American mole (<spn>Scalops
aquaticus</spn>). Its fine, soft fur is gray with iridescent purple
tints.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrew</ent><br/
<hw>Shrew</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Shrew</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Beshrew</er>.]</ety> <def>To beshrew; to
curse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/I <xex>shrew</xex>
myself.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrewd</ent><br/
<hw>Shrewd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shrewder</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shrewdest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Originally the p. p. of
<ets>shrew</ets>, v.t.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Inclining to shrew;
disposing to curse or scold; hence, vicious; malicious; evil; wicked;
mischievous; vexatious; rough; unfair; shrewish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Egypt] hath many <qex>shrewd</qex> havens, because of the great
rocks that ben strong and dangerous to pass by.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Mandeville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every of this happy number<br/
That have endured <qex>shrewd</qex> days and nights with us.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Artful; wily; cunning; arch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These women are <qex>shrewd</qex> tempters with their tongues.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Able or clever in practical affairs; sharp in
business; astute; sharp-witted; sagacious; keen; <as>as, a
<ex>shrewd</ex> observer; a <ex>shrewd</ex> design; a <ex>shrewd</ex>
reply.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Professing to despise the ill opinion of mankind creates a
<qex>shrewd</qex> suspicion that we have deserved it.</q>
<rj><qau>Secker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Keen; critical; subtle; artful; astute;
sagacious; discerning; acute; penetrating.</syn> --
<usage><er>Shrewd</er>, <er>Sagacious</er>. One who is shrewd is keen to
detect errors, to penetrate disguises, to foresee and guard against the
selfishness of others. <xex>Shrewd</xex> is a word of less dignity than
<xex>sagacious</xex>, which implies a comprehensive as well as
penetrating mind, whereas <xex>shrewd</xex> does not.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Shrewd"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Shrewd"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrewish</ent><br/
<hw>Shrew"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>having the qualities
of a shrew; having a scolding disposition; froward; peevish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My wife is <qex>shrewish</qex> when I keep not hours.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Shrew"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Shrew"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrewmouse</ent><br/
<hw>Shrew"mouse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A shrew; especially, the erd shrew.</def> <-- Sorex vulgaris
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shriek</ent><br/
<hw>Shriek</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shrieked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shrieking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shriken</ets>, originallythe same word as E. <ets>screech</ets>.
See <er>Screech</er>, and cf. <er>Screak</er>.]</ety> <def>To utter a
loud, sharp, shrill sound or cry, as do some birds and beasts; to
scream, as in a sudden fright, in horror or anguish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was the owl that <qex>shrieked</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At this she <qex>shrieked</qex> aloud; the mournful train<br/
Echoed her grief.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shriek</ent><br/
<hw>Shriek</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To utter sharply and
shrilly; to utter in or with a shriek or shrieks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On top whereof aye dwelt the ghostly owl,<br/
<qex>Shrieking</qex> his baleful note.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She <qex>shrieked</qex> his name<br/
To the dark woods.</q> <rj><qau>Moore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shriek</ent><br/
<hw>Shriek</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sharp, shrill outcry or scream; a
shrill wild cry such as is caused by sudden or extreme terror, pain, or
the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Shrieks</qex>, clamors, murmurs, fill the frighted town.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shriek owl</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>The screech owl</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The swift; -- so called from
its cry.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrieker</ent><br/
<hw>Shriek"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who utters a
shriek.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrieval</ent><br/
<hw>Shriev"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
a sheriff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrievalty</ent><br/
<hw>Shriev"al*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contr. from
<ets>sheriffalty</ets>. See <er>Shrieve</er>, <pos>n.</pos>
<er>Sheriff</er>.]</ety> <def>The office, or sphere of jurisdiction, of
a sheriff; sheriffalty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was ordained by 28 Edward I that the people shall have election
of sheriff in every shire where the <qex>shrievalty</qex> is not of
inheritance.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrieve</ent><br/
<hw>Shrieve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contr. from OE.
<ets>shereve</ets>. See <er>Sheriff</er>.]</ety> <def>A sheriff.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrieve</ent><br/
<hw>Shrieve</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To shrive; to question.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/She gan him soft to
<xex>shrieve</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrift</ent><br/
<hw>Shrift</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shrift</ets>,
<ets>schrift</ets>, AS. <ets>scrift</ets>, fr. <ets>scr<imac/fan</ets>
to shrive. See <er>Shrive</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
shriving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>shrift</qex> and preaching is my diligence.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Confession made to a priest, and the absolution
consequent upon it.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Have you got leave to go to <qex>shrift</qex> to-day?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Therefore, my lord, address you to your <qex>shrift</qex>,<br/
And be yourself; for you must die this instant.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shrift father</b></col>, <cd>a priest to whom confession
is made.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shright</ent><br/
<hw>Shright</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> of <er>Shriek</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She cried alway and <qex>shright</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shright</ent><br/
<hw>Shright</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Shriek</er>.]</ety> <def>A
shriek; shrieking.</def> <mark>[Obs]</mark> <au>Spenser.</au> <ldquo/All
hoarse for <xex>shright</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrike</ent><br/
<hw>Shrike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to Icel.
<ets>skr<imac/kja</ets> a shrieker, the shrike, and E.
<ets>shriek</ets>; cf. AS. <ets>scr<imac/c</ets> a thrush. See
<er>Shriek</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one
of numerous species of oscinine birds of the family <fam>Laniidae</fam>,
having a strong hooked bill, toothed at the tip. Most shrikes are
insectivorous, but the common European gray shrike (<spn>Lanius
excubitor</spn>), the great northern shrike (<spn>Lanius
borealis</spn>), and several others, kill mice, small birds, etc., and
often impale them on thorns, and are, on that account called also
<altname>butcher birds</altname>. See under <er>Butcher</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The ant shrikes, or bush shrikes, are clamatorial birds
of the family <fam>Formicaridae</fam>. The cuckoo shrikes of the East
Indies and Australia are Oscines of the family <fam>Campephagidae</fam>.
The drongo shrikes of the same regions belong to the related family
<fam>Dicruridae</fam>. See <er>Drongo</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Crow shrike</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Crow</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Shrike thrush</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any one of several
species of Asiatic timaline birds of the genera
<gen>Thamnocataphus</gen>, <gen>Gampsorhynchus</gen>, and allies</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Any one of several species of shrikelike Australian
singing birds of the genus <gen>Colluricincla</gen>.</cd> --
<col><b>Shrike tit</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any one of several
Australian birds of the genus <gen>Falcunculus</gen>, having a strong
toothed bill and sharp claws. They creep over the bark of trees, like
titmice, in search of insects.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Any one of several
species of small Asiatic birds belonging to <gen>Allotrius</gen>,
<gen>Pteruthius</gen>, <gen>Cutia</gen>, <gen>Leioptila</gen>, and
allied genera, related to the true tits. Called also <altname>hill
tit</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Swallow shrike</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Swallow</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrill</ent><br/
<hw>Shrill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Shriller</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shrillest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shril</ets>,
<ets>schril</ets>; akin to LG. <ets>schrell</ets>, G.
<ets>schrill</ets>. See <er>Shrill</er>,<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Acute; sharp; piercing; having or emitting a sharp, piercing tone
or sound; -- said of a sound, or of that which produces a
sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hear the <qex>shrill</qex> whistle which doth order give<br/
To sounds confused.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let winds be <qex>shrill</qex>, let waves roll high.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrill</ent><br/
<hw>Shrill</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shrill sound.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrill</ent><br/
<hw>Shrill</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shrilled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shrilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>schrillen</ets>, akin
to G. <ets>schrillen</ets>; cf. AS. <ets>scralletan</ets> to resound
loudly, Icel. <ets>skr<oum/lta</ets> to jolt, Sw. <ets>skr<aum/lla</ets>
to shrill, Norw. <ets>skryla</ets>, <ets>skr<?/la</ets>. Cf.
<er>Skirl</er>.]</ety> <def>To utter an acute, piercing sound; to sound
with a sharp, shrill tone; to become shrill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Break we our pipes, that <qex>shrilled</qex>loud as lark.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No sounds were heard but of the <qex>shrilling</qex> cock.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His voice <qex>shrilled</qex> with passion.</q> <rj><qau>L.
Wallace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrill</ent><br/
<hw>Shrill</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To utter or express in a shrill
tone; to cause to make a shrill sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How poor Andromache <qex>shrills</qex> her dolors forth.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrill-gorged</ent><br/
<hw>Shrill"-gorged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
throat which produces a shrill note.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrillness</ent><br/
<hw>Shrill"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
shrill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrill-tongued</ent><br/
<hw>Shrill"-tongued`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
shrill voice.</def> <ldquo/When <xex>shrill-tongued</xex> Fulvia
scolds.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrilly</ent><br/
<hw>Shril"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shrill manner; acutely;
with a sharp sound or voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrilly</ent><br/
<hw>Shril"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat shrill.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some kept up a <qex>shrilly</qex> mellow sound.</q>
<rj><qau>Keats.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrimp</ent><br/
<hw>Shrimp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS.
<ets>scrimman</ets> to dry up, wither, MHG. <ets>schrimpfen</ets> to
shrink, G. <ets>schrumpfen</ets>, Dan. <ets>skrumpe</ets>,
<ets>skrumpes</ets>, Da. & Sw. <ets>skrumpen</ets> shriveled. Cf.
<er>Scrimp</er>, <er>Shrink</er>, <er>Shrivel</er>.]</ety> <def>To
contract; to shrink.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrimp</ent><br/
<hw>Shrimp</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>shrimp</ets>; -- probably
so named from its shriveled appearance. See <er>Shrimp</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any one of numerous species of macruran Crustacea belonging to
<gen>Crangon</gen> and various allied genera, having a slender body and
long legs. Many of them are used as food. The larger kinds are called
also <stype>prawns</stype>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In a more general
sense, any species of the macruran tribe <fam>Caridea</fam>, or any
species of the order Schizopoda, having a similar form.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>In a loose sense, any small crustacean, including some
amphipods and even certain entomostracans; <as>as, the fairy
<ex>shrimp</ex>, and brine <ex>shrimp</ex></as>. See under
<er>Fairy</er>, and <er>Brine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1335 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively, a little wrinkled man; a dwarf; -- in
contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This weak and writhled <qex>shrimp</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Opossum shrimp</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Opossum</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Spector shrimp</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Skeleton shrimp</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any slender amphipod crustacean of the genus <gen>Caprella</gen> and
allied genera.</cd> -- <col><b>Shrimp catcher</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the little tern (<spn>Sterna minuta</spn>).</cd>
-- <col><b>Shrimp net</b></col>, <cd>a dredge net fixed upon a pole, or
a sweep net dragged over the fishing ground.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrimper</ent><br/
<hw>Shrimp"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who fishes for
shrimps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrine</ent><br/
<hw>Shrine</hw> <pr>(shr<imac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schrin</ets>, AS. <ets>scr<imac/n</ets>, from L.
<ets>scrinium</ets> a case, chest, box.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A case,
box, or receptacle, especially one in which are deposited sacred relics,
as the bones of a saint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any sacred place, as an altar, tomb, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Too weak the sacred <qex>shrine</qex> guard.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A place or object hallowed from its history or
associations; <as>as, a <ex>shrine</ex> of art</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Short for <col><b>Ancient Arabic Order of Nobles of
the Mystic Shrine</b></col>, a secret fraternal organization professedly
originated by one <person>Kalif Alu</person>, a son-in-law of Mohammed,
at Mecca, in the year of the Hegira 25 (about 646 <sc>a. d.</sc>) In the
modern order, established in the United States in 1872, only Knights
Templars or thirty-second degree Masons are eligible for admission,
though the order itself is not Masonic. A member of the order is
popularly called a <member>Shriner</member>, and the order itself is
sometimes called the <altname>Shriners</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrine</ent><br/
<hw>Shrine</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To enshrine; to place reverently,
as in a shrine.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Shrined</xex> in his sanctuary.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shriner</ent><br/
<hw>Shrin"er</hw> <pr>(shr<imac/n"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
member of the <membof>Ancient Arabic Order of Nobles of the Mystic
Shrine</membof>. See <er>shrine{4}</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrink</ent><br/
<hw>Shrink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Shrank</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Shrunk</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>
<pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shrunk</conjf> or <conjf>Shrunken</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>, but the latter is now seldom used except as a
participial adjective; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shrinking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shrinken</ets>,
<ets>schrinken</ets>, AS. <ets>scrincan</ets>; akin to OD.
<ets>schrincken</ets>, and probably to Sw. <ets>skrynka</ets> a wrinkle,
<ets>skrynkla</ets> to wrinkle, to rumple, and E. <ets>shrimp</ets>, n.
& v., <ets>scrimp</ets>. CF. <er>Shrimp</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
wrinkle, bend, or curl; to shrivel; hence, to contract into a less
extent or compass; to gather together; to become compacted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And on a broken reed he still did stay<br/
His feeble steps, which <qex>shrunk</qex> when hard thereon he lay.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have not found that water, by mixture of ashes, will
<qex>shrink</qex> or draw into less room.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Against this fire do I <qex>shrink</qex> up.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>shrink</qex> like parchment in consuming fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the boards did <qex>shrink</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To withdraw or retire, as from danger; to decline
action from fear; to recoil, as in fear, horror, or distress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What happier natures <qex>shrink</qex> at with affright,<br/
The hard inhabitant contends is right.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They assisted us against the Thebans when you <qex>shrank</qex>
from the task.</q> <rj><qau>Jowett (Thucyd.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To express fear, horror, or pain by contracting the
body, or part of it; to shudder; to quake.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrink</ent><br/
<hw>Shrink</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to contract
or shrink; <as>as, to <ex>shrink</ex> finnel by immersing it in boiling
water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw back; to withdraw.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Libyc Hammon <qex>shrinks</qex> his horn.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shrink on</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>to fix
(one piece or part) firmly around (another) by natural contraction in
cooling, as a tire on a wheel, or a hoop upon a cannon, which is made
slightly smaller than the part it is to fit, and expanded by heat till
it can be slipped into place.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrink</ent><br/
<hw>Shrink</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act shrinking;
shrinkage; contraction; also, recoil; withdrawal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet almost wish, with sudden <qex>shrink</qex>,<br/
That I had less to praise.</q> <rj><qau>Leigh Hunt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Contraction of head-<ets>shrink</ets>er, a
colloquial term for psychiatrist.]</ety> <def>a psychiatrist.</def>
<mark>[Coll.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrinkage</ent><br/
<hw>Shrink"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of shrinking; a contraction into less bulk or measurement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The amount of such contraction; the bulk or
dimension lost by shrinking, as of grain, castings, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Decrease in value; depreciation.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrinker</ent><br/
<hw>Shrink"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who shrinks; one
who withdraws from danger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrinking</ent><br/
<hw>Shrink"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Shrink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shrinking head</b></col> <fld>(Founding)</fld>, <cd>a
body of molten metal connected with a mold for the purpose of supplying
metal to compensate for the shrinkage of the casting; -- called also
<altname>sinking head</altname>, and
<altname>riser</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrinkingly</ent><br/
<hw>Shrink"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shrinking
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shrinkpack</ent><br/
<ent>shrink-pack</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>shrink"-pack</hw>, <hw>shrink"pack</hw></mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>same as <er>shrink-wrap</er>, <pos>n.</pos>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shrinkwrap</ent><br/
<ent>shrink-wrap</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>shrink"-wrap</hw>, <hw>shrink"wrap</hw></mhw>
<pr>(shr<icr/<nsm/k"r<acr/p)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>shrink-wrapped</conjf> or
<conjf>shrink"wrapped</conjf> <pr>(shr<icr/<nsm/k"r<acr/pt)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>shrink"wrapping</conjf> or
<conjf>shrink"-wrapping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>to wrap and seal (an
object for sale) in a transparent sheet of plastic material that is
shrunken to a tight-fitting wrapping by the application of
heat.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shrinkwrap</ent><br/
<ent>shrink-wrap</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>shrink"-wrap</hw>, <hw>shrink"wrap</hw></mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>a type of plastic film, usually transparent, that shrinks upon
application of heat, and may be used to form a wrapping around objects
that fits tightly and closely follows the contours of the wrapped
object; -- also called <altname>shrink-pack</altname>,
<altname>shrinkpack</altname> or
<altname>shrink-wrapping</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrivalty</ent><br/
<hw>Shriv"al*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Shrievalty.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrive</ent><br/
<hw>Shrive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Shrived</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Shrove</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shriven</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or
<conjf>Shrived</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shriving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shriven</ets>,
<ets>schriven</ets>, AS. <ets>scr<imac/van</ets> to shrive, to impose
penance or punishment; akin to OFries. <ets>skr<imac/va</ets> to impose
punishment; cf. OS. bi<ets>skr<imac/ban</ets> to be troubled. Cf.
<er>Shrift</er>, <er>Shrovetide</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To hear or
receive the confession of; to administer confession and absolution to;
-- said of a priest as the agent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That they should <qex>shrive</qex> their parishioners.</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Doubtless he <qex>shrives</qex> this woman, . . . <br/
Else ne'er could he so long protract his speech.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till my guilty soul be <qex>shriven</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To confess, and receive absolution; -- used
reflexively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Get you to the church and <qex>shrive</qex> yourself.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrive</ent><br/
<hw>Shrive</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To receive confessions, as a
priest; to administer confession and absolution.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrivel</ent><br/
<hw>Shriv"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shriveled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or
<conjf>Shrivelled</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shriveling</conjf> or <conjf>Shrivelling</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Probably akin to <ets>shrimp</ets>, <ets>shrink</ets>; cf. dial.
AS. <ets>screpa</ets> to pine away, Norw. <ets>skrypa</ets> to waste,
<ets>skryp</ets>, <ets>skryv</ets>, transitory, frail, Sw.
<ets>skr<oum/pling</ets> feeble, Dan. <ets>skr<oum/belig</ets>, Icel.
<ets>skrj<?/pr</ets> brittle, frail.]</ety> <def>To draw, or be drawn,
into wrinkles; to shrink, and form corrugations; <as>as, a leaf
<ex>shrivels</ex> in the hot sun; the skin <ex>shrivels</ex> with
age</as>; -- often with <xex>up</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrivel</ent><br/
<hw>Shriv"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to
shrivel or contract; to cause to shrink onto corruptions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shriven</ent><br/
<hw>Shriv"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Shrive</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shriver</ent><br/
<hw>Shriv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who shrives; a
confessor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shriving</ent><br/
<hw>Shriv"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Shrift; confession.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroff</ent><br/
<hw>Shroff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sarr<amac/f</ets>.]</ety> <def>A banker, or changer of money.</def>
<mark>[East Indies]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroffage</ent><br/
<hw>Shroff"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A money dealer's
commission; also, more commonly, the examination of coins, and the
separation of the good from the debased.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrood</ent><br/
<hw>Shrood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Shroud</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>shroud</asp>, and
<asp>shrowd</asp>.]</altsp> <def>To trim; to lop.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shropshire</ent><br/
<hw>Shrop"shire</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<etsep>Shropshire</etsep>, country of England.]</ety> <def>An English
breed of black-faced hornless sheep similar to the Southdown, but
larger, now extensively raised in many parts of the world.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroud</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud</hw> <pr>(shroud)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shroud</ets>, <ets>shrud</ets>, <ets>schrud</ets>, AS.
<ets>scr<umac/d</ets> a garment, clothing; akin to Icel.
<ets>skru<edh/</ets> the shrouds of a ship, furniture of a church, a
kind of stuff, Sw. <ets>skrud</ets> dress, attire, and E.
<ets>shred</ets>. See <er>Shred</er>, and cf. <er>Shrood</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>That which clothes, covers, conceals, or protects; a
garment.</def> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Swaddled, as new born, in sable <qex>shrouds</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, the dress for the dead; a winding
sheet.</def> <ldquo/A dead man in his <xex>shroud</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which covers or shelters like a
shroud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jura answers through her misty <qex>shroud</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A covered place used as a retreat or shelter, as a
cave or den; also, a vault or crypt.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shroud</qex> to which he won<br/
His fair-eyed oxen.</q> <rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A vault, or <qex>shroud</qex>, as under a church.</q>
<rj><qau>Withals.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The branching top of a tree; foliage.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Assyrian wad a cedar in Lebanon, with fair branches and with a
shadowing <qex>shroad</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ezek. xxxi. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A set of ropes
serving as stays to support the masts. The lower shrouds are secured to
the sides of vessels by heavy iron bolts and are passed around the head
of the lower masts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>One of the two annular plates at
the periphery of a water wheel, which form the sides of the buckets; a
shroud plate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bowsprit shrouds</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>ropes
extending from the head of the bowsprit to the sides of the vessel.</cd>
-- <col><b>Futtock shrouds</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>iron rods
connecting the topmast rigging with the lower rigging, passing over the
edge of the top.</cd> -- <col><b>Shroud plate</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>An iron plate extending from the dead-eyes to the
ship's side</cd>. <au>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</au> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld> <cd>A shroud. See def. 7, above.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroud</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shrouded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shrouding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>scr<?/dan</ets>.
See <er>Shroud</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover with
a shroud; especially, to inclose in a winding sheet; to dress for the
grave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ancient Egyptian mummies were <qex>shrouded</qex> in a number
of folds of linen besmeared with gums.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover, as with a shroud; to protect completely;
to cover so as to conceal; to hide; to veil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One of these trees, with all his young ones, may <qex>shroud</qex>
four hundred horsemen.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some tempest rise,<br/
And blow out all the stars that light the skies,<br/
To <qex>shroud</qex> my shame.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroud</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To take shelter or harbor.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If your stray attendance be yet lodged,<br/
Or <qex>shroud</qex> within these limits.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroud</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To lop. See
<er>Shrood</er>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrouded</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Provided with a shroud or
shrouds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shrouded gear</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a
cogwheel or pinion having flanges which form closed ends to the spaces
between the teeth and thus strengthen the teeth by tying them
together.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrouding</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The shrouds. See
<er>Shroud</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 7.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroud-laid</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud"-laid`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Composed of four
strands, and laid right-handed with a heart, or center; -- said of
rope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroudless</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a shroud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroudy</ent><br/
<hw>Shroud"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affording
shelter.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrove</ent><br/
<hw>Shrove</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Shrive</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shrove Sunday</b></col>, <cd>Quinguagesima Sunday.</cd>
-- <col><b>Shrove Tuesday</b></col>, <cd>the Tuesday following
Quinguagesima Sunday, and preceding the first day of Lent, or Ash
Wednesday.</cd> <note>It was formerly customary in England, on this day,
for the people to confess their sins to their parish priests, after
which they dined on pancakes, or fritters, and the occasion became one
of merriment. The bell rung on this day is popularly called <xex>Pancake
Bell</xex>, and the day itself <xex>Pancake Tuesday</xex>. <au>P.
Cyc.</au></note></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrove</ent><br/
<hw>Shrove</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To join in the festivities of
Shrovetide; hence, to make merry.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>J.
Fletcher.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrovetide</ent><br/
<hw>Shrove"tide`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>shrive</ets> to take a confession (OE. imp. <ets>shrof</ets>, AS.
<ets>scr<amac/f</ets>) + <ets>tide</ets>.]</ety> <def>The days
immediately preceding Ash Wednesday, especially the period between the
evening before Quinguagesima Sunday and the morning of Ash
Wednesday.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shroving</ent><br/
<hw>Shrov"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The festivity of
Shrovetide.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrow</ent><br/
<hw>Shrow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shrew.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrowd</ent><br/
<hw>Shrowd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shrood</er>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrub</ent><br/
<hw>Shrub</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar. <ets>shirb</ets>,
<ets>shurb</ets>, a drink, beverage, fr. <ets>shariba</ets> to drink.
Cf. <er>Sirup</er>, <er>Sherbet</er>.]</ety> <def>A liquor composed of
vegetable acid, especially lemon juice, and sugar, with spirit to
preserve it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrub</ent><br/
<hw>Shrub</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>schrob</ets>, AS.
<ets>scrob</ets>, <ets>scrobb</ets>; akin to Norw. <ets>skrubba</ets>
the dwarf cornel tree.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A woody plant of
less size than a tree, and usually with several stems from the same
root.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrub</ent><br/
<hw>Shrub</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To lop; to prune.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Anderson (1573).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrubbery</ent><br/
<hw>Shrub"ber*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Shrubberies</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
collection of shrubs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place where shrubs are planted.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrubbiness</ent><br/
<hw>Shrub"bi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
shrubby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrubby</ent><br/
<hw>Shrub"by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Shrubbier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Shrubbiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Full of shrubs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of the nature of a shrub; resembling a shrub.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Shrubby</xex> browse.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J.
Philips.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrubless</ent><br/
<hw>Shrub"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>having no shrubs.</def>
<rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shruff</ent><br/
<hw>Shruff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Scruff</er>,
<er>Scurf</er>.]</ety> <def>Rubbish.</def>
<specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Dross or refuse of
metals.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Light, dry wood, or
stuff used for fuel.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrug</ent><br/
<hw>Shrug</hw> <pr>(shr<ucr/g)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Shrugged</conjf> <pr>(shr<ucr/gd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Shrugging</conjf>
<pr>(shr<ucr/g"g<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably akin to
<ets>shrink</ets>, p. p. <ets>shrunk</ets>; cf. Dan. <ets>skrugge</ets>,
<ets>skrukke</ets>, to stoop, dial. Sw. <ets>skrukka</ets>,
<ets>skruga</ets>, to crouch.]</ety> <def>To draw up or contract (the
shoulders), especially by way of expressing doubt, indifference,
dislike, dread, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>shrugs</qex> his shoulders when you talk of
securities.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrug</ent><br/
<hw>Shrug</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To raise or draw up the shoulders,
as in expressing doubt, indifference, dislike, dread, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They grin, they <qex>shrug</qex>.<br/
They bow, they snarl, they snatch, they hug.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shrug</ent><br/
<hw>shrug</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A gesture consisting of drawing up
the shoulders, -- a motion usually expressing doubt, indifference, or
dislike; -- it is sometimes accompanied by a slight turning of the hands
outward or upward. Such a gesture may be made, as in answering "who
knows" to a question, suggesting utter ignorance of an answer and a
disinclination to pursue the topic further.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>On Sept. 23, in a major speech in New York, the chairman of the
<org>Securities and Exchange Commision</org>, <person>Arthur
Levitt</person> asked the <org>Big Board</org> to spike the rule [Rule
390] in the interest of free and unfettered markets. . . . <person>Mr.
Grasso</person> responded with a <qex>shrug</qex>, saying that he had no
plans to kill the rule.</q> <rj><qau>Gretchen Morgenson (N. Y. Times
Nov. 28, 1999 sect. 3 p. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Spaniards talk in dialogues<br/
Of heads and shoulders, nods and <qex>shrugs</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shrug off</ent><br/
<hw>shrug off</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To ignore; to disregard; to
brush aside; to minimize{2} the effects of; <as>as, to <ex>shrug
off</ex> predictions of disaster</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shrunken</ent><br/
<hw>Shrunk"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p. & a.</pos> from
<er>Shrink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shtetl</ent><br/
<hw>shtetl</hw> <pr>(sht<ecr/t"'l)</pr>; <plu><it>pl.</it> Eng.
<plw>shtetls</plw> <pr>(sht<ecr/t"'lz)</pr>, Yiddish
<plw>shtetlach</plw> <pr>(sht<ecr/t"l<aum/kh)</pr>.</plu>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Yiddish, from MHG <ets>stetel</ets>, dim. of <ets>stat</ets>,
place, town.]</ety> <def>A village or small town; -- usually referring
to Jewish towns in Eastern Europe.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>shtik</ent><br/
<ent>shtick</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>shtick</hw>, <hw>shtik</hw></mhw> <pr>(sht<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Yiddish, pranks; fr. MHG <ets>st<uum/cke</ets>,
pieces.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A person's special talent, line of
business, or habitual activity.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Show business)</fld> <def>A comic routine or a
specific gag inserted in a show for laughs.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuck</ent><br/
<hw>Shuck</hw> <pr>(sh<ucr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shock of
grain.</def> <mark>[Prev. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuck</ent><br/
<hw>Shuck</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps akin to G. <ets>shote</ets>
a husk, pod, shell.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A shell, husk, or pod;
especially, the outer covering of such nuts as the hickory nut,
butternut, peanut, and chestnut.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The shell of an oyster or clam.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuck</ent><br/
<hw>Shuck</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shucked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shucking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To deprive of the
shucks or husks; <as>as, to <ex>shuck</ex> walnuts, Indian corn,
oysters, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To remove or take off (shucks); hence, to discard;
to lay aside; -- usually with <ptcl>off</ptcl>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></p>

<p><q><ldquo/<qex>Shucking</qex><rdquo/ his coronet, after he had
imbibed several draughts of fire water.</q> <rj><qau>F. A.
Ober.</qau></rj></p>

<p><q>He had only been in Africa long enough to <qex>shuck off</qex> the
notions he had acquired about the engineering of a west coast
colony.</q> <rj><qau>Pall Mall Mag.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shucker</ent><br/
<hw>Shuck"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who shucks
oysters or clams</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shudder</ent><br/
<hw>Shud"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shuddered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;<pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shuddering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>shoderen</ets>, <ets>schuderen</ets>; akin to LG.
<ets>schuddern</ets>, D. <ets>schudden</ets> to shake, OS.
<ets>skuddian</ets>, G. <ets>schaudern</ets> to shudder,
<ets>sch<uum/tteln</ets> to shake, <ets>sch<uum/tten</ets> to pour, to
shed, OHG. <ets>scutten</ets>, <ets>scuten</ets>, to shake.]</ety>
<def>To tremble or shake with fear, horror, or aversion; to shiver with
cold; to quake.</def> <ldquo/With <xex>shuddering</xex> horror
pale.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>shuddering</qex> tennant of the frigid zone.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shudder</ent><br/
<hw>Shud"der</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of shuddering, as with
fear.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shudderingly</ent><br/
<hw>Shud"der*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shuddering
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shude</ent><br/
<hw>Shude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The husks and other
refuse of rice mills, used to adulterate oil cake, or linseed
cake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuffle</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shuffled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Shuffling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Originally the same word as <ets>scuffle</ets>, and properly a
freq. of <ets>shove</ets>. See <er>Shove</er>, and
<er>Scuffle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shove one way and the
other; to push from one to another; <as>as, to <ex>shuffle</ex> money
from hand to hand</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mix by pushing or shoving; to confuse; to throw
into disorder; especially, to change the relative positions of, as of
the cards in a pack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man may <qex>shuffle</qex> cards or rattle dice from noon to
midnight without tracing a new idea in his mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Rombler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To remove or introduce by artificial
confusion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was contrived by your enemies, and <qex>shuffled</qex> into the
papers that were seizen.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shuffe off</b></col>, <cd>to push off; to rid one's
self of.</cd> -- <col><b>To shuffe up</b></col>, <cd>to throw together
in hastel to make up or form in confusion or with fraudulent disorder;
<as>as, he <ex>shuffled up</ex> a peace</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuffle</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To change the
relative position of cards in a pack; <as>as, to <ex>shuffle</ex> and
cut</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To change one's position; to shift ground; to evade
questions; to resort to equivocation; to prevaricate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I myself, . . . hiding mine honor in my necessity, am fain to
<qex>shuffle</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To use arts or expedients; to make shift.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your life, good master,<br/
Must <qex>shuffle</qex> for itself.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To move in a slovenly, dragging manner; to drag or
scrape the feet in walking or dancing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The aged creature came<br/
<qex>Shuffling</qex> along with ivory-headed wand.</q>
<rj><qau>Keats.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To equivicate; prevaricate; quibble; cavil;
shift; sophisticate; juggle.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuffle</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of shuffling;
a mixing confusedly; a slovenly, dragging motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The unguided agitation and rude <qex>shuffles</qex> of matter.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A trick; an artifice; an evasion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The gifts of nature are beyond all shame and
<qex>shuffles</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuffleboard</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fle*board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shovelboard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shufflecap</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fle*cap`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A play performed
by shaking money in a hat or cap.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuffler</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
shuffles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Either one of the three common
American scaup ducks. See <xex>Scaup duck</xex>, under
<er>Scaup</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shufflewing</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fle*wing`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The hedge sparrow.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuffling</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Moving
with a dragging, scraping step.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>shuffling</xex>
nag.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Evasive; <as>as, a <ex>shuffling</ex>
excuse</as>.</def> <rj><au>T. Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuffling</ent><br/
<hw>Shuf"fling</hw>, <pos>v.</pos> <def>In a shuffling manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shug</ent><br/
<hw>Shug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Shrug</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To writhe the body so as to
produce friction against one's clothes, as do those who have the
itch.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to crawl; to sneak.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There I 'll <qex>shug</qex> in and get a noble countenance.</q>
<rj><qau>Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shumac</ent><br/
<hw>Shu"mac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Sumac.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shun</ent><br/
<hw>Shun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shunned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shunning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shunien</ets>,
<ets>schunien</ets>, <ets>schonien</ets>, AS. <ets>scunian</ets>,
<ets>sceonian</ets>; cf. D. <ets>schuinen</ets> to slepe,
<ets>schuin</ets> oblique, sloping, Icel. <ets>skunda</ets>,
<ets>skynda</ets>, to hasten. Cf. <er>Schooner</er>, <er>Scoundrel</er>,
<er>Shunt</er>.]</ety> <def>To avoid; to keep clear of; to get out of
the way of; to escape from; to eschew; <as>as, to <ex>shun</ex> rocks,
shoals, vice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am pure from the blood of all men. For I have not shunned to
declare unto you all the counsel of God.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xx.
26,27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Scarcity and want shall <qex>shun</qex> you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Avoid</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shunless</ent><br/
<hw>Shun"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not to be shunned; inevitable;
unavoidable.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Shunless</xex>
destiny.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1336 --></p>

<p><ent>Shunt</ent><br/
<hw>Shunt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shunted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shunting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Prov. E., to move from, to put
off, fr. OE. <ets>shunten</ets>, <ets>schunten</ets>,
<ets>schounten</ets>; cf. D. <ets>schuinte</ets> a slant, slope, Icel.
<ets>skunda</ets> to hasten. Cf. <er>Shun</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To shun; to move from.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to move suddenly; to give a sudden start
to; to shove.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To turn off to one side; especially, to turn off, as
a grain or a car upon a side track; to switch off; to shift.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For <qex>shunting</qex> your late partner on to me.</q>
<rj><qau>T. Hughes.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>To provide with a shunt; <as>as,
to <ex>shunt</ex> a galvanometer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shunt</ent><br/
<hw>Shunt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To go aside; to turn
off.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shunt</ent><br/
<hw>Shunt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>schuinte</ets> slant,
slope, declivity. See <er>Shunt</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Railroad)</fld> <def>A turning off to a side or short
track, that the principal track may be left free.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A conducting circuit joining two
points in a conductor, or the terminals of a galvanometer or dynamo, so
as to form a parallel or derived circuit through which a portion of the
current may pass, for the purpose of regulating the amount passing in
the main circuit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gunnery)</fld> <def>The shifting of the studs on a
projectile from the deep to the shallow sides of the grooves in its
discharge from a shunt gun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shunt dynamo</b></col> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>, <cd>a dynamo
in which the field circuit is connected with the main circuit so as to
form a shunt to the letter, thus employing a portion of the current from
the armature to maintain the field.</cd> -- <col><b>Shunt gun</b></col>,
<cd>a firearm having shunt rifling. See under
<er>Rifling</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shunter</ent><br/
<hw>Shunt"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Railroad)</fld>
<def>A person employed to shunt cars from one track to
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shunting</ent><br/
<hw>Shunt"ing</hw>, <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Shunt</er>.</mord> <specif>Specif.:</specif> <pos>vb. n.</pos>
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Railroads)</fld> <def>Switching; <as>as,
<ex>shunting</ex> engine, yard, etc.</as></def> <mark>[British]</mark>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Finance)</fld> <def>Arbitrage conducted between
certain local markets without the necessity of the exchange involved in
foreign arbitrage.</def> <mark>[Great Britain]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shunt valve</ent><br/
<hw>Shunt valve</hw>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A valve permitting a fluid
under pressure an easier avenue of escape than normally; specif., a
valve, actuated by the governor, used in one system of marine-engine
governing to connect both ends of the low-pressure cylinder as a
supplementary control.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shunt winding</ent><br/
<hw>Shunt winding</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A winding so arranged as
to divide the armature current and lead a portion of it around the
field-magnet coils; -- opposed to <contr>series winding</contr>.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Shunt"-wound`</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shut</ent><br/
<hw>Shut</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Shut</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shutting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>shutten</ets>,
<ets>schutten</ets>, <ets>shetten</ets>, <ets>schitten</ets>, AS.
<ets>scyttan</ets> to shut or lock up (akin to D. <ets>schutten</ets>,
G. <ets>sch<uum/tzen</ets> to protect), properly, to fasten with a bolt
or bar <ets>shot</ets> across, fr. AS. <ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets> to
shoot. <root/159. See <er>Shoot</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To close
so as to hinder ingress or egress; <as>as, to <ex>shut</ex> a door or a
gate; to <ex>shut</ex> one's eyes or mouth.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To forbid entrance into; to prohibit; to bar;
<as>as, to <ex>shut</ex> the ports of a country by a
blockade</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shall that be <qex>shut</qex> to man which to the beast<br/
Is open?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To preclude; to exclude; to bar out.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Shut</xex> from every shore.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To fold together; to close over, as the fingers; to
close by bringing the parts together; <as>as, to <ex>shut</ex> the hand;
to <ex>shut</ex> a book.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To shut in</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To inclose; to
confine</cd>. <ldquo/The Lord <xex>shut</xex> him <xex>in</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Cen. vii. 16.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To cover or intercept the view
of; as, one point <xex>shuts in</xex> another.</cd> -- <col><b>To shut
off</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To exclude</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
prevent the passage of, as steam through a pipe, or water through a
flume, by closing a cock, valve, or gate.</cd> -- <col><b>To shut
out</b></col>, <cd>to preclude from entering; to deny admission to; to
exclude; <as>as, <ex>to shut out</ex> rain by a tight roof</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>To shut together</b></col>, <cd>to unite; to close, especially
to close by welding.</cd> -- <col><b>To shut up</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To close; to make fast the entrances into; <as>as, <ex>to shut
up</ex> a house</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To obstruct</cd>.
<ldquo/Dangerous rocks <xex>shut up</xex> the passage.<rdquo/ <au>Sir W.
Raleigh.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To inclose; to confine; to imprison; to
fasten in; <as>as, <ex>to shut up</ex> a prisoner</as>.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before faith came, we were kept under the law, <qex>shut up</qex>
unto the faith which should afterwards be revealed.</q> <rj><qau>Gal.
iii. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To end; to terminate; to conclude</cd>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When the scene of life is <qex>shut up</qex>, the slave will be
above his master if he has acted better.</q>
<rj><qau>Collier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <cd>To unite, as two pieces of metal by welding.</cd>
<sd>(f)</sd> <cd>To cause to become silent by authority, argument, or
force</cd>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Shut</ent><br/
<hw>Shut</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To close itself; to become closed;
<as>as, the door <ex>shuts</ex>; it <ex>shuts</ex> hard.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To shut up</b></col>, <cd>to cease speaking.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Hughes.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shut</ent><br/
<hw>Shut</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Closed or fastened;
<as>as, a <ex>shut</ex> door</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rid; clear; free; <as>as, to get <ex>shut</ex> of a
person</as>.</def> <mark>[Now dialectical or local, Eng. & U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Phon.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Formed by complete
closure of the mouth passage, and with the nose passage remaining
closed; stopped, as are the mute consonants, <it>p</it>, <it>t</it>,
<it>k</it>, <it>b</it>, <it>d</it>, and hard <it>g</it>.</def> <au>H.
Sweet.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Cut off sharply and abruptly by a
following consonant in the same syllable, as the English short vowels,
<acr/, <ecr/, <icr/, <ocr/, <ucr/, always are.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shut</ent><br/
<hw>Shut</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or time of shutting; close;
<as>as, the <ex>shut</ex> of a door</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Just then returned at <qex>shut</qex> of evening flowers.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A door or cover; a shutter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir I. Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The line or place where two pieces of metal are
united by welding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cold shut</b></col>, <cd>the imperfection in a casting
caused by the flowing of liquid metal upon partially chilled metal;
also, the imperfect weld in a forging caused by the inadequate heat of
one surface under working.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shute</ent><br/
<hw>Shute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>Chute</er>,
or <er>Shoot</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shutter</ent><br/
<hw>Shut"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
shuts or closes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A movable cover or screen for a window, designed to
shut out the light, to obstruct the view, or to be of some strength as a
defense; a blind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A removable cover, or a gate, for closing an
aperture of any kind, as for closing the passageway for molten iron from
a ladle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>A mechanical device of various
forms, attached to the aperture of a camera lens for opening and closing
to expose the plate. It is usually designed so that the time during
which the aperture is opened may be varied by a manual dial or by some
automatic mechanism, thereby allowing proper exposure of a photographic
film under different intensities of light.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuttered</ent><br/
<hw>Shut"tered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with
shutters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuttle</ent><br/
<hw>Shut"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Also
<ets>shittle</ets>, OE. <ets>schitel</ets>, <ets>scytyl</ets>,
<ets>schetyl</ets>; cf. OE. <ets>schitel</ets> a bolt of a door, AS.
<ets>scyttes</ets>; all from AS. <ets>sce<oacute/tan</ets> to shoot;
akin to Dan. <ets>skyttel</ets>, <ets>skytte</ets>, shuttle, dial. Sw.
<ets>skyttel</ets>, <ets>sk<oum/ttel</ets>. <root/159. See
<er>Shoot</er>, and cf. <er>Shittle</er>, <er>Skittles</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An instrument used in weaving for passing or shooting
the thread of the woof from one side of the cloth to the other between
the threads of the warp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like <qex>shuttles</qex> through the loom, so swiftly glide<br/
My feathered hours.</q> <rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The sliding thread holder in a sewing machine, which
carries the lower thread through a loop of the upper thread, to make a
lock stitch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A shutter, as for a channel for molten metal.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shuttle box</b></col> <fld>(Weaving)</fld>, <cd>a case at
the end of a shuttle race, to receive the shuttle after it has passed
the thread of the warp; also, one of a set of compartments containing
shuttles with different colored threads, which are passed back and forth
in a certain order, according to the pattern of the cloth woven.</cd> --
<col><b>Shutten race</b></col>, <cd>a sort of shelf in a loom, beneath
the warp, along which the shuttle passes; a channel or guide along which
the shuttle passes in a sewing machine.</cd> -- <col><b>Shuttle
shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of
marine gastropods of the genus <gen>Volva</gen>, or <gen>Radius</gen>,
having a smooth, spindle-shaped shell prolonged into a channel at each
end.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuttle</ent><br/
<hw>Shut"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To move backwards
and forwards, like a shuttle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had to fly far and wide, <qex>shutting</qex> athwart the big
Babel, wherever his calls and pauses had to be.</q>
<rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuttlecock</ent><br/
<hw>Shut"tle*cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A cork stuck
with feathers, which is to be struck by a battledoor in play; also, the
play itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuttlecock</ent><br/
<hw>Shut"tle*cock</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To send or toss to and
fro; to bandy; <as>as, to <ex>shuttlecock</ex> words</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuttlecork</ent><br/
<hw>Shut"tle*cork`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shuttlecock</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shuttlewise</ent><br/
<hw>Shut"tle*wise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Back and
forth, like the movement of a shuttle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shwan-pan</ent><br/
<hw>Shwan"-pan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Schwan-pan</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shy</ent><br/
<hw>Shy</hw> <pr>(sh<imac/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Shier</adjf>
<pr>(sh<imac/"<etil/r)</pr> or <adjf>Shyer</adjf>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Shiest</adjf> or <adjf>Shyest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>schey</ets>, <ets>skey</ets>, <ets>sceouh</ets>, AS.
<ets>sce<oacute/h</ets>; akin to Dan. <ets>sky</ets>, Sw.
<ets>skygg</ets>, D. <ets>schuw</ets>, MHG. <ets>schiech</ets>, G.
<ets>scheu</ets>, OHG. <ets>sciuhen</ets> to be or make timid. Cf.
<er>Eschew</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Easily frightened; timid;
<as>as, a <ex>shy</ex> bird</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The horses of the army . . . were no longer <qex>shy</qex>, but
would come up to my very feet without starting.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Reserved; coy; disinclined to familiar
approach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What makes you so <qex>shy</qex>, my good friend? There's nobody
loves you better than I.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The embarrassed look of <qex>shy</qex> distress<br/
And maidenly shamefacedness.</q> <rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Cautious; wary; suspicious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am very <qex>shy</qex> of using corrosive liquors in the
preparation of medicines.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Princes are, by wisdom of state, somewhat <qex>shy</qex> of thier
successors.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Inadequately supplied; short; lacking; <as>as, the
team is <ex>shy</ex> two players</as>.</def><mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Poker)</fld>, <def>owing money to the pot; -- in
cases where an opponent's bet has exceeded a player's available stake or
chips, but the player chooses to continue playing the hand before adding
the required bet to the pot.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To fight shy</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Fight</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shy</ent><br/
<hw>Shy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Shied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Shying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Shy</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To start suddenly aside through fright or
suspicion; -- said especially of horses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shy</ent><br/
<hw>Shy</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To throw sidewise with a jerk; to
fling; <as>as, to <ex>shy</ex> a stone; to <ex>shy</ex> a
slipper</as>.</def> <rj><au>T. Hughes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shy</ent><br/
<hw>Shy</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sudden start aside, as by
a horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A side throw; a throw; a fling.</def>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If Lord Brougham gets a stone in his hand, he must, it seems, have
a <qex>shy</qex> at somebody.</q> <rj><qau>Punch.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shyly</ent><br/
<hw>Shy"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a shy or timid manner; not
familiarly; with reserve.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>shily</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shyness</ent><br/
<hw>Shy"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
shy.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>shiness</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Frequency in heavenly contemplation is particularly important to
prevent a <qex>shyness</qex> bewtween God and thy soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Baxter.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Bashfulness; reserve; coyness; timidity;
diffidence. See <er>Bashfulness</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Shyster</ent><br/
<hw>Shy"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perh. from G.
<ets>scheisse</ets> excrement.]</ety> <def>A trickish knave; one who
carries on any business, especially legal business, in a mean and
dishonest way.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Si</ent><br/
<hw>Si</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[It.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A
syllable applied, in solmization, to the note B; more recently, to the
seventh tone of any major diatonic scale. It was added to Guido's scale
by Le Maire about the end of the 17th century.</def><-- now called ti
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siaga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*a"ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The ahu, or jairou.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sialogogue</ent><br/
<hw>Si*al"o*gogue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>si`alon</grk> saliva + <?/<?/<?/<?/ leading, from <?/<?/<?/ to
lead: cf. F. <ets>sialagogue</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An
agent which promotes the flow of saliva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siamang</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si"a*mang`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Malay
<ets>si<amac/mang</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A gibbon
(<spn>Hylobates syndactylus</spn>), native of Sumatra. It has the second
and third toes partially united by a web.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siamese</ent><br/
<hw>Si`a*mese"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Siam, its native people, or their language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siamese</ent><br/
<hw>Si`a*mese`</hw>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native
or inhabitant of Siam; <pluf>pl.</pluf>, the people of Siam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <singf>sing.</singf> <def>The language of the
Siamese.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sib</ent><br/
<hw>Sib</hw> <pr>(s<icr/b)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sibb</ets>
alliance, <ets>gesib</ets> a relative. <root/289. See
<er>Gossip</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A blood relation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Nash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a <er>sibling</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sib</ent><br/
<hw>Sib</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Related by blood; akin.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your kindred is but . . . little <qex>sib</qex> to you.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] is no fairy birn, ne <qex>sib</qex> at all<br/
To elfs, but sprung of seed terrestrial.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibbens</ent><br/
<hw>Sib"bens</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A contagious disease, endemic
in Scotland, resembling the yaws. It is marked by ulceration of the
throat and nose and by pustules and soft fungous excrescences upon the
surface of the body. In the Orkneys the name is applied to the
itch.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sivvens</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siberian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*be"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Siberia</ets>, Russ. <ets>Sibire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to Siberia, a region comprising all northern Asia and
belonging to Russia; <as>as, a <ex>Siberian</ex> winter</as>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Siberia.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Siberian crab</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
Siberian crab apple. See <cref>Crab apple</cref>, under
<er>Crab</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Siberian dog</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>one of a large breed of dogs having erect ears
and the hair of the body and tail very long. It is distinguished for
endurance of fatigue when used for the purpose of draught.</cd> --
<col><b>Siberian pea tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small
leguminous tree (<spn>Cragana arborescens</spn>) with yellow flowers. It
is a native of Siberia.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibilancy</ent><br/
<ent>Sibilance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sib"i*lance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sib"i*lan*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sibilant; sibilation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Milton would not have avoided them for their <qex>sibilancy</qex>,
he who wrote . . . verses that hiss like Medusa's head in wrath.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibilant</ent><br/
<hw>Sib"i*lant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sibilans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sibilare</ets> to
hiss: cf. F. <ets>sibilant</ets>.]</ety> <def>Making a hissing sound;
uttered with a hissing sound; hissing; <as>as, <it>s</it>, <it>z</it>,
<it>sh</it>, and <it>zh</it>, are <ex>sibilant</ex> elementary
sounds</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A sibiliant
letter.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibilate</ent><br/
<hw>Sib"i*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To
pronounce with a hissing sound, like that of the letter <it>s</it>; to
mark with a character indicating such pronunciation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibilation</ent><br/
<hw>Sib`i*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sibilatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Utterance with a hissing sound; also,
the sound itself; a hiss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He, with a long, low <qex>sibilation</qex>, stared.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibilatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sib"i*la*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hissing;
sibilant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibilous</ent><br/
<hw>Sib"i*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sibilus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having a hissing sound; hissing;
sibilant.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Pennant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sibling</ent><br/
<hw>sib"ling</hw> <ety>[<ets>sub</ets> + <ets>-ling</ets>.]</ety>
<pr>(s<icr/b"l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a brother or a
sister.</def> <note>Siblings have at least one parent in common. Those
related only by a common mother are <stype>uterine siblings</stype>;
those related only by a common father are <stype>agnate siblings</stype>
or <stype>consanguine siblings</stype> (a legal term). A sibling having
both parents in common is a <stype>sibling-german</stype> or a
<stype>full brother</stype> or <stype>full sister</stype>. These
modifying terms are more commonly used for the more specific
<stype>uterine brother</stype>, <stype>uterine sister</stype>,
<stype>agnate brother</stype>, <stype>brother-german</stype>,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sibling</ent><br/
<hw>sib"ling</hw> <pr>(s<icr/b"l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>of or
pertaining to a <er>sibling</er>, <pos>n.</pos>; <as>as,
<ex>sibling</ex> rivalry: the common rivalry between
siblings.</as></def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibyl</ent><br/
<hw>Sib"yl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sibylla</ets>,
Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A
woman supposed to be endowed with a spirit of prophecy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The number of the sibyls is variously stated by
different authors; but the opinion of Varro, that there were ten, is
generally adopted. They dwelt in various parts of Persia, Greece, and
Italy.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A female fortune teller; a pythoness; a
prophetess.</def> <ldquo/An old highland <xex>sibyl</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibylist</ent><br/
<hw>Sib"yl*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who believes in a sibyl or
the sibylline prophecies.</def> <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sibylline</ent><br/
<hw>Sib"yl*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sibyllinus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to the sibyls; uttered,
written, or composed by sibyls; like the productions of
sibyls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sibylline books</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Rom.
Antiq.)</fld> <cd>Books or documents of prophecies in verse concerning
the fate of the Roman empire, said to have been purchased by Tarquin the
Proud from a sibyl.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Certain Jewish and early
Christian writings purporting to have been prophetic and of sibylline
origin. They date from 100 <sc>b. c.</sc> to <sc>a. d.</sc>
500.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sic</ent><br/
<hw>Sic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Such.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sic</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>Thus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word is sometimes inserted in a quotation
[<it>sic</it>], to call attention to the fact that some remarkable or
inaccurate expression, misspelling, or the like, is literally
reproduced.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicamore</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"a*more</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sycamore</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sic"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sikka</ets>.]</ety> <def>A seal; a coining die; -- used adjectively
to designate the silver currency of the Mogul emperors, or the Indian
rupee of 192 grains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sicca rupee</b></col>, <cd>an East Indian coin, valued
nominally at about two shillings sterling, or fifty cents.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siccate</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>siccatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>siccare</ets> to dry, fr.
<ets>siecus</ets> dry.]</ety> <def>To dry.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siccation</ent><br/
<hw>Sic*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>siccatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or process of drying.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siccative</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"ca*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>siccativus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Drying; causing to dry.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>That which promotes drying.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siccific</ent><br/
<hw>Sic*cif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>siccificus</ets>; <ets>siccus</ets> dry + <ets>facere</ets> to
make. See <er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <def>Causing dryness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siccity</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"ci*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>siccitas</ets>, fr. <ets>siccus</ets> dry.]</ety> <def>Dryness;
aridity; destitution of moisture.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>siccity</qex> and dryness of its flesh.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sice</ent><br/
<hw>Sice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>six</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>sex</ets> six. See <er>Six</er>.]</ety> <def>The number six at
dice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicer</ent><br/
<hw>Si"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sicera</ets>.
See <er>Cider</er>.]</ety> <def>A strong drink; cider.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sich</ent><br/
<hw>Sich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Such.</def> <mark>[Obs.
or Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicilian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*cil"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to Sicily or its inhabitants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sicilian vespers</b></col>, <cd>the great massacre of the
French in Sicily, in the year 1282, on the evening of Easter Monday, at
the hour of vespers.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicilian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*cil"i*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Sicily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siciliano</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*ci`li*a"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.,
Sicilian.]</ety> <def>A Sicilian dance, resembling the pastorale, set to
a rather slow and graceful melody in 12-8 or 6-8 measure; also, the
music to the dance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicilienne</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si`ci`lienne"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fem. of
<ets>sicilien</ets> Sicilian.]</ety> <def>A kind of rich
poplin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sick</ent><br/
<hw>Sick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sicker</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sickest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sek</ets>,
<ets>sik</ets>, ill, AS. <ets>se<oacute/c</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>siok</ets>, <ets>seoc</ets>, OFries. <ets>siak</ets>, D.
<ets>ziek</ets>, G. <ets>siech</ets>, OHG. <ets>sioh</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sj<?/kr</ets>, Sw. <ets>sjuk</ets>, Dan. <ets>syg</ets>, Goth.
<ets>siuks</ets> ill, <ets>siukan</ets> to be ill.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Affected with disease of any kind; ill; indisposed; not in health.
See the Synonym under <er>Illness</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Simon's wife's mother lay <qex>sick</qex> of a fever.</q>
<rj><qau>Mark i. 30.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Behold them that are <qex>sick</qex> with famine.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. xiv. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Affected with, or attended by, nausea; inclined to
vomit; <as>as, <ex>sick</ex> at the stomach; a <ex>sick</ex>
headache.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having a strong dislike; disgusted; surfeited; --
with <xex>of</xex>; <as>as, to be <ex>sick</ex> of
flattery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was not so <qex>sick</qex> of his master as of his work.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Corrupted; imperfect; impaired; weakened.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So great is his antipathy against episcopacy, that, if a seraphim
himself should be a bishop, he would either find or make some
<qex>sick</qex> feathers in his wings.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sick bay</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>an apartment
in a vessel, used as the ship's hospital.</cd> -- <col><b>Sick
bed</b></col>, <cd>the bed upon which a person lies sick.</cd> --
<col><b>Sick berth</b></col>, <cd>an apartment for the sick in a ship of
war.</cd> -- <col><b>Sick headache</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
variety of headache attended with disorder of the stomach and
nausea.</cd> -- <col><b>Sick list</b></col>, <cd>a list containing the
names of the sick.</cd> -- <col><b>Sick room</b></col>, <cd>a room in
which a person lies sick, or to which he is confined by sickness.</cd>
<note>[These terms, <xex>sick bed</xex>, <xex>sick berth</xex>, etc.,
are also written both hyphened and solid.]</note></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Diseased; ill; disordered; distempered;
indisposed; weak; ailing; feeble; morbid.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sick</ent><br/
<hw>Sick</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sickness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sick</ent><br/
<hw>Sick</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fall sick; to sicken.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sick-brained</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"-brained`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Disordered in
the brain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicken</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sickened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sickening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make
sick; to disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Raise this strength, and <qex>sicken</qex> that to death.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make qualmish; to nauseate; to disgust; <as>as,
to <ex>sicken</ex> the stomach</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To impair; to weaken.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicken</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"en</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To become sick; to
fall into disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The judges that sat upon the jail, and those that attended,
<qex>sickened</qex> upon it and died.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be filled to disgust; to be disgusted or
nauseated; to be filled with abhorrence or aversion; to be surfeited or
satiated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mine eyes did <qex>sicken</qex> at the sight.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1337 --></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To become disgusting or tedious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The toiling pleasure <qex>sickens</qex> into pain.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To become weak; to decay; to languish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All pleasures <qex>sicken</qex>, and all glories sink.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickening</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"en*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Causing sickness;
specif., causing surfeit or disgust; nauseating.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sick"en*ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicker</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sicerian</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>To percolate,
trickle, or ooze, as water through a crack.</def> <altsp>[Also written
<asp>sigger</asp>, <asp>zigger</asp>, and <asp>zifhyr</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siker</ent><br/
<ent>Sicker</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sick"er</hw>, <hw>Sik"er</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>siker</ets>; cf. OS. <ets>sikur</ets>, LG.
<ets>seker</ets>, D. <ets>zeker</ets>, Dan. <ets>sikker</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sihhur</ets>, G. <ets>sicher</ets>; all fr. L. <ets>securus</ets>.
See <er>Secure</er>, <er>Sure</er>.]</ety> <def>Sure; certain;
trusty.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When he is <qex>siker</qex> of his good name.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siker</ent><br/
<ent>Sicker</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sick"er</hw>, <hw>Sik"er</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Surely; certainly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Believe this as <qex>siker</qex> as your creed.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sicker</qex>, Willye, thou warnest well.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sikerly</ent><br/
<ent>Sickerly</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sick"er*ly</hw>, <hw>Sik"er*ly</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Surely; securely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But <qex>sikerly</qex>, withouten any fable.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sikerness</ent><br/
<ent>Sickerness</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sick"er*ness</hw>, <hw>Sik"er*ness</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being sicker, or
certain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.
Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickish</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Somewhat sick or
diseased.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Somewhat sickening; <as>as, a <ex>sickish</ex>
taste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sick"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sick"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickle</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sikel</ets>,
AS. <ets>sicol</ets>; akin to D. <ets>sikkel</ets>, G.
<ets>sichel</ets>, OHG. <ets>sihhila</ets>, Dan. <ets>segel</ets>,
<ets>segl</ets>, L. <ets>secula</ets>, fr. <ets>secare</ets> to cut; or
perhaps from L. <ets>secula</ets>. See <er>Saw</er> a cutting
instrument.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A reaping instrument consisting of a
steel blade curved into the form of a hook, and having a handle fitted
on a tang. The sickle has one side of the blade notched, so as always to
sharpen with a serrated edge. Cf. <cref>Reaping hook</cref>, under
<er>Reap</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When corn has once felt the <qex>sickle</qex>, it has no more
benefit from the sunshine.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A group of stars in the
constellation Leo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sickle pod</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of
rock cress (<spn>Arabis Canadensis</spn>) having very long curved
pods.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicklebill</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"kle*bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of three species of humming birds of the genus
<gen>Eutoxeres</gen>, native of Central and South America. They have a
long and strongly curved bill. Called also the <altname>sickle-billed
hummer</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A curlew.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>A bird of the genus <gen>Epimachus</gen> and allied
genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickled</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"kled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with a
sickle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickleman</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"kle*man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sicklemen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who uses a sickle; a
reaper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You sunburned <qex>sicklemen</qex>, of August weary.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickler</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"kler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who uses a
sickle; a sickleman; a reaper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickless</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from
sickness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give me long breath, young beds, and <qex>sickless</qex> ease.</q>
<rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicklewort</ent><br/
<hw>Sic"kle*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sicolwyrt</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A plant
of the genus <gen>Coronilla</gen> (<spn>Coronilla scorpioides</spn>); --
so named from its curved pods.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The healall
(<spn>Brunella vulgaris</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicklied</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"lied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Made sickly. See
<er>Sickly</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickliness</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sickly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickly</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sicklier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sickliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Somewhat sick;
disposed to illness; attended with disease; <as>as, a <ex>sickly</ex>
body</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This physic but prolongs thy <qex>sickly</qex> days.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Producing, or tending to, disease; <as>as, a
<ex>sickly</ex> autumn; a <ex>sickly</ex> climate</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Appearing as if sick; weak; languid; pale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The moon grows <qex>sickly</qex> at the sight of day.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor torrid summer's <qex>sickly</qex> smile.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Tending to produce nausea; sickening; <as>as, a
<ex>sickly</ex> smell; <ex>sickly</ex> sentimentality.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Diseased; ailing; infirm; weakly; unhealthy;
healthless; weak; feeble; languid; faint.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickly</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sick manner or condition;
ill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My people <qex>sickly</qex> [with ill will] beareth our
marriage.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickly</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"ly</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make sick or sickly; -- with
<xex>over</xex>, and probably only in the past participle.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sicklied</qex> o'er with the pale cast of thought.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sentiments <qex>sicklied</qex> over . . . with that cloying
heaviness into which unvaried sweetness is too apt to subside.</q>
<rj><qau>Jeffrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sickness</ent><br/
<hw>Sick"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>se<oacute/cness</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state
of being sick or diseased; illness; disease or malady.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do lament the <qex>sickness</qex> of the king.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trust not too much your now resistless charms;<br/
Those, age or <qex>sickness</qex> soon or late disarms.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nausea; qualmishness; <as>as, <ex>sickness</ex> of
stomach</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Illness; disease; malady. See
<er>Illness</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sicle</ent><br/
<hw>Si"cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>silcus</ets>, Heb. <ets>shegel</ets>. See <er>Shekel</er>.]</ety>
<def>A shekel.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The holy mother brought five <qex>sicles</qex> and a pair of
turtledoves to redeem the Lamb of God.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ a kind of plant.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of
malvaceous plants common in the tropics. All the species are
mucilaginous, and some have tough ligneous fibers which are used as a
substitute for hemp and flax.</def> <rj><au>Balfour (Cyc. of
India).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siddow</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"dow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Soft; pulpy.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side</ent><br/
<hw>Side</hw> <pr>(s<imac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<imac/de</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zijde</ets>, G. <ets>seite</ets>,
OHG. <ets>s<imac/ta</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<imac/<?/a</ets>, Dan.
<ets>side</ets>, Sw. <ets>sida</ets>; cf. AS. <ets>s<imac/d</ets> large,
spacious, Icel. <ets>s<imac/<?/r</ets> long, hanging.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The margin, edge, verge, or border of a surface; especially (when
the thing spoken of is somewhat oblong in shape), one of the longer
edges as distinguished from the shorter edges, called <xex>ends</xex>; a
bounding line of a geometrical figure; <as>as, the <ex>side</ex> of a
field, of a square or triangle, of a river, of a road,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any outer portion of a thing considered apart from,
and yet in relation to, the rest; <as>as, the upper <ex>side</ex> of a
sphere</as>; also, any part or position viewed as opposite to or
contrasted with another; <as>as, this or that <ex>side</ex></as>.</def>
<-- any part of the surface which can be viewed from one vantage point.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Looking round on every <qex>side</qex> beheld<br/
A pathless desert.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the halves of the body, of an
animals or man, on either side of the mesial plane; or that which
pertains to such a half; <as>as, a <ex>side</ex> of beef; a
<ex>side</ex> of sole leather.</as></def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The right or
left part of the wall or trunk of the body; <as>as, a pain in the
<ex>side</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One of the soldiers with a spear pierced his <qex>side</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>John xix. 34.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A slope or declivity, as of a hill, considered as
opposed to another slope over the ridge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Along the <qex>side</qex> of yon small hill.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The position of a person or party regarded as
opposed to another person or party, whether as a rival or a foe; a body
of advocates or partisans; a party; hence, the interest or cause which
one maintains against another; a doctrine or view opposed to
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God on our <qex>side</qex>, doubt not of victory.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have not always been of the . . . same <qex>side</qex> in
politics.</q> <rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sets the passions on the <qex>side</qex> of truth.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A line of descent traced through one parent as
distinguished from that traced through another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To sit upon thy father David's throne,<br/
By mother's <qex>side</qex> thy father.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Fig.: Aspect or part regarded as contrasted with
some other; <as>as, the bright <ex>side</ex> of poverty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>By the side of</b></col>, <cd>close at hand; near
to.</cd> -- <col><b>Exterior side</b></col>. <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Exterior</er>, and <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Ravelin</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Interior side</b></col> <fld>(Fort.)</fld>, <cd>the line drawn
from the center of one bastion to that of the next, or the line curtain
produced to the two oblique radii in front.</cd> <au>H. L. Scott.</au>
-- <col><b>Side by side</b></col>, <cd>close together and abreast; in
company or along with.</cd> -- <col><b>To choose sides</b></col>, <cd>to
select those who shall compete, as in a game, on either side.</cd> --
<col><b>To take sides</b></col>, <cd>to attach one's self to, or give
assistance to, one of two opposing sides or parties.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side</ent><br/
<hw>Side</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a side, or the sides; being on the side, or toward the
side; lateral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One mighty squadron with a <qex>side</qex> wind sped.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, indirect; oblique; collateral; incidental;
<as>as, a <ex>side</ex> issue; a <ex>side</ex> view or
remark.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The law hath no <qex>side</qex> respect to their persons.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<imac/d</ets>. Cf <er>Side</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Long; large; extensive.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His gown had <qex>side</qex> sleeves down to mid leg.</q>
<rj><qau>Laneham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Side action</b></col>, <cd>in breech-loading firearms, a
mechanism for operating the breech block, which is moved by a lever that
turns sidewise.</cd> -- <col><b>Side arms</b></col>, <cd>weapons worn at
the side, as sword, bayonet, pistols, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Side
ax</b></col>, <cd>an ax of which the handle is bent to one side.</cd> --
<col><b>Side-bar rule</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Law.)</fld>, <cd>a rule
authorized by the courts to be granted by their officers as a matter of
course, without formal application being made to them in open court; --
so called because anciently moved for by the attorneys at <xex>side
bar</xex>, that is, informally.</cd> <au>Burril.</au> -- <col><b>Side
box</b></col>, <cd>a box or inclosed seat on the side of a
theater.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To insure a <qex>side-box</qex> station at half price.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Side chain</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>one of two safety
chains connecting a tender with a locomotive, at the sides.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>a chain of atoms attached to the
main structure of a large molecule, especially of a polymer.</cd> --
<col><b>Side cut</b></col>, <cd>a canal or road branching out from the
main one.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Side dish</b></col>,
<cd>one of the dishes subordinate to the main course.</cd> --
<col><b>Side glance</b></col>, <cd>a glance or brief look to one
side.</cd> -- <col><b>Side hook</b></col> <fld>(Carp.)</fld>, <cd>a
notched piece of wood for clamping a board to something, as a
bench.</cd> -- <col><b>Side lever</b></col>, <cd> a working beam of a
side-lever engine.</cd> -- <col><b>Side-lever engine</b></col>, <cd>a
marine steam engine having a working beam of each side of the cylinder,
near the bottom of the engine, communicating motion to a crank that is
above them.</cd> -- <col><b>Side pipe</b></col> <fld>(Steam
Engine)</fld>, <cd>a steam or exhaust pipe connecting the upper and
lower steam chests of the cylinder of a beam engine.</cd> --
<col><b>Side plane</b></col>, <cd>a plane in which the cutting edge of
the iron is at the side of the stock.</cd> -- <col><b>Side
posts</b></col> <fld>(Carp.)</fld>, <cd>posts in a truss, usually placed
in pairs, each post set at the same distance from the middle of the
truss, for supporting the principal rafters, hanging the tiebeam,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Side rod</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>One of the
rods which connect the piston-rod crosshead with the side levers, in a
side-lever engine.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>See <cref>Parallel rod</cref>,
under <er>Parallel</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Side screw</b></col>
<fld>(Firearms)</fld>, <cd>one of the screws by which the lock is
secured to the side of a firearm stock.</cd> -- <col><b>Side
table</b></col>, <cd>a table placed either against the wall or aside
from the principal table.</cd> -- <col><b>Side tool</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a cutting tool, used in a lathe or planer,
having the cutting edge at the side instead of at the point.</cd> --
<col><b>Side wind</b></col>, <cd>a wind from one side; hence, an
indirect attack, or indirect means.</cd> <au>Wright.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Side</ent><br/
<hw>Side</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sided</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Siding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lean on one
side.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To embrace the opinions of one party, or engage in
its interest, in opposition to another party; to take sides; <as>as, to
<ex>side</ex> with the ministerial party</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>side</qex> in parties, and begin the attack.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side</ent><br/
<hw>Side</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be or stand at the
side of; to be on the side toward.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His blind eye that <qex>sided</qex> Paridell.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To suit; to pair; to match.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>To work (a timber or rib)
to a certain thickness by trimming the sides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To furnish with a siding; <as>as, to <ex>side</ex> a
house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideboard</ent><br/
<hw>Side"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A piece of
dining-room furniture having compartments and shelves for keeping or
displaying articles of table service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At a stately <qex>sideboard</qex>, by the wine,<br/
That fragrant smell diffused.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidebone</ent><br/
<hw>Side"bone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A
morbid growth or deposit of bony matter and at the sides of the coronet
and coffin bone of a horse.</def> <rj><au>J. H. Walsh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side-chain theory</ent><br/
<hw>Side"-chain` the`o*ry</hw>. <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A
theory proposed by <person>P. Ehrlich</person> as a chemical explanation
of immunity phenomena. In brief outline it is as follows: Animal cells
and bacteria are complex aggregations of molecules, which are themselves
complex. Complex molecules react with one another through certain of
their side chains, but only when these side chains have a definite
correspondence in structure (this accounts for the specific action of
antitoxins).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sided</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having (such or so
many) sides; -- used in composition; <as>as, one-<ex>sided</ex>;
many-<ex>sided</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideflash</ent><br/
<hw>Side"flash`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>
<def>A disruptive discharge between a conductor traversed by an
oscillatory current of high frequency (as lightning) and neighboring
masses of metal, or between different parts of the same
conductor.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidehill</ent><br/
<hw>Side"hill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The side or slope
of a hill; sloping ground; a descent.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side line</ent><br/
<ent>Sideline</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Sideline</hw>, <hw>Side line</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A line pertaining or attached to the side
of a thing.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Specif., a line for hobbling an
animal by connecting the fore and the hind feet of the same
side.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A line of goods sold in addition to one's principal
articles of trade; a course of business pursued aside from one's regular
occupation.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A secondary road; esp., a byroad at right angles to
a main road.</def> <mark>[Canada]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Sports)</fld> <def>Either of two marked lines which
delimit the side boundaries of a playing field, as in football or
soccer.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Sports)</fld> <mark>[plural]</mark> <def>The areas
in a sports arena just beyond the sidelines{4}, where coaches and
alternative players sit or stand.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>on the sidelines</b></col> <cd>not actively involved in
an activity, such as a sporting event, but available for use when
needed; in reserve; <as>as, the coach kept him <ex>on the sidelines</ex>
for a week</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideline</ent><br/
<hw>Sideline</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To render unable to participate
in an activity, such as a sporting event; <as>as, the pitcher was
<ex>sidelined</ex> by a groin injury</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideling</ent><br/
<hw>Side"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sideling</ets>, fr. <ets>side</ets> side. See <er>Side</er>, and
cf. <er>Sidelong</er>, <er>Headlong</er>.]</ety> <def>Sidelong; on the
side; laterally; also, obliquely; askew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A fellow nailed up maps . . . some <qex>sideling</qex>, and others
upside down.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideling</ent><br/
<hw>Side"ling</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inclining to one side; directed
toward one side; sloping; inclined; <as>as, <ex>sideling</ex>
ground</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidelong</ent><br/
<hw>Side"long`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sideling</er>, <pos>adv.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Laterally;
obliquely; in the direction of the side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>On the side; <as>as, to lay a thing
<ex>sidelong</ex></as>.</def> <note>[See <er>Sideling</er>,
<pos>adv.</pos> ]</note> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidelong</ent><br/
<hw>Side"long`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lateral; oblique; not being
directly in front; <as>as, a <ex>sidelong</ex> glance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bashful virgin's <qex>sidelong</qex> looks of love.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidepiece</ent><br/
<hw>Side"piece`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Joinery)</fld>
<def>The jamb, or cheek, of an opening in a wall, as of door or
window.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sider</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who takes a
side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sider</ent><br/
<hw>Si"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Cider.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideral</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"er*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sideralis</ets>. See <er>Sidereal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Relating to the stars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>Affecting unfavorably by the
supposed influence of the stars; baleful.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Sideral</xex> blast.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderated</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"er*a`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sideratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>siderari</ets> to be blasted by a
constellation, fr. <ets>sidus</ets>, <ets>sideris</ets>, a
constellation.]</ety> <def>Planet-struck; blasted.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideration</ent><br/
<hw>Sid`er*a"tion</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sideratio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state of being siderated, or
planet-struck; esp., blast in plants; also, a sudden and apparently
causeless stroke of disease, as in apoplexy or paralysis.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidereal</ent><br/
<hw>Si*de"re*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sidereus</ets>, from <ets>sidus</ets>, <ets>sideris</ets>, a
constellation, a star. Cf. <er>Sideral</er>, <er>Consider</er>,
<er>Desire</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating to the stars; starry;
astral; <as>as, <ex>sidereal</ex> astronomy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Measuring by the apparent
motion of the stars; designated, marked out, or accompanied, by a return
to the same position in respect to the stars; <as>as, the
<ex>sidereal</ex> revolution of a planet; a <ex>sidereal</ex>
day.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sidereal clock</b></col>, <col><b>day</b></col>,
<col><b>month</b></col>, <col><b>year</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under
<er>Clock</er>, <er>Day</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Sideral
time</b></col>, <cd>time as reckoned by sideral days, or, taking the
sidereal day as the unit, the time elapsed since a transit of the vernal
equinox, reckoned in parts of a sidereal day. This is, strictly,
<xex>apparent sidereal time</xex>, <xex>mean sidereal time</xex> being
reckoned from the transit, not of the <xex>true</xex>, but of the
<xex>mean</xex>, equinoctial point.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderealize</ent><br/
<hw>Si*de"re*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To elevate
to the stars, or to the region of the stars; to etherealize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>German literature transformed, <qex>siderealized</qex>, as we see
it in Goethe, reckons Winckelmann among its initiators.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Pater.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidereous</ent><br/
<hw>Si*de"re*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sidereus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sidereal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderite</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"er*ite</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sideritis</ets>
loadstone, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/<?/, of iron, from <?/<?/<?/<?/
iron.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Carbonate
of iron, an important ore of iron occurring generally in cleavable
masses, but also in rhombohedral crystals. It is of a light yellowish
brown color. Called also <altname>sparry iron</altname>,
<altname>spathic iron</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A meteorite
consisting solely of metallic iron.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>An
indigo-blue variety of quartz.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>Formerly,
magnetic iron ore, or loadstone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of the genus
<gen>Sideritis</gen>; ironwort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderographical</ent><br/
<ent>Siderographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sid`er*o*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sid`er*o*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Of or pertaining to siderography; executed by engraved plates of
steel; <as>as, <ex>siderographic</ex> art; <ex>siderographic</ex>
impressions.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderographist</ent><br/
<hw>Sid`er*og"ra*phist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled
in siderography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderography</ent><br/
<hw>Sid`er*og"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ iron + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art or practice of
steel engraving; especially, the process, invented by Perkins, of
multiplying facsimiles of an engraved steel plate by first rolling over
it, when hardened, a soft steel cylinder, and then rolling the cylinder,
when hardened, over a soft steel plate, which thus becomes a facsimile
of the original. The process has been superseded by
<xex>electrotypy</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderolite</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"er*o*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
iron + <ets>-lite</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of meteorite. See under
<er>Meteorite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideromancy</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"er*o*man`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
iron + <ets>-mancy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Divination by burning straws on
red-hot iron, and noting the manner of their burning.</def>
<rj><au>Craig.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideroscope</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"er*o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
iron + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for detecting small
quantities of iron in any substance by means of a very delicate
combination of magnetic needles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sid`e*ro"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ iron.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A sort of pneumonia
occurring in iron workers, produced by the inhalation of particles of
iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siderostat</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"er*o*stat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sidus</ets>, <ets>sideris</ets>, a star + Gr. <?/<?/<?/ standing,
fixed, fr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ to place.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>An
apparatus consisting essentially of a mirror moved by clockwork so as to
throw the rays of the sun or a star in a fixed direction; -- a more
general term for <stype>heliostat</stype>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideroxylon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sid`e*rox"y*lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/<?/<?/ iron + <?/<?/<?/ wood.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
genus of tropical sapotaceous trees noted for their very hard wood;
ironwood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidesaddle</ent><br/
<hw>Side"sad`dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A saddle for
women, in which the rider sits with both feet on one side of the animal
mounted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sidesaddle flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
plant with hollow leaves and curiously shaped flowers; -- called also
<altname>huntsman's cup</altname>. See
<er>Sarracenia</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side-slip</ent><br/
<hw>Side"-slip`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See <er>Skid</er>,
below.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side slip</ent><br/
<hw>Side slip</hw>. <def>See <er>Skid</er>, below.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1338 --></p>

<p><ent>Sidesman</ent><br/
<hw>Sides"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sidesmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A party man;
a partisan.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An assistant to the churchwarden; a
questman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side-taking</ent><br/
<hw>Side"-tak`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A taking sides,
as with a party, sect, or faction.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidetrack</ent><br/
<hw>Side"track`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sidetracked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sidetracking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Railroads)</fld> <def>To transfer to a siding from a main line of
track.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, fig., to divert or reduce to a position or
condition that is relatively secondary or subordinate in activity,
importance, effectiveness, or the like; to switch off; to turn aside, as
from a purpose.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></p>

<p><q>Such a project was, in fact, <qex>sidetracked</qex> in favor of
the census of school children.</q> <rj><qau>Pop. Sci.
Monthly.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidewalk</ent><br/
<hw>Side"walk`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A walk for foot
passengers at the side of a street or road; a foot pavement.</def>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sideways</ent><br/
<hw>Side"ways`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the side;
sidewise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A second refraction made <qex>sideways</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir I.
Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His beard, a good palm's length, at least, . . . <br/
Shot <qex>sideways</qex>, like a swallow's wings.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Side-wheel</ent><br/
<hw>Side"-wheel`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a paddle wheel on each
side; -- said of steam vessels; <as>as, a <ex>side-wheel</ex>
steamer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidewinder</ent><br/
<hw>Side"wind`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See Horned rattler, under
<er>Horned</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidewise</ent><br/
<sn>2.</sn> <def>A heavy swinging blow from the side, which disables an
adversary.</def> <mark>[Slang.]</mark> <hw>Side"wise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>On or toward one side; laterally;
sideways.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I saw them mask their awful glance<br/
<qex>Sidewise</qex> meek in gossamer lids.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- the informal name of a specific type of heat-seeking air-to-air
missile. Also, sidewinder missile. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siding</ent><br/
<hw>Sid"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Attaching
one's self to a party.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A side track, as a railroad; a turnout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>The covering of the outside wall
of a frame house, whether made of weatherboards, vertical boarding with
cleats, shingles, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>The thickness of a rib or
timber, measured, at right angles with its side, across the curved edge;
<as>as, a timber having a <ex>siding</ex> of ten inches</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sidle</ent><br/
<hw>Si"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sidled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sidling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From
<er>Side</er>.]</ety> <def>To go or move with one side foremost; to move
sidewise; <as>as, to <ex>sidle</ex> through a crowd or narrow
opening</as>.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . then <qex>sidled</qex> close to the astonished girl.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siege</ent><br/
<hw>Siege</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sege</ets>,
OF. <ets>siege</ets>, F. <ets>si<egrave/ge</ets> a seat, a siege; cf.
It. <ets>seggia</ets>, <ets>seggio</ets>, <ets>zedio</ets>, a seat,
<ets>asseggio</ets>, <ets>assedio</ets>, a siege, F.
<ets>assi<eacute/ger</ets> to besiege, It. & LL. <ets>assediare</ets>,
L. <ets>obsidium</ets> a siege, besieging; all ultimately fr. L.
<ets>sedere</ets> to sit. See <er>Sit</er>, and cf. <er>See</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A seat; especially, a royal seat;
a throne.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Upon the very
<xex>siege</xex> of justice.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A stately <qex>siege</qex> of sovereign majesty,<br/
And thereon sat a woman gorgeous gay.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In our great hall there stood a vacant chair . . . <br/
And Merlin called it <ldquo/The <qex>siege</qex> perilous.<rdquo/</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, place or situation; seat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ah! traitorous eyes, come out of your shameless <qex>siege</qex>
forever.</q> <rj><qau>Painter (Palace of Pleasure).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Rank; grade; station; estimation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I fetch my life and being<br/
From men of royal <qex>siege</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Passage of excrements; stool; fecal matter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>siege</qex> of this mooncalf.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The sitting of an army around or before a fortified
place for the purpose of compelling the garrison to surrender; the
surrounding or investing of a place by an army, and approaching it by
passages and advanced works, which cover the besiegers from the enemy's
fire. See the Note under <er>Blockade</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Hence, a continued attempt to gain
possession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Love stood the <qex>siege</qex>, and would not yield his
breast.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The floor of a glass-furnace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A workman's bench.</def>
<rj><au>Knught.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Siege gun</b></col>, <cd>a heavy gun for siege
operations.</cd> -- <col><b>Siege train</b></col>, <cd>artillery adapted
for attacking fortified places.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siege</ent><br/
<hw>Siege</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To besiege; to beset.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Through all the dangers that can <qex>siege</qex><br/
The life of man.</q> <rj><qau>Buron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siegework</ent><br/
<hw>Siege"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A temporary fort
or parallel where siege guns are mounted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siemens-Martin process</ent><br/
<hw>Sie"mens-Mar`tin proc"ess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See
<cref>Open-hearth process</cref>, etc., under <er>Open</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siemens-Martin steel</ent><br/
<hw>Sie"mens-Mar"tin steel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <cref>Open-hearth
steel</cref>, under <er>Open</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sienite</ent><br/
<hw>Si"e*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Syenite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sienitic</ent><br/
<hw>Si`e*nit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Syenitic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sienna</ent><br/
<hw>Si*en"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>terra di
Siena</ets>, fr. <ets>Siena</ets> in Italy.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Clay that is colored red or brown by the oxides of iron or
manganese, and used as a pigment. It is used either in the raw state or
burnt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Burnt sienna</b></col>, <cd>sienna made of a much redder
color by the action of fire.</cd> -- <col><b>Raw sienna</b></col>,
<cd>sienna in its natural state, of a transparent yellowish brown
color.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siennese</ent><br/
<hw>Si`en*nese"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to Sienna, a city of Italy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sierra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*er"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., properly, a
saw, fr. L. <ets>serra</ets> a saw. See <er>Serrate</er>.]</ety> <def>A
ridge of mountain and craggy rocks, with a serrated or irregular
outline; <as>as, the <ex>Sierra</ex> Nevada</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wild <qex>sierra</qex> overhead.</q>
<rj><qau>Whitter.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siesta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*es"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., probably
fr. L. <ets>sessitare</ets> to sit much or long, v. freq. of
<ets>sedere</ets>, <ets>sessum</ets>, to sit. See <er>Sit</er>.]</ety>
<def>A short sleep taken about the middle of the day, or after dinner; a
midday nap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sieur</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sieur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., abbrev. from
seigneur. Cf. <er>Monsieur</er>, <er>Seignior</er>.]</ety> <def>Sir; --
a title of respect used by the French.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sieva</ent><br/
<hw>Sie"va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
small variety of the Lima bean (<spn>Phaseolus lunatus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sieve</ent><br/
<hw>Sieve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sive</ets>,
AS. <ets>sife</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zeef</ets>, <ets>zift</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sib</ets>, G. <ets>sieb</ets>. <root/151<it>a</it>. Cf.
<er>Sift</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A utensil for separating the
finer and coarser parts of a pulverized or granulated substance from
each other. It consist of a vessel, usually shallow, with the bottom
perforated, or made of hair, wire, or the like, woven in meshes.</def>
<ldquo/In a <xex>sieve</xex> thrown and sifted.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of coarse basket.</def>
<rj><au>Simmonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sieve cells</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>cribriform
cells. See under <er>Cribriform</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sifac</ent><br/
<hw>Si"fac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
white indris of Madagascar. It is regarded by the natives as
sacred.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sifflement</ent><br/
<hw>Sif"fle*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., a whistling
or hissing.]</ety> <def>The act of whistling or hissing; a whistling
sound; sibilation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>A.
Brewer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sifilet</ent><br/
<hw>Sif"i*let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>siflet</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The six-shafted bird
of paradise. See <cref>Paradise bird</cref>, under
<er>Paradise</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sift</ent><br/
<hw>Sift</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sifted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sifting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>siftan</ets>, from
<ets>sife</ets> sieve. <root/151<it>a</it>. See <er>Sieve</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To separate with a sieve, as the fine part of a
substance from the coarse; <as>as, to <ex>sift</ex> meal or flour; to
<ex>sift</ex> powder; to <ex>sift</ex> sand or lime.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To separate or part as if with a sieve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When yellow sands are <qex>sifted</qex> from below,<br/
The glittering billows give a golden show.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To examine critically or minutely; to
scrutinize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sifting</qex> the very utmost sentence and syllable.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Opportunity I here have had<br/
To try thee, <qex>sift</qex> thee.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let him but narrowly <qex>sift</qex> his ideas.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sift out</b></col>, <cd>to search out with care, as if
by sifting.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sifter</ent><br/
<hw>Sift"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, sifts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any lamellirostral bird, as a
duck or goose; -- so called because it sifts or strains its food from
the water and mud by means of the lamell<?/ of the beak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sig</ent><br/
<hw>Sig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to AS.
<ets>s<imac/gan</ets> to fall. <root/151<it>a.</it> <ets>See</ets>
<er>Sink</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Urine.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigaultian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*gaul"ti*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to <xex>Sigault</xex>, a French physician. See
<er>Symphyseotomy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigger</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"ger</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>Same as</def> <er>Sicker</er>.
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigh</ent><br/
<hw>Sigh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sighed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sighing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sighen</ets>,
<ets>si<?/en</ets>; cf. also OE. <ets>siken</ets>, AS.
<ets>s<imac/can</ets>, and OE. <ets>sighten</ets>, <ets>si<?/ten</ets>,
<ets>sichten</ets>, AS. <ets>siccettan</ets>; all, perhaps, of imitative
origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To inhale a larger quantity of air than
usual, and immediately expel it; to make a deep single audible
respiration, especially as the result or involuntary expression of
fatigue, exhaustion, grief, sorrow, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to lament; to grieve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>sighed</qex> deeply in his spirit.</q> <rj><qau>Mark viii.
12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make a sound like sighing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the coming wind did roar more loud,<br/
And the sails did <qex>sigh</qex> like sedge.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The winter winds are wearily <qex>sighing</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ An extraordinary pronunciation of this word as
<xex>s<imac/th</xex> is still heard in England and among the illiterate
in the United States.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigh</ent><br/
<hw>Sigh</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To exhale (the breath)
in sighs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Never man <qex>sighed</qex> truer breath.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To utter sighs over; to lament or mourn
over.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ages to come, and men unborn,<br/
Shall bless her name, and <qex>sigh</qex> her fate.</q>
<rj><qau>Pior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To express by sighs; to utter in or with
sighs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They . . . <qex>sighed</qex> forth proverbs.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The gentle swain . . . <qex>sighs</qex> back her grief.</q>
<rj><qau>Hoole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigh</ent><br/
<hw>Sigh</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sigh</ets>; cf. OE.
<ets>sik</ets>. See <er>Sigh</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A deep and prolonged audible inspiration or respiration of air, as
when fatigued or grieved; the act of sighing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I could drive the boat with my <qex>sighs</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively, a manifestation of grief; a
lament.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With their <qex>sighs</qex> the air<br/
Frequenting, sent from hearts contrite.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigh-born</ent><br/
<hw>Sigh"-born`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sorrowful;
mournful.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Sigh-born</xex>
thoughts.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigher</ent><br/
<hw>Sigh"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sighs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sighing</ent><br/
<hw>Sigh"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Uttering sighs; grieving;
lamenting.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sighing</xex> millions.<rdquo/
<au>Cowper.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Sigh"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sight</ent><br/
<hw>Sight</hw> <pr>(s<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sight</ets>, <ets>si<thorn/t</ets>, <ets>siht</ets>, AS.
<ets>siht</ets>, ge<ets>siht</ets>, ge<ets>sih<edh/</ets>,
ge<ets>sieh<edh/</ets>, ge<ets>syh<edh/</ets>; akin to D.
ge<ets>zicht</ets>, G. <ets>sicht</ets>, ge<ets>sicht</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sigte</ets>, Sw. <ets>sigt</ets>, from the root of E.
<ets>see</ets>. See <er>See</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of seeing; perception of objects by the eye; view; <as>as,
to gain <ex>sight</ex> of land</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A cloud received him out of their <qex>sight</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts. i. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The power of seeing; the faculty of vision, or of
perceiving objects by the instrumentality of the eyes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy <qex>sight</qex> is young,<br/
And thou shalt read when mine begin to dazzle.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O loss of <qex>sight</qex>, of thee I most complain!</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The state of admitting unobstructed vision;
visibility; open view; region which the eye at one time surveys; space
through which the power of vision extends; <as>as, an object within
<ex>sight</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A spectacle; a view; a show; something worth
seeing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moses said, I will now turn aside and see this great
<qex>sight</qex>, why the bush is not burnt.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. iii.
3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They never saw a <qex>sight</qex> so fair.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The instrument of seeing; the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why cloud they not their <qex>sights</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Inspection; examination; <as>as, a letter intended
for the <ex>sight</ex> of only one person</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Mental view; opinion; judgment; <as>as, in their
<ex>sight</ex> it was harmless</as>.</def> <rj><au>Wake.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That which is highly esteemed among men is abomination in the
<qex>sight</qex> of God.</q> <rj><qau>Luke xvi. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A small aperture or optical device through which
objects are to be seen, and by which their direction is settled or
ascertained; -- used on surveying instruments; <as>as, the
<ex>sight</ex> of a quadrant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thier eyes of fire sparking through <qex>sights</qex> of
steel.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>An optical device or small piece of metal, fixed or
movable, on the breech, muzzle, center, or trunnion of a gun, or on the
breech and the muzzle of a rifle, pistol, etc., by means of which the
eye is guided in aiming. A telescope mounted on a weapon, such as a
rifle, and used for accurate aiming at distant targets is called a
<stype>telescopic sight</stype>.</def> <rj><au>Farrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>In a drawing, picture, etc., that part of the
surface, as of paper or canvas, which is within the frame or the border
or margin. In a frame or the like, the open space, the
opening.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A great number, quantity, or sum; <as>as, a
<ex>sight</ex> of money</as>.</def> <mark>[<plain>Now</plain>
colloquial]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sight</xex> in this last sense was formerly
employed in the best usage. <ldquo/A <xex>sight</xex> of
lawyers.<rdquo/</note> <rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A wonder <qex>sight</qex> of flowers.</q>
<rj><qau>Gower.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At sight</b></col>, <cd>as soon as seen, or presented to
sight; <as>as, a draft payable <ex>at sight</ex>: to read Greek <ex>at
sight</ex>; to shoot a person <ex>at sight</ex></as>.</cd> --
<col><b>Front sight</b></col> <fld>(Firearms)</fld>, <cd>the sight
nearest the muzzle.</cd> -- <col><b>Open sight</b></col>.
<fld>(Firearms)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A front sight through which the
objects aimed at may be seen, in distinction from one that hides the
object</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A rear sight having an open notch instead
of an aperture.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Peep sight</b></col>, <col><b>Rear
sight</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Peep</er>, and
<er>Rear</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sight draft</b></col>, <cd>an order, or
bill of exchange, directing the payment of money at sight.</cd> --
<col><b>To take sight</b></col>, <cd>to take aim; to look for the
purpose of directing a piece of artillery, or the like.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Vision; view; show; spectacle; representation;
exhibition.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sight</ent><br/
<hw>Sight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sighted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sighting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To get sight of; to
see; <as>as, to <ex>sight</ex> land; to <ex>sight</ex> a
wreck.</as></def> <rj><au>Kane.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To look at through a sight; to see accurately;
<as>as, to <ex>sight</ex> an object, as a star</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To apply sights to; to adjust the sights of; also,
to give the proper elevation and direction to by means of a sight;
<as>as, to <ex>sight</ex> a rifle or a cannon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sight</ent><br/
<hw>Sight</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To take aim by a
sight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sighted</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having sight, or seeing, in a
particular manner; -- used in composition; <as>as,
long-<ex>sighted</ex>, short-<ex>sighted</ex>, quick-<ex>sighted</ex>,
sharp-<ex>sighted</ex>, and the like</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sightful</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Easily or clearly
seen; distinctly visible; perspicuous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Testament of Love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sightfulness</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"ful*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being sightful;
perspicuity.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sight-hole</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"-hole`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hole for
looking through; a peephole.</def> <ldquo/Stop all
<xex>sight-holes</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sighting</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Sight</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sighting shot</b></col>, <cd>a shot made to ascertain
whether the sights of a firearm are properly adjusted; a trial
shot.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sightless</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Lacking sight;
without sight; blind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of all who blindly creep or <qex>sightless</qex> soar.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That can not be seen; invisible.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sightless</qex> couriers of the air.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Offensive or unpleasing to the eye; unsightly;
<as>as, <ex>sightless</ex> stains</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sight"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>-
<wf>Sight"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sightliness</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being sightly; comeliness; conspicuousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sightly</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pleasing
to the sight; comely.</def> <ldquo/Many brave, <xex>sightly</xex>
horses.<rdquo/ <rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Open to sight; conspicuous; <as>as, a house stands
in a <ex>sightly</ex> place</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sightproof</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"proof`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Undiscoverable to
sight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hidden in their own <qex>sightproof</qex> bush.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sight-seeing</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"-see`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Engaged in, or
given to, seeing sights; eager for novelties or curiosities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sight-seeing</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"-see`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of seeing sights;
eagerness for novelties or curiosities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sight-seer</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"-se`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One given to
seeing sights or noted things, or eager for novelties or
curiosities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sight-shot</ent><br/
<hw>Sight"-shot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Distance to which
the sight can reach or be thrown.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cowley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sightsman</ent><br/
<hw>Sights"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sightsmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>One
who reads or performs music readily at first sight.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <-- now, sight-reader --> <rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigil</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"il</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sigillum</ets>. See <er>Seal</er> a stamp.]</ety> <def>A seal; a
signature.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of talismans and <qex>sigils</qex> knew the power.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigillaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sig`il*la"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.,
from <ets>sigillum</ets> a seal. See <er>Sigil</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Rom.
Antic.)</fld> <def>Little images or figures of earthenware exposed for
sale, or given as presents, on the last two days of the Saturnalia;
hence, the last two, or the sixth and seventh, days of the
Saturnalia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigillaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sig`il*la"ri*a</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fem sing. fr. L.
<ets>sigillum</ets> a seal.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of
fossil trees principally found in the coal formation; -- so named from
the seallike leaf scars in vertical rows on the surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigillarid</ent><br/
<hw>Sig`il*la"rid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>One of an extinct family of cryptagamous trees, including the genus
<gen>Sigillaria</gen> and its allies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigillated</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"il*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sigillatus</ets> adorned with little images.]</ety> <def>Decorated
by means of stamps; -- said of pottery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigillative</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"il*la*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sigillum</ets> a seal: cf. OF. <ets>sigillatif</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Fit to seal; belonging to a seal; composed of wax.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigillum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*gil"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sigilla</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. & Old
Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A seal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sig"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>The signs, abbreviations, letters, or characters standing for
words, shorthand, etc., in ancient manuscripts, or on coins, medals,
etc.</def> <rj><au>W. Savage.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sig"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sigmas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., from Gr. <?/<?/<?/,
<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <def>The Greek letter <SIGMA/, <sigma/, or <sigmat/
(English <it>S</it>, or <it>s</it>). It originally had the form of the
English C.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigmodont</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"mo*dont</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
sigma (<?/) + <?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/, a tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of a tribe (<fam>Sigmodontes</fam>) of rodents which
includes all the indigenous rats and mice of America. So called from the
form of the ridges of enamel on the crowns of the worn molars. Also used
adjectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigmoidal</ent><br/
<ent>Sigmoid</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sig"moid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sig*moid"al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/; <?/<?/<?/ sigma
+ <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form, likeness: cf. F.
<ets>sigmo<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <def>Curved in two directions, like the
letter S, or the Greek <sigmat/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sigmoid flexure</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
last curve of the colon before it terminates in the rectum.</cd> --
<col><b>Sigmoid valves</b></col>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Semilunar valves</cref>, under <er>Semilunar</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sigmoidally</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*moid"al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sigmoidal
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1339 --></p>

<p><ent>Sign</ent><br/
<hw>Sign</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>signe</ets>, L.
<ets>signum</ets>; cf. AS. <ets>segen</ets>, <ets>segn</ets>, a sign,
standard, banner, also fr. L. <ets>signum</ets>. Cf. <er>Ensign</er>,
<er>Resign</er>, <er>Seal</er> a stamp, <er>Signal</er>,
<er>Signet</er>.]</ety> <def>That by which anything is made known or
represented; that which furnishes evidence; a mark; a token; an
indication; a proof.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A remarkable
event, considered by the ancients as indicating the will of some deity;
a prodigy; an omen.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An event considered by the
Jews as indicating the divine will, or as manifesting an interposition
of the divine power for some special end; a miracle; a wonder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Through mighty <qex>signs</qex> and wonders, by the power of the
Spirit of God.</q> <rj><qau>Rom. xv. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It shall come to pass, if they will not believe thee, neither
hearken to the voice of the first <qex>sign</qex>, that they will
believe the voice of the latter <qex>sign</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. iv.
8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>Something serving to indicate the existence, or
preserve the memory, of a thing; a token; a memorial; a
monument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What time the fire devoured two hundred and fifty men, and they
became a <qex>sign</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Num. xxvi. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>Any symbol or emblem which prefigures, typifies, or
represents, an idea; a type; hence, sometimes, a picture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The holy symbols, or <qex>signs</qex>, are not barely
significative; but what they represent is as certainly delivered to us
as the symbols themselves.</q> <rj><qau>Brerewood.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Saint George of Merry England, the <qex>sign</qex> of victory.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <def>A word or a character regarded as the outward
manifestation of thought; as, words are the <xex>sign</xex> of
ideas.</def> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>A motion, an action, or a gesture by
which a thought is expressed, or a command or a wish made
known.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They made <qex>signs</qex> to his father, how he would have him
called.</q> <rj><qau>Luke i. 62.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(g)</sd> <def>Hence, one of the gestures of pantomime, or of a
language of a signs such as those used by the North American Indians, or
those used by the deaf and dumb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Educaters of the deaf distinguish between <xex>natural
signs</xex>, which serve for communicating ideas, and
<xex>methodical</xex>, or <xex>systematic</xex>, <xex>signs</xex>,
adapted for the dictation, or the rendering, of written language, word
by word; and thus the <xex>signs</xex> are to be distinguished from the
<xex>manual alphabet</xex>, by which words are spelled on the
fingers.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(h)</sd> <def>A military emblem carried on a banner or a
standard.</def> <au>Milton.</au> <sd>(i)</sd> <def>A lettered board, or
other conspicuous notice, placed upon or before a building, room, shop,
or office to advertise the business there transacted, or the name of the
person or firm carrying it on; a publicly displayed token or
notice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The shops were, therefore, distinguished by painted
<qex>signs</qex>, which gave a gay and grotesque aspect to the
streets.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(j)</sd> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The twelfth part of the
ecliptic or zodiac.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>signs</xex> are reckoned from the point of
intersection of the ecliptic and equator at the vernal equinox, and are
named, respectively, <stype>Aries</stype> (<Aries/),
<stype>Taurus</stype> (<Taurus/), <stype>Gemini</stype> (II),
<stype>Cancer</stype> (<Cancer/), <stype>Leo</stype> (<Leo/),
<stype>Virgo</stype> (<Virgo/), <stype>Libra</stype> (<Libra/),
<stype>Scorpio</stype> (<Scorpio/), <stype>Sagittarius</stype>
(<Sagittarius/), <stype>Capricornus </stype> (<Capricorn/),
<stype>Aquarius</stype> (<Aquarius/), <stype>Pisces</stype> (<Pisces/).
These names were originally the names of the constellations occupying
severally the divisions of the zodiac, by which they are still retained;
but, in consequence of the procession of the equinoxes, the signs have,
in process of time, become separated about 30 degrees from these
constellations, and each of the latter now lies in the sign next in
advance, or to the east of the one which bears its name, as the
constellation Aries in the sign Taurus, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(k)</sd> <fld>(Alg.)</fld> <def>A character indicating the
relation of quantities, or an operation performed upon them; <as>as, the
<ex>sign</ex> + (plus); the <ex>sign</ex> -- (minus); the <ex>sign</ex>
of division <divide/, and the like</as>.</def> <sd>(l)</sd>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An objective evidence of disease; that is, one
appreciable by some one other than the patient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The terms <xex>symptom</xex> and and <xex>sign</xex> are
often used synonymously; but they may be discriminated. A
<xex>sign</xex> differs from a <xex>symptom</xex> in that the latter is
perceived only by the patient himself. The term <xex>sign</xex> is often
further restricted to the purely local evidences of disease afforded by
direct examination of the organs involved, as distinguished from those
evidence of general disturbance afforded by observation of the
temperature, pulse, etc. In this sense it is often called <xex>physical
sign</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(m)</sd> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Any character, as a flat, sharp,
dot, etc.</def> <sd>(n)</sd> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>That which, being
external, stands for, or signifies, something internal or spiritual; --
a term used in the Church of England in speaking of an ordinance
considered with reference to that which it represents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An outward and visible <qex>sign</qex> of an inward and spiritual
grace.</q> <rj><qau>Bk. of Common Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ See the Table of <er>Arbitrary Signs</er>, p.
1924.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sign manual</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>
<cd>The royal signature superscribed at the top of bills of grants and
letter patent, which are then sealed with the privy signet or great
seal, as the case may be, to complete their validity</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>The signature of one's name in one's own handwriting.</cd>
<au>Craig. Tomlins. Wharton.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Token; mark; note; symptom; indication; signal;
symbol; type; omen; prognostic; presage; manifestation. See
<er>Emblem</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sign</ent><br/
<hw>Sign</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Signed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Signing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>seinen</ets> to bless,
originally, to make the sign of the cross over; in this sense fr. ASS.
<ets>segnian</ets> (from <ets>segn</ets>, n.), or OF.
<ets>seignier</ets>, F. <ets>signer</ets>, to mark, to sign (in sense
3), fr. L. <ets>signare</ets> to mark, set a mark upon, from
<ets>signum</ets>. See <er>Sign</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To represent by a sign; to make known in a typical or emblematic
manner, in distinction from speech; to signify.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>signed</qex> to Browne to make his retreat.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a sign upon; to mark with a sign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We receive this child into the congregation of Christ's flock, and
do <qex>sign</qex> him with the sign of the cross.</q> <rj><qau>Bk. of
Com Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To affix a signature to; to ratify by hand or seal;
to subscribe in one's own handwriting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Inquire the Jew's house out, give him this deed,<br/
And let him <qex>sign</qex> it.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To assign or convey formally; -- used with
<xex>away</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To mark; to make distinguishable.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sign</ent><br/
<hw>Sign</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be a
sign or omen.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a sign or signal; to communicate directions
or intelligence by signs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Especially:</specif> <def>To communicate in
<er>sign language</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To write one's name, esp. as a token of assent,
responsibility, or obligation; <as>as, he <ex>signed</ex> in red
ink</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signable</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suitable to be
signed; requiring signature; <as>as, a legal document <ex>signable</ex>
by a particular person</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signal</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. LL.
<ets>signale</ets>, fr. L. <ets>signum</ets>. See <er>Sign</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sign made for the purpose of
giving notice to a person of some occurrence, command, or danger; also,
a sign, event, or watchword, which has been agreed upon as the occasion
of concerted action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All obeyed<br/
The wonted <qex>signal</qex> and superior voice<br/
Of this great potentate.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A token; an indication; a foreshadowing; a sign;
anything taken as evidence of some process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>The weary sun . . . <br/
Gives <qex>signal</qex> of a goodly day to-morrow.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was not the least <qex>signal</qex> of the calamity to be
seen.</q> <rj><qau>De Foc.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <fld>(Electronics)</fld> <def>A
measurable electrical quantity, such as voltage or current, that conveys
information by varying in magnitude over time; <as>as, the
<ex>signals</ex> from the strongest commercial radio stations can be
received over hundreds of miles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signal to noise</ent><br/
<ent>Signal to noise ratio</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Sig"nal to noise" ra`ti*o</hw>, <hw>Sig"nal to
noise"</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Electronics,
Information science, Experimental science)</fld> <def>The ratio of the
intensity of a signal{2} to the background noise detected by a measuring
instrument, especially in a communications channel; the higher the
ratio, the more accurately the information contained can be interpreted.
The term is applied not only to human communications, but to the
detection of information in any system that is being studied to gain
knowledge; <as>as, the <ex>signal-to-noise ratio</ex> of light signals
in older optic fibers drops to an unusable level after several
miles.</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signal</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nal</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>signal</ets>, n.: cf. F.
<ets>signal<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Noticeable;
distinguished from what is ordinary; eminent; remarkable; memorable;
<as>as, a <ex>signal</ex> exploit; a <ex>signal</ex> service; a
<ex>signal</ex> act of benevolence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As <qex>signal</qex> now in low, dejected state<br/
As erst in highest, behold him where he lies.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to signals, or the use of signals
in conveying information; <as>as, a <ex>signal</ex> flag or
officer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The signal service</b></col>, <cd>a bureau of the
government (in the United States connected with the War Department)
organized to collect from the whole country simultaneous raports of
local meteorological conditions, upon comparison of which at the central
office, predictions concerning the weather are telegraphed to various
sections, where they are made known by signals publicly displayed.</cd>
-- <col><b>Signal station</b></col>, <cd>the place where a signal is
displayed; specifically, an observation office of the signal
service.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Eminent; remarkable; memorable; extraordinary;
notable; conspicuous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signal</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nal</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Signaled</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr> <or/ <conjf>Signalled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Signaling</conjf> or
<conjf>Signalling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To communicate by
signals; <as>as, to <ex>signal</ex> orders</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To notify by a signals; to make a signal or signals
to; <as>as, to <ex>signal</ex> a fleet to anchor</as>.</def> <rj><au>M.
Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signalist</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nal*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes signals; one who
communicates intelligence by means of signals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signality</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*nal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being signal or remarkable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signalize</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nal*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Signalized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Signalizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From
<er>Signal</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make signal or
eminent; to render distinguished from what is common; to
distinguish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is this passion which drives men to all the ways we see in use
of <qex>signalizing</qex> themselves.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To communicate with by means of a signal; <as>as, a
ship <ex>signalizes</ex> its consort</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To indicate the existence, presence, or fact of, by
a signal; <as>as, to <ex>signalize</ex> the arrival of a
steamer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signally</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a signal manner;
eminently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signalman</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nal*man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-men</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A man whose business is to
manage or display signals; especially, one employed in setting the
signals by which railroad trains are run or warned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signalment</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nal*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
signaling, or of signalizing; hence, description by peculiar,
appropriate, or characteristic marks.</def> <rj><au>Mrs.
Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signate</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>signatus</ets>, p. p. See <er>Sign</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having definite color markings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signation</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>signatio</ets>. See <er>Sign</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Sign given; marking.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"na*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>signatorius</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating to a seal; used
in sealing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Signing; joining or sharing in a signature; <as>as,
<ex>signatory</ex> powers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"na*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-ries</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A signer; one who signs or subscribes;
<as>as, a conference of <ex>signatories</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signature</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"na*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. (cf. It.
<ets>signatura</ets>, <ets>segnatura</ets>, Sp. & LL.
<ets>signatura</ets>), from L. <ets>signare</ets>, <ets>signatum</ets>.
See <er>Sign</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sign,
stamp, or mark impressed, as by a seal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The brain, being well furnished with various traces,
<qex>signatures</qex>, and images.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The natural and indelible <qex>signature</qex> of God, which human
souls . . . are supposed to be stamped with.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, the name of any person, written with his
own hand, employed to signify that the writing which precedes accords
with his wishes or intentions; a sign manual; an autograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An outward mark by which
internal characteristics were supposed to be indicated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some plants bear a very evident <qex>signature</qex> of their
nature and use.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Old Med.)</fld> <def>A resemblance between the
external characters of a disease and those of some physical agent, for
instance, that existing between the red skin of scarlet fever and a red
cloth; -- supposed to indicate this agent in the treatment of the
disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The designation of the key (when
not C major, or its relative, A minor) by means of one or more sharps or
flats at the beginning of the staff, immediately after the clef,
affecting all notes of the same letter throughout the piece or movement.
Each minor key has the same signature as its relative major.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A letter or figure
placed at the bottom of the first page of each sheet of a book or
pamphlet, as a direction to the binder in arranging and folding the
sheets.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The printed sheet so marked, or the form
from which it is printed; <as>as, to reprint one or more
<ex>signatures</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Star signatures (as A*, 1*) are the same characters,
with the addition of asterisks, used on the first pages of offcuts, as
in 12mo sheets.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Pharm.)</fld> <def>That part of a prescription
which contains the directions to the patient. It is usually prefaced by
<it>S</it> or <it>Sig.</it> (an abbreviation for the Latin
<it>signa</it>, imperative of <it>signare</it> to sign or
mark).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signature</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"na*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To mark with,
or as with, a signature or signatures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signaturist</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"na*tur`ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who holds
to the doctrine of signatures impressed upon objects, indicative of
character or qualities.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signboard</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A board, placed on
or before a shop, office, etc., on which some notice is given, as the
name of a firm, of a business, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signer</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who signs or
subscribes his name; <as>as, a memorial with a hundred
<ex>signers</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signet</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>signet</ets>
a signet, F., a bookmark, dim. of <ets>signe</ets>. See <er>Sign</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and cf. <er>Sennet</er>.]</ety> <def>A seal; especially,
in England, the seal used by the sovereign in sealing private letters
and grants that pass by bill under the sign manual; -- called also
<altname>privy signet</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had my father's <qex>signet</qex> in my purse.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Signet ring</b></col>, <cd>a ring containing a signet or
private seal.</cd> -- <col><b>Writer to the signet</b></col> <fld>(Scots
Law)</fld>, <cd>a judicial officer who prepares warrants, writs, etc.;
originally, a clerk in the office of the secretary of
state.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signeted</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"net*ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stamped or marked with a
signet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signifer</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"ni*fer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L., from
<ets>signum</ets> sign + <ets>ferre</ets> to bear.]</ety> <def>Bearing
signs.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>signifer</xex> sphere,
or zodiac.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significancy</ent><br/
<ent>Significance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sig*nif"i*cance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sig*nif"i*can*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>significantia</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state
of being significant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is signified; meaning; import; <as>as,
the <ex>significance</ex> of a nod, of a motion of the hand, or of a
word or expression</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Importance; moment; weight; consequence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With this brain I must work, in order to give
<qex>significancy</qex> and value to the few facts which I possess.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significant</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*nif"i*cant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>significans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>significare</ets>. See <er>Signify</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Fitted or designed to signify or make known something having a
meaning; standing as a sign or token; expressive or suggestive; <as>as,
a <ex>significant</ex> word or sound; a <ex>significant</ex>
look</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was well said of Plotinus, that the stars were
<qex>significant</qex>, but not efficient.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Deserving to be considered; important; momentous;
<as>as, a <ex>significant</ex> event</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Significant figures</b></col> <fld>(Arith.)</fld>,
<cd>the figures which remain to any number, or decimal fraction, after
the ciphers at the right or left are canceled. <as>Thus, the
<ex>significant figures</ex> of 25,000, or of .0025, are
25</as>.</cd></cs><-- to (n) significant figures --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significant</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*nif"i*cant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which has significance;
a sign; a token; a symbol.</def> <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In dumb <qex>significants</qex> proclaim your thoughts.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significantly</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*nif"i*cant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a significant
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significate</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*nif"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>significatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>significare</ets>. See
<er>Signify</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>One of several things
signified by a common term.</def> <rj><au>Whately.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signification</ent><br/
<hw>Sig`ni*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>signification</ets>, L. <ets>significatio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of signifying; a making known by signs or other
means.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>signification</qex> of being pleased.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All speaking or <qex>signification</qex> of one's mind implies an
act or addres of one man to another.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is signified or made known; that meaning
which a sign, character, or token is intended to convey; <as>as, the
<ex>signification</ex> of words</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significative</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*nif"i*ca*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>significativus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>significatif</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Betokening or representing by an external
sign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The holy symbols or signs are not barely
<qex>significative</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Brerewood.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having signification or meaning; expressive of a
meaning or purpose; significant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Neither in the degrees of kindred they were destitute of
<qex>significative</qex> words.</q> <rj><qau>Camden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sig*nif"i*ca*tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sig*nif"i*ca*tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significator</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"ni*fi*ca`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>significateur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which,
signifies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In this diagram there was one <qex>significator</qex> which
pressed remarkably upon our astrologer's attention.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sig*nif"i*ca*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>significatorius</ets>.]</ety> <def>Significant.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is significatory.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Significavit</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sig`ni*fi*ca"vit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., (he)
has signified, perf. ind. of <ets>significare</ets> to signify.]</ety>
<fld>(Eng. Eccl. Law)</fld> <def>Formerly, a writ issuing out of
chancery, upon certificate given by the ordinary, of a man's standing
excommunicate by the space of forty days, for the laying him up in
prison till he submit himself to the authority of the church.</def>
<rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signify</ent><br/
<hw>Sig"ni*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Signified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Signifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>signifier</ets>, L. <ets>significare</ets>; <ets>signum</ets> a
sign + <ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.) to make. See <er>Sign</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and <er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To show by a
sign; to communicate by any conventional token, as words, gestures,
signals, or the like; to announce; to make known; to declare; to
express; <as>as, a <ex>signified</ex> his desire to be
present</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I 'll to the king; and <qex>signify</qex> to him<br/
That thus I have resign'd my charge to you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The government should <qex>signify</qex> to the Protestants of
Ireland that want of silver is not to be remedied.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mean; to import; to denote; to betoken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He bade her tell him what it <qex>signified</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A tale<br/
Told by an idiot, full of sound and fury,<br/
<qex>Signifying</qex> nothing.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Signify</xex> is often used impersonally; as, it
<xex>signifies</xex> nothing, it does not <xex>signify</xex>, that is,
it is of no importance.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To express; manifest; declare; utter; intimate;
betoken; denote; imply; mean.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>signing</ent><br/
<hw>sign"ing</hw> <pr>(s<imac/n"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Participle of <er>sign</er>, sense 3.]</ety> <def>The procedure or
process of communicating by use of a <er>sign language</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signior</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"ior</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sir; Mr. The English
form and pronunciation for the Italian <xex>Signor</xex> and the Spanish
<xex>Se<ntil/or</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signiorize</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"ior*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Seigniorize</er>.]</ety> <def>To exercise dominion over; to lord it
over.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1340 --></p>

<p><ent>Signiorize</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"ior*ize</hw> <pr>(s<emac/n"y<etil/r*<imac/z)</pr>, <pos>v.
i.</pos> <def>To exercise dominion; to seigniorize.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hewyt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signiorship</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"ior*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State or position of a
signior.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signiory</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"ior*y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Seigniory</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sign language</ent><br/
<hw>sign" lang`uage</hw> <pr>(s<imac/n" l<acr/ng`w<asl/j)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A form of language for communicating by use of
gestures made by the hands, rather than by speech. It includes alphabets
made by hand gestures, as well as proper languages formed from signs.
Among the latter is the <stype>American Sign Language</stype> (ASL),
used by the deaf. See also <er>dactylology</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sign off</ent><br/
<hw>sign` off"</hw> <pr>(s<imac/n` <add/f")</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<def>To terminate a communication, especially one conducted by radio
waves; to terminate a broadcast over radio or television; <as>as, this
is your reporter in Cairo, <ex>signing off</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sign on</ent><br/
<hw>sign` on"</hw> <pr>(s<imac/n` <ocr/n")</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<def>To begin a communication, especially one conducted by radio
waves.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signore</ent><br/
<ent>Signor</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Si*gnor"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Si*gno"re</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. See
<er>Seignior</er>.]</ety> <def>Sir; Mr.; -- a title of address or
respect among the Italians. Before a noun the form is
<xex>Signor</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*gno"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>Madam; Mrs; -- a title of address or respect among the
Italians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signorina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si`gno*ri"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>Miss; -- a title of address among the Italians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Signpost</ent><br/
<hw>Sign"post`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A post on which a
sign hangs, or on which papers are placed to give public notice of
anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sike</ent><br/
<ent>Sik</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sik</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Such. See <er>Such</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Sike</xex> fancies weren foolerie.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sike</ent><br/
<hw>Sike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<imac/c</ets>.
Cf. <er>Sig</er>.]</ety> <def>A gutter; a stream, such as is usually dry
in summer.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sike</ent><br/
<hw>Sike</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sick</er>.]</ety> <def>A sick
person.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sike</ent><br/
<hw>Sike</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To sigh.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That for his wife weepeth and <qex>siketh</qex> sore.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sike</ent><br/
<hw>Sike</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sigh.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sikerness</ent><br/
<ent>Sikerly</ent><br/
<ent>Siker</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sik"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos>,
<hw>Sik"er*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos>, <hw>Sik"er*ness</hw>,
<pos>n.</pos>, etc.}</mhw> <def>See 2d <er>Sicker</er>,
<er>Sickerly</er>, etc.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sikhs</ent><br/
<hw>Sikhs</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>; <sing>sing.
<singw>Sikh</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <ety>[Hind. <ets>Sikh</ets>,
properly, a disciple.]</ety> <def>A religious sect noted for warlike
traits, founded in the Punjab at the end of the 15th century.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silage</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>Short for
<er>Ensilage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sile</ent><br/
<hw>Sile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Akin to Sw.
<ets>sila</ets> to strain, <ets>sil</ets> sieve, G. <ets>sielen</ets> to
draw away or lead off water. <root/151<it>a.</it> See
<er>Silt</er>.]</ety> <def>To strain, as fresh milk.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sile</ent><br/
<hw>Sile</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To drop; to flow; to fall.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sile</ent><br/
<hw>Sile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sieve with fine
meshes.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Filth; sediment.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sile</ent><br/
<hw>Sile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>s<imac/ld</ets> herring;
akin to Sw. <ets>sill</ets>, Dan. <ets>sild</ets>. Cf. <er>Sill</er> the
young of a herring.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A young or small
herring.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Pennant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silence</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>silentium</ets>. See <er>Silent</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of being silent; entire absence of sound
or noise; absolute stillness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I saw and heared; for such a numerous host<br/
Fled not in <qex>silence</qex> through the frighted deep.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Forbearance from, or absence of, speech;
taciturnity; muteness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Secrecy; <as>as, these things were transacted in
<ex>silence</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The administration itself keeps a profound <qex>silence</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>D. Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The cessation of rage, agitation, or tumult;
calmness; quiet; <as>as, the elements were reduced to
<ex>silence</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Absence of mention; oblivion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And what most merits fame, in <qex>silence</qex> hid.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silence</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lence</hw>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>Be silent; -- used
elliptically for <xex>let there be silence</xex>, or <xex>keep
silence</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silence</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lence</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Silenced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Silencing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
compel to silence; to cause to be still; to still; to hush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Silence</qex> that dreadful bell; it frights the isle.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put to rest; to quiet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This would <qex>silence</qex> all further opposition.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These would have <qex>silenced</qex> their scruples.</q>
<rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To restrain from the exercise of any function,
privilege of instruction, or the like, especially from the act of
preaching; <as>as, to <ex>silence</ex> a minister of the
gospel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Rev. Thomas Hooker of Chelmsford, in Essex, was
<qex>silenced</qex> for nonconformity.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Trumbull.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to cease firing, as by a vigorous
cannonade; <as>as, to <ex>silence</ex> the batteries of an
enemy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silencer</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lenc*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One that
silences;</def> <specif>specif.</specif>: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The muffler
of an internal-combustion engine.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any of various
devices to silence the humming noise of telegraph wires.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A device for silencing the report of a firearm
shooting its projectiles singly, as a tubular attachment for the muzzle
having circular plates that permit the passage of the projectile but
impart a rotary motion to, and thus retard, the exploding
gases.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silene</ent><br/
<hw>Si*le"ne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>Silenus</ets>, the attendant of Bacchus.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A genus of caryophyllaceous plants, usually covered with a viscid
secretion by which insects are caught; catchfly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bon Sil<egrave/ne</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Sil<egrave/ne</er>, in the Vocabulary.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silent</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>silens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>silere</ets> to be silent; akin to
Goth. ana-silan.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Free from sound or noise;
absolutely still; perfectly quiet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How <qex>silent</qex> is this town!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not speaking; indisposed to talk; speechless; mute;
taciturn; not loquacious; not talkative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ulysses, adds he, was the most eloquent and most <qex>silent</qex>
of men.</q> <rj><qau>Broome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This new-created world, whereof in hell<br/
Fame is not <qex>silent</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Keeping at rest; inactive; calm; undisturbed;
<as>as, the wind is <ex>silent</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Parnell. Sir W.
Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Pron.)</fld> <def>Not pronounced; having no sound;
quiescent; <as>as, <it>e</it> is <ex>silent</ex> in
<ldquo/fable.<rdquo/</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Having no effect; not operating; inefficient.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cause . . . <qex>silent</qex>, virtueless, and dead.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Silent partner</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Dormant
partner</cref>, under <er>Dormant</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Mute; taciturn; dumb; speechless; quiet; still.
See <er>Mute</er>, and <er>Taciturn</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silent</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is silent; a time of
silence.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>silent</xex> of the
night.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silentiary</ent><br/
<hw>Si*len"ti*a*ry</hw> <pr>(s<isl/*l<ecr/n"sh<icr/*<asl/*r<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>silentiarius</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>silenciaire</ets>. See <er>Silence</er>.]</ety> <def>One appointed
to keep silence and order in court; also, one sworn not to divulge
secrets of state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silentious</ent><br/
<hw>Si*len"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>silentiosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>silencieux</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Habitually silent; taciturn; reticent.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silently</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lent*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a silent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silentness</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lent*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State of being silent;
silence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silenus</ent><br/
<hw>Si*le"nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Silenus</ets> the tutor and attendant of Bacchus.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Wanderoo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silesia</ent><br/
<hw>Si*le"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind
of linen cloth, originally made in <xex>Silesia</xex>, a province of
Prussia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A twilled cotton fabric, used for dress
linings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silesian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*le"si*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to Silesia.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Silesia.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silex</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a finit, a
pebblestone.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Silica, <chform>SiO2</chform>
as found in nature, constituting quarz, and most sands and sandstones.
See <er>Silica</er>, and <er>Silicic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silhouette</ent><br/
<hw>Sil`hou*ette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.; -- so
called from Etienne de <ets>Silhoutte</ets>, a French minister of
finance in 1759, whise diversion it was to make such portraits on the
walls of his apartments.]</ety> <def>A representation of the outlines of
an object filled in with a black color; a profile portrait in black,
such as a shadow appears to be.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silhouette</ent><br/
<hw>Sil`hou*ette"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To represent by a
silhouette; to project upon a background, so as to be like a
silhouette.</def> <mark>[Recent]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A flock of roasting vultures <qex>silhouetted</qex> on the
sky.</q> <rj><qau>The Century.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silica</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from L.
<ets>silex</ets>, <ets>silics</ets>, a flint.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Silicon dioxide, SiO<?/. It constitutes ordinary quartz (also opal
and tridymite), and is artificially prepared as a very fine, white,
tasteless, inodorous powder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicate</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>silicate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of silicic
acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In mineralogical chemistry the silicates include; the
<xex>unisilicates</xex> or <xex>orthosilicates</xex>, salts of
orthosilicic acid; the <xex>bisilicates</xex> or
<xex>metasilicates</xex>, salts of metasilicic acid; the
<xex>polysilicates</xex> or <xex>acid silicates</xex>, salts of the
polysilicic acids; the <xex>basic silicates</xex> or
<xex>subsilicates</xex>, in which the equivalent of base is greater than
would be required to neutralize the acid; and the <xex>hydrous
silicates</xex>, including the zeolites and many hydrated decomposition
products.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicated</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*ca`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Combined or impregnated with silicon or silica; <as>as,
<ex>silicated</ex> hydrogen; <ex>silicated</ex> rocks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Silicated soap</b></col>, <cd>a hard soap containing
silicate of soda.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicatization</ent><br/
<hw>Sil`i*ca*ti*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Silicification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*lic"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Silicoidea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliceous</ent><br/
<hw>Si*li"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>siliceus</ets>, fr. <ets>silex</ets>, <ets>silicis</ets>, a
flint.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to silica; containing silica, or
partaking of its nature.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>silisious</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicic</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>silex</ets>, <ets>silicis</ets>, a flint: cf. F.
<ets>silicique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to,
derived from, or resembling, silica; specifically, designating compounds
of silicon; <as>as, <ex>silicic</ex> acid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Silicic acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an
amorphous gelatinous substance, <chform>Si(HO)4</chform>, very unstable
and easily dried to silica, but forming many stable salts; -- called
also <altname>orthosilicic acid</altname> <it>or</it> <altname>normal
silicic acid</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicicalcareous</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic`i*cal*ca"re*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Consisting of silica and calcareous matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicide</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*cide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A binary compound of silicon, or one regarded as binary.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Hydrogen silicide</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
colorless, spontaneously inflammable gas, <chform>SiH4</chform>,
produced artifically from silicon, and analogous to methane; -- called
also <altname>silico-methane</altname>, <altname>silicon
hydride</altname>, and formerly <altname>siliciureted
hydrogen</altname>.</cd></cs><-- now called silane, silicon hydride, or
silicane. The term silane is used as the theoretical parent compound of
a large series of derivatives in which one or more of the hydrogens are
substituted; the term is also used generically to refer to any one of a
large series of silicon compounds, including a series containing
silicon-silicon bonds, analogous to the compounds containing carbon.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliciferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sil`i*cif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>silex</ets>, <ets>silicis</ets>, a flint +
<ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Producing silica; united with
silica.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicification</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Silicify</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Thae act or process of
combining or impregnating with silicon or silica; the state of being so
combined or impregnated; <as>as, the <ex>silicification</ex> of
wood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicified</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic"i*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Combined or impregnated with silicon or silica, especially the
latter; <as>as, <ex>silicified</ex> wood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicify</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Silicified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Silicifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>silex</ets>, <ets>silicis</ets>, a flint + <ets>-fy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>silicifier</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To convert into,
or to impregnate with, silica, or with the compounds of
silicon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The specimens found . . . are completely
<qex>silicified</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Say.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The silica may take the form of agate, chalcedony,
flint, hornstone, or crystalline quartz.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicify</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic"i*fy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become converted into
silica, or to be impregnated with silica.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicioidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sil`i*ci*oi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Silicoidea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicious</ent><br/
<hw>Si*li"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Siliceous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicispongiae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sil`i*ci*spon"gi*ae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Silex</er>, and <er>Sponge</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Silicoidea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicited</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic"it*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Silicified.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicium</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Silicon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliciureted</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic"i*u*ret`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>siliciuretted</asp>.]</altsp> <fld>(Old. Chem.)</fld>
<def>Combined or impregnated with silicon.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Siliciureted hydrogen</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<cd>Hydrogen silicide.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicle</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>silicula</ets>, dim. of <ets>siliqua</ets> a pod or husk: cf. F.
<ets>silicule</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A seed vessel
resembling a silique, but about as broad as it is long. See
<er>Silique</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silico-</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*co-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A combining
form (also used adjectively) denoting <xex>the presence of silicon</xex>
or <xex>its compounds</xex>; <as>as, <ex>silico</ex>benzoic,
<ex>silico</ex>fluoride, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicofluoric</ent><br/
<hw>Sil`i*co*flu*or"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Containing, or composed of, silicon and
fluorine; especially, denoting the compounds called
<xex>silicofluorides</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Silicofluoric acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
compound of hydrofluoric acid and silicon fluoride, known only in watery
solution. It is produced by the action of silicon fluoride on water, and
is regarded as an acid, <chform>H2SiF6</chform>, and the type and origin
of the silicofluorides.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicofluoride</ent><br/
<hw>Sil`i*co*flu"or*ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A fluosilicate; a salt of silicofluoric
acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicoidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sil`i*coi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Silex</er>, and <er>-oid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
extensive order of Porifera, which includes those that have the skeleton
composed mainly of siliceous fibers or spicules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicon</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Silica</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A nonmetalic element
analogous to carbon. It always occurs combined in nature, and is
artificially obtained in the free state, usually as a dark brown
amorphous powder, or as a dark crystalline substance with a meetallic
luster. Its oxide is silica, or common quartz, and in this form, or as
silicates, it is, next to oxygen, the most abundant element of the
earth's crust. Silicon is characteristically the element of the mineral
kingdom, as carbon is of the organic world. Symbol Si. Atomic weight 28.
Called also <altname>silicium</altname>.</def> <-- it is used as the
basis for the most common type of transistors, in the form of a highly
purified silicon impregnated with small quantities of imourities such as
phosphorus or antimony, giving it special semiconductor properties. In
this application, it forms the primary basis for the modern (post-1970)
electronics industry, with integrated circuits containing millions of
electronic componnets being imprinted by special processes on silicon
chips less than one inch in diameter. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicotungstic</ent><br/
<hw>Sil`i*co*tung"stic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, any one of a
series of double acids of silicon and tungsten, known in the free state,
and also in their salts (called <xex>silicotungstates</xex>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*lic"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A silicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silicule</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*cule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
silicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliculose</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lic"u*lose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>siliculosus</ets>, fr. L. <ets>silicula</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>siliculeux</ets>. See <er>Silicle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Bearing silicles; pertaining to, or resembling,
silicles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Full of, or consisting of, husks; husky.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliginose</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lig"i*nose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>siligineus</ets>, fr. <ets>siligo</ets>, <ets>-inis</ets>, fine and
very white wheat.]</ety> <def>Made of fine wheat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siling</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Sile</er> to strain.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Siling dish</b></col>, <cd>a colander.</cd> <mark>[Obs.
or Prov. Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliqua</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*qua</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Siliquae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. See
<er>Silique</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Silique</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A weight of four grains; a carat; -- a term used by
jewelers, and refiners of gold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silique</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ique</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>siliqua</ets> a pod or husk, a very small weight or measure: cf. F.
<ets>silique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An oblong or elongated
seed vessel, consisting of two valves with a dissepiment between, and
opening by sutures at either margin. The seeds are attached to both
edges of the dissepiment, alternately upon each side of it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliquiform</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"i*qui*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Silique</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>siliquiforme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the form
of a silique.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliquosa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sil`i*quo"sa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Siliquose</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Linnaean order of
plants including those which bear siliques.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siliquous</ent><br/
<ent>Siliquose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sil"i*quose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sil"i*quous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>siliquosus</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>siliqueux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Bearing siliques;
<as>as, <ex>siliquose</ex> plants</as>; pertaining to, or resembling,
siliques; <as>as, <ex>siliquose</ex> capsules</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silk</ent><br/
<hw>Silk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>silk</ets>,
<ets>selk</ets>, AS. <ets>seolc</ets>, <ets>seoloc</ets>; akin to Icel.
<ets>silki</ets>, SW. & Dan. <ets>silke</ets>; prob. through Slavic from
an Oriental source; cf. Lith. <ets>szilkai</ets>, Russ.
<ets>shelk'</ets>, and also L. <ets>sericum</ets> Seric stuff, silk. Cf.
<er>Sericeous</er>. <er>Serge</er> a woolen stuff.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The fine, soft thread produced by various species of caterpillars
in forming the cocoons within which the worm is inclosed during the pupa
state, especially that produced by the larvae of <spn>Bombyx
mori</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, thread spun, or cloth woven, from the
above-named material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which resembles silk, as the filiform styles of
the female flower of maize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Raw silk</b></col>, <cd>silk as it is wound off from the
cocoons, and before it is manufactured.</cd> -- <col><b>Silk
cotton</b></col>, <cd>a cottony substance enveloping the seeds of the
silk-cotton tree.</cd> -- <col><b>Silk-cotton tree</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name for several tropical trees of the genera
<gen>Bombax</gen> and <gen>Eriodendron</gen>, and belonging to the order
<ord>Bombaceae</ord>. The trees grow to an immense size, and have their
seeds enveloped in a cottony substance, which is used for stuffing
cushions, but can not be spun.</cd> -- <col><b>Silk flower</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The silk tree</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>A similar tree (<spn>Calliandra trinervia</spn>) of Peru.</cd> --
<col><b>Silk fowl</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a breed of domestic
fowls having silky plumage.</cd> -- <col><b>Silk gland</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a gland which secretes the material of silk, as
in spider or a silkworm; a sericterium.</cd> -- <col><b>Silk
gown</b></col>, <cd>the distinctive robe of a barrister who has been
appointed king's or queen's counsel; hence, the counsel himself. Such a
one has precedence over mere barristers, who wear <xex>stuff
gowns</xex>.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Silk grass</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of grass (<spn>Stipa comata</spn>) of the
Western United States, which has very long silky awns. The name is also
sometimes given to various species of the genera <gen>Aqave</gen> and
<gen>Yucca</gen>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silk moth</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the adult moth of any silkworm. See
<er>Silkworm</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silk shag</b></col>, <cd>a coarse,
rough-woven silk, like plush, but with a stiffer nap.</cd> --
<col><b>Silk spider</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large spider
(<spn>Nephila plumipes</spn>), native of the Southern United States,
remarkable for the large quantity of strong silk it produces and for the
great disparity in the sizes of the sexes.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Silk
thrower</b></col>, <col><b>Silk throwster</b></col></mcol>, <cd>one who
twists or spins silk, and prepares it for weaving.</cd> <au>Brande &
C.</au> -- <col><b>Silk tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an Asiatic
leguminous tree (<spn>Albizzia Julibrissin</spn>) with finely bipinnate
leaves, and large flat pods; -- so called because of the abundant long
silky stamens of its blossoms. Also called <altname>silk
flower</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silk vessel</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Silk gland</cref>, above.</cd> --
<col><b>Virginia silk</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a climbing plant
(<spn>Periploca Gr<ae/ca</spn>) of the Milkweed family, having a silky
tuft on the seeds. It is native in Southern Europe.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silken</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>seolcen</ets>, <ets>seolocen</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to silk; made of, or resembling, silk; <as>as,
<ex>silken</ex> cloth; a <ex>silken</ex> veil</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Soft; delicate; tender; smooth; <as>as,
<ex>silken</ex> language</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Silken</xex> terms
precise.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Dressed in silk.</def> <ldquo/A . . .
<xex>silken</xex> wanton.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silken</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"en</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To render silken or
silklike.</def> <rj><au>Dyer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silkiness</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being silky or silken; softness and
smoothness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Effeminacy; weakness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silkman</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Silkmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A dealer in silks; a silk
mercer.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1341 --></p>

<p><ent>Silkness</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Silkiness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silk-stocking</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"-stock`ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wearing silk stockings
(which among men were formerly worn chiefly by the luxurious or
aristocratic); hence, elegantly dressed; aristocratic; luxurious; --
chiefly applied to men, often by way of reproach.</def></p>

<p><q>[They] will find their levees crowded with
<qex>silk-stocking</qex> gentry, but no yeomanry; an army of officers
without soldiers.</q> <rj><qau>Jefferson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silkweed</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genera <gen>Asclepias</gen> and
<gen>Acerates</gen> whose seed vessels contain a long, silky down;
milkweed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silkworm</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>seolcwyrm</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The larva of any
one of numerous species of bombycid moths, which spins a large amount of
strong silk in constructing its cocoon before changing to a
pupa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common species (<spn>Bombyx mori</spn>) feeds on the
leaves of the white mulberry tree. It is native of China, but has long
been introduced into other countries of Asia and Europe, and is reared
on a large scale. In America it is reared only to small extent. The
Ailanthus silkworm (<spn>Philosamia cynthia</spn>) is a much larger
species, of considerable importance, which has been introduced into
Europe and America from China. The most useful American species is the
Polyphemus. See <er>Polyphemus</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pernyi silkworm</b></col>, <cd>the larva of the Pernyi
moth. See <er>Pernyi moth</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silkworm gut</b></col>,
<cd>a substance prepared from the contents of the silk glands of
silkworms and used in making lines for angling. See <er>Gut</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Silkworm rot</b></col>, <cd>a disease of silkworms;
muscardine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silky</ent><br/
<hw>Silk"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Silkier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Silkiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
silk; made of, or resembling, silk; silken; silklike; <as>as, a
<ex>silky</ex> luster</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, soft and smooth; <as>as, <ex>silky</ex>
wine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Covered with soft hairs pressed close to the
surface, as a leaf; sericeous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Silky oak</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a lofty
Australian tree (<spn>Grevillea robusta</spn>) with silky tomentose
lobed or incised leaves. It furnishes a valuable timber.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sill</ent><br/
<hw>Sill</hw> <pr>(s<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sille</ets>, <ets>sylle</ets>, AS. <ets>syl</ets>, <ets>syll</ets>;
akin to G. <ets>schwelle</ets>, OHG. <ets>swelli</ets>, Icel.
<ets>syll</ets>, <ets>svill</ets>, Sw. <ets>syll</ets>, Dan.
<ets>syld</ets>, Goth. <ets>gasuljan</ets> to lay a foundation, to
found.]</ety> <def>The basis or foundation of a thing; especially, a
horizontal piece, as a timber, which forms the lower member of a frame,
or supports a structure; <as>as, the <ex>sills</ex> of a house, of a
bridge, of a loom, and the like</as>.</def> Hence: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
timber or stone at the foot of a door; the threshold.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The timber or stone on which a window frame stands; or, the lowest
piece in a window frame.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The floor of a gallery
or passage in a mine.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A piece of timber across
the bottom of a canal lock for the gates to shut against.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sill course</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a
horizontal course of stone, terra cotta, or the like, built into a wall
at the level of one or more window sills, these sills often forming part
of it.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sill</ent><br/
<hw>Sill</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Thill</er>.]</ety> <def>The
shaft or thill of a carriage.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sill</ent><br/
<hw>Sill</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. 4th <er>Sile</er>.]</ety> <def>A
young herring.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sillabub</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"la*bub</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<ets>sile</ets> to strain, and <ets>bub</ets> liquor, also Prov. E.
<ets>sillibauk</ets>.]</ety> <def>A dish made by mixing wine or cider
with milk, and thus forming a soft curd; also, sweetened cream, flavored
with wine and beaten to a stiff froth.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>syllabub</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siller</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Silver.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sillily</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"li*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Silly</er>.]</ety> <def>In a silly manner; foolishly.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sillimanite</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"li*man*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[After Benjamin
<ets>Siliman</ets>, an American meneralogist.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Fibrolite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silliness</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"li*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
silly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sillock</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"lock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The pollock, or coalfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sillon</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., a furrow.]</ety>
<fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A work raised in the middle of a wide ditch, to
defend it.</def> <rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silly</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sillier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Silliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>seely</ets>,
<ets>sely</ets>, AS. <ets>s<?/lig</ets>, ge<ets>s<?/lig</ets>, happy,
good, fr. <ets>s<?/l</ets>, <ets>s<?/l</ets>, good, happy,
<ets>s<?/l</ets> good fortune, happines; akin to OS.
<ets>s<amac/lig</ets>, a, good, happy, D. <ets>zalig</ets> blessed, G.
<ets>selig</ets>, OHG. <ets>s<amac/l<imac/g</ets>, Icel.
<ets>s<?/l</ets>, Sw. <ets>s<aum/ll</ets>, Dan. <ets>salig</ets>, Goth.
<ets>s<?/ls</ets> good, kind, and perh. also to L. <ets>sollus</ets>
whole, entire, Gr. <?/<?/<?/, Skr. <ets>sarva</ets>. Cf. <er>Seel</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Happy; fortunate; blessed.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Harmless; innocent; inoffensive.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/This <xex>silly</xex>, innocent
Custance.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>silly</qex> virgin strove him to withstand.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>silly</qex>, innocent hare murdered of a dog.</q>
<rj><qau>Robynson (More's Utopia).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Weak; helpless; frail.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>After long storms . . . <br/
With which my <qex>silly</qex> bark was tossed sore.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>silly</qex> buckets on the deck.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Rustic; plain; simple; humble.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A fourth man, in a <qex>silly</qex>habit.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All that did their <qex>silly</qex> thoughts so busy keep.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Weak in intellect; destitute of ordinary strength of
mind; foolish; witless; simple; <as>as, a <ex>silly</ex>
woman</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Proceeding from want of understanding or common
judgment; characterized by weakness or folly; unwise; absurd; stupid;
<as>as, <ex>silly</ex> conduct; a <ex>silly</ex>
question</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Simple; brainless; witless; shallow; foolish;
unwise; indiscreet. See <er>Simple</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sillyhow</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ly*how</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>silly-hew</ets>; cf. AS. <ets>s<aemac/lig</ets> happy, good, and
<ets>h<umac/fe</ets> a cap, hood. See <er>Silly</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>A caul. See <er>Caul</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
3.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silo</ent><br/
<hw>Si"lo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A pit or
vat for packing away green fodder for winter use so as to exclude air
and outside moisture. See <er>Ensilage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silt</ent><br/
<hw>Silt</hw> <pr>(s<icr/lt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>silte</ets> gravel, fr. <ets>silen</ets> to drain, E.
<ets>sile</ets>; probably of Scand. origin; cf. Sw. <ets>sila</ets>,
prob. akin to AS. <ets>se<oacute/n</ets> to filter,
<ets>s<imac/gan</ets> to fall, sink, cause to sink, G. <ets>seihen</ets>
to strain, to filter, OHG. <ets>s<imac/han</ets>, Icel.
<ets>s<imac/a</ets>, Skr. <ets>sic</ets> to pour; cf. Gr.
<grk>'ikma`s</grk> moisture. Cf. <er>Sig</er>, <er>Sile</er>.]</ety>
<def>Mud or fine earth deposited from running or standing
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silt</ent><br/
<hw>Silt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Silted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Silting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To choke, fill, or obstruct with
silt or mud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silt</ent><br/
<hw>Silt</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To flow through crevices; to
percolate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silty</ent><br/
<hw>Silt"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of silt;
resembling silt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silundum</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lun"dum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Trade
name]</mark> <def>A form of silicon carbide, produced in the electric
furnace, possessing great hardness, and high electrical resistance, and
not subject to oxidation below 2880<deg/ F., or 1600<deg/ C.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silure</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lure"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>silurus</ets> a sort of river fish, Gr. <?/<?/<?/: cf. F.
<ets>silure</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A fish of the genus
<gen>Silurus</gen>, as the sheatfish; a siluroid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silurian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lu"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>Silures</ets>, a people who anciently inhabited a part of England
and Wales.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
country of the ancient Silures; -- a term applied to the earliest of the
Paleozoic eras, and also to the strata of the era, because most plainly
developed in that country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The Silurian formation, so named by Murchison, is
divided into the <xex>Upper Silurian</xex> and <xex>Lower
Silurian</xex>. The lower part of the Lower Silurian, with some
underlying beds, is now separated under the name <xex>Cambrian</xex>,
first given by Sedwick. Recently the term <xex>Ordovician</xex> has been
proposed for the Lower Silurian, leawing the original word to apply only
to the Upper Silurian.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silurian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lu"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The Silurian age.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siluridan</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lu"ri*dan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any fish of the family <fam>Siluridae</fam> or of the order
Siluroidei.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siluroid</ent><br/
<hw>Si*lu"roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Silurus</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Belonging to the
Siluroidei, or Nematognathi, an order of fishes including numerous
species, among which are the American catfishes and numerous allied
fresh-water species of the Old World, as the sheatfish (<spn>Silurus
glanis</spn>) of Europe.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A siluroid
fish.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siluroidei</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sil`u*roi"de*i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of fishes, the
Nematognathi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silurus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*lu"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Silure</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of large
malacopterygious fishes of the order Siluroidei. They inhabit the inland
waters of Europe and Asia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silva</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Silvas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Silvae</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., properly, a wood, forest.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sylva</asp>.]</altsp> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The forest trees of a region or country, considered
collectively.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A description or history of the
forest trees of a country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silvan</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"van</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>silva</ets>,
less correctly <ets>sylva</ets>, a wood or grove, perh. akin to Gr.
<grk>"y`lh</grk>; cf. L. <ets>Silvanus</ets> Silvanus the god of woods:
cf. F. <ets>sylvain</ets> silvan. Cf. <er>Savage</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to woods; composed of woods or groves; woody.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sylvan</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Betwixt two rows of rocks, a <qex>silvan</qex> scene<br/
Appears above, and groves forever green.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silvan</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"van</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Sylvanium</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silvanite</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"van*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sylvanite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Selvas</ent><br/
<ent>Silvas</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sil"vas</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <or/ <hw>Sel"vas</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>silva</ets> a forest, Sp.
<ets>selva</ets>.]</ety> <def>Vast woodland plains of South
America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silvate</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"vate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sylvate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silver</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver</hw> <pr>(s<icr/l"v<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>silver</ets>, <ets>selver</ets>, <ets>seolver</ets>, AS.
<ets>seolfor</ets>, <ets>siolfur</ets>, <ets>siolufr</ets>,
<ets>silofr</ets>, <ets>sylofr</ets>; akin to OS. <ets>silubar</ets>,
OFries. <ets>selover</ets>, D. <ets>zilver</ets>, LG. <ets>sulver</ets>,
OHG. <ets>silabar</ets>, <ets>silbar</ets>, G. <ets>silber</ets>, Icel.
<ets>silfr</ets>, Sw. <ets>silfver</ets>, Dan. <ets>s<oum/lv</ets>,
Goth. <ets>silubr</ets>, Russ. <ets>serebro</ets>, Lith.
<ets>sidabras</ets>; of unknown origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A soft white metallic element, sonorous,
ductile, very malleable, and capable of a high degree of polish. It is
found native, and also combined with sulphur, arsenic, antimony,
chlorine, etc., in the minerals argentite, proustite, pyrargyrite,
ceragyrite, etc. Silver is one of the <ldquo/noble<rdquo/ metals,
so-called, not being easily oxidized, and is used for coin, jewelry,
plate, and a great variety of articles. Symbol Ag (<xex>Argentum</xex>).
Atomic weight 107.7. Specific gravity 10.5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Silver was known under the name of <xex>luna</xex> to
the ancients and also to the alchemists. Some of its compounds, as the
halogen salts, are remarkable for the effect of light upon them, and are
used in photography.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Coin made of silver; silver money.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything having the luster or appearance of
silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The color of silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Silver</xex> is used in the formation of many
compounds of obvious meaning; as, <xex>silver</xex>-armed,
<xex>silver</xex>-bright, <xex>silver</xex>-buskined,
<xex>silver</xex>-coated, <xex>silver</xex>-footed,
<xex>silver</xex>-haired, <xex>silver</xex>-headed,
<xex>silver</xex>-mantled, <xex>silver</xex>-plated,
<xex>silver</xex>-slippered, <xex>silver</xex>-sounding,
<xex>silver</xex>-studded, <xex>silver</xex>-tongued,
<xex>silver</xex>-white. See <er>Silver</er>, <pos>a.</pos></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Black silver</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>stephanite;
-- called also <altname>brittle silver ore</altname>, or
<altname>brittle silver glance</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Fulminating
silver</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A black
crystalline substance, <chform>Ag2O.(NH3)2</chform>, obtained by
dissolving silver oxide in aqua ammonia. When dry it explodes violently
on the slightest percussion.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Silver fulminate, a
white crystalline substance, <chform>Ag2C2N2O2</chform>, obtained by
adding alcohol to a solution of silver nitrate; -- also called
<altname>fulminate of silver</altname>. When dry it is violently
explosive.</cd> -- <col><b>German silver</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>German</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Gray silver</b></col>.
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Freieslebenite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Horn
silver</b></col>. <fld>(Min.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Cerargyrite</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>King's silver</b></col>. <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Postfine</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Red silver</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Ruby silver</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Proustite</er>, and <er>Pyrargyrite</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Silver beater</b></col>, <cd>one who beats silver into silver
leaf or silver foil.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Silver glance</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Vitreous silver</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Argentine</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silver</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
silver; made of silver; <as>as, <ex>silver</ex> leaf; a <ex>silver</ex>
cup.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resembling silver.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Bright; resplendent; white.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Silver</xex>
hair.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Others, on <qex>silver</qex> lakes and rivers, bathed<br/
Their downy breast.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>Precious; costly.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Giving a
clear, ringing sound soft and clear.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Silver</xex>
voices.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>Sweet; gentle;
peaceful.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Silver</xex> slumber.<rdquo/
<au>Spenser.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>American silver fir</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
balsam fir. See under <er>Balsam</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
age</b></col> <fld>(Roman Lit.)</fld>, <cd>the latter part (<sc>a.
d.</sc> 14-180) of the classical period of Latinity, -- the time of
writers of inferior purity of language, as compared with those of the
previous <xex>golden age</xex>, so-called.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver-bell
tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an American shrub or small tree
(<spn>Halesia tetraptera</spn>) with white bell-shaped flowers in
clusters or racemes; the snowdrop tree.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
bush</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a shrubby leguminous plant
(<spn>Anthyllis Barba-Jovis</spn>) of Southern Europe, having silvery
foliage.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver chub</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the fallfish.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver eel</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The cutlass fish.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>A pale variety of the common eel.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
fir</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a coniferous tree (<spn>Abies
pectinata</spn>) found in mountainous districts in the middle and south
of Europe, where it often grows to the height of 100 or 150 feet. It
yields Burgundy pitch and Strasburg turpentine.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
foil</b></col>, <cd>foil made of silver.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
fox</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of the common fox
(<spn>Vulpes vulpes</spn>, variety <varn>argenteus</varn>) found in the
northern parts of Asia, Europe, and America. Its fur is nearly black,
with silvery tips, and is highly valued. Called also <altname>black
fox</altname>, and <altname>silver-gray fox</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Silver gar</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Billfish</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver grain</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the lines or narrow plates of cellular tissue
which pass from the pith to the bark of an exogenous stem; the medullary
rays. In the wood of the oak they are much larger than in that of the
beech, maple, pine, cherry, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver grebe</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the red-throated diver.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
hake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the American whiting.</cd> --
<col><b>Silver leaf</b></col>, <cd>leaves or sheets made of silver
beaten very thin.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver lunge</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the namaycush.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
moonfish</b></col>.<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Moonfish</er>
<sd>(b)</sd>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a lepisma.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver owl</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the barn owl.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver perch</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the mademoiselle, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
pheasant</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of
beautiful crested and long-tailed Asiatic pheasants, of the genus
<gen>Euplocamus</gen>. They have the tail and more or less of the upper
parts silvery white. The most common species (<spn>Euplocamus
nychtemerus</spn>) is native of China.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver
plate</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>domestic utensils made of a base metal
coated with silver.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>a plating of silver on a base
metal.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver plover</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the knot.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver salmon</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a salmon (<spn>Oncorhynchus kisutch</spn>)
native of both coasts of the North Pacific. It ascends all the American
rivers as far south as the Sacramento. Called also
<altname>kisutch</altname>, <altname>whitefish</altname>, and
<altname>white salmon</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver shell</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a marine bivalve of the genus Anomia. See
<er>Anomia</er>.</cd><-- ##?why Anomia not italicised? --> --
<col><b>Silver steel</b></col>, <cd>an alloy of steel with a very small
proportion of silver.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver stick</b></col>, <cd>a
title given to the title field officer of the Life Guards when on duty
at the palace.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <au>Thackeray.</au> --
<col><b>Silver tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a South African
tree (<spn>Leucadendron argenteum</spn>) with long, silvery, silky
leaves.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver trout</b></col>, <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Trout</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver wedding</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Wedding</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver whiting</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a marine sciaenoid food fish (<spn>Menticirrus
littoralis</spn>) native of the Southern United States; -- called also
<altname>surf whiting</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Silver witch</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>A lepisma.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silver</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Silvered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Silvering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover
with silver; to give a silvery appearance to by applying a metal of a
silvery color; <as>as, to <ex>silver</ex> a pin; to <ex>silver</ex> a
glass mirror plate with an amalgam of tin and mercury.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To polish like silver; to impart a brightness to,
like that of silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And smiling calmness <qex>silvered</qex> o'er the deep.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make hoary, or white, like silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His head was <qex>silvered</qex> o'er with age.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silver</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To acquire a silvery
color.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The eastern sky began to <qex>silver</qex> and shine.</q>
<rj><qau>L. Wallace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverback</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The knot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverberry</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*ber`ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A tree or shrub (<spn>Elaeagnus argentea</spn>) with silvery
foliage and fruit.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverbill</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An Old World finch of the genus <gen>Minia</gen>, as the <spn>Minia
Malabarica</spn> of India, and <spn>Minia cantans</spn> of
Africa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverboom</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*boom`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>zilver</ets> silver + <ets>boom</ets> tree.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See <er>Leucadendron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silver certificate</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver cer*tif"i*cate</hw>. <def>A certificate issued by a
government that there has been deposited with it silver to a specified
amount, payable to the bearer on demand. In the United States and its
possessions, it is issued against the deposit of silver coin, and is not
legal tender, but is receivable for customs, taxes, and all public dues.
In the United States the redeemability in silver of <ex>silver
certificates</ex> was discontinued in the 1970's; they are still (1997)
accepted as money at the face dollar value, but cannot be redeemed in
silver.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverfin</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*fin`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A small North American fresh-water cyprinoid fish (<spn>Notropis
Whipplei</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverfish</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The tarpum.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A white variety of the goldfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>one of a variety of insects of the order
<ord>Thysanura</ord>, especially <spn>Lepisma saccharina</spn>, which
may infest houses, and eats starched clothing and sized papers. See
<er>Lepisma.</er></def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silver-gray</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver-gray`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a gray
color with a silvery luster; <as>as, <ex>silver-gray</ex>
hair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silveriness</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being silvery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silvering</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>The art or
process of covering metals, wood, paper, glass, etc., with a thin film
of metallic silver, or a substance resembling silver; also, the firm do
laid on; <as>as, the <ex>silvering</ex> of a glass
speculum</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverite</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who favors the
use or establishment of silver as a monetary standard; -- so called by
those who favor the gold standard.</def> <mark>[Colloq. or
Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverize</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Silverized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Silverizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cover with
silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverless</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no silver; hence,
without money; impecunious.</def> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverling</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*ling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small silver coin.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A thousand vines at a thousand <qex>silverings</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. vii. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverly</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Like silver in appearance or
in sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let me wipe off this honorable dew,<br/
That <qex>silverly</qex> doth progress on thy cheeks.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silvern</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"vern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>seolfern</ets>, <ets>sylfren</ets>.]</ety> <def>Made of
silver.</def> <mark>[Archaic.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif (Acts xix.
24).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Speech is <qex>silvern</qex>; silence is golden.</q> <rj><qau>Old
Proverb.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silversides</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*sides`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of several species of small fishes of the family
<fam>Atherinidae</fam>, having a silvery stripe along each side of the
body. The common species of the American coast (<spn>Menidia
notata</spn>) is very abundant. Called also
<altname>silverside</altname>, <altname>sand smelt</altname>,
<altname>friar</altname>, <altname>tailor</altname>, and
<altname>tinker</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Brook silversides</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
small fresh-water North American fish (<spn>Labadesthes sicculus</spn>)
related to the marine silversides.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1342 --></p>

<p><ent>Silversmith</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*smith`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One whose
occupation is to manufacture utensils, ornaments, etc., of silver; a
worker in silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverspot</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*spot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of butterflies of the genus
<gen>Argynnis</gen> and allied genera, having silvery spots on the under
side of the wings..</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silver State</ent><br/
<hw>Silver State</hw>. <def>Nevada; -- a nickname alluding to its silver
mines.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverware</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*ware`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Dishes, vases,
ornaments, and utensils of various sorts, made of silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silverweed</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A perennial rosaceous herb (<spn>Potentilla Anserina</spn>) having
the leaves silvery white beneath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silvery</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"ver*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Resembling, or having the luster of, silver; grayish white and
lustrous; of a mild luster; bright.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the enameled race, whose <qex>silvery</qex> wing<br/
Waves to the tepid zephyrs of the spring.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Besprinkled or covered with silver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having the clear, musical tone of silver; soft and
clear in sound; <as>as, <ex>silvery</ex> voices; a <ex>silvery</ex>
laugh</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Silvery iron</b></col> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a
peculiar light-gray fine-grained cast iron, usually obtained from clay
iron ore.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silviculture</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"vi*cul`ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>silviculture</ets>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Sylviculture</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Silvics</ent><br/
<hw>Sil"vics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
science treating of the life of trees in the forest.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Habit or behavior of a forest tree.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sima</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A
cyma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simagre</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"a*gre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>simagr<eacute/e</ets>.]</ety> <def>A grimace.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simar</ent><br/
<hw>Si*mar"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>simarre</ets>. See <er>Chimere</er>.]</ety> <def>A woman's long
dress or robe; also light covering; a scarf.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>cimar</asp>, <asp>cymar</asp>, <asp>samare</asp>,
<asp>simare</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simarre</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si`marre"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Simar</er>.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simblot</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"blot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>simbleau</ets>.]</ety> <def>The harness of a drawloom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sim"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., an ape; cf.
<ets>simus</ets> flatnosed, snub-nosed, Gr. <?/<?/<?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A Linnaean genus of Quadrumana which included
the types of numerous modern genera. By modern writers it is usually
restricted to the genus which includes the orang-outang.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simial</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Simian; apelike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simian</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>simia</ets>
an ape.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the family
<fam>Simiadae</fam>, which, in its widest sense, includes all the Old
World apes and monkeys; also, apelike.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>Any Old World monkey or ape.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similar</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>similaire</ets>, fr. L. <ets>similis</ets> like, similar. See
<er>Same</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Simulate</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Exactly corresponding; resembling in all respects;
precisely like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nearly corresponding; resembling in many respects;
somewhat like; having a general likeness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Homogenous; uniform.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Similar figures</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>figures
which differ from each other only in magnitude, being made up of the
same number of like parts similarly situated.</cd> -- <col><b>Similar
rectilineal figures</b></col>, <cd>such as have their several angles
respectively equal, each to each, and their sides about the equal angles
proportional.</cd> -- <col><b>Similar solids</b></col>, <cd>such as are
contained by the same number of similar planes, similarly situated, and
having like inclination to one another.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similar</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*lar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is similar to, or
resembles, something else, as in quality, form, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similarity</ent><br/
<hw>Sim`i*lar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>similarit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
similar; likeness; resemblance; <as>as, a <ex>similarity</ex> of
features</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hardly is there a <qex>similarity</qex> detected between two or
three facts, than men hasten to extend it to all.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similarly</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*lar*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a similar
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similary</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*lar*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Similar.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rhyming cadences of <qex>similarly</qex> words.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similative</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*la*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Implying or
indicating likeness or resemblance.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>similative</qex> or instrumental relation to a pa. pple.
[past participle], as almond-leaved, -scented, etc.</q> <rj><qau>New
English Dict.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simile</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Similes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., from
<ets>similis</ets>. See <er>Similar</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld>
<def>A word or phrase by which anything is likened, in one or more of
its aspects, to something else; a similitude; a poetical or imaginative
comparison.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A good swift <qex>simile</qex>, but something currish.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similiter</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*mil"i*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., in like
manner.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The technical name of the form by
which either party, in pleading, accepts the issue tendered by his
opponent; -- called sometimes a <altname>joinder in
issue</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similitude</ent><br/
<hw>Si*mil"i*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>similitude</ets>, L. <ets>similitudo</ets>, from <ets>similis</ets>
similar. See <er>Similar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being similar or like; resemblance; likeness; similarity;
<as>as, <ex>similitude</ex> of substance</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let us make now man in our image, man<br/
In our <qex>similitude</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If fate some future bard shall join<br/
In sad <qex>similitude</qex> of griefs to mine.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of likening, or that which likens, one thing
to another; fanciful or imaginative comparison; a simile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tasso, in his <qex>similitudes</qex>, never departed from the
woods; that is, all his comparisons were taken from the country.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is like or similar; a representation,
semblance, or copy; a facsimile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Man should wed his <qex>similitude</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similitudinary</ent><br/
<hw>Si*mil`i*tu"di*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Involving
or expressing similitude.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similize</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*lize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To liken; to
compare; <as>as, to <ex>similize</ex> a person, thing, or
act</as>.</def> <rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Similor</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*lor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>similus</ets> similar + F. <ets>or</ets> gold, L. <ets>aurum</ets>.
Cf. <er>Semilor</er>.]</ety> <def>An alloy of copper and zinc,
resembling brass, but of a golden color.</def>
<rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simious</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L. <ets>simia</ets>
an ape.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Simi<ae/;
monkeylike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That strange <qex>simious</qex>, schoolboy passion of giving pain
to others.</q> <rj><qau>Sydney Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simitar</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"i*tar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scimiter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simmer</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Simmered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Simmering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Prov. E. also
<ets>simper</ets>; -- an onomatopoetic word.]</ety> <def>To boil gently,
or with a gentle hissing; to begin to boil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>simmer</qex> as liquor doth on the fire before it beginneth
to boil.</q> <rj><qau>Palsgrave.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simmer</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"mer</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to boil gently; to cook
in liquid heated almost or just to the boiling point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simnel</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>simenel</ets> cake or bread of wheat flour, LL.
<ets>simenellus</ets> wheat bread, fr. L. <ets>simila</ets> the finest
wheat flour. Cf. <er>Semolina</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of
cake made of fine flour; a cracknel.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not common bread, but vastel bread, or <qex>simnels</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of rich plum cake, eaten especially on
Mid-Lent Sunday.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Herrick.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simoniac</ent><br/
<hw>Si*mo"ni*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>simoniacus</ets>. See <er>Simony</er>.]</ety> <def>One who
practices simony, or who buys or sells preferment in the church.</def>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simoniacal</ent><br/
<hw>Sim`o*ni"a*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to simony; guilty of simony; consisting of simony.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sim"o*ni`a*cal*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The flagitious profligacy of their lives, and the
<qex>simoniacal</qex> arts by which they grasped at the popedom.</q>
<rj><qau>J. S. Harford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simonial</ent><br/
<hw>Si*mo"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Simoniacal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simonian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*mo"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[See
<er>Simony</er>.]</ety> <def>One of the followers of Simon Magus; also,
an adherent of certain heretical sects in the early Christian
church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simonious</ent><br/
<hw>Si*mo"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Simoniacal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simonist</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"o*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who practices
simony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simon-pure</ent><br/
<hw>Si"mon-pure"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Genuine; true;
real; authentic; -- a term alluding to the comedy character Simon Pure,
who is impersonated by another and is obliged to prove himself to be the
<ldquo/real Simon Pure.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simony</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>simonie</ets>, LL. <ets>simonia</ets>, fr. <ets>Simon</ets> Magus,
who wished to purchase the power of conferring the Holy Spirit.
<ets>Acts</ets> viii.]</ety> <def>The crime of buying or selling
ecclesiastical preferment; the corrupt presentation of any one to an
ecclesiastical benefice for money or reward.</def> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simoon</ent><br/
<ent>Simoom</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Si*moom"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Si*moon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar. <ets>sam<?/m</ets>, fr. <ets>samma</ets>
to poison. Cf. <er>Samiel</er>.]</ety> <def>A hot, dry, suffocating,
dust-laden wind, that blows occasionally in Arabia, Syria, and
neighboring countries, generated by the extreme heat of the parched
deserts or sandy plains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simous</ent><br/
<hw>Si"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L. <ets>simus</ets>,
Gr. <?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <def>Having a very flat or snub nose, with the end
turned up.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simpai</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"pai</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[Malay
<ets>simpei</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A long-tailed monkey
(<spn>Semnopitchecus melalophus</spn>) native of Sumatra. It has a crest
of black hair. The forehead and cheeks are fawn color, the upper parts
tawny and red, the under parts white. Called also <altname>black-crested
monkey</altname>, and <altname>sinpae</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simper</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Simpered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Simpering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Norw.
<ets>semper</ets> fine, smart, dial. Dan. <ets>semper</ets>,
<ets>simper</ets>, affected, coy, prudish, OSw. <ets>semper</ets> one
who affectedly refrains from eating, Sw. <ets>sipp</ets> finical, prim,
LG. <ets>sipp</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To smile in a silly,
affected, or conceited manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Behold yond <qex>simpering</qex> dame.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a made countenance about her mouth, between
<qex>simpering</qex> and smiling.</q> <rj><qau>ir. P.
Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To glimmer; to twinkle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet can I mark how stars above<br/
<qex>Simper</qex> and shine.</q> <rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simper</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"per</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A constrained, self-conscious
smile; an affected, silly smile; a smirk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The conscious <qex>simper</qex>, and the jealous leer.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simperer</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"per*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
simpers.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>simperer</qex> that a court affords.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Nevile.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simpering</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"per*ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Simper</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simperingly</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"per*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a simpering
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simple</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Simpler</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Simplest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[F., fr. L. <ets>simplus</ets>, or
<ets>simplex</ets>, gen. <ets>simplicis</ets>. The first part of the
Latin words is probably akin to E. <ets>same</ets>, and the sense, one,
one and the same; cf. L. <ets>semel</ets> once, <ets>singuli</ets> one
to each, single. Cg. <er>Single</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, <er>Same</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, and for the last part of the word cf. <er>Double</er>,
<er>Complex</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Single; not complex; not
infolded or entangled; uncombined; not compounded; not blended with
something else; not complicated; <as>as, a <ex>simple</ex> substance; a
<ex>simple</ex> idea; a <ex>simple</ex> sound; a <ex>simple</ex>
machine; a <ex>simple</ex> problem; <ex>simple</ex>
tasks.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Plain; unadorned; <as>as, <ex>simple</ex>
dress</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Simple</xex> truth.<rdquo/
<au>Spenser.</au> <ldquo/His <xex>simple</xex> story.<rdquo/
<au>Burns.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Mere; not other than; being only.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A medicine . . . whose <qex>simple</qex> touch<br/
Is powerful to araise King Pepin.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not given to artifice, stratagem, or duplicity;
undesigning; sincere; true.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Full many fine men go upon my score, as <qex>simple</qex> as I
stand here, and I trust them.</q> <rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Must thou trust Tradition's <qex>simple</qex> tongue?</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be simple is to be great.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Artless in manner; unaffected; unconstrained;
natural; inartificial;; straightforward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>simple</qex> manners all the secret lies.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Direct; clear; intelligible; not abstruse or
enigmatical; <as>as, a <ex>simple</ex> statement; <ex>simple</ex>
language</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Weak in intellect; not wise or sagacious; of but
moderate understanding or attainments; hence, foolish; silly.</def>
<ldquo/You have <xex>simple</xex> wits.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>simple</qex> believeth every word; but the prudent man
looketh well to his going.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xiv. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Not luxurious; without much variety; plain; <as>as,
a <ex>simple</ex> diet; a <ex>simple</ex> way of living</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy <qex>simple</qex> fare and all thy plain delights.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Humble; lowly; undistinguished.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>simple</qex> husbandman in garments gray.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Clergy and laity, male and female, gentle and <qex>simple</qex>
made the fuel of the same fire.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(BOt.)</fld> <def>Without subdivisions; entire;
<as>as, a <ex>simple</ex> stem; a <ex>simple</ex> leaf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Not capable of being decomposed
into anything more simple or ultimate by any means at present known;
elementary; thus, atoms are regarded as <xex>simple</xex> bodies. Cf.
<er>Ultimate</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A <xex>simple</xex> body is one that has not as yet been
decomposed. There are indications that many of our simple elements are
still compound bodies, though their actual decomposition into anything
simpler may never be accomplished.</note><-- see <er>fundamental
particle</er> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Homogenous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Consisting of a single
individual or zooid; <as>as, a <ex>simple</ex> ascidian; -- opposed to
<ex>compound</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Simple contract</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>any
contract, whether verbal or written, which is not of record or under
seal.</cd> <au>J. W. Smith.</au> <au>Chitty.</au> -- <col><b>Simple
equation</b></col> <fld>(Alg.)</fld>, <cd>an equation containing but one
unknown quantity, and that quantity only in the first degree.</cd> --
<col><b>Simple eye</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an eye having a
single lens; -- opposed to <ant>compound eye</ant>.</cd> --
<col><b>Simple interest</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Interest</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Simple larceny</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Larceny</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Simple obligation</b></col> <fld>(Rom.
Law)</fld>, <cd>an obligation which does not depend for its execution
upon any event provided for by the parties, or is not to become void on
the happening of any such event.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Single; uncompounded; unmingled; unmixed; mere;
uncombined; elementary; plain; artless; sincere; harmless; undesigning;
frank; open; unaffected; inartificial; unadorned; credulous; silly;
foolish; shallow; unwise.</syn> -- <usage><er>Simple</er>,
<er>Silly</er>. One who is <xex>simple</xex> is sincere, unaffected, and
inexperienced in duplicity, -- hence liable to be duped. A
<xex>silly</xex> person is one who is ignorant or weak and also
self-confident; hence, one who shows in speech and act a lack of good
sense. <xex>Simplicity</xex> is incompatible with duplicity, artfulness,
or vanity, while <xex>silliness</xex> is consistent with all three.
<xex>Simplicity</xex> denotes lack of knowledge or of guile;
<xex>silliness</xex> denotes want of judgment or right purpose, a defect
of character as well as of education.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am a <qex>simple</qex> woman, much too weak<br/
To oppose your cunning.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is the companion of the <qex>silliest</qex> people in their
most <qex>silly</qex> pleasure; he is ready for every impertinent
entertainment and diversion.</q> <rj><qau>Law.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simple</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Simple</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something not
mixed or compounded.</def> <ldquo/Compounded of many
<xex>simples</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicinal plant; -- so called
because each vegetable was supposed to possess its particular virtue,
and therefore to constitute a simple remedy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What virtue is in this remedy lies in the naked <qex>simple</qex>
itself as it comes over from the Indies.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A drawloom.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A part of the apparatus for raising the heddles of a
drawloom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A feast which is not a double
or a semidouble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simple</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ple</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To gather simples, or medicinal
plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As <qex>simpling</qex> on the flowery hills she [Circe]
strayed.</q> <rj><qau>Garth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simple-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ple-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sincere;
ingenuous; guileless.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simple-minded</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ple-mind`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Artless;
guileless; simple-hearted; undesigning; unsuspecting; devoid of
duplicity.</def> <au>Blackstone.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Sim"ple-mind`ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simpleness</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ple*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
simple; simplicity.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simpler</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"pler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who collects
simples, or medicinal plants; a herbalist; a simplist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Simpler's joy</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>Vervain.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simpless</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"pless</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>simplesse</ets>.]</ety> <def>Simplicity; silliness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simplesse</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"plesse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a trade name for a
fat substitute prepared as microparticles of protein from whey. It has
little fat, but has a consistency and taste resembling fat, and is thus
used to prepare foods low in fat for dietetic purposes.</def>
<mark>[Trade name]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simpleton</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ple*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>simplet</ets>, It. <ets>semplicione</ets>.]</ety> <def>A person of
weak intellect; a silly person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simplician</ent><br/
<hw>Sim*pli"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>simplicien</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who is simple.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Arnway.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simplicity</ent><br/
<hw>Sim*plic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>simplicit<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>simplicitas</ets>. See
<er>Simple</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being
simple, unmixed, or uncompounded; <as>as, the <ex>simplicity</ex> of
metals or of earths</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being not complex, or of
consisting of few parts; <as>as, the <ex>simplicity</ex> of a
machine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Artlessness of mind; freedom from cunning or
duplicity; lack of acuteness and sagacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Marquis Dorset, a man, for his harmless <qex>simplicity</qex>
neither misliked nor much regarded.</q> <rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In wit a man; <qex>simplicity</qex> a child.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Freedom from artificial ornament, pretentious style,
or luxury; plainness; <as>as, <ex>simplicity</ex> of dress, of style, or
of language; <ex>simplicity</ex> of diet; <ex>simplicity</ex> of
life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Freedom from subtlety or abstruseness; clearness;
<as>as, the <ex>simplicity</ex> of a doctrine; the <ex>simplicity</ex>
of an explanation or a demonstration</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Weakness of intellect; silliness; folly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How long, ye simple ones, will ye love <qex>simplicity</qex>? and
the scorners delight in their scorning?</q> <rj><qau>Prov. i.
22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simplification</ent><br/
<hw>Sim`pli*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>simplification</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of simplifying.</def>
<rj><au>A. Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simplify</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"pli*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Simplified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Simplifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>simplifier</ets>, LL. <ets>simplificare</ets>. See <er>Simple</er>,
and <er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <def>To make simple; to make less complex; to
make clear by giving the explanation for; to show an easier or shorter
process for doing or making.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The collection of duties is drawn to a point, and so far
<qex>simplified</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>A. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is important, in scientific pursuits, to be caitious in
<qex>simplifying</qex> our deductions.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Nicholson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simplist</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"plist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled in
simples, or medicinal plants; a simpler.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simplistic</ent><br/
<hw>Sim*plis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to simples, or a simplist.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wilkinson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simplity</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"pli*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Simplicity.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simploce</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"plo*ce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Symploce</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simply</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ply</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a
simple manner or state; considered in or by itself; without addition;
along; merely; solely; barely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They] make that now good or evil, . . . which otherwise of itself
were not <qex>simply</qex> the one or the other.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Simply</qex> the thing I am<br/
Shall make me live.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1343 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Plainly; without art or subtlety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Subverting worldly strong and worldly wise<br/
By <qex>simply</qex> meek.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Weakly; foolishly.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simulachre</ent><br/
<ent>Simulacher</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sim"u*la`cher</hw>, <hw>Sim"u*la`chre</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>simulacre</ets>.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Simulacrum</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simulacrum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sim`u*la"crum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Simulacra</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. See
<er>Simulate</er>.]</ety> <def>A likeness; a semblance; a mock
appearance; a sham; -- now usually in a derogatory sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beneath it nothing but a great <qex>simulacrum</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simular</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>simulator</ets>, F. <ets>simulateur</ets>. See
<er>Simulate</er>.]</ety> <def>One who pretends to be what he is not;
one who, or that which, simulates or counterfeits something; a
pretender.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Christ calleth the Pharisees hypocrites, that is to say,
<qex>simulars</qex>, and painted sepulchers.</q>
<rj><qau>Tyndale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simular</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"u*lar</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>False; specious;
counterfeit.</def> <mark>[R. & Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Thou
<xex>simular</xex> man of virtue.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simulate</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>simulatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>simulare</ets> to simulate; akin to
<ets>simul</ets> at the same time, together, <ets>similis</ets> like.
See <er>Similar</er>, and cf. <er>Dissemble</er>,
<er>Semblance</er>.]</ety> <def>Feigned; pretended.</def>
<rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simulate</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Simulated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Simulating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To assume the mere
appearance of, without the reality; to assume the signs or indications
of, falsely; to counterfeit; to feign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Puritans, even in the depths of the dungeons to which she had
sent them, prayed, and with no <qex>simulated</qex> fervor, that she
might be kept from the dagger of the assassin.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simulation</ent><br/
<hw>Sim`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>simulation</ets>, L. <ets>simulatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of
simulating, or assuming an appearance which is feigned, or not true; --
distinguished from <xex>dissimulation</xex>, which disguises or conceals
what is true.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Counterfeiting; feint; pretense.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simulator</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"u*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who simulates, or feigns.</def> <rj><au> De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simulatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"u*la*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Simulated, or
capable of being simulated.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simultaneity</ent><br/
<hw>Si`mul*ta*ne"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being simultaneous; simultaneousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simultaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Si`mul*ta"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>simultim</ets> at the same time, fr. L. <ets>simul</ets>. See
<er>Simulate</er>.]</ety> <def>Existing, happening, or done, at the same
time; <as>as, <ex>simultaneous</ex> events</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Si`mul*ta"ne*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Si`mul*ta"ne*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Simultaneous equations</b></col> <fld>(Alg.)</fld>,
<cd>two or more equations in which the values of the unknown quantities
entering them are the same at the same time in both or in
all.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Simulty</ent><br/
<hw>Sim"ul*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>simultas</ets> a hostile encounter, drudge, originally, a (hostile)
coming together, fr. <ets>simul</ets> together: cf. OF.
<ets>simult<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Private grudge or quarrel;
<as>as, domestic <ex>simulties</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>simvastatin</ent><br/
<hw>sim`va*stat"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a chemical
substance (<chform>C25H38O5</chform>) having inhibitory activity on the
enzyme HMG-CoA reductase, and used in medicine to reduce cholesterol
levels in blood.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> synvinolin; MK-733; Zocor.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sin</ent><br/
<hw>Sin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv., prep., & conj.</pos> <def>Old form
of <er>Since</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sin</qex> that his lord was twenty year of age.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sin</ent><br/
<hw>Sin</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sinne</ets>, AS.
<ets>synn</ets>, <ets>syn</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zonde</ets>, OS.
<ets>sundia</ets>, OHG. <ets>sunta</ets>, G. <ets>s<uum/nde</ets>,
Icel., Dan. & Sw. <ets>synd</ets>, L. <ets>sons</ets>,
<ets>sontis</ets>, guilty, perhaps originally from the p. pr. of the
verb signifying, to be, and meaning, the one who it is. Cf.
<er>Authentic</er>, <er>Sooth</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Transgression of the law of God; disobedience of the divine
command; any violation of God's will, either in purpose or conduct;
moral deficiency in the character; iniquity; <as>as, <ex>sins</ex> of
omission and <ex>sins</ex> of commission</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whosoever committeth <qex>sin</qex> is the servant of
<qex>sin</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>John viii. 34.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sin</qex> is the transgression of the law.</q> <rj><qau>1
John iii. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I think 't no <qex>sin</qex>.<br/
To cozen him that would unjustly win.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Enthralled<br/
By <qex>sin</qex> to foul, exorbitant desires.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An offense, in general; a violation of propriety; a
misdemeanor; <as>as, a <ex>sin</ex> against good manners</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I grant that poetry's a crying <qex>sin</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sin offering; a sacrifice for sin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He hath made him to be <qex>sin</qex> for us, who knew no sin.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Cor. v. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An embodiment of sin; a very wicked person.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy ambition,<br/
Thou scarlet <qex>sin</qex>, robbed this bewailing land<br/
Of noble Buckingham.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sin</xex> is used in the formation of some compound
words of obvious signification; as, <xex>sin</xex>-born;
<xex>sin</xex>-bred, <xex>sin</xex>-oppressed, <xex>sin</xex>-polluted,
and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Actual sin</b></col>, <col><b>Canonical
sins</b></col>, <col><b>Original sin</b></col>, <col><b>Venial
sin</b></col>.</mcol> <cd>See under <er>Actual</er>, <er>Canonical</er>,
etc.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Deadly sins</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Mortal sins</b></col></mcol> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>, <cd>willful
and deliberate transgressions, which take away divine grace; -- in
distinction from <xex>vental sins</xex>. The <xex>seven deadly
sins</xex> are pride, covetousness, lust, wrath, gluttony, envy, and
sloth.</cd> -- <col><b>Sin eater</b></col>, <cd>a man who (according to
a former practice in England) for a small gratuity ate a piece of bread
laid on the chest of a dead person, whereby he was supposed to have
taken the sins of the dead person upon himself.</cd> -- <col><b>Sin
offering</b></col>, <cd>a sacrifice for sin; something offered as an
expiation for sin.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Iniquity; wickedness; wrong. See
<er>Crime</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sin</ent><br/
<hw>Sin</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sinned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sinning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sinnen</ets>,
<ets>singen</ets>, <ets>sinegen</ets>, AS. <ets>syngian</ets>. See
<er>Sin</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To depart
voluntarily from the path of duty prescribed by God to man; to violate
the divine law in any particular, by actual transgression or by the
neglect or nonobservance of its injunctions; to violate any known rule
of duty; -- often followed by <xex>against</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Against thee, thee only, have I <qex>sinned</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. li. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All have <qex>sinned</qex>, and come short of the glory of
God.</q> <rj><qau>Rom. iii. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To violate human rights, law, or propriety; to
commit an offense; to trespass; to transgress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am a man<br/
More <qex>sinned</qex> against than <qex>sinning</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who but wishes to invert the laws<br/
Of order, <qex>sins</qex> against the eternal cause.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinaitic</ent><br/
<ent>Sinaic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Si*na"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Si`na*it"ic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From Mount
<ets>Sinai</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Mount Sinai; given or
made at Mount Sinai; <as>as, the <ex>Sinaitic</ex> law</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sinaitic manuscript</b></col>, <cd>a fourth century Greek
manuscript of the part Bible, discovered at Mount Sinai (the greater
part of it in 1859) by Tisschendorf, a German Biblical critic; -- called
also <altname>Codex Sinaiticus</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinalbin</ent><br/
<hw>Sin*al"bin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>Sin</ets>apis + <ets>alba</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
glucoside found in the seeds of white mustard (<spn>Brassica alba</spn>,
formerly <spn>Sinapis alba</spn>), and extracted as a white crystalline
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinamine</ent><br/
<hw>Sin*am"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sin</ets>apis
+ mel<ets>amine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A bitter white
crystalline nitrogenous substance, obtained indirectly from oil of
mustard and ammonia; -- called also <altname>allyl
melamine</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinapate</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"a*pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of sinapic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinapic</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"a*pic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to sinapine; specifically, designating an acid
(<chform>C11H12O5</chform>) related to gallic acid, and obtained by the
decomposition of sinapine, as a white crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinapine</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"a*pine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sinapi</ets>, <ets>sinapis</ets>, mustard, Gr. <?/<?/<?/: cf. F.
<ets>sinapine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An alkaloid
occurring in the seeds of mustard. It is extracted, in combination with
sulphocyanic acid, as a white crystalline substance, having a hot,
bitter taste. When sinapine is isolated it is unstable and undergoes
decomposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinapis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*na"pis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A disused generic name for mustard; -- now called
<altname>Brassica</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinapisin</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"a*pis`in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A substance extracted from mustard seed and probably identical with
sinalbin.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinapism</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"a*pism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sinapismus</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/, the use of a mustard blister,
fr. <?/<?/<?/ to apply a mustard blister, fr. <?/<?/<?/<?/
mustard.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A plaster or poultice composed
principally of powdered mustard seed, or containing the volatile oil of
mustard seed. It is a powerful irritant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinapoleic</ent><br/
<hw>Sin`a*po*le"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sina</ets>pis + <ets>oleic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to mustard oil; specifically, designating an acid
of the oleic acid series said to occur in mustard oil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinapoline</ent><br/
<hw>Si*nap"o*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sinapis</ets> + L. <ets>oleum</ets> oil.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A nitrogenous base,
<chform>CO.(NH.C3H5)2</chform>, related to urea, extracted from mustard
oil, and also produced artificially, as a white crystalline substance;
-- called also <altname>diallyl urea</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sincaline</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ca*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called because
obtained by the action of al<ets>kal</ets>ies on
<ets>sin</ets>apine.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Choline.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sinkaline</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Since</ent><br/
<hw>Since</hw> <pr>(s<icr/ns)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>sins</ets>, contr. fr. OE. <ets>sithens</ets>, <ets>sithenes</ets>,
formed by an adverbial ending (cf. <er>Besides</er>) from OE.
<ets>sithen</ets>, also shortened into <ets>sithe</ets>, <ets>sin</ets>,
AS. <ets>si<edh/<edh/an</ets>, <ets>sy<edh/<edh/an</ets>,
<ets>seo<edh/<edh/an</ets>, afterward, then, since, after; properly,
after that; fr. <ets>s<imac/<edh/</ets> after, later, adv. and prep.
(originally a comparative adv., akin to OS. <ets>s<imac/<edh/</ets>
afterward, since, OHG. <ets>s<imac/d</ets>, G. <ets>seit</ets> since,
Goth. <ets>sei<thorn/us</ets> late, ni <thorn/ana<ets>sei<thorn/s</ets>
no longer) + <ets><edh/on</ets> instrumental of the demonstrative and
article. See <er>That</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>From a definite past
time until now; <as>as, he went a month ago, and I have not seen him
<ex>since</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>since</qex> become the slaves to one man's lust.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the time past, counting backward from the
present; before this or now; ago.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How many ages <qex>since</qex> has Virgil writ?</q>
<rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>About two years <qex>since</qex>, it so fell out, that he was
brought to a great lady's house.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P.
Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>When or that.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do you remember <qex>since</qex> we lay all night in the windmill
in St. George's field?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Since</ent><br/
<hw>Since</hw>, <pos>prep.</pos> <def>From the time of; in or during the
time subsequent to; subsequently to; after; -- usually with a past event
or time for the object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord hath blessed thee, <qex>since</qex> my coming.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xxx. 30.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have a model by which he build a nobler poem than any extant
<qex>since</qex> the ancients.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Since</ent><br/
<hw>Since</hw>, <pos>conj.</pos> <def>Seeing that; because; considering;
-- formerly followed by <xex>that</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Since</qex> that my penitence comes after all,<br/
Imploring pardon.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Since</qex> truth and constancy are vain,<br/
<qex>Since</qex> neither love, nor sense of pain,<br/
Nor force of reason, can persuade,<br/
Then let example be obeyed.</q> <rj><qau>Granville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Because; for; as; inasmuch as; considering. See
<er>Because</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sincere</ent><br/
<hw>Sin*cere"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Sincerer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Sincerest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>sincerus</ets>, of uncertain origin; the first part perhaps akin to
<ets>sin-</ets> in <ets>singuli</ets> (see <er>Single</er>), and the
second to <ets>cernere</ets> to separate (cf. <er>Discern</er>): cf. F.
<ets>sinc</ets><egrave/re.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pure; unmixed;
unadulterated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is no <qex>sincere</qex> acid in any animal juice.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A joy which never was <qex>sincere</qex> till now.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Whole; perfect; unhurt; uninjured.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The inviolable body stood <qex>sincere</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Being in reality what it appears to be; having a
character which corresponds with the appearance; not falsely assumed;
genuine; true; real; <as>as, a <ex>sincere</ex> desire for knowledge; a
<ex>sincere</ex> contempt for meanness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sincere</qex> intention of pleasing God in all our
actions.</q> <rj><qau>Law.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Honest; free from hypocrisy or dissimulation;
<as>as, a <ex>sincere</ex> friend; a <ex>sincere</ex>
person</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The more <qex>sincere</qex> you are, the better it will fare with
you at the great day of account.</q> <rj><qau>Waterland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Honest; unfeigned; unvarnished; real; true;
unaffected; inartificial; frank; upright. See <er>Hearty</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sincerely</ent><br/
<hw>Sin*cere"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sincere manner.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Purely; without alloy.</def>
<au>Milton.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Honestly; unfeignedly; without
dissimulation; <as>as, to speak one's mind <ex>sincerely</ex>; to love
virtue <ex>sincerely</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sincereness</ent><br/
<hw>Sin*cere"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sincerity</er>.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sincerity</ent><br/
<hw>Sin*cer"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sinceritas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sinc<eacute/rit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or state of being sincere; honesty of mind or
intention; freedom from simulation, hypocrisy, disguise, or false
pretense; sincereness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I protest, in the <qex>sincerity</qex> of love.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sincerity</qex> is a duty no less plain than important.</q>
<rj><qau>Knox.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinch</ent><br/
<hw>Sinch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Cinch</er>.]</ety> <def>A saddle girth made of leather, canvas,
woven horsehair, or woven grass.</def> <mark>[Western U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinch</ent><br/
<hw>Sinch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To gird with a sinch; to tighten
the sinch or girth of (a saddle); <as>as, to <ex>sinch</ex> up a
sadle</as>.</def> <mark>[Western U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sincipital</ent><br/
<hw>Sin*cip"i*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the sinciput; being in the region of the
sinciput.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinciput</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ci*put</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., half a head;
<ets>semi</ets>half + <ets>caput</ets> the head.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The fore part of the head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The part of the head of a bird
between the base of the bill and the vertex.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sindi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sind"i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>Sind<imac/</ets>, fr. <ets>Sind</ets> Indian, Skr.
<ets>sindhu</ets> river, sea, the river Indus, the country along the
Indus. Cf. <er>Indian</er>, <er>Hindoo</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>
<def>A native of Sind, India, esp. one of the native Hindoo
stock.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sindon</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a kind of fine
Indian cotton stuff, Gr. <?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
wrapper.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Wrapped in <xex>sindons</xex>
of linen.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>A small rag or pledget introduced
into the hole in the cranium made by a trephine.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sine</ent><br/
<hw>Sine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>sinus</ets> a
sine, L. <ets>sinus</ets> bosom, used in translating the Ar.
<ets>jaib</ets>, properly, bosom, but probably read by mistake (the
consonants being the same) for an original <ets>j<imac/ba</ets> sine,
from Skr. <ets>j<imac/va</ets> bowstring, chord of an arc, sine.]</ety>
<fld>(Trig.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The length of a perpendicular drawn
from one extremity of an arc of a circle to the diameter drawn through
the other extremity.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The perpendicular itself.
See <cref>Sine of angle</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Artificial sines</b></col>, <cd>logarithms of the natural
sines, or logarithmic sines.</cd> -- <col><b>Curve of sines</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Sinusoid</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Natural sines</b></col>,
<cd>the decimals expressing the values of the sines, the radius being
unity.</cd> -- <col><b>Sine of an angle</b></col>, <cd>in a circle whose
radius is unity, the sine of the arc that measures the angle; in a
right-angled triangle, the side opposite the given angle divided by the
hypotenuse. See <cref>Trigonometrical function</cref>, under
<er>Function</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Versed sine</b></col>, <cd>that part
of the diameter between the sine and the arc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sine</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si"ne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prep.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>Without.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinecural</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ne*cu`ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a sinecure; being in the nature of a sinecure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinecure</ent><br/
<hw>Si`ne*cure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sine</ets>
without + <ets>cura</ets> care, LL., a cure. See <er>Cure</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An ecclesiastical benefice without the care of
souls.</def> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any office or position which requires or involves
little or no responsibility, labor, or active service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A lucrative <qex>sinecure</qex> in the Excise.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinecure</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ne*cure</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To put or place in a
sinecure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinecurism</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ne*cu*rism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
having a sinecure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinecurist</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ne*cu*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who has a
sinecure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinew</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sinewe</ets>,
<ets>senewe</ets>, AS. <ets>sinu</ets>, <ets>seonu</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>zenuw</ets>, OHG. <ets>senawa</ets>, G. <ets>sehne</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sin</ets>, Sw. <ets>sena</ets>, Dan. <ets>sene</ets>; cf. Skr.
<ets>sn<amac/va</ets>. <root/290.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A tendon or tendonous tissue. See
<er>Tendon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Muscle; nerve.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir
J. Davies.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: That which supplies strength or
power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The portion and <qex>sinew</qex> of her fortune, her marriage
dowry.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bodies of men, munition, and money, may justly be called the
<qex>sinews</qex> of war.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Money alone is often called <xex>the sinews of
war</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinew</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ew</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sinewed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sinewing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To knit together, or make strong
with, or as with, sinews.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wretches, now stuck up for long tortures . . . might, if properly
treated, serve to <qex>sinew</qex> the state in time of danger.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinewed</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ewed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Furnished
with sinews; <as>as, a strong-<ex>sinewed</ex> youth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Equipped; strengthened.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When he sees<br/
Ourselves well <qex>sinewed</qex> to our defense.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinewiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ew*i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
sinewy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinewish</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ew*ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sinewy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinewless</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ew*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no sinews; hence, having
no strength or vigor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinewous</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ew*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sinewy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinew-shrunk</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ew-shrunk`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Far.)</fld>
<def>Having the sinews under the belly shrunk by excessive
fatigue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinewy</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ew*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Pertaining to, consisting of, or resembling, a sinew or
sinews.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sinewy</qex> thread my brain lets fall.</q>
<rj><qau>Donne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Well braced with, or as if with, sinews; nervous;
vigorous; strong; firm; tough; <as>as, the <ex>sinewy</ex>
Ajax</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man whose words . . . were so close and <qex>sinewy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hare.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinful</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AAS.
<ets>synfull</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tainted with, or full of, sin; wicked;
iniquitous; criminal; unholy; <as>as, <ex>sinful</ex> men;
<ex>sinful</ex> thoughts</as>.</def> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ah <qex>sinful</qex> nation, a people laden with iniquity.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. i. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sin"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sin"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sing</ent><br/
<hw>Sing</hw> <pr>(s<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Sung</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or
<conjf>Sang</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sung</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Singing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>singan</ets>; akin to
D. <ets>zingen</ets>, OS. & OHG. <ets>singan</ets>, G.
<ets>singen</ets>, Icel. <ets>syngja</ets>, Sw. <ets>sjunga</ets>, Dan.
<ets>synge</ets>, Goth. <ets>siggwan</ets>, and perhaps to E.
<ets>say</ets>, v.t., or cf. Gr. <?/<?/<?/ voice. Cf. <er>Singe</er>,
<er>Song</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter sounds with musical
inflections or melodious modulations of voice, as fancy may dictate, or
according to the notes of a song or tune, or of a given part (as alto,
tenor, etc.) in a chorus or concerted piece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The noise of them that <qex>sing</qex> do I hear.</q> <rj><qau>Ex.
xxxii. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To utter sweet melodious sounds, as birds
do.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On every bough the briddes heard I <qex>sing</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Singing</qex> birds, in silver cages hung.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make a small, shrill sound; <as>as, the air
<ex>sings</ex> in passing through a crevice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O'er his head the flying spear<br/
<qex>Sang</qex> innocent, and spent its force in air.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To tell or relate something in numbers or verse; to
celebrate something in poetry.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bid her . . . <qex>sing</qex><br/
Of human hope by cross event destroyed.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1344 --></p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To cry out; to complain.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They should <qex>sing</qex> if thet they were bent.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sing</ent><br/
<hw>Sing</hw> <pr>(s<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
utter with musical inflections or modulations of voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And they <qex>sing</qex> the song of Moses, the servant of God,
and the song of the Lamb.</q> <rj><qau>Rev. xv. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And in the darkness <qex>sing</qex> your carol of high praise.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To celebrate in song; to give praises to in verse;
to relate or rehearse in numbers, verse, or poetry.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Arms and the man I <qex>sing</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The last, the happiest British king,<br/
Whom thou shalt paint or I shall <qex>sing</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To influence by singing; to lull by singing; <as>as,
to <ex>sing</ex> a child to sleep</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To accompany, or attend on, with singing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I heard them <qex>singing</qex> home the bride.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singe</ent><br/
<hw>Singe</hw> <pr>(s<icr/nj)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Singed</conjf> <pr>(s<icr/njd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Singeing</conjf>
<pr>(s<icr/nj"<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sengen</ets>, AS.
<ets>sengan</ets> in be<ets>sengan</ets> (akin to D. <ets>zengen</ets>,
G. <ets>sengen</ets>), originally, to cause to sing, fr. AS.
<ets>singan</ets> to sing, in allusion to the <ets>singing</ets> or
hissing sound often produced when a substance is singed, or slightly
burned. See <er>Sing</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To burn slightly or
superficially; to burn the surface of; to burn the ends or outside of;
<as>as, to <ex>singe</ex> the hair or the skin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You sulphurous and thought-executing fires, . . . <br/
<qex>Singe</qex> my white head!</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>singed</qex> the toes of an ape through a burning
glass.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To remove the nap of (cloth), by
passing it rapidly over a red-hot bar, or over a flame, preliminary to
dyeing it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To remove the hair or down from (a
plucked chicken or the like) by passing it over a flame.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singe</ent><br/
<hw>Singe</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A burning of the surface; a slight
burn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singer</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ger</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"j<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Singe</er>.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which, singes.</def>
<specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One employed to singe
cloth.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A machine for singeing cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singer</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sing</er>.]</ety> <def>One who sings; especially, one whose
profession is to sing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singeress</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"er*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A songstress.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singhalese</ent><br/
<hw>Sin`gha*lese"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>Si<ntil/hala</ets> Ceylon.]</ety> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Cingalese</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singing</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Sing</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Singing bird</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>Popularly, any bird that sings; a song bird</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>Specifically, any one of the Oscines.</cd> -- <col><b>Singing
book</b></col>, <cd>a book containing music for singing; a book of
tunes.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Singing falcon</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Singing hawk</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Chanting falcon</cref>, under <er>Chanting</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Singing fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a California
toadfish (<spn>Porichthys porosissimus</spn>), called also
<altname>midshipman</altname>; -- so called because it produces a
buzzing sound with its air bladder.</cd> -- <col><b>Singing
flame</b></col> <fld>(Acoustics)</fld>, <cd>a flame, as of hydrogen or
coal gas, burning within a tube and so adjusted as to set the air within
the tube in vibration, causing sound. The apparatus is called also
<altname>chemical harmonicon</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Singing
master</b></col>, <cd>a man who teaches vocal music.</cd> --
<col><b>Singing school</b></col>, <cd>a school in which persons are
instructed in singing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singingly</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With sounds like singing;
with a kind of tune; in a singing tone.</def> <rj><au>G. North
(1575).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>singulus</ets>, a dim. from the root in <ets>simplex</ets> simple;
cf. OE. & OF. <ets>sengle</ets>, fr. L. <ets>singulus</ets>.
<ets>See</ets> <er>Simple</er>, and cf. <er>Singular</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One only, as distinguished from more than one;
consisting of one alone; individual; separate; <as>as, a <ex>single</ex>
star</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No <qex>single</qex> man is born with a right of controlling the
opinions of all the rest.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Alone; having no companion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who <qex>single</qex> hast maintained,<br/
Against revolted multitudes, the cause<br/
Of truth.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, unmarried; <as>as, a <ex>single</ex> man or
woman</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Grows, lives, and dies in <qex>single</qex> blessedness.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Single</qex> chose to live, and shunned to wed.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not doubled, twisted together, or combined with
others; <as>as, a <ex>single</ex> thread; a <ex>single</ex> strand of a
rope</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Performed by one person, or one on each side;
<as>as, a <ex>single</ex> combat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These shifts refuted, answer thy appellant, . . . <br/
Who now defles thee thrice ti <qex>single</qex> fight.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Uncompounded; pure; unmixed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Simple ideas are opposed to complex, and <qex>single</qex> to
compound.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Not deceitful or artful; honest; sincere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I speak it with a <qex>single</qex> heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Simple; not wise; weak; silly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He utters such <qex>single</qex> matter in so infantly a
voice.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Single ale</b></col>, <col><b>Single
beer</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Single drink</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>small ale, etc., as contrasted with <contr>double ale</contr>, etc.,
which is stronger.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Nares.</au> --
<col><b>Single bill</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a written
engagement, generally under seal, for the payment of money, without a
penalty.</cd> <au>Burril.</au> -- <col><b>Single court</b></col>
<fld>(Lawn Tennis)</fld>, <cd>a court laid out for only two
players.</cd> -- <col><b>Single-cut file</b></col>. <cd>See the Note
under 4th <er>File</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Single entry</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Bookkeeping</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Single file</b></col>.
<cd>See under 1st <er>File</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Single flower</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a flower with but one set of petals, as a wild
rose.</cd> -- <col><b>Single knot</b></col>. -- <col><b>Single
whip</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a single rope running through a
fixed block.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Singled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Singling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
select, as an individual person or thing, from among a number; to choose
out from others; to separate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dogs who hereby can <qex>single</qex> out their master in the
dark.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His blood! she faintly screamed her mind<br/
Still <qex>singling</qex> one from all mankind.</q>
<rj><qau>More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sequester; to withdraw; to retire.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An agent <qex>singling</qex> itself from consorts.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To take alone, or one by one.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men . . . commendable when they are <qex>singled</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To take the irregular gait
called single-foot; -- said of a horse. See
<er>Single-foot</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many very fleet horses, when overdriven, adopt a disagreeable
gait, which seems to be a cross between a pace and a trot, in which the
two legs of one side are raised almost but not quite, simultaneously.
Such horses are said to <qex>single</qex>, or to be single-footed.</q>
<rj><qau>W. S. Clark.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A unit; one; <as>as, to
score a <ex>single</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The reeled filaments of silk,
twisted without doubling to give them firmness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A handful of gleaned grain.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.
& Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law Tennis)</fld> <def>A game with but one player
on each side; -- usually in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>A hit by a batter which
enables him to reach first base only.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single-acting</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle-act`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having
simplicity of action; especially <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, acting or exerting
force during strokes in one direction only; -- said of a reciprocating
engine, pump, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single-breasted</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle-breast`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lapping over
the breast only far enough to permit of buttoning, and having buttons on
one edge only; <as>as, a <ex>single-breasted</ex> coast</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single-foot</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle-foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An irregular
gait of a horse; -- called also <altname>single-footed pace</altname>.
See <er>Single</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Single-foot</qex> is an irregular pace, rather rare,
distinguished by the posterior extremities moving in the order of a fast
walk, and the anterior extremities in that of a slow trot.</q>
<rj><qau>Stillman (The Horse in Motion.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single-foot</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle-foot`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To proceed by means of the
single-foot, as a horse or other quadruped.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sin"gle-foot`er</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>single-handed</ent><br/
<hw>sin"gle-hand"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having but one
hand, or one workman; also, alone; unassisted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>single-handedly</ent><br/
<hw>sin"gle-hand"ed*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By
oneself; alone; unassisted.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle-heart"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having an
honest heart; free from duplicity.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sin"gle-heart"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>single-minded</ent><br/
<hw>sin"gle-mind"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
single purpose; concentrating on a single goal; hence, artless;
guileless; single-hearted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>single-mindedness</ent><br/
<hw>sin"gle-mind"ed*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality of being single-minded.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singleness</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being single, or separate from all others; the opposite of
doubleness, complication, or multiplicity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Freedom from duplicity, or secondary and selfish
ends; purity of mind or purpose; simplicity; sincerity; <as>as,
<ex>singleness</ex> of purpose; <ex>singleness</ex> of
heart</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>singles</ent><br/
<hw>sin"gles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Single</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>singles</ent><br/
<hw>sin"gles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>For unmarried
persons, or catering especially to unmarried persons; <as>as, a
<ex>singles bar</ex>; a <ex>singles</ex> party</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singlestick</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle*stick`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>In
England and Scotland, a cudgel used in fencing or fighting; a
backsword.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The game played with singlesticks, in
which he who first brings blood from his adversary's head is pronounced
victor; backsword; cudgeling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single-surfaced</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle-sur"faced</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having one
surface; -- said specif. of aeroplanes or aerocurves that are covered
with fabric, etc., on only one side.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singlet</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"glet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An unlined or undyed
waistcoat; a single garment; -- opposed to <contr>doublet</contr>.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Single tax</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle tax`</hw>. <fld>(Pol. Econ.)</fld> <def>A tax levied upon
land alone, irrespective of improvements, -- advocated by certain
economists as the sole source of public revenue.</def></p>

<p><q>Whatever may be thought of Henry George's <qex>single-tax</qex>
theory as a whole, there can be little question that a relatively higher
assessment of ground rent, with corresponding relief for those who have
made improvements, is a much-needed reform.</q> <rj><qau>A. T.
Hadley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singleton</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>In certain games
at cards, as whist, a single card of any suit held at the deal by a
player; <as>as, to lead a <ex>singleton</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singletree</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gle*tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Swingletree</er>.]</ety> <def>The pivoted or swinging bar to which
the traces of a harnessed horse are fixed; a whiffletree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ When two horses draw abreast, a <xex>singletree</xex> is
fixed at each end of another crosspiece, called the
<xex>doubletree</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singly</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Individually; particularly; severally; <as>as, to make men
<ex>singly</ex> and personally good</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Only; by one's self; alone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Look thee, 't is so! Thou <qex>singly</qex> honest man.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Without partners, companions, or associates;
single-handed; <as>as, to attack another <ex>singly</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At omber <qex>singly</qex> to decide their doom.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Honestly; sincerely; simply.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Singularly; peculiarly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sing-sing</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"-sing`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The kob.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singsong</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"song`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Bad
singing or poetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A drawling or monotonous tone, as of a badly
executed song.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singsong</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"song`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Drawling; monotonous; having a
monotonous cadence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singsong</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"song`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To write poor poetry.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singspiel</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sing"spiel`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.;
<ets>singen</ets> to sing + <ets>spiel</ets> to play.]</ety>
<fld>(Music)</fld> <def>A dramatic work, partly in dialogue and partly
in song, of a kind popular in Germany in the latter part of the 18th
century. It was often comic, had modern characters, and patterned its
music on folk song with strictly subordinated accompaniment.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singster</ent><br/
<hw>Sing"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A songstress.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singular</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(s<icr/<nsm/"g<usl/*l<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>singuler</ets>, F. <ets>singulier</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>singularius</ets>, <ets>singularis</ets>, fr.
<ets>singulus</ets> single. See <er>Single</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Separate or apart from others; single; distinct.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And God forbid that all a company<br/
Should rue a <qex>singular</qex> man's folly.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Engaged in by only one on a side; single.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To try the matter thus together in a <qex>singular</qex>
combat.</q> <rj><qau>Holinshed.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>Existing by itself; single;
individual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The idea which represents one . . . determinate thing, is called a
<qex>singular</qex> idea, whether simple, complex, or compound.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Each; individual; <as>as, to convey
several parcels of land, all and <ex>singular</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Denoting one person or thing;
<as>as, the <ex>singular</ex> number</as>; -- opposed to
<contr>dual</contr> and <ant>plural</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Standing by itself; out of the ordinary course;
unusual; uncommon; strange; <as>as, a <ex>singular</ex>
phenomenon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So <qex>singular</qex> a sadness<br/
Must have a cause as strange as the effect.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Distinguished as existing in a very high degree;
rarely equaled; eminent; extraordinary; exceptional; <as>as, a man of
<ex>singular</ex> gravity or attainments</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Departing from general usage or expectations; odd;
whimsical; -- often implying disapproval or censure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His zeal<br/
None seconded, as out of season judged,<br/
Or <qex>singular</qex> and rash.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be <qex>singular</qex> in anything that is wise and worthy, is
not a disparagement, but a praise.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Being alone; belonging to, or being, that of which
there is but one; unique.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These busts of the emperors and empresses are all very scarce, and
some of them almost <qex>singular</qex> in their kind.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Singular point in a curve</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>,
<cd>a point at which the curve possesses some peculiar properties not
possessed by other points of the curve, as a cusp point, or a multiple
point.</cd> -- <col><b>Singular proposition</b></col>
<fld>(Logic)</fld>, <cd>a proposition having as its subject a singular
term, or a common term limited to an individual by means of a singular
sign.</cd> <au>Whately.</au> -- <col><b>Singular succession</b></col>
<fld>(Civil Law)</fld>, <cd>division among individual successors, as
distinguished from <xex>universal succession</xex>, by which an estate
descended in intestacy to the heirs in mass.</cd> -- <col><b>Singular
term</b></col> <fld>(Logic)</fld>, <cd>a term which represents or stands
for a single individual.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Unexampled; unprecedented; eminent;
extraordinary; remarkable; uncommon; rare; unusual; peculiar; strange;
odd; eccentric; fantastic.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singular</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gu*lar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An individual
instance; a particular.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram)</fld> <def>The singular number, or the number
denoting one person or thing; a word in the singular number.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singularist</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gu*lar*ist</hw> <pr>(s<icr/<nsm/"g<usl/*l<etil/r*<icr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who affects singularity.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A clownish <qex>singularist</qex>, or nonconformist to ordinary
usage.</q> <rj><qau>Borrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singularity</ent><br/
<hw>Sin`gu*lar"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(s<icr/<nsm/`g<usl/*l<acr/r"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Singularities</plw>
<pr>(s<icr/<nsm/`g<usl/*l<acr/r"<icr/*t<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>singularitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>singularit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being singular; some character
or quality of a thing by which it is distinguished from all, or from
most, others; peculiarity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pliny addeth this <qex>singularity</qex> to that soil, that the
second year the very falling down of the seeds yieldeth corn.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir. W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I took notice of this little figure for the <qex>singularity</qex>
of the instrument.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything singular, rare, or curious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your gallery<br/
Have we passed through, not without much content<br/
In many <qex>singularities</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Possession of a particular or exclusive privilege,
prerogative, or distinction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No bishop of Rome ever took upon him this name of
<qex>singularity</qex> [universal bishop].</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Catholicism . . . must be understood in opposition to the legal
<qex>singularity</qex> of the Jewish nation.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Pearson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Celibacy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singularize</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gu*lar*ize</hw> <pr>(s<icr/<nsm/"g<usl/*l<etil/r*<imac/z)</pr>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make singular or single; to distinguish.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singularly</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gu*lar*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a singular
manner; in a manner, or to a degree, not common to others;
extraordinarily; <as>as, to be <ex>singularly</ex> exact in one's
statements; <ex>singularly</ex> considerate of others.</as></def>
<ldquo/<xex>Singularly</xex> handsome.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Strangely; oddly; <as>as, to behave
<ex>singularly</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>So as to express one, or the singular
number.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singult</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"gult</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>singultus</ets>.]</ety> <def>A sigh or sobbing; also, a
hiccough.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au> <au>W.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singultous</ent><br/
<hw>Sin*gul"tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Relating to, or affected with, hiccough.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Singultus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sin*gul"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Hiccough.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinic</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ic</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sinologue</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
the Chinese and allied races; Chinese.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinical</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"i*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Trig.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a
sine; employing, or founded upon, sines; <as>as, a <ex>sinical</ex>
quadrant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinicism</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"i*cism</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<icr/*s<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>Anything peculiar to the Chinese; esp., a
Chinese peculiarity in manners or customs.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinigrin</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"i*grin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From NL.
<ets>Sinapis nigra</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A glucoside
found in the seeds of black mustard (<spn>Brassica nigra</spn>, formerly
<spn>Sinapis nigra</spn>) It resembles sinalbin, and consists of a
potassium salt of myronic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinister</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*ter</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<icr/s*t<etil/r; 277)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <note>[Accented on the middle syllable by the older poets,
as Shakespeare, Milton, Dryden.]</note> <ety>[L. <ets>sinister</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>sinistre</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>On the left hand, or
the side of the left hand; left; -- opposed to <ant>dexter</ant>, or
<ant>right</ant>.</def> <ldquo/Here on his <xex>sinister</xex>
cheek.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My mother's blood<br/
Runs on the dexter cheek, and this <qex>sinister</qex><br/
Bounds in my father's</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In heraldy the <xex>sinister</xex> side of an escutcheon
is the side which would be on the left of the bearer of the shield, and
opposite the right hand of the beholder.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unlucky; inauspicious; disastrous; injurious; evil;
-- the left being usually regarded as the unlucky side; <as>as,
<ex>sinister</ex> influences</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the several ills that visit earth,<br/
Brought forth by night, with a <qex>sinister</qex> birth.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Wrong, as springing from indirection or obliquity;
perverse; dishonest; corrupt; <as>as, <ex>sinister</ex>
aims</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nimble and <qex>sinister</qex> tricks and shifts.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He scorns to undermine another's interest by any
<qex>sinister</qex> or inferior arts.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He read in their looks . . . <qex>sinister</qex> intentions
directed particularly toward himself.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Indicative of lurking evil or harm; boding covert
danger; <as>as, a <ex>sinister</ex> countenance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bar sinister</b></col>. <fld>(Her.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Bar</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Sinister aspect</b></col>
<fld>(Astrol.)</fld>, <cd>an appearance of two planets happening
according to the succession of the signs, as Saturn in Aries, and Mars
in the same degree of Gemini.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sinister
base</b></col>, <col><b>Sinister chief</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under
<er>Escutcheon</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinister-handed</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*ter-hand"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Left-handed; hence, unlucky.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Lovelace.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinisterly</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*ter*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sinister manner.</def>
<rj><au>Wood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistrad</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*trad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sinistra</ets> the left hand + <ets>ad</ets> to.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.
& Zool.)</fld> <def>Toward the left side; sinistrally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistral</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the left, inclining to the left; sinistrous; -- opposed to
<ant>dextral</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the whorls of the spire
revolving or rising to the left; reversed; -- said of certain spiral
shells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistrality</ent><br/
<hw>Sin`is*tral"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being sinistral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistrally</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*tral*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the
left; in a sinistral manner.</def> <rj><au>J. Le Conte.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistrin</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*trin</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<icr/s*tr<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>sinister</ets> left.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
mucilaginous carbohydrate, resembling achroodextrin, extracted from
squill as a colorless amorphous substance; -- so called because it is
levorotatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistrorsal</ent><br/
<hw>Sin`is*tror"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sinistrorsus</ets>, <ets>sinistroversus</ets>, turned toward the
left side; <ets>sinister</ets> left + <ets>vertere</ets>,
<ets>vortere</ets>, <ets>versum</ets>, <ets>vorsum</ets>, to
turn.]</ety> <def>Rising spirally from right to left (of the spectator);
sinistrorse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistrorse</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*trorse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sinistrolsal</er>.]</ety> <def>Turning to the left (of the
spectator) in the ascending line; -- the opposite of
<xex>dextrorse</xex>. See <er>Dextrorse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistrous</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*trous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sinister</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Being on the left side;
inclined to the left; sinistral.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sinistrous</xex>
gravity.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1345 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wrong; absurd; perverse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A knave or fool can do no harm, even by the most
<qex>sinistrous</qex> and absurd choice.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinistrously</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"is*trous*ly</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<icr/s*tr<ucr/s*l<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a sinistrous manner; perversely;
wrongly; unluckily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>With a tendency to use the left hand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many, in their infancy, are <qex>sinistrously</qex> disposed, and
divers continue all their life left-handed.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sink</ent><br/
<hw>Sink</hw> <pr>(s<icr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Sunk</conjf> <pr>(s<ucr/<nsm/k)</pr>, or
(<conjf>Sank</conjf> <pr>(s<acr/<nsm/k)</pr>); <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sunk</conjf> (<mark>obs.</mark> <conjf>Sunken</conjf>, -- now
used as <pos>adj.</pos>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sinking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sinken</ets>, AS.
<ets>sincan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zinken</ets>, OS. <ets>sincan</ets>,
G. <ets>sinken</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<oum/kkva</ets>, Dan.
<ets>synke</ets>, Sw. <ets>sjunka</ets>, Goth. <ets>siggan</ets>, and
probably to E. <ets>silt</ets>. Cf. <er>Silt</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To fall by, or as by, the force of gravity; to descend lower and
lower; to decline gradually; to subside; <as>as, a stone <ex>sinks</ex>
in water; waves rise and <ex>sink</ex>; the sun <ex>sinks</ex> in the
west</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>sink</qex> in deep mire.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. lxix.
2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To enter deeply; to fall or retire beneath or below
the surface; to penetrate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stone <qex>sunk</qex> into his forehead.</q> <rj><qau>1 San.
xvii. 49.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, to enter so as to make an abiding impression;
to enter completely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let these sayings <qex>sink</qex> down into your ears.</q>
<rj><qau>Luke ix. 44.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be overwhelmed or depressed; to fall slowly, as
so the ground, from weakness or from an overburden; to fail in strength;
to decline; to decay; to decrease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I think our country <qex>sinks</qex> beneath the yoke.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>sunk</qex> down in his chariot.</q> <rj><qau>2 Kings ix.
24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let not the fire <qex>sink</qex> or slacken.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To decrease in volume, as a river; to subside; to
become diminished in volume or in apparent height.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Alps and Pyreneans <qex>sink</qex> before him.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To fall; subside; drop; droop; lower; decline;
decay; decrease; lessen.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sink</ent><br/
<hw>Sink</hw> <pr>(s<icr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To cause to sink; to put under water; to immerse or submerge in a
fluid; <as>as, to <ex>sink</ex> a ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The Athenians] fell upon the wings and <qex>sank</qex> a single
ship.</q> <rj><qau>Jowett (Thucyd.).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively: To cause to decline; to depress; to
degrade; hence, to ruin irretrievably; to destroy, as by drowning;
<as>as, to <ex>sink</ex> one's reputation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I raise of <qex>sink</qex>, imprison or set free.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If I have a conscience, let it <qex>sink</qex> me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy cruel and unnatural lust of power<br/
Has <qex>sunk</qex> thy father more than all his years.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make (a depression) by digging, delving, or
cutting, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>sink</ex> a pit or a well; to
<ex>sink</ex> a die.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To bring low; to reduce in quantity; to
waste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You <qex>sunk</qex> the river repeated draughts.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To conceal and appropriate.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If sent with ready money to buy anything, and you happen to be out
of pocket, <qex>sink</qex> the money, and take up the goods on
account.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To keep out of sight; to suppress; to
ignore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A courtly willingness to <qex>sink</qex> obnoxious truths.</q>
<rj><qau>Robertson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To reduce or extinguish by payment; <as>as, to
<ex>sink</ex> the national debt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sink</ent><br/
<hw>Sink</hw> <pr>(s<icr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
drain to carry off filthy water; a jakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A shallow box or vessel of wood, stone, iron, or
other material, connected with a drain, and used for receiving filthy
water, etc., as in a kitchen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A hole or low place in land or rock, where waters
sink and are lost; -- called also <altname>sink hole</altname>.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The lowest part of a natural hollow or closed basin
whence the water of one or more streams escapes by evaporation; <as>as,
the <ex>sink</ex> of the Humboldt River</as>.</def> <mark>[Western U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sink hole</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The opening to a
sink drain</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A cesspool</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>Same
as <er>Sink</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinker</ent><br/
<hw>Sink"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, sinks.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A weight on
something, as on a fish line, to sink it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In
knitting machines, one of the thin plates, blades, or other devices,
that depress the loops upon or between the needles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dividing sinker</b></col>, <cd>in knitting machines, a
sinker between two jack sinkers and acting alternately with them.</cd>
-- <col><b>Jack sinker</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Jack</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Sinker bar</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>In
knitting machines, a bar to which one set of the sinkers is
attached</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>In deep well boring, a heavy bar forming
a connection between the lifting rope and the boring tools, above the
jars.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinking</ent><br/
<hw>Sink"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Sink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sinking fund</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Fund</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sinking head</b></col> <fld>(Founding)</fld>, <cd>a riser
from which the mold is fed as the casting shrinks. See <er>Riser</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 4.</cd> -- <col><b>Sinking pump</b></col>, <cd>a pump
which can be lowered in a well or a mine shaft as the level of the water
sinks.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinless</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from sin.</def>
<rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sin"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sin"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinner</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who has sinned;
especially, one who has sinned without repenting; hence, a persistent
and incorrigible transgressor; one condemned by the law of
God.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinner</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ner</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act as a sinner.</def>
<mark>[Humorous]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whether the charmer <qex>sinner</qex> it or saint it.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinneress</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ner*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who sins.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinnet</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sennit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinological</ent><br/
<hw>Sin`o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sinologue</er>.]</ety> <def>Relating to the Chinese language,
literature or culture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinologist</ent><br/
<hw>Si*nol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A student of
China and the Chinese; one versed in the Chinese language, literature,
history, politics and culture. Same as <er>sinologue</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinologue</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"o*logue</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<osl/*l<ocr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[From L. <ets>Sinae</ets>, an Oriental people mentioned by Ptolemy,
or Ar. <ets>Sin</ets> China or the Chinese + Gr. <grk>lo`gos</grk>
discourse; formed like <ets>theologue</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sinologue</ets>.]</ety> <def>A student of China and the Chinese;
one versed in the Chinese language, literature, history, politics and
culture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinology</ent><br/
<hw>Si*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sinologie</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch of systematized knowledge
which treats of the Chinese, their language, literature, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinoper</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"o*per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Sinople.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinopis</ent><br/
<ent>Sinopia</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Si*no"pi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Si*no"pis</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A red pigment made from
sinopite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinopite</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"o*pite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>sinopis</ets> (sc. <ets>terra</ets>), a red earth or ocher found in
<ets>Sinope</ets>, a town in Paphlagoma, on the Black Sea, Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A brick-red ferruginous clay
used by the ancients for red paint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinople</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"o*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Ferruginous quartz, of a blood-red or brownish red color, sometimes
with a tinge of yellow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinople</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"o*ple</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. LL. <ets>sinopis</ets>.
See <er>Sinople</er> a mineral.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>The
tincture vert; green.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinque</ent><br/
<hw>Sinque</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Cinque</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinsring</ent><br/
<hw>Sins"ring</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Banxring</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinter</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G. Cf.
<er>Cinder</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Dross, as of iron; the
scale which files from iron when hammered; -- applied as a name to
various minerals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Calcareous sinter</b></col>, <cd>a loose banded variety
of calcite formed by deposition from lime-bearing waters; calcareous
tufa; travertine.</cd> -- <col><b>Ceraunian sinter</b></col>,
<cd>fulgurite.</cd> -- <col><b>Siliceous sinter</b></col>, <cd>a light
cellular or fibrous opal; especially, geyserite (see
<er>Geyserite</er>). It has often a pearly luster, and is then called
<xex>pearl sinter</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sintoist</ent><br/
<ent>Sintoism</ent><br/
<ent>Sintu</ent><br/
<ent>Sinto</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sin"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>Sin"tu</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sin"to*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sin"to*ist</hw>
}</mhw>. <def>See <er>Shinto</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sintoc</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sin"toc</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of spice
used in the East Indies, consisting of the bark of a species of
<gen>Cinnamomum.</gen></def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sindoc</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinuate</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"u*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sinuatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sinuare</ets> to wind, bend, fr.
<ets>sinus</ets> a bend.]</ety> <def>Having the margin alternately
curved inward and outward; having rounded lobes separated by rounded
sinuses; sinuous; wavy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinuate</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"u*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sinuated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sinuating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To bend or curve in
and out; to wind; to turn; to be sinuous.</def>
<rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinuated</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"u*a`ted</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sinuate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinuation</ent><br/
<hw>Sin`u*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sinuatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>A winding or bending in and
out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinuose</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"u*ose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sinuous.</def>
<rj><au>Loudon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinuosity</ent><br/
<hw>Sin`u*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sinuosities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sinuosit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Quality or state of
being sinuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bend, or a series of bends and turns; a winding,
or a series of windings; a wave line; a curve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A line of coast certainly amounting, with its
<qex>sinuosities</qex>, to more than 700 miles.</q> <rj><qau>Sydney
Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinuous</ent><br/
<hw>Sin"u*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sinuosus</ets>, fr. <ets>sinus</ets> a bent surface, a curve: cf.
F. <ets>sinueux</ets>. See <er>Sinus</er>.]</ety> <def>Bending in and
out; of a serpentine or undulating form; winding; crooked.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sin"u*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Streaking the ground with <qex>sinuous</qex> trace.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gardens bright with <qex>sinuous</qex> rills.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinupalliate</ent><br/
<hw>Si`nu*pal"li*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a pallial sinus. See under <er>Sinus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinus</ent><br/
<hw>Si"nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Sinus</plw>, E. <plw>Sinuses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a
bent surface, a curve, the folds or bosom of a garment, etc., a bay. Cf.
<er>Sine</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An opening; a
hollow; a bending.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bay of the sea; a recess in the shore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat. & Zool.)</fld> <def>A cavity; a
depression.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A cavity in a bone or
other part, either closed or with a narrow opening.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A dilated vessel or canal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A narrow, elongated cavity, in
which pus is collected; an elongated abscess with only a small
orifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A depression between adjoining
lobes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A sinus may be rounded, as in the leaf of the white oak,
or acute, as in that of the red maple.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pallial sinus</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Pallial</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sinus venosus</b></col> <pr>(?)</pr>.
<ety>[L., venous dilatation.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>The main part of the cavity of the right auricle of the heart in the
higher vertebrates.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>In the lower vertebrates, a
distinct chamber of the heart formed by the union of the large
systematic veins and opening into the auricle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinusoid</ent><br/
<hw>Si"nus*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sinus</ets> +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The curve whose
ordinates are proportional to the sines of the abscissas, the equation
of the curve being <mathex>y = a sin x</mathex>. It is also called the
<altname>curve of sines</altname>.</def><-- pref. sine curve --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sinusoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Si`nus*oid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a sinusoid; like a sinusoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siogoon</ent><br/
<hw>Sio"goon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shogun</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siogoonate</ent><br/
<hw>Sio*goon"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Shogunate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sioux</ent><br/
<hw>Sioux</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. sing. & pl.</pos>
<fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A nation of American Indians; see
<er>Dakotas</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sioux State</ent><br/
<hw>Sioux State</hw>. <def>North Dakota; -- a nickname.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sip</ent><br/
<hw>Sip</hw> <pr>(s<icr/p)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sipped</conjf> <pr>(s<icr/pt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sipping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sippen</ets>;
akin to OD. <ets>sippen</ets>, and AS. <ets>s<?/pan</ets> to sip, suck
up, drink. See <er>Sup</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
drink or imbibe in small quantities; especially, to take in with the
lips in small quantities, as a liquid; <as>as, to <ex>sip</ex>
tea</as>.</def> <ldquo/Every herb that <xex>sips</xex> the dew.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw into the mouth; to suck up; <as>as, a bee
<ex>sips</ex> nectar from the flowers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To taste the liquor of; to drink out of.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They skim the floods, and <qex>sip</qex> the purple flowers.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sip</ent><br/
<hw>Sip</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To drink a small quantity; to take a
fluid with the lips; to take a sip or sips of something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[She] raised it to her mouth with sober grace;<br/
Then, <qex>sipping</qex>, offered to the next in place.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sip</ent><br/
<hw>Sip</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sipping; the
taking of a liquid with the lips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small draught taken with the lips; a slight
taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One <qex>sip</qex> of this<br/
Will bathe the drooping spirits in delight<br/
Beyond the bliss of dreams.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sip</qex> is all that the public ever care to take from
reservoirs of abstract philosophy.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipage</ent><br/
<hw>Sip"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seepage</er>.</def> <mark>[Scot. & U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipe</ent><br/
<hw>Sipe</hw> <pr>(s<imac/p)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seep</er>.</def> <mark>[Scot. & U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphilis</ent><br/
<hw>Siph"i*lis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Syphilis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphoid</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phoid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sipho</ets>
a siphon + <ets>-oid</ets>: cf. F. <ets>vase sipho<ium/de</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A siphon bottle. See under <er>Siphon</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphon</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>siphon</ets>,
L. <ets>sipho</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a siphon, tube,
pipe.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A device, consisting of a pipe or tube
bent so as to form two branches or legs of unequal length, by which a
liquid can be transferred to a lower level, as from one vessel to
another, over an intermediate elevation, by the action of the pressure
of the atmosphere in forcing the liquid up the shorter branch of the
pipe immersed in it, while the continued excess of weight of the liquid
in the longer branch (when once filled) causes a continuous flow. The
flow takes place only when the discharging extremity of the pipe is
lower than the higher liquid surface, and when no part of the pipe is
higher above the surface than the same liquid will rise by atmospheric
pressure; that is, about 33 feet for water, and 30 inches for mercury,
near the sea level.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the tubes or
folds of the mantle border of a bivalve or gastropod mollusk by which
water is conducted into the gill cavity. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Mya</er>, and <er>Lamellibranchiata</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The anterior prolongation of the margin of any gastropod shell for
the protection of the soft siphon.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The tubular
organ through which water is ejected from the gill cavity of a
cephaloid. It serves as a locomotive organ, by guiding and confining the
jet of water. Called also <altname>siphuncle</altname>. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Loligo</er>, and
<er>Dibranchiata</er>.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The siphuncle of a
cephalopod shell.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>The sucking proboscis of
certain parasitic insects and crustaceans.</def> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>A
sproutlike prolongation in front of the mouth of many gephyreans.</def>
<sd>(g)</sd> <def>A tubular organ connected both with the esophagus and
the intestine of certain sea urchins and annelids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A siphon bottle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Inverted siphon</b></col>, <cd>a tube bent like a siphon,
but having the branches turned upward; specifically <fld>(Hydraulic
Engineering)</fld>, a pipe for conducting water beneath a depressed
place, as from one hill to another across an intervening valley,
following the depression of the ground.</cd> -- <col><b>Siphon
barometer</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Barometer</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Siphon bottle</b></col>, <cd>a bottle for holding aerated water,
which is driven out through a bent tube in the neck by the gas within
the bottle when a valve in the tube is opened; -- called also
<altname>gazogene</altname>, and <altname>siphoid</altname>.</cd><--
seltzer bottle? --> -- <col><b>Siphon condenser</b></col>, <cd>a
condenser for a steam engine, in which the vacuum is maintained by the
downward flow of water through a vertical pipe of great height.</cd> --
<col><b>Siphon cup</b></col>, <cd>a cup with a siphon attached for
carrying off any liquid in it; specifically <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, an oil
cup in which oil is carried over the edge of a tube in a cotton wick,
and so reaches the surface to be lubricated.</cd> -- <col><b>Siphon
gauge</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Gauge</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Siphon
pump</b></col>, <cd>a jet pump. See under <er>Jet</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphon</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>To convey, or draw off, by means of a siphon, as a liquid from one
vessel to another at a lower level.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonage</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phon*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The action of a
siphon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonal</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phon*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
a siphon; resembling a siphon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Siphonal stomach</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
stomach which is tubular and bent back upon itself, like a siphon, as in
the salmon.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonarid</ent><br/
<hw>Si`pho*na"rid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of limpet-shaped pulmonate gastropods
of the genus <gen>Siphonaria</gen>. They cling to rocks between high and
low water marks and have both lunglike organs and gills.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Si`pho*na"rid</wf>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si`pho*na"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tribe of bivalve mollusks in
which the posterior mantle border is prolonged into two tubes or
siphons. Called also <altname>Siphoniata</altname>. See <er>Siphon</er>,
2 <sd>(a)</sd>, and <er>Quahaug</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonate</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phon*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having
a siphon or siphons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Belonging to the
Siphonata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonet</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phon*et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the two dorsal tubular organs on the hinder part of the
abdomen of aphids. They give exit to the honeydew. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Aphis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*pho"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A former name for a euphorbiaceous genus
(<gen>Hevea</gen>) of South American trees, the principal source of
caoutchouc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphoniata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*pho`ni*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Siphonata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonic</ent><br/
<hw>Si*phon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
a siphon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonifer</ent><br/
<hw>Si*phon"i*fer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>sipho</ets>, <ets>-onis</ets>, siphon + <ets>ferre</ets> to
bear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any cephalopod having a siphonate
shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphoniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phon*if"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Siphon</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Siphon-bearing, as the shell of the nautilus and other
cephalopods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*pho"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Siphonia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/, dim. of <?/<?/<?/<?/. See <er>Siphon</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A bony tube which, in some birds, connects the
tympanium with the air chambers of the articular piece of the
mandible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonobranchiata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si`pho*no*bran`chi*a"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Siphon</er>, and <er>Branchia</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tribe of gastropods having the mantle border,
on one or both sides, prolonged in the form of a spout through which
water enters the gill cavity. The shell itself is not always
siphonostomatous in this group.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1346 --></p>

<p><ent>Siphonobranchiate</ent><br/
<hw>Si`pho*no*bran"chi*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a siphon, or siphons, to convey water to
the gills; belonging or pertaining to the Siphonobranchiata.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Siphonobranchiata.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonoglyphe</ent><br/
<hw>Si`pho*nog"ly*phe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Siphon</ets> + Gr. <?/<?/<?/ to engrave.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A gonidium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonophora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si`pho*noph"o*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a siphon + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to
bear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of pelagic Hydrozoa
including species which form complex free-swimming communities composed
of numerous zooids of various kinds, some of which act as floats or as
swimming organs, others as feeding or nutritive zooids, and others as
reproductive zooids. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Physallia</er>,
and <er>Porpita</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonophoran</ent><br/
<hw>Si`pho*noph"o*ran</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Belonging to the <ord>Siphonophora</ord>.</def>
-- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the
<ord>Siphonophora</ord>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonophore</ent><br/
<hw>Si*phon"o*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the <ord>Siphonophora</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si`pho*nop"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Siphon</er>, and <er>-poda</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of Scaphopoda including those in which the foot
terminates in a circular disk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonostomata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si`pho*no*stom"a*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Siphon</er>, and <er>Stoma</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A tribe of parasitic copepod
Crustacea including a large number of species that are parasites of
fishes, as the lerneans. They have a mouth adapted to suck blood.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>An artificial division of gastropods including those
that have siphonostomatous shells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonostomatous</ent><br/
<hw>Si`pho*no*stom"a*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having the front edge of the
aperture of the shell prolonged in the shape of a channel for the
protection of the siphon; -- said of certain gastropods.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Pertaining to the Siphonostomata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphonostome</ent><br/
<hw>Si`pho*nos"tome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
a siphon + <?/<?/<?/ mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any parasitic entomostracan of the tribe Siphonostomata.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A siphonostomatous shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphorhinal</ent><br/
<hw>Si`pho*rhi"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Siphon</ets> + <ets>rhinal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having tubular nostrils, as the petrels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphorhinian</ent><br/
<hw>Si`pho*rhin"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A siphorhinal bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphuncle</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phun`cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>siphunculus</ets>, <ets>sipunculus</ets>, dim. of <ets>sipho</ets>.
See <er>Siphon</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The tube which runs
through the partitions of chambered cephalopod shells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphuncled</ent><br/
<hw>Si"phun`cled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a siphuncle; siphunculated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphuncular</ent><br/
<hw>Si*phun"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the siphuncle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siphunculated</ent><br/
<hw>Si*phun"cu*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a siphuncle.</def>
<rj><au>Huxley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipid</ent><br/
<hw>Sip"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Insipid</er>,
<er>Sapid</er>.]</ety> <def>Having a taste or flavor; savory;
sapid.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cockeram.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipper</ent><br/
<hw>Sip"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One which
sips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sippet</ent><br/
<hw>Sip"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sip</er>,
<er>Sop</er>.]</ety> <def>A small sop; a small, thin piece of toasted
bread soaked in milk, broth, or the like; a small piece of toasted or
fried bread cut into some special shape and used for
garnishing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your sweet <qex>sippets</qex> in widows' houses.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipple</ent><br/
<hw>Sip"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>sip</ets>.]</ety> <def>To sip often.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sippling</ent><br/
<hw>Sip"pling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sipping often.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Taken after a <xex>sippling</xex>
sort.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipunculacea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*pun`cu*la"ce*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., from <ets>Sipunculus</ets>, the typical genus. See
<er>Siphuncle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A suborder of
Gephyrea, including those which have the body unarmed and the intestine
opening anteriorly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipunculoid</ent><br/
<hw>Si*pun"cu*loid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Sipunculus</ets>, the typical genus + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to the Sipunculoidea.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Sipunculoidea.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sipunculoidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*pun`cu*loi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Same as
<er>Gephyrea</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In a restricted sense, same
as <er>Sipunculacea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Si quis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si` quis"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L., if any one (the first
words of the notice in Latin).]</ety> <fld>(Ch. of Eng.)</fld> <def>A
notification by a candidate for orders of his intention to inquire
whether any impediment may be alleged against him.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sir</ent><br/
<hw>Sir</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sire</ets>, F.
<ets>sire</ets>, contr. from the nominative L. <ets>senior</ets> an
elder, elderly person, compar. of <ets>senex</ets>,<ets>senis</ets>, an
aged person; akin to Gr. <?/<?/<?/ old, Skr. <ets>sana</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sineigs</ets> old, <ets>sinista</ets> eldest, Ir. & Gael.
<ets>sean</ets> old, W. <ets>hen</ets>. Cf. <er>Seignior</er>,
<er>Senate</er>, <er>Seneschal</er>, <er>Senior</er>, <er>Senor</er>,
<er>Signor</er>, <er>Sire</er>, <er>Sirrah</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A man of social authority and dignity; a lord; a master; a
gentleman; -- in this sense usually spelled <xex>sire</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was crowned lord and <qex>sire</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Gower.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the election of a <qex>sir</qex> so rare.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A title prefixed to the Christian name of a knight
or a baronet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sir</qex> Horace Vere, his brother, was the principal in the
active part.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An English rendering of the Latin
<xex>Dominus</xex>, the academical title of a bachelor of arts; --
formerly colloquially, and sometimes contemptuously, applied to the
clergy.</def> <rj><au>Nares.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Instead of a faithful and painful teacher, they hire a
<qex>Sir</qex> John, which hath better skill in playing at tables, or in
keeping of a garden, than in God's word.</q>
<rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A respectful title, used in addressing a man,
without being prefixed to his name; -- used especially in speaking to
elders or superiors; sometimes, also, used in the way of emphatic
formality.</def> <ldquo/What's that to you, <xex>sir</xex>?<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sheridan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Anciently, this title, was often used when a person was
addressed as a man holding a certain office, or following a certain
business. <ldquo/<xex>Sir</xex> man of law.<rdquo/ <ldquo/<xex>Sir</xex>
parish priest.<rdquo/</note> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sir reverance</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Reverence</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siraskier</ent><br/
<hw>Si*ras"kier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seraskier</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siraskierate</ent><br/
<hw>Si*ras"kier*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seraskierate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirbonian</ent><br/
<hw>Sir*bo"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Serbonian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sircar</ent><br/
<hw>Sir*car"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind. & Per.
<ets>sark<amac/r</ets> a superintendant, overseer, chief; Per.
<ets>sar</ets> the head + <ets>k<amac/r</ets> action, work.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A Hindoo clerk or accountant.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A district or province; a circar.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The government; the supreme authority of the
state.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirdar</ent><br/
<hw>Sir*dar"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind. & Per.
<ets>sard<amac/r</ets> a chief, general; <ets>sar</ets> the head, top +
<ets>d<amac/r</ets> holding, possessing.]</ety> <def>A native chief in
Hindostan; a headman.</def> <rj><au>Malcom.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In Turkey, Egypt, etc., a commander in chief, esp.
the one commanding the Anglo-Egyptian army.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sire</ent><br/
<hw>Sire</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sire</ets>,
originally, an older person. See <er>Sir</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
lord, master, or other person in authority. See <er>Sir</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pain and distress, sickness and ire,<br/
And melancholy that angry <qex>sire</qex>,<br/
Be of her palace senators.</q> <rj><qau>Rom. of R.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tittle of respect formerly used in speaking to
elders and superiors, but now only in addressing a sovereign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A father; the head of a family; the
husband.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jankin thet was our <qex>sire</qex> [i.e., husband].</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And raise his issue, like a loving <qex>sire</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A creator; a maker; an author; an
originator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] was the <qex>sire</qex> of an immortal strain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shelley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The male parent of a beast; -- applied especially to
horses; <as>as, the horse had a good <ex>sire</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sire</xex> is often used in composition; as in
grand<xex>sire</xex>, grandfather; great-grand<xex>sire</xex>,
great-grandfather.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sire</ent><br/
<hw>Sire</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sired</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Siring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To beget; to procreate; -- used of
beasts, and especially of stallions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siredon</ent><br/
<hw>Si*re"don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ a siren.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The larval form of any
salamander while it still has external gills; especially, one of those
which, like the axolotl (<spn>Amblystoma Mexicanum</spn>), sometimes lay
eggs while in this larval state, but which under more favorable
conditions lose their gills and become normal salamanders. See also
<er>Axolotl</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siren</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ren</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/:
cf. F. <ets>sir<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Class.
Myth.)</fld> <def>One of three sea nymphs, -- or, according to some
writers, of two, -- said to frequent an island near the coast of Italy,
and to sing with such sweetness that they lured mariners to
destruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Next where the <qex>sirens</qex> dwell you plow the seas;<br/
Their song is death, and makes destruction please.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An enticing, dangerous woman.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something which is insidious or deceptive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Consumption is a <qex>siren</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A mermaid.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any long, slender amphibian of
the genus <gen>Siren</gen> or family <fam>Sirenidae</fam>, destitute of
hind legs and pelvis, and having permanent external gills as well as
lungs. They inhabit the swamps, lagoons, and ditches of the Southern
United States. The more common species (<spn>Siren lacertina</spn>) is
dull lead-gray in color, and becomes two feet long.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <ety>[F. <ets>sir<egrave/ne</ets>, properly, a siren in
sense 1.]</ety> <fld>(Acoustics)</fld> <def>An instrument for producing
musical tones and for ascertaining the number of sound waves or
vibrations per second which produce a note of a given pitch. The sounds
are produced by a perforated rotating disk or disks. A form with two
disks operated by steam or highly compressed air is used sounding an
alarm to vessels in fog.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sirene</asp>,
and <asp>syren</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siren</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ren</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a siren;
bewitching, like a siren; fascinating; alluring; <as>as, a
<ex>siren</ex> song</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirene</ent><br/
<hw>Si*rene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Siren</er>,
6.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirenia</ent><br/
<hw>Si*re"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of large aquatic herbivorous mammals,
including the manatee, dugong, rytina, and several fossil
genera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The hind limbs are either rudimentary or wanting, and
the front ones are changed to paddles. They have horny plates on the
front part of the jaws, and usually flat-crowned molar teeth. The
stomach is complex and the intestine long, as in other herbivorous
mammals. See <er>Cetacea</er> <sd>(b)</sd>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirenian</ent><br/
<hw>Si*re"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of Sirenia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirenical</ent><br/
<hw>Si*ren"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like, or
appropriate to, a siren; fascinating; deceptive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here's couple of <qex>sirenical</qex> rascals shall enchant
ye.</q> <rj><qau>Marton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirenize</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ren*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To use the
enticements of a siren; to act as a siren; to fascinate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siriasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si*ri"a*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/, fr. <?/<?/<?/ the Dog Star, properly, scorching.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A sunstroke.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The act of exposing to a sun bath. <mark>[Obs.]</mark> Cf.
<er>Insolation</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirius</ent><br/
<hw>Sir"i*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/, properly, scorching.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The Dog
Star. See <er>Dog Star</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirkeer</ent><br/
<hw>Sir"keer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of several species of Asiatic cuckoos of the genus
<gen>Taccocua</gen>, as the Bengal <ex>sirkeer</ex> (<spn>Taccocua
sirkee</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirloin</ent><br/
<hw>Sir"loin`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption of
<ets>surloin</ets>. Not so called because this cut of beef was once
jocosely knighted (dubbed Sir Loin) by an English king, as according to
a popular story.]</ety> <def>A loin of beef, or a part of a loin.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>surloin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirname</ent><br/
<hw>Sir"name`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Surname</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siroc</ent><br/
<hw>Si"roc</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sirocco</er>.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark>
<rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirocco</ent><br/
<hw>Si*roc"co</hw> <pr>(s<icr/*r<ocr/k"k<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Siroccos</plw>
<pr>(s<icr/*r<ocr/k"k<omac/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It. <ets>sirocco</ets>,
<ets>scirocco</ets>, Ar. <ets>shorug</ets>, fr. <ets>sharq</ets> the
rising of the sun, the east, fr, <ets>sharaca</ets> to rise as the sun.
Cf. <er>Saracen</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An oppressive, relaxing
wind from the Libyan deserts, chiefly experienced in Italy, Malta, and
Sicily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In general, any hot dry wind of cyclonic origin,
blowing from arid or heated regions, including the desert wind of
Southern California, the <stype>harmattan</stype> of the west coasts of
Africa, the hot winds of Kansas and Texas, the <stype>kamsin</stype> of
Egypt, the <stype>leste</stype> of the Madeira Islands, and the
<stype>leveche</stype> of Spain.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirrah</ent><br/
<hw>Sir"rah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably from Icel.
<ets>s<imac/ra</ets>, fr. F. <ets>sire</ets>. See <er>Sir</er>.]</ety>
<def>A term of address implying inferiority and used in anger, contempt,
reproach, or disrespectful familiarity, addressed to a man or boy, but
sometimes to a woman. In sililoquies often preceded by <xex>ah</xex>.
Not used in the plural.</def> <ldquo/Ah, <xex>sirrah</xex>
mistress.<rdquo/ <-- archaic --> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Go, <qex>sirrah</qex>, to my cell.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirt</ent><br/
<hw>Sirt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Syrt</er>.]</ety> <def>A quicksand.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrup</ent><br/
<ent>Sirup</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sir"up</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <hw>Syr"up</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sirop</ets> (cf. It. <ets>siroppo</ets>, Sp.
<ets>jarabe</ets>, <ets>jarope</ets>, LL. <ets>siruppus</ets>,
<ets>syrupus</ets>), fr. Ar. <ets>shar<amac/b</ets> a drink, wine,
coffee, sirup. Cf. <er>Sherbet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thick and
viscid liquid made from the juice of fruits, herbs, etc., boiled with
sugar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thick and viscid saccharine solution of superior
quality (as sugarhouse <xex>sirup</xex> or molasses, maple
<xex>sirup</xex>); specifically, in pharmacy and often in cookery, a
saturated solution of sugar and water (<xex>simple sirup</xex>), or such
a solution flavored or medicated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lucent <qex>sirups</qex> tinct with cinnamon.</q>
<rj><qau>Keats.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Mixing sirup</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Dextrose</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syruped</ent><br/
<ent>Siruped</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sir"uped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syr"uped</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Moistened, covered, or sweetened with sirup, or sweet
juice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrupy</ent><br/
<ent>Sirupy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sir"up*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syr"up*y</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Like sirup, or partaking of its qualities.</def>
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sirvente</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sir`vente"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sirvente</ets>, fr. Pr. <ets>sirventes</ets>,
<ets>sirventesc</ets>, originally, the poem of, or concerning, a
<ets>sirvent</ets>, fr. <ets>sirvent</ets>, properly, serving, n., one
who serves (<it>e. g.</it>, as a soldier), fr. <ets>servir</ets> to
serve, L. <ets>servire</ets>.]</ety> <def>A peculiar species of poetry,
for the most part devoted to moral and religious topics, and commonly
satirical, -- often used by the troubadours of the Middle
Ages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sis</ent><br/
<hw>Sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A colloquial abbreviation
of <er>Sister</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sis</ent><br/
<hw>Sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Six. See
<er>Sise</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sisal hemp</ent><br/
<ent>Sisal grass</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Si*sal" grass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Si*sal" hemp`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>,}</mhw> <def>The prepared fiber of the <spn>Agave
Americana</spn>, or American aloe, used for cordage; -- so called from
<etsep>Sisal</etsep>, a port in Yucatan. See <cref>Sisal hemp</cref>,
under <er>Hemp</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siscowet</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"co*wet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF American Indian
origin.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large, fat variety of the
namaycush found in Lake Superior; -- called also
<altname>siskawet</altname>, <altname>siskiwit</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sise</ent><br/
<hw>Sise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Assize</er>.]</ety> <def>An assize.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sise</ent><br/
<hw>Sise</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sice</er>.]</ety> <def>Six; the highest number on a die; the cast of
six in throwing dice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the new casting of a die, when ace is on the top,
<qex>sise</qex> must needs be at the bottom.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sisel</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>ziesel</ets>. Cf. <er>Zizel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The suslik.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siser</ent><br/
<hw>Si"ser</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Cider. See
<er>Sicer</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siserary</ent><br/
<ent>Siserara</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sis"e*ra*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sis"e*ra*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hard blow.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siskin</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dan.
<ets>sisgen</ets>; cf. Sw. <ets>siska</ets>, G. <ets>zeisig</ets>, D.
<ets>sijsje</ets>; of Slav. origin; cf. Pol.
<ets>czy<zdot/</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
small green and yellow European finch (<spn>Spinus spinus</spn>, or
<spn>Carduelis spinus</spn>); -- called also
<altname>aberdevine</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The American
pinefinch (<spn>Spinus pinus</spn>); -- called also <altname>pine
siskin</altname>. See <er>Pinefinch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is applied also to several other related
species found in Asia and South America.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Siskin green</b></col>, <cd>a delicate shade of yellowish
green, as in the mineral torbernite.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siskiwit</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"ki*wit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The siscowet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sismograph</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"mo*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seismograph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sismometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sis*mom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Seismometer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siss</ent><br/
<hw>Siss</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Of imitative origin;
cf. D. <ets>sissen</ets>, G. <ets>zischen</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make a
hissing sound; <as>as, a flatiron hot enough to <ex>siss</ex> when
touched with a wet finger</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U. S.; Local,
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siss</ent><br/
<hw>Siss</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hissing noise.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sissoo</ent><br/
<hw>Sis*soo"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>s<imac/s<?/</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A leguminous tree
(<spn>Dalbergia Sissoo</spn>) of the northern parts of India; also, the
dark brown compact and durable timber obtained from it. It is used in
shipbuilding and for gun carriages, railway ties, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sist</ent><br/
<hw>Sist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sistere</ets>
to bring to a stand, to stop.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld>
<def>To stay, as judicial proceedings; to delay or suspend; to
stop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to take a place, as at the bar of a court;
hence, to cite; to summon; to bring into court.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some, however, have preposterously <qex>sisted</qex> nature as the
first or generative principle.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1347 --></p>

<p><ent>Sist</ent><br/
<hw>Sist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld> <def>A
stay or suspension of proceedings; an order for a stay of
proceedings.</def> <rj><au>Burril.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sister</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sister</ets>, fr. Icel. systir; also <ets>suster</ets>, from AS.
<ets>sweostor</ets>, <ets>sweoster</ets>, <ets>swuster</ets>, akin to
OFries. <ets>sweester</ets>, <ets>suster</ets>, LG.
<ets>s<uum/ster</ets>, <ets>suster</ets>, D. <ets>zuster</ets>, OS. &
OHG. <ets>swestar</ets>, G. <ets>schwester</ets>, Icel.
<ets>systir</ets>, Sw. <ets>syster</ets>, Dan. <ets>s<oum/ster</ets>,
Goth. <ets>swistar</ets>, Lith. <ets>ses<?/</ets>, Russ.
<ets>sestra</ets>, Pol. <ets>siostra</ets>, L. <ets>soror</ets>, Skr.
<ets>svasr</ets>. <root/298. Cf. <er>Cousin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A female who has the same parents with another person, or who has
one of them only. In the latter case, she is more definitely called a
<xex>half sister</xex>. The correlative of <xex>brother</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am the <qex>sister</qex> of one Claudio.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A woman who is closely allied to, or associated
with, another person, as in the sister faith, society, order, or
community.</def> <rj><au>James ii. 15.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One of the same kind, or of the same condition; --
generally used adjectively; <as>as, <ex>sister</ex> fruits</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sister Block</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a tackle
block having two sheaves, one above the other.</cd> -- <col><b>Sister
hooks</b></col>, <cd>a pair of hooks fitted together, the shank of one
forming a mousing for the other; -- called also <altname>match
hook</altname>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sister of charity</b></col>,
<col><b>Sister of mercy</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Charity</er>, and <er>Mercy</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sister</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To be sister to; to resemble
closely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sisterhood</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"ter*hood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sister</ets> + <ets>hood</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
state or relation of being a sister; the office or duty of a
sister.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She . . . abhorr'd<br/
Her proper blood, and left to do the part<br/
Of <qex>sisterhood</qex>, to do that of a wife.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A society of sisters; a society of women united in
one faith or order; sisters, collectively.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>sisterhood</xex> of holy nuns.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fair young flowers . . . a beauteous
<qex>sisterhood</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bryant.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sistering</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"ter*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Contiguous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sister-in-law</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"ter-in-law`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sisters-in-law</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The sister of one's
husband or wife; also, the wife of one's brother; sometimes, the wife of
one's husband's or wife's brother.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sisterly</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"ter*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a sister; becoming a
sister, affectionate; <as>as, <ex>sisterly</ex> kindness;
<ex>sisterly</ex> remorse.</as></def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sistine</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[It.
<ets>sistino</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Pope
Sixtus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sistine chapel</b></col>, <cd>a chapel in the Vatican at
Rome, built by Pope Sixtus IV., and decorated with frescoes by Michael
Angelo and others.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sistren</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"tren</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Sisters.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sistrum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sis"trum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/, from
<?/<?/<?/ to shake.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An instrument
consisting of a thin metal frame, through which passed a number of metal
rods, and furnished with a handle by which it was shaken and made to
rattle. It was peculiarly Egyptian, and used especially in the worship
of Isis. It is still used in Nubia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sisyphean</ent><br/
<hw>Sis`y*phe"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to
Sisyphus; incessantly recurring; <as>as, <ex>Sisyphean</ex>
labors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sisyphus</ent><br/
<hw>Sis"y*phus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sisyphus</ets>, Sisyphus, fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Class.
Myth.)</fld> <def>A king of Corinth, son of Aeolus, famed for his
cunning. He was killed by Theseus, and in the lower world was condemned
by Pluto to roll to the top of a hill a huge stone, which constantly
rolled back again, making his task incessant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sit</ent><br/
<hw>Sit</hw> <pr>(s<icr/t)</pr>, <def><pos>obs. 3d pers. sing.
pres.</pos> of <er>Sit</er>, for <xex>sitteth</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sit</ent><br/
<hw>Sit</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Sat</conjf> <pr>(s<acr/t)</pr> (<conjf>Sate</conjf>
<pr>(s<amac/t)</pr>, <mark>archaic</mark>); <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sat</conjf> (<conjf>Sitten</conjf> <pr>(s<icr/t"t'n)</pr>,
<mark>obs.</mark>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sitten</ets>, AS.
<ets>sittan</ets>; akin to OS. <ets>sittian</ets>, OFries.
<ets>sitta</ets>, D. <ets>zitten</ets>, G. <ets>sitzen</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sizzen</ets>, Icel. <ets>sitja</ets>, SW. <ets>sitta</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sidde</ets>, Goth. <ets>sitan</ets>, Russ. <ets>sidiete</ets>, L.
<ets>sedere</ets>, Gr. <grk>'e`zesqai</grk>, Skr. <ets>sad</ets>.
<root/154. Cf. <er>Assess</er>,<er>Assize</er>, <er>Cathedral</er>,
<er>Chair</er>, <er>Dissident</er>, <er>Excise</er>, <er>Insidious</er>,
<er>Possess</er>, <er>Reside</er>, <er>Sanhedrim</er>,
<er>S<eacute/ance</er>, <er>Seat</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Sedate</er>,
<er>4th Sell</er>, <er>Siege</er>, <er>Session</er>, <er>Set</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, <er>Sizar</er>, <er>Size</er>,
<er>Subsidy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To rest upon the haunches, or
the lower extremity of the trunk of the body; -- said of human beings,
and sometimes of other animals; <as>as, to <ex>sit</ex> on a sofa, on a
chair, or on the ground</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And he came and took the book put of the right hand of him that
<qex>sate</qex> upon the seat.</q> <rj><qau>Bible (1551) (Rev. v.
7.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I pray you, jest, sir, as you <qex>sit</qex> at dinner.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To perch; to rest with the feet drawn up, as birds
do on a branch, pole, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To remain in a state of repose; to rest; to abide;
to rest in any position or condition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And Moses said to . . . the children of Reuben, Shall your
brethren go to war, and shall ye <qex>sit</qex> here?</q> <rj><qau>Num.
xxxii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a demigod here <qex>sit</qex> I in the sky.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To lie, rest, or bear; to press or weigh; -- with
<xex>on</xex>; <as>as, a weight or burden <ex>sits</ex> lightly upon
him</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The calamity <qex>sits</qex> heavy on us.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To be adjusted; to fit; <as>as, a coat <ex>sits</ex>
well or ill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This new and gorgeous garment, majesty,<br/
<qex>Sits</qex> not so easy on me as you think.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To suit one well or ill, as an act; to become; to
befit; -- used impersonally.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To cover and warm eggs for hatching, as a fowl; to
brood; to incubate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As the partridge <qex>sitteth</qex> on eggs, and hatcheth them
not.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. xvii. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To have position, as at the point blown from; to
hold a relative position; to have direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a good miller that knows how to grind, which way soever the
wind <qex>sits</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Selden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sits</qex> the wind in that quarter?</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To occupy a place or seat as a member of an official
body; <as>as, to <ex>sit</ex> in Congress</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To hold a session; to be in session for official
business; -- said of legislative assemblies, courts, etc.; <as>as, the
court <ex>sits</ex> in January; the aldermen <ex>sit</ex>
to-night</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To take a position for the purpose of having some
artistic representation of one's self made, as a picture or a bust;
<as>as, to <ex>sit</ex> to a painter</as>.</def> <-- sit for seems more
common now --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sit at</b></col>, <cd>to rest under; to be subject
to.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A farmer can not husband his ground
so well if he <xex>sit at</xex> a great rent<rdquo/. <au>Bacon.</au> --
<mcol><col><b>To sit at meat</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To sit at
table</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to be at table for eating.</cd> --
<col><b>To sit down</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To place one's self on a
chair or other seat; <as>as, <ex>to sit down</ex> when tired</as>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To begin a siege; <as>as, the enemy <ex>sat down</ex>
before the town</as>.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To settle; to fix a
permanent abode</cd>. <au>Spenser.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To rest; to
cease as satisfied.</cd> <ldquo/Here we can not <xex>sit down</xex>, but
still proceed in our search.<rdquo/ <au>Rogers.</au> -- <col><b>To sit
for a fellowship</b></col>, <cd>to offer one's self for examination with
a view to obtaining a fellowship.</cd> <mark>[Eng. Univ.]</mark> --
<col><b>To sit out</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To be without engagement
or employment</cd>. <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Bp. Sanderson.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To outstay.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>to refrain from
participating in [an activity such as a dance or hand at cards]; used
especially after one has recently participated in an earlier such
activity. The one sitting out does not necessarily have to sit during
the activity foregone.</cd> -- <col><b>To sit under</b></col>, <cd>to be
under the instruction or ministrations of; as, <ex>to sit under</ex> a
preacher; <ex>to sit under</ex> good preaching.</cd> -- <col><b>To sit
up</b></col>, <cd>to rise from, or refrain from, a recumbent posture or
from sleep; to sit with the body upright; as, <ex>to sit up</ex> late at
night; also, to watch; <as>as, <ex>to sit up</ex> with a sick
person</as>.</cd> <ldquo/He that was dead <xex>sat up</xex>, and began
to speak.<rdquo/ <au>Luke vii. 15.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sit</ent><br/
<hw>Sit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sit
upon; to keep one's seat upon; <as>as, he <ex>sits</ex> a horse
well</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hardly the muse can <qex>sit</qex> the headstrong horse.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to be seated or in a sitting posture; to
furnish a seat to; -- used reflexively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>sat</qex> them down to weep.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sit</qex> you down, father; rest you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To suit (well <it>or</it> ill); to become.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Site</ent><br/
<hw>Site</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>situs</ets>, fr.
<ets>sinere</ets>, <ets>situm</ets>, to let, p. p. <ets>situs</ets>
placed, lying, situate: cf. F. <ets>site</ets>. Cf.
<er>Position</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The place where anything is
fixed; situation; local position; <as>as, the <ex>site</ex> of a city or
of a house</as>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place fitted or chosen for any certain permanent
use or occupation; <as>as, a <ex>site</ex> for a church</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The posture or position of a thing.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The semblance of a lover fixed<br/
In melancholy <qex>site</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sited</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a site;
situated.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The garden] <qex>sited</qex> was in fruitful soil.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitfast</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"fast`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sit</ets> +
<ets>fast</ets>.]</ety> <def>Fixed; stationary; immovable.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is good, when you have crossed the sea and back,<br/
To find the <qex>sitfast</qex> acres where you left them.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitfast</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"fast`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A callosity
with inflamed edges, on the back of a horse, under the saddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sith</ent><br/
<hw>Sith</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prep., adv., & conj.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Since</er>.]</ety> <def>Since; afterwards; seeing that.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We need not fear them, <qex>sith</qex> Christ is with us.</q>
<rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sith</qex> thou art rightful judge.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sithe</ent><br/
<ent>Sith</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sith</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sithe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <?/<?/<?/ a path, way, time, occasion.]</ety>
<def>Time.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And humbly thanked him a thousand <qex>sithes</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sithe</ent><br/
<hw>Sithe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sigh</er>.]</ety> <def>To sigh.</def> <note>[A spelling of a corrupt
and provincial pronunciation.]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sithe</ent><br/
<hw>Sithe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scythe.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sithe</ent><br/
<hw>Sithe</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cut with a scythe; to
scythe.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sithed</ent><br/
<hw>Sithed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Scythed.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitheman</ent><br/
<hw>Sithe"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mower.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sithen</ent><br/
<hw>Sith"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv. & conj.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Since</er>.]</ety> <def>Since; afterwards. See 1st
<er>Sith</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fortune was first friend and <qex>sithen</qex> foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sithens</ent><br/
<ent>Sithence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sith"ence</hw>, <hw>Sith"ens</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>adv. & conj.</pos> <def>Since. See <er>Sith</er>, and
<er>Sithen</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siththen</ent><br/
<hw>Sith"then</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv. & conj.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sithen</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Siththen</qex> that the world began.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitology</ent><br/
<hw>Si*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/
food + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A treatise on the regulation of the
diet; dietetics.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sitiology</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitophobia</ent><br/
<hw>Si`to*pho"bi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ food + <?/<?/<?/<?/ fear.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
version to food; refusal to take nourishment.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sitiophobia</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitten</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos>
of <er>Sit</er>, for <xex>sat</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitter</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
sits; esp., one who sits for a portrait or a bust.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bird that sits or incubates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sittine</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"tine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>sitta</ets>
the nuthatch, from Gr. <?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to the family <fam>Sittidae</fam>, or nuthatches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitting</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being in the state,
or the position, of one who, or that which, sits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitting</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"ting</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or act of
one who sits; the posture of one who occupies a seat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A seat, or the space occupied by or allotted for a
person, in a church, theater, etc.; <as>as, the hall has 800
<ex>sittings</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act or time of sitting, as to a portrait
painter, photographer, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The actual presence or meeting of any body of men in
their seats, clothed with authority to transact business; a session;
<as>as, a <ex>sitting</ex> of the judges of the King's Bench, or of a
commission</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sitting</qex> closed in great agitation.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The time during which one sits while doing
something, as reading a book, playing a game, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the understanding of any one of St. Paul's Epistles I read it
all through at one <qex>sitting</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A brooding over eggs for hatching, as by
fowls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The male bird . . . amuses her [the female] with his songs during
the whole time of her <qex>sitting</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sitting room</b></col>, <cd>an apartment where the
members of a family usually sit, as distinguished from a drawing-room,
parlor, chamber, or kitchen.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Situated</ent><br/
<ent>Situate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sit"u*ate</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <hw>Sit"u*a`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>situatus</ets>, from
<ets>situare</ets> to place, fr. L. <ets>situs</ets> situation, site.
See <er>Site</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having a site, situation, or
location; being in a relative position; permanently fixed; placed;
located; <as>as, a town <ex>situated</ex>, or <ex>situate</ex>, on a
hill or on the seashore</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Placed; residing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pleasure <qex>situate</qex> in hill and dale.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Situate</xex> is now less used than
<xex>situated</xex>, but both are well authorized.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Situate</ent><br/
<hw>Sit"u*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To place.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Landor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Situation</ent><br/
<hw>Sit`u*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>situatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>situation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Manner in which an object is placed; location, esp. as related to
something else; position; locality site; <as>as, a house in a pleasant
<ex>situation</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Position, as regards the conditions and
circumstances of the case.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>situation</qex> of the greatest ease and tranquillity.</q>
<rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Relative position; circumstances; temporary state or
relation at a moment of action which excites interest, as of persons in
a dramatic scene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There's <qex>situation</qex> for you! there's an heroic group!</q>
<rj><qau>Sheridan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Permanent position or employment; place; office;
<as>as, a <ex>situation</ex> in a store; a <ex>situation</ex> under
government</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- State; position; seat; site; station; post;
place; office; condition; case; plight. See <er>State</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Situs</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
situation.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The method in which the parts
of a plant are arranged; also, the position of the parts.</def>
<rj><au>Henslow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sitz bath</ent><br/
<hw>Sitz" bath`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[G. <ets>sitzbad</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A tub in which one bathes in a sitting posture; also, a bath so
taken; a hip bath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siva</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si"va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>Civa</ets>, properly, kind, gracious.]</ety> <fld>(Hindoo
Myth.)</fld> <def>One of the triad of Hindoo gods. He is the avenger or
destroyer, and in modern worship symbolizes the reproductive power of
nature.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sivan</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Si"van</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Heb.
<ets>s<imac/v<amac/n</ets>.]</ety> <def>The third month of the Jewish
ecclesiastical year; -- supposed to correspond nearly with our month of
June.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sivatherium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Siv`a*the"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
E. <ets>Siva</ets> + Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ a beast, an animal.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of very large extinct ruminants found
in the Tertiary formation of India. The snout was prolonged in the form
of a proboscis. The male had four horns, the posterior pair being large
and branched. It was allied to the antelopes, but very much larger than
any existing species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siver</ent><br/
<hw>Siv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To simmer.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sivvens</ent><br/
<hw>Siv"vens</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sibbens</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siwin</ent><br/
<hw>Si"win</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same
as <er>Sewen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Six</ent><br/
<hw>Six</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>six</ets>,
<ets>seox</ets>, <ets>siex</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>sex</ets>, D.
<ets>zes</ets>, OS. & OHG. <ets>sehs</ets>, G. <ets>sechs</ets>, Icel.,
Sw., & Dan. <ets>sex</ets>, Goth. <ets>sa<iacute/hs</ets>, Lith.
<ets>szeszi</ets>, Russ. <ets>sheste</ets>, Gael. & Ir. <ets>se</ets>,
W. <ets>chwech</ets>, L. <ets>sex</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/, Per.
<ets>shesh</ets>, Skr. <ets>shash</ets>. <root/304. Cf.
<er>Hexagon</er>, <er>Hexameter</er>, <er>Samite</er>, <er>Senary</er>,
<er>Sextant</er>, <er>Sice</er>.]</ety> <def>One more than five; twice
three; <as>as, <ex>six</ex> yards</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Six Nations</b></col> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>, <cd>a
confederation of North American Indians formed by the union of the
Tuscaroras and the Five Nations.</cd> -- <col><b>Six points
circle</b></col>. <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Nine points
circle</cref>, under <er>Nine</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Six</ent><br/
<hw>Six</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The number greater by a
unit than five; the sum of three and three; six units or
objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A symbol representing six units, as 6, vi., or
VI.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To be at six and seven</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>To be at sixes and sevens</b></col></mcol>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>to
be in disorder.</cd> <au>Bacon.</au> <au>Shak.</au> <au>Swift.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>to be in a dispute or disagreement; -- often used with
<ptcl>with</ptcl>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixfold</ent><br/
<hw>Six"fold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sixfealand</ets>.]</ety> <def>Six times repeated; six times as much
or as many.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Six-footer</ent><br/
<hw>Six"-foot`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is six
feet tall.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixpence</ent><br/
<hw>Six"pence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sixpences</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>An English silver coin
of the value of six pennies; half a shilling, or about twelve
cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixpenny</ent><br/
<hw>Six"pen`ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the value of, or
costing, sixpence; <as>as, a <ex>sixpenny</ex> loaf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixscore</ent><br/
<hw>Six"score`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Six</ets> + <ets>score</ets>, n.]</ety> <def>Six times
twenty; one hundred and twenty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Six-shooter</ent><br/
<hw>Six"-shoot`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pistol or
other firearm which can be fired six times without reloading especially,
a six-chambered revolver.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixteen</ent><br/
<hw>Six"teen`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sixt<?/ne</ets>, <ets>sixt<?/ne</ets>. See <er>Six</er>, and
<er>Ten</er>, and cf. <er>Sixty</er>.]</ety> <def>Six and ten;
consisting of six and ten; fifteen and one more.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixteen</ent><br/
<hw>Six"teen`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The number greater by
a unit than fifteen; the sum of ten and six; sixteen units or
objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A symbol representing sixteen units, as 16, or
xvi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixteenmo</ent><br/
<hw>Six*teen"mo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sixteenmos</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>See
<er>Sextodecimo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixteenth</ent><br/
<hw>Six"teenth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sixteen</er>: cf. AS. <ets>sixte<oacute/<?/a</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Sixth after the tenth; next in order after the
fifteenth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Constituting or being one of sixteen equal parts
into which anything is divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sixteenth note</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>the
sixteenth part of a whole note; a semiquaver.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixteenth</ent><br/
<hw>Six"teenth`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quotient of a
unit divided by sixteen; one of sixteen equal parts of one
whole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The next in order after the fifteenth; the sixth
after the tenth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An interval comprising two octaves
and a second.</def> <rj><au>Moore (Encyc. of Music.)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixth</ent><br/
<hw>Sixth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From Six: cf. AS.
<ets>sixta</ets>, <ets>siexta</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>First after
the fifth; next in order after the fifth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Constituting or being one of six equal parts into
which anything is divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1348 --></p>

<p><ent>Sixth</ent><br/
<hw>Sixth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quotient
of a unit divided by six; one of six equal parts which form a
whole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The next in order after the fifth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The interval embracing six
diatonic degrees of the scale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixthly</ent><br/
<hw>Sixth"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the sixth place.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixtieth</ent><br/
<hw>Six"ti*eth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[As.
<ets>sixtiogo<edh/a</ets>, <ets>sixtigo<edh/a</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Next in order after the fifty-ninth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Constituting or being one one of sixty equal parts
into which anything is divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixtieth</ent><br/
<hw>Six"ti*eth</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quotient of a
unit divided by sixty; one of sixty equal parts forming a
whole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The next in order after the fifty-ninth; the tenth
after the fiftieth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixty</ent><br/
<hw>Six"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>siextig</ets>; akin to G. <ets>sechzig</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sa<iacute/hs tigjus</ets>. See <er>Six</er>, <er>Ten</er>, and cf.
<er>Sixteen</er>.]</ety> <def>Six times ten; fifty-nine and one more;
threescore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixty</ent><br/
<hw>Six"ty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sixties</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The sum of six times ten; sixty
units or objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A symbol representing sixty units, as 60, lx., or
LX.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sixty-fourth</ent><br/
<hw>Six`ty-fourth"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Constituting or
being one of sixty-four equal parts into which a thing is
divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sixty-fourth note</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>the
sixty-fourth part of a whole note; a hemi-demi-semiquaver.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizable</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
considerable size or bulk.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>sizable</xex>
volume.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp. Hurd.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Being of reasonable or suitable size; <as>as,
<ex>sizable</ex> timber; <ex>sizable</ex> bulk</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizar</ent><br/
<hw>Si"zar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of a body of
students in the universities of Cambridge (Eng.) and Dublin, who, having
passed a certain examination, are exempted from paying college fees and
charges. A <xex>sizar</xex> corresponded to a <xex>servitor</xex> at
Oxford.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sizar</qex> paid nothing for food and tuition, and very
little for lodging.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They formerly waited on the table at meals; but this is
done away with. They were probably so called from being thus employed in
distributing the <xex>size</xex>, or provisions. See 4th <er>Size</er>,
2.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizarship</ent><br/
<hw>Si"zar*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The position or standing of a
sizar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Size</ent><br/
<hw>Size</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sice</er>, and
<er>Sise</er>.]</ety> <def>Six.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Size</ent><br/
<hw>Size</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OIt. <ets>sisa</ets>
glue used by painters, shortened fr. <ets>assisa</ets>, fr.
<ets>assidere</ets>, p. p. <ets>assiso</ets>, to make to sit, to seat,
to place, L. <ets>assidere</ets> to sit down; <ets>ad</ets> +
<ets>sidere</ets> to sit down, akin to <ets>sedere</ets> to sit. See
<er>Sit</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, and cf. <er>Assize</er>, <er>Size</er>
bulk.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thin, weak glue used in various trades,
as in painting, bookbinding, paper making, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any viscous substance, as gilder's
varnish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Size</ent><br/
<hw>Size</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sizing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cover with size; to prepare
with size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Size</ent><br/
<hw>Size</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. from <ets>assize</ets>. See
<er>Assize</er>, and cf. <er>Size</er> glue.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
settled quantity or allowance. See <er>Assize</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/To scant my <xex>sizes</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Univ. of Cambridge, Eng.)</fld> <def>An allowance
of food and drink from the buttery, aside from the regular dinner at
commons; -- corresponding to <xex>battel</xex> at Oxford.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Extent of superficies or volume; bulk; bigness;
magnitude; <as>as, the <ex>size</ex> of a tree or of a mast; the
<ex>size</ex> of a ship or of a rock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Figurative bulk; condition as to rank, ability,
character, etc.; <as>as, the office demands a man of larger
<ex>size</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men of a less <qex>size</qex> and quality.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The middling or lower <qex>size</qex> of people.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A conventional relative measure of dimension, as for
shoes, gloves, and other articles made up for sale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An instrument consisting of a number of perforated
gauges fastened together at one end by a rivet, -- used for ascertaining
the size of pearls.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Size roll</b></col>, <cd>a small piese of parchment added
to a roll.</cd> -- <col><b>Size stick</b></col>, <cd>a measuring stick
used by shoemakers for ascertaining the size of the foot.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Dimension; bigness; largeness; greatness;
magnitude.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Size</ent><br/
<hw>Size</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fix the standard
of.</def> <ldquo/To <xex>size</xex> weights and measures.<rdquo/
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To adjust or arrange according to size or
bulk.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To take
the height of men, in order to place them in the ranks according to
their stature.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>To sift, as
pieces of ore or metal, in order to separate the finer from the coarser
parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To swell; to increase the bulk of.</def>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>To bring or adjust anything
exactly to a required dimension, as by cutting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To size up</b></col>, <cd>to estimate or ascertain the
character and ability of. See 4th <er>Size</er>, 4.</cd> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We had <qex>to size up</qex> our fellow legislators.</q>
<rj><qau>The Century.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Size</ent><br/
<hw>Size</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take greater size;
to increase in size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our desires give them fashion, and so,<br/
As they wax lesser, fall, as they <qex>size</qex>, grow.</q>
<rj><qau>Donne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Univ. of Cambridge, Eng.)</fld> <def>To order food
or drink from the buttery; hence, to enter a score, as upon the buttery
book.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sized</ent><br/
<hw>Sized</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Adjusted
according to size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a particular size or magnitude; -- chiefly
used in compounds; <as>as, large-<ex>sized</ex>;
common-<ex>sized</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizel</ent><br/
<hw>Si"zel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Scissel</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizer</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>See
<er>Sizar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An instrument or
contrivance to size articles, or to determine their size by a standard,
or to separate and distribute them according to size.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>An instrument or tool for bringing anything to an exact
size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Siziness</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sizy; viscousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizing</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act of covering or
treating with size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A weak glue used in various trades; size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizing</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sorting with
respect to size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of bringing anything to a certain
size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Univ. of Cambridge, Eng.)</fld> <def>Food and drink
ordered from the buttery by a student.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizy</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From 2d
<er>Size</er>.]</ety> <def>Sizelike; viscous; glutinous; <as>as,
<ex>sizy</ex> blood</as>.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizzle</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"zle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sizzled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sizzling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Siss</er>.]</ety> <def>To make a hissing sound; to fry, or to dry
and shrivel up, with a hissing sound.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq.
U. S.]</mark> <rj><au>Forby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizzle</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"zle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hissing sound, as of something
frying over a fire.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sizzling</ent><br/
<hw>Siz"zling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Sizzle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skaddle</ent><br/
<hw>Skad"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>scath</ets>.]</ety> <def>Hurt; damage.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skaddle</ent><br/
<hw>Skad"dle</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hurtful.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skaddon</ent><br/
<hw>Skad"don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The larva of a bee.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skag</ent><br/
<hw>Skag</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>An
additional piece fastened to the keel of a boat to prevent lateral
motion. See <er>Skeg</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skain</ent><br/
<hw>Skain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skein</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skain</ent><br/
<hw>Skain</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Skean</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skainsmate</ent><br/
<hw>Skains"mate`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps
originally, a companion in winding thread (see <er>Skein</er>), or a
companion in arms, from <ets>skain</ets> a sword (see
<er>Skean</er>).]</ety> <def>A messmate; a companion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Scurvy knave! I am none of his firt-gills; I am none of his
<qex>skainsmates</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skaith</ent><br/
<hw>Skaith</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scatch</er>.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skald</ent><br/
<hw>Skald</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 5th
<er>Scald</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skaldic</ent><br/
<hw>Skald"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scaldic</er>.</def> <rj><au>Max M<uum/ller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skall</ent><br/
<hw>Skall</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To scale; to
mount.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skare</ent><br/
<ent>Skar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Skar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Skare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From the root of <ets>scare</ets>.]</ety> <def>Wild;
timid; shy.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skart</ent><br/
<hw>Skart</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Scarf</er> a
cormorant.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The shag.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skat</ent><br/
<hw>Skat</hw> <pr>(sk<aum/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G., fr. It.
<ets>scartare</ets> to discard.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A three-handed
card game played with 32 cards, of which two constitute the skat (sense
2), or widow. The players bid for the privilege of attempting any of
several games or tasks, in most of which the player undertaking the game
must take tricks counting in aggregate at least 61 (the counting cards
being ace 11, ten 10, king 4, queen 3, jack 2). The four jacks are the
best trumps, ranking club, spade, heart, diamond, and ten outranks king
or queen (but when the player undertakes to lose all the tricks, the
cards rank as in whist). The value of hands depends upon the game
played, trump suit, points taken, and number of matadores.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Skat)</fld> <def>A widow of two cards.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skate</ent><br/
<hw>Skate</hw> <pr>(sk<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>schaats</ets>. Cf. <er>Scatches</er>.]</ety> <def>A metallic runner
with a frame shaped to fit the sole of a shoe, -- made to be fastened
under the foot, and used for moving rapidly on ice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Batavia rushes forth; and as they sweep,<br/
On sounding <qex>skates</qex>, a thousand different ways,<br/
In circling poise, swift as the winds, along,<br/
The then gay land is maddened all to joy.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Roller skate</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Roller</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skate</ent><br/
<hw>Skate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To move on skates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skate</ent><br/
<hw>Skate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skata</ets>; cf. Prov. G.
<ets>schatten</ets>, meer<ets>-schatten</ets>, L. <ets>squatus</ets>,
<ets>squatina</ets>, and E. <ets>shad</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of large, flat elasmobranch fishes of
the genus <gen>Raia</gen>, having a long, slender tail, terminated by a
small caudal fin. The pectoral fins, which are large and broad and
united to the sides of the body and head, give a somewhat rhombic form
to these fishes. The skin is more or less spinose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Some of the species are used for food, as the European
blue or gray skate (<spn>Raia batis</spn>), which sometimes weighs
nearly 200 pounds. The American smooth, or barn-door, skate (<spn>Raia
laevis</spn>) is also a large species, often becoming three or four feet
across. The common spiny skate (<spn>Raia erinacea</spn>) is much
smaller.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skate's egg</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Sea purse</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Skate sucker</b></col>, <cd>any marine leech of the genus
<gen>Pontobdella</gen>, parasitic on skates.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skater</ent><br/
<hw>Skat"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
skates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
hemipterous insects belonging to <gen>Gerris</gen>,
<gen>Pyrrhocoris</gen>, <gen>Prostemma</gen>, and allied genera. They
have long legs, and run rapidly over the surface of the water, as if
skating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skatol</ent><br/
<hw>Ska"tol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/, dung +
<ets>-ol</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A constituent of
human faeces formed in the small intestines as a product of the
putrefaction of albuminous matter. It is also found in reduced indigo.
Chemically it is methyl indol, <chform>C9H9N</chform>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skayles</ent><br/
<hw>Skayles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>[<root/159.]
Skittles.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skean</ent><br/
<hw>Skean</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ir <ets>sgian</ets>;
akin to Gael. <ets>sgian</ets>, W. <ets>ysgien</ets> a large knife, a
scimiter.]</ety> <def>A knife or short dagger, esp. that in use among
the Highlanders of Scotland. <altsp>[Variously spelt.]</altsp></def>
<ldquo/His <xex>skean</xex>, or pistol.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skedaddle</ent><br/
<hw>Ske*dad"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Skedaddled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Skedaddling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of
uncertain etymology.]</ety> <def>To betake one's self to flight, as if
in a panic; to flee; to run away.</def> <mark>[Slang, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skee</ent><br/
<hw>Skee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dan. <ets>ski</ets>;
Icel. <ets>sk<imac/<?/</ets> a billet of wood. See <er>Skid</er>.]</ety>
<def>A long strip of wood, curved upwards in front, used on the foot for
sliding; now usually spelled <asp>ski</asp>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeed</ent><br/
<hw>Skeed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeel</ent><br/
<hw>Skeel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>skj<?/la</ets> a pail, bucket.]</ety> <def>A shallow wooden vessel
for holding milk or cream.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeelgoose</ent><br/
<ent>Skeelduck</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Skeel"duck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Skeel"goose`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sheldrake</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common European sheldrake.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeet</ent><br/
<hw>Skeet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A scoop with a long handle,
used to wash the sides of a vessel, and formerly to wet the sails or
deck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeg</ent><br/
<hw>Skeg</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E., also a stump
of a branch, a wooden peg; cf. Icel. <ets>sk<?/gr</ets> a wood, Sw.
<ets>skog</ets>. Cf. <er>Shaw</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sort of
wild plum.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A kind of oats.</def> <rj><au>Farm.
Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The after part of the keel of a
vessel, to which the rudder is attached.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skegger</ent><br/
<hw>Skeg"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The parr.</def> <rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skein</ent><br/
<hw>Skein</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>skeyne</ets>,
OF. <ets>escaigne</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/cagne</ets>, probably of Celtic
origin; cf. Ir. <ets>sgainne</ets>, Gael. <ets>sgeinnidh</ets> thread,
small twine; or perhaps the English word is immediately from
Celtic.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A quantity of yarn, thread, or the like,
put up together, after it is taken from the reel, -- usually tied in a
sort of knot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A <xex>skein</xex> of cotton yarn is formed by eighty
turns of the thread round a fifty-four inch reel.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Wagon Making)</fld> <def>A metallic strengthening
band or thimble on the wooden arm of an axle.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skein</ent><br/
<hw>Skein</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A flight of wild
fowl (wild geese or the like).</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeine</ent><br/
<hw>Skeine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skean</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelder</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>To deceive; to cheat; to trick.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelder</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"der</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vagrant; a cheat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skieldrake</ent><br/
<ent>Skeldrake</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Skel"drake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Skiel"drake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The common European
sheldrake.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The oyster catcher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelet</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A skeleton. See
<er>Scelet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeletal</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"e*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the
skeleton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeletogenous</ent><br/
<hw>Skel`e*tog"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Skeleton</ets> + <ets>-genous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Forming or
producing parts of the skeleton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeletology</ent><br/
<hw>Skel`e*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Skeleton</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That part of
anatomy which treats of the skeleton; also, a treatise on the
skeleton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeleton</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"e*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ (sc. <?/<?/<?/) a dried body, a mummy, fr. <?/<?/<?/<?/
dried up, parched, <?/<?/<?/<?/ to dry, dry up, parch.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The bony and
cartilaginous framework which supports the soft parts of a vertebrate
animal.</def> <note>[See <xex>Illust.</xex> of the Human Skeleton, in
Appendix.]</note> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The more or less firm or hardened
framework of an invertebrate animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In a wider sense, the skeleton includes the whole
connective-tissue framework with the integument and its appendages. See
<er>Endoskeleton</er>, and <er>Exoskeleton</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> Hence, figuratively: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A very thin or
lean person.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The framework of anything; the
principal parts that support the rest, but without the
appendages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The great <qex>skeleton</qex> of the world.</q> <rj><qau>Sir M.
Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>The heads and outline of a literary production,
especially of a sermon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeleton</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"e*ton</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of, or resembling, a
skeleton; consisting merely of the framework or outlines; having only
certain leading features of anything; <as>as, a <ex>skeleton</ex>
sermon; a <ex>skeleton</ex> crystal.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skeleton bill</b></col>, <cd>a bill or draft made out in
blank as to the amount or payee, but signed by the acceptor.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Skeleton key</b></col>, <cd>a key with
nearly the whole substance of the web filed away, to adapt it to avoid
the wards of a lock; a master key; -- used for opening locks to which it
has not been especially fitted.</cd> -- <col><b>Skeleton leaf</b></col>,
<cd>a leaf from which the pulpy part has been removed by chemical means,
the fibrous part alone remaining.</cd> -- <col><b>Skeleton
proof</b></col>, <cd>a proof of a print or engraving, with the
inscription outlined in hair strokes only, such proofs being taken
before the engraving is finished.</cd> -- <col><b>Skeleton
regiment</b></col>, <cd>a regiment which has its complement of officers,
but in which there are few enlisted men.</cd> -- <col><b>Skeleton
shrimp</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small crustacean of the genus
<gen>Caprella</gen>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Laemodipoda</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeletonize</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"e*ton*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Skeletonized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Skeletonizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To prepare a skeleton of; also, to reduce,
as a leaf, to its skeleton.</def> <rj><au>Pop. Sci.
Monthly.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeletonizer</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"e*ton*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any small moth whose larva eats the parenchyma of leaves, leaving
the skeleton; <as>as, the apple-leaf
<ex>skeletonizer</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skellum</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dan.
<ets>schelm</ets>, fr. G. <ets>schelm</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
scoundrel.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Pepys.
Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelly</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>skele</ets>, Sw. <ets>skela</ets>.]</ety> <def>To squint.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelly</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"ly</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A squint.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelp</ent><br/
<hw>Skelp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Prov. E.
<ets>skelp</ets> to kick severely, to move rapidly; Gael.
<ets>sgealp</ets>, n., a slap with the palm of the hand, v., to strike
with the palm of the hand.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A blow; a smart
stroke.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Brockett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A squall; also, a heavy fall of rain.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelp</ent><br/
<hw>Skelp</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike; to
slap.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>C. Reade.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form into skelp, as a plate or bar of iron by
rolling; also, to bend round (a skelp) in tube making.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelp</ent><br/
<hw>Skelp</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wrought-iron plate from which a gun
barrel or pipe is made by bending and welding the edges together, and
drawing the thick tube thus formed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skelter</ent><br/
<hw>Skel"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Helter-skelter</er>.]</ety> <def>To run off helter-skelter; to
hurry; to scurry; -- with <xex>away</xex> or <xex>off</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>A. R. Wallace.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sken</ent><br/
<hw>Sken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To squint.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skene</ent><br/
<hw>Skene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skean</er>.</def> <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skep</ent><br/
<hw>Skep</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skeppa</ets>
a measure, bushel; cf. Gael. <ets>sgeap</ets> a basket, a
beehive.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A coarse round farm basket.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A beehive.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeptic</ent><br/
<hw>Skep"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>skeptiko`s</grk> thoughtful, reflective, fr. <grk>ske`ptesqai</grk>
to look carefully or about, to view, consider: cf. L.
<ets>scepticus</ets>, F. <ets>sceptique</ets>. See
<er>Scope</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sceptic</asp>.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is yet undecided as to what is true; one who is
looking or inquiring for what is true; an inquirer after facts or
reasons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1349 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>A doubter as to whether any
fact or truth can be certainly known; a universal doubter; a Pyrrhonist;
hence, in modern usage, occasionally, a person who questions whether any
truth or fact can be established on philosophical grounds; sometimes, a
critical inquirer, in opposition to a dogmatist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All this criticism [of Hume] proceeds upon the erroneous
hypothesis that he was a dogmatist. He was a <qex>skeptic</qex>; that
is, he accepted the principles asserted by the prevailing dogmatism: and
only showed that such and such conclusions were, on these principles,
inevitable.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>A person who doubts the
existence and perfections of God, or the truth of revelation; one who
disbelieves the divine origin of the Christian religion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Suffer not your faith to be shaken by the sophistries of
<qex>skeptics</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>S. Clarke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word and its derivatives are often written with
<xex>c</xex> instead of <xex>k</xex> in the first syllable, --
<xex>sceptic</xex>, <xex>sceptical</xex>, <xex>scepticism</xex>, etc.
Dr. Johnson, struck with the extraordinary irregularity of giving
<xex>c</xex> its hard sound before <xex>e</xex>, altered the spelling,
and his example has been followed by most of the lexicographers who have
succeeded him; yet the prevalent practice among English writers and
printers is in favor of the other mode. In the United States this
practice is reversed, a large and increasing majority of educated
persons preferring the orthography which is most in accordance with
etymology and analogy.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Infidel; unbeliever; doubter. -- See
<er>Infidel</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skeptical</ent><br/
<ent>Skeptic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Skep"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Skep"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>sceptic</ets>, <ets>sceptical</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a sceptic or skepticism; characterized by skepticism;
hesitating to admit the certainly of doctrines or principles; doubting
of everything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>Doubting or denying the truth of
revelation, or the sacred Scriptures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>skeptical</qex> system subverts the whole foundation of
morals.</q> <rj><qau>R. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Skep"tac*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Skep"tic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skepticism</ent><br/
<hw>Skep"ti*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>scepticisme</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>scepticism</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An undecided, inquiring
state of mind; doubt; uncertainty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That momentary amazement, and irresolution, and confusion, which
is the result of <qex>skepticism</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hune.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>The doctrine that no fact or
principle can be certainly known; the tenet that all knowledge is
uncertain; Pyrrohonism; universal doubt; the position that no fact or
truth, however worthy of confidence, can be established on philosophical
grounds; critical investigation or inquiry, as opposed to the positive
assumption or assertion of certain principles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>A doubting of the truth of
revelation, or a denial of the divine origin of the Christian religion,
or of the being, perfections, or truth of God.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let no . . . secret <qex>skepticism</qex> lead any one to doubt
whether this blessed prospect will be realized.</q> <rj><qau>S.
Miller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skepticize</ent><br/
<hw>Skep"ti*cize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To doubt; to
pretend to doubt of everything.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>skepticize</qex>, where no one else will . . .
hesitate.</q> <rj><qau>Shaftesbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skerry</ent><br/
<hw>Sker"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Skerries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Of Scand. origin; cf.
Icel. <ets>sker</ets>, Sw. <ets>sk<aum/r</ets>, Dan. <ets>ski<?/r</ets>.
Cf. <er>Scar</er> a bank.]</ety> <def>A rocky isle; an insulated
rock.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sketch</ent><br/
<hw>Sketch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>schets</ets>,
fr. It. <ets>schizzo</ets> a sketch, a splash (whence also F.
<ets>esquisse</ets>; cf. <er>Esquisse</er>.); cf. It.
<ets>schizzare</ets> to splash, to sketch.]</ety> <def>An outline or
general delineation of anything; a first rough or incomplete draught or
plan of any design; especially, in the fine arts, such a representation
of an object or scene as serves the artist's purpose by recording its
chief features; also, a preliminary study for an original
work.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Outline; delineation; draught; plan;
design.</syn> -- <usage><er>Sketch</er>, <er>Outline</er>,
<er>Delineation</er>. An <xex>outline</xex> gives only the bounding
lines of some scene or picture. A <xex>sketch</xex> fills up the
<xex>outline</xex> in part, giving broad touches, by which an imperfect
idea may be conveyed. A <xex>delineation</xex> goes further, carrying
out the more striking features of the picture, and going so much into
detail as to furnish a clear conception of the whole. Figuratively, we
may speak of the <xex>outlines</xex> of a plan, of a work, of a project,
etc., which serve as a basis on which the subordinate parts are formed,
or of <xex>sketches</xex> of countries, characters, manners, etc., which
give us a general idea of the things described.</usage>
<rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sketch</ent><br/
<hw>Sketch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sketched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sketching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf D. <ets>schetsen</ets>, It.
<ets>schizzare</ets>. See <er>Sketch</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To draw the outline or chief features of; to make a
drought of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To plan or describe by giving the principal points
or ideas of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To delineate; design; draught; depict.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sketch</ent><br/
<hw>Sketch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make sketches, as of
landscapes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sketchbook</ent><br/
<hw>Sketch"book`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A book of sketches or for
sketches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sketcher</ent><br/
<hw>Sketch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sketches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sketchily</ent><br/
<hw>Sketch"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sketchy or
incomplete manner.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sketchily</xex> descriptive.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sketchiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sketch"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sketchy; lack of finish; incompleteness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sketchy</ent><br/
<hw>Sketch"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing only an
outline or rough form; being in the manner of a sketch;
incomplete.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The execution is <qex>sketchy</qex> throughout; the head, in
particular, is left in the rough.</q> <rj><qau>J. S.
Harford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skew</ent><br/
<hw>Skew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>scheef</ets>. Dan. <ets>ski<?/v</ets>, Sw. <ets>skef</ets>, Icel.
<ets>skeifr</ets>, G. <ets>schief</ets>, also E. <ets>shy</ets>, a. & v.
i.]</ety> <def>Awry; obliquely; askew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skew</ent><br/
<hw>Skew</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Turned or twisted to one side;
situated obliquely; skewed; -- chiefly used in technical
phrases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skew arch</b></col>, <cd>an oblique arch. See under
<er>Oblique</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Skew back</b></col>. <fld>(Civil
Engin.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The course of masonry, the stone, or the
iron plate, having an inclined face, which forms the abutment for the
voussoirs of a segmental arch</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A plate, cap, or
shoe, having an inclined face to receive the nut of a diagonal brace,
rod, or the end of an inclined strut, in a truss or frame.</cd> --
<col><b>Skew bridge</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Bridge</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Skew curve</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>,
<cd>a curve of double curvature, or a twisted curve. See <cref>Plane
curve</cref>, under <er>Curve</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Skew
gearing</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Skew bevel
gearing</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>toothed gearing,
generally resembling bevel gearing, for connecting two shafts that are
neither parallel nor intersecting, and in which the teeth slant across
the faces of the gears.</cd> -- <col><b>Skew surface</b></col>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a ruled surface such that in general two
successive generating straight lines do not intersect; a warped surface;
as, the helicoid is a <xex>skew surface</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Skew
symmetrical determinant</b></col> <fld>(Alg.)</fld>, <cd>a determinant
in which the elements in each column of the matrix are equal to the
elements of the corresponding row of the matrix with the signs changed,
as in (1), below.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><illust> (1) <matrix><row>0 2 -3</row><row>-2 0 5</row><row>3 -5
0</row></matrix> (2) <matrix><row>4 -1 7</row><row>1 8 -2</row><row>-7 2
1</row></matrix> </illust><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> <note>This requires that the numbers in the diagonal from the upper
left to lower right corner be zeros. A like determinant in which the
numbers in the diagonal are not zeros is a <xex>skew determinant</xex>,
as in (2), above.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skew</ent><br/
<hw>Skew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A
stone at the foot of the slope of a gable, the offset of a buttress, or
the like, cut with a sloping surface and with a check to receive the
coping stones and retain them in place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skew</ent><br/
<hw>Skew</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skewed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skewing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To walk obliquely; to
go sidling; to lie or move obliquely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Child, you must walk straight, without <qex>skewing</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To start aside; to shy, as a horse.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To look obliquely; to squint; hence, to look
slightingly or suspiciously.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skew</ent><br/
<hw>Skew</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Skew</er>,
<pos>adv.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shape or form in an oblique
way; to cause to take an oblique position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To throw or hurl obliquely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skewbald</ent><br/
<hw>Skew"bald`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Marked with spots
and patches of white and some color other than black; -- usually
distinguished from <xex>piebald</xex>, in which the colors are properly
white and black. Said of horses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skewer</ent><br/
<hw>Skew"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably of Scand,
origin; cf. Sw. & Dan. <ets>skifer</ets> a slate. Cf. <er>Shuver</er> a
fragment.]</ety> <def>A pin of wood or metal for fastening meat to a
spit, or for keeping it in form while roasting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Meat well stuck with <qex>skewers</qex> to make it look round.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skewer</ent><br/
<hw>Skew"er</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skewered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skewering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To fasten with
skewers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Ski</ent><br/
<hw>Ski</hw> <pr>(sk<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>skis</plw> <pr>(sk<emac/z)</pr></plu>. <ety>[Dan. <ets>ski</ets>;
Icel. <ets>sk<imac/<edh/</ets> a billet of wood. See
<er>Skid</er>.]</ety> <def>A long, flat, narrow runner made of wood,
plastic or metal, curved upwards in front, having a fitting allowing it
to be attached to the foot, and used for gliding or sliding over snow.
Commonly used in the plural, to designate the pair.</def> <altsp>[Also
spelled <asp>skee</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skiagraphy</ent><br/
<ent>Skiagraph</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Ski"a*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<hw>Ski*ag"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, etc.</mhw>
<def>See <er>Sciagraph</er>, <er>Sciagraphy</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sciascope</ent><br/
<ent>Skiascope</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ski"a*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sci"a*scope</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a shadow +
<ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A device for
determining the refractive state of the eye by observing the movements
of the retinal lights and shadows.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Ski*as"co*py</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <wf>Ski*as"co*py</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skid</ent><br/
<hw>Skid</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>sk<imac/<edh/</ets> a billet of wood. See <er>Shide</er>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>skeed</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A shoe
or clog, as of iron, attached to a chain, and placed under the wheel of
a wagon to prevent its turning when descending a steep hill; a drag; a
skidpan; also, by extension, a hook attached to a chain, and used for
the same purpose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A piece of timber used as a support, or to receive
pressure.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <pluf>pl.</pluf>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Large fenders hung over a vessel's side to
protect it in handling a cargo.</def> <au>Totten.</au> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>One of a pair of timbers or bars, usually arranged so as to form an
inclined plane, as form a wagon to a door, along which anything is moved
by sliding or rolling.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>One of a pair of
horizontal rails or timbers for supporting anything, as a boat, a
barrel, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Aeronautics)</fld> <def>A runner (one or two) under
some flying machines, used for landing.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A low movable platform for supporting heavy items to
be transported, typically of two layers, and having a space between the
layers into which the fork of a fork lift can be inserted; it is used to
conveniently transport heavy objects by means of a fork lift; -- a
<ex>skid</ex> without wheels is the same as a <er>pallet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Declining fortunes; a movement
toward defeat or downfall; -- used mostly in the phrase <col><b>on the
skids</b></col> and <col><b>hit the skids</b></col>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <ety>[From the <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>Act of skidding;
-- called also <altname>side slip</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skid</ent><br/
<hw>Skid</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skidded</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skidding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To protect or
support with a skid or skids; also, to cause to move on skids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To check with a skid, as wagon wheels.</def>
<rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Forestry)</fld> <def>To haul (logs) to a skid and
load on a skidway.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skid</ent><br/
<hw>Skid</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To slide without
rotating; -- said of a wheel held from turning while the vehicle moves
onward.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fail to grip the roadway; specif., to slip
sideways on the road; to side-slip; -- said esp. of a cycle or
automobile.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skiddaw</ent><br/
<hw>Skid"daw`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The black guillemot.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skidder</ent><br/
<hw>Skid"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One that skids; one
that uses a skid;</def> <specif>specif.:</specif> <fld>(Logging)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>One that skids logs.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An engine
for hauling the cable used in skidding logs.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
foreman of a construction gang making a skid road.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skidpan</ent><br/
<hw>Skid"pan`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Skid</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 1.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skid road</ent><br/
<hw>Skid road</hw>. <fld>(Logging)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A road along
which logs are dragged to the skidway or landing; -- called also
<altname>travois road</altname> <it>or</it> <altname>travoy
road</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A road having partly sunken
transverse logs (called <xex>skids</xex>) at intervals of about five
feet.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skied</ent><br/
<hw>Skied</hw> <pr>(sk<imac/d)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sky</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skied</ent><br/
<hw>Skied</hw> <pr>(sk<emac/d)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Ski</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos></def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skiey</ent><br/
<hw>Ski"ey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skyey</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shelley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skiff</ent><br/
<hw>Skiff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>esquif</ets>,
fr. OHG. <ets>skif</ets>, G. <ets>schiff</ets>. See
<er>Ship</er>.]</ety> <def>A small, light boat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pilot of some small night-foundered <qex>skiff</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skiff caterpillar</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
larva of a moth (<spn>Limacodes scapha</spn>); -- so called from its
peculiar shape.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skiff</ent><br/
<hw>Skiff</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skiffed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skiffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To navigate in a skiff.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skiffling</ent><br/
<hw>Skif"fling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Quarrying)</fld>
<def>Rough dressing by knocking off knobs or projections;
knobbing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skilder</ent><br/
<hw>Skil"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To beg; to pilfer;
to skelder.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skilful</ent><br/
<hw>Skil"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skillful</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skill</ent><br/
<hw>Skill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skil</ets> a
distinction, discernment; akin to <ets>skilja</ets> to separate, divide,
distinguish, Sw. <ets>skilja</ets>,. <ets>skille</ets> to separate,
<ets>skiel</ets> reason, right, justice, Sw. <ets>sk<aum/l</ets> reason,
Lith. <ets>skelli</ets> to cleave. Cf. <er>Shell</er>, <er>Shoal</er>, a
multitude.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Discrimination; judgment; propriety;
reason; cause.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/As it was
<xex>skill</xex> and right.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For great <qex>skill</qex> is, he prove that he wrought.</q> [For
with good reason he should test what he created.]
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Knowledge; understanding.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That by his fellowship he color might<br/
Both his estate and love from <qex>skill</qex> of any wight.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor want we <qex>skill</qex> or art.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The familiar knowledge of any art or science, united
with readiness and dexterity in execution or performance, or in the
application of the art or science to practical purposes; power to
discern and execute; ability to perceive and perform; expertness;
aptitude; <as>as, the <ex>skill</ex> of a mathematician, physician,
surgeon, mechanic, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Phocion, . . . by his great wisdom and <qex>skill</qex> at
negotiations, diverted Alexander from the conquest of Athens.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where patience her sweet <qex>skill</qex> imparts.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Display of art; exercise of ability; contrivance;
address.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Richard . . . by a thousand princely <qex>skills</qex>, gathering
so much corn as if he meant not to return.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Any particular art.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Learned in one <qex>skill</qex>, and in another kind of learning
unskillful.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Dexterity; adroitness; expertness; art; aptitude;
ability.</syn> -- <usage><er>Skill</er>, <er>Dexterity</er>,
<er>Adroitness</er>. <xex>Skill</xex> is more intelligent, denoting
familiar knowledge united to readiness of performance.
<xex>Dexterity</xex>, when applied to the body, is more mechanical, and
refers to habitual ease of execution. <xex>Adroitness</xex> involves the
same image with <xex>dexterity</xex>, and differs from it as implaying a
general facility of movement (especially in avoidance of danger or in
escaping from a difficalty). The same distinctions apply to the
figurative sense of the words. A man is <xex>skillful</xex> in any
employment when he understands both its theory and its practice. He is
<xex>dexterous</xex> when he maneuvers with great lightness. He is
<xex>adroit</xex> in the use od quick, sudden, and well-directed
movements of the body or the mind, so as to effect the object he has in
view.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skill</ent><br/
<hw>Skill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To know; to
understand.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>skill</qex> the arts of expressing our mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skill</ent><br/
<hw>Skill</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be knowing; to have
understanding; to be dexterous in performance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I can not <qex>skill</qex> of these thy ways.</q>
<rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a difference; to signify; to matter; -- used
impersonally.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>skills</qex> it, if a bag of stones or gold<br/
About thy neck do drown thee?</q> <rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It <qex>skills</qex> not talking of it.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skilled</ent><br/
<hw>Skilled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having familiar
knowledge united with readiness and dexterity in its application;
familiarly acquainted with; expert; skillful; -- often followed by
<xex>in</xex>; <as>as, a person <ex>skilled</ex> in drawing or
geometry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skillet</ent><br/
<hw>Skil"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escuelette</ets>, dim. of <ets>escuelle</ets> a porringer, F.
<ets>ecuelle</ets>, fr. L. <ets>scutella</ets>, dim. of
<ets>scutra</ets>, <ets>scuta</ets>, a dish. Cf. <er>Scuttle</er> a
basket.]</ety> <def>A small vessel of iron, copper, or other metal, with
a handle, used for culinary purpose, as for stewing meat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skillful</ent><br/
<hw>Skill"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>skilful</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Discerning; reasonable;
judicious; cunning.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Of
<xex>skillful</xex> judgment.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Possessed of, or displaying, skill; knowing and
ready; expert; well-versed; able in management; <as>as, a
<ex>skillful</ex> mechanic</as>; -- often followed by <xex>at</xex>,
<xex>in</xex>, or <xex>of</xex>; <as>as, <ex>skillful</ex> at the organ;
<ex>skillful</ex> in drawing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And they shall call the husbandman to mourning, and such as are
<qex>skillful</qex> of lamentations to wailing.</q> <rj><qau>Amos v.
16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Expert; skilled; dexterous; adept; masterly;
adroit; clever; cunning.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Skill"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Skill"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skilligalee</ent><br/
<hw>Skil`li*ga*lee"</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/l`l<icr/*g<adot/*l<emac/")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of thin, weak broth or oatmeal porridge,
served out to prisoners and paupers in England; also, a drink made of
oatmeal, sugar, and water, sometimes used in the English navy or
army.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>skilligolee</asp>,
<asp>skillygalee</asp>, etc.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skilling</ent><br/
<hw>Skil"ling</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/l"l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sheeling</er>.]</ety> <def>A bay of a barn; also, a slight addition
to a cottage.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skilling</ent><br/
<hw>Skil"ling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sw. & Dan. See
<er>Shilling</er>.]</ety> <def>A money of account in Sweden, Norway,
Denmark, and North Germany, and also a coin. It had various values, from
three fourths of a cent in Norway to more than two cents in
L<uum/beck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skill-less</ent><br/
<hw>Skill"-less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lacking skill.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skilts</ent><br/
<hw>Skilts</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>A kind of
large, coarse, short trousers formerly worn.</def> <mark>[Local, U.
S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skilty</ent><br/
<hw>Skil"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The water rail.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skim</ent><br/
<hw>Skim</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/m)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Skimmed</conjf> <pr>(sk<icr/md)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Skimming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Sw.
<ets>skymma</ets> to darken. <root/158. See <er>Scum</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To clear (a liquid) from scum or substance floating or
lying thereon, by means of a utensil that passes just beneath the
surface; <as>as, to <ex>skim</ex> milk; to <ex>skim</ex>
broth.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take off by skimming; <as>as, to <ex>skim</ex>
cream</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pass near the surface of; to brush the surface
of; to glide swiftly along the surface of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Homer describes Mercury as flinging himself from the top of
Olympus, and <qex>skimming</qex> the surface of the ocean.</q>
<rj><qau>Hazlitt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: To read or examine superficially and rapidly,
in order to cull the principal facts or thoughts; <as>as, to
<ex>skim</ex> a book or a newspaper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skim</ent><br/
<hw>Skim</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pass lightly; to
glide along in an even, smooth course; to glide along near the
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not so when swift Camilla scours the plain,<br/
Flies o'er the unbending corn, and <qex>skims</qex> along the main.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hasten along with superficial
attention.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>skim</qex> over a science in a very superficial
survey.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To put on the finishing coat of plaster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skim</ent><br/
<hw>Skim</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Contraction of <er>Skimming</er> and
<er>Skimmed</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skim coat</b></col>, <cd>the final or finishing coat of
plaster.</cd> -- <col><b>Skim colter</b></col>, <cd>a colter for paring
off the surface of land.</cd> -- <col><b>Skim milk</b></col>,
<cd>skimmed milk; milk from which the cream has been
taken.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Srim</ent><br/
<hw>Srim</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Scum; refuse.</def>
<rj><au>Bryskett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimback</ent><br/
<hw>Skim"back`</hw> <pr>(sk<icr/m"b<acr/k`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The quillback.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimble-scamble</ent><br/
<hw>Skim"ble-scam`ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[A
reduplication of <ets>scamble</ets>.]</ety> <def>Rambling; disorderly;
unconnected.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such a deal of <qex>skimble-scamble</qex> stuff.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimitry</ent><br/
<hw>Skim"i*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skimmington</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimmer</ent><br/
<hw>Skim"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, skims; esp., a utensil with which liquids are
skimmed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of longwinged marine
birds of the genus <gen>Rhynchops</gen>, allied to the terns, but having
the lower mandible compressed and much longer than the upper one. These
birds fly rapidly along the surface of the water, with the lower
mandible immersed, thus skimming out small fishes. The American species
(<spn>Rhynchops nigra</spn>) is common on the southern coasts of the
United States. Called also <altname>scissorbill</altname>, and
<altname>shearbill</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several large bivalve
shells, sometimes used for skimming milk, as the sea clams, and large
scallops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1350 --></p>

<p><ent>Skimmerton</ent><br/
<hw>Skim"mer*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skimmington</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimming</ent><br/
<hw>Skim"ming</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of one who skims.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is skimmed from the surface of a liquid;
-- chiefly used in the plural; <as>as, the <ex>skimmings</ex> of
broth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimmingly</ent><br/
<hw>Skim"ming*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a skimming
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimmington</ent><br/
<hw>Skim"ming*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain. Perhaps the name of some notorius scold.]</ety> <def>A word
employed in the phrase, <xex>To ride Skimmington</xex>; that is to ride
on a horse with a woman, but behind her, facing backward, carrying a
distaff, and accompanied by a procession of jeering neighbors making
mock music; a cavalcade in ridicule of a henpecked man. The custom was
in vogue in parts of England.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimp</ent><br/
<hw>Skimp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Skimped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skimping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. <er>Skinch</er>,
<er>Scamp</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To slight; to
do carelessly; to scamp.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make insufficient allowance for; to scant; to
scrimp.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq. U. S.]</mark> <-- used with
"on"; to skimp on clothing so as to have enough food. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimp</ent><br/
<hw>Skimp</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To save; to be parsimonious or
niggardly.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skimp</ent><br/
<hw>Skimp</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Scanty.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skin</ent><br/
<hw>Skin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skinn</ets>;
akin to Sw. <ets>skinn</ets>, Dan. <ets>skind</ets>, AS.
<ets>scinn</ets>, G. <ets>schined</ets> to skin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The external membranous integument of an
animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In man, and the vertebrates generally, the skin consist
of two layers, an outer nonsensitive and nonvascular
<xex>epidermis</xex>, <xex>cuticle</xex>, or <xex>skarfskin</xex>,
composed of cells which are constantly growing and multiplying in the
deeper, and being thrown off in the superficial, layers; and an inner
sensitive, and vascular <xex>dermis</xex>, <xex>cutis</xex>,
<xex>corium</xex>, or <xex>true skin</xex>, composed mostly of
connective tissue.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The hide of an animal, separated from the body,
whether green, dry, or tanned; especially, that of a small animal, as a
calf, sheep, or goat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A vessel made of skin, used for holding liquids. See
<er>Bottle</er>, 1.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Skins</xex> of wine.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The bark or husk of a plant or fruit; the exterior
coat of fruits and plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>That part of a sail,
when furled, which remains on the outside and covers the whole.</def>
<au>Totten.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The covering, as of planking or iron
plates, outside the framing, forming the sides and bottom of a vessel;
the shell; also, a lining inside the framing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Skin friction</b></col>, <col><b>Skin
resistance</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the friction, or
resistance, caused by the tendency of water to adhere to the immersed
surface (skin) of a vessel.</cd> -- <col><b>Skin graft</b></col>
<fld>(Surg.)</fld>, <cd>a small portion of skin used in the process of
grafting. See <er>Graft</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Skin
moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any insect which destroys the
prepared skins of animals, especially the larva of Dermestes and
Anthrenus.</cd> -- <col><b>Skin of the teeth</b></col>, <cd>nothing, or
next to nothing; the least possible hold or advantage.</cd> <au>Job xix.
20.</au> -- <col><b>Skin wool</b></col>, <cd>wool taken from dead
sheep.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skin</ent><br/
<hw>Skin</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skinned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skinning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strip off the
skin or hide of; to flay; to peel; <as>as, to <ex>skin</ex> an
animal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover with skin, or as with skin; hence, to cover
superficially.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It will but <qex>skin</qex> and film the ulcerous place.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To strip of money or property; to cheat.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skin</ent><br/
<hw>Skin</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To become covered with
skin; <as>as, a wound <ex>skins</ex> over</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To produce, in recitation, examination, etc., the
work of another for one's own, or to use in such exercise cribs,
memoranda, etc., which are prohibited.</def> <mark>[College Cant,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinbound</ent><br/
<hw>Skin"bound`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the skin
adhering closely and rigidly to the flesh; hidebound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skinbound disease</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See
<xex>Sclerema neonatorum</xex>, under <er>Sclerema</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinch</ent><br/
<hw>Skinch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Skinched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Skinching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Scant</er>.]</ety> <def>To give scant measure; to squeeze or pinch
in order to effect a saving.</def> <mark>[Prev. Eng. & Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skin-deep</ent><br/
<hw>Skin"-deep`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not deeper than
the skin; hence, superficial.</def> <rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinflint</ent><br/
<hw>Skin"flint`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Skin</ets> +
<ets>flint</ets>.]</ety> <def>A penurious person; a miser; a
niggard.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinful</ent><br/
<hw>Skin"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Skinfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>As much as a skin can
hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skink</ent><br/
<hw>Skink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>scincus</ets>,
Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>scink</asp>.]</altsp>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of regularly scaled
harmless lizards of the family <fam>Scincidae</fam>, common in the
warmer parts of all the continents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The officinal skink (<spn>Scincus officinalis</spn>)
inhabits the sandy plains of South Africa. It was believed by the
ancients to be a specific for various diseases. A common slender species
(<spn>Seps tridactylus</spn>) of Southern Europe was formerly believed
to produce fatal diseases in cattle by mere contact. The American skinks
include numerous species of the genus <gen>Eumeces</gen>, as the
blue-tailed skink (<spn>Eumeces fasciatus</spn>) of the Eastern United
States. The ground skink, or ground lizard (<spn>Oligosoma
laterale</spn>) inhabits the Southern United States.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skink</ent><br/
<hw>Skink</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skinked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skinking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Icel. <ets>skenja</ets>; akin
to Sw. <ets>sk<aum/ka</ets>, Dan. <ets>skienke</ets>, AS.
<ets>scencan</ets>, D. & G. <ets>schenken</ets>. As. <ets>scencan</ets>
is usually derived from <ets>sceonc</ets>, <ets>sceanc</ets>, shank, a
hollow bone being supposed to have been used to draw off liquor from a
cask. <root/161. See <er>Shank</er>, and cf. <er>Nunchion</er>.]</ety>
<def>To draw or serve, as drink.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bacchus the wine them <qex>skinketh</qex> all about.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such wine as Ganymede doth <qex>skink</qex> to Jove.</q>
<rj><qau>Shirley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skink</ent><br/
<hw>Skink</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To serve or draw liquor.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skink</ent><br/
<hw>Skink</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Drink; also, pottage.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinker</ent><br/
<hw>Skink"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who serves
liquor; a tapster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinless</ent><br/
<hw>Skin"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no skin, or a
very thin skin; <as>as, <ex>skinless</ex> fruit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinner</ent><br/
<hw>Skin"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
skins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who deals in skins, pelts, or hides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinniness</ent><br/
<hw>Skin"ni*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
skinny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skinny</ent><br/
<hw>Skin"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting, or chiefly
consisting, of skin; wanting flesh.</def> <ldquo/Her <xex>skinny</xex>
lips.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He holds him with a <qex>skinny</qex> hand.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skip</ent><br/
<hw>Skip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Skep</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A basket. See
<er>Skep</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A basket on wheels, used in cotton
factories.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>An iron bucket, which slides
between guides, for hoisting mineral and rock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Sugar Manuf.)</fld> <def>A charge of sirup in the
pans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A beehive; a skep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skip</ent><br/
<hw>Skip</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skipped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skipping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>skippen</ets>, of
uncertain origin; cf. Icel. <ets>skopa</ets> run, <ets>skoppa</ets> to
spin like a top, OSw. & dial. Sw. <ets>skimmpa</ets> to run,
<ets>skimpa</ets>, <ets>skompa</ets>, to hop, skip; or Ir.
<ets>sgiob</ets> to snatch, Gael. <ets>sgiab</ets> to start or move
suddenly, to snatch, W. <ets>ysgipio</ets> to snatch.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To leap lightly; to move in leaps and hounds; -- commonly implying
a sportive spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lamb thy riot dooms to bleed to-day,<br/
Had he thy reason, would he <qex>skip</qex> and play?</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So she drew her mother away <qex>skipping</qex>, dancing, and
frisking fantastically.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To leave matters unnoticed, as in reading,
speaking, or writing; to pass by, or overlook, portions of a thing; --
often followed by <xex>over</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skip</ent><br/
<hw>Skip</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To leap lightly over;
<as>as, to <ex>skip</ex> the rope</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pass over or by without notice; to omit; to miss;
<as>as, to <ex>skip</ex> a line in reading; to <ex>skip</ex> a
lesson</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They who have a mind to see the issue may <qex>skip</qex> these
two chapters.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to skip; <as>as, to <ex>skip</ex> a
stone</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skip</ent><br/
<hw>Skip</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A light leap or
bound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of passing over an interval from one thing
to another; an omission of a part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A passage from one sound to
another by more than a degree at once.</def>
<rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skip kennel</b></col>, <cd>a lackey; a footboy.</cd>
<mark>[Slang.]</mark> <au>Swift.</au> -- <col><b>Skip
mackerel</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Bluefish</er>,
1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skipjack</ent><br/
<hw>Skip"jack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
upstart.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An elater; a snap bug, or
snapping beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A name given to several kinds of
a fish, as the common bluefish, the alewife, the bonito, the butterfish,
the cutlass fish, the jurel, the leather jacket, the runner, the saurel,
the saury, the threadfish, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A shallow sailboat with a
rectilinear or V-shaped cross section.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skipper</ent><br/
<hw>Skip"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, skips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A young, thoughtless person.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The saury (<spn>Scomberesox
saurus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The cheese maggot. See <cref>Cheese fly</cref>,
under <er>Cheese</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
small butterflies of the family <fam>Hesperiadae</fam>; -- so called
from their peculiar short, jerking flight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skipper</ent><br/
<hw>Skip"per</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>schipper</ets>. See
<er>Shipper</er>, and <er>Ship</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The master of a fishing or small trading vessel;
hence, the master, or captain, of any vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A ship boy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Congreve.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skippet</ent><br/
<hw>Skip"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>skip</ets>, E. <ets>skipper</ets>. See <er>Ship</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A small boat; a skiff.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A little <qex>skippet</qex> floating did appear.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small round box for keeping records.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skippingly</ent><br/
<hw>Skip"ping*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a skipping
manner; by skips, or light leaps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirl</ent><br/
<hw>Skirl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[Of Scand.
origin, and originally the same word as E. <ets>shrill</ets>.]</ety>
<def>To utter in a shrill tone; to scream.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirl</ent><br/
<hw>Skirl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shrill cry or sound.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirlcock</ent><br/
<hw>Skirl"cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The missel thrush; -- so called from its harsh alarm note.</def>
<mark>[Prev. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirlcrake</ent><br/
<hw>Skirl"crake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
turnstone.</def> <mark>[Prev. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirling</ent><br/
<hw>Skirl"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shrill cry or sound; a crying
shrilly; a skirl.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When the <qex>skirling</qex> of the pipes cleft the air his cold
eyes softened.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs. J. H. Ewing.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirling</ent><br/
<hw>Skirl"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small trout
or salmon; -- a name used loosely.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirmish</ent><br/
<hw>Skir"mish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Skirmished</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Skirmishing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>skirmishen</ets>, <ets>scarmishen</ets>, OF. <ets>escremir</ets>,
<ets>eskermir</ets>, to fence, fight, F. <ets>escrimer</ets>, of German
origin; cf. OHG. <ets>scirmen</ets> to protect, defend, G.
<ets>schirmen</ets>, OHG. <ets>scirm</ets>, <ets>scerm</ets>,
protection, shield, G. <ets>schirm</ets>; perhaps akin to Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ a sunshade. Cf. <er>Scaramouch</er>,
<er>Scrimmage</er>.]</ety> <def>To fight slightly or in small parties;
to engage in a skirmish or skirmishes; to act as skirmishers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirmish</ent><br/
<hw>Skir"mish</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[OE. <ets>scarmishe</ets>,
<ets>scrymishe</ets>. See <er>Skirmish</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A slight fight in war; a light or desultory combat
between detachments from armies, or between detached and small bodies of
troops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A slight contest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They never meet but there's a <qex>skirmish</qex> of wit.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirmisher</ent><br/
<hw>Skir"mish*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
skirmishes.</def> Specifically: <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>
<def>Soldiers deployed in loose order, to cover the front or flanks of
an advancing army or a marching column.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirr</ent><br/
<hw>Skirr</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Scur</er>,
<er>Scurry</er>.]</ety> <def>To ramble over in order to clear; to
scour.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirr</ent><br/
<hw>Skirr</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To scour; to scud; to run.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirr</ent><br/
<hw>Skirr</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tern.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirret</ent><br/
<hw>Skir"ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corrupted form
equivalent to <ets>sugarwort</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An
umbelliferous plant (<spn>Sium Sisarum</spn> syn. <spn>Pimpinella
Sisarum</spn>). It is a native of Asia, but has been long cultivated in
Europe for its edible clustered tuberous roots, which are very
sweet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirrhus</ent><br/
<hw>Skir"rhus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Scirrhus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirt</ent><br/
<hw>Skirt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>skyrt</ets>,
of Scand. origin; cf. Icel. <ets>skyrta</ets> a shirt, Sw.
<ets>sk<oum/rt</ets> a skirt, <ets>skjorta</ets> a shirt. See
<er>Shirt</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The lower and loose part of a
coat, dress, or other like garment; the part below the waist; <as>as,
the <ex>skirt</ex> of a coat, a dress, or a mantle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A loose edging to any part of a dress.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A narrow lace, or a small <qex>skirt</qex> of ruffled linen, which
runs along the upper part of the stays before, and crosses the breast,
being a part of the tucker, is called the modesty piece.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Border; edge; margin; extreme part of anything</def>
<ldquo/Here in the <xex>skirts</xex> of the forest.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A petticoat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The diaphragm, or midriff, in animals.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirt</ent><br/
<hw>Skirt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skirted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skirting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover with a
skirt; to surround.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Skirted</qex> his loins and thighs with downy gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To border; to form the border or edge of; to run
along the edge of; <as>as, the plain was <ex>skirted</ex> by rows of
trees</as>.</def> <ldquo/When sundown <xex>skirts</xex> the moor.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirt</ent><br/
<hw>Skirt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To be on the border; to live near
the border, or extremity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Savages . . . who <qex>skirt</qex> along our western
frontiers.</q> <rj><qau>S. S. Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skirting</ent><br/
<hw>Skirt"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A
skirting board.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Skirts, taken collectively; material for
skirts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skirting board</b></col>, <cd>the board running around a
room on the wall next the floor; baseboard.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skit</ent><br/
<hw>Skit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>skit</ets>to slide, as adj., hasty, precipitate, of Scand. origin,
and akin to E. <ets>shoot</ets>, v.t.; cf. Icel. <ets>skyti</ets>,
<ets>skytja</ets>, <ets>skytta</ets>, a marksman, shooter,
<ets>skj<omac/ta</ets> to shoot, <ets>sk<umac/ta</ets> a taunt.
<root/159. See <er>Shoot</er>.]</ety> <def>To cast reflections on; to
asperse.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Crose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skit</ent><br/
<hw>Skit</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A reflection; a jeer or
gibe; a sally; a brief satire; a squib.</def>
<rj><au>Tooke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A similar vein satire upon the emptiness of writers is given in
his <ldquo/Tritical Essay upon the Faculties of the Human Mind;<rdquo/
but that is a mere <qex>skit</qex> compared with this strange
performance.</q> <rj><qau>Leslie Stephen.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A wanton girl; a light wench.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A short theatrical presentation, often comical or
satirical. Comical <ex>skits</ex> are sometimes presented by amateur or
ad hoc groups at parties, dinners, or other social gatherings.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skitter</ent><br/
<hw>Skit"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Skit</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To move or pass (something)
over a surface quickly so that it touches only at intervals; to
skip.</def></p>

<p><q>The angler, standing in the bow, <qex>'skitters'</qex> or skips
the spoon over the surface.</q> <rj><qau>James A.
Henshall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skitter</ent><br/
<hw>Skit"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To pass or glide lightly or
with quick touches at intervals; to skip; to skim.</def></p>

<p><q>Some kinds of ducks in lighting strike the water with their tails
first, and <qex>skitter</qex> along the surface for a feet before
settling down.</q> <rj><qau>T. Roosevelt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skittish</ent><br/
<hw>Skit"tish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Skit</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Easily frightened; timorous;
shy; untrustworthy; <as>as, a <ex>skittish</ex> colt</as>.</def>
<ldquo/A restiff, <xex>skittish</xex> jade.<rdquo/
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wanton; restive; freakish; volatile; changeable;
fickle.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Skittish</xex> Fortune's hall.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Skit"tish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Skit"tish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skittle</ent><br/
<hw>Skit"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the
game of skittles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skittle alley</b></col>, <cd>an alley or court in which
the game of skittles is played.</cd> -- <col><b>Skittle ball</b></col>,
<cd>a disk or flattish ball of wood for throwing at the pins in the game
of skittles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skittle-dog</ent><br/
<hw>Skit"tle-dog`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The piked dogfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skittles</ent><br/
<hw>Skit"tles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Of Scand.
origin. <root/159. See <er>Shoot</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Shuttle</er>, <er>Skit</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>An English
game resembling ninepins, but played by throwing wooden disks, instead
of rolling balls, at the pins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skitty</ent><br/
<hw>Skit"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Skittish</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A rail; as, the water
rail (called also <altname>skitty cock</altname>, and <altname>skitty
coot</altname>); the spotted crake (<spn>Porzana maruetta</spn>), and
the moor hen.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skive</ent><br/
<hw>Skive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>sk<imac/fa</ets> a shaving, slice, E. <ets>shive</ets>,
<ets>sheave</ets>.]</ety> <def>The iron lap used by diamond polishers in
finishing the facets of the gem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skive</ent><br/
<hw>Skive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pare or shave off
the rough or thick parts of (hides or leather).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skiver</ent><br/
<hw>Skiv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Skewer</er>,
<er>Shiver</er> a fragment.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An inferior quality
of leather, made of split sheepskin, tanned by immersion in sumac, and
dyed. It is used for hat linings, pocketbooks, bookbinding,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The cutting tool or machine used in splitting
leather or skins, as sheepskins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skiving</ent><br/
<hw>Ski"ving</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of paring or splitting leather or skins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A piece made in paring or splitting leather;
specifically, the part from the inner, or flesh, side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sklayre</ent><br/
<hw>Sklayre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schleier</ets>.]</ety> <def>A veil.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sklere</ent><br/
<hw>Sklere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To shelter; to
cover.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skolezite</ent><br/
<ent>Skolecite</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Skol"e*cite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Skol"e*zite</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Scolecite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skonce</ent><br/
<hw>Skonce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sconce</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skopje</ent><br/
<hw>Skop"je</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>The capital
<isa>city</isa> of the Republic of Macedonia, population 448,229.<br/
Skopje is modern city, but it is at the same time an ancient city with
deep roots in the past and with a rich history. During the Roman period
it was known under the name of Scupi. Later the town acquired its Slav
name of Skopje. Skopje experienced a swift development after World War
II. This trend was interrupted in 1963 when it was hit by a disastrous
earthquake. Thanks to the solidarity of the whole world, Skopje was
quickly renewed and rebuilt. Today Skopje is the political, economic,
cultural and academic center of Macedonia (he Former Yugoslav Republic
of Macedonia. See also <er>Macedonia</er>{2}.</def> <au>Goce
Vasilevski</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skopster</ent><br/
<hw>Skop"ster</hw> <pr>(sk<ocr/p"st<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
saury.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skoptsy</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Skop*tsy"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Raskolnik</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skorodite</ent><br/
<hw>Skor"o*dite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Scorodite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skout</ent><br/
<hw>Skout</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
guillemot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skowitz</ent><br/
<hw>Sko"witz</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Nisqually (American
Indian) name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The silver
salmon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skreen</ent><br/
<hw>Skreen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Screen</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skrike</ent><br/
<hw>Skrike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To
shriek.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skrike</ent><br/
<hw>Skrike</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The missel
thrush.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skrimmage</ent><br/
<hw>Skrim"mage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scrimmage</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skrimp</ent><br/
<hw>Skrimp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scrimp</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skringe</ent><br/
<hw>Skringe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scringe</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skrite</ent><br/
<hw>Skrite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
skrike.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skua</ent><br/
<hw>Sku"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>sk<?/fr</ets>, <ets>sk<?/mr</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any jager gull; especially, the <spn>Megalestris skua</spn>; --
called also <altname>boatswain</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skue</ent><br/
<hw>Skue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Skew</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skulk</ent><br/
<hw>Skulk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Skulked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Skulking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of Scand. origin; cf. Dan.
<ets>skulke</ets> to spare or save one's self, to play the truant, Sw.
<ets>skolka</ets> to be at leisure, to shirk, Icel. <ets>skolla</ets>.
Cf. <er>Scowl</er>.]</ety> <def>To hide, or get out of the way, in a
sneaking manner; to lie close, or to move in a furtive way; to
lurk.</def> <ldquo/Want <xex>skulks</xex> in holes and crevices.<rdquo/
<rj><au>W. C. Bryant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Discovered and defeated of your prey,<br/
You <qex>skulked</qex> behind the fence, and sneaked away.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skulk</ent><br/
<hw>Skulk</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel. <ets>skollr</ets>,
<ets>skolli</ets>, a fox, and E. <ets>skulk</ets>, v.i.]</ety> <def>A
number of foxes together.</def> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1351 --></p>

<p><ent>Skulker</ent><br/
<ent>Skulk</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Skulk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Skulk"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that which, skulks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skulkingly</ent><br/
<hw>Skulk"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a skulking
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skull</ent><br/
<hw>Skull</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>School</er> a
multitude.]</ety> <def>A school, company, or shoal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A knavish <qex>skull</qex> of boys and girls did pelt at him.</q>
<rj><au>Warner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These fishes enter in great flotes and skulls.</q>
<rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skull</ent><br/
<hw>Skull</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>skulle</ets>,
<ets>sculle</ets>, <ets>scolle</ets>; akin to Scot. <ets>skull</ets>,
<ets>skoll</ets>, a bowl, Sw. <ets>skalle</ets> skull, <ets>skal</ets> a
shell, and E. <ets>scale</ets>; cf. G. hirn<ets>schale</ets>, Dan.
hierne<ets>skal</ets>. Cf. <er>Scale</er> of a balance.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The skeleton of the head of a
vertebrate animal, including the brain case, or cranium, and the bones
and cartilages of the face and mouth. See <xex>Illusts</xex>. of
<er>Carnivora</er>, of <cref>Facial angles</cref> under <er>Facial</er>,
and of <cref>Skeleton</cref>, in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In many fishes the skull is almost wholly cartilaginous
but in the higher vertebrates it is more or less completely ossified,
several bones are developed in the face, and the cranium is made up,
wholly or partially, of bony plates arranged in three segments, the
<xex>frontal</xex>, <xex>parietal</xex>, and <xex>occipital</xex>, and
usually closely united in the adult.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The head or brain; the seat of intelligence;
mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Skulls</qex> that can not teach, and will not learn.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A covering for the head; a skullcap.</def>
<mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let me put on my <qex>skull</qex> first.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. &
Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sort of oar. See <er>Scull</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Skull and crossbones</b></col>, <cd>a symbol of death.
See <er>Crossbones</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skullcap</ent><br/
<hw>Skull"cap`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A cap
which fits the head closely; also, formerly, a headpiece of iron sewed
inside of a cap for protection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of the labiate genus
<gen>Scutellaria</gen>, the calyx of whose flower appears, when
inverted, like a helmet with the visor raised.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Lophiomys.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Mad-dog skullcap</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an
American herb (<spn>Scetellaria lateriflora</spn>) formerly prescribed
as a cure for hydrophobia.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skullfish</ent><br/
<hw>Skull"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A whaler's name
for a whale more than two years old.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skulpin</ent><br/
<hw>Skul"pin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sculpin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skun</ent><br/
<hw>Skun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skunk</ent><br/
<hw>Skunk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contr. from the Abenaki
(American Indian) <ets>seganku</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
one of several species of American musteline carnivores of the genus
<gen>Mephitis</gen> and allied genera. They have two glands near the
anus, secreting an extremely fetid liquid, which the animal ejects at
pleasure as a means of defense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common species of the Eastern United States
(<spn>Mephitis mephitica</spn>) is black with more or less white on the
body and tail. The spotted skunk (<spn>Spilogale putorius</spn>), native
of the Southwestern United States and Mexico, is smaller than the common
skunk, and is variously marked with black and white.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Skunk bird</b></col>, <col><b>Skunk
blackbird</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the bobolink; -- so
called because the male, in the breeding season, is black and white,
like a skunk.</cd> -- <col><b>Skunk cabbage</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>an American aroid herb (<spn>Symplocarpus f<oe/tidus</spn>) having a
reddish hornlike spathe in earliest spring, followed by a cluster of
large cabbagelike leaves. It exhales a disagreeable odor. Also called
<altname>swamp cabbage</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Skunk
porpoise</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Porpoise</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skunk</ent><br/
<hw>Skunk</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>In games of chance and skill: To
defeat (an opponent) (as in cards) so that he fails to gain a point, or
(in checkers) to get a king.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skunkball</ent><br/
<hw>Skunk"ball`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The surf duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skunkhead</ent><br/
<hw>Skunk"head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The surf duck.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A duck
(<spn>Camptolaimus Labradorus</spn>) which formerly inhabited the
Atlantic coast of New England. It is now supposed to be extinct. Called
also <altname>Labrador duck</altname>, and <altname>pied
duck</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skunkish</ent><br/
<hw>Skunk"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like the skunk, especially in
odor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skunktop</ent><br/
<hw>Skunk"top`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The surf duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skunkweed</ent><br/
<hw>Skunk"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Skunk cabbage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skurry</ent><br/
<hw>Skur"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Scurry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skute</ent><br/
<hw>Skute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>sk<?/ta</ets>; akin to Sw. <ets>skuta</ets>, Dan. <ets>skude</ets>,
D. <ets>schuit</ets>, Lg. <ets>sch<uum/te</ets>, and E.
<ets>schoot</ets>, v.t.]</ety> <def>A boat; a small vessel.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir R. Williams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skutterudite</ent><br/
<hw>Skut"ter*ud*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Skutterud</ets>, in Norway, whence it is obtained.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A mineral of a bright metallic luster and
tin-white to pale lead-gray color. It consists of arsenic and
cobalt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sky</ent><br/
<hw>Sky</hw> <pr>(sk<imac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Skies</plw> <pr>(sk<imac/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>skie</ets> a
cloud, Icel. <ets>sk<ymac/</ets>; akin to Sw. & Dan. <ets>sky</ets>; cf.
AS. <ets>sc<umac/a</ets>, <ets>sc<umac/wa</ets>, shadow, Icel.
<ets>skuggi</ets>; probably from the same root as E. <ets>scum</ets>.
<root/158. See <er>Scum</er>, and cf. <er>Hide</er> skin,
<er>Obscure</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A cloud.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[A wind] that blew so hideously and high,<br/
That it ne lefte not a <qex>sky</qex><br/
In all the welkin long and broad.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a shadow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She passeth as it were a <qex>sky</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Gower.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The apparent arch, or vault, of heaven, which in a
clear day is of a blue color; the heavens; the firmament; -- sometimes
in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Norweyan banners flout the <qex>sky</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The weather; the climate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou wert better in thy grave than to answer with thy uncovered
body this extremity of the <qex>skies</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sky</xex> is often used adjectively or in the
formation of self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>sky</xex> color,
<xex>sky</xex>light, <xex>sky</xex>-aspiring, <xex>sky</xex>-born,
<xex>sky</xex>-pointing, <xex>sky</xex>-roofed, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sky blue</b></col>, <cd>an azure color.</cd> --
<col><b>Sky scraper</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a skysail of a
triangular form.</cd> <au>Totten.</au> -- <col><b>Under open
sky</b></col>, <cd>out of doors.</cd> <ldquo/<xex>Under open sky</xex>
adored.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au></cs> <-- sky scraper, a tall building,
usu. skyscraper --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sky</ent><br/
<hw>Sky</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Skied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Skyed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Skying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To hang (a picture on exhibition) near the top of a wall, where it
can not be well seen.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Brother Academicians who <qex>skied</qex> his pictures.</q>
<rj><qau>The Century.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To throw towards the sky; <as>as, to <ex>sky</ex> a
ball at cricket</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sky-blue</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"-blue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the blue
color of the sky; azure; <as>as, a <ex>sky-blue</ex> stone</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skyed</ent><br/
<hw>Skyed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Surrounded by sky.</def>
<mark>[Poetic & R.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>skyed</xex> mountain.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skye terrier</ent><br/
<hw>Skye" ter"ri*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Terrier</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skyey</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"ey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like the sky; ethereal;
being in the sky.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Skyey</xex> regions.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sublime on the towers of my <qex>skyey</qex> bowers,<br/
Lightning, my pilot, sits.</q> <rj><qau>Shelley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sky-high</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"-high`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv. & a.</pos> <def>Very
high.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skyish</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like the sky, or approaching the
sky; lofty; ethereal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skylark</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"lark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
lark that mounts and sings as it files, especially the common species
(<spn>Alauda arvensis</spn>) found in Europe and in some parts of Asia,
and celebrated for its melodious song; -- called also <altname>sky
laverock</altname>. See under <er>Lark</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The Australian skylark (<spn>Cincloramphus
cantillans</spn>) is a pipit which has the habit of ascending
perpendicularly like a skylark, but it lacks the song of a true lark.
The Missouri skylark is a pipit (<spn>Anthus Spraguei</spn>) of the
Western United States, resembling the skylark in habit and
song.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skylarking</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"lark"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of running about the
rigging of a vessel in sport; hence, frolicking; scuffing; sporting;
carousing.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skylight</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"light`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A window placed in
the roof of a building, in the ceiling of a room, or in the deck of a
ship, for the admission of light from above.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skyman</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu>pl.
<plw>-men</plw>.</plu> <def>An aeronaut.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> airman; pilot.</syn><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sky pilot</ent><br/
<hw>Sky pilot</hw>. <fld>(Aeronautics)</fld> <def>A person licensed as
an airplane pilot.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>skyrocket</ent><br/
<hw>sky"rock`et</hw> <pr>(sk<imac/"r<ocr/k`<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A rocket that ascends high and burns as it flies; a species of
fireworks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>skyrocket</ent><br/
<hw>sky"rock`et</hw> <pr>(sk<imac/"r<ocr/k`<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>v.
i.</pos> <def>To rise rapidly; -- usually used figuratively, as of
prices.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skysail</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"sail</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The sail set next above the royal. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Sail</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skyscraper</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"scrap`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> (1) <def>A skysail of a triangular form.</def>
<mark>[Rare]</mark> (2) <def>A name for the one of the fancy sails
alleged to have been sometimes set above the skysail.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A very tall building, especially
one over 20 stories high.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Hence, anything
usually large, high, or excessive.</def> <mark>[Slang or
Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Skyward</ent><br/
<hw>Sky"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos> <def>Toward the
sky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slab</ent><br/
<hw>Slab</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>slabbe</ets>,
of uncertain origin; perhaps originally meaning, a smooth piece, and
akin to <ets>slape</ets>, Icel. <ets>sleipr</ets> slippery, and E.
<ets>slip</ets>, v. i.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thin piece of anything,
especially of marble or other stone, having plane surfaces.</def>
<rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An outside piece taken from a log or timber in
sawing it into boards, planks, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The wryneck.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The slack part of a
sail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slab line</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a line or
small rope by which seamen haul up the foot of the mainsail or
foresail.</cd> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slab</ent><br/
<hw>Slab</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gael. & Ir. <ets>slaib</ets> mud,
mire left on a river strand, and E. <ets>slop</ets> puddle.]</ety>
<def>Thick; viscous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Make the gruel thick and <qex>slab</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slab</ent><br/
<hw>Slab</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is slimy or viscous; moist
earth; mud; also, a puddle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabber</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slabbered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Slabbering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slaberen</ets>; akin to LG. & D. <ets>slabbern</ets>, G.
<ets>schlabbern</ets>, LG. & D. <ets>slabben</ets>, G.
<ets>schlabben</ets>, Icel. <ets>slafra</ets>. Cf. <er>Slaver</er>,
<er>Slobber</er>, <er>Slubber</er>.]</ety> <def>To let saliva or some
liquid fall from the mouth carelessly, like a child or an idiot; to
drivel; to drool.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>slaver</asp>, and
<asp>slobber</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabber</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"ber</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To wet and foul
spittle, or as if with spittle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>slabbered</qex> me over, from cheek to cheek, with his
great tongue.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To spill liquid upon; to smear carelessly; to spill,
as liquid food or drink, in careless eating or drinking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The milk pan and cream pot so <qex>slabbered</qex> and tost<br/
That butter is wanting and cheese is half lost.</q>
<rj><qau>Tusser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabber</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"ber</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Spittle; saliva; slaver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabber</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 1st
<er>Slab</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A saw for
cutting slabs from logs.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A slabbing
machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabberer</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"ber*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who slabbers,
or drools; hence, an idiot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabbery</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"ber*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like, or covered
with, slabber or slab; slippery; sloppy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabbiness</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"bi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
slabby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabbing</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"bing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 1st
<er>Slab</er>.]</ety> <def>Adapted for forming slabs, or for dressing
flat surfaces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slabbing machine</b></col>, <cd>a milling
machine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slabby</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Slabbier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Slabbiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[See <er>Slab</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Thick; viscous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They present you with a cup, and you must drink of a
<qex>slabby</qex> stuff.</q> <rj><qau>Selden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sloppy; slimy; miry. See <er>Sloppy</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Gay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slab-sided</ent><br/
<hw>Slab"-sid`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having flat
sides; hence, tall, or long and lank.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slack</ent><br/
<hw>Slack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Slag</er>.]</ety> <def>Small coal; also, coal dust; culm.</def>
<rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slack</ent><br/
<hw>Slack</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>slakki</ets> a slope on a
mountain edge.]</ety> <def>A valley, or small, shallow dell.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slack</ent><br/
<hw>Slack</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Slacker</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Slackest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slak</ets>, AS.
<ets>sleac</ets>; akin to OS. <ets>slak</ets>, OHG. <ets>slah</ets>,
Prov. G. <ets>schlack</ets>, Icel. <ets>slakr</ets>, Sw.
<ets>slak</ets>; cf. Skr. <ets>s<rsdot/j</ets> to let loose, to throw.
Cf. <er>Slake</er>.]</ety> <def>Lax; not tense; not hard drawn; not
firmly extended; <as>as, a <ex>slack</ex> rope</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Weak; not holding fast; <as>as, a <ex>slack</ex>
hand</as>.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Remiss; backward; not using due diligence or care;
not earnest or eager; <as>as, <ex>slack</ex> in duty or
service</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord is not <qex>slack</qex> concerning his promise, as some
men count slackness.</q> <rj><qau>2 Pet. iii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not violent, rapid, or pressing; slow; moderate;
easy; <as>as, business is <ex>slack</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/With
<xex>slack</xex> pace.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Caesar . . . about sunset, hoisting sail with a <qex>slack</qex>
southwest, at midnight was becalmed.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slack in stays</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>slow in
going about, as a ship.</cd> -- <col><b>Slack water</b></col>, <cd>the
time when the tide runs slowly, or the water is at rest; or the interval
between the flux and reflux of the tide.</cd> -- <col><b>Slack-water
navigation</b></col>, <cd>navigation in a stream the depth of which has
been increased, and the current diminished, by a dam or
dams.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Loose; relaxed; weak; remiss; backward; abated;
diminished; inactive; slow; tardy; dull.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slack</ent><br/
<hw>Slack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Slackly; <as>as,
<ex>slack</ex> dried hops</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slack</ent><br/
<hw>Slack</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The part of anything that hangs
loose, having no strain upon it; <as>as, the <ex>slack</ex> of a rope or
of a sail</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slacken</ent><br/
<ent>Slack</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Slack"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slacked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, <conjf>Slackened</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Slacking</conjf>,
<conjf>Slackening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Slack</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To become slack; to be made less
tense, firm, or rigid; to decrease in tension; <as>as, a wet cord
<ex>slackens</ex> in dry weather</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be remiss or backward; to be negligent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To lose cohesion or solidity by a chemical
combination with water; to slake; <as>as, lime
<ex>slacks</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To abate; to become less violent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whence these raging fires<br/
Will <qex>slacken</qex>, if his breath stir not their flames.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To lose rapidity; to become more slow; <as>as, a
current of water <ex>slackens</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To languish; to fail; to flag.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To end; to cease; to desist; to slake.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That through your death your lineage should <qex>slack</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They will not of that firste purpose <qex>slack</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slacken</ent><br/
<ent>Slack</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slack</hw>, <hw>Slack"en</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To render slack; to make less tense or firm; <as>as, to
<ex>slack</ex> a rope; to <ex>slacken</ex> a bandage</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Wycklif (Acts xxvii. 40)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To neglect; to be remiss in.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Slack</qex> not the pressage.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To deprive of cohesion by combining chemically with
water; to slake; <as>as, to <ex>slack</ex> lime</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to become less eager; to repress; to make
slow or less rapid; to retard; <as>as, to <ex>slacken</ex> pursuit; to
<ex>slacken</ex> industry</as>.</def> <ldquo/Rancor for to
<xex>slack</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I should be grieved, young prince, to think my presence<br/
Unbent your thoughts, and <qex>slackened</qex> 'em to arms.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In this business of growing rich, poor men should <qex>slack</qex>
their pace.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With such delay<br/
Well plased, they <qex>slack</qex> their course.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To cause to become less intense; to mitigate; to
abate; to ease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To respite, or deceive, or <qex>slack</qex> thy pain<br/
Of this ill mansion.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Air-slacked lime</b></col>, <cd>lime slacked by exposure
to the air, in consequence of the absorption of carton dioxide and
water, by which it is converted into carbonate of lime and hydrate of
lime.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slacken</ent><br/
<hw>Slack"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>A
spongy, semivitrifled substance which miners or smelters mix with the
ores of metals to prevent their fusion.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>slakin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slackly</ent><br/
<hw>Slack"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a slack manner.</def>
<rj><au>Trench.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slackness</ent><br/
<hw>Slack"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
slack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slade</ent><br/
<hw>Slade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sl<?/d</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A little dell or valley; a
flat piece of low, moist ground.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The sole of a plow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slag</ent><br/
<hw>Slag</hw> <pr>(sl<acr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sw.
<ets>slagg</ets>, or LG. <ets>slacke</ets>, whence G.
<ets>schlacke</ets>; originally, perhaps, the splinters struck off from
the metal by hammering. See <er>Slay</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The dross, or recrement, of a metal; also, vitrified
cinders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The scoria of a volcano.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>A product of smelting,
containing, mostly as silicates, the substances not sought to be
produced as matte or metal, and having a lower specific gravity than the
latter; -- called also, esp. in iron smelting,
<altname>cinder</altname>. The slag of iron blast furnaces is
essentially silicate of calcium, magnesium, and aluminium; that of lead
and copper smelting furnaces contains iron.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Slag furnace</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Slag
hearth</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a furnace, or hearth,
for extracting lead from slags or poor ore.</cd> -- <col><b>Slag
wool</b></col>, <cd>mineral wool. See under
<er>Mineral</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slag</ent><br/
<hw>Slag</hw>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slagged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slagging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>To form, or
form into, a slag; to agglomerate when heated below the fusion
point.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaggy</ent><br/
<hw>Slag"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
slag; resembling slag; <as>as, <ex>slaggy</ex> cobalt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaie</ent><br/
<hw>Slaie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sley</er>.]</ety> <def>A weaver's reed; a sley.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slake</ent><br/
<hw>Slake</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slaked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slaken</ets> to render
slack, to slake, AS. <ets>sleacian</ets>, fr. <ets>sleac</ets> slack.
See <er>Slack</er>, <pos>v. & a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To allay;
to quench; to extinguish; <as>as, to <ex>slake</ex> thirst</as>.</def>
<ldquo/And <xex>slake</xex> the heavenly fire.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It could not <qex>slake</qex> mine ire nor ease my heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mix with water, so that a true chemical
combination shall take place; to slack; <as>as, to <ex>slake</ex>
lime</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slake</ent><br/
<hw>Slake</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To go out; to become
extinct.</def> <ldquo/His flame did <xex>slake</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir
T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To abate; to become less decided.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To slacken; to become relaxed.</def> <ldquo/When the
body's strongest sinews <xex>slake</xex>.<rdquo/ <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir J. Davies.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To become mixed with water, so that a true chemical
combination takes place; <as>as, the lime
<ex>slakes</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slake trough</b></col>, <cd>a trough containing water in
which a blacksmith cools a forging or tool.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slakeless</ent><br/
<hw>Slake"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not capable of being
slaked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slakin</ent><br/
<hw>Slak"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>
<def>Slacken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slam</ent><br/
<hw>Slam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slammed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slamming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of Scand. origin; cf. Icel.
<ets>slamra</ets>, <ets>slambra</ets>, <ets>sl<?/ma</ets>, Norw.
<ets>slemba</ets>, <ets>slemma</ets>, dial. Sw.
<ets>sl<aum/mma</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shut with force and a
loud noise; to bang; <as>as, he <ex>slammed</ex> the
door</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put in or on some place with force and loud
noise; -- usually with <xex>down</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>slam</ex> a trunk
down on the pavement</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To strike with some implement with force; hence, to
beat or cuff.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To strike down; to slaughter.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To defeat (opponents at cards) by winning all the
tricks of a deal or a hand.</def> <rj><au>Hoyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To slam to</b></col>, <cd>to shut or close with a
slam.</cd> <ldquo/He <xex>slammed to</xex> the door.<rdquo/ <au>W. D.
Howells.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slam</ent><br/
<hw>Slam</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To come or swing against something,
or to shut, with sudden force so as to produce a shock and noise;
<as>as, a door or shutter <ex>slams</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slam</ent><br/
<hw>Slam</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who, or
that which, slams.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The shock and noise produced in slamming.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>slam</qex> and the scowl were lost upon Sam.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>Winning all the tricks of
a deal (called, in bridge, <col><b>grand slam</b></col>, the winning of
all but one of the thirteen tricks being called a <col><b>little
slam</b></col> or <col><b>small slam</b></col>).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The refuse of alum works.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slam-bang</ent><br/
<hw>Slam"-bang`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With great
violence; with a slamming or banging noise.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slammerkin</ent><br/
<ent>Slamkin</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slam"kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Slam"mer*kin</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>schlampe</ets>,
<ets>schlamp</ets>, dim. <ets>schl<aum/mpchen</ets>;
<ets>schlampen</ets> to dangle, to be slovenly in one's dress.]</ety>
<def>A slut; a slatternly woman.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slander</ent><br/
<hw>Slan"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sclandere</ets>, OF. <ets>esclandre</ets>, <ets>esclandle</ets>,
<ets>escandre</ets>, F. <ets>esclandre</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>scandalum</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a snare, stumbling block, offense,
scandal; probably originally, the spring of a trap, and akin to Skr.
<ets>skand</ets> to spring, leap. See <er>Scan</er>, and cf.
<er>Scandal</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A false tale or report
maliciously uttered, tending to injure the reputation of another; the
malicious utterance of defamatory reports; the dissemination of
malicious tales or suggestions to the injury of another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whether we speak evil of a man to his face or behind his back; the
former way, indeed, seems to be the most generous, but yet is a great
fault, and that which we call <ldquo/reviling;<rdquo/ the latter is more
mean and base, and that which we properly call
<ldquo/<qex>slander</qex><rdquo/, or <ldquo/Backbiting.<rdquo/</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[We] make the careful magistrate<br/
The mark of <qex>slander</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1352 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Disgrace; reproach; dishonor; opprobrium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou <qex>slander</qex> of thy mother's heavy womb.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Formerly, defamation generally,
whether oral or written; in modern usage, defamation by words spoken;
utterance of false, malicious, and defamatory words, tending to the
damage and derogation of another; calumny. See the Note under
<er>Defamation</er>.</def> <rj><au>Burril.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slander</ent><br/
<hw>Slan"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slandered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Slandering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
defame; to injure by maliciously uttering a false report; to tarnish or
impair the reputation of by false tales maliciously told or propagated;
to calumniate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, do not <qex>slander</qex> him, for he is kind.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bring discredit or shame upon by one's
acts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tax not so bad a voice<br/
To <qex>slander</qex> music any more than once.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To asperse; defame; calumniate; vilify; malign;
belie; scandalize; reproach. See <er>Asperse</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slanderer</ent><br/
<hw>Slan"der*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who slanders;
a defamer; a calumniator.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slanderous</ent><br/
<hw>Slan"der*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Given
or disposed to slander; uttering slander.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Slanderous</xex> tongue.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Embodying or containing slander; calumnious; <as>as,
<ex>slanderous</ex> words, speeches, or reports</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Slan"der*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Slan"der*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slang</ent><br/
<hw>Slang</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of <er>Sling</er>.
Slung.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slang</ent><br/
<hw>Slang</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any long, narrow piece of land; a
promontory.</def> <mark>[Local, Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slang</ent><br/
<hw>Slang</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Sling</er>.]</ety> <def>A
fetter worn on the leg by a convict.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slang</ent><br/
<hw>Slang</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Said to be of Gypsy origin; but
probably from Scand., and akin to E. <ets>sling</ets>; cf. Norw.
<ets>sleng</ets> a slinging, an invention, device, <ets>slengja</ets> to
sling, to cast, <ets>slengja kjeften</ets> (literally, to sling the jaw)
to use abusive language, to use slang, <ets>slenjeord</ets>
(<ets>ord</ets> = word) an insulting word, a new word that has no just
reason for being.]</ety> <def>Low, vulgar, unauthorized language; a
popular but unauthorized word, phrase, or mode of expression; also, the
jargon of some particular calling or class in society; low popular cant;
<as>as, the <ex>slang</ex> of the theater, of college, of sailors,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slang</ent><br/
<hw>Slang</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slanged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slanging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To address with slang or
ribaldry; to insult with vulgar language.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every gentleman abused by a cabman or <qex>slanged</qex> by a
bargee was bound there and then to take off his coat and challenge him
to fisticuffs.</q> <rj><qau>London Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slanginess</ent><br/
<hw>Slang"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
slangy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slangous</ent><br/
<hw>Slan"gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slangy.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>John Bee.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slang-whanger</ent><br/
<hw>Slang"-whang`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Slang</ets> + <ets>whang</ets> to beat.]</ety> <def>One who
uses abusive slang; a ranting partisan.</def> <mark>[Colloq. or
Humorous]</mark> <rj><au>W. Irving.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slangy</ent><br/
<hw>Slang"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
slang; of the nature of slang; disposed to use slang.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>slangey</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slank</ent><br/
<hw>Slank</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Slink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slant</ent><br/
<hw>Slant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slanted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slanting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slenten</ets> to
slope, slide; cf. Sw. <ets>slinta</ets> to slide.]</ety> <def>To be
turned or inclined from a right line or level; to lie obliquely; to
slope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On the side of younder <qex>slanting</qex> hill.</q>
<rj><qau>Dodsley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slant</ent><br/
<hw>Slant</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To turn from a direct line; to
give an oblique or sloping direction to; <as>as, to <ex>slant</ex> a
line</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slant</ent><br/
<hw>Slant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A slanting direction or
plane; a slope; <as>as, it lies on a <ex>slant</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An oblique reflection or gibe; a sarcastic
remark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slant or wind</b></col>, <cd>a local variation of the
wind from its general direction.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slant</ent><br/
<hw>Slant</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. dial. Sw. <ets>slant</ets>. See
<er>Slant</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>Inclined from a direct
line, whether horizontal or perpendicular; sloping; oblique.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>slant</xex> lightning.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slanting</ent><br/
<hw>Slant"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Oblique; sloping.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Slant"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slantly</ent><br/
<ent>Slantwise</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slant"wise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Slant"ly</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>In an inclined direction; obliquely;
slopingly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slap</ent><br/
<hw>Slap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>slappe</ets>;
akin to LG. <ets>slappe</ets>, G. <ets>schlappe</ets>; probably of
imitative origin.]</ety> <def>A blow, esp. one given with the open hand,
or with something broad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slap</ent><br/
<hw>Slap</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slapped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slapping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To strike with the open hand, or
with something broad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slap</ent><br/
<hw>Slap</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LG. <ets>slap</ets>, G.
<ets>schlapp</ets>. See <er>Slap</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>With a
sudden and violent blow; hence, quickly; instantly; directly.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/The railroad cars drive <xex>slap</xex>
into the city.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slapdash</ent><br/
<hw>Slap"dash`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Slap</ets>
+ <ets>dash</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a bold, careless manner;
at random.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>With a slap; all at once; slap.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slapdash</ent><br/
<hw>Slap"dash`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To apply, or apply something
to, in a hasty, careless, or rough manner; to roughcast; <as>as, to
<ex>slapdash</ex> mortar or paint on a wall, or to <ex>slapdash</ex> a
wall</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slape</ent><br/
<hw>Slape</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>sleipr</ets>
slippery; akin to E. <ets>slip</ets>.]</ety> <def>Slippery; smooth;
crafty; hypocritical.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slape ale</b></col>, <cd>plain ale, as opposed to
<contr>medicated</contr> or <contr>mixed</contr> ale.</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slapeface</ent><br/
<hw>Slape"face`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A soft-spoken,
crafty hypocrite.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slapjack</ent><br/
<hw>Slap"jack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A flat batter cake
cooked on a griddle; a flapjack; a griddlecake.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slapper</ent><br/
<hw>Slap"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, slaps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything monstrous; a whopper.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slapping</ent><br/
<ent>Slapper</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slap"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Slap"ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Very large; monstrous; big.</def>
<mark>[Slang.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slash</ent><br/
<hw>Slash</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slashed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slashing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slaschen</ets>, of
uncertain origin; cf. OF. <ets>esclachier</ets> to break,
<ets>esclechier</ets>, <ets>esclichier</ets>, to break, and E.
<ets>slate</ets>, <ets>slice</ets>, <ets>slit</ets>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut by striking violently and at
random; to cut in long slits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lash; to ply the whip to.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>King.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To crack or snap, as a whip.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slash</ent><br/
<hw>Slash</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To strike violently and at random,
esp. with an edged instrument; to lay about one indiscriminately with
blows; to cut hastily and carelessly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hewing and <qex>slashing</qex> at their idle shades.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slash</ent><br/
<hw>Slash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long cut; a cut made at
random.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A large slit in the material of any garment, made to
show the lining through the openings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Cf. <er>Slashy</er>.]</ety> <pluf>pl.</pluf>
<def>Swampy or wet lands overgrown with bushes.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A opening or gap in a forest made by wind, fire, or
other destructive agency.</def></p>

<p><q>We passed over the shoulder of a ridge and around the edge of a
fire <qex>slash</qex>, and then we had the mountain fairly before
us.</q> <rj><qau>Henry Van Dyke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slashed</ent><br/
<hw>Slashed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Marked or
cut with a slash or slashes; deeply gashed; especially, having long,
narrow openings, as a sleeve or other part of a garment, to show rich
lining or under vesture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A gray jerkin, with scarlet and <qex>slashed</qex> sleeves.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Divided into many narrow parts or
segments by sharp incisions; laciniate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slasher</ent><br/
<hw>Slash"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Textile
Manuf.)</fld> <def>A machine for applying size to warp yarns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slash pine</ent><br/
<hw>Slash" pine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of
pine tree (<spn>Pinus Cubensis</spn>) found in Southern Florida and the
West Indies; -- so called because it grows in
<ldquo/slashes.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slashy</ent><br/
<hw>Slash"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sw.
<ets>slaska</ets> to dabble in water. Cf. <er>Slush</er>.]</ety>
<def>Wet and dirty; slushy.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slat</ent><br/
<hw>Slat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. <er>Slot</er> a
bar.]</ety> <def>A thin, narrow strip or bar of wood or metal; <as>as,
the <ex>slats</ex> of a window blind</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slat</ent><br/
<hw>Slat</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slatted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slatting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slatten</ets>; cf.
Icel. <ets>sletta</ets> to slap, to dab.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
slap; to strike; to beat; to throw down violently.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng. & Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How did you kill him?<br/
<qex>Slat<plain>[t]</plain>ed</qex> his brains out.</q>
<rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To split; to crack.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To set on; to incite. See 3d <er>Slate</er>.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slatch</ent><br/
<hw>Slatch</hw> <pr>(sl<acr/ch)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Slack</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The period
of a transitory breeze.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An interval of fair
weather.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The loose or slack part of a rope;
slack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slate</ent><br/
<hw>Slate</hw> <pr>(sl<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slat</ets>, <ets>sclat</ets>, OF. <ets>esclat</ets> a shiver,
splinter, F. <ets><eacute/clat</ets>, fr. OF. <ets>esclater</ets> to
shiver, to chip, F. <ets><eacute/clater</ets>, fr. OHG.
<ets>sleizen</ets> to tear, slit, split, fr. <ets>sl<imac/zan</ets> to
slit, G. <ets>schleissen</ets>. See <er>Slit</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Eclat</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>An
argillaceous rock which readily splits into thin plates; argillite;
argillaceous schist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any rock or stone having a slaty
structure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A prepared piece of such stone.</def> Especially:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A thin, flat piece, for roofing or covering houses,
etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A tablet for writing upon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An artificial material, resembling slate, and used
for the above purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A thin plate of any material; a flake.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Politics)</fld> <def>A list of candidates, prepared
for nomination or for election; a list of candidates, or a programme of
action, devised beforehand.</def> <mark>[Cant, U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Adhesive slate</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of
slate of a greenish gray color, which absorbs water rapidly, and adheres
to the tongue; whence the name.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Aluminous
slate</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Alum slate</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of slate containing sulphate of alumina,
-- used in the manufacture of alum.</cd> -- <col><b>Bituminous
slate</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a soft species of sectile clay
slate, impregnated with bitumen.</cd> -- <col><b>Hornblende
slate</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a slaty rock, consisting
essentially of hornblende and feldspar, useful for flagging on account
of its toughness.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Slate ax</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Slate axe</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a mattock with an ax end, used
in shaping slates for roofs, and making holes in them for the
nails.</cd> -- <col><b>Slate clay</b></col> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, <cd>an
indurated clay, forming one of the alternating beds of the coal
measures, consisting of an infusible compound of alumina and silica, and
often used for making fire bricks.</cd> <au>Tomlinson.</au> --
<col><b>Slate globe</b></col>, <cd>a globe the surface of which is made
of an artificial slatelike material.</cd> -- <col><b>Slate
pencil</b></col>, <cd>a pencil of slate, or of soapstone, used for
writing on a slate.</cd> -- <col><b>Slate rocks</b></col>
<fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>rocks which split into thin laminae, not
necessarily parallel to the stratification; foliated rocks.</cd> --
<col><b>Slate spar</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of calcite
of silvery white luster and of a slaty structure.</cd> --
<col><b>Transparent slate</b></col>, <cd>a plate of translucent
material, as ground glass, upon which a copy of a picture, placed
beneath it, can be made by tracing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slate</ent><br/
<hw>Slate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cover with slate,
or with a substance resembling slate; <as>as, to <ex>slate</ex> a roof;
to <ex>slate</ex> a globe.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To register (as on a slate and subject to revision),
for an appointment.</def> <mark>[Polit. Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slate</ent><br/
<hw>Slate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>sl<aemac/ting</ets>
a privilege of hunting.]</ety> <def>To set a dog upon; to bait; to slat.
See 2d <er>Slat</er>, 3.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>slete</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. To schedule. 3. To reserve or designate for a specific
purpose. --> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slate-color</ent><br/
<hw>Slate"-col`or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A dark bluish gray
color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slate-gray</ent><br/
<hw>Slate"-gray`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of a dark gray,
like slate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slater</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lays slates,
or whose occupation is to slate buildings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slater</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any terrestrial
isopod crustacean of the genus <gen>Porcellio</gen> and allied genera; a
sow bug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slating</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of covering
with slate, slates, or a substance resembling slate; the work of a
slater.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Slates, collectively; also, material for
slating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slatt</ent><br/
<hw>Slatt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Slat</er> a
strip of board.]</ety> <def>A slab of stone used as a veneer for coarse
masonry.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slatter</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[E.
<ets>slat</ets> to throw or dash about.]</ety> <def>To be careless,
negligent, or awkward, esp. with regard to dress and neatness; to be
wasteful.</def> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slattern</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"tern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who is
negligent of her dress or house; one who is not neat and nice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slattern</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"tern</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a slattern; sluttish;
slatterny.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>slattern</xex> air.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slattern</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"tern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To consume
carelessly or wastefully; to waste; -- with <xex>away</xex>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Chesterfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slatternliness</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"tern*li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being slatternly; slovenliness; untidiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slatternly</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"tern*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a slattern;
sluttish; negligent; dirty.</def> -- <def2><pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
slatternly manner.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slatterpouch</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"ter*pouch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dance or
game played by boys, requiring active exercise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slatting</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def>Slats, collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slatting</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"ting</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The violent shaking or flapping
of anything hanging loose in the wind, as of a sail, when being hauled
down.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaty</ent><br/
<hw>Slat"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Slate</er>.]</ety> <def>Resembling slate; having the nature,
appearance, or properties, of slate; composed of thin parallel plates,
capable of being separated by splitting; <as>as, a <ex>slaty</ex> color
or texture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slaty cleavage</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>cleavage,
as of rocks, into thin leaves or plates, like those of slate; -- applied
especially to those cases in which the planes of cleavage are not
parallel to the planes of stratification. It is now believed to be
caused by the compression which the strata have undergone.</cd> --
<col><b>Slaty gneiss</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of
gneiss in which the scales of mica or crystals of hornblende, which are
usually minute, form thin laminae, rendering the rock easily
cleavable.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaughter</ent><br/
<hw>Slaugh"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slautir</ets>, <ets>slaughter</ets>, <ets>slaghter</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sl<amac/tr</ets> slain flesh, modified by OE. <ets>slaught</ets>,
<ets>slaht</ets>, slaughter, fr. AS. <ets>sleaht</ets> a stroke, blow;
both from the root of E. slay. See <er>Slay</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Onslaught</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of killing.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The extensive, violent, bloody, or
wanton destruction of life; carnage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On war and mutual <qex>slaughter</qex> bent.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The act of killing cattle or other beasts for
market.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Carnage; massacre; butchery; murder;
havoc.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaughter</ent><br/
<hw>Slaugh"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slaughtered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slaughtering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To visit with
great destruction of life; to kill; to slay in battle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your castle is surprised; your wife and babes<br/
Savagely <qex>slaughtered</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To butcher; to kill for the market, as
beasts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaughterer</ent><br/
<hw>Slaugh"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
slaughters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaughterhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Slaugh"ter*house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A house
where beasts are butchered for the market.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaughterman</ent><br/
<hw>Slaugh"ter*man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Slaughtermen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One employed in
slaughtering.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaughterous</ent><br/
<hw>Slaugh"ter*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destructive;
murderous.</def> <au>Shak.</au> <au>M. Arnold.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Slaugh"ter*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slav</ent><br/
<hw>Slav</hw> <pr>(sl<aum/v <it>or</it> sl<acr/v)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Slavs</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[A word
originally meaning, intelligible, and used to contrast the people so
called with foreigners who spoke languages unintelligible to the Slavs;
akin to OSlav. <ets>slovo</ets> a word, <ets>slava</ets> fame, Skr.
<ets><cced/ru</ets> to hear. Cf. <er>Loud</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>One of a race of people occupying a large part
of Eastern and Northern Europe, including the Russians, Bulgarians,
Roumanians, Servo-Croats, Slovenes, Poles, Czechs, Wends or Sorbs,
Slovaks, etc.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>Slave</asp>, and
<asp>Sclav</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slave</ent><br/
<hw>Slave</hw> <pr>(sl<aum/v <it>or</it> sl<acr/v; 277)</pr>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Slav</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slave</ent><br/
<hw>Slave</hw> <pr>(sl<amac/v)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>esclave</ets>, D. <ets>slaaf</ets>, Dan. <ets>slave</ets>,
<ets>sclave</ets>, Sw. <ets>slaf</ets>, all fr. G. <ets>sklave</ets>,
MHG. also <ets>slave</ets>, from the national name of the Slavonians, or
Sclavonians (in LL. <ets>Slavi</ets> or <ets>Sclavi</ets>), who were
frequently made slaves by the Germans. See <er>Slav</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A person who is held in bondage to another; one who is
wholly subject to the will of another; one who is held as a chattel; one
who has no freedom of action, but whose person and services are wholly
under the control of another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Art thou our <qex>slave</qex>,<br/
Our captive, at the public mill our drudge?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who has lost the power of resistance; one who
surrenders himself to any power whatever; <as>as, a <ex>slave</ex> to
passion, to lust, to strong drink, to ambition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A drudge; one who labors like a slave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An abject person; a wretch.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slave ant</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any species
of ants which is captured and enslaved by another species, especially
<spn>Formica fusca</spn> of Europe and America, which is commonly
enslaved by <spn>Formica sanguinea</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Slave
catcher</b></col>, <cd>one who attempted to catch and bring back a
fugitive slave to his master.</cd> -- <col><b>Slave coast</b></col>,
<cd>part of the western coast of Africa to which slaves were brought to
be sold to foreigners.</cd> -- <col><b>Slave driver</b></col>, <cd>one
who superintends slaves at their work; hence, figuratively, a cruel
taskmaster.</cd> -- <col><b>Slave hunt</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A
search after persons in order to reduce them to slavery</cd>.
<au>Barth.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A search after fugitive slaves, often
conducted with bloodhounds.</cd> -- <col><b>Slave ship</b></col>, <cd>a
vessel employed in the slave trade or used for transporting slaves; a
slaver.</cd> -- <col><b>Slave trade</b></col>, <cd>the business of
dealing in slaves, especially of buying them for transportation from
their homes to be sold elsewhere.</cd> -- <col><b>Slave
trader</b></col>, <cd>one who traffics in slaves.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Bond servant; bondman; bondslave; captive;
henchman; vassal; dependent; drudge. See <er>Serf</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slave</ent><br/
<hw>Slave</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slaved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slaving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To drudge; to toil; to labor as a
slave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slave</ent><br/
<hw>Slave</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To enslave.</def>
<rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaveborn</ent><br/
<hw>Slave"born`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Born in
slavery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaveholder</ent><br/
<hw>Slave"hold`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who holds
slaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaveholding</ent><br/
<hw>Slave"hold`ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Holding persons in
slavery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaveocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Slave*oc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Slavocracy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaver</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A vessel
engaged in the slave trade; a slave ship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person engaged in the purchase and sale of slaves;
a slave merchant, or slave trader.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>slaver's</qex> hand was on the latch,<br/
He seemed in haste to go.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaver</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slavered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Slavering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>slafra</ets>. See <er>Slabber</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
suffer spittle, etc., to run from the mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be besmeared with saliva.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaver</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"er</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To smear with saliva issuing
from the mouth; to defile with drivel; to slabber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaver</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Saliva driveling from the
mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of all mad creatures, if the learned are right,<br/
It is the <qex>slaver</qex> kills, and not the bite.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaverer</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"er*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A driveler; an
idiot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1353 --></p>

<p><ent>Slavering</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"er*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Drooling; defiling
with saliva.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Slav"er*ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavery</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Slaveries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See 2d
<er>Slave</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The condition of a slave; the
state of entire subjection of one person to the will of
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Disguise thyself as thou wilt, still, <qex>slavery</qex>, said I,
still thou art a bitter draught!</q> <rj><qau>Sterne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I wish, from my soul, that the legislature of this state
[Virginia] could see the policy of a gradual abolition of
<qex>slavery</qex>. It might prevent much future mischief.</q>
<rj><qau>Washington.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A condition of subjection or submission
characterized by lack of freedom of action or of will.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The vulgar <qex>slaveries</qex> rich men submit to.</q>
<rj><qau>C. Lever.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a <qex>slavery</qex> that no legislation can abolish, --
the <qex>slavery</qex> of caste.</q> <rj><qau>G. W.
Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The holding of slaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Bondage; servitude; inthrallment; enslavement;
captivity; bond service; vassalage.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavey</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"ey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A maidservant.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. & Jocose Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavic</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slavonic.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The group of allied languages spoken by the
Slavs.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavish</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
slaves; such as becomes or befits a slave; servile; excessively
laborious; <as>as, a <ex>slavish</ex> life; a <ex>slavish</ex>
dependance on the great.</as></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Slav"ish*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Slav"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavism</ent><br/
<hw>Slav"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The common feeling
and interest of the Slavonic race.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Slav*oc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Slave</ets> + <ets>-cracy</ets>, as in
<ets>democracy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The persons or interest formerly
representing slavery politically, or wielding political power for the
preservation or advancement of slavery.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavonic</ent><br/
<ent>Slavonian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sla*vo"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sla*von"ic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
Slavonia, or its inhabitants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the Slavs, or their
language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavonian</ent><br/
<hw>Sla*vo"ni*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Slavonia; ethnologically, a Slav.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slavophile</ent><br/
<ent>Slavophil</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slav"o*phil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Slav"o*phile</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Slavic</ets> + Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ loving.]</ety> <def>One, not being a Slav, who is interested
in the development and prosperity of that race.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slaw</ent><br/
<hw>Slaw</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>sla</ets>,
contr. fr. <ets>salade</ets>, OD. <ets>salaet</ets>, <ets>salad</ets>.
See <er>Salad</er>.]</ety> <def>Sliced cabbage served as a salad, cooked
or uncooked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slawen</ent><br/
<ent>Slaw</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slaw</hw>, <hw>Slaw"en</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of <er>Slee</er>, to
slay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a sword drawn out he would have <qex>slaw</qex> himself.</q>
<rj><qau>Wyclif (Acts xvi. 27.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slay</ent><br/
<hw>Slay</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Slew</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Slain</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slaying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slan</ets>,
<ets>sl<?/n</ets>, <ets>sleen</ets>, <ets>slee</ets>, AS.
<ets>sle<aacute/n</ets> to strike, beat, slay; akin to OFries.
<ets>sl<amac/</ets>, D. <ets>slaan</ets>, OS. & OHG. <ets>slahan</ets>,
G. <ets>schlagen</ets>, Icel. <ets>sl<amac/</ets>, Dan.
<ets>slaae</ets>, Sw. <ets>sl<?/</ets>, Goth. <ets>slahan</ets>; perhaps
akin to L. <ets>lacerare</ets> to tear to pieces, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/, E.
<ets>lacerate</ets>. Cf. <er>Slaughter</er>, <er>Sledge</er> a hammer,
<er>Sley</er>.]</ety> <def>To put to death with a weapon, or by
violence; hence, to kill; to put an end to; to destroy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With this sword then will I <qex>slay</qex> you both.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>slay</qex> the last of them with the sword.</q>
<rj><qau>Amos ix. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll <qex>slay</qex> more gazers than the basilisk.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To kill; murder; slaughter; butcher.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slayer</ent><br/
<hw>Slay"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who slays; a
killer; a murderer; a destroyer of life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slazy</ent><br/
<hw>Sla"zy</hw> <pr>(sl<amac/"z<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sleazy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sle</ent><br/
<hw>Sle</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To slay.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleave</ent><br/
<hw>Sleave</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/v)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>sl<oum/if</ets>, a knot loop, Sw. <ets>slejf</ets>, G.
<ets>schleife</ets> a knot, sliding knot, and E. <ets>slip</ets>,
v.i.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The knotted or entangled part of silk or
thread.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Silk not yet twisted; floss; -- called
also <altname>sleave silk</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sleep that knits up the ravell'd <qex>sleave</qex> of care.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleave</ent><br/
<hw>Sleave</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sleaved</conjf> <pr>(sl<emac/vd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sleaving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To separate, as threads; to
divide, as a collection of threads; to sley; -- a weaver's
term.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleaved</ent><br/
<hw>Sleaved</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/vd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Raw; not spun
or wrought; <as>as, <ex>sleaved</ex> thread or silk</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleaziness</ent><br/
<hw>Slea"zi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
sleazy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleazy</ent><br/
<hw>Slea"zy</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/"z<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schleissig</ets> worn out, threadbare, from <ets>schleissen</ets>
to slit, split, decay, or E. <ets>leasy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Lacking
firmness of texture or substance; thin; flimsy; <as>as, <ex>sleazy</ex>
silk or muslin</as>.</def> <altsp>[Spelt also
<asp>slazy</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sled</ent><br/
<hw>Sled</hw> <pr>(sl<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to D.
<ets>slede</ets>, G. <ets>schlitten</ets>, OHG. <ets>slito</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sle<edh/i</ets>, Sw. <ets>sl<aum/de</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sl<ae/de</ets>, and E. <ets>slide</ets>, v. See <er>Slide</er>, and
cf. <er>Sledge</er> a vehicle, <er>Sleigh</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A vehicle on runners, used for conveying loads over the snow or
ice; -- in England called <altname>sledge</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small, light vehicle with runners, used, mostly by
young persons, for sliding on snow or ice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sled</ent><br/
<hw>Sled</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sledded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sledding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To convey or transport on a
sled; <as>as, to <ex>sled</ex> wood or timber</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sledding</ent><br/
<hw>Sled"ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of transporting or riding on a sled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of the snow which admits of the running of
sleds; <as>as, the <ex>sledding</ex> is good</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sledge</ent><br/
<hw>Sledge</hw> <pr>(sl<ecr/j)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps from
<ets>sleds</ets>, pl. of <ets>sled</ets>, confused with
<ets>sledge</ets> a hammer. See <er>Sled</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A strong vehicle with low runners or low wheels; or one
without wheels or runners, made of plank slightly turned up at one end,
used for transporting loads upon the snow, ice, or bare ground; a
sled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hurdle on which, formerly, traitors were drawn to
the place of execution.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sleigh.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A game at cards; -- called also <altname>old
sledge</altname>, and <altname>all fours</altname>.</def> <-- also
called seven-up. See def above. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sledge</ent><br/
<hw>Sledge</hw> <pr>(sl<ecr/j)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sledged</conjf>
<pr>(sl<ecr/jd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sledging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To travel or convey in a sledge
or sledges.</def> <rj><au>Howitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sledge</ent><br/
<hw>Sledge</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>slecge</ets>,from
<ets>sle<aacute/n</ets> to strike, beat. See <er>Slay</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <def>A large, heavy hammer, usually wielded with both
hands; -- called also <altname>sledge hammer</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With his heavy <qex>sledge</qex> he can it beat.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slee</ent><br/
<hw>Slee</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Slay</er>.]</ety> <def>To slay.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleek</ent><br/
<hw>Sleek</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Sleeker</adjf>
<pr>(sl<emac/k"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sleekest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slik</ets>; akin to
Icel. <ets>sl<imac/kr</ets>, and OE. <ets>sliken</ets> to glide, slide,
G. <ets>schleichen</ets>, OHG. <ets>sl<imac/hhan</ets>, D.
<ets>slik</ets>, <ets>slijk</ets>, mud, slime, and E. <ets>slink</ets>.
Cf. <er>Slick</er>, <er>Slink</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having an
even, smooth surface; smooth; hence, glossy; <as>as, <ex>sleek</ex>
hair</as>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So <qex>sleek</qex> her skin, so faultless was her make.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not rough or harsh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those rugged names to our like mouths grow <qex>sleek</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleek</ent><br/
<hw>Sleek</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With ease and dexterity.</def>
<mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleek</ent><br/
<hw>Sleek</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which makes smooth;
varnish.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A slick.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleek</ent><br/
<hw>Sleek</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sleeked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sleeking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make even and smooth; to
render smooth, soft, and glossy; to smooth over.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sleeking</qex> her soft alluring locks.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gentle, my lord, <qex>sleek</qex> o'er your rugged looks.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleekly</ent><br/
<hw>Sleek"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sleek manner;
smoothly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleekness</ent><br/
<hw>Sleek"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sleek; smoothness and glossiness of surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeky</ent><br/
<hw>Sleek"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of a
sleek, or smooth, and glossy appearance.</def>
<rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fawning and deceitful; sly.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleep</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
Sleep. Slept.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleep</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slept</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sleeping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slepen</ets>, AS.
<ets>sl<?/pan</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>sl<?/pa</ets>, OS.
<ets>sl<amac/pan</ets>, D. <ets>slapen</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sl<amac/fan</ets>, G. <ets>schlafen</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sl<?/pan</ets>, and G. <ets>schlaff</ets> slack, loose, and L.
<ets>labi</ets> to glide, slide, <ets>labare</ets> to totter. Cf.
<er>Lapse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take rest by a suspension of
the voluntary exercise of the powers of the body and mind, and an apathy
of the organs of sense; to slumber.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Watching at the head of these that <qex>sleep</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> Figuratively: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To be careless,
inattentive, or uncouncerned; not to be vigilant; to live
thoughtlessly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>sleep</qex> over our happiness.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To be dead; to lie in the grave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Them also which <qex>sleep</qex> in Jesus will God bring with
him.</q> <rj><qau>1 Thess. iv. 14. </qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To be, or appear to be, in repose; to be quiet; to
be unemployed, unused, or unagitated; to rest; to lie dormant; <as>as, a
question <ex>sleeps</ex> for the present; the law
<ex>sleeps</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How sweet the moonlight <qex>sleep</qex> upon this bank!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleep</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be slumbering in;
-- followed by a cognate object; <as>as, to <ex>sleep</ex> a dreamless
sleep</as>.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give sleep to; to furnish with accomodations for
sleeping; to lodge.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Blackw.
Mag.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sleep away</b></col>, <cd>to spend in sleep; <as>as,
<ex>to sleep away</ex> precious time</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To sleep
off</b></col>, <cd>to become free from by sleep; <as>as, <ex>to sleep
off</ex> drunkeness or fatigue</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleep</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sl<aemac/p</ets>; akin to
OFries. <ets>sl<emac/p</ets>, OS. <ets>sl<amac/p</ets>, D.
<ets>slaap</ets>, OHG. <ets>sl<amac/f</ets>, G. <ets>schlaf</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sl<emac/ps</ets>. See <er>Sleep</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<def>A natural and healthy, but temporary and periodical, suspension of
the functions of the organs of sense, as well as of those of the
voluntary and rational soul; that state of the animal in which there is
a lessened acuteness of sensory perception, a confusion of ideas, and a
loss of mental control, followed by a more or less unconscious
state.</def> <ldquo/A man that waketh of his <xex>sleep</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O <qex>sleep</qex>, thou ape of death.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Sleep is attended by a relaxation of the muscles, and
the absence of voluntary activity for any rational objects or purpose.
The pulse is slower, the respiratory movements fewer in number but more
profound, and there is less blood in the cerebral vessels. It is
susceptible of greater or less intensity or completeness in its control
of the powers.</note> <-- the dreaming portions of sleep occurs
periodically, and is associated with "rapid eye movements" (REM), and in
this state the sleeper is more easily wakened; the dreamiong alternates
with a more profound sleep, from which it is more difficult to awake the
sleeper. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sleep of plants</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a state
of plants, usually at night, when their leaflets approach each other,
and the flowers close and droop, or are covered by the folded
leaves.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Slumber; repose; rest; nap; doze;
drowse.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleep-at-noon</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"-at-noon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A plant (<spn>Tragopogon pratensis</spn>) which closes its flowers
at midday; a kind of goat's beard.</def> <rj><au>Dr.
Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleep-charged</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"-charged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Heavy with
sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeper</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
sleeps; a slumberer; hence, a drone, or lazy person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which lies dormant, as a law.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sleeping car.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An animal that hibernates, as the
bear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A large fresh-water
gobioid fish (<spn>Eleotris dormatrix</spn>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
nurse shark. See under <er>Nurse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeper</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Norw. <ets>sleip</ets> a
sleeper (a timber), as adj., slippery, smooth. See
<er>Slape</er>.]</ety> <def>Something lying in a reclining posture or
position.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the pieces of timber, stone, or iron, on or
near the level of the ground, for the support of some superstructure, to
steady framework, to keep in place the rails of a railway, etc.; a
stringpiece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the joists, or roughly shaped timbers, laid
directly upon the ground, to receive the flooring of the ground
story.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>One of the knees which connect
the transoms to the after timbers on the ship's quarter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The lowest, or bottom, tier of
casks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepful</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Strongly inclined to
sleep; very sleepy.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sleep"ful*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepily</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sleepy
manner; drowsily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sleepy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeping</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Sleep</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sleeping car</b></col>, <cd>a railway car or carrriage,
arranged with apartments and berths for sleeping.</cd> --
<col><b>Sleeping partner</b></col> <fld>(Com.)</fld>, <cd>a dormant
partner. See under <er>Dormant</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sleeping
table</b></col> <fld>(Mining)</fld>, <cd>a stationary inclined platform
on which pulverized ore is washed; a kind of buddle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepish</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Disposed to sleep;
sleepy; drowsy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your <qex>sleepish</qex>, and more than <qex>sleepish</qex>,
security.</q> <rj><qau>Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepless</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having no sleep;
wakeful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having no rest; perpetually agitated.</def>
<ldquo/Biscay's <xex>sleepless</xex> bay.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sleep"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sleep"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepmarken</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"mark`en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See 1st <er>Hag</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepwaker</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"wak`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>On in a state of
magnetic or mesmeric sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepwaking</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"wak`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of one mesmerized,
or in a partial and morbid sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepwalker</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"walk`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who walks in
his sleep; a somnambulist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepwalking</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"walk`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Walking in one's
sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepy</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sleepier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sleepiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>sl<?/pig</ets>. See
<er>Sleep</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Drowsy; inclined
to, or overcome by, sleep.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She waked her <qex>sleepy</qex> crew.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tending to induce sleep; soporiferous; somniferous;
<as>as, a <ex>sleepy</ex> drink or potion</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Dull; lazy; heavy; sluggish.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'Tis not <qex>sleepy</qex> business;<br/
But must be looked to speedily and strongly.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Characterized by an absence of watchfulness; <as>as,
<ex>sleepy</ex> security</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sleepy duck</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the ruddy
duck.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleepyhead</ent><br/
<hw>Sleep"y*head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
sleepy person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To bed, to bed, says <qex>Sleepyhead</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Mother
Goose.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The ruddy duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleer</ent><br/
<hw>Sle"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slayer.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleet</ent><br/
<hw>Sleet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gun.)</fld> <def>The
part of a mortar extending from the chamber to the trunnions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleet</ent><br/
<hw>Sleet</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sleet</ets>; akin to MHG.
<ets>sl<?/z</ets>, <ets>sl<?/ze</ets> hailstone, G. <ets>schlosse</ets>;
of uncertain origin.]</ety> <def>Hail or snow, mingled with rain,
usually falling, or driven by the wind, in fine particles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleet</ent><br/
<hw>Sleet</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sleeted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sleeting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To snow or hail with a mixture
of rain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleetch</ent><br/
<hw>Sleetch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Slush</er>,
<er>Slutch</er>.]</ety> <def>Mud or slime, such as that at the bottom of
rivers.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleetiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sleet"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being sleety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleety</ent><br/
<hw>Sleet"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
sleet; characterized by sleet; <as>as, a <ex>sleety</ex> storm;
<ex>sleety</ex> weather</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeve</ent><br/
<hw>Sleeve</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/v)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sleave</er>, untwisted thread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeve</ent><br/
<hw>Sleeve</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sleeve</ets>,
<ets>sleve</ets>, AS. <ets>sl<?/fe</ets>, <ets>sl<?/fe</ets>; akin to
<ets>sl<?/fan</ets> to put on, to clothe; cf. OD. <ets>sloove</ets> the
turning up of anything, <ets>sloven</ets> to turn up one's sleeves,
<ets>sleve</ets> a sleeve, G. <ets>schlaube</ets> a husk, pod.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The part of a garment which covers the arm; <as>as, the
<ex>sleeve</ex> of a coat or a gown</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A narrow channel of water.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Celtic Sea, called oftentimes the <qex>Sleeve</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A tubular part made
to cover, sustain, or steady another part, or to form a connection
between two parts.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A long bushing or thimble, as
in the nave of a wheel.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A short piece of pipe
used for covering a joint, or forming a joint between the ends of two
other pipes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A double tube of copper, in
section like the figure 8, into which the ends of bare wires are pushed
so that when the tube is twisted an electrical connection is made. The
joint thus made is called <col><b>a McIntire joint</b></col>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sleeve button</b></col>, <cd>a detachable button to
fasten the wristband or cuff.</cd> -- <col><b>Sleeve links</b></col>,
<cd>two bars or buttons linked together, and used to fasten a cuff or
wristband.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To laugh in the sleeve</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>To laugh up one's sleeve</b></col></mcol> <cd>to
laugh privately or unperceived, especially while apparently preserving a
grave or serious demeanor toward the person or persons laughed at; that
is, perhaps, originally, by hiding the face in the wide sleeves of
former times.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To pinon the sleeve of</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>To hang on the sleeve of</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to
be, or make, dependent upon.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeve</ent><br/
<hw>Sleeve</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sleeved</conjf> <pr>(sl<emac/vd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sleeving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To furnish with sleeves; to put
sleeves into; <as>as, to <ex>sleeve</ex> a coat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeved</ent><br/
<hw>Sleeved</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/vd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having
sleeves; furnished with sleeves; -- often in composition; <as>as,
long-<ex>sleeved</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleevefish</ent><br/
<hw>Sleeve"fish`</hw> <pr>(sl<emac/v"f<icr/sh`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A squid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleevehand</ent><br/
<hw>Sleeve"hand`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The part of a
sleeve nearest the hand; a cuff or wristband.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleeveless</ent><br/
<hw>Sleeve"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sl<emac/fle<aacute/s</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having no
sleeves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Lacking a cover, pretext, or palliation;
unreasonable; profitless; bootless; useless.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The vexation of a <qex>sleeveless</qex> errand.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleid</ent><br/
<hw>Sleid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sleided</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sleiding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Sley</er>.]</ety>
<def>To sley, or prepare for use in the weaver's sley, or slaie.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleigh</ent><br/
<hw>Sleigh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleigh</ent><br/
<hw>Sleigh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. & LG.
<ets>slede</ets>, <ets>slee</ets>, Icel. <ets>sle<?/i</ets>. See
<er>Sled</er>.]</ety> <def>A vehicle moved on runners, and used for
transporting persons or goods on snow or ice; -- in England commonly
called a <xex>sledge</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sleigh bell</b></col>, <cd>a small bell attached either
to a horse when drawing a slegh, or to the sleigh itself; especially a
globular bell with a loose ball which plays inside instead of a
clapper.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleighing</ent><br/
<hw>Sleigh"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of riding in
a sleigh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of the snow or ice which admits of running
sleighs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleight</ent><br/
<hw>Sleight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sleighte</ets>, <ets>sleihte</ets>, <ets>sleithe</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sl<?/g<?/</ets> (for <ets>sl<?/g<?/</ets>) slyness, cunning, fr.
<ets>sl<?/gr</ets> (for <ets>sl<?/gr</ets>) sly, cunning. See
<er>Sly</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Cunning; craft; artful
practice.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/His <xex>sleight</xex> and
his covin.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An artful trick; sly artifice; a feat so dexterous
that the manner of performance escapes observation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The world hath many subtle <qex>sleights</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Dexterous practice; dexterity; skill.</def>
<au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/The juggler's <xex>sleight</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sleight of hand</b></col>, <cd>legerdemain;
prestidigitation.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1354 --></p>

<p><ent>Sleightful</ent><br/
<hw>Sleight"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cunning;
dexterous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleightly</ent><br/
<hw>Sleight"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Cinningly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Huloet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleighty</ent><br/
<hw>Sleight"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cinning; sly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Huloet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slender</ent><br/
<hw>Slen"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Slenderer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Slenderest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slendre</ets>, <ets>sclendre</ets>, fr. OD. <ets>slinder</ets>
thin, slender, perhaps through a French form; cf. OD.
<ets>slinderen</ets>, <ets>slidderen</ets>, to creep; perh. akin to E.
<ets>slide</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Small or narrow in proportion
to the length or the height; not thick; slim; <as>as, a <ex>slender</ex>
stem or stalk of a plant</as>.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>slender</xex>,
choleric man.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She, as a veil down to the <qex>slender</qex> waist,<br/
Her unadorned golden tresses wore.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Weak; feeble; not strong; slight; <as>as,
<ex>slender</ex> hope; a <ex>slender</ex> constitution</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mighty hearts are held in <qex>slender</qex> chains.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They have inferred much from <qex>slender</qex> premises.</q>
<rj><qau>J. H. Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>slender</qex> utterance of the consonants.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Byrne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Moderate; trivial; inconsiderable; slight; <as>as, a
man of <ex>slender</ex> intelligence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>slender</qex> degree of patience will enable him to enjoy
both the humor and the pathos.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Small; inadequate; meager; pitiful; <as>as,
<ex>slender</ex> means of support; a <ex>slender</ex>
pittance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Frequent begging makes <qex>slender</qex> alms.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Spare; abstemious; frugal; <as>as, a
<ex>slender</ex> diet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The good Ostorius often deigned<br/
To grace my <qex>slender</qex> table with his presence.</q>
<rj><qau>Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Phon.)</fld> <def>Uttered with a thin tone; -- the
opposite of <xex>broad</xex>; <as>as, the <ex>slender</ex> vowels long
<ex>e</ex> and <ex>i</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Slen"der*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Slen"der*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slent</ent><br/
<hw>Slent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Slant</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slep</ent><br/
<hw>Slep</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Sleep</er>. Slept.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slepez</ent><br/
<hw>Sle*pez"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>sliepets'</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A burrowing rodent
(<spn>Spalax typhlus</spn>), native of Russia and Asia Minor. It has the
general appearance of a mole, and is destitute of eyes. Called also
<altname>mole rat</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slept</ent><br/
<hw>Slept</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sleep</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleuth</ent><br/
<hw>Sleuth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>sl<omac/<edh/</ets>. See <er>Slot</er> a track.]</ety> <def>The
track of man or beast as followed by the scent.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sleuthhound</ent><br/
<hw>Sleuth"hound`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sleuth</er>, and cf. <er>Slothound</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A hound that tracks animals by the scent; specifically, a
bloodhound.</def> <altsp>[Spelt variously <asp>slouthhound</asp>,
<asp>sluthhound</asp>, etc.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slew</ent><br/
<hw>Slew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Slay</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slew</ent><br/
<hw>Slew</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See <er>Slue</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slew</ent><br/
<hw>Slew</hw> <pr>(sl<oomac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Slough</er> a wet place.]</ety> <def>A wet place; a river
inlet.</def></p>

<p><q>The praire round about is wet, at times almost marshy, especially
at the borders of the great reedy <qex>slews</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Roosevelt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slewed</ent><br/
<hw>Slewed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat drunk.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slewth</ent><br/
<hw>Slewth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sloth; idleness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sley</ent><br/
<hw>Sley</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sl<?/</ets>,
fr. <ets>sle<aacute/n</ets> to strike. See <er>Slay</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A weaver's reed.</def> <altsp>[Spelt
also <asp>slaie</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A guideway in a knitting machine.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>The number of ends per inch in
the cloth, provided each dent in the reed in which it was made contained
an equal number of ends.</def> <rj><au>E. Whitworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sley</ent><br/
<hw>Sley</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To separate or part the threads of,
and arrange them in a reed; -- a term used by weavers. See
<er>Sleave</er>, and <er>Sleid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slibber</ent><br/
<hw>Slib"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slippery.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slice</ent><br/
<hw>Slice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>slice</ets>,
<ets>sclice</ets>, OF. <ets>esclice</ets>, from <ets>esclicier</ets>,
<ets>esclichier</ets>, to break to pieces, of German origin; cf. OHG.
<ets>sl<imac/zan</ets> to split, slit, tear, G. <ets>schleissen</ets> to
slit. See <er>Slit</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
thin, broad piece cut off; <as>as, a <ex>slice</ex> of bacon; a
<ex>slice</ex> of cheese; a <ex>slice</ex> of bread.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is thin and broad, like a slice.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A broad, thin piece of plaster.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A salver, platter, or tray.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A knife with a thin, broad blade for taking up or
serving fish; also, a spatula for spreading anything, as paint or
ink.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A plate of iron with a handle, forming a
kind of chisel, or a spadelike implement, variously proportioned, and
used for various purposes, as for stripping the planking from a vessel's
side, for cutting blubber from a whale, or for stirring a fire of coals;
a slice bar; a peel; a fire shovel.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark>
<sd>(e)</sd> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>One of the wedges by which
the cradle and the ship are lifted clear of the building blocks to
prepare for launching.</def> <sd>(f)</sd> <fld>(Printing)</fld> <def>A
removable sliding bottom to galley.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slice bar</b></col>, <cd>a kind of fire iron resembling a
poker, with a broad, flat end, for stirring a fire of coals, and
clearing it and the grate bars from clinkers, ashes, etc.; a
slice.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slice</ent><br/
<hw>Slice</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sliced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slicing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut
into thin pieces, or to cut off a thin, broad piece from.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cut into parts; to divide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To clear by means of a slice bar, as a fire or the
grate bars of a furnace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>To hit (the ball) so that the face
of the club draws across the face of the ball and deflects it.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slicer</ent><br/
<hw>Sli"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, slices; specifically, the circular saw of the lapidary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slick</ent><br/
<ent>Slich</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Slick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>See <er>Schlich</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slick</ent><br/
<hw>Slick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sleek</er>.]</ety> <def>Sleek; smooth.</def> <ldquo/Both
<xex>slick</xex> and dainty.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slick</ent><br/
<hw>Slick</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make sleek or smoth.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Slicked</xex> all with sweet oil.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slick</ent><br/
<hw>Slick</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Joinery)</fld> <def>A wide paring
chisel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slick</ent><br/
<hw>Slick</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slick, or smooth and slippery,
surface or place; a sleek.</def></p>

<p><q>The action of oil upon the water is upon the crest of the wave;
the oil forming a <qex>slick</qex> upon the surface breaks the
crest.</q> <rj><qau>The Century.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slicken</ent><br/
<hw>Slick"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sleek; smooth.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slickens</ent><br/
<hw>Slick"ens</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Slick</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The pulverized matter from
a quartz mill, or the lighter soil of hydraulic mines.</def>
<mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slickensides</ent><br/
<hw>Slick"en*sides`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The smooth, striated, or partially polished surfaces of a fissure
or seam, supposed to have been produced by the sliding of one surface on
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A variety of galena found in Derbyshire,
England.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slicker</ent><br/
<hw>Slick"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which makes
smooth or sleek.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A kind of
burnisher for leather.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>A
curved tool for smoothing the surfaces of a mold after the withdrawal of
the pattern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slicker</ent><br/
<hw>Slick"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A waterproof coat.</def>
<mark>[Western U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slicking</ent><br/
<hw>Slick"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process of
smoothing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Narrow veins of
ore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slickness</ent><br/
<hw>Slick"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of being
slick; smoothness; sleekness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slid</ent><br/
<hw>Slid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Slide</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slidden</ent><br/
<hw>Slid"den</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Slide</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slidder</ent><br/
<hw>Slid"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sliderian</ets>. See <er>Slide</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To slide with interruption.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sliddery</ent><br/
<ent>Slidderly</ent><br/
<ent>Slidder</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slid"der</hw>, <hw>Slid"der*ly</hw>, <hw>Slid"der*y</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>slidor</ets>. See
<er>Slide</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Slippery.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To a drunk man the way is <qex>slidder</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slide</ent><br/
<hw>Slide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Slid</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slidden</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, <conjf>Slid</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Slidding</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sliden</ets>, AS. <ets>sl<imac/dan</ets>; akin to MHG.
<ets>sl<imac/ten</ets>, also to AS. <ets>slidor</ets> slippery, E.
<ets>sled</ets>, Lith. <ets>slidus</ets> slippery. Cf.
<er>Sled</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To move along the surface of any
body by slipping, or without walking or rolling; to slip; to glide;
<as>as, snow <ex>slides</ex> down the mountain's side</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, to move over snow or ice with a smooth,
uninterrupted motion, as on a sled moving by the force of gravity, or on
the feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They bathe in summer, and in winter <qex>slide</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pass inadvertently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beware thou <qex>slide</qex> not by it.</q> <rj><qau>Ecclus.
xxviii. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To pass along smoothly or unobservedly; to move
gently onward without friction or hindrance; <as>as, a ship or boat
<ex>slides</ex> through the water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ages shall <qex>slide</qex> away without perceiving.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Parts answering parts shall <qex>slide</qex> into a whole.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To slip when walking or standing; to fall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their foot shall <qex>slide</qex> in due time.</q> <rj><qau>Deut.
xxxii. 35.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To pass from one note to another
with no perceptible cassation of sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To pass out of one's thought as not being of any
consequence.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With good hope let he sorrow <qex>slide</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a calm carelessness letting everything <qex>slide</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slide</ent><br/
<hw>Slide</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to slide; to
thrust along; <as>as, to <ex>slide</ex> one piece of timber along
another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pass or put imperceptibly; to slip; <as>as, to
<ex>slide</ex> in a word to vary the sense of a question</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slide</ent><br/
<hw>Slide</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sl<imac/de</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sliding; <as>as, a <ex>slide</ex> on the
ice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Smooth, even passage or progress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A better <qex>slide</qex> into their business.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That on which anything moves by sliding.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An inclined plane on which heavy bodies
slide by the force of gravity, esp. one constructed on a mountain side
for conveying logs by sliding them down.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
surface of ice or snow on which children slide for amusement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which operates by sliding.</def> Specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A cover which opens or closes an aperture by sliding
over it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A moving piece which
is guided by a part or parts along which it slides.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>A clasp or brooch for a belt, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A plate or slip of glass on which is a picture or
delineation to be exhibited by means of a magic lantern, stereopticon,
or the like; a plate on which is an object to be examined with a
microscope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The descent of a mass of earth, rock, or snow down a
hill or mountain side; <as>as, a land <ex>slide</ex>, or a snow
<ex>slide</ex></as>; also, the track of bare rock left by a land
slide.</def><-- also mudslide --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A small dislocation in beds of
rock along a line of fissure.</def> <rj><au>Dana.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A grace consisting of
two or more small notes moving by conjoint degrees, and leading to a
principal note either above or below.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An
apparatus in the trumpet and trombone by which the sounding tube is
lengthened and shortened so as to produce the tones between the
fundamental and its harmonics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <def>A sound which, by a gradual
change in the position of the vocal organs, passes imperceptibly into
another sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Steam Engine)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Same as
<cref>Guide bar</cref>, under <er>Guide</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
slide valve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slide box</b></col> <fld>(Steam Engine)</fld>, <cd>a
steam chest. See under <er>Steam</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Slide
lathe</b></col>, <cd>an engine lathe. See under <er>Lathe</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Slide rail</b></col>, <cd>a transfer table. See under
<er>Transfer</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Slide rest</b></col> <fld>(Turning
lathes)</fld>, <cd>a contrivance for holding, moving, and guiding, the
cutting tool, made to slide on ways or guides by screws or otherwise,
and having compound motion.</cd> -- <col><b>Slide rule</b></col>, <cd>a
mathematical instrument consisting of two parts, one of which slides
upon the other, for the mechanical performance of addition and
subtraction, and, by means of logarithmic scales, of multiplication and
division.</cd> -- <col><b>Slide valve</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any
valve which opens and closes a passageway by sliding over a port</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A particular kind of sliding valve, often used in steam
engines for admitting steam to the piston and releasing it, alternately,
having a cuplike cavity in its face, through which the exhaust steam
passes. It is situated in the steam chest, and moved by the valve gear.
It is sometimes called a <altname>D valve</altname>, -- a name which is
also applied to a semicylindrical pipe used as a sliding
valve.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- illustration of a slide valve --> <illust>In the illustration,
<xex>a</xex> is the cylinder of a steam engine, in which plays the
piston <xex>p</xex>; <xex>b</xex> the steam chest, receiving its supply
from the pipe <xex>i</xex>, and containing the slide valve <xex>s</xex>,
which is shown as admitting steam to one end of the cylinder through the
port <xex>e</xex>, and opening communication between the exhaust passage
<xex>f</xex> and the port <xex>c</xex>, for the release of steam from
the opposite end of the cylinder.</illust><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slidegroat</ent><br/
<hw>Slide"groat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The game of
shovelboard.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slider</ent><br/
<hw>Slid"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Slidder</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slider</ent><br/
<hw>Slid"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which,
slides; especially, a sliding part of an instrument or
machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The red-bellied terrapin
(<spn>Pseudemys rugosa</spn>).</def> <mark>[Local, U. S. ]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>any of several North American
freshwater turtles of the genus <gen>Chrysemis</gen>; some, such as
<spn>Chrysemis scripta</spn> are sold commercially as pets.</def>
<au>[RHUD]</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>a fast pitch that breaks
slightly just in front of the batter, in the same direction as a curve
ball (i. e. , away from the side from which it was thrown).</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slider pump</b></col>, <cd>a form of rotary
pump.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slide rule</ent><br/
<hw>Slide" rule`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a thin, flat calculating
device consisting of a fixed outer piece and a movable middle piece.
Both pieces are graduated in such a way (as, by a logarithmic scale)
that multiplication, division, and other mathematical functions of an
input variable may be rapidly determined by movement of the middle
pieces to a location on one scale corresponding to the input value, and
reading off the result on another scale. A movable window with a
hairline assists in alignment of the scales. This device has been
largely superseded by the electronic calculator, which has a greater
precision than the slide rule. Also called colloquially
<altname>slipstick</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slideway</ent><br/
<hw>Slide"way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A way along which
something slides.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sliding</ent><br/
<hw>Slid"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
slides or slips; gliding; moving smoothly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Slippery; elusory.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>sliding</qex> science hath me made so bare.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sliding friction</b></col> <fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>the
resistance one body meets with in sliding along the surface of another,
as distinguished from <xex>rolling friction</xex>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sliding gunter</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a topmast
arranged with metallic fittings so as to be hoisted and lowered by means
of halyards.</cd> -- <col><b>Sliding keel</b></col> <fld>(Naut)</fld>,
<cd>a movable keel, similar to a centeboard.</cd> -- <col><b>Sliding
pair</b></col>. <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <cd>See the Note under <er>Pair</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 7.</cd> -- <col><b>Sliding rule</b></col>. <cd>Same as
<cref>Slide rule</cref>, under <er>Slide</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd> --
<col><b>Sliding scale</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A scale for raising or
lowering imposts in proportion to the fall or rise of prices</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A variable scale of wages or of prices</cd>.
<sd>(c)</sd> <cd>A slide rule.</cd> -- <col><b>Sliding ways</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the timber guides used in launching a
vessel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slidometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sli*dom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Slide</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument
for indicating and recording shocks to railway cars occasioned by sudden
stopping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slight</ent><br/
<hw>Slight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sleight.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slight</ent><br/
<hw>Slight</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>slechten</ets> to
level, to demolish.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To overthrow; to
demolish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make even or level.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hexham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To throw heedlessly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The rogue <qex>slighted</qex> me into the river.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slight</ent><br/
<hw>Slight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Slighter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Slightest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sli<?/t</ets>,
<ets>sleght</ets>, probably from OD. <ets>slicht</ets>,
<ets>slecht</ets>, simple, plain, D. <ets>slecht</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>sliucht</ets>, G. <ets>schlecht</ets>, <ets>schlicht</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sleht</ets> smooth, simple, Icel. <ets>sl<?/ttr</ets> smooth, Sw.
<ets>sl<aum/t</ets>, Goth. <ets>sla<iacute/hts</ets>; or uncertain
origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not decidedly marked; not forcible;
inconsiderable; unimportant; insignificant; not severe; weak; gentle; --
applied in a great variety of circumstances; <as>as, a <ex>slight</ex>
(<it>i. e.</it>, feeble) effort; a <ex>slight</ex> (<it>i. e.</it>,
perishable) structure; a <ex>slight</ex> (<it>i. e.</it>, not deep)
impression; a <ex>slight</ex> (<it>i. e.</it>, not convincing) argument;
a <ex>slight</ex> (<it>i. e.</it>, not thorough) examination;
<ex>slight</ex> (<it>i. e.</it>, not severe) pain, and the
like.</as></def> <ldquo/At one <xex>slight</xex> bound.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Slight</qex> is the subject, but not so the praise.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some firmly embrace doctrines upon <qex>slight</qex> grounds.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not stout or heavy; slender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His own figure, which was formerly so <qex>slight</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Foolish; silly; weak in intellect.</def>
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slight</ent><br/
<hw>Slight</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slighted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slighting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To disregard, as of little
value and unworthy of notice; to make light of; <as>as, to
<ex>slight</ex> the divine commands</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wretch who <qex>slights</qex> the bounty of the skies.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To slight off</b></col>, to treat slightingly; to
drive off; to remove. <mark>[R.]</mark> -- <col><b>To slight
over</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to run over in haste; to perform
superficially; to treat carelessly; <as>as, <ex>to slight over</ex> a
theme</as>.</cd> <ldquo/They will but <xex>slight</xex> it over.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To neglect; disregard; disdain; scorn.</syn> --
<usage><er>Slight</er>, <er>Neglect</er>. To <xex>slight</xex> is
stronger than to <xex>neglect</xex>. We may neglect a duty or person
from inconsiderateness, or from being over-occupied in other concerns.
To <xex>slight</xex> is always a positive and intentional act, resulting
from feelings of dislike or contempt. We ought to put a kind
construction on what appears <xex>neglect</xex> on the part of a friend;
but when he <xex>slights</xex> us, it is obvious that he is our friend
no longer.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beware . . . lest the like befall . . . <br/
If they transgress and <qex>slight</qex> that sole command.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This my long-sufferance, and my day of grace,<br/
Those who <qex>neglect</qex> and scorn shall never taste.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slight</ent><br/
<hw>Slight</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of slighting; the
manifestation of a moderate degree of contempt, as by neglect or
oversight; neglect; indignity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Neglect; disregard; inattention; contempt;
disdain; scorn; disgrace; indignity; disparagement.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slight</ent><br/
<hw>Slight</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Slightly.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Think not so <qex>slight</qex> of glory.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slighten</ent><br/
<hw>Slight"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To slight.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slighter</ent><br/
<hw>Slight"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
slights.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slightful</ent><br/
<hw>Slight"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sleightful</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slighting</ent><br/
<hw>Slight"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Characterized by neglect or
disregard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slightingly</ent><br/
<hw>Slight"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a slighting
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slightly</ent><br/
<hw>Slight"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a slight
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Slightingly; negligently.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slightness</ent><br/
<hw>Slight"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
slight; slenderness; feebleness; superficiality; also, formerly,
negligence; indifference; disregard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slighty</ent><br/
<hw>Slight"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slight.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Echard.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slik</ent><br/
<hw>Slik</hw> <pr>(sl<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Such</er>.]</ety> <def>Such.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Used by Chaucer as of the Northern dialect.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slikensides</ent><br/
<hw>Slik"en*sides`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Slickensides</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slily</ent><br/
<hw>Sli"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>See
<er>Slyly</er>.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slim</ent><br/
<hw>Slim</hw> <pr>(sl<icr/m)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Slimmer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Slimmest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Formerly, bad,
worthless, weak, slight, awry, fr. D. <ets>slim</ets>; akin to G.
<ets>schlimm</ets>, MHG. <ets>slimp</ets> oblique, awry; of uncertain
origin. The meaning of the English word seems to have been influenced by
<ets>slender</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Worthless; bad.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Weak; slight; unsubstantial; poor; <as>as, a
<ex>slim</ex> argument</as>.</def> <ldquo/That was a <xex>slim</xex>
excuse.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of small diameter or thickness in proportion to the
height or length; slender; <as>as, a <ex>slim</ex> person; a
<ex>slim</ex> tree.</as></def> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slime</ent><br/
<hw>Slime</hw> <pr>(sl<imac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slim</ets>, AS. <ets>sl<imac/m</ets>; akin to D. <ets>slijm</ets>,
G. <ets>schleim</ets>, MHG. <ets>sl<imac/men</ets> to make smooth, Icel.
<ets>sl<imac/m</ets> slime, Dan. <ets>sliim</ets>; cf. L.
<ets>limare</ets> to file, polish, <ets>levis</ets> smooth, Gr.
<?/<?/<?/; or cf. L. <ets>limus</ets> mud.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Soft,
moist earth or clay, having an adhesive quality; viscous mud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As it [Nilus] ebbs, the seedsman<br/
Upon the <qex>slime</qex> and ooze scatters his grain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any mucilaginous substance; any substance of a dirty
nature, that is moist, soft, and adhesive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Script.)</fld> <def>Bitumen.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Slime</qex> had they for mortar.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xi.
3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1355 --></p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>Mud containing
metallic ore, obtained in the preparatory dressing.</def>
<rj><au>Pryce.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A mucuslike substance which
exudes from the bodies of certain animals.</def>
<rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slime eel</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See 1st
<er>Hag</er>, 4.</cd> -- <col><b>Slime pit</b></col>, <cd>a pit for the
collection of slime or bitumen.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slime</ent><br/
<hw>Slime</hw> <pr>(sl<imac/m)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Slimed</conjf> <pr>(sl<imac/md)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sliming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To smear with
slime.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slime mould</ent><br/
<ent>slime mold</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>slime mold</hw>, <hw>slime mould</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An unusual fungus-like protist of the phylum
<phylum>Myxomycota</phylum> or the class <class>Myxomycetes</class>,
having a stage of growth in which it comprises a naked noncellular
multinucleate mass of creeping protoplasm having characteristics of both
plants and animals; it also has a propagative phase in which it develops
fruiting bodies bearing spores; it is sometimes classified as a protist.
It is called also <altname><ecol><b>acellular slime
mold</b></ecol></altname>.</def>
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any of several remarkable amoebalike organisms of
the phylum <phylum>Acrasiomycota</phylum>, mostly terrestrial, having a
fruiting phase resembling that of the <cref>acellular slime
molds</cref>, but being cellular and nucleate throughout their life
cycle; called also <altname><ecol><b>cellular slime
mold</b></ecol></altname>. The most studied species is
<styp><spn>Dictyostelium discoideum</spn></styp>. In their feeding
phase, they live like amoebae as individual cells, engulfing bacteria as
a prime food source. When the food source diminishes, they begin to
aggregate, swarming together to form clumps which may move toward heat
and light, so as to reach the surface of the ground; they then
differentiate into a form with spores contained within a sporangium
resting on a stalk. When the spores are carried to another location with
adequate food supplies, the spores may germinate to resume the life
cycle. The phase of aggregation appears to be initiated by release of
cyclic AMP, serving as a signal between the individual cells. The
formation of the fruiting body has some similarities to differentiation
in multicellular organisms, but the mechanisms are still under study.
Some biologists object to the classification of <gen>Dictyostelium</gen>
as a <ex>slime mold</ex>, as it is neither a mold nor slimy.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slimily</ent><br/
<hw>Slim"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a slimy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sliminess</ent><br/
<hw>Slim"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
slimy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slimly</ent><br/
<hw>Slim"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a state of
slimness; in a slim manner; slenderly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slimness</ent><br/
<hw>Slim"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
slim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slimsy</ent><br/
<hw>Slim"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Flimsy; frail.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slimy</ent><br/
<hw>Slim"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Slimier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Slimiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Of or pertaining to slime;
resembling slime; of the nature of slime; viscous; glutinous; also,
covered or daubed with slime; yielding, or abounding in,
slime.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Slimy</qex> things did crawl with legs<br/
Upon the <qex>slimy</qex> sea.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sliness</ent><br/
<hw>Sli"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Slyness</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sling</ent><br/
<hw>Sling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>slinge</ets>;
akin to OD. <ets>slinge</ets>, D. <ets>slinger</ets>, OHG.
<ets>slinga</ets>; cf. OF. <ets>eslingue</ets>, of German origin. See
<er>Sling</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An instrument
for throwing stones or other missiles, consisting of a short strap with
two strings fastened to its ends, or with a string fastened to one end
and a light stick to the other. The missile being lodged in a hole in
the strap, the ends of the string are taken in the hand, and the whole
whirled rapidly round until, by loosing one end, the missile is let fly
with centrifugal force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or motion of hurling as with a sling; a
throw; figuratively, a stroke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>slings</qex> and arrows of outrageous fortune.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At one <qex>sling</qex><br/
Of thy victorius arm, well-pleasing Son.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A contrivance for sustaining anything by
suspension</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A kind of hanging bandage put
around the neck, in which a wounded arm or hand is supported.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A loop of rope, or a rope or chain with hooks, for
suspending a barrel, bale, or other heavy object, in hoisting or
lowering.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A strap attached to a firearm, for
suspending it from the shoulder.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A band of rope or iron for securing a yard to a mast; -- chiefly in
the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sling cart</b></col>, <cd>a kind of cart used to
transport cannon and their carriages, large stones, machines, etc., the
objects transported being slung, or suspended by a chain attached to the
axletree.</cd> -- <col><b>Sling dog</b></col>, <cd>one of a pair of iron
hooks used as part of a sling. See def. 3 <sd>(b)</sd>
above.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sling</ent><br/
<hw>Sling</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Slung</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>Archaic</mark>
<conjf>Slang</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slung</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slinging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>slingan</ets>; akin to
D. <ets>slingeren</ets>, G. <ets>schlingen</ets>, to wind, to twist, to
creep, OHG. <ets>slingan</ets> to wind, to twist, to move to and fro,
Icel. <ets>slyngva</ets>, <ets>sl<oum/ngva</ets>, to sling, Sw.
<ets>slunga</ets>, Dan. <ets>slynge</ets>, Lith. <ets>slinkti</ets> to
creep.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw with a sling.</def> <ldquo/Every
one could <xex>sling</xex> stones at an hairbreadth, and not
miss.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Judg. xx. 16.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To throw; to hurl; to cast.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To hang so as to swing; <as>as, to <ex>sling</ex> a
pack</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut)</fld> <def>To pass a rope round, as a cask,
gun, etc., preparatory to attaching a hoisting or lowering
tackle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sling</ent><br/
<hw>Sling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>schlingen</ets> to
swallow.]</ety> <def>A drink composed of spirit (usually gin) and water
sweetened.</def> <-- as, a Singapore <ex>sling.</ex> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slinger</ent><br/
<hw>Sling"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who slings, or
uses a sling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slinger ring</ent><br/
<hw>slinger ring</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>tubular ring around the
propeller hub of an airplane through which antifreeze solution is spread
over the blades.</def>
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slink</ent><br/
<hw>Slink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Slunk</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>Archaic</mark>
<conjf>Slank</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slunk</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slinking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>slincan</ets>;
probably akin to G. <ets>schleichen</ets>, E. <ets>sleek</ets>. See
<er>Sleek</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To creep away
meanly; to steal away; to sneak.</def> <ldquo/To <xex>slink</xex> away
and hide.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tale of Beryn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Back to the thicket <qex>slunk</qex><br/
The guilty serpent.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There were some few who <qex>slank</qex> obliquely from them as
they passed.</q> <rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To miscarry; -- said of female beasts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slink</ent><br/
<hw>Slink</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cast prematurely; -- said of
female beasts; <as>as, a cow that <ex>slinks</ex> her
calf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slink</ent><br/
<hw>Slink</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Produced prematurely;
<as>as, a <ex>slink</ex> calf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Thin; lean.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slink</ent><br/
<hw>Slink</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The young of a beast
brought forth prematurely, esp. a calf brought forth before its
time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thievish fellow; a sneak.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.
& Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slinky</ent><br/
<hw>Slink"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Thin; lank.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slip</ent><br/
<hw>Slip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slipped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slipping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slippen</ets>; akin to
LG. & D. <ets>slippen</ets>, MHG. <ets>slipfen</ets> (cf. Dan.
<ets>slippe</ets>, Sw. <ets>slippa</ets>, Icel. <ets>sleppa</ets>), and
fr. OE. <ets>slipen</ets>, AS. <ets>sl<imac/pan</ets> (in comp.), akin
to G. <ets>schleifen</ets> to slide, glide, drag, whet, OHG.
<ets>sl<imac/fan</ets> to slide, glide, make smooth, Icel.
<ets>sl<imac/pa</ets> to whet; cf. also AS. <ets>sl<?/pan</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sliupan</ets>, OS. <ets>slopian</ets>, OHG. <ets>sliofan</ets>, G.
<ets>schliefen</ets>, <ets>schl<?/pfen</ets>, which seem to come from a
somewhat different root form. Cf. <er>Slope</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To move along the surface of a thing without bounding,
rolling, or stepping; to slide; to glide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To slide; to lose one's footing or one's hold; not
to tread firmly; <as>as, it is necessary to walk carefully lest the foot
should <ex>slip</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To move or fly (out of place); to shoot; -- often
with <xex>out</xex>, <xex>off</xex>, etc.; <as>as, a bone may
<ex>slip</ex> out of its place</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To depart, withdraw, enter, appear, intrude, or
escape as if by sliding; to go or come in a quiet, furtive manner;
<as>as, some errors <ex>slipped</ex> into the work</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus one tradesman <qex>slips</qex> away,<br/
To give his partner fairer play.</q> <rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thrice the flitting shadow <qex>slipped</qex> away.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To err; to fall into error or fault.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is one that <qex>slippeth</qex> in his speech, but not from
his heart.</q> <rj><qau>Ecclus. xix. 16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To let slip</b></col>, <cd>to loose from the slip or
noose, as a hound; to allow to escape.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cry, <ldquo/Havoc,<rdquo/ and <qex>let slip</qex> the dogs of
war.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slip</ent><br/
<hw>Slip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause
to move smoothly and quickly; to slide; to convey gently or
secretly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He tried to <qex>slip</qex> a powder into her drink.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To omit; to loose by negligence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>slip</qex> no advantage<br/
That my secure you.</q> <rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cut slips from; to cut; to take off; to make a
slip or slips of; <as>as, to <ex>slip</ex> a piece of cloth or
paper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The branches also may be <qex>slipped</qex> and planted.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To let loose in pursuit of game, as a
greyhound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lucento <qex>slipped</qex> me like his greyhound.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To cause to slip or slide off, or out of place;
<as>as, a horse <ex>slips</ex> his bridle; a dog <ex>slips</ex> his
collar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To bring forth (young) prematurely; to
slink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To slip a cable</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Cable</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To slip off</b></col>, <cd>to take
off quickly; <as>as, <ex>to slip off</ex> a coat</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>To slip on</b></col>, <cd>to put on in haste or loosely; <as>as,
<ex>to slip on</ex> a gown or coat</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slip</ent><br/
<hw>Slip</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>slipe</ets>,
<ets>slip</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of slipping; <as>as, a
<ex>slip</ex> on the ice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An unintentional error or fault; a false
step.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This good man's <qex>slip</qex> mended his pace to martyrdom.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A twig separated from the main stock; a cutting; a
scion; hence, a descendant; <as>as, a <ex>slip</ex> from a
vine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A native <qex>slip</qex> to us from foreign seeds.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The girlish <qex>slip</qex> of a Sicilian bride.</q> <rj><qau>R.
Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A slender piece; a strip; <as>as, a <ex>slip</ex> of
paper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moonlit <qex>slips</qex> of silver cloud.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A thin <qex>slip</qex> of a girl, like a new moon<br/
Sure to be rounded into beauty soon.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A leash or string by which a dog is held; -- so
called from its being made in such a manner as to slip, or become loose,
by relaxation of the hand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We stalked over the extensive plains with Killbuck and Lena in the
<qex>slips</qex>, in search of deer.</q> <rj><qau>Sir S.
Baker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An escape; a secret or unexpected desertion; <as>as,
to give one the <ex>slip</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A portion of the columns of a
newspaper or other work struck off by itself; a proof from a column of
type when set up and in the galley.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Any covering easily slipped on.</def> Specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A loose garment worn by a woman.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A child's pinafore.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>An outside covering or
case; <as>as, a pillow <ex>slip</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The
<ex>slip</ex> or sheath of a sword, and the like.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A counterfeit piece of money, being brass covered
with silver.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>Matter found in troughs of grindstones after the
grinding of edge tools.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Petty.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Potter's clay in a very liquid state, used for the
decoration of ceramic ware, and also as a cement for handles and other
applied parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>A particular quantity of yarn.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>An inclined plane on which a vessel is built, or
upon which it is hauled for repair.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>An opening or space for vessels to lie in, between
wharves or in a dock; <as>as, Peck <ex>slip</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <def>A narrow passage between buildings.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <def>A long seat or narrow pew in churches, often
without a door.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>17.</sn> <fld>(Mining.)</fld> <def>A dislocation of a lead,
destroying continuity.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>18.</sn> <fld>(Engin.)</fld> <def>The motion of the center of
resistance of the float of a paddle wheel, or the blade of an oar,
through the water horozontally, or the difference between a vessel's
actual speed and the speed which she would have if the propelling
instrument acted upon a solid; also, the velocity, relatively to still
water, of the backward current of water produced by the
propeller.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>19.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A fish, the sole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>20.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>A fielder stationed on the off
side and to the rear of the batsman. There are usually two of them,
called respectively <stype>short slip</stype>, and <stype>long
slip</stype>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 21. A slip dock (see below) --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>22.</sn><fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The retrograde
movement on a pulley of a belt as it slips.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In a
link motion, the undesirable sliding movement of the link relatively to
the link block, due to swinging of the link.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>23.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The difference between the
actual and synchronous speed of an induction motor.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>23.</sn> <fld>(Marine Insurance)</fld> <def>A memorandum of the
particulars of a risk for which a policy is to be executed. It usually
bears the broker's name and is initiated by the underwrites.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To give one the slip</b></col>, <cd>to slip away from
one; to elude one.</cd> -- <col><b>Slip dock</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Dock</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Slip link</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>,
<cd>a connecting link so arranged as to allow some play of the parts, to
avoid concussion.</cd> -- <col><b>Slip rope</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a rope by which a cable is secured preparatory
to slipping.</cd> <au>Totten.</au> -- <col><b>Slip stopper</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>an arrangement for letting go the anchor
suddenly.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipboard</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A board sliding in
grooves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slip coach</ent><br/
<ent>slip carriage</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>slip carriage</hw>, <hw>slip coach</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>a railway car at the end of the train; it can be detached without
stopping the train.</def> <mark>[British]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> slip coach</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slip clutch</ent><br/
<hw>slip clutch</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a friction clutch that will slip
when the torque is too great</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> slip friction clutch.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipcoat cheese</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"coat` cheese"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A rich variety of new
cheese, resembling butter, but white.</def>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipes</ent><br/
<hw>Slipes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Slip</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>Sledge runners on which a skip is dragged in a
mine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slip-joint pliers</ent><br/
<hw>slip-joint pliers</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>pliers with a joint
adjustable to two positions in order to increase the opening of the
jaws.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipknot</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"knot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>knot which slips
along the rope or line around which it is made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slip of the tongue</ent><br/
<hw>slip of the tongue</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>an unintentional
utterance; a mistake in speaking.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> slip of the lip.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slip-on</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"-on`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of overcoat
worn upon the shoulders in the manner of a cloak.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slippage</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"page</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of slipping;
also, the amount of slipping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipper</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, slips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of light shoe, which may be slipped on with
ease, and worn in undress; a slipshoe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A kind of apron or pinafore for children.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A kind of brake or shoe for a wagon wheel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A piece, usually a plate, applied
to a sliding piece, to receive wear and afford a means of adjustment; --
also called <altname>shoe</altname>, and
<altname>gib</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slipper animalcule</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
ciliated infusorian of the genus <gen>Paramecium</gen>.</cd> --
<col><b>Slipper flower</b></col>.<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Slipperwort.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Slipper limpet</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Slipper
shell</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a boat
shell.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipper</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"per</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>slipur</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Slippery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O! trustless state of earthly things, and <qex>slipper</qex>
hope<br/
Of mortal men.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slippered</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"pered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wearing
slippers.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipperily</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"per*i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a slippery
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipperiness</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"per*i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
slippery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipperness</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"per*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Slipperiness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slipper orchid</ent><br/
<hw>slipper orchid</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>any of several chiefly
American wildflowers having an inflated pouchlike lip; difficult or
impossible to cultivate in the garden.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> lady's slipper, lady's-slipper,
lady-slipper.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipperwort</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"per*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Calceolaria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slippery</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"per*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Slipper</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the
quality opposite to adhesiveness; allowing or causing anything to slip
or move smoothly, rapidly, and easily upon the surface; smooth; glib;
<as>as, oily substances render things <ex>slippery</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not affording firm ground for confidence; <as>as, a
<ex>slippery</ex> promise</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>slippery</qex> tops of human state.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not easily held; liable or apt to slip
away.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>slippery</qex> god will try to loose his hold.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Liable to slip; not standing firm.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Unstable; changeable; mutable; uncertain;
inconstant; fickle.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>slippery</xex> state of
kings.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Denham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Uncertain in effect.</def>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Wanton; unchaste; loose in morals.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slippery elm</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>An American tree (<spn>Ulmus fulva</spn>) with a mucilagenous and
slightly aromatic inner bark which is sometimes used medicinally; also,
the inner bark itself</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A malvaceous shrub
(<spn>Fremontia Californica</spn>); -- so called on the Pacific
coast.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slippiness</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"pi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Slipperiness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The
<xex>slippiness</xex> of the way.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slippy</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>slipeg</ets>.]</ety> <def>Slippery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slip ring</ent><br/
<hw>slip ring</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a metal ring on a rotating part of
a machine to provide a continuous electrical connection through brushes
on stationary contacts</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slip road</ent><br/
<hw>slip road</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a short road giving access to an
expressway</def> <mark>[British]</mark> <ldquo/in England they call an
access road a slip road.<rdquo/<br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> access road, slip road.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipshod</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"shod`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Wearing
shoes or slippers down at the heel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The shivering urchin bending as he goes,<br/
With <qex>slipshod</qex> heels.</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively: Careless in dress, manners, style,
etc.; slovenly; shuffling; <as>as, <ex>slipshod</ex> manners; a
<ex>slipshod</ex> or loose style of writing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy wit shall ne'er go <qex>slipshod</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipshoe</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"shoe`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slipper.</def>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipskin</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"skin`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Evasive.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipslop</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"slop`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A reduplication of
<ets>slop</ets>.]</ety> <def>Weak, poor, or flat liquor; weak,
profitless discourse or writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slip stitch</ent><br/
<hw>slip stitch</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>loose stitch catching only a
thread or two of fabric, designed to be invisible from the right
side.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipstring</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"string`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who has
shaken off restraint; a prodigal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slipthrift</ent><br/
<hw>Slip"thrift`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
spendthrift.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slish</ent><br/
<hw>Slish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption of
<ets>slash</ets>.]</ety> <def>A cut; <as>as, <ex>slish</ex> and
slash</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slit</ent><br/
<hw>Slit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>3d. pers. sing.
pres.</pos> of <er>Slide</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slit</ent><br/
<hw>Slit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slit</conjf> or <conjf>Slitted</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Slitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slitten</ets>, fr. <ets>sliten</ets>, AS. <ets>st<imac/tan</ets> to
tear; akin to D. <ets>slijten</ets> to wear out, G.
<ets>schleissen</ets> to slit, split, OHG. <ets>sl<imac/zan</ets> to
split, tear, wear out, Icel. <ets>st<imac/ta</ets> to break, tear, wear
out, Sw. <ets>slita</ets>, Dan. <ets>slide</ets>. Cf. <er>Eclat</er>,
<er>Slate</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Slice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To cut lengthwise; to cut into long pieces or strips; <as>as, to
<ex>slit</ex> iron bars into nail rods; to <ex>slit</ex> leather into
straps.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cut or make a long fissure in or upon; <as>as, to
<ex>slit</ex> the ear or the nose</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cut; to sever; to divide.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>slits</qex> the thin-spun life.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slit</ent><br/
<hw>Slit</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>slite</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
long cut; a narrow opening; <as>as, a <ex>slit</ex> in the
ear</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Gill slit</b></col>. <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Gill opening</cref>, under <er>Gill</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slither</ent><br/
<hw>slith"er</hw> <pr>(sl<icr/<th/"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. G. <ets>schlittern</ets>, LG. <ets>schliddern</ets>. See
<er>slide</er>.]</ety> <def>To slide; to glide.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slit-shell</ent><br/
<hw>slit"-shell`</hw> <pr>(sl<icr/t"sh<ecr/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of <gen>Pleurotomaria</gen>, a genus
of beautiful, pearly, spiral gastropod shells having a deep slit in the
outer lip. Many fossil species are known, and a few living ones are
found in deep water in tropical seas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slitter</ent><br/
<hw>slit"ter</hw> <pr>(sl<icr/t"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who, or that which, slits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slitting</ent><br/
<hw>slit"ting</hw> <pr>(sl<icr/t"t<icr/ng)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos>
from <er>slit</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>slitting file</b></col>. <cd>See <xex>Illust.</xex>
<sd>(i)</sd> of <er>sile</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>slitting mill</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A mill where iron bars or plates are slit into narrow
strips, as nail rods, and the like</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A machine used
by lapidaries for slicing stones, usually by means of a revolving disk,
called a <part>slicer</part>, supplied with diamond powder.</cd> --
<col><b>slitting roller</b></col>, <cd>one of a pair of rollers
furnished with ribs entering between similar ribs in the other roller,
and cutting like shears, -- used in slitting metals.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slive</ent><br/
<hw>Slive</hw> <pr>(sl<imac/v)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Slip</er>.]</ety> <def>To sneak.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slive</ent><br/
<hw>Slive</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sliven</ets> to split,
cleave, AS. <ets>sl<imac/fan</ets>.]</ety> <def>To cut; to split; to
separate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sliver</ent><br/
<hw>sliv"er</hw> <pr>(sl<icr/v"<etil/r <it>or</it> sl<imac/"v<etil/r;
277)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>slivered</conjf> <pr>(sl<icr/v"<etil/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>slivering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Slive</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To cut or divide into long, thin pieces, or
into very small pieces; to cut or rend lengthwise; to slit; <as>as, to
<ex>sliver</ex> wood</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They 'll <qex>sliver</qex> thee like a turnip.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sliver</ent><br/
<hw>Sliv"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long piece cut or
rent off; a sharp, slender fragment, as of glass; a splinter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A strand, or slender roll, of cotton or other fiber
in a loose, untwisted state, produced by a carding machine and ready for
the roving or slubbing which preceeds spinning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Bait made of pieces of small fish.
Cf. <er>Kibblings</er>.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sloakan</ent><br/
<hw>sloak"an</hw> <pr>(sl<omac/k"<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A species of seaweed. See 3d
<er>Laver</er>.</def> <altsp>[Spelled also
<asp>slowcawn</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sloam</ent><br/
<hw>sloam</hw> <pr>(sl<omac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld>
<def>A layer of earth between coal seams.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sloat</ent><br/
<hw>sloat</hw> <pr>(sl<omac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>slot</er> a bar.]</ety> <def>A narrow piece of timber which holds
together large pieces; a slat; <as>as, the <ex>sloats</ex> of a
cart</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slobber</ent><br/
<hw>Slob"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Slabber</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slobber</ent><br/
<hw>Slob"ber</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>See
<er>Slabber</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A jellyfish.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Vet.)</fld>
<def>Salivation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slobberer</ent><br/
<hw>Slob"ber*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who slobbers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A slovenly farmer; a jobbing tailor.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slobbery</ent><br/
<hw>Slob"ber*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wet; sloppy, as
land.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slocken</ent><br/
<ent>Slock</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Slock"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To quench; to allay; to slake. See
<er>Slake</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slocking</ent><br/
<hw>Slock"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Slock</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slocking stone</b></col>, <cd>a rich piece of ore
displayed in order to tempt persons to embark in a mining
enterprise.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloe</ent><br/
<hw>Sloe</hw> <pr>(sl<omac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slo</ets>, AS. <ets>sl<amac/</ets>; akin to D. <ets>slee</ets>, G.
<ets>schlehe</ets>, OHG. <ets>sl<emac/ha</ets>, Dan. <ets>slaaen</ets>,
Sw. <ets>sl<aring/n</ets>, perhaps originally, that which blunts the
teeth, or sets them on edge (cf. <er>Slow</er>); cf. Lith.
<ets>slywa</ets> a plum, Russ. <ets>sliva</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A small, bitter, wild European plum, the fruit of
the blackthorn (<spn>Prunus spinosa</spn>); also, the tree
itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slog</ent><br/
<hw>Slog</hw> <pr>(sl<ocr/g)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Slug</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To hit hard, esp. with
little attention to aim or the like, as in cricket or boxing; to
slug.</def> <mark>[Cant or Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slog</ent><br/
<hw>Slog</hw> <pr>(sl<ocr/g)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to
walk heavily; to plod; to walk through resisting terrain, as in
mud.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To work steadily and ploddingly; to toil.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slogger</ent><br/
<hw>Slog"ger</hw> <pr>(sl<ocr/g"g<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
hard hitter; a slugger.</def> <mark>[Cant or Slang]</mark> <rj><au>T.
Hughes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slogan</ent><br/
<hw>Slo"gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>sluagh-ghairm</ets>, i.e., an army cry; <ets>sluagh</ets> army +
<ets>gairm</ets> a call, calling.]</ety> <def>The war cry, or gathering
word, of a Highland clan in Scotland.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A distinctive motto, phrase,
or cry used by any person or party to express a purpose or ideal; a
catchphrase; a rallying cry.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1356 --></p>

<p><ent>sloggy</ent><br/
<hw>slog"gy</hw> <pr>(sl<ocr/g"g<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sluggish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Somnolence that is <qex>sloggy</qex> slumbering</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sloke</ent><br/
<hw>sloke</hw> <pr>(sl<omac/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>sloakan</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>slue</ent><br/
<ent>sloo</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>sloo</hw> <pr>(sl<oomac/)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>slue</hw>
<pr>(sl<umac/)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slough; a run or wet
place. See 2d <er>Slough</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloom</ent><br/
<hw>Sloom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Slumber.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloomy</ent><br/
<hw>Sloom"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sluggish; slow.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloop</ent><br/
<hw>Sloop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[D. <ets>sloep</ets>, of
uncertain origin. Cf. <er>Shallop</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A vessel having one mast and fore-and-aft rig,
consisting of a boom-and-gaff mainsail, jibs, staysail, and gaff
topsail. The typical sloop has a fixed bowsprit, topmast, and standing
rigging, while those of a cutter are capable of being readily shifted.
The sloop usually carries a centerboard, and depends for stability upon
breadth of beam rather than depth of keel. The two types have rapidly
approximated since 1880. One radical distinction is that a sloop may
carry a centerboard. See <er>Cutter</er>, and <xex>Illustration</xex> in
Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>In modern usage, a sailing vessel
having one mast, commonly with a Bermuda rig, with either a center-board
or a keel. In the United States, a sloop may have one or two headsails,
while in Western Europe and Great Britain a sloop has only one
headsail.</def><br/
[<source>RH</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sloop of war</b></col>, <cd>formerly, a vessel of war
rigged either as a ship, brig, or schooner, and mounting from ten to
thirty-two guns; now, any war vessel larger than a gunboat, and carrying
guns on one deck only.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slop</ent><br/
<hw>Slop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sloppe</ets> a
pool; akin to As. <ets>sloppe</ets>, <ets>slyppe</ets>, the sloppy
droppings of a cow; cf. AS. <ets>sl<?/pan</ets> to slip, and E.
<ets>slip</ets>, v.i. Cf. <er>Cowslip</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Water or other liquid carelessly spilled or thrown aboyt, as upon a
table or a floor; a puddle; a soiled spot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Mean and weak drink or liquid food; -- usually in
the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Dirty water; water in which
anything has been washed or rinsed; water from wash-bowls,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Slop basin</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Slop
bowl</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a basin or bowl for holding slops, especially
for receiving the rinsings of tea or coffee cups at the table.</cd> --
<col><b>Slop molding</b></col> <fld>(Brickmaking)</fld>, <cd>a process
of manufacture in which the brick is carried to the drying ground in a
wet mold instead of on a pallet.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slop</ent><br/
<hw>Slop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slopped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slopping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to
overflow, as a liquid, by the motion of the vessel containing it; to
spill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To spill liquid upon; to soil with a liquid
spilled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slop</ent><br/
<hw>Slop</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To overflow or be spilled as a
liquid, by the motion of the vessel containing it; -- often with
<xex>over</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slop</ent><br/
<hw>Slop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>slop</ets> a frock or
over-garment, fr. <ets>sl<?/pan</ets> to slip, to slide; akin to Icel.
<ets>sloppr</ets> a thin garment; cf. OHG. <ets>slouf</ets> a garment.
Cf. <er>Slip</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any kind of
outer garment made of linen or cotton, as a night dress, or a smock
frock.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A loose lower garment; loose breeches; chiefly used
in the plural.</def> <ldquo/A pair of <xex>slops</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There's a French salutation to your French <qex>slop</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Ready-made clothes; also, among
seamen, clothing, bedding, and other furnishings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slope</ent><br/
<hw>Slope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formed (like
<ets>abode</ets> fr. <ets>abide</ets>) from OE. <ets>slipen</ets>. See
<er>Slip</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An oblique
direction; a line or direction including from a horizontal line or
direction; also, sometimes, an inclination, as of one line or surface to
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any ground whose surface forms an angle with the
plane of the horizon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> buildings the summit and <qex>slope</qex> of a hill.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Under the <qex>slopes</qex> of Pisgah.</q> <rj><qau>Deut. iv. 49.
(Rev. Ver.).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The part of a continent descending toward, and
draining to, a particular ocean; <as>as, the Pacific
<ex>slope</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A <xex>slope</xex>, considered as <xex>descending</xex>,
is a <xex>declivity</xex>; considered as <xex>ascending</xex>, an
<xex>acclivity</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slope of a plane</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>the
direction of the plane; <as>as, parallel planes have the same
<ex>slope</ex></as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slope</ent><br/
<hw>Slope</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sloping.</def> <ldquo/Down the
<xex>slope</xex> hills.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A bank not steep, but gently <qex>slope</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slope</ent><br/
<hw>Slope</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sloping manner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slope</ent><br/
<hw>Slope</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sloped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sloping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To form with a slope; to give an
oblique or slanting direction to; to direct obliquely; to incline; to
slant; <as>as, to <ex>slope</ex> the ground in a garden; to
<ex>slope</ex> a piece of cloth in cutting a garment.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slope</ent><br/
<hw>Slope</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take an oblique
direction; to be at an angle with the plane of the horizon; to incline;
<as>as, the ground <ex>slopes</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To depart; to disappear suddenly.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slopeness</ent><br/
<hw>Slope"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State of being slope.</def>
<rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slopewise</ent><br/
<hw>Slope"wise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Obliquely.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloping</ent><br/
<hw>Slop"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inclining or inclined from the
plane of the horizon, or from a horizontal or other right line; oblique;
declivous; slanting.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Slop"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sloping</qex> land recedes into the clouds.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloppiness</ent><br/
<hw>Slop"pi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sloppy; muddiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloppy</ent><br/
<hw>Slop"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sloppier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sloppiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Slop</er>.]</ety>
<def>Wet, so as to spatter easily; wet, as with something slopped over;
muddy; plashy; <as>as, a <ex>sloppy</ex> place, walk,
road</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slopseller</ent><br/
<hw>Slop"sell`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sells
slops, or ready-made clothes. See 4th <er>Slop</er>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slopshop</ent><br/
<hw>Slop"shop`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shop where slops.
or ready-made clothes, are sold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slopwork</ent><br/
<hw>Slop"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The manufacture of
slops, or cheap ready-made clothing; also, such clothing; hence, hasty,
slovenly work of any kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No <qex>slopwork</qex> ever dropped from his [Carlyle's] pen.</q>
<rj><qau>Froude.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slopy</ent><br/
<hw>Slop"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sloping;
inclined.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloshy</ent><br/
<ent>Slosh</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Slosh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Slosh"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>. <def>See <er>Slush</er>, <er>Slushy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slot</ent><br/
<hw>Slot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LG. & D. <ets>slot</ets>
a lock, from a verb meaning to close., to shut, D. <ets>sluiten</ets>;
akin to G. <ets>schliessen</ets>, OHG. <ets>sliozan</ets>, OFries.
<ets>sl<?/ta</ets>, and probably to L. <ets>claudere</ets>. Cf.
<er>Close</er>, <er>Sluice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A broad, flat,
wooden bar; a slat or sloat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bolt or bar for fastening a door.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A narrow depression, perforation, or aperture; esp.,
one for the reception of a piece fitting or sliding in it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slot</ent><br/
<hw>Slot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Slot</er> a
bar.]</ety> <def>To shut with violence; to slam; <as>as, to
<ex>slot</ex> a door</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slot</ent><br/
<hw>Slot</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel. <ets>sl<?/<?/</ets>, and E.
<ets>sleuth</ets>.]</ety> <def>The track of a deer; hence, a track of
any kind.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As a bloodhound follows the <qex>slot</qex> of a hurt deer.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloth</ent><br/
<hw>Sloth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>slouthe</ets>,
<ets>sleuthe</ets>, AS. <ets>sl<?/w<?/</ets>, fr. <ets>sl<amac/w</ets>
slow. See <er>Slow</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Slowness;
tardiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These cardinals trifle with me; I abhor<br/
This dilatory <qex>sloth</qex> and tricks of Rome.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Disinclination to action or labor; sluggishness;
laziness; idleness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They] change their course to pleasure, ease, and
<qex>sloth</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sloth</qex>, like rust, consumes faster than labor wears.</q>
<rj><qau>Franklin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
arboreal edentates constituting the family <fam>Bradypodidae</fam>, and
the suborder Tardigrada. They have long exserted limbs and long
prehensile claws. Both jaws are furnished with teeth (see
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Edentata</er>), and the ears and tail are
rudimentary. They inhabit South and Central America and
Mexico.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The three-toed sloths belong to the genera
<gen>Bradypus</gen> and <gen>Arctopithecus</gen>, of which several
species have been described. They have three toes on each foot. The
best-known species are collared sloth (<spn>Bradypus tridactylus</spn>),
and the ai (<spn>Arctopitheus ai</spn>). The two-toed sloths, consisting
the genus <gen>Cholopus</gen>, have two toes on each fore foot and three
on each hind foot. The best-known is the unau (<spn>Cholopus
didactylus</spn>) of South America. See <er>Unau</er>. Another species
(<spn>Cholopus Hoffmanni</spn>) inhabits Central America.<br/
Various large extinct terrestrial edentates, such as Megatherium and
Mylodon, are often called <ex>sloths</ex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Australian sloth</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Native sloth</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
koala.</cd> -- <col><b>Sloth animalcule</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a tardigrade.</cd> -- <col><b>Sloth bear</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a black or brown long-haired bear (<spn>Melursus
ursinus</spn>, <it>or</it> <spn>Melursus labiatus</spn>), native of
India and Ceylon; -- called also <altname>aswail</altname>,
<altname>labiated bear</altname>, and <altname>jungle bear</altname>. It
is easily tamed and can be taught many tricks.</cd> -- <col><b>Sloth
monkey</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a loris.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloth</ent><br/
<hw>Sloth</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be idle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gower.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slothful</ent><br/
<hw>Sloth"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Addicted to sloth;
inactive; sluggish; lazy; indolent; idle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He also that is <qex>slothful</qex> in his work is brother to him
that is a great waster.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xviii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sloth"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sloth"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slothhound</ent><br/
<hw>Sloth"hound`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Slot</er> a track, and cf. <er>Sleuthhound</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Sleuthhound</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slot machine</ent><br/
<hw>Slot" ma*chine"</hw>. <def>A machine the operation of which is
started by dropping a coin into a slot, for delivering small articles of
merchandise, showing one's weight, exhibiting pictures, throwing dice,
gambling, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slotted</ent><br/
<hw>Slot"ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
slot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slotting</ent><br/
<hw>Slot"ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or process
of making slots, or mortises.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slouch</ent><br/
<hw>Slouch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>sl<?/kr</ets>a slouching felloew, and E. <ets>slack</ets>,
<ets>slug</ets>, a lazy fellow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A hanging down
of the head; a drooping attitude; a limp appearance; an ungainly,
clownish gait; a sidewise depression or hanging down, as of a hat
brim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An awkward, heavy, clownish fellow.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slouth hat</b></col>, <cd>a soft, limp hat of unstiffened
cloth or felt.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slouch</ent><br/
<hw>Slouch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slouched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slouching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To droop, as the
head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To walk in a clumsy, lazy manner.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slouch</ent><br/
<hw>Slouch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to hang down; to depress
at the side; <as>as, to <ex>slouth</ex> the hat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slouching</ent><br/
<hw>Slouch"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hanging down at the side; limp;
drooping; without firmness or shapeliness; moving in an ungainly
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slouchy</ent><br/
<hw>Slouch"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slouching.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slough</ent><br/
<hw>Slough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slough</ent><br/
<hw>Slough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>slogh</ets>,
<ets>slough</ets>, AS. <ets>sl<omac/h</ets> a hollow place; cf. MHG.
<ets>sl<umac/ch</ets> an abyss, gullet, G. <ets>schlucken</ets> to
swallow; also Gael. & Ir. <ets>sloc</ets> a pit, pool. ditch, Ir.
<ets>slug</ets> to swallow. Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ to hiccough, to
sob.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place of deep mud or mire; a hole full of
mire.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He's here stuck in a <qex>slough</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pr>[<it>Pronounced</it> sl<oomac/.]</pr> <def>A wet
place; a swale; a side channel or inlet from a river.</def> <note>[In
this sense local or provincial; also spelt <asp>sloo</asp>, and
<asp>slue</asp>.]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slough grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name in
the Mississippi valley for grasses of the genus <gen>Muhlenbergia</gen>;
-- called also <altname>drop seed</altname>, and <altname>nimble
Will</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slough</ent><br/
<hw>Slough</hw>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Slee</er>, to slay. Slew.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slough</ent><br/
<hw>Slough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>slugh</ets>,
<ets>slouh</ets>; cf. MHG. <ets>sl<?/ch</ets> the skin of a serpent, G.
<ets>schlauch</ets> a skin, a leather bag or bottle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The skin, commonly the cast-off skin, of a serpent or of some
similar animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The dead mass separating from a
foul sore; the dead part which separates from the living tissue in
mortification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slough</ent><br/
<hw>Slough</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sloughed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sloughing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To form a
slough; to separate in the form of dead matter from the living tissues;
-- often used with <xex>off</xex>, or <xex>away</xex>; <as>as, a
<ex>sloughing</ex> ulcer; the dead tissues <ex>slough</ex> off
slowly.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slough</ent><br/
<hw>Slough</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cast off; to discard as
refuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>New tint the plumage of the birds,<br/
And <qex>slough</qex> decay from grazing herds.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloughing</ent><br/
<hw>Slough"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The act of casting off the skin or shell, as do insects and
crustaceans; ecdysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloughy</ent><br/
<hw>Slough"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of sloughs,
miry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloughy</ent><br/
<hw>Slough"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling, or of the
nature of, a slough, or the dead matter which separates from living
flesh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloven</ent><br/
<hw>Slov"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>slaf</ets>
careless, negligent, a sloven; akin to LG. <ets>sluf</ets>
slovenly.]</ety> <def>A man or boy habitually negligent of neathess and
order; -- the correlative term to <xex>slattern</xex>, or
<xex>slut</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He became a confirmed <qex>sloven</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slovenliness</ent><br/
<hw>Slov"en*li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being slovenly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slovenly</ent><br/
<hw>Slov"en*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the habits of
a sloven; negligent of neatness and order, especially in
dress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>slovenly</qex>, lazy fellow, lolling at his ease.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characteristic of a sloven; lacking neatness and
order; evincing negligence; <as>as, <ex>slovenly</ex>
dress</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slovenly</ent><br/
<hw>Slov"en*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>a slovenly manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slovenness</ent><br/
<hw>Slov"en*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Slovenliness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slovenry</ent><br/
<hw>Slov"en*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Slovenliness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slow</ent><br/
<hw>Slow</hw> <pr>(sl<omac/)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos>
<mord>of <er>Slee</er>, to slay.</mord> <def>Slew.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slow</ent><br/
<hw>Slow</hw> <pr>(sl<omac/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Slower</adjf>
<pr>(sl<omac/"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Slowest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>slow</ets>,
<ets>slaw</ets>, AS. <ets>sl<amac/w</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>sl<emac/u</ets> blunt, dull, D. <ets>sleeuw</ets>, <ets>slee</ets>,
sour, OHG. <ets>sl<emac/o</ets> blunt, dull, Icel. <ets>sl<omac/r</ets>,
<ets>sl<ae/r</ets>, Dan. <ets>sl<oum/v</ets>, Sw. <ets>sl<oum/</ets>.
Cf. <er>Sloe</er>, and <er>Sloth</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Moving a
short space in a relatively long time; not swift; not quick in motion;
not rapid; moderate; deliberate; <as>as, a <ex>slow</ex> stream; a
<ex>slow</ex> motion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not happening in a short time; gradual;
late.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These changes in the heavens, though <qex>slow</qex>, produced<br/
Like change on sea and land, sidereal blast.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not ready; not prompt or quick; dilatory; sluggish;
<as>as, <ex>slow</ex> of speech, and <ex>slow</ex> of
tongue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fixed on defense, the Trojans are not <qex>slow</qex><br/
To guard their shore from an expected foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not hasty; not precipitate; acting with
deliberation; tardy; inactive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that is <qex>slow</qex> to wrath is of great understanding.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xiv. 29.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Behind in time; indicating a time earlier than the
true time; <as>as, the clock or watch is <ex>slow</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Not advancing or improving rapidly; <as>as, the
<ex>slow</ex> growth of arts and sciences</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Heavy in wit; not alert, prompt, or spirited;
wearisome; dull.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.
Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Slow</xex> is often used in the formation of
compounds for the most part self-explaining; as, <xex>slow</xex>-gaited,
<xex>slow</xex>-paced, <xex>slow</xex>-sighted, <xex>slow</xex>-winged,
and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slow coach</b></col>, <cd>a slow person. See def.7,
above.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <mcol><col><b>Slow
lemur</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Slow loris</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd> an East Indian nocturnal lemurine animal
(<spn>Nycticebus tardigradus</spn>) about the size of a small cat; -- so
called from its slow and deliberate movements. It has very large round
eyes and is without a tail. Called also <altname>bashful
Billy</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Slow match</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Match</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Dilatory; late; lingering; tardy; sluggish; dull;
inactive.</syn> -- <usage><er>Slow</er>, <er>Tardy</er>,
<er>Dilatory</er>. <xex>Slow</xex> is the wider term, denoting either a
want of rapid motion or inertness of intellect. <xex>Dilatory</xex>
signifies a proneness to <xex>defer</xex>, a habit of delaying the
performance of what we know must be done. <xex>Tardy</xex> denotes the
habit of being behind hand; as, <xex>tardy</xex> in making up one's
acounts.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slow</ent><br/
<hw>Slow</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Slowly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let him have time to mark how <qex>slow</qex> time goes<br/
In time of sorrow.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slow</ent><br/
<hw>Slow</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slowed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slowing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To render slow; to slacken the
speed of; to retard; to delay; <as>as, to <ex>slow</ex> a
steamer</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slow</ent><br/
<hw>Slow</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To go slower; -- often with
<xex>up</xex>; <as>as, the train <ex>slowed</ex> up before crossing the
bridge</as>.</def> <-- also with down. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slow</ent><br/
<hw>Slow</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A moth.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Rom. of R.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slowback</ent><br/
<hw>Slow"back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A lubber; an idle
fellow; a loiterer.</def> <mark>[Old Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Dr.
Favour.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slowh</ent><br/
<hw>Slowh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Slee</er>,to slay.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slowhound</ent><br/
<hw>Slow"hound`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
sleuthhound.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slowly</ent><br/
<hw>Slow"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a slow manner; moderately; not
rapidly; not early; not rashly; not readly; tardly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slowness</ent><br/
<hw>Slow"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
slow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slows</ent><br/
<hw>Slows</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Milk
sickness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slow-witted</ent><br/
<hw>Slow"-wit`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dull of
apprehension; not possessing quick intelligence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slowworm</ent><br/
<hw>Slow"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sl<amac/wyrm</ets>; the first part is probably akin to
<ets>sle<aacute/n</ets> to strike, the reptile being supposed to be very
poisonous. See <er>Slay</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and
<er>Worm</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A lecertilian reptile; the
blindworm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sloyd</ent><br/
<hw>Sloyd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>slojd</asp>, and <asp>sloid</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[Sw.
<ets>sl<oum/jd</ets> skill, dexterity, esp. skilled labor, hence,
manufacture, wood carving.]</ety> <def>Lit., skilled mechanical work,
such as that required in wood carving; trade work; hence, a system
(usually called the <xex>sloyd system</xex>) of manual training in the
practical use of the tools and materials used in the trades, and of
instruction in the making and use of the plans and specifications
connected with trade work. The sloyd system derives its name from the
fact that it was adopted or largely developed from a similar Swedish
system, in which wood carving was a chief feature. Its purpose is not
only to afford practical skill in some trade, but also to develop the
pupils mentally and physically.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slub</ent><br/
<hw>Slub</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A roll of wool slightly twisted; a rove; --
called also <altname>slubbing</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slub</ent><br/
<hw>Slub</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slubbed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slubbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To draw out and twist slightly;
-- said of slivers of wool.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slubber</ent><br/
<hw>Slub"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slubbered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Slubbering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>slubbre</ets>to swallow, to sup up, D. <ets>slobberen</ets> to lap,
to slabber. Cf. <er>Slabber</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To do lazily,
imperfectly, or coarsely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Slubber</qex> not business for my sake.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To daub; to stain; to cover carelessly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is no art that hath more . . . <qex>slubbered</qex> with
aphorisming pedantry than the art of policy.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slubber</ent><br/
<hw>Slub"ber</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slubbing machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slubberdegullion</ent><br/
<hw>Slub"ber*de*gul`lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Slubber</ets> + Prov. E. <ets>gullion</ets> a wretch.]</ety>
<def>A mean, dirty wretch.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slubberingly</ent><br/
<hw>Slub"ber*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a slovenly, or hurried
and imperfect, manner.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slubbing</ent><br/
<hw>Slub"bing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Slub</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Slubbing billy</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Slubbing machine</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the machine by which
slubs are formed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sludge</ent><br/
<hw>Sludge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Slush</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Mud; mire; soft mud;
slush.</def> <rj><au>Mortimer. Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Small floating pieces of ice, or masses of saturated
snow.</def> <rj><au>Kane.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>See <er>Slime</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Anything resembling mud or slush; as: <sd>(a)</sd> A
muddy or slimy deposit from sweage. <sd>(b)</sd> Mud from a drill hole
in boring. <sd>(c)</sd> Muddy sediment in a steam boiler. <sd>(d)</sd>
Settling of cottonseed oil, used in making soap, etc. <sd>(e)</sd> A
residuum of crude paraffin-oil distillation.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sludge hole</b></col>, <cd>the hand-hole, or manhole, in
a steam boiler, by means of which sediment can be removed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sludge acid</ent><br/
<hw>Sludge acid</hw>. <def>Impure dark-colored sulphuric acid that has
been used in the refining of petroleum.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sludger</ent><br/
<hw>Sludg"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shovel for sludging
out drains, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1357 --></p>

<p><ent>Sludger</ent><br/
<hw>Slud"ger</hw> <pr>(sl<ucr/j"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
bucket for removing mud from a bored hole; a sand pump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sludy</ent><br/
<hw>Slud"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Miry; slushy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slue</ent><br/
<hw>Slue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slued</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sluing</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>slew</ets> to turn round, Scot. to lean or incline to a side; cf.
Icel. <ets>sn<?/a</ets> to turn, bend.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>slew</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To turn
about a fixed point, usually the center or axis, as a spar or piece of
timber; to turn; -- used also of any heavy body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In general, to turn about; to twist; -- often used
reflexively and followed by <xex>round</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They laughed, and <qex>slued</qex> themselves round.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slue</ent><br/
<hw>Slue</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To turn about; to turn from the
course; to slip or slide and turn from an expected or desired course; --
often followed by <xex>round</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slue</ent><br/
<hw>Slue</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Sloough</er>, 2.</def>
<mark>[Local]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slug</ent><br/
<hw>Slug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>slugge</ets>
slothful, <ets>sluggen</ets> to be slothful; cf. LG. <ets>slukk</ets>
low-spirited, sad, E. <ets>slack</ets>, <ets>slouch</ets>, D.
<ets>slak</ets>, <ets>slek</ets>, a snail.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
drone; a slow, lazy fellow; a sluggard.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hindrance; an obstruction.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
terrestrial pulmonate mollusks belonging to Limax and several related
genera, in which the shell is either small and concealed in the mantle,
or altogether wanting. They are closely allied to the land
snails.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any smooth, soft larva of a
sawfly or moth which creeps like a mollusk; <as>as, the pear
<ex>slug</ex>; rose <ex>slug</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A ship that sails slowly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His rendezvous for his fleet, and for all <qex>slugs</qex> to come
to, should be between Calais and Dover.</q>
<rj><qau>Pepys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <ety>[Perhaps a different word.]</ety> <def>An
irregularly shaped piece of metal, used as a missile for a gun.</def><--
also, a colloq. term for bullet. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A thick strip of metal less than
type high, and as long as the width of a column or a page, -- used in
spacing out pages and to separate display lines, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sea slug</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>Any nudibranch mollusk</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A holothurian.</cd> --
<col><b>Slug caterpillar</b></col>. <cd>Same as
<er>Slugworm</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slug</ent><br/
<hw>Slug</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To move slowly; to lie idle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>slug</qex> in sloth and sensual delight.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slug</ent><br/
<hw>Slug</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make sluggish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slug</ent><br/
<hw>Slug</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slugged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slugging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To load
with a slug or slugs; <as>as, to <ex>slug</ex> a gun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strike heavily.</def> <mark>[Cant or
Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slug</ent><br/
<hw>Slug</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become reduced in diameter, or
changed in shape, by passing from a larger to a smaller part of the bore
of the barrel; -- said of a bullet when fired from a gun, pistol, or
other firearm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slugabed</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"a*bed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who indulges
in lying abed; a sluggard.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Fie, you
<xex>slugabed</xex>!<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluggard</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"gard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Slug</ets> +
<ets>-ard</ets>.]</ety> <def>A person habitually lazy, idle, and
inactive; a drone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Go to the ant, thou <qex>sluggard</qex>; consider her ways, and be
wise.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. vi. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluggard</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"gard</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sluggish; lazy.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluggardize</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"gard*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make
lazy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluggardy</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"gard*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sloggardye</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state of being a sluggard;
sluggishness; sloth.</def> <rj><au>Gower.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Idleness is rotten <qex>sluggardy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slugger</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who strikes heavy
blows; hence, a boxer; a prize fighter.</def> <mark>[Cant or
Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- (Baseball) A player with a high batting average, esp. one who
hits many home runs. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slugging match</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"ging match</hw>. <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A boxing match or prize
fight marked rather by heavy hitting than skill.</def> <mark>[Cant or
Slang]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A ball game, esp. a baseball game, in
which there is much hard hitting of the ball.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slugging average</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"ging av`er*age</hw>. <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>a measure of
the effectiveness of a batter at reaching base and advancing other
runners, calculated as the sum of the number of bases reached on each
hit, divided by the total number of times at bat. A double counts two
bases, a triple three, a home run four. Thus a batter with four singles,
two doubles and a triple in 20 official times at bat would have a
<ex>slugging average</ex> of 0.550, and a <er>batting average</er> of
0.350. Also called <altname>slugging percentage</altname>. Compare
<er>batting average</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluggish</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"gish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Habitually idle and lazy; slothful; dull; inactive; <as>as, a
<ex>sluggish</ex> man</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Slow; having little motion; <as>as, a
<ex>sluggish</ex> stream</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having no power to move one's self or itself;
inert.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Matter, being impotent, <qex>sluggish</qex>, and inactive, hath no
power to stir or move itself.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the <qex>sluggish</qex> land slumbers in utter neglect.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Characteristic of a sluggard; dull; stupid; tame;
simple.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/So <xex>sluggish</xex> a
conceit.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Inert; idle; lazy; slothful; indolent; dronish;
slow; dull; drowsy; inactive. See <er>Inert</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Slug"gish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Slug"gish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluggy</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sluggish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slug-horn</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"-horn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>An erroneous form
of the Scotch word <asp>slughorne</asp>, or <asp>sloggorne</asp>,
meaning <xex>slogan</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slugs</ent><br/
<hw>Slugs</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld>
<def>Half-roasted ore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slugworm</ent><br/
<hw>Slug"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any caterpillar which has the general appearance of a slug, as do
those of certain moths belonging to <gen>Limacodes</gen> and allied
genera, and those of certain sawflies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluice</ent><br/
<hw>Sluice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escluse</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/cluse</ets>, LL. <ets>exclusa</ets>,
<ets>sclusa</ets>, from L. <ets>excludere</ets>, <ets>exclusum</ets>, to
shut out: cf. D. <ets>sluis</ets> sluice, from the Old French. See
<er>Exclude</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An artifical passage for
water, fitted with a valve or gate, as in a mill stream, for stopping or
regulating the flow; also, a water gate or flood gate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, an opening or channel through which anything
flows; a source of supply.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each <qex>sluice</qex> of affluent fortune opened soon.</q>
<rj><qau>Harte.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This home familiarity . . . opens the <qex>sluices</qex> of
sensibility.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The stream flowing through a flood gate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A long box or trough through
which water flows, -- used for washing auriferous earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sluice gate</b></col>, <cd>the sliding gate of a
sluice.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluice</ent><br/
<hw>Sluice</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sluiced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sluicing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To emit
by, or as by, flood gates.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wet copiously, as by opening a sluice; <as>as, to
<ex>sluice</ex> meadows</as>.</def> <rj><au>Howitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He dried his neck and face, which he had been <qex>sluicing</qex>
with cold water.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To wash with, or in, a stream of water running
through a sluice; <as>as, to <ex>sluice</ex> eart or gold dust in
mining</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluiceway</ent><br/
<hw>Sluice"way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An artificial
channel into which water is let by a sluice; specifically, a trough
constructed over the bed of a stream, so that logs, lumber, or rubbish
can be floated down to some convenient place of delivery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluicy</ent><br/
<hw>Slui`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Falling copiously or
in streams, as from a sluice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And oft whole sheets descend of <qex>sluicy</qex> rain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slum</ent><br/
<hw>Slum</hw> <pr>(sl<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Slump</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A foul back street
of a city, especially one filled with a poor, dirty, degraded, and often
vicious population; any low neighborhood or dark retreat; -- usually in
the plural; <as>as, Westminster <ex>slums</ex> are haunts for
theives</as>.</def> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Slimes</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slum</ent><br/
<hw>Slum</hw> <pr>(sl<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To visit or
frequent slums, esp. out of curiosity, or for purposes of study, etc.
Also called <altname>go slumming</altname>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumber</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Slumbered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Slumbering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slombren</ets>, <ets>slumberen</ets>, <ets>slumeren</ets>, AS.
<ets>slumerian</ets>, fr. <ets>sluma</ets> slumber; akin to D.
<ets>sluimeren</ets> to slumber, MHG. <ets>slummern</ets>,
<ets>slumen</ets>, G. <ets>schlummern</ets>, Dan. <ets>slumre</ets>, Sw.
<ets>slumra</ets>, Goth. <ets>slawan</ets> to be silent.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To sleep; especially, to sleep lightly; to doze.</def>
<rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that keepeth Israel shall neither <qex>slumber</qex> nor
sleep.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. cxxi. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be in a state of negligence, sloth, supineness,
or inactivity.</def> <ldquo/Why <xex>slumbers</xex> Pope?<rdquo/
<rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumber</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ber</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay to
sleep.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stun; to stupefy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumber</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ber</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sleep; especially, light sleep;
sleep that is not deep or sound; repose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He at last fell into a <qex>slumber</qex>, and thence into a fast
sleep, which detained him in that place until it was almost night.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fast asleep? It is no matter;<br/
Enjoy the honey-heavy dew of <qex>slumber</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rest to my soul, and <qex>slumber</qex> to my eyes.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumberer</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ber*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who slumbers;
a sleeper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumberingly</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ber*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a slumbering
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumberless</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ber*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without slumber;
sleepless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumberous</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ber*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Inviting slumber; soporiferous.</def> <ldquo/Pensive in the
<xex>slumberous</xex> shade.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Being in the repose of slumber; sleepy;
drowsy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His quiet and almost <qex>slumberous</qex> countenance.</q>
<rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumbery</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ber*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sleepy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumbrous</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"brous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slumberous.</def>
<rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumgum</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"gum`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The impure residue,
consisting of cocoons, propolis, etc., remaining after the wax is
extracted from honeycombs.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumming</ent><br/
<hw>Slum"ming</hw>, <pos>vb. n.</pos> <def>Visiting slums. Sometimes
used humorously, in reference to the appearance of a dignified person in
a situation generally thought of as low-class.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slump</ent><br/
<hw>Slump</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>slomp</ets>
a mass, heap, Dan. <ets>slump</ets> a quantity, and E. <ets>slump</ets>,
v.t.]</ety> <def>The gross amount; the mass; the lump.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slump</ent><br/
<hw>Slump</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Lump</er>; also Sw.
<ets>slumpa</ets> to bargain for the lump.]</ety> <def>To lump; to throw
into a mess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These different groups . . . are exclusively <qex>slumped</qex>
together under that sense.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slump</ent><br/
<hw>Slump</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Slumped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Slumping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Scot. <ets>slump</ets> a dull
noise produced by something falling into a hole, a marsh, a
swamp.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fall or sink suddenly through or in,
when walking on a surface, as on thawing snow or ice, partly frozen
ground, a bog, etc., not strong enough to bear the person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The latter walk on a bottomless quag, into which unawares they may
<qex>slump</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To slide or slip on a declivity, so that the motion
is perceptible; -- said of masses of earth or rock.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To undergo a slump, or sudden decline or falling
off; <as>as, the stock <ex>slumped</ex> ten points</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slump</ent><br/
<hw>Slump</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A boggy place.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The noise made by anything falling into a hole, or
into a soft, miry place.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A falling or declining, esp. suddenly and markedly;
a falling off; <as>as, a <ex>slump</ex> in trade, in stock market
prices, in a batter's average, etc.</as></def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slumpy</ent><br/
<hw>Slump"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Easily broken through;
boggy; marshy; swampy.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Colloq. U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slung</ent><br/
<hw>Slung</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sling</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Slung shot</b></col>, <cd>a metal ball of small size,
with a string attached, used by ruffians for striking.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slunk</ent><br/
<hw>Slunk</hw> <pr>(sl<ucr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Slink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slur</ent><br/
<hw>Slur</hw> <pr>(sl<ucir/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Slurred</conjf> <pr>(sl<ucir/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Slurring</conjf>
<pr>(sl<ucir/r"r<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OE. <ets>sloor</ets>
mud, clay, Icel. <ets>sl<omac/ra</ets>, <ets>slo<edh/ra</ets>, to trail
or drag one's self along, D. <ets>sleuren</ets>, <ets>sloren</ets>, to
train, to drag, to do negligently and slovenly, D. <ets>sloor</ets>,
<ets>sloerie</ets>, a sluttish girl.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To soil; to
sully; to contaminate; to disgrace.</def>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To disparage; to traduce.</def>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cover over; to disguise; to conceal; to pass over
lightly or with little notice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With periods, points, and tropes, he <qex>slurs</qex> his
crimes.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cheat, as by sliding a die; to trick.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>slur</qex> men of what they fought for.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To pronounce indistinctly; <as>as, to <ex>slur</ex>
syllables; to <ex>slur</ex> one's words</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To sing or perform in a smooth,
gliding style; to connect smoothly in performing, as several notes or
tones.</def> <rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>To blur or double, as an
impression from type; to mackle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slur</ent><br/
<hw>Slur</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mark or stain; hence, a
slight reproach or disgrace; a stigma; a reproachful intimation; an
innuendo.</def> <ldquo/Gaining to his name a lasting
<xex>slur</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A trick played upon a person; an imposition.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A mark, thus [<upslur/ or
<downslur/], connecting notes that are to be sung to the same syllable,
or made in one continued breath of a wind instrument, or with one stroke
of a bow; a tie; a sign of legato.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In knitting machines, a contrivance for depressing
the sinkers successively by passing over them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slurred</ent><br/
<hw>Slurred</hw> <pr>(sl<ucir/rd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>Marked with a slur; performed in a smooth, gliding style, like
notes marked with a slur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slush</ent><br/
<hw>Slush</hw> <pr>(sl<ucr/sh)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sw.
<ets>slaska</ets> to paddle in water, <ets>slask</ets> wet,
filth.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>slosh</asp>.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Soft mud.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mixture of snow and water; half-melted
snow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A soft mixture of grease and other materials, used
for lubrication.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The refuse grease and fat collected in cooking,
especially on shipboard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A mixture of white lead and lime,
with which the bright parts of machines, such as the connecting rods of
steamboats, are painted to be preserved from oxidation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slush</ent><br/
<hw>Slush</hw> <pr>(sl<ucr/sh)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Slushed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Slushing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To smear
with slush or grease; <as>as, to <ex>slush</ex> a mast</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To paint with a mixture of white lead and
lime.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slushy</ent><br/
<hw>Slush"y</hw> <pr>(sl<ucr/sh"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Abounding in slush; characterized by soft mud or half-melted snow;
<as>as, the streets are <ex>slushy</ex>; the snow is
<ex>slushy</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/A dark, drizzling, <xex>slushy</xex>
day.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Blackw. Mag.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slut</ent><br/
<hw>Slut</hw> <pr>(sl<ucr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>slutte</ets>; cf. OD. <ets>slodde</ets> a slut, Icel.
<ets>sl<oum/ttr</ets> a heavy, loglike fellow, <ets>slota</ets> to
droop.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An untidy woman; a slattern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sluts</qex> are good enough to make a sloven's porridge.</q>
<rj><qau>Old Proverb.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A servant girl; a drudge.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our little girl Susan is a most admirable <qex>slut</qex>, and
pleases us mightily, doing more service than both the others.</q>
<rj><qau>Pepys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A female dog; a bitch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slutch</ent><br/
<hw>Slutch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Sludge</er>.]</ety> <def>Slush.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slutchy</ent><br/
<hw>Slutch"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slushy.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Pennant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluthhound</ent><br/
<hw>Sluth"hound`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sleuthhound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluttery</ent><br/
<hw>Slut"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The qualities and
practices of a slut; sluttishness; slatternlines.</def>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sluttish</ent><br/
<hw>Slut"tish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a slut; untidy;
indecently negligent of cleanliness; disorderly; <as>as, a
<ex>sluttish</ex> woman</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why is thy lord so <qex>slutish</qex>, I thee pray.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An air of liberal, though <qex>sluttish</qex>, plenty, indicated
the wealthy farmer.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Slut"tish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Slut"tish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sly</ent><br/
<hw>Sly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Slier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <adjf>Slyer</adjf>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Sliest</adjf> or <adjf>Slyest</adjf>.]</amorph>
<ety>[OE. <ets>sli</ets>, <ets>slegh</ets>, <ets>sleih</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sl<?/gr</ets>, for <ets>sl<?/gr</ets>; akin to Sw. <ets>slug</ets>,
Dan. <ets>slu</ets>, LG. <ets>slou</ets>, G. <ets>schlau</ets>; probably
to E. <ets>slay</ets>, v.t.; cf. G. ver<ets>schlagen</ets> sly. See
<er>Slay</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Sleight</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Dexterous in performing an action, so as to escape
notice; nimble; skillful; cautious; shrewd; knowing; -- in a good
sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be ye <qex>sly</qex> as serpents, and simple as doves.</q>
<rj><qau>Wyclif (Matt. x. 16).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whom graver age<br/
And long experience hath made wise and <qex>sly</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Artfully cunning; secretly mischievous;
wily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For my <qex>sly</qex> wiles and subtle craftiness,<br/
The litle of the kingdom I possess.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Done with, and marked by, artful and dexterous
secrecy; subtle; <as>as, a <ex>sly</ex> trick</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Envy works in a <qex>sly</qex> and imperceptible manner.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Light or delicate; slight; thin.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>By the sly</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>On the
sly</b></col></mcol>, <cd>in a sly or secret manner.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/Gazed on Hetty's charms <xex>by the
sly</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>G. Eliot.</au> -- <col><b>Sly goose</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the common sheldrake; -- so named from its
craftiness.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Cunning; crafty; subtile; wily. See
<er>Cunning</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sly</ent><br/
<hw>Sly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Slyly.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slyboots</ent><br/
<hw>Sly"boots`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A humerous
appellation for a sly, cunning, or waggish person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Slyboots</qex> was cursedly cunning to hide 'em.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slyly</ent><br/
<hw>Sly"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sly manner; shrewdly;
craftily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Honestly and <qex>slyly</qex> he it spent.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slyness</ent><br/
<hw>Sly"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Slype</ent><br/
<hw>Slype</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>sluipen</ets> to sneak.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A narrow
passage between two buildings, as between the transept and chapter house
of a monastery.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smack</ent><br/
<hw>Smack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>smak</ets>;
akin to LG. <ets>smack</ets>, <ets>smak</ets>, Dan. <ets>smakke</ets>,
G. <ets>schmacke</ets>, F. <ets>semaque</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A small sailing vessel, commonly rigged as a sloop, used chiefly in
the coasting and fishing trade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smack</ent><br/
<hw>Smack</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>smak</ets>, AS.
<ets>ssm<?/c</ets> taste, savor; akin to D. <ets>smaak</ets>, G.
ge<ets>schmack</ets>, OHG. <ets>smac</ets>; cf. Lith. <ets>smagus</ets>
pleasant. Cf. <er>Smack</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Taste or flavor, esp. a slight taste or flavor; savor; tincture;
<as>as, a <ex>smack</ex> of bitter in the medicine</as>. Also used
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So quickly they have taken a <qex>smack</qex> in covetousness.</q>
<rj><qau>Robynson (More's Utopia).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They felt the <qex>smack</qex> of this world.</q>
<rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small quantity; a taste.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A loud kiss; a buss.</def> <ldquo/A clamorous
<xex>smack</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A quick, sharp noise, as of the lips when suddenly
separated, or of a whip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A quick, smart blow; a slap.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smack</ent><br/
<hw>Smack</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>As if with a smack or slap.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smack</ent><br/
<hw>Smack</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smacked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smacking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>smaken</ets> to taste,
have a taste, -- from the noun; cf. AS. <ets>smecan</ets> taste; akin to
D. <ets>smaken</ets>, G. <ets>schmecken</ets>, OHG. <ets>smechen</ets>
to taste, smach<?/n to have a taste (and, derived from the same source,
G. <ets>schmatzen</ets> to smack the lips, to kiss with a sharp noise,
MHG. <ets>smatzen</ets>, <ets>smackzeen</ets>), Icel. <ets>smakka</ets>
to taste, Sw. <ets>smaka</ets>, Dan. <ets>smage</ets>. See 2d
<er>Smack</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To have a smack;
to be tinctured with any particular taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have or exhibit and indication or suggestion of
the presence of any character or quality; to have a taste, or flavor; --
used with <ptcl>of</ptcl>; <as>as, a remark <ex>smacking</ex> of
contempt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q> All sects, all ages, <qex>smack</qex> of this vice.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To kiss with a close compression of the lips, so as
to make a sound when they separate; to kiss with a sharp noise; to
buss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make a noise by the separation of the lips after
tasting anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smack</ent><br/
<hw>Smack</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To kiss with a sharp
noise; to buss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To open, as the lips, with an inarticulate sound
made by a quick compression and separation of the parts of the mouth; to
make a noise with, as the lips, by separating them in the act of kissing
or after tasting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Drinking off the cup, and <qex>smacking</qex> his lips with an air
of ineffable relish.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make a sharp noise by striking; to crack; <as>as,
to <ex>smack</ex> a whip</as>.</def> <ldquo/She <xex>smacks</xex> the
silken thong.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smack</ent><br/
<hw>Smack</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as <er>heroin</er>; -- a slang
term.</def> <mark>[slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smacking</ent><br/
<hw>Smack"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sharp, quick noise; a
smack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like the faint <qex>smacking</qex> of an after kiss.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1358 --></p>

<p><ent>Smacking</ent><br/
<hw>Smack"ing</hw> <pr>(sm<acr/k"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Making a sharp, brisk sound; hence, brisk; <as>as, a
<ex>smacking</ex> breeze</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Small</ent><br/
<hw>Small</hw> <pr>(sm<add/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Smaller</adjf>
<pr>(sm<add/l"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Smallest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>small</ets>, AS.
<ets>smael</ets>; akin to D. <ets>smal</ets> narrow, OS. & OHG.
<ets>smal</ets> small, G. <ets>schmal</ets> narrow, Dan. & Sw.
<ets>smal</ets>, Goth. <ets>smals</ets> small, Icel. <ets>smali</ets>
small cattle, sheep, or goats; cf. Gr. <grk>mh^lon</grk> a sheep or
goat.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having little size, compared with other
things of the same kind; little in quantity or degree; diminutive; not
large or extended in dimension; not great; not much; inconsiderable;
<as>as, a <ex>small</ex> man; a <ex>small</ex> river</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To compare<br/
Great things with <qex>small</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Being of slight consequence; feeble in influence or
importance; unimportant; trivial; insignificant; <as>as, a
<ex>small</ex> fault; a <ex>small</ex> business</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Envincing little worth or ability; not large-minded;
-- sometimes, in reproach, paltry; mean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A true delineation of the <qex>smallest</qex> man is capable of
interesting the greatest man.</q> <rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj> <-- the
"g" of "greatest" was a blank in orig. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not prolonged in duration; not extended in time;
short; <as>as, after a <ex>small</ex> space</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Weak; slender; fine; gentle; soft; not loud.</def>
<ldquo/A still, <xex>small</xex> voice.<rdquo/ <rj><au>1 Kings xix.
12.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Great and small</b></col>,<cd>of all ranks or degrees; --
used especially of persons.</cd> <ldquo/His quests, <xex>great and
small</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>Small arms</b></col>,
<cd>muskets, rifles, pistols, etc., in distinction from cannon.</cd> --
<col><b>Small beer</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Beer</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Small coal</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Little coals of wood
formerly used to light fires</cd>. <au>Gay.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Coal
about the size of a hazelnut, separated from the coarser parts by
screening.</cd> -- <col><b>Small craft</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>a vessel, or vessels in general, of a small size.</cd> --
<col><b>Small fruits</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Fruit</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Small hand</b></col>, <cd>a certain size of paper. See under
<er>Paper</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Small hours</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Hour</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Small letter</b></col>.
<fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>a lower-case letter. See <er>Lower-case</er>,
and <cref>Capital letter</cref>, under <er>Capital</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Small piece</b></col>, <cd>a Scotch coin
worth about 2<frac14/d. sterling, or about 4<frac12/cents.</cd> --
<col><b>Small register</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under 1st
<er>Register</er>, 7.</cd> -- <col><b>Small stuff</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>spun yarn, marline, and the smallest kinds of
rope.</cd> <au>R. H. Dana, Jr.</au> -- <col><b>Small talk</b></col>,
<cd>light or trifling conversation; chitchat.</cd> -- <col><b>Small
wares</b></col> <fld>(Com.)</fld>, <cd>various small textile articles,
as tapes, braid, tringe, and the like.</cd>
<au>M<lsquo/Culloch.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Small</ent><br/
<hw>Small</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In or to small extent,
quantity, or degree; little; slightly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/I wept but <xex>small</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/It
<xex>small</xex> avails my mood.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not loudly; faintly; timidly.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Humorous]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You may speak as <qex>small</qex> as you will.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Small</ent><br/
<hw>Small</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The small or slender part
of a thing; <as>as, the <ex>small</ex> of the leg or of the
back</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Smallclothes.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Hood. Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Same as <cref>Little go</cref>. See
under <er>Little</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Small</ent><br/
<hw>Small</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make little or less.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smallage</ent><br/
<hw>Small"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Small</ets> +
F. <ets>ache</ets> smallage. See <er>Ach</er> parsley.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A biennial umbelliferous plant (<spn>Apium
graveolens</spn>) native of the seacoats of Europe and Asia. When
deprived of its acrid and even poisonous properties by cultivation, it
becomes <altname>celery</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smallclothes</ent><br/
<hw>Small"clothes`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>A man's
garment for the hips and thighs; breeches. See
<er>Breeches</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smallish</ent><br/
<hw>Small"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat small.</def> <rj><au>G.
W. Cable.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smallness</ent><br/
<hw>Small"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
small.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smallpox</ent><br/
<hw>Small"pox`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Small</ets> +
<ets>pox</ets>, <ets>pocks</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
contagious, constitutional, febrile disease characterized by a peculiar
eruption; variola. The cutaneous eruption is at first a collection of
papules which become vesicles (first flat, subsequently umbilicated) and
then pustules, and finally thick crusts which slough after a certain
time, often leaving a pit, or scar.</def><-- now no longer observed,
after a long campaing of vaccination apparently succeeded in eliminating
all human carriers by 1995. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smalls</ent><br/
<hw>Smalls</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See <er>Small</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smallsword</ent><br/
<hw>Small"sword`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A light sword
used for thrusting only; especially, the sword worn by civilians of rank
in the eighteenth century.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smally</ent><br/
<hw>Smal"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a small quantity
or degree; with minuteness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ascham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smalt</ent><br/
<hw>Smalt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It. <ets>smalto</ets>,
LL. <ets>smaltum</ets>; of Teutonic origin; cf. OHG. <ets>smalz</ets>
grease, butter, G. <ets>schmalz</ets> grease, OHG. <ets>smelzan</ets> to
melt, G. <ets>schmelzen</ets>. See <er>Smelt</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Amel</er>, <er>Enamel</er>.]</ety> <def>A deep blue pigment or
coloring material used in various arts. It is a vitreous substance made
of cobalt, potash, and calcined quartz fused, and reduced to a
powder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smalt-blue</ent><br/
<hw>Smalt"-blue`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Deep blue, like
smalt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smaltite</ent><br/
<ent>Smaltine</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smalt"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Smalt"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Smalt</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A tin-white or gray mineral of metallic luster. It is an arsenide
of cobalt, nickel, and iron. Called also
<altname>speiskobalt</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smaragd</ent><br/
<hw>Smar"agd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>smaragdus</ets>. See <er>Emerald</er>.]</ety> <def>The
emerald.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smaragdine</ent><br/
<hw>Sma*rag"dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>smaragdinus</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining
to emerald; resembling emerald; of an emerald green.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smaragdite</ent><br/
<hw>Sma*rag"dite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>smaragdite</ets>; -- so called from its emerald-green color. See
<er>Smaragd</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A green foliated kind of
amphibole, observed in eclogite and some varietis of gabbro.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smart</ent><br/
<hw>Smart</hw> <pr>(sm<aum/rt)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Smarted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smarting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>smarten</ets>, AS.
<ets>smeortan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>smarten</ets>, <ets>smerten</ets>,
G. <ets>schmerzen</ets>, OHG. <ets>smerzan</ets>, Dan.
<ets>smerte</ets>, Sw. <ets>sm<aum/rta</ets>, D. <ets>smart</ets>,
<ets>smert</ets>, a pain, G. <ets>schmerz</ets>, OHG. <ets>smerzo</ets>,
and probably to L. <ets>mordere</ets> to bite; cf. Gr.
<grk>smerdno`s</grk>, <grk>smerdale`os</grk>, terrible, fearful, Skr.
<ets>m<rsdot/d</ets> to rub, crush. Cf. <er>Morsel</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To feel a lively, pungent local pain; -- said of some
part of the body as the seat of irritation; <as>as, my finger
<ex>smarts</ex>; these wounds <ex>smart</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au> <au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To feel a pungent pain of mind; to feel sharp pain
or grief; to suffer; to feel the sting of evil; <as>as, the team is
still <ex>smarting</ex> from its loss of the
championship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No creature <qex>smarts</qex> so little as a fool.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that is surety for a stranger shall <qex>smart</qex> for
it.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xi. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smart</ent><br/
<hw>Smart</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause a smart in.</def>
<ldquo/A goad that . . . <xex>smarts</xex> the flesh.<rdquo/ <rj><au>T.
Adams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smart</ent><br/
<hw>Smart</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>smerte</ets>. See
<er>Smart</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Quick, pungent,
lively pain; a pricking local pain, as the pain from puncture by
nettles.</def> <ldquo/In pain's <xex>smart</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Severe, pungent pain of mind; pungent grief; <as>as,
the <ex>smart</ex> of affliction</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To stand 'twixt us and our deserved <qex>smart</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Counsel mitigates the greatest <qex>smart</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A fellow who affects smartness, briskness, and
vivacity; a dandy.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>Fielding.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Smart money (see below).</def>
<mark>[Canf]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smart</ent><br/
<hw>Smart</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Smarter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Smartest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>smerte</ets>. See
<er>Smart</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Causing a
smart; pungent; pricking; <as>as, a <ex>smart</ex> stroke or
taste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How <qex>smart</qex> lash that speech doth give my conscience.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Keen; severe; poignant; <as>as, <ex>smart</ex>
pain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Vigorous; sharp; severe.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Smart</xex> skirmishes, in which many fell.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Accomplishing, or able to accomplish, results
quickly; active; sharp; clever.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Efficient; vigorous; brilliant.</def> <ldquo/The
stars shine <xex>smarter</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Marked by acuteness or shrewdness; quick in
suggestion or reply; vivacious; witty; <as>as, a <ex>smart</ex> reply; a
<ex>smart</ex> saying</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who, for the poor renown of being <qex>smart</qex><br/
Would leave a sting within a brother's heart?</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A sentence or two, . . . which I thought very
<qex>smart</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Pretentious; showy; spruce; <as>as, a <ex>smart</ex>
gown</as>.</def> <-- in modifying dress or appearance, now used in the
sense of "neat, trim", or "stylish, attractive, elegant." --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Brisk; fresh; <as>as, a <ex>smart</ex>
breeze</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smart money</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Money paid by a
person to buy himself off from some unpleasant engagement or some
painful situation</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <cd>Money allowed
to soldiers or sailors, in the English service, for wounds and injures
received; also, a sum paid by a recruit, previous to being sworn in, to
procure his release from service</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<cd>Vindictive or exemplary damages; damages beyond a full compensation
for the actual injury done</cd>. <au>Burrill.</au>
<au>Greenleaf.</au><-- = punitive damages?. (d) (Finance) Knowledgeable
investors or bettors. "The smart money says that technology stocks are
at a peak." --> -- <col><b>Smart ticket</b></col>, <cd>a certificate
given to wounded seamen, entitling them to smart money.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark> <au>Brande & C.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Pungent; poignant; sharp; tart; acute; quick;
lively; brisk; witty; clever; keen; dashy; showy.</syn> --
<usage><er>Smart</er>, <er>Clever</er>. <xex>Smart</xex> has been much
used in New England to describe a person who is intelligent, vigorous,
and active; as, a <xex>smart</xex> young fellow; a <xex>smart</xex>
workman, etc., conciding very nearly with the English sense of
<xex>clever</xex>. The nearest approach to this in England is in such
expressions as, he was <xex>smart</xex> (pungent or witty) in his reply,
etc.; but <xex>smart</xex> and smartness, when applied to persons, more
commonly refer to dress; as, a <xex>smart</xex> appearance; a
<xex>smart</xex> gown, etc.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smarten</ent><br/
<hw>Smart"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make smart or
spruce; -- usually with <xex>up</xex>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She had to go and <qex>smarten</qex> herself up somewhat.</q>
<rj><qau>W. Black.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smartle</ent><br/
<hw>Smar"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To waste
away.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smartly</ent><br/
<hw>Smart"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a smart
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smartness</ent><br/
<hw>Smart"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
smart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smartweed</ent><br/
<hw>Smart"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An acrid plant of the genus <gen>Polygonum</gen> (<spn>Polygonum
Hydropiper</spn>), which produces smarting if applied where the skin is
tender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smash</ent><br/
<hw>Smash</hw> <pr>(sm<acr/sh)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Smashed</conjf> <pr>(sm<acr/sht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Smashing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Sw.
<ets>smisk</ets> a blow, stroke, <ets>smiska</ets> to strike, dial. Sw.
<ets>smaske</ets> to kiss with a noise, and E. <ets>smack</ets> a loud
kiss, a slap.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To break in pieces by violence; to
dash to pieces; to crush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here everything is broken and <qex>smashed</qex> to pieces.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Lawn Tennis)</fld> <def>To hit (the ball) from
above the level of the net with a very hard overhand stroke.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smash</ent><br/
<hw>Smash</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To break up, or to pieces
suddenly, as the result of collision or pressure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smash</ent><br/
<hw>Smash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A breaking or dashing to
pieces; utter destruction; wreck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, bankruptcy.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smasher</ent><br/
<hw>Smash"er</hw> <pr>(sm<acr/sh"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, smashes or breaks things to
pieces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything very large or extraordinary.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who passes counterfeit coin.</def> <mark>[Cant,
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smatch</ent><br/
<hw>Smatch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>smach</ets>,
<ets>smak</ets>. See <er>Smack</er> taste.]</ety> <def>Taste; tincture;
smack.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy life hath had some <qex>smatch</qex> of honor in it.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smatch</ent><br/
<hw>Smatch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To smack.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Banister (1578).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smatter</ent><br/
<hw>Smat"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>smateren</ets> to make a noise; cf. Sw. <ets>smattra</ets> to
clatter, to crackle, G. <ets>schmettern</ets> to dash, crash, to warble,
quaver.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To talk superficially or ignorantly; to
babble; to chatter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of state affairs you can not <qex>smatter</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have a slight taste, or a slight, superficial
knowledge, of anything; to smack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smatter</ent><br/
<hw>Smat"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To talk
superficially about.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To gain a slight taste of; to acquire a slight,
superficial knowledge of; to smack.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smatter</ent><br/
<hw>Smat"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Superficial knowledge; a
smattering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smatterer</ent><br/
<hw>Smat"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who has only a
slight, superficial knowledge; a sciolist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smattering</ent><br/
<hw>Smat"ter*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slight, superficial
knowledge of something; sciolism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had a great desire, not able to attain to a superficial skill in
any, to have some <qex>smattering</qex> in all.</q>
<rj><qau>Burton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smear</ent><br/
<hw>Smear</hw> <pr>(sm<emac/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Smeared</conjf> <pr>(sm<emac/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Smearing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>smeren</ets>, <ets>smerien</ets>, AS. <ets>smierwan</ets>,
<ets>smyrwan</ets>, fr. <ets>smeoru</ets> fat, grease; akin to D.
<ets>smeren</ets>, OHG. <ets>smirwen</ets>, G. <ets>schmieren</ets>,
Icel. <ets>smyrja</ets> to anoint. See <er>Smear</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To overspread with anything
unctuous, viscous, or adhesive; to daub; <as>as, to <ex>smear</ex>
anything with oil</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Smear</xex> the sleepy grooms
with blood.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To soil in any way; to contaminate; to pollute; to
stain morally; <as>as, to be <ex>smeared</ex> with infamy</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To smudge, blur, or render indistinct (writing,
pictures, etc.).</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>to vilify (a person); to damage (a person's
reputation), especially falsely or by unfair innuendo, and with
malicious intent.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smear</ent><br/
<hw>Smear</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>smere</ets>,.
<ets>smeoru</ets> fat, grease; akin to D. <ets>smeer</ets>, G.
<ets>schmeer</ets>, OHG. <ets>smero</ets>, Icel. <ets>smj<oum/r</ets>,
Sw. & Dan. <ets>sm<oum/r</ets> butter, Goth.
<ets>sma<iacute/r<thorn/r</ets> fatness, <ets>smarna</ets> dung; cf.
Lith. <ets>smarsas</ets> fat. Cf. <er>Smirch</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A fat, oily substance; oinment.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a spot made by, or as by, an unctuous or
adhesive substance; a blot or blotch; a daub; a stain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Slow broke the morn,<br/
All damp and rolling vapor, with no sun,<br/
But in its place a moving <qex>smear</qex> of light.</q>
<rj><qau>Alexander Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smearcase</ent><br/
<hw>Smear"case`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>schmierk<aum/se</ets>; <ets>schmier</ets> grease (or
<ets>schmieren</ets> to smear) + <ets>k<aum/se</ets> cheese.]</ety>
<def>Cottage cheese.</def> <mark>[Local, U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smear dab</ent><br/
<hw>Smear" dab"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The sand
fluke <sd>(b)</sd>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeared</ent><br/
<hw>Smeared</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the color mark ings ill defined, as if rubbed; <as>as, the
<ex>smeared</ex> dagger moth (<spn>Apatela oblinita</spn>)</as>.</def>
<-- #sic ?sp. Under "dagger moth", it says genus = Apatalea --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeary</ent><br/
<hw>Smear"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to smear or
soil; adhesive; viscous.</def> <rj><au>Rowe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeath</ent><br/
<hw>Smeath</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
smew.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smectite</ent><br/
<hw>Smec"tite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>smectit</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ a kind of fuller's earth,
fr. <?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ to wipe off.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A hydrous
silicate of alumina, of a greenish color, which, in certain states of
humidity, appears transparent and almost gelatinous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smee</ent><br/
<hw>Smee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Smew</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The pintail
duck.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The widgeon.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
poachard.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The smew.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeeth</ent><br/
<hw>Smeeth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>To smoke; to blacken with smoke; to rub with
soot.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeeth</ent><br/
<hw>Smeeth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sme<?/en</ets>, AS. <ets>sm<?/<?/ian</ets>. See
<er>Smooth</er>.]</ety> <def>To smooth.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smegma</ent><br/
<hw>Smeg"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ soap, fr. <?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ to wash off.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The matter secreted by any of the sebaceous
glands.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The soapy substance
covering the skin of newborn infants.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
cheesy, sebaceous matter which collects between the glans penis and the
foreskin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smegmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Smeg*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being of the
nature of soap; soapy; cleansing; detersive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeir</ent><br/
<hw>Smeir</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A salt glaze on pottery,
made by adding common salt to an earthenware glaze.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smell</ent><br/
<hw>Smell</hw> <pr>(sm<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Smelled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, <conjf>Smelt</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>smellen</ets>,
<ets>smillen</ets>, <ets>smullen</ets>; cf. LG. <ets>smellen</ets>,
<ets>smelen</ets>, <ets>sm<oum/len</ets>, <ets>schmelen</ets>, to smoke,
to reek, D. <ets>smeulen</ets> to smolder, and E. <ets>smolder</ets>.
Cf. <er>Smell</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To perceive by
the olfactory nerves, or organs of smell; to have a sensation of,
excited through the nasal organs when affected by the appropriate
materials or qualities; to obtain the scent of; <as>as, to
<ex>smell</ex> a rose; to <ex>smell</ex> perfumes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To detect or perceive, as if by the sense of smell;
to scent out; -- often with <xex>out</xex>.</def> <ldquo/I
<xex>smell</xex> a device.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Can you <qex>smell</qex> him out by that?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To give heed to.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From that time forward I began to <qex>smell</qex>the Word of God,
and forsook the school doctors.</q> <rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To smell a rat</b></col>, <cd>to have a sense of
something wrong, not clearly evident; to have reason for suspicion.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <col><b>To smell out</b></col>, <cd>to find
out by sagacity.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smell</ent><br/
<hw>Smell</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To affect the
olfactory nerves; to have an odor or scent; -- often followed by
<xex>of</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>smell</ex> of smoke, or of
musk</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To have a particular tincture or smack of any
quality; to savor; <as>as, a report <ex>smells</ex> of
calumny</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Praises in an enemy are superfluous, or <qex>smell</qex> of
craft.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To exercise the sense of smell.</def> <rj><au>Ex.
xxx. 38.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To exercise sagacity.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smell</ent><br/
<hw>Smell</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>smel</ets>,
<ets>smil</ets>, <ets>smul</ets>, <ets>smeol</ets>. See <er>Smell</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The sense
or faculty by which certain qualities of bodies are perceived through
the instrumentally of the olfactory nerves. See
<er>Sense</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality of any thing or substance, or emanation
therefrom, which affects the olfactory organs; odor; scent; fragrance;
perfume; <as>as, the <ex>smell</ex> of mint</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Breathing the <qex>smell</qex> of field and grove.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That which, above all others, yields the sweetest <qex>smell</qex>
in the air, is the violent.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Scent; odor; perfume; fragrance.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeller</ent><br/
<hw>Smell"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
smells, or perceives by the sense of smell; one who gives out
smell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The nose.</def> <mark>[Pugilists' Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smell-feast</ent><br/
<hw>Smell"-feast`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who is apt to find and frequent good tables; a parasite; a
sponger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The epicure and the <qex>smell-feast</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A feast at which the guests are supposed to feed
upon the odors only of the viands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smelling</ent><br/
<hw>Smell"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who
smells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The sense by which odors are perceived; the sense of
smell.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smelling bottle</b></col>, <cd>a small bottle filled with
something suited to stimulate the sense of smell, or to remove
faintness, as spirits of ammonia.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smelling salts</ent><br/
<hw>Smell"ing salts</hw>. <def>An aromatic preparation of carbonate of
ammonia and, often, some scent, to avoid or relieve faintness, headache,
or the like.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smell-less</ent><br/
<hw>Smell"-less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of smell; having no
odor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Daisies <qex>smell-less</qex>, yet most quaint.</q> <rj><qau>Beau.
& Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smelt</ent><br/
<hw>Smelt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Smell</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smelt</ent><br/
<hw>Smelt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>smelt</ets>,
<ets>smylt</ets>; akin to Dan. <ets>smelt</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of small silvery
salmonoid fishes of the genus <gen>Osmerus</gen> and allied genera,
which ascend rivers to spawn, and sometimes become landlocked in lakes.
They are esteemed as food, and have a peculiar odor and taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The most important species are the European smelt
(<spn>Osmerus eperlans</spn>) (called also <altname>eperlan</altname>,
<altname>sparling</altname>, and <altname>spirling</altname>), the
Eastern American smelt (<spn>Osmerus mordax</spn>), the California smelt
(<spn>Osmerus thalichthys</spn>), and the surf smelt (<spn>Hypomesus
olidus</spn>). The name is loosely applied to various other small
fishes, as the lant, the California tomcod, the spawn eater, the
silversides.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>A gull; a simpleton.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sand smelt</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
silverside.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smelt</ent><br/
<hw>Smelt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smelted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smelting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of foreign origin; cf. Sw.
<ets>sm<aum/lta</ets>, D. <ets>smelten</ets>, Dan. <ets>smelte</ets>,
Icel. <ets>smelta</ets>, G. <ets>schmelzen</ets> OHG.
<ets>smelzan</ets>, <ets>smelzen</ets>; probably akin to Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/<?/. Cf. <er>Enamel</er>, <er>Melt</er>, <er>Mute</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>, <er>Smalt</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>To
melt or fuse, as, ore, for the purpose of separating and refining the
metal; hence, to reduce; to refine; to flux or scorify; <as>as, to
<ex>smelt</ex> tin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1359 --></p>

<p><ent>Smelter</ent><br/
<hw>Smelt"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, smelts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeltery</ent><br/
<hw>Smelt"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A house or place
for smelting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smeltie</ent><br/
<hw>Smelt"ie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fish, the
bib.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smelting</ent><br/
<hw>Smelt"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Smelt</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smelting furnace</b></col> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a
furnace in which ores are smelted or reduced.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smerk</ent><br/
<hw>Smerk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Smirk</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smerky</ent><br/
<ent>Smerk</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smerk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Smerk"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Smart; jaunty; spruce. See <er>Smirk</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So <qex>smerk</qex>, so smooth, his pricked ears.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smerlin</ent><br/
<hw>Smer"lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
small loach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smew</ent><br/
<hw>Smew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps for
<ets>ice-mew</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>small
European merganser (<spn>Mergus albellus</spn>) which has a white crest;
-- called also <altname>smee</altname>, <altname>smee duck</altname>,
<altname>white merganser</altname>, and <altname>white
nun</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The hooded merganser.</def>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smicker</ent><br/
<hw>Smick"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Akin to Sw.
<ets>smickra</ets> to flatter, Dan. <ets>smigre</ets>, and perhaps to G.
<ets>schmeicheln</ets>, and E. <ets>smile</ets>. Cf. <er>Smicker</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To look amorously or wantonly; to
smirk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smicker</ent><br/
<hw>Smick"er</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>smicere</ets> tasteful,
trim. See <er>Smicker</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>Amorous; wanton;
gay; spruce.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smickering</ent><br/
<hw>Smick"er*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Amorous glance or
inclination.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>smickering</xex> to
our young lady.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smicket</ent><br/
<hw>Smick"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>smock</ets>.]</ety> <def>A woman's under-garment; a smock.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smickly</ent><br/
<hw>Smick"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Smugly; finically.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smiddy</ent><br/
<hw>Smid"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Smithy</er>.]</ety> <def>A smithy.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smift</ent><br/
<hw>Smift</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A match for firing a
charge of powder, as in blasting; a fuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smight</ent><br/
<hw>Smight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To smite.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smilacin</ent><br/
<hw>Smil"a*cin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>similacine</ets>. See <er>Smilax</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Parrilin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smilax</ent><br/
<hw>Smi"lax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., bindweed, Gr.
<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A genus of
perennial climbing plants, usually with a prickly woody stem; green
brier, or cat brier. The rootstocks of certain species are the source of
the medicine called sarsaparilla.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A delicate
trailing plant (<spn>Myrsiphyllum asparagoides</spn>) much used for
decoration. It is a native of the Cape of Good Hope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smile</ent><br/
<hw>Smile</hw> <pr>(sm<imac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Smiled</conjf> <pr>(sm<imac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Smiling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>smilen</ets>; akin to Dan. <ets>smile</ets>, Sw. <ets>smila</ets>,
MHG. <ets>smielen</ets>, <ets>smieren</ets>, L. <ets>mirari</ets> to
wonder at, Skr. <ets>smi</ets> to smile; and probably to E.
<ets>smicker</ets>. <root/173. Cf. <er>Admire</er>, <er>Marvel</er>,
<er>Smirk</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To express amusement, pleasure,
moderate joy, or love and kindness, by the features of the face; to
laugh silently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He doth nothing but frown. . . . He hears merry tales and
<qex>smiles</qex> not.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She <qex>smiled</qex> to see the doughty hero slain.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When last I saw thy young blue eyes, they <qex>smiled</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To express slight contempt by a look implying
sarcasm or pity; to sneer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T was what I said to Craggs and Child,<br/
Who praised my modesty, and <qex>smiled</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To look gay and joyous; to have an appearance suited
to excite joy; <as>as, <ex>smiling</ex> spring; <ex>smiling</ex>
plenty.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The desert <qex>smiled</qex>,<br/
And paradise was opened in the wild.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be propitious or favorable; to favor; to
countenance; -- often with <xex>on</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>smile</ex> on
one's labors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smile</ent><br/
<hw>Smile</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To express by a smile;
<as>as, to <ex>smile</ex> consent; to <ex>smile</ex> a welcome to
visitors.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To affect in a certain way with a smile.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And sharply <qex>smile</qex> prevailing folly dead.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smile</ent><br/
<hw>Smile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. Dan. <ets>smiil</ets>, Sw.
<ets>smil</ets>. See <er>Smile</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of smiling; a peculiar change or brightening of the face,
which expresses pleasure, moderate joy, mirth, approbation, or kindness;
-- opposed to <contr>frown</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sweet intercourse<br/
Of looks and <qex>smiles</qex>: for <qex>smiles</qex> from reason
flow.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A somewhat similar expression of countenance,
indicative of satisfaction combined with malevolent feelings, as
contempt, scorn, etc; <as>as, a scornful <ex>smile</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Favor; countenance; propitiousness; <as>as, the
<ex>smiles</ex> of Providence</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>smile</xex> of
heaven.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Gay or joyous appearance; <as>as, the
<ex>smiles</ex> of spring</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The brightness of their [the flowers'] <qex>smile</qex> was
gone.</q> <rj><qau>Bryant.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smileless</ent><br/
<hw>Smile"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not having a
smile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smiler</ent><br/
<hw>Smil"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who smiles.</def>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smilet</ent><br/
<hw>Smil"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little smile.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those happy <qex>smilets</qex><br/
That played on her ripe lip.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smilingly</ent><br/
<hw>Smil"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a smiling manner.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smilingness</ent><br/
<hw>Smil"ing*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
smiling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And made despair a <qex>smilingness</qex> assume.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smilodon</ent><br/
<hw>Smi"lo*don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a
carving knife + <?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/, tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>An extinct genus of saber-toothed tigers. See
<er>Mach<?/rodus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smilt</ent><br/
<hw>Smilt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To melt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sminthurid</ent><br/
<hw>Smin*thu"rid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a
mouse + <?/<?/<?/ tail.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous small species of springtails, of the family
<fam>Sminthuridae</fam>, -- usually found on flowers. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Collembola</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smirch</ent><br/
<hw>Smirch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[From the root of
<ets>smear</ets>.]</ety> <def>To smear with something which stains, or
makes dirty; to smutch; to begrime; to soil; to sully.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll . . . with a kind of umber <qex>smirch</qex> my face.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smirch</ent><br/
<hw>Smirch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A smutch; a dirty
stain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smirk</ent><br/
<hw>Smirk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Smirked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smirking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>smirken</ets>, ASS.
<ets>smercian</ets>, <ets>smearcian</ets>; cf. MHG. <ets>smieren</ets>,
<ets>smielen</ets>, to smile. See <er>Smile</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To smile in an affected or conceited manner; to
smile with affected complaisance; to simper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smirk</ent><br/
<hw>Smirk</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A forced or affected smile; a
simper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bride, all <qex>smirk</qex> and blush, had just entered.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smirk</ent><br/
<hw>Smirk</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nice,; smart; spruce; affected;
simpering.</def> <ldquo/So <xex>smirk</xex>, so smooth.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smirkingly</ent><br/
<hw>Smirk"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With smirking; with a
smirk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smirky</ent><br/
<hw>Smirk"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Smirk;
smirking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smit</ent><br/
<hw>Smit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>rare</mark> <def><pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> of <er>Smite</er>.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Smit</qex> with the beauty of so fair a scene.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smit</ent><br/
<hw>Smit</hw>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>3d. pers. sing. pres.</pos>
of <er>Smite</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smite</ent><br/
<hw>Smite</hw> <pr>(sm<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Smote</conjf> <pr>(sm<omac/t)</pr>,
rarely <conjf>Smit</conjf> <pr>(sm<icr/t)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smitten</conjf> <pr>(sm<icr/t"t'n)</pr>, rarely
<conjf>Smit</conjf>, or <conjf>Smote</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smiting</conjf> <pr>(sm<imac/t"<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sm<imac/tan</ets> to smite, to soil, pollute; akin to OFries.
<ets>sm<imac/ta</ets> to smite, LG. <ets>smiten</ets>, D.
<ets>smijten</ets>, G. <ets>schmeissen</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sm<imac/zan</ets> to smear, stroke, OSw. & dial. Sw.
<ets>smita</ets> to smite, Dan. <ets>smide</ets> to throw, Goth.
bi<ets>smeitan</ets>, to anoint, besmear; cf. Skr. <ets>m<emac/d</ets>
to be fat. The original sense seems to have been, to daub on, to smear.
Cf. <er>Smut</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike; to inflict a blow
upon with the hand, or with any instrument held in the hand, or with a
missile thrown by the hand; <as>as, to <ex>smite</ex> with the fist,
with a rod, sword, spear, or stone</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whosoever shall <qex>smite</qex> thee on thy right cheek, turn to
him the other also.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. v. 39.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And David . . . took thence a stone, and slang it, and
<qex>smote</qex> the Philistine in his forehead.</q> <rj><qau>1 Sam.
xvii. 49.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to strike; to use as an instrument in
striking or hurling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Prophesy, and <qex>smite</qex> thine hands together.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. xxi. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Saul . . . <qex>smote</qex> the javelin into the wall.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Sam. xix. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To destroy the life of by beating, or by weapons of
any kind; to slay by a blow; to kill; <as>as, to <ex>smite</ex> one with
the sword, or with an arrow or other instrument</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To put to rout in battle; to overthrow by
war.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To blast; to destroy the life or vigor of, as by a
stroke or by some visitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The flax and the barly was <qex>smitten</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ex.
ix. 31.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To afflict; to chasten; to punish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let us not mistake God's goodness, nor imagine, because he
<qex>smites</qex> us, that we are forsaken by him.</q>
<rj><qau>Wake.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To strike or affect with passion, as love or
fear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The charms that <qex>smite</qex> the simple heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Smit</qex> with the love of sister arts we came.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To smite off</b></col>, <cd>to cut off.</cd> --
<col><b>To smite out</b></col>, <cd>to knock out, as a tooth.</cd>
<au>Exod. xxi. 27.</au> -- <col><b>To smite with the tongue</b></col>,
<cd>to reproach or upbraid; to revile.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jer. xviii. 18.</au></rj> </cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smite</ent><br/
<hw>Smite</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To strike; to collide; to
beat.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The heart melteth, and the knees smite together.</q> <rj><qau>Nah.
ii. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smite</ent><br/
<hw>Smite</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of smiting; a blow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smiter</ent><br/
<hw>Smit"er</hw> <pr>(sm<imac/t"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who smites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I give my back to the <qex>smiters</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Isa. l.
6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smith</ent><br/
<hw>Smith</hw> <pr>(sm<icr/th)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>smi<edh/</ets>; akin to D. <ets>smid</ets>, G. <ets>schmied</ets>,
OHG. <ets>smid</ets>, Icel. <ets>smi<edh/r</ets>, Dan. & Sw.
<ets>smed</ets>, Goth. <ets>smi<thorn/a</ets> (in comp.); cf. Gr.
<grk>smi`lh</grk> a sort of knife, <grk>sminy`h</grk> a hoe,
mattock.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who forges with the hammer; one who
works in metals; <as>as, a black<ex>smith</ex>, gold<ex>smith</ex>,
silver<ex>smith</ex>, and the like</as>.</def> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor yet the <qex>smith</qex> hath learned to form a sword.</q>
<rj><qau>Tate.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who makes or effects anything.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smith</ent><br/
<hw>Smith</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>smi<edh/ian</ets>. See
<er>Smith</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To beat into shape; to
forge.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What smith that any [weapon] <qex>smitheth</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smithcraft</ent><br/
<hw>Smith"craft`</hw> <pr>(-kr<adot/ft`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
art or occupation of a smith; smithing.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smither</ent><br/
<hw>Smith"er</hw> <pr>(sm<icr/<th/"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Light, fine rain.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Fragments; atoms; finders.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Smash the bottle to <qex>smithers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smithereens</ent><br/
<hw>Smith`er*eens"</hw> <pr>(sm<icr/<th/`<etil/r*<emac/nz")</pr>,
<pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Fragments; atoms; smithers.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>W. Black.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smithery</ent><br/
<hw>Smith"er*y</hw> <pr>(sm<icr/th"<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-ies</plw> <pr>(-<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The workshop of a smith; a smithy or stithy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Work done by a smith; smithing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The din of all his <qex>smithery</qex> may some time or other
possibly wake this noble duke.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smithing</ent><br/
<hw>Smith"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or art of working or
forging metals, as iron, into any desired shape.</def>
<rj><au>Moxon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smithsonian</ent><br/
<hw>Smith*so"ni*an</hw> <pr>(-s<omac/"n<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Englishman J. L. M.
<etsep>Smithson</etsep>, or to the national institution of learning
which he endowed at Washington, D. C.; <as>as, the <ex>Smithsonian</ex>
Institution; <ex>Smithsonian</ex> Reports</as>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The Smithsonian Institution.</def></def2><--
capitalized --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smithsonite</ent><br/
<hw>Smith"son*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Smithsonian</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Native zinc
carbonate. It generally occurs in stalactitic, reniform, or botryoidal
shapes, of a white to gray, green, or brown color. See Note under
<er>Calamine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smithy</ent><br/
<hw>Smith"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>smi<edh/<edh/e</ets>, fr. <ets>smi<edh/</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>smidse</ets>, <ets>smids</ets>, OHG. <ets>smitta</ets>, G.
<ets>schmiede</ets>, Icel. <ets>smi<edh/ja</ets>. See <er>Smith</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>The workshop of a smith, esp. a blacksmith; a
smithery; a stithy.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>smiddy</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Under a spreading chestnut tree<br/
The village <qex>smithy</qex> stands.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smitt</ent><br/
<hw>Smitt</hw> <pr>(sm<icr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF. G.
<ets>schmitz</ets> a stain, <ets>schmitzen</ets> besmear. See
<er>Smite</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Fine clay or ocher made up
into balls, used for marking sheep.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smitten</ent><br/
<hw>Smit"ten</hw> <pr>(sm<icr/t"t'n)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Smite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smittle</ent><br/
<hw>Smit"tle</hw> <pr>(-t'l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Freq. fr. OE.
<ets>smitten</ets> to befoul. See <er>Smite</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To infect.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smittle</ent><br/
<hw>Smit"tle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Infection.</def> <mark>[Pov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smittlish</ent><br/
<ent>Smittle</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smit"tle</hw> <pr>(sm<icr/t"t'l)</pr>, <hw>Smit"tlish</hw>
<pr>(-tl<icr/sh)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Infectious;
catching.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>H.
Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smock</ent><br/
<hw>Smock</hw> <pr>(sm<ocr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>smocc</ets>; akin to OHG. <ets>smocho</ets>, Icel.
<ets>smokkr</ets>, and from the root of AS. <ets>sm<umac/gan</ets> to
creep, akin to G. <ets>schmiegen</ets> to cling to, press close, MHG.
<ets>smiegen</ets>, Icel. <ets>smj<umac/ga</ets> to creep through, to
put on a garment which has a hole to put the head through; cf. Lith.
<ets>smukti</ets> to glide. Cf. <er>Smug</er>, <er>Smuggle</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A woman's under-garment; a shift; a chemise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In her <qex>smock</qex>, with head and foot all bare.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A blouse; a smoock frock.</def>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smock</ent><br/
<hw>Smock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
smock; resembling a smock; hence, of or pertaining to a woman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smock mill</b></col>, <cd>a windmill of which only the
cap turns round to meet the wind, in distinction from a <xex>post
mill</xex>, whose whole building turns on a post.</cd> -- <col><b>Smock
race</b></col>, <cd>a race run by women for the prize of a smock.</cd>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smock</ent><br/
<hw>Smock</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To provide with, or clothe in, a
smock or a smock frock.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smock-faced</ent><br/
<hw>Smock"-faced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
feminine countenance or complexion; smooth-faced; girlish.</def>
<rj><au>Fenton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smock frock</ent><br/
<hw>Smock" frock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A coarse frock, or shirt, worn
over the other dress, as by farm laborers.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smockless</ent><br/
<hw>Smock"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lacking a smock.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smokable</ent><br/
<hw>Smok"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
smoked; suitable or ready to be smoked; <as>as, <ex>smokable</ex>
tobacco</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoke</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke</hw> <pr>(sm<omac/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>smoca</ets>, fr. <ets>sme<oacute/can</ets> to smoke; akin to LG. &
D. <ets>smook</ets> smoke, Dan. <ets>sm<oum/g</ets>, G.
<ets>schmauch</ets>, and perh. to Gr. <?/<?/<?/ to burn in a smoldering
fire; cf. Lith. <ets>smaugti</ets> to choke.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
visible exhalation, vapor, or substance that escapes, or expelled, from
a burning body, especially from burning vegetable matter, as wood, coal,
peat, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The gases of hydrocarbons, raised to a red heat or
thereabouts, without a mixture of air enough to produce combustion,
disengage their carbon in a fine powder, forming <xex>smoke</xex>. The
disengaged carbon when deposited on solid bodies is
<xex>soot</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which resembles smoke; a vapor; a
mist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything unsubstantial, as idle talk.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act of smoking, esp. of smoking tobacco; <as>as,
to have a <ex>smoke</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Smoke</xex> is sometimes joined with other word.
forming self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>smoke</xex>-consuming,
<xex>smoke</xex>-dried, <xex>smoke</xex>-stained, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smoke arch</b></col>, <cd>the smoke box of a
locomotive.</cd> -- <col><b>Smoke ball</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>,
<cd>a ball or case containing a composition which, when it burns, sends
forth thick smoke.</cd> -- <col><b>Smoke black</b></col>,
<cd>lampblack.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>Smoke
board</b></col>, <cd>a board suspended before a fireplace to prevent the
smoke from coming out into the room.</cd> -- <col><b>Smoke
box</b></col>, <cd>a chamber in a boiler, where the smoke, etc., from
the furnace is collected before going out at the chimney.</cd> --
<col><b>Smoke sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a small sail in the
lee of the galley stovepipe, to prevent the smoke from annoying people
on deck.</cd> -- <col><b>Smoke tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
shrub (<spn>Rhus Cotinus</spn>) in which the flowers are mostly abortive
and the panicles transformed into tangles of plumose pedicels looking
like wreaths of smoke.</cd> -- <col><b>To end in smoke</b></col>, <cd>to
burned; hence, to be destroyed or ruined; figuratively, to come to
nothing.</cd><-- same as go up in smoke. --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Fume; reek; vapor.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoke</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smoked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb n.</pos>
<conjf>Smoking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>smocian</ets>; akin to
D. <ets>smoken</ets>, G. <ets>schmauchen</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sm<oum/ge</ets>. See <er>Smoke</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To emit smoke; to throw off volatile matter in the form
of vapor or exhalation; to reek.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hard by a cottage chimney <qex>smokes</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to burn; to be kindled; to rage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The anger of the Lord and his jealousy shall <qex>smoke</qex>
agains. that man.</q> <rj><qau>Deut. xxix. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To raise a dust or smoke by rapid motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Proud of his steeds, he <qex>smokes</qex> along the field.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To draw into the mouth the smoke of tobacco burning
in a pipe or in the form of a cigar, cigarette, etc.; to habitually use
tobacco in this manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To suffer severely; to be punished.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some of you shall <qex>smoke</qex> for it in Rome.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- To be smoking, (a) [Colloq] (Entertainment, sports) To perform in
an exciting manner. (b) (Gambling) To be winning in a long streak
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoke</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To apply smoke to; to
hang in smoke; to disinfect, to cure, etc., by smoke; <as>as, to
<ex>smoke</ex> or fumigate infected clothing; to <ex>smoke</ex> beef or
hams for preservation.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fill or scent with smoke; hence, to fill with
incense; to perfume.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Smoking</xex> the temple.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To smell out; to hunt out; to find out; to
detect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I alone<br/
<qex>Smoked</qex> his true person, talked with him.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was first <qex>smoked</qex> by the old Lord Lafeu.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Upon that . . . I began to <qex>smoke</qex> that they were a
parcel of mummers.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To ridicule to the face; to quiz.</def> <mark>[Old
Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To inhale and puff out the smoke of, as tobacco; to
burn or use in smoking; <as>as, to <ex>smoke</ex> a pipe or a
cigar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To subject to the operation of smoke, for the
purpose of annoying or driving out; -- often with <xex>out</xex>;
<as>as, to <ex>smoke</ex> a woodchuck out of his burrow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- also used metaphorically, to expose, to cause to be made public;
to drive out, as if by smoke. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoke ball</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke ball</hw>. <def>Same as <er>Puffball</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoke-dry</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke"-dry`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To dry by or in
smoke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smokehouse</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A building where
meat or fish is cured by subjecting it to a dense smoke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smokejack</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke"jack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A contrivance for
turning a spit by means of a fly or wheel moved by the current of
ascending air in a chimney.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smokeless</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Making or having no smoke.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Smokeless</xex> towers.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smokeless powder</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke"less pow"der</hw>. <def>A high-explosive gunpowder whose
explosion produces little, if any, smoke. It is usually based on
guncotton (nitrocellulose).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoke out</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke` out"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To drive from a
refuge or hiding place by causing dense smoke or other noxious fumes to
permeate the refuge; <as>as, the police <ex>smoked out</ex> the bank
robbers with tear gas</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <mark>[metaphorical]</mark>
<def>To expose; to force into public view; to reveal in its true light;
<as>as, the reporter <ex>smoked out</ex> the waffling candidate with a
direct question, exposing his view on the issue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoker</ent><br/
<hw>Smok"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
dries or preserves by smoke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who smokes tobacco or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A smoking car or compartment, such as a car on a
train where smoking is allowed.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A gathering for smoking and social
intercourse.</def> <mark>[Obsolescent.]</mark></p>

<p><q>That evening A Company had a <ldquo/<qex>smoker</qex><rdquo/ in
one of the disused huts of Shorncliffe Camp.</q> <rj><qau>Strand
Mag.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An amateurish pornographic movie.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></p>

<p><cs><col><b>black smoker</b></col>, <cd>a vent at the bottom of the
ocean, usually at a mid-ocean ridge, through which large quantities of
water carrying minerals flow, producing a jet of fluid with the
appearance of black smoke. The ocean water in crevices below the vent is
heated to temperatures near 400<deg/ C, and dissolves quantities of
metal salts, such as of copper, zinc, gold, and manganese. When the
saturated mineral solutions exit the vent, cooling by contact with the
ocean causes the metals to precipitate, mainly as sulfide or sulfate
salts. Unusual forms of life such as <er>tube worms</er> have been found
to live in the areas near <ex>black smokers</ex>. Additional information
is available from <a
href="https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/survival-at-hydrothermal-vents.html">The
Natural History Museum of London</a> and <a
href="https://web.archive.org/web/20000817092048/http://www.amnhonline.org/expeditions/blacksmokers/">The
American Museum of Natural History Expeditions</a>.</cd></cs></p>

<p><ent>Smokestack</ent><br/
<hw>Smoke"stack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A chimney; esp.,
a pipe serving as a chimney, as the pipe which carries off the smoke of
a locomotive, the funnel of a steam vessel, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1360 --></p>

<p><ent>Smokily</ent><br/
<hw>Smok"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a smoky
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smokiness</ent><br/
<hw>Smok"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
smoky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoking</ent><br/
<hw>Smok"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Smoke</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smoking bean</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the long
pod of the catalpa, or Indian-bean tree, often smoked by boys as a
substitute for cigars.</cd> -- <col><b>Smoking car</b></col>, <cd>a
railway car carriage reserved for the use of passengers who smoke
tobacco.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>smoking gun</ent><br/
<hw>smok"ing gun`</hw> <pr>(sm<omac/k"<icr/ng g<ucr/n`)</pr> ,
<pos>n.</pos> <mark>[metaphorical]</mark> <ety>[from imagined situations
where a person holding a pistol, still smoking from having been fired,
is found standing over a dead person]</ety> <def>Incontrovertible
evidence; convincing evidence.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoky</ent><br/
<hw>Smok"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Smokier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Smokiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Emitting smoke, esp.
in large quantities or in an offensive manner; fumid; <as>as,
<ex>smoky</ex> fires</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the appearance or nature of smoke; <as>as, a
<ex>smoky</ex> fog</as>.</def> <ldquo/Unlustrous as the <xex>smoky</xex>
light.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Filled with smoke, or with a vapor resembling smoke;
thick; <as>as, a <ex>smoky</ex> atmosphere</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Subject to be filled with smoke from chimneys or
fireplace; <as>as, a <ex>smoky</ex> house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Tarnished with smoke; noisome with smoke; <as>as,
<ex>smoky</ex> rafters; <ex>smoky</ex> cells.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Suspicious; open to suspicion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Foote.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smoky quartz</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a variety
of quartz crystal of a pale to dark smoky-brown color. See
<er>Quartz</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoulder</ent><br/
<ent>Smolder</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smol"der</hw>, <hw>Smoul"der</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smoldered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Smouldered</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Smoldering</conjf> or
<conjf>Smouldering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>smolderen</ets>;
cf. Prov. G. <ets>sm<oum/len</ets>, <ets>smelen</ets>, D.
<ets>smeulen</ets>. Cf. <er>Smell</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To burn
and smoke without flame; to waste away by a slow and supressed
combustion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>smoldering</qex> dust did round about him smoke.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exist in a state of suppressed or smothered
activity; to burn inwardly; <as>as, a <ex>smoldering</ex>
feud</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoulder</ent><br/
<ent>Smolder</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smol"der</hw>, <hw>Smoul"der</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To smother; to suffocate; to choke.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holinshed. Palsgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoulder</ent><br/
<ent>Smolder</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smol"der</hw>, <hw>Smoul"der</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Smoke; smother.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>smolder</qex> stops our nose with stench.</q>
<rj><qau>Gascoigne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smouldering</ent><br/
<ent>Smoldering</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smol"der*ing</hw>, <hw>Smoul"der*ing</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Being in a state of suppressed activity; quiet but
not dead.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some evil chance<br/
Will make the <qex>smoldering</qex> scandal break and blaze.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoulderingness</ent><br/
<ent>Smolderingness</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smol"der*ing*ness</hw>, <hw>Smoul"der*ing*ness</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of smoldering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smouldry</ent><br/
<ent>Smoldry</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Smol"dry</hw>, <hw>Smoul"dry</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Smoldering; suffocating; smothery.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A flaming fire ymixt with <qex>smoldry</qex> smoke.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smolt</ent><br/
<hw>Smolt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
young salmon two or three years old, when it has acquired its silvery
color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooch</ent><br/
<hw>Smooch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Smutch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. To kiss; to neck; to pet. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Smooch, n. a kiss. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoor</ent><br/
<hw>Smoor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>smorian</ets>; akin to D. & LG. <ets>smoren</ets>, G.
<ets>schmoren</ets> to stew. Cf. <er>Smother</er>.]</ety> <def>To
suffocate or smother.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>smore</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. More. Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooth</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth</hw> <pr>(sm<oomac/<th/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Smoother</adjf>
<pr>(sm<oomac/<th/"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Smoothest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>smothe</ets>,
<ets>smethe</ets>, AS. <ets>sm<emac/<edh/e</ets>,
<ets>sm<oe/<edh/e</ets>, where <emac/, <oe/, come from an older <omac/;
cf. LG. <ets>sm<oum/de</ets>, <ets>sm<oum/e</ets>,
<ets>sm<oum/dig</ets>; of uncertain origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having an even surface, or a surface so even that no roughness or
points can be perceived by the touch; not rough; <as>as, <ex>smooth</ex>
glass; <ex>smooth</ex> porcelain.</as></def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The outlines must be <qex>smooth</qex>, imperceptible to the
touch, and even, without eminence or cavities.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Evenly spread or arranged; sleek; <as>as,
<ex>smooth</ex> hair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Gently flowing; moving equably; not ruffled or
obstructed; <as>as, a <ex>smooth</ex> stream</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Flowing or uttered without check, obstruction, or
hesitation; not harsh; voluble; even; fluent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The only <qex>smooth</qex> poet of those times.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Waller was <qex>smooth</qex>; but Dryden taught to join<br/
The varying verse, the full-resounding line.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When sage Minerva rose,<br/
From her sweet lips <qex>smooth</qex> elocution flows.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Bland; mild; smoothing; fattering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>smooth</qex> discourse and mild behavior oft<br/
Conceal a traitor.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mech. & Physics)</fld> <def>Causing no resistance
to a body sliding along its surface; frictionless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Smooth</xex> is often used in the formation of
self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>smooth</xex>-bodied,
<xex>smooth</xex>-browed, <xex>smooth</xex>-combed,
<xex>smooth</xex>-faced, <xex>smooth</xex>-finished,
<xex>smooth</xex>-gliding, <xex>smooth</xex>-grained,
<xex>smooth</xex>-leaved, <xex>smooth</xex>-sliding,
<xex>smooth</xex>-speaking, <xex>smooth</xex>-woven, and the
like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Even; plain; level; flat; polished; glossy;
sleek; soft; bland; mild; soothing; voluble; flattering; adulatory;
deceptive.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooth</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Smoothly.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Smooth</qex> runs the water where the brook is deep.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooth</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of making
smooth; a stroke which smooths.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is smooth; the smooth part of
anything.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>smooth</xex> of his neck.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gen. xxvii. 16.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooth</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smoothed</conjf> <pr>(sm<oomac/thd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Smoothing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>smothen</ets>, <ets>smethen</ets>, AS. <ets>sm<emac/<edh/ian</ets>;
cf. LG. <ets>sm<oum/den</ets>. See <er>Smooth</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To make smooth; to make even on the surface by any means; <as>as,
to <ex>smooth</ex> a board with a plane; to <ex>smooth</ex> cloth with
an iron.</as></def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>To free from obstruction; to make easy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou, Abelard! the last sad office pay,<br/
And <qex>smooth</qex> my passage to the realms of day.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To free from harshness; to make flowing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In their motions harmony divine<br/
So <qex>smooths</qex> her charming tones that God's own ear<br/
Listens delighted.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To palliate; to gloze; <as>as, to <ex>smooth</ex>
over a fault</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>To give a smooth or calm appearance to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each perturbation <qex>smoothed</qex> with outward calm.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <def>To ease; to regulate.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooth</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To flatter; to use
blandishment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Because I can not flatter and speak fair,<br/
Smile in men's faces, <qex>smooth</qex>, deceive and cog.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoothbore</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"bore`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Gun.)</fld>
<def>Having a bore of perfectly smooth surface; -- distinguished from
<contr>rifled</contr>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A smoothbore
firearm.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooth-chinned</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"-chinned`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
smooth chin; beardless.</def> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoothen</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make
smooth.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoother</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, smooths.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoothing</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"ing</hw>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>fr. <er>Smooth</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smoothing iron</b></col>, <cd>an iron instrument with a
polished face, for smoothing clothes; a sadiron; a flatiron.</cd> --
<col><b>Smoothing plane</b></col>, <cd>a short, finely set plane, for
smoothing and finishing work.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoothly</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a smooth manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoothness</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
smooth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooth-spoken</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"-spo`ken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Speaking
smoothly; plausible; flattering; smooth-tongued.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smooth-tongued</ent><br/
<hw>Smooth"-tongued`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
smooth tongue; plausible; flattering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smore</ent><br/
<hw>Smore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To smother. See
<er>Smoor</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some dying vomit blood, and some were <qex>smored</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Du Bartas.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smorsato</ent><br/
<ent>Smorzando</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Smor*zan"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Smor*sa"to</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>Growing gradually fainter and softer; dying away;
morendo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smote</ent><br/
<hw>Smote</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. (<and/ rare p. p.)</pos> of
<er>Smite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoterlich</ent><br/
<hw>Smo"ter*lich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Smut</er>.]</ety> <def>Dirty; foul.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smother</ent><br/
<hw>Smoth"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Smothered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Smothering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>smotheren</ets>; akin to E. <ets>smoor</ets>. See
<er>Smoor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To destroy the life of by
suffocation; to deprive of the air necessary for life; to cover up
closely so as to prevent breathing; to suffocate; <as>as, to
<ex>smother</ex> a child</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To affect as by suffocation; to stife; to deprive of
air by a thick covering, as of ashes, of smoke, or the like; <as>as, to
<ex>smother</ex> a fire</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, to repress the action of; to cover from
public view; to suppress; to conceal; <as>as, to <ex>smother</ex> one's
displeasure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smother</ent><br/
<hw>Smoth"er</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be suffocated or
stifled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To burn slowly, without sufficient air; to
smolder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smother</ent><br/
<hw>Smoth"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>smorther</ets>. See
<er>Smother</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Stifling
smoke; thick dust.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A state of suppression.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not to keep their suspicions in <qex>smother</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which smothers or causes a sensation of
smothering, as smoke, fog, the foam of the sea, a confused multitude of
things.</def></p>

<p><q>Then they vanished, swallowed up in the grayness of the evening
and the smoke and <qex>smother</qex> of the storm.</q> <rj><qau>The
Century.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smother fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an
aphid.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smothered mate</ent><br/
<hw>Smoth"ered mate</hw>. <fld>(Chess)</fld> <def>Checkmate given when
movement of the king is completely obstructed by his own men. This can
only occur when an opponent moves a knight into a position to give a
check to the king.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smotheriness</ent><br/
<hw>Smoth"er*i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being smothery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smotheringly</ent><br/
<hw>Smoth"er*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a smothering
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smothery</ent><br/
<hw>Smoth"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to smother;
stifling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smouch</ent><br/
<hw>Smouch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Akin to
<ets>smack</ets>.]</ety> <def>To kiss closely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>P. Stubbes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smouch</ent><br/
<hw>Smouch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Smutch</er>.]</ety>
<def>To smutch; to soil; <as>as, to <ex>smouch</ex> the
face</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smouch</ent><br/
<hw>Smouch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dark soil or stain; a
smutch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smoulder</ent><br/
<hw>Smoul"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Smolder</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smouldry</ent><br/
<hw>Smoul"dry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Smoldry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smudge</ent><br/
<hw>Smudge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>smuds</ets> smut, E. <ets>smutch</ets>, or <ets>smoke</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A suffocating smoke.</def> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A heap of damp combustibles partially ignited and
burning slowly, placed on the windward side of a house, tent, or the
like, in order, by the thick smoke, to keep off mosquitoes or other
insects.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is smeared upon anything; a stain; a
blot; a smutch; a smear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- smudge pot, a device containing combustibles which burns smokily,
used by farmers to inhibit damage to crops caused by frost. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smudge</ent><br/
<hw>Smudge</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smudged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smudging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To stifle or smother
with smoke; to smoke by means of a smudge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To smear; to smutch; to soil; to blacken with
smoke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smudginess</ent><br/
<hw>Smudg"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being smudged, soiled, or blurred.</def> <rj><au>C. A.
Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smug</ent><br/
<hw>Smug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Of. Scand. or Low German
origin; cf. LG. <ets>smuck</ets>, G. <ets>schmuck</ets>, Dan.
<ets>smuk</ets>, OSw. <ets>smuck</ets>, <ets>sm<oum/ck</ets>, and E.
<ets>smock</ets>, <ets>smuggle</ets>; cf. G. <ets>schmuck</ets>
ornament. See <er>Smock</er>.]</ety> <def>Studiously neat or nice,
especially in dress; spruce; affectedly precise; smooth and
prim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They be so <qex>smug</qex> and smooth.</q> <rj><qau>Robynson
(More's Utopia).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>smug</qex> and scanty draperies of his style.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A young, <qex>smug</qex>, handsome holiness has no fellow.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smug</ent><br/
<hw>Smug</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smugged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smugging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make smug, or spruce.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus said, he <qex>smugged</qex> his beard, and stroked up
fair.</q> <rj><qau>Dryton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smuggle</ent><br/
<hw>Smug"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Smuggled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Smuggling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of Low
German or Scand. origin; cf. LG. <ets>smuggeln</ets>, D.
<ets>smokkelen</ets>, G. <ets>schmuggeln</ets>, Dan. <ets>smugle</ets>,
Sw. <ets>smyga</ets> to introduce or convey secretly, Dan. <ets>i
smug</ets> secretly, D. <ets>smuigen</ets> to eat in secret, AS.
<ets>sm<?/gan</ets> to creep. See <er>Smock</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To import or export secretly, contrary to the law; to import or
export without paying the duties imposed by law; <as>as, to
<ex>smuggle</ex> lace</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To convey or introduce
clandestinely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smuggle</ent><br/
<hw>Smug"gle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To import or export in
violation of the customs laws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smuggler</ent><br/
<hw>Smug"gler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
smuggles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A vessel employed in smuggling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smugly</ent><br/
<hw>Smug"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a smug manner.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Gay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smugness</ent><br/
<hw>Smug"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
smug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smut</ent><br/
<hw>Smut</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to Sw.
<ets>smuts</ets>, Dan. <ets>smuds</ets>, MHG. <ets>smuz</ets>, G.
<ets>schmutz</ets>, D. <ets>smet</ets> a spot or stain,
<ets>smoddig</ets>, <ets>smodsig</ets>, <ets>smodderig</ets>, dirty,
<ets>smodderen</ets> to smut; and probably to E. <ets>smite</ets>. See
<er>Smite</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Smitt</er>,
<er>Smutch</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Foul matter, like soot or coal
dust; also, a spot or soil made by such matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>Bad, soft coal, containing much
earthy matter, found in the immediate locality of faults.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An infection of cereal grains
producing a swelling which is at length resolved into a powdery sooty
mass. It is caused by parasitic fungi of the genus <gen>Ustilago</gen>.
<spn>Ustilago segetum</spn>, or <spn>Ustilago Carbo</spn>, is the
commonest kind; that of Indian corn is <spn>Ustilago
maydis</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Obscene language; ribaldry; obscenity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He does not stand upon decency . . . but will talk
<qex>smut</qex>, <qex>though a priest and his mother be in the
room</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Smut mill</b></col>, <cd>a machine for cleansing grain
from smut.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smut</ent><br/
<hw>Smut</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Smutted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smutting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To stain or mark
with smut; to blacken with coal, soot, or other dirty
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To taint with mildew, as grain.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To blacken; to sully or taint; to tarnish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To clear of smut; <as>as, to <ex>smut</ex> grain for
the mill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smut</ent><br/
<hw>Smut</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To gather smut; to be
converted into smut; to become smutted.</def>
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give off smut; to crock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smutch</ent><br/
<hw>Smutch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prob. for
<ets>smuts</ets>. See <er>Smut</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A stain;
a dirty spot.</def> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smutch</ent><br/
<hw>Smutch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Smutched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Smutching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To blacken with smoke, soot, or
coal.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>smooch</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smutchin</ent><br/
<hw>Smutch"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Snuff.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smutty</ent><br/
<hw>Smut"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Smuttier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Smuttiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Soiled with smut;
smutted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tainted with mildew; <as>as, <ex>smutty</ex>
corn</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Obscene; not modest or pure; <as>as, a
<ex>smutty</ex> saying</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>smutty</qex> joke, ridiculously lewd.</q>
<rj><qau>Smollett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Smut"ti*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Smut"ti*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Smyrniot</ent><br/
<hw>Smyr"ni*ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Smyrna.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Smyrna.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snack</ent><br/
<hw>Snack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Snatch</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A share; a part or portion; --
obsolete, except in the colloquial phrase, <cref>to go snacks</cref>, i.
e., to share.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At last he whispers, <ldquo/Do, and we <qex>go
snacks</qex>.<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A slight, hasty repast.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snacket</ent><br/
<hw>Snack"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Snecket</er>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snacot</ent><br/
<hw>Snac"ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Said to be corrupted
fr. NL. <ets>syngnathus</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> together +
<grk>gna`qos</grk> jaw, because the jaws can be only slightly
separated.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A pipefish of the genus
<gen>Syngnathus</gen>. See <er>Pipefish</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snaffle</ent><br/
<hw>Snaf"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>snavel</ets>
a beak, bill, snout; akin to G. <ets>schnabel</ets>, OHG.
<ets>snabul</ets>,. <ets>sneb</ets>, <ets>snebbe</ets>, OFries.
<ets>snavel</ets> mouth, Dan. & Sw. <ets>snabel</ets> beak, bill, Lith.
<ets>snapas</ets>, and to E. <ets>snap</ets>, v. See <er>Snap</er>, and
cf. <er>Neb</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of bridle bit, having a joint in
the part to be placed in the mouth, and rings and cheek pieces at the
ends, but having no curb; -- called also <altname>snaffle
bit</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snaffle</ent><br/
<hw>Snaf"fle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snaffled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snaffling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To put a snaffle
in the mouth of; to subject to the snaffle; to bridle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snag</ent><br/
<hw>Snag</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E., n., a lump on
a tree where a branch has been cut off; v., to cut off the twigs and
small branches from a tree, of Celtic origin; cf. Gael.
<ets>snaigh</ets>, <ets>snaidh</ets>, to cut down, to prune, to sharpen,
p. p. <ets>snaighte</ets>, <ets>snaidhte</ets>, cut off, lopped, Ir.
<ets>snaigh</ets> a hewing, cutting.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A stump or
base of a branch that has been lopped off; a short branch, or a sharp or
rough branch; a knot; a protuberance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The coat of arms<br/
Now on a naked <qex>snag</qex> in triumph borne.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tooth projecting beyond the rest; contemptuously,
a broken or decayed tooth.</def> <rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A tree, or a branch of a tree, fixed in the bottom
of a river or other navigable water, and rising nearly or quite to the
surface, by which boats are sometimes pierced and sunk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the secondary branches of
an antler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 5. Any sharp protuberant part of an object, which may catch,
scratch, or tear other objects brought into contact with it. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snag boat</b></col>, <cd>a steamboat fitted with
apparatus for removing snags and other obstructions in navigable
streams.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Snag tooth</b></col>.
<cd>Same as <er>Snag</er>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How thy <qex>snag teeth</qex> stand orderly,<br/
Like stakes which strut by the water side.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Cotgrave.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snag</ent><br/
<hw>Snag</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snagged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snagging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut
the snags or branches from, as the stem of a tree; to hew roughly.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To injure or destroy, as a steamboat or other
vessel, by a snag, or projecting part of a sunken tree.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 3. To catch on a snag{5}. 4. (Fig.) To obtain by a quick action,
as though by snagging{3} something passing by; -- often used of an
opportunistic or fortunate action. --> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snagged</ent><br/
<hw>Snag"ged</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of snags;
snaggy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snaggy</ent><br/
<hw>Snag"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of
snags; full of short, rough branches or sharp points; abounding with
knots.</def> <ldquo/Upon a <xex>snaggy</xex> oak.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Snappish; cross; ill-tempered.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snail</ent><br/
<hw>Snail</hw> <pr>(sn<amac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>snaile</ets>, AS. <ets>sn<ae/gel</ets>, <ets>snegel</ets>,
<ets>sn<ae/gl</ets>; akin to G. <ets>schnecke</ets>, OHG.
<ets>snecko</ets>, Dan. <ets>snegl</ets>, Icel.
<ets>snigill</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Any one of numerous species of terrestrial air-breathing gastropods
belonging to the genus Helix and many allied genera of the family
<fam>Helicidae</fam>. They are abundant in nearly all parts of the world
except the arctic regions, and feed almost entirely on vegetation; a
land snail.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any gastropod having a general
resemblance to the true snails, including fresh-water and marine
species. See <cref>Pond snail</cref>, under <er>Pond</er>, and <er>Sea
snail</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a drone; a slow-moving person or
thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A spiral cam, or a flat piece of
metal of spirally curved outline, used for giving motion to, or changing
the position of, another part, as the hammer tail of a striking
clock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A tortoise; in ancient warfare, a movable roof or
shed to protect besiegers; a testudo.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They had also all manner of gynes [engines] . . . that needful is
[in] taking or sieging of castle or of city, as <qex>snails</qex>, that
was naught else but hollow pavises and targets, under the which men,
when they fought, were heled [protected], . . . as the <qex>snail</qex>
is in his house; therefore they cleped them <qex>snails</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Vegetius (Trans.).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The pod of the sanil
clover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Ear snail</b></col>, <col><b>Edible
snail</b></col>, <col><b>Pond snail</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under
<er>Ear</er>, <er>Edible</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Snail
borer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a boring univalve mollusk; a
drill.</cd> -- <col><b>Snail clover</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
cloverlike plant (<spn>Medicago scuttellata</spn>, also, <spn>Medicago
Helix</spn>); -- so named from its pods, which resemble the shells of
snails; -- called also <altname>snail trefoil</altname>, <altname>snail
medic</altname>, and <altname>beehive</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Snail
flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a leguminous plant
(<spn>Phaseolus Caracalla</spn>) having the keel of the carolla spirally
coiled like a snail shell.</cd> -- <col><b>Snail shell</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the shell of snail.</cd> -- <col><b>Snail
trefoil</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Snail clover</cref>,
above.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snailfish</ent><br/
<hw>Snail"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sea snail</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1361 --></p>

<p><ent>Snail-like</ent><br/
<hw>Snail"-like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like or suiting a
snail; <as>as, <ex>snail-like</ex> progress</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snail-like</ent><br/
<hw>Snail"-like`</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the manner of a snail;
slowly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snail-paced</ent><br/
<hw>Snail"-paced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slow-moving,
like a snail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bid the <qex>snail-paced</qex> Ajax arm for shame.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>'Snails</ent><br/
<hw>'Snails</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>God's nails, or
His nails, that is, the nails with which the Savior was fastened to the
cross; -- an ancient form of oath, corresponding to '<xex>Od's
bodikins</xex> (dim. of <xex>body</xex>, i.e., God's dear body).</def>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snake</ent><br/
<hw>Snake</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>snaca</ets>;
akin to LG. <ets>snake</ets>, <ets>schnake</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sn<amac/kr</ets>, <ets>sn<?/kr</ets>, Dan. <ets>snog</ets>, Sw.
<ets>snok</ets>; of uncertain origin.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
species of the order Ophidia; an ophidian; a serpent, whether harmless
or venomous. See <er>Ophidia</er>, and <er>Serpent</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Snakes are abundant in all warm countries, and much the
larger number are harmless to man.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Blind snake</b></col>, <col><b>Garter
snake</b></col>, <col><b>Green snake</b></col>, <col><b>King
snake</b></col>, <col><b>Milk snake</b></col>, <col><b>Rock
snake</b></col>, <col><b>Water snake</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See
under <er>Blind</er>, <er>Garter</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Fetich
snake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large African snake
(<spn>Python Sebae</spn>) used by the natives as a fetich.</cd> --
<col><b>Ringed snake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a common European
columbrine snake (<spn>Tropidonotus natrix</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Snake
eater</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The markhoor</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The secretary bird.</cd> -- <col><b>Snake
fence</b></col>, <cd>a worm fence (which see).</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark>
-- <col><b>Snake fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
several species of neuropterous insects of the genus
<gen>Rhaphidia</gen>; -- so called because of their large head and
elongated neck and prothorax.</cd> -- <col><b>Snake gourd</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a cucurbitaceous plant (<spn>Trichosanthes
anguina</spn>) having the fruit shorter and less snakelike than that of
the serpent cucumber.</cd> -- <col><b>Snake killer</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The secretary bird</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The chaparral cock.</cd> -- <col><b>Snake
moss</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the common club moss
(<spn>Lycopodium clavatum</spn>). See <er>Lycopodium</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Snake nut</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the fruit of a
sapindaceous tree (<spn>Ophiocaryon paradoxum</spn>) of Guiana, the
embryo of which resembles a snake coiled up.</cd> -- <col><b>Tree
snake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of
colubrine snakes which habitually live in trees, especially those of the
genus <gen>Dendrophis</gen> and allied genera.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snake</ent><br/
<hw>Snake</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snaked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To drag or draw, as a
snake from a hole; -- often with <xex>out</xex>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To wind round spirally, as a
large rope with a smaller, or with cord, the small rope lying in the
spaces between the strands of the large one; to worm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snake</ent><br/
<hw>Snake</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To crawl like a snake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakebird</ent><br/
<hw>Snake"bird`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named from its
snakelike neck.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any one of
four species of aquatic birds of the genus <gen>Anhinga</gen> or
<gen>Plotus</gen>. They are allied to the gannets and cormorants, but
have very long, slender, flexible necks, and sharp bills.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The American species (<spn>Anhinga anhinga</spn> syn.
<spn>Plotus anhinga</spn>) inhabits the <geog>Southern United
States</geog> and <geog>tropical America</geog>; -- called also
<altname>darter</altname>, and <altname>water turkey</altname>. The
Asiatic species (<spn>Anhinga melanogaster</spn>) is native of
<geog>Southern Asia</geog> and the <geog>East Indies</geog>. Two other
species inhabit <geog>Africa</geog> and <country>Australia</country>
respectively.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The wryneck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakefish</ent><br/
<hw>Snake"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The band fish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The lizard
fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakehead</ent><br/
<hw>Snake"head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
loose, bent-up end of one of the strap rails, or flat rails, formerly
used on American railroads. It was sometimes so bent by the passage of a
train as to slip over a wheel and pierce the bottom of a car.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The turtlehead.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The Guinea-hen flower. See <er>Snake's-head</er>, and
under <er>Guinea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakeneck</ent><br/
<hw>Snake"neck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The snakebird, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakeroot</ent><br/
<hw>Snake"root`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any one of several plants of different genera and species, most of
which are (or were formerly) reputed to be efficacious as remedies for
the bites of serpents; also, the roots of any of these.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The Virginia snakeroot is <spn>Aristolochia
Serpentaria</spn>; black snakeroot is <gen>Sanicula</gen>, esp.
<spn>Sanicula Marilandica</spn>, also <spn>Cimicifuga racemosa</spn>;
Seneca snakeroot is <spn>Polygala Senega</spn>; button snakeroot is
<gen>Liatris</gen>, also <gen>Eryngium</gen>; white snakeroot is
<spn>Eupatorium ageratoides</spn>. The name is also applied to some
others besides these.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snake's-head</ent><br/
<hw>Snake's"-head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The Guinea-hen flower; -- so called in England because its spotted
petals resemble the scales of a snake's head.</def> <rj><au>Dr.
Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snake's-head iris</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an
iridaceous plant (<spn>Hermodactylus tuberosus</spn>) of the
Mediterranean region. The flowers slightly resemble a serpent's open
mouth.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakestone</ent><br/
<hw>Snake"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
kind of hone slate or whetstone obtained in Scotland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An ammonite; -- so called from
its form, which resembles that of a coiled snake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snake's-tongue</ent><br/
<hw>Snake's-tongue`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Adder's-tongue</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakeweed</ent><br/
<hw>Snake"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A kind of knotweed (<spn>Polygonum
Bistorta</spn>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The Virginia snakeroot. See
<er>Snakeroot</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakewood</ent><br/
<hw>Snake"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>An East Indian climbing plant (<spn>Strychnos
colubrina</spn>) having a bitter taste, and supposed to be a remedy for
the bite of the hooded serpent.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An East Indian
climbing shrub (<spn>Ophioxylon serpentinum</spn>) which has the roots
and stems twisted so as to resemble serpents.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>Same as <er>Trumpetwood</er>.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>A tropical
American shrub (<spn>Plumieria rubra</spn>) which has very fragrant red
blossoms.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>Same as <er>Letterwood</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snakish</ent><br/
<hw>Snak"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the qualities
or characteristics of a snake; snaky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snaky</ent><br/
<hw>Snak"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a snake or snakes; resembling a snake; serpentine;
winding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The red light playing upon its gilt and carving gave it an
appearance of <qex>snaky</qex> life.</q> <rj><qau>L.
Wallace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sly; cunning; insinuating; deceitful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So to the coast of Jordan he directs<br/
His easy steps, girded with <qex>snaky</qex> wiles.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Covered with serpents; having serpents; <as>as, a
<ex>snaky</ex> rod or wand</as>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>snaky</qex>-headed, Gorgon shield.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snap</ent><br/
<hw>Snap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snapped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snapping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[LG. or D. <ets>snappen</ets> to
snap up, to snatch; akin to G. <ets>schnappen</ets>, MHG.
<ets>snaben</ets>, Dan. <ets>snappe</ets>, and to D. <ets>snavel</ets>
beak, bill. Cf. <er>Neb</er>, <er>Snaffle</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To break at once; to break short, as substances that
are brittle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Breaks the doors open, <qex>snaps</qex> the locks.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strike, to hit, or to shut, with a sharp
sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To bite or seize suddenly, especially with the
teeth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He, by playing too often at the mouth of death, has been
<qex>snapped</qex> by it at last.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To break upon suddenly with sharp, angry words; to
treat snappishly; -- usually with <xex>up</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Granville.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To crack; to cause to make a sharp, cracking noise;
<as>as, to <ex>snap</ex> a whip</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>MacMorian <qex>snapped</qex> his fingers repeatedly.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To project with a snap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>To catch out sharply (a batsman
who has just snicked a bowled ball).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To snap back</b></col> <fld>(Football)</fld>, <cd>to roll
the ball back with the foot; -- done only by the center rush, who thus
delivers the ball to the quarter back on his own side when both sides
are ranged in line.</cd><-- now passed back with the hands, but still
called "snap" --> -- <col><b>To snap off</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To
break suddenly</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To bite off
suddenly.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snap</ent><br/
<hw>Snap</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To break short, or at
once; to part asunder suddenly; <as>as, a mast <ex>snaps</ex>; a needle
<ex>snaps</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But this weapon will <qex>snap</qex> short, unfaithful to the hand
that employs it.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give forth, or produce, a sharp, cracking noise;
to crack; <as>as, blazing firewood <ex>snaps</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make an effort to bite; to aim to seize with the
teeth; to catch eagerly (at anything); -- often with <xex>at</xex>;
<as>as, a dog <ex>snaps</ex>at a passenger; a fish <ex>snaps</ex> at the
bait.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To utter sharp, harsh, angry words; -- often with
<xex>at</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>snap</ex> at a child</as>.</def><-- usu.
impulsively and as a quick reaction to some perceived provocation
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To miss fire; <as>as, the gun
<ex>snapped</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Of the eyes, to emit sudden, brief sparkles like
those of a snapping fire, as sometimes in anger.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snap</ent><br/
<hw>Snap</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>snap</ets> a snatching.
See <er>Snap</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sudden
breaking or rupture of any substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sudden, eager bite; a sudden seizing, or effort to
seize, as with the teeth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sudden, sharp motion or blow, as with the finger
sprung from the thumb, or the thumb from the finger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sharp, abrupt sound, as that made by the crack of
a whip; <as>as, the <ex>snap</ex> of the trigger of a
gun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A greedy fellow.</def>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>That which is, or may be, snapped up; something
bitten off, seized, or obtained by a single quick movement; hence, a
bite, morsel, or fragment; a scrap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He's a nimble fellow,<br/
And alike skilled in every liberal science,<br/
As having certain <qex>snaps</qex> of all.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A sudden severe interval or spell; -- applied to the
weather; <as>as, a cold <ex>snap</ex></as>.</def> <-- but not a heat
snap --> <rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A small catch or fastening held or closed by means
of a spring, or one which closes with a snapping sound, as the catch of
a bracelet, necklace, clasp of a book, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A snap beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A thin, crisp cake, usually small, and flavored
with ginger; -- used chiefly in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Briskness; vigor; energy; decision.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>Any circumstance out of which money may be made or
an advantage gained.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>Any task, labor, set of circumstances, or the like,
that yields satisfactory results or gives pleasure with little trouble
or effort, as an easy course of study, a job where work is light, a
bargain, etc.</def> <mark>[Slang, Chiefly U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>A snap shot with a firearm.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>A snapshot.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <def>Something of no value; <as>as, not worth a
<ex>snap</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>17.</sn> <fld>(Football)</fld> <def>The action of snapping the
ball back, from the center usu. to the quarterback, which commences the
play (down), and, if the clock had stopped, restarts the timer clock; a
<cref>snap back</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snap back</b></col> <fld>(Football)</fld>, <cd>the act of
snapping back the ball.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Snap beetle</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Snap bug</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any beetle of the family <fam>Elateridae</fam>, which, when laid on
its back, is able to leap to a considerable height by means of a
thoracic spring; -- called also <cref>snapping beetle</cref>.</cd> --
<col><b>Snap flask</b></col> <fld>(Molding)</fld>, <cd>a flask for small
work, having its sides separable and held together by latches, so that
the flask may be removed from around the sand mold.</cd> -- <col><b>Snap
judgment</b></col>, <cd>a judgment formed on the instant without
deliberation.</cd> -- <col><b>Snap lock</b></col>, <cd>a lock shutting
with a catch or snap.</cd> -- <col><b>Snap riveting</b></col>,
<cd>riveting in which the rivets have snapheads formed by a die or
swaging tool.</cd> -- <col><b>Snap shot</b></col>, <cd>a quick offhand
shot, without deliberately taking aim.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snap</ent><br/
<hw>Snap</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Done, performed, made, executed,
carried through, or the like, quickly and without deliberation; <as>as,
a <ex>snap</ex> judgment or decision; a <ex>snap</ex> political
convention</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snapdragon</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"drag`on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any plant of the scrrophulariaceous
genus <gen>Antirrhinum</gen>, especially the cultivated <spn>Antirrhinum
majus</spn>, whose showy flowers are fancifully likened to the face of a
dragon.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A West Indian herb (<spn>Ruellia
tuberosa</spn>) with curiously shaped blue flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A play in which raisins are snatched from a vessel
containing burning brandy, and eaten; also, that which is so eaten. See
<er>Flapdragon</er>.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snape</ent><br/
<hw>Snape</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld>
<def>To bevel the end of a timber to fit against an inclined
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snaphance</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"hance`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>snaphaan</ets> a gun, originally, the snapping cock of a gun. See
<er>Snap</er>, and <er>Hen</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A spring lock
for discharging a firearm; also, the firearm to which it is
attached.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A trifling or second-rate thing or person.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snaphead</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hemispherical or
rounded head to a rivet or bolt; also, a swaging tool with a cavity in
its face for forming such a rounded head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snapper</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, snaps; <as>as, a <ex>snapper</ex> up of trifles; the
<ex>snapper</ex> of a whip.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
large sparoid food fishes of the genus <gen>Lutjanus</gen>, abundant on
the southern coasts of the United States and on both coasts of tropical
America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The red snapper (<spn>Lutjanus aya</spn> syn.
<spn>Lutjanus Blackfordi</spn>) and the gray, or mangrove, snapper
(<spn>Lutjanus griseus</spn>) are large and abundant species. The name
is loosely applied to various other fishes, as the bluefish, the
rosefish, the red grouper, etc. See <er>Rosefish</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A snapping turtle; <as>as, the
alligator <ex>snapper</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The green woodpecker, or
yaffle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A snap beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Teleg.)</fld> <def>A device with a flexible metal
tongue for producing clicks like those of the sounder.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A string bean.</def> <mark>[Colloq., U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snapping</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Snap</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snapping beetle</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Snap beetle</cref>, under <er>Snap</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Snapping
turtle</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A large and
voracious aquatic turtle (<spn>Chelydra serpentina</spn>) common in the
fresh waters of the United States; -- so called from its habit of
seizing its prey by a snap of its jaws. Called also <altname>mud
turtle</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>See <cref>Alligator
snapper</cref>, under <er>Alligator</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snappish</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"pish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Apt to
snap at persons or things; eager to bite; <as>as, a <ex>snapping</ex>
cur</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sharp in reply; apt to speak angrily or testily;
easily provoked; tart; peevish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The taunting address of a <qex>snappish</qex> misanthrope.</q>
<rj><qau>Jeffrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Snap"pish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Snap"pish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snappy</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Snappish.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snapsack</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"sack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sw.
<ets>snapps<aum/ck</ets>, G. <ets>schnappsack</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
knapsack.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snap shot</ent><br/
<ent>Snapshot</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"shot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<altsp>Commonly <hw>Snap shot</hw>.</altsp> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A quick
offhand shot, made without deliberately taking aim over the
sights.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>Act of taking a
snapshot (in sense 2).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instantaneous photograph made, usually with a
hand camera, without formal posing of, and often without the
foreknowledge of, the subject.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>a brief summary or appraisal, especially one that
describes the state of a situation at one particular time; <as>as, a
<ex>snapshot</ex> of the moon project in 1966</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snapweed</ent><br/
<hw>Snap"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Impatiens</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snar</ent><br/
<hw>Snar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Akin to LG. & OD.
<ets>snarren</ets>, G. <ets>schnarren</ets>, E. <ets>snore</ets>. See
<er>Snore</er>, and cf. <er>Snarl</er> to growl.]</ety> <def>To
snarl.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snare</ent><br/
<hw>Snare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>snear</ets>a
cord, a string; akin to D. <ets>snoer</ets>, G. <ets>schnur</ets>, OHG.
<ets>snour</ets> a cord, <ets>snarahha</ets> a noose, Dan.
<ets>snare</ets>, Sw. & Icel. <ets>snara</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sn<?/rj<?/</ets> a basket; and probably also to E.
<ets>needle</ets>. See <er>Needle</er>, and cf. <er>Snarl</er> to
entangle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A contrivance, often consisting of a
noose of cord, or the like, by which a bird or other animal may be
entangled and caught; a trap; a gin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, anything by which one is entangled and
brought into trouble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If thou retire, the Dauphin, well appointed,<br/
Stands with the <qex>snares</qex> of war to tangle thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The gut or string stretched across the lower head of
a drum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An instrument, consisting usually
of a wireloop or noose, for removing tumors, etc., by
avulsion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snare drum</b></col>, <cd>the smaller common military
drum, as distinguished from the <xex>bass drum</xex>; -- so called
because (in order to render it more resonant) it has stretched across
its lower head a catgut string or strings.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snare</ent><br/
<hw>Snare</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snaring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To catch with a snare; to
insnare; to entangle; hence, to bring into unexpected evil, perplexity,
or danger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lest that too heavenly form . . . <qex>snare</qex> them.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mournful crocodile<br/
With sorrow <qex>snares</qex> relenting passengers.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarer</ent><br/
<hw>Snar"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lays snares,
or entraps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarl</ent><br/
<hw>Snarl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snarled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vvb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Snarling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>To form raised work upon the outer surface of
(thin metal ware) by the repercussion of a snarling iron upon the inner
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarl</ent><br/
<hw>Snarl</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Snare</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To entangle; to complicate; to involve
in knots; <as>as, to <ex>snarl</ex> a skein of thread</as>.</def>
<ldquo/Her <xex>snarled</xex> hair.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To embarrass; to insnare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The] question that they would have <qex>snarled</qex> him
with.</q> <rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarl</ent><br/
<hw>Snarl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A knot or complication of hair,
thread, or the like, difficult to disentangle; entanglement; hence,
intricate complication; embarrassing difficulty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarl</ent><br/
<hw>Snarl</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Snar</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To growl, as an angry or surly dog; to gnarl; to utter
grumbling sounds.</def> <ldquo/An angry cur <xex>snarls</xex> while he
feeds.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden & Lee.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To speak crossly; to talk in rude, surly
terms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is malicious and unmanly to <qex>snarl</qex> at the little
lapses of a pen, from which Virgil himself stands not exempted.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarl</ent><br/
<hw>Snarl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of snarling; a growl; a surly
or peevish expression; an angry contention.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarler</ent><br/
<hw>Snarl"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who snarls; a
surly, growling animal; a grumbling, quarrelsome fellow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarler</ent><br/
<hw>Snarl"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes use of a snarling
iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snarling</ent><br/
<hw>Snarl"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Snarl</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snarling iron</b></col>, <cd>a tool with a long beak,
used in the process of snarling. When one end is held in a vise, and the
shank is struck with a hammer, the repercussion of the other end, or
beak, within the article worked upon gives the requisite blow for
producing raised work. See 1st <er>Snarl</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snary</ent><br/
<hw>Snar"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Snare</er>.]</ety> <def>Resembling, or consisting of, snares;
entangling; insidious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Spiders in the vault their <qex>snary</qex> webs have spread.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snast</ent><br/
<hw>Snast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Snite</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>The snuff, or burnt wick, of a
candle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snatch</ent><br/
<hw>Snatch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snatched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Snatching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>snachen</ets>, <ets>snechen</ets>; akin to D. <ets>snakken</ets> to
gasp, to long (for), to desire. Cf. <er>Snack</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Sneck</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take or seize hastily,
abruptly, or without permission or ceremony; <as>as, to <ex>snatch</ex>
a loaf or a kiss</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When half our knowledge we must <qex>snatch</qex>, not take.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To seize and transport away; to rap.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Snatch</xex> me to heaven.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To twitch; pluck; grab; catch; grasp;
gripe.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snatch</ent><br/
<hw>Snatch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To attempt to seize something
suddenly; to catch; -- often with <xex>at</xex>; <as>as, to
<ex>snatch</ex> at a rope</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1362 --></p>

<p><ent>Snatch</ent><br/
<hw>Snatch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A hasty
catching or seizing; a grab; a catching at, or attempt to seize,
suddenly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A short period of vigorous action; <as>as, a
<ex>snatch</ex> at weeding after a shower</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They move by fits and <qex>snatches</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Wilkins.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small piece, fragment, or quantity; a broken part;
a scrap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have often little <qex>snatches</qex> of sunshine.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Leave me your <qex>snatches</qex>, and yield me a direct
answer.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snatch block</ent><br/
<hw>Snatch block</hw> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <def>a kind of block with an
opening in one side to receive the bight of a rope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snatcher</ent><br/
<hw>Snatch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who snatches, or
takes abruptly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snatchingly</ent><br/
<hw>Snatch"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By snatching;
abruptly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snath</ent><br/
<hw>Snath</hw> <pr>(sn<acr/th)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS.
<ets>sn<imac/<edh/an</ets> to cut, to mow, <ets>sn<aemac/d</ets> a bite,
bit, snip.]</ety> <def>The handle of a scythe; a snead.</def>
<altsp>[Variously written in England <asp>snead</asp>, <asp>sneed</asp>,
<asp>sneath</asp>, <asp>sneeth</asp>, <asp>snathe</asp>, etc.; in
Scotland written <asp>sned</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snathe</ent><br/
<hw>Snathe</hw> <pr>(sn<amac/<th/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
Icel. <ets>snei<edh/a</ets> to cut into alices,
<ets>sn<imac/<edh/a</ets> to cut; akin to AS. be<ets>sn<aemac/dan</ets>,
<ets>sn<imac/<edh/an</ets>, G. <ets>schneiden</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sn<imac/dan</ets>, Goth. <ets>snei<thorn/an</ets> to cut, to reap,
and E. <ets>snath</ets>, <ets>snithe</ets>.]</ety> <def>To lop; to
prune.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snattock</ent><br/
<hw>Snat"tock</hw> <pr>(sn<acr/t"t<ucr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Snathe</er>.]</ety> <def>A chip; a slice.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Gayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snaw</ent><br/
<hw>Snaw</hw> <pr>(sn<add/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Snow.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snead</ent><br/
<hw>Snead</hw> <pr>(sn<emac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Snath</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A snath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A line or cord; a string.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneak</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak</hw> <pr>(sn<emac/k)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sneaked</conjf> <pr>(sn<emac/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sneaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sniken</ets>, AS. <ets>sn<imac/can</ets> to creep; akin to Dan.
<ets>snige sig</ets>; cf. Icel. <ets>sn<imac/kja</ets> to hanker
after.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To creep or steal (away or about)
privately; to come or go meanly, as a person afraid or ashamed to be
seen; <as>as, to <ex>sneak</ex> away from company</as>.</def> <-- ?
<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> "snuck" is more common now, but not even
mentioned here. In MW10, simply "sneaked or snuck" --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You skulked behind the fence, and <qex>sneaked</qex> away.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To act in a stealthy and cowardly manner; to behave
with meanness and servility; to crouch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneak</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To hide, esp. in a mean or
cowardly manner.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/[Slander]
<xex>sneaks</xex> its head.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Wake.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneak</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mean, sneaking
fellow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A set of simpletons and superstitious <qex>sneaks</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>A ball bowled so as to roll
along the ground; -- called also <altname>grub</altname>.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark> <rj><au>R. A. Proctor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneak-cup</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak"-cup`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sneaks
from his cups; one who balks his glass.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneak current</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak current</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A current which, though
too feeble to blow the usual fuse or to injure at once telegraph or
telephone instruments, will in time burn them out.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneaker</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak"er</hw> <pr>(sn<emac/k"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who sneaks.</def> <rj><au>Lamb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A vessel of drink.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sneaker</qex> of five gallons.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A type of soft shoe with a flat, pliable, typically
rubber or other soft sole, and canvas-like upper, used in sports such as
tennis, or for comfort. Called <ex>sneaker</ex> because they give no
warning of one's approach. Usually used in the
<pluf>pl.</pluf></def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A punch bowl.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneakiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
being sneaky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneaking</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Marked by cowardly concealment;
deficient in openness and courage; underhand; mean; crouching.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sneak"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sneak"ing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneaksby</ent><br/
<hw>Sneaks"by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A paltry fellow; a
sneak.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Such a bashful
<xex>sneaksby</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneaky</ent><br/
<hw>Sneak"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Like a sneak;
sneaking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneap</ent><br/
<hw>Sneap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>sneypa</ets> to dishonor, disgrace, chide, but also E.
<ets>snip</ets>, and <ets>snub</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To check;
to reprimand; to rebuke; to chide.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To nip; to blast; to blight.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Biron is like an envious, <qex>sneaping</qex> frost.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneap</ent><br/
<hw>Sneap</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A reprimand; a rebuke.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My lord, I will not undergo this <qex>sneap</qex> without
reply.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneathe</ent><br/
<ent>Sneath</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sneath</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sneathe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Snath</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneb</ent><br/
<hw>Sneb</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Snib</er>.]</ety> <def>To reprimand; to sneap.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Scold and <xex>sneb</xex> the good
oak.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneck</ent><br/
<hw>Sneck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Snatch</er>.]</ety> <def>To fasten by a hatch; to latch, as a
door.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sneck up</b></col>, <cd>be silent; shut up; hold your
peace.</cd></cs> <-- Obs or archaic? --> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneck</ent><br/
<hw>Sneck</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A door latch.</def> <mark>[Scot. &
Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sneck band</b></col>, <cd>a latchstring.</cd>
<au>Burns.</au> -- <col><b>Sneck drawer</b></col>, <cd>a latch lifter; a
bolt drawer; hence, a sly person; a cozener; a cheat; -- called also
<altname>sneckdraw</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sneck drawing</b></col>,
<cd>lifting the latch.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snecket</ent><br/
<hw>Sneck"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A door latch, or
sneck.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sned</ent><br/
<hw>Sned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To lop; to
snathe.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneed</ent><br/
<ent>Sned</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sneed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Snath</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneer</ent><br/
<hw>Sneer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sneered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sneering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sneren</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sn<?/rre</ets> to snarl or grin (like a dog); cf. Prov. E.
<ets>sneer</ets> to grin, <ets>sner</ets> to snort, <ets>snert</ets> to
sneer at. See <er>Snore</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To show contempt by turning up the nose, or by a particular facial
expression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inssinuate contempt by a covert expression; to
speak derisively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I could be content to be a little <qex>sneared</qex> at.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To show mirth awkwardly.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To scoff; gibe; jeer.</syn> --
<usage><er>Sneer</er>, <er>Scoff</er>, <er>Jeer</er>. The verb to
<xex>sneer</xex> implies to cast contempt indirectly or by covert
expressions. To <xex>jeer</xex> is stronger, and denotes the use of
several sarcastic reflections. To <xex>scoff</xex> is stronger still,
implying the use of insolent mockery and derision.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>sneers</qex> as learnedly as they,<br/
Like females o'er their morning tea.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Midas, exposed to all their <qex>jeers</qex>,<br/
Had lost his art, and kept his ears.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fop, with learning at defiance,<br/
<qex>Scoffs</qex> at the pedant and science.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneer</ent><br/
<hw>Sneer</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter with a
grimace or contemptuous expression; to utter with a sneer; to say
sneeringly; <as>as, to <ex>sneer</ex> fulsome lies at a
person</as>.</def> <rj><au>Congreve.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/A ship of fools,<rdquo/ he <qex>sneered</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To treat with sneers; to affect or move by
sneers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor <qex>sneered</qex> nor bribed from virtue into shame.</q>
<rj><qau>Savage.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneer</ent><br/
<hw>Sneer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
sneering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A smile, grin, or contortion of the face, indicative
of contempt; an indirect expression or insinuation of contempt.</def>
<ldquo/Who can refute a <xex>sneer</xex>?<rdquo/
<rj><au>Raley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneerer</ent><br/
<hw>Sneer"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sneers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneerful</ent><br/
<hw>Sneer"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Given to
sneering.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneeringly</ent><br/
<hw>Sneer"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sneering
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneeze</ent><br/
<hw>Sneeze</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sneezed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sneezing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>snesen</ets>; of
uncertain origin; cf. D. <ets>snuse</ets> to sniff, E. <ets>neese</ets>,
and AS. <ets>fne<oacute/san</ets>.]</ety> <def>To emit air, chiefly
through the nose, audibly and violently, by a kind of involuntary
convulsive force, occasioned by irritation of the inner membrane of the
nose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Not to be sneezed at</b></col>, <cd>not to be despised or
contemned; not to be treated lightly.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<ldquo/He had to do with old women who were <xex>not to be sneezed
at</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Prof. Wilson.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneeze</ent><br/
<hw>Sneeze</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sudden and violent ejection of air
with an audible sound, chiefly through the nose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneezeweed</ent><br/
<hw>Sneeze"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A yellow-flowered composite plant (<spn>Helenium autumnale</spn>)
the odor of which is said to cause sneezing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneezewood</ent><br/
<hw>Sneeze"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The wood of a South African tree. See <er>Neishout</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneezewort</ent><br/
<hw>Sneeze"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A European herbaceous plant (<spn>Achillea Ptarmica</spn>) allied
to the yarrow, having a strong, pungent smell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sneezing</ent><br/
<hw>Sneez"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The act of
violently forcing air out through the nasal passages while the cavity of
the mouth is shut off from the pharynx by the approximation of the soft
palate and the base of the tongue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snell</ent><br/
<hw>Snell</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>snell</ets>;
akin to D. <ets>snel</ets>, G. <ets>schnell</ets>, OHG. <ets>snel</ets>,
Icel. <ets>snjallr</ets> valiant.]</ety> <def>Active; brisk; nimble;
quick; sharp.</def> <mark>[Archaic or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That horny-handed, <qex>snell</qex>, peremptory little man.</q>
<rj><qau>Dr. J. Brown.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snell</ent><br/
<hw>Snell</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A short line of horsehair, gut, etc.,
by which a fishhook is attached to a longer line.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snet</ent><br/
<hw>Snet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schnitt</ets> that which is cut, fr. <ets>schneiden</ets> to cut,
E. <ets>snath</ets>.]</ety> <def>The fat of a deer.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snet</ent><br/
<hw>Snet</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Snot</er>.]</ety> <def>The
clear of mucus; to blow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Snetting</xex> his nose.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snew</ent><br/
<hw>Snew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To snow; to
abound.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It <qex>snewed</qex> in his house of meat and drink.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snib</ent><br/
<hw>Snib</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>snibben</ets>; cf. Dan. <ets>snibbe</ets>, and E. <ets>snub</ets>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To check; to sneap; to sneb.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him would he <qex>snib</qex> sharply for the nones.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snib</ent><br/
<hw>Snib</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A reprimand; a snub.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snick</ent><br/
<hw>Snick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>snick</ets> a notch; cf. Icel. <ets>snikka</ets> nick, cut.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A small cut or mark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>A slight hit or tip of the
ball, often unintentional.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Fiber)</fld> <def>A knot or irregularity in
yarn.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Furriery)</fld> <def>A snip or cut, as in the hair
of a beast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snick and snee</b></col> <ety>[cf. D. <ets>snee</ets>,
<ets>snede</ets>, a cut]</ety>, <cd>a combat with knives.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snick</ent><br/
<hw>Snick</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snicked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snicking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut slightly; to
strike, or strike off, as by cutting.</def> <rj><au>H.
Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>To hit (a ball) lightly.</def>
<rj><au>R. A. Proctor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snick</ent><br/
<hw>Snick</hw>, <pos>n. & v. t.</pos> <def>See <er>Sneck</er>.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Snick up</b></col>, <cd>shut up; silenced. See <xex>Sneck
up</xex>, under <er>Sneck</er>.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give him money, George, and let him go <qex>snick up</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Snicker</ent><br/
<hw>Snick"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snickered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Snickering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>snikken</ets> to sob, to sigh.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>snigger</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To laugh slyly; to laugh
in one's sleeve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To laugh with audible catches of voice, as when
persons attempt to suppress loud laughter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snicker</ent><br/
<hw>Snick"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A half suppressed, broken
laugh.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>snigger</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snide</ent><br/
<hw>Snide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Tricky;
deceptive; contemptible; <as>as, a <ex>snide</ex> lawyer; <ex>snide</ex>
goods.</as></def> <mark>[Slang, archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>derogatory in an insinuating manner; <as>as, a
<ex>snide</ex> remark</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snider</ent><br/
<ent>Snider rifle</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sni"der ri"fle</hw>, <it>or</it> <hw>Sni"der</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A breech-loading rifle formerly
used in the British service; -- so called from the inventor.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sniff</ent><br/
<hw>Sniff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sniffed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Snift</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sniffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sneven</ets>; akin to <ets>snivel</ets>, <ets>snuff</ets>; cf. Dan.
<ets>snive</ets> to sniff. See <er>Snuff</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To draw air audibly up the nose; to snuff; -- sometimes done as a
gesture of suspicion, offense, or contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So ye grow squeamish, gods, and <qex>sniff</qex> at heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sniff</ent><br/
<hw>Sniff</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To draw in with the
breath through the nose; <as>as, to <ex>sniff</ex> the air of the
country</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To perceive as by sniffing; to snuff, to scent; to
smell; <as>as, to <ex>sniff</ex> danger</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sniff</ent><br/
<hw>Sniff</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of sniffing; perception by
sniffing; that which is taken by sniffing; <as>as, a <ex>sniff</ex> of
air</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sniffing</ent><br/
<hw>Sniff"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A rapid
inspiratory act, in which the mouth is kept shut and the air drawn in
through the nose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sniffle</ent><br/
<hw>Snif"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>sniff</ets>. See <er>Snivel</er>.]</ety> <def>To snuffle, as one
does with a catarrh.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snift</ent><br/
<hw>Snift</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snifted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snifting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Sniff</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To snort.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Resentment
expressed by <xex>snifting</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sniff; to snuff; to smell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It now appears that they were still <qex>snifing</qex> and
hankering after their old quarters.</q> <rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snift</ent><br/
<hw>Snift</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A moment.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Slight snow; sleet.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snifting</ent><br/
<hw>Snift"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Snift</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snifting valve</b></col>, <cd>a small valve opening into
the atmosphere from the cylinder or condenser of a steam engine, to
allow the escape of air when the piston makes a stroke; -- so called
from the noise made by its action.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snig</ent><br/
<hw>Snig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Snick</er> a
small cut.]</ety> <def>To chop off; to cut.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snig</ent><br/
<hw>Snig</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sneak</er>.]</ety> <def>To
sneak.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snigg</ent><br/
<ent>Snig</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Snig</hw>, <hw>Snigg</hw>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sneak</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small eel.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snigger</ent><br/
<hw>Snig"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Snicker</er>.</def> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snigger</ent><br/
<hw>Snig"ger</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Snicker</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sniggle</ent><br/
<hw>Snig"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sniggled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sniggling</conjf><pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Snig</er> a kind of eel.]</ety> <def>To fish for eels by thrusting
the baited hook into their holes or hiding places.</def>
<rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sniggle</ent><br/
<hw>Snig"gle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To catch, as an eel, by
sniggling; hence, to hook; to insnare.</def> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snip</ent><br/
<hw>Snip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snipped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snipping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[D. <ets>snippen</ets>; akin to
G. <ets>schnippen</ets>.]</ety> <def>To cut off the nip or neb of, or to
cut off at once with shears or scissors; to clip off suddenly; to nip;
hence, to break off; to snatch away.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Curbed and <qex>snipped</qex> in my younger years by fear of my
parents from those vicious excrescences to which that age was
subject.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The captain seldom ordered anything out of the ship's stores . . .
but I <qex>snipped</qex> some of it for my own share.</q> <rj><qau>De
Foe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snip</ent><br/
<hw>Snip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A single cut,
as with shears or scissors; a clip.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small shred; a bit cut off.</def>
<rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A share; a snack.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>L'Estrange</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A tailor.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Nares.
C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Small hand shears for cutting sheet metal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snipe</ent><br/
<hw>Snipe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>snipe</ets>;
akin to D. <ets>snep</ets>, <ets>snip</ets>, LG. <ets>sneppe</ets>,
<ets>snippe</ets>, G. <ets>schnepfe</ets>, Icel. <ets>sn<imac/pa</ets>
(in comp.), Dan. <ets>sneppe</ets>, Sw. <ets>sn<aum/ppa</ets> a
sanpiper, and possibly to E. <ets>snap</ets>. See <er>Snap</er>,
<er>Snaffle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of limicoline game birds of the family
<fam>Scolopacidae</fam>, having a long, slender, nearly straight
beak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common, or whole, snipe (<spn>Gallinago
c<oe/lestis</spn>) and the great, or double, snipe (<spn>Gallinago
major</spn>), are the most important European species. The Wilson's
snipe (<spn>Gallinago delicata</spn>) (sometimes erroneously called
<stype>English snipe</stype>) and the gray snipe, or dowitcher
(<spn>Macrohamphus griseus</spn>), are well-known American
species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fool; a blockhead.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Half snipe</b></col>, <cd>the dunlin; the jacksnipe.</cd>
-- <col><b>Jack snipe</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Jacksnipe</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Quail snipe</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Quail</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Robin snipe</b></col>, <cd>the knot.</cd> -- <col><b>Sea
snipe</b></col>. <cd>See in the Vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Shore
snipe</b></col>, <cd>any sandpiper.</cd> -- <col><b>Snipe
hawk</b></col>, <cd>the marsh harrier.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Stone snipe</b></col>, <cd>the tattler.</cd> -- <col><b>Summer
snipe</b></col>, <cd>the dunlin; the green and the common European
sandpipers.</cd> -- <col><b>Winter snipe</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Rock
snipe</cref>, under <er>Rock</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Woodcock
snipe</b></col>, <cd>the great snipe.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snipe</ent><br/
<hw>Snipe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sniped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sniping</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shoot
or hunt snipe.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To shoot at detached men of an enemy's forces at
long range, esp. when not in action; -- often with
<ptcl>at</ptcl>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>snipe at</b></col>, <cd>to aim petty or snide criticisms
at (a person) in his absence.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snipe</ent><br/
<hw>Snipe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To shoot
at (detached men of an enemy's force) at long range, esp. when not in
action.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To nose (a log) to make it drag or slip easily in
skidding.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snipebill</ent><br/
<hw>Snipe"bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
plane for cutting deep grooves in moldings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bolt by which the body of a cart is fastened to
the axle.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snipefish</ent><br/
<hw>Snipe"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The bellows fish.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A long,
slender deep-sea fish (<spn>Nemichthys scolopaceus</spn>) with a slender
beak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snippack</ent><br/
<hw>Snip"pack</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Snipe</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common snipe.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snipper</ent><br/
<hw>Snip"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
snips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snipper-snaper</ent><br/
<hw>Snip"per-snap`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small,
insignificant fellow.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snippet</ent><br/
<hw>Snip"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small part or
piece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be cut into <qex>snippets</qex> and shreds.</q> <rj><qau>F.
Harrison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snippety</ent><br/
<hw>Snip"pet*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Ridiculously small;
petty.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Snippety</xex> facts.<rdquo/ <rj><au>London
Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. short-tempered; snappish. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Snippy, adj. snippety. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snip-snap</ent><br/
<hw>Snip"-snap`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Reduplication of
<ets>snap</ets>.]</ety> <def>A tart dialogue with quick replies.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snip-snap</ent><br/
<hw>Snip"-snap`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Quick; short; sharp;
smart.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snipy</ent><br/
<hw>Snip"y</hw> <pr>(sn<imac/p"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a
snipe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snite</ent><br/
<hw>Snite</hw> <pr>(sn<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A snipe.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snite</ent><br/
<hw>Snite</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>sn<imac/fa</ets>. See
<er>Snout</er>.]</ety> <def>To blow, as the nose; to snuff, as a
candle.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snithy</ent><br/
<ent>Snithe</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Snithe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Snith"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sn<imac/<edh/an</ets> to cut. See
<er>Snathe</er>.]</ety> <def>Sharp; piercing; cutting; -- applied to the
wind.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snivel</ent><br/
<hw>Sniv"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sniveled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or
<conjf>Snivelled</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sniveling</conjf> or <conjf>Snivelling</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[OE. <ets>snivelen</ets>, <ets>snevelen</ets>, <ets>snuvelen</ets>,
freg. of <ets>sneven</ets>. See <er>Sniff</er>, and cf.
<er>Snuffle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To run at the nose; to make a
snuffling noise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cry or whine with snuffling, as children; to cry
weakly or whiningly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Put stop to thy <qex>sniveling</qex> ditty.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snivel</ent><br/
<hw>Sniv"el</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>snofel</ets>. Cf.
<er>Snivel</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>Mucus from the nose;
snot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sniveler</ent><br/
<hw>Sniv"el*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>sniveller</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who snivels, esp. one who snivels
habitually.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snively</ent><br/
<hw>Sniv"el*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Running at the nose;
sniveling pitiful; whining.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snob</ent><br/
<hw>Snob</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>sn<amac/pr</ets> a dolt, impostor, charlatan. Cf.
<er>Snub</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A vulgar person who affects to be
better, richer, or more fashionable, than he really is; a vulgar
upstart; one who apes his superiors.</def>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Essentially vulgar, a <qex>snob</qex>. -- a gilded
<qex>snob</qex>, but none the less a <qex>snob</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>R. G.
White.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Univ.)</fld> <def>A townsman.</def>
<mark>[Canf]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A journeyman shoemaker.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A workman who accepts lower than the usual wages, or
who refuses to strike when his fellows do; a rat; a knobstick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those who work for lower wages during a strike are called
<qex>snobs</qex>, the men who stand out being <ldquo/nobs<rdquo/</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 5. One who adopts an offensive air of superiority to those he
considers as inferiors, esp. by avoiding or ignoring them. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snobbery</ent><br/
<hw>Snob"ber*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
being snobbish; snobbishness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snobbish</ent><br/
<hw>Snob"bish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
a snob; characteristic of, or befitting, a snob; vulgarly
pretentious.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Snob"bish*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snobbishness</ent><br/
<hw>Snob"bish*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Vulgar affectation or
ostentation; mean admiration of mean things; conduct or manners of a
snob.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snobbism</ent><br/
<hw>Snob"bism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Snobbery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1363 --></p>

<p><ent>Snobby</ent><br/
<hw>Snob"by</hw> <pr>(sn<ocr/b"b<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Snobbish.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>E. B.
Ramsay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snobling</ent><br/
<hw>Snob"ling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little snob.</def>
<mark>[Jocose]</mark> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snobocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Snob*oc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(sn<ocr/b*<ocr/k"r<adot/*s<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Snob</ets> + <ets>-cracy</ets>, as in
<ets>aristocracy</ets>, <ets>mobocracy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Snobs,
collectively.</def> <mark>[Hybrid & Recent]</mark> <rj><au>C.
Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snod</ent><br/
<hw>Snod</hw> <pr>(sn<ocr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Snood</er>.]</ety> <def>A fillet; a headband; a snood.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snod</ent><br/
<hw>Snod</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Scot. <ets>snod</ets> to prune, put
in order.]</ety> <def>Trimmed; smooth; neat; trim; sly; cunning;
demure.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snoff</ent><br/
<hw>Snoff</hw> <pr>(sn<ocr/f; 115)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Snuff</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A short candle end used
for igniting a fuse.</def> <rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snood</ent><br/
<hw>Snood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sn<omac/d</ets>. Cf. <ets>Snare</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
fillet which binds the hair of a young unmarried woman, and is
emblematic of her maiden character.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And seldom was a <qex>snood</qex> amid<br/
Such wild, luxuriant ringlets hid.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A short line (often of horsehair) connecting a
fishing line with the hook; a snell; a leader.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snood</ent><br/
<hw>Snood</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To bind or braid up, as the hair,
with a snood.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snooded</ent><br/
<hw>Snood"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wearing or having a snood.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>snooded</xex> daughter.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Whittier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snook</ent><br/
<hw>Snook</hw> <pr>(sn<oomac/k)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>snook</ets> to search out, to follow by the scent; cf. Sw.
<ets>snoka</ets> to lurk, LG. <ets>sn<oum/ggen</ets>,
<ets>snuckern</ets>, <ets>sn<oum/kern</ets>, to snuffle, to smell about,
to search for.]</ety> <def>To lurk; to lie in ambush.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snook</ent><br/
<hw>Snook</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>snoek</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A large perchlike marine food fish
(<spn>Centropomus undecimalis</spn>) found both on the Atlantic and
Pacific coasts of tropical America; -- called also
<altname>ravallia</altname>, and <altname>robalo</altname>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The cobia.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
garfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snooze</ent><br/
<hw>Snooze</hw> <pr>(sn<oomac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot.
<ets>snooze</ets> to sleep; cf. Dan. & Sw. <ets>snus</ets> snuff.]</ety>
<def>A short sleep; a nap.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snooze</ent><br/
<hw>Snooze</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snoozed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snoozing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To doze; to drowse; to take a
short nap; to slumber.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snore</ent><br/
<hw>Snore</hw> <pr>(sn<omac/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Snored</conjf> <pr>(sn<omac/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Snoring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>snoren</ets>, AS. <ets>snora</ets> a snoring; akin to LG.
<ets>snoren</ets>, <ets>snorken</ets>, <ets>snurken</ets>, to snore, D.
<ets>snorken</ets>, G. <ets>schnarchen</ets> to snore,
<ets>schnarren</ets> to rattle, MHG. <ets>snarren</ets>, Sw.
<ets>snarka</ets> to snore, Icel. <ets>snarka</ets> to sputter, fizzle.
Cf. <er>Snarl</er> to growl, <er>Sneer</er>, <er>Snort</er>. See
<er>Snoring</er>.]</ety> <def>To breathe with a rough, hoarse, nasal
voice in sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snore</ent><br/
<hw>Snore</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A harsh nasal noise made in
sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snorer</ent><br/
<hw>Snor"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
snores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snoring</ent><br/
<hw>Snor"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The act of
respiring through the open mouth so that the currents of inspired and
expired air cause a vibration of the uvula and soft palate, thus giving
rise to a sound more or less harsh. It is usually unvoluntary, but may
be produced voluntarily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snort</ent><br/
<hw>Snort</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snorted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snorting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>snorten</ets>; akin to
<ets>snoren</ets>. <ets>See</ets> <er>Snore</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To force the air with violence through the nose, so as to make a
noise, as do high-spirited horsed in prancing and play.</def>
<rj><au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To snore.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The
<xex>snorting</xex> citizens.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To laugh out loudly.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snort</ent><br/
<hw>Snort</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of snorting; the sound
produced in snorting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snort</ent><br/
<hw>Snort</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To expel through the nostrils with
a snort; to utter with a snort.</def> <rj><au>Keats.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snorter</ent><br/
<hw>Snort"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
snorts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The wheatear; -- so called from
its cry.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snot</ent><br/
<hw>Snot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>snot</ets>;
akin to D. <ets>snot</ets>, LG. <ets>snotte</ets>, Dan. <ets>snot</ets>,
and to E. <ets>snout</ets>. <ets>See</ets> <er>Snout</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Mucus secreted in, or discharged from, the nose.</def>
<mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mean, insignificant fellow.</def>
<mark>[Low]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snot</ent><br/
<hw>Snot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To blow, wipe, or clear, as the
nose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snotter</ent><br/
<hw>Snot"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Snot</er>.]</ety> <def>To snivel; to cry or whine.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snotter</ent><br/
<hw>Snot"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.]</ety>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A rope going over a yardarm, used to bend a
tripping line to, in sending down topgallant and royal yards in vessels
of war; also, the short line supporting the heel of the sprit in a small
boat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snottery</ent><br/
<hw>Snot"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Filth;
abomination.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To purge the <qex>snottery</qex> of our slimy time.</q>
<rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snotty</ent><br/
<hw>Snot"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Foul with snot; hence,
mean; dirty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Snot"ti*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Snot"ti*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snout</ent><br/
<hw>Snout</hw> <pr>(snout)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>snoute</ets>, probably of Scand, or Low German origin; cf. LG.
<ets>snute</ets>, D. <ets>snuit</ets>, G. <ets>schnauze</ets>, Sw.
<ets>snut</ets>, <ets>snyte</ets>, Dan. <ets>snude</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sn<?/ta</ets> to blow the nose; probably akin to E.
<ets>snuff</ets>, v.t. Cf. <er>Snite</er>, <er>Snot</er>,
<er>Snuff</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The long, projecting nose of a
beast, as of swine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The nose of a man; -- in contempt.</def>
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The nozzle of a pipe, hose, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The anterior
prolongation of the head of a gastropod; -- called also
<altname>rostrum</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The anterior
prolongation of the head of weevils and allied beetles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snout beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
many species of beetles having an elongated snout and belonging to the
tribe Rhynchophora; a weevil.</cd> -- <col><b>Snout moth</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any pyralid moth. See
<er>Pyralid</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snout</ent><br/
<hw>Snout</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To furnish with a nozzle or
point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snouty</ent><br/
<hw>Snout"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a beast's
snout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The nose was ugly, long, and big,<br/
Broad and <qex>snouty</qex> like a pig.</q>
<rj><qau>Otway.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow</ent><br/
<hw>Snow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LG. <ets>snaue</ets>, or
D. <ets>snaauw</ets>, from LG. <ets>snau</ets> a snout, a beak.]</ety>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A square-rigged vessel, differing from a brig
only in that she has a trysail mast close abaft the mainmast, on which a
large trysail is hoisted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow</ent><br/
<hw>Snow</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>snow</ets>, <ets>snaw</ets>,
AS. <ets>sn<amac/w</ets>; akin to D. <ets>sneeuw</ets>, OS. & OHG.
<ets>sn<emac/o</ets>, G. <ets>schnee</ets>, Icel. <ets>sn<ae/r</ets>,
<ets>snj<omac/r</ets>, <ets>snaj<amac/r</ets>, Sw. <ets>sn<oum/</ets>,
Dan. <ets>snee</ets>, Goth. <ets>snaiws</ets>, Lith.
<ets>sn<eum/gas</ets>, Russ. <ets>snieg'</ets>, Ir. & Gael.
<ets>sneachd</ets>, W. <ets>nyf</ets>, L. <ets>nix</ets>,
<ets>nivis</ets>, Gr. acc. <grk>ni`fa</grk>, also AS.
<ets>sn<imac/wan</ets> to snow, G. <ets>schneien</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sn<imac/wan</ets>, Lith. <ets>snigti</ets>, L. <ets>ningit</ets> it
snows, Gr. <grk>ni`fei</grk>, Zend <ets>snizh</ets> to snow; cf. Skr.
<ets>snih</ets> to be wet or sticky. <root/172.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Watery particles congealed into white or transparent crystals or
flakes in the air, and falling to the earth, exhibiting a great variety
of very beautiful and perfect forms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Snow</xex> is often used to form compounds, most of
which are of obvious meaning; as, <xex>snow</xex>-capped,
<xex>snow</xex>-clad, <xex>snow</xex>-cold, <xex>snow</xex>-crowned,
<xex>snow</xex>-crust, <xex>snow</xex>-fed, <xex>snow</xex>-haired,
<xex>snow</xex>like, <xex>snow</xex>-mantled, <xex>snow</xex>-nodding,
<xex>snow</xex>-wrought, and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Something white like snow, as the white color
(argent) in heraldry; something which falls in, or as in,
flakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The field of <qex>snow</qex> with eagle of black therein.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Red snow</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Red</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snow bunting</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Snowbird</er>, 1.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow cock</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the snow pheasant.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow
flea</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small black leaping poduran
(<spn>Achorutes nivicola</spn>) often found in winter on the snow in
vast numbers.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow flood</b></col>, <cd>a flood from
melted snow.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow flower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the fringe tree.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Snow fly</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Snow insect</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of several species of neuropterous insects of the genus
<gen>Boreus</gen>. The male has rudimentary wings; the female is
wingless. These insects sometimes appear creeping and leaping on the
snow in great numbers.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow gnat</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any wingless dipterous insect of the genus
<gen>Chionea</gen> found running on snow in winter.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow
goose</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of
arctic geese of the genus <gen>Chen</gen>. The common snow goose
(<spn>Chen hyperborea</spn>), common in the Western United States in
winter, is white, with the tips of the wings black and legs and bill
red. Called also <altname>white brant</altname>,
<altname>wavey</altname>, and <altname>Texas goose</altname>. The blue,
or blue-winged, snow goose (<spn>Chen coerulescens</spn>) is varied with
grayish brown and bluish gray, with the wing quills black and the head
and upper part of the neck white. Called also <altname>white
head</altname>, <altname>white-headed goose</altname>, and <altname>bald
brant</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow leopard</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the ounce.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow line</b></col>,
<cd>lowest limit of perpetual snow. In the Alps this is at an altitude
of 9,000 feet, in the Andes, at the equator, 16,000 feet.</cd> --
<col><b>Snow mouse</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a European vole
(<spn>Arvicola nivalis</spn>) which inhabits the Alps and other high
mountains.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow pheasant</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of several species of large, handsome gallinaceous birds of
the genus <gen>Tetraogallus</gen>, native of the lofty mountains of
Asia. The Himalayn snow pheasant (<spn>Tetraogallus Himalayensis</spn>)
in the best-known species. Called also <altname>snow cock</altname>, and
<altname>snow chukor</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow
partridge</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Partridge</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow pigeon</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a pigeon (<spn>Columba leuconota</spn>) native
of the Himalaya mountains. Its back, neck, and rump are white, the top
of the head and the ear coverts are black.</cd> -- <col><b>Snow
plant</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a fleshy parasitic herb
(<spn>Sarcodes sanguinea</spn>) growing in the coniferous forests of
California. It is all of a bright red color, and is fabled to grow from
the snow, through which it sometimes shoots up.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow</ent><br/
<hw>Snow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snowed</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Snowing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To fall in or as snow;
-- chiefly used impersonally; <as>as, it <ex>snows</ex>; it
<ex>snowed</ex> yesterday.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow</ent><br/
<hw>Snow</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To scatter like snow; to cover
with, or as with, snow.</def> <rj><au>Donne. Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowball</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"ball`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A round
mass of snow pressed or roller together, or anything resembling such a
mass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The Guelder-rose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>a shrub of the genus
<gen>Viburnum</gen>, having large clusters of white flowers.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Snowball bush</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Snowball tree</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
<er>Guelder-rose</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>a snowball's chance in
hell</b></col>, <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <cd>no chance; an infinitesimal
chance.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowball</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"ball`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snowballed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snowballing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To pelt with snowballs; to
throw snowballs at.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowball</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"ball`</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw
snowballs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To increase in magnitude at an accelerating rate,
achieving large proportions; -- by analogy with a snowball rolling down
a steep hill, causing a large snow slide.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow banner</ent><br/
<hw>Snow banner</hw>. <def>A bannerlike stream of snow blown into the
air from a mountain peak, often having a pinkish color and extending
horizontally for several miles across the sky.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowberry</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"ber`ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A name of several shrubs with white berries; as, the
<spn>Symphoricarpus racemosus</spn> of the Northern United States, and
the <spn>Chiococca racemosa</spn> of Florida and tropical
America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Creeping snowberry</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Creeping</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowbird</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"bird</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>An arctic finch (<spn>Plectrophenax nivalis</spn> syn.
<spn>Plectrophanes nivalis</spn>) common, in winter, both in
<geog>Europe</geog> and the <country>United States</country>, and often
appearing in large flocks during snowstorms. It is partially white, but
variously marked with chestnut and brown. Called also <altname>snow
bunting</altname>, <altname>snowflake</altname>,
<altname>snowfleck</altname>, and <altname>snowflight</altname>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any finch of the genus <gen>Junco</gen> which appears
in flocks in winter time, especially <spn>Junco hyemalis</spn> in the
Eastern United States; -- called also <altname>blue snowbird</altname>.
See <er>Junco</er>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The fieldfare.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow-blind</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"-blind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected with
blindness by the brilliancy of snow.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Snow"-blind`ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow-bound</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"-bound`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Enveloped in, or
confined by, snow.</def> <rj><au>Whittier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow-broth</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"-broth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Snow and water
mixed, or snow just melted; very cold liquor.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowcap</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"cap`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
very small humming bird (<spn>Microchaera albocoronata</spn>) native of
New Grenada.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The feathers of the top of the head are white and
snining, the body blue black with a purple and bronzy luster. The name
is applied also to <spn>Microchaera parvirostris</spn> of Central
America, which is similar in color.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow-capped</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"-capped`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the top
capped or covered with snow; <as>as, <ex>snow-capped</ex>
mountains</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowdrift</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"drift`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bank of drifted
snow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowdrop</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"drop`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
bulbous plant (<spn>Galanthus nivalis</spn>) bearing white flowers,
which often appear while the snow is on the ground. It is cultivated in
gardens for its beauty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snowdrop tree</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Silver-bell
tree</cref>, under <er>Silver</er>, <pos>a.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowflake</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"flake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
flake, or small filmy mass, of snow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Snowbird</er>,
1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name given to several bulbous
plants of the genus <gen>Leucoium</gen> (<spn>Leucoium vernum</spn>,
<spn>Leucoium aestivum</spn>, etc.) resembling the snowdrop, but having
all the perianth leaves of equal size.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowfleck</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"fleck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Snowbird</er>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowl</ent><br/
<hw>Snowl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
hooded merganser.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowless</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of
snow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowplough</ent><br/
<ent>Snowplow</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Snow"plow`</hw>, <hw>Snow"plough`</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>An implement operating like a plow, but on a larger
scale, for clearing away the snow from roads, railways, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowshed</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"shed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shelter to protect
from snow, esp. a long roof over an exposed part of a
railroad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowshoe</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"shoe`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slight frame of
wood three or four feet long and about one third as wide, with thongs or
cords stretched across it, and having a support and holder for the foot;
-- used by persons for walking on soft snow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowshoeing</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"shoe`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Traveling on
snowshoes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowshoer</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"sho`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who travels on
snowshoes; an expert in using snowshoes.</def> <rj><au>W. G.
Beers.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowslip</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"slip`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A large mass or
avalanche of snow which slips down the side of a mountain,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowstorm</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"storm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A storm with
falling snow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snow-white</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"-white`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>White as snow;
very white.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Snow-white</xex> and rose-red<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snowy</ent><br/
<hw>Snow"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>White like
snow.</def> <ldquo/So shows a <xex>snowy</xex> dove trooping with
crows.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Abounding with snow; covered with snow.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>snowy</xex> top of cold Olympus.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: Pure; unblemished; unstained;
spotless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There did he lose his <qex>snowy</qex> innocence.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Hall (1646).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snowy heron</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a white
heron, or egret (<spn>Ardea candidissima</spn>), found in the Southern
United States, and southward to Chile; -- called also <altname>plume
bird</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Snowy lemming</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the collared lemming (<spn>Cuniculus
torquatus</spn>), which turns white in winter.</cd> -- <col><b>Snowy
owl</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large arctic owl (<spn>Nyctea
Scandiaca</spn>, or <spn>Nyctea nivea</spn>) common all over the
northern parts of the United States and Europe in winter time. Its
plumage is sometimes nearly pure white, but it is usually more or less
marked with blackish spots. Called also <altname>white
owl</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Snowy plover</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small plover (<spn>Aegialitis nivosa</spn>) of
the western parts of the United States and Mexico. It is light gray
above, with the under parts and portions of the head
white.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snub</ent><br/
<hw>Snub</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>snuiven</ets> to snort, to pant, G. <ets>schnauben</ets>, MHG.
<ets>sn<umac/ben</ets>, Prov. G. <ets>schnupfen</ets>, to sob, and E.
<ets>snuff</ets>, v.t.]</ety> <def>To sob with convulsions.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snub</ent><br/
<hw>Snub</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snubbed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snubbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Icel. <ets>ssnubba</ets> to
snub, chide, Sw. <ets>snubba</ets>, Icel. <ets>snubb<omac/ttr</ets>
snubbed, nipped, and E. <ets>snib</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To clip
or break off the end of; to check or stunt the growth of; to
nop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To check, stop, or rebuke, with a tart, sarcastic
reply or remark; to reprimand; to check.</def> <rj><au>J.
Foster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To treat with contempt or neglect, as a forward or
pretentious person; to slight designedly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To snub a cable</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To
snub a rope</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to check it
suddenly in running out.</cd> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snub</ent><br/
<hw>Snub</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A knot; a protuberance; a
song.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[A club] with ragged <qex>snubs</qex> and knotty grain.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A check or rebuke; an intended slight.</def>
<rj><qau>J. Foster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snub nose</b></col>, <cd>a short or flat nose.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Snub post</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Snubbing
post</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a post on a dock or shore,
around which a rope is thrown to check the motion of a
vessel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snub-nosed</ent><br/
<hw>Snub"-nosed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a short,
flat nose, slightly turned up; <as>as, the <ex>snub-nosed</ex>
eel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snub-nosed cachalot</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
pygmy sperm whale.</cd></cs> <-- snub-nosed revolver, a revolver with a
very short barrel. -- --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snudge</ent><br/
<hw>Snudge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Snug</er>.]</ety> <def>To lie snug or quiet.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snudge</ent><br/
<hw>Snudge</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A miser; a sneaking fellow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuff</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schnuppe</ets> candle snuff, <ets>schnuppen</ets> to snuff a candle
(see <er>Snuff</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, to snuff a candle), or cf.
<er>Snub</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>The part of a candle wick
charred by the flame, whether burning or not.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the burning <qex>snuff</qex> happens to get out of the
snuffers, you have a chance that it may fall into a dish of soup.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuff</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snuffed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snuffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>snuffen</ets>. See
<er>Snuff</er> of a candle <er>Snuff</er> to sniff.]</ety> <def>To crop
the snuff of, as a candle; to take off the end of the snuff
of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To snuff out</b></col>, <cd>to extinguish by
snuffing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1364 --></p>

<p><ent>Snuff</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos><ety>[Akin to D.
<ets>snuffen</ets>, G. <ets>schnupfen</ets>, <ets>schnuppen</ets>, to
snuff, <ets>schnupfen</ets> a cold in the head, <ets>schnuppen</ets> to
snuff (air), also, to snuff (a candle). Cf. <er>Sniff</er>,
<er>Snout</er>, <er>Snub</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To draw in, or to inhale, forcibly through the nose; to
sniff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>snuffs</qex> the wind, his heels the sand excite.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To perceive by the nose; to scent; to
smell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuff</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To inhale air through
the nose with violence or with noise, as do dogs and horses.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To turn up the nose and inhale air, as an expression
of contempt; hence, to take offense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do the enemies of the church rage and <qex>snuff</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuff</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of snuffing;
perception by snuffing; a sniff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pulverized tobacco, etc., prepared to be taken into
the nose; also, the amount taken at once.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Resentment, displeasure, or contempt, expressed by a
snuffing of the nose.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Snuff dipping</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Dipping</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 5.</cd> -- <col><b>Snuff taker</b></col>, <cd>one who
uses snuff by inhaling it through the nose.</cd> -- <col><b>To take it
in snuff</b></col>, <cd>to be angry or offended.</cd> <au>Shak.</au> --
<col><b>Up to snuff</b></col>, <cd>not likely to be imposed upon;
knowing; acute.</cd> <mark>[Slang]</mark><-- also, competent, able to do
[the task] --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuffbox</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff"box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small box for
carrying snuff about the person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuffer</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
snuffs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common porpoise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuffers</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff"ers</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>An instrument
for cropping and holding the snuff of a candle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuffingly</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a snuffing
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuffle</ent><br/
<hw>Snuf"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snuffled</conjf><pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snuffling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>snuff</ets>, v.i.; akin to LG. <ets>snuffeln</ets>, G.
<ets>schn<uum/ffeln</ets>, D. <ets>snuffeln</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sn<oum/vle</ets>. Cf. <er>Sniffle</er>.]</ety> <def>To speak
through the nose; to breathe through the nose when it is obstructed, so
as to make a broken sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One clad in purple<br/
Eats, and recites some lamentable rhyme . . . <br/
<qex>Snuffling</qex> at nose, and croaking in his throat.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuffle</ent><br/
<hw>Snuf"fle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of snuffing; a
sound made by the air passing through the nose when
obstructed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This dread sovereign, Breath, in its passage, gave a snort or
<qex>snuffle</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An affected nasal twang; hence, cant;
hypocrisy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Obstruction of the nose by mucus;
nasal catarrh of infants or children.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuffler</ent><br/
<hw>Snuf"fler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who snuffles;
one who uses cant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuffy</ent><br/
<hw>Snuff"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Soiled
with snuff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sulky; angry; vexed.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snug</ent><br/
<hw>Snug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Snugger</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Snuggest</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</amorph> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>snug</ets> tight, handsome; cf. Icel. <ets>sn<oum/ggr</ets> smooth,
ODan. <ets>sn<oum/g</ets> neat, Sw. <ets>snugg</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Close and warm; <as>as, an infant lies
<ex>snug</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Close; concealed; not exposed to notice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lie <qex>snug</qex>, and hear what critics say.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Compact, convenient, and comfortable; <as>as, a
<ex>snug</ex> farm, house, or property</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snug</ent><br/
<hw>Snug</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Lug</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snug</ent><br/
<hw>Snug</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Snugged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Snugging</conjf><pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To lie close; to
snuggle; to snudge; -- often with <xex>up</xex>, or <xex>together</xex>;
<as>as, a child <ex>snugs</ex> up to its mother</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snug</ent><br/
<hw>Snug</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place snugly.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To rub, as twine or rope, so as to make it smooth
and improve the finish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuggery</ent><br/
<hw>Snug"ger*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Snuggeries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A snug, cozy
place.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snuggle</ent><br/
<hw>Snug"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Snuggled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Snuggling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>snug</ets>.]</ety> <def>To move one way and the other so as to get
a close place; to lie close for comfort; to cuddle; to nestle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snugly</ent><br/
<hw>Snug"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a snug manner; closely;
safely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snugness</ent><br/
<hw>Snug"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
snug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sny</ent><br/
<hw>Sny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>sn<umac/a</ets> to turn.]</ety> <def>An upward bend in a piece of
timber; the sheer of a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Snying</ent><br/
<hw>Sny"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A curved plank,
placed edgewise, to work in the bows of a vessel.</def> <rj><au>R. H.
Dana, Jr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>So</ent><br/
<hw>So</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>so</ets>,
<ets>sa</ets>, <ets>swa</ets>, AS. <ets>sw<amac/</ets>; akin to OFries,
<ets>s<amac/</ets>, <ets>s<?/</ets>, D. <ets>zoo</ets>, OS. & OHG.
<ets>s<?/</ets>, G. <ets>so</ets>, Icel. <ets>sv<amac/</ets>,
<ets>sv<?/</ets>, <ets>svo</ets>, <ets>so</ets>, Sw. <ets>s<?/</ets>,
Dan. <ets>saa</ets>, Goth. <ets>swa</ets> so, <ets>sw<?/</ets> as; cf.
L. <ets>suus</ets> one's own, Skr. <ets>sva</ets> one's own, one's self.
<root/192. Cf. As, <er>Custom</er>, <er>Ethic</er>, <er>Idiom</er>,
<er>Such</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In that manner or degree; <as>as,
indicated (in any way), or as implied, or as supposed to be
known</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why is his chariot <qex>so</qex> long in coming?</q>
<rj><qau>Judges v. 28.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In like manner or degree; in the same way; thus; for
like reason; whith equal reason; -- used correlatively, following
<xex>as</xex>, to denote comparison or resemblance; sometimes, also,
following <xex>inasmuch as</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As a war should be undertaken upon a just motive, <qex>so</qex> a
prince ought to consider the condition he is in.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In such manner; to such degree; -- used
correlatively with <xex>as</xex> or <xex>that</xex> following; <as>as,
he was <ex>so</ex> fortunate as to escape</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I viewed in may mind, <qex>so</qex> far as I was able, the
beginning and progress of a rising world.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is very much in Sir Roger's esteem, <qex>so</qex> that he lives
in the family rather as a relation than dependent.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Very; in a high degree; that is, in such a degree as
can not well be expressed; <as>as, he is <ex>so</ex> good; he planned
<ex>so</ex> wisely</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>In the same manner; as has been stated or suggested;
in this or that condition or state; under these circumstances; in this
way; -- with reflex reference to something just asserted or implied;
used also with the verb <xex>to be</xex>, as a predicate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Use him [your tutor] with great respect yourself, and cause all
your family to do <qex>so</qex> too.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It concerns every man, with the greatest seriousness, to inquire
into those matters, whether they be <qex>so</qex> or not.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is Sir Robert's son, and <qex>so</qex> art thou.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The case being such; therefore; on this account; for
this reason; on these terms; -- used both as an adverb and a
conjuction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God makes him in his own image an intellectual creature, and
<qex>so</qex> capable of dominion.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here, then, exchange we mutually forgiveness;<br/
So may the guilt of all my broken vows,<br/
My perjuries to thee, be all forgotten.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>It is well; let it be as it is, or let it come to
pass; -- used to express assent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And when 't is writ, for my sake read it over,<br/
And if it please you, <qex>so</qex>; if not, why, <qex>so</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is Percy; if your father will do me any honor,
<qex>so</qex>; if not, let him kill the next Percy himself.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Well; the fact being as stated; -- used as an
expletive; <as>as, <ex>so</ex> the work is done, is it?</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Is it thus? do you mean what you say? -- with an
upward tone; <as>as, do you say he refuses? <ex>So</ex>?</as></def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>About the number, time, or quantity specified;
thereabouts; more or less; <as>as, I will spend a week or <ex>so</ex> in
the country; I have read only a page or <ex>so</ex>.</as></def> <-- s.b.
collocation "or so" --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A week or <qex>so</qex> will probably reconcile us.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ See the Note under <er>Ill</er>,
<pos>adv.</pos></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><mcol><col><b>So</b></col> . . . <col><b>as</b></col></mcol>.
<cd><xex>So</xex> is now commonly used as a demonstrative correlative of
<xex>as</xex> when it is the puprpose to emphasize the equality or
comparison suggested, esp. in negative assertions, and questions
implying a negative answer. By Shakespeare and others <xex>so</xex> . .
. <xex>as</xex> was much used where <xex>as</xex> . . . <xex>as</xex> is
now common. See the Note under <er>As</er>, 1.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>So</qex> do, <qex>as</qex> thou hast said.</q> <rj><qau>Gen.
xviii. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>As</qex> a flower of the field, <qex>so</qex> he
flourisheth.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. ciii. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Had woman been <qex>so</qex> strong <qex>as</qex> men.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No country suffered <qex>so</qex> much <qex>as</qex> England.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>So far</b></col>, <cd>to that point or extent; in that
particular.</cd> <ldquo/The song was moral, and <xex>so far</xex> was
right.<rdquo/ <au>Cowper.</au> -- <col><b>So far forth</b></col>, <cd>as
far; to such a degree.</cd> <au>Shak.</au> <au>Bacon.</au> -- <col><b>So
forth</b></col>, <cd>further in the same or similar manner; more of the
same or a similar kind. See <cref>And so forth</cref>, under
<er>And</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>So, so</b></col>, <cd>well, well.</cd>
<ldquo/<xex>So, so</xex>, it works; now, mistress, sit you fast.<rdquo/
<au>Dryden.</au> <cd>Also, moderately or tolerably well; passably; as,
he succeeded but <xex>so so</xex>.</cd> <ldquo/His leg is but <xex>so
so</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>So that</b></col>, <cd>to the
end that; in order that; with the effect or result that.</cd> --
<col><b>So then</b></col>, <cd>thus then it is; therefore; the
consequence is.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>So</ent><br/
<hw>So</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>conj.</pos> <def>Provided that; on
condition that; in case that; if.</def><-- R. or archaic --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though all the winds of doctrine were let loose play upon the
earth, <qex>so</qex> truth be in the field, we do injuriously, by
licensing and prohibiting, to misdoubt her strength.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>So</ent><br/
<hw>So</hw>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>Be as you are; stand still; stop;
that will do; right as you are; -- a word used esp. to cows; also used
by sailors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soak</ent><br/
<hw>Soak</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Soaked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Soaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>soken</ets>, AS.
<ets>socian</ets> to sioak, steep, fr. <ets>s<?/can</ets>,
<ets>s<?/gan</ets>, to suck. See <er>Suck</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To cause or suffer to lie in a fluid till the substance has imbibed
what it can contain; to macerate in water or other liquid; to steep, as
for the purpose of softening or freshening; <as>as, to <ex>soak</ex>
cloth; to <ex>soak</ex> bread; to <ex>soak</ex> salt meat, salt fish, or
the like.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To drench; to wet thoroughly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their land shall be <qex>soaked</qex> with blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xxiv. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To draw in by the pores, or through small passages;
<as>as, a sponge <ex>soaks</ex> up water; the skin <ex>soaks</ex> in
moisture.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make (its way) by entering pores or interstices;
-- often with <xex>through</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The rivulet beneath <qex>soaked</qex> its way obscurely through
wreaths of snow.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Fig.: To absorb; to drain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soak</ent><br/
<hw>Soak</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lie steeping in
water or other liquid; to become sturated; <as>as, let the cloth lie and
<ex>soak</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To enter (into something) by pores or interstices;
<as>as, water <ex>soaks</ex> into the earth or other porous
matter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To drink intemperately or gluttonously.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soakage</ent><br/
<hw>Soak"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of soaking,
or the state of being soaked; also, the quantity that enters or issues
by soaking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soaker</ent><br/
<hw>Soak"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, soaks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hard drinker.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soaking</ent><br/
<hw>Soak"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wetting thoroughly; drenching;
<as>as, a <ex>soaking</ex> rain</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Soak"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soaky</ent><br/
<hw>Soak"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of moisture; wet;
soppy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soal</ent><br/
<hw>Soal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The sole of a
shoe.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Sole</er>, the
fish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soal</ent><br/
<hw>Soal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sol</ets> mire. Cf.
<er>Sully</er>.]</ety> <def>A dirty pond.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soam</ent><br/
<hw>Soam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A chain by which a
leading horse draws a plow.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soap</ent><br/
<hw>Soap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sope</ets>, AS.
<ets>s<amac/pe</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zeep</ets>, G. <ets>seife</ets>,
OHG. <ets>seifa</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<amac/pa</ets>, Sw.
<ets>s<?/pa</ets>, Dan. <ets>s<?/be</ets>, and perhaps to AS.
<ets>s<imac/pan</ets> to drip, MHG. <ets>s<imac/fen</ets>, and L.
<ets>sebum</ets> tallow. Cf. <er>Saponaceous</er>.]</ety> <def>A
substance which dissolves in water, thus forming a lather, and is used
as a cleansing agent. Soap is produced by combining fats or oils with
alkalies or alkaline earths, usually by boiling, and consists of salts
of sodium, potassium, etc., with the fatty acids (oleic, stearic,
palmitic, etc.). See the Note below, and cf. <er>Saponification</er>. By
extension, any compound of similar composition or properties, whether
used as a cleaning agent or not.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In general, soaps are of two classes, <xex>hard</xex>
and <xex>soft</xex>. Calcium, magnesium, lead, etc., form soaps, but
they are insoluble and useless.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The purifying action of <qex>soap</qex> depends upon the fact that
it is decomposed by a large quantity of water into free alkali and an
insoluble acid salt. The first of these takes away the fatty dirt on
washing, and the latter forms the <qex>soap</qex> lather which envelops
the greasy matter and thus tends to remove it.</q> <rj><qau>Roscoe &
Schorlemmer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Castile soap</b></col>, <cd>a fine-grained hard soap,
white or mottled, made of olive oil and soda; -- called also
<altname>Marseilles soap</altname> <it>or</it> <altname>Venetian
soap</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Hard soap</b></col>, <cd>any one of a
great variety of soaps, of different ingredients and color, which are
hard and compact. All solid soaps are of this class.</cd> --
<col><b>Lead soap</b></col>, <cd>an insoluble, white, pliable soap made
by saponifying an oil (olive oil) with lead oxide; -- used externally in
medicine. Called also <altname>lead plaster</altname>,
<altname>diachylon</altname>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Marine
soap</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Marine</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pills of
soap</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>pills containing soap and
opium.</cd> -- <col><b>Potash soap</b></col>, <cd>any soap made with
potash, esp. the soft soaps, and a hard soap made from potash and castor
oil.</cd> -- <col><b>Pumice soap</b></col>, <cd>any hard soap charged
with a gritty powder, as silica, alumina, powdered pumice, etc., which
assists mechanically in the removal of dirt.</cd> -- <col><b>Resin
soap</b></col>, <cd>a yellow soap containing resin, -- used in
bleaching.</cd> -- <col><b>Silicated soap</b></col>, <cd>a cheap soap
containing water glass (sodium silicate).</cd> -- <col><b>Soap
bark</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Quillaia bark</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Soap bubble</b></col>, <cd>a hollow iridescent globe, formed
by blowing a film of soap suds from a pipe; figuratively, something
attractive, but extremely unsubstantial.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>soap bubble</qex> of the metaphysicians.</q> <rj><qau>J.
C. Shairp.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Soap cerate</b></col>, <cd>a cerate formed of soap, olive
oil, white wax, and the subacetate of lead, sometimes used as an
application to allay inflammation.</cd> -- <col><b>Soap fat</b></col>,
<cd>the refuse fat of kitchens, slaughter houses, etc., used in making
soap.</cd> -- <col><b>Soap liniment</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
liniment containing soap, camphor, and alcohol.</cd> -- <col><b>Soap
nut</b></col>, <cd>the hard kernel or seed of the fruit of the soapberry
tree, -- used for making beads, buttons, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Soap
plant</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>one of several plants used in the
place of soap, as the <spn>Chlorogalum pomeridianum</spn>, a California
plant, the bulb of which, when stripped of its husk and rubbed on wet
clothes, makes a thick lather, and smells not unlike new brown soap. It
is called also <altname>soap apple</altname>, <altname>soap
bulb</altname>, and <altname>soap weed</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Soap
tree</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Same as <er>Soapberry
tree</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Soda soap</b></col>, <cd>a soap containing a
sodium salt. The soda soaps are all hard soaps.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft
soap</b></col>, <cd>a soap of a gray or brownish yellow color, and of a
slimy, jellylike consistence, made from potash or the lye from wood
ashes. It is strongly alkaline and often contains glycerin, and is used
in scouring wood, in cleansing linen, in dyehouses, etc. Figuratively,
flattery; wheedling; blarney.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> --
<col><b>Toilet soap</b></col>, <cd>hard soap for the toilet, usually
colored and perfumed.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Soap</ent><br/
<hw>Soap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Soaped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Soaping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To rub or wash over
with soap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To flatter; to wheedle.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soapberry tree</ent><br/
<hw>Soap"ber`ry tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any tree
of the genus <gen>Sapindus</gen>, esp. <spn>Sapindus saponaria</spn>,
the fleshy part of whose fruit is used instead of soap in washing linen;
-- also called <altname>soap tree</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soapfish</ent><br/
<hw>Soap"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any serranoid fish of the genus <gen>Rhypticus</gen>; -- so called
from the soapy feeling of its skin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soapiness</ent><br/
<hw>Soap"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state
of being soapy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soaproot</ent><br/
<hw>Soap"root`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
perennial herb (<spn>Gypsophila Struthium</spn>) the root of which is
used in Spain as a substitute for soap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soapstone</ent><br/
<hw>Soap"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Steatite</er>, and <er>Talc</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soapsuds</ent><br/
<hw>Soap"suds`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Suds made with
soap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soapwort</ent><br/
<hw>Soap"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
common plant (<spn>Saponaria officinalis</spn>) of the Pink family; --
so called because its bruised leaves, when agitated in water, produce a
lather like that from soap. Called also <altname>Bouncing
Bet</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soapy</ent><br/
<hw>Soap"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Soapier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Soapiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Resembling soap;
having the qualities of, or feeling like, soap; soft and
smooth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Smeared with soap; covered with soap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soar</ent><br/
<hw>Soar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Soared</conjf> <pr>(#)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Soaring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>s'essorer</ets> to soar,
<ets>essorer</ets> to dry (by exposing to the air), fr. L. <ets>ex</ets>
out + <ets>aura</ets> the air, a breeze; akin to Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fly aloft, as a bird; to
mount upward on wings, or as on wings.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When <qex>soars</qex> Gaul's vulture with his wings unfurled.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To rise in thought, spirits, or imagination;
to be exalted in mood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where the deep transported mind may <qex>soar</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Valor <qex>soars</qex> above<br/
What the world calls misfortune.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Aeronautics)</fld> <def>To fly by wind power; to
glide indefinitely without loss of altitude.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soar</ent><br/
<hw>Soar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of soaring; upward
flight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This apparent <qex>soar</qex> of the hooded falcon.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soar</ent><br/
<hw>Soar</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See 3d <er>Sore</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soar</ent><br/
<hw>Soar</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Sore</er>, reddish
brown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soar falcon</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Sore falcon</cref>, under <er>Sore</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soaring</ent><br/
<hw>Soar"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Soar</er>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Soar"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soave</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*a"ve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Sweet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soavemente</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*a`ve*men"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<ety>[It.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Sweetly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sob</ent><br/
<hw>Sob</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/b)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sop</er>.]</ety> <def>To soak.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sob</ent><br/
<hw>Sob</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sobbed</conjf> <pr>(s<ocr/bd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sobbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sobben</ets>; akin to
AS. <ets>se<oacute/fian</ets>, <ets>si<oacute/fian</ets>, to complain,
bewail, <ets>se<oacute/fung</ets>, <ets>si<oacute/fung</ets>, sobbing,
lamentation; cf. OHG. <ets>s<umac/ft<omac/n</ets>,
<ets>s<umac/ftj<omac/n</ets>, to sigh, MHG. <ets>siuften</ets>,
<ets>siufzen</ets>, G. <ets>seufzen</ets>, MHG. <ets>s<umac/ft</ets> a
sigh, properly, a drawing in of breath, from <ets>s<umac/fen</ets> to
drink, OHG. <ets>s<umac/fan</ets>. Cf. <er>Sup</er>.]</ety> <def>To sigh
with a sudden heaving of the breast, or with a kind of convulsive
motion; to sigh with tears, and with a convulsive drawing in of the
breath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sobbing</qex> is the same thing [as sighing], stronger.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She sighed, she <qex>sobbed</qex>, and, furious with despair.<br/
She rent her garments, and she tore her hair.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sob</ent><br/
<hw>Sob</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sobbing; a
convulsive sigh, or inspiration of the breath, as in sorrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Break, heart, or choke with <qex>sobs</qex> my hated breath.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any sorrowful cry or sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tremulous <qex>sob</qex> of the complaining owl.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sobbing</ent><br/
<hw>Sob"bing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A series of short,
convulsive inspirations, the glottis being suddenly closed so that
little or no air enters into the lungs.</def> <-- a less intense form of
crying --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sober</ent><br/
<hw>Sober</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Soberer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Soberest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sobre</ets>, F.
<ets>sobre</ets>, from L. <ets>sobrius</ets>, probably from a prefix
<ets>so-</ets> expressing separation + <ets>ebrius</ets> drunken. Cf.
<er>Ebriety</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Temperate in the use of
spirituous liquors; habitually temperate; <as>as, a <ex>sober</ex>
man</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That we may hereafter live a godly, righteous, and
<qex>sober</qex> life, to the glory of Thy holy name.</q> <rj><qau>Bk.
of Com. Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not intoxicated or excited by spirituous liquors;
<as>as, the sot may at times be <ex>sober</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not mad or insane; not wild, visionary, or heated
with passion; exercising cool, dispassionate reason; self-controlled;
self-possessed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was not a <qex>sober</qex> person to be had; all was
tempestuous and blustering.</q> <rj><au>Druden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No <qex>sober</qex> man would put himself into danger for the
applause of escaping without breaking his neck.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1365 --></p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not proceeding from, or attended with, passion;
calm; <as>as, <ex>sober</ex> judgment; a man in his <ex>sober</ex>
senses.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Serious or subdued in demeanor, habit, appearance,
or color; solemn; grave; sedate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What parts gay France from <qex>sober</qex> Spain?</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>See her <qex>sober</qex> over a sampler, or gay over a jointed
baby.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Twilight gray<br/
Had in her <qex>sober</qex> livery all things clad.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Grave; temperate; abstinent; abstemious;
moderate; regular; steady; calm; quiet; cool; collected; dispassionate;
unimpassioned; sedate; staid; serious; solemn; somber. See
<er>Grave</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sober</ent><br/
<hw>So"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sobered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sobering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make sober.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There shallow draughts intoxicate the brain,<br/
And drinking largely <qex>sobers</qex> us again.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sober</ent><br/
<hw>So"ber</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become sober; -- often with
<xex>down</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Vance gradually <qex>sobered</qex> down.</q> <rj><qau>Ld.
Lytton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soberize</ent><br/
<hw>So"ber*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To
sober.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Crabbe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soberly</ent><br/
<hw>So"ber*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sober manner; temperately;
cooly; calmly; gravely; seriously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soberly</ent><br/
<hw>So"ber*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Grave; serious; solemn;
sad.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] looked hollow and thereto <qex>soberly</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sober-minded</ent><br/
<hw>So"ber-mind`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
disposition or temper habitually sober.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>So"ber-mind`ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soberness</ent><br/
<hw>So"ber*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sober.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soboles</ent><br/
<hw>Sob"o*les</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a short.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A shoot running along under ground,
forming new plants at short distances.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A sucker,
as of tree or shrub.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soboliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sob`o*lif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>soboles + -ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Producing
soboles. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Houseleek</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sobranje</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*bran"je</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Bulgarian,
lit., assembly.]</ety> <def>The unicameral national assembly of
Bulgaria, elected for a term of five years by universal suffrage of
adult males.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sobriety</ent><br/
<hw>So*bri"e*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sobrietas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sobri<eacute/t<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Sober</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Habitual soberness or temperance
as to the use of spirituous liquors; <as>as, a man of
<ex>sobriety</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Public <qex>sobriety</qex> is a relative duty.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Habitual freedom from enthusiasm, inordinate
passion, or overheated imagination; calmness; coolness; gravity;
seriousness; <as>as, the <ex>sobriety</ex> of riper
years</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mirth makes them not mad,<br/
Nor <qex>sobriety</qex> sad.</q> <rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Soberness; temperance; abstinence;
abstemiousness; moderation; regularity; steadness; calmness; coolness;
sober-mindeness; sedateness; staidness; gravity; seriousness;
solemnity.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sobriquet</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So`bri`quet"</hw> <pr>(s<osl/`br<esl/`k<asl/")</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos><ety>[F. <ets>sobriquet</ets>, OF. <ets>soubzbriquet</ets>,
<ets>soubriquet</ets>, a chuck under the chin, hence, an affront, a
nickname; of uncertain origin; cf. It. <ets>sottobecco</ets> a chuck
under the chin.]</ety> <def>An assumed name; a fanciful epithet or
appellation; a nickname.</def> <altsp>[Sometimes less correctly written
<asp>soubriquet</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soc</ent><br/
<hw>Soc</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<omac/c</ets> the power of holding court, sway, domain, properly,
the right of investigating or seeking; akin to E. <ets>sake</ets>,
<ets>seek</ets>. <er>Sake</er>, <er>Seek</er>, and cf. <er>Sac</er>, and
<er>Soke</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sock</asp>, and
<asp>soke</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The lord's power or privilege of holding a court in a
district, as in manor or lordship; jurisdiction of causes, and the
limits of that jurisdiction.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Liberty or
privilege of tenants excused from customary burdens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An exclusive privilege formerly claimed by millers
of grinding all the corn used within the manor or township which the
mill stands.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soc and sac</b></col> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>the
full right of administering justice in a manor or
lordship.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socage</ent><br/
<hw>Soc"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[From <er>Soc</er>; cf.
LL. <ets>socagium</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(O.Eng. Law)</fld> <def>A tenure of
lands and tenements by a certain or determinate service; a tenure
distinct from chivalry or knight's service, in which the obligations
were uncertain. The service must be certain, in order to be denominated
<ex>socage</ex>, as to hold by fealty and twenty shillings rent.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>soccage</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ex>Socage</ex> is of two kinds; <stype>free
socage</stype>, where the services are not only certain, but honorable;
and <stype>villein socage</stype>, where the services, though certain,
are of a baser nature.</note> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socager</ent><br/
<hw>Soc"a*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>A tennant by socage; a socman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>So-called</ent><br/
<hw>So"-called`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>So named; called
by such a name (but perhaps called thus with doubtful
propriety).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociability</ent><br/
<hw>So`cia*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sociabilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality of being
sociable; sociableness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociable</ent><br/
<hw>So"cia*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>sociabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>sociare</ets> to associate, fr.
<ets>socius</ets> a companion. See <er>Social</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of being, or fit to be, united in one body or company;
associable.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are <qex>sociable</qex> parts united into one body.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inclined to, or adapted for, society; ready to unite
with others; fond of companions; social.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Society is no comfort to one not <qex>sociable</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What can be more uneasy to this <qex>sociable</qex> creature than
the dry, pensive retirements of solitude?</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Ready to converse; inclined to talk with others; not
taciturn or reserved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Affording opportunites for conversation;
characterized by much conversation; <as>as, a <ex>sociable</ex>
party</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>No longer hostile; friendly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sociable bird</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sociable weaver</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
weaver bird which builds composite nests. See <er>Republican</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 3. <sd>(b)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Social; companionable; conversible; friendly;
familiar; communicative; accessible.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociable</ent><br/
<hw>So"cia*ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A gathering of
people for social purposes; an informal party or reception; <as>as, a
church <ex>sociable</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A carriage having two double seats facing each
other, and a box for the driver.</def> <rj><au>Miss
Edgeworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociableness</ent><br/
<hw>So"cia*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
sociable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociably</ent><br/
<hw>So"cia*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sociable
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Social</ent><br/
<hw>So"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>socialis</ets>, from <ets>socius</ets> a companion; akin to
<ets>sequi</ets> to follow: cf. F. <ets>social</ets>. See <er>Sue</er>
to follow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to society; relating
to men living in society, or to the public as an aggregate body; <as>as,
<ex>social</ex> interest or concerns; <ex>social</ex> pleasure;
<ex>social</ex> benefits; <ex>social</ex> happiness; <ex>social</ex>
duties.</as></def> <ldquo/<xex>Social</xex> phenomena.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J.
S. Mill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Ready or disposed to mix in friendly converse;
companionable; sociable; <as>as, a <ex>social</ex>
person</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Consisting in union or mutual intercourse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Best with thyself accompanied, seek'st not<br/
<qex>Social</qex> communication.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Naturally growing in groups or
masses; -- said of many individual plants of the same species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Living in
communities consisting of males, females, and neuters, as do ants and
most bees.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Forming compound groups or colonies
by budding from basal processes or stolons; <as>as, the <ex>social</ex>
ascidians</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Social science</b></col>, <cd>the science of all that
relates to the social condition, the relations and institutions which
are involved in man's existence and his well-being as a member of an
organized community; sociology. It concerns itself with questions of the
public health, education, labor, punishment of crime, reformation of
criminals, and the like.</cd> -- <col><b>Social whale</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the blackfish.</cd> -- <col><b>The social
evil</b></col>, <cd>prostitution.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sociable; companionable; conversible; friendly;
familiar; communicative; convival; festive.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socialism</ent><br/
<hw>So"cial*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>socialisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>A theory or system of social reform
which contemplates a complete reconstruction of society, with a more
just and equitable distribution of property and labor. In popular usage,
the term is often employed to indicate any lawless, revolutionary social
scheme. See <er>Communism</er>, <er>Fourierism</er>,
<er>Saint-Simonianism</er>, forms of socialism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[<qex>Socialism</qex>] was first applied in England to Owen's
theory of social reconstruction, and in France to those also of St.
Simon and Fourier . . . The word, however, is used with a great variety
of meaning, . . . even by economists and learned critics. The general
tendency is to regard as socialistic any interference undertaken by
society on behalf of the poor, . . . radical social reform which
disturbs the present system of private property . . . The tendency of
the present <qex>socialism</qex> is more and more to ally itself with
the most advanced democracy.</q> <rj><qau>Encyc. Brit.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We certainly want a true history of <qex>socialism</qex>, meaning
by that a history of every systematic attempt to provide a new social
existence for the mass of the workers.</q> <rj><qau>F.
Harrison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs>-- <col><b>Socialism of the chair</b></col> <ety>[G.
<ets>katheder socialismus</ets>]</ety>, <cd>a term applied about 1872,
at first in ridicule, to a group of German political economists who
advocated state aid for the betterment of the working
classes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socialist</ent><br/
<hw>So"cial*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>socialiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who advocates or practices the
doctrines of socialism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socialistic</ent><br/
<ent>Socialist</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So"cial*ist</hw>, <hw>So`cial*is"tic</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or of the nature of,
socialism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociality</ent><br/
<hw>So`ci*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>socialist<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>socialitas</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
quality of being social; socialness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socialize</ent><br/
<hw>So"cial*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
render social.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To subject to, or regulate by, socialism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socially</ent><br/
<hw>So"cial*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a social manner;
sociably.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socialness</ent><br/
<hw>So"cial*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
social.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociate</ent><br/
<hw>So"ci*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sociatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sociare</ets> to associate, fr.
<ets>socius</ets> companion.]</ety> <def>Associated.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociate</ent><br/
<hw>So"ci*ate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An associate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As for you, Dr. Reynolds, and your <qex>sociates</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociate</ent><br/
<hw>So"ci*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To
associate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shelford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Societarian</ent><br/
<hw>So*ci`e*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to society; social.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The all-sweeping besom of <qex>societarian</qex> reformation.</q>
<rj><qau>Lamb.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Societary</ent><br/
<hw>So*ci"e*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Societarian.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Society</ent><br/
<hw>So*ci"e*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Societies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>societas</ets>,
fr. <ets>socius</ets> a companion: cf. F.
<ets>soci<eacute/t<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Social</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The relationship of men to one another when associated
in any way; companionship; fellowship; company.</def> <ldquo/Her loved
<xex>society</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is <qex>society</qex> where none intrudes<br/
By the deep sea, and music in its roar.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Connection; participation; partnership.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The meanest of the people and such as have the least
<qex>society</qex> with the acts and crimes of kings.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A number of persons associated for any temporary or
permanent object; an association for mutual or joint usefulness,
pleasure, or profit; a social union; a partnership; <as>as, a missionary
<ex>society</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The persons, collectively considered, who live in
any region or at any period; any community of individuals who are united
together by a common bond of nearness or intercourse; those who
recognize each other as associates, friends, and
acquaintances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Specifically, the more cultivated portion of any
community in its social relations and influences; those who mutually
give receive formal entertainments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Society of Jesus</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Jesuit</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Society verses</b></col> <ety>[a translation of F. <ets>vers
de soci<eacute/t<eacute/</ets>]</ety>, <cd>the lightest kind of lyrical
poetry; verses for the amusement of polite society.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socinian</ent><br/
<hw>So*cin"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to <xex>Socinus</xex>, or the Socinians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socinian</ent><br/
<hw>So*cin"i*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the followers of
Socinus; a believer in Socinianism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socinianism</ent><br/
<hw>So*cin"i*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>The tenets or doctrines of Faustus Socinus, an Italian
theologian of the sixteenth century, who denied the Trinity, the deity
of Christ, the personality of the Devil, the native and total depravity
of man, the vicarious atonement, and the eternity of future punishment.
His theory was, that Christ was a man divinely commissioned, who had no
existence before he was conceived by the Virgin Mary; that human sin was
the imitation of Adam's sin, and that human salvation was the imitation
and adoption of Christ's virtue; that the Bible was to be interpreted by
human reason; and that its language was metaphorical, and not to be
taken literally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socinianize</ent><br/
<hw>So*cin"i*an*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Socinianized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Socinianizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To cause to conform to Socinianism; to
regulate by, or imbue with, the principles of Socinianism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociological</ent><br/
<ent>Sociologic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So`ci*o*log"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>So`ci*o*log"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr> }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to sociology,
or social science.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>So`ci*o*log"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociologist</ent><br/
<hw>So`ci*ol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who treats
of, or devotes himself to, the study of sociology.</def> <rj><au>J. S.
Mill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sociology</ent><br/
<hw>So`ci*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>socius</ets> a companion + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That
branch of philosophy which treats of the constitution, phenomena, and
development of human society; social science.</def> <rj><au>H.
Spencer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sock</ent><br/
<hw>Sock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>soc</ets>, LL.
<ets>soccus</ets>, perhaps of Celtic origin.]</ety> <def>A
plowshare.</def> <rj><au>Edin. Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sock</ent><br/
<hw>Sock</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sock</ets>, AS.
<ets>socc</ets>, fr. L. <ets>soccus</ets> a kind of low-heeled, light
shoe. Cf. <er>Sucket</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The shoe worn by
actors of comedy in ancient Greece and Rome, -- used as a symbol of
comedy, or of the comic drama, as distinguished from tragedy, which is
symbolized by the <contr>buskin</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Great Fletcher never treads in buskin here,<br/
Nor greater Jonson dares in <qex>socks</qex> appear.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A knit or woven covering for the foot and lower leg;
a stocking with a short leg.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A warm inner sole for a shoe.</def>
<rj><au>Simmonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sock</ent><br/
<hw>Sock</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/k)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Perh. shortened
fr. <ets>sockdolager</ets>.]</ety> <def>To hurl, drive, or strike
violently; -- often with <xex>it</xex> as an object.</def> <mark>[Prov.
or Vulgar]</mark> <rj><au>Kipling.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sock away</ent><br/
<hw>Sock away</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>to save (money);
to put (something) into a reserve store.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sock in</ent><br/
<hw>Sock in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>to cause (an
airport) to cease functioning, by severe weather conditions, such as fog
or a snowstorm. Used often in the passive; <as>the airport was
<ex>socked in</ex> for two days by a blizzard</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sockdolager</ent><br/
<hw>Sock*dol"a*ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A corruption
of <ets>doxology</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sockdologer</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which finishes or
ends a matter; a settler; a poser, as a heavy blow, a conclusive answer,
and the like.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Angling)</fld> <def>A combination of two hooks
which close upon each other, by means of a spring, as soon as the fish
bites.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something unusually large.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socket</ent><br/
<hw>Sock"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>soket</ets>,
a dim. through OF. fr. L. <ets>soccus</ets>. See <er>Sock</er> a
covering for the foot.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An opening into which
anything is fitted; any hollow thing or place which receives and holds
something else; <as>as, the <ex>sockets</ex> of the
teeth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His eyeballs in their hollow <qex>sockets</qex> sink.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, the hollow tube or place in which a
candle is fixed in the candlestick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Electricity)</fld> <def>the receptacle of an
electric lamp into which a light bulb is inserted, containing contacts
to conduct electricity to the bulb.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Electricity)</fld> <def>the receptacle fixed in a
wall and connected by conductive wiring to an electrical supply,
containing contacts to conduct electricity, and into which the plug of
an electrical device is inserted; -- called also a <altname>wall
socket</altname> or <altname>outlet</altname>. The <ex>socket</ex> will
typically have two or three contacts; if three, the third is connected
to a ground for safety.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>And in the <qex>sockets</qex> oily bubbles dance.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Socket bolt</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a bolt that
passes through a thimble that is placed between the parts connected by
the bolt.</cd> -- <col><b>Socket chisel</b></col>. <cd>Same as
<altname>Framing chisel</altname>. See under <er>Framing</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Socket pipe</b></col>, <cd>a pipe with an expansion at one end
to receive the end of a connecting pipe.</cd> -- <col><b>Socket
pole</b></col>, <cd>a pole armed with iron fixed on by means of a
socket, and used to propel boats, etc.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> --
<col><b>Socket wrench</b></col>, <cd>a wrench consisting of a socket at
the end of a shank or rod, for turning a nut, bolthead, etc., in a
narrow or deep recess.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socketed</ent><br/
<hw>Sock"et*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
socket.</def> <rj><au>Dawkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sockless</ent><br/
<hw>Sock"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of socks or
shoes.</def> <rj><au>B. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socky</ent><br/
<hw>Sock"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wet; soaky.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socle</ent><br/
<hw>So"cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>socculus</ets>, dim. of <ets>soccus</ets>. See <er>Sock</er> a
covering for the foot. Cf. <er>Zocco</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A plain block or plinth forming a low pedestal; any
base; especially, the base of a statue, column, or the like. See
<er>Plinth</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A plain face or plinth at the
lower part of a wall.</def> <rj><au>Oxf. Gloss.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socman</ent><br/
<hw>Soc"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Socmen</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See <er>Socage</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>One who holds lands or tenements by
socage; a socager.</def> <rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socmanry</ent><br/
<hw>Soc"man*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(O.E. Law)</fld>
<def>Tenure by socage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socome</ent><br/
<hw>Soc"ome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<omac/cen</ets>, <ets>s<omac/cn</ets>, searching, or the right of
searching, the lord's court. See <er>Soc</er>.]</ety> <fld>(O.Eng.
Law)</fld> <def>A custom of tenants to grind corn at the lord's
mill.</def> <rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socotrine</ent><br/
<hw>Soc"o*trine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to Socotra, an island in the Indian Ocean, on the east coast of
Africa.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Socotra.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socratical</ent><br/
<ent>Socratic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So*crat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>So*crat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Socraticus</ets>, Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Socrates, the Grecian sage
and teacher. (<sc>b. c.</sc> 469-399), or to his manner of teaching and
philosophizing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>Socratic</xex> method of reasoning and
instruction was by a series of questions leading the one to whom they
were addressed to perceive and admit what was true or false in doctrine,
or right or wrong in conduct.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socratically</ent><br/
<hw>So*crat"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the Socratic
method.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socratism</ent><br/
<hw>Soc"ra*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The philosophy or
the method of Socrates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Socratist</ent><br/
<hw>Soc"ra*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <def>A disciple or follower of Socrates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sod</ent><br/
<hw>Sod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
rock dove.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sod</ent><br/
<hw>Sod</hw>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Seethe</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sod</ent><br/
<hw>Sod</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to LG. <ets>sode</ets>, D.
<ets>zode</ets>, OD. <ets>sode</ets>, <ets>soode</ets>, OFries. satha,
and E. <ets>seethe</ets>. So named from its <ets>sodden</ets> state in
wet weather. See <er>Seethe</er>.]</ety> <def>That stratum of the
surface of the soil which is filled with the roots of grass, or any
portion of that surface; turf; sward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She there shall dress a sweeter <qex>sod</qex><br/
Than Fancy's feet have ever trod.</q> <rj><qau>Collins.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sod</ent><br/
<hw>Sod</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sodden</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sodding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cover with sod; to
turf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soda</ent><br/
<hw>So"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., soda, in OIt.,
ashes used in making glass, fr. L. <ets>solida</ets>, fem. of
<ets>solidus</ets> solid; <ets>solida</ets> having probably been a name
of glasswort. See <er>Solid</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Sodium oxide or hydroxide.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Popularly, sodium carbonate or bicarbonate. Sodium bicarbonate is
also called <altname>baking soda</altname></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>same as <er>sodium</er>, used in terms such as
<cref>bicarbonate of soda</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>same as <er>soda water</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>a non-alcoholic beverage, sweetened by various
means, containing flavoring and supersaturated with carbon dioxide, so
as to be effervescent when the container is opened; -- in different
localities it is variously called also <altname>soda pop</altname>,
<altname>pop</altname>, <altname>mineral water</altname>, and
<altname>minerals</altname>. It has many variants. The sweetening agent
may be natural, such as cane sugar or corn syrup, or artificial, such as
saccharin or aspartame. The flavoring varies widely, popular variants
being fruit or cola flavoring.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Caustic soda</b></col>, <cd>sodium hydroxide.</cd> --
<col><b>Cooking soda</b></col>, <cd>sodium bicarbonate.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <col><b>Sal soda</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Sodium carbonate</cref>, under <er>Sodium</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Soda alum</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a mineral consisting
of the hydrous sulphate of alumina and soda.</cd> -- <col><b>Soda
ash</b></col>, <cd>crude sodium carbonate; -- so called because formerly
obtained from the ashes of sea plants and certain other plants, as
saltwort (<gen>Salsola</gen>). See under <er>Sodium</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Soda fountain</b></col>, <cd>an apparatus for drawing soda
water, fitted with delivery tube, faucets, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Soda
lye</b></col>, <cd>a lye consisting essentially of a solution of sodium
hydroxide, used in soap making.</cd> -- <col><b>Soda niter</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Nitratine</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Soda salts</b></col>,
<cd>salts having sodium for the base; specifically, sodium sulphate or
Glauber's salts.</cd> -- <col><b>Soda waste</b></col>, <cd>the waste
material, consisting chiefly of calcium hydroxide and sulphide, which
accumulates as a useless residue or side product in the ordinary Leblanc
process of soda manufacture; -- called also <altname>alkali
waste</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Washing soda</b></col>, <cd>sodium
carbonate.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1366 --></p>

<p><ent>Soda biscuit</ent><br/
<hw>So"da biscuit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a bicuit
leavened with baking soda and with buttermilk or sour milk.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soda bread</ent><br/
<hw>So"da bread</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a bread made with
buttermilk and leavened with baking soda.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soda fountain</ent><br/
<hw>So"da foun`tain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a counter at
which people may sit and be served soda pop, ice cream, or light meals.
Such counters may be located for example in restaurants, ice cream
shops, drugstores, departments stores.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodaic</ent><br/
<hw>So*da"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or
containing, soda.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sodaic</xex> powder.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soda jerk</ent><br/
<hw>So"da jerk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a person who serves
soda, ice cream, similar foods from behind a <er>soda
fountain</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodalite</ent><br/
<hw>So"da*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Soda</ets> +
<ets>-lite</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sodalithe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A mineral of a white to blue or gray color, occuring commonly in
dodecahedrons, also massive. It is a silicate of alumina and soda with
some chlorine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodality</ent><br/
<hw>So*dal"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sodalities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>sodalitas</ets>,
fr. <ets>sodalis</ets> a comrade.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A fellowship
or fraternity; a brotherhood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>Specifically, a lay
association for devotion or for charitable purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodamide</ent><br/
<hw>Sod*am"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A greenish or reddish crystalline substance,
<chform>NaNH2</chform>, obtained by passing ammonia over heated
sodium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soda water</ent><br/
<hw>So"da wat`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>originally, a beverage consisting of a weak solution of sodium
bicarbonate to which an acid (such as phosphoric acid) has been added to
generate carbon dioxide and thus cause effervescence; the term now is
also used as a synonym for <altname>soda pop</altname>. Soda water
without flavoring is used as a beverage or as a component of a mixed
beverage. It is typically bottled or canned under pressure so that the
carbon dioxide and effervescence will be preserved until use.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a beverage containing supersaturated carbon dioxide
(carbonic acid) and thus effervescent.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>same as <er>soda pop</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soda pop</ent><br/
<hw>So"da pop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a popular
non-alcoholic beverage, sweetened by various means, containing flavoring
and supersaturated with carbon dioxide, so as to be effervescent when
the container is opened; -- in different localities it is variously
called also <altname>soda</altname>, <altname>pop</altname>,
<altname>mineral water</altname>, and <altname>minerals</altname>. It
has many variants. The sweetening agent may be natural, such as cane
sugar or corn syrup, or artificial, such as saccharin or aspartame. The
flavoring varies widely, popular variants being fruit juices, fruit
sirups, cream, or cola flavoring; the <ex>soda pop</ex> is usually
served chilled.</def> <note>Several large corporations started primarily
as bottlers of <ex>soda pop</ex>, such as <stype>Coca-Cola</stype>,
<stype>Pepsi-Cola</stype>, and <stype>Dr. Pepper</stype>.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodden</ent><br/
<hw>Sod"den</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> [<pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Seethe</er>.] <def>Boiled; seethed; also, soaked; heavy with
moisture; saturated; <as>as, <ex>sodden</ex> beef; <ex>sodden</ex>
bread; <ex>sodden</ex> fields</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodden</ent><br/
<hw>Sod"den</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be seethed; to become
sodden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodden</ent><br/
<hw>Sod"den</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To soak; to make heavy with
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodden-witted</ent><br/
<hw>Sod"den-wit`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Heavy;
dull.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soddy</ent><br/
<hw>Sod"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sod</er>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of sod; covered with sod;
turfy.</def> <rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soder</ent><br/
<hw>Sod"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Solder</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodger</ent><br/
<hw>So"dger</hw> <pr>(s<omac/"j<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n. & v. i.</pos>
<def>Variant of <er>Soldier</er>.</def> <mark>[Dial. or
Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodic</ent><br/
<hw>So"dic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to sodium; containing sodium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodio-</ent><br/
<hw>So"di*o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A combining form
(also used adjectively) denoting <xex>the presence of sodium</xex> or
<xex>one of its compounds</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodium</ent><br/
<hw>So"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.E.
<ets>soda</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A common metallic
element of the alkali group, in nature always occuring combined, as in
common salt, in albite, etc. It is isolated as a soft, waxy, white,
unstable metal, so highly reactive that it combines violently with
water, and to be preserved must be kept under petroleum or some similar
liquid. Sodium is used combined in many salts, in the free state as a
reducer, and as a means of obtaining other metals (as magnesium and
aluminium) is an important commercial product. Symbol Na
(<altname>Natrium</altname>). Atomic weight 22.990. Specific gravity
0.97.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sodium amalgam</b></col>, <cd>an alloy of sodium and
mercury, usually produced as a gray metallic crystalline substance,
which is used as a reducing agent, and otherwise.</cd> -- <col><b>Sodium
carbonate</b></col>, <cd>a white crystalline substance,
<chform>Na2CO3.10H2O</chform>, having a cooling alkaline taste, found in
the ashes of many plants, and produced artifically in large quantities
from common salt. It is used in making soap, glass, paper, etc., and as
alkaline agent in many chemical industries. Called also <altname>sal
soda</altname>, <altname>washing soda</altname>, or
<altname>soda</altname>. Cf. <er>Sodium bicarbonate</er>, and
<er>Trona</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sodium chloride</b></col>, <cd>common,
or table, salt, <chform>NaCl</chform>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sodium
hydroxide</b></col>, <cd>a white opaque brittle solid,
<chform>NaOH</chform>, having a fibrous structure, produced by the
action of quicklime, or of calcium hydrate (milk of lime), on sodium
carbonate. It is a strong alkali, and is used in the manufacture of
soap, in making wood pulp for paper, etc. Called also <altname>sodium
hydrate</altname>, and <altname>caustic soda</altname>. By extension, a
solution of sodium hydroxide.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodium bicarbonate</ent><br/
<hw>Sodium bicarbonate</hw>, <def>a white crystalline substance,
<chform>HNaCO3</chform>, with a slight alkaline taste resembling that of
sodium carbonate. It is found in many mineral springs and also produced
artificially,. It is used in cookery, in baking powders, and as a source
of carbonic acid gas (carbon dioxide) for soda water. Called also
<altname>baking soda</altname>, <altname>cooking soda</altname>,
<altname>bicarbonate of soda</altname>, <altname>bicarb</altname>,
<altname>saleratus</altname>, and technically, <altname>acid sodium
carbonate</altname>, <altname>sodium acid carbonate</altname>,
<altname>primary sodium carbonate</altname>, <altname>sodium
dicarbonate</altname>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodium sulphate</ent><br/
<hw>So"di*um sul"phate</hw>. <def>A salt well known as a catharic under
the name of <altname>Glauber's salt</altname>, which term is properly
applied to the hydrate, <chform>Na2SO4.10H2O</chform>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodomite</ent><br/
<hw>Sod"om*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
inhabitant of Sodom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One guilty of sodomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodomitical</ent><br/
<hw>Sod`om*it"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to,
or of the nature of, sodomy.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sod`om*it"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sodomy</ent><br/
<hw>Sod"om*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Sodom</ets>. a country mentioned in the Bible: cf. F.
<ets>sodomite</ets>.]</ety> <def>Carnal copulation in a manner against
nature; buggery.</def> <rj><au>Gen. xix. 5.</au></rj> <-- can we be more
explicit? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soe</ent><br/
<hw>Soe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot. <ets>sae</ets>,
<ets>say</ets>, <ets>saye</ets>; cf. Icel. <ets>s<amac/r</ets> a large
cask, Sw. <ets>s<?/</ets> a tub.]</ety> <def>A large wooden vessel for
holding water; a cowl.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soever</ent><br/
<hw>So*ev"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A word compounded of <xex>so</xex>
and <xex>ever</xex>, used in composition with <xex>who</xex>,
<xex>what</xex>, <xex>where</xex>, <xex>when</xex>, <xex>how</xex>,
etc., and indicating any out of all possible or supposable persons,
things, places, times, ways, etc. It is sometimes used separate from the
pronoun or adverb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For unto whom<qex>soever</qex> much is given, of him shall be much
required.</q> <rj><qau>Luke xii. 48.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What great thing <qex>soever</qex> a man proposed to do in his
life, he should think of achieving it by fifty.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sofa</ent><br/
<hw>So"fa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sofas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Ar. <ets>soffah</ets>, from
<ets>saffa</ets> to dispose in order: cf. F. <ets>sofa</ets>, It.
<ets>sof<agrave/</ets>.]</ety> <def>A long seat, usually with a
cushioned bottom, back, and ends; -- much used as a comfortable piece of
furniture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let fall the curtains, wheel the <qex>sofa</qex> round.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sofa bed</b></col>, <cd>a sofa so contrived that it may
be extended to form a bed; -- called also <altname>sofa
bedstead</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soffit</ent><br/
<hw>Sof"fit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>soffitta</ets>, <ets>soffitto</ets>, fr. <ets>soffiggere</ets> to
hide, properly, to fix or fasten under, L. <ets>suffigere</ets> to
fasten beneath or below; <ets>sub</ets> under, beneath +
<ets>figere</ets> to fix, faste: cf. F. <ets>soffite</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The under side of the subordinate parts and
members of buildings, such as staircases, entablatures, archways,
cornices, or the like. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Lintel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sofi</ent><br/
<hw>So"fi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sofis</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>Same as
<er>Sufi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sofism</ent><br/
<hw>So"fism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sufism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft</ent><br/
<hw>Soft</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/ft; 115)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Softer</adjf>
<pr>(s<ocr/ft"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Softest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>softe</ets>, AS.
<ets>s<omac/fte</ets>, properly adv. of <ets>s<emac/fte</ets>, adj.;
akin to OS. <ets>s<amac/fto</ets>, adv., D. <ets>zacht</ets>, OHG.
<ets>samfto</ets>, adv., <ets>semfti</ets>, adj., G. <ets>sanft</ets>,
LG. <ets>sacht</ets>; of uncertain origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Easily yielding to pressure; easily impressed, molded, or cut; not
firm in resisting; impressible; yielding; also, malleable; -- opposed to
<ant>hard</ant>; <as>as, a <ex>soft</ex> bed; a <ex>soft</ex> peach;
<ex>soft</ex> earth; <ex>soft</ex> wood or metal.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not rough, rugged, or harsh to the touch; smooth;
delicate; fine; <as>as, <ex>soft</ex> silk; a <ex>soft</ex>
skin.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They that wear <qex>soft</qex> clothing are in king's houses.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xi. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, agreeable to feel, taste, or inhale; not
irritating to the tissues; <as>as, a <ex>soft</ex> liniment;
<ex>soft</ex> wines</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>soft</xex>, delicious
air.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not harsh or offensive to the sight; not glaring;
pleasing to the eye; not exciting by intensity of color or violent
contrast; <as>as, <ex>soft</ex> hues or tints</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sun, shining upon the upper part of the clouds . . . made the
<qex>softest</qex> lights imaginable.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Not harsh or rough in sound; gentle and pleasing to
the ear; flowing; <as>as, <ex>soft</ex> whispers of
music</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her voice was ever <qex>soft</qex>,<br/
Gentle, and low, -- an excellent thing in woman.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Soft</qex> were my numbers; who could take offense?</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Easily yielding; susceptible to influence; flexible;
gentle; kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would to God my heart were flint, like Edward's;<br/
Or Edward's <qex>soft</qex> and pitiful, like mine.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The meek or <qex>soft</qex> shall inherit the earth.</q>
<rj><qau>Tyndale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Expressing gentleness, tenderness, or the like;
mild; conciliatory; courteous; kind; <as>as, <ex>soft</ex>
eyes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>soft</qex> answer turneth away wrath.</q> <rj><qau>Prov.
xv. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A face with gladness overspread,<br/
<qex>Soft</qex> smiles, by human kindness bred.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Effeminate; not courageous or manly, weak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A longing after sensual pleasures is a dissolution of the spirit
of a man, and makes it loose, <qex>soft</qex>, and wandering.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Gentle in action or motion; easy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On her <qex>soft</qex> axle, white she paces even,<br/
And bears thee soft with the smooth air along.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>Weak in character; impressible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The deceiver soon found this <qex>soft</qex> place of Adam's.</q>
<rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Somewhat weak in intellect.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He made <qex>soft</qex> fellows stark noddies, and such as were
foolish quite mad.</q> <rj><qau>Burton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>Quiet; undisturbed; paceful; <as>as, <ex>soft</ex>
slumbers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>Having, or consisting of, a gentle curve or curves;
not angular or abrupt; <as>as, <ex>soft</ex> outlines</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>Not tinged with mineral salts; adapted to decompose
soap; <as>as, <ex>soft</ex> water is the best for
washing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Applied to a
palatal, a sibilant, or a dental consonant (as <xex>g</xex> in
<xex>gem</xex>, <xex>c</xex> in <xex>cent</xex>, etc.) as distinguished
from a guttural mute (as <xex>g</xex> in <xex>go</xex>, <xex>c</xex> in
<xex>cone</xex>, etc.); -- opposed to <ant>hard</ant>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Belonging to the class of sonant elements as
distinguished from the surd, and considered as involving less force in
utterance; <as>as, <ex>b</ex>, <ex>d</ex>, <ex>g</ex>, <ex>z</ex>,
<ex>v</ex>, etc.</as>, in contrast with <xex>p</xex>, <xex>t</xex>,
<xex>k</xex>, <xex>s</xex>, <xex>f</xex>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soft clam</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the common or
long clam (<spn>Mya arenaria</spn>). See <er>Mya</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Soft coal</b></col>, <cd>bituminous coal, as distinguished from
<xex>anthracite</xex>, or <xex>hard, coal</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft
crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any crab which has recently shed
its shell.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft dorsal</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the posterior part of the dorsal fin of fishes when supported by
soft rays.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft grass</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Velvet grass</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft money</b></col>,
<cd>paper money, as distinguished from <xex>coin</xex>, or <xex>hard
money</xex>.</cd> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Soft
mute</b></col>. <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <cd>See <er>Media</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Soft palate</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Palate</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft ray</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a fin ray which is articulated and usually branched.</cd> --
<col><b>Soft soap</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Soap</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Soft-tack</b></col>, <cd>leavened bread, as distinguished from
<contr>hard-tack</contr>, or <contr>ship bread</contr>.</cd> --
<col><b>Soft tortoise</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any river
tortoise of the genus Trionyx. See <er>Trionyx</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft</ent><br/
<hw>Soft</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A soft or foolish person;
an idiot.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft</ent><br/
<hw>Soft</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Softly; without roughness or
harshness; gently; quietly.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A knight <qex>soft</qex> riding toward them.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft</ent><br/
<hw>Soft</hw>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>Be quiet; hold; stop; not so
fast.</def><-- archaic or obs. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Soft</qex>, you; a word or two before you go.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Softa</ent><br/
<hw>Sof"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corruption of Per.
<ets>s<omac/khtah</ets> one who burns, is ardent or zealous.]</ety>
<def>Any one attached to a Mohammedan mosque, esp. a student of the
higher branches of theology in a mosque school.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>sophta</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soften</ent><br/
<hw>Sof"ten</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Softened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Softening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make soft or more
soft.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>To render less hard; -- said of matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their arrow's point they <qex>soften</qex> in the flame.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>To mollify; to make less fierce or
intractable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Diffidence conciliates the proud, and <qex>softens</qex> the
severe.</q> <rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>To palliate; to represent as less enormous; <as>as,
to <ex>soften</ex> a fault</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>To compose; to mitigate; to assuage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Music can <qex>soften</qex> pain to ease.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> To make calm and placid.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All that cheers or <qex>softens</qex> life.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(f)</sd> <def>To make less harsh, less rude, less offensive, or
less violent, or to render of an opposite quality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He bore his great commision in his look,<br/
But tempered awe, and <qex>softened</qex> all he spoke.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(g)</sd> <def>To make less glaring; to tone down; <as>as, to
<ex>soften</ex> the coloring of a picture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(h)</sd> <def>To make tender; to make effeminate; to enervate;
<as>as, troops <ex>softened</ex> by luxury</as>.</def> <-- weaken
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(i)</sd> <def>To make less harsh or grating, or of a quality the
opposite; <as>as, to <ex>soften</ex> the voice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soften</ent><br/
<hw>Sof"ten</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become soft or softened, or
less rude, harsh, severe, or obdurate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Softener</ent><br/
<hw>Sof"ten*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, softens.</def> <mark>[Written also, less properly,
softner.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Water softener, a device which exchanges sodium ions for
polyvalent metal cations in water, thus converting the "hard"
mineral-containing water into "soft" water. This treatment renders the
water more usable for washing, as the polyvalent cations interfere with
the detergent action of soaps. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Softening</ent><br/
<hw>Sof"ten*ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Soften</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Softening of the brain</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Cerebral softening</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
localized softening of the brain substance, due to hemorrhage or
inflammation. Three varieties, distinguished by their color and
representing different stages of the morbid process, are known
respectively as <xex>red</xex>, <xex>yellow</xex>, and <xex>white</xex>,
<xex>softening</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft-finned</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"-finned`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the fin rays cartilaginous or flexible; without spines; --
said of certain fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft-headed</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"-head`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Weak in
intellect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having softness
or tenderness of heart; susceptible of pity or other kindly affection;
gentle; meek.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Soft"-heart`ed*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Softish</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat soft.</def>
<rj><au>De Witt Clinton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Softling</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A soft, effeminate
person; a voluptuary.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Woolton.
.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Softly</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a soft manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Softner</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Softener</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Softness</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<?/ftness</ets>, <ets>s<?/ftnyss</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or
state of being soft; -- opposed to <ant>hardness</ant>, and used in the
various specific senses of the adjective.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft-shelled</ent><br/
<ent>Soft-shell</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Soft"-shell`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Soft"-shelled`</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a soft or fragile
shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soft-shell clam</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
long clam. See <er>Mya</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft-shelled
crab</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See the Note under <er>Crab</er>,
1.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft-shelled turtle</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>Same as <cref>Soft tortoise</cref>, under
<er>Soft</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft-spoken</ent><br/
<hw>Soft"-spo`ken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Speaking softly;
having a mild or gentle voice; hence, mild; affable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soft steel</ent><br/
<hw>Soft steel</hw>. <def>Steel low in carbon; mild steel; ingot
iron.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soger</ent><br/
<hw>So"ger</hw> <pr>(s<omac/"j<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n. & v. i.</pos>
<def>Var. of <er>Soldier</er>.</def> <mark>[Dial. or Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>R. H. Dana, Jr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sogginess</ent><br/
<hw>Sog"gi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being soggy; soddenness; wetness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soggy</ent><br/
<hw>Sog"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Soggier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Soggiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Cf. Icel. <ets>s<oum/ggr</ets>
damp, wet, or E. <ets>soak</ets>.]</ety> <def>Filled with water; soft
with moisture; sodden; soaked; wet; <as>as, <ex>soggy</ex> land or
timber</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soho</ent><br/
<hw>So*ho"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <def>Ho; -- a word used
in calling from a distant place; a sportsman's halloo.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soi-disant</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Soi`-di*sant"</hw> <pr>(sw<aum/"d<esl/`z<aum/N")</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.; <ets>soi</ets> one's self + <ets>disant</ets>,
p. pr. of <ets>dire</ets> to say.]</ety> <def>Self-named; self-styled;
pretended; would-be.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soil</ent><br/
<hw>Soil</hw> <pr>(soil)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Soiled</conjf> <pr>(soild)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Soiling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>saoler</ets>,
<ets>saouler</ets>, to satiate, F. <ets>so<ucir/ler</ets>, L.
<ets>satullare</ets>, fr. <ets>satullus</ets>, dim. of <ets>satur</ets>
sated. See <er>Satire</er>.]</ety> <def>To feed, as cattle or horses, in
the barn or an inclosure, with fresh grass or green food cut for them,
instead of sending them out to pasture; hence (such food having the
effect of purging them), to purge by feeding on green food; <as>as, to
<ex>soil</ex> a horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soil</ent><br/
<hw>Soil</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>soile</ets>, F.
<ets>sol</ets>, fr. L. <ets>solum</ets> bottom, soil; but the word has
probably been influenced in form by <ets>soil</ets> a miry place. Cf.
<er>Saloon</er>, <er>Soil</er> a miry place, <er>Sole</er> of the
foot.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The upper stratum of the earth; the mold,
or that compound substance which furnishes nutriment to plants, or which
is particularly adapted to support and nourish them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Land; country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Must I thus leave thee, Paradise? thus leave<br/
Thee, native <qex>soil</qex>?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Dung; faeces; compost; manure; <as>as, night
<ex>soil</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Improve land by dung and other sort of <qex>soils</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soil pipe</b></col>, <cd>a pipe or drain for carrying off
night soil.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soil</ent><br/
<hw>Soil</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To enrich with soil or muck; to
manure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men . . . <qex>soil</qex> their ground, not that they love the
dirt, but that they expect a crop.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soil</ent><br/
<hw>Soil</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>soil</ets>,
<ets>souil</ets>, F. <ets>souille</ets>, from OF. <ets>soillier</ets>,
F. <ets>souiller</ets>. See <er>Soil</er> to make dirty.]</ety> <def>A
marshy or miry place to which a hunted boar resorts for refuge; hence, a
wet place, stream, or tract of water, sought for by other game, as
deer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As deer, being stuck, fly through many <qex>soils</qex>,<br/
Yet still the shaft sticks fast.</q> <rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To take soil</b></col>, <cd>to run into the mire or
water; hence, to take refuge or shelter.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, sir, have you <qex>taken soil</qex> here? It is well a man may
reach you after three hours' running.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soil</ent><br/
<hw>Soil</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos><ety>[OE. <ets>soilen</ets>, OF.
<ets>soillier</ets>, F. <ets>souiller</ets>, (assumed) LL.
<ets>suculare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sucula</ets> a little pig, dim. of
<ets>sus</ets> a swine. See <er>Sow</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To make dirty or unclean on the surface; to foul; to
dirty; to defile; <as>as, to <ex>soil</ex> a garment with
dust</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our wonted ornaments now <qex>soiled</qex> and stained.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stain or mar, as with infamy or disgrace; to
tarnish; to sully.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To foul; dirt; dirty; begrime; bemire; bespatter;
besmear; daub; bedaub; stain; tarnish; sully; defile; pollute.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soil</ent><br/
<hw>Soil</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become soiled; <as>as, light
colors <ex>soil</ex> sooner than dark ones</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soil</ent><br/
<hw>Soil</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Soil</er> to make dirty,
<er>Soil</er> a miry place.]</ety> <def>That which soils or pollutes; a
soiled place; spot; stain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A lady's honor . . . will not bear a <qex>soil</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soiliness</ent><br/
<hw>Soil"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stain;
foulness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soilless</ent><br/
<hw>Soil"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of soil or
mold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soil pipe</ent><br/
<hw>Soil pipe</hw>. <def>A pipe or drain for carrying off night
soil.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soilure</ent><br/
<hw>Soil"ure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>soillure</ets>, F. <ets>souillure</ets>. See <er>Soil</er> to make
dirty.]</ety> <def>Stain; pollution.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then fearing rust or <qex>soilure</qex>, fashioned for it<br/
A case of silk.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soily</ent><br/
<hw>Soil"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dirty; soiled.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soiree</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Soi`ree"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>soir</ets> evening, fr. L. <ets>serus</ets> late, <ets>serum</ets>
late time. Cf. <er>Serenade</er>.]</ety> <def>An evening party; --
distinguished from <xex>levee</xex>, and
<xex>matin<eacute/e</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soja</ent><br/
<hw>So"ja</hw> <pr>(s<omac/"j<adot/ <or/ s<omac/"y<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An Asiatic leguminous herb
(<spn>Glycine max</spn>, formerly <spn>Glycine Soja</spn>) the seeds of
which (called <prod>soy beans</prod>) are used in preparing the sauce
called <prod>soy</prod>. Called also <altname>soya</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sojer</ent><br/
<hw>So"jer</hw> <pr>(s<omac/"j<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n. & v. i.</pos>
<def>Var. of <er>Soldier</er>.</def> <mark>[Dial. or Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sojourn</ent><br/
<hw>So"journ</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sojourned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sojourning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sojornen</ets>, <ets>sojournen</ets>, OF. <ets>sojorner</ets>,
<ets>sejorner</ets>, F. <ets>s<eacute/journer</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>sub</ets> under, about + <ets>diurnus</ets> belonging to the day.
See <er>Journal</er>, <er>Diurnal</er>.]</ety> <def>To dwell for a time;
to dwell or live in a place as a temporary resident or as a stranger,
not considering the place as a permanent habitation; to delay; to
tarry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Abram went down into Egypt to <qex>sojourn</qex> there.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xii. 30.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Home he goeth, he might not longer <qex>sojourn</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The soldiers first assembled at Newcastle, and there
<qex>sojourned</qex> three days.</q> <rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sojourn</ent><br/
<hw>So"journ</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>sujurn</ets>,
<ets>sujur</ets>, <ets>sejor</ets>, F. <ets>s<eacute/jour</ets>. See
<er>Sojourn</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>A temporary residence, as
that of a traveler in a foreign land.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though long detained<br/
In that obscure <qex>sojourn</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sojourner</ent><br/
<hw>So"journ*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sojourns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are strangers before thee, and <qex>sojourners</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>1. Chron. xxix. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sojourning</ent><br/
<hw>So"journ*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or state of one who
sojourns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sojournment</ent><br/
<hw>So"journ*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Temporary
residence, as that of a stranger or a traveler.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soke</ent><br/
<hw>Soke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eng.
Law)</fld> <def>See <er>Soc</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1367 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the small territorial divisions into which
Lincolnshire, England, is divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sokeman</ent><br/
<hw>Soke"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Socman</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sokemanry</ent><br/
<hw>Soke"man*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Socmanry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soken</ent><br/
<hw>Sok"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Socome</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A toll. See <er>Soc</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Great <qex>sooken</qex> had this miller, out of doubt.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A district held by socage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soko</ent><br/
<hw>So"ko</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
African anthropoid ape, supposed to be a variety of the
chimpanzee.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sol</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Alchem.)</fld> <def>Gold; -- so called from its
brilliancy, color, and value.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sol</ent><br/
<hw>Sol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A syllable applied in solmization to
the note G, or to the fifth tone of any diatonic scale.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The tone itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sol</ent><br/
<hw>Sol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sou</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A sou.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A silver and gold coin of Peru. The silver
<xex>sol</xex> is the unit of value, and is worth about 68
cents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sole</ent><br/
<ent>Sol</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sol</hw> <hw>Sole</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[From <ets>hydrosol</ets> an aqueous colloidal solution, confused
with G. <ets>sole</ets>, <ets>soole</ets>, salt water from which salt is
obtained.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A fluid mixture of a colloid
and a liquid; a liquid colloidal solution or suspension.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sola</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L., fem. of
<ets>solus</ets>.]</ety> <def>See <er>Solus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sola</ent><br/
<hw>So"la</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Native name.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A leguminous plant (<spn>Aeschynomene
aspera</spn>) growing in moist places in Southern India and the East
Indies. Its pithlike stem is used for making hats, swimming-jackets,
etc.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>solah</asp>,
<asp>shola</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solace</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"ace</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>solas</ets>,
<ets>ssoulaz</ets>, L. <ets>solacium</ets>, <ets>solatium</ets>, fr.
<ets>solari</ets> to comfort, console. Cf. <er>Console</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Comfort in grief; alleviation of grief
or anxiety; also, that which relieves in distress; that which cheers or
consoles; relief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In business of mirth and of <qex>solace</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The proper <qex>solaces</qex> of age are not music and
compliments, but wisdom and devotion.</q>
<rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rest; relaxation; ease.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To make his steed some <qex>solace</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Comfort; consolation; alleviation;
relief.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solace</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"ace</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Solaced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Solacing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>solacier</ets>, <ets>soulacier</ets>, F. <ets>solacier</ets>, LL.
<ets>solatiare</ets>. See <er>Solace</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To cheer in grief or under calamity; to comfort; to
relieve in affliction, solitude, or discomfort; to console; -- applied
to persons; <as>as, to <ex>solace</ex> one with the hope of future
reward</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To allay; to assuage; to soothe; <as>as, to
<ex>solace</ex> grief</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To comfort; assuage; allay. See
<er>Comfort</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solace</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"ace</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To take comfort; to be
cheered.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solacement</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"ace*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
solacing, or the state of being solaced; also, that which solaces.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solacious</ent><br/
<hw>So*la"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>solacieux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Affording solace; <as>as, a
<ex>solacious</ex> voice</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solanaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`a*na"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to plants of the natural order
<ord>Solanaceae</ord>, of which the nightshade (<gen>Solanum</gen>) is
the type. The order includes also the tobacco, ground cherry, tomato,
eggplant, red pepper, and many more.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soland</ent><br/
<hw>So"land</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
solan goose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solander</ent><br/
<hw>So*lan"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sallenders</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solan goose</ent><br/
<hw>So"lan goose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Icel. <ets>s<?/la</ets>; akin
to Norw. <ets>sula</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common
gannet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solania</ent><br/
<hw>So*la"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Solanine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solanicine</ent><br/
<hw>So*lan"i*cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Solanine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An alkaloid produced
by the action of hydrochloric acid on solanidine, as a tasteless yellow
crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solanidine</ent><br/
<hw>So*lan"i*dine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Solanine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An alkaloid produced
by the decomposition of solanine, as a white crystalline substance
having a harsh bitter taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solanine</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"a*nine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solanum</ets> nightshade.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
poisonous alkaloid glucoside extracted from the berries of common
nightshade (<spn>Solanum nigrum</spn>), and of bittersweet, and from
potato sprouts, as a white crystalline substance having an acrid,
burning taste; -- called also <altname>solonia</altname>, and
<altname>solanina</altname>.</def> <-- C45H73NO15 --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solano</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*la"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <ety>[Sp., fr. L. <ets>solanus</ets>
(sc. <ets>ventus</ets>), from <ets>sol</ets> the sun.]</ety> <def>A hot,
oppressive wind which sometimes blows in the Mediterranean, particularly
on the eastern coast of Spain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solanoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"a*noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Solanum</ets>
+ <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Resembling a potato; --
said of a kind of cancer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solanum</ent><br/
<hw>So*la"num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
nightshade.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of plants
comprehending the potato (<spn>Solanum tuberosum</spn>), the eggplant
(<spn>Solanum melongena</spn>, and several hundred other species;
nightshade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solar</ent><br/
<hw>So"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>soler</ets>,
AS. <ets>solere</ets>, L. <ets>solarium</ets>, from <ets>sol</ets> the
sun. See <er>Solar</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>A loft or upper
chamber; a garret room.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>soler</asp>, <asp>solere</asp>, <asp>sollar</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Oxf. Gloss.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solar</ent><br/
<hw>So"lar</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>solaris</ets>, fr.
<ets>sol</ets> the sun; akin to As. <ets>s<omac/l</ets>, Icel.
<ets>s<omac/l</ets>, Goth. <ets>sauil</ets>, Lith. <ets>saule</ets>, W.
<ets>haul</ets>,. <ets>sul</ets>, Skr. <ets>svar</ets>, perhaps to E.
<ets>sun</ets>:F. <ets>solaire</ets>. Cf. <er>Parasol</er>.
<er>Sun</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the sun;
proceeding from the sun; <as>as, the <ex>solar</ex> system;
<ex>solar</ex> light; <ex>solar</ex> rays; <ex>solar</ex>
influence</as>. See <cref>Solar system</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>Born under the predominant
influence of the sun.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And proud beside, as <qex>solar</qex> people are.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Measured by the progress or revolution of the sun in
the ecliptic; <as>as, the <ex>solar</ex> year</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Produced by the action of the sun, or peculiarly
affected by its influence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They denominate some herbs <qex>solar</qex>, and some lunar.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Solar cycle</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Cycle</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Solar day</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Day</er>, 2.</cd> --
<col><b>Solar engine</b></col>, <cd>an engine in which the energy of
solar heat is used to produce motion, as in evaporating water for a
steam engine, or expanding air for an air engine.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar
flowers</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>flowers which open and shut
daily at certain hours.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar lamp</b></col>, <cd>an
argand lamp.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar microscope</b></col>, <cd>a
microscope consisting essentially, first, of a mirror for reflecting a
beam of sunlight through the tube, which sometimes is fixed in a window
shutter; secondly, of a condenser, or large lens, for converging the
beam upon the object; and, thirdly, of a small lens, or magnifier, for
throwing an enlarged image of the object at its focus upon a screen in a
dark room or in a darkened box.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Illustration of solar microscope --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <col><b>Solar month</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Month</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Solar oil</b></col>, <cd>a paraffin oil used an illuminant
and lubricant.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar phosphori</b></col>
<fld>(Physics)</fld>, <cd>certain substances, as the diamond, siulphide
of barium (Bolognese or Bologna phosphorus), calcium sulphide, etc.,
which become phosphorescent, and shine in the dark, after exposure to
sunlight or other intense light.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar plexus</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a nervous plexus situated in the dorsal and
anterior part of the abdomen, consisting of several sympathetic ganglia
with connecting and radiating nerve fibers; -- so called in allusion to
the radiating nerve fibers.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar spots</b></col>.
<cd>See <cref>Sun spots</cref>, under <er>Sun</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Solar system</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the sun, with
the group of celestial bodies which, held by its attraction, revolve
round it. The system comprises the major planets, with their satellites;
the minor planets, or asteroids, and the comets; also, the meteorids,
the matter that furnishes the zodiacal light, and the rings of Saturn.
The satellites that revolve about the major planets are twenty-two in
number, of which the Earth has one (see <er>Moon</er>.), Mars two,
Jupiter five, Saturn nine, Uranus four, and Neptune one. The asteroids,
between Mars and Jupiter, thus far discovered (1900), number about five
hundred, the first four of which were found near the beginning of the
century, and are called Ceres, Pallas, Juno, and Vesta.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<note><p>The principal elements of the major planets, and of the comets
seen at more than one perihelion passage, are exhibited in the following
tables: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>
