</table>
<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<cs><p>-- <col><b>Solar telegraph</b></col>, <cd>telegraph for signaling by
flashes of reflected sunlight.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar time</b></col>.
<cd>See <cref>Apparent time</cref>, under <er>Time</er>.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Solarium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*la"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Solaria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. See <er>Solar</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An apartment freely exposed to the
sun; anciently, an apartment or inclosure on the roof of a house; in
modern times, an apartment in a hospital, used as a resort for
convalescents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
handsome marine spiral shells of the genus <gen>Solarium</gen> and
allied genera. The shell is conical, and usually has a large, deep
umbilicus exposing the upper whorls. Called also <altname>perspective
shell</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solarization</ent><br/
<hw>So`lar*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>Injury of a photographic picture caused by
exposing it for too long a time to the sun's light in the camera;
burning; excessive insolation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solarize</ent><br/
<hw>So"lar*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Solarized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Solarizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>To injure by too long exposure to the light of
the sun in the camera; to burn.</def><-- now overexposure --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solarize</ent><br/
<hw>So"lar*ize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Photog.)</fld> <def>To
become injured by undue or too long exposure to the sun's rays in the
camera; -- an older term now replaced by
<altname>overexpose</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solar myth</ent><br/
<hw>So"lar myth</hw>. <def>A myth which essentially consists of allegory
based upon ideas as to the sun's course, motion, influence, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solar parallax</ent><br/
<hw>Solar parallax</hw>. <def>The parallax of the sun, that is, the
angle subtended at the sun by the semidiameter of the earth. It is
8.80<prime/<prime/, and is the fundamental datum.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solary</ent><br/
<hw>So"la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Solar.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solas</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"as</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Solace.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solatium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*la"ti*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Solace</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Anything which alleviates or
compensates for suffering or loss; a compensation; esp., an additional
allowance, as for injured feelings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sold</ent><br/
<hw>Sold</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sell</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sold</ent><br/
<hw>Sold</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>solde</ets>. See
<er>Soldier</er>, and cf. <er>Sou</er>.]</ety> <def>Solary; military
pay.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldan</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[OE. <ets>soudan</ets>,
F. <ets>soudan</ets>, from the Arabic. See <er>Sultan</er>.]</ety>
<def>A sultan.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldanel</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"da*nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
plant of the genus <gen>Soldanella</gen>, low Alpine herbs of the
Primrose family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldanrie</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dan*rie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The country ruled
by a soldan, or sultan; a sultanate{2}.</def> <mark>[Poet.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solder</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formerly
<ets>soder</ets>; F. <ets>soudure</ets>, OF. <ets>soudeure</ets>, fr.
OF. & F. <ets>souder</ets> to solder, L. <ets>solidare</ets> to fasten,
to make solid. See <er>Solid</er>, and cf. <er>Sawder</er>.]</ety>
<def>A metal or metallic alloy used when melted for uniting adjacent
metallic edges or surfaces; a metallic cement.</def>
<specif>Hence,</specif> <def>anything which unites or cements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Hard solder</b></col>, <cd>a solder which fuses only at a
red heat, as one composed of zinc and copper, or silver and copper,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Soft solder</b></col>, <cd>a solder fusible at
comparatively low temperatures; <as>as, plumbers' solder, consisting of
two parts lead and one part tin, is a <ex>soft
solder</ex></as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solder</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"der</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Soldered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Soldering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Formerly<ets>soder</ets>. See
<er>Solder</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To unite
(metallic surfaces or edges) by the intervention of a more fusible metal
or metallic alloy applied when melted; to join by means of metallic
cement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mend; to patch up.</def> <ldquo/To
<xex>solder</xex> up a broken cause.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solderer</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"der*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
solders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldering</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"der*ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Solder</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Soldering iron</b></col>, <col><b>Soldering
tool</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an instrument for soldering, consisting of a
bit or bolt of copper having a pointed or wedge-shaped end, and
furnished with a handle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldier</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>souldier</ets>, <ets>soudiour</ets>, <ets>souder</ets>, OF.
<ets>soldier</ets>, <ets>soldoier</ets>, <ets>soldeier</ets>,
<ets>sodoier</ets>, <ets>soudoier</ets>, <ets>soudier</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>solidus</ets> a piece of money (hence applied to the pay of a
soldier), fr. <ets>solidus</ets> solid. See <er>Solid</er>, and cf.
<er>Sold</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is engaged
in military service as an officer or a private; one who serves in an
army; one of an organized body of combatants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am a <qex>soldier</qex> and unapt to weep.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, a private in military service, as
distinguished from an officer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It were meet that any one, before he came to be a captain, should
have been a <qex>soldier</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A brave warrior; a man of military experience and
skill, or a man of distinguished valor; -- used by way of emphasis or
distinction.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The red or cuckoo gurnard
(<spn>Trigla pini</spn>.)</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the asexual polymorphic
forms of white ants, or termites, in which the head and jaws are very
large and strong. The soldiers serve to defend the nest. See
<er>Termite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soldier beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an
American carabid beetle (<spn>Chauliognathus Americanus</spn>) whose
larva feeds upon other insects, such as the plum curculio.</cd> --
<col><b>Soldier bug</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any hemipterous
insect of the genus <gen>Podisus</gen> and allied genera, as the spined
<ex>soldier bug</ex> (<spn>Podius spinosus</spn>). These bugs suck the
blood of other insects.</cd> -- <col><b>Soldier crab</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The hermit crab.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>The fiddler crab.</cd> -- <col><b>Soldier fish</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a bright-colored etheostomoid fish
(<spn>Etheostoma coeruleum</spn>) found in the Mississippi River; --
called also <altname>blue darter</altname>, and <altname>rainbow
darter</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Soldier fly</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of small dipterous
flies of the genus <gen>Stratyomys</gen> and allied genera. They are
often bright green, with a metallic luster, and are ornamented on the
sides of the back with markings of yellow, like epaulets or shoulder
straps.</cd> -- <col><b>Soldier moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
large geometrid moth (<spn>Euschema militaris</spn>), having the wings
bright yellow with bluish black lines and spots.</cd> -- <col><b>Soldier
orchis</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of orchis (<spn>Orchis
militaris</spn>).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldier</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To serve as a
soldier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make a pretense of doing something, or of
performing any task.</def> <mark>[Colloq.U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense the vulgar pronounciation
<pr>(s<omac/"j<etil/r)</pr> is jocosely preserved.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It needs an opera glass to discover whether the leaders are
pulling, or only <qex>soldiering</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>C. D.
Warner.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldieress</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female soldier.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldiering</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of serving
as a soldier; the state of being a soldier; the occupation of a
soldier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of feigning to work. See the Note under
<er>Soldier</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, 2.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldierlike</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier*like"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a soldier;
soldierly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldierly</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like or becoming a real
soldier; brave; martial; heroic; honorable; soldierlike.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Soldierly</xex> discipline.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldiership</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Military qualities or state;
martial skill; behavior becoming a soldier.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldierwood</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier*wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A showy leguminous plant (<spn>Calliandra purpurea</spn>) of the
West Indies. The flowers have long tassels of purple stamens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldiery</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"dier*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A body
of soldiers; soldiers, collectively; the military.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A camp of faithful <qex>soldiery</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Military service.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soldo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sol"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Soldi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It. See <er>Sou</er>.]</ety>
<def>A small Italian coin worth a sou or a cent; the twentieth part of a
lira.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sole</ent><br/
<hw>Sole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sole</ets>, L.
<ets>solea</ets>; -- so named from its flat shape. See <er>Sole</er> of
the foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of several
species of flatfishes of the genus <gen>Solea</gen> and allied genera of
the family <fam>Soleidae</fam>, especially the common European species
(<spn>Solea vulgaris</spn>), which is a valuable food fish.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any one of several American flounders somewhat
resembling the true sole in form or quality, as the California sole
(<spn>Lepidopsetta bilineata</spn>), the long-finned sole
(<spn>Glyptocephalus zachirus</spn>), and other species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Lemon</b></col>, <or/ <col><b>French</b></col>,
<col><b>sole</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a European species
of sole (<spn>Solea pegusa</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Smooth sole</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the megrim.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1368 --></p>

<p><ent>Sole</ent><br/
<hw>Sole</hw> <pr>(s<omac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sole</ets>, fr. L. <ets>soolea</ets> (or rather an assumed L.
<ets>sola</ets>), akin to <ets>solum</ets>round, soil, sole of the foot.
Cf. <er>Exile</er>, <er>Saloon</er>, <er>Soil</er> earth, <er>Sole</er>
the fish.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The bottom of the foot; hence, also,
rarely, the foot itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dove found no rest for the <qex>sole</qex> of her foot.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. viii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hast wandered through the world now long a day,<br/
Yet ceasest not thy weary <qex>soles</qex> to lead.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The bottom of a shoe or boot, or the piece of
leather which constitutes the bottom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <ldquo/caliga<rdquo/ was a military shoe, with a very thick
<qex>sole</qex>, tied above the instep.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The bottom or lower part of anything, or that on
which anything rests in standing.</def> Specifially: <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>The bottom of the body of a plow; -- called
also <altname>slade</altname>; also, the bottom of a furrow.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>The horny substance under a horse's
foot, which protects the more tender parts.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>The bottom of an embrasure.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A piece of timber attached to the lower part of
the rudder, to make it even with the false keel.</def> <au>Totten.</au>
<sd>(e)</sd> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The seat or bottom of a mine; --
applied to horizontal veins or lodes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sole leather</b></col>, <cd>thick, strong, used for
making the soles of boots and shoes, and for other
purposes.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sole</ent><br/
<hw>Sole</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Soled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Soling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To furnish with a sole; <as>as, to
<ex>sole</ex> a shoe</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sole</ent><br/
<hw>Sole</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>solus</ets>, or OF.
<ets>sol</ets>, F. <ets>seul</ets> (fr. L. <ets>solus</ets>; cf. L.
<ets>sollus</ets> whole, entire. Cf. <er>Desolate</er>, <er>Solemn</er>,
<er>Solo</er>, <er>Sullen</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Being or acting
without another; single; individual; only.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>sole</xex> son of my queen.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He, be sure . . . first and last will reign<br/
<qex>Sole</qex> king.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Single; unmarried; <as>as, a feme
<ex>sole</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Corporation sole</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Corporation</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Single; individual; only; alone;
solitary.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solecism</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"e*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[F.
<ets>sol<eacute/cisme</ets>, L. <ets>soloecismus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>soloikismo`s</grk>, fr. <grk>soloiki`zein</grk> to speak or write
incorrectly, fr. <grk>so`loikos</grk> speaking incorrectly, from the
corruption of the Attic dialect among the Athenian colonists of
<grk>So`loi</grk> in Cilicia.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An impropriety or
incongruity of language in the combination of words or parts of a
sentence; esp., deviation from the idiom of a language or from the rules
of syntax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A barbarism may be in one word; a <qex>solecism</qex> must be of
more.</q> <rj><qau>Johnson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any inconsistency, unfitness, absurdity, or
impropriety, as in deeds or manners.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Caesar, by dismissing his guards and retaining his power,
committed a dangerous <qex>solecism</qex> in politics.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Middleton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The idea of having committed the slightest <qex>solecism</qex> in
politeness was agony to him.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Barbarism; impropriety; absurdity.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solecist</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"e*cist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <def>One who commits a solecism.</def>
<rj><au>Blackwall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solecistic</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`e*cis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Solecistical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solecistical</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`e*cis"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining
to, or involving, a solecism; incorrect.</def> <ldquo/He thought it made
the language <xex>solecistical</xex> and absurd.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Blackwall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solecistically</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`e*cis"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a solecistic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solecize</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"e*cize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <def>To commit a solecism.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solely</ent><br/
<hw>Sole"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Singly; alone; only;
without another; <as>as, to rest a cause <ex>solely</ex> one argument;
to rely <ex>solely</ex>n one's own strength</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemn</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"emn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>solempne</ets>, OF. <ets>solempne</ets>, L. <ets>solemnis</ets>,
<ets>solennis</ets>, <ets>sollemnis</ets>, <ets>sollennis</ets>;
<ets>sollus</ets> all, entire + <ets>annus</ets> a year; properly, that
takes place every year; -- used especially of religious solemnities. Cf.
<er>Silly</er>, <er>Annual</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Marked with
religious rites and pomps; enjoined by, or connected with, religion;
sacred.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His holy rites and <qex>solemn</qex> feasts profaned.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The worship of this image was advanced, and a <qex>solemn</qex>
supplication observed everry year.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Stillingfleet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to a festival; festive; festal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/On this <xex>solemn</xex> day.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Stately; ceremonious; grand.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His feast so <qex>solemn</qex> and so rich.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To-night we hold a <qex>splemn</qex> supper.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fitted to awaken or express serious reflections;
marked by seriousness; serious; grave; devout; <as>as, a <ex>solemn</ex>
promise; <ex>solemn</ex> earnestness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor wanting power to mitigate and swage<br/
With <qex>solemn</qex> touches troubled thoughts.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There reigned a <qex>solemn</qex> silence over all.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Real; earnest; downright.</def> <mark>[Obs. &
R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Frederick, the emperor, . . . has spared no expense in
strengthening this city; since which time we find no <qex>solemn</qex>
taking it by the Turks.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Affectedly grave or serious; <as>as, to put on a
<ex>solemn</ex> face</as>.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>solemn</xex>
coxcomb.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Made in form; ceremonious; <as>as,
<ex>solemn</ex> war</as>; conforming with all legal requirements;
<as>as, probate in <ex>solemn</ex> form</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au> <au>Jarman.</au> <au>Greenleaf.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Solemn League and Covenant</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Covenant</er>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Grave; formal; ritual; ceremonial; sober;
serious; reverential; devotional; devout. See <er>Grave</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemness</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"em*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def><er>Solemnness</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some think he wanted <qex>solemnes</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H.
Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemnity</ent><br/
<hw>So*lem"ni*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Solemnities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>solemnitas</ets>, <ets>solennitas</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>solennit<eacute/</ets>, <ets>solemnit<eacute/</ets>, OF. also
<ets>sollempnit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A rite or
ceremony performed with religious reverence; religious or ritual
ceremony; <as>as, the <ex>solemnity</ex> of a funeral, a
sacrament</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Great was the cause; our old <qex>solemnities</qex><br/
From no blind zeal or fond tradition rise,<br/
But saved from death, our Argives yearly pay<br/
These grateful honors to the god of day.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>ceremony adapted to impress with awe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The forms and <qex>solemnities</qex> of the last judgment.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterburry.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Ceremoniousness; impressiveness; seriousness; grave
earnestness; formal dignity; gravity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With much glory and great <qex>solemnity</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The statelines and gravity of the Spaniards shows itself in the
<qex>solemnity</qex> of their language.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These promises were often made with great <qex>solemnity</qex> and
confirmed with an oath.</q> <rj><qau>J. Edwards.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hence, affected gravity or seriousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Solemnity</qex> 's a cover for a sot.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Solemn state or feeling; awe or reverence; also,
that which produces such a feeling; <as>as, the <ex>solemnity</ex> of an
audience; the <ex>solemnity</ex> of Westminster Abbey</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A solemn or formal observance;
proceeding according to due form; the formality which is necessary to
render a thing done valid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemnizate</ent><br/
<hw>So*lem"ni*zate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
solemnize; <as>as, to <ex>solemnizate</ex> matrimony</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemnization</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`em*ni*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>solemnisation</ets>, <ets>solennisation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act
of solemnizing; celebration; <as>as, the <ex>solemnization</ex> of a
marriage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemnize</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"em*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Solemnized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Solemnizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>solemniser</ets>, <ets>sollemniser</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To perform with solemn or ritual ceremonies, or according to legal
forms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Baptism to be administered in one place, and marriage
<qex>solemnized</qex> in another.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To dignify or honor by ceremonies; to
celebrate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their choice nobility and flowers . . . <br/
Met from all parts to <qex>solemnize</qex> this feast.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make grave, serious, and reverential.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wordsworth was <qex>solemnizzed</qex> and elevated by this his
first look on Yarrow.</q> <rj><qau>J. C. Shairp.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every Israelite . . . arose, <qex>solemnized</qex> his face,
looked towards Jerusalem . . . and prayed.</q> <rj><qau>L.
Wallace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemnize</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"em*nize</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Solemnization.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though spoused, yet wanting wedlock's <qex>solemnize</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemnizer</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"em*ni`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
solemnizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemnly</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"emn*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a solemn
manner; with gravity; seriously; formally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There in deaf murmurs <qex>solemnly</qex> are wise.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do <qex>solemnly</qex> assure the reader.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solemnness</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"emn*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of being
solemn; solemnity; impressiveness; gravity; <as>as, the
<ex>solemnness</ex> of public worship</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>solemness</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solempne</ent><br/
<hw>So*lemp"ne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Solemn</er>.]</ety> <def>Solemn; grand; stately; splendid;
magnificent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solen</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"len</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ channel, a shellfish.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A cradle, as for a broken limb. See <er>Cradle</er>, 6.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any marine bivalve mollusk
belonging to <gen>Solen</gen> or allied genera of the family
<fam>Solenidae</fam>; a razor shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenacean</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`e*na"cean</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>.
<def>Any species of marine bivalve shells belonging to the family
<fam>Solenidae</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`e*na"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the solens or family
<fam>Solenidae</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soleness</ent><br/
<hw>Sole"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
sole, or alone; singleness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chesterfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenette</ent><br/
<hw>Sole*nette"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A small European sole (<spn>Solea minuta</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenoconcha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*le`no*con"cha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Solen</er>, and <er>Conch</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Scaphopoda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenodon</ent><br/
<hw>So*le"no*don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/
a channel + <?/<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/, a tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Either one of two species of singular West Indian insectivores,
allied to the tenrec. One species (<spn>Solendon paradoxus</spn>),
native of St. Domingo, is called also <stype>agouta</stype>; the other
(<spn>Solendon Cubanus</spn>), found in Cuba, is called
<stype>almique</stype>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenogastra</ent><br/
<hw>So*le`no*gas"tra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ channel + <?/<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/<?/,
stomach.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of lowly organized
Mollusca belonging to the Isopleura. A narrow groove takes the place of
the foot of other gastropods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenoglyph</ent><br/
<hw>So*le"no*glyph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to the Selenoglypha. See <er>Ophidia</er>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Selenoglypha.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenoglypha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So`le*nog"ly*pha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ a channel + <?/<?/<?/<?/ to engrave.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A suborder of serpents including those which
have tubular erectile fangs, as the viper and rattlesnake. See
<er>Fang</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenoid</ent><br/
<hw>So"len*oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
channel + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>An
electrodynamic spiral having the conjunctive wire turned back along its
axis, so as to neutralize that component of the effect of the current
which is due to the length of the spiral, and reduce the whole effect to
that of a series of equal and parallel circular currents. When traversed
by a current the solenoid exhibits polarity and attraction or repulsion,
like a magnet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. a switch or valve using such a solenoid circuit to drive a
metal bar which opens or closes an electric circuit or a valve
controlling fluid movement. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solenostomi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So`le*nos"to*mi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ a channel + <?/<?/<?/<?/ a mouth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tribe of lophobranch fishes having a tubular
snout. The female carries the eggs in a ventral pouch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soleplate</ent><br/
<hw>Sole"plate`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A bedplate; <as>as, the <ex>soleplate</ex> of a steam
engine</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The plate forming the back of a
waterwheel bucket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solere</ent><br/
<ent>Soler</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So"ler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>So"lere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. See <er>Solar</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<def>A loft or garret. See <er>Solar</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solert</ent><br/
<hw>So"lert</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>solers</ets>,
<ets>sollers</ets>, <ets>-ertis</ets>,clever, skillful.]</ety>
<def>Skillful; clever; crafty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solertiousness</ent><br/
<hw>So*ler"tious*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being solert.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hacket.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soleship</ent><br/
<hw>Sole"ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
sole, or alone; soleness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir E.
Dering.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sole trader</ent><br/
<hw>Sole trader</hw>. <def>A feme sole trader.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sol-fa</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`-fa"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sol-faed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sol-faing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[It. <ets>solfa</ets> the
gamut, from the syllables <ets>fa</ets>, <ets>sol</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
sing the notes of the gamut, ascending or descending; <as>as,
<ex>do</ex> or <ex>ut</ex>, <ex>re</ex>, <ex>mi</ex>, <ex>fa</ex>,
<ex>sol</ex>, <ex>la</ex>, <ex>si</ex>, <ex>do</ex>, or the same in
reverse order</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet can I neither <qex>solfe</qex> ne sing.</q> <rj><qau>Piers
Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sol-fa</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`-fa"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To sing to
solmization syllables.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sol-fa</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"-fa"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The gamut, or musical scale. See
<cref>Tonic sol-fa</cref>, under <er>Tonic</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solfanaria</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`fa*na"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., from
<ets>solfo</ets> sulphur.]</ety> <def>A sulphur mine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solfatara</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sol`fa*ta"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[It., from
<ets>solfo</ets> brimstone, sulphur, L. <ets>sulfur</ets>, E.
<ets>sulphur</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A volcanic area or
vent which yields only sulphur vapors, steam, and the like. It
represents the stages of the volcanic activity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solfeggiare</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sol`feg*gia"re</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v.
i.</pos><ety>[It.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To sol-fa. See
<er>Sol-fa</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solfeggio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sol*feg"gio</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[It., fr.
<ets>solfa</ets> the gamut.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The system of
arranging the scale by the names <xex>do</xex>, <xex>re</xex>,
<xex>mi</xex>, <xex>fa</xex>, <xex>sol</xex>, <xex>la</xex>,
<xex>si</xex>, by which singing is taught; a singing exercise upon these
syllables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solferino</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`fe*ri"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A brilliant deep
pink color with a purplish tinge, one of the dyes derived from aniline;
-- so called from Solferino in Italy, where a battle was fought about
the time of its discovery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soli</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Solo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicit</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Solicited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Soliciting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>sollicier</ets>, L.
<ets>sollicitare</ets>, <ets>solicitare</ets>, <ets>-atum</ets>, fr.
<ets>sollicitus</ets> wholly (<it>i. e.</it>, violently) moved;
<ets>sollus</ets> whole + <ets>citus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>ciere</ets> to
move, excite. See <er>Solemn</er>, <er>Cite</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To ask from with earnestness; to make petition to; to apply to for
obtaining something; <as>as, to <ex>solicit</ex> person for
alms</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Did I <qex>solicit</qex> thee<br/
From darkness to promote me?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To endeavor to obtain; to seek; to plead for;
<as>as, to <ex>solicit</ex> an office; to <ex>solicit</ex> a
favor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I view my crime, but kindle at the view,<br/
Repent old pleasures, and <qex>solicit</qex> new.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To awake or excite to action; to rouse desire in; to
summon; to appeal to; to invite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That fruit . . . <qex>solicited</qex> her longing eye.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sounds and some tangible qualities <qex>solicit</qex> their proper
senses, and force an entrance to the mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To urge the claims of; to plead; to act as solicitor
for or with reference to.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Should<br/
My brother henceforth study to forget<br/
The vow that he hath made thee, I would ever<br/
<qex>Solicit</qex> thy deserts.</q> <rj><qau>Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To disturb; to disquiet; -- a Latinism rarely
used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hath any ill <qex>solicited</qex> thine ears?</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But anxious fears <qex>solicit</qex> my weak breast.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> To beseech; ask; request; crave; supplicate;
entreat; beg; implore; importune. See <er>Beseech</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicitant</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic"it*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>sollicitans</ets>, p. pr. ]</ety> <def>One who solicits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicitate</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic"it*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Solicitous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Eden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicitation</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sollicitation</ets>, or L. <ets>sollicitatio</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of soliciting; earnest request; persistent
asking; importunity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Excitement; invitation; <as>as, the
<ex>solicitation</ex> of the senses</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicitor</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic"it*or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>solliciteur</ets>, L. <ets>sollicitator</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who solicits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An attorney or
advocate; one who represents another in court; -- formerly, in English
practice, the professional designation of a person admitted to practice
in a court of chancery or equity. See the Note under
<er>Attorney</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The law officer of a city,
town, department, or government; <as>as, the city <ex>solicitor</ex>;
the <ex>solicitor</ex> of the treasury.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicitor-general</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic"it*or-gen"er*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
second law officer in the government of Great Britain; also, a similar
officer under the United States government, who is associated with the
attorney-general; also, the chief law officer of some of the
States.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicitous</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic"it*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>sollicitus</ets>, <ets>solicitus</ets>. See <er>Solicit</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Disposed to solicit; eager to obtain
something desirable, or to avoid anything evil; concerned; anxious;
careful.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Solicitous</xex> of my reputation.<rdquo/
<au>Dryden.</au> <ldquo/He was <xex>solicitous</xex> for his
advice.<rdquo/ <au>Calerendon.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Enjoy the present, whatsoever it be, and be not
<qex>solicitous</qex> about the future.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The colonel had been intent upon other things, and not enough
<qex>solicitous</qex> to finish the fortifications.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>So*lic"it*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>So*lic"it*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicitress</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic"it*ress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who
solicits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solicitude</ent><br/
<hw>So*lic"i*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sollicitude</ets>,r L. <ets>sollicitudo</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
state of being solicitous; uneasiness of mind occasioned by fear of evil
or desire good; anxiety.</def> <-- used now mainly of concern for the
well-being of others --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The many cares and great labors of worldly men, their
<qex>solicitude</qex> and outward shows.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mother looked at her with fond <qex>solicitude</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>G. W. Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Carefulness; concern; anxiety. See
<er>Care</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1369 --></p>

<p><ent>Solid</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"id</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/l"<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solidus</ets>, probably akin to <ets>sollus</ets> whole, entire,
Gr. <?/<?/<?/: cf. F. <ets>solide</ets>. Cf.
<er>Consolidate</er>,<er>Soda</er>, <er>Solder</er>, <er>Soldier</er>,
<er>Solemn</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the constituent parts so
compact, or so firmly adhering, as to resist the impression or
penetration of other bodies; having a fixed form; hard; firm; compact;
-- opposed to <contr>fluid</contr> and <contr>liquid</contr> or to
<contr>plastic</contr>, like clay, or to <contr>incompact</contr>, like
sand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not hollow; full of matter; <as>as, a <ex>solid</ex>
globe or cone, as distinguished from a <ant>hollow</ant> one</as>; not
spongy; dense; hence, sometimes, heavy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arith.)</fld> <def>Having all the geometrical
dimensions; cubic; <as>as, a <ex>solid</ex> foot contains 1,728
<ex>solid</ex> inches</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense, <xex>cubic</xex>s now generally
used.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Firm; compact; strong; stable; unyielding; <as>as, a
<ex>solid</ex> pier; a <ex>solid</ex> pile; a <ex>solid</ex>
wall.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Applied to a compound word whose parts are closely
united and form an unbroken word; -- opposed to
<contr>hyphened</contr>.</def><-- unhyphenated, ligated? fused? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Fig.: Worthy of credit, trust, or esteem;
substantial, as opposed to <contr>frivolous</contr> or
<contr>fallacious</contr>; weighty; firm; strong; valid; just;
genuine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>solid</qex> purpose of a sincere and virtuous answer.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These, wanting wit, affect gravity, and go by the name of
<qex>solid</qex> men.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The genius of the Italians wrought by <qex>solid</qex> toil what
the myth-making imagination of the Germans had projected in a poem.</q>
<rj><qau>J. A. Symonds.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Sound; not weakly; <as>as, a <ex>solid</ex>
constitution of body</as>.</def> <rj><au>I. Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of a fleshy, uniform, undivided
substance, as a bulb or root; not spongy or hollow within, as a
stem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>Impenetrable; resisting or
excluding any other material particle or atom from any given portion of
space; -- applied to the supposed ultimate particles of
matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>Not having the lines separated
by leads; not open.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>United; without division; unanimous; <as>as, the
delegation is <ex>solid</ex> for a candidate</as>.</def> <mark>[Polit.
Cant. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Solid angle</b></col>. <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Angle</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Solid color</b></col>, <cd>an even
color; one not shaded or variegated.</cd> -- <col><b>Solid
green</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Emerald green</cref> <sd>(a)</sd>, under
<er>Green</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Solid measure</b></col>
<fld>(Arith.)</fld>, <cd>a measure for volumes, in which the units are
each a cube of fixed linear magnitude, as a cubic foot, yard, or the
like; thus, a foot, in <xex>solid measure</xex>, or a <xex>solid
foot</xex>, contains 1,728 solid inches.</cd> -- <col><b>Solid
newel</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a newel into which the ends of
winding stairs are built, in distinction from a <xex>hollow newel</xex>.
See under <er>Hollow</er>, <pos>a.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Solid
problem</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a problem which can be
construed geometrically, only by the intersection of a circle and a
conic section or of two conic sections.</cd> <au>Hutton.</au> --
<col><b>Solid square</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a square body or
troops in which the ranks and files are equal.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Hard; firm; compact; strong; substantial; stable;
sound; real; valid; true; just; weighty; profound; grave;
important.</syn> -- <usage><er>Solid</er>, <er>Hard</er>. These words
both relate to the internal constitution of bodies; but
<xex>hard</xex>notes a more impenetrable nature or a firmer adherence of
the component parts than <xex>solid</xex>. <xex>Hard</xex> is opposed to
<xex>soft</xex>, and <xex>solid</xex> to <xex>fluid</xex>,
<xex>liquid</xex>, <xex>open</xex>, or <xex>hollow</xex>. Wood is
usually <xex>solid</xex>; but some kinds of wood are <xex>hard</xex>,
and others are <xex>soft</xex>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Repose you there; while I [return] to this <qex>hard</qex>
house,<br/
More <qex>harder</qex> than the stones whereof 't is raised.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I hear his thundering voice resound,<br/
And trampling feet than shake the <qex>solid</qex> ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solid</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"id</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A substance that is held
in a fixed form by cohesion among its particles; a substance not
fluid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A magnitude which has length,
breadth, and thickness; a part of space bounded on all sides.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Solid of revolution</b></col>. <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Revolution</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 5.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidago</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sol`i*da"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>solidare</ets> to strengthen, unite; -- so called in allusion to
its reputed healing qualities.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of
yellow-flowered composite perennial herbs; golden-rod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidare</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*dare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>solidus</ets>. Cf. <er>Sou</er>.]</ety> <def>A small piece of
money.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidarity</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`i*dar"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>solidarit<eacute/</ets>, fr. <ets>solide</ets>. See
<er>Solid</er>.]</ety> <def>An entire union or consolidation of
interests and responsibilities; fellowship; community.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Solidarity</qex> [a word which we owe to the French
Communists], signifies a fellowship in gain and loss, in honor and
dishonor, in victory and defeat, a being, so to speak, all in the same
boat.</q> <rj><qau>Trench.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>solidarity</qex> . . . of Breton and Welsh poetry.</q>
<rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidary</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*da*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having community
of interests and responsibilities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> Men are <qex>solidary</qex>, or copartners; and not isolated.</q>
<rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidate</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*date</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solidatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>solidare</ets>. See
<er>Solder</er>.]</ety> <def>To make solid or firm.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cowley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solid-drawn</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"id-drawn`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Drawn out from a heated solid
bar, as by a process of spiral rolling which first hollows the bar and
then expands the cavity by forcing the bar over a pointed mandrel fixed
in front of the rolls; -- said of a weldless tube.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidifiable</ent><br/
<hw>So*lid"i*fi`a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being solidified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidification</ent><br/
<hw>So*lid`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>solidification</ets>.]</ety> <def>Act of solidifying, or state of
being solidified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidify</ent><br/
<hw>So*lid"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Solidified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Solidifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[<ets>Solid</ets> + <ets>-fy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>solidifier</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make solid or compact.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every machine is a <qex>solidified</qex> mechanical theorem.</q>
<rj><qau>H. Spencer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidify</ent><br/
<hw>So*lid"i*fy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become solid; to
harden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidism</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"id*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The doctrine that refers all diseases to morbid changes of the
solid parts of the body. It rests on the view that the solids alone are
endowed with vital properties, and can receive the impression of agents
tending to produce disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidist</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"id*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An advocate
of, or believer in, solidism.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidity</ent><br/
<hw>So*lid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>soliditas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>solidit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or quality of being solid; density;
consistency, -- opposed to <contr>fluidity</contr>; compactness;
fullness of matter, -- opposed to <contr>openness</contr> or
<contr>hollowness</contr>; strength; soundness, -- opposed to
<contr>weakness</contr> or <contr>instability</contr>; the primary
quality or affection of matter by which its particles exclude or resist
all others; hardness; massiveness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That which hinders the approach of two bodies when they are moving
one toward another, I call <qex>solidity</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Moral firmness; soundness; strength; validity;
truth; certainty; -- as opposed to <contr>weakness</contr> or
<contr>fallaciousness</contr>; <as>as, the <ex>solidity</ex> of
arguments or reasoning; the <ex>solidity</ex> of principles, triuths, or
opinions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The solid contents of a body;
volume; amount of inclosed space.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Firmness; solidness; hardness; density;
compactness; strength; soundness; validity; certainty.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidly</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"id*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a solid manner;
densely; compactly; firmly; truly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidness</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"id*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>State or quality of
being solid; firmness; compactness; solidity, as of material
bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Soundness; strength; truth; validity, as of
arguments, reasons, principles, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidungula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sol`id*un"gu*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from L. <ets>solidus</ets> solid + <ets>ungula</ets> a hoof.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tribe of ungulates which includes the horse,
ass, and related species, constituting the family
<fam>Equidae</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidungular</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`id*un"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Solipedous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidungulate</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`id*un"gu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Solid</ets> + <ets>ungulate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Soliped</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solidungulous</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`id*un"gu*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Solipedous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solifidian</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`i*fid"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solus</ets> alone + <ets>fides</ets> faith.]</ety>
<fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>One who maintains that faith alone, without
works, is sufficient for justification; -- opposed to
<contr>nullifidian</contr>.</def> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solifidian</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`i*fid"i*an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Holding the tenets of
Solifidians; of or pertaining to the solifidians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solifidianism</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`i*fid"i*an*ism</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
Solifidians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soliform</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sol</ets>
sun + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Like the sun in form, appearance, or
nature; resembling the sun.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Soliform</xex> things.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solifugae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*lif"u*gae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from L. <ets>solifuga</ets> (better <ets>solipuga</ets>), a kind of
venomous ant, or spider.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
arachnids having large, powerful fangs and a segmented abdomen; --
called also <altname>Solpugidea</altname>, and
<altname>Solpugides</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soliloquize</ent><br/
<hw>So*lil"o*quize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Soliloquized</conjf>
<pr>(#)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Soliloquizing</conjf>
<pr>(#)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To utter a soliloquy; to talk to one's
self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soliloquy</ent><br/
<hw>So*lil"o*quy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Soliloquies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>soliloquium</ets>; <ets>solus</ets> alone + <ets>loqui</ets> to
speak. See <er>Sole</er> ly, and <er>Loquacious</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of talking to one's self; a discourse made by one in
solitude to one's self; monologue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lovers are always allowed the comfort of <qex>soliloquy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A written composition, reciting what it is supposed
a person says to himself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The whole poem is a <qex>soliloquy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soliped</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*ped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>solip<egrave/de</ets>, It. <ets>solipede</ets>, Sp.
<ets>solipedo</ets>; apparently fr. L. <ets>solus</ets> alone +
<ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, a foot; but probably fr. L.
<ets>solidipes</ets> solid-footed, whole-hoofed. See <er>Solid</er>, and
<er>Pedal</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A mammal having a single
hoof on each foot, as the horses and asses; a solidungulate.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>solipede</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>solipeds</qex>, or firm-hoofed animals, as horses, asses,
and mules, etc., -- they are, also, in mighty number.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solipedous</ent><br/
<hw>So*lip"e*dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having single
hoofs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solipsism</ent><br/
<hw>So*lip"sism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solus</ets> alone + <ets>ipse</ets> self.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Ethics)</fld> <def>Egotism.</def>
<rj><au>Krauth-Fleming.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>Egoism.</def>
<rj><au>Krauth-Fleming.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solisequious</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`i*se"qui*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sol</ets> sun + <ets>sequi</ets> to follow.]</ety> <def>Following
the course of the sun; <as>as, <ex>solisequious</ex> plants</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solitaire</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`i*taire"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Solitary</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A person who lives in
solitude; a recluse; a hermit.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A single diamond in a setting; also, sometimes, a
precious stone of any kind set alone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Diamond <qex>solitaires</qex> blazing on his breast and
wrists.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs. R. H. Davis.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A game which one person can play alone; -- applied
to many games of cards, etc.; also, to a game played on a board with
pegs or balls, in which the object is, beginning with all the places
filled except one, to remove all but one of the pieces by
<ldquo/jumping,<rdquo/ as in draughts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A large extinct bird
(<spn>Pezophaps solitaria</spn>) which formerly inhabited the islands of
Mauritius and Rodrigeuz. It was larger and taller than the wild turkey.
Its wings were too small for flight. Called also
<altname>solitary</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any species of
American thrushlike birds of the genus <gen>Myadestes</gen>. They are
noted their sweet songs and retiring habits. Called also
<altname>fly-catching thrush</altname>. A West Indian species
(<spn>Myadestes sibilans</spn>) is called the <altname>invisible
bird</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solitarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`i*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Solitary</er>.]</ety> <def>A hermit; a solitary.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir R. Twisden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solitariety</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`i*ta*ri"e*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being solitary; solitariness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solitarily</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*ta*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a solitary
manner; in solitude; alone.</def> <rj><au>Mic. vii. 14.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solitariness</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*ta*ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Condition of being
solitary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solitary</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solitarius</ets>, fr. <ets>solus</ets> alone: cf. F.
<ets>solitaire</ets>. See <er>Sole</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Solitaire</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Living or being by one's
self; having no companion present; being without associates; single;
alone; lonely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those rare and <qex>solitary</qex>, these in flocks.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hie home unto my chamber,<br/
Where thou shalt find me, sad and <qex>solitary</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Performed, passed, or endured alone; <as>as, a
<ex>solitary</ex> journey; a <ex>solitary</ex> life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Satan . . . explores his <qex>solitary</qex> flight.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not much visited or frequented; remote from society;
retired; lonely; <as>as, a <ex>solitary</ex> residence or
place</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not inhabited or occupied; without signs of
inhabitants or occupation; desolate; deserted; silent; still; hence,
gloomy; dismal; <as>as, the <ex>solitary</ex> desert</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How doth the city sit <qex>solitary</qex>, that was full of
people.</q> <rj><qau>Lam. i. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let that night be <qex>solitary</qex>; let no joyful voice come
therein.</q> <rj><qau>Job iii. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Single; individual; sole; <as>as, a
<ex>solitary</ex> instance of vengeance; a <ex>solitary</ex>
example</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Not associated with others of the
same kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Solitary ant</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
solitary hymenopterous insect of the family <fam>Mutillidae</fam>. The
female of these insects is destitute of wings and has a powerful sting.
The male is winged and resembles a wasp. Called also <altname>spider
ant</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Solitary bee</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any species of bee which does not form
communities.</cd> -- <col><b>Solitary sandpiper</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an American tattler (<spn>Totanus
solitarius</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Solitary snipe</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the great snipe.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
-- <col><b>Solitary thrush</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>the
starling.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solitary</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*ta*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lives
alone, or in solitude; an anchoret; a hermit; a recluse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solitude</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"i*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>solitudo</ets>, <ets>solus</ets> alone. See <er>Sole</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>state of being alone, or withdrawn
from society; a lonely life; loneliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whosoever is delighted with <qex>solitude</qex> is either a wild
beast or a god.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O <qex>Solitude</qex>! where are the charms<br/
That sages have seen in thy face?</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Remoteness from society; destitution of company;
seclusion; -- said of places; <as>as, the <ex>solitude</ex> of a
wood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>solitude</qex> of his little parish is become matter of
great comfort to him.</q> <rj><qau>Law.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>solitary or lonely place; a desert or
wilderness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In these deep <qex>solitudes</qex> and awful cells<br/
Where heavenly pensive contemplation dwells.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> Loneliness; soitariness; loneness; retiredness;
recluseness. -- <er>Solitude</er>, <er>Retirement</er>,
<er>Seclusion</er>, <er>Loneliness</er>.</syn>
<usage><xex>Retirement</xex> is a withdrawal from general society,
implying that a person has been engaged in its scenes.
<xex>Solitude</xex> describes the fact that a person is alone;
<xex>seclusion</xex>, that he is shut out from others, usually by his
own choice; <xex>loneliness</xex>, that he feels the pain and oppression
of being alone. Hence, <xex>retirement</xex> is opposed to a gay,
active, or public life; <xex>solitude</xex>, to society;
<xex>seclusion</xex>, to freedom of access on the part of others; and
<xex>loneliness</xex>, enjoyment of that society which the heart
demands.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O blest <qex>retirement</qex>, friend to life's decline.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such only can enjoy the country who are capable of thinking when
they are there; then they are prepared for <qex>solitude</qex>; and in
that [the country] <qex>solitude</qex> is prepared for them.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a place of <qex>seclusion</qex> from the external world.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Horsley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These evils . . . seem likely to reduce it [a city] ere long to
the <qex>loneliness</qex> and the insignificance of a village.</q>
<rj><qau>Eustace.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solivagant</ent><br/
<hw>So*liv"a*gant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solus</ets> alone + <ets>vagans</ets> wandering.]</ety>
<def>Wandering alone.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>T.
Grander.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solivagous</ent><br/
<hw>So*liv"a*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solivagus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Solivagant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sollar</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>See
<er>Solar</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A platform in a shaft,
especially one of those between the series of ladders in a
shaft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sollar</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"lar</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cover, or provide with, a
sollar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sollein</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"lein</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sullen; sad.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solleret</ent><br/
<hw>Sol*ler*et"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>soleret</ets>im. fr. OF. <ets>soler</ets> shoe.]</ety> <def>A
flexible steel shoe (or one of the plates forming such a shoe), worn
with mediaeval armor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solmization</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`mi*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>solmisation</ets>, fr. <ets>solmiser</ets> to sol-fa; -- called
from the musical notes <ets>sol</ets>, <ets>mi</ets>. See
<er>Sol-fa</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The act of
sol-faing.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>solmisation</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><hand/ This art was practiced by the Greeks; but six of the
seven syllables now in use are generally attributed to Guido d' Arezzo,
an Italian monk of the eleventh century, who is said to have taken them
from the first syllables of the first six lines of the following stanza
of a monkish hymn to St. John the Baptist. --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Ut</qex> queant laxis<br/
<qex>Re</qex>sonare fibris<br/
<qex>Mi</qex>ra gestorum<br/
<qex>Fa</qex>muli tuorum<br/
<qex>Sol</qex>ve polluti<br/
<qex>La</qex>bii reatum,<br/
Sancte Joannes.</q><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>Professor Skeat says the name of the seventh note, <xex>si</xex>, was
also formed by him [Guido] from the initials of the two words of the
last line; but this is disputed, Littr<eacute/ attributing the first use
of it to Anselm of Flanders long afterwards. The syllable <xex>do</xex>
is often substituted for <xex>ut</xex>.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<p><ent>Solo</ent><br/
<hw>So"lo</hw> <pr>(s<omac/"l<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> E. <plw>Solos</plw> <pr>(s<omac/"l<omac/z)</pr>, It.
<plw>Soli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It., from L. <ets>solus</ets>
alone. See <er>Sole</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A
tune, air, strain, or a whole piece, played by a single person on an
instrument, or sung by a single voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solo</ent><br/
<hw>So"lo</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Music)</fld> <def>Performing, or
performed, alone; uncombined, except with subordinate parts, voices, or
instruments; not concerted.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soloist</ent><br/
<hw>So"lo*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>One who sings
or plays a solo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solomon</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"o*mon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the kings of
Israel, noted for his superior wisdom and magnificent reign; hence, a
very wise man.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sol`o*mon"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Solomon's seal</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
perennial liliaceous plant of the genus <gen>Polygonatum</gen>, having
simple erect or curving stems rising from thick and knotted rootstocks,
and with white or greenish nodding flowers. The commonest European
species is <spn>Polygonatum multiflorum</spn>. <spn>Polygonatum
biflorum</spn> and <spn>Polygonatum giganteum</spn> are common in the
Eastern United States. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Rootstock</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>False Solomon's seal</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any plant of the liliaceous genus
<gen>Smilacina</gen> having small whitish flowers in terminal racemes or
panicles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solomon's seal</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"o*mon's seal</hw>. <def>A mystic symbol consisting of two
interlaced triangles forming a star with six points, often with one
triangle dark and one light, symbolic of the union of soul and body. It
is shaped identically to the <er>hexagram</er> and <er>Star of
David</er>, distinguished only in its usage.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solon</ent><br/
<hw>So"lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A celebrated Athenian
lawmaker, born about 638 <sc>b. c.</sc>; hence, a legislator; a
publicist; -- often used ironically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solo whist</ent><br/
<hw>Solo whist</hw>. <def>A card game played with the full pack ranking
as at whist, each player declaring for which of seven different points
he proposes to play.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solpugid</ent><br/
<hw>Sol*pu"gid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Solifugae.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>One of the Solifugae.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solpugidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sol`pu*gid"e*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Solifugae</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Solifugae</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solstice</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"stice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>solstitium</ets>; <ets>sol</ets> the sun + <ets>sistere</ets> to
cause to stand, akin to <ets>stare</ets> to stand: cf. F.
<ets>solstice</ets>. See <er>Solar</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, <er>Stand</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A stopping or standing still of
the sun.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The point in the
ecliptic at which the sun is farthest from the equator, north or south,
namely, the first point of the sign Cancer and the first point of the
sign Capricorn, the former being the <xex>summer solstice</xex>, latter
the <xex>winter solstice</xex>, in northern latitudes; -- so called
because the sun then apparently stands still in its northward or
southward motion.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The time of the sun's passing
the solstices, or solstitial points, namely, about June 21 and December
21. See <xex>Illust.</xex> in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1370 pr=RH --></p>

<p><ent>Solstitial</ent><br/
<hw>Sol*sti"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solstitialis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>solsticial</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a solstice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Happening at a solstice; esp. (with reference to the
northern hemisphere), happening at the summer solstice, or
midsummer.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Solstitial</xex> summer's heat.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solubility</ent><br/
<hw>Sol`u*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>solubilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality,
condition, or degree of being soluble or solvable; <as>as, the
<ex>solubility</ex> of a salt; the <ex>solubility</ex> of a problem or
intricate difficulty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The tendency to separate readily
into parts by spurious articulations, as the pods of tick
trefoil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soluble</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"u*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solubilis</ets>, fr. <ets>solvere</ets>, <ets>solutum</ets>, to
loosen, to dissolve: cf. F. <ets>soluble</ets>. See <er>Solve</er>, and
cf. <er>Solvable</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Susceptible of being
dissolved in a fluid; capable of solution; <as>as, some substances are
<ex>soluble</ex> in alcohol which are not <ex>soluble</ex> in
water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sugar is . . . <qex>soluble</qex> in water and fusible in
fire.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Susceptible of being solved; <as>as, a
<ex>soluble</ex> algebraic problem</as>; susceptible of being
disentangled, unraveled, or explained; <as>as, the mystery is perhaps
<ex>soluble</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/More <xex>soluble</xex> is this
knot.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Relaxed; open or readily opened.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The bowels must be kept
<xex>soluble</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soluble glass</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Glass</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solubleness</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"u*ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
soluble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sola</ent><br/
<ent>Solus</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>So"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>masc. a.</pos>,
<hw>So"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>fem. a.</pos> }</mhw> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>Alone; -- chiefly used in stage directions, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solute</ent><br/
<hw>So*lute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solutus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>solvere</ets> to loosen. See
<er>Solve</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Loose; free; liberal; <as>as, a
<ex>solute</ex> interpretation</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relaxed; hence; merry; cheerful.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A brow <qex>solute</qex>, and ever-laughing eye.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Soluble; <as>as, a <ex>solute</ex> salt</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Not adhering; loose; -- opposed to
<contr>adnate</contr>; <as>as, a <ex>solute</ex> stipule</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solute</ent><br/
<hw>So*lute"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To dissolve; to
resolve.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To absolve; <as>as, to <ex>solute</ex>
sin</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solution</ent><br/
<hw>So*lu"tion</hw> <pr>(s<osl/*l<umac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>solucion</ets>, OF. <ets>solucion</ets>, F.
<ets>solution</ets>, fr. L. <ets>solutio</ets>, fr. <ets>solvere</ets>,
<ets>solutum</ets>, to loosen, dissolve. See <er>Solve</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of separating the parts of any body, or the
condition of undergoing a separation of parts; disruption;
breach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In all bodies there is an appetite of union and evitation of
<qex>solution</qex> of continuity.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of solving, or the state of being solved;
the disentanglement of any intricate problem or difficult question;
explanation; clearing up; -- used especially in mathematics, either of
the process of solving an equation or problem, or the result of the
process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The state of being dissolved or disintegrated;
resolution; disintegration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is unquestionably an enterprise of more promise to assail the
nations in their hour of faintness and <qex>solution</qex>, than at a
time when magnificent and seductive systems of worship were at their
height of energy and splendor.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Chem.Phys.)</fld> <def>The act or process by which
a body (whether solid, liquid, or gaseous) is absorbed into a liquid,
and, remaining or becoming fluid, is diffused throughout the solvent;
also, the product resulting from such absorption.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ When a solvent will not take in any more of a substance
the solution is said to be <xex>saturated</xex>. Solution is of two
kinds; viz.: (<it>a</it>) <stype>Mechanical solution</stype>, in which
no marked chemical change takes place, and in which, in the case of
solids, the dissolved body can be regained by evaporation, as in the
solution of salt or sugar in water. (<it>b</it>) <stype>Chemical
solution</stype>, in which there is involved a decided chemical change,
as when limestone or zinc undergoes solution in hydrochloric acid.
<stype>Mechanical solution</stype> is regarded as a form of molecular or
atomic attraction, and is probably occasioned by the formation of
certain very weak and unstable compounds which are easily dissociated
and pass into new and similar compounds.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word is not used in chemistry or mineralogy for
<xex>fusion</xex>, or the melting of bodies by the heat of
fire.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Release; deliverance; discharge.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The termination of a
disease; resolution.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A crisis.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A liquid medicine or preparation (usually aqueous) in
which the solid ingredients are wholly soluble.</def> <rj><au>U. S.
Disp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Fehling's solution</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
standardized solution of cupric hydrate in sodium potassium tartrate,
used as a means of determining the reducing power of certain sugars and
sirups by the amount of red cuprous oxide thrown down.</cd> --
<col><b>Heavy solution</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a liquid of high
density, as a solution of mercuric iodide in potassium iodide (called
the <stype>Sonstadt solution</stype> or <stype>Thoulet solution</stype>)
having a maximum specific gravity of 3.2, or of borotungstate of cadium
(<stype>Klein solution</stype>, specific gravity 3.6), and the like.
Such solutions are much used in determining the specific gravities of
minerals, and in separating them when mechanically mixed as in a
pulverized rock.</cd> -- <col><b>Nessler's solution</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Nesslerize</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Solution of continuity</b></col>,
<cd>the separation of connection, or of connected substances or parts;
-- applied, in surgery, to a fracture, laceration, or the like.</cd>
<ldquo/As in the natural body a wound, or <xex>solution of
continuity</xex>, is worse than a corrupt humor, so in the
spiritual.<rdquo/ <au>Bacon.</au> -- <col><b>Standardized
solution</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a solution which is used as a
reagent, and is of a known and standard strength; specifically, a normal
solution, containing in each cubic centimeter as many milligrams of the
element in question as the number representing its atomic weight; thus,
a normal solution of silver nitrate would contain 107.7 mgr. of silver
in each cubic centimeter.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solutive</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"u*tive</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/l"<usl/*t<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>solutif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to dissolve;
loosening; laxative.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solvability</ent><br/
<hw>Solv`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>solvabilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being solvable; <as>as, the <ex>solvability</ex> of a
difficulty; the <ex>solvability</ex> of a problem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The condition of being solvent; ability to pay all
just debts; solvency; <as>as, the <ex>solvability</ex> of a
merchant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solvable</ent><br/
<hw>Solv"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>solvable</ets>. See <er>Solve</er>, and cf. <er>Soluble</er>,
<er>Solvible</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Susceptible of being solved,
resolved, or explained; admitting of solution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being paid and discharged; <as>as,
<ex>solvable</ex> obligations</as>.</def> <rj><au>Tooke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Able to pay one's debts; solvent.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solvableness</ent><br/
<hw>Solv"a*ble*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of
being solvable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solve</ent><br/
<hw>Solve</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/lv)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Solved</conjf> <pr>(s<ocr/lvd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Solving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>solvere</ets>, <ets>solutum</ets>; from a prefix <ets>so-</ets>
expressing separation (cf. <er>Sober</er>) + <ets>luere</ets> to loosen;
cf. OF. <ets>soldre</ets>, <ets>soudre</ets>. See <er>Loose</er>, and
cf. <er>Absolve</er>.]</ety> <def>To explain; to resolve; to unfold; to
clear up (what is obscure or difficult to be understood); to work out to
a result or conclusion; <as>as, to <ex>solve</ex> a doubt; to
<ex>solve</ex> difficulties; to <ex>solve</ex> a problem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>True piety would effectually <qex>solve</qex> such scruples.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God shall <qex>solve</qex> the dark decrees of fate.</q>
<rj><qau>Tickell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To explain; resolve; unfold; clear up.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solve</ent><br/
<hw>Solve</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A solution; an explanation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solvency</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"ven*cy</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/l"v<eit/n*s<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[See <er>Solvent</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
solvent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solvend</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"vend</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/l"v<ecr/nd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solvendus</ets> to be loosened or dissolved, fr.
<ets>solvere</ets>. See <er>Solution</er>.]</ety> <def>A substance to be
dissolved.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solvent</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"vent</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/l"v<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>solvens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>solvere</ets>. See
<er>Solvable</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the power of
dissolving; dissolving; <as>as, a <ex>solvent</ex> fluid</as>.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>solvent</xex> body.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Able or sufficient to pay all just debts; <as>as, a
<ex>solvent</ex> merchant; the estate is
<ex>solvent</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solvent</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"vent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A substance
(usually liquid) suitable for, or employed in, solution, or in
dissolving something; <as>as, water is the appropriate <ex>solvent</ex>
of most salts, alcohol of resins, ether of fats, and mercury or acids of
metals, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which resolves; <as>as, a <ex>solvent</ex> of
mystery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solver</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"ver</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/lv"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who, or that which, solves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Solvible</ent><br/
<hw>Solv"i*ble</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*b'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Solvable</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soly</ent><br/
<hw>Sol"y</hw> <pr>(s<omac/l"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Solely.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"ma</hw> <pr>(s<omac/"m<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>sw^ma</grk>, <grk>sw`matos</grk>, the body.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The whole axial portion of an animal, including
the head, neck, trunk, and tail.</def> <rj><au>B. G.
Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Samaj</ent><br/
<ent>Somaj</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So"maj"</hw> <pr>(s<osl/*m<aum/j")</pr>, <hw>Sa*maj"</hw>
<pr>(s<adot/*m<aum/j")</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A society; a
congregation, a worshiping assembly, or church, esp. of the
Brahmo-somaj.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somal</ent><br/
<ent>Somali</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So*ma"li</hw> <pr>(s<osl/*m<aum/"l<esl/)</pr>,
<hw>So*mal"</hw> <pr>(s<osl/*m<aum/l")</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld> <def>A Hamitic people of East Central
Africa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>of or pertaining to Somalia or to the inhabitants of
Somalia.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatic</ent><br/
<hw>So*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(s<osl/*m<acr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>swmatiko`s</grk>, fr. <grk>sw^ma</grk> the body.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the body as a whole; corporeal;
<as>as, <ex>somatic</ex> death; <ex>somatic</ex> changes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the wall of the body;
somatopleuric; parietal; <as>as, the <ex>somatic</ex> stalk of the yolk
sac of an embryo</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Somatic death</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Death</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatical</ent><br/
<hw>So*mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Somatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatics</ent><br/
<hw>So*mat"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The science which
treats of the general properties of matter; somatology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatist</ent><br/
<hw>So"ma*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who admits the
existence of material beings only; a materialist.</def>
<rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatocyst</ent><br/
<hw>So"ma*to*cyst</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sw^ma</grk>, <grk>sw`matos</grk>, body + <grk>ky`stis</grk> a
bladder.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A cavity in the primary
nectocalyx of certain <ord>Siphonophora</ord>. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
under <er>Nectocalyx</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatology</ent><br/
<hw>So`ma*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sw^ma</grk>, <grk>sw`matos</grk>, body + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The doctrine or the science of the general properties
of material substances; somatics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A treatise on the human body; anatomy.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The science which treats of
anatomy and physiology, apart from psychology.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anthropol.)</fld> <def>The consideration of the
physical characters of races and classes of men and of mankind in
general.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatome</ent><br/
<hw>So"ma*tome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sw^ma</grk>, body + <grk>te`mnein</grk> to cut.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.
& Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Somite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatopleure</ent><br/
<hw>So"ma*to*pleure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sw^ma</grk>, <grk>sw`matos</grk>, body + <grk>pleyra`</grk>
side.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The outer, or parietal, one of the
two lamellae into which the vertebrate blastoderm divides on either side
of the notochord, and from which the walls of the body and the amnion
are developed. See <er>Splanchnopleure</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatopleuric</ent><br/
<hw>So`ma*to*pleu"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the somatopleure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somatotropism</ent><br/
<hw>So`ma*tot"ro*pism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sw^ma</grk>, <grk>sw`matos</grk>, the body + <grk>tre`pein</grk> to
turn.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A directive influence exercised
by a mass of matter upon growing organs.</def> <rj><au>Encyc.
Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sombre</ent><br/
<ent>Somber</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Som"ber</hw>, <hw>Som"bre</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sombre</ets>; cf. Sp. <ets>sombra</ets>,
shade, prob. from LL. <ets>subumbrare</ets> to put in the shade; L.
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>umbra</ets> shade. See
<er>Umbrage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Dull; dusky; somewhat dark;
gloomy; <as>as, a <ex>somber</ex> forest; a <ex>somber</ex>
house.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Melancholy; sad; grave; depressing; <as>as, a
<ex>somber</ex> person; <ex>somber</ex> reflections.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dinner was silent and <qex>somber</qex>; happily it was also
short.</q> <rj><qau>Beaconsfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sombre</ent><br/
<ent>Somber</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Som"ber</hw>, <hw>Som"bre</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To make somber, or dark; to make shady.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sombre</ent><br/
<ent>Somber</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Som"ber</hw>, <hw>Som"bre</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Gloom; obscurity; duskiness; somberness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sombrely</ent><br/
<ent>Somberly</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Som"ber*ly</hw>, <hw>Som"bre*ly</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In a somber manner; sombrously; gloomily; despondingly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sombreness</ent><br/
<ent>Somberness</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Som"ber*ness</hw>, <hw>Som"bre*ness</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being somber;
gloominess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sombrero</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Som*bre"ro</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp., from
<ets>sombra</ets> shade. See <er>Sombre</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of
broad-brimmed hat, worn in Spain and in Spanish America.</def>
<rj><au>Marryat.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sombrous</ent><br/
<hw>Som"brous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sp.
<ets>sombroso</ets>.]</ety> <def>Gloomy; somber.</def> <ldquo/Tall and
<xex>sombrous</xex> pines.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Som"brous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Som"brous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-some</ent><br/
<hw>-some</hw> <pr>(-s<omac/m)</pr>. <def>A combining form or suffix
from Gr. <grk>sw^ma</grk> (gen. <grk>sw`matos</grk>) the
<xex>body</xex>; <as>as in mero<ex>some</ex>, a body segment;
cephalo<ex>some</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- add chromosome, ribosome, --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-some</ent><br/
<hw>-some</hw> <pr>(-s<ucr/m)</pr>. <ety>[AS. <ets>-sum</ets>; akin to
G. & OHG. <ets>-sam</ets>, Icel. <ets>samr</ets>, Goth.
lustu<ets>sams</ets> longed for. See <er>Same</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Some</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>An adjective suffix having
primarily the sense of <xex>like</xex> or <xex>same</xex>, and
indicating a considerable degree of the thing or quality denoted in the
first part of the compound; as in mettle<xex>some</xex>, full of mettle
or spirit; glad<xex>some</xex>, full of gladness; win<xex>some</xex>,
blithe<xex>some</xex>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Some</ent><br/
<hw>Some</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>som</ets>, <ets>sum</ets>, AS. <ets>sum</ets>; akin to OS.,
OFries., & OHG. <ets>sum</ets>, OD. <ets>som</ets>, D.
<ets>sommig</ets>, Icel. <ets>sumr</ets>, Dan. <ets>somme</ets> (pl.),
Sw. <ets>somlige</ets> (pl.), Goth. <ets>sums</ets>, and E.
<ets>same</ets>. <root/191. See <er>Same</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf.
<er>-some</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of a greater or less
portion or sum; composed of a quantity or number which is not stated; --
used to express an indefinite quantity or number; <as>as, <ex>some</ex>
wine; <ex>some</ex> water; <ex>some</ex> persons</as>. Used also
pronominally; <as>as, I have <ex>some</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Some</qex> theoretical writers allege that there was a time
when there was no such thing as society.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A certain; one; -- indicating a person, thing,
event, etc., as not known individually, or designated more specifically;
<as>as, <ex>some</ex> man, that is, some one man</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Some</xex> brighter clime.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mrs.
Barbauld.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Some</qex> man praiseth his neighbor by a wicked intent.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Most gentlemen of property, at <qex>some</qex> period or other of
their lives, are ambitious of representing their county in
Parliament.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not much; a little; moderate; <as>as, the censure
was to <ex>some</ex> extent just</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>About; near; more or less; -- used commonly with
numerals, but formerly also with a singular substantive of time or
distance; <as>as, a village of <ex>some</ex> eighty houses;
<ex>some</ex> two or three persons; <ex>some</ex> hour
hence</as>.</def><-- approximately. --> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The number slain on the rebel's part were <qex>some</qex> two
thousand.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Considerable in number or quantity.</def>
<ldquo/Bore us <xex>some</xex> leagues to sea.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On its outer point, <qex>some</qex> miles away.<br/
The lighthouse lifts its massive masonry.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Certain; those of one part or portion; -- in
distinction from <contr>other</contr> or <contr>others</contr>; <as>as,
<ex>some</ex> men believe one thing, and <ex>others</ex>
another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Some</qex> [seeds] fell among thorns; . . . but
<qex>other</qex> fell into good ground.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. xiii. 7,
8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A part; a portion; -- used pronominally, and
followed sometimes by <xex>of</xex>; <as>as, <ex>some</ex> of our
provisions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your edicts <qex>some</qex> reclaim from sins,<br/
But most your life and blest example wins.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>All and some</b></col>, <cd>one and all. See under
<er>All</er>, <pos>adv.</pos></cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The illiterate in the United States and Scotland often
use <xex>some</xex> as an adverb, instead of <xex>somewhat</xex>, or an
equivalent expression; as, I am <xex>some</xex> tired; he is
<xex>some</xex> better; it rains <xex>some</xex>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Some . . . some</b></col>, <cd>one part . . . another
part; these . . . those; -- used distributively.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Some</qex> to the shores do fly,<br/
<qex>Some</qex> to the woods, or whither fear advised.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Formerly used also of single persons or things: this one
. . . that one; one . . . another.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Some</qex> in his bed, <qex>some</qex> in the deep sea.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somebody</ent><br/
<hw>Some"bod*y</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/m"b<ocr/d*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A person unknown or uncertain; a person indeterminate;
some person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jesus said, <qex>Somebody</qex> hath touched me.</q> <rj><qau>Luke
viii. 46.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We must draw in <qex>somebody</qex> that may stand<br/
'Twixt us and danger.</q> <rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person of consideration or importance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before these days rose up Theudas, boasting himself to be
<qex>somebody</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Acts v. 36.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somedeal</ent><br/
<hw>Some"deal`</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/m"d<emac/l`)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In some degree; somewhat.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sumdel</asp>, <asp>sumdeale</asp>, and <asp>sumdele</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/She was <xex>somedeal</xex> deaf.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou lackest <qex>somedeal</qex> their delight.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somehow</ent><br/
<hw>Some"how`</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/m"hou`)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In one
way or another; in some way not yet known or designated; by some means;
<as>as, the thing must be done <ex>somehow</ex>; he lives
<ex>somehow</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By their action upon one another they may be swelled
<qex>somehow</qex>, so as to shorten the length.</q>
<rj><qau>Cheyne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The indefiniteness of <xex>somehow</xex> is emphasized
by the addition of <xex>or other</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Although youngest of the familly, he has <qex>somehow or
other</qex> got the entire management of all the others.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somerset</ent><br/
<ent>Somersault</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Som"er*sault</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Som"er*set</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>soubresaut</ets> a
jump, leap, OF. <ets>soubresault</ets>, It. <ets>soprassalto</ets> an
overleap, fr. L. <ets>supra</ets> over + <ets>saltus</ets> a leap, fr.
<ets>salire</ets> to leap; or the French may be from Sp.
<ets>sobresalto</ets> a sudden asault, a surprise. See <er>Supra</er>,
and <ets>Salient</ets>.]</ety> <def>A leap in which a person turns his
heels over his head and lights upon his feet; a turning end over
end.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>summersault</asp>,
<asp>sommerset</asp>, <asp>summerset</asp>, etc.]</altsp> <ldquo/The
vaulter's <xex>sombersalts</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now I'll only<br/
Make him break his neck in doing a <qex>sommerset</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Something</ent><br/
<hw>Some"thing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Anything unknown, undetermined, or not specifically designated; a
certain indefinite thing; an indeterminate or unknown event; an
unspecified task, work, or thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is <qex>something</qex> in the wind.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The whole world has <qex>something</qex> to do,
<qex>something</qex> to talk of, <qex>something</qex> to wish for, and
<qex>something</qex> to be employed about.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Something</qex> attemped, <qex>something</qex> done,<br/
Has earned a night's repose.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A part; a portion, more or less; an indefinite
quantity or degree; a little.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Something</qex> yet of doubt remains.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Something</qex> of it arises from our infant state.</q>
<rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A person or thing importance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If a man thinketh himself to be <qex>something</qex>, when he is
nothing, he deceiveth himself.</q> <rj><qau>Gal. vi. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Something</ent><br/
<hw>Some"thing</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In some degree; somewhat; to
some extent; at some distance.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>something</qex> fear my father's wrath.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have <qex>something</qex> fairer play than a reasoner could
have expected formerly.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My sense of touch is <qex>something</qex> coarse.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It must be done to-night,<br/
And <qex>something</qex> from the palace.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sometime</ent><br/
<hw>Some"time`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>At a
past time indefinitely referred to; once; formerly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Did they not <qex>sometime</qex> cry <ldquo/All hail<rdquo/ to
me?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>At a time undefined; once in a while; now and then;
sometimes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sometime</qex> we see a cloud that's dragonish,<br/
A vapor <qex>sometime</qex> like a bear or lion.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>At one time or other hereafter; <as>as, I will do it
<ex>sometime</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sometime</xex> he reckon
shall.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1371 pr=RH --></p>

<p><ent>Sometime</ent><br/
<hw>Some"time`</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/m"t<imac/m`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Having been formerly; former; late; whilom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our <qex>sometime</qex> sister, now our queen.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ion, our <qex>sometime</qex> darling, whom we prized.</q>
<rj><qau>Talfourd.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sometimes</ent><br/
<hw>Some"times`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sometime</ets> + adverbial ending <ets>-s</ets>, as in
<ets>-wards</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Formerly; sometime.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That fair and warlike form<br/
In which the majesty of buried Denmark<br/
Did <qex>sometimes</qex> march.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>At times; at intervals; not always; now and then;
occasionally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is good that we <qex>sometimes</qex> be contradicted.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sometimes . . . sometimes</b></col>, <cd>at certain times
. . . at certain other times; <as>as, <ex>sometimes</ex> he is earnest,
<ex>sometimes</ex> he is frivolous</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sometimes</ent><br/
<hw>Some"times`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Former; sometime.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy <qex>sometimes</qex> brother's wife.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somewhat</ent><br/
<hw>Some"what`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>More or
less; a certain quantity or degree; a part, more or less;
something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These salts have <qex>somewhat</qex> of a nitrous taste.</q>
<rj><qau>Grew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Somewhat</qex> of his good sense will suffer, in this
transfusion, and much of the beauty of his thoughts will be lost.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person or thing of importance; a
somebody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here come those that worship me.<br/
They think that I am <qex>somewhat</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somewhat</ent><br/
<hw>Some"what`</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In some degree or measure; a
little.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His giantship is gone, <qex>somewhat</qex> crestfallen.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Somewhat</qex> back from the village street.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somewhen</ent><br/
<hw>Some"when`</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>At some indefinite time.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somewhere</ent><br/
<hw>Some"where`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In some place
unknown or not specified; in one place or another.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Somewhere</xex> nigh at hand.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somewhile</ent><br/
<hw>Some"while`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Once; for a
time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though, under color of shepherds, <qex>somewhile</qex><br/
There crept in wolves, full of fraud and guile.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somewhither</ent><br/
<hw>Some"whith`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>To some
indeterminate place; to some place or other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Driven by the winds of temptation <qex>somewhither</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somite</ent><br/
<hw>So"mite</hw> <pr>(s<omac/"m<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sw^ma</grk> body.]</ety> <fld>(Anat. & Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
actual or ideal serial segments of which an animal, esp. an articulate
or vertebrate, is composed; somatome; metamere.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>So*mit`ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sommeil</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Som`meil"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Slumber; sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sommerset</ent><br/
<hw>Som"mer*set</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Somersault</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambular</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam"bu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to somnambulism; somnambulistic.</def> <rj><au>Mrs.
Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambulate</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam"bu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To
walk when asleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambulation</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam`bu*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>somnus</ets> sleep + <ets>ambulatio</ets> a walking about, from
<ets>ambulare</ets> to walk. See <er>Somnolent</er>,
<er>Amble</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of walking in sleep, called also
<altname>sleepwalking</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambulator</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam"bu*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
somnambulist.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> sleepwalker.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambule</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam"bule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
somnambulist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambulic</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam"bu*lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Somnambulistic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambulism</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam"bu*lism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>somnambulisme</ets>. See <er>Somnambulation</er>.]</ety> <def>A
condition of the nervous system in which an individual during sleep
performs actions appropriate to the waking state; a state of sleep in
which some of the senses and voluntary powers are partially awake;
noctambulism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambulist</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam"bu*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person who
is subject to somnambulism; one who walks in his sleep; a sleepwalker; a
noctambulist.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> somnambulator.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnambulistic</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nam`bu*lis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a somnambulist or somnambulism; affected by somnambulism;
appropriate to the state of a somnambulist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whether this was an intentional and waking departure, or a
<qex>somnambulistic</qex> leave-taking and walking in her sleep, may
remain a subject of contention.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somne</ent><br/
<hw>Som"ne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To summon.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somner</ent><br/
<hw>Som"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A summoner; esp., one
who summons to an ecclesiastical court.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnial</ent><br/
<hw>Som"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>somnialis</ets> dream bringing, fr. <ets>somnium</ets> dream, fr.
<ets>somnus</ets> sleep.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to sleep or
dreams.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>somnial</qex> magic superinduced on, without suspending,
the active powers of the mind.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniative</ent><br/
<hw>Som"ni*a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somnial;
somniatory.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniatory</ent><br/
<hw>Som"ni*a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to
sleep or dreams; somnial.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Urquhart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniculous</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nic"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>somniculosus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Inclined to sleep; drowsy;
sleepy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>somnifer</ets>; <ets>somnus</ets> sleep + <ets>ferre</ets> to
bring.]</ety> <def>Causing or inducing sleep; soporific; dormitive;
<as>as, a <ex>somniferous</ex> potion</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnific</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>somnificus</ets>; <ets>somnus</ets> sleep + <ets>facere</ets> to
make.]</ety> <def>Causing sleep; somniferous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnifugous</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nif"u*gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>somnus</ets> sleep + <ets>fugare</ets> to put to flight.]</ety>
<def>Driving away sleep.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniloquence</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nil"o*quence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
talking in one's sleep; somniloquism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniloquism</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nil"o*quism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
habit of talking in one's sleep; somniloquy.</def>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniloquist</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nil"o*quist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who talks in his
sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniloquous</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nil"o*quous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>somnus</ets> sleep + <ets>loqui</ets> to speak.]</ety> <def>Apt to
talk in sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somniloquy</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nil"o*quy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A talking in
sleep; the talking of one in a state of somnipathy.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnipathist</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nip"a*thist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person in a
state of somniapathy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnipathy</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nip"a*thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>somnus</ets> sleep + Gr. <?/ a suffering of the body, fr. <?/, <?/,
to suffer.]</ety> <def>Sleep from sympathy, or produced by mesmerism or
the like.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>somnopathy</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnolency</ent><br/
<ent>Somnolence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Som"no*lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Som"no*len*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>somnolentia</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>somnolence</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sleepiness; drowsiness;
inclination to sleep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnolent</ent><br/
<hw>Som"no*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>somnolent</ets>, L. <ets>somnolentus</ets>, from <ets>somnus</ets>
sleep, akin to Gr. <?/, Skr. <ets>svapna</ets> sleep, dream,
<ets>svap</ets> to sleep, Icel. <ets>sofa</ets>, AS. <ets>swefn</ets>
sleep. Cf. <er>Hypnotic</er>, <er>Somnambulism</er>,
<er>Soporific</er>.]</ety> <def>Sleepy; drowsy; inclined to sleep.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Som"no*lent*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had no eye for such phenomena, because he had a
<qex>somnolent</qex> want of interest in them.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>somnolism</ent><br/
<hw>som"no*lism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
somnolent state induced by animal magnetism (hypnotism); the hypnotic
state.</def> <mark>[archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Thomas (Med.
Dict.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>the act or process of inducing the hypnotic state;
hypnotism; hyonotizing.</def> <au>Stedman.</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnopathy</ent><br/
<hw>Som*nop"a*thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Somnipathy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somnour</ent><br/
<hw>Som"nour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A summoner; an
apparitor; a sompnour.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somonce</ent><br/
<ent>Somonaunce</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Som"on*aunce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Som"once</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Summon</er>,
<er>Summons</er>.]</ety> <def>A summons; a citation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Somonour</ent><br/
<hw>Som"on*our</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A summoner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sompne</ent><br/
<hw>Somp"ne</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To summon;
to cite.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sompnour</ent><br/
<hw>Somp"nour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A summoner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Son</ent><br/
<hw>Son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sone</ets>,
<ets>sune</ets>, AS. <ets>sunu</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zoon</ets>, OS.,
OFries., & OHG. <ets>sunu</ets>, G. <ets>sohn</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sonr</ets>, Sw. <ets>son</ets>, Dan. <ets>s<oum/n</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sunus</ets>, Lith. <ets>sunus</ets>, Russ. <ets>suin'</ets>, Skr.
<ets>s<umac/nu</ets> (from <ets>s<umac/</ets> to beget, to bear), and
Gr. <?/ son. <root/293. Cf. <er>Sow</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A male child; the male issue, or offspring, of a
parent, father or mother.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sarah conceived, and bare Abraham a <qex>son</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xxi. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A male descendant, however distant; hence, in the
plural, descendants in general.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am the <qex>son</qex> of the wise, the <qex>son</qex> of ancient
kings.</q> <rj><qau>Isa. xix. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am the Lord, I change not; therefore ye <qex>sons</qex> of Jacob
are not consumed.</q> <rj><qau>Mal. iii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any young male person spoken of as a child; an
adopted male child; a pupil, ward, or any other young male
dependent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The child grew, and she brought him unto Pharaoh's daughter, and
he became her <qex>son</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. ii. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be plain, good <qex>son</qex>, and homely in thy drift.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A native or inhabitant of some specified place;
<as>as, <ex>sons</ex> of Albion; <ex>sons</ex> of New
England</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The produce of anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Earth's tall <qex>sons</qex>, the cedar, oak, and pine.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackmore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> (Commonly with the def. article) <def>Jesus Christ, the
Savior; -- called the <xex>Son</xex> of God, and the <xex>Son</xex> of
man.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We . . . do testify that the Father sent the <qex>Son</qex> to be
the Savior of the world.</q> <rj><qau>1 John iv. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who gave His <qex>Son</qex> sure all has given.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The expressions <xex>son of pride</xex>, <xex>sons of
light</xex>, <xex>son of Belial</xex>, are Hebraisms, which denote
persons possessing the qualitites of pride, of light, or of Belial, as
children inherit the qualities of their ancestors.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sons of the prophets</b></col>. <cd>See <xex>School of
the prophets</xex>, under <er>Prophet</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonance</ent><br/
<hw>So"nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sound;
a tune; <as>as, to sound the tucket <ex>sonance</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being sonant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonant</ent><br/
<hw>So"nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sonans</ets>,
<ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sonare</ets> to sound. See
<er>Sound</er> a noise.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
sound; sounding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <def>Uttered, as an element of
speech, with tone or proper vocal sound, as distinguished from mere
breath sound; intonated; voiced; vocal; tonic; the opposite of
<contr>nonvocal</contr>, or <contr>surd</contr>; -- said of the vowels,
semivowels, liquids, and nasals, and particularly of the consonants
<xex>b</xex>, <xex>d</xex>, <xex>g</xex> hard, <xex>v</xex>, etc., as
compared with their cognates <xex>p</xex>, <xex>t</xex>, <xex>k</xex>,
<xex>f</xex>, etc., which are called <xex>nonvocal</xex>,
<xex>surd</xex>, or <xex>aspirate</xex>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A sonant letter.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonata</ent><br/
<hw>So*na"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., fr. It. & L.
<ets>sonare</ets> to sound. See <er>Sound</er> a noise.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An extended composition for one or two
instruments, consisting usually of three or four movements; <as>as,
Beethoven's <ex>sonatas</ex> for the piano, for the violin and piano,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The same general structure prevails in symphonies,
instrumental trios, quartets, etc., and even in classical concertos. The
sonata form, distinctively, characterizes the quick opening movement,
which may have a short, slow introduction; the second, or slow, movement
is either in the song or variation form; third comes the playful minuet
or the more modern scherzo; then the quick finale in the rondo form. But
both form and order are sometimes exceptional.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonatina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So`na*ti"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A short and simple sonata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonsy</ent><br/
<ent>Soncy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Son"cy</hw>, <hw>Son"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Scot. <ets>sonce</ets>, <ets>sons</ets>, prosperity,
happiness, fr. Gael. & Ir. <ets>sonas</ets>.]</ety> <def>Lucky;
fortunate; thriving; plump.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonde</ent><br/
<ent>Sond</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sond</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sonde</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[AS. <ets>sand</ets>. See <er>Send</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is sent; a message or messenger; hence,
also, a visitation of providence; an affliction or trial.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye have enough, parde, of Goddes <qex>sond</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rocketry)</fld> <def>a rocket or balloon sent into
the upper atmosphere to measure atmospheric conditions or other
phenomena at altitudes unattainable by aircraft.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sondeli</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Son"de*li</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The musk shrew. See under <er>Musk</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonderclass</ent><br/
<hw>Son"der*class`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>sonderklasse</ets> special class.]</ety> <fld>(Yachting)</fld>
<def>A special class of small yachts developed in Germany under the
patronage of <person>Emperor William</person> and <person>Prince
Henry</person> of Prussia, and so called because these yachts do not
conform to the restrictions for the regular classes established by the
rules of the International Yacht Racing Union. In yachts of the
sonderclass, as prescribed for the season of 1911, the aggregate of the
length on water line, extreme beam, and extreme draft must be not more
than 32 feet; the weight, not less than 4,035 pounds (without crew); the
sail area, not more than 550 square yards; and the cost of construction
(for American boats) not more than $2400. The crew must be amateurs and
citizens of the country in which the yacht was built.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Song</ent><br/
<hw>Song</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/ng; 115)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>song</ets>, <ets>sang</ets>, fr. <ets>singan</ets> to sing; akin to
D. <ets>zang</ets>, G. <ets>sang</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<oum/ngr</ets>,
Goth. <ets>saggws</ets>. See <er>Sing</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
which is sung or uttered with musical modulations of the voice, whether
of a human being or of a bird, insect, etc.</def> <ldquo/That most
ethereal of all sounds, the <xex>song</xex> of crickets.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A lyrical poem adapted to vocal music; a
ballad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>More generally, any poetical strain; a
poem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bard that first adorned our native tongue<br/
Tuned to his British lyre this ancient <qex>song</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Poetical composition; poetry; verse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This subject for heroic <qex>song</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An object of derision; a laughingstock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And now am I their <qex>song</qex>, yea, I am their byword.</q>
<rj><qau>Job xxx. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A trifle; an insignificant sum of money; <as>as, he
bought it for a <ex>song</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/The soldier's pay is a
<xex>song</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Silliman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Old song</b></col>, <cd>a trifle; nothing of value.</cd>
<ldquo/I do not intend to be thus put off with an <xex>old
song</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Dr. H. More.</au> -- <col><b>Song bird</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any singing bird; one of the
<ord>Oscines</ord>.</cd> -- <col><b>Song sparrow</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a very common North American sparrow
(<spn>Melospiza fasciata</spn>, or <spn>Melospiza melodia</spn>) noted
for the sweetness of its song in early spring. Its breast is covered
with dusky brown streaks which form a blotch in the center.</cd> --
<col><b>Song thrush</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a common European
thrush (<spn>Turdus musicus</spn>), noted for its melodius song; --
called also <altname>mavis</altname>, <altname>throstle</altname>, and
<altname>thrasher</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sonnet; ballad; canticle; carol; canzonet; ditty;
hymn; descant; lay; strain; poesy; verse.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Songcraft</ent><br/
<hw>Song"craft`</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/ng"kr<adot/ft`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The art of making songs or verses; metrical composition;
versification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A half-effaced inscription,<br/
Written with little skill of <qex>songcraft</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Songful</ent><br/
<hw>Song"ful</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/ng"f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Disposed to sing; full of song.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Songish</ent><br/
<hw>Song"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of songs.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Songless</ent><br/
<hw>Song"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of the power of song;
without song; <as>as, <ex>songless</ex> birds; <ex>songless</ex>
woods</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Songster</ent><br/
<hw>Song"ster</hw> <pr>(s<ocr/ng"st<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[AS. <ets>sangestre</ets> a female singer.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who sings; one skilled in singing; -- not often applied to
human beings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A singing bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Songstress</ent><br/
<hw>Song"stress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Songster</er>, and <er>-ess</er>.]</ety> <def>A woman who sings;
also, a female singing bird.</def> <rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonifer</ent><br/
<hw>Son"i*fer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Soniferous</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of ear trumpet for the deaf, or
the partially deaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soniferous</ent><br/
<hw>So*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sonus</ets> sound + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sounding;
producing sound; conveying sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonification</ent><br/
<hw>Son`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sonus</ets> sound + <ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.) to make. See
<er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of producing sound, as the
stridulation of insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Son-in-law</ent><br/
<hw>Son"-in-law`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sons-in-law</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The husband of one's
daughter; a man in his relationship to his wife's parents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To take me as for thy <qex>son in lawe</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonless</ent><br/
<hw>Son"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being without a son.</def>
<rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As no baron who was <qex>sonless</qex> could give a husband to his
daughter, save with his lord's consent.</q> <rj><qau>J. R.
Green.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonnet</ent><br/
<hw>Son"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. It.
<ets>sonetto</ets>, fr. <ets>suono</ets> a sound, a song, fr. L.
<ets>sonus</ets> a sound. See <er>Sound</er> noise.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A short poem, -- usually amatory.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had a wonderful desire to chant a <qex>sonnet</qex> or hymn
unto <person>Apollo Pythius</person>.</q>
<rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A poem of fourteen lines, -- two stanzas, called the
<part>octave</part>, being of four verses each, and two stanzas, called
the <part>sestet</part>, of three verses each, the rhymes being adjusted
by a particular rule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the proper sonnet each line has five accents, and the
octave has but two rhymes, the second, third, sixth, and seventh lines
being of one rhyme, and the first, fourth, fifth, and eighth being of
another. In the sestet there are sometimes two and sometimes three
rhymes; but in some way its two stazas rhyme together. Often the three
lines of the first stanza rhyme severally with the three lines of the
second. In Shakespeare's sonnets, the first twelve lines are rhymed
alternately, and the last two rhyme together.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonnet</ent><br/
<hw>Son"net</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To compose sonnets.</def>
<ldquo/Strains that come almost to <xex>sonneting</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonneteer</ent><br/
<hw>Son`net*eer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A composer of
sonnets, or small poems; a small poet; -- usually in contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What woful stuff this madrigal would be<br/
In some starved hackney <qex>sonneteer</qex> or me!</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonneteer</ent><br/
<hw>Son`net*eer"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To compose sonnets.</def>
<rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonneter</ent><br/
<hw>Son"net*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A composer of
sonnets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonnetist</ent><br/
<hw>Son"net*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sonneter, or sonneteer.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonnetize</ent><br/
<hw>Son"net*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To compose
sonnets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonnish</ent><br/
<hw>Son"nish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like the sun; sunny;
golden.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Her <xex>sonnish</xex>
hairs.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonnite</ent><br/
<hw>Son"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sunnite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonometer</ent><br/
<hw>So*nom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sonus</ets> a sound + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An instrument for exhibiting the transverse
vibrations of cords, and ascertaining the relations between musical
notes. It consists of a cord stretched by weight along a box, and
divided into different lengths at pleasure by a bridge, the place of
which is determined by a scale on the face of the box.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument for testing the hearing
capacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonoran</ent><br/
<hw>So*no"ran</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biogeography)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to or designating the arid division of the Austral zone,
including the warmer parts of the western United States and central
Mexico. It is divided into the <col><b>Upper Sonoran</b></col>, which
lies next to the Transition zone, and the <col><b>Lower
Sonoran</b></col>, next to the Tropical.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonorific</ent><br/
<hw>Son`o*rif"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sonor</ets>, <ets>-oris</ets>, a sound + <ets>facere</ets> to make.
See <er>Sonorous</er>.]</ety> <def>Producing sound; <as>as, the
<ex>sonorific</ex> quality of a body</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>I. Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonority</ent><br/
<hw>So*nor"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sonoritas</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
sonorous; sonorousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonorous</ent><br/
<hw>So*no"rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sonorus</ets>, fr. <ets>sonor</ets>, <ets>-oris</ets>, a sound,
akin to <ets>sonus</ets> a sound. See <er>Sound</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Giving sound when struck; resonant; <as>as, <ex>sonorous</ex>
metals</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Loud-sounding; giving a clear or loud sound; <as>as,
a <ex>sonorous</ex> voice</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Yielding sound; characterized by sound; vocal;
sonant; <as>as, the vowels are <ex>sonorous</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Impressive in sound; high-sounding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Italian opera, amidst all the meanness and familiarty of the
thoughts, has something beautiful and <qex>sonorous</qex> in the
expression.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is nothing of the artificial Johnsonian balance in his
style. It is as often marked by a pregnant brevity as by a
<qex>sonorous</qex> amplitude.</q> <rj><qau>E. Everett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Sonant; vibrant; hence, of sounds
produced in a cavity, deep-toned; <as>as, <ex>sonorous</ex>
rhonchi</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sonorous figures</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>,
<cd>figures formed by the vibrations of a substance capable of emitting
a musical tone, as when the bow of a violin is drawn along the edge of a
piece of glass or metal on which sand is strewed, and the sand arranges
itself in figures according to the musical tone. Called also
<altname>acoustic figures</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sonorous
tumor</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a tumor which emits a clear,
resonant sound on percussion.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>So*no"rous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>So*no"rous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonship</ent><br/
<hw>Son"ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being a
son, or of bearing the relation of a son; filiation.</def> <rj><au>Dr.
H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sonsy</ent><br/
<hw>Son"sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Soncy</er>.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sontag</ent><br/
<hw>Son"tag</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from from
Mme. Henriette <ets>Sontag</ets>, a famous singer.]</ety> <def>A knitted
worsted jacket, worn over the waist of a woman's dress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1372 --></p>

<p><ent>Sonties</ent><br/
<hw>Son"ties</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Probably from
<ldquo/<xex>saintes</xex><rdquo/ saints, or from <xex>sanctities</xex>;
-- used as an oath.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soochong</ent><br/
<hw>Soo*chong"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Souchong</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soodra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Soo"dra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Same as
<er>Sudra</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soofeeism</ent><br/
<ent>Soofee</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Soo"fee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Soo"fee*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>. <def>Same as <er>Sufi</er>, <er>Sufism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soojee</ent><br/
<hw>Soo"jee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Suji</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soon</ent><br/
<hw>Soon</hw> <pr>(s<oomac/n)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sone</ets>, AS. <ets>s<omac/na</ets>; cf. OFries.
<ets>s<omac/n</ets>, OS. <ets>s<amac/na</ets>, <ets>s<amac/no</ets>,
OHG. <ets>s<amac/r</ets>, Goth. <ets>suns</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>In a short time; shortly after any time specified or supposed;
<as>as, <ex>soon</ex> after sunrise</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sooner</xex>
said than done.<rdquo/ <au>Old Proverb.</au> <ldquo/As <xex>soon</xex>
as it might be.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She finished, and the subtle fiend his lore<br/
<qex>Soon</qex> learned.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Without the usual delay; before any time supposed;
early.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How is it that ye are come so <qex>soon</qex> to-day?</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. ii. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Promptly; quickly; easily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Small lights are <qex>soon</qex> blown out, huge fires abide.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Readily; willingly; -- in this sense used with
<xex>would</xex>, or some other word expressing
<xex>will</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would as <qex>soon</qex> see a river winding through woods or in
meadows, as when it is tossed up in so many whimsical figures at
Versailles.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>As soon as</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>So soon
as</b></col></mcol>, <cd>immediately at or after another event.</cd>
<ldquo/<xex>As soon as</xex> he came nigh unto the camp . . . he saw the
calf, and the dancing.<rdquo/ <au>Ex. xxxii. 19.</au> See <cref>So . . .
as</cref>, under <er>So</er>. -- <col><b>Soon at</b></col>, <cd>as soon
as; or, as soon as the time referred to arrives.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/I shall be sent for <xex>soon at</xex>
night.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>Sooner or later</b></col>,
<cd>at some uncertain time in the future; <as>as, he will discover his
mistake <ex>sooner or later</ex></as>.</cd> -- <col><b>With the
soonest</b></col>, <cd>as soon as any; among the earliest; too
soon.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Holland.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soon</ent><br/
<hw>Soon</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Speedy; quick.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soonee</ent><br/
<hw>Soo"nee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sunnite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sooner</ent><br/
<hw>Soon"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>In the western United
States, one who settles on government land before it is legally open to
settlement in order to gain the prior claim that the law gives to the
first settler when the land is opened to settlement; hence, any one who
does a thing prematurely or anticipates another in acting in order to
gain an unfair advantage.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sooner State</ent><br/
<hw>Sooner State</hw>. <def>Oklahoma; -- a nickname.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soonly</ent><br/
<hw>Soon"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Soon.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soord</ent><br/
<hw>Soord</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Skin of bacon.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soorma</ent><br/
<hw>Soor"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind. & Per.
<ets>surma</ets>.]</ety> <def>A preparation of antimony with which
Mohammedan men anoint their eyelids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sooshong</ent><br/
<hw>Soo*shong"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Souchong</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soosoo</ent><br/
<hw>Soo"soo</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
kind of dolphin (<spn>Platanista Gangeticus</spn>) native of the river
Ganges; the Gangetic dolphin. It has a long, slender, somewhat spatulate
beak.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>susu</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soot</ent><br/
<hw>Soot</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sot</ets>, AS. <ets>s<?/t</ets>; akin to Icel. <ets>s<?/t</ets>,
Sw. <ets>sot</ets>, Dan. <ets>sod</ets>, OD. <ets>soet</ets>, Lith.
<ets>s<?/dis</ets>; cf. Gael. <ets>suith</ets>, Ir.
<ets>suth</ets>.]</ety> <def>A black substance formed by combustion, or
disengaged from fuel in the process of combustion, which rises in fine
particles, and adheres to the sides of the chimney or pipe conveying the
smoke; strictly, the fine powder, consisting chiefly of carbon, which
colors smoke, and which is the result of imperfect combustion. See
<er>Smoke</er>.</def><-- of "incomplete" combustion --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soot</ent><br/
<hw>Soot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sooted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sooting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cover or dress with soot; to
smut with, or as with, soot; <as>as, to <ex>soot</ex> land</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soote</ent><br/
<ent>Soot</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Soot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Soot"e</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sweet</er>.]</ety> <def>Sweet.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The <xex>soote</xex> savour of the
vine.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sooterkin</ent><br/
<hw>Soot"er*kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Prov. G.
<ets>suttern</ets> to boil gently.]</ety> <def>A kind of false birth,
fabled to be produced by Dutch women from sitting over their stoves;
also, an abortion, in a figurative sense; an abortive scheme.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fruits of dull heat, and <qex>sooterkins</qex> of wit.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sooth</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth</hw> <pr>(s<oomac/th)</pr>, <pos>a.; <plain>also</plain>
adv.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Soother</adjf>
<pr>(s<oomac/th"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Soothest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>soth</ets>, AS.
<ets>s<omac/<edh/</ets>, for <ets>san<edh/</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>s<omac/<edh/</ets>, OHG. <ets>sand</ets>, Icel. <ets>sannr</ets>,
Sw. <ets>sann</ets>, Dan. <ets>sand</ets>, Skr. <ets>sat</ets>,
<ets>sant</ets>, real, genuine, present, being; properly p. pr. from a
root meaning, to be, Skr. <ets>as</ets>, L. <ets>esse</ets>; also akin
to Goth. <ets>sunjis</ets> true, Gr. <grk>'eteo`s</grk>, Skr.
<ets>satya</ets>. <root/9. Cf. <er>Absent</er>, <er>Am</er>,
<er>Essence</er>, <er>Is</er>, <er>Soothe</er>, <er>Sutee</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>True; faithful; trustworthy.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sentence [meaning] of it <qex>sooth</qex> is, out of
doubt.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That shall I <qex>sooth</qex> (said he) to you declare.</q>
<rj><qau>Spensser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pleasing; delightful; sweet.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>soothest</qex> shepherd that ever piped on plains.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With jellies <qex>soother</qex> than the creamy curd.</q>
<rj><qau>Keats.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sooth</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<omac/<edh/</ets>. See
<er>Sooth</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Truth;
reality.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sooth</qex> it this, the cut fell to the knight.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>sooth</qex>, I know not why I am so sad.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In good <qex>sooth</qex>,<br/
Its mystery is love, its meaninng youth.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Augury; prognostication.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>soothe</qex> of birds by beating of their wings.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Blandishment; cajolery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothe</ent><br/
<hw>Soothe</hw> <pr>(s<oomac/<th/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Soothed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Soothing</conjf>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Originally, to assent to as true; OE. <ets>so<edh/ien</ets> to
verify, AS. <ets>ges<omac/<edh/ian</ets> to prove the truth of, to bear
witness. See <er>Sooth</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
assent to as true.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Testament of
Love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To assent to; to comply with; to gratify; to humor
by compliance; to please with blandishments or soft words; to
flatter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Good, my lord, <qex>soothe</qex> him, let him take the fellow.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I've tried the force of every reason on him,<br/
<qex>Soothed</qex> and caressed, been angry, <qex>soothed</qex>
again.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To assuage; to mollify; to calm; to comfort; <as>as,
to <ex>soothe</ex> a crying child; to <ex>soothe</ex> one's
sorrows</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Music hath charms to <qex>soothe</qex> the savage breast,<br/
To soften rocks, or bend a knotted oak.</q>
<rj><qau>Congreve.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though the sound of Fame<br/
May for a moment <qex>soothe</qex>, it can not slake<br/
The fever of vain longing.</q> <rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To soften; assuage; allay; compose; mollify;
tranquilize; pacify; mitigate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soother</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, soothes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothfast</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"fast`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sooth</ets>
+ <ets>fast</ets>, that is, fast or firm with respect to truth.]</ety>
<def>Firmly fixed in, or founded upon, the thruth; true; genuine; real;
also, truthful; faithful.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Sooth"fast`ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark></wordforms> <ldquo/In very
<xex>soothfastness.</xex><rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why do not you . . . bear leal and <qex>soothfast</qex> evidence
in her behalf, as ye may with a clear conscience!</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj> <-- leal = loyal, but not marked as archaic in this
work. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothfast</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"fast`</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Soothly; really; in
fact.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I care not if the pomps you show<br/
Be what they <qex>soothfast</qex> appear.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothing</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Soothe</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothingly</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a soothing
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothly</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In truth; truly;
really; verily.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Soothly</xex> for
to say.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothness</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Truth; reality.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothsay</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"say`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sooth</ets> + <ets>say</ets>; properly to say truth, tell the
truth.]</ety> <def>To foretell; to predict.</def> <ldquo/You can not
<xex>soothsay</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/Old
<xex>soothsaying</xex> Glaucus' spell.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothsay</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"say`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A true saying; a
proverb; a prophecy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Omen; portent. Having</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God turn the same to good <qex>soothsay</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothsayer</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"say`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who foretells events by the art of soothsaying; a
prognosticator.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A mantis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soothsaying</ent><br/
<hw>Sooth"say`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A true saying;
truth.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of one who soothsays; the foretelling of
events; the art or practice of making predictions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A damsel, possessed with a spirit of divination . . . which
brought her masters much gain by <qex>soothsaying</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts xvi. 16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A prediction; a prophecy; a
prognostication.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Divinations and <qex>soothsayings</qex> and dreams are vain.</q>
<rj><qau>Eclus. xxxiv. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sootiness</ent><br/
<hw>Soot"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sooty; fuliginousness.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sootish</ent><br/
<hw>Soot"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sooty.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sooty</ent><br/
<hw>Soot"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sootier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sootiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<?/tig</ets>. See
<er>Soot</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to soot;
producing soot; soiled by soot.</def> <ldquo/Fire of <xex>sooty</xex>
coal.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a dark brown or black color like soot;
fuliginous; dusky; dark.</def> <ldquo/The grisly legions that troop
under the <xex>sooty</xex> flag of Acheron.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sooty albatross</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an
albatross (<spn>Phoebetria fuliginosa</spn>) found chiefly in the
Pacific Ocean; -- called also <altname>nellie</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sooty tern</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a tern (<spn>Sterna
fuliginosa</spn>) found chiefly in tropical seas.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sooty</ent><br/
<hw>Soot"y</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To black or foul with soot.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sootied</qex> with noisome smoke.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sop</ent><br/
<hw>Sop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sop</ets>,
<ets>soppe</ets>; akin to AS. <ets>s<?/pan</ets> to sup, to sip, to
drink, D. <ets>sop</ets> sop, G. <ets>suppe</ets> soup, Icel.
<ets>soppa</ets> sop. See <er>Sup</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Soup</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything steeped, or dipped and
softened, in any liquid; especially, something dipped in broth or liquid
food, and intended to be eaten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He it is to whom I shall give a <qex>sop</qex>, when I have dipped
it.</q> <rj><qau>John xiii. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sops</qex> in wine, quantity, inebriate more than wine
itself.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bounded waters<br/
Should lift their bosoms higher than the shores,<br/
And make a <qex>sop</qex> of all this solid globe.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything given to pacify; -- so called from the sop
given to Cerberus, as related in mythology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All nature is cured with a <qex>sop</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A thing of little or no value.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>P. Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Sops in wine</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an old name
of the clove pink, alluding to its having been used to flavor
wine.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Garlands of roses and <qex>sops in wine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Sops of wine</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an old
European variety of apple, of a yellow and red color, shading to deep
red; -- called also <altname>sopsavine</altname>, and <altname>red
shropsavine</altname>.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Sop</ent><br/
<hw>Sop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sopped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sopping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To steep or dip in any
liquid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sope</ent><br/
<hw>Sope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Soap</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soph</ent><br/
<hw>Soph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eng. Univ.)</fld> <def>A
contraction of <er>Soph ister</er>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soph</ent><br/
<hw>Soph</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Amer. Colleges)</fld> <def>A
contraction of <er>Sophomore</er>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophi</ent><br/
<hw>So"phi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sophis</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>See
<er>Sufi</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophical</ent><br/
<ent>Sophic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Soph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Soph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ wise, <?/ wisdom.]</ety>
<def>Teaching wisdom.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>S.
Harris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophime</ent><br/
<hw>So*phime"</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>soffime</ets>, <ets>sophisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sophism.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I trow ye study aboute some <qex>sophime</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophism</ent><br/
<hw>Soph"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sophisme</ets>, L. <ets>sophisma</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to
make wise, <?/ to be become wise, to play the sophist, fr. <?/
wise.]</ety> <def>The doctrine or mode of reasoning practiced by a
sophist; hence, any fallacy designed to deceive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When a false argument puts on the appearance of a true one, then
it is properly called a <qex>sophism</qex>, or
<ldquo/fallacy<rdquo/.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let us first rid ourselves of <qex>sophisms</qex>, those of
depraved men, and those of heartless philosophers.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophist</ent><br/
<hw>Soph"ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sophiste</ets>, L.
<ets>sophistes</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/. See <er>Sophism</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One of a class of men who taught eloquence, philosophy,
and politics in ancient Greece; especially, one of those who, by their
fallacious but plausible reasoning, puzzled inquirers after truth,
weakened the faith of the people, and drew upon themselves general
hatred and contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many of the <qex>Sophists</qex> doubdtless card not for truth or
morality, and merely professed to teach how to make the worse appear the
better reason; but there scems no reason to hold that they were a
special class, teaching special opinions; even Socrates and Plato were
sometimes styled <qex>Sophists</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Liddell &
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, an impostor in argument; a captious or
fallacious reasoner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophister</ent><br/
<hw>Soph"ist*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
sophist. See <er>Sophist</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Univ.)</fld> <def>A student who is advanced
beyond the first year of his residence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The entire course at the university consists of three
years and one term, during which the students have the titles of
<xex>first-year men</xex>, or <xex>freshmen</xex>; <xex>second-year
men</xex> or <xex>junior sophs</xex> or <xex>sophisters</xex>;
<xex>third-year men</xex>, or <xex>senior sophs</xex> or
<xex>sophisters</xex>; and, in the last term, <xex>questionists</xex>,
with reference to the approaching examination. In the older American
colleges, the junior and senior classes were originally called, and in
some of them are still called, <xex>junior sophisters</xex> and
<xex>senior sophisters</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophister</ent><br/
<hw>Soph"ist*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To maintain by
sophistry, or by a fallacious argument.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>obham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophistical</ent><br/
<ent>Sophistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So*phis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>So*phis"tic*al</hw>
}</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sophisticus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>sophistique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a sophist;
embodying sophistry; fallaciously subtile; not sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His argument . . . is altogether <qex>sophistical</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>So*phis"tic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>So*phis"tic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophisticate</ent><br/
<hw>So*phis"ti*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sophisticated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sophisticating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sophisticatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sophisticare</ets> to
sophisticate.]</ety> <def>To render worthless by admixture; to
adulterate; to damage; to pervert; <as>as, to <ex>sophisticate</ex>
wine</as>.</def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>sophisticate</qex> the understanding.</q>
<rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet Butler professes to stick to plain facts, not to
<qex>sophisticate</qex>, not to refine.</q> <rj><qau>M.
Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They purchase but <qex>sophisticated</qex> ware.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To adulterate; debase; corrupt;
vitiate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophisticated</ent><br/
<ent>Sophisticate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So*phis"ti*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>So*phis"ti*ca`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Adulterated; not pure; not
genuine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So truth, while only one supplied the state,<br/
Grew scare and dear, and yet <qex>sophisticate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophistication</ent><br/
<hw>So*phis`ti*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>sophisticatio</ets>, F. <ets>sophistication</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
act of sophisticating; adulteration; <as>as, the <ex>sophistication</ex>
of drugs</as>.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophisticator</ent><br/
<hw>So*phis"ti*ca`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sophisticates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophistry</ent><br/
<hw>Soph"ist*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sophistrie</ets>, OF. <ets>sophisterie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The art or process of reasoning; logic.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The practice of a sophist; fallacious reasoning;
reasoning sound in appearance only.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The juggle of <qex>sophistry</qex> consists, for the most part, in
usig a word in one sense in the premise, and in another sense in the
conclusion.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Fallacy</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophomore</ent><br/
<hw>Soph"o*more</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably fr.
<ets>soph</ets> or <ets>sophister</ets> + Gr. <?/ foolish. The word was
probably introduced into the United States at an early date, from the
University of Cambridge, England. Among the cant terms at that
university, as given in the Gradus ad Cantabrigiam, we find
<ets>Soph-Mor</ets> as <ldquo/the next distinctive appellation to
Freshman,<rdquo/ but the term has now almost ceased to be known at the
English university from whence it came.]</ety> <def>One belonging to the
second of the four classes in an American college, or one next above a
freshman.</def> <altsp>[Formerly written also
<asp>sophimore</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophomorical</ent><br/
<ent>Sophomoric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Soph`o*mor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Soph`o*mor"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
sophomore; resembling a sophomore; hence, pretentious; inflated in style
or manner; <as>as, <ex>sophomoric</ex> affectation</as>.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. Poorly informed yet overconfident of one's own knowledge;
asserting incorrect facts based on scant knowledge. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophora</ent><br/
<hw>So*pho"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets><?/ufair</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A genus
of leguminous plants.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A tree (<spn>Sophora
Japonica</spn>) of Eastern Asia, resembling the common locust;
occasionally planted in the United States.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sophta</ent><br/
<hw>Soph"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Softa</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sopite</ent><br/
<hw>So"pite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sopitus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sopire</ets> to put to sleep; akin to
<ets>sopor</ets> a sleeping draught, a heavy sleep.]</ety> <def>To lay
asleep; to put to sleep; to quiet.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king's declaration for the <qex>sopiting</qex> of all Arminian
heresies.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sopition</ent><br/
<hw>So*pi"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of putting
to sleep, or the state of being put to sleep; sleep.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dementation and <qex>sopition</qex> of reason.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sopor</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"por</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Profound sleep from which a person can be roused
only with difficulty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soporate</ent><br/
<hw>Sop"o*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>soporatus</ets>, p. p. or <ets>soporare</ets> to put to sleep, fr.
<ets>sopor</ets> a heavy sleep.]</ety> <def>To lay or put to sleep; to
stupefy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soporiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sop`o*rif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>soparifer</ets>; <ets>sopor</ets> a heavy sleep + <ets>ferere</ets>
to bring.]</ety> <def>Causing sleep; somniferous; soporific.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Soporiferous</xex> medicine.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sop`o*rif"er*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sop`o*rif"er*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soporific</ent><br/
<hw>Sop`o*rif"ic</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sopor</ets> a heavy sleep (akin to <ets>somnus</ets> sleep) +
<ets>facere</ets> to make. See <er>Somnolent</er>, <er>Fact</er>.]</ety>
<def>Causing sleep; tending to cause sleep; soporiferous; <as>as, the
<ex>soporific</ex> virtues of opium</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Somniferous; narcotic; opiate; anodyne.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soporific</ent><br/
<hw>Sop`o*rif"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A medicine, drug, plant, or
other agent that has the quality of inducing sleep; a
narcotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soporous</ent><br/
<ent>Soporose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sop"o*rose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sop"o*rous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Sopor</er>; cf. L.
<ets>soporus</ets>, fr. <ets>sopor</ets> a heavy sleep; F.
<ets>soporeux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Causing sleep; sleepy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sopper</ent><br/
<hw>Sop"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sops.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sopping</ent><br/
<-- <hw>sopping</hw>, more recent version of <it>soppy.</it> Used esp. in phrase <it>sopping wet</it>. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soppy</ent><br/
<hw>Sop"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Soaked or saturated with liquid or moisture; very wet or sloppy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It [Yarmouth] looked rather spongy and <qex>soppy</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sopra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"pra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[It., from L. <ets>supra</ets> above.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Above; before; over; upon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sopranist</ent><br/
<hw>So*pra"nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A treble singer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soprano</ent><br/
<hw>So*pra"no</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E. <plw>Sopranos</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, It. <plw>Soprani</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It., fr. <ets>soprano</ets> superior, highest, fr. <ets>sopra</ets> above, L. <ets>supra</ets>. See <er>Sovereign</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The treble; the highest vocal register; the highest kind of female or boy's voice; the upper part in harmony for mixed voices.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A singer, commonly a woman, with a treble voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sopsavine</ent><br/
<hw>Sops"a*vine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <cref>Sops of wine</cref>, under <er>Sop</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1373 --></p>

<p><ent>Sora</ent><br/
<hw>So"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
North American rail (<spn>Porzana Carolina</spn>) common in the Eastern
United States. Its back is golden brown, varied with black and white,
the front of the head and throat black, the breast and sides of the head
and neck slate-colored. Called also <altname>American rail</altname>,
<altname>Carolina rail</altname>, <altname>Carolina crake</altname>,
<altname>common rail</altname>, <altname>sora rail</altname>,
<altname>soree</altname>, <altname>meadow chicken</altname>, and
<altname>orto</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>King sora</b></col>, <cd>the Florida
gallinule.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorance</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"ance</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Soreness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorb</ent><br/
<hw>Sorb</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L. <ets>sorbus the
tree</ets>, <ets>sorbum</ets> the fruit; cf. F. <ets>sorbe</ets>. See
<er>Service tree</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
wild service tree (<spn>Pyrus torminalis</spn>) of Europe; also, the
rowan tree.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The fruit of these trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sorb apple</b></col>, <cd>the fruit of the sorb, or wild
service tree.</cd> -- <col><b>Sorb tree</b></col>, <cd>the wild service
tree.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbate</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"bate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sorbate</ets>. See <er>Sorbic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of sorbic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbefacient</ent><br/
<hw>Sor`be*fa"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sorbere</ets> to suck in, absorb + <ets>faciens</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Producing
absorption.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A medicine or substance
which produces absorption.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbent</ent><br/
<hw>Sorb"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sorbens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sorbere</ets> to suck in, to
absorb.]</ety> <def>An absorbent.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbet</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"bet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sorbet</ets>
or It. <ets>sorbetto</ets> or Sp. <ets>sorbete</ets>, from the same
source as E. <ets>sherbet</ets>. See <er>Sherbet</er>.]</ety> <def>A
kind of beverage; sherbet.</def> <rj><au>Smolett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbic</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"bic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sorbique</ets>. See <er>Sorb</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or obtained from, the rowan tree, or sorb;
specifically, designating an acid, C<?/H<?/CO<?/H, of the acetylene
series, found in the unripe berries of this tree, and extracted as a
white crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbile</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"bile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sorbilis</ets>, fr. <ets>sorbere</ets> to suck in, to drink
down.]</ety> <def>Fit to be drunk or sipped.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbin</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"bin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An
unfermentable sugar, isomeric with glucose, found in the ripe berries of
the rowan tree, or sorb, and extracted as a sweet white crystalline
substance; -- called also <altname>mountain-ash
sugar</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbite</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"bite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sorbus</ets>
service tree.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A sugarlike substance,
isomeric with mannite and dulcite, found with sorbin in the ripe berries
of the sorb, and extracted as a sirup or a white crystalline
substance.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sor*bit"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbition</ent><br/
<hw>Sor*bi"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sorbitio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of drinking or sipping.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- sorbitol. A hexitol, C6H14O6, found in the mountain ash, now
produced by hydrogenation or electrolytic reduction of glucose. It has a
variet of uses, notably as a sugar substitute for diabetics, and to
improve the shelf-life of candy, as an osmotic agent, and as an
excipient in pharmaceutical preparations. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbonical</ent><br/
<hw>Sor*bon"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Belonging to the
Sorbonne or to a Sorbonist.</def> <rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorbonist</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"bon*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sorboniste</ets>.]</ety> <def>A doctor of the <xex>Sorbonne</xex>,
or theological college, in the University of Paris, founded by Robert de
<xex>Sorbon</xex>, <sc>a. d.</sc> 1252. It was suppressed in the
Revolution of 1789.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorcerer</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"cer*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sorcier</ets>. See <er>Sorcery</er>.]</ety> <def>A conjurer; an
enchanter; a magician.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> <q>Pharaoh also called the wise men and the
<qex>sorcerers</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. vii. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorceress</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"cer*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female sorcerer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorcering</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"cer*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act or practice of using
sorcery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorcerous</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"cer*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to sorcery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorcery</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"cer*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sorceries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>sorcerie</ets>,
OF. <ets>sorcerie</ets>, fr. OF. & F. <ets>sorcier</ets> a sorcerer, LL.
<ets>sortiarius</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sors</ets>, <ets>sortis</ets>, a lot,
decision by lot, fate, destiny. See <er>Sort</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Divination by the assistance, or supposed assistance, of evil
spirits, or the power of commanding evil spirits; magic; necromancy;
witchcraft; enchantment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Adder's wisdom I have learned,<br/
To fence my ear against thy <qex>sorceries</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sord</ent><br/
<hw>Sord</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sward</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sordes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sor"des</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>sordere</ets> to be dirty or foul.]</ety> <def>Foul matter;
excretion; dregs; filthy, useless, or rejected matter of any kind;
specifically <fld>(Med.)</fld>, the foul matter that collects on the
teeth and tongue in low fevers and other conditions attended with great
vital depression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sordet</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"det</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sordine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A sordine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sordid</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"did</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sordidus</ets>, fr. <ets>sordere</ets> to be filthy or dirty;
probably akin to E. <ets>swart</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sordide</ets>. See
<er>Swart</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Filthy; foul;
dirty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sordid</qex> god; down from his hoary chin<br/
A length of beard descends, uncombed, unclean.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Vile; base; gross; mean; <as>as, vulgar,
<ex>sordid</ex> mortals</as>.</def> <ldquo/To scorn the
<xex>sordid</xex> world.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Meanly avaricious; covetous; niggardly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He may be old,<br/
And yet <qex>sordid</qex>, who refuses gold.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sordidly</ent><br/
<hw>Sor*did"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sordidness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sordidly</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"did*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sordid
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sordidness</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"did*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sordid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sordine</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"dine</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>sordina</ets>, <ets>sordino</ets>, from <ets>sordo</ets> deaf,
dull-sounding, L. <ets>surdus</ets>. See <er>Surd</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>See <er>Damper</er>, and 5th
<er>Mute</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sore</ent><br/
<hw>Sore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>saure</ets>,
<ets>sore</ets>, <ets>sor</ets>; faucon <ets>sor</ets> a sore falcon.
See <er>Sorrel</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Reddish brown;
sorrel.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sore falcon</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Sore</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sore</ent><br/
<hw>Sore</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A young hawk or
falcon in the first year.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A young buck in the fourth year.
See the Note under <er>Buck</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sore</ent><br/
<hw>Sore</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sorer</adjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sorest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sor</ets>, <ets>sar</ets>,
AS. <ets>s<amac/r</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zeer</ets>, OS. & OHG.
<ets>s<?/r</ets>, G. <ets>sehr</ets> very, Icel. <ets>s<amac/rr</ets>,
Sw. <ets>s<aring/r</ets>, Goth. <ets>sair</ets> pain. Cf.
<er>Sorry</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Tender to the touch; susceptible
of pain from pressure; inflamed; painful; -- said of the body or its
parts; <as>as, a <ex>sore</ex> hand</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Sensitive; tender; easily pained, grieved, or
vexed; very susceptible of irritation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Malice and hatred are very fretting and vexatious, and apt to make
our minds <qex>sore</qex> and uneasy.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Severe; afflictive; distressing; <as>as, a
<ex>sore</ex> disease; <ex>sore</ex> evil or calamity</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Criminal; wrong; evil.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sore throat</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>inflammation
of the throat and tonsils; pharyngitis. See <er>Cynanche</er>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Malignant sore throat</b></col>, <col><b>Ulcerated sore
throat</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Putrid sore
throat</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See <er>Angina</er>, and under
<er>Putrid</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sore</ent><br/
<hw>Sore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sor</ets>,
<ets>sar</ets>, AS. <ets>s<amac/r</ets>. See <er>Sore</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place in an animal body where
the skin and flesh are ruptured or bruised, so as to be tender or
painful; a painful or diseased place, such as an ulcer or a
boil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dogs came and licked his <qex>sores</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Luke
xvi. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Grief; affliction; trouble; difficulty.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I see plainly where his <qex>sore</qex> lies.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Gold sore</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Gold</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sore</ent><br/
<hw>Sore</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<amac/re</ets>. See
<er>Sore</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a sore manner;
with pain; grievously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy hand presseth me <qex>sore</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. xxxviii.
2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Greatly; violently; deeply.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Hannah] prayed unto the Lord and wept <qex>sore</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Sam. i. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sore</qex> sighed the knight, who this long sermon heard.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soredia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*re"di*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos>
of <er>Soredium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorediate</ent><br/
<hw>So*re"di*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Sorediferous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorediiferous</ent><br/
<ent>Sorediferous</ent><br/
<mhw>{<hw>Sor`e*dif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>So*re`di*if"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Soredium</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Bearing soredia; sorediate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soredium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*re"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Soredia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a
heap.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A patch of granular bodies on the
surface of the thallus of lichens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soree</ent><br/
<hw>So"ree</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Sora</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorehead</ent><br/
<hw>Sore"head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is
disgruntled by a failure in politics, or the like.</def> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorehon</ent><br/
<hw>Sore"hon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted from
<ets>sojourn</ets>, Scot. <ets>soirne</ets>, <ets>sorn</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Formerly, in Ireland, a kind of servile tenure which subjected the
tenant to maintain his chieftain gratuitously whenever he wished to
indulge in a revel.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorel</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[A diminutive. See
<er>Sore</er> reddish brown.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A young buck in the third year. See the Note under
<er>Buck</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A yellowish or reddish brown color;
sorrel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorely</ent><br/
<hw>Sore"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sore manner;
grievously; painfully; <as>as, to be <ex>sorely</ex>
afflicted</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorema</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*re"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/
a heap.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A heap of carpels belonging to one
flower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soreness</ent><br/
<hw>Sore"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state
of being sore; tenderness; painfully; <as>as, the <ex>soreness</ex> of a
wound; the <ex>soreness</ex> of an affliction</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorex</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"rex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
shrew.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of small Insectivora,
including the common shrews.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorghe</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"ghe</hw> <pr>(s<ocir/r"g<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The three-bearded rockling, or
whistlefish.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorghum</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"ghum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., probably of
Chinese origin.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A genus of
grasses, properly limited to two species, <spn>Sorghum Halepense</spn>,
the Arabian millet, or Johnson grass (see <er>Johnson grass</er>), and
<spn>Sorghum vulgare</spn>, the Indian millet (see <cref>Indian
millet</cref>, under <er>Indian</er>).</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A variety
of <spn>Sorghum vulgare</spn>, grown for its saccharine juice; the
Chinese sugar cane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorgo</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It.
<ets>sorgo</ets>. See <er>Sorghum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Indian millet and its varieties. See <er>Sorghum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sori</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"ri</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Sorus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soricine</ent><br/
<hw>So"ri*cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sorricinus</ets>, fr. <ets>sorex</ets> a shrew.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Shrew family
(<fam>Soricidae</fam>); like a shrew in form or habits; <as>as, the
<ex>soricine</ex> bat (<spn>Glossophaga soricina</spn>)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorites</ent><br/
<hw>So*ri"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from Gr.
<grk>swrei`ths</grk> (sc. <grk>syllogismo`s</grk>), properly, heaped up
(hence, a heap of syllogisms), fr. <grk>swro`s</grk> a heap.]</ety>
<fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>An abridged form of stating of syllogisms in a
series of propositions so arranged that the predicate of each one that
precedes forms the subject of each one that follows, and the conclusion
unites the subject of the first proposition with the predicate of the
last proposition</def>, as in following example; --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The soul is a thinking agent;<br/
A thinking agent can not be severed into parts;<br/
That which can not be severed can not be destroyed;<br/
Therefore the soul can not be destroyed.</q><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ When the series is arranged in the reverse order, it is
called the <xex>Goclenian sorites</xex>, from Goclenius, a philosopher
of the sixteenth century.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Destructive sorities</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Destructive</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Sorites paradox, (philos.) The paradox that arises from the
assertion that if one item is removed from a heap (sorites) of objects,
what remains is still a heap. Continued application of that rule for any
finite heap ultimately causes a contradiction, when the "heap" has no
objects left. Similar definitional problems prompted the invention of
"fuzzy logic" --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soritical</ent><br/
<hw>So"rit"ic*al</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a sorites;
resembling a sorites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorn</ent><br/
<hw>Sorn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sorehon</er>.]</ety> <def>To obtrude one's self on another for bed
and board.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorner</ent><br/
<hw>Sorn"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who obtrudes
himself on another for bed and board.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>De Quncey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sororal</ent><br/
<hw>So*ro"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>soror</ets>
sister: cf. F. <ets>sororal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Relating to a sister;
sisterly.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sororicide</ent><br/
<hw>So*ror"i*cide</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sororocida</ets>, and <ets>sororicidium</ets>; <ets>soror</ets> a
sister + <ets>caedere</ets> to kill.]</ety> <def>The murder of one's
sister; also, one who murders or kills one's own sister.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sororize</ent><br/
<hw>So*ro"rize</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>soror</ets>, <ets>sororis</ets>, a sister.]</ety> <def>To
associate, or hold fellowship, as sisters; to have sisterly feelings; --
analogous to <xex>fraternize</xex>.</def> <mark>[Recent & R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorosis</ent><br/
<hw>So*ro"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sororize</er>.]</ety> <def>A woman's club; an association of
women.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So*ro"sis</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a
heap.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A fleshy fruit formed by the
consolidation of many flowers with their receptacles, ovaries, etc., as
the breadfruit, mulberry, and pineapple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrage</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"rage</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sorrel</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>The blades of green or
barley.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrance</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"rance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sorance</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrel</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"rel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>saur</ets>,
<ets>saure</ets>, OF. <ets>sor</ets>, <ets>sore</ets>, probably of
Teutonic origin; cf. D. <ets>zoor</ets> dry, LG. <ets>soor</ets>; the
meaning probably coming from the color of dry leaves. See <er>Sear</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Sorel</er>.]</ety> <def>Of a yellowish or
reddish brown color; <as>as, a <ex>sorrel</ex> horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrel</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"rel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A yellowish or reddish
brown color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrel</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"rel</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>surelle</ets>, fr.
<ets>sur</ets> sour, fr. OHG. <ets>s<?/r</ets> sour. See
<er>Sour</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of various plants
having a sour juice; especially, a plant of the genus <gen>Rumex</gen>,
as <spn>Rumex Acetosa</spn>, <spn>Rumex Acetosella</spn>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Mountain sorrel</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Mountain</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Red sorrel</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A malvaceous plant (<spn>Hibiscus
Sabdariffa</spn>) whose acid calyxes and capsules are used in the West
Indies for making tarts and acid drinks</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
troublesome weed (<spn>Rumex Acetosella</spn>), also called
<altname>sheep sorrel</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Salt of
sorrel</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>binoxalate of potassa; -- so
called because obtained from the juice of <spn>Rumex Acetosella</spn>,
or <spn>Rumex Axetosa</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sorrel tree</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small ericaceous tree (<spn>Oxydendrum
arboreum</spn>) whose leaves resemble those of the peach and have a sour
taste. It is common along the Alleghanies. Called also
<altname>sourwood</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Wood sorrel</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any plant of the genus Oxalis.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrento work</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"ren"to work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Ornamental work, mostly
carved in olivewood, decorated with inlay, made at or near Sorrento,
Italy. Hence, more rarely, jig-saw work and the like done
anywhere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrily</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sorry manner;
poorly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy pipe, O Pan, shall help, though I sing <qex>sorrily</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorriness</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"ri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sorry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrow</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"row</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sorwe</ets>,
<ets>sorewe</ets>, <ets>sor<?/e</ets>, AS. <ets>sorg</ets>,
<ets>sorh</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zorg</ets> care, anxiety, OS.
<ets>sorga</ets>, OHG. <ets>sorga</ets>, <ets>soraga</ets>,
<ets>suorga</ets>, G. <ets>sorge</ets>, Icel., Sw., & Dan.
<ets>sorg</ets>, Goth. <ets>sa<uacute/rga</ets>; of unknown
origin.]</ety> <def>The uneasiness or pain of mind which is produced by
the loss of any good, real or supposed, or by disappointment in the
expectation of good; grief at having suffered or occasioned evil;
regret; unhappiness; sadness.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How great a <qex>sorrow</qex> suffereth now Arcite!</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The safe and general antidote against <qex>sorrow</qex> is
employment.</q> <rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Grief; unhappiness; regret; sadness; heaviness;
mourning; affliction. See <er>Affliction</er>, and
<er>Grief</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrow</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"row</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sorrowed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sorrowing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sorowen</ets>,
<ets>sorwen</ets>, <ets>sorhen</ets>, AS. <ets>sorgian</ets>; akin to
Goth. <ets>sa<uacute/rgan</ets>. See <er>Sorrow</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To feel pain of mind in consequence of evil
experienced, feared, or done; to grieve; to be sad; to be
sorry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sorrowing</qex> most of all . . . that they should see his
face no more.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xx. 38.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I desire no man to <qex>sorrow</qex> for me.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrowed</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"rowed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Accompanied with sorrow; sorrowful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrowful</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"row*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sorweful</ets>, AS. <ets>sorgful</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Full of sorrow; exhibiting sorrow; sad; dejected; distressed.</def>
<ldquo/This <xex>sorrowful</xex> prisoner.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My soul is exceeding <qex>sorrowful</qex>, even unto death.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. xxvi. 38.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Producing sorrow; exciting grief; mournful;
lamentable; grievous; <as>as, a <ex>sorrowful</ex>
accident</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sad; mournful; dismal; disconsolate; drear;
dreary; grievous; lamentable; doleful; distressing.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sor"row*ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sor"row*ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorrowless</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"row*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from sorrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorry</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sorrier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sorriest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sory</ets>,
<ets>sary</ets>, AS. <ets>s<amac/rig</ets>, fr. <ets>s<amac/r</ets>,
<pos>n.</pos>, <ets>sore</ets>. <ets>See</ets> <er>Sore</er>,
<pos>n.</pos> & <pos>a.</pos> The original sense was, painful; hence,
miserable, sad.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Grieved for the loss of some
good; pained for some evil; feeling regret; -- now generally used to
express light grief or affliction, but formerly often used to express
deeper feeling.</def> <ldquo/I am <xex>sorry</xex> for my sins.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye were made <qex>sorry</qex> after a godly manner.</q> <rj><qau>2
Cor. vii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am <qex>sorry</qex> for thee, friend; 't is the duke's
pleasure.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She entered, were he lief or <qex>sorry</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Melancholy; dismal; gloomy; mournful.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All full of chirking was this <qex>sorry</qex> place.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Poor; mean; worthless; <as>as, a <ex>sorry</ex>
excuse</as>.</def> <ldquo/With <xex>sorry</xex> grace.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cheeks of <qex>sorry</qex> grain will serve.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> Good fruit will sometimes grow on a <qex>sorry</qex> tree.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Hurt; afflicted; mortified; vexed; chagrined;
melancholy; dismal; poor; mean; pitiful.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sors</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sors</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sortes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A lot; also,
a kind of divination by means of lots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sortes Homericae</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sortes Virgilianae</b></col></mcol> <ety>[L., Homeric or
Virgilian lots]</ety>, <cd>a form of divination anciently practiced,
which consisted in taking the first passage on which the eye fell, upon
opening a volume of <persfn>Homer</persfn> or <persfn>Virgil</persfn>,
or a passage drawn from an urn which several were deposited, as
indicating future events, or the proper course to be pursued. In later
times the <book>Bible</book> was used for the same purpose by
Christians.</cd></cs> <-- not very different from Chinese fortune
cookies in concept. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sort</ent><br/
<hw>Sort</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sorl</ets>, L.
<ets>sors</ets>, <ets>sortis</ets>. See <er>Sort</er> kind.]</ety>
<def>Chance; lot; destiny.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By aventure, or <qex>sort</qex>, or cas [chance].</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let blockish Ajax draw<br/
The <qex>sort</qex> to fight with Hector.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1374 --></p>

<p><ent>Sort</ent><br/
<hw>Sort</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sorie</ets> (cf. It.
<ets>sorta</ets>, <ets>sorte</ets>), from L. <ets>sors</ets>,
<ets>sorti</ets>, a lot, part, probably akin to <ets>serere</ets> to
connect. See <er>Series</er>, and cf. <er>Assort</er>, <er>Consort</er>,
<er>Resort</er>, <er>Sorcery</er>, <er>Sort</er> lot.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A kind or species; any number or collection of individual persons
or things characterized by the same or like qualities; a class or order;
<as>as, a <ex>sort</ex> of men; a <ex>sort</ex> of horses; a
<ex>sort</ex> of trees; a <ex>sort</ex> of poems.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Manner; form of being or acting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which for my part I covet to perform,<br/
In <qex>sort</qex> as through the world I did proclaim.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Flowers, in such <qex>sort</qex> worn, can neither be smelt nor
seen well by those that wear them.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll deceive you in another <qex>sort</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To Adam in what <qex>sort</qex><br/
Shall I appear?</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I shall not be wholly without praise, if in some <qex>sort</qex> I
have copied his style.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Condition above the vulgar; rank.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A chance group; a company of persons who happen to
be together; a troop; also, an assemblage of animals.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>sort</xex> of shepherds.<rdquo/
<au>Spenser.</au> <ldquo/A <xex>sort</xex> of steers.<rdquo/
<au>Spenser.</au> <ldquo/A <xex>sort</xex> of doves.<rdquo/
<au>Dryden.</au> <ldquo/A <xex>sort</xex> of rogues.<rdquo/
<au>Massinger.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A boy, a child, and we a <qex>sort</qex> of us,<br/
Vowed against his voyage.</q> <rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A pair; a set; a suit.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>Letters,
figures, points, marks, spaces, or quadrats, belonging to a case,
separately considered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Out of sorts</b></col> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>with some
letters or sorts of type deficient or exhausted in the case or font;
hence, colloquially, out of order; ill; vexed; disturbed.</cd> --
<col><b>To run upon sorts</b></col> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>to use or
require a greater number of some particular letters, figures, or marks
than the regular proportion, as, for example, in making an
index.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Kind; species; rank; condition.</syn> --
<usage><er>Sort</er>, <er>Kind</er>. <xex>Kind</xex> originally denoted
things of the same family, or bound together by some natural affinity;
and hence, a class. <xex>Sort</xex> signifies that which constitutes a
particular lot of parcel, not implying necessarily the idea of affinity,
but of mere assemblage. the two words are now used to a great extent
interchangeably, though <xex>sort</xex> (perhaps from its original
meaning of <xex>lot</xex>) sometimes carries with it a slight tone of
disparagement or contempt, as when we say, that <xex>sort</xex> of
people, that <xex>sort</xex> of language.</usage> <-- surprisingly,
"type" is not included in this synonym-list! in MW10, the list under
"type" includes kind and sort. "class" is mentioned in the def, but not
on the list of synonyms. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As when the total <qex>kind</qex><br/
Of birds, in orderly array on wing,<br/
Came summoned over Eden to receive<br/
Their names of there.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>None of noble <qex>sort</qex><br/
Would so offend a virgin.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sort</ent><br/
<hw>Sort</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sorted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sorting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To separate, and
place in distinct classes or divisions, as things having different
qualities; <as>as, to <ex>sort</ex> cloths according to their colors; to
<ex>sort</ex> wool or thread according to its fineness.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rays which differ in refrangibility may be parted and
<qex>sorted</qex> from one another.</q> <rj><qau>Sir I.
Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To reduce to order from a confused state.</def>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To conjoin; to put together in distribution; to
class.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shellfish have been, by some of the ancients, compared and
<qex>sorted</qex> with insects.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She <qex>sorts</qex> things present with things past.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir J. Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To choose from a number; to select; to
cull.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That he may <qex>sort</qex> out a worthy spouse.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll <qex>sort</qex> some other time to visit you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To conform; to adapt; to accommodate.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I pray thee, <qex>sort</qex> thy heart to patience.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sort</ent><br/
<hw>Sort</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To join or associate
with others, esp. with others of the same kind or species; to
agree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor do metals only <qex>sort</qex> and herd with metals in the
earth, and minerals with minerals.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The illiberality of parents towards children makes them base, and
<qex>sort</qex> with any company.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To suit; to fit; to be in accord; to
harmonize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are happy whose natures <qex>sort</qex> with their
vocations.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Things <qex>sort</qex> not to my will.</q>
<rj><qau>herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I can not tell you precisely how they <qex>sorted</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortable</ent><br/
<hw>Sort"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sortable</ets> suitable.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Capable of being
sorted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Suitable; befitting; proper.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>con.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortably</ent><br/
<hw>Sort"a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Suitable.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>otgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortal</ent><br/
<hw>Sort"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to a
sort.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortance</ent><br/
<hw>Sort"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Sort</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>Suitableness; agreement.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorter</ent><br/
<hw>Sort"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, sorts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sor"tes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Sors</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortie</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"tie</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>sortir</ets> to go out, to issue, probably fr. L.
<ets>sortus</ets>, for <ets>surrectus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>surgere</ets>
to raise up, to rise up. See <er>Source</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>
<def>The sudden issuing of a body of troops, usually small, from a
besieged place to attack or harass the besiegers; a sally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortilege</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"ti*lege</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sortil<egrave/ge</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sors</ets>, <ets>sortis</ets>,
a lot + <ets>legere</ets> to gather, to select.]</ety> <def>The act or
practice of drawing lots; divination by drawing lots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A woman infamous for <qex>sortileges</qex> and witcheries.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortilegious</ent><br/
<hw>Sor`ti*le"gious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to
sortilege.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortilegy</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"til"e*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sortilege.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortita</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sor*ti"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., a coming
out.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The air sung by any of the principal
characters in an opera on entering.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A closing voluntary; a postlude.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortition</ent><br/
<hw>Sor*ti"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sortitio</ets>, from <ets>sortiri</ets> to draw or cast lots, fr.
<ets>sors</ets>, <ets>sortis</ets>, a lot.]</ety> <def>Selection or
appointment by lot.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sortment</ent><br/
<hw>Sort"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Assortiment.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>So"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sori</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a
heap.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of the fruit dots,
or small clusters of sporangia, on the back of the fronds of
ferns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>In parasitic fungi, any mass of spores
bursting through the epidermis of a host plant.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>In lichens, a heap of soredia on the thallus.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorwe</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"we</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>Sorrow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sorweful</ent><br/
<hw>Sor"we*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sorrowful.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sory</ent><br/
<hw>So"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sory</ets>, Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Old Min. Chem.)</fld> <def>Green vitriol, or some
earth imregnated with it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>SOS</ent><br/
<hw>SOS</hw>. <def>The letters signified by the signal ( . . . --- . . .
) prescribed by the International Radiotelegraphic Convention of 1912
for use by ships in distress.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>So-so</ent><br/
<hw>So"-so`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>So</ets> +
<ets>so</ets>.]</ety> <def>Neither very good nor very bad; middling;
passable; tolerable; indifferent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In some Irish houses, where things are <qex>so-so</qex>,<br/
One gammon of bacon hangs up for a show.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [Burns] certainly wrote some <qex>so-so</qex> verses to the
Tree of Liberty.</q> <rj><qau>Prof. Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>So-so</ent><br/
<hw>So"-so`</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Tolerably; passably.</def>
<rj><au>H. James.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soss</ent><br/
<hw>Soss</hw> <pr>(?; 115)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Souse</er>.]</ety> <def>To fall at once into a chair or seat; to sit
lazily.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soss</ent><br/
<hw>Soss</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To throw in a negligent or careless
manner; to toss.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soss</ent><br/
<hw>Soss</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A lazy fellow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A heavy fall.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hallowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soss</ent><br/
<hw>Soss</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sesspol</er>.]</ety>
<def>Anything dirty or muddy; a dirty puddle.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sostenuto</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sos`te*nu"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Sustained; -- applied to a movement or passage
the sounds of which are to sustained to the utmost of the nominal value
of the time; also, to a passage the tones of which are to be somewhat
prolonged or protacted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sot</ent><br/
<hw>Sot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. LL.
<ets>sottus</ets>; of unknown origin, cf. Ir. <ets>sotal</ets> pride,
<ets>soithir</ets> proud, or Chald. & NHeb. <ets>shoten</ets>
foolish.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A stupid person; a blockhead; a dull
fellow; a dolt.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>outh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In Egypt oft has seen the <qex>sot</qex> bow down,<br/
And reverence some d<?/ified baboon.</q> <rj><qau>Oldham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person stupefied by excessive drinking; an
habitual drunkard.</def> <ldquo/A brutal <xex>sot</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Granville.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every sign<br/
That calls the staring <qex>sots</qex> to nasty wine.</q>
<rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sot</ent><br/
<hw>Sot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sottish; foolish; stupid;
dull.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Rich, but <xex>sot</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sot</ent><br/
<hw>Sot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To stupefy; to infatuate; to
besot.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I hate to see a brave, bold fellow <qex>sotted</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sot</ent><br/
<hw>Sot</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To tipple to stupidity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sotadean</ent><br/
<hw>So`ta*de"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sotadic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sotadic</ent><br/
<hw>So*tad"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or
resembling, the lascivious compositions of the Greek poet
<xex>Sotades</xex>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A Sotadic verse or
poem.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sote</ent><br/
<hw>sote</hw> <pr>(s<omac/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sweet.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au> <au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sotil</ent><br/
<ent>sotel</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>so"tel</hw> <pr>(s<omac/"t<ecr/l)</pr>, <hw>So"til</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Subtile.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>soteriology</ent><br/
<hw>so*te`ri*ol"o*gy</hw>
<pr>(s<osl/*t<emac/`r<icr/*<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>swthri`a</grk> safety (from <grk>swth`rios</grk> saving,
<grk>swth`r</grk> a savior, <grk>sw`zein</grk> to save) +
<ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A discourse on health, or the
science of promoting and preserving health.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The doctrine of salvation by
<person>Jesus Christ</person>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sothe</ent><br/
<hw>Sothe</hw> <pr>(s<omac/th <it>or</it> s<omac/"th<eit/)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Sooth.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sothic</ent><br/
<ent>Sothiac</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>So"thi*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Soth"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to <persfn>Sothis</persfn>,
the Egyptian name for the Dog Star; taking its name from the Dog Star;
canicular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sothiac year</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Sothic
year</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Chronol.)</fld>, <cd>the Egyptian year of
365 days and 6 hours, as distinguished from the Egyptian <xex>vague
year</xex>, which contained 365 days. The <xex>Sothic period</xex>
consists of 1,460 Sothic years, being equal to 1,461 vague years. One of
these periods ended in July, <sc>a. d.</sc> 139.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sotilte</ent><br/
<hw>So"til*te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Subtlety.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sotted</ent><br/
<hw>Sot"ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sot</er>. Befooled; deluded; besotted.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/This <xex>sotted</xex> priest.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sottery</ent><br/
<hw>Sot"ter*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Folly.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gauden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sottish</ent><br/
<hw>Sot"tish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sot</er>.]</ety> <def>Like a sot; doltish; very foolish;
drunken.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How ignorant are <qex>sottish</qex> pretenders to astrology!</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Dull; stupid; senseless; doltish;
infatuate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Sot"tish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sot"tish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sotto voce</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sot`to vo"ce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[It.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>With a restrained voice or moderate force; in an
undertone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Spoken low or in an undertone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sou</ent><br/
<hw>Sou</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sous</plw> <pr>(#)</pr> <it>or</it> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>sou</ets>, OF. <ets>sol</ets>, from L. <ets>solidus</ets> a gold
coin, in LL., a coin of less value. See <er>Sold</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Solid</er>, and and cf. <er>Sol</er>, <er>Soldo</er>.]</ety> <def>An
old French copper coin, equivalent in value to, and now displaced by,
the five-centime piece (<frac1x20/ of a franc), which is popularly
called a <ex>sou</ex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souari nut</ent><br/
<hw>Sou*a"ri nut`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The large
edible nutlike seed of a tall tropical American tree (<spn>Caryocar
nuciferum</spn>) of the same natural order with the tea plant; -- also
called <altname>butternut</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sawarra nut</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soubah</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sou"bah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Subah</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soubahdar</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sou"bah*dar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Subahdar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soubise</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sou`bise"</hw> <pr>(s<oomac/`b<emac/z")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A sauce made of white onions and melted
butter mixed with velout<eacute/ sauce.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of cravat worn by men in the late 18th
century.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soubrette</ent><br/
<hw>Sou`brette"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A female
servant or attendant; specifically, as a term of the theater, a lady's
maid, in comedies, who acts the part of an intrigante; a meddlesome,
mischievous female servant or young woman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soubriquet</ent><br/
<hw>Sou`bri`quet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sobriquet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souce</ent><br/
<hw>Souce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 1st
<er>Souse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souce</ent><br/
<hw>Souce</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>See <er>Souse</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>penser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souchong</ent><br/
<hw>Sou*chong"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Chin. <ets>seou
chong</ets> little plant or sort.]</ety> <def>A kind of black tea of a
fine quality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soudan</ent><br/
<hw>Sou*dan"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[F.]</ety> <def>A
sultan.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soudet</ent><br/
<ent>Souded</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Soud"ed</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <hw>Soud"et</hw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr></mhw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Solder</er>.]</ety>
<def>United; consolidated; made firm; strengthened.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O martyr <qex>souded</qex> for virginity!</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souffle</ent><br/
<hw>Souf"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A murmuring or blowing sound; <as>as, the uterine
<ex>souffle</ex> heard over the pregnant uterus</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souffle</ent><br/
<hw>Souf"fl<eacute/</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>souffl<eacute/</ets>, p. p. of <ets>souffler</ets> to puff.]</ety>
<fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>A side dish served hot from the oven at
dinner, made of eggs, milk, and flour or other farinaceous substance,
beaten till very light, and flavored with fruits, liquors, or
essence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souffle</ent><br/
<ent>Soufflee</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Souf`fl<eacute/"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>souffl<eacute/</ets>, p. p. of <ets>souffler</ets> to puff.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Ceramics)</fld> <def>Decorated with very small drops
or sprinkles of color, as if blown from a bellows.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <altsp>Often
\'d8<hw>Souffl<eacute/e</hw>.</altsp> <def>Filled with air by beating,
and baked; <as>as, an omelette <ex>souffl<eacute/</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sough</ent><br/
<hw>Sough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sough</ent><br/
<hw>Sough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A small drain; an adit.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>W. M. Buchanan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sough</ent><br/
<hw>Sough</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>s<?/gr</ets> (in comp.) a rushing sound, or OE. <ets>swough</ets>,
<ets>swogh</ets>, a sound, AS. <ets>sw<?/gan</ets> to rustle. Cf.
<er>Surf</er>, <er>Swoon</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The sound produced by soughing; a hollow murmur or
roaring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The whispering leaves or solemn <qex>sough</qex> of the
forest.</q> <rj><qau>W. Howitt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a vague rumor or flying report.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A cant or whining mode of speaking, especially in
preaching or praying.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sough</ent><br/
<hw>Sough</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To whistle or sigh, as the
wind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sought</ent><br/
<hw>Sought</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Seek</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souke</ent><br/
<hw>Souke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To suck.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soul</ent><br/
<hw>Soul</hw> <pr>(s<omac/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sole.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soul</ent><br/
<hw>Soul</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>so<ucir/ler</ets> to
satiate. See <er>Soil</er> to feed.]</ety> <def>To afford suitable
sustenance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Warner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soul</ent><br/
<hw>Soul</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>soule</ets>,
<ets>saule</ets>, AS. <ets>s<amac/wel</ets>, <ets>s<amac/wl</ets>; akin
to OFries. <ets>s<?/le</ets>, OS. <ets>s<?/ola</ets>, D.
<ets>ziel</ets>, G. <ets>seele</ets>, OHG. <ets>s<?/la</ets>,
<ets>s<?/ula</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<amac/la</ets>, Sw. <ets>sj<aum/l</ets>,
Dan. <ets>siael</ets>, Goth. <ets>saiwala</ets>; of uncertain origin,
perhaps akin to L. <ets>saeculum</ets> a lifetime, age (cf.
<er>Secular</er>.)]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The spiritual, rational, and
immortal part in man; that part of man which enables him to think, and
which renders him a subject of moral government; -- sometimes, in
distinction from the higher nature, or spirit, of man, the so-called
animal soul, that is, the seat of life, the sensitive affections and
phantasy, exclusive of the voluntary and rational powers; -- sometimes,
in distinction from the mind, the moral and emotional part of man's
nature, the seat of feeling, in distinction from intellect; --
sometimes, the intellect only; the understanding; the seat of knowledge,
as distinguished from <xex>feeling</xex>. In a more general sense,
<ldquo/an animating, separable, surviving entity, the vehicle of
individual personal existence.<rdquo/</def> <rj><au>Tylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The eyes of our <qex>souls</qex> only then begin to see, when our
bodily eyes are closing.</q> <rj><qau>Law.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The seat of real life or vitality; the source of
action; the animating or essential part.</def> <ldquo/The hidden
<xex>soul</xex> of harmony.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou sun, of this great world both eye and <qex>soul</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The leader; the inspirer; the moving spirit; the
heart; <as>as, the <ex>soul</ex> of an enterprise; an able general is
the <ex>soul</ex> of his army</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is the very <qex>soul</qex> of bounty!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Energy; courage; spirit; fervor; affection, or any
other noble manifestation of the heart or moral nature; inherent power
or goodness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That he wants algebra he must confess;<br/
But not a <qex>soul</qex> to give our arms success.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A human being; a person; -- a familiar appellation,
usually with a qualifying epithet; <as>as, poor
<ex>soul</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As cold waters to a thirsty <qex>soul</qex>, so is good news from
a far country.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xxv. 25.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God forbid so many simple <qex>souls</qex><br/
Should perish by the sword!</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now mistress Gilpin (careful <qex>soul</qex>).</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A pure or disembodied spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That to his only Son . . . every <qex>soul</qex> in heaven<br/
Shall bend the knee.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A perceived shared community and awareness among
African-Americans.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Soul music.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Soul</xex> is used in the formation of numerous
compounds, most of which are of obvious signification; as,
<xex>soul</xex>-betraying, <xex>soul</xex>-consuming,
<xex>soul</xex>-destroying, <xex>soul</xex>-distracting,
<xex>soul</xex>-enfeebling, <xex>soul</xex>-exalting,
<xex>soul</xex>-felt, <xex>soul</xex>-harrowing,
<xex>soul</xex>-piercing, <xex>soul</xex>-quickening,
<xex>soul</xex>-reviving, <xex>soul</xex>-stirring,
<xex>soul</xex>-subduing, <xex>soul</xex>-withering, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Spirit; life; courage; fire; ardor.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cure of souls</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Cure</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Soul bell</b></col>, <cd>the passing
bell.</cd> <au>Bp. Hall.</au> -- <col><b>Soul foot</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Soul scot</cref>, below.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>Soul
scot</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Soul shot</b></col>.
<ety>[<ets>Soul</ets> + <ets>scot</ets>, or <ets>shot</ets>; cf. AS.
<ets>s<amac/welsceat</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(O. Eccl. Law)</fld> <cd>A
funeral duty paid in former times for a requiem for the soul.</cd>
<au>Ayliffe.</au> </cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soul</ent><br/
<hw>Soul</hw> <pr>(s<omac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To indue with a
soul; to furnish with a soul or mind.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>soul</ent><br/
<hw>soul</hw> <pr>(s<omac/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>By or for
African-Americans, or characteristic of their culture; <as>as,
<ex>soul</ex> music; <ex>soul</ex> newspapers; <ex>soul</ex>
food</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souled</ent><br/
<hw>Souled</hw> <pr>(s<omac/ld)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with
a soul; possessing soul and feeling; -- used chiefly in composition;
<as>as, great-<ex>souled</ex> Hector</as>.</def> <ldquo/Grecian chiefs .
. . largely <xex>souled</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soulili</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sou"li*li`</hw> <pr>(s<oomac/"l<esl/*l<emac/`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A long-tailed, crested Javan
monkey (<spn>Semnopithecus mitratus</spn>). The head, the crest, and the
upper surface of the tail, are black.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>soul kiss</ent><br/
<hw>soul" kiss`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kiss in which
both parties have their mouths open and pressed together, and the tongue
of one or both is maneuvered within the mouth of the other.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> French kiss, deep kiss.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soulless</ent><br/
<hw>Soul"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being without a
soul, or without greatness or nobleness of mind; mean;
spiritless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Slave, <qex>souless</qex> villain, dog!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soullessly</ent><br/
<hw>Soul"less*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a soulless manner.</def>
<rj><au>Tylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soun</ent><br/
<hw>Soun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>Sound.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sund</ets> a
swimming, akin to E. <ets>swim</ets>. See <er>Swim</er>.]</ety> <def>The
air bladder of a fish; <as>as, cod <ex>sounds</ex> are an esteemed
article of food</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
cuttlefish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sounder</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Soundest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sound</ets>, AS.
<ets>sund</ets>; akin to D. ge<ets>zond</ets>, G. ge<ets>sund</ets>,
OHG. gi<ets>sunt</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>sund</ets>, and perhaps to L.
<ets>sanus</ets>. Cf. <er>Sane</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Whole;
unbroken; unharmed; free from flaw, defect, or decay; perfect of the
kind; <as>as, <ex>sound</ex> timber; <ex>sound</ex> fruit; a
<ex>sound</ex> tooth; a <ex>sound</ex> ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Healthy; not diseased; not being in a morbid state;
-- said of body or mind; <as>as, a <ex>sound</ex> body; a <ex>sound</ex>
constitution; a <ex>sound</ex> understanding</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Firm; strong; safe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The brasswork here, how rich it is in beams,<br/
And how, besides, it makes the whole house <qex>sound</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Free from error; correct; right; honest; true;
faithful; orthodox; -- said of persons; <as>as, a <ex>sound</ex> lawyer;
a <ex>sound</ex> thinker</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do not I know you a favorer<br/
Of this new seat? Ye are nor <qex>sound</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Founded in truth or right; supported by justice; not
to be overthrown on refuted; not fallacious; <as>as, <ex>sound</ex>
argument or reasoning; a <ex>sound</ex> objection; <ex>sound</ex>
doctrine; <ex>sound</ex> principles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hold fast the form of <qex>sound</qex> words, which thou hast
heard of me.</q> <rj><qau>2 Tim. i. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>heavy; laid on with force; <as>as, a <ex>sound</ex>
beating</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Undisturbed; deep; profound; <as>as, <ex>sound</ex>
sleep</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Founded in law; legal; valid; not defective; <as>as,
a <ex>sound</ex> title to land</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sound</xex> is sometimes used in the formation of
self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>sound</xex>-headed,
<xex>sound</xex>-hearted, <xex>sound</xex>-timbered, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sound currency</b></col> <fld>(Com.)</fld>, <cd>a
currency whose actual value is the same as its nominal value; a currency
which does not deteriorate or depreciate or fluctuate in comparision
with the standard of values.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Soundly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So <qex>sound</qex> he slept that naught might him awake.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sund</ets> a narrow sea or
strait; akin to Icel., Sw., Dan. & G. <ets>sund</ets>, probably so named
because it could be <ets>swum</ets> across. See <er>Swim</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geog.)</fld> <def>A narrow passage of water, or a strait between
the mainland and an island; also, a strait connecting two seas, or
connecting a sea or lake with the ocean; <as>as, the <ex>Sound</ex>
between the Baltic and the german Ocean; Long Island
<ex>Sound</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>Sound</qex> of Denmark, where ships pay toll.</q>
<rj><qau>Camden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sound dues</b></col>, <cd>tolls formerly imposed by
Denmark on vessels passing through the Baltic Sound.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sounded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sounding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>sonder</ets>; cf. AS.
<ets>sund</ets>gyrd a sounding rod, <ets>sund</ets>line a sounding line
(see <er>Sound</er> a narrow passage of water).]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To measure the depth of; to fathom; especially, to ascertain the
depth of by means of a line and plummet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To ascertain, or try to ascertain, the
thoughts, motives, and purposes of (a person); to examine; to try; to
test; to probe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I was in jest,<br/
And by that offer meant to <qex>sound</qex> your breast.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I've <qex>sounded</qex> my Numidians man by man.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To explore, as the bladder or
urethra, with a sound; to examine with a sound; also, to examine by
auscultation or percussion; <as>as, to <ex>sound</ex> a
patient</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1375 --></p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To ascertain the
depth of water with a sounding line or other device.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>sound</qex> as a shipman <qex>soundeth</qex> in the sea
with his plummet to know the depth of sea.</q>
<rj><qau>Palsgrave.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sonde</ets>. See
<er>Sound</er> to fathom.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Any elongated
instrument or probe, usually metallic, by which cavities of the body are
sounded or explored, especially the bladder for stone, or the urethra
for a stricture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>soun</ets>, OF.
<ets>son</ets>, <ets>sun</ets>, F. <ets>son</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>sonus</ets> akin to Skr. <ets>svana</ets> sound, <ets>svan</ets> to
sound, and perh. to E. <ets>swan</ets>. Cf. <er>Assonant</er>,
<er>Consonant</er>, <er>Person</er>, <er>Sonata</er>, <er>Sonnet</er>,
<er>Sonorous</er>, <er>Swan</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The peceived
object occasioned by the impulse or vibration of a material substance
affecting the ear; a sensation or perception of the mind received
through the ear, and produced by the impulse or vibration of the air or
other medium with which the ear is in contact; the effect of an
impression made on the organs of hearing by an impulse or vibration of
the air caused by a collision of bodies, or by other means; noise;
report; <as>as, the <ex>sound</ex> of a drum; the <ex>sound</ex> of the
human voice; a horrid <ex>sound</ex>; a charming <ex>sound</ex>; a
sharp, high, or shrill <ex>sound</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The warlike <qex>sound</qex><br/
Of trumpets loud and clarions.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The occasion of sound; the impulse or vibration
which would occasion sound to a percipient if present with unimpaired;
hence, the theory of vibrations in elastic media such cause sound;
<as>as, a treatise on <ex>sound</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense, sounds are spoken of as
<xex>audible</xex> and <xex>inaudible</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Noise without signification; empty noise; noise and
nothing else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sense and not <qex>sound</qex> . . . must be the principle.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sound boarding</b></col>, <cd>boards for holding pugging,
placed in partitions of under floors in order to deaden sounds.</cd> --
<col><b>Sound bow</b></col>, <cd>in a series of transverse sections of a
bell, that segment against which the clapper strikes, being the part
which is most efficacious in producing the sound. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
of <er>Bell</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sound post</b></col>.
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Sounding post</cref>, under
<er>Sounding</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sounen</ets>,
<ets>sownen</ets>, OF. <ets>soner</ets>, <ets>suner</ets>, F.
<ets>sonner</ets>, from L. <ets>sonare</ets>. See <er>Sound</er> a
noise.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make a noise; to utter a voice; to
make an impulse of the air that shall strike the organs of hearing with
a perceptible effect.</def> <ldquo/And first taught speaking trumpets
how to <xex>sound</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How silver-sweet <qex>sound</qex> lovers' tongues!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be conveyed in sound; to be spread or published;
to convey intelligence by sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From you <qex>sounded</qex> out the word of the Lord.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Thess. i. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make or convey a certain impression, or to have a
certain import, when heard; hence, to seem; to appear; <as>as, this
reproof <ex>sounds</ex> harsh; the story <ex>sounds</ex> like an
invention</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Good sir, why do you start, and seem to fear<br/
Things that do <qex>sound</qex> so fair?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><mcol><col><b>To sound in</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To sound
into</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to tend to; to partake of the nature of; to
be consonant with.</cd> <mark>[Obs., except in the phrase To sound in
damages, below.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Soun[d]ing in</qex> moral virtue was his speech.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>To sound in damages</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>to
have the essential quality of damages. This is said of an action
brought, not for the recovery of a specific thing, as replevin, etc.,
but for damages only, as trespass, and the like.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Sound</ent><br/
<hw>Sound</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to make a
noise; to play on; <as>as, to <ex>sound</ex> a trumpet or a horn; to
<ex>sound</ex> an alarm</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A bagpipe well could he play and <qex>soun</qex>[d].</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to exit as a sound; <as>as, to
<ex>sound</ex> a note with the voice, or on an
instrument</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To order, direct, indicate, or proclain by a sound,
or sounds; to give a signal for by a certain sound; <as>as, to
<ex>sound</ex> a retreat; to <ex>sound</ex> a parley</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The clock <qex>sounded</qex> the hour of noon.</q> <rj><qau>G. H.
Lewes.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To celebrate or honor by sounds; to cause to be
reported; to publish or proclaim; <as>as, to <ex>sound</ex> the praises
of fame of a great man or a great exploit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To examine the condition of (anything) by causing
the same to emit sounds and noting their character; <as>as, to
<ex>sound</ex> a piece of timber; to <ex>sound</ex> a vase; to
<ex>sound</ex> the lungs of a patient</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To signify; to import; to denote.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Soun[d]ing</qex> alway the increase of his winning.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soundable</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
sounded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soundage</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Dues for
soundings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sound-board</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"-board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
sounding-board.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To many a row of pipes the <qex>sound-board</qex> breathes.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sounder</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which; sounds; specifically, an instrument used in telegraphy in place
of a register, the communications being read by sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sounder</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A herd of wild
hogs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sounding</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Making or emitting sound; hence,
sonorous; <as>as, <ex>sounding</ex> words</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sounding</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who,
or that which, sounds (in any of the senses of the several
verbs).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <ety>[From <er>Sound</er> to
fathom.]</ety> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>measurement by sounding; also, the
depth so ascertained.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any place or part of the
ocean, or other water, where a sounding line will reach the bottom; --
usually in the plural.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The sand, shells, or the
like, that are brought up by the sounding lead when it has touched
bottom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sounding lead</b></col>, <cd>the plummet at the end of a
sounding line.</cd> -- <col><b>Sounding line</b></col>, <cd>a line
having a plummet at the end, used in making soundings.</cd> --
<col><b>Sounding post</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>a small post in a
violin, violoncello, or similar instrument, set under the bridge as a
support, for propagating the sounds to the body of the instrument; --
called also <altname>sound post</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sounding
rod</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a rod used to ascertain the depth
of water in a ship's hold.</cd> -- <col><b>In soundings</b></col>,
<cd>within the eighty-fathom line.</cd> <rj><au>Ham. Nav.
Encyc.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sounding balloon</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"ing bal*loon"</hw>. <def>An unmanned balloon sent aloft for
meteorological or aeronautic purposes.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sounding-board</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"ing-board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A thin board which propagates the sound in a
piano, in a violin, and in some other musical instruments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A board or structure placed behind or over a pulpit
or rostrum to give distinctness to a speaker's voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>See <cref>Sound boarding</cref>,
under <er>Sound</er>, a noise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soundless</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not capable of
being sounded or fathomed; unfathomable.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soundless</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no sound; noiseless;
silent.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sound"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sound"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soundly</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sound manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soundness</ent><br/
<hw>Sound"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sound; <as>as, the <ex>soundness</ex> of timber, of fruit, of the teeth,
etc.; the <ex>soundness</ex> of reasoning or argument;
<ex>soundness</ex> of faith.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Firmness; strength; solidity; healthiness; truth;
rectitude.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soune</ent><br/
<hw>Soune</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To sound.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sounst</ent><br/
<hw>Sounst</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Soused. See
<er>Souse</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soup</ent><br/
<hw>Soup</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>soupe</ets>, OF.
<ets>sope</ets>, <ets>supe</ets>, <ets>soupe</ets>, perhaps originally,
a piece of bread; probably of Teutonic origin; cf. D. <ets>sop</ets>
sop, G. <ets>suppe</ets> soup. See <er>Sop</er> something dipped in a
liquid, and cf. <er>Supper</er>.]</ety> <def>A liquid food of many
kinds, usually made by boiling meat and vegetables, or either of them,
in water, -- commonly seasoned or flavored; strong broth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Soup kitchen</b></col>, <cd>an establishment for
preparing and supplying soup to the poor.</cd> -- <col><b>Soup
ticket</b></col>, <cd>a ticket conferring the privilege of receiving
soup at a soup kitchen.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soup</ent><br/
<hw>Soup</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To sup or swallow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soup</ent><br/
<hw>Soup</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To breathe out.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>amden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soup</ent><br/
<hw>Soup</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To sweep. See <er>Sweep</er>, and
<er>Swoop</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soupcon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Soup`<cced/on"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A suspicion; a suggestion; hence, a very small portion; a taste;
<as>as, coffee with a <ex>soup<cced/on</ex> of brandy; a
<ex>soup<cced/on</ex> of coquetry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soupe-maigre</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Soupe`-mai"gre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>Soup made chiefly from vegetables or fish with
a little butter and a few condiments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souple</ent><br/
<hw>Sou"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That part of a flail
which strikes the grain.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soupy</ent><br/
<hw>Soup"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling soup;
souplike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sour</ent><br/
<hw>Sour</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sourer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sourest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sour</ets>,
<ets>sur</ets>, AS. <ets>s<?/r</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zuur</ets>, G.
<ets>sauer</ets>, OHG. <ets>s<?/r</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<?/rr</ets>, Sw.
<ets>sur</ets>, Dan. <ets>suur</ets>, Lith. <ets>suras</ets> salt, Russ.
<ets>surovui</ets> harsh, rough. Cf. <er>Sorrel</er>, the plant.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Having an acid or sharp, biting taste, like vinegar,
and the juices of most unripe fruits; acid; tart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>sour</qex> things, as vinegar, provoke appetite.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Changed, as by keeping, so as to be acid, rancid, or
musty, turned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Disagreeable; unpleasant; hence; cross; crabbed;
peevish; morose; <as>as, a man of a <ex>sour</ex> temper; a
<ex>sour</ex> reply.</as></def> <ldquo/A <xex>sour</xex>
countenance.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was a scholar . . . <br/
Lofty and <qex>sour</qex> to them that loved him not,<br/
But to those men that sought him sweet as summer.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Afflictive; painful.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sour</xex>
adversity.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Cold and unproductive; <as>as, <ex>sour</ex> land; a
<ex>sour</ex> marsh.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sour dock</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>sorrel.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sour gourd</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the gourdlike
fruit <spn>Adansonia Gregorii</spn>, and <spn>Adansonia digitata</spn>;
also, either of the trees bearing this fruit. See
<er>Adansonia</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sour grapes</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Grape</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sour gum</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Turelo</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sour plum</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the edible acid fruit of an Australian tree
(<spn>Owenia venosa</spn>); also, the tree itself, which furnished a
hard reddish wood used by wheelwrights.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Acid; sharp; tart; acetous; acetose; harsh;
acrimonious; crabbed; currish; peevish.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sour</ent><br/
<hw>Sour</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sour or acid substance; whatever
produces a painful effect.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sour</ent><br/
<hw>Sour</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>s<?/rian</ets> to sour,
to become sour.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to become sour; to
cause to turn from sweet to sour; <as>as, exposure to the air
<ex>sours</ex> many substances</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So the sun's heat, with different powers,<br/
Ripens the grape, the liquor <qex>sours</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make cold and unproductive, as soil.</def>
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make unhappy, uneasy, or less
agreeable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>sour</qex> your happiness I must report,<br/
The queen is dead.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause or permit to become harsh or
unkindly.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Souring</xex> his cheeks.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pride had not <qex>sour'd</qex> nor wrath debased my heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Harte.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To macerate, and render fit for plaster or mortar;
<as>as, to <ex>sour</ex> lime for business purposes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sour</ent><br/
<hw>Sour</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Soured</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Souring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To become sour; to turn from
sweet to sour; <as>as, milk soon <ex>sours</ex> in hot weather; a kind
temper sometimes <ex>sours</ex> in adversity.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They keep out melancholy from the virtuous, and hinder the hatred
of vice from <qex>souring</qex> into severity.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Source</ent><br/
<hw>Source</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sours</ets>,
OF. <ets>sourse</ets>, <ets>surse</ets>, <ets>sorse</ets>, F.
<ets>source</ets>, fr. OF. <ets>sors</ets>, p. p. of OF.
<ets>sordre</ets>, <ets>surdre</ets>, <ets>sourdre</ets>, to spring
forth or up, F. <ets>sourdre</ets>, fr. L. <ets>surgere</ets> to lift or
raise up, to spring up. See <er>Surge</er>, and cf. <er>Souse</er> to
plunge or swoop as a bird upon its prey.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of rising; a rise; an ascent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Therefore right as an hawk upon a <qex>sours</qex><br/
Up springeth into the air, right so prayers . . . <br/
Maken their <qex>sours</qex> to Goddes ears two.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The rising from the ground, or beginning, of a
stream of water or the like; a spring; a fountain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where as the Poo out of a welle small<br/
Taketh his firste springing and his <qex>sours</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Kings that rule<br/
Behind the hidden <qex>sources</qex> of the Nile.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That from which anything comes forth, regarded as
its cause or origin; the person from whom anything originates; first
cause.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>source</qex> of ideas every man has wholly in
himself.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>source</qex> of Newton's light, of Bacon's sense.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Origin</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sourcrout</ent><br/
<hw>Sour"crout`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sauerkraut</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sourde</ent><br/
<hw>Sourde</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sourdre</ets>. See <er>Source</er>.]</ety> <def>To have origin or
source; to rise; to spring.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now might men ask whereof that pride <qex>sourdeth</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souring</ent><br/
<hw>Sour"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any
sour apple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sourish</ent><br/
<hw>Sour"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat sour; moderately acid;
<as>as, <ex>sourish</ex> fruit; a <ex>sourish</ex> taste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sourkrout</ent><br/
<hw>Sour"krout`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Sauerkraut</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sourly</ent><br/
<hw>Sour"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sour manner; with
sourness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sourness</ent><br/
<hw>Sour"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sour.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sours</ent><br/
<hw>Sours</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Source. See
<er>Source</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soursop</ent><br/
<hw>Sour"sop`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The large succulent and slightly acid fruit of a small tree
(<spn>Anona muricata</spn>) of the West Indies; also, the tree itself.
It is closely allied to the custard apple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sourwood</ent><br/
<hw>Sour"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The sorrel tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souse</ent><br/
<ent>Sous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sous</hw>, <hw>Souse</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(F. s<oomac/;
<xex>colloq. Eng</xex>. sous)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A corrupt form of
Sou.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Colman, the Elder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souse</ent><br/
<hw>Souse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>sausse</ets>.
See <er>Sauce</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>souce</asp>,
<asp>sowce</asp>, and <asp>sowse</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pickle
made with salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something kept or steeped in pickle; esp., the
pickled ears, feet, etc., of swine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And he that can rear up a pig in his house,<br/
Hath cheaper his bacon, and sweeter his <qex>souse</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tusser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The ear; especially, a hog's ear.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act of sousing; a plunging into water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souse</ent><br/
<hw>Souse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A drunkard.</def>
<mark>[slang]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souse</ent><br/
<hw>Souse</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Soused</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sousing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>saucer</ets> to wet
with sauce. See <er>Souse</er> pickle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To steep
in pickle; to pickle.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>soused</xex> gurnet.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To plunge or immerse in water or any
liquid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>soused</qex> me over head and ears in water.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To drench, as by an immersion; to wet
throughly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Although I be well <qex>soused</qex> in this shower.</q>
<rj><qau>Gascoigne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souse</ent><br/
<hw>Souse</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Probably fr. OF. <ets>sors</ets>,
p. p. of <ets>sordre</ets> to rise, and first used of an upward swood,
then of a swoop in general, but also confused with <er>Souse</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> See <er>Source</er>.]</ety> <def>To swoop or plunge, as
a bird upon its prey; to fall suddenly; to rush with speed; to make a
sudden attack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For then I viewed his plunge and <qex>souse</qex><br/
Into the foamy main.</q> <rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Jove's bird will <qex>souse</qex> upon the timorous hare.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Dryden. Jr.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souse</ent><br/
<hw>Souse</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pounce upon.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The gallant monarch] like eagle o'er his serie towers,<br/
To <qex>souse</qex> annoyance that comes near his nest.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souse</ent><br/
<hw>Souse</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of sousing, or
swooping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As a falcon fair<br/
That once hath failed or her <qex>souse</qex> full near.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souse</ent><br/
<hw>Souse</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With a sudden swoop;
violently.</def> <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soused</ent><br/
<hw>Soused</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adj.</pos> <def>Thoroughly drunken;
inebriated.</def> <mark>[slang]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> bombed; pickled; drunk; intoxicated.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souslik</ent><br/
<hw>Sous"lik</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Suslik</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sout</ent><br/
<hw>Sout</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Soot.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soutache</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sou`tache"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>A kind of narrow braid, usually of silk; -- also known as
<altname>Russian braid</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soutage</ent><br/
<hw>Sout"age</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>That in which anything is packed; bagging, as for
hops.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soutane</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sou`tane"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. Sp.
<ets>sotana</ets>, or It. <ets>sottana</ets>, LL. <ets>subtana</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>subtus</ets> below, beneath, fr. <ets>sub</ets>
under.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Costume)</fld> <def>A close garnment with
straight sleeves, and skirts reaching to the ankles, and buttoned in
front from top to bottom; especially, the black garment of this shape
worn by the clergy in France and Italy as their daily dress; a
cassock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souter</ent><br/
<hw>Sou"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<?/t<?/re</ets>, fr. It. <ets>sutor</ets>, fr. <ets>suere</ets> to
sew.]</ety> <def>A shoemaker; a cobbler.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is no work better than another to please God: . . . to wash
dishes, to be a <qex>souter</qex>, or an apostle, -- all is one.</q>
<rj><qau>Tyndale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souterly</ent><br/
<hw>Sou"ter*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a cobbler or
cobblers; like a cobbler; hence, vulgar; low.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souterrain</ent><br/
<hw>Sou"ter*rain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Subterranean</er>.]</ety> <def>A grotto or cavern under
ground.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>South</ent><br/
<hw>South</hw> <pr>(?; <xex>by sailors</xex> sou)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>south</ets>, <ets>su<thorn/</ets>, AS.
<ets>s<umac/<edh/</ets> for <ets>sun<edh/</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>zuid</ets>, OHG. <ets>sund</ets>, G. <ets>s<uum/d</ets>,
<ets>s<uum/den</ets>, Icel. <ets>su<edh/r</ets>, <ets>sunnr</ets>, Dan.
<ets>syd</ets>, <ets>s<oum/nden</ets>, Sw. <ets>syd</ets>,
<ets>s<oum/der</ets>, <ets>sunnan</ets>; all probably akin to E.
<ets>sun</ets>, meaning, the side towards the sun. <root/297. See
<er>Sun</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That one of the four cardinal
points directly opposite to the north; the region or direction to the
right or direction to the right of a person who faces the
east.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A country, region, or place situated farther to the
south than another; the southern section of a country.</def> <ldquo/The
queen of the <xex>south</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Matt. xii.
42.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Specifically: That part of the United States which
is south of Mason and Dixon's line. See under <er>Line</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The wind from the south.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>South</ent><br/
<hw>South</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lying toward the south; situated at
the south, or in a southern direction from the point of observation or
reckoning; proceeding toward the south, or coming from the south;
blowing from the south; southern; <as>as, the <ex>south</ex>
pole</as>.</def> <ldquo/At the <xex>south</xex> entry.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>South-Sea tea</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Yaupon</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>South</ent><br/
<hw>South</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Toward the south;
southward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>From the south; <as>as, the wind blows
<ex>south</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>South</ent><br/
<hw>South</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Southed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Southing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To turn or move
toward the south; to veer toward the south.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>To come to the meridian; to
cross the north and south line; -- said chiefly of the moon; <as>as, the
moon <ex>souths</ex> at nine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southcottian</ent><br/
<hw>South*cot"ti*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>A follower of Joanna <xex>Southcott</xex> (1750-1814),
an Englishwoman who, professing to have received a miraculous calling,
preached and prophesied, and committed many impious
absurdities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southdown</ent><br/
<hw>South"down`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the South Downs, a range of pasture hills south of the Thames, in
England.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Southdown sheep</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
celebrated breed of shortwooled, hornless sheep, highly valued on
account of the delicacy of their flesh. So called from the South Downs
where the breed originated.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southdown</ent><br/
<hw>South"down`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Southdown sheep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1376 --></p>

<p><ent>Southeast</ent><br/
<hw>South`east"</hw> <pr>(?; <xex>by sailors</xex> sou"-)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The point of the compass equally distant from the
south and the east; the southeast part or region.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southeast</ent><br/
<hw>South`east</hw> <pr>(?; <xex>by sailors</xex> sou"-)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the southeast; proceeding toward,
or coming from, the southeast; <as>as, a <ex>southeast</ex> course; a
<ex>southeast</ex> wind.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southeaster</ent><br/
<hw>South`east"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A storm, strong
wind, or gale coming from the southeast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southeaster</ent><br/
<hw>South`east"er</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the
southeast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southeastern</ent><br/
<hw>South`east"ern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the southeast; southeasterly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southeastwardly</ent><br/
<ent>Southeastward</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>South`east"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>South`east"ward*ly</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the
southeast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souther</ent><br/
<hw>South"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A strong wind, gale,
or storm from the south.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southerliness</ent><br/
<hw>South"er*li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being southerly; direction toward the south.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southerly</ent><br/
<hw>South"er*ly</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Southern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southern</ent><br/
<hw>South"ern</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>s<umac/<edh/ern</ets>. See <er>South</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the south; situated in, or proceeding from, the south;
situated or proceeding toward the south.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Southern Cross</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a
constellation of the southern hemisphere containing several bright stars
so related in position as to resemble a cross.</cd> -- <col><b>Southern
Fish</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a constelation of the southern
hemisphere (<spn>Piscis Australis</spn>) containing the bright star
Fomalhaut.</cd> -- <col><b>Southern States</b></col> <fld>(U.S. Hist. &
Geog.)</fld>, <cd>the States of the American Union lying south of
Pennsylvania and the Ohio River, with Arkansas, Louisiana, and Texas.
Before the Civil War, Missouri also, being a slave State, was classed as
one of the Southern States.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southern</ent><br/
<hw>South"ern</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Southerner.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southerner</ent><br/
<hw>South"ern*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An inhabitant or
native of the south, esp. of the Southern States of North America;
opposed to <contr>Northerner</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southernliness</ent><br/
<hw>South"ern*li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Southerliness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southernly</ent><br/
<hw>South"ern*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
southern.</def> -- <def2><pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a southerly manner or
course; southward.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southernmost</ent><br/
<hw>South"ern*most`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Farthest
south.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southernwood</ent><br/
<hw>South"ern*wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A shrubby species of wormwood (<spn>Artemisia Abrotanum</spn>)
having aromatic foliage. It is sometimes used in making beer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southing</ent><br/
<hw>South"ing</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Tendency or progress southward; <as>as, the <ex>southing</ex> of
the sun</as>.</def> <rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The time at which the moon, or other heavenly body,
passes the meridian of a place.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Distance of any heavenly body
south of the equator; south declination; south latitude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Surv. & Navigation)</fld> <def>Distance southward
from any point departure or of reckoning, measured on a meridian; --
opposed to <contr>northing</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southly</ent><br/
<hw>South"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Southerly.</def>
<mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southmost</ent><br/
<hw>South"most`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Farthest toward
the south; southernmost.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southness</ent><br/
<hw>South"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A tendency in the end of a
magnetic needle to point toward the south pole.</def>
<rj><au>Faraday.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southpaw</ent><br/
<hw>South"paw`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Baseball)</fld>
<def>Using the left hand in pitching; said of a pitcher.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southpaw</ent><br/
<hw>South"paw`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pitcher who pitches with the
left hand.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southren</ent><br/
<hw>South"ren</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Southern.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/I am a <xex>Southren</xex> man.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southron</ent><br/
<hw>South"ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An inhabitant of the
more southern part of a country; formerly, a name given in Scotland to
any Englishman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southsay</ent><br/
<hw>South"say`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Soothsay</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southsayer</ent><br/
<hw>South"say`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Soothsayer</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>South southerly</ent><br/
<hw>South` south"er*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>the
old squaw; -- so called in imitation of its cry. Called also
<altname>southerly</altname>, and <altname>southerland</altname>. See
under <er>Old</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southwards</ent><br/
<ent>Southward</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>South"ward</hw> <pr>(?; <it>colloq.</it> <?/)</pr>,
<hw>South"wards</hw> <pr>(?; <it>colloq.</it> <?/)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the south, or toward a point nearer the
south than the east or west point; <as>as, to go
<ex>southward</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southward</ent><br/
<hw>South"ward</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Toward the south.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southward</ent><br/
<hw>South"ward</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The southern regions or
countries; the south.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southwardly</ent><br/
<hw>South"ward*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a southern
direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southwest</ent><br/
<hw>South`west</hw> <pr>(?; <it>colloq.</it> sou"-.)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The point of the compass equally from the south and the west; the
southwest part or region.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southwest</ent><br/
<hw>South`west"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or in the
direction of, the southwest; proceeding toward the southwest; coming
from the southwest; <as>as, a <ex>southwest</ex> wind</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southwester</ent><br/
<hw>South`west"er</hw> <pr>(?; <xex>colloq</xex>. <?/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A storm, gale, or strong wind from the
southwest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A hat made of painted canvas, oiled cloth, or the
like, with a flap at the back, -- worn in stormy weather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southwesterly</ent><br/
<hw>South`west"er*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>To ward or from the
southwest; <as>as, a <ex>southwesterly</ex> course; a
<ex>southwesterly</ex> wind.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southwestern</ent><br/
<hw>South`west"ern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the southwest; southwesterly; <as>as, to sail a
<ex>southwestern</ex> course</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Southwestwardly</ent><br/
<ent>Southwestward</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>South`west"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>South`west"ward*ly</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the
southwest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sovenaunce</ent><br/
<ent>Souvenance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sou"ve*nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>So"ve*naunce</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>souvenance</ets>.]</ety> <def>Remembrance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of his way he had no <qex>sovenance</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Souvenir</ent><br/
<hw>Sou`ve*nir</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>souvenir</ets> to remember, fr. L. <ets>subvenire</ets> to come up,
come to mind; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>venire</ets> to come, akin to
E. <ets>come</ets>. <ets>See</ets> <er>Come</er>, and cf.
<er>Subvention</er>.]</ety> <def>That which serves as a reminder; a
remembrancer; a memento; a keepsake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sovereign</ent><br/
<hw>Sov"er*eign</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>soverain</ets>, <ets>sovereyn</ets>, OF. <ets>soverain</ets>,
<ets>suvrain</ets>, F. <ets>souverain</ets>, LL. <ets>superanus</ets>,
fr. L. <ets>superus</ets> that is above, upper, higher, fr.
<ets>super</ets> above. See <er>Over</er>, <er>Super</er>, and cf.
<er>Soprano</er>. The modern spelling is due to a supposed connection
with <ets>reign</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Supreme or highest in
power; superior to all others; chief; <as>as, our <ex>sovereign</ex>
prince</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Independent of, and unlimited by, any other;
possessing, or entitled to, original authority or jurisdiction; <as>as,
a <ex>sovereign</ex> state; a <ex>sovereign</ex>
discretion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Princely; royal.</def> <ldquo/Most
<xex>sovereign</xex> name.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At Babylon was his <qex>sovereign</qex> see.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Predominant; greatest; utmost; paramount.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We acknowledge him [God] our <qex>sovereign</qex> good.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Efficacious in the highest degree; effectual;
controlling; <as>as, a <ex>sovereign</ex> remedy</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such a <qex>sovereign</qex> influence has this passion upon the
regulation of the lives and actions of men.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sovereign state</b></col>, <cd>a state which administers
its own government, and is not dependent upon, or subject to, another
power.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sovereign</ent><br/
<hw>Sov"er*eign</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The person, body, or state in which independent and supreme
authority is vested; especially, in a monarchy, a king, queen, or
emperor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No question is to be made but that the bed of the Mississippi
belongs to the <qex>sovereign</qex>, that is, to the nation.</q>
<rj><qau>Jefferson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A gold coin of Great Britain, on which an effigy of
the head of the reigning king or queen is stamped, valued at one pound
sterling, or about $4.86.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any butterfly of the tribe
<fam>Nymphalidi</fam>, or genus <gen>Basilarchia</gen>, as the ursula
and the viceroy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- King; prince; monarch; potentate;
emperor.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sovereignize</ent><br/
<hw>Sov"er*eign*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To exercise
supreme authority.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sovereignly</ent><br/
<hw>Sov"er*eign*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sovereign manner; in
the highest degree; supremely.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sovereignty</ent><br/
<hw>Sov"er*eign*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sovereignties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE.
<ets>soverainetee</ets>, OF. <ets>sovrainet<eacute/</ets>, F.
<ets>souverainet<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being sovereign, or of being a sovereign; the exercise of, or right to
exercise, supreme power; dominion; sway; supremacy; independence; also,
that which is sovereign; a sovereign state; <as>as, Italy was formerly
divided into many <ex>sovereignties</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Woman desiren to have <qex>sovereignty</qex><br/
As well over their husband as over their love.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sovran</ent><br/
<hw>Sov"ran</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>A variant of
<er>Sovereign</er>.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On thy bald, awful head, O <qex>sovran</qex> Blanc.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sow</ent><br/
<hw>Sow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To sew. See
<er>Sew</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sow</ent><br/
<hw>Sow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sowe</ets>,
<ets>suwe</ets>, AS. <ets>sugu</ets>, akin to <ets>s<umac/</ets>, D.
<ets>zog</ets>, <ets>zeug</ets>, OHG. <ets>s<umac/</ets>, G.
<ets>sau</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<ymac/r</ets>, Dan. <ets>so</ets>, Sw.
<ets>sugga</ets>, <ets>so</ets>, L. <ets>sus</ets>. Gr. <grk>"y^s</grk>,
<grk>sy^s</grk>, Zend. hu boar; probably from the root seen in Skr.
<ets>s<umac/</ets> to beget, to bear; the animal being named in allusion
to its fecundity. <root/294. Cf. <er>Hyena</er>, <er>Soil</er> to stain,
<er>Son</er>, <er>Swine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The female of swine, or of the hog kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A sow bug.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A channel or runner
which receives the rows of molds in the pig bed.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The bar of metal which remains in such a runner.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>A mass of solidified metal in a furnace hearth; a
salamander.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A kind of covered shed, formerly
used by besiegers in filling up and passing the ditch of a besieged
place, sapping and mining the wall, or the like.</def>
<rj><au>Craig.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sow bread</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Cyclamen</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sow bug</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sowbug</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
numerous species of terrestrial <ord>Isopoda</ord> belonging to
<gen>Oniscus</gen>, <gen>Porcellio</gen>, and allied genera of the
family <fam>Oniscidae</fam>. They feed chiefly on decaying vegetable
substances.</cd> -- <col><b>Sow thistle</b></col> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sugepistel</ets>]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a composite plant
(<spn>Sonchus oleraceus</spn>) said to be eaten by swine and some other
animals.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sow</ent><br/
<hw>Sow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Sowed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sown</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Sowed</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sowing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sowen</ets>,
<ets>sawen</ets>, AS. <ets>s<amac/wan</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>s<?/a</ets>, D. <ets>zaaijen</ets>, OS. & HG.
<ets>s<amac/jan</ets>, G. <ets>s<aum/en</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<amac/</ets>,
Sw. <ets>s<aring/</ets>, Dan. <ets>saae</ets>, Goth. <ets>saian</ets>,
Lith. <ets>s<emac/ti</ets>, Russ. <ets>sieiate</ets>, L.
<ets>serere</ets>, <ets>sevi</ets>. Cf. <er>Saturday</er>,
<er>Season</er>, <er>Seed</er>, <er>Seminary</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To scatter, as seed, upon the earth; to plant by strewing; <as>as,
to <ex>sow</ex> wheat</as>. Also used figuratively: To spread abroad; to
propagate.</def> <ldquo/He would <xex>sow some difficulty</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A sower went forth to <qex>sow</qex>; and when he
<qex>sowed</qex>, some seeds fell by the wayside.</q> <rj><qau>Matt.
xiii. 3, 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>sow</qex> dissension in the hearts of brothers.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To scatter seed upon, in, or over; to supply or
stock, as land, with seeds. Also used figuratively: To scatter over; to
besprinkle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The intellectual faculty is a goodly field, . . . and it is the
worst husbandry in the world to <qex>sow</qex> it with trifles.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir M. Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He] <qex>sowed</qex> with stars the heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now morn . . . <qex>sowed</qex> the earth with orient pearl.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sow</ent><br/
<hw>Sow</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To scatter seed for growth and the
production of a crop; -- literally or figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They that <qex>sow</qex> in tears shall reap in joi.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. cxxvi. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowans</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"ans</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sowens</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowar</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per.
<ets>saw<amac/r</ets> a horseman.]</ety> <def>In India, a mounted
soldier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowbane</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"bane`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The red goosefoot (<spn>Chenopodium rubrum</spn>), -- said to be
fatal to swine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowce</ent><br/
<hw>Sowce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Souse</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowdan</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"dan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>soudan</ets>.
See <er>Soldan</er>.]</ety> <def>Sultan.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowdanesse</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"dan*esse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
sultaness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowens</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"ens</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Scottish;
cf. AS. <ets>se<aacute/w</ets> juice, glue, paste.]</ety> <def>A
nutritious article of food, much used in Scotland, made from the husk of
the oat by a process not unlike that by which common starch is made; --
called <altname>flummery</altname> in England.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>sowans</asp>, and <asp>sowins</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sower</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that which,
sows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowins</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"ins</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sowens</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowle</ent><br/
<ent>Sowl</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sowl</hw>, <hw>Sowle</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v.
t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. prov. G. <ets>zaulen</ets>, <ets>zauseln</ets>, G.
<ets>zausen</ets> to tug, drag.]</ety> <def>To pull by the ears; to drag
about.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowl</ent><br/
<hw>Sowl</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See <er>Soul</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sown</ent><br/
<hw>Sown</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sow</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowne</ent><br/
<hw>Sowne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To sound.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowse</ent><br/
<hw>Sowse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Souse</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>ryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sowter</ent><br/
<hw>Sow"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Souter</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soya</ent><br/
<ent>Soy</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Soy</hw> <pr>(soi)</pr>, <hw>Soy"a</hw></mhw>
<pr>(soi"<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Chinese
<ets>sh<omac/y<umac/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A Chinese and
Japanese liquid sauce for fish, etc., made by subjecting boiled beans
(esp. soybeans), or beans and meal, to long fermentation and then long
digestion in salt and water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The <er>soybean</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soybean</ent><br/
<hw>Soy"bean</hw> <pr>(soi"b<emac/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An Asiatic leguminous herb (<spn>Glycine
max</spn>, formerly <spn>Glycine Soja</spn>) the seeds of which (also
called <prod>soy beans</prod>) are used in preparing the sauce called
<prod>soy</prod>. Called also <altname>soya bean</altname> and
<altname>soya</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>the seeds of the <spn>Glycine max</spn>, which
produce <er>soybean oil</er>; -- called also <altname>soya
bean</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soybean oil</ent><br/
<hw>Soy"bean oil</hw> <pr>(soi"b<emac/n` oil)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>an oil obtained from the soybean (<spn>Glycine max</spn>), rich in
protein, fats, sterols, and phospholipids, used as a food and in paints
and varnishes and in various industrial applications; -- called also
<altname>soya oil</altname>. It is also used in preparing the sauce
called <prod>soy</prod> or <prod>soy sauce</prod>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soyle</ent><br/
<hw>Soyle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Aphetic form of
<ets>assoil</ets>.]</ety> <def>To solve, to clear up; <as>as, to
<ex>soyl</ex> all other texts</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Tyndate.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soyle</ent><br/
<hw>Soyle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Soil</er> to feed.]</ety>
<def>Prey.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Soyned</ent><br/
<hw>Soyn"ed</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>soigner</ets> to care.]</ety> <def>Filled with care; anxious.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mir. for Mag.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sozzle</ent><br/
<hw>Soz"zle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Freq. from
<ets>soss</ets>, v.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To splash or wet carelessly;
<as>as, to <ex>sozzle</ex> the feet in water</as>.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To heap up in confusion.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Forby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sozzle</ent><br/
<hw>Soz"zle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who spills water or
other liquids carelessly; specifically, a sluttish woman.</def>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mass, or heap, confusedly mingled.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spa</ent><br/
<hw>Spa</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A spring or mineral
water; -- so called from a place of this name in Belgium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaad</ent><br/
<hw>Spaad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>spath</ets>
spar. See <er>Spar</er> the mineral.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
kind of spar; earth flax, or amianthus.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>oodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space</ent><br/
<hw>Space</hw> <pr>(sp<amac/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>space</ets>, F. <ets>espace</ets>, from L. <ets>spatium</ets>
space; cf. Gr. <grk>spa^n</grk> to draw, to tear; perh. akin to E.
<ets>span</ets>. Cf. <er>Expatiate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Extension, considered independently of anything which it may
contain; that which makes extended objects conceivable and
possible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pure <qex>space</qex> is capable neither of resistance nor
motion.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Place, having more or less extension;
room.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They gave him chase, and hunted him as hare;<br/
Long had he no <qex>space</qex> to dwell [in].</q> <rj><qau>R. of
Brunne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While I have time and <qex>space</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A quantity or portion of extension; distance from
one thing to another; an interval between any two or more objects;
<as>as, the <ex>space</ex> between two stars or two hills; the sound was
heard for the <ex>space</ex> of a mile</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Put a <qex>space</qex> betwixt drove and drove.</q> <rj><qau>Gen.
xxxii. 16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Quantity of time; an interval between two points of
time; duration; time.</def> <ldquo/Grace God gave him here, this land to
keep long <xex>space</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>R. of brunne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nine times the <qex>space</qex> that measures day and night.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God may defer his judgments for a time, and give a people a longer
<qex>space</qex> of repentance.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A short time; a while.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/To stay your deadly strife a <xex>space</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Walk; track; path; course.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This ilke [same] monk let old things pace,<br/
And held after the new world the <qex>space</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A small piece of
metal cast lower than a face type, so as not to receive the ink in
printing, -- used to separate words or letters.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The distance or interval between words or letters in the lines, or
between lines, as in books, on a computer screen, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Spaces are of different thicknesses to enable the
compositor to arrange the words at equal distances from each other in
the same line.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>One of the intervals, or open
places, between the lines of the staff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>that portion of the universe outside the earth or
its atmosphere; -- called also <altname>outer space</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Absolute space</b></col>, <col><b>Euclidian
space</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Absolute</er>,
<er>Euclidian</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>deep space</b></col>, <cd>the
part of outer space which is beyond the limits of the solar system.</cd>
-- <col><b>Space line</b></col> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>a thin piece of
metal used by printers to open the lines of type to a regular distance
from each other, and for other purposes; a lead.</cd> <au>Hansard.</au>
-- <col><b>Space rule</b></col> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>a fine, thin,
short metal rule of the same height as the type, used in printing short
lines in tabular matter.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space</ent><br/
<hw>Space</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>espacier</ets>, L.
<ets>spatiari</ets>. See <er>Space</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To
walk; to rove; to roam.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And loved in forests wild to <qex>space</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space</ent><br/
<hw>Space</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spaced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spacong</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>espacer</ets>. See <er>Space</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>To arrange or adjust the spaces in or between;
<as>as, to <ex>space</ex> words, lines, or letters</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space age</ent><br/
<hw>Space age</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>the time during which humans
engaged in space exploration; -- generally considered as beginning on
October 4, 1957 when the Soviet Union placed the first artificial
satellite into orbit around the earth.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>space key</ent><br/
<ent>space bar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>space bar</hw> <it>or</it> <hw>space key</hw> }</mhw>.
<fld>(Mach., Computers)</fld> <def>A bar or key, in a typewriter,
typesetting machine, or other keyboard (as a computer keyboard), used to
insert a space between letters.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space cadet</ent><br/
<hw>Space cadet</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a person who appears
disconnected from reality, or living in his own world; -- sometimes used
of people who are under the influence of mind-altering drugs.</def>
<mark>[derogatory]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spacecraft</ent><br/
<hw>space"craft</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a vehicle capable of travelling
in or into outer space; at present, all such vehicles are powered by
rocket engine.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space flight</ent><br/
<hw>Space flight</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>the process or event in which
vehicles travel into outer space.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaceful</ent><br/
<hw>Space"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wide;
extensive.</def> <rj><au>Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>space heater</ent><br/
<hw>space" heat`er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a portable heating device
used for warming the air of a single room; -- it may be electrical or
use a combustible fuel.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaceless</ent><br/
<hw>Space"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without space.</def>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spaceman</ent><br/
<hw>space"man`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>an
<er>astronaut</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>an intelligent creature from outer space; an
extraterrestrial; -- a fictional or hypothetical being, never actually
observed.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spaceport</ent><br/
<hw>space"port</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[by analogy with
<ets>airport</ets>.]</ety> <def>a facility where vehicles are launched
into space.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>space probe</ent><br/
<hw>space" probe</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>an unmanned rocket carrying
instruments to explore objects or conditions in outer space; -- used
mostly for vehicles travelling beyond the earth's gravitational field,
rather than in orbital or suborbital flight.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>space shuttle</ent><br/
<hw>space" shut`tle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a reusable vehicle that can
carry people or objects into orbit around the earth and return to be
used again for subsequent trips; -- distinguished from rockets that are
used only once. The present (1997) American space shuttle is a manned
vehicle, having an external fuel tank that is expendable and not
re-used.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>space sickness</ent><br/
<hw>space" sick`ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a group of symptoms,
prominently nausea, but sometimes including lethargy, headache, and
sweating, occuring under the weightless conditions of space
flight.</def> <au>[RHUD]</au><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>space station</ent><br/
<hw>space" sta`tion</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a manned artificial
satellite orbiting the earth designed for extended occupation and use by
multiple crews in succession.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space suit</ent><br/
<hw>Space suit</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a protective suit covering the
whole body, completely airtight and designed to withstand internal
pressure, worn by astronauts to protect them from the vacuum and other
hazards of outer space. It typically contains an air supply and other
life-support equipment to allow functioning independent of any other
equipment for a period of time while in space.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>space-time</ent><br/
<hw>space-time</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>the four-dimensional coordinate
system in which all physical objects of the known universe are located,
and in which all physical events occur; it consists of three spatial
dimensions and one time dimension; -- also called the
<altname>space-time continuum</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space travel</ent><br/
<hw>Space travel</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>the process of travelling in
or into outer space.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Space walk</ent><br/
<hw>Space walk</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>any activity by a human outside
of the protective environment of a spacecraft while it is in outer
space, requiring a space suit to protect the person from the vacuum and
other hazards of space.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spacial</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spatial</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spacially</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"cial*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>See <er>Spatially</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spacious</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spatiousus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>spacieux</ets>. See <er>Space</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Extending far and wide; vast in
extent.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>spacious</xex> plain outstretched in circuit
wide.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inclosing an extended space; having large or ample
room; not contracted or narrow; capacious; roomy; <as>as,
<ex>spacious</ex> bounds; a <ex>spacious</ex> church; a
<ex>spacious</ex> hall.</as></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Spa"cious*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Spa"cious*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spad</ent><br/
<hw>Spad</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld>
<def>A nail one or two inches long, of iron, brass, tin, or tinner iron,
with a hole through the flattened head, used to mark stations in
underground surveying.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadassin</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spa`das`sin"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. It.
<ets>spadaccino</ets> a swordsman, from <ets>spada</ets> a sword.]</ety>
<def>A bravo; a bully; a duelist.</def> <rj><au>Ld.
Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaddle</ent><br/
<hw>Spad"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little spade.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spade</ent><br/
<hw>Spade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Spay</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A hart or stag
three years old.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>spaid</asp>,
<asp>spayade</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Cf. L. <ets>spado</ets>.]</ety> <def>A castrated
man or beast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spade</ent><br/
<hw>Spade</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>spaed</ets>;
<ets>spada</ets>; akin to D. <ets>spade</ets>, G. <ets>spaten</ets>,
Icel. <ets>spa<edh/i</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>spade</ets>, L.
<ets>spatha</ets> a spatula, a broad two-edged sword, a spathe, Gr.
<grk>spa`qh</grk>. Cf. <er>Epaulet</er>, <er>Spade</er> at cards,
<er>Spathe</er>, <er>Spatula</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An implement
for digging or cutting the ground, consisting usually of an oblong and
nearly rectangular blade of iron, with a handle like that of a
shovel.</def> <ldquo/With <xex>spade</xex> and pickax armed.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Sp. <ets>espada</ets>, literally, a sword; -- so
caused because these cards among the Spanish bear the figure of a sword.
Sp. <ets>espada</ets> is fr. L. <ets>spatha</ets>, Gr.
<grk>spa`qh</grk>. See the Etymology above.]</ety> <def>One of that suit
of cards each of which bears one or more figures resembling a
spade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/Let <qex>spades</qex> be trumps!<rdquo/ she said.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A cutting instrument used in flensing a
whale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spade bayonet</b></col>, <cd>a bayonet with a broad blade
which may be used digging; -- called also <altname>trowel
bayonet</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spade handle</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>the forked end of a connecting rod in which a
pin is held at both ends. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Knuckle
joint</cref>, under <er>Knuckle</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1377 --></p>

<p><ent>Spade</ent><br/
<hw>Spade</hw> <pr>(sp<amac/d)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spaded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To dig with a spade; to pare off
the sward of, as land, with a spade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadebone</ent><br/
<hw>Spade"bone`</hw> <pr>(sp<amac/d"b<omac/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Shoulder blade.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadefish</ent><br/
<hw>Spade"fish`</hw> <pr>(sp<amac/d"f<icr/sh`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An American market fish (<spn>Chaetodipterus
faber</spn>) common on the southern coasts; -- called also
<altname>angel fish</altname>, <altname>moonfish</altname>, and
<altname>porgy</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadefoot</ent><br/
<hw>Spade"foot`</hw> <pr>(sp<amac/d"f<oocr/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of burrowing toads of the genus
<gen>Scaphiopus</gen>, esp. <spn>Scaphiopus Holbrookii</spn>, of the
Eastern United States; -- called also <altname>spade
toad</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadeful</ent><br/
<hw>Spade"ful</hw> <pr>(sp<amac/d"f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spadefuls</plw>
<pr>(sp<amac/d"f<usdot/lz)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[<ets>Spade</ets> +
<ets>full</ets>.]</ety> <def>As much as a spade will hold or
lift.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spader</ent><br/
<hw>Spad"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, spades; specifically, a digging machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadiceous</ent><br/
<hw>Spa*di"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spadix</ets>, <ets>-icis</ets>, a date-brown or nut-brown color.
See <er>Spadix</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of a bright clear brown or
chestnut color.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Bearing flowers on a spadix; of
the nature of a spadix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadicose</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"di*cose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Spadiceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadille</ent><br/
<hw>Spa*dille"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. Sp.
<ets>espadilla</ets>, dim. of <ets>espada</ets>. See <er>Spade</er> a
card.]</ety> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>The ace of spades in omber
and quadrille.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadix</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"dix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Spadices</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Spadixes</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a palm branch broken off, with its fruit,
Gr. <?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A fleshy spike of
flowers, usually inclosed in a leaf called a
<xex>spathe</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A special organ of the nautilus,
due to a modification of the posterior tentacles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spado</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spa"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spadones</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Spade</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An impotent person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spadroon</ent><br/
<hw>Spa*droon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. & Sp.
<ets>espadon</ets>, It. <ets>spadone</ets>. See <er>Espadon</er>,
<er>Spade</er>.]</ety> <def>A sword, especially a broadsword, formerly
used both to cut and thrust.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spae</ent><br/
<hw>Spae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spaed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spaeing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Scot. <ets>spae</ets>,
<ets>spay</ets>, to foretell, to divine, Icel.
<ets>sp<amac/</ets>.]</ety> <def>To foretell; to divine.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaeman</ent><br/
<hw>Spae"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A prophet; a
diviner.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaewife</ent><br/
<hw>Spae"wife`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female fortune
teller.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaghetti</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spa*ghet"ti</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<def>A variety or macaroni made in tubes of small diameter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spagyrical</ent><br/
<ent>Spagyric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spa*gyr"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spa*gyr"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>sparygicus</ets>, fr.
Gr. <?/ to draw, to separate + <?/ to assemble; cf. F.
<ets>spagirique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Chemical; alchemical.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spagyric</ent><br/
<hw>Spa*gyr"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A spagyrist.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spagyrist</ent><br/
<hw>Spag"y*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>spagiriste</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A chemist, esp. one
devoted to alchemistic pursuits.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of a sect which arose in the days of alchemy,
who sought to discover remedies for disease by chemical means. The
spagyrists historically preceded the iatrochemists.</def> <rj><au>Encyc.
Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spahee</ent><br/
<ent>Spahi</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Spa"hi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Spa"hee</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per., Turk., & Hind. <ets>sip<amac/h<imac/</ets>:
cf. F. <ets>spahi</ets>. See <er>Seroy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Formerly, one of the Turkish cavalry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An Algerian cavalryman in the French army.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaid</ent><br/
<hw>Spaid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See 1st
<er>Spade</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spake</ent><br/
<hw>Spake</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>archaic</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos>
of <er>Speak</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spakenet</ent><br/
<hw>Spake"net`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A net for catching crabs.</def>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaky</ent><br/
<hw>Spak"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Specky.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>hapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spalding knife</ent><br/
<hw>Spald"ing knife`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A spalting knife.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spale</ent><br/
<hw>Spale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Spell</er> a
splinter.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A lath; a shaving or chip, as of wood
or stone.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>A strengthening cross
timber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spall</ent><br/
<hw>Spall</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>espaule</ets>;
cf. It. <ets>spalla</ets>. See <er>Epaule</er>.]</ety> <def>The
shoulder.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spall</ent><br/
<hw>Spall</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E. <ets>spall</ets>,
<ets>spell</ets>. See <er>Spale</er>, <er>Spell</er> a splinter.]</ety>
<def>A chip or fragment, especially a chip of stone as struck off the
block by the hammer, having at least one feather-edge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spall</ent><br/
<hw>Spall</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>To
break into small pieces, as ore, for the purpose of separating from
rock.</def> <rj><au>Pryce.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>To reduce, as irregular blocks
of stone, to an approximately level surface by hammering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spall</ent><br/
<hw>Spall</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To give off spalls, or
wedge-shaped chips; -- said of stone, as when badly set, with the weight
thrown too much on the outer surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spalpeen</ent><br/
<hw>Spal"peen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ir.
<ets>spailpin</ets>, fr. <ets>spailp</ets> a beau, pride,
self-conceit.]</ety> <def>A scamp; an Irish term for a good-for-nothing
fellow; -- often used in good-humored contempt or ridicule.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spalt</ent><br/
<hw>Spalt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>spalt</ets>stein, from <ets>spalten</ets> to split. See 1st
<er>Spell</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>Spelter.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spalt</ent><br/
<hw>Spalt</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See 1st <er>Spell</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Liable to break or split; brittle; <as>as,
<ex>spalt</ex> timber</as>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Heedless; clumsy; pert; saucy.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spalt</ent><br/
<hw>Spalt</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OE. <ets>spalden</ets>.
See <er>Spalt</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To split off; to cleave
off, as chips from a piece of timber, with an ax.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng. & Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spalting knife</ent><br/
<hw>Spalt"ing knife`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A knife used in splitting
codfish.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>spalding
knife</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Span</ent><br/
<hw>Span</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>archaic</mark> <def><pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> of <er>Spin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Span</ent><br/
<hw>Span</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>spann</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>span</ets>, OHG. <ets>spanna</ets>, G. <ets>spanne</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sp<oum/nn</ets>. <root/170. See <er>Span</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The space from the thumb to the end of the
little finger when extended; nine inches; eighth of a fathom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a small space or a brief portion of
time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet not to earth's contracted <qex>span</qex><br/
Thy goodness let me bound.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Life's but a <qex>span</qex>; I'll every inch enjoy.</q>
<rj><qau>Farquhar.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The spread or extent of an arch between its
abutments, or of a beam, girder, truss, roof, bridge, or the like,
between its supports.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A rope having its ends made fast
so that a purchase can be hooked to the bight; also, a rope made fast in
the center so that both ends can be used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <ety>[Cf. D. <ets>span</ets>, Sw. <ets>spann</ets>, Dan.
<ets>spaend</ets>, G. <ets>gespann</ets>. See <er>Span</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos> ]</ety> <def>A pair of horses or other animals driven together;
usually, such a pair of horses when similar in color, form, and
action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Span blocks</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>blocks at
the topmast and topgallant-mast heads, for the studding-sail
halyards.</cd> -- <col><b>Span counter</b></col>, <cd>an old English
child's game, in which one throws a counter on the ground, and another
tries to hit it with his counter, or to get his counter so near it that
he can span the space between them, and touch both the counters.</cd>
<au>Halliwell.</au> <ldquo/Henry V., in whose time boys went to
<xex>span counter</xex> for French crowns.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> --
<col><b>Span iron</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a special kind of
harpoon, usually secured just below the gunwale of a whaleboat.</cd> --
<col><b>Span roof</b></col>, <cd>a common roof, having two slopes and
one ridge, with eaves on both sides.</cd> <au>Gwilt.</au> --
<col><b>Span shackle</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a large bolt
driven through the forecastle deck, with a triangular shackle in the
head to receive the heel of the old-fashioned fish davit.</cd> <au>Ham.
Nav. Encyc.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Span</ent><br/
<hw>Span</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spanned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spanning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>pannan</ets>; akin to
D. & G. <ets>spannen</ets>, OHG. <ets>spannan</ets>, Sw.
<ets>sp<aum/nna</ets>, Dan. <ets>spaende</ets>, Icel. <ets>spenna</ets>,
and perh. to Gr. <?/ to draw, to drag, L. <ets>spatium</ets> space.
<root/170. Cf. <er>Spin</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, <er>Space</er>,
<er>Spasm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To measure by the span of the
hand with the fingers extended, or with the fingers encompassing the
object; <as>as, to <ex>span</ex> a space or distance; to <ex>span</ex> a
cylinder.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My right hand hath <qex>spanned</qex> the heavens.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xiviii. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To reach from one side of to the order; to stretch
over as an arch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The rivers were <qex>spanned</qex> by arches of solid masonry.</q>
<rj><qau>prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fetter, as a horse; to hobble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Span</ent><br/
<hw>Span</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be matched, as horses.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spanaemia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spa*nae"mi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ scarce + <?/ blood.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A condition of
impoverishment of the blood; a morbid state in which the red corpuscles,
or other important elements of the blood, are deficient.</def> <--
[Obs.] this and next word not in Stedman's. Presumably succeded by
anaemia, which is in this dictionary. Why no cross-ref? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spanaemic</ent><br/
<hw>Spa*nae"mic</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to spanaemia; having
impoverished blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spancel</ent><br/
<hw>Span"cel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Perhaps
<ets>span</ets> + AS. <ets>s<amac/l</ets> a rope.]</ety> <def>A rope
used for tying or hobbling the legs of a horse or cow.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Local, U.S.]</mark> <-- a different usage is in
White's "Once and future king" --> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spancel</ent><br/
<hw>Span"cel</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spanceled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Spancelled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Spanceling</conjf> <or/
<conjf>Spancelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To tie or hobble with a
spancel.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Local, U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Malone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spandex</ent><br/
<hw>Span"dex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos> <def>An elastic
textile material, used for clothing</def> <mark>[Trade name]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spandogs</ent><br/
<hw>Span"dogs`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>A pair of
grappling dogs for hoisting logs and timber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spandrel</ent><br/
<hw>Span"drel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Span</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The irregular
triangular space between the curve of an arch and the inclosing right
angle; or the space between the outer moldings of two contiguous arches
and a horizontal line above them, or another arch above and inclosing
them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A narrow mat or passe partout for a picture.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spane</ent><br/
<hw>Spane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Akin to G.
<ets>sp<aum/nen</ets>, LG. & D. <ets>spennen</ets>, AS. <ets>spanu</ets>
a teat.]</ety> <def>To wean.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spang</ent><br/
<hw>Spang</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To spangle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spang</ent><br/
<hw>Spang</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To spring; to bound; to
leap.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But when they <qex>spang</qex> o'er reason's fence,<br/
We smart for't at our own expense.</q> <rj><qau>Ramsay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spang</ent><br/
<hw>Spang</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bound or spring.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spang</ent><br/
<hw>Spang</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>spange</ets> a clasp or
fastening; akin to D. <ets>spang</ets>, G. <ets>spange</ets>, OHG.
<ets>spanga</ets>, Icel. <ets>sp<oum/ng</ets> a spangle.]</ety> <def>A
spangle or shining ornament.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With glittering <qex>spangs</qex> that did like stars appear.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spangle</ent><br/
<hw>Span"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>spangel</ets>, dim. of AS. <ets>spange</ets>. See <er>Spang</er> a
spangle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small plate or boss of shining metal;
something brilliant used as an ornament, especially when stitched on the
dress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Figuratively, any little thing that sparkless.</def>
<ldquo/The rich <xex>spangles</xex> that adorn the sky.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Waller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Oak spangle</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Oak</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spangle</ent><br/
<hw>Span"gle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spangled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spangling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To set or sprinkle
with, or as with, spangles; to adorn with small, distinct, brilliant
bodies; <as>as, a <ex>spangled</ex> breastplate</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What stars do <qex>spangle</qex> heaven with such beauty?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spangled coquette</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
tropical humming bird (<spn>Lophornis reginae</spn>). See
<er>Coquette</er>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spangle</ent><br/
<hw>Span"gle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To show brilliant spots or
points; to glisten; to glitter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some men by feigning words as dark as mine<br/
Make truth to <qex>spangle</qex>, and its rays to shine.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spangler</ent><br/
<hw>Span"gler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, spangles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spangly</ent><br/
<hw>Span"gly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling, or
consisting of, spangles; glittering; <as>as, <ex>spangly</ex>
light</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaniard</ent><br/
<hw>Span"iard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A native or
inhabitant of Spain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaniel</ent><br/
<hw>Span"iel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espagneul</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/pagneul</ets>, <ets>espagnol</ets>
Spanish, Sp. <ets>espa<ntil/nol</ets>, fr. <ets>Espa<ntil/a</ets> Spain,
from L. <ets>Hispania</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of a breed of small dogs having long and thick hair and large
drooping ears. The legs are usually strongly feathered, and the tail
bushy. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Clumber</er>, and
<er>Cocker</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ There are several varieties of spaniels, some of which,
known as <xex>field spaniels</xex>, are used in hunting; others are used
for toy or pet dogs, as the Blenheim spaniel, and the King Charles
spaniel (see under <er>Blenheim</er>). Of the field spaniels, the larger
kinds are called <xex>springers</xex>, and to these belong the Sussex,
Norfolk, and Clumber spaniels (see <er>Clumber</er>). The smaller field
spaniels, used in hunting woodcock, are called <xex>cocker
spaniels</xex> (see Cocker). Field spaniels are remarkable for their
activity and intelligence.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As a <qex>spaniel</qex> she will on him leap.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A cringing, fawning person.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaniel</ent><br/
<hw>Span"iel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cringing;
fawning.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaniel</ent><br/
<hw>Span"iel</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fawn; to cringe; to be
obsequious.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Churchill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaniel</ent><br/
<hw>Span"iel</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To follow like a spaniel.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spanish</ent><br/
<hw>Span"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Spain or the Spaniards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spanish bayonet</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
liliaceous plant (<spn>Yucca alorifolia</spn>) with rigid spine-tipped
leaves. The name is also applied to other similar plants of the
Southwestern United States and mexico. Called also <altname>Spanish
daggers</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish bean</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See the Note under <er>Bean</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish black</b></col>, <cd>a black pigment obtained by
charring cork.</cd> <au>Ure.</au> -- <col><b>Spanish broom</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a leguminous shrub (<spn>Spartium junceum</spn>)
having many green flexible rushlike twigs.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish
brown</b></col>, <cd>a species of earth used in painting, having a dark
reddish brown color, due to the presence of sesquioxide of iron.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish buckeye</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small tree
(<spn>Ungnadia speciosa</spn>) of Texas, New Mexico, etc., related to
the buckeye, but having pinnate leaves and a three-seeded fruit.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish burton</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a purchase
composed of two single blocks. A <col><b>double Spanish burton</b></col>
has one double and two single blocks.</cd> <au>Luce (Textbook of
Seamanship).</au> -- <col><b>Spanish chalk</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>,
<cd>a kind of steatite; -- so called because obtained from Aragon in
Spain.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish cress</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
cruciferous plant (<spn>Lepidium Cadamines</spn>), a species of
peppergrass.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish curlew</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the long-billed curlew.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark>
-- <col><b>Spanish daggers</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Spanish bayonet</cref>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish elm</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a large West Indian tree (<spn>Cordia
Gerascanthus</spn>) furnishing hard and useful timber.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish feretto</b></col>, <cd>a rich reddish brown pigment
obtained by calcining copper and sulphur together in closed
crucibles.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish flag</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the California rockfish (<spn>Sebastichthys rubrivinctus</spn>). It
is conspicuously colored with bands of red and white.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a brilliant green
beetle, common in the south of Europe, used for raising blisters. See
<cref>Blister beetle</cref> under <er>Blister</er>, and
<er>Cantharis</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish fox</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a yarn twisted against its lay.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish grass</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Esparto</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish juice</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>licorice.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish
leather</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Cordwain</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish
mackerel</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A species of
mackerel (<spn>Scomber colias</spn>) found both in Europe and America.
In America called <altname>chub mackerel</altname>, <altname>big-eyed
mackerel</altname>, and <altname>bull mackerel</altname>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>In the United States, a handsome mackerel having bright
yellow round spots (<spn>Scomberomorus maculatus</spn>), highly esteemed
as a food fish. The name is sometimes erroneously applied to other
species. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under Mackerel.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish
main</b></col>, <cd>the name formerly given to the southern portion of
the Caribbean Sea, together with the contiguous coast, embracing the
route traversed by Spanish treasure ships from the New to the Old
World.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish moss</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Tillandsia</er> (and note at that entry).</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish
needles</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a composite weed (<spn>Bidens
bipinnata</spn>) having achenia armed with needlelike awns.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish nut</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a bulbous plant
(<spn>Iris Sisyrinchium</spn>) of the south of Europe.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish potato</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the sweet
potato. See under <er>Potato</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish
red</b></col>, <cd>an ocherous red pigment resembling Venetian red, but
slightly yellower and warmer.</cd> <au>Fairholt.</au> -- <col><b>Spanish
reef</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a knot tied in the head of a
jib-headed sail.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish sheep</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a merino.</cd> -- <col><b>Spanish
white</b></col>, <cd>an impalpable powder prepared from chalk by
pulverizing and repeated washings, -- used as a white pigment.</cd> --
<col><b>Spanish windlass</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a wooden
roller, with a rope wound about it, into which a marline spike is thrust
to serve as a lever.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spanish</ent><br/
<hw>Span"ish</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The language of Spain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spank</ent><br/
<hw>Spank</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spanked</conjf>
<pr>(sp<acr/<nsm/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spanking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of unknown origin; cf. LG.
<ets>spakken</ets>, <ets>spenkern</ets>, to run and spring about
quickly.]</ety> <def>To strike, as the breech, with the open hand; to
slap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spank</ent><br/
<hw>Spank</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A blow with the open hand; a
slap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spank</ent><br/
<hw>Spank</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To move with a quick, lively step
between a trot and gallop; to move quickly.</def>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spanker</ent><br/
<hw>Spank"er</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/<nsm/k"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who spanks, or anything used as an instrument for
spanking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The after sail of a ship or bark,
being a fore-and-aft sail attached to a boom and gaff; -- sometimes
called <altname>driver</altname>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Sail</er>.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who takes long, quick strides in walking; also,
a fast horse.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Something very large, or larger than common; a
whopper, as a stout or tall person.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spanker boom</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a boom to
which a spanker sail is attached. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Ship</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spanker</ent><br/
<hw>Spank"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small coin.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spanking</ent><br/
<hw>spank"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Moving with a quick,
lively pace, or capable of so doing; dashing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Four <qex>spanking</qex> grays ready harnessed.</q> <rj><qau>G.
Colman, the Younger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Large; considerable.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Very; extremely; -- used especially in the phrase
<ecol><b>spanking new</b></ecol> or <ecol><b>brand spanking
new</b></ecol>, meaning completely new and unused.</def>
<mark>[informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spanking breeze</ent><br/
<hw>spanking breeze</hw> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <def>a strong
breeze.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spanless</ent><br/
<hw>span"less</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/n"l<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Incapable of being spanned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spanner</ent><br/
<hw>span"ner</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/n"n<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, spans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The lock of a fusee or carbine; also, the fusee or
carbine itself.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An iron instrument having a jaw to fit a nut or the
head of a bolt, and used as a lever to turn it with; a wrench;
specifically, a wrench for unscrewing or tightening the couplings of
hose.</def> <mark>[Chiefly British usage]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A contrivance in some of the
earlier steam engines for moving the valves for the alternate admission
and shutting off of the steam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>span-new</ent><br/
<hw>span"-new`</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/n"n<umac/`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Icel. <ets>sp<amac/nn<ymac/r</ets>, properly, new as a ship just
split; <ets>sp<amac/nn</ets> chip + <ets>n<ymac/r</ets> new. See
<er>Spoon</er>, and <er>New</er>.]</ety> <def>Quite new; brand-new;
fire-new.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>span-new</xex> archbishop's chair.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spannishing</ent><br/
<hw>span"nish*ing</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/n"n<icr/sh*<icr/ng)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From OF. <ets>espanir</ets> to spread, F.
<ets><eacute/panouir</ets>. See <er>Expand</er>.]</ety> <def>The full
blooming of a flower.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Rom. of
R.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spanpiece</ent><br/
<hw>span"piece</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/n"p<emac/s`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The collar of a roof; sparpiece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spanworm</ent><br/
<hw>span"worm`</hw> <pr>(sp<acr/n"w<ucir/rm`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The larva of any geometrid moth, as the
cankerworm; a geometer; a measuring worm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1378 --></p>

<p><ent>spar</ent><br/
<hw>spar</hw> <pr>(sp<aum/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>spaer</ets> in <ets>spaerst<amac/n</ets> chalkstone; akin to MHG.
<ets>spar</ets>, G. <ets>spar</ets>kalk plaster.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>An old name for a nonmetallic mineral, usually
cleavable and somewhat lustrous; <as>as, calc <ex>spar</ex>, or calcite,
fluor <ex>spar</ex>, etc.</as> It was especially used in the case of the
gangue minerals of a metalliferous vein.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Blue spar</b></col>, <col><b>Cube
spar</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Blue</er>, <er>Cube</er>,
etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spar</ent><br/
<hw>Spar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sparre</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>spar</ets>, G. <ets>sparren</ets>, OHG. <ets>sparro</ets>, Dan. &
Sw. <ets>sparre</ets>, Icel. <ets>sparri</ets>; of uncertain origin.
<root/171. Cf. <er>Spar</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos> ]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A general term any round piece of timber used as
a mast, yard, boom, or gaff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Formerly, a piece of timber, in a
general sense; -- still applied locally to rafters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The bar of a gate or door.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spar buoy</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a buoy
anchored by one end so that the other end rises above the surface of the
water.</cd> -- <col><b>Spar deck</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the
upper deck of a vessel; especially, in a frigate, the deck which is
continued in a straight line from the quarter-deck to the forecastle,
and on which spare spars are usually placed. See under
<er>Deck</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spar torpedo</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a torpedo carried on the end of a spar usually
projecting from the bow of a vessel, and intended to explode upon
contact with an enemy's ships.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spar</ent><br/
<hw>Spar</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sparren</ets>, AS.
<ets>sparrian</ets>; akin to G. <ets>sperren</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sperra</ets>; from the noun. <root/171. See <er>Spara</er> beam,
bar.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bolt; to bar.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To To supply or equip with spars, as a
vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A vessel equipped with spars that are too large or too
small is said to be <xex>oversparred</xex> or
<xex>undersparred</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spar</ent><br/
<hw>Spar</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sparred</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sparring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of uncertain origin; cf. OF.
<ets>esparer</ets> to kick, F. <ets><eacute/parer</ets>, or Icel.
<ets>sperra</ets> to stretch out the legs, to struggle.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike with the feet or spurs, as cocks
do.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To use the fists and arms scientifically in attack
or defense; to contend or combat with the fists, as for exercise or
amusement; to box.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Made believe to <qex>spar</qex> at Paul with great science.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To contest in words; to wrangle.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- sparring partner, (Boxing) one who spars with a boxer as an
opponent for training purposes. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spar</ent><br/
<hw>Spar</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A contest at sparring or
boxing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A movement of offense or defense in
boxing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparable</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted from
<ets>sparrow bill</ets>.]</ety> <def>A kind of small nail used by
shoemakers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparada</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"a*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
small California surf fish (<spn>Micrometrus aggregatus</spn>); --
called also <altname>shiner</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparadrap</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"a*drap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sparadrap</ets>; cf. It. <ets>sparadrappo</ets>, NL.
<ets>sparadrapa</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A cerecloth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Any adhesive plaster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparagrass</ent><br/
<ent>Sparagus</ent><br/
<ent>Sparage</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spar"age</hw> <pr>(?; 48)</pr>, <hw>Spar"a*gus</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spar"a*grass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Obs. or corrupt forms of <er>Asparagus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparble</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>esparpiller</ets> to scatter, F.
<ets><eacute/parpiller</ets>.]</ety> <def>To scatter; to disperse; to
rout.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king's host was <qex>sparbled</qex> and chased.</q>
<rj><qau>Fabyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spare</ent><br/
<hw>Spare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sparing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>sparian</ets>, fr.
<ets>spaer</ets> spare, sparing, saving; akin to D. & G.
<ets>sparen</ets>, OHG. <ets>spar<?/n</ets>, Icel. & Sw.
<ets>spara</ets>, Dan. <ets>spare</ets> See <er>Spare</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To use frugally or stintingly, as
that which is scarce or valuable; to retain or keep unused; to
save.</def> <ldquo/No cost would he <xex>spare</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Thou] thy Father's dreadful thunder didst not
<qex>spare</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that hath knowledge, <qex>spareth</qex> his words.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xvii. 27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To keep to one's self; to forbear to impart or
give.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be pleased your plitics to <qex>spare</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Spare</qex> my sight the pain<br/
Of seeing what a world of tears it costs you.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To preserve from danger or punishment; to forbear to
punish, injure, or harm; to show mercy to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Spare</qex> us, good Lord.</q> <rj><qau>Book of Common
Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dim sadness did not <qex>spare</qex><br/
That time celestial visages.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Man alone can whom he conquers <qex>spare</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To save or gain, as by frugality; to reserve, as
from some occupation, use, or duty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the time he could <qex>spare</qex> from the necessary cares of
his weighty charge, he bestowed on . . . serving of God.</q>
<rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To deprive one's self of, as by being frugal; to do
without; to dispense with; to give up; to part with.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where angry Jove did never <qex>spare</qex><br/
One breath of kind and temperate air.</q>
<rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I could have better <qex>spared</qex> a better man.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To spare one's self</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To act
with reserve.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her thought that a lady should <qex>her spare</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To save one's self labor, punishment, or
blame.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Spare</ent><br/
<hw>Spare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be
frugal; not to be profuse; to live frugally; to be
parsimonious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I, who at some times spend, at others <qex>spare</qex>,<br/
Divided between carelessness and care.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To refrain from inflicting harm; to use mercy or
forbearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He will not <qex>spare</qex> in the day of vengeance.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. vi. 34.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To desist; to stop; to refrain.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spare</ent><br/
<hw>Spare</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sparer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sparest</adjf>; -- not used in all the senses of the
word.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>spaer</ets> sparing. Cf. <er>Spare</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> ]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Scanty; not abundant or
plentiful; <as>as, a <ex>spare</ex> diet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sparing; frugal; parsimonious; chary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was <qex>spare</qex>, but discreet of speech.</q>
<rj><qau>Carew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Being over and above what is necessary, or what must
be used or reserved; not wanted, or not used; superfluous; <as>as, I
have no <ex>spare</ex> time</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If that no <qex>spare</qex> clothes he had to give.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Held in reserve, to be used in an emergency; <as>as,
a <ex>spare</ex> anchor; a <ex>spare</ex> bed or room.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Lean; wanting flesh; meager; thin; gaunt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, give me the <qex>spare</qex> men, and spare me the great
ones.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Slow.</def> <mark>[Obs. or prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spare</ent><br/
<hw>Spare</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
sparing; moderation; restraint.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Killing for sacrifice, without any <qex>spare</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Parsimony; frugal use.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Poured out their plenty without spite or <qex>spare</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An opening in a petticoat or gown; a placket.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which has not been used or expended.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Tenpins)</fld> <def>The right of bowling again at a
full set of pins, after having knocked all the pins down in less than
three bowls. If all the pins are knocked down in one bowl it is a
<xex>double spare</xex>; in two bowls, a <xex>single spare</xex>. For
the meaning in modern bowling, see sense 6.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Bowling)</fld> <def>The act of knocking down all
ten pins in two bowls, which entitles the bowler to add the number of
pins knocked down in the next bowl to the score for the frame in which
the spare occurred.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spareful</ent><br/
<hw>Spare"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sparing;
chary.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spare"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms> <rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spareless</ent><br/
<hw>Spare"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Unsparing.</def>
<rj><au>Sylvester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparely</ent><br/
<hw>Spare"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a spare manner;
sparingly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spareness</ent><br/
<hw>Spare"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>spaernis</ets>
frugality.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being lean or thin;
leanness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparer</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
spares.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparerib</ent><br/
<hw>Spare"rib`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Spare</ets>,
a. + <ets>rib</ets>.]</ety> <def>A piece of pork, consisting or ribs
with little flesh on them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparge</ent><br/
<hw>Sparge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spargere</ets>; cf. F. <ets>asperger</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
sprinkle; to moisten by sprinkling; <as>as, to <ex>sparge</ex>
paper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spargefaction</ent><br/
<hw>Spar`ge*fac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spargere</ets> to strew + <ets>facere</ets>, <ets>factum</ets>, to
make.]</ety> <def>The act of sprinkling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparger</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>asperger</ets> to sprinkle, L. <ets>aspergere</ets>,
<ets>spargere</ets>.]</ety> <def>A vessel with a perforated cover, for
sprinkling with a liquid; a sprinkler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. A tube with a perforated or fritted end, to pass gases as
small bubbles through a liquid. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparhawk</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"hawk`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sperhauke</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The sparrow
hawk.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spar-hung</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"-hung`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hung with spar, as
a cave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparing</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Spare; saving;
frugal; merciful.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spar"ing*ly</wf>, adv. -- <wf>Spar"ing*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spark</ent><br/
<hw>Spark</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sparke</ets>,
AS. <ets>spearca</ets>; akin to D. <ets>spark</ets>, <ets>sperk</ets>;
cf. Icel. <ets>spraka</ets> to crackle, Lith. <ets>sprag<eum/ti</ets>,
Gr. <?/ a bursting with a noise, Skr. <ets>sph<?/rj</ets> to crackle, to
thunder. Cf. <er>Speak</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small particle of
fire or ignited substance which is emitted by a body in
combustion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Man is born unto trouble, as the <qex>sparks</qex> fly upward.</q>
<rj><qau>Job v. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small, shining body, or transient light; a
sparkle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which, like a spark, may be kindled into a
flame, or into action; a feeble germ; an elementary principle.</def>
<ldquo/If any <xex>spark</xex> of life be yet remaining.<rdquo/
<au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/Small intellectual <xex>spark</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Macaulay.</au> <ldquo/Vital <xex>spark</xex> of heavenly
flame.<rdquo/ <au>Pope.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have here and there a little clear light, some <qex>sparks of
bright knowledge</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bright gem instinct with music, vocal <qex>spark</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spark arrester</b></col>, <cd>a contrivance to prevent
the escape of sparks while it allows the passage of gas, -- chiefly used
in the smokestack of a wood-burning locomotive. Called also
<altname>spark consumer</altname>.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spark</ent><br/
<hw>Spark</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>sparkr</ets> lively,
sprightly.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A brisk, showy, gay man.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The finest <qex>sparks</qex> and cleanest beaux.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A lover; a gallant; a beau.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spark</ent><br/
<hw>Spark</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sparkle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>To produce, or give off, sparks,
as a dynamo at the commutator when revolving under the collecting
brushes.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spark</ent><br/
<hw>Spark</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To play the spark, beau, or
lover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A sure sign that his master was courting, or, as it is termed,
<qex>sparking</qex>, within.</q> <rj><qau>W. Irwing.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spark coil</ent><br/
<hw>Spark coil</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An induction
coil used for producing sparks, esp. in an internal-combustion engine,
wireless telegraph apparatus, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
self-induction coil used to increase the spark in an electric
gas-lighting apparatus.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparker</ent><br/
<hw>Spark"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A spark
arrester.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkful</ent><br/
<hw>Spark"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lively; brisk;
gay.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Our <xex>sparkful</xex>
youth.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spark gap</ent><br/
<hw>Spark gap</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The space filled with air or
other dielectric between high potential terminals (as of an
electrostatic machine, induction coil, or condenser), through which the
discharge passes; the air gap of a jump spark.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkish</ent><br/
<hw>Spark"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Like a spark; airy;
gay.</def> <rj><au>W. Walsh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Showy; well-dressed; fine.</def>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkle</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>spark</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A little spark; a
scintillation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As fire is wont to quicken and go<br/
From a <qex>sparkle</qex> sprungen amiss,<br/
Till a city brent up is.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The shock was sufficiently strong to strike out some
<qex>sparkles</qex> of his fiery temper.</q>
<rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Brilliancy; luster; <as>as, the <ex>sparkle</ex> of
a diamond</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkle</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"kle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sparkled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sparkling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Sparkle</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, Spark of fire.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To emit sparks; to throw off ignited or incandescent particles; to
shine as if throwing off sparks; to emit flashes of light; to
scintillate; to twinkle; <as>as, the blazing wood <ex>sparkles</ex>; the
stars <ex>sparkle</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A mantelet upon his shoulder hanging<br/
Bretful of rubies red, as fire <qex>sparkling</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To manifest itself by, or as if by, emitting sparks;
to glisten; to flash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I see bright honor <qex>sparkle</qex> through your eyes.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To emit little bubbles, as certain kinds of liquors;
to effervesce; <as>as, <ex>sparkling</ex> wine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To shine; glisten; scintillate; radiate;
coruscate; glitter; twinkle.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkle</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"kle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To emit in the form or likeness
of sparks.</def> <ldquo/Did <xex>sparkle</xex> forth great light.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkle</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"kle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Sparble</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To disperse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Landgrave hath <qex>sparkled</qex> his army without any
further enterprise.</q> <rj><qau>State Papers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To scatter on or over.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Purchas.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkler</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"kler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who scatters;
esp., one who scatters money; an improvident person.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkler</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"kler</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that which,
sparkles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkler</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"kler</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tiger
beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparklet</ent><br/
<hw>Spark"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small spark.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkliness</ent><br/
<hw>Spark"li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Vivacity.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Aubrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparkling</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"kling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Emitting sparks;
glittering; flashing; brilliant; lively; <as>as, <ex>sparkling</ex>
wine; <ex>sparkling</ex> eyes.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spar"kling*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spar"kling*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Brilliant; shining. See
<er>Shining</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spark plug</ent><br/
<hw>Spark plug</hw>. <def>In internal-combustion engines with electric
ignition, a plug, screwed into the cylinder head, connected with the
induction coil or magneto circuit on the outside, and used to produce a
spark to ignite the fuel-air mixture in the engine.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparling</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to G.
<ets>spierling</ets>, <ets>spiering</ets>, D. <ets>spiering</ets>: cf.
F. <ets><eacute/perlan</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The European smelt (<spn>Osmerus eperlanus</spn>).</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A young salmon.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A tern.</def>
<mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparlyre</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"lyre`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>spear-lira</ets>.]</ety> <def>The calf of the leg.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif (Deut. xxviii. 35).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparoid</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"roid</hw> <pr>(?; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sparus</ets> the gilthead + <ets>-oid</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sparo<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to the <fam>Sparidae</fam>, a family of spinous-finned fishes which
includes the scup, sheepshead, and sea bream.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the
<fam>Sparidae</fam>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparpiece</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"piece`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>The collar beam of a roof; the spanpiece.</def>
<rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparpoil</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"poil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sparble</er>.]</ety> <def>To scatter; to spread; to disperse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparrow</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"row</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sparwe</ets>, AS. <ets>spearwa</ets>; akin to OHG.
<ets>sparo</ets>, G. <ets>sper</ets>ling, Icel. <ets>sp<oum/rr</ets>,
Dan. <ets>spurv</ets>, <ets>spurre</ets>, Sw. <ets>sparf</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sparwa</ets>; -- originally, probably, the quiverer or flutterer,
and akin to E. <ets>spurn</ets>. See <er>Spurn</er>, and cf.
<er>Spavin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of many
species of small singing birds of the family <fam>Fringilligae</fam>,
having conical bills, and feeding chiefly on seeds. Many sparrows are
called also <stype>finches</stype>, and <stype>buntings</stype>. The
common sparrow, or house sparrow, of Europe (<spn>Passer
domesticus</spn>) is noted for its familiarity, its voracity, its
attachment to its young, and its fecundity. See <cref>House
sparrow</cref>, under <er>House</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The following American species are well known; the
<stype>chipping sparrow</stype>, or <stype>chippy</stype>, the
<stype>sage sparrow</stype>, the <stype>savanna sparrow</stype>, the
<stype>song sparrow</stype>, the <stype>tree sparrow</stype>, and the
<stype>white-throated sparrow</stype> (see <er>Peabody bird</er>). See
these terms under <er>Sage</er>, <er>Savanna</er>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several small singing
birds somewhat resembling the true sparrows in form or habits, as the
European hedge sparrow. See under <er>Hedge</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that doth the ravens feed,<br/
Yea, providently caters for the <qex>sparrow</qex>,<br/
Be comfort to my age!</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Field sparrow</b></col>, <col><b>Fox
sparrow</b></col>, etc.</mcol> <cd>See under <er>Field</er>,
<er>Fox</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Sparrow bill</b></col>, <cd>a small
nail; a castiron shoe nail; a sparable.</cd> -- <col><b>Sparrow
hawk</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A small European
hawk (<spn>Accipiter nisus</spn>) or any of the allied species.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A small American falcon (<spn>Falco
sparverius</spn>).</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>The Australian collared sparrow
hawk (<spn>Accipiter torquatus</spn>).</cd> <note>The name is applied to
other small hawks, as the European kestrel and the New Zealand quail
hawk.</note> -- <col><b>Sparrow owl</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
small owl (<spn>Glaucidium passerinum</spn>) found both in the Old World
and the New. The name is also applied to other species of small
owls.</cd> -- <col><b>Sparrow spear</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the female of the reed bunting.</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparrowgrass</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"row*grass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted
from <ets>asparagus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Asparagus.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <see>See the Note under
<er>Asparagus</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparrowwort</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"row*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An evergreen shrub of the genus <gen>Erica</gen> (<spn>Erica
passerina</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparry</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Spar</er>.]</ety> <def>Resembling spar, or consisting of spar;
abounding with spar; having a confused crystalline structure;
spathose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sparry iron</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>siderite.
See <er>Siderite</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sparry
limestone</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>a coarsely crystalline
marble.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparse</ent><br/
<hw>Sparse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sparser</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sparsest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[L. <ets>sparsus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>spargere</ets> to strew, scatter. Cf. <er>Asperse</er>,
<er>Disperse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Thinly scattered; set or
planted here and there; not being dense or close together; <as>as, a
<ex>sparse</ex> population</as>.</def> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Placed irregularly and distantly;
scattered; -- applied to branches, leaves, peduncles, and the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparse</ent><br/
<hw>Sparse</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sparsus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>spargere</ets> to scatter.]</ety> <def>To scatter; to
disperse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparsedly</ent><br/
<hw>Spars"ed*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Sparsely.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparsely</ent><br/
<hw>Sparse"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a scattered or sparse
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparseness</ent><br/
<hw>Sparse"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sparse; <as>as, <ex>sparseness</ex> of population</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparsim</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spar"sim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>spargere</ets> to scatter.]</ety> <def>Sparsely; scatteredly; here
and there.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spartan</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"tan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Spartanus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Sparta, especially
to ancient Sparta; hence, hardy; undaunted; <as>as, <ex>Spartan</ex>
souls; <ex>Spartan</ex> bravey.</as></def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A
native or inhabitant of Sparta; figuratively, a person of great courage
and fortitude.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- marked by avoidance of luxury or comfort; spare, simple; marked
by self-denial --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparteine</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"te*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A narcotic alkaloid extracted from the tops of the common broom
(<spn>Cytisus scoparius</spn>, formerly <spn>Spartium scoparium</spn>),
as a colorless oily liquid of aniline-like odor and very bitter
taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparterie</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"ter*ie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from Sp.
<ets>esparto</ets> esparto, L. <ets>spartum</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<def>Articles made of the blades or fiber of the <spn>Lygeum
Spartum</spn> and <spn>Stipa tenacissima</spn> (syn. <spn>Macrochloa
tenacissima</spn>), kinds of grass used in <country>Spain</country> and
other countries for making ropes, mats, baskets, nets, and
mattresses.</def> <rj><au>Loudon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sparth</ent><br/
<hw>Sparth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>spar<?/a</ets>.]</ety> <def>An Anglo-Saxon battle-ax, or
halberd.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He hath a <qex>sparth</qex> of twenty pound of weight.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1379 --></p>

<p><ent>Sparve</ent><br/
<hw>Sparve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
hedge sparrow.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spary</ent><br/
<hw>Spar"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sparing;
parsimonious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spasm</ent><br/
<hw>Spasm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>spasme</ets>,
L. <ets>spasmus</ets>, Gr. <?/, from <?/, <?/, to draw, to cause
convulsion. Cf. <er>Span</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An involuntary and unnatural contraction of one
or more muscles or muscular fibers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Spasm are usually either <xex>clonic</xex> or
<xex>tonic</xex>. In <xex>clonic spasm</xex>, the muscles or muscular
fibers contract and relax alternately in very quick succession. In
<xex>tonic spasm</xex>, the contraction is steady and uniform, and
continues for a comparatively long time, as in tetanus.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sudden, violent, and temporary effort or emotion;
<as>as, a <ex>spasm</ex> of repentance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cynic spasm</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Cynic</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spasm of the chest</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Angina pectoris</cref>, under <er>Angina</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spasmatical</ent><br/
<hw>Spas*mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Spasmodic.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spasmodic</ent><br/
<hw>Spas"mod"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/; <?/ a
convulsion + <?/ likeness: cf. F. <ets>spasmotique</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to spasm; consisting
in spasm; occuring in, or characterized by, spasms; <as>as, a
<ex>spasmodic</ex> asthma</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Soon relaxed or exhausted; convulsive; intermittent;
<as>as, <ex>spasmodic</ex> zeal or industry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spasmodic croup</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>an
affection of childhood characterized by a stoppage of brathing developed
suddenly and without fever, and produced by spasmodic contraction of the
vocal cords. It is sometimes fatal. Called also <altname>laryngismus
stridulus</altname>, and <altname>childcrowing</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spasmodic stricture</b></col>, <cd>a stricture caused by
muscular spasm without structural change. See <cref>Organic
stricture</cref>, under <er>Organic</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spasmodic</ent><br/
<hw>Spas*mod"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine
for spasm.</def><-- = antispasmodic --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spasmodical</ent><br/
<hw>Spas*mod"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Spasmodic</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spas*mod"ic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spastic</ent><br/
<hw>Spas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spasticus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to draw: cf. F.
<ets>spastique</ets>. See <er>Spasm</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to spasm; spasmodic; especially, pertaining to
tonic spasm; tetanic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spastically</ent><br/
<hw>Spas"tic*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Spasmodically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spasticity</ent><br/
<hw>Spas*tic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
state of spasm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The tendency to, or capability of suffering,
spasm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spat</ent><br/
<hw>Spat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of <er>Spit</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs. <plain>or</plain> R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spat</ent><br/
<hw>Spat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the root of <ets>spit</ets>;
hence, literally, that which is ejected.]</ety> <def>A young oyster or
other bivalve mollusk, both before and after it first becomes adherent,
or such young, collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spat</ent><br/
<hw>Spat</hw>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To emit spawn; to emit, as
spawn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spat</ent><br/
<hw>Spat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Pat</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A light blow with something flat.</def> <mark>[U.S. & Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a petty combat, esp. a verbal one; a little
quarrel, dispute, or dissension.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spat</ent><br/
<hw>Spat</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To dispute.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spat</ent><br/
<hw>Spat</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spatted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spatting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To slap, as with the open hand;
to clap together; as the hands.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Little Isabel leaped up and down, <qex>spatting</qex> her
hands.</q> <rj><qau>Judd.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spat</ent><br/
<hw>Spat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Short for
<er>Spatterdash</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A legging; a gaiter.</def>
<mark>[Scot. & Dial. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of short cloth or leather gaiter worn over
the upper part of the shoe and fastened beneath the instep; -- chiefly
in <pluf>pl.</pluf></def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatangoid</ent><br/
<hw>Spa*tan"goid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the <ord>Spatangoidea</ord>.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the
<ord>Spatangoidea</ord>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatangoidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spat`an*goi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Spatangus</er>, and <er>-oid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An order of irregular sea urchins, usually having a more or less
heart-shaped shell with four or five petal-like ambulacra above. The
mouth is edentulous and situated anteriorly, on the under
side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatangus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spa*tan"gus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>spatangius</ets> a kind of sea urchin, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of heart-shaped sea urchins belonging to
the <ord>Spatangoidea</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatchcock</ent><br/
<hw>Spatch"cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spitchcock</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spate</ent><br/
<hw>Spate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of Celtic origin; cf.
Ir. <ets>speid</ets>.]</ety> <def>A river flood; an overflow or
inundation.</def> <rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gareth in a showerful spring<br/
Stared at the <qex>spate</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spa"tha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spathae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A spathe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Spa*tha"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having a spathe; resembling a spathe; spathal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathal</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"thal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Furnished with a spathe; <as>as, <ex>spathal</ex>
flowers</as>.</def> <rj><au>Howitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathe</ent><br/
<hw>Spathe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spatha</ets>,
Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>spathe</ets>. See <er>Spade</er> for
digging.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A special involucre formed of one
leaf and inclosing a spadix, as in aroid plants and palms. See the Note
under <er>Bract</er>, and <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Spadix</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is also given to the several-leaved involucre
of the iris and other similar plants.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathed</ent><br/
<hw>Spathed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having a spathe or calyx like a sheath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathic</ent><br/
<hw>Spath"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>spathique</ets>, fr. F. & G. <ets>spath</ets> spar.]</ety>
<def>Like spar; foliated or lamellar; spathose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spathic iron</b></col> <fld>(Min.)</fld>, <cd>siderite.
See <er>Siderite</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathiform</ent><br/
<hw>Spath"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>spathiforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling spar in form.</def>
<ldquo/The ocherous, <xex>spathiform</xex>, and mineralized forms of
uranite.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Lavoisier (Trans.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathose</ent><br/
<hw>Spath"ose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Spathic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathose</ent><br/
<hw>Spath"ose`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spathe</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having a spathe; resembling a spathe;
spatheceous; spathal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathous</ent><br/
<hw>Spath"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Spathose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spathulate</ent><br/
<hw>Spath"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spatulate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatial</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
space.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Spatial</xex> quantity and relations.<rdquo/
<rj><au>L. H. Atwater.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatially</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"tial*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>As regards
space.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatiate</ent><br/
<hw>Spa"ti*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spatiatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>spatiari</ets>, fr.
<ets>spatiatum</ets>. See <er>Space</er>.]</ety> <def>To rove; to
ramble.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatter</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spattered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Spattering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From the root of
<ets>spit</ets> salvia.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sprinkle with a
liquid or with any wet substance, as water, mud, or the like; to make
wet of foul spots upon by sprinkling; <as>as, to <ex>spatter</ex> a
coat; to <ex>spatter</ex> the floor; to <ex>spatter</ex> boots with
mud</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Upon any occasion he is to be <qex>spattered</qex> over with the
blood of his people.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To distribute by sprinkling; to sprinkle around;
<as>as, to <ex>spatter</ex> blood</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: To injure by aspersion; to defame; to soil;
also, to throw out in a defamatory manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatter</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To throw something out of the
mouth in a scattering manner; to sputter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That mind must needs be irrecoverably depraved, which, . . .
tasting but once of one just deed, <qex>spatters</qex> at it, and abhors
the relish ever after.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatterdashed</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"ter*dashed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wearing
spatterdashes.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatterdashes</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"ter*dash`es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spatter</ets> + <ets>dash</ets>.]</ety> <def>Coverings for
the legs, to protect them from water and mud; long gaiters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatter-dock</ent><br/
<hw>Spat`ter-dock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The common yellow water lily (<spn>Nuphar advena</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spattle</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Spawl; spittle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spattle</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"tle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A spatula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Pottery)</fld> <def>A tool or implement for
mottling a molded article with coloring matter</def>
<rj><au>Knoght.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spattling-poppy</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"tling-pop"py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>spattle</ets> to spit + E. <ets>poppy</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of catchfly (<spn>Silene inflata</spn>)
which is sometimes frothy from the action of captured insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatula</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"u*la</hw> <pr>(?; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spatula</ets>, <ets>spathula</ets>, dim. of <ets>spatha</ets> a
spatula: F. <ets>spatule</ets>. See <er>Spade</er> for digging.]</ety>
<def>An implement shaped like a knife, flat, thin, and somewhat
flexible, used for spreading paints, fine plasters, drugs in compounding
prescriptions, etc. Cf. <cref>Palette knife</cref>, under
<er>Palette</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spatulate</ent><br/
<hw>Spat"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>spatulatus</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld> <def>Shaped like
spatula, or like a battledoor, being roundish, with a long, narrow,
linear base.</def> <altsp>[Also written
<asp>spathulate</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spauld</ent><br/
<hw>Spauld</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spall</er> the
shoulder.]</ety> <def>The shoulder.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spavin</ent><br/
<hw>Spav"in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>spaveyne</ets>, OF. <ets>esparvain</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/parvin</ets>; akin to OF. <ets>espervier</ets> a sparrow
hawk, F. <ets><eacute/pervier</ets>, fr. OHG. <ets>sparw<amac/ri</ets>
(G. <ets>sperber</ets>), fr. OHG. <ets>sparo</ets> sparrow, because this
disease makes the horse raise the infirm leg in the manner of a sparrow
hawk or sparrow. See <er>Sparrow</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A
disease of horses characterized by a bony swelling developed on the hock
as the result of inflammation of the bones; also, the swelling itself.
The resulting lameness is due to the inflammation, and not the bony
tumor as popularly supposed.</def> <rj><au>Harbaugh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bog spavin</b></col>, <cd>a soft swelling produced by
distention of the capsular ligament of the hock; -- called also
<altname>blood spavin</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Bone spavin</b></col>,
<cd>spavin attended with exostosis; ordinary spavin.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spavined</ent><br/
<hw>Spav"ined</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affected with
spavin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spaw</ent><br/
<hw>Spaw</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spa</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spawl</ent><br/
<hw>Spawl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A splinter or fragment,
as of wood or stone. See <er>Spall</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spawl</ent><br/
<hw>Spawl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>sp<amac/tl</ets>, fr.
<ets>sp<aemac/tan</ets> to spit; probably akin to
<ets>sp<imac/wan</ets>, E. <ets>spew</ets>. Cf. <er>Spew</er>.]</ety>
<def>Scattered or ejected spittle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spawl</ent><br/
<hw>Spawl</hw>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spawled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spawling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. AS.
<ets>sp<amac/tlian</ets>.]</ety> <def>To scatter spittle from the mouth;
to spit, as saliva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why must he sputter, <qex>spawl</qex>, and slaver it<br/
In vain, against the people's favorite.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spawling</ent><br/
<hw>Spawl"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is spawled, or spit
out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spawn</ent><br/
<hw>Spawn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spawned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spawning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>spanen</ets>, OF.
<ets>espandre</ets>, properly, to shed, spread, L. <ets>expandere</ets>
to spread out. See <er>Expand</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To produce
or deposit (eggs), as fishes or frogs do.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bring forth; to generate; -- used in
contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One edition [of books] <qex>spawneth</qex> another.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spawn</ent><br/
<hw>Spawn</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To deposit eggs, as
fish or frogs do.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To issue, as offspring; -- used
contemptuously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spawn</ent><br/
<hw>Spawn</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<root/170. See <er>Spawn</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The ova, or eggs, of fishes,
oysters, and other aquatic animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any product or offspring; -- used
contemptuously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Hort.)</fld> <def>The buds or branches produced
from underground stems.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The white fibrous matter forming
the matrix from which fungi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spawn eater</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small
American cyprinoid fish (<spn>Notropis Hudsonius</spn>) allied to the
dace.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spawner</ent><br/
<hw>Spawn"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A mature female fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The barbel, for the preservation or their seed, both the
<qex>spawner</qex> and the milter, cover their spawn with sand.</q>
<rj><qau>Walton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Whatever produces spawn of any kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spay</ent><br/
<hw>Spay</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spayed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spaying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Armor. <ets>spac'hein</ets>,
<ets>spaza</ets> to geld, W. <ets>dyspaddu</ets> to geld, L.
<ets>spado</ets> a eunuch, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>To remove or extirpate
the ovaries of, as a sow or a bitch; to castrate (a female
animal).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spay</ent><br/
<hw>Spay</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Spade</er> a spay,
<er>Spay</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The male
of the red deer in his third year; a spade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spayade</ent><br/
<ent>Spayad</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spay"ad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spay"ade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A spay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speak</ent><br/
<hw>Speak</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Spoke</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> (<conjf>Spake</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>
<mark>Archaic</mark>); <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spoken</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr> (<conjf>Spoke</conjf>, <mark>Obs. <plain>or</plain>
Colloq.</mark>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Speaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>speken</ets>, AS.
<ets>specan</ets>, <ets>sprecan</ets>; akin to OF.ries.
<ets>spreka</ets>, D. <ets>spreken</ets>, OS. <ets>spreken</ets>, G.
<ets>sprechen</ets>, OHG. <ets>sprehhan</ets>, and perhaps to Skr.
<ets>sph<umac/rj</ets> to crackle, to thunder. Cf. <er>Spark</er> of
fire, <er>Speech</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter words or
articulate sounds, as human beings; to express thoughts by words;
<as>as, the organs may be so obstructed that a man may not be able to
<ex>speak</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till at the last <qex>spake</qex> in this manner.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Speak</qex>, Lord; for thy servant heareth.</q> <rj><qau>1
Sam. iii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To express opinions; to say; to talk; to
converse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That fluid substance in a few minutes begins to set, as the
tradesmen <qex>speak</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An honest man, is able to <qex>speak</qex> for himself, when a
knave is not.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>During the century and a half which followed the Conquest, there
is, to <qex>speak</qex> strictly, no English history.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To utter a speech, discourse, or harangue; to adress
a public assembly formally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many of the nobility made themselves popular by
<qex>speaking</qex> in Parliament against those things which were most
grateful to his majesty.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To discourse; to make mention; to tell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lycan <qex>speaks</qex> of a part of Caesar's army that came to
him from the Leman Lake.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To give sound; to sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Make all our trumpets <qex>speak</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To convey sentiments, ideas, or intelligence as if
by utterance; <as>as, features that <ex>speak</ex> of
self-will</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thine eye begins to <qex>speak</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To speak of</b></col>, <cd>to take account of, to make
mention of.</cd> <au>Robynson (More's Utopia).</au> -- <col><b>To speak
out</b></col>, <cd>to speak loudly and distinctly; also, to speak
unreservedly.</cd> -- <col><b>To speak well for</b></col>, <cd>to
commend; to be favorable to.</cd> -- <col><b>To speak with</b></col>,
<cd>to converse with.</cd> <ldquo/Would you <xex>speak with</xex>
me?<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To say; tell; talk; converse; discourse;
articulate; pronounce; utter.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speak</ent><br/
<hw>Speak</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter
with the mouth; to pronounce; to utter articulately, as human
beings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They sat down with him upn ground seven days and seven nights, and
none <qex>spake</qex> a word unto him.</q> <rj><qau>Job. ii.
13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To utter in a word or words; to say; to tell; to
declare orally; <as>as, to <ex>speak</ex> the truth; to <ex>speak</ex>
sense</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To declare; to proclaim; to publish; to make known;
to exhibit; to express in any way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is my father;s muste<br/
To <qex>speak</qex> your deeds.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Speaking</qex> a still good morrow with her eyes.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And for the heaven's wide circuit, let it <qex>speak</qex><br/
The maker's high magnificence.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Report <qex>speaks</qex> you a bonny monk.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To talk or converse in; to utter or pronounce, as in
conversation; <as>as, to <ex>speak</ex> Latin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And French she <qex>spake</qex> full fair and fetisely.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To address; to accost; to speak to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[He will] thee in hope; he will <qex>speak</qex> thee fair.</q>
<rj><qau>Ecclus. xiii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>each village senior paused to scan<br/
And <qex>speak</qex> the lovely caravan.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To speak a ship</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to hail
and speak to her captain or commander.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speakable</ent><br/
<hw>Speak"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of being spoken; fit to be spoken.</def>
<rj><au>Ascham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Able to speak.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>speakeasy</ent><br/
<hw>speak"eas`y</hw>, <pr>(sp<emac/k"<emac/`z<esl/)</pr> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>An establishment where alcoholic beverages were sold and drunk
illegally, especially one operating during the prohibition era in the
U.S. (1920-1932); a tavern or nightclub illegally selling alcoholic
beverages.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speaker</ent><br/
<hw>Speak"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
speaks.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One who utters or
pronounces a discourse; usually, one who utters a speech in public;
<as>as, the man is a good <ex>speaker</ex>, or a bad
<ex>speaker</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One who is the mouthpiece
of others; especially, one who presides over, or speaks for, a
delibrative assembly, preserving order and regulating the debates;
<as>as, the <ex>Speaker</ex> of the House of Commons, originally, the
mouthpiece of the House to address the king; the <ex>Speaker</ex> of a
House of Representatives.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A book of selections for declamation.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speakership</ent><br/
<hw>Speak"er*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of speaker;
<as>as, the <ex>speakership</ex> of the House of
Representatives</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speaking</ent><br/
<hw>Speak"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Uttering speech; used
for conveying speech; <as>as, man is a <ex>speaking</ex> animal; a
<ex>speaking</ex> tube.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Seeming to be capable of speech; hence, lifelike;
<as>as, a <ex>speaking</ex> likeness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>A speaking acquaintance</b></col>, <cd>a slight
acquaintance with a person, or one which merely permits the exchange of
salutations and remarks on indifferent subjects.</cd> --
<col><b>Speaking trumpet</b></col>, <cd>an instrument somewhat
resembling a trumpet, by which the sound of the human voice may be so
intensified as to be conveyed to a great distance.</cd> --
<col><b>Speaking tube</b></col>, <cd>a tube for conveying speech,
especially from one room to another at a distance.</cd> -- <col><b>To be
on speaking terms</b></col>, <cd>to be slightly
acquainted.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speaking</ent><br/
<hw>Speak"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of uttering
words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Public declamation; oratory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spear</ent><br/
<hw>Spear</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spere</ets>,
AS. <ets>spere</ets>; akin to D. & G. <ets>speer</ets>, OS. & OHS.
<ets>sper</ets>, Icel. spj<oum/r, pl., Dan. <ets>spaer</ets>, L.
<ets>sparus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long, pointed weapon, used
in war and hunting, by thrusting or throwing; a weapon with a long shaft
and a sharp head or blade; a lance.</def> <note>[See <xex>Illust.</xex>
of <er>Spearhead</er>.]</note> <ldquo/A sharp ground
<xex>spear</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their
<qex>spears</qex> into pruning hooks.</q> <rj><qau>Micah iv.
3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: A spearman.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sharp-pointed instrument with barbs, used for
stabbing fish and other animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A shoot, as of grass; a spire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The feather of a horse. See <er>Feather</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The rod to which the bucket, or plunger, of a pump
is attached; a pump rod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spear foot</b></col>, <cd>the off hind foot of a
horse.</cd> -- <col><b>Spear grass</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The common reed. See <er>Reed</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
1.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>meadow grass. See under <er>Meadow</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Spear hand</b></col>, <cd>the hand in which a horseman holds
a spear; the right hand.</cd> <au>Crabb.</au> -- <col><b>Spear
side</b></col>, <cd>the male line of a family.</cd> <au>Lowell.</au> --
<col><b>Spear thistle</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the common
thistle (<spn>Cnicus lanceolatus</spn>).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spear</ent><br/
<hw>Spear</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Speared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spearing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To pierce with a spear; to kill
with a spear; <as>as, to <ex>spear</ex> a fish</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spear</ent><br/
<hw>Spear</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To shoot into a long stem, as some
plants. See <er>Spire</er>.</def> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spearer</ent><br/
<hw>Spear"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who uses a spear;
<as>as, a <ex>spearer</ex> of fish</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spearfish</ent><br/
<hw>Spear"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A large and powerful fish (<spn>Tetrapturus
albidus</spn>) related to the swordfish, but having scales and ventral
fins. It is found on the American coast and the Mediterranean.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The carp sucker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1380 --></p>

<p><ent>Spearhead</ent><br/
<hw>Spear"head`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The pointed head,
or end, of a spear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spearman</ent><br/
<hw>Spear"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spearmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who is armed with a
spear.</def> <rj><au>Acts xxiii. 23.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spearmint</ent><br/
<hw>Spear"mint`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named from its
<ets>spiry</ets>, not capitate, inflorescence. <au>Dr.
Prior.</au>]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A species of mint (<spn>Mentha
viridis</spn>) growing in moist soil. It vields an aromatic oil. See
<er>Mint</er>, and <er>Mentha</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spearwood</ent><br/
<hw>Spear"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An Australian tree (<spn>Acacia Doratoxylon</spn>), and its tough
wood, used by the natives for spears.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spearwort</ent><br/
<hw>Spear"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sperewyrt</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name given to
several species of crowfoot (<gen>Ranunculus</gen>) which have
spear-shaped leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speary</ent><br/
<hw>Spear"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the form of a
spear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spece</ent><br/
<hw>Spece</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Species; kind.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specht</ent><br/
<hw>Specht</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Speight</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A woodpecker.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Sherwood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Special</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>specialis</ets>, fr. <ets>species</ets> a particular sort, kind, or
quality: cf. F. <ets>sp<eacute/cial</ets>. See <er>Species</er>, and cf.
<er>Especial</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a
species; constituting a species or sort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>special</qex> is called by the schools a
<ldquo/species<rdquo/.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Particular; peculiar; different from others;
extraordinary; uncommon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our Savior is represented everywhere in Scripture as the
<qex>special</qex> patron of the poor and the afficted.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To this <qex>special</qex> evil an improvement of style would
apply a <qex>special</qex> redress.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Appropriate; designed for a particular purpose,
occasion, or person; <as>as, a <ex>special</ex> act of Parliament or of
Congress; a <ex>special</ex> sermon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Limited in range; confined to a definite field of
action, investigation, or discussion; <as>as, a <ex>special</ex>
dictionary of commercial terms; a <ex>special</ex> branch of
study</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Chief in excellence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king hath drawn<br/
The <qex>special</qex> head of all the land together.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Special administration</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an
administration limited to certain specified effects or acts, or one
granted during a particular time or the existence of a special cause, as
during a controversy respecting the probate of a will, or the right of
administration, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Special agency</b></col>, <cd>an
agency confined to some particular matter.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Special
bail</b></col>, <col><b>Bail above</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Bail
to the action</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>sureties who
undertake that, if the defendant is convicted, he shall satisfy the
plaintiff, or surrender himself into custody.</cd> <au>Tomlins.</au>
<au>Wharton (Law Dict.).</au> -- <col><b>Special constable</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Constable</er>.</cd> <au>Bouvier.</au> --
<col><b>Special damage</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a damage
resulting from the act complained of, as a natural, but not the
necessary, consequence of it.</cd> -- <col><b>Special demurrer</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a demurrer for some defect of form in the opposite
party pleading, in which the cause of demurrer is particularly
stated.</cd> -- <col><b>Special deposit</b></col>, <cd>a deposit made of
a specific thing to be kept distinct from others.</cd> --
<col><b>Special homology</b></col>. <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Homology</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Special injuction</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an injuction granted on special grounds, arising
of the circumstances of the case.</cd> <au>Daniell.</au> --
<col><b>Special issue</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an issue produced
upon a special plea.</cd> <au>Stephen.</au> -- <col><b>Special
jury</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a jury consisting of persons of
some particular calling, station, or qualification, which is called upon
motion of either party when the cause is supposed to require it; a
struck jury.</cd> -- <col><b>Special orders</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>,
<cd>orders which do not concern, and are not published to, the whole
command, such as those relating to the movement of a particular corps, a
detail, a temporary camp, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Special
partner</b></col>, <cd>a limited partner; a partner with a limited or
restricted responsibility; -- unknown at common law.</cd> --
<col><b>Special partnership</b></col>, <cd>a limited or particular
partnership; -- a term sometimes applied to a partnership in a
particular business, operation, or adventure.</cd> -- <col><b>Special
plea in bar</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a plea setting forth
particular and new matter, distinguished from the general issue.</cd>
<au>Bouvier.</au> -- <col><b>Special pleader</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>,
<cd>originally, a counsel who devoted himself to drawing special counts
and pleas; in a wider sense, a lawyer who draws pleadings.</cd> --
<col><b>Special pleading</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the allegation
of special or new matter, as distingiushed from a direct denial of
matter previously alleged on the side.</cd> <au>Bouvier.</au> <cd>The
popular denomination of the whole science of pleading.</cd>
<au>Stephen.</au> <cd>The phrase is sometimes popularly applied to the
specious, but unsound, argumentation of one whose aim is victory, and
not truth.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au> -- <col><b>Special property</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a qualified or limited ownership possession, as in
wild animals, things found or bailed.</cd> -- <col><b>Special
session</b></col>, <cd>an extraordinary session; a session at an unusual
time or for an unusual purpose; <as>as, a <ex>special session</ex> of
Congress or of a legislature</as>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Special
statute</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Special law</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>an act of the legislature which has reference to a particular
person, place, or interest; a <cref>private law</cref>; -- in
distinction from a <contr>general law</contr> or <contr>public
law</contr>.</cd> -- <col><b>Special verdict</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>,
<cd>a special finding of the facts of the case, leaving to the court the
application of the law to them.</cd> <au>Wharton (Law
Dict.).</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Peculiar; appropriate; specific; dictinctive;
particular; exceptional; singular. See <er>Peculiar</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Special</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cial</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A particular.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One appointed for a special service or
occasion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In special</b></col>, <cd>specially; in particular.</cd>
<au>Chaucer.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specialism</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cial*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Devotion to a
particular and restricted part or branch of knowledge, art, or science;
<as>as, medical <ex>specialism</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specialist</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cial*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who devotes
himself to some specialty; <as>as, a medical <ex>specialist</ex>, one
who devotes himself to diseases of particular parts of the body, as the
eye, the ear, the nerves, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speciality</ent><br/
<hw>Spe`ci*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Specialities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See <er>Special</er>,
and <er>Specialty</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A particular or peculiar
case; a particularity.</def> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>See <er>Specialty</er>,
3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The special or peculiar mark or characteristic of a
person or thing; that for which a person is specially distinguished; an
object of special attention; a special occupation or object of
attention; a specialty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On these two general heads all other <qex>specialities</qex> are
depedent.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Strive, while improving your one talent, to enrich your whole
capital as a man. It is in this way that you escape from the wretched
narrow-mindedness which is the characteristic of every one who
cultivates his <qex>speciality</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ld.
Lytton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We 'll say, instead, the inconsequent creature man, -<br/
For that'a his <qex>speciality</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs.
Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Think of this, sir, . . . remote from the impulses of passion, and
apart from the <qex>specialities</qex> -- if I may use that strong
remark -- of prejudice.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An attribute or quality peculiar to a
species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specialization</ent><br/
<hw>Spe`cial*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of specializing, or the state of being
spezialized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The setting apart of a particular
organ for the performance of a particular function.</def>
<rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specialize</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cial*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
mention specially; to particularize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To apply to some specialty or limited object; to
assign to a specific use; <as>as, <ex>specialized</ex>
knowledge</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>To supply with an organ or organs
having a special function or functions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specially</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cial*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a special
manner; particularly; especially.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>For a particular purpose; <as>as, a meeting of the
legislature is <ex>specially</ex> summoned</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specialty</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cial*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Specialties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F.
<ets>sp<eacute/cialit</ets><eacute/. Cf. <er>Speciality</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Particularity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Specialty</qex> of rule hath been neglected.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A particular or peculiar case.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A contract or obligation under
seal; a contract by deed; a writing, under seal, given as security for a
debt particularly specified.</def> <rj><au>Chitty.</au>
<au>Bouvier.</au> <au>Wharton (Law Dict.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let <qex>specialties</qex> be therefore drawn between us.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That for which a person is distinguished, in which
he is specially versed, or which he makes an object of special
attention; a speciality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men of boundless knowledge, like Humbold, must have had once their
<qex>specialty</qex>, their pet subject.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Kingsley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specie</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spe"ci*e</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>abl.</pos> of L.
<xex>species</xex> sort, kind. Used in the phrase <xex>in specie</xex>,
that is, in sort, in kind, in (its own) form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/[The king] expects a return in <qex>specie</qex> from
them<rdquo/ [<it>i. e.</it>, kindness for kindness].</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In specie</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>in precise or
definite form; specifically; according to the exact terms; of the very
thing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specie</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formed as a singular
from <ets>species</ets>, in sense 5.]</ety> <def>Coin; hard
money.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Species</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cies</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <ety>[L., a
sight, outward appearance, shape, form, a particular sort, kind, or
quality, a species. See <er>Spice</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Specie</er>, <er>Special</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Visible or
sensible presentation; appearance; a sensible percept received by the
imagination; an image.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The
<xex>species</xex> of the letters illuminated with indigo and
violet.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir I. Newton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wit, . . . the faculty of imagination in the writer, which
searches over all the memory for the <qex>species</qex> or ideas of
those things which it designs to represent.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the scholastic philosophy, the species was
<xex>sensible</xex> and <xex>intelligible</xex>. The <xex>sensible</xex>
species was that in any material, object which was in fact discerned by
the mind through the organ of perception, or that in any object which
rendered it possible that it should be perceived. The sensible species,
as apprehended by the understanding in any of the relations of thought,
was called an <xex>intelligible</xex> species. <ldquo/An apparent
diversity between the <xex>species</xex> visible and audible is, that
the visible doth not mingle in the medium, but the audible
doth.<rdquo/</note> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>A group of individuals agreeing
in common attributes, and designated by a common name; a conception
subordinated to another conception, called a genus, or generic
conception, from which it differs in containing or comprehending more
attributes, and extending to fewer individuals. Thus, <er>man</er> is a
<xex>species</xex>, under <er>animal</er> as a <xex>genus</xex>; and
<xex>man</xex>, in its turn, may be regarded as a <xex>genus</xex> with
respect to <er>European</er>, <er>American</er>, or the like, as
<xex>species</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In science, a more or less permanent group of
existing things or beings, associated according to attributes, or
properties determined by scientific observation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In mineralogy and chemistry, objects which possess the
same definite chemical structure, and are fundamentally the same in
crystallization and physical characters, are classed as belonging to a
<xex>species</xex>. In Zoology and botany, a <xex>species</xex> is an
ideal group of individuals which are believed to have descended from
common ancestors, which agree in essential characteristics, and are
capable of indefinitely continued fertile reproduction through the
sexes. A <xex>species</xex>, as thus defined, differs from a
<xex>variety</xex> or <xex>subspecies</xex> only in the greater
stability of its characters and in the absence of individuals
intermediate between the related groups.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sort; a kind; a variety; <as>as, a
<ex>species</ex> of low cunning; a <ex>species</ex> of generosity; a
<ex>species</ex> of cloth.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Coin, or coined silver, gold, or other metal, used
as a circulating medium; specie.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was, in the splendor of the Roman empire, a less quantity of
current <qex>species</qex> in Europe than there is now.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A public spectacle or exhibition.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Pharmacy)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A component part
of a compound medicine; a simple.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>An officinal mixture or compound powder of any kind; esp., one used
for making an aromatic tea or tisane; a tea mixture.</def>
<rj><au>Quincy.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld> <def>The form or shape given to
materials; fashion or shape; form; figure.</def>
<rj><au>Burill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Incipient species</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
subspecies, or variety, which is in process of becoming permanent, and
thus changing to a true species, usually by isolation in localities from
which other varieties are excluded.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specifiable</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"i*fi`a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Admitting
specification; capable of being specified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>specific</ent><br/
<hw>spe*cif"ic</hw> <pr>(sp<esl/*s<icr/f"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>sp<eacute/cifique</ets>, or NL. <ets>spesificus</ets>; L.
<ets>species</ets> a particular sort or kind + <ets>facere</ets> to
make. Cf. <er>specify</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
a species; characterizing or constituting a species; possessing the
peculiar property or properties of a thing which constitute its species,
and distinguish it from other things; <as>as, the <ex>specific</ex> form
of an animal or a plant; the <ex>specific</ex> qualities of a drug; the
<ex>specific</ex> distinction between virtue and vice.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Specific</qex> difference is that primary attribute which
distinguishes each species from one another.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifying; definite, or making definite; limited;
precise; discriminating; <as>as, a <ex>specific</ex>
statement</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Exerting a peculiar influence over
any part of the body; preventing or curing disease by a peculiar
adaptation, and not on general principles; <as>as, quinine is a
<ex>specific</ex> medicine in cases of malaria</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In fact, all medicines will be found <qex>specific</qex> in the
perfection of the science.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Specific character</b></col> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld>,
<cd>a characteristic or characteristics distinguishing one species from
every other species of the same genus.</cd> -- <col><b>Specific
disease</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A disease which
produces a determinate definite effect upon the blood and tissues or
upon some special tissue</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A disease which is
itself uniformly produced by a definite and peculiar poison or
organism.</cd> -- <col><b>Specific duty</b></col>. <fld>(Com.)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>Duty</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Specific gravity</b></col>.
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Gravity</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Specific heat</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>, <cd>the quantity
of heat required to raise the temperature of a body one degree, taking
as the unit of measure the quantity required to raise the same weight of
water from zero to one degree; <as>thus, the <ex>specific heat</ex> of
mercury is 0.033, that of water being 1.000</as>.</cd> --
<col><b>Specific inductive capacity</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>,
<cd>the effect of a dielectric body in producing static electric
induction as compared with that of some other body or bodies referred to
as a standard.</cd> -- <col><b>Specific legacy</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a bequest of a particular thing, as of a
particular animal or piece of furniture, specified and distinguished
from all others.</cd> <au>Wharton.</au> <au>Burrill.</au> --
<col><b>Specific name</b></col> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld>, <cd>the name
which, appended to the name of the genus, constitutes the distinctive
name of the species; -- originally applied by Linnaeus to the essential
character of the species, or the <xex>essential difference</xex>. The
present <ex>specific name</ex> he at first called the <altname>trivial
name</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Specific performance</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the peformance of a contract or agreement as
decreed by a court of equity.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specific</ent><br/
<hw>Spe*cif"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
specific remedy. See <er>Specific</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His parents were weak enough to believe that the royal touch was a
<qex>specific</qex> for this malady.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything having peculiar adaption to the purpose to
which it is applied.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specifical</ent><br/
<hw>Spe*cif"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Specific.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specifically</ent><br/
<hw>Spe*cif"ic*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
specific manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specificalness</ent><br/
<hw>Spe*cif"ic*al*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
specific.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specificate</ent><br/
<hw>Spe*cif"i*cate</hw> <pr>(sp<esl/*s<icr/f"<icr/*k<amac/t)</pr>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Specify</er>.]</ety> <def>To show, mark,
or designate the species, or the distinguishing particulars of; to
specify.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>ir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>specification</ent><br/
<hw>spec`i*fi*ca"tion</hw>
<pr>(sp<ecr/s`<icr/*f<icr/*k<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>sp<eacute/cification</ets>, LL.
<ets>specificatio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of specifying
or determining by a mark or limit; notation of limits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>specification</qex> or limitation of the question
hinders the disputers from wandering away from the precise point of
inquiry.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The designation of particulars; particular mention;
<as>as, the <ex>specification</ex> of a charge against an
officer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A written statement containing a minute description
or enumeration of particulars, as of charges against a public officer,
the terms of a contract, the description of an invention, as in a
patent; also, a single article, item, or particular, an allegation of a
specific act, as in a charge of official misconduct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A detailed listing or description of the required
properties of some object proposed to be built or bought; -- usually
used in the <pluf>plural</pluf>; <as>as, the building
<ex>specifications</ex> require that it withstand an earthquake of
magnitude 8; the program <ex>specifications</ex> require an option to
change the menus</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specificness</ent><br/
<hw>Spe*cif"ic*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being specific.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specify</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Specified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Specifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>sp<eacute/cifier</ets>, or OF. <ets>especifier</ets>, fr. LL.
<ets>specificare</ets>. See <er>Species</er>, <er>-fy</er>.]</ety>
<def>To mention or name, as a particular thing; to designate in words so
as to distinguish from other things; <as>as, to <ex>specify</ex> the
uses of a plant; to <ex>specify</ex> articles purchased</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He has there given us an exact geography of Greece, where the
countries and the uses of their soils are <qex>specified</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specillum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spe*cil"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See <er>Stylet</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specimen</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"i*men</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>specere</ets> to look, to behold. See <er>Spy</er>.]</ety> <def>A
part, or small portion, of anything, or one of a number of things,
intended to exhibit the kind and quality of the whole, or of what is not
exhibited; a sample; <as>as, a <ex>specimen</ex> of a man's handwriting;
a <ex>specimen</ex> of a person's blood; a <ex>specimen</ex> of
painting; a<ex>specimen</ex> of one's art</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sample; model; pattern.</syn> --
<usage><er>Specimen</er>, <er>Sample</er>. A <xex>specimen</xex> is a
representative of the class of things to which it belongs; as, a
<xex>specimen</xex> of photography. A <xex>sample</xex> is a part of the
thing itself, designed to show the quality of the whole; as, a
<xex>sample</xex> of sugar or of broadcloth. A cabinet of minerals
consists of <xex>specimens</xex>; if a part be broken off from any one
of these, it is a <xex>sample</xex> of the mineral to which it belongs.
<ldquo/Several persons have exhibited <xex>specimens</xex> of this art
before multitudes of beholders.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au> <ldquo/I design
this but for a <xex>sample</xex> of what I hope more fully to
discuss.<rdquo/ <au>Woodward.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speciosity</ent><br/
<hw>Spe`ci*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Speciocities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<er>speciositas</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of
being specious; speciousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Professions built so largely on <qex>speciosity</qex>, instead of
performance.</q> <rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is specious.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1381 --></p>

<p><ent>Specious</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>speciosus</ets>good-looking, beautiful, specious, fr.
<ets>species</ets> look, show, appearance; cf. F.
<ets>sp<eacute/coeux</ets>. See <er>Species</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Presenting a pleasing appearance; pleasing in form or look;
showy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some [serpents] <qex>specious</qex> and beautiful to the eye.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Richardson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The rest, far greater part,<br/
Will deem in outward rites and <qex>specious</qex> forms<br/
Religion satisfied.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Apparently right; superficially fair, just, or
correct, but not so in reality; appearing well at first view; plausible;
<as>as, <ex>specious</ex> reasoning; a <ex>specious</ex>
argument.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Misled for a moment by the <qex>specious</qex> names of religion,
liberty, and property.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In consequence of their greater command of <qex>specious</qex>
expression.</q> <rj><qau>J. Morley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Plausible; showy; ostensible; colorable;
feasible. See <er>Plausible</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spe"xious*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spe"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speck</ent><br/
<hw>Speck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>spik</ets> blubber, AS. <ets>spic</ets>, D. <ets>spek</ets>, G.
<ets>speck</ets>.]</ety> <def>The blubber of whales or other marine
mammals; also, the fat of the hippopotamus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Speck falls</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>falls or
ropes rove through blocks for hoisting the blubber and bone of whales on
board a whaling vessel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speck</ent><br/
<hw>Speck</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spekke</ets>, AS.
<ets>specca</ets>; cf. LG. <ets>spaak</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
small discolored place in or on anything, or a small place of a color
different from that of the main substance; a spot; a stain; a blemish;
<as>as, a <ex>speck</ex> on paper or loth; <ex>specks</ex> of decay in
fruit</as>.</def> <ldquo/Gray sand, with black <xex>specks</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Anson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A very small thing; a particle; a mite; <as>as,
<ex>specks</ex> of dust; he has not a <ex>speck</ex> of
money.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many bright <qex>specks</qex> bubble up along the blue Egean.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small etheostomoid fish
(<spn>Ulocentra stigmaea</spn>) common in the Eastern United
States.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speck</ent><br/
<hw>Speck</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Specked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Specking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cause the presence of specks
upon or in, especially specks regarded as defects or blemishes; to spot;
to speckle; <as>as, paper <ex>specked</ex> by impurities in the water
used in its manufacture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Carnation, purple, azure, or <qex>specked</qex> with gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speckle</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>speck</ets>; cf. D. <ets>spikkel</ets>.]</ety> <def>A little or
spot in or anything, of a different substance or color from that of the
thing itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An huge great serpent, all with <qex>speckles</qex> pied.</q>
<rj><qau>Spebser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speckle</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"kle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Speckled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Speckling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To mark with small
spots of a different color from that of the rest of the surface; to
variegate with spots of a different color from the ground or
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speckled</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"kled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Marked or variegated
with small spots of a different color from that of the rest of the
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Speckled Indians</b></col> <fld>(Ethnol.)</fld>, <cd>the
Pintos.</cd> -- <col><b>Speckled trout</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The common American brook trout. See
<er>Trout</er>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The rainbow trout.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speckled-belly</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"kled-bel`ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The gadwall.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speckled-bill</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"kled-bill"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The American white-fronted goose (<spn>Anser
albifrons</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speckledness</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"kled*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
speckled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specksioneer</ent><br/
<hw>Speck`sion*eer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The chief
harpooner, who also directs in cutting up the speck, or blubber; -- so
called among whalers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speckt</ent><br/
<hw>Speckt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woodpecker. See
<er>Speight</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectacle</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"ta*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>spectaculum</ets>, fr. <ets>spectare</ets> to look at, to behold,
v. intens. fr. <ets>specere</ets>. See <er>Spy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Something exhibited to view; usually, something presented to view
as extraordinary, or as unusual and worthy of special notice; a
remarkable or noteworthy sight; a show; a pageant; a
gazingstock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, piteous <qex>spectacle</qex>? O, bloody times!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A spy-glass; a looking-glass.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Poverty a <qex>spectacle</qex> is, as thinketh me,<br/
Through which he may his very friends see.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>An optical instrument consisting of
two lenses set in a light frame, and worn to assist sight, to obviate
some defect in the organs of vision, or to shield the eyes from bright
light.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Fig.: An aid to the intellectual
sight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shakespeare . . . needed not the <qex>spectacles</qex> of books to
read nature.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Show; sight; exhibition; representation;
pageant.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectacled</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"ta*cled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Furnished with spectacles; wearing spectacles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As <qex>spectacled</qex> she sits in chimney nook.</q>
<rj><qau>Keats.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the eyes surrounded by
color markings, or patches of naked skin, resembling
spectacles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spectacled bear</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a South
American bear (<spn>Tremarclos ornatus</spn>) which inhabits the high
mountains of Chili and Peru. It has a light-colored ring around each
eye.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Spectacled coot</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Spectacled duck</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
surf scoter, or surf duck.</cd> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> --
<col><b>Spectacled eider</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Eider</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spectacled goose</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the gannet.</cd> -- <col><b>Spectacled
snake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the cobra de
capello.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectacular</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*tac"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to a shows; of the nature of a show.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Spectacular</xex> sports.<rdquo/ <rj><au>G.
Hickes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Adapted to excite wonder and admiration by a display
of pomp or of scenic effects; <as>as, a <ex>spectacular</ex> celebration
of some event; a <ex>spectacular</ex> play.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Pertaining to spectacles, or glasses for the
eyes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 4. Unusual and striking. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectant</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spectans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>spectare</ets> to look at.]</ety>
<def>Looking forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectation</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spectatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Regard; aspect; appearance.</def>
<rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectator</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*ta"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spectator</ets>: cf. F. <ets>spectateur</ets>. See
<er>Spectacle</er>.]</ety> <def>One who on; one who sees or beholds; a
beholder; one who is personally present at, and sees, any exhibition;
<as>as, the <ex>spectators</ex> at a show</as>.</def> <ldquo/Devised and
played to take <xex>spectators</xex>.<rdquo/ <-- an eyewitness -->
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Looker-on; beholder; observer; witness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectatorial</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`ta*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a spectator.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectatorship</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*ta"tor*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The office or quality of a spectator.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of beholding.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectatrix</ent><br/
<ent>Spectatress</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spec*ta"tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spec*ta"trix</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spectatrix</ets>.]</ety> <def>A female beholder or looker-on.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>spectatress</xex> of the whole scene.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Jeffrey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectre</ent><br/
<ent>Specter</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spec"ter</hw>, <hw>Spec"tre</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>spectre</ets>, fr. L. <ets>spectrum</ets> an
appearance, image, specter, fr. <ets>specere</ets> to look. See
<er>Spy</er>, and cf. <er>Spectrum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Something preternaturally visible; an apparition; a ghost; a
phantom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ghosts of traitors from the bridge descend,<br/
With bold fanatic <qex>specters</qex> to rejoice.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The tarsius.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A stick insect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Specter bat</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
phyllostome bat.</cd> -- <col><b>Specter candle</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a belemnite.</cd> -- <col><b>Specter
shrimp</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a skeleton shrimp. See under
<er>Skeleton</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectioneer</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`tion*eer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Specsioneer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectral</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a specter; ghosty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that feels timid at the <qex>spectral</qex> form of evil is not
the man to spread light.</q> <rj><qau>F. W. Robertson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the spectrum;
made by the spectrum; <as>as, <ex>spectral</ex> colors;
<ex>spectral</ex> analysis.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spectral lemur</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Tarsius</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrally</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"tral*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the form or manner of a
specter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectre</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"tre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Specter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrobolometer</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`tro*bo*lom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A combination of spectroscope and bolometer
for determining the distribution of energy in a spectrum.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spec`tro*bo`lo*met"ric</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectroelectric</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`tro*e*lec"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pert. to
or designating any form of spark tube the electric discharge within
which is used in spectroscopic observations.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrogram</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"tro*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spectrum</ets> + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A photograph, map, or diagram of a
spectrum.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrograph</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"tro*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spectrum</ets> + <ets>graph</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An apparatus for photographing or
mapping a spectrum.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A photograph or picture of a
spectrum.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Spec`tro*graph"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> -- <wf>Spec`tro*graph"ic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Spec*trog"ra*phy</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectroheliogram</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`tro*he"li*o*gram`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spectrum</ets> + <ets>heloi-</ets> + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Astrophysics)</fld> <def>A photograph of the sun made by
monochromatic light, usually of the calcium line (k), and showing the
sun's faculae and prominences.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectroheliograph</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`tro*he"li*o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Astrophysics)</fld> <def>An apparatus for making
spectroheliograms, consisting of a spectroscopic camera used in
combination with a telescope, and provided with clockwork for moving the
sun's image across the slit.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spec`tro*he`li*o*graph"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrological</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`tro*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to spectrology; <as>as, <ex>spectrological</ex> studies or
experiments</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Spec`tro*log"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrology</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*trol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spectrum</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.Phys.)</fld> <def>The science of spectrum analysis in any or
all of its relations and applications.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrometer</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*trom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spectrum</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A spectroscope fitted for measurements of the
luminious spectra observed with it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrometry</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*trom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Art or process of using a spectrometer, of any
type; -- when used alone, it usually refers to the use of a spectrometer
using light in the visible, infrared, or ultraviolet region.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spec`tro*met"ric</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>mass spectrometry</b></col>, <cd>the art or process of
using a mass spectrometer to analyse chemical samples.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrophone</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"tro*phone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spectrum</ets> + Gr. <?/ sound.]</ety> <def>An instrument
constructed on the principle of the photophone and used in spectrum
analysis as an adjunct to the spectroscope.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spec`tro*phon"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrophotometer</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`tro*pho*tom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spectrum</ets> + <ets>photometer</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>An instrument for measuring or comparing the
intensities of the colors of the spectrum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem., Physics)</fld> <def>An analytical instrument
for measuring the degree of absorption of light at different wavelengths
by a chemical substance. A specific pattern of absorption intensities at
a series of wavelengths is a characteristic of each different chemical
substance.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrophotometry</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`tro*pho*tom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The art of comparing, photometrically, the brightness of two
spectra, wave length by wave length; the use of the
spectrophotometer.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem., Physics)</fld> <def>the art or process of
measuring the degree of absorption of light at different wavelengths by
a chemical substance, by means of a spectrometer or spectrophotometer.
It is a technique for chemical analysis.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p> -- <wordforms><wf>Spec`tro*pho`to*met"ric</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectroscope</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"tro*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spectrum</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>An optical instrument for forming and
examining spectra (as that of solar light, or those produced by flames
in which different substances are volatilized), so as to determine, from
the position of the spectral lines, the composition of the
substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectroscopical</ent><br/
<ent>Spectroscopic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spec`tro*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Spec`tro*scop"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to a spectroscope, or spectroscopy.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spec`tro*scop"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectroscopist</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*tros"co*pist</hw> <pr>(? <or/ ?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who investigates by means of a spectroscope; one skilled in the use of
the spectroscope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectroscopy</ent><br/
<hw>Spec*tros"co*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>the art and
science dealing with the use of a spectroscope, and the production and
analysis of spectra; the action of using a spectroscope.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spectrum</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"trum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spectra</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. See
<er>Specter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An apparition; a
specter.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The several colored
and other rays of which light is composed, separated by the refraction
of a prism or other means, and observed or studied either as spread out
on a screen, by direct vision, by photography, or otherwise. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Light</er>, and <er>Spectroscope</er>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A luminous appearance, or an image seen after the eye
has been exposed to an intense light or a strongly illuminated object.
When the object is colored, the image appears of the complementary
color, as a green image seen after viewing a red wafer lying on white
paper. Called also <altname>ocular spectrum</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Absorption spectrum</b></col>, <cd>the spectrum of light
which has passed through a medium capable of absorbing a portion of the
rays. It is characterized by dark spaces, bands, or lines.</cd> --
<col><b>Chemical spectrum</b></col>, <cd>a spectrum of rays considered
solely with reference to their chemical effects, as in photography.
These, in the usual photogrophic methods, have their maximum influence
at and beyond the violet rays, but are not limited to this region.</cd>
-- <col><b>Chromatic spectrum</b></col>, <cd>the visible colored rays of
the solar spectrum, exhibiting the seven principal colors in their
order, and covering the central and larger portion of the space of the
whole spectrum.</cd> -- <col><b>Continous spectrum</b></col>, <cd>a
spectrum not broken by bands or lines, but having the colors shaded into
each other continously, as that from an incandescent solid or liquid, or
a gas under high pressure.</cd> -- <col><b>Diffraction
spectrum</b></col>, <cd>a spectrum produced by diffraction, as by a
grating.</cd> -- <col><b>Gaseous spectrum</b></col>, <cd>the spectrum of
an incandesoent gas or vapor, under moderate, or especially under very
low, pressure. It is characterized by bright bands or lines.</cd> --
<col><b>Normal spectrum</b></col>, <cd>a representation of a spectrum
arranged upon conventional plan adopted as standard, especially a
spectrum in which the colors are spaced proportionally to their wave
lengths, as when formed by a diffraction grating.</cd> -- <col><b>Ocular
spectrum</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Spectrum</er>, 2 <sd>(b)</sd>,
above.</cd> -- <col><b>Prismatic spectrum</b></col>, <cd>a spectrum
produced by means of a prism.</cd> -- <col><b>Solar spectrum</b></col>,
<cd>the spectrum of solar light, especially as thrown upon a screen in a
darkened room. It is characterized by numerous dark lines called
<xex>Fraunhofer lines</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spectrum
analysis</b></col>, <cd>chemical analysis effected by comparison of the
different relative positions and qualities of the fixed lines of spectra
produced by flames in which different substances are burned or
evaporated, each substance having its own characteristic system of
lines.</cd> -- <col><b>Thermal spectrum</b></col>, <cd>a spectrum of
rays considered solely with reference to their heating effect,
especially of those rays which produce no luminous
phenomena.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Specular</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>specularis</ets> (cf., from the same root, <ets>specula</ets> a
lookout, watchtower): cf. F. <ets>sp<eacute/culaire</ets>. See
<er>Speculum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the qualities of a
speculum, or mirror; having a smooth, reflecting surface; <as>as, a
<ex>specular</ex> metal; a <ex>specular</ex> surface.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a speculum;
conducted with the aid of a speculum; <as>as, a <ex>specular</ex>
examination</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Assisting sight, as a lens or the like.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy <qex>specular</qex> orb<br/
Apply to well-dissected kernels; lo!<br/
In each observe the slender threads<br/
Of first-beginning trees.</q> <rj><qau>J. Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Affording view.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Look
once more, ere we leave this <xex>specular</xex> mount.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Specular iron</b></col>. <fld>(Min.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Hematite</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculate</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Speculated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Speculating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>speculatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>speculari</ets> to spy out,
observe, fr. <ets>specula</ets> a lookout, fr. <ets>specere</ets> to
look. See <er>Spy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To consider by turning a
subject in the mind, and viewing it in its different aspects and
relations; to meditate; to contemplate; to theorize; <as>as, to
<ex>speculate</ex> on questions in religion; to <ex>speculate</ex> on
political events.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is remarkable that persons who <qex>speculate</qex> the most
boldly often conform with the most pefect quietude to the external
regulations of society.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Philos.)</fld> <def>To view subjects from certain
premises given or assumed, and infer conclusions respecting them <xex>a
priori</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>To purchase with the expectation
of a contingent advance in value, and a consequent sale at a profit; --
often, in a somewhat depreciative sense, of unsound or hazardous
transactions; <as>as, to <ex>speculate</ex> in coffee, in sugar, or in
bank stock</as>.</def> <-- (finance) --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculate</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*late</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To consider attentively;
<as>as, to <ex>speculate</ex> the nature of a thing</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculation</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>speculatio</ets> a spying out, observation: cf. F.
<ets>sp<eacute/culation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
speculating.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>Examination by the eye; view.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>Mental view of anything in its various aspects and
relations; contemplation; intellectual examination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thenceforth to <qex>speculations</qex> high or deep<br/
I turned my thoughts.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Philos.)</fld> <def>The act or process of
reasoning <it>a priori</it> from premises given or assumed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>The act or practice of buying
land, goods, shares, etc., in expectation of selling at a higher price,
or of selling with the expectation of repurchasing at a lower price; a
trading on anticipated fluctuations in price, as distinguished from
trading in which the profit expected is the difference between the
retail and wholesale prices, or the difference of price in different
markets.</def> <-- buying long is considered speculation only when the
time of holding the object is short. Longer-term trading (<gt/ 1 year)
is considered investment. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sudden fortunes, indeed, are sometimes made in such places, by
what is called the trade of <qex>speculation</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>A.
Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Speculation</qex>, while confined within moderate limits, is
the agent for equalizing supply and demand, and rendering the
fluctuations of price less sudden and abrupt than they would otherwise
be.</q> <rj><qau>F. A. Walker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <def>Any business venture in involving unusual risks,
with a chance for large profits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A conclusion to which the mind comes by speculating;
mere theory; view; notion; conjecture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From him Socrates derived the principles of morality, and most
part of his natural <qex>speculations</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To his <qex>speculations</qex> on these subjects he gave the lofty
name of the <ldquo/Oracles of Reason.<rdquo/</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Power of sight.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou hast no <qex>speculation</qex> in those eyes.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A game at cards in which the players buy from one
another trumps or whole hands, upon a chance of getting the highest
trump dealt, which entitles the holder to the pool of stakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculatist</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*la*tist</hw> <pr>(sp<ecr/k"<usl/*l<adot/*t<icr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who speculates, or forms theories; a speculator;
a theorist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The very ingenious <qex>speculatist</qex>, Mr. Hume.</q>
<rj><qau>V. Knox.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculative</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*la*tive</hw> <pr>(sp<ecr/k"<usl/*l<adot/*t<icr/v)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>sp<eacute/culatif</ets>, L.
<ets>speculativus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Given to speculation;
contemplative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind of man being by nature <qex>speculative</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Involving, or formed by, speculation; ideal;
theoretical; not established by demonstration.</def>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to vision; also, prying;
inquisitive; curious.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to speculation in land, goods,
shares, etc.; <as>as, a <ex>speculative</ex> dealer or
enterprise</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>speculative</qex> merchant exercises no one regular,
established, or well-known branch of business.</q> <rj><qau>A.
Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Finance)</fld> <def>More risky than typical
investments; not investment grade.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spec"u*la*tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spec"u*la*tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculator</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*la`tor</hw> <pr>(sp<ecr/k"<usl/*l<amac/`t<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a spy, explorer, investigator: cf. F.
<ets>sp<eacute/culateur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who speculates.
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> An observer; a contemplator; hence, a spy; a
watcher.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>One who forms theories; a theorist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>speculator</qex> who had dared to affirm that the human
soul is by nature mortal.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>One who engages in speculation;
one who buys and sells goods, land, etc., with the expectation of
deriving profit from fluctuations in price.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1382 --></p>

<p><ent>Speculatorial</ent><br/
<hw>Spec`u*la*to"ri*al</hw>
<pr>(sp<ecr/k`<usl/*l<adot/*t<omac/"r<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Speculatory; speculative.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculatory</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*la*to*ry</hw>
<pr>(sp<ecr/k"<usl/*l<adot/*t<osl/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>speculatorius</ets> belonging to spies or scouts.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Intended or adapted for viewing or espying; having
oversight.</def> <rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exercising speculation; speculative.</def>
<rj><au>T. Carew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculist</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*list</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who observes
or considers; an observer.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speculum</ent><br/
<hw>Spec"u*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Specula</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Speculum</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. <ets>specere</ets> to look, behold.
See <er>Spy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mirror, or looking-glass;
especially, a metal mirror, as in Greek and Roman archaeology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A reflector of polished metal, especially one used
in reflecting telescopes. See <cref>Speculum metal</cref>,
below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>An instrument for dilating
certain passages of the body, and throwing light within them, thus
facilitating examination or surgical operations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A bright and lustrous patch of
color found on the wings of ducks and some other birds. It is usually
situated on the distal portions of the secondary quills, and is much
more brilliant in the adult male than in the female.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Speculum metal</b></col>, <cd>a hard, brittle alloy used
for making the reflectors of telescopes and other instruments, usually
consisting of copper and tin in various proportions, one of the best
being that in which there are 126.4 parts of copper to 58.9 parts of
tin, with sometimes a small proportion of arsenic, antimony, or zinc
added to improve the whiteness.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sped</ent><br/
<hw>Sped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Speed</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speece</ent><br/
<hw>Speece</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Species; sort.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speech</ent><br/
<hw>Speech</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>speche</ets>,
AS. <ets>sp<?/c</ets>, <ets>spr<?/</ets>, fr. <ets>specan</ets>,
<ets>sprecan</ets>, to speak; akin to D. <ets>spraak</ets> speech, OHG.
<ets>spr<amac/hha</ets>, G. <ets>sprache</ets>, Sw. <ets>spr<?/k</ets>,
Dan. <ets>sprog</ets>. See <er>Speak</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
faculty of uttering articulate sounds or words; the faculty of
expressing thoughts by words or articulate sounds; the power of
speaking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is none comparable to the variety of instructive expressions
by <qex>speech</qex>, wherewith man alone is endowed for the
communication of his thoughts.</q> <rj><qau>Holder.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>he act of speaking; that which is spoken; words, as
expressing ideas; language; conversation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Speech is voice modulated by the throat, tongue, lips,
etc., the modulation being accomplished by changing the form of the
cavity of the mouth and nose through the action of muscles which move
their walls.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O goode God! how gentle and how kind<br/
Ye seemed by your <qex>speech</qex> and your visage<br/
The day that maked was our marriage.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The acts of God . . . to human ears<br/
Can nort without process of <qex>speech</qex> be told.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A particular language, as distinct from others; a
tongue; a dialect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>People of a strange <qex>speech</qex> and of an hard language.</q>
<rj><qau>Ezek. iii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Talk; mention; common saying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The duke . . . did of me demand<br/
What was the <qex>speech</qex> among the Londoners<br/
Concerning the French journey.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>formal discourse in public; oration;
harangue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The constant design of these orators, in all their
<qex>speeches</qex>, was to drive some one particular point.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Any declaration of thoughts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I. with leave of <qex>speech</qex> implored, . . . replied.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> Harangue; language; address; oration. See
<er>Harangue</er>, and <er>Language</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speech</ent><br/
<hw>Speech</hw>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To make a speech; to
harangue.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speechful</ent><br/
<hw>Speech"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of speech or
words; voluble; loquacious.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speechification</ent><br/
<hw>Speech`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[See
<er>Spechify</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of speechifying.</def>
<mark>[Used humorously or in contempt.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speechifier</ent><br/
<hw>Speech"i*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes a
speech or speeches; an orator; a declaimer.</def> <mark>[Used humorously
or in contempt.]</mark> <rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speechify</ent><br/
<hw>Speech"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Speechified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Speechifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[<ets>Speech</ets> + <ets>-fy</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make a speech;
to harangue.</def> <mark>[Used derisively or humorously.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speechifying</ent><br/
<hw>Speech"i*fy`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of making a speech
or speeches.</def> <mark>[Used derisively or humorously.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dinner and <qex>speechifying</qex> . . . at the opening of the
annual season for the buckhounds.</q> <rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speeching</ent><br/
<hw>Speech"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of making a
speech.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speechless</ent><br/
<hw>Speech"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Destitute or
deprived of the faculty of speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not speaking for a time; dumb; mute;
silent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Speechless</qex> with wonder, and half dead with fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Speech"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Speech"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speechmaker</ent><br/
<hw>Speech"mak`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes
speeches; one accustomed to speak in a public assembly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speed</ent><br/
<hw>Speed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sp<?/d</ets>
success, swiftness, from <ets>sp<?/wan</ets> to succeed; akin to D.
<ets>spoed</ets>d, OHG. <ets>spuot</ets> success, <ets>spuot</ets> to
succees, Skr. <ets>sph<amac/</ets> to increase, grow fat.
<root/170<it>b.</it>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Prosperity in an
undertaking; favorable issue; success.</def> <ldquo/For common
<xex>speed</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O Lord God of my master Abraham, I pray thee, send me good
<qex>speed</qex> this day.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xxiv. 12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or state of moving swiftly; swiftness;
velocity; rapidly; rate of motion; dispatch; <as>as, the <ex>speed</ex>
a horse or a vessel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Speed</qex>, to describe whose swiftness number fails.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In kinematics, <xex>speed</xex>is sometimes used to
denote the amount of velocity without regard to direction of motion,
while <xex>velocity</xex> is not regarded as known unless both the
direction and the amount are known.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, causes or promotes speed or
success.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Hercules be thy
<xex>speed</xex>!<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>God speed</b></col>, <cd>Good speed; prosperity. See
<er>Godspeed</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Speed gauge</b></col>,
<col><b>Speed indicator</b></col>, <and/ <col><b>Speed
recorder</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>devices for indicating
or recording the rate of a body's motion, as the number of revolutions
of a shaft in a given time.</cd> -- <col><b>Speed lathe</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a power lathe with a rapidly revolving spindle,
for turning small objects, for polishing, etc.; a hand lathe.</cd> --
<col><b>Speed pulley</b></col>, <cd>a cone pulley with
steps.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Haste; swiftness; celerity; quickness; dispatch;
expedition; hurry; acceleration. See <er>Haste</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speed</ent><br/
<hw>Speed</hw> <pr>(sp<emac/d)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sped</conjf> <pr>(sp<ecr/d)</pr>,
<conjf>Speeded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Speeding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>sp<emac/dan</ets>, fr.
<ets>sp<emac/d</ets>, n.; akin to D. <ets>spoeden</ets>, G. sich
<ets>sputen</ets>. See <er>Speed</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To go; to fare.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To warn him now he is too farre <qex>sped</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Remedy of Love.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To experience in going; to have any condition, good
or ill; to fare.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ships heretofore in seas like fishes <qex>sped</qex>;<br/
The mightiest still upon the smallest fed.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fare well; to have success; to prosper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Save London, and send true lawyers their meed!<br/
For whoso wants money with them shall not <qex>speed</qex>!</q>
<rj><qau>Lydgate.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I told ye then he should prevail, and <qex>speed</qex><br/
On his bad errand.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make haste; to move with celerity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have <qex>speeded</qex> hither with the very extremest inch of
possibility.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To be expedient.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wyclif (2 Cor. xii. 1.)</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speed</ent><br/
<hw>Speed</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to be
successful, or to prosper; hence, to aid; to favor.</def> <ldquo/Fortune
<xex>speed</xex> us!<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With rising gales that <qex>speed</qex> their happy flight.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to make haste; to dispatch with celerity;
to drive at full speed; hence, to hasten; to hurry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>sped</qex> him thence home to his habitation.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To hasten to a conclusion; to expedite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Judicial acts . . . are <qex>sped</qex> in open court at the
instance of one or both of the parties.</q>
<rj><qau>Ayliffe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To hurry to destruction; to put an end to; to ruin;
to undo.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sped</xex> with spavins.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A dire dilemma! either way I 'm <qex>sped</qex>.<br/
If foes, they write, if friends, they read, me dead.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To wish success or god fortune to, in any
undertaking, especially in setting out upon a journey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Welcome the coming, <qex>speed</qex> the parting guest.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>God speed you</b></col>,
<col><b>them</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc., may God speed you; or, may you
have good speed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To dispatch; hasten; expedite; accelerate;
hurry.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speed counter</ent><br/
<hw>Speed counter</hw>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A device for
automatically counting the revolutions or pulsations of an engine or
other machine; -- called also simply
<altname>counter</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speeder</ent><br/
<hw>Speed"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, speeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Spinning)</fld> <def>A machine for drawing and
twisting slivers to form rovings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speedful</ent><br/
<hw>Speed"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of speed (in
any sense).</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speedfully</ent><br/
<hw>Speed"ful*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a speedful manner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speedily</ent><br/
<hw>Speed"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a speedy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speediness</ent><br/
<hw>Speed"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
speedy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speedless</ent><br/
<hw>Speed"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being without speed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speedwell</ent><br/
<hw>Speed"well</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Veronica</gen>, mostly low herbs with
pale blue corollas, which quickly fall off.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speedy</ent><br/
<hw>Speed"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Speedier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Speediest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sp<emac/dyg</ets>.]</ety> <def>Not dilatory or slow; quick; swift;
nimble; hasty; rapid in motion or performance; <as>as, a <ex>speedy</ex>
flight; on <ex>speedy</ex> foot.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will wish her <qex>speedy</qex> strength.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Darts, which not the good could shun,<br/
The <qex>speedy</qex> ould outfly.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speer</ent><br/
<hw>Speer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sphere.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speer</ent><br/
<hw>Speer</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To ask.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<see>See <er>Spere</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speet</ent><br/
<hw>Speet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>speten</ets>. See <er>Spit</er> an iron prong.]</ety> <def>To
stab.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gammer Gurton's
Needle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speight</ent><br/
<hw>Speight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G. <ets>specht</ets>,
probably akin to L. <ets>picus</ets>: cf. D. <ets>specht</ets>.
<root/169. See <er>Pie</er> a magpie.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
woodpecker; -- called also <altname>specht</altname>,
<altname>spekt</altname>, <altname>spight</altname>.</def> <mark>[Obs.
or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speir</ent><br/
<hw>Speir</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To ask. See
<er>Spere</er>.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speiskobalt</ent><br/
<hw>Speis`ko"balt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.]</ety>
<def>Smaltite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speiss</ent><br/
<hw>Speiss</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>speise</ets> food, mixed metal for bells, etc.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>A regulus consisting essentially of nickel,
obtained as a residue in fusing cobalt and nickel ores with silica and
sodium carbonate to make smalt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>Impure metallic arsenides,
principally of iron, produced in copper and lead smelting.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spekboom</ent><br/
<hw>Spek"boom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D., lit. fat
tree.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The purslane tree of South Africa,
-- said to be the favorite food of elephants.</def> <rj><au>Balfour
(Cyc. of India).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speke</ent><br/
<hw>Speke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To speak.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spekehouse</ent><br/
<hw>Speke"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The parlor or
reception room of a convent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelding</ent><br/
<hw>Spel"ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot.
<ets>speld</ets> to spread out, <ets>spelder</ets> to split. spread
open; cf. G. <ets>spalten</ets> split.]</ety> <def>A haddock or other
small fish split open and dried in the sun; -- called also
<altname>speldron</altname>.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelicans</ent><br/
<hw>Spel"i*cans</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spilikin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelk</ent><br/
<hw>Spelk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>spelc</ets>,
<ets>spilc</ets>, a little rod by which a thing is kept straight, a
splint for binding up broken bones, akin to Icel. <ets>spelkur</ets>,
pl., a splint. Cf. <er>Spell</er> a splinter.]</ety> <def>A small stick
or rod used as a spike in thatching; a splinter.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spell</ent><br/
<hw>Spell</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>speld</ets>,
AS. <ets>speld</ets> a spill to light a candle with; akin to D.
<ets>speld</ets> a pin, OD. <ets>spelle</ets>, G. <ets>spalten</ets> to
split, OHG. <ets>spaltan</ets>, MHG. <ets>spelte</ets> a splinter, Icel.
<ets>spjald</ets> a square tablet, Goth. <ets>spilda</ets> a writing
tablet. Cf. <er>Spill</er>splinter, roll of paper, <er>Spell</er> to
tell the letters of.]</ety> <def>A spelk, or splinter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spell</ent><br/
<hw>Spell</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spelled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>spelian</ets> to
supply another's place.]</ety> <def>To supply the place of for a time;
to take the turn of, at work; to relieve; <as>as, to <ex>spell</ex> the
helmsman</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spell</ent><br/
<hw>Spell</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The relief of one person
by another in any piece of work or watching; also, a turn at work which
is carried on by one person or gang relieving another; <as>as, a
<ex>spell</ex> at the pumps; a <ex>spell</ex> at the
masthead</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>spell</qex> at the wheel is called a trick.</q>
<rj><qau>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The time during which one person or gang works until
relieved; hence, any relatively short period of time, whether a few
hours, days, or weeks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing new has happened in this quarter, except the setting in of
a severe <qex>spell</qex> of cold weather.</q>
<rj><qau>Washington.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One of two or more persons or gangs who work by
spells.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their toil is so extreme that they can not endure it above four
hours in a day, but are succeeded by <qex>spells</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Garew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A gratuitous helping forward of another's work;
<as>as, a logging <ex>spell</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spell</ent><br/
<hw>Spell</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[AS. <ets>spell</ets> a
saying, tale, speech; akin to OS. & OHG. <ets>spel</ets>, Icel.
<ets>spjall</ets>,Goth. <ets>spill</ets>. Cf. <er>Gospel</er>,
<er>Spell</er> to tell the letters of.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A story;
a tale.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Hearken to my
<xex>spell</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stanza, verse, or phrase supposed to be endowed
with magical power; an incantation; hence, any charm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q> Start not; her actions shall be holy as<br/
You hear my <qex>spell</qex> is lawful.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spell</ent><br/
<hw>Spell</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spelled</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr> or <conjf>Spelt</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>spellen</ets>,
<ets>spellien</ets>, tell, relate, AS. <ets>spellian</ets>, fr.
<ets>spell</ets> a saying, tale; akin to MHG. <ets>spellen</ets> to
relate, Goth. <ets>spill<?/n</ets>.e <er>Spell</er> a tale. In sense 4
and those following, OE. <ets>spellen</ets>, perhaps originally a
different word, and from or influenced by <ets>spell</ets> a splinter,
from the use of a piece of wood to point to the letters in schools: cf.
D. <ets>spellen</ets> to spell. Cf. <er>Spell</er> splinter.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To tell; to relate; to teach.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Might I that legend find,<br/
By fairies <qex>spelt</qex> in mystic rhymes.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Warton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put under the influence of a spell; to affect by
a spell; to bewitch; to fascinate; to charm.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Spelled</xex> with words of power.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was much <qex>spelled</qex> with Eleanor Talbot.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir G. Buck.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To constitute; to measure.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Saxon heptarchy, when seven kings put together did
<qex>spell</qex> but one in effect.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To tell or name in their proper order letters of, as
a word; to write or print in order the letters of, esp. the proper
letters; to form, as words, by correct orthography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The word <ldquo/satire<rdquo/ ought to be <qex>spelled</qex> with
<it>i</it>, and not with <it>y</it>.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To discover by characters or marks; to read with
difficulty; -- usually with <xex>out</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>spell</ex>
out the sense of an author; to <ex>spell</ex> out a verse in the
Bible.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>spell</qex> out a God in the works of creation.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To sit <qex>spelling</qex> and observing divine justice upon every
accident.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spell</ent><br/
<hw>Spell</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To form words with
letters, esp. with the proper letters, either orally or in
writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When what small knowledge was, in them did dwell,<br/
And he a god, who could but read or <qex>spell</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To study by noting characters; to gain knowledge or
learn the meaning of anything, by study.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where I may sit and rightly <qex>spell</qex><br/
Of every star that heaven doth shew,<br/
And every herb that sips the dew.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spellable</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
spelt.</def> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spellbind</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"bind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spellbound</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Spellbinding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To bind or hold by,
or as if by, a spell or charm; to entrance or fascinate, esp. by
eloquence of speech, as in a political campaign.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spell"bind`er</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spellbound</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"bound`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bound by, or as
by, a spell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Speller</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
spells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A spelling book.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spellful</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in spells,
or charms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here, while his eyes the learned leaves peruse,<br/
Each <qex>spellful</qex> mystery explained he views.</q>
<rj><qau>Hoole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelling</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of one who spells;
formation of words by letters; orthography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelling</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
spelling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spelling bee</b></col>, <cd>a spelling match.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Spelling book</b></col>, <cd>a book with
exercises for teaching children to spell; a speller.</cd> --
<col><b>Spelling match</b></col>, <cd>a contest of skill in spelling
words, between two or more persons.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spellken</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"ken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A theater.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spellwork</ent><br/
<hw>Spell"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Power or effect of
magic; that which is wrought by magic; enchantment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like those Peri isles of light<br/
That hang by <qex>spellwork</qex> in the air.</q>
<rj><qau>Moore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelt</ent><br/
<hw>Spelt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Spell</er>. Spelled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelt</ent><br/
<hw>Spelt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>spelt</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>spelta</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A species of grain
(<spn>Triticum Spelta</spn>) much cultivated for food in Germany and
Switzerland; -- called also <altname>German wheat</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelt</ent><br/
<hw>Spelt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spalt</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>Spelter.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelt</ent><br/
<hw>Spelt</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spell</er> a
splinter.]</ety> <def>To split; to break; to spalt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelter</ent><br/
<hw>Spel"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LG.
<ets>spialter</ets>, G. & D. <ets>spiauter</ets>. Cf.
<er>Pewter</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>Zinc; -- especially so
called in commerce and arts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spelunc</ent><br/
<hw>Spe*lunc"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spelunca</ets> cave.]</ety> <def>A cavern; a cave.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spence</ent><br/
<hw>Spence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>despense</ets>, F. <ets>d<eacute/pense</ets>, buffet, buttery, fr.
OF. <ets>despendre</ets> to spend, distribute, L. <ets>dispendere</ets>,
<ets>dispensum</ets>. See <er>Dispense</er>, <er>Spend</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A place where provisions are kept; a buttery; a larder;
a pantry.</def> <-- <mark>Chiefly Brit. dial. [MW10]</mark> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In . . . his <qex>spence</qex>, or <ldquo/pantry<rdquo/ were hung
the carcasses of a sheep or ewe, and two cows lately slaughtered.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bluff Harry broke into the <qex>spence</qex>,<br/
And turned the cowls adrift.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The inner apartment of a country house; also, the
place where the family sit and eat.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spencer</ent><br/
<hw>Spen"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>despensier</ets>. See <er>Spence</er>, and cf.
<er>Dispenser</er>.]</ety> <def>One who has the care of the spence, or
buttery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Promptorium
Parvulorum.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spencer</ent><br/
<hw>Spen"cer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the third Earl
<ets>Spencer</ets>, who first wore it, or brought it into
fashion.]</ety> <def>A short jacket worn by men and by women.</def>
<rj><au>Ld. Lutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spencer</ent><br/
<hw>Spen"cer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A fore-and-aft
sail, abaft the foremast or the mainmast, hoisted upon a small
supplementary mast and set with a gaff and no boom; a trysail carried at
the foremast or mainmast; -- named after its inventor, Knight
<xex>Spencer</xex>, of England [1802].</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spencer mast</b></col>, <cd>a small mast just abaft the
foremast or mainmast, for hoisting the spencer.</cd> <au>R. H. Dana,
Jr.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spend</ent><br/
<hw>Spend</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spent</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>spendan</ets> (in
comp.), fr. L. <ets>expendere</ets> or <ets>dispendere</ets> to weigh
out, to expend, dispense. See <er>Pendant</er>, and cf.
<er>Dispend</er>, <er>Expend</er>, <er>Spence</er>,
<er>Spencer</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To weigh or lay out; to
dispose of; to part with; <as>as, to <ex>spend</ex> money for
clothing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Spend</qex> thou that in the town.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wherefore do ye <qex>spend</qex> money for that which is not
bread?</q> <rj><qau>Isa. lv. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1383 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bestow; to employ; -- often with <xex>on</xex> or
<xex>upon</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I . . . am never loath<br/
To <qex>spend</qex> my judgment.</q> <rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To consume; to waste; to squander; to exhaust;
<as>as, to <ex>spend</ex> an estate in gaming or other
vices</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To pass, as time; to suffer to pass away; <as>as, to
<ex>spend</ex> a day idly; to <ex>spend</ex> winter
abroad</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>spend</qex> our years as a tale that is told.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. xc. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To exhaust of force or strength; to waste; to wear
away; <as>as, the violence of the waves was
<ex>spent</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their bodies <qex>spent</qex> with long labor and thirst.</q>
<rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spend</ent><br/
<hw>Spend</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To expend
money or any other possession; to consume, use, waste, or part with,
anything; <as>as, he who gets easily <ex>spends</ex>
freely</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>spends</qex> as a person who knows that he must come to a
reckoning.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To waste or wear away; to be consumed; to lose force
or strength; to vanish; <as>as, energy <ex>spends</ex> in the using of
it</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sound <qex>spendeth</qex> and is dissipated in the open
air.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be diffused; to spread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The vines that they use for wine are so often cut, that their sap
<qex>spendeth</qex> into the grapes.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>To break ground; to continue
working.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spender</ent><br/
<hw>Spen"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who spends; esp.,
one who spends lavishly; a prodigal; a spendthrift.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spending</ent><br/
<hw>Spend"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of expending;
expenditure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spending money</b></col>, <cd>money set apart for extra
(not necessary) personal expenses; pocket money.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spendthrift</ent><br/
<hw>Spend"thrift`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who spends
money profusely or improvidently; a prodigal; one who lavishes or wastes
his estate. Also used figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A woman who was a generous <qex>spendthrift</qex> of life.</q>
<rj><qau>Mrs. R. H. Davis.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spendthrift</ent><br/
<hw>Spend"thrift</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Prodigal; extravagant;
wasteful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spendthrifty</ent><br/
<hw>Spend"thrift`y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Spendthrift;
prodigal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spenserian</ent><br/
<hw>Spen*se"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to the English poet <ets>Spenser</ets>; -- specifically applied to the
stanza used in his poem <ldquo/The Fa<eum/rie Queene.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spent</ent><br/
<hw>Spent</hw> <pr>(sp<ecr/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Exhausted; worn out; having lost energy or motive force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now thou seest me<br/
<qex>Spent</qex>, overpowered, despairing of success.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Heaps of <qex>spent</qex> arrows fall and strew the ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Exhausted of spawn or sperm; --
said especially of fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spent ball</b></col>, <cd>a ball shot from a firearm,
which reaches an object without having sufficient force to penetrate
it.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperre</ent><br/
<ent>Sper</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sper</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sperre</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>v.
t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spar</er> bar.]</ety> <def>To shut in; to
support; to inclose; to fasten.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/To
<xex>sperre</xex> the gate.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperable</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"ra*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spearabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>sperare</ets> to hope.]</ety>
<def>Within the range of hope; proper to be hoped for.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperable</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sperable</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperage</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Asperagus.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sylvester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperate</ent><br/
<hw>Spe"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>speratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sperare</ets> to hope.]</ety>
<def>Hoped for, or to be hoped for.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spere</ent><br/
<hw>Spere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>spyrian</ets> to inquire, properly, to follow the track; akin to D.
<ets>speuren</ets>, G. <ets>sp<uum/ren</ets>, Icel. <ets>spyrja</ets>.
<root/171. See Spoor.]</ety> <def>To search; to pry; to ask; to
inquire.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>speer</asp>, <asp>speir</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spere</ent><br/
<hw>Spere</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sphere</er>.]</ety> <def>A
sphere.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperge</ent><br/
<hw>Sperge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Distilling)</fld>
<def>A charge of wash for the still.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperling</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sparling</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A smelt;
a sparling.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A young
herring.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperm</ent><br/
<hw>Sperm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[F. <ets>sperme</ets>, L.
<ets>sperma</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/, from <?/<?/<?/<?/ to sow.
Cf. <er>Spore</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The male
fecundating fluid; semen. See <er>Semen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sperm cell</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>one of
the cells from which the spermatozoids are developed.</cd> --
<col><b>Sperm morula</b></col>. <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Spermosphere</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperm</ent><br/
<hw>Sperm</hw>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[Contr. fr.
<ets>spermaceti</ets>.]</ety> <def>Spermaceti.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sperm oil</b></col>, <cd>a fatty oil found as a liquid,
with spermaceti, in the head cavities of the sperm whale.</cd> --
<col><b>Sperm whale</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See in the
Vocabulary.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermaceti</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`ma*ce"ti</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sperma</ets> sperm + <ets>cetus</ets>,gen. <ets>ceti</ets>, any
large sea animal, a whale, Gr. <?/<?/<?/. See <er>Sperm</er>,
<er>Cetaceous</er>.]</ety> <def>A white waxy substance obtained from
cavities in the head of the sperm whale, and used making candles,
oilments, cosmetics, etc. It consists essentially of ethereal salts of
palmitic acid with ethal and other hydrocarbon bases. The substance of
spermaceti after the removal of certain impurities is sometimes called
<xex>cetin</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spermaceti whale</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
sperm whale.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermalist</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mal*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Spermist</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermaphore</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ sperm + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>That part of the ovary from which the ovules
arise; the placenta.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermary</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>An organ in which spermatozoa are developed; a sperm gland; a
testicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatheca</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper`ma*the"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spermathecae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
from Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ seed + <?/<?/<?/<?/ case, or receptacle.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small sac connected with the female
reproductive organs of insects and many other invertebrates, serving to
receive and retain the spermatozoa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sper*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spermaticus</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/: cf. F. <ets>spermatique</ets>.
See <er>Sperm</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to semen; <as>as, the <ex>spermatic</ex> fluid, the <ex>spermatic</ex>
vessels, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spermatic cord</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the cord
which suspends the testicle within the scrotum. It is made up of a
connective tissue sheath inclosing the spermatic duct and accompanying
vessels and nerves.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatical</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mat"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Spermatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatin</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*tin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A substance allied to alkali albumin and to mucin,
present in semen, to which it is said to impart the mucilaginous
character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatism</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>The emission of sperm, or semen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper*ma"ti*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spermatia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of the motionless
spermatozoids in the conceptacles of certain fungi.</def> <rj><au>J. H.
Balfour.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatize</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/. See <er>Sperm</er>.]</ety> <def>To yield seed; to emit seed,
or sperm.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermo-</ent><br/
<ent>Spermato-</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sper"ma*to-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sper"mo-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>
}</mhw>. <def>Combining forms from Gr. <grk>spe`rma</grk>,
<grk>-atos</grk>, <xex>seed</xex>, <xex>sperm</xex>, <xex>semen</xex>
(of plants or animals); <as>as, <ex>spermato</ex>blast,
<ex>spermo</ex>blast</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatoblast</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*to*blast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Spermoblast</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatocyte</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*to*cyte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermato-</ets> + Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ a hollow vessel.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Spermoblast</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatogemma</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`ma*to*gem"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Spermato-</er>, and <er>Gemma</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Spermosphere</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatogenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`ma*to*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermato-</ets> + <ets>genesis</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The development of the spermatozoids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatogenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`ma*to*ge*net"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Relating to, or connected with,
spermatogenesis; <as>as, <ex>spermatogenetic</ex>
function</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatogenous</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`ma*tog"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermato-</ets> + <ets>-genous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Sperm-producing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatogonium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper`ma*to*go"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/, sperm + <?/<?/<?/ offspring.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A primitive seminal cell, occurring in masses
in the seminal tubules. It divides into a mass (spermosphere) of small
cells (spermoblast), which in turn give rise to spermatozoids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermato-</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Spermlike; resembling sperm, or
semen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatoon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper`ma*to"on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spermatoa</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>spe`rma</grk>, <grk>-atos</grk>, seed + <?/<?/<?/ an
egg.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A spermoblast.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sper`ma*to"al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<au>Owen.</au></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatophore</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*to*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermato-</ets> + Gr. <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Spermospore</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A capsule or pocket inclosing a
number of spermatozoa. They are present in many annelids, brachiopods,
mollusks, and crustaceans. In cephalopods the structure of the capsule
is very complex.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatophorous</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`ma*toph"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Producing seed, or sperm; seminiferous;
<as>as, the so-called <ex>spermatophorous</ex> cells</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatophyta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper`ma*toph"y*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.; <ets>spermato-</ets> + Gr. <?/ plant.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A phylum embracing the highest plants, or those
that produce seeds; the seed plants, or flowering plants. They form the
most numerous group, including over 120,000 species. In general, the
group is characterized by the marked development of the sporophyte, with
great differentiation of its parts (root, stem, leaves, flowers, etc.);
by the extreme reduction of the gametophyte; and by the development of
seeds. All the Spermatophyta are heterosporous; fertilization of the egg
cell is either through a <col><b>pollen tube</b></col> emitted by the
microspore or (in a few gymnosperms) by spermatozoids.</def> <note>The
phrase <ldquo/flowering plants<rdquo/ is less distinctive than
<ldquo/seed plants,<rdquo/ since the conifers, grasses, sedges, oaks,
etc., do not produce flowers in the popular sense. For this reason the
terms <altname>Anthrophyta</altname>, <altname>Phaenogamia</altname>,
and <altname>Panerogamia</altname> have been superseded as names of the
phylum by <ex>Spermatophyta</ex>.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatophyte</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*to"phyte`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any plant of
the phylum Spermatophyta.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sper`ma*to*phyt"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatorrhoea</ent><br/
<ent>Spermatorrhea</ent><br/
<mhw>{<hw>Sper`ma*tor*rhe"a</hw>, <hw>Sper`ma*tor*rhoe"a</hw>,
<pr>(<?/)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>spe`rma</grk>, <grk>-atos</grk>, seed + <?/<?/<?/ to flow.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Abnormally frequent involuntary emission of the
semen without copulation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatospore</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ma*to*spore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Spermospore</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatozoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`ma*to*zo"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermatozoon</ets> + Gr. <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The male germ cell in animals and plants, the
essential element in fertilization; a microscopic animalcule-like
particle, usually provided with one or more cilia by which it is capable
of active motion. In animals, the familiar type is that of a small, more
or less ovoid head, with a delicate threadlike cilium, or tail. Called
also <altname>spermatozoon</altname>. In plants the more usual term is
<stype>antherozoid</stype>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatozooid</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`ma*to*zo"oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>A spermatozoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermatozoon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper`ma*to*zo"'\'94n</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spermatozoa</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/, sperm + <grk>zw^,on</grk> an animal.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Spermatozoid</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermic</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
sperm, or semen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermidium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper*mid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spermidia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Nl.,
fr. Gr. <grk>spe`rma</grk> seed.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An
achenium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermism</ent><br/
<hw>Sperm"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ seed, sperm
+ <ets>-ism</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The theory, formerly
held by many, that the sperm or spermatozoon contains the germ of the
future embryo; animalculism.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermist</ent><br/
<hw>Sperm"ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
believer in the doctrine, formerly current, of encasement in the male
(see <er>Encasement</er>), in which the seminal thread, or spermatozoid,
was considered as the real animal germ, the head being the true animal
head and the tail the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermoblast</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mo*blast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermo-</ets> + <ets>-blast</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>One of the cells formed by the division of
the spermospore, each of which is destined to become a spermatozoid; a
spermatocyte; a spermatoblast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermococcus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper`mo*coc"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Spermo-</er>, and <er>Coccus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>The nucleus of the sperm cell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermoderm</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mo*derm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermo-</ets> + <ets>derm</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>spermoderme</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The covering of a
seed; -- sometimes limited to the outer coat or testa.</def>
<rj><au>Lindley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermogonium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper`mo*go"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.;
<ets>spermo-</ets> + Gr. <?/<?/<?/ offspring.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A conceptacle of certain lichens, which contains
spermatia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermologist</ent><br/
<hw>Sper*mol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ picking up seeds; <grk>spe`rma</grk> sperm, seed +
<?/<?/<?/<?/ to gather.]</ety> <def>One who treats of, or collects,
seeds.</def> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermophile</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mo*phile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>spe`rma</grk> a seed + <grk>fi`los</grk> loving, fond.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any ground squirrel of the genus
<gen>Spermophilus</gen>; a gopher. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Gopher</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermophore</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mo*phore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A spermatophore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermophyta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper*moph"y*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Nl.,
from Gr. <grk>spe`rma</grk> a seed + <grk>fyto`n</grk> a plant.]</ety>
<def>Plants which produce seed; phaenogamia. These plants constitute the
highest grand division of the vegetable kingdom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermophyte</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mo*phyte</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant which produces true seeds; -- a term recently proposed to
replace <xex>ph<ae/nogam</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermophytic</ent><br/
<hw>Sper`mo*phyt"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Capable of producing seeds; ph<ae/nogamic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermoplasma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sper`mo*plas"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Spermo-</er>, and <er>Plasma</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>The protoplasm of the sperm cell.</def>
<rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermosphere</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mo*sphere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermo-</ets> + <ets>sphere</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A mass or ball of cells formed by the
repeated division of a male germinal cell (spermospore), each
constituent cell (spermoblast) of which is converted into a
spermatozoid; a spermatogemma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermospore</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mo*spore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spermo-</ets> + <ets>spore</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The male germinal or seminal cell, from the
breaking up of which the spermoblasts are formed and ultimately the
spermatozoids; a spermatospore.</def> <rj><au>Balfour.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spermule</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"mule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. fr.
<ets>sperm</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A sperm cell.</def>
<rj><au>Haeckel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperm whale</ent><br/
<hw>Sperm" whale`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A very
large toothed whale (<spn>Physeter macrocephalus</spn>), having a head
of enormous size. The upper jaw is destitute of teeth. In the upper part
of the head, above the skull, there is a large cavity, or case, filled
with oil and spermaceti. This whale sometimes grows to the length of
more than eighty feet. It is found in the warmer parts of all the
oceans. Called also <altname>cachalot</altname>, and <altname>spermaceti
whale</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pygmy sperm whale</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
small whale (<spn>Kogia breviceps</spn>), seldom twenty feet long,
native of tropical seas, but occasionally found on the American coast.
Called also <altname>snub-nosed cachalot</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sperm-whale porpoise</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a toothed
cetacean (<spn>Hyperoodon bidens</spn>), found on both sides of the
Atlantic and valued for its oil. The adult becomes about twenty-five
feet long, and its head is very large and thick. Called also
<altname>bottle-nosed whale</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperrylite</ent><br/
<hw>Sper"ry*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[Named after F. L.
<ets>Sperry</ets>, who discovered it.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>An
arsenide of platinum occurring in grains and minute isometric crystals
of tin-white color. It is found near Sudbury, Ontario Canada, and is the
only known compound of platinum occurring in nature.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sperse</ent><br/
<hw>Sperse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To disperse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spessartite</ent><br/
<hw>Spes"sart*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[From
<ets>Spessart</ets>, in Germany.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
manganesian variety of garnet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spet</ent><br/
<hw>Spet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sp<?/tan</ets>. See <er>Spit</er>.]</ety> <def>To spit; to throw
out.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spet</ent><br/
<hw>Spet</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Spittle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spetches</ent><br/
<hw>Spetch"es</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Parings and
refuse of hides, skins, etc., from which glue is made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spew</ent><br/
<hw>Spew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spewed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spewing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>spewen</ets>,
<ets>speowen</ets>, AS. <ets>sp<imac/wan</ets>;n to D. <ets>spuwen</ets>
to spit. OS & OHG. <ets>sp<imac/wan</ets>, G. <ets>speien</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sp<?/ja</ets> to spew, Sw. <ets>spy</ets>, Dan. <ets>spye</ets>,
Goth. <ets>spiewan</ets>, th. <ets>spjauti</ets>, L. <ets>spuere</ets>
to split, Gr. <?/<?/<?/, Skr. <ets>shtiv</ets>, <ets>shth<imac/v</ets>.
Cf. <er>Pyke</er>, <er>Spit</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>spue</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To eject from the stomach; to
vomit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cast forth with abhorrence or disgust; to
eject.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Because thou art lukewarm, and neither hot nor cold, I will
<qex>spew</qex> thee out of my mouth.</q> <rj><qau>Rev. ii.
16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spew</ent><br/
<hw>Spew</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To vomit.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To eject seed, as wet land swollen with
frost.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spew</ent><br/
<hw>Spew</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is vomited;
vomit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spewer</ent><br/
<hw>Spew"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
spews.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spewiness</ent><br/
<hw>Spew"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
spewy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spewy</ent><br/
<hw>Spew"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wet; soggy; inclined to
spew.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphacel</ent><br/
<hw>Sphac"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/: cf.
F. <ets>sphac<egrave/le</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Gangrene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphacelate</ent><br/
<hw>Sphac"e*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sphacelated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sphacelating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[NL.
<ets>sphacelare</ets>, <ets>sphacelatum</ets>, mortify: cf. F.
<ets>sphac<eacute/ler</ets>. See <er>Sphacelus</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To die, decay, or become gangrenous, as flesh or
bone; to mortify.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphacelate</ent><br/
<hw>Sphac"e*late</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To affect
with gangrene.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphacelated</ent><br/
<ent>Sphacelate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sphac"e*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sphac"e*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Affected with
gangrene; mortified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1384 --></p>

<p><ent>Sphacelation</ent><br/
<hw>Sphac`e*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The process of becoming or making gangrenous;
mortification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphacelus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sphac"e*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Gangrenous part; gangrene;
slough.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphaerenchyma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sphae*ren"chy*ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/<?/<?/ sphere + <ets>-enchyma</ets> as in
<ets>parenchima</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Vegetable tissue
composed of thin-walled rounded cells, -- a modification of
parenchyma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphaeridium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sphae*rid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sphaeridia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a sphere.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A peculiar
sense organ found upon the exterior of most kinds of sea urchins, and
consisting of an oval or spherical head surmounting a short pedicel. It
is generally supposed to be an olfactory organ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphaerospore</ent><br/
<hw>Sphae"ro*spore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ sphere + E. <ets>spore</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>One of the nonsexual spores found in red algae; a
tetraspore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphaerulite</ent><br/
<hw>Sphaer"u*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Spherulite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphagnicolous</ent><br/
<hw>Sphag*nic"o*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sphagnum</ets> + L. <ets>colere</ets> to inhabit.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Growing in moss of the genus
<gen>Sphagnum</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphagnous</ent><br/
<hw>Sphag"nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to moss of the genus <gen>Sphagnum</gen>, or bog moss;
abounding in peat or bog moss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphagnum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sphag"num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr/ Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ a kind of moss.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of
mosses having white leaves slightly tinged with red or green and found
growing in marshy places; bog moss; peat moss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphalerite</ent><br/
<hw>Sphal"er*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
slippery, uncertain. See <er>Blende</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Zinc sulphide; -- called also <altname>blende</altname>,
<altname>black-jack</altname>, <altname>false galena</altname>, etc. See
<er>Blende</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphene</ent><br/
<hw>Sphene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sph<egrave/ne</ets>, fr. Gr. <grk>sfh`n</grk> a wedge.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A mineral found usually in thin, wedge-shaped
crystals of a yellow or green to black color. It is a silicate of
titanium and calcium; titanite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenethmoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*neth"moid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sphen</ets>oid + <ets>ethmoid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to both the sphenoidal and the
ethmoidal regions of the skull, or the sphenethmoid bone;
sphenethmoidal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sphenethmoid bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a
bone of the skull which surrounds the anterior end of the brain in many
amphibia; the girdle bone.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenethmoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*neth"moid</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
sphenethmoid bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenethmoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe`neth*moid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Relating to the sphenoethmoid bone;
sphenoethmoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheniscan</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*nis"can</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of penguin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheno-</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"no-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form used in anatomy to
indicate <xex>connection with</xex>, or <xex>relation to</xex>, <xex>the
sphenoid bone</xex>; as in <xex>spheno</xex>maxillary,
<xex>spheno</xex>palatine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenodon</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"no*don</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sfh`n</grk> a wedge + <?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/, a tooth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Hatteria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenoethmoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"no*eth*moid`al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Sphenethmoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenogram</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"no*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sfh`n</grk> a wedge + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety> <def>A cuneiform, or
arrow-headed, character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenographer</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*nog"ra*pher</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled in
sphenography; a sphenographist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenographic</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe`no*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to sphenography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenographist</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*nog"ra*phist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
sphenographer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenography</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sfh`n</grk> a wedge + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art of
writing in cuneiform characters, or of deciphering inscriptions made in
such characters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/;
<grk>sfh`n</grk> a wedge + <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form: cf. F.
<ets>sph<eacute/no<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Wedge-shaped;
<as>as, a <ex>sphenoid</ex> crystal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the sphenoid
bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sphenoid bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>an
irregularly shaped bone in front of the occipital in the base of the
skull of the higher vertebrates. It is composed of several fetal bones
which become united the adult. See <er>Alisphenoid</er>,
<er>Basisphenoid</er>, <er>Orbitosphenoid</er>,
<er>Presphenoid</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>A wedge-shaped crystal bounded by four
equal isosceles triangles. It is the hemihedral form of a square
pyramid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The sphenoid bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*noid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Sphenoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Crystalloq.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or
resembling, a sphenoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenotic</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*not"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spheno-</ets> + <?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/, the ear.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, the sphenotic
bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sphenotic bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a bone
on the anterior side of the auditory capsule of many fishes, and
connected with, or adjoining, the sphenoid bone.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphenotic</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*not"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
sphenotic bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheral</ent><br/
<hw>Spher"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to a sphere or the spheres.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rounded like a sphere; sphere-shaped; hence,
symmetrical; complete; perfect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphere</ent><br/
<hw>Sphere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spere</ets>,
OF. <ets>espere</ets>, F. <ets>sph<egrave/re</ets>, L.
<ets>sphaera</ets>,. Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a sphere, a ball.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A body or space contained under a single
surface, which in every part is equally distant from a point within
called its <xex>center</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, any globe or globular body, especially a
celestial one, as the sun, a planet, or the earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of celestial bodies, first the sun,<br/
A mighty <qex>sphere</qex>, he framed.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The apparent
surface of the heavens, which is assumed to be spherical and everywhere
equally distant, in which the heavenly bodies appear to have their
places, and on which the various astronomical circles, as of right
ascension and declination, the equator, ecliptic, etc., are conceived to
be drawn; an ideal geometrical sphere, with the astronomical and
geographical circles in their proper positions on it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>In ancient astronomy, one of the concentric and eccentric revolving
spherical transparent shells in which the stars, sun, planets, and moon
were supposed to be set, and by which they were carried, in such a
manner as to produce their apparent motions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>The extension of a general
conception, or the totality of the individuals or species to which it
may be applied.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Circuit or range of action, knowledge, or influence;
compass; province; employment; place of existence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be called into a huge <qex>sphere</qex>, and not to be seen to
move in 't.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Taking her out of the ordinary relations with humanity, and
inclosing her in a <qex>sphere</qex> by herself.</q>
<rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each in his hidden <qex>sphere</qex> of joy or woe<br/
Our hermit spirits dwell.</q> <rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Rank; order of society; social positions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>An orbit, as of a star; a socket.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Armillary sphere</b></col>, <col><b>Crystalline
sphere</b></col>, <col><b>Oblique sphere</b></col></mcol>,. <cd>See
under <er>Armillary</er>, <er>Crystalline</er>,.</cd> --
<col><b>Doctrine of the sphere</b></col>, <cd>applications of the
principles of spherical trigonometry to the properties and relations of
the circles of the sphere, and the problems connected with them, in
astronomy and geography, as to the latitudes and longitudes, distance
and bearing, of places on the earth, and the right ascension and
declination, altitude and azimuth, rising and setting, etc., of the
heavenly bodies; spherical geometry.</cd> -- <col><b>Music of the
spheres</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Music</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Globe; orb; circle. See <er>Globe</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphere</ent><br/
<hw>Sphere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sphered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sphering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place in a
sphere, or among the spheres; to insphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The glorious planet Sol<br/
In noble eminence enthroned and <qex>sphered</qex><br/
Amidst the other.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form into roundness; to make spherical, or
spheral; to perfect.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheric</ent><br/
<ent>Spherical</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spher"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spher"ic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sphaericus</ets>, Gr.
<?/<?/<?/: cf. F. <ets>sph<eacute/rique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having the form of a sphere; like a sphere; globular; orbicular;
<as>as, a <ex>spherical</ex> body</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a sphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the heavenly orbs, or to the
sphere or spheres in which, according to ancient astronomy and
astrology, they were set.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Knaves, thieves, and treachers by <qex>spherical</qex>
predominance.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though the stars were suns, and overburned<br/
Their <qex>spheric</qex> limitations.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs.
Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Spherical angle</b></col>, <col><b>Spherical
coordinate</b></col>, <col><b>Spherical excess</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>etc. See under <er>Angle</er>, <er>Coordinate</er>, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Spherical geometry</b></col>, <cd>that branch of geometry which
treats of spherical magnitudes; the doctrine of the sphere, especially
of the circles described on its surface.</cd> -- <col><b>Spherical
harmonic analysis</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Harmonic</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Spherical lune</b></col>,<cd>portion of
the surface of a sphere included between two great semicircles having a
common diameter.</cd> -- <col><b>Spherical opening</b></col>, <cd>the
magnitude of a solid angle. It is measured by the portion within the
solid angle of the surface of any sphere whose center is the angular
point.</cd> -- <col><b>Spherical polygon</b></col>,<cd>portion of the
surface of a sphere bounded by the arcs of three or more great
circles.</cd> -- <col><b>Spherical projection</b></col>, <cd>the
projection of the circles of the sphere upon a plane. See
<er>Projection</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spherical sector</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Sector</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spherical segment</b></col>,
<cd>the segment of a sphere. See under <er>Segment</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spherical triangle</b></col>,<cd>re on the surface of a sphere,
bounded by the arcs of three great circles which intersect each
other.</cd> -- <col><b>Spherical trigonometry</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Trigonometry</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spher"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spher"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphericity</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*ric"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sph<eacute/ricit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being spherial; roundness; <as>as, the <ex>sphericity</ex> of the
planets, or of a drop of water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphericle</ent><br/
<hw>Spher"i*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small
sphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherics</ent><br/
<hw>Spher"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>The doctrine of the sphere; the science of the properties and
relations of the circles, figures, and other magnitudes of a sphere,
produced by planes intersecting it; spherical geometry and
trigonometry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherobacteria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sphe`ro*bac*te"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>;
<sing>sing. <singw>Spherobacterium</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>.
<ety>[NL. See <er>Sphere</er>, <er>and Bacterium</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>See the Note under
<er>Microbacteria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheroconic</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe`ro*con"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>A nonplane curve formed by the intersection of the surface of an
oblique cone with the surface of a sphere whose center is at the vertex
of the cone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherograph</ent><br/
<hw>Spher"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sphere</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument
for facilitating the practical use of spherics in navigation and
astronomy, being constructed of two cardboards containing various
circles, and turning upon each other in such a manner that any possible
spherical triangle may be readily found, and the measures of the parts
read off by inspection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheroid</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spheroides</ets> ball-like, spherical, Gr. <?/<?/<?/; <?/<?/<?/<?/
sphere + <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form: cf. F.
<ets>sph<eacute/ro<ium/de</ets>.]</ety> <def>A body or figure
approaching to a sphere, but not perfectly spherical; esp., a solid
generated by the revolution of an ellipse about one of its
axes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Oblate spheroid</b></col>, <col><b>Prolate
spheroid</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See <er>Oblate</er>, <er>Prolate</er>,
and <er>Ellipsoid</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheroidal</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*roid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sph<eacute/ro<ium/dal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form of a
spheroid.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sphe*roid"al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spheroidal state</b></col> <fld>(Physics.)</fld>, <cd>the
state of a liquid, as water, when, on being thrown on a surface of
highly heated metal, it rolls about in spheroidal drops or masses, at a
temperature several degrees below ebullition, and without actual contact
with the heated surface, -- a phenomenon due to the repulsive force of
heat, the intervention of a cushion of nonconducting vapor, and the
cooling effect of evaporation.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheroidical</ent><br/
<ent>Spheroidic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sphe*roid"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sphe*roid"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Spheroidal</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Cheyne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheroidity</ent><br/
<ent>Spheroidicity</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sphe`roi*dic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sphe*roid"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
spheroidal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spheromere</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"ro*mere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sphere</ets> + <ets>-mere</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of the several symmetrical segments arranged around the
central axis and composing the body of a radiate animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe*rom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sphere</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sph<eacute/rom<egrave/tre</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>An instrument for measuring the curvature of spherical surface, as
of lenses for telescope, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherosiderite</ent><br/
<hw>Spher`o*sid"er*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sphere</ets> + <ets>siderite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Siderite occurring in spheroidal masses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherosome</ent><br/
<hw>Sphe"ro*some</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sphere</ets> + <ets>-some</ets> body.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The body wall of any radiate animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherulate</ent><br/
<hw>Spher"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered or set
with spherules; having one or more rows of spherules, or minute
tubercles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherule</ent><br/
<hw>Spher"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spherula</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sph<eacute/rule</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
little sphere or spherical body; <as>as, quicksilver, when poured upon a
plane, divides itself into a great number of minute
<ex>spherules</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherulite</ent><br/
<hw>Spher"u*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sph<eacute/rulite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A minute
spherical crystalline body having a radiated structure, observed in some
vitreous volcanic rocks, as obsidian and pearlstone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spherulitic</ent><br/
<hw>Spher`u*lit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a spherulite; characterized by the presence of
spherulites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphery</ent><br/
<hw>Spher"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Round;
spherical; starlike.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Hermia's
<xex>sphery</xex> eyne.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the spheres.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She can teach ye how to climb<br/
Higher than the <qex>sphery</qex> chime.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphex</ent><br/
<hw>Sphex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/,
<?/<?/<?/, a wasp.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous
species of sand wasps of the genus <gen>Sphex</gen> and allied genera.
These wasps have the abdomen attached to the thorax by a slender
pedicel. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Sand wasp</cref>, under
<er>Sand</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sphex fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
numerous species of small dipterous flies of the genus <gen>Conops</gen>
and allied genera. The form of the body is similar to that of a
sphex.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphigmometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sphig*mom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sphygmometer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphincter</ent><br/
<hw>Sphinc"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. <?/<?/<?/
to bind tight.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A muscle which surrounds,
and by its contraction tends to close, a natural opening; <as>as, the
<ex>sphincter</ex> of the bladder</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphincter</ent><br/
<hw>Sphinc"ter</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or designating, a sphincter; <as>as, a <ex>sphincter</ex>
muscle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphingid</ent><br/
<hw>Sphin"gid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
sphinx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphingid</ent><br/
<hw>Sphin"gid</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a sphinx, or
the family <fam>Sphingidae</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphinx</ent><br/
<hw>Sphinx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from Gr.
<grk>sfi`gx</grk>, usually derived from <grk>sfi`ggein</grk> to bind
tight or together, as if the Throttler.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>In Egyptian art, an image of granite or porphyry, having a human
head, or the head of a ram or of a hawk, upon the wingless body of a
lion.</def> <-- ETY sic.? something missing? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The awful ruins of the days of old . . .<br/
Or jasper tomb, or mutilated <qex>sphinx</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shelley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>On Greek art and mythology, a she-monster, usually
represented as having the winged body of a lion, and the face and breast
of a young woman.</def> <note>The most famous Grecian sphinx, that of
Thebes in B<oe/otia, is said to have proposed a riddle to the Thebans,
and killed those who were unable to guess it. The enigma was solved by
<OE/dipus, whereupon the sphinx slew herself.</note> <ldquo/Subtle as
<xex>sphinx</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: A person of enigmatical character and
purposes, especially in politics and diplomacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
large moths of the family <fam>Sphingidae</fam>; -- called also
<altname>hawk moth</altname>. See also <cref>tomato
worm</cref>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The larva is a stout naked caterpillar which, when at
rest, often assumes a position suggesting the Egyptian sphinx, whence
the name.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Guinea, or sphinx, baboon
(<spn>Cynocephalus sphinx</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sphinx baboon</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large
West African baboon (<spn>Cynocephalus sphinx</spn>), often kept in
menageries.</cd> -- <col><b>Sphinx moth</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>Same as <er>Sphinx</er>, 3.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphragide</ent><br/
<hw>Sphrag"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>sphragis</ets>, <ets>-idis</ets>, Lemnian earth, fr. Gr. <?/<?/<?/,
<?/<?/<?/, a seal; -- so called because sold in sealed packets.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Lemnian earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphragistics</ent><br/
<hw>Sphra*gis"tics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ of or for sealing, fr. <?/<?/<?/ a seal.]</ety> <def>The
science of seals, their history, age, distinctions, etc., esp. as
verifying the age and genuiness of documents.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphrigosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sphri*go"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/ to be full of strength.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
condition of vegetation in which there is too abundant growth of the
stem and leaves, accompanied by deficiency of flowers and
fruit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphygmic</ent><br/
<hw>Sphyg"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/ the
pulse.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
pulse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphygmogram</ent><br/
<hw>Sphyg"mo*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ pulse + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>A tracing, called a pulse tracing, consisting of a series of curves
corresponding with the beats of the heart, obtained by the application
of the sphygmograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphygmograph</ent><br/
<hw>Sphyg"mo*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
the pulse + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An
instrument which, when applied over an artery, indicates graphically the
movements or character of the pulse. See <er>Sphygmogram</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphygmographic</ent><br/
<hw>Sphyg`mo*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Phusiol.)</fld> <def>Relating to, or produced by, a sphygmograph;
<as>as, a <ex>sphygmographic</ex> tracing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1385 --></p>

<p><ent>Sphygmometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sphyg*mom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ pulse + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>
<def>An instrument for measuring the strength of the pulse beat; a
sphygmograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphygmophone</ent><br/
<hw>Sphyg"mo*phone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
the pulse + <?/<?/<?/ sound.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An
electrical instrument for determining by the ear the rhythm of the pulse
of a person at a distance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphygmoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Sphyg"mo*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/
the pulse + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Sphygmograph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sphyraenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sphy*rae"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>sphyraena</ets> a kind of sea fish (Gr. <grk>sfy`raina</grk>) +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
<fam>Sphyraenidae</fam>, a family of marine fishes including the
barracudas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spial</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A spy; a scout.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spica</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spi"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spicae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., an ear, as of
corn.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A kind of bandage
passing, by successive turns and crosses, from an extremity to the
trunk; -- so called from its resemblance to a spike of a
barley.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A star of the first magnitude
situated in the constellation Virgo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicated</ent><br/
<ent>Spicate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spi"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spi"ca*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spicatus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>spicare</ets> furnish with spikes, or ears, fr. <ets>spica</ets> a
spike, or ear.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the form of a spike,
or ear; arranged in a spike or spikes.</def> <rj><au>Lee.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiccato</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spic*ca"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It., p. p. of
<ets>spicare</ets> to detach, to separate.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>Detached; separated; -- a term indicating that every note is to be
performed in a distinct and pointed manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spice</ent><br/
<hw>Spice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spice</ets>,
<ets>spece</ets>, spice, species, OF. <ets>espice</ets>,
<ets>espece</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/pice</ets> spice,
<ets>esp<egrave/ce</ets> species, fr. L. <ets>species</ets> particular
sort or kind, a species, a sight, appearance, show, LL., spices, drugs,
etc., of the same sort, fr. L. <ets>specere</ets> to look. See
<er>Spy</er>, and cf. <er>Species</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Species;
kind.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spices</qex> of penance ben three.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Abstain you from all evil <qex>spice</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Wyclif
(1. Thess,v. 22).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Justice, although it be but one entire virtue, yet is described in
two kinds of <qex>spices</qex>. The one is named justice distributive,
the other is called commutative.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Elyot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A vegetable production of many kinds, fragrant or
aromatic and pungent to the taste, as pepper, cinnamon, nutmeg, mace,
allspice, ginger, cloves, etc., which are used in cookery and to flavor
sauces, pickles, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hast thou aught in thy purse [bag] any hot <qex>spices</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Figuratively, that which enriches or alters the
quality of a thing in a small degree, as spice alters the taste of food;
that which gives zest or pungency; a slight flavoring; a relish; hence,
a small quantity or admixture; a sprinkling; <as>as, a <ex>spice</ex> of
mischief</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So much of the will, with a <qex>spice</qex> of the willful.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spice</ent><br/
<hw>Spice</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spiced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spicing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
season with spice, or as with spice; to mix aromatic or pungent
substances with; to flavor; to season; <as>as, to <ex>spice</ex> wine;
to <ex>spice</ex> one's words with wit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She 'll receive thee, but will <qex>spice</qex> thy bread<br/
With flowery poisons.</q> <rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fill or impregnate with the odor of
spices.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the <qex>spiced</qex> Indian air, by night.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To render nice or dainty; hence, to render
scrupulous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>spiced</xex>
conscience.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicebush</ent><br/
<hw>Spice"bush`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Spicewood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicenut</ent><br/
<hw>Spice"nut`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A small crisp cake, highly
spiced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicer</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>espicier</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/picier</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who seasons with spice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who deals in spice.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicery</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"cer*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espicerie</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/picerie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Spices, in general.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A repository of spices.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicewood</ent><br/
<hw>Spice"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An American shrub (<spn>Lindera Benzoin</spn>), the bark of which
has a spicy taste and odor; -- called also <altname>Benjamin</altname>,
<altname>wild allspice</altname>, and <altname>fever
bush</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiciferous</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*cif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spicifer</ets> bearing spikes, or ears; <ets>spica</ets> ear +
<ets>ferre</ets> to bear.]</ety> <def>Bearing ears, or spikes;
spicate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiciform</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ci*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spica</ets> a spike, ear + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Spike-shaped.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicily</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ci*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a spicy manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiciness</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ci*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
spicy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spick</ent><br/
<hw>Spick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sw.
<ets>spik</ets>. See <er>Spike</er> a nail.]</ety> <def>A spike or
nail.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spick and span</b></col>, <cd>quite new; that is, as new
as a spike or nail just made and a chip just split; brand-new; as, a
<xex>spick and span</xex> novelty. See <er>Span-new</er>.</cd></cs> <--
now hypehnated, and more commonly meaning spotlessly clean -->
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicknel</ent><br/
<hw>Spick"nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contr. from
<ets>spike nail</ets> a large, long nail; -- so called in allusion to
the shape of its capillary leaves.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An
umbelliferous herb (<spn>Meum Athamanticum</spn>) having finely divided
leaves, common in Europe; -- called also <altname>baldmoney</altname>,
<altname>mew</altname>, and <altname>bearwort</altname>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>spignel</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicose</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*cose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spica</ets>
a spike, or ear.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having spikes, or ears,
like corn spikes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicosity</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*cos"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
having, or being full of, ears like corn.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicous</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Spicose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spic"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spiculae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., dim. of L.
<ets>spica</ets> a spike, ear.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A little spike; a spikelet.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A pointed
fleshy appendage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicular</ent><br/
<hw>Spic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spiculum</ets> a dart: cf. F. <ets>spiculaire</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Resembling a dart; having sharp points.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiculate</ent><br/
<hw>Spic"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spiculatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>spiculare</ets> to sharpen, to
point, fr. <ets>spiculum</ets> a dart.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered
with, or having, spicules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Covered with minute spiculae, or
pointed fleshy appendages; divided into small spikelets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiculate</ent><br/
<hw>Spic"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To sharpen to a
point.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/With <xex>spiculated</xex>
paling.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mason.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicule</ent><br/
<hw>Spic"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spiculum</ets> a little point, a dart.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
minute, slender granule, or point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Spicula</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any small calcareous or siliceous
body found in the tissues of various invertebrate animals, especially in
sponges and in most Alcyonaria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Spicules vary exceedingly in size and shape, and some of
those found in siliceous sponges are very complex in structure and
elegant in form. They are of great use in classification.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Description of the Illustration</qex>:</q><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>a</qex> Acerate; <qex>b</qex> Tricurvate, <qex>or</qex>
Bowshaped; <qex>c d</qex> Hamate; <qex>e</qex> Broomshaped; <qex>f</qex>
Scepterellate; <qex>g</qex> Spinispirulate; <qex>h</qex>
Inequi-anchorate; <qex>i</qex> Sexradiate; <qex>j</qex> A Trichite
Sheaf; <qex>k</qex> Six-rayed Capitate; <qex>l</qex> Rosette of
<qex>Esperia</qex>; <qex>m</qex> Equi-anchorate.</q><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiculiform</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*cu"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the shape of a spicule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiculigenous</ent><br/
<hw>Spic`u*lig"e*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spiculum + -genous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Producing
or containing spicules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiculispongiae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spic`u*li*spon"gi*ae</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of sponges including
those which have independent siliceous spicules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiculum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spic"u*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spicula</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a little point.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Spicule</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spicy</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Spicier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Spiciest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Spice</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Flavored with, or containing, spice or spices;
fragrant; aromatic; <as>as, <ex>spicy</ex> breezes</as>.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>spicy</xex> nut-brown ale.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Led by new stars, and borne by <qex>spicy</qex> gales.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Producing, or abounding with, spices.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In hot Ceylon <qex>spicy</qex> forests grew.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: Piquant; racy; <as>as, a <ex>spicy</ex>
debate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Aromatic; fragrant; smart; pungent; pointed;
keen. See <er>Racy</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spider</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[OE.
<ets>spi<thorn/re</ets>, fr. AS. <ets>spinnan</ets> to spin; -- so named
from spinning its web; cf. D. <ets>spin</ets> a spider, G.
<ets>spinne</ets>, Sw. <ets>spindel</ets>. See <er>Spin</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
arachnids comprising the order Araneina. Spiders have the mandibles
converted into poison fangs, or falcers. The abdomen is large and not
segmented, with two or three pairs of spinnerets near the end, by means
of which they spin threads of silk to form cocoons, or nests, to protect
their eggs and young. Many species spin also complex webs to entrap the
insects upon which they prey. The eyes are usually eight in number
(rarely six), and are situated on the back of the cephalothorax. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Araneina</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Spiders are divided into two principal groups: the
Dipneumona, having two lungs: and the Tetrapneumona, having four lungs.
See <er>Mygale</er>. The former group includes several tribes; as, the
jumping spiders (see <er>Saltigradae</er>), the wolf spiders, or
<fam>Citigradae</fam> (see under <er>Wolf</er>), the crab spiders, or
<fam>Laterigradae</fam> (see under <er>Crab</er>), the garden, or
geometric, spiders, or <fam>Orbitellae</fam> (see under
<er>Geometrical</er>, and <er>Garden</er>), and others. See <cref>Bird
spider</cref>, under <er>Bird</er>, <cref>Grass spider</cref>, under
<er>Grass</er>, <cref>House spider</cref>, under <er>House</er>,
<cref>Silk spider</cref>, under <er>Silk</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of various other
arachnids resembling the true spiders, especially certain mites, as the
red spider (see under <er>Red</er>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An iron pan with a long handle, used as a kitchen
utensil in frying food. Originally, it had long legs, and was used over
coals on the hearth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A trevet to support pans or pots over a
fire.</def><-- = trivet --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A skeleton, or frame, having
radiating arms or members, often connected by crosspieces; as, a casting
forming the hub and spokes to which the rim of a fly wheel or large gear
is bolted; the body of a piston head; a frame for strengthening a core
or mold for a casting, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spider ant</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<cref>Solitary ant</cref>, under <er>Solitary</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spider crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
numerous species of maioid crabs having a more or less triangular body
and ten long legs. Some of the species grow to great size, as the great
Japanese spider crab (<spn>Macrocheira Kempferi</spn>), measuring
sometimes more than fifteen feet across the legs when they are
extended.</cd> -- <col><b>Spider fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of numerous species of parasitic dipterous insects of the
family <fam>Hippoboscidae</fam>. They are mostly destitute of wings, and
live among the feathers of birds and the hair of bats. Called also
<altname>bird tick</altname>, and <altname>bat tick</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spider hunter</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
several species of East Indian sunbirds of the genus
<gen>Arachnothera</gen>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spider lines</b></col>,
<cd>filaments of a spider's web crossing the field of vision in optical
instruments; -- used for determining the exact position of objects and
making delicate measurements. Fine wires, silk fibers, or lines on glass
similarly placed, are called <xex>spider lines</xex>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spider mite</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any
one of several species of parasitic mites of the genus <gen>Argas</gen>
and allied genera. See <er>Argas</er>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Any one of
numerous small mites injurious to plants.</cd> -- <col><b>Spider
monkey</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of
South American monkeys of the genus <gen>Ateles</gen>, having very long
legs and a long prehensile tail.</cd> -- <col><b>Spider orchis</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a European orchidaceous plant (<spn>Ophrys
aranifera</spn>), having flowers which resemble spiders.</cd> --
<col><b>Spider shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any shell of the
genus <gen>Pteroceras</gen>. See <er>Pteroceras</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spidered</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"dered</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Infested by spiders;
cobwebbed.</def> <rj><au>Wolcott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiderlike</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"der*like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a
spider.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spider stitch</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"der stitch</hw>. <def>A stitch in lace making used to fill in
open spaces with threads resembling a cobweb.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spider's web</ent><br/
<ent>Spider web</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spi"der web"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it> <hw>Spi"der's
web"</hw> }</mhw>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The silken web which is
formed by most kinds of spiders, particularly the web spun to entrap
their prey; -- also called <altname>cobweb</altname>. See
<cref>Geometric spider</cref>, <cref>Triangle spider</cref>, under
<er>Geometric</er>, and <er>Triangle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiderwort</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"der*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An American endogenous plant (<spn>Tradescantia Virginica</spn>),
with long linear leaves and ephemeral blue flowers. The name is
sometimes extended to other species of the same genus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spied</ent><br/
<hw>Spied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Spy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiegeleisen</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spie"gel*ei`sen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>spiegel</ets> mirror + <ets>eisen</ets> iron.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Spiegel iron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiegel iron</ent><br/
<hw>Spie"gel i`ron</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[G. <ets>spiegel</ets> mirror
+ E. <ets>iron</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>A fusible white
cast iron containing a large amount of carbon (from three and a half to
six per cent) and some manganese. When the manganese reaches twenty-five
per cent and upwards it has a granular structure, and constitutes the
alloy <xex>ferro manganese</xex>, largely used in the manufacture of
Bessemer steel. Called also <altname>specular pig iron</altname>,
<altname>spiegel</altname>, and
<altname>spiegeleisen</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spight</ent><br/
<hw>Spight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>Spite.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spight</ent><br/
<hw>Spight</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woodpecker. See
<er>Speight</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spignel</ent><br/
<hw>Spig"nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Spickenel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spignet</ent><br/
<hw>Spig"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted fr.
<ets>spikenard</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An aromatic plant of
America. See <er>Spikenard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spigot</ent><br/
<hw>Spig"ot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>spick</ets>,or <ets>spike</ets>; cf. Ir. & Gael.
<ets>spiocaid</ets> a spigot, Ir. <ets>spice</ets> a spike. See
<er>Spike</er>.]</ety> <def>A pin or peg used to stop the vent in a
cask; also, the plug of a faucet or cock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spigot and faucet joint</b></col>, <cd>a joint for
uniting pipes, formed by the insertion of the end of one pipe, or pipe
fitting, into a socket at the end of another.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spigurnel</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*gur"nel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>Formerly the title of the sealer of writs in chancery.</def>
<rj><au>Mozley & W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spike</ent><br/
<hw>Spike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to LG.
<ets>spiker</ets>, <ets>spieker</ets>, a large nail, D.
<ets>spijker</ets>, Sw. <ets>spik</ets>, Dan. <ets>spiger</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sp<imac/k</ets>; all perhaps from L. <ets>spica</ets> a point, an
ear of grain; but in the sense of nail more likely akin to E.
<ets>spoke</ets> of a wheel. Cf. <er>Spine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A sort of very large nail; also, a piece of pointed iron set with
points upward or outward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything resembling such a nail in shape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He wears on his head the <qex>corona radiata</qex> . . . ; the
<qex>spikes</qex> that shoot out represent the rays of the sun.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An ear of corn or grain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of flower cluster in which
sessile flowers are arranged on an unbranched elongated axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spike grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>either of
two tall perennial American grasses (<spn>Uniola paniculata</spn>, and
<spn>Uniola latifolia</spn>) having broad leaves and large flattened
spikelets.</cd> -- <col><b>Spike rush</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>Rush</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spike shell</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any pteropod of the genus <gen>Styliola</gen>
having a slender conical shell.</cd> -- <col><b>Spike team</b></col>,
<cd>three horses, or a horse and a yoke of oxen, harnessed together, a
horse leading the oxen or the span.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spike</ent><br/
<hw>Spike</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spiked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spiking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fasten with
spikes, or long, large nails; <as>as, to <ex>spike</ex> down
planks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set or furnish with spikes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fix on a spike.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To stop the vent of (a gun or cannon) by driving a
spike nail, or the like into it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- (Sport) To throw violently to the ground, so that it bounces.
Sometimes done by football players after scoring a goal, as a victory
celebration. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spike</ent><br/
<hw>Spike</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>spieke</ets>, L.
<ets>spica</ets> an ear of grain. See <er>Spikenard</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Spike lavender. See <er>Lavender</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Oil of spike</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
colorless or yellowish aromatic oil extracted from the European
broad-leaved lavender, or aspic (<spn>Lavendula Spica</spn>), used in
artist's varnish and in veterinary medicine. It is often adulterated
with oil of turpentine, which it much resembles.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spikebill</ent><br/
<hw>Spike"bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The hooded merganser.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
marbled godwit (<spn>Limosa fedoa</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiked</ent><br/
<hw>Spiked</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished or set with
spikes, as corn; fastened with spikes; stopped with spikes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A youth, leaping over the <qex>spiked</qex> pales, . . . was
caught by those spikes.</q> <rj><qau>Wiseman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spikefish</ent><br/
<hw>Spike"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Sailfish</er> <sd>(a)</sd></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spikelet</ent><br/
<hw>Spike"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
small or secondary spike; especially, one of the ultimate parts of the
in florescence of grasses. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Quaking
grass</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spikenard</ent><br/
<hw>Spike"nard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[For <ets>spiked
nard</ets>; cf. G. <ets>spieknarde</ets>, NL. <ets>spica nardi</ets>.
See <er>Spike</er> an ear, and <er>Nard</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An aromatic plant. In the United States it is the
<spn>Aralia racemosa</spn>, often called <altname>spignet</altname>, and
used as a medicine. The spikenard of the ancients is the
<spn>Nardostachys Jatamansi</spn>, a native of the Himalayan region.
From its blackish roots a perfume for the hair is still prepared in
India.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fragrant essential oil, as that from the
<spn>Nardostachys Jatamansi</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiketail</ent><br/
<hw>Spike"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The pintail duck.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiky</ent><br/
<hw>Spik"y</hw> <pr>(sp<imac/k"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Like a spike; spikelike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These <qex>spiky</qex>, vivid outbursts of metallic vapors.</q>
<rj><qau>C. A. Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a sharp point, or sharp points; furnished or
armed with spikes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Or by the <qex>spiky</qex> harrow cleared away.</q>
<rj><qau>Dyer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spiky</qex> wheels through heaps of carnage tore.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spile</ent><br/
<hw>Spile</hw> <pr>(sp<imac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LG.
<ets>spile</ets>, dial. G. <ets>speil</ets>, <ets>speiler</ets>, D.
<ets>spijl</ets>. <root/170.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small plug or
wooden pin, used to stop a vent, as in a cask.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1386 --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small tube or spout inserted in a tree for
conducting sap, as from a sugar maple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A large stake driven into the ground as a support
for some superstructure; a pile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>One of the thick laths or poles
driven horizontally ahead on top of a set of the main timbering in
advancing a level in loose ground.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> forepole; spill{2d}.</syn><br/
[<source>RDH</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spile hole</b></col>, <cd>a small air hole in a cask; a
vent.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spile</ent><br/
<hw>Spile</hw> <pr>(sp<imac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To supply
with a spile or a spigot; to make a small vent in, as a cask.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spilikin</ent><br/
<hw>Spil"i*kin</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/l"<icr/*k<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OD. <ets>spelleken</ets> a small pin. See <er>Spill</er> a
splinter.]</ety> <def>One of a number of small pieces or pegs of wood,
ivory, bone, or other material, for playing a game, or for counting the
score in a game, as in cribbage. In the plural
(<plw><it>spilikins</it></plw>), a game played with such pieces;
pushpin.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>spillikin</asp>,
<asp>spilliken</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spill</ent><br/
<hw>Spill</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<root/170. Cf.
<er>Spell</er> a splinter.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A bit of wood split
off; a splinter.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A slender piece of anything.</def> Specifically:
--<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>A peg or pin for plugging a hole, as in a cask; a
spile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A metallic rod or pin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>A small roll of paper, or slip of wood, used as a
lamplighter, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>One of the thick laths or poles
driven horizontally ahead on top of a set of the main timbering in
advancing a level in loose ground.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> forepole; spile{4}.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A little sum of money.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spill</ent><br/
<hw>Spill</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spilt</conjf> <pr>(sp<icr/lt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To cover or decorate with
slender pieces of wood, metal, ivory, etc.; to inlay.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spill</ent><br/
<hw>Spill</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spilled</conjf> <pr>(sp<icr/ld)</pr>, or
<conjf>Spilt</conjf> <pr>(sp<icr/lt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>spillen</ets>,
usually, to destroy, AS. <ets>spillan</ets>, <ets>spildan</ets>, to
destroy; akin to Icel. <ets>spilla</ets> to destroy, Sw.
<ets>spilla</ets> to spill, Dan. <ets>spilde</ets>, LG. & D.
<ets>spillen</ets> to squander, OHG. <ets>spildan</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To destroy; to kill; to put an end to.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And gave him to the queen, all at her will<br/
To choose whether she would him save or <qex>spill</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Greater glory think [it] to save than <qex>spill</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mar; to injure; to deface; hence, to destroy by
misuse; to waste.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They [the colors] disfigure the stuff and <qex>spill</qex> the
whole workmanship.</q> <rj><qau>Puttenham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Spill</qex> not the morning, the quintessence of day, in
recreations.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To suffer to fall or run out of a vessel; to lose,
or suffer to be scattered; -- applied to fluids and to substances whose
particles are small and loose; <as>as, to <ex>spill</ex> water from a
pail; to <ex>spill</ex> quicksilver from a vessel; to <ex>spill</ex>
powder from a paper; to <ex>spill</ex> sand or flour</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Spill</xex> differs from <xex>pour</xex> in
expressing accidental loss, -- a loss or waste contrary to
purpose.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to flow out and be lost or wasted; to shed,
or suffer to be shed, as in battle or in manslaughter; <as>as, a man
<ex>spills</ex> another's blood, or his own blood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And to revenge his blood so justly <qex>spilt</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To relieve a sail from the
pressure of the wind, so that it can be more easily reefed or furled, or
to lessen the strain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spilling line</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a rope
used for spilling, or dislodging, the wind from the belly of a
sail.</cd> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Spill, n. An instance of spilling. Oil spill, an accidental
release of oil, usually into the ocean, due to damage to an oil tanker
or uncontrolled release from an underwater well. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spill</ent><br/
<hw>Spill</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be destroyed,
ruined, or wasted; to come to ruin; to perish; to waste.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That thou wilt suffer innocents to <qex>spill</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be shed; to run over; to fall out, and be lost or
wasted.</def> <ldquo/He was so topful of himself, that he let it
<xex>spill</xex> on all the company.<rdquo/ <rj><au>I.
Watts.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiller</ent><br/
<hw>Spill"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, spills.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of fishing line with many hooks; a
boulter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spilliard fishing</ent><br/
<ent>Spillet fishing</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spil"let fish`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spil"liard
fish`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <def>A system or method of fishing
by means of a number of hooks set on snoods all on one line; -- in North
America, called <altname>trawl fishing</altname>,
<altname>bultow</altname>, or <altname>bultow fishing</altname>, and
<altname>long-line fishing</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spillikin</ent><br/
<hw>Spil"li*kin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spilikin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spillway</ent><br/
<hw>Spill"way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sluiceway or
passage for superfluous water in a reservoir, to prevent too great
pressure on the dam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spilt</ent><br/
<hw>Spilt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Spill</er>. Spilled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spilter</ent><br/
<hw>Spil"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Spill</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Any one of the small branches on a stag's
head.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spilth</ent><br/
<hw>Spilth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Spill</er>.]</ety> <def>Anything spilt, or freely poured out; slop;
effusion.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <ldquo/With drunken
<xex>spilth</xex> of wine.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Choicest cates, and the flagon's best <qex>spilth</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>R. Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spin</ent><br/
<hw>Spin</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spun</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> (<mark>Archaic</mark>
<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Span</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>); <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Spinning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS.
<ets>spinnan</ets>; akin to D. & G. <ets>spinnen</ets>, Icel. & Sw.
<ets>spinna</ets>, Dan. <ets>spinde</ets>, Goth. <ets>spinnan</ets>, and
probably to E. <ets>span</ets>. <root/170. Cf. <er>Span</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, <er>Spider</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To draw out, and
twist into threads, either by the hand or machinery; <as>as, to
<ex>spin</ex> wool, cotton, or flax; to <ex>spin</ex> goat's hair</as>;
to produce by drawing out and twisting a fibrous material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the yarn she [Penelope] <qex>spun</qex> in Ulysses' absence
did but fill Ithaca full of moths.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw out tediously; to form by a slow process, or
by degrees; to extend to a great length; -- with <xex>out</xex>; <as>as,
to <ex>spin</ex> out large volumes on a subject</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do you mean that story is tediously <qex>spun</qex> out?</q>
<rj><qau>Sheridan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To protract; to spend by delays; <as>as, to
<ex>spin</ex> out the day in idleness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By one delay after another they <qex>spin</qex> out their whole
lives.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to turn round rapidly; to whirl; to twirl;
<as>as, to <ex>spin</ex> a top</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To form (a web, a cocoon, silk, or the like) from
threads produced by the extrusion of a viscid, transparent liquid, which
hardens on coming into contact with the air; -- said of the spider, the
silkworm, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>To shape, as malleable sheet
metal, into a hollow form, by bending or buckling it by pressing against
it with a smooth hand tool or roller while the metal revolves, as in a
lathe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To spin a yarn</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to tell
a story, esp. a long or fabulous tale.</cd> -- <col><b>To spin
hay</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>to twist it into ropes for
convenient carriage on an expedition.</cd> -- <col><b>To spin street
yarn</b></col>, <cd>to gad about gossiping.</cd>
<mark>[Collog.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spin</ent><br/
<hw>Spin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
practice spinning; to work at drawing and twisting threads; to make yarn
or thread from fiber; <as>as, the woman knows how to <ex>spin</ex>; a
machine or jenny <ex>spins</ex> with great exactness.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They neither know to <qex>spin</qex>, nor care to toll.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move round rapidly; to whirl; to revolve, as a
top or a spindle, about its axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Round about him <qex>spun</qex> the landscape,<br/
Sky and forest reeled together.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a whirligig of jubilant mosquitoes <qex>spinning</qex> about
each head.</q> <rj><qau>G. W. Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To stream or issue in a thread or a small current or
jet; <as>as, blood <ex>spins</ex>from a vein</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To move swiftly; <as>as, to <ex>spin</ex> along the
road in a carriage, on a bicycle, etc.</as></def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spin</ent><br/
<hw>Spin</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of spinning;
<as>as, the <ex>spin</ex> of a top; a <ex>spin</ex> a
bicycle</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Kinematics)</fld> <def>Velocity of rotation about
some specified axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Politics)</fld> <def>an interpretation of an event
which is favorable to the interpreter or to the person s/he supports. A
person whose task is to provide such interpretations for public
relations purposes is called a <er>spin doctor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><-- <col><b>go for a spin</b></col>, <col><b>take a spin</b></col>,
<cd>take a trip in a wheeled vehicle, usu. an automobile.</cd> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spina bifida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spi"na bif"i*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <ety>[L.,
cleft spine.]</ety> <def>A congenital malformation in which the spinal
column is cleft at its lower portion, and the membranes of the spinal
cord project as an elastic swelling from the gap thus formed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*na"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or resembling, the plant spinach, or the family
of plants to which it belongs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinage</ent><br/
<ent>Spinach</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spin"ach</hw>, <hw>Spin"age</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>espinache</ets>, <ets>espinoche</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/pinard</ets>; cf. F. <ets>spinace</ets>, Sp.
<ets>espinaca</ets>; all fr. Ar. <ets>isf<amac/n<amac/j</ets>,
<ets>isfin<amac/j</ets>, <ets>aspan<amac/kh</ets>, probably of Persian
origin.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A common pot herb (<spn>Spinacia
oleracea</spn>) belonging to the Goosefoot family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Mountain spinach</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Garden
orache</cref>, under <er>Orache</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>New Zealand
spinach</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a coarse herb (<spn>Tetragonia
expansa</spn>), a poor substitute for spinach.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Various other pot herbs are locally called
<xex>spinach</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spinach beet</ent><br/
<hw>spinach beet</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>a beet lacking a
swollen root; it is grown as a vegetable for its edible leaves and
stalks.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> chard, Swiss chard, spinach beet, leaf beet, chard
plant, Beta vulgaris cicla.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><b>2.</b> <def>a plant with long succulent whitish stalks with large
green leaves.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> chard, Swiss chard, spinach beet, leaf
beet.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spinach blight</ent><br/
<hw>spinach blight</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a disease of spinach
plants.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinal</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spinalis</ets>, fr. <ets>spina</ets> the spine: cf. F.
<ets>spinal</ets>. See <er>Spine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or in the region of, the
backbone, or vertebral column; rachidian; vertebral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a spine or spines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spinal accessory nerves</b></col>, <cd>the eleventh pair
of cranial nerves in the higher vertebrates. They originate from the
spinal cord and pass forward into the skull, from which they emerge in
company with the pneumogastrics.</cd> -- <col><b>Spinal
column</b></col>, <cd>the backbone, or connected series or vertebrae
which forms the axis of the vertebrate skeleton; the spine; rachis;
vertebral column.</cd> -- <col><b>Spinal cord</b></col>, <cd>the great
nervous cord extending backward from the brain along the dorsal side of
the spinal column of a vertebrate animal, and usually terminating in a
threadlike appendage called the <xex>filum terminale</xex>; the spinal,
or vertebral, marrow; the myelon. The nervous tissue consists of nerve
fibers and nerve cells, the latter being confined to the so-called
<xex>gray matter</xex> of the central portions of the cord, while the
peripheral <xex>white matter</xex> is composed of nerve fibers only. The
center of the cord is traversed by a slender canal connecting with the
ventricles of the brain.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinate</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bearing a spine;
spiniform.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindle</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>spinal</ets>, fr. <ets>spinnan</ets> to spin; akin to D.
<ets>spil</ets>, G. <ets>spille</ets>, <ets>spindel</ets>, OHG.
<ets>spinnala</ets>. <root/170. See <er>Spin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The long, round, slender rod or pin in spinning wheels by which the
thread is twisted, and on which, when twisted, it is wound; also, the
pin on which the bobbin is held in a spinning machine, or in the shuttle
of a loom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A slender rod or pin on which anything turns; an
axis; <as>as, the <ex>spindle</ex> of a vane</as>.</def> Specifically:
--<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>The shaft, mandrel, or arbor, in
a machine tool, as a lathe or drilling machine, etc., which causes the
work to revolve, or carries a tool or center, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>The vertical rod on which the
runner of a grinding mill turns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>A shaft or pipe on which a
core of sand is formed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The fusee of a watch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A long and slender stalk resembling a
spindle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A yarn measure containing, in cotton yarn, 15,120
yards; in linen yarn, 14,400 yards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A solid generated by the
revolution of a curved line about its base or double ordinate or
chord.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any marine univalve
shell of the genus <gen>Rostellaria</gen>; -- called also
<altname>spindle stromb</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any marine
gastropod of the genus <gen>Fusus</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dead spindle</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a spindle
in a machine tool that does not revolve; the spindle of the tailstock of
a lathe.</cd> -- <col><b>Live spindle</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>,
<cd>the revolving spindle of a machine tool; the spindle of the
headstock of a turning lathe.</cd> -- <col><b>Spindle shell</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Spindle</er>, 7. above.</cd> --
<col><b>Spindle side</b></col>, <cd>the female side in descent; in the
female line; opposed to <ant>spear side</ant>.</cd> <au>Ld. Lytton.</au>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/King Lycaon, grandson, by the <xex>spindle
side</xex>, of Oceanus.<rdquo/ <au>Lowell.</au> -- <col><b>Spindle
tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any shrub or tree of the genus
<gen>Eunymus</gen>. The wood of <spn>Eunymus Europaeus</spn> was used
for spindles and skewers. See <er>Prickwood</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindle</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spindled</conjf><pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spindling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To shoot or grow
into a long, slender stalk or body; to become disproportionately tall
and slender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It has begun to <qex>spindle</qex> into overintellectuality.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindle-legged</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle-legged`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having long,
slender legs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindlelegs</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle*legs`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
spindleshanks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindle-shanked</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle-shanked`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having long,
slender legs.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindleshanks</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle*shanks`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person with
slender shanks, or legs; -- used humorously or in contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindle-shaped</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle-shaped`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having the shape of a spindle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Thickest in the middle, and
tapering to both ends; fusiform; -- applied chiefly to roots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindletail</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle*tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The pintail duck.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindleworm</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dle*worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The larva of a noctuid moth (<spn>Achatodes zeae</spn>) which feeds
inside the stalks of corn (maize), sometimes causing much damage. It is
smooth, with a black head and tail and a row of black dots across each
segment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindling</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Long and slender,
or disproportionately tall and slender; <as>as, a <ex>spindling</ex>
tree; a <ex>spindling</ex> boy.</as></def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> spindly.</syn>
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindly</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"dly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Long and slender, or
disproportionately tall and slender; <as>as, <ex>spindly</ex>
legs.</as>. Used in some cases to suggest weakness.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> spindling.</syn>
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spin doctor</ent><br/
<hw>spin doctor</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a spokesperson for a political
party or candidate who tries to forestall negative publicity.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spin dryer</ent><br/
<ent>spin drier</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>spin drier</hw>, <hw>spin dryer</hw></mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
machine that uses centrifugal motion to dry the clothes that are put
into it.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spindrift</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"drift</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Spoondrift</er>.</def></p>

<p><q>The ocean waves are broken up by wind, ultimately producing the
storm wrack and <qex>spindrift</qex> of the tempest-tossed sea.</q>
<rj><qau>J. E. Marr.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spine</ent><br/
<hw>Spine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spina</ets>
thorn, the spine; akin to <ets>spica</ets> a point: cf. OF.
<ets>espine</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/pine</ets>. Cf. <er>Spike</er>,
<er>Spinet</er> a musical instrument, <er>Spinny</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A sharp appendage to any of a plant;
a thorn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A rigid and sharp
projection upon any part of an animal.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of
the rigid and undivided fin rays of a fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The backbone, or spinal column,
of an animal; -- so called from the projecting processes upon the
vertebrae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Anything resembling the spine or backbone; a
ridge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spineback</ent><br/
<hw>Spine"back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A fish having spines in, or in front of, the dorsal fins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinebill</ent><br/
<hw>Spine"bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any species of Australian birds of the genus
<gen>Acanthorhynchus</gen>. They are related to the honey
eaters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spined</ent><br/
<hw>Spined</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished with spines;
spiny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spine-finned</ent><br/
<hw>Spine"-finned`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having fine supported by spinous fin rays; -- said of certain
fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinelle</ent><br/
<ent>Spinel</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spi*nel"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spi*nelle"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>spinelle</ets>, or LL.
<ets>spinellus</ets>, perhaps from L. <ets>spina</ets> a thorn, a
prickle, in allusion to its pointed crystals.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A mineral occurring in octahedrons of great hardness and various
colors, as red, green, blue, brown, and black, the red variety being the
gem spinel ruby. It consist essentially of alumina and magnesia, but
commonly contains iron and sometimes also chromium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>spinel group</xex> includes spinel proper, also
magnetite, chromite, franklinite, gahnite, etc., all of which may be
regarded as composed of a sesquioxide and a protoxide in equal
proportions.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinel</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Bleached yarn in
making the linen tape called <xex>inkle</xex>; unwrought inkle.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spineless</ent><br/
<hw>Spine"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having
no spine, or vertebral column.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having no thorns or barbs.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>lacking in strength of character; cowardly, in a
physical or moral sense.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinescence</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*nes"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or
quality of being spinescent or spiny; also, a spiny growth or covering,
as of certain animals.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinescent</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*nes"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>spinescens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>spinescere</ets> to know to grow thorny, fr. <ets>spina</ets> a
thorn: cf. F. <ets>spinescent</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Becoming hard and thorny; tapering gradually to a rigid, leafless
point; armed with spines.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinet</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espinete</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/pinette</ets> (cf. It.
<ets>spinetta</ets>), fr. L. <ets>spina</ets> a thorn; -- so called
because its quills resemble thorns. See <er>Spine</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A keyed instrument of music resembling a
harpsichord, but smaller, with one string of brass or steel wire to each
note, sounded by means of leather or quill plectrums or jacks. It was
formerly much used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dumb spinet</b></col>. <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Manichordon</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinet</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spinetum</ets>. See <er>Spinny</er>.]</ety> <def>A spinny.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinetail</ent><br/
<hw>Spine"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one or several species of swifts of the genus
<gen>Acanthylis</gen>, or <gen>Chaetura</gen>, and allied genera, in
which the shafts of the tail feathers terminate in rigid spines.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any one of several species of South American and
Central American clamatorial birds belonging to <gen>Synallaxis</gen>
and allied genera of the family <fam>Dendrocolaptidae</fam>. They are
allied to the ovenbirds.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The ruddy duck.</def>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spine-tailed</ent><br/
<hw>Spine"-tailed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the tail quills ending in sharp, naked tips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spine-tailed swift</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Spinetail</er> <sd>(a)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spineted</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"et*ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slit; cleft.</def>
<mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spinifer</ets>; <ets>spina</ets> thorn + <ets>ferre</ets> to
produce.]</ety> <def>Producing spines; bearing thorns or spines; thorny;
spiny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinifex</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"i*fex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.; L.
<ets>spina</ets> spine + <ets>facere</ets> to make.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of chiefly Australian grasses, the seeds
of which bear an elastic spine. <spn>Spinifex hirsutus</spn> (black
grass) and <spn>Spinifex longifolius</spn> are useful as sand binders.
<spn>Spinifex paradoxus</spn>is a valuable perennial fodder plant. Also,
a plant of this genus.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any of several Australian grasses of the genus
<gen>Tricuspis</gen>, which often form dense, almost impassable growth,
their leaves being stiff and sharp-pointed.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiniform</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shaped like a
spine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinigerous</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*nig"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spiniger</ets>; <ets>spina</ets> spine + <ets>gerere</ets> to
bear.]</ety> <def>Bearing a spine or spines; thorn-bearing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spininess</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
spiny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spini-spirulate</ent><br/
<hw>Spin`i-spir"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having spines arranged spirally. See
<er>Spicule</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spink</ent><br/
<hw>Spink</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. dial.
Sw. <ets>spink</ets> a kind of small bird, Gr. <grk>spi`ggos</grk>, and
E. <ets>finch</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
chaffinch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinnaker</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"na*ker</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/n"n<adot/*k<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A large triangular sail set upon a boom, -- used
when running before the wind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinner</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ner</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/n"n<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, spins one skilled in spinning;
a spinning machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A spider.</def> <ldquo/Long-legged
<xex>spinners</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A goatsucker; -- so called from
the peculiar noise it makes when darting through the air.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A spinneret.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ring spinner</b></col>, <cd>a machine for spinning, in
which the twist, given to the yarn by a revolving bobbin, is regulated
by the drag of a small metal loop which slides around a ring encircling
the bobbin, instead of by a throstle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinneret</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ner*et</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/n"n<etil/r*<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the special jointed organs situated on
the under side, and near the end, of the abdomen of spiders, by means of
which they spin their webs. Most spiders have three pairs of spinnerets,
but some have only two pairs. The ordinary silk line of the spider is
composed of numerous smaller lines jointed after issuing from the
spinnerets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinnerule</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ner*ule</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/n"n<etil/r*<usl/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the numerous small spinning tubes on the
spinnerets of spiders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinney</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ney</hw> <pr>(sp<icr/n"n<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spinneys</plw> <pr>(sp<icr/n"n<icr/z)</pr>.</plu>
<def>Same as <er>Spinny</er>.</def> <rj><au>T. Hughes.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1387 --></p>

<p><ent>Spinning</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ning</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Spin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spinning gland</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>one of
the glands which form the material for spinning the silk of silkworms
and other larvae.</cd> -- <col><b>Spinning house</b></col>, <cd>formerly
a common name for a house of correction in England, the women confined
therein being employed in spinning.</cd> -- <col><b>Spinning
jenny</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>an engine or machine for
spinning wool or cotton, by means of a large number of spindles
revolving simultaneously.</cd> -- <col><b>Spinning mite</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the red spider.</cd> -- <col><b>Spinning
wheel</b></col>, <cd>a machine for spinning yarn or thread, in which a
wheel drives a single spindle, and is itself driven by the hand, or by
the foot acting on a treadle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinny</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spinnies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espinaye</ets>,<ets>espinoye</ets>, <ets>espinei</ets>,
<ets>espanoi</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/pinaie</ets>, from L.
<ets>spinetum</ets> a thicket of thorns, fr. <ets>spina</ets> a thorn.
See <er>Spine</er>.]</ety> <def>A small thicket or grove with
undergrowth; a clump of trees.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>spinney</asp>, and <asp>spinny</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The downs rise steep, crowned with black fir
<qex>spinnies</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>C. Kingsley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinny</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ny</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Spiny</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Thin and long; slim; slender.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinose</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*nose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>spinosus</ets>, from <ets>spina</ets> a thorn.]</ety> <def>Full of
spines; armed with thorns; thorny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinosity</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*nos"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being spiny or thorny; spininess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinous</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Spinose;
thorny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the form of a spine or thorn;
spinelike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spinous process of a vertebra</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the dorsal process of the neural arch of a
vertebra; a neurapophysis.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinozism</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*no"zism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The form of
Pantheism taught by Benedict <xex>Spinoza</xex>, that there is but one
substance, or infinite essence, in the universe, of which the so-called
material and spiritual beings and phenomena are only modes, and that one
this one substance is God.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Spinosism</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinozist</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*no"zist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A believer in
Spinozism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinster</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Spin</ets> +
<ets>-ster</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A woman who spins, or whose
occupation is to spin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She spake to <qex>spinster</qex> to spin it out.</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spinsters</qex> and the knitters in the sun.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A man who spins.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An unmarried or single woman; --
used in legal proceedings as a title, or addition to the
surname.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If a gentlewoman be termed a <qex>spinster</qex>, she may abate
the writ.</q> <rj><qau>Coke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A woman of evil life and character; -- so called
from being forced to spin in a house of correction.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinstress</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"stress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who
spins.</def> <rj><au>T. Brown.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinstry</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"stry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The business of one
who spins; spinning.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinthariscope</ent><br/
<hw>Spin*thar"i*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>spinqari`s</grk> spark + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small
instrument containing a minute particle of a radium compound mounted in
front of a fluorescent screen and viewed with magnifying lenses. The
tiny flashes produced by the continual bombardment of the screen by the
<alpha/ rays are thus rendered visible.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Spin*thar`i*scop"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spin the bottle</ent><br/
<hw>spin the bottle</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a party game in which a
player spins a bottle and kisses the person that it points to when it
stops spinning; -- usually played by children.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spin the platter</ent><br/
<ent>spin the plate</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>spin the plate</hw>, <hw>spin the platter</hw></mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>a game in which something round (as a plate) is spun
on edge and the name of a player is called; the named player must catch
the spinning object before it falls or pay a forfeit.</def><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinule</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spinula</ets>, dim. of <ets>spina</ets> a spine: cf. F.
<ets>spinule</ets>.]</ety> <def>A minute spine.</def>
<rj><au>Dana.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinulescent</ent><br/
<hw>Spin`u*les"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having small spines; somewhat thorny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spinulous</ent><br/
<ent>Spinulose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spin"u*lose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spin"u*lous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>spinuleux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Covered with small spines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiny</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Spine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of spines; thorny; <as>as,
a <ex>spiny</ex> tree</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Like a spine in shape; slender.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Spiny</xex> grasshoppers sit chirping.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: Abounding with difficulties or
annoyances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spiny</qex> deserts of scholastic philosophy.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spiny lobster</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<cref>Rock lobster</cref>, under <er>Rock</er>. See also
<er>Lobster</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiny</ent><br/
<hw>Spin"y</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Spinny</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiodea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spi*o"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
<ets>Spio</ets> the typical genus.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
extensive division of marine Annelida, including those that are without
oral tentacles or cirri, and have the gills, when present, mostly
arranged along the sides of the body. They generally live in burrows or
tubes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirable</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ra*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spirabilis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being breathed;
respirable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Nash.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiracle</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"a*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>spiraculum</ets>, fr. <ets>spirare</ets> to breathe: cf. F.
<ets>spiracule</ets>. See <er>Spirit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The nostril, or one of the nostrils, of whales,
porpoises, and allied animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the external
openings communicating with the air tubes or tracheae of insects,
myriapods, and arachnids. They are variable in number, and are usually
situated on the sides of the thorax and abdomen, a pair to a segment.
These openings are usually elliptical, and capable of being closed. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Coleoptera</er>.</def> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
tubular orifice communicating with the gill cavity of certain ganoid and
all elasmobranch fishes. It is the modified first gill cleft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any small aperture or vent for air or other
fluid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiracular</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*rac"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a spiracle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiraea</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*rae"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/<?/<?/, fr. <?/<?/<?/ a coil.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus
of shrubs or perennial herbs including the meadowsweet and the
hardhack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiraeic</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*rae"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or derived from, the meadowsweet
(<gen>Spiraea</gen>); formerly, designating an acid which is now called
<xex>salicylic acid</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiral</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>spiral</ets>. See <er>Spire</er> a winding line.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Winding or circling round a center or pole and gradually receding
from it; <as>as, the <ex>spiral</ex> curve of a watch
spring</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Winding round a cylinder or imaginary axis, and at
the same time rising or advancing forward; winding like the thread of a
screw; helical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a spiral;
like a spiral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Spiral gear</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Spiral
wheel</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a gear resembling in
general a spur gear, but having its teeth cut at an angle with its axis,
or so that they form small portions of screws or spirals.</cd> --
<col><b>Spiral gearing</b></col>, <cd>a kind of gearing sometimes used
in light machinery, in which spiral gears, instead of bevel gears, are
used to transmit motion between shafts that are not parallel.</cd> --
<col><b>Spiral operculum</b></col>, <cd>an operculum whih has spiral
lines of growth.</cd> -- <col><b>Spiral shell</b></col>, <cd>any shell
in which the whorls form a spiral or helix.</cd> -- <col><b>Spiral
spring</b></col>. <cd>See the <xex>Note</xex> under <er>Spring</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 4.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiral</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>spirale</ets>. See <er>Spiral</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A plane curve, not reentrant,
described by a point, called the <xex>generatrix</xex>, moving along a
straight line according to a mathematical law, while the line is
revolving about a fixed point called the <xex>pole</xex>. Cf.
<er>Helix</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything which has a spiral form, as a spiral
shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Equiangular spiral</b></col>,<cd>a plane curve which cuts
all its generatrices at the same angle. Same as <altname>Logarithmic
spiral</altname>, under <er>Logarithmic</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spiral of
Archimedes</b></col>, <cd>a spiral the law of which is that the
generatrix moves uniformly along the revolving line, which also moves
uniformly.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirality</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*ral"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
states of being spiral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirally</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ral*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a spiral form,
manner, or direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiralozooid</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*ra`lo*zo"oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spiral</ets> + <ets>zooid</ets>. So called because they often
have a spiral form when contracted.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One
of the special defensive zooids of certain hydroids. They have the form
of long, slender tentacles, and bear lasso cells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirant</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"rant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spirans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>spirare</ets> to
breathe. See <er>Spirit</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Phon.)</fld> <def>A term used
differently by different authorities; -- by some as equivalent to
<xex>fricative</xex>, -- that is, as including all the continuous
consonants, except the nasals <xex>m</xex>, <xex>n</xex>, <xex>ng</xex>;
with the further exception, by others, of the liquids <xex>r</xex>,
<xex>l</xex>, and the semivowels <xex>w</xex>, <xex>y</xex>; by others
limited to <xex>f</xex>, <xex>v</xex>, <xex>th</xex> surd and sonant,
and the sound of German <xex>ch</xex>, -- thus excluding the sibilants,
as well as the nasals, liquids, and semivowels. See <xex>Guide to
Pronunciation</xex>, <sect/<sect/ 197-208.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiranthy</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*ran"thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/ a
coil + <?/<?/<?/ flower.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The occasional
twisted growth of the parts of a flower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiration</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spiratio</ets>, fr. <ets>spirare</ets> to breathe.]</ety> <def>The
act of breathing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spire</ent><br/
<hw>Spire</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spirare</ets> to breathe. See <er>Spirit</er>.]</ety> <def>To
breathe.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shenstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spire</ent><br/
<hw>Spire</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spire</ets>,
<ets>spir</ets>, a blade of grass, a young shoot, AS.
<ets>sp<imac/r</ets>; akin to G. <ets>spier</ets> a blade of grass, Dan.
<ets>spire</ets> a sprout, sprig, Sw. <ets>spira</ets> a spar, Icel.
<ets>sp<imac/ra</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A slender stalk or blade
in vegetation; <as>as, a <ex>spire</ex> grass or of
wheat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An oak cometh up a little <qex>spire</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tapering body that shoots up or out to a point in
a conical or pyramidal form. Specifically <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, the roof
of a tower when of a pyramidal form and high in proportion to its width;
also, the pyramidal or aspiring termination of a tower which can not be
said to have a roof, such as that of Strasburg cathedral; the tapering
part of a steeple, or the steeple itself.</def> <ldquo/With glistering
<xex>spires</xex> and pinnacles adorned.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>spire</qex> of land that stand apart,<br/
Cleft from the main.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tall <qex>spire</qex> from which the sound of cheerful bells<br/
Just undulates upon the listening ear.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A tube or fuse for communicating
fire to the chargen in blasting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The top, or uppermost point, of anything; the
summit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spire</qex> and top of praises.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spire</ent><br/
<hw>Spire</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spired</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spiring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To shoot forth, or up in, or as
if in, a spire.</def> <rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is not so apt to <qex>spire</qex> up as the other sorts, being
more inclined to branch into arms.</q> <rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spire</ent><br/
<hw>Spire</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spira</ets> coil, twist;
akin to Gr. <?/<?/<?/: cf. F. <ets>spire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A spiral; a curl; a whorl; a twist.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>The part of a spiral generated in
one revolution of the straight line about the pole. See <er>Spiral</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spire bearer</b></col>. <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Spirifer</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spired</ent><br/
<hw>Spired</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a spire; being
in the form of a spire; <as>as, a <ex>spired</ex> steeple</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Mason.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiricle</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ri*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim., fr. L.
<ets>spira</ets> a coil.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of certain
minute coiled threads in the coating of some seeds. When moistened these
threads protrude in great numbers.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirifer</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ri*fer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>spira</ets> a coil + <ets>ferre</ets>to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of fossil
brachipods of the genus <gen>Spirifer</gen>, or <gen>Delthyris</gen>,
and allied genera, in which the long calcareous supports of the arms
form a large spiral, or helix, on each side.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirillum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spi*ril"lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., dim. of
L. <ets>spira</ets> a coil.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
genus of common gram-negative motile microorganisms (family
<fam>Spirillaceae</fam>) having the form of spiral-shaped filaments.
They are obligately microaerophilic, and do not ferment
carbohydrates.</def> <au>Stedman.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A species of bacteria (<spn>Borrelia
recurrentis</spn>, also known as <altname>Obermeier's
spirillum</altname>), once believed to belong to the genus
<gen>Spirillum</gen>, which causes relapsing fever.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiring</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shooting up in a
spire or spires.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>spiring</xex> grass.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirit</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espirit</ets>, <ets>esperit</ets>, F. <ets>esprit</ets>, L.
<ets>spiritus</ets>, from <ets>spirare</ets> to breathe, to blow. Cf.
<er>Conspire</er>, <er>Expire</er>, <er>Esprit</er>,
<er>Sprite</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Air set in motion by breathing;
breath; hence, sometimes, life itself.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/All of <xex>spirit</xex> would deprive.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mild air, with season moderate,<br/
Gently attempered, and disposed eo well,<br/
That still it breathed foorth sweet <qex>spirit</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rough breathing; an aspirate, as the letter
<xex>h</xex>; also, a mark to denote aspiration; a breathing.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be it a letter or <qex>spirit</qex>, we have great use for it.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Life, or living substance, considered independently
of corporeal existence; an intelligence conceived of apart from any
physical organization or embodiment; vital essence, force, or energy, as
distinct from matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The intelligent, immaterial and immortal part of
man; the soul, in distinction from the body in which it resides; the
agent or subject of vital and spiritual functions, whether spiritual or
material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a <qex>spirit</qex> in man; and the inspiration of the
Almighty giveth them understanding.</q> <rj><qau>Job xxxii.
8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As the body without the <qex>spirit</qex> is dead, so faith
without works is dead also.</q> <rj><qau>James ii. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Spirit</qex> is a substance wherein thinking, knowing,
doubting, and a power of moving, do subsist.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Specifically, a disembodied soul; the human soul
after it has left the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then shall the dust return to the earth as it was, and the
<qex>spirit</qex> shall return unto God who gave it.</q> <rj><qau>Eccl.
xii. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye gentle <qex>spirits</qex> far away,<br/
With whom we shared the cup of grace.</q> <rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Any supernatural being, good or bad; an apparition;
a specter; a ghost; also, sometimes, a sprite,; a fairy; an
elf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whilst young, preserve his tender mind from all impressions of
<qex>spirits</qex> and goblins in the dark.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Energy, vivacity, ardor, enthusiasm, courage,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/Write it then, quickly,<rdquo/ replied Bede; and summoning
all his <qex>spirits</qex> together, like the last blaze of a candle
going out, he indited it, and expired.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>One who is vivacious or lively; one who evinces
great activity or peculiar characteristics of mind or temper; <as>as, a
ruling <ex>spirit</ex>; a schismatic <ex>spirit</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such <qex>spirits</qex> as he desired to please, such would I
choose for my judges.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Temper or disposition of mind; mental condition or
disposition; intellectual or moral state; -- often in the plural;
<as>as, to be cheerful, or in good <ex>spirits</ex>; to be downhearted,
or in bad <ex>spirits</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God has . . . made a <qex>spirit</qex> of building succeed a
<qex>spirit</qex> of pulling down.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A perfect judge will read each work of wit<br/
With the same <qex>spirit</qex> that its author writ.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>Intent; real meaning; -- opposed to the
<xex>letter</xex>, or to <xex>formal statement</xex>; also,
characteristic quality, especially such as is derived from the
individual genius or the personal character; <as>as, the <ex>spirit</ex>
of an enterprise, of a document, or the like</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Tenuous, volatile, airy, or vapory substance,
possessed of active qualities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All bodies have <qex>spirits</qex> . . . within them.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>Any liquid produced by distillation; especially,
alcohol, the <xex>spirits</xex>, or <xex>spirit</xex>, of wine (it
having been first distilled from wine): -- often in the
plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Rum, whisky, brandy, gin, and
other distilled liquors having much alcohol, in distinction from wine
and malt liquors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A solution in alcohol of a
volatile principle. Cf. <er>Tincture</er>.</def> <rj><au>U. S.
Disp.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <fld>(Alchemy)</fld> <def>Any one of the four
substances, sulphur, sal ammoniac, quicksilver, or arsenic (or,
according to some, orpiment).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The four <qex>spirits</qex> and the bodies seven.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <fld>(Dyeing)</fld> <def>Stannic chloride. See under
<er>Stannic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Spirit</xex> is sometimes joined with other words,
forming compounds, generally of obvious signification; as,
<xex>spirit</xex>-moving, <xex>spirit</xex>-searching,
<xex>spirit</xex>-stirring, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Astral spirits</b></col>, <col><b>Familiar
spirits</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Astral</er>,
<er>Familiar</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Animal spirits</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <cd>The fluid which at one time was
supposed to circulate through the nerves and was regarded as the agent
of sensation and motion; -- called also the <altname>nervous
fluid</altname>, or <altname>nervous principle</altname>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Physical health and energy; frolicsomeness;
sportiveness.</cd> -- <col><b>Ardent spirits</b></col>, <cd>strong
alcoholic liquors, as brandy, rum, whisky, etc., obtained by
distillation.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Holy Spirit</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>The Spirit</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Theol.)</fld>, <cd>the Spirit
of God, or the third person of the Trinity; the Holy Ghost. <xex>The
spirit</xex> also signifies the human spirit as influenced or animated
by the Divine Spirit.</cd> -- <col><b>Proof spirit</b></col>.
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Proof</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Rectified spirit</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>spirit
rendered purer or more concentrated by redistillation, so as to increase
the percentage of absolute alcohol.</cd> -- <col><b>Spirit
butterfly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species
of delicate butterflies of tropical America belonging to the genus
<gen>Ithomia</gen>. The wings are gauzy and nearly destitute of
scales.</cd> -- <col><b>Spirit duck</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The buffle-headed duck</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The
golden-eye.</cd> -- <col><b>Spirit lamp</b></col> <fld>(Art)</fld>,
<cd>a lamp in which alcohol or methylated spirit is burned.</cd> --
<col><b>Spirit level</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Level</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spirit of hartshorn</b></col>. <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Hartshorn</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spirit of Mindererus</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>an aqueous solution of acetate of ammonium; --
named after R. <etsep>Minderer</etsep>, physician of Augsburg.</cd> --
<col><b>Spirit of nitrous ether</b></col> <fld>(Med. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
pale yellow liquid, of a sweetish taste and a pleasant ethereal odor. It
is obtained by the distillation of alcohol with nitric and sulphuric
acids, and consists essentially of ethyl nitrite with a little acetic
aldehyde. It is used as a diaphoretic, diuretic, antispasmodic, etc.
Called also <altname>sweet spirit of niter</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spirit of salt</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>hydrochloric
acid; -- so called because obtained from salt and sulphuric acid.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>Spirit of sense</b></col>, <cd>the utmost
refinement of sensation.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Shak.</au> --
<mcol><col><b>Spirits of turpentine</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Spirit of turpentine</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>,
<cd>rectified oil of turpentine, a transparent, colorless, volatile, and
very inflammable liquid, distilled from the turpentine of the various
species of pine; camphine. It is commonly used to remove paint from
surfaces, or to dissole oil-based paint. See <er>Camphine</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spirit of vitriol</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sulphuric
acid; -- so called because formerly obtained by the distillation of
green vitriol.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>Spirit of vitriolic
ether</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>ethyl ether; -- often but
incorrectly called <altname>sulphuric ether</altname>. See
<er>Ether</er>.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <mcol><col><b>Spirits of
wine</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Spirit of wine</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>alcohol; -- so called because formerly obtained
by the distillation of wine.</cd> -- <col><b>Spirit rapper</b></col>,
<cd>one who practices spirit rapping; a <ldquo/medium<rdquo/ so
called.</cd> -- <col><b>Spirit rapping</b></col>, <cd>an alleged form of
communication with the spirits of the dead by raps. See
<er>Spiritualism</er>, 3.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet spirit of
niter</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Spirit of nitrous ether</cref>,
above.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1388 --></p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Life; ardor; energy; fire; courage; animatioon;
cheerfulness; vivacity; enterprise.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirit</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Spirited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spiriting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To animate with
vigor; to excite; to encourage; to inspirit; <as>as, civil dissensions
often <ex>spirit</ex> the ambition of private men</as>; -- sometimes
followed by <xex>up</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many officers and private men <qex>spirit</qex> up and assist
those obstinate people to continue in their rebellion.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To convey rapidly and secretly, or mysteriously, as
if by the agency of a spirit; to kidnap; -- often with <xex>away</xex>,
or <xex>off</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ministry had him <qex>spirited</qex> away, and carried abroad
as a dangerous person.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot & Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I felt as if I had been <qex>spirited</qex> into some castle of
antiquity.</q> <rj><qau>Willis.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spiriting away</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>causing to
leave; the offense of inducing a witness to leave a jurisdiction so as
to evade process requiring attendance at trial.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritally</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos><ety>[L.
<ets>spiritalis</ets> belonging to breathing.]</ety> <def>By means of
the breath.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirited</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Animated or
possessed by a spirit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/So talked the
<xex>spirited</xex>, sly snake.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Animated; full of life or vigor; lively; full of
spirit or fire; <as>as, a <ex>spirited</ex> oration; a <ex>spirited</ex>
answer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Spirited</xex> is much used in composition; as in
high-<xex>spirited</xex>, low-<xex>spirited</xex>,
mean-<xex>spirited</xex>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Lively; vivacious; animated; ardent; active;
bold; courageous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spir"it*ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spir"it*ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritful</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of spirit;
spirited.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spiritful</qex> and orderly life of our own grown
men.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spir"it*ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spir"it*ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritism</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Spiritualsm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritist</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A spiritualist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritless</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Destitute of
spirit; wanting animation; wanting cheerfulness; dejected;
depressed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Destitute of vigor; wanting life, courage, or
fire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A men so faint, so <qex>spiritless</qex>,<br/
So dull, so dead in lock, so woebegone.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having no breath; extinct; dead.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>spiritless</xex> body.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Greenhill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spir"it*less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spir"it*less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritoso</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spi`ri*to"so</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos>
<ety>[It.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Spirited; spiritedly; -- a
direction to perform a passage in an animated, lively manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritous</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Spirituous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Like spirit; refined;
defecated; pure.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>More refined, more <qex>spirituous</qex> and pure.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Ardent; active.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritousness</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
spiritous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritual</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spiritualis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>spirituel</ets>. See
<er>Spirit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of spirit; not
material; incorporeal; <as>as, a <ex>spiritual</ex> substance or
being</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is sown a natural body, it is raised a <qex>spiritual</qex>
body.</q> <rj><qau>1 Cor. xv. 44.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the intellectual and higher
endowments of the mind; mental; intellectual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the moral feelings or states of
the soul, as distinguished from the external actions; reaching and
affecting the spirits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God's law is <qex>spiritual</qex>; it is a transcript of the
divine nature, and extends its authority to the acts of the soul of
man.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the soul or its affections as
influenced by the Spirit; controlled and inspired by the divine Spirit;
proceeding from the Holy Spirit; pure; holy; divine; heavenly-minded; --
opposed to <ant>carnal</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That I may impart unto you some <qex>spiritual</qex> gift.</q>
<rj><qau>Rom. i. ll.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who hath
blessed us with all <qex>spiritual</qex> blessings.</q> <rj><qau>Eph. i.
3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If a man be overtaken in a fault, ye which are
<qex>spiritual</qex>, restore such an one.</q> <rj><qau>Gal. vi.
1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Not lay or temporal; relating to sacred things;
ecclesiastical; <as>as, the <ex>spiritual</ex> functions of the clergy;
lords <ex>spiritual</ex> and temporal; a <ex>spiritual</ex>
corporation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spiritual coadjuctor</b></col>. <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<cd>See the Note under <er>Jesuit</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spiritual
court</b></col> <fld>(Eccl. Law)</fld>, <cd>an ecclesiastical court, or
a court having jurisdiction in ecclesiastical affairs; a court held by a
bishop or other ecclesiastic.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritual</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A spiritual function, office,
or affair. See <er>Spirituality</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He assigns supremacy to the pope in <qex>spirituals</qex>, and to
the emperor in temporals.</q> <rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualism</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or state of being spiritual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The doctrine, in opposition to
the materialists, that all which exists is spirit, or soul -- that what
is called the external world is either a succession of notions impressed
on the mind by the Deity, as maintained by Berkeley, or else the mere
educt of the mind itself, as taught by Fichte.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A belief that departed spirits hold intercourse with
mortals by means of physical phenomena, as by rapping, or during
abnormal mental states, as in trances, or the like, commonly manifested
through a person of special susceptibility, called a <xex>medium</xex>;
spiritism; the doctrines and practices of spiritualists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What is called <qex>spiritualism</qex> should, I think, be called
a mental species of materialism.</q> <rj><qau>R. H.
Hutton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualist</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who professes a regard for spiritual things only; one whose
employment is of a spiritual character; an ecclesiastic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who maintains the doctrine of
spiritualism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who believes in direct intercourse with departed
spirits, through the agency of persons commonly called
<xex>mediums</xex>, by means of physical phenomena; one who attempts to
maintain such intercourse; a spiritist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualist</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al*ist</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Spiritualistic.</def>
<rj><au>Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualistic</ent><br/
<hw>Spir`it*u*al*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating
to, or connected with, spiritualism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirituality</ent><br/
<hw>Spir`it*u*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spiritualities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>spiritualitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>spiritualit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being spiritual;
incorporeality; heavenly-mindedness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A pleasure made for the soul, suitable to its
<qex>spirituality</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If this light be not spiritual, yet it approacheth nearest to
<qex>spirituality</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Much of our <qex>spirituality</qex> and comfort in public worship
depends on the state of mind in which we come.</q>
<rj><qau>Bickersteth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>That which belongs to the church,
or to a person as an ecclesiastic, or to religion, as distinct from
temporalities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>During the vacancy of a see, the archbishop is guardian of the
<qex>spiritualities</qex> thereof.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An ecclesiastical body; the whole body of the
clergy, as distinct from, or opposed to, the temporality.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Five entire subsidies were granted to the king by the
<qex>spirituality</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualization</ent><br/
<hw>Spir`it*u*al*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act
of spiritualizing, or the state of being spiritualized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualize</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spiritualized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Spiritualizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>spiritualiser</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To refine intellectually or morally; to purify from the
corrupting influence of the world; to give a spiritual character or
tendency to; <as>as, to <ex>spiritualize</ex> soul</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This seen in the clear air, and the whole <qex>spiritualized</qex>
by endless recollections, fills the eye and the heart more forcibly than
I can find words to say.</q> <rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give a spiritual meaning to; to take in a
spiritual sense; -- opposed to <contr>literalize</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>To extract spirit from; also,
to convert into, or impregnate with, spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualizer</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
spiritualizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritually</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a spiritual manner;
with purity of spirit; like a spirit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritual-minded</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al-mind`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having
the mind set on spiritual things, or filled with holy desires and
affections.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Spir"it*u*al-mind`ed*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualness</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being spiritual or spiritual-minded; spirituality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiritualty</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*al*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Spirituality</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>An ecclesiastical
body; a spirituality.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirituelle</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spi`ri`tu`elle"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Of the nature, or having the appearance, of a spirit; pure;
refined; ethereal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirituosity</ent><br/
<hw>Spir`it*u*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being spirituous; spirituousness.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirituous</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. (for sense
2) F. <ets>spiritueux</ets>. Cf. <er>Spiritous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having the quality of spirit; tenuous in substance, and having
active powers or properties; ethereal; immaterial; spiritual;
pure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Containing, or of the nature of, alcoholic (esp.
distilled) spirit; consisting of refined spirit; alcoholic; ardent;
<as>as, <ex>spirituous</ex> liquors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Lively; gay; vivid; airy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind of man is of that <qex>spirituous</qex>, stirring nature,
that it is perpetually at work.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirituousness</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"it*u*ous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being spirituous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirketing</ent><br/
<hw>Spirk"et*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The planking from the waterways up to the port sills.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirling</ent><br/
<hw>Spirl"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sparling.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirobacteria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spi`ro*bac*te"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>;
<sing>sing. <singw>Spirobacterium</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>.
<ety>[NL. See 4th <er>Spire</er>, and <er>Bacterium</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>See the Note under
<er>Microbacteria</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirochaete</ent><br/
<ent>Spirochaeta</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Spi`ro*chae"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
\'d8<hw>Spi`ro*chae"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spira</ets> a coil + Gr. <?/<?/<?/ hair.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>A genus of Spirobacteria similar to Spirillum, but distinguished by
its motility. One species, the <spn>Spirochaete Obermeyeri</spn>, is
supposed to be the cause of relapsing fever.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirograph</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ro*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spirare</ets> to breathe + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An instrument for recording the respiratory
movements, as the sphygmograph does those of the pulse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirometer</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*rom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spirare</ets> to breathe + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
instrument for measuring the vital capacity of the lungs, or the volume
of air which can be expelled from the chest after the deepest possible
inspiration. Cf. <er>Pneumatometer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirometry</ent><br/
<hw>Spi*rom"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
process of measuring the chest capacity by means of a
spirometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiroscope</ent><br/
<hw>Spi"ro*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spirare</ets> to breathe + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A wet meter used to determine the breathing
capacity of the lungs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiroylous</ent><br/
<ent>Spiroylic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spi*royl"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Spi*royl"ous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>Spir<?/</ets>
meadowsweet (a source of salicylal) + <ets>-yl</ets> + <ets>-ic</ets>,
<ets>-ous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or
designating, a substance now called <xex>salicylal</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirt</ent><br/
<hw>Spirt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Spurt</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirtle</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To spirt in a
scattering manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spir"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., dim. of L.
<ets>spira</ets> a coil.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
cephalopods having a multilocular, internal, siphunculated shell in the
form of a flat spiral, the coils of which are not in contact.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spirulate</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the color spots, or structural parts, arranged
spirally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiry</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Spire</er> a
winding line.]</ety> <def>Of a spiral form; wreathed; curled;
serpentine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hid in the <qex>spiry</qex> volumes of the snake.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiry</ent><br/
<hw>Spir"y</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[FR. <er>Spire</er> a
steeple.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a spire; like a spire, tall,
slender, and tapering; abounding in spires; <as>as, <ex>spiry</ex>
turrets</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Spiry</xex> towns.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spiss</ent><br/
<hw>Spiss</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spissus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Thick; crowded; compact; dense.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>spiss</qex> and . . . copious, yet concise,
treatise.</q> <rj><qau>Brerewood.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spissated</ent><br/
<hw>Spis"sa*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Rendered dense or
compact, as by evaporation; inspissated; thickened.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spissated</qex> juice of the poppy.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spissitude</ent><br/
<hw>Spis"si*tude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spissitudo</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
spissated; <as>as, the <ex>spissitude</ex> of coagulated blood, or of
any coagulum</as>.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spit</ent><br/
<hw>Spit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spite</ets>,
AS. <ets>spitu</ets>; akin to D. <ets>spit</ets>, G. <ets>spiess</ets>,
OHG. <ets>spiz</ets>, Dan. <ets>spid</ets>. Sw. <ets>spett</ets>, and to
G. <ets>spitz</ets> pointed. <root/170.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long,
slender, pointed rod, usually of iron, for holding meat while
roasting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small point of land running into the sea, or a
long, narrow shoal extending from the shore into the sea; <as>as, a
<ex>spit</ex> of sand</as>.</def> <rj><au>Cook.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The depth to which a spade goes in digging; a spade;
a spadeful.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spit</ent><br/
<hw>Spit</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spitted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Spit</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>; cf. <er>Speet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To thrust a
spit through; to fix upon a spit; hence, to thrust through or impale;
<as>as, to <ex>spit</ex> a loin of veal</as>.</def> <ldquo/Infants
<xex>spitted</xex> upon pikes.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To spade; to dig.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spit</ent><br/
<hw>Spit</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To attend to a spit; to use a
spit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She's <qex>spitting</qex> in the kitchen.</q> <rj><qau>Old
Play.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spit</ent><br/
<hw>Spit</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spit</conjf> (<conjf>Spat</conjf>, <mark>archaic</mark>); <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Spitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS.
<ets>spittan</ets>; akin to G. <ets>sp<uum/tzen</ets>, Dan.
<ets>spytte</ets>, Sw. <ets>spotta</ets>,Icel. <ets>sp<?/ta</ets>, and
prob. E. <ets>spew</ets>. The past tense <ets>spat</ets> is due to AS.
<ets>sp<?/tte</ets>, from <ets>sp<?/tan</ets> to spit. Cf.
<er>Spat</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Spew</er>, <er>Spawl</er>,
<er>Spot</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To eject from the
mouth; to throw out, as saliva or other matter, from the mouth.</def>
<ldquo/Thus <xex>spit</xex> I out my venom.<rdquo/ <-- spat is a common
pp and not archaic --> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To eject; to throw out; to belch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Spitted</xex> was sometimes used as the preterit
and the past participle. <ldquo/He . . . shall be mocked, and spitefully
entreated, and <xex>spitted</xex> on.<rdquo/</note> <rj><au>Luke xviii.
32.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spit</ent><br/
<hw>Spit</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The secretion formed by the glands of
the mouth; spitle; saliva; sputum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spit</ent><br/
<hw>Spit</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To throw out saliva
from the mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To rain or snow slightly, or with
sprinkles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It had been <qex>spitting</qex> with rain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To spit on</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To spit
upon</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to insult grossly; to treat with
contempt.</cd> <ldquo/<xex>Spitting upon</xex> all antiquity.<rdquo/
<rj><au>South.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spital</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbreviated from
<ets>hospital</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>spittle</asp>.]</altsp> <def>A hospital.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitalhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"al*house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
hospital.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spit ball</ent><br/
<ent>spitball</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>spit"ball`</hw>, <hw>spit" ball`</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Paper chewed, and rolled into a ball, to
be thrown as a missile; -- a childish prank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>A pitched ball in throwing
which the pitcher grips the ball between two, or three, fingers on one
side (which is made slippery, as by saliva) and the thumb on the other
side, and delivers it so that it slips off the fingers with the least
possible friction; -- also called <altname>spitter</altname>. When
pitched directly overhand a spit ball darts downward, when pitched with
the arm extended sidewise it darts down and out. The use of such a pitch
is against the rules of professional baseball.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark>
-- <wordforms><wf>Spit baller</wf>.</wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitbox</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vessel to receive
spittle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitchcock</ent><br/
<hw>Spitch"cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[1st
<ets>spit</ets> + <ets>cock</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>To
split (as an eel) lengthwise, and broil it, or fry it in hot
fat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitchcock</ent><br/
<hw>Spitch"cock`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>An eel
split and broiled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitchcocked</ent><br/
<hw>Spitch"cocked`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Cookery)</fld>
<def>Broiled or fried after being split lengthwise; -- said of
eels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spit curl</ent><br/
<hw>Spit" curl`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A little lock of hair, plastered
in a spiral form on the temple or forehead with spittle, or other
adhesive substance.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spite</ent><br/
<hw>Spite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbreviated fr.
<ets>despite</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Ill-will or hatred toward
another, accompanied with the disposition to irritate, annoy, or thwart;
petty malice; grudge; rancor; despite.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is the deadly <qex>spite</qex> that angers.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Vexation; chagrin; mortification.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>In spite of</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Spite
of</b></col></mcol>, <cd>in opposition to all efforts of; in defiance or
contempt of; notwithstanding.</cd> <ldquo/Continuing, <xex>spite
of</xex> pain, to use a knee after it had been slightly injured.<rdquo/
<au>H. Spenser.</au> <ldquo/And saved me <xex>in spite of</xex> the
world, the devil, and myself.<rdquo/ <au>South.</au> <ldquo/<xex>In
spite of</xex> all applications, the patient grew worse every
day.<rdquo/ <au>Arbuthnot.</au> <see>See Syn. under
<er>Notwithstanding</er>.</see> -- <col><b>To owe one a spite</b></col>,
<cd>to entertain a mean hatred for him.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Pique, rancor; malevolence; grudge.</syn> --
<usage><er>Spite</er>, <er>Malice</er>. <xex>Malice</xex> has more
reference to the disposition, and <xex>spite</xex> to the manifestation
of it in words and actions. It is, therefore, meaner than
<xex>malice</xex>, thought not always more criminal. <ldquo/
<xex>Malice</xex> . . . is more frequently employed to express the
dispositions of inferior minds to execute every purpose of mischief
within the more limited circle of their abilities.<rdquo/
<au>Cogan.</au> <ldquo/Consider eke, that <xex>spite</xex> availeth
naught.<rdquo/ <au>Wyatt.</au> See <er>Pique</er>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spite</ent><br/
<hw>Spite</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spiting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be angry at; to
hate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Danes, then . . . pagans, <qex>spited</qex> places of
religion.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To treat maliciously; to try to injure or
thwart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fill with spite; to offend; to vex.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Darius, <qex>spited</qex> at the Magi, endeavored to abolish not
only their learning, but their language.</q> <rj><qau>Sir. W.
Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1389 --></p>

<p><ent>Spiteful</ent><br/
<hw>Spite"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Filled with, or
showing, spite; having a desire to vex, annoy, or injure; malignant;
malicious; <as>as, a <ex>spiteful</ex> person or act</as>.</def>
<au>Shak.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Spite"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<wf>Spite"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitfire</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"fire`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A violent,
irascible, or passionate person.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitful</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Spitfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A spadeful.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitous</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having spite;
spiteful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitously</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"ous*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Spitefully.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitscocked</ent><br/
<hw>Spits"cocked`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Spitchcocked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitted</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Spit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Put upon a spit; pierced as if by
a spit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Shot out long; -- said of antlers.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitted</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"ted</hw>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of <er>Spit</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>, to eject, to spit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitter</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spit</er> to
eject from the mouth.]</ety> <def>One who ejects saliva from the
mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitter</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spit</er> an iron
prong.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who puts meat on a spit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A young deer whose antlers begin
to shoot or become sharp; a brocket, or pricket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spittle</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spital</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spittle</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"tle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spit</er> to
spade.]</ety> <def>To dig or stir with a small spade.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spittle</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"tle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small sort of spade.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spittle</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"tle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Spit</er> to eject from
the mouth: cf. <er>Spattle</er>, and AS. <ets>sp<amac/tl</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The thick, moist matter which is secreted by the salivary glands;
saliva; spit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spittle insect</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Cuckoo spit</cref> <sd>(b)</sd>, under
<er>Cuckoo</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spittly</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"tly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like spittle;
slimy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spittoon</ent><br/
<hw>Spit*toon"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A spitbox; a
cuspidor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spit-venom</ent><br/
<hw>Spit"-ven"om</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Poison spittle;
poison ejected from the mouth.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitz dog</ent><br/
<hw>Spitz" dog"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[G. <ets>spitz</ets>,
<ets>spitzhund</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A breed of dogs
having erect ears and long silky hair, usually white; -- called also
<altname>Pomeranian dog</altname>, and
<altname>louploup</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spitzenburgh</ent><br/
<hw>Spitz"en*burgh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of red
and yellow apple, of medium size and spicy flavor. It originated at
Newtown, on Long Island.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splanchnapophysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Splanch`napoph"y*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Splanchnapophyses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Splanchnic</er>, and <er>Apophysis</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Any element of the skeleton in relation with the
alimentary canal, as the jaws and hyoidean apparatus.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Splanch`nap`o*phys"i*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au> Mivart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splanchnic</ent><br/
<hw>Splanch"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/<?/<?/ an
entrail.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the viscera;
visceral.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splanchnography</ent><br/
<hw>Splanch*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ an entrail + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Splanchnology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splanchnology</ent><br/
<hw>Splanch*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ an entrail + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That part of
anatomy which treats of the viscera; also, a treatise on the
viscera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splanchnopleure</ent><br/
<hw>Splanch"no*pleure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ an entrail + <?/<?/<?/<?/ side.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The inner, or visceral, one of the two lamellae into which the
vertebrate blastoderm divides on either side of the notochord, and from
which the walls of the enteric canal and the umbilical vesicle are
developed. See <er>Somatopleure</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Splanch`no*pleu"ric</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splanchno-skeleton</ent><br/
<hw>Splanch`no-skel"e*ton</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/ an entrail + E. <ets>skeleton</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>That part of the skeleton connected with the
sense organs and the viscera.</def> <rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splanchnotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Splanch*not"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/<?/<?/<?/<?/ an entrail + <?/<?/<?/<?/ to cut.]</ety> <def>The
dissection, or anatomy, of the viscera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splandrel</ent><br/
<hw>Splan"drel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spandrel</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splash</ent><br/
<hw>Splash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Splashed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Splashing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to
<ets>plash</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike and dash about, as
water, mud, etc.; to plash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To spatter water, mud, etc., upon; to wet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splash</ent><br/
<hw>Splash</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To strike and dash about water,
mud, etc.; to dash in such a way as to spatter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splash</ent><br/
<hw>Splash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Water, or water and
dirt, thrown upon anything, or thrown from a puddle or the like; also, a
spot or daub, as of matter which wets or disfigures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A noise made by striking upon or in a
liquid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splashboard</ent><br/
<hw>Splash"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A guard in the
front part of vehicle, to prevent splashing by a mud or water from the
horse's heels; -- in the United States commonly called
<xex>dashboard</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splasher</ent><br/
<hw>Splash"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, splashes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the guards over the wheels, as of a carriage,
locomotive, etc.</def> <rj><au>Weale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A guard to keep off splashes from
anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splashy</ent><br/
<hw>Splash"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of dirty water;
wet and muddy, so as be easily splashed about; slushy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splatter</ent><br/
<hw>Splat"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To spatter;
to splash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splatterdash</ent><br/
<hw>Splat"ter*dash`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Uproar.</def>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splay</ent><br/
<hw>Splay</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. of
<ets>display</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To display; to spread.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Our ensigns <xex>splayed</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Gascoigne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To dislocate, as a shoulder bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To spay; to castrate.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To turn on one side; to render oblique; to slope or
slant, as the side of a door, window, etc.</def> <rj><au>Oxf.
Gloss.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splay</ent><br/
<hw>Splay</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Displayed; spread out; turned
outward; hence, flat; ungainly; <as>as, <ex>splay</ex>
shoulders</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sonwthing <qex>splay</qex>, something blunt-edged, unhandy, and
infelicitous.</q> <rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splay</ent><br/
<hw>Splay</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A slope or bevel,
especially of the sides of a door or window, by which the opening is
made larged at one face of the wall than at the other, or larger at each
of the faces than it is between them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splayfoot</ent><br/
<hw>Splay"foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Splayfeet</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A foot that is
abnormally flattened and spread out; flat foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splayfooted</ent><br/
<ent>Splayfoot</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Splay"foot`</hw>, <hw>Splay"foot`ed</hw> }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a splayfoot or splayfeet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splaymouth</ent><br/
<hw>Splay"mouth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Splaymouths</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A wide mouth; a mouth
stretched in derision.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splaymouthed</ent><br/
<hw>Splay`mouthed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
splaymouth.</def> <rj><au>T. Brown.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleen</ent><br/
<hw>Spleen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>splen</ets>,
Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ the milt or spleen, affection of the spleen; cf. L.
<ets>lien</ets>, <ets>plihan</ets>, <ets>pl<imac/han</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A peculiar glandlike but ductless
organ found near the stomach or intestine of most vertebrates and
connected with the vascular system; the milt. Its exact function in not
known.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anger; latent spite; ill humor; malice; <as>as, to
vent one's <ex>spleen</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In noble minds some dregs remain,<br/
Not yet purged off, of <qex>spleen</qex> and sour disdain.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A fit of anger; choler.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A sudden motion or action; a fit; a freak; a
whim.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A thousand <qex>spleens</qex> bear her a thousand ways.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Melancholy; hypochondriacal affections.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bodies changed to various forms by <qex>spleen</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a luxury in self-dispraise:<br/
And inward self-disparagement affords<br/
To meditative <qex>spleen</qex> a grateful feast.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A fit of immoderate laughter or merriment.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy silly thought enforces my <qex>spleen</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleen</ent><br/
<hw>Spleen</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To dislike.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hacket.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleened</ent><br/
<hw>Spleened</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Deprived
of the spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Angered; annoyed.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>R. North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleenful</ent><br/
<hw>Spleen"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Displaying, or
affected with, spleen; angry; fretful; melancholy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Myself have calmed their <qex>spleenful</qex> mutiny.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then rode Geraint, a little <qex>spleenful</qex> yet,<br/
Across the bridge that spann'd the dry ravine.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleenish</ent><br/
<hw>Spleen"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Spleeny; affected with spleen;
fretful.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Spleen"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spleen"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleenless</ent><br/
<hw>Spleen"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no spleen; hence, kind;
gentle; mild.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleenwort</ent><br/
<hw>Spleen"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spleen</ets> + <ets>wort</ets>; cf. L. <ets>splenium</ets>,
<ets>asplenium</ets>, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/<?/, <?/<?/<?/. ]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any fern of the genus <gen>Asplenium</gen>, some
species of which were anciently used as remedies for disorders of the
spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleeny</ent><br/
<hw>Spleen"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Irritable; peevish; fretful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Spleeny</qex> Lutheran, and not wholesome to<br/
Our cause.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Affected with nervous complaints;
melancholy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleget</ent><br/
<hw>Spleg"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Pledget</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A cloth dipped in a
liquid for washing a sore.</def> <rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenalgia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sple*nal"gi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/<?/<?/ spleen + <?/<?/<?/ pain.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Pain over the region of the spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenculus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Splen"cu*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Splenculi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., dim. of L.
<ets>splen</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A lienculus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendent</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>splendens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>splendere</ets>
to shine.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Shining; glossy; beaming with light;
lustrous; <as>as, <ex>splendent</ex> planets; <ex>splendent</ex>
metals</as>. See the Note under 3d <er>Luster</er>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Very conspicuous; illustrious.</def> <ldquo/Great
and <xex>splendent</xex> fortunes.<rdquo/<-- MW10 "brillian; splendent
genius" --> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendid</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"did</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>splendidus</ets>, fr. <ets>splendere</ets> shine; cf. Lith.
<ets>splend<eum/ti</ets>: cf. F. <ets>splendide</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Possessing or displaying splendor; shining; very
bright; <as>as, a <ex>splendid</ex> sun</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Showy; magnificent; sumptuous; pompous; <as>as, a
<ex>splendid</ex> palace; a <ex>splendid</ex> procession or
pageant.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Illustrious; heroic; brilliant; celebrated; famous;
<as>as, a <ex>splendid</ex> victory or reputation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendidious</ent><br/
<hw>Splen*did"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Splendid.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendidly</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"did*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a splendid
manner; magnificently.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendidness</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"did*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
splendid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendidous</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"did*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Splendid.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Splen*dif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Splendor-bearing; splendid.</def> <au>Bale (1538).</au> <ldquo/A
<xex>splendiferous</xex> woman.<rdquo/ <au>Haliburton.</au> <mark>[Now
used humorously.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendor</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"dor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L. fr.
<ets>splendere</ets> to shine: cf. F. <ets>splendeur</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Great brightness; brilliant luster; brilliancy; <as>as,
the <ex>splendor</ex> of the sun</as>.</def> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Magnificence; pomp; parade; <as>as, the
<ex>splendor</ex> of equipage, ceremonies, processions, and the
like</as>.</def> <ldquo/Rejoice in <xex>splendor</xex> of mine
own.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Brilliancy; glory; <as>as, the <ex>splendor</ex> of
a victory</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Luster; brilliancy; magnifience; gorgeousness;
display; showiness; pomp; parade; grandeur.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splendorous</ent><br/
<ent>Splendrous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Splen"drous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Splen"dor*ous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Splendid.</def>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"e*tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spleneticus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>spl<eacute/n<eacute/tique</ets>. See
<er>Spleen</er>.]</ety> <def>Affected with spleen; malicious; spiteful;
peevish; fretful.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Splenetic</xex> guffaw.<rdquo/
<rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You humor me when I am sick;<br/
Why not when I am <qex>splenetic</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Morese; gloomy; sullen; peevish;
fretful.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenetic</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"e*tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person affected with
spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenetical</ent><br/
<hw>Sple*net"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Splenetic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenetically</ent><br/
<hw>Sple*net"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a splenetical
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenial</ent><br/
<hw>Sple"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>splenium</ets> a plaster, a patch, Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ a
bandage.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Designating the
splenial bone.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to the splenial
bone or splenius muscle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Splenial bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a thin
splintlike bone on the inner side of the proximal portion of the
mandible of many vertebrates.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenial</ent><br/
<hw>Sple"ni*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The splenial
bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenic</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>splenicus</ets>, Gr. <grk>splhniko`s</grk>; cf. F.
<ets>spl<eacute/nique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to the spleen; lienal; <as>as, the <ex>splenic</ex>
vein</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Splenic apoplexy</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Splenic fever</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Anthrax</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenical</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Splenic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenish</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Spleenish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sple*ni"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>splhni^tis</grk> of the spleen.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenitive</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Splenetic.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Even and smooth as seemed the temperament of the nonchalant,
languid Virginian -- not <qex>splenitive</qex> or rash.</q> <rj><qau>T.
N. Page.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sple"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos><ety>[L., a plaster,
a patch, from Gr. <?/<?/<?/<?/ a bandage, compress.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The thickened posterior border of the corpus
callosum; -- so called in allusion to its shape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenius</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sple"ni*us</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A flat muscle of the back of the neck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenization</ent><br/
<hw>Splen`i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A morbid state of the lung produced by inflammation, in which its
tissue resembles that of the spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenocele</ent><br/
<hw>Splen"o*cele</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>splh`n</grk> spleen + <?/<?/<?/ a tumor.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Hernia formed by the spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenography</ent><br/
<hw>Sple*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>splh`n</grk> spleen + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A description
of the spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sple"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos><ety>[Gr.
<grk>splh`n</grk> spleen + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Resembling the spleen; spleenlike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenology</ent><br/
<hw>Sple*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>splh`n</grk> spleen + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The branch of
science which treats of the spleen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splenotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Sple*not"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>splh`n</grk> spleen + <grk>te`mnein</grk> to cut.]</ety>
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Dissection or anatomy of the
spleen.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An incision into the
spleen; removal of the spleen by incision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splent</ent><br/
<hw>Splent</hw> <pr>(spl<ecr/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>See <er>Splent</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>See <cref>Splent coal</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Splent coal</b></col>, <cd>an inferior kind of cannel
coal from Scotch collieries; -- called also <altname>splent</altname>,
<altname>splint</altname>, and <altname>splint
coal</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spleuchan</ent><br/
<hw>Spleu"chan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>spliuchan</ets>.]</ety> <def>A pouch, as for tobacco.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splice</ent><br/
<hw>Splice</hw> <pr>(spl<imac/s)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spliced</conjf>
<pr>(spl<imac/st)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Splicing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[D.
<ets>splitsen</ets>, <ets>splitten</ets>; akin to G.
<ets>splissen</ets>, Sw. <ets>splissa</ets>, Dan. <ets>splisse</ets>,
and E. <ets>split</ets>; -- from the dividing or splitting the ends into
separate strands. See <er>Split</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To unite, as two ropes, or parts of a rope, by a
particular manner of interweaving the strands, -- the union being
between two ends, or between an end and the body of a rope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To unite, as spars, timbers, rails, etc., by lapping
the two ends together, or by applying a piece which laps upon the two
ends, and then binding, or in any way making fast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To unite in marriage.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Splice grafting</b></col>.<cd>ee under
<er>Grafting</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To splice the main brace</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to give out, or drink, an extra allowance of
spirits on occasion of special exposure to wet or cold, or to severe
fatigue; hence, to take a dram.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splice</ent><br/
<hw>Splice</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A junction or joining made by
splicing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spline</ent><br/
<hw>Spline</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
rectangular piece fitting grooves like key seats in a hub and a shaft,
so that while the one may slide endwise on the other, both must revolve
together; a feather; also, sometimes, a groove to receive such a
rectangular piece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A long, flexible piece of wood sometimes used as a
ruler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splining</ent><br/
<hw>Splin"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
spline.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Splining machine</b></col>, <cd>a machine tool for
cutting grooves, key seats, or slots; a slotting machine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splint</ent><br/
<hw>Splint</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Akin to D.
<ets>splinter</ets>,G. <ets>splinter</ets>, <ets>splitter</ets>, Dan.
<ets>splint</ets>, Sw. <ets>splint</ets> a kind of spike, a forelock (in
nautical use), Sw. <ets>splinta</ets>to splint, splinter, Dan.
<ets>splinte</ets>, and E. <ets>split</ets>. See <er>Split</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Splent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A piece split
off; a splinter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>A thin piece of wood, or other
substance, used to keep in place, or protect, an injured part,
especially a broken bone when set.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A splint bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A disease affecting the splint
bones, as a callosity or hard excrescence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Anc. Armor.)</fld> <def>One of the small plates of
metal used in making splint armor. See <cref>Splint armor</cref>,
below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The knees and feet were defended by <qex>splints</qex>, or thin
plates of steel.</q> <rj><qau>Sir. W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Splint, or splent, coal. See <cref>Splent
coal</cref>, under <er>Splent</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Splint armor</b></col>,<cd>a kind of ancient armor formed
of thin plates of metal, usually overlapping each other and allowing the
limbs to move freely.</cd> -- <col><b>Splint bone</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>one of the rudimentary, splintlike metacarpal or
metatarsal bones on either side of the cannon bone in the limbs of the
horse and allied animals.</cd> -- <col><b>Splint coal</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Splent coal</cref>, under <er>Splent</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splint</ent><br/
<hw>Splint</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Splinted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Splinting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To split into splints, or thin,
slender pieces; to splinter; to shiver.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Florio.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fasten or confine with splints, as a broken limb.
See <er>Splint</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splinter</ent><br/
<hw>Splin"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Splintered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Splintering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. LG.
<ets>splittern</ets>, <ets>splinteren</ets>. See <er>Splint</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, <er>Split</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To split or rend
into long, thin pieces; to shiver; <as>as, the lightning
<ex>splinters</ex> a tree</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>After <qex>splintering</qex> their lances, they wheeled about, and
. . . abandoned the field to the enemy.</q>
<rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fasten or confine with splinters, or splints, as
a broken limb.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Wren.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splinter</ent><br/
<hw>Splin"ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become split into long
pieces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splinter</ent><br/
<hw>Splin"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Splinter</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>, or <er>Splint</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A thin
piece split or rent off lengthwise, as from wood, bone, or other solid
substance; a thin piece; a sliver; <as>as, <ex>splinters</ex> of a
ship's mast rent off by a shot</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1390 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Splinter bar</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A crossbar in a
coach, which supports the springs</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The bar to
which the traces are attached; a roller bolt; a
whiffletree.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splinterproof</ent><br/
<hw>Splin"ter*proof`</hw> <pr>(spl<icr/n"t<etil/r*pr<oomac/f`)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>Proof against the splinters, or
fragments, of bursting shells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splintery</ent><br/
<hw>Splin"ter*y</hw> <pr>(spl<icr/n"t<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Consisting of splinters; resembling splinters; <as>as, the
<ex>splintery</ex> fracture of a mineral</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split</ent><br/
<hw>Split</hw> <pr>(spl<icr/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Split</conjf> (<conjf>Splitted</conjf>,
<mark>R.</mark>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Splitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably of Scand. or Low
German origin; cf. Dan. <ets>splitte</ets>, LG. <ets>splitten</ets>, OD.
<ets>splitten</ets>, <ets>spletten</ets>, D. <ets>splijten</ets>, G.
<ets>spleissen</ets>, MHG. <ets>spl<imac/zen</ets>. Cf. <er>Splice</er>,
<er>Splint</er>, <er>Splinter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To divide
lengthwise; to separate from end to end, esp. by force; to divide in the
direction of the grain or layers; to rive; to cleave; <as>as, to
<ex>split</ex> a piece of timber or a board; to <ex>split</ex> a gem; to
<ex>split</ex> a sheepskin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cold winter <qex>split</qex> the rocks in twain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To burst; to rupture; to rend; to tear
asunder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A huge vessel of exceeding hard marble <qex>split</qex> asunder by
congealed water.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To divide or break up into parts or divisions, as by
discord; to separate into parts or parties, as a political party; to
disunite.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To divide or separate into
components; -- often used with <xex>up</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>split</ex>
up sugar into alcohol and carbonic acid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To split hairs</b></col>, <cd>to make distinctions of
useless nicety.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split</ent><br/
<hw>Split</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To part asunder; to be
rent; to burst; <as>as, vessels <ex>split</ex> by the freezing of water
in them</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be broken; to be dashed to pieces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ship <qex>splits</qex> on the rock.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To separate into parties or factions.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To burst with laughter.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each had a gravity would make you <qex>split</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To divulge a secret; to betray confidence; to
peach.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Blackjack)</fld> <def>To divide one hand of
blackjack into two hands; -- a strategy allowed to a player when the
first two cards dealt to the player have the same value.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To leave; to depart (from a place or gathering);
<as>as, let's <ex>split</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To split on a rock</b></col>, <cd>to fail; to to err
fatally; to have the hopes and designs frustrated.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split</ent><br/
<hw>Split</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A crack, rent, or
longitudinal fissure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A breach or separation, as in a political party; a
division.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A piece that is split off, or made thin, by
splitting; a splinter; a fragment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <specif>Specif:</specif> <fld>(Leather Manuf.)</fld>,
<def>One of the sections of a skin made by dividing it into two or more
thicknesses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Faro)</fld> <def>A division of a stake happening
when two cards of the kind on which the stake is laid are dealt in the
same turn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Basketwork)</fld> <def>Any of the
three or four strips into which osiers are commonly cleft for certain
kinds of work; -- usually in <pluf>pl.</pluf></def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>Any of the dents of a reed.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>Any of the air currents in a mine formed by dividing a larger
current.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Short for <er>Split shot</er> or <er>split
stroke</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Gymnastics)</fld> <def>The feat of going down to
the floor so that the legs extend in a straight line, either with one on
each side or with one in front and the other behind.</def> <mark>[Cant
or Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A small bottle (containing about half a pint) of
some drink; -- so called as containing half the quantity of the
customary smaller commercial size of bottle; also, a drink of half the
usual quantity; a half glass.</def> <mark>[Cant or Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Finance)</fld> <def>The substitution of more than
one share of a corporation's stock for one share. The market price of
the stock usually drops in proportion to the increase in outstanding
shares of stock. The <ex>split</ex> may be in any ratio, <as>as, a
two-for-one <ex>split</ex>; a three-for-two
<ex>split</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Blackjack)</fld> <def>The division by a player of
one hand of blackjack into two hands, allowed when the first two cards
dealt to a player have the same value; the player who chooses to split
is obliged to increase the amount wagered by placing a sum equal to the
original bet on the new hand thus created. See <er>split</er>{6},
<pos>v.i.</pos></def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split</ent><br/
<hw>Split</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Divided; cleft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Divided deeply; cleft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Exchanges)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Divided so as to
be done or executed part at one time or price and part at another time
or price; -- said of an order, sale, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of
quotations, given in sixteenth, quotations in eighths being regular;
<as>as, 10<frac3x16/ is a <ex>split</ex> quotation</as>.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(London Stock Exchange)</fld> <def>Designating
ordinary stock that has been divided into preferred ordinary and
deferred ordinary.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Split pease</b></col>, <cd>hulled pease split for making
soup, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Split pin</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a
pin with one end split so that it may be spread open to secure it in its
place.</cd> -- <col><b>Split pulley</b></col>, <cd>a parting pulley. See
under <er>Pulley</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Split ring</b></col>, <cd>a ring
with overlapped or interlocked ends which may be sprung apart so that
objects, as keys, may be strung upon the ring or removed from it.</cd>
-- <col><b>Split ticket</b></col>, <cd>a ballot in which a voter votes
for a portion of the candidates nominated by one party, candidates of
other parties being substituted for those omitted.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split dynamometer</ent><br/
<hw>Split dynamometer</hw>. <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>An electric
dynamometer having two coils so arranged that one carries the primary
current, and the other the secondary current, of a
transformer.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splitfeet</ent><br/
<hw>Split"feet`</hw> <pr>(spl<icr/t"f<emac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Fissipedia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split infinitive</ent><br/
<hw>Split infinitive</hw>. <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A simple infinitive
with <it>to</it>, having a modifier between the verb and the
<it>to</it>; as in, <xex>to largely decrease</xex>. Called also
<altname>cleft infinitive</altname>.</def> <note>The use of the
<ex>split infinitive</ex> is commonly considered to be ungrammatical,
but by most grammarians it is considered acceptible.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split key</ent><br/
<hw>Split key</hw>. <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A key split at one end like
a split pin, for the same purpose.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>split stroke</ent><br/
<ent>split shot</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>split shot</hw> <it>or</it> <hw>split stroke</hw> }</mhw>.
<def>In croquet, etc., a shot or stroke in which one drives in different
directions one's own and the opponent's ball placed in
contact.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split stitch</ent><br/
<hw>Split stitch</hw>. <def>A stitch used in stem work to produce a fine
line, much used in old church embroidery to work the hands and faces of
figures.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split stuff</ent><br/
<hw>Split stuff</hw>. <def>Timber sawn into lengths and then
split.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split switch</ent><br/
<hw>Split switch</hw>. <fld>(Railroading)</fld> <def>same as <er>Point
switch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split-tail</ent><br/
<hw>Split"-tail`</hw> <pr>(-t<amac/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A California market fish
(<spn>Pogonichthys macrolepidotus</spn>) belonging to the Carp
family.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The pintail duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splitter</ent><br/
<hw>Split"ter</hw> <pr>(-t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or
that which, splits.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split-tongued</ent><br/
<hw>Split"-tongued`</hw> <pr>(spl<icr/t"t<ucr/ngd`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a forked tongue, as that of snakes and
some lizards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Split wheel</ent><br/
<hw>Split wheel</hw>. <def>same as <er>Split pulley</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splotch</ent><br/
<hw>Splotch</hw> <pr>(spl<ocr/ch)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Splash</er>.]</ety> <def>A spot; a stain; a daub.</def> <rj><au>R.
Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splotchy</ent><br/
<hw>Splotch"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered or
marked with splotches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splurge</ent><br/
<hw>Splurge</hw> <pr>(spl<ucir/rj)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A blustering
demonstration, or great effort; a great display.</def> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splurge</ent><br/
<hw>Splurge</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make a great display in any
way, especially in oratory.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. To spend money freely or extravagantly, in one episode; usu.
with "on" -- as, to <ex>splurge</ex> on a new hi-fi system. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- v.t. to spend extravagantly. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splutter</ent><br/
<hw>Splut"ter</hw> <pr>(spl<ucr/t"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spluttered</conjf>
<pr>(-t<etil/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spluttering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>splutter</ets>, eqivalent to <ets>sputter</ets>. Cf.
<er>Sputter</er>.]</ety> <def>To speak hastily and confusedly; to
sputter.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Carleton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splutter</ent><br/
<hw>Splut"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A confused noise, as of hasty
speaking.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Splutterer</ent><br/
<hw>Splut"ter*er</hw> <pr>(spl<ucr/t"t<etil/r<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who splutters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spodomancy</ent><br/
<hw>Spod"o*man`cy</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/d"<osl/*m<acr/n`s<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>spodo`s</grk> ashes +
<ets>-mancy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Divination by means of ashes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spodomantic</ent><br/
<hw>Spod`o*man"tic</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/d"<osl/*m<acr/n"t<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to spodomancy, or divination by means of
ashes.</def> <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spodumene</ent><br/
<hw>Spod"u*mene</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/d"<usl/*m<emac/n; 135)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, p. pr. pass. from <?/ to burn to ashes,
from <grk>spodo`s</grk> ashes; cf. F.
<ets>spodum<egrave/ne</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A mineral of
a white to yellowish, purplish, or emerald-green color, occurring in
prismatic crystals, often of great size. It is a silicate of alumina and
lithia. See <er>Hiddenite</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoffish</ent><br/
<hw>Spof"fish</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/f"f<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Probably from Prov. E. <ets>spoffle</ets> to be spoffish.]</ety>
<def>Earnest and active in matters of no moment; bustling.</def>
<mark>[Colloq. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoil</ent><br/
<hw>Spoil</hw> <pr>(spoil)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spoiled</conjf> <pr>(spoild)</pr> or
<conjf>Spoilt</conjf> <pr>(spoilt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spoiling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>spolier</ets>, OF.
<ets>espoillier</ets>, fr. L. <ets>spoliare</ets>, fr.
<ets>spolium</ets> spoil. Cf. <er>Despoil</er>,
<er>Spoliation</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To plunder; to strip by
violence; to pillage; to rob; -- with <xex>of</xex> before the name of
the thing taken; <as>as, to <ex>spoil</ex> one of his goods or
possessions</as>.</def> <ldquo/Ye shall <xex>spoil</xex> the
Egyptians.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ex. iii. 22.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My sons their old, unhappy sire despise,<br/
<qex>Spoiled</qex> of his kingdom, and deprived of eyes.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To seize by violence; to take by force; to
plunder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No man can enter into a strong man's house, and <qex>spoil</qex>
his goods, except he will first bind the strong man.</q> <rj><qau>Mark
iii. 27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to decay and perish; to corrupt; to
vitiate; to mar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Spiritual pride <qex>spoils</qex> many graces.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To render useless by injury; to injure fatally; to
ruin; to destroy; <as>as, to <ex>spoil</ex> paper; to have the crops
<ex>spoiled</ex> by insects; to <ex>spoil</ex> the eyes by
reading.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoil</ent><br/
<hw>Spoil</hw> <pr>(spoil)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
practice plunder or robbery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Outlaws, which, lurking in woods, used to break forth to rob and
<qex>spoil</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lose the valuable qualities; to be corrupted; to
decay; <as>as, fruit will soon <ex>spoil</ex> in warm
weather</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoil</ent><br/
<hw>Spoil</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>espoille</ets>, L.
<ets>spolium</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is taken from
another by violence; especially, the plunder taken from an enemy;
pillage; booty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gentle gales,<br/
Fanning their odoriferous wings, dispense<br/
Native perfumes, and whisper whence they stole<br/
Those balmy <qex>spoils</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Public offices and their emoluments regarded as the
peculiar property of a successful party or faction, to be bestowed for
its own advantage; -- commonly in the plural; <as>as, to the victor
belong the <ex>spoils</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From a principle of gratitude I adhered to the coalition; my vote
was counted in the day of battle, but I was overlooked in the division
of the <qex>spoil</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Gibbon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is gained by strength or
effort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each science and each art his <qex>spoil</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The act or practice of plundering; robbery;
waste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The man that hath no music in himself,<br/
Nor is not moved with concord of sweet sounds,<br/
Is fit for treason, stratagems, and <qex>spoils</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Corruption; cause of corruption.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Villainous company hath been the <qex>spoil</qex> of me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The slough, or cast skin, of a serpent or other
animal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spoil bank</b></col>, <cd>a bank formed by the earth
taken from an excavation, as of a canal.</cd> -- <col><b>The spoils
system</b></col>, <cd>the theory or practice of regarding public offices
and their emoluments as so much plunder to be distributed among their
active partisans by those who are chosen to responsible offices of
administration.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoilable</ent><br/
<hw>Spoil"a*ble</hw> <pr>(spoil"<adot/*b'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Capable of being spoiled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoiler</ent><br/
<hw>Spoil"er</hw> <pr>(spoil"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who spoils; a plunderer; a pillager; a robber; a
despoiler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who corrupts, mars, or renders
useless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Aeronautics)</fld> <def>A device attached to the
wing of an airplane or other airfoil, which breaks the smooth flow of
air during flight and decreases the lift of the airfoil; -- used to
control the attitude of the airplane during banking or
descent.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A device attached to a car to decrease lift and
increase traction, usually shaped as a flat surface and attached above
the rear of the car, and working on the same principal as the aircraft
spoiler{3}.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoilfive</ent><br/
<hw>Spoil"five`</hw> <pr>(spoil"f<imac/v`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
certain game at cards in which, if no player wins three of the five
tricks possible on any deal, the game is said to be
<xex>spoiled</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoilful</ent><br/
<hw>Spoil"ful</hw> <pr>(spoil"f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Wasteful; rapacious.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoilsman</ent><br/
<hw>Spoils"man</hw> <pr>(spoilz"m<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spoilsmen</plw> <pr>(spoilz"m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu>
<def>One who serves a cause or a party for a share of the spoils; in
United States politics, one who makes or recognizes a demand for public
office on the ground of partisan service; also, one who sanctions such a
policy in appointments to the public service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoilsmonger</ent><br/
<hw>Spoils"mon`ger</hw> <pr>(spoilz"m<ucr/<nsm/`g<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who promises or distributes public offices and
their emoluments as the price of services to a party or its
leaders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoke</ent><br/
<hw>Spoke</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/k)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Speak</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoke</ent><br/
<hw>Spoke</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spoke</ets>,
<ets>spake</ets>, AS. <ets>sp<amac/ca</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>speek</ets>, LG. <ets>speke</ets>, OHG. <ets>speihha</ets>, G.
<ets>speiche</ets>. <root/170. Cf. <er>Spike</er> a nail.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The radius or ray of a wheel; one of the small bars
which are inserted in the hub, or nave, and which serve to support the
rim or felly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A projecting handle of a steering
wheel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A rung, or round, of a ladder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A contrivance for fastening the wheel of a vehicle,
to prevent it from turning in going down a hill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To put a spoke in one's wheel</b></col>, <cd>to thwart or
obstruct one in the execution of some design.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoke</ent><br/
<hw>Spoke</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spoked</conjf> <pr>(sp<omac/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spoking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To furnish with spokes, as a
wheel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoken</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"ken</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"k'n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[p. p. of
<er>Speak</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Uttered in speech; delivered by
word of mouth; oral; <as>as, a <ex>spoken</ex> narrative; the
<ex>spoken</ex> word</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Characterized by a certain manner or style in
speaking; -- often in composition; <as>as, a pleasant-<ex>spoken</ex>
man</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Methinks you 're better <qex>spoken</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spokeshave</ent><br/
<hw>Spoke"shave`</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/k"sh<amac/v`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A kind of drawing knife or planing tool for dressing the spokes of
wheels, the shells of blocks, and other curved work.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spokesman</ent><br/
<hw>Spokes"man</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/ks"m<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spokesmen</plw> <pr>(-m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[<ets>Speak</ets>, <ets>spoke</ets> + <ets>man</ets>.]</ety>
<def>One who speaks for another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He shall be thy <qex>spokesman</qex> unto the people.</q>
<rj><qau>Ex. iv. 16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoliate</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"li*ate</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"l<icr/*<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t. &
i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spoliated</conjf>
<pr>(-<amac/`t<ecr/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spoliating</conjf> <pr>(-<amac/`t<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[L. <ets>spoliatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>spoliare</ets> to spoil.
See <er>Spoil</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To plunder; to pillage;
to despoil; to rob.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoliation</ent><br/
<hw>Spo`li*a"tion</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"l<icr/*<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spoliatio</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>spoliation</ets>. See <er>Spoil</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of plundering; robbery; deprivation;
despoliation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Legal <qex>spoliation</qex>, which will impoverish one part of the
community in order to corrupt the remainder.</q> <rj><qau>Sir G. C.
Lewis.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Robbery or plunder in war; especially, the
authorized act or practice of plundering neutrals at sea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The act of an
incumbent in taking the fruits of his benefice without right, but under
a pretended title.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A process for possession of a church in a spiritual
court.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Injury done to a
document.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoliative</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"li*a*tive</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"l<icr/*<asl/*t<icr/v)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>spoliatif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Serving to
take away, diminish, or rob; esp. <fld>(Med.)</fld>, serving to diminish
sensibly the amount of blood in the body; <as>as, <ex>spoliative</ex>
bloodletting</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoliator</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"li*a`tor</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"l<icr/*<amac/`t<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who spoliates; a spoiler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoliatory</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"li*a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"l<icr/*<adot/*t<osl/*r<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to spoil; destructive; spoliative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spondaical</ent><br/
<ent>Spondaic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spon*da"ic</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n*d<amac/"<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Spon*da"ic*al</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>spondaicus</ets>, <ets>spondiacus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>sponda<ium/que</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a
spondee; consisting of spondees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Containing spondees in excess; marked by spondees;
<as>as, a <ex>spondaic</ex> hexameter, <it>i. e.</it>, one which has a
spondee instead of a dactyl in the fifth foot</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spondee</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"dee</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n"d<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spondeus</ets>, Gr. <?/ (sc. <?/), fr. <?/ a drink offering,
libation, fr. <?/ to pour out, make a libation: cf. F.
<ets>spond<eacute/e</ets>. So called because at libations slow, solemn
melodies were used, chiefly in this meter.]</ety> <fld>(Pros.)</fld>
<def>A poetic foot of two long syllables, as in the Latin word
<xex>l<emac/g<emac/s</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spondulics</ent><br/
<hw>Spon*du"lics</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n*d<umac/"l<icr/ks)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Money.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spondyle</ent><br/
<ent>Spondyl</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spon"dyl</hw>, <hw>Spon"dyle</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(sp<ocr/n"d<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spondylus</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/: cf. F. <ets>spondyle</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A joint of the backbone; a vertebra.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spong</ent><br/
<hw>Spong</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>An irregular, narrow, projecting part of a
field.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponge</ent><br/
<hw>Sponge</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/nj)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>esponge</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/ponge</ets>, L. <ets>spongia</ets>,
Gr. <grk>spoggia`</grk>, <grk>spo`ggos</grk>. Cf. <er>Fungus</er>,
<er>Spunk</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Formerly written also
<asp>spunge</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one
of numerous species of Spongiae, or Porifera. See <xex>Illust.</xex> and
Note under <er>Spongiae</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The elastic fibrous skeleton of many species of
horny Spongiae (Keratosa), used for many purposes, especially the
varieties of the genus <gen>Spongia</gen>. The most valuable sponges are
found in the Mediterranean and the Red Sea, and on the coasts of Florida
and the West Indies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>One who lives upon others; a
pertinacious and indolent dependent; a parasite; a sponger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Any spongelike substance.</def>
<specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Dough before it is
kneaded and formed into loaves, and after it is converted into a light,
spongy mass by the agency of the yeast or leaven.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Iron from the puddling furnace, in a pasty condition.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>Iron ore, in masses, reduced but not melted or
worked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Gun.)</fld> <def>A mop for cleaning the bore of a
cannon after a discharge. It consists of a cylinder of wood, covered
with sheepskin with the wool on, or cloth with a heavy looped nap, and
having a handle, or staff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>The extremity, or point, of a
horseshoe, answering to the heel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bath sponge</b></col>, <cd>any one of several varieties
of coarse commercial sponges, especially <spn>Spongia equina</spn>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Cup sponge</b></col>, <cd>a toilet sponge growing in a
cup-shaped form.</cd> -- <col><b>Glass sponge</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Glass-sponge</er>, in the Vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Glove
sponge</b></col>, <cd>a variety of commercial sponge (<spn>Spongia
officinalis</spn>, variety <varn>tubulifera</varn>), having very fine
fibers, native of Florida, and the West Indies.</cd> -- <col><b>Grass
sponge</b></col>, <cd>any one of several varieties of coarse commercial
sponges having the surface irregularly tufted, as <spn>Spongia
graminea</spn>, and <spn>Spongia equina</spn>, variety
<varn>cerebriformis</varn>, of Florida and the West Indies.</cd> --
<col><b>Horse sponge</b></col>, <cd>a coarse commercial sponge,
especially <spn>Spongia equina</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Platinum
sponge</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Platinum</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pyrotechnical sponge</b></col>, <cd>a
substance made of mushrooms or fungi, which are boiled in water, dried,
and beaten, then put in a strong lye prepared with saltpeter, and again
dried in an oven. This makes the black match, or tinder, brought from
Germany.</cd> -- <col><b>Sheep's-wool sponge</b></col>, <cd>a fine and
durable commercial sponge (<spn>Spongia equina</spn>, variety
<varn>gossypina</varn>) found in Florida and the West Indies. The
surface is covered with larger and smaller tufts, having the oscula
between them.</cd> -- <col><b>Sponge cake</b></col>, <cd>a kind of sweet
cake which is light and spongy.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sponge
lead</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Spongy lead</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>metallic lead brought to a spongy form by
reduction of lead salts, or by compressing finely divided lead; -- used
in secondary batteries and otherwise.</cd> -- <col><b>Sponge
tree</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a tropical leguminous tree
(<spn>Acacia Farnesiana</spn>), with deliciously fragrant flowers, which
are used in perfumery.</cd> -- <col><b>Toilet sponge</b></col>, <cd>a
very fine and superior variety of Mediterranean sponge (<spn>Spongia
officinalis</spn>, variety <varn>Mediterranea</varn>); -- called also
<altname>Turkish sponge</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>To set a
sponge</b></col> <fld>(Cookery)</fld>, <cd>to leaven a small mass of
flour, to be used in leavening a larger quantity.</cd> -- <col><b>To
throw up the sponge</b></col>, <cd>to give up a contest; to acknowledge
defeat; -- from a custom of the prize ring, the person employed to
sponge a pugilist between rounds throwing his sponge in the air in token
of defeat; -- now, <altname>throw in the towel</altname> is more common,
and has the same origin and meaning.</cd> <mark>[Cant or Slang]</mark>
<ldquo/He was too brave a man <xex>to throw up the sponge</xex> to
fate.<rdquo/ <au>Lowell.</au> -- <col><b>Vegetable sponge</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Loof</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Velvet
sponge</b></col>, <cd>a fine, soft commercial sponge (<spn>Spongia
equina</spn>, variety <varn>meandriniformis</varn>) found in Florida and
the West Indies.</cd> -- <col><b>Vitreous sponge</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Glass-sponge</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Yellow sponge</b></col>, <cd>a
common and valuable commercial sponge (<spn>Spongia agaricina</spn>,
variety <varn>corlosia</varn>) found in Florida and the West
Indies.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponge</ent><br/
<hw>Sponge</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sponged</conjf> <pr>(sp<ucr/njd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sponging</conjf> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To cleanse or wipe with a sponge; <as>as, to
<ex>sponge</ex> a slate or a cannon</as>; to wet with a sponge; <as>as,
to <ex>sponge</ex> cloth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wipe out with a sponge, as letters or writing; to
efface; to destroy all trace of.</def> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: To deprive of something by imposition.</def>
<ldquo/How came such multitudes of our nation . . . to be
<xex>sponged</xex> of their plate and their money?<rdquo/
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: To get by imposition or mean arts without
cost; <as>as, to <ex>sponge</ex> a breakfast</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponge</ent><br/
<hw>Sponge</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To suck in, or
imbibe, as a sponge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To gain by mean arts, by intrusion, or hanging
on; <as>as, an idler <ex>sponges</ex> on his neighbor</as>.</def>
<rj><au>E. Eggleston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fly is an intruder, and a common smell-feast, that
<qex>sponges</qex> upon other people's trenchers.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be converted, as dough, into a light, spongy mass
by the agency of yeast, or leaven.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongelet</ent><br/
<hw>Sponge"let</hw> <pr>(-l<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spongiole</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongeous</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"geous</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Spongious</er>.]</ety> <def>Resembling sponge; having the nature or
qualities of sponge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponger</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"ger</hw> <pr>(-j<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who sponges, or uses a sponge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One employed in gathering sponges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: A parasitical dependent; a
hanger-on.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1391 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Spongiae</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spon"gi*ae</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n"j<icr/*<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sponge</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
grand division of the animal kingdom which includes the sponges; --
called also <altname>Spongida</altname>, <altname>Spongiaria</altname>,
<altname>Spongiozoa</altname>, and
<altname>Porifera</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the Spongiae, the soft sarcode of the body is usually
supported by a skeleton consisting of horny fibers, or of siliceous or
calcareous spicules. The common sponges contain larger and smaller
cavities and canals, and numerous small ampullae which are lined with
ciliated cells capable of taking in solid food. The outer surface
usually has minute pores through which water enters, and large openings
for its exit. Sponges produce eggs and spermatozoa, and the egg when
fertilized undergoes segmentation to form a ciliated embryo.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spon"gi*da</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n"j<icr/*d<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>Spongiae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongiform</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"gi*form</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a sponge; soft and porous;
porous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongilla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spon*gil"la</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n*j<icr/l"l<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., dim. of <ets>spongia</ets> a sponge.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of siliceous sponges found in fresh
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongin</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"gin</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>The chemical basis of sponge tissue, a
nitrogenous, hornlike substance which on decomposition with sulphuric
acid yields leucin and glycocoll.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponginess</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"gi*ness</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The quality or state of being spongy.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponging</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"ging</hw> <pr>(-j<icr/ng)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Sponge</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sponging house</b></col> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>a
bailiff's or other house in which debtors are put before being taken to
jail, or until they compromise with their creditors. At these houses
extortionate charges are commonly made for food, lodging,
etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongiole</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"gi*ole</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<icr/*<omac/l; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spongiola</ets> a rose gall, small roots,
dim. of <ets>spongia</ets>: cf. F. <ets>spongiole</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A supposed spongelike expansion of the tip of a
rootlet for absorbing water; -- called also
<altname>spongelet</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongiolite</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"gi*o*lite</hw> <pr>(-<osl/*l<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>spoggia`</grk> sponge + <ets>-lite</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>One of the microscopic siliceous spicules
which occur abundantly in the texture of sponges, and are sometimes
found fossil, as in flints.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongiopilin</ent><br/
<hw>Spon`gi*o*pi"lin</hw> <pr>(-<osl/*p<imac/"l<icr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>spoggi`on</grk>, dim. of
<grk>spo`ggos</grk> a sponge + <grk>pi^los</grk> felt.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A kind of cloth interwoven with small pieces of
sponge and rendered waterproof on one side by a covering of rubber. When
moistened with hot water it is used as a poultice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongious</ent><br/
<ent>Spongiose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spon"gi*ose`</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<icr/*<omac/s`)</pr>,
<hw>Spon"gi*ous</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<icr/*<ucr/s)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spongiosus</ets>, <ets>spongeosus</ets>: cf.
F. <ets>spongieux</ets>. See <er>Sponge</er>.]</ety> <def>Somewhat
spongy; spongelike; full of small cavities like sponge; <as>as,
<ex>spongious</ex> bones</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongiozoa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spon`gi*o*zo"a</hw>
<pr>(sp<ocr/n`j<icr/*<osl/*z<omac/"<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., Gr. <grk>spoggia`</grk> sponge + <grk>zw^,on</grk> an
animal.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Spongiae</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongoblast</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"go*blast</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/<nsm/"g<osl/*bl<acr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>spo`ggos</grk> sponge +
<ets>-blast</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the cells
which, in sponges, secrete the spongin, or the material of the horny
fibers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongoid</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"goid</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"goid <it>or</it> sp<ocr/<nsm/"-;
277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>spo`ggos</grk> sponge +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling sponge; like sponge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spongy</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"gy</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n"j<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Soft, and full of cavities; of an open, loose, pliable texture;
<as>as, a <ex>spongy</ex> excrescence; <ex>spongy</ex> earth;
<ex>spongy</ex> cake; <ex>spongy</ex> bones.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wet; drenched; soaked and soft, like sponge;
rainy.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Spongy</xex> April.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having the quality of imbibing fluids, like a
sponge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spongy lead</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sponge
lead. See under <er>Sponge</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spongy
platinum</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Platinum</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponk</ent><br/
<hw>Sponk</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spunk</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponsal</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"sal</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n"s<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sponsalis</ets>, fr. <ets>sponsus</ets> a betrothal, fr.
<ets>spondere</ets>, <ets>sponsum</ets>, to betroth. See
<er>Spouse</er>, and cf. <er>Espousal</er>, <er>Spousal</er>.]</ety>
<def>Relating to marriage, or to a spouse; spousal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponsible</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"si*ble</hw> <pr>(-s<icr/*b'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev.
from <ets>responsible</ets>.]</ety> <def>Responsible; worthy of
credit.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponsion</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"sion</hw> <pr>(-sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sponsio</ets>, fr. <ets>spondere</ets>, <ets>sponsum</ets>, to
promise solemnly.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of becoming surety for
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Internat. Law)</fld> <def>An act or engagement on
behalf of a state, by an agent not specially authorized for the purpose,
or by one who exceeds the limits of authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponsional</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"sion*al</hw> <pr>(-<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a pledge or agreement; responsible.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is righteous even in that representative and
<qex>sponsional</qex> person he put on.</q> <rj><qau>Abp.
Leighton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponson</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"son</hw> <pr>(-s<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the triangular
platforms in front of, and abaft, the paddle boxes of a steamboat.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the slanting supports under the guards of a
steamboat.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>One of the armored projections fitted
with gun ports, used on modern war vessels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponsor</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"sor</hw> <pr>(-s<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from
<ets>spondere</ets>, <ets>sponsum</ets>, to engage one's self. See
<er>Spouse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who binds himself to answer
for another, and is responsible for his default; a surety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who at the baptism of an infant professes the
Christian faith in its name, and guarantees its religious education; a
godfather or godmother.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A person who vouches for another as fit for some
post or task; <as>as, one needs two <ex>sponsors</ex> to be considered
for membership</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A person or group that assumes financial
responsibility for some activity, and may or may not participate in its
organization and execution.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A person or organization, usually a commercial
organization, which pays the cost of an activity, such as a radio or
television broadcast, and in return is given the right to advertise
itself or its products as part of the activity; <as>as, now a word from
our <ex>sponsor</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponsorial</ent><br/
<hw>Spon*so"ri*al</hw> <pr>(-s<omac/"r<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pertaining to a sponsor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sponsorship</ent><br/
<hw>Spon"sor*ship</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n"s<etil/r*sh<icr/p)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>State of being a sponsor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spontaneity</ent><br/
<hw>Spon`ta*ne"i*ty</hw>
<pr>(sp<ocr/n`t<adot/*n<emac/"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spontaneities</plw> <pr>(-t<icr/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>spontan<eacute/it<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or state of being spontaneous, or acting from native
feeling, proneness, or temperament, without constraint or external
force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Romney Leigh, who lives by diagrams,<br/
And crosses not the <qex>spontaneities</qex><br/
Of all his individual, personal life<br/
With formal universals.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs. Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The tendency to
undergo change, characteristic of both animal and vegetable organisms,
and not restrained or checked by the environment.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The tendency to activity of muscular tissue, including the
voluntary muscles, when in a state of healthful vigor and
refreshment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spontaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Spon*ta"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n*t<amac/"n<esl/*<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spontaneus</ets>, fr. <ets>sponte</ets> of
free will, voluntarily.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Proceeding from natural
feeling, temperament, or disposition, or from a native internal
proneness, readiness, or tendency, without constraint; <as>as, a
<ex>spontaneous</ex> gift or proposition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Proceeding from, or acting by, internal impulse,
energy, or natural law, without external force; <as>as,
<ex>spontaneous</ex> motion; <ex>spontaneous</ex> growth.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Produced without being planted, or without human
labor; <as>as, a <ex>spontaneous</ex> growth of wood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spontaneous combustion</b></col>, <cd>combustion produced
in a substance by the evolution of heat through the chemical action of
its own elements; as, the <xex>spontaneous combustion</xex> of waste
matter saturated with oil.</cd> -- <col><b>Spontaneous
generation</b></col>. <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Generation</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<usage><p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Voluntary; uncompelled; willing.</syn> --
<er>Spontaneous</er>, <er>Voluntary</er>. What is
<xex>voluntary</xex> is the result of a <xex>volition</xex>, or act of
choice; it therefore implies some degree of consideration, and may be
the result of mere reason without excited feeling. What is
<xex>spontaneous</xex> springs wholly from feeling, or a sudden impulse
which admits of no reflection; as, a <xex>spontaneous</xex> burst of
applause. Hence, the term is also applied to things inanimate when they
are produced without the determinate purpose or care of man.
<ldquo/Abstinence which is but <xex>voluntary</xex> fasting, and . . .
exercise which is but <xex>voluntary</xex> labor.<rdquo/ <au>J.
Seed.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Spontaneous</qex> joys, where nature has its play,<br/
The soul adopts, and owns their firstborn away.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></usage>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spon*ta"ne*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spon*ta"ne*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spontoon</ent><br/
<hw>Spon*toon"</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/n*t<oomac/n")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>sponton</ets>, <ets>esponton</ets>, It.
<ets>spontone</ets>, <ets>spuntone</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>
<def>A kind of half-pike, or halberd, formerly borne by inferior
officers of the British infantry, and used in giving signals to the
soldiers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spook</ent><br/
<hw>Spook</hw> <pr>(sp<oomac/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>spook</ets>; akin to G. <ets>spuk</ets>, Sw. <ets>sp<oum/ke</ets>,
Dan. <ets>sp<oum/gelse</ets> a specter, <ets>sp<oum/ge</ets> to play,
sport, joke, <ets>sp<oum/g</ets> a play, joke.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
spirit; a ghost; an apparition; a hobgoblin.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>spuke</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Ld. Lytton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The chimaera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spool</ent><br/
<hw>Spool</hw> <pr>(sp<oomac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>spole</ets>, OD. <ets>spoele</ets>, D. <ets>spoel</ets>; akin to G.
<ets>spule</ets>, OHG. <ets>spuola</ets>, Dan. & Sw.
<ets>spole</ets>.]</ety> <def>A piece of cane or reed with a knot at
each end, or a hollow cylinder of wood with a ridge at each end, used to
wind thread or yarn upon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spool stand</b></col>, <cd>an article holding spools of
thread, turning on pins, -- used by women at their work.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spool</ent><br/
<hw>Spool</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spooled</conjf> <pr>(sp<oomac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Spooling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To wind on a spool or
spools.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spooler</ent><br/
<hw>Spool"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or
that which, spools.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoom</ent><br/
<hw>Spoom</hw> <pr>(sp<oomac/m)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Probably
fr. <ets>spume</ets> foam. See <er>Spume</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>To be driven steadily and swiftly, as before a strong wind; to be
driven before the wind without any sail, or with only a part of the
sails spread; to scud under bare poles.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>spoon</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When virtue <qex>spooms</qex> before a prosperous gale,<br/
My heaving wishes help to fill the sail.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoon</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon</hw> <pr>(sp<oomac/n)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See <er>Spoom</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We might have <qex>spooned</qex> before the wind as well as
they.</q> <rj><qau>Pepys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoon</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spon</ets>, AS.
<ets>sp<omac/n</ets>, a chip; akin to D. <ets>spaan</ets>, G.
<ets>span</ets>, Dan. <ets>spaan</ets>, Sw. <ets>sp<aring/n</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sp<aacute/nn</ets>, <ets>sp<oacute/nn</ets>, a chip, a spoon.
<root/170. Cf. <er>Span-new</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An implement
consisting of a small bowl (usually a shallow oval) with a handle, used
especially in preparing or eating food.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/Therefore behoveth him a full long <qex>spoon</qex><br/
That shall eat with a fiend,<rdquo/ thus heard I say.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He must have a long <qex>spoon</qex> that must eat with the
devil.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything which resembles a spoon in shape; esp.
<fld>(Fishing)</fld>, a spoon bait.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: A simpleton; a spooney.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Hood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>


<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>A wooden club with a lofted
face.</def> <rj><au>Encyc. of Sport.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spoon bait</b></col> <fld>(Fishing)</fld>, <cd>a lure
used in trolling, consisting of a glistening metallic plate shaped like
the bowl of a spoon with a fishhook attached.</cd> -- <col><b>Spoon
bit</b></col>, <cd>a bit for boring, hollowed or furrowed along one
side.</cd> -- <col><b>Spoon net</b></col>, <cd>a net for landing
fish.</cd> -- <col><b>Spoon oar</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Oar</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoon</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take up in, or as
in, a spoon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Fishing)</fld> <def>To catch by fishing with a
spoon bait.</def></p>

<p><q>He had with him all the tackle necessary for <qex>spooning</qex>
pike.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs. Humphry Ward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In croquet, golf, etc., to push or shove (a ball)
with a lifting motion, instead of striking with an audible
knock.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoon</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To act with demonstrative or
foolish fondness, as one in love.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoon</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fish with a spoon
bait.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In croquet, golf, etc., to spoon a ball.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoonbill</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"bill`</hw> <pr>(-b<icr/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of several species of
wading birds of the genera <gen>Ajaja</gen> and <gen>Platalea</gen>, and
allied genera, in which the long bill is broadly expanded and flattened
at the tip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The roseate spoonbill of America (<spn>Ajaja
ajaja</spn>), and the European spoonbill (<spn>Platalea
leucorodia</spn>) are the best known. The royal spoonbill (<spn>Platalea
regia</spn>) of Australia is white, with the skin in front of the eyes
naked and black. The male in the breeding season has a fine
crest.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The shoveler. See <er>Shoveler</er>, 2.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>The ruddy duck. See under <er>Ruddy</er>.</def>
<sd>(d)</sd> <def>The paddlefish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoon-billed</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"-billed`</hw> <pr>(-b<icr/ld`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the bill expanded and spatulate at the
end.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoondrift</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"drift</hw> <pr>(-dr<icr/ft)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spoom</ets> + <ets>drift</ets>.]</ety> <def>Spray blown from
the tops of waves during a gale at sea; also, snow driven in the wind at
sea; -- <altsp>written also <asp>spindrift</asp></altsp>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spooney</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"ey</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Weak-minded;
demonstratively fond; <as>as, <ex>spooney</ex> lovers</as>.</def>
<altsp>[Spelt also <asp>spoony.</asp>]</altsp>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spooney</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"ey</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spooneys</plw>
<pr>(-<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <def>A weak-minded or silly person; one who is
foolishly fond.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is no doubt, whatever, that I was a lackadaisical young
<qex>spooney</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoonflower</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"flow`er</hw> <pr>(-flou`<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
yautia.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoonful</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"ful</hw> <pr>(-f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spoonfuls</plw> <pr>(-f<usdot/lz)</pr>.</plu>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quantity which a spoon contains, or is able to
contain; <as>as, a tea<ex>spoonful</ex>; a
table<ex>spoonful</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a small quantity.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoonily</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"i*ly</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
spoony manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoon-meat</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"-meat`</hw> <pr>(-m<emac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Food
that is, or must be, taken with a spoon; liquid food.</def> <ldquo/Diet
most upon <xex>spoon-meats</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoonwood</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"wood`</hw> <pr>(-w<oocr/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The mountain laurel (<spn>Kalmia
latifolia</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoonworm</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"worm`</hw> <pr>(sp<oomac/n"w<ucir/rm`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A gephyrean worm of the genus
<gen>Thalassema</gen>, having a spoonlike proboscis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoonwort</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"wort`</hw> <pr>(-w<ucir/rt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Scurvy grass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoony</ent><br/
<hw>Spoon"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Spooney</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoor</ent><br/
<hw>Spoor</hw> <pr>(sp<oomac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>spoor</ets>; akin to AS. <ets>spor</ets>, G. <ets>spur</ets>, and
from the root of E. <ets>spur</ets>. <root/171. See
<er>Spur</er>.]</ety> <def>The track or trail of any wild animal;
<as>as, the <ex>spoor</ex> of an elephant; -- used originally by
travelers in South Africa</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoor</ent><br/
<hw>Spoor</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To follow a spoor or trail.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporades</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spor"a*des</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/r"<adot/*d<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>spora`des</grk>. Cf.
<er>Sporadic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Stars not included
in any constellation; -- called also <xex>informed</xex>, or
<xex>unformed</xex>, stars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporadial</ent><br/
<hw>Spo*ra"di*al</hw> <pr>(sp<osl/*r<amac/"d<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Sporadic.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporadic</ent><br/
<hw>Spo*rad"ic</hw> <pr>(-r<acr/d"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/ scattered, fr. <?/, <?/, scattered, fr. <?/ to sow seed, to scatter
like seed: cf. F. <ets>sporadique</ets>. See <er>Spore</er>.]</ety>
<def>Occurring singly, or apart from other things of the same kind, or
in scattered instances; separate; single; <as>as, a <ex>sporadic</ex>
fireball; a <ex>sporadic</ex> case of disease; a <ex>sporadic</ex>
example of a flower.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sporadic disease</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
disease which occurs in single and scattered cases. See the Note under
<er>Endemic</er>, <pos>a.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporadical</ent><br/
<hw>Spo*rad"ic*al</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sporadic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporadically</ent><br/
<hw>Spo*rad"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sporadic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporangiophore</ent><br/
<hw>Spo*ran"gi*o*phore</hw>
<pr>(sp<osl/*r<acr/n"j<icr/*<osl/*f<omac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sporangium</ets> + Gr. <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The axis or receptacle in certain ferns (as
<gen>Trichomanes</gen>), which bears the sporangia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporangium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spo*ran"gi*um</hw> <pr>(sp<osl/*r<acr/n"j<icr/*<ucr/m)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sporangia</plw>
<pr>(sp<osl/*r<acr/n"j<icr/*<adot/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a
sowing, seed + <?/ a receptacle.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A spore
case in the cryptogamous plants, as in ferns, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spore</ent><br/
<hw>Spore</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
sowing, seed, from <?/ to sow. Cf. <er>Sperm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the minute grains in
flowerless plants, which are analogous to seeds, as serving to reproduce
the species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Spores are produced differently in the different classes
of cryptogamous plants, and as regards their nature are often so unlike
that they have only their minuteness in common. The peculiar spores of
diatoms (called <stype>auxospores</stype>) increase in size, and at
length acquire a siliceous coating, thus becoming new diatoms of full
size. Compare <er>Macrospore</er>, <er>Microspore</er>,
<er>Oospore</er>, <er>Resting spore</er>, <er>Sphaerospore</er>,
<er>Swarmspore</er>, <er>Tetraspore</er>, <er>Zoospore</er>, and
<er>Zygospore</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>An embryo sac or embryonal vesicle in the ovules of
flowering plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A minute grain or
germ; a small, round or ovoid body, formed in certain organisms, and by
germination giving rise to a new organism; <as>as, the reproductive
<ex>spores</ex> of bacteria, etc.</as></def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of
the parts formed by fission in certain Protozoa. See <cref>Spore
formation</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spore formation</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Biol)</fld>
<cd>A mode of reproduction resembling multiple fission, common among
Protozoa, in which the organism breaks up into a number of pieces, or
spores, each of which eventually develops into an organism like the
parent form.</cd> <au>Balfour.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The formation of
reproductive cells or spores, as in the growth of bacilli.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporid</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"rid</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"r<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A sporidium.</def> <rj><au>Lindley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporidiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Spo`ri*dif"er*ous</hw>
<pr>(sp<omac/`r<icr/*d<icr/f"<etil/r*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sporidium</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Bearing sporidia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporidium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spo*rid"i*um</hw> <pr>(sp<osl/*r<icr/d"<icr/*<ucr/m)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sporidia</plw>
<pr>(-<adot/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Spore</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A secondary spore, or a filament
produced from a spore, in certain kinds of minute fungi.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A spore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Spo*rif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(-r<icr/f"<etil/r*<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Bearing or producing spores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporification</ent><br/
<hw>Spo`ri*fi*ca"tion</hw>
<pr>(sp<omac/`r<icr/*f<icr/*k<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + L. <ets>-ficare</ets> (in comp.) to make. See
<er>-fy</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Spore formation. See
<cref>Spore formation</cref> <sd>(b)</sd>, under
<er>Spore</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporocarp</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"ro*carp</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"r<osl/*k<aum/rp)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + Gr. <?/ fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A closed body or conceptacle containing one or more
masses of spores or sporangia.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
sporangium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporocyst</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"ro*cyst</hw> <pr>(-s<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
seed + <?/ bladder.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
asexual zooid, usually forming one of a series of larval forms in the
agamic reproduction of various trematodes and other parasitic worms. The
sporocyst generally develops from an egg, but in its turn produces other
larvae by internal budding, or by the subdivision of a part or all of
its contents into a number of minute germs. See
<er>Redia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any protozoan when it becomes
encysted and produces germs by sporulation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporogenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Spo`ro*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(-j<ecr/n"<esl/*s<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + <ets>genesis</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Reproduction by spores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporogony</ent><br/
<hw>Spo*rog"o*ny</hw> <pr>(sp<osl/*r<ocr/g"<osl/*n<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + root of Gr. <?/ to be
born.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The growth or development of an
animal or a zooid from a nonsexual germ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporophore</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"ro*phore</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"r<osl/*f<omac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + Gr. <grk>fe`rein</grk> to bear.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A placenta.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>That alternately produced form of certain cryptogamous plants, as
ferns, mosses, and the like, which is nonsexual, but produces spores in
countless numbers. In ferns it is the leafy plant, in mosses the
capsule. Cf. <er>Oophore</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporophoric</ent><br/
<hw>Spo`ro*phor"ic</hw> <pr>(-f<ocr/r"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the nature of a sporophore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporophyte</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"ro*phyte</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"r<osl/*f<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + Gr. <grk>fyto`n</grk> plant.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>In plants exhibiting alternation of generations,
the generation which bears asexual spores; -- opposed to
<contr>gametophyte</contr>. It is not clearly differentiated in the life
cycle of the lower plants.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Spo`ro*phyt"ic</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1392 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Sporosac</ent><br/
<hw>Spo"ro*sac</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/"r<osl/*s<acr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + <ets>sac</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A hydrozoan reproductive zooid or gonophore which does
not become medusoid in form or structure. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Athecata</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An early or simple larval
stage of trematode worms and some other invertebrates, which is capable
of reproducing other germs by asexual generation; a nurse; a
redia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporozoa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spo`ro*zo"a</hw> <pr>(-z<omac/"<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>spo`ros</grk> a spore + <grk>zw^,on</grk> an
animal.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extensive division of
parasitic Protozoa, which increase by sporulation. It includes the
Gregarinida.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporozoid</ent><br/
<hw>Spo`ro*zo"id</hw> <pr>(-<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spore</ets> + Gr. <grk>zw^,on</grk> an animal.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Zoospore</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporozoite</ent><br/
<hw>Spo`ro*zo"ite</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/`r<osl/*z<omac/"<imac/t)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>In certain Sporozoa, a small
active, usually elongate, sickle-shaped or somewhat amoeboid spore, esp.
one of those produced by division of the passive spores into which the
zygote divides. The sporozoites reproduce asexually.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporran</ent><br/
<hw>Spor"ran</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/r"r<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>sporan</ets>.]</ety> <def>A large purse or pouch made of skin with
the hair or fur on, worn in front of the kilt by Highlanders when in
full dress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sport</ent><br/
<hw>Sport</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/rt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbreviated
from <ets>disport</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which diverts, and
makes mirth; pastime; amusement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is as <qex>sport</qex> to a fool to do mischief.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. x. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her <qex>sports</qex> were such as carried riches of knowledge
upon the stream of delight.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Think it but a minute spent in <qex>sport</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Mock; mockery; contemptuous mirth;
derision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then make <qex>sport</qex> at me; then let me be your jest.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That with which one plays, or which is driven about
in play; a toy; a plaything; an object of mockery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Flitting leaves, the <qex>sport</qex> of every wind.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Never does man appear to greater disadvantage than when he is the
<qex>sport</qex> of his own ungoverned passions.</q> <rj><qau>John
Clarke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Play; idle jingle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An author who should introduce such a <qex>sport</qex> of words
upon our stage would meet with small applause.</q>
<rj><qau>Broome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Diversion of the field, as fowling, hunting,
fishing, racing, games, and the like, esp. when money is
staked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld> <def>A plant or an animal, or
part of a plant or animal, which has some peculiarity not usually seen
in the species; an abnormal variety or growth. See <cref>Sporting
plant</cref>, under <er>Sporting</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A sportsman; a gambler.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In sport</b></col>, <cd>in jest; for play or
diversion.</cd> <ldquo/So is the man that deceiveth his neighbor, and
saith, Am not I <xex>in sport</xex>?<rdquo/ <rj><au>Prov. xxvi.
19.</au></rj> </cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Play; game; diversion; frolic; mirth; mock;
mockery; jeer.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sport</ent><br/
<hw>Sport</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sported</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sporting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To play; to frolic;
to wanton.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Fish], <qex>sporting</qex> with quick glance,<br/
Show to the sun their waved coats dropt with gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To practice the diversions of the field or the turf;
to be given to betting, as upon races.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To trifle.</def> <ldquo/He <xex>sports</xex> with
his own life.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tillotson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld> <def>To assume suddenly a new
and different character from the rest of the plant or from the type of
the species; -- said of a bud, shoot, plant, or animal. See
<er>Sport</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 6.</def> <rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To play; frolic; game; wanton.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sport</ent><br/
<hw>Sport</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To divert; to amuse;
to make merry; -- used with the reciprocal pronoun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Against whom do ye <qex>sport</qex> yourselves?</q> <rj><qau>Isa.
lvii. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To represent by any kind of play.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now <qex>sporting</qex> on thy lyre the loves of youth.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To exhibit, or bring out, in public; to use or wear;
<as>as, to <ex>sport</ex> a new equipage</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To give utterance to in a sportive manner; to throw
out in an easy and copious manner; -- with <xex>off</xex>; <as>as, to
<ex>sport</ex> off epigrams</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sport one's oak</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Oak</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportability</ent><br/
<hw>Sport`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(-<adot/*b<icr/l"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Sportiveness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportal</ent><br/
<hw>Sport"al</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/rt"<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to sports; used in sports.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Sportal</xex> arms.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporter</ent><br/
<hw>Sport"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sports; a sportsman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As this gentleman and I have been old fellow <qex>sporters</qex>,
I have a friendship for him.</q> <rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportful</ent><br/
<hw>Sport"ful</hw> <pr>(-f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Full of sport; merry; frolicsome; full of jesting; indulging in
mirth or play; playful; wanton; <as>as, a <ex>sportful</ex>
companion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Down he alights among the <qex>sportful</qex> herd.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Done in jest, or for mere play; sportive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are no <qex>sportful</qex> productions of the soil.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sport"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sport"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporting</ent><br/
<hw>Sport"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of, pertaining to, or engaging
in, sport or sports; exhibiting the character or conduct of one who, or
that which, sports.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sporting book</b></col>, <cd>a book containing a record
of bets, gambling operations, and the like.</cd> <au>C. Kingsley.</au>
-- <col><b>Sporting house</b></col>, <cd>a house frequented by
sportsmen, gamblers, and the like.</cd> -- <col><b>Sporting
man</b></col>, <cd>one who practices field sports; also, a horse racer,
a pugilist, a gambler, or the like.</cd> -- <col><b>Sporting
plant</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a plant in which a single bud or
offset suddenly assumes a new, and sometimes very different, character
from that of the rest of the plant.</cd> <au>Darwin.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportingly</ent><br/
<hw>Sport"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In sport;
sportively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The question you there put, you do it, I suppose, but
<qex>sportingly</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Hammond.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportive</ent><br/
<hw>Sport"ive</hw> <pr>(-<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to,
engaged in, or provocative of, sport; gay; frolicsome; playful;
merry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is it I<br/
That drive thee from the <qex>sportive</qex> court?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sport"ive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sport"ive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportless</ent><br/
<hw>Sport"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without sport or mirth;
joyless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportling</ent><br/
<hw>Sport"ling</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little
person or creature engaged in sports or in play.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When again the lambkins play --<br/
Pretty <qex>sportlings</qex>, full of May.</q>
<rj><qau>Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportsman</ent><br/
<hw>Sports"man</hw> <pr>(sp<omac/rts"m<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>;<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sportsmen</plw>
<pr>(-m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who pursues the sports of the field;
one who hunts, fishes, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportsmanship</ent><br/
<hw>Sports"man*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The practice of sportsmen;
skill in field sports.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- conduct becoming to one participating in sport or competition, as
fair play, or graciousness in winning or losing. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spor"tu*la</hw> <pr>(sp<ocir/r"t<usl/*l<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sportulae</plw>
<pr>(-l<emac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A gift; a present; a
prize; hence, an alms; a largess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To feed luxuriously, to frequent sports and theaters, to run for
the <qex>sportula</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportulary</ent><br/
<hw>Spor"tu*la*ry</hw> <pr>(sp<ocir/r"t<usl/*l<asl/*r<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Subsisting on alms or charitable contributions.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sportule</ent><br/
<hw>Spor"tule</hw> <pr>(-t<umac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sportula</ets> a little basket, a gift, dim. of <ets>sporta</ets> a
basket: cf. F. <ets>sportule</ets>.]</ety> <def>A charitable gift or
contribution; a gift; an alms; a dole; a largess; a sportula.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporulation</ent><br/
<hw>Spor`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/r`<usl/*l<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The act or process of forming
spores; spore formation. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Bacillus</er>,
<sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporule</ent><br/
<hw>Spor"ule</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/r"<usl/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim.
of <ets>spore</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A small spore; a
spore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sporuliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Spor`u*lif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(-<usl/*l<icr/f"<etil/r*<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sporule</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Producing sporules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spot</ent><br/
<hw>Spot</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Scot. & D.
<ets>spat</ets>, Dan. <ets>spette</ets>, Sw. <ets>spott</ets> spittle,
slaver; from the root of E. <ets>spit</ets>. See <er>Spit</er> to eject
from the mouth, and cf. <er>Spatter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mark
on a substance or body made by foreign matter; a blot; a place
discolored.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Out, damned <qex>spot</qex>! Out, I say!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stain on character or reputation; something that
soils purity; disgrace; reproach; fault; blemish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet Chloe, sure, was formed without a <qex>spot</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small part of a different color from the main
part, or from the ground upon which it is; <as>as, the <ex>spots</ex> of
a leopard; the <ex>spots</ex> on a playing card</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A small extent of space; a place; any particular
place.</def> <ldquo/Fixed to one <xex>spot</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Otway.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>spot</qex> to which I point is Paradise.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/A jolly place,<rdquo/ said he, <ldquo/in times of old!<br/
But something ails it now: the <qex>spot</qex> is cursed.<rdquo/</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A variety of the common domestic
pigeon, so called from a spot on its head just above its beak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A sciaenoid food
fish (<spn>Liostomus xanthurus</spn>) of the Atlantic coast of the
United States. It has a black spot behind the shoulders and fifteen
oblique dark bars on the sides. Called also <altname>goody</altname>,
<altname>Lafayette</altname>, <altname>masooka</altname>, and
<altname>old wife</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The southern
redfish, or red horse, which has a spot on each side at the base of the
tail. See <er>Redfish</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Commodities, as merchandise and
cotton, sold for immediate delivery.</def> <mark>[Broker's
Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Crescent spot</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
butterfly of the family <fam>Melitaeidae</fam> having crescent-shaped
white spots along the margins of the red or brown wings.</cd> --
<col><b>Spot lens</b></col> <fld>(Microscopy)</fld>, <cd>a condensing
lens in which the light is confined to an annular pencil by means of a
small, round diaphragm (the <xex>spot</xex>), and used in dark-field
illumination; -- called also <altname>spotted lens</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spot rump</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the Hudsonian godwit
(<spn>Limosa haemastica</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Spots on the
sun</b></col>. <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Sun spot</cref>, ander
<er>Sun</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>On the spot</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Upon the spot</b></col></mcol>, <cd>immediately; before moving;
without changing place; <as>as, he made his decision <ex>on the
spot</ex></as>.</cd><br/
<br/
<q>It was determined <qex>upon the spot</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Stain; flaw; speck; blot; disgrace; reproach;
fault; blemish; place; site; locality.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spot</ent><br/
<hw>Spot</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spotted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spotting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make visible
marks upon with some foreign matter; to discolor in or with spots; to
stain; to cover with spots or figures; <as>as, to <ex>spot</ex> a
garment; to <ex>spot</ex> paper.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mark or note so as to insure recognition; to
recognize; to detect; <as>as, to <ex>spot</ex> a criminal</as>.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To stain; to blemish; to taint; to disgrace; to
tarnish, as reputation; to asperse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My virgin life no <qex>spotted</qex> thoughts shall stain.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If ever I shall close these eyes but once,<br/
May I live <qex>spotted</qex> for my perjury.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. &
Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To spot timber</b></col>, <cd>to cut or chip it, in
preparation for hewing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spot</ent><br/
<hw>Spot</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become stained with
spots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spot</ent><br/
<hw>Spot</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lit., being on the
spot, or place;</def> <specif>hence</specif> <fld>(Com.)</fld>, <def>on
hand for immediate delivery after sale; -- said of commodities; <as>as,
<ex>spot</ex> wheat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spot cash</ent><br/
<hw>Spot cash</hw>. <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>Cash paid or ready for
payment at once upon delivery of property purchased.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spotless</ent><br/
<hw>Spot"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a spot; especially, free
from reproach or impurity; pure; untainted; innocent; <as>as, a
<ex>spotless</ex> mind; <ex>spotless</ex> behavior</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>spotless</qex> virgin, and a faultless wife.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Blameless; unspotted; unblemished; pure;
immaculate; irreproachable. See <er>Blameless</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spot"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spot"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spotlight</ent><br/
<hw>Spot"light`</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/t"l<imac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The projected spot or circle of light used to
illuminate brilliantly a single person or object or group on the stage,
leaving the rest of the stage more or less unilluminated.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>hence, conspicuous public notice.</def> <mark>[Cant
or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A lighting apparatus designed to throw an intense
beam of light onto a small area.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spot stroke</ent><br/
<hw>Spot stroke</hw>. <fld>(Eng. Billiards)</fld> <def>The pocketing of
the red ball in a top corner pocket from off its own spot so as to leave
the cue ball in position for an easy winning hazard in either top corner
pocket.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spotted</ent><br/
<hw>Spot"ted</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Marked with spots; <as>as, a
<ex>spotted</ex> garment or character</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>spotted</xex> panther.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spotted fever</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a name
applied to various eruptive fevers, esp. to typhus fever and
cerebro-spinal meningitis.</cd> -- <col><b>Spotted tree</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an Australian tree (<spn>Flindersia
maculosa</spn>); -- so called because its bark falls off in
spots.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spottedness</ent><br/
<hw>Spot"ted*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>State or quality of being
spotted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spotter</ent><br/
<hw>spot"ter</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/t"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who spots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spottiness</ent><br/
<hw>spot"ti*ness</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/t"t<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The state or quality of being spotty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spotty</ent><br/
<hw>Spot"ty</hw> <pr>(sp<ocr/t"t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full of
spots; marked with spots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spousage</ent><br/
<hw>Spous"age</hw> <pr>(spouz"<asl/j; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espousaige</ets>, from <ets>espouser</ets>. See <er>Spouse</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Espousal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spousal</ent><br/
<hw>Spous"al</hw> <pr>(-<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Espousal</er>, <er>Sponsal</er>, and <er>Spouse</er>.]</ety> <def>Of
or pertaining to a spouse or marriage; nuptial; matrimonial; conjugal;
connubial; bridal; <as>as, <ex>spousal</ex> rites; <ex>spousal</ex>
ornaments</as>.</def> <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spousal</ent><br/
<hw>Spous"al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Espousal</er>,
<er>Spouse</er>.]</ety> <def>Marriage; nuptials; espousal; -- generally
used in the plural; <as>as, the <ex>spousals</ex> of
Hippolita</as>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Boweth your head under that blissful yoke . . .<br/
Which that men clepeth <qex>spousal</qex> or wedlock.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spousals</qex> of the newborn year.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spouse</ent><br/
<hw>Spouse</hw> <pr>(spouz)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espous</ets>, <ets>espos</ets>, fem. <ets>espouse</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/poux</ets>, <ets><eacute/pouse</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>sponsus</ets>, <ets>sponsa</ets>, prop. p. p. of
<ets>spondere</ets>, <ets>sponsum</ets>, to promise solemnly, to engage
one's self. Cf. <er>Despond</er>, <er>Espouse</er>, <er>Respond</er>,
<er>Sponsor</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A man or woman engaged or
joined in wedlock; a married person, husband or wife.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At last such grace I found, and means I wrought,<br/
That I that lady to my <qex>spouse</qex> had won.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A married man, in distinction from a
<contr>spousess</contr> or <contr>married woman</contr>; a bridegroom or
husband.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At which marriage was [were] no persons present but the
<qex>spouse</qex>, the spousess, the Duchess of Bedford her mother, the
priest, two gentlewomen, and a young man.</q>
<rj><qau>Fabyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spouse</ent><br/
<hw>Spouse</hw> <pr>(spouz)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Espouse</er>, and <er>Spouse</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To wed;
to espouse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This markis hath her <qex>spoused</qex> with a ring.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though <qex>spoused</qex>, yet wanting wedlock's solemnize.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She was found again, and <qex>spoused</qex> to Marinell.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spouse-breach</ent><br/
<hw>Spouse"-breach`</hw> <pr>(-br<emac/ch`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Adultery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spouseless</ent><br/
<hw>Spouse"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of a spouse;
unmarried.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spousess</ent><br/
<hw>Spous"ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wife or bride.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fabyan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spout</ent><br/
<hw>Spout</hw> <pr>(spout)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spouted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spouting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Sw. <ets>sputa</ets>,
<ets>spruta</ets>, to spout, D. <ets>spuit</ets> a spout,
<ets>spuiten</ets> to spout, and E. <ets>spurt</ets>, <ets>sprit</ets>,
v., <ets>sprout</ets>, <ets>sputter</ets>; or perhaps akin to E.
<ets>spit</ets> to eject from the mouth.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
throw out forcibly and abundantly, as liquids through an orifice or a
pipe; to eject in a jet; <as>as, an elephant <ex>spouts</ex> water from
his trunk</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who kept Jonas in the fish's maw<br/
Till he was <qex>spouted</qex> up at Ninivee?</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Next on his belly floats the mighty whale . . . <br/
He <qex>spouts</qex> the tide.</q> <rj><qau>Creech.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To utter magniloquently; to recite in an oratorical
or pompous manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pray, <qex>spout</qex> some French, son.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. &
Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pawn; to pledge; <as>as, to <ex>spout</ex> a
watch</as>.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spout</ent><br/
<hw>Spout</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To issue with
violence, or in a jet, as a liquid through a narrow orifice, or from a
spout; <as>as, water <ex>spouts</ex> from a hole; blood <ex>spouts</ex>
from an artery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the glittering hill<br/
Is bright with <qex>spouting</qex> rills.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To eject water or liquid in a jet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To utter a speech, especially in a pompous
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spout</ent><br/
<hw>Spout</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sw. <ets>spruta</ets> a squirt,
a syringe. See <er>Spout</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>That through which anything spouts; a discharging lip, pipe, or
orifice; a tube, pipe, or conductor of any kind through which a liquid
is poured, or by which it is conveyed in a stream from one place to
another; <as>as, the <ex>spout</ex> of a teapot; a <ex>spout</ex> for
conducting water from the roof of a building.</as></def>
<au>Addison.</au> <ldquo/A conduit with three issuing
<xex>spouts</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In whales . . . an ejection thereof [water] is contrived by a
fistula, or <qex>spout</qex>, at the head.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From silver <qex>spouts</qex> the grateful liquors glide.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A trough for conducting grain, flour, etc., into a
receptacle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A discharge or jet of water or other liquid, esp.
when rising in a column; also, a waterspout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To put up the spout</b></col>, <col><b>To shove up
the spout</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To pop up the
spout</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to pawn or pledge at a pawnbroker's; -- in
allusion to the <xex>spout</xex> up which the pawnbroker sent the
ticketed articles.</cd> <mark>[Cant]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spouter</ent><br/
<hw>Spout"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or
that which, spouts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoutfish</ent><br/
<hw>Spout"fish`</hw> <pr>(-f<icr/sh`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A marine animal that spouts water; -- applied
especially to certain bivalve mollusks, like the long clams
(<gen>Mya</gen>), which spout, or squirt out, water when retiring into
their holes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoutless</ent><br/
<hw>Spout"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no spout.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spoutshell</ent><br/
<hw>Spout"shell`</hw> <pr>(-sh<ecr/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any marine gastropod shell of the genus
<gen>Apporhais</gen> having an elongated siphon. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
under <er>Rostrifera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprack</ent><br/
<hw>Sprack</hw> <pr>(spr<acr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>spraekr</ets> sprightly, dial. Sw. <ets>spr<aum/k</ets>,
<ets>spr<aum/g</ets>, spirited, mettlesome; or Gael. <ets>spraic</ets>
vigor.]</ety> <def>Quick; lively; alert.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprad</ent><br/
<hw>Sprad</hw> <pr>(spr<acr/d)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p.
p.</pos> of <er>Spread</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spradde</ent><br/
<hw>Sprad"de</hw> <pr>(-d<eit/)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark>
<def><pos>imp.</pos> of <er>Spread</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprag</ent><br/
<hw>Sprag</hw> <pr>(spr<acr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>spraka</ets> a small flounder.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
young salmon.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprag</ent><br/
<hw>Sprag</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spray</er> a branch.]</ety>
<def>A billet of wood; a piece of timber used as a prop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprag</ent><br/
<hw>Sprag</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spragged</conjf> <pr>(spr<acr/gd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Spragging</conjf> <pr>(-g<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To check the motion of, as a carriage on a steep grade,
by putting a sprag between the spokes of the wheel.</def> <rj><au>R. S.
Poole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To prop or sustain with a sprag.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprag</ent><br/
<hw>Sprag</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Sprack</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprain</ent><br/
<hw>Sprain</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/n)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sprained</conjf>
<pr>(spr<amac/nd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spraining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>espreindre</ets>to
press, to force out, F. <ets><eacute/preindre</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>exprimere</ets>. See <er>Express</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Spraints</er>.]</ety> <def>To weaken, as a joint, ligament, or
muscle, by sudden and excessive exertion, as by wrenching; to
overstrain, or stretch injuriously, but without luxation; <as>as, to
<ex>sprain</ex> one's ankle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprain</ent><br/
<hw>Sprain</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or result of spraining;
lameness caused by spraining; <as>as, a bad <ex>sprain</ex> of the
wrist</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sprain fracture</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>the
separation of a tendon from its point of insertion, with the detachment
of a shell of bone to which the tendon is attached.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spraints</ent><br/
<hw>Spraints</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/nts)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>espraintes</ets>, <ets>espreintes</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/preintes</ets>, from <ets>espreinte</ets> a desire to go to
stool, from <ets>espreindre</ets>. See <er>Sprain</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <def>The dung of an otter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprang</ent><br/
<hw>Sprang</hw> <pr>(spr<acr/ng)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Spring</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprat</ent><br/
<hw>Sprat</hw> <pr>(spr<acr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sprot</ets>, <ets>sprotte</ets>, D. <ets>sprot</ets>; akin to G.
<ets>sprotte</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A small
European herring (<spn>Clupea sprattus</spn>) closely allied to the
common herring and the pilchard; -- called also
<altname>garvie</altname>. The name is also applied to small herring of
different kinds.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A California surf-fish
(<spn>Rhacochilus toxotes</spn>); -- called also
<altname>alfione</altname>, and <altname>perch</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sprat borer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
red-throated diver; -- so called from its fondness for sprats. See
<er>Diver</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sprat loon</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The young of the great northern diver</cd>.
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The red-throated diver. See
<er>Diver</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sprat mew</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the kittiwake gull.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1393 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Sprawl</ent><br/
<hw>Sprawl</hw> <pr>(spr<add/l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sprawled</conjf>
<pr>(spr<add/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sprawling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>spraulen</ets>; cf.
Sw. <ets>sprattla</ets> to sprawl, dial. Sw. <ets>spralla</ets>, Dan.
<ets>spraelle</ets>, <ets>spraelde</ets>, D. <ets>spartelen</ets>,
<ets>spertelen</ets>, to flounder, to struggle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To spread and stretch the body or limbs carelessly in a horizontal
position; to lie with the limbs stretched out ungracefully.</def> <--
also sprawl out, as to sprawl out all over the couch. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To spread irregularly, as vines, plants, or trees;
to spread ungracefully, as chirography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To move, when lying down, with awkward extension and
motions of the limbs; to scramble in creeping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The birds were not fledged; but upon <qex>sprawling</qex> and
struggling to get clear of the flame, down they tumbled.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>sprawl</ent><br/
<hw>sprawl</hw> <pr>(spr<add/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The position or
state resulting from sprawling; <as>as, he sat on the couch in a
<ex>sprawl</ex>; uncontrolled urban <ex>sprawl</ex></as>.</def> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprawls</ent><br/
<hw>Sprawls</hw> <pr>(spr<add/lz)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Small
branches of a tree; twigs; sprays.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spray</ent><br/
<hw>Spray</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>sprag</ets>. See <er>Sprig</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small
shoot or branch; a twig.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> sprig.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The painted birds, companions of the spring,<br/
Hopping from <qex>spray</qex> to <qex>spray</qex>, were heard to
sing.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A collective body of small branches, or cut flowers
with long stems; <as>as, the tree has a beautiful <ex>spray</ex>; many
<ex>sprays</ex> were sent in condolence to the funeral
home</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>And from the trees did lop the needless <qex>spray</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A side channel or
branch of the runner of a flask, made to distribute the metal in all
parts of the mold.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A group of castings made in
the same mold and connected by sprues formed in the runner and its
branches.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spray drain</b></col> <fld>(Agric.)</fld>, <cd>a drain
made by laying under earth the sprays or small branches of trees, which
keep passages open.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spray</ent><br/
<hw>Spray</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably from a Dutch or Low German
form akin to E. <ets>spread</ets>. See <er>Spread</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Water flying in small drops or
particles, as by the force of wind, or the dashing of waves, or from a
waterfall, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A jet of fine
medicated vapor, used either as an application to a diseased part or to
charge the air of a room with a disinfectant or a deodorizer.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>An instrument for applying such a spray; an
atomizer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spray condenser</b></col> <fld>(Steam Engine)</fld>,
<cd>an injection condenser in which the steam is condensed by a spray of
water which mingles with it.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spray</ent><br/
<hw>Spray</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To let fall in the
form of spray.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>M.
Arnold.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To throw spray upon; to treat with a liquid in the
form of spray; <as>as, to <ex>spray</ex> a wound, or a surgical
instrument, with carbolic acid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprayboard</ent><br/
<hw>Spray"board`</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/"b<omac/rd`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See <er>Dashboard</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2 <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A screen raised above any part of
the gunwale of a boat to keep out spray.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spray can</ent><br/
<hw>Spray" can`</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/"k<acr/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
small can containing a liquid under pressure of a gas that forces the
liquid out in an aerosol form when the valve is opened.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprayer</ent><br/
<hw>Spray"er</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
that sprays; any instrument for vaporizing and spraying
liquids.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spray gun</ent><br/
<hw>Spray" gun`</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/"g<ucr/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a
container for liquid, fitted with a conduit that can be supplied with
compressed air, designed to allowing the contents to be sprayed as an
aerosol. Such devices are used to spray paints and various other
liquids.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spray paint</ent><br/
<hw>Spray" paint`</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/"p<amac/nt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>paint packaged in a <er>spray can</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spray-paint</ent><br/
<hw>Spray"-paint</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/"p<amac/nt)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To paint (a surface) by means of a spray, using a <er>spray
can</er> or <er>spray gun</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spread</ent><br/
<hw>Spread</hw> <pr>(spr<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spread</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Spreading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>spreden</ets>, AS. <ets>spraedan</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>spreiden</ets>, <ets>spreijen</ets>, LG. <ets>spreden</ets>,
<ets>spreen</ets>, <ets>spreien</ets>, G. <ets>spreiten</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sprede</ets>, Sw. <ets>sprida</ets>. Cf. <er>Spray</er> water
flying in drops.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To extend in length and
breadth, or in breadth only; to stretch or expand to a broad or broader
surface or extent; to open; to unfurl; <as>as, to <ex>spread</ex> a
carpet; to <ex>spread</ex> a tent or a sail</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He bought a parcel of a field where he had <qex>spread</qex> his
tent.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xxxiii. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here the Rhone<br/
Hath <qex>spread</qex> himself a couch.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To extend so as to cover something; to extend to a
great or greater extent in every direction; to cause to fill or cover a
wide or wider space.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rose, as in a dance, the stately trees, and <qex>spread</qex><br/
Their branches hung with copious fruit.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To divulge; to publish, as news or fame; to cause to
be more extensively known; to disseminate; to make known fully; <as>as,
to <ex>spread</ex> a report; -- often accompanied by
<ex>abroad</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They, when they were departed, <qex>spread</qex> abroad his fame
in all that country.</q> <rj><qau>Matt. ix. 31.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To propagate; to cause to affect great numbers;
<as>as, to <ex>spread</ex> a disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To diffuse, as emanations or effluvia; to emit;
<as>as, odoriferous plants <ex>spread</ex> their
fragrance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To strew; to scatter over a surface; <as>as, to
<ex>spread</ex> manure; to <ex>spread</ex> lime on the
ground</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To prepare; to set and furnish with provisions;
<as>as, to <ex>spread</ex> a table</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Boiled the flesh, and <qex>spread</qex> the board.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To spread cloth</b></col>, <cd>to unfurl sail.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To diffuse; propagate; disperse; publish;
distribute; scatter; circulate; disseminate; dispense.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spread</ent><br/
<hw>Spread</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To extend in length
and breadth in all directions, or in breadth only; to be extended or
stretched; to expand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Plants, if they <qex>spread</qex> much, are seldom tall.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Governor Winthrop, and his associates at Charlestown, had for a
church a large, <qex>spreading</qex> tree.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Trumbull.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be extended by drawing or beating; <as>as, some
metals <ex>spread</ex> with difficulty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be made known more extensively, as
news.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be propagated from one to another; <as>as, the
disease <ex>spread</ex> into all parts of the city</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spread</ent><br/
<hw>Spread</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Extent;
compass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have got a fine <qex>spread</qex> of improvable land.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expansion of parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No flower hath <qex>spread</qex> like that of the woodbine.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A cloth used as a cover for a table or a
bed.</def><-- bedspread --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A table, as spread or furnished with a meal; hence,
an entertainment of food; a feast.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A privilege which one person buys of another, of
demanding certain shares of stock at a certain price, or of delivering
the same shares of stock at another price, within a time agreed
upon.</def> <mark>[Brokers' Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>An unlimited expanse of
discontinuous points.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Finance)</fld> <def>An arbitrage transaction
operated by buying and selling simultaneously in two separate markets,
as Chicago and New York, when there is an abnormal difference in price
between the two markets. It is called a <col><b>back
spread</b></col>when the difference in price is less than the normal
one.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Gems)</fld> <def>Surface in proportion to the depth
of a cut stone.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spread</ent><br/
<hw>Spread</hw>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of <er>Spread</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spread eagle</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>An eagle with
outspread wings, the national emblem of the United States.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The figure of an eagle, with its wings elevated and its
legs extended; often met as a device upon military ornaments, and the
like.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <cd>An eagle displayed; an
eagle with the wings and legs extended on each side of the body, as in
the double-headed eagle of Austria and Russia. See <er>Displayed</er>,
2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spread-eagle</ent><br/
<hw>spread"-ea`gle</hw> <pr>(spr<ecr/d"<emac/`g'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Characterized by a pretentious, boastful, exaggerated style;
defiantly or extravagantly bombastic, especially regarding of the
greatness of the U. S.; <as>as, a <ex>spread-eagle</ex> orator; a
<ex>spread-eagle</ex> speech</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq. &
Humorous]</mark> <au>[MW10]</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spread-eagle</ent><br/
<hw>spread"-ea`gle</hw> <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To assume a spread-eagled
position; -- it may be done reclining, for relaxation, or momentarily,
as an exhibitionary maneuver in a sport.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spread-eagled</ent><br/
<hw>spread"-ea`gled</hw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being in a position with the
arms and legs extended fully.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spreader</ent><br/
<hw>Spread"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who, or that which, spreads, expands, or propagates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A machine for combining and drawing fibers of flax
to form a sliver preparatory to spinning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spreadingly</ent><br/
<hw>Spread"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Increasingly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The best times were <qex>spreadingly</qex> infected.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprechery</ent><br/
<hw>Sprech"er*y</hw> <pr>(spr<ecr/ch"<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. Gael. <ets>spreidh</ets> cattle.]</ety> <def>Movables of an
inferior description; especially, such as have been collected by
depredation.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spree</ent><br/
<hw>Spree</hw> <pr>(spr<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Ir.
<ets>spre</ets> a spark, animation, spirit, Gael. <ets>spraic</ets>. Cf.
<er>Sprack</er>.]</ety> <def>A merry frolic; especially, a drinking
frolic; a carousal.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>spending spree</b></col> <cd>an incident in which one
spends money freely; -- usually designating indiscreet or reckless
spending on unneeded items.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprenge</ent><br/
<hw>Sprenge</hw> <pr>(spr<ecr/nj)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sprengen</ets>, p. p. <ets>sprent</ets>, <ets>spreint</ets>, from
AS. <ets>sprengan</ets> to sprinkle. See <er>Sprinkle</er>.]</ety>
<def>To sprinkle; to scatter.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif
(1 Pet. i. 2).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprengel pump</ent><br/
<hw>Spreng"el pump`</hw> <pr>(spr<ecr/ng"<eit/l p<ucr/mp`)</pr>.
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A form of air pump in which exhaustion is
produced by a stream of mercury running down a narrow tube, in the
manner of an aspirator; -- named from the inventor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprent</ent><br/
<hw>Sprent</hw> <pr>(spr<ecr/nt)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p.
p.</pos> of <er>Sprenge</er>. Sprinkled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the ground with purple blood was <qex>sprent</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprew</ent><br/
<hw>Sprew</hw> <pr>(spr<udd/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>sprouw</ets>, <ets>spruw</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Thrush.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spreynd</ent><br/
<hw>Spreynd</hw> <pr>(spr<amac/nd)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p.
p.</pos> of <er>Sprenge</er>. Sprinkled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When <qex>spreynd</qex> was holy water.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprig</ent><br/
<hw>Sprig</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sprec</ets>; akin to Icel. <ets>sprek</ets> a stick. Cf.
<er>Spray</er> a branch.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small shoot or twig
of a tree or other plant; a spray; <as>as, a <ex>sprig</ex> of laurel or
of parsley</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A youth; a lad; -- used humorously or in slight
disparagement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sprig</qex> whom I remember, with a whey-face and a
satchel, not so many years ago.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A brad, or nail without a head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A small eyebolt ragged or barbed
at the point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprig</ent><br/
<hw>Sprig</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sprigged</conjf> <pr>(spr<icr/gd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sprigging</conjf> <pr>(-g<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<def>To mark or adorn with the representation of small branches; to work
with sprigs; <as>as, to <ex>sprig</ex> muslin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprigged</ent><br/
<hw>Sprigged</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/gd <it>or</it> spr<icr/g"g<ecr/d)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Having sprigs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spriggy</ent><br/
<hw>Sprig"gy</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/g"g<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Full
of sprigs or small branches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spright</ent><br/
<hw>Spright</hw> <pr>(spr<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sprite</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Spirit; mind; soul; state of
mind; mood.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The high heroic
<xex>spright</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wondrous great grief groweth in my <qex>spright</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A supernatural being; a spirit; a shade; an
apparition; a ghost.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> sprite.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Forth he called, out of deep darkness dread,<br/
Legions of <qex>sprights</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To thee, O Father, Son, and Sacred <qex>Spright</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A kind of short arrow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spright</ent><br/
<hw>Spright</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To haunt, as a spright.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprightful</ent><br/
<hw>Spright"ful</hw> <pr>(-f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Spright</ets> sprite + <ets>full</ets>.]</ety> <def>Full of
spirit or of life; spirited; earnest; vivacious; lively; brisk; nimble;
gay.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <wordforms><wf>Spright"ful*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spright"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Spoke like a <qex>sprightful</qex> noble gentleman.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Steeds <qex>sprightful</qex> as the light.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprightless</ent><br/
<hw>Spright"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of life; dull;
sluggish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprightliness</ent><br/
<hw>Spright"li*ness</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The quality or state of being sprightly; liveliness; life;
briskness; vigor; activity; gayety; vivacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In dreams, observe with what a <qex>sprightliness</qex> and
alacrity does she [the soul] exert herself!</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprightly</ent><br/
<hw>Spright"ly</hw> <pr>(-l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Sprightlier</adjf>
<pr>(-l<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sprightliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[See <er>Sprite</er>.]</ety>
<def>Sprightlike, or spiritlike; lively; brisk; animated; vigorous;
airy; gay; <as>as, a <ex>sprightly</ex> youth; a <ex>sprightly</ex> air;
a <ex>sprightly</ex> dance.</as></def> <ldquo/<xex>Sprightly</xex> wit
and love inspires.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sprightly</qex> Sylvia trips along the green.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprigtail</ent><br/
<hw>Sprig"tail`</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/g"t<amac/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The pintail duck; -- called also
<altname>sprig</altname>, and <altname>spreet-tail</altname>.</def>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sharp-tailed
grouse.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spring</ent><br/
<hw>Spring</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Sprang</conjf> <pr>(spr<acr/ng)</pr> or
<conjf>Sprung</conjf> <pr>(spr<ucr/ng)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sprung</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Springing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>springan</ets>; akin
to D. & G. <ets>springen</ets>, OS. & OHG. <ets>springan</ets>, Icel. &
Sw. <ets>springa</ets>, Dan. <ets>springe</ets>; cf. Gr.
<grk>spe`rchesqai</grk> to hasten. Cf. <er>Springe</er>,
<er>Sprinkle</er>.]</ety><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <def>To leap; to bound; to jump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mountain stag that <qex>springs</qex><br/
From height to height, and bounds along the plains.</q>
<rj><qau>Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To issue with speed and violence; to move with
activity; to dart; to shoot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And sudden light<br/
<qex>Sprung</qex> through the vaulted roof.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To start or rise suddenly, as from a
covert.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Watchful as fowlers when their game will <qex>spring</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Otway.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To fly back; <as>as, a bow, when bent,
<ex>springs</ex> back by its elastic power</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To bend from a straight direction or plane surface;
to become warped; <as>as, a piece of timber, or a plank, sometimes
<ex>springs</ex> in seasoning</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To shoot up, out, or forth; to come to the light; to
begin to appear; to emerge; as a plant from its seed, as streams from
their source, and the like; -- often followed by <ptcl>up</ptcl>,
<ptcl>forth</ptcl>, or <ptcl>out</ptcl>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till well nigh the day began to <qex>spring</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To satisfy the desolate and waste ground, and to cause the bud of
the tender herb to <qex>spring</qex> forth.</q> <rj><qau>Job xxxviii.
27.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do not blast my <qex>springing</qex> hopes.</q>
<rj><qau>Rowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, <qex>spring</qex> to light; auspicious Babe, be born.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To issue or proceed, as from a parent or ancestor;
to result, as from a cause, motive, reason, or principle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They found] new hope to <qex>spring</qex><br/
Out of despair, joy, but with fear yet linked.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To grow; to thrive; to prosper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What makes all this, but Jupiter the king,<br/
At whose command we perish, and we <qex>spring</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To spring at</b></col>, <cd>to leap toward; to attempt to
reach by a leap.</cd> -- <col><b>To spring forth</b></col>, <cd>to leap
out; to rush out.</cd> -- <col><b>To spring in</b></col>, <cd>to rush
in; to enter with a leap or in haste.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To spring
on</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To spring upon</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to
leap on; to rush on with haste or violence; to assault.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spring</ent><br/
<hw>Spring</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To cause to spring up; to start or rouse, as game; to cause to rise
from the earth, or from a covert; <as>as, to <ex>spring</ex> a
pheasant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To produce or disclose suddenly or unexpectedly;
<as>as, to <ex>spring</ex> a surprise on someone; to <ex>spring</ex> a
joke</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She starts, and leaves her bed, and <qex>springs</qex> a
light.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The friends to the cause <qex>sprang</qex> a new project.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to explode; <as>as, to <ex>spring</ex> a
mine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To crack or split; to bend or strain so as to
weaken; <as>as, to <ex>spring</ex> a mast or a yard</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To cause to close suddenly, as the parts of a trap
operated by a spring; <as>as, to <ex>spring</ex> a trap</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To bend by force, as something stiff or strong; to
force or put by bending, as a beam into its sockets, and allowing it to
straighten when in place; -- often with <xex>in</xex>, <xex>out</xex>,
etc.; <as>as, to <ex>spring</ex> in a slat or a bar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To pass over by leaping; <as>as, to <ex>spring</ex>
a fence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To release (a person) from confinement, especially
from a prison.</def> <mark>[colloquial]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To spring a butt</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to
loosen the end of a plank in a ship's bottom.</cd> -- <col><b>To spring
a leak</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to begin to leak.</cd> --
<col><b>To spring an arch</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>to build an
arch; -- a common term among masons; <as>as, <ex>to spring an arch</ex>
over a lintel</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To spring a rattle</b></col>, <cd>to
cause a rattle to sound. See <cref>Watchman's rattle</cref>, under
<er>Watchman</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To spring the luff</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to ease the helm, and sail nearer to the wind
than before; -- said of a vessel.</cd> <au>Mar. Dict.</au> --
<mcol><col><b>To spring a mast</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To spring a
spar</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to strain it so that it is
unserviceable.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spring</ent><br/
<hw>Spring</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>spring</ets> a fountain, a
leap. See <er>Spring</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
leap; a bound; a jump.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The prisoner, with a <qex>spring</qex>, from prison broke.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A flying back; the resilience of a body recovering
its former state by its elasticity; <as>as, the <ex>spring</ex> of a
bow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Elastic power or force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Heavens! what a <qex>spring</qex> was in his arm!</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An elastic body of any kind, as steel, India rubber,
tough wood, or compressed air, used for various mechanical purposes, as
receiving and imparting power, diminishing concussion, regulating
motion, measuring weight or other force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The principal varieties of springs used in mechanisms
are the <stype>spiral spring</stype> (Fig. <it>a</it>), the <stype>coil
spring</stype> (Fig. <it>b</it>), the <stype>elliptic spring</stype>
(Fig. <it>c</it>), the <stype>half-elliptic spring</stype> (Fig.
<it>d</it>), the <stype>volute spring</stype>, the <stype>India-rubber
spring</stype>, the <stype>atmospheric spring</stype>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Any source of supply; especially, the source from
which a stream proceeds; an issue of water from the earth; a natural
fountain.</def> <ldquo/All my <xex>springs</xex> are in thee.<rdquo/
<au>Ps. lxxxvii. 7.</au> <ldquo/A secret <xex>spring</xex> of spiritual
joy.<rdquo/ <au>Bentley.</au> <ldquo/The sacred <xex>spring</xex> whence
right and honor streams.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir J. Davies.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Any active power; that by which action, or motion,
is produced or propagated; cause; origin; motive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our author shuns by vulgar <qex>springs</qex> to move<br/
The hero's glory, or the virgin's love.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>That which springs, or is originated, from a
source;</def> <specif>as:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A race;
lineage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chapman.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
youth; a springal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Spenser.</au>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>A shoot; a plant; a young tree; also, a grove of
trees; woodland.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Spenser. Milton.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>That which causes one to spring; specifically, a
lively tune.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>The season of the year when plants begin to vegetate
and grow; the vernal season, usually comprehending the months of March,
April, and May, in the middle latitudes north of the equator.</def>
<ldquo/The green lap of the new-come <xex>spring</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Spring</xex> of the astronomical year begins with
the vernal equinox, about March 21st, and ends with the summer solstice,
about June 21st.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>The time of growth and progress; early portion;
first stage; <as>as, the <xex>spring</xex> of life</as>.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>spring</xex> of the day.<rdquo/ <rj><au>1 Sam. ix.
26.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O how this <qex>spring</qex> of love resembleth<br/
The uncertain glory of an April day.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A crack or fissure
in a mast or yard, running obliquely or transversely.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A line led from a vessel's quarter to her cable so that by
tightening or slacking it she can be made to lie in any desired
position; a line led diagonally from the bow or stern of a vessel to
some point upon the wharf to which she is moored.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Air spring</b></col>, <col><b>Boiling
spring</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Air</er>,
<er>Boiling</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring back</b></col>
<fld>(Bookbinding)</fld>, <cd>a back with a curved piece of thin sheet
iron or of stiff pasteboard fastened to the inside, the effect of which
is to make the leaves of a book thus bound (as a ledger or other account
or blank book) spring up and lie flat.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring
balance</b></col>, <cd>a contrivance for measuring weight or force by
the elasticity of a spiral spring of steel.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring
beam</b></col>, <cd>a beam that supports the side of a paddle box. See
<cref>Paddle beam</cref>, under <er>Paddle</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd> --
<col><b>Spring beauty</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Any
plant of the genus <gen>Claytonia</gen>, delicate herbs with somewhat
fleshy leaves and pretty blossoms, appearing in springtime</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>A small, elegant American butterfly
(<spn>Erora laeta</spn>) which appears in spring. The hind wings of the
male are brown, bordered with deep blue; those of the female are mostly
blue.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring bed</b></col>, <cd>a mattress, under bed,
or bed bottom, in which springs, as of metal, are employed to give the
required elasticity.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring beetle</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a snapping beetle; an elater.</cd> --
<col><b>Spring box</b></col>, <cd>the box or barrel in a watch, or other
piece of mechanism, in which the spring is contained.</cd> --
<col><b>Spring fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a caddice fly; --
so called because it appears in the spring.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring
grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>vernal grass. See under
<er>Vernal</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring gun</b></col>, <cd>a firearm
discharged by a spring, when this is trodden upon or is otherwise
moved.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring hook</b></col> <fld>(Locomotive
Engines)</fld>, <cd>one of the hooks which fix the driving-wheel spring
to the frame.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring latch</b></col>, <cd>a latch that
fastens with a spring.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring lock</b></col>, <cd>a lock
that fastens with a spring.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring mattress</b></col>,
<cd>a spring bed.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring of an arch</b></col>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Springing line of an arch</cref>, under
<er>Springing</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring of pork</b></col>, <cd>the
lower part of a fore quarter, which is divided from the neck, and has
the leg and foot without the shoulder.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Nares.</au><br/
<br/
<q>Sir, pray hand the <qex>spring of pork</qex> to me.</q>
<rj><qau>Gayton.</qau></rj><br/
<br/
-- <col><b>Spring pin</b></col> <fld>(Locomotive Engines)</fld>, <cd>an
iron rod fitted between the springs and the axle boxes, to sustain and
regulate the pressure on the axles.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring
rye</b></col>, <cd>a kind of rye sown in the spring; -- in distinction
from <xex>winter rye</xex>, sown in autumn.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring
stay</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a preventer stay, to assist the
regular one.</cd> <au>R. H. Dana, Jr.</au> -- <col><b>Spring
tide</b></col>, <cd>the tide which happens at, or soon after, the new
and the full moon, and which rises higher than common tides.</cd> See
<er>Tide</er>. -- <col><b>Spring wagon</b></col>, <cd>a wagon in which
springs are interposed between the body and the axles to form elastic
supports.</cd> -- <col><b>Spring wheat</b></col>, <cd>any kind of wheat
sown in the spring; -- in distinction from <xex>winter wheat</xex>,
which is sown in autumn.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1394 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Springall</ent><br/
<ent>Springald</ent><br/
<ent>Springal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spring"al</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/ng"<ait/l)</pr>,
<hw>Spring"ald</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/ng"<ait/ld)</pr>, <hw>Spring"all</hw>
<pr>(spr<icr/ng"<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot.
<ets>springald</ets>, <ets>springel</ets>, fr. Scot. & E.
<ets>spring</ets>.]</ety> <def>An active, springy young man.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/There came two <xex>springals</xex> of full
tender years.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Joseph, when he was sold to Potiphar, that great man, was a fair
young <qex>springall</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springal</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>espringale</ets>; of
Teutonic origin, akin to E. <ets>spring</ets>.]</ety> <def>An ancient
military engine for casting stones and arrows by means of a
spring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springboard</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"board`</hw> <pr>(-b<omac/rd`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
elastic board, secured at the ends, or at one end, often by elastic
supports, used in performing feats of agility or in
exercising.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springbuck</ent><br/
<ent>Springbok</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Spring"bok`</hw> <pr>(-b<ocr/k`)</pr>,
<hw>Spring"buck`</hw> <pr>(-b<ucr/k`)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[D. <ets>springbok</ets>; <ets>springen</ets> to spring, leap +
<ets>bok</ets> a he-goat, buck.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A South
African gazelle (<spn>Gazella euchore</spn>) noted for its graceful form
and swiftness, and for its peculiar habit of springing lightly and
suddenly into the air. It has a white dorsal stripe, expanding into a
broad patch of white on the rump and tail. Called also
<altname>springer</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>springboc</asp>, and <asp>springbock</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springe</ent><br/
<hw>Springe</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/nj)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Spring</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>: cf. G. <ets>sprenkel</ets>, Prov. E.
<ets>springle</ets>.]</ety> <def>A noose fastened to an elastic body,
and drawn close with a sudden spring, whereby it catches a bird or other
animal; a gin; a snare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As a woodcock to mine own <qex>springe</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springe</ent><br/
<hw>Springe</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To catch in a springe; to
insnare.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springe</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"e</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/ng"<eit/ <or/ spr<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>v.
t.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sprengen</ets>. See <er>Sprinkle</er>.]</ety>
<def>To sprinkle; to scatter.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He would sowen some difficulty,<br/
Or <qex>springen</qex> cockle in our cleane corn.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springer</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"er</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/ng"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, springs; specifically, one who
rouses game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A young plant.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The impost, or point
at which an arch rests upon its support, and from which it seems to
spring.</def> Hence: <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The bottom stone of an arch,
which lies on the impost. The skew back is one form of springer.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>The rib of a groined vault, as being the solid
abutment for each section of vaulting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The grampus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A variety of the field spaniel.
See <er>Spaniel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A species of antelope; the
springbok.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springhalt</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"halt`</hw> <pr>(-h<add/lt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A kind of lameness in a horse. See
<er>Stringhalt</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springhead</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"head`</hw> <pr>(-h<ecr/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
fountain or source.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springiness</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"i*ness</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
state or quality of being springy.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springing</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process
of one who, or that which, springs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Growth; increase; also, that which springs up; a
shoot; a plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou blessest the <qex>springing</qex> thereof.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
lxv. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Springing line of an arch</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>,
<cd>the horizontal line drawn through the junction of the vertical face
of the impost with the curve of the intrados; -- called also
<altname>spring of an arch</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springle</ent><br/
<hw>Sprin"gle</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/<nsm/"g'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
springe.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springlet</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"let</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/ng"l<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
little spring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But yet from out the little hill<br/
Oozes the slender <qex>springlet</qex> still.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spring steel</ent><br/
<hw>Spring steel</hw>. <def>A variety of steel, elastic, strong, and
tough, rolled for springs, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springtail</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"tail`</hw> <pr>(-t<amac/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of small apterous
insects belonging to the order <ord>Thysanura</ord>. They have two
elastic caudal stylets which can be bent under the abdomen and then
suddenly extended like a spring, thus enabling them to leap to a
considerable distance. See <er>Collembola</er>, and
<er>Podura</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springtide</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"tide`</hw> <pr>(-t<imac/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The time
of spring; springtime.</def> <rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springtime</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"time`</hw> <pr>(-t<imac/m`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
season of spring; springtide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Springy</ent><br/
<hw>Spring"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Springier</adjf>
<pr>(-<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Springiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Spring</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Resembling, having the qualities of, or pertaining to,
a spring; elastic; <as>as, <ex>springy</ex> steel; a <ex>springy</ex>
step.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though her little frame was slight, it was firm and
<qex>springy</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Abounding with springs or fountains; wet; spongy;
<as>as, <ex>springy</ex> land</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprinkle</ent><br/
<hw>Sprin"kle</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/<nsm/"k'l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sprinkled</conjf>
<pr>(-k'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sprinkling</conjf>
<pr>(-kl<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sprenkelen</ets>, freq.
of <ets>sprengen</ets> to sprinkle, to scatter, AS. <ets>sprengan</ets>,
properly, to make to spring, causative of <ets>springan</ets> to spring;
akin to D. <ets>sprenkelen</ets> to sprinkle, G. <ets>sprengen</ets>.
See <er>Spring</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, and cf. <er>Sprent</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To scatter in small drops or particles, as water, seed,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To scatter on; to disperse something over in small
drops or particles; to besprinkle; <as>as, to <ex>sprinkle</ex> the
earth with water; to <ex>sprinkle</ex> a floor with sand</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To baptize by the application of a few drops, or a
small quantity, of water; hence, to cleanse; to purify.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Having our hearts <qex>sprinkled</qex> from an evil
conscience.</q> <rj><qau>Heb. x. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprinkle</ent><br/
<hw>Sprin"kle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To scatter a
liquid, or any fine substance, so that it may fall in
particles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the priest shall . . . <qex>sprinkle</qex> of the oil with his
finger seven times before the Lord.</q> <rj><qau>Lev. xiv.
16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To rain moderately, or with scattered drops falling
now and then; <as>as, it <ex>sprinkles</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fly or be scattered in small drops or
particles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprinkle</ent><br/
<hw>Sprin"kle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small quantity
scattered, or sparsely distributed; a sprinkling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A utensil for sprinkling; a sprinkler.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprinkler</ent><br/
<hw>Sprin"kler</hw> <pr>(-kl<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who sprinkles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument or vessel used in sprinkling;
specifically, a watering pot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprinkling</ent><br/
<hw>Sprin"kling</hw> <pr>(-kl<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of one who, or that which, sprinkles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Baptism may well enough be performed by <qex>sprinkling</qex> or
effusion of water.</q> <rj><qau>Ayliffe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small quantity falling in distinct drops or
particles; <as>as, a <ex>sprinkling</ex> of rain or snow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, a moderate number or quantity distributed
like separate drops, or as if scattered like drops.</def>
<rj><au>Craik.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprint</ent><br/
<hw>Sprint</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/nt)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sprinted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sprinting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sprunt</er>.]</ety> <def>To run very rapidly; to run at full
speed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A runner [in a quarter-mile race] should be able to
<qex>sprint</qex> the whole way.</q> <rj><qau>Encyc.
Brit.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprint</ent><br/
<hw>Sprint</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of sprinting; a run of a
short distance at full speed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sprint race</b></col>, <cd>a foot race at the highest
running speed; -- usually limited to distances under a quarter of a
mile.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprinter</ent><br/
<hw>Sprint"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sprints; one who runs in sprint races; <as>as, a champion
<ex>sprinter</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprit</ent><br/
<hw>Sprit</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Akin to G.
<ets>spritzen</ets>, <ets>spr<uum/tzen</ets>. See <er>Sprit</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To throw out with force from a narrow
orifice; to eject; to spurt out.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir
T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprit</ent><br/
<hw>Sprit</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>spryttan</ets> to
sprout, bud. See <er>Sprout</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Spurt</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, <er>Sprit</er> a spar.]</ety> <def>To
sprout; to bud; to germinate, as barley steeped for malt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprit</ent><br/
<hw>Sprit</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shoot; a sprout.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprit</ent><br/
<hw>Sprit</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spret</ets>, AS.
<ets>spre<oacute/t</ets> a sprit; spear; akin to D. <ets>spriet</ets>,
and E. <ets>sprout</ets>, <ets>sprit</ets>, v.t. & i. See
<er>Sprout</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A small
boom, pole, or spar, which crosses the sail of a boat diagonally from
the mast to the upper aftmost corner, which it is used to extend and
elevate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprite</ent><br/
<hw>Sprite</hw> <pr>(spr<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sprit</ets>, F. <ets>esprit</ets>, fr. L. <ets>spiritus</ets>. See
<er>Spirit</er>, and cf. <er>Sprightly</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
spirit; a soul; a shade; also, an apparition. See
<er>Spright</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gaping graves received the wandering, guilty
<qex>sprite</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An elf; a fairy; a goblin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The green woodpecker, or
yaffle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spritely</ent><br/
<ent>Spriteliness</ent><br/
<ent>Spritefully</ent><br/
<ent>Spriteful</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Sprite"ful</hw> <pr>(-f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>,
<hw>Sprite"ful*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos>, <hw>Sprite"li*ness</hw>
<pr>(-l<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <hw>Sprite"ly</hw>,
<pos>a.</pos>, etc.</mhw> <def>See <er>Sprightful</er>,
<er>Sprightfully</er>, <er>Sprightliness</er>, <er>Sprightly</er>,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spritsail</ent><br/
<hw>Sprit"sail</hw> <pr>(spr<icr/t"s<amac/l; <it>among seamen</it>
spr<icr/t"s'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>A sail extended by a sprit.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A sail formerly
hung under the bowsprit, from the spritsail yard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprocket</ent><br/
<hw>Sprock"et</hw> <pr>(spr<ocr/k"<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Etymology uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
tooth or projection, as on the periphery of a wheel, shaped so as to
engage with a chain.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A sprocket wheel.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprocket wheel</ent><br/
<hw>Sprock"et wheel`</hw> <pr>(spr<ocr/k"<ecr/t hw<emac/l`)</pr>.
<ety>[Etymology of <ets>sprocket</ets> is uncertain.]</ety>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Chain wheel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprod</ent><br/
<hw>Sprod</hw> <pr>(spr<ocr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gael. & Ir.
<ets>bradan</ets> a salmon.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A salmon in
its second year.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprong</ent><br/
<hw>Sprong</hw> <pr>(spr<ocr/ng)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos>
<mord>of <er>Spring</er>.</mord> <def>Sprung.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprout</ent><br/
<hw>Sprout</hw> <pr>(sprout)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sprouted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sprouting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sprouten</ets>,
<ets>spruten</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>spr<umac/ta</ets>, AS.
<ets>spre<oacute/tan</ets>, D. <ets>spruiten</ets>, G.
<ets>spriessen</ets>, Sw. <ets>spruta</ets> to squirt, to spout. Cf.
<er>Sprit</er>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos>, <er>Sprit</er> a spar,
<er>Spout</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, <er>Spurt</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To shoot, as the seed of a plant; to germinate; to push out new
shoots; hence, to grow like shoots of plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To shoot into ramifications.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprout</ent><br/
<hw>Sprout</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to sprout;
<as>as, the rain will <ex>sprout</ex> the seed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To deprive of sprouts; <as>as, to <ex>sprout</ex>
potatoes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprout</ent><br/
<hw>Sprout</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>sprote</ets> a sprout,
sprig; akin to Icel. <ets>sproti</ets>, G. <ets>sprosse</ets>. See
<er>Sprout</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The shoot of a
plant; a shoot from the seed, from the stump, or from the root or tuber,
of a plant or tree; more rarely, a shoot from the stem of a plant, or
the end of a branch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Young coleworts; Brussels
sprouts.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Brussels sprouts</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Brussels</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spruce</ent><br/
<hw>Spruce</hw> <pr>(spr<udd/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>Spruce</ets> or <ets>Pruse</ets>, Prussia, Prussian. So named
because it was first known as a native of Prussia, or because its
sprouts were used for making, <ets>spruce beer</ets>. Cf. <ets>Spruce
beer</ets>, below, <er>Spruce</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any coniferous tree of the genus
<gen>Picea</gen>, as the Norway spruce (<spn>Picea excelsa</spn>), and
the white and black spruces of America (<spn>Picea alba</spn> and
<spn>Picea nigra</spn>), besides several others in the far Northwest.
See <er>Picea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The wood or timber of the spruce tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Prussia leather; pruce.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Spruce</qex>, a sort of leather corruptly so called for
Prussia leather.</q> <rj><qau>E. Phillips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Douglas spruce</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
valuable timber tree (<spn>Pseudotsuga Douglasii</spn>) of Northwestern
America.</cd> -- <col><b>Essence of spruce</b></col>, <cd>a thick,
dark-colored, bitterish, and acidulous liquid made by evaporating a
decoction of the young branches of spruce.</cd> -- <col><b>Hemlock
spruce</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a graceful coniferous tree
(<spn>Tsuga Canadensis</spn>) of North America. Its timber is valuable,
and the bark is largely used in tanning leather.</cd> -- <col><b>Spruce
beer</b></col>. <ety>[G. <ets>sprossenbier</ets>; <ets>sprosse</ets>
sprout, shoot (akin to E. <ets>sprout</ets>, n.) + <ets>bier</ets> beer.
The word was changed into <ex>spruce</ex> beer because the beer came
from Prussia (OE. <ets>Spruce</ets>), or because it was made from the
sprouts of the spruce. See <er>Sprout</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Beer</er>, and cf. <er>Spruce</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <cd>A kind
of beer which is tinctured or flavored with spruce, either by means of
the extract or by decoction.</cd> -- <col><b>Spruce grouse</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Spruce partridge</cref>,
below.</cd> -- <col><b>Spruce leather</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Spruce</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</cd> -- <col><b>Spruce
partridge</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a handsome American grouse
(<spn>Dendragapus Canadensis</spn>) found in Canada and the Northern
United States; -- called also <altname>Canada
grouse</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spruce</ent><br/
<hw>Spruce</hw> <pr>(spr<udd/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Sprucer</adjf>
<pr>(spr<udd/"s<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Sprucest</adjf>
<pr>(spr<udd/"s<ecr/st)</pr>.]</amorph> <ety>[Perhaps fr.
<ets>spruce</ets> a sort of leather from Prussia, which was an article
of finery. See <er>Spruce</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Neat, without elegance or dignity; smart; trim; -- formerly applied
to things with a serious meaning; now chiefly applied to persons.</def>
<ldquo/Neat and <xex>spruce</xex> array.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Remedy of
Love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Sprightly; dashing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Now, my <xex>spruce</xex> companions.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is so <qex>spruce</qex> that he can never be genteel.</q>
<rj><qau>Tatler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Finical; neat; trim. See
<er>Finical</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spruce"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spruce"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spruce</ent><br/
<hw>Spruce</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spruced</conjf> <pr>(spr<udd/st)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sprucing</conjf> <pr>(spr<udd/"s<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
dress with affected neatness; to trim; to make spruce; -- often used
with <ptcl>up</ptcl>; <as>as, to <ex>spruce</ex> up the house for
Company</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spruce</ent><br/
<hw>Spruce</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To dress one's self with affected
neatness; <as>as, to <ex>spruce</ex> up</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprue</ent><br/
<hw>Sprue</hw> <pr>(spr<udd/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Strictly, the hole through which melted metal is poured into the
gate, and thence into the mold.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The waste piece
of metal cast in this hole; hence, dross.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Sprew</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprug</ent><br/
<hw>Sprug</hw> <pr>(spr<ucr/g)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Prov. E.
<ets>sprug up</ets> to dress neatly, <ets>sprag</ets> to prop, a.,
lively.]</ety> <def>To make smart.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprung</ent><br/
<hw>Sprung</hw> <pr>(spr<ucr/ng)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Spring</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprung</ent><br/
<hw>Sprung</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Said of a spar
that has been cracked or strained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprunt</ent><br/
<hw>Sprunt</hw> <pr>(spr<ucr/nt)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sprout</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To spring up; to
germinate; to spring forward or outward.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sprunt up</b></col>, <cd>to draw one's self up
suddenly, as in anger or defiance; to bristle up.</cd> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprunt</ent><br/
<hw>Sprunt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything short and
stiff.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A leap; a spring.</def> <mark>[Obs. <or/ Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A steep ascent in a road.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sprunt</ent><br/
<hw>Sprunt</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Active; lively; vigorous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Kersey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spruntly</ent><br/
<hw>Sprunt"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sprunt manner; smartly;
vigorously; youthfully.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spry</ent><br/
<hw>Spry</hw> <pr>(spr<imac/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Sprier</adjf> or <adjf>Spryer</adjf>
<pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Spriest</adjf> or
<adjf>Spryest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Cf. dial. Sw. <ets>sprygg</ets>
lively, skittish, and E. <ets>sprag</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having great
power of leaping or running; nimble; active.</def> <mark>[U.S. & Local
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She is as <qex>spry</qex> as a cricket.</q> <rj><qau>S. Judd
(Margaret).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If I'm not so large as you,<br/
You are not so small as I,<br/
And not half so <qex>spry</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spud</ent><br/
<hw>Spud</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>spyd</ets> a spear.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sharp, narrow spade,
usually with a long handle, used by farmers for digging up large-rooted
weeds; a similarly shaped implement used for various purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My <qex>spud</qex> these nettles from the stone can part.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A dagger.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything short and thick; specifically, a piece of
dough boiled in fat.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A potato.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spue</ent><br/
<hw>Spue</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spew</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spuilzie</ent><br/
<hw>Spuil"zie</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/l"z<icr/ <or/ -y<icr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Spulzie</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spuke</ent><br/
<hw>Spuke</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Spook</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spuller</ent><br/
<hw>Spul"ler</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/l"l<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<altsp>[For <asp>spooler</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[See
<er>Spool</er>.]</ety> <def>One employed to inspect yarn, to see that it
is well spun, and fit for the loom.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>spulzie</ent><br/
<hw>spul"zie</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/l"z<icr/ <or/ sp<ucr/l"y<icr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Spoil</er>.]</ety> <def>Plunder, or
booty.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>spuilzie</asp>, and
<asp>spulye</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spume</ent><br/
<hw>Spume</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spuma</ets>. Cf. <er>Pumice</er>, <er>Spoom</er>.]</ety>
<def>Frothy matter raised on liquids by boiling, effervescence, or
agitation; froth; foam; scum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Materials dark and crude,<br/
Of spiritous and fiery <qex>spume</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spume</ent><br/
<hw>Spume</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spumed</conjf> <pr>(sp<umac/md)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spuming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>spumare</ets>.]</ety>
<def>To froth; to foam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spumeous</ent><br/
<hw>Spume"ous</hw> <pr>(-<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Spumous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spumescence</ent><br/
<hw>Spu*mes"cence</hw> <pr>(sp<usl/*m<ecr/s"s<eit/ns)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Spumescent</er>.]</ety> <def>The state of
being foamy; frothiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spumescent</ent><br/
<hw>Spu*mes"cent</hw> <pr>(-s<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spumescens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>spumescere</ets> to grow foamy,
from <ets>spuma</ets> foam.]</ety> <def>Resembling froth or foam;
foaming.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spumid</ent><br/
<hw>Spum"id</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/m"<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spumidus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Spumous; frothy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spumiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Spu*mif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(sp<usl/*m<icr/f"<etil/r*<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>spumifier</ets>; <ets>spuma</ets> foam +
<ets>ferre</ets> to bear.]</ety> <def>Producing foam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spuminess</ent><br/
<hw>Spum"i*ness</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/m"<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The quality or condition of being spumy; spumescence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1395 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Spumy</ent><br/
<ent>Spumous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spum"ous</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/m"<ucr/s)</pr>, <hw>Spum"y</hw>
<pr>(sp<umac/m"<ycr/)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>spumosus</ets>, fr. <ets>spuma</ets> foam: cf. F.
<ets>spumeux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of, containing, or covered
with, froth, scum, or foam; frothy; foamy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spumous</qex> and florid state of the blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>spumy</qex> waves proclaim the watery war.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spun</ent><br/
<hw>Spun</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/n)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Spin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spun hay</b></col>, <cd>hay twisted into ropes for
convenient carriage, as on a military expedition.</cd> -- <col><b>Spun
silk</b></col>, <cd>a cheap article produced from floss, or
short-fibered, broken, and waste silk, carded and spun, in distinction
from the long filaments wound from the cocoon. It is often mixed with
cotton.</cd> -- <col><b>Spun yarn</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a
line formed of two or more rope-yarns loosely twisted.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spunge</ent><br/
<hw>Spunge</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/nj)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sponge.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spunk</ent><br/
<hw>Spunk</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>spong</ets>, or Ir. <ets>sponc</ets>, tinder, sponge; cf. AS.
<ets>sponge</ets> a sponge (L. <ets>spongia</ets>), <ets>sp<omac/n</ets>
a chip. Cf. <er>Sponge</er>, <er>Punk</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sponk</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Wood that readily takes
fire; touchwood; also, a kind of tinder made from a species of fungus;
punk; amadou.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An inflammable temper; spirit; mettle; pluck;
<as>as, a man of <ex>spunk</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A lawless and dangerous set, men of <qex>spunk</qex>, and spirit,
and power, both of mind and body.</q> <rj><qau>Prof.
Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spunky</ent><br/
<hw>Spunk"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Spunkier</adjf>
<pr>(-<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Spunkiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Full of spunk; quick;
spirited.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spur</ent><br/
<hw>Spur</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sparrow</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
sparrow.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A tern.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spur</ent><br/
<hw>Spur</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>spure</ets>,
<ets>spore</ets>, AS. <ets>spura</ets>, <ets>spora</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>spoor</ets>, G. <ets>sporn</ets>, OHG. <ets>sporo</ets>, Icel.
<ets>spori</ets>, Dan. <ets>spore</ets>, Sw. <ets>sporre</ets>, and to
AS. <ets>spor</ets> a trace, footstep, <ets>spyrian</ets> to trace,
track, examine, and E. <ets>spurn</ets>. <root/171. Cf.
<er>Sparrow</er>, <er>Spere</er>, <er>Spoor</er>, <er>Spurn</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An implement secured to the heel, or above the heel, of
a horseman, to urge the horse by its pressure. Modern spurs have a small
wheel, or rowel, with short points. Spurs were the badge of
knighthood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And on her feet a pair of <qex>spurs</qex> large.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- To earn one's spurs, win one's spurs, to earn recognition for
achievement or for expertise. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which goads to action; an incitement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fame is the <qex>spur</qex> that the clear spirit doth raise<br/
(That last infirmity of noble mind)<br/
To scorn delights and live laborious days.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something that projects; a snag.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One of the large or principal roots of a tree.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any stiff, sharp spine, as on the
wings and legs of certain birds, on the legs of insects, etc.;
especially, the spine on a cock's leg.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A mountain that shoots from any other mountain, or
range of mountains, and extends to some distance in a lateral direction,
or at right angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A spiked iron worn by seamen upon the bottom of the
boot, to enable them to stand upon the carcass of a whale, to strip off
the blubber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>A brace strengthening a post and
some connected part, as a rafter or crossbeam; a strut.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The short wooden
buttress of a post.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A projection from the round
base of a column, occupying the angle of a square plinth upon which the
base rests, or bringing the bottom bed of the base to a nearly square
form. It is generally carved in leafage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any projecting
appendage of a flower looking like a spur.</def> <au>Gray.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Ergotized rye or other grain.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>A wall that crosses a part of a
rampart and joins to an inner wall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A piece of
timber fixed on the bilge ways before launching, having the upper ends
bolted to the vessel's side.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A curved piece of
timber serving as a half beam to support the deck where a whole beam can
not be placed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A branch of a vein.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>The track of an animal, as an otter; a
spoor.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spur fowl</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
several species of Asiatic gallinaceous birds of the genus
<gen>Galloperdix</gen>, allied to the jungle fowl. The males have two or
more spurs on each leg.</cd> -- <col><b>Spur gear</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a cogwheel having teeth which project radially
and stand parallel to the axis; a spur wheel.</cd> -- <col><b>Spur
gearing</b></col>, <cd>gearing in which spur gears are used. See under
<er>Gearing</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spur pepper</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See the Note under <er>Capsicum</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Spur wheel</b></col>. <cd>Same as <cref>Spur gear</cref>,
above.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spur</ent><br/
<hw>Spur</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spurred</conjf> <pr>(sp<ucir/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spurring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To prick with spurs;
to incite to a more hasty pace; to urge or goad; <as>as, to
<ex>spur</ex> a horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To urge or encourage to action, or to a more
vigorous pursuit of an object; to incite; to stimulate; to instigate; to
impel; to drive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Love will not be <qex>spurred</qex> to what it loathes.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To put spurs on; <as>as, a <ex>spurred</ex>
boot</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spur</ent><br/
<hw>Spur</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To spur on one's horse; to travel
with great expedition; to hasten; hence, to press forward in any
pursuit.</def> <ldquo/Now <xex>spurs</xex> the lated traveler.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Parthians shall be there,<br/
And, <qex>spurring</qex> from the fight, confess their fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The roads leading to the capital were covered with multitudes of
yeomen, <qex>spurring</qex> hard to Westminster.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some bold men, . . . by <qex>spurring</qex> on, refine
themselves.</q> <rj><qau>Grew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurgall</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"gall`</hw> <pr>(-g<add/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place
galled or excoriated by much using of the spur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurgall</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"gall`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To gall or wound with a
spur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurge</ent><br/
<hw>Spurge</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/rj)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>To emit foam; to froth; -- said of the emission
of yeast from beer in course of fermentation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>W. Cartright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurge</ent><br/
<hw>Spurge</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>espurge</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/purge</ets>, from OF. <ets>espurgier</ets> to purge, L.
<ets>expurgare</ets>. See <er>Expurgate</er>, <er>Purge</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of the genus Euphorbia. See
<er>Euphorbia</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spurge flax</b></col>, <cd>an evergreen shrub
(<spn>Daphne Gnidium</spn>) with crowded narrow leaves. It is a native
of Southern Europe.</cd> -- <col><b>Spurge laurel</b></col>, <cd>a
European shrub (<spn>Daphne Laureola</spn>) with oblong evergreen
leaves.</cd> -- <col><b>Spurge nettle</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Nettle</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Spurge olive</b></col>, <cd>an
evergreen shrub (<spn>Daphne oleoides</spn>) found in the Mediterranean
region.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurgewort</ent><br/
<hw>Spurge"wort`</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/rj"w<ucir/rt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any euphorbiaceous plant.</def>
<rj><au>Lindley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurging</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"ging</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r"j<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[See 2d <er>Spurge</er>.]</ety> <def>A purging.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurious</ent><br/
<hw>Spu"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/"r<icr/*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>spurius</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not proceeding from
the true source, or from the source pretended; not genuine; counterfeit;
false; adulterate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not legitimate; bastard; <as>as, <ex>spurious</ex>
issue</as>.</def> <ldquo/Her <xex>spurious</xex> firstborn.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Spurious primary</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Spurious quill</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
first, or outer, primary quill when rudimentary or much reduced in size,
as in certain singing birds.</cd> -- <col><b>Spurious wing</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the bastard wing, or alula.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Counterfeit; false; adulterate; supposititious;
fictitious; bastard.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Spu"ri*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Spu"ri*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurless</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"less</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r"l<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Having no spurs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurling</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"ling</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sparling</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A tern.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurling-line</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"ling-line`</hw> <pr>(-l<imac/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
Prov. E. <ets>spurling</ets> the rut of a wheel, a cart rut, AS.
<ets>spor</ets> a track, trace, E. <ets>spoor</ets>, Scot.
<ets>spurl</ets> to sprawl.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The line
which forms the communication between the steering wheel and the
telltale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurn</ent><br/
<hw>Spurn</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/rn)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spurned</conjf>
<pr>(sp<ucir/rnd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spurning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>spurnen</ets> to kick
against, to stumble over, AS. <ets>spurnan</ets> to kick, offend; akin
to <ets>spura</ets> spur, OS. & OHG. <ets>spurnan</ets> to kick, Icel.
<ets>spyrna</ets>, L. <ets>spernere</ets> to despise, Skr.
<ets>sphur</ets> to jerk, to push. <root/171. See <er>Spur</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To drive back or away, as with the foot; to
kick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The bird] with his foot will <qex>spurn</qex> adown his cup.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>spurn</qex> thee like a cur out of my way.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To reject with disdain; to scorn to receive or
accept; to treat with contempt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What safe and nicely I might well delay<br/
By rule of knighthood, I disdain and <qex>spurn</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Domestics will pay a more cheerful service when they find
themselves not <qex>spurned</qex> because fortune has laid them at their
master's feet.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurn</ent><br/
<hw>Spurn</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To kick or toss up the
heels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The miller <qex>spurned</qex> at a stone.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The drunken chairman in the kennel <qex>spurns</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To manifest disdain in rejecting anything; to make
contemptuous opposition or resistance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nay, more, to <qex>spurn</qex> at your most royal image.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurn</ent><br/
<hw>Spurn</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kick; a blow with the
foot.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What defense can properly be used in such a despicable encounter
as this but either the slap or the <qex>spurn</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Disdainful rejection; contemptuous
treatment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The insolence of office, and the <qex>spurns</qex><br/
That patient merit of the unworthy takes.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A body of coal left to sustain
an overhanging mass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurner</ent><br/
<hw>Spurn"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
spurns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurn-water</ent><br/
<hw>Spurn"-wa`ter</hw> <pr>(-w<add/`t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A channel at the end of a deck to restrain the
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurred</ent><br/
<hw>Spurred</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/rd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Wearing spurs; furnished with a spur or spurs; having shoots like
spurs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Affected with spur, or ergot; <as>as,
<ex>spurred</ex> rye</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spurred corolla</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
corolla in which there are one or more petals with a spur.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurrer</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"rer</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r"r<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who spurs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurrey</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"rey</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/r"r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See <er>Spurry</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurrier</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"ri*er</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r"r<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One whose occupation is to make spurs.</def> <au>B. Jonson.</au>
<ldquo/The saddlers and <xex>spurriers</xex> would be ruined by
thousands.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spur-royal</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"-roy`al</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r"roi`<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A gold coin, first made in the reign of Edward IV., having a star
on the reverse resembling the rowel of a spur. In the reigns of
Elizabeth and of James I., its value was fifteen shillings.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>spur-rial</asp>, and
<asp>spur-ryal</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurry</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"ry</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/r"r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. or
OF. <ets>spurrie</ets>; cf. G. <ets>spergel</ets>, NL.
<ets>spergula</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An annual herb
(<spn>Spergula arvensis</spn>) with whorled filiform leaves, sometimes
grown in Europe for fodder.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>spurrey</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sand spurry</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any low herb
of the genus <gen>Lepigonum</gen>, mostly found in sandy
places.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spur-shell</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"-shell`</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r"sh<ecr/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of handsome marine
gastropod shells of the genus <gen>Trochus</gen>, or
<gen>Imperator</gen>. The shell is conical, with the margin toothed
somewhat like the rowel of a spur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurt</ent><br/
<hw>Spurt</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/rt)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Written
also <ets>spirt</ets>, and originally the same word as <ets>sprit</ets>;
OE. <ets>sprutten</ets> to sprout, AS. <ets>spryttan</ets>. See
<er>Sprit</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, <er>Sprout</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To gush or issue suddenly or violently out in a
stream, as liquor from a cask; to rush from a confined place in a small
stream or jet; to spirt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus the small jet, which hasty hands unlock,<br/
<qex>Spurts</qex> in the gardener's eyes who turns the cock.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurt</ent><br/
<hw>Spurt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To throw out, as a liquid, in a
stream or jet; to drive or force out with violence, as a liquid from a
pipe or small orifice; <as>as, to <ex>spurt</ex> water from the
mouth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurt</ent><br/
<hw>Spurt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sudden or violent
ejection or gushing of a liquid, as of water from a tube, orifice, or
other confined place, or of blood from a wound; a jet; a
spirt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A shoot; a bud.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>A sudden outbreak; <as>as, a
<ex>spurt</ex> of jealousy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spurt grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a rush fit
for basket work.</cd> <rj><au>Dr. Prior.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurt</ent><br/
<hw>Spurt</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/rt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>sprettr</ets> a spurt, spring, run, <ets>spretta</ets> to spirt,
spring.]</ety> <def>A sudden and energetic effort, as in an emergency;
an increased exertion for a brief space.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The long, steady sweep of the so-called <ldquo/paddle<rdquo/ tried
him almost as much as the breathless strain of the <qex>spurt</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>T. Hughes.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurt</ent><br/
<hw>Spurt</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Spurted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Spurting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make a sudden and violent
exertion, as in an emergency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurtle</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"tle</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r"t'l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Freq.
of <ets>spurt</ets>.]</ety> <def>To spurt or shoot in a scattering
manner.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spurway</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"way`</hw> <pr>(sp<ucir/r"w<amac/`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Prov. E. <ets>spoor</ets> a track, trace (AS. <ets>spor</ets>) +
<ets>way</ets>.]</ety> <def>A bridle path.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spur-winged</ent><br/
<hw>Spur"-winged`</hw> <pr>(-w<icr/ngd`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having one or more spurs on the bend of the
wings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spur-winged goose</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
one of several species of long-legged African geese of the genus
<gen>Plectropterus</gen> and allied genera, having a strong spur on the
bend of the wing, as the Gambo goose (<spn>Plectropterus
Gambensis</spn>) and the Egyptian, or Nile, goose (<spn>Alopochen
Aegyptiaca</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Spur-winged plover</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an Old World plover (<spn>Hoplopterus
spinosus</spn>) having a sharp spur on the bend of the wing. It inhabits
Northern Africa and the adjacent parts of Asia and Europe.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sput</ent><br/
<hw>Sput</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Steam
Boiler)</fld> <def>An annular reenforce, to strengthen a place where a
hole is made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sputation</ent><br/
<hw>Spu*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(sp<usl/*t<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>sputare</ets> to spit, v. intens. fr. <ets>spuere</ets> to
spit: cf. F. <ets>sputation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of spitting;
expectoration.</def> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sputative</ent><br/
<hw>Spu"ta*tive</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/"t<adot/*t<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Inclined to spit; spitting much.</def> <rj><au>Sir H.
Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spute</ent><br/
<hw>Spute</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. from
<ets>dispute</ets>.]</ety> <def>To dispute; to discuss.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sputter</ent><br/
<hw>Sput"ter</hw> <pr>(sp<ucr/t"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sputtered</conjf>
<pr>(-t<etil/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sputtering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From the root of
<ets>spout</ets> or <ets>spit</ets> to eject from the mouth. Cf.
<er>Splutter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To spit, or to emit saliva
from the mouth in small, scattered portions, as in rapid
speaking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To utter words hastily and indistinctly; to speak so
rapidly as to emit saliva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They could neither of them speak their rage, and so fell a
<qex>sputtering</qex> at one another, like two roasting apples.</q>
<rj><qau>Congreve.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To throw out anything, as little jets of steam, with
a noise like that made by one sputtering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like the green wood . . . <qex>sputtering</qex> in the flame.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sputter</ent><br/
<hw>Sput"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To spit out hastily by quick,
successive efforts, with a spluttering sound; to utter hastily and
confusedly, without control over the organs of speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the midst of caresses, and without the least pretended
incitement, to <qex>sputter</qex> out the basest accusations.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sputter</ent><br/
<hw>Sput"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Moist matter thrown out in small
detached particles; also, confused and hasty speech.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sputterer</ent><br/
<hw>Sput"ter*er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sputters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sputum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Spu"tum</hw> <pr>(sp<umac/"t<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sputa</plw> <pr>(-t<adot/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.,
from <ets>spuere</ets>, <ets>sputum</ets>, to spit.]</ety> <def>That
which is expectorated; a salival discharge; spittle; saliva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spy</ent><br/
<hw>Spy</hw> <pr>(sp<imac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Spied</conjf> <pr>(sp<imac/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Spying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>spien</ets>,
<ets>espien</ets>, OF. <ets>espier</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/pier</ets>,
OHG. <ets>speh<omac/n</ets>, G. <ets>sp<aum/hen</ets>; akin to L.
<ets>specere</ets> to see, Skr. <ets>spa<cced/</ets>. <root/ 169. Cf.
<er>Espy</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, <er>Aspect</er>, <er>Auspice</er>,
<er>Circumspect</er>, <er>Conspicuous</er>, <er>Despise</er>,
<er>Frontispiece</er>, <er>Inspect</er>, <er>Prospect</er>,
<er>Respite</er>, <er>Scope</er>, <er>Specimen</er>, <er>Spectacle</er>,
<er>Specter</er>, <er>Speculate</er>, <er>Spice</er>, <er>Spite</er>,
<er>Suspicion</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To gain sight of; to
discover at a distance, or in a state of concealment; to espy; to
see.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One, in reading, skipped over all sentences where he
<qex>spied</qex> a note of admiration.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To discover by close search or
examination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Look about with your eyes; <qex>spy</qex> what things are to be
reformed in the church of England.</q> <rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To explore; to view, inspect, and examine secretly,
as a country; -- usually with <ptcl>out</ptcl>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moses sent to <qex>spy</qex> out Jaazer, and they took the
villages thereof.</q> <rj><qau>Num. xxi. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spy</ent><br/
<hw>Spy</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To search narrowly; to
scrutinize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is my nature's plague<br/
To <qex>spy</qex> into abuses.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spy</ent><br/
<hw>Spy</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Spies</plw>
<pr>(sp<imac/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See <er>Spy</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Espy</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who keeps a
constant watch of the conduct of others.</def> <ldquo/These wretched
<xex>spies</xex> of wit.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A person sent secretly into an
enemy's camp, territory, or fortifications, to inspect his works,
ascertain his strength, movements, or designs, and to communicate such
intelligence to the proper officer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Spy money</b></col>, <cd>money paid to a spy; the reward
for private or secret intelligence regarding the enemy.</cd> --
<col><b>Spy Wednesday</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>the Wednesday
immediately preceding the festival of Easter; -- so called in allusion
to the betrayal of Christ by Judas Iscariot.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Emissary</er>, and
<er>Scout</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spyboat</ent><br/
<hw>Spy"boat`</hw> <pr>(-b<omac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A boat sent
to make discoveries and bring intelligence.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spyglass</ent><br/
<hw>Spy"glass`</hw> <pr>(-gl<adot/s`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small
telescope for viewing distant terrestrial objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spyism</ent><br/
<hw>Spy"ism</hw> <pr>(-<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act or
business of spying.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Spyne</ent><br/
<ent>Spynace</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Spy"nace</hw> <pr>(sp<imac/"n<asl/s; 48)</pr>, <hw>Spyne</hw>
<pr>(sp<imac/n)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pinnace</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1 <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squab</ent><br/
<hw>Squab</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/b)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. dial. Sw.
<ets>sqvabb</ets> a soft and fat body, <ets>sqvabba</ets> a fat woman,
Icel. <ets>kvap</ets> jelly, jellylike things, and E.
<ets>quab</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Fat; thick; plump;
bulky.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor the <qex>squab</qex> daughter nor the wife were nice.</q>
<rj><qau>Betterton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unfledged; unfeathered; <as>as, a <ex>squab</ex>
pigeon</as>.</def> <rj><au>King.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squab</ent><br/
<hw>Squab</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
nestling of a pigeon or other similar bird, esp. when very fat and not
fully fledged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person of a short, fat figure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gorgonious sits abdominous and wan,<br/
Like a fat <qex>squab</qex> upon a Chinese fan.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A thickly stuffed cushion; especially, one used for
the seat of a sofa, couch, or chair; also, a sofa.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Punching the <qex>squab</qex> of chairs and sofas.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On her large <qex>squab</qex> you find her spread.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squab</ent><br/
<hw>Squab</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[Cf. dial. Sw. <ets>sqvapp</ets>, a
word imitative of a splash, and E. <ets>squab</ets> fat,
unfledged.]</ety> <def>With a heavy fall; plump.</def>
<mark>[Vulgar]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The eagle took the tortoise up into the air, and dropped him down,
<qex>squab</qex>, upon a rock.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squab</ent><br/
<hw>Squab</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To fall plump; to strike at one
dash, or with a heavy stroke.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squabash</ent><br/
<hw>Squa*bash"</hw> <pr>(skw<adot/*b<acr/sh")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To crush; to quash; to squash.</def> <mark>[Colloq. or Slang,
Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squabbish</ent><br/
<hw>Squab"bish</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/b"b<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Thick; fat; heavy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1396 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Squabble</ent><br/
<hw>Squab"ble</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/b"b'l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squabbled</conjf>
<pr>(-b'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Squabbling</conjf>
<pr>(-bl<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. dial. Sw. <ets>skvabbel</ets>
a dispute, <ets>skvappa</ets> to chide.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
contend for superiority in an unseemly manner; to scuffle; to struggle;
to wrangle; to quarrel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To debate peevishly; to dispute.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sense of these propositions is very plain, though logicians
might <qex>squabble</qex> a whole day whether they should rank them
under negative or affirmative.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To dispute; contend; scuffle; wrangle; quarrel;
struggle.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squabble</ent><br/
<hw>Squab"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>To
disarrange, so that the letters or lines stand awry or are mixed and
need careful readjustment; -- said of type that has been set
up.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squabble</ent><br/
<hw>Squab"ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A scuffle; a wrangle; a
brawl.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squabbler</ent><br/
<hw>Squab"bler</hw> <pr>(-bl<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
squabbles; a contentious person; a brawler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squabby</ent><br/
<hw>Squab"by</hw> <pr>(-b<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Short and
thick; squabbish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squab-chick</ent><br/
<hw>Squab"-chick`</hw> <pr>(-ch<icr/k`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A young chicken before it is fully
fledged.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squacco</ent><br/
<hw>Squac"co</hw> <pr>(skw<acr/k"k<osl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Squaccos</plw> <pr>(-k<omac/z)</pr>.</plu>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A heron (<spn>Ardea comata</spn>) found in Asia,
Northern Africa, and Southern Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squad</ent><br/
<hw>Squad</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>escouade</ets>, fr. Sp. <ets>escuadra</ets>, or It.
<ets>squadra</ets>, (assumed) LL. <ets>exquadrare</ets> to square; L.
<ets>ex + quadra</ets> a square. See <er>Square</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A small party of men assembled for drill,
inspection, or other purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, any small party.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squad</ent><br/
<hw>Squad</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sloppy mud.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squadron</ent><br/
<hw>Squad"ron</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/d"r<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>escadron</ets>, formerly also <ets>esquadron</ets>, Sp.
<ets>escuadron</ets>, or It. <ets>squadrone</ets>. See
<er>Squad</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Primarily, a square; hence, a
square body of troops; a body of troops drawn up in a square.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those half-rounding quards<br/
Just met, and, closing, stood in <qex>squadron</qex> joined.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A body of cavalry comprising two
companies or troops, and averaging from one hundred and twenty to two
hundred men.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A detachment of vessels employed
on any particular service or station, under the command of the senior
officer; <as>as, the North Atlantic <ex>Squadron</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Flying squadron</b></col>, <cd>a squadron of observation
or practice, that cruises rapidly about from place to place.</cd></cs>
<rj><qau>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squadroned</ent><br/
<hw>Squad"roned</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/d"r<ucr/nd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Formed into squadrons, or squares.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squail</ent><br/
<hw>Squail</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To throw
sticks at cocks; to throw anything about awkwardly or irregularly.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Southey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squaimous</ent><br/
<hw>Squai"mous</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"m<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Squeamish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squali</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Squa"li</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"l<imac/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>squalus</ets> a kind of sea fish.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The suborder of elasmobranch fishes which
comprises the sharks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squalid</ent><br/
<hw>Squal"id</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/l"<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>squalidus</ets>, fr. <ets>squalere</ets> to be foul or
filthy.]</ety> <def>Dirty through neglect; foul; filthy; extremely
dirty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Uncombed his locks, and <qex>squalid</qex> his attire.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those <qex>squalid</qex> dens, which are the reproach of large
capitals.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squalidity</ent><br/
<hw>Squa*lid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(skw<adot/*l<icr/d"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>squaliditas</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality
or state of being squalid; foulness; filthiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squalidly</ent><br/
<hw>Squal"id*ly</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/l"<icr/d*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In a squalid manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squalidness</ent><br/
<hw>Squal"id*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
squalid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squall</ent><br/
<hw>Squall</hw> <pr>(skw<add/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sw.
<ets>sqval</ets> an impetuous running of water, <ets>sqval</ets>regn a
violent shower of rain, <ets>sqvala</ets> to stream, to gush.]</ety>
<def>A sudden and violent gust of wind often attended with rain or
snow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The gray skirts of a lifting <qex>squall</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Black squall</b></col>, <cd>a squall attended with dark,
heavy clouds.</cd> -- <col><b>Thick squall</b></col>, <cd>a black squall
accompanied by rain, hail, sleet, or snow.</cd> <au>Totten.</au> --
<col><b>White squall</b></col>, <cd>a squall which comes unexpectedly,
without being marked in its approach by the clouds.</cd>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squall</ent><br/
<hw>Squall</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Squalled</conjf> <pr>(skw<add/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Squalling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>skvala</ets>. Cf. <er>Squeal</er>.]</ety> <def>To cry out; to
scream or cry violently, as a woman frightened, or a child in anger or
distress; <as>as, the infant <ex>squalled</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squall</ent><br/
<hw>Squall</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A loud scream; a harsh
cry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There oft are heard the notes of infant woe, --<br/
The short, thick sob, loud scream, and shriller <qex>squall</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squaller</ent><br/
<hw>Squall"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
squalls; a screamer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squally</ent><br/
<hw>Squall"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Abounding with squalls; disturbed often with sudden and violent
gusts of wind; gusty; <as>as, <ex>squally</ex> weather</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>Interrupted by unproductive
spots; -- said of a field of turnips or grain.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>Not equally good throughout;
not uniform; uneven; faulty; -- said of cloth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squalodon</ent><br/
<hw>Squa"lo*don</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"l<osl/*d<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL. <ets>Squalus</ets> a genus of sharks + Gr. <grk>'odoy`s</grk>,
<grk>'odo`ntos</grk>, a tooth.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus
of fossil whales belonging to the Phocodontia; -- so called because
their teeth are serrated, like a shark's.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squalodont</ent><br/
<hw>Squa"lo*dont</hw> <pr>(-d<ocr/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to Squalodon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squaloid</ent><br/
<hw>Squa"loid</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"loid)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Squalus</ets> a genus of sharks (fr. L. <ets>squalus</ets> a kind
of sea fish) + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like or
pertaining to a shark or sharks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squalor</ent><br/
<hw>Squa"lor</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"l<ocir/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
fr. <ets>squalere</ets> to be foul or filthy.]</ety> <def>Squalidness;
foulness; filthiness; squalidity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The heterogeneous indigent multitude, everywhere wearing nearly
the same aspect of <qex>squalor</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To bring this sort of <qex>squalor</qex> among the upper
classes.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squam</ent><br/
<hw>Squam</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>Squam</ets>, shortened fr. <etsep>Annisquam</etsep>, name of a
village on the coast of Massachusetts.]</ety> <def>An oilskin hat or
southwester; -- a fisherman's name.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squama</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Squa"ma</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"m<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Squamae</plw> <pr>(-m<emac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
a scale.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A scale cast off from the skin; a
thin dry shred consisting of epithelium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Squa*ma"ceous</hw> <pr>(skw<adot/*m<amac/"sh<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Squamose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Squa*ma"ta</hw> <pr>(skw<adot/*m<amac/"t<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.
pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L. <ets>squamatus</ets> scaly.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of edentates having the body covered
with large, imbricated horny scales. It includes the
pangolins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamated</ent><br/
<ent>Squamate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Squa"mate</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"m<asl/t)</pr>,
<hw>Squa"ma*ted</hw> <pr>(-m<asl/*t<ecr/d)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>squamatus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Squamose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squam duck</ent><br/
<hw>Squam" duck`</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/m" d<ucr/k`)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The American eider duck.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squame</ent><br/
<hw>Squame</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>squama</ets> scale.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A scale.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Iron <xex>squames</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The scale, or exopodite, of an
antenna of a crustacean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamella</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Squa*mel"la</hw> <pr>(skw<adot/*m<ecr/l"l<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Squamellae</plw>
<pr>(-l<emac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., dim. fr. L. <ets>squama</ets> a
scale.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A diminutive scale or bractlet,
such as those found on the receptacle in many composite plants; a
palea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamellate</ent><br/
<hw>Squa*mel"late</hw> <pr>(-l<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished
or covered with little scales; squamulose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamiform</ent><br/
<hw>Squa"mi*form</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"m<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>squama</ets> a scale +
<ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the shape of a scale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamigerous</ent><br/
<hw>Squa*mig"er*ous</hw> <pr>(skw<adot/*m<icr/j"<etil/r*<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>squamiger</ets>; <ets>squama</ets> a scale +
<ets>gerere</ets> to bear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Bearing
scales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamipen</ent><br/
<hw>Squam"i*pen</hw> <pr>(skw<acr/m"<icr/*p<ecr/n <or/
skw<amac/"m<icr/-)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Squamipennes</plw> <pr>(-p<ecr/n"n<emac/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>squama</ets> a scale + <ets>penna</ets> a fin: cf. F.
<ets>squamipenne</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of a
group of fishes having the dorsal and anal fins partially covered with
scales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They are compressed and mostly, bright-colored tropical
fishes, belonging to <gen>Chaetodon</gen> and allied genera. Many of
them are called <stype>coral fishes</stype>, and <stype>angel
fishes</stype>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamoid</ent><br/
<hw>Squa"moid</hw> <pr>(skw<amac/"moid)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>squama</ets> scale + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling a
scale; also, covered with scales; scaly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamosal</ent><br/
<hw>Squa*mo"sal</hw> <pr>(skw<adot/*m<omac/"s<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Scalelike; squamous; <as>as, the
<ex>squamosal</ex> bone</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining
to the squamosal bone.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The squamous
part of the temporal bone, or a bone corresponding to it, in many of the
lower vertebrates. See <ex>Temporal bone</ex>, under
<er>Temporal</er>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamous</ent><br/
<ent>Squamose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Squa*mose"</hw> <pr>(skw<adot/*m<omac/s" <or/
skw<amac/"m<omac/s`)</pr>, <hw>Squa"mous</hw>
<pr>(skw<amac/"m<ucr/s)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>squamosus</ets>, fr. <ets>squama</ets> a scale: cf. F.
<ets>squameux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered with, or consisting
of, scales; resembling a scale; scaly; <as>as, the <ex>squamose</ex>
cones of the pine; <ex>squamous</ex> epithelial cells; the
<ex>squamous</ex> portion of the temporal bone, which is so called from
a fancied resemblance to a scale.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the squamosal
bone; squamosal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamozygomatic</ent><br/
<hw>Squa`mo*zyg`o*mat"ic</hw>
<pr>(skw<amac/`m<osl/*z<icr/g`<osl/*m<acr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to both the squamosal and
zygomatic bones; -- applied to a bone, or a center of ossification, in
some fetal skulls.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A squamozygomatic
bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Squam"u*la</hw> <pr>(skw<acr/m"<usl/*l<adot/ <or/
skw<amac/"m<usl/*l<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Squamulae</plw> <pr>(-l<emac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., dim. of
<ets>squama</ets> a scale.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of the
little hypogynous scales found in the flowers of grasses; a
lodicule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamulate</ent><br/
<hw>Squam"u*late</hw> <pr>(-l<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Squamulose</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamule</ent><br/
<hw>Squam"ule</hw> <pr>(-<usl/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Squamula</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squamulose</ent><br/
<hw>Squam"u*lose`</hw> <pr>(-l<omac/s`; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Having little scales; squamellate; squamulate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squander</ent><br/
<hw>Squan"der</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/n"d<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squandered</conjf>
<pr>(-d<etil/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squandering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Scot.
<ets>squatter</ets> to splash water about, to scatter, to squander,
Prov. E. <ets>swatter</ets>, Dan. <ets>sqvatte</ets>, Sw.
<ets>sqv<aum/tta</ets> to squirt, <ets>sqv<aum/ttra</ets> to squander,
Icel. <ets>skvetta</ets> to squirt out, to throw out water.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To scatter; to disperse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our <qex>squandered</qex> troops he rallies.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To spend lavishly or profusely; to spend prodigally
or wastefully; to use without economy or judgment; to dissipate; <as>as,
to <ex>squander</ex> an estate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The crime of <qex>squandering</qex> health is equal to the
folly.</q> <rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To spend; expend; waste; scatter;
dissipate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squander</ent><br/
<hw>Squan"der</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To spend lavishly;
to be wasteful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They often <qex>squandered</qex>, but they never gave.</q>
<rj><qau>Savage.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wander at random; to scatter.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wise man's folly is anatomized<br/
Even by <qex>squandering</qex> glances of the fool.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squander</ent><br/
<hw>Squan"der</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of squandering;
waste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squanderer</ent><br/
<hw>Squan"der*er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
squanders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squanderingly</ent><br/
<hw>Squan"der*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a squandering
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Square</ent><br/
<hw>Square</hw> <pr>(skw<acir/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>esquarre</ets>, <ets>esquierre</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/querre</ets>
a carpenter's square (cf. It. <ets>squadra</ets>), fr. (assumed) LL.
<ets>exquadrare</ets> to make square; L. <ets>ex + quadrus</ets> a
square, fr. <ets>quattuor</ets> four. See <er>Four</er>, and cf.
<er>Quadrant</er>, <er>Squad</er>, <er>Squire</er> a square.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The corner, or angle,
of a figure.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A parallelogram
having four equal sides and four right angles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, anything which is square, or nearly so</def>;
as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A square piece or fragment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He bolted his food down his capacious throat in <qex>squares</qex>
of three inches.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A pane of glass.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A certain number of lines, forming a portion of
a column, nearly square; -- used chiefly in reckoning the prices of
advertisements in newspapers.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Carp.)</fld>
<def>One hundred superficial feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An area of four sides, generally with houses on each
side; sometimes, a solid block of houses; also, an open place or area
for public use, as at the meeting or intersection of two or more
streets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The statue of Alexander VII. stands in the large <qex>square</qex>
of the town.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mech. & Joinery)</fld> <def>An instrument having at
least one right angle and two or more straight edges, used to lay out or
test square work. It is of several forms, as the T square, the
carpenter's square, the try-square., etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Hence, a pattern or rule.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Arith. & Alg.)</fld> <def>The product of a number
or quantity multiplied by itself; <as>thus, 64 is the <ex>square</ex> of
8, for <mathex>8 <times/ 8 = 64</mathex>; the <ex>square</ex> of
<mathex>a + b</mathex> is <mathex>a<exp>2</exp> + 2ab +
b<exp>2</exp></mathex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Exact proportion; justness of workmanship and
conduct; regularity; rule.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They of Galatia [were] much more out of <qex>square</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have not kept my <qex>square</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A body of troops formed in a
square, esp. one formed to resist a charge of cavalry; a squadron.</def>
<ldquo/The brave <xex>squares</xex> of war.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Fig.: The relation of harmony, or exact agreement;
equality; level.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We live not on the <qex>square</qex> with such as these.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>The position of planets
distant ninety degrees from each other; a quadrate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>The act of squaring, or quarreling; a
quarrel.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>The front of a woman's dress over the bosom,
usually worked or embroidered.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>fair and square</b></col> <cd>in a fair, straightforward,
and honest manner; justly; <as>as, he beat me <ex>fair and
square</ex></as>.</cd> -- <col><b>Geometrical square</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Quadrat</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Hollow
square</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a formation of troops in the
shape of a square, each side consisting of four or five ranks, and the
colors, officers, horses, etc., occupying the middle.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Least square</b></col>, <col><b>Magic
square</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Least</er>,
<er>Magic</er>, etc.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>On the square</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Upon the square</b></col></mcol>, <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>in an open, fair manner; honestly, or upon honor; justly.</cd>
<mark>[Obs or Colloq.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>at right angles.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>On the square with</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Upon the
square with</b></col></mcol>, <cd>upon equality with; even with.</cd>
<au>Nares.</au> -- <col><b>To be all squares</b></col>, <cd>to be all
settled.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <au>Dickens.</au> -- <col><b>To be
at square</b></col>, <cd>to be in a state of quarreling.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Nares.</au> -- <col><b>To break no
squares</b></col>, <cd>to give no offense; to make no difference.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>To break squares</b></col>, <cd>to depart
from an accustomed order.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>To see how
the squares go</b></col>, <cd>to see how the game proceeds; -- a phrase
taken from the game of chess, the chessboard being formed with
squares.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>L'Estrange.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Square</ent><br/
<hw>Square</hw> <pr>(skw<acir/r)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>Having four equal sides and four right angles;
<as>as, a <ex>square</ex> figure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Forming a right angle; <as>as, a <ex>square</ex>
corner</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having a shape broad for the height, with
rectilineal and angular rather than curving outlines; <as>as, a man of a
<ex>square</ex> frame</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Exactly suitable or correspondent; true;
just.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She's a most triumphant lady, if report be <qex>square</qex> to
her.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Rendering equal justice; exact; fair; honest;
<as>as, <ex>square</ex> dealing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Even; leaving no balance; <as>as, to make or leave
the accounts <ex>square</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Leaving nothing; hearty; vigorous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By Heaven, <qex>square</qex> eaters.<br/
More meat, I say.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>At right angles with the mast or
the keel, and parallel to the horizon; -- said of the yards of a
square-rigged vessel when they are so braced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <ex>Square</ex> is often used in self-explaining
compounds or combinations, as in <ex>square</ex>-built,
<ex>square</ex>-cornered, <ex>square</ex>-cut, <ex>square</ex>-nosed,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Square foot</b></col>, <cd>an area equal to that of a
square the sides of which are twelve inches; 144 square inches.</cd> --
<col><b>Square knot</b></col>, <cd>a knot in which the terminal and
standing parts are parallel to each other; a reef knot. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Knot</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Square
measure</b></col>, <cd>the measure of a superficies or surface which
depends on the length and breadth taken conjointly. The units of square
measure are squares whose sides are the linear measures; as,
<xex>square</xex> inches, <xex>square</xex> feet, <xex>square</xex>
meters, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Square number</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Square</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 6.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Square root of
a number</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Square root of a
quantity</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>that number or
quantity which, multiplied by itself, produces the given number or
quantity.</cd> -- <col><b>Square sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>a four-sided sail extended upon a yard suspended by the middle;
sometimes, the foresail of a schooner set upon a yard; also, a cutter's
or sloop's sail boomed out. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Sail</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Square stern</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a stern having a transom and joining the counter
timbers at an angle, as distinguished from a <xex>round stern</xex>,
which has no transom.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Three-square</b></col>,
<col><b>Five-square</b></col>, etc.</mcol>, <cd>having three, five,
etc., equal sides; as, a <xex>three-square</xex> file.</cd> --
<col><b>To get square with</b></col>, <cd>to get even with; to pay
off.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Square</ent><br/
<hw>Square</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Squared</conjf> <pr>(skw<acir/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Squaring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>escarrer</ets>, <ets>esquarrer</ets>. See <er>Square</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To form with four equal sides and
four right angles.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form with right angles and straight lines, or
flat surfaces; <as>as, to <ex>square</ex> masons' work</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To compare with, or reduce to, any given measure or
standard.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To adjust; to regulate; to mold; to shape; to fit;
<as>as, to <ex>square</ex> our actions by the opinions of
others</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Square</qex> my trial<br/
To my proportioned strength.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To make even, so as to leave no remainder or
difference; to balance; <as>as, to <ex>square</ex>
accounts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>To multiply by itself; <as>as, to
<ex>square</ex> a number or a quantity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>To hold a quartile position
respecting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The icy Goat and Crab that <qex>square</qex> the Scales.</q>
<rj><qau>Creech.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To place at right angles with the
keel; <as>as, to <ex>square</ex> the yards</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To square one's shoulders</b></col>, <cd>to raise the
shoulders so as to give them a square appearance, -- a movement
expressing contempt or dislike.</cd> <au>Sir W. Scott.</au> --
<col><b>To square the circle</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>to
determine the exact contents of a circle in square measure. The solution
of this famous problem is now generally admitted to be
impossible.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Square</ent><br/
<hw>Square</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To accord or agree
exactly; to be consistent with; to conform or agree; to suit; to
fit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No works shall find acceptance . . .<br/
That <qex>square</qex> not truly with the Scripture plan.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To go to opposite sides; to take an attitude of
offense or defense, or of defiance; to quarrel.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Are you such fools<br/
To <qex>square</qex> for this?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To take a boxing attitude; -- often with
<xex>up</xex>, sometimes with <xex>off</xex>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squarely</ent><br/
<hw>Square"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a square form or
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squareness</ent><br/
<hw>Square"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being square;
<as>as, an instrument to try the <ex>squareness</ex> of
work</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squarer</ent><br/
<hw>Squar"er</hw> <pr>(skw<acir/r"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, squares.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who squares, or quarrels; a hot-headed,
contentious fellow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1397 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Square-rigged</ent><br/
<hw>Square"-rigged`</hw> <pr>(skw<acir/r"r<icr/gd`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Having the sails extended upon yards suspended
horizontally by the middle, as distinguished from fore-and-aft sails;
thus, a ship and a brig are <xex>square-rigged</xex> vessels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Square-toed</ent><br/
<hw>Square"-toed`</hw> <pr>(-t<omac/d`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having
the toe square.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Obsolete as fardingales, ruffs, and <qex>square-toed</qex>
shoes.</q> <rj><qau>V. Knox.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Square-toes</ent><br/
<hw>Square"-toes`</hw> <pr>(-t<omac/z`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
precise person; -- used contemptuously or jocularly.</def>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squarish</ent><br/
<hw>Squar"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly square.</def>
<rj><au>Pennant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squarrose</ent><br/
<hw>Squar*rose"</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/r*r<ocr/s" <or/ skw<ocr/r"r<omac/s`;
277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>squarrosus</ets> (perhaps)
scurfy, scabby.]</ety> <def>Ragged or full of loose scales or projecting
parts; rough; jagged</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld>
<def>Consisting of scales widely divaricating; having scales, small
leaves, or other bodies, spreading widely from the axis on which they
are crowded; -- said of a calyx or stem.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Divided into shreds or jags, raised above the
plane of the leaf, and not parallel to it; -- said of a leaf.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having scales spreading every way,
or standing upright, or at right angles to the surface; -- said of a
shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Squarrose-slashed</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>doubly
slashed, with the smaller divisions at right angles to the others, as a
leaf.</cd> <rj><au>Lindley.</au></rj></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squarroso-dentate</ent><br/
<hw>Squar*ro`so-den"tate</hw>
<pr>(skw<ocr/r*r<omac/`s<osl/-d<ecr/n"t<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the teeth bent out of the plane of the
lamina; -- said of a leaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squarrous</ent><br/
<hw>Squar"rous</hw> <pr>(skw<acr/r"r<ucr/s <or/ skw<ocr/r"-)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Squarrose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squarrulose</ent><br/
<hw>Squar"ru*lose`</hw> <pr>(-r<usdot/*l<omac/s`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Dim. of <ets>squarrose</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Somewhat squarrose; slightly squarrose.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squash</ent><br/
<hw>Squash</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/sh)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Musquash</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An American animal
allied to the weasel.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squash</ent><br/
<hw>Squash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Massachusetts Indian
<ets>asq</ets>, pl. <ets>asquash</ets>, raw, green, immature, applied to
fruit and vegetables which were used when green, or without cooking;
<ets>askutasquash</ets> vine apple.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
plant and its fruit of the genus <gen>Cucurbita</gen>, or gourd
kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The species are much confused. The long-neck squash is
called <spn>Cucurbita verrucosa</spn>, the Barbary or China squash,
<spn>Cucurbita moschata</spn>, and the great winter squash,
<spn>Cucurbita maxima</spn>, but the distinctions are not
clear.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Squash beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small
American beetle (<spn>Diabrotica vittata</spn>, syn. <spn>Galeruca
vittata</spn>) which is often abundant and very injurious to the leaves
of squash, cucumber, etc. It is striped with yellow and black. The name
is applied also to other allied species.</cd> -- <col><b>Squash
bug</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large black American hemipterous
insect (<spn>Coreus tristis</spn> syn. <spn>Anasa tristis</spn>)
injurious to squash vines.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squash</ent><br/
<hw>Squash</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Squashed</conjf> <pr>(skw<ocr/sht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Squashing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>squachen</ets>, OF. <ets>escachier</ets>, <ets>esquachier</ets>, to
squash, to crush, F. <ets><eacute/cacher</ets>, perhaps from (assumed)
LL. <ets>excoacticare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>ex</ets> + <ets>coactare</ets>
to constrain, from <ets>cogere</ets>, <ets>coactum</ets>, to compel. Cf.
<er>Cogent</er>, <er>Squat</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To beat or
press into pulp or a flat mass; to crush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squash</ent><br/
<hw>Squash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something soft and
easily crushed; especially, an unripe pod of pease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not yet old enough for a man, nor young enough for a boy; as a
<qex>squash</qex> is before 't is a peascod.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, something unripe or soft; -- used in
contempt.</def> <ldquo/This <xex>squash</xex>, this gentleman.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sudden fall of a heavy, soft body; also, a shock
of soft bodies.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My fall was stopped by a terrible <qex>squash</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A game much like rackets, played in a walled court
with soft rubber balls and bats like tennis rackets; -- called also
<altname>squash rackets</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squasher</ent><br/
<hw>Squash"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or
that which, squashes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squashiness</ent><br/
<hw>Squash"i*ness</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being squashy, or soft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squashy</ent><br/
<hw>Squash"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Easily squashed;
soft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squat</ent><br/
<hw>Squat</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The angel fish (<spn>Squatina angelus</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squat</ent><br/
<hw>Squat</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Squatted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squatting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>squatten</ets> to
crush, OF. <ets>esquater</ets>, <ets>esquatir</ets> (cf. It.
<ets>quatto</ets> squat, cowering), perhaps fr. L. <ets>ex</ets> +
<ets>coactus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>cogere</ets> to drive or urge
together. See <er>Cogent</er>, <er>Squash</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To sit down upon the hams or heels; <as>as, the savages
<ex>squatted</ex> near the fire</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sit close to the ground; to cower; to stoop, or
lie close, to escape observation, as a partridge or rabbit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To settle on another's land without title; also, to
settle on common or public lands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squat</ent><br/
<hw>Squat</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To bruise or make flat by a
fall.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squat</ent><br/
<hw>Squat</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Sitting on the hams or
heels; sitting close to the ground; cowering; crouching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him there they found,<br/
<qex>Squat</qex> like a toad, close at the ear of Eve.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Short and thick, like the figure of an animal
squatting.</def> <ldquo/The round, <xex>squat</xex> turret.<rdquo/
<rj><au>R. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The head [of the squill insect] is broad and <qex>squat</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Grew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squat</ent><br/
<hw>Squat</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The posture of one that
sits on his heels or hams, or close to the ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sudden or crushing fall.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A small vein of
ore.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A mineral consisting of tin ore and
spar.</def> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au> <au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Squat snipe</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
jacksnipe; -- called also <altname>squatter</altname>.</cd>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squaterole</ent><br/
<hw>Squat"er*ole</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r*<omac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The black-bellied plover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squatter</ent><br/
<hw>Squat"ter</hw> <pr>(-t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who squats;</def> <specif>specifically,</specif> <def>one who
settles unlawfully upon land without a title. In the United States and
Australia the term is sometimes applied also to a person who settles
lawfully upon government land under legal permission and restrictions,
before acquiring title.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In such a tract, <qex>squatters</qex> and trespassers were
tolerated to an extent now unknown.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <cref>Squat snipe</cref>,
under <er>Squat</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Squatter sovereignty</b></col>, <cd>the right claimed by
the squatters, or actual residents, of a Territory of the United States
to make their own laws.</cd> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark>
<au>Bartlett.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squatty</ent><br/
<hw>Squat"ty</hw> <pr>(skw<ocr/t"t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Squat;
dumpy.</def> <rj><au>J. Burroughs.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squaw</ent><br/
<hw>Squaw</hw> <pr>(skw<add/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Massachusetts
Indian <ets>squa</ets>, <ets>eshqua</ets>; Narragansett
<ets>squ<acir/ws</ets>; Delaware <ets>ochqueu</ets>, and
<ets>khqueu</ets>; used also in compound words (as the names of animals)
in the sense of <ets>female</ets>.]</ety> <def>A female; a woman,
especially a married woman; a wife; -- in the language of Indian tribes
of the Algonquin family, correlative of <inv>sannup</inv>.</def>
<mark>[Considered offensive by some American indians.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Old squaw</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Old</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squawberry</ent><br/
<hw>Squaw"ber`ry</hw> <pr>(-b<ecr/r`r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A local name for the partridge berry; also, for
the deerberry.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squawk</ent><br/
<hw>Squawk</hw> <pr>(skw<add/k)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squawked</conjf>
<pr>(skw<add/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squawking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Squeak</er>.]</ety>
<def>To utter a shrill, abrupt scream; to squeak harshly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Squawking thrush</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
missel turush; -- so called from its note when alarmed.</cd>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squawk</ent><br/
<hw>Squawk</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act of squawking; a
harsh squeak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The American night heron. See
under <er>Night</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Squawk duck</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
bimaculate duck (<spn>Anas glocitans</spn>). It has patches of reddish
brown behind, and in front of, each eye.</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squawl</ent><br/
<hw>Squawl</hw> <pr>(skw<add/l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>See
<er>Squall</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squaw man</ent><br/
<hw>Squaw man</hw>. <def>A white man who has married an Indian squaw;
sometimes, one who has gained tribal rights by such a marriage; -- often
a term of contempt.</def> <mark>[Western U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squawroot</ent><br/
<hw>Squaw"root`</hw> <pr>(skw<add/"r<oomac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A scaly parasitic plant (<spn>Conopholis
Americana</spn>) found in oak woods in the United States; -- called also
<altname>cancer root</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squaw vine</ent><br/
<hw>Squaw vine</hw>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The partridge berry
(<spn>Mitchella repens</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squawweed</ent><br/
<hw>Squaw"weed`</hw> <pr>(skw<add/"w<emac/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The golden ragwort. See under
<er>Ragwort</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeak</ent><br/
<hw>Squeak</hw> <pr>(skw<emac/k)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squeaked</conjf>
<pr>(skw<emac/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squeaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably of imitative origin;
cf. Sw. <ets>sqv<aum/ka</ets> to croak, Icel. <ets>skvakka</ets> to give
a sound as of water shaken in a bottle.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter
a sharp, shrill cry, usually of short duration; to cry with an acute
tone, as an animal; or, to make a sharp, disagreeable noise, as a pipe
or quill, a wagon wheel, a door; to creak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who can endure to hear one of the rough old Romans
<qex>squeaking</qex> through the mouth of an eunuch?</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Zoilus calls the companions of Ulysses the
<ldquo/<qex>squeaking</qex> pigs<rdquo/ of Homer.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To break silence or secrecy for fear of pain or
punishment; to speak; to confess.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> squeal.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If he be obstinate, put a civil question to him upon the rack, and
he <qex>squeaks</qex>, I warrant him.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeak</ent><br/
<hw>Squeak</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sharp, shrill, disagreeable sound
suddenly uttered, either of the human voice or of any animal or
instrument, such as is made by carriage wheels when dry, by the soles of
leather shoes, or by a pipe or reed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeaker</ent><br/
<hw>Squeak"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who, or that which, squeaks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Australian gray crow shrike
(<spn>Strepera anaphonesis</spn>); -- so called from its note.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A contest won by a small margin.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A narrow escape from harm.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeakingly</ent><br/
<hw>Squeak"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a squeaking
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeal</ent><br/
<hw>Squeal</hw> <pr>(skw<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squealed</conjf>
<pr>(skw<emac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squealing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Of Scand. origin; cf. Sw.
<ets>sqv<aum/la</ets>, Norw. <ets>skvella</ets>. Cf. <er>Squeak</er>,
<er>Squall</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cry with a sharp, shrill,
prolonged sound, as certain animals do, indicating want, displeasure, or
pain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To turn informer; to betray a secret.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeal</ent><br/
<hw>Squeal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A shrill, sharp, somewhat prolonged
cry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squealer</ent><br/
<hw>Squeal"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who, or that which, squeals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The European
swift.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The harlequin duck.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>The American golden plover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeamish</ent><br/
<hw>Squeam"ish</hw> <pr>(skw<emac/m"<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>squaimous</ets>, <ets>sweymous</ets>, probably from OE.
<ets>sweem</ets>, <ets>swem</ets>, dizziness, a swimming in the head;
cf. Icel. <ets>sveimr</ets> a bustle, a stir, Norw. <ets>sveim</ets> a
hovering about, a sickness that comes upon one, Icel. <ets>svimi</ets> a
giddiness, AS. <ets>sw<imac/ma</ets>. The word has been perhaps confused
with <ets>qualmish</ets>. Cf. <er>Swim</er> to be dizzy.]</ety>
<def>Having a stomach that is easily turned or nauseated; hence, nice to
excess in taste; fastidious; easily disgusted; apt to be offended at
trifling improprieties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Quoth he, that honor's very <qex>squeamish</qex><br/
That takes a basting for a blemish.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His muse is rustic, and perhaps too plain<br/
The men of <qex>squeamish</qex> taste to entertain.</q>
<rj><qau>Southern.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So ye grow <qex>squeamish</qex>, Gods, and sniff at heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Fastidious; dainty; overnice; scrupulous. See
<er>Fastidious</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Squeam"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Squeam"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeamous</ent><br/
<hw>Squeam"ous</hw> <pr>(-<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Squeamish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeasiness</ent><br/
<hw>Squea"si*ness</hw> <pr>(skw<emac/"z<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Queasiness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeasy</ent><br/
<hw>Squea"sy</hw> <pr>(-z<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Queasy; nice;
squeamish; fastidious; scrupulous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Earle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeegee</ent><br/
<hw>Squee"gee</hw> <pr>(skw<emac/"j<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Formerly, a small swab for drying a vessel's deck; now, a kind of
scraper having a blade or edge of rubber or of leather, -- used for
removing superfluous, water or other liquids, as from a vessel's deck
after washing, from window panes, photographer's plates, etc.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>squilgee</asp>,
<asp>squillagee</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeegee</ent><br/
<hw>Squee"gee</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Squeegeed</conjf> <pr>(skw<emac/"j<emac/d;
skw<emac/*j<emac/d")</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squeegeeing</conjf> <pr>(skw<emac/"j<emac/*<icr/ng;
skw<emac/*j<emac/"<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To smooth, clean, press,
or treat with a squeegee; to squilgee.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeegee roller</ent><br/
<hw>Squeegee roller</hw>. <def>A small India-rubber roller with a
handle, used esp. in printing and photography as a squeegee.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeeze</ent><br/
<hw>Squeeze</hw> <pr>(skw<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squeezed</conjf>
<pr>(skw<emac/zd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squeezing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>queisen</ets>, AS.
<ets>cw<emac/san</ets>, <ets>cw<ymac/san</ets>, <ets>cw<imac/san</ets>,
of uncertain origin. The <ets>s-</ets> was probably prefixed through the
influence of <ets>squash</ets>, v.t.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To press
between two bodies; to press together closely; to compress; often, to
compress so as to expel juice, moisture, etc.; <as>as, to
<ex>squeeze</ex> an orange with the fingers; to <ex>squeeze</ex> the
hand in friendship.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To oppress with hardships, burdens, or taxes;
to harass; to crush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In a civil war, people must expect to be crushed and
<qex>squeezed</qex> toward the burden.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To force, or cause to pass, by compression; often
with <xex>out</xex>, <xex>through</xex>, etc.; <as>as, to
<ex>squeeze</ex> water through felt</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To compress; hug; pinch; gripe; crowd.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeeze</ent><br/
<hw>Squeeze</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To press; to urge one's way, or
to pass, by pressing; to crowd; -- often with <xex>through</xex>,
<xex>into</xex>, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>squeeze</ex> hard to get through a
crowd</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeeze</ent><br/
<hw>Squeeze</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who
squeezes; compression between bodies; pressure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A facsimile impression taken in some soft substance,
as pulp, from an inscription on stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The gradual closing of workings
by the weight of the overlying strata.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Pressure or constraint used to force the making of a
gift, concession, or the like; exaction; extortion; <as>as, to put the
<ex>squeeze</ex> on someone</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></p>

<p><q>One of the many <ldquo/<qex>squeezes</qex><rdquo/ imposed by the
mandarins.</q> <rj><qau>A. R. Colquhoun.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeezer</ent><br/
<hw>Squeez"er</hw> <pr>(skw<emac/z"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, squeezes; <as>as, a lemon
<ex>squeezer</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Forging)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A machine like a
large pair of pliers, for shingling, or squeezing, the balls of metal
when puddled; -- used only in the plural.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
machine of several forms for the same purpose; -- used in the
singular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squeezing</ent><br/
<hw>Squeez"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of pressing;
compression; oppression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>That which is forced out by
pressure; dregs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Squeeze</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squelch</ent><br/
<hw>Squelch</hw> <pr>(skw<ecr/lch)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squelched</conjf>
<pr>(skw<ecr/lcht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squelching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Prov. E.
<ets>quelch</ets> a blow, and <ets>quell</ets> to crush, to kill.]</ety>
<def>To quell; to crush; to silence or put down.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Oh 't was your luck and mine to be <qex>squelched</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If you deceive us you will be <qex>squelched</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squelch</ent><br/
<hw>Squelch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A heavy fall, as of
something flat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A crushing reply; <as>as,
the perfect <ex>squelch</ex> for a conceited remark</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squelch</ent><br/
<hw>Squelch</hw> <pr>(skw<ecr/lch)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Perh.
imitative. Cf. <er>Squelch</er>.]</ety> <def>To make a sound like that
made by the feet of one walking in mud or slush; to make a kind of
swashing sound; to squish; also, to move with such a sound.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><q>He turned and strode to the fire, his boots <qex>squelching</qex>
as he walked.</q> <rj><qau>P. L. Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><q>A crazy old collier <qex>squelching</qex> along under squared
yards.</q> <rj><qau>W. C. Russell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>squeteague</ent><br/
<hw>sque*teague"</hw> <pr>(skw<esl/*t<emac/g")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[From the North American Indian name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An American sciaenoid fish (<spn>Cynoscion regalis</spn>), abundant
on the Atlantic coast of the United States, and much valued as a food
fish. It is of a bright silvery color, with iridescent reflections.
Called also <altname>weakfish</altname>, <altname>squitee</altname>,
<altname>chickwit</altname>, and <altname>sea trout</altname>. The
spotted squeteague (<spn>Cynoscion nebulosus</spn>) of the Southern
United States is a similar fish, but the back and upper fins are spotted
with black. It is called also <altname>spotted weakfish</altname> and
<altname>squit</altname>, and, locally, <altname>sea trout</altname>,
and <altname>sea salmon</altname>. See also under
<er>squitee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squib</ent><br/
<hw>Squib</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/b)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>squippen</ets>, <ets>swippen</ets>, to move swiftly, Icel.
<ets>svipa</ets> to swoop, flash, dart, whip; akin to AS.
<ets>swipian</ets> to whip, and E. <ets>swift</ets>, a. See
<er>Swift</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A little pipe, or
hollow cylinder of paper, filled with powder or combustible matter, to
be thrown into the air while burning, so as to burst there with a
crack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lampoons, like <qex>squibs</qex>, may make a present blaze.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The making and selling of fireworks, and <qex>squibs</qex> . . .
is punishable.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A kind of slow match or safety
fuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sarcastic speech or publication; a petty lampoon;
a brief, witty essay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who copied his <qex>squibs</qex>, and reechoed his jokes.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A writer of lampoons.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>squibs</qex> are those who in the common phrase of the
world are called libelers, lampooners, and pamphleteers.</q>
<rj><qau>Tatler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A paltry fellow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squib</ent><br/
<hw>Squib</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Squibbed</conjf> <pr>(skw<icr/bd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Squibbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To throw squibs; to
utter sarcastic or severe reflections; to contend in petty dispute;
<as>as, to <ex>squib</ex> a little in debate</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squid</ent><br/
<hw>Squid</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Squirt</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of
numerous species of ten-armed cephalopods having a long, tapered body,
and a caudal fin on each side; especially, any species of
<gen>Loligo</gen>, <gen>Ommastrephes</gen>, and related genera. See
<er>Calamary</er>, <er>Decacerata</er>, <er>Dibranchiata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Some of these squids are very abundant on the Atlantic
coast of North America, and are used in large quantities for bait,
especially in the cod fishery. The most abundant of the American squids
are the northern squid (<spn>Ommastrephes illecebrosus</spn>), ranging
from Southern New England to Newfoundland, and the southern squid
(<spn>Loligo Pealii</spn>), ranging from Virginia to
Massachusetts.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fishhook with a piece of bright lead, bone, or
other substance, fastened on its shank to imitate a squid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Flying squid</b></col>, <col><b>Giant
squid</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Flying</er>, and <er>Giant</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Squid
hound</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the striped bass.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squier</ent><br/
<hw>Squier</hw> <pr>(skw<imac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A square. See
1st <er>Squire</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not the worst of the three but jumps twelve foot and a half by the
<qex>squier</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squiery</ent><br/
<ent>Squierie</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Squi"er*ie</hw>, <hw>Squi"er*y</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(skw<imac/"<etil/r*<icr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>escuierie</ets>. See <er>Esquire</er>.]</ety> <def>A company of
squires; the whole body of squires.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word is found in Tyrwhitt's Chaucer, but is not in
the modern editions.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squiffy</ent><br/
<hw>Squif"fy</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/f"f<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Somewhat intoxicated; tipsy.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>Kipling.</au></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squiggle</ent><br/
<hw>Squig"gle</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/g"g'l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
Prov. E. <ets>swiggle</ets> to drink greedily, to shake liquor in a
close vessel, and E. <ets>swig</ets>.]</ety> <def>To shake and wash a
fluid about in the mouth with the lips closed.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Forby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squiggle</ent><br/
<hw>Squig"gle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Squirm</er>,
<er>Wiggle</er>.]</ety> <def>To move about like an eel; to squirm.</def>
<mark>[Low, U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1398 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Squilgee</ent><br/
<hw>Squil"gee</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/l"j<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Formerly, a small swab for drying a vessel's deck; now, a kind of
scraper having a blade or edge of rubber or of leather, -- used for
removing superfluous water or other liquids, as from a vessel's deck
after washing, from window panes, photographer's plates, etc.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>squillgee</asp>, <asp>squillagee</asp>,
<asp>squeegee.</asp>]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squilgee</ent><br/
<hw>Squil"gee</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/l"j<esl/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squilgeed</conjf>
<pr>(skw<icr/l"j<emac/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squilgeeing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To swab, press, or treat with
a squilgee; <as>as, to <ex>squilgee</ex> a vessel's deck</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squill</ent><br/
<hw>Squill</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>squille</ets> (also <ets>scille</ets> a squill, in sense 1), L.
<ets>squilla</ets>, <ets>scilla</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A European bulbous liliaceous plant
(<spn>Urginea maritima</spn>, formerly <spn>Scilla maritima</spn>), of
acrid, expectorant, diuretic, and emetic properties, used in medicine.
Called also <altname>sea onion</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any
bulbous plant of the genus <gen>Scilla</gen>; <as>as, the bluebell
<ex>squill</ex> (<spn>Scilla mutans</spn>)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A squilla.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A mantis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squilla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Squil"la</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/l"l<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> E. <plw>Squillas</plw> <pr>(-l<adot/z)</pr>, L.
<plw>Squillae</plw> <pr>(-l<emac/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a sea onion,
also, a prawn or shrimp. See <er>Squill</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous stomapod crustaceans of the genus
<gen>Squilla</gen> and allied genera. They make burrows in mud or
beneath stones on the seashore. Called also <altname>mantis
shrimp</altname>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Stomapoda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squillitic</ent><br/
<hw>Squill*it"ic</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/l*<icr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Of or pertaining to squills.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Squillitic</xex> vinegar.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squinancy</ent><br/
<ent>Squinance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Squin"ance</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/n"<ait/ns)</pr>,
<hw>Squin"an*cy</hw> <pr>(-<ait/n*s<ycr/)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>esquinancie</ets>, OF. <ets>squinance</ets>,
<ets>esquinance</ets>. See <er>Quinsy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The quinsy. See <er>Quinsy</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A European perennial herb
(<spn>Asperula cynanchica</spn>) with narrowly linear whorled leaves; --
formerly thought to cure the quinsy. Also called
<altname>quincewort</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Squinancy berries</b></col>, <cd>black currants; -- so
called because used to cure the quinsy.</cd> <au>Dr.
Prior.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squinch</ent><br/
<hw>Squinch</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/nch)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted
fr. <ets>sconce</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A small arch
thrown across the corner of a square room to support a superimposed
mass, as where an octagonal spire or drum rests upon a square tower; --
called also <altname>sconce</altname>, and
<altname>sconcheon</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squinsy</ent><br/
<hw>Squin"sy</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/n"z<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See <er>Quinsy</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squint</ent><br/
<hw>Squint</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>schuinte</ets> a slope, <ets>schuin</ets>, <ets>schuinsch</ets>,
sloping, oblique, <ets>schuins</ets> slopingly. Cf. <er>Askant</er>,
<er>Askance</er>, <er>Asquint</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Looking
obliquely.</def> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<def>not having the optic axes coincident; -- said of the eyes. See
<er>Squint</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>Looking askance.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Squint</xex> suspicion.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squint</ent><br/
<hw>Squint</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Squinted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squinting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To see or look
obliquely, asquint, or awry, or with a furtive glance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some can <qex>squint</qex> when they will.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>To have the axes of the eyes not
coincident; to be cross-eyed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To deviate from a true line; to run
obliquely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To have an indirect bearing, reference, or
implication; to have an allusion to, or inclination towards,
something.</def></p>

<p><q>Yet if the following sentence means anything, it is a
<qex>squinting</qex> toward hypnotism.</q> <rj><qau>The
Forum.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To look with the eyes partly closed.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squint</ent><br/
<hw>Squint</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To turn to an oblique
position; to direct obliquely; <as>as, to <ex>squint</ex> an
eye</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to look with noncoincident optic
axes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He . . . <qex>squints</qex> the eye, and makes the harelid.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squint</ent><br/
<hw>Squint</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or habit of
squinting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A want of coincidence of the axes
of the eyes; strabismus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Hagioscope</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squinter</ent><br/
<hw>Squint"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
squints.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squint-eye</ent><br/
<hw>Squint"-eye`</hw> <pr>(-<imac/`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An eye
that squints.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squint-eyed</ent><br/
<hw>Squint"-eyed`</hw> <pr>(-<imac/d`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having eyes that squint; having eyes with axes not coincident;
cross-eyed; also called <altname>squinty-eyed</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Looking obliquely, or asquint; malignant; <as>as,
<ex>squint-eyed</ex> praise; <ex>squint-eyed</ex>
jealousy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squintifego</ent><br/
<hw>Squint`i*fe"go</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*f<emac/"g<osl/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Squinting.</def> <mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squinting</ent><br/
<hw>Squint"ing</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/nt"<icr/ng)</pr>, <def><pos>a. &
n.</pos> from <er>Squint</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Squint"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squiny</ent><br/
<hw>Squin"y</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/n"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To
squint.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squinzey</ent><br/
<hw>Squin"zey</hw> <pr>(-z<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Quinsy</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squir</ent><br/
<hw>Squir</hw> <pr>(skw<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To throw
with a jerk; to throw edge foremost.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>squirr</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squiralty</ent><br/
<hw>Squir"al*ty</hw> <pr>(skw<imac/r"<acr/l*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Same as <er>Squirarchy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That such weight and influence be put thereby into the hands of
the <qex>squiralty</qex> of my kingdom.</q>
<rj><qau>Sterne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirarch</ent><br/
<hw>Squir"arch</hw> <pr>(skw<imac/r"<aum/rk)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Squire</ets> + <ets>-arch</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who belongs
to the squirarchy.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Squir"arch*al</wf>
<pr>(-<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirarchy</ent><br/
<hw>Squir"arch*y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Squire</ets> + <ets>-archy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The gentlemen,
or gentry, of a country, collectively.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>squirearchy</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squire</ent><br/
<hw>Squire</hw> <pr>(skw<imac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>esquierre</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/querre</ets>. See <er>Square</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A square; a measure; a rule.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/With golden <xex>squire</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squire</ent><br/
<hw>Squire</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Aphetic form of
<ets>esquire</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A shield-bearer or
armor-bearer who attended a knight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A title of dignity next in degree below
<xex>knight</xex>, and above <xex>gentleman</xex>. See
<er>Esquire</er>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <ldquo/His privy knights and
<xex>squires</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A male attendant on a great personage; also
(<mark>Colloq.</mark>), a devoted attendant or follower of a lady; a
beau.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A title of office and courtesy. See under
<er>Esquire</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squire</ent><br/
<hw>Squire</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>squired</conjf> <pr>(skw<imac/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>squiring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To attend
as a squire.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To attend as a beau, or gallant, for aid and
protection; <as>as, to <ex>squire</ex> a lady</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squireen</ent><br/
<hw>Squir*een"</hw> <pr>(skw<imac/r*<emac/n")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One who is half squire and half farmer; -- used humorously.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirehood</ent><br/
<hw>Squire"hood</hw> <pr>(skw<imac/r"h<oocr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The rank or state of a squire; squireship.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>squireling</ent><br/
<hw>squire"ling</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A petty
squire.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirely</ent><br/
<hw>Squire"ly</hw>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos> <def>Becoming a squire; like a
squire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>squireship</ent><br/
<hw>squire"ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Squirehood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirm</ent><br/
<hw>Squirm</hw> <pr>(skw<etil/rm)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squirmed</conjf>
<pr>(skw<etil/rmd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Squirming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. <er>Swarm</er> to climb a
tree.]</ety> <def>To twist about briskly with contortions like an eel or
a worm; to wriggle; to writhe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirr</ent><br/
<hw>Squirr</hw> <pr>(skw<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Squir</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirrel</ent><br/
<hw>Squir"rel</hw> <pr>(skw<etil/r"r<ecr/l <or/ skw<icr/r"-; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>squirel</ets>, OF. <ets>esquirel</ets>,
<ets>escurel</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/cureuil</ets>, LL.
<ets>squirelus</ets>, <ets>squirolus</ets>, <ets>scuriolus</ets>, dim.
of L. <ets>sciurus</ets>, Gr. <grk>si`oyros</grk>; <grk>skia`</grk>
shade + <grk>o'yra`</grk> tail. Cf. <er>Shine</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous
species of small rodents belonging to the genus <gen>Sciurus</gen> and
several allied genera of the family <fam>Sciuridae</fam>. Squirrels
generally have a bushy tail, large erect ears, and strong hind legs.
They are commonly arboreal in their habits, but many species live in
burrows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Among the common North American squirrels are the gray
squirrel (<spn>Sciurus Carolinensis</spn>) and its black variety; the
fox, or cat, squirrel (<spn>Sciurus cinereus</spn>, or <spn>Sciurus
niger</spn>) which is a large species, and variable in color, the
southern variety being frequently black, while the northern and western
varieties are usually gray or rusty brown; the red squirrel (see
<er>Chickaree</er>); the striped, or chipping, squirrel (see
<er>Chipmunk</er>); and the California gray squirrel (<spn>Sciurus
fossor</spn>). Several other species inhabit Mexico and Central America.
The common European species (<spn>Sciurus vulgaris</spn>) has a long
tuft of hair on each ear. The so-called Australian <xex>squirrels</xex>
are marsupials. See <er>Petaurist</er>, and
<er>Phalanger</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the small rollers of a carding machine which
work with the large cylinder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Barking squirrel</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
prairie dog.</cd> -- <col><b>Federation squirrel</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the striped gopher. See <er>Gopher</er>, 2.</cd>
-- <col><b>Flying squirrel</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>. <cd>See
<er>Flying squirrel</er>, in the Vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Java
squirrel</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>. <cd>See <er>Jelerang</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Squirrel corn</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a North
American herb (<spn>Dicentra Canadensis</spn>) bearing little yellow
tubers.</cd> -- <col><b>Squirrel cup</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the blossom of the <spn>Hepatica triloba</spn>, a low perennial herb
with cup-shaped flowers varying from purplish blue to pink or even
white. It is one of the earliest flowers of spring.</cd> --
<col><b>Squirrel fish</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A
sea bass (<spn>Serranus fascicularis</spn>) of the Southern United
States.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The sailor's choice (<spn>Diplodus
rhomboides</spn>).</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>The redmouth, or grunt.</cd>
<sd>(d)</sd> <cd>A market fish of Bermuda (<spn>Holocentrum
Ascensione</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Squirrel grass</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a pestiferous grass (<spn>Hordeum murinum</spn>)
related to barley. In California the stiffly awned spikelets work into
the wool of sheep, and into the throat, flesh, and eyes of animals,
sometimes even producing death.</cd> -- <col><b>Squirrel hake</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a common American hake (<spn>Phycis
tenuis</spn>); -- called also <altname>white hake</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Squirrel hawk</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any rough-legged
hawk; especially, the California species <spn>Archibuteo
ferrugineus</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Squirrel monkey</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any one of several species of small,
soft-haired South American monkeys of the genus <gen>Callithrix</gen>.
They are noted for their graceful form and agility. See
<er>Teetee</er>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A marmoset.</cd> --
<col><b>Squirrel petaurus</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a flying
phalanger of Australia. See <er>Phalanger</er>, <er>Petaurist</er>, and
<cref>Flying phalanger</cref> under <er>Flying</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Squirrel shrew</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
several species of East Indian and Asiatic insectivores of the genus
<gen>Tupaia</gen>. They are allied to the shrews, but have a bushy tail,
like that of a squirrel.</cd> -- <col><b>Squirrel-tail grass</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a grass (<spn>Hordeum jubatum</spn>) found in
salt marshes and along the Great Lakes, having a dense spike beset with
long awns.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirt</ent><br/
<hw>Squirt</hw> <pr>(skw<etil/rt)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Squirted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Squirting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. LG.
<ets>swirtjen</ets> to squirt, OSw. <ets>sqv<aum/tta</ets>, E.
<ets>squander</ets>.]</ety> <def>To drive or eject in a stream out of a
narrow pipe or orifice; <as>as, to <ex>squirt</ex> water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The hard-featured miscreant coolly rolled his tobacco in his
cheek, and <qex>squirted</qex> the juice into the fire grate.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Squirting cucumber</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Ecballium</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirt</ent><br/
<hw>Squirt</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be thrown out, or
ejected, in a rapid stream, from a narrow orifice; -- said of
liquids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to throw out or utter words rapidly; to
prate.</def> <mark>[Low]</mark> <rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirt</ent><br/
<hw>Squirt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An instrument out of
which a liquid is ejected in a small stream with force.</def>
<rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small, quick stream; a jet.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Hydrodynamics)</fld> <def>The whole system of flow
in the vicinity of a source.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>a youngster.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>a short, overly assertive, or impudent person,
especially when young; -- used in contempt.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squirter</ent><br/
<hw>Squirt"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or
that which, squirts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squiry</ent><br/
<hw>Squir"y</hw> <pr>(skw<imac/r"<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Squiery</er>.]</ety> <def>The body of squires, collectively
considered; squirarchy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The flower of chivalry and <qex>squiry</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ld.
Berners.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>squish</ent><br/
<hw>squish</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Perh.
imitative. Cf. <er>Squelch</er>.]</ety> <def>To make the sound of
squirting water, like that made by the feet of one walking in mud or
slush; to make a kind of gushing, swashing, or splashing sound; to move
with such a sound.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>squish</ent><br/
<hw>squish</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sound like
that made by the feet of one walking in mud or slush; a gushing,
swashing, or splashing sound; a squishing sound.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squitch grass</ent><br/
<hw>Squitch" grass`</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/ch" gr<adot/s`)</pr>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Quitch grass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Squitee</ent><br/
<hw>Squi*tee"</hw> <pr>(skw<icr/*t<emac/")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[From the N. American Indian name.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The squeteague; -- called also <altname>squit</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stab</ent><br/
<hw>Stab</hw> <pr>(st<acr/b)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stabbed</conjf> <pr>(st<acr/bd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Stabbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OD.
<ets>staven</ets> to fix, fasten, fr. <ets>stave</ets>,
<ets>staff</ets>, a staff, rod; akin to G. <ets>stab</ets> a staff,
stick, E. <ets>staff</ets>; also Gael. <ets>stob</ets> to stab, as n., a
stake, a stub. Cf. <er>Staff</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pierce
with a pointed weapon; to wound or kill by the thrust of a pointed
instrument; <as>as, to <ex>stab</ex> a man with a dagger</as>; also, to
thrust; <as>as, to <ex>stab</ex> a dagger into a person</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To injure secretly or by malicious falsehood
or slander; <as>as, to <ex>stab</ex> a person's
reputation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stab</ent><br/
<hw>Stab</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To give a wound with a
pointed weapon; to pierce; to thrust with a pointed weapon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>None shall dare<br/
With shortened sword to <qex>stab</qex> in closer war.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wound or pain, as if with a pointed
weapon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She speaks poniards, and every word <qex>stabs</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stab at</b></col>, <cd>to offer or threaten to stab;
to thrust a pointed weapon at.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stab</ent><br/
<hw>Stab</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The thrust of a pointed
weapon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A wound with a sharp-pointed weapon; <as>as, to fall
by the <ex>stab</ex> of an assassin</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: An injury inflicted covertly or suddenly;
<as>as, a <ex>stab</ex> given to character</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stabat Mater</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sta"bat Ma"ter</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"b<acr/t
m<amac/"t<etil/r)</pr>. <ety>[L., the mother was standing.]</ety> <def>A
celebrated Latin hymn, beginning with these words, commemorating the
sorrows of the mother of our Lord at the foot of the cross. It is read
in the Mass of the Sorrows of the Virgin Mary, and is sung by Catholics
when making <ldquo/the way of the cross<rdquo/ (<xex>Via Crucis</xex>).
See <er>Station</er>, 7 <sd>(c)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stabber</ent><br/
<hw>Stab"ber</hw> <pr>(st<acr/b"b<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, stabs; a privy
murderer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A small marline spike; a
pricker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stabbingly</ent><br/
<hw>Stab"bing*ly</hw> <pr>(-b<icr/ng*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>By stabbing; with intent to injure covertly.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Parker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stab culture</ent><br/
<hw>Stab culture</hw>. <fld>(Bacteriol.)</fld> <def>A culture made by
inoculating a solid medium, as gelatin, with the puncture of a needle or
wire; -- called also <altname>stab</altname>. The growths are usually of
characteristic form.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stabiliment</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*bil"i*ment</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*b<icr/l"<icr/*m<eit/nt)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stabilimentum</ets>, fr.
<ets>stabilire</ets> to make firm or stable, fr. <ets>stabilis</ets>.
See <er>Stable</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>The act of making firm;
firm support; establishment.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They serve for <qex>stabiliment</qex>, propagation, and shade.</q>
<rj><qau>Derham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stabilitate</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*bil"i*tate</hw> <pr>(-t<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>stabilitatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>stabilitare</ets> to make
stable.]</ety> <def>To make stable; to establish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stability</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*b<icr/l"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stabilitas</ets>; cf. F.
<ets>stabilit<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Stable</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The state or quality of being stable, or firm;
steadiness; stableness; firmness; strength to stand without being moved
or overthrown; <as>as, the <ex>stability</ex> of a structure; the
<ex>stability</ex> of a throne or a constitution.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Steadiness or firmness of character; firmness of
resolution or purpose; the quality opposite to <ant>fickleness</ant>,
<ant>irresolution</ant>, or <ant>inconstancy</ant>; constancy;
steadfastness; <as>as, a man of little <ex>stability</ex>, or of unusual
<ex>stability</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fixedness; -- as opposed to
<ant>fluidity</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Since fluidness and <qex>stability</qex> are contrary
qualities.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Steadiness; stableness; constancy; immovability;
firmness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stable</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ble</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"b'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estable</ets>, F. <ets>stable</ets>, fr. L. <ets>stabilis</ets>,
fr. <ets>stare</ets> to stand. See <er>Stand</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos> and
cf. <er>Establish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Firmly established; not
easily moved, shaken, or overthrown; fixed; <as>as, a <ex>stable</ex>
government</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In this region of chance, . . . where nothing is
<qex>stable</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Steady in purpose; constant; firm in resolution; not
easily diverted from a purpose; not fickle or wavering; <as>as, a man of
<ex>stable</ex> character</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And to her husband ever meek and <qex>stable</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Durable; not subject to overthrow or change; firm;
<as>as, a <ex>stable</ex> foundation; a <ex>stable</ex>
position.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>So placed as to resist forces
tending to cause motion; of such structure as to resist distortion or
molecular or chemical disturbance; -- said of any body or
substance.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stable equilibrium</b></col> <fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>the
kind of equilibrium of a body so placed that if disturbed it returns to
its former position, as in the case when the center of gravity is below
the point or axis of support; -- opposed to <contr>unstable
equilibrium</contr>, in which the body if disturbed does not tend to
return to its former position, but to move farther away from it, as in
the case of a body supported at a point below the center of gravity. Cf.
<cref>Neutral equilibrium</cref>, under <er>Neutral</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Fixed; steady; constant; abiding; strong;
durable; firm.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stable</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To fix; to establish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stable</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>estable</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/table</ets>, from L. <ets>stabulum</ets>, fr.
<ets>stare</ets> to stand. See <er>Stand</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<def>A house, shed, or building, for beasts to lodge and feed in; esp.,
a building or apartment with stalls, for horses; <as>as, a horse
<ex>stable</ex>; a cow <ex>stable</ex>.</as></def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stable fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a common
dipterous fly (<spn>Stomoxys calcitrans</spn>) which is abundant about
stables and often enters dwellings, especially in autumn; called also
<altname>biting house fly</altname>. These flies, unlike the common
house flies, which they resemble, bite severely, and are troublesome to
horses and cattle. They differ from the larger <er>horse
fly</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stable</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stabled</conjf> <pr>(-b'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stabling</conjf> <pr>(-bl<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To put or
keep in a stable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stable</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ble</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To dwell or lodge in a stable;
to dwell in an inclosed place; to kennel.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stableman</ent><br/
<ent>Stableboy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sta"ble*boy`</hw> <pr>(-boi`)</pr>, <hw>Sta"ble*man</hw>
<pr>(-m<acr/n)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A boy or man who attends
in a stable; a groom; a hostler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stableness</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ble*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stable, or firmly established; stability.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stabler</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"bler</hw> <pr>(-bl<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stable
keeper.</def> <rj><au>De Foe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stable stand</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ble stand`</hw> <pr>(st<acr/nd`)</pr>. <fld>(O.Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>The position of a man who is found at his standing in the forest,
with a crossbow or a longbow bent, ready to shoot at a deer, or close by
a tree with greyhounds in a leash ready to slip; -- one of the four
presumptions that a man intends stealing the king's deer.</def>
<rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stabling</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"bling</hw> <pr>(-bl<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or practice of keeping horses and cattle in a
stable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A building, shed, or room for horses and
cattle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stablish</ent><br/
<hw>Stab"lish</hw> <pr>(st<acr/b"l<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<ety>[Aphetic form of <ets>establish</ets>.]</ety> <def>To settle
permanently in a state; to make firm; to establish; to fix.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>2 Sam. vii. 13.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stablishment</ent><br/
<hw>Stab"lish*ment</hw> <pr>(-m<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Establishment.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stably</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"bly</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"bl<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
stable manner; firmly; fixedly; steadily; <as>as, a government
<ex>stably</ex> settled</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stabulation</ent><br/
<hw>Stab`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(st<acr/b`<usl/*l<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stabulatio</ets>, fr. <ets>stabulari</ets>
to stable cattle, fr. <ets>stabulum</ets>. See <er>Stable</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stabling or housing
beasts.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place for lodging beasts; a stable.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staccato</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stac*ca"to</hw> <pr>(st<adot/k*k<aum/"t<osl/)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It., p. p. of <ets>staccare</ets>, equivalent to
<ets>distaccare</ets>. See <er>Detach</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Disconnected; separated; distinct; -- a direction
to perform the notes of a passage in a short, distinct, and pointed
manner. It is opposed to <contr>legato</contr>, and often indicated by
heavy accents written over or under the notes, or by dots when the
performance is to be less distinct and emphatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expressed in a brief, pointed manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Staccato</qex> and peremptory [literary criticism].</q>
<rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stack</ent><br/
<hw>Stack</hw> <pr>(st<acr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>stakkr</ets>; akin to Sw. <ets>stack</ets>, Dan. <ets>stak</ets>.
Cf. <er>Stake</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A large and to some degree
orderly pile of hay, grain, straw, or the like, usually of a nearly
conical form, but sometimes rectangular or oblong, contracted at the top
to a point or ridge, and sometimes covered with thatch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But corn was housed, and beans were in the <qex>stack</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>An orderly pile of any type
of object, indefinite in quantity; -- used especially of piles of wood.
A <ex>stack</ex> is usually more orderly than a
<contr>pile</contr></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>Against every pillar was a <qex>stack</qex> of billets above a
man's height.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>A pile of wood
containing 108 cubic feet.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A large quantity; <as>as, a
<ex>stack</ex> of cash</as>.</def> <mark>[Informal]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A number of flues
embodied in one structure, rising above the roof.</def>
<specif>Hence:</specif> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any single insulated and
prominent structure, or upright pipe, which affords a conduit for smoke;
<as>as, the brick smoke<ex>stack</ex> of a factory; the
smoke<ex>stack</ex> of a steam vessel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Computer programming)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
section of memory in a computer used for temporary storage of data, in
which the last datum stored is the first retrieved.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A data structure within random-access memory used to simulate a
hardware stack; <as>as, a push-down <ex>stack</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The section of a library containing
shelves which hold books less frequently requested.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stack of arms</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a number
of muskets or rifles set up together, with the bayonets crossing one
another, forming a sort of conical self-supporting pile.</cd> --
<col><b>to blow one's stacks</b></col> <cd>to become very angry and lose
one's self-control, and especially to display one's fury by
shouting.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stack</ent><br/
<hw>Stack</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stacked</conjf> <pr>(st<acr/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stacking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Sw. <ets>stacka</ets>, Dan.
<ets>stakke</ets>. See <er>Stack</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To lay in a conical or other pile; to make into a large pile;
<as>as, to <ex>stack</ex> hay, cornstalks, or grain; to <ex>stack</ex>
or place wood.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>To place in a
vertical arrangement so that each item in a pile is resting on top of
another item in the pile, except for the bottom item; <as>as, to
<ex>stack</ex> the papers neatly on the desk; to <ex>stack</ex> the
bricks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To select or arrange dishonestly so as to achieve an
unfair advantage; <as>as, to <ex>stack</ex> a deck of cards; to
<ex>stack</ex> a jury with persons prejudiced against the
defendant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stack arms</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>to set up
a number of muskets or rifles together, with the bayonets crossing one
another, and forming a sort of conical pile.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1399 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Stackage</ent><br/
<hw>Stack"age</hw> <pr>(st<acr/k"<asl/j)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Hay, grain, or the like, in stacks; things stacked.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tax on things stacked.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stacket</ent><br/
<hw>Stack"et</hw> <pr>(-<ecr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>estacade</ets> and E. <ets>stockade</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>
<def>A stockade.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stack-guard</ent><br/
<hw>Stack"-guard`</hw> <pr>(-g<aum/rd`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
covering or protection, as of canvas, for a stack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stacking</ent><br/
<hw>Stack"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Stack</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Stacking band</b></col>, <col><b>Stacking
belt</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a band or rope used in binding thatch or
straw upon a stack.</cd> -- <col><b>Stacking stage</b></col>, <cd>a
stage used in building stacks.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stackstand</ent><br/
<hw>Stack"stand`</hw> <pr>(-st<acr/nd`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
staging for supporting a stack of hay or grain; a staddle{2}; a
rickstand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stackyard</ent><br/
<hw>Stack"yard`</hw> <pr>(-y<aum/rd`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A yard or
inclosure for stacks of hay or grain.</def> <rj><au>A.
Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stacte</ent><br/
<hw>Stac"te</hw> <pr>(st<acr/k"t<esl/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
Gr. <grk>stakth`</grk>, strictly fem. of <grk>stakto`s</grk> oozing out
in drops, fr. <grk>sta`zein</grk> to drop.]</ety> <def>One of the sweet
spices used by the ancient Jews in the preparation of incense. It was
perhaps an oil or other form of myrrh or cinnamon, or a kind of
storax.</def> <rj><au>Ex. xxx. 34.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staddle</ent><br/
<hw>Stad"dle</hw> <pr>(st<acr/d"d'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sta<edh/ol</ets>, <ets>sta<edh/ul</ets>, a foundation, firm seat;
akin to E. <ets>stand</ets>. <root/163. See <er>Stand</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <altsp>[Formerly written <asp>stadle</asp>.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Anything which serves for support; a staff; a prop; a
crutch; a cane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His weak steps governing<br/
And aged limbs on cypress <qex>stadle</qex> stout.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The frame of a stack of hay or grain.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A row of dried or drying hay, etc.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A small tree of any kind, especially a forest
tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In America, trees are called <xex>staddles</xex> from
the time that they are three or four years old till they are six or
eight inches in diameter, or more. This is also the sense in which the
word is used by Bacon and Tusser.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staddle</ent><br/
<hw>Stad"dle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To leave the
staddles, or saplings, of, as a wood when it is cut.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To form into staddles, as hay.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stade</ent><br/
<hw>Stade</hw> <pr>(st<amac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stade</ets>.]</ety> <def>A stadium.</def>
<rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stade</ent><br/
<hw>Stade</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>gestade</ets>
shore.]</ety> <def>A landing place or wharf.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stadimeter</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*dim"e*ter</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*d<icr/m"<esl/*t<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Stadium</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A horizontal graduated bar mounted on a staff, used as a stadium,
or telemeter, for measuring distances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stadia wires</ent><br/
<ent>Stadia hairs</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sta"di*a hairs</hw> <or/ <hw>Sta"di*a wires</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(st<amac/"d<icr/*<adot/ h<acir/rz)</pr>. <fld>(Surv.)</fld> <def>In
a theodolite, etc., horizontal cross wires or hairs equidistant from the
central horizontal cross wire.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stadium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sta"di*um</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"d<icr/*<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stadia</plw>
<pr>(st<amac/"d<icr/*<adot/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a stadium (in sense
1), from Gr. <grk>sta`dion</grk>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A Greek
measure of length, being the chief one used for itinerary distances,
also adopted by the Romans for nautical and astronomical measurements.
It was equal to 600 Greek or 625 Roman feet, or 125 Roman paces, or to
606 feet 9 inches English. This was also called the <altname>Olympic
stadium</altname>, as being the exact length of the foot-race course at
Olympia.</def> <rj><au>Dr. W. Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A race course; especially,
the Olympic course for foot races.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A modern structure, with its
inclosure, resembling the ancient stadium{2}, used for athletic games
which are typically played out-of-doors; such stadiums are usually large
structures without roofs, though some modern stadiums may have a
protective dome overhead. It may be contrasted with the
<contr>arena</contr>, the term commonly used for smaller structures at
which indoor games are played.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A kind of telemeter for measuring the distance of an
object of known dimensions, by observing the angle it subtends;
especially <fld>(Surveying)</fld>, a graduated rod used to measure the
distance of the place where it stands from an instrument having a
telescope, by observing the number of the graduations of the rod that
are seen between certain parallel wires (<xex>stadia wires</xex>) in the
field of view of the telescope; -- also called
<altname>stadia</altname>, and <altname>stadia rod</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stadtholder</ent><br/
<hw>Stadt"hold`er</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t"h<omac/ld`<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>stadhouder</ets>; <ets>stad</ets> a city, a
town + <ets>houder</ets> a holder.]</ety> <def>Formerly, the chief
magistrate of the United Provinces of Holland; also, the governor or
lieutenant governor of a province.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stadtholdership</ent><br/
<ent>Stadtholderate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stadt"hold`er*ate</hw> <pr>(-<asl/t)</pr>,
<hw>Stadt"hold`er*ship</hw> <pr>(-sh<icr/p)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The office or position of a stadtholder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stafette</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*fette"</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*f<ecr/t")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. G. <ets>stafette</ets>. See <er>Estafet</er>.]</ety> <def>An
estafet.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staff</ent><br/
<hw>Staff</hw> <pr>(st<adot/f)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Staves</plw> <pr>(st<amac/vz <or/ st<aum/vz; 277)</pr> or
<plw>Staffs</plw> <pr>(st<adot/fs)</pr> in senses 1-9, <plw>Staffs</plw>
in senses 10, 11.</plu> <ety>[AS. <ets>staef</ets> a staff; akin to LG.
& D. <ets>staf</ets>, OFries. <ets>stef</ets>, G. <ets>stab</ets>, Icel.
<ets>stafr</ets>, Sw. <ets>staf</ets>, Dan. <ets>stav</ets>, Goth.
<ets>stabs</ets> element, rudiment, Skr. <ets>sth<amac/pay</ets> to
cause to stand, to place. See <er>Stand</er>, and cf. <er>Stab</er>,
<er>Stave</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long piece of
wood; a stick; the long handle of an instrument or weapon; a pole or
stick, used for many purposes; <as>as, a surveyor's <ex>staff</ex>; the
<ex>staff</ex> of a spear or pike.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And he put the <qex>staves</qex> into the rings on the sides of
the altar to bear it withal.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xxxviii. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With forks and <qex>staves</qex> the felon to pursue.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stick carried in the hand for support or defense
by a person walking; hence, a support; that which props or
upholds.</def> <ldquo/Hooked <xex>staves</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The boy was the very <qex>staff</qex> of my age.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He spoke of it [beer] in <ldquo/The Earnest Cry,<rdquo/ and
likewise in the <ldquo/Scotch Drink,<rdquo/ as one of the
<qex>staffs</qex> of life which had been struck from the poor man's
hand.</q> <rj><qau>Prof. Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A pole, stick, or wand borne as an ensign of
authority; a badge of office; <as>as, a constable's
<ex>staff</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Methought this <qex>staff</qex>, mine office badge in court,<br/
Was broke in twain.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All his officers brake their <qex>staves</qex>; but at their
return new <qex>staves</qex> were delivered unto them.</q>
<rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A pole upon which a flag is supported and
displayed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The round of a ladder.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I ascended at one [ladder] of six hundred and thirty-nine
<qex>staves</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. J. Campbell (E. Brown's
Travels).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A series of verses so disposed that, when it is
concluded, the same order begins again; a stanza; a stave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cowley found out that no kind of <qex>staff</qex> is proper for an
heroic poem, as being all too lyrical.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The five lines and the spaces on
which music is written; -- formerly called
<altname>stave</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>An arbor, as of a wheel or a
pinion of a watch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>The grooved director for the
gorget, or knife, used in cutting for stone in the bladder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <ety>[From <er>Staff</er>, 3, a badge of office.]</ety>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>An establishment of officers in various
departments attached to an army, to a section of an army, or to the
commander of an army. The general's staff consists of those officers
about his person who are employed in carrying his commands into
execution. See <er><Eacute/tat Major</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Hence: A body of assistants serving to carry into
effect the plans of a superintendent or manager; sometimes used for the
entire group of employees of an enterprise, excluding the top
management; <as>as, the <ex>staff</ex> of a newspaper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Jacob's staff</b></col> <fld>(Surv.)</fld>, <cd>a single
straight rod or staff, pointed and iron-shod at the bottom, for
penetrating the ground, and having a socket joint at the top, used,
instead of a tripod, for supporting a compass.</cd> -- <col><b>Staff
angle</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a square rod of wood standing
flush with the wall on each of its sides, at the external angles of
plastering, to prevent their being damaged.</cd> -- <col><b>The staff of
life</b></col>, <cd>bread.</cd> <ldquo/Bread is <xex>the staff of
life</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Swift.</au> -- <col><b>Staff tree</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any plant of the genus <gen>Celastrus</gen>,
mostly climbing shrubs of the northern hemisphere. The American species
(<spn>Celastrus scandens</spn>) is commonly called
<stype>bittersweet</stype>. See 2d <er>Bittersweet</er>, 3
<sd>(b)</sd>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To set up one's staff</b></col>,
<col><b>To put up one's staff</b></col>, <col><b>To set down one's
staff</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To put down one's
staff</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to take up one's residence; to lodge.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staff</ent><br/
<hw>Staff</hw> <pr>(st<adot/f)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>staffiren</ets> to fill or fit out, adorn, fr. D.
<ets>stoffeeren</ets>, OF. <ets>estoffer</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/toffer</ets>, fr. OF. <ets>estoffe</ets> stuff, F.
<ets><eacute/toffe</ets>. See <er>Stuff</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Plaster combined with fibrous and other
materials so as to be suitable for sculpture in relief or in the round,
or for forming flat plates or boards of considerable size which can be
nailed to framework to make the exterior of a larger structure, forming
joints which may afterward be repaired and concealed with fresh
plaster.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staffier</ent><br/
<hw>Staf"fi*er</hw> <pr>(st<adot/f"f<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>An attendant bearing a staff.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Staffiers</xex> on foot.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staffish</ent><br/
<hw>Staff"ish</hw> <pr>(st<adot/f"<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Stiff; harsh.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ascham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staffman</ent><br/
<hw>Staff"man</hw> <pr>(st<adot/f"m<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Staffmen</plw> <pr>(-m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu> <def>A
workman employed in silk throwing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stag</ent><br/
<hw>Stag</hw> <pr>(st<acr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>steggr</ets> the male of several animals; or a doubtful AS.
<ets>stagga</ets>. Cf. <er>Steg</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The adult male of the red deer
(<spn>Cervus elaphus</spn>), a large European species closely related to
the American elk, or wapiti.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The male of certain
other species of large deer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A colt, or filly; also, a romping girl.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A castrated bull; -- called also <altname>bull
stag</altname>, and <altname>bull seg</altname>. See the Note under
<er>Ox</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Stock Exchange)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An outside
irregular dealer in stocks, who is not a member of the exchange.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One who applies for the allotment
of shares in new projects, with a view to sell immediately at a premium,
and not to hold the stock.</def> <mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European wren.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stag beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
numerous species of lamellicorn beetles belonging to <gen>Lucanus</gen>
and allied genera, especially <spn>Lucanus cervus</spn> of Europe and
<spn>Lucanus dama</spn> of the United States. The mandibles are large
and branched, or forked, whence the name. The larva feeds on the rotten
wood of dead trees. Called also <altname>horned bug</altname>, and
<altname>horse beetle</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stag dance</b></col>,
<cd>a dance by men only.</cd> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stag
hog</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the babiroussa.</cd> --
<col><b>Stag-horn coral</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
several species of large branching corals of the genus
<gen>Madrepora</gen>, which somewhat resemble the antlers of the stag,
especially <spn>Madrepora cervicornis</spn>, and <spn>Madrepora
palmata</spn>, of Florida and the West Indies.</cd> -- <col><b>Stag-horn
fern</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an Australian and West African
fern (<spn>Platycerium alcicorne</spn>) having the large fronds branched
like a stag's horns; also, any species of the same genus.</cd> --
<col><b>Stag-horn sumac</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a common
American shrub (<spn>Rhus typhina</spn>) having densely velvety
branchlets. See <er>Sumac</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stag party</b></col>,
<cd>a party consisting of men only.</cd> <mark>[Slang, U. S.]</mark> --
<col><b>Stag tick</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a parasitic
dipterous insect of the family <fam>Hippoboscidae</fam>, which lives
upon the stag and is usually wingless. The same species lives also upon
the European grouse, but in that case has wings.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stag</ent><br/
<hw>Stag</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>To act as a
<ldquo/stag,<rdquo/ or irregular dealer in stocks.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stag</ent><br/
<hw>Stag</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To watch; to dog, or keep track
of.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. or Slang]</mark> <rj><au>H.
Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stage</ent><br/
<hw>Stage</hw> <pr>(st<amac/j)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estage</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tage</ets>, (assumed) LL.
<ets>staticum</ets>, from L. <ets>stare</ets> to stand. See
<er>Stand</er>, and cf. <er>Static</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A floor
or story of a house.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An elevated platform on which an orator may speak, a
play be performed, an exhibition be presented, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A floor elevated for the convenience of mechanical
work, or the like; a scaffold; a staging.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A platform, often floating, serving as a kind of
wharf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The floor for scenic performances; hence, the
theater; the playhouse; hence, also, the profession of representing
dramatic compositions; the drama, as acted or exhibited.</def> <-- stars
of stage and screen --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Knights, squires, and steeds, must enter on the
<qex>stage</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lo! where the <qex>stage</qex>, the poor, degraded
<qex>stage</qex>,<br/
Holds its warped mirror to a gaping age.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Sprague.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A place where anything is publicly exhibited; the
scene of any noted action or career; the spot where any remarkable
affair occurs; <as>as, politicians must live their lives on the public
<ex>stage</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>When we are born, we cry that we are come<br/
To this great <qex>stage</qex> of fools.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Music and ethereal mirth<br/
Wherewith the <qex>stage</qex> of air and earth did ring.</q>
<rj><qau>Miton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The platform of a microscope, upon which an object
is placed to be viewed. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Microscope</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A place of rest on a regularly traveled road; a
stage house; a station; a place appointed for a relay of
horses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A degree of advancement in a journey; one of several
portions into which a road or course is marked off; the distance between
two places of rest on a road; <as>as, a <ex>stage</ex> of ten
miles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>stage</qex> . . . signifies a certain distance on a
road.</q> <rj><qau>Jeffrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He traveled by gig, with his wife, his favorite horse performing
the journey by easy <qex>stages</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Smiles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A degree of advancement in any pursuit, or of
progress toward an end or result.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such a polity is suited only to a particular <qex>stage</qex> in
the progress of society.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A large vehicle running from station to station for
the accommodation of the public; a stagecoach; an omnibus.</def>
<ldquo/A parcel sent you by the <xex>stage</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj> <mark>[Obsolescent]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I went in the sixpenny <qex>stage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>One of several marked phases or
periods in the development and growth of many animals and plants;
<as>as, the larval <ex>stage</ex>; pupa <ex>stage</ex>; zoea
<ex>stage</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stage box</b></col>, <cd>a box close to the stage in a
theater.</cd> -- <col><b>Stage carriage</b></col>, <cd>a
stagecoach.</cd> -- <col><b>Stage door</b></col>, <cd>the actors' and
workmen's entrance to a theater.</cd> -- <col><b>Stage lights</b></col>,
<cd>the lights by which the stage in a theater is illuminated.</cd> --
<col><b>Stage micrometer</b></col>, <cd>a graduated device applied to
the stage of a microscope for measuring the size of an object.</cd> --
<col><b>Stage wagon</b></col>, <cd>a wagon which runs between two places
for conveying passengers or goods.</cd> -- <col><b>Stage
whisper</b></col>, <cd>a loud whisper, as by an actor in a theater,
supposed, for dramatic effect, to be unheard by one or more of his
fellow actors, yet audible to the audience; an aside.</cd></cs> <--
<col><b>stage of the game</b></col>, [Colloq.] stage n. 10. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stage</ent><br/
<hw>Stage</hw> <pr>(st<amac/j)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To exhibit
upon a stage, or as upon a stage; to display publicly.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagecoach</ent><br/
<hw>Stage"coach`</hw> <pr>(st<amac/j"k<omac/ch`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A coach that runs regularly from one stage, station, or place to
another, for the conveyance of passengers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagecoachman</ent><br/
<hw>Stage"coach`man</hw> <pr>(st<amac/j"k<omac/ch`m<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stagecoachmen</plw>
<pr>(st<amac/j"k<omac/ch`m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who drives a
stagecoach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stage director</ent><br/
<hw>Stage director</hw>. <fld>(Theat.)</fld> <def>One who prepares a
play for production. He arranges the details of the stage settings, the
business to be used, all stage effects, and instructs the actors,
excepting usually the star, in the general interpretation of their
parts.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stage fright</ent><br/
<hw>Stage fright</hw>. <def>Nervousness felt before an
audience.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagehouse</ent><br/
<hw>Stage"house`</hw> <pr>(-hous`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A house
where a stage regularly stops for passengers or a relay of
horses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagely</ent><br/
<hw>Stage"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to a stage; becoming
the theater; theatrical.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stage manager</ent><br/
<hw>Stage manager</hw>. <fld>(Theat.)</fld> <def>One in control of the
stage during the production of a play. He directs the stage hands,
property man, etc., has charge of all details behind the curtain, except
the acting, and has a general oversight of the actors. Sometimes he is
also the <er>stage director</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stageplay</ent><br/
<hw>Stage"play`</hw> <pr>(-pl<amac/`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
dramatic or theatrical entertainment.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stageplayer</ent><br/
<hw>Stage"play`er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An actor
on the stage; one whose occupation is to represent characters on the
stage; <as>as, <person>Garrick was</person> a celebrated
<ex>stageplayer</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stager</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ger</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"j<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A player.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who has long acted on the stage of life; a
practitioner; a person of experience, or of skill derived from long
experience.</def> <ldquo/You will find most of the old
<xex>stagers</xex> still stationary there.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A horse used in drawing a stage.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagery</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ger*y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Exhibition on
the stage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stage-struck</ent><br/
<hw>Stage"-struck`</hw> <pr>(st<amac/j"str<ucr/k`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Fascinated by the stage; seized by a passionate desire to become an
actor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stag-evil</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"-e`vil</hw> <pr>(st<acr/g"<emac/`v'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A kind of palsy affecting the jaw of a
horse.</def> <rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staggard</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"gard</hw> <pr>(-g<etil/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Stag</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The male red deer when
four years old.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagger</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"ger</hw> <pr>(-g<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Staggered</conjf>
<pr>(-g<etil/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Staggering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stakeren</ets>,
Icel. <ets>stakra</ets> to push, to stagger, fr. <ets>staka</ets> to
punt, push, stagger; cf. OD. <ets>staggeren</ets> to stagger. Cf.
<er>Stake</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To move to one
side and the other, as if about to fall, in standing or walking; not to
stand or walk with steadiness; to sway; to reel or totter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Deep was the wound; he <qex>staggered</qex> with the blow.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cease to stand firm; to begin to give way; to
fail.</def> <ldquo/The enemy <xex>staggers</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To begin to doubt and waver in purpose; to become
less confident or determined; to hesitate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [Abraham] <qex>staggered</qex> not at the promise of God
through unbelief.</q> <rj><qau>Rom. iv. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagger</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"ger</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to reel or
totter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That hand shall burn in never-quenching fire<br/
That <qex>staggers</qex> thus my person.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to doubt and waver; to make to hesitate; to
make less steady or confident; to shock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whosoever will read the story of this war will find himself much
<qex>staggered</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Howell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Grants to the house of Russell were so enormous, as not only to
outrage economy, but even to <qex>stagger</qex> credibility.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To arrange (a series of parts) on each side of a
median line alternately, as the spokes of a wheel or the rivets of a
boiler seam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagger</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"ger</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An unsteady movement
of the body in walking or standing, as if one were about to fall; a
reeling motion; vertigo; -- often in the plural; <as>as, the
<ex>stagger</ex> of a drunken man</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A disease of
horses and other animals, attended by reeling, unsteady gait or sudden
falling; <as>as, parasitic <ex>staggers</ex>; apoplectic or sleepy
<ex>staggers</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Bewilderment; perplexity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stomach staggers</b></col> <fld>(Far.)</fld>,
<cd>distention of the stomach with food or gas, resulting in
indigestion, frequently in death.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staggerbush</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"ger*bush`</hw> <pr>(-b<usdot/sh`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An American shrub (<spn>Andromeda Mariana</spn>)
having clusters of nodding white flowers. It grows in low, sandy places,
and is said to poison lambs and calves.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staggeringly</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"ger*ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a staggering
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staggerwort</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"ger*wort`</hw> <pr>(-w<ucir/rt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of ragwort (<spn>Senecio
Jacobaea</spn>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stag-horn fern</ent><br/
<ent>Stag-horn coral</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stag"-horn` cor"al</hw> <pr>(st<acr/g"h<ocir/rn`
k<ocr/r"<ait/l)</pr>, <hw>Stag"-horn` fern`</hw> <pr>(f<etil/rn`)</pr>,
etc. }</mhw> <def>See under <er>Stag</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stag-horned</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"-horned`</hw> <pr>(-h<ocir/rnd`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the mandibles large and palmate, or
branched somewhat like the antlers of a stag; -- said of certain
beetles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staghound</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"hound`</hw> <pr>(-hound`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A large and powerful hound formerly used in
hunting the stag, the wolf, and other large animals. The breed is nearly
extinct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staging</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ging</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"j<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
structure of posts and boards for supporting workmen, etc., as in
building.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The business of running stagecoaches; also, the act
of journeying in stagecoaches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagirite</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"i*rite</hw> <pr>(st<acr/j"<icr/*r<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A native of, or resident in, <xex>Stagira</xex>, in ancient
Macedonia; especially, Aristotle.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Stagyrite</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagnancy</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"nan*cy</hw> <pr>(st<acr/g"n<ait/n*s<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>State of being stagnant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagnant</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"nant</hw> <pr>(-n<ait/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stagnans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>stagnare</ets>.
See <er>Stagnate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That stagnates; not
flowing; not running in a current or steam; motionless; hence, impure or
foul from want of motion; <as>as, a <ex>stagnant</ex> lake or pond;
<ex>stagnant</ex> blood in the veins.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not active or brisk; dull; <as>as, business is
<ex>stagnant</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That gloomy slumber of the <qex>stagnant</qex> soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Johnson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For him a <qex>stagnant</qex> life was not worth living.</q>
<rj><qau>Palfrey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagnantly</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"nant*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stagnant
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1400 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Stagnate</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"nate</hw> <pr>(st<acr/g"n<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stagnated</conjf>
<pr>(-n<asl/*t<ecr/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stagnating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>stagnatus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>stagnare</ets> to stagnate, make stagnant, from
<ets>stagnum</ets> a piece of standing water. See <er>Stank</er> a pool,
and cf. <er>Stanch</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
cease to flow; to be motionless; <as>as, blood <ex>stagnates</ex> in the
veins of an animal</as>; hence, to become impure or foul by want of
motion; <as>as, air <ex>stagnates</ex> in a close room</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cease to be brisk or active; to become dull or
inactive; <as>as, commerce <ex>stagnates</ex>; business
<ex>stagnates</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ready-witted tenderness . . . never <qex>stagnates</qex> in vain
lamentations while there is any room for hope.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagnate</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"nate</hw> <pr>(-n<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Stagnant.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>stagnate</xex>
mass of vapors.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagnation</ent><br/
<hw>Stag*na"tion</hw> <pr>(-n<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>stagnation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
condition of being stagnant; cessation of flowing or circulation, as of
a fluid; the state of being motionless; <as>as, the <ex>stagnation</ex>
of the blood; the <ex>stagnation</ex> of water or air; the
<ex>stagnation</ex> of vapors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The cessation of action, or of brisk action; the
state of being dull; <as>as, the <ex>stagnation</ex> of
business</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagworm</ent><br/
<hw>Stag"worm`</hw> <pr>(st<acr/g"w<ucir/rm`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The larva of any species of botfly which is
parasitic upon the stag, such as <spn>Oestrus actaeon</spn>, or
<spn>Hypoderma actaeon</spn>, which burrows beneath the skin, and
<spn>Cephalomyia auribarbis</spn>, which lives in the
nostrils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stagy</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"gy</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"j<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>stagey</asp>.]</altsp> <def>Having an air or
manner characteristic of the stage; theatrical; artificial; <as>as, a
<ex>stagy</ex> tone or bearing</as>; -- chiefly used
depreciatively.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stahlian</ent><br/
<hw>Stahl"ian</hw> <pr>(st<aum/l"y<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pertaining to, or taught by, <persfn><etsep>Stahl</etsep></persfn>,
a German physician and chemist of the 17th century; <as>as, the
<ex>Stahlian</ex> theory of phlogiston</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stahlian</ent><br/
<hw>Stahl"ian</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A believer in, or advocate of,
Stahlism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stahlianism</ent><br/
<ent>Stahlism</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stahl"ism</hw> <pr>(st<aum/l"<icr/z'm)</pr>,
<hw>Stahl"ian*ism</hw> <pr>(-y<ait/n*<icr/z'm)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The Stahlian theory, that every vital action is a
function or operation of the soul.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staid</ent><br/
<hw>Staid</hw> <pr>(st<amac/d)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Stay</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staid</ent><br/
<hw>Staid</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Stay</er> to stop.]</ety>
<def>Sober; grave; steady; sedate; composed; regular; not wild,
volatile, flighty, or fanciful.</def> <ldquo/Sober and <xex>staid</xex>
persons.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O'erlaid with black, <qex>staid</qex> Wisdom's hue.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sober; grave; steady; steadfast; composed;
regular; sedate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staidly</ent><br/
<hw>Staid"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a staid manner,
sedately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staidness</ent><br/
<hw>Staid"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
staid; seriousness; steadiness; sedateness; regularity; -- the opposite
of <contr>wildness</contr>, or <ant>levity</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If sometimes he appears too gay, yet a secret gracefulness of
youth accompanies his writings, though the <qex>staidness</qex> and
sobriety of age be wanting.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sobriety; gravity; steadiness; regularity;
constancy; firmness; stability; sedateness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stail</ent><br/
<hw>Stail</hw> <pr>(st<amac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A handle, as of
a mop; a stale.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stain</ent><br/
<hw>Stain</hw> <pr>(st<amac/n)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stained</conjf> <pr>(st<amac/nd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Staining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Abbrev. fr.
<ets>distain</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To discolor by the
application of foreign matter; to make foul; to spot; <as>as, to
<ex>stain</ex> the hand with dye; armor <ex>stained</ex> with
blood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To color, as wood, glass, paper, cloth, or the like,
by processes affecting, chemically or otherwise, the material itself; to
tinge with a color or colors combining with, or penetrating, the
substance; to dye; <as>as, to <ex>stain</ex> wood with acids, colored
washes, paint rubbed in, etc.; to <ex>stain</ex> glass</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To spot with guilt or infamy; to bring reproach on;
to blot; to soil; to tarnish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of honor void,<br/
Of innocence, of faith, of purity,<br/
Our wonted ornaments now soiled and <qex>stained</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To cause to seem inferior or soiled by
comparison.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She <qex>stains</qex> the ripest virgins of her age.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That did all other beasts in beauty <qex>stain</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stained glass</b></col>, <cd>glass colored or stained by
certain metallic pigments fused into its substance, -- often used for
making ornamental windows.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To paint; dye; blot; soil; sully; discolor;
disgrace; taint.</syn> -- <usage><er>Paint</er>, <er>Stain</er>,
<er>Dye</er>. These denote three different processes; the first
mechanical, the other two, chiefly chemical. To <xex>paint</xex> a thing
is to spread a coat of coloring matter over it; to <xex>stain</xex> or
<xex>dye</xex> a thing is to impart color to its substance. To
<xex>stain</xex> is said chiefly of solids, as wood, glass, paper; to
<xex>dye</xex>, of fibrous substances, textile fabrics, etc.; the one,
commonly, a simple process, as applying a wash; the other more complex,
as fixing colors by mordants.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stain</ent><br/
<hw>Stain</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To give or receive a stain; to
grow dim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stain</ent><br/
<hw>Stain</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A discoloration by
foreign matter; a spot; <as>as, a <ex>stain</ex> on a garment or
cloth</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A natural spot of a color different from the
ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Swift trouts, diversified with crimson <qex>stains</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Taint of guilt; tarnish; disgrace;
reproach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor death itself can wholly wash their <qex>stains</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our opinion . . . is, I trust, without any blemish or
<qex>stain</qex> of heresy.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Cause of reproach; shame.</def> <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A tincture; a tinge.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have some <qex>stain</qex> of soldier in you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Blot; spot; taint; pollution; blemish; tarnish;
color; disgrace; infamy; shame.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stainer</ent><br/
<hw>Stain"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who stains or tarnishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A workman who stains; <as>as, a <ex>stainer</ex> of
wood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stainless</ent><br/
<hw>Stain"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from stain;
immaculate.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The very care he took to keep his name<br/
<qex>Stainless</qex>, with some was evidence of shame.</q>
<rj><qau>Crabbe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Blameless; spotless; faultless. See
<er>Blameless</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stainlessly</ent><br/
<hw>Stain"less*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stainless
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stair</ent><br/
<hw>Stair</hw> <pr>(st<acir/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>steir</ets>, <ets>steyer</ets>, AS. <ets>st<aemac/ger</ets>, from
<ets>st<imac/gan</ets> to ascend, rise. <root/164. See <er>Sty</er> to
ascend.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One step of a series for ascending or
descending to a different level; -- commonly applied to those within a
building.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A series of steps, as for passing from one story of
a house to another; -- commonly used in the plural; but originally used
in the singular only.</def> <ldquo/I a winding <xex>stair</xex>
found.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer's Dream.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Below stairs</b></col>, <cd>in the basement or lower part
of a house, where the servants are.</cd> -- <col><b>Flight of
stairs</b></col>, <cd>the stairs which make the whole ascent of a
story.</cd> -- <col><b>Pair of stairs</b></col>, <cd>a set or flight of
stairs. -- <xex>pair</xex>, in this phrase, having its old meaning of a
<xex>set</xex>. See <er>Pair</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</cd> -- <col><b>Run
of stairs</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a single set of stairs, or
section of a stairway, from one platform to the next.</cd> --
<col><b>Stair rod</b></col>, <cd>a rod, usually of metal, for holding a
stair carpet to its place.</cd> -- <col><b>Up stairs</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Upstairs</er> in the Vocabulary.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staircase</ent><br/
<hw>Stair"case`</hw> <pr>(st<acir/r"k<amac/s`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A flight of stairs with their supporting framework, casing,
balusters, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To make a complete <qex>staircase</qex> is a curious piece of
architecture.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Staircase shell</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any scalaria, or wentletrap.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Any
species of <gen>Solarium</gen>, or perspective shell.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stairhead</ent><br/
<hw>Stair"head`</hw> <pr>(-h<ecr/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The head
or top of a staircase.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stairway</ent><br/
<hw>Stair"way`</hw> <pr>(-w<amac/`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A flight of
stairs or steps; a staircase.</def> <ldquo/A rude and narrow
<xex>stairway</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Moore.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staith</ent><br/
<hw>Staith</hw> <pr>(st<amac/th)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stae<edh/</ets> a bank, shore, from the root of E.
<ets>stead</ets>.]</ety> <def>A landing place; an elevated staging upon
a wharf for discharging coal, etc., as from railway cars, into
vessels.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staithman</ent><br/
<hw>Staith"man</hw> <pr>(-m<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A man
employed in weighing and shipping at a staith.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stake</ent><br/
<hw>Stake</hw> <pr>(st<amac/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>staca</ets>, from the root of E. <ets>stick</ets>; akin to OFries.
& LG. <ets>stake</ets>, D. <ets>staak</ets>, Sw. <ets>stake</ets>, Dan.
<ets>stage</ets>. See <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Estacade</er>, <er>Stockade</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A piece of
wood, usually long and slender, pointed at one end so as to be easily
driven into the ground as a support or stay; <as>as, a <ex>stake</ex> to
support vines, fences, hedges, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A sharpened <qex>stake</qex> strong Dryas found.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stick inserted upright in a loop, eye, or mortise,
at the side or end of a cart, a flat car, or the like, to prevent goods
from falling off.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The piece of timber to which a martyr was affixed to
be burned; hence, martyrdom by fire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A small anvil usually furnished with a tang to enter
a hole in a bench top, -- used by tinsmiths, blacksmiths, etc., for
light work, punching upon, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That which is laid down as a wager; that which is
staked or hazarded; a pledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mormon Ch.)</fld> <def>A territorial division; --
called also <altname>stake of Zion</altname>.</def></p>

<p><q>Every city, or <ldquo/<qex>stake</qex>,<rdquo/ including a chief
town and surrounding towns, has its president, with two counselors; and
this president has a high council of chosen men.</q>
<rj><qau>Schaff-Herzog Encyc.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At stake</b></col>, <cd>in danger; hazarded;
pledged.</cd> <ldquo/I see my reputation is <xex>at stake</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stake</ent><br/
<hw>Stake</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Staked</conjf> <pr>(st<amac/kd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Staking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fasten, support,
or defend with stakes; <as>as, to <ex>stake</ex> vines or
plants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mark the limits of by stakes; -- with
<xex>out</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>stake</ex> out land; to <ex>stake</ex>
out a new road</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To put at hazard upon the issue of competition, or
upon a future contingency; to wager; to pledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'll <qex>stake</qex> yon lamb, that near the fountain plays.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To pierce or wound with a stake.</def>
<rj><au>Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stake-driver</ent><br/
<hw>Stake"-driv`er</hw> <pr>(-dr<imac/v`<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The common American bittern (<spn>Botaurus
lentiginosus</spn>); -- so called because one of its notes resembles the
sound made in driving a stake into the mud. Called also <altname>meadow
hen</altname>, and <altname>Indian hen</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stakehead</ent><br/
<hw>Stake"head`</hw> <pr>(st<amac/k"h<ecr/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Rope Making)</fld> <def>A horizontal bar on a stake, used for
supporting the yarns which are kept apart by pins in the bar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stakeholder</ent><br/
<hw>Stake"hold`er</hw> <pr>(-h<omac/ld`<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The holder of a stake; one with whom the bets are deposited when a
wager is laid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staktometer</ent><br/
<hw>Stak*tom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(st<acr/k*t<ocr/m"<esl/*t<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>stakto`s</grk> falling by drops +
<ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>A drop measurer; a glass tube tapering to
a small orifice at the point, and having a bulb in the middle, used for
finding the number of drops in equal quantities of different liquids.
See <er>Pipette</er>.</def> <rj><au>Sir D. Brewster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stal</ent><br/
<hw>Stal</hw> <pr>(st<aum/l)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos>
<mord>of <er>Steal</er></mord>. <def>Stole.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalactical</ent><br/
<ent>Stalactic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sta*lac"tic</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*l<acr/k"t<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Sta*lac"tic*al</hw> <pr>(-t<icr/"k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Stalactitic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalactiform</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*lac"ti*form</hw> <pr>(-t<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Like a stalactite; resembling a stalactite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalactite</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*lac"tite</hw> <pr>(-t<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stalactites</plw> <pr>(-t<imac/ts)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>stalakto`s</grk> oozing out in drops, dropping, fr.
<grk>stala`zein</grk> to drop: cf. F. <ets>stalactite</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A pendent cone or cylinder of
calcium carbonate resembling an icicle in form and mode of attachment.
Stalactites are found depending from the roof or sides of caverns, and
are produced by deposition from waters which have percolated through,
and partially dissolved, the overlying limestone rocks.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>In an extended sense, any mineral or rock of similar
form and origin; <as>as, a <ex>stalactite</ex> of lava</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalactites</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stal`ac*ti"tes</hw> <pr>(st<acr/l`<acr/k*t<imac/"t<emac/z)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>A stalactite.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalactitical</ent><br/
<ent>Stalactitic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stal`ac*tit"ic</hw> <pr>(-t<icr/t"<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Stal`ac*tit"ic*al</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>stalactitique</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a stalactite; having the
form or characters of a stalactite; stalactic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalactitiform</ent><br/
<hw>Stal`ac*tit"i*form</hw>
<pr>(st<acr/l`<acr/k*t<icr/t"<icr/*f<ocir/rm)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Having the form of a stalactite; stalactiform.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalagmite</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*lag"mite</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*l<acr/g"m<imac/t)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>sta`lagma</grk> that which drops, a drop,
fr. <grk>stala`zein</grk> to drop: cf. F. <ets>stalagmite</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A deposit more or less resembling an inverted
stalactite, formed by calcareous water dropping on the floors of
caverns; hence, a similar deposit of other material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalagmitical</ent><br/
<ent>Stalagmitic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stal`ag*mit"ic</hw>
<pr>(st<acr/l`<acr/g*m<icr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <hw>Stal`ag*mit"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(-m<icr/t"<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
form or structure of stalagmites.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Stal`ag*mit"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalder</ent><br/
<hw>Stal"der</hw> <pr>(st<add/l"d<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
the root of <ets>stall</ets>.]</ety> <def>A wooden frame to set casks
on.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stale</ent><br/
<hw>Stale</hw> <pr>(st<amac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stale</ets>, <ets>stele</ets>, AS. <ets>stael</ets>,
<ets>stel</ets>; akin to LG. & D. <ets>steel</ets>, G. <ets>stiel</ets>;
cf. L. <ets>stilus</ets> stake, stalk, stem, Gr. <grk>steleo`n</grk> a
handle, and E. <ets>stall</ets>, <ets>stalk</ets>, n.]</ety> <def>The
stock or handle of anything; <as>as, the <ex>stale</ex> of a
rake</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>steal</asp>,
<asp>stele</asp>, etc.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But seeing the arrow's <qex>stale</qex> without, and that the head
did go<br/
No further than it might be seen.</q> <rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stale</ent><br/
<hw>Stale</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Akin to <ets>stale</ets> urine, and
to <ets>stall</ets>, n.; probably from Low German or Scandinavian. Cf.
<er>Stale</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Vapid or
tasteless from age; having lost its life, spirit, and flavor, from being
long kept; <as>as, <ex>stale</ex> beer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not new; not freshly made; <as>as, <ex>stale</ex>
bread</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having lost the life or graces of youth; worn out;
decayed.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>stale</xex> virgin.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Worn out by use or familiarity; having lost its
novelty and power of pleasing; trite; common.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wit itself, if <qex>stale</qex> is less pleasing.</q>
<rj><qau>Grew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How weary, <qex>stale</qex> flat, and unprofitable<br/
Seem to me all the uses of this world!</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stale affidavit</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an
affidavit held above a year.</cd> <au>Craig.</au> -- <col><b>Stale
demand</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a claim or demand which has not
been pressed or demanded for a long time.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stale</ent><br/
<hw>Stale</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Staled</conjf> <pr>(st<amac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Staling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make vapid or tasteless; to
destroy the life, beauty, or use of; to wear out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Age can not wither her, nor custom <qex>stale</qex><br/
Her infinite variety.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stale</ent><br/
<hw>Stale</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Akin to D. & G.
<ets>stallen</ets>, Dan. <ets>stalle</ets>, Sw. <ets>stalla</ets>, and
E. <ets>stall</ets> a stable. <root/ 163. See <er>Stall</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and cf. <er>Stale</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To make
water; to discharge urine; -- said especially of horses and
cattle.</def> <rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stale</ent><br/
<hw>Stale</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stale</er>, <pos>a.</pos> &
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is stale or worn out
by long keeping, or by use.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A prostitute.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Urine, esp. that of beasts.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Stale</xex> of horses.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stale</ent><br/
<hw>Stale</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>estal</ets> place,
position, abode, market, F. <ets><eacute/tal</ets> a butcher's stall,
OHG. <ets>stal</ets> station, place, stable, G. <ets>stall</ets> (see
<er>Stall</er>, <pos>n.</pos>); or from OE. <ets>stale</ets> theft, AS.
<ets>stalu</ets> (see <er>Steal</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>).]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Something set, or offered to view, as an allurement to
draw others to any place or purpose; a decoy; a stool pigeon.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Still, as he went, he crafty <qex>stales</qex> did lay.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stalking-horse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chess)</fld> <def>A stalemate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A laughingstock; a dupe.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalely</ent><br/
<hw>Stale"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a stale
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of old; long since.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalemate</ent><br/
<hw>Stale"mate`</hw> <pr>(-m<amac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chess)</fld> <def>The position of the king when he can not move
without being placed in check and there is no other piece which can be
moved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalemate</ent><br/
<hw>Stale"mate`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Chess)</fld> <def>To
subject to a stalemate; hence, to bring to a stand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staleness</ent><br/
<hw>Stale"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalk</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk</hw> <pr>(st<add/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stalke</ets>, fr. AS. <ets>stael</ets>, <ets>stel</ets>, a stalk.
See <er>Stale</er> a handle, <er>Stall</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The stem or main axis of a plant;
<as>as, a <ex>stalk</ex> of wheat, rye, or oats; the <ex>stalks</ex> of
maize or hemp.</as></def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The petiole, pedicel, or
peduncle, of a plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which resembles the stalk of a plant, as the
stem of a quill.</def> <rj><au>Grew.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An ornament in the Corinthian
capital resembling the stalk of a plant, from which the volutes and
helices spring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One of the two upright pieces of a ladder.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To climb by the rungs and the <qex>stalks</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A stem or peduncle,
as of certain barnacles and crinoids.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The narrow
basal portion of the abdomen of a hymenopterous insect.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>The peduncle of the eyes of decapod
crustaceans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>An iron bar with projections
inserted in a core to strengthen it; a core arbor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stalk borer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the larva
of a noctuid moth (<spn>Gortyna nitela</spn>), which bores in the stalks
of the raspberry, strawberry, tomato, asters, and many other garden
plants, often doing much injury.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1401 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Stalk</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stalked</conjf> <pr>(st<add/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stalking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>staelcan</ets>,
<ets>stealcian</ets> to go slowly; cf. <ets>stealc</ets> high, elevated,
Dan. <ets>stalke</ets> to stalk; probably akin to 1st
<ets>stalk</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To walk slowly and cautiously;
to walk in a stealthy, noiseless manner; -- sometimes used with a
reflexive pronoun.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Into the chamber he <qex>stalked</qex> him full still.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Bertran] <qex>stalks</qex> close behind her, like a witch's
fiend,<br/
Pressing to be employed.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To walk behind something as a screen, for the
purpose of approaching game; to proceed under cover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king . . . crept under the shoulder of his led horse; . . .
<ldquo/I must <qex>stalk</qex>,<rdquo/ said he.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One underneath his horse, to get a shoot doth
<qex>stalk</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To walk with high and proud steps; -- usually
implying the affectation of dignity, and indicating dislike. The word is
used, however, especially by the poets, to express dignity of
step.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With manly mien he <qex>stalked</qex> along the ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then <qex>stalking</qex> through the deep,<br/
He fords the ocean.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I forbear myself from entering the lists in which he has long
<qex>stalked</qex> alone and unchallenged.</q>
<rj><qau>Merivale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalk</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk</hw> <pr>(st<add/k)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
approach under cover of a screen, or by stealth, for the purpose of
killing, as game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As for shooting a man from behind a wall, it is cruelly like to
<qex>stalking</qex> a deer.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To follow (a person) persistently, with or without
attempts to evade detection; <as>as, the paparazzi <ex>stalk</ex>
celebrities to get candid photographs; obsessed fans may <ex>stalk</ex>
their favorite movie stars</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalk</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A high, proud, stately
step or walk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus twice before, . . . <br/
With martial <qex>stalk</qex> hath he gone by our watch.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The which with monstrous <qex>stalk</qex> behind him stepped.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or process of stalking.</def></p>

<p><q>When the <qex>stalk</qex> was over (the antelope took alarm and
ran off before I was within rifle shot) I came back.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Roosevelt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalked</ent><br/
<hw>Stalked</hw> <pr>(st<add/kt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a stalk
or stem; borne upon a stem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stalked barnacle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
goose barnacle, or anatifer; -- called also <altname>stalk
barnacle</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stalked crinoid</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any crinoid having a jointed stem.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalker</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk"er</hw> <pr>(st<add/k"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who stalks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of fishing net.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalk-eyed</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk"-eyed`</hw> <pr>(st<add/k"<imac/d`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the eyes raised on a stalk, or peduncle;
-- opposed to <contr>sessile-eyed</contr>. Said especially of
podophthalmous crustaceans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stalk-eyed crustaceans</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Podophthalmia</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalking-horse</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk"ing-horse`</hw> <pr>(st<add/k"<icr/ng-h<ocir/rs`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A horse, or a figure resembling a horse,
behind which a hunter conceals himself from the game he is aiming to
kill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>Something used to cover up a
secret project; a mask; a pretense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hypocrisy is the devil's <qex>stalking-horse</qex> under an
affectation of simplicity and religion.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How much more abominable is it to make of him [Christ] and
religion a <qex>stalking-horse</qex>, to get and enjoy the world!</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalkless</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no stalk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalky</ent><br/
<hw>Stalk"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hard as a stalk;
resembling a stalk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At the top [it] bears a great <qex>stalky</qex> head.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stall</ent><br/
<hw>Stall</hw> <pr>(st<add/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stal</ets>, AS. <ets>steall</ets>, <ets>stall</ets>, a place, seat,
or station, a stable; akin to D. & OHG. <ets>stal</ets>, G. & Sw.
<ets>stall</ets>, Icel. <ets>stallr</ets>, Dan. <ets>stald</ets>,
originally, a standing place; akin also to G. <ets>stelle</ets> a place,
<ets>stellen</ets> to place, Gr. <grk>ste`llein</grk> to set, place,
send, and E. <ets>stand</ets>. <root/163. See <er>Stand</er>, and cf.
<er>Apostle</er>, <er>Epistle</er>, <er>Forestall</er>,
<er>Install</er>, <er>Stale</er>, <pos>a.</pos> & <pos>v. i.</pos>, 1st
<er>Stalk</er>, <er>Stallion</er>, <er>Still</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A stand; a station; a fixed spot; hence, the stand or place where a
horse or an ox is kept and fed; the division of a stable, or the
compartment, for one horse, ox, or other animal.</def> <ldquo/In an oxes
<xex>stall</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stable; a place for cattle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At last he found a <qex>stall</qex> where oxen stood.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small apartment or shed in which merchandise is
exposed for sale; <as>as, a butcher's <ex>stall</ex>; a
book<ex>stall</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A bench or table on which small articles of
merchandise are exposed for sale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How peddlers' <qex>stalls</qex> with glittering toys are laid.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A seat in the choir of a church, for one of the
officiating clergy. It is inclosed, either wholly or partially, at the
back and sides. The stalls are frequently very rich, with canopies and
elaborate carving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dignified clergy, out of humility, have called their thrones
by the names of <qex>stalls</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Loud the monks sang in their <qex>stalls</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>In the theater, a seat with arms or otherwise partly
inclosed, as distinguished from the benches, sofas, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The space left by excavation
between pillars. See <cref>Post and stall</cref>, under
<er>Post</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A covering or sheath, as of leather, horn, of iron,
for a finger or thumb; a cot; <as>as, a thumb <ex>stall</ex>; a finger
<ex>stall</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stall reader</b></col>, <cd>one who reads books at a
stall where they are exposed for sale.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cries the <qex>stall reader</qex>, <ldquo/Bless us! what a word
on<br/
A titlepage is this!<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stall</ent><br/
<hw>Stall</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stalled</conjf> <pr>(st<add/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stalling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Sw. <ets>stalla</ets>, Dan.
<ets>stalde</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put into a stall or
stable; to keep in a stall or stalls; <as>as, to <ex>stall</ex> an
ox</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where King Latinus then his oxen <qex>stalled</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fatten; <as>as, to <ex>stall</ex>
cattle</as>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To place in an office with the customary
formalities; to install.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To plunge into mire or snow so as not to be able to
get on; to set; to fix; <as>as, to <ex>stall</ex> a cart</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His horses had been <qex>stalled</qex> in the snow.</q>
<rj><qau>E. E. Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To forestall; to anticipate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is not to be <qex>stall'd</qex> by my report.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To keep close; to keep secret.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stall</qex> this in your bosom.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stall</ent><br/
<hw>Stall</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>steallian</ets> to have
room. See <er>Stall</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To live
in, or as in, a stall; to dwell.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We could not <qex>stall</qex> together<br/
In the whole world.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To kennel, as dogs.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be set, as in mire or snow; to stick
fast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be tired of eating, as cattle.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stallage</ent><br/
<hw>Stall"age</hw> <pr>(st<add/l"<asl/j ; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>estallage</ets>, of German origin. See
<er>Stall</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>
<def>The right of erecting a stall or stalls in fairs; rent paid for a
stall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Dung of cattle or horses, mixed with straw.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stallation</ent><br/
<hw>Stal*la"tion</hw> <pr>(st<ocr/l*l<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Installation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalled</ent><br/
<hw>Stalled</hw> <pr>(st<add/ld <or/ st<add/l"<ecr/d)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Put or kept in a stall; hence, fatted.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>stalled</xex> ox.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Prov. xv. 17.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staller</ent><br/
<hw>Stall"er</hw> <pr>(st<add/l"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
standard bearer.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stall-feed</ent><br/
<hw>Stall"-feed`</hw> <pr>(-f<emac/d`)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stall-fed</conjf>
<pr>(-f<ecr/d`)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stall-feeding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To feed and fatten in a
stall or on dry fodder; <as>as, to <ex>stall-feed</ex> an
ox</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalling</ent><br/
<hw>Stall"ing</hw> <pr>(st<add/l"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Stabling.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stallion</ent><br/
<hw>Stal"lion</hw> <pr>(st<acr/l"y<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stalon</ets>, OF. <ets>estalon</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/talon</ets>,
fr. OHG. <ets>stal</ets> a stable. See <er>Stall</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>A male horse not castrated; a male horse kept
for breeding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stallman</ent><br/
<hw>Stall"man</hw> <pr>(st<add/l"m<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stallmen</plw> <pr>(-m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu>
<def>One who keeps a stall for the sale of merchandise, especially
books.</def> <rj><au>Sterne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stallon</ent><br/
<hw>Stal"lon</hw> <pr>(st<acr/l"l<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A slip
from a plant; a scion; a cutting.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalworth</ent><br/
<ent>Stalwart</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stal"wart</hw> <pr>(st<ocr/l"w<etil/rt <or/ st<add/l"-;
277)</pr>, <hw>Stal"worth</hw> <pr>(-w<etil/rth)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stalworth</ets>, AS.
<ets>staelwyr<edh/</ets> serviceable, probably originally, good at
stealing, or worth stealing or taking, and afterwards extended to other
causes of estimation. See <er>Steal</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>,
<er>Worth</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Brave; bold; strong;
redoubted; daring; vehement; violent.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>stalwart</xex>
tiller of the soil.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Prof. Wilson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fair man he was and wise, <qex>stalworth</qex> and bold.</q>
<rj><qau>R. of Brunne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Stalworth</xex> is now disused, or but little used,
<xex>stalwart</xex> having taken its place.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalwartly</ent><br/
<hw>Stal"wart*ly</hw> <pr>(-w<etil/rt*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In a stalwart manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalwartness</ent><br/
<hw>Stal"wart*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of being
stalwart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stalworthness</ent><br/
<ent>Stalworthhood</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stal"worth*hood</hw> <pr>(-w<etil/rth*h<oocr/d)</pr>,
<hw>Stal"worth*ness</hw> <pr>(-w<etil/rth*n<ecr/s)</pr>}</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being stalworth;
stalwartness; boldness; daring.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamen</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"men</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"m<ecr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> E. <plw>Stamens</plw> <pr>(-m<ecr/nz)</pr> (used only
in the second sense); L. <plw>Stamina</plw>
<pr>(st<acr/m"<icr/*n<adot/)</pr> (in the first sense).</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>stamen</ets> the warp, a thread, fiber, akin to Gr.
<grk>sth`mwn</grk> the warp, fr. <grk>'ista`nai</grk> to stand, akin to
E. <ets>stand</ets>. See <er>Stand</er>, and cf. <er>Stamin</er>,
<er>Stamina</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thread; especially, a warp
thread.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <plu>(<it>pl.</it> <er>Stamens</er>, rarely
<er>Stamina</er>.)</plu> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The male organ of
flowers for secreting and furnishing the pollen or fecundating dust. It
consists of the <part>anther</part> and <part>filament</part>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamened</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"mened</hw> <pr>(-m<ecr/nd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Furnished
with stamens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamin</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"min</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"m<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estamine</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tamine</ets>, LL.
<ets>staminea</ets>, <ets>stamineum</ets>, fr. L. <ets>stamineus</ets>
consisting of threads, fr. <ets>stamen</ets> a thread. See
<er>Stamen</er>, and cf. <er>Stamineous</er>, 2d <er>Stammel</er>,
<er>Tamine</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of woolen cloth.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>stamine</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamina</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"i*na</hw> <pr>(st<acr/m"<icr/*n<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<def>See <er>Stamen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamina</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"i*na</hw>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The fixed, firm
part of a body, which supports it or gives it strength and solidity;
<as>as, the bones are the <ex>stamina</ex> of animal bodies; the
ligneous parts of trees are the <ex>stamina</ex> which constitute their
strength</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Whatever constitutes the principal strength or
support of anything; backbone; vigor; <as>as, the <ex>stamina</ex> of a
constitution or of life; the <ex>stamina</ex> of a State</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He succeeded to great captains who had sapped the whole
<qex>stamina</qex> and resistance of the contest.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>The power of endurance; the
ability to withstand fatigue, disease, deprivation, etc., and continue
working.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staminal</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"i*nal</hw> <pr>(-n<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>staminal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to stamens or stamina;
consisting in stamens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staminate</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"i*nate</hw> <pr>(-n<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>staminatus</ets> consisting of threads, fr. <ets>stamen</ets>
thread: cf. F. <ets>stamin<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Furnished with stamens; producing stamens.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having stamens, but lacking pistils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staminate</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"i*nate</hw> <pr>(-n<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
indue with stamina.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamineous</ent><br/
<ent>Stamineal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sta*min"e*al</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*m<icr/n"<esl/*<ait/l)</pr>,
<hw>Sta*min"e*ous</hw> <pr>(-m<icr/n"<esl/*<ucr/s)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stamineus</ets>, from <ets>stamen</ets>
thread.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Consisting of stamens or
threads.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the stamens;
possessing stamens; also, attached to the stamens; <as>as, a
<ex>stamineous</ex> nectary</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staminiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Stam`i*nif"er*ous</hw>
<pr>(st<acr/m`<icr/*n<icr/f"<etil/r*<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stamen</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Bearing or
having stamens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staminode</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"i*node</hw> <pr>(st<acr/m"<icr/*n<omac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A staminodium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staminodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stam`i*no"di*um</hw> <pr>(-n<omac/"d<icr/*<ucr/m)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Staminodia</plw>
<pr>(-<adot/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Stamen</er>, and
<er>-oid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An abortive stamen, or any
organ modified from an abortive stamen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammel</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mel</hw> <pr>(st<acr/m"m<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
large, clumsy horse.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wright.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammel</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mel</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>estamel</ets>; cf. OF.
<ets>estamet</ets> a coarse woolen cloth, LL. <ets>stameta</ets> a kind
of cloth, the same as <ets>staminea</ets>, and OF. <ets>estame</ets> a
woolen stuff. See <er>Stamin</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of
woolen cloth formerly in use. It seems to have been often of a red
color.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A red dye, used in England in the 15th and 16th
centuries.</def> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammel</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mel</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of the color of stammel; having a
red color, thought inferior to scarlet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammer</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mer</hw> <pr>(st<acr/m"m<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stammered</conjf>
<pr>(-m<etil/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stammering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stameren</ets>, fr.
AS. <ets>stamur</ets>, <ets>stamer</ets>, stammering; akin to D. & LG.
<ets>stameren</ets> to stammer, G. <ets>stammeln</ets>, OHG.
<ets>stammal<omac/n</ets>, <ets>stamm<emac/n</ets>, Dan.
<ets>stamme</ets>, Sw. <ets>stamma</ets>, Icel. <ets>stama</ets>,
<ets>stamma</ets>, OHG. & Dan. <ets>stam</ets> stammering, Icel.
<ets>stamr</ets>, Goth. <ets>stamms</ets>, and to G. <ets>stemmen</ets>
to bear against, <ets>stumm</ets> dumb, D. <ets>stom</ets>. Cf.
<er>Stem</er> to resist, <er>Stumble</er>.]</ety> <def>To make
involuntary stops in uttering syllables or words; to hesitate or falter
in speaking; to speak with stops and difficulty; to stutter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would thou couldst <qex>stammer</qex>, that thou mightest pour
this concealed man out of thy mouth, as wine comes out of a
narrow-mouthed bottle, either too much at once, or none at all.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammer</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mer</hw> <pr>(st<acr/m"m<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
utter or pronounce with hesitation or imperfectly; -- sometimes with
<xex>out</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammer</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Defective utterance, or
involuntary interruption of utterance; a stutter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammerer</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mer*er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
stammers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammering</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mer*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Apt to stammer; hesitating in
speech; stuttering.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Stam"mer*ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stammering</ent><br/
<hw>Stam"mer*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A
disturbance in the formation of sounds. It is due essentially to
long-continued spasmodic contraction of the diaphragm, by which
expiration is prevented, and hence it may be considered as a spasmodic
inspiration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamp</ent><br/
<hw>Stamp</hw> <pr>(st<acr/mp)</pr> <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stamped</conjf> <pr>(st<acr/mt ; 215)</pr>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Stamping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stampen</ets>; akin to LG. & D. <ets>stampen</ets>, G.
<ets>stampfen</ets>, OHG. <ets>stampf<omac/n</ets>, Dan.
<ets>stampe</ets>, Sw. <ets>stampa</ets>, Icel. <ets>stappa</ets>, G.
<ets>stampf</ets> a pestle and E. <ets>step</ets>. See <er>Step</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>, and cf. <er>Stampede</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
strike beat, or press forcibly with the bottom of the foot, or by
thrusting the foot downward.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He frets, he fumes, he stares, he <qex>stamps</qex> the
ground.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bring down (the foot) forcibly on the ground or
floor; <as>as, he <ex>stamped</ex> his foot with rage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To crush; to pulverize; specifically
<fld>(Metal.)</fld>, to crush by the blow of a heavy stamp, as ore in a
mill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I took your sin, the calf which ye had made, and burnt it with
fire, and <qex>stamped</qex> it, and ground it very small.</q>
<rj><qau>Deut. ix. 21.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To impress with some mark or figure; <as>as, to
<ex>stamp</ex> a plate with arms or initials</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Fig.: To impress; to imprint; to fix deeply; <as>as,
to <ex>stamp</ex> virtuous principles on the heart</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God . . . has <qex>stamped</qex> no original characters on our
minds wherein we may read his being.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To cut out, bend, or indent, as paper, sheet metal,
etc., into various forms, by a blow or suddenly applied pressure with a
stamp or die, etc.; to mint; to coin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To put a stamp on, as for postage; <as>as, to
<ex>stamp</ex> a letter; to <ex>stamp</ex> a legal
document</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stamp out</b></col>, <cd>to put an end to by sudden
and energetic action; to extinguish; <as>as, <ex>to stamp out</ex> a
rebellion</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamp</ent><br/
<hw>Stamp</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike; to beat; to
crush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These cooks how they <qex>stamp</qex> and strain and grind.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strike the foot forcibly downward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But starts, exclaims, and <qex>stamps</qex>, and raves, and
dies.</q> <rj><qau>Dennis.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamp</ent><br/
<hw>Stamp</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stamping, as
with the foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The which stamps; any instrument for making
impressions on other bodies, as a die.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is gold so pure<br/
It can not bear the <qex>stamp</qex> without alloy.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The mark made by stamping; a mark imprinted; an
impression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That sacred name gives ornament and grace,<br/
And, like his <qex>stamp</qex>, makes basest metals pass.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which is marked; a thing stamped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hanging a golden <qex>stamp</qex> about their necks.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <ety>[F. <ets>estampe</ets>, of German origin. See
<er>Stamp</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>A picture cut in wood or
metal, or made by impression; a cut; a plate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At Venice they put out very curious <qex>stamps</qex> of the
several edifices which are most famous for their beauty and
magnificence.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An official mark set upon things chargeable with a
duty or tax to government, as evidence that the duty or tax is paid;
<as>as, the <ex>stamp</ex> on a bill of exchange</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <def>A stamped or printed device,
usually paper, issued by the government at a fixed price, and required
by law to be affixed to, or stamped on, certain papers, as evidence that
the government dues are paid; <as>as, a postage <ex>stamp</ex>; a tax
<ex>stamp</ex>; a receipt <ex>stamp</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>An instrument for cutting out, or shaping,
materials, as paper, leather, etc., by a downward pressure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A character or reputation, good or bad, fixed on
anything as if by an imprinted mark; current value; authority; <as>as,
these persons have the <ex>stamp</ex> of dishonesty; the Scriptures bear
the <ex>stamp</ex> of a divine origin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of the same <qex>stamp</qex> is that which is obtruded on us, that
an adamant suspends the attraction of the loadstone.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>Make; cast; form; character; <as>as, a man of the
same <ex>stamp</ex>, or of a different <ex>stamp</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A soldier of this season's <qex>stamp</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A kind of heavy hammer, or pestle, raised by water
or steam power, for beating ores to powder; anything like a pestle, used
for pounding or beating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>A half-penny.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Money, esp. paper money.</def>
<mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stamp act</b></col>, <cd>an act of the British Parliament
[1765] imposing a duty on all paper, vellum, and parchment used in the
American colonies, and declaring all writings on unstamped materials to
be null and void.</cd> -- <col><b>Stamp collector</b></col>,
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>an officer who receives or collects stamp duties.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>one who collects postage or other stamps, as an
avocation or for investment; a philatelist.</cd> -- <col><b>Stamp
duty</b></col>, <cd>a duty, or tax, imposed on paper and parchment used
for certain writings, as deeds, conveyances, etc., the evidence of the
payment of the duty or tax being a stamp.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Stamp hammer</b></col>, <cd>a hammer, worked by power, which
rises and falls vertically, like a stamp in a stamp mill.</cd> --
<col><b>Stamp head</b></col>, <cd>a heavy mass of metal, forming the
head or lower end of a bar, which is lifted and let fall, in a stamp
mill.</cd> -- <col><b>Stamp mill</b></col> <fld>(Mining)</fld>, <cd>a
mill in which ore is crushed with stamps; also, a machine for stamping
ore.</cd> -- <col><b>Stamp note</b></col>, <cd>a stamped certificate
from a customhouse officer, which allows goods to be received by the
captain of a ship as freight.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stamp
office</b></col>, <cd>an office for the issue of stamps and the
reception of stamp duties.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stampede</ent><br/
<hw>Stam*pede"</hw> <pr>(st<acr/m*p<emac/d")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Sp. <ets>estampida</ets> (in America) a stampede,
<ets>estampido</ets> a crackling, akin to <ets>estampar</ets> to stamp,
of German origin. See <er>Stamp</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A wild, headlong scamper, or running away, of a number
of animals; usually caused by fright; hence, any sudden flight or
dispersion, as of a crowd or an army in consequence of a
panic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any sudden unconcerted moving or acting together of
a number of persons, as from some common impulse; <as>as, a
<ex>stampede</ex> to the gold regions; a <ex>stampede</ex> in a
convention</as>; a <ex>stampede</ex> toward U. S. bonds in the credit
markets.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><q>She and her husband would join in the general
<qex>stampede</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>W. Black.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1402 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Stampede</ent><br/
<hw>Stam*pede"</hw> <pr>(st<acr/m*p<emac/d")</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<def>To run away in a panic; -- said of droves of cattle, horses, etc.,
also of armies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stampede</ent><br/
<hw>Stam*pede"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To disperse by causing sudden
fright, as a herd or drove of animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamper</ent><br/
<hw>Stamp"er</hw> <pr>(st<acr/mp"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who stamps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument for pounding or stamping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stamping</ent><br/
<hw>Stamp"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Stamp</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stamping ground</b></col>, <cd>a place frequented, and
much trodden, by animals, wild or domesticated</cd>; hence
(<mark>Colloq.</mark>), <cd>the scene of one's labors or exploits; also,
one's favorite resort; in this sense, often called <altname>stomping
ground</altname>.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stamping
machine</b></col>, <cd>a machine for forming metallic articles or
impressions by stamping.</cd> -- <col><b>Stamping mill</b></col>
<fld>(Mining)</fld>, <cd>a stamp mill.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stance</ent><br/
<hw>Stance</hw> <pr>(st<acr/ns)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estance</ets>. See <er>Stanza</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
stanza.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A station; a position; a site.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Golf, Baseball)</fld> <def>The position of a
player's feet, relative to each other and to the ball, when he is making
a stroke or at bat.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>the mental attitude, position, or approach a person
adopts in respect to something.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanch</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch</hw> <pr>(st<adot/nch)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stanched</conjf>
<pr>(st<adot/ncht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stanching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>estanchier</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/tancher</ets> to stop a liquid from flowing; akin to Pr.,
Sp., & Pg. <ets>estancar</ets>, It. <ets>stancare</ets> to weary, LL.
<ets>stancare</ets>, <ets>stagnare</ets>, to stanch, fr. L.
<ets>stagnare</ets> to be or make stagnant. See
<er>Stagnate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To stop the flowing of, as
blood; to check; also, to stop the flowing of blood from; <as>as, to
<ex>stanch</ex> a wound</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>staunch</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Iron or a stone laid to the neck doth <qex>stanch</qex> the
bleeding of the nose.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To extinguish; to quench, as fire or thirst.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanch</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To cease, as the flowing of
blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Immediately her issue of blood <qex>stanched</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Luke viii. 44.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanch</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which stanches or
checks.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A flood gate by which water is accumulated, for
floating a boat over a shallow part of a stream by its release.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanch</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stancher</adjf> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stanchest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Stanch</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, and hence literally signifying, stopped or stayed; cf. Sp.
<ets>estanco</ets> stopped, tight, not leaky, as a ship. See
<er>Stanch</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>staunch</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Strong and tight; sound;
firm; <as>as, a <ex>stanch</ex> ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One of the closets is parqueted with plain deal, set in diamond,
exceeding <qex>stanch</qex> and pretty.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Firm in principle; constant and zealous; loyal;
hearty; steady; steadfast; <as>as, a <ex>stanch</ex> churchman; a
<ex>stanch</ex> friend or adherent</as>.</def> <rj><au>V.
Knox.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In politics I hear you 're <qex>stanch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Close; secret; private.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is to be kept <qex>stanch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanch</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To prop; to make stanch, or
strong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His gathered sticks to <qex>stanch</qex> the wall<br/
Of the snow tower when snow should fall.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanchel</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"chel</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n"sh<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
stanchion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stancher</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch"er</hw> <pr>(st<adot/nch"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One who, or that which, stanches, or stops, the flowing, as of
blood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanchion</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"chion</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n"sh<ucr/n ; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OF. <ets>estanson</ets>, <ets>estan<cced/on</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/tan<cced/on</ets>, from OF. <ets>estance</ets> a stay, a
prop, from L. <ets>stans</ets>, <ets>stantis</ets>, standing, p. pr. of
<ets>stare</ets> to stand. See <er>Stand</er>, and cf.
<er>Stanza</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stanchel</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A prop
or support; a piece of timber in the form of a stake or post, used for a
support or stay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Any upright post or beam used as
a support, as for the deck, the quarter rails, awnings, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A vertical bar for confining cattle in a
stall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanchless</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch"less</hw> <pr>(st<adot/nch"l<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Incapable of being stanched, or stopped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unquenchable; insatiable.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanchly</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stanch manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanchness</ent><br/
<hw>Stanch"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stanch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stand</ent><br/
<hw>Stand</hw> <pr>(st<acr/nd)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stood</conjf> <pr>(st<oocr/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Standing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>standen</ets>; AS. <ets>standan</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>stonda</ets>, <ets>st<amac/n</ets>, D. <ets>staan</ets>, OS.
<ets>standan</ets>, <ets>st<amac/n</ets>, OHG. <ets>stantan</ets>,
<ets>st<amac/n</ets>, G. <ets>stehen</ets>, Icel. <ets>standa</ets>,
Dan. <ets>staae</ets>, Sw. <ets>st<aring/</ets>, Goth.
<ets>standan</ets>, Russ. <ets>stoiate</ets>, L. <ets>stare</ets>, Gr.
<grk>'ista`nai</grk> to cause to stand, <grk>sth^nai</grk> to stand,
Skr. <ets>sth<amac/</ets>. <root/163. Cf. <er>Assist</er>,
<er>Constant</er>, <er>Contrast</er>, <er>Desist</er>, <er>Destine</er>,
<er>Ecstasy</er>, <er>Exist</er>, <er>Interstice</er>,
<er>Obstacle</er>, <er>Obstinate</er>, <er>Prest</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Rest</er> remainder, <er>Solstice</er>, <er>Stable</er>, <pos>a. &
n.</pos>, <er>Staff</er>, <er>Stage</er>, <er>Stall</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<er>Stamen</er>, <er>Stanchion</er>, <er>Stanza</er>, <er>State</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, <er>Statute</er>, <er>Stead</er>, <er>Steed</er>,
<er>Stool</er>, <er>Stud</er> of horses, <er>Substance</er>,
<er>System</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be at rest in an erect
position; to be fixed in an upright or firm position</def>; as:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>To be supported on the feet, in an erect or nearly
erect position; -- opposed to <contr>lie</contr>, <contr>sit</contr>,
<contr>kneel</contr>, etc.</def> <ldquo/I pray you all, <xex>stand</xex>
up!<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To continue upright in a
certain locality, as a tree fixed by the roots, or a building resting on
its foundation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It <qex>stands</qex> as it were to the ground yglued.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ruined wall<br/
<qex>Stands</qex> when its wind-worn battlements are gone.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To occupy or hold a place; to have a situation; to
be situated or located; <as>as, Paris <ex>stands</ex> on the
Seine</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wite ye not where there <qex>stands</qex> a little town?</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cease from progress; not to proceed; to stop; to
pause; to halt; to remain stationary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I charge thee, <qex>stand</qex>,<br/
And tell thy name.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The star, which they saw in the east, went before them, till it
came and <qex>stood</qex> over where the young child was.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. ii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To remain without ruin or injury; to hold good
against tendencies to impair or injure; to be permanent; to endure; to
last; hence, to find endurance, strength, or resources.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My mind on its own center <qex>stands</qex> unmoved.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To maintain one's ground; to be acquitted; not to
fail or yield; to be safe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Readers by whose judgment I would <qex>stand</qex> or fall.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To maintain an invincible or permanent attitude; to
be fixed, steady, or firm; to take a position in resistance or
opposition.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>standing</xex> pattern of their
imitation.<rdquo/ <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king granted the Jews . . . to gather themselves together, and
to <qex>stand</qex> for their life.</q> <rj><qau>Esther viii.
11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To adhere to fixed principles; to maintain moral
rectitude; to keep from falling into error or vice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We must labor so as to <qex>stand</qex> with godliness, according
to his appointment.</q> <rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To have or maintain a position, order, or rank; to
be in a particular relation; <as>as, Christian charity, or love,
<ex>stands</ex> first in the rank of gifts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To be in some particular state; to have essence or
being; to be; to consist.</def> <ldquo/Sacrifices . . . which
<xex>stood</xex> only in meats and drinks.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Heb. ix.
10.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Accomplish what your signs foreshow;<br/
I <qex>stand</qex> resigned, and am prepared to go.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou seest how it <qex>stands</qex> with me, and that I may not
tarry.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To be consistent; to agree; to accord.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Doubt me not; by heaven, I will do nothing<br/
But what may <qex>stand</qex> with honor.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To hold a course at sea; <as>as,
to <ex>stand</ex> from the shore; to <ex>stand</ex> for the
harbor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From the same parts of heaven his navy <qex>stands</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>To offer one's self, or to be offered, as a
candidate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>stood</qex> to be elected one of the proctors of the
university.</q> <rj><qau>Walton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>To stagnate; not to flow; to be
motionless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Or the black water of Pomptina <qex>stands</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>To measure when erect on the feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Six feet two, as I think, he <qex>stands</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To be or remain as it
is; to continue in force; to have efficacy or validity; to abide.</def>
<au>Bouvier.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To appear in court.</def>
<au>Burrill.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>To be, or signify that
one is, willing to play with one's hand as dealt.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stand by</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a preparatory
order, equivalent to <altname>Be ready</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>To
stand against</b></col>, <cd>to oppose; to resist.</cd> -- <col><b>To
stand by</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To be near; to be a spectator; to
be present</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To be aside; to be set aside with
disregard</cd>. <ldquo/In the interim [we] let the commands <xex>stand
by</xex> neglected.<rdquo/ <au>Dr. H. More.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To
maintain; to defend; to support; not to desert; <as>as, <ex>to stand
by</ex> one's principles or party</as>.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To rest on
for support; to be supported by</cd>. <au>Whitgift.</au> <sd>(e)</sd>
<cd>To remain as a spectator, and take no part in an action; <as>as, we
can't just <ex>stand</ex> idly <ex>by</ex> while people are being
killed</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand corrected</b></col>, <cd>to be set
right, as after an error in a statement of fact; to admit having been in
error.</cd> <au>Wycherley.</au> -- <col><b>To stand fast</b></col>,
<cd>to be fixed; to be unshaken or immovable.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand
firmly on</b></col>, <cd>to be satisfied or convinced of.</cd>
<ldquo/Though Page be a secure fool, and <xex>stands</xex> so
<xex>firmly on</xex> his wife's frailty.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> --
<col><b>To stand for</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To side with; to
espouse the cause of; to support; to maintain, or to profess or attempt
to maintain; to defend.</cd> <ldquo/I <xex>stand</xex> wholly
<xex>for</xex> you.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To be in the
place of; to be the substitute or representative of; to represent;
<as>as, a cipher at the left hand of a figure <ex>stands for</ex>
nothing</as>.</cd> <ldquo/I will not trouble myself, whether these names
<xex>stand for</xex> the same thing, or really include one
another.<rdquo/ <au>Locke.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To tolerate; <as>as, I
won't <ex>stand for</ex> any delay</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand
in</b></col>, <cd>to cost.</cd> <ldquo/The same <xex>standeth</xex> them
<xex>in</xex> much less cost.<rdquo/ <au>Robynson (More's
Utopia).</au><br/
<br/
<q>The Punic wars could not have <qex>stood</qex> the human race
<qex>in</qex> less than three millions of the species.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
<br/
-- <col><b>To stand in hand</b></col>, <cd>to conduce to one's interest;
to be serviceable or advantageous.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand
off</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To keep at a distance</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>Not to comply</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To keep at a distance in
friendship, social intercourse, or acquaintance</cd>. <sd>(d)</sd>
<cd>To appear prominent; to have relief</cd>. <ldquo/Picture is best
when it <xex>standeth off</xex>, as if it were carved.<rdquo/ <au>Sir H.
Wotton.</au> -- <col><b>To stand off and on</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to remain near a coast by sailing toward land
and then from it.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand on</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to continue on the same tack or course.</cd> --
<col><b>To stand out</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To project; to be
prominent</cd>. <ldquo/Their eyes <xex>stand out</xex> with
fatness.<rdquo/ <au>Psalm lxxiii. 7.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To persist in
opposition or resistance; not to yield or comply; not to give way or
recede.</cd><br/
<br/
<q>His spirit is come in,<br/
That so <qex>stood out</qex> against the holy church.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
<br/
-- <col><b>To stand to</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To ply; to urge; to
persevere in using</cd>. <ldquo/<xex>Stand to</xex> your tackles, mates,
and stretch your oars.<rdquo/ <au>Dryden.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
remain fixed in a purpose or opinion.</cd> <ldquo/I will <xex>stand
to</xex> it, that this is his sense.<rdquo/ <au>Bp. Stillingfleet.</au>
<sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To abide by; to adhere to; as to a contract, assertion,
promise, etc.; <as>as, <ex>to stand to</ex> an award; <ex>to stand
to</ex> one's word</as>.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>Not to yield; not to fly;
to maintain, as one's ground</cd>. <ldquo/Their lives and fortunes were
put in safety, whether they <xex>stood to</xex> it or ran away.<rdquo/
<au>Bacon.</au> <sd>(e)</sd> <cd>To be consistent with; to agree with;
<as>as, it <ex>stands to</ex> reason that he could not have done
so</as>; same as <cref>stand with</cref>, below .</cd> <sd>(f)</sd>
<cd>To support; to uphold</cd>. <ldquo/<xex>Stand to</xex> me in this
cause.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>To stand together</b></col>,
<cd>to be consistent; to agree.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand to
reason</b></col> <cd>to be reasonable; to be expected.</cd> --
<col><b>To stand to sea</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to direct the
course from land.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand under</b></col>, <cd>to
undergo; to withstand.</cd> <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>To stand
up</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To rise from sitting; to be on the
feet</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To arise in order to speak or act</cd>.
<ldquo/Against whom, when the accusers <xex>stood up</xex>, they brought
none accusation of such things as I supposed.<rdquo/ <au>Acts xxv.
18.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To rise and stand on end, as the hair.</cd>
<sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To put one's self in opposition; to contend</cd>.
<ldquo/Once we <xex>stood up</xex> about the corn.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au>
-- <col><b>To stand up for</b></col>, <cd>to defend; to justify; to
support, or attempt to support; <as>as, <ex>to stand up for</ex> the
administration</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand upon</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To concern; to interest</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
value; to esteem</cd>. <ldquo/We highly esteem and <xex>stand</xex> much
<xex>upon</xex> our birth.<rdquo/ <au>Ray.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To
insist on; to attach much importance to; <as>as, <ex>to stand upon</ex>
security; <ex>to stand upon</ex> ceremony</as>.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To
attack; to assault.</cd> <mark>[A Hebraism]</mark> <ldquo/So I
<xex>stood upon</xex> him, and slew him.<rdquo/ <au>2 Sam. i. 10.</au>
-- <col><b>To stand with</b></col>, <cd>to be consistent with.</cd>
<ldquo/It <xex>stands with</xex> reason that they should be rewarded
liberally.<rdquo/ <au>Sir J. Davies.</au></cs><-- usu. . --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stand</ent><br/
<hw>Stand</hw> <pr>(st<acr/nd)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To endure; to sustain; to bear; <as>as, I can not <ex>stand</ex>
the cold or the heat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To resist, without yielding or receding; to
withstand.</def> <ldquo/Love <xex>stood</xex> the siege.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>stood</qex> the furious foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To abide by; to submit to; to suffer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bid him disband his legions, . . . <br/
And <qex>stand</qex> the judgment of a Roman senate.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To set upright; to cause to stand; <as>as, to
<ex>stand</ex> a book on the shelf; to <ex>stand</ex> a man on his
feet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To be at the expense of; to pay for; <as>as, to
<ex>stand</ex> a treat</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stand fire</b></col>, <cd>to receive the fire of arms
from an enemy without giving way.</cd> -- <col><b>To stand one's
ground</b></col>, <cd>to keep the ground or station one has taken; to
maintain one's position.</cd> <ldquo/Peasants and burghers, however
brave, are unable <xex>to stand their ground</xex> against veteran
soldiers.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au> -- <col><b>To stand trial</b></col>,
<cd>to sustain the trial or examination of a cause; not to give up
without trial.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stand</ent><br/
<hw>Stand</hw> <pr>(st<acr/nd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stand</ets>. See <er>Stand</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of standing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I took my <qex>stand</qex> upon an eminence . . . to look into
their several ladings.</q> <rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A halt or stop for the purpose of defense,
resistance, or opposition; <as>as, to come to, or to make, a
<ex>stand</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Vice is at <qex>stand</qex>, and at the highest flow.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A place or post where one stands; a place where one
may stand while observing or waiting for something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have found you out a <qex>stand</qex> most fit,<br/
Where you may have such vantage on the duke,<br/
He shall not pass you.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A station in a city or town where carriages or
wagons stand for hire; <as>as, a cab <ex>stand</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dickens.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A raised platform or station where a race or other
outdoor spectacle may be viewed; <as>as, the judge's or the grand
<ex>stand</ex> at a race course</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A small table; also, something on or in which
anything may be laid, hung, or placed upright; <as>as, a
hat<ex>stand</ex>; an umbrella <ex>stand</ex>; a music
<ex>stand</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The place where a witness stands to testify in
court.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>The situation of a shop, store, hotel, etc.; <as>as,
a good, bad, or convenient <ex>stand</ex> for business</as>.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Rank; post; station; standing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Father, since your fortune did attain<br/
So high a <qex>stand</qex>, I mean not to descend.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A state of perplexity or embarrassment; <as>as, to
be at a <ex>stand</ex> what to do</as>.</def>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A young tree, usually reserved when other trees are
cut; also, a tree growing or standing upon its own root, in distinction
from one produced from a scion set in a stock, either of the same or
another kind of tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>A weight of from two hundred and
fifty to three hundred pounds, -- used in weighing pitch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Microscope stand</b></col>, <cd>the instrument, excepting
the eyepiece, objective, and other removable optical parts.</cd> --
<col><b>Stand of ammunition</b></col>, <cd>the projectile, cartridge,
and sabot connected together.</cd> -- <col><b>Stand of arms</b></col>.
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Arms</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stand of
colors</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a single color, or flag.</cd>
<au>Wilhelm (Mil. Dict.)</au> -- <col><b>To be at a stand</b></col>,
<cd>to be stationary or motionless; to be at a standstill; hence, to be
perplexed; to be embarrassed.</cd> -- <col><b>To make a stand</b></col>,
<cd>to halt for the purpose of offering resistance to a pursuing
enemy.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Stop; halt; rest; interruption; obstruction;
perplexity; difficulty; embarrassment; hesitation.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standage</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"age</hw> <pr>(-<asl/j ; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A reservoir in which water accumulates at the
bottom of a mine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standard</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"ard</hw> <pr>(-<etil/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estendart</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tendard</ets>, probably fr. L.
<ets>extendere</ets> to spread out, extend, but influenced by E.
<ets>stand</ets>. See <er>Extend</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A flag;
colors; a banner; especially, a national or other ensign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His armies, in the following day,<br/
On those fair plains their <qex>standards</qex> proud display.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is established by authority as a rule for
the measure of quantity, extent, value, or quality; esp., the original
specimen weight or measure sanctioned by government, as the standard
pound, gallon, or yard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is established as a rule or model by
authority, custom, or general consent; criterion; test.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The court, which used to be the <qex>standard</qex> of propriety
and correctness of speech.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A disposition to preserve, and an ability to improve, taken
together, would be my <qex>standard</qex> of a statesman.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Coinage)</fld> <def>The proportion of weights of
fine metal and alloy established by authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By the present <qex>standard</qex> of the coinage, sixty-two
shillings is coined out of one pound weight of silver.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Hort.)</fld> <def>A tree of natural size supported
by its own stem, and not dwarfed by grafting on the stock of a smaller
species nor trained upon a wall or trellis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In France part of their gardens is laid out for flowers, others
for fruits; some <qex>standards</qex>, some against walls.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The upper petal or banner of a
papilionaceous corolla.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mech. & Carp.)</fld> <def>An upright support, as
one of the poles of a scaffold; any upright in framing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>An inverted knee timber
placed upon the deck instead of beneath it, with its vertical branch
turned upward from that which lies horizontally.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>The sheth of a plow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A large drinking cup.</def>
<rj><au>Greene.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Standard bearer</b></col>, <cd>an officer of an army,
company, or troop, who bears a standard; -- commonly called <xex>color
sergeant</xex>or <xex>color bearer</xex>; hence, the leader of any
organization; <as>as, the <ex>standard bearer</ex> of a political
party</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standard</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"ard</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Being, affording, or
according with, a standard for comparison and judgment; <as>as,
<ex>standard</ex> time; <ex>standard</ex> weights and measures; a
<ex>standard</ex> authority as to nautical terms; <ex>standard</ex> gold
or silver</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: Having a recognized and permanent value;
<as>as, <ex>standard</ex> works in history; <ex>standard</ex>
authors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Hort.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Not supported by, or
fastened to, a wall; <as>as, <ex>standard</ex> fruit trees</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Not of the dwarf kind; <as>as, a <ex>standard</ex>
pear tree</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Standard candle</b></col>, <col><b>Standard
gauge</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Candle</er>, and
<er>Gauge</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Standard solution</b></col>.
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Standardized solution</cref>, under
<er>Solution</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standard-bred</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"ard-bred`</hw> <pr>(-br<ecr/d`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bred
in conformity to a standard. Specif., applied to a registered trotting
horse which comes up to the standard adopted by the <org>National
Association of Trotting-horse Breeders</org>.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standardize</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"ard*ize</hw> <pr>(-<imac/z)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To reduce to a normal standard; to calculate or
adjust the strength of, by means of, and for uses in,
analysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standard-wing</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"ard-wing`</hw> <pr>(st<acr/nd"<etil/rd-w<icr/ng`)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A curious paradise bird
(<spn>Semioptera Wallacii</spn>) which has two long special feathers
standing erect on each wing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1403 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Stand-by</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"-by`</hw> <pr>(st<acr/nd"b<imac/`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One who, or that which, stands by one in need; something upon which
one relies for constant use or in an emergency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standel</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"el</hw> <pr>(-<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A young tree,
especially one reserved when others are cut.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stander</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who stands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Standel</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ascham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staenderat</ent><br/
<ent>Staenderat</ent><br/
<ent>Standerath</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>St<aum/n"de*rath`</hw>, <or/ <hw>St<aum/n"de*rat`</hw>
<pr>(sht<ecr/n"d<etil/*r<aum/t`)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[G.]</ety> <fld>(Switzerland)</fld> <def>The legislature of
<country>Switzerland</country>. See <er>Legislature</er>,
above.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stander-by</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"er-by`</hw> <pr>(-b<imac/`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
stands near; one who is present; a bystander.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standergrass</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"er*grass`</hw> <pr>(-gr<adot/s`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant (<spn>Orchis mascula</spn>); -- called
also <altname>standerwort</altname>, and <altname>long purple</altname>.
See <cref>Long purple</cref>, under <er>Long</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standgale</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"gale`</hw> <pr>(-g<amac/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Stannel</er>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standing</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Remaining erect; not
cut down; <as>as, <ex>standing</ex> corn</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not flowing; stagnant; <as>as, <ex>standing</ex>
water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not transitory; not liable to fade or vanish;
lasting; <as>as, a <ex>standing</ex> color</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Established by law, custom, or the like; settled;
continually existing; permanent; not temporary; <as>as, a
<ex>standing</ex> army; legislative bodies have <ex>standing</ex> rules
of proceeding and <ex>standing</ex> committees</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Not movable; fixed; <as>as, a <ex>standing</ex> bed
(distinguished from a <ex>trundle</ex>-bed)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Standing army</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Standing
army</cref>, under <er>Army</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Standing
bolt</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Stud bolt</cref>, under <er>Stud</er>, a
stem.</cd> -- <col><b>Standing committee</b></col>, <cd>in legislative
bodies, etc., a committee appointed for the consideration of all
subjects of a particular class which shall arise during the session or a
stated period.</cd> -- <col><b>Standing cup</b></col>, <cd>a tall
goblet, with a foot and a cover.</cd> -- <col><b>Standing
finish</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>that part of the interior
fittings, esp. of a dwelling house, which is permanent and fixed in its
place, as distinguished from doors, sashes, etc.</cd> --
<col><b>Standing order</b></col> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>,
<cd>the denomination (Congregational) established by law; -- a term
formerly used in Connecticut. See also under <er>Order</er>.</cd>
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <cd>an order for goods which are to be
delivered periodically, without the need for renewal of the order before
each delivery.</cd> -- <col><b>Standing part</b></col>.
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>That part of a tackle which is made
fast to a block, point, or other object</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>That part
of a rope around which turns are taken with the running part in making a
knot or the like.</cd> -- <col><b>Standing rigging</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the cordage or ropes which sustain the masts and
remain fixed in their position, as the shrouds and stays, --
distinguished from <contr>running rigging</contr>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standing</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stopping,
or coming to a stand; the state of being erect upon the feet;
stand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Maintenance of position; duration; duration or
existence in the same place or condition; continuance; <as>as, a custom
of long <ex>standing</ex>; an officer of long
<ex>standing</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An ancient thing of long <qex>standing</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Place to stand in; station; stand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will provide you a good <qex>standing</qex> to see his
entry.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I sink in deep mire, where there is no <qex>standing</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ps. lxix. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Condition in society; relative position; reputation;
rank; <as>as, a man of good <ex>standing</ex>, or of high
<ex>standing</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Standing off</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>sailing
from the land.</cd> -- <col><b>Standing on</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>sailing toward land.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standish</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"ish</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Stand</ets> +
<ets>dish</ets>.]</ety> <def>A stand, or case, for pen and
ink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I bequeath to Dean Swift, Esq., my large silver
<qex>standish</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>standoff</ent><br/
<hw>stand"off</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A situation in a
contest in which the winner is undecided; a draw or stalemate.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> draw; tie; stalemate; deadlock</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A successful stand.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> repulsion, repelling</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something that serves to keep one object at a
distance from another, such as a prop attached to the top of a ladder to
keep it from leaning directly against the vertical surface which
supports it.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>standoff</ent><br/
<hw>stand"off</hw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Serving to keep two objects or
parts of a machine separated; <as>as, a <ex>standoff</ex>
insulator</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>stand-offish</ent><br/
<ent>standoffish</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>stand*off"ish</hw>, <hw>stand-off"ish</hw></mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Shy or distant in manner; cold; aloof; reserved; not
friendly.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> offish.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>stand*off"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>stand*off"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standpipe</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"pipe`</hw> <pr>(-p<imac/p`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Engin.)</fld> <def>A vertical pipe, open at the top, between a
hydrant and a reservoir, to equalize the flow of water; also, a large
vertical pipe, near a pumping engine, into which water is forced up, so
as to give it sufficient head to rise to the required level at a
distance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Steam Boiler)</fld> <def>A supply pipe of
sufficient elevation to enable the water to flow into the boiler,
notwithstanding the pressure of the steam.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standpoint</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"point`</hw> <pr>(-point`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>standpunkt</ets>.]</ety> <def>A fixed point or station; a basis or
fundamental principle; a position from which objects or principles are
viewed, and according to which they are compared and judged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>stands</ent><br/
<hw>stands</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a structure (often made of wood and
sometimes temporary) with seats or benches where people can sit to watch
an event (such as a game or parade).</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> stand</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 1.5</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Standstill</ent><br/
<hw>Stand"still`</hw> <pr>(-st<icr/l`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
standing without moving forward or backward; a stop; a state of
rest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stane</ent><br/
<hw>Stane</hw> <pr>(st<amac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stone.</def>
<mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stang</ent><br/
<hw>Stang</hw> <pr>(st<acr/ng)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Sting</er>.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stang</ent><br/
<hw>Stang</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stange</ets>, of Scand. or
Dutch origin; cf. Icel. <ets>st<oum/ng</ets>, akin to Dan.
<ets>stang</ets>, Sw. <ets>st<aring/ng</ets>, D. <ets>stang</ets>, G.
<ets>stange</ets>, OHG. <ets>stanga</ets>, AS. <ets>steng</ets>; from
the root of E. <ets>sting</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long bar; a
pole; a shaft; a stake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In land measure, a pole, rod, or perch.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stang ball</b></col>, <cd>a projectile consisting of two
half balls united by a bar; a bar shot. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<cref>Bar shot</cref>, under <er>Bar</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To ride the
stang</b></col>, <cd>to be carried on a pole on men's shoulders. This
method of punishing wife beaters, etc., was once in vogue in some parts
of England.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stang</ent><br/
<hw>Stang</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Akin to <ets>sting</ets>; cf.
Icel. <ets>stanga</ets> to prick, to goad.]</ety> <def>To shoot with
pain.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanhope</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"hope</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n"h<omac/p ; <mark>colloq.</mark>
st<acr/n"<ucr/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A light two-wheeled, or
sometimes four-wheeled, carriage, without a top; -- so called from Lord
<ets>Stanhope</ets>, for whom it was contrived.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staniel</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"iel</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n"y<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <er>Stannel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanielry</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"iel*ry</hw> <pr>(-r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Hawking with
staniels, or stannels, -- a base kind of falconry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stank</ent><br/
<hw>Stank</hw> <pr>(st<acr/<nsm/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estanc</ets>, or It. <ets>stanco</ets>. See <er>Stanch</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Weak; worn out.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stank</ent><br/
<hw>Stank</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Sw. <ets>st<aring/nka</ets>
to pant. <root/165.]</ety> <def>To sigh.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stank</ent><br/
<hw>Stank</hw>, <pos>imp.</pos> <mord>of <er>Stink</er></mord>.
<def>Stunk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stank</ent><br/
<hw>Stank</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>estang</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/tang</ets>, from L. <ets>stagnum</ets> a pool. Cf.
<er>Stagnate</er>, <er>Tank</er> a cistern.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Water retained by an embankment; a pool of water.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Robert of
Brunne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A dam or mound to stop water.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stank hen</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the moor hen;
-- called also <altname>stankie</altname>.</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannary</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"na*ry</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n"n<adot/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>stannum</ets> tin, an alloy of silver and lead.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to tin mines, or tin works.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>stannary</qex> courts of Devonshire and Cornwall, for the
administration of justice among the tinners therein, are also courts of
record.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannary</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"na*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stannaries</plw> <pr>(-r<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL.
<ets>stannaria</ets>.]</ety> <def>A tin mine; tin works.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannate</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"nate</hw> <pr>(-n<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stannate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of stannic
acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannel</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"nel</hw> <pr>(-n<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>st<amac/ngella</ets>, <ets>stangilla</ets>; properly, stone yeller,
<it>i. e.</it>, a bird that yells from the rocks. See <er>Stone</er>,
and <er>Yell</er>, and cf. <er>Stonegall</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The kestrel; -- called also <altname>standgale</altname>,
<altname>standgall</altname>, <altname>stanchel</altname>,
<altname>stand hawk</altname>, <altname>stannel hawk</altname>,
<altname>steingale</altname>, <altname>stonegall</altname>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>staniel</asp>, <asp>stannyel</asp>, and
<asp>stanyel</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With what wing the <qex>staniel</qex> checks at it.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannic</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"nic</hw> <pr>(-n<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stannum</ets> tin: cf. F. <ets>stannique</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to tin; derived from or
containing tin; specifically, designating those compounds in which the
element has a higher valence as contrasted with <contr>stannous</contr>
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stannic acid</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A hypothetical
substance, <chform>Sn(OH)4</chform>, analogous to silicic acid, and
called also <altname>normal stannic acid</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>Metastannic acid.</cd> -- <col><b>Stannic chloride</b></col>, <cd>a
thin, colorless, fuming liquid, <chform>SnCl4</chform>, used as a
mordant in calico printing and dyeing; -- formerly called
<altname>spirit of tin</altname>, or <altname>fuming liquor of
Libavius</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stannic oxide</b></col>, <cd>tin
oxide, <chform>SnO2</chform>, produced artificially as a white amorphous
powder, and occurring naturally in the mineral cassiterite. It is used
in the manufacture of white enamels, and, under the name of
<altname>putty powder</altname>, for polishing glass, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Stan*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n*n<icr/f"<etil/r*<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stannum</ets> tin +
<ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Containing or affording tin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannite</ent><br/
<ent>Stannine</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stan"nine</hw> <pr>(-n<icr/n)</pr>, <hw>Stan"nite</hw>
<pr>(-n<imac/t)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
mineral of a steel-gray or iron-black color; tin pyrites. It is a
sulphide of tin, copper, and iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanno-</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"no-</hw> <pr>(-n<osl/-)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>stannum</ets>
tin.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A combining form (also used
adjectively) denoting <sig>relating to tin</sig>, or <sig>connected with
tin</sig>, or indicating <sig>containing tin as an
ingredient</sig>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannofluoride</ent><br/
<hw>Stan`no*flu"or*ide</hw> <pr>(-fl<umac/"<ocr/r*<icr/d <it>or</it>
-<imac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any one of a
series of double fluorides of tin (<it>stannum</it>) and some other
element.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannoso-</ent><br/
<hw>Stan*no"so-</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n*n<omac/"s<osl/-)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A combining form (also used adjectively)
denoting <sig>relation to stannous compounds</sig>, or <sig>connection
with stannous compounds</sig>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannotype</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"no*type</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n"n<osl/*t<imac/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stanno-</ets> + <ets>-type</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Photog.)</fld>
<def>A photograph taken upon a tin plate; a tintype.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannous</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"nous</hw> <pr>(-n<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or containing, tin; specifically, designating those
compounds in which the element has a lower valence as contrasted with
<contr>stannic</contr> compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stannous chloride</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
white crystalline substance, <chform>SnCl2.(H2O)2</chform>, obtained by
dissolving tin in hydrochloric acid. It is used as a mordant in
dyeing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stannum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stan"num</hw> <pr>(-n<ucr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., alloy
of silver and lead; later, tin.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The
technical name of tin. See <er>Tin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanyel</ent><br/
<ent>Stannyel</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stann"yel</hw>, <hw>Stan"yel</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(st<acr/n"y<ecr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Stannel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stont</ent><br/
<ent>Stant</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stant</hw> <pr>(st<aum/nt)</pr>, <hw>Stont</hw>
<pr>(st<ocr/nt)</pr>, }</mhw> <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>3d pers. sing.
pres.</pos> <mord>of <er>Stand</er>, for <xex>standeth</xex></mord>.
<def>Stands.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanza</ent><br/
<hw>Stan"za</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n"z<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stanzas</plw> <pr>(-z<adot/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[It. <ets>stanza</ets> a room, habitation, a stanza, <it>i.
e.</it>, a stop, fr. L. <ets>stans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>stare</ets> to
stand. See <er>Stand</er>, and cf. <er>Estancia</er>, <er>Stance</er>,
<er>Stanchion</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A number of lines or verses
forming a division of a song or poem, and agreeing in meter, rhyme,
number of lines, etc., with other divisions; a part of a poem,
ordinarily containing every variation of measure in that poem; a
combination or arrangement of lines usually recurring, whether like or
unlike, in measure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Horace confines himself strictly to one sort of verse, or
<qex>stanza</qex>, in every ode.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An apartment or division in a
building; a room or chamber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stanzaic</ent><br/
<hw>Stan*za"ic</hw> <pr>(st<acr/n*z<amac/"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Pertaining to, or consisting of, stanzas; <as>as, a couplet in
<ex>stanzaic</ex> form</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stapedial</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*pe"di*al</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*p<emac/"d<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>stapes</ets> stirrup.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the stapes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stapelia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sta*pe"li*a</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/*<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL. So named after <person>John Bodaeus a
<etsep>Stapel</etsep></person>, a physician of Amsterdam.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An extensive and curious genus of African plants
of the natural order <ord>Asclepiadaceae</ord> (Milkweed family). They
are succulent plants without leaves, frequently covered with dark
tubercles giving them a very grotesque appearance. The odor of the
blossoms is like that of carrion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stapes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sta"pes</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"p<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[LL., a stirrup.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The innermost of
the ossicles of the ear; the stirrup, or stirrup bone; -- so called from
its form. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Ear</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staphyline</ent><br/
<hw>Staph"y*line</hw> <pr>(st<acr/f"<icr/*l<imac/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>stafy`linos</grk> botryoidal, from <grk>stafylh`</grk> a
bunch of grapes.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the
uvula or the palate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staphylinid</ent><br/
<hw>Staph`y*li"nid</hw> <pr>(st<acr/f`<icr/*l<imac/"n<icr/d)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>stafyli^nos</grk> a kind of insect.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any rove beetle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staphyloma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Staph`y*lo"ma</hw> <pr>(-l<omac/"m<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>stafy`lwma</grk>, from <grk>stafylh`</grk> a
bunch of grapes.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A protrusion of any part
of the globe of the eye; <as>as, a <ex>staphyloma</ex> of the
cornea</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staphylomatous</ent><br/
<hw>Staph`y*lo"ma*tous</hw>
<pr>(st<acr/f`<icr/*l<omac/"m<adot/*t<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to staphyloma; affected with
staphyloma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staphyloplasty</ent><br/
<hw>Staph"y*lo*plas`ty</hw>
<pr>(st<acr/f"<icr/*l<osl/*pl<acr/s`t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <?/ a bunch of grapes, also, the uvula when swollen at the
lower end + <ets>-plasty</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>The
operation for restoring or replacing the soft palate when it has been
lost.</def> <au>Dunglison.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Staph`y*lo*plas"tic</wf>
<pr>(-l<osl/*pl<acr/s"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staphylorrhaphy</ent><br/
<ent>Staphyloraphy</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Staph`y*lor"a*phy</hw>, <hw>Staph`y*lor"rha*phy</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(-l<ocr/r"<adot/*f<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>stafylh`</grk> the uvula when swollen + <grk>"ra`ptein</grk> to
sew: cf. F. <ets>staphylorraphie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>
<def>The operation of uniting a cleft palate, consisting in paring and
bringing together the edges of the cleft.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Staph`y*lo*raph"ic</wf>
<pr>(st<acr/f`<icr/*l<osl/*r<acr/f"<icr/k)</pr>,
<wf>Staph`y*lor*rhaph"ic</wf> <pr>(-l<ocr/r*r<acr/f"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staphylotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Staph`y*lot"o*my</hw> <pr>(-l<ocr/t"<osl/*m<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ the uvula when swollen + <?/ to cut.]</ety>
<fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>The operation of removing a staphyloma by
cutting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staple</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ple</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"p'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stapul</ets>, <ets>stapol</ets>, <ets>stapel</ets>, a step, a prop,
post, table, fr. <ets>stapan</ets> to step, go, raise; akin to D.
<ets>stapel</ets> a pile, stocks, emporium, G. <ets>stapel</ets>a heap,
mart, stake, <ets>staffel</ets> step of a ladder, Sw. <ets>stapel</ets>,
Dan. <ets>stabel</ets>, and E. <ets>step</ets>; cf. OF.
<ets>estaple</ets> a mart, F. <ets><eacute/tape</ets>. See
<er>Step</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A settled mart; an emporium; a
city or town to which merchants brought commodities for sale or
exportation in bulk; a place for wholesale traffic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The customs of Alexandria were very great, it having been the
<qex>staple</qex> of the Indian trade.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the increase of trade and the encouragement of the worthy
burgesses of Woodstock, her majesty was minded to erect the town into a
<qex>staple</qex> for wool.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In England, formerly, the king's <xex>staple</xex> was
established in certain ports or towns, and certain goods could not be
exported without being first brought to these places to be rated and
charged with the duty payable to the king or the public. The principal
commodities on which customs were levied were wool, skins, and leather;
and these were originally the <ex>staple</ex> commodities.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence: Place of supply; source; fountain
head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whitehall naturally became the chief <qex>staple</qex> of news.
Whenever there was a rumor that any thing important had happened or was
about to happen, people hastened thither to obtain intelligence from the
fountain head.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The principal commodity of traffic in a market; a
principal commodity or production of a country or district; <as>as,
wheat, maize, and cotton are great <ex>staples</ex> of the United
States</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We should now say, Cotton is the great <qex>staple</qex>, that is,
the established merchandise, of Manchester.</q>
<rj><qau>Trench.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The principal constituent in anything; chief
item.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Unmanufactured material; raw material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The fiber of wool, cotton, flax, or the like;
<as>as, a coarse <ex>staple</ex>; a fine <ex>staple</ex>; a long or
short <ex>staple</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A loop of metal such as iron, or a bar or wire, bent
and formed with two points to be driven into wood, to hold a hook, pin,
or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>A small loop of metal
such as steel, bent into a U-shape with the points sharpened, used to
fasten sheets of paper together by driving the staple{8} through the
stacked sheets and into a formed receptacle which curls the ends in and
backward, thus holding the papers firmly together; also, a similar,
slightly larger such fastener which may be driven into wood to fasten
objects to a wooden backing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A shaft, smaller
and shorter than the principal one, joining different levels.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A small pit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A district granted to an abbey.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staple</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ple</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or being
a market or staple for, commodities; <as>as, a <ex>staple</ex>
town</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Established in commerce; occupying the markets;
settled; <as>as, a <ex>staple</ex> trade</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fit to be sold; marketable.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Regularly produced or manufactured in large
quantities; belonging to wholesale traffic; principal; chief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wool, the great <qex>staple</qex> commodity of England.</q>
<rj><qau>Hallam.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>staple</ent><br/
<hw>sta"ple</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>stapled</conjf> <pr>(-p'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>stapling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sort according to
its staple; <as>as, to <ex>staple</ex> cotton</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fasten together with a staple{9} or staples;
<as>as, to <ex>staple</ex> a check to a letter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>staple gun</ent><br/
<hw>sta"ple gun`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A device used to drive a heavy
staple{8} through multiple objects, so as to fasten them together; it
has a spring mechanism which stores force as a lever is pulled by the
operator's hand, and the force is released all at once to drive the
staple fully into the object to be fastened.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stapler</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"pler</hw> <pr>(-pl<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A dealer in staple goods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One employed to assort wool according to its
staple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A device used to drive a staple{8} into objects so
as to fasten them together.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star</ent><br/
<hw>Star</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sterre</ets>, AS. <ets>steorra</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>stera</ets>, OS. <ets>sterro</ets>, D. <ets>ster</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sterno</ets>, <ets>sterro</ets>, G. <ets>stern</ets>, Icel.
<ets>stjarna</ets>, Sw. <ets>stjerna</ets>, Dan. <ets>stierne</ets>,
Goth. <ets>sta<iacute/rn<omac/</ets>, Armor. & Corn. <ets>steren</ets>,
L. <ets>stella</ets>, Gr. <grk>'asth`r</grk>, <grk>'a`stron</grk>, Skr.
<ets>star</ets>; perhaps from a root meaning, to scatter, Skr.
<ets>st<rsdot/</ets>, L. <ets>sternere</ets> (cf. <er>Stratum</er>), and
originally applied to the stars as being strewn over the sky, or as
being scatterers or spreaders of light. <root/296. Cf. <er>Aster</er>,
<er>Asteroid</er>, <er>Constellation</er>, <er>Disaster</er>,
<er>Stellar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of the innumerable
luminous bodies seen in the heavens; any heavenly body other than the
sun, moon, comets, and nebulae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His eyen twinkled in his head aright,<br/
As do the <qex>stars</qex> in the frosty night.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The stars are distinguished as <stype>planets</stype>,
and <stype>fixed stars</stype>. See <er>Planet</er>, <cref>Fixed
stars</cref> under <er>Fixed</er>, and <cref>Magnitude of a star</cref>
under <er>Magnitude</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The polestar; the north star.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astrol.)</fld> <def>A planet supposed to influence
one's destiny; (usually <pluf>pl.</pluf>) a configuration of the
planets, supposed to influence fortune.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O malignant and ill-brooding <qex>stars</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Blesses his <qex>stars</qex>, and thinks it luxury.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which resembles the figure of a star, as an
ornament worn on the breast to indicate rank or honor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On whom . . . <br/
Lavish Honor showered all her <qex>stars</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Specifically, a radiated mark in writing or
printing; an asterisk [thus, *]; -- used as a reference to a note, or to
fill a blank where something is omitted, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Pyrotechny)</fld> <def>A composition of combustible
matter used in the heading of rockets, in mines, etc., which, exploding
in the air, presents a starlike appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A person of brilliant and attractive qualities,
especially on public occasions, as a distinguished orator, a leading
theatrical performer, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Star</xex> is used in the formation of compound
words generally of obvious signification; as, <xex>star</xex>-aspiring,
<xex>star</xex>-bespangled, <xex>star</xex>-bestudded,
<xex>star</xex>-blasting, <xex>star</xex>-bright,
<xex>star</xex>-crowned, <xex>star</xex>-directed, <xex>star</xex>-eyed,
<xex>star</xex>-headed, <xex>star</xex>-paved, <xex>star</xex>-roofed,
<xex>star</xex>-sprinkled, <xex>star</xex>-wreathed.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1404 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Blazing star</b></col>, <col><b>Double
star</b></col>, <col><b>Multiple star</b></col>, <col><b>Shooting
star</b></col>, etc.</mcol> <cd>See under <er>Blazing</er>,
<er>Double</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Nebulous star</b></col>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a small well-defined circular nebula, having a
bright nucleus at its center like a star.</cd> -- <col><b>Star
anise</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any plant of the genus Illicium;
-- so called from its star-shaped capsules.</cd> -- <col><b>Star
apple</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a tropical American tree
(<spn>Chrysophyllum Cainito</spn>), having a milky juice and oblong
leaves with a silky-golden pubescence beneath. It bears an applelike
fruit, the carpels of which present a starlike figure when cut across.
The name is extended to the whole genus of about sixty species, and the
natural order (<ord>Sapotaceae</ord>) to which it belongs is called the
<xex>Star-apple family</xex>.</cd> -- <col><b>Star conner</b></col>,
<cd>one who cons, or studies, the stars; an astronomer or an
astrologer.</cd> <au>Gascoigne.</au> -- <col><b>Star coral</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of stony corals
belonging to <gen>Astraea</gen>, <gen>Orbicella</gen>, and allied
genera, in which the calicles are round or polygonal and contain
conspicuous radiating septa.</cd> -- <col><b>Star cucumber</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Cucumber</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Star
flower</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A plant of the
genus <gen>Ornithogalum</gen>; star-of-Bethlehem</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>See <er>Starwort</er> <sd>(b)</sd></cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>An
American plant of the genus <gen>Trientalis</gen> (<spn>Trientalis
Americana</spn>)</cd>. <au>Gray.</au> -- <col><b>Star fort</b></col>
<fld>(Fort.)</fld>, <cd>a fort surrounded on the exterior with
projecting angles; -- whence the name.</cd> -- <col><b>Star
gauge</b></col> <fld>(Ordnance)</fld>, <cd>a long rod, with adjustable
points projecting radially at its end, for measuring the size of
different parts of the bore of a gun.</cd> -- <col><b>Star
grass</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A small grasslike
plant (<spn>Hypoxis erecta</spn>) having star-shaped yellow
flowers</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The colicroot. See
<er>Colicroot</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Star hyacinth</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a bulbous plant of the genus <gen>Scilla</gen>
(<spn>Scilla autumnalis</spn>); -- called also <altname>star-headed
hyacinth</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Star jelly</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several gelatinous plants (<spn>Nostoc
commune</spn>, <spn>Nostoc edule</spn>, etc.). See <er>Nostoc</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Star lizard</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<er>Stellion</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Star-of-Bethlehem</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a bulbous liliaceous plant (<spn>Ornithogalum
umbellatum</spn>) having a small white starlike flower.</cd> --
<col><b>Star-of-the-earth</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a plant of
the genus <gen>Plantago</gen> (<spn>Plantago coronopus</spn>), growing
upon the seashore.</cd> -- <col><b>Star polygon</b></col>
<fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>a polygon whose sides cut each other so as to
form a star-shaped figure.</cd> -- <col><b>Stars and Stripes</b></col>,
<cd>a popular name for the flag of the United States, which consists of
thirteen horizontal stripes, alternately red and white, and a union
having, in a blue field, white stars to represent the several States,
one for each.</cd><br/
<br/
<q>With the old flag, the true American flag, the Eagle, and the
<qex>Stars and Stripes</qex>, waving over the chamber in which we
sit.</q> <rj><qau>D. Webster.</qau></rj><br/
<br/
-- <col><b>Star showers</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Shooting star</cref>,
under <er>Shooting</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Star thistle</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an annual composite plant (<spn>Centaurea
solstitialis</spn>) having the involucre armed with stout radiating
spines.</cd> -- <col><b>Star wheel</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a
star-shaped disk, used as a kind of ratchet wheel, in repeating watches
and the feed motions of some machines.</cd> -- <col><b>Star
worm</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a gephyrean.</cd> --
<col><b>Temporary star</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a star which
appears suddenly, shines for a period, and then nearly or quite
disappears. These stars were supposed by some astronomers to be variable
stars of long and undetermined periods. More recently, variations star
in start intensity are classified more specifically, and this term is
now obsolescent. See also <er>nova</er>.</cd> <mark>[Obsolescent]</mark>
-- <col><b>Variable star</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>a star
whose brilliancy varies periodically, generally with regularity, but
sometimes irregularly; -- called <stype>periodical star</stype> when its
changes occur at fixed periods.</cd> -- <col><b>Water star
grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an aquatic plant (<spn>Schollera
graminea</spn>) with small yellow starlike blossoms.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star</ent><br/
<hw>Star</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Starred</conjf> <pr>(st<aum/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Starring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To set or adorn
with stars, or bright, radiating bodies; to bespangle; <as>as, a robe
<ex>starred</ex> with gems</as>.</def> <ldquo/A sable curtain
<xex>starred</xex> with gold.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star</ent><br/
<hw>Star</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be bright, or attract attention,
as a star; to shine like a star; to be brilliant or prominent; to play a
part as a theatrical star.</def> <-- i.e., to be the most prominent or
one of the two most prominent actors in the cast of a drama or film. -->
<rj><au>W. Irving.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star-blind</ent><br/
<hw>Star"-blind`</hw> <pr>(-bl<imac/nd`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Half
blind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starboard</ent><br/
<hw>Star"board`</hw> <pr>(-b<omac/rd` <or/ -b<etil/rd)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sterbord</ets>, AS.
<ets>ste<oacute/rbord</ets>, i.e., steer board. See <er>Steer</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, <er>Board</er> of a vessel, and cf.
<er>Larboard</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>That side of a vessel
which is on the right hand of a person who stands on board facing the
bow; -- opposed to <ant>larboard</ant>, or <ant>port</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starboard</ent><br/
<hw>Star"board`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Pertaining
to the right-hand side of a ship; being or lying on the right side;
<as>as, the <ex>starboard</ex> shrouds; <ex>starboard</ex> quarter;
<ex>starboard</ex> tack.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starboard</ent><br/
<hw>Star"board`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To put to
the right, or starboard, side of a vessel; <as>as, to <ex>starboard</ex>
the helm</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star-bowlines</ent><br/
<hw>Star"-bow`lines</hw> <pr>(-b<omac/`l<icr/nz)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The men in the starboard watch.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>R. H. Dana, Jr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starch</ent><br/
<hw>Starch</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rch)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stearc</ets> stark, strong, rough. See <er>Stark</er>.]</ety>
<def>Stiff; precise; rigid.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Killingbeck.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starch</ent><br/
<hw>Starch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <ets>starch</ets> stiff, cf.
G. <ets>st<aum/rke</ets>, fr. <ets>stark</ets> strong.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A widely diffused vegetable
substance found especially in seeds, bulbs, and tubers, and extracted
(as from potatoes, corn, rice, etc.) as a white, glistening, granular or
powdery substance, without taste or smell, and giving a very peculiar
creaking sound when rubbed between the fingers. It is used as a food, in
the production of commercial grape sugar, for stiffening linen in
laundries, in making paste, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Starch is a carbohydrate, being the typical amylose,
<chform>C6H10O5</chform>, and is detected by the fine blue color given
to it by free iodine. It is not fermentable as such, but is changed by
diastase into dextrin and maltose, and by heating with dilute acids into
dextrose. Cf. <er>Sugar</er>, <er>Inulin</er>, and
<er>Lichenin</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: A stiff, formal manner; formality.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Starch hyacinth</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the
grape hyacinth; -- so called because the flowers have the smell of
boiled starch. See under <er>Grape</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starch</ent><br/
<hw>Starch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Starched</conjf> <pr>(st<aum/rcht)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Starching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To stiffen with
starch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star-chamber</ent><br/
<ent>Star chamber</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Star" cham`ber</hw>, <hw>Star"-cham`ber</hw></mhw>
<pr>(st<aum/r"ch<amac/m`b<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called
(as conjectured by Blackstone) from being held in a room at the
Exchequer where the chests containing certain Jewish contracts and
obligations called <ets>starrs</ets> (from the Heb. <ets>shetar</ets>,
pron. <ets>shtar</ets>) were kept; or from the <ets>stars</ets> with
which the ceiling is supposed to have been decorated.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Eng. Hist.)</fld> <def>An ancient high court exercising
jurisdiction in certain cases, mainly criminal, which sat without the
intervention of a jury. It consisted of the king's council, or of the
privy council only with the addition of certain judges. It could proceed
on mere rumor or examine witnesses; it could apply torture. It was
abolished by the Long Parliament in 1641.</def> <rj><au>Encyc.
Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <mark>(Metaphorical)</mark>
<def>Any court, committee, or other tribunal which exercises arbitrary
and unaccountable power, or uses unfair or illegal methods, in
investigation or judgment of persons.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starched</ent><br/
<hw>Starched</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rcht)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Stiffened with starch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Stiff; precise; formal.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starchedness</ent><br/
<hw>Starch"ed*ness</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rch"<ecr/d*n<ecr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being starched or starchy{5};
stiffness in manners; formality.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> starchiness, starchness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starcher</ent><br/
<hw>Starch"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
starches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>starchiness</ent><br/
<hw>starch"i*ness</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rch"<esl/*n<ecr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being starchy; stiffness in
manners; formality.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> starchedness, starchness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starchly</ent><br/
<hw>Starch"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a starched or starch
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starchness</ent><br/
<hw>Starch"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
starched or starchy{5}; stiffness of manner; preciseness.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> starchedness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starchwort</ent><br/
<hw>Starch"wort`</hw> <pr>(-w<ucir/rt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The cuckoopint, the tubers of which yield a fine
quality of starch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>starchy</ent><br/
<hw>starch"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to starch.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Containing or consisting of starch; -- used
especially of foods.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Resembling starch; stiff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Stiffened by use of starch; starched{1}; -- of
clothing.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Formal in manner; precise.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> starch.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starcraft</ent><br/
<hw>Star"craft</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"kr<adot/ft)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Astrology.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star-crossed</ent><br/
<hw>Star"-crossed`</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"kr<ocr/st` ; 115)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Not favored by the stars; ill-fated.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such is my <qex>star-crossed</qex> destiny.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star drift</ent><br/
<hw>Star drift</hw>. <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Similar and probably
related motion of the stars of an asterism, as distinguished from
apparent change of place due to solar motion.-- ##?? = star streaming?
--</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stare</ent><br/
<hw>Stare</hw> <pr>(st<acir/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>staer</ets>. See <er>Starling</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The starling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>stare</ent><br/
<hw>stare</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>stared</conjf> <pr>(st<acir/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>staring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>starian</ets>; akin to
LG. & D. <ets>staren</ets>, OHG. <ets>star<emac/n</ets>, G.
<ets>starren</ets>, Icel. <ets>stara</ets>; cf. Icel. <ets>stira</ets>,
Dan. <ets>stirre</ets>, Sw. <ets>stirra</ets>, and G. <ets>starr</ets>
stiff, rigid, fixed, Gr. <grk>stereo`s</grk> solid (E.
<ets>stereo-</ets>), Skr. <ets>sthira</ets> firm, strong. <root/166. Cf.
<er>Sterile</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To look with fixed eyes wide
open, as through fear, wonder, surprise, impudence, etc.; to fasten an
earnest and prolonged gaze on some object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For ever upon the ground I see thee <qex>stare</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Look not big, nor stamp, nor <qex>stare</qex>, nor fret.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be very conspicuous on account of size,
prominence, color, or brilliancy; <as>as, <ex>staring</ex> windows or
colors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To stand out; to project; to bristle.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Makest my blood cold, and my hair to <qex>stare</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Take off all the <qex>staring</qex> straws and jags in the
hive.</q> <rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To gaze; to look earnestly. See
<er>Gaze</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stare</ent><br/
<hw>Stare</hw> <pr>(st<acir/r)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To look
earnestly at; to gaze at.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>stare</qex> him out of his wits.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stare in the face</b></col>, <cd>to be before the
eyes, or to be undeniably evident.</cd> <ldquo/The law . . .
<xex>stares</xex> them <xex>in the face</xex> whilst they are breaking
it.<rdquo/ <au>Locke.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stare</ent><br/
<hw>Stare</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of staring; a fixed look with
eyes wide open.</def> <ldquo/A dull and stupid <xex>stare</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Churchill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starer</ent><br/
<hw>Star"er</hw> <pr>(st<acir/r"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who stares, or gazes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starf</ent><br/
<hw>Starf</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rf)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos>
<mord>of <er>Starve</er></mord>. <def>Starved.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starfinch</ent><br/
<hw>Star"finch`</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"f<icr/nch`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The European redstart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starfish</ent><br/
<hw>Star"fish`</hw> <pr>(-f<icr/sh`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of echinoderms
belonging to the class <class>Asterioidea</class>, in which the body is
star-shaped and usually has five rays, though the number of rays varies
from five to forty or more. The rays are often long, but are sometimes
so short as to appear only as angles to the disklike body. Called also
<altname>sea star</altname>, <altname>five-finger</altname>, and
<altname>stellerid</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The ophiuroids are also sometimes called starfishes. See
<er>Brittle star</er>, and <er>Ophiuroidea</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The dollar fish, or
butterfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stargazer</ent><br/
<hw>Star"gaz`er</hw> <pr>(-g<amac/z`<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who gazes at the stars; an astrologer; sometimes,
in derision or contempt, an astronomer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
spiny-rayed marine fishes belonging to <gen>Uranoscopus</gen>,
<gen>Astroscopus</gen>, and allied genera, of the family
<fam>Uranoscopidae</fam>. The common species of the Eastern United
States are <spn>Astroscopus anoplus</spn>, and <spn>Astroscopus
guttatus</spn>. So called from the position of the eyes, which look
directly upward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stargazing</ent><br/
<hw>Star"gaz`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or
practice of observing the stars with attention; contemplation of the
stars as connected with astrology or astronomy.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, absent-mindedness; abstraction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staringly</ent><br/
<hw>Star"ing*ly</hw> <pr>(st<acir/r"<icr/ng*l<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <def>With a staring look.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stark</ent><br/
<hw>Stark</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rk)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Starker</adjf> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Starkest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stark</ets> stiff, strong, AS. <ets>stearc</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>starc</ets> strong, D. <ets>sterk</ets>, OHG. <ets>starc</ets>,
<ets>starah</ets>, G. & Sw. <ets>stark</ets>, Dan. <ets>staerk</ets>,
Icel. <ets>sterkr</ets>, Goth. ga<ets>sta<uacute/rknan</ets> to become
dried up, Lith. <ets>str<eum/gti</ets> to stiffen, to freeze. Cf.
<er>Starch</er>, <pos>a.</pos> & <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Stiff; rigid.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whose senses all were straight benumbed and <qex>stark</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His heart gan wax as <qex>stark</qex> as marble stone.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many a nobleman lies <qex>stark</qex> and stiff<br/
Under the hoofs of vaunting enemies.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The north is not so <qex>stark</qex> and cold.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Complete; absolute; full; perfect; entire.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Consider the <qex>stark</qex> security<br/
The common wealth is in now.</q> <rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Strong; vigorous; powerful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>stark</qex>, moss-trooping Scot.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stark</qex> beer, boy, stout and strong beer.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Severe; violent; fierce.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/In <xex>starke</xex> stours<rdquo/ [<it>i. e.</it>, in fierce
combats]. <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Mere; sheer; gross; entire; downright.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He pronounces the citation <qex>stark</qex> nonsense.</q>
<rj><qau>Collier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rhetoric is very good or <qex>stark</qex> naught; there's no
medium in rhetoric.</q> <rj><qau>Selden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stark</ent><br/
<hw>Stark</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rk)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Wholly;
entirely; absolutely; quite; <as>as, <ex>stark</ex> mad</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Held him strangled in his arms till he was <qex>stark</qex>
dead.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stark naked</b></col>, <cd>wholly naked; quite
bare.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Strip your sword <qex>stark naked</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ According to Professor Skeat,
<ldquo/<xex>stark-naked</xex><rdquo/ is derived from
<xex>steort-naked</xex>, or <xex>start-naked</xex>, literally
<xex>tail-naked</xex>, and hence <xex>wholly naked</xex>. If this
etymology be true the preferable form is
<xex>stark-naked</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stark</ent><br/
<hw>Stark</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To stiffen.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If horror have not <qex>starked</qex> your limbs.</q> <rj><qau>H.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starkly</ent><br/
<hw>Stark"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stark manner; stiffly;
strongly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Its onward force too <qex>starkly</qex> pent<br/
In figure, bone, and lineament.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starkness</ent><br/
<hw>Stark"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starless</ent><br/
<hw>Star"less</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"l<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being
without stars; having no stars visible; <as>as, a <ex>starless</ex>
night</as>.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starlight</ent><br/
<hw>Star"light`</hw> <pr>(-l<imac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The light
given by the stars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor walk by moon,<br/
Or glittering <qex>starlight</qex>, without thee is sweet.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starlight</ent><br/
<hw>Star"light`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lighted by the stars, or by the
stars only; <as>as, a <ex>starlight</ex> night</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>starlight</qex> evening and a morning fair.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starlike</ent><br/
<hw>Star"like`</hw> <pr>(-l<imac/k`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Resembling a star; stellated; radiated like a star; <as>as,
<ex>starlike</ex> flowers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Shining; bright; illustrious.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The having turned many to righteousness shall confer a
<qex>starlike</qex> and immortal brightness.</q>
<rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starling</ent><br/
<hw>Star"ling</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sterlyng</ets>, a dim. of OE. <ets>stare</ets>, AS.
<ets>staer</ets>; akin to AS. <ets>stearn</ets>, G. <ets>star</ets>,
<ets>staar</ets>, OHG. <ets>stara</ets>, Icel. <ets>starri</ets>,
<ets>stari</ets>, Sw. <ets>stare</ets>, Dan. <ets>staer</ets>, L.
<ets>sturnus</ets>. Cf. <er>Stare</er> a starling.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any passerine bird belonging to
<gen>Sturnus</gen> and allied genera. The European starling
(<spn>Sturnus vulgaris</spn>) is dark brown or greenish black, with a
metallic gloss, and spotted with yellowish white. It is a sociable bird,
and builds about houses, old towers, etc. Called also
<altname>stare</altname>, and <altname>starred</altname>. The pied
starling of India is <spn>Sternopastor contra</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A California fish; the rock
trout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A structure of piles driven round the piers of a
bridge for protection and support; -- called also
<altname>sterling</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Rose-colored starling</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Pastor</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starlit</ent><br/
<hw>Star"lit`</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/t`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lighted by
the stars; starlight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starmonger</ent><br/
<hw>Star"mon`ger</hw> <pr>(-m<ucr/<nsm/`g<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>A fortune teller; an astrologer; -- used in contempt.</def>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starn</ent><br/
<hw>Starn</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rn)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The European starling.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starnose</ent><br/
<hw>Star"nose`</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"n<omac/z`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A curious American mole (<spn>Condylura
cristata</spn>) having the nose expanded at the end into a stellate
disk; -- called also <altname>star-nosed mole</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star of David</ent><br/
<hw>Star" of Dav"id</hw>. <def>A symbol consisting of two overlapped
equilateral triangles forming a star with six points, used as a symbol
of Judaism. It is also called <altname>Magen David</altname>,
<altname>Mogen David</altname>, and <altname>Shield of David</altname>,
and is shaped identically to the <er>hexagram</er> and <er>Solomon's
seal</er>. It is used on the flag of the modern state of
Israel.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starost</ent><br/
<hw>Star"ost</hw> <pr>(st<acr/r"<ocr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pol.
<ets>starosta</ets>, from <ets>stary</ets> old.]</ety> <def>A nobleman
who possessed a starosty.</def> <mark>[Poland]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starosty</ent><br/
<hw>Star"os*ty</hw> <pr>(-<ocr/s*t<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
castle and domain conferred on a nobleman for life.</def>
<mark>[Poland]</mark> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starproof</ent><br/
<hw>Star"proof`</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"pr<oomac/f`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Impervious to the light of the stars; <as>as, a <ex>starproof</ex>
elm</as>.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star-read</ent><br/
<hw>Star"-read`</hw> <pr>(-r<emac/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Doctrine
or knowledge of the stars; star lore; astrology; astronomy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which in <qex>star-read</qex> were wont have best insight.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starred</ent><br/
<hw>Starred</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Star</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Adorned or studded with stars;
bespangled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Influenced in fortune by the stars.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My third comfort,<br/
<qex>Starred</qex> most unluckily.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starriness</ent><br/
<hw>Star"ri*ness</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"r<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The quality or state of being starry; <as>as, the
<ex>starriness</ex> of the heavens</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starry</ent><br/
<hw>Star"ry</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Abounding with stars; adorned with stars.</def> <ldquo/Above the
<xex>starry</xex> sky.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting of, or proceeding from, the stars;
stellar; stellary; <as>as, <ex>starry</ex> light; <ex>starry</ex>
flame</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do not Christians and Heathens, Jews and Gentiles, poets and
philosophers, unite in allowing the <qex>starry</qex> influence?</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Shining like stars; sparkling; <as>as,
<ex>starry</ex> eyes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Arranged in rays like those of a star;
stellate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Starry ray</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a European
skate (<spn>Raia radiata</spn>); -- so called from the stellate bases of
the dorsal spines.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starshine</ent><br/
<hw>Star"shine`</hw> <pr>(-sh<imac/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
light of the stars.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>starshine</qex> lights upon our heads.</q> <rj><qau>R. L.
Stevenson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starshoot</ent><br/
<hw>Star"shoot`</hw> <pr>(-sh<oomac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Nostoc</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star-spangled</ent><br/
<hw>Star"-span`gled</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"-sp<acr/<nsm/`g'ld)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Spangled or studded with stars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Star-spangled banner</b></col>, <cd>the popular name for
the national ensign of the United States; also the name of a poem, the
words of which were composed by Francis Scott Key, which was adopted as
the national anthem of the United States.</cd> <au>F. S.
Key.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Star stereogram</ent><br/
<hw>Star stereogram</hw>. <def>A view of the universe of brighter stars
as it would appear to an observer transported into space outside or
beyond our universe of stars.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starstone</ent><br/
<hw>Star"stone`</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"st<omac/n`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Asteriated sapphire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>start</ent><br/
<hw>start</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rt)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>started</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>starting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sterten</ets>; akin to
D. <ets>storten</ets> to hurl, rush, fall, G. <ets>st<uum/rzen</ets>,
OHG. <ets>sturzen</ets> to turn over, to fall, Sw. <ets>st<oum/rta</ets>
to cast down, to fall, Dan. <ets>styrte</ets>, and probably also to E.
<ets>start</ets> a tail; the original sense being, perhaps, to show the
tail, to tumble over suddenly. <root/166. Cf. <er>Start</er> a
tail.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To leap; to jump.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move suddenly, as with a spring or leap, from
surprise, pain, or other sudden feeling or emotion, or by a voluntary
act.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And maketh him out of his sleep to <qex>start</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>start</qex> as from some dreadful dream.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Keep your soul to the work when ready to <qex>start</qex>
aside.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But if he <qex>start</qex>,<br/
It is the flesh of a corrupted heart.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To set out; to commence a course, as a race or
journey; to begin; <as>as, to <ex>start</ex> in business</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At once they <qex>start</qex>, advancing in a line.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At intervals some bird from out the brakes<br/
<qex>Starts</qex> into voice a moment, then is still.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To become somewhat displaced or loosened; <as>as, a
rivet or a seam may <ex>start</ex> under strain or
pressure</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To start after</b></col>, <cd>to set out after; to
follow; to pursue.</cd> -- <col><b>To start against</b></col>, <cd>to
act as a rival candidate against.</cd> -- <col><b>To start
for</b></col>, <cd>to be a candidate for, as an office.</cd> --
<col><b>To start up</b></col>, <cd>to rise suddenly, as from a seat or
couch; to come suddenly into notice or importance.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1405 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Start</ent><br/
<hw>Start</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rt)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To cause to move suddenly; to disturb suddenly; to startle; to
alarm; to rouse; to cause to flee or fly; <as>as, the hounds
<ex>started</ex> a fox</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Upon malicious bravery dost thou come<br/
To <qex>start</qex> my quiet?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Brutus will <qex>start</qex> a spirit as soon as Caesar.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bring into being or into view; to originate; to
invent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sensual men agree in the pursuit of every pleasure they can
<qex>start</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to move or act; to set going, running, or
flowing; <as>as, to <ex>start</ex> a railway train; to <ex>start</ex> a
mill; to <ex>start</ex> a stream of water; to <ex>start</ex> a rumor; to
<ex>start</ex> a business</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I was engaged in conversation upon a subject which the people love
to <qex>start</qex> in discourse.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To move suddenly from its place or position; to
displace or loosen; to dislocate; <as>as, to <ex>start</ex> a bone; the
storm <ex>started</ex> the bolts in the vessel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One, by a fall in wrestling, <qex>started</qex> the end of the
clavicle from the sternum.</q> <rj><qau>Wiseman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <ety>[Perh. from D. <ets>storten</ets>, which has this
meaning also.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To pour out; to empty; to
tap and begin drawing from; <as>as, to <ex>start</ex> a water
cask</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Start</ent><br/
<hw>Start</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of starting; a
sudden spring, leap, or motion, caused by surprise, fear, pain, or the
like; any sudden motion, or beginning of motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fright awakened Arcite with a <qex>start</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A convulsive motion, twitch, or spasm; a spasmodic
effort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For she did speak in <qex>starts</qex> distractedly.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nature does nothing by <qex>starts</qex> and leaps, or in a
hurry.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A sudden, unexpected movement; a sudden and
capricious impulse; a sally; <as>as, <ex>starts</ex> of
fancy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To check the <qex>starts</qex> and sallies of the soul.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The beginning, as of a journey or a course of
action; first motion from a place; act of setting out; the outset; --
opposed to <ant>finish</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>start</qex> of first performance is all.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I see you stand like greyhounds in the slips,<br/
Straining upon the <qex>start</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>At a start</b></col>, <cd>at once; in an instant.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>At a start</qex> he was betwixt them two.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To get the start</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To
have the start</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to begin before another; to gain or
have the advantage in a similar undertaking; -- usually with
<xex>of</xex>.</cd> <ldquo/<xex>Get the start</xex> of the majestic
world.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/She might have forsaken him if he
had not <xex>got the start</xex> of her.<rdquo/
<au>Dryden.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Start</ent><br/
<hw>Start</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stert</ets> a tail, AS.
<ets>steort</ets>; akin to LG. <ets>stert</ets>, <ets>steert</ets>, D.
<ets>staart</ets>, G. <ets>sterz</ets>, Icel. <ets>stertr</ets>, Dan.
<ets>stiert</ets>, Sw. <ets>stjert</ets>. <root/166. Cf. <ets>Stark
naked</ets>, under <er>Stark</er>, <er>Start</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A tail, or anything projecting like a
tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The handle, or tail, of a plow; also, any long
handle.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The curved or inclined front and bottom of a
water-wheel bucket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The arm, or lever, of a gin,
drawn around by a horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>START</ent><br/
<hw>START</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>S</ets>trategic <ets>A</ets>rms <ets>R</ets>eduction
<ets>T</ets>reaty.]</ety> <def>A Treaty between the United States and
the Soviet Union which provided for stepwise reductions in the number of
nuclear weapons possessed by each country.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starter</ent><br/
<hw>Start"er</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rt"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, starts; <as>as, a
<ex>starter</ex> on a journey; the <ex>starter</ex> of a
race</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A dog that rouses game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startful</ent><br/
<hw>Start"ful</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rt"f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Apt to start; skittish.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startfulness</ent><br/
<hw>Start"ful*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Aptness to start.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starthroat</ent><br/
<hw>Star"throat`</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"thr<omac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any humming bird of the genus
<gen>Heliomaster</gen>. The feathers of the throat have a brilliant
metallic luster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starting</ent><br/
<hw>Start"ing</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rt"<icr/ng)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos>
from <er>Start</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Starting bar</b></col> <fld>(Steam Eng.)</fld>, <cd>a
hand lever for working the valves in starting an engine.</cd> --
<col><b>Starting hole</b></col>, <cd>a loophole; evasion.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>Starting point</b></col>, <cd>the point
from which motion begins, or from which anything starts.</cd> --
<col><b>Starting post</b></col>, <cd>a post, stake, barrier, or place
from which competitors in a race start, or begin the race.</cd></cs><--
starting pistol, a pistol used to fire blank cartridges to signal the
start of a race; a starter's pistol. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startingly</ent><br/
<hw>Start"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By sudden fits or starts;
spasmodically.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startish</ent><br/
<hw>Start"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Apt to start; skittish; shy; --
said especially of a horse.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startle</ent><br/
<hw>Star"tle</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"t'l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Startled</conjf>
<pr>(st<aum/r"t'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Startling</conjf> <pr>(st<aum/r"tl<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Freq. of <ets>start</ets>.]</ety> <def>To move suddenly, or be
excited, on feeling alarm; to start.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why shrinks the soul<br/
Back on herself, and <qex>startles</qex> at destruction?</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startle</ent><br/
<hw>Star"tle</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"t'l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To excite by sudden alarm, surprise, or apprehension; to frighten
suddenly and not seriously; to alarm; to surprise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The supposition, at least, that angels do sometimes assume bodies
need not <qex>startle</qex> us.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To deter; to cause to deviate.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To start; shock; fright; frighten;
alarm.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startle</ent><br/
<hw>Star"tle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sudden motion or shock caused by
an unexpected alarm, surprise, or apprehension of danger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>After having recovered from my first <qex>startle</qex>, I was
very well pleased with the accident.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startlingly</ent><br/
<hw>Star"tling*ly</hw> <pr>(-tl<icr/ng*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In a startling manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Startlish</ent><br/
<hw>Star"tlish</hw> <pr>(-tl<icr/sh)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Easily
startled; apt to start; startish; skittish; -- said especially of a
horse.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Start-up</ent><br/
<hw>Start"-up`</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rt"<ucr/p`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who comes suddenly into notice; an upstart.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of high rustic shoe.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>startuppe</qex>, or clownish shoe.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Start-up</ent><br/
<hw>Start"-up`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Upstart.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starvation</ent><br/
<hw>Star*va"tion</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r*v<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of starving, or the state of being
starved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word was first used, according to Horace Walpole,
by Henry Dundas, the first Lord Melville, in a speech on American
affairs in 1775, which obtained for him the nickname of
<xex>Starvation</xex> Dundas.<br/
<ldquo/<xex>Starvation</xex>, we are also told, belongs to the class of
'vile compounds' from being a mongrel; as if English were not full of
mongrels, and as if it would not be in distressing straits without
them.<rdquo/ <au>Fitzed. Hall.</au></note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starve</ent><br/
<hw>Starve</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rv)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Starved</conjf>
<pr>(st<aum/rvd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Starving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sterven</ets> to die,
AS. <ets>steorfan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>sterven</ets>, G.
<ets>sterben</ets>, OHG. <ets>sterban</ets>, Icel. <ets>starf</ets>
labor, toil.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To die; to perish.</def>
<mark>[Obs., except in the sense of perishing with cold or
hunger.]</mark> <rj><au>Lydgate.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In hot coals he hath himself raked . . . <br/
Thus <qex>starved</qex> this worthy mighty Hercules.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To perish with hunger; to suffer extreme hunger or
want; to be very indigent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sometimes virtue <qex>starves</qex>, while vice is fed.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To perish or die with cold.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Have I seen the naked <qex>starve</qex> for cold?</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Starving</qex> with cold as well as hunger.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In this sense, still common in England, but rarely used
in the United States.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starve</ent><br/
<hw>Starve</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To destroy with
cold.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From beds of raging fire, to <qex>starve</qex> in ice<br/
Their soft ethereal warmth.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To kill with hunger; <as>as, maliciously to
<ex>starve</ex> a man is, in law, murder</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To distress or subdue by famine; <as>as, to
<ex>starve</ex> a garrison into a surrender</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Attalus endeavored to <qex>starve</qex> Italy by stopping their
convoy of provisions from Africa.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To destroy by want of any kind; <as>as, to
<ex>starve</ex> plants by depriving them of proper light and
air</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To deprive of force or vigor; to disable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pens of historians, writing thereof, seemed <qex>starved</qex>
for matter in an age so fruitful of memorable actions.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The powers of their minds are <qex>starved</qex> by disuse.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starvedly</ent><br/
<hw>Starv"ed*ly</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rv"<ecr/d*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In the condition of one starved or starving;
parsimoniously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some boasting housekeeper which keepth open doors for one day, . .
. and lives <qex>starvedly</qex> all the year after.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starveling</ent><br/
<hw>Starve"ling</hw> <pr>(st<aum/rv"l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Starve</ets> + <ets>-ling</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who, or that
which, pines from lack of food, or nutriment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Old Sir John hangs with me, and thou knowest he is no
<qex>starveling</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starveling</ent><br/
<hw>Starve"ling</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hungry; lean; pining with
want.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Starwort</ent><br/
<hw>Star"wort`</hw> <pr>(st<aum/r"w<ucir/rt`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any plant of the genus
<gen>Aster</gen>. See <er>Aster</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A small
plant of the genus <gen>Stellaria</gen>, having star-shaped flowers;
star flower; chickweed.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Water starwort</b></col>, <cd>an aquatic plant
(<spn>Callitriche verna</spn>) having some resemblance to
chickweed.</cd> -- <col><b>Yellow starwort</b></col>, <cd>a plant of the
genus <gen>Inula</gen>; elecampane.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stasimon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stas"i*mon</hw> <pr>(st<acr/s"<icr/*m<ocr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stasima</plw>
<pr>(st<acr/s"<icr/*m<adot/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>sta`simon</grk>, neut. of <grk>sta`simos</grk> stationary,
steadfast.]</ety> <def>In the Greek tragedy, a song of the chorus,
continued without the interruption of dialogue or anapaestics.</def>
<rj><au>Liddell & Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sta"sis</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"s<icr/s <or/ st<acr/s"<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>sta`sis</grk> a standing
still.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>A slackening or arrest of the
blood current in the vessels, due not to a lessening of the heart's
beat, but presumably to some abnormal resistance of the capillary walls.
It is one of the phenomena observed in the capillaries in
inflammation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statable</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"a*ble</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"<adot/*b'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>That can be stated; <as>as, a <ex>statable</ex> grievance; the
question at issue is <ex>statable</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statal</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tal</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or existing with reference to, a State of the American
Union, as distinguished from the general government.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have no knowledge of any other kind of political citizenship,
higher or lower, <qex>statal</qex> or national.</q> <rj><qau>Edward
Bates.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statant</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tant</hw> <pr>(-t<ait/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stare</ets> to stand.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>In a standing
position; <as>as, a lion <ex>statant</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*ta"ri*an</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*t<amac/"r<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Fixed; settled; steady; statary.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statarianly</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*ta"ri*an*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Fixedly; steadily.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statary</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"ta*ry</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<adot/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>statarius</ets> standing fast, fr. <ets>stare</ets> to
stand.]</ety> <def>Fixed; settled.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The
set and <xex>statary</xex> times of paring of nails and cutting of
hair.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>State</ent><br/
<hw>State</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stat</ets>, OF. <ets>estat</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tat</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>status</ets> a standing, position, fr. <ets>stare</ets>,
<ets>statum</ets>, to stand. See <er>Stand</er>, and cf.
<er>Estate</er>, <er>Status</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
circumstances or condition of a being or thing at any given
time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>State</qex> is a term nearly synonymous with
<ldquo/mode,<rdquo/ but of a meaning more extensive, and is not
exclusively limited to the mutable and contingent.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Declare the past and present <qex>state</qex> of things.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Keep the <qex>state</qex> of the question in your eye.</q>
<rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rank; condition; quality; <as>as, the <ex>state</ex>
of honor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy honor, <qex>state</qex>, and seat is due to me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Condition of prosperity or grandeur; wealthy or
prosperous circumstances; social importance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She instructed him how he should keep <qex>state</qex>, and yet
with a modest sense of his misfortunes.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Can this imperious lord forget to reign,<br/
Quit all his <qex>state</qex>, descend, and serve again?</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Appearance of grandeur or dignity; pomp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where least of <qex>state</qex> there most of love is shown.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A chair with a canopy above it, often standing on a
dais; a seat of dignity; also, the canopy itself.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His high throne, . . . under <qex>state</qex><br/
Of richest texture spread.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When he went to court, he used to kick away the <qex>state</qex>,
and sit down by his prince cheek by jowl.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Estate; possession.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your <qex>state</qex>, my lord, again is yours.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A person of high rank.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Latimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Any body of men united by profession, or
constituting a community of a particular character; <as>as, the civil
and ecclesiastical <ex>states</ex>, or the lords spiritual and temporal
and the commons, in Great Britain</as>. Cf. <er>Estate</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 6.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>The principal persons in a government.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bold design<br/
Pleased highly those infernal <qex>states</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>The bodies that constitute the legislature of a
country; <as>as, the <ex>States</ex>-general of Holland</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A form of government which is not monarchial, as a
republic.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Well monarchies may own religion's name,<br/
But <qex>states</qex> are atheists in their very fame.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>A political body, or body politic; the whole body
of people who are united under one government, whatever may be the form
of the government; a nation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Municipal law is a rule of conduct prescribed by the supreme power
in a <qex>state</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Puritans in the reign of Mary, driven from their homes, sought
an asylum in Geneva, where they found a <qex>state</qex> without a king,
and a church without a bishop.</q> <rj><qau>R. Choate.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>In the United States, one of the commonwealths, or
bodies politic, the people of which make up the body of the nation, and
which, under the national constitution, stand in certain specified
relations with the national government, and are invested, as
commonwealths, with full power in their several spheres over all matters
not expressly inhibited.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <xex>State</xex>, in its technical sense, is
used in distinction from the federal system, <it>i. e.</it>, the
government of the United States.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>Highest and stationary condition, as that of
maturity between growth and decline, or as that of crisis between the
increase and the abating of a disease; height; acme.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ When <xex>state</xex> is joined with another word, or
used adjectively, it denotes public, or what belongs to the community or
body politic, or to the government; also, what belongs to the States
severally in the American Union; as, <xex>state</xex> affairs;
<xex>state</xex> policy; <xex>State</xex> laws of Iowa.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Nascent state</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Nascent</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Secretary of state</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Secretary</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</cd> -- <col><b>State
barge</b></col><cd>a royal barge, or a barge belonging to a
government.</cd> -- <col><b>State bed</b></col>, <cd>an elaborately
carved or decorated bed.</cd> -- <col><b>State carriage</b></col>, <cd>a
highly decorated carriage for officials going in state, or taking part
in public processions.</cd> -- <col><b>State paper</b></col>, <cd>an
official paper relating to the interests or government of a state.</cd>
<au>Jay.</au> -- <col><b>State prison</b></col>, <cd>a public prison or
penitentiary; -- called also <altname>State's prison</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>State prisoner</b></col>, <cd>one in confinement, or under
arrest, for a political offense.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>State
rights</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>States' rights</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>the rights of the several independent States, as distinguished from
the rights of the Federal government. It has been a question as to what
rights have been vested in the general government.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>State's evidence</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Probator</er>, 2, and under <er>Evidence</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>State
sword</b></col>, <cd>a sword used on state occasions, being borne before
a sovereign by an attendant of high rank.</cd> -- <col><b>State
trial</b></col>, <cd>a trial of a person for a political offense.</cd>
-- <col><b>States of the Church</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Ecclesiastical</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>State</er>, <er>Situation</er>,
<er>Condition</er>.</syn> <usage><xex>State</xex> is the generic term,
and denotes in general the mode in which a thing stands or exists. The
<xex>situation</xex> of a thing is its state in reference to external
objects and influences; its <xex>condition</xex> is its internal state,
or what it is in itself considered. Our <xex>situation</xex> is good or
bad as outward things bear favorably or unfavorably upon us; our
<xex>condition</xex> is good or bad according to the state we are
actually in as respects our persons, families, property, and other
things which comprise our sources of enjoyment.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do not, brother,<br/
Infer as if I thought my sister's <qex>state</qex><br/
Secure without all doubt or controversy.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We hoped to enjoy with ease what, in our <qex>situation</qex>,
might be called the luxuries of life.</q> <rj><qau>Cook.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And, O, what man's <qex>condition</qex> can be worse<br/
Than his whom plenty starves and blessings curse?</q>
<rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>State</ent><br/
<hw>State</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Stately.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Belonging to the state, or body politic;
public.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>State</ent><br/
<hw>State</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To set; to settle; to
establish.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I myself, though meanest <qex>stated</qex>,<br/
And in court now almost hated.</q> <rj><qau>Wither.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who calls the council, <qex>states</qex> the certain day.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To express the particulars of; to set down in detail
or in gross; to represent fully in words; to narrate; to recite; <as>as,
to <ex>state</ex> the facts of a case, one's opinion,
etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To state it</b></col>. <cd>To assume state or
dignity.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Rarely dressed up, and taught
<xex>to state it</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Beau. & Fl.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>State</ent><br/
<hw>State</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A statement; also, a document
containing a statement.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statecraft</ent><br/
<hw>State"craft`</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"kr<adot/ft`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The art of conducting state affairs; state management;
statesmanship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1406 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Stated</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ed</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Settled; established; fixed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is capable of corruption who receives more than what is the
<qex>stated</qex> and unquestionable fee of his office.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Recurring at regular times; not occasional; <as>as,
<ex>stated</ex> preaching; <ex>stated</ex> business
hours</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statedly</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ed*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>At stated times;
regularly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stateful</ent><br/
<hw>State"ful</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Full of state; stately.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A
<xex>stateful</xex> silence.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statehood</ent><br/
<hw>State"hood</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"h<oocr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The condition of being a State; <as>as, a territory seeking
<ex>Statehood</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statehouse</ent><br/
<hw>State"house`</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"hous`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
building in which a State legislature holds its sessions; a State
capitol.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stateless</ent><br/
<hw>State"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without state or pomp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statelily</ent><br/
<hw>State"li*ly</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"l<icr/*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In a stately manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stateliness</ent><br/
<hw>State"li*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For <qex>stateliness</qex> and majesty, what is comparable to a
horse?</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stately</ent><br/
<hw>State"ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Statelier</adjf> <pr>(st<amac/t"l<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Stateliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Evincing
state or dignity; lofty; majestic; grand; <as>as, <ex>stately</ex>
manners; a <ex>stately</ex> gait</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>stately</xex> homes of England!<rdquo/ <au>Mrs. Hemans.</au>
<ldquo/Filled with <xex>stately</xex> temples.<rdquo/
<au>Prescott.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here is a silly <qex>stately</qex> style indeed!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Lofty; dignified; majestic; grand; august;
magnificent.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stately</ent><br/
<hw>State"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Majestically; loftily.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statement</ent><br/
<hw>State"ment</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"m<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stating, reciting, or presenting, orally or
on paper; <as>as, to interrupt a speaker in the <ex>statement</ex> of
his case</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is stated; a formal embodiment in
language of facts or opinions; a narrative; a recital.</def>
<ldquo/Admirable perspicuity of <xex>statement</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Brougham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statemonger</ent><br/
<hw>State"mon`ger</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"m<ucr/<nsm/`g<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One versed in politics, or one who dabbles in state
affairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>State prison</ent><br/
<hw>State` pris"on</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t` pr<icr/z"'n)</pr>. <def>See
under <er>State</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stater</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"er</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
who states.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stater</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sta"ter</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>stater</ets>, Gr. <grk>stath`r</grk>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>The principal gold coin of ancient Greece. It varied
much in value, the stater best known at Athens being worth about
<pound/1 2s., or about $5.35 (in 1890 value). The Attic silver
<altname>tetradrachm</altname> was in later times called
<ex>stater</ex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stateroom</ent><br/
<hw>State"room`</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"r<oomac/m`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A magnificent room in a palace or great
house.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small apartment for lodging or sleeping in the
cabin, or on the deck, of a vessel; also, a somewhat similar apartment
in a railway sleeping car.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>States-general</ent><br/
<hw>States"-gen"er*al</hw> <pr>(st<amac/ts"j<ecr/n"<etil/r*<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In France, before the Revolution, the
assembly of the three orders of the kingdom, namely, the clergy, the
nobility, and the third estate, or commonalty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the Netherlands, the legislative body, composed
of two chambers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statesman</ent><br/
<hw>States"man</hw> <pr>(-m<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Statesmen</plw> <pr>(-m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A man versed in public affairs and in the principles
and art of government; especially, one eminent for political
abilities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The minds of some of our <qex>statesmen</qex>, like the pupil of
the human eye, contract themselves the more, the stronger light there is
shed upon them.</q> <rj><qau>Moore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One occupied with the affairs of government, and
influential in shaping its policy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small landholder.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statesmanlike</ent><br/
<hw>States"man*like`</hw> <pr>(-l<imac/k`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Having the manner or wisdom of statesmen; becoming a
statesman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statesmanly</ent><br/
<hw>States"man*ly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Becoming a
statesman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statesmanship</ent><br/
<hw>States"man*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The qualifications, duties,
or employments of a statesman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>State socialism</ent><br/
<hw>State socialism</hw>. <def>A form of socialism, esp. advocated in
Germany, which, while retaining the right of private property and the
institution of the family and other features of the present form of the
state, would intervene by various measures intended to give or maintain
equality of opportunity, as compulsory state insurance, old-age
pensions, etc., answering closely to socialism of the chair.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stateswoman</ent><br/
<hw>States"wom`an</hw> <pr>(-w<oocr/m`<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stateswomen</plw>
<pr>(-w<icr/m`<ecr/n)</pr>.</plu> <def>A woman concerned in public
affairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A rare <qex>stateswoman</qex>; I admire her bearing.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stathmograph</ent><br/
<hw>Stath"mo*graph</hw> <pr>(st<acr/th"m<osl/*gr<adot/f)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>sta`qmh</grk> a measuring line +
<ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>A contrivance for recording the speed of
a railway train.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statical</ent><br/
<ent>Static</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stat"ic</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <hw>Stat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(-<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>statiko`s</grk> causing to stand, skilled in weighing, fr.
<grk>'ista`nai</grk> to cause to stand: cf. F. <ets>statique</ets>. See
<er>Stand</er>, and cf. <er>Stage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Resting;
acting by mere weight without motion; <as>as, <ex>statical</ex>
pressure; <ex>static</ex> objects.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to bodies at rest or in
equilibrium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Static electricity</b></col>, <col><b>Statical
electricity</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Electricity</er>, 1.</cd> -- <col><b>Statical moment</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Moment</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statically</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a statical
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statics</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ics</hw> <pr>(-<icr/ks)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>statique</ets>, Gr. <grk>statikh`</grk> the art of weighing, fr.
<grk>statiko`s</grk>. See <er>Static</er>.]</ety> <def>That branch of
mechanics which treats of the equilibrium of forces, or relates to
bodies as held at rest by the forces acting on them; -- distinguished
from <contr>dynamics</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Social statics</b></col>, <cd>the study of the conditions
which concern the existence and permanence of the social
state.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stating</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ing</hw> <pr>(st<amac/t"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
act of one who states anything; statement; <as>as, the
<ex>stating</ex>of one's opinions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Station</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
fr. L. <ets>statio</ets>, from <ets>stare</ets>, <ets>statum</ets>, to
stand. See <er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of standing;
also, attitude or pose in standing; posture.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>station</qex> like the herald, Mercury.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their manner was to stand at prayer, whereupon their meetings unto
that purpose . . . had the names of <qex>stations</qex> given them.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A state of standing or rest; equilibrium.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All progression is performed by drawing on or impelling forward
some part which was before in <qex>station</qex>, or at quiet.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The spot or place where anything stands, especially
where a person or thing habitually stands, or is appointed to remain for
a time; <as>as, the <ex>station</ex> of a sentinel</as>.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A regular stopping place in a stage road
or route; a place where railroad trains regularly come to a stand, for
the convenience of passengers, taking in fuel, moving freight,
etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The headquarters of the police force of any
precinct.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The place at which an instrument is
planted, or observations are made, as in surveying.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The particular place, or kind of situation, in
which a species naturally occurs; a habitat.</def> <sd>(e)</sd>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A place to which ships may resort, and where
they may anchor safely.</def> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>A place or region to
which a government ship or fleet is assigned for duty.</def>
<sd>(g)</sd> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A place calculated for the
rendezvous of troops, or for the distribution of them; also, a spot well
adapted for offensive or defensive measures.</def> <au>Wilhelm (Mil.
Dict.)</au>. <sd>(h)</sd> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>An enlargement in a
shaft or galley, used as a landing, or passing place, or for the
accommodation of a pump, tank, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Post assigned; office; the part or department of
public duty which a person is appointed to perform; sphere of duty or
occupation; employment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By spending this day [Sunday] in religious exercises, we acquire
new strength and resolution to perform God's will in our several
<qex>stations</qex> the week following.</q> <rj><qau>R.
Nelson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Situation; position; location.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fig and date -- why love they to remain<br/
In middle <qex>station</qex>, and an even plain?</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>State; rank; condition of life; social
status.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The greater part have kept, I see,<br/
Their <qex>station</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They in France of the best rank and <qex>station</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The fast of the
fourth and sixth days of the week, Wednesday and Friday, in memory of
the council which condemned Christ, and of his passion.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>A church in which the
procession of the clergy halts on stated days to say stated
prayers.</def> <au>Addis & Arnold.</au> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>One of the
places at which ecclesiastical processions pause for the performance of
an act of devotion; formerly, the tomb of a martyr, or some similarly
consecrated spot; now, especially, one of those representations of the
successive stages of our Lord's passion which are often placed round the
naves of large churches and by the side of the way leading to sacred
edifices or shrines, and which are visited in rotation, stated services
being performed at each; -- called also <altname>Station of the
cross</altname>.</def> <au>Fairholt.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>In Australia, a sheep run or cattle run, together
with the buildings belonging to it; also, the homestead and buildings
belonging to such a run.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Station bill</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<cref>Quarter bill</cref>, under <er>Quarter</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Station house</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The house serving for
the headquarters of the police assigned to a certain district, and as a
place of temporary confinement</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The house used as
a shelter at a railway station.</cd> -- <col><b>Station
master</b></col>, <cd>one who has charge of a station, esp. of a railway
station.</cd> -- <col><b>Station pointer</b></col> <fld>(Surv.)</fld>,
<cd>an instrument for locating on a chart the position of a place from
which the angles subtended by three distant objects, whose positions are
known, have been observed.</cd> -- <col><b>Station staff</b></col>
<fld>(Surv.)</fld>, <cd>an instrument for taking angles in
surveying.</cd> <au>Craig.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Station</er>, <er>Depot</er>.</syn> <usage>
In the United States, a stopping place on a railway for passengers and
freight is commonly called a <xex>depot</xex>: but to a considerable
extent in official use, and in common speech, the more appropriate name,
<xex>station</xex>, has been adopted.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Station</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stationed</conjf>
<pr>(-sh<ucr/nd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stationing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To place; to set; to appoint
or assign to the occupation of a post, place, or office; <as>as, to
<ex>station</ex> troops on the right of an army; to <ex>station</ex> a
sentinel on a rampart; to <ex>station</ex> ships on the coast of
Africa</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He gained the brow of the hill, where the English phalanx was
<qex>stationed</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Lyttelton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stational</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion*al</hw> <pr>(-<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stationalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>stationnale</ets>
(<ets><eacute/glise</ets>).]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
station.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stationariness</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion*a*ri*ness</hw> <pr>(-<asl/*r<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being stationary;
fixity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stationary</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(-<asl/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stationarius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>stationnaire</ets>. Cf.
<er>Stationer</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not moving; not appearing to
move; stable; fixed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Charles Wesley, who is a more <qex>stationary</qex> man, does not
believe the story.</q> <rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not improving or getting worse; not growing wiser,
greater, better, more excellent, or the contrary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Appearing to be at rest, because moving in the line
of vision; not progressive or retrograde, as a planet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stationary air</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>the
air which under ordinary circumstances does not leave the lungs in
respiration.</cd> -- <col><b>Stationary engine</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>A steam engine that is permanently placed, in distinction from a
portable engine, locomotive, marine engine, etc.</cd>
<specif>Specifically:</specif> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A factory engine, in
distinction from a blowing, pumping, or other kind of engine which is
also permanently placed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stationary</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion*a*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ries</plw> <pr>(-r<icr/z)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who, or that which,
is stationary, as a planet when apparently it has neither progressive
nor retrograde motion.</def> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stationer</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion*er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Stationary</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A bookseller
or publisher; -- formerly so called from his occupying a stand, or
<xex>station</xex>, in the market place or elsewhere.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who sells paper, pens, quills, inkstands,
pencils, blank books, and other articles used in writing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stationery</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion*er*y</hw> <pr>(-<ecr/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
articles usually sold by stationers, as paper, pens, ink, quills, blank
books, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stationery</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tion*er*y</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Belonging to, or sold by, a
stationer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statism</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tism</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<icr/z'm)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[From <er>State</er>.]</ety> <def>The art of governing a state;
statecraft; policy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The enemies of God . . . call our religion <qex>statism</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statist</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tist</hw> <pr>(-t<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>State</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A statesman; a politician; one
skilled in government.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Statists</qex> indeed,<br/
And lovers of their country.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A statistician.</def> <rj><au>Fawcett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statistical</ent><br/
<ent>Statistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sta*tis"tic</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*t<icr/s"t<icr/k)</pr>,
<hw>Sta*tis"tic*al</hw> <pr>(-t<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>statistique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to statistics; <as>as, <ex>statistical</ex> knowledge;
<ex>statistical</ex> tabulation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statistically</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*tis"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the way of
statistics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statistician</ent><br/
<hw>Stat`is*ti"cian</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t`<icr/s*t<icr/sh"<ait/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>statisticien</ets>.]</ety> <def>One
versed in statistics; one who collects and classifies facts for
statistics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statistics</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*tis"tics</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*t<icr/s"t<icr/ks)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>statistique</ets>, G.
<ets>statistik</ets>. See <er>State</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The science which has to do with the collection,
classification, and analysis of facts of a numerical nature regarding
any topic.</def> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>The science dealing
with collection, tabulation, and analysis of facts respecting the
condition of the people in a state.</def> <note>[In this sense
grammatically singular.]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Classified facts of a numerical
nature regarding any topic.</def> <specif>Specifically:</specif>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Numerical facts respecting the condition of the people
in a state, their health, their longevity, domestic economy, arts,
property, and political strength, their resources, the state of the
country, etc., or respecting any particular class or interest;
especially, those facts which can be stated in numbers, or in tables of
numbers, or in any tabular and classified arrangement.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Sport)</fld> <def>Numerical facts regarding the
performance of athletes or athletic teams, such as winning percentages,
numbers of games won or lost in a season, batting averages (for baseball
players), total yards gained (for football players). The creation and
classification of such numbers is limited only by the imagination of
those wishing to describe athletic performance numerically.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> stats.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The branch of mathematics which studies methods for
the calculation of probabilities.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statistology</ent><br/
<hw>Stat`is*tol"o*gy</hw>
<pr>(st<acr/t`<icr/s*t<ocr/l"<osl/*j<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Statistics</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Statistics</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stative</ent><br/
<hw>Sta"tive</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<icr/v)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stativus</ets>, fr. <ets>stare</ets>, <ets>statum</ets>, to
stand.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a fixed camp,
or military posts or quarters.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statoblast</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"o*blast</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t"<osl/*bl<acr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>stato`s</grk> standing (<it>i. e.</it>, remaining) +
<ets>-blast</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of a peculiar kind
of internal buds, or germs, produced in the interior of certain Bryozoa
and sponges, especially in the fresh-water species; -- also called
<altname>winter buds</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They are protected by a firm covering, and are usually
destined to perpetuate the species during the winter season. They burst
open and develop in the spring. In some fresh-water sponges they serve
to preserve the species during the dry season. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
under <er>Phylactolaemata</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*toc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*t<ocr/k"r<adot/*s<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>State</ets> + <ets>-cracy</ets>, as in
<ets>democracy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Government by the state, or by
political power, in distinction from government by ecclesiastical
power.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>O. A. Brownson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>stator</ent><br/
<hw>sta"tor</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<etil/r; st<acr/t"<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A stationary part in or about
which another part (the rotor) revolves, esp. when both are large;</def>
<specif>as,</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>The stationary
member of an electrical machine, as of an induction motor.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Steam Turbine)</fld> <def>The case inclosing a
turbine wheel; the body of stationary blades or nozzles.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>stats</ent><br/
<hw>stats</hw> <pr>(st<acr/ts)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Shortened form
of <er>statistics</er>{2} <sd>(b)</sd>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statua</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"u*a</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t"<usl/*<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A statue.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They spake not a word;<br/
But, like dumb <qex>statuas</qex> or breathing stones,<br/
Gazed each on other.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statuary</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"u*a*ry</hw> <pr>(-<asl/*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Statuaries</plw> <pr>(-r<icr/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L. <ets>statuarius</ets>, n., fr. <ets>statuarius</ets>, a., of or
belonging to statues, fr. <ets>statua</ets> statue: cf. F.
<ets>statuaire</ets>. See <er>Statue</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who practices the art of making statues.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On other occasions the <qex>statuaries</qex> took their subjects
from the poets.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[L. <ets>statuaria</ets> (sc. <ets>ars</ets>): cf.
F. <ets>statuaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art of carving statues or
images as representatives of real persons or things; a branch of
sculpture.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Temple.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A collection of statues; statues,
collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statue</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ue</hw> <pr>(st<acr/ch"<oomac/; also, st<acr/t"<usl/;
135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L. <ets>statua</ets> (akin to
<ets>stativus</ets> standing still), fr. <ets>stare</ets>,
<ets>statum</ets>, to stand. See <er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The likeness of a living being sculptured or modeled in some solid
substance, as marble, bronze, or wax; an image; <as>as, a
<ex>statue</ex> of Hercules, or of a lion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will raise her <qex>statue</qex> in pure gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A portrait.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statue</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ue</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Statued</conjf> <pr>(-<usl/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Statuing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To place, as a statue; to form a
statue of; to make into a statue.</def> <ldquo/The whole man becomes as
if <xex>statued</xex> into stone and earth.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statued</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ued</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t"<usl/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Adorned
with statues.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>statued</xex> hall.<rdquo/
<au>Longfellow.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Statued</xex> niches.<rdquo/ <au>G.
Eliot.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statueless</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ue*less</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t"<usl/*l<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Without a statue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statuelike</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ue*like`</hw> <pr>(st<acr/ch"<oomac/*l<imac/k` <it>or</it>
st<acr/t"<usl/*l<imac/k`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a statue;
motionless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statuesque</ent><br/
<hw>Stat`u*esque"</hw> <pr>(st<acr/ch`<oomac/*<ecr/sk" <it>or</it>
st<acr/t`<usl/*<ecr/sk")</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partaking of, or
exemplifying, the characteristics of a statue; having the symmetry, or
other excellence, of a statue artistically made; <as>as,
<ex>statuesque</ex>limbs; a <ex>statuesque</ex> attitude</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their characters are mostly <qex>statuesque</qex> even in this
respect, that they have no background.</q> <rj><qau>Hare.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statuesquely</ent><br/
<hw>Stat`u*esque"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a statuesque manner;
in a way suggestive of a statue; like a statue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A character <qex>statuesquely</qex> simple in its details.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statuette</ent><br/
<hw>Stat`u*ette"</hw> <pr>(st<acr/ch`<oomac/*<ecr/t" <it>or</it>
st<acr/t`<usl/*<ecr/t")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., cf. It.
<ets>statuetta</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small statue; -- usually applied to
a figure much less than life size, especially when of marble or bronze,
or of plaster or clay as a preparation for the marble or bronze, as
distinguished from a figure in terra cotta or the like. Cf.
<er>Figurine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statuminate</ent><br/
<hw>Sta*tu"mi*nate</hw> <pr>(st<adot/*t<umac/"m<icr/*n<amac/t)</pr>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>statuminatus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>statuminare</ets> to prop, fr. <ets>statumen</ets> a prop, fr.
<ets>statuere</ets> to place.]</ety> <def>To prop or support.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stature</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ure</hw> <pr>(st<acr/ch"<etil/r; <it>also</it>,
st<acr/t"<usl/r; 135)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>stature</ets>,
OF. <ets>estature</ets>, from L. <ets>statura</ets>, originally, an
upright posture; hence, height or size of the body, from
<ets>stare</ets>, <ets>statum</ets>, to stand. See
<er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <def>The natural height of an animal body; --
generally used of the human body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Foreign men of mighty <qex>stature</qex> came.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statured</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ured</hw> <pr>(-<usl/rd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Arrived at
full stature.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Status</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sta"tus</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <def>State; condition; position of affairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Status quo</ent><br/
<ent>Status in quo</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Sta"tus in` quo"</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<ucr/s <icr/n`
kw<omac/")</pr>, \'d8<hw>Sta"tus quo"</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"t<ucr/s
kw<omac/")</pr>.}</mhw> <ety>[L., state in which.]</ety> <def>The state
in which anything is already. The phrase is also used retrospectively,
as when, on a treaty of peace, matters return to the <it>status quo ante
bellum</it>, or are left <it>in statu quo ante bellum</it>, i.e., the
state (or, in the state) before the war. The form <ex>status quo</ex> is
in most common use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statutable</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"u*ta*ble</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t"<usl/*t<adot/*b'l ; 135)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Made or introduced by statute; proceeding
from an act of the legistature; <as>as, a <ex>statutable</ex> provision
or remedy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Made or being in conformity to statute; standard;
<as>as, <ex>statutable</ex> measures</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statutably</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"u*ta*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Conformably to
statute.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Statute</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"ute</hw> <pr>(-<usl/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>statut</ets>, LL. <ets>statutum</ets>, from L. <ets>statutus</ets>,
p. p. of <ets>statuere</ets> to set, station, ordain, fr.
<ets>status</ets> position, station, fr. <ets>stare</ets>,
<ets>statum</ets>, to stand. See <er>Stand</er>, and cf.
<er>Constitute</er>, <er>Destitute</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An act
of the legislature of a state or country, declaring, commanding, or
prohibiting something; a positive law; the written will of the
legislature expressed with all the requisite forms of legislation; --
used in distinction from <cref>common law</cref>. See <cref>Common
law</cref>, under <er>Common</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Statute</xex> is commonly applied to the acts of a
legislative body consisting of representatives. In monarchies, the laws
of the sovereign are called <xex>edicts</xex>, <xex>decrees</xex>,
<xex>ordinances</xex>, <xex>rescripts</xex>, etc. In works on
international law and in the Roman law, the term is used as embracing
all laws imposed by competent authority. Statutes in this sense are
divided into statutes real, statutes personal, and statutes mixed;
<xex>statutes real</xex> applying to immovables; <xex>statutes
personal</xex> to movables; and <xex>statutes mixed</xex> to both
classes of property.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An act of a corporation or of its founder, intended
as a permanent rule or law; <as>as, the <ex>statutes</ex> of a
university</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An assemblage of farming servants (held possibly by
statute) for the purpose of being hired; -- called also <altname>statute
fair</altname>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> Cf. 3d <er>Mop</er>, 2.
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Statute book</b></col>, <cd>a record of laws or
legislative acts.</cd> <au>Blackstone.</au> -- <col><b>Statute
cap</b></col>, <cd>a kind of woolen cap; -- so called because enjoined
to be worn by a statute, dated in 1571, in behalf of the trade of
cappers.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Halliwell.</au> -- <col><b>Statute
fair</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Statute</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3, above.</cd>
-- <col><b>Statute labor</b></col>, <cd>a definite amount of labor
required for the public service in making roads, bridges, etc., as in
certain English colonies.</cd> -- <col><b>Statute merchant</b></col>
<fld>(Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>a bond of record pursuant to the stat. 13
Edw. I., acknowledged in form prescribed, on which, if not paid at the
day, an execution might be awarded against the body, lands, and goods of
the debtor, and the obligee might hold the lands until out of the rents
and profits of them the debt was satisfied; -- called also a
<altname>pocket judgment</altname>. It is now fallen into disuse.</cd>
<au>Tomlins.</au> <au>Bouvier.</au> -- <col><b>Statute mile</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Mile</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Statute of
limitations</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a statute assigning a
certain time, after which rights can not be enforced by action.</cd> --
<col><b>Statute staple</b></col>, <cd>a bond of record acknowledged
before the mayor of the staple, by virtue of which the creditor may, on
nonpayment, forthwith have execution against the body, lands, and goods
of the debtor, as in the <xex>statute merchant</xex>. It is now
disused.</cd> <au>Blackstone.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Act; regulation; edict; decree. See
<er>Law</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1407 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Statutory</ent><br/
<hw>Stat"u*to*ry</hw> <pr>(st<acr/t"<usl/*t<osl/*r<ycr/; 135)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Enacted by statute; depending on statute for its
authority; <as>as, a <ex>statutory</ex> provision</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staunchness</ent><br/
<ent>Staunchly</ent><br/
<ent>Staunch</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Staunch</hw> <pr>(st<aum/nch)</pr>, <hw>Staunch"ly</hw>,
<hw>Staunch"ness</hw>, etc. }</mhw> <def>See <er>Stanch</er>,
<er>Stanchly</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staurolite</ent><br/
<hw>Stau"ro*lite</hw> <pr>(st<add/"r<osl/*l<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>stayro`s</grk> a cross + <ets>-lite</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A mineral of a brown to black color occurring in
prismatic crystals, often twinned so as to form groups resembling a
cross. It is a silicate of alumina and iron, and is generally found
imbedded in mica schist. Called also <altname>granatite</altname>, and
<altname>grenatite</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staurolitic</ent><br/
<hw>Stau`ro*lit"ic</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to staurolite; resembling or
containing staurolite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stauroscope</ent><br/
<hw>Stau"ro*scope</hw> <pr>(st<add/"r<osl/*sk<omac/p)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>stayro`s</grk> a cross +
<ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Crystallog.)</fld> <def>An optical
instrument used in determining the position of the planes of
light-vibration in sections of crystals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staurotide</ent><br/
<hw>Stau"ro*tide</hw> <pr>(st<add/"r<osl/*t<imac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F. <ets>staurotide</ets>, from Gr. <grk>stayrwto`s</grk> cruciform
(from Gr. <grk>stayro`s</grk> a cross) + <grk>e'i^dos</grk> form.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Staurolite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stave</ent><br/
<hw>Stave</hw> <pr>(st<amac/v)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Staff</er>, and corresponding to the pl. <ets>staves</ets>. See
<er>Staff</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One of a number of narrow strips
of wood, or narrow iron plates, placed edge to edge to form the sides,
covering, or lining of a vessel or structure; esp., one of the strips
which form the sides of a cask, a pail, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One of the cylindrical bars of a lantern wheel; one
of the bars or rounds of a rack, a ladder, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A metrical portion; a stanza; a staff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let us chant a passing <qex>stave</qex><br/
In honor of that hero brave.</q> <rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The five horizontal and parallel
lines on and between which musical notes are written or printed; the
staff{7}.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stave jointer</b></col>, <cd>a machine for dressing the
edges of staves.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stave</ent><br/
<hw>Stave</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Staved</conjf> <pr>(st<amac/vd)</pr> or <conjf>Stove</conjf>
<pr>(st<omac/v)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Staving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Stave</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, or <er>Staff</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To break in a stave or the staves of; to break a hole in; to burst;
-- often with <xex>in</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>stave</ex> a cask; to
<ex>stave</ex> in a boat.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To push, as with a staff; -- with
<xex>off</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The condition of a servant <qex>staves</qex> him off to a
distance.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To delay by force or craft; to drive away; --
usually with <xex>off</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>stave</ex> off the execution
of a project</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And answered with such craft as women use,<br/
Guilty or guiltless, to <qex>stave</qex> off a chance<br/
That breaks upon them perilously.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To suffer, or cause, to be lost by breaking the
cask.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the wine in the city has been <qex>staved</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To furnish with staves or rundles.</def>
<rj><au>Knolles.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To render impervious or solid by driving with a
calking iron; <as>as, to <ex>stave</ex> lead, or the joints of pipes
into which lead has been run</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stave and tail</b></col>, <cd>in bear baiting,
(<xex>to stave</xex>) to interpose with the staff, doubtless to stop the
bear; (<xex>to tail</xex>) to hold back the dog by the tail.</cd>
<au>Nares.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stave</ent><br/
<hw>Stave</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To burst in pieces by striking
against something; to dash into fragments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like a vessel of glass she <qex>stove</qex> and sank.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staves</ent><br/
<hw>Staves</hw> <pr>(st<amac/vz <or/ st<aum/vz ; 277)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos>; <def><pos>pl.</pos> of <er>Staff</er>.</def>
<ldquo/Banners, scarves and <xex>staves</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>R.
Browning.</au> <def2>Also <pr>(st<amac/vz)</pr>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Stave</er>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stavesacre</ent><br/
<hw>Staves"a`cre</hw> <pr>(st<amac/vz"<amac/`k<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted from NL. <ets>staphis agria</ets>, Gr.
<grk>stafi`s</grk> dried grape + <grk>'a`grios</grk> wild.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A kind of larkspur (<spn>Delphinium
Staphysagria</spn>), and its seeds, which are violently purgative and
emetic. They are used as a parasiticide, and in the East for poisoning
fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stavewood</ent><br/
<hw>Stave"wood`</hw> <pr>(st<amac/v"w<oocr/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A tall tree (<spn>Simaruba amara</spn>) growing
in tropical America. It is one of the trees which yields
quassia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staving</ent><br/
<hw>Stav"ing</hw> <pr>(st<amac/v"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
casing or lining of staves; especially, one encircling a water
wheel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staw</ent><br/
<hw>Staw</hw> <pr>(st<add/)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>staae</ets> to stand, Sw. <ets>st<aring/</ets>. <root/163.]</ety>
<def>To be fixed or set; to stay.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stay</ent><br/
<hw>Stay</hw> <pr>(st<amac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>staeg</ets>, akin to D., G., Icel., Sw., & Dan. <ets>stag</ets>;
cf. OF. <ets>estai</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tai</ets>, of Teutonic
origin.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A large, strong rope, employed to
support a mast, by being extended from the head of one mast down to some
other, or to some part of the vessel. Those which lead forward are
called <xex>fore-and-aft stays</xex>; those which lead to the vessel's
side are called <xex>backstays</xex>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Ship</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>In stays</b></col>, <or/ <col><b>Hove in
stays</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>in the act or situation
of staying, or going about from one tack to another.</cd> <au>R. H.
Dana, Jr.</au> -- <col><b>Stay holes</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>openings in the edge of a staysail through which the hanks pass
which join it to the stay.</cd> -- <col><b>Stay tackle</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a tackle attached to a stay and used for
hoisting or lowering heavy articles over the side.</cd> -- <col><b>To
miss stays</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to fail in the attempt to
go about.</cd> <au>Totten.</au> -- <col><b>Triatic stay</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a rope secured at the ends to the heads of the
foremast and mainmast with thimbles spliced to its bight into which the
stay tackles hook.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stay</ent><br/
<hw>Stay</hw> <pr>(st<amac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stayed</conjf> <pr>(st<amac/d)</pr> or
<conjf>Staid</conjf> <pr>(st<amac/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Staying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>estayer</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/tayer</ets> to prop, fr. OF. <ets>estai</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/tai</ets>, a prop, probably fr. OD. <ets>stade</ets>,
<ets>staeye</ets>, a prop, akin to E. <ets>stead</ets>; or cf.
<ets>stay</ets> a rope to support a mast. Cf. <er>Staid</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, <er>Stay</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To stop from motion or falling; to prop; to fix firmly; to hold up;
to support.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Aaron and Hur <qex>stayed</qex> up his hands, the one on the one
side, and the other on the other side.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xvii.
12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sallows and reeds . . . for vineyards useful found<br/
To <qex>stay</qex> thy vines.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To support from sinking; to sustain with strength;
to satisfy in part or for the time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He has devoured a whole loaf of bread and butter, and it has not
<qex>staid</qex> his stomach for a minute.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To bear up under; to endure; to support; to resist
successfully.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She will not <qex>stay</qex> the siege of loving terms,<br/
Nor bide the encounter of assailing eyes.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To hold from proceeding; to withhold; to restrain;
to stop; to hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him backward overthrew and down him <qex>stayed</qex><br/
With their rude hands and grisly grapplement.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All that may <qex>stay</qex> their minds from thinking that true
which they heartily wish were false.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To hinder; to delay; to detain; to keep
back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your ships are <qex>stayed</qex> at Venice.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This business <qex>staid</qex> me in London almost a week.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I was willing to <qex>stay</qex> my reader on an argument that
appeared to me new.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To remain for the purpose of; to wait for.</def>
<ldquo/I <xex>stay</xex> dinner there.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To cause to cease; to put an end to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stay</qex> your strife.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For flattering planets seemed to say<br/
This child should ills of ages <qex>stay</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Engin.)</fld> <def>To fasten or secure with stays;
<as>as, to <ex>stay</ex> a flat sheet in a steam boiler</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To tack, as a vessel, so that the
other side of the vessel shall be presented to the wind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stay a mast</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to
incline it forward or aft, or to one side, by the stays and
backstays.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stay</ent><br/
<hw>Stay</hw> <pr>(st<amac/)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[<root/163. See
<er>Stay</er> to hold up, prop.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To remain; to
continue in a place; to abide fixed for a space of time; to stop; to
stand still.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She would command the hasty sun to <qex>stay</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stay</qex>, I command you; <qex>stay</qex> and hear me
first.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>stay</qex> a little longer, as one <qex>stays</qex><br/
To cover up the embers that still burn.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To continue in a state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The flames augment, and <qex>stay</qex><br/
At their full height, then languish to decay.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To wait; to attend; to forbear to act.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I 'll tell thee all my whole device<br/
When I am in my coach, which <qex>stays</qex> for us.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The father can not <qex>stay</qex> any longer for the fortune.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To dwell; to tarry; to linger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I must <qex>stay</qex> a little on one action.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To rest; to depend; to rely; to stand; to
insist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>stay</qex> here on my bond.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye despise this word, and trust in oppression and perverseness,
and <qex>stay</qex> thereon.</q> <rj><qau>Isa. xxx. 12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To come to an end; to cease; <as>as, that day the
storm <ex>stayed</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here my commission <qex>stays</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To hold out in a race or other contest; <as>as, a
horse <ex>stays</ex> well</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To change tack, as a
ship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stay</ent><br/
<hw>Stay</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>estai</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/tai</ets> support, and E. <ets>stay</ets> a rope to support
a mast.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which serves as a prop; a
support.</def> <ldquo/My only strength and <xex>stay</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trees serve as so many <qex>stays</qex> for their vines.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lord Liverpool is the single <qex>stay</qex> of this ministry.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A corset stiffened with whalebone
or other material, worn by women, and rarely by men.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How the strait <qex>stays</qex> the slender waist constrain.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Continuance in a place; abode for a space of time;
sojourn; <as>as, you make a short <ex>stay</ex> in this
city</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Make haste, and leave thy business and thy care;<br/
No mortal interest can be worth thy <qex>stay</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Embrace the hero and his <qex>stay</qex> implore.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Cessation of motion or progression; stand;
stop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Made of sphere metal, never to decay<br/
Until his revolution was at <qex>stay</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Affairs of state seemed rather to stand at a <qex>stay</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Hindrance; let; check.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They were able to read good authors without any <qex>stay</qex>,
if the book were not false.</q> <rj><qau>Robynson (More's
Utopia).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Restraint of passion; moderation; caution;
steadiness; sobriety.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Not grudging that
thy lust hath bounds and <xex>stays</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wisdom, <qex>stay</qex>, and moderation of the king.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With prudent <qex>stay</qex> he long deferred<br/
The rough contention.</q> <rj><qau>Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Engin.)</fld> <def>Strictly, a part in tension to
hold the parts together, or stiffen them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stay bolt</b></col> <fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>a bolt or
short rod, connecting opposite plates, so as to prevent them from being
bulged out when acted upon by a pressure which tends to force them
apart, as in the leg of a steam boiler.</cd> -- <col><b>Stay
busk</b></col>, <cd>a stiff piece of wood, steel, or whalebone, for the
front support of a woman's stays. Cf. <er>Busk</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Stay rod</b></col>, <cd>a rod which acts as a stay, particularly
in a steam boiler.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stayed</ent><br/
<hw>Stayed</hw> <pr>(st<amac/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Staid; fixed;
settled; sober; -- now written <xex>staid</xex>. See
<er>Staid</er>.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au> <au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stayedly</ent><br/
<hw>Stayed"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Staidly. See
<er>Staidly</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stayedness</ent><br/
<hw>Stayed"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Staidness.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>W. Whately.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Solidity; weight.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stayer</ent><br/
<hw>Stay"er</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
upholds or supports that which props; one who, or that which, stays,
stops, or restrains; also, colloquially, a horse, man, etc., that has
endurance, as in a race.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staylace</ent><br/
<hw>Stay"lace`</hw> <pr>(-l<amac/s`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A lace for
fastening stays.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stayless</ent><br/
<hw>Stay"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without stop or delay.</def>
<rj><au>Mir. for Mag.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staymaker</ent><br/
<hw>Stay"mak`er</hw> <pr>(-m<amac/k`<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One whose occupation is to make stays.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staynil</ent><br/
<hw>Stay"nil</hw> <pr>(-n<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The European starling.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Staysail</ent><br/
<hw>Stay"sail`</hw> <pr>(st<amac/"s<amac/l`;<it>among sailors</it>,
st<amac/"s'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Any sail
extended on a stay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stayship</ent><br/
<hw>Stay"ship`</hw> <pr>(-sh<icr/p`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A remora, -- fabled to stop ships by attaching
itself to them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stead</ent><br/
<hw>Stead</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stede</ets> place, AS. <ets>stede</ets>; akin to LG. & D.
<ets>stede</ets>, OS. <ets>stad</ets>, <ets>stedi</ets>, OHG.
<ets>stat</ets>, G. <ets>statt</ets>, <ets>st<aum/tte</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sta<edh/r</ets>, Dan. <ets>sted</ets>, Sw. <ets>stad</ets>, Goth.
<ets>sta<thorn/s</ets>, and E. <ets>stand</ets>. <root/163. See
<er>Stand</er>, and cf. <er>Staith</er>, <er>Stithy</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Place, or spot, in general.</def> <mark>[Obs., except
in composition.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fly, therefore, fly this fearful <qex>stead</qex> anon.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Place or room which another had, has, or might
have.</def> <ldquo/Stewards of your <xex>steads</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In <qex>stead</qex> of bounds, he a pillar set.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A frame on which a bed is laid; a bedstead.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The genial bed,<br/
Sallow the feet, the borders, and the <qex>stead</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A farmhouse and offices.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The word is now commonly used as the last part of a
compound; as, farm<xex>stead</xex>, home<xex>stead</xex>,
road<xex>stead</xex>, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In stead of</b></col>, <cd>in place of. See
<er>Instead</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To stand in stead</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>To do stead</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to be of use or
great advantage.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The smallest act . . . shall <qex>stand</qex> us <qex>in</qex>
great <qex>stead</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here thy sword can <qex>do</qex> thee little <qex>stead</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stead</ent><br/
<hw>Stead</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To help; to support;
to benefit; to assist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Perhaps my succour or advisement meet,<br/
Mote <qex>stead</qex> you much your purpose to subdue.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It nothing <qex>steads</qex> us<br/
To chide him from our eaves.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fill the place of.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steadfast</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"fast</hw> <pr>(-f<adot/st)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stead</ets> + <ets>fast</ets>, that is, fast in place.]</ety>
<ety>[Written also <ets>stedfast</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Firmly
fixed or established; fast fixed; firm.</def> <ldquo/This
<xex>steadfast</xex> globe of earth.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not fickle or wavering; constant; firm; resolute;
unswerving; steady.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Steadfast</xex> eye.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Abide <qex>steadfast</qex> unto him [thy neighbor] in the time of
his trouble.</q> <rj><qau>Ecclus. xxii. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whom resist <qex>steadfast</qex> in the faith.</q> <rj><qau>1 Pet.
v. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steadfastly</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"fast*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a steadfast manner;
firmly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Steadfastly</qex> believe that whatever God has revealed is
infallibly true.</q> <rj><qau>Wake.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steadfastness</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"fast*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being steadfast; firmness; fixedness; constancy.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>steadfastness</xex> of your faith.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Col. ii.
5.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To prove her wifehood and her <qex>steadfastness</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steadily</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"i*ly</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
steady manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steadiness</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
steady.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Steadiness</qex> is a point of prudence as well as of
courage.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Constancy; resolution;
unchangeableness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steading</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"ing</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/d"<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
barns, stables, cattle-yards, etc., of a farm; -- called also
<altname>onstead</altname>, <altname>farmstead</altname>, <altname>farm
offices</altname>, or <altname>farmery</altname>.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steady</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Steadier</adjf>
<pr>(-<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Steadiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>stedig</ets>
sterile, barren, <ets>stae<edh/<edh/ig</ets>, steady (in
<ets>gestae<edh/<edh/ig</ets>), D. <ets>stedig</ets>, <ets>stadig</ets>,
<ets>steeg</ets>, G. <ets>st<aum/tig</ets>, <ets>stetig</ets>. See
<er>Stead</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Firm in standing
or position; not tottering or shaking; fixed; firm.</def> <ldquo/The
softest, <xex>steadiest</xex> plume.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Keble.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their feet <qex>steady</qex>, their hands diligent, their eyes
watchful, and their hearts resolute.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P.
Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Constant in feeling, purpose, or pursuit; not
fickle, changeable, or wavering; not easily moved or persuaded to alter
a purpose; resolute; <as>as, a man <ex>steady</ex> in his principles, in
his purpose, or in the pursuit of an object</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Regular; constant; undeviating; uniform; <as>as, the
<ex>steady</ex> course of the sun; a <ex>steady</ex> breeze of
wind.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Fixed; regular; uniform; undeviating; invariable;
unremitted; stable.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Steady rest</b></col> <fld>(Mach)</fld>, <cd>a rest in a
turning lathe, to keep a long piece of work from
trembling.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steady</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"y</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Steadied</conjf> <pr>(-<icr/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Steadying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To make steady; to hold or keep
from shaking, reeling, or falling; to make or keep firm; to support; to
make constant, regular, or resolute.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steady</ent><br/
<hw>Stead"y</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become steady; to regain a
steady position or state; to move steadily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Without a breeze, without a tide,<br/
She <qex>steadies</qex> with upright keel.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steak</ent><br/
<hw>Steak</hw> <pr>(st<amac/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>steike</ets>, Icel. <ets>steik</ets>, akin to Icel.
<ets>steikja</ets> to roast, <ets>stikna</ets> to be roasted or
scorched, and E. <ets>stick</ets>, the steak being broiled on a spit.
See <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>A slice of beef,
broiled, or cut for broiling; -- also extended to the meat of other
large animals; <as>as, venison <ex>steak</ex>; bear <ex>steak</ex>; pork
<ex>steak</ex>; turtle <ex>steak</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steal</ent><br/
<hw>Steal</hw> <pr>(st<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stale</er> a handle.]</ety> <def>A handle; a stale, or stele.</def>
<mark>[Archaic or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And in his hand a huge poleax did bear.<br/
Whose <qex>steale</qex> was iron-studded but not long.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steal</ent><br/
<hw>Steal</hw> <pr>(st<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Stole</conjf> <pr>(st<omac/l)</pr>;
<pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stolen</conjf> <pr>(st<omac/"l'n)</pr>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Stealing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stelen</ets>, AS. <ets>stelan</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>stela</ets>, D. <ets>stelen</ets>, OHG. <ets>stelan</ets>, G.
<ets>stehlen</ets>, Icel. <ets>stela</ets>, SW. <ets>stj<aum/la</ets>,
Dan. <ets>stiaele</ets>, Goth. <ets>stilan</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To take, and carry away, feloniously; to take without right or
leave, and with intent to keep wrongfully; <as>as, to <ex>steal</ex> the
personal goods of another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Maugre thy heed, thou must for indigence<br/
Or <qex>steal</qex>, or beg, or borrow, thy dispense.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The man who <qex>stole</qex> a goose and gave away the giblets in
alms.</q> <rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1408 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To withdraw or convey clandestinely (reflexive);
hence, to creep furtively, or to insinuate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They could insinuate and <qex>steal</qex> themselves under the
same by their humble carriage and submission.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He will <qex>steal</qex> himself into a man's favor.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To gain by insinuating arts or covert
means.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So Absalom <qex>stole</qex> the hearts of the men of Israel.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Sam. xv. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To get into one's power gradually and by
imperceptible degrees; to take possession of by a gradual and
imperceptible appropriation; -- with <xex>away</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Variety of objects has a tendency to <qex>steal</qex> away the
mind from its steady pursuit of any subject.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To accomplish in a concealed or unobserved manner;
to try to carry out secretly; <as>as, to <ex>steal</ex> a
look</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Always, when thou changest thine opinion or course, profess it
plainly, . . . and do not think to <qex>steal</qex> it.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To steal a march</b></col>, <cd>to march in a covert way;
to gain an advantage unobserved; -- formerly followed by <xex>of</xex>,
but now by <xex>on</xex> or <xex>upon</xex>, and sometimes by
<xex>over</xex>; as, <xex>to steal a march</xex> upon one's political
rivals.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She yesterday wanted <qex>to steal a march</qex> of poor
Liddy.</q> <rj><qau>Smollett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fifty thousand men can not easily <qex>steal a march</qex> over
the sea.</q> <rj><qau>Walpole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To filch; pilfer; purloin; thieve.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steal</ent><br/
<hw>Steal</hw> <pr>(st<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To practice, or be guilty of, theft; to commit larceny or
theft.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt not <qex>steal</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xx.
15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To withdraw, or pass privily; to slip in, along, or
away, unperceived; to go or come furtively.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fixed of mind to avoid further entreaty, and to fly all company,
one night she <qex>stole</qex> away.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P.
Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From whom you now must <qex>steal</qex>, and take no leave.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A soft and solemn breathing sound<br/
Rose like a steam of rich, distilled perfumes,<br/
And <qex>stole</qex> upon the air.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealer</ent><br/
<hw>Steal"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who steals; a thief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>The endmost plank of a
strake which stops short of the stem or stern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealing</ent><br/
<hw>Steal"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of taking
feloniously the personal property of another without his consent and
knowledge; theft; larceny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is stolen; stolen property; -- chiefly
used in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealingly</ent><br/
<hw>Steal"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By stealing, or as by
stealing, furtively, or by an invisible motion.</def> <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealth</ent><br/
<hw>Stealth</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/lth)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stal<thorn/e</ets>. See <er>Steal</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stealing; theft.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The owner proveth the <qex>stealth</qex> to have been committed
upon him by such an outlaw.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The thing stolen; stolen property.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Sluttish dens . . . serving to cover
<xex>stealths</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W. Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The bringing to pass anything in a secret or
concealed manner; a secret procedure; a clandestine practice or action;
-- in either a good or a bad sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Do good by <qex>stealth</qex>, and blush to find it fame.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The monarch, blinded with desire of wealth,<br/
With steel invades the brother's life by <qex>stealth</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I told him of your <qex>stealth</qex> unto this wood.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealthful</ent><br/
<hw>Stealth"ful</hw> <pr>(-f<usdot/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Given to
stealth; stealthy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Stealth"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
-- <wf>Stealth"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealthily</ent><br/
<hw>Stealth"i*ly</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In
a stealthy manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealthiness</ent><br/
<hw>Stealth"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state, quality, or
character of being stealthy; stealth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealthlike</ent><br/
<hw>Stealth"like`</hw> <pr>(-l<imac/k`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Stealthy; sly.</def> <rj><au>Wordsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stealthy</ent><br/
<hw>Stealth"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Stealthier</adjf>
<pr>(-<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stealthiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Done by stealth; accomplished
clandestinely; unperceived; secret; furtive; sly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Withered murder] with his <qex>stealthy</qex> pace, . . . <br/
Moves like a ghost.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steam</ent><br/
<hw>Steam</hw> <pr>(st<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stem</ets>, <ets>steem</ets>, vapor, flame, AS.
<ets>ste<aacute/m</ets> vapor, smoke, odor; akin to D. <ets>stoom</ets>
steam, perhaps originally, a pillar, or something rising like a pillar;
cf. Gr. <grk>sty`ein</grk> to erect, <grk>sty^los</grk> a pillar, and E.
<ets>stand</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The elastic, aeriform fluid
into which water is converted when heated to the boiling point; water in
the state of vapor; gaseous water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The mist formed by condensed vapor; visible vapor;
-- so called in popular usage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any exhalation.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>steam</xex> of
rich, distilled perfumes.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Dry steam</b></col>, <cd>steam which does not contain
water held in suspension mechanically; -- sometimes applied to
superheated steam.</cd> -- <col><b>Exhaust steam</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Exhaust</er>.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>High steam</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>High-pressure steam</b></col></mcol>, <cd>steam of
which the pressure greatly exceeds that of the atmosphere.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Low steam</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Low-pressure
steam</b></col></mcol>, <cd>steam of which the pressure is less than,
equal to, or not greatly above, that of the atmosphere.</cd> --
<col><b>Saturated steam</b></col>, <cd>steam at the temperature of the
boiling point which corresponds to its pressure; -- sometimes also
applied to <altname>wet steam</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Superheated
steam</b></col>, <cd>steam heated to a temperature higher than the
boiling point corresponding to its pressure. It can not exist in contact
with water, nor contain water, and resembles a perfect gas; -- called
also <altname>surcharged steam</altname>, <altname>anhydrous
steam</altname>, and <altname>steam gas</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Wet
steam</b></col>, <cd>steam which contains water held in suspension
mechanically; -- called also <altname>misty
steam</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Steam</xex> is often used adjectively, and in
combination, to denote, <xex>produced by heat</xex>, or <xex>operated by
power</xex>, <xex>derived from steam</xex>, in distinction from other
sources of power; as in <xex>steam</xex> boiler or
<xex>steam</xex>-boiler, <xex>steam</xex> dredger or
<xex>steam</xex>-dredger, <xex>steam</xex> engine or
<xex>steam</xex>-engine, <xex>steam</xex> heat, <xex>steam</xex> plow or
<xex>steam</xex>-plow, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Steam blower</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A blower for
producing a draught consisting of a jet or jets of steam in a chimney or
under a fire</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A fan blower driven directly by a
steam engine.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam boiler</b></col>, <cd>a boiler for
producing steam. See <er>Boiler</er>, 3, and Note. In the illustration,
the shell <xex>a</xex> of the boiler is partly in section, showing the
tubes, or flues, which the hot gases, from the fire beneath the boiler,
enter, after traversing the outside of the shell, and through which the
gases are led to the smoke pipe <xex>d</xex>, which delivers them to the
chimney; <xex>b</xex> is the manhole; <xex>c</xex> the dome;
<xex>e</xex> the steam pipe; <xex>f</xex> the feed and blow-off pipe;
<xex>g</xex> the safety valve; <xex>h</xex>the water gauge.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam car</b></col>, <cd>a car driven by steam power, or drawn
by a locomotive.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam carriage</b></col>, <cd>a
carriage upon wheels moved on common roads by steam.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam casing</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Steam jacket</cref>, under
<er>Jacket</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam chest</b></col>, <cd>the box or
chamber from which steam is distributed to the cylinder of a steam
engine, steam pump, etc., and which usually contains one or more valves;
-- called also <altname>valve chest</altname>, and <altname>valve
box</altname>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Slide valve</cref>, under
<er>Slide</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam chimney</b></col>, <cd>an annular
chamber around the chimney of a boiler furnace, for drying steam.</cd>
-- <col><b>Steam coil</b></col>, <cd>a coil of pipe, or a collection of
connected pipes, for containing steam; -- used for heating, drying,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam colors</b></col> <fld>(Calico
Printing)</fld>, <cd>colors in which the chemical reaction fixing the
coloring matter in the fiber is produced by steam.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam
cylinder</b></col>, <cd>the cylinder of a steam engine, which contains
the piston. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Slide valve</cref>, under
<er>Slide</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam dome</b></col> <fld>(Steam
Boilers)</fld>, <cd>a chamber upon the top of the boiler, from which
steam is conducted to the engine. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <xex>Steam
boiler</xex>, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam fire engine</b></col>, <cd>a
fire engine consisting of a steam boiler and engine, and pump which is
driven by the engine, combined and mounted on wheels. It is usually
drawn by horses, but is sometimes made self-propelling.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam fitter</b></col>, <cd>a fitter of steam pipes.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam fitting</b></col>, <cd>the act or the occupation of a
steam fitter; also, a pipe fitting for steam pipes.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam gas</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Superheated steam</cref>,
above.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam gauge</b></col>, <cd>an instrument for
indicating the pressure of the steam in a boiler. The <stype>mercurial
steam gauge</stype> is a bent tube partially filled with mercury, one
end of which is connected with the boiler while the other is open to the
air, so that the steam by its pressure raises the mercury in the long
limb of the tube to a height proportioned to that pressure. A more
common form, especially for high pressures, consists of a spring pressed
upon by the steam, and connected with the pointer of a dial. The spring
may be a flattened, bent tube, closed at one end, which the entering
steam tends to straighten, or it may be a diaphragm of elastic metal, or
a mass of confined air, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam gun</b></col>, <cd>a
machine or contrivance from which projectiles may be thrown by the
elastic force of steam.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam hammer</b></col>, <cd>a
hammer for forging, which is worked directly by steam; especially, a
hammer which is guided vertically and operated by a vertical steam
cylinder located directly over an anvil. In the variety known as
<xex>Nasmyth's</xex>, the cylinder is fixed, and the hammer is attached
to the piston rod. In that known as <xex>Condie's</xex>, the piston is
fixed, and the hammer attached to the lower end of the cylinder.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam heater</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A radiator heated by
steam</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An apparatus consisting of a steam boiler,
radiator, piping, and fixures for warming a house by steam.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam jacket</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Jacket</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam packet</b></col>, <cd>a packet or vessel propelled by
steam, and running periodically between certain ports.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam pipe</b></col>, <cd>any pipe for conveying steam;
specifically, a pipe through which steam is supplied to an engine.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Steam plow</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Steam
plough</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a plow, or gang of plows, moved by a steam
engine.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam port</b></col>, <cd>an opening for steam
to pass through, as from the steam chest into the cylinder.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam power</b></col>, <cd>the force or energy of steam applied
to produce results; power derived from a steam engine.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam propeller</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Propeller</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Steam pump</b></col>, <cd>a small pumping engine operated by
steam. It is usually direct-acting.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam room</b></col>
<fld>(Steam Boilers)</fld>, <cd>the space in the boiler above the water
level, and in the dome, which contains steam.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam
table</b></col>, <cd>a table on which are dishes heated by steam for
keeping food warm in the carving room of a hotel, restaurant, etc.</cd>
-- <col><b>Steam trap</b></col>, <cd>a self-acting device by means of
which water that accumulates in a pipe or vessel containing steam will
be discharged without permitting steam to escape.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam
tug</b></col>, <cd>a steam vessel used in towing or propelling
ships.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam vessel</b></col>, <cd>a vessel propelled by
steam; a steamboat or steamship; a steamer.</cd> -- <col><b>Steam
whistle</b></col>, <cd>an apparatus attached to a steam boiler, as of a
locomotive, through which steam is rapidly discharged, producing a loud
whistle which serves as a warning or a signal. The steam issues from a
narrow annular orifice around the upper edge of the lower cup or
hemisphere, striking the thin edge of the bell above it, and producing
sound in the manner of an organ pipe or a common whistle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steam</ent><br/
<hw>Steam</hw> <pr>(st<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Steamed</conjf> <pr>(st<emac/md)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Steaming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
emit steam or vapor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My brother's ghost hangs hovering there,<br/
O'er his warm blood, that <qex>steams</qex> into the air.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let the crude humors dance<br/
In heated brass, <qex>steaming</qex> with fire intense.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Philips.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To rise in vapor; to issue, or pass off, as
vapor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The dissolved amber . . . <qex>steamed</qex> away into the
air.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To move or travel by the agency of steam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The vessel <qex>steamed</qex> out of port.</q> <rj><qau>N. P.
Willis.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To generate steam; <as>as, the boiler
<ex>steams</ex> well</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steam</ent><br/
<hw>Steam</hw> <pr>(st<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To exhale.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To expose to the action of steam; to apply steam to
for softening, dressing, or preparing; <as>as, to <ex>steam</ex> wood;
to <ex>steam</ex>cloth; to <ex>steam</ex> food, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steamboat</ent><br/
<hw>Steam"boat`</hw> <pr>(-b<omac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A boat or
vessel propelled by steam power; -- generally used of river or coasting
craft, as distinguished from ocean steamers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steamboating</ent><br/
<hw>Steam"boat`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The occupation
or business of running a steamboat, or of transporting merchandise,
passengers, etc., by steamboats.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bookbinding)</fld> <def>The shearing of a pile of
books which are as yet uncovered, or out of boards.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steam engine</ent><br/
<hw>Steam" en"gine</hw> <pr>(<ecr/n"j<icr/n)</pr>. <def>An engine moved
by steam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In its most common forms its essential parts are a
<xex>piston</xex>, a <xex>cylinder</xex>, and a <xex>valve gear</xex>.
The piston works in the cylinder, to which steam is admitted by the
action of the valve gear, and communicates motion to the machinery to be
actuated. Steam engines are thus classified: 1. According to the way the
steam is used or applied, as <xex>condensing</xex>,
<xex>noncondensing</xex>, <xex>compound</xex>, <xex>double-acting</xex>,
<xex>single-acting</xex>, <xex>triple-expansion</xex>, etc. 2. According
to the motion of the piston, as <xex>reciprocating</xex>,
<xex>rotary</xex>, etc. 3. According to the motion imparted by the
engine, as <xex>rotative</xex> and <xex>nonrotative</xex>. 4. According
to the arrangement of the engine, as <xex>stationary</xex>,
<xex>portable</xex>, and <xex>semiportable</xex> engines,
<xex>horizontal</xex> and <xex>vertical</xex> engines, <xex>beam</xex>
engine, <xex>oscillating</xex> engine, <xex>direct-acting</xex> and
<xex>back-acting</xex> engines, etc. 5. According to their uses, as
<xex>portable</xex>, <xex>marine</xex>, <xex>locomotive</xex>,
<xex>pumping</xex>, <xex>blowing</xex>, <xex>winding</xex>, and
<xex>stationary</xex> engines, the latter term referring to factory
engines, etc., and not technically to pumping or blowing engines.
<xex>Locomotive</xex> and <xex>portable</xex> engines are usually
high-pressure, noncondensing, rotative, and direct-acting.
<xex>Marine</xex> engines are high or low pressure, rotative, and
generally condensing, double-acting, and compound. <xex>Paddle</xex>
engines are generally beam, side-lever, oscillating, or direct-acting.
<xex>Screw</xex> engines are generally direct-acting, back-acting, or
oscillating. <xex>Stationary</xex> engines belong to various classes,
but are generally rotative. A horizontal or inclined stationary steam
engine is called a <xex>left-hand</xex> or a <xex>right-hand</xex>
engine when the crank shaft and driving pulley are on the left-hand
side, or the right-hand side, respectively, of the engine, to a person
looking at them from the cylinder, and is said to run <xex>forward</xex>
or <xex>backward</xex> when the crank traverses the upward half, or
lower half, respectively, of its path, while the piston rod makes its
stroke outward from the cylinder. A marine engine, or the engine of a
locomotive, is said to run forward when its motion is such as would
propel the vessel or the locomotive forward. Steam engines are further
classified as <xex>double-cylinder</xex>, <xex>disk</xex>,
<xex>semicylinder</xex>, <xex>trunk</xex> engines, etc. Machines, such
as cranes, hammers, etc., of which the steam engine forms a part, are
called <xex>steam cranes</xex>, <xex>steam hammers</xex>, etc. See
<xex>Illustration</xex> in Appendix.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Back-acting steam engine</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Back-action steam engine</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a steam engine in
which the motion is transmitted backward from the crosshead to a crank
which is between the crosshead and the cylinder, or beyond the
cylinder.</cd> -- <col><b>Portable steam engine</b></col>, <cd>a steam
engine combined with, and attached to, a boiler which is mounted on
wheels so as to admit of easy transportation; -- used for driving
machinery in the field, as thrashing machines, draining pumps, etc.</cd>
-- <col><b>Semiportable steam engine</b></col>, <cd>a steam engine
combined with, and attached to, a steam boiler, but not mounted on
wheels.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steamer</ent><br/
<hw>Steam"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
vessel propelled by steam; a steamship or steamboat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A steam fire engine. See under
<er>Steam</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A road locomotive for use on common roads, as in
agricultural operations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A vessel in which articles are subjected to the
action of steam, as in washing, in cookery, and in various processes of
manufacture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The steamer duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Steamer duck</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a sea duck
(<spn>Tachyeres cinereus</spn>), native of Patagonia and Terra del
Fuego, which swims and dives with great agility, but which, when full
grown, is incapable of flight, owing to its very small wings. Called
also <altname>loggerhead</altname>, <altname>race horse</altname>, and
<altname>side-wheel duck</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steaminess</ent><br/
<hw>Steam"i*ness</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or condition of being steamy; vaporousness; mistiness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steamship</ent><br/
<hw>Steam"ship`</hw> <pr>(-sh<icr/p`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A ship or
seagoing vessel propelled by the power of steam; a steamer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>steam up</ent><br/
<hw>Steam` up"</hw> <pr>(st<emac/m` <ucr/p")</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<def>To cause to be covered by a translucent layer of condensed water in
fine droplets, such as by breathing on a cold window; to fog; <as>as, to
<ex>steam up</ex> one's eyeglasses</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steamy</ent><br/
<hw>Steam"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Consisting of, or resembling, steam; vaporous; misty.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Emitting steam; full of steam.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Erotic; consisting of or depicting passionate sexual
activity; <as>as, a <ex>steamy</ex> love affair; the <ex>steamy</ex>
scenes were cut from the film to avoid an X rating</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stean</ent><br/
<hw>Stean</hw> <pr>(st<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Steen</er>.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steaning</ent><br/
<hw>Stean"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Steening</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steapsin</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*ap"sin</hw> <pr>(st<esl/*<acr/p"s<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>An unorganized ferment or enzyme
present in pancreatic juice. It decomposes neutral fats into glycerin
and fatty acids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stearate</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"a*rate</hw> <pr>(st<emac/"<adot/*r<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of stearic acid; <as>as, ordinary soap
consists largely of sodium or potassium
<ex>stearates</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1409 pr=VMG --></p>

<p><ent>Stearic</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*ar"ic</hw> <pr>(st<esl/*<acr/r"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>st<eacute/arique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or obtained from, stearin or tallow;
resembling tallow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stearic acid</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
monobasic fatty acid, obtained in the form of white crystalline scales,
soluble in alcohol and ether. It melts to an oily liquid at
69<deg/C.</cd></cs><-- b.p. 383<deg/. <chform>C18H36O2</chform>,
<chform>CH3.(CH2)16.COOH</chform>; sodium stearate, with sodium
palmitate, is the main component of ordinary bar soaps (Such as Ivory
soap). --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stearin</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"a*rin</hw> <pr>(st<emac/"<adot/*r<icr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>ste`ar</grk> tallow, suet: cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/arine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>One
of the constituents of animal fats and also of some vegetable fats, as
the butter of cacao. It is especially characterized by its solidity, so
that when present in considerable quantity it materially increases the
hardness, or raises the melting point, of the fat, as in mutton tallow.
Chemically, it is a compound of glyceryl with three molecules of stearic
acid, and hence is technically called <xex>tristearin</xex>, or
<xex>glyceryl tristearate</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stearolic</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`a*rol"ic</hw> <pr>(-r<ocr/l"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stear</ets>ic + <ets>ol</ets>eic + <ets>-ic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, an acid of
the acetylene series, isologous with stearic acid, and obtained, as a
white crystalline substance, from oleic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stearone</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"a*rone</hw> <pr>(st<emac/"<adot/*r<omac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The ketone of stearic acid, obtained as a white
crystalline substance, (<chform>C17H35)2.CO</chform>, by the
distillation of calcium stearate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stearoptene</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`a*rop"tene</hw> <pr>(-r<ocr/p"t<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stear</ets>ic + <ets>-optene</ets> as in
elae<ets>optene</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The more solid
ingredient of certain volatile oils; -- contrasted with
<xex>elaeoptene</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stearrhea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ste`ar*rhe"a</hw> <pr>(st<emac/`<ait/r*r<emac/"<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>ste`ar</grk> tallow +
<grk>"rei^n</grk> to flow.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Seborrhea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stearyl</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"a*ryl</hw> <pr>(st<emac/"<adot/*r<icr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stear</ets>ic + <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The hypothetical radical characteristic of stearic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steatite</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"a*tite</hw> <pr>(-t<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>ste`ar</grk>, <grk>ste`atos</grk>, fat, tallow: cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/atite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A massive
variety of talc, of a grayish green or brown color. It forms extensive
beds, and is quarried for fireplaces and for coarse utensils. Called
also <altname>potstone</altname>, <altname>lard stone</altname>, and
<altname>soapstone</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steatitic</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`a*tit"ic</hw> <pr>(-t<icr/t"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or of the nature of, steatite;
containing or resembling steatite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steatoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ste`a*to"ma</hw> <pr>(-t<omac/"m<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L., fr. Gr. <grk>stea`twma</grk>, fr. <grk>steatoy^n</grk> to turn
into tallow or suet, fr. <grk>ste`ar</grk>, <grk>-atos</grk>, fat,
suet.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A cyst containing matter like
suet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steatomatous</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`a*tom"a*tous</hw> <pr>(-t<ocr/m"<adot/*t<ucr/s <or/
-t<omac/"m<adot/- ; 277)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/atomateux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of the
nature of a steatoma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steatopyga</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ste`a*top"y*ga</hw> <pr>(-t<ocr/p"<icr/*g<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>ste`ar</grk>, <grk>ste`atos</grk>,
fat + <grk>pygh`</grk> the buttocks.]</ety> <def>A remarkable accretion
of fat upon the buttocks of Africans of certain tribes, especially of
Hottentot (Khoikhoi) women.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steatopygous</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`a*top"y*gous</hw> <pr>(-g<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having
fat buttocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Specimens of the <qex>steatopygous</qex> Abyssinian breed.</q>
<rj><qau>Burton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stedfastly</ent><br/
<ent>Stedfast</ent><br/
<ent>Sted</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Sted</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<hw>Sted"fast</hw> <pr>(-f<adot/st)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>,
<hw>Sted"fast*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos>, etc.</mhw> <def>See
<er>Stead</er>, <er>Steadfast</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stee</ent><br/
<hw>Stee</hw> <pr>(st<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>stiege</ets>. <root/164. See <er>Stair</er>.]</ety> <def>A
ladder.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stey</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steed</ent><br/
<hw>Steed</hw> <pr>(st<emac/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stede</ets>, AS. <ets>st<emac/da</ets> a stud-horse, war horse, fr.
<ets>st<omac/d</ets> a stud of breeding steeds; akin to G.
<ets>stute</ets> a mare, Icel. <ets>stedda</ets>,
<ets>st<omac/<edh/</ets>, a stud. <root/163. See <er>Stud</er> of
horses.]</ety> <def>A horse, especially a spirited horse for state or
war; -- used chiefly in poetry or stately prose.</def> <ldquo/A knight
upon a <xex>steed</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mounted upon a hot and fiery <qex>steed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steedless</ent><br/
<hw>Steed"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no steed; without a
horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steik</ent><br/
<ent>Steek</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Steek</hw>, <hw>Steik</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(st<emac/k)</pr>,
<pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<def>To pierce with a sharp instrument; hence, to stitch; to sew; also,
to fix; to fasten.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steel</ent><br/
<hw>Steel</hw> <pr>(st<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>st<emac/l</ets>, <ets>st<ymac/l</ets>, <ets>st<ymac/le</ets>; akin
to D. <ets>staal</ets>, G. <ets>stahl</ets>, OHG. <ets>stahal</ets>,
Icel. <ets>st<amac/l</ets>, Dan. <ets>staal</ets>, Sw.
<ets>st<aring/l</ets>, Old Prussian <ets>stakla</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Metal)</fld> <def>A variety of iron intermediate in
composition and properties between wrought iron and cast iron
(containing between one half of one per cent and one and a half per cent
of carbon), and consisting of an alloy of iron with an iron carbide.
Steel, unlike wrought iron, can be tempered, and retains magnetism. Its
malleability decreases, and fusibility increases, with an increase in
carbon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument or implement made of steel</def>; as:
--<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>A weapon, as a sword, dagger, etc.</def>
<ldquo/Brave Macbeth . . . with his brandished <xex>steel</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While doubting thus he stood,<br/
Received the <qex>steel</qex> bathed in his brother's blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>An instrument of steel (usually a round rod) for
sharpening knives.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>A piece of steel for striking sparks from
flint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: Anything of extreme hardness; that which is
characterized by sternness or rigor.</def> <ldquo/Heads of
<xex>steel</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Johnson.</au> <ldquo/Manhood's heart of
<xex>steel</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Byron.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A chalybeate medicine.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Steel</xex> is often used in the formation of
compounds, generally of obvious meaning; as, <xex>steel</xex>-clad,
<xex>steel</xex>-girt, <xex>steel</xex>-hearted,
<xex>steel</xex>-plated, <xex>steel</xex>-pointed, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bessemer steel</b></col> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <cd>See in
the Vocabulary.</cd> -- <col><b>Blister steel</b></col>.
<fld>(Metal.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Blister</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Cast
steel</b></col> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a fine variety of steel,
originally made by smelting blister or cementation steel; hence,
ordinarily, steel of any process of production when remelted and
cast.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Chrome steel</b></col>, <col><b>Chromium
steel</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a hard, tenacious
variety containing a little chromium, and somewhat resembling
<cref>tungsten steel</cref>.</cd> -- <col><b>Mild steel</b></col>
<fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of steel having a lower proportion of
carbon than ordinary steel, rendering it softer and more malleable.</cd>
-- <col><b>Puddled steel</b></col> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of
steel produced from cast iron by the puddling process.</cd> --
<col><b>Steel duck</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the goosander, or
merganser.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Steel
mill</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Firearms)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Wheel
lock</cref>, under <er>Wheel</er>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A mill which
has steel grinding surfaces</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>A mill where steel is
manufactured.</cd> -- <col><b>Steel trap</b></col>, <cd>a trap for
catching wild animals. It consists of two iron jaws, which close by
means of a powerful steel spring when the animal disturbs the catch, or
tongue, by which they are kept open.</cd> -- <col><b>Steel
wine</b></col>, <cd>wine, usually sherry, in which steel filings have
been placed for a considerable time, -- used as a medicine.</cd> --
<col><b>Tincture of steel</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>an alcoholic
solution of the chloride of iron.</cd> -- <col><b>Tungsten
steel</b></col> <fld>(Metal.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of steel containing a
small amount of tungsten, and noted for its tenacity and hardness, as
well as for its malleability and tempering qualities. It is also noted
for its magnetic properties.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steel</ent><br/
<hw>Steel</hw> <pr>(st<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Steeled</conjf> <pr>(st<emac/ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr.
& vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Steeling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS.
<ets>st<ymac/lan</ets>: cf. Icel. <ets>staela</ets>. See <er>Steel</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To overlay, point, or edge with
steel; <as>as, to <ex>steel</ex> a razor; to <ex>steel</ex> an
ax.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>To make hard or strong; hence, to
make insensible or obdurate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lies well <qex>steeled</qex> with weighty arguments.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O God of battles! <qex>steel</qex> my soldiers' hearts.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why will you fight against so sweet a passion,<br/
And <qex>steel</qex> your heart to such a world of charms?</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <mark>Fig.:</mark> <def>To cause to resemble steel, as in
smoothness, polish, or other qualities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These waters, <qex>steeled</qex><br/
By breezeless air to smoothest polish.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>To cover, as an electrotype
plate, with a thin layer of iron by electrolysis. The iron thus
deposited is very hard, like steel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steelbow goods</ent><br/
<hw>Steel"bow` goods"</hw> <pr>(st<emac/l"bou` g<oocr/dz")</pr>.
<fld>(Scots Law)</fld> <def>Those goods on a farm, such as corn, cattle,
implements of husbandry, etc., which may not be carried off by a
removing tenant, as being the property of the landlord.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeler</ent><br/
<hw>Steel"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
points, edges, or covers with steel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeler</ent><br/
<hw>Steel"er</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Stealer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steelhead</ent><br/
<hw>Steel"head`</hw> <pr>(-h<ecr/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A North Pacific salmon (<spn>Salmo
Gairdneri</spn>) found from Northern California to Siberia; -- called
also <altname>hardhead</altname>, and
<altname>preestl</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The ruddy duck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeliness</ent><br/
<hw>Steel"i*ness</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality of being steely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeling</ent><br/
<hw>Steel"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The process of pointing, edging,
or overlaying with steel; specifically, acierage. See <er>Steel</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steely</ent><br/
<hw>Steel"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Made
of steel; consisting of steel.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>steely</xex> point
of Clifford's lance.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Around his shop the <qex>steely</qex> sparkles flew.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resembling steel; hard; firm; having the color of
steel.</def> <ldquo/His hair was <xex>steely</xex> gray.<rdquo/
<rj><au>The Century.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She would unarm her noble heart of that <qex>steely</qex>
resistance against the sweet blows of love.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P.
Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Steely iron</b></col>, <cd>a compound of iron containing
less than one half of one per cent of carbon.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steelyard</ent><br/
<hw>Steel"yard</hw> <pr>(st<emac/l"y<aum/rd ; <mark>colloq.</mark>
st<icr/l"y<etil/rd ; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named from a
place in London called the <ets>Steelyard</ets>, which was a yard in
which steel was sold.]</ety> <def>A form of balance in which the body to
be weighed is suspended from the shorter arm of a lever, which turns on
a fulcrum, and a counterpoise is caused to slide upon the longer arm to
produce equilibrium, its place upon this arm (which is notched or
graduated) indicating the weight; a Roman balance; -- very commonly used
also in the plural form, <xex>steelyards</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steem</ent><br/
<hw>Steem</hw> <pr>(st<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Esteem</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steem</ent><br/
<hw>Steem</hw>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>See 1st and 2nd
<er>Stem</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steen</ent><br/
<hw>Steen</hw> <pr>(st<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>st<aemac/na</ets>. See <er>Stone</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stean</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A vessel of clay or
stone.</def> <ldquo/An huge great earth-pot <xex>steane</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A wall of brick, stone, or cement, used as a lining,
as of a well, cistern, etc.; a steening.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steen</ent><br/
<hw>Steen</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>st<aemac/nan</ets> to
adorn with stones or gems. See <er>Stone</er>.]</ety> <def>To line, as a
well, with brick, stone, or other hard material.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>stean</asp>, and <asp>stein</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steenbok</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Steen"bok`</hw> <pr>(-b<ocr/k`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>steen</ets> stone + <ets>bok</ets> buck.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Steinbock</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steening</ent><br/
<hw>Steen"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A lining made of brick, stone, or
other hard material, as for a well.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>steaning</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steinkirk</ent><br/
<ent>Steenkirk</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Steen"kirk`</hw> <pr>(st<emac/n"k<etil/rk`)</pr>,
<hw>Stein"kirk`</hw> <pr>(st<imac/n"-)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[So called from the battle of <ets>Steinkirk</ets>, in 1692, on
which occasion the French nobles had no time to arrange their lace
neckcloths.]</ety> <def>A kind of neckcloth worn in a loose and
disorderly fashion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steep</ent><br/
<hw>Steep</hw> <pr>(st<emac/p)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bright;
glittering; fiery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His eyen <qex>steep</qex>, and rolling in his head.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steep</ent><br/
<hw>Steep</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Steeped</conjf> <pr>(st<emac/pt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Steeping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stepen</ets>, probably
fr. Icel. <ets>steypa</ets> to cause to stoop, cast down, pour out, to
cast metals, causative of <ets>st<umac/pa</ets> to stoop; cf. Sw.
<ets>st<oum/pa</ets> to cast, to steep, Dan. <ets>st<oum/be</ets>, D. &
G. <ets>stippen</ets> to steep, to dip. Cf. <er>Stoop</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To soak in a liquid; to macerate; to extract the
essence of by soaking; <as>as, to soften seed by <ex>steeping</ex> it in
water</as>. Often used figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let fancy still my sense in Lethe <qex>steep</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In refreshing dew to <qex>steep</qex><br/
The little, trembling flowers.</q> <rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The learned of the nation were <qex>steeped</qex> in Latin.</q>
<rj><qau>Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steep</ent><br/
<hw>Steep</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To undergo the process of soaking
in a liquid; <as>as, the tea is <ex>steeping</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steep</ent><br/
<hw>Steep</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Something steeped, or
used in steeping; a fertilizing liquid to hasten the germination of
seeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rennet bag.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steep</ent><br/
<hw>Steep</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Steeper</adjf> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Steepest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>steep</ets>,
<ets>step</ets>, AS. <ets>ste<aacute/p</ets>; akin to Icel.
<ets>steyp<edh/r</ets> steep, and <ets>st<umac/pa</ets> to stoop, Sw.
<ets>stupa</ets> to fall, to tilt; cf. OFries. <ets>stap</ets> high. Cf.
<er>Stoop</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, <er>Steep</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>,
<er>Steeple</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Making a large angle with the
plane of the horizon; ascending or descending rapidly with respect to a
horizontal line or a level; precipitous; <as>as, a <ex>steep</ex> hill
or mountain; a <ex>steep</ex> roof; a <ex>steep</ex> ascent; a
<ex>steep</ex> declivity; a <ex>steep</ex> barometric
gradient</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Difficult of access; not easily reached; lofty;
elevated; high.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Excessive; <as>as, a <ex>steep</ex>
price</as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steep</ent><br/
<hw>Steep</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A precipitous place, hill, mountain,
rock, or ascent; any elevated object sloping with a large angle to the
plane of the horizon; a precipice.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We had on each side naked rocks and mountains broken into a
thousand irregular <qex>steeps</qex> and precipices.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bare <qex>steeps</qex>, where desolation stalks.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steep-down</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"-down`</hw> <pr>(st<emac/p"doun`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Deep and precipitous; having steep descent.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wash me in <qex>steep-down</qex> gulfs of liquid fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steepen</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"en</hw> <pr>(st<emac/p"'n)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Steepened</conjf>
<pr>(st<emac/p"'nd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Steepening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To become steep or
steeper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As the way <qex>steepened</qex> . . . I could detect in the hollow
of the hill some traces of the old path.</q> <rj><qau>H.
Miller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeper</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vessel, vat,
or cistern, in which things are steeped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steepiness</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"i*ness</hw> <pr>(-<icr/*n<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Steepness.</def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steepish</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat steep.</def>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeple</ent><br/
<hw>Stee"ple</hw> <pr>(st<emac/"p'l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stepel</ets>, AS. <ets>st<emac/pel</ets>, <ets>st<ymac/pel</ets>;
akin to E. <ets>steep</ets>, a.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A spire;
also, the tower and spire taken together; the whole of a structure if
the roof is of spire form. See <er>Spire</er>.</def> <ldquo/A
weathercock on a <xex>steeple</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Rood steeple</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Rood tower</cref>,
under <er>Rood</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Steeple bush</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a low shrub (<spn>Spiraea tomentosa</spn>) having
dense panicles of minute rose-colored flowers; hardhack.</cd> --
<col><b>Steeple chase</b></col>, <cd>a race across country between a
number of horsemen, to see which can first reach some distant object, as
a church steeple; hence, a race over a prescribed course obstructed by
such obstacles as one meets in riding across country, as hedges, walls,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Steeple chaser</b></col>, <cd>one who rides in a
steeple chase; also, a horse trained to run in a steeple chase.</cd> --
<col><b>Steeple engine</b></col>, <cd>a vertical back-acting steam
engine having the cylinder beneath the crosshead.</cd> --
<col><b>Steeple house</b></col>, <cd>a church.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Jer. Taylor.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeplechasing</ent><br/
<hw>Stee"ple*chas`ing</hw> <pr>(-ch<amac/s`<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The act of riding steeple chases.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeple-crowned</ent><br/
<hw>Stee"ple-crowned`</hw> <pr>(-kround`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Bearing a steeple; <as>as, a <ex>steeple-crowned</ex>
building</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a crown shaped like a steeple; <as>as, a
<ex>steeple-crowned</ex> hat</as>; also, wearing a hat with such a
crown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This grave, bearded, sable-cloaked, and <qex>steeple-crowned</qex>
progenitor.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steepled</ent><br/
<hw>Stee"pled</hw> <pr>(st<emac/"p'ld)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Furnished with, or having the form of, a steeple; adorned with
steeples.</def> <rj><au>Fairfax.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeply</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"ly</hw> <pr>(st<emac/p"l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
steep manner; with steepness; with precipitous declivity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steepness</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Quality or state of
being steep; precipitous declivity; <as>as, the <ex>steepness</ex>of a
hill or a roof</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Height; loftiness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steep-up</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"-up`</hw> <pr>(-<ucr/p`)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lofty and
precipitous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her stand she takes upon a <qex>steep-up</qex> hill.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steepy</ent><br/
<hw>Steep"y</hw> <pr>(-<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Steep;
precipitous.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No more, my goats, shall I behold you climb<br/
The <qex>steepy</qex> cliffs, or crop the flow'ry thyme.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steer</ent><br/
<hw>Steer</hw> <pr>(st<emac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>steer</ets>, AS. <ets>ste<oacute/r</ets>; akin to D. & G.
<ets>stier</ets> a bull, OHG. <ets>stior</ets>, Icel.
<ets>stj<omac/rr</ets>, <ets><thorn/j<omac/rr</ets>, Sw.
<ets>tjur</ets>, Dan. <ets>tyr</ets>, Goth. <ets>stiur</ets>, Russ.
<ets>tur'</ets>, Pol. <ets>tur</ets>, Ir. & Gael. <ets>tarbh</ets>, W.
<ets>tarw</ets>, L. <ets>taurus</ets>, Gr. <grk>tay^ros</grk>, Skr.
<ets>sth<umac/ra</ets> strong, stout, AS. <ets>stor</ets> large, Icel.
<ets>st<omac/rr</ets>, OHG. <ets>st<umac/ri</ets>, <ets>stiuri</ets>.
<root/168. Cf. <er>Stirk</er>, <er>Taurine</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>A young male of the ox kind; especially, a common ox; a castrated
taurine male from two to four years old. See the Note under
<er>Ox</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steer</ent><br/
<hw>Steer</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To castrate; -- said of male
calves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steer</ent><br/
<hw>Steer</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Steered</conjf> <pr>(st<emac/rd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Steering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>steeren</ets>,
<ets>steren</ets>, AS. <ets>sti<eacute/ran</ets>,
<ets>st<ymac/ran</ets>, <ets>ste<oacute/ran</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>stiora</ets>, <ets>stiura</ets>, D. <ets>sturen</ets>, OD.
<ets>stieren</ets>, G. <ets>steuern</ets>, OHG. <ets>stiuren</ets> to
direct, support, G. <ets>steuer</ets> contribution, tax, Icel.
<ets>st<ymac/ra</ets> to steer, govern, Sw. <ets>styra</ets>, Dan.
<ets>styre</ets>, Goth. <ets>stiurjan</ets> to establish, AS.
<ets>ste<oacute/r</ets> a rudder, a helm, and probably to Icel.
<ets>staurr</ets> a pale, stake, Gr. <grk>stayro`s</grk>, and perhaps
ultimately to E. <ets>stand</ets>. <root/168. Cf. <er>Starboard</er>,
<er>Stern</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To direct the course of; to
guide; to govern; -- applied especially to a vessel in the
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That with a staff his feeble steps did <qex>steer</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steer</ent><br/
<hw>Steer</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To direct a vessel in
its course; to direct one's course.</def> <ldquo/No helmsman
<xex>steers</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be directed and governed; to take a direction, or
course; to obey the helm; <as>as, the boat <ex>steers</ex>
easily</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where the wind<br/
Veers oft, as oft [a ship] so <qex>steers</qex>, and shifts her
sail.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To conduct one's self; to take or pursue a course of
action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steer</ent><br/
<hw>Steer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>ste<oacute/r</ets>,
<ets>sti<oacute/r</ets>; akin to D. <ets>stuur</ets>, G.
<ets>steuer</ets>, Icel. <ets>st<ymac/ri</ets>. <root/168. See
<er>Steer</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stere</asp>.]</altsp> <def>A rudder or helm.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steer</ent><br/
<hw>Steer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>ste<oacute/ra</ets>. See
<er>Steer</er> a rudder.]</ety> <def>A helmsman; a pilot.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steerable</ent><br/
<hw>Steer"a*ble</hw> <pr>(-<adot/*b'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable
of being steered; dirigible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steerage</ent><br/
<hw>Steer"age</hw> <pr>(st<emac/r"<asl/j ; 48)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or practice of steering, or directing; <as>as,
the <ex>steerage</ex> of a ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He left the city, and, in a most tempestuous season, forsook the
helm and <qex>steerage</qex> of the commonwealth.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The effect of the
helm on a ship; the manner in which an individual ship is affected by
the helm.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The hinder part of a vessel; the
stern.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <au>Swift.</au> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>Properly, the space in the after part of a vessel, under the cabin,
but used generally to indicate any part of a vessel having the poorest
accommodations and occupied by passengers paying the lowest rate of
fare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Direction; regulation; management;
guidance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that hath the <qex>steerage</qex> of my course.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That by which a course is directed.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here he hung on high,<br/
The <qex>steerage</qex> of his wings.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Steerage passenger</b></col>, <cd>a passenger who takes
passage in the steerage of a vessel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steerageway</ent><br/
<hw>Steer"age*way`</hw> <pr>(-w<amac/`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A rate of motion through the water sufficient to
render a vessel governable by the helm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steerer</ent><br/
<hw>Steer"er</hw> <pr>(-<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
steers; <as>as, a boat <ex>steerer</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steering</ent><br/
<hw>Steer"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Steer</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Steering wheel</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the
wheel by means of which the rudder of a vessel is turned and the vessel
is steered.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steerless</ent><br/
<hw>Steer"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no rudder.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steerling</ent><br/
<hw>Steer"ling</hw> <pr>(-l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A young or
small steer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1410 --></p>

<p><ent>Steersman</ent><br/
<hw>Steers"man</hw> <pr>(st<emac/rz"m<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Steersmen</plw>
<pr>(st<emac/rz"m<eit/n)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[<ets>Steer</ets> a rudder +
<ets>man</ets>: cf. AS. <ets>ste<oacute/rmann</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who
steers; the helmsman of a vessel.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steersmate</ent><br/
<hw>Steers"mate</hw> <pr>(st<emac/rz"m<amac/t`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Steer</ets> a rudder + <ets>mate</ets> a companion.]</ety>
<def>One who steers; steersman.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeve</ent><br/
<hw>Steeve</hw> <pr>(st<emac/v)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Steeved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Steeving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
OD. <ets>steve</ets> staff, E. <ets>stem</ets>, n.]</ety>
<fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>To project upward, or make an angle with
the horizon or with the line of a vessel's keel; -- said of the
bowsprit, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeve</ent><br/
<hw>Steeve</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld>
<def>To elevate or fix at an angle with the horizon; -- said of the
bowsprit, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stow, as bales in a vessel's hold, by means of a
steeve. See <er>Steeve</er>, <pos>n.</pos> <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeve</ent><br/
<hw>Steeve</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
angle which a bowsprit makes with the horizon, or with the line of the
vessel's keel; -- called also <altname>steeving</altname>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A spar, with a block at one end, used in stowing
cotton bales, and similar kinds of cargo which need to be packed
tightly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steeving</ent><br/
<hw>Steev"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or practice
of one who steeves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>See <er>Steeve</er>,
<pos>n.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steg</ent><br/
<hw>Steg</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/g)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>steggr</ets> the male of several animals. Cf. <er>Stag</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A gander.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stag</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steganographist</ent><br/
<hw>Steg`a*nog"ra*phist</hw>
<pr>(st<ecr/g`<adot/*n<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<icr/st)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>One skilled in steganography; a cryptographer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steganography</ent><br/
<hw>Steg`a*nog"ra*phy</hw>
<pr>(st<ecr/g`<adot/*n<ocr/g"r<adot/*f<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>stegano`s</grk> covered (fr. <grk>ste`gein</grk> to cover
closely) + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art of
writing in cipher, or in characters which are not intelligible except to
persons who have the key; cryptography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>The branch of
cryptography in which messages are hidden inside other messages; -- used
commonly for the process of hiding messages inside a computerized image
file, as for example hiding the name and copyright notice of the owner
of an image as protection against violation of the copyright.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steganophthalmata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Steg`a*noph*thal"ma*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., from Gr. <grk>stegano`s</grk> covered + <grk>'ofqalmo`s</grk>
the eye.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The Discophora, or
Phanerocarpae. Called also
<altname>Steganophthalmia</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steganopod</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*gan"o*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Steganopodes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steganopodes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Steg`a*nop"o*des</hw>
<pr>(st<ecr/g`<adot/*n<ocr/p"<osl/*d<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>stegano`poys</grk>, <grk>stegano`podos</grk>,
web-footed; <grk>stegano`s</grk> covered + <grk>poy`s</grk> foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of swimming birds in which all four
toes are united by a broad web. It includes the pelicans, cormorants,
gannets, and others.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steganopodous</ent><br/
<hw>Steg`a*nop"o*dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having all four toes webbed together.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stegnosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Steg*no"sis</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/g*n<omac/"s<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>ste`gnwsis</grk>. See
<er>Stegnotic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Constipation; also,
constriction of the vessels or ducts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stegnotic</ent><br/
<hw>Steg*no"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>stegnwtiko`s</grk>, fr. <grk>stegnoy^n</grk> to cover, to make
costive, fr. <grk>stegno`s</grk>, <grk>stegano`s</grk>, covered,
closed.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Tending to render costive, or to
diminish excretions or discharges generally.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A stegnotic medicine; an
astringent.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stegocephala</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Steg`o*ceph"a*la</hw>
<pr>(st<ecr/g`<osl/*s<ecr/f"<adot/*l<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>ste`gh</grk> roof + <grk>kefalh`</grk>
head.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An extinct order of amphibians
found fossil in the Mesozoic rocks; called also
<altname>Stegocephali</altname>, and
<altname>Labyrinthodonta</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Their teeth, in transverse sections, usually show a
labyrinthiform arrangement of the cement and dentine. The under side of
the body was covered with bony plates. Some of the Stegocephala were of
very large size, and the form of the body varied from short, stout forms
to others that were as slender as serpents.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stegosauria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Steg`o*sau"ri*a</hw>
<pr>(st<ecr/g`<osl/*s<add/"r<icr/*<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL. See <er>Stegosaurus</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>An
extinct order of herbivorous dinosaurs, including the genera
<styp><gen>Stegosaurus</gen></styp>, <styp><gen>Omosaurus</gen></styp>,
and their allies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stegosaurus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Steg`o*sau"rus</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/g`<osl/*s<add/"r<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>ste`gh</grk> roof +
<grk>say^ros</grk> a lizard.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>A genus of
large Jurassic dinosaurs remarkable for a powerful dermal armature of
plates and spines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steik</ent><br/
<hw>Steik</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Steek</er>.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stein</ent><br/
<hw>Stein</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Steen</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steinbock</ent><br/
<hw>Stein"bock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>stein</ets> stone + <ets>bock</ets> buck, D. <ets>bok</ets>. Cf.
<er>Steenbok</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
European ibex.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A small South African antelope
(<spn>Nanotragus tragulus</spn>) which frequents dry, rocky districts;
-- called also <altname>steenbok</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>steinboc</asp>, and <asp>steinbok</asp>; also called
<asp>stonebock</asp>, and <asp>stonebuck</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steingale</ent><br/
<hw>Stein"gale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The stannel.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steining</ent><br/
<hw>Stein"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Steening</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steinkirk</ent><br/
<hw>Stein"kirk`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Steenkirk</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steinkle</ent><br/
<hw>Stein"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The wheater.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stela</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ste"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stelae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., from Gr. <?/ a post, an
upright stone.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A small column or
pillar, used as a monument, milestone, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stele</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ste"le</hw> <pr>(st<emac/"l<emac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Stela</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One of these <qex>steles</qex>, containing the Greek version of
the ordinance, has recently been discovered.</q> <rj><qau>I. Taylor (The
Alphabet).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stele</ent><br/
<hw>Stele</hw> <pr>(st<emac/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stale</er> a handle.]</ety> <def>A stale, or handle; a stalk.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au> <au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stelene</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"lene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stela</er>.]</ety> <def>Resembling, or used as, a stela;
columnar.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stell</ent><br/
<hw>Stell</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stellan</ets>. <root/163.]</ety> <def>To place or fix firmly or
permanently.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stell</ent><br/
<hw>Stell</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stell</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A prop; a support, as for the feet in
standing or climbing.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A partial inclosure made by a wall or trees, to
serve as a shelter for sheep or cattle.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellary</ent><br/
<ent>Stellar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stel"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stel"la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stellaris</ets>, fr.
<ets>stella</ets> a star. See <er>Star</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to stars; astral; <as>as, a <ex>stellar</ex> figure;
<ex>stellary</ex> orbs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[These soft fires] in part shed down<br/
Their <qex>stellar</qex> virtue.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Full of stars; starry; <as>as, <ex>stellar</ex>
regions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellated</ent><br/
<ent>Stellate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stel"late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stel"la*ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stellatus</ets>, p. p.
of <ets>stellare</ets> to set or cover with stars, from
<ets>stella</ets> a star. See <er>Stellar</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Resembling a star; pointed or radiated, like the emblem of a
star.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Starlike; having similar parts
radiating from a common center; <as>as, <ex>stellate</ex>
flowers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellation</ent><br/
<hw>Stel*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Radiation of
light.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stelled</ent><br/
<hw>Stelled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stell</er> to
place.]</ety> <def>Firmly placed or fixed.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/The <xex>stelled</xex> fires<rdquo/ [the stars]. <au>Shak.</au>
<note>[In this passage by some defined as <ldquo/starry,<rdquo/ as if
from <xex>stellatus</xex>.]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>steller</ent><br/
<hw>stel"ler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[After <person>Geo.
W. <etsep>Steller</etsep></person>, a German naturalist.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The rytina; -- called also
<altname>stellerine</altname> and <altname>Steller's sea cow</altname>.
See <er>rytina</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellerid</ent><br/
<hw>Stel"ler*id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stella</ets> a star.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
starfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellerida</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stel*ler"i*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extensive group of
echinoderms, comprising the starfishes and ophiurans.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stelleridean</ent><br/
<ent>Stelleridan</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stel*ler"i*dan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stel`ler*id"e*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A starfish,
or brittle star.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steller's sea cow</ent><br/
<hw>Stel"ler's sea" cow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>prop. n.</pos>
<ety>[After <person>Geo. W. <etsep>Steller</etsep></person>, a German
naturalist.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The rytina; -- called also
<altname>stellerine</altname> and <altname>steller</altname>. See
<er>rytina</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stelliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Stel*lif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stellifer</ets>; <ets>stella</ets> star + <ets>ferre</ets> to
bear.]</ety> <def>Having, or abounding with, stars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stelliform</ent><br/
<hw>Stel"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stella</ets> a star + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Like a star;
star-shaped; radiated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellify</ent><br/
<hw>Stel"li*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stella</ets> a star + <ets>-fy</ets>.]</ety> <def>To turn into a
star; to cause to appear like a star; to place among the stars, or in
heaven.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellion</ent><br/
<hw>Stel"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stellio</ets> a newt having starlike spots on its back, fr.
<ets>stella</ets> a star.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A lizard
(<spn>Stellio vulgaris</spn>), common about the Eastern Mediterranean
among ruins. In color it is olive-green, shaded with black, with small
stellate spots. Called also <altname>hardim</altname>, and <altname>star
lizard</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellionate</ent><br/
<hw>Stel"lion*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stellionatus</ets> cozenage, trickery, fr. <ets>stellio</ets> a
newt, a crafty, knavish person.]</ety> <fld>(Scots & Roman Law)</fld>
<def>Any fraud not distinguished by a more special name; -- chiefly
applied to sales of the same property to two different persons, or
selling that for one's own which belongs to another, etc.</def>
<rj><au>Erskine.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellular</ent><br/
<hw>Stel"lu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stellula</ets>, dim. of <ets>stella</ets> a star.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Having the shape or appearance of little stars;
radiated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Marked with starlike spots of color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stellulate</ent><br/
<hw>Stel"lu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Minutely stellate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stelmatopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stel`ma*top"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a block, post + <?/, <?/, eye + <?/, <?/, foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Gymnolaemata</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stelography</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*log"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
post, slab, pillar + <ets>-graphy</ets>: cf. Gr. <?/ an inscription on a
tablet.]</ety> <def>The art of writing or inscribing characters on
pillars.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Stackhouse.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steem</ent><br/
<ent>Stem</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Steem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To gleam.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His head bald, that shone as any glass, . . . <br/
[And] <qex>stemed</qex> as a furnace of a leed [caldron].</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steem</ent><br/
<ent>Stem</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stem</hw>, <hw>Steem</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A gleam
of light; flame.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stem</ent><br/
<hw>Stem</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stemn</ets>, <ets>stefn</ets>, <ets>staefn</ets>; akin to OS.
<ets>stamn</ets> the stem of a ship, D. <ets>stam</ets> stem,
<ets>steven</ets> stem of a ship, G. <ets>stamm</ets> stem,
<ets>steven</ets> stem of a ship, Icel. <ets>stafn</ets>,
<ets>stamn</ets>, stem of a ship, <ets>stofn</ets>, <ets>stomn</ets>,
stem, Sw. <ets>stam</ets> a tree trunk, Dan. <ets>stamme</ets>. Cf.
<er>Staff</er>, <er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The principal
body of a tree, shrub, or plant, of any kind; the main stock; the part
which supports the branches or the head or top.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>After they are shot up thirty feet in length, they spread a very
large top, having no bough nor twig in the trunk or the
<qex>stem</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lowering spring, with lavish rain,<br/
Beats down the slender <qex>stem</qex> and breaded grain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A little branch which connects a fruit, flower, or
leaf with a main branch; a peduncle, pedicel, or petiole; <as>as, the
<ex>stem</ex> of an apple or a cherry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The stock of a family; a race or generation of
progenitors.</def> <ldquo/All that are of noble <xex>stem</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While I do pray, learn here thy <qex>stem</qex><br/
And true descent.</q> <rj><qau>Herbert.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A branch of a family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is a <qex>stem</qex><br/
Of that victorious stock.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A curved piece of timber to which
the two sides of a ship are united at the fore end. The lower end of it
is scarfed to the keel, and the bowsprit rests upon its upper end.
Hence, the forward part of a vessel; the bow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Fig.: An advanced or leading position; the
lookout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wolsey sat at the <qex>stem</qex> more than twenty years.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Anything resembling a stem or stalk; <as>as, the
<ex>stem</ex> of a tobacco pipe; the <ex>stem</ex> of a watch case, or
that part to which the ring, by which it is suspended, is
attached.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>That part of a plant which bears
leaves, or rudiments of leaves, whether rising above ground or wholly
subterranean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The entire central
axis of a feather.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The basal portion of the body
of one of the Pennatulacea, or of a gorgonian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The short perpendicular line
added to the body of a note; the tail of a crotchet, quaver, semiquaver,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The part of an inflected word
which remains unchanged (except by euphonic variations) throughout a
given inflection; theme; base.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>From stem to stern</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>from
one end of the ship to the other, or through the whole length.</cd> --
<col><b>Stem leaf</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a leaf growing from
the stem of a plant, as contrasted with a basal or radical
leaf.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stem</ent><br/
<hw>Stem</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To remove the stem or
stems from; <as>as, to <ex>stem</ex> cherries</as>; to remove the stem
and its appendages (ribs and veins) from; <as>as, to <ex>stem</ex>
tobacco leaves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To ram, as clay, into a blasting hole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stem</ent><br/
<hw>Stem</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stemmed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stemming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Either from <ets>stem</ets>,
n., or akin to <ets>stammer</ets>; cf. G. <ets>stemmen</ets> to press
against.]</ety> <def>To oppose or cut with, or as with, the stem of a
vessel; to resist, or make progress against; to stop or check the flow
of, as a current.</def> <ldquo/An argosy to <xex>stem</xex> the
waves.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They] <qex>stem</qex> the flood with their erected breasts.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stemmed</qex> the wild torrent of a barbarous age.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stem</ent><br/
<hw>Stem</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To move forward against an
obstacle, as a vessel against a current.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stemming</qex> nightly toward the pole.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stem-clasping</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"-clasp`ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Embracing the stem with its base; amplexicaul, as a leaf or
petiole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stemless</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no stem;</def>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>acaulescent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stemlet</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small or young
stem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stemma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stem"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stemmata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, pl. <?/,
a garland or chaplet.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of
the ocelli of an insect. See <er>Ocellus</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>One of the facets of a compound eye of any arthropod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stemmer</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, stems (in any of the senses of the verbs).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stemmery</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"mer*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A large building in
which tobacco is stemmed.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stemmy</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in stems, or
mixed with stems; -- said of tea, dried currants, etc.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stemple</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>stempel</ets> a stamp, a prop, akin to E. <ets>stamp</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A crossbar of wood in a shaft, serving as a
step.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stemson</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stem</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and <er>Keelson</er>, and cf. <er>Sternson</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>A piece of curved timber bolted to the
stem, keelson, and apron in a ship's frame near the bow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stem-winder</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"-wind`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
stem-winding watch.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Hence:</specif> <mark>(Fig.)</mark> <def>Anything
of superior quality, as was attributed to the stem-winding watch; --
esp. used to describe a stirring speech, as in the phrase <ldquo/a
<ex>stem-winder</ex> of a speech<rdquo/ or <ldquo/delivered a
<ex>stem-winder</ex><rdquo/.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stem-winding</ent><br/
<hw>Stem"-wind`ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wound by mechanism connected
with the stem; <as>as, a <ex>stem-winding</ex> watch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stench</ent><br/
<hw>Stench</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To stanch.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stench</ent><br/
<hw>Stench</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stenc</ets> a strong
smell, fr. <ets>stincan</ets>. See <er>Stink</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A smell; an odor.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Clouds of savory <qex>stench</qex> involve the sky.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An ill smell; an offensive odor; a stink.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stench trap</b></col>, <cd>a contrivance to prevent
stench or foul air from rising from the openings of sewers, drains,
etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stench</ent><br/
<hw>Stench</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stencan</ets> to emit a
smell, fr. <ets>stincan</ets> to smell. See <er>Stench</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To cause to emit a disagreeable odor; to cause
to stink.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenchy</ent><br/
<hw>Stench"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
stench.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dyer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stencil</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"cil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably from OF.
<ets>estincelle</ets> spangle, spark, F. <ets><eacute/tincelle</ets>
spark, L. <ets>scintilla</ets>. See <er>Scintillate</er>, and cf.
<er>Tinsel</er>.]</ety> <def>A thin plate of metal, leather, or other
material, used in painting, marking, etc. The pattern is cut out of the
plate, which is then laid flat on the surface to be marked, and the
color brushed over it. Called also <altname>stencil
plate</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stencil</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"cil</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stenciled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Stencilled</conjf>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Stenciling</conjf> or
<conjf>Stencilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To mark, paint, or color in
figures with stencils; to form or print by means of a stencil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenciler</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"cil*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who paints or
colors in figures by means of stencil.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stenciller</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenoderm</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"o*derm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>steno`s</grk> narrow, little + <ets>-derm</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of bat belonging to the genus
<gen>Stenoderma</gen>, native of the West Indies and South America.
These bats have a short or rudimentary tail and a peculiarly shaped nose
membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenodermine</ent><br/
<hw>Sten`o*der"mine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the genus <gen>Stenoderma</gen>, which includes
several West Indian and South American nose-leaf bats.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenograph</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stenographed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Stenographing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<def>To write or report in stenographic characters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenograph</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"o*graph</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A production of stenography;
anything written in shorthand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I saw the reporters' room, in which they redact their hasty
<qex>stenographs</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenographer</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*nog"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is
skilled in stenography; a writer of shorthand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenographical</ent><br/
<ent>Stenographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sten`o*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sten`o*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>st<eacute/nographique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to stenography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenographist</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*nog"ra*phist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
stenographer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenography</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*nog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>steno`s</grk> narrow, close + <ets>graphy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/nographie</ets>, G. <ets>stenographie</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The art of writing in shorthand, by using abbreviations or
characters for whole words; shorthand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenophyllous</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*noph"yl*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>steno`s</grk> narrow + <grk>fy`llon</grk> leaf.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having narrow leaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ste*no"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>steno`s</grk> narrow.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A narrowing of
the opening or hollow of any passage, tube, or orifice; <as>as,
<ex>stenosis</ex> of the pylorus</as>. It differs from
<xex>stricture</xex> in being applied especially to diffused rather than
localized contractions, and in always indicating an origin organic and
not spasmodic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenostome</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"o*stome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>steno`s</grk> narrow, little + <grk>sto`ma</grk> mouth.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a small or narrow mouth; -- said of
certain small ground snakes (<fam>Opoterodonta</fam>), which are unable
to dilate their jaws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stent</ent><br/
<hw>Stent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>Obs.
imp.</pos> <conjf>Stente</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>obs. p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stent</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Stint</er>.]</ety> <def>To
keep within limits; to restrain; to cause to stop, or cease; to
stint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then would he weep, he might not be <qex>stent</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet n'ould she <qex>stent</qex><br/
Her bitter railing and foul revilement.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stent</ent><br/
<hw>Stent</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To stint; to stop; to
cease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And of this cry they would never <qex>stenten</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stent</ent><br/
<hw>Stent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An allotted portion; a stint.</def>
<ldquo/Attain'd his journey's <xex>stent</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mir. for
Mag.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stenting</ent><br/
<hw>Stent"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An opening in a wall in a coal
mine.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>stenton</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stentor</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Stentor</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A herald, in the
Iliad, who had a very loud voice; hence, any person having a powerful
voice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of ciliated Infusoria
belonging to the genus <gen>Stentor</gen> and allied genera, common in
fresh water. The stentors have a bell-shaped, or cornucopia-like, body
with a circle of cilia around the spiral terminal disk. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Heterotricha</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A howling monkey, or
howler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1411 --></p>

<p><ent>Stentorian</ent><br/
<hw>Sten*to"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stentoreus</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to a
stentor; extremely loud; powerful; <as>as, a <ex>stentorian</ex> voice;
<ex>stentorian</ex> lungs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stentorin</ent><br/
<hw>Sten"to*rin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A blue coloring matter found in some stentors. See
<er>Stentor</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stentorious</ent><br/
<hw>Sten*to"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Stentorian.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stentoronic</ent><br/
<hw>Sten`to*ron"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Stentorian.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stentorophonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sten`to*ro*phon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
Stentor + <?/ a sound, voice. See <er>Stentor</er>.]</ety> <def>Speaking
or sounding very loud; stentorian.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of this <qex>stentorophonic</qex> horn of Alexander there is a
preserved in the Vatican.</q> <rj><qau>Derham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Step</ent><br/
<hw>Step</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stepped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stepping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>staeppan</ets>; akin
to OFries. <ets>steppa</ets>, D. <ets>stappen</ets> to step,
<ets>stap</ets> a step, OHG. <ets>stepfen</ets> to step, G.
<ets>stapfe</ets> a footstep, OHG. <ets>stapfo</ets>, G.
<ets>stufe</ets> a step to step on; cf. Gr. <?/ to shake about, handle
roughly, stamp (?). Cf. <er>Stamp</er>, <pos>n.</pos> &
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To move the foot in walking; to
advance or recede by raising and moving one of the feet to another
resting place, or by moving both feet in succession.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To walk; to go on foot; esp., to walk a little
distance; <as>as, to <ex>step</ex> to one of the
neighbors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To walk slowly, gravely, or resolutely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Home the swain retreats,<br/
His flock before him <qex>stepping</qex> to the fold.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: To move mentally; to go in
imagination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are <qex>stepping</qex> almost three thousand years back into
the remotest antiquity.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>To step aside</b></col>, <cd>to walk a little distance
from the rest; to retire from company.</cd> -- <col><b>To step
forth</b></col>, <cd>to move or come forth.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To
step in</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To step into</b></col></mcol>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To walk or advance into a place or state, or to advance
suddenly in.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whosoever then first, after the troubling of the water,
<qex>stepped in</qex>, was made whole of whatsoever disease he had.</q>
<rj><qau>John v. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To enter for a short time; <as>as, I just
<ex>stepped into</ex> the house</as>.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To obtain
possession without trouble; to enter upon easily or suddenly; <as>as,
<ex>to step into</ex> an estate</as></cd>. -- <col><b>To step
out</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <cd>To increase the
length, but not the rapidity, of the step, extending it to thirty-tree
inches.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To go out for a short distance or a short
time</cd>. -- <col><b>To step short</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>to
diminish the length or rapidity of the step according to the established
rules.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Step</ent><br/
<hw>Step</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To set, as the
foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To fix the foot of (a mast) in
its step; to erect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To step off</b></col>, <cd>to measure by steps, or paces;
hence, to divide, as a space, or to form a series of marks, by
successive measurements, as with dividers.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Step</ent><br/
<hw>Step</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>staepe</ets>. See
<er>Step</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An advance or
movement made by one removal of the foot; a pace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A rest, or one of a set of rests, for the foot in
ascending or descending, as a stair, or a round of a ladder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The breadth of every single <qex>step</qex> or stair should be
never less than one foot.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The space passed over by one movement of the foot in
walking or running; <as>as, one <ex>step</ex> is generally about three
feet, but may be more or less</as>. Used also figuratively of any kind
of progress; <as>as, he improved <ex>step</ex> by <ex>step</ex>, or by
<ex>steps</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To derive two or three general principles of motion from
phenomena, and afterwards to tell us how the properties and actions of
all corporeal things follow from those manifest principles, would be a
very great <qex>step</qex> in philosophy.</q> <rj><qau>Sir I.
Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A small space or distance; <as>as, it is but a
<ex>step</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A print of the foot; a footstep; a footprint;
track.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Gait; manner of walking; <as>as, the approach of a
man is often known by his <ex>step</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Proceeding; measure; action; an act.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The reputation of a man depends on the first <qex>steps</qex> he
makes in the world.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beware of desperate <qex>steps</qex>. The darkest day,<br/
Live till to-morrow, will have passed away.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have lately taken <qex>steps</qex> . . . to relieve the old
gentleman's distresses.</q> <rj><qau>G. W. Cable.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Walk; passage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Conduct my <qex>steps</qex> to find the fatal tree.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A portable framework of stairs,
much used indoors in reaching to a high position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>In general, a framing in wood or
iron which is intended to receive an upright shaft; specif., a block of
wood, or a solid platform upon the keelson, supporting the heel of the
mast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of a series of
offsets, or parts, resembling the steps of stairs, as one of the series
of parts of a cone pulley on which the belt runs.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A bearing in which the lower extremity of a spindle or a vertical
shaft revolves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The interval between two
contiguous degrees of the scale.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The word <xex>tone</xex> is often used as the name of
this interval; but there is evident incongruity in using <xex>tone</xex>
for indicating the interval between tones. As the word <xex>scale</xex>
is derived from the Italian <xex>scala</xex>, a ladder, the intervals
may well be called <xex>steps</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <fld>(Kinematics)</fld> <def>A change of position
effected by a motion of translation.</def> <rj><au>W. K.
Clifford.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Fives)</fld> <def>At Eton College, England, a
shallow step dividing the court into an inner and an outer
portion.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Back step</b></col>, <col><b>Half
step</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Back</er>, <er>Half</er>,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Step grate</b></col>, <cd>a form of grate for
holding fuel, in which the bars rise above one another in the manner of
steps.</cd> -- <col><b>To take steps</b></col>, <cd>to take action; to
move in a matter.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Step-</ent><br/
<hw>Step-</hw>. <ety>[AS. <ets>ste<oacute/p-</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>stiap-</ets>, <ets>stiep-</ets>, D. & G. <ets>stief-</ets>, OHG.
<ets>stiuf-</ets>, Icel. <ets>stj<?/p-</ets>, Sw. <ets>styf-</ets>, and
to AS. <ets><amac/st<emac/pan</ets>, <ets><amac/ste<oacute/pan</ets>, to
deprive, bereave, as children of their parents, OHG.
<ets>stiufen</ets>.]</ety> <def>A prefix used before <xex>father</xex>,
<xex>mother</xex>, <xex>brother</xex>, <xex>sister</xex>,
<xex>son</xex>, <xex>daughter</xex>, <xex>child</xex>, etc., to indicate
that the person thus spoken of is not a blood relative, but is a
relative by the marriage of a parent; <as>as, a <ex>step</ex>mother to X
is the wife of the father of X, married by him after the death of the
mother of X</as>. See <er>Stepchild</er>, <er>Stepdaughter</er>,
<er>Stepson</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepbrother</ent><br/
<hw>Step"broth`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A brother by the
marriage of one's father with the mother of another, or of one's mother
with the father of another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepchild</ent><br/
<hw>Step"child`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>ste<oacute/pcild</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A bereaved child;
one who has lost father or mother.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A son or daughter of one's wife or husband by a
former marriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepdame</ent><br/
<hw>Step"dame`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stepmother.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepdaughter</ent><br/
<hw>Step"daugh`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>ste<oacute/pdohtor</ets>.]</ety> <def>A daughter of one's wife or
husband by a former marriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Step-down</ent><br/
<hw>Step"-down`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>Transforming
or converting a current of high potential or voltage into one of low
voltage; <as>as, a <ex>step-down</ex> transformer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepfather</ent><br/
<hw>Step"fa`ther</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>ste<oacute/pfaeder</ets>.]</ety> <def>The husband of one's mother
by a subsequent marriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stephanion</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*pha"ni*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/ a crown.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The point on the side of the
skull where the temporal line, or upper edge of the temporal fossa,
crosses the coronal suture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stephanite</ent><br/
<hw>Steph"an*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named after
the Archduke <ets>Stephan</ets>, mining director of Austria.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A sulphide of antimony and silver of an
iron-black color and metallic luster; called also <altname>black
silver</altname>, and <altname>brittle silver ore</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stephanotis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Steph`a*no"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ fit for a crown, fr. <?/ crown.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of climbing asclepiadaceous shrubs, of
Madagascar, Malaya, etc. They have fleshy or coriaceous opposite leaves,
and large white waxy flowers in cymes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A perfume said to be prepared from the flowers of
<spn>Stephanotis floribunda</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepladder</ent><br/
<hw>Step"lad`der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A portable set of
steps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepmother</ent><br/
<hw>Step"moth`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>ste<oacute/pm<omac/der</ets>.]</ety> <def>The wife of one's father
by a subsequent marriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepparent</ent><br/
<hw>Step"par`ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stepfather or
stepmother.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steppe</ent><br/
<hw>Steppe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From Russ.
<ets>stepe</ets>, through G. or F. <ets>steppe</ets>.]</ety> <def>One of
the vast plains in Southeastern Europe and in Asia, generally elevated,
and free from wood, analogous to many of the prairies in Western North
America. See <er>Savanna</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Steppe murrain</b></col>. <fld>(Far.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Rinderpest</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepped</ent><br/
<hw>Stepped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Provided with a step
or steps; having a series of offsets or parts resembling the steps of
stairs; <as>as, a <ex>stepped</ex> key</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stepped gear</b></col>, <cd>a cogwheel of which the teeth
cross the face in a series of steps.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepper</ent><br/
<hw>Step"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, steps; <as>as, a quick <ex>stepper</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- a stepping motor --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepping-stone</ent><br/
<hw>Step"ping-stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
stone to raise the feet above the surface of water or mud in
walking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: A means of progress or advancement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These obstacles his genius had turned into
<qex>stepping-stones</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That men may rise on <qex>stepping-stones</qex><br/
Of their dead selves to higher things.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepsister</ent><br/
<hw>Step"sis`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A daughter of
one's stepfather or stepmother by a former marriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepson</ent><br/
<hw>Step"son`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>ste<oacute/psunu</ets>.]</ety> <def>A son of one's husband or wife
by a former marriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stepstone</ent><br/
<hw>Step"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stone laid
before a door as a stair to rise on in entering the house.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Step-up</ent><br/
<hw>Step"-up`</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>Transforming
or converting a low-voltage current into one of high voltage; <as>as, a
<ex>step-up</ex> transformer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-ster</ent><br/
<hw>-ster</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[OE. & AS. <ets>-estre</ets>,
<ets>-istre</ets>.]</ety> <def>A suffix denoting <xex>the agent</xex>
(originally a woman), especially a person who does something <xex>with
skill</xex> or <xex>as an occupation</xex>; as in spin<xex>ster</xex>
(originally, a woman who spins), song<xex>ster</xex>, ba<xex>xter</xex>
(= bake<xex>ster</xex>), young<xex>ster</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Brewing, baking, and weaving were formerly feminine
labors, and consequently <xex>brewster</xex>, <xex>baxter</xex>, and
<xex>webster</xex> meant, originally, the woman (not the man) who brews,
bakes, or weaves. When men began to perform these duties the feminine
appellations were retained.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercobilin</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`co*bi"lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stercus</ets> dung + E. <ets>bilin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>A coloring matter found in the faeces, a product of
the alteration of the bile pigments in the intestinal canal, --
identical with <altname>hydrobilirubin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercolin</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"co*lin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stercus</ets> dung + <ets>ol</ets>eum oil.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Serolin</er> <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercoraceous</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`co*ra"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stercus</ets>, <ets>-oris</ets>, dung.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining
to dung; partaking of the nature of, or containing, dung.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercoranism</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"co*ra*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.
Hist.)</fld> <def>The doctrine or belief of the Stercoranists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercoranist</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"co*ra*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>stercoranista</ets>, fr. L. <ets>stercus</ets>, <ets>-oris</ets>,
dung.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>A nickname formerly given to
those who held, or were alleged to hold, that the consecrated elements
in the eucharist undergo the process of digestion in the body of the
recipient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercorarian</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`co*ra"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
Stercoranist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercorary</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"co*ra*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>stercorarium</ets>, from L. <ets>stercorarius</ets> belonging to
dung.]</ety> <def>A place, properly secured from the weather, for
containing dung.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercorate</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"co*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Excrement;
dung.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercoration</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`co*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stercoratio</ets>, from <ets>stercorare</ets> to dung.]</ety>
<def>Manuring with dung.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercorianism</ent><br/
<hw>Ster*co"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The doctrine or belief of the
Stercoranists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercorin</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"co*rin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stercus</ets>, <ets>-oris</ets>, dung.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.
Chem.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Serolin</er> <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stercory</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"co*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Excrement;
dung.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterculiaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Ster*cu`li*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Sterculia</ets>, the typical genus, fr. L. <ets>Sterculius</ets>
the deity that presided over manuring, from <ets>stercus</ets> dung. So
called because one of the original species is fetid.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a natural order
(<ord>Sterculiaceae</ord>) of polypetalous exogenous plants, mostly
tropical. The cacao (<spn>Theobroma Cacao</spn>) is the most useful
plant of the order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stere</ent><br/
<hw>Stere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>st<egrave/re</ets>, fr. Gr. <?/ solid.]</ety> <def>A unit of cubic
measure in the metric system, being a cubic meter, or kiloliter, and
equal to 35.3 cubic feet, or nearly 1<frac13/ cubic yards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stere</ent><br/
<hw>Stere</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To stir.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stere</ent><br/
<hw>Stere</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A rudder. See 5th
<er>Steer</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stere</ent><br/
<hw>Stere</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Helmsman. See 6th
<er>Steer</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterelmintha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ster`el*min"tha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>stereo`s</grk> solid + <?/ a worm.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Platyelminthes</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereo-</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <grk>stereo`s</grk> solid.
See <er>Stare</er> to gaze.]</ety> <def>A combining form meaning
<xex>solid</xex>, <xex>hard</xex>, <xex>firm</xex>, as in
<xex>stereo</xex>-chemistry, <xex>stereo</xex>graphy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereobate</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*bate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>stereo`s</grk> solid + <?/ that treads or covers, akin to <?/ base;
cf. F. <ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/obate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>The lower part or basement of a building or pedestal; -- used
loosely for several different forms of basement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereo-chemical</ent><br/
<ent>Stereo-chemic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ste`re*o-chem"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ste`re*o-chem"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or illustrating, the hypothetical
space relations of atoms in the molecule; <as>as, a
<ex>stereo-chemic</ex> formula</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereo-chemistry</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o-chem"is*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>chemistry</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Chemistry considered with reference to the space
relations of atoms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereochrome</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*chrome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stereochromic
picture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereochromic</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o*chro"mic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining
to the art of stereochromy; produced by stereochromy.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Ste`re*o*chro"mic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereochromy</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*och"ro*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + Gr. <grk>chrw^ma</grk> color.]</ety> <def>A
style of painting on plastered walls or stone, in which the colors are
rendered permanent by sprinklings of water, in which is mixed a
proportion of soluble glass (a silicate of soda).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereoelectric</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o*e*lec"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>electric</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the generation of
electricity by means of solid bodies alone; <as>as, a
<ex>stereoelectric</ex> current is one obtained by means of solids,
without any liquid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereogram</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*gram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>-gram</ets>.]</ety> <def>A diagram or
picture which represents objects in such a way as to give the impression
of relief or solidity; also, a stereograph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereograph</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <def>Any picture,
or pair of pictures, prepared for exhibition in the stereoscope.
Stereographs are now commonly made by means of photography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereographical</ent><br/
<ent>Stereographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ste`re*o*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ste`re*o*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/ographique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Made
or done according to the rules of stereography; delineated on a plane;
<as>as, a <ex>stereographic</ex> chart of the earth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stereographic projection</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>,
<cd>a method of representing the sphere in which the center of
projection is taken in the surface of the sphere, and the plane upon
which the projection is made is at right andles to the diameter passing
through the center of projection.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereographically</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o*graph"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
stereographical manner; by delineation on a plane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereography</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*og"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>graphy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/ographie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art of
delineating the forms of solid bodies on a plane; a branch of solid
geometry which shows the construction of all solids which are regularly
defined.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Illustration: "Stereography". 5 figures, illustrating
arrangements of triangles, squares, or pentagons which can be drawn on a
surface and folded into the five regular polyhedra. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ By cutting pieces of cardboard, or other suitable
material, in the forms represented in the cut, folding them along the
lines indicated, and joining their edges, the five regular solids may be
formed.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereometer</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*om"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An instrument for measuring the solid contents of a
body, or the capacity of a vessel; a volumenometer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument for determining the specific gravity
of liquid bodies, porous bodies, and powders, as well as
solids.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereometrical</ent><br/
<ent>Stereometric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ste`re*o*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ste`re*o*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/om<eacute/trique</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to stereometry; performed or obtained by
stereometry.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Ste`re*o*met"ric*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereometry</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*om"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>-metry</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/om<eacute/trie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art of
measuring and computing the cubical contents of bodies and figures; --
distinguished from <xex>planimetry</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereomonoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o*mon"o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>mono-</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety>
<def>An instrument with two lenses, by which an image of a single
picture projected upon a screen of ground glass is made to present an
appearance of relief, and may be viewed by several persons at
once.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereoplasm</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*plasm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ anything formed or molded.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The solid or insoluble portion of the cell
protoplasm. See <er>Hygroplasm</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereopticon</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*op"ti*con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Stereo-</er>, and <er>Optic</er>.]</ety> <def>An instrument,
consisting essentially of a magic lantern in which photographic pictures
are used, by which the image of a landscape, or any object, may be
thrown upon a screen in such a manner as to seem to stand out in relief,
so as to form a striking and accurate representation of the object
itself; also, a pair of magic lanterns for producing the effect of
dissolving views.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <def>An optical
instrument for giving to pictures the appearance of solid forms, as seen
in nature. It combines in one, through a bending of the rays of light,
two pictures, taken for the purpose from points of view a little way
apart. It is furnished with two eyeglasses, and by refraction or
reflection the pictures are superimposed, so as to appear as one to the
observer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the <xex>reflecting stereoscope</xex>, the rays from
the two pictures are turned into the proper direction for stereoscopic
vision by two plane mirrors set at an angle with each other, and between
the pictures. In the <xex>lenticular stereoscope</xex>, the form in
general use, the eyeglasses are semilenses, or marginal portions of the
same convex lenses, set with their edges toward each other, so that they
deflect the rays coming from the picture so as to strike the eyes as if
coming direct from an intermediate point, where the two pictures are
seen apparently as one.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereoscopical</ent><br/
<ent>Stereoscopic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ste`re*o*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ste`re*o*scop"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to the stereoscope; characteristic of, or adapted to, the
stereoscope; <as>as, a <ex>stereoscopic</ex> effect; the
<ex>stereoscopic</ex> function of the eyeglasses; <ex>stereoscopic</ex>
views</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Ste`re*o*scop"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. Permitting the perception of depth by exhibiting different
views to each eye; as, a sereoscopic microscope, i.e. one in which each
eye views the subject from a different angle, through separate optical
paths. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereoscopist</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*os"co*pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled
in the use or construction of stereoscopes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1412 --></p>

<p><ent>Stereoscopy</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*os"co*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art or
science of using the stereoscope, or of constructing the instrument or
the views used with it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereostatic</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o*stat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>static</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Civil.
Engin.)</fld> <def>Geostatic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotomical</ent><br/
<ent>Stereotomic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Ste`re*o*tom"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Ste`re*o*tom"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to stereotomy; performed by stereotomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*ot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + Gr. <?/ to cut: cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/otomie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The science or art of
cutting solids into certain figures or sections, as arches, and the
like; especially, the art of stonecutting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotype</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*type</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>-type</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/otype</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A plate
forming an exact facsimile of a page of type or of an engraving, used in
printing books, etc.; specifically, a plate with type-metal face, used
for printing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A <xex>stereotype</xex>, or <xex>stereotypr plate</xex>,
is made by setting movable type as for ordinary printing; from these a
cast is taken in plaster of Paris, paper pulp, or the like, and upon
this cast melted type metal is poured, which, when hardened, makes a
solid page or column, from which the impression is taken as from
type.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The art or process of making such plates, or of
executing work by means of them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stereotype block</b></col>, <cd>a block, usually of wood,
to which a stereotype plate is attached while being used in
printing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotype</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*type</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stereotyped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stereotyping</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/otyper</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
prepare for printing in stereotype; to make the stereotype plates of;
<as>as, to <ex>stereotype</ex> the Bible</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To make firm or permanent; to fix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Powerful causes tending to <qex>stereotype</qex> and aggravate the
poverty of old conditions.</q> <rj><qau>Duke of Argyll
(1887).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotyped</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*typed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Formed into, or printed from, stereotype plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Formed in a fixed, unchangeable manner;
<as>as, <ex>stereotyped</ex> opinions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our civilization, with its <qex>stereotyped</qex> ways and smooth
conventionalities.</q> <rj><qau>J. C. Shairp.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotyper</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*ty`per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
stereotypes; one who makes stereotype plates, or works in a stereotype
foundry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotypery</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*ty`per*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The art, process, or employment of making stereotype
plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place where stereotype plates are made; a
stereotype foundry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotypic</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o*typ"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to stereotype, or stereotype plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotypist</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*ty`pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
stereotyper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotypographer</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o*ty*pog"ra*pher</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
stereotype printer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotypography</ent><br/
<hw>Ste`re*o*ty*pog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>typography</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or
art of printing from stereotype plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stereotypy</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"re*o*ty`py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/r<eacute/otypie</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art or process of
making stereotype plates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterhydraulic</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`hy*drau"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stereo-</ets> + <ets>hydraulic</ets>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining
to, or designating, a kind of hydraulic press; resembling such a press
in action or principle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sterhydraulic press</b></col>, <cd>an hydraulic press
producing pressure or motion by the introduction of a solid substance
(as a long rod, or a cord wound on a roller) into a cylinder previously
filled with a liquid.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterile</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"ile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>st<eacute/rile</ets>, L. <ets>sterilis</ets>, akin to Gr.
<grk>stereo`s</grk> stiff, solid, <grk>stei^ros</grk> barren,
<grk>stei^ra</grk> a cow that has not calved, Goth.
<ets>stair<omac/</ets>, fem., barren. See <er>Stare</er> to gaze.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Producing little or no crop; barren; unfruitful;
unproductive; not fertile; <as>as, <ex>sterile</ex> land; a
<ex>sterile</ex> desert; a <ex>sterile</ex> year</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Incapable of
reproduction; unfitted for reproduction of offspring; not able to
germinate or bear fruit; unfruitful; <as>as, a <ex>sterile</ex> flower,
which bears only stamens</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Free from
reproductive spores or germs; <as>as, a <ex>sterile</ex>
fluid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: Barren of ideas; destitute of sentiment;
<as>as, a <ex>sterile</ex> production or author</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterility</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*ril"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sterilitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>st<eacute/rilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or condition of being sterile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Quality of being sterile;
infecundity; also, the state of being free from germs or
spores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterilization</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`il*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The act or process of sterilizing, or rendering
sterile; also, the state of being sterile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterilize</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"il*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sterilized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sterilizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>st<eacute/riliser</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make sterile or
unproductive; to impoverish, as land; to exhaust of fertility.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Sterilizing</xex> the earth.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>To deprive of the power of
reproducing; to render incapable of germination or fecundation; to make
sterile.</def></p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Microbiology, Medicine)</fld> <def>To destroy all
spores or germs in (an organic fluid or mixture) or on (a medical
instrument), as by heat, so as to prevent contamination by bacteria or
other organisms. A common method of sterilization in laboratories and
medical facilities is to heat a liquid sample or an instrument in an
autoclave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To destroy all spores or germs on (a surface) by
wetting with an antiseptic liquid, such as an alcoholic
solution.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterilizer</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"il*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One that
sterilizes anything; specif., an apparatus for sterilizing equipment or
an organic fluid.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterlet</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>sterliade</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A small sturgeon
(<spn>Acipenser ruthenus</spn>) found in the Caspian Sea and its rivers,
and highly esteemed for its flavor. The finest caviar is made from its
roe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterling</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Engin.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Starling</er>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterling</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"ling</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sterlynge</ets>,
<ets>starling</ets>, for <ets>easterling</ets>, LL.
<ets>esterlingus</ets>, probably from <ets>Easterling</ets>, once the
popular name of German trades in England, whose money was of the purest
quality: cf. MHG. <ets>sterlink</ets> a certain coin. Cf. <er>East</er>.
<ldquo/Certain merchants of Norwaie, Denmarke, and of others those
parties, called Ostomanni, or (as in our vulgar language we tearme
them), <ets>easterlings</ets>, because they lie <ets>east</ets> in
respect of us.<rdquo/ <au>Holinshed.</au> <ldquo/In the time of . . .
King Richard the First, monie coined in the east parts of Germanie began
to be of especiall request in England for the puritie thereof, and was
called <ets>Easterling</ets> monie, as all inhabitants of those parts
were called <ets>Easterlings</ets>, and shortly after some of that
countrie, skillful in mint matters and allaies, were sent for into this
realme to bring the coine to perfection; which since that time was
called of them <ets>sterling</ets>, for <ets>Easterling</ets>.<rdquo/
<au>Camden.</au> <ldquo/Four thousand pound of
<ets>sterlings</ets>.<rdquo/ <au>R. of Gloucester.</au>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Any English coin of standard value; coined
money.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So that ye offer nobles or <qex>sterlings</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And Roman wealth in English <qex>sterling</qex> view.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A certain standard of quality or value for
money.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sterling</qex> was the known and approved standard in
England, in all probability, from the beginning of King Henry the
Second's reign.</q> <rj><qau>S. M. Leake.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterling</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Belonging to, or relating to, the standard British money of
account, or the British coinage; <as>as, a pound <ex>sterling</ex>; a
shilling <ex>sterling</ex>; a penny <ex>sterling</ex>; -- now chiefly
applied to the lawful money of England; but <ex>sterling</ex> cost,
<ex>sterling</ex> value, are used.</as></def> <ldquo/With
<xex>sterling</xex> money.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Genuine; pure; of excellent quality; conforming to
the highest standard; of full value; <as>as, a work of <ex>sterling</ex>
merit; a man of <ex>sterling</ex> good sense</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stern</ent><br/
<hw>Stern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stearn</ets> a
kind of bird. See <er>Starling</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
black tern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stern</ent><br/
<hw>Stern</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sterner</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sternest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sterne</ets>,
<ets>sturne</ets>, AS. <ets>styrne</ets>; cf. D. <ets>stuurish</ets>
stern, Sw. <ets>stursk</ets> refractory. <root/166.]</ety> <def>Having a
certain hardness or severity of nature, manner, or aspect; hard; severe;
rigid; rigorous; austere; fixed; unchanging; unrelenting; hence,
serious; resolute; harsh; <as>as, a <ex>stern</ex>resolve; a
<ex>stern</ex> necessity; a <ex>stern</ex> heart; a <ex>stern</ex> gaze;
a <ex>stern</ex> decree.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sterne</qex> wind so loud gan to rout.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would outstare the <qex>sternest</qex> eyes that look.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When that the poor have cried, Caesar hath wept;<br/
Ambition should be made of <qex>sterner</qex> stuff.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stern</qex> as tutors, and as uncles hard.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These barren rocks, your <qex>stern</qex> inheritance.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Gloomy; sullen; forbidding; strict; unkind;
hard-hearted; unfeeling; cruel; pitiless.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stern</ent><br/
<hw>Stern</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>stj<omac/rn</ets> a
steering, or a doubtful AS. <ets>ste<oacute/rn</ets>. <root/166. See
<er>Steer</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The helm or
tiller of a vessel or boat; also, the rudder.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The after or rear end of a ship
or other vessel, or of a boat; the part opposite to the stem, or
prow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: The post of management or direction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And sit chiefest <qex>stern</qex> of public weal.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The hinder part of anything.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The tail of an animal; -- now used only of the tail
of a dog.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>By the stern</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>By the head</cref>, under <er>By</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stern</ent><br/
<hw>Stern</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being in the stern, or being astern;
<as>as, the <ex>stern</ex> davits</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stern board</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a going or
falling astern; a loss of way in making a tack; <as>as, to make a
<ex>stern board</ex></as>. See <er>Board</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 8
<sd>(b)</sd>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stern chase</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>See under <er>Chase</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A stern chaser.</cd> -- <col><b>Stern chaser</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a cannon placed in a ship's stern, pointing
backward, and intended to annoy a ship that is in pursuit.</cd> --
<col><b>Stern fast</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a rope used to
confine the stern of a ship or other vessel, as to a wharf or buoy.</cd>
-- <col><b>Stern frame</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the framework
of timber forms the stern of a ship.</cd> -- <col><b>Stern
knee</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Sternson</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stern
port</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a port, or opening, in the stern
of a ship.</cd> -- <col><b>Stern sheets</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>that part of an open boat which is between the stern and the aftmost
seat of the rowers, -- usually furnished with seats for passengers.</cd>
-- <col><b>Stern wheel</b></col>, <cd>a paddle wheel attached to the
stern of the steamboat which it propels.</cd></cs><-- thus,
<col><b>stern wheeler</b></col>. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternage</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stern.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternal</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of
or pertaining to the sternum; in the region of the sternum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sternal ribs</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Rib</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternbergite</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"berg*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named after
Count Kaspar <ets>Sternberg</ets> of Prague.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A sulphide of silver and iron, occurring in soft flexible laminae
varying in color from brown to black.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternebra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Ster"ne*bra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sternebrae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr.
<ets>sternum</ets> + <ets>-bra</ets> of <ets>vertebra</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>One of the segments of the sternum.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Ster"ne*bral</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterned</ent><br/
<hw>Sterned</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a stern of a
particular shape; -- used in composition; <as>as,
square-<ex>sterned</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterner</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See 3d
<er>Stern</er>.]</ety> <def>A director.</def> <mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dr. R. Clerke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternforemost</ent><br/
<hw>Stern`fore"most`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With the
stern, instead of the bow, in advance; hence, figuratively, in an
awkward, blundering manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A fatal genius for going <qex>sternforemost</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternite</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sternum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The sternum of an
arthropod somite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternly</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stern
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternmost</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"most`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Farthest in the
rear; farthest astern; <as>as, the <ex>sternmost</ex> ship in a
convoy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternness</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterno-</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"no-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A combining form used in anatomy to
indicate <xex>connection with</xex>, or <xex>relation to</xex>, <xex>the
sternum</xex>; <as>as, <ex>sterno</ex>costal,
<ex>sterno</ex>scapular</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternocoracoid</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`no*cor"a*coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the sternum and the
coracoid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternocostal</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`no*cos"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sterno-</ets> + <ets>costal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the sternum and the ribs; <as>as, the
<ex>sternocostal</ex> cartilages</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternohyoid</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`no*hy"oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the sternum and the hyoid bone or
cartilage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternomastoid</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`no*mas"toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the sternum and the mastoid process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternothyroid</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`no*thy"roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the sternum and the thyroid
cartilage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternpost</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"post`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A straight piece of timber, or an iron bar or beam, erected on the
extremity of the keel to support the rudder, and receive the ends of the
planks or plates of the vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternsman</ent><br/
<hw>Sterns"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A steersman.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternson</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"son</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stern</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, and cf. <er>Stemson</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The end of a ship's keelson, to which the sternpost is bolted; --
called also <altname>stern knee</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternum</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> L.
<plw>Sterna</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, E. <plw>Sternums</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/, the breast, chest.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A plate of cartilage, or a series of
bony or cartilaginous plates or segments, in the median line of the
pectoral skeleton of most vertebrates above fishes; the
breastbone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The sternum is connected with the ribs or the pectorial
girdle, or with both. In man it is a flat bone, broad anteriorly,
narrowed behind, and connected with the clavicles and the cartilages of
the seven anterior pairs of ribs. In most birds it has a high median
keel for the attachment of the muscles of the wings.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The ventral part of any one of
the somites of an arthropod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternutation</ent><br/
<hw>Ster`nu*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sternutatio</ets>, fr. <ets>sternutare</ets> to sneeze, intens.
from <ets>sternuere</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of sneezing.</def>
<rj><au>Quincy.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternutative</ent><br/
<hw>Ster*nu"ta*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
quality of provoking to sneeze.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternutatory</ent><br/
<hw>Ster*nu"ta*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sternutative.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A sternutatory
substance or medicine.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sternway</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The movement of a ship backward, or with her stern
foremost.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stern-wheel</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"-wheel`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a paddle
wheel at the stern; <as>as, a <ex>stern-wheel</ex>
steamer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stern-wheeler</ent><br/
<hw>Stern"-wheel`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A steamboat
having a stern wheel instead of side wheels.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterquilinous</ent><br/
<hw>Ster*quil"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sterquilinium</ets> a dung pit, fr. <ets>stercus</ets> dung.]</ety>
<def>Pertaining to a dunghill; hence, mean; dirty; paltry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterre</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"re</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A star.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterrink</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"rink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The crab-eating seal (<spn>Lobodon carcinophaga</spn>) of the
Antarctic Ocean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterrometal</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"ro*met`al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ firm,
solid + E. <ets>metal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Any alloy of copper, zinc, tin,
and iron, of which cannon are sometimes made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stert</ent><br/
<hw>Stert</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>p. p.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Start</er></mord>. <def>Started.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterte</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos>
of <er>Start</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stertorious</ent><br/
<hw>Ster*to"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(st<etil/r*t<omac/"r<icr/*<ucr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Stertorous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stertorous</ent><br/
<hw>Ster"to*rous</hw> <pr>(st<etil/r"t<osl/*r<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>stertere</ets> to snore: cf. F.
<ets>stertoreux</ets>.]</ety> <def>Characterized by a deep snoring,
which accompanies inspiration in some diseases, especially apoplexy;
hence, hoarsely breathing; snoring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Burning, <qex>stertorous</qex> breath that hurt her cheek.</q>
<rj><qau>Mrs. Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The day has ebbed away, and it is night in his room, before his
<qex>stertorous</qex> breathing lulls.</q>
<rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sterve</ent><br/
<hw>Sterve</hw> <pr>(st<etil/rv)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To
die, or cause to die; to perish. See <er>Starve</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au> <au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stet</ent><br/
<hw>Stet</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/t)</pr>, <ety>L., <pos>subj. 3d pers.
sing.</pos> of <ets>stare</ets> to stand, remain.</ety> <ety>[See
<er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>Let it stand; -- a word
used by proof readers to signify that something once erased, or marked
for omission, is to remain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stet</ent><br/
<hw>Stet</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stetted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stetting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>To cause or
direct to remain after having been marked for omission; to mark with the
word <xex>stet</xex>, or with a series of dots below or beside the
matter; <as>as, the proof reader <ex>stetted</ex> a deled
footnote</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stethal</ent><br/
<hw>Steth"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Ste</ets>aric +
<ets>ethal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>One of the higher
alcohols of the methane series, homologous with ethal, and found in
small quantities as an ethereal salt of stearic acid in
spermaceti.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stethograph</ent><br/
<hw>Steth"o*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ the
breast + <ets>-graph</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Pneumatograph</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stethometer</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*thom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ chest
+ <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An apparatus for
measuring the external movements of a given point of the chest wall,
during respiration; -- also called
<altname>thoracometer</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stethoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Steth"o*scope</hw> <pr>(st<ecr/th"<osl/*sk<omac/p)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>sth^qos</grk> the breast +
<ets>-scope</ets>: cf. F. <ets>st<eacute/thoscope</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An instrument used in auscultation for examining
the organs of the chest, as the heart and lungs, by conveying to the ear
of the examiner the sounds produced in the thorax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stethoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Steth"o*scope</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To auscultate, or examine,
with a stethoscope.</def> <rj><au>M. W. Savage.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stethoscopical</ent><br/
<ent>Stethoscopic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Steth`o*scop"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Steth`o*scop"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>st<eacute/thoscopique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to a stethoscope; obtained or made by means of a
stethoscope.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Steth`o*scop"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stethoscopist</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*thos"co*pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled
in the use of the stethoscope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stethoscopy</ent><br/
<hw>Ste*thos"co*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The art or
process of examination by the stethoscope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steve</ent><br/
<hw>Steve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stevedore</er>.]</ety> <def>To pack or stow, as cargo in a ship's
hold. See <er>Steeve</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stevedore</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"ve*dore`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Sp.
<ets>estivador</ets> a packer, a stower, fr. <ets>estivar</ets> to pack,
to stow, L. <ets>stipare</ets> to press, compress, probably akin to E.
<ets>stiff</ets>. See <er>Stiff</er>, <er>Stive</er> to stuff.]</ety>
<def>One whose occupation is to load and unload vessels in port; one who
stows a cargo in a hold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steven</ent><br/
<hw>Ste"ven</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stefn</ets>,
<ets>stemn</ets>, voice; akin to D. <ets>stem</ets>, G.
<ets>stimme</ets>, Goth. <ets>stibna</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Voice; speech; language.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye have as merry a <qex>steven</qex><br/
As any angel hath that is in heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An outcry; a loud call; a clamor.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To set steven</b></col>, <cd>to make an appointment.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>setten steven</qex> for to meet<br/
To playen at the dice.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stew</ent><br/
<hw>Stew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Stow</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small pond or pool where fish
are kept for the table; a vivarium.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer. Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An artificial bed of oysters.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stew</ent><br/
<hw>Stew</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stewed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stewing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stuven</ets>, OF.
<ets>estuver</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tuver</ets>, fr. OF.
<ets>estuve</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tuve</ets>, a sweating house, a room
heated for a bath; probably of Teutonic origin, and akin to E.
<ets>stove</ets>. See <er>Stove</er>, and cf. <er>Stive</er> to
stew.]</ety> <def>To boil slowly, or with the simmering or moderate
heat; to seethe; to cook in a little liquid, over a gentle fire, without
boiling; <as>as, to <ex>stew</ex> meat; to <ex>stew</ex> oysters; to
<ex>stew</ex> apples</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1413 --></p>

<p><ent>Stew</ent><br/
<hw>Stew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be seethed or
cooked in a slow, gentle manner, or in heat and moisture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stew</ent><br/
<hw>Stew</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stue</ets>,
<ets>stuwe</ets>, OF. <ets>estuve</ets>. See <er>Stew</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A place of stewing or seething; a place
where hot bathes are furnished; a hothouse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As burning Aetna from his boiling <qex>stew</qex><br/
Doth belch out flames.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lydians were inhibited by Cyrus to use any armor, and give
themselves to baths and <qex>stews</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Abp.
Abbot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A brothel; -- usually in the plural.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon. South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There be that hate harlots, and never were at the
<qex>stews</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Aschman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A prostitute.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir
A. Weldon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A dish prepared by stewing; <as>as, a
<ex>stew</ex>of pigeons</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A state of agitating excitement; a state of worry;
confusion; <as>as, to be in a <ex>stew</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steward</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"ard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stiward</ets>, AS. <ets>st<imac/weard</ets>, <ets>stigweard</ets>,
literally, a sty ward; <ets>stigu</ets> sty + <ets>weard</ets> warden,
guardian, -- his first duty having been probably to attend to the
domestic animals. <root/164. See <er>Sty</er> pen for swine,
<er>Ward</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A man employed in a large family,
or on a large estate, to manage the domestic concerns, supervise other
servants, collect the rents or income, keep accounts, and the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Worthy to be <qex>stewards</qex> of rent and land.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They came near to the <qex>steward</qex> of Joseph's house.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xliii. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As good <qex>stewards</qex> of the manifold grace of God.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Pet. iv. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person employed in a hotel, or a club, or on board
a ship, to provide for the table, superintend the culinary affairs, etc.
In naval vessels, the captain's <xex>steward</xex>, wardroom
<xex>steward</xex>, steerage <xex>steward</xex>, warrant officers
<xex>steward</xex>, etc., are petty officers who provide for the messes
under their charge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A fiscal agent of certain bodies; <as>as, a
<ex>steward</ex> in a Methodist church</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In some colleges, an officer who provides food for
the students and superintends the kitchen; also, an officer who attends
to the accounts of the students.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>In Scotland, a magistrate appointed by the crown to
exercise jurisdiction over royal lands.</def>
<rj><au>Erskine.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Lord high steward</b></col>, <cd>formerly, the first
officer of the crown; afterward, an officer occasionally appointed, as
for a coronation, or upon the trial of a peer.</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Steward</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"ard</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To manage as a steward.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stewardess</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"ard*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female steward;
specifically, a woman employed in passenger vessels to attend to the
wants of female passengers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stewardly</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"ard*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a manner, or with the
care, of a steward.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be <qex>stewardly</qex> dispensed, not wastefully spent.</q>
<rj><qau>Tooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stewardship</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"ard*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
steward.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stewartry</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"art*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
overseer or superintendent.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The
<xex>stewartry</xex> of provisions.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tooke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The office of a steward; stewardship.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In Scotland, the jurisdiction of a steward; also,
the lands under such jurisdiction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stewish</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suiting a stew, or brothel.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stewpan</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"pan`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pan used for
stewing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stewpot</ent><br/
<hw>Stew"pot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A pot used for
stewing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stey</ent><br/
<hw>Stey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Stee</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sthenic</ent><br/
<hw>Sthen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ strength:
cf. F. <ets>sth<eacute/nique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Strong; active; -- said especially of morbid states attended with
excessive action of the heart and blood vessels, and characterized by
strength and activity of the muscular and nervous system; <as>as, a
<ex>sthenic</ex> fever</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sthenic theory</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Stimulism</er>
<sd>(a)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiacciato</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sti*ac*cia"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.,
crushed, flattened.]</ety> <fld>(Sculp.)</fld> <def>The lowest relief,
-- often used in Italian sculpture of the 15th and 16th
centuries.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stian</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sty on the eye. See
<er>Styan</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibborn</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"born</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stubborn.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibial</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stibium</er>.]</ety> <def>Like, or having the qualities of,
antimony; antimonial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibialism</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"i*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Antimonial intoxication or poisoning.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibiated</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"i*a`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>stibiatus</ets>, from L. <ets>stibium</ets> antimony.]</ety>
<fld>(Med. Chem.)</fld> <def>Combined or impregnated with antimony
(<xex>stibium</xex>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stibiated tartar</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Tartar
emetic</cref>, under <er>Tartar</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibic</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Antimonic; -- used with reference to certain compounds of
antimony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibiconite</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"i*co*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A native oxide of antimony occurring in masses of a yellow
color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibine</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Antimony hydride, or hydrogen antimonide, a colorless gas produced
by the action of nascent hydrogen on antimony. It has a characteristic
odor and burns with a characteristic greenish flame. Formerly called
also <altname>antimoniureted hydrogen</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibious</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Antimonious.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stib"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stibium</ets>, <ets>stibi</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The technical name of antimony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Stibnite.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibnite</ent><br/
<hw>Stib"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
mineral of a lead-gray color and brilliant metallic luster, occurring in
prismatic crystals; sulphide of antimony; -- called also
<altname>antimony glance</altname>, and <altname>gray
antimony</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stibonium</ent><br/
<hw>Sti*bo"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The hypothetical radical <chform>SbH4</chform>, analogous to
ammonium; -- called also <altname>antimonium</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sticcado</ent><br/
<hw>Stic*ca"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. It.
<ets>steccato</ets> a palisade.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>An
instrument consisting of small bars of wood, flat at the bottom and
rounded at the top, and resting on the edges of a kind of open box. They
are unequal in size, gradually increasing from the smallest to the
largest, and are tuned to the diatonic scale. The tones are produced by
striking the pieces of wood with hard balls attached to flexible
sticks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stich</ent><br/
<hw>Stich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>sti`chos</grk>
a row, line, akin to to go, march, E. <ets>sty</ets>, v. i.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A verse, of whatever measure or number of
feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A line in the Scriptures; specifically <fld>(Hebrew
Scriptures)</fld>, one of the rhythmic lines in the poetical books and
passages of the Old Treatment, as written in the oldest Hebrew
manuscripts and in the Revised Version of the English Bible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A row, line, or rank of trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stichic</ent><br/
<hw>Stich"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>stichiko`s</grk>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to stichs, or lines;
consisting of stichs, or lines.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stichidium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sti*chid"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stichida</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/, dim. of <?/ a row.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A special
podlike or fusiform branch containing tetraspores. It is found in
certain red algae.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stichomancy</ent><br/
<hw>Stich"o*man`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a line
+ <ets>-mancy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Divination by lines, or passages of
books, taken at hazard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stichometrical</ent><br/
<hw>Stich`o*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to stichometry; characterized by stichs, or lines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stichometry</ent><br/
<hw>Stich*om"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a line
+ <ets>-metry</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Measurement of books by the
number of lines which they contain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Division of the text of a book into lines;
especially, the division of the text of books into lines accommodated to
the sense, -- a method of writing manuscripts used before punctuation
was adopted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stichwort</ent><br/
<hw>Stich"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A kind of chickweed (<spn>Stellaria Holostea</spn>).</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>stitchwort</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stick</ent><br/
<hw>Stick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sticke</ets>,
AS. <ets>sticca</ets>; akin to <ets>stician</ets> to stab, prick,
pierce, G. <ets>stecken</ets> a stick, staff, OHG. <ets>steccho</ets>,
Icel. <ets>stik</ets> a stick. See <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small shoot, or branch, separated,
as by a cutting, from a tree or shrub; also, any stem or branch of a
tree, of any size, cut for fuel or timber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Withered <qex>sticks</qex> to gather, which might serve<br/
Against a winter's day.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any long and comparatively slender piece of wood,
whether in natural form or shaped with tools; a rod; a wand; a staff;
<as>as, the <ex>stick</ex> of a rocket; a walking
<ex>stick</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything shaped like a stick; <as>as, a
<ex>stick</ex> of wax</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A derogatory expression for a person; one who is
inert or stupid; <as>as, an odd <ex>stick</ex>; a poor
<ex>stick</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A composing stick. See under
<er>Composing</er>. It is usually a frame of metal, but for posters,
handbills, etc., one made of wood is used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A thrust with a pointed instrument; a
stab.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>A stick of eels</b></col>, <cd>twenty-five eels.</cd>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stick chimney</b></col>, <cd>a
chimney made of sticks laid crosswise, and cemented with clay or mud, as
in some log houses.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stick
insect</b></col>, <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of various species of
wingless orthopterous insects of the family <fam>Phasmidae</fam>, which
have a long round body, resembling a stick in form and color, and long
legs, which are often held rigidly in such positions as to make them
resemble small twigs. They thus imitate the branches and twigs of the
trees on which they live. The common American species is
<spn>Diapheromera femorata</spn>. Some of the Asiatic species are more
than a foot long.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To cut one's stick</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>To cut stick</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to run away.</cd>
<mark>[Slang]</mark> <au>De Quincey.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stick</ent><br/
<hw>Stick</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stuck</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> (<mark>Obs.</mark>
<conjf>Sticked</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sticking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stikien</ets>, v.t. &
i., combined with <ets>steken</ets>, whence E. <ets>stuck</ets>), AS.
<ets>stician</ets>, v.t. & i., and (assumed) <ets>stecan</ets>, v.t.;
akin to OFries. <ets>steka</ets>, OS. <ets>stekan</ets>, OHG.
<ets>stehhan</ets>, G. <ets>stechen</ets>, and to Gr. <?/ to prick, Skr.
<ets>tij</ets> to be sharp. Cf. <er>Distinguish</er>,
<er>Etiquette</er>, <er>Extinct</er>, <er>Instigate</er>,
<er>Instinct</er>, <er>Prestige</er>, <er>Stake</er>, <er>Steak</er>,
<er>Stick</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Stigma</er>, <er>Stimulate</er>,
<er>Sting</er>, <er>Stitch</er> in sewing, <er>Style</er> for or in
writing.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To penetrate with a pointed instrument;
to pierce; to stab; hence, to kill by piercing; <as>as, to
<ex>stick</ex> a beast</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>sticked</qex> him with bodkins anon.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was a shame . . . to <qex>stick</qex> him under the other
gentleman's arm while he was redding the fray.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to penetrate; to push, thrust, or drive, so
as to pierce; <as>as, to <ex>stick</ex> a needle into one's
finger</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou <qex>stickest</qex> a dagger in me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fasten, attach, or cause to remain, by thrusting
in; hence, also, to adorn or deck with things fastened on as by
piercing; <as>as, to <ex>stick</ex> a pin on the sleeve</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My shroud of white, <qex>stuck</qex> all with yew.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The points of spears are <qex>stuck</qex> within the shield.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To set; to fix in; <as>as, to <ex>stick</ex> card
teeth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To set with something pointed; <as>as, to
<ex>stick</ex> cards</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To fix on a pointed instrument; to impale; <as>as,
to <ex>stick</ex> an apple on a fork</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To attach by causing to adhere to the surface;
<as>as, to <ex>stick</ex> on a plaster; to <ex>stick</ex> a stamp on an
envelope; also, to attach in any manner</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>To compose; to set, or arrange,
in a composing stick; <as>as, to <ex>stick</ex> type</as>.</def>
<mark>[Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Joinery)</fld> <def>To run or plane (moldings) in a
machine, in contradistinction to working them by hand. Such moldings are
said to be <xex>stuck</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To cause to stick; to bring to a stand; to pose; to
puzzle; <as>as, to <ex>stick</ex> one with a hard problem</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To impose upon; to compel to pay; sometimes, to
cheat.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stick out</b></col>, <cd>to cause to project or
protrude; to render prominent.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stick</ent><br/
<hw>Stick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
adhere; <as>as, glue <ex>sticks</ex> to the fingers; paste
<ex>sticks</ex> to the wall</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The green caterpillar breedeth in the inward parts of roses not
blown, where the dew <qex>sticketh</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To remain where placed; to be fixed; to hold fast to
any position so as to be moved with difficulty; to cling; to abide; to
cleave; to be united closely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A friend that <qex>sticketh</qex> closer than a brother.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xviii. 24.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am a kind of bur; I shall <qex>stick</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If on your fame our sex a bolt has thrown,<br/
'T will ever <qex>stick</qex> through malice of your own.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be prevented from going farther; to stop by
reason of some obstacle; to be stayed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had most need of blessing, and <ldquo/Amen<rdquo/<br/
<qex>Stuck</qex> in my throat.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The trembling weapon passed<br/
Through nine bull hides, . . . and <qex>stuck</qex> within the last.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be embarrassed or puzzled; to hesitate; to be
deterred, as by scruples; to scruple; -- often with
<xex>at</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They will <qex>stick</qex> long at part of a demonstration for
want of perceiving the connection of two ideas.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some <qex>stick</qex> not to say, that the parson and attorney
forged a will.</q> <rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To cause difficulties, scruples, or
hesitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is the difficulty that <qex>sticks</qex> with the most
reasonable.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stick by</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To adhere closely
to; to be firm in supporting</cd>. <ldquo/We are your only friends;
<xex>stick by</xex> us, and we will <xex>stick by</xex> you.<rdquo/
<au>Davenant.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To be troublesome by adhering.</cd>
<ldquo/I am satisfied to trifle away my time, rather than let it
<xex>stick by</xex> me.<rdquo/ <au>Pope.</au> -- <col><b>To stick
out</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To project; to be prominent.</cd>
<ldquo/His bones that were not seen <xex>stick out</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Job
xxxiii. 21.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To persevere in a purpose; to hold
out; as, the garrison <xex>stuck out</xex> until relieved.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><-- also <pos>v. i.</pos> to stick it out. --> --
<col><b>To stick to</b></col>, <cd>to be persevering in holding to;
<as>as, <ex>to stick to</ex> a party or cause</as>.</cd> <ldquo/The
advantage will be on our side if we <xex>stick to</xex> its
essentials.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au> -- <col><b>To stick up</b></col>,
<cd>to stand erect; <as>as, his hair <ex>sticks up</ex></as>.</cd> --
<col><b>To stick up for</b></col>, <cd>to assert and defend; <as>as,
<ex>to stick up for</ex> one's rights or for a friend</as>.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <col><b>To stick upon</b></col>, <cd>to dwell
upon; not to forsake.</cd> <ldquo/If the matter be knotty, the mind must
stop and buckle to it, and <xex>stick upon</xex> it with labor and
thought.<rdquo/ <au>Locke.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sticked</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos>
<mord>of <er>Stick</er></mord>. <def>Stuck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And in the sand her ship <qex>sticked</qex> so fast.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>sticked</qex> not to give their bodies to be burnt.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sticker</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, sticks; <as>as, a bill <ex>sticker</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which causes one to stick; that which puzzles
or poses.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Tackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>In the organ, a small wooden rod
which connects (in part) a key and a pallet, so as to communicate motion
by pushing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Paster</er>, 2.</def> <mark>[Political
Cant, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stickful</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stickfuls</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>As
much set type as fills a composing stick.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stickiness</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
being sticky; <as>as, the <ex>stickiness</ex> of glue or
paste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sticking</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Stick</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Sticking piece</b></col>, <cd>a piece of beef cut from
the neck.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Sticking place</b></col>,
<cd>the place where a thing sticks, or remains fast; sticking
point.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But screw your courage to the <qex>sticking place</qex>,<br/
And we'll not fail.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Sticking plaster</b></col>, <cd>an adhesive plaster for
closing wounds, and for similar uses.</cd> -- <col><b>Sticking
point</b></col>. <cd>Same as <cref>Sticking place</cref>,
above.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Stickit</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stuck; spoiled in
making.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stickit minister</b></col>, <cd>a candidate for the
clerical office who fails, disqualified by incompetency or
immorality.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stick-lac</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"-lac`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See the Note under
<er>Lac</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stickle</ent><br/
<hw>Stic"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stickled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stickling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably fr. OE.
<ets>stightlen</ets>, <ets>sti<?/tlen</ets>, to dispose, arrange,
govern, freq. of <ets>stihten</ets>, AS. <ets>stihtan</ets>: cf. G.
<ets>stiften</ets> to found, to establish.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
separate combatants by intervening.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When he [the angel] sees half of the Christians killed, and the
rest in a fair way of being routed, he <qex>stickles</qex> betwixt the
remainder of God's host and the race of fiends.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To contend, contest, or altercate, esp. in a
pertinacious manner on insufficient grounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fortune, as she 's wont, turned fickle,<br/
And for the foe began to <qex>stickle</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While for paltry punk they roar and <qex>stickle</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The obstinacy with which he <qex>stickles</qex> for the wrong.</q>
<rj><qau>Hazlitt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To play fast and loose; to pass from one side to the
other; to trim.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stickle</ent><br/
<hw>Stic"kle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To separate, as
combatants; hence, to quiet, to appease, as disputants.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which [question] violently they pursue,<br/
Nor <qex>stickled</qex> would they be.</q>
<rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To intervene in; to stop, or put an end to, by
intervening; hence, to arbitrate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They ran to him, and, pulling him back by force,
<qex>stickled</qex> that unnatural fray.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P.
Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stickle</ent><br/
<hw>Stic"kle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>stick</er>, <pos>v. t. &
i.</pos>]</ety> <def>A shallow rapid in a river; also, the current below
a waterfall.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Patient anglers, standing all the day<br/
Near to some shallow <qex>stickle</qex> or deep bay.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stickleback</ent><br/
<hw>Stic"kle*back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. & Prov E.
<ets>stickle</ets> a prickle, spine, sting (AS. <ets>sticel</ets>) +
<ets>back</ets>. See <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Banstickle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous
species of small fishes of the genus <gen>Gasterosteus</gen> and allied
genera. The back is armed with two or more sharp spines. They inhabit
both salt and brackish water, and construct curious nests. Called also
<altname>sticklebag</altname>, <altname>sharpling</altname>, and
<altname>prickleback</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1414 --></p>

<p><ent>Stickler</ent><br/
<hw>Stic"kler</hw> <pr>(st<icr/k"kl<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[See <er>Stickle</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>One who
stickles.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>One who arbitrates a duel; a sidesman to a fencer;
a second; an umpire.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Basilius, the judge, appointed <qex>sticklers</qex> and trumpets
whom the others should obey.</q> <rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our former chiefs, like <qex>sticklers</qex> of the war,<br/
First sought to inflame the parties, then to poise.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>One who pertinaciously contends for some trifling
things, as a point of etiquette; an unreasonable, obstinate contender;
<as>as, a <ex>stickler</ex> for ceremony</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Tory or High-church were the greatest <qex>sticklers</qex>
against the exorbitant proceedings of King James II.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stick-seed</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"-seed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A plant (<spn>Echinospermum Lappula</spn>) of the Borage family,
with small blue flowers and prickly nutlets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sticktail</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The ruddy
duck.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>stick-tight</ent><br/
<hw>stick"-tight`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>any of several plants having seeds tipped with barbs
that may cling to fur or clothing, especially those of the genus
<gen>Bidens</gen>, also called <altname>bur marigold</altname>,
<altname>beggar-ticks</altname>, and <altname>Beggar's ticks</altname>,
which have prickly flattened achenes.</def> <au>RHUD</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a seed from a sticktight{1}.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sticky</ent><br/
<hw>Stick"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stickier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stickiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <def>Having the quality of sticking to
a surface; adhesive; gluey; viscous; viscid; glutinous;
tenacious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Herbs which last longest are those of strong smell, and with a
<qex>sticky</qex> stalk.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiddy</ent><br/
<hw>Stid"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stithy</er>.]</ety> <def>An anvil; also, a smith shop. See
<er>Stithy</er>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiff</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stiffer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stiffest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stif</ets>, AS.
<ets>st<imac/f</ets>; akin to D. <ets>stijf</ets>, G. <ets>steif</ets>,
Dan. <ets>stiv</ets>, Sw. <ets>styf</ets>, Icel. <ets>st<imac/fr</ets>,
Lith. <ets>stipti</ets> to be stiff; cf. L. <ets>stipes</ets> a post,
trunk of a tree, <ets>stipare</ets> to press, compress. Cf.
<er>Costive</er>, <er>Stifle</er>, <er>Stipulate</er>, <er>Stive</er> to
stuff.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not easily bent; not flexible or pliant;
not limber or flaccid; rigid; firm; <as>as, <ex>stiff</ex> wood, paper,
joints</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They] rising on <qex>stiff</qex> pennons, tower<br/
The mid aerial sky.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not liquid or fluid; thick and tenacious;
inspissated; neither soft nor hard; <as>as, the paste is
<ex>stiff</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Firm; strong; violent; difficult to oppose; <as>as,
a <ex>stiff</ex> gale or breeze</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not easily subdued; unyielding; stubborn; obstinate;
pertinacious; <as>as, a <ex>stiff</ex> adversary</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a shame to stand <qex>stiff</qex> in a foolish argument.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A war ensues: the Cretans own their cause,<br/
<qex>Stiff</qex> to defend their hospitable laws.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Not natural and easy; formal; constrained; affected;
starched; <as>as, <ex>stiff</ex> behavior; a <ex>stiff</ex>
style</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The French are open, familiar, and talkative; the Italians
<qex>stiff</qex>, ceremonious, and reserved.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Harsh; disagreeable; severe; hard to bear.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/This is <xex>stiff</xex>
news.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Bearing a press of canvas without
careening much; <as>as, a <ex>stiff</ex> vessel</as>; -- opposed to
<contr>crank</contr>.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Very large, strong, or costly; powerful; <as>as, a
<ex>stiff</ex> charge; a <ex>stiff</ex> price</as>.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stiff neck</b></col>, <cd>a condition of the neck such
that the head can not be moved without difficulty and
pain.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Rigid; inflexible; strong; hardly; stubborn;
obstinate; pertinacious; harsh; formal; constrained; affected; starched;
rigorous.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiff-backed</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"-backed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Obstinate.</def> <rj><au>J. H. Newman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiffen</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stiffened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stiffening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Stiff</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make stiff; to make less
pliant or flexible; <as>as, to <ex>stiffen</ex> cloth with
starch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stiffen</qex> the sinews, summon up the blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To inspissate; to make more thick or viscous;
<as>as, to <ex>stiffen</ex> paste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make torpid; to benumb.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiffen</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"en</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become stiff or stiffer, in
any sense of the adjective.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like bristles rose my <qex>stiffening</qex> hair.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tender soil then <qex>stiffening</qex> by degrees.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some souls we see,<br/
Grow hard and <qex>stiffen</qex> with adversity.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiffener</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"en*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, stiffens anything, as a piece of stiff cloth in a
cravat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiffening</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"en*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act or process of
making stiff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something used to make anything stiff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stiffening order</b></col> <fld>(Com.)</fld>, <cd>a
permission granted by the customs department to take cargo or ballast on
board before the old cargo is out, in order to steady the
ship.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiff-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stiff</ets> + <ets>heart</ets>.]</ety> <def>Obstinate;
stubborn; contumacious.</def> <rj><au>Ezek. ii. 4.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiffish</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat stiff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiffly</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stiff
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiff-necked</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"-necked`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stubborn;
inflexibly obstinate; contumacious; <as>as, <ex>stiff-necked</ex> pride;
a <ex>stiff-necked</ex> people.</as></def> <rj><au>Ex. xxxii.
9.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiff-neckedness</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"-neck`ed*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being stiff-necked; stubbornness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiffness</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stiff; <as>as, the <ex>stiffness</ex> of cloth or of paste;
<ex>stiffness</ex> of manner; <ex>stiffness</ex> of
character.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The vices of old age have the <qex>stiffness</qex> of it too.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stifftail</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The ruddy
duck.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiff-tailed</ent><br/
<hw>Stiff"-tailed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the quill feathers of the tail somewhat rigid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stifle</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Stiff</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>The joint next above the
hock, and near the flank, in the hind leg of the horse and allied
animals; the joint corresponding to the knee in man; -- called also
<altname>stifle joint</altname>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Horse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stifle bone</b></col>, <cd>a small bone at the stifle
joint; the patella, or kneepan.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stifle</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"fle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stifled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stifling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of OE.
<ets>stif</ets> stiff; cf. Icel. <ets>st<imac/fla</ets> to dam
up.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To stop the breath of by crowding something
into the windpipe, or introducing an irrespirable substance into the
lungs; to choke; to suffocate; to cause the death of by such means;
<as>as, to <ex>stifle</ex> one with smoke or dust</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stifled</qex> with kisses, a sweet death he dies.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I took my leave, being half <qex>stifled</qex> with the closeness
of the room.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stop; to extinguish; to deaden; to quench;
<as>as, to <ex>stifle</ex> the breath; to <ex>stifle</ex> a fire or
flame</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bodies . . . <qex>stifle</qex> in themselves the rays which they
do not reflect or transmit.</q> <rj><qau>Sir I. Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To suppress the manifestation or report of; to
smother; to conceal from public knowledge; <as>as, to <ex>stifle</ex> a
story; to <ex>stifle</ex> passion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I desire only to have things fairly represented as they really
are; no evidence smothered or <qex>stifled</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waterland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stifle</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"fle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To die by reason of
obstruction of the breath, or because some noxious substance prevents
respiration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You shall <qex>stifle</qex> in your own report.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stifled</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"fled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stifling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The close and <qex>stifled</qex> study.</q>
<rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stifler</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"fler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, stifles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>See <er>Camouflet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigma</ent><br/
<hw>Stig"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Stigmas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Stigmata</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a mark, a brand, from Gr. <?/, <?/, the
prick or mark of a pointed instrument, a spot, mark, from <?/ to prick,
to brand. See <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
mark made with a burning iron; a brand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any mark of infamy or disgrace; sign of moral
blemish; stain or reproach caused by dishonorable conduct; reproachful
characterization.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The blackest <qex>stigma</qex> that can be fastened upon him.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All such slaughters were from thence called Bartelmies, simply in
a perpetual <qex>stigma</qex> of that butchery.</q> <rj><qau>Sir G.
Buck.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>That part of a pistil which has no
epidermis, and is fitted to receive the pollen. It is usually the
terminal portion, and is commonly somewhat glutinous or viscid. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Stamen</er> and of <er>Flower</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A small spot, mark, scar, or a
minute hole; -- applied especially to a spot on the outer surface of a
Graafian follicle, and to spots of intercellular substance in scaly
epithelium, or to minute holes in such spots.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Pathol.)</fld> <def>A red speck upon the skin,
produced either by the extravasation of blood, as in the bloody sweat
characteristic of certain varieties of religious ecstasy, or by
capillary congestion, as in the case of drunkards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>One of the external
openings of the tracheae of insects, myriapods, and other arthropods; a
spiracle.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the apertures of the pulmonary
sacs of arachnids. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Scorpion</er>.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>One of the apertures of the gill of an ascidian, and
of Amphioxus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A point so connected by any law
whatever with another point, called an <xex>index</xex>, that as the
index moves in any manner in a plane the first point or stigma moves in
a determinate way in the same plane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>Marks
believed to have been supernaturally impressed upon the bodies of
certain persons in imitation of the wounds on the crucified body of
Christ. See def. 5, above.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmaria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stig*ma"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Stigma</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld> <def>The fossil root stem
of a coal plant of the genus <gen>Sigillaria</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stig"ma*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <def><pos>pl.</pos>
of <er>Stigma</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Stig*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
notorious profligate or criminal who has been branded; one who bears the
marks of infamy or punishment.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bullokar.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person who is marked or deformed by nature.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- <sn>3.</sn> <def>A person bearing the wounds on the hands and
feet resembling those of Jesus Christ caused by His crucifixion; -- for
true <ex>stigmantics</ex> the wounds are supposed to have been caused
miraculously, as a sign of great holiness.</def> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmatical</ent><br/
<ent>Stigmatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stig*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stig*mat"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stigma</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Marked with a stigma, or with something reproachful to
character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Impressing with infamy or reproach.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot., Anat., etc)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a
stigma or stigmata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Stigmatic geometry</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Stigmatics</b></col></mcol>, <cd>that science in which the
correspondence of index and stigma (see <er>Stigma</er>, 7) is made use
of to establish geometrical proportions.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmatically</ent><br/
<hw>Stig*mat"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>With a stigma, or mark
of infamy or deformity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmatist</ent><br/
<hw>Stig"ma*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One believed to
be supernaturally impressed with the marks of Christ's wounds. See
<er>Stigma</er>, 8.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmatization</ent><br/
<hw>Stig`ma*ti*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of stigmatizing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld> <def>The production of stigmata
upon the body. See <er>Stigma</er>, 8.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmatize</ent><br/
<hw>Stig"ma*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stigmatized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stigmatizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>stigmatiser</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To mark with a
stigma, or brand; <as>as, the ancients <ex>stigmatized</ex> their slaves
and soldiers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That . . . hold out both their ears with such delight and
ravishment, to be <qex>stigmatized</qex> and bored through in witness of
their own voluntary and beloved baseness.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set a mark of disgrace on; to brand with some
mark of reproach or infamy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To find virtue extolled and vice <qex>stigmatized</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigmatose</ent><br/
<hw>Stig"ma*tose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Stigmatic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stigonomancy</ent><br/
<hw>Stig"o*no*man`cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<?/, one who is marked, or one who marks (<?/ to mark with a pointed
instrument, to prick) + <ets>-mancy</ets>.]</ety> <def>Divination by
writing on the bark of a tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stike</ent><br/
<hw>Stike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stich</er>.]</ety> <def>Stanza.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sackville.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilar</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Stile</er> a
style.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the style of a dial.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>stylar</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilbene</ent><br/
<hw>Stil"bene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stilbite</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hydrocarbon,
<chform>C14H12</chform>, produced artificially in large, fine crystals;
-- called also <altname>diphenyl ethylene</altname>,
<altname>toluylene</altname>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilbite</ent><br/
<hw>Stil"bite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to glitter,
shine: cf. F. <ets>stilbite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
common mineral of the zeolite family, a hydrous silicate of alumina and
lime, usually occurring in sheaflike aggregations of crystals, also in
radiated masses. It is of a white or yellowish color, with pearly luster
on the cleavage surface. Called also
<altname>desmine</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stile</ent><br/
<hw>Stile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Style</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pin set on the face of a dial,
to cast a shadow; a style. See <er>Style</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Moxon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Mode of composition. See <er>Style</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>May I not write in such a <qex>stile</qex> as this?</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stile</ent><br/
<hw>Stile</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stile</ets>, AS.
<ets>stigel</ets> a step, a ladder, from <ets>st<imac/gan</ets> to
ascend; akin to OHG. <ets>stigila</ets> a stile. <root/164. See
<er>Sty</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, and cf. <er>Stair</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A step, or set of steps, for ascending and descending,
in passing a fence or wall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There comes my master . . . over the <qex>stile</qex>, this
way.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Over this <qex>stile</qex> in the way to Doubting Castle.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>One of the upright pieces in a
frame; one of the primary members of a frame, into which the secondary
members are mortised.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In an ordinary door the principal upright pieces are
called <xex>stiles</xex>, the subordinate upright pieces
<xex>mullions</xex>, and the crosspieces <xex>rails</xex>. In
wainscoting the principal pieces are sometimes called <xex>stiles</xex>,
even when horizontal.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Hanging stile</b></col>, <col><b>Pulley
stile</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Hanging</er>, and
<er>Pulley</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilet</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>stilette</ets>, and <ets>stylet</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
stiletto.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>See <er>Stylet</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiletto</ent><br/
<hw>Sti*let"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stilettos</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It., dim. of
<ets>stilo</ets> a dagger, fr. L. <ets>stilus</ets> a pointed
instrument. See <er>Style</er> for writing, and cf.
<er>Stylet</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of dagger with a
slender, rounded, and pointed blade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pointed instrument for making eyelet holes in
embroidery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A beard trimmed into a pointed form.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The very quack of fashions, the very he that<br/
Wears a <qex>stiletto</qex> on his chin.</q>
<rj><qau>Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiletto</ent><br/
<hw>Sti*let"to</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stilettoed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stilettoing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To stab or kill
with a stiletto.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still</ent><br/
<hw>Still</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stiller</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stillest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stille</ets>, AS.
<ets>stille</ets>; akin to D. <ets>stil</ets>, OS. & OHG.
<ets>stilli</ets>, G. <ets>still</ets>, Dan. <ets>stille</ets>, Sw.
<ets>stilla</ets>, and to E. <ets>stall</ets>; from the idea of coming
to a stand, or halt. Cf. <er>Still</er>, <pos>adv.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Motionless; at rest; quiet; <as>as, to stand
<ex>still</ex>; to lie or sit <ex>still</ex></as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Still</xex> as any stone.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Uttering no sound; silent; <as>as, the audience is
<ex>still</ex>; the animals are <ex>still</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sea that roared at thy command,<br/
At thy command was <qex>still</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not disturbed by noise or agitation; quiet; calm;
<as>as, a <ex>still</ex> evening; a <ex>still</ex>
atmosphere</as>.</def> <ldquo/When all the woods are
<xex>still</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Comparatively quiet or silent; soft; gentle;
low.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>still</xex> small voice.<rdquo/ <rj><au>1 Kings
xix. 12.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Constant; continual.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By <qex>still</qex> practice learn to know thy meaning.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Not effervescing; not sparkling; <as>as,
<ex>still</ex> wines</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Still life</b></col>. <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>Inanimate objects</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Painting)</fld> <cd>The
class or style of painting which represents inanimate objects, as fruit,
flowers, dead game, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Quiet; calm; noiseless; serene; motionless;
inert; stagnant.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still</ent><br/
<hw>Still</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>stille</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Freedom from noise; calm; silence; <as>as, the
<ex>still</ex> of midnight</as>.</def> <mark>[Poetic]</mark><-- the
still of the night --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A steep hill or ascent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>W. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still</ent><br/
<hw>Still</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stille</ets> quietly. See
<er>Still</er>, <pos>a.</pos> The modern senses come from the idea of
stopping and <ets>staying</ets> still, or motionless.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To this time; until and during the time now present; now no less
than before; yet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It hath been anciently reported, and is <qex>still</qex>
received.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the future as now and before.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hourly joys be <qex>still</qex> upon you!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In continuation by successive or repeated acts;
always; ever; constantly; uniformly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The desire of fame betrays an ambitious man into indecencies that
lessen his reputation; he is <qex>still</qex> afraid lest any of his
actions should be thrown away in private.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Chemists would be rich if they could <qex>still</qex> do in great
quantities what they have sometimes done in little.</q>
<rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>In an increasing or additional degree; even more; --
much used with comparatives.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The guilt being great, the fear doth <qex>still</qex> exceed.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Notwithstanding what has been said or done; in spite
of what has occured; nevertheless; -- sometimes used as a conjunction.
See Synonym of <er>But</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As sunshine, broken in the rill,<br/
Though turned astray, is sunshine <qex>still</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Moore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>After that; after what is stated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the primitive church, such as by fear being compelled to
sacrifice to strange gods, after repented, and kept <qex>still</qex> the
office of preaching the gospel.</q> <rj><qau>Whitgift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Still and anon</b></col>, <cd>at intervals and
repeatedly; continually; ever and anon; now and then.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And like the watchful minutes to the hour,<br/
<qex>Still and anon</qex> cheered up the heavy time.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still</ent><br/
<hw>Still</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stilled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>stillan</ets>, from
<ets>stille</ets> still, quiet, firm. See <er>Still</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To stop, as motion or agitation;
to cause to become quiet, or comparatively quiet; to check the agitation
of; <as>as, to <ex>still</ex> the raging sea</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He having a full sway over the water, had power to
<qex>still</qex> and compose it, as well as to move and disturb it.</q>
<rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stop, as noise; to silence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With his name the mothers <qex>still</qex> their babies.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To appease; to calm; to quiet, as tumult, agitation,
or excitement; <as>as, to <ex>still</ex> the passions</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Toil that would, at least, have <qex>stilled</qex> an unquiet
impulse in me.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To quiet; calm; allay; lull; pacify; appease;
subdue; suppress; silence; stop; check; restrain.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1415 --></p>

<p><ent>Still</ent><br/
<hw>Still</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OE.
<ets>stillatorie</ets>. See <er>Still</er>, <pos>v.</pos>, to
distill.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A vessel, boiler, or copper used in the
distillation of liquids; specifically, one used for the distillation of
alcoholic liquors; a retort. The name is sometimes applied to the whole
apparatus used in in vaporization and condensation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A house where liquors are distilled; a
distillery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Still watcher</b></col>, <cd>a device for indicating the
progress of distillation by the density of the liquid given over.</cd>
<au>Knight.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still</ent><br/
<hw>Still</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Abbreviated fr.
<ets>distill</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to fall by
drops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To expel spirit from by heat, or to evaporate and
condense in a refrigeratory; to distill.</def>
<rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still</ent><br/
<hw>Still</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stillare</ets>. Cf.
<er>Distill</er>.]</ety> <def>To drop, or flow in drops; to
distill.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillage</ent><br/
<hw>Stil"lage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bleaching)</fld>
<def>A low stool to keep the goods from touching the floor.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillatitious</ent><br/
<hw>Stil`la*ti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stillaticius</ets>, fr. <ets>stillare</ets> to drop,
<ets>stilla</ets> a drop.]</ety> <def>Falling in drops; drawn by a
still.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillatory</ent><br/
<hw>Stil"la*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[From <er>Still</er>, for
<ets>distill</ets>. Cf. <er>Still</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and
<er>Distillatory</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An alembic;
a vessel for distillation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A laboratory; a place or room in which distillation
is performed.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au> <au>Sir
H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillbirth</ent><br/
<hw>Still"birth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
birth of a dead fetus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a fetus which was born dead.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillborn</ent><br/
<hw>Still"born`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Dead
at the birth; <as>as, a <ex>stillborn</ex> child</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: Abortive; <as>as, a <ex>stillborn</ex>
poem</as>.</def> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still-burn</ent><br/
<hw>Still"-burn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <er>still-burnt</er> <pr>(?)</pr> or <er>Still-burned</er>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<er>Still-burning</er>.]</ety> <def>To burn in the process of
distillation; <as>as, to <ex>still-burn</ex> brandy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still-closing</ent><br/
<hw>Still"-clos"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Ever
closing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Still-clothing</xex>
waters.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiller</ent><br/
<hw>Still"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who stills, or
quiets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Still"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A house in which
distillation is carried on; a distillery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still-hunt</ent><br/
<hw>Still"-hunt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hunting for
game in a quiet and cautious manner, or under cover; stalking; hence,
colloquially, the pursuit of any object quietly and cautiously.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Still"-hunt`er</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Still"-hunt`ing</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillicide</ent><br/
<hw>Stil"li*cide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stillicidium</ets>; <ets>stilla</ets> a drop + <ets>cadere</ets> to
fall.]</ety> <def>A continual falling or succession of drops; rain water
falling from the eaves.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillicidious</ent><br/
<hw>Stil`li*cid"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Falling in
drops.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilliform</ent><br/
<hw>Stil"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stilla</ets> a drop + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the form
of a drop.</def> <rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilling</ent><br/
<hw>Still"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LG.
<ets>stelling</ets>, G. <ets>stellen</ets> to set, to place.]</ety>
<def>A stillion.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillion</ent><br/
<hw>Stil"lion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stilling</er>.]</ety> <def>A stand, as for casks or vats in a
brewery, or for pottery while drying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still life</ent><br/
<hw>Still" life</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>a type
of painting in which inanimate objects such as flowers or fruit are
represented.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>the inanimate subject matter of a <er>still
life</er> painting or photograph; <as>as, to painted only <ex>still
life</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillness</ent><br/
<hw>Still"ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or state of being still; quietness; silence; calmness;
inactivity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Painting, then, was the art demanded by the modern intellect upon
its emergence from the <qex>stillness</qex> of the Middle Ages.</q>
<rj><qau>J. A. Symonds.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Habitual silence or quiet; taciturnity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The gravity and <qex>stillness</qex> of your youth<br/
The world hath noted.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillroom</ent><br/
<hw>Still"room`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A room
for distilling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An apartment in a house where liquors, preserves,
and the like, are kept.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Floors are rubbed bright, . . . <qex>stillroom</qex> and kitchen
cleared for action.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillson wrench</ent><br/
<hw>Still"son wrench</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A pipe wrench having an
adjustable <universbold>L</universbold>-shaped jaw piece sliding in a
sleeve that is pivoted to, and loosely embraces, the handle. Pressure on
the handle increases the grip.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stillstand</ent><br/
<hw>Still"stand`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
standstill.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still water</ent><br/
<hw>Still" wat*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a section of a
stream that is flat and moves slowly.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Still wine</ent><br/
<hw>Still" wine`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a table wine
which is not effervescent; a non-sparkling wine.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilly</ent><br/
<hw>Still"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Still; quiet;
calm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>stilly</qex> hour when storms are gone.</q>
<rj><qau>Moore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilly</ent><br/
<hw>Stil"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a still manner;
quietly; silently; softly.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The hum of either army <qex>stilly</qex> sounds.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilpnomelane</ent><br/
<hw>Stilp*nom"e*lane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>stilpno`s</grk> shining + <grk>me`las</grk>, <grk>-anos</grk>,
black.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A black or greenish black mineral
occurring in foliated flates, also in velvety bronze-colored
incrustations. It is a hydrous silicate of iron and alumina.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilt</ent><br/
<hw>Stilt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stilte</ets>;
akin to Dan. <ets>stylte</ets>, Sw. <ets>stylta</ets>, LG. & D.
<ets>stelt</ets>, OHG. <ets>stelza</ets>, G. <ets>stelze</ets>, and
perh. to E. <ets>stout</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pole, or piece
of wood, constructed with a step or loop to raise the foot above the
ground in walking. It is sometimes lashed to the leg, and sometimes
prolonged upward so as to be steadied by the hand or arm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ambition is but avarice on <qex>stilts</qex>, and masked.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A crutch; also, the handle of a plow.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any species of limicoline birds
belonging to <gen>Himantopus</gen> and allied genera, in which the legs
are remarkably long and slender. Called also
<altname>longshanks</altname>, <altname>stiltbird</altname>,
<altname>stilt plover</altname>, and
<altname>lawyer</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The American species (<spn>Himantopus Mexicanus</spn>)
is well known. The European and Asiatic stilt (<spn>Himantopus
candidus</spn>) is usually white, except the wings and interscapulars,
which are greenish black. The white-headed stilt (<spn>Himantopus
leucocephalus</spn>) and the banded stilt (<spn>Cladorhynchus
pectoralis</spn>) are found in Australia.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stilt plover</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
stilt.</cd> -- <col><b>Stilt sandpiper</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>an American sandpiper (<spn>Micropalama himantopus</spn>) having
long legs. The bill is somewhat expanded at the tip.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilt</ent><br/
<hw>Stilt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stilted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stilting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To raise on stilts, or as if on
stilts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiltbird</ent><br/
<hw>Stilt"bird`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Stilt</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilted</ent><br/
<hw>Stilt"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Elevated as if on stilts; hence,
pompous; bombastic; <as>as, a <ex>stilted</ex> style; <ex>stilted</ex>
declamation.</as></def> <-- also, formal or stiff; -- said of linguistic
style --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stilted arch</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>an arch in
which the springing line is some distance above the impost, the space
between being occupied by a vertical member, molded or ornamented, as a
continuation of the archivolt, intrados, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiltify</ent><br/
<hw>Stilt"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stilt</ets> + <ets>-fy</ets>.]</ety> <def>To raise upon
stilts, or as upon stilts; to stilt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilton</ent><br/
<ent>Stilton cheese</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stil"ton cheese"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Stil"ton</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A peculiarly flavored
unpressed cheese made from milk with cream added; -- so called from the
village or parish of <etsep>Stilton</etsep>, England, where it was
originally made. It is very rich in fat.</def></p>

<p><q>Thus, in the outset he was gastronomic; discussed the dinner from
the soup to the <qex>stilton</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Lever.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stilty</ent><br/
<hw>Stilt"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Unreasonably elevated;
pompous; stilted; <as>as, a <ex>stilty</ex> style</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stime</ent><br/
<hw>Stime</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymology
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A slight gleam or glimmer; a glimpse.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimie</ent><br/
<ent>Stimey</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sti"mey</hw>, <hw>Sti"mie</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.
& v. t.</pos> <def>See <er>Stymie</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulant</ent><br/
<hw>Stim"u*lant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stimulans</ets>, p. pr.; cf. F. <ets>stimulant</ets>. See
<er>Stimulate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Serving to
stimulate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Produced increased vital
action in the organism, or in any of its parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulant</ent><br/
<hw>Stim"u*lant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stimulant</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which stimulates,
provokes, or excites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His feelings had been exasperated by the constant application of
<qex>stimulants</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol. & Med.)</fld> <def>An agent which produces
a temporary increase of vital activity in the organism, or in any of its
parts; -- sometimes used without qualification to signify an alcoholic
beverage used as a stimulant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulate</ent><br/
<hw>Stim"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stimulated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stimulating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>stimulatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>stimulare</ets> to prick or goad
on, to incite, fr. <ets>stimulus</ets> a goad. See
<er>Stimulus</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To excite as if with a goad;
to excite, rouse, or animate, to action or more vigorous exertion by
some pungent motive or by persuasion; <as>as, to <ex>stimulate</ex> one
by the hope of reward, or by the prospect of glory</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To excite and <qex>stimulate</qex> us thereunto.</q> <rj><qau>Dr.
J. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>To excite; to irritate;
especially, to excite the activity of (a nerve or an irritable muscle),
as by electricity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To animate; incite; encourage; impel; urge;
instigate; irritate; exasperate; incense.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulation</ent><br/
<hw>Stim`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stimulatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>stimulation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of stimulating, or the state of being stimulated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The irritating action of
various agents (stimuli) on muscles, nerves, or a sensory end organ, by
which activity is evoked; especially, the nervous impulse produced by
various agents on nerves, or a sensory end organ, by which the part
connected with the nerve is thrown into a state of activity;
irritation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulative</ent><br/
<hw>Stim"u*la*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
quality of stimulating.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>That which
stimulates.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulator</ent><br/
<hw>Stim"u*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.: cf. F.
<ets>stimulateur</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who stimulates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulatress</ent><br/
<hw>Stim"u*la`tress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who
stimulates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulism</ent><br/
<hw>Stim"u*lism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The theory of medical practice which regarded life as
dependent upon stimulation, or excitation, and disease as caused by
excess or deficiency in the amount of stimulation.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The practice of treating disease by alcoholic stimulants.</def>
<rj><au>Dr. H. Hartshorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimulus</ent><br/
<hw>Stim"u*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stimuli</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., for
<ets>stigmulus</ets>, akin to L. <ets>instigare</ets> to stimulate. See
<er>Instigare</er>, <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A goad; hence, something that rouses the mind or spirits; an
incentive; <as>as, the hope of gain is a powerful <ex>stimulus</ex> to
labor and action</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which excites or produces a temporary increase
of vital action, either in the whole organism or in any of its parts;
especially <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, any substance or agent capable of
evoking the activity of a nerve or irritable muscle, or capable of
producing an impression upon a sensory organ or more particularly upon
its specific end organ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Of the stimuli applied to the sensory apparatus,
physiologists distinguish two kinds: (<xex>a</xex>) <stype>Homologous
stimuli</stype>, which act only upon the end organ, and for whose action
the sense organs are especially adapted, as the rods and cones of the
retina for the vibrations of the either. (<xex>b</xex>)
<stype>Heterologous stimuli</stype>, which are mechanical, chemical,
electrical, etc., and act upon the nervous elements of the sensory
apparatus along their entire course, producing, for example, the flash
of light beheld when the eye is struck.</note> <rj><au>Landois &
Stirling.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sting</ent><br/
<hw>Sting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sting</ets> a
sting. See <er>Sting</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any sharp organ of offense and defense,
especially when connected with a poison gland, and adapted to inflict a
wound by piercing; as the caudal <xex>sting</xex> of a scorpion. The
sting of a bee or wasp is a modified ovipositor. The caudal sting, or
spine, of a sting ray is a modified dorsal fin ray. The term is
sometimes applied to the fang of a serpent. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Scorpion</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A sharp-pointed hollow hair seated
on a gland which secrets an acrid fluid, as in nettles. The points of
these hairs usually break off in the wound, and the acrid fluid is
pressed into it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything that gives acute pain, bodily or mental;
<as>as, the <ex>stings</ex> of remorse; the <ex>stings</ex> of
reproach.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sting</qex> of death is sin.</q> <rj><qau>1 Cor. xv.
56.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The thrust of a sting into the flesh; the act of
stinging; a wound inflicted by stinging.</def> <ldquo/The lurking
serpent's mortal <xex>sting</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A goad; incitement.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The point of an epigram or other sarcastic
saying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sting moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an
Australian moth (<spn>Doratifera vulnerans</spn>) whose larva is armed,
at each end of the body, with four tubercles bearing powerful stinging
organs.</cd> -- <col><b>Sting ray</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
under 6th <er>Ray</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sting winkle</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a spinose marine univalve shell of the genus
Murex, as the European species (<spn>Murex erinaceus</spn>). See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Murex</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sting</ent><br/
<hw>Sting</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stung</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> (<mark>Archaic</mark>
<conjf>Stang</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stinging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>stingan</ets>; akin to
Icel. & Sw. <ets>stinga</ets>, Dan. <ets>stinge</ets>, and probably to
E. <ets>stick</ets>, v.t.; cf. Goth. us<ets>stiggan</ets> to put out,
pluck out. Cf. <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To pierce or wound with a sting; <as>as, bees will <ex>sting</ex>
an animal that irritates them; the nettles <ex>stung</ex> his
hands.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pain acutely; <as>as, the conscience is
<ex>stung</ex> with remorse</as>; to bite.</def> <ldquo/Slander
<xex>stings</xex> the brave.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To goad; to incite, as by taunts or
reproaches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingaree</ent><br/
<hw>Sting`a*ree"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any sting ray. See under 6th <er>Ray</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingbull</ent><br/
<hw>Sting"bull`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The European greater weever fish (<spn>Trachinus draco</spn>),
which is capable of inflicting severe wounds with the spinous rays of
its dorsal fin. See <er>Weever</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinger</ent><br/
<hw>Sting"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, stings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Professor E. Forbes states that only a small minority of the
medusae of our seas are <qex>stingers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Owen.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingfish</ent><br/
<hw>Sting"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The weever.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingily</ent><br/
<hw>Stin"gi*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stingy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinginess</ent><br/
<hw>Stin"gi*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stingy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinging</ent><br/
<hw>Sting"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Piercing, or capable
of piercing, with a sting; inflicting acute pain as if with a sting,
goad, or pointed weapon; pungent; biting; <as>as, <ex>stinging</ex>
cold; a <ex>stinging</ex> rebuke.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sting"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stinging cell</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<cref>Lasso cell</cref>, under <er>Lasso</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingless</ent><br/
<hw>Sting"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no sting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingo</ent><br/
<hw>Stin"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sting</er>.]</ety> <def>Old beer; sharp or strong liquor.</def>
<mark>[Old Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shall I set a cup of old <qex>stingo</qex> at your elbow?</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingray</ent><br/
<ent>Sting ray</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sting ray</hw> <it>or</it> <hw>Sting"ray`</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>Any one of numerous rays of the family
<fam>Dasyatidae</fam>, syn. <fam>Trygonidae</fam>, having one or more
large sharp barbed dorsal spines, on the whiplike tail, capable of
inflicting severe wounds. Some species reach a large size, and some,
esp., on the American Pacific coast, are very destructive to
oysters.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingtail</ent><br/
<hw>Sting"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A sting ray.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingy</ent><br/
<hw>Sting"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stinging; able to
sting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stingy</ent><br/
<hw>Stin"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stingier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stingiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Probably from <ets>sting</ets>,
and meaning originally, stinging; hence, biting, nipping (of the wind),
churlish, avaricious; or cf. E. <ets>skinch</ets>.]</ety> <def>Extremely
close and covetous; meanly avaricious; niggardly; miserly; penurious;
<as>as, a <ex>stingy</ex> churl</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>stingy</qex>, narrow-hearted fellow that had a deal of
choice fruit, had not the heart to touch it till it began to be
rotten.</q> <rj><qau>L'estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stink</ent><br/
<hw>Stink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stunk</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, <conjf>Stank</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stinking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>stinkan</ets> to have
a smell (whether good or bad); akin to OHG. <ets>stinchan</ets>, G. & D.
<ets>stinken</ets> to stink; of uncertain origin; cf. Icel.
<ets>st<oum/kkva</ets> to leap, to spring, Goth. <ets>stigqan</ets> to
push, strike, or Gr. <?/ rancid. Cf. <er>Stench</er>.]</ety> <def>To
emit a strong, offensive smell; to send out a disgusting odor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stink</ent><br/
<hw>Stink</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to stink; to affect by a
stink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stink</ent><br/
<hw>Stink</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stinc</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
strong, offensive smell; a disgusting odor; a stench.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Fire stink</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Fire</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Stink-fire lance</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Lance</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stink rat</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the musk turtle.</cd> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stink
shad</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the gizzard shad.</cd>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stink trap</b></col>, <cd>a stench
trap. See under <er>Stench</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinkard</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"ard</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A mean,
stinking, paltry fellow.</def> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The teledu of the East Indies. It
emits a disagreeable odor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinkball</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"ball`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A composition of
substances which in combustion emit a suffocating odor; -- used formerly
in naval warfare.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinker</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, stinks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of the several species of
large antarctic petrels which feed on blubber and carrion and have an
offensive odor, as the giant fulmar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinkhorn</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"horn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A kind of fungus of the genus <gen>Phallus</gen>, which emits a
fetid odor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinking</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Stink</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stinking badger</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
teledu.</cd> -- <col><b>Stinking cedar</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>the California nutmeg tree; also, a related tree of Florida
(<spn>Torreya taxifolia</spn>).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinkingly</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stinking manner; with
an offensive smell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinkpot</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"pot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
earthen jar charged with powder, grenades, and other materials of an
offensive and suffocating smell, -- sometimes used in boarding an
enemy's vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A vessel in which disinfectants are
burned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The musk turtle, or musk
tortoise. See under <er>Musk</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinkstone</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>One of the varieties of calcite, barite, and feldspar, which emit a
fetid odor on being struck; -- called also
<altname>swinestone</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinkweed</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Stramonium. See <er>Jamestown weed</er>, and
<er>Datura</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1416 --></p>

<p><ent>Stinkwood</ent><br/
<hw>Stink"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A name given to several kinds of wood with an unpleasant smell, as
that of the <spn>Foetidia Mauritiana</spn> of the Mauritius, and that of
the South African <spn>Ocotea bullata</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stint</ent><br/
<hw>Stint</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of several species of small sandpipers, as the
sanderling of Europe and America, the dunlin, the little stint of India
(<spn>Tringa minuta</spn>), etc. Called also
<altname>pume</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A phalarope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stint</ent><br/
<hw>Stint</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stinted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stinting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stinten</ets>,
<ets>stenten</ets>, <ets>stunten</ets>, to cause to cease, AS.
<ets>styntan</ets> (in comp.) to blunt, dull, fr. <ets>stunt</ets> dull,
stupid; akin to Icel. <ets>stytta</ets> to shorten, <ets>stuttr</ets>
short, dial, Sw. <ets>stynta</ets> to shorten, <ets>stunt</ets> short.
Cf. <er>Stent</er>, <er>Stunt</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To restrain
within certain limits; to bound; to confine; to restrain; to restrict to
a scant allowance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I shall not go about to extenuate the latitude of the curse upon
the earth, or <qex>stint</qex> it only to the production of weeds.</q>
<rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She <qex>stints</qex> them in their meals.</q>
<rj><qau>Law.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put an end to; to stop.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To assign a certain (<it>i. e.</it>, limited) task
to (a person), upon the performance of which one is excused from further
labor for the day or for a certain time; to stent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To serve successfully; to get with foal; -- said of
mares.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The majority of maiden mares will become <qex>stinted</qex> while
at work.</q> <rj><qau>J. H. Walsh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stint</ent><br/
<hw>Stint</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To stop; to cease.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They can not <qex>stint</qex> till no thing be left.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>stint</qex> thou too, I pray thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The damsel <qex>stinted</qex> in her song.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. to be parsimonious in expending some resource; -- used with
"on" to indicate the item conserved. "Don't stint on the potatos!"
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stint</ent><br/
<hw>Stint</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Also written <ets>stent</ets>. See
<er>Stint</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Limit; bound;
restraint; extent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God has wrote upon no created thing the utmost <qex>stint</qex> of
his power.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Quantity or task assigned; proportion
allotted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His old <qex>stint</qex> -- three thousand pounds a year.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 3. A period of work at a specific task; as, to do one's stint in
the army, an actor who did a stint as a cab driver. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stintance</ent><br/
<hw>Stint"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Restraint;
stoppage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stintedness</ent><br/
<hw>Stint"ed*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
stinted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stinter</ent><br/
<hw>Stint"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, stints.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stintless</ent><br/
<hw>Stint"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without stint or
restraint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>stintless</qex>tears of old Heraclitus.</q>
<rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipe</ent><br/
<hw>Stipe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stipes</ets> a
stock, post, branch: cf. F. <ets>stipe</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The stalk or petiole of a frond, as of a fern.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The stalk of a pistil.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
trunk of a tree.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The stem of a fungus or
mushroom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipel</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"pel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stipule</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The stipule of a
leaflet.</def> <rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipellate</ent><br/
<hw>Sti*pel"late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having stipels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipend</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"pend</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stipendium</ets>; <ets>stips</ets>, gen. <ets>stipis</ets>, a gift,
donation, given in small coin + <ets>pendere</ets> to weigh or pay
out.]</ety> <def>Settled pay or compensation for services, whether paid
daily, monthly, or annually.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipend</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"pend</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pay by settled wages.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipendiarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sti*pen`di*a"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Acting from
mercenary considerations; stipendiary.</def> <rj><au>A.
Seward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipendiary</ent><br/
<hw>Sti*pen"di*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stipendiarius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>stipendiaire</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Receiving wages, or salary; performing services for a stated price
or compensation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His great <qex>stipendiary</qex> prelates came with troops of
evil-appointed horseman not half full.</q>
<rj><qau>Knolles.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipendiary</ent><br/
<hw>Sti*pen"di*a*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stipendiaries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who receives a
stipend.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If thou art become<br/
A tyrant's vile <qex>stipendiary</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Glover.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipendiate</ent><br/
<hw>Sti*pen"di*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stipendiatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>stipendiari</ets> to receive
pay.]</ety> <def>To provide with a stipend, or salary; to support; to
pay.</def> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is good to endow colleges, and to found chairs, and to
<qex>stipendiate</qex> professors.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipendless</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"pend*less</hw> <pr>(st<imac/"p<ecr/nd*l<ecr/s)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no stipend.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sti"pes</hw> <pr>(-p<emac/z)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stipites</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a
stock.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The second joint of a
maxilla of an insect or a crustacean.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An
eyestalk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipitate</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>stipitatus</ets>, from L. <ets>stipes</ets>, gen.
<ets>stipitis</ets>, a stock. See <er>Stipe</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Supported by a stipe; elevated on a stipe, as the
fronds of most ferns, or the pod of certain cruciferous
plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipitiform</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"i*ti*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stipes</ets> + <ets>-form</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having the shape of a stalk; stalklike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipple</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stippled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stippling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[D.
<ets>stippelen</ets> to make points, to spot, dot, from
<ets>stippel</ets>, dim. of <ets>stip</ets> a dot, spot.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To engrave by means of dots, in distinction from
engraving in lines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The interlaying of small pieces can not altogether avoid a broken,
<qex>stippled</qex>, spotty effect.</q> <rj><qau>Milman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To paint, as in water colors, by small, short
touches which together produce an even or softly graded
surface.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stippling</ent><br/
<ent>Stipple</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stip"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stip"pling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Engraving)</fld> <def>A mode of execution
which produces the effect by dots or small points instead of
lines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>A mode of execution in which a
flat or even tint is produced by many small touches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiptic</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Styptic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stip"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Stipulas</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Stipulae</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a stalk, stem.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A stipule.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A newly sprouted
feather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipular</ent><br/
<ent>Stipulaceous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stip`u*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stip"u*lar</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stipulac<eacute/</ets>, <ets>stipulaire</ets>. See
<er>Stipula</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
stipules; resembling stipules; furnished with stipules; growing on
stipules, or close to them; occupying the position of stipules; <as>as,
<ex>stipular</ex> glands and <ex>stipular</ex> tendrils</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipulary</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"u*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to stipules; stipular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipulate</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Furnished with stipules; <as>as, a <ex>stipulate</ex>
leaf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipulate</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stipulated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stipulating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>stipulatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>stipulari</ets> to stipulate, fr.
OL. <ets>stipulus</ets> firm, fast; probably akin to L.
<ets>stipes</ets> a post. Cf. <er>Stiff</er>.]</ety> <def>To make an
agreement or covenant with any person or company to do or forbear
anything; to bargain; to contract; to settle terms; <as>as, certain
princes <ex>stipulated</ex> to assist each other in resisting the armies
of France</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipulation</ent><br/
<hw>Stip`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stipulatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>stipulation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of stipulating; a contracting or bargaining; an
agreement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is stipulated, or agreed upon; that which
is definitely arranged or contracted; an agreement; a covenant; a
contract or bargain; also, any particular article, item, or condition,
in a mutual agreement; <as>as, the <ex>stipulations</ex> of the allied
powers to furnish each his contingent of troops</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A material article of an agreement;
an undertaking in the nature of bail taken in the admiralty courts; a
bargain.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier. Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Agreement; contract; engagement. See
<er>Covenant</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipulation</ent><br/
<hw>Stip`u*la"tion</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stipule</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The situation,
arrangement, and structure of the stipules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipulator</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"u*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who stipulates, contracts, or covenants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipule</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"ule</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stipula</ets> a stalk, stem, straw: cf. F. <ets>stipule</ets>. Cf.
<er>Stubble</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An appendage at the base
of petioles or leaves, usually somewhat resembling a small leaf in
texture and appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stipuled</ent><br/
<hw>Stip"uled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Furnished with stipules, or leafy appendages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stir</ent><br/
<hw>Stir</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stirred</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stirring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stiren</ets>,
<ets>steren</ets>, <ets>sturen</ets>, AS. <ets>styrian</ets>; probably
akin to D. <ets>storen</ets> to disturb, G. <ets>st<oum/ren</ets>, OHG.
<ets>st<omac/ren</ets> to scatter, destroy. <root/166.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To change the place of in any manner; to
move.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My foot I had never yet in five days been able to
<qex>stir</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To disturb the relative position of the particles
of, as of a liquid, by passing something through it; to agitate; <as>as,
to <ex>stir</ex> a pudding with a spoon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My mind is troubled, like a fountain <qex>stirred</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To bring into debate; to agitate; to moot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stir</qex> not questions of jurisdiction.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To incite to action; to arouse; to instigate; to
prompt; to excite.</def> <ldquo/To <xex>stir</xex> men to
devotion.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An Ate, <qex>stirring</qex> him to blood and strife.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And for her sake some mutiny will <qex>stir</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In all senses except the first, <xex>stir</xex> is often
followed by <xex>up</xex> with an intensive effect; as, to
<xex>stir</xex> up fire; to <xex>stir</xex> up sedition.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To move; incite; awaken; rouse; animate;
stimulate; excite; provoke.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stir</ent><br/
<hw>Stir</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To move; to change
one's position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had not power to <qex>stir</qex> or strive,<br/
But felt that I was still alive.</q> <rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be in motion; to be active or bustling; to exert
or busy one's self.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All are not fit with them to <qex>stir</qex> and toil.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The friends of the unfortunate exile, far from resenting his
unjust suspicions, were <qex>stirring</qex> anxiously in his behalf.</q>
<rj><qau>Merivale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To become the object of notice; to be on
foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They fancy they have a right to talk freely upon everything that
<qex>stirs</qex> or appears.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To rise, or be up, in the morning.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stir</ent><br/
<hw>Stir</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or result of
stirring; agitation; tumult; bustle; noise or various
movements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why all these words, this clamor, and this <qex>stir</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Consider, after so much <qex>stir</qex> about genus and species,
how few words we have yet settled definitions of.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Public disturbance or commotion; tumultuous
disorder; seditious uproar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Being advertised of some <qex>stirs</qex> raised by his unnatural
sons in England.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J. Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Agitation of thoughts; conflicting
passions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirabout</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"a*bout`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dish formed of
oatmeal boiled in water to a certain consistency and frequently stirred,
or of oatmeal and dripping mixed together and stirred about in a pan; a
hasty pudding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiriated</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"i*a`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stiria</ets> an icicle.]</ety> <def>Adorned with pendants like
icicles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirious</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stiria an
icicle</ets>.]</ety> <def>Resembling icicles.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirk</ent><br/
<hw>Stirk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stric</ets>,
from <ets>ste<oacute/r</ets> a steer. See <er>Steer</er> a young
ox.]</ety> <def>A young bullock or heifer.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirless</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without stirring;
very quiet; motionless.</def> <ldquo/Lying helpless and
<xex>stirless</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hare.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirp</ent><br/
<hw>Stirp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stirps</ets>,
<ets>stirpis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Stock; race; family.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirpiculture</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"pi*cul`ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stirps</ets>, <ets>stirpis</ets>, stem, stock, race +
<ets>cultura</ets> culture.]</ety> <def>The breeding of special stocks
or races.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirps</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stirps</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stirpes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., stem, stock.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Stock; race; family.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A race, or a fixed and permanent
variety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirrage</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"rage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of stirring;
stir; commotion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>T.
Granger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirrer</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"rer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, stirs something; also, one who moves about, especially after
sleep; <as>as, an early <ex>stirrer</ex></as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stirrer up</b></col>, <cd>an instigator or inciter.</cd>
<au>Atterbury.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirring</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"ring</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Putting in motion,
or being in motion; active; active in business; habitually employed in
some kind of business; accustomed to a busy life.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A more <qex>stirring</qex> and intellectual age than any which had
gone before it.</q> <rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Animating; arousing; awakening; stimulating;
quickening; exciting.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirrup</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"rup</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stirop</ets>, AS. <ets>stigr<amac/p</ets>; <ets>st<imac/gan</ets>
to mount, ascend + <ets>r<amac/p</ets> a rope; akin to G.
<ets>stegreif</ets> a stirrup. <root/164. See <er>Sty</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>, and <er>Rope</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of ring, or
bent piece of metal, wood, leather, or the like, horizontal in one part
for receiving the foot of a rider, and attached by a strap to the
saddle, -- used to assist a person in mounting a horse, and to enable
him to sit steadily in riding, as well as to relieve him by supporting a
part of the weight of the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our host upon his <qex>stirpoes</qex> stood anon.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Carp. & Mach.)</fld> <def>Any piece resembling in
shape the stirrup of a saddle, and used as a support, clamp, etc. See
<er>Bridle iron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A rope secured to a yard, with a
thimble in its lower end for supporting a footrope.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stirrup bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
stapes.</cd> -- <col><b>Stirrup cup</b></col>, <cd>a parting cup taken
after mounting.</cd> -- <col><b>Stirrup iron</b></col>, <cd>an iron
stirrup.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Stirrup leather</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Stirrup strap</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the strap which attaches a
stirrup to the saddle. See <er>Stirrup</er>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirt</ent><br/
<hw>Stirt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>p. p.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Start</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos></mord> <def>Started; leaped.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They privily be <qex>stirt</qex> into a well.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stirte</ent><br/
<hw>Stir"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Start</er>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos></def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stitch</ent><br/
<hw>Stitch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stiche</ets>,
AS. <ets>stice</ets> a pricking, akin to <ets>stician</ets> to prick.
See <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A single
pass of a needle in sewing; the loop or turn of the thread thus
made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A single turn of the thread round a needle in
knitting; a link, or loop, of yarn; <as>as, to let down, or drop, a
<ex>stitch</ex>; to take up a <ex>stitch</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Cf. OE. <ets>sticche</ets>, <ets>stecche</ets>,
<ets>stucche</ets>, a piece, AS. <ets>stycce</ets>. Cf.
<er>Stock</er>.]</ety> <def>A space of work taken up, or gone over, in a
single pass of the needle; hence, by extension, any space passed over;
distance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have gone a good <qex>stitch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In Syria the husbandmen go lightly over with their plow, and take
no deep <qex>stitch</qex> in making their furrows.</q>
<rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A local sharp pain; an acute pain, like the piercing
of a needle; <as>as, a <ex>stitch</ex> in the side</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was taken with a cold and with <qex>stitches</qex>, which was,
indeed, a pleurisy.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A contortion, or twist.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If you talk,<br/
Or pull your face into a <qex>stitch</qex> again,<br/
I shall be angry.</q> <rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Any least part of a fabric or dress; <as>as, to wet
every <ex>stitch</ex> of clothes</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A furrow.</def> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>An arrangement of stitches, or method of stitching
in some particular way or style; <as>as, cross-<ex>stitch</ex>;
herringbone <ex>stitch</ex>, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Chain stitch</b></col>, <col><b>Lock
stitch</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See in the Vocabulary.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Pearl stitch</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Purl
stitch</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See 2nd <er>Purl</er>, 2.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stitch</ent><br/
<hw>Stitch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stitched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stitching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To form stitches
in; especially, to sew in such a manner as to show on the surface a
continuous line of stitches; <as>as, to <ex>stitch</ex> a shirt
bosom</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sew, or unite together by stitches; <as>as, to
<ex>stitch</ex> printed sheets in making a book or a
pamphlet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>To form land into
ridges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stitch up</b></col>, <cd>to mend or unite with a
needle and thread; <as>as, <ex>to stitch up</ex> a rent; <ex>to stitch
up</ex> an artery</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stitch</ent><br/
<hw>Stitch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To practice stitching, or
needlework.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stitchel</ent><br/
<hw>Stitch"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of hairy
wool.</def> <mark>[Prov.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stitcher</ent><br/
<hw>Stitch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who stitches; a
seamstress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stitchery</ent><br/
<hw>Stitch"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Needlework; -- in
contempt.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stitching</ent><br/
<hw>Stitch"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of one who
stitches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Work done by sewing, esp. when a continuous line of
stitches is shown on the surface; stitches, collectively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stitchwort</ent><br/
<hw>Stitch"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Stichwort</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stith</ent><br/
<hw>Stith</hw> <pr>(st<icr/th)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>st<imac/<edh/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Strong; stiff; rigid.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stith</ent><br/
<hw>Stith</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel. <ets>ste<edh/i</ets> an anvil,
akin to <ets>sta<edh/r</ets> place. See <er>Stead</er>.]</ety> <def>An
anvil; a stithy.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He invented also pincers, hammers, iron crows, and the anvil, or
<qex>stith</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Holland.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stithy</ent><br/
<hw>Stith"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stith</er>,
and cf. <er>Stiddy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An anvil.</def>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A smith's shop; a smithy; a smithery; a forge.</def>
<ldquo/As foul as Vulcan's <xex>stithy</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stithy</ent><br/
<hw>Stith"y</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To forge on an anvil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The forge that <qex>stithied</qex> Mars his helm.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stive</ent><br/
<hw>Stive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stived</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stiving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably fr. F.
<ets>estiver</ets> to compress, stow, L. <ets>stipare</ets>: cf. It.
<ets>stivare</ets>, Sp. <ets>estivar</ets>. Cf. <er>Stevedore</er>,
<er>Stiff</er>.]</ety> <def>To stuff; to crowd; to fill full; hence, to
make hot and close; to render stifling.</def>
<rj><au>Sandys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His chamber was commonly <qex>stived</qex> with friends or suitors
of one kind or other.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H. Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stive</ent><br/
<hw>Stive</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be stifled or
suffocated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stive</ent><br/
<hw>Stive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The floating dust in flour mills
caused by the operation or grinding.</def> <rj><au>De
Colange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stiver</ent><br/
<hw>Sti"ver</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>stuiver</ets>; akin to G. <ets>st<uum/ber</ets>, Dan.
<ets>styver</ets>, Sw. <ets>styfver</ets>.]</ety> <def>A Dutch coin, and
money of account, of the value of two cents, or about one penny
sterling; hence, figuratively, anything of little worth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stives</ent><br/
<hw>Stives</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[OE. See
<er>Stew</er>.]</ety> <def>Stews; a brothel.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoak</ent><br/
<hw>Stoak</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>stocken</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To stop; to
choke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoat</ent><br/
<hw>Stoat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stot</ets> a
stoat, horse, bullock; perhaps originally only of male animals, and akin
to D. <ets>stooten</ets> to push, E. <ets>stutter</ets>; cf. Icel.
<ets>st<?/tr</ets> a bull, Sw. <ets>stut</ets> a bullock. Cf.
<er>Stot</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The ermine in its summer
pelage, when it is reddish brown, but with a black tip to the tail. The
name is sometimes applied also to other brown weasels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stocah</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"cah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ir. & Gael.
<ets>stocach</ets> an idle fellow who lives on the industry of others, a
lounger.]</ety> <def>A menial attendant.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoccade</ent><br/
<hw>Stoc*cade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Stockade</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoccado</ent><br/
<hw>Stoc*ca"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>estocade</ets>, fr. Sp. <ets>estocada</ets>, or It.
<ets>stoccata</ets>, from Sp. <ets>estoque</ets>, or It.
<ets>stocco</ets>, a rapier, fr. G. <ets>stock</ets> a stick. See
<er>Stock</er>.]</ety> <def>A stab; a thrust with a rapier.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1417 --></p>

<p><ent>Stochastic</ent><br/
<hw>Sto*chas"tic</hw> <pr>(st<osl/*k<acr/s"t<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>stochastiko`s</grk>, from <grk>stocha`zesqai</grk> to
aim, to guess, fr. <grk>sto`chos</grk> mark or aim.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Conjectural; able to conjecture.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Whitefoot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>random; chance; involving probability; opposite of
<ant>deterministic</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>GG</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Statistics)</fld> <def>of or pertaining to a
process in which a series of calculations, selections, or observations
are made, each one being randomly determined as a sample from a
probability distribution.</def> <note>Where physical phenomena are
modelled as a stochastic process, each subsequent calculation of a
series may depend on the result of the previous calculation, as in the
modelling of the process of diffusion of molecules. Many series may be
calculated, and the results averaged, to estimate the most likely
result. See also <er>Markov chain</er>.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>sto*chas"tic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stock</ent><br/
<hw>Stock</hw> <pr>(st<ocr/k)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stocc</ets> a stock, trunk, stick; akin to D. <ets>stok</ets>, G.
<ets>stock</ets>, OHG. <ets>stoc</ets>, Icel. <ets>stokkr</ets>, Sw.
<ets>stock</ets>, Dan. <ets>stok</ets>, and AS. <ets>stycce</ets> a
piece; cf. Skr. <ets>tuj</ets> to urge, thrust. Cf. <er>Stokker</er>,
<er>Stucco</er>, and <er>Tuck</er> a rapier.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
stem, or main body, of a tree or plant; the fixed, strong, firm part;
the trunk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though the root thereof wax old in the earth, and the
<qex>stock</qex> thereof die in the ground, yet through the scent of
water it will bud, and bring forth boughs like a plant.</q> <rj><qau>Job
xiv. 8,9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The stem or branch in which a graft is
inserted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The scion overruleth the <qex>stock</qex> quite.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A block of wood; something fixed and solid; a
pillar; a firm support; a post.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All our fathers worshiped <qex>stocks</qex> and stones.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Item, for a <qex>stock</qex> of brass for the holy water, seven
shillings; which, by the canon, must be of marble or metal, and in no
case of brick.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Hence, a person who is as dull and lifeless as a
stock or post; one who has little sense.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let's be no stoics, nor no <qex>stocks</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The principal supporting part; the part in which
others are inserted, or to which they are attached.</def> Specifically:
--<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>The wood to which the barrel, lock, etc., of a
rifle or like firearm are secured; also, a long, rectangular piece of
wood, which is an important part of several forms of gun
carriage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The handle or contrivance by which bits are held in
boring; a bitstock; a brace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Joinery)</fld> <def>The block of wood or metal
frame which constitutes the body of a plane, and in which the plane iron
is fitted; a plane stock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The wooden or iron crosspiece to
which the shank of an anchor is attached. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Anchor</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <def>The support of the block in which an anvil is
fixed, or of the anvil itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(f)</sd> <def>A handle or wrench forming a holder for the dies
for cutting screws; a diestock.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(g)</sd> <def>The part of a tally formerly struck in the
exchequer, which was delivered to the person who had lent the king money
on account, as the evidence of indebtedness. See
<er>Counterfoil</er>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The original progenitor; also, the race or line of a
family; the progenitor of a family and his direct descendants; lineage;
family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And stand betwixt them made, when, severally,<br/
All told their <qex>stock</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy mother was no goddess, nor thy <qex>stock</qex><br/
From Dardanus.</q> <rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Finance)</fld> <def>Money or capital which an
individual or a firm employs in business; fund; in the United States,
the capital of a bank or other company, in the form of transferable
shares, each of a certain amount; money funded in government securities,
called also <altname>the public funds</altname>; in the plural, property
consisting of shares in joint-stock companies, or in the obligations of
a government for its funded debt; -- so in the United States, but in
England the latter only are called <altname>stocks</altname>, and the
former <altname>shares</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Bookkeeping)</fld> <def>Same as <cref>Stock
account</cref>, below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Supply provided; store; accumulation; especially, a
merchant's or manufacturer's store of goods; <as>as, to lay in a
<ex>stock</ex> of provisions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Add to that <qex>stock</qex> which justly we bestow.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>Domestic animals or beasts
collectively, used or raised on a farm; <as>as, a <ex>stock</ex> of
cattle or of sheep, etc.</as>; -- called also <altname>live
stock</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>That portion of a pack of
cards not distributed to the players at the beginning of certain games,
as gleek, etc., but which might be drawn from afterward as occasion
required; a bank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I must buy the <qex>stock</qex>; send me good cardings.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>A thrust with a rapier; a stoccado.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <ety>[Cf. <er>Stocking</er>.]</ety> <def>A covering for
the leg, or leg and foot; <as>as, upper <ex>stocks</ex> (breeches);
nether <ex>stocks</ex> (stockings).</as></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a linen <qex>stock</qex> on one leg.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>A kind of stiff, wide band or cravat for the neck;
<as>as, a silk <ex>stock</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A frame of timber, with holes in
which the feet, or the feet and hands, of criminals were formerly
confined by way of punishment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He shall rest in my <qex>stocks</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Piers
Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>The
frame or timbers on which a ship rests while building.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>17.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Red and gray bricks, used for the
exterior of walls and the front of buildings.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>18.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any cruciferous plant of the
genus <gen>Matthiola</gen>; <as>as, common <ex>stock</ex>
(<spn>Matthiola incana</spn>) (see <er>Gilly-flower</er>); ten-weeks
<ex>stock</ex> (<spn>Matthiola annua</spn>)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>19.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>An irregular metalliferous mass
filling a large cavity in a rock formation, as a <xex>stock</xex> of
lead ore deposited in limestone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>20.</sn> <def>A race or variety in a species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>21.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>In tectology, an aggregate or
colony of persons (see <er>Person</er>), as trees, chains of salpae,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>22.</sn> <def>The beater of a fulling mill.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>23.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>A liquid or jelly containing
the juices and soluble parts of meat, and certain vegetables, etc.,
extracted by cooking; -- used in making soup, gravy, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>24.</sn> <def>Raw material; that out of which something is
manufactured; <as>as, paper <ex>stock</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>25.</sn> <fld>(Soap Making)</fld> <def>A plain soap which is made
into toilet soap by adding perfumery, coloring matter, etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Bit stock</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Bitstock</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Dead stock</b></col> <fld>(Agric.)</fld>, <cd>the implements of
husbandry, and produce stored up for use; -- in distinction from
<xex>live stock</xex>, or the domestic animals on the farm. See def. 10,
above.</cd> -- <col><b>Head stock</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Headstock</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Paper stock</b></col>, <cd>rags and
other material of which paper is made.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock
account</b></col> <fld>(Bookkeeping)</fld>, <cd>an account on a
merchant's ledger, one side of which shows the original capital, or
stock, and the additions thereto by accumulation or contribution, the
other side showing the amounts withdrawn.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock
car</b></col>, <cd>a railway car for carrying cattle.</cd> --
<col><b>Stock company</b></col> <fld>(Com.)</fld>, <cd>an incorporated
company the capital of which is represented by marketable shares having
a certain equal par value.</cd><-- also, joint-stock company --> --
<col><b>Stock duck</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the mallard.</cd>
-- <col><b>Stock exchange</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The building or
place where stocks are bought and sold; stock market; hence,
transactions of all kinds in stocks</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An
association or body of stockbrokers who meet and transact business by
certain recognized forms, regulations, and usages</cd>. <au>Wharton.
Brande & C.</au> -- <col><b>Stock farmer</b></col>, <cd>a farmer who
makes it his business to rear live stock.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock
gillyflower</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the common stock. See
<er>Stock</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 18.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock gold</b></col>,
<cd>gold laid up so as to form a stock, or hoard.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock
in trade</b></col>, <cd>the goods kept for sale by a shopkeeper; the
fittings and appliances of a workman.</cd> <au>Simmonds.</au> --
<col><b>Stock list</b></col>, <cd>a list of stocks, or shares, dealt in,
of transactions, and of prices.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock lock</b></col>,
<cd>a lock inclosed in a wooden case and attached to the face of a
door.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock market</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A place
where stocks are bought and sold; the stock exchange</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>A market for live stock.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock pigeon</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <er>Stockdove</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock
purse</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A common purse, as distinguished from
a private purse.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <cd>Moneys saved
out of the expenses of a company or regiment, and applied to objects of
common interest</cd>. <mark>[Eng.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stock
shave</b></col>, <cd>a tool used by blockmakers.</cd> -- <col><b>Stock
station</b></col>, <cd>a place or district for rearing stock.</cd>
<mark>[Australia]</mark> <au>W. Howitt.</au> -- <col><b>Stock
tackle</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a tackle used when the anchor
is hoisted and secured, to keep its stock clear of the ship's
sides.</cd> <au>Totten.</au> -- <col><b>Stock taking</b></col>, <cd>an
examination and inventory made of goods or stock in a shop or warehouse;
-- usually made periodically.</cd> -- <col><b>Tail stock</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Tailstock</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To have something on the
stock</b></col>, <cd>to be at work at something.</cd> -- <col><b>To take
stock</b></col>, <cd>to take account of stock; to make an inventory of
stock or goods on hand.</cd> <au>Dickens.</au> -- <col><b>To take stock
in</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To subscribe for, or purchase, shares in
a stock company.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To put faith in; to accept as
trustworthy; <as>as, <ex>to take stock in</ex> a person's
fidelity</as></cd>. <mark>[Slang]</mark> -- <col><b>To take stock
of</b></col>, <cd>to take account of the stock of; to take an inventory
of; hence, to ascertain the facts in regard to (something).</cd>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At the outset of any inquiry it is proper <qex>to take stock
of</qex> the results obtained by previous explorers of the same
field.</q> <rj><qau>Leslie Stephen.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Fund; capital; store; supply; accumulation;
hoard; provision.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stock</ent><br/
<hw>Stock</hw> <pr>(st<ocr/k)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stocked</conjf> <pr>(st<ocr/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Stocking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay
up; to put aside for future use; to store, as merchandise, and the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To provide with material requisites; to store; to
fill; to supply; <as>as, to <ex>stock</ex> a warehouse, that is, to fill
it with goods; to <ex>stock</ex> a farm, that is, to supply it with
cattle and tools; to <ex>stock</ex> land, that is, to occupy it with a
permanent growth, especially of grass.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To suffer to retain milk for twenty-four hours or
more previous to sale, as cows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To put in the stocks.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stock an anchor</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>to
fit it with a stock, or to fasten the stock firmly in place.</cd> --
<col><b>To stock cards</b></col> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld>, <cd>to
arrange cards in a certain manner for cheating purposes; -- also called
<altname>to stack the deck</altname>.</cd> <mark>[Cant]</mark> --
<col><b>To stock down</b></col> <fld>(Agric.)</fld>, <cd>to sow, as
plowed land, with grass seed, in order that it may become swarded, and
produce grass.</cd> -- <col><b>To stock up</b></col>, <cd>to extirpate;
to dig up.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stock</ent><br/
<hw>Stock</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Used or employed for constant service
or application, as if constituting a portion of a stock or supply;
standard; permanent; standing; <as>as, a <ex>stock</ex> actor; a
<ex>stock</ex> play; a <ex>stock</ex> phrase; a <ex>stock</ex> response;
a <ex>stock</ex> sermon.</as></def> <ldquo/A <xex>stock</xex> charge
against Raleigh.<rdquo/ <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stock company</b></col> <fld>(Theater)</fld>, <cd>a
company of actors regularly employed at one theater, or permanently
acting together in various plays under one management.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockade</ent><br/
<hw>Stock*ade"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>estacade</ets> stockade, boom (confused in French with
<ets>estocade</ets>; see 1st <er>Stoccado</er>); fr. It.
<ets>steccata</ets> a palisade (influenced by OF. <ets>estach</ets>,
<ets>estaque</ets>, a stake, post), or from Sp. <ets>estacada</ets> a
palisade; both of German origin, and akin to E. <ets>stake</ets>,
<ets>stick</ets>; cf. G. <ets>stecken</ets> stick, OHG.
<ets>steccho</ets>. See <er>Stake</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Stick</er>,
<pos>n.</pos> & <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Estacade</er>,
<er>Stacket</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A line of
stout posts or timbers set firmly in the earth in contact with each
other (and usually with loopholes) to form a barrier, or defensive
fortification.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stoccade</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An inclosure, or pen, made with posts and
stakes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockade</ent><br/
<hw>Stock*ade"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stockaded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stockading</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To surround, fortify, or
protect with a stockade.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stock-blind</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"-blind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Blind as a
stock; wholly blind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockbroker</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"bro`ker</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A broker who
deals in stocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockdove</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"dove`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A common European wild pigeon (<spn>Columba aenas</spn>), so called
because at one time believed to be the stock of the domestic pigeon, or,
according to some, from its breeding in the stocks, or trunks, of
trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is applied, also, to other related species, as
the Indian <xex>stockdove</xex> (<spn>Palumbaena
Eversmanni</spn>).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stocker</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes or fits
stocks, as of guns or gun carriages, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockfish</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>stokvisch</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Salted and dried fish,
especially codfish, hake, ling, and torsk; also, codfish dried without
being salted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Young fresh cod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockholder</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"hold`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is a
holder or proprietor of stock in the public funds, or in the funds of a
bank or other stock company.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockinet</ent><br/
<hw>Stock`i*net"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An elastic
textile fabric imitating knitting, of which stockings, under-garments,
etc., are made.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stocking</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Stock</er>, which was formerly used of a covering for the legs and
feet, combining breeches, or upper stocks, and <ets>stockings</ets>, or
nether stocks.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A close-fitting covering for the
foot and leg, usually knit or woven.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any of various things resembling, or likened to, a
stocking{1};</def> <specif>as:</specif> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A broad ring
of color, differing from the general color, on the lower part of the leg
of a quadruped; esp., a white ring between the coronet and the hock or
knee of a dark-colored horse.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A knitted hood of
cotton thread which is eventually converted by a special process into an
incandescent mantle for gas lighting.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Blue stocking</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Bluestocking</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stocking frame</b></col>, <cd>a
machine for knitting stockings or other hosiery goods.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stocking</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"ing</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To dress in GBs.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockinger</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"ing*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stocking
weaver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockish</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a stock; stupid;
blockish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Since naught so <qex>stockish</qex>, hard, and full of rage,<br/
But music for the time doth change his nature.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockjobber</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"job`ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stock</ets> + <ets>job</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who speculates
in stocks for gain; one whose occupation is to buy and sell stocks. In
England a jobber acts as an intermediary between brokers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockjobbing</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"job`bing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or art
of dealing in stocks; the business of a stockjobber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockman</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stockmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A herdsman; a ranchman;
one owning, or having charge of, herds of live stock.</def>
<mark>[Australia & U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>W. Howitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stock-still</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"-still`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[CF. G.
<ets>stock-still</ets>.]</ety> <def>Still as a stock, or fixed post;
perfectly still.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His whole work stands <qex>stock-still</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sterne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stockwork</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>stockwerk</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A
system of working in ore, etc., when it lies not in strata or veins, but
in solid masses, so as to be worked in chambers or stories.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A metalliferous deposit
characterized by the impregnation of the mass of rock with many small
veins or nests irregularly grouped. This kind of deposit is especially
common with tin ore. Such deposits are worked in floors or
stories.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stocky</ent><br/
<hw>Stock"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Stock</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Short and thick; thick rather
than tall or corpulent.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stocky</qex>, twisted, hunchback stems.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs. H.
H. Jackson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Headstrong.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stodgy</ent><br/
<hw>Stodg"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wet.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>G. Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoechiometry</ent><br/
<ent>Stoechiology</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Stoech`i*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>,
<hw>Stoech`i*om"e*try</hw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, etc.</mhw>
<def>See <er>Stoichiology</er>, <er>Stoichiometry</er>, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stogie</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"gie</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stogies</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr></plu>. <def>an inexpensive cigar; same as
<er>stogy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stogy</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Etym. uncertain. Cf.
<er>Stocky</er>.]</ety> <def>heavy; coarse; clumsy.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stogy</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"gy</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stogies</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr></plu>. <altsp>[Written also <asp>stogie</asp>.]</altsp>
<ety>[<ets>Colloq</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A stout, coarse boot or
shoe; a brogan.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of cheap, but not necessary inferior, cigar
made in the form of a cylindrical roll.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoic</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stoicus</ets>,
Gr. <?/, fr. <?/, adj., literally, of or pertaining to a colonnade, from
<?/ a roofed colonnade, a porch, especially, a porch in Athens where
Zeno and his successors taught.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A disciple of
the philosopher Zeno; one of a Greek sect which held that men should be
free from passion, unmoved by joy or grief, and should submit without
complaint to unavoidable necessity, by which all things are
governed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, a person not easily excited; an apathetic
person; one who is apparently or professedly indifferent to pleasure or
pain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>Stoic</qex> of the woods, a man without a tear.</q>
<rj><qau>Campbell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>School of Stoics</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>The
Porch</cref>, under <er>Porch</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoical</ent><br/
<ent>Stoic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sto"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sto"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stoicus</ets>, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>sto<ium/que</ets>. See <er>Stoic</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the Stoics; resembling the Stoics
or their doctrines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not affected by passion; manifesting indifference to
pleasure or pain; especially, bearing pain, suffering, or bad fortune
without complaint.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sto"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Sto"ic*al*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoichiological</ent><br/
<hw>Stoi`chi*o*log"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to stoichiology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoichiology</ent><br/
<hw>Stoi`chi*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
first element + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stoechiology</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That part of the
science of physiology which treats of the elements, or principles,
composing animal tissues.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>The doctrine of the elementary
requisites of mere thought.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The statement or discussion of the first principles
of any science or art.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoichiometrical</ent><br/
<ent>Stoichiometric</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stoi`chi*o*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Stoi`chi*o*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Of or pertaining to stoichiometry; employed in, or obtained by,
stoichiometry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>of, pertaining to, using, or
consuming, reagents in the exact proportions required for a given
reaction; <as>as, a <ex>stoichiometric</ex> reaction, <it>i. e.</it>, a
reaction which goes to completion, rather than stopping partway at an
equilibrium point</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoichiometry</ent><br/
<hw>Stoi`chi*om"e*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
first principle, or element + <ets>-metry</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art or
process of calculating the atomic proportions, combining weights, and
other numerical relations of chemical elements and their
compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoicism</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"i*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sto<ium/cisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The opinions and
maxims of the Stoics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A real or pretended indifference to pleasure or
pain; insensibility; impassiveness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoicity</ent><br/
<hw>Sto*ic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stoicism.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoke</ent><br/
<hw>Stoke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stoken</ets>, fr. D. <ets>stoken</ets>, fr. <ets>stok</ets> a stick
(cf. OF. <ets>estoquier</ets> to thrust, stab; of Teutonic origin, and
akin to D. <ets>stok</ets>). See <er>Stock</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To stick; to thrust; to stab.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor short sword for to <qex>stoke</qex>, with point biting.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To poke or stir up, as a fire; hence, to tend, as
the fire of a furnace, boiler, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoke</ent><br/
<hw>Stoke</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To poke or stir up a fire; hence,
to tend the fires of furnaces, steamers, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stokehold</ent><br/
<hw>Stoke"hold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>The space, or any of the spaces, in front of the boilers of a ship,
from which the furnaces are fed; the stokehole of a ship; also, a room
containing a ship's boilers; <as>as, forced draft with closed
<ex>stokehold</ex></as>; -- called also, in American ships,
<altname>fireroom</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stokehole</ent><br/
<hw>Stoke"hole`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The mouth to the
grate of a furnace; also, the space in front of the furnace, where the
stokers stand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoker</ent><br/
<hw>Stok"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. See
<er>Stoke</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is
employed to tend a furnace and supply it with fuel, especially the
furnace of a locomotive or of a marine steam boiler; also, a machine for
feeding fuel to a fire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fire poker.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>C.
Richardson (Dict.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stokey</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"key</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Close; sultry.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1418 --></p>

<p><ent>Stola</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sto"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stolae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. See <er>Stole</er> a
garment.]</ety> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A long garment, descending
to the ankles, worn by Roman women.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>stola</qex> was not allowed to be worn by courtesans, or
by women who had been divorced from their husbands.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairholt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stole</ent><br/
<hw>Stole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Steal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stole</ent><br/
<hw>Stole</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stolo</ets>,
<ets>-onis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A stolon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stole</ent><br/
<hw>Stole</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stole</ets>, L.
<ets>stola</ets>, Gr. <?/ a stole, garment, equipment, fr. <?/ to set,
place, equip, send, akin to E. <ets>stall</ets>. See
<er>Stall</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long, loose garment reaching
to the feet.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But when mild morn, in saffron <qex>stole</qex>,<br/
First issues from her eastern goal.</q> <rj><qau>T.
Warton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A narrow band of silk or stuff,
sometimes enriched with embroidery and jewels, worn on the left shoulder
of deacons, and across both shoulders of bishops and priests, pendent on
each side nearly to the ground. At Mass, it is worn crossed on the
breast by priests. It is used in various sacred functions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Groom of the stole</b></col>, <cd>the first lord of the
bedchamber in the royal household.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <au>Brande &
C.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoled</ent><br/
<hw>Stoled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having or wearing a
stole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>After them flew the prophets, brightly <qex>stoled</qex><br/
In shining lawn.</q> <rj><qau>G. Fletcher.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stolen</ent><br/
<hw>Stol"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Steal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stolid</ent><br/
<hw>Stol"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stolidus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Hopelessly insensible or stupid; not
easily aroused or excited; dull; impassive; foolish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stolidity</ent><br/
<hw>Sto*lid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stoliditas</ets>.]</ety> <def>The state or quality of being stolid;
dullness of intellect; obtuseness; stupidity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Indocile, intractable fools, whose <qex>stolidity</qex> can baffle
all arguments, and be proof against demonstration itself.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stolidness</ent><br/
<hw>Stol"id*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Stolidity</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stolon</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"lon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stolo</ets>,
<ets>-onis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>stolon</ets>. Cf. <er>Stole</er> a stolon,
1st <er>Stool</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A trailing
branch which is disposed to take root at the end or at the joints; a
stole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An extension of the integument of
the body, or of the body wall, from which buds are developed, giving
rise to new zooids, and thus forming a compound animal in which the
zooids usually remain united by the stolons. Such stolons are often
present in Anthozoa, Hydroidea, Bryozoa, and social ascidians. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Scyphistoma</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoloniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Stol`o*nif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stolon</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>stolonif<egrave/re</ets>.]</ety> <def>Producing stolons; putting
forth suckers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sto"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stomata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, <?/, a
mouth.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>One of the minute
apertures between the cells in many serous membranes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The minute breathing
pores of leaves or other organs opening into the intercellular spaces,
and usually bordered by two contractile cells.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>The line of dehiscence of the sporangium of a fern. It is usually
marked by two transversely elongated cells. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Sporangium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A stigma. See <er>Stigma</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 6 <sd>(a)</sd> & <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomach</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stomak</ets>, F. <ets>estomac</ets>, L. <ets>stomachus</ets>, fr.
Gr. <grk>sto`machos</grk> stomach, throat, gullet, fr. <grk>sto`ma</grk>
a mouth, any outlet or entrance.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>An enlargement, or series of enlargements, in the anterior part of
the alimentary canal, in which food is digested; any cavity in which
digestion takes place in an animal; a digestive cavity. See
<er>Digestion</er>, and <cref>Gastric juice</cref>, under
<er>Gastric</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The desire for food caused by hunger; appetite;
<as>as, a good <ex>stomach</ex> for roast beef</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence appetite in general; inclination;
desire.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He which hath no <qex>stomach</qex> to this fight,<br/
Let him depart.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Violence of temper; anger; sullenness; resentment;
willful obstinacy; stubbornness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Stern was his look, and full of <qex>stomach</qex> vain.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This sort of crying proceeding from pride, obstinacy, and
<qex>stomach</qex>, the will, where the fault lies, must be bent.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Pride; haughtiness; arrogance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was a man<br/
Of an unbounded <qex>stomach</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stomach pump</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a small
pump or syringe with a flexible tube, for drawing liquids from the
stomach, or for injecting them into it.</cd> -- <col><b>Stomach
tube</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a long flexible tube for
introduction into the stomach.</cd> -- <col><b>Stomach worm</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the common roundworm (<spn>Ascaris
lumbricoides</spn>) found in the human intestine, and rarely in the
stomach.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomach</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stomached</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stomaching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. L. <ets>stomachari</ets>,
v.t. & i., to be angry or vexed at a thing.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
resent; to remember with anger; to dislike.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lion began to show his teeth, and to <qex>stomach</qex> the
affront.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Parliament sit in that body . . . to be his counselors and
dictators, though he <qex>stomach</qex> it.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bear without repugnance; to brook.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomach</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be angry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomachal</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stomacal</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the
stomach; gastric.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Helping the stomach; stomachic; cordial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomachal</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stomachic.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomacher</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who stomachs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pr>(<?/ <or/ <?/)</pr> <def>An ornamental covering for
the breast, worn originally both by men and women. Those worn by women
were often richly decorated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A stately lady in a diamond <qex>stomacher</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Johnson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomachful</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach*ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Willfully
obstinate; stubborn; perverse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Stom"ach*ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
-- <wf>Stom"ach*ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomachical</ent><br/
<ent>Stomachic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sto*mach"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sto*mach"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stomachicus</ets>, Gr.
<?/: cf. F. <ets>stomachique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the stomach; <as>as, <ex>stomachic</ex>
vessels</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Strengthening to the stomach; exciting the action of
the stomach; stomachal; cordial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomachic</ent><br/
<hw>Sto*mach"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine
that strengthens the stomach and excites its action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomaching</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Resentment.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomachless</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Being without a
stomach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having no appetite.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomachous</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stomachosus</ets> angry, peexish. See <er>Stomach</er>.]</ety>
<def>Stout; sullen; obstinate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With stern looks and <qex>stomachous</qex> disdain.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomachy</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"ach*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Obstinate; sullen;
haughty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A little, bold, solemn, <qex>stomachy</qex> man, a great professor
of piety.</q> <rj><qau>R. L. Stevenson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomapod</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ma*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Stomapoda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomapoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sto*map"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Stoma</er>, and <er>-poda</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
order of Crustacea including the squillas. The maxillipeds are leglike
in form, and the large claws are comblike. They have a large and
elongated abdomen, which contains a part of the stomach and heart; the
abdominal appendages are large, and bear the gills. Called also
<altname>Gastrula</altname>, <altname>Stomatopoda</altname>, and
<altname>Squilloidea</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomate</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
stoma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sto*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a stoma; of the nature of a stoma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sto*mat"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>sto`ma</grk>,
<grk>-atos</grk>, mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A medicine for
diseases of the mouth.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Stom`a*tif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>sto`ma</grk>, <grk>-atos</grk> mouth + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Having or producing stomata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stom`a*ti"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>sto`ma</grk>, <grk>-atos</grk>, mouth +
<ets>-itis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inflammation of the
mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stom`a*to"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/, <?/, mouth.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of
Protozoa in which a mouthlike opening exists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatodaeum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stom`a*to*dae"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Stomodaeum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatode</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"a*tode</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having a mouth; -- applied to certain Protozoa.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the Stomatoda.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatogastric</ent><br/
<hw>Stom`a*to*gas"tric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<?/, mouth + E. <ets>gastric</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the
mouth and the stomach; <as>as, the <ex>stomatogastric</ex> ganglion of
certain Mollusca</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatology</ent><br/
<hw>Sto`ma*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
mouth + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Scientific study
or knowledge of the mouth.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatoplastic</ent><br/
<hw>Stom`a*to*plas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<?/, mouth + <ets>-plastic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to the operation of forming a mouth where the aperture has
been contracted, or in any way deformed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatoplasty</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"a*to*plas`ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<?/, mouth + <ets>-plasty</ets>.]</ety> <def>Plastic surgery of the
mouth.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatopod</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"a*to*pod</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Stomatopoda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatopoda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stom`a*top"o*da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Stoma</er>, and <er>-pod</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Stomapoda</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatopodous</ent><br/
<hw>Stom`a*top"o*dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the Stomatopoda.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatoscope</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"a*to*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/,
mouth + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An apparatus
for examining the interior of the mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomatous</ent><br/
<hw>Stom"a*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
stoma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomodaeum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stom`o*dae"um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/, <?/, mouth + <?/ to divide.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A part of the alimentary canal. See under
<er>Mesenteron</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The primitive mouth and esophagus
of the embryo of annelids and arthropods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stomp</ent><br/
<hw>Stomp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stamp</er>.]</ety> <def>To stamp with the foot.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/In gallant procession, the priests mean to
<xex>stomp</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>R. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>stomping ground</ent><br/
<hw>stomping ground</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<cref>stamping ground</cref>. See under <er>stamp</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stond</ent><br/
<hw>Stond</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For
<ets>stand</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Stop; halt; hindrance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stand; a post; a station.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stond</ent><br/
<hw>Stond</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To stand.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone</ent><br/
<hw>Stone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>ston</ets>,
<ets>stan</ets>, AS. <ets>st<amac/n</ets>; akin to OS. & OFries.
<ets>st<emac/n</ets>, D. <ets>steen</ets>, G. <ets>stein</ets>, Icel.
<ets>steinn</ets>, Sw. <ets>sten</ets>, Dan. <ets>steen</ets>, Goth.
<ets>stains</ets>, Russ. <ets>stiena</ets> a wall, Gr. <?/, <?/, a
pebble. <root/167. Cf. <er>Steen</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Concreted
earthy or mineral matter; also, any particular mass of such matter;
<as>as, a house built of <ex>stone</ex>; the boy threw a <ex>stone</ex>;
pebbles are rounded <ex>stones</ex>.</as></def> <ldquo/Dumb as a
<xex>stone</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They had brick for <qex>stone</qex>, and slime . . . for
mortar.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xi. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In popular language, very large masses of stone are
called <xex>rocks</xex>; small masses are called <xex>stones</xex>; and
the finer kinds, <xex>gravel</xex>, or <xex>sand</xex>, or <xex>grains
of sand</xex>. Stone is much and widely used in the construction of
buildings of all kinds, for walls, fences, piers, abutments, arches,
monuments, sculpture, and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A precious stone; a gem.</def> <ldquo/Many a rich
<xex>stone</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/Inestimable
<xex>stones</xex>, unvalued jewels.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something made of stone. Specifically: -</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>The glass of a mirror; a mirror.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lend me a looking-glass;<br/
If that her breath will mist or stain the <qex>stone</qex>,<br/
Why, then she lives.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A monument to the dead; a gravestone.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Should some relenting eye<br/
Glance on the where our cold relics lie.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A calculous concretion, especially
one in the kidneys or bladder; the disease arising from a
calculus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>One of the testes; a testicle.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The hard endocarp of drupes;
<as>as, the <ex>stone</ex> of a cherry or peach</as>. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Endocarp</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A weight which legally is fourteen pounds, but in
practice varies with the article weighed.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>stone</xex> of butchers' meat or fish is
reckoned at 8 lbs.; of cheese, 16 lbs.; of hemp, 32 lbs.; of glass, 5
lbs.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Fig.: Symbol of hardness and insensibility;
torpidness; insensibility; <as>as, a heart of
<ex>stone</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have not yet forgot myself to <qex>stone</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <def>A stand or table with a smooth,
flat top of stone, commonly marble, on which to arrange the pages of a
book, newspaper, etc., before printing; -- called also <altname>imposing
stone</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Stone</xex> is used adjectively or in composition
with other words to denote <xex>made of stone</xex>, <xex>containing a
stone</xex> or <xex>stones</xex>, <xex>employed on stone</xex>, or, more
generally, <xex>of</xex> or <xex>pertaining to stone</xex> or
<xex>stones</xex>; as, <xex>stone</xex> fruit, or
<xex>stone</xex>-fruit; <xex>stone</xex>-hammer, or <xex>stone</xex>
hammer; <xex>stone</xex> falcon, or <xex>stone</xex>-falcon. Compounded
with some adjectives it denotes a degree of the quality expressed by the
adjective equal to that possessed by a stone; as, <xex>stone</xex>-dead,
<xex>stone</xex>-blind, <xex>stone</xex>-cold, <xex>stone</xex>-still,
etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Atlantic stone</b></col>, <cd>ivory.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Citron tables, or <xex>Atlantic
stone</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au> -- <col><b>Bowing stone</b></col>.
<cd>Same as <er>Cromlech</er>.</cd> <au>Encyc. Brit.</au> --
<col><b>Meteoric stones</b></col>, <cd>stones which fall from the
atmosphere, as after the explosion of a meteor.</cd> --
<col><b>Philosopher's stone</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Philosopher</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Rocking stone</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Rocking-stone</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone age</b></col>, <cd>a
supposed prehistoric age of the world when stone and bone were
habitually used as the materials for weapons and tools; -- called also
<altname>flint age</altname>. The <cref>bronze age</cref> succeeded to
this.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone bass</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any
one of several species of marine food fishes of the genus
<gen>Serranus</gen> and allied genera, as <spn>Serranus Couchii</spn>,
and <spn>Polyprion cernium</spn> of Europe; -- called also <altname>sea
perch</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone biter</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the wolf fish.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
boiling</b></col>, <cd>a method of boiling water or milk by dropping hot
stones into it, -- in use among savages.</cd> <au>Tylor.</au> --
<col><b>Stone borer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any animal that
bores stones; especially, one of certain bivalve mollusks which burrow
in limestone. See <er>Lithodomus</er>, and <er>Saxicava</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Stone bramble</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a European
trailing species of bramble (<spn>Rubus saxatilis</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Stone-break</b></col>. <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>steinbrech</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Any plant of the
genus <gen>Saxifraga</gen>; saxifrage.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
bruise</b></col>, <cd>a sore spot on the bottom of the foot, from a
bruise by a stone.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone canal</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as <cref>Sand canal</cref>, under
<er>Sand</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone cat</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of several species of small fresh-water North American
catfishes of the genus <gen>Noturus</gen>. They have sharp pectoral
spines with which they inflict painful wounds.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
coal</b></col>, <cd>hard coal; mineral coal; anthracite coal.</cd> --
<col><b>Stone coral</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any hard
calcareous coral.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone crab</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A large crab (<spn>Menippe
mercenaria</spn>) found on the southern coast of the United States and
much used as food.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A European spider crab
(<spn>Lithodes maia</spn>)</cd>. <col><b>Stone crawfish</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a European crawfish (<spn>Astacus
torrentium</spn>), by many writers considered only a variety of the
common species (<spn>Astacus fluviatilis</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
curlew</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A large plover
found in Europe (<spn>Edicnemus crepitans</spn>). It frequents stony
places. Called also <altname>thick-kneed plover</altname> or
<altname>bustard</altname>, and <altname>thick-knee</altname>.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The whimbrel</cd>. <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<sd>(c)</sd> <cd>The willet.</cd> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> --
<col><b>Stone crush</b></col>. <cd>Same as <cref>Stone bruise</cref>,
above.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone eater</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>Same as <cref>Stone borer</cref>, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
falcon</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the merlin.</cd> --
<col><b>Stone fern</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a European fern
(<spn>Asplenium Ceterach</spn>) which grows on rocks and walls.</cd> --
<col><b>Stone fly</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of many
species of pseudoneuropterous insects of the genus <gen>Perla</gen> and
allied genera; a perlid. They are often used by anglers for bait. The
larvae are aquatic.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone fruit</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any fruit with a stony endocarp; a drupe, as a
peach, plum, or cherry.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone grig</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the mud lamprey, or pride.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
hammer</b></col>, <cd>a hammer formed with a face at one end, and a
thick, blunt edge, parallel with the handle, at the other, -- used for
breaking stone.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone hawk</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the merlin; -- so called from its habit of sitting on bare
stones.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone jar</b></col>, <cd>a jar made of
stoneware.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone lily</b></col> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>,
<cd>a fossil crinoid.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone lugger</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Stone roller</cref>, below.</cd> --
<col><b>Stone marten</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a European marten
(<spn>Mustela foina</spn>) allied to the pine marten, but having a white
throat; -- called also <altname>beech marten</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Stone mason</b></col>, <cd>a mason who works or builds in
stone.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone-mortar</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a
kind of large mortar formerly used in sieges for throwing a mass of
small stones short distances.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone oil</b></col>,
<cd>rock oil, petroleum.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone parsley</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an umbelliferous plant (<spn>Seseli
Labanotis</spn>). See under <er>Parsley</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
pine</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>A nut pine. See the Note under
<er>Pine</er>, and <er>Pi<ntil/on</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
pit</b></col>, <cd>a quarry where stones are dug.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
pitch</b></col>, <cd>hard, inspissated pitch.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone
plover</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The European stone
curlew</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Any one of several species of Asiatic
plovers of the genus <gen>Esacus</gen>; <as>as, the large <ex>stone
plover</ex> (<spn>Esacus recurvirostris</spn>)</as>.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd>
<cd>The gray or black-bellied plover</cd>. <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<sd>(d)</sd> <cd>The ringed plover.</cd> <sd>(e)</sd> <cd>The bar-tailed
godwit. <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> Also applied to other species of
limicoline birds.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone roller</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>An American fresh-water fish
(<spn>Catostomus nigricans</spn>) of the Sucker family. Its color is
yellowish olive, often with dark blotches. Called also <altname>stone
lugger</altname>, <altname>stone toter</altname>, <altname>hog
sucker</altname>, <altname>hog mullet</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
common American cyprinoid fish (<spn>Campostoma anomalum</spn>); --
called also <altname>stone lugger</altname>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Stone's cast</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Stone's
throw</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the distance to which a stone may be thrown
by the hand; <as>as, they live a <ex>stone's throw</ex> from each
other</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stone snipe</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the greater yellowlegs, or tattler.</cd> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark>
-- <col><b>Stone toter</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>See <cref>Stone roller</cref> <sd>(a)</sd>, above</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>A cyprinoid fish (<spn>Exoglossum maxillingua</spn>) found in the
rivers from <state>Virginia</state> to <stype>New York</stype>. It has a
three-lobed lower lip; -- called also <altname>cutlips</altname>.</cd>
-- <col><b>To leave no stone unturned</b></col>, <cd>to do everything
that can be done; to use all practicable means to effect an
object.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone</ent><br/
<hw>Stone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stoned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stoning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Stone</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>: cf. AS. <ets>st<?/nan</ets>, Goth.
<ets>stainjan</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pelt, beat, or kill with
stones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And they <qex>stoned</qex> Stephen, calling upon God, and saying,
Lord Jesus, receive my spirit.</q> <rj><qau>Acts vii. 59.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make like stone; to harden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O perjured woman! thou dost <qex>stone</qex> my heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To free from stones; also, to remove the seeds of;
<as>as, to <ex>stone</ex> a field; to <ex>stone</ex> cherries; to
<ex>stone</ex> raisins</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To wall or face with stones; to line or fortify with
stones; <as>as, to <ex>stone</ex> a well; to <ex>stone</ex> a
cellar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To rub, scour, or sharpen with a stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- get stoned, be stoned? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1419 --></p>

<p><ent>Stonebird</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"bird`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The yellowlegs; --
called also <altname>stone snipe</altname>. See <er>Tattler</er>,
2.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone-blind</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"-blind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>As blind as a
stone; completely blind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonebow</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"bow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of crossbow
formerly used for shooting stones.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonebrash</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"brash`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A subsoil made up
of small stones or finely-broken rock; brash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonebrearer</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"brear`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A machine for
crushing or hammering stone.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonebuck</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"buck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Steinbock</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonechat</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"chat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Stone</ets>
+ <ets>chat</ets>.]</ety> <ety>[So called from the similarity of its
alarm note to the clicking together of two pebbles.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A small, active, and very common
European singing bird (<spn>Pratincola rubicola</spn>); -- called also
<altname>chickstone</altname>, <altname>stonechacker</altname>,
<altname>stonechatter</altname>, <altname>stoneclink</altname>,
<altname>stonesmith</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
wheatear.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The blue titmouse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The name is sometimes applied to various species of
<gen>Saxicola</gen>, <gen>Pratincola</gen>, and allied genera; as, the
pied <xex>stonechat</xex> of India (<spn>Saxicola
picata</spn>).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone-cold</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"-cold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cold as a
stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stone-cold</qex> without, within burnt with love's flame.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonecray</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"cray`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Stone</ets>
+ F. <ets>craie</ets> chalk, L. <ets>creta</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
distemper in hawks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonecrop</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"crop`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>st<amac/ncropp</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sort of tree.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any low succulent plant of the
genus <gen>Sedum</gen>, esp. <spn>Sedum acre</spn>, which is common on
bare rocks in Europe, and is spreading in parts of America. See
<er>Orpine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Virginian stonecrop</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Ditch stonecrop</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an American plant
(<spn>Penthorum sedoides</spn>).</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonecutter</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"cut`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One whose
occupation is to cut stone; also, a machine for dressing
stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonecutting</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"cut`ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Hewing or
dressing stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone-dead</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"-dead`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>As dead as a
stone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone-deaf</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"-deaf`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>As deaf as a
stone; completely deaf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonegall</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"gall`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>steengal</ets>, G. <ets>steingall</ets>. See
<er>Stannel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Stannel</er>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonehatch</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"hatch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The ring plover, or dotterel.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Hard-hearted;
cruel; pitiless; unfeeling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonehenge</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"henge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An assemblage of
upright stones with others placed horizontally on their tops, on
Salisbury Plain, England, -- generally supposed to be the remains of an
ancient Druidical temple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone-horse</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"-horse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stallion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoner</ent><br/
<hw>Ston"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
stones; one who makes an assault with stones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who walls with stones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoneroot</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"root`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A North American plant (<spn>Collinsonia Canadensis</spn>) having a
very hard root; horse balm. See <cref>Horse balm</cref>, under
<er>Horse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonerunner</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"run`ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The ring plover, or the ringed dotterel.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The dotterel.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonesmickle</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"smic`kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The stonechat; -- called also <altname>stonesmitch</altname>.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stone-still</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"-still`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>As still as a
stone.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoneware</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"ware`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A species of
coarse potter's ware, glazed and baked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoneweed</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Lithospermum</gen>, herbs having a
fruit composed of four stony nutlets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonework</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Work or wall
consisting of stone; mason's work of stone.</def>
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonewort</ent><br/
<hw>Stone"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Any plant of the genus Chara; -- so called because they are often
incrusted with carbonate of lime. See <er>Chara</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonily</ent><br/
<hw>Ston"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stony
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoniness</ent><br/
<hw>Ston"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stonish</ent><br/
<hw>Ston"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stony.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/Possessed with <xex>stonish</xex> insensibility.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Robynson (More's Utopia).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stont</ent><br/
<hw>Stont</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>3d pers. sing.
present</pos> of <er>Stand</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stony</ent><br/
<hw>Ston"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stonier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stoniest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>st<amac/nig</ets>. See
<er>Stone</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to stone,
consisting of, or abounding in, stone or stones; resembling stone; hard;
<as>as, a <ex>stony</ex> tower; a <ex>stony</ex> cave; <ex>stony</ex>
ground; a <ex>stony</ex> crust</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Converting into stone; petrifying;
petrific.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>stony</qex> dart of senseless cold.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Inflexible; cruel; unrelenting; pitiless; obdurate;
perverse; cold; morally hard; appearing as if petrified; <as>as, a
<ex>stony</ex> heart; a <ex>stony</ex> gaze</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stony coral</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<cref>Stone coral</cref>, under <er>Stone</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stood</ent><br/
<hw>Stood</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Stand</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stooge</ent><br/
<hw>Stooge</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A person of
unquestioning obedience.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> flunky, flunkey, yes-man.</syn></p> <p><sn>2.</sn>
<def>A victim of ridicule or prank.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> butt, goat, laughingstock.</syn><br/
[<source>WordNet 3.0</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stooge</ent><br/
<hw>Stooge</hw>, <pos>v.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Cruise in slow or
routine flights.</def> <sn>2.</sn> <def>Act as the stooge; <p><q>"His
role was to stooge for the popular comedian."</q></p></def> <sn>2.</sn>
<def>Act as a stooge, in a compliant or subordinate manner; <p><q>"He
stooged for the flamboyant Senator."</q></p></def><br/
[<source>WordNet 3.0</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stook</ent><br/
<hw>Stook</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Scot. <ets>stook</ets>,
<ets>stouk</ets>; cf. LG. <ets>stuke</ets> a heap, bundle, G.
<ets>stauche</ets> a truss, bundle of flax.]</ety> <fld>(Agric.)</fld>
<def>A small collection of sheaves set up in the field; a shock; in
England, twelve sheaves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stook</ent><br/
<hw>Stook</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stooked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stooking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>To set up,
as sheaves of grain, in stooks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stool</ent><br/
<hw>Stool</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stolo</ets>.
See <er>Stolon</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Hort.)</fld> <def>A plant from which
layers are propagated by bending its branches into the soil.</def>
<rj><au>P. Henderson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stool</ent><br/
<hw>Stool</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>To ramify; to
tiller, as grain; to shoot out suckers.</def> <rj><au>R. D.
Blackmore.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stool</ent><br/
<hw>Stool</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>st<omac/l</ets> a seat; akin to OFries. & OS. <ets>st<omac/l</ets>,
D. <ets>stoel</ets>, G. <ets>stuhl</ets>, OHG. <ets>stuol</ets>, Icel.
<ets>st<omac/ll</ets>, Sw. & Dan. <ets>stol</ets>, Goth.
<ets>st<omac/ls</ets>, Lith. <ets>stalas</ets> a table, Russ.
<ets>stol'</ets>; from the root of E. <ets>stand</ets>. <root/163. See
<er>Stand</er>, and cf. <er>Fauteuil</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
single seat with three or four legs and without a back, made in various
forms for various uses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A seat used in evacuating the bowels; hence, an
evacuation; a discharge from the bowels.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A stool pigeon, or decoy bird.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A small channel on the side of a
vessel, for the dead-eyes of the backstays.</def>
<rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A bishop's seat or see; a bishop-stool.</def>
<rj><au>J. P. Peters.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A bench or form for resting the feet or the knees; a
footstool; <as>as, a kneeling <ex>stool</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Material, such as oyster shells, spread on the sea
bottom for oyster spat to adhere to.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Stool of a window</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Window stool</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>the flat
piece upon which the window shuts down, and which corresponds to the
sill of a door; in the United States, the narrow shelf fitted on the
inside against the actual sill upon which the sash descends. This is
called a <xex>window seat</xex> when broad and low enough to be used as
a seat.</cd> -- <col><b>Stool of repentance</b></col>, <cd>the
cuttystool.</cd> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> -- <col><b>Stool pigeon</b></col>,
<cd>a pigeon used as a decoy to draw others within a net; hence, a
person used as a decoy for others.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoolball</ent><br/
<hw>Stool"ball`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of game
with balls, formerly common in England, esp. with young women.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nausicaa<br/
With other virgins did at <qex>stoolball</qex> play.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoom</ent><br/
<hw>Stoom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>stommen</ets> to adulterate, to drug (wine). <root/163. Cf.
<er>Stum</er>.]</ety> <def>To stum.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoop</ent><br/
<hw>Stoop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>stoep</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Originally, a covered
porch with seats, at a house door; the Dutch <xex>stoep</xex> as
introduced by the Dutch into New York. Afterward, an out-of-door flight
of stairs of from seven to fourteen steps, with platform and parapets,
leading to an entrance door some distance above the street; the French
<xex>perron</xex>. Hence, any porch, platform, entrance stairway, or
small veranda, at a house door.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoop</ent><br/
<hw>Stoop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stope</ets>, Icel.
<ets>staup</ets>; akin to AS. <ets>ste<aacute/p</ets>, D.
<ets>stoop</ets>, G. <ets>stauf</ets>, OHG. <ets>stouph</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A vessel of liquor; a flagon.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stoup</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fetch me a <qex>stoop</qex> of liquor.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoop</ent><br/
<hw>Stoop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel. <ets>staup</ets> a knobby
lump.]</ety> <def>A post fixed in the earth.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoop</ent><br/
<hw>Stoop</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stooped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stooping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stoupen</ets>; akin to
AS. <ets>st<?/pian</ets>, OD. <ets>stuypen</ets>, Icel.
<ets>st<umac/pa</ets>, Sw. <ets>stupa</ets> to fall, to tilt. Cf 5th
<er>Steep</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bend the upper part of the
body downward and forward; to bend or lean forward; to incline forward
in standing or walking; to assume habitually a bent position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To yield; to submit; to bend, as by compulsion; to
assume a position of humility or subjection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mighty in her ships stood Carthage long, . . . <br/
Yet <qex>stooped</qex> to Rome, less wealthy, but more strong.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These are arts, my prince,<br/
In which your Zama does not <qex>stoop</qex> to Rome.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To descend from rank or dignity; to
condescend.</def> <ldquo/She <xex>stoops</xex> to conquer.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where men of great wealth <qex>stoop</qex> to husbandry, it
multiplieth riches exceedingly.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To come down as a hawk does on its prey; to pounce;
to souse; to swoop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bird of Jove, <qex>stooped</qex> from his aery tour,<br/
Two birds of gayest plume before him drove.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To sink when on the wing; to alight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>stoop</qex> with closing pinions from above.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cowering low<br/
With blandishment, each bird <qex>stooped</qex> on his wing.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To lean; yield; submit; condescend; descend;
cower; shrink.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoop</ent><br/
<hw>Stoop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bend forward and
downward; to bow down; <as>as, to <ex>stoop</ex> the body</as>.</def>
<ldquo/Have <xex>stooped</xex> my neck.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to incline downward; to slant; <as>as, to
<ex>stoop</ex> a cask of liquor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to submit; to prostrate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many of those whose states so tempt thine ears<br/
Are <qex>stooped</qex> by death; and many left alive.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To degrade.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoop</ent><br/
<hw>Stoop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stooping, or
bending the body forward; inclination forward; also, an habitual bend of
the back and shoulders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Descent, as from dignity or superiority;
condescension; an act or position of humiliation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Can any loyal subject see<br/
With patience such a <qex>stoop</qex> from sovereignty?</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The fall of a bird on its prey; a swoop.</def>
<rj><au>L'Estrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stooper</ent><br/
<hw>Stoop"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
stoops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stooping</ent><br/
<hw>Stoop"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Stoop</er>.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Stoop"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoor</ent><br/
<hw>Stoor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>storen</ets> to disturb. Cf. <er>Stir</er>.]</ety> <def>To rise in
clouds, as dust.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stor</ent><br/
<ent>Stoor</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stoor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>st<omac/r</ets>; akin to LG.
<ets>stur</ets>, Icel. <ets>st<omac/rr</ets>.]</ety> <def>Strong;
powerful; hardy; bold; audacious.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O stronge lady <qex>stoor</qex>, what doest thou?</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stop</ent><br/
<hw>Stop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stopped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stopping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stoppen</ets>, AS.
<ets>stoppian</ets> (in comp.); akin to LG. & D. <ets>stoppen</ets>, G.
<ets>stopfen</ets>, Icel. <ets>stoppa</ets>, Sw. <ets>stoppa</ets>, Dan.
<ets>stoppe</ets>; all probably fr. LL. <ets>stopare</ets>,
<ets>stupare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>stuppa</ets> the coarse part of flax,
tow, oakum. Cf. <er>Estop</er>, <er>Stuff</er>, <er>Stupe</er> a
fomentation.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To close, as an aperture, by
filling or by obstructing; <as>as, to <ex>stop</ex> the ears</as>;
hence, to stanch, as a wound.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To obstruct; to render impassable; <as>as, to
<ex>stop</ex> a way, road, or passage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To arrest the progress of; to hinder; to impede; to
shut in; <as>as, to <ex>stop</ex> a traveler; to <ex>stop</ex> the
course of a stream, or a flow of blood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To hinder from acting or moving; to prevent the
effect or efficiency of; to cause to cease; to repress; to restrain; to
suppress; to interrupt; to suspend; <as>as, to <ex>stop</ex> the
execution of a decree, the progress of vice, the approaches of old age
or infirmity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whose disposition all the world well knows<br/
Will not be rubbed nor <qex>stopped</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To regulate the sounds of, as
musical strings, by pressing them against the finger board with the
finger, or by shortening in any way the vibrating part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To point, as a composition; to punctuate.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If his sentences were properly <qex>stopped</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To make fast; to
stopper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To obstruct; hinder; impede; repress; suppress;
restrain; discontinue; delay; interrupt.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stop off</b></col> <fld>(Founding)</fld>, <cd>to fill
(a part of a mold) with sand, where a part of the cavity left by the
pattern is not wanted for the casting.</cd> -- <col><b>To stop the
mouth</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Mouth</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stop</ent><br/
<hw>Stop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cease
to go on; to halt, or stand still; to come to a stop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He bites his lip, and starts;<br/
<qex>Stops</qex> on a sudden, looks upon the ground;<br/
Then lays his finger on his temple: strait<br/
Springs out into fast gait; then <qex>stops</qex> again.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cease from any motion, or course of
action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stop</qex>, while ye may, suspend your mad career!</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To spend a short time; to reside temporarily; to
stay; to tarry; <as>as, to <ex>stop</ex> with a friend</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By <qex>stopping</qex> at home till the money was gone.</q>
<rj><qau>R. D. Blackmore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stop over</b></col>, <cd>to stop at a station or
airport beyond the time of the departure of the train or airplane on
which one came, with the purpose of continuing one's journey on a
subsequent train or airplane; to break one's journey. See
<er>stopover</er>, <pos>n.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stop</ent><br/
<hw>Stop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stopping, or
the state of being stopped; hindrance of progress or of action;
cessation; repression; interruption; check; obstruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is doubtful . . . whether it contributed anything to the
<qex>stop</qex> of the infection.</q> <rj><qau>De Foe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Occult qualities put a <qex>stop</qex> to the improvement of
natural philosophy.</q> <rj><qau>Sir I. Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a great step toward the mastery of our desires to give this
<qex>stop</qex> to them.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which stops, impedes, or obstructs; as
obstacle; an impediment; an obstruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A fatal <qex>stop</qex> traversed their headlong course.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So melancholy a prospect should inspire us with zeal to oppose
some <qex>stop</qex> to the rising torrent.</q>
<rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>A device, or piece, as a pin,
block, pawl, etc., for arresting or limiting motion, or for determining
the position to which another part shall be brought.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The closing of an
aperture in the air passage, or pressure of the finger upon the string,
of an instrument of music, so as to modify the tone; hence, any
contrivance by which the sounds of a musical instrument are
regulated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The organ sound a time survives the <qex>stop</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>In the organ, one of the knobs or handles at each
side of the organist, by which he can draw on or shut off any register
or row of pipes; the register itself; as, the vox humana
<xex>stop</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A member, plain or molded, formed
of a separate piece and fixed to a jamb, against which a door or window
shuts. This takes the place, or answers the purpose, of a rebate. Also,
a pin or block to prevent a drawer from sliding too far.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A point or mark in writing or printing intended to
distinguish the sentences, parts of a sentence, or clauses; a mark of
punctuation. See <er>Punctuation</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Opt.)</fld> <def>The diaphragm used in optical
instruments to cut off the marginal portions of a beam of light passing
through lenses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The depression in the face of a
dog between the skull and the nasal bones. It is conspicuous in the
bulldog, pug, and some other breeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <def>Some part of the articulating
organs, as the lips, or the tongue and palate, closed <sd>(a)</sd> so as
to cut off the passage of breath or voice through the mouth and the nose
(distinguished as a <xex>lip-stop</xex>, or a <xex>front-stop</xex>,
etc., as in <xex>p</xex>, <xex>t</xex>, <xex>d</xex>, etc.), or
<sd>(b)</sd> so as to obstruct, but not entirely cut off, the passage,
as in <xex>l</xex>, <xex>n</xex>, etc.; also, any of the consonants so
formed.</def> <rj><au>H. Sweet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stop bead</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>the molding
screwed to the inner side of a window frame, on the face of the pulley
stile, completing the groove in which the inner sash is to slide.</cd>
-- <col><b>Stop motion</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>an automatic
device for arresting the motion of a machine, as when a certain
operation is completed, or when an imperfection occurs in its
performance or product, or in the material which is supplied to it,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Stop plank</b></col>, <cd>one of a set of planks
employed to form a sort of dam in some hydraulic works.</cd> --
<col><b>Stop valve</b></col>, <cd>a valve that can be closed or opened
at will, as by hand, for preventing or regulating flow, as of a liquid
in a pipe; -- in distinction from a valve which is operated by the
action of the fluid it restrains.</cd> -- <col><b>Stop watch</b></col>,
<cd>a watch the hands of which can be stopped in order to tell exactly
the time that has passed, as in timing a race. See <cref>Independent
seconds watch</cref>, under <er>Independent</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Cessation; check; obstruction; obstacle;
hindrance; impediment; interruption.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopcock</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A bib,
faucet, or short pipe, fitted with a turning stopper or plug for
permitting or restraining the flow of a liquid or gas; a cock or valve
for checking or regulating the flow of water, gas, etc., through or from
a pipe, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The turning plug, stopper, or spigot of a
faucet.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stope</ent><br/
<hw>Stope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Step</er>,
<pos>n.</pos> & <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A
horizontal working forming one of a series, the working faces of which
present the appearance of a flight of steps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stope</ent><br/
<hw>Stope</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stoped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stoping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>To excavate in the form of stopes.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To fill
in with rubbish, as a space from which the ore has been worked
out.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1420 --></p>

<p><ent>Stopen</ent><br/
<ent>Stope</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sto"pen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>p. p.</pos> <mord>of <er>Step</er></mord>. <def>Stepped;
gone; advanced.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A poor widow, somedeal <qex>stope</qex> in age.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stop-gap</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"-gap`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which closes
or fills up an opening or gap; hence, a temporary expedient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Moral prejudices are the <qex>stop-gaps</qex> of virtue.</q>
<rj><qau>Hare.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoping</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld>
<def>The act of excavating in the form of stopes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopless</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not to be
stopped.</def> <rj><au>Davenant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stop order</ent><br/
<hw>Stop order</hw>. <fld>(Finance)</fld> <def>An order in a financial
market that aims to limit losses by fixing a figure at which purchases
shall be sold or sales bought in, as where stock is bought at 100 and
the broker is directed to sell if the market price drops to 98.</def>
<note>For a simple <ex>stop order</ex>, when the specified price has
been reached by a traded security, the order becomes a market order to
buy or sell at the market price. Thus in the given example, the stock
bought at 100 might in fact be sold at 97 if the market is dropping
rapidly. A <contr>stop-limit order</contr> is allowed by some exchanges
and traders, in which case, when the specified price has been reached by
a traded security, the order becomes a <contr>limit order</contr> to buy
or sell, with the possibility that the ordered transaction may not take
place.</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stop-over</ent><br/
<ent>Stopover</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Stop"o`ver</hw>, <hw>Stop"-o`ver</hw></mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Permitting one to stop over; <as>as, a
<ex>stop-over</ex> check or ticket</as>. See <cref>To stop over</cref>,
under <er>Stop</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stop-over</ent><br/
<ent>Stopover</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Stop"o`ver</hw>, <hw>Stop"-o`ver</hw></mhw>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>the act or privilege of stopping over; stopping at a
station or airport beyond the time of the departure of the train or
airplane on which one came, with the purpose of continuing one's journey
on a subsequent train or airplane; the temporary interruption of one's
journey.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a brief visit.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoppage</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"page</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of stopping,
or arresting progress, motion, or action; also, the state of being
stopped; <as>as, the <ex>stoppage</ex> of the circulation of the blood;
the <ex>stoppage</ex> of commerce.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopped</ent><br/
<hw>Stopped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld>
<def>Made by complete closure of the mouth organs; shut; -- said of
certain consonants (<xex>p</xex>, <xex>b</xex>, <xex>t</xex>,
<xex>d</xex>, etc.).</def> <rj><au>H. Sweet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- glottal stop? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopper</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
stops, closes, shuts, or hinders; that which stops or obstructs; that
which closes or fills a vent or hole in a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A short piece of rope having a
knot at one or both ends, with a lanyard under the knot, -- used to
secure something.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A name to several trees of the
genus Eugenia, found in Florida and the West Indies; <as>as, the red
<ex>stopper</ex></as>. See <er>Eugenia</er>.</def> <rj><au>C. S.
Sargent.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ring stopper</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a short
rope or chain passing through the anchor ring, to secure the anchor to
the cathead.</cd> -- <col><b>Stopper bolt</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>,
<cd>a large ringbolt in a ship's deck, to which the deck stoppers are
hooked.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopper</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"per</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stoppered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stoppering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To close or secure with a
stopper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopping</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Material
for filling a cavity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A partition or door to direct or
prevent a current of air.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A pad or poultice of dung or other
material applied to a horse's hoof to keep it moist.</def>
<rj><au>Youatt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopping-out</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"ping-out`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A method
adopted in etching, to keep the acid from those parts which are already
sufficiently corroded, by applying varnish or other covering matter with
a brush, but allowing the acid to act on the other parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopple</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>st<oum/pfel</ets>, <ets>st<oum/psel</ets>. See <er>Stop</er>,
<pos>n.</pos> & <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>That which stops or closes
the mouth of a vessel; a stopper; <as>as, a glass <ex>stopple</ex>; a
cork <ex>stopple</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopple</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"ple</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stoppled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stoppling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To close the mouth of anything
with a stopple, or as with a stopple.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stopship</ent><br/
<hw>Stop"ship`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A remora. It was fabled to stop ships by attaching itself to
them.</def> <rj><au>Sylvester.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stor</ent><br/
<hw>Stor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Stoor</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storage</ent><br/
<hw>Stor"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of depositing in a store or warehouse for safe keeping; also, the safe
keeping of goods in a warehouse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Space for the safe keeping of goods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The price changed for keeping goods in a
store.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Storage battery</b></col>. <fld>(Physics)</fld> <cd>See
the Note under <er>Battery</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storax</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"rax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>storax</ets>,
<ets>styrax</ets>, Gr. <?/. Cf. <er>Styrax</er>.]</ety> <def>Any one of
a number of similar complex resins obtained from the bark of several
trees and shrubs of the Styrax family. The most common of these is
<stype>liquid storax</stype>, a brown or gray semifluid substance of an
agreeable aromatic odor and balsamic taste, sometimes used in perfumery,
and in medicine as an expectorant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A yellow aromatic honeylike substance, resembling, and
often confounded with, storax, is obtained from the American sweet gum
tree (<spn>Liquidambar styraciflua</spn>), and is much used as a chewing
gum, called <xex>sweet gum</xex>, and <xex>liquid storax</xex>. Cf.
<er>Liquidambar</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Store</ent><br/
<hw>Store</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stor</ets>,
<ets>stoor</ets>, OF. <ets>estor</ets>, provisions, supplies, fr.
<ets>estorer</ets> to store. See <er>Store</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is accumulated, or massed together; a source
from which supplies may be drawn; hence, an abundance; a great quantity,
or a great number.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ships are fraught with <qex>store</qex> of victuals.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With <qex>store</qex> of ladies, whose bright eyes<br/
Rain influence, and give the prize.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place of deposit for goods, esp. for large
quantities; a storehouse; a warehouse; a magazine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any place where goods are sold, whether by wholesale
or retail; a shop.</def> <mark>[U.S. & British Colonies]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Articles, especially of food,
accumulated for some specific object; supplies, as of provisions, arms,
ammunition, and the like; <as>as, the <ex>stores</ex> of an army, of a
ship, of a family</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His swine, his horse, his <qex>stoor</qex>, and his poultry.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In store</b></col>, <cd>in a state of accumulation; in
keeping; hence, in a state of readiness.</cd> <ldquo/I have better news
in <xex>store</xex> for thee.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>Store
clothes</b></col>, clothing purchased at a shop or store; -- in
distinction from that which is <xex>home-made</xex>. <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Store pay</b></col>, payment for goods or work
in articles from a shop or store, instead of money. <mark>[U.S.]</mark>
-- <col><b>To set store by</b></col>, <cd>to value greatly; to have a
high appreciation of.</cd> -- <col><b>To tell no store of</b></col>,
<cd>to make no account of; to consider of no importance.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Fund; supply; abundance; plenty; accumulation;
provision.</syn> -- <usage><er>Store</er>, <er>Shop</er>. The English
call the place where goods are sold (however large or splendid it may
be) a <xex>shop</xex>, and confine the word <xex>store</xex> to its
original meaning; viz., a warehouse, or place where goods are
<xex>stored</xex>. In America the word <xex>store</xex> is applied to
all places, except the smallest, where goods are sold. In some British
colonies the word <xex>store</xex> is used as in the United
States.</usage> <-- also syn. = stock --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In his needy <qex>shop</qex> a tortoise hung,<br/
An alligator stuffed, and other skins<br/
Of ill-shaped fishes; and about his shelves<br/
A beggarly account of empty boxes.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sulphurous and nitrous foam, . . . <br/
Concocted and adjusted, they reduced<br/
To blackest grain, and into <qex>store</qex> conveyed.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Store</ent><br/
<hw>Store</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Accumulated; hoarded.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Store</ent><br/
<hw>Store</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stored</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Storing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>storen</ets>, OF.
<ets>estorer</ets> to construct, restore, store, LL.
<ets>staurare</ets>, for L. <ets>instaurare</ets> to renew, restore;
<ets>in + staurare</ets> (in comp.) Cf. <er>Instore</er>,
<er>Instaurate</er>, <er>Restore</er>, <er>Story</er> a floor.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To collect as a reserved supply; to accumulate; to lay
away.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dora <qex>stored</qex> what little she could save.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To furnish; to supply; to replenish; esp., to stock
or furnish against a future time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her mind with thousand virtues <qex>stored</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wise Plato said the world with men was <qex>stored</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Having <qex>stored</qex> a pond of four acres with carps, tench,
and other fish.</q> <rj><qau>Sir M. Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To deposit in a store, warehouse, or other building,
for preservation; to warehouse; <as>as, to <ex>store</ex>
goods</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stored</ent><br/
<hw>Stored</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Collected or
accumulated as a reserve supply; <as>as, <ex>stored</ex>
electricity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is charged with <qex>stored</qex> virtue.</q>
<rj><qau>Bagehot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storehouse</ent><br/
<hw>Store"house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
building for keeping goods of any kind, especially provisions; a
magazine; a repository; a warehouse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Joseph opened all the <qex>storehouses</qex>, and sold unto
Egyptians.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xli. 56.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Scripture of God is a <qex>storehouse</qex> abounding with
estimable treasures of wisdom and knowledge.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A mass or quality laid up.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storekeeper</ent><br/
<hw>Store"keep`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
man in charge of stores or goods of any kind; <as>as, a naval
<ex>storekeeper</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who keeps a <ldquo/store;<rdquo/ a shopkeeper.
See 1st <er>Store</er>, 3.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storer</ent><br/
<hw>Stor"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who lays up or
forms a store.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storeroom</ent><br/
<hw>Store"room`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Room in a
storehouse or repository; a room in which articles are stored.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storeship</ent><br/
<hw>Store"ship`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vessel used to
carry naval stores for a fleet, garrison, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storey</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"rey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Story</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storge</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stor"ge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/, <?/, to love.]</ety> <def>Parental affection; the instinctive
affection which animals have for their young.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storial</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Historical.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storied</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ried</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Story</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Told in a story.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having a history; interesting from the stories which
pertain to it; venerable from the associations of the past.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some greedy minion, or imperious wife,<br/
The trophied arches, <qex>storied</qex> halls, invade.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Can <qex>storied</qex> urn, or animated bust,<br/
Back to its mansion call the fleeting breath?</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Having (such or so many) stories; -- chiefly in
composition; <as>as, a two-<ex>storied</ex> house</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storier</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ri*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A relater of
stories; an historian.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Peacock.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storify</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ri*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Story</ets>
+ <ets>-fy</ets>.]</ety> <def>To form or tell stories of; to narrate or
describe in a story.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stork</ent><br/
<hw>Stork</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>storc</ets>;
akin to G. <ets>storch</ets>, OHG. <ets>storah</ets>, Icel.
<ets>storkr</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>stork</ets>, and perhaps to Gr. <?/ a
vulture.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
large wading birds of the family <fam>Ciconidae</fam>, having long legs
and a long, pointed bill. They are found both in the Old World and in
America, and belong to <gen>Ciconia</gen> and several allied genera. The
European white stork (<spn>Ciconia alba</spn>) is the best known. It
commonly makes its nests on the top of a building, a chimney, a church
spire, or a pillar. The black stork (<spn>Ciconia nigra</spn>) is native
of Asia, Africa, and Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Black-necked stork</b></col>, <cd>the East Indian
jabiru.</cd> -- <col><b>Hair-crested stork</b></col>, <cd>the smaller
adjutant of India (<spn>Leptoptilos Javanica</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Giant stork</b></col>, <cd>the adjutant.</cd> -- <col><b>Marabou
stork</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Marabou</er>. -- Saddle-billed stork, the
African jabiru. See <er>Jabiru</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stork's
bill</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>any plant of the genus
<gen>Pelargonium</gen>; -- so called in allusion to the beaklike
prolongation of the axis of the receptacle of its flower. See
<er>Pelargonium</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stork-billed</ent><br/
<hw>Stork"-billed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a bill
like that of the stork.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storm</ent><br/
<hw>Storm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>storm</ets>;
akin to D. <ets>storm</ets>, G. <ets>sturm</ets>, Icel.
<ets>stormr</ets>; and perhaps to Gr. <?/ assault, onset, Skr.
<ets>s<?/</ets> to flow, to hasten, or perhaps to L. <ets>sternere</ets>
to strew, prostrate (cf. <er>Stratum</er>). <root/166.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A violent disturbance of the atmosphere, attended by
wind, rain, snow, hail, or thunder and lightning; hence, often, a heavy
fall of rain, snow, or hail, whether accompanied with wind or
not.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We hear this fearful tempest sing,<br/
Yet seek no shelter to avoid the <qex>storm</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A violent agitation of human society; a civil,
political, or domestic commotion; sedition, insurrection, or war;
violent outbreak; clamor; tumult.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will stir up in England some black <qex>storm</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her sister<br/
Began to scold and raise up such a <qex>storm</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A heavy shower or fall, any adverse outburst of
tumultuous force; violence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A brave man struggling in the <qex>storms</qex> of fate.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A violent assault on a fortified
place; a furious attempt of troops to enter and take a fortified place
by scaling the walls, forcing the gates, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Storm</xex> is often used in the formation of
self-explained compounds; as, <xex>storm</xex>-presaging,
<xex>storm</xex>proof, <xex>storm</xex>-tossed, and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Anticyclonic storm</b></col> <fld>(Meteor.)</fld>, <cd>a
storm characterized by a central area of high atmospheric pressure, and
having a system of winds blowing spirally outward in a direction
contrary to that cyclonic storms. It is attended by low temperature, dry
air, infrequent precipitation, and often by clear sky. Called also
<altname>high-area storm</altname>, <altname>anticyclone</altname>. When
attended by high winds, snow, and freezing temperatures such storms have
various local names, as <stype>blizzard</stype>, <stype>wet
norther</stype>, <stype>purga</stype>, <stype>buran</stype>, etc.</cd>
-- <col><b>Cyclonic storm</b></col>. <fld>(Meteor.)</fld> <cd>A cyclone,
or low-area storm. See <er>Cyclone</er>, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Magnetic
storm</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Magnetic</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Storm-and-stress period</b></col> <ety>[a translation of G.
<ets>sturm und drang periode</ets>]</ety>, <cd>a designation given to
the literary agitation and revolutionary development in Germany under
the lead of Goethe and Schiller in the latter part of the 18th
century.</cd> -- <col><b>Storm center</b></col> <fld>(Meteorol.)</fld>,
<cd>the center of the area covered by a storm, especially by a storm of
large extent.</cd> -- <col><b>Storm door</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>,
<cd>an extra outside door to prevent the entrance of wind, cold, rain,
etc.; -- usually removed in summer.</cd><-- or replaced with a screen
door; storm and screen door. --> -- <col><b>Storm path</b></col>
<fld>(Meteorol.)</fld>, <cd>the course over which a storm, or storm
center, travels.</cd> -- <col><b>Storm petrel</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Stormy petrel</cref>, under
<er>Petrel</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Storm sail</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>any one of a number of strong, heavy sails that
are bent and set in stormy weather.</cd> -- <col><b>Storm
scud</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under <er>Cloud</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Tempest; violence; agitation; calamity.</syn> --
<usage><er>Storm</er>, <er>Tempest</er>. <xex>Storm</xex> is violent
agitation, a commotion of the elements by wind, etc., but not
necessarily implying the fall of anything from the clouds. Hence, to
call a mere fall or rain without wind a <xex>storm</xex> is a departure
from the true sense of the word. A <xex>tempest</xex> is a sudden and
violent storm, such as those common on the coast of Italy, where the
term originated, and is usually attended by a heavy rain, with lightning
and thunder.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Storms</qex> beat, and rolls the main;<br/
O! beat those <qex>storms</qex>, and roll the seas, in vain.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What at first was called a gust, the same<br/
Hath now a <qex>storm's</qex>, anon a <qex>tempest's</qex> name.</q>
<rj><qau>Donne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storm</ent><br/
<hw>Storm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stormed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Storming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To assault; to
attack, and attempt to take, by scaling walls, forcing gates, breaches,
or the like; <as>as, to <ex>storm</ex> a fortified town</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storm</ent><br/
<hw>Storm</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS.
<ets>styrman</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To raise a tempest.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To blow with violence; also, to rain, hail, snow, or
the like, usually in a violent manner, or with high wind; -- used
impersonally; <as>as, it <ex>storms</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To rage; to be in a violent passion; to
fume.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The master <qex>storms</qex>, the lady scolds.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storm-beat</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"-beat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Beaten, injured,
or impaired by storms.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stormcock</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"cock`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The missel thrush.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
fieldfare.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The green woodpecker.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stormfinch</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"finch`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The storm petrel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stormful</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding with
storms.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>stormful</xex> east.<rdquo/
<au>Carlyle.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Storm"ful*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stormglass</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"glass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A glass vessel,
usually cylindrical, filled with a solution which is sensitive to
atmospheric changes, indicating by a clouded appearance, rain, snow,
etc., and by clearness, fair weather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stormily</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stormy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storminess</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being stormy;
tempestuousness; boisterousness; impetuousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storming</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Storm</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Storming party</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a party
assigned to the duty of making the first assault in storming a
fortress.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stormless</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without storms.</def>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stormwind</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"wind`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A heavy wind; a
wind that brings a storm; the blast of a storm.</def>
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stormy</ent><br/
<hw>Storm"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stormier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stormiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Characterized by, or
proceeding from, a storm; subject to storms; agitated with furious
winds; boisterous; tempestuous; <as>as, a <ex>stormy</ex> season; a
<ex>stormy</ex> day or week.</as></def> <ldquo/Beyond the
<xex>stormy</xex> Hebrides.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Proceeding from violent agitation or fury; <as>as, a
<ex>stormy</ex> sound; <ex>stormy</ex> shocks.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Violent; passionate; rough; <as>as, <ex>stormy</ex>
passions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stormy</qex> chiefs of a desert but extensive domain.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storthing</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stor"thing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Norw.
<ets>storting</ets>; <ets>stor</ets> great + <ets>ting</ets> court,
court of justice; cf. Dan. <ets>ting</ets>, <ets>thing</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The Parliament of Norway, chosen by indirect election once in three
years, but holding annual sessions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storven</ent><br/
<hw>Stor"ven</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos>
of <er>Starve</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Story</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stories</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estor<eacute/</ets>, <ets>estor<eacute/e</ets>, built, erected, p.
p. of <ets>estorer</ets> to build, restore, to store. See
<er>Store</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>A set of rooms on the same
floor or level; a floor, or the space between two floors. Also, a
horizontal division of a building's exterior considered architecturally,
which need not correspond exactly with the stories within.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>storey</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A <xex>story</xex> comprehends the distance from one
floor to another; as, a <xex>story</xex> of nine or ten feet elevation.
The spaces between floors are numbered in order, from below upward; as,
the lower, second, or third <xex>story</xex>; a house of one
<xex>story</xex>, of two <xex>stories</xex>, of five
<xex>stories</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Story post</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a vertical
post used to support a floor or superincumbent wall.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Story</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>storie</ets>, OF.
<ets>estoire</ets>, F. <ets>histoire</ets>, fr. L. <ets>historia</ets>.
See <er>History</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A narration or recital of
that which has occurred; a description of past events; a history; a
statement; a record.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One malcontent who did indeed get a name in <qex>story</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Venice, with its unique city and its Impressive
<qex>story</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ed. Rev.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The four great monarchies make the subject of ancient
<qex>story</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The relation of an incident or minor event; a short
narrative; a tale; especially, a fictitious narrative less elaborate
than a novel; a short romance.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A euphemism or child's word for <ldquo/a lie;<rdquo/
a fib; <as>as, to tell a <ex>story</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Story</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ry</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Storied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Storying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To tell in historical relation;
to make the subject of a story; to narrate or describe in
story.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How worthy he is I will leave to appear hereafter, rather than
<qex>story</qex> him in his own hearing.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is <qex>storied</qex> of the brazen colossus in Rhodes, that it
was seventy cubits high.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Wilkins.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Storybook</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ry*book`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A book containing
stories, or short narratives, either true or false.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Story-teller</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ry-tell`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who tells stories; a narrator of anecdotes,incidents, or fictitious
tales; <as>as, an amusing <ex>story-teller</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An historian; -- in contempt.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A euphemism or child's word for <ldquo/a
liar.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Story-telling</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ry-tell`ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being accustomed to tell
stories.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or practice of
telling stories.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Story-writer</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ry-writ`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who writes short stories, as for magazines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An historian; a chronicler.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Rathums, the <xex>story-writer</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>1 Esdr. ii. 17.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stot</ent><br/
<hw>Stot</hw> <pr>(st<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stotte</ets> a hack, jade, or worthless horse; cf. Sw.
<ets>stut</ets> a bull, Dan. <ets>stud</ets> an ox. Cf.
<er>Stoat</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A horse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer. Thorold Rogers.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A young bull or ox, especially one three years
old.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1421 --></p>

<p><ent>Stote</ent><br/
<hw>Stote</hw> <pr>(st<omac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Stoat</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stound</ent><br/
<hw>Stound</hw> <pr>(stound)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Astound</er>, <er>Stun</er>.]</ety> <def>To be in pain or
sorrow.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stound</ent><br/
<hw>Stound</hw> <pr>(stound)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stound</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>Stunned.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stound</ent><br/
<hw>Stound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sudden, severe pain or
grief; peril; alarm.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Astonishment; amazement.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser. Gay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stound</ent><br/
<hw>Stound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>stund</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>stond</ets>, G. <ets>stunde</ets>, Icel. <ets>stund</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Hour; time; season.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A brief space of time; a moment.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In a stound</b></col>, <cd>suddenly.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stound</ent><br/
<hw>Stound</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <def>A
vessel for holding small beer.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoup</ent><br/
<hw>Stoup</hw> <pr>(st<oomac/p)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stoop</er> a vessel.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A flagon; a vessel or
measure for liquids.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A basin at the entrance of Roman
Catholic churches for containing the holy water with which those who
enter, dipping their fingers in it, cross themselves; -- called also
<altname>holy-water stoup</altname> or <altname>holy-water
font</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stour</ent><br/
<hw>Stour</hw> <pr>(stour <it>or</it> st<oomac/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OF. <ets>estour</ets>, <ets>estor</ets>, tumult, combat, of
Teutonic origin. See <er>Storm</er>.]</ety> <def>A battle or tumult;
encounter; combat; disturbance; passion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Fairfax.</au> <ldquo/That woeful <xex>stowre</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She that helmed was in starke <qex>stours</qex> [fierce
conflicts].</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stour</ent><br/
<hw>Stour</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stoor</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Tall; strong; stern.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stout</ent><br/
<hw>Stout</hw> <pr>(stout)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Stouter</adjf>
<pr>(stout"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Stoutest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[D. <ets>stout</ets> bold (or OF.
<ets>estout</ets> bold, proud, of Teutonic origin); akin to AS.
<ets>stolt</ets>, G. <ets>stolz</ets>, and perh. to E.
<ets>stilt</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Strong; lusty; vigorous;
robust; sinewy; muscular; hence, firm; resolute; dauntless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With hearts stern and <qex>stout</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>stouter</qex> champion never handled sword.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He lost the character of a bold, <qex>stout</qex>, magnanimous
man.</q> <rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lords all stand<br/
To clear their cause, most resolutely <qex>stout</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Proud; haughty; arrogant; hard.</def>
<mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your words have been <qex>stout</qex> against me.</q>
<rj><qau>Mal. iii. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Commonly . . . they that be rich are lofty and
<qex>stout</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Latimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Firm; tough; materially strong; enduring; <as>as, a
<ex>stout</ex> vessel, stick, string, or cloth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Large; bulky; corpulent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Stout</er>, <er>Corpulent</er>,
<er>Portly</er>.</syn> <usage> <xex>Corpulent</xex> has reference simply
to a superabundance or excess of flesh. <xex>Portly</xex> implies a kind
of stoutness or corpulence which gives a dignified or imposing
appearance. <xex>Stout</xex>, in our early writers (as in the English
Bible), was used chiefly or wholly in the sense of <xex>strong</xex> or
<xex>bold</xex>; <as>as, a <ex>stout</ex> champion; a <ex>stout</ex>
heart; a <ex>stout</ex> resistance, etc.</as> At a later period it was
used for <xex>thickset</xex> or <xex>bulky</xex>, and more recently,
especially in England, the idea has been carried still further, so that
Taylor says in his Synonyms: <ldquo/The <xex>stout</xex> man has the
proportions of an ox; he is corpulent, fat, and fleshy in relation to
his size.<rdquo/ In America, <xex>stout</xex> is still commonly used in
the original sense of <xex>strong</xex> as, a <xex>stout</xex> boy; a
<xex>stout</xex> pole.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>stout</ent><br/
<hw>stout</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A strong, dark malt brew having a
higher percentage of hops than porter; strong porter; a popular variety
sold in the U. S. is <stype>Guinness' stout</stype>.</def>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stout-hearted</ent><br/
<hw>Stout"-heart`ed</hw> <pr>(stout"h<aum/rt`<ecr/d)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Having a brave heart; courageous.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Stout"-heart`ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoutish</ent><br/
<hw>Stout"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat stout; somewhat
corpulent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoutly</ent><br/
<hw>Stout"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stout manner; lustily;
boldly; obstinately; <as>as, he <ex>stoutly</ex> defended
himself</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stoutness</ent><br/
<hw>Stout"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of being
stout.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Strength; bulk; courage; force; valor; lustiness;
brawniness; boldness; fortitude; stubbornness.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stovine</ent><br/
<ent>Stovain</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sto"va*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> Also
<hw>Sto"vine</hw> }</mhw>. <ety>[<ets>Stove</ets> (a translation of the
name of the discoverer, <ets>Fourneau</ets> + <ets>-in</ets>,
<ets>-ine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Pharm.)</fld> <def>A substance,
<chform>C14H22O2NCl</chform>, the hydrochloride of an amino compound
containing benzol, used, in solution with strychnine, as a local
anaesthetic, esp. by injection into the sheath of the spinal cord,
producing anaesthesia below the point of introduction. Called also
<altname>amylocaine hydrochloride</altname>. Chemically it is the
hydrochloride of the benzoyl ester of
<chname>1-(dimethylaminomethyl)-1-methyl propanol</chname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stove</ent><br/
<hw>Stove</hw> <pr>(st<omac/v)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Stave</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stove</ent><br/
<hw>Stove</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>stoof</ets> a foot stove,
originally, a heated room, a room for a bath; akin to G.
<ets>stube</ets> room, OHG. <ets>stuba</ets> a heated room, AS.
<ets>stofe</ets>, Icel. <ets>stofa</ets> a room, bathing room, Sw.
<ets>stufva</ets>, <ets>stuga</ets>, a room, Dan. <ets>stue</ets>; of
unknown origin. Cf. <er>Estufa</er>, <er>Stew</er>,
<er>Stufa</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A house or room artificially
warmed or heated; a forcing house, or hothouse; a drying room; --
formerly, designating an artificially warmed dwelling or room, a parlor,
or a bathroom, but now restricted, in this sense, to heated houses or
rooms used for horticultural purposes or in the processes of the
arts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When most of the waiters were commanded away to their supper, the
parlor or <qex>stove</qex> being nearly emptied, in came a company of
musketeers.</q> <rj><qau>Earl of Strafford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How tedious is it to them that live in <qex>stoves</qex> and caves
half a year together, as in Iceland, Muscovy, or under the pole!</q>
<rj><qau>Burton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An apparatus, consisting essentially of a receptacle
for fuel, made of iron, brick, stone, or tiles, and variously
constructed, in which fire is made or kept for warming a room or a
house, or for culinary or other purposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <specif>Hence, in modern dwellings:</specif> <def>An
appliance having a top surface with fittings suitable for heating pots
and pans for cooking, frying, or boiling food, most commonly heated by
gas or electricity, and often combined with an oven in a single unit; a
<cref>cooking stove</cref>. Such units commonly have two to six heating
surfaces, called <part>burners</part>, even if they are heated by
electricity rather than a gas flame.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Cooking stove</b></col>, <cd>a stove with an oven,
opening for pots, kettles, and the like, -- used for cooking.</cd> --
<col><b>Dry stove</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Dry</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Foot stove</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Foot</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Franklin stove</b></col>. <cd>See in the Vocabulary.</cd> --
<col><b>Stove plant</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a plant which
requires artificial heat to make it grow in cold or cold temperate
climates.</cd> -- <col><b>Stove plate</b></col>, <cd>thin iron castings
for the parts of stoves.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stove</ent><br/
<hw>Stove</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stoved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stoving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To keep warm, in a
house or room, by artificial heat; <as>as, to <ex>stove</ex> orange
trees</as>.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To heat or dry, as in a stove; <as>as, to
<ex>stove</ex> feathers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stovehouse</ent><br/
<hw>Stove"house`</hw> <pr>(st<omac/v"hous`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
hothouse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stovepipe</ent><br/
<hw>Stove"pipe`</hw> <pr>(st<omac/v"p<imac/p`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Pipe made of sheet iron in length and angular or curved pieces
fitting together, -- used to connect a portable stove with a chimney
flue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stovepipe hat</b></col>, <cd>A tall silk hat with a brim,
worn commonly as an item of formal dress by gentlemen in the late
1800's.</cd> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stover</ent><br/
<hw>Sto"ver</hw> <pr>(st<omac/"v<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>estoveir</ets>, <ets>estovoir</ets>, necessity, provisions,
properly an inf., <ldquo/to be necessary.<rdquo/ Cf.
<er>Estovers</er>.]</ety> <def>Fodder for cattle, especially straw or
coarse hay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where live nibbling sheep,<br/
And flat meads thatched with <qex>stover</qex> them to keep.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thresh barley as yet but as need shall require,<br/
Fresh threshed for <qex>stover</qex> thy cattle desire.</q>
<rj><qau>Tusser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stow</ent><br/
<hw>Stow</hw> <pr>(st<omac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stowed</conjf> <pr>(st<omac/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Stowing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stowen</ets>, fr. <ets>stowe</ets> a place, AS. <ets>stow</ets>;
cf. Icel. eld<ets>st<omac/</ets>a fireplace, hearth, OFries.
<ets>st<omac/</ets>, and E. <ets>stand</ets>. <root/163.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To place or arrange in a compact mass; to put in its
proper place, or in a suitable place; to pack; <as>as, to
<ex>stow</ex>bags, bales, or casks in a ship's hold; to <ex>stow</ex>
hay in a mow; to <ex>stow</ex> sheaves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some <qex>stow</qex> their oars, or stop the leaky sides.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put away in some place; to hide; to
lodge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Foul thief! where hast thou <qex>stowed</qex> my daughter?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To arrange anything compactly in; to fill, by
packing closely; <as>as, to <ex>stow</ex> a box, car, or the hold of a
ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stowage</ent><br/
<hw>Stow"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
or method of stowing; <as>as, the <ex>stowage</ex> of provisions in a
vessel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Room in which things may be stowed.</def>
<rj><au>Cook.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In every vessel is <qex>stowage</qex> for immense treasures.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The state of being stowed, or put away.</def>
<ldquo/To have them in safe <xex>stowage</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Things stowed or packed.</def> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Money paid for stowing goods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stowaway</ent><br/
<hw>Stow"a*way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who conceals
himself board of a vessel about to leave port, or on a railway train, in
order to obtain a free passage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stowboard</ent><br/
<hw>Stow"board</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place into which
rubbish is put.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stowbord</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stowce</ent><br/
<hw>Stowce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A windlass.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A wooden landmark,
to indicate possession of mining land.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stowing</ent><br/
<hw>Stow"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A
method of working in which the waste is packed into the space formed by
excavating the vein.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stowre</ent><br/
<hw>Stowre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Stour</er>,
<pos>a.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stowre</ent><br/
<hw>Stowre</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Stour</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strabism</ent><br/
<hw>Stra"bism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Strabismus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strabismometer</ent><br/
<hw>Stra`bis*mom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Strabismus</ets> + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An instrument for measuring the amount of
strabismus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strabismus</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*bis"mus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ to squint, fr. <?/ distorted, squinting.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An affection of one or both eyes, in which the
optic axes can not be directed to the same object, -- a defect due
either to undue contraction or to undue relaxation of one or more of the
muscles which move the eyeball; squinting; cross-eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strabotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*bot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
squinting + <?/ to cut.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>The operation for
the removal of squinting by the division of such muscles as distort the
eyeball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straddle</ent><br/
<hw>Strad"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Straddled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Straddling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq.
from the root of <ets>stride</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To part the
legs wide; to stand or to walk with the legs far apart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stand with the ends staggered; -- said of the
spokes of a wagon wheel where they join the hub.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straddle</ent><br/
<hw>Strad"dle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To place one leg on one side
and the other on the other side of; to stand or sit astride of; <as>as,
to <ex>straddle</ex> a fence or a horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straddle</ent><br/
<hw>Strad"dle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of standing,
sitting, or walking, with the feet far apart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The position, or the distance between the feet, of
one who straddles; as, a wide <xex>straddle</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A stock option giving the holder the double
privilege of a <ldquo/put<rdquo/ and a <ldquo/call,<rdquo/ <it>i.
e.</it>, securing to the buyer of the option the right either to demand
of the seller at a certain price, within a certain time, certain
securities, or to require him to take at the same price, and within the
same time, the same securities.</def> <mark>[Broker's Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straddling</ent><br/
<hw>Strad"dling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Applied to spokes
when they are arranged alternately in two circles in the hub. See
<er>Straddle</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, and <er>Straddle</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, 3.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stradometrical</ent><br/
<hw>Strad`o*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>strada</ets> street or road + E. <ets>metrical</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Of, or relating to, the measuring of streets or roads.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straggle</ent><br/
<hw>Strag"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Straggled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Straggling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq.
of OE. <ets>straken</ets> to roam, to stroke. See <er>Stroke</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To wander from the direct
course or way; to rove; to stray; to wander from the line of march or
desert the line of battle; <as>as, when troops are on the march, the men
should not <ex>straggle</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wander at large; to roam idly about; to
ramble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wolf spied out a <qex>straggling</qex> kid.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To escape or stretch beyond proper limits, as the
branches of a plant; to spread widely apart; to shoot too far or widely
in growth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trim off the small, superfluous branches on each side of the hedge
that <qex>straggle too far out</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be dispersed or separated; to occur at
intervals.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Straggling</xex> pistol shots.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They came between Scylla and Charybdis and the
<qex>straggling</qex> rocks.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straggle</ent><br/
<hw>Strag"gle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of straggling.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straggler</ent><br/
<hw>Strag"gler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
straggles, or departs from the direct or proper course, or from the
company to which he belongs; one who falls behind the rest; one who
rambles without any settled direction.</def><-- they shot all the
stragglers --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A roving vagabond.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something that shoots, or spreads out, beyond the
rest, or too far; an exuberant growth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let thy hand supply the pruning knife,<br/
And crop luxuriant <qex>stragglers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Something that stands alone or by itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straggling</ent><br/
<hw>Strag"gling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Straggle</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stragglingly</ent><br/
<hw>Strag"gling*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a straggling
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stragulum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strag"u*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stragula</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a spread or covering,
from <ets>sternere</ets> to spread out.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The mantle, or pallium, of a bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight</ent><br/
<hw>Straight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>A variant of
<er>Strait</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Egypt is a long country, but it is <qex>straight</qex>, that is to
say, narrow.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J. Mandeville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight</ent><br/
<hw>Straight</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Straighter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Straightest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>strei<?/t</ets>,
properly p. p. of <ets>strecchen</ets> to stretch, AS.
<ets>streht</ets>, p. p. of <ets>streccan</ets> to stretch, to extend.
See <er>Stretch</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Right, in a mathematical
sense; passing from one point to another by the nearest course; direct;
not deviating or crooked; <as>as, a <ex>straight</ex> line or course; a
<ex>straight</ex> piece of timber</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the crooked shall be made <qex>straight</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xl. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are many several sorts of crooked lines, but there is only
one which is <qex>straight</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Approximately straight; not much
curved; <as>as, <ex>straight</ex> ribs are such as pass from the base of
a leaf to the apex, with a small curve</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>Composed of cards which
constitute a regular sequence, as the ace, king, queen, jack, and
ten-spot; <as>as, a <ex>straight</ex> hand; a <ex>straight</ex>
flush.</as></def> <-- previously called also sequence, which see.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Conforming to justice and rectitude; not deviating
from truth or fairness; upright; <as>as, <ex>straight</ex>
dealing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Unmixed; undiluted; <as>as, to take liquor
<ex>straight</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Making no exceptions or deviations in one's support
of the organization and candidates of a political party; <as>as, a
<ex>straight</ex> Republican; a <ex>straight</ex> Democrat</as>; also,
containing the names of all the regularly nominated candidates of a
party and no others; <as>as, a <ex>straight</ex> ballot</as>.</def>
<mark>[Political Cant, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Straight arch</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a form of
arch in which the intrados is straight, but with its joints drawn
radially, as in a common arch.</cd> -- <col><b>A straight
face</b></col>, <cd>one giving no evidence of merriment or other
emotion.</cd> -- <col><b>A straight line</b></col>. <cd><ldquo/That
which lies evenly between its extreme points.<rdquo/ <au>Euclid.</au>
<ldquo/The shortest line between two points.<rdquo/ <au>Chauvenet.</au>
<ldquo/A line which has the same direction through its whole
length.<rdquo/ <au>Newcomb.</au></cd> -- <col><b>Straight-way
valve</b></col>, <cd>a valve which, when opened widely, affords a
straight passageway, as for water.</cd></cs> <-- the straight and
narrow, proper ethical conduct; -- used esp. in the phrase <it>walk the
straight and narrow</it>. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight</ent><br/
<hw>Straight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a straight
manner; directly; rightly; forthwith; immediately; <as>as, the arrow
went <ex>straight</ex> to the mark</as>.</def> <ldquo/Floating
<xex>straight</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I know thy generous temper well;<br/
Fling but the appearance of dishonor on it,<br/
It <qex>straight</qex> takes fire, and mounts into a blaze.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Everything was going on <qex>straight</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Black.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight</ent><br/
<hw>Straight</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Poker)</fld> <def>A hand of five
cards in consecutive order as to value; a sequence. When they are of one
suit, it is calles <xex>straight flush</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight</ent><br/
<hw>Straight</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To straighten.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>A Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightedge</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"edge`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A board, or
piece of wood or metal, having one edge perfectly straight, -- used to
ascertain whether a line is straight or a surface even, and for drawing
straight lines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straighten</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Straighted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Straighting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make
straight; to reduce from a crooked to a straight form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make right or correct; to reduce to order;
<as>as, to <ex>straighten</ex> one's affairs; to <ex>straighten</ex> an
account</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To straighten one's face</b></col>, <cd>to cease laughing
or smiling, etc., and compose one's features.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straighten</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"en</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>A variant of
<er>Straiten</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightener</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"en*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or
that which, straightens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightforth</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"forth`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Straightway.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightforward</ent><br/
<hw>Straight`for"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Proceeding
in a straight course or manner; not deviating; honest; frank.</def> --
<def2><pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a straightforward manner.</def></def2> --
<wordforms><wf>Straight`for"ward*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Straight`for"ward*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straighthorn</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"horn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>An orthoceras.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight-joint</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"-joint`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>Having straight joints.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Applied to a floor the boards of which are so laid that the joints
form a continued line transverse to the length of the boards
themselves.</def> <au>Brandle & C.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In the United
States, applied to planking or flooring put together without the tongue
and groove, the pieces being laid edge to edge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight-lined</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"-lined`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having
straight lines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightly</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a right line; not
crookedly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightly</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>A variant of
<er>Straitly</er>. See 1st <er>Straight</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightness</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality, condition, or
state, of being straight; <as>as, the <ex>straightness</ex> of a
path</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightness</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A variant of
<er>Straitness</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight-out</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"-out`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Acting without
concealment, obliquity, or compromise; hence, unqualified;
thoroughgoing.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Straight-out</qex> and generous indignation.</q>
<rj><qau>Mrs. Stowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight-pight</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"-pight`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Straight in
form or upright in position; erect.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straight-spoken</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"-spo`ken</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Speaking
with directness; plain-spoken.</def> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightway</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Immediately;
without loss of time; without delay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He took the damsel by the hand, and said unto her, Talitha cumi. .
. . And <qex>straightway</qex> the damsel arose.</q> <rj><qau>Mark v.
41,42.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straightways</ent><br/
<hw>Straight"ways`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>Straightway.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straik</ent><br/
<hw>Straik</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A strake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strain</ent><br/
<hw>Strain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Strene</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Race; stock; generation;
descent; family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is of a noble <qex>strain</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With animals and plants a cross between different varieties, or
between individuals of the same variety but of another
<qex>strain</qex>, gives vigor and fertility to the offspring.</q>
<rj><qau>Darwin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hereditary character, quality, or
disposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Intemperance and lust breed diseases, which, propogated, spoil the
<qex>strain</qex> of nation.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Rank; a sort.</def> <ldquo/The common
<xex>strain</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Hort.)</fld> <def>A cultural subvariety that is
only slightly differentiated.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strain</ent><br/
<hw>Strain</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Strained</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Straining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>estraindre</ets>,
<ets>estreindre</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/treindre</ets>, L.
<ets>stringere</ets> to draw or bind tight; probably akin to Gr. <?/ a
halter, <?/ that which is squeezwd out, a drop, or perhaps to E.
<ets>strike</ets>. Cf. <er>Strangle</er>, <er>Strike</er>,
<er>Constrain</er>, <er>District</er>, <er>Strait</er>, <pos>a.</pos>
<er>Stress</er>, <er>Strict</er>, <er>Stringent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To draw with force; to extend with great effort; to stretch;
<as>as, to <ex>strain</ex> a rope; to <ex>strain</ex> the shrouds of a
ship; to <ex>strain</ex> the cords of a musical instrument</as>.</def>
<ldquo/To <xex>strain</xex> his fetters with a stricter care.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>To act upon, in any way, so as to
cause change of form or volume, as forces on a beam to bend
it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To exert to the utmost; to ply vigorously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He sweats,<br/
<qex>Strains</qex> his young nerves.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>strain</qex> their warbling throats<br/
To welcome in the spring.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To stretch beyond its proper limit; to do violence
to, in the matter of intent or meaning; <as>as, to <ex>strain</ex> the
law in order to convict an accused person</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There can be no other meaning in this expression, however some may
pretend to <qex>strain</qex> it.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To injure by drawing, stretching, or the exertion of
force; <as>as, the gale <ex>strained</ex> the timbers of the
ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To injure in the muscles or joints by causing to
make too strong an effort; to harm by overexertion; to sprain; <as>as,
to <ex>strain</ex> a horse by overloading; to <ex>strain</ex> the wrist;
to <ex>strain</ex> a muscle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Prudes decayed about may track,<br/
<qex>Strain</qex> their necks with looking back.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To squeeze; to press closely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Evander with a close embrace<br/
<qex>Strained</qex> his departing friend.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To make uneasy or unnatural; to produce with
apparent effort; to force; to constrain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He talks and plays with Fatima, but his mirth<br/
Is forced and <qex>strained</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The quality of mercy is not <qex>strained</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To urge with importunity; to press; <as>as, to
<ex>strain</ex> a petition or invitation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Note, if your lady <qex>strain</qex> his entertainment.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To press, or cause to pass, through a strainer, as
through a screen, a cloth, or some porous substance; to purify, or
separate from extraneous or solid matter, by filtration; to filter;
<as>as, to <ex>strain</ex> milk through cloth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To strain a point</b></col>, <cd>to make a special
effort; especially, to do a degree of violence to some principle or to
one's own feelings.</cd> -- <col><b>To strain courtesy</b></col>, <cd>to
go beyond what courtesy requires; to insist somewhat too much upon the
precedence of others; -- often used ironically.</cd>
<au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1422 --></p>

<p><ent>Strain</ent><br/
<hw>Strain</hw> <pr>(str<amac/n)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To make violent efforts.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Straining</xex> with too
weak a wing.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To build his fortune I will <qex>strain</qex> a little.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To percolate; to be filtered; <as>as, water
<ex>straining</ex> through a sandy soil</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strain</ent><br/
<hw>Strain</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of straining, or
the state of being strained.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>A violent effort; an excessive and hurtful exertion
or tension, as of the muscles; <as>as, he lifted the weight with a
<ex>strain</ex>; the <ex>strain</ex> upon a ship's rigging in a
gale;</as> also, the hurt or injury resulting; a sprain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whether any poet of our country since Shakespeare has exerted a
greater variety of powers with less <qex>strain</qex> and less
ostentation.</q> <rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Credit is gained by custom, and seldom recovers a
<qex>strain</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Mech. Physics)</fld> <def>A change of form or
dimensions of a solid or liquid mass, produced by a stress.</def>
<rj><au>Rankine.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A portion of music divided off by
a double bar; a complete musical period or sentence; a movement, or any
rounded subdivision of a movement.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their heavenly harps a lower <qex>strain</qex> began.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any sustained note or movement; a song; a distinct
portion of an ode or other poem; also, the pervading note, or burden, of
a song, poem, oration, book, etc.; theme; motive; manner; style; also, a
course of action or conduct; <as>as, he spoke in a noble
<ex>strain</ex>; there was a <ex>strain</ex> of woe in his story; a
<ex>strain</ex> of trickery appears in his career</as>.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>strain</xex> of gallantry.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such take too high a <qex>strain</qex> at first.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The genius and <qex>strain</qex> of the book of Proverbs.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It [Pilgrim's Progress] seems a novelty, and yet contains<br/
Nothing but sound and honest gospel <qex>strains</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Turn; tendency; inborn disposition. Cf. 1st
<er>Strain</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Because heretics have a <qex>strain</qex> of madness, he applied
her with some corporal chastisements.</q>
<rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strainable</ent><br/
<hw>Strain"a*ble</hw> <pr>(-<adot/*b'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of being strained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Violent in action.</def>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strainably</ent><br/
<hw>Strain"a*bly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Violently.</def>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strained</ent><br/
<hw>Strained</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Subjected
to great or excessive tension; wrenched; weakened; <as>as,
<ex>strained</ex> relations between old friends</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Done or produced with straining or excessive effort;
<as>as, his wit was <ex>strained</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strainer</ent><br/
<hw>Strain"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
strains.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That through which any liquid is passed for
purification or to separate it from solid matter; anything, as a screen
or a cloth, used to strain a liquid; a device of the character of a
sieve or of a filter; specifically, an openwork or perforated screen, as
for the end of the suction pipe of a pump, to prevent large solid bodies
from entering with a liquid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straining</ent><br/
<hw>Strain"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Strain</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Straining piece</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a short
piece of timber in a truss, used to maintain the ends of struts or
rafters, and keep them from slipping. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Queen-post</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straint</ent><br/
<hw>Straint</hw> <pr>(str<amac/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estrainte</ets>, <ets>estreinte</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/trainte</ets>. See 2nd <er>Strain</er>.]</ety>
<def>Overexertion; excessive tension; strain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait</ent><br/
<hw>Strait</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>A variant of
<er>Straight</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait</ent><br/
<hw>Strait</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Straiter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Straitest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>straight</ets>,
<ets>streyt</ets>, <ets>streit</ets>, OF. <ets>estreit</ets>,
<ets>estroit</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/troit</ets>, from L.
<ets>strictus</ets> drawn together, close, tight, p. p. of
<ets>stringere</ets> to draw tight. See 2nd <er>Strait</er>, and cf.
<er>Strict</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Narrow; not broad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Strait</qex> is the gate, and narrow is the way, which
leadeth unto life, and few there be that find it.</q> <rj><qau>Matt.
vii. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Too <qex>strait</qex> and low our cottage doors.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tight; close; closely fitting.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Close; intimate; near; familiar.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>strait</xex> degree of favor.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Strict; scrupulous; rigorous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some certain edicts and some <qex>strait</qex> decrees.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>straitest</qex> sect of our religion.</q> <rj><qau>Acts
xxvi. 5 (Rev. Ver.).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Difficult; distressful; straited.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To make your <qex>strait</qex> circumstances yet
<qex>straiter</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Secker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Parsimonious; niggargly; mean.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I beg cold comfort, and you are so <qex>strait</qex>,<br/
And so ingrateful, you deny me that.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait</ent><br/
<hw>Strait</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Strictly;
rigorously.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait</ent><br/
<hw>Strait</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Straits</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>straight</ets>, <ets>streit</ets>,
OF. <ets>estreit</ets>, <ets>estroit</ets>. See <er>Strait</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A narrow pass or
passage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He brought him through a darksome narrow <qex>strait</qex><br/
To a broad gate all built of beaten gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Honor travels in a <qex>strait</qex> so narrow<br/
Where one but goes abreast.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> Specifically: <fld>(Geog.)</fld> <def>A (comparatively)
narrow passageway connecting two large bodies of water; -- often in the
plural; <as>as, the <ex>strait</ex>, or <ex>straits</ex>, of Gibraltar;
the <ex>straits</ex> of Magellan; the <ex>strait</ex>, or
<ex>straits</ex>, of Mackinaw.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We steered directly through a large outlet which they call a
<qex>strait</qex>, though it be fifteen miles broad.</q> <rj><qau>De
Foe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A neck of land; an isthmus.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A dark <qex>strait</qex> of barren land.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: A condition of narrowness or restriction;
doubt; distress; difficulty; poverty; perplexity; -- sometimes in the
plural; <as>as, reduced to great <ex>straits</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For I am in a <qex>strait</qex> betwixt two.</q> <rj><qau>Phil. i.
23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let no man, who owns a Providence, grow desperate under any
calamity or <qex>strait</qex> whatsoever.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ulysses made use of the pretense of natural infirmity to conceal
the <qex>straits</qex> he was in at that time in his thoughts.</q>
<rj><qau>Broome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait</ent><br/
<hw>Strait</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To put to difficulties.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straiten</ent><br/
<hw>Strait"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Straitened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Straitening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make
strait; to make narrow; hence, to contract; to confine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Waters, when <qex>straitened</qex>, as at the falls of bridges,
give a roaring noise.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In narrow circuit, <qex>straitened</qex> by a foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make tense, or tight; to tighten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>straiten</qex> at each end the cord.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To restrict; to distress or embarrass in respect of
means or conditions of life; -- used chiefly in the past participle; --
as, a man <xex>straitened</xex> in his circumstances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait-handed</ent><br/
<hw>Strait"-hand`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Parsimonious;
sparing; niggardly.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Strait"-hand`ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[R.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait-jacket</ent><br/
<hw>Strait"-jack`et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dress of
strong materials for restraining maniacs or those who are violently
delirious. It has long sleeves, which are closed at the ends, confining
the hands, and may be tied behind the back.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait-laced</ent><br/
<hw>Strait"-laced`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Bound with stays.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let nature have scope to fashion the body as she thinks best; we
have few well-shaped that are <qex>strait-laced</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Restricted; stiff; constrained.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Rigid in opinion; strict in manners or
morals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straitly</ent><br/
<hw>Strait"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a strait manner;
narrowly; strictly; rigorously.</def> <rj><au>Mark i. 43.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Closely; intimately.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straitness</ent><br/
<hw>Strait"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or condition of
being strait; especially, a pinched condition or situation caused by
poverty; <as>as, the <ex>straitness</ex>of their
circumstances</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strait-waistcoat</ent><br/
<hw>Strait"-waist`coat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Strait-jacket</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strake</ent><br/
<hw>Strake</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Strike</er>.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strake</ent><br/
<hw>Strake</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Streak</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A streak.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<au>Spenser.</au><ldquo/White <xex>strake</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Gen. xxx.
37.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An iron band by which the fellies of a wheel are
secured to each other, being not continuous, as the tire is, but made up
of separate pieces.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>One breadth of planks or
plates forming a continuous range on the bottom or sides of a vessel,
reaching from the stem to the stern; a streak.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The planks or plates next the keel are called the
<xex>garboard strakes</xex>; the next, or the heavy strakes at the
bilge, are the <xex>bilge strakes</xex>; the next, from the water line
to the lower port sill, the <xex>wales</xex>; and the upper parts of the
sides, the <xex>sheer strakes</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A trough for washing broken ore,
gravel, or sand; a launder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strale</ent><br/
<hw>Strale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pupil of the eye.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stram</ent><br/
<hw>Stram</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LG.
<ets>strammen</ets> to strain, straiten, stretch, D. <ets>stram</ets>
strained, tight, G. <ets>stramm</ets>.]</ety> <def>To spring or recoil
with violence.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stram</ent><br/
<hw>Stram</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To dash down; to beat.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stramash</ent><br/
<hw>Stram"ash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Stramazoun</er>.]</ety> <def>To strike, beat, or bang; to break; to
destroy.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stramash</ent><br/
<hw>Stram"ash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A turmoil; a broil; a fray; a
fight.</def> <mark>[Scot. & Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stramazoun</ent><br/
<hw>Stram"a*zoun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>estrama<cced/on</ets>, It. <ets>stramazzone</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
direct descending blow with the edge of a sword.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stramineous</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*min"e*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stramineus</ets>, fr. <ets>stramen</ets> straw, fr.
<ets>sternere</ets>, <ets>stratum</ets>, to spread out, to strew.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Strawy; consisting of straw.</def>
<rj><au>Robinson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Chaffy; like straw; straw-colored.</def>
<rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stramonium</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*mo"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.; Cf. F.
<ets>stramoine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A poisonous plant
(<spn>Datura Stramonium</spn>); stinkweed. See <er>Datura</er>, and
<er>Jamestown weed</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stramony</ent><br/
<hw>Stram"o*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Stramonium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strand</ent><br/
<hw>Strand</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably fr. D.
<ets>streen</ets> a skein; akin to G. <ets>str<aum/hne</ets> a skein,
lock of hair, strand of a rope.]</ety> <def>One of the twists, or
strings, as of fibers, wires, etc., of which a rope is
composed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strand</ent><br/
<hw>Strand</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To break a strand of (a
rope).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strand</ent><br/
<hw>Strand</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>strand</ets>; akin to D.,
G., Sw., & Dan. <ets>strand</ets>, Icel. <ets>str<oum/nd</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The shore, especially the beach of a sea, ocean, or large lake;
rarely, the margin of a navigable river.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strand birds</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Shore birds</cref>, under <er>Shore</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Strand
plover</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a black-bellied plover. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Plover</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Strand
wolf</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the brown hyena.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strand</ent><br/
<hw>Strand</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stranded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stranding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To drive on a strand; hence, to
run aground; <as>as, to <ex>strand</ex> a ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strand</ent><br/
<hw>Strand</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To drift, or be
driven, on shore to run aground; <as>as, the ship <ex>stranded</ex> at
high water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strang</ent><br/
<hw>Strang</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Strong</er>.]</ety> <def>Strong.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strange</ent><br/
<hw>Strange</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stranger</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Strangest</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>estrange</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/trange</ets>, fr. L.
<ets>extraneus</ets> that is without, external, foreign, fr.
<ets>extra</ets> on the outside. See <er>Extra</er>, and cf.
<er>Estrange</er>, <er>Extraneous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Belonging to another country; foreign.</def> <ldquo/To seek
<xex>strange</xex> strands.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One of the <qex>strange</qex> queen's lords.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do not contemn the knowledge of <qex>strange</qex> and divers
tongues.</q> <rj><qau>Ascham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to others; not one's own; not
pertaining to one's self; not domestic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So she, impatient her own faults to see,<br/
Turns from herself, and in <qex>strange</qex> things delights.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir J. Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not before known, heard, or seen; new.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here is the hand and seal of the duke; you know the character, I
doubt not; and the signet is not <qex>strange</qex> to you.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Not according to the common way; novel; odd;
unusual; irregular; extraordinary; unnatural; queer.</def> <ldquo/He is
sick of a <xex>strange</xex> fever.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sated at length, erelong I might perceive<br/
<qex>Strange</qex> alteration in me.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Reserved; distant in deportment.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She may be <qex>strange</qex> and shy at first, but will soon
learn to love thee.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Backward; slow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who, loving the effect, would not be <qex>strange</qex><br/
In favoring the cause.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Not familiar; unaccustomed; inexperienced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In thy fortunes am unlearned and <qex>strange</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Strange</xex> is often used as an
exclamation.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Strange</qex>! what extremes should thus preserve the
snow<br/
High on the Alps, or in deep caves below.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strange sail</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>an unknown
vessel.</cd> -- <col><b>Strange woman</b></col> <fld>(Script.)</fld>,
<cd>a harlot.</cd> <au>Prov. v. 3.</au> -- <col><b>To make it
strange</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To assume ignorance, suspicion, or
alarm, concerning it</cd>. <au>Shak.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To make it a
matter of difficulty.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au> --
<mcol><col><b>To make strange</b></col>, <col><b>To make one's self
strange</b></col></mcol>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To profess ignorance or
astonishment</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To assume the character of a
stranger</cd>. <au>Gen. xlii. 7.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Foreign; new; outlandish; wonderful; astonishing;
marvelous; unusual; odd; uncommon; irregular; queer;
eccentric.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strange</ent><br/
<hw>Strange</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Strangely.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Most <qex>strange</qex>, but yet most truly, will I speak.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strange</ent><br/
<hw>Strange</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To alienate; to estrange.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strange</ent><br/
<hw>Strange</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be estranged or
alienated.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wonder; to be astonished.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangely</ent><br/
<hw>Strange"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>As something
foreign, or not one's own; in a manner adapted to something foreign and
strange.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In the manner of one who does not know another;
distantly; reservedly; coldly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You all look <qex>strangely</qex> on me.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do in justice charge thee . . . <br/
That thou commend it <qex>strangely</qex> to some place<br/
Where chance may nurse or end it.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In a strange manner; in a manner or degree to excite
surprise or wonder; wonderfully.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How <qex>strangely</qex> active are the arts of peace!</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It would <qex>strangely</qex> delight you to see with what spirit
he converses.</q> <rj><qau>Law.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangeness</ent><br/
<hw>Strange"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state or quality of being
strange (in any sense of the adjective).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stranger</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estrangier</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tranger</ets>. See
<er>Strange</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is strange, foreign,
or unknown.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>One who comes from a foreign land; a
foreigner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am a most poor woman and a <qex>stranger</qex>,<br/
Born out of your dominions.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>One whose home is at a distance from the place
where he is, but in the same country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>One who is unknown or unacquainted; <as>as, the
gentleman is a <ex>stranger</ex> to me</as>; hence, one not admitted to
communication, fellowship, or acquaintance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Melons on beds of ice are taught to bear,<br/
And <qex>strangers</qex> to the sun yet ripen here.</q>
<rj><qau>Granville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My child is yet a <qex>stranger</qex> in the world.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I was no <qex>stranger</qex> to the original.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One not belonging to the family or household; a
guest; a visitor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To honor and receive<br/
Our heavenly <qex>stranger</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One not privy or party an act,
contract, or title; a mere intruder or intermeddler; one who interferes
without right; <as>as, actual possession of land gives a good title
against a <ex>stranger</ex> having no title; as to <ex>strangers</ex>, a
mortgage is considered merely as a pledge; a mere <ex>stranger</ex> to
the levy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stranger</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"ger</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To estrange; to
alienate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangle</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Strangled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Strangling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estrangler</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/trangler</ets>, L.
<ets>strangulare</ets>, Gr. <?/, <?/, fr. <?/ a halter; and perhaps akin
to E. <ets>string</ets>, n. Cf. <er>Strain</er>, <er>String</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To compress the windpipe of (a person or animal) until
death results from stoppage of respiration; to choke to death by
compressing the throat, as with the hand or a rope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our Saxon ancestors compelled the adulteress to
<qex>strangle</qex> herself.</q> <rj><qau>Ayliffe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stifle, choke, or suffocate in any
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shall I not then be stifled in the vault, . . . <br/
And there die <qex>strangled</qex> ere my Romeo comes?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To hinder from appearance; to stifle; to
suppress.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Strangle</xex> such thoughts.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangle</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be strangled, or
suffocated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangleable</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gle*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being strangled.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chesterfield.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangle hold</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gle hold</hw>. <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Wrestling)</fld> <def>a hold
by which one's opponent is choked. It is usually not allowed.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a forceful influence that strongly inhibits freedom
of action; <as>as, the oil price jump put a <ex>stranglehold</ex> on the
economy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>Zona believes that Microsoft's entry into this fray breaks what
has been, by all appearances, Netscape's <qex>stranglehold</qex> on the
Internet. Microsoft is not licensing any technology from Netscape, and
as part of this announcement, several alliances which historically would
have been with Netscape were included. The impact of this announcement
on Netscape was huge.</q><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q>SAN FRANCISCO, November 6, 1996 -- If Microsoft Corp. CEO Bill
Gates were a plumber, he would control all the water in the world and
force anyone who wanted some of the precious liquid to cut a deal with
him.<br/
With that statement, Oracle Corp. CEO Larry Ellison closed a
question-and-answer session with reporters and analysts held here
Tuesday at Oracle OpenWorld. During his hour-long appearance, Ellison
agitated in favor of low-cost, generic network computers and against
Windows-based PCs.<br/
"The notion of one company controlling the whole [computer] industry is
shocking and unacceptable," Ellison said, referring to what he describes
as Microsoft's <qex>stranglehold</qex> on PCs through its ubiquitous
Windows operating systems.</q><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangler</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, strangles.</def> <ldquo/The very <xex>strangler</xex> of their
amity.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1423 --></p>

<p><ent>Strangles</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gles</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A disease in horses
and swine, in which the upper part of the throat, or groups of lymphatic
glands elsewhere, swells.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangulate</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Strangulated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangulated</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gu*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Having the circulation stopped by compression;
attended with arrest or obstruction of circulation, caused by
constriction or compression; <as>as, a <ex>strangulated</ex>
hernia</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Contracted at irregular intervals,
if tied with a ligature; constricted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strangulated hernia</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Hernia</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangulation</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gu*la`tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strangulatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>strangulation</ets>. See
<er>Strangle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of strangling, or the
state of being strangled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Inordinate compression or
constriction of a tube or part, as of the throat; especially, such as
causes a suspension of breathing, of the passage of contents, or of the
circulation, as in cases of hernia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangurious</ent><br/
<hw>Stran*gu"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stranguriosus</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to strangury.</def> <rj><au>Cheyne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strangury</ent><br/
<hw>Stran"gu*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stranguria</ets>, Gr. <?/; <?/, <?/, a drop + <?/ to make water,
<?/ urine: cf. F. <ets>strangurie</ets>. See <er>Strangle</er>, and
<er>Urine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A painful
discharge of urine, drop by drop, produced by spasmodic muscular
contraction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A swelling or other disease in a
plant, occasioned by a ligature fastened tightly about it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strany</ent><br/
<hw>Stra"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
guillemot.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strap</ent><br/
<hw>Strap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>strope</ets>,
AS. <ets>stropp</ets>, L. <ets>stroppus</ets>, <ets>struppus</ets>,
perhaps fr. Gr. <?/ a band or cord, fr. <?/ to twist, to turn (cf.
<er>Strophe</er>). Cf. <er>Strop</er> a strap, a piece of rope.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A long, narrow, pliable strip of leather, cloth, or the
like; specifically, a strip of thick leather used in flogging.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A lively cobbler that . . . had scarce passed a day without giving
her [his wife] the discipline of the <qex>strap</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something made of such a strip, or of a part of one,
or a combination of two or more for a particular use; <as>as, a boot
<ex>strap</ex>, shawl <ex>strap</ex>, stirrup
<ex>strap</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A piece of leather, or strip of wood covered with a
suitable material, for sharpening a razor; a strop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A narrow strip of anything, as of iron or
brass.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Carp. & Mach.)</fld> <def>A band, plate, or loop
of metal for clasping and holding timbers or parts of a
machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A piece of rope or metal passing
around a block and used for fastening it to anything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The flat part of the
corolla in ligulate florets, as those of the white circle in the
daisy.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The leaf, exclusive of its sheath, in
some grasses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A shoulder strap. See under
<er>Shoulder</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strap bolt</b></col>, <cd>a bolt of which one end is a
flat bar of considerable length.</cd> -- <col><b>Strap head</b></col>
<fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a journal box, or pair of brasses, secured to
the end of a connecting rod by a strap. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<cref>Gib and key</cref>, under <er>Gib</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Strap
hinge</b></col>, <cd>a hinge with long flaps by which it is fastened, as
to a door or wall.</cd> -- <col><b>Strap rail</b></col>
<fld>(Railroads)</fld>, <cd>a flat rail formerly used.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strap</ent><br/
<hw>Strap</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Strapped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Strapping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To beat or chastise
with a strap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fasten or bind with a strap.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To sharpen by rubbing on a strap, or strop; <as>as,
to <ex>strap</ex> a razor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strappado</ent><br/
<hw>Strap*pa"do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Strappadoes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It.
<ets>strappata</ets> a pull, the strappado, from <ets>strappare</ets> to
pull, from Prov. G. <ets>strapfen</ets>: cf. G. <ets>straff</ets> tense,
stretched.]</ety> <def>A military punishment formerly practiced, which
consisted in drawing an offender to the top of a beam and letting him
fall to the length of the rope, by which means a limb was often
dislocated.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strappado</ent><br/
<hw>Strap*pa"do</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To punish or torture by the
strappado.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strapper</ent><br/
<hw>Strap"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
uses strap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person or thing of uncommon size.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strapping</ent><br/
<hw>Strap"ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tall; strong;
lusty; large; <as>as, a <ex>strapping</ex> fellow</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are five and thirty <qex>strapping</qex> officers gone.</q>
<rj><qau>Farquhar.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strapple</ent><br/
<hw>Strap"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To hold or bind
with, or as with, a strap; to entangle.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strap-shaped</ent><br/
<hw>Strap"-shaped`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shaped like a
strap; ligulate; <as>as, a <ex>strap-shaped</ex> corolla</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strapwork</ent><br/
<hw>Strap"work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>A kind of ornament consisting of a narrow fillet or band folded,
crossed, and interlaced.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strass</ent><br/
<hw>Strass</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from its
inventor, a German jeweler: cf. F. <ets>stras</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A brilliant glass, used in the manufacture of
artificial paste gems, which consists essentially of a complex
borosilicate of lead and potassium. Cf. <er>Glass</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strata</ent><br/
<hw>Stra"ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <def><pos>pl.</pos> of
<er>Stratum</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratagem</ent><br/
<hw>Strat"a*gem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>stratag<egrave/me</ets> (cf. Sp. <ets>estratagema</ets>, It.
<ets>stratagemma</ets>), L. <ets>strategema</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to
be leader of an army, fr. <?/ a general; <?/ an army (probably as being
spread out; cf. <er>Stratum</er>) + <?/ to lead. See
<er>Agent</er>.]</ety> <def>An artifice or trick in war for deceiving
the enemy; hence, in general, artifice; deceptive device; secret plot;
evil machination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fit for treasons, <qex>stratagems</qex>, and spoils.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those oft are <qex>stratagems</qex> which error seem,<br/
Nor is it Homer nods, but we that dream.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratagemical</ent><br/
<hw>Strat`a*gem"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing
stratagem; <as>as, a <ex>stratagemical</ex> epistle</as>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratarithmetry</ent><br/
<hw>Strat`a*rith"me*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
army + <?/ number + <ets>-metry</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>The
art of drawing up an army, or any given number of men, in any
geometrical figure, or of estimating or expressing the number of men in
such a figure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strategetical</ent><br/
<ent>Strategetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Strat`e*get"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Strat`e*get"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Strategic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strategetics</ent><br/
<hw>Strat`e*get"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Strategy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strategical</ent><br/
<ent>Strategic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stra*te"gic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stra*te"gic*al</hw>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ of or for a general: cf. F.
<ets>strat<eacute/gique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to strategy;
effected by artifice.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Stra*te"gic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strategic line</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a line
joining strategic points.</cd> -- <col><b>Strategic point</b></col>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>any point or region in the theater or warlike
operations which affords to its possessor an advantage over his
opponent, as a mountain pass, a junction of rivers or roads, a fortress,
etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strategics</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*te"gics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Strategy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strategist</ent><br/
<hw>Strat"e*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>strat<eacute/giste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One skilled in strategy, or
the science of directing great military movements.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strategus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stra*te"gus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Strategi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/. See
<er>Stratagem</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gr. Antiq.)</fld> <def>The leader or
commander of an army; a general.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strategy</ent><br/
<hw>Strat"e*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>strat<eacute/gie</ets>. See <er>Stratagem</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The science of military command, or the science of projecting
campaigns and directing great military movements; generalship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The use of stratagem or artifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 3. a plan of action encompassing the methods to be adopted from
beginning to end of a task or endeavor, focussing on the general
methods; -- contrasted with tactics, which is a plan for accomplishing
subgoals of lesser extent than the primary goal. Thus, a strategy is a
plan for winning a war, and a tactic is a plan for winning a battle. 4.
Biol. A behavior evolved and exhibited by a living organism to
accomplish some important goal, as a foraging strategy. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strath</ent><br/
<hw>Strath</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gael.
<ets>srath</ets>.]</ety> <def>A valley of considerable size, through
which a river runs; a valley bottom; -- often used in composition with
the name of the river; <as>as, <ex>Strath</ex> Spey, <ex>Strath</ex>don,
<ex>Strath</ex>more</as>.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The long green <qex>strath</qex> of Napa valley.</q> <rj><qau>R.
L. Stevenson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strathspey</ent><br/
<hw>Strath"spey`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from
the district of <ets>Strath Spey</ets> in Scotland.]</ety> <def>A lively
Scottish dance, resembling the reel, but slower; also, the
tune.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straticulate</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*tic"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Dim. Fr.
<ets>stratum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>Characterized by the
presence of thin parallel strata, or layers, as in an agate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratification</ent><br/
<hw>Strat`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stratification</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process of
laying in strata, or the state of being laid in the form of strata, or
layers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The deposition of material in
successive layers in the growth of a cell wall, thus giving rise to a
stratified appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratified</ent><br/
<hw>Strat"i*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having its
substance arranged in strata, or layers; <as>as, <ex>stratified</ex>
rock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratiform</ent><br/
<hw>Strat"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the form
of strata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratify</ent><br/
<hw>Strat"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stratified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stratifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[<ets>Stratum</ets> + <ets>-fy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>stratifier</ets>.]</ety> <def>To form or deposit in strata, or
layers, as substances in the earth; to arrange in strata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratigraphical</ent><br/
<ent>Stratigraphic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Strat`i*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Strat`i*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or depended upon, the order or
arrangement of strata; <as>as, <ex>stratigraphical</ex>
evidence</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Strat`i*graph"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>-ical</ent><br/
<ent>Stratigraphic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Strat`i*graph"ic</hw>, <hw>-ic*al</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>See <er>Stratographic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratigraphy</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*tig"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stratum</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch
of geology which treats of the arrangement and succession of
strata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strato-cirrus</ent><br/
<hw>Stra`to-cir"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stratus</ets> + <ets>cirrus</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Meteor.)</fld> <def>An alto-stratus cloud.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratocracy</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*toc"ra*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ an
army + <ets>-cracy</ets>, as in demo<ets>cracy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>stratocratie</ets>.]</ety> <def>A military government; government
by military chiefs and an army.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strato-cumulus</ent><br/
<hw>Stra`to-cu"mu*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stratus</ets> + <ets>cumulus</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Meteor.)</fld> <def>Large balls or rolls of dark cloud which
frequently cover the whole sky, esp. in winter, and give it at times an
undulated appearance.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratographical</ent><br/
<ent>Stratographic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Strat`o*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Strat`o*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of
or pertaining to stratography.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratography</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*tog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ an
army + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A description of an army, or of
what belongs to an army.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratonic</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ an
army.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to an army.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratotic</ent><br/
<hw>Stra*tot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Warlike;
military.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratum</ent><br/
<hw>Stra"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Stratums</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Strata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>. The
latter is more common.</plu> <ety>[L., from <ets>sternere</ets>,
<ets>stratum</ets>, to spread; akin to Gr. <?/ to spread, strew. See
<er>Strew</er>, and cf. <er>Consternation</er>, <er>Estrade</er>,
<er>Prostrate</er>, <er>Stratus</er>, <er>Street</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A bed of earth or rock of one kind,
formed by natural causes, and consisting usually of a series of layers,
which form a rock as it lies between beds of other kinds. Also used
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bed or layer artificially made; a
course.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stratus</ent><br/
<hw>Stra"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stratus</ets> a spreading out, scattering, from
<ets>sternere</ets>, <ets>stratum</ets>, to spread.]</ety>
<fld>(Meteor.)</fld> <def>A form of clouds in which they are arranged in
a horizontal band or layer. See <er>Cloud</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straught</ent><br/
<hw>Straught</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> of <er>Stretch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straught</ent><br/
<hw>Straught</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To stretch; to make
straight.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>straucht</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straw</ent><br/
<hw>Straw</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To spread or scatter.
See <er>Strew</er>, and <er>Strow</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straw</ent><br/
<hw>Straw</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>straw</ets>,
<ets>stre</ets>, <ets>stree</ets>, AS. <ets>stre<aacute/w</ets>, from
the root of E. <ets>strew</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>str<emac/</ets>,
D. <ets>stroo</ets>, G. <ets>stroh</ets>, OHG. <ets>str<omac/</ets>,
Icel. <ets>str<amac/</ets>, Dan. <ets>straa</ets>, Sw.
<ets>str<aring/</ets>. <root/166. See <er>Strew</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A stalk or stem of certain species of grain, pulse, etc.,
especially of wheat, rye, oats, barley, more rarely of buckwheat, beans,
and pease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The gathered and thrashed stalks of certain species
of grain, etc.; <as>as, a bundle, or a load, of rye
<ex>straw</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything proverbially worthless; the least possible
thing; a mere trifle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I set not a <qex>straw</qex> by thy dreamings.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Straw</xex> is often used in the formation of
self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>straw</xex>-built,
<xex>straw</xex>-crowned, <xex>straw</xex>-roofed,
<xex>straw</xex>-stuffed, and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Man of straw</b></col>, <cd>an effigy formed by stuffing
the garments of a man with straw; hence, a fictitious person; an
irresponsible person; a puppet.</cd><-- now usu. straw man, as in
<it>set up a straw man</it>; -- used in disputation. Typically, one
party accuses an opponent of <it>setting up a straw man</it>, meaning
that the opponent is distorting his true opinion in order to make it
look absurd. --> -- <col><b>Straw bail</b></col>, <cd>worthless bail, as
being given by irresponsible persons.</cd> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark>
-- <col><b>Straw bid</b></col>, <cd>a worthless bid; a bid for a
contract which the bidder is unable or unwilling to fulfill.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Straw cat</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the pampas cat.</cd> -- <col><b>Straw
color</b></col>, <cd>the color of dry straw, being a delicate
yellow.</cd> -- <col><b>Straw drain</b></col>, <cd>a drain filled with
straw.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Straw plait</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Straw plat</b></col></mcol>, <cd>a strip formed by plaiting
straws, used for making hats, bonnets, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>To be in the
straw</b></col>, <cd>to be brought to bed, as a pregnant woman.</cd>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><-- archaic? Similar is "a roll in the hay"
--></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strawberry</ent><br/
<hw>Straw"ber*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stre<aacute/wberige</ets>; <ets>stre<aacute/w</ets> straw +
<ets>berie</ets> berry; perhaps from the resemblance of the runners of
the plant to straws.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A fragrant edible
berry, of a delicious taste and commonly of a red color, the fruit of a
plant of the genus <gen>Fragaria</gen>, of which there are many
varieties. Also, the plant bearing the fruit. The common American
strawberry is <spn>Fragaria virginiana</spn>; the European,
<spn>Fragaria vesca</spn>. There are also other less common
species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strawberry bass</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Calico bass</cref>, under <er>Calico</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Strawberry blite</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Blite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry borer</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of insects whose
larvae burrow in the crown or roots of the strawberry vine.</cd>
Especially: <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The root borer (<spn>Anarsia
lineatella</spn>), a very small dark gray moth whose larvae burrow both
in the larger roots and crown, often doing great damage.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The crown borer (<spn>Tyloderma fragariae</spn>), a
small brown weevil whose larva burrows in the crown and kills the
plant.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry bush</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>an American shrub (<spn>Euonymus Americanus</spn>), a kind of
spindle tree having crimson pods and the seeds covered with a scarlet
aril.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a small European spider crab (<spn>Eurynome aspera</spn>); -- so
called because the back is covered with pink tubercles.</cd> --
<col><b>Strawberry fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
amadavat.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry geranium</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of saxifrage (<spn>Saxifraga
sarmentosa</spn>) having reniform leaves, and producing long runners
like those of the strawberry.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry leaf</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The leaf of the strawberry</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The
symbol of the rank or estate of a duke, because the ducal coronet is
twined with strawberry leaves</cd>. <ldquo/The <xex>strawberry
leaves</xex> on her chariot panels are engraved on her ladyship's
heart.<rdquo/ <au>Thackeray.</au> -- <col><b>Strawberry-leaf
roller</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of
moths whose larvae roll up, and feed upon, the leaves of the strawberry
vine; especially, <spn>Phoxopteris fragariae</spn>, and <spn>Eccopsis
permundana</spn>.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry moth</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several species of moth whose larvae
feed on the strawberry vines</cd>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The smeared
dagger (<spn>Apatela oblinita</spn>), whose large hairy larva is velvety
black with two rows of bright yellow spots on each side.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A geometrid (<spn>Angerona crocataria</spn>) which is
yellow with dusky spots on the wings. Called also <altname>currant
moth</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry pear</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the red ovoid fruit of a West Indian plant of the
genus <gen>Cereus</gen> (<spn>Cereus triangularia</spn>). It has a
sweetish flavor, and is slightly acid, pleasant, and cooling. Also, the
plant bearing the fruit.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry sawfly</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small black sawfly (<spn>Emphytus
maculatus</spn>) whose larva eats the leaves of the strawberry
vine.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry tomato</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Alkekengi</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry tree</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Arbutus</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry
vine</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the plant which yields the
strawberry.</cd> -- <col><b>Strawberry worm</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the larva of any moth which feeds on the
strawberry vine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strawboard</ent><br/
<hw>Straw"board`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Pasteboard made
of pulp of straw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straw-colored</ent><br/
<hw>Straw"-col`ored</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being of a
straw color. See <cref>Straw color</cref>, under <er>Straw</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Straw-cutter</ent><br/
<hw>Straw"-cut`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An instrument
to cut straw for fodder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strawed</ent><br/
<hw>Strawed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Straw</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strawworm</ent><br/
<hw>Straw"worm`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A caddice
worm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strawy</ent><br/
<hw>Straw"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
straw; made of, or resembling, straw.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stray</ent><br/
<hw>Stray</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Strayed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Straying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>estraier</ets>,
<ets>estraer</ets>, to stray, or as adj., stray, fr. (assumed) L.
<ets>stratarius</ets> roving the streets, fr. L. <ets>strata</ets> (sc.
<ets>via</ets>) a paved road. See <er>Street</er>, and <er>Stray</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To wander, as from a direct
course; to deviate, or go out of the way.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thames among the wanton valleys <qex>strays</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wander from company, or from the proper limits;
to rove at large; to roam; to go astray.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now, until the break of day,<br/
Through this house each fairy <qex>stray</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A sheep doth very often <qex>stray</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Figuratively, to wander from the path of duty or
rectitude; to err.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have erred and <qex>strayed</qex> from thy ways.</q>
<rj><qau><?/<?/<?/ of Com. Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While meaner things, whom instinct leads,<br/
Are rarely known to <qex>stray</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To deviate; err; swerve; rove; roam;
wander.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stray</ent><br/
<hw>Stray</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to stray.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stray</ent><br/
<hw>Stray</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF. <ets>estrai<eacute/</ets>,
p. p. of <ets>estraier</ets>. See <er>Stray</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Astray</er>, <er>Estray</er>.]</ety> <def>Having gone astray;
strayed; wandering; <as>as, a <ex>stray</ex>horse or
sheep</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stray line</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>that portion
of the log line which is veered from the reel to allow the chip to get
clear of the stern eddies before the glass is turned.</cd> --
<col><b>Stray mark</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>the mark indicating
the end of the stray line.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stray</ent><br/
<hw>Stray</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any domestic animal that
has an inclosure, or its proper place and company, and wanders at large,
or is lost; an estray. Used also figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Seeing him wander about, I took him up for a <qex>stray</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of wandering or going astray.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1424 --></p>

<p><ent>Strayer</ent><br/
<hw>Stray"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who strays; a
wanderer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stre</ent><br/
<hw>Stre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Straw.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streak</ent><br/
<hw>Streak</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Stretch</er>, <er>Streek</er>.]</ety> <def>To stretch; to extend;
hence, to lay out, as a dead body.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. &
Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streak</ent><br/
<hw>Streak</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>streke</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>streek</ets> a line, stroke, G. <ets>strich</ets>, AS.
<ets>strica</ets>, Sw. <ets>strek</ets>, Dan. <ets>streg</ets>, Goth.
<ets>stricks</ets>, and E. <ets>strike</ets>, <ets>stroke</ets>. See
<er>Strike</er>, <er>Stroke</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Strake</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A line or long mark of a
different color from the ground; a stripe; a vein.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What mean those colored <qex>streaks</qex> in heaven?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>A strake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>The fine powder or mark yielded by
a mineral when scratched or rubbed against a harder surface, the color
of which is sometimes a distinguishing character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The rung or round of a ladder.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streak</ent><br/
<hw>Streak</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Streaked</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Streaking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To form streaks or
stripes in or on; to stripe; to variegate with lines of a different
color, or of different colors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A mule . . . <qex>streaked</qex> and dappled with white and
black.</q> <rj><qau>Sandys.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now <qex>streaked</qex> and glowing with the morning red.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>With <xex>it</xex> as an object: To run
swiftly.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streaked</ent><br/
<hw>Streaked</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Marked or
variegated with stripes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Uncomfortable; out of sorts.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streaky</ent><br/
<hw>Streak"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Streaked</er>, 1.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>streaky</xex> west.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stream</ent><br/
<hw>Stream</hw> <pr>(str<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>stre<aacute/m</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>str<amac/m</ets>, OS.
<ets>str<omac/m</ets>, D. <ets>stroom</ets>, G. <ets>strom</ets>, OHG.
<ets>stroum</ets>, <ets>str<umac/m</ets>, Dan. & Sw.
<ets>str<oum/m</ets>, Icel. <ets>straumr</ets>, Ir. <ets>sroth</ets>,
Lith. <ets>srove</ets>, Russ. <ets>struia</ets>, Gr. <grk>"ry`sis</grk>
a flowing, <grk>"rei^n</grk> to flow, Skr. <ets>sru</ets>. <root/174.
Cf. <er>Catarrh</er>, <er>Diarrhea</er>, <er>Rheum</er>,
<er>Rhythm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A current of water or other
fluid; a liquid flowing continuously in a line or course, either on the
earth, as a river, brook, etc., or from a vessel, reservoir, or
fountain; specifically, any course of running water; <as>as, many
<ex>streams</ex> are blended in the Mississippi; gas and steam came from
the earth in <ex>streams</ex>; a <ex>stream</ex> of molten lead from a
furnace; a <ex>stream</ex> of lava from a volcano.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A beam or ray of light.</def> <ldquo/Sun
<xex>streams</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything issuing or moving with continued succession
of parts; <as>as, a <ex>stream</ex> of words; a <ex>stream</ex> of
sand</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>stream</xex> of beneficence.<rdquo/
<au>Atterbury.</au> <ldquo/The <xex>stream</xex> of emigration.<rdquo/
<au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A continued current or course; <as>as, a
<ex>stream</ex> of weather</as>.</def> <ldquo/The very <xex>stream</xex>
of his life.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Current; drift; tendency; series of tending or
moving causes; <as>as, the <ex>stream</ex> of opinions or
manners</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Gulf stream</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Gulf</er>.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Stream anchor</b></col>, <col><b>Stream
cable</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Anchor</er>, and <er>Cable</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Stream
ice</b></col>, <cd>blocks of ice floating in a mass together in some
definite direction.</cd> -- <col><b>Stream tin</b></col>, <cd>particles
or masses of tin ore found in alluvial ground; -- so called because a
<xex>stream</xex> of water is the principal agent used in separating the
ore from the sand and gravel.</cd> -- <col><b>Stream works</b></col>
<fld>(Cornish Mining)</fld>, <cd>a place where an alluvial deposit of
tin ore is worked.</cd> <au>Ure.</au> -- <col><b>To float with the
stream</b></col>, <cd>figuratively, to drift with the current of
opinion, custom, etc., so as not to oppose or check it.</cd> <-- Colloq.
= go with the flow, blow with the wind. --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Current; flow; rush; tide; course.</syn> --
<usage><er>Stream</er>, <er>Current</er>. These words are often properly
interchangeable; but <xex>stream</xex> is the broader word, denoting a
prevailing onward course. The <xex>stream</xex> of the Mississippi rolls
steadily on to the Gulf of Mexico, but there are reflex
<xex>currents</xex> in it which run for a while in a contrary
direction.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stream</ent><br/
<hw>Stream</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Streamed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Streaming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To issue or flow in
a stream; to flow freely or in a current, as a fluid or whatever is
likened to fluids; <as>as, tears <ex>streamed</ex> from her
eyes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Beneath those banks where rivers <qex>stream</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pour out, or emit, a stream or streams.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A thousand suns will <qex>stream</qex> on thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To issue in a stream of light; to radiate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To extend; to stretch out with a wavy motion; to
float in the wind; <as>as, a flag <ex>streams</ex> in the
wind</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stream</ent><br/
<hw>Stream</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To send forth in a current or
stream; to cause to flow; to pour; <as>as, his eyes <ex>streamed</ex>
tears</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It may so please that she at length will <qex>stream</qex><br/
Some dew of grace into my withered heart.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To mark with colors or embroidery in long
tracts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The herald's mantle is <qex>streamed</qex> with gold.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To unfurl.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stream the buoy</b></col>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Buoy</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stream clock</ent><br/
<hw>Stream clock</hw>. <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>An instrument for
ascertaining the velocity of the blood in a vessel.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streamer</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
ensign, flag, or pennant, which floats in the wind; specifically, a
long, narrow, ribbonlike flag.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Brave Rupert from afar appears,<br/
Whose waving <qex>streamers</qex> the glad general knows.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A stream or column of light shooting upward from the
horizon, constituting one of the forms of the aurora borealis.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>While overhead the North's dumb <qex>streamers</qex> shoot.</q>
<rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A searcher for stream
tin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Journalism)</fld> <def>a banner.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streamful</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in
streams, or in water.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>streamful</xex> tide.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stream gold</ent><br/
<hw>Stream gold</hw>. <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>Gold in alluvial
deposits; placer gold.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streaminess</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being streamy; a trailing.</def> <rj><au>R. A. Proctor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streaming</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sending forth streams.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streaming</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or operation
of that which streams; the act of that which sends forth, or which runs
in, streams.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>The reduction of stream tin;
also, the search for stream tin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>the rapid flow of cytoplasm
within a cell; -- called also <altname>protoplasmic
streaming</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streamless</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of streams, or of a
stream, as a region of country, or a dry channel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streamlet</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small stream; a
rivulet; a rill.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stream line</ent><br/
<hw>Stream line</hw>. <def>The path of a constituent particle of a
flowing fluid undisturbed by eddies or the like.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streamline</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"line`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pert. to a
stream line; designating a motion or flow that is free from turbulence,
like that of a particle in a streamline; hence, designating a surface,
body, etc., that is designed so as to afford an unbroken flow of a fluid
about it, esp. when the resistance to flow is the least possible;
<as>as, a <ex>streamline</ex> body for an automobile or airship</as>; --
the current usuage prefers the term
<altname>streamlined</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>streamline</ent><br/
<hw>stream"line</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to design or
modify so as to present the least possible resistance to fluid flow; --
used mostly of vehicles, such as automobiles, airplanes, or
ships.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <mark>[Fig.]</mark> <def>to change so as to make more
efficient; -- used especially of organizations, procedures, or
methods.</def> <ldquo/The <ex>streamlined</ex> company became a
formidable competitor.<rdquo/<br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stream wheel</ent><br/
<hw>Stream wheel</hw>. <def>A wheel used for measuring, by its motion
when submerged, the velocity of flowing water; a current
wheel.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streamy</ent><br/
<hw>Stream"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding
with streams, or with running water; streamful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Arcadia<br/
However <qex>streamy</qex> now, adust and dry,<br/
Denied the goddess water.</q> <rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resembling a stream; issuing in a stream.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His nodding helm emits a <qex>streamy</qex> ray.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stree</ent><br/
<hw>Stree</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Straw.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streek</ent><br/
<hw>Streek</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To stretch; also, to
lay out, as a dead body. See <er>Streak</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streel</ent><br/
<hw>Streel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Stroll</er>.]</ety> <def>To trail along; to saunter or be drawn
along, carelessly, swaying in a kind of zigzag motion.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streen</ent><br/
<hw>Streen</hw> <pr>(str<emac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Strene</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Street</ent><br/
<hw>Street</hw> <pr>(str<emac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>strete</ets>, AS. <ets>str<aemac/t</ets>, fr. L. <ets>strata</ets>
(sc. <ets>via</ets>) a paved way, properly fem. p. p. of
<ets>sternere</ets>, <ets>stratum</ets>, to spread; akin to E.
<ets>strew</ets>. See <er>Strew</er>, and cf. <er>Stratum</er>,
<er>Stray</er>, <pos>v.</pos> & <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Originally, a paved way or road; a public highway; now commonly, a
thoroughfare in a city or village, bordered by dwellings or business
houses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He removed [the body of] Amasa from the <qex>street</qex> unto the
field.</q> <rj><qau>Coverdale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At home or through the high <qex>street</qex> passing.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In an extended sense, <xex>street</xex> designates
besides the roadway, the walks, houses, shops, etc., which border the
thoroughfare.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His deserted mansion in Duke <qex>Street</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>the roadway of a street{1}, as distinguished from
the sidewalk; <as>as, children playing in the
<ex>street</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>the inhabitants of a particular street; <as>as, the
whole <ex>street</ex> knew about their impending divorce</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The street</b></col> <fld>(Broker's Cant)</fld>, <cd>that
thoroughfare of a city where the leading bankers and brokers do
business; also, figuratively, those who do business there; <as>as,
<ex>the street</ex> would not take the bonds</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>on
the street</b></col>, <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>homeless.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>unemployed.</cd> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>not in prison, or released from
prison; <as>the murderer is still <ex>on the street</ex></as>.</cd>
--<mcol><col><b>Street Arab</b></col>, <col><b>Street
broker</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Arab</er>,
<er>Broker</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Street door</b></col>, <cd>a door
which opens upon a street, or is nearest the street.</cd> --
<col><b>street person</b></col>, <cd>a homeless person; a
vagrant.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Way</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streetwalker</ent><br/
<hw>Street"walk`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A common
prostitute who walks the streets to find customers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streetward</ent><br/
<hw>Street"ward`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An officer, or
ward, having the care of the streets.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streetward</ent><br/
<hw>Street"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Facing toward the
street.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their little <qex>streetward</qex> sitting room.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streight</ent><br/
<hw>Streight</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a., n., & adv.</pos> <def>See 2nd
<er>Strait</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streighten</ent><br/
<hw>Streight"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Straiten</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strein</ent><br/
<hw>Strein</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To strain.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streit</ent><br/
<hw>Streit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stretch</er>.]</ety> <def>Drawn.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pyrrhus with his <qex>streite</qex> sword.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streit</ent><br/
<hw>Streit</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Close; narrow; strict.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <see>See <er>Strait</er>.</see><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streite</ent><br/
<hw>Streite</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Narrowly; strictly;
straitly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strelitz</ent><br/
<hw>Strel"itz</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>strieli<eacute/ts'</ets> a shooter, archer.]</ety> <def>A soldier
of the ancient Muscovite guard or Russian standing army; also, the guard
itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strelitzia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stre*litz"i*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., named
after Charlotte, Princess of Mecklenburg-<etsep>Strelitz</etsep>, and
queen of George III of Great Britain.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
genus of plants related to the banana, found at the Cape of Good Hope.
They have rigid glaucous distichous leaves, and peculiar richly colored
flowers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strene</ent><br/
<hw>Strene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stren</ets>,
<ets>streen</ets>, <ets>streon</ets>, AS. ge<ets>stri<eacute/nan</ets>,
ge<ets>str<?/nan</ets>, ge<ets>stre<oacute/nan</ets>, to beget, to
obtain, ge<ets>stre<oacute/n</ets> gain, wealth; akin to OHG.
<ets>striunan</ets> to gain. Cf. <er>Strian</er> race, family.]</ety>
<def>Race; offspring; stock; breed; strain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengest</ent><br/
<ent>Strenger</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stren"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stren"gest</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <def>the original <pos>compar.</pos> &
<pos>superl.</pos> of <er>Strong</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Two of us shall <qex>strenger</qex> be than one.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strength</ent><br/
<hw>Strength</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>strengthe</ets>, AS. <ets>streng<edh/u</ets>, fr. <ets>strang</ets>
strong. See <er>Strong</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or
state of being strong; ability to do or to bear; capacity for exertion
or endurance, whether physical, intellectual, or moral; force; vigor;
power; <as>as, <ex>strength</ex> of body or of the arm;
<ex>strength</ex> of mind, of memory, or of judgment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All his [Samson's] <qex>strength</qex> in his hairs were.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou must outlive<br/
Thy youth, thy <qex>strength</qex>, thy beauty.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Power to resist force; solidity or toughness; the
quality of bodies by which they endure the application of force without
breaking or yielding; -- in this sense opposed to
<contr>frangibility</contr>; <as>as, the <ex>strength</ex> of a bone, of
a beam, of a wall, a rope, and the like</as>.</def> <ldquo/The brittle
<xex>strength</xex> of bones.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Power of resisting attacks; impregnability.</def>
<ldquo/Our castle's <xex>strength</xex> will laugh a siege to
scorn.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That quality which tends to secure results;
effective power in an institution or enactment; security; validity;
legal or moral force; logical conclusiveness; <as>as, the
<ex>strength</ex> of social or legal obligations; the <ex>strength</ex>
of law; the <ex>strength</ex> of public opinion; <ex>strength</ex> of
evidence; <ex>strength</ex> of argument</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, is regarded as embodying or
affording force, strength, or firmness; that on which confidence or
reliance is based; support; security.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God is our refuge and <qex>strength</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ps. xlvi.
1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What they boded would be a mischief to us, you are providing shall
be one of our principal <qex>strengths</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sprat.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Certainly there is not a greater <qex>strength</qex> against
temptation.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Force as measured; amount, numbers, or power of any
body, as of an army, a navy, and the like; as, what is the
<xex>strength</xex> of the enemy by land, or by sea?</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Vigor or style; force of expression; nervous
diction; -- said of literary work.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And praise the easy vigor of a life<br/
Where Denham's <qex>strength</qex> and Waller's sweetness join.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Intensity; -- said of light or color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bright Phoebus in his <qex>strength</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Intensity or degree of the distinguishing and
essential element; spirit; virtue; excellence; -- said of liquors,
solutions, etc.; <as>as, the <ex>strength</ex> of wine or of
acids</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A strong place; a stronghold.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>On the strength of</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Upon the strength of</b></col></mcol>, <cd>in reliance
upon.</cd> <ldquo/The allies, after a successful summer, are too apt,
<xex>upon the strength of</xex> it, to neglect their preparations for
the ensuing campaign.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Force; robustness; toughness; hardness;
stoutness; brawniness; lustiness; firmness; puissance; support; spirit;
validity; authority. See <er>Force</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strength</ent><br/
<hw>Strength</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To strengthen.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthen</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Strengthened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Strengthening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
make strong or stronger; to add strength to; <as>as, to
<ex>strengthen</ex> a limb, a bridge, an army; to <ex>strengthen</ex> an
obligation; to <ex>strengthen</ex> authority</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let noble Warwick, Cobham, and the rest, . . . <br/
With powerful policy <qex>strengthen</qex> themselves.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To animate; to encourage; to fix in
resolution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Charge Joshua, and encourage him, and <qex>strengthen</qex>
him.</q> <rj><qau>Deut. iii. 28.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To invigorate; confirm; establish; fortify;
animate; encourage.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthen</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To grow strong
or stronger.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The young disease, that must subdue at length,<br/
Grows with his growth, and <qex>strengthens</qex> with his strength.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthener</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"en*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or
that which, gives or adds strength.</def> <rj><au>Sir W.
Temple.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthening</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"en*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That strengthens; giving or
increasing strength.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Strength"en*ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strengthening plaster</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
plaster containing iron, and supposed to have tonic
effects.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthful</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in
strength; full of strength; strong.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Strength"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Florence my friend, in court my faction<br/
Not meanly <qex>strengthful</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Marston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthing</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stronghold.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthless</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of strength.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthner</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Strengthener</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strengthy</ent><br/
<hw>Strength"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having strength;
strong.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strenuity</ent><br/
<hw>Stre*nu"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strenuatis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Strenuousness; activity.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strenuous</ent><br/
<hw>Stren"u*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strenuus</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/ strong, hard, rough, harsh.]</ety>
<def>Eagerly pressing or urgent; zealous; ardent; earnest; bold;
valiant; intrepid; <as>as, a <ex>strenuous</ex> advocate for national
rights; a <ex>strenuous</ex> reformer; a <ex>strenuous</ex> defender of
his country</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And spirit-stirring wine, that <qex>strenuous</qex> makes.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Strenuous</qex>, continuous labor is pain.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Stren"u*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Stren"u*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strepent</ent><br/
<hw>Strep"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strepens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>strepere</ets> to make a
noise.]</ety> <def>Noisy; loud.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shenstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streperous</ent><br/
<hw>Strep"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>streperus</ets>, fr. L. <ets>strepere</ets>. See <er>Strepent</er>,
and cf. <er>Obstreperous</er>.]</ety> <def>Loud; boisterous.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strepitores</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strep`i*to"res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. L. <ets>strepitus</ets> clamor.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
division of birds, including the clamatorial and picarian birds, which
do not have well developed singing organs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strepsipteran</ent><br/
<ent>Strepsipter</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Strep*sip"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Strep*sip"ter*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the
Strepsiptera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strepsiptera</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strep*sip"te*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a turning (fr. <?/ to twist) + <?/ a wing.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group of small insects having the anterior
wings rudimentary, and in the form of short and slender twisted
appendages, while the posterior ones are large and membranous. They are
parasitic in the larval state on bees, wasps, and the like; -- called
also <altname>Rhipiptera</altname>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Rhipipter</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strepsipterous</ent><br/
<hw>Strep*sip"ter*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Strepsiptera</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to Strepsiptera.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strepsorhina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strep`so*rhi"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a turning + <?/, <?/, the nose.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Lemuroidea</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strepsorhine</ent><br/
<hw>Strep"so*rhine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having twisted nostrils; -- said of the lemurs.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Strepsorhina; a
lemur. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Monkey</er>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streptobacteria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strep`to*bac*te"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>;
<sing>sing. <singw>Streptobracterium</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>.
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ pliant, bent + E. & NL.
<ets>bacteria</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A so-called variety
of bacterium, consisting in reality of several bacteria linked together
in the form of a chain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streptococcus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strep`to*coc"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Streptococci</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ pliant, curved + <?/ a grain, seed.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A long or short chain of micrococci, more or
less curved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Streptomyces. A genus of gram-positive bacteria growing in long
filamentous, often branched chains. They are plentiful in soil and
produce the characteristic odor of soils. Some of the species have
proved to be useful as sources of important pharmaceutical agents. Of
these, the best known are streptomycin, chloramphenicaol, and
tetracycline. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Streptomycete. Any member of the family of bacteria called
Streptomycetaceae, including the genus Streptomyces. They are typically
aerobic saprophytes producing begetative areial mycelium. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streptoneura</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strep`to*neu"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ curved + <?/ a sinew.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
extensive division of gastropod Mollusca in which the loop or visceral
nerves is twisted, and the sexes separate. It is nearly to equivalent to
Prosobranchiata.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Streptothrix</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strep"to*thrix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ pliant, bent + <?/ a hair.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
genus of bacilli occurring of the form of long, smooth and apparently
branched threads, either straight or twisted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stress</ent><br/
<hw>Stress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Abbrev. fr.
<ets>distress</ets>; or cf. OF. <ets>estrecier</ets> to press, pinch,
(assumed) LL. <ets>strictiare</ets>, fr. L. <ets>strictus</ets>. See
<er>Distress</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Distress.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sad hersal of his heavy <qex>stress</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pressure, strain; -- used chiefly of immaterial
things; except in mechanics; hence, urgency; importance; weight;
significance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The faculties of the mind are improved by exercise, yet they must
not be put to a <qex>stress</qex> beyond their strength.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A body may as well lay too little as too much <qex>stress</qex>
upon a dream.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mech. & Physics)</fld> <def>The force, or
combination of forces, which produces a strain; force exerted in any
direction or manner between contiguous bodies, or parts of bodies, and
taking specific names according to its direction, or mode of action, as
<xex>thrust</xex> or <xex>pressure</xex>, <xex>pull</xex> or
<xex>tension</xex>, <xex>shear</xex> or <xex>tangential
stress</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Rankine.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stress</qex> is the mutual action between portions of
matter.</q> <rj><qau>Clerk Maxwell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Pron.)</fld> <def>Force of utterance expended upon
words or syllables. Stress is in English the chief element in accent and
is one of the most important in emphasis. See <cref>Guide to
pronunciation</cref>, <sect/<sect/ 31-35.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld> <def>Distress; the act of
distraining; also, the thing distrained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stress of voice</b></col>, <cd>unusual exertion of the
voice.</cd> -- <col><b>Stress of weather</b></col>, <cd>constraint
imposed by continued bad weather; <as>as, to be driven back to port by
<ex>stress of weather</ex></as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To lay stress
upon</b></col>, <cd>to attach great importance to; to emphasize.</cd>
<ldquo/Consider how great a <xex>stress</xex> is <xex>laid upon</xex>
this duty.<rdquo/ <au>Atterbury.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>To put stress
upon</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>To put to a stress</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>to strain.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1425 --></p>

<p><ent>Stress</ent><br/
<hw>Stress</hw> <pr>(str<ecr/s)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To press; to urge; to distress; to put to difficulties.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To subject to stress, pressure, or strain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To subject to phonetic stress; to accent.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To place emphasis on; to make emphatic;
emphasize.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stressful</ent><br/
<hw>Stress"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having much
stress.</def> <rj><au>Rush.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stretch</ent><br/
<hw>Stretch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stretched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stretching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>strecchen</ets>, AS. <ets>streccan</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>strekken</ets>, G. <ets>strecken</ets>, OHG. <ets>strecchen</ets>,
Sw. <ets>str<aum/cka</ets>, Dan. <ets>straekke</ets>; cf. AS.
<ets>straeck</ets>, <ets>strec</ets>, strong, violent, G.
<ets>strack</ets> straight; of uncertain origin, perhaps akin to E.
<ets>strong</ets>. Cf. <er>Straight</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
reach out; to extend; to put forth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>stretch</qex> forth his neck long and small.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I in conquest <qex>stretched</qex> mine arm.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw out to the full length; to cause to extend
in a straight line; <as>as, to <ex>stretch</ex> a cord or
rope</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to extend in breadth; to spread; to expand;
<as>as, to <ex>stretch</ex> cloth; to <ex>stretch</ex> the
wings</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make tense; to tighten; to distend
forcibly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ox hath therefore <qex>stretched</qex> his yoke in vain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To draw or pull out to greater length; to strain;
<as>as, to <ex>stretch</ex> a tendon or muscle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Awake, my soul, <qex>stretch</qex> every nerve.</q>
<rj><qau>Doddridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To exaggerate; to extend too far; <as>as, to
<ex>stretch</ex> the truth; to <ex>stretch</ex> one's
credit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They take up, one day, the most violent and <qex>stretched</qex>
prerogative.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stretch</ent><br/
<hw>Stretch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be extended; to
be drawn out in length or in breadth, or both; to spread; to reach;
<as>as, the iron road <ex>stretches</ex> across the continent; the lake
<ex>stretches</ex> over fifty square miles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As far as <qex>stretcheth</qex> any ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Gower.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To extend or spread one's self, or one's limbs;
<as>as, the lazy man yawns and <ex>stretches</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be extended, or to bear extension, without
breaking, as elastic or ductile substances.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The inner membrane . . . because it would <qex>stretch</qex> and
yield, remained umbroken.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To strain the truth; to exaggerate; <as>as, a man
apt to <ex>stretch</ex> in his report of facts</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.
or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To sail by the wind under press
of canvas; <as>as, the ship <ex>stretched</ex> to the
eastward</as>.</def> <rj><au>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stretch out</b></col>, <cd>an order to rowers to extend
themselves forward in dipping the oar.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stretch</ent><br/
<hw>Stretch</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act of stretching, or
state of being stretched; reach; effort; struggle; strain; <as>as, a
<ex>stretch</ex> of the limbs; a <ex>stretch</ex> of the
imagination</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By <qex>stretch</qex> of arms the distant shore to gain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those put a lawful authority upon the <qex>stretch</qex>, to the
abuse of yower, under the color of prerogative.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A continuous line or surface; a continuous space of
time; <as>as, grassy <ex>stretches</ex> of land</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A great <qex>stretch</qex> of cultivated country.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Black.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But all of them left me a week at a <qex>stretch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>E. Eggleston.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The extent to which anything may be
stretched.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Quotations, in their utmost <qex>stretch</qex>, can signify no
more than that Luther lay under severe agonies of mind.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is the utmost <qex>stretch</qex> that nature can.</q>
<rj><qau>Granville.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The reach or extent of a vessel's
progress on one tack; a tack or board.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Course; direction; <as>as, the <ex>stretch</ex> of
seams of coal</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To be on the stretch</b></col>, <cd>to be obliged to use
one's utmost powers.</cd> -- <col><b>Home stretch</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Home</er>, <pos>a.</pos></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stretcher</ent><br/
<hw>Stretch"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who, or that which, stretches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>A brick or stone laid with its
longer dimension in the line of direction of the wall.</def>
<rj><au>Gwilt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A piece of timber used in
building.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A narrow crosspiece
of the bottom of a boat against which a rower braces his feet.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A crosspiece placed between the sides of a boat to
keep them apart when hoisted up and griped.</def>
<rj><au>Dana.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A litter, or frame, for carrying disabled, wounded,
or dead persons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An overstretching of the truth; a lie.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>One of the rods in an umbrella, attached at one end
to one of the ribs, and at the other to the tube sliding upon the
handle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>An instrument for stretching boots or
gloves.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>The frame upon which canvas is stretched for a
painting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stretching</ent><br/
<hw>Stretch"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Stretch</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stretching course</b></col> <fld>(Masonry)</fld>, <cd>a
course or series of stretchers. See <er>Stretcher</er>, 2.</cd>
<au>Britton.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stretto</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stret"to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It., close or
contacted, pressed.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
crowding of answer upon subject near the end of a fugue.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>In an opera or oratorio, a coda, or winding up, in an
accelerated time.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stretta</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strew</ent><br/
<hw>Strew</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Strewed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>strewn</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Strewing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>strewen</ets>,
<ets>strawen</ets>, AS. <ets>strewian</ets>,
<ets>stre<oacute/wian</ets>; akin to Ofries. <ets>strewa</ets>, OS.
<ets>strewian</ets>, D. <ets>strooijen</ets>, G. <ets>streuen</ets>,
OHG. <ets>strewen</ets>, Icel. <ets>str<amac/</ets>, Sw.
<ets>str<oum/</ets>, Dan. <ets>str<oum/e</ets>, Goth.
<ets>straujan</ets>, L. <ets>sternere</ets>, <ets>stratum</ets>, Gr.
<?/, <?/, Skr. <ets>st<?/</ets>. <root/166. Cf. <er>Stratum</er>,
<er>Straw</er>, <er>Street</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To scatter; to
spread by scattering; to cast or to throw loosely apart; -- used of
solids, separated or separable into parts or particles; <as>as, to
<ex>strew</ex> seed in beds; to <ex>strew</ex> sand on or over a floor;
to <ex>strew</ex> flowers over a grave.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>strewed</qex> his mangled limbs about the field.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>On a principal table a desk was open and many papers [were]
<qex>strewn</qex> about.</q> <rj><qau>Beaconsfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover more or less thickly by scattering
something over or upon; to cover, or lie upon, by having been scattered;
<as>as, they <ex>strewed</ex> the ground with leaves; leaves
<ex>strewed</ex> the ground.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The snow which does the top of Pindus <qex>strew</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Is thine alone the seed that <qex>strews</qex> the plain?</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To spread abroad; to disseminate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She may <qex>strew</qex> dangerous conjectures.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strewing</ent><br/
<hw>Strew"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of scattering or spreading.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything that is, or may be, strewed; -- used
chiefly in the plural.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strewment</ent><br/
<hw>Strew"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Anything scattered,
as flowers for decoration.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strewn</ent><br/
<hw>Strewn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Strew</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stria</ent><br/
<hw>Stri"a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Striae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a furrow, channel,
hollow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A minute groove, or channel; a
threadlike line, as of color; a narrow structural band or line; a
striation; <as>as, the <ex>striae</ex>, or groovings, produced on a rock
by a glacier passing over it; the <ex>striae</ex> on the surface of a
shell; a <ex>stria</ex> of nervous matter in the brain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A fillet between the flutes of
columns, pilasters, or the like.</def> <rj><au>Oxf. Gloss.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striate</ent><br/
<hw>Stri"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Striated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Striating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Striate</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To mark with striae.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Striated</xex> longitudinally.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striated</ent><br/
<ent>Striate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stri"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stri"a*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>striatus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>striare</ets> to furnish with channels, from <ets>stria</ets> a
channel.]</ety> <def>Marked with striae, or fine grooves, or lines of
color; showing narrow structural bands or lines; <as>as, a
<ex>striated</ex> crystal; <ex>striated</ex> muscular
fiber</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striation</ent><br/
<hw>Stri*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
quality or condition of being striated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stria; <as>as, the <ex>striations</ex> on a
shell</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striatum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stri*a"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The corpus striatum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striature</ent><br/
<hw>Stri"a*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>striatura</ets>.]</ety> <def>A stria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strich</ent><br/
<hw>Strich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>strix</ets>, <ets>strigs</ets>, a streech owl.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An owl.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strick</ent><br/
<hw>Strick</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bunch of hackled flax prepared for
drawing into slivers.</def> <rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stricken</ent><br/
<hw>Strick"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>p. p. & a.</pos> from
<er>Strike</er>. <sn>1.</sn> <def>Struck; smitten; wounded; <as>as, the
<ex>stricken</ex> deer</as>.</def> <note>[See <er>Strike</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Worn out; far gone; advanced. See <er>Strike</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, 21.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Abraham was old and well <qex>stricken</qex> in age.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. xxiv. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Whole; entire; -- said of the hour as marked by the
striking of a clock.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He persevered for a <qex>stricken</qex> hour in such a torrent of
unnecessary tattle.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Speeches are spoken by the <qex>stricken</qex> hour, day after
day, week, perhaps, after week.</q> <rj><qau>Bayne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strickle</ent><br/
<hw>Stric"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Strike</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An instrument to strike grain
to a level with the measure; a strike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument for whetting scythes; a
rifle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>An instrument used for
smoothing the surface of a core.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Carp. & Mason.)</fld> <def>A templet; a
pattern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An instrument used in dressing flax.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strickler</ent><br/
<hw>Stric"kler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Strickle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strickless</ent><br/
<hw>Strick"less</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Strickle</er>.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strict</ent><br/
<hw>Strict</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stricter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Strictest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[L. <ets>strictus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>stringere</ets> to draw or bind tight, to strain. See
<er>Strain</er>, and cf. <er>Strait</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Strained; drawn close; tight; <as>as, a <ex>strict</ex>
embrace; a <ex>strict</ex> ligature</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Tense; not relaxed; <as>as, a <ex>strict</ex>
fiber</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Exact; accurate; precise; rigorously nice; <as>as,
to keep <ex>strict</ex> watch; to pay <ex>strict</ex>
attention</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It shall be still in <qex>strictest</qex> measure.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Governed or governing by exact rules; observing
exact rules; severe; rigorous; <as>as, very <ex>strict</ex> in observing
the Sabbath</as>.</def> <ldquo/Through the <xex>strict</xex>
senteries.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Rigidly; interpreted; exactly limited; confined;
restricted; <as>as, to understand words in a <ex>strict</ex>
sense</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Upright, or straight and narrow;
-- said of the shape of the plants or their flower clusters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Exact; accurate; nice; close; rigorous;
severe.</syn> -- <usage><er>Strict</er>, <er>Severe</er>.
<xex>Strict</xex>, applied to a person, denotes that he conforms in his
motives and acts to a principle or code by which he is bound;
<xex>severe</xex> is strict with an implication often, but not always,
of harshness. <xex>Strict</xex> is opposed to <xex>lax</xex>;
<xex>severe</xex> is opposed to <xex>gentle</xex>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And rules as <qex>strict</qex> his labored work confine,<br/
As if the Stagirite o'erlooked each line.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Soon moved with touch of blame, thus Eve: -<br/
<ldquo/What words have passed thy lips, Adam
<qex>severe</qex>!<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>The Strict Observance</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Friars of the Strict Observance</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(R. C.
Ch.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Observance</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striction</ent><br/
<hw>Stric"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strictio</ets>. See <er>Stringent</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
constricting, or the state of being constricted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Line of striction</b></col> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>, <cd>the
line on a skew surface that cuts each generator in that point of it that
is nearest to the succeeding generator.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strictly</ent><br/
<hw>Strict"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a strict manner; closely;
precisely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strictness</ent><br/
<hw>Strict"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
strict.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stricture</ent><br/
<hw>Stric"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strictura</ets> a contraction, from <ets>stringere</ets>,
<ets>strictum</ets>, to draw tight: cf. F. <ets>stricture</ets>. See
<er>Strict</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Strictness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man of <qex>stricture</qex> and firm abstinence.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stroke; a glance; a touch.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A touch of adverse criticism; censure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[I have] given myself the liberty of these <qex>strictures</qex>
by way of reflection on all and every passage.</q>
<rj><qau>Hammond.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A localized morbid contraction of
any passage of the body. Cf. <cref>Organic stricture</cref>, and
<cref>Spasmodic stricture</cref>, under <er>Organic</er>, and
<er>Spasmodic</er>.</def> <rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strictured</ent><br/
<hw>Stric"tured</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Affected with a stricture; <as>as, a <ex>strictured</ex>
duct</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strid</ent><br/
<hw>Strid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stride</er>.]</ety> <def>A narrow passage between precipitous rocks
or banks, which looks as if it might be crossed at a stride.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Howitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This striding place is called the <qex>Strid</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Wordsworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stride</ent><br/
<hw>Stride</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Strode</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> (<mark>Obs</mark>.
<conjf>Strid</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>); <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stridden</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> (<mark>Obs.</mark>
<conjf>Strid</conjf>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Striding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>str<imac/dan</ets> to
stride, to strive; akin to LG. <ets>striden</ets>, OFries.
<ets>str<imac/da</ets> to strive, D. <ets>strijden</ets> to strive, to
contend, G. <ets>streiten</ets>, OHG. <ets>str<imac/tan</ets>; of
uncertain origin. Cf. <er>Straddle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To walk
with long steps, especially in a measured or pompous manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mars in the middle of the shining shield<br/
Is graved, and <qex>strides</qex> along the liquid field.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To stand with the legs wide apart; to
straddle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stride</ent><br/
<hw>Stride</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pass over at a
step; to step over.</def> <ldquo/A debtor that not dares to
<xex>stride</xex> a limit.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To straddle; to bestride.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I mean to <qex>stride</qex> your steed.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stride</ent><br/
<hw>Stride</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of striding; a long step;
the space measured by a long step; <as>as, a masculine
<ex>stride</ex></as>.</def> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God never meant that man should scale the heavens<br/
By <qex>strides</qex> of human wisdom.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strident</ent><br/
<hw>Stri"dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stridens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>stridere</ets> to
make a grating or creaking noise.]</ety> <def>Characterized by
harshness; grating; shrill.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>strident</xex>
voice.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stridor</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stri"dor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from
<ets>stridere</ets> to make any harsh, grating, or creaking
sound.]</ety> <def>A harsh, shrill, or creaking noise.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stridulate</ent><br/
<hw>Strid"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Stridulous</er>.]</ety> <def>To make a shrill, creaking noise</def>;
specifically <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <def>to make a shrill or musical sound,
such as is made by the males of many insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stridulation</ent><br/
<hw>Strid`u*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
stridulating.</def> Specifically: <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The act of making shrill sounds or musical notes by rubbing
together certain hard parts, as is done by the males of many insects,
especially by Orthoptera, such as crickets, grasshoppers, and
locusts.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The noise itself.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The crickets stridulate by rubbing together the strong
nervures of the fore wings. Many grasshoppers stridulate by rubbing the
hind legs across strong nervures on the fore wings. The green
grasshoppers and katydids stridulate by means of special organs at the
base of the fore wings.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stridulator</ent><br/
<hw>Strid"u*la`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>That which stridulates.</def> <rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stridulatory</ent><br/
<hw>Strid"u*la*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Stridulous;
able to stridulate; used in stridulating; adapted for
stridulation.</def> <rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stridulous</ent><br/
<hw>Strid"u*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stridulus</ets>. See <er>Strident</er>.]</ety> <def>Making a
shrill, creaking sound.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Sarmatian boor driving his <qex>stridulous</qex> cart.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stridulous laryngitis</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
form of croup, or laryngitis, in children, associated with dyspnoea,
occurring usually at night, and marked by crowing or stridulous
breathing.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strife</ent><br/
<hw>Strife</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>estrif</ets>.
See <er>Strive</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of striving;
earnest endeavor.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exertion or contention for superiority; contest of
emulation, either by intellectual or physical efforts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Doting about questions and <qex>strifes</qex> of words.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Tim. vi. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus gods contended -- noble <qex>strife</qex> -<br/
Who most should ease the wants of life.</q>
<rj><qau>Congreve.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Altercation; violent contention; fight;
battle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Twenty of them fought in this black <qex>strife</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These vows, thus granted, raised a <qex>strife</qex> above<br/
Betwixt the god of war and queen of love.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which is contended against; occasion of
contest.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Lamenting her unlucky
<xex>strife</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Contest; struggle; quarrel. See
<er>Contention</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strifeful</ent><br/
<hw>Strife"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Contentious;
discordant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ape was <qex>strifeful</qex> and ambitious.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strigate</ent><br/
<hw>Stri"gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having transverse bands of color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striges</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stri"ges</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L., pl. of
<ets>strix</ets> a streech owl; cf. Gr. <?/ a screaming night
bird.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The tribe of birds which comprises
the owls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strigil</ent><br/
<hw>Strig"il</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strigilis</ets>, from <ets>stringere</ets> to graze, scrape.]</ety>
<fld>(Gr. & Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>An instrument of metal, ivory, etc.,
used for scraping the skin at the bath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strigillose</ent><br/
<hw>Strig"il*lose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Dim. fr.
<ets>strigose</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Set with stiff,
slender bristles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strigine</ent><br/
<hw>Stri"gine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to owls; owl-like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strigment</ent><br/
<hw>Strig"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strigmentum</ets>.]</ety> <def>Scraping; that which is scraped
off.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strigose</ent><br/
<hw>Stri*gose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>strigueux</ets>. See <er>Strigil</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Set with stiff, straight bristles; hispid; <as>as, a
<ex>strigose</ex> leaf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strigous</ent><br/
<hw>Stri"gous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Strigose.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1426 --></p>

<p><ent>Strike</ent><br/
<hw>Strike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Struck</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Struck</conjf>, <conjf>Stricken</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>
(<conjf>Stroock</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>, <conjf>Strucken</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>, <mark>Obs.</mark>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Striking</conjf>. <xex>Struck</xex> is more commonly used in the
p. p. than <xex>stricken</xex>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>striken</ets>
to strike, proceed, flow, AS. <ets>str<imac/can</ets> to go, proceed,
akin to D. <ets>strijken</ets> to rub, stroke, strike, to move, go, G.
<ets>streichen</ets>, OHG. <ets>str<imac/hhan</ets>, L.
<ets>stringere</ets> to touch lightly, to graze, to strip off (but
perhaps not to L. <ets>stringere</ets> in sense to draw tight),
<ets>striga</ets> a row, a furrow. Cf. <er>Streak</er>,
<er>Stroke</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To touch or hit with some
force, either with the hand or with an instrument; to smite; to give a
blow to, either with the hand or with any instrument or
missile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He at Philippi kept<br/
His sword e'en like a dancer; while I <qex>struck</qex><br/
The lean and wrinkled Cassius.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To come in collision with; to strike against;
<as>as, a bullet <ex>struck</ex> him; the wave <ex>struck</ex> the boat
amidships; the ship <ex>struck</ex> a reef</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To give, as a blow; to impel, as with a blow; to
give a force to; to dash; to cast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They shall take of the blood, and <qex>strike</qex> it on the two
sideposts.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xii. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who would be free, themselves must <qex>strike</qex> the blow.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To stamp or impress with a stroke; to coin; <as>as,
to <ex>strike</ex> coin from metal: to <ex>strike</ex> dollars at the
mint</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To thrust in; to cause to enter or penetrate; to set
in the earth; <as>as, a tree <ex>strikes</ex> its roots
deep</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To punish; to afflict; to smite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To punish the just is not good, nor <qex>strike</qex> princes for
equity.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xvii. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To cause to sound by one or more beats; to indicate
or notify by audible strokes; <as>as, the clock <ex>strikes</ex> twelve;
the drums <ex>strike</ex> up a march</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To lower; to let or take down; to remove; <as>as, to
<ex>strike</ex> sail; to <ex>strike</ex> a flag or an ensign, as in
token of surrender; to <ex>strike</ex> a yard or a topmast in a gale; to
<ex>strike</ex> a tent; to <ex>strike</ex> the centering of an
arch</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To make a sudden impression upon, as by a blow; to
affect sensibly with some strong emotion; <as>as, to <ex>strike</ex> the
mind, with surprise; to <ex>strike</ex> one with wonder, alarm, dread,
or horror</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nice works of art <qex>strike</qex> and surprise us most on the
first view.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They please as beauties, here as wonders <qex>strike</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To affect in some particular manner by a sudden
impression or impulse; <as>as, the plan proposed <ex>strikes</ex> me
favorably; to <ex>strike</ex> one dead or blind</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How often has <qex>stricken</qex> you dumb with his irony!</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To cause or produce by a stroke, or suddenly, as by
a stroke; <as>as, to <ex>strike</ex> a light</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Waving wide her myrtle wand,<br/
She <qex>strikes</qex> a universal peace through sea and land.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>To cause to ignite; <as>as, to <ex>strike</ex> a
match</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>To make and ratify; <as>as, to <ex>strike</ex> a
bargain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Probably borrowed from the L. <xex>foedus ferrire</xex>,
to <xex>strike</xex> a compact, so called because an animal was
<xex>struck</xex> and killed as a sacrifice on such
occasions.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>To take forcibly or fraudulently; <as>as, to
<ex>strike</ex> money</as>.</def> <mark>[Old Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <def>To level, as a measure of grain, salt, or the like,
by scraping off with a straight instrument what is above the level of
the top.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>To cut off, as a mortar joint,
even with the face of the wall, or inward at a slight angle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>17.</sn> <def>To hit upon, or light upon, suddenly; <as>as, my
eye <ex>struck</ex> a strange word; they soon <ex>struck</ex> the
trail</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>18.</sn> <def>To borrow money of; to make a demand upon; <as>as,
he <ex>struck</ex> a friend for five dollars</as>.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>19.</sn> <def>To lade into a cooler, as a liquor.</def>
<rj><au>B. Edwards.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>20.</sn> <def>To stroke or pass lightly; to wave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Behold, I thought, He will . . . <qex>strike</qex> his hand over
the place, and recover the leper.</q> <rj><qau>2 Kings v.
11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>21.</sn> <def>To advance; to cause to go forward; -- used only in
past participle.</def> <ldquo/Well <xex>struck</xex> in years.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To strike an attitude</b></col>, <col><b>To strike
a balance</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See under <er>Attitude</er>, and
<er>Balance</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike a jury</b></col>
<fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>to constitute a special jury ordered by a court,
by each party striking out a certain number of names from a prepared
list of jurors, so as to reduce it to the number of persons required by
law.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au> -- <col><b>To strike a lead</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <cd>To find a vein of ore.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Fig.: To find a way to fortune.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <mcol><col><b>To strike a ledger</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>To strike an account</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to
balance it.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike hands with</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>To shake hands with</cd>. <au>Halliwell.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To
make a compact or agreement with; to agree with.</cd> -- <col><b>To
strike off</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To erase from an account; to
deduct; <as>as, <ex>to strike off</ex> the interest of a debt</as></cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Print.)</fld> <cd>To impress; to print; <as>as,
<ex>to strike off</ex> a thousand copies of a book</as></cd>.<-- = to
run off? [copies] --> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To separate by a blow or any
sudden action; <as>as, <ex>to strike off</ex> what is superfluous or
corrupt</as>.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike oil</b></col>, <cd>to find
petroleum when boring for it; figuratively, to make a lucky hit
financially.</cd> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>To strike one
luck</b></col>, <cd>to shake hands with one and wish good luck.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Beau. & Fl.</au> -- <col><b>To strike
out</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To produce by collision; to force out,
<as>as, <ex>to strike out</ex> sparks with steel</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>To blot out; to efface; to erase</cd>. <ldquo/To methodize is as
necessary as <xex>to strike out</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Pope.</au>
<sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To form by a quick effort; to devise; to invent; to
contrive, <as>as, <ex>to strike out</ex> a new plan of
finance</as>.</cd> <sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <cd>To cause a
player to strike out; -- said of the pitcher. See <cref>To strike
out</cref>, under <er>Strike</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>To
strike sail</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Sail</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To
strike up</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To cause to sound; to begin to
beat</cd>. <ldquo/<xex>Strike up</xex> the drums.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To begin to sing or play; as, <xex>to strike up</xex> a
tune.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To raise (as sheet metal), in making diahes,
pans, etc., by blows or pressure in a die.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike
work</b></col>, <cd>to quit work; to go on a strike.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strike</ent><br/
<hw>Strike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To move; to advance;
to proceed; to take a course; <as>as, to <ex>strike</ex> into the
fields</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A mouse . . . <qex>struck</qex> forth sternly [bodily].</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To deliver a quick blow or thrust; to give
blows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And fiercely took his trenchant blade in hand,<br/
With which he <qex>stroke</qex> so furious and so fell.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Strike</qex> now, or else the iron cools.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To hit; to collide; to dush; to clash; <as>as, a
hammer <ex>strikes</ex> against the bell of a clock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To sound by percussion, with blows, or as with
blows; to be struck; <as>as, the clock <ex>strikes</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A deep sound <qex>strikes</qex> like a rising knell.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To make an attack; to aim a blow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A puny subject <qex>strikes</qex><br/
At thy great glory.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Struck</qex> for throne, and <qex>striking</qex> found his
doom.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To touch; to act by appulse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hinder light but from <qex>striking</qex> on it [porphyry], and
its colors vanish.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To run upon a rock or bank; to be stranded; <as>as,
the ship <ex>struck</ex> in the night</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To pass with a quick or strong effect; to dart; to
penetrate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Till a dart <qex>strike</qex> through his liver.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. vii. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now and then a glittering beam of wit or passion
<qex>strikes</qex> through the obscurity of the poem.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To break forth; to commence suddenly; -- with
<xex>into</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>strike</ex> into reputation; to
<ex>strike</ex> into a run</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To lower a flag, or colors, in token of respect, or
to signify a surrender of a ship to an enemy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That the English ships of war should not <qex>strike</qex> in the
Danish seas.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To quit work in order to compel an increase, or
prevent a reduction, of wages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>To become attached to something; -- said of the
spat of oysters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>To steal money.</def> <mark>[Old Slang,
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Nares.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To strike at</b></col>, <cd>to aim a blow at.</cd> --
<col><b>To strike for</b></col>, <cd>to start suddenly on a course
for.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike home</b></col>, <cd>to give a blow which
reaches its object, to strike with effect.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike
in</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To enter suddenly</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>To disappear from the surface, with internal effects, as an eruptive
disease</cd>. <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>To come in suddenly; to interpose; to
interrupt</cd>. <ldquo/I proposed the embassy of Constantinople for Mr.
Henshaw, but my Lord Winchelsea <xex>struck in</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Evelyn.</au> <sd>(d)</sd> <cd>To join in after another has begun,as
in singing.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike in with</b></col>, <cd>to conform
to; to suit itself to; to side with, to join with at once.</cd>
<ldquo/To assert this is <xex>to strike in with</xex> the known enemies
of God's grace.<rdquo/ <au>South.</au> -- <col><b>To strike
out</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To start; to wander; to make a sudden
excursion; <as>as, <ex>to strike out</ex> into an irregular course of
life</as>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To strike with full force</cd>.
<sd>(c)</sd> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <cd>To be put out for not hitting the
ball during one's turn at the bat.</cd> -- <col><b>To strike
up</b></col>, <cd>to commence to play as a musician; to begin to sound,
as an instrument.</cd> <ldquo/Whilst any trump did sound, or drum
<xex>struck up</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strike</ent><br/
<hw>Strike</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
striking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An instrument with a straight edge for leveling a
measure of grain, salt, and the like, scraping off what is above the
level of the top; a strickle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A bushel; four pecks.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An old measure of four bushels.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Fullness of measure; hence, excellence of
quality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Three hogsheads of ale of the first <qex>strike</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An iron pale or standard in a gate or fence.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>The act of quitting work; specifically, such an act
by a body of workmen, usually organized by a labor union, done as a
means of enforcing compliance with demands made on their
employer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Strikes</qex> are the insurrections of labor.</q> <rj><qau>F.
A. Walker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Iron Working)</fld> <def>A puddler's
stirrer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>The horizontal direction of the
outcropping edges of tilted rocks; or, the direction of a horizontal
line supposed to be drawn on the surface of a tilted stratum. It is at
right angles to the dip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>The extortion of money, or the attempt to extort
money, by threat of injury; blackmailing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A sudden finding of rich ore in mining; hence, any
sudden success or good fortune, esp. financial.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Bowling, U. S.)</fld> <def>The act of leveling all
the pins with the first bowl; also, the score thus made. Sometimes
called <altname>double spare</altname>. Throwing a strike entitles the
player to add to the score for that frame the total number of pins
knocked down in the next two bowls.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>Any actual or constructive
striking at the pitched ball, three of which, if the ball is not hit
fairly, cause the batter to be put out; hence, any of various acts or
events which are ruled as equivalent to such a striking, as failing to
strike at a ball so pitched that the batter should have struck at
it.</def> <ldquo/It's one, two, three <ex>strikes</ex> you're out in the
old ball game.<rdquo/ <au>[Take me out to the ball game]</au><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Tenpins)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Ten-strike</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strike block</b></col> <fld>(Carp.)</fld>, <cd>a plane
shorter than a jointer, used for fitting a short joint.</cd>
<au>Moxon.</au> -- <col><b>Strike of flax</b></col>, <cd>a handful that
may be hackled at once.</cd> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>Strike of sugar</b></col>. <fld>(Sugar
Making)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The act of emptying the teache, or last
boiler, in which the cane juice is exposed to heat, into the
coolers</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The quantity of the sirup thus emptied at
once.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strikeout</ent><br/
<hw>Strike"out</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Baseball)</fld>
<def>an out made by a batter against whom three strikes were called in a
single at-bat; also, the same out as credited to the pitcher, in the
scoring of the game.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striker</ent><br/
<hw>Strik"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, strikes; specifically, a blacksmith's helper who wields
the sledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A harpoon; also, a harpooner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wherever we come to an anchor, we always send out our
<qex>strikers</qex>, and put out hooks and lines overboard, to try
fish.</q> <rj><qau>Dampier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A wencher; a lewd man.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A workman who is on a strike.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A blackmailer in politics; also, one whose political
influence can be bought.</def> <mark>[Political Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strike zone</ent><br/
<hw>Strike" zone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Baseball)</fld>
<def>the area above home plate where, if a pitched ball passes through
and is not hit by the batter, the pitch counts as a strike.
Specifically, it is directly above home plate and at a height from the
knees to the shoulders of the batter.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striking</ent><br/
<hw>Strik"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Strike</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Striking distance</b></col>, <cd>the distance through
which an object can be reached by striking; the distance at which a
force is effective when directed to a particular object.</cd><-- the
distance which can be covered in one easy leg of a journey. --> --
<col><b>Striking plate</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The plate against
which the latch of a door lock strikes as the door is closed</cd>.
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A part of the centering of an arch, which is driven
back to loosen the centering in striking it.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striking</ent><br/
<hw>Strik"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affecting with strong emotions;
surprising; forcible; impressive; very noticeable; <as>as, a
<ex>striking</ex> representation or image; a <ex>striking</ex>
resemblance.</as></def> <ldquo/A <xex>striking</xex> fact.<rdquo/ <au>De
Quincey.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Strik"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Strik"ing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strikle</ent><br/
<hw>Strik"le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Strickle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>String</ent><br/
<hw>String</hw> <pr>(str<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>string</ets>, <ets>streng</ets>, AS. <ets>streng</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>streng</ets>, G. <ets>strang</ets>, Icel. <ets>strengr</ets>, Sw.
<ets>str<aum/ng</ets>, Dan. <ets>straeng</ets>; probably from the adj.,
E. <ets>strong</ets> (see <er>Strong</er>); or perhaps originally
meaning, twisted, and akin to E. <ets>strangle</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A small cord, a line, a twine, or a slender strip of leather, or
other substance, used for binding together, fastening, or tying things;
a cord, larger than a thread and smaller than a rope; <as>as, a shoe
<ex>string</ex>; a bonnet <ex>string</ex>; a silken
<ex>string</ex>.</as></def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Round Ormond's knee thou tiest the mystic <qex>string</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A thread or cord on which a number of objects or
parts are strung or arranged in close and orderly succession; hence, a
line or series of things arranged on a thread, or as if so arranged; a
succession; a concatenation; a chain; <as>as, a <ex>string</ex> of
shells or beads; a <ex>string</ex> of dried apples; a <ex>string</ex> of
houses; a <ex>string</ex> of arguments.</as></def> <ldquo/A
<xex>string</xex> of islands.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Gibbon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A strip, as of leather, by which the covers of a
book are held together.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The cord of a musical instrument, as of a piano,
harp, or violin; specifically (<pluf>pl.</pluf>), the stringed
instruments of an orchestra, in distinction from the wind instruments;
<as>as, the <ex>strings</ex> took up the theme</as>.</def> <ldquo/An
instrument of ten <xex>strings</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ps. xxx. iii.
2.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Me softer airs befit, and softer <qex>strings</qex><br/
Of lute, or viol still.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The line or cord of a bow.</def> <rj><au>Ps. xi.
2.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He twangs the grieving <qex>string</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A fiber, as of a plant; a little, fibrous
root.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Duckweed putteth forth a little <qex>string</qex> into the water,
from the bottom.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A nerve or tendon of an animal body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>string</qex> of his tongue was loosed.</q> <rj><qau>Mark
vii. 35.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>An inside range of ceiling
planks, corresponding to the sheer strake on the outside and bolted to
it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The tough fibrous substance that
unites the valves of the pericap of leguminous plants, and which is
readily pulled off; <as>as, the <ex>strings</ex> of
beans</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A small, filamentous
ramification of a metallic vein.</def> <rj><au>Ure.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Stringcourse</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <fld>(Billiards)</fld> <def>The points made in a
game.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>In various indoor games, a score or
tally, sometimes, as in American billiard games, marked by buttons
threaded on a string or wire.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In various games,
competitions, etc., a certain number of turns at play, of rounds,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <fld>(Billiards & Pool)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The line
from behind and over which the cue ball must be played after being out
of play as by being pocketed or knocked off the table; -- called also
<altname>string line</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Act of stringing
for break.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <def>A hoax; a trumped-up or <ldquo/fake<rdquo/
story.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <def>a sequence of similar objects or events
sufficiently close in time or space to be perceived as a group; <as>a
<ex>string</ex> of accidents; a <ex>string</ex> of restaurants on a
highway</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>17.</sn> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A one-dimensional string-like
mathematical object used as a means of representing the properties of
fundamental particles in <er>string theory</er>, one theory of particle
physics; such hypothetical objects are one-dimensional and very small
(10<exp>-33</exp> cm) but exist in more than four spatial dimensions,
and have various modes of vibration. Considering particles as
<ex>strings</ex> avoids some of the problems of treating particles as
points, and allows a unified treatment of gravity along with the other
three forces (electromagnetism, the weak force, and the strong force) in
a manner consistent with quantum mechanics. See also <er>string
theory</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>String band</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>a band of
musicians using only, or chiefly, stringed instruments.</cd> --
<col><b>String beans</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A dish prepared from
the unripe pods of several kinds of beans; -- so called because the
strings are stripped off</cd>. <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Any kind of beans in
which the pods are used for cooking before the seeds are ripe; usually,
the low bush bean.</cd> -- <col><b>To have two strings to one's
bow</b></col>, <cd>to have a means or expedient in reserve in case the
one employed fails.</cd></cs> <-- = to have other strings in one's bow
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>String</ent><br/
<hw>String</hw> <pr>(str<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Strung</conjf> <pr>(str<ucr/ng)</pr>;
<pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Strung</conjf> (<mark>R.</mark>
<conjf>Stringed</conjf> <pr>(str<icr/ngd)</pr>); <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stringing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To furnish
with strings; <as>as, to <ex>string</ex> a violin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Has not wise nature <qex>strung</qex> the legs and feet<br/
With firmest nerves, designed to walk the street?</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put in tune the strings of, as a stringed
instrument, in order to play upon it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For here the Muse so oft her harp has <qex>strung</qex>,<br/
That not a mountain rears its head unsung.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To put on a string; to file; <as>as, to
<ex>string</ex> beads</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make tense; to strengthen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Toil <qex>strung</qex> the nerves, and purified the blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To deprive of strings; to strip the strings from;
<as>as, to <ex>string</ex> beans</as>. See <er>String</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 9.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To hoax; josh; jolly; often used with
<ptcl>along</ptcl>; <as>as, we <ex>strung</ex> him <ex>along</ex> all
day until he realized we were kidding</as>.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>String</ent><br/
<hw>String</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To form into a string or strings,
as a substance which is stretched, or people who are moving along,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringboard</ent><br/
<hw>String"board`</hw> <pr>(-b<omac/rd`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same
as <er>Stringpiece</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringcourse</ent><br/
<hw>String"course`</hw> <pr>(-k<omac/rs`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A horizontal band in a building, forming a part
of the design, whether molded, projecting, or carved, or in any way
distinguished from the rest of the work.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringed</ent><br/
<hw>Stringed</hw> <pr>(str<icr/ngd)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having strings; <as>as, a <ex>stringed</ex> instrument</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Ps. cl. 4.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Produced by strings.</def> <ldquo/Answering the
<xex>stringed</xex> noise.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringency</ent><br/
<hw>Strin"gen*cy</hw> <pr>(str<icr/n"j<eit/n*s<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The quality or state of being stringent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringendo</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strin"gen*do</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Urging or hastening the time, as to a
climax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringent</ent><br/
<hw>Strin"gent</hw> <pr>(str<icr/n"j<eit/nt)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>stringens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>stringere</ets> to draw or bind tight. See <er>Strain</er>.]</ety>
<def>Binding strongly; making strict requirements; restrictive; rigid;
severe; <as>as, <ex>stringent</ex> rules</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They must be subject to a sharper penal code, and to a more
<qex>stringent</qex> code of procedure.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Strin"gent*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Strin"gent*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringer</ent><br/
<hw>String"er</hw> <pr>(str<icr/ng"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>One who strings; one who makes or provides strings,
especially for bows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be content to put your trust in honest <qex>stringers</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Ascham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A libertine; a wencher.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Railroad)</fld> <def>A longitudinal
sleeper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>A streak of planking
carried round the inside of a vessel on the under side of the
beams.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>A long horizontal timber to
connect uprights in a frame, or to support a floor or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Newspapers)</fld> <def>A reporter or correspondent
who works for a news agency on a part-time basis, especially one
covering local news for a newspaper published in a different area; --
called also <altname>string correspondent</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Aviation)</fld> <def>a longitudinal supporting
structure to reinforce the skin of an airplane fuselage.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringhalt</ent><br/
<hw>String"halt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Far.)</fld>
<def>An habitual sudden twitching of the hinder leg of a horse, or an
involuntary or convulsive contraction of the muscles that raise the
hock.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>springhalt</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringiness</ent><br/
<hw>String"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
stringy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringless</ent><br/
<hw>String"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no strings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His tongue is now a <qex>stringless</qex> instrument.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringpiece</ent><br/
<hw>String"piece`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A long piece of timber, forming a margin or edge of
any piece of construction; esp.:</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>One of the
longitudinal pieces, supporting the treads and rises of a flight or run
of stairs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>string theory</ent><br/
<hw>string" the`o*ry</hw> <pr>(str<icr/ng" th<emac/`<osl/*r<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>A mathematical theory for
describing the properties of fundamental particles, which represents the
particles as one-dimensional string-like objects, which exist in the
normal four dimensions of space-time plus additional dimensions, the
total dimensions being ten, eleven, or twenty-six depending on the
version of the theory. The properties of fundamental particles in string
theory and their manner of interaction with each other depend upon the
modes of vibration of the strings{17}. The attractiveness of this theory
rests in part on its ability to provide a unified treatment of gravity
as well as the three other basic forces of nature, in a manner
consistent with quantum mechanics. The great difficulty of doing the
calculations required by the theory, however, has thus far (1999) made
it impossible to calculate the observable properties, such as the mass,
of known particles, such as the electron, proton, mesons, quarks, and
neutron; thus there is as yet no experimental verification for the
theory. The most popular version of the theory depends on a mathematical
property called supersymmetry, and the theory derived form this
principle is properly called <altname>superstring theory</altname>, a
term which is often used interchangeably with <ex>string theory</ex>.
See also string{17}.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stringy</ent><br/
<hw>String"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Consisting of strings, or small threads; fibrous; filamentous;
<as>as, a <ex>stringy</ex> root</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of being drawn into a string, as a glutinous
substance; ropy; viscid; gluely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stringy bark</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a name
given in Australia to several trees of the genus <gen>Eucalyptus</gen>
(as <spn>Eucalyptus amygdalina</spn>, <spn>Eucalyptus obliqua</spn>,
<spn>Eucalyptus capitellata</spn>, <spn>Eucalyptus macrorhyncha</spn>,
<spn>Eucalyptus piperita</spn>, <spn>Eucalyptus pilularis</spn>, <and/
<spn>Eucalyptus tetradonta</spn>), which have a fibrous bark used by the
aborigines for making cordage and cloth.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strip</ent><br/
<hw>Strip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stripped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stripping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stripen</ets>, <ets>strepen</ets>, AS. <ets>str<?/pan</ets> in
be<ets>str<?/pan</ets> to plunder; akin to D. <ets>stroopen</ets>, MHG.
<ets>stroufen</ets>, G. <ets>streifen</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
deprive; to bereave; to make destitute; to plunder; especially, to
deprive of a covering; to skin; to peel; <as>as, to <ex>strip</ex> a man
of his possession, his rights, his privileges, his reputation; to
<ex>strip</ex> one of his clothes; to <ex>strip</ex> a beast of his
skin; to <ex>strip</ex> a tree of its bark.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>strippen</qex> her out of her rude array.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>stripped</qex> Joseph out of his coat.</q> <rj><qau>Gen.
xxxvii. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Opinions which . . . no clergyman could have avowed without
imminent risk of being <qex>stripped</qex> of his gown.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To divest of clothing; to uncover.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before the folk herself <qex>strippeth</qex> she.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Strip</qex> your sword stark naked.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To dismantle; <as>as, to
<ex>strip</ex> a ship of rigging, spars, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>To pare off the surface of, as
land, in strips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To deprive of all milk; to milk dry; to draw the
last milk from; hence, to milk with a peculiar movement of the hand on
the teats at the last of a milking; <as>as, to <ex>strip</ex> a
cow</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To pass; to get clear of; to outstrip.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When first they <qex>stripped</qex> the Malean promontory.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before he reached it he was out of breath,<br/
And then the other <qex>stripped</qex> him.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. &
Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To pull or tear off, as a covering; to remove; to
wrest away; <as>as, to <ex>strip</ex> the skin from a beast; to
<ex>strip</ex> the bark from a tree; to <ex>strip</ex> the clothes from
a man's back; to <ex>strip</ex> away all disguises.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>strip</qex> bad habits from a corrupted heart, is
<qex>stripping</qex> off the skin.</q> <rj><qau>Gilpin.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To tear off (the
thread) from a bolt or nut; <as>as, the thread is
<ex>stripped</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To tear off the thread
from (a bolt or nut); <as>as, the bolt is
<ex>stripped</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To remove the metal coating from (a plated article),
as by acids or electrolytic action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Carding)</fld> <def>To remove fiber, flock, or
lint from; -- said of the teeth of a card when it becomes partly
clogged.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To pick the cured leaves from the stalks of
(tobacco) and tie them into <ldquo/hands<rdquo/; to remove the midrib
from (tobacco leaves).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- strip mine. A mine in which the unwanted layers (called the
overburdewn) above the desirable ore is stripped, i.e. removed by
excavation, leaving a pit in which the ore is exposed; in contrast with
mines in which the ore is accessed and removed through a shaft or
tunnel, without removing the layers of earth above it. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- striptease, an act in which a performer (usu. female) removes her
clothing piece by piece; -- often performed to musical accompaniment. It
was popular in burlesque theaters. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1427 --></p>

<p><ent>Strip</ent><br/
<hw>Strip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take
off, or become divested of, clothes or covering; to undress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>To fail in the thread; to lose
the thread, as a bolt, screw, or nut. See <er>Strip</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, 8.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strip</ent><br/
<hw>Strip</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A narrow piece, or one
comparatively long; <as>as, a <ex>strip</ex> of cloth; a <ex>strip</ex>
of land.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A trough for washing
ore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gunnery)</fld> <def>The issuing of a projectile
from a rifled gun without acquiring the spiral motion.</def>
<rj><au>Farrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stripe</ent><br/
<hw>Stripe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OD. <ets>strijpe</ets>
a stripe, streak; akin to LG. <ets>stripe</ets>, D. <ets>streep</ets>,
Dan. <ets>stribe</ets>, G. <ets>strief</ets>, <ets>striefen</ets>, MHG.
<ets>striefen</ets> to glide, march.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A line, or
long, narrow division of anything of a different color or structure from
the ground; hence, any linear variation of color or structure; <as>as, a
<ex>stripe</ex>, or streak, of red on a green ground; a raised
<ex>stripe</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>A pattern produced by arranging
the warp threads in sets of alternating colors, or in sets presenting
some other contrast of appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A strip, or long, narrow piece attached to something
of a different color; <as>as, a red or blue <ex>stripe</ex> sewed upon a
garment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A stroke or blow made with a whip, rod, scourge, or
the like, such as usually leaves a mark.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Forty <qex>stripes</qex> he may give him, and not exceed.</q>
<rj><qau>Deut. xxv. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A long, narrow discoloration of the skin made by the
blow of a lash, rod, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cruelty marked him with inglorious <qex>stripes</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Color indicating a party or faction; hence,
distinguishing characteristic; sign; likeness; sort; <as>as, persons of
the same political <ex>stripe</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>The chevron on
the coat of a noncommissioned officer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stars and Stripes</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Star</er>,
<pos>n.</pos></cd></cs> <-- To earn one's stripes, to acquire recognized
credentials by competent performance at a germane task. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stripe</ent><br/
<hw>Stripe</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Striped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Striping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make stripes
upon; to form with lines of different colors or textures; to variegate
with stripes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strike; to lash.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striped</ent><br/
<hw>Striped</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having stripes of
different colors; streaked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Striped bass</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Bass</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Striped maple</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a slender American tree (<spn>Acer
Pennsylvanicum</spn>) with finely striped bark. Called also
<altname>striped dogwood</altname>, and
<altname>moosewood</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Striped mullet</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Mullet</er>, 2.</cd> --
<col><b>Striped snake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the garter
snake.</cd> -- <col><b>Striped squirrel</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the chipmunk.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strip-leaf</ent><br/
<hw>Strip"-leaf`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Tobacco which has
been stripped of its stalks before packing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stripling</ent><br/
<hw>Strip"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>strip</ets>; as if a small strip from the main stock or
steam.]</ety> <def>A youth in the state of adolescence, or just passing
from boyhood to manhood; a lad.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Inquire thou whose son the <qex>stripling</qex> is.</q> <rj><qau>1
Sam. xvii. 56.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stripper</ent><br/
<hw>Strip"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, strips;</def> <specif>specifically,</specif> <def>a
machine for stripping cards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>A cow that has nearly stopped
giving milk, so that it can be obtained from her only by
stripping.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>A harvesting machine that strips
the seed from the stalks of grain plants.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>a solvent or chemical solution used to remove
coatings of paint, varnish, lacquer, etc. prior to refinishing a
surface.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>a person (usually a woman) who strips off her
clothes as an entertainment performance; one who does a strip-tease; --
also called an <altname>ecdysiast</altname>, <altname>exotic
dancer</altname>, or <altname>strip-teaser</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strippet</ent><br/
<hw>Strip"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Dim. of
<ets>strip</ets>.]</ety> <def>A small stream.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/A little brook or <xex>strippet</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stripping</ent><br/
<hw>Strip"ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of one who strips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mutual bows and courtesies . . . are remants of the original
prostrations and <qex>strippings</qex> of the captive.</q> <rj><qau>H.
Spencer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Never were cows that required such <qex>stripping</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Mrs. Gaskell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The last milk drawn from a cow at a
milking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strisores</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stri*so"res</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.; cf.
L. <ets>stridere</ets> to creak, whiz, buzz.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of passerine birds including the humming birds, swifts,
and goatsuckers. It is now generally considered an artificial
group.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strive</ent><br/
<hw>Strive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Strove</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Striven</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> (<mark>Rarely</mark>,
<conjf>Strove</conjf>); <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Striving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>estriver</ets>; of
Teutonic origin, and akin to G. <ets>streben</ets>, D.
<ets>streven</ets>, Dan. <ets>straebe</ets>, Sw. <ets>str<aum/fva</ets>.
Cf. <er>Strife</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make efforts; to use
exertions; to endeavor with earnestness; to labor hard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Was for this his ambition <qex>strove</qex><br/
To equal Caesar first, and after, Jove?</q>
<rj><qau>Cowley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To struggle in opposition; to be in contention or
dispute; to contend; to contest; -- followed by <xex>against</xex> or
<xex>with</xex> before the person or thing opposed; <as>as,
<ex>strive</ex> against temptation; <ex>strive</ex> for the
truth</as>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My Spirit shall not always <qex>strive</qex> with man.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. vi. 3.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why dost thou <qex>strive</qex> against him?</q> <rj><qau>Job
xxxiii. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now private pity <qex>strove</qex> with public hate,<br/
Reason with rage, and eloquence with fate.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To vie; to compete; to be a rival.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Not] that sweet grove<br/
Of Daphne, by Orontes and the inspired<br/
Castalian spring, might with this paradise<br/
Of Eden <qex>strive</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To contend; vie; struggle; endeavor;
aim.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strive</ent><br/
<hw>Strive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An effort; a
striving.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Strife; contention.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wyclif (luke xxi. 9).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strived</ent><br/
<hw>Strived</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>p. p.</pos>
<mord>of <er>Strive</er></mord>. <def>Striven.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yea, so have I <qex>strived</qex> to preach the gospel.</q>
<rj><qau>Rom. xv. 20.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striven</ent><br/
<hw>Striv"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Strive</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striver</ent><br/
<hw>Striv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
strives.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Striving</ent><br/
<hw>Striv"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Strive</er>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Striv"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strix</ent><br/
<hw>Strix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>strix</ets>,
<ets>strigis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>One of the flutings
of a column.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroam</ent><br/
<hw>Stroam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Prov. E.
<ets>strome</ets> to walk with long strides.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
wander about idly and vacantly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To take long strides in walking.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strobila</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stro*bi"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Strobilae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ anything
twisted, a pine cone.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A form
of the larva of certain Discophora in a state of development succeeding
the scyphistoma. The body of the strobila becomes elongated, and
subdivides transversely into a series of lobate segments which
eventually become ephyrae, or young medusae.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
mature tapeworm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strobilaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Strob`i*la"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Strobila</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Of or
pertaining to a strobile or cone.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Producing
strobiles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strobilation</ent><br/
<hw>Strob`i*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The act or phenomenon of spontaneously dividing transversely, as do
certain species of annelids and helminths; transverse fission. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Syllidian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strobile</ent><br/
<hw>Strob"ile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strobilus</ets> a pine cone, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>strobole</ets>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>strobil</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A scaly
multiple fruit resulting from the ripening of an ament in certain
plants, as the hop or pine; a cone. See <er>Cone</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>An individual asexually producing
sexual individuals differing from itself also in other respects, as the
tapeworm, -- one of the forms that occur in metagenesis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Strobila</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strobiliform</ent><br/
<hw>Stro*bil"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Shaped like a
strobile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strobiline</ent><br/
<hw>Strob"i*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a strobile; strobilaceous; strobiliform; <as>as, <ex>strobiline</ex>
fruits</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroboscope</ent><br/
<hw>Strob"o*scope</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
whirling + <ets>-scope</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An instrument for
studying or observing the successive phases of a periodic or varying
motion by means of light which is periodically interrupted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An optical toy similar to the phenakistoscope. See
<er>Phenakistoscope</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strockle</ent><br/
<hw>Stroc"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Glass Manuf.)</fld>
<def>A shovel with a turned-up edge, for frit, sand, etc.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>strocal</asp>, <asp>strocle</asp>,
<asp>strokal</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strode</ent><br/
<hw>Strode</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Strude</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strode</ent><br/
<hw>Strode</hw>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of <er>Stride</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroke</ent><br/
<hw>Stroke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Strike</er></mord>. <def>Struck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroke</ent><br/
<hw>Stroke</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>strok</ets>,
<ets>strook</ets>, <ets>strak</ets>, fr. <ets>striken</ets>. See
<er>Strike</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
striking; a blow; a hit; a knock; esp., a violent or hostile attack made
with the arm or hand, or with an instrument or weapon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His hand fetcheth a <qex>stroke</qex> with the ax to cut down the
tree.</q> <rj><qau>Deut. xix. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A fool's lips enter into contention and his mouth calleth for
<qex>strokes</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xviii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He entered and won the whole kingdom of Naples without striking a
<qex>stroke</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The result of effect of a striking; injury or
affliction; soreness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the day that Lord bindeth up the breach of his people, and
healeth the <qex>stroke</qex> of their wound.</q> <rj><qau>Isa. xxx.
26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The striking of the clock to tell the
hour.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Well, but what's o'clock?<br/
- Upon the <qex>stroke</qex> of ten. -- Well, let is strike.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A gentle, caressing touch or movement upon
something; a stroking.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A mark or dash in writing or printing; a line; the
touch of a pen or pencil; <as>as, an up <ex>stroke</ex>; a firm
<ex>stroke</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O, lasting as those colors may they shine,<br/
Free as thy <qex>stroke</qex>, yet faultless as thy line.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Hence, by extension, an addition or amandment to a
written composition; a touch; <as>as, to give some finishing
<ex>strokes</ex> to an essay</as>.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A sudden attack of disease; especially, a fatal
attack; a severe disaster; any affliction or calamity, especially a
sudden one; <as>as, a <ex>stroke</ex> of apoplexy; the <ex>stroke</ex>
of death</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At this one <qex>stroke</qex> the man looked dead in law.</q>
<rj><qau>Harte.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A throb or beat, as of the heart.</def>
<rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>One of a series of beats or movements against a
resisting medium, by means of which movement through or upon it is
accomplished; <as>as, the <ex>stroke</ex> of a bird's wing in flying, or
an oar in rowing, of a skater, swimmer, etc.</as></def>; also:
<fld>(Rowing)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The rate of succession of stroke;
<as>as, a quick <ex>stroke</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The oar
nearest the stern of a boat, by which the other oars are guided; --
called also <altname>stroke oar</altname>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
rower who pulls the stroke oar; the strokesman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A powerful or sudden effort by which something is
done, produced, or accomplished; also, something done or accomplished by
such an effort; <as>as, a <ex>stroke</ex> of genius; a <ex>stroke</ex>
of business; a master <ex>stroke</ex> of policy.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>The movement, in either
direction, of the piston plunger, piston rod, crosshead, etc., as of a
steam engine or a pump, in which these parts have a reciprocating
motion; <as>as, the forward <ex>stroke</ex> of a piston</as>; also, the
entire distance passed through, as by a piston, in such a movement;
<as>as, the piston is at half <ex>stroke</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The respective strokes are distinguished as
<xex>up</xex> and <xex>down</xex> strokes, <xex>outward</xex> and
<xex>inward</xex> strokes, <xex>forward</xex> and <xex>back</xex>
strokes, the forward stroke in stationary steam engines being toward the
crosshead, but in locomotives toward the front of the
vehicle.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>Power; influence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Where money beareth [hath] all the <xex>stroke</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Robynson (More's Utopia).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He has a great <qex>stroke</qex> with the reader.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>Appetite.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To keep stroke</b></col>, <cd>to make strokes in
unison.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The oars where silver,<br/
Which to the tune of flutes <qex>kept stroke</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroke</ent><br/
<hw>Stroke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Strokeed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Strokeing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stroken</ets>, <ets>straken</ets>, AS. <ets>str<amac/cian</ets>,
fr. <ets>str<imac/can</ets> to go over, pass. See <er>Strike</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Straggle</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
strike.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye mote with the plat sword again<br/
<qex>Stroken</qex> him in the wound, and it will close.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To rib gently in one direction; especially, to pass
the hand gently over by way of expressing kindness or tenderness; to
caress; to soothe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He dried the falling drops, and, yet more kind,<br/
He <qex>stroked</qex> her cheeks.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make smooth by rubbing.</def>
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Masonry)</fld> <def>To give a finely fluted surface
to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To row the stroke oar of; <as>as, to <ex>stroke</ex>
a boat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroker</ent><br/
<hw>Strok"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who strokes;
also, one who pretends to cure by stroking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cures worked by Greatrix the <qex>stroker</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strokesman</ent><br/
<hw>Strokes"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Strokesman</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Rowing)</fld> <def>The
man who rows the aftermost oar, and whose stroke is to be followed by
the rest.</def> <rj><au>Totten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroking</ent><br/
<hw>Strok"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of rubbing gently with the hand, or of smoothing; a stroke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I doubt not with one gentle <qex>stroking</qex> to wipe away ten
thousand tears.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Needlework)</fld> <def>The act of laying small
gathers in cloth in regular order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>See <er>Stripping</er>, 2.</def>
<rj><au>Smollett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroll</ent><br/
<hw>Stroll</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Strolled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Strolling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Dan.
<ets>stryge</ets> to stroll, Sw. <ets>stryka</ets> to stroke, to ramble,
dial. Sw. <ets>strykel</ets> one who strolls about, Icel.
<ets>strj<?/ka</ets> to stroke, D. <ets>struikelen</ets> to stumble, G.
<ets>straucheln</ets>. Cf. <er>Struggle</er>.]</ety> <def>To wander on
foot; to ramble idly or leisurely; to rove.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These mothers <qex>stroll</qex> to beg sustenance for their
helpless infants.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To rove; roam; range; stray.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroll</ent><br/
<hw>Stroll</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wandering on foot; an idle and
leisurely walk; a ramble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroller</ent><br/
<hw>Stroll"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who strolls; a
vagrant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stro"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stromata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a bed covering, Gr.
<?/ a couch or bed.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>The connective tissue or supporting framework of an organ; <as>as,
the <ex>stroma</ex> of the kidney</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The
spongy, colorless framework of a red blood corpuscle or other
cell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A layer or mass of cellular
tissue, especially that part of the thallus of certain fungi which
incloses the perithecia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stromatic</ent><br/
<hw>Stro*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ coverlet
of a bed, pl. <?/ patchwork (for such a coverlet), also applied to
several <ets>miscellaneous</ets> writings, fr. <?/ anything spread out
for resting upon, a bed, fr. <?/ to spread out.]</ety>
<def>Miscellaneous; composed of different kinds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stromatology</ent><br/
<hw>Stro`ma*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<?/, a bed + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>The
history of the formation of stratified rocks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stromb</ent><br/
<hw>Stromb</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
marine univalve mollusk of the genus Strombus and allied genera. See
<er>Conch</er>, and <er>Strombus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strombite</ent><br/
<hw>Strom"bite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Paleon.)</fld>
<def>A fossil shell of the genus Strombus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stromboid</ent><br/
<hw>Strom"boid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Strombus</ets> + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or like, Strombus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strombuliform</ent><br/
<hw>Strom*bu"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>strombulus</ets>, dim. of <ets>strombus + -form</ets>. See
<er>Strombus</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Formed or
shaped like a top.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Coiled into the shape of a screw
or a helix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strombus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strom"bus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of marine gastropods in
which the shell has the outer lip dilated into a broad wing. It includes
many large and handsome species commonly called <altname>conch
shells</altname>, or <altname>conchs</altname>. See
<er>Conch</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stromeyerite</ent><br/
<hw>Stro"mey`er*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named from
the German chemist Friedrich <ets>Stromeyer</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A steel-gray mineral of metallic luster. It is a
sulphide of silver and copper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strond</ent><br/
<hw>Strond</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Strand; beach.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1428 --></p>

<p><ent>Strong</ent><br/
<hw>Strong</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Stronger</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Strongest</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS.
<ets>strang</ets>, <ets>strong</ets>; akin to D. & G. <ets>streng</ets>
strict, rigorous, OHG. <ets>strengi</ets> strong, brave, harsh, Icel.
<ets>strangr</ets> strong, severe, Dan. <ets>streng</ets>, Sw.
<ets>str<aum/ng</ets> strict, severe. Cf. <er>Strength</er>,
<er>Stretch</er>, <er>String</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Having active
physical power, or great physical power to act; having a power of
exerting great bodily force; vigorous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That our oxen may be <qex>strong</qex> to labor.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
cxliv. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Orses the <qex>strong</qex> to greater strength must yield.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having passive physical power; having ability to
bear or endure; firm; hale; sound; robust; <as>as, a <ex>strong</ex>
constitution; <ex>strong</ex> health.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Solid; tough; not easily broken or injured; able to
withstand violence; able to sustain attacks; not easily subdued or
taken; <as>as, a <ex>strong</ex> beam; a <ex>strong</ex> rock; a
<ex>strong</ex> fortress or town.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Having great military or naval force; powerful;
<as>as, a <ex>strong</ex> army or fleet; a nation <ex>strong</ex> at
sea.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Having great wealth, means, or resources; <as>as, a
<ex>strong</ex> house, or company of merchants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Reaching a certain degree or limit in respect to
strength or numbers; <as>as, an army ten thousand
<ex>strong</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Moving with rapidity or force; violent; forcible;
impetuous; <as>as, a <ex>strong</ex> current of water or wind; the wind
was <ex>strong</ex> from the northeast; a <ex>strong</ex>
tide.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Adapted to make a deep or effectual impression on
the mind or imagination; striking or superior of the kind; powerful;
forcible; cogent; <as>as, a <ex>strong</ex> argument; <ex>strong</ex>
reasons; <ex>strong</ex> evidence; a <ex>strong</ex> example;
<ex>strong</ex> language.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Ardent; eager; zealous; earnestly engaged; <as>as, a
<ex>strong</ex> partisan; a <ex>strong</ex> Whig or Tory.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her mother, ever <qex>strong</qex> against that match.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>Having virtues of great efficacy; or, having a
particular quality in a great degree; <as>as, a <ex>strong</ex> powder
or tincture; a <ex>strong</ex> decoction; <ex>strong</ex> tea or
coffee.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Full of spirit; containing a large proportion of
alcohol; intoxicating; <as>as, <ex>strong</ex> liquors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>Affecting any sense powerfully; <as>as,
<ex>strong</ex> light, colors, etc.; a <ex>strong</ex> flavor of onions;
a <ex>strong</ex> scent.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <def>Solid; nourishing; <as>as, <ex>strong</ex>
meat</as>.</def> <rj><au>Heb. v. 12.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <def>Well established; firm; not easily overthrown or
altered; <as>as, a <ex>strong</ex> custom; a <ex>strong</ex>
belief.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>15.</sn> <def>Violent; vehement; earnest; ardent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had offered up prayers and supplications with <qex>strong</qex>
crying and tears.</q> <rj><qau>Heb. v. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>16.</sn> <def>Having great force, vigor, power, or the like, as
the mind, intellect, or any faculty; <as>as, a man of a <ex>strong</ex>
mind, memory, judgment, or imagination</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I was <qex>stronger</qex> in prophecy than in criticism.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>17.</sn> <def>Vigorous; effective; forcible; powerful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like her sweet voice is thy harmonious song,<br/
As high, as sweet, as easy, and as <qex>strong</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>E.
Smith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>18.</sn> <fld>(Stock Exchange)</fld> <def>Tending to higher
prices; rising; <as>as, a <ex>strong</ex> market</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>19.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Pertaining to, or
designating, a verb which forms its preterit (imperfect) by a variation
in the root vowel, and the past participle (usually) by the addition of
<xex>-en</xex> (with or without a change of the root vowel); as in the
verbs <xex>strive</xex>, <xex>strove</xex>, <xex>striven</xex>;
<xex>break</xex>, <xex>broke</xex>, <xex>broken</xex>; <xex>drink</xex>,
<xex>drank</xex>, <xex>drunk</xex>. Opposed to <xex>weak</xex>, or
<xex>regular</xex>. See <er>Weak</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Applied
to forms in Anglo-Saxon, etc., which retain the old declensional
endings. In the Teutonic languages the vowel stems have held the
original endings most firmly, and are called <xex>strong</xex>; the
stems in <xex>-n</xex> are called <xex>weak</xex> other constant stems
conform, or are irregular.</def> <rj><au>F. A. March.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Strong conjugation</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>the
conjugation of a strong verb; -- called also <altname>old
conjugation</altname>, or <altname>irregular conjugation</altname>, and
distinguished from the <contr>weak conjugation</contr> or <contr>regular
conjugation</contr>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Strong</xex> is often used in the formation of
self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>strong</xex>-backed,
<xex>strong</xex>-based, <xex>strong</xex>-bodied,
<xex>strong</xex>-colored, <xex>strong</xex>-fisted,
<xex>strong</xex>-handed, <xex>strong</xex>-ribbed,
<xex>strong</xex>-smelling, <xex>strong</xex>-voiced, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Vigorous; powerful; stout; solid; firm; hardy;
muscular; forcible; cogent; valid. See <er>Robust</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>strongbox</ent><br/
<hw>strong"box`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A box of rigid and
durable construction fitted with a lock, used for the purpose of
protecting valuable items, such as jewelry or money.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> lockbox.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stronghand</ent><br/
<hw>Strong"hand`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Violence; force;
power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was their meaning to take what they needed by
<qex>stronghand</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stronghold</ent><br/
<hw>Strong"hold`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fastness; a
fort or fortress; fortfield place; a place of security.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strongish</ent><br/
<hw>Strong"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat strong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strongly</ent><br/
<hw>Strong"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a strong manner; so as to be
strong in action or in resistance; with strength; with great force;
forcibly; powerfully; firmly; vehemently; <as>as, a town
<ex>strongly</ex> fortified; he objected
<ex>strongly</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strong-minded</ent><br/
<hw>Strong"-mind`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
vigorous mind; esp., having or affecting masculine qualities of mind; --
said of women.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Strong"-mind`ed*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strong-water</ent><br/
<hw>Strong"-wa`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
acid.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Distilled or ardent spirits; intoxicating
liquor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strongylid</ent><br/
<hw>Stron"gy*lid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Strongyloid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strongyloid</ent><br/
<hw>Stron"gy*loid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>Strongylus</ets> the genus (from Gr. <?/ round) +
<ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like, or pertaining to,
<gen>Strongylus</gen>, a genus of parasitic nematode worms of which many
species infest domestic animals. Some of the species, especially those
living in the kidneys, lungs, and bronchial tubes, are often very
injurious.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A strongyloid
worm.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strontia</ent><br/
<hw>Stron"ti*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>strontia</ets>, fr. <ets>Strontian</ets>, in Argyleshire, Scotland,
where <ets>strontianite</ets> was first found.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An earth of a white color resembling lime in appearance, and baryta
in many of its properties. It is an oxide of the metal
strontium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strontian</ent><br/
<hw>Stron"ti*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Strontia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strontianite</ent><br/
<hw>Stron"ti*an*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Strontium carbonate, a mineral of a white, greenish, or yellowish
color, usually occurring in fibrous massive forms, but sometimes in
prismatic crystals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strontic</ent><br/
<hw>Stron"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to strontium; containing, or designating the
compounds of, strontium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strontitic</ent><br/
<hw>Stron*tit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Strontic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strontium</ent><br/
<hw>Stron"ti*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Strontia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A metallic element of
the calcium group, always naturally occurring combined, as in the
minerals strontianite, celestite, etc. It is isolated as a yellowish
metal, somewhat malleable but harder than calcium. It is chiefly
employed (as in the nitrate) to color pyrotechnic flames red. Symbol Sr.
Atomic weight 87.3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Strontium-90. <def>A radioactive isotope of strontium produced by
certain nuclear reactions, and constituting one of the prominent harmful
components of radioactive fallout from nuclear explosions; also called
<altname>radiostrontium</altname>. It has a half-life of 28 years.</def>
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strook</ent><br/
<hw>Strook</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Strike</er>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strook</ent><br/
<hw>Strook</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stroke.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroot</ent><br/
<hw>Stroot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Strut</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To swell out; to strut.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strop</ent><br/
<hw>Strop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Strap</er>.]</ety> <def>A strap; specifically, same as
<er>Strap</er>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strop</ent><br/
<hw>Strop</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stropped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stropping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To draw over, or rub upon, a
strop with a view to sharpen; <as>as, to <ex>strop</ex> a
razor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strop</ent><br/
<hw>Strop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>estrope</ets>,
<ets><eacute/trope</ets>, fr. L. <ets>struppus</ets>. See <er>Strop</er>
a strap.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A piece of rope spliced into a
circular wreath, and put round a block for hanging it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strophanthus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stro*phan"thus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from
Gr. <?/ a turning + <?/ a flower.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus
of tropical apocynaceous shrubs having singularly twisted flowers. One
species (<spn>Strophanthus hispidus</spn>) is used medicinally as a
cardiac sedative and stimulant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strophe</ent><br/
<hw>Stro"phe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Strophes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
to twist, to turn; perh. akin to E. <ets>strap</ets>.]</ety> <def>In
Greek choruses and dances, the movement of the chorus while turning from
the right to the left of the orchestra; hence, the strain, or part of
the choral ode, sung during this movement. Also sometimes used of a
stanza of modern verse. See the Note under
<er>Antistrophe</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strophic</ent><br/
<hw>Stroph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to,
containing, or consisting of, strophes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strophiolated</ent><br/
<ent>Strophiolate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Stro"phi*o*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Stro"phi*o*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Furnished
with a strophiole, or caruncle, or that which resembles it.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strophiole</ent><br/
<hw>Stro"phi*ole</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strophiolum</ets> a little chaplet, dim. of <ets>strophium</ets> a
band, Gr. <?/, dim. of <?/ a twisted band: cf. F.
<ets>strophiole</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A crestlike
excrescence about the hilum of certain seeds; a caruncle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strophulus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stroph"u*lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See <er>Red-gum</er>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroud</ent><br/
<hw>Stroud</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of coarse
blanket or garment used by the North American Indians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strouding</ent><br/
<hw>Stroud"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Material for strouds; a kind of
coarse cloth used in trade with the North American Indians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strout</ent><br/
<hw>Strout</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Strut</er>.]</ety> <def>To swell; to puff out; to project.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strout</ent><br/
<hw>Strout</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to project or swell out;
to enlarge affectedly; to strut.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strove</ent><br/
<hw>Strove</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Strive</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strow</ent><br/
<hw>Strow</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Strowed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Strown</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> <or/
<conjf>Strowed</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>Same as <er>Strew</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thick as autumnal leaves that <qex>strow</qex> the brooks<br/
In Vallombrosa.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A manner turbid . . . and <qex>strown</qex> with blemished.</q>
<rj><qau>M. Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strowl</ent><br/
<hw>Strowl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To stroll.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strown</ent><br/
<hw>Strown</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Strow</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stroy</ent><br/
<hw>Stroy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To destroy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struck</ent><br/
<hw>Struck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Strike</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Struck jury</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a special
jury, composed of persons having special knowledge or qualifications,
selected by striking from the panel of jurors a certain number for each
party, leaving the number required by law to try the
cause.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strucken</ent><br/
<hw>Struck"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>p. p.</pos>
of <er>Strike</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Structural</ent><br/
<hw>Struc"tur*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to structure; affecting structure; <as>as, a
<ex>structural</ex> error</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to organit
structure; <as>as, a <ex>structural</ex> element or cell; the
<ex>structural</ex> peculiarities of an animal or a
plant.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Structural formula</ent><br/
<hw>Struc"tur*al form"u*la</hw>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>a symbolic
representation of the structure of one molecule of a chemical compound,
showing the attachments of the atoms to each other, and representing
their arrangement in space; the spatial representation is symbolic and
approximate, and it may or may not depict the stereochemical relations
of the bonds. Distinguished from <cref>empirical formula</cref>. See
also <cref>Rational formula</cref>, under <er>Formula</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Structural shape</ent><br/
<hw>Struc"tur*al shape</hw>. <fld>(Engin. & Arch.)</fld> <def>The shape
of a member especially adapted to structural purposes, esp. in giving
the greatest strength with the least material.</def>
<specif>Hence,</specif> <mark>Colloq.</mark>, <def>any steel or iron
member of such shape, as channel irons, <universbold>I</universbold>
beams, <universbold>T</universbold> beams, etc., or, sometimes, a
column, girder, etc., built up with such members.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Structural steel</ent><br/
<hw>Structural steel</hw>. <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Rolled steel in structural
shapes.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A kind of strong mild steel, suitable
for structural shapes.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Structure</ent><br/
<hw>Struc"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>structura</ets>, from <ets>struere</ets>, <ets>structum</ets>, to
arrange, build, construct; perhaps akin to E. <ets>strew</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>structure</ets>. Cf. <er>Construe</er>, <er>Destroy</er>,
<er>Instrument</er>, <er>Obstruct</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of building; the practice of erecting buildings; construction.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His son builds on, and never is content<br/
Till the last farthing is in <qex>structure</qex> spent.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Dryden, Jr.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Manner of building; form; make;
construction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Want of insight into the <qex>structure</qex> and constitution of
the terraqueous globe.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Arrangement of parts, of organs, or of constituent
particles, in a substance or body; <as>as, the <ex>structure</ex> of a
rock or a mineral; the <ex>structure</ex> of a sentence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It [basalt] has often a prismatic <qex>structure</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dana.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Manner of organization; the
arrangement of the different tissues or parts of animal and vegetable
organisms; <as>as, organic <ex>structure</ex>, or the <ex>structure</ex>
of animals and plants; cellular <ex>structure</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That which is built; a building; esp., a building of
some size or magnificence; an edifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There stands a <qex>structure</qex> of majestic frame.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Columnar structure</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Columnar</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Structured</ent><br/
<hw>Struc"tured</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>Having a definite organic structure; showing differentiation of
parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The passage from a structureless state to a <qex>structured</qex>
state is itself a vital process.</q> <rj><qau>H. Spencer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Structureless</ent><br/
<hw>Struc"ture*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without a
definite structure, or arrangement of parts; without organization;
devoid of cells; homogeneous; <as>as, a <ex>structureless</ex>
membrane</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Structurist</ent><br/
<hw>Struc"tur*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who forms
structures; a builder; a constructor.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strude</ent><br/
<hw>Strude</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stock of breeding
mares.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>strode</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struggle</ent><br/
<hw>Strug"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Struggled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Struggling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>strogelen</ets>; cf. Icel. <ets>strj<?/ka</ets> to stroke, to beat,
to flog, Sw. <ets>stryka</ets> to stroke, to strike, Dan.
<ets>stryge</ets>, G. <ets>straucheln</ets> to stumble. Cf.
<er>Stroll</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strive, or to make efforts,
with a twisting, or with contortions of the body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To use great efforts; to labor hard; to strive; to
contend forcibly; <as>as, to <ex>struggle</ex> to save one's life; to
<ex>struggle</ex> with the waves; to <ex>struggle</ex> with
adversity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The brave men, living and dead, who <qex>struggled</qex> here,
have consecrated it [Gettysburg] far above our power to add or
detract.</q> <rj><qau>Lincoln.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To labor in pain or anguish; to be in agony; to
labor in any kind of difficulty or distress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is wisdom to beware,<br/
And better shun the bait than <qex>struggle</qex> in the snare.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To strive; contend; labor; endeavor.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struggle</ent><br/
<hw>Strug"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
violent effort or efforts with contortions of the body; agony;
distress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Great labor; forcible effort to obtain an object, or
to avert an evil.</def> <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Contest; contention; strife.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An honest might look upon the <qex>struggle</qex> with
indifference.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Endeavor; effort; contest; labor;
difficulty.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struggler</ent><br/
<hw>Strug"gler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
struggles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strull</ent><br/
<hw>Strull</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bar so placed as to
resist weight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strum</ent><br/
<hw>Strum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Strummed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Strumming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably of imitative
origin. Cf. <er>Thrum</er>.]</ety> <def>To play on an instrument of
music, or as on an instrument, in an unskillful or noisy way; to thrum;
<as>as, to <ex>strum</ex> a piano</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stru"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a scrofulous
tumor.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Scrofula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A cushionlike swelling on any
organ; especially, that at the base of the capsule in many
mosses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strumatic</ent><br/
<hw>Stru*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Scrofulous;
strumous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strumose</ent><br/
<hw>Stru*mose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strumosus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>strumeux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Strumous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having a struma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strumous</ent><br/
<hw>Stru"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Scrofulous; having struma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strumousness</ent><br/
<hw>Stru"mous*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
strumous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strumpet</ent><br/
<hw>Strum"pet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>strumpet</ets>, <ets>strompet</ets>; cf. OF. <ets>stupe</ets>
debauchery, F. <ets>stupe</ets>, L. <ets>stuprare</ets>,
<ets>stupratum</ets>, to debauch, <ets>stuprum</ets> debauchery, Gael. &
Ir. <ets>striopach</ets> a prostitute.]</ety> <def>A prostitute; a
harlot.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strumpet</ent><br/
<hw>Strum"pet</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a strumpet;
characteristic of a strumpet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Out on thy more than <qex>strumpet</qex> impudence.</q>
<rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strumpet</ent><br/
<hw>Strum"pet</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To debauch.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To dishonor with the reputation of being a strumpet;
hence, to belie; to slander.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With his untrue reports, <qex>strumpet</qex> your fame.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strumstrum</ent><br/
<hw>Strum"strum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A rude musical
instrument somewhat like a cittern.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dampier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strung</ent><br/
<hw>Strung</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>String</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strunt</ent><br/
<hw>Strunt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Spirituous
liquor.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Burns.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struntian</ent><br/
<hw>Strun"tian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of worsted
braid, about an inch broad.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struse</ent><br/
<hw>Struse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>strug'</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A Russian river craft
used for transporting freight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strut</ent><br/
<hw>Strut</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Strutted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Strutting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>struten</ets>, <ets>strouten</ets>, to swell; akin to G.
<ets>strozen</ets> to be swelled, to be puffed up, to strut, Dan.
<ets>strutte</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To swell; to bulge
out.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bellying canvas <qex>strutted</qex> with the gale.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To walk with a lofty, proud gait, and erect head; to
walk with affected dignity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Does he not hold up his head, . . . and <qex>strut</qex> in his
gait?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strut</ent><br/
<hw>Strut</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For senses 2 & 3 cf. LG.
<ets>strutt</ets> rigid.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of strutting; a
pompous step or walk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>In general, any piece of a frame
which resists thrust or pressure in the direction of its own length. See
<er>Brace</er>, and <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Frame</er>, and
<er>Roof</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Engin.)</fld> <def>Any part of a machine or
structure, of which the principal function is to hold things apart; a
brace subjected to compressive stress; -- the opposite of
<xex>stay</xex>, and <xex>tie</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strut</ent><br/
<hw>Strut</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To hold apart. Cf. <er>Strut</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strut</ent><br/
<hw>Strut</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Protuberant.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struthian</ent><br/

<hw>Stru"thi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Struthious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struthio</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stru"thi*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Struthiones</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., an ostrich, fr. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of birds including the
African ostriches.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struthioidea</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stru`thi*oi"de*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Struthio</er>, and <er>-oid</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Struthiones</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struthiones</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stru`thi*o"nes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Struthio</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
division, or order, of birds, including only the African
ostriches.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In a wider sense, an extensive group
of birds including the ostriches, cassowaries, emus, moas, and allied
birds incapable of flight. In this sense it is equivalent to
<ord>Ratitae</ord>, or <ord>Dromaeognathae</ord>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struthionine</ent><br/
<hw>Stru`thi*o"nine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Struthious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struthious</ent><br/
<hw>Stru"thi*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>struthius</ets>, <ets>strutheus</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the Struthiones, or Ostrich tribe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strutter</ent><br/
<hw>Strut"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
struts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strutting</ent><br/
<hw>Strut"ting</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Strut</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Strut"ting*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Struvite</ent><br/
<hw>Struv"ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[After the Russian
minister Von <ets>Struve</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A
crystalline mineral found in guano. It is a hydrous phosphate of
magnesia and ammonia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strychnia</ent><br/
<hw>Strych"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Strychnine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Strychnine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strychnic</ent><br/
<hw>Strych"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
strychnine; produced by strychnine; <as>as, <ex>strychnic</ex>
compounds; <ex>strychnic</ex> poisoning</as></def>; specifically
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <def>used to designate an acid, called also
<xex>igasuric acid</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strychnine</ent><br/
<hw>Strych"nine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>strychnos</ets> a kind of nightshade, Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>strychnine</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A very poisonous
alkaloid resembling brucine, obtained from various species of plants,
especially from species of <fam>Loganiaceae</fam>, as from the seeds of
the St. Ignatius bean (<spn>Strychnos Ignatia</spn>) and from nux
vomica. It is obtained as a white crystalline substance, having a very
bitter acrid taste, and is employed in medicine (chiefly in the form of
the sulphate) as a powerful neurotic stimulant. Called also
<altname>strychnia</altname>, and formerly
<altname>strychnina</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Strychnos</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Strych"nos</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a kind of
nightshade, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of tropical
trees and shrubs of the family <fam>Loganiaceae</fam>. See <er>Nux
vomica</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1429 --></p>

<p><ent>Stryphnic</ent><br/
<hw>Stryph"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
astringent.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or
designating, a complex nitrogenous acid, obtained by the action of
acetic acid and potassium nitrite on uric acid, as a yellow crystalline
substance, with a bitter, astringent taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stub</ent><br/
<hw>Stub</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stubbe</ets>,
AS. <ets>stub</ets>, <ets>styb</ets>; akin to D. <ets>stobbe</ets>, LG.
<ets>stubbe</ets>, Dan. <ets>stub</ets>, Sw. <ets>stubbe</ets>, Icel.
<ets>stubbr</ets>, <ets>stubbi</ets>; cf. Gr. <?/.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The stump of a tree; that part of a tree or plant which remains
fixed in the earth when the stem is cut down; -- applied especially to
the stump of a small tree, or shrub.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Stubs</qex> sharp and hideous to behold.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And prickly <qex>stubs</qex> instead of trees are found.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A log; a block; a blockhead.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The short blunt part of anything after larger part
has been broken off or used up; hence, anything short and thick; <as>as,
the <ex>stub</ex> of a pencil, candle, or cigar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A part of a leaf in a check book, after a check is
torn out, on which the number, amount, and destination of the check are
usually recorded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A pen with a short, blunt nib.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A stub nail; an old horseshoe nail; also, stub
iron.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stub end</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>the enlarged
end of a connecting rod, to which the strap is fastened.</cd> --
<col><b>Stub iron</b></col>, <cd>iron made from stub nails, or old
horseshoe nails, -- used in making gun barrels.</cd> -- <col><b>Stub
mortise</b></col> <fld>(Carp.)</fld>, <cd>a mortise passing only partly
through the timber in which it is formed.</cd> -- <col><b>Stub
nail</b></col>, <cd>an old horseshoe nail; a nail broken off; also, a
short, thick nail.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Stub short</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Stub shot</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Lumber
Manuf.)</fld>, <cd>the part of the end of a sawn log or plank which is
beyond the place where the saw kerf ends, and which retains the plank in
connection with the log, until it is split off.</cd> -- <col><b>Stub
twist</b></col>, <cd>material for a gun barrel, made of a spirally
welded ribbon of steel and stub iron combined.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stub</ent><br/
<hw>Stub</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stubbed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stubbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To grub up by the
roots; to extirpate; <as>as, to <ex>stub</ex> up edible
roots</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>stubbing</qex>, plowing, digging, and harrowing is to a
piece of land.</q> <rj><qau>Berkley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To remove stubs from; <as>as, to <ex>stub</ex>
land</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To strike as the toes, against a stub, stone, or
other fixed object.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stubbed</ent><br/
<hw>Stub"bed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Reduced
to a stub; short and thick, like something truncated; blunt;
obtuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Abounding in stubs; stubby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A bit of <qex>stubbed</qex> ground, once a wood.</q> <rj><qau>R.
Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Not nice or delicate; hardy; rugged.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Stubbed</xex>, vulgar constitutions.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Berkley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stubbedness</ent><br/
<hw>Stub"bed*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
stubbed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stubbiness</ent><br/
<hw>Stub"bi*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being stubby.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stubble</ent><br/
<hw>Stub"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stobil</ets>, <ets>stoble</ets>, OF. <ets>estouble</ets>,
<ets>estuble</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tuele</ets>, LL. <ets>stupla</ets>,
<ets>stupula</ets>, L. <ets>stipula</ets> stubble, stalk; cf. D. & G.
<ets>stopped</ets>, OHG. <ets>stupfila</ets>. Cf.
<er>Stipule</er>.]</ety> <def>The stumps of wheat, rye, barley, oats, or
buckwheat, left in the ground; the part of the stalk left by the scythe
or sickle.</def> <ldquo/After the first crop is off, they plow in the
wheast <xex>stubble</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stubble goose</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
graylag goose.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au> --
<col><b>Stubble rake</b></col>, <cd>a rake with long teeth for gleaning
in stubble.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stubbled</ent><br/
<hw>Stub"bled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Covered
with stubble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A crow was strutting o'er the <qex>stubbled</qex> plain.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Stubbed; <as>as, <ex>stubbled</ex> legs</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Skelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stubbly</ent><br/
<hw>Stub"bly</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered with stubble;
stubbled.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stubborn</ent><br/
<hw>Stub"born</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stoburn</ets>, <ets>stiborn</ets>; probably fr. AS. <ets>styb</ets>
a stub. See <er>Stub</er>.]</ety> <def>Firm as a stub or stump; stiff;
unbending; unyielding; persistent; hence, unreasonably obstinate in will
or opinion; not yielding to reason or persuasion; refractory; harsh; --
said of persons and things; <as>as, <ex>stubborn</ex> wills;
<ex>stubborn</ex> ore; a <ex>stubborn</ex> oak; as <ex>stubborn</ex> as
a mule.</as></def> <ldquo/Bow, <xex>stubborn</xex> knees.<rdquo/
<au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Stubborn</xex> attention and more than common
application.<rdquo/ <au>Locke.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Stubborn</xex>
Stoics.<rdquo/ <au>Swift.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And I was young and full of ragerie [wantonness]<br/
<qex>Stubborn</qex> and strong, and jolly as a pie.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These heretics be so stiff and <qex>stubborn</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your <qex>stubborn</qex> usage of the pope.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Obstinate; inflexible; obdurate; headstrong;
stiff; hardy; firm; refractory; intractable; rugged; contumacious;
heady.</syn> -- <usage><er>Stubborn</er>, <er>Obstinate</er>.
<xex>Obstinate</xex> is used of either active or passive persistence in
one's views or conduct, in spite of the wishes of others.
<xex>Stubborn</xex> describes an extreme degree of passive
<xex>obstinacy</xex>.</usage> -- <wordforms><wf>Stub"born*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Stub"born*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stubby</ent><br/
<hw>Stub"by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Abounding
with stubs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Short and thick; short and strong, as
bristles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stucco</ent><br/
<hw>Stuc"co</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Stuccoes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, <plw>Stuccos</plw>.</plu> <ety>[It.,
fr. OHG. <ets>stucchi</ets> a crust, piece, G. <ets>st<uum/ck</ets>
piece; akin to AS. <ets>stycce</ets>. See <er>Stock</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Plaster of any kind used as a coating for walls,
especially, a fine plaster, composed of lime or gypsum with sand and
pounded marble, used for internal decorations and fine work.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Work made of stucco; stuccowork.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stucco</ent><br/
<hw>Stuc"co</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stuccoed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stuccoing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To overlay or
decorate with stucco, or fine plaster.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuccoer</ent><br/
<hw>Stuc"co*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
stuccoes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuccowork</ent><br/
<hw>Stuc"co*work`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Work done in
stucco.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuck</ent><br/
<hw>Stuck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Stick</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuck</ent><br/
<hw>Stuck</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. 1st <er>Stoccado</er>.]</ety>
<def>A thrust.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuckle</ent><br/
<hw>Stuc"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Stook</er>.]</ety> <def>A number of sheaves set together in the
field; a stook.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuck-up</ent><br/
<hw>Stuck"-up`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Self-important and
supercilious, conceited; vain; arrogant.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The airs of small, <qex>stuck-up</qex>, men.</q> <rj><qau>A. K. H.
Boyd.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stud</ent><br/
<hw>Stud</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stod</ets>,
<ets>stood</ets>, AS. <ets>st<omac/d</ets>; akin to OHG.
<ets>stuota</ets>, G. <ets>stute</ets> a mare, Icel.
<ets>st<omac/<?/</ets> stud, Lith. <ets>stodas</ets> a herd, Russ.
<ets>stado</ets>, and to E. <ets>stand</ets>. The sense is properly, a
stand, an establishment. <root/163. See <er>Stand</er>, and cf.
<er>Steed</er>.]</ety> <def>A collection of breeding horses and mares,
or the place where they are kept; also, a number of horses kept for a
racing, riding, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the <qex>studs</qex> of Ireland, where care is taken, we see
horses bred of excellent shape, vigor, and size.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had the finest <qex>stud</qex> in England, and his delight was
to win plates from Tories.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stud</ent><br/
<hw>Stud</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>studu</ets> a
post; akin to Sw. <ets>st<oum/d</ets> a prop, Icel. <ets>sto<?/</ets> a
post, <ets>sty<?/ja</ets> to prop, and probably ultimately to E.
<ets>stand</ets>; cf. D. <ets>stut</ets> a prop, G.
<ets>st<uum/tze</ets>. See <er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
stem; a trunk.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Seest not this same hawthorn <qex>stud</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An upright scanting, esp. one of
the small uprights in the framing for lath and plaster partitions, and
furring, and upon which the laths are nailed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A kind of nail with a large head, used chiefly for
ornament; an ornamental knob; a boss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A belt of straw and ivy buds,<br/
With coral clasps and amber <qex>studs</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Marlowe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Crystal and myrrhine cups, embossed with gems<br/
And <qex>studs</qex> of pearl.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>An ornamental button of various forms, worn in a
shirt front, collar, wristband, or the like, not sewed in place, but
inserted through a buttonhole or eyelet, and transferable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A short rod or pin,
fixed in and projecting from something, and sometimes forming a
journal.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A stud bolt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>An iron brace across the shorter diameter of the
link of a chain cable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stud bolt</b></col>, <cd>a bolt with threads on both
ends, to be screwed permanently into a fixed part at one end and receive
a nut upon the other; -- called also <altname>standing
bolt</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stud</ent><br/
<hw>Stud</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Studded</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Studding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To adorn with
shining studs, or knobs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy horses shall be trapped,<br/
Their harness <qex>studded</qex> all with gold and pearl.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To set with detached ornaments or prominent objects;
to set thickly, as with studs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sloping sides and summits of our hills, and the extensive
plains that stretch before our view, are <qex>studded</qex> with
substantial, neat, and commodious dwellings of freemen.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Hobart.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studbook</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"book`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A genealogical
register of a particular breed or stud of horses, esp.
thoroughbreds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studdery</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"der*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stud, or
collection of breeding horses and mares; also, a place for keeping a
stud.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>King Henry the Eighth erected a noble <qex>studdery</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Holinshed.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studding</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"ding</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Material for studs,
or joists; studs, or joists, collectively; studs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studding sail</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"ding sail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A light
sail set at the side of a principal or square sail of a vessel in free
winds, to increase her speed. Its head is bent to a small spar which is
called the <xex>studding-sail boom</xex>. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Sail</er>.</def> <rj><au>Toten.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Student</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"dent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>studens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>studere</ets> to
study. See <er>Study</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
person engaged in study; one who is devoted to learning; a learner; a
pupil; a scholar; especially, one who attends a school, or who seeks
knowledge from professional teachers or from books; <as>as, the
<ex>students</ex> of an academy, a college, or a university; a medical
<ex>student</ex>; a hard <ex>student</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Keep a gamester from the dice, and a good <qex>student</qex> from
his book.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who studies or examines in any manner; an
attentive and systematic observer; <as>as, a <ex>student</ex> of human
nature, or of physical nature</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studentry</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"dent*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A body of
students.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studentship</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"dent*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being a
student.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studfish</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of several species of small American minnows of the genus
<gen>Fundulus</gen>, as <spn>Fundulus catenatus</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stud-horse</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"-horse`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>st<omac/d-hors</ets>.]</ety> <def>A stallion, esp. one kept for
breeding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studied</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"ied</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Closely examined; read
with diligence and attention; made the subject of study; well
considered; <as>as, a <ex>studied</ex> lesson</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Well versed in any branch of learning; qualified by
study; learned; <as>as, a man well <ex>studied</ex> in
geometry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I shrewdly suspect that he is little <qex>studied</qex> of a
theory of moral proportions.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Premeditated; planned; designed; <as>as, a
<ex>studied</ex> insult</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Studied</xex>
magnificence.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Intent; inclined.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studiedly</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"ied*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a studied
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studier</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"i*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A student.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>W. Irving.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lipsius was a great <qex>studier</qex> of the stoical
philosophy.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studio</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"di*o</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Studios</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[It. <ets>studio</ets>,
properly, study. See <er>Study</er>.]</ety> <def>The working room of an
artist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Studious</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"di*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>studious</ets>: cf. F. <ets>studieux</ets>. See
<er>Study</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Given to study; devoted to the
acquisition of knowledge from books; <as>as, a <ex>studious</ex>
scholar</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Given to thought, or to the examination of subjects
by contemplation; contemplative.</def> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Earnest in endeavors; aiming sedulously; attentive;
observant; diligent; -- usually followed by an infinitive or by
<xex>of</xex>; <as>as, be <ex>studious</ex> to please; <ex>studious</ex>
to find new friends and allies</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You that are so <qex>studious</qex><br/
Of my affairs, wholly neglect your own.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Planned with study; deliberate; studied.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the frigid villainy of <qex>studious</qex> lewdness, . . .
with apology can be invented?</q> <rj><qau>Rambler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Favorable to study; suitable for thought and
contemplation; <as>as, the <ex>studious</ex> shade</as>.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But let my due feet never fail<br/
To walk the <qex>studious</qex> cloister's pale.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Stu"di*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Stu"di*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Study</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Studies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>studie</ets>, L.
<ets>studium</ets>, akin to <ets>studere</ets> to study; possibly akin
to Gr. <?/ haste, zeal, <?/ to hasten; cf. OF. <ets>estudie</ets>,
<ets>estude</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/tude</ets>. Cf. <er>Etude</er>,
<er>Student</er>, <er>Studio</er>, <er>Study</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A setting of the mind or thoughts upon
a subject; hence, application of mind to books, arts, or science, or to
any subject, for the purpose of acquiring knowledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hammond . . . spent thirteen hours of the day in
<qex>study</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Fell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Study</qex> gives strength to the mind; conversation,
grace.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Mental occupation; absorbed or thoughtful attention;
meditation; contemplation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Just men they seemed, and all their <qex>study</qex> bent<br/
To worship God aright, and know his works.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Any particular branch of learning that is studied;
any object of attentive consideration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Holy Scriptures, especially the New Testament, are her daily
<qex>study</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Law.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The proper <qex>study</qex> of mankind is man.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A building or apartment devoted to study or to
literary work.</def> <ldquo/His cheery little <xex>study</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <def>A representation or rendering
of any object or scene intended, not for exhibition as an original work
of art, but for the information, instruction, or assistance of the
maker; <as>as, a <ex>study</ex> of heads or of hands for a figure
picture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A piece for special practice. See
<er>Etude</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Study</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Studied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Studying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>studien</ets>, OF. <ets>estudier</ets>, F.
<ets><eacute/tudier</ets>. See <er>Study</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To fix the mind closely upon a subject; to dwell upon
anything in thought; to muse; to ponder.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I found a moral first, and then <qex>studied</qex> for a
fable.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To apply the mind to books or learning.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To endeavor diligently; to be zealous.</def>
<rj><au>1 Thes. iv. 11.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Study</ent><br/
<hw>Stud"y</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To apply the mind to;
to read and examine for the purpose of learning and understanding;
<as>as, to <ex>study</ex> law or theology; to <ex>study</ex>
languages</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To consider attentively; to examine closely; <as>as,
to <ex>study</ex> the work of nature</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Study</qex> thyself; what rank or what degree<br/
The wise Creator has ordained for thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To form or arrange by previous thought; to con over,
as in committing to memory; <as>as, to <ex>study</ex> a
speech</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make an object of study; to aim at sedulously; to
devote one's thoughts to; <as>as, to <ex>study</ex> the welfare of
others; to <ex>study</ex> variety in composition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For their heart <qex>studieth</qex> destruction.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. xxiv. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stufa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stu"fa</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.
<ets>stufa</ets> a stove. See <er>Stove</er>.]</ety> <def>A jet of steam
issuing from a fissure in the earth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuff</ent><br/
<hw>Stuff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>estoffe</ets>,
F. <ets><eacute/toffe</ets>; of uncertain origin, perhaps of Teutonic
origin and akin to E. <ets>stop</ets>, v.t. Cf. <er>Stuff</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Material which is to be worked up in
any process of manufacture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the <qex>stuff</qex> they had was sufficient for all the work
to make it, and too much.</q> <rj><qau>Ex. xxxvi. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ambitions should be made of sterner <qex>stuff</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The workman on his <qex>stuff</qex> his skill doth show,<br/
And yet the <qex>stuff</qex> gives not the man his skill.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir J. Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The fundamental material of which anything is made
up; elemental part; essence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet do I hold it very <qex>stuff</qex> o' the conscience<br/
To do no contrived murder.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Woven material not made into garments; fabric of any
kind; specifically, any one of various fabrics of wool or worsted;
sometimes, worsted fiber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>stuff</qex> wilt have a kirtle of?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It [the arras] was of <qex>stuff</qex> and silk mixed, though,
superior kinds were of silk exclusively.</q> <rj><qau>F. G.
Lee.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Furniture; goods; domestic vessels or
utensils.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He took away locks, and gave away the king's <qex>stuff</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A medicine or mixture; a potion.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Refuse or worthless matter; hence, also, foolish or
irrational language; nonsense; trash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Anger would indite<br/
Such woeful <qex>stuff</qex> as I or Shadwell write.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A melted mass of turpentine,
tallow, etc., with which the masts, sides, and bottom of a ship are
smeared for lubrication.</def> <rj><qau>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Paper stock ground ready for use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ When partly ground, called <xex>half stuff</xex>.</note>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Clear stuff</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Clear</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Small stuff</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>all kinds of
small cordage.</cd> <au>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</au> -- <col><b>Stuff
gown</b></col>, <cd>the distinctive garb of a junior barrister; hence, a
junior barrister himself. See <altname>Silk gown</altname>, under
<er>Silk</er>.</cd></cs><-- <col><b>stuff and nonsense</b></col>. (See
def. 6 for stuff) <cd>balderdash, twaddle, nonsense, foolishness.</cd>
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuff</ent><br/
<hw>Stuff</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stuffed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stuffing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stoffen</ets>; cf. OF.
<ets>estoffer</ets>, F. <ets><eacute/toffer</ets>, to put stuff in, to
stuff, to line, also, OF. <ets>estouffer</ets> to stifle, F.
<ets><eacute/touffer</ets>; both perhaps of Teutonic origin, and akin to
E. <ets>stop</ets>. Cf. <er>Stop</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, <er>Stuff</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fill by crowding something
into; to cram with something; to load to excess; <as>as, to
<ex>stuff</ex> a bedtick</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sometimes this crook drew hazel bought adown,<br/
And <qex>stuffed</qex> her apron wide with nuts so brown.</q>
<rj><qau>Gay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lest the gods, for sin,<br/
Should with a swelling dropsy <qex>stuff</qex> thy skin.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To thrust or crowd; to press; to pack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Put roses into a glass with a narrow mouth, <qex>stuffing</qex>
them close together . . . and they retain smell and color.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fill by being pressed or packed into.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With inward arms the dire machine they load,<br/
And iron bowels <qex>stuff</qex> the dark abode.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>To fill with a seasoning
composition of bread, meat, condiments, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>stuff</ex>
a turkey</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To obstruct, as any of the organs; to affect with
some obstruction in the organs of sense or respiration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'm <qex>stuffed</qex>, cousin; I can not smell.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To fill the skin of, for the purpose of preserving
as a specimen; -- said of birds or other animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To form or fashion by packing with the necessary
material.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An Eastern king put a judge to death for an iniquitous sentence,
and ordered his hide to be <qex>stuffed</qex> into a cushion, and placed
upon the tribunal.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To crowd with facts; to cram the mind of; sometimes,
to crowd or fill with false or idle tales or fancies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To put fraudulent votes into (a ballot box).</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1430 --></p>

<p><ent>Stuff</ent><br/
<hw>Stuff</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To feed gluttonously;
to cram.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Taught harmless man to cram and <qex>stuff</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuffer</ent><br/
<hw>Stuff"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, stuffs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuffiness</ent><br/
<hw>Stuff"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality of
being stuffy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuffing</ent><br/
<hw>Stuff"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is used
for filling anything; <as>as, the <ex>stuffing</ex> of a saddle or
cushion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Cookery)</fld> <def>Any seasoning preparation used
to stuff meat; especially, a composition of bread, condiments, spices,
etc.; forcemeat; dressing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A mixture of oil and tallow used in softening and
dressing leather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stuffing box</b></col>, <cd>a device for rendering a
joint impervious where there is a hole through which a movable
cylindrical body, as the paston rod of a steam engine, or the plunger of
a pump, slides back and forth, or in which a shaft turns. It usually
consists of a box or chamber, made by an enlargement of part of the
hole, forming a space around the rod or shaft for containing packing
which is compressed and made to fill the space closely by means of a
sleeve, called the <xex>gland</xex>, which fits loosely around the rod,
and is pressed upon the packing by bolts or other means.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuffy</ent><br/
<hw>Stuff"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Stout;
mettlesome; resolute.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Angry and obstinate; sulky.</def> <mark>[U.
S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Ill-ventilated; close.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuke</ent><br/
<hw>Stuke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stucco.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stull</ent><br/
<hw>Stull</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[CF.
<er>Stum</er>.]</ety> <def>A framework of timber covered with boards to
support rubbish; also, a framework of boards to protect miners from
falling stones.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stulm</ent><br/
<hw>Stulm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>stollen</ets> a post, a stulm, E. <ets>stall</ets>,
<ets>stand</ets>.]</ety> <def>A shaft or gallery to drain a mine.</def>
<mark>[Local, Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stulp</ent><br/
<hw>Stulp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>st<omac/lpi</ets>, Dan., Sw., & OD. <ets>stolpe</ets>.]</ety>
<def>A short, stout post used for any purpose, a to mark a
boundary.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stultification</ent><br/
<hw>Stul`ti*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
stultifying, or the state of being stultified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stultifier</ent><br/
<hw>Stul"ti*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
stultifies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stultify</ent><br/
<hw>Stul"ti*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stultified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stultifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>stultus</ets> foolish + <ets>-fy</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
make foolish; to make a fool of; <as>as, to <ex>stultify</ex> one by
imposition; to <ex>stultify</ex> one's self by silly reasoning or
conduct</as>.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To regard as a fool, or as foolish.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The modern sciolist <qex>stultifies</qex> all understanding but
his own, and that which he conceives like his own.</q>
<rj><au>Hazlitt.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To allege or prove to be of unsound
mind, so that the performance of some act may be avoided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stultiloquence</ent><br/
<hw>Stul*til"o*quence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stultiloquentia</ets>; <ets>stultus</ets> foolish +
<ets>loquentia</ets> a talking, fr. <ets>loquens</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>loqui</ets> to talk.]</ety> <def>Silly talk; babbling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stultiloquent</ent><br/
<hw>Stul*til"o*quent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>stultiloquus</ets>. See <er>Stultiloquence</er>.]</ety> <def>Given
to, or characterized by, silly talk; babbling.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Stul*til"o*quent*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stultiloquy</ent><br/
<hw>Stul*til"o*quy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stultiloquium</ets>.]</ety> <def>Foolish talk; silly discource;
babbling.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stulty</ent><br/
<hw>Stul"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stultus</ets>
foolish.]</ety> <def>Foolish; silly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Testament of Love.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stum</ent><br/
<hw>Stum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>stom</ets> must,
new wort, properly, dumb; cf. F. <ets>vin muet</ets> stum. Cf.
<er>Stammer</er>, <er>Stoom</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Unfermented
grape juice or wine, often used to raise fermentation in dead or vapid
wines; must.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let our wines, without mixture of <qex>stum</qex>, be all
fine.</q> <rj><qau>B. Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And with thy <qex>stum</qex> ferment their fainting cause.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Wine revived by new fermentation, reulting from the
admixture of must.</def> <rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stum</ent><br/
<hw>Stum</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stummed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stumming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To renew, as wine, by mixing
must with it and raising a new fermentation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>stum</qex> our wines to renew their spirits.</q>
<rj><qau>Floyer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumble</ent><br/
<hw>Stum"ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stumbled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stumbling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>stumblen</ets>, <ets>stomblen</ets>; freq. of a word akin to E.
<ets>stammer</ets>. See <er>Stammer</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
trip in walking or in moving in any way with the legs; to strike the
foot so as to fall, or to endanger a fall; to stagger because of a false
step.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There <qex>stumble</qex> steeds strong and down go all.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The way of the wicked is as darkness: they know at what they
<qex>stumble</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. iv. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To walk in an unsteady or clumsy manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>stumbled</qex> up the dark avenue.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fall into a crime or an error; to err.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that loveth his brother abideth in the light, and there is none
occasion og <qex>stumbling</qex> in him.</q> <rj><qau>1 John ii.
10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To strike or happen (upon a person or thing) without
design; to fall or light by chance; -- with <xex>on</xex>,
<xex>upon</xex>, or <xex>against</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ovid <qex>stumbled</qex>, by some inadvertency, upon Livia in a
bath.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Forth as she waddled in the brake,<br/
A gray goose <qex>stumbled</qex> on a snake.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Smart.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumble</ent><br/
<hw>Stum"ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to stumble
or trip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To mislead; to confound; to perplex; to cause
to err or to fall.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>False and dazzling fires to <qex>stumble</qex> men.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One thing more <qex>stumbles</qex> me in the very foundation of
this hypothesis.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumble</ent><br/
<hw>Stum"ble</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A trip in walking or
running.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A blunder; a failure; a fall from
rectitude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One <qex>stumble</qex> is enough to deface the character of an
honorable life.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumbler</ent><br/
<hw>Stum"bler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
stumbles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumbling-block</ent><br/
<hw>Stum"bling-block`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any cause of
stumbling, perplexity, or error.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We preach Christ crucified, unto the Jews a
<qex>stumbling-block</qex>, and unto the Greeks foolishness.</q>
<rj><qau>1 Cor. i. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumblingly</ent><br/
<hw>Stum"bling*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a stumbling
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumbling-stone</ent><br/
<hw>Stum"bling-stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
stumbling-block.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>stumbling-stone</qex> we hope to take away.</q>
<rj><qau>T. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stump</ent><br/
<hw>Stump</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stumpe</ets>,
<ets>stompe</ets>; akin to D. <ets>stomp</ets>, G. <ets>stumpf</ets>,
Icel. <ets>stumpr</ets>, Dan. & Sw. <ets>stump</ets>, and perhaps also
to E. <ets>stamp</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The part of a tree or
plant remaining in the earth after the stem or trunk is cut off; the
stub.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The part of a limb or other body remaining after a
part is amputated or destroyed; a fixed or rooted remnant; a stub;
<as>as, the <ex>stump</ex> of a leg, a finger, a tooth, or a
broom</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The legs; <as>as, to stir one's
<ex>stumps</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <def>One of the three pointed rods
stuck in the ground to form a wicket and support the bails.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A short, thick roll of leather or paper, cut to a
point, or any similar implement, used to rub down the lines of a crayon
or pencil drawing, in shading it, or for shading drawings by producing
tints and gradations from crayon, etc., in powder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A pin in a tumbler lock which forms an obstruction
to throwing the bolt, except when the gates of the tumblers are properly
arranged, as by the key; a fence; also, a pin or projection in a lock to
form a guide for a movable piece.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Leg stump</b></col> <fld>(Cricket)</fld>, <cd>the stump
nearest to the batsman.</cd> -- <col><b>Off stump</b></col>
<fld>(Cricket)</fld>, <cd>the stump farthest from the batsman.</cd> --
<col><b>Stump tracery</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>a term used to
describe late German Gothic tracery, in which the molded bar seems to
pass through itself in its convolutions, and is then cut off short, so
that a section of the molding is seen at the end of each similar
stump.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>To go on the stump</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>To take the stump</b></col></mcol>, <cd>to engage in making
public addresses for electioneering purposes; -- a phrase derived from
the practice of using a stump for a speaker's platform in newly-settled
districts. Hence also the phrases <xex>stump orator</xex>, <xex>stump
speaker</xex>, <xex>stump speech</xex>, <xex>stump oratory</xex>,
etc.</cd> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark></cs> -- <col><b>on the
stump</b></col> <cd>campaigning for public office; running for election
to office.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stump</ent><br/
<hw>Stump</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stumped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stumping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cut off a part
of; to reduce to a stump; to lop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Around the <qex>stumped</qex> top soft moss did grow.</q>
<rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strike, as the toes, against a stone or something
fixed; to stub.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To challenge; also, to nonplus.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To travel over, delivering speeches for
electioneering purposes; <as>as, to <ex>stump</ex> a State, or a
district</as>. See <cref>To go on the stump</cref>, under
<er>Stump</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def> <mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Cricket)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To put (a batsman)
out of play by knocking off the bail, or knocking down the stumps of the
wicket he is defending while he is off his allotted ground; -- sometimes
with <xex>out</xex>.</def> <au>T. Hughes.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>To bowl
down the stumps of, as, of a wicket.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A herd of boys with clamor bowled,<br/
And <qex>stumped</qex> the wicket.</q> <rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stump it</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To go afoot;
hence, to run away; to escape</cd>. <mark>[Slang]</mark> <au>Ld.
Lytton.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To make electioneering speeches.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq. U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stump</ent><br/
<hw>Stump</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To walk clumsily, as if on
stumps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To stump up</b></col>, <cd>to pay cash.</cd> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <au>Halliwell.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumpage</ent><br/
<hw>Stump"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Timber
in standing trees, -- often sold without the land at a fixed price per
tree or per stump, the stumps being counted when the land is
cleared.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Only trees above a certain size are allowed to be cut by loggers
buying <qex>stumpage</qex> from the owners of land.</q> <rj><qau>C. S.
Sargent.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A tax on the amount of timber cut, regulated by the
price of lumber.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> <rj><au>The
Nation.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumper</ent><br/
<hw>Stump"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
stumps.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A boastful person.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A puzzling or incredible story.</def> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumpiness</ent><br/
<hw>Stump"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of
being stumpy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stump-tailed</ent><br/
<hw>Stump"-tailed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a short,
thick tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stump-tailed lizard</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
singular Australian scincoid lizard (<spn>Trachydosaurus rugosus</spn>)
having a short, thick tail resembling its head in form; -- called also
<altname>sleeping lizard</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stumpy</ent><br/
<hw>Stump"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Full of
stumps; hard; strong.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Short and thick; stubby.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <ldquo/A <xex>stumpy</xex> little man.<rdquo/
<rj><au>J. C. Harris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stun</ent><br/
<hw>Stun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stunned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stunning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>stonien</ets>,
<ets>stownien</ets>; either fr. AS. <ets>stunian</ets> to resound (cf.
D. <ets>stenen</ets> to groan, G. <ets>st<oum/hnen</ets>, Icel.
<ets>stynja</ets>, Gr. <?/, Skr. <ets>stan</ets> to thunder, and E.
<ets>thunder</ets>), or from the same source as E. <ets>astonish</ets>.
<root/168.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make senseless or dizzy by
violence; to render senseless by a blow, as on the head.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One hung a poleax at his saddlebow,<br/
And one a heavy mace to <qex>stun</qex> the foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To dull or deaden the sensibility of; to overcome;
especially, to overpower one's sense of hearing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>stunned</qex> him with the music of the spheres.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To astonish; to overpower; to bewilder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>William was quite <qex>stunned</qex> at my discourse.</q>
<rj><qau>De Foe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stun</ent><br/
<hw>Stun</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The condition of being
stunned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stundist</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stun"dist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>shtundist</ets>, prob. fr. G. <ets>stunde</ets> hour; -- from their
meetings for Bible reading.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of
a large sect of Russian dissenters founded, about 1860, in the village
of Osnova, near Odessa, by a peasant, Onishchenko, who had apparently
been influenced by a German sect settled near there. They zealously
practice Bible reading and reject priestly dominion and all external
rites of worship.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Stun"dism</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stung</ent><br/
<hw>Stung</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sting</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stunk</ent><br/
<hw>Stunk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Stink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stunner</ent><br/
<hw>Stun"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who,
or that which, stuns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something striking or amazing in quality; something
of extraordinary excellence.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stunning</ent><br/
<hw>Stun"ning</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Overpowering consciousness; overpowering the senses; especially,
overpowering the sense of hearing; confounding with noise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Striking or overpowering with astonishment,
especially on account of excellence; <as>as, <ex>stunning</ex>
poetry</as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark> <au>C. Kingsley.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Stun"ning*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Slang]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stunsail</ent><br/
<hw>Stun"sail</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A
contraction of <er>Studding sail</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With every rag set, <qex>stunsails</qex>, sky scrapers and
all.</q> <rj><qau>Lowell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stunt</ent><br/
<hw>Stunt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Stunted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stunting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Stint</er>.]</ety>
<def>To hinder from growing to the natural size; to prevent the growth
of; to stint, to dwarf; <as>as, to <ex>stunt</ex> a child; to
<ex>stunt</ex> a plant</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When, by a cold penury, I blast the abilities of a nation, and
<qex>stunt</qex> the growth of its active energies, the ill or may do is
beyond all calculation.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stunt</ent><br/
<hw>Stunt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A check in
growth; also, that which has been checked in growth; a stunted animal or
thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically: A whale two years old, which, having
been weaned, is lean, and yields but little blubber.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stunt</ent><br/
<hw>Stunt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Stint</er> a
task.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A feat hard to perform; an act which is
striking for the skill, strength, or the like, required to do it; a
feat.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></p>

<p><q>An extraordinary man does three or four different
<ldquo/<qex>stunts</qex><rdquo/ with remarkable dexterity.</q>
<rj><qau>The Bookman.</qau></rj></p>

<p><q>He does not try to do <qex>stunts</qex>; and, above all, he does
not care to go in swimming.</q> <rj><qau>L. Hutton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>an unusual action performed to gain public
attention; <as>as, a publicity <ex>stunt</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stunted</ent><br/
<hw>Stunt"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dwarfed.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Stunt"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuntness</ent><br/
<hw>Stunt"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Stuntedness; brevity.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Earle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stu"pa</hw> <pr>(st<oomac/"p<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Skr. <ets>st<umac/pa</ets>.]</ety> <def>A mound or monument
commemorative of Buddha.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupa</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stu"pa</hw> <pr>(st<umac/"p<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>See 1st <er>Stupe</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupe</ent><br/
<hw>Stupe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stupa</ets>, or
better <ets>stuppa</ets>, tow. Cf. <er>Stop</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Cloth or flax dipped in warm
water or medicaments and applied to a hurt or sore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupe</ent><br/
<hw>Stupe</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stuped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stuping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To foment with a stupe.</def>
<rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupe</ent><br/
<hw>Stupe</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Stupid</er>.]</ety> <def>A
stupid person.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupefacient</ent><br/
<hw>Stu`pe*fa"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stupefaciens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>stupefacere</ets> to stupefy;
<ets>stupere</ets> to be stupefied + <ets>facere</ets> to make. Cf.
<er>Stupefy</er>.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stupifacient</asp>.]</altsp> <def>Producing stupefaction;
stupefactive.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Anything promoting stupefaction; a narcotic.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupefaction</ent><br/
<hw>Stu`pe*fac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stup<eacute/faction</ets>. See <er>Stupefacient</er>.]</ety>
<def>The act of stupefying, or the state of being stupefied.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>stupifaction</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Resistance of the dictates of conscience brings a hardness and
<qex>stupefaction</qex> upon it.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupefactive</ent><br/
<hw>Stu`pe*fac"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stup<eacute/factif</ets>, LL. <ets>stupefactivus</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Stupefacient</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stupifactive</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupefied</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"pe*fied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having been made
stupid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupefiedness</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"pe*fied`ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
stupid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupefier</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"pe*fi`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, stupefies; a stupefying agent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupefy</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"pe*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stupefied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stupefying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>stup<eacute/fier</ets>, fr. L. <ets>stupere</ets> to be stupefied +
<ets>ficare</ets> (in comp.) to make, akin to <ets>facere</ets>. See
<er>Stupid</er>, <er>Fact</er>, and cf. <er>Stupefacient</er>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>stupify</asp>, especially in England.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To make stupid; to make dull; to blunt the faculty of
perception or understanding in; to deprive of sensibility; to make
torpid.</def><-- temporarily! as by excessive dullness or repetition
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fumes of drink discompose and <qex>stupefy</qex> the
brain.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To deprive of material mobility.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is not malleable; but yet is not fluent, but
<qex>stupefied</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupendous</ent><br/
<hw>Stu*pen"dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stupendus</ets> astonishing, p. future pass. of <ets>stupere</ets>
to be astonished at. Cf. <er>Stupid</er>.]</ety> <def>Astonishing;
wonderful; amazing; especially, astonishing in magnitude or elevation;
<as>as, a <ex>stupendous</ex> pile</as>.</def> <ldquo/A
<xex>stupendous</xex> sum.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All are but parts of one <qex>stupendous</qex> whole.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Stu*pen"dous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Stu*pen"dous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupeous</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"pe*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stupa</ets>, or better <ets>stuppa</ets>, tow; cf. L.
<ets>stuppeus</ets> made of tow. Cf. <er>Stupose</er>.]</ety>
<def>Resembling tow; having long, loose scales, or matted filaments,
like tow; stupose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupid</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"pid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stupidus</ets>, fr. <ets>stupere</ets> to be stupefied: cf. F.
<ets>stupide</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Very dull; insensible;
senseless; wanting in understanding; heavy; sluggish; in a state of
stupor; -- said of persons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O that men . . . should be so <qex>stupid</qex> grown . . . <br/
As to forsake the living God!</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With wild surprise,<br/
A moment <qex>stupid</qex>, motionless he stood.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resulting from, or evincing, stupidity; formed
without skill or genius; dull; heavy; -- said of things.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Observe what loads of <qex>stupid</qex> rhymes<br/
Oppress us in corrupted times.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Simple; insensible; sluggish; senseless; doltish;
sottish; dull; heavy; clodpated.</syn> -- <wordforms><wf>Stu"pid*ly</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Stu"pid*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupidity</ent><br/
<hw>Stu*pid"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stupiditas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>stupidit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being stupid; extreme dullness
of perception or understanding; insensibility; sluggishness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Stupor; astonishment; stupefaction.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>stupidity</qex><br/
Past admiration strikes me, joined with fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupify</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"pi*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Stupefy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupor</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"por</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., from
<ets>stupere</ets> to be struck senseless.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Great
diminution or suspension of sensibility; suppression of sense or
feeling; lethargy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Intellectual insensibility; moral stupidity;
heedlessness or inattention to one's interests.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupose</ent><br/
<hw>Stu*pose</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>stupa</ets>,
or better <ets>stuppa</ets>, tow. Cf. <er>Stupeous</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Composed of, or having, tufted or matted
filaments like tow; stupeous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuprate</ent><br/
<hw>Stu"prate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stupratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>stuprare</ets> to ravish, fr.
<ets>stuprum</ets> defilement.]</ety> <def>To ravish; to debauch.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Heywood.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stupration</ent><br/
<hw>Stu*pra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Violation of
chastity by force; rape.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuprum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stu"prum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>Stupration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturb</ent><br/
<hw>Sturb</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To disturb.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturdily</ent><br/
<hw>Stur"di*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sturdy
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturdiness</ent><br/
<hw>Stur"di*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
sturdy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturdy</ent><br/
<hw>Stur"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sturdier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sturdiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sturdi</ets>
inconsiderable, OF. <ets>estourdi</ets> stunned, giddy, thoughtless,
rash, F. <ets><eacute/tourdi</ets>, p. p. of OF. <ets>estourdir</ets> to
stun, to render giddy, to amaze, F. <ets><eacute/tourdir</ets>; of
uncertain origin. The sense has probably been influenced by E.
<ets>stout</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Foolishly obstinate or
resolute; stubborn; unrelenting; unfeeling; stern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>sturdy</qex> marquis gan his hearte dress<br/
To rue upon her wifely steadfastness.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This must be done, and I would fain see<br/
Mortal so <qex>sturdy</qex> as to gainsay.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sturdy</qex>, hardened sinner shall advance to the utmost
pitch of impiety with less reluctance than he took the first steps.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resolute, in a good sense; or firm, unyielding
quality; <as>as, a man of <ex>sturdy</ex> piety or
patriotism</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Characterized by physical strength or force; strong;
lusty; violent; <as>as, a <ex>sturdy</ex> lout</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How bowed the woods beneath their <qex>sturdy</qex> stroke!</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Stiff; stout; strong; <as>as, a <ex>sturdy</ex>
oak</as>.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was not of any delicate contexture; his limbs rather
<qex>sturdy</qex> than dainty.</q> <rj><qau>Sir H.
Wotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Hardy; stout; strong; firm; robust;
stiff.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1431 --></p>

<p><ent>Sturdy</ent><br/
<hw>Stur"dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>estourdi</ets> giddiness, stupefaction.]</ety> <fld>(Vet.)</fld>
<def>A disease in sheep and cattle, marked by great nervousness, or by
dullness and stupor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturgeon</ent><br/
<hw>Stur"geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>esturgeon</ets>, LL. <ets>sturio</ets>, <ets>sturgio</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sturjo</ets>, G. <ets>st<oum/r</ets>; akin to AS.
<ets>styria</ets>, <ets>styriga</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of large cartilaginous ganoid fishes
belonging to <gen>Acipenser</gen> and allied genera of the family
<fam>Acipenseridae</fam>. They run up rivers to spawn, and are common on
the coasts and in the large rivers and lakes of North America, Europe,
and Asia. Caviar is prepared from the roe, and isinglass from the air
bladder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common North American species are <spn>Acipenser
sturio</spn> of the Atlantic coast region, <spn>Acipenser
transmontanus</spn> of the Pacific coast, and <spn>Acipenser
rubicundus</spn> of the Mississippi River and its tributaries. In
Europe, the common species is <spn>Acipenser sturio</spn>, and other
well-known species are the sterlet and the huso. The sturgeons are
included in the order <ord>Chondrostei</ord>. Their body is partially
covered by five rows of large, carinated, bony plates, of which one row
runs along the back. The tail is heterocercal. The toothless and
protrusile mouth is beneath the head, and has four barbels in
front.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Shovel-nosed sturgeon</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Shovelnose</er> <sd>(d)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturiones</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Stu`ri*o"nes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from LL. <ets>sturio</ets>. See <er>Sturgeon</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of fishes including the
sturgeons.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturionian</ent><br/
<hw>Stu`ri*o"ni*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the family of fishes of which the sturgeon is the
type.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturk</ent><br/
<hw>Sturk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Stirk</er>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturnoid</ent><br/
<hw>Stur"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sturnus</ets> a starling + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Like or pertaining to the starlings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturt</ent><br/
<hw>Sturt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Start</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <def>To vex; to annoy; to startle.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturt</ent><br/
<hw>Sturt</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Disturbance; annoyance;
care.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Scot.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Sturt</xex> and care.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J.
Rolland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mining)</fld> <def>A bargain in tribute mining by
which the tributor profits.</def> <rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sturtion</ent><br/
<hw>Stur"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A corruption of
<er>Nasturtion</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stut</ent><br/
<hw>Stut</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To stutter.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Skelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stutter</ent><br/
<hw>Stut"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Stuttered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Stuttering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>stut</ets>, OE. <ets>stoten</ets>; probably of Dutch or Low German
origin; cf. D. & LG. <ets>stotteren</ets>, G. <ets>stottern</ets>, D.
<ets>stooten</ets> to push, to strike; akin to G. <ets>stossen</ets>,
Icel. <ets>stauta</ets>, Sw. <ets>st<oum/ta</ets>, Dan.
<ets>st<oum/de</ets>, Goth. <ets>stautan</ets>, L. <ets>tundere</ets>,
Skr. <ets>tud</ets> to thrust. Cf. <er>Contuse</er>,
<er>Obtuse</er>.]</ety> <def>To hesitate or stumble in uttering words;
to speak with spasmodic repetition or pauses; to stammer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trembling, <qex>stuttering</qex>, calling for his confessor.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stutter</ent><br/
<hw>Stut"ter</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of stuttering;
a stammer. See <er>Stammer</er>, and <er>Stuttering</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who stutters; a stammerer.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stutterer</ent><br/
<hw>Stut"ter*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who stutters;
a stammerer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuttering</ent><br/
<hw>Stut"ter*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of one who stutters;
-- restricted by some physiologists to defective speech due to inability
to form the proper sounds, the breathing being normal, as distinguished
from <xex>stammering</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stuttering</ent><br/
<hw>Stut"ter*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Apt to stutter; hesitating;
stammering.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Stut"ter*ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sty</ent><br/
<hw>Sty</hw> <pr>(st<imac/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sties</plw> <pr>(st<imac/z)</pr>.</plu> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stigh</asp>.]</altsp> <ety>[AS. <ets>stigu</ets>, fr.
<ets>st<imac/gan</ets> to rise; originally, probably, a place into which
animals climbed or went up. <root/164. See <er>Sty</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>, and cf. <er>Steward</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A pen or
inclosure for swine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A place of bestial debauchery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To roll with pleasure in a sensual <qex>sty</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sty</ent><br/
<hw>Sty</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Stied</conjf> <pr>(st<imac/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Stying</conjf> <pr>(st<imac/"<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
shut up in, or as in, a sty.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sty</ent><br/
<hw>Sty</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stien</ets>,
<ets>sti<yogh/en</ets>, AS. <ets>st<imac/gan</ets> to rise; akin to D.
<ets>stijgen</ets>, OS. & OHG. <ets>st<imac/gan</ets>, G.
<ets>steigen</ets>, Icel. <ets>st<imac/ga</ets>, Sw. <ets>stiga</ets>,
Dan. <ets>stige</ets>, Goth. <ets>steigan</ets>, L. <ets>vestigium</ets>
footstep, Gr. <?/ to walk, to go, Skr. <ets>stigh</ets> to mount. Cf.
<er>Distich</er>, <er>Stair</er> steps, <er>Stirrup</er>, <er>Sty</er> a
boil, a pen for swine, <er>Vestige</er>.]</ety> <def>To soar; to ascend;
to mount. See <er>Stirrup</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With bolder wing shall dare aloft to <qex>sty</qex>,<br/
To the last praises of this Faery Queene.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sty</ent><br/
<hw>Sty</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For older <ets>styan</ets>,
<ets>styanye</ets>, understood as <ets>sty on eye</ets>, AS.
<ets>st<imac/gend</ets> (sc. <ets>e<aacute/ge</ets> eye), properly,
rising, or swelling (eye), p. p. of <ets>st<imac/gan</ets> to rise. See
<er>Sty</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An inflamed
swelling or boil on the edge of the eyelid.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stye</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styan</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Sty</er>, a
boil.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>De quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styca</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"ca</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL., fr. AS.
<ets>stic</ets>, <ets>styc</ets>, <ets>stycge</ets>.]</ety> <def>An
anglo-Saxon copper coin of the lowest value, being worth half a
farthing.</def> <rj><au>S. M. Leake.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stycerin</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"cer*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sty</ets>ryl
+ gly<ets>cerin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A triacid alcohol,
related to glycerin, and obtained from certain styryl derivatives as a
yellow, gummy, amorphous substance; -- called also <altname>phenyl
glycerin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stye</ent><br/
<hw>Stye</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Sty</er>, a boil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stygial</ent><br/
<hw>Styg"i*al</hw> <pr>(st<icr/j"<icr/*<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Stygian.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Skelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stygian</ent><br/
<hw>Styg"i*an</hw> <pr>(st<icr/j"<icr/*<ait/n)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>Stygius</ets>, fr. <ets>Styx</ets>, <ets>Stygis</ets>, Gr.
<grk>Sty`x</grk>, <grk>Stygo`s</grk>, the Styx.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to the river Styx; hence, hellish; infernal. See
<er>Styx</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At that so sudden blaze, the <qex>Stygian</qex> throng<br/
Bent their aspect.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylagalmaic</ent><br/
<hw>Sty`la*gal*ma"ic</hw>
<pr>(st<imac/`l<adot/*g<acr/l*m<amac/"<icr/k)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>sty^los</grk> a column + <grk>'a`galma</grk> an
image.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Performing the office of columns;
<as>as, Atlantes and Caryatides are <ex>stylagalmaic</ex> figures or
images</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>stylogalmaic</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylar</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See
<er>Stilar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylaster</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sty*las"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<grk>sty^los</grk> pillar + <grk>'asth`r</grk> star.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of delicate, usually
pink, calcareous hydroid corals of the genus
<gen>Stylaster</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Style</ent><br/
<hw>Style</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>stile</ets>,
F. <ets>style</ets>, Of. also <ets>stile</ets>, L. <ets>stilus</ets> a
style or writing instrument, manner or writing, mode of expression;
probably for <ets>stiglus</ets>, meaning, a pricking instrument, and
akin to E. <ets>stick</ets>. See <er>Stick</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and
cf. <er>Stiletto</er>. The spelling with <it>y</it> is due to a supposed
connection with Gr. <grk>sty^los</grk> a pillar.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>An instrument used by the ancients in writing on tablets covered
with wax, having one of its ends sharp, and the other blunt, and
somewhat expanded, for the purpose of making erasures by smoothing the
wax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, anything resembling the ancient style in
shape or use.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>A pen; an author's pen.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>A sharp-pointed tool used in engraving; a
graver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>A kind of blunt-pointed surgical
instrument.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A long, slender, bristlelike
process, as the anal <xex>styles</xex> of insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(e)</sd> <ety>[Perhaps fr. Gr. <grk>sty^los</grk> a
pillar.]</ety> <def>The pin, or gnomon, of a dial, the shadow of which
indicates the hour. See <er>Gnomon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(f)</sd> <ety>[Probably fr. Gr. <grk>sty^los</grk> a
pillar.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The elongated part of a pistil
between the ovary and the stigma. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Stamen</er>, and of <er>Pistil</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Mode of expressing thought in language, whether oral
or written; especially, such use of language in the expression of
thought as exhibits the spirit and faculty of an artist; choice or
arrangement of words in discourse; rhetorical expression.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>High <qex>style</qex>, as when that men to kinges write.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Style</qex> is the dress of thoughts.</q>
<rj><qau>Chesterfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Proper words in proper places make the true definition of
<qex>style</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is <qex>style</qex> alone by which posterity will judge of a
great work.</q> <rj><qau>I. Disraeli.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Mode of presentation, especially in music or any of
the fine arts; a characteristic of peculiar mode of developing in idea
or accomplishing a result.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ornamental <qex>style</qex> also possesses its own peculiar
merit.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J. Reynolds.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Conformity to a recognized standard; manner which is
deemed elegant and appropriate, especially in social demeanor;
fashion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>According to the usual <qex>style</qex> of dedications.</q>
<rj><qau>C. Middleton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Mode or phrase by which anything is formally
designated; the title; the official designation of any important body;
mode of address; <as>as, the <ex>style</ex> of Majesty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One <qex>style</qex> to a gracious benefactor, another to a proud,
insulting foe.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Chron.)</fld> <def>A mode of reckoning time, with
regard to the Julian and Gregorian calendars.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Style</xex> is <xex>Old</xex> or <xex>New</xex>.
The <xex>Old</xex> Style follows the Julian manner of computing the
months and days, or the calendar as established by Julius Caesar, in
which every fourth year consists of 366 days, and the other years of 365
days. This is about 11 minutes in a year too much. Pope Georgy XIII.
reformed the calendar by retrenching 10 days in October, 1582, in order
to bring back the vernal equinox to the same day as at the time of the
Council of Nice, <sc>a. d.</sc> 325. This reformation was adopted by act
of the British Parliament in 1751, by which act 11 days in September,
1752, were retrenched, and the third day was reckoned the fourteenth.
This mode of reckoning is called <xex>New</xex> Style, according to
which every year divisible by 4, unless it is divisible by 100 without
being divisible by 400, has 366 days, and any other year 365
days.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Style of court</b></col>, <cd>the practice or manner
observed by a court in its proceedings.</cd> <au>Ayliffe.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Diction; phraseology; manner; course; title. See
<er>Diction</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Style</ent><br/
<hw>Style</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Styled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Styling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To entitle; to term, name, or
call; to denominate.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Styled</xex> great
conquerors.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How well his worth and brave adventures <qex>styled</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To call; name; denominate; designate; term;
characterize.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylet</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., dim. of
<ets>style</ets>; cf. It. <ets>stiletto</ets>. See
<er>Stiletto</er>.]</ety> <def>A small poniard; a stiletto.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An instrument for
examining wounds and fistulas, and for passing setons, and the like; a
probe, -- called also <altname>specillum</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>A stiff wire, inserted in catheters or other tubular instruments to
maintain their shape and prevent clogging.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any small, more or less rigid,
bristlelike organ; <as>as, the caudal <ex>stylets</ex> of certain
insects; the ventral <ex>stylets</ex> of certain
Infusoria</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styliferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sty*lif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Style</ets> + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Bearing one or more styles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styliform</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Style</ets>
+ <ets>-form</ets>: cf. F. <ets>styliforme</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the
form of, or resembling, a style, pin, or pen; styloid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylish</ent><br/
<hw>Styl"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having style or
artistic quality; given to, or fond of, the display of style; highly
fashionable; modish; <as>as, a <ex>stylish</ex> dress, house,
manner</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Styl"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-- <wf>Styl"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylist</ent><br/
<hw>Styl"ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who is a master or a model of
style, especially in writing or speaking; a critic of style.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Distinguished as a <qex>stylist</qex>, for ease.</q>
<rj><qau>Fitzed. Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylistic</ent><br/
<hw>Sty*lis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to style in language.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/<xex>Stylistic</xex> trifles.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. A.
Symonds.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The great <qex>stylistic</qex> differences in the works ascribed
to him [Wyclif].</q> <rj><qau>G. P. Marsh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylite</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"lite</hw> <pr>(st<imac/"l<imac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>styli`ths</grk>, fr. <grk>sty^los</grk> a pillar.]</ety>
<fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of a sect of anchorites in the early
church, who lived on the tops of pillars for the exercise of their
patience; -- called also <altname>pillarist</altname> and
<altname>pillar saint</altname>.</def><-- precursors of the
flagpole-sitters! --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The two other holy men in Gregory's narrative had more exotic
origins than the pair that has just been seen. Gregory encountered one
of them when on a journey to the north-eastern parts of the Frankish
kingdom. This was a Lombard, named Vulfolaic, who had spent some years
in the arduous exercise of being a <ex>stylite</ex>, the Christian
equivalent of a flagpole sitter; in other words, Vulfolaic was a monk
whose main austerity consisted in living on top of a pillar. By carrying
out this feat in the rain, snow, and frost of the Moselle valley,
Vulfolaic had convinced the local population to overthrow and abandon
the idol of Diana to which they were addicted.</q> <rj><qau>Walter
Goffart, FOREIGNERS IN THE HISTORIES OF GREGORY OF TOURS
(https://journals.lib.unb.ca/index.php/flor/article/download/15346/20501).</qau></rj></p>

<p><ent>Stylo-</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"lo-</hw> <pr>(st<imac/"l<osl/-)</pr>. <def>A combining form used
in anatomy to indicate <xex>connection with</xex>, or <xex>relation
to</xex>, <xex>the styloid process of the temporal bone</xex>; <as>as,
<ex>stylo</ex>hyal, <ex>stylo</ex>mastoid,
<ex>stylo</ex>maxillary</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylobate</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"lo*bate</hw> <pr>(st<imac/"l<osl/*b<asl/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[L. <ets>stylobates</ets>, <ets>stylobata</ets>, Gr.
<grk>styloba`ths</grk>; <grk>sty^los</grk> a pillar + <grk>ba`ths</grk>
one that treads, fr. <grk>bai`nein</grk> to go.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The uninterrupted and continuous flat band,
coping, or pavement upon which the bases of a row of columns are
supported. See <er>Sub-base</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styloglossal</ent><br/
<hw>Sty`lo*glos"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stylo-</ets> + <ets>glossal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to styloid process and the tongue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylograph</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"lo*graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A stylographic
pen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylographic</ent><br/
<hw>Sty`lo*graph"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to stylography; used in stylography; <as>as,
<ex>stylographic</ex> tablets</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or used in, stylographic pen; <as>as,
<ex>stylographic</ex> ink</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stylographic pen</b></col>, <cd>a pen with a conical
point like that of a style, combined with a reservoir for supplying it
with ink.</cd> -- <col><b>Stylographic pencil</b></col>, <cd>a pencil
used in stylography.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylographical</ent><br/
<hw>Sty`lo*graph"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Stylographic</er>, 1.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sty`lo*graph"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylography</ent><br/
<hw>Sty*log"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Style</ets> + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A mode of
writing or tracing lines by means of a style on cards or
tablets.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylohyal</ent><br/
<hw>Sty`lo*hy"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Stylo-</ets> + the Gr. letter <?/.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>A segment in the hyoidean arch between the epihyal and
tympanohyal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylohyoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sty`lo*hy"oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the styloid process and the hyoid
bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styloid</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"loid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Style</ets> +
<ets>-oid</ets>: cf. F. <ets>stylo<ium/de</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Styliform; <as>as, the <ex>styloid</ex>
process</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the styloid
process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Styloid process</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a long
and slender process from the lower side of the temporal bone of man,
corresponding to the tympanohyal and stylohyal of other
animals.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylomastoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sty`lo*mas"toid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the styloid and mastoid processes of the
temporal bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylomaxillary</ent><br/
<hw>Sty`lo*max"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the styloid process and the
maxilla.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sty*lom"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ column
+ <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>An instrument for measuring
columns.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylommata</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sty*lom"ma*ta</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a pillar + <?/, <?/, the eye.]</ety> <def>Same as
<er>Stylommatophora</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylommatophora</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sty*lom`ma*toph"o*ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ column + <?/ eye + <grk>fe`rein</grk> to
bear.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of Pulmonata in which
the eyes are situated at the tips of the tentacles. It includes the
common land snails and slugs. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Snail</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylommatophorous</ent><br/
<hw>Sty*lom`ma*toph"o*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to Stylommatophora.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylopodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sty`lo*po"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Stylopodia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Style</er>, and <er>Podium</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>An expansion at the base of the style, as in umbelliferous
plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylops</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sty"lops</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/
a pillar + <?/, <?/, the eye.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of
minute insects parasitic, in their larval state, on bees and wasps. It
is the typical genus of the group Strepsiptera, formerly considered a
distinct order, but now generally referred to the Coleoptera. See
<er>Strepsiptera</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stylus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sty"lus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stylus</ets>, or better <ets>stilus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>An instrument for writing. See <er>Style</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That needle-shaped part at the tip of the playing
arm of phonograph which sits in the groove of a phonograph record while
it is turning, to detect the undulations in the phonograph groove and
convert them into vibrations which are transmitted to a system (since
1920 electronic) which converts the signal into sound; also called
<altname>needle</altname>. The <ex>stylus</ex> is frequently composed of
a hard metal or of diamond.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The needle-like device used to cut the grooves which
record the sound on the original disc during recording of a phonograph
record; it is moved by the vibrations given to the diaphragm by a sound,
and produces the indented record.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Computers)</fld> <def>A pen-shaped pointing device
used to specify the cursor position on a graphics tablet.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimy</ent><br/
<ent>Stymie</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sty"mie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> Also <hw>Sti"my</hw>
}</mhw>. <ety>[Orig. uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>The
position of two balls on the putting green such that, being more than
six inches apart, one ball lies directly between the other and the hole
at which the latter must be played; also, the act of bringing the balls
into this position.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stimy</ent><br/
<ent>Stymie</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sty"mie</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> Also <hw>Sti"my</hw> }</mhw>.
<fld>(Golf)</fld> <def>To bring into the position of, or impede by, a
stymie.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styphnate</ent><br/
<hw>Styph"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of styphnic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styphnic</ent><br/
<hw>Styph"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. (spurious)
<grk>sty`fein</grk> to contract.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, a yellow crystalline astringent
acid, <chform>(NO2)3.C6H.(OH)2</chform>, obtained by the action of
nitric acid on resorcin. <xex>Styphnic acid</xex> resembles picric acid,
but is not bitter. It acts like a strong dibasic acid, having a series
of well defined salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styptic</ent><br/
<hw>Styp"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>stypticus</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to contract.]</ety>
<def>Producing contraction; stopping bleeding; having the quality of
restraining hemorrhage when applied to the bleeding part;
astringent.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>stiptic</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Styptic weed</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an American
leguminous herb (<spn>Cassia occidentalis</spn>) closely related to the
wild senna.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styptic</ent><br/
<hw>Styp"tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A styptic
medicine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styptical</ent><br/
<hw>Styp"tic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Styptic;
astringent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stypticity</ent><br/
<hw>Styp*tic"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>stypticit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
styptic; astringency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styracin</ent><br/
<hw>Styr"a*cin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Styrax</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white crystalline
tasteless substance extracted from gum storax, and consisting of a salt
of cinnamic acid with cinnamic alcohol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styrax</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"rax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>styrax</ets>,
<ets>storax</ets>, Gr. <?/. See <er>Storax</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of shrubs and trees, mostly American or
Asiatic, abounding in resinous and aromatic substances. <spn>Styrax
officinalis</spn> yields storax, and <spn>Styrax Benzoin</spn> yields
benzoin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Storax</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styrol</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"rol</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Styr</ets>ax +
L. <ets>ol</ets>eum oil.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Styrolene</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styrolene</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"ro*lene</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An unsaturated hydrocarbon, <chform>C8H8</chform>, obtained by the
distillation of storax, by the decomposition of cinnamic acid, and by
the condensation of acetylene, as a fragrant, aromatic, mobile liquid;
-- called also <altname>phenyl ethylene</altname>, <altname>vinyl
benzene</altname>, <altname>styrol</altname>,
<altname>styrene</altname>, and <altname>cinnamene</altname>.</def><--
most commonly, styrene --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styrone</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"rone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
white crystalline substance having a sweet taste and a hyacinthlike
odor, obtained by the decomposition of styracin; -- properly called
<altname>cinnamic alcohol</altname> <it>or</it> <altname>styryl
alcohol</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1432 --></p>

<p><ent>Styryl</ent><br/
<hw>Sty"ryl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Styr</ets>ax +
<ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hypothetical radical
found in certain derivatives of styrolene and cinnamic acid; -- called
also <altname>cinnyl</altname>, or
<altname>cinnamyl</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stythe</ent><br/
<hw>Stythe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mining)</fld>
<def>Choke damp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Stythy</ent><br/
<hw>Styth"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Stithy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Styx</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Styx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Class. Myth.)</fld> <def>The principal river of the
lower world, which had to be crossed in passing to the regions of the
dead.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suability</ent><br/
<hw>Su`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>Liability to be sued; the state of being subjected by law to civil
process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suable</ent><br/
<hw>Su"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>Capable of being sued; subject by law to be called to answer in
court.</def> <rj><au>Story.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suade</ent><br/
<hw>Suade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suadere</ets>.]</ety> <def>To persuade.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suadible</ent><br/
<hw>Suad"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suadibilis</ets>.]</ety> <def>Suasible.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wyclif (James iii. 17).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suage</ent><br/
<hw>Suage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To assuage.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suant</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Sue</er> to
pursue.]</ety> <def>Spread equally over the surface; uniform;
even.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>suent</asp>.]</altsp>
<mark>[Local, U.S. & Prov. Eng.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Su"ant*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> <mark>[Local, U.S. &
Prov. Eng.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suasible</ent><br/
<hw>Sua"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suadere</ets>, <ets>suasum</ets>, to persuade.]</ety> <def>Capable
of being persuaded; easily persuaded.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suasion</ent><br/
<hw>Sua"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suasio</ets>, fr. <ets>suadere</ets>, <ets>suasum</ets>, to advise,
persuade, fr. <ets>suadus</ets> persuading, persuasive; akin to
<ets>suavis</ets> sweet: cf. OF. <ets>suasion</ets>. See <er>Suave</er>,
and cf. <er>Dissuade</er>, <er>Persuade</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
persuading; persuasion; <as>as, moral <ex>suasion</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suasive</ent><br/
<hw>Sua"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having power to
persuade; persuasive; suasory.</def> <au>South.</au> <ldquo/Genial and
<xex>suasive</xex> satire.<rdquo/ <au>Earle.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Sua"sive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suasory</ent><br/
<hw>Sua"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suasorius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suasoire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to
persuade; suasive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suave</ent><br/
<hw>Suave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>suavis</ets>
sweet, pleasant: cf. F. <ets>suave</ets>. See <er>Sweet</er>, and cf.
<er>Suasion</er>.]</ety> <def>Sweet; pleasant; delightful; gracious or
agreeable in manner; bland.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Suave"ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suavify</ent><br/
<hw>Suav"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suavified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suavifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[<ets>Suave</ets> + <ets>-fy</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make affable or
suave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suaviloquent</ent><br/
<hw>Sua*vil"o*quent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suaviloquens</ets>; <ets>suavis</ets> sweet + <ets>loquens</ets>,
p. pr. of <ets>loqui</ets> to speak.]</ety> <def>Sweetly speaking; using
agreeable speech.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suaviloquy</ent><br/
<hw>Sua*vil"o*quy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suaviloquium</ets>.]</ety> <def>Sweetness of speech.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suavity</ent><br/
<hw>Suav"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suavitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suavit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Sweetness to the taste.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality of being sweet or pleasing to the mind;
agreeableness; softness; pleasantness; gentleness; urbanity; <as>as,
<ex>suavity</ex> of manners; <ex>suavity</ex> of language, conversation,
or address</as>.</def> <rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sub-</ent><br/
<hw>Sub-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>sub</ets> under, below; akin
to Gr. <?/, Skr. <ets>upa</ets> to, on, under, over. Cf. <er>Hypo-</er>,
<er>Super-</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A prefix signifying
<xex>under</xex>, <xex>below</xex>, <xex>beneath</xex>, and hence often,
<xex>in an inferior position</xex> or <xex>degree</xex>, <xex>in an
imperfect</xex> or <xex>partial state</xex>, as in <xex>sub</xex>scribe,
<xex>sub</xex>struct, <xex>sub</xex>serve, <xex>sub</xex>ject,
<xex>sub</xex>ordinate, <xex>sub</xex>acid, <xex>sub</xex>astringent,
<xex>sub</xex>granular, <xex>sub</xex>orn. <xex>Sub-</xex> in Latin
compounds often becomes <xex>sum-</xex> before <xex>m</xex>,
<xex>sur</xex> before <xex>r</xex>, and regularly becomes
<xex>suc-</xex>, <xex>suf-</xex>, <xex>sug-</xex>, and <xex>sup-</xex>
before <xex>c</xex>, <xex>f</xex>, <xex>g</xex>, and <xex>p</xex>
respectively. Before <xex>c</xex>, <xex>p</xex>, and <xex>t</xex> it
sometimes takes form <xex>sus-</xex> (by the dropping of <xex>b</xex>
from a collateral form, <xex>subs-</xex>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A prefix denoting that the
ingredient (of a compound) signified by the term to which it is
prefixed,is present in <xex>only a small proportion</xex>, or <xex>less
than the normal amount</xex>; <as>as, <ex>sub</ex>sulphide,
<ex>sub</ex>oxide, etc.</as> Prefixed to the name of a salt it is
equivalent to <xex>basic</xex>; <as>as, <ex>sub</ex>acetate or
<ex>basic</ex> acetate</as>.</def> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sub</ent><br/
<hw>Sub</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A subordinate; a
subaltern.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a shortened form of <er>submarine</er>, the
boat.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>a shortened form of <er>submarine sandwich</er>;
also called <altname>hero</altname>, <altname>hero sandwich</altname>,
and <altname>grinder</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subacetate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ac"e*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An acetate containing an excess of the basic constituent.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subacid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ac"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subacidus</ets>. See <er>Sub-</er>, <er>Acid</er>.]</ety>
<def>Moderately acid or sour; <as>as, some plants have <ex>subacid</ex>
juices</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A substance moderately
acid.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subacrid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ac"rid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Moderately acrid or
harsh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subacromial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`a*cro"mi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated beneath the acromial process of the scapula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subact</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*act"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subactus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>subigere</ets> to subdue;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>agere</ets> to lead, bring.]</ety> <def>To
reduce; to subdue.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaction</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>subactio</ets> a working up, discipline.]</ety> <def>The act of
reducing to any state, as of mixing two bodies completely.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subacute</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`a*cute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Moderately
acute.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaduncate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`a*dun"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Somewhat hooked or curved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subadvocate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ad"vo*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An under or
subordinate advocate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaerial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`a*e"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Beneath the sky;
in the open air; specifically <fld>(Geol.)</fld>, taking place on the
earth's surface, as opposed to <xex>subaqueous</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subagency</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*a"gen*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A subordinate
agency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subagent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*a"gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A
person employed by an agent to transact the whole, or a part, of the
business intrusted to the latter.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.
Chitty.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subagitation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ag`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subagitatio</ets>, fr. <ets>subagitare</ets> to lie with
illicity.]</ety> <def>Unlawful sexual intercourse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subah</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"bah</hw> <pr>(s<oomac/"b<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Per. & Hind. <ets>s<umac/bah</ets>.]</ety> <def>A province; a
government, as of a viceroy; also, a subahdar.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subahdar</ent><br/
<hw>Su"bah*dar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Per. & Hind.
<ets>s<umac/bah-d<amac/r</ets>, Per. <ets>s<umac/bah</ets> a province +
<ets>d<amac/r</ets> holding, keeping.]</ety> <def>A viceroy; a governor
of a subah; also, a native captain in the British native army.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subahship</ent><br/
<ent>Subahdary</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su"bah*dar`y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Su"bah*ship</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or jurisdiction of a
subahdar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*aid"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To aid secretly;
to assist in a private manner, or indirectly.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subalmoner</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*al"mon*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An under
almoner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subalpine</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*al"pine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subalpinus</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld> <def>Inhabiting
the somewhat high slopes and summits of mountains, but considerably
below the snow line.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaltern</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*al"tern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>subalterne</ets>, LL. <ets>subalternus</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sub</ets>
under + <ets>alter</ets> the one, the other of two. See
<er>Alter</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Ranked or ranged below;
subordinate; inferior; specifically <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, ranking as a
junior officer; being below the rank of captain; <as>as, a
<ex>subaltern</ex> officer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>Asserting only a part of what is
asserted in a related proposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subaltern genus</b></col>. <fld>(Logic)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Genus</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaltern</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*al"tern</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
person holding a subordinate position; specifically, a commissioned
military officer below the rank of captain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>A subaltern proposition.</def>
<rj><au>Whately.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subalternant</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`al*ter"nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Logic)</fld>
<def>A universal proposition. See <er>Subaltern</er>, 2.</def>
<rj><au>Whately.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subalternate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`al*ter"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Succeeding by turns; successive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Subordinate; subaltern; inferior.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All their <qex>subalternate</qex> and several kinds.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subalternate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`al*ter"nate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>A
particular proposition, as opposed to a universal one. See
<er>Subaltern</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subalternating</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*al"ter*na`ting</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Subalternate; successive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subalternation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*al"ter*na`tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state
of being subalternate; succession of turns; subordination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subangular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*an"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slightly
angular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subapennine</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ap"en*nine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Under, or at
the foot of, the Apennine mountains; -- applied, in geology, to a series
of Tertiary strata of the older Pliocene period.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subapical</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ap"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being under the
apex; of or pertaining to the part just below the apex.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaquaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`a*qua"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subaquaneus</ets>; <ets>sub + aqua</ets> water.]</ety>
<def>Subaqueous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaqueous</ent><br/
<ent>Subaquatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub`a*quat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*a"que*ous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Being under water,
or beneath the surface of water; adapted for use under water; submarine;
<as>as, a <ex>subaqueous</ex> helmet</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Formed in or under water; <as>as,
<ex>subaqueous</ex> deposits</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subarachnoidal</ent><br/
<ent>Subarachnoid</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub`a*rach"noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sub*ar`ach*noid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the arachnoid membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subarctic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*arc"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Approximately
arctic; belonging to a region just without the arctic circle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subarcuated</ent><br/
<ent>Subarcuate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*ar"cu*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*ar"cu*a`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a figure resembling that
of a bow; somewhat curved or arched.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subarration</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ar*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>arra</ets>, <ets>arrha</ets>, earnest money.
See <er>Earnest</er> a pledge.]</ety> <def>The ancient custom of
betrothing by the bestowal, on the part of the man, of marriage gifts or
tokens, as money, rings, or other presents, upon the woman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subarytenoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`a*ryt"e*noid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the arytenoid cartilage of the larynx.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subastral</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*as"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Beneath the stars
or heavens; terrestrial.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Warburton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subastringent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`as*trin"gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
astringent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subatom</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*at"om</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
hypothetical component of a chemical atom, on the theory that the
elements themselves are complex substances; -- called also
<altname>atomicule</altname>.</def> <-- Add subatomic particles --
protons, neutrons, gluons, quarks, etc. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaud</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*aud"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subaudire</ets>, <ets>subauditum</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>audire</ets> to hear.]</ety> <def>To understand or supply in an
ellipsis.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaudition</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`au*di"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subauditio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of understanding, or
supplying, something not expressed; also, that which is so understood or
supplied.</def> <rj><au>Trench.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subaxillary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ax"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the axilla, or armpit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Placed under the axil, or angle
formed by the branch of a plant with the stem, or a leaf with the
branch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subbasal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ba"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Near the base.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sub-base</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"-base`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>The lowest member of a base when divided horizontally, or of a
baseboard, pedestal, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sub-bass</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"-bass`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The deepest
pedal stop, or the lowest tones of an organ; the fundamental or ground
bass.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sub-base</asp>.]</altsp>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subbeadle</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*bea"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An under
beadle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subbrachial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*brach"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to the subbrachians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subbrachiales</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sub*brach`i*a"les</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.
See <er>Sub-</er>, and <er>Brachial</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A division of soft-finned fishes in which the ventral fins are
situated beneath the pectorial fins, or nearly so.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subbrachian</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*brach"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + <ets>brachium</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of the Subbrachiales.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subbreed</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"breed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A race or strain differing in certain characters from the parent
breed; an incipient breed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subbronchial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*bron"chi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the bronchi; <as>as, the
<ex>subbronchial</ex> air sacs of birds</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcaliber</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cal"i*ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Smaller than the
caliber of a firearm.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>subcalibre</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subcaliber projectile</b></col>, <cd>a projectile having
a smaller diameter than the caliber of the arm from which it is fired,
and to which it is fitted by means of a sabot.</cd>
<au>Knight.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcarbonate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*car"bon*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A carbonate containing an excess of the basic
constituent.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcarboniferous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*car`bon*if"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the lowest division of the
Carboniferous formations underlying the proper coal measures. It was a
marine formation characterized in general by beds of limestone.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The Subcarboniferous period or
formation.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcarbureted</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*car"bu*ret`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>United with, or containing, carbon in less than
the normal proportion.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>subcarburetted</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcartilaginous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*car`ti*lag"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Situated under or beneath a
cartilage or cartilages.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Partially
cartilaginous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcaudal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cau"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the tail; <as>as, the
<ex>subcaudal</ex>, or chevron, bones</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcelestial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ce*les"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being beneath
the heavens; <as>as, <ex>subcelestial</ex> glories</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcellar</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cel"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A cellar beneath
another story wholly or partly underground; usually, a cellar under a
cellar.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcentral</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cen"tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Under
the center.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nearly central; not quite central.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subchanter</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*chant"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>An underchanter; a precentor's deputy in a cathedral; a
succentor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcircular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cir"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly
circular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subclass</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"class`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the natural
groups, more important than an order, into which some classes are
divided; <as>as, the angiospermous <ex>subclass</ex> of
exogens</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subclavian</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cla"vi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>clavis</ets> a key. See
<er>Clavicle</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the
clavicle, or collar bone; <as>as, the <ex>subclavian</ex>
arteries</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcolumnar</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`co*lum"nar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>Having an imperfect or interrupted columnar structure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcommittee</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`com*mit"tee</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An under
committee; a part or division of a committee.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Yet by their sequestrators and <qex>subcommittees</qex> abroad . .
. those orders were commonly disobeyed.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcompressed</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`com*pressed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not fully
compressed; partially or somewhat compressed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subconcave</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con"cave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slightly
concave.</def> <rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subconformable</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`con*form"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partially
conformable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subconical</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slightly
conical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subconjunctival</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con`junc*ti"val</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the conjunctiva.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subconscious</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con"scious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Occurring without the possibility or the fact of an attendant
consciousness; -- said of states of the soul.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Partially conscious; feebly conscious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subconsciousness</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con"scious*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state
or quality of being subconscious; a state of mind in which perception
and other mental processes occur without distinct
consciousness.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subconstellation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con`stel*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A subordinate constellation.</def> <rj><au>Sir
T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcontract</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con"tract</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A contract
under, or subordinate to, a previous contract.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcontracted</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`con*tract"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Contracted after a former contract.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Betrothed for the second time.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcontractor</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`con*tract"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who takes
a portion of a contract, as for work, from the principal
contractor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcontrary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con"tra*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Contrary in an inferior degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>Having, or being in, a contrary
order; -- said of a section of an oblique cone having a circular base
made by a plane not parallel to the base, but so inclined to the axis
that the section is a circle; applied also to two similar triangles when
so placed as to have a common angle at the vertex, the opposite sides
not being parallel.</def> <rj><au>Brande & C.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>Denoting the relation of
opposition between the <xex>particular affirmative</xex> and
<xex>particular negative</xex>. Of these both may be true and only one
can be false.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcontrary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*con"tra*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Subcontraries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>A
subcontrary proposition; a proposition inferior or contrary in a lower
degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcoracoid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cor"a*coid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the coracoid process of the scapula; <as>as, the
<ex>subcoracoid</ex> dislocation of the humerus</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcordate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cor"date</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat cordate;
somewhat like a heart in shape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcorneous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cor"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Situated under a horny part or layer.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Partially horny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcostal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cos"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat. &
Zool.)</fld> <def>Situated below the costas, or ribs; <as>as, the
<ex>subcostal</ex> muscles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The <xex>subcostal</xex> muscles are distinct from, and
within, the <xex>intercostal</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcostal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cos"tal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>A subcostal muscle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the principal nervures of
the wings of an insect. It is situated next beneath or behind the
costal. See <er>Nervure</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcranial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*cra"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the cranium;
facial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcrustaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`crus*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Occurring
beneath a crust or scab; <as>as, a <ex>subcrustaceous</ex>
cicatrization</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcrystalline</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*crys"tal*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Imperfectly
crystallized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcultrated</ent><br/
<ent>Subcultrate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*cul"trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*cul"tra*ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a
form resembling that of a colter, or straight on one side and curved on
the other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcutaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`cu*ta"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Situated
under the skin; hypodermic.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sub`cu*ta"ne*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subcutaneous operation</b></col> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>,
<cd>an operation performed without opening that part of the skin
opposite to, or over, the internal section.</cd></cs> <-- subcutaneous
injection, (Med) an injection method in which the injected liquid is
delivered directly under the skin, as contrasted with intramuscular or
intravenous injection. Also called subdermal injection. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcuticular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`cu*tic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the cuticle, or scarfskin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subcylindric</ent><br/
<ent>Subcylindrical</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub`cy*lin"dric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sub`cy*lin"dric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Imperfectly cylindrical; approximately cylindrical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdeacon</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*dea"con</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + <ets>deacon</ets>: cf. L.
<ets>subdiaconus</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>One belonging to
an order in the Roman Catholic Church, next interior to the order of
deacons; also, a member of a minor order in the Greek Church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdeaconship</ent><br/
<ent>Subdeaconry</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*dea"con*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*dea"con*ship</hw>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The order or office of
subdeacon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdean</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"dean`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + <ets>dean</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sousdoyen</ets>.]</ety>
<def>An under dean; the deputy or substitute of a dean.</def>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdeanery</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*dean"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Office or rank
of subdean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdecanal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*dec"a*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a subdean or subdeanery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdecuple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*dec"u*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing one
part of ten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdelegate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*del"e*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A subordinate
delegate, or one with inferior powers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdelegate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*del"e*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To appoint
to act as subdelegate, or as a subordinate; to depete.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdented</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*dent"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Indented
beneath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdepartment</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`de*part"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A subordinate
department; a bureau. See the Note under <er>Bureau</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1433 --></p>

<p><ent>Subdeposit</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`de*pos"it</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is
deposited beneath something else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subderisorious</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`der*i*so"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>derisorius</ets>. See
<er>Derisory</er>.]</ety> <def>Ridiculing with moderation.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subderivative</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`de*riv"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A word
derived from a derivative, and not directly from the root; <as>as,
<ldquo/friendliness<rdquo/ is a <ex>subderivative</ex>, being derived
from <ldquo/friendly<rdquo/, which is in turn a derivative from
<ldquo/friend.<rdquo/</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdiaconate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*ac"o*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a subdeacon, or to the office or rank of a
subdeacon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdiaconate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*ac"o*nate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or rank of a
subdeacon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*di"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subdialis</ets> in the open air.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
the open air; being under the open sky.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>N. Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdialect</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*di"a*lect</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A subordinate
dialect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdichotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*chot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
subordinate, or inferior, division into parts; a subdivision.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many <qex>subdichatomies</qex> of petty schisms.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdilated</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*lat"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partially
dilated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdititious</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*ti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subdititius</ets>, <ets>subditicius</ets>, fr. <ets>subdere</ets>
to substitute.]</ety> <def>Put secretly in the place of something else;
foisted in.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdiversify</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*ver"si*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
diversify aggain what is already diversified.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdivide</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*vide"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subdivided</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subdividing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>subdividere</ets>, <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>dividere</ets> to
divide. See <er>Divide</er>.]</ety> <def>To divide the parts of
(anything) into more parts; to part into smaller divisions; to divide
again, as what has already been divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The progenies of Cham and Japhet swarmed into colonies, and those
colonies were <qex>subdivided</qex> into many others.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdivide</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*vide"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be, or to become,
subdivided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdivine</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*vine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partaking of
divinity; divine in a partial or lower degree.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdivisible</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*vis"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Susceptible
of subdivision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdivision</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`di*vi"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subdivisio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>subdivision</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of subdividing, or separating a part into smaller
parts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A part of a thing made by subdividing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the decimal table, the <qex>subdivision</qex> of the cubit, as
span, palm, and digit, are deduced from the shorter cubit.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdolous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"do*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subdolus</ets>, <ets>sub + dolus</ets> deceit.]</ety> <def>Sly;
crafty; cunning; artful.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdominant</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*dom"i*nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>The fourth tone above, or fifth below, the tonic; -- so called as
being under the dominant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subduable</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*du"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Able to be
subdued.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdual</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*du"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act of
subduing.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Warburton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subduct</ent><br/
<ent>Subduce</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*duce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*duct"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>subducere</ets>,
<ets>subductum</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>ducere</ets> to lead,
to draw. See <er>Duke</er>, and cf. <er>Subdue</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To withdraw; to take away.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To subtract by arithmetical operation; to
deduct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If, out of that infinite multitude of antecedent generations, we
should <qex>subduce</qex> ten.</q> <rj><qau>Sir M. Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subduction</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*duc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subductio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of subducting or
taking away.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Arithmetical subtraction.</def> <rj><au>Sir M.
Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdue</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*due"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Subdued</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subduing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>soduen</ets>, OF.
<ets>sosduire</ets> to seduce, L. <ets>subtus</ets> below (fr.
<ets>sub</ets> under) + <ets>ducere</ets> to lead. See <er>Duke</er>,
and cf. <er>Subduct</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bring under; to
conquer by force or the exertion of superior power, and bring into
permanent subjection; to reduce under dominion; to vanquish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>subdue</qex> all thine enemies.</q> <rj><qau>1 Chron.
xvii. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To overpower so as to disable from further
resistance; to crush.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing could have <qex>subdued</qex> nature<br/
To such a lowness, but his unkind daughters.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If aught . . . were worthy to <qex>subdue</qex><br/
The soul of man.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To destroy the force of; to overcome; <as>as,
medicines <ex>subdue</ex> a fever</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To render submissive; to bring under command; to
reduce to mildness or obedience; to tame; <as>as, to <ex>subdue</ex> a
stubborn child; to <ex>subdue</ex> the temper or
passions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To overcome, as by persuasion or other mild means;
<as>as, to <ex>subdue</ex> opposition by argument or
entreaties</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To reduce to tenderness; to melt; to soften; <as>as,
to <ex>subdue</ex> ferocity by tears</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To make mellow; to break, as land; also, to destroy,
as weeds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To reduce the intensity or degree of; to tone down;
to soften; <as>as, to <ex>subdue</ex> the brilliancy of
colors</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To conquer; overpower; overcome; surmount;
vanquish. See <er>Conquer</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdued</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*dued"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Conquered; overpowered; crushed; submissive; mild.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not glaring in color; soft in tone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subduement</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*due"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Subdual.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subduer</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*du"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, subdues; a conqueror.</def> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdulcid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*dul"cid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub</ets> + L. <ets>dulcis</ets> sweet.]</ety> <def>Somewhat sweet;
sweetish.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subduple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"du*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>Indicating one part of two; in the ratio of one to two.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subduple ratio</b></col>, <cd>the ratio of 1 to 2: thus,
3:6 is a <xex>subduple ratio</xex>, as 6:3 is a <xex>duple
ratio</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subduplicate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*du"pli*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>Expressed by the square root; -- said of ratios.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subduplicate ratio</b></col>, <cd>the ratio of the square
roots, or the square root of a ratio; <as>thus, the <ex>subduplicate
ratio</ex> of <it>a</it> to <it>b</it> is <root/<it>a</it> to
<root/<it>b</it>, or
<root/<fract><it>a</it>/<it>b</it></fract></as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subdural</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*du"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the dura mater, or between the dura mater and the
arachnoid membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subeditor</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ed"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An assistant
editor, as of a periodical or journal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subelongate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`e*lon"gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not fully
elongated; somewhat elongated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subendocardial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*en`do*car"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the endocardium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subendymal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*en"dy*mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub
+ endyma</ets>.]</ety> <def>Situated under the endyma.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subepidermal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ep`i*der"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Situated
immediately below the epidermis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subepiglottic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ep`i*glot"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the epiglottis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subepithelial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ep`i*the"li*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the epithelium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subequal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*e"qual</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly
equal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suberate</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ber*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sub<eacute/rate</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of
suberic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subereous</ent><br/
<hw>Su*be"re*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subereus</ets> of the cork tree.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
cork; of the nature of cork; suberose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suberic</ent><br/
<hw>Su*ber"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>suber</ets>
the cork tree: cf. F. <ets>sub<eacute/reque</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to cork; specifically,
designating an acid, <chform>C6H12.(CO2H)2</chform>, homologous with
oxalic acid, and obtained from cork and certain fatty oils, as a white
crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suberin</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ber*in</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>suber</ets>
the cork tree: cf. F. <ets>sub<eacute/rine</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A material found in the cell walls of cork. It is
a modification of lignin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suberite</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ber*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suber</ets> the cork tree.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
sponge of the genus <gen>Suberites</gen> and allied genera. These
sponges have a fine and compact texture, and contain minute siliceous
spicules.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suberization</ent><br/
<hw>Su`ber*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Conversion of the cell walls into cork tissue by development of
suberin; -- commonly taking place in exposed tissues, as when a callus
forms over a wound. Suberized cell walls are impervious to
water.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suberize</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ber*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>-ized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>-izing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>suber</ets> cork.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>To effect
suberization of.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suberone</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ber*one</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The hypothetical ketone of suberic acid.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A colorless liquid, analogous suberone proper, having
a pleasant peppermint odor. It is obtained by the distillation of
calcium suberate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suberous</ent><br/
<ent>Suberose</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su"ber*ose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Su"ber*ous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>suber</ets> the cork
tree: cf. F. <ets>sub<eacute/reux</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having a corky texture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subesophageal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*e`so*phag"e*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Situated beneath the esophagus.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>suboesophageal</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subesophageal ganglion</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a large special ganglion situated beneath the esophagus of
arthropods, annelids, and some other invertebrates.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subfamily</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*fam"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>One of the subdivisions, of more importance than genus, into which
certain families are divided.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subfibrous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*fi"brous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
fibrous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>subfusc</ent><br/
<hw>sub*fusc"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subfuscus</ets>, <ets>suffuscus</ets>. See <er>Sub-</er>, and
<er>Fuscous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Same as
<er>subfuscous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Drab; dingy; dull.</def> <mark>[Chiefty
Brit.]</mark> <br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subfuscous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*fus"cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subfuscus</ets>, <ets>suffuscus</ets>. See <er>Sub-</er>, and
<er>Fuscous</er>.]</ety> <def>Duskish; dusky; moderately dark; brownish;
tawny.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> subfusc{1}.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subfusk</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*fusk"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Subfuscous.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subgelatinous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ge*lat"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Imperfectly
or partially gelatinous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subgeneric</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ge*ner"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a subgenus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subgenus</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ge"nus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Subgenera</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
subdivision of a genus, comprising one or more species which differ from
other species of the genus in some important character or characters;
<as>as, the azaleas now constitute a <ex>subgenus</ex> of
Rhododendron</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subglacial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*gla"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining or
belonging to the under side of a glacier; being beneath a glacier;
<as>as, <ex>subglacial</ex> streams</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subglobose</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`glo*bose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not quite
globose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subglobular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*glob"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly
globular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subglossal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*glos"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the tongue; sublingual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subglottic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*glot"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated below the glottis; -- applied to that part of the cavity
of the larynx below the true vocal cords.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subglumaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`glu*ma"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
glumaceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subgovernor</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*gov"ern*or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A subordinate
or assistant governor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subgranular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*gran"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
granular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subgroup</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"group`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>A subdivision of a group, as of animals.</def>
<rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subhastation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`has*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subhastatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>A public sale or auction.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subhepatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`he*pat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the liver; -- applied to
the interlobular branches of the portal vein.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subhornblendic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`horn*blend"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Containing hornblende in a scattered state; of or relating to rocks
containing disseminated hornblende.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subhumerate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*hu"mer*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sub-</er>, <er>Humerus</er>.]</ety> <def>To place the shoulders
under; to bear.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing surer ties a friend than freely to <qex>subhumerate</qex>
the burden which was his.</q> <rj><qau>Feltham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subhyaloid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*hy"a*loid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the hyaliod membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subhyoidean</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`hy*oid"e*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat. &
Med.)</fld> <def>Situated or performed beneath the hyoid bone; <as>as,
<ex>subhyoidean</ex> laryngotomy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subimago</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sub`i*ma"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sub-</er>, and <er>Imago</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
stage in the development of certain insects, such as the May flies,
intermediate between the pupa and imago. In this stage, the insect is
able to fly, but subsequently sheds a skin before becoming mature.
Called also <altname>pseudimago</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subincusation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*in`cu*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub</ets> + L. <ets>incusatio</ets> accusation, fr.
<ets>incusare</ets> to accuse.]</ety> <def>A slight charge or
accusation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subindex</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*in"dex</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Subindices</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>A
number or mark placed opposite the lower part of a letter or symbol to
distinguish the symbol; <as>thus, <it>a</it><subs>0</subs>,
<it>b</it><subs>1</subs>, <it>c</it><subs>2</subs>,
<it>x</it><subs>n</subs>, have 0, 1, 2, and <it>n</it> as
<ex>subindices</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subindicate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*in"di*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub + indicate</ets>: cf. L. <ets>subindicare</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
indicate by signs or hints; to indicate imperfectly.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subindication</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*in`di*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
indicating by signs; a slight indication.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/The <xex>subindication</xex> and shadowing of heavenly
things.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subindividual</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*in`di*vid"u*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A division
of that which is individual.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An individual can not branch itself into
<qex>subindividuals</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subinduce</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`in*duce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To insinuate;
to offer indirectly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir E.
Dering.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subinfer</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`in*fer"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <def>To infer
from an inference already made.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subinfeudation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*in`feu*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The granting of lands by inferior lords to their
dependents, to be held by themselves by feudal tenure.</def>
<au>Craig.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Subordinate tenancy;
undertenancy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The widow is immediate tenant to the heir, by a kind of
<qex>subinfeudation</qex>, or undertenancy.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subingression</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`in*gres"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Secret
entrance.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subintestinal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`in*tes"ti*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the
intestine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subinvolution</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*in`vo*lu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Partial or
incomplete involution; <as>as, <ex>subinvolution</ex> of the
uterus</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subitaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`i*ta"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subitaneus</ets>. See <er>Sudden</er>.]</ety> <def>Sudden;
hasty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Bullokar.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Sub`i*ta"ne*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subitany</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"i*ta*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Subitaneous;
sudden; hasty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hales.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subito</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"bi*to</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[It. &
L.]</ety> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>In haste; quickly; rapidly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjacent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ja"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subjacens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>subjacere</ets> to lie under;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>jacere</ets> to lie.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Lying under or below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Being in a lower situation, though not directly
beneath; <as>as, hills and <ex>subjacent</ex> valleys</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subject</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ject"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>suget</ets>, OF. <ets>souzget</ets>, <ets>sougit</ets> (in which
the first part is L. <ets>subtus</ets> below, fr. <ets>sub</ets> under),
<ets>subgiet</ets>, <ets>subject</ets>, F. <ets>sujet</ets>, from L.
<ets>subjectus</ets> lying under, subjected, p. p. of
<ets>subjicere</ets>, <ets>subicere</ets>, to throw, lay, place, or
bring under; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>jacere</ets> to throw. See
<er>Jet</er> a shooting forth.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Placed or
situated under; lying below, or in a lower situation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Placed under the power of another; specifically
<fld>(International Law)</fld>, owing allegiance to a particular
sovereign or state; <as>as, Jamaica is <ex>subject</ex> to Great
Britain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Esau was never <qex>subject</qex> to Jacob.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Exposed; liable; prone; disposed; <as>as, a country
<ex>subject</ex> to extreme heat; men <ex>subject</ex> to
temptation.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All human things are <qex>subject</qex> to decay.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Obedient; submissive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Put them in mind to be <qex>subject</qex> to principalities.</q>
<rj><qau>Titus iii. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Liable; subordinate; inferior; obnoxious;
exposed. See <er>Liable</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subject</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ject"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L. <ets>subjectus</ets>,
through an old form of F. <ets>sujet</ets>. See <er>Subject</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is placed under the
authority, dominion, control, or influence of something else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically: One who is under the authority of a
ruler and is governed by his laws; one who owes allegiance to a
sovereign or a sovereign state; <as>as, a <ex>subject</ex> of Queen
Victoria; a British <ex>subject</ex>; a <ex>subject</ex> of the United
States.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Was never <qex>subject</qex> longed to be a king,<br/
As I do long and wish to be a <qex>subject</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>subject</qex> must obey his prince, because God commands
it, human laws require it.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In international law, the term <xex>subject</xex> is
convertible with <xex>citizen</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is subjected, or submitted to, any
physical operation or process; specifically <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, a dead
body used for the purpose of dissection.</def> <-- also, an animal or
person which is studied in a scientific experiment. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which is brought under thought or examination;
that which is taken up for discussion, or concerning which anything is
said or done.</def> <ldquo/This <xex>subject</xex> for heroic
song.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Make choice of a <qex>subject</qex>, beautiful and noble, which .
. . shall afford an ample field of matter wherein to expatiate.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The unhappy <qex>subject</qex> of these quarrels.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The person who is treated of; the hero of a piece;
the chief character.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Writers of particular lives . . . are apt to be prejudiced in
favor of their <qex>subject</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Middleton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Logic & Gram.)</fld> <def>That of which anything is
affirmed or predicated; the theme of a proposition or discourse; that
which is spoken of; <as>as, the nominative case is the <ex>subject</ex>
of the verb</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>subject</qex> of a proposition is that concerning which
anything is affirmed or denied.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>That in which any quality, attribute, or relation,
whether spiritual or material, inheres, or to which any of these
appertain; substance; substratum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That which manifests its qualities -- in other words, that in
which the appearing causes inhere, that to which they belong -- is
called their <qex>subject</qex> or substance, or substratum.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>Hence, that substance or being which is conscious of
its own operations; the mind; the thinking agent or principal; the ego.
Cf. <er>Object</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The philosophers of mind have, in a manner, usurped and
appropriated this expression to themselves. Accordingly, in their hands,
the phrases <qex>conscious</qex> or <qex>thinking subject</qex>, and
<qex>subject</qex>, mean precisely the same thing.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The principal theme, or leading
thought or phrase, on which a composition or a movement is
based.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The earliest known form of <qex>subject</qex> is the
ecclesiastical <qex>cantus firmus</qex>, or plain song.</q>
<rj><qau>Rockstro.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Fine Arts)</fld> <def>The incident, scene, figure,
group, etc., which it is the aim of the artist to represent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subject</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ject"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subjected</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subjecting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bring
under control, power, or dominion; to make subject; to subordinate; to
subdue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Firmness of mind that <qex>subjects</qex> every gratification of
sense to the rule of right reason.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Middleton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In one short view <qex>subjected</qex> to our eye,<br/
Gods, emperors, heroes, sages, beauties, lie.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is the most <qex>subjected</qex>, the most <?/nslaved, who is
so in his understanding.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To expose; to make obnoxious or liable; <as>as,
credulity <ex>subjects</ex> a person to impositions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To submit; to make accountable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>God is not bound to <qex>subject</qex> his ways of operation to
the scrutiny of our thoughts.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make subservient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Subjected</qex> to his service angel wings.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To cause to undergo; <as>as, to <ex>subject</ex> a
substance to a white heat; to <ex>subject</ex> a person to a rigid
test</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1434 --></p>

<p><ent>Subjected</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ject"ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Subjacent.</def> <ldquo/Led them direct . . . to the
<xex>subjected</xex> plain.<rdquo/ <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Reduced to subjection; brought under the dominion of
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Exposed; liable; subject; obnoxious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjection</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*jec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subjectio</ets>: cf. OF. <ets>subjection</ets>, F.
<ets>subj<eacute/tion</ets>. See <er>Subject</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of subjecting, or of bringing under the
dominion of another; the act of subduing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The conquest of the kingdom, and <qex>subjection</qex> of the
rebels.</q> <rj><qau>Sir M. Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being subject, or under the power,
control, and government of another; a state of obedience or
submissiveness; <as>as, the safety of life, liberty, and property
depends on our <ex>subjection</ex> to the laws</as>.</def> <ldquo/To be
bound under <xex>subjection</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Likewise, ye wives, be in <qex>subjection</qex> to your own
husbands.</q> <rj><qau>1 Peter iii. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Because the <qex>subjection</qex> of the body to the will is by
natural necessity, the <qex>subjection</qex> of the will unto God
voluntary, we stand in need of direction after what sort our wills and
desires may be rightly conformed to His.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjectist</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ject*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld>
<def>One skilled in subjective philosophy; a subjectivist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjective</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*jec"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subjectivus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>subjectif</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of or pertaining to a subject.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, pertaining to, or derived from, one's
own consciousness, in distinction from external observation; ralating to
the mind, or intellectual world, in distinction from the outward or
material excessively occupied with, or brooding over, one's own internal
states.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In the philosophy of the mind, <xex>subjective</xex>
denotes what is to be referred to the thinking subject, the
<xex>ego</xex>; <xex>objective</xex>, what belongs to the object of
thought, the <xex>non-ego</xex>. See <er>Objective</er>, <pos>a.</pos>,
2.</note> <rj><au>Sir W. Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Lit. & Art)</fld> <def>Modified by, or making
prominent, the individuality of a writer or an artist; <as>as, a
<ex>subjective</ex> drama or painting; a <ex>subjective</ex>
writer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Objective</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subjective sensation</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>,
<cd>one of the sensations occurring when stimuli due to internal causes
excite the nervous apparatus of the sense organs, as when a person
imagines he sees figures which have no objective reality.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sub*jec"tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sub*jec"tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjectivism</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*jec"tiv*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>Any philosophical doctrine which refers all
knowledge to, and founds it upon, any subjective states;
egoism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjectivist</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*jec"tiv*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>One
who holds to subjectivism; an egoist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjectivity</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`jec*tiv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality
or state of being subjective; character of the subject.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjectless</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ject*less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no
subject.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subject-matter</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ject-mat`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The matter or
thought presented for consideration in some statement or discussion;
that which is made the object of thought or study.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As to the <qex>subject-matter</qex>, words are always to be
understood as having a regard thereto.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As science makes progress in any <qex>subject-matter</qex>, poetry
recedes from it.</q> <rj><qau>J. H. Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjectness</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ject*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality of being
subject.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjicible</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*jic"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
subjected.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjoin</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*join"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subjoined</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subjoining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>subjoindre</ets>, L. <ets>subjungere</ets>. See <er>Sub-</er>, and
<er>Join</er>, and cf. <er>Subjective</er>.]</ety> <def>To add after
something else has been said or written; to ANNEX; <as>as, to
<ex>subjoin</ex> an argument or reason</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To add; annex; join; unite.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjoinder</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*join"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An additional
remark.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sub judice</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sub ju"di*ce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>Before the
judge, or court; not yet decided; under judicial
consideration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjugate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ju*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subjugated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subjugating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>subjugatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>subjugare</ets> to subjugate;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>jugum</ets> a yoke. See
<er>Yoke</er>.]</ety> <def>To subdue, and bring under the yoke of power
or dominion; to conquer by force, and compel to submit to the government
or absolute control of another; to vanquish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>subjugated</qex> a king, and called him his
<ldquo/vassal.<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Baker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To conquer; subdue; overcome. See
<er>Conquer</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjugation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ju*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>subjugation</ets>, LL. <ets>subjugatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act
of subjugating, or the state of being subjugated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjugator</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ju*ga`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who subjugates; a conqueror.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjunction</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*junc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Subjunctive</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act of subjoining, or
state of being subjoined.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Something subjoined; <as>as, a <ex>subjunction</ex>
to a sentence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjunctive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*junc"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subjunctivus</ets>, fr. <ets>subjungere</ets>,
<ets>subjunctum</ets>, to subjoin: cf. F. <ets>subjonctif</ets>. See
<er>Subjoin</er>.]</ety> <def>Subjoined or added to something before
said or written.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subjunctive mood</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>that
form of a verb which express the action or state not as a fact, but only
as a conception of the mind still contingent and dependent. It is
commonly subjoined, or added as subordinate, to some other verb, and in
English is often connected with it by <xex>if</xex>, <xex>that</xex>,
<xex>though</xex>, <xex>lest</xex>, <xex>unless</xex>,
<xex>except</xex>, <xex>until</xex>, etc., as in the following sentence:
<ldquo/<xex>If there were</xex> no honey, they [bees] <xex>would
have</xex> no object in visiting the flower.<rdquo/ <au>Lubbock.</au> In
some languages, as in Latin and Greek, the subjunctive is often
independent of any other verb, being used in wishes, commands,
exhortations, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subjunctive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*junc"tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The
subjunctive mood; also, a verb in the subjunctive mood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subkingdom</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*king"dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the
several primary divisions of either the animal, or vegetable kingdom,
as, in Zoology, the Vertebrata, Tunicata, Mollusca, Articulata,
Molluscoidea, Echinodermata, Coelentera, and the Protozoa; in botany,
the Phanerogamia, and the Cryptogamia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublapsarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`lap*sa"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub + lapse</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sublapsarien</ets>,
<ets>sublapsarie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Infralapsarian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublapsarianism</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`lap*sa"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Infralapsarianism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublapsary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lap"sa*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sublapsarian.</def> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>sublatus</ets>, used as p. p. of <ets>tollere</ets> to take away.
See <er>Tolerate</er>.]</ety> <def>To take or carry away; to
remove.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>E. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sublatio</ets>, fr. <ets>sublatus</ets>, used as p. p. of
<ets>tollere</ets> to take away.]</ety> <def>The act of taking or
carrying away; removal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublative</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"la*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having power, or
tending, to take away.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Harris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublease</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"lease`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A
lease by a tenant or lessee to another person; an underlease.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublessee</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`les*see"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A holder of a
sublease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublet</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*let"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sublet</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subletting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To underlet; to lease, as when
a lessee leases to another person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublevation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`le*va"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sublevare</ets> to lift up; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>levare</ets> to lift, raise: cf. L. <ets>sublevatio</ets> an
allevation.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of raising on high;
elevation.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An uprising; an insurrection.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W. Temple.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublibrarian</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`li*bra"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An under or
assistant librarian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublieutenant</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`lieu*ten"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub</ets> + <ets>lieutenant</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sous-lieutenant</ets>.]</ety> <def>An inferior or second
lieutenant; in the British service, a commissioned officer of the lowest
rank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subligation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`li*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subligatio</ets>, from <ets>subligare</ets> to bind below;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>ligare</ets> to bind.]</ety> <def>The act of
binding underneath.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimable</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lim"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sublimable</ets>. See <er>Sublime</er>., <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Capable of being sublimed or sublimated.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sub*lim"a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"li*mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sublimated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sublimating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>sublimatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sublimare</ets> to raise, elevate,
fr. <ets>sublimis</ets> high: cf. F. <ets>sublimer</ets>. See
<er>Sublime</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Surlime</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bring by heat into the state of
vapor, which, on cooling, returns again to the solid state; <as>as, to
<ex>sublimate</ex> sulphur or camphor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To refine and exalt; to heighten; to
elevate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The precepts of Christianity are . . . so apt to cleanse and
<qex>sublimate</qex> the more gross and corrupt.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H.
More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Psychology)</fld> <def>To redirect the energy (of
sexual or other biological drives) into a more socially acceptable or
constructive form.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"li*mate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sublimatum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A product obtained
by sublimation; hence, also, a purified product so obtained.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Corrosive sublimate</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<cd>mercuric chloride. See <cref>Corrosive sublimate</cref> under
<er>Corrosive</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"li*mate</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sublimatus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Brought into a state of vapor by
heat, and again condensed as a solid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimated</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"li*ma`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Refined by, or
as by, sublimation; exalted; purified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Words] whose weight best suits a <qex>sublimated</qex>
strain.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"li*ma`tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>sublimatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sublimation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The act or process of subliming, or the state or
result of being sublimed.</def> <-- the process of vaporizing a solid
and recondensing it into a solid, without it having first passed into
the liquid state. Certain solids, such as camphor, have a suffiently
high vapor pressure in the solid phase to make this a practical method
for purification. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of heightening or improving; exaltation;
elevation; purification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is sublimed; the product of a purifying
process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Religion is the perfection, refinement, and <qex>sublimation</qex>
of morality.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"li*ma*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Used for
sublimation; <as>as, <ex>sublimatory</ex> vessels</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"li*ma*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A vessel used for
sublimation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Vials, crosslets, and <qex>sublimatories</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublime</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lime"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Sublimer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Sublimest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>sublimis</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + (perhaps) a word akin to
<ets>limen</ets> lintel, sill, thus meaning, up to the lintel: cf. F.
<ets>sublime</ets>. Cf. <er>Eliminate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Lifted up; high in place; exalted aloft; uplifted; lofty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sublime</qex> on these a tower of steel is reared.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Distinguished by lofty or noble traits; eminent; --
said of persons.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>sublime</xex> Julian
leader.<rdquo/ <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Awakening or expressing the emotion of awe,
adoration, veneration, heroic resolve, etc.; dignified; grand; solemn;
stately; -- said of an impressive object in nature, of an action, of a
discourse, of a work of art, of a spectacle, etc.; <as>as,
<ex>sublime</ex> scenery; a <ex>sublime</ex> deed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Easy in words thy style, in sense <qex>sublime</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Know how <qex>sublime</qex> a thing it is<br/
To suffer and be strong.</q> <rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Elevated by joy; elate.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their hearts were jocund and <qex>sublime</qex>,<br/
Drunk with idolatry, drunk with wine.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Lofty of mien; haughty; proud.</def>
<mark>[Poetic]</mark> <ldquo/Countenance <xex>sublime</xex> and
insolent.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His fair, large front and eye <qex>sublime</qex> declared<br/
Absolute rule.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Exalted; lofty; noble; majestic. See
<er>Grand</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublime</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lime"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which is sublime; -- with
the definite article</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A grand or lofty style
in speaking or writing; a style that expresses lofty
conceptions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sublime</qex> rises from the nobleness of thoughts, the
magnificence of words, or the harmonious and lively turn of the
phrase.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>That which is grand in nature or art, as
distinguished from the merely beautiful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublime</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lime"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sublimed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subliming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. L. <ets>sublimare</ets>,
F. <ets>sublimer</ets> to subject to sublimation. See <er>Sublime</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Sublimate</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To raise on high.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A soul <qex>sublimed</qex> by an idea above the region of vanity
and conceit.</q> <rj><qau>E. P. Whipple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To subject to the process of
sublimation; to heat, volatilize, and condense in crystals or powder; to
distill off, and condense in solid form; hence, also, to
purify.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To exalt; to heighten; to improve; to
purify.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sun . . . <br/
Which not alone the southern wit <qex>sublimes</qex>,<br/
But ripens spirits in cold, northern climes.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To dignify; to ennoble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An ordinary gift can not <qex>sublime</qex> a person to a
supernatural employment.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublime</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lime"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>To pass off in vapor, with immediate condensation; specifically, to
evaporate or volatilize from the solid state without apparent melting;
-- said of those substances, like arsenic, benzoic acid, etc., which do
not exhibit a liquid form on heating, except under increased
pressure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimed</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*limed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Having been subjected to the process of sublimation; hence, also,
purified.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sublimed</xex> mercurie.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimely</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lime"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sublime
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimeness</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lime"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sublime; sublimity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimification</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lim`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sublimis</ets> sublime + <ets>-ficare</ets> to make. See
<er>-ry</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of making sublime, or state of being
made sublime.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subliminal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lim"i*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>limen</ets> threshold.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Philos.)</fld> <def>Existing in the mind, but below the surface or
threshold of consciousness; that is, existing as feeling rather than as
clear ideas.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>having or using an intensity of sensory stimulus
insufficient to be perceived consciously, but having an effect on
unconscious mental processes; <as>as, <ex>subliminal</ex>
advertising</as>.</def> <note>The existence of such an effect is still
not universally accepted.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublimity</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lim"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sublimities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>sublimitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sublimit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being sublime (in any sense of
the adjective).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is sublime; <as>as, the
<ex>sublimities</ex> of nature</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Grandeur; magnificence.</syn> --
<usage><er>Sublimity</er>, <er>Grandeur</er>. The mental state indicated
by these two words is the same, namely, a mingled emotion of
astonishment and awe. In speaking of the quality which produces this
emotion, we call it <xex>grandeur</xex> when it springs from what is
vast in space, power, etc.; we call it <xex>sublimity</xex> when it
springs from what is elevated far above the ordinary incidents of
humanity. An immense plain is <xex>grand</xex>. The heavens are not only
<xex>grand</xex>, but <xex>sublime</xex> (as the predominating emotion),
from their immense height. Exalted intellect, and especially exalted
virtue under severe trials, give us the sense of moral
<xex>sublimity</xex>, as in the case of our Savior in his prayer for his
murderers. We do not speak of Satan, when standing by the fiery gulf,
with his <ldquo/unconquerable will and study of revenge,<rdquo/ as a
<xex>sublime</xex> object; but there is a melancholy <xex>grandeur</xex>
thrown around him, as of an <ldquo/archangel ruined.<rdquo/</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublineation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lin`e*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mark of a
line or lines under a word in a sentence, or under another line;
underlining.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublingua</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sub*lin"gua</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sublinguae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A process or fold below the tongue in some
animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublingual</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lin"gual</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub +
lingual</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sublingual</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Situated under the tongue; <as>as, the
<ex>sublingual</ex> gland</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining
to the sublingual gland; <as>as, <ex>sublingual</ex>
salvia</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublition</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*li"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sublinere</ets>, <ets>sublitum</ets>, to smear, to lay on as a
ground color.]</ety> <fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>The act or process of
laying the ground in a painting.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublittoral</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lit"to*ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Under the
shore.</def> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublobular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lob"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or at the bases of, the lobules of the
liver.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublumbar</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*lum"bar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the lumbar region of the
vertebral column.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublunary</ent><br/
<ent>Sublunar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*lu"nar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub"lu*na*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub + lunar</ets>,
or <ets>lunary</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sublunaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Situated
beneath the moon; hence, of or pertaining to this world; terrestrial;
earthly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All things <qex>sublunary</qex> are subject to change.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All <qex>sublunary</qex> comforts imitate the changeableness, as
well as feel the influence, of the planet they are under.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sublunary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"lu*na*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any worldly thing.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subluxation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`lux*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub
+ luxation</ets>: cf. F. <ets>subluxation</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>An incomplete or partial dislocation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submammary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mam"ma*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Situated under
the mammae; <as>as, <ex>submammary</ex> inflammation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submarine</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ma*rine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being, acting, or
growing, under water in the sea; <as>as, <ex>submarine</ex> navigators;
<ex>submarine</ex> plants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Submarine armor</b></col>, <cd>a waterproof dress of
strong material, having a helmet into which air for breathing is pumped
through a tube leading from above the surface to enable a diver to
remain under water.</cd> -- <col><b>Submarine cable</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Telegraph cable</cref>, under <er>Telegraph</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Submarine mine</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Torpedo</er>, 2
<sd>(a)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submarine</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ma*rine"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A submarine plant or
animal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submarine</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ma*rine"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
submarine boat; a ship that can travel under the surface of the water.
Most such ships are ships of war, as part of a navy, but submarines are
also used for oceanic research. Also called <altname>sub</altname> and
(from the German <ets>U-Boot</ets>) <altname>U-boat</altname>.</def>
<specif>esp.,</specif> <fld>Nav.</fld>, <def>a submarine torpedo boat;
-- called specif. <altname>submergible submarine</altname> when capable
of operating at various depths and of traveling considerable distances
under water, and <altname>submersible submarine</altname> when capable
of being only partly submerged, i.e., so that the conning tower, etc.,
is still above water. The latter type and most of the former type are
submerged as desired by regulating the amount of water admitted to the
ballast tanks and sink on an even keel; some of the former type effect
submersion while under way by means of horizontal rudders, in some cases
also with admission of water to the ballast tanks.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stowaway on a seagoing vessel.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A <er>submarine sandwich</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. Nuclear submarine. A submarine powered by a nuclear reactor.
Attack submarine. A submarine designed to attack other ships, including
other submarines.--></p>

<p><ent>submarine sandwich</ent><br/
<hw>sub`ma*rine" sand"wich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A large
sandwich on an elongated roll, usually incompletely cut into two halves,
filed with various cold cuts, meatballs, lettuce, cheese, tomatoes,
olives, etc., and spiced variously, and often having oil or other
dressing applied; called also <altname>hoagie</altname>,
<altname>hero</altname>, <altname>hero sandwich</altname>,
<altname>grinder</altname>, <altname>sub</altname>,
<altname>submarine</altname>, <altname>poor boy</altname>, and
<altname>Italian sandwich</altname>. A single such sandwich may
consitute a substantial meal. Very large variants are sometimes prepared
for social gatherings and cut into pieces for individual
consumption.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submarshal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mar"shal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An under or
deputy marshal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submaxillary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*max"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Situated under the maxilla, or lower jaw;
inframaxillary; <as>as, the <ex>submaxillary</ex> gland</as>.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to submaxillary gland; <as>as,
<ex>submaxillary</ex> salvia</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submedial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*me"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lying under the
middle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submedian</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*me"di*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Next to the median (on either side); <as>as, the <ex>submedian</ex>
teeth of mollusks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submediant</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*me"di*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>The sixth tone of the scale; the under mediant, or third below the
keynote; the superdominant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submental</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the chin; <as>as, the <ex>submental</ex>
artery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submentum</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*men"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Submenta</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See <er>Sub-</er>, and
<er>Mentum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The basal part of the
labium of insects. It bears the mentum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submerge</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*merge"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Submerged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Submerging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>submergere</ets>, <ets>submersum</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>mergere</ets> to plunge: cf. F. <ets>submerger</ets>. See
<er>Merge</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To put under water; to
plunge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover or overflow with water; to inundate; to
flood; to drown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I would thou didst,<br/
So half my Egypt were <qex>submerged</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1435 --></p>

<p><ent>Submerge</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*merge"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To plunge into
water or other fluid; to be buried or covered, as by a fluid; to be
merged; hence, to be completely included.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some say swallows <qex>submerge</qex> in ponds.</q> <rj><qau>Gent.
Mag.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submergence</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mer"gence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>submergens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>The act of submerging, or the
state of being submerged; submersion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submerse</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*merse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Submersed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submersed</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mersed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>submersus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>submergere</ets>. See
<er>Submerge</er>.]</ety> <def>Being or growing under water, as the
leaves of aquatic plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submersion</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mer"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>submersio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>submersion</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of submerging, or putting under water or other fluid, or of
causing to be overflowed; the act of plunging under water, or of
drowning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being put under water or other fluid,
or of being overflowed or drowned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submetallic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`me*tal"lic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Imperfectly
metallic; <as>as, a <ex>submetallic</ex> luster</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subminister</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*min"is*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subministrare</ets>, <ets>subministratum</ets>. See <er>Sub-</er>,
and <er>Ministre</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>To supply; to
afford.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir M. Hale.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subminister</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*min"is*ter</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be subservient; to be
useful.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Our passions . . .
<xex>subminister</xex> to the best and worst purposes.<rdquo/
<rj><au>L'EStrange.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subministrant</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*min"is*trant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subministrans</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Subordinate;
subservient.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subministrate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*min"is*trate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Subminister</er>.]</ety> <def>To supply; to afford; to
subminister.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subministration</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*min`is*tra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subministratio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of subministering.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submiss</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*miss"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>submissus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>submittere</ets> to let down, to
lower. See <er>Submit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Submissive; humble;
obsequious.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <ldquo/Soft Silence and
<xex>submiss</xex> Obedience.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au> <ldquo/Stooping
and <xex>submiss</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>R. L. Stevenson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Gentle; soft; calm; <as>as, <ex>submiss</ex>
voices</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submission</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mis"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>submissio</ets> a letting down, lowering: cf. F.
<ets>soumission</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of submitting;
the act of yielding to power or authority; surrender of the person and
power to the control or government of another; obedience;
compliance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Submission</qex>, dauphin! 't is a mere French word;<br/
We English warrious wot not what it means.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being submissive; acknowledgement of
inferiority or dependence; humble or suppliant behavior; meekness;
resignation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In all <qex>submission</qex> and humility<br/
York doth present himself unto your highness.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No duty in religion is more justly required by God . . . than a
perfect <qex>submission</qex> to his will in all things.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Acknowledgement of a fault; confession of
error.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Be not as extreme in <qex>submission</qex><br/
As in offense.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An agreement by which parties
engage to submit any matter of controversy between them to the decision
of arbitrators.</def> <rj><au>Wharton (Law Dict.).
Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submissive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mis"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Inclined or ready to submit; acknowledging one's inferiority;
yielding; obedient; humble.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not at his feet <qex>submissive</qex> in distress,<br/
Creature so fair his reconcilement seeking.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Showing a readiness to submit; expressing
submission; <as>as, a <ex>submissive</ex> demeanor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a <qex>submissive</qex> step I hasted down.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Obedient; compliant; yielding; obsequious;
subservient; humble; modest; passive.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sub*mis"sive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sub*mis"sive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submissly</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*miss"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a submissive
manner; with a submission.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submissness</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*miss"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Submissiveness.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submit</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mit"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Submitted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Submitting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>submittere</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>mittere</ets> to
send: cf. F. <ets>soumettre</ets>. See <er>Missile</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To let down; to lower.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sometimes the hill <qex>submits</qex> itself a while.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To put or place under.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bristled throat<br/
Of the <qex>submitted</qex> sacrifice with ruthless steel he cut.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To yield, resign, or surrender to power, will, or
authority; -- often with the reflexive pronoun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye ben <qex>submitted</qex> through your free assent.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The angel of the Lord said unto her, Return to thy mistress, and
<qex>submit</qex> thyself under her hands.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xvi.
9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wives, <qex>submit</qex> yourselves unto your own husbands.</q>
<rj><qau>Eph. v. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To leave or commit to the discretion or judgment of
another or others; to refer; <as>as, to <ex>submit</ex> a controversy to
arbitrators; to <ex>submit</ex> a question to the court</as>; -- often
followed by a dependent proposition as the object.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Whether the condition of the clergy be able to bear a heavy
burden, is <qex>submitted</qex> to the house.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We <qex>submit</qex> that a wooden spoon of our day would not be
justified in calling Galileo and Napier blockheads because they never
heard of the differential calculus.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submit</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mit"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To yield one's
person to the power of another; to give up resistance; to
surrender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The revolted provinces presently <qex>submitted</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>C. Middleton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To yield one's opinion to the opinion of authority
of another; to be subject; to acquiesce.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To thy husband's will<br/
Thine shall <qex>submit</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be submissive or resigned; to yield without
murmuring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our religion requires from us . . . to <qex>submit</qex> to pain,
disgrace, and even death.</q> <rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submitter</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mit"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
submits.</def> <rj><au>Whitlock.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submonish</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mon"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>submonere</ets>. See <er>Summon</er>, and <er>-ish</er>.]</ety>
<def>To suggest; to prompt.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The
<xex>submonishing</xex> inclinations of my senses.<rdquo/ <rj><au>T.
Granger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submonition</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`mo*ni"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>submonitio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Suggestion; prompting.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>T. Granger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submucous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mu"cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under a mucous membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submultiple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mul"ti*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>A number or quality which is contained in another an exact number
of times, or is an aliquot part of it; thus, 7 is the
<xex>submultiple</xex> of 56, being contained in it eight
times.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submultiple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mul"ti*ple</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Of or
pertaining to a submultiple; being a submultiple; <as>as, a
<ex>submultiple</ex> number; <ex>submultiple</ex> ratio</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Submuscular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*mus"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Situated
underneath a muscle or muscles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnarcotic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`nar*cot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Moderately narcotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnasal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*na"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the nose; <as>as, the <ex>subnasal</ex> point, or
the middle point of the inferior border of the anterior nasal
aperture</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnascent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*nas"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subnascens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>subnasci</ets> to grow under;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>nasci</ets> to be born.]</ety> <def>Growing
underneath.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnect</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*nect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subnectere</ets>, <ets>subnextum</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>nectere</ets> to tie.]</ety> <def>To tie or fasten beneath; to join
beneath.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnex</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*nex"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Subnect</er>.]</ety> <def>To subjoin; to subnect.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnormal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*nor"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>That part of the axis of a curved line which is intercepted between
the ordinate and the normal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnotation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`no*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subnotatio</ets> a signing underneath, fr. <ets>subnotare</ets> to
subscribe; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>notare</ets> to note or
mark.]</ety> <def>A rescript.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnotochordal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*no`to*chor"dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated on the ventral side of the notochord;
<as>as, the <ex>subnotochordal</ex> rod</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subnuvolar</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*nu"vo*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub</ets> + It. <ets>nuvola</ets> cloud: cf. L.
<ets>subnubilus</ets> somewhat cloudy.]</ety> <def>Under the clouds;
attended or partly covered or obscured by clouds; somewhat cloudy.</def>
<mark>[R. & Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Subnuvolar</qex> lights of evening sharply slant.</q>
<rj><qau>Milnes.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subobscurely</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ob*scure"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Somewhat
obscurely or darkly.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Donne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subobtuse</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ob*tuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partially
obtuse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suboccipital</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`oc*cip"i*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or posterior to, the occiput; <as>as, the
<ex>suboccipital</ex>, or first cervical, nerve</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suboctuple</ent><br/
<ent>Suboctave</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*oc"tave</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*oc"tu*ple</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing one part of eight;
having the ratio of one to eight.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Wilkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subocular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*oc"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub +
ocular</ets>: cf. L. <ets>subocularis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subofficer</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*of"fi*cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub
+ officer</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sous-officer</ets>.]</ety> <def>An under or
subordinate officer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subopercular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`o*per"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated below the operculum; pertaining to the suboperculum.</def>
-- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The suboperculum.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suboperculum</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`o*per"cu*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Sub-</er>, <er>Operculum</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The
lower opercular bone in fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suborbiculate</ent><br/
<ent>Suborbicular</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub`or*bic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sub`or*bic"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Almost orbiculate or orbicular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suborbitar</ent><br/
<ent>Suborbital</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*or"bit*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*or"bit*ar</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated
under or below the orbit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suborder</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*or"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld>
<def>A division of an order; a group of genera of a little lower rank
than an order and of greater importance than a tribe or family; <as>as,
cichoraceous plants form a <ex>suborder</ex> of
Compositae</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subordinacy</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*or"di*na*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Subordinate</er>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
subordinate, or subject to control; subordination, <as>as, to bring the
imagination to act in <ex>subordinacy</ex> to reason</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Spectator.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subordinancy</ent><br/
<ent>Subordinance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*or"di*nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*or"di*nan*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub</ets> + L.
<ets>ordinans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>ordinare</ets>. See
<er>Subordinate</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Subordinacy;
subordination.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H. More. Sir W.
Temple.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subordinary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*or"di*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>One of several heraldic bearings somewhat less common than an
ordinary. See <er>Ordinary</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Different writers name different bearings as
subordinaries, but the <xex>bar</xex>, <xex>bend</xex>,
<xex>sinister</xex>, <xex>pile</xex>, <xex>inescutcheon bordure</xex>,
<xex>gyron</xex>, and <xex>quarter</xex>, are always considered
subordinaries by those who do not class them as ordinaries.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subordinate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*or"di*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub</ets> + L. <ets>ordinatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>ordinare</ets>
to set in order, to arrange. See <er>Ordain</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Placed in a lower order, class, or rank; holding a lower or
inferior position.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The several kinds and <qex>subordinate</qex> species of each are
easily distinguished.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inferior in order, nature, dignity, power,
importance, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It was <qex>subordinate</qex>, not enslaved, to the
understanding.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subordinate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*or"di*nate</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who stands in order or
rank below another; -- distinguished from a <xex>principal</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subordinate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*or"di*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subordinated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subordinating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To place in a
lower order or class; to make or consider as of less value or
importance; <as>as, to <ex>subordinate</ex> one creature to
another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make subject; to subject or subdue; <as>as, to
<ex>subordinate</ex> the passions to reason</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sub*or"di*nate*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sub*or"di*nate*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subordination</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*or`di*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>subordination</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
subordinating, placing in a lower order, or subjecting.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being subordinate or
inferior to an other; inferiority of rank or dignity;
subjection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Natural creature having a local <qex>subordination</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Holyday.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Place of inferior rank.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Persons who in their several <qex>subordinations</qex> would be
obliged to follow the example of their superiors.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subordinative</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*or"di*na*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to
subordinate; expressing subordination; used to introduce a subordinate
sentence; <as>as, a <ex>subordinative</ex> conjunction</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suborn</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*orn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Suborned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suborning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>suborner</ets>, L. <ets>subornare</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under,
secretly + <ets>ornare</ets> to furnish, provide, equip, adorn. See
<er>Ornament</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To procure
or cause to take a false oath amounting to perjury, such oath being
actually taken.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. O. Russell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To procure privately, or by collusion; to procure by
indirect means; to incite secretly; to instigate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou art <qex>suborned</qex> against his honor.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those who by despair <qex>suborn</qex> their death.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subornation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`or*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>subornation</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The act
of suborning; the crime of procuring a person to take such a false oath
as constitutes perjury.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The sin or offense of procuring one to do a criminal
or bad action, as by bribes or persuasion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Foul <qex>subornation</qex> is predominant.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sort of chicanery attending the <qex>subornation</qex> of
managers in the Leibnitz controversy.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quinsey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suborner</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*orn"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who suborns or
procures another to take, a false oath; one who procures another to do a
bad action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suboval</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*o"val</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat oval;
nearly oval.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subovate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*o"vate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly in the form
of an egg, or of the section of an egg, but having the inferior
extremity broadest; nearly ovate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subovated</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*o"va*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Subovate.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suboxide</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ox"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An oxide containing a relatively small amount of oxygen, and less
than the normal proportion; <as>as, potassium <ex>suboxide</ex>,
<chform>K4O</chform></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpeduncular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`pe*dun"cu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated beneath the peduncle; <as>as, the
<ex>subpeduncular</ex> lobe of the cerebellum</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpedunculate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`pe*dun"cu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot. &
Zool.)</fld> <def>Supported on, or growing from, a very short stem;
having a short peduncle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpellucid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`pel*lu"cid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
pellucid; nearly pellucid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpena</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pe"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v. t.</pos> <def>See
<er>Subpoena</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpentangular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`pen*tan"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly or
approximately pentangular; almost pentangular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpericardial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*per`i*car"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the cardiac
pericardium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subperiosteal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*per`i*os"te*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the periosteum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subperiosteal operation</b></col> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>,
<cd>a removal of bone effected without taking away the
periosteum.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subperitoneal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*per`i*to"ne*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the peritoneal
membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpetiolar</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pet"i*o*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Concealed within the base of the petiole, as the leaf buds of the
plane tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpleural</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pleu"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the pleural membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpodophyllous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pod`o*phyl"lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated under the podophyllous tissue of the
horse's foot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpoena</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*poe"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>poena</ets> punishment. See
<er>Pain</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A writ commanding the
attendance in court, as a witness, of the person on whom it is served,
under a penalty; the process by which a defendant in equity is commanded
to appear and answer the plaintiff's bill.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>subpena</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs>\'d8<col><b>Subpoena ad testificandum</b></col> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <cd>A writ used to procure the attendance of a witness
for the purpose of testifying.</cd> -- \'d8<col><b>Subpoena duces
tecum</b></col> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <ety>[NL.]</ety> <cd>A writ which
requires a witness to attend and bring certain documents.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpoena</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*poe"na</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Subpoenaed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subpoenaing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To serve
with a writ of subpoena; to command attendance in court by a legal writ,
under a penalty in case of disobedience.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpoenal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*poe"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Required or done
under penalty.</def> <rj><au>Gauden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpolar</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*po"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Situated below the
poles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpolygonal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`po*lyg"o*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Approximately
polygonal; somewhat or almost polygonal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subprehensile</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`pre*hen"sile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
prehensile; prehensile in an inferior degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subprior</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pri"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub +
prior</ets>: cf. F. <ets>sous-prieur</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>The vicegerent of a prior; a claustral officer who assists the
prior.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpubic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pu"bic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or posterior to, the pubic bones.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpulmonary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pul"mo*na*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the lungs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpurchaser</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pur"chas*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A purchaser
who buys from a purchaser; one who buys at second hand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subpyriform</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*pyr"i*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
pyriform.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subquadrate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*quad"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly or
approximately square; almost square.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subquadruple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*quad"ru*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing one
part of four; in the ratio of one to four; <as>as, <ex>subquadruple</ex>
proportion</as>.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Wilkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subquinquefid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*quin"que*fid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Almost
quinquefid; nearly quinquefid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subquintuple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*quin"tu*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
ratio of one to five; <as>as, <ex>subquintuple</ex>
proportion</as>.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Wilkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subreader</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*read"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>An under reader in the inns of court, who reads the texts of law
the reader is to discourse upon.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subrector</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*rec"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An assistant
restor.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subreligion</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`re*li"gion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A secondary
religion; a belief or principle held in a quasi religious
veneration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Loyalty is in the English a <qex>subreligion</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subreption</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*rep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subreptio</ets>, fr. <ets>subripere</ets>, <ets>subreptum</ets>, to
snatch or take away secretly: cf. F. <ets>subreption</ets>. See
<er>Surreptitious</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of obtaining a favor by
surprise, or by unfair representation through suppression or fraudulent
concealment of facts.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subreptitious</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`rep*ti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subreptitius</ets>. See <er>Surreptitious</er>.]</ety>
<def>Surreptitious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Sub`rep*ti"tious*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1436 --></p>

<p><ent>Subreptive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*rep"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subreptivus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Surreptitious.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subrigid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*rig"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat rigid or
stiff.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subriguous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*rig"u*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subriguus</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>riguus</ets> watered,
akin to <ets>rigare</ets> to water.]</ety> <def>Watered or wet beneath;
well-watered.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Blount.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subrogate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ro*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subrogatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>subrogare</ets>. See
<er>Surrogate</er>.]</ety> <def>To put in the place of another; to
substitute.</def> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subrogation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ro*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>subrogation</ets>, LL. <ets>subrogatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act
of subrogating.</def> Specifically: <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The
substitution of one person in the place of another as a creditor, the
new creditor succeeding to the rights of the former; the mode by which a
third person who pays a creditor succeeds to his rights against the
debtor.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier. Burrill. Abbott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subrotund</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ro*tund"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
rotund.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsacral</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sa"cral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the sacrum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsaline</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`sa*line"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Moderately saline
or salt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsalt</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"salt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
basic salt. See the Note under <er>Salt</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsannation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`san*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subsannatio</ets>, fr. <ets>subsannare</ets> to deride by mimicking
gestures.]</ety> <def>Derision; mockery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscapulary</ent><br/
<ent>Subscapular</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*scap"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*scap"u*la*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated
beneath the scapula; infrascapular; <as>as, the <ex>subscapular</ex>
muscle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscribable</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*scrib"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being subscribed.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscribe</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*scribe"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subscribed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subscribing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>subscribere</ets>, <ets>subscriptum</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>scribere</ets> to write: cf. F. <ets>souscrire</ets>. See
<er>Scribe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To write underneath, as one's
name; to sign (one's name) to a document.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They] <qex>subscribed</qex> their names under them.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sign with one's own hand; to give consent to, as
something written, or to bind one's self to the terms of, by writing
one's name beneath; <as>as, parties <ex>subscribe</ex> a covenant or
contract; a man <ex>subscribes</ex> a bond</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All the bishops <qex>subscribed</qex> the sentence.</q>
<rj><qau>Milman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To attest by writing one's name beneath; <as>as,
officers <ex>subscribe</ex> their official acts, and secretaries and
clerks <ex>subscribe</ex> copies or records</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To promise to give, by writing one's name with the
amount; <as>as, each man <ex>subscribed</ex> ten dollars</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To sign away; to yield; to surrender.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To declare over one's signature; to publish.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Either or must shortly hear from him, or I will
<qex>subscribe</qex> him a coward.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscribe</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*scribe"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sign one's
name to a letter or other document.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give consent to something written, by signing
one's name; hence, to assent; to agree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So spake, so wished, much humbled Eve; but Fate<br/
<qex>Subscribed</qex> not.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To become surely; -- with <xex>for</xex>.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To yield; to admit one's self to be inferior or in
the wrong.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>subscribe</qex>, and say I wronged the duke.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To set one's name to a paper in token of promise to
give a certain sum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To enter one's name for a newspaper, a book,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscriber</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*scrib"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who subscribes; one who contributes to an undertaking by
subscribing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who enters his name for a paper, book, map, or
the like.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscript</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"script</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subscriptus</ets>, p. p. See <er>Subscribe</er>.]</ety>
<def>Written below or underneath; <as>as, iota <ex>subscript</ex></as>.
(See under <er>Iota</er>.) Specifically <fld>(Math.)</fld>, said of
marks, figures, or letters (suffixes), written below and usually to the
right of other letters to distinguish them; <as>as, <it>a</it>,
<it>n</it>, 2, in the symbols <ex>X<subs>a</subs></ex>,
<ex>A<subs>n</subs></ex>, <ex>Y<subs>2</subs></ex></as>. See
<er>Suffix</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 2, and <er>Subindex</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscript</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"script</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Anything written below.</def>
<rj><au>Bentley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscription</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*scrip"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subscriptio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>souscription</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of subscribing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is subscribed.</def> Specifically:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A paper to which a signature is attached.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The signature attached to a paper.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>Consent or attestation by underwriting the name.</def> <sd>(d)</sd>
<def>Sum subscribed; amount of sums subscribed; <as>as, an individual
<ex>subscription</ex> to a fund</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The acceptance of articles, or
other tests tending to promote uniformity; esp. <fld>(Ch. of
Eng.)</fld>, formal assent to the Thirty-nine Articles and the Book of
Common Prayer, required before ordination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Submission; obedience.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You owe me no <qex>subscription</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Pharm.)</fld> <def>That part of a prescription
which contains the direction to the apothecary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 6. <def>A method of purchasing items produced periodically in a
series, as newspapers or magazines, in which a certain number of the
items are delivered as produced, without need for ordering each item
individually; also, the purchase thus executed.</def> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> <note>The right to attend a series of public performances of ballet,
opera, or music are also often sold by subscription. The payment for a
subscription may be made prior to delivery of any items (common with
magazines and performances), or after a certain number of the items have
been delivered (common with newspapers or works of art produced in a
series).</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> 7. An application to purchase a certain number of securities to be
delivered when they are newly issued. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subscriptive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*scrip"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a subscription, or signature.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>subscriptive</xex> part.<rdquo/ <au>Richardson.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Sub*scrip"tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsecute</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"se*cute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subsecutus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>subsequi</ets>. See
<er>Subsequent</er>.]</ety> <def>To follow closely, or so as to
overtake; to pursue.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To follow and detain him, if by any possibility he could be
<qex>subsecuted</qex> and overtaken.</q> <rj><qau>E.
Hall.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsecutive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sec"u*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>subs<eacute/cutif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Following in a train or
succession.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsellium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sub*sel"li*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Subsellia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Arch.)</fld> <def>One of the stalls of the
lower range where there are two ranges. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Stall</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsemitone</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sem"i*tone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>The sensible or leading note, or sharp seventh, of any key;
subtonic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsensible</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sen"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Deeper than the
reach of the senses.</def> <ldquo/That <xex>subsensible</xex>
world.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tyndall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subseptuple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sep"tu*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
ratio of one to seven.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Wilkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsequency</ent><br/
<ent>Subsequence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub"se*quence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub"se*quen*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or state of following;
-- opposed to <xex>precedence</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsequent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"se*quent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subsequens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>subsequi</ets>
to follow, succeed: cf. F. <ets>subs<eacute/quent</ets>. See
<er>Sue</er> to follow.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Following in time;
coming or being after something else at any time, indefinitely; <as>as,
<ex>subsequent</ex> events; <ex>subsequent</ex> ages or years; a period
long <ex>subsequent</ex> to the foundation of Rome</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Following in order of place; succeeding; <as>as, a
<ex>subsequent</ex> clause in a treaty</as>.</def> <ldquo/The
<xex>subsequent</xex> words come on before the precedent vanish.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsequently</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"se*quent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>At a later time;
afterwards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subserous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*se"rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under a serous membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subserve</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*serve"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subserved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subserving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>subservire</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>servire</ets> to
serve. See <er>Serve</er>.]</ety> <def>To serve in subordination or
instrumentally; to be subservient to; to help forward; to
promote.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a great credit to know the ways of captivating Nature, and
making her <qex>subserve</qex> our purposes, than to have learned all
the intrigues of policy.</q> <rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subserve</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*serve"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To be subservient or
subordinate; to serve in an inferior capacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not made to rule,<br/
But to <qex>subserve</qex> where wisdom bears command.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subserviency</ent><br/
<ent>Subservience</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*serv"i*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*serv"i*en*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
subservient; instrumental fitness or use; hence, willingness to serve
another's purposes; in a derogatory sense, servility.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The body wherein appears much fitness, use, and
<qex>subserviency</qex> to infinite functions.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is a regular subordination and <qex>subserviency</qex> among
all the parts to beneficial ends.</q> <rj><qau>Cheyne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subservient</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*serv"i*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subserviens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. See
<er>Subserve</er>.]</ety> <def>Fitted or disposed to subserve; useful in
an inferior capacity; serving to promote some end; subordinate; hence,
servile, truckling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Scarce ever reading anything which he did not make
<qex>subservient</qex> in one kind or other.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Fell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These ranks of creatures are <qex>subservient</qex> one to
another.</q> <rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their temporal ambition was wholly <qex>subservient</qex> to their
proselytizing spirit.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subserviently</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*serv"i*ent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a subservient
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsesqui-</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ses"qui-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub-</ets> +
<ets>sesqui-</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A prefix (also used
adjectively) denoting the combination of constituents (especially
electro-negative and electro-positive bodies) in the proportion of
<xex>two</xex> to <xex>three</xex>; <as>as, a <ex>subsesqui</ex>
acetate, <it>i. e.</it>, a salt having two equivalents of acetic acid to
three of the base.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsextuple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sex"tu*ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the
ratio of one to six; <as>as, a <ex>sub</ex>sextuple
proportion</as>.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Wilkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subside</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*side"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subsided</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subsiding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>subsidere</ets>;
<ets>sub</ets> under, below + <ets>sidere</ets> to sit down, to settle;
akin to <ets>sedere</ets> to sit, E. <ets>sit</ets>. See
<er>Sit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sink or fall to the bottom; to
settle, as lees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To tend downward; to become lower; to descend; to
sink.</def> <ldquo/Heaven's <xex>subsiding</xex> hill.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fall into a state of quiet; to cease to rage; to
be calmed; to settle down; to become tranquil; to abate; <as>as, the sea
<ex>subsides</ex>; the tumults of war will <ex>subside</ex>; the fever
has <ex>subsided</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/In cases of danger, pride and
envy naturally <xex>subside</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>C.
Middleton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Abate</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsidency</ent><br/
<ent>Subsidence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*sid"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*sid"en*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>subsidens</ets>,
<ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>subsidere</ets>. See
<er>Subside</er>.]</ety> <def>The act or process of subsiding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The subdual or <qex>subsidence</qex> of the more violent
passions.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsidiarily</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sid"i*a*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a
subsidiary manner; so as to assist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsidiary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sid"i*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subsidiarius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>subsidiaire</ets>. See
<er>Subsidy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Furnishing aid; assisting;
auxiliary; helping; tributary; especially, aiding in an inferior
position or capacity; <as>as, a <ex>subsidiary</ex>
stream</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Chief ruler and principal head everywhere, not suffragant and
<qex>subsidiary</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Florio.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They constituted a useful <qex>subsidiary</qex> testimony of
another state of existence.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a subsidy; constituting a
subsidy; being a part of, or of the nature of, a subsidy; <as>as,
<ex>subsidiary</ex> payments to an ally</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>George the Second relied on his <qex>subsidiary</qex>
treaties.</q> <rj><qau>Ld. Mahon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsidiary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sid"i*a*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Subsidiaries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who, or that
which, contributes aid or additional supplies; an assistant; an
auxiliary.</def> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsidize</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"si*dize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subsidized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subsidizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From
<er>Subsidy</er>.]</ety> <def>To furnish with a subsidy; to purchase the
assistance of by the payment of a subsidy; to aid or promote, as a
private enterprise, with public money; <as>as, to <ex>subsidize</ex> a
steamship line</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He employed the remittances from Spain to <qex>subsidize</qex> a
large body of German mercenaries.</q> <rj><qau>Prescott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsidy</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"si*dy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Subsidies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>subsidium</ets>
the troops stationed in reserve in the third line of battlem reserve,
support, help, fr. <ets>subsidere</ets> to sit down, lie in wait: cf. F.
<ets>subside</ets>. See <er>Subside</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Support; aid; cooperation; esp., extraordinary aid in money
rendered to the sovereign or to a friendly power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They advised the king to send speedy aids, and with much alacrity
granted a great rate of <qex>subsidy</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Subsidies</xex> were taxes, not immediately on on
property, but on persons in respect of their reputed estates, after the
nominal rate of 4s. the pound for lands, and 2s. 8d. for goods.</note>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically: A sum of money paid by one sovereign
or nation to another to purchase the cooperation or the neutrality of
such sovereign or nation in war.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A grant from the government, from a municipal
corporation, or the like, to a private person or company to assist the
establishment or support of an enterprise deemed advantageous to the
public; a subvention; <as>as, a <ex>subsidy</ex> to the owners of a line
of ocean steamships</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Tribute; grant.</syn> -- <usage><er>Subsidy</er>,
<er>Tribute</er>. A <xex>subsidy</xex> is voluntary; a
<xex>tribute</xex> is exacted.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsign</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sign"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subsigned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subsigning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>subsignare</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>signare</ets> to
mark: cf. F. <ets>soussigner</ets>. See <er>Sign</er>.]</ety> <def>To
sign beneath; to subscribe.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsignation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`sig*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subsignatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of writing the name under
something, as for attestation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shelton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsilicate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sil"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A basic
silicate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsist</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sist"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subsisted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subsisting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>subsistere</ets> to
stand still, stay, remain alive; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>sistere</ets> to stand, to cause to stand, from <ets>stare</ets> to
stand: cf. F. <ets>subsister</ets>. See <er>Stand</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To be; to have existence; to inhere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And makes what happiness we justly call,<br/
<qex>Subsist</qex> not in the good of one, but all.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To continue; to retain a certain state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Firm we <qex>subsist</qex>, yet possible to swerve.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be maintained with food and clothing; to be
supported; to live.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>subsist</qex> on other men's charity.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsist</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sist"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To support with provisions; to
feed; to maintain; <as>as, to <ex>subsist</ex> one's
family</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He laid waste the adjacent country in order to render it more
difficult for the enemy to <qex>subsist</qex> their army.</q>
<rj><qau>Robertson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsistence</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sist"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>subsistance</ets>, L. <ets>subsistentia</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Real being; existence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not only the things had <qex>subsistence</qex>, but the very
images were of some creatures existing.</q>
<rj><qau>Stillingfleet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inherency; <as>as, the <ex>subsistence</ex> of
qualities in bodies</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which furnishes support to animal life; means
of support; provisions, or that which produces provisions; livelihood;
<as>as, a meager <ex>subsistence</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His viceroy could only propose to himself a comfortable
<qex>subsistence</qex> out of the plunder of his province.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Hypostasis</er>,
2.</def> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsistence Department</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sist"ence De*part"ment</hw>. <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A staff
department of the United States army charged, under the supervision of
the Chief of Staff, with the purchasing and issuing to the army of such
supplies as make up the ration. It also supplies, for authorized sales,
certain articles of food and other minor stores. It is commanded by any
officer of the rank of brigadier general, called commissary general, and
the department is popularly called the <altname>Commissary
Department</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsistency</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sist"en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Subsistence.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsistent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sist"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subsistens</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Subsist</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having real being; <as>as, a <ex>subsistent</ex>
spirit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Inherent; <as>as, qualities <ex>subsistent</ex> in
matter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsizar</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*si"zar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An under sizar; a
student of lower rank than a sizar.</def> <mark>[Cambridge Univ.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Bid my <qex>subsizar</qex> carry my hackney to the buttery and
give him his bever.</q> <rj><qau>J. Fletcher.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsoil</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"soil`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The bed, or stratum,
of earth which lies immediately beneath the surface soil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subsoil plow</b></col>, <cd>a plow having a share and
standard but no moldboard. It follows in the furrow made by an ordinary
plow, and loosens the soil to an additional depth without bringing it to
the surface.</cd> <au>Knight.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsoil</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"soil`</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To turn up the subsoil
of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsolary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*so"la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being under the
sun; hence, terrestrial; earthly; mundane.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subspecies</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*spe"cies</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A group somewhat
lessdistinct than speciesusually are, but based on characters more
important than those which characterize ordinary varieties; often, a
geographical variety or race.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsphenoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`sphe*noid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under, or on the ventral side of, the body of the sphenoid
bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subspherical</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*spher"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly
spherical; having a figure resembling that of a sphere.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subspinous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*spi"nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Subvertebral.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Situated beneath a spinous process, as that of
the scapula; <as>as, <ex>subspinous</ex> dislocation of the
humerus</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substance</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>substantia</ets>, fr. <ets>substare</ets> to be under or present,
to stand firm; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>stare</ets> to stand. See
<er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which underlies all outward
manifestations; substratum; the permanent subject or cause of phenomena,
whether material or spiritual; that in which properties inhere; that
which is real, in distinction from that which is apparent; the abiding
part of any existence, in distinction from any accident; that which
constitutes anything what it is; real or existing essence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These cooks, how they stamp, and strain, and grind,<br/
And turn <qex>substance</qex> into accident!</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Heroic virtue did his actions guide,<br/
And he the <qex>substance</qex>, not the appearance, chose.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The most important element in any existence; the
characteristic and essential components of anything; the main part;
essential import; purport.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This edition is the same in <qex>substance</qex> with the
Latin.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is insolent in words, in manner; but in <qex>substance</qex> it
is not only insulting, but alarming.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Body; matter; material of which a thing is made;
hence, substantiality; solidity; firmness; <as>as, the
<ex>substance</ex> of which a garment is made; some textile fabrics have
little <ex>substance</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Material possessions; estate; property;
resources.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And there wasted his <qex>substance</qex> with riotous living.</q>
<rj><qau>Luke xv. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy <qex>substance</qex>, valued at the highest rate,<br/
Can not amount unto a hundred marks.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are destroying many thousand lives, and exhausting our
<qex>substance</qex>, but not for our own interest.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Hypostasis</er>,
2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substance</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stance</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To furnish or endow with
substance; to supply property to; to make rich.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substanceless</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stance*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no substance;
unsubstantial.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substant</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>substans</ets>, <ets>-antis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>substare</ets> to
be firm.]</ety> <def>Substantial; firm.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<ldquo/[The glacier's] <xex>substant</xex> ice.<rdquo/ <rj><au>The
Century.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stan"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>substantiel</ets>, L. <ets>substantialis</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Belonging to substance; actually existing; real; <as>as,
<ex>substantial</ex> life</as>.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If this atheist would have his chance to be real and
<qex>substantial</qex> agent, he is more stupid than the vulgar.</q>
<rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not seeming or imaginary; not illusive; real; solid;
true; veritable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If happinessbe a <qex>substantial</qex> good.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>substantial</qex> ornaments of virtue.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Corporeal; material; firm.</def> <ldquo/Most
ponderous and <xex>substantial</xex> things.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The rainbow [appears to be] a large <qex>substantial</qex>
arch.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Having good substance; strong; stout; solid; firm;
<as>as, <ex>substantial</ex> cloth; a <ex>substantial</ex> fence or
wall</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Possessed of goods or an estate; moderately wealthy;
responsible; <as>as, a <ex>substantial</ex> freeholder</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Substantial</xex> yeomen and burghers.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1437 --></p>

<p><ent>Substantiality</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stan`ti*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being substantial; corporiety;
materiality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The soul is a stranger to such gross
<qex>substantiality</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantialize</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stan"tial*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Substantialized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Substantializing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To make substantial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantially</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stan"tial*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a substantial manner;
in substance; essentially.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In him all his Father shone,<br/
<qex>Substantially</qex> expressed.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The laws of this religion would make men, if they would truly
observe them, <qex>substantially</qex> religious toward God, chastle,
and temperate.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantialness</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stan"tial*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being substantial; <as>as, the <ex>substantialness</ex> of a wall or
column</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantials</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stan"tials</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Essential
parts.</def> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantiate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stan"ti*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Substantiated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Substantiating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make to
exist; to make real.</def> <rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To establish the existence or truth of by proof or
competent evidence; to verify; <as>as, to <ex>substantiate</ex> a charge
or allegation; to <ex>substantiate</ex> a declaration</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Observation is, in turn, wanted to direct and
<qex>substantiate</qex> the course of experiment.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantiation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stan`ti*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
substantiating or proving; evidence; proof.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantival</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`stan*ti"val</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a substantive; of the nature of substantive.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sub`stan*ti"val*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stan*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>substantivus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>substantif</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Betokening or expressing existence; <as>as, the
<ex>substantive</ex> verb, that is, the verb <ex>to
be</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Depending on itself; independent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He considered how sufficient and <qex>substantive</qex> this land
was to maintain itself without any aid of the foreigner.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Enduring; solid; firm; substantial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Strength and magnitude are qualities which impress the imagination
in a powerful and <qex>substantive</qex> manner.</q>
<rj><qau>Hazlitt.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Pertaining to, or constituting, the essential part
or principles; <as>as, the law <ex>substantive</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Noun substantive</b></col> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>, <cd>a noun
which designates an object, material or immaterial; a substantive.</cd>
-- <col><b>Substantive color</b></col>, <cd>one which communicates its
color without the aid of a mordant or base; -- opposed to <xex>adjective
color</xex>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stan*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>substantif</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A noun or name;
the part of speech which designates something that exists, or some
object of thought, either material or immaterial; <as>as, the words
<ex>man</ex>, <ex>horse</ex>, <ex>city</ex>, <ex>goodness</ex>,
<ex>excellence</ex>, are <ex>substantives</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stan*tive</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To substantivize.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantively</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stan*tive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a
substantive manner; in substance; essentially.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>As a substantive, name, or noun;
<as>as, an adjective may be used <ex>substantively</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stan*tive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being substantive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substantivize</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stan*tiv*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To convert
into a substantive; <as>as, to <ex>substantivize</ex> an
adjective</as>.</def> <rj><au>Fitzed. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substile</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stile`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Dialing)</fld>
<def>See <er>Substyle</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substituent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stit"u*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>substituens</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Substitute</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Any atom, group, or radical substituted for
another, or entering a molecule in place of some other part which is
removed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substitute</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stit"ute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>substitutus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>substituere</ets> to put under,
put in the place of; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>statuere</ets> to put,
place: cf. F. <ets>substitut</ets>. See <er>Statute</er>.]</ety>
<def>One who, or that which, is substituted or put in the place of
another; one who acts for another; that which stands in lieu of
something else</def>; specifically <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <def>a person who
enlists for military service in the place of a conscript or drafted
man.</def><-- archaic, no longer legal. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hast thou not made me here thy <qex>substitute</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ladies [in Shakespeare's age] . . . wore masks as the sole
<qex>substitute</qex> known to our ancestors for the modern parasol.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substitute</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stit"ute</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Substituted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Substituting</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Substitute</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To put in the place of
another person or thing; to exchange.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some few verses are inserted or <qex>substituted</qex> in the room
of others.</q> <rj><qau>Congreve.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substituted</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"stit"uted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Exchanged; put in the place of another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Containing substitutions or
replacements; having been subjected to the process of substitution, or
having some of its parts replaced; <as>as, alcohol is a
<ex>substituted</ex> water; methyl amine is a <ex>substituted</ex>
ammonia</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Substituted executor</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an
executor appointed to act in place of one removed or
resigned.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substitution</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`sti*tu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>substitutio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>substitution</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of substituting or putting one person or thing
in the place of another; <as>as, the <ex>substitution</ex> of an agent,
attorney, or representative to act for one in his absense; the
<ex>substitution</ex> of bank notes for gold and silver as a circulating
medium.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being substituted for
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The office or authority of one acting for another;
delegated authority.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld> <def>The designation of a person
in a will to take a devise or legacy, either on failure of a former
devisee or legatee by incapacity or unwillingness to accept, or after
him.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The doctrine that Christ
suffered vicariously, being substituted for the sinner, and that his
sufferings were expiatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The act or process of
substituting an atom or radical for another atom or radical; metathesis;
also, the state of being so substituted. See
<er>Metathesis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substitutional</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`sti*tu"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to substitution; standing in the place of another;
substituted.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sub`sti*tu"tion*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substitutionary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`sti*tu"tion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to substitution; substitutional.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substitutive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"sti*tu`tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>substitutif</ets>, L. <ets>substitutivus</ets> conditional.]</ety>
<def>Tending to afford or furnish a substitute; making substitution;
capable of being substituted.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Wilkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substract</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stract"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>suostraire</ets>; L. <ets>subtus</ets> below (from <ets>sub</ets>
under) + <ets>trahere</ets> to draw. See <er>Substract</er>.]</ety>
<def>To subtract; to withdraw.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substraction</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*strac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>substraction</ets>, F. <ets>soustraction</ets>. See
<er>Subtract</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Subtraction; deduction.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>See <er>Subtraction</er>,
3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substractor</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stract"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who subtracts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A detractor; a slanderer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substrate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"strate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A substratum.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substrate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"strate</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having very slight
furrows.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substrate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*strate"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>substratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>substrahere</ets>. See
<er>Substratum</er>.]</ety> <def>To strew or lay under anything.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The melted glass being supported by the <qex>substrated</qex>
sand.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substratum</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*stra"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Substrata</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>substratus</ets>,
p. p. of <ets>substernere</ets> to strew under; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>sternere</ets> to strew. See <er>Stratum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>That which is laid or spread under; that which underlies something,
as a layer of earth lying under another; specifically
<fld>(Agric.)</fld>, the subsoil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>The permanent subject of
qualities or cause of phenomena; substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substruct</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*struct"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Substruction</er>.]</ety> <def>To build beneath something; to lay as
the foundation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>substructs</qex> the religion of Asia as the base.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substruction</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*struc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>substructio</ets>, fr. <ets>substruere</ets>,
<ets>substructum</ets>, to build beneath; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>struere</ets> to build.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>Underbuilding; the foundation, or any preliminary structure
intended to raise the lower floor or basement of a building above the
natural level of the ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a magnificent strong building, with a
<qex>substruction</qex> very remarkable.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substructure</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*struc"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + <ets>structure</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Substruction</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An under structure; a foundation;
groundwork.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substylar</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sty"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to the
substyle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Substyle</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"style`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Dialing)</fld>
<def>A right line on which the style, or gnomon, of a dial is erected;
being the common section of the face of the dial and a plane
perpendicular to it passing through the style.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>substile</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsulphate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sul"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A sulphate with an excess of the base.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsulphide</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sul"phide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A nonacid compound consisting of one equivalent of sulphur and more
than one equivalent of some other body, as a metal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsultive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sul"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Subsultory.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Berkley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsultory</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sul"to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subsilire</ets>, <ets>subsultum</ets>, to spring up; <ets>sub</ets>
under + <ets>salire</ets> to leap.]</ety> <def>Bounding; leaping; moving
by sudden leaps or starts.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> --
<wordforms><wf>Sub*sul"to*ri*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<mark>[R.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Flippancy opposed to solemnity, the <qex>subsultory</qex> to the
continuous, -- these are the two frequent extremities to which the
French manner betrays men.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsultus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sub*sul"tus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Subsultory</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A starting,
twitching, or convulsive motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsumable</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sum"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
subsumed.</def> <rj><au>J. B. Stallo.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsume</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sume"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>sumere</ets> to take.]</ety> <def>To take up
into or under, as individual under species, species under genus, or
particular under universal; to place (any one cognition) under another
as belonging to it; to include under something else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>subsume</qex> one proposition under another.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A principle under which one might <qex>subsume</qex> men's most
strenuous efforts after righteousness.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Pater.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsumption</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sump"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of subsuming, or of including under another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The first act of consciousness was a <qex>subsumption</qex> of
that of which we were conscious under this notion.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is subsumed, as the minor clause or
premise of a syllogism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But whether you see cause to go against the rule, or the
<qex>subsumption</qex> under the rule.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subsumptive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*sump"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Relating to, or
containing, a subsumption.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtangent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tan"gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geom.)</fld>
<def>The part of the axis contained between the ordinate and tangent
drawn to the same point in a curve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtartarean</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`tar*ta"re*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being or
living under Tartarus; infernal.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Subtartarean</xex>
powers.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtectacle</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tec"ta*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>tectum</ets> a roof.]</ety> <def>A space under
a roof; a tabernacle; a dwelling.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Davies (Holy Roode).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtegulaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*teg`u*la"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subtegulaneus</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>tegulare</ets>
tiles for a roof.]</ety> <def>Under the roof or eaves; within
doors.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtenant</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ten"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>One who rents a tenement, or land, etc., of one who is also a
tenant; an undertenant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtend</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subtended</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subtending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>subtendere</ets>;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>tendere</ets> to stretch, extend. See
<er>Tend</er>.]</ety> <def>To extend under, or be opposed to; <as>as,
the line of a triangle which <ex>subtends</ex> the right angle; the
chord <ex>subtends</ex> an arc</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtense</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tense"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subtendere</ets>, <ets>subtentum</ets>. See <er>Subtend</er>,
<er>Tense</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A line
subtending, or stretching across; a chord; <as>as, the <ex>subtense</ex>
of an arc</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtepid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tep"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Slightly
tepid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterete</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`te*rete"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
terete.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterfluous</ent><br/
<ent>Subterfluent</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*ter"flu*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*ter"flu*ous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>subterfluens</ets>, p.
pr. of <ets>subterfluere</ets> to flow beneath; <ets>subter</ets> under
+ <ets>fluere</ets> to flow.]</ety> <def>Running under or beneath.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterfuge</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ter*fuge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from LL.
<ets>subterfugium</ets>, fr. L. <ets>subterfugere</ets> to flee
secretly, to escape; <ets>subter</ets> under + <ets>fugere</ets> to
flee. See <er>Fugitive</er>.]</ety> <def>That to which one resorts for
escape or concealment; an artifice employed to escape censure or the
force of an argument, or to justify opinions or conduct; a shift; an
evasion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Affect not little shifts and <qex>subterfuges</qex>, to avoid the
force of an argument.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By a miserable <qex>subterfuge</qex>, they hope to render this
position safe by rendering it nugatory.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterrane</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ter*rane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>subterraneum</ets>, F. <ets>souterrain</ets>. See
<er>Subterranean</er>.]</ety> <def>A cave or room under ground.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>J. Bryant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterraneal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ter*ra"ne*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Subterranean.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterraneous</ent><br/
<ent>Subterranean</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub`ter*ra"ne*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sub`ter*ra"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subterraneus</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>terra</ets> earth.
See <er>Terrace</er>.]</ety> <def>Being or lying under the surface of
the earth; situated within the earth, or under ground; <as>as,
<ex>subterranean</ex> springs; a <ex>subterraneous</ex>
passage.</as></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sub`ter*ra"ne*ous*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterranity</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ter*ran"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place under
ground; a subterrany.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterrany</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"ter*ra*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Subterranean.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Bacon.</au> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A subterranean place.</def></def2>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterrene</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ter*rene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subterrenus</ets>, equiv. to <ets>subterraneus</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Subterraneous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subterrestrial</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ter*res"tri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Subterranean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subthalamic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`tha*lam"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the optic thalamus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtile</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subtilis</ets>. See <er>Subtile</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Thin;
not dense or gross; rare; <as>as, <ex>subtile</ex> air; <ex>subtile</ex>
vapor; a <ex>subtile</ex> medium</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Delicately constituted or constructed; nice; fine;
delicate; tenuous; finely woven.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>sotil</xex>
[subtile] twine's thread.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>More <qex>subtile</qex> web Arachne can not spin.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do distinguish plain<br/
Each <qex>subtile</qex> line of her immortal face.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Acute; piercing; searching.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The slow disease and <qex>subtile</qex> pain.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Characterized by nicety of discrimination;
discerning; delicate; refined; subtle.</def> <altsp>[In this sense now
commonly written <asp>subtle</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The genius of the Spanish people is exquisitely
<qex>subtile</qex>, without being at all acute; hence there is so much
humor and so little wit in their literature. The genius of the Italians,
on the contrary, is acute, profound, and sensual, but not
<qex>subtile</qex>; hence what they think to be humorous, is merely
witty.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>subtile</qex> influence of an intellect like
Emerson's.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Sly; artful; cunning; crafty; subtle; <as>as, a
<ex>subtile</ex> person; a <ex>subtile</ex> adversary; a
<ex>subtile</ex> scheme.</as></def> <altsp>[In this sense now commonly
written <asp>subtle</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Subtile</er>, <er>Acute</er>.</syn> <usage>
In <xex>acute</xex> the image is that of a needle's point; in
<xex>subtile</xex> that of a thread spun out to fineness. The
<xex>acute</xex> intellect pierces to its aim; the <xex>subtile</xex>
(or <xex>subtle</xex>) intellect winds its way through
obstacles.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sub"tile*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sub"tile*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtiliate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*til"i*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>subtiliare</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make thin or rare.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Harvey.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Sub`til*i*a"tion</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Boyle.</au></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtilism</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"til*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being subtile; subtility; subtlety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The high orthodox <qex>subtilism</qex> of Duns Scotus.</q>
<rj><qau>Milman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtility</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*til"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subtilitas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>subtilit<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Subtle</er>.]</ety> <def>Subtilty.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtilization</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`til*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>subtilization</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of making
subtile.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <def>The operation of making so
volatile as to rise in steam or vapor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Refinement; subtlety; extreme attenuation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtilize</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"til*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subtilized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subtilizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>subtiliser</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make thin or fine; to
make less gross or coarse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To refine; to spin into niceties; <as>as, to
<ex>subtilize</ex> arguments</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nor as yet have we <qex>subtilized</qex> ourselves into
savages.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtilize</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"til*ize</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To refine in argument; to
make very nice distinctions.</def> <rj><au>Milner.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtilizer</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"til*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
subtilizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtilty</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"til*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Contr. fr.
<ets>subtility</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of
being subtile; thinness; fineness; <as>as, the <ex>subtility</ex> of air
or light</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Refinement; extreme acuteness; subtlety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Intelligible discourses are spoiled by too much
<qex>subtility</qex> in nice divisions.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Cunning; skill; craft.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To learn a lewd man this <qex>subtility</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Slyness in design; artifice; guile; a cunning design
or artifice; a trick; subtlety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O full of all <qex>subtility</qex> and all mischief.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts xiii. 10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In senses 2, 3, and 4 the word is more commonly written
<xex>subtlety</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtle</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Subtler</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Subtlest</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sotil</ets>, <ets>subtil</ets>, OF. <ets>soutil</ets>, later
<ets>subtil</ets>, F. <ets>subtil</ets>, L. <ets>subtilis</ets>;
probably, originally, woven fine, and fr. <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>tela</ets> a web, fr. <ets>texere</ets> to weave. See
<er>Text</er>, and cf. <er>Subtile</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Sly in
design; artful; cunning; insinuating; subtile; -- applied to persons;
<as>as, a <ex>subtle</ex> foe</as>.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>subtle</xex>
traitor.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cunningly devised; crafty; treacherous; <as>as, a
<ex>subtle</ex> stratagem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Characterized by refinement and niceness in drawing
distinctions; nicely discriminating; -- said of persons; <as>as, a
<ex>subtle</ex> logician</as>; refined; tenuous; sinuous; insinuating;
hence, penetrative or pervasive; -- said of the mind; its faculties, or
its operations; <as>as, a <ex>subtle</ex> intellect; a <ex>subtle</ex>
imagination; a <ex>subtle</ex> process of thought</as>; also, difficult
of apprehension; elusive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Things remote from use, obscure and <qex>subtle</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Smooth and deceptive.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like to a bowl upon a <qex>subtle</qex> ground [bowling
ground].</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Artful; crafty; cunning; shrewd; sly; wily.</syn>
<usage> <xex>Subtle</xex> is the most comprehensive of these epithets
and implies the finest intellectual quality. See <er>Shrewd</er>, and
<er>Cunning</er>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1438 --></p>

<p><ent>Subtleness</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"tle*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being subtle; subtlety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtlety</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"tle*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Subtleties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>sotelte</ets>,
<ets>sutilte</ets>, OF. <ets>sotillete</ets>, L. <ets>subtilitas</ets>.
See <er>Subtle</er>, and cf. <er>Subtility</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The quality or state of being subtle, or sly; cunning; craftiness;
artfulness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fox which lives by <qex>subtlety</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nice discernment with delicacy of mental action;
nicety of discrimination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something that is sly, crafty, or
delusive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Unlearned in the world's false <qex>subtleties</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtly</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"tly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a subtle manner;
slyly; artfully; cunningly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou seest how <qex>subtly</qex> to detain thee I devise.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Nicely; delicately.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the nice bee what sense so <qex>subtly</qex> true.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Subtly</qex> communicating itself to my sensibilities, but
evading the analysis of my mind.</q> <rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Deceitfully; delusively.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld>
<def>Applied to, or distinguishing, a speech element consisting of tone,
or proper vocal sound, not pure as in the vowels, but dimmed and
otherwise modified by some kind of obstruction in the oral or the nasal
passage, and in some cases with a mixture of breath sound; -- a term
introduced by Dr. James Rush in 1833. See <xex>Guide to
Pronunciation</xex>, <sect/<sect/155, 199-202.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ton"ic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld>
<def>A subtonic sound or element; a vocal consonant, as <it>b</it>,
<it>d</it>, <it>g</it>, <it>n</it>, etc.; a subvocal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The seventh tone of the scale, or
that immediately below the tonic; -- called also
<altname>subsemitone</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtorrid</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tor"rid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly
torrid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtract</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tract"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subtracted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subtracting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>subtractus</ets>, p.
p. of <ets>subtrahere</ets> to draw from beneath, withdraw, remove;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>trahere</ets> to draw. See <er>Trace</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Substract</er>.]</ety> <def>To withdraw,
or take away, as a part from the whole; to deduct; <as>as,
<ex>subtract</ex> 5 from 9, and the remainder is 4</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtracter</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tract"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who subtracts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The subtrahend.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtraction</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*trac"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subtractio</ets> a drawing back. See <er>Subtract</er>, and cf.
<er>Substraction</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or operation of
subtracting or taking away a part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>The taking of a lesser number or
quantity from a greater of the same kind or denomination; an operation
for finding the difference between two numbers or quantities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The withdrawing or withholding from
a person of some right to which he is entitled by law.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Thus the <xex>subtraction</xex> of conjugal rights is
when either the husband or wife withdraws from the other and lives
separate without sufficient reason. The <xex>subtraction</xex> of a
legacy is the withholding or detailing of it from the legatee by the
executor. In like manner, the withholding of any service, rent, duty, or
custom, is a <xex>subtraction</xex>, for which the law gives a
remedy.</note> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtractive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*trac"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Tending, or having power, to subtract.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Having the negative sign, or sign
minus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtrahend</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"tra*hend`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subtrahendus</ets> that is to be subtracted, p.fut.pess. of
<ets>subtrahere</ets>. See <er>Subtract</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>The sum or number to be subtracted, or taken from another (the
<contr>minuend</contr>) to find the <contr>difference</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtranslucent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`trans*lu"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not
perfectly translucent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtransparent</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`trans*pa"rent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not
perfectly transparent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtreasurer</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*treas"ur*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The public
officer who has charge of a subtreasury.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtreasury</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*treas"ur*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Subtreasuries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A subordinate
treasury, or place of deposit; <as>as, the United States
<ex>subtreasury</ex> at New York</as>.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtriangular</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`tri*an"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly, but
not perfectly, triangular.</def> <rj><au>Darwin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtribe</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"tribe`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot. &
Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of a tribe; a group of genera of a little
lower rank than a tribe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtrihedral</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`tri*he"dral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Approaching
the form of a three-sided pyramid; <as>as, the <ex>subtrihedral</ex>
crown of a tooth</as>.</def> <rj><au>Owen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtriple</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tri"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>Containing a third, or one part to three.</def> <rj><au>Bp.
Wilkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtriplicate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*trip"li*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>Expressed by the cube root; -- said especially of ratios.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Subtriplicate ratio</b></col>, <cd>the ratio of the cube
root; thus, the <xex>subtriplicate ratio</xex> of <it>a</it> to
<it>b</it> is <cuberoot/<it>a</it> to <cuberoot/<it>b</it>, or
<cuberoot/<fract><it>a</it>/<it>b</it></fract>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtropical</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*trop"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nearly
tropical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtrude</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*trude"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subtruded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subtruding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub-</ets> + L.
<ets>trudere</ets> to thrust.]</ety> <def>To place under; to
insert.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subturriculate</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`tur*ric"u*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Somewhat turriculate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtutor</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*tu"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An under
tutor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subtypical</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*typ"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Deviating somewhat from the type of a species, genus, or other
group; slightly aberrant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subulated</ent><br/
<ent>Subulate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su"bu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Su"bu*la`ted</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>subulatus</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>subula</ets> an awl.]</ety> <def>Very narrow, and tapering
gradually to a fine point from a broadish base; awl-shaped;
linear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subulicornes</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su`bu*li*cor"nes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from L. <ets>subula</ets> an awl + <ets>cornu</ets> horn.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of insects having slender or subulate
antennae. The dragon flies and May flies are examples.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subuliform</ent><br/
<hw>Su"bu*li*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Subulate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subulipalp</ent><br/
<hw>Su"bu*li*palp`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subula</ets> an awl + E. <ets>palp</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of a group of carabid beetles having slender palpi.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subumbonal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`um*bo"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Beneath or forward of the umbos of a bivalve shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subumbrella</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`um*brel"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The integument of the under surface of the bell, or disk-shaped
body, of a jellyfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subundation</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`un*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>unda</ets> a wave.]</ety> <def>A flood; a
deluge.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Huloet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subungual</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*un"gual</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Under the nail or
hoof.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suburb</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"urb</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suburbium</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under, below, near +
<ets>urbs</ets> a city. See <er>Urban</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
outlying part of a city or town; a smaller place immediately adjacent to
a city; in the plural, the region which is on the confines of any city
or large town; <as>as, a house stands in the <ex>suburbs</ex>; a garden
situated in the <ex>suburbs</ex> of Paris</as>.</def> <ldquo/In the
<xex>suburbs</xex> of a town.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[London] could hardly have contained less than thirty or forty
thousand souls within its walls; and the <qex>suburbs</qex> were very
populous.</q> <rj><qau>Hallam.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the confines; the outer part; the
environment.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>suburbs</xex> . . . of sorrow.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>suburb</qex> of their straw-built citadel.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Suburb roister</b></col>, <cd>a rowdy; a loafer.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Milton.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suburban</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ur"ban</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suburbanus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to suburbs;
inhabiting, or being in, the suburbs of a city.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Suburban</xex> taverns.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Suburban</qex> villas, highway-side retreats, . . . <br/
Delight the citizen.</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suburban</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ur"ban</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who dwells in the
suburbs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suburbed</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"urbed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a suburb or
suburbs on its outer part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suburbian</ent><br/
<ent>Suburbial</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*ur"bi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sub*ur"bi*an</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suburban.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Suburbial</xex> fields.<rdquo/
<au>Warton.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Suburbian</xex> muse.<rdquo/
<au>Dryden.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suburbicary</ent><br/
<ent>Suburbicarian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sub*ur`bi*ca"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Sub*ur"bi*ca*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>suburbicarius</ets>, equiv. to L. <ets>suburbanus</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>suburbicaire</ets>. See <er>Suburban</er>.]</ety> <def>Being in the
suburbs; -- applied to the six dioceses in the suburbs of Rome subject
to the pope as bishop of Rome.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pope having stretched his authority beyond the bounds of his
<qex>suburbicarian</qex> precincts.</q> <rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suburethral</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`u*re"thral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the urethra, or under its orifice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvaginal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vag"i*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under or inside a sheath or vaginal membrane; <as>as, the
<ex>subvaginal</ex>, or subdural, spaces about the optic
nerve</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvariety</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`va*ri"e*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>-ties</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A subordinate variety, or a
division of a variety.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvene</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subvened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Subvening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sub-</ets>
+ L. <ets>venire</ets> to come. See <er>Subvention</er>.]</ety> <def>To
come under, as a support or stay; to happen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A future state must needs <qex>subvene</qex> to prevent the whole
edifice from falling into ruin.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subventaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ven*ta"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>ventus</ets> wind.]</ety> <def>Produced by the
wind.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvention</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ven"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. LL.
<ets>subventio</ets>, fr. L. <ets>subvenire</ets> to come up to one's
assistance, to assist. See <er>Souvenir</er>, and cf.
<er>Subvene</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of coming under.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>subvention</xex> of a cloud.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Stackhouse.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of relieving, as of a burden; support; aid;
assistance; help.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A government aid or bounty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvention</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ven"tion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To subventionize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subventionize</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ven"tion*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To come to
the aid of; to subsidize; to support.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subventitious</ent><br/
<hw>Sub`ven*ti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Helping;
aiding; supporting.</def> <rj><au>Urquhart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subverse</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*verse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subversus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>subvertere</ets>. See
<er>Subvert</er>.]</ety> <def>To subvert.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subversion</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ver"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>subversio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>subversion</ets>. See
<er>Subvert</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of overturning, or the state of
being overturned; entire overthrow; an overthrow from the foundation;
utter ruin; destruction; <as>as, the <ex>subversion</ex> of a
government; the <ex>subversion</ex> of despotic power; the
<ex>subversion</ex> of the constitution.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>subversion</qex> [by a storm] of woods and timber . . .
through my whole estate.</q> <rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Laws have been often abused to the oppression and
<qex>subversion</qex> of that order they were intended to preserve.</q>
<rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subversionary</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ver"sion*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Promoting
destruction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subversive</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ver"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>subversif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to subvert; having a tendency
to overthrow and ruin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lying is a vice <qex>subversive</qex> of the very ends and design
of conversation.</q> <rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvert</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vert"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Subverted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Subverting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>subvertere</ets>,
<ets>subversum</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>vertere</ets> to turn:
cf. F. <ets>subvertir</ets>. See <er>Verse</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To overturn from the foundation; to overthrow; to ruin
utterly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These are his substance, sinews, arms, and strength,<br/
With which he yoketh your rebellious necks,<br/
Razeth your cities, and <qex>subverts</qex> your towns.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This would <qex>subvert</qex> the principles of all knowledge.</q>
<rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pervert, as the mind, and turn it from the truth;
to corrupt; to confound.</def> <rj><au>2 Tim. iii. 14.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To overturn; overthrow; destroy; invert; reverse;
extinguish.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvert</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vert"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To overthrow
anything from the foundation; to be subversive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They have a power given to them like that of the evil principle,
to <qex>subvert</qex> and destroy.</q><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvertant</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vert"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>Reversed.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvertebral</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*ver"te*bral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated beneath, or on the ventral side of, the vertebral column;
situated beneath, or inside of, the endoskeleton; hypaxial;
hyposkeletal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subverter</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vert"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, subverts; an overthrower.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvertible</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vert"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
subverted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvitalized</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vi"tal*ized</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Imperfectly
vitalized; having naturally but little vital power or energy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subvocal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*vo"cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Subtonic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subway</ent><br/
<hw>Sub"way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
underground way or gallery; especially, a passage under a street, in
which water mains, gas mains, telegraph wires, etc., are
conducted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An underground railroad, usually having trains
powered by electricity provided by an electric line running through the
underground tunnel. It is usually confined to the center portion of
cities; -- called also <altname>tube</altname>, and in Britain,
<altname>underground</altname>. In certain other countries (as in France
or Russia) it is called the <altname>metro</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subworker</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*work"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A subordinate
worker or helper.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subzonal</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*zon"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under a zone, or zona; -- applied to a membrane between
the zona radiata and the umbilical vesicle in the mammal
embryo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Subzygomatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sub*zyg`o*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated under the zygoma or zygomatic process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succade</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cade</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succus</ets>, <ets>sucus</ets>, juice: cf. F. <ets>succade</ets> a
sugarbox. Cf. <er>Sucket</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
sweetmeat.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>Sweetmeats, or
preserves in sugar, whether fruit, vegetables, or confections.</def>
<rj><au>Blakely.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Succade gourd</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>Same as
<cref>Vegetable marrow</cref>, under <er>Vegetable</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succedane</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"ce*dane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
succedaneum.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succedaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`ce*da"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succedaneus</ets>. See <er>Succeed</er>.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to,
or acting as, a succedaneum; supplying the place of something else;
being, or employed as, a substitute for another.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succedaneum</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`ce*da"ne*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Succedanea</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Succedaneous</er>.]</ety> <def>One who, or that which, succeeds to
the place of another; that which is used for something else; a
substitute;</def> <specif>specifically</specif> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<def>a remedy used as a substitute for another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In lieu of me, you will have a very charming
<qex>succedaneum</qex>, Lady Harriet Stanhope.</q>
<rj><qau>Walpole.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succeed</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ceed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Succeeded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Succeeding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>succedere</ets>,
<ets>successum</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>cedere</ets> to go, to
go along, approach, follow, succeed: cf. F. <ets>succ<eacute/der</ets>.
See <er>Cede</er>, and cf. <er>Success</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
follow in order; to come next after; hence, to take the place of;
<as>as, the king's eldest son <ex>succeeds</ex> his father on the
throne; autumn <ex>succeeds</ex> summer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As he saw him nigh <qex>succeed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fall heir to; to inherit.</def> <mark>[Obs. &
R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To come after; to be subsequent or consequent to; to
follow; to pursue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Destructive effects . . . <qex>succeeded</qex> the curse.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To support; to prosper; to promote.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Succeed</qex> my wish and second my design.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succeed</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ceed"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To come in the
place of another person, thing, or event; to come next in the usual,
natural, or prescribed course of things; to follow; hence, to come next
in the possession of anything; -- often with <xex>to</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the father left only daughters, they equally
<qex>succeeded</qex> to him in copartnership.</q> <rj><qau>Sir M.
Hale.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Enjoy till I return<br/
Short pleasures; for long woes are to <qex>succeed</qex>!</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <specif>Specifically:</specif> <def>To ascend the throne
after the removal the death of the occupant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No woman shall <qex>succeed</qex> in Salique land.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To descend, as an estate or an heirloom, in the same
family; to devolve.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To obtain the object desired; to accomplish what is
attempted or intended; to have a prosperous issue or termination; to be
successful; <as>as, he <ex>succeeded</ex> in his plans; his plans
<ex>succeeded</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is almost impossible for poets to <qex>succeed</qex> without
ambition.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Spenser endeavored it in Shepherd's Kalendar; but neither will it
<qex>succeed</qex> in English.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To go under cover.</def> <mark>[A latinism.
Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Will you to the cooler cave <qex>succeed</qex>!</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To follow; pursue. See <er>Follow</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succeedant</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ceed"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>Succeeding one another; following.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succeeder</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ceed"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A successor.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au> <au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succeeding</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ceed"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of one who, or that
which, succeeds; also, that which succeeds, or follows after;
consequence.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succentor</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cen*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL., an
accompanier in singing, fr. <ets>succinere</ets> to sing, to accompany;
<ets>sub</ets> under, after + <ets>canere</ets> to sing.]</ety>
<fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A subchanter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Success</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cess"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>successus</ets>: cf. F. <ets>succ<egrave/s</ets>. See
<er>Succeed</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Act of succeeding;
succession.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then all the sons of these five brethren reigned<br/
By due <qex>success</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which comes after; hence, consequence, issue,
or result, of an endeavor or undertaking, whether good or bad; the
outcome of effort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men . . . that are like to do that, that is committed to them, and
to report back again faithfully the <qex>success</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Perplexed and troubled at his bad <qex>success</qex><br/
The tempter stood.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The favorable or prosperous termination of anything
attempted; the attainment of a proposed object; prosperous
issue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Dream of <qex>success</qex> and happy victory!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Or teach with more <qex>success</qex> her son<br/
The vices of the time to shun.</q> <rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Military <qex>successes</qex>, above all others, elevate the minds
of a people.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which meets with, or one who accomplishes,
favorable results, as a play or a player.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1439 --></p>

<p><ent>Successary</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"ces*sa*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Succession.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My peculiar honors, not derived<br/
From <qex>successary</qex>, but purchased with my blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Successful</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cess"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resulting in
success; assuring, or promotive of, success; accomplishing what was
proposed; having the desired effect; hence, prosperous; fortunate;
happy; <as>as, a <ex>successful</ex> use of medicine; a
<ex>successful</ex> experiment; a <ex>successful</ex>
enterprise</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Welcome, nephews, from <qex>successful</qex> wars.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Happy; prosperous; fortunate; auspicious; lucky.
See <er>Fortunate</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Suc*cess"ful*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Suc*cess"ful*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succession</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ces"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>successio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>succession</ets>. See
<er>Succeed</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of succeeding, or
following after; a following of things in order of time or place, or a
series of things so following; sequence; <as>as, a <ex>succession</ex>
of good crops; a <ex>succession</ex> of disasters</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A series of persons or things according to some
established rule of precedence; <as>as, a <ex>succession</ex> of kings,
or of bishops; a <ex>succession</ex> of events in
chronology</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was in the <qex>succession</qex> to an earldom.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An order or series of descendants; lineage; race;
descent.</def> <ldquo/A long <xex>succession</xex> must ensue.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The power or right of succeeding to the station or
title of a father or other predecessor; the right to enter upon the
office, rank, position, etc., held ny another; also, the entrance into
the office, station, or rank of a predecessor; specifically, the
succeeding, or right of succeeding, to a throne.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have the voice of the king himself for your
<qex>succession</qex> in Denmark.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The animosity of these factions did not really arise from the
dispute about the <qex>succession</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The right to enter upon the possession of the
property of an ancestor, or one near of kin, or one preceding in an
established order.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The person succeeding to rank or office; a successor
or heir.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Apostolical succession</b></col>. <fld>(Theol.)</fld>
<cd>See under <er>Apostolical</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Succession
duty</b></col>, <cd>a tax imposed on every succession to property,
according to its value and the relation of the person who succeeds to
the previous owner.</cd><-- = death duties? --> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Succession of crops</b></col>. <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Rotation of crops</cref>, under <er>Rotation</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Successional</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ces"sion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a succession; existing in a regular order;
consecutive.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Successional</xex> teeth.<rdquo/
<au>Flower.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Suc*ces"sion*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Successionist</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ces"sion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A person who insists on
the importance of a regular succession of events, offices, etc.;
especially <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, one who insists that apostolic succession
alone is valid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Successive</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ces"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>successif</ets>. See <er>Succeed</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Following in order or in uninterrupted course; coming after without
interruption or interval; following one after another in a line or
series; consecutive; <as>as, the <ex>successive</ex> revolution of
years; the <ex>successive</ex> kings of Egypt; <ex>successive</ex>
strokes of a hammer.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Send the <qex>successive</qex> ills through ages down.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having or giving the right of succeeding to an
inheritance; inherited by succession; hereditary; <as>as, a
<ex>successive</ex> title; a <ex>successive</ex> empire</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Successive induction</b></col>. <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Induction</er>, 5.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Successively</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ces"sive*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a successive
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The whiteness, at length, changed <qex>successively</qex> into
blue, indigo, and violet.</q> <rj><qau>Sir I. Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Successiveness</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ces"sive*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of
being successive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Successless</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cess"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no
success.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Successless</qex> all her soft caresses prove.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Suc*cess"less*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Suc*cess"less*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Successor</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ces"sor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>successour</ets>, OF. <ets>successur</ets>, <ets>successor</ets>,
F. <ets>successeur</ets>, L. <ets>successor</ets>. See
<er>Succeed</er>.]</ety> <def>One who succeeds or follows; one who takes
the place which another has left, and sustains the like part or
character; -- correlative to <xex>predecessor</xex>; <as>as, the
<ex>successor</ex> of a deceased king</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A gift to a corporation, either of lands or of chattels, without
naming their <qex>successors</qex>, vests an absolute property in them
so lond as the corporation subsists.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succiduous</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cid"u*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succiduus</ets>, fr. <ets>succidere</ets> to fall under.]</ety>
<def>Ready to fall; falling.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succiferous</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cif"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succus</ets>, <ets>sucus</ets>, juice, sap +
<ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety> <def>Producing or conveying sap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinamate</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`cin*am"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of succinamic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinamic</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`cin*am"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, an acid amide derivative of
succinic acid, obtained as a white crystalline substance, and forming a
series of salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinate</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"ci*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succinum</ets>, <ets>sucinum</ets>, amber, from <ets>succus</ets>,
<ets>sucus</ets>, juice, sap: cf. F. <ets>succinate</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of succinic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinct</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cinct"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succinctus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>succingere</ets> to gird below or
from below, to tuck up; <ets>sub + cingere</ets> to gird. Cf.
<er>Cincture</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Girded or tucked up; bound;
drawn tightly together.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His habit fit for speed <qex>succinct</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Compressed into a narrow compass; brief;
concise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let all your precepts be <qex>succinct</qex> and clear.</q>
<rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The shortest and most <qex>succinct</qex> model that ever grasped
all the needs and necessities of mankind.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Short; brief; concise; summary; compendious;
laconic; terse.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Suc*cinct"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Suc*cinct"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinic</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cin"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>succinique</ets>. See <er>Succinate</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or derived from, amber; specif., designating a
dibasic acid, C<?/H<?/.(CO<?/H)<?/, first obtained by the dry
distillation of amber. It is found in a number of plants, as in lettuce
and wormwood, and is also produced artificially as a white crystalline
substance having a slightly acid taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinimide</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`cin*im"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A white crystalline nitrogenous substance,
<chform>C2H4.(CO)2.NH</chform>, obtained by treating succinic anhydride
with ammonia gas. It is a typical imido acid, and forms a series of
salts. See <cref>Imido acid</cref>, under <er>Imido</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinite</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"ci*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>succinite</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Amber.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A garnet of an amber
color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinous</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"ci*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>succinum</ets> amber.]</ety> <def>Succinic.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinurate</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`cin*u"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of succinuric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinuric</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`cin*u"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or designating, an acid amide, analogous to
succinamic acid, which is obtained as a white crystalline substance by
heating urea with succinic anhydride. It is known also in its
salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succinyl</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cin*yl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Succin</ets>ic + <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A hypothetical radical characteristic of succinic acid and certain
of its derivatives.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succise</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Succision</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Appearing as if a part
were cut off at the extremity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succision</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*ci"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succisio</ets>, fr. <ets>succidere</ets>, <ets>succisum</ets>, to
cut away below, <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>caedere</ets> to cut.]</ety>
<def>The act of cutting down, as of trees; the act of cutting off.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succor</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Succored</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Succoring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>socouren</ets>, OF. <ets>sucurre</ets>, <ets>soucourre</ets>,
<ets>secorre</ets>, F. <ets>secourir</ets>, L. <ets>succurrere</ets>,
<ets>succursum</ets>, to run under, run to the aid of, help, succor;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>currere</ets> to run. See
<er>Current</er>.]</ety> <def>To run to, or run to support; hence, to
help or relieve when in difficulty, want, or distress; to assist and
deliver from suffering; to relieve; <as>as, to <ex>succor</ex> a
besieged city</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>succour</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is able to <qex>succor</qex> them that are tempted.</q>
<rj><qau>Heb. ii. 18.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To aid; assist; relieve; deliver; help;
comfort.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succor</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cor</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>socours</ets>,
<ets>sucurs</ets>, OF. <ets>sucurs</ets>, <ets>socors</ets>,
<ets>secors</ets>, F. <ets>secours</ets>, L. <ets>succursus</ets>, fr.
L. <ets>succurrere</ets>. See <er>Succor</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Aid; help; assistance; esp., assistance that relieves
and delivers from difficulty, want, or distress.</def> <ldquo/We beseech
mercy and <xex>succor</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My noble father . . . <br/
Flying for <qex>succor</qex> to his servant Bannister.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The person or thing that brings relief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This mighty <qex>succor</qex>, which made glad the foe.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succorable</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cor*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
succored or assisted; admitting of relief.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succorer</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cor*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who affords
succor; a helper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succorless</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cor*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of succor.</def>
<rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succory</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"co*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Corrupted from
<ets>chicory</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A plant of the genus
<gen>Cichorium</gen>. See <er>Chicory</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succotash</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"co*tash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Narragansett
Indian <ets>m'sickquatash</ets> corn boiled whole.]</ety> <def>Green
maize and beans boiled together. The dish is borrowed from the native
Indians.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>suckatash</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succoteague</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`co*teague"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The squeteague.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succuba</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Suc"cu*ba</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Succubae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>succubare</ets> to lie under; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>cubare</ets> to lie down; cf. L. <ets>succuba</ets>,
<ets>succubo</ets>, one who lies under another.]</ety> <def>A female
demon or fiend. See <er>Succubus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though seeming in shape a woman natural<br/
Was a fiend of the kind that <qex>succubae</qex> some call.</q>
<rj><qau>Mir. for Mag.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succubine</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cu*bine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to succuba.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succubous</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cu*bous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Succuba</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the leaves so
placed that the upper part of each one is covered by the base of the
next higher leaf, as in hepatic mosses of the genus
<gen>Plagiochila</gen>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succubus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Suc"cu*bus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Succubi</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See
<er>Succuba</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A demon or fiend; especially,
a lascivious spirit supposed to have sexual intercourse with the men by
night; a succuba. Cf. <er>Incubus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The nightmare. See
<er>Nightmare</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succula</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cu*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sucula</ets> a winch, windlass, capstan.]</ety> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>
<def>A bare axis or cylinder with staves or levers in it to turn it
round, but without any drum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succulency</ent><br/
<ent>Succulence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Suc"cu*lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Suc"cu*len*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Succulent</er>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or condition of being succulent; juiciness; <as>as, the
<ex>succulence</ex> of a peach</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succulent</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cu*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succulentus</ets>, <ets>suculentus</ets>, fr. <ets>succus</ets>,
<ets>sucus</ets>, juice; perhaps akin to E. <ets>suck</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>succulent</ets>.]</ety> <def>Full of juice; juicy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Succulent</b></col><cd> plants <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, plants
which have soft and juicy leaves or stems, as the houseleek, the live
forever, and the species of Mesembryanthemum.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succulently</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cu*lent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a succulent
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succulous</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"cu*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Succulent;
juicy.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succumb</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cumb"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Succumbed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Succumbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>succumbere</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>cumbere</ets> (in
comp.), akin to <ets>cubare</ets> to lie down. See <er>Incumbent</er>,
<er>Cubit</er>.]</ety> <def>To yield; to submit; to give up
unresistingly; <as>as, to <ex>succumb</ex> under calamities; to
<ex>succumb</ex> to disease.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succumbent</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cum"bent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succumbens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Submissive; yielding.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succursal</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cur"sal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>succursale</ets>. See <er>Succor</er>, <pos>n.</pos> & <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Serving to aid or help; serving as a chapel of
ease; tributary.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not a city was without its cathedral, surrounded by its
<qex>succursal</qex> churches, its monasteries, and convents.</q>
<rj><qau>Milman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Suc"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Succi</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The
expressed juice of a plant, for medicinal use.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs>\'d8<col><b>Succus entericus</b></col> <pr>(<?/)</pr>. <ety>[NL.,
literally, juice of the intestines.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <cd>A
fluid secreted in small by certain glands (probably the glands of
Lieberk<uum/hn) of the small intestines. Its exact action is somewhat
doubtful.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succussation</ent><br/
<hw>Suc`cus*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succussare</ets> to jolt, v. intens. fr. <ets>succutere</ets>,
<ets>succussum</ets>, to fling up from below, to toss up; <ets>sub</ets>
under + <ets>quatere</ets> to shake.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A trot or
trotting.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A shaking; succussion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succussion</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cus"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>succussio</ets>, from <ets>succutere</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>succussion</ets>. See <er>Succussation</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
shaking; a shake; esp. <fld>(Med.)</fld>, a shaking of the body to
ascertain if there be a liquid in the thorax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Succussive</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*cus"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Characterized by
a shaking motion, especially an up and down movement, and not merely
tremulous oscillation; <as>as, the <ex>succussive</ex> motion in
earthquakes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Such</ent><br/
<hw>Such</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>such</ets>,
<ets>sich</ets>, <ets>sech</ets>, <ets>sik</ets>, <ets>swich</ets>,
<ets>swilch</ets>, <ets>swulch</ets>, <ets>swilc</ets>,
<ets>swulc</ets>, AS. <ets>swelc</ets>, <ets>swilc</ets>,
<ets>swylc</ets>; akin to OFries. <ets>selik</ets>, D. <ets>zulk</ets>,
OS. <ets>sulic</ets>, OHG. <ets>sulih</ets>, <ets>solih</ets>, G.
<ets>solch</ets>, Icel. <ets>sl<imac/kr</ets>, OSw. <ets>salik</ets>,
Sw. <ets>slik</ets>, Dan. <ets>slig</ets>, Goth. <ets>swaleiks</ets>;
originally meaning, so shaped. <root/192. See <er>So</er>,
<er>Like</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and cf. <er>Which</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Of that kind; of the like kind; like; resembling; similar; <as>as,
we never saw <ex>such</ex> a day</as>; -- followed by <xex>that</xex> or
<xex>as</xex> introducing the word or proposition which defines the
similarity, or the standard of comparison; <as>as, the books are not
<ex>such</ex> that I can recommend them, or, not <ex>such</ex> as I can
recommend; these apples are not <ex>such</ex> as those we saw yesterday;
give your children <ex>such</ex> precepts as tend to make them
better</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And in his time <qex>such</qex> a conqueror<br/
That greater was there none under the sun.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His misery was <qex>such</qex> that none of the bystanders could
refrain from weeping.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The indefinite article <xex>a</xex> or <xex>an</xex>
never precedes <xex>such</xex>, but is placed between it and the noun to
which it refers; as, <xex>such a</xex> man; <xex>such an</xex> honor.
The indefinite adjective <xex>some</xex>, <xex>several</xex>,
<xex>one</xex>, <xex>few</xex>, <xex>many</xex>, <xex>all</xex>, etc.,
precede <xex>such</xex>; as, one <xex>such</xex> book is enough; all
<xex>such</xex> people ought to be avoided; few <xex>such</xex> ideas
were then held.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Having the particular quality or character
specified.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That thou art happy, owe to God;<br/
That thou continuest <qex>such</qex>, owe to thyself.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The same that; -- with <xex>as</xex>; <as>as, this
was the state of the kingdom at <ex>such</ex> time as the enemy
landed</as>.</def> <ldquo/[It] hath <xex>such</xex> senses as we
have.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Certain; -- representing the object as already
particularized in terms which are not mentioned.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In rushed one and tells him <qex>such</qex> a knight<br/
Is new arrived.</q> <rj><qau>Daniel.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To-day or to-morrow we will go into <qex>such</qex> a city, and
continue there a year.</q> <rj><qau>James iv. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<note><p><hand/ <xex>Such</xex> is used pronominally. <ldquo/He was the
father of <xex>such</xex> as dwell in tents.<rdquo/ <au>Gen. iv.
20.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Such</xex> as I are free in spirit when our limbs
are chained.<rdquo/ <au>Sir W. Scott.</au> <xex>Such</xex> is also used
before adjectives joined to substantives; as, the fleet encountered
<xex>such</xex> a terrible storm that it put back. <ldquo/Everything was
managed with so much care, and <xex>such</xex> excellent order was
observed.<rdquo/ <au>De Foe.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Temple sprung from a family which . . . long after his death
produced so many eminent men, and formed <qex>such</qex> distinguished
alliances, that, etc.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><xex>Such</xex> is used emphatically, without the correlative.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now will he be mocking:<br/
I shall have <qex>such</qex> a life.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><xex>Such</xex> was formerly used with numerals in the sense of
<xex>times as much</xex> or <xex>as many</xex>; as, <xex>such ten</xex>,
or ten times as many.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></note>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Such and such</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Such
or such</b></col></mcol>, <cd>certain; some; -- used to represent the
object indefinitely, as already particularized in one way or another, or
as being of one kind or another.</cd> <ldquo/In <xex>such and such</xex>
a place shall be my camp.<rdquo/ <au>2 Kings vi. 8.</au>
<ldquo/Sovereign authority may enact a law commanding <xex>such and
such</xex> an action.<rdquo/ <au>South.</au> -- <mcol><col><b>Such
like</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Such character</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>of the like kind.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And many other <qex>such like</qex> things ye do.</q>
<rj><qau>Mark vii. 8.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suchospondylous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`cho*spon"dy*lous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
crocodile + <?/ a vertebra.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having dorsal
vertebrae with long and divided transverse processes; -- applied to
certain reptiles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suchwise</ent><br/
<hw>Such"wise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a such a
manner; so.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suck</ent><br/
<hw>Suck</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/k)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sucked</conjf> <pr>(s<ucr/kt)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sucking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>suken</ets>,
<ets>souken</ets>, AS. <ets>s<umac/can</ets>, <ets>s<umac/gan</ets>;
akin to D. <ets>zuigen</ets>, G. <ets>saugen</ets>, OHG.
<ets>s<umac/gan</ets>, Icel. <ets>s<umac/ga</ets>,
<ets>sj<umac/ga</ets>, Sw. <ets>suga</ets>, Dan. <ets>suge</ets>, L.
<ets>sugere</ets>. Cf. <er>Honeysuckle</er>, <er>Soak</er>,
<er>Succulent</er>, <er>Suction</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To draw,
as a liquid, by the action of the mouth and tongue, which tends to
produce a vacuum, and causes the liquid to rush in by atmospheric
pressure; to draw, or apply force to, by exhausting the air.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw liquid from by the action of the mouth;
<as>as, to <ex>suck</ex> an orange</as>; specifically, to draw milk from
(the mother, the breast, etc.) with the mouth; <as>as, the young of an
animal <ex>sucks</ex> the mother, or dam; an infant <ex>sucks</ex> the
breast</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To draw in, or imbibe, by any process resembles
sucking; to inhale; to absorb; <as>as, to <ex>suck</ex> in air; the
roots of plants <ex>suck</ex> water from the ground</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To draw or drain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Old ocean, <qex>sucked</qex> through the porous globe.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To draw in, as a whirlpool; to swallow up.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As waters are by whirlpools <qex>sucked</qex> and drawn.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To suck in</b></col>, <cd>to draw into the mouth; to
imbibe; to absorb.</cd> -- <col><b>To suck out</b></col>, <cd>to draw
out with the mouth; to empty by suction.</cd> -- <col><b>To suck
up</b></col>, <cd>to draw into the mouth; to draw up by suction or
absorption.</cd></cs> <-- suck up (b) to be obsequious; to act as a
toady. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suck</ent><br/
<hw>Suck</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To draw, or attempt to
draw, something by suction, as with the mouth, or through a
tube.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Where the bee <qex>sucks</qex>, there <qex>suck</qex> I.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw milk from the breast or udder; <as>as, a
child, or the young of an animal, is first nourished by
<ex>sucking</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To draw in; to imbibe; to partake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The crown had <qex>sucked</qex> too hard, and now, being full, was
like to draw less.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be objectionable, of very poor quality, or
offensive; <as>as, telemarketing calls really <ex>suck</ex>; he's a good
actor, but his singing <ex>sucks</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suck</ent><br/
<hw>Suck</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of drawing with
the mouth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is drawn into the mouth by sucking;
specifically, mikl drawn from the breast.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small draught.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Juice; succulence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suckanhock</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"an*hock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of American
Indian origin.]</ety> <def>A kind of seawan. See Note under
<er>Seawan</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suckatash</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"a*tash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Succotash</er>.</def> <rj><au>Bartlett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucken</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Socome</er>,
<er>Soc</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld> <def>The jurisdiction of a
mill, or that extent of ground astricted to it, the tenants of which are
bound to bring their grain thither to be ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1440 --></p>

<p><ent>Sucker</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"er</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/k"<etil/r)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One who, or that which, sucks; esp., one of the organs by which
certain animals, as the octopus and remora, adhere to other
bodies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A suckling; a sucking animal.</def> <rj><au>Beau. &
Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The embolus, or bucket, of a pump; also, the valve
of a pump basket.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A pipe through which anything is drawn.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A small piece of leather, usually round, having a
string attached to the center, which, when saturated with water and
pressed upon a stone or other body having a smooth surface, adheres, by
reason of the atmospheric pressure, with such force as to enable a
considerable weight to be thus lifted by the string; -- used by children
as a plaything.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A shoot from the roots or lower
part of the stem of a plant; -- so called, perhaps, from diverting
nourishment from the body of the plant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of numerous
species of North American fresh-water cyprinoid fishes of the family
<fam>Catostomidae</fam>; so called because the lips are protrusile. The
flesh is coarse, and they are of little value as food. The most common
species of the Eastern United States are the northern sucker
(<spn>Catostomus Commersoni</spn>), the white sucker (<spn>Catostomus
teres</spn>), the hog sucker (<spn>Catostomus nigricans</spn>), and the
chub, or sweet sucker (<spn>Erimyzon sucetta</spn>). Some of the large
Western species are called <stype>buffalo fish</stype>, <stype>red
horse</stype>, <stype>black horse</stype>, and
<stype>suckerel</stype>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The remora.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>The lumpfish.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The hagfish, or
myxine.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>A California food fish (<spn>Menticirrus
undulatus</spn>) closely allied to the kingfish <sd>(a)</sd>; -- called
also <altname>bagre</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>A parasite; a sponger. See def. 6, above.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They who constantly converse with men far above their estates
shall reap shame and loss thereby; if thou payest nothing, they will
count thee a <qex>sucker</qex>, no branch.</q>
<rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>A hard drinker; a soaker.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A greenhorn; someone easily cheated, gulled, or
deceived.</def> <mark>[Slang, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>A nickname applied to a native of Illinois.</def>
<mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>A person strongly attracted to something; --
usually used with <ptcl>for</ptcl>; <as>as, he's a <ex>sucker</ex> for
tall blondes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>Any thing or person; -- usually implying annoyance
or dislike; <as>as, I went to change the blade and cut my finger on the
<ex>sucker</ex></as>.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Carp sucker</b></col>, <col><b>Cherry
sucker</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Carp</er>,
<er>Cherry</er>, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Sucker fish</b></col>. <cd>See
<cref>Sucking fish</cref>, under <er>Sucking</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sucker rod</b></col>, <cd>a pump rod. See under
<er>Pump</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sucker tube</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>one of the external ambulacral tubes of an echinoderm, -- usually
terminated by a sucker and used for locomotion. Called also
<altname>sucker foot</altname>. See <er>Spatangoid</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucker</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Suckered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suckering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strip
off the suckers or shoots from; to deprive of suckers; <as>as, to
<ex>sucker</ex> maize</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cheat or deceive (a gullible person); to make a
sucker of (someone).</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucker</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"er</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To form suckers; <as>as, corn
<ex>suckers</ex> abundantly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucker State</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"er State</hw>. <def>Illinois; -- a nickname.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucket</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Suck</er>,
<pos>v. t.</pos>, <er>Succades</er>.]</ety> <def>A sweetmeat; a dainty
morsel.</def> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>suck face</ent><br/
<hw>suck face</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To engage in french kissing
(soul-kissing).</def> <mark>[slang]</mark><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suckfish</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A sucker fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucking</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Drawing milk from the mother or
dam; hence, colloquially, young, inexperienced, <as>as, a
<ex>sucking</ex> infant; a <ex>sucking</ex> calf</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I suppose you are a young barrister, <qex>sucking</qex> lawyer, or
that sort of thing.</q> <rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sucking bottle</b></col>, <cd>a feeding bottle. See under
<er>Bottle</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sucking fish</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the remora. See <er>Remora</er>.</cd>
<au>Baird.</au> -- <col><b>Sucking pump</b></col>, <cd>a suction pump.
See under <er>Suction</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sucking stomach</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the muscular first stomach of certain insects
and other invertebrates which suck liquid food.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suckle</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"kle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A teat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suckle</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"kle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Suckled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Suckling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>suck</ets>.]</ety> <def>To give suck to; to nurse at the
breast.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The breasts of Hecuba<br/
When she did <qex>suckle</qex> Hector, looked not lovelier.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are not weak, <qex>suckled</qex> by Wisdom.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suckle</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"kle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To nurse; to suck.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suckler</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"kler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
animal that suckles its young; a mammal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suckling</ent><br/
<hw>Suck"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sokeling</ets>. See <er>Suck</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A young child or animal nursed at the breast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A small kind of yellow clover (<spn>Trifolium
filiforme</spn>) common in Southern Europe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucrate</ent><br/
<hw>Su"crate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
compound of sucrose (or of some related carbohydrate) with some base,
after the analogy of a salt; <as>as, sodium
<ex>sucrate</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucre</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"cre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A silver coin of
Ecuador, worth 68 cents (ca. 1900).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sucrose</ent><br/
<hw>Su"crose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sucre</ets>
sugar. See <er>Sugar</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A common
variety of sugar found in the juices of many plants, as the sugar cane,
sorghum, sugar maple, beet root, etc. It is extracted as a sweet, white
crystalline substance which is valuable as a food product, and, being
antiputrescent, is largely used in the preservation of fruit. Called
also <altname>saccharose</altname>, <altname>cane sugar</altname>, etc.
At one time the term was used by extension, for any one of the class of
isomeric substances (as <xex>lactose</xex>, <xex>maltose</xex>, etc.) of
which sucrose proper is the type; however this usage is now
archaic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Sucrose proper is a dextrorotatory carbohydrate,
<chform>C12H22O11</chform>. It does not reduce Fehling's solution, and
though not directly fermentable, yet on standing with yeast it is
changed by the diastase present to invert sugar (<xex>dextrose</xex> and
<xex>levulose</xex>), which then breaks down to alcohol and carbon
dioxide. It is also decomposed to invert sugar by heating with acids,
whence it is also called a <altname>disaccharate</altname><--
disaccharide-->. Sucrose possesses at once the properties of an alcohol
and a ketone, and also forms compounds (called <xex>sucrates</xex>)
analogous to salts. Cf. <er>Sugar</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suction</ent><br/
<hw>Suc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sugere</ets>, <ets>suctum</ets>, to suck; cf. OF.
<ets>suction</ets>. See <er>Suck</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>The
act or process of sucking; the act of drawing, as fluids, by exhausting
the air.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Suction chamber</b></col>, <cd>the chamber of a pump into
which the suction pipe delivers.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Suction
pipe</b></col>, <col><b>Suction valve</b></col></mcol>, <cd>the
induction pipe, and induction valve, of a pump, respectively.</cd> --
<col><b>Suction pump</b></col>, <cd>the common pump, in which the water
is raised into the barrel by atmospheric pressure. See
<xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Pump</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suctoria</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Suc*to"ri*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Suction</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An order of
Infusoria having the body armed with somewhat stiff, tubular processes
which they use as suckers in obtaining their food. They are usually
stalked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Rhizocephala</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suctorial</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*to"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sugere</ets>, <ets>suctum</ets>, to suck.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Adapted for sucking; living by sucking; <as>as,
the humming birds are <ex>suctorial</ex> birds</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Capable of adhering by suction;
<as>as, the <ex>suctorial</ex> fishes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suctorian</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*to"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A cartilaginous fish with a mouth adapted for
suction, as the lampery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the Suctoria.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suctorious</ent><br/
<hw>Suc*to"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suctorial.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudamina</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su*dam"i*na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>, <sing>sing.
<singw>Sudamen</singw> <pr>(<?/)</pr></sing>. <ety>[NL.
<ets>sudamen</ets>, <ets>-inis</ets>, fr. <ets>sudare</ets> to sweat.
See <er>Sweat</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Minute vesicles
surrounded by an area of reddened skin, produced by excessive
sweating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudarium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su*da"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., a
handkerchief.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The handkerchief upon which
the Savior is said to have impressed his own portrait miraculously, when
wiping his face with it, as he passed to the crucifixion.</def><-- =
Veronica's veil. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudary</ent><br/
<hw>Su"da*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudarium</ets>, fr. <ets>sudare</ets> to sweat. See
<er>Sweat</er>.]</ety> <def>A napkin or handkerchief.</def> <mark>[Obs.
or R.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif. R. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudation</ent><br/
<hw>Su*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudatio</ets>, fr. <ets>sudare</ets> to sweat: cf. F.
<ets>sudation</ets>.]</ety> <def>A sweating.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudatorium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su`da*to"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sudatoria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <def>A sudatory.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudatory</ent><br/
<hw>Su"da*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudatorius</ets>, fr. <ets>sudare</ets> to sweat: cf. F.
<ets>sudatoire</ets>. See <er>Sweat</er>.]</ety> <def>Sweating;
perspiring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudatory</ent><br/
<hw>Su"da*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sudatories</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudatorium</ets>.]</ety> <def>A bagnio; a sweating bath; a vapor
bath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These <qex>sudatories</qex> are much in request for many
infirmities.</q> <rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudd</ent><br/
<hw>Sudd</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sadd</ets> barrier.]</ety> <def>A tangled mass of floating vegetal
matter obstructing navigation.</def> <mark>[Central Africa]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudden</ent><br/
<hw>Sud"den</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sodian</ets>, <ets>sodein</ets>, OF. <ets>sodain</ets>,
<ets>sudain</ets>, F. <ets>soudain</ets>, L. <ets>subitaneus</ets>, fr.
<ets>subitus</ets> sudden, that has come unexpectedly, p. p. of
<ets>subire</ets> to come on, to steal upon; <ets>sub</ets> under,
secretly + <ets>ire</ets> to go. See <er>Issue</er>, and cf.
<er>Subitaneous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Happening without previous
notice or with very brief notice; coming unexpectedly, or without the
common preparation; immediate; instant; speedy.</def> <ldquo/O
<xex>sudden</xex> wo!<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au> <ldquo/For fear of
<xex>sudden</xex> death.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sudden</qex> fear troubleth thee.</q> <rj><qau>Job xxii.
10.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hastly prepared or employed; quick; rapid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Never was such a <qex>sudden</qex> scholar made.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The apples of Asphaltis, appearing goodly to the <qex>sudden</qex>
eye.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hasty; violent; rash; precipitate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Unexpected; unusual; abrupt;
unlooked-for.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sud"den*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sud"den*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudden</ent><br/
<hw>Sud"den</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Suddenly; unexpectedly.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Herbs of every leaf that <qex>sudden</qex> flowered.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudden</ent><br/
<hw>Sud"den</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An unexpected occurrence; a
surprise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>All of a sudden</b></col>, <col><b>On a
sudden</b></col>, <col><b>Of a sudden</b></col></mcol>, <cd>sooner than
was expected; without the usual preparation; suddenly.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How art thou lost! how <qex>on a sudden</qex> lost!</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He withdrew his opposition <qex>all of a sudden</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suddenty</ent><br/
<hw>Sud"den*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>soudainet<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Suddenness; a sudden.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>On a suddenty</b></col>, <cd>on a sudden.</cd>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <au>Sir W. Scott.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudoral</ent><br/
<hw>Su"dor*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudor</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to sweat; <as>as,
<ex>sudoral</ex> eruptions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudoriferous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`dor*if"er*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudor</ets> sweat + <ets>-ferous</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Producing, or secreting, sweat;
sudoriparous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sudoriferous glands</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>,
<cd>small convoluted tubular glands which are situated in the
subcutaneous tissues and discharge by minute orifices in the surface of
the skin; the sweat glands.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudorific</ent><br/
<hw>Su`dor*if"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudor</ets> sweat (akin to E. <ets>sweat</ets>) + <ets>facere</ets>
to make.]</ety> <def>Causing sweat; <as>as, <ex>sudorific</ex>
herbs</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A sudorific medicine. Cf.
<er>Diaphoretic</er>.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudoriparous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`dor*ip"a*rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudor</ets> sweat + <ets>parere</ets> to produce.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Sudoriferous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudorous</ent><br/
<hw>Su"dor*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sudorus</ets>, fr. <ets>sudor</ets> sweat.]</ety> <def>Consisting
of sweat.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sudra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"dra</hw> <pr>(s<oomac/"dr<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Skr. <ets><cced/<umac/dra</ets>.]</ety> <def>The lowest of the
four great castes among the Hindoos. See <er>Caste</er>.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>Soorah</asp>, <asp>Soodra</asp>, and
<asp>Sooder</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suds</ent><br/
<hw>Suds</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[Akin to
<ets>sodden</ets>, <ets>seethe</ets>. See <er>Seethe</er>.]</ety>
<def>Water impregnated with soap, esp. when worked up into bubbles and
froth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In the suds</b></col>, <cd>in turmoil or difficulty.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <au>Beau. & Fl.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sue</ent><br/
<hw>Sue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sued</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Suing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>suen</ets>,
<ets>sewen</ets>, <ets>siwen</ets>, OF. <ets>sivre</ets> (pres.ind. 3d
sing. il <ets>siut</ets>, <ets>suit</ets>, he follows, nous
<ets>sevons</ets> we follow), LL. <ets>sequere</ets>, for L.
<ets>sequi</ets>, <ets>secutus</ets>; akin to Gr. <?/, Skr.
<ets>sac</ets> to accompany, and probably to E. <ets>see</ets>, v.t. See
<er>See</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Consequence</er>,
<er>Ensue</er>, <er>Execute</er>, <er>Obsequious</er>, <er>Pursue</er>,
<er>Second</er>, <er>Sect</er> in religion, <er>Sequence</er>,
<er>Suit</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To follow up; to chase; to seek
after; to endeavor to win; to woo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For yet there was no man that haddle him <qex>sued</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I was beloved of many a gentle knight,<br/
And <qex>sued</qex> and sought with all the service due.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sue</qex> me, and woo me, and flatter me.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To seek justice or
right from, by legal process; to institute process in law against; to
bring an action against; to prosecute judicially.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>To proceed with, as an action, and follow it up to its proper
termination; to gain by legal process.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Falconry)</fld> <def>To clean, as the beak; -- said
of a hawk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To leave high and dry on shore;
<as>as, to <ex>sue</ex> a ship</as>.</def> <rj><au>R. H. Dana,
Jr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sue out</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>to petition
for and take out, or to apply for and obtain; <as>as, <ex>to sue
out</ex> a writ in chancery; <ex>to sue out</ex> a pardon for a
criminal</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sue</ent><br/
<hw>Sue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To seek by
request; to make application; to petition; to entreat; to
plead.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>By adverse destiny constrained to <qex>sue</qex><br/
For counsel and redress, he <qex>sues</qex> to you.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Caesar came to Rome to <qex>sue</qex> for the double honor of a
triumph and the consulship.</q> <rj><qau>C. Middleton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Indians were defeated and <qex>sued</qex> for peace.</q>
<rj><qau>Jefferson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To prosecute; to make legal claim;
to seek (for something) in law; <as>as, to <ex>sue</ex> for
damages</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To woo; to pay addresses as a lover.</def>
<rj><au>Massinger.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To be left high and dry on the
shore, as a ship.</def> <rj><au>R. H. Dana, Jr.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suede</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>Suede</hw>, <hw>Su<egrave/de</hw></mhw> <pr>(sw<amac/d,
sw<asl/d, <it>or</it> sw<acir/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.,
Sweden.]</ety> <def>Swedish glove leather, -- usually made from
lambskins tanned with willow bark. Also used adjectively; <as>as,
<ex>suede</ex> gloves; blue <ex>suede</ex> shoes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> + <source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suent</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Uniformly or evenly
distributed or spread; even; smooth. See <er>Suant</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Thoreau.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suently</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ent*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Evenly; smoothly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suer</ent><br/
<hw>Su"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sues; a
suitor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suet</ent><br/
<hw>Su"et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>suet</ets>,
dim. fr. OF. <ets>seu</ets>, <ets>suif</ets>, F. <ets>suif</ets>, L.
<ets>sebum</ets>. Cf. <er>Soap</er>, <er>Sebaceous</er>.]</ety> <def>The
fat and fatty tissues of an animal, especially the harder fat about the
kidneys and loins in beef and mutton, which, when melted and freed from
the membranes, forms tallow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suety</ent><br/
<hw>Su"et*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of, or
resembling, suet; <as>as, a <ex>suety</ex> substance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suf-</ent><br/
<hw>Suf-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A form of the prefix
<er>Sub-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffer</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Suffered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suffering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>suffren</ets>, <ets>soffren</ets>, OF. <ets>sufrir</ets>,
<ets>sofrir</ets>, F. <ets>souffrir</ets>, (assumed) LL.
<ets>sofferire</ets>, for L. <ets>sufferre</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>ferre</ets> to bear, akin to E. <ets>bear</ets>. See <er>Bear</er>
to support.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To feel, or endure, with pain,
annoyance, etc.; to submit to with distress or grief; to undergo;
<as>as, to <ex>suffer</ex> pain of body, or grief of
mind</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To endure or undergo without sinking; to support; to
sustain; to bear up under.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Our spirit and strength entire,<br/
Strongly to <qex>suffer</qex> and support our pains.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To undergo; to be affected by; to sustain; to
experience; <as>as, most substances <ex>suffer</ex> a change when long
exposed to air and moisture; to <ex>suffer</ex> loss or
damage.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If your more ponderous and settled project<br/
May <qex>suffer</qex> alteration.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To allow; to permit; not to forbid or hinder; to
tolerate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou shalt in any wise rebuke thy neighbour, and not
<qex>suffer</qex> sin upon him.</q> <rj><qau>Lev. xix.
17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>suffer</qex> them to enter and possess.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To permit; bear; endure; support; sustain; allow;
admit; tolerate. See <er>Permit</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffer</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fer</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To feel or undergo
pain of body or mind; to bear what is inconvenient; <as>as, we
<ex>suffer</ex> from pain, sickness, or sorrow; we <ex>suffer</ex> with
anxiety</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>O well for him whose will is strong!<br/
He <qex>suffers</qex>, but he will not <qex>suffer</qex> long.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To undergo punishment; specifically, to undergo the
penalty of death.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The father was first condemned to <qex>suffer</qex> upon a day
appointed, and the son afterwards the day following.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be injured; to sustain loss or damage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Public business <qex>suffers</qex> by private infirmities.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufferable</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fer*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>souffrable</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Able to suffer or endure;
patient.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Ye must be
<xex>sufferable</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That may be suffered, tolerated, or permitted;
allowable; tolerable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Suf"fer*a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Suf"fer*a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufferance</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fer*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>suffrance</ets>, OF. <ets>sufrance</ets>, <ets>soufrance</ets>, F.
<ets>souffrance</ets>, L. <ets>sufferentia</ets>, from
<ets>sufferens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>sufferre</ets>.
See <er>Suffer</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of suffering; the
bearing of pain; endurance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He must not only die the death,<br/
But thy unkindness shall his death draw out<br/
To lingering <qex>sufferance</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pain endured; misery; suffering; distress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The seeming <qex>sufferances</qex> that you had borne.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Loss; damage; injury.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A grievous . . . <qex>sufferance</qex> on most part of their
fleet.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Submission under difficult or oppressive
circumstances; patience; moderation.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But hasty heat tempering with <qex>sufferance</qex> wise.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Negative consent by not forbidding or hindering;
toleration; permission; allowance; leave.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In their beginning they are weak and wan,<br/
But soon, through <qex>sufferance</qex>, grow to fearful end.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Somewhiles by <qex>sufferance</qex>, and somewhiles by special
leave and favor, they erected to themselves oratories.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A permission granted by the customs authorities for
the shipment of goods.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Estate of sufferance</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the
holding by a tenant who came in by a lawful title, but remains, after
his right has expired, without positive leave of the owner.</cd>
<au>Blackstone.</au> -- <col><b>On sufferance</b></col>, <cd>by mere
toleration; <as>as, to remain in a house <ex>on
sufferance</ex></as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Endurance; pain; misery; inconvenience; patience;
moderation; toleration; permission.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufferer</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fer*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
suffers; one who endures or undergoes suffering; one who sustains
inconvenience or loss; <as>as, <ex>sufferers</ex> by poverty or
sickness; men are <ex>sufferers</ex> by fire or by losses at
sea.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who permits or allows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffering</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fer*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The bearing of pain,
inconvenience, or loss; pain endured; distress, loss, or injury
incurred; <as>as, <ex>sufferings</ex> by pain or sorrow;
<ex>sufferings</ex> by want or by wrongs.</as></def> <ldquo/Souls in
<xex>sufferings</xex> tried.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Keble.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffering</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fer*ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being in pain or grief; having
loss, injury, distress, etc.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Suf"fer*ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1441 --></p>

<p><ent>Suffice</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fice"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sufficed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sufficing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>suffisen</ets>, OF. <ets>soufire</ets>, F. <ets>suffire</ets> (cf.
<ets>suffisant</ets>, p. pr.), L. <ets>sufficere</ets> to put under, to
substitute, to avail for, to suffice; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>facere</ets> to make. See <er>Fact</er>.]</ety> <def>To be enough,
or sufficient; to meet the need (of anything); to be equal to the end
proposed; to be adequate.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To recount almighty works,<br/
What words or tongue of seraph can <qex>suffice</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffice</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fice"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To satisfy; to
content; to be equal to the wants or demands of.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let it <qex>suffice</qex> thee; speak no more unto me of this
matter.</q> <rj><qau>Deut. iii. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To furnish; to supply adequately.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The power appeased, with winds <qex>sufficed</qex> the sail.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufficience</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fi"cience</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sufficiently.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufficiency</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fi"cien*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sufficientia</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suffisance</ets>. See
<er>Suffice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being
sufficient, or adequate to the end proposed; adequacy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His <qex>sufficiency</qex> is such that he bestows and possesses,
his plenty being unexhausted.</q> <rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Qualification for any purpose; ability;
capacity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A substitute or most allowed <qex>sufficiency</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am not so confident of my own <qex>sufficiency</qex> as not
willingly to admit the counsel of others.</q> <rj><qau>Eikon
Basilike.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Adequate substance or means; competence.</def>
<ldquo/An elegant <xex>sufficiency</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Supply equal to wants; ample stock or
fund.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Conceit; self-confidence;
self-sufficiency.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sufficiency</qex> is a compound of vanity and ignorance.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir W. Temple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufficient</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fi"cient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sufficiens</ets>, <ets>-entis</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>sufficere</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suffisant</ets>. See
<er>Suffice</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Equal to the end proposed;
adequate to wants; enough; ample; competent; <as>as, provision
<ex>sufficient</ex> for the family; an army <ex>sufficient</ex> to
defend the country</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My grace is <qex>sufficient</qex> for thee.</q> <rj><qau>2 Cor.
xii. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Possessing adequate talents or accomplishments; of
competent power or ability; qualified; fit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who is <qex>sufficient</qex> for these things?</q> <rj><qau>2 Cor.
ii. 16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Capable of meeting obligations;
responsible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The man is, notwithstanding, <qex>sufficient</qex> . . . I think I
may take his bond.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Self-sufficient; self-satisfied; content.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thou art the most <qex>sufficient</qex> (I'll say for thee),<br/
Not to believe a thing.</q> <rj><qau>Beau. & Fl.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Enough; adequate; competent; full; satisfactory;
ample.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufficiently</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fi"cient*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>To a sufficient degree;
to a degree that answers the purpose, or gives content; enough; <as>as,
we are <ex>sufficiently</ex> supplied with food; a man
<ex>sufficiently</ex> qualified for the discharge of his official
duties</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufficing</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fi"cing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Affording enough;
satisfying.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Suf*fi"cing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-- <wf>Suf*fi"cing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffisance</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fi"sance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Sufficiency</er>.]</ety> <def>Sufficiency; plenty; abundance;
contentment.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He could in little thing have <qex>suffisaunce</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffisant</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fi"sant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sufficient.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffix</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fix</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffixus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suffigere</ets> to fasten on, to
affix; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>figere</ets> to fix: cf. F.
<ets>suffixe</ets>. See <er>Fix</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A letter,
letters, syllable, or syllables added or appended to the end of a word
or a root to modify the meaning; a postfix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>A subscript mark, number, or
letter. See <er>Subscript</er>, <pos>a.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffix</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fix"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Suffixed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suffixing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To add or annex to the
end, as a letter or syllable to a word; to append.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffixion</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fix"ion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
suffixing, or the state of being suffixed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffixment</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fix"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Suffixion.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Earle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufflaminate</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*flam"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sufflaminatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sufflaminare</ets> to hold back
by a clog, from <ets>sufflamen</ets> a clog.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
retard the motion of, as a carriage, by preventing one or more of its
wheels from revolving, either by means of a chain or otherwise.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to stop; to impede.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufflate</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*flate"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sufflatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>sufflare</ets> to blow up, inflate;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>flare</ets> to blow.]</ety> <def>To blow up;
to inflate; to inspire.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>T.
Ward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufflation</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fla"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sufflatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of blowing up or
inflating.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Coles.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffocate</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fo*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffocatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suffocare</ets> to choke;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>fauces</ets> the throat. Cf.
<er>Faucal</er>.]</ety> <def>Suffocated; choked.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffocate</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fo*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suffocated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suffocating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To choke
or kill by stopping respiration; to stifle; to smother.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let not hemp his windpipe <qex>suffocate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To destroy; to extinguish; <as>as, to
<ex>suffocate</ex> fire</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffocate</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fo*cate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become choked, stifled,
or smothered.</def> <ldquo/A swelling discontent is apt to
<xex>suffocate</xex> and strangle without passage.<rdquo/
<rj><au>collier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffocating</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fo*ca`ting</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Suffocate</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Suf"fo*ca`ting*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffocation</ent><br/
<hw>Suf`fo*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffocatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suffocation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
act of suffocating, or the state of being suffocated; death caused by
smothering or choking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <xex>suffocation</xex> is sometimes employed
synonymously with <xex>asphyxia</xex>. In the strict medico-legal sense
it signifies <xex>asphyxia</xex> induced by obstruction of the
respiration otherwise than by direct pressure on the neck (hanging,
strangulation) or submersion (drowning).</note>
<rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffocative</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fo*ca*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending or able
to choke or stifle.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Suffocative</xex> catarrhs.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffossion</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fos"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffossio</ets>, from <ets>suffodere</ets>, <ets>suffossum</ets>,
to dig under; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>fodere</ets> to dig.]</ety>
<def>A digging under; an undermining.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffragan</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fra*gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>suffragant</ets>, L. <ets>suffragans</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>suffragari</ets> to support with one's vote, to be favorable. See
<er>Suffrage</er>.]</ety> <def>Assisting; assistant; <as>as, a
<ex>suffragan</ex> bishop</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffragan</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fra*gan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>suffragant</ets>: cf. LL. <ets>suffraganeus</ets>. See
<er>Suffragan</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
assistant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A bishop considered as an
assistant, or as subject, to his metropolitan; an assistant
bishop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffraganship</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fra*gan*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
suffragan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffragant</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fra*gant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & n.</pos>
<def>Suffragan.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffragate</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fra*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suffragated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Suffragating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>suffragatus</ets>,
p. p. of <ets>suffragari</ets>. See <er>Suffragan</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>To vote or vote with.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Suffragating</xex> tribes.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffragator</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fra*ga`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who assists or favors by his vote.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffrage</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"frage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr. L.
<ets>suffragium</ets>; perhaps originally, a broken piece, a potsherd,
used in voting, and fr. <ets>sub</ets> under + the root of
<ets>frangere</ets> to break. See <er>Break</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>A vote given in deciding a controverted question, or in the choice
of a man for an office or trust; the formal expression of an opinion;
assent; vote.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I ask your voices and your <qex>suffrages</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Testimony; attestation; witness; approval.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lactantius and St. Austin confirm by their <qex>suffrage</qex> the
observation made by heathen writers.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every miracle is the <qex>suffrage</qex> of Heaven to the truth of
a doctrine.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A short petition, as
those after the creed in matins and evensong.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
prayer in general, as one offered for the faithful departed.</def>
<rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I firmly believe that there is a purgatory, and that the souls
therein detained are helped by the <qex>suffrages</qex> of the
faithful.</q> <rj><qau>Creed of Pope Pius IV.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Aid; assistance.</def> <mark>[A Latinism]</mark>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>The right to vote; franchise.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffrage</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"frage</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To vote for; to elect.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffragette</ent><br/
<hw>Suf`fra*gette"</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who advocates the
right to vote for women; a woman suffragist.</def> <note>This term was
applied mostly to women in the United States prior to the adoption of
the 19th amendment to the constitution in 1920, giving women the right
to vote. Modern use of this term usually refers to the women who
advocated female suffrage{5} in the years prior to 1920.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffraginous</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*frag"i*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffraginosus</ets> diseased in the hock, fr. <ets>suffrago</ets>
the pastern, or hock.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the hock of a
beast.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffragist</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"fra*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who possesses or exercises the political right of suffrage; a
voter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who has certain opinions or desires about the
political right of suffrage; <as>as, a woman
<ex>suffragist</ex></as>.</def><-- if female, usu. suffragette. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is curious that . . . Louisa Castelefort should be obliged
after her marriage immediately to open her doors and turn ultra liberal,
or an universal <qex>suffragist</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Miss
Edgeworth.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffrago</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Suf*fra"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., the hock,
from <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>frangere</ets> to break.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The heel joint.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffrance</ent><br/
<hw>Suf"france</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sufferance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffrutescent</ent><br/
<hw>Suf`fru*tes"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>suf-</ets> + <ets>frutescent</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Slightly woody at the base.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffruticose</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fru"ti*cose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>suf-</ets> + <ets>fruticose</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Woody in the lower part of the stem, but with the yearly branches
herbaceous, as sage, thyme, hyssop, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffruticous</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fru"ti*cous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Suffruticose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffumigate</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fu"mi*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suffumigated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Suffumigating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffumigatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suffumigare</ets> to fumigate
from below. See <er>Sub-</er>, and <er>Fumigate</er>.]</ety> <def>To
apply fumes or smoke to the parts of, as to the body in medicine; to
fumigate in part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffumigation</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fu`mi*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffumigatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suffumigation</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The operation of suffumigating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffumige</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fu"mige</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>suffumigium</ets>.]</ety> <def>A medical fume.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Harvey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffuse</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suffused</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suffusing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffusus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suffundere</ets> to overspread;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>fundere</ets> to pour. See <er>Fuse</er> to
melt.]</ety> <def>To overspread, as with a fluid or tincture; to fill or
cover, as with something fluid; <as>as, eyes <ex>suffused</ex> with
tears; cheeks <ex>suffused</ex> with blushes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When purple light shall next <qex>suffuse</qex> the skies.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suffusion</ent><br/
<hw>Suf*fu"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suffusio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suffusion</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or process of suffusing, or state of being suffused; an
overspreading.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To those that have the jaundice, or like <qex>suffusion</qex> of
eyes, objects appear of that color.</q> <rj><qau>Ray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That with which a thing is suffused.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A blending of one color into
another; the spreading of one color over another, as on the feathers of
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufi</ent><br/
<hw>Su"fi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From the name of a
dynasty of Persian kings, <ets>Saf<imac/</ets>, <ets>Safav<imac/</ets>;
said to come from name <ets>Saf<imac/-ud-d<imac/n</ets> of an ancestor
of the family, confused with <ets>s<?/f<imac/</ets> pious.]</ety> <def>A
title or surname of the king of Persia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufi</ent><br/
<hw>Su"fi</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar. & Per. <ets>s<?/f<imac/</ets>,
wise, pious, devout.]</ety> <def>One of a certain order of religious men
in Persia.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sofi</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sufism</ent><br/
<hw>Su"fism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A refined mysticism
among certain classes of Mohammedans, particularly in Persia, who hold
to a kind of pantheism and practice extreme asceticism in their
lives.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sofism</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sug</ent><br/
<hw>Sug</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of worm or
larva.</def> <rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugar</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sugre</ets>,
F. <ets>sucre</ets> (cf. It. <ets>zucchero</ets>, Sp.
<ets>az<uacute/car</ets>), fr. Ar. <ets>sukkar</ets>,
<ets>assukkar</ets>, fr. Skr. <ets><cced/arkar<amac/</ets> sugar,
gravel; cf. Per. <ets>shakar</ets>. Cf. <er>Saccharine</er>,
<er>Sucrose</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sweet white (or brownish
yellow) crystalline substance, of a sandy or granular consistency,
obtained by crystallizing the evaporated juice of certain plants, as the
sugar cane, sorghum, beet root, sugar maple, etc. It is used for
seasoning and preserving many kinds of food and drink. Ordinary sugar is
essentially sucrose. See the Note below.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The term <xex>sugar</xex> includes several commercial
grades, as the <xex>white</xex> or <xex>refined</xex>,
<xex>granulated</xex>, <xex>loaf</xex> or <xex>lump</xex>, and the
<xex>raw brown</xex> or <xex>muscovado</xex>. In a more general sense,
it includes several distinct chemical compounds, as the
<xex>glucoses</xex>, or <xex>grape sugars</xex> (including glucose
proper, dextrose, and levulose), and the <xex>sucroses</xex>, or true
sugars (as cane sugar). All sugars are carbohydrates. See
<er>Carbohydrate</er>. The <xex>glucoses</xex>, or <xex>grape
sugars</xex>, are ketone alcohols of the formula
<chform>C6H12O6</chform>, and they turn the plane of polarization to the
right or the left. They are produced from the amyloses and sucroses, as
by the action of heat and acids of ferments, and are themselves
decomposed by fermentation into alcohol and carbon dioxide. The only
sugar (called <xex>acrose</xex>) as yet produced artificially belongs to
this class. The <xex>sucroses</xex>, or <xex>cane sugars</xex>, are
doubled glucose anhydrides of the formula <chform>C12H22O11</chform>.
They are usually not fermentable as such (cf. <er>Sucrose</er>), and
they act on polarized light.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>By extension, anything resembling sugar in taste or
appearance; <as>as, <ex>sugar</ex> of lead (lead acetate), a poisonous
white crystalline substance having a sweet taste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Compliment or flattery used to disguise or render
acceptable something obnoxious; honeyed or soothing words.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<cs><p><col><b>Acorn sugar</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Quercite</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Cane sugar</b></col>, <cd>sugar made from the sugar cane;
sucrose, or an isomeric sugar. See <er>Sucrose</er>.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Diabetes sugar</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Diabetic
sugar</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Med. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of sugar
(grape sugar or dextrose) excreted in the urine in diabetes mellitus; --
the presence of such a sugar in the urine is used to diagnose the
illness.</cd> -- <col><b>Fruit sugar</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Fruit</er>, and <er>Fructose</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Grape
sugar</b></col>, <cd>a sirupy or white crystalline sugar (dextrose or
glucose) found as a characteristic ingredient of ripe grapes, and also
produced from many other sources. See <er>Dextrose</er>, and
<er>Glucose</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Invert sugar</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Invert</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Malt sugar</b></col>, <cd>a variety of
sugar isomeric with sucrose, found in malt. See <er>Maltose</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Manna sugar</b></col>, <cd>a substance found in manna,
resembling, but distinct from, the sugars. See <er>Mannite</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Milk sugar</b></col>, <cd>a variety of sugar characteristic of
fresh milk, and isomeric with sucrose. See <er>Lactose</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Muscle sugar</b></col>, <cd>a sweet white crystalline substance
isomeric with, and formerly regarded to, the glucoses. It is found in
the tissue of muscle, the heart, liver, etc. Called also <altname>heart
sugar</altname>. See <er>Inosite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Pine
sugar</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Pinite</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Starch
sugar</b></col> <fld>(Com. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of dextrose made
by the action of heat and acids on starch from corn, potatoes, etc.; --
called also <altname>potato sugar</altname>, <altname>corn
sugar</altname>, and, inaccurately, <altname>invert sugar</altname>. See
<er>Dextrose</er>, and <er>Glucose</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar
barek</b></col>, <cd>one who refines sugar.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar
beet</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of beet (<spn>Beta
vulgaris</spn>) with very large white roots, extensively grown, esp. in
Europe, for the sugar obtained from them.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar
berry</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the hackberry.</cd> --
<col><b>Sugar bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several
species of small South American singing birds of the genera
<gen>Coereba</gen>, <gen>Dacnis</gen>, and allied genera belonging to
the family <fam>Coerebidae</fam>. They are allied to the honey
eaters.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar bush</b></col>. <cd>See <er>Sugar
orchard</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar camp</b></col>, <cd>a place in or
near a sugar orchard, where maple sugar is made.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar
candian</b></col>, <cd>sugar candy.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> --
<col><b>Sugar candy</b></col>, <cd>sugar clarified and concreted or
crystallized; candy made from sugar.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar
cane</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a tall perennial grass
(<spn>Saccharum officinarium</spn>), with thick short-jointed stems. It
has been cultivated for ages as the principal source of sugar.</cd> --
<col><b>Sugar loaf</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A loaf or mass of refined
sugar, usually in the form of a truncated cone.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
hat shaped like a sugar loaf.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why, do not or know you, grannam, and that <qex>sugar
loaf</qex>?</q> <rj><qau>J. Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <col><b>Sugar maple</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the rock
maple (<spn>Acer saccharinum</spn>). See <er>Maple</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sugar mill</b></col>, <cd>a machine for pressing out the juice
of the sugar cane, usually consisting of three or more rollers, between
which the cane is passed.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar mite</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A small mite (<spn>Tyroglyphus
sacchari</spn>), often found in great numbers in unrefined sugar.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The lepisma.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar of lead</b></col>.
<cd>See <er>Sugar</er>, 2, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar of
milk</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Milk</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar
orchard</b></col>, <cd>a collection of maple trees selected and
preserved for purpose of obtaining sugar from them; -- called also,
sometimes, <altname>sugar bush</altname>.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark>
<au>Bartlett.</au> -- <col><b>Sugar pine</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>an immense coniferous tree (<spn>Pinus Lambertiana</spn>) of
California and Oregon, furnishing a soft and easily worked timber. The
resinous exudation from the stumps, etc., has a sweetish taste, and has
been used as a substitute for sugar.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar
squirrel</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an Australian flying
phalanger (<spn>Belideus sciureus</spn>), having a long bushy tail and a
large parachute. It resembles a flying squirrel. See <xex>Illust.</xex>
under <er>Phlanger</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar tongs</b></col>,
<cd>small tongs, as of silver, used at table for taking lumps of sugar
from a sugar bowl.</cd> -- <col><b>Sugar tree</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Sugar maple</cref>, above.</cd><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p></cs>

<p><ent>Sugar</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>In making maple
sugar, to complete the process of boiling down the sirup till it is
thick enough to crystallize; to approach or reach the state of
granulation; -- with the preposition <xex>off</xex>.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><-- field = sugar making --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugar</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sugared</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sugaring</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To impregnate,
season, cover, or sprinkle with sugar; to mix sugar with.</def>
<ldquo/When I <xex>sugar</xex> my liquor.<rdquo/ <rj><au>G.
Eliot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cover with soft words; to disguise by flattery;
to compliment; to sweeten; <as>as, to <ex>sugar</ex>
reproof</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With devotion's visage<br/
And pious action we do <qex>sugar</qex> o'er<br/
The devil himself.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugared</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ared</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sweetened.</def>
<ldquo/The <xex>sugared</xex> liquor.<rdquo/ <au>Spenser.</au> <def>Also
used figuratively; <as>as, <ex>sugared</ex> kisses</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1442 --></p>

<p><ent>Sugar-house</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar-house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A building in
which sugar is made or refined; a sugar manufactory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugariness</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar*i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sugary, or sweet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugaring</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of covering
or sweetening with sugar; also, the sugar thus used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act or process of making sugar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugarless</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Without sugar; free from
sugar.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugarplum</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar*plum`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of candy
or sweetneat made up in small balls or disks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugary</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"ar*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Resembling or containing sugar; tasting of sugar; sweet.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fond of sugar or sweet things; <as>as, a
<ex>sugary</ex> palate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sugescent</ent><br/
<hw>Su*ges"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sugere</ets> to suck.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
sucking.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Paley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggest</ent><br/
<hw>Sug*gest"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suggested</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suggesting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>suggestus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suggerere</ets> to put under,
furnish, suggest; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>gerere</ets> to carry, to
bring. See <er>Jest</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To introduce
indirectly to the thoughts; to cause to be thought of, usually by the
agency of other objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some ideas . . . are <qex>suggested</qex> to the mind by all the
ways of sensation and reflection.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To propose with difference or modesty; to hint; to
intimate; <as>as, to <ex>suggest</ex> a difficulty</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To seduce; to prompt to evil; to tempt.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Knowing that tender youth is soon <qex>suggested</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To inform secretly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To hint; allude to; refer to;
insinuate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggest</ent><br/
<hw>Sug*gest"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make suggestions; to
tempt.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And ever weaker grows through acted crime,<br/
Or seeming-genial, venial fault,<br/
Recurring and <qex>suggesting</qex> still.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggester</ent><br/
<hw>Sug*gest"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
suggests.</def> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggestion</ent><br/
<hw>Sug*ges"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>suggestion</ets>, L. <ets>suggestio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of suggesting; presentation of an idea.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is suggested; an intimation; an
insinuation; a hint; a different proposal or mention; also, formerly, a
secret incitement; temptation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Why do I yield to that <qex>suggestion</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Charge; complaint; accusation.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A false <xex>suggestion</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Information without oath; an entry
of a material fact or circumstance on the record for the information of
the court, at the death or insolvency of a party.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Physiol. & Metaph.)</fld> <def>The act or power of
originating or recalling ideas or relations, distinguished as original
and relative; -- a term much used by Scottish metaphysicians from
Hutcherson to Thomas Brown.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Hypnotism)</fld> <def>The control of the mind of an
hypnotic subject by ideas in the mind of the hypnotizer.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Hint; allusion; intimation; insinuation.</syn> --
<usage><er>Suggestion</er>, <er>Hint</er>. A <xex>hint</xex> is the
briefest or most indirect mode of calling one's attention to a subject.
A <xex>suggestion</xex> is a putting of something before the mind for
consideration, an indirect or guarded mode of presenting argument or
advice. A <xex>hint</xex> is usually something slight or covert, and may
by merely negative in its character. A <xex>suggestion</xex> is
ordinarily intended to furnish us with some practical assistance or
direction. <ldquo/He gave me a <xex>hint</xex> of my danger, and added
some <xex>suggestions</xex> as to the means of avoiding
it.<rdquo/</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Willing to wound, and yet afraid to strike,<br/
Just <qex>hint</qex> a fault, and hesitate dislike.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Arthur, whom they say is killed to-night<br/
On your <qex>suggestion</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggestive</ent><br/
<hw>Sug*gest"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Containing a
suggestion, hint, or intimation.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sug*gest"ive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sug*gest"ive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggestive medicine</ent><br/
<hw>Sug*gest"ive med"i*cine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Treatment by
commands or positive statements addressed to a more or less hypnotized
patient.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggestment</ent><br/
<hw>Sug*gest"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Suggestion.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They fancy that every thought must needs have an immediate outward
<qex>suggestment</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Hare.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggestress</ent><br/
<hw>Sug*gest"ress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A woman who
suggests.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>suggestress</xex> of suicides.<rdquo/
<rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggil</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"gil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suggillare</ets>, <ets>sugillare</ets>, <ets>suggillatum</ets>,
<ets>sugillatum</ets>, literally, to beat black and blue.]</ety> <def>To
defame.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Abp. Parker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggillate</ent><br/
<hw>Sug"gil*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Suggil</er>.]</ety> <def>To beat livid, or black and blue.</def>
<rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suggillation</ent><br/
<hw>Sug`gil*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suggillatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suggillation</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
livid, or black and blue, mark; a blow; a bruise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suicidal</ent><br/
<hw>Su"i*ci`dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Partaking of, or
of the nature of, the crime or suicide.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Su"i*ci`dal*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suicide</ent><br/
<hw>Su"i*cide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sui</ets>
of one's self (akin to <ets>suus</ets> one's own) + <ets>caedere</ets>
to slay, to kill. Cf. <er>So</er>, <pos>adv.</pos>,
<er>Homicide</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of taking one's own
life voluntary and intentionally; self-murder; specifically
<fld>(Law)</fld>, the felonious killing of one's self; the deliberate
and intentional destruction of one's own life by a person of years of
discretion and of sound mind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One guilty of self-murder; a felo-de-se.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Ruin of one's own interests.</def> <ldquo/Intestine
war, which may be justly called political <xex>suicide</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>V. Knox.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suicidical</ent><br/
<hw>Su`i*cid"i*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Suicidal.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suicidism</ent><br/
<hw>Su"i*ci*dism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being suicidal, or self-murdering.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suicism</ent><br/
<hw>Su"i*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>suus</ets>
one's own.]</ety> <def>Selfishness; egoism.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Whitlock.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sui generis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"i gen"e*ris</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>Of his or
its own kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suillage</ent><br/
<hw>Su"il*lage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>souillage</ets>, <ets>soillage</ets>, fr. <ets>souiller</ets>,
<ets>soiller</ets>. See <er>Soil</er> to stain, and cf.
<er>Sullage</er>.]</ety> <def>A drain or collection of filth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sulliage</asp>, and
<asp>sullage</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suilline</ent><br/
<hw>Su"il*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sus</ets>
hog.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a hog or the Hog
family (<fam>Suidae</fam>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suine</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Suint</er>.]</ety> <def>A mixture of oleomargarine with lard or
other fatty ingredients. It is used as a substitute for butter. See
<er>Butterine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suing</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>suer</ets>
to sweat, L. <ets>sudare</ets>.]</ety> <def>The process of soaking
through anything.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suingly</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ing*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sue</er> to
follow.]</ety> <def>In succession; afterwards.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suint</ent><br/
<hw>Su"int</hw> <pr>(s<umac/"<icr/nt)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[F.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A peculiar substance obtained
from the wool of sheep, consisting largely of potash mixed with fatty
and earthy matters. It is used as a source of potash and also for the
manufacture of gas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suiogoths</ent><br/
<hw>Su`i*o*goths"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Suiones</ets> (a Teutonic tribe in what is now Sweeden) + E.
<ets>Goth</ets>.]</ety> <def>The Scandinavian Goths. See the Note under
<er>Goths</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suist</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>suus</ets> belinging to
himself or to one's self.]</ety> <def>One who seeks for things which
gratify merely himself; a selfish person; a selfist.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Whitlock.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suit</ent><br/
<hw>Suit</hw> <pr>(s<umac/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>suite</ets>, F. <ets>suite</ets>, OF. <ets>suite</ets>,
<ets>sieute</ets>, fr. <ets>suivre</ets> to follow, OF.
<ets>sivre</ets>; perhaps influenced by L. <ets>secta</ets>. See
<er>Sue</er> to follow, and cf. <er>Sect</er>, <er>Suite</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of following or pursuing, as game;
pursuit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of suing; the process by which one endeavors
to gain an end or an object; an attempt to attain a certain result;
pursuit; endeavor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thenceforth the <qex>suit</qex> of earthly conquest shone.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The act of wooing in love; the solicitation of a
woman in marriage; courtship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rebate your loves, each rival <qex>suit</qex> suspend,<br/
Till this funereal web my labors end.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The attempt to gain an end by legal
process; an action or process for the recovery of a right or claim;
legal application to a court for justice; prosecution of right before
any tribunal; <as>as, a civil <ex>suit</ex>; a criminal <ex>suit</ex>; a
<ex>suit</ex> in chancery.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I arrest thee at the <qex>suit</qex> of Count Orsino.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In England the several <qex>suits</qex>, or remedial instruments
of justice, are distinguished into three kinds -- actions personal,
real, and mixed.</q> <rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>That which follows as a retinue; a company of
attendants or followers; the assembly of persons who attend upon a
prince, magistrate, or other person of distinction; -- often written
<asp>suite</asp>, and pronounced <pr>sw<emac/t</pr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Things that follow in a series or succession; the
individual objects, collectively considered, which constitute a series,
as of rooms, buildings, compositions, etc.; -- often written
<asp>suite</asp>, and pronounced <pr>sw<emac/t</pr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>A number of things used together, and generally
necessary to be united in order to answer their purpose; a number of
things ordinarily classed or used together; a set; <as>as, a
<ex>suit</ex> of curtains; a <ex>suit</ex> of armor; a <ex>suit</ex> of
clothes; a three-piece business <ex>suit</ex>.</as></def> <ldquo/Two
rogues in buckram <xex>suits</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <fld>(Playing Cards)</fld> <def>One of the four sets of
cards which constitute a pack; -- each set consisting of thirteen cards
bearing a particular emblem, as hearts, spades, clubs, or diamonds;
also, the members of each such suit held by a player in certain games,
such as bridge; <as>as, hearts were her long
<ex>suit</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To deal and shuffle, to divide and sort<br/
Her mingled <qex>suits</qex> and sequences.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>Regular order; succession.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every five and thirty years the same kind and <qex>suit</qex> of
weather comes again.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <specif>Hence: (derived from def 7)</specif>
<def>Someone who dresses in a business suit, as contrasted with more
informal attire;</def> <specif>specifically,</specif> <def>a person,
such as business executive, or government official, who is apt to view a
situation formalistically, bureaucratically, or according to formal
procedural criteria; -- used derogatively for one who is inflexible,
esp. when a more humanistic or imaginative approach would be
appropriate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Out of suits</b></col>, <cd>having no
correspondence.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Shak.</au> -- <col><b>Suit
and service</b></col> <fld>(Feudal Law)</fld>, <cd>the duty of
feudatories to attend the courts of their lords or superiors in time of
peace, and in war to follow them and do military service; -- called also
<altname>suit service</altname>.</cd> <au>Blackstone.</au> --
<col><b>Suit broker</b></col>, <cd>one who made a trade of obtaining the
suits of petitioners at court.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> -- <col><b>Suit
court</b></col> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>the court in which tenants
owe attendance to their lord.</cd> -- <col><b>Suit covenant</b></col>
<fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld>, <cd>a covenant to sue at a certain court.</cd>
-- <col><b>Suit custom</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>a service which
is owed from time immemorial.</cd> -- <col><b>Suit service</b></col>.
<fld>(Feudal Law)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Suit and service</cref>,
above.</cd> -- <col><b>To bring suit</b></col>. <fld>(Law)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To bring <xex>secta</xex>, followers or witnesses, to
prove the plaintiff's demand.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>In modern usage, to institute an action.</cd> -- <col><b>To follow
suit</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Follow</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To mimic the
action of another person; to perform an action similar to what has
preceded; <as>as, when she walked in, John left the room and his wife
<ex>followed suit</ex></as>.</cd> -- <col>long suit</col> <sd>(a)</sd>
<fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <cd>the suit{8} of which a player has the
largest number of cards in his hand; <as>as, his <ex>long suit</ex> was
clubs, but his partner insisted on making hearts trumps.</as>.</cd>
<specif>Hence:</specif> <mark>[fig.]</mark> <cd>that quality or
capability which is a person's best asset; <as>as, we could see from the
mess in his room that neatness was not his <ex>long suit</ex></as>.</cd>
-- <col>strong suit</col> <cd>same as <cref>long suit</cref>,
<sd>(b)</sd>.</cd> <ldquo/I think our <qex>strong suit</qex> is that we
can score from both the perimeter and the post.<rdquo/ <au>Bill Disbrow
(basketball coach) 1998.</au> <ldquo/Rigid ideological consistency has
never been a <qex>strong suit</qex> of the Whole Earth Catalogue.<rdquo/
<au>Bruce Sterling (The Hacker Crackdown, 1994)</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suit</ent><br/
<hw>Suit</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Suited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Suiting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fit; to adapt; to
make proper or suitable; <as>as, to <ex>suit</ex> the action to the
word</as>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To be fitted to; to accord with; to become; to
befit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ill <qex>suits</qex> his cloth the praise of railing well.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Raise her notes to that sublime degree<br/
Which <qex>suits</qex> song of piety and thee.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To dress; to clothe.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So went he <qex>suited</qex> to his watery tomb.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To please; to make content; <as>as, he is well
<ex>suited</ex> with his place; to <ex>suit</ex> one's
taste</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suit</ent><br/
<hw>Suit</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To agree; to accord; to be fitted;
to correspond; -- usually followed by <xex>with</xex> or
<xex>to</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The place itself was <qex>suiting</qex> to his care.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Give me not an office<br/
That <qex>suits</qex> with me so ill.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To agree; accord; comport; tally; correspond;
match; answer.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suitability</ent><br/
<hw>Suit`a*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being suitable; suitableness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suitable</ent><br/
<hw>Suit"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of suiting;
fitting; accordant; proper; becoming; agreeable; adapted; <as>as,
ornaments <ex>suitable</ex> to one's station; language <ex>suitable</ex>
for the subject</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Suit"a*ble*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos> -- <wf>Suit"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Proper; fitting; becoming; accordant; agreeable;
competent; correspondent; compatible; consonant; congruous;
consistent.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suite</ent><br/
<hw>Suite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See <er>Suit</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A retinue or company of
attendants, as of a distinguished personage; <as>as, the <ex>suite</ex>
of an ambassador</as>. See <er>Suit</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 5.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A connected series or succession of objects; a
number of things used or clessed together; a set; <as>as, a
<ex>suite</ex> of rooms; a <ex>suite</ex> of minerals</as>. See
<er>Suit</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 6.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mr. Barnard took one of the candles that stood upon the king's
table, and lighted his majesty through a <qex>suite</qex> of rooms till
they came to a private door into the library.</q>
<rj><qau>Boswell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>One of the old musical forms,
before the time of the more compact <xex>sonata</xex>, consisting of a
string or series of pieces all in the same key, mostly in various dance
rhythms, with sometimes an elaborate prelude. Some composers of the
present day affect the <xex>suite</xex> form.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suiting</ent><br/
<hw>Suit"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Among tailors, cloth
suitable for making entire suits of clothes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suitor</ent><br/
<hw>Suit"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
sues, petitions, or entreats; a petitioner; an applicant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She hath been a <qex>suitor</qex> to me for her brother.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, one who solicits a woman in marriage; a
wooer; a lover.</def> <rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who sues or
prosecutes a demand in court; a party to a suit, as a plaintiff,
petitioner, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(O. Eng. Law)</fld> <def>One
who attends a court as plaintiff, defendant, petitioner, appellant,
witness, juror, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suitress</ent><br/
<hw>Suit"ress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female
supplicant.</def> <rj><au>Rowe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suji</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"ji</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind.
<ets>s<?/f<imac/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Indian wheat, granulated but not
pulverized; a kind of semolina.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>soojee</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Icel.
<ets>s<?/la</ets> the gannet. See <er>Solan goose</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of sea birds including the booby and the
common gannet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulcated</ent><br/
<ent>Sulcate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sul"cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sul"ca*ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sulcatus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>sulcare</ets> to furrow, fr. <ets>sulcus</ets> a furrow.]</ety>
<def>Scored with deep and regular furrows; furrowed or grooved; <as>as,
a <ex>sulcated</ex> stem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulcation</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A channel or
furrow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulciform</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"ci*form</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the form of
a sulcus; <as>as, <ex>sulciform</ex> markings</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulcus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sul"cus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sulci</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a furrow.]</ety> <def>A
furrow; a groove; a fissure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suleah fish</ent><br/
<hw>Su"le*ah fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A coarse
fish of India, used in making a breakfast relish called
<xex>burtah</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulk</ent><br/
<hw>Sulk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sulcus</ets>.]</ety> <def>A furrow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulk</ent><br/
<hw>Sulk</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sulkiness</er>.]</ety>
<def>To be silently sullen; to be morose or obstinate.</def> <rj><au>T.
Hook.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulker</ent><br/
<hw>Sulk"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sulks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulkily</ent><br/
<hw>Sulk"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sulky
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulkiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sulk"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[For <ets>sulkenness</ets>, fr.
AS. <ets>solcen</ets> slothful, remiss, in <amac/<ets>solcen</ets>,
be<ets>solcen</ets>, properly p. p. of <ets>sealcan</ets> in
<amac/<ets>sealcan</ets> to be weak or slothful; of uncertain
origin.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being sulky; sullenness;
moroseness; <as>as, <ex>sulkiness</ex> of disposition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulks</ent><br/
<hw>Sulks</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>The condition of
being sulky; a sulky mood or humor; <as>as, to be in the
<ex>sulks</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulky</ent><br/
<hw>Sulk"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sulkier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sulkiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[See <er>Sulkiness</er>, and cf.
<er>Sulky</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Moodly silent; sullen; sour;
obstinate; morose; splenetic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Sullen</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulky</ent><br/
<hw>Sulk"y</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sulkies</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[From <er>Sulky</er>, <pos>a.</pos>; -- so
called from the owner's desire of riding alone.]</ety> <def>A light
two-wheeled carriage for a single person.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sulky</xex> is used adjectively in the names of
several agricultural machines drawn by horses to denote that the machine
is provided with wheels and a seat for the driver; as, <xex>sulky</xex>
plow; <xex>sulky</xex> harrow; <xex>sulky</xex> rake, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sull</ent><br/
<hw>Sull</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>suluh</ets>,
<ets>sulh</ets>, a plow; cf. OHG. <ets>suohili</ets> a little
plow.]</ety> <def>A plow.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ainsworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sullage</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"lage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Suillage</er>, <er>Sulliage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Drainage
of filth; filth collected from the street or highway; sewage.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The streets were exceedingly large, well paved, having many vaults
and conveyances under them for <qex>sullage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Evelyn.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which sullies or defiles.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is the privilege of the celestial luminaries to receive no
tincture, <qex>sullage</qex>, or difilement from the most noisome sinks
and dunghills here below.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>The scoria on the surface of
molten metal in the ladle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Hydraul. Engin.)</fld> <def>Silt; mud deposited by
water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sullage piece</b></col> <fld>(Founding)</fld>, <cd>the
sprue of a casting. See <er>Sprue</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1
<sd>(b)</sd>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sullen</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"len</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>solein</ets>, <ets>solain</ets>, lonely, sullen; through Old French
fr. (assumed) LL. <ets>solanus</ets> solitary, fr. L. <ets>solus</ets>
alone. See <er>Sole</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Lonely;
solitary; desolate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wyclif (Job iii.
14).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Gloomy; dismal; foreboding.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Solemn hymns so <qex>sullen</qex> dirges change.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Mischievous; malignant; unpropitious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such <qex>sullen</qex> planets at my birth did shine.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Gloomily angry and silent; cross; sour; affected
with ill humor; morose.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>sullen</qex> I forsook the imperfect feast.</q>
<rj><qau>Prior.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Obstinate; intractable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Things are as <qex>sullen</qex> as we are.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Heavy; dull; sluggish.</def> <ldquo/The larger
stream was placid, and even <xex>sullen</xex>, in its course.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sulky; sour; cross; ill-natured; morose; peevish;
fretful; ill-humored; petulant; gloomy; malign; intractable.</syn> --
<usage><er>Sullen</er>, <er>Sulky</er>. Both <xex>sullen</xex> and
<xex>sulky</xex> show themselves in the demeanor. <xex>Sullenness</xex>
seems to be an habitual sulkiness, and <xex>sulkiness</xex> a temporary
sullenness. The former may be an innate disposition; the latter, a
disposition occasioned by recent injury. Thus we are in a
<xex>sullen</xex> mood, and in a <xex>sulky</xex> fit.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No cheerful breeze this <qex>sullen</qex> region knows;<br/
The dreaded east is all the wind that blows.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sul"len*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sul"len*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sullen</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"len</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is solitary, or
lives alone; a hermit.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Piers
Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Sullen feelings or manners; sulks;
moroseness; <as>as, to have the <ex>sullens</ex></as>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sullen</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"len</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make sullen or
sluggish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sullens</qex> the whole body with . . . laziness.</q>
<rj><qau>Feltham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sullevate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"le*vate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sublevare</ets> to raise up. Cf. <er>Sublevation</er>.]</ety>
<def>To rouse; to excite.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1443 --></p>

<p><ent>Sulliage</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"li*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sullage</er>, <er>Suillage</er>, or <er>Sully</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Foulness; filth.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though we wipe away with never so much care the dirt thrown at us,
there will be left some <qex>sulliage</qex> behind.</q> <rj><qau>Gov. of
Tongue.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sully</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sullied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sullying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sulien</ets>, AS. <ets>sylian</ets>, fr. <ets>sol</ets> mire; akin
to G. <ets>suhle</ets> mire, <ets>sich</ets>, <ets>s<uum/hlen</ets> to
wallow, Sw. <ets>s<oum/la</ets> to bemire, Dan. <ets>s<oum/le</ets>,
Goth. bi<ets>saulijan</ets> to defile.]</ety> <def>To soil; to dirty; to
spot; to tarnish; to stain; to darken; -- used literally and
figuratively; <as>as, to <ex>sully</ex> a sword; to <ex>sully</ex> a
person's reputation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Statues <qex>sullied</qex> yet with sacrilegious smoke.</q>
<rj><qau>Roscommon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No spots to <qex>sully</qex> the brightness of this solemnity.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sully</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"ly</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become soiled or
tarnished.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Silvering will <qex>sully</qex> and canker more than gilding.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sully</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"ly</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Sullies</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>Soil; tarnish; stain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A noble and triumphant merit breaks through little spots and
<qex>sullies</qex> in his reputation.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphacid</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*ac"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sulpho-</ets> + <ets>acid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An acid in which, to a greater or less extent, sulphur plays a part
analogous to that of oxygen in an oxyacid; thus, thiosulphuric and
sulpharsenic acids are <xex>sulphacids</xex>; -- called also
<altname>sulphoacid</altname>. See the Note under <er>Acid</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphamate</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*am"ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of sulphamic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphamic</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*am"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a sulphamide; derived from, or related to, a
sulphamide; specifically, designating an amido acid derivative,
<chform>NH2.SO2.OH</chform>, of sulphuric acid (analogous to sulphonic
acid) which is not known in the free state, but is known in its
salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphamide</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*am"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Any one of a series of amido compounds obtained by treating
sulphuryl chloride with various amines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphanilic</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph`a*nil"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<ets>sulph</ets>uric + <ets>ani</ets>lene.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, an anilene sulphonic acid which
is obtained as a white crystalline substance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphantimonate</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*an`ti*mo"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphantimonic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphantimonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*an`ti*mon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sulph</ets>o- + <ets>antimonic</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a
hypothetical sulphacid of antimony (called also <xex>thioantimonic
acid</xex>) analogous to sulpharsenic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphantimonious</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*an`ti*mo"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a
hypothetical sulphacid of antimony (called also <xex>thioantimonious
acid</xex>) analogous to sulpharsenious acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphantimonite</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*an"ti*mo*nite`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphantimonious acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulpharsenate</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*ar"se*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of sulpharsenic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulpharsenic</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph`ar*sen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sulph</ets>o- + <ets>arsenic</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a hypothetical sulphacid (called
also <xex>thioarsenic acid</xex>) analogous to arsenic acid, and known
only in its salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulpharsenious</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph`ar*se"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a
hypothetical sulphacid (called also <xex>thioarsenious acid</xex>)
analogous to arsenious acid, and known only in its salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulpharsenite</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*ar"se*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of sulpharsenious acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>sulphas</ets>, <ets>sulphatis</ets>, fr. L. <ets>sulphur</ets>,
<ets>sulfur</ets>, brimstone, sulphur: cf. F. <ets>sulfate</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphuric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphatic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*phat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, resembling, or containing, a sulphate or
sulphates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphato-</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"pha*to-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A combining
form (also used adjectively) denoting a <xex>sulphate as an
ingredient</xex> in certain double salts; <as>as,
<ex>sulphato</ex>-carbonate</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphaurate</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*au"rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of sulphauric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphauric</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*au"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sulph</ets>o- + <ets>aurum</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a hypothetical sulphacid of gold
(<xex>aurum</xex>), known only in its salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphide</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
binary compound of sulphur, or one so regarded; -- formerly called
<altname>sulphuret</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Double sulphide</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
compound of two sulphides.</cd> -- <col><b>Hydrogen sulphide</b></col>.
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Hydrogen</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Metallic sulphide</b></col>, <cd>a binary compound of sulphur
with a metal.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphinate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phi*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of a sulphinic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphindigotic</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*in`di*got"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a sulphonic
acid obtained, as a blue solution, by dissolving indigo in sulphuric
acid; -- formerly called also <altname>cerulic sulphuric</altname> acid,
but properly called <altname>indigo-disulphonic
acid</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphine</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Any one of a series of basic compounds which consist essentially of
sulphur united with hydrocarbon radicals. In general they are oily or
crystalline deliquescent substances having a peculiar odor; as,
<stype>trimethyl sulphine</stype>, <chform>(CH3)3S.OH</chform>. Cf.
<er>Sulphonium</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphinic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*phin"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, any one of a series of acids
regarded as acid ethereal salts of hyposulphurous acid; <as>as, methyl
<ex>sulphinic</ex> acid, <chform>CH3.SO.OH</chform>, a thick unstable
liquid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphinide</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phi*nide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sulph</ets>o- + am<ets>in</ets>e +
anhydr<ets>ide</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A white or
yellowish crystalline substance, <chform>C6H4.(SO2.CO).NH</chform>,
produced artificially by the oxidation of a sulphamic derivative of
toluene. It is the sweetest substance known, having over two hundred
times the sweetening power of sugar, and is known in commerce under the
name of <altname>saccharine</altname>. It has acid properties and forms
salts (which are inaccurately called <xex>saccharinates</xex>).</def>
<rj><au>I. Remsen.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphion</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phi*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sulph</ets>o-
+ <ets>ion</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hypothetical radical,
<chform>SO4</chform>, regarded as forming the acid or negative
constituent of sulphuric acid and the sulphates in electrolytic
decomposition; -- so called in accordance with the binary theory of
salts.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sulphione</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphionide</ent><br/
<hw>Sulph*i"on*ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A binary compound of sulphion, or one so regarded; <as>thus,
sulphuric acid, <chform>H2SO4</chform>, is a
<ex>sulphionide</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphite</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sulfite</ets>. See <er>Sulphur</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphurous acid; -- called also
<altname>sulfite</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person who is spontaneous and original in his
habits of thought and conversation.</def> <mark>[Archaic Slang]</mark>
-- <wordforms><wf>Sul*phit"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<mark>[Slang]</mark></wordforms></p>

<p><q>A <qex>sulphite</qex> is a person who does his own thinking, he is
a person who has surprises up his sleeve. He is explosive.</q>
<rj><qau>Gelett Burgess.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulpho-</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"pho-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A prefix (also
used adjectively) designating <xex>sulphur as an ingredient</xex> in
certain compounds. Cf. <er>Thio-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphoarsenic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*ar*sen"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or containing, sulphur and
arsenic; -- said of an acid which is the same as arsenic acid with the
substitution of sulphur for oxygen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphocarbonate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*car"bon*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphocarbonic acid; a
thiocarbonate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphocarbonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*car*bon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a sulphacid,
<chform>H2CSO2</chform> (called also <altname>thiocarbonic</altname>
<it>acid</it>), or an acid, <chform>H2CS3</chform>, analogous to
carbonic acid, obtained as a yellow oily liquid of a pungent odor, and
forming salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphocyanate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*cy"a*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphocyanic acid; -- also called
<altname>thiocyanate</altname>, and formerly inaccurately
<altname>sulphocyanide</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ferric sulphocyanate</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a
dark red crystalline substance usually obtained in a blood-red solution,
and recognized as a test for ferric iron.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphocyanic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*cy*an"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sulphur</er>, <er>Cyanic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of,
pertaining to, derived from, or designating, a sulphacid,
<chform>HSCN</chform>, analogous to cyanic acid, and obtained as a
colorless deliquescent crystalline substance, having a bitter saline
taste, and not poisonous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphocyanide</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*cy"a*nide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See <er>Sulphocyanate</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphocyanogen</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*cy*an"o*gen</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See <er>Persulphocyanogen</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphonal</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"pho*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A substance employed as a hypnotic, produced by the union of
mercaptan and acetone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphonate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"pho*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of sulphonic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphone</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phone</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Any one of a series of compounds analogous to the ketones, and
consisting of the sulphuryl group united with two hydrocarbon radicals;
<as>as, dimethyl <ex>sulphone</ex>, (CH<?/)<?/</as>.SO<?/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*phon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Pertaining to, or derived from, a sulphone; -- used specifically to
designate any one of a series of acids (regarded as acid ethereal salts
of sulphurous acid) obtained by the oxidation of the mercaptans, or by
treating sulphuric acid with certain aromatic bases (as benzene);
<as>as, phenyl <ex>sulphonic</ex> acid, <chform>C6H5.SO2.OH</chform>, a
stable colorless crystalline substance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sulphonic group</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>the
hypothetical radical, <chform>SO2.OH</chform>, the characteristic
residue of sulphonic acids.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphonium</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*pho"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sulph</ets>ur + amm<ets>onium</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A hypothetical radical, <chform>SH3</chform>,
regarded as the type and nucleus of the sulphines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphophosphate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*phos"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphophosphoric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphophosphite</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*phos"phite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphophosphorous acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphophosphoric</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*phos*phor"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or designating, a hypothetical sulphacid of phosphorus,
analogous to phosphoric acid, and known in its salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphophosphorous</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*phos"phor*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a
hypothetical acid of phosphorus, analogous to phosphorous acid, and
known in its salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphosalt</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"pho*salt`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A salt of a sulphacid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphostannate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*stan"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphostannic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphostannic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*stan"nic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, a sulphacid of tin (more exactly
called <xex>metasulphostannic acid</xex>), which is obtained as a dark
brown amorphous substance, H<?/SnS<?/, forming a well-known series of
salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphotungstate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*tung"state</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A salt of sulphotungstic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphotungstic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*tung"stic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, hypothetical
sulphacid of tungsten (called also <xex>sulphowolframic acid</xex>),
analogous to sulphuric acid, and known in its salts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphovinic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`pho*vin"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sulpho-</ets> + <ets>vinum</ets> wine: cf. F.
<ets>sulfovinique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining
to, and formerly designating, ethylsulphuric acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphur</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phur</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., better
<ets>sulfur</ets>: cf. F. <ets>soufre</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A nonmetallic element occurring naturally in
large quantities, either combined as in the sulphides (as
<xex>pyrites</xex>) and sulphates (as <xex>gypsum</xex>), or native in
volcanic regions, in vast beds mixed with gypsum and various earthy
materials, from which it is melted out. Symbol S. Atomic weight 32. The
specific gravity of ordinary octohedral sulphur is 2.05; of prismatic
sulphur, 1.96.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It is purified by distillation, and is obtained as a
lemon-yellow powder (by sublimation), called <xex>flour</xex>, or
<xex>flowers</xex>, <xex>of sulphur</xex>, or in cast sticks called
<xex>roll sulphur</xex>, or <xex>brimstone</xex>. It burns with a blue
flame and a peculiar suffocating odor. It is an ingredient of gunpowder,
is used on friction matches, and in medicine (as a laxative and
insecticide), but its chief use is in the manufacture of sulphuric acid.
Sulphur can be obtained in two crystalline modifications, in
orthorhombic octahedra, or in monoclinic prisms, the former of which is
the more stable at ordinary temperatures. Sulphur is the type, in its
chemical relations, of a group of elements, including
<xex>selenium</xex> and <xex>tellurium</xex>, called collectively the
<xex>sulphur group</xex>, or <xex>family</xex>. In many respects sulphur
resembles oxygen.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
yellow or orange butterflies of the subfamily <fam>Pierinae</fam>;
<as>as, the clouded <ex>sulphur</ex> (<spn>Eurymus philodice</spn> syn.
<spn>Colias philodice</spn>), which is the common yellow butterfly of
the Eastern United States</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Amorphous sulphur</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an
elastic variety of sulphur of a resinous appearance, obtained by pouring
melted sulphur into water. On standing, it passes back into a brittle
crystalline modification.</cd> -- <col><b>Liver of sulphur</b></col>.
<fld>(Old Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Hepar</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sulphur
acid</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Sulphacid</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sulphur alcohol</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Mercaptan</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sulphur auratum</b></col>
<ety>[L.]</ety> <fld>(Old Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a golden yellow powder,
consisting of antimonic sulphide, <chform>Sb2S5</chform>, -- formerly a
famous nostrum.</cd> -- <col><b>Sulphur base</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>an alkaline sulphide capable of acting as a base
in the formation of sulphur salts according to the old dual theory of
salts.</cd> <mark>[Archaic]</mark> -- <col><b>Sulphur dioxide</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a colorless gas, <chform>SO2</chform>, of a
pungent, suffocating odor, produced by the burning of sulphur. It is
employed chiefly in the production of sulphuric acid, and as a reagent
in bleaching; -- called also <altname>sulphurous anhydride</altname>,
and formerly <altname>sulphurous acid</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sulphur
ether</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a sulphide of hydrocarbon
radicals, formed like the ordinary ethers, which are oxides, but with
sulphur in the place of oxygen.</cd> -- <col><b>Sulphur salt</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a salt of a sulphacid; a sulphosalt.</cd> --
<col><b>Sulphur showers</b></col>, <cd>showers of yellow pollen,
resembling sulphur in appearance, often carried from pine forests by the
wind to a great distance.</cd> -- <col><b>Sulphur trioxide</b></col>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a white crystalline solid, <chform>SO3</chform>,
obtained by oxidation of sulphur dioxide. It dissolves in water with a
hissing noise and the production of heat, forming sulphuric acid, and is
employed as a dehydrating agent. Called also <altname>sulphuric
anhydride</altname>, and formerly <altname>sulphuric
acid</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sulphur whale</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Sulphur-bottom</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Vegetable sulphur</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>lycopodium
powder. See under <er>Lycopodium</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphurate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phu*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sulphuratus</ets>, <ets>sulfuratus</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Sulphureous.</def> <mark>[Poetic & R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphurate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phu*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sulphurated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sulphurating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>To sulphurize.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphuration</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`phu*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sulfuration</ets>, L. <ets>sulphuratio</ets>,
<ets>sulfuratio</ets>, a vein of sulphur.]</ety> <def>The act or process
of combining or impregnating with sulphur or its compounds; also, the
state of being so combined or impregnated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphurator</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phu*ra`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An apparatus
for impregnating with, or exposing to the action of, sulphur;
especially, an apparatus for fumigating or bleaching by means of the
fumes of burning sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphur-bottom</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`phur-bot"tom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A very large whalebone whale of the genus <gen>Sibbaldius</gen>,
having a yellowish belly; especially, <spn>Sibbaldius sulfureus</spn> of
the North Pacific, and <spn>Sibbaldius borealis</spn> of the North
Atlantic; -- called also <altname>sulphur whale</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphureity</ent><br/
<hw>Sul`phu*re"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sulphureous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphureous</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*phu"re*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sulphureus</ets>, <ets>sulfureus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Consisting of
sulphur; having the qualities of sulphur, or brimstone; impregnated with
sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her snakes united, <qex>sulphureous</qex> waters drink.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sul*phu"re*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sul*phu"re*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphuret</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phu*ret</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A sulphide; <as>as, a <ex>sulphuret</ex> of potassium</as>.</def>
<mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphureted</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phu*ret`ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Combined
or impregnated with sulphur; sulphurized.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sulphuretted</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sulphureted hydrogen</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<cd>See <cref>Hydrogen sulphide</cref>, under
<er>Hydrogen</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphuric</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*phu"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sulfurique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
sulphur; <as>as, a <ex>sulphuric</ex> smell</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Derived from, or containing,
sulphur; specifically, designating those compounds in which the element
has a higher valence as contrasted with the <xex>sulphurous</xex>
compounds; <as>as, <ex>sulphuric</ex> acid</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sulphuric acid</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Sulphur
trioxide (see under <er>Sulphur</er>); -- formerly so called on the
dualistic theory of salts.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
heavy, corrosive, oily liquid, <chform>H2SO4</chform>, colorless when
pure, but usually yellowish or brownish, produced by the combined action
of sulphur dioxide, oxygen (from the air), steam, and nitric fumes. It
attacks and dissolves many metals and other intractable substances, sets
free most acids from their salts, and is used in the manufacture of
hydrochloric and nitric acids, of soda, of bleaching powders, etc. It is
also powerful dehydrating agent, having a strong affinity for water, and
eating and corroding paper, wood, clothing, etc. It is thus used in the
manufacture of ether, of imitation parchment, and of nitroglycerin. It
is also used in etching iron, in removing iron scale from forgings, in
petroleum refining, etc., and in general its manufacture is the most
important and fundamental of all the chemical industries. Formerly
called <altname>vitriolic acid</altname>, and now popularly
<altname>vitriol</altname>, and <altname>oil of vitriol</altname>.</cd>
-- <mcol><col><b>Fuming sulphuric acid</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Nordhausen sulphuric acid</b></col></mcol>. <cd>See
<cref>Disulphuric acid</cref>, under <er>Disulphuric</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sulphuric anhydride</b></col>, <cd>sulphur trioxide. See under
<er>Sulphur</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sulphuric ether</b></col>, <cd>common
anaesthetic ether; -- so called because made by the catalytic action of
sulphuric acid on alcohol. See <er>Ether</er>, 3 <sd>(a)</sd>.</cd><--
now usu. ether or ethyl ether. --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphurine</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phur*ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sulphureous.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphuring</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phur*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Exposure to the fumes of
burning sulphur, as in bleaching; the process of bleaching by exposure
to the fumes of sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphurize</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phur*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>To combine or impregnate with sulphur or any of its compounds;
<as>as, to <ex>sulphurize</ex> caoutchouc in vulcanizing</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1444 --></p>

<p><ent>Sulphurous</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phur*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sulphurosus</ets>, <ets>sulfurosus</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>sulfureux</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Derived from, or
containing, sulphur; specifically, designating those compounds in which
the element has a lower valence as contrasted with the
<xex>sulphuric</xex> compounds.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having the
characteristic odor of sulphur dioxide, or of hydrogen sulphide, or of
other sulphur compounds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sulphurous acid</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Sulphur
dioxide. See under <er>Sulphur</er>.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An acid, <chform>H2SO3</chform>, not known in the free
state except as a solution of sulphur dioxide in water, but forming a
well-known series of salts (the <xex>sulphites</xex>).</cd> --
<col><b>Sulphurous anhydride</b></col> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>, <cd>sulphur
dioxide. See under <er>Sulphur</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphurwort</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phur*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The hog's fennel. See under <er>Fennel</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphury</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phur*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling, or
partaking of the nature of, sulphur; having the qualities of
sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphuryl</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"phur*yl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Sulphur</ets> + <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>The hypothetical radical <chform>SO2</chform>; -- called also
<altname>sulphon</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sulphuryl chloride</b></col>, <cd>a chloride, pungent,
fuming liquid, <chform>SO2.Cl2</chform>, obtained by the action of
phosphorus pentachloride on sulphur trioxide. On treatment with water it
decomposes into sulphuric and hydrochloric acids, and is hence called
also <altname>sulphuric chloranhydride</altname>.</cd></cs><-- used in
organic chemistry to prepare acyl chlorides fro organic acids. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphydrate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*phy"drate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A compound, analogous to a hydrate, regarded as a salt of
sulphydric acid, or as a derivative of hydrogen sulphide in which one
half of the hydrogen is replaced by a base (as potassium
<xex>sulphydrate</xex>, <chform>KSH</chform>), or as a hydrate in which
the oxygen has been wholly or partially replaced by sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulphydric</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*phy"dric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, hydrogen sulphide, which is
regarded as an acid, especially when in solution.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulpician</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*pi"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called after
the parish of St. <ets>Sulpice</ets> in Paris, of which the founder,
Jean Jacques Olier, was pastor in 1643.]</ety> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>
<def>One of an order of priests established in France in 1642 to educate
men for the ministry. The order was introduced soon afterwards into
Canada, and in 1791 into the United States.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Sulpitian</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultan</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sultan</ets>
(cf. Sp. <ets>soldan</ets>, It. <ets>sultano</ets>, <ets>soldano</ets>),
Ar. <ets>sult<amac/n</ets> sultan, dominion. Cf. <er>Soldan</er>.]</ety>
<def>A ruler, or sovereign, of a Mohammedan state; specifically, the
ruler of the Turks; the Padishah, or Grand Seignior; -- officially so
called.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sultan flower</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Sweet sultan</cref>, under <er>Sweet</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultana</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*ta"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[It.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The wife of a sultan; a sultaness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A kind of seedless raisin produced
near <city>Smyrna</city> in Asiatic Turkey.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sultana bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
hyacinthine, or purple, gallinule. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Gallinule</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultanate</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tan*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sultanat</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The rule or dominion of a
sultan; sultanship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The territory ruled by a sultan.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> soldanrie.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultaness</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tan*ess</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
sultana.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultanic</ent><br/
<hw>Sul*tan"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to a
sultan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultan-red</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tan-red`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a deep red
color.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultanry</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tan*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The dominions of a
sultan.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultanship</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tan*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office or dignity of a
sultan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultany</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tan*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sultanry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultrily</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sultry
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultriness</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"tri*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
sultry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sultry</ent><br/
<hw>Sul"try</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/l"tr<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Sultrier</adjf>
<pr>(s<ucr/l"tr<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sultriest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[From <er>Sweltry</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Very hot, burning, and oppressive; <as>as, Libya's
<ex>sultry</ex> deserts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such as, born beneath the burning sky<br/
And <qex>sultry</qex> sun, betwixt the tropics lie.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Very hot and humid, or hot, close, stagnant, and
oppressive, as air.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When in the <qex>sultry</qex> glebe I faint,<br/
Or on the thirsty mountain plant.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sulu</ent><br/
<hw>Su*lu"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Malay
<ets>Suluk</ets>.]</ety> <def>A member of the most prominent tribe of
the Moro tribes, occupying the Sulu Archipelago; also, their
language.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sum</ent><br/
<hw>Sum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>summe</ets>,
<ets>somme</ets>, OF. <ets>sume</ets>, <ets>some</ets>, F.
<ets>somme</ets>, L. <ets>summa</ets>, fr. <ets>summus</ets> highest, a
superlative from <ets>sub</ets> under. See <er>Sub-</er>, and cf.
<er>Supreme</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The aggregate of two or more
numbers, magnitudes, quantities, or particulars; the amount or whole of
any number of individuals or particulars added together; <as>as, the
<ex>sum</ex> of 5 and 7 is 12</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Take ye the <qex>sum</qex> of all the congregation.</q>
<rj><qau>Num. i. 2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sum</xex> is now commonly applied to an aggregate
of numbers, and <xex>number</xex> to an aggregate of persons or
things.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A quantity of money or currency; any amount,
indefinitely; <as>as, a <ex>sum</ex> of money; a small <ex>sum</ex>, or
a large <ex>sum</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>sum</xex> of forty
pound.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With a great <qex>sum</qex> obtained I this freedom.</q>
<rj><qau>Acts xxii. 28.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The principal points or thoughts when viewed
together; the amount; the substance; compendium; <as>as, this is the
<ex>sum</ex> of all the evidence in the case; this is the <ex>sum</ex>
and substance of his objections</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Height; completion; utmost degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thus have I told thee all my state, and brought<br/
My story to the <qex>sum</qex> of earthly bliss.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Arith.)</fld> <def>A problem to be solved, or an
example to be wrought out.</def> <rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sum</qex> in arithmetic wherein a flaw discovered at a
particular point is <qex>ipso facto</qex> fatal to the whole.</q>
<rj><qau>Gladstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A large sheet of paper . . . covered with long
<qex>sums</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Dickens.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Algebraic sum</b></col>, <cd>as distinguished from
<xex>arithmetical sum</xex>, the aggregate of two or more numbers or
quantities taken with regard to their signs, as + or -, according to the
rules of addition in algebra; thus, the <xex>algebraic sum</xex> of -2,
8, and -1 is 5.</cd> -- <col><b>In sum</b></col>, <cd>in short; in
brief.</cd> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>In sum</xex>, the gospel . .
. prescribes every virtue to our conduct, and forbids every sin.<rdquo/
<au>Rogers.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sum</ent><br/
<hw>Sum</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Summed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Summing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>sommer</ets>, LL.
<ets>summare</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bring together into one
whole; to collect into one amount; to cast up, as a column of figures;
to ascertain the totality of; -- usually with <xex>up</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind doth value every moment, and then the hour doth rather
<qex>sum</qex> up the moments, than divide the day.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To bring or collect into a small compass; to
comprise in a few words; to condense; -- usually with
<xex>up</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><ldquo/Go to the ant, thou sluggard,<rdquo/ in few words
<qex>sums</qex> up the moral of this fable.</q>
<rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>sums</qex> their virtues in himself alone.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Falconry)</fld> <def>To have (the feathers) full
grown; to furnish with complete, or full-grown, plumage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But feathered soon and fledge<br/
They <qex>summed</qex> their pens [wings].</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Summing up</b></col>, <cd>a compendium or abridgment; a
recapitulation; a r<eacute/sum<eacute/; a summary.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To cast up; collect; comprise; condense;
comprehend; compute.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumach</ent><br/
<ent>Sumac</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su"mac</hw>, <hw>Su"mach</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sumac</ets>, formerly <ets>sumach</ets> (cf.
Sp. <ets>zumaque</ets>), fr. Ar. <ets>summ<amac/q</ets>.]</ety>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>shumac</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any plant of the genus <gen>Rhus</gen>, shrubs or
small trees with usually compound leaves and clusters of small flowers.
Some of the species are used in tanning, some in dyeing, and some in
medicine. One, the Japanese <spn>Rhus vernicifera</spn>, yields the
celebrated Japan varnish, or lacquer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The powdered leaves, peduncles, and young branches
of certain species of the sumac plant, used in tanning and
dyeing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Poison sumac</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Poison</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumatra leaf</ent><br/
<hw>Su*ma"tra leaf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>A thin, elastic, uniformly
light-colored tobacco leaf, raised in Sumatra and extensively used for
cigar wrappers.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumatran</ent><br/
<hw>Su*ma"tran</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Sumatra or its inhabitants.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native
of Sumatra.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumbul</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"bul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pers.]</ety> <def>The
musky root of an Asiatic umbelliferous plant, <spn>Ferula Sumbul</spn>.
It is used in medicine as a stimulant.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>sumbal</asp>.]</altsp> -- <wordforms><wf>Sum*bul"ic</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumerian</ent><br/
<hw>Su*me"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Sumirian</asp>.]</altsp> <def>Of or pertaining to the region of
lower Babylonia, which was anciently called <xex>Sumer</xex>, or its
inhabitants or their language.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumerian</ent><br/
<hw>Su*me"ri*an</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>Sumirian</asp>.]</altsp> <def>A native of lower Babylonia,
anciently called Sumer.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumless</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not to be summed up
or computed; so great that the amount can not be ascertained;
incalculable; inestimable.</def> <ldquo/Sumless treasure.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summarily</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"ma*ri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a summary
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summarist</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"ma*rist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
summarized.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summarize</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"ma*rize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To comprise in,
or reduce to, a summary; to present briefly.</def>
<rj><au>Chambers.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summary</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"ma*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sommaire</ets>. See <er>Sum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Formed
into a sum; summed up; reduced into a narrow compass, or into few words;
short; brief; concise; compendious; <as>as, a <ex>summary</ex> statement
of facts</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, rapidly performed; quickly executed; <as>as,
a <ex>summary</ex> process; to take <ex>summary</ex>
vengeance</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Short; brief; concise; compendious;
succinct.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summary</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"ma*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Summaries</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>sommaire</ets>,
or L. <ets>summarium</ets>. See <er>Summary</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<def>A general or comprehensive statement; an abridged account; an
abstract, abridgment, or compendium, containing the sum or substance of
a fuller account.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summation</ent><br/
<hw>Sum*ma"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sommation</ets>. See <er>Sum</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>The
act of summing, or forming a sum, or total amount; also, an
aggregate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of this series no <qex>summation</qex> is possible to a finite
intellect.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summer</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Sum</er>,
<pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <def>One who sums; one who casts up an
account.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summer</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sommier</ets> a rafter,
the same word as <ets>sommier</ets> a beast of burden. See
<er>Sumpter</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A large stone or beam
placed horizontally on columns, piers, posts, or the like, serving for
various uses. Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> The lintel of a door or window.
<sd>(b)</sd> The commencement of a cross vault. <sd>(c)</sd> A central
floor timber, as a girder, or a piece reaching from a wall to a girder.
Called also <altname>summertree</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summer</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sumer</ets>,
<ets>somer</ets>, AS. <ets>sumor</ets>, <ets>sumer</ets>; akin to
OFries. <ets>sumur</ets>, D. <ets>zomer</ets>, OS. <ets>sumar</ets>, G.
<ets>sommer</ets>, OHG. & Icel. <ets>sumar</ets>, Dan.
<ets>sommer</ets>, Sw. <ets>sommar</ets>, W. <ets>haf</ets>, Zend
<ets>hama</ets>, Skr. <ets>sam<amac/</ets> year. <root/292.]</ety>
<def>The season of the year in which the sun shines most directly upon
any region; the warmest period of the year.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ North of the equator summer is popularly taken to
include the months of June, July, and August. Astronomically it may be
considered, in the northern hemisphere, to begin with the summer
solstice, about June 21st, and to end with the autumnal equinox, about
September 22d.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Indian summer</b></col>, <cd>in North America, a period
of warm weather late in autumn, usually characterized by a clear sky,
and by a hazy or smoky appearance of the atmosphere, especially near the
horizon. The name is derived probably from the custom of the Indians of
using this time in preparation for winter by laying in stores of
food.</cd> -- <col><b>Saint Martin's summer</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Saint</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Summer bird</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the wryneck.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Summer colt</b></col>, <cd>the undulating state of the air near
the surface of the ground when heated.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Summer complaint</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a popular term
for any diarrheal disorder occurring in summer, especially when produced
by heat and indigestion.</cd> -- <col><b>Summer coot</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the American gallinule.</cd> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Summer cypress</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>an annual plant (<spn>Kochia Scoparia</spn>) of the Goosefoot
family. It has narrow, ciliate, crowded leaves, and is sometimes seen in
gardens.</cd> -- <col><b>Summer duck</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The wood duck.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The garganey, or
summer teal. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <cref>Wood duck</cref>, under
<er>Wood</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Summer fallow</b></col>, <cd>land
uncropped and plowed, etc., during the summer, in order to pulverize the
soil and kill the weeds.</cd> -- <col><b>Summer rash</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>prickly heat. See under <er>Prickly</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Summer sheldrake</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the hooded
merganser.</cd> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Summer
snipe</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The dunlin.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The common European sandpiper.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd>
<cd>The green sandpiper.</cd> -- <col><b>Summer tanager</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a singing bird (<spn>Piranga rubra</spn>) native
of the Middle and Southern United States. The male is deep red, the
female is yellowish olive above and yellow beneath. Called also
<altname>summer redbird</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Summer teal</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, the blue-winged teal. <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark> --
<col><b>Summer wheat</b></col>, <cd>wheat that is sown in the spring,
and matures during the summer following. See <er>Spring wheat</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Summer yellowbird</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Yellowbird</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summer</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Summered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Summering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To pass the summer; to spend
the warm season; <as>as, to <ex>summer</ex> in
Switzerland</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fowls shall <qex>summer</qex> upon them.</q> <rj><qau>Isa.
xviii. 6.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summer</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To keep or carry through the
summer; to feed during the summer; <as>as, to <ex>summer</ex>
stock</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summer-fallow</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer-fal"low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To plow and
work in summer, in order to prepare for wheat or other crop; to plow and
let lie fallow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summerhouse</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer*house`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Summerhouses</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A rustic house or
apartment in a garden or park, to be used as a pleasure resort in
summer.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summerliness</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer*li*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being like summer.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summerset</ent><br/
<ent>Summersault</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sum"mer*sault</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sum"mer*set</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Somersault</er>,
<er>Somerset</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summerstir</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer*stir`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
summer-fallow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summertide</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer*tide`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Summer
time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summertree</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer*tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Summer</ets> a beam + <ets>tree</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>A summer. See 2d <er>Summer</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summery</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mer*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
summer; like summer; <as>as, a <ex>summery</ex> day</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summist</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who sums up; one
who forms an abridgment or summary.</def> <rj><au>Sir E.
Dering.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summit</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sommet</ets>,
dim. of OF. <ets>som</ets>, <ets>sum</ets>, top, from L.
<ets>summum</ets>, from <ets>summus</ets> highest. See <er>Sum</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The top; the highest
point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fixed on the <qex>summit</qex> of the highest mount.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The highest degree; the utmost elevation; the acme;
<as>as, the <ex>summit</ex> of human fame</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The most elevated part of a
bivalve shell, or the part in which the hinge is situated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Summit level</b></col>, <cd>the highest level of a canal,
a railroad, or the like, in surmounting an ascent.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summitless</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mit*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no summit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summity</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mit*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>summitas</ets>, fr. <ets>summus</ets> highest: cf. F.
<ets>sommit<eacute/</ets>. See <er>Sum</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The height or top of anything.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The utmost degree; perfection.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hallywell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summon</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Summoned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Summoning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>somonen</ets>, OF. <ets>sumundre</ets>, <ets>semondre</ets>, F.
<ets>semondre</ets>, from (assumed) LL. <ets>summon<ecr/re</ets>, for L.
<ets>summon<emac/re</ets> to give a hint; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>monere</ets> to admonish, to warn. See <er>Monition</er>, and cf.
<er>Submonish</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To call, bid, or cite; to
notify to come to appear; -- often with <xex>up</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Stiffen the sinews, <qex>summon</qex> up the blood.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Trumpets <qex>summon</qex> him to war.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give notice to, or command to appear, as in
court; to cite by authority; <as>as, to <ex>summon</ex>
witnesses</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To call upon to surrender, as a
fort.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To call; cite; notify; convene; convoke; excite;
invite; bid. See <er>Call</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summoner</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mon*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>somner</ets>, <ets>sompnour</ets>, OF. <ets>semoneor</ets>, F.
<ets>semonneur</ets>. See <er>Summon</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<def>One who summons; one who cites by authority; specifically, a petty
officer formerly employed to summon persons to appear in court; an
apparitor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summons</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mons</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Summonses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>somouns</ets>,
OF. <ets>sumunse</ets>, <ets>semonse</ets>, <ets>semonce</ets>, F.
<ets>semonce</ets>, <ets>semondre</ets> to summon, OF. p. p.
<ets>semons</ets>. See <er>Summon</er>, <pos>v.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of summoning; a call by authority, or by the command of a
superior, to appear at a place named, or to attend to some
duty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Special <qex>summonses</qex> by the king.</q>
<rj><qau>Hallam.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>summons</qex> . . . unfit either to dispute or
disobey.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Fell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He sent to summon the seditious, and to offer pardon; but neither
<qex>summons</qex> nor pardon was regarded.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Hayward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A warning or citation to appear in
court; a written notification signed by the proper officer, to be served
on a person, warning him to appear in court at a day specified, to
answer to the plaintiff, testify as a witness, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A demand to surrender.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summons</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"mons</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To summon.</def> <mark>[R. or
Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Summum bonum</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sum"mum bo"num</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Philos.)</fld> <def>The supreme or highest good, -- referring to
the object of human life.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumner</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"ner</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A summoner.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumoom</ent><br/
<hw>Su*moom"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Simoom</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sump</ent><br/
<hw>Sump</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>sumpf</ets>
a sump in a mine, a swamp, akin to LG. <ets>sump</ets>, D.
<ets>somp</ets> a swamp, Dan. & Sw. <ets>sump</ets>, and perhaps to E.
<ets>swamp</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Metal.)</fld> <def>A round
pit of stone, lined with clay, for receiving the metal on its first
fusion.</def> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The cistern or reservoir made at the lowest point of
a mine, from which is pumped the water which accumulates
there.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A pond of water for salt works.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A puddle or dirty pool.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sump fuse</b></col>, <cd>a fuse used in blasting under
water.</cd> -- <col><b>Sump men</b></col> <fld>(Mining)</fld>, <cd>the
men who sink the sump in a mine.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumph</ent><br/
<hw>Sumph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A dunce; a
blockhead.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumpitan</ent><br/
<hw>Sum"pi*tan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of blowgun
for discharging arrows, -- used by the savages of Borneo and adjacent
islands.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumpter</ent><br/
<hw>Sump"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>sommetier</ets> the driver of a pack horse; akin to OF. & F.
<ets>sommier</ets> a pack horse, L. <ets>sagmarius</ets>, fr.
<ets>sagma</ets> a pack saddle, in LL., a load, Gr. <?/ a pack saddle,
fr. <?/ to pack, load; cf. Skr. <ets>saj</ets>, <ets>sa<ntil/j</ets>, to
hang on. Cf. <er>Seam</er> a weight, <er>Summer</er> a beam.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The driver of a pack horse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Skeat.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A pack; a burden.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>An animal, especially a horse, that carries packs or
burdens; a baggage horse.</def> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumpter</ent><br/
<hw>Sump"ter</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Carrying pack or burdens on the
back; <as>as, a <ex>sumpter</ex> horse; a <ex>sumpter</ex>
mule.</as></def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumption</ent><br/
<hw>Sump"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sumptio</ets>, fr. <ets>sumere</ets>, <ets>sumptum</ets>, to
take.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A taking.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>The major premise of a
syllogism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumptuary</ent><br/
<hw>Sump"tu*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sumptuarius</ets>, fr. <ets>sumptus</ets> expense, cost, fr.
<ets>sumere</ets>, <ets>sumptum</ets>, to take, use, spend;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>emere</ets> to take, buy: cf. F.
<ets>somptuaire</ets>. See <er>Redeem</er>.]</ety> <def>Relating to
expense; regulating expense or expenditure.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Sumptuary laws</b></col> <it>or</it>
<col><b>Sumptuary regulations</b></col></mcol>, <cd>laws intended to
restrain or limit the expenditure of citizens in apparel, food,
furniture, etc.; laws which regulate the prices of commodities and the
wages of labor; laws which forbid or restrict the use of certain
articles, as of luxurious apparel.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sumptuosity</ent><br/
<hw>Sump`tu*os"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sumptuositas</ets>: cf. F. <ets>somptuosit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Expensiveness; costliness; sumptuousness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Raleigh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1445 --></p>

<p><ent>Sumptuous</ent><br/
<hw>Sump"tu*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sumptuosus</ets>, fr. <ets>sumptus</ets> expanse, cost: cf. F.
<ets>somptueux</ets>. See <er>Sumptuary</er>.]</ety> <def>Involving
large outlay or expense; costly; expensive; hence, luxurious; splendid;
magnificient; <as>as, a <ex>sumptuous</ex> house or table;
<ex>sumptuous</ex> apparel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are too magnificient and <qex>sumptuous</qex> in our tables and
attendance.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She spoke, and turned her <qex>sumptuous</qex> head, with eyes<br/
Of shining expectation fixed on mine.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sump"tu*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sump"tu*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sun</ent><br/
<hw>Sun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Sunn</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sun</ent><br/
<hw>Sun</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>sunne</ets>,
<ets>sonne</ets>, AS. <ets>sunne</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>sunne</ets>, D. <ets>zon</ets>, OS. & OHG. <ets>sunna</ets>, G.
<ets>sonne</ets>, Icel. <ets>sunna</ets>, Goth. <ets>sunna</ets>; perh.
fr. same root as L. <ets>sol</ets>. <root/297. Cf. <er>Solar</er>,
<er>South</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The luminous orb, the light of
which constitutes day, and its absence night; the central body round
which the earth and planets revolve, by which they are held in their
orbits, and from which they receive light and heat. Its mean distance
from the earth is about 92,500,000 miles, and its diameter about
860,000.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Its mean apparent diameter as seen from the earth is
32<prime/ 4<sec/, and it revolves on its own axis once in 25<frac13/
days. Its mean density is about one fourth of that of the earth, or
1.41, that of water being unity. Its luminous surface is called the
<xex>photosphere</xex>, above which is an envelope consisting partly of
hydrogen, called the <xex>chromosphere</xex>, which can be seen only
through the spectroscope, or at the time of a total solar eclipse. Above
the chromosphere, and sometimes extending out millions of miles, are
luminous rays or streams of light which are visible only at the time of
a total eclipse, forming the solar <xex>corona</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Any heavenly body which forms the center of a system
of orbs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The direct light or warmth of the sun;
sunshine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lambs that did frisk in the <qex>sun</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>That which resembles the sun, as in splendor or
importance; any source of light, warmth, or animation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the Lord God is a <qex>sun</qex> and shield.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
lxxiv. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will never consent to put out the <qex>sun</qex> of sovereignity
to posterity.</q> <rj><qau>Eikon Basilike.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sun and planet wheels</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>,
<cd>an ingenious contrivance for converting reciprocating motion, as
that of the working beam of a steam engine, into rotatory motion. It
consists of a toothed wheel (called the <xex>sun wheel</xex>), firmly
secured to the shaft it is desired to drive, and another wheel (called
the <xex>planet wheel</xex>) secured to the end of a connecting rod. By
the motion of the connecting rod, the planet wheel is made to circulate
round the central wheel on the shaft, communicating to this latter a
velocity of revolution the double of its own.</cd> <au>G. Francis.</au>
-- <col><b>Sun angel</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a South American
humming bird of the genus <gen>Heliangelos</gen>, noted for its
beautiful colors and the brilliant luster of the feathers of its
throat.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun animalcute</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<cd>See <er>Heliozoa</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun bath</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>exposure of a patient to the sun's rays;
insolation.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun bear</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
species of bear (<spn>Helarctos Malayanus</spn>) native of Southern Asia
and Borneo. It has a small head and short neck, and fine short glossy
fur, mostly black, but brownish on the nose. It is easily tamed. Called
also <altname>bruang</altname>, and <altname>Malayan
bear</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun beetle</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any small lustrous beetle of the genus <gen>Amara</gen>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sun bittern</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a singular South
American bird (<spn>Eurypyga helias</spn>), in some respects related
both to the rails and herons. It is beautifully variegated with white,
brown, and black. Called also <altname>sunbird</altname>, and
<altname>tiger bittern</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun fever</b></col>
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>the condition of fever produced by sun
stroke.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun gem</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
Brazilian humming bird (<spn>Heliactin cornutus</spn>). Its head is
ornamented by two tufts of bright colored feathers, fiery crimson at the
base and greenish yellow at the tip. Called also <altname>Horned
hummer</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun grebe</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the finfoot.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun
picture</b></col>, <cd>a picture taken by the agency of the sun's rays;
a photograph.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun spots</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>,
<cd>dark spots that appear on the sun's disk, consisting commonly of a
black central portion with a surrounding border of lighter shade, and
usually seen only by the telescope, but sometimes by the naked eye. They
are very changeable in their figure and dimensions, and vary in size
from mere apparent points to spaces of 50,000 miles in diameter. The
term <xex>sun spots</xex> is often used to include bright spaces (called
<xex>faculae</xex>) as well as dark spaces (called <xex>maculae</xex>).
Called also <altname>solar spots</altname>. See <xex>Illustration</xex>
in Appendix.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun star</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of several species of starfishes belonging to
<gen>Solaster</gen>, <gen>Crossaster</gen>, and allied genera, having
numerous rays.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun trout</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>the squeteague.</cd> -- <col><b>Sun wheel</b></col>.
<fld>(Mach.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Sun and planet wheels</cref>,
above.</cd> -- <col><b>Under the sun</b></col>, <cd>in the world; on
earth.</cd> <ldquo/There is no new thing <xex>under the
sun</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Eccl. i. 9.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sun</xex> is often used in the formation of
compound adjectives of obvious meaning; as, <xex>sun</xex>-bright,
<xex>sun</xex>-dried, <xex>sun</xex>-gilt, <xex>sun</xex>like,
<xex>sun</xex>-lit, <xex>sun</xex>-scorched, and the like.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sun</ent><br/
<hw>Sun</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Sunned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sunning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To expose to the sun's rays; to
warm or dry in the sun; <as>as, to <ex>sun</ex> cloth; to <ex>sun</ex>
grain.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then to <qex>sun</qex> thyself in open air.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunbeam</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"beam`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sunnebeam</ets>.]</ety> <def>A beam or ray of the sun.</def>
<ldquo/Evening <xex>sunbeams</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Keble.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thither came Uriel, gliding through the even<br/
On a <qex>sunbeam</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunbird</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"bird`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of numerous species of small brilliantly
colored birds of the family <fam>Nectariniidae</fam>, native of Africa,
Southern Asia, the East Indies, and Australia. In external appearance
and habits they somewhat resemble humming birds, but they are true
singing birds (Oscines).</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The sun
bittern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunblink</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"blink`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A glimpse or flash
of the sun.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunbonnet</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"bon"net</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bonnet,
generally made of some thin or light fabric, projecting beyond the face,
and commonly having a cape, -- worn by women as a protection against the
sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunbow</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"bow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A rainbow; an
iris.</def> <rj><au>Byron.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunburn</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"burn`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sunburned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or
<conjf>Sunburnt</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sunburning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To burn or discolor by the
sun; to tan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sunburnt</qex> and swarthy though she be.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunburn</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"burn`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The burning or discoloration
produced on the skin by the heat of the sun; tan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sun-burner</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"-burn`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A circle or
cluster of gas-burners for lighting and ventilating public
buildings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunburning</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"burn`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sunburn; tan.</def>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunburst</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"burst`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A burst of
sunlight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suncup</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"cup`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A yellow flowered
evening primrose (<spn>Taraxia ovata</spn>, syn. <spn>Oenothera
ovata</spn>) native of California.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundart</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"dart`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sunbeam.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Mrs. Hemans.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunday</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"day</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sunnandaeg</ets>; <ets>sunne</ets>, gen. <ets>sunnan</ets>, the sun
+ <ets>daeg</ets> day; akin to D. <ets>zondag</ets>, G.
<ets>sonntag</ets>; -- so called because this day was anciently
dedicated to the <ets>sun</ets>, or to its worship. See <er>Sun</er>,
and <er>Day</er>.]</ety> <def>The first day of the week, -- consecrated
among Christians to rest from secular employments, and to religious
worship; the Christian Sabbath; the Lord's Day.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Advent Sunday</b></col>, <col><b>Low
Sunday</b></col>, <col><b>Passion Sunday</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See
under <er>Advent</er>, <er>Low</er>, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Sabbath</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunday</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"day</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Belonging to the Christian
Sabbath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sunday letter</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Dominical
letter</cref>, under <er>Dominical</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sunday
school</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>School</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunder</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sundered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sundering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sundren</ets>, AS. <ets>sundrain</ets> (in
<amac/<ets>sundrain</ets>, ge<ets>sundrain</ets>), from
<ets>sundor</ets> asunder, separately, apart; akin to D.
<ets>zonder</ets>, prep., without, G. <ets>sonder</ets> separate, as
prep., without, <ets>sondern</ets> but, OHG. <ets>suntar</ets>
separately, Icel. <ets>sundr</ets> asunder, Sw. & Dan.
<ets>s<oum/nder</ets>, Goth. <ets>sundr<omac/</ets> alone,
separately.]</ety> <def>To disunite in almost any manner, either by
rending, cutting, or breaking; to part; to put or keep apart; to
separate; to divide; to sever; <as>as, to <ex>sunder</ex> a rope; to
<ex>sunder</ex> a limb; to <ex>sunder</ex> friends.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is <qex>sundered</qex> from the main land by a sandy plain.</q>
<rj><qau>Carew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunder</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"der</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To part; to separate.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunder</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"der</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sunder</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>, and cf. <er>Asunder</er>.]</ety> <def>A separation into parts;
a division or severance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>In sunder</b></col>, <cd>into parts.</cd> <ldquo/He
breaketh the bow, and cutteth the spear <xex>in sunder</xex>.<rdquo/
<au>Ps. xlvi. 9.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunder</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"der</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To expose to the sun and
wind.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundew</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"dew`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any
plant of the genus Drosera, low bog plants whose leaves are beset with
pediceled glands which secrete a viscid fluid that glitters like
dewdrops and attracts and detains insects. After an insect is caught,
the glands curve inward like tentacles and the leaf digests it. Called
also <altname>lustwort</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundial</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"di`al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An instrument to
show the time of day by means of the shadow of a gnomon, or style, on a
plate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sundial shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any shell
of the genus Solarium. See <er>Solarium</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundog</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"dog`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Meteorol.)</fld>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A luminous spot occasionally seen a few degrees from
the sun, supposed to be formed by the intersection of two or more halos,
or in a manner similar to that of halos.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fragmentary rainbow; a small rainbow near the
horizon; -- called also <altname>dog</altname> and
<altname>weathergaw</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundown</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"down`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
setting of the sun; sunset.</def> <ldquo/When <xex>sundown</xex> skirts
the moor.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A kind of broad-brimmed sun hat worn by
women.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundowner</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"down`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A tramp or
vagabond in the Australian bush; -- so called from his coming to sheep
stations at sunset of ask for supper and a bed, when it is too late to
work; -- called also <altname>traveler</altname> and
<altname>swagman</altname> (but not all <xex>swagmen</xex> are
<ex>sundowners</ex>).</def></p>

<p><q><qex>Sundowners</qex>, -- men who loaf about till sunset, and then
come in with the demand for unrefusable rations.</q> <rj><qau>Francis
Adams.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sun-dried</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"-dried`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dried by the heat
of the sun; <as>as, <ex>sun-dried</ex> tomatoes</as>.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Sun-dried</xex> brick.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T.
Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundries</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"dries</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Many different
or small things; sundry things.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundrily</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"dri*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In sundry ways;
variously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundrops</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"drops`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sun</ets> +
<ets>drop</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Any one of the several
species of <gen>Kneiffia</gen>, esp. <spn>Kneiffia fruticosa</spn> (syn.
<spn>Oenothera fruticosa</spn>), of the Evening-primrose family, having
flowers that open by daylight.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundry</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"dry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sundry</ets>, <ets>sondry</ets>, AS. <ets>syndrig</ets>, fr.
<ets>sundor</ets> asunder. See <er>Sunder</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Several; divers; more than one or two; various.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Sundry</xex> wines.<rdquo/ <au>Chaucer.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Sundry</xex> weighty reasons.<rdquo/ <au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With many a sound of <qex>sundry</qex> melody.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sundry</qex> foes the rural realm surround.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Separate; diverse.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every church almost had the Bible of a <qex>sundry</qex>
translation.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>All and sundry</b></col>, <cd>all collectively, and each
separately.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sundryman</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"dry*man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sundrymen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who deals in
sundries, or a variety of articles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunfish</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A very large oceanic plectognath fish (<spn>Mola
mola</spn>, <spn>Mola rotunda</spn>, or <spn>Orthagoriscus mola</spn>)
having a broad body and a truncated tail.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any
one of numerous species of perch-like North American fresh-water fishes
of the family <fam>Centrachidae</fam>. They have a broad, compressed
body, and strong dorsal spines. Among the common species of the Eastern
United States are <spn>Lepomis gibbosus</spn> (called also
<altname>bream</altname>, <altname>pondfish</altname>, <altname>pumpkin
seed</altname>, and <altname>sunny</altname>), the blue sunfish, or
dollardee (<spn>Lepomis pallidus</spn>), and the long-eared sunfish
(<spn>Lepomis auritus</spn>). Several of the species are called also
<stype>pondfish</stype>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The moonfish, or
bluntnosed shiner.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>The opah.</def> <sd>(e)</sd>
<def>The basking, or liver, shark.</def> <sd>(f)</sd> <def>Any large
jellyfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunflower</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"flow`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any plant of the
genus <gen>Helianthus</gen>; -- so called probably from the form and
color of its flower, which is large disk with yellow rays. The commonly
cultivated sunflower is <spn>Helianthus annuus</spn>, a native of
America.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunflower State</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"flow`er State</hw>. <def>Kansas; a nickname.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sung</ent><br/
<hw>Sung</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sing</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunglass</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"glass`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sunglasses</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A convex lens of glass
for producing heat by converging the sun's rays into a focus.</def>
<ldquo/Lighting a cigar with a <xex>sunglass</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Hawthorne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunglow</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"glow`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A rosy flush in the
sky seen after sunset.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunk</ent><br/
<hw>Sunk</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sink</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sunk fence</b></col>, <cd>a ditch with a retaining wall,
used to divide lands without defacing a landscape; a
ha-ha.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunken</ent><br/
<hw>Sunk"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lying on the bottom of
a river or other water; sunk.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunless</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute or deprived
of the sun or its rays; shaded; shadowed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sunken glen whose <qex>sunless</qex> shrubs must weep.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunlight</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"light`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The light of the
sun.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunlike</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like or resembling
the sun.</def> <ldquo/A spot of <xex>sunlike</xex> brilliancy.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Tyndall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunlit</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"lit`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Lighted by the
sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunn</ent><br/
<hw>Sunn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind. <ets>san</ets>,
fr. Skr. <ets><cced/ana</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An East
Indian leguminous plant (<spn>Crotalaria juncea</spn>) and its fiber,
which is also called <altname>sunn hemp</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>sun</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunna</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sun"na</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>sunnah</ets> rule, law.]</ety> <def>A collection of traditions
received by the orthodox Mohammedans as of equal authority with the
Koran.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunniah</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"ni*ah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the sect of
Sunnites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunniness</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"ni*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being sunny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunnite</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the orthodox
Mohammedans who receive the Sunna as of equal importance with the
Koran.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunnud</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"nud</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Hind., fr. Ar.
<ets>sanad</ets>.]</ety> <def>A charter or warrant; also, a deed of
gift.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunny</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sunnier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sunniest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to
the sun; proceeding from, or resembling the sun; hence, shining; bright;
brilliant; radiant.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sunny</xex> beams.<rdquo/
<au>Spenser.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Sunny</xex> locks.<rdquo/
<au>Shak.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exposed to the rays of the sun; brightened or warmed
by the direct rays of the sun; <as>as, a <ex>sunny</ex> room; the
<ex>sunny</ex> side of a hill</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her blooming mountains and her <qex>sunny</qex> shores.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Cheerful; genial; <as>as, a <ex>sunny</ex>
disposition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My decayed fair<br/
A <qex>sunny</qex> look of his would soon repair.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunny</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"ny</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Sunfish</er> <sd>(b)</sd>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunproof</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"proof`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Impervious to the
rays of the sun.</def> <ldquo/Darksome yew, <xex>sunproof</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunrising</ent><br/
<ent>Sunrise</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sun"rise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sun"ris`ing</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The first appearance of the sun above the
horizon in the morning; more generally, the time of such appearance,
whether in fair or cloudy weather; <as>as, to begin work at
<ex>sunrise</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/The tide of <xex>sunrise</xex>
swells.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Keble.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the region where the sun rises; the
east.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which were beyond Jordan toward the <qex>sunrising</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Deut. iv. 47 (Rev. Ver.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Full hot and fast the Saxon rides, with rein of travel slack,<br/
And, bending o'ev his saddle, leaves the <qex>sunrise</qex> at his
back.</q> <rj><qau>Whittier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunroof</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"roof`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a panel in the roof
of an automobile, usually above the driver's seat, that can be
opened.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunsetting</ent><br/
<ent>Sunset</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sun"set"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sun"set`ting</hw>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The descent of the sun below the horizon;
also, the time when the sun sets; evening. Also used
figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is the <qex>sunset</qex> of life gives me mystical lore.</q>
<rj><qau>Campbell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the region where the sun sets; the
west.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sunset shell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a West
Indian marine bivalve (<spn>Tellina radiata</spn>) having a smooth shell
marked with radiating bands of varied colors resembling those seen at
sunset or before sunrise; -- called also <altname>rising
sun</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunshade</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"shade`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Anything used as a
protection from the sun's rays.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
small parasol.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An awning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunshine</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"shine`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
light of the sun, or the place where it shines; the direct rays of the
sun, the place where they fall, or the warmth and light which they
give.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But all <qex>sunshine</qex>, as when his beams at noon<br/
Culminate from the equator.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything which has a warming and cheering influence
like that of the rays of the sun; warmth; illumination;
brightness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That man that sits within a monarch's heart,<br/
And ripens in the <qex>sunshine</qex> of his favor.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1446 --></p>

<p><ent>Sunshine</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"shine`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sunshiny;
bright.</def> <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Sunshine</xex> hours.<rdquo/
<au>Keble.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunshiny</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"shin`y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Bright
with the rays of the sun; clear, warm, or pleasant; <as>as, a
<ex>sunshiny</ex> day</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Bright like the sun; resplendent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Flashing beams of that <qex>sunshiny</qex> shield.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Beaming with good spirits; cheerful.</def>
<ldquo/Her <xex>sunshiny</xex> face.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunsquall</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"squall`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any large jellyfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sun star</ent><br/
<hw>Sun" star`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See <cref>Sun
star</cref>, under <er>Sun</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunsted</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"sted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sun</ets> +
<ets>stead</ets> a place.]</ety> <def>Solstice.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The summer <xex>sunsted</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunstone</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Aventurine feldspar. See under <er>Aventurine</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunstroke</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"stroke`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Any affection produced by the action of the sun on some part of the
body; especially, a sudden prostration of the physical powers, with
symptoms resembling those of apoplexy, occasioned by exposure to
excessive heat, and often terminating fatally; coup de soleil.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sun-struck</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"-struck`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Overcome by, or affected with, sunstroke; <as>as,
<ex>sun-struck</ex> soldiers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunup</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"up`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sunrise.</def>
<mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such a horse as that might get over a good deal of ground atwixt
<qex>sunup</qex> and sundown.</q> <rj><qau>Cooper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunward</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"ward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Toward the
sun.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sunwise</ent><br/
<hw>Sun"wise`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the direction
of the sun's apparent motion, or from the east southward and westward,
and so around the circle; also, in the same direction as the movement of
the hands of a watch lying face upward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sup</ent><br/
<hw>Sup</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/p)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Supping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>soupen</ets>
to drink, AS. <ets>s<umac/pan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zuipen</ets>, G.
<ets>saufen</ets>, OHG. <ets>s<umac/fan</ets>, Icel.
<ets>s<umac/pa</ets>, Sw. <ets>supa</ets>, Dan. <ets>s<oum/be</ets>. Cf.
<er>Sip</er>, <er>Sop</er>, <er>Soup</er>, <er>Supper</er>.]</ety>
<def>To take into the mouth with the lips, as a liquid; to take or drink
by a little at a time; to sip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There I'll <qex>sup</qex><br/
Balm and nectar in my cup.</q> <rj><qau>Crashaw.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sup</ent><br/
<hw>Sup</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A small mouthful, as of liquor or
broth; a little taken with the lips; a sip.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Tom Thumb had got a little <qex>sup</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sup</ent><br/
<hw>Sup</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Supper</er>.]</ety> <def>To
eat the evening meal; to take supper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I do entreat that we may <qex>sup</qex> together.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sup</ent><br/
<hw>Sup</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To treat with supper.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sup</qex> them well and look unto them all.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supawn</ent><br/
<hw>Su*pawn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Of American Indian
origin.]</ety> <def>Boiled Indian meal; hasty pudding; mush.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>sepawn</asp>, <asp>sepon</asp>, and
<asp>suppawn</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supe</ent><br/
<hw>Supe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A super.</def>
<mark>[Theatrical Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Super-</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>super</ets> over, above;
akin to Gr. <?/, L. <ets>sub</ets> under, and E. <ets>over</ets>. See
<er>Over</er>, and cf. <er>Hyper-</er>, <er>Sub-</er>, <er>Supra-</er>,
<er>Sur-</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A prefix signifying
<xex>above</xex>, <xex>over</xex>, <xex>beyond</xex>, and hence often
denoting <xex>in a superior position</xex>, <xex>in excess</xex>,
<xex>over and above</xex>, <xex>in addition</xex>,
<xex>exceedingly</xex>; as in <xex>super</xex>impose,
<xex>super</xex>sede, <xex>super</xex>natural,
<xex>super</xex>abundance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A prefix formerly much used to
denote that the ingredient to the name of which it was prefixed was
present in a <xex>large</xex>, or <xex>unusually large</xex>, proportion
as compared with the other ingredients; as in calcium
<xex>super</xex>phosphate. It has been superseded by <xex>per-</xex>,
<xex>bi-</xex>, <xex>di-</xex>, <xex>acid</xex>, etc. (as
<xex>per</xex>oxide, <xex>bi</xex>carbonate, <xex>di</xex>sulphide, and
<xex>acid</xex> sulphate), which retain the old meanings of
<xex>super-</xex>, but with sharper definition. Cf. <er>Acid</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>, <er>Bi-</er>, <er>Di-</er>, and <er>Per-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Super</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A contraction of
<er>Supernumerary</er>, in sense 2.</def> <mark>[Theatrical
Cant]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superable</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superabilis</ets>, from <ets>superare</ets> to go over, to
surmount, fr. <ets>super</ets> above, over.]</ety> <def>Capable of being
overcome or conquered; surmountable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Antipathies are generally <qex>superable</qex> by a single
effort.</q> <rj><qau>Johnson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Su"per*a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Su"per*a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superabound</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*a*bound"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superabundare</ets>: cf. OF. <ets>superabonder</ets>. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Abound</er>.]</ety> <def>To be very abundant or
exuberant; to be more than sufficient; <as>as, the country
<ex>superabounds</ex> with corn</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superabundance</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*a*bun"dance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superabundantia</ets>: cf. OF. <ets>superabondance</ets>.]</ety>
<def>The quality or state of being superabundant; a superabundant
quantity; redundancy; excess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superabundant</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*a*bun"dant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superabundans</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>superabundare</ets>. See
<er>Superabound</er>.]</ety> <def>Abounding to excess; being more than
is sufficient; redundant; <as>as, <ex>superabundant</ex>
zeal</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Su`per*a*bun"dant*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superacidulated</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*a*cid"u*la`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Acidulated to excess.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superadd</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*add"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superadded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Superadding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L. <ets>superaddere</ets>.
See <er>Super-</er>, and <er>Add</er>.]</ety> <def>To add over and
above; to add to what has been added; to annex, as something
extrinsic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The strength of any living creature, in those external motion, is
something distinct from, and <qex>superadded</qex> unto, its natural
gravity.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Wilkins.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The peacock laid it extremely to heart that he had not the
nightingale's voice <qex>superadded</qex> to the beauty of his
plumes.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superaddition</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ad*di"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
adding something in excess or something extraneous; also, something
which is added in excess or extraneously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>superaddition</qex> is nothing but fat.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superadvenient</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ad*ven"ient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Coming
upon; coming in addition to, or in assistance of, something.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He has done bravely by the <qex>superadvenient</qex> assistance of
his God.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superalimentation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*al`i*men*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
act of overfeeding, or making one take food in excess of the natural
appetite for it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superaltar</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*al`tar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>A raised shelf or stand on the back of an altar, on which different
objects can be placed; a predella or gradino.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superangelic</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*an*gel"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Superior to
the angels in nature or rank.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superannuate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*an"nu*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superannuated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Superannuating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>super-</ets> +
L. <ets>annus</ets> a year.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To impair or
disquality on account of age or infirmity.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give a pension to, on account of old age or other
infirmity; to cause to retire from service on a pension.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superannuate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*an"nu*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To last
beyond the year; -- said of annual plants.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superannuation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*an`nu*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state
of being superannuated, or too old for office or business; the state of
being disqualified by old age; decrepitude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The world itself is in a state of <qex>superannuation</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Slyness blinking through the watery eye of
<qex>superannuation</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superb</ent><br/
<hw>Su*perb"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>superbe</ets>, L. <ets>superbus</ets>, fr. <ets>super</ets> over.
See <er>Super-</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Grand; magnificent; august;
stately; <as>as, a <ex>superb</ex> edifice; a <ex>superb</ex>
colonnade</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Rich; elegant; <as>as, <ex>superb</ex> furniture or
decorations</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Showy; excellent; grand; <as>as, a <ex>superb</ex>
exhibition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Superb paradise bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
bird of paradise (<spn>Paradisaea superba</spn> syn. <spn>Lophorina
superba</spn>) having the scapulars erectile, and forming a large
ornamental tuft on each shoulder, and a large gorget of brilliant
feathers on the breast . The color is deep violet, or nearly black, with
brilliant green reflections. The gorget is bright metallic green.</cd>
-- <col><b>Superb warber</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<cref>Blue wren</cref>, under <er>Wren</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Su*perb"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Su*perb"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superbiate</ent><br/
<hw>Su*per"bi*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>superbiare</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make (a person) haughty.</def>
<mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark> <rj><au>Feltham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercarbonate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*car"bon*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A bicarbonate.</def> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercarbureted</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*car"bu*ret`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Bicarbureted.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>supercarburetted</asp>.]</altsp> <mark>[Obsoles.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercargo</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*car"go</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Super-</ets> + <ets>cargo</ets>: cf. Sp.
<ets>sobrecargo.</ets> Cf. <er>Surcharge</er>.]</ety> <def>An officer or
person in a merchant ship, whose duty is to manage the sales, and
superintend the commercial concerns, of the voyage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercarpal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*car"pal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated above, or in the upper part of, the carpus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercelestial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ce*les"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + <ets>celestial</ets>: cf. L.
<ets>supercaelestis</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Situated above the
firmament, or great vault of heaven.</def>
<rj><au>Waterland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Higher than celestial; superangelic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercharge</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*charge"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supercharged</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Supercharging</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>super-</ets> +
<ets>charge</ets>. Cf. <er>Surcharge</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>To charge (a bearing) upon another bearing; <as>as, to
<ex>supercharge</ex> a rose upon a fess</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercharge</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*charge"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Her.)</fld>
<def>A bearing charged upon another bearing.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superchemical</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*chem"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Above or
beyond chemistry; inexplicable by chemical laws.</def> <rj><au>J. Le
Conte.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superchery</ent><br/
<hw>Su*perch"er*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>supercherie</ets>.]</ety> <def>Deceit; fraud; imposition.</def>
<mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superciliary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*cil"i*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supercilium</ets> an eyebrow. See <er>Supercilious</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the eyebrows;
supraorbital.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having a distinct streak of color
above the eyes; <as>as, the <ex>superciliary</ex>
woodpecker</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercilious</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*cil"i*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superciliosus</ets>, fr. <ets>supercilium</ets> an eyebrow, pride;
<ets>super</ets> over, + <ets>cilium</ets> an eyelid; probably akin to
<ets>celare</ets> to conceal. Cf. <er>Conceal</er>.]</ety> <def>Lofty
with pride; haughty; dictatorial; overbearing; arrogant; <as>as, a
<ex>supercilious</ex> officer; a<ex>supercilious</ex> air;
<ex>supercilious</ex> behavior.</as></def> --
<wordforms><wf>Su`per*cil"i*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Su`per*cil"i*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercilium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su`per*cil"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The eyebrow, or the region of the
eyebrows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercolumniation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*co*lum`ni*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>The putting of one order above another; also, an
architectural work produced by this method; <as>as, the putting of the
Doric order in the ground story, Ionic above it, and Corinthian or
Composite above this</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superconception</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*con*cep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Superfetation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superconsequence</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*con"se*quence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Remote
consequence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercrescence</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*cres"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Supercrescent</er>.]</ety> <def>That which grows upon another
growing thing; a parasite.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercrescent</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*cres"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supercrescens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>supercrescere</ets>;
<ets>super</ets> above + <ets>crescere</ets> to grow.]</ety>
<def>Growing on some other growing thing.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercretaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*cre*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Same as <er>Supracretaceous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- supercritical. Being at a temperature and pressure above the
critical temperature and pressure. In a condition neither liquid nor
gaseous. Supercritical carbon dioxide is used as a solvent in separation
chemistry. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supercurious</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*cu"ri*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Excessively
curious or inquisitive.</def> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superdominant</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*dom"i*nant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>The sixth tone of the scale; that next above the dominant; --
called also <altname>submediant</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superdreadnought</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*dread"nought`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Dreadnought</er>, above.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supereminency</ent><br/
<ent>Supereminence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`per*em"i*nence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Su`per*em"i*nen*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supereminentia</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
supereminent; distinguished eminence; <as>as, the <ex>supereminence</ex>
of Cicero as an orator, or Lord Chatham as a statesman</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Ayliffe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was not forever beset with the consciousness of his own
<qex>supereminence</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Prof. Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supereminent</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*em"i*nent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supereminens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>supereminere</ets>. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Eminent</er>.]</ety> <def>Eminent in a superior
degree; surpassing others in excellence; <as>as, a <ex>supereminent</ex>
divine; the <ex>supereminent</ex> glory of Christ</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Su`per*em"i*nent*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supererogant</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*er"o*gant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supererogans</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Supererogate</er>.]</ety>
<def>Supererogatory.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supererogate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*er"o*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supererogated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Supererogating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>supererogatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>supererogare</ets> to spend or
pay out over and above; <ets>super</ets> over, above +
<ets>erogare</ets> to expend or pay out money from the public treasury
after asking the consent of the people. See <er>Super-</er>, and
<er>Erogate</er>, <er>Rogation</er>.]</ety> <def>To do more than duty
requires; to perform works of supererogation; to atone (for a dificiency
in another) by means of a surplus action or quality.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The fervency of one man in prayer can not <qex>supererogate</qex>
for the coldness of another.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supererogation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*er`o*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supererogatio</ets> a payment in addition.]</ety> <def>The act of
supererogating; performance of more than duty or necessity
requires.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Works of supererogation</b></col> <fld>(R. C. Ch.)</fld>,
<cd>those good deeds believed to have been performed by saints, or
capable of being performed by men, over and above what is required for
their own salvation.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supererogative</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*e*rog"a*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Supererogatory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supererogatory</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*e*rog"a*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Performed
to an extent not enjoined, or not required, by duty or necessity;
<as>as, <ex>supererogatory</ex> services</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superessential</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*es*sen"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Essential
above others, or above the constitution of a thing.</def> <rj><au>J.
Ellis.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superethical</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*eth"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>More than
ethical; above ethics.</def> <rj><au>Bolingbroke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superexalt</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ex*alt"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superexalted</conjf>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Superexalting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To exalt
to a superior degree; to exalt above others.</def>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superexaltation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ex`al*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Elevation
above the common degree.</def> <rj><au>Holyday.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superexcellence</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ex"cel*lence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Superior
excellence; extraordinary excellence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superexcellent</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ex"cel*lent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + <ets>excellent</ets>: cf. L.
<ets>superexcellens</ets>.]</ety> <def>Excellent in an uncommon degree;
very excellent.</def> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superexcitation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ex`ci*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Excessive, or more than normal, excitation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superexcrescence</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ex*cres"cence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Something
growing superfluously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfamily</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*fam`i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A group intermediate between a family and a suborder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfecundation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fec`un*da"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Fertilization of two ova, at the same
menstruation, by two different acts of coition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfecundity</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fe*cun"di*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Superabundant fecundity or multiplication of the species.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfetate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fe"tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superfetare</ets>; <ets>super</ets> above, over + <ets>fetare</ets>
to bring forth.]</ety> <def>To conceive after a prior conception, but
before the birth of the offspring.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The female . . . is said to <qex>superfetate</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Grew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfetation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fe*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>superf<eacute/tation</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The
formation of a fetus at the result of an impregnation occurring after
another impregnation but before the birth of the offspring produced by
it. This is possible only when there is a double uterus, or where
menstruation persists up to the time of the second
impregnation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In then became a <qex>superfetation</qex> upon, and not an
ingredient in, the national character.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfete</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fete"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To
superfetate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfete</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fete"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To conceive (another fetus)
after a former conception.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfice</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*fice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
superficies.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superficial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fi"cial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superficialis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>superficiel</ets>. See
<er>Superficies</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to the
superficies, or surface; lying on the surface; shallow; not deep;
<as>as, a <ex>superficial</ex> color; a <ex>superficial</ex> covering;
<ex>superficial</ex> measure or contents; <ex>superficial</ex>
tillage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Reaching or comprehending only what is obvious or
apparent; not deep or profound; shallow; -- said especially in respect
to study, learning, and the like; <as>as, a <ex>superficial</ex>
scholar; <ex>superficial</ex> knowledge</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This <qex>superficial</qex> tale<br/
Is but a preface of her worthy praise.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is a presumptuous and <qex>superficial</qex> writer.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That <qex>superficial</qex> judgment, which happens to be right
without deserving to be so.</q> <rj><qau>J. H. Newman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Su`per*fi"cial*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Su`per*fi"cial*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superficialist</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fi"cial*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who attends to
anything superficially; a superficial or shallow person; a sciolist; a
smatterer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superficiality</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fi`ci*al"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>superficialit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of
being superficial; also, that which is superficial.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superficialize</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fi"cial*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
attend to, or to treat, superficially, or in a shallow or slighting
way.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a characteristic weakness of the day to
<qex>superficialize</qex> evil.</q> <rj><qau>E. P.
Whipple.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superficiary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fi"ci*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Rom.
Law)</fld> <def>One to whom a right of surface occupation is granted;
one who pays quitrent for a house built upon another man's
ground.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superficiary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fi"ci*a*ry</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to the superficies, or surface; superficial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Law)</fld> <def>Situated or built on another
man's land, as a house.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superficies</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fi"cies</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr.
<ets>super</ets> above, over + <ets>facies</ets> make, figure, shape.
See <er>Surface</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The surface; the exterior
part, superficial area, or face of a thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Everything on
the surface of a piece of ground, or of a building, so closely connected
by art or nature as to constitute a part of it, as houses, or other
superstructures, fences, trees, vines, etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
real right consisting of a grant by a landed proprietor of a piece of
ground, bearing a strong resemblance to the long building leases granted
by landholders in England, in consideration of a rent, and under
reservation of the ownership of the soil.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.
Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfine</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*fine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Very
fine, or most fine; being of surpassing fineness; of extra nice or fine
quality; <as>as, <ex>superfine</ex> cloth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Excessively fine; too nice; over particular; <as>as,
<ex>superfine</ex> distinctions; <ex>superfine</ex>
tastes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfineness</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*fine`ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being
superfine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfinical</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fin"i*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Extremely
finical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfluence</ent><br/
<hw>Su*per"flu*ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superfluens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>superfluere</ets> to flow or run
over. See <er>Superfluous</er>.]</ety> <def>Superfluity.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfluitant</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*flu"i*tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>super</ets> above + <ets>fluitans</ets>, p. pr. of
<ets>fluitare</ets> intensive fr. <ets>fluere</ets> to flow.]</ety>
<def>Floating above or on the surface.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Sir
T. Browne.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Su`per*flu"i*tance</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <mark>[Obs.]</mark></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1447 --></p>

<p><ent>Superfluity</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*flu"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Superfluities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L.
<ets>superfluit<eacute/</ets>, L. <ets>superfluitas</ets>. See
<er>Superfluous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A greater quantity than is
wanted; superabundance; <as>as, a <ex>superfluity</ex> of water; a
<ex>superfluity</ex> of wealth.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A quiet mediocrity is still to be preferred before a troubled
<qex>superfluity</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Suckling.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state or quality of being superfluous;
excess.</def> <ldquo/By a <xex>superfluity</xex> abominable.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Something beyond what is needed; something which
serves for show or luxury.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Superabundance; excess; redundancy.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfluous</ent><br/
<hw>Su*per"flu*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superfluus</ets> overflowing; <ets>super</ets> over, above +
<ets>fluere</ets> to flow. See <er>Super-</er>, and
<er>Fluent</er>.]</ety> <def>More than is wanted or is sufficient;
rendered unnecessary by superabundance; unnecessary; useless; excessive;
<as>as, a <ex>superfluous</ex> price</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>An authority which makes all further argument or illustration
<qex>superfluous</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>E. Everett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Superfluous interval</b></col> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>an
interval that exceeds a major or perfect interval by a
semitone.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Unnecessary; useless; exuberant; excessive;
redundant; needless.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Su*per"flu*ous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Su*per"flu*ous*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superflux</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*flux</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Superabundance;
superfluity; an overflowing.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfoetation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*foe*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Superfetation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfoliation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fo`li*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Excess of
foliation.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfrontal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fron"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>A cloth which is placed over the top of an altar, and often hangs
down a few inches over the frontal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superfuse</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*fuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>To pour
(something) over or on something else.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superheat</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*heat"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superheated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Superheating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To heat too
much, to overheat; <as>as, to <ex>superheat</ex> an oven</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Steam Engine)</fld> <def>To heat, as steam, apart
from contact with water, until it resembles a perfect gas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To heat (a liquid) above its boiling point without
converting it into vapor.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superheat</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*heat`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The increase of temperature
communicated to steam by superheating it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superheated</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*heat`ed</hw> <def>heated to a temperature above the boiling
point at the ambient pressure; -- said of liquids. In such an unstable
condition, a small disturbance of the liquid can cause a rapid and
sometimes violent ebullition of vapor.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superheater</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*heat`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Steam
Engine)</fld> <def>An apparatus for superheating steam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superhive</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*hive`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A removable upper
part of a hive. The word is sometimes contracted to
<xex>super</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superhuman</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*hu"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Above or beyond
what is human; sometimes, divine; <as>as, <ex>superhuman</ex> strength;
<ex>superhuman</ex> wisdom</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superimpose</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*im*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superimposed</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Superimposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To lay or impose on
something else; <as>as, a stratum of earth <ex>superimposed</ex> on
another stratum</as>.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Su`per*im`po*si"tion</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superimpregnation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*im`preg*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act
of impregnating, or the state of being impregnated, in addition to a
prior impregnation; superfetation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superincumbency</ent><br/
<ent>Superincumbence</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`per*in*cum"bence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Su`per*in*cum"ben*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The quality or state of being superincumbent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superincumbent</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*cum"bent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superincumbens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>superincumbere</ets>. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Incumbent</er>.]</ety> <def>Lying or resting on
something else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superinduce</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*duce"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superinduced</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Superinducing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>super-</ets> +
<ets>induce</ets>: cf. L. <ets>superinducere</ets> to draw over.]</ety>
<def>To bring in, or upon, as an addition to something.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Long custom of sinning <qex>superinduces</qex> upon the soul new
and absurd desires.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superinducement</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*duce"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Superinduction.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superinduction</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*duc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
superinducing, or the state of being superinduced.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superinfuse</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*fuse"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + <ets>infuse</ets>: cf. L. <ets>superinfundere</ets>,
<ets>superinfusum</ets>, to pour over.]</ety> <def>To infuse over.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superinjection</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*jec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An
injection succeeding another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superinspect</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*spect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + <ets>inspect</ets>: cf. L.
<ets>superinspicere</ets>, <ets>superinspectum</ets>.]</ety> <def>To
over see; to superintend by inspection.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Maydman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superinstitution</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in`sti*tu"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One
institution upon another, as when A is instituted and admitted to a
benefice upon a title, and B instituted and admitted upon the
presentation of another.</def> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superintellectual</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in`tel*lec"tu*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being
above intellect.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superintend</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*tend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superintended</conjf>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Superintending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>superintendere</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>, and
<er>Intend</er>.]</ety> <def>To have or exercise the charge and
oversight of; to oversee with the power of direction; to take care of
with authority; to supervise; <as>as, an officer <ex>superintends</ex>
the building of a ship or the construction of a fort</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king may appoint a council, who may <qex>superintend</qex> the
works of this nature.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- <er>Superintend</er>, <er>Supervise</er>.</syn>
<usage> These words in general use are the synonymous. As sometimes
used, <xex>supervise</xex> implies the more general, and
<xex>superintend</xex>, the more particular and constant, inspection or
direction. Among architects there is a disposition to use the word
<xex>supervise</xex> in the sense of a general oversight of the main
points of construction with reference to the design, etc., and to employ
the word <xex>superintend</xex> to signify a constant, careful attention
to all the details of construction. But this technical distinction is
not firmly established.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superintendence</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*tend"ence</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>superintendance</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of superintending; care
and oversight for the purpose of direction; supervision.</def>
<rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Inspection; oversight; care; direction; control;
guidance.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superintendency</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*tend"en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>-cies</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>The act of
superintending; superintendence.</def> <rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superintendent</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*tend"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superintendens</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Superintend</er>.]</ety>
<def>Overseeing; superintending.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superintendent</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*tend"ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>superintendant</ets>, F. <ets>surintendant</ets>. Cf.
<er>Surintendant</er>.]</ety> <def>One who has the oversight and charge
of some place, institution, or organization, affairs, etc., with the
power of direction; <as>as, the <ex>superintendent</ex> of an almshouse;
the <ex>superintendent</ex> of public works</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Inspector; overseer; manager; director; curator;
supervisor.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superintender</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*tend"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
superintendent.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superinvestiture</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*in*vest"i*ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An outer
vestment or garment.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Horne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superior</ent><br/
<hw>Su*pe"ri*or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L., compar. of
<ets>superus</ets> being above, fr. <ets>super</ets> above, over: cf. F.
<ets>sup<eacute/rieur</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>, and cf.
<er>Supreme</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>More elevated in place or
position; higher; upper; <as>as, the <ex>superior</ex> limb of the sun;
the <ex>superior</ex> part of an image</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Higher in rank or office; more exalted in dignity;
<as>as, a <ex>superior</ex> officer; a <ex>superior</ex> degree of
nobility</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Higher or greater in excellence; surpassing others
in the greatness, or value of any quality; greater in quality or degree;
<as>as, a man of <ex>superior</ex> merit; or of <ex>superior</ex>
bravery</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Beyond the power or influence of; too great or firm
to be subdued or affected by; -- with <xex>to</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is not in earth a spectacle more worthy than a great man
<qex>superior</qex> to his sufferings.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>More comprehensive; as a term in classification;
<as>as, a genus is <ex>superior</ex> to a species</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Above the ovary; --
said of parts of the flower which, although normally below the ovary,
adhere to it, and so appear to originate from its upper part; also of an
ovary when the other floral organs are plainly below it in position, and
free from it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Belonging to the part of an
axillary flower which is toward the main stem; posterior.</def>
<sd>(c)</sd> <def>Pointing toward the apex of the fruit; ascending; --
said of the radicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Superior conjunction</b></col>, <col><b>Superior
planets</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See <er>Conjunction</er>,
<er>Planet</er>, etc.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Superior figure</b></col>,
<col><b>Superior letter</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Print.)</fld>, <cd>a
figure or letter printed above the line, as a reference to a note or an
index of a power, etc; <as>as, in <mathex>x<exp>2</exp> +
y<exp>n</exp></mathex>, 2 is a <ex>superior figure</ex>, <it>n</it> a
<ex>superior letter</ex></as>. Cf. <cref>Inferior figure</cref>, under
<er>Inferior</er>.</cd><-- = superscripted number, letter --></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superior</ent><br/
<hw>Su*pe"ri*or</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who is above,
or surpasses, another in rank, station, office, age, ability, or merit;
one who surpasses in what is desirable; <as>as, Addison has no
<ex>superior</ex> as a writer of pure English</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The head of a monastery, convent,
abbey, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superioress</ent><br/
<hw>Su*pe"ri*or*ess</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A woman
who acts as chief in a convent, abbey, or nunnery; a lady
superior.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superiority</ent><br/
<hw>Su*pe`ri*or"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sup<eacute/riorit<eacute/</ets>, LL.
<ets>superioritas</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality, state, or condition of
being superior; <as>as, <ex>superiority</ex> of rank;
<ex>superiority</ex> in merit.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Preeminence; excellence; predominancy;
prevalence; ascendency; odds; advantage.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superiorly</ent><br/
<hw>Su*pe"ri*or*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a superior
position or manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superjacent</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ja"cent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superjacens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>superjacere</ets>;
<ets>super</ets> above + <ets>jacere</ets> to lie.]</ety> <def>Situated
immediately above; <as>as, <ex>superjacent</ex> rocks</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superlation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superlatio</ets>. See <er>Superlative</er>.]</ety> <def>Exaltation
of anything beyond truth or propriety.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superlative</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*la"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superlativus</ets>, fr. <ets>superlatus</ets> excessive, used as p.
p. of <ets>superiorferre</ets>, but from a different root: cf. F.
<ets>superlatif</ets>. See <er>Elate</er>, <er>Tolerate</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Lifted up to the highest degree; most eminent;
surpassing all other; supreme; <as>as, <ex>superlative</ex> wisdom or
prudence; a woman of <ex>superlative</ex> beauty; the
<ex>superlative</ex> glory of the divine character.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Expressing the highest or lowest
degree of the quality, manner, etc., denoted by an adjective or an
adverb. The superlative degree is formed from the positive by the use of
<xex>-est</xex>, <xex>most</xex>, or <xex>least</xex>; <as>as,
high<ex>est</ex>, <ex>most</ex> pleasant, <ex>least</ex>
bright</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Su`per*la"tive*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Su`per*la"tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superlative</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*la"tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which is
highest or most eminent; the utmost degree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The superlative
degree of adjectives and adverbs; also, a form or word by which the
superlative degree is expressed; <as>as, <ex>strongest</ex>,
<ex>wisest</ex>, <ex>most stormy</ex>, <ex>least windy</ex>, are all
<ex>superlatives</ex></as>.</def> <-- #sic. there is no definition (b)!
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Absolute superlative</b></col>, <cd>a superlative in an
absolute rather than in a comparative or exclusive sense. See
<er>Elative</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superlucration</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*lu*cra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + L. <ets>lucratio</ets> gain.]</ety> <def>Excessive
or extraordinary gain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Davenant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superlunary</ent><br/
<ent>Superlunar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`per*lu"nar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Su`per*lu"na*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being above the moon; not
belonging to this world; -- opposed to <xex>sublunary</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The head that turns at <qex>superlunar</qex> things.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superman</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*man`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>same
as <er>Overman</er>, above.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a fictional character of cartoons, movies and
television, with superhuman powers such as great strength, the ability
to fly, and x-ray vision. In the cartoon tale, he was born on the planet
Krypton and sent by his parents into space before it exploded, and
landed on earth, where he fights for "truth, justice, and the American
way". He works incognito as a reporter at the Daily Planet, and is
constantly trying to avoid the uncovering of his secret identity by a
co-worker, Lois Lane.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supermaterial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ma*te"ri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being
above, or superior to, matter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supermaxilla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su`per*max*il"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Maxilla</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The supermaxilla.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supermaxillary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*max"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Supermaxillary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supermedial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*me"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Above the
middle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supermundane</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*mun"dane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being above
the world; -- opposed to <xex>inframundane</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Cudworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supermundial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*mun"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Supermundane.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernacular</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nac"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like
supernaculum; first-rate; <as>as, a <ex>supernacular</ex>
wine</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernaculum</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nac"u*lum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv. & n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
from L. <ets>super</ets> over + G. <ets>nagel</ets>, a nail, as of the
finger, or a corruption of L. <ets>super</ets> and <ets>ungulam</ets>
claw.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of mock Latin term intended to
mean, upon the nail; -- used formerly by topers.</def>
<rj><au>Nares.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Drinking <qex>super nagulum</qex> [<qex>supernaculum</qex>], a
device of drinking, new come out of France, which is, after a man hath
turned up the bottom of the cup, to drop it on his nail and make a pearl
with that is left; which if it slide, and he can not make it stand on by
reason there is too much, he must drink again for his penance.</q>
<rj><qau>Nash.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Good liquor, of which not enough is left to wet
one's nail.</def> <rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernal</ent><br/
<hw>Su*per"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supernus</ets>, from <ets>super</ets> above: cf. F.
<ets>supernel</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Being
in a higher place or region; locally higher; <as>as, the
<ex>supernal</ex> orbs; <ex>supernal</ex> regions</as>.</def>
<ldquo/That <xex>supernal</xex> judge.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relating or belonging to things above; celestial;
heavenly; <as>as, <ex>supernal</ex> grace</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not by the sufferance of <qex>supernal</qex> power.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernatant</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*na"tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supernatanus</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>supernatare</ets> to swim above;
<ets>super</ets> above + <ets>natare</ets> to swim.]</ety> <def>Swimming
above; floating on the surface; <as>as, oil <ex>supernatant</ex> on
water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernatant</ent><br/
<--<hw>Su`per*na"tant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The liquid remaining after solids suspended in the liquid have been sedimented by gravity or by centrifugation.  Contrasted with the solid <contr>sediment</contr>, or (in centrifugation) the <contr>pellet</contr>.</def> --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernatation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*na*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of floating on the surface of a fluid.</def>  <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernatural</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nat"u*ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>super-</ets> + <ets>natural</ets>: cf. OF. <ets>supernaturel</ets>, F. <ets>surnaturel</ets>.]</ety> <def>Being beyond, or exceeding, the power or laws of nature; miraculous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Preternatural.</syn>  -- <usage><er>Supernatural</er>, <er>Preternatural</er>. <xex>Preternatural</xex> signifies <xex>beside</xex> nature, and <xex>supernatural</xex>, <xex>above</xex> or <xex>beyond</xex> nature. What is very greatly aside from the ordinary course of things is <xex>preternatural</xex>; what is above or beyond the established laws of the universe is <xex>supernatural</xex>. The dark day which terrified all Europe nearly a century ago was <xex>preternatural</xex>; the resurrection of the dead is <xex>supernatural</xex>. <ldquo/That form which the earth is under at present is <xex>preternatural</xex>, like a statue made and broken again.<rdquo/ <au>T. Burnet.</au> <ldquo/Cures wrought by medicines are natural operations; but the miraculous ones wrought by Christ and his apostles were <xex>supernatural</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Boyle.</au></usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That is <qex>supernatural</qex>, whether it be, that is either not in the chain of natural cause and effect, or which acts on the chain of cause and effect in nature, from without the chain.</q> <rj><qau>Bushnell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We must not view creation as <qex>supernatural</qex>, but we do look upon it as miraculous.</q> <rj><qau>McCosh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The supernatural</b></col>, <cd>whatever is above and beyond the scope, or the established course, of the laws of nature.</cd> <ldquo/Nature and the <xex>supernatural</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>H. Bushnell.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernaturalism</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nat"u*ral*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality or state of being supernatural; supernaturalness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The doctrine of a divine and supernatural agency in the production of the miracles and revelations recorded in the Bible, and in the grace which renews and sanctifies men, -- in opposition to the doctrine which denies the agency of any other than physical or natural causes in the case.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>supranaturalism</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernaturalist</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nat"u*ral*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who holds to the principles of supernaturalism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernaturalistic</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nat`u*ral*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to supernaturalism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernaturality</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nat`u*ral"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being supernatural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernaturalize</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nat"u*ral*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To treat or regard as supernatural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernaturally</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nat"u*ral*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a supernatural manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernaturalness</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nat"u*ral*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being supernatural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernumerary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nu"mer*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>supernumerarius</ets>: cf. OF. <ets>supernum<eacute/raire</ets>, F. <ets>surnum<eacute/raire</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>, and <er>Numerary</er>, <er>Number</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Exceeding the number stated or prescribed; <as>as, a <ex>supernumerary</ex> officer in a regiment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Exceeding a necessary, usual, or required number or quality; superfluous; <as>as, <ex>supernumerary</ex> addresses; <ex>supernumerary</ex> expense</as>.</def>  <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supernumerary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*nu"mer*a*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Supernumeraries</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A person or thing beyond the number stated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A person or thing beyond what is necessary or usual; especially, a person employed not for regular service, but only to fill the place of another in case of need; specifically, in theaters, a person who is not a regular actor, but is employed to appear in a stage spectacle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superoccipital</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*oc*cip"i*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Supraoccipital.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superorder</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*or"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A group intermediate in importance between an order and a subclass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superordination</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*or`di*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>super-</ets> + <ets>ordination</ets>: cf. L. <ets>superordinatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The ordination of a person to fill a station already occupied; especially, the ordination by an ecclesiastical official, during his lifetime, of his successor.</def>  <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superoxide</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ox"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>See <er>Peroxide</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superparticular</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*par*tic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>superparticularis</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>, and <er>Particular</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a ratio when the excess of the greater term over the less is a unit, as the ratio of 1 to 2, or of 3 to 4.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superpartient</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*par"tient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>superpartiens</ets>; <ets>super</ets> over + <ets>partiens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>partire</ets> to divide.]</ety> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a ratio when the excess of the greater term over the less is more than a unit, as that of 3 to 5, or 7 to 10.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superphosphate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*phos"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An acid phosphate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Superphosphate of lime</b></col> <fld>(Com. Chem.)</fld>, <cd>a fertilizer obtained by trating bone dust, bone black, or phosphorite with sulphuric acid, whereby the insoluble neutral calcium phosphate, <chform>Ca3(PO4)2</chform>, is changed to the primary or acid calcium phosphate <chform>Ca(H2PO4)2</chform>, which is soluble and therefore available for the soil.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superphysical</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*phys"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Above or beyond physics; not explainable by physical laws.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Something <qex>superphysical</qex> and superchemical.</q> <rj><qau>J. Le Conte.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superplant</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*plant`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A plant growing on another, as the mistletoe; an epiphyte.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superplease</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*please"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To please exceedingly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superplus</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*plus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>super-</ets> + L. <ets>plus</ets> more. See <er>Surplus</er>.]</ety> <def>Surplus.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Goldsmith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superplusage</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*plus`age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Surplusage.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/There yet remained a <xex>superplusage</xex>.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Bp. Fell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superpolitic</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*pol"i*tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>More than politic; above or exceeding policy.</def>  <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1448 --></p>

<p><ent>Superponderate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*pon"der*ate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To wiegh
over and above.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superposable</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*pos"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being superposed, as one figure upon another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superpose</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superposed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Superposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>superposer</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>, and <er>Pose</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To lay upon, as one kind of rock on another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>To lay (a figure) upon another in
such a manner that all the parts of the one coincide with the parts of
the other; <as>as, to <ex>superpose</ex> one plane figure on
another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superposition</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*po*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>superposition</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>, and
<er>Position</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of superposing, or the state of
being superposed; <as>as, the <ex>superposition</ex> of rocks; the
<ex>superposition</ex> of one plane figure on another, in
geometry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superpraise</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*praise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To praise to
excess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To vow, and swear, and <qex>superpraise</qex> my parts.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superproportion</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*pro*por"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Overplus
or excess of proportion.</def> <rj><au>Sir K. Digby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superpurgation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*pur*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Excessive
purgation.</def> <rj><au>Wiseman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superreflection</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*re*flec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
reflection of a reflected image or sound.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superregal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*re"gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>More than regal;
worthy of one greater than a king.</def>
<rj><au>Waterland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superreward</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*re*ward"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To reward
to an excessive degree.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superroyal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*roy"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Larger than
royal; -- said of a particular size of printing and writing paper. See
the Note under <er>Paper</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersacral</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sa"cral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated over, or on the dorsal side of, the sacrum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersaliency</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sa"li*en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
leaping on anything.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersalient</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sa"li*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + L. <ets>saliens</ets> p. pr. of <ets>salire</ets> to
leap.]</ety> <def>Leaping upon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersalt</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*salt"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An acid salt. See <cref>Acid salt</cref> <sd>(a)</sd>, under
<er>Salt</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersaturate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sat"u*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To add to
beyond saturation; <as>as, to <ex>supersaturate</ex> a
solution</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersaturation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sat`u*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
operation of supersaturating, or the state of being
supersaturated.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superscribe</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*scribe"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superscribed</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Superscribing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>superscribere</ets>, <ets>superscriptum</ets>; <ets>super</ets>
over + <ets>scribere</ets> to write. See <er>Super-</er>, and
<er>Scribe</er>.]</ety> <def>To write or engrave (a name, address,
inscription, or the like) on the top or surface; to write a name,
address, or the like, on the outside or cover of (anything); <as>as, to
<ex>superscribe</ex> a letter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superscript</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*script</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Superscription.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/I will overglance
the <xex>superscript</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superscription</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*scrip"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superscriptio</ets>. See <er>Superscribe</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of superscribing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is written or engraved on the surface,
outside, or above something else; specifically, an address on a letter,
envelope, or the like.</def> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>superscription</qex> of his accusation was written over,
The King of the Jews.</q> <rj><qau>Mark xv. 26.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Pharm.)</fld> <def>That part of a prescription
which contains the Latin word <xex>recipe</xex> (Take) or the sign
<?/.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersecular</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sec"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being above
the world, or secular things.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersede</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sede"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Superseded</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Superseding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>supersedere</ets>, <ets>supersessum</ets>, to sit above, be
superior to, forbear, omit; <ets>super</ets> above + <ets>sedere</ets>
to sit: cf. F. <ets>supers<eacute/der</ets>. See <er>Sit</er>, and cf.
<er>Surcease</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To come, or be placed, in the
room of; to replace.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To displace, or set aside, and put another in place
of; <as>as, to <ex>supersede</ex> an officer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make void, inefficacious, or useless, by superior
power, or by coming in the place of; to set aside; to render
unnecessary; to suspend; to stay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing is supposed that can <qex>supersede</qex> the known laws
of natural motion.</q> <rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Old Law)</fld> <def>To omit; to forbear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersedeas</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su`per*se"de*as</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
suspend, set aside, stay, 2d pers. sing. present subjunctive of
<ets>supersedere</ets>. See <er>Supersede</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>A writ of command to suspend the powers of an officer in certain
cases, or to stay proceedings under another writ.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersedure</ent><br/
<hw>Su*per*se"dure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
superseding, or setting aside; supersession; <as>as, the
<ex>supersedure</ex> of trial by jury</as>.</def> <rj><au>A.
Hamilton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superseminate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sem"i*nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To sow,
as seed, over something previously sown.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That can not be done with joy, when it shall be indifferent to any
man to <qex>superseminate</qex> what he please.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersemination</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sem`i*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
sowing of seed over seed previously sown.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Abp. Bramhall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersensible</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sen"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + <ets>sensible</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>supersensible</ets>.]</ety> <def>Beyond the reach of the senses;
above the natural powers of perception.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersensitive</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sen"si*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Excessively
sensitive; morbidly sensitive.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Su`per*sen"si*tive*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersensual</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sen"su*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Supersensible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersensuous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sen"su*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Supersensible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Excessively sensuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superserviceable</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*serv"ice*a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Overofficious; doing more than is required or desired.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>superserviceable</xex>, finical rogue.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersession</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ses"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OF.
<ets>supersession</ets>. See <er>Supersede</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
superseding, or the state of being superseded; supersedure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The general law of diminishing return from land would have
undergone, to that extent, a temporary <qex>supersession</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>J. S. Mill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersolar</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*so"lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Above the
sun.</def> <rj><au>Emerson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>supersonic</ent><br/
<hw>su`per*son"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Moving at a speed greater than the speed of sound in air; -- this
speed varies with altitude, since the speed of sound varies with
altitude, as well as with temperature and humidity, being approximately
1129 feet per second at sea level and 984 feet per second at 16 miles
altitude.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of moving at speeds higher than the speed of
sound; -- of aircraft.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersphenoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sphe*noid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated above, or on the dorsal side of, the
body of the sphenoid bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superspinous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*spi"nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Supraspinuos.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstition</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sti"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>superstition</ets>, L. <ets>superstitio</ets>, originally, a
standing still over or by a thing; hence, amazement, wonder, dread,
especially of the divine or supernatural, fr. <ets>superstare</ets> to
stand over; <ets>super</ets> over + <ets>stare</ets> to stand. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
excessive reverence for, or fear of, that which is unknown or
mysterious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An ignorant or irrational worship of the Supreme
Deity; excessive exactness or rigor in religious opinions or practice;
extreme and unnecessary scruples in the observance of religious rites
not commanded, or of points of minor importance; also, a rite or
practice proceeding from excess of sculptures in religion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the truth<br/
With <qex>superstitions</qex> and traditions taint.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The worship of a false god or gods; false religion;
religious veneration for objects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The accusers] had certain questions against him of their own
<qex>superstition</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xxv. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Belief in the direct agency of superior powers in
certain extraordinary or singular events, or in magic, omens,
prognostics, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Excessive nicety; scrupulous exactness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Fanaticism.</syn> --
<usage><er>Superstition</er>, <er>Fanaticism</er>.
<xex>Superstition</xex> springs from religious feeling misdirected or
unenlightened. <xex>Fanaticism</xex> arises from this same feeling in a
state of high-wrought and self-confident excitement. The former leads in
some cases to excessive rigor in religious opinions or practice; in
others, to unfounded belief in extraordinary events or in charms, omens,
and prognostics, hence producing weak fears, or excessive scrupulosity
as to outward observances. The latter gives rise to an utter disregard
of reason under the false assumption of enjoying a guidance directly
inspired. <xex>Fanaticism</xex> has a secondary sense as applied to
politics, etc., which corresponds to the primary.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstitionist</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sti"tion*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One addicted to
superstition.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Blind
<xex>superstitionists</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dr. H. More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstitious</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>superstitieux</ets>, L. <ets>superstitiosus</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to superstition; proceeding from, or
manifesting, superstition; <as>as, <ex>superstitious</ex> rites;
<ex>superstitious</ex> observances</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Evincing superstition; overscrupulous and rigid in
religious observances; addicted to superstition; full of idle fancies
and scruples in regard to religion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye men of Athens, I perceive that in all things ye are too
<qex>superstitious</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Acts xvii. 22.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Overexact; scrupulous beyond need.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Superstitious use</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the use
of a gift or bequest, as of land, etc., for the maintenance of the rites
of a religion not tolerated by the law.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<au>Mozley & W.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Su`per*sti"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Su`per*sti"tious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstrain</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*strain"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
overstrain.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstratum</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*stra"tum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Superstrata</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.: cf. L.
<ets>supersternere</ets>, <ets>superstratum</ets>, to spread upon. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Stratum</er>.]</ety> <def>A stratum, or layer,
above another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstruct</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*struct"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superstructus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>superstruere</ets> to build
upon; <ets>super</ets> over + <ets>struere</ets> to build. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Structure</er>.]</ety> <def>To build over or
upon another structure; to erect upon a foundation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is the only proper basis on which to <qex>superstruct</qex>
first innocency and then virtue.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H.
More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstruction</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*struc"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of superstructing, or building upon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is superstructed, or built upon some
foundation; an edifice; a superstructure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My own profession hath taught me not to erect new
<qex>superstructions</qex> upon an old ruin.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstructive</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*struct"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Built or
erected on something else.</def> <rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstructor</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*struct"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
builds a superstructure.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>R.
North.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superstructure</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*struc"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>superstructure</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Any material
structure or edifice built on something else; that which is raised on a
foundation or basis</def>; esp. <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <def>all that part
of a building above the basement. Also used figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You have added to your natural endowments the
<qex>superstructure</qex> of study.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Railway Engin.)</fld> <def>The sleepers, and
fastenings, in distinction from the roadbed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersubstantial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sub*stan"tial</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + <ets>substantial</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>supersubstantiel</ets>.]</ety> <def>More than substantial;
spiritual.</def> <ldquo/The heavenly <xex>supersubstantial</xex>
bread.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersubtle</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sub"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Too
subtle.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersulphate</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sul"phate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>An acid sulphate.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersulphureted</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sul"phu*ret`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Supersulphurized.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>-sulphuretted</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supersulphurize</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*sul"phur*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To impregnate or combine with an excess of
sulphur.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supertax</ent><br/
<hw>Su"per*tax`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Super-</ets>
+ <ets>tax</ets>.]</ety> <def>A tax in addition to the usual or normal
tax; specif., in the United Kingdom, an income tax of sixpence for every
pound in addition to the normal income tax of one shilling and twopence
for every pound, imposed, by the Finance Act of 1909-1910 (c. 8, ss 66,
72), on the amount by which the income of any person exceeds
<pound/3,000 when his total income exceeds <pound/5,000.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supertemporal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*tem"po*ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That which
is more than temporal; that which is eternal.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superterranean</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ter*ra"ne*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being
above ground.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Superterranean</xex> quarries.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Mrs. Trollope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superterrene</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ter*rene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + <ets>terrene</ets>: cf. L.
<ets>superterrenus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Being above ground, or above the
earth.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Superterrestrial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ter*res"tri*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being
above the earth, or above what belongs to the earth.</def>
<rj><au>Buckminster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supertonic</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mus.)</fld>
<def>The note next above the keynote; the second of the scale.</def>
<rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supertragical</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*trag"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tragical to
excess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supertuberation</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*tu`ber*a"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + <ets>tuber</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
production of young tubers, as potatoes, from the old while still
growing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervacaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*va*ca"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supervacaneus</ets>, <ets>supervacuus</ets>; <ets>super</ets> over
+ <ets>vacuus</ets> empty.]</ety> <def>Serving no purpose; superfluous;
needless.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervene</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supervened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Supervening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>supervenire</ets>, <ets>superventum</ets>, to come over, to come
upon; <ets>super</ets> over + <ets>venire</ets> to come. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Come</er>, and cf. <er>Overcome</er>.]</ety>
<def>To come as something additional or extraneous; to occur with
reference or relation to something else; to happen upon or after
something else; to be added; to take place; to happen.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Such a mutual gravitation can never <qex>supervene</qex> to matter
unless impressed by divine power.</q> <rj><qau>Bentley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A tyrany immediately <qex>supervened</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervenient</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ven"ient</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superveniens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Coming as something
additional or extraneous; coming afterwards.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That branch of belief was in him <qex>supervenient</qex> to
Christian practice.</q> <rj><qau>Hammond.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Divorces can be granted, <qex>a mensa et toro</qex>, only for
<qex>supervenient</qex> causes.</q> <rj><qau>Z. Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervention</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*ven"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>superventio</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of supervening.</def>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervisal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vis"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Supervision.</def> <rj><au>Walpole.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervise</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supervised</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Supervising</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>super-</ets> + L. <ets>visere</ets> to look at attentively, to
view, surely, intens. from <ets>videre</ets>, <ets>visum</ets>, to see.
Cf. <er>Survise</er>, and <er>Survey</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
oversee for direction; to superintend; to inspect with authority;
<as>as, to <ex>supervise</ex> the construction of a steam engine, or the
printing of a book</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To look over so as to read; to peruse.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Superintend</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervise</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vise"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Supervision; inspection.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervision</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vi"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
overseeing; inspection; superintendence; oversight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervisive</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vi"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Supervisory.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervisor</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vis"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who supervises; an overseer; an inspector; a superintendent; <as>as, a
<ex>supervisor</ex> of schools</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A spectator; a looker-on.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervisory</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vi"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to supervision; <as>as, <ex>supervisory</ex>
powers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervive</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vive"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supervivere</ets>. See <er>Survive</er>.]</ety> <def>To survive; to
outlive.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supervolute</ent><br/
<hw>Su`per*vo*lute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supervolutus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>supervolvere</ets> to roll over;
<ets>super</ets> over + <ets>volvere</ets> to roll.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having a plaited and convolute arrangement in the
bud, as in the morning-glory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supination</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pi*na"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supinare</ets>, <ets>supinatum</ets>, to bend or lay backward, fr.
<ets>supinus</ets> supine: cf. F. <ets>supination</ets>. See
<er>Supine</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The act
of turning the hand palm upward; also, position of the hand with the
palm upward.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The act or state of lying with the
face upward. Opposed to <ant>pronation</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supinator</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pi*na"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A muscle which produces the motion of
supination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supine</ent><br/
<hw>Su*pine"</hw> <pr>(s<usl/*p<imac/n")</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supinus</ets>, akin to <ets>sub</ets> under, <ets>super</ets>
above. Cf. <er>Sub-</er>, <er>Super-</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Lying
on the back, or with the face upward; -- opposed to
<ant>prone</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Leaning backward, or inclining with exposure to the
sun; sloping; inclined.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If the vine<br/
On rising ground be placed, or hills <qex>supine</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Negligent; heedless; indolent; listless.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He became pusillanimous and <qex>supine</qex>, and openly exposed
to any temptation.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Negligent; heedless; indolent; thoughtless;
inattentive; listless; careless; drowsy.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Su*pine"ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Su*pine"ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supine</ent><br/
<hw>Su"pine</hw> <pr>(s<umac/"p<imac/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supinum</ets> (sc. <ets>verbum</ets>), from <ets>supinus</ets> bent
or thrown backward, perhaps so called because, although furnished with
substantive case endings, it rests or falls back, as it were, on the
verb: cf. F. <ets>supin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Lat. Gram.)</fld> <def>A
verbal noun; or (according to C.F.Becker), a case of the infinitive mood
ending in <xex>-um</xex> and <xex>-u</xex>, that in <xex>-um</xex> being
sometimes called the <xex>former supine</xex>, and that in <xex>-u</xex>
the <xex>latter supine</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supinity</ent><br/
<hw>Su*pin"i*ty</hw> <pr>(s<usl/*p<icr/n"<icr/*t<ycr/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>supinitas</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Supineness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppage</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"page</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Sup</er>.]</ety> <def>What may be supped; pottage.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hooker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppalpation</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`pal*pa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suppalpari</ets> to caress a little; <ets>sub</ets> under, a little
+ <ets>palpare</ets> to caress.]</ety> <def>The act of enticing by soft
words; enticement.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supparasitation</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*par`a*si*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Supparasite</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of flattering to gain favor;
servile approbation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp.
Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supparasite</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*par"a*site</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supparasitari</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under, a little +
<ets>parasitus</ets> a parasite.]</ety> <def>To flatter; to cajole; to
act the parasite.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. R.
Clerke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppawn</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pawn"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Supawn</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppedaneous</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`pe*da"ne*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sub-</ets> + L. <ets>pes</ets>, <ets>pedis</ets>, a foot: cf. L.
<ets>suppedaneum</ets> a footstool.]</ety> <def>Being under the
feet.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppeditate</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*ped"i*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suppeditatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suppeditare</ets> to
supply.]</ety> <def>To supply; to furnish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hammond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppeditation</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*ped`i*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suppeditatio</ets>.]</ety> <def>Supply; aid afforded.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supper</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>soper</ets>,
<ets>super</ets>, OF. <ets>super</ets>, <ets>soper</ets>, F.
<ets>souper</ets>; originally an infinitive, to sup, take a meal. See
<er>Soup</er>, and cf. <er>Sup</er> to take supper.]</ety> <def>A meal
taken at the close of the day; the evening meal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Supper</xex> is much used in an obvious sense,
either adjectively or as the first part of a compound; as,
<xex>supper</xex> time or <xex>supper</xex>-time, <xex>supper</xex>
bell, <xex>supper</xex> hour, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supper</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"per</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To take supper; to sup.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supper</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"per</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To supply with supper.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/Kester was <xex>suppering</xex> the
horses.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mrs. Gaskell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supperless</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"per*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no supper; deprived of
supper; <as>as, to go <ex>supperless</ex> to bed</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supping</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of one who sups; the act of taking supper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is supped; broth.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplace</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*place"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
replace.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>J. Bascom.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplant</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*plant"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supplanted</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Supplanting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>supplanter</ets>, L. <ets>supplantare</ets> to trip up one's heels,
to throw down; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>planta</ets> the sole of the
foot, also, a sucker, slip, sprout. Cf. <er>Plant</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To trip up.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Supplanted</xex>, down he fell.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To displace and take the place of; to supersede; to
remove or displace by stratagem; <as>as, a rival <ex>supplants</ex>
another in the favor of a mistress or a prince</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Suspecting that the courtier had <qex>supplanted</qex> the
friend.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Fell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To overthrow, undermine, or force away, in order to
get a substitute in place of.</def> <mark>[obsolescent]</mark> <br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You never will <qex>supplant</qex> the received ideas of God.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To remove (a thing) and replace it with something
else.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To remove; displace; overpower; undermine;
overthrow; supersede.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1449 --></p>

<p><ent>Supplantation</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`plan*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/p`pl<acr/n*t<amac/"sh<ucr/n)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>supplantation</ets>, L.
<ets>supplantatio</ets> hypocritical deceit.]</ety> <def>The act of
supplanting or displacing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Habitual <qex>supplantation</qex> of immediate selfishness.</q>
<rj><qau>Cloeridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplanter</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*plan"ter</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/p*pl<acr/nt"<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who supplants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supple</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple</hw> <pr>(s<ucr/p"p'l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>souple</ets>, F. <ets>souple</ets>, from L. <ets>supplex</ets>
suppliant, perhaps originally, being the knees. Cf.
<er>Supplicate</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Pliant; flexible; easily
bent; <as>as, <ex>supple</ex> joints; <ex>supple</ex>
fingers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Yielding; compliant; not obstinate; submissive to
guidance; <as>as, a <ex>supple</ex> horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If punishment . . . makes not the will <qex>supple</qex>, it
hardens the offender.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Bending to the humor of others; flattering; fawning;
obsequious.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Pliant; flexible; yielding; compliant; bending;
flattering; fawning; soft.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supple</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Suppled</conjf> <pr>(s<ucr/p"p'ld)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suppling</conjf> <pr>(s<ucr/p"pl<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To make soft and pliant; to render flexible; <as>as, to
<ex>supple</ex> leather</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The flesh therewith she <qex>suppled</qex> and did steep.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make compliant, submissive, or
obedient.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A mother persisting till she had bent her daughter's mind and
<qex>suppled</qex> her will.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They should <qex>supple</qex> our stiff willfulness.</q>
<rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supple</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become soft and
pliant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The stones . . . <br/
<qex>Suppled</qex> into softness as they fell.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supple-chapped</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple-chapped`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
limber tongue.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/A
<xex>supple-chapped</xex> flatterer.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supple-jack</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple-jack`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A climbing shrub (<spn>Berchemia volubilus</spn>) of
the Southern United States, having a tough and pliable stem.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A somewhat similar tropical American plant
(<spn>Paullinia Curassavica</spn>); also, a walking stick made from its
stem.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He was in form and spirit like a <qex>supple-jack</qex>, . . .
yielding, but tough; though he bent, he never broke.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This name is given to various plants of similar habit in
different British colonies.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplely</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a supple manner; softly;
pliantly; mildly.</def> <rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplement</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>suppl<eacute/ment</ets>, L. <ets>supplementum</ets>, fr.
<ets>supplere</ets> to fill up. See <er>Supply</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which supplies a deficiency, or
meets a want; a store; a supply.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which fills up, completes, or makes an addition
to, something already organized, arranged, or set apart; specifically, a
part added to, or issued as a continuation of, a book or paper, to make
good its deficiencies or correct its errors.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Trig.)</fld> <def>The number of degrees which, if
added to a specified arc, make it 180<deg/; the quantity by which an arc
or an angle falls short of 180 degrees, or an arc falls short of a
semicircle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Appendix.</syn> -- <usage><er>Appendix</er>,
<er>Supplement</er>. An <xex>appendix</xex> is that which is appended to
something, but is not essential to its completeness; a
<xex>supplement</xex> is that which supplements, or serves to complete
or make perfect, that to which it is added.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplement</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple*ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supplemented</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Supplementing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To fill up or supply by
addition; to add something to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Causes of one kind must be <qex>supplemented</qex> by bringing to
bear upon them a causation of another kind.</q> <rj><qau>I.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplementary</ent><br/
<ent>Supplemental</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sup`ple*men"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sup`ple*men"ta*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>suppl<eacute/mentaire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Added to supply what is
wanted; additional; being, or serving as, a supplement; <as>as, a
<ex>supplemental</ex> law; a <ex>supplementary</ex> sheet or
volume</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Supplemental air</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>, <cd>the
air which in addition to the residual air remains in the lungs after
ordinary expiration, but which, unlike the residual air, can be
expelled; reserve air.</cd> -- <col><b>Supplemental bill</b></col>
<fld>(Equity)</fld>, <cd>a bill filed in aid of an original bill to
supply some deffect in the latter, or to set forth new facts which can
not be done by amendment.</cd> <au>Burrill.</au> <au>Daniel.</au> --
<col><b>Supplementary chords</b></col> <fld>(Math.)</fld>, <cd>in an
ellipse or hyperbola, any two chords drawn through the extremities of a
diameter, and intersecting on the curve.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplementation</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`ple*men*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
supplementing.</def> <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppleness</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or
state of being supple; flexibility; pliableness; pliancy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppletory</ent><br/
<ent>Suppletive</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sup"ple*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sup"ple*to*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>suppl<eacute/tif</ets>, LL. <ets>suppletivus</ets>, from L.
<ets>supplere</ets>, <ets>suppletum</ets>, to fill up. See
<er>Supply</er>.]</ety> <def>Supplying deficiencies; supplementary;
<as>as, a <ex>suppletory</ex> oath</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppletory</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"ple*to*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Suppletories</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>That which is to
supply what is wanted.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Invent <qex>suppletories</qex> to excuse an evil man.</q>
<rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplial</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pli"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
supplying; a supply.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>supplial</xex> of a
preposition.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Fitzed. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppliance</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pli"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Supply</er>.]</ety> <def>That which supplies a want; assistance; a
gratification; satisfaction.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The perfume and <qex>suppliance</qex> of a minute.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppliance</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pli"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Suppliant</er>.]</ety> <def>Supplication; entreaty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When Greece her knee in <qex>suppliance</qex> bent.</q>
<rj><qau>Halleck.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppliant</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., p. pr. of
<ets>supplier</ets> to entreat, L. <ets>supplicare</ets>. See
<er>Supplicate</er>, and cf. <er>Supplicant</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Asking earnestly and submissively; entreating; beseeching;
supplicating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The rich grow <qex>suppliant</qex>, and the poor grow proud.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Manifesting entreaty; expressive of
supplication.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To bow and sue for grace<br/
With <qex>suppliant</qex> knee.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Entreating; beseeching; suing; begging;
supplicating; imploring.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sup"pli*ant*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sup"pli*ant*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppliant</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*ant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who supplicates; a humble
petitioner; one who entreats submissively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hear thy <qex>suppliant's</qex> prayer.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicancy</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*can*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Supplication.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicant</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*cant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supplicans</ets>, p. pr. See <er>Supplicate</er>, and cf.
<er>Suppliant</er>.]</ety> <def>Entreating; asking submissively.</def>
<au>Shak.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Sup"pli*cant*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicant</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*cant</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who supplicates; a
suppliant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The wise <qex>supplicant</qex> . . . left the event to God.</q>
<rj><qau>Rogers.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicat</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sup"pli*cat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., he
supplicates.]</ety> <fld>(Eng. Universities)</fld> <def>A petition;
esp., a written one, with a certificate that the conditions have been
complied with.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicate</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supplicated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Supplicating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>supplicatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>supplicare</ets> to supplicate; of
uncertain origin, cf. <ets>supplex</ets>, <ets>supplicis</ets>, humbly
begging or entreating; perhaps fr. <ets>sub</ets> under + a word akin to
<ets>placare</ets> to reconcile, appease (cf. <er>Placable</er>), or fr.
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>plicare</ets> to fold, whence the idea of
bending the knees (cf. <er>Ply</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>). Cf.
<er>Supple</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To entreat for; to seek by
earnest prayer; to ask for earnestly and humbly; <as>as, to
<ex>supplicate</ex> blessings on Christian efforts to spread the
gospel</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To address in prayer; to entreat as a supplicant;
<as>as, to <ex>supplicate</ex> the Deity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To beseech; entreat; beg; petition; implore;
importune; solicit; crave. See <er>Beseech</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicate</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*cate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make petition with
earnestness and submission; to implore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man can not brook to <qex>supplicate</qex> or beg.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicatingly</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*ca`ting*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a supplicating
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplication</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`pli*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>supplication</ets>, L. <ets>supplicatio</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of supplicating; humble and earnest prayer, as in
worship.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A humble petition; an earnest request; an
entreaty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Rom. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A religious solemnity
observed in consequence of some military success, and also, in times of
distress and danger, to avert the anger of the gods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Entreaty; petition; solicitation;
craving.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicator</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*ca`tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>One who supplicates; a supplicant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplicatory</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pli*ca*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>supplicatoire</ets>.]</ety> <def>Containing supplication; humble;
earnest.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplier</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pli"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
supplies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supply</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*ply"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Supplied</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Supplying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[For
older <ets>supploy</ets>, F. <ets>suppl<eacute/er</ets>, OF. also
<ets>supployer</ets>, (assumed) LL. <ets>suppletare</ets>, from L.
<ets>supplere</ets>, <ets>suppletum</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>plere</ets> to fill, akin to <ets>plenus</ets> full. See
<er>Plenty</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fill up, or keep full; to
furnish with what is wanted; to afford, or furnish with, a sufficiency;
<as>as, rivers are <ex>supplied</ex> by smaller streams; an aqueduct
<ex>supplies</ex> an artificial lake</as>; -- often followed by
<xex>with</xex> before the thing furnished; <as>as, to <ex>supply</ex> a
furnace with fuel; to <ex>supply</ex> soldiers with
ammunition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To serve instead of; to take the place of.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Burning ships the banished sun <qex>supply</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sun was set, and Vesper, to <qex>supply</qex><br/
His absent beams, had lighted up the sky.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To fill temporarily; to serve as substitute for
another in, as a vacant place or office; to occupy; to have possession
of; <as>as, to <ex>supply</ex> a pulpit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To give; to bring or furnish; to provide; <as>as, to
<ex>supply</ex> money for the war</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To furnish; provide; administer; minister;
contribute; yield; accommodate.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supply</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*ply"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Supplies</plw>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of supplying;
supplial.</def> <rj><au>A. Tucker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which supplies a want; sufficiency of things
for use or want.</def> Specifically: --<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>Auxiliary troops or reenforcements.</def> <ldquo/My
promised <xex>supply</xex> of horsemen.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>The food, and the like, which meets the daily
necessities of an army or other large body of men; store; -- used
chiefly in the plural; <as>as, the army was discontented for lack of
<ex>supplies</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>An amount of money provided, as by Parliament or
Congress, to meet the annual national expenditures; generally in the
plural; <as>as, to vote <ex>supplies</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>A person who fills a place for a time; one who
supplies the place of another; a substitute; esp., a clergyman who
supplies a vacant pulpit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Stated supply</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>a
clergyman employed to supply a pulpit for a definite time, but not
settled as a pastor.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> -- <col><b>Supply and
demand</b></col>. <fld>(Polit. Econ.)</fld> <cd><ldquo/<xex>Demand</xex>
means the quantity of a given article which would be taken at a given
price. <xex>Supply</xex> means the quantity of that article which could
be had at that price.<rdquo/ <au>F. A. Walker.</au></cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supply</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*ply"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Serving to contain, deliver, or
regulate a supply of anything; <as>as, a <ex>supply</ex> tank or
valve</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Supply system</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
system of tubes and canals in sponges by means of which food and water
are absorbed. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of <er>Spongiae</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplyant</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*ply"ant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Supplying or
aiding; auxiliary; suppletory.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supplyment</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*ply"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A supplying or
furnishing; supply.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Support</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supported</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Supporting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>supporter</ets>, L.
<ets>supportare</ets> to carry on, to convey, in LL., to support,
sustain; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>portare</ets> to carry. See
<er>Port</er> demeanor.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To bear by being under;
to keep from falling; to uphold; to sustain, in a literal or physical
sense; to prop up; to bear the weight of; <as>as, a pillar
<ex>supports</ex> a structure; an abutment <ex>supports</ex> an arch;
the trunk of a tree <ex>supports</ex> the branches.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To endure without being overcome, exhausted, or
changed in character; to sustain; <as>as, to <ex>support</ex> pain,
distress, or misfortunes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This fierce demeanor and his insolence<br/
The patience of a god could not <qex>support</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To keep from failing or sinking; to solace under
affictive circumstances; to assist; to encourage; to defend; <as>as, to
<ex>support</ex> the courage or spirits</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To assume and carry successfully, as the part of an
actor; to represent or act; to sustain; <as>as, to <ex>support</ex> the
character of King Lear</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To furnish with the means of sustenance or
livelihood; to maintain; to provide for; <as>as, to <ex>support</ex> a
family; to <ex>support</ex> the ministers of the gospel.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To carry on; to enable to continue; to maintain;
<as>as, to <ex>support</ex> a war or a contest; to <ex>support</ex> an
argument or a debate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To verify; to make good; to substantiate; to
establish; to sustain; <as>as, the testimony is not sufficient to
<ex>support</ex> the charges; the evidence will not <ex>support</ex> the
statements or allegations.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To urge such arguments, as though they were sufficient to
<qex>support</qex> and demonstrate a whole scheme of moral
philosophy.</q> <rj><qau>J. Edwards.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To vindicate; to maintain; to defend successfully;
<as>as, to be able to <ex>support</ex> one's own cause</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To uphold by aid or countenance; to aid; to help; to
back up; <as>as, to <ex>support</ex> a friend or a party; to
<ex>support</ex> the present administration.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wherefore, bold pleasant,<br/
Darest thou <qex>support</qex> a published traitor?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>A attend as an honorary assistant; <as>as, a
chairman <ex>supported</ex> by a vice chairman; O'Connell left the
prison, <ex>supported</ex> by his two sons.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Support arms</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a command
in the manual of arms in responce to which the piece is held vertically
at the shoulder, with the hammer resting on the left forearm, which is
passed horizontally across the body in front; also, the position assumed
in response to this command.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To maintain; endure; verify; substantiate;
countenance; patronize; help; back; second; succor; relieve; uphold;
encourage; favor; nurture; nourish; cherish; shield; defend; protect;
stay; assist; forward.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Support</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act, state, or operation of supporting, upholding,
or sustaining.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which upholds, sustains, or keeps from falling,
as a prop, a pillar, or a foundation of any kind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which maintains or preserves from being
overcome, falling, yielding, sinking, giving way, or the like;
subsistence; maintenance; assistance; reenforcement; <as>as, he gave his
family a good <ex>support</ex>, the <ex>support</ex> of national credit;
the assaulting column had the <ex>support</ex> of a
battery.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Points of support</b></col> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>, <cd>the
horizontal area of the solids of a building, walls, piers, and the like,
as compared with the open or vacant spaces.</cd> -- <col><b>Right of
support</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>an easement or servitude by
which the owner of a house has a right to rest his timber on the walls
of his neighbor's house.</cd> <au>Kent.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Stay; prop; maintenance; subsistence; assistance;
favor; countenance; encouragement; patronage; aid; help; succor;
nutriment; sustenance; food.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supportable</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>supportable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being supported,
maintained, or endured; endurable.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sup*port"a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Sup*port"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supportance</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Support.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supportation</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`por*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Maintenance;
support.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer. Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supporter</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
who, or that which, supports; <as>as, oxygen is a <ex>supporter</ex> of
life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sockets and <qex>supporters</qex> of flowers are figured.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The saints have a . . . <qex>supporter</qex> in all their
miseries.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, an adherent; one who sustains,
advocates, and defends; <as>as, the <ex>supporter</ex> of a party,
faction, or candidate</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>A knee placed under the
cathead.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>A figure, sometimes of a man, but
commonly of some animal, placed on either side of an escutcheon, and
exterior to it. Usually, both supporters of an escutcheon are similar
figures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A broad band or truss for
supporting the abdomen or some other part or organ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- athletic supporter. A supprter (Def. 5), specifically to support
the male testicles while engaged in vigorous athletics. Also called a
jock or a jockstrap. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supportful</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding with
support.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supportless</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having no support.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supportment</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Support.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supportress</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*port"ress</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A female
supporter.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You are my gracious patroness and <qex>supportress</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supposable</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pos"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being
supposed, or imagined to exist; <as>as, that is not a
<ex>supposable</ex> case</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sup*pos"a*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Sup*pos"a*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supposal</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pos"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
supposing; also, that which is supposed; supposition; opinion.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Interest, with a Jew, never proceeds but upon <qex>supposal</qex>,
at least, of a firm and sufficient bottom.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppose</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Supposed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Supposing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>supposer</ets>; pref. <ets>sub-</ets> under + <ets>poser</ets> to
place; -- corresponding in meaning to L. <ets>supponere</ets>,
<ets>suppositum</ets>, to put under, to substitute, falsify,
counterfeit. See <er>Pose</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To represent to
one's self, or state to another, not as true or real, but as if so, and
with a view to some consequence or application which the reality would
involve or admit of; to imagine or admit to exist, for the sake of
argument or illustration; to assume to be true; as, let us
<xex>suppose</xex> the earth to be the center of the system, what would
be the result?</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Suppose</qex> they take offence without a cause.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When we have as great assurance that a thing is, as we could
possibly, <qex>supposing</qex> it were, we ought not to make any doubt
of its existence.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To imagine; to believe; to receive as
true.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How easy is a bush <qex>supposed</qex> a bear!</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let not my lord <qex>suppose</qex> that they have slain all the
young men, the king's sons; for Amnon only is dead.</q> <rj><qau>2 Sam.
xiii. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To require to exist or to be true; to imply by the
laws of thought or of nature; <as>as, purpose <ex>supposes</ex>
foresight</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>One falsehood always <qex>supposes</qex> another, and renders all
you can say suspected.</q> <rj><qau>Female Quixote.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To put by fraud in the place of another.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To imagine; believe; conclude; judge; consider;
view; regard; conjecture; assume.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1450 --></p>

<p><ent>Suppose</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pose"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To make
supposition; to think; to be of opinion.</def> <rj><au>Acts ii.
15.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppose</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pose"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Supposition.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Shak.</au> <ldquo/A base <xex>suppose</xex> that
he is honest.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supposeer</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pose"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
supposes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supposition</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`po*si"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>supposition</ets>, L. <ets>suppositio</ets> a placing under, a
substitution, fr. <ets>supponere</ets>, <ets>suppositium</ets>, to put
under, to substitute. The word has the meaning corresponding to
<ets>suppose</ets>. See <er>Sub-</er>, and <er>Position</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of supposing, laying down, imagining, or
considering as true or existing, what is known not to be true, or what
is not proved.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is supposed; hypothesis; conjecture;
surmise; opinion or belief without sufficient evidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This is only an infallibility upon <qex>supposition</qex> that if
a thing be true, it is imposible to be false.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He means are in <qex>supposition</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppositional</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`po*si"tion*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resting on
supposition; hypothetical; conjectural; supposed.</def>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supposititious</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pos`i*ti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suppositicus</ets>. See <er>Supposition</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Fraudulently substituted for something else; not being what is
purports to be; not genuine; spurious; counterfeit; <as>as, a
<ex>supposititious</ex> child; a <ex>supposititious</ex>
writing</as>.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Suppositional; hypothetical.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Woodward.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sup*pos`i*ti"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sup*pos`i*ti"tious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppositive</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pos"i*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>suppositif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Including or implying supposition, or
hypothesis; supposed.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sup*pos"i*tive*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> <au>Hammond.</au></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppositive</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pos"i*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A word denoting or implying
supposition, as the words <xex>if</xex>, <xex>granting</xex>,
<xex>provided</xex>, etc.</def> <rj><au>Harris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppositor</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pos"i*tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>An apparatus for the introduction of suppositories into the
rectum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppository</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pos"i*to*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Suppositories</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[LL.
<ets>suppositorium</ets>, fr. L. <ets>suppositorius</ets> that is placed
underneath: cf. F. <ets>suppositoire</ets>. See
<er>Supposition</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A pill or bolus for
introduction into the rectum; esp., a cylinder or cone of medicated
cacao butter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supposure</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*po"sure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Supposition;
hypothesis; conjecture.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppress</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*press"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suppressed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suppressing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>suppressus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>supprimere</ets> to suppress;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>premere</ets>, <ets>pressum</ets>, to press.
See <er>Sub-</er>, and <er>Press</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
overpower and crush; to subdue; to put down; to quell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every rebellion, when it is <qex>suppressed</qex>, doth make the
subject weaker, and the prince stronger.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Davies.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To keep in; to restrain from utterance or vent;
<as>as, to <ex>suppress</ex> the voice; to <ex>suppress</ex> a
smile</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To retain without disclosure; to conceal; not to
reveal; to prevent publication of; <as>as, to <ex>suppress</ex>
evidence; to <ex>suppress</ex> a pamphlet; to <ex>suppress</ex> the
truth</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She <qex>suppresses</qex> the name, and this keeps him in a
pleasing suspense.</q> <rj><qau>Broome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To stop; to restrain; to arrest the discharges of;
<as>as, to <ex>suppress</ex> a diarrhea, or a hemorrhage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To repress; restrain; put down; overthrow;
overpower; overwhelm; conceal; stifle; stop; smother.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppressible</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*press"i*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
suppressed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppression</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pres"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suppressio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suppression</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of suppressing, or the state of being suppressed;
repression; <as>as, the <ex>suppression</ex> of a riot, insurrection, or
tumult; the <ex>suppression</ex> of truth, of reports, of evidence, and
the like.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Complete stoppage of a natural
secretion or excretion; <as>as, <ex>suppression</ex> of urine; -- used
in contradiction to <ex>retention</ex>, which signifies that the
secretion or excretion is retained without expulsion</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>Omission; <as>as, the
<ex>suppression</ex> of a word</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Overthrow; destruction; concealment; repression;
detention; retention; obstruction.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppressive</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*press"ive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tending to
suppress; subduing; concealing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppressor</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*press"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.,
hider.]</ety> <def>One who suppresses.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supprise</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*prise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
surprise.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppurant</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pu*rant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A suppurative.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppurate</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pu*rate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suppurated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suppurating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>suppuratus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suppurare</ets> to suppurate, cause
to suppurate; <ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>pus</ets>, <ets>puris</ets>,
matter. See <er>Pus</er>.]</ety> <def>To generate pus; <as>as, a boil or
abscess <ex>suppurates</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppurate</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pu*rate</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to generate pus;
<as>as, to <ex>suppurate</ex> a sore</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Arbuthnot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppuration</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`pu*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suppuratio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suppuration</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act or process of suppurating.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The matter produced by suppuration; pus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppurative</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pu*ra*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>suppuratif</ets>.]</ety> <def>Tending to suppurate; promoting
suppuration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Suppurative fever</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<cd>pyaemia.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppurative</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pu*ra*tive</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
suppurative medicine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supputate</ent><br/
<hw>Sup"pu*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supputatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>supputare</ets>. See
<er>Suppute</er>.]</ety> <def>To suppute.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supputation</ent><br/
<hw>Sup`pu*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supputatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>supputation</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Reckoning; account.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suppute</ent><br/
<hw>Sup*pute"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>supputer</ets>, or L. <ets>supputare</ets>; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>putare</ets> to reckon.]</ety> <def>To reckon; to compute; to
suppose; to impute.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra</ent><br/
<hw>Su"pra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[L.; akin to
<ets>super</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>.]</ety> <def>Over; above; before;
also, beyond; besides; -- much used as a prefix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra-acromial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra-a*cro"mi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated above the acromial process of the
scapula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra-angular</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra-an"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Surangular</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra-auricular</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra-au*ric"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Situated above the ear coverts, or auriculars;
-- said of certain feathers of birds.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>A supra-auricular feather.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra-axillary</ent><br/
<hw>Su"pra-ax"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Growing above the axil; inserted above the axil,
as a peduncle. See <er>Suprafoliaceous</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprabranchial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*bran"chi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Situated above the branchiae; -- applied
especially to the upper division of the gill cavity of bivalve
mollusks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprachoroidal</ent><br/
<ent>Suprachoroid</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra*cho"roid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Su`pra*cho*roid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated above the choroid; -- applied to the
layer of the choroid coat of the eyeball next to the
sclerotic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraciliary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*cil"i*a*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Superciliary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraclavicle</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*clav"i*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A bone which usually connects the clavicle with
the post-temporal in the pectorial arch of fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraclavicular</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*cla*vic"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Situated above the clavicle.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or pertaining to the supraclavicle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supracondyloid</ent><br/
<ent>Supracondylar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra*con"dy*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Su`pra*con"dy*loid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated above a condyle or condyles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supracostal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*cos"tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated above, or on the outside of, the ribs.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supracranial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*cra"ni*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated above, or in the roof of, the cranium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supracretaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*cre*ta"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Lying above the chalk;
Supercretaceous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supradecompound</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*de*com"pound</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>More than decompound; divided many
times.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra-esophageal</ent><br/
<ent>Supra-esophagal</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra-e*soph"a*gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Su`pra-e`so*phag"e*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot. & Zool.)</fld> <def>Situated above, or on the dorsal side of,
the esophagus; <as>as, the <ex>supra-esophageal</ex> ganglion of
Crustacea</as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>supra-oesophagal</asp>,
and <asp>supra-oesophageal</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra-ethmoid</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra-eth"moid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Above, or on the dorsal side of, the ethmoid bone or
cartilage.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprafoliaceous</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*fo`li*a"ceous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Inserted into the stem above the leaf, petiole,
or axil, as a peduncle or flower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraglotic</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*glot"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated above the glottis; -- applied to that part of the cavity
of the larynx above the true vocal cords.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprahepatic</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*he*pat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated over, or on the dorsal side of, the liver; -- applied to
the branches of the hepatic veins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprahyoid</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*hy"oid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Hyomental.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra-ilium</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra-il"i*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The cartilaginous cap at the sacral end of the ilium of some
animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supralapsarian</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*lap*sa"ri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Supra-</ets> + <ets>lapse</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>supralapsaire</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>One of
that class of Calvinists who believed that God's decree of election
determined that man should fall, in order that the opportunity might be
furnished of securing the redemption of a part of the race, the decree
of salvation being conceived of as formed <xex>before</xex> or
<xex>beyond</xex>, and not <xex>after</xex> or <xex>following</xex>, the
lapse, or fall. Cf. <er>Infralapsarian</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supralapsarian</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*lap*sa"ri*an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to the
Supralapsarians, or their doctrine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supralapsarianism</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*lap*sa"ri*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
doctrine, belief, or principles of the Supralapsarians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supralapsary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*lap"sa*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Supralapsarian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supralapsary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*lap"sa*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
Supralapsarian.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraloral</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*lo"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Situated above the lores; <as>as, the <ex>supraloral</ex> feathers
of a bird</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A supraloral
feather.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supralunary</ent><br/
<ent>Supralunar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra*lu"nar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Su`pra*lu"na*ry</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Beyond the moon; hence, very
lofty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supramaxilla</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*max"il*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Supramaxillae</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The upper jaw or maxilla.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supramaxillary</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*max"il*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Situated over the lower jaw;
<as>as, the <ex>supramaxillary</ex> nerve</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Of or pertaining to the upper jaw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supramundane</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*mun"dane</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being or
situated above the world or above our system; celestial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supranaturalism</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*nat"u*ral*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state
of being supernatural; belief in supernatural agency or revelation;
supernaturalism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supranaturalist</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*nat"u*ral*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
supernaturalist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supranaturalistic</ent><br/
<ent>Supranaturalist</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra*nat"u*ral*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Su`pra*nat`u*ral*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Of or pertaining to supernaturalism; supernaturalistic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraoccipital</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*oc*cip"i*tal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated over, or in the upper part of, the
occiput; of or pertaining to the supraoccipital bone.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The supraoccipital bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Supraoccipital bone</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a
bone on the dorsal side of the great foramen of the skull, usually
forming a part of the occipital in the adult, but distinct in the
young.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraocular</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*oc"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Above the eyes; -- said of certain scales of fishes and
reptiles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supra-oesophagal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra-oe*soph"a*gal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>See <er>Supra-esophagal</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraorbitar</ent><br/
<ent>Supraorbital</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra*or"bit*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Su`pra*or"bit*ar</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated
above the orbit of the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Supraorbital point</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>the
middle point of the supraorbital line, which is a line drawn across the
narrowest part of the forehead, separating the face from the cranium;
the ophryon.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprapedal</ent><br/
<hw>Su*prap"e*dal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Situated above the foot of a mollusk; <as>as, the
<ex>suprapedal</ex> gland</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraprotest</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*pro"test</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Mercantile
Law)</fld> <def>An acceptance of a bill by a third person after protest
for nonacceptance by the drawee.</def> <rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprapubic</ent><br/
<ent>Suprapubian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra*pu"bi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Su`pra*pu"bic</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated
above, or anterior to, the pubic bone.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprarenal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*re"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated above, or anterior to, the kidneys.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A suprarenal capsule.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Suprarenal capsules</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>two
small bodies of unknown function in front of, or near, the kidneys in
most vertebrates. Also called <altname>renal capsules</altname>, and
<altname>suprarenal bodies</altname>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprascalpulary</ent><br/
<ent>Suprascalpular</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra*scalp"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<hw>Su`pra*scalp"u*la*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated above, or on the anterior side of, the
scapula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprasphenoidal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*sphe*noid"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated above the sphenoidal bone; <as>as, the
<ex>suprasphenoidal</ex> appendage, or pituitary body</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraspinal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*spi"nal</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Situated above the vertebral column.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Situated above a spine or spines; supraspinate;
supraspinous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supraspinous</ent><br/
<ent>Supraspinate</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Su`pra*spi"nate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Su`pra*spi"nous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated
above a spine or spines; especially, situated above, or on the dorsal
side of, the neural spines of the vertebral column, or above, or in
front of, the spine of the scapula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprastapedial</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*sta*pe"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, that part of
the columella of the ear which projects above the connection with the
stapes, as in many animals.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The
suprastapedial part of the columella.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suprasternal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*ster"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated above, or anterior to, the sternum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supratemporal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*tem"po*ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated above the temporal bone or temporal
fossa.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A supratemporal
bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supratrochlear</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*troch"le*ar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Situated over or above a trochlea or trochlear
surface; -- applied esp. to one of the subdivisions of the trigeminal
nerve.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supravaginal</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*vag"i*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Situated above or outside a sheath or vaginal membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supravision</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*vi"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Supervision.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supravisor</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*vis"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
supervisor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supravulgar</ent><br/
<hw>Su`pra*vul"gar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being above the
vulgar or common people.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Collier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supremacy</ent><br/
<hw>Su*prem"a*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>supr<eacute/matie</ets>. See <er>Supreme</er>.]</ety> <def>The
state of being supreme, or in the highest station of power; highest or
supreme authority or power; <as>as, the <ex>supremacy</ex> of a king or
a parliament</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The usurped power of the pope being destroyed, the crown was
restored to its <qex>supremacy</qex> over spiritual men and causes.</q>
<rj><qau>Blackstone.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Oath supremacy</b></col>, <cd>an oath which acknowledges
the supremacy of the sovereign in spiritual affairs, and renounced or
abjures the supremacy of the pope in ecclesiastical or temporal
affairs.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <au>Brande & C.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supreme</ent><br/
<hw>Su*preme"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>supremus</ets>, superlative of <ets>superus</ets> that is above,
upper, fr. <ets>super</ets> above: cf. F. <ets>supr<ecir/me</ets>. See
<er>Super-</er>, and cf. <er>Sum</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Highest
in authority; holding the highest place in authority, government, or
power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that is the <qex>supreme</qex> King of kings.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Highest; greatest; most excellent or most extreme;
utmost; greatist possible (sometimes in a bad sense); <as>as,
<ex>supreme</ex> love; <ex>supreme</ex> glory; <ex>supreme</ex>
magnanimity; <ex>supreme</ex> folly</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Each would be <qex>supreme</qex> within its own sphere, and those
spheres could not but clash.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Situated at the highest part or
point.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>The Supreme</b></col>, <cd>the Almighty;
God.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supremely</ent><br/
<hw>Su*preme"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a supreme
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Supremity</ent><br/
<hw>Su*prem"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>supremitas</ets>.]</ety> <def>Supremacy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sur-</ent><br/
<hw>Sur-</hw>. <ety>[F. <ets>sur</ets> over, above, contr. fr. L.
<ets>super</ets>, <ets>supra</ets>. See <er>Super-</er>.]</ety> <def>A
prefix signifying <xex>over</xex>, <xex>above</xex>, <xex>beyond</xex>,
<xex>upon</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sura</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"ra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar., a step, a
degree.]</ety> <def>One of the sections or chapters of the Koran, which
are one hundred and fourteen in number.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suradanni</ent><br/
<hw>Su`ra*dan"ni</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A valuable kind
of wood obtained on the shores of the Demerara River in South America,
much used for timbers, rails, naves and fellies of wheels, and the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suraddition</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`ad*di"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Something added or appended, as to a name.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surah</ent><br/
<hw>Su"rah</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A soft twilled silk
fabric much used for women's dresses; -- called also <altname>surah
silk</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sural</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sura</ets> the
calf of the leg: cf. F. <ets>sural</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the calf of the leg; <as>as, the <ex>sural</ex>
arteries</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surance</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Assurance.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surangular</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*an"gu*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sur-</ets> + <ets>angular</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Above the angular bone; supra-angular; -- applied to a bone of the
lower jaw in many reptiles and birds.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<def>The surangular bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surbase</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"base`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sur-</ets> + <ets>base</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>A cornice, or series of moldings, on the top of the base of a
pedestal, podium, etc. See <xex>Illust.</xex> of
<er>Column</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A board or group of moldings running round a room on
a level with the tops of the chair backs.</def>
<rj><au>Knight.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surbased</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"based`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having a surbase, or molding above the base.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <ety>[F. <ets>surbaiss<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having
the vertical height from springing line to crown less than the half
span; -- said of an arch; <as>as, a segmental arch is
<ex>surbased</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surbate</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*bate"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Surbated</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Surbating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>solbatu</ets>, p. p.,
bruised (said of a horse's foot); <ets>sole</ets> a sole (of a horse's
foot) + <ets>battu</ets>, p. p. of <ets>battre</ets> to beat.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To make sore or bruise, as the feet by travel.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lest they their fins should bruise, and <qex>surbate</qex>
sore<br/
Their tender feet upon the stony ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Chalky land <qex>surbates</qex> and spoils oxen's feet.</q>
<rj><qau>Mortimer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To harass; to fatigue.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Clarendon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1451 --></p>

<p><ent>Surbeat</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*beat"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Surbate</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surbed</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*bed"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Surbedded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Surbedding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sur-</ets> +
<ets>bed</ets>.]</ety> <def>To set edgewise, as a stone; that is, to set
it in a position different from that which it had in the
quarry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It . . . has something of a grain parallel with the horizon, and
therefore should not be <qex>surbedded</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Gilbert
White.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surbet</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*bet"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Surbate</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surbet</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*bet"</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Surbated; bruised.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcease</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*cease"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sursis</ets>, from <ets>sursis</ets>, p. p. of <ets>surseoir</ets>
to suspend, postpone, defer, in OF., to delay, refrain from, forbear, L.
<ets>supersedere</ets>. <ets>Surcease</ets> is not connected with E.
<ets>cease</ets>. See <er>Supersede</er>.]</ety> <def>Cessation; stop;
end.</def> <ldquo/Not desire, but its <xex>surcease</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Longfellow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is time that there were an end and <qex>surcease</qex> made of
this immodest and deformed manner of writing.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcease</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*cease"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To cause to cease; to
end.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/The waves . . . their range
<xex>surceast</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The nations, overawed, <qex>surceased</qex> the fight.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcease</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*cease"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To cease.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surceaseance</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*cease"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Cessation.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcharge</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*charge"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Surcharged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surcharging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>surcharger</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Charge</er>, and cf.
<er>Overcharge</er>, <er>Supercharge</er>, <er>Supercargo</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To overload; to overburden; to overmatch; to
overcharge; <as>as, to <ex>surcharge</ex> a beast or a ship; to
<ex>surcharge</ex> a cannon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Four charged two, and two <qex>surcharged</qex> one.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your head reclined, as hiding grief from view,<br/
Droops like a rose <qex>surcharged</qex> with morning dew.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>To overstock;
especially, to put more cattle into, as a common, than the person has a
right to do, or more than the herbage will sustain.
<xex>Blackstone</xex>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Equity)</fld> <def>To
show an omission in (an account) for which credit ought to have been
given.</def> <rj><au>Story. Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To print or write a surcharge on (a postage
stamp).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcharge</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*charge"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
overcharge; an excessive load or burden; a load greater than can well be
borne.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A numerous nobility causeth poverty and inconvenience in a state,
for it is <qex>surcharge</qex> of expense.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The putting, by a
commoner, of more beasts on the common than he has a right to.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Equity)</fld> <def>The showing an omission, as in an
account, for which credit ought to have been given.</def>
<rj><au>Burrill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Railroads)</fld> <def>A charge over the usual or
legal rates.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Something printed or written on a postage stamp to
give it a new legal effect, as a new valuation, a place, a date, etc.;
also (<mark>Colloq.</mark>), a stamp with a surcharge.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surchargement</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*charge"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
surcharging; also, surcharge, surplus.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcharger</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*char"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
surcharges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcingle</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"cin`gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sursengle</ets>, OF. <ets>sursangle</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and
<er>Cingle</er>, <er>Shingles</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A belt,
band, or girth which passes over a saddle, or over anything laid on a
horse's back, to bind it fast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>The girdle of a cassock, by which
it is fastened round the waist.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcingled</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"cin`gled</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bound with the
surcingle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcle</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>surculus</ets>.]</ety> <def>A little shoot; a twig; a sucker.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcloy</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"cloy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Surcloyed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surcloying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To surfeit.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcoat</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"coat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>surcote</ets>, OF. <ets>surcote</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and
<er>Coat</er>, and cf. <er>Overcoat</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A coat
worn over the other garments; especially, the long and flowing garment
of knights, worn over the armor, and frequently emblazoned with the arms
of the wearer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A long <qex>surcoat</qex> of pers upon he had..</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At night, or in the rain,<br/
He dons a <qex>surcoat</qex> which he doffs at morn.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A name given to the outer garment of either sex at
different epochs of the Middle Ages.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surcrew</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"crew`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From F.
<ets>surcro<icir/t</ets> increase, or <ets>surcr<ucir/</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>surcro<icir/tre</ets> to overgrow.]</ety> <def>Increase; addition;
surplus.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surculate</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"cu*late</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>surculatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>surculare</ets> to purne, from
<ets>surculus</ets> a shoot, sprout. See <er>Surcle</er>.]</ety> <def>To
purne; to trim.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Cockeram.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surculation</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`cu*la"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act of
purning.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surculose</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"cu*lose`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[CF. L.
<ets>sucrulosus</ets> woody. See <er>Surcle</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Producing suckers, or shoots resembling
suckers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surd</ent><br/
<hw>Surd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>surdus</ets>
deaf (whence the meaning, deaf to reason, irrational), perhaps akin to
E. <ets>swart</ets>. Cf. <er>Sordine</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Net
having the sense of hearing; deaf.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/A
<xex>surd</xex> . . . generation.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Unheard.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Kenrick.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Involving surds; not capable of
being expressed in rational numbers; radical; irrational; <as>as, a
<ex>surd</ex> expression or quantity; a <ex>surd</ex>
number</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Phonetics)</fld> <def>Uttered, as an element of
speech, without tone, or proper vocal sound; voiceless; unintonated;
nonvocal; atonic; whispered; aspirated; sharp; hard, as <xex>f</xex>,
<xex>p</xex>, <xex>s</xex>, etc.; -- opposed to <xex>sonant</xex>. See
<xex>Guide to Pronunciation</xex>, <sect/<sect/169, 179, 180.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surd</ent><br/
<hw>Surd</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
quantity which can not be expressed by rational numbers; <as>thus,
<root/2 is a <ex>surd</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Phon.)</fld> <def>A surd element of speech. See
<er>Surd</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, 4.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surdal</ent><br/
<hw>Surd"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Math.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Surd</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surdiny</ent><br/
<hw>Surd"i*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sardine.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surdity</ent><br/
<hw>Surd"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>surditas</ets>.]</ety> <def>Deafness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sure</ent><br/
<hw>Sure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Surer</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Surest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sur</ets>, OF.
<ets>se<uum/r</ets>, F. <ets>s<ucir/r</ets>, L. <ets>securus</ets>;
<ets>se</ets> aside, without + <ets>cura</ets> care. See
<er>Secure</er>, and cf. <er>Assure</er>, <er>Insure</er>,
<er>Sicker</er> sure.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Certainly knowing and
believing; confident beyond doubt; implicity trusting; unquestioning;
positive.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We are <qex>sure</qex> that the judgment of God is according to
truth against them which commit such things.</q> <rj><qau>Rom. ii.
2.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I'm <qex>sure</qex> care 's an enemy of life.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Certain to find or retain; <as>as, to be
<ex>sure</ex> of game; to be <ex>sure</ex> of success; to be
<ex>sure</ex> of life or health</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fit or worthy to be depended on; certain not to fail
or disappoint expectation; unfailing; strong; permanent; enduring.</def>
<ldquo/His <xex>sure</xex> word.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Keble.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Lord will certainly make my lord a <qex>sure</qex> house;
because my lord fighteth the battles of the Lord.</q> <rj><qau>1 Sam.
xxv. 28.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The testimony of the Lord is <qex>sure</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Ps.
xix. 7.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Which put in good <qex>sure</qex> leather sacks.</q>
<rj><qau>Chapman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Betrothed; engaged to marry.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The king was <qex>sure</qex> to Dame Elizabeth Lucy, and her
husband before God.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. More.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I presume . . . that you had been <qex>sure</qex> as fast as faith
could bind you, man and wife.</q> <rj><qau>Brome.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Free from danger; safe; secure.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fear not; the forest is not three leagues off;<br/
If we recover that we are <qex>sure</qex> enough.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <cs><mcol><col><b>To be sure</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Be
sure</b></col></mcol>, <cd>certainly; without doubt; as, Shall you do?
<xex>To be sure</xex> I shall.</cd> -- <col><b>To make sure</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>To make certain; to secure so that there can be no
failure of the purpose or object.</cd> <ldquo/<xex>Make</xex> Cato
<xex>sure</xex>.<rdquo/ <au>Addison.</au> <ldquo/A peace can not fail,
provided we <xex>make sure</xex> of Spain.<rdquo/ <au>Sir W.
Temple.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>To betroth.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She that's <qex>made sure</qex> to him she loves not well.</q>
<rj><qau>Cotgrave.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Certain; unfailing; infallible; safe; firm;
permanent; steady; stable; strong; secure; indisputable; confident;
positive.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sure</ent><br/
<hw>Sure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sure manner;
safely; certainly.</def> <ldquo/Great, <xex>sure</xex>, shall be thy
meed.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>'T is pleasant, <qex>sure</qex>, to see one's name in print.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sure-footed</ent><br/
<hw>Sure"-foot`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Not liable to
stumble or fall; <as>as, a <ex>sure-footed</ex> horse</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surely</ent><br/
<hw>Sure"ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>In a
sure or certain manner; certainly; infallibly; undoubtedly;
assuredly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt <qex>surely</qex>
die.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. ii. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that created something out of nothing, <qex>surely</qex> can
raise great things out of small.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Without danger; firmly; steadly; securely.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that walketh uprightly walketh <qex>surely</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Prov. x. 9.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surement</ent><br/
<hw>Sure"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A making sure;
surety.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every <qex>surement</qex> and every bond.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sureness</ent><br/
<hw>Sure"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being sure;
certainty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For more <qex>sureness</qex> he repeats it.</q>
<rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The law holds with equal <qex>sureness</qex> for all right
action.</q> <rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suresby</ent><br/
<hw>Sures"by</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol. uncertain.
See <er>Rudesby</er>.]</ety> <def>One to be sure of, or to be relied
on.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There is one which is <qex>suresby</qex>, as they say, to serve,
if anything will serve.</q> <rj><qau>Bradford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suretiship</ent><br/
<hw>Sure"ti*ship</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Suretyship.</def>
<rj><au>Prov. xi. 15.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surety</ent><br/
<hw>Sure"ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sureties</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[OE. <ets>seurte</ets>, OF.
<ets>se<uum/rt<eacute/</ets>, F. <ets>s<ucir/ret<eacute/</ets>. See
<er>Sure</er>, <er>Security</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of
being sure; certainty; security.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Know of a <qex>surety</qex>, that thy seed shall be a stranger in
a land that is not theirs.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xv. 13.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For the more <qex>surety</qex> they looked round about.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which makes sure; that which confirms; ground
of confidence or security.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[We] our happy state<br/
Hold, as you yours, while our obedience holds;<br/
On other <qex>surety</qex> none.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Security against loss or damage; security for
payment, or for the performance of some act.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There remains unpaid<br/
A hundred thousand more; in <qex>surety</qex> of the which<br/
One part of Aquitaine is bound to us.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>One who is bound with and for
another who is primarily liable, and who is called the
<xex>principal</xex>; one who engages to answer for another's appearance
in court, or for his payment of a debt, or for performance of some act;
a bondsman; a bail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that is <qex>surety</qex> for a stranger shall smart for
it.</q> <rj><qau>Prov. xi. 15.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Hence, a substitute; a hostage.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Evidence; confirmation; warrant.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She called the saints to <qex>surety</qex>,<br/
That she would never put it from her finger,<br/
Unless she gave it to yourself.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surety</ent><br/
<hw>Sure"ty</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To act as surety for.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suretyship</ent><br/
<hw>Sure"ty*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being surety; the
obligation of a person to answer for the debt, default, or miscarriage
of another.</def> <rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surf</ent><br/
<hw>Surf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formerly spelled
<ets>suffe</ets>, and probably the same word as E.
<ets>sough</ets>.]</ety> <def>The swell of the sea which breaks upon the
shore, esp. upon a sloping beach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Surf bird</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a ploverlike
bird of the genus <gen>Aphriza</gen>, allied to the turnstone.</cd> --
<col><b>Surf clam</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a large clam living
on the open coast, especially <spn>Mactra solidissima</spn> (syn.
<spn>Spisula solidissima</spn>). See <er>Mactra</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Surf duck</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several
species of sea ducks of the genus <gen>Oidemia</gen>, especially
<spn>Oidemia percpicillata</spn>; -- called also <altname>surf
scoter</altname>. See the Note under <er>Scoter</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Surf fish</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous
species of California embiotocoid fishes. See <er>Embiotocoid</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Surf smelt</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Smelt</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Surf whiting</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Whiting</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surf</ent><br/
<hw>Surf</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The bottom of a drain.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surface</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"face`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See
<er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Face</er>, and cf. <er>Superficial</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The exterior part of anything that has length and
breadth; one of the limits that bound a solid, esp. the upper face;
superficies; the outside; <as>as, the <ex>surface</ex> of the earth; the
<ex>surface</ex> of a diamond; the <ex>surface</ex> of the
body</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The bright <qex>surface</qex> of this ethereous mold.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, outward or external appearance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Vain and weak understandings, which penetrate no deeper than the
<qex>surface</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>V. Knox.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <def>A magnitude that has length and
breadth without thickness; superficies; <as>as, a plane
<ex>surface</ex>; a spherical <ex>surface</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>That part of the side which is
terminated by the flank prolonged, and the angle of the nearest
bastion.</def> <rj><au>Stocqueler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Caustic surface</b></col>, <col><b>Heating
surface</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Caustic</er>,
<er>Heating</er>, etc.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Surface
condensation</b></col>, <col><b>Surface condenser</b></col></mcol>.
<cd>See under <er>Condensation</er>, and <er>Condenser</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Surface gauge</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>an instrument
consisting of a standard having a flat base and carrying an adjustable
pointer, for gauging the evenness of a surface or its height, or for
marking a line parallel with a surface.</cd> -- <col><b>Surface
grub</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the larva of the great yellow
underwing moth (<spn>Triphoena pronuba</spn>). It is often destructive
to the roots of grasses and other plants.</cd> -- <col><b>Surface
plate</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a plate having an accurately
dressed flat surface, used as a standard of flatness by which to test
other surfaces.</cd> -- <col><b>Surface printing</b></col>, <cd>printing
from a surface in relief, as from type, in distinction from <xex>plate
printing</xex>, in which the ink is contained in engraved
lines.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surface</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"face</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Surfaced</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surfacing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To give a surface to; especially, to cause to have a smooth or
plain surface; to make smooth or plain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To work over the surface or soil of, as ground, in
hunting for gold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surface</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"face</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
rise from the depths of a liquid to the surface; <as>as, the submarine
<ex>surfaced</ex> to recharge its batteries</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To become known or public; -- said of
information.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To show up, as a person who was in hiding; <as>as,
he absconded with the payroll and <ex>surfaced</ex> in
Argentina</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surface loading</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"face load`ing</hw>. <fld>(Aeronautics)</fld> <def>The weight
supported per square unit of surface; the quotient obtained by dividing
the gross weight, in pounds, of a fully loaded flying machine, by the
total area, in square feet, of its supporting surface.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfacer</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"fa*cer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A form of machine
for dressing the surface of wood, metal, stone, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surface tension</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"face ten"sion</hw>. <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>That property, due
to molecular forces, which exists in the <xex>surface film</xex> of all
liquids and tends to bring the contained volume into a form having the
least superficial area. The thickness of this film, amounting to less
than a thousandth of a millimeter, is considered to equal the radius of
the sphere of molecular action, that is, the greatest distance at which
there is cohesion between two particles. Particles lying below this
film, being equally acted on from all sides, are in equilibrium as to
forces of cohesion, but those in the film are on the whole attracted
inward, and tension results.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfboat</ent><br/
<hw>Surf"boat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>
<def>A boat intended for use in heavy surf. It is built with a
pronounced sheer, and with a view to resist the shock of waves and of
contact with the beach.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfeit</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"feit</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>surfet</ets>, OF. <ets>surfait</ets>, <ets>sorfait</ets>, excess,
arrogance, crime, fr. <ets>surfaire</ets>, <ets>sorfaire</ets>, to
augment, exaggerate, F. <ets>surfaire</ets> to overcharge;
<ets>sur</ets> over + <ets>faire</ets> to make, do, L.
<ets>facere</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Fact</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Excess in eating and drinking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let not Sir <qex>Surfeit</qex> sit at thy board.</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now comes the sick hour that his <qex>surfeit</qex> made.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fullness and oppression of the system, occasioned
often by excessive eating and drinking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To prevent <qex>surfeit</qex> and other diseases that are incident
to those that heat their blood by travels.</q>
<rj><qau>Bunyan.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Disgust caused by excess; satiety.</def> <rj><au>Sir
P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Matter and argument have been supplied abundantly, and even to
<qex>surfeit</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfeit</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"feit</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To load the stomach
with food, so that sickness or uneasiness ensues; to eat to
excess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They are as sick that <qex>surfeit</qex> with too much as they
that starve with nothing.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To indulge to satiety in any
gratification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfeit</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"feit</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Surfeited</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Surfeiting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To feed so as to
oppress the stomach and derange the function of the system; to overfeed,
and produce satiety, sickness, or uneasiness; -- often reflexive;
<as>as, to <ex>surfeit</ex> one's self with sweets</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fill to satiety and disgust; to cloy; <as>as, he
<ex>surfeits</ex> us with compliments</as>.</def> <rj><au>V.
Knox.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfeiter</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"feit*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
surfeits.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfeit-water</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"feit-wa`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Water for the
cure of surfeits.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfle</ent><br/
<ent>Surfel</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sur"fel</hw>, <hw>Sur"fle</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v.
t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Sulphur</er>.]</ety> <def>To wash, as the face,
with a cosmetic water, said by some to be prepared from the
sulphur.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She shall no oftener powder her hair, [or] <qex>surfel</qex> her
cheeks, . . . but she shall as often gaze on my picture.</q>
<rj><qau>Ford.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfer</ent><br/
<hw>Surf"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
surf duck.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfman</ent><br/
<hw>Surf"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Surmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who serves in a
surfboat in the life-saving service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfoot</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Tired or sore of
foot from travel; lamed.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Nares.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surfy</ent><br/
<hw>Surf"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of,
abounding in, or resembling, surf; <as>as, a <ex>surfy</ex>
shore</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Scarce had they cleared the <qex>surfy</qex> waves<br/
That foam around those frightful caves.</q>
<rj><qau>Moore.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surge</ent><br/
<hw>Surge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>surgere</ets>,
<ets>surrectum</ets>, to raise, to rise; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>regere</ets> to direct: cf. OF. <ets>surgeon</ets>,
<ets>sourgeon</ets>, fountain. See <er>Regent</er>, and cf.
<er>Insurrection</er>, <er>Sortie</er>, <er>Source</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A spring; a fountain.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<ldquo/Divers <xex>surges</xex> and springs of water.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Ld.
Berners.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A large wave or billow; a great, rolling swell of
water, produced generally by a high wind.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He that doubteth is like the <qex>surge</qex> of the sea driven by
the wind and tossed.</q> <rj><qau>James i. 6 (Rev. Ver.)</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He flies aloft, and, with impetuous roar,<br/
Pursues the foaming <qex>surges</qex> to the shore.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The motion of, or produced by, a great
wave.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The tapered part of a windlass barrel or a capstan,
upon which the cable surges, or slips.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surge</ent><br/
<hw>Surge</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To swell; to rise hifg
and roll.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>surging</qex> waters like a mountain rise.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To slip along a
windlass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surge</ent><br/
<hw>Surge</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Surged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Surging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>surgir</ets> to cast anchor, to land. Cf. <er>Surge</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To let go or slacken
suddenly, as a rope; <as>as, to <ex>surge</ex> a hawser or
messenger</as>; also, to slacken the rope about (a capstan).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgeful</ent><br/
<hw>Surge"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Abounding in surges;
surgy.</def> <ldquo/Tossing the <xex>surgeful</xex> tides.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgeless</ent><br/
<hw>Surge"less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Free from surges; smooth;
calm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgent</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"gent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>surgens</ets>, p. pr.]</ety> <def>Rising; swelling, as a
flood.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Robert Greene.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgeon</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"geon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>surgien</ets>, OF. <ets>surgien</ets>, contr. fr.
<ets>chirurgien</ets>. See <er>Chirurgeon</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One whose profession or occupation is to cure diseases or injuries
of the body by manual operation; one whose occupation is to cure local
injuries or disorders (such as wounds, dislocations, tumors, etc.),
whether by manual operation, or by medication and constitutional
treatment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
chaetodont fishes of the family <fam>Teuthidae</fam>, or
<fam>Acanthuridae</fam>, which have one or two sharp lancelike spines on
each side of the base of the tail. Called also <altname>surgeon
fish</altname>, <altname>doctor fish</altname>, <altname>lancet
fish</altname>, and <altname>sea surgeon</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Surgeon apothecary</b></col>, <cd>one who unites the
practice of surgery with that of the apothecary.</cd>
<au>Dunglison.</au> -- <col><b>Surgeon dentist</b></col>, <cd>a dental
surgeon; a dentist.</cd> -- <col><b>Surgeon fish</b></col>. <cd>See def.
2, above.</cd> -- <col><b>Surgeon general</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>In
the United States army, the chief of the medical department.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>In the British army, a surgeon ranking next below the
chief of the medical department.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1452 --></p>

<p><ent>Surgeoncy</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"geon*cy</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"j<ucr/n*s<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>The office or employment of a surgeon, as in the naval or military
service.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgeonry</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"geon*ry</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"j<ucr/n*r<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Surgery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgery</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ge*ry</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"j<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OE. <ets>surgenrie</ets>, <ets>surgerie</ets>; cf. OF.
<ets>cirurgie</ets>, F. <ets>chirurgie</ets>, L. <ets>chirurgia</ets>,
Gr. <?/. See <er>Surgeon</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The art of
healing by manual operation; that branch of medical science which treats
of manual operations for the healing of diseases or injuries of the
body; that branch of medical science which has for its object the cure
of local injuries or diseases, as wounds or fractures, tumors, etc.,
whether by manual operation or by medicines and constitutional
treatment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A surgeon's operating room or laboratory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgical</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"gi*cal</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"j<icr/*k<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Of or pertaining to surgeons or surgery; done by means of surgery;
used in surgery; <as>as, a <ex>surgical</ex> operation;
<ex>surgical</ex> instruments</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Surgical fever</b></col>. <fld>(Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd>
<cd>Pyaemia.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Traumatic fever, or the fever
accompanying inflammation.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgically</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"gi*cal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By means of
surgery.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surgy</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"gy</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"j<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Rising in
surges or billows; full of surges; resembling surges in motion or
appearance; swelling.</def> <ldquo/Over the <xex>surgy</xex>
main.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Pope.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>suricate</ent><br/
<ent>suricat</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>su"ri*cat</hw>, <hw>su"ri*cate</hw></mhw>
<pr>(s<umac/"r<icr/*k<acr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>surikate</ets>, from the native name in South Africa.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A South African mongooselike viverrine
(<spn>Suricata suricatta</spn> or <spn>Suricata tetradactyla</spn>)
having a lemurlike face and only four toes; called also
<altname>mierkat</altname>, <altname>yellow mongoose</altname> and
<altname>zenick</altname>.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>surikate</asp>.]</altsp> <note>The animal called <er>zenick</er>
seems also to be related to or identical to the mierkat, but no
reference can be found on the internet.</note><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> meerkat.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source> + <source>WordNet 1.5</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surinam toad</ent><br/
<hw>Su`ri*nam" toad"</hw> <pr>(s<oomac/`r<icr/*n<aum/m" t<omac/d`)</pr>,
<pos>prop. n.</pos>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A species of toad native of
Surinam. See <er>Pipa</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surintendant</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`in*tend"ant</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r`<icr/n*t<ecr/nd"<ait/nt)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. See <er>Superintendent</er>.]</ety>
<def>Superintendent.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surlily</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"li*ly</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"l<icr/*l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<def>In a surly manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surliness</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"li*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
surly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surling</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ling</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"l<icr/ng)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Surly</er>.]</ety> <def>A sour, morose fellow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Camden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surloin</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"loin`</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"loin`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>surlonge</ets>; <ets>sur</ets> upon + <ets>longe</ets> loin. See
<er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Loin</er>.]</ety> <def>A loin of beef, or the
upper part of the loin. See <er>Sirloin</er>, the more usual, but not
etymologically preferable, orthography.</def> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>sirloin</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surly</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ly</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"l<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Surlier</adjf>
<pr>(s<ucir/r"l<icr/*<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Surliest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[Probably from <ets>sir</ets>, and
originally meaning, <ets>sirlike</ets>, i.e., proud. See <er>Sir</er>,
and <er>Like</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Arrogant;
haughty.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cotgrave.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Gloomily morose; ill-natured, abrupt, and rude;
severe; sour; crabbed; rough; sullen; gloomy; <as>as, a <ex>surly</ex>
groom; a <ex>surly</ex> dog; <ex>surly</ex> language; a <ex>surly</ex>
look</as>.</def> <ldquo/That <xex>surly</xex> spirit, melancholy.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Rough; dark; tempestuous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now softened into joy the <qex>surly</qex> storm.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmark</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"mark`</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r"m<aum/rk`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Shipbuilding)</fld> <def>A mark made on the molds of a ship, when
building, to show where the angles of the timbers are to be
placed.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>sirmark</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmisable</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mis"a*ble</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r*m<imac/z"<adot/*b'l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of being surmised; <as>as, a
<ex>surmisable</ex> result</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmisal</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mis"al</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r*m<imac/z"<ait/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Surmise.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmise</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mise"</hw> <pr>(s<ucir/r*m<imac/z")</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[OF. <ets>surmise</ets> accusation, fr. <ets>surmettre</ets>, p. p.
<ets>surmis</ets>, to impose, accuse; <ets>sur</ets> (see <er>Sur-</er>)
+ <ets>mettre</ets> to put, set, L. <ets>mittere</ets> to send. See
<er>Mission</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A thought, imagination, or
conjecture, which is based upon feeble or scanty evidence; suspicion;
guess; <as>as, the <ex>surmises</ex> of jealousy or of
envy</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[We] double honor gain<br/
From his <qex>surmise</qex> proved false.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No man ought to be charged with principles he actually disowns,
unless his practicies contradict his profession; not upon small
<qex>surmises</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Reflection; thought.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Conjecture; supposition; suspicion;
doubt.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmise</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mise"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Surmised</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Surmising</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To imagine without certain
knowledge; to infer on slight grounds; to suppose, conjecture, or
suspect; to guess.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It wafted nearer yet, and then she knew<br/
That what before she but <qex>surmised</qex>, was true.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This change was not wrought by altering the form or position of
the earth, as was <qex>surmised</qex> by a very learned man, but by
dissolving it.</q> <rj><qau>Woodward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmiser</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mis"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
surmises.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmising</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mis"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Surmise</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmount</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mount"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Surmounted</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Surmounting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sourmounten</ets>,
OF. <ets>surmonter</ets>, <ets>sormonter</ets>, F. <ets>surmonter</ets>;
<ets>sur</ets> over + <ets>monter</ets> to mount. See <er>Sur-</er>, and
<er>Mount</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To rise above;
to be higher than; to overtop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mountains of Olympus, Athos, and Atlas, overreach and
<qex>surmount</qex> all winds and clouds.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Raleigh.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To conquer; to overcome; <as>as, to
<ex>surmount</ex> difficulties or obstacles</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Macaulay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To surpass; to exceed.</def>
<rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What <qex>surmounts</qex> the reach<br/
Of human sense I shall delineate.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To conquer; overcome; vanquish; subdue; surpass;
exceed.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmountable</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mount"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>surmontable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable of being surmounted or
overcome; superable.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sur*mount"a*ble*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmounted</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mount"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Arch.)</fld>
<def>Having its vertical height greater than the half span; -- said of
an arch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Her.)</fld> <def>Partly covered by another charge;
-- said of an ordinary or other bearing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmounter</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mount"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, surmounts.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmullet</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*mul"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>surmulet</ets>; <ets>saur</ets>, <ets>saure</ets>, brownish yellow,
red + <ets>mulet</ets> a mullet. See <er>Sorrel</er>, <pos>a.</pos>, and
<er>Mullet</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of various
species of mullets of the family <fam>Millidae</fam>, esp. the European
species (<spn>Millus surmulletus</spn>), which is highly prized as a
food fish. See <er>Mullet</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surmulot</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"mu*lot</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The brown, or Norway, rat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surname</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"name`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sur +
name</ets>; really a substitution for OE. <ets>sournoun</ets>, from F.
<ets>surnom</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Noun</er>,
<er>Name</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A name or appellation which is
added to, or over and above, the baptismal or Christian name, and
becomes a family name.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Surnames originally designated occupation, estate, place
of residence, or some particular thing or event that related to the
person; thus, Edmund <xex>Ironsides</xex>; Robert <xex>Smith</xex>, or
the <xex>smith</xex>; William <xex>Turner</xex>. Surnames are often also
patronymics; as, John <xex>Johnson</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An appellation added to the original name; an
agnomen.</def> <ldquo/My <xex>surname</xex>, Coriolanus.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ This word has been sometimes written <xex>sirname</xex>,
as if it signified <xex>sire</xex>-name, or the name derived from one's
father.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surname</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*name"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Surnamed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surnaming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>surnommer</ets>.]</ety> <def>To name or call by an appellation
added to the original name; to give a surname to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Another shall subscribe with his hand unto the Lord, and
<qex>surname</qex> himself by the name of Israel.</q> <rj><qau>Isa.
xliv. 5.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And Simon he <qex>surnamed</qex> Peter.</q> <rj><qau>Mark iii.
16.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surnominal</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*nom"i*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining
to a surname or surnames.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suroxidate</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ox"i*date</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>To combine with oxygen so as to form a suroxide or peroxide.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suroxide</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ox"ide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>suroxyde</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Oxide</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A peroxide.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surpass</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*pass"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Surpassed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surpassing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>surpasser</ets>; <ets>sur</ets> over + <ets>passer</ets> to pass.
See <er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Pass</er>.]</ety> <def>To go beyond in
anything good or bad; to perform (an activity) better than; to exceed;
to excel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This would <qex>surpass</qex><br/
Common revenge and interrupt his joy.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To exceed; excel; outdo; outstrip.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surpassable</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*pass"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
surpassed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surpassing</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*pass"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Eminently excellent; exceeding
others.</def> <ldquo/With <xex>surpassing</xex> glory crowned.<rdquo/
<au>Milton.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Sur*pass"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-- <wf>Sur*pass"ing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surphul</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"phul</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To surfel.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Marston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surplice</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"plice</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>surplis</ets>, OF. <ets>surpeiz</ets>, LL.
<ets>superpellicium</ets>; <ets>super</ets> over + <ets>pellicium</ets>,
<ets>pelliceum</ets>, a robe of fur, L. <ets>pellicius</ets> made of
skins. See <er>Pelisse</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>A white
garment worn over another dress by the clergy of the Roman Catholic,
Episcopal, and certain other churches, in some of their
ministrations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Surplice fees</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>fees paid
to the English clergy for occasional duties.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surpliced</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"pliced</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Wearing a
surplice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surplus</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"plus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>sur</ets> over + <ets>plus</ets> more. See <er>Sur-</er>, and
<er>Plus</er>, and cf. <er>Superplus</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
which remains when use or need is satisfied, or when a limit is reached;
excess; overplus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, an amount in the public treasury at
any time greater than is required for the ordinary purposes of the
government.</def><-- = budgetary surplus --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surplus</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"plus</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Being or constituting a surplus;
more than sufficient; <as>as, <ex>surplus</ex> revenues;
<ex>surplus</ex> population; <ex>surplus</ex> words.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When the price of corn falleth, men give over <qex>surplus</qex>
tillage, and break no more ground.</q> <rj><qau>Carew.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surplusage</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"plus*age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Surplus</er>, and cf. <er>Superplusage</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Surplus; excess; overplus; <as>as, <ex>surplusage</ex> of grain or
goods beyond what is wanted</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Take what thou please of all this <qex>surplusage</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>surplusage</qex> given to one part is paid out of a
reduction from another part of the same creature.</q>
<rj><qau>Emerson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>Matter in pleading which is not
necessary or relevant to the case, and which may be rejected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Accounts)</fld> <def>A greater disbursement than
the charge of the accountant amounts to.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Rees.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surprisal</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*pris"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Surprise</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>The act of surprising, or
state of being surprised; surprise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>How to secure the lady from <qex>surprisal</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Because death is uncertain, let us prevent its
<qex>surprisal</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Barrow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surprise</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*prise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>surprise</ets>, fr. <ets>surprendre</ets>, <ets>surpris</ets>;
<ets>sur</ets> over + <ets>prendre</ets> to take, L.
<ets>prehendere</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and
<er>Prehensile</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of coming upon, or
taking, unawares; the act of seizing unexpectedly; surprisal; <as>as,
the fort was taken by <ex>surprise</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The state of being surprised, or taken unawares, by
some act or event which could not reasonably be foreseen; emotion
excited by what is sudden and strange; a suddenly excited feeling of
wonder or astonishment.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Pure <qex>surprise</qex> and fear<br/
Made me to quit the house.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Anything that causes such a state or
emotion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A dish covered with a crust of raised paste, but
with no other contents.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>King.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Surprise party</b></col>, <cd>a party of persons who
assemble by mutual agreement, and without invitation, at the house of a
common friend.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> <au>Bartlett.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Wonder; astonishment; amazement.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surprise</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*prise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Surprised</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surprising</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From
<er>Surprise</er>, <pos>n.</pos>: cf. F. <ets>surprendre</ets>, p. p.
<ets>surpris</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To come or fall suddenly and
unexpectedly; to take unawares; to seize or capture by unexpected
attack.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fearfulness hath <qex>surprised</qex> the hypocrites.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xxxiii. 14.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The castle of Macduff I will <qex>surprise</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who can speak<br/
The mingled passions that <qex>surprised</qex> his heart?</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To strike with wonder, astonishment, or confusion,
by something sudden, unexpected, or remarkable; to confound; <as>as, his
conduct <ex>surprised</ex> me</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am <qex>surprised</qex> with an uncouth fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Up he starts,<br/
Discovered and <qex>surprised</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To lead (one) to do suddenly and without
forethought; to bring (one) into some unexpected state; -- with
<xex>into</xex>; <as>as, to be <ex>surprised</ex> into an indiscretion;
to be <ex>surprised</ex> into generosity</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To hold possession of; to hold.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not with me,<br/
That in my hands <qex>surprise</qex> the sovereignity.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Webster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- See <er>Astonish</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surprisement</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*prise"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Surprisal.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Daniel.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surpriser</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*pris"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
surprises.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surprising</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*pris"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Exciting surprise;
extraordinary; of a nature to excite wonder and astonishment; <as>as,
<ex>surprising</ex> bravery; a <ex>surprising</ex> escape from
danger.</as></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sur*pris"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Sur*pris"ing*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Wonderful; extraordinary; unexpected;
astonishing; striking.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surquedrous</ent><br/
<ent>Surquedous</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sur"que*dous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sur"que*drous</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having or exhibiting surquedry;
arrogant; insolent.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Gower. James II.
of Scot.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surquidry</ent><br/
<ent>Surquedry</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sur"que*dry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sur"qui*dry</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. <ets>surcuidier</ets> to presume; <ets>sur</ets>
over + <ets>cuidier</ets> to think, L. <ets>cogitare</ets>. See
<er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Cogitate</er>.]</ety> <def>Overweening pride;
arrogance; presumption; insolence.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then pay you the price of your <qex>surquedry</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrebound</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`re*bound"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To give back
echoes; to reecho.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrebut</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`re*but"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sur
+ rebut</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To reply, as a plaintiff to
a defendant's rebutter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrebuter</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`re*but"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>The reply of a plaintiff to a defendant's rebutter.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrein</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"rein`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Pref. <ets>sur +
rein</ets>.]</ety> <def>To override; to exhaust by riding.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrejoin</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`re*join"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sur + rejoin</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To reply, as a
plaintiff to a defendant's rejoinder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrejoinder</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`re*join"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>The answer of a plaintiff to a defendant's rejoinder.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrender</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ren"der</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Surrendered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surrendering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>surrendre</ets> to deliver; <ets>sur</ets> over + <ets>rendre</ets>
to render. See <er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Render</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To yield to the power of another; to give or deliver up possession
of (anything) upon compulsion or demand; <as>as, to <ex>surrender</ex>
one's person to an enemy or to an officer; to <ex>surrender</ex> a fort
or a ship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give up possession of; to yield; to resign;
<as>as, to <ex>surrender</ex> a right, privilege, or
advantage</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To <qex>surrender</qex> up that right which otherwise their
founders might have in them.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To yield to any influence, emotion, passion, or
power; -- used reflexively; <as>as, to <ex>surrender</ex> one's self to
grief, to despair, to indolence, or to sleep</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To yield; to render or deliver up;
to give up; <as>as, a principal <ex>surrendered</ex> by his bail, a
fugitive from justice by a foreign state, or a particular estate by the
tenant thereof to him in remainder or reversion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrender</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ren"der</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To give up one's self into
the power of another; to yield; <as>as, the enemy, seeing no way of
escape, <ex>surrendered</ex> at the first summons</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrender</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ren"der</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
surrendering; the act of yielding, or resigning one's person, or the
possession of something, into the power of another; <as>as, the
<ex>surrender</ex> of a castle to an enemy; the <ex>surrender</ex> of a
right</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That he may secure some liberty he makes a <qex>surrender</qex> in
trust of the whole of it.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The yielding of a
particular estate to him who has an immediate estate in remainder or
reversion.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The giving up of a principal into
lawful custody by his bail.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The delivery up of
fugitives from justice by one government to another, as by a foreign
state. See <er>Extradition</er>.</def> <rj><au>Wharton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Insurance)</fld> <def>The voluntary cancellation of
the legal liability of the company by the insured and beneficiary for a
consideration (called the <col><b>surrender value</b></col>).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrenderee</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ren`der*ee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>The person to whom a surrender is made.</def> <rj><au>Mozley &
W.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrenderer</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ren"der*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
surrenders.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrenderor</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ren`der*or"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>One who makes a surrender, as of an estate.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrendry</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*ren"dry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Surrender.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surreption</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*rep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>surreptio</ets>, or <ets>subreptio</ets>. Cf.
<er>Subreption</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act or process of
getting in a surreptitious manner, or by craft or stealth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fame by <qex>surreption</qex> got<br/
May stead us for the time, but lasteth not.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A coming unperceived or suddenly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surreptitious</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`rep*ti"tious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>surreptitius</ets>, or <ets>subreptitius</ets>, fr.
<ets>surripere</ets>, <ets>subripere</ets>, to snatch away, to withdraw
privily; <ets>sub-</ets> under + <ets>rapere</ets> to snatch. See
<er>Sub-</er>, and <er>Ravish</er>.]</ety> <def>Done or made by stealth,
or without proper authority; made or introduced fraudulently;
clandestine; stealthy; <as>as, a <ex>surreptitious</ex> passage in an
old manuscript; a <ex>surreptitious</ex> removal of goods</as>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sur`rep*ti"tious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrey</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"rey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A four-wheeled
pleasure carriage, (commonly two-seated) somewhat like a phaeton, but
having a straight bottom.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrogate</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ro*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>surrogatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>surrogare</ets>,
<ets>subrogare</ets>, to put in another's place, to substitute;
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>rogare</ets> to ask, ask for a vote, propose
a law. See <er>Rogation</er>, and cf. <er>Subrogate</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A deputy; a delegate; a substitute.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The deputy of an ecclesiastical judge, most commonly
of a bishop or his chancellor, especially a deputy who grants marriage
licenses.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>In some States of the United States, an officer who
presides over the probate of wills and testaments and yield the
settlement of estates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>a <er>surrogate mother</er>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1453 --></p>

<p><ent>Surrogate</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ro*gate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To put in the
place of another; to substitute.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrogate mother</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ro*gate mother</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>a person or who serves in place of the biological mother for child,
as for caring or nurturing.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>a female animal that raises and nurses the offspring
of another animal.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>a woman who becomes pregnant by an artificial
procedure (one other than copulation), and bears a child to be raised by
another person. The person on whose behalf the child is gestated and
born is usually one of the biological parents. The embryo carried by the
surrogate mother may be created by <it>in vitro</it> fertilization, and
may have no genetic material from the surrogate mother, or the surrogate
mother may be impregnated by artificial insemination, usually by sperm
donated by the father who is to raise the child.</def> <note>The
procedure is common but controversial, and in some states of the United
States, contracts for surrogacy are not considered
enforceable.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrogateship</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ro*gate*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
surrogate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrogation</ent><br/
<hw>Sur`ro*ga"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Surrogate</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf. <er>Subrogation</er>.]</ety>
<def>The act of substituting one person in the place of another.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Killingbeck.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surround</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*round"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Surrounded</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Surrounding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF. <ets>suronder</ets> to
overflow, LL. <ets>superundare</ets>; fr. L. <ets>super</ets> over +
<ets>undare</ets> to rise in waves, overflow, fr. <ets>unda</ets> wave.
The English sense is due to the influence of E. <ets>round</ets>. See
<er>Super-</er>, and <er>Undulate</er>, and cf. <er>Abound</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To inclose on all sides; to encompass; to
environ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To lie or be on all sides of; to encircle; <as>as, a
wall <ex>surrounds</ex> the city</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But could instead, and ever-during dark<br/
<qex>Surrounds</qex> me.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pass around; to travel about; to circumnavigate;
<as>as, to <ex>surround</ex> the world</as>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>To inclose, as a body of troops,
between hostile forces, so as to cut off means of communication or
retreat; to invest, as a city.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To encompass; encircle; environ; invest; hem in;
fence about.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surround</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*round"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A method of hunting some
animals, as the buffalo, by surrounding a herd, and driving them over a
precipice, into a ravine, etc.</def> <mark>[U.S.]</mark>
<rj><au>Baird.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrounding</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*round"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inclosing;
encircling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surrounding</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*round"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
encompassing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The things which surround or
environ; external or attending circumstances or conditions.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surroyal</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*roy"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sur-</ets> + <ets>royal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One
of the terminal branches or divisions of the beam of the antler of the
stag or other large deer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sursanure</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"sa*nure</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[(Assumed) OF.
<ets>sursane<uum/re</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Sane</er>.]</ety>
<def>A wound healed or healing outwardly only.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Of a <qex>sursanure</qex><br/
In surgery is perilous the cure.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surseance</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"se*ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF., fr. OF. & F.
<ets>surseoir</ets>. See <er>Surcease</er>.]</ety> <def>Peace;
quiet.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sursolid</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*sol"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sursolide</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and <er>Solid</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>The fifth power of a number; <as>as,
<ex>a<?/</ex> is the <ex>sursolid</ex> of <ex>a</ex>, or 32 that of
2</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Hutton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surstyle</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*style"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
surname.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sursum corda</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sur"sum cor"da</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[L. <ets>sursum</ets>
upward + <ets>corda</ets> hearts.]</ety> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>In the
Eucharist, the versicles immediately before the preface, inviting the
people to join in the service by <ldquo/lifting up the heart<rdquo/ to
God.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surtax</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"tax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An additional or extra
tax.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surtax</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*tax"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To impose an
additional tax on.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surtout</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*tout"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>sur</ets> over + <ets>tout</ets> all.]</ety> <def>A man's coat to
be worn over his other garments; an overcoat, especially when long, and
fitting closely like a body coat.</def> <rj><au>Gay.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surturbrand</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"tur*brand</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Icel.
<ets>surtarbrandr</ets>; <ets>svartr</ets> black + <ets>brandr</ets> a
firebrand.]</ety> <def>A fibrous brown coal or bituminous
wood.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surucucu</ent><br/
<hw>Su`ru*cu"cu</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>See <cref>Bush master</cref>, under <er>Bush</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surveillance</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*veil"lance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>surveiller</ets> to watch over; <ets>sur</ets> over +
<ets>veiller</ets> to watch, L. <ets>vigilare</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>,
and <er>Vigil</er>.]</ety> <def>Oversight; watch; inspection;
supervision.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That sort of <qex>surveillance</qex> of which . . . the young have
accused the old.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surveillant</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*veil"lant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Surveillants</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F., fr.
<ets>surveiller</ets> to watch over. See <er>Surveillance</er>.]</ety>
<def>One who watches over another; an overseer; a spy; a
supervisor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surveillant</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*veil"lant</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Overseeing;
watchful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survene</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vene"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Survened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Survening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>survenir</ets>. See <er>Supervene</er>.]</ety> <def>To supervene
upon; to come as an addition to.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A suppuration that <qex>survenes</qex> lethargies.</q>
<rj><qau>Harvey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survenue</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"ve*nue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF. See
<er>Survene</er>.]</ety> <def>A sudden or unexpected coming or stepping
on.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survey</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vey"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Surveyed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surveying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OF.
<ets>surveoir</ets>, <ets>surveer</ets>; <ets>sur</ets>, <ets>sor</ets>,
over, E. <ets>sur + veoir</ets>, <ets>veeir</ets>, to see, F.
<ets>voir</ets>, L. <ets>videre</ets>. See <er>Sur-</er>, and
<er>Vision</er>, and cf. <er>Supervise</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
inspect, or take a view of; to view with attention, as from a high
place; to overlook; <as>as, to stand on a hill, and <ex>survey</ex> the
surrounding country</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Round he <qex>surveys</qex> and well might, where he stood,<br/
So high above.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To view with a scrutinizing eye; to
examine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With such altered looks, . . . <br/
All pale and speechless, he <qex>surveyed</qex> me round.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To examine with reference to condition, situation,
value, etc.; to examine and ascertain the state of; <as>as, to
<ex>survey</ex> a building in order to determine its value and exposure
to loss by fire</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To determine the form, extent, position, etc., of,
as a tract of land, a coast, harbor, or the like, by means of linear and
angular measurements, and the application of the principles of geometry
and trigonometry; <as>as, to <ex>survey</ex> land or a
coast</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To examine and ascertain, as the boundaries and
royalties of a manor, the tenure of the tenants, and the rent and value
of the same.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Jacob (Law
Dict.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survey</ent><br/
<hw>Sur"vey</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Formerly accentuated
universally on the last syllable, and still so accented by many
speakers.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of surveying; a general view,
as from above.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Under his proud <qex>survey</qex> the city lies.</q> <rj><qau>Sir
J. Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A particular view; an examination, especially an
official examination, of all the parts or particulars of a thing, with a
design to ascertain the condition, quantity, or quality; <as>as, a
<ex>survey</ex> of the stores of a ship; a <ex>survey</ex> of roads and
bridges; a <ex>survey</ex> of buildings</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The operation of finding the contour, dimensions,
position, or other particulars of, as any part of the earth's surface,
whether land or water; also, a measured plan and description of any
portion of country, or of a road or line through it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Survey of dogs</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Court of
regard</cref>, under <er>Regard</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Trigonometrical
survey</b></col>, <cd>a survey of a portion of country by measuring a
single base, and connecting it with various points in the tract surveyed
by a series of triangles, the angles of which are carefully measured,
the relative positions and distances of all parts being computed from
these data.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Review; retrospect; examination;
prospect.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surveyal</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vey"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Survey.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Barrow.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surveyance</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vey"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Survey;
inspection.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surveying</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vey"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>That branch of applied
mathematics which teaches the art of determining the area of any portion
of the earth's surface, the length and directions of the bounding lines,
the contour of the surface, etc., with an accurate delineation of the
whole on paper; the act or occupation of making surveys.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Geodetic surveying</b></col>, <cd>geodesy.</cd> --
<mcol><col><b>Maritime surveying</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Nautical
surveying</b></col></mcol>, <cd>that branch of surveying which
determines the forms of coasts and harbors, the entrances of rivers,
with the position of islands, rocks, and shoals, the depth of water,
etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Plane surveying</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Plane</er>, <pos>a.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>Topographical
surveying</b></col>, <cd>that branch of surveying which involves the
process of ascertaining and representing upon a plane surface the
contour, physical features, etc., of any portion of the surface of the
earth.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surveyor</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vey"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One
placed to superintend others; an overseer; an inspector.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Were 't not madness then,<br/
To make the fox <qex>surveyor</qex> of the fold?</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who views and examines for the purpose of
ascertaining the condition, quantity, or quality of anything; <as>as, a
<ex>surveyor</ex> of highways, ordnance, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One who surveys or measures land; one who practices
the art of surveying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Customs)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An officer who
ascertains the contents of casks, and the quantity of liquors subject to
duty; a gauger.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>In the United States, an officer
whose duties include the various measures to be taken for ascertaining
the quantity, condition, and value of merchandise brought into a
port.</def> <au>Abbot.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Surveyor general</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A principal
surveyor; <as>as, the <ex>surveyor general</ex> of the king's manors, or
of woods and parks</as>.</cd> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An
officer having charge of the survey of the public lands of a land
district.</cd> <mark>[U.S.]</mark> <au>Davies & Peck (Math. Dict.).</au>
-- <col><b>Surveyor's compass</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Circumferentor</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Surveyor's level</b></col>.
<cd>See under <er>Level</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surveyorship</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vey"or*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The office of a
surveyor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surview</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*view"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sur-</ets> + <ets>view</ets>. Cf. <er>Survey</er>.]</ety> <def>To
survey; to make a survey of.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/To
<xex>surview</xex> his ground.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surview</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*view"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A survey.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Sanderson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survise</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vise"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Supervise</er>, and <er>Survey</er>.]</ety> <def>To look over; to
supervise.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survival</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*viv"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Survive</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A living or continuing longer
than, or beyond the existence of, another person, thing, or event; an
outliving.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Arhaeol. & Ethnol.)</fld> <def>Any habit, usage, or
belief, remaining from ancient times, the origin of which is often
unknown, or imperfectly known.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The close bearing of the doctrine of <qex>survival</qex> on the
study of manners and customs.</q> <rj><qau>Tylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Survival of the fittest</b></col>. <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<cd>See <cref>Natural selection</cref>, under
<er>Natural</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survivancy</ent><br/
<ent>Survivance</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sur*viv"ance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sur*viv"an*cy</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>survivance</ets>.]</ety> <def>Survivorship.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His son had the <qex>survivance</qex> of the stadtholdership.</q>
<rj><qau>Bp. Burnet.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survive</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vive"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Survived</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Surviving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>survivre</ets>, L. <ets>supervivere</ets>; <ets>super</ets> over +
<ets>vivere</ets> to live. See <er>Super-</er>, and
<er>Victuals</er>.]</ety> <def>To live beyond the life or existence of;
to live longer than; to outlive; to outlast; <as>as, to <ex>survive</ex>
a person or an event</as>.</def> <rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> <q>I'll assure her of<br/
Her widowhood, be it that she <qex>survive</qex> me,<br/
In all my lands and leases whatsoever.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survive</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*vive"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To remain alive; to continue
to live.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Thy pleasure,<br/
Which, when no other enemy <qex>survives</qex>,<br/
Still conquers all the conquerors.</q> <rj><qau>Sir J.
Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Alike are life and death,<br/
When life in death <qex>survives</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survivency</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*viv"en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Survivorship.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surviver</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*viv"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who survives; a
survivor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Surviving</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*viv"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Remaining alive; yet living or
existing; <as>as, <ex>surviving</ex> friends; <ex>surviving</ex>
customs</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survivor</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*viv"or</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
survives or outlives another person, or any time, event, or
thing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>survivor</qex> bound<br/
In filial obligation for some term<br/
To do obsequious sorrow.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The longer liver of two joint
tenants, or two persons having a joint interest in anything.</def>
<rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Survivorship</ent><br/
<hw>Sur*viv"or*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state of
being a survivor.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The right of a joint tenant, or
other person who has a joint interest in an estate, to take the whole
estate upon the death of other.</def> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Chance of survivorship</b></col>, <cd>the chance that a
person of a given age has of surviving another of a giving age; thus, by
the Carlisle tables of mortality the chances of survivorship for two
persons, aged 25 and 65, are 89 and 11 respectively, or about 8 to 1
that the elder die first.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susceptibility</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*cep`ti*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Susceptibilities</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[Cf. F. <ets>susceptibilit<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The state or quality of being susceptible; the capability of
receiving impressions, or of being affected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Specifically, capacity for deep feeling or emotional
excitement; sensibility, in its broadest acceptation; impressibility;
sensitiveness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Magnetic susceptibility</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>,
<cd>the intensity of magnetization of a body placed in a uniform
megnetic field of unit strength.</cd> <au>Sir W. Thomson.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Capability; sensibility; feeling;
emotion.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susceptible</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*cep"ti*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>suscipere</ets>, <ets>susceptum</ets>, to take up, to support,
undertake, recognize, admit; pref. <ets>sus</ets> (see <er>Sub-</er>) +
<ets>capere</ets> to take. See <er>Capable</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Capable of admitting anything additional, or any change, affection,
or influence; readily acted upon; <as>as, a body <ex>susceptible</ex> of
color or of alteration</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It sheds on souls <qex>susceptible</qex> of light,<br/
The glorious dawn of our eternal day.</q> <rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Capable of impression; having nice sensibility;
impressible; tender; sensitive; <as>as, children are more
<ex>susceptible</ex> than adults; a man of a <ex>susceptible</ex>
heart.</as></def><-- = impressionable --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Candidates are . . . not very <qex>susceptible</qex> of
affronts.</q> <rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I am constitutionally <qex>susceptible</qex> of noises.</q>
<rj><qau>Lamb.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sus*cep"ti*ble*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos> --
<wf>Sus*cep"ti*bly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susception</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*cep"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>susceptio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>susception</ets>. See
<er>Susceptible</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of taking;
reception.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susceptive</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*cep"tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Susceptible.</def> <au>I. Watts.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Sus*cep"tive*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susceptivity</ent><br/
<hw>Sus`cep*tiv"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Capacity for
receiving; susceptibility.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Wollaston.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susceptor</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*cep"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. See
<er>Susceptible</er>.]</ety> <def>One who undertakes anything;
specifically, a godfather; a sponsor; a guardian.</def> <rj><au>Puller.
Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suscipiency</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*cip"i*en*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Admission.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suscipient</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*cip"i*ent</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suscipiens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>suscipere</ets>. See
<er>Susceptible</er>.]</ety> <def>Receiving; admitting.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suscipient</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*cip"i*ent</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who takes or admits; one
who receives.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Jer. Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suscitability</ent><br/
<hw>Sus`ci*ta*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Capability
of being suscitated; excitability.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suscitate</ent><br/
<hw>Sus"ci*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suscitated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Suscitating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>suscitatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suscitare</ets> to lift up, to
rouse; pref. <ets>sus-</ets> (see <er>Sub-</er>) + <ets>citare</ets> to
rouse, excite. Cf. <er>Excite</er>, <er>Incite</er>.]</ety> <def>To
rouse; to excite; to call into life and action.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suscitation</ent><br/
<hw>Sus`ci*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suscitatio</ets>: cf. F. <ets>suscitation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The
act of raising or exciting.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A mere <qex>suscitation</qex> or production of a thing.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suslik</ent><br/
<hw>Sus"lik</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Russ.
<ets>s<uacute/slik'</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A ground
squirrel (<spn>Spermophilus citillus</spn>) of Europe and Asia. It has
large cheek pouches.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>souslik</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspect</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suspectus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>suspicere</ets> to look up, admire,
esteem, to look at secretly or askance, to mistrust; <ets>sub</ets>
under + <ets>specere</ets> to look: cf. F. <ets>suspect</ets> suspected,
suspicious. See <er>Spy</er>, and cf. <er>Suspicion</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Suspicious; inspiring distrust.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Suspect</qex> [was] his face, <qex>suspect</qex> his word
also.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Suspected; distrusted.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What I can do or offer is <qex>suspect</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspect</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[LL. <ets>suspectus</ets>. See
<er>Suspect</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Suspicion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>So with <qex>suspect</qex>, with fear and grief, dismayed.</q>
<rj><qau>Fairfax.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, is suspected; an object of
suspicion; -- formerly applied to persons and things; now, only to
persons suspected of crime.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspect</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Suspected</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Suspecting</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To imagine to
exist; to have a slight or vague opinion of the existence of, without
proof, and often upon weak evidence or no evidence; to mistrust; to
surmise; -- commonly used regarding something unfavorable, hurtful, or
wrong; <as>as, to <ex>suspect</ex> the presence of
disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nothing makes a man <qex>suspect</qex> much, more than to know
little; and therefore men should remedy suspicion by procuring to know
more.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>From her hand I could <qex>suspect</qex> no ill.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To imagine to be guilty, upon slight evidence, or
without proof; <as>as, to <ex>suspect</ex> one of
equivocation</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To hold to be uncertain; to doubt; to mistrust; to
distruct; <as>as, to <ex>suspect</ex> the truth of a story</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To look up to; to respect.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To mistrust; distrust; surmise; doubt.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspect</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To imagine guilt; to have a
suspicion or suspicions; to be suspicious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>If I <qex>suspect</qex> without cause, why then make sport at
me.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspectable</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>That may be
suspected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspected</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Distrusted; doubted.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sus*pect"ed*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sus*pect"ed*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspecter</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
suspects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspectful</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Apt to suspect or
mistrust; full of suspicion; suspicious; <as>as, to be
<ex>suspectful</ex> of the motives of others</as>.</def>
<au>Milton.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Sus*pect"ful*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspection</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pec"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Suspicion.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspectiousness</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pec"tious*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Suspiciousness; cause for suspicion.</def> <mark>[Obs. & R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Ld. Berners.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspectless</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pect"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Not
suspecting; having no suspicion.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir T.
Herbert.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Not suspected; not mistrusted.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Beau. & Fl.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspend</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Suspended</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Suspending</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F. <ets>suspendre</ets>, or
OF. <ets>souspendre</ets> (where the prefix is L. <ets>subtus</ets>
below, from <ets>sub</ets> under), L. <ets>suspendere</ets>,
<ets>suspensum</ets>; pref. <ets>sus-</ets> (see <er>Sub-</er>) +
<ets>pendere</ets> to hang. See <er>Pedant</er>, and cf.
<er>Suspense</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To attach to
something above; to hang; <as>as, to <ex>suspend</ex> a ball by a
thread; to <ex>suspend</ex> a needle by a loadstone</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make to depend; <as>as, God hath
<ex>suspended</ex> the promise of eternal life on the condition of
obedience and holiness of life</as>.</def> <mark>[Archaic]</mark>
<rj><au>Tillotson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to cease for a time; to hinder from
proceeding; to interrupt; to delay; to stay.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Suspend</qex> your indignation against my brother.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The guard nor fights nor fies; their fate so near<br/
At once <qex>suspends</qex> their courage and their fear.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To hold in an undetermined or undecided state;
<as>as, to <ex>suspend</ex> one's judgment or opinion</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Locke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To debar, or cause to withdraw temporarily, from any
privilege, from the execution of an office, from the enjoyment of
income, etc.; <as>as, to <ex>suspend</ex> a student from college; to
<ex>suspend</ex> a member of a club.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Good men should not be <qex>suspended</qex> from the exercise of
their ministry and deprived of their livelihood for ceremonies which are
on all hands acknowledged indifferent.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Sanderson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To cause to cease for a time from operation or
effect; <as>as, to <ex>suspend</ex> the habeas corpus act; to
<ex>suspend</ex> the rules of a legislative body.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>To support in a liquid, as an
insoluble powder, by stirring, to facilitate chemical action.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To suspend payment</b></col> <fld>(Com.)</fld>, <cd>to
cease paying debts or obligations; to fail; -- said of a merchant, a
bank, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To hang; interrupt; delay; intermit; stay;
hinder; debar.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1454 --></p>

<p><ent>Suspend</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pend"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To cease from
operation or activity; esp., to stop payment, or be unable to meet
obligations or engagements (said of a commercial firm or a
bank).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspender</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pend"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, suspends; esp., one of a pair of straps or braces worn over the
shoulders, for holding up the trousers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensation</ent><br/
<hw>Sus`pen*sa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. LL.
<ets>suspensatio</ets> suspension from a charge or benefice.]</ety>
<def>The act of suspending, or the state of being suspended, especially
for a short time; temporary suspension.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspense</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pense"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>suspens</ets>, L. <ets>suspensus</ets>, p. p. of
<ets>suspendere</ets>. See <er>Suspend</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Held or lifted up; held or prevented from proceeding.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The great light of day] <qex>suspense</qex> in heaven.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Expressing, or proceeding from, suspense or
doubt.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Expectation held his look
<xex>suspense</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspense</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pense"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From F. <ets>suspens</ets>, a.
See <er>Suspense</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The state
of being suspended; specifically, a state of uncertainty and
expectation, with anxiety or apprehension; indetermination; indecision;
<as>as, the <ex>suspense</ex> of a person waiting for the verdict of a
jury</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ten days the prophet in <qex>suspense</qex> remained.</q>
<rj><qau>Denham.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Upon the ticklish balance of <qex>suspense</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cessation for a time; stop; pause.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A cool <qex>suspense</qex> from pleasure and from pain.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>suspense</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>A temporary cessation of one's right; suspension, as when the rent
or other profits of land cease by unity of possession of land and
rent.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Suspense account</b></col> <fld>(Bookkeeping)</fld>,
<cd>an account in which receipts or disbursements are temporarily
entered until their proper position in the books is
determined.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensely</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pense"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In suspense.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hales.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensibility</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pen`si*bil"i*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
quality or state of being suspensible.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensible</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pen"si*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Capable of
being suspended; capable of being held from sinking.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspension</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pen"sion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>suspension</ets>, L. <ets>suspensio</ets> arched work, imperfect
pronunciation. See <er>Suspend</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
suspending, or the state of being suspended; pendency; <as>as,
<ex>suspension</ex> from a hook</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, temporary delay, interruption, or
cessation</def>; as: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Of labor, study, pain, etc.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of decision, determination, judgment, etc.; <as>as, to
ask a <ex>suspension</ex> of judgment or opinion in view of evidence to
be produced</as>.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>Of the payment of what is due;
<as>as, the <ex>suspension</ex> of a mercantile firm or of a
bank</as>.</def> <sd>(d)</sd> <def>Of punishment, or sentence of
punishment.</def> <sd>(e)</sd> <def>Of a person in respect of the
exercise of his office, powers, prerogative, etc.; <as>as, the
<ex>suspension</ex> of a student or of a clergyman</as>.</def>
<sd>(f)</sd> <def>Of the action or execution of law, etc.; <as>as, the
<ex>suspension</ex> of the habeas corpus act</as>.</def> <-- # each of
the above lettered definitions is elliptical; needs special handling for
analysis. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A conditional withholding, interruption, or delay;
<as>as, the <ex>suspension</ex> of a payment on the performance of a
condition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The state of a solid when its particles are mixed
with, but undissolved in, a fluid, and are capable of separation by
straining; also, any substance in this state.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A keeping of the hearer in doubt
and in attentive expectation of what is to follow, or of what is to be
the inference or conclusion from the arguments or observations
employed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Scots Law)</fld> <def>A stay or postponement of
execution of a sentence condemnatory by means of letters of suspension
granted on application to the lord ordinary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The prolongation of one or more
tones of a chord into the chord which follows, thus producing a
momentary discord, suspending the concord which the ear expects. Cf.
<er>Retardation</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Pleas in suspension</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>pleas
which temporarily abate or suspend a suit.</cd> -- <col><b>Points of
suspension</b></col> <fld>(Mech.)</fld>, <cd>the points, as in the axis
or beam of a balance, at which the weights act, or from which they are
suspended.</cd> -- <col><b>Suspension bridge</b></col>, <cd>a bridge
supported by chains, ropes, or wires, which usually pass over high piers
or columns at each end, and are secured in the ground beyond.</cd> --
<col><b>Suspension of arms</b></col> <fld>(Mil.)</fld>, <cd>a short
truce or cessation of operations agreed on by the commanders of
contending armies, as for burying the dead, making proposal for
surrender or for peace, etc.</cd> -- <col><b>Suspension scale</b></col>,
<cd>a scale in which the platform hangs suspended from the weighing
apparatus instead of resting upon it.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Delay; interruption; intermission;
stop.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensive</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pen"sive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>suspensif</ets>. See <er>Suspend</er>.]</ety> <def>Tending to
suspend, or to keep in suspense; causing interruption or delay;
uncertain; doubtful.</def> <ldquo/In <xex>suspensive</xex>
thoughts.<rdquo/ <au>Beaumont.</au> <ldquo/A <xex>suspensive</xex>
veto.<rdquo/ <au>Macaulay.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The provisional and <qex>suspensive</qex> attitude.</q>
<rj><qau>J. Morley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Suspensive</b></col><cd> condition <fld>(Scots
Law)</fld>, a condition precedent, or a condition without the
performance of which the contract can not be completed.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensor</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pen"sor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A suspensory.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The cord which suspends the
embryo; and which is attached to the radicle in the young state; the
proembryo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensorium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sus`pen*so"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Suspensoria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Anything which suspends or
holds up a part: especially, the mandibular suspensorium (a series of
bones, or of cartilages representing them) which connects the base of
the lower jaw with the skull in most vertebrates below
mammals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensory</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pen"so*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Suspended; hanging; depending.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fitted or serving to suspend; suspending; <as>as, a
<ex>suspensory</ex> muscle</as>.</def> <rj><au>Ray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to a
suspensorium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspensory</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pen"so*ry</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>suspensoir</ets>, <ets>suspensoire</ets>.]</ety> <def>That which
suspends, or holds up, as a truss</def>; specifically <fld>(Med.)</fld>,
<def>a bandage or bag for supporting the scrotum.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspicable</ent><br/
<hw>Sus"pi*ca*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suspacabilis</ets>, fr. <ets>suspicari</ets> to suspect, akin to
<ets>suspicere</ets>. See <er>Suspect</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<def>Liable to suspicion; suspicious.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is a very <qex>suspicable</qex> business.</q> <rj><qau>Dr. H.
more.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspiciency</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pi"cien*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From L.
<ets>suspiciens</ets>, p. pr. of <ets>suspicere</ets>. See
<er>Suspect</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Suspiciousness;
suspicion.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Hopkins.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspicion</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pi"cion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>suspecioun</ets>, OF. <ets>souspe<cced/on</ets>, F.
<ets>soup<cced/on</ets>, L. <ets>suspectio</ets> a looking up to, an
esteeming highly, suspicion, fr. <ets>suspicere</ets> to look up, to
esteem, to mistrust. The modern form <ets>suspicion</ets> in English and
French is in imitation of L. <ets>suspicio</ets> mistrust, suspicion.
See <er>Suspect</er>, and cf. <er>Suspicious</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The act of suspecting; the imagination or apprehension of the
existence of something (esp. something wrong or hurtful) without proof,
or upon very slight evidence, or upon no evidence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Suspicions</qex> among thoughts are like bats among birds,
they ever fly by twilight.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Slight degree; suggestion; hint.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The features are mild but expressive, with just a
<qex>suspicion</qex> . . . of saturnine or sarcastic humor.</q>
<rj><qau>A. W. Ward.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Jealousy; distrust; mistrust; diffidence;
doubt.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspicion</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pi"cion</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To view with suspicion; to
suspect; to doubt.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Low]</mark>
<rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspicious</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pi"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>suspecious</ets>; cf. L. <ets>suspiciosus</ets>. See
<er>Suspicion</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Inclined to suspect; given
or prone to suspicion; apt to imagine without proof.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Nature itself, after it has done an injury, will ever be
<qex>suspicious</qex>; and no man can love the person he suspects.</q>
<rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many mischievous insects are daily at work to make men of merit
<qex>suspicious</qex> of each other.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Indicating suspicion, mistrust, or fear.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>We have a <qex>suspicious</qex>, fearful, constrained
countenance.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Liable to suspicion; adapted to raise suspicion;
giving reason to imagine ill; questionable; <as>as, an author of
<ex>suspicious</ex> innovations; <ex>suspicious</ex>
circumstances</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I spy a black, <qex>suspicious</qex>, threatening could.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Jealous; distrustful; mistrustful; doubtful;
questionable. See <er>Jealous</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sus*pi"cious*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sus*pi"cious*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspiral</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pir"al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Suspire</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A breathing hole; a vent or
ventiduct.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A spring of water passing under ground toward a
cistern or conduit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspiration</ent><br/
<hw>Sus`pi*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suspiratio</ets>. See <er>Suspire</er>.]</ety> <def>The act of
sighing, or fetching a long and deep breath; a deep respiration; a
sigh.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Windy <qex>suspiration</qex> of forced breath.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspire</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pire"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>suspirare</ets> to breathe out, to sigh; <ets>sub</ets> under +
<ets>spirare</ets> to breathe: cf. F. <ets>souspirer</ets>, OF.
<ets>souspirer</ets>.]</ety> <def>To fetch a long, deep breath; to sigh;
to breathe.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fireflies that <qex>suspire</qex><br/
In short, soft lapses of transported flame.</q> <rj><qau>Mrs.
Browning.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspire</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pire"</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>suspirium</ets>.]</ety> <def>A long, deep breath; a sigh.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suspired</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*pired"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Ardently desired or
longed for; earnestly coveted.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir H.
Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustain</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*tain"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sustained</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sustaining</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sustenen</ets>, <ets>susteinen</ets>, OF. <ets>sustenir</ets>,
<ets>sostenir</ets>, F. <ets>soutenir</ets> (the French prefix is
properly fr. L. <ets>subtus</ets> below, fr. <ets>sub</ets> under), L.
<ets>sustinere</ets>; pref. <ets>sus-</ets> (see <er>Sub-</er>) +
<ets>tenere</ets> to hold. See <er>Tenable</er>, and cf.
<er>Sustenance</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To keep from falling; to
bear; to uphold; to support; <as>as, a foundation <ex>sustains</ex> the
superstructure; a beast <ex>sustains</ex> a load; a rope
<ex>sustains</ex> a weight</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every pillar the temple to <qex>sustain</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, to keep from sinking, as in despondence, or
the like; to support.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No comfortable expectations of another life to <qex>sustain</qex>
him under the evils in this world.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To maintain; to keep alive; to support; to subsist;
to nourish; <as>as, provisions to <ex>sustain</ex> an
army</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To aid, comfort, or relieve; to vindicate.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His sons, who seek the tyrant to <qex>sustain</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To endure without failing or yielding; to bear up
under; <as>as, to <ex>sustain</ex> defeat and
disappointment</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To suffer; to bear; to undergo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Shall Turnus, then, such endless toil <qex>sustain</qex>?</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You shall <qex>sustain</qex> more new disgraces.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To allow the prosecution of; to admit as valid; to
sanction; to continue; not to dismiss or abate; <as>as, the court
<ex>sustained</ex> the action or suit</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To prove; to establish by evidence; to corroborate
or confirm; to be conclusive of; <as>as, to <ex>sustain</ex> a charge,
an accusation, or a proposition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To support; uphold; subsist; assist; relieve;
suffer; undergo.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustain</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*tain"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, upholds or sustains; a sustainer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I waked again, for my <qex>sustain</qex> was the Lord.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustainable</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*tain"a*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>soutenable</ets>, OF. <ets>soustenable</ets>.]</ety> <def>Capable
of being sustained or maintained; <as>as, the action is not
<ex>sustainable</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustained</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*tained"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Held up to a
certain pitch, degree, or level; uniform; <as>as, <ex>sustained</ex>
pasion; a <ex>sustained</ex> style of writing; a <ex>sustained</ex> note
in music</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustainer</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*tain"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, sustains.</def> <rj><au>Waterland.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustainment</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*tain"ment</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
sustaining; maintenance; support.</def> <rj><au>Milton.
Lowell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustaltic</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*tal"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
contractile, fr. <?/ to draw together, to moderate; <?/ together + <?/
to place.]</ety> <def>Mournful; -- said of a species of music among the
ancient Greeks.</def> <rj><au>Busby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustenance</ent><br/
<hw>Sus"te*nance</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OF.
<ets>sustenance</ets>, <ets>sostenance</ets>, <ets>soustenance</ets>:
cf. L. <ets>sustenentia</ets> endurance. See <er>Sustain</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sustaining; support; maintenance;
subsistence; <as>as, the <ex>sustenance</ex> of the body; the
<ex>sustenance</ex> of life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which supports life; food; victuals;
provisions; means of living; <as>as, the city has ample
<ex>sustenance</ex></as>.</def> <ldquo/A man of little
<xex>sustenance</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For lying is thy <qex>sustenance</qex>, thy food.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustentacle</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*ten"ta*cle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sustentaculum</ets>. See <er>Sustentation</er>.]</ety>
<def>Sustenance.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustentacular</ent><br/
<hw>Sus`ten*tac"u*lar</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sustenance</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Supporting;
sustaining; <as>as, a <ex>sustentacular</ex> tissue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustentate</ent><br/
<hw>Sus"ten*tate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
sustain.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>C. Reade.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustentation</ent><br/
<hw>Sus`ten*ta"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sustentatio</ets> sustenance, maintenance, fr.
<ets>sustentare</ets> to support, maintain, v. intens. fr.
<ets>sustinere</ets> to sustain: cf. F. <ets>sustentation</ets>. See
<er>Sustain</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sustaining, or the
state of being sustained; preservation from falling; support;
sustenance; maintenance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The aggregate of the functions
by which a living organism is maintained in a normal condition of weight
and growth.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sustentation fund</b></col> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>, <cd>a
fund of a religious body for support of its ministers, chapels, etc.;
<as>as, the <ex>sustentation fund</ex> of the Free Church of
Scotland</as>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustentative</ent><br/
<hw>Sus"ten*ta*tive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Adapted to
sustain, strengthen, or corroborate; <as>as, <ex>sustentative</ex>
citations or quotations</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sustentative functions</b></col> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld>,
<cd>those functions of the body which affect its material composition
and thus determine its mass.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustention</ent><br/
<hw>Sus*ten"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sustentation.</def> <mark>[R. or Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In fine images, in <qex>sustention</qex>, in irony, they surpass
anything that Burke ever wrote.</q> <rj><qau>J. Morley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sustre</ent><br/
<ent>Suster</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sus"ter</hw>, <hw>Sus"tre</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Susters</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>,
<plw>Sustres</plw>, <it>or</it> <plw>Sustren</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu>
<def>Sister.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There are seven <qex>sustren</qex>, that serve truth ever.</q>
<rj><qau>Piers Plowman.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susu</ent><br/
<hw>Su"su</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>See
<er>Soosoo</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susurrant</ent><br/
<hw>Su*sur"rant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>susurrans</ets>, p. pr. from <ets>susurrare</ets> to
whisper.]</ety> <def>Whispering.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/The soft
<xex>susurrant</xex> sigh.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Poetry of
Anti-Jacobin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susurration</ent><br/
<hw>Su`sur*ra"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>susurratio</ets>, fr. <ets>susurrare</ets> to whisper: cf. F.
<ets>susurration</ets>.]</ety> <def>A whispering; a soft murmur.</def>
<ldquo/Soft <xex>susurrations</xex> of the trees.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susurringly</ent><br/
<hw>Su*sur"ring*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In the manner
of a whisper.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susurrous</ent><br/
<hw>Su*sur"rous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>susurrus</ets>.]</ety> <def>Whispering; rustling; full of
whispering sounds.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Susurrus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su*sur"rus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>The act of whispering; a whisper; a murmur.</def> <rj><au>De
Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The soft <qex>susurrus</qex> and sighs of the branches.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutile</ent><br/
<hw>Su"tile</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sutilis</ets>, fr. <ets>suere</ets> to sew: cf. F.
<ets>sutile</ets>.]</ety> <def>Done by stitching.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Boswell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutler</ent><br/
<hw>Sut"ler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D.
<ets>zoetelaar</ets>, OD. <ets>soetelaar</ets>, a small trader,
especially in camps, fr. <ets>soetelen</ets> to undertake low offices;
cf. G. <ets>sudeln</ets> to do dirty work, to sully, soil, E.
<ets>suds</ets>.]</ety> <def>A person who follows an army, and sells to
the troops provisions, liquors, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutlership</ent><br/
<hw>Sut"ler*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The condition or occupation of
a sutler.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutling</ent><br/
<hw>Sut"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Belonging to sutlers;
engaged in the occupation of a sutler.</def>
<rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutor</ent><br/
<hw>Su"tor</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of sirup made by
the Indians of Arizona from the fruit of some cactaceous plant (probably
the <xex>Cereus giganteus</xex>).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutra</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Su"tra</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sutras</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[Skr. <ets>s<?/tra</ets> a
thread, a string of rules; an aphorism; fr. <ets>siv</ets> to
sew.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A precept; an aphorism; a
brief rule.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A collection of such
aphorisms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>A body of Hindoo literature
containing aphorisms on grammar, meter, law, and philosophy, and forming
a connecting link between the Vedic and later Sanscrit literature.</def>
<rj><au>Balfour (Cyc. of India).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suttee</ent><br/
<hw>Sut*tee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Skr.
<ets>sat<imac/</ets> a faithful wife, fem. of <ets>sant</ets> existing,
real, true, good, p. pr. of <ets>as</ets> to be. Cf.
<er>Sooth</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A Hindoo widow who immolates
herself, or is immolated, on the funeral pile of her husband; -- so
called because this act of self-immolation is regarded as envincing
excellence of wifely character.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of burning a widow on the funeral pile of
her husband.</def> <mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The practice, though abolished in British India law in
1829, is not wholly prevented.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutteeism</ent><br/
<hw>Sut*tee"ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The practice of
self-immolation of widows in Hindostan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1455 --></p>

<p><ent>Suttle</ent><br/
<hw>Sut"tle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Com.)</fld> <def>The weight when the tare has
been deducted, and tret is yet to be allowed.</def>
<rj><au>M<?/Culloch.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suttle</ent><br/
<hw>Sut"tle</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sutler</er>.]</ety>
<def>To act as sutler; to supply provisions and other articles to
troops.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutural</ent><br/
<hw>Su"tur*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sutural</ets>, NL. <ets>suturals</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to a suture, or seam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Taking place at a suture; <as>as,
a <ex>sutural</ex> dehiscence</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suturally</ent><br/
<hw>Su"tur*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sutural
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suturated</ent><br/
<hw>Su"tur*a`ted</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sewed or knit
together; united by a suture; stitched.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suture</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>sutura</ets>,
fr. <ets>suere</ets>, <ets>sutum</ets>, to sew or stitch: cf. F.
<ets>suture</ets>. See <er>Sew</er> to unite with thread.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of sewing; also, the line along which two
things or parts are sewed together, or are united so as to form a seam,
or that which resembles a seam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Surg.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The uniting of the
parts of a wound by stitching.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The stitch by
which the parts are united.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The line of union, or seam, in an
immovable articulation, like those between the bones of the skull; also,
such an articulation itself; synarthrosis. See <cref>Harmonic
suture</cref>, under <er>Harmonic</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The line, or seam,
formed by the union of two margins in any part of a plant; <as>as, the
ventral <ex>suture</ex> of a legume</as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A line
resembling a seam; <as>as, the dorsal <ex>suture</ex> of a legume, which
really corresponds to a midrib</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The line at which
the elytra of a beetle meet and are sometimes confluent.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A seam, or impressed line, as between the segments of
a crustacean, or between the whorls of a univalve shell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Glover's suture</b></col>, <col><b>Harmonic
suture</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Glover</er>,
<er>Harmonic</er>, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sutured</ent><br/
<hw>Su"tured</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a suture or
sutures; knit or united together.</def> <rj><au>Pennant.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suwarrow</ent><br/
<hw>Su*war"row</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The giant cactus (<spn>Cereus giganteus</spn>); -- so named by the
Indians of Arizona. Called also <altname>saguaro</altname>.</def> <--
Saguaro is the most common now --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suzerain</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ze*rain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., formed fr.
<ets>sus</ets> above, L. <ets>susum</ets>, <ets>sursum</ets> (fr.
<ets>sub</ets> under + <ets>versum</ets>, p. p. of <ets>vertere</ets> to
turn), after the analogy of <ets>souverain</ets>, E.
<ets>sovereign</ets>. See <er>Sub-</er>, and <er>Verse</er>.]</ety>
<def>A superior lord, to whom fealty is due; a feudal lord; a lord
paramount.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Suzerainty</ent><br/
<hw>Su"ze*rain*ty</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>suzerainet<eacute/</ets>.]</ety> <def>The dominion or authority of
a suzerain; paramount authority.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swa</ent><br/
<hw>Swa</hw> <pr>(sw<aum/)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>So</er>.]</ety> <def>So.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swab</ent><br/
<hw>Swab</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swabbed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swabbing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Swabber</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To clean with a mop or swab; to wipe when very
wet, as after washing; <as>as, to <ex>swab</ex> the desk of a
ship</as>.</def> <altsp>[Spelt also <asp>swob</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swab</ent><br/
<hw>Swab</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Written also <ets>swob</ets>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>A kind of mop for cleaning floors, the desks of
vessels, etc., esp. one made of rope-yarns or threads.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A bit of sponge, cloth, or the like, fastened to a
handle, for cleansing the mouth of a sick person, applying medicaments
to deep-seated parts, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>An epaulet.</def> <mark>[Sailor's
Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Marryat.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A cod, or pod, as of beans or pease.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bailey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A sponge, or other suitable substance, attached to a
long rod or handle, for cleaning the bore of a firearm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swabber</ent><br/
<hw>Swab"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To swab.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swabber</ent><br/
<hw>Swab"ber</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[D. <ets>zwabber</ets>; cf.D.
<ets>zwabberen</ets> to swab, G. <ets>schwabbern</ets>, Dan.
<ets>svabre</ets>, Sw. <ets>svab</ets> a swab, <ets>svabla</ets> to
swab.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who swabs a floor or desk.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Formerly, an interior officer on
board of British ships of war, whose business it was to see that the
ship was kept clean.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Same as <er>Swobber</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swad</ent><br/
<hw>Swad</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Probably fr. AS.
<ets>swe<?/ian</ets> to bind.]</ety> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>swod</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A cod, or pod, as of beans or
pease.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Swad</qex>, in the north, is a peascod shell -- thence used
for an empty, shallow-headed fellow.</q> <rj><qau>Blount.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A clown; a country bumpkin.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark> <ldquo/Country swains, and silly
<xex>swads</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Greene.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There was one busy fellow was their leader,<br/
A blunt, squat <qex>swad</qex>, but lower than yourself.</q> <rj><qau>B.
Jonson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A lump of mass; also, a crowd.</def> <mark>[Low,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Coal Mining)</fld> <def>A thin layer of refuse at
the bottom of a seam.</def> <rj><au>Raymond.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaddle</ent><br/
<hw>Swad"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>swe<?/il</ets>, <ets>swe<?/el</ets>, fr. <ets>swe<?/ain</ets> to
bind. See <er>Swathe</er>.]</ety> <def>Anything used to swaddle with, as
a cloth or band; a swaddling band.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They put me in bed in all my swaddles.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaddle</ent><br/
<hw>Swad"dle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Swaddled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swaddling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
bind as with a bandage; to bind or warp tightly with clothes; to swathe;
-- used esp. of infants; <as>as, to <ex>swaddle</ex> a
baby</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>swaddled</qex> me up in my nightgown with long pieces of
linen.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To beat; to cudgel.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Hudibras.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaddlebill</ent><br/
<hw>Swad"dle*bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The shoveler.</def> <mark>[Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaddler</ent><br/
<hw>Swad"dler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A term of contempt
for an Irish Methodist.</def> <rj><au>Shipley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaddling</ent><br/
<hw>Swad"dling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from
<er>Swaddle</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Swaddling band</b></col>, <col><b>Swaddling
cloth</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Swaddling clout</b></col></mcol>,
<cd>a band or cloth wrapped round an infant, especially round a newborn
infant.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye shall find the babe wrapped in <qex>swaddling clothes</qex>,
lying in a manger.</q> <rj><qau>Luke ii. 12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swag</ent><br/
<hw>Swag</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swagged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swagging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>sveggja</ets>, <ets>sveigja</ets> to bend, to sway, Norw.
<ets>svaga</ets> to sway. See <er>Sway</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
hang or move, as something loose and heavy; to sway; to swing.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sink down by its weight; to sag.</def>
<rj><au>Sir H. Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>swag</qex> as a fat person's belly <qex>swaggeth</qex> as
he goeth.</q> <rj><qau>Palsgrave.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>


<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To tramp carrying a swag.</def>
<mark>[Australia]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swag</ent><br/
<hw>Swag</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A swaying, irregular
motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A burglar's or thief's booty; boodle.</def>
<mark>[Cant or Slang]</mark> <rj><au>Charles Reade.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <mark>[Australia]</mark> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A tramping
bushman's luggage, rolled up either in canvas or in a blanket so as to
form a long bundle, and carried on the back or over the shoulder; --
called also a <altname>bluey</altname>, or a
<altname>drum</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any bundle of luggage
similarly rolled up; hence, luggage in general.</def></p>

<p><q>He tramped for years till the <qex>swag</qex> he bore seemed part
of himself.</q> <rj><qau>Lawson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swag-bellied</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"-bel`lied</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
prominent, overhanging belly.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swagbelly</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"bel`ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
prominent, overhanging belly.</def> <rj><au>Smollett.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Any large tumor developed in the
abdomen, and neither fluctuating nor sonorous.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swage</ent><br/
<hw>Swage</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Swaged</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Swaging</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Equiv. to
<ets>suage</ets>, abbrev. fr. <ets>assuage</ets>.]</ety> <def>See
<er>Assuage</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swage</ent><br/
<hw>Swage</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A tool, variously shaped or grooved
on the end or face, used by blacksmiths and other workers in metals, for
shaping their work, whether sheet metal or forging, by holding the swage
upon the work, or the work upon the swage, and striking with a
sledge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swage block</b></col>, <cd>a perforated block of iron,
having grooved sides and adapted for use in heading bolts and swaging
objects of large size.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swage</ent><br/
<hw>Swage</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To shape by means of a swage; to
fashion, as a piece of iron, by forcing it into a groove or mold having
the required shape.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swagger</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swaggered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Swaggering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>swag</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To walk with a swaying motion;
hence, to walk and act in a pompous, consequential manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A man who <qex>swaggers</qex> about London clubs.</q>
<rj><qau>Beaconsfield.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To boast or brag noisily; to be ostentatiously proud
or vainglorious; to bluster; to bully.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>What a pleasant it is . . . to <qex>swagger</qex> at the bar!</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be great is not . . . to <qex>swagger</qex> at our footmen.</q>
<rj><qau>Colier.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swagger</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"ger</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To bully.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swagger</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"ger</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or manner of a
swaggerer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He gave a half <qex>swagger</qex>, half leer, as he stepped forth
to receive us.</q> <rj><qau>W. Irving.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swagger</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A swagman.</def>
<mark>[Australia]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaggerer</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"ger*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who swaggers;
a blusterer; a bully; a boastful, noisy fellow.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaggie</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"gie</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A swagman.</def>
<mark>[Australia]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaggy</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Inclined to swag;
sinking, hanging, or leaning by its weight.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swagman</ent><br/
<hw>Swag"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bushman carrying a
swag and traveling on foot; -- called also <altname>swagsman</altname>,
<altname>swagger</altname>, and <altname>swaggie</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><q>Once a jolly <qex>swagman</qex> sat beside a billabong<br/
Under the shade of a coolibah tree.<br/
And he sang as he sat and watched his billy boiling,<br/
`Who'll come a-waltzing Matilda with me?'</q> <rj><qau>[Waltzing
Matilda, an Australian tune.]</qau></rj><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swagsman</ent><br/
<hw>Swags"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A swagman.</def>
<mark>[Australia]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swain</ent><br/
<hw>Swain</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>swain</ets>,
<ets>swein</ets>, Icel. <ets>sveinn</ets> a boy, servant; akin to Sw.
<ets>sven</ets>, Dan. <ets>svend</ets>, AS. <ets>sw<amac/n</ets>, OHG.
<ets>swein</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A servant.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Him behoves serve himself that has no <qex>swain</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A young man dwelling in the country; a rustic; esp.,
a cuntry gallant or lover; -- chiefly in poetry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It were a happy life<br/
To be no better than a homely <qex>swain</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Blest <qex>swains</qex>! whose nymphs in every grace excel.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swainish</ent><br/
<hw>Swain"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to, or resembling, a
swain; rustic; ignorant.</def> <ldquo/An ungentle and
<xex>swainish</xex> beast.<rdquo/ <au>Milton.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Swain"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos>
<au>Emerson.</au></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swainling</ent><br/
<hw>Swain"ling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A little
swain.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swainmote</ent><br/
<hw>Swain"mote`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Swain</ets>
+ <ets>mote</ets> meeting: cf. LL. <ets>swanimotum</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Eng. Forest Law)</fld> <def>A court held before the verders of the
forest as judges, by the steward of the court, thrice every year, the
swains, or freeholders, within the forest composing the jury.</def>
<altsp>[Written also <asp>swanimote</asp>, and
<asp>sweinmote</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Blackstone.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swainship</ent><br/
<hw>Swain"ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The condition of a
swain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaip</ent><br/
<hw>Swaip</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Sweep</er>.]</ety> <def>To walk proudly; to sweep along.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Todd.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swal</ent><br/
<hw>Swal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos> <mord>of
<er>Swell</er></mord>. <def>Swelled.</def>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swale</ent><br/
<hw>Swale</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Icel.
<ets>svalr</ets> cool, <ets>svala</ets> to cool.]</ety> <def>A valley or
low place; a tract of low, and usually wet, land; a moor; a fen.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng. & Local, U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swale</ent><br/
<hw>Swale</hw>, <pos>v. i. & t.</pos> <def>To melt and waste away; to
singe. See <er>Sweal</er>, <pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swale</ent><br/
<hw>Swale</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A gutter in a candle.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallet</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schwall</ets> a sea swell, from <ets>schwellen</ets> to swell, E.
<ets>swell</ets>.]</ety> <def>Water breaking in upon the miners at their
work; -- so called among tin miners.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallow</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>swalowe</ets>, AS. <ets>swalewe</ets>, <ets>swealwe</ets>; akin to
D. <ets>zwaluw</ets>, OHG. <ets>swalawa</ets>, G. <ets>schwalbe</ets>,
Icel. & Sw. <ets>svala</ets>, Dan. <ets>svale</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of passerine birds
of the family <fam>Hirundinidae</fam>, especially one of those species
in which the tail is deeply forked. They have long, pointed wings, and
are noted for the swiftness and gracefulness of their flight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The most common North American species are the barn
swallow (see under <er>Barn</er>), the cliff, or eaves, swallow (see
under <er>Cliff</er>), the white-bellied, or tree, swallow
(<spn>Tachycineta bicolor</spn>), and the bank swallow (see under
<er>Bank</er>). The common European swallow (<spn>Chelidon
rustica</spn>), and the window swallow, or martin (<spn>Chelidon
urbica</spn>), are familiar species.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
swifts which resemble the true swallows in form and habits, as the
common American chimney swallow, or swift.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>The aperture in a block through
which the rope reeves.</def> <rj><au>Ham. Nav. Encyc.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swallow plover</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one
of several species of fork-tailed ploverlike birds of the genus
<gen>Glareola</gen>, as <spn>Glareola orientalis</spn> of India; a
pratincole.</cd> -- <col><b>Swallow shrike</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>any one of several species of East Indian and Asiatic birds of the
family <fam>Artamiidae</fam>, allied to the shrikes but similar to
swallows in appearance and habits. The ashy swallow shrike (<spn>Artamus
fuscus</spn>) is common in India.</cd> -- <col><b>Swallow
warbler</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of numerous species of
East Indian and Australian singing birds of the genus
<gen>Dicaeum</gen>. They are allied to the honeysuckers.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallow</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swallowed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Swallowing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>swolewen</ets>, <ets>swolwen</ets>, <ets>swolhen</ets>, AS.
<ets>swelgan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zwelgen</ets>, OHG.
<ets>swelahan</ets>, <ets>swelgan</ets>, G. <ets>schwelgen</ets> to
feast, to revel, Icel. <ets>svelgia</ets> to swallow, SW.
<ets>sv<aum/lja</ets>, Dan. <ets>svaelge</ets>. Cf. <er>Groundsel</er> a
plant.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To take into the stomach; to receive
through the gullet, or esophagus, into the stomach; <as>as, to
<ex>swallow</ex> food or drink</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As if I had <qex>swallowed</qex> snowballs for pills.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To draw into an abyss or gulf; to ingulf; to absorb
-- usually followed by <xex>up</xex>.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The earth opened her mouth, and <qex>swallowed</qex> them up, and
their houses.</q> <rj><qau>Num. xvi. 32.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To receive or embrace, as opinions or belief,
without examination or scruple; to receive implicitly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Though that story . . . be not so readily
<qex>swallowed</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir T. Browne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To engross; to appropriate; -- usually with
<xex>up</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Homer excels . . . in this, that he <qex>swallowed</qex> up the
honor of those who succeeded him.</q> <rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To occupy; to take up; to employ.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The necessary provision of the life <qex>swallows</qex> the
greatest part of their time.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To seize and waste; to exhaust; to
consume.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Corruption <qex>swallowed</qex> what the liberal hand<br/
Of bounty scattered.</q> <rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To retract; to recant; <as>as, to <ex>swallow</ex>
one's opinions</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Swallowed</xex> his vows
whole.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To put up with; to bear patiently or without
retaliation; <as>as, to <ex>swallow</ex> an affront or
insult</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To absorb; imbibe; ingulf; engross; consume. See
<er>Absorb</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallow</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To perform the act of
swallowing; <as>as, his cold is so severe he is unable to
<ex>swallow</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallow</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
swallowing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The gullet, or esophagus; the throat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Taste; relish; inclination; liking.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have no <qex>swallow</qex> for it.</q>
<rj><qau>Massinger.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Capacity for swallowing; voracity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>There being nothing too gross for the <qex>swallow</qex> of
political rancor.</q> <rj><qau>Prof. Wilson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>As much as is, or can be, swallowed at once; <as>as,
a <ex>swallow</ex> of water</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>That which ingulfs; a whirlpool.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fabyan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallower</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who swallows;
also, a glutton.</def> <rj><au>Tatler.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallowfish</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low*fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The European sapphirine gurnard (<spn>Trigla hirundo</spn>). It has
large pectoral fins.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallowtail</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low*tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>A kind of tenon or tongue used in making joints.
See <er>Dovetail</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A species of willow.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Fort.)</fld> <def>An outwork with converging sides,
its head or front forming a reentrant angle; -- so called from its form.
Called also <altname>priestcap</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A swallow-tailed coat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This Stultz coat, a blue <qex>swallowtail</qex>, with yellow
buttons.</q> <rj><qau>Thackeray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>An arrow.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
large and handsome butterflies, belonging to Papilio and allied genera,
in which the posterior border of each hind wing is prolongated in the
form of a long lobe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The black swallowtail, or asterias (see
<er>Papilio</er>), the blue swallowtail, or philenor, the tiger
swallowtail, or turnus (see <er>Turnus</er>), and the zebra swallowtail,
or ajax (see under <er>Zebra</er>) are common American species. See also
<er>Troilus</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallow-tailed</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low-tailed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having a tail like that of a swallow; hence, like a swallow's tail
in form; having narrow and tapering or pointed skirts; <as>as, a
<ex>swallow-tailed</ex> coat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>United by dovetailing;
dovetailed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swallow-tailed duck</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the
old squaw.</cd> -- <col><b>Swallow-tailed gull</b></col>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an Arctic gull (<spn>Xema furcata</spn>), which
has a deeply forked tail.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Swallow-tailed
hawk</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>Swallow-tailed kite</b></col></mcol>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the fork-tailed kite.</cd> --
<col><b>Swallow-tailed moth</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a European
moth (<spn>Urapteryx sambucaria</spn>) having tail-like lobes on the
hind wings.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swallowwort</ent><br/
<hw>Swal"low*wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>See <er>Celandine</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A
poisonous plant (<spn>Vincetoxicum officinale</spn>) of the Milkweed
family, at one time used in medicine; -- also called <altname>white
swallowwort</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>African swallowwort</b></col>, <cd>a plant of the genus
Stapelia.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swam</ent><br/
<hw>Swam</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Swim</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swamp</ent><br/
<hw>Swamp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS. <ets>swam</ets>
a fungus, OD. <ets>swam</ets> a sponge, D. <ets>zwam</ets> a fungus, G.
<ets>schwamm</ets> a sponge, Icel. <ets>sv<oum/ppr</ets>, Dan. & Sw.
<ets>swamp</ets>, Goth. <ets>swamms</ets>, Gr. <grk>somfo`s</grk>
porous, spongy.]</ety> <def>Wet, spongy land; soft, low ground saturated
with water, but not usually covered with it; marshy ground away from the
seashore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Gray <qex>swamps</qex> and pools, waste places of the hern.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>swamp</qex> differs from a bog and a marsh in producing
trees and shrubs, while the latter produce only herbage, plants, and
mosses.</q> <rj><qau>Farming Encyc. (E. Edwards, Words).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swamp blackbird</b></col>. <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Redwing</er> <sd>(b)</sd>.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp cabbage</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>skunk cabbage.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp
deer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>an Asiatic deer (<spn>Rucervus
Duvaucelli</spn>) of India.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp hen</b></col>.
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>An Australian azure-breasted bird
(<spn>Porphyrio bellus</spn>); -- called also
<altname>goollema</altname>.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>An Australian water
crake, or rail (<spn>Porzana Tabuensis</spn>); -- called also
<altname>little swamp hen</altname>.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd> <cd>The European
purple gallinule.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp honeysuckle</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an American shrub (<spn>Azalea viscosa</spn> syn.
<spn>Rhododendron viscosa</spn> or <spn>Rhododendron viscosum</spn>)
growing in swampy places, with fragrant flowers of a white color, or
white tinged with rose; -- called also <altname>swamp pink</altname> and
<altname>white swamp honeysuckle</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp
hook</b></col>, <cd>a hook and chain used by lumbermen in handling logs.
Cf. <er>Cant hook</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp itch</b></col>.
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Prairie itch</cref>, under
<er>Prairie</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp laurel</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a shrub (<spn>Kalmia glauca</spn>) having small
leaves with the lower surface glaucous.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp
maple</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>red maple. See
<er>Maple</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp oak</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>a name given to several kinds of oak which grow in swampy places, as
swamp Spanish oak (<spn>Quercus palustris</spn>), swamp white oak
(<spn>Quercus bicolor</spn>), swamp post oak (<spn>Quercus
lyrata</spn>).</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp ore</b></col><cd>
<fld>(Min.)</fld>, bog ore; limonite.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp
partridge</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of several
Australian game birds of the genera <gen>Synoicus</gen> and
<gen>Excalfatoria</gen>, allied to the European partridges.</cd> --
<col><b>Swamp robin</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the chewink.</cd>
-- <col><b>Swamp sassafras</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small
North American tree of the genus <gen>Magnolia</gen> (<spn>Magnolia
glauca</spn>) with aromatic leaves and fragrant creamy-white blossoms;
-- called also <altname>sweet bay</altname>.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp
sparrow</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a common North American
sparrow (<spn>Melospiza Georgiana</spn>, or <spn>Melospiza
palustris</spn>), closely resembling the song sparrow. It lives in low,
swampy places.</cd> -- <col><b>Swamp willow</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>See <cref>Pussy willow</cref>, under <er>Pussy</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1456 --></p>

<p><ent>Swamp</ent><br/
<hw>Swamp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swamped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swamping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To plunge or sink
into a swamp.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To cause (a boat) to become
filled with water; to capsize or sink by whelming with water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Fig.: To plunge into difficulties and perils; to
overwhelm; to ruin; to wreck.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Whig majority of the house of Lords was <qex>swamped</qex> by
the creation of twelve Tory peers.</q> <rj><qau>J. R.
Green.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Having <qex>swamped</qex> himself in following the ignis fatuus of
a theory.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swamp</ent><br/
<hw>Swamp</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To sink or stick in a
swamp; figuratively, to become involved in insuperable
difficulties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To become filled with water, as a boat; to founder;
to capsize or sink; figuratively, to be ruined; to be wrecked.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swampy</ent><br/
<hw>Swamp"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Consisting of swamp;
like a swamp; low, wet, and spongy; <as>as, <ex>swampy</ex>
land</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swan</ent><br/
<hw>Swan</hw> <pr>(sw<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>swan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zwaan</ets>, OHG. <ets>swan</ets>, G.
<ets>schwan</ets>, Icel. <ets>svanr</ets>, Sw. <ets>svan</ets>, Dan.
<ets>svane</ets>; and perhaps to E. <ets>sound</ets> something
audible.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous
species of large aquatic birds belonging to <gen>Cygnus</gen>,
<gen>Olor</gen>, and allied genera of the subfamily <fam>Cygninae</fam>.
They have a large and strong beak and a long neck, and are noted for
their graceful movements when swimming. Most of the northern species are
white. In literature the swan was fabled to sing a melodious song,
especially at the time of its death.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The European white, or mute, swan (<spn>Cygnus
gibbus</spn>), which is most commonly domesticated, bends its neck in an
S-shaped curve. The whistling, or trumpeting, swans of the genus
<gen>Olor</gen> do not bend the neck in an S-shaped curve, and are noted
for their loud and sonorous cry, due to complex convolutions of the
windpipe. To this genus belong the European whooper, or whistling swan
(<spn>Olor cygnus</spn>), the American whistling swan (<spn>Olor
Columbianus</spn>), and the trumpeter swan (<spn>Olor buccinator</spn>).
The Australian black swan (<spn>Chenopis atrata</spn>) is dull black
with white on the wings, and has the bill carmine, crossed with a white
band. It is a very graceful species and is often domesticated. The South
American black-necked swan (<spn>Sthenelides melancorypha</spn>) is a
very beautiful and graceful species, entirely white, except the head and
neck, which are dark velvety seal-brown. Its bill has a double bright
rose-colored knob.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: An appellation for a sweet singer, or a poet
noted for grace and melody; <as>as Shakespeare is called the
<ex>swan</ex> of Avon</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>The constellation
Cygnus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swan goose</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a bird of
India (<spn>Cygnopsis cygnoides</spn>) resembling both the swan and the
goose.</cd> -- <col><b>Swan shot</b></col>, <cd>a large size of shot
used in fowling.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swang</ent><br/
<hw>Swang</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Swing</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swang</ent><br/
<hw>Swang</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Swamp</er>.]</ety> <def>A
swamp.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swanherd</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"herd`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who tends or
marks swans; <as>as, the royal <ex>swanherd</ex> of
England</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swan-hopping</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"-hop`ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A corruption of
<er>Swan-upping</er>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Encyc.
Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swanimote</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"i*mote</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Eng. Forest
Law)</fld> <def>See <er>Swainmote</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swanky</ent><br/
<ent>Swankie</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Swank"ie</hw>, <hw>Swank"y</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>schwank</ets> flexible, pliant.]</ety>
<def>An active and clever young fellow.</def> <mark>[Scot.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swanlike</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Resembling a
swan.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swanmark</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"mark`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A mark of ownership
cut on the bill or swan.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Encyc.
Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swannery</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"ner*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A place where swans
are bred.</def> <ldquo/The largest <xex>swannery</xex> in
England.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Encyc. Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swanny</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Swanlike; <as>as, a
<ex>swanny</ex> glossiness of the neck</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Richardson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swanpan</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"pan</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Schwanpan</er>.]</ety> <def>The Chinese abacus; a
schwanpan.</def><-- also, suan-pan --> <rj><au>S. W.
Williams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swans-down</ent><br/
<ent>Swan's-down</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Swan's"-down`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <it>or</it>
<hw>Swans"-down`</hw> }</mhw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The down,
or fine, soft feathers, of the swan, used on various articles of
dress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A fine, soft, thick cloth of wool mixed with silk or
cotton; a sort of twilled fustian, like moleskin.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swan's-down cotton</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Cotton
flannel</cref>, under <er>Cotton</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swanskin</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"skin`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act
of a swan with the down or the feathers on.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A species of soft flannel, thick and warm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swan-upping</ent><br/
<hw>Swan"-up`ping</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A yearly
expedition on the Thames to take up young swans and mark them, as by
Companies of Dyers and Vintners; -- called also
<altname>swan-hopping</altname>.</def> <mark>[Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Encyc. Brit.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swap</ent><br/
<hw>Swap</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swapped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swapping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swappen</ets> to
strike; cf. E. to <ets>strike</ets> a bargain; perh. akin to E.
<ets>sweep</ets>. Cf. <er>Swap</er> a blow, <er>Swap</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <altsp>[Written also <asp>swop</asp>.]</altsp>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike; -- with <xex>off</xex>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Swap</xex> off his head!<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exchange (usually two things of the same kind);
to swap.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Miss
Edgeworth.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swap</ent><br/
<hw>Swap</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Swap</er>, <pos>v.
t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fall or descend; to rush hastily or
violently.</def> <rj><au>C. Richardson (Dict.).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All suddenly she <qex>swapt</qex> adown to ground.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To beat the air, or ply the wings, with a sweeping
motion or noise; to flap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swap</ent><br/
<hw>Swap</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>schwapp</ets>, n., a
slap, swap, <ets>schwapp</ets>, <ets>schwapps</ets>, interj., slap!
smack! and E. <ets>swap</ets>, v.t.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A blow; a
stroke.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An exchange; a barter.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swap</ent><br/
<hw>Swap</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Swap</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>Hastily.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swape</ent><br/
<hw>Swape</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Sweep</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 12.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sward</ent><br/
<hw>Sward</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>sweard</ets>
skin, covering; akin to OFries. <ets>swarge</ets>, D. <ets>zwoord</ets>,
G. <ets>schwarte</ets>, Icel. <ets>sv<oum/r<?/r</ets> skin, sward of the
earth.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Skin; covering.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The grassy surface of land; that part of the soil
which is filled with the roots of grass; turf.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sward</qex> was trim as any garden lawn.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sward pork</b></col>, <cd>bacon in large fitches.</cd>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sward</ent><br/
<hw>Sward</hw>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Swarded</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swarding</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To produce sward upon; to cover,
or be covered, with sward.</def> <rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sward-cutter</ent><br/
<hw>Sward"-cut`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A
plow for turning up grass land.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A lawn
mower.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarded</ent><br/
<hw>Sward"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered with sward.</def>
<rj><au>Mrs. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swardy</ent><br/
<hw>Sward"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Covered with sward or
grass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sware</ent><br/
<hw>Sware</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Swear</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Poetic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Cophetua <qex>sware</qex> a royal oath.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarf</ent><br/
<hw>Swarf</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Swerve</er>.]</ety> <def>To grow languid; to faint.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark> <ldquo/To <xex>swarf</xex> for very hunger.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarf</ent><br/
<hw>Swarf</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Swerve</er>.]</ety> <def>The
grit worn away from grindstones in grinding cutlery wet.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarm</ent><br/
<hw>Swarm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Swerve</er>.]</ety> <def>To climb a tree, pole, or the like, by
embracing it with the arms and legs alternately. See
<er>Shin</er>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>At the top was placed a piece of money, as a prize for those who
could <qex>swarm</qex> up and seize it.</q> <rj><qau>W.
Coxe.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarm</ent><br/
<hw>Swarm</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>swarm</ets>, AS.
<ets>swearm</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zwerm</ets>, G. <ets>schwarm</ets>,
OHG. <ets>swaram</ets>, Icel. <ets>svarmr</ets> a tumult, Sw.
<ets>sv<aum/rm</ets> a swarm, Dan. <ets>svaerm</ets>, and G.
<ets>schwirren</ets> to whiz, to buzz, Skr. <ets>svar</ets> to sound,
and perhaps to E. <ets>swear</ets>. <root/177. Cf. <er>Swerve</er>,
<er>Swirl</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A large number or mass of small
animals or insects, especially when in motion.</def> <ldquo/A deadly
<xex>swarm</xex> of hornets.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Especially, a great number of honeybees which
emigrate from a hive at once, and seek new lodgings under the direction
of a queen; a like body of bees settled permanently in a hive.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>swarm</xex> of bees.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Hence, any great number or multitude, as of people
in motion, or sometimes of inanimate objects; <as>as, a <ex>swarm</ex>
of meteorites</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Those prodigious <qex>swarms</qex> that had settled themselves in
every part of it [Italy].</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Multitude; crowd; throng.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarm</ent><br/
<hw>Swarm</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Swarmed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swarming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To collect, and
depart from a hive by flight in a body; -- said of bees; <as>as, bees
<ex>swarm</ex> in warm, clear days in summer</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To appear or collect in a crowd; to throng together;
to congregate in a multitude.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be crowded; to be thronged with a multitude of
beings in motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Every place <qex>swarms</qex> with soldiers.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To abound; to be filled (with).</def>
<rj><au>Atterbury.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To breed multitudes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Not so thick <qex>swarmed</qex> once the soil<br/
Bedropped with blood of Gorgon.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarm</ent><br/
<hw>Swarm</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To crowd or throng.</def>
<rj><au>Fanshawe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarmspore</ent><br/
<hw>Swarm"spore`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>One of innumerable minute, motile, reproductive
bodies, produced asexually by certain algae and fungi; a
zoospore.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the minute flagellate
germs produced by the sporulation of a protozoan; -- called also
<altname>zoospore</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swart</ent><br/
<hw>Swart</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sward.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swart</ent><br/
<hw>Swart</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>swart</ets>,
AS. <ets>sweart</ets> black; akin to OFries, OS. & LG. <ets>swart</ets>,
D. <ets>zwart</ets>, G. <ets>schwartz</ets>, OHG. <ets>swarz</ets>,
Icel. <ets>svarir</ets>, Sw. <ets>svart</ets>, Dan. <ets>sort</ets>,
Goth. <ets>swarts</ets>; cf. L. <ets>sordes</ets> dirt,
<ets>sordere</ets> to be dirty. Cf. <er>Sordid</er>,
<er>Surd</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of a dark hue; moderately black;
swarthy; tawny.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Swart</xex> attendants.<rdquo/
<au>Trench.</au> <ldquo/<xex>Swart</xex> savage maids.<rdquo/
<au>Hawthorne.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A nation strange, with visage <qex>swart</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Gloomy; malignant.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swart star</b></col>, <cd>the Dog Star; -- so called from
its appearing during the hot weather of summer, which makes swart the
countenance.</cd> <mark>[R.]</mark> <au>Milton.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swart</ent><br/
<hw>Swart</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make swart or tawny; <as>as, to
<ex>swart</ex> a living part</as>.</def> <rj><au>Sir T.
Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swartback</ent><br/
<hw>Swart"back`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The black-backed gull (<spn>Larus marinus</spn>); -- called also
<altname>swarbie</altname>.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarth</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Swart; swarthy.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>swarth</xex> complexion.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarth</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An apparition of a
person about to die; a wraith.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Grose.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarth</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sward</er>.]</ety>
<def>Sward; short grass.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Grassy <qex>swarth</qex>, close cropped by nibbling sheep.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarth</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Swath</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarthily</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a swarthy
manner; with a tawny hue; duskily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarthiness</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
swarthy; a dusky or dark complexion; tawniness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarthness</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Swarthiness.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. R. Clerke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarthy</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Swarthier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>superl.</pos> <adjf>Swarthiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[See
<er>Swart</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Being of a dark hue or dusky
complexion; tawny; swart; <as>as, <ex>swarthy</ex> faces</as>.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>swarthy</xex> Ethiope.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their <qex>swarthy</qex> hosts would darken all our plains.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarthy</ent><br/
<hw>Swarth"y</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To make swarthy.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Cowley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swartiness</ent><br/
<hw>Swart"i*ness</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Swarthiness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swartish</ent><br/
<hw>Swart"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat swart, dark, or
tawny.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swartness</ent><br/
<hw>Swart"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
swart.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarty</ent><br/
<hw>Swart"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Swarthy; tawny.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Burton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swarve</ent><br/
<hw>Swarve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Swerve</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To swerve.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser. Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To climb.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swash</ent><br/
<hw>Swash</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Swash</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>, <er>Squash</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>An oval figure, whose moldings are oblique to
the axis of the work.</def> <rj><au>Moxon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swash plate</b></col> <fld>(Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a revolving
circular plate, set obliquely on its shaft, and acting as a cam to give
a reciprocating motion to a rod in a direction parallel to the
shaft.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swash</ent><br/
<hw>Swash</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Swash</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>, <er>Squash</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <def>Soft, like fruit
too ripe; swashy.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Pegge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swash</ent><br/
<hw>Swash</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Swashed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swashing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Probably of imitative origin;
cf. Sw. <ets>svasska</ets> to splash, and, for sense 3, Sw.
<ets>svassa</ets> to bully, to rodomontade.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
dash or flow noisily, as water; to splash; <as>as, water
<ex>swashing</ex> on a shallow place</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To fall violently or noisily.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To bluster; to make a great noise; to vapor or
brag.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swash</ent><br/
<hw>Swash</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Impulse of water flowing
with violence; a dashing or splashing of water.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A narrow sound or channel of water lying within a
sand bank, or between a sand bank and the shore, or a bar over which the
sea washes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Liquid filth; wash; hog mash.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A blustering noise; a swaggering behavior.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A swaggering fellow; a swasher.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swashbuckler</ent><br/
<hw>Swash"buc`kler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bully or
braggadocio; a swaggering, boastful fellow; a swaggerer.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swasher</ent><br/
<hw>Swash"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who makes a
blustering show of valor or force of arms.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swashing</ent><br/
<hw>Swash"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Swaggering;
hectoring.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>swashing</xex> and martial
outside.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Resounding; crushing.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Swashing</xex> blow.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swashway</ent><br/
<hw>Swash"way`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as 4th
<er>Swash</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swashy</ent><br/
<hw>Swash"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Soft, like fruit that
is too ripe; quashy; swash.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swastica</ent><br/
<ent>Swastika</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Swas"ti*ka</hw>, <hw>Swas"ti*ca</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Also <asp>suastica</asp>, <asp>svastika</asp>,
etc.]</altsp> <ety>[Skr. <ets>svastika</ets>, fr. <ets>svasti</ets>
walfare; <ets>su</ets> well + <ets>asti</ets> being.]</ety> <def>A
symbol or ornament in the form of a Greek cross with the ends of the
arms at right angles all in the same direction, and each prolonged to
the height of the parallel arm of the cross. A great many modified forms
exist, ogee and volute as well as rectilinear, while various decorative
designs, as Greek fret or meander, are derived from or closely
associated with it. The swastika is found in remains from the Bronze Age
in various parts of Europe, esp. at Hissarlik (Troy), and was in
frequent use as late as the 10th century. It is found in ancient Persia,
in India, where both Jains and Buddhists used (or still use) it as
religious symbol, in China and Japan, and among Indian tribes of North,
Central, and South America. It is usually thought to be a charm,
talisman, or religious token, esp. a sign of good luck or benediction.
<person>Max M<uum/ller</person> distinguished from the
<ex>swastika</ex>, with arms prolonged to the right, the
<contr>suavastika</contr>, with arms prolonged to the left, but this
distinction is not commonly recognized. Other names for the swastika are
<altname>fylfot</altname> and <altname>gammadion</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p> <note>The <ex>swastika</ex> with arms bent to the right came to be
used as a symbol of Aryan supremacy by the Nazi party in Germany, 1933 -
1945; hence, it is now associated in the United States and European
countries with Nazism or antisemitism. It is sometimes used by
neo-nazis, or by antisemites as an antisemitic symbol.</note><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swat</ent><br/
<hw>Swat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Sweat</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swat</ent><br/
<hw>Swat</hw> <pr>(sw<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>to
hit, especially with an instrument in the hand; to smack.</def></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>to hit (a baseball)
powerfully, and for a long distance.</def></p>

<p><ent>Swat</ent><br/
<hw>Swat</hw> <pr>(sw<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <altsp>[Also spelled
<asp>swot</asp>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>a sharp blow, especially one
made with an instrument in the hand.</def></p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Baseball)</fld> <def>a powerful hit, especially a
home run.</def></p>

<p><ent>Swat</ent><br/
<hw>Swat</hw> <pr>(sw<ocr/t)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a former state in
Northwest India, now a part of Pakistan. It was ruled by a prince called
a <it>Wali</it>.</def></p>

<p><ent>Swat team</ent><br/
<ent>SWAT team</ent><br/
<mhw><hw>SWAT" team`</hw>, <hw>Swat" team`</hw></mhw> <pr>(sw<ocr/t"
t<emac/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[acronym: <ets>S</ets>pecial
<ets>W</ets>eapons <ets>a</ets>nd <ets>T</ets>raining.]</ety> <def>a
special group of police trained to deal with unusually dangerous or
violent situations, and having special weapons, such as rifles more
powerful than those carried by regular police officers. They are
employed, for example, in situations when hostages are being held, or
heavily armed persons need to be captured. Called also
<altname>SWAT</altname> or <altname>S.W.A.T.</altname>.</def></p>

<p><ent>Swatch</ent><br/
<hw>Swatch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
swath.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Tusser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A piece, pattern, or sample, generally of
cloth.</def> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au> <au>Jamieson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swate</ent><br/
<hw>Swate</hw> <pr>(sw<amac/t)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark>
<def><pos>imp.</pos> of <er>Sweat</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swath</ent><br/
<hw>Swath</hw> <pr>(sw<add/th; 277)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>swa<edh/u</ets> a track, trace; akin to D. <ets>zwaad</ets>,
<ets>zwad</ets>, <ets>zwade</ets>, a swath of grass, G.
<ets>schwad</ets>, <ets>schwaden</ets>; perhaps, originally, a shred.
Cf. <er>Swathe</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A line of
grass or grain cut and thrown together by the scythe in mowing or
cradling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The whole sweep of a scythe, or the whole breadth
from which grass or grain is cut by a scythe or a machine, in mowing or
cradling; <as>as, to cut a wide <ex>swath</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A band or fillet; a swathe.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swath bank</b></col>, <cd>a row of new-mown grass.</cd>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swathe</ent><br/
<hw>Swathe</hw> <pr>(sw<amac/<th/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Swathed</conjf>
<pr>(sw<amac/<th/d)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swathing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swathen</ets>, AS.
<ets>swe<edh/ain</ets>. See <er>Swath</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Swaddle</er>.]</ety> <def>To bind with a swathe, band, bandage, or
rollers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their children are never <qex>swathed</qex> or bound about with
any thing when they are first born.</q> <rj><qau>Abp.
Abbot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swathe</ent><br/
<hw>Swathe</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A bandage; a band; a
swath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wrapped me in above an hundred yards of <qex>swathe</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Milk and a <qex>swathe</qex>, at first, his whole demand.</q>
<rj><qau>Young.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The solemn glory of the afternoon, with its long
<qex>swathes</qex> of light between the far off rows of limes.</q>
<rj><qau>G. Eliot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swather</ent><br/
<hw>Swath"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Swath</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Agric.)</fld> <def>A device attached to a
mowing machine for raising the uncut fallen grain and marking the limit
of the swath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swatte</ent><br/
<hw>Swat"te</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Sweat</er>.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sway</ent><br/
<hw>Sway</hw> <pr>(sw<amac/)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Swayed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Swaying</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>sweyen</ets>,
Icel. <ets>sveigja</ets>, akin to E. <ets>swing</ets>; cf. D.
<ets>zwaaijen</ets> to wield, swing. See <er>Swing</er>, and cf.
<er>Swag</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To move or wield
with the hand; to swing; to wield; <as>as, to <ex>sway</ex> the
scepter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As sparkles from the anvil rise,<br/
When heavy hammers on the wedge are <qex>swayed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To influence or direct by power and authority; by
persuasion, or by moral force; to rule; to govern; to guide.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The will of man is by his reason <qex>swayed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She could not <qex>sway</qex> her house.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This was the race<br/
To <qex>sway</qex> the world, and land and sea subdue.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To cause to incline or swing to one side, or
backward and forward; to bias; to turn; to bend; warp; <as>as, reeds
<ex>swayed</ex> by wind; judgment <ex>swayed</ex> by
passion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As bowls run true by being made<br/
On purpose false, and to be <qex>swayed</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Hudibras.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Let not temporal and little advantages <qex>sway</qex> you against
a more durable interest.</q> <rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To hoist; <as>as, to
<ex>sway</ex> up the yards</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- To bias; rule; govern; direct; influence; swing;
move; wave; wield.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1457 --></p>

<p><ent>Sway</ent><br/
<hw>Sway</hw> <pr>(sw<amac/)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
be drawn to one side by weight or influence; to lean; to
incline.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The balance <qex>sways</qex> on our part.</q>
<rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move or swing from side to side; or backward and
forward.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To have weight or influence.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The example of sundry churches . . . doth <qex>sway</qex>
much.</q> <rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To bear sway; to rule; to govern.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Hadst thou <qex>swayed</qex> as kings should do.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sway</ent><br/
<hw>Sway</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of swaying; a
swaying motion; the swing or sweep of a weapon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With huge two-handed <qex>sway</qex> brandished aloft.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Influence, weight, or authority that inclines to one
side; <as>as, the <ex>sway</ex> of desires</as>.</def> <rj><au>A.
Tucker.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Preponderance; turn or cast of balance.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Expert<br/
When to advance, or stand, or turn the <qex>sway</qex><br/
Of battle.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Rule; dominion; control.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>When vice prevails, and impious men bear <qex>sway</qex>,<br/
The post of honor is a private station.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A switch or rod used by thatchers to bind their
work.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Rule; dominion; power; empire; control;
influence; direction; preponderance; ascendency.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sway-backed</ent><br/
<hw>Sway"-backed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the back
hollow or sagged, whether naturally or as the result of injury or
weakness; -- said of horses and other animals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sway bar</ent><br/
<hw>Sway bar</hw>. <fld>(Vehicles)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A bar
attached to the hounds, in the rear of the front axle, so as to slide on
the reach as the axle is swung in turning the vehicle.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Either of the two bars used on coupling the front and
rear sleds of a logging sled; also, the bar used to couple two logging
cars.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sway-bracing</ent><br/
<hw>Sway"-bra`cing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Engin.)</fld>
<def>The horizontal bracing of a bridge, which prevents its
swaying.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swayed</ent><br/
<hw>Swayed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Bent down, and hollow
in the back; sway-backed; -- said of a horse.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swayful</ent><br/
<hw>Sway"ful</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Able to sway.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Rush.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swaying</ent><br/
<hw>Sway"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An injury caused by violent
strains or by overloading; -- said of the backs of horses.</def>
<rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweal</ent><br/
<hw>Sweal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swealed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swealing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swelen</ets> to burn,
AS. <ets>swelan</ets>; akin to G. <ets>schwelen</ets> to burn slowly,
<ets>schw<uum/l</ets> sultry, Icel. <ets>svaela</ets> a thick
smoke.]</ety> <def>To melt and run down, as the tallow of a candle; to
waste away without feeding the flame.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>swale</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Sir W. Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweal</ent><br/
<hw>Sweal</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To singe; to scorch; to swale;
<as>as, to <ex>sweal</ex> a pig by singeing off the hair</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swear</ent><br/
<hw>Swear</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Swore</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, formerly <conjf>Sware</conjf>
<pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sworn</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>;
<pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Swearing</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>swerien</ets>, AS. <ets>swerian</ets>; akin to D.
<ets>zweren</ets>, OS. <ets>swerian</ets>, OHG. <ets>swerien</ets>, G.
<ets>schw<oum/ren</ets>, Icel. <ets>sverja</ets>, Sw.
<ets>sv<aum/rja</ets>, Dan. <ets>svaerge</ets>, Icel. & Sw.
<ets>svara</ets> to answer, Dan. <ets>svare</ets>, Dan. & Sw.
<ets>svar</ets> an answer, Goth. <ets>swaran</ets> to swear, and perhaps
to E. <ets>swarm</ets>. <root/177. Cf. <er>Answer</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To affirm or utter a solemn declaration, with an appeal
to God for the truth of what is affirmed; to make a promise, threat, or
resolve on oath; also, to affirm solemnly by some sacred object, or one
regarded as sacred, as the Bible, the Koran, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye shall <qex>swear</qex> by my name falsely.</q> <rj><qau>Lev.
xix. 12.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>swear</qex> by all the Roman gods.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To give evidence on oath; <as>as,
to <ex>swear</ex> to the truth of a statement; he <ex>swore</ex> against
the prisoner</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make an appeal to God in an irreverant manner; to
use the name of God or sacred things profanely; to call upon God in
imprecation; to curse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[I] <qex>swore</qex> little; diced not above seven times a
week.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To swear by</b></col>, <cd>to place great confidence in a
person or thing; to trust implicitly as an authority.</cd> <ldquo/I
simply meant to ask if you are one of those who <xex>swear by</xex> Lord
Verulam.<rdquo/ <au>Miss Edgeworth.</au> -- <col><b>To swear
off</b></col>, <cd>to make a solemn vow, or a serious resolution, to
abstain from something; <as>as, <ex>to swear off</ex> smoking</as>.</cd>
<mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swear</ent><br/
<hw>Swear</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To utter or affirm
with a solemn appeal to God for the truth of the declaration; to make (a
promise, threat, or resolve) under oath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Swear</qex> unto me here by God, that thou wilt not deal
falsely with me.</q> <rj><qau>Gen. xxi. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>swore</qex> consent to your succession.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>To put to an oath; to cause to take
an oath; to administer an oath to; -- often followed by <xex>in</xex> or
<xex>into</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>swear</ex> witnesses; to <ex>swear</ex>
a jury; to <ex>swear</ex> in an officer; he was <ex>sworn</ex> into
office</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To declare or charge upon oath; <as>as, he
<ex>swore</ex> treason against his friend</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To appeal to by an oath.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now, by Apollo, king,<br/
Thou <qex>swear'st</qex> thy gods in vain.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To swear the peace against one</b></col>, <cd>to make
oath that one is under the actual fear of death or bodily harm from the
person, in which case the person must find sureties that he will keep
the peace.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swearer</ent><br/
<hw>Swear"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
swears; one who calls God to witness for the truth of his
declaration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A profane person; one who uses profane
language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Then the liars and <qex>swearers</qex> are fools.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swearing</ent><br/
<hw>Swear"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Swear</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Idle <qex>swearing</qex> is a cursedness.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweat</ent><br/
<hw>Sweat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sweat</conjf> or <conjf>Sweated</conjf>
(<mark>Obs.</mark> <conjf>Swat</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>); <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Sweating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sweten</ets>, AS. <ets>swaetan</ets>, fr. <ets>sw<amac/t</ets>, n.,
sweat; akin to OFries. & OS. <ets>sw<emac/t</ets>, D. <ets>zweet</ets>,
OHG. <ets>sweiz</ets>, G. <ets>schweiss</ets>, Icel. <ets>sviti</ets>,
<ets>sveiti</ets>, Sw. <ets>svett</ets>, Dan. <ets>sved</ets>, L.
<ets>sudor</ets> sweat, <ets>sudare</ets> to sweat, Gr. <?/, <?/, sweat,
<?/ to sweat, Skr. <ets>sv<emac/da</ets> sweat, <ets>svid</ets> to
sweat. <root/178. Cf. <er>Exude</er>, <er>Sudary</er>,
<er>Sudorific</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To excrete sensible moisture
from the pores of the skin; to perspire.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: To perspire in toil; to work hard; to
drudge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He 'd have the poets <qex>sweat</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Waller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To emit moisture, as green plants in a
heap.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweat</ent><br/
<hw>Sweat</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to excrete
moisture from the skin; to cause to perspire; <as>as, his physicians
attempted to <ex>sweat</ex> him by most powerful
sudorifics</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To emit or suffer to flow from the pores; to
exude.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It made her not a drop for <qex>sweat</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With exercise she <qex>sweat</qex> ill humors out.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To unite by heating, after the application of
soldier.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To get something advantageous, as money, property,
or labor from (any one), by exaction or oppression; <as>as, to
<ex>sweat</ex> a spendthrift; to <ex>sweat</ex> laborers</as>.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To sweat coin</b></col>, <cd>to remove a portion of a
piece of coin, as by shaking it with others in a bag, so that the
friction wears off a small quantity of the metal.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The only use of it [money] which is interdicted is to put it in
circulation again after having diminished its weight by
<ldquo/<qex>sweating</qex><rdquo/, or otherwise, because the quantity of
metal contains is no longer consistent with its impression.</q>
<rj><qau>R. Cobden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweat</ent><br/
<hw>Sweat</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OE.
<ets>swot</ets>, AS. <ets>sw<amac/t</ets>. See <er>Sweat</er>, <pos>v.
i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>The fluid which
is excreted from the skin of an animal; the fluid secreted by the
sudoriferous glands; a transparent, colorless, acid liquid with a
peculiar odor, containing some fatty acids and mineral matter;
perspiration. See <er>Perspiration</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In the <qex>sweat</qex> of thy face shalt thou eat bread.</q>
<rj><qau>Gen. iii. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The act of sweating; or the state of one who sweats;
hence, labor; toil; drudgery.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Moisture issuing from any substance; <as>as, the
<ex>sweat</ex> of hay or grain in a mow or stack</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Mortimer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The sweating sickness.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Man.)</fld> <def>A short run by a race horse in
exercise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sweat box</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a small
closet in which refractory men are confined.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweat
glands</b></col> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>sudoriferous glands. See under
<er>Sudoriferous</er>.</cd></cs> <-- sweat suit. A suit comprising a top
and trousers, having full arms and legs, used while performing physical
exercises, esp. out-of-doors. Sweat equity. The rights to a portion of
ownership or profit, hypothetically owned by a worker who participated
in producing a product, such as in improving a piece of real estate.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweater</ent><br/
<hw>Sweat"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
sweats.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who, or that which, causes to sweat</def>; as:
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A sudorific.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A woolen jacket
or jersey worn by athletes.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>An employer who
oppresses his workmen by paying low wages.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<-- jersey worn by lots of people, not just athletes. Needs expansion.
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweatily</ent><br/
<hw>Sweat"i*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sweaty
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweatiness</ent><br/
<hw>Sweat"i*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Quality or state of being
sweaty.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweating</ent><br/
<hw>Sweat"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Sweat</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sweating bath</b></col>, <cd>a bath producing sensible
sweat; a stove or sudatory.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweating house</b></col>,
<cd>a house for sweating persons in sickness.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweating
iron</b></col>, <cd>a kind of knife, or a piece of iron, used to scrape
off sweat, especially from horses; a horse scraper.</cd> --
<col><b>Sweating room</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A room for sweating
persons.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <fld>(Dairying)</fld> <cd>A room for sweating
cheese and carrying off the superfluous juices.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweating
sickness</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a febrile epidemic disease
which prevailed in some countries of Europe, but particularly in
England, in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, characterized by
profuse sweating. Death often occured in a few hours.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweaty</ent><br/
<hw>Sweat"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sweatier</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sweatiest</adjf>.]</amorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Moist with sweat;
<as>as, a <ex>sweaty</ex> skin; a <ex>sweaty</ex>
garment.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting of sweat; of the nature of
sweat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>No noisome whiffs or <qex>sweaty</qex> streams.</q>
<rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Causing sweat; hence, laborious; toilsome;
difficult.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>sweaty</xex> forge.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Prior.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swede</ent><br/
<hw>Swede</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>Schwede</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A native or inhabitant of
Sweden.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Swedish turnip. See under
<er>Turnip</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swedenborgian</ent><br/
<hw>Swe`den*bor"gi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
holds the doctrines of the New Jerusalem church, as taught by Emanuel
<xex>Swedenborg</xex>, a Swedish philosopher and religious writer, who
was born <sc>a. d.</sc> 1688 and died 1772. Swedenborg claimed to have
intercourse with the spiritual world, through the opening of his
spiritual senses in 1745. He taught that the Lord Jesus Christ, as
comprehending in himself all the fullness of the Godhead, is the one
only God, and that there is a spiritual sense to the Scriptures, which
he (Swedenborg) was able to reveal, because he saw the correspondence
between natural and spiritual things.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swedenborgian</ent><br/
<hw>Swe`den*bor"gi*an</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
Swedenborg or his views.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swedenborgianism</ent><br/
<hw>Swe`den*bor"gi*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The
doctrines of the Swedenborgians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swedish</ent><br/
<hw>Swed"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schwedisch</ets>, Sw. <ets>svensk</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to Sweden or its inhabitants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swedish turnip</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Turnip</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swedish</ent><br/
<hw>Swed"ish</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The language of Swedes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweeny</ent><br/
<hw>Swee"ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>An
atrophy of the muscles of the shoulder in horses; also, atrophy of any
muscle in horses.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>swinney</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweep</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swept</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Sweeping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swepen</ets>; akin to
AS. <ets>sw<amac/pan</ets>. See <er>Swoop</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To pass a broom across (a surface) so as to remove
loose dirt, dust, etc.; to brush, or rub over, with a broom for the
purpose of cleaning; <as>as, to <ex>sweep</ex> a floor, the street, or a
chimney</as>. Used also figuratively.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I will <qex>sweep</qex> it with the besom of destruction.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xiv. 23.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To drive or carry along or off with a broom or a
brush, or as if with a broom; to remove by, or as if by, brushing;
<as>as, to <ex>sweep</ex> dirt from a floor; the wind <ex>sweeps</ex>
the snow from the hills; a freshet <ex>sweeps</ex> away a dam, timber,
or rubbish; a pestilence <ex>sweeps</ex> off multitudes.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The hail shall <qex>sweep</qex> away the refuge of lies.</q>
<rj><qau>Isa. xxviii. 17.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I have already <qex>swept</qex> the stakes.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To brush against or over; to rub lightly
along.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their long descending train,<br/
With rubies edged and sapphires, <qex>swept</qex> the plain.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To carry with a long, swinging, or dragging motion;
hence, to carry in a stately or proud fashion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And like a peacock <qex>sweep</qex> along his tail.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To strike with a long stroke.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Wake into voice each silent string,<br/
And <qex>sweep</qex> the sounding lyre.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To draw or drag something over;
<as>as, to <ex>sweep</ex> the bottom of a river with a
net</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To pass over, or traverse, with the eye or with an
instrument of observation; <as>as, to <ex>sweep</ex> the heavens with a
telescope</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To sweep a mold</b></col> <it>or</it> <col><b>To
sweep up a mold</b></col></mcol> <fld>(Founding)</fld>, <cd>to form the
sand into a mold by a templet, instead of compressing it around the
pattern.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweep</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To clean
rooms, yards, etc., or to clear away dust, dirt, litter, etc., with a
broom, brush, or the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To brush swiftly over the surface of anything; to
pass with switness and force, as if brushing the surface of anything; to
move in a stately manner; <as>as, the wind <ex>sweeps</ex> across the
plain; a woman <ex>sweeps</ex> through a drawing-room.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To pass over anything comprehensively; to range
through with rapidity; <as>as, his eye <ex>sweeps</ex> through
space</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweep</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
sweeping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The compass or range of a stroke; <as>as, a long
<ex>sweep</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The compass of any turning body or of any motion;
<as>as, the <ex>sweep</ex> of a door; the <ex>sweep</ex> of the
eye</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The compass of anything flowing or brushing; <as>as,
the flood carried away everything within its
<ex>sweep</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Violent and general destruction; <as>as, the
<ex>sweep</ex> of an epidemic disease</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Direction and extent of any motion not rectilinear;
<as>as, the <ex>sweep</ex> of a compass</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Direction or departure of a curve, a road, an arch,
or the like, away from a rectilinear line.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The road which makes a small <qex>sweep</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W.
Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>One who sweeps; a sweeper; specifically, a chimney
sweeper.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <fld>(Founding)</fld> <def>A movable templet for making
molds, in loam molding.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The mold of a ship
when she begins to curve in at the rungheads; any part of a ship shaped
in a segment of a circle.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A large oar used in
small vessels, partly to propel them and partly to steer them.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <fld>(Refining)</fld> <def>The almond furnace.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>12.</sn> <def>A long pole, or piece of timber, moved on a
horizontal fulcrum fixed to a tall post and used to raise and lower a
bucket in a well for drawing water.</def> <altsp>[Variously written
<asp>swape</asp>, <asp>sweep</asp>, <asp>swepe</asp>, and
<asp>swipe</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>13.</sn> <fld>(Card Playing)</fld> <def>In the game of casino, a
pairing or combining of all the cards on the board, and so removing them
all; in whist, the winning of all the tricks (thirteen) in a hand; a
slam.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>14.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>The sweeping of workshops where
precious metals are worked, containing filings, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sweep net</b></col>, <cd>a net for drawing over a large
compass.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweep of the tiller</b></col>
<fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a circular frame on which the tiller
traverses.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweepage</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"age</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The crop of hay got
in a meadow.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweeper</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, sweeps, or cleans by sweeping; a sweep; <as>as, a carpet
<ex>sweeper</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is oxygen which is the great <qex>sweeper</qex> of the
economy.</q> <rj><qau>Huxley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweeping</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Cleaning off surfaces, or
cleaning away dust, dirt, or litter, as a broom does; moving with
swiftness and force; carrying everything before it; including in its
scope many persons or things; <as>as, a <ex>sweeping</ex> flood; a
<ex>sweeping</ex> majority; a <ex>sweeping</ex> accusation.</as></def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Sweep"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos>
-<wf>Sweep"ing*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms> <-- should be
separate senses --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweepings</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"ings</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>Things
collected by sweeping; rubbish; <as>as, the <ex>sweepings</ex> of a
street</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweep-saw</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"-saw`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
bow-saw.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweepstake</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"stake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
winning of all the stakes or prizes.</def> <rj><au>Heylin.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A complete removal or carrying away; a clean
sweep.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hacket.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweepstakes</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"stakes`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
winning of all the stakes or prizes; a sweepstake.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pos>sing. <or/ pl.</pos> <def>The whole money or other
things staked at a horse race, a given sum being put up for each horse,
all of which goes to the winner, or is divided among several, as may be
previously agreed.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A race for all the sums staked or prizes
offered.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweepwasher</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"wash`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who extracts
the residuum of precious metals from the sweepings, potsherds, etc., of
refineries of gold and silver, or places where these metals are
used.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweepy</ent><br/
<hw>Sweep"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Moving with a sweeping
motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The branches bend before their <qex>sweepy</qex> away.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1458 --></p>

<p><ent>Sweet</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos>
<adjf>Sweeter</adjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Sweetest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swete</ets>,
<ets>swote</ets>, <ets>sote</ets>, AS. <ets>sw<emac/te</ets>; akin to
OFries. <ets>sw<emac/te</ets>, OS. <ets>sw<omac/ti</ets>, D.
<ets>zoet</ets>, G. <ets>s<uum/ss</ets>, OHG. <ets>suozi</ets>, Icel.
<ets>saetr</ets>, <ets>soetr</ets>, Sw. <ets>s<oum/t</ets>, Dan.
<ets>s<oum/d</ets>, Goth. <ets>suts</ets>, L. <ets>suavis</ets>, for
<ets>suadvis</ets>, Gr. <?/, Skr. <ets>sv<amac/du</ets> sweet,
<ets>svad</ets>, <ets>sv<amac/d</ets>, to sweeten. <root/175. Cf.
<er>Assuage</er>, <er>Suave</er>, <er>Suasion</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Having an agreeable taste or flavor such as that of sugar;
saccharine; -- opposed to <xex>sour</xex> and <xex>bitter</xex>; <as>as,
a <ex>sweet</ex> beverage; <ex>sweet</ex> fruits; <ex>sweet</ex>
oranges.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Pleasing to the smell; fragrant; redolent; balmy;
<as>as, a <ex>sweet</ex> rose; <ex>sweet</ex> odor; <ex>sweet</ex>
incense.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The breath of these flowers is <qex>sweet</qex> to me.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Pleasing to the ear; soft; melodious; harmonious;
<as>as, the <ex>sweet</ex> notes of a flute or an organ; <ex>sweet</ex>
music; a <ex>sweet</ex> voice; a <ex>sweet</ex> singer.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To make his English <qex>sweet</qex> upon his tongue.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A voice <qex>sweet</qex>, tremulous, but powerful.</q>
<rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Pleasing to the eye; beautiful; mild and attractive;
fair; <as>as, a <ex>sweet</ex> face; a <ex>sweet</ex> color or
complexion.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sweet</qex> interchange<br/
Of hill and valley, rivers, woods, and plains.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Fresh; not salt or brackish; <as>as, <ex>sweet</ex>
water</as>.</def> <rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Not changed from a sound or wholesome state.
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> Not sour; <as>as, <ex>sweet</ex> milk or
bread</as>. <sd>(b)</sd> Not state; not putrescent or putrid; not
rancid; <as>as, <ex>sweet</ex> butter; <ex>sweet</ex> meat or
fish</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>Pleasing to the mind; mild; gentle; calm; amiable;
winning; persuasive; <as>as, <ex>sweet</ex> manners</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Canst thou bind the <qex>sweet</qex> influence of Pleiades?</q>
<rj><qau>Job xxxviii. 31.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mildness and <qex>sweet</qex> reasonableness is the one
established rule of Christian working.</q> <rj><qau>M.
Arnold.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Sweet</xex> is often used in the formation of
self-explaining compounds; as, <xex>sweet</xex>-blossomed,
<xex>sweet</xex>-featured, <xex>sweet</xex>-smelling,
<xex>sweet</xex>-tempered, <xex>sweet</xex>-toned, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sweet alyssum</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Alyssum</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet apple</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Any apple of sweet flavor.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>See <er>Sweet-sop</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet
bay</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The laurel
(<spn>Laurus nobilis</spn>).</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>Swamp sassafras.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sweet calabash</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a plant of
the genus <gen>Passiflora</gen> (<spn>Passiflora maliformis</spn>)
growing in the West Indies, and producing a roundish, edible fruit, the
size of an apple.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet cicely</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>Either of the North American plants
of the umbelliferous genus <gen>Osmorrhiza</gen> having aromatic roots
and seeds, and white flowers.</cd> <au>Gray.</au> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A
plant of the genus <gen>Myrrhis</gen> (<spn>Myrrhis odorata</spn>)
growing in England.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Sweet calamus</b></col>,
<it>or</it> <col><b>Sweet cane</b></col></mcol>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<cd>Same as <cref>Sweet flag</cref>, below.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet
Cistus</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an evergreen shrub (<spn>Cistus
Ladanum</spn>) from which the gum ladanum is obtained.</cd> --
<col><b>Sweet clover</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See
<er>Melilot</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet coltsfoot</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind of butterbur (<spn>Petasites
sagittata</spn>) found in Western North America.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet
corn</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of the maize of a sweet
taste. See the Note under <er>Corn</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet
fern</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a small North American shrub
(<spn>Comptonia asplenifolia</spn> syn. <spn>Myrica asplenifolia</spn>)
having sweet-scented or aromatic leaves resembling fern leaves.</cd> --
<col><b>Sweet flag</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an endogenous plant
(<spn>Acorus Calamus</spn>) having long flaglike leaves and a rootstock
of a pungent aromatic taste. It is found in wet places in Europe and
America. See <er>Calamus</er>, 2.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet gale</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a shrub (<spn>Myrica Gale</spn>) having bitter
fragrant leaves; -- also called <altname>sweet willow</altname>, and
<altname>Dutch myrtle</altname>. See 5th <er>Gale</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sweet grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>holy, or Seneca,
grass.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet gum</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an
American tree (<spn>Liquidambar styraciflua</spn>). See
<er>Liquidambar</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet herbs</b></col>,
<cd>fragrant herbs cultivated for culinary purposes.</cd> --
<col><b>Sweet John</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a variety of the
sweet William.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet leaf</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>horse sugar. See under <er>Horse</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet
marjoram</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Marjoram</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sweet marten</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>the pine
marten.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet maudlin</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
composite plant (<spn>Achillea Ageratum</spn>) allied to milfoil.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sweet oil</b></col>, <cd>olive oil.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet
pea</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Pea</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sweet potato</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under
<er>Potato</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet rush</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>,
<cd>sweet flag.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet spirits of niter</b></col>
<fld>(Med. Chem.)</fld> <cd>See <cref>Spirit of nitrous ether</cref>,
under <er>Spirit</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sweet sultan</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>an annual composite plant (<spn>Centaurea
moschata</spn>), also, the yellow-flowered (<spn>Centaurea
odorata</spn>); -- called also <altname>sultan flower</altname>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sweet tooth</b></col>, <cd>an especial fondness for sweet things
or for sweetmeats.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> -- <col><b>Sweet
William</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>A species of pink
(<spn>Dianthus barbatus</spn>) of many varieties.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>The willow warbler.</cd> <sd>(c)</sd>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <cd>The European goldfinch; -- called also
<altname>sweet Billy</altname>.</cd> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark> --
<col><b>Sweet willow</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>sweet gale.</cd>
-- <col><b>Sweet wine</b></col>. <cd>See <cref>Dry wine</cref>, under
<er>Dry</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>To be sweet on</b></col>, <cd>to have a
particular fondness for, or special interest in, as a young man for a
young woman.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <au>Thackeray.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sugary; saccharine; dulcet; luscious.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweet</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That which
is sweet to the taste; -- used chiefly in the plural.</def>
Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Confectionery, sweetmeats, preserves,
etc.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Home-made wines, cordials, metheglin,
etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which is sweet or pleasant in odor; a
perfume.</def> <ldquo/A wilderness of <xex>sweets</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>That which is pleasing or grateful to the mind;
<as>as, the <ex>sweets</ex> of domestic life</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A little bitter mingled in our cup leaves no relish of the
<qex>sweet</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Locke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>One who is dear to another; a darling; -- a term of
endearment.</def> <ldquo/Wherefore frowns my <xex>sweet</xex>?<rdquo/
<rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweet</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Sweetly.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweet</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To sweeten.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Udall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetbread</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"bread`</hw> <pr>(sw<emac/t"br<ecr/d`)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Either the thymus gland or the pancreas, the former
being called <stype>neck sweetbread</stype> or <stype>throat
sweetbread</stype>, the latter <stype>belly sweetbread</stype>. The
sweetbreads of ruminants, esp. of the calf, are highly esteemed as food.
See <er>Pancreas</er>, and <er>Thymus</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The pancreas.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweet-breasted</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"-breast`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a
sweet, musical voice, as the nightingale. Cf. <er>Breast</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>, 6.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetbrier</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"bri`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A kind of rose (<spn>Rosa rubiginosa</spn>) with minutely glandular
and fragrant foliage. The small-flowered sweetbrier is <spn>Rosa
micrantha</spn>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweeten</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sweetened</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sweetening</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See <er>Sweet</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make sweet to the taste;
<as>as, to <ex>sweeten</ex> tea</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make pleasing or grateful to the mind or
feelings; <as>as, to <ex>sweeten</ex> life; to <ex>sweeten</ex>
friendship</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To make mild or kind; to soften; <as>as, to
<ex>sweeten</ex> the temper</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To make less painful or laborious; to relieve;
<as>as, to <ex>sweeten</ex> the cares of life</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>sweeten</qex> every secret tear.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To soften to the eye; to make delicate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Correggio has made his memory immortal by the strength he has
given to his figures, and by <qex>sweetening</qex> his lights and
shadows, and melting them into each other.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To make pure and salubrious by destroying noxious
matter; <as>as, to <ex>sweeten</ex> rooms or apartments that have been
infected; to <ex>sweeten</ex> the air.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To make warm and fertile; -- opposed to
<xex>sour</xex>; <as>as, to dry and <ex>sweeten</ex>
soils</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To restore to purity; to free from taint; <as>as, to
<ex>sweeten</ex> water, butter, or meat</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 9. To make more attractive; -- said of offers in negotiations,
as, to sweeten the deal by increasing the price offered. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweeten</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"en</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To become sweet.</def>
<rj><au>Bacon.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetener</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"en*er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who, or that
which, sweetens; one who palliates; that which moderates
acrimony.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetening</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"en*ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of making
sweet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That which sweetens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetheart</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"heart`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A lover of
mistress.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweethearting</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"heart`ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Making love.</def>
<ldquo/To play at <xex>sweethearting</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>W.
Black.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweeting</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sweet apple.</def>
<rj><au>Ascham.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A darling; -- a word of endearment.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetish</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Somewhat
sweet.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sweet"ish*ness</wf>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetly</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sw<emac/tlice</ets>.]</ety> <def>In a sweet manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetmeat</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"meat`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Fruit
preserved with sugar, as peaches, pears, melons, nuts, orange peel,
etc.; -- usually in the plural; a confect; a confection.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The paint used in making patent leather.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A boat shell (<spn>Crepidula
fornicata</spn>) of the American coast.</def> <mark>[Local,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetness</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sw<emac/tness</ets>.]</ety> <def>The quality or state of being
sweet (in any sense of the adjective); gratefulness to the taste or to
the smell; agreeableness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetroot</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"root`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Licorice.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweet-scented</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"-scent`ed</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having a sweet
scent or smell; fragrant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sweet-scented shrub</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a
shrub of the genus <gen>Calycanthus</gen>, the flowers of which, when
crushed, have a fragrance resembling that of strawberries.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweet-sop</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"-sop`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A kind of custard apple (<spn>Anona squamosa</spn>). See under
<er>Custard</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetwater</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"wa`ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A variety of white grape, having a sweet watery juice; -- also
called <altname>white sweetwater</altname>, and <altname>white
muscadine</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetweed</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"weed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A name for two tropical American weeds (<spn>Capraria
biflora</spn>, and <spn>Scoparia dulcis</spn>) of the Figwort
family.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetwood</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"wood`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The true laurel (<spn>Laurus nobilis</spn>.)</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The timber of the tree <spn>Oreodaphne
Leucoxylon</spn>, growing in Jamaica. The name is also applied to the
timber of several other related trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweetwort</ent><br/
<hw>Sweet"wort`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any plant of a
sweet taste.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweigh</ent><br/
<hw>Sweigh</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sway; movement.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweinmote</ent><br/
<hw>Swein"mote`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Swainmote</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swell</ent><br/
<hw>Swell</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Swelled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Swelled</conjf> or <conjf>Swollen</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p.
pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Swelling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS.
<ets>swellan</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zwellen</ets>, OS. & OHG.
<ets>swellan</ets>, G. <ets>schwellen</ets>, Icel. <ets>svella</ets>,
Sw. <ets>sv<aum/lla</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To grow larger; to
dilate or extend the exterior surface or dimensions, by matter added
within, or by expansion of the inclosed substance; <as>as, the legs
<ex>swell</ex> in dropsy; a bruised part <ex>swells</ex>; a bladder
<ex>swells</ex> by inflation.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To increase in size or extent by any addition; to
increase in volume or force; <as>as, a river <ex>swells</ex>, and
overflows its banks; sounds <ex>swell</ex> or diminish.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To rise or be driven into waves or billows; to
heave; <as>as, in tempest, the ocean <ex>swells</ex> into
waves</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To be puffed up or bloated; <as>as, to
<ex>swell</ex> with pride</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>You <qex>swell</qex> at the tartan, as the bull is said to do at
scarlet.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Scott.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To be inflated; to belly; <as>as, the sails
<ex>swell</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>To be turgid, bombastic, or extravagant; <as>as,
<ex>swelling</ex> words; a <ex>swelling</ex> style.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>7.</sn> <def>To protuberate; to bulge out; <as>as, a cask
<ex>swells</ex> in the middle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>8.</sn> <def>To be elated; to rise arrogantly.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Your equal mind yet <qex>swells</qex> not into state.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>9.</sn> <def>To grow upon the view; to become larger; to
expand.</def> <ldquo/Monarchs to behold the <xex>swelling</xex>
scene!<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>10.</sn> <def>To become larger in amount; <as>as, many little
debts added, <ex>swell</ex> to a great amount</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>11.</sn> <def>To act in a pompous, ostentatious, or arrogant
manner; to strut; to look big.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Here he comes, <qex>swelling</qex> like a turkey cock.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swell</ent><br/
<hw>Swell</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To increase the size,
bulk, or dimensions of; to cause to rise, dilate, or increase; <as>as,
rains and dissolving snow <ex>swell</ex> the rivers in spring;
immigration <ex>swells</ex> the population</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[The Church] <qex>swells</qex> her high, heart-cheering tone.</q>
<rj><qau>Keble.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To aggravate; to heighten.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It is low ebb with his accuser when such peccadilloes are put to
<qex>swell</qex> the charge.</q> <rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To raise to arrogance; to puff up; to inflate;
<as>as, to be <ex>swelled</ex> with pride or haughtiness</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To augment gradually in force or
loudness, as the sound of a note.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swell</ent><br/
<hw>Swell</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
swelling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Gradual increase.</def> Specifically: <sd>(a)</sd>
<def>Increase or augmentation in bulk; protuberance.</def> <sd>(b)</sd>
<def>Increase in height; elevation; rise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Little River affords navigation during a <qex>swell</qex> to
within three miles of the Miami.</q> <rj><qau>Jefferson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(c)</sd> <def>Increase of force, intensity, or volume of
sound.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Music arose with its voluptuous <qex>swell</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(d)</sd> <def>Increase of power in style, or of rhetorical
force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>swell</qex> and subsidence of his periods.</q>
<rj><qau>Landor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A gradual ascent, or rounded elevation, of land;
<as>as, an extensive plain abounding with little
<ex>swells</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A wave, or billow; especially, a succession of large
waves; the roll of the sea after a storm; <as>as, a heavy <ex>swell</ex>
sets into the harbor</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>swell</qex><br/
Of the long waves that roll in yonder bay.</q>
<rj><qau>Tennyson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The gigantic <qex>swells</qex> and billows of the snow.</q>
<rj><qau>Hawthorne.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A gradual increase and decrease of
the volume of sound; the crescendo and diminuendo combined; -- generally
indicated by the sign.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>A showy, dashing person; a dandy.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Ground swell</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Ground</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Organ swell</b></col>
<fld>(Mus.)</fld>, <cd>a certain number of pipes inclosed in a box, the
uncovering of which by means of a pedal produces increased sound.</cd>
-- <col><b>Swell shark</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a small shark
(<spn>Scyllium ventricosum</spn>) of the west coast of North America,
which takes in air when caught, and swells up like a
swellfish.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swell</ent><br/
<hw>Swell</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Having the characteristics of a
person of rank and importance; showy; dandified; distinguished; <as>as,
a <ex>swell</ex> person; a <ex>swell</ex> neighborhood.</as></def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swell mob</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Mob</er>.</cd>
<mark>[Slang]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelldom</ent><br/
<hw>Swell"dom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>People of rank and
fashion; the class of swells, collectively.</def>
<mark>[Jocose]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swellfish</ent><br/
<hw>Swell"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any plectognath fish that dilates itself, as the bur fish, puffer,
or diodon.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelling</ent><br/
<hw>Swell"ing</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of that which
swells; <as>as, the <ex>swelling</ex> of rivers in spring; the
<ex>swelling</ex> of the breast with pride.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Rise to the <qex>swelling</qex> of the voiceless sea.</q>
<rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A protuberance; a prominence</def>; especially
<fld>(Med.)</fld>, <def>an unnatural prominence or protuberance; <as>as,
a scrofulous <ex>swelling</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The superficies of such plates are not even, but have many
cavities and <qex>swellings</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Sir I.
Newton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swellish</ent><br/
<hw>Swell"ish</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Dandified; stylish.</def>
<mark>[Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelltoad</ent><br/
<hw>Swell"toad`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A swellfish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelt</ent><br/
<hw>Swelt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Swell</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelt</ent><br/
<hw>Swelt</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>swelten</ets> to die, to
swoon or faint, AS. <ets>sweltan</ets> to die; akin to OD.
<ets>swelten</ets> to hunger, to fail, OS. <ets>sweltan</ets> to die,
Icel. <ets>svelta</ets> to die, to hunger, Sw. <ets>sv<aum/lta</ets> to
hunger, Dan. <ets>sulte</ets>, Goth. <ets>sviltan</ets> to die. Cf.
<er>Swelter</er>, <er>Sweltry</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To die; to
perish.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To faint; to swoon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Night she <qex>swelt</qex> for passing joy.</q>
<rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelt</ent><br/
<hw>Swelt</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To overpower, as with heat; to
cause to faint; to swelter.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelter</ent><br/
<hw>Swel"ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Sweltered</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sweltering</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[From <er>Swelt</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To be overcome and faint with
heat; to be ready to perish with heat.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Sweltered</xex>
cattle.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To welter; to soak.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelter</ent><br/
<hw>Swel"ter</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To oppress with
heat.</def> <rj><au>Bentley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To exude, like sweat.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweltry</ent><br/
<hw>Swel"try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Swelter</er>, <er>Swelt</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, and cf.
<er>Sultry</er>.]</ety> <def>Suffocating with heat; oppressively hot;
sultry.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Evelyn.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swelve</ent><br/
<hw>Swel"ve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To swallow.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swept</ent><br/
<hw>Swept</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Sweep</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swerd</ent><br/
<hw>Swerd</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See <er>Sward</er>,
<pos>n. & v.</pos></def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swerd</ent><br/
<hw>Swerd</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Sword.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swerve</ent><br/
<hw>Swerve</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swerved</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swerving</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swerven</ets>, AS.
<ets>sweorfan</ets> to wipe off, to file, to polish; akin to OFries.
<ets>swerva</ets> to creep, D. <ets>zwerven</ets> to swerve, to rope,
OS. <ets>swerban</ets> to wipe off, MHG. <ets>swerben</ets> to be
whirled, OHG. <ets>swerban</ets> to wipe off, Icel. <ets>sverfa</ets> to
file, Goth. <ets>swa<iacute/rban</ets> (in comp.) to wipe, and perhaps
to E. <ets>swarm</ets>. Cf. <er>Swarm</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
stray; to wander; to rope.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A maid thitherward did run,<br/
To catch her sparrow which from her did <qex>swerve</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Sir P. Sidney.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To go out of a straight line; to deflect.</def>
<ldquo/The point [of the sword] <xex>swerved</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir
P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To wander from any line prescribed, or from a rule
or duty; to depart from what is established by law, duty, custom, or the
like; to deviate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I <qex>swerve</qex> not from thy commandments.</q> <rj><qau>Bk. of
Com. Prayer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They <qex>swerve</qex> from the strict letter of the law.</q>
<rj><qau>Clarendon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Many who, through the contagion of evil example, <qex>swerve</qex>
exceedingly from the rules of their holy religion.</q>
<rj><qau>Atterbury.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To bend; to incline.</def> <ldquo/The battle
<xex>swerved</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To climb or move upward by winding or
turning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The tree was high;<br/
Yet nimbly up from bough to bough I <qex>swerved</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swerve</ent><br/
<hw>Swerve</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To turn aside.</def>
<rj><au>Gauden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sweven</ent><br/
<hw>Swe"ven</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS. <ets>swefen</ets>
sleep, dream; akin to <ets>swebban</ets>, <ets>swefian</ets>, to put to
sleep, to kill. <root/176. See <er>Somnolent</er>.]</ety> <def>A vision
seen in sleep; a dream.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Wycliff (Acts
ii. 17).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I defy both <qex>sweven</qex> and dream.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swich</ent><br/
<hw>Swich</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Such</er>.]</ety> <def>Such.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Swich</qex> things as that I know I will declare.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swietenia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Swie*te"ni*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. Named
after Gerard Van <ets>Sweiten</ets>, physician to Maria Theresa of
Austria.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A genus of meliaceous trees
consisting of one species (<spn>Sweitenia Mahogoni</spn>), the mahogany
tree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swift</ent><br/
<hw>Swift</hw> <pr>(sw<icr/ft)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<amorph>[<pos>Compar.</pos> <adjf>Swifter</adjf>
<pr>(sw<icr/ft"<etil/r)</pr>; <pos>superl.</pos>
<adjf>Swiftest</adjf>.]</amorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>swift</ets>; akin to
<ets>sw<amac/pan</ets> to sweep, <ets>swipu</ets> a whip; cf.
<ets>sw<imac/fan</ets> to move quickly, to revolve. See <er>Swoop</er>,
<pos>v. i.</pos>, and cf. <er>Swivel</er>, <er>Squib</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Moving a great distance in a short time; moving with
celerity or velocity; fleet; rapid; quick; speedy; prompt.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My beloved brethren, let every man be <qex>swift</qex> to hear,
slow to speak, slow to wrath.</q> <rj><qau>James i. 19.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Swift</qex> of dispatch and easy of access.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And bring upon themselves <qex>swift</qex> destruction.</q>
<rj><qau>2 Pet. ii. 1.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of short continuance; passing away quickly.</def>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Swift</xex> is often used in the formation of
compounds which are generally self-explaining; as,
<xex>swift</xex>-darting, <xex>swift</xex>-footed,
<xex>swift</xex>-winged, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Quick; fleet; speedy; rapid;
expeditious.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swift</ent><br/
<hw>Swift</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Swiftly.</def> <mark>[Obs. or
Poetic]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ply <qex>swift</qex> and strong the oar.</q>
<rj><qau>Southey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swift</ent><br/
<hw>Swift</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The current of a
stream.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Walton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of
small, long-winged, insectivorous birds of the family
<fam>Micropodidae</fam>. In form and habits the swifts resemble
swallows, but they are destitute of complex vocal muscles and are not
singing birds, but belong to a widely different group allied to the
humming birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ The common European swift (<spn>Cypselus apus</spn> syn.
<spn>Micropus apus</spn>) nests in church steeples and under the tiles
of roofs, and is noted for its rapid flight and shrill screams. It is
called also <altname>black martin</altname>, <altname>black
swift</altname>, <altname>hawk swallow</altname>, <altname>devil
bird</altname>, <altname>swingdevil</altname>, <altname>screech
martin</altname>, and <altname>shriek owl</altname>. The common
American, or chimney, swift (<spn>Chaetura pelagica</spn>) has sharp
rigid tips to the tail feathers. It attaches its nest to the inner walls
of chimneys, and is called also <altname>chimney swallow</altname>. The
Australian swift (<spn>Chaetura caudacuta</spn>) also has sharp naked
tips to the tail quills. The European Alpine swift (<spn>Cypselus
melba</spn>) is whitish beneath, with a white band across the breast.
The common Indian swift is <spn>Cypselus affinis</spn>. See also
<cref>Palm swift</cref>, under <er>Palm</er>, and <cref>Tree
swift</cref>, under <er>Tree</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
lizards, as the pine lizard.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The ghost moth. See under
<er>Ghost</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <ety>[Cf. <er>Swivel</er>.]</ety> <def>A reel, or turning
instrument, for winding yarn, thread, etc.; -- used chiefly in the
plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>The main card cylinder of a flax-carding
machine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1459 --></p>

<p><ent>Swifter</ent><br/
<hw>Swift"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Swivel</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A rope used
to retain the bars of the capstan in their sockets while men are turning
it.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A rope used to encircle a boat
longitudinally, to strengthen and defend her sides.</def> <sd>(c)</sd>
<def>The forward shroud of a lower mast.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swifter</ent><br/
<hw>Swift"er</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To tighten,
as slack standing rigging, by bringing the opposite shrouds
nearer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiftfoot</ent><br/
<hw>Swift"foot`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Nimble;
fleet.</def> <rj><au>Mir. for Mag.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiftfoot</ent><br/
<hw>Swift"foot`</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
courser.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiftlet</ent><br/
<hw>Swift"let</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Any one of numerous species of small East Indian and Asiatic swifts
of the genus <gen>Collocalia</gen>. Some of the species are noted for
furnishing the edible bird's nest. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under
<er>Edible</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiftly</ent><br/
<hw>Swift"ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a swift manner; with quick
motion or velocity; fleetly.</def> <rj><au>Wyclif.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiftness</ent><br/
<hw>Swift"ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The quality or state of being
swift; speed; quickness; celerity; velocity; rapidity; <as>as, the
<ex>swiftness</ex> of a bird; the <ex>swiftness</ex> of a stream;
<ex>swiftness</ex> of descent in a falling body; <ex>swiftness</ex> of
thought, etc.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swig</ent><br/
<hw>Swig</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. D.
<ets>zwelgen</ets> to swallow, E. <ets>swallow</ets>, v.t.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To drink in long draughts; to gulp; <as>as, to
<ex>swig</ex> cider</as>.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To suck.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The lambkins <qex>swig</qex> the teat.</q>
<rj><qau>Creech.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swig</ent><br/
<hw>Swig</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A long draught.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Marryat.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>A tackle with ropes which are not
parallel.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A beverage consisting of warm beer flavored with
spices, lemon, etc.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swig</ent><br/
<hw>Swig</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Prov. E. <ets>swig</ets> to
leak out, AS. <ets>sw<imac/jian</ets> to be silent,
<ets>sw<imac/can</ets> to evade, escape.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
castrate, as a ram, by binding the testicles tightly with a string, so
that they mortify and slough off.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To pull upon (a tackle) by
throwing the weight of the body upon the fall between the block and a
cleat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swill</ent><br/
<hw>Swill</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swilled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swilling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swilen</ets> to wash,
AS. <ets>swilian</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To wash; to
drench.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>As fearfully as doth a galled rock<br/
O'erhang and jutty his confounded base,<br/
<qex>Swilled</qex> with the wild and wasteful ocean.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <ety>[Properly, to drink like a pig. See <er>Swill</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To drink in great draughts; to swallow
greedily.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Well-dressed people, of both sexes, . . . devouring sliced beef,
and <qex>swilling</qex> pork, and punch, and cider.</q>
<rj><qau>Smollett.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To inebriate; to fill with drink.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I should be loth<br/
To meet the rudeness and <qex>swilled</qex> insolence<br/
Of such late wassailers.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swill</ent><br/
<hw>Swill</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To drink greedily or swinishly; to
drink to excess.</def> <rj><au>South.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swill</ent><br/
<hw>Swill</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The wash, or mixture of
liquid substances, given to swine; hogwash; -- called also
<altname>swillings</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Large draughts of liquor; drink taken in excessive
quantities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiller</ent><br/
<hw>Swill"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
swills.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swillings</ent><br/
<hw>Swill"ings</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <def>See
<er>Swill</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swim</ent><br/
<hw>Swim</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos>
<conjf>Swam</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr> or <conjf>Swum</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>;
<pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Swum</conjf>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swimming</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>swimman</ets>; akin to
D. <ets>zwemmen</ets>, OHG. <ets>swimman</ets>, G. <ets>schwimmen</ets>,
Icel. <ets>svimma</ets>, Dan. <ets>sw<oum/mme</ets>, Sw.
<ets>simma</ets>. Cf. <er>Sound</er> an air bladder, a strait.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To be supported by water or other fluid; not to sink;
to float; <as>as, any substance will <ex>swim</ex>, whose specific
gravity is less than that of the fluid in which it is
immersed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move progressively in water by means of strokes
with the hands and feet, or the fins or the tail.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Leap in with me into this angry flood,<br/
And <qex>swim</qex> to yonder point.</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To be overflowed or drenched.</def> <rj><au>Ps. vi.
6.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sudden the ditches swell, the meadows <qex>swim</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Thomson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Fig.: To be as if borne or floating in a
fluid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[They] now <qex>swim</qex> in joy.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To be filled with swimming animals.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Streams] that <qex>swim</qex> full of small fishes.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swim</ent><br/
<hw>Swim</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To pass or move over or
on by swimming; <as>as, to <ex>swim</ex> a stream</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sometimes he thought to <qex>swim</qex> the stormy main.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause or compel to swim; to make to float;
<as>as, to <ex>swim</ex> a horse across a river</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To immerse in water that the lighter parts may
float; <as>as, to <ex>swim</ex> wheat in order to select
seed</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swim</ent><br/
<hw>Swim</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of swimming; a
gliding motion, like that of one swimming.</def> <rj><au>B.
Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The sound, or air bladder, of a fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A part of a stream much frequented by fish.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swim bladder</b></col>, <cd>an air bladder of a
fish.</cd> -- <col><b>To be in the swim</b></col>, <cd>to be in a
favored position; to be associated with others in active affairs.</cd>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swim</ent><br/
<hw>Swim</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[OE. <ets>swime</ets> dizziness,
vertigo, AS. <ets>sw<imac/ma</ets>; akin to D. <ets>zwijm</ets>, Icel.
<ets>svimi</ets> dizziness, <ets>svina</ets> to subside,
<ets>sv<imac/a</ets> to abate, G. <ets>schwindel</ets> dizziness,
<ets>schwinden</ets> to disappear, to dwindle, OHG.
<ets>sw<imac/nan</ets> to dwindle. Cf. <er>Squemish</er>,
<er>Swindler</er>.]</ety> <def>To be dizzy; to have an unsteady or
reeling sensation; <as>as, the head <ex>swims</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimbel</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"bel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <def>A moaning or sighing sound or noise; a
sough.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimmer</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"mer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
swims.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>A protuberance on the leg of a
horse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A swimming bird; one of the
natatores.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Little swimmer</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
phalarope.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimmeret</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"mer*et</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>One of a series of flat, fringed, and usually bilobed, appendages,
of which several pairs occur on the abdominal somites of many
crustaceans. They are used as fins in swimming.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimming</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"ming</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>That
swims; capable of swimming; adapted to, or used in, swimming; <as>as, a
<ex>swimming</ex> bird; a <ex>swimming</ex> motion</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Suffused with moisture; <as>as, <ex>swimming</ex>
eyes</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swimming bell</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
nectocalyx. See <xex>Illust.</xex> under <er>Siphonophora</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Swimming crab</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>any one of
numerous species of marine crabs, as those of the family
<fam>Protunidae</fam>, which have some of the joints of one or more
pairs of legs flattened so as to serve as fins.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimming</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"ming</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of one who
swims.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimming</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"ming</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From <er>Swim</er> to be
dizzy.]</ety> <def>Being in a state of vertigo or dizziness; <as>as, a
<ex>swimming</ex> brain</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimming</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"ming</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Vertigo; dizziness; <as>as, a
<ex>swimming</ex> in the head</as>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimmingly</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"ming*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In an easy, gliding manner,
as if swimming; smoothly; successfully; prosperously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swimmingness</ent><br/
<hw>Swim"ming*ness</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act or state of swimming;
suffusion.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>swimmingness</xex> in the eye.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Congreve.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinck</ent><br/
<hw>Swinck</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Swink</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swindle</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"dle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swindled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Swindling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Swindler</er>.]</ety> <def>To cheat defraud grossly, or with
deliberate artifice; <as>as, to <ex>swindle</ex> a man out of his
property</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Lammote . . . has <qex>swindled</qex> one of them out of three
hundred livres.</q> <rj><qau>Carlyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swindle</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"dle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or process of swindling; a
cheat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swindler</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"dler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[G.
<ets>schwindler</ets>, fr. <ets>schwindlen</ets> to be dizzy, to act
thoughtlessly, to cheat, fr. <ets>schwindel</ets> dizziness, fr.
<ets>schwinden</ets> to vanish, to disappear, to dwindle. See
<er>Swim</er> to be dizzy.]</ety> <def>One who swindles, or defrauds
grossly; one who makes a practice of defrauding others by imposition or
deliberate artifice; a cheat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Sharper; rogue.</syn> --
<usage><er>Swindler</er>, <er>Sharper</er>. These words agree in
describing persons who take unfair advantages. A <xex>swindler</xex> is
one who obtains money or goods under false pretenses. A
<xex>sharper</xex> is one who cheats by sharp practice, as in playing at
cards or staking what he can not pay.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fraud and injustice soon follow, and the dignity of the British
merchant is sunk in the scandalous appellation of a
<qex>swindler</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>V. Knox.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Perhaps you 'll think I act the same<br/
As a sly <qex>sharper</qex> plays his game.</q>
<rj><qau>Cotton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swindlery</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"dler*y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Swindling;
rougery.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <ldquo/<xex>Swindlery</xex> and
blackguardism.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swine</ent><br/
<hw>Swine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>swin</ets>, AS. <ets>sw<imac/n</ets>; akin to OFries. & OS.
<ets>swin</ets>, D. <ets>zwijn</ets>, G. <ets>schwein</ets>, OHG.
<ets>sw<imac/n</ets>, Icel. <ets>sv<imac/n</ets>, Sw. <ets>svin</ets>,
Dan. <ets>sviin</ets>, Goth. <ets>swein</ets>; originally a diminutive
corresponding to E. <ets>sow</ets>. See <er>Sow</er>,
<pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any animal of the hog kind,
especially one of the domestical species. Swine secrete a large amount
of subcutaneous fat, which, when extracted, is known as <xex>lard</xex>.
The male is specifically called <xex>boar</xex>, the female,
<xex>sow</xex>, and the young, <xex>pig</xex>. See <er>Hog</er>.</def>
<ldquo/A great herd of <xex>swine</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Mark v.
11.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swine grass</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>knotgrass
(<spn>Polygonum aviculare</spn>); -- so called because eaten by
swine.</cd> -- <col><b>Swine oat</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a kind
of oat sometimes grown for swine.</cd> -- <col><b>Swine's
cress</b></col> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>a species of cress of the genus
<gen>Senebiera</gen> (<spn>Senebiera Coronopus</spn>).</cd> --
<col><b>Swine's head</b></col>, <cd>a dolt; a blockhead.</cd>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <au>Chaucer.</au> -- <col><b>Swine thistle</b></col>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld>, <cd>the sow thistle.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinebread</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"bread`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>The truffle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinecase</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"case`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hogsty.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinecote</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"cote`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A hogsty.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinecrue</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"crue`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Swine</ets>
+ Prov. E. <ets>crue</ets> a coop.]</ety> <def>A hogsty.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinefish</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The wolf fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swineherd</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"herd`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A keeper of
swine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinepipe</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"pipe`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The European redwing.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swine-pox</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"-pox`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>A variety of the chicken pox, with acuminated vesicles containing a
watery fluid; the water pox.</def> <rj><au>Pepys.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinery</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"er*y</hw> <pr>(sw<imac/n"<etil/r*<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Same as <er>Piggery</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinestone</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"stone`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Stinkstone</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinesty</ent><br/
<hw>Swine"sty`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A sty, or pen, for
swine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swing</ent><br/
<hw>Swing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swung</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>Archaic imp.</pos>
<conjf>Swang</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swinging</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swingen</ets>, AS.
<ets>swingan</ets> to scourge, to fly, to flutter; akin to G.
<ets>schwingen</ets> to winnow, to swingle, oscillate, <ets>sich
schwingen</ets> to leap, to soar, OHG. <ets>swingan</ets> to throw, to
scourge, to soar, Sw. <ets>svinga</ets> to swing, to whirl, Dan.
<ets>svinge</ets>. Cf. <er>Swagger</er>, <er>Sway</er>, <er>Swinge</er>,
<er>Swink</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To move to and fro, as a body
suspended in the air; to wave; to vibrate; to oscillate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I tried if a pendulum would <qex>swing</qex> faster, or continue
<qex>swinging</qex> longer, in case of exsuction of the air.</q>
<rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To sway or move from one side or direction to
another; <as>as, the door <ex>swung</ex> open</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To use a swing; <as>as, a boy <ex>swings</ex> for
exercise or pleasure</as>. See <er>Swing</er>, <pos>n.</pos>,
3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>To turn round by action of wind
or tide when at anchor; <as>as, a ship <ex>swings</ex> with the
tide</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>To be hanged.</def> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark>
<rj><au>D. Webster.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>To swing round the circle</b></col>, <cd>to make a
complete circuit.</cd> <mark>[Colloq.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He had <qex>swung round the circle</qex> of theories and systems
in which his age abounded, without finding relief.</q> <rj><qau>A. V. G.
Allen.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swing</ent><br/
<hw>Swing</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to swing or
vibrate; to cause to move backward and forward, or from one side to the
other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He <qex>swings</qex> his tail, and swiftly turns his round.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They get on ropes, as you must have seen the children, and are
<qex>swung</qex> by their men visitants.</q>
<rj><qau>Spectator.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To give a circular movement to; to whirl; to
brandish; <as>as, to <ex>swing</ex> a sword; to <ex>swing</ex> a
club</as>; hence, colloquially, to manage; <as>as, to <ex>swing</ex> a
business</as>.</def><-- or, to accomplish --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mach.)</fld> <def>To admit or turn (anything) for
the purpose of shaping it; -- said of a lathe; <as>as, the lathe can
<ex>swing</ex> a pulley of 12 inches diameter</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>To swing a door</b></col>, <col><b>gate</b></col>,
etc.</mcol> <fld>(Carp.)</fld>, <cd>to put it on hinges so that it can
swing or turn.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swing</ent><br/
<hw>Swing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The act of
swinging; a waving, oscillating, or vibratory motion of a hanging or
pivoted object; oscillation; <as>as, the <ex>swing</ex> of a
pendulum</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Swaying motion from one side or direction to the
other; <as>as, some men walk with a <ex>swing</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A line, cord, or other thing suspended and hanging
loose, upon which anything may swing; especially, an apparatus for
recreation by swinging, commonly consisting of a rope, the two ends of
which are attached overhead, as to the bough of a tree, a seat being
placed in the loop at the bottom; also, any contrivance by which a
similar motion is produced for amusement or exercise.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>Influence of power of a body put in swaying
motion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The ram that batters down the wall,<br/
For the great <qex>swing</qex> and rudeness of his poise,<br/
They place before his hand that made the engine.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Capacity of a turning lathe, as determined by the
diameter of the largest object that can be turned in it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Free course; unrestrained liberty or license;
tendency.</def> <ldquo/Take thy <xex>swing</xex>.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To prevent anything which may prove an obstacle to the full
<qex>swing</qex> of his genius.</q> <rj><qau>Burke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Full swing</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Full</er>.</cd>
-- <col><b>Swing beam</b></col> <fld>(Railway Mach.)</fld>, <cd>a
crosspiece sustaining the car body, and so suspended from the framing of
a truck that it may have an independent lateral motion.</cd> --
<col><b>Swing bridge</b></col>, <cd>a form of drawbridge which swings
horizontally, as on a vertical pivot.</cd> -- <mcol><col><b>Swing
plow</b></col>, <it>or</it> <col><b>Swing plough</b></col></mcol>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A plow without a fore wheel under the beam.</cd>
<sd>(b)</sd> <cd>A reversible or sidehill plow.</cd> -- <col><b>Swing
wheel</b></col>. <sd>(a)</sd> <cd>The scape-wheel in a clock, which
drives the pendulum.</cd> <sd>(b)</sd> <cd>The balance of a
watch.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingdevil</ent><br/
<hw>Swing"dev`il</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<ety>[So named from its swift flight and dark color, which give it an
uncanny appearance.]</ety> <def>The European swift.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinge</ent><br/
<hw>Swinge</hw> <pr>(sw<icr/nj)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Singe</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinge</ent><br/
<hw>Swinge</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Swinged</conjf> <pr>(sw<icr/njd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swingeing</conjf> <pr>(sw<icr/nj"<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[OE. <ets>swengen</ets>, AS. <ets>swengan</ets> to shake, causative
of <ets>swingan</ets>. See <er>Swing</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
beat soundly; to whip; to chastise; to punish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I had <qex>swinged</qex> him soundly.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And <qex>swinges</qex> his own vices in his son.</q> <rj><qau>C.
Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To move as a lash; to lash.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Swinges</qex> the scaly horror of his folded tail.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinge</ent><br/
<hw>Swinge</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The sweep of anything in
motion; a swinging blow; a swing.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Waller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Power; sway; influence.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingebuckler</ent><br/
<hw>Swinge"buc`kler</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A
swashbuckler; a bully; a roisterer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingeing</ent><br/
<hw>Swinge"ing</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Huge; very large.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <au>Arbuthnot.</au> <au>Byron.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Swinge"ing*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingel</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"gel</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>swingele</ets> whip, scourge. See <er>Swing</er>.]</ety> <def>The
swinging part of a flail which falls on the grain in thrashing; the
swiple.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinger</ent><br/
<hw>Swing"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who swings or
whirls.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinger</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"ger</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who
swinges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Anything very large, forcible, or astonishing.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Herrick.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 3. [Colloq.] <def>A person who engages frequently in lively and
fashionable pursuits, such as attending night clubs or discos.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p> 4. <def>A person who engages freely in sexual intercourse.</def>
--><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingle</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"gle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <ety>[Freq. of
<ets>swing</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To dangle; to wave
hanging.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Johnson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To swing for pleasure.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Prov.
Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingle</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"gle</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Swingled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swingling</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Swingel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To clean, as flax, by beating
it with a swingle, so as to separate the coarse parts and the woody
substance from it; to scutch.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To beat off the tops of without pulling up the
roots; -- said of weeds.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark>
<rj><au>Forby.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingle</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"gle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A wooden instrument like a large
knife, about two feet long, with one thin edge, used for beating and
cleaning flax; a scutcher; -- called also <altname>swingling
knife</altname>, <altname>swingling staff</altname>, and
<altname>swingling wand</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<-- 2.  contraction for <it>swinging single</it>, i .e. an unmarried swinger[sense 3 or 4] [Colloq.] -->

<p><ent>Swinglebar</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"gle*bar`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A swingletree.</def>  <rj><au>De Quincey.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingletail</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"gle*tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The thrasher, or fox shark. See <er>Thrasher</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingletree</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"gle*tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So named in allusion to its <ets>swinging</ets>. See <er>Swingle</er>, <pos>v. i.</pos>, and cf. <er>Swingtree</er>.]</ety> <def>A whiffletree, or whippletree. See <er>Singletree</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingling</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"gling</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Swingle</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swingling tow</b></col>, <cd>the coarse part of flax, separated from the finer by swingling and hatcheling.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swingtree</ent><br/
<hw>Swing"tree`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The bar of a carriage to which the traces are fastened; the whiffletree.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinish</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to swine; befitting swine; like swine; hoggish; gross; beasty; <as>as, a <ex>swinish</ex> drunkard or sot</as>.</def> <ldquo/<xex>Swinish</xex> gluttony.<rdquo/  <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj> -- <wordforms><wf>Swin"ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> -- <wf>Swin"ish*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swink</ent><br/
<hw>Swink</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.</pos> <conjf>Swank</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>, <conjf>Swonk</conjf> <pr>(<?/)</pr>; <pos>p. p.</pos> <conjf>Swonken</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Swinking</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[AS. <ets>swincan</ets>, akin to <ets>swingan</ets>. See <er>Swing</er>.]</ety> <def>To labor; to toil; to salve.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Archaic]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Or <qex>swink</qex> with his hands and labor.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>For which men <qex>swink</qex> and sweat incessantly.</q> <rj><qau>Spenser.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>swinking</qex> crowd at every stroke pant <ldquo/Ho.<rdquo/</q> <rj><qau>Sir Samuel Freguson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swink</ent><br/
<hw>Swink</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To cause to toil or drudge; to tire or exhaust with labor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And the <qex>swinked</qex> hedger at his supper sat.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To acquire by labor.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To devour all that others <qex>swink</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swink</ent><br/
<hw>Swink</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[As. <ets>swinc</ets>, ge<ets>swinc</ets>.]</ety> <def>Labor; toil; drudgery.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Chaucer. Spenser.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinker</ent><br/
<hw>Swink"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A laborer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>  <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swinney</ent><br/
<hw>Swin"ney</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Far.)</fld> <def>See <er>Sweeny</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swipe</ent><br/
<hw>Swipe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. <er>Sweep</er>, <er>Swiple</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A swape or sweep. See <er>Sweep</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A strong blow given with a sweeping motion, as with a bat or club.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Swipes</qex> [in cricket] over the blower's head, and over either of the long fields.</q> <rj><qau>R. A. Proctor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Poor, weak beer; small beer.</def> <mark>[Slang, Eng.]</mark> <altsp>[Written also <asp>swypes</asp>.]</altsp>  <rj><au>Craig.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1460 --></p>

<p><ent>Swipe</ent><br/
<hw>Swipe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swiped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swiping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To give a swipe to;
to strike forcibly with a sweeping motion, as a ball.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Loose balls may be <qex>swiped</qex> almost <it>ad
libitum</it>.</q> <rj><qau>R. A. Proctor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pluck; to snatch; to steal.</def> <mark>[Slang,
U.S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiple</ent><br/
<hw>Swi"ple</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Swipe</er>.]</ety> <def>That part of a flail which strikes the grain
in thrashing; a swingel.</def> <altsp>[Written also <asp>swipel</asp>,
and <asp>swipple</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swipper</ent><br/
<hw>Swip"per</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From AS.
<ets>swipian</ets> to whip, shake, whirl; akin to <ets>sw<amac/pan</ets>
to sweep. See <er>Swoop</er>.]</ety> <def>Nimble; quick.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Prov. Eng. & Slang]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swirl</ent><br/
<hw>Swirl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t. & i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Swirled</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Swirling</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Akin to Norw.
<ets>svirla</ets> to whirl, freq. of <ets>sverra</ets> to whirl, Dan.
<ets>svirre</ets>, G. <ets>schwirren</ets> to whiz, to buzz. <root/177.
See <er>Swarm</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <def>To whirl, or cause to
whirl, as in an eddy.</def> <ldquo/The river <xex>swirled</xex>
along.<rdquo/ <rj><au>C. Kingsley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swirl</ent><br/
<hw>Swirl</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A whirling motion; an eddy, as of
water; a whirl.</def> <ldquo/The silent <xex>swirl</xex> of bats.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Mrs. Browning.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swish</ent><br/
<hw>Swish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[From the sound. Cf.
<er>Swash</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To flourish, so as to make the
sound <xex>swish</xex>.</def> <rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To flog; to lash.</def> <mark>[Slang]</mark>
<rj><au>Thackeray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swish</ent><br/
<hw>Swish</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To dash; to swash.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swish</ent><br/
<hw>Swish</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sound of quick
movement, as of something whirled through the air.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Naut.)</fld> <def>Light driven spray.</def>
<mark>[Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiss</ent><br/
<hw>Swiss</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. sing. & pl.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>Suisse</ets>, of German origin. Cf. <er>Switzer</er>.]</ety> <def>A
native or inhabitant of Switzerland; a Switzer; the people of
Switzerland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swiss</ent><br/
<hw>Swiss</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to Switzerland, or
the people of Switzerland.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switch</ent><br/
<hw>Switch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. OD.
<ets>swick</ets> a scourage, a whip. Cf. <er>Swink</er>,
<er>Swing</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A small, flexible twig or
rod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Mauritania, on the fifth medal, leads a horse with something like
a thread; in her other hand she holds a <qex>switch</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Railways)</fld> <def>A movable part of a rail; or
of opposite rails, for transferring cars from one track to
another.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A separate mass or trees of hair, or of some
substance (at jute) made to resemble hair, worn on the head by
women.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Elec.)</fld> <def>A device for shifting an electric
current to another circuit, or for making and breaking a
circuit.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Safety switch</b></col> <fld>(Railways)</fld>, <cd>a form
of switch contrived to prevent or lessen the danger of derailment of
trains.</cd> -- <col><b>Switch back</b></col> <fld>(Railways)</fld>,
<cd>an arrangement of tracks whereby elevations otherwise insurmountable
are passed. The track ascends by a series of zigzags, the engine running
alternately forward and back, until the summit is reached.</cd> --
<col><b>Switch board</b></col> <fld>(Elec.)</fld>, <cd>a collection of
switches in one piece of apparatus, so arranged that a number of
circuits may be connected or combined in any desired manner.</cd><--
need "switchboard" for telephony --> -- <col><b>Switch grass</b></col>.
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See under <er>Grass</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switch</ent><br/
<hw>Switch</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Switched</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Switching</conjf>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To strike with a
switch or small flexible rod; to whip.</def>
<rj><au>Chapman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To swing or whisk; <as>as, to <ex>switch</ex> a
cane</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To trim, <as>as, a hedge</as>.</def> <mark>[Prov.
Eng.]</mark> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>To turn from one railway track to another; to
transfer by a switch; -- generally with <xex>off</xex>, <xex>from</xex>,
etc.; <as>as, to <ex>switch</ex> off a train; to <ex>switch</ex> a car
from one track to another</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld> <def>To shift to another
circuit.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switch</ent><br/
<hw>Switch</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To walk with a jerk.</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>switchboard</ent><br/
<hw>switch"board`</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>an elctrical
apparatus consisting predominantly of a panel on which are switches or
other means of completing electrical circuits; -- used especially for
the devices used in telephone exchanges. See sense 2.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Telephone)</fld> <def>An apparatus containing
switches by means of which a connection may be made from an incoming
telephone line to any one of numerous outgoing lines. The switches may
be either mechanical or electronic, and the switching action may be
automatic, controlled by signals in the incoming call, or manual,
controlled by a switchboard operator. In older style manual
switchboards, the circuits were connected by the use of patchcords,
inserted into plugs.</def><br/
<syn><b>Syn. --</b> patchboard, plugboard, telephone exchange.</syn><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switchel</ent><br/
<hw>Switch"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sweet</er>.]</ety> <def>A beverage of molasses and water, seasoned
with vinegar and ginger.</def> <mark>[U. S.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>switcheroo</ent><br/
<hw>switch"er*oo</hw> <pos>n.</pos> <def>a sudden and unexpected change
or reversal of position, attitude, or action.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switching</ent><br/
<hw>Switch"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Switch</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Switching engine</b></col>, <cd>a locomotive for
switching cars from one track to another, and making up trains; --
called also <altname>switch engine</altname>.</cd>
<mark>[U.S.]</mark></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switching power supply</ent><br/
<hw>Switch"ing power supply</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>a device used as
part of an electronic device, which transforms electrical current from
an AC line circuit to DC for use in electronic devices, and which can
use either 110 volt or 220 volt AC line curent.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switchman</ent><br/
<hw>Switch"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Switchmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>One who tends a switch
on a railway.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switchy</ent><br/
<hw>Switch"y</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Whisking.</def>
<mark>[Colloq.]</mark> <rj><au>Coombe.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swithe</ent><br/
<hw>Swithe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sw<imac/<?/e</ets> strongly, violently.]</ety> <def>Instantly;
quickly; speedily; rapidly.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That thou doest, do thou <qex>swithe</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Wyclif
(John xiii. 27).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Switzer</ent><br/
<hw>Switz"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G.
<ets>schweizer</ets>. Cf. <er>Swiss</er>.]</ety> <def>A native or
inhabitant of Switzerland; a Swiss.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swive</ent><br/
<hw>Swive</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>swiven</ets>, fr. AS. <ets>sw<imac/fan</ets>. See
<er>Swivel</er>.]</ety> <def>To copulate with (a woman).</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swivel</ent><br/
<hw>Swiv"el</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[AS.
<ets>sw<imac/fan</ets> to move quickly, to remove; akin to Icel.
<ets>sveifla</ets> to whirl, shake, <ets>sv<imac/fa</ets> to ramble, to
turn. See <er>Swoop</er>, and cf. <er>Swift</er> a reel, <er>Swift</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mech.)</fld> <def>A piece, as a
ring or hook, attached to another piece by a pin, in such a manner as to
permit rotation about the pin as an axis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mil.)</fld> <def>A small piece of ordnance, turning
on a point or swivel; -- called also <altname>swivel
gun</altname>.</def> <rj><au>Wilhelm.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swivel bridge</b></col>, <cd>a kind of drawbridge that
turns round on a vertical axis; a swing bridge.</cd> -- <col><b>Swivel
hook</b></col>, <cd>a hook connected with the iron strap of a pulley
block by a swivel joint, for readily taking the turns out of a
tackle.</cd> -- <col><b>Swivel joint</b></col>, <cd>a joint, the two
pieces composing which turn round, with respect to each other, on a
longitudinal pin or axis, as in a chain, to prevent
twisting.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swivel</ent><br/
<hw>Swiv"el</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To swing or turn, as on a pin or
pivot.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swivel-eyed</ent><br/
<hw>Swiv"el-eyed`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Squint-eyed.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swizzle</ent><br/
<hw>Swiz"zle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To drink; to
swill.</def> <rj><au>Halliwell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swizzle</ent><br/
<hw>Swiz"zle</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Ale and beer mixed; also, drink
generally.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swob</ent><br/
<hw>Swob</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Swab</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swobber</ent><br/
<hw>Swob"ber</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>See
<er>Swabber</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <pluf>pl.</pluf> <def>Four privileged cards, formerly
used in betting at the game of whist.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>swabber</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Swift.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swollen</ent><br/
<hw>Swoll"en</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Swell</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swollen</ent><br/
<hw>Swoll"en</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Enlarged by swelling; immoderately
increased; <as>as, <ex>swollen</ex> eyes; <ex>swollen</ex>
streams.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swoln</ent><br/
<hw>Swoln</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>Contraction of <er>Swollen</er>,
<pos>p. p.</pos></def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swom</ent><br/
<hw>Swom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Swim</er>.</def> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swoon</ent><br/
<hw>Swoon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swooned</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swooning</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swounen</ets>,
<ets>swoghenen</ets>, for <ets>swo<?/nien</ets>, fr. <ets>swo<?/en</ets>
to sigh deeply, to droop, AS. <ets>sw<omac/gan</ets> to sough, sigh; cf.
ge<ets>sw<omac/gen</ets> senseless, swooned, ge<ets>sw<omac/wung</ets> a
swooning. Cf. <er>Sough</er>.]</ety> <def>To sink into a fainting fit,
in which there is an apparent suspension of the vital functions and
mental powers; to faint; -- often with <xex>away</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sucklings <qex>swoon</qex> in the streets of the city.</q>
<rj><qau>Lam. ii. 11.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The most in years . . . <qex>swooned</qex> first away for
pain.</q> <rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He seemed ready to <qex>swoon</qex> away in the surprise of
joy.</q> <rj><qau>Tatler.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swoon</ent><br/
<hw>Swoon</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fainting fit; syncope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swooning</ent><br/
<hw>Swoon"ing</hw>, <def><pos>a. & n.</pos> from <er>Swoon</er>,
<pos>v.</pos></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Swoon"ing*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swoop</ent><br/
<hw>Swoop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p.
p.</pos> <conjf>Swooped</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Swooping</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[OE. <ets>swopen</ets>, usually,
to sweep, As. <ets>sw<amac/pan</ets> to sweep, to rush; akin to G.
<ets>schweifen</ets> to rove, to ramble, to curve, OHG.
<ets>sweifan</ets> to whirl, Icel. <ets>sveipa</ets> to sweep; also to
AS. <ets>sw<imac/fan</ets> to move quickly. Cf. <er>Sweep</er>,
<er>Swift</er>, <pos>a.</pos> & <pos>n.</pos>, <er>Swipe</er>,
<er>Swivel</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To fall on at once and seize;
to catch while on the wing; <as>as, a hawk <ex>swoops</ex> a
chicken</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To seize; to catch up; to take with a
sweep.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>And now at last you came to <qex>swoop</qex> it all.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The grazing ox which <qex>swoops</qex> it [the medicinal herb] in
with the common grass.</q> <rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swoop</ent><br/
<hw>Swoop</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To descend with closed
wings from a height upon prey, as a hawk; to stoop.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To pass with pomp; to sweep.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Drayton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swoop</ent><br/
<hw>Swoop</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A falling on and seizing, as the prey
of a rapacious bird; the act of swooping.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The eagle fell, . . . and carried away a whole litter of cubs at a
<qex>swoop</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>L'Estrange.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swoopstake</ent><br/
<hw>Swoop"stake`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sweepstake</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swoopstake</ent><br/
<hw>Swoop"stake`</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Altogether;
indiscriminately.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swop</ent><br/
<hw>Swop</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Swap</er>.</def> <rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sword</ent><br/
<hw>Sword</hw> <pr>(s<omac/rd)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>swerd</ets>, AS. <ets>sweord</ets>; akin to OFries.
<ets>swerd</ets>, <ets>swird</ets>, D. <ets>zwaard</ets>, OS.
<ets>swerd</ets>, OHG. <ets>swert</ets>, G. <ets>schwert</ets>, Icel.
<ets>sver<edh/</ets>, Sw. <ets>sv<aum/rd</ets>, Dan. <ets>svaerd</ets>;
of uncertain origin.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An offensive weapon, having
a long and usually sharp-pointed blade with a cutting edge or edges. It
is the general term, including the small sword, rapier, saber, scimiter,
and many other varieties.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the emblem of judicial vengeance or
punishment, or of authority and power.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He [the ruler] beareth not the <qex>sword</qex> in vain.</q>
<rj><qau>Rom. xiii. 4.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>She quits the balance, and resigns the <qex>sword</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Destruction by the sword, or in battle; war;
dissension.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I came not to send peace, but a <qex>sword</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Matt. x. 34.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The military power of a country.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He hath no more authority over the <qex>sword</qex> than over the
law.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Weaving)</fld> <def>One of the end bars by which
the lay of a hand loom is suspended.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sword arm</b></col>, <cd>the right arm.</cd> --
<col><b>Sword bayonet</b></col>, <cd>a bayonet shaped somewhat like a
sword, and which can be used as a sword.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword
bearer</b></col>, <cd>one who carries his master's sword; an officer in
London who carries a sword before the lord mayor when he goes
abroad.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword belt</b></col>, <cd>a belt by which a
sword is suspended, and borne at the side.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword
blade</b></col>, <cd>the blade, or cutting part, of a sword.</cd> --
<col><b>Sword cane</b></col>, <cd>a cane which conceals the blade of a
sword or dagger, as in a sheath.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword dance</b></col>.
<sd>(a)</sd> <cd>A dance in which swords are brandished and clashed
together by the male dancers.</cd> <au>Sir W. Scott.</au> <sd>(b)</sd>
<cd>A dance performed over swords laid on the ground, but without
touching them.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword fight</b></col>, <cd>fencing; a
combat or trial of skill with swords; swordplay.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword
grass</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Gladen</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sword knot</b></col>, <cd>a ribbon tied to the hilt of a
sword.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword law</b></col>, <cd>government by the sword,
or by force; violence.</cd> <au>Milton.</au> -- <col><b>Sword
lily</b></col>. <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <cd>See <er>Gladiolus</er>.</cd> --
<col><b>Sword mat</b></col> <fld>(Naut.)</fld>, <cd>a mat closely woven
of yarns; -- so called from a wooden implement used in its
manufacture.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword shrimp</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>,
<cd>a European shrimp (<spn>Pasiphaea sivado</spn>) having a very thin,
compressed body.</cd> -- <col><b>Sword stick</b></col>, <cd>a sword
cane.</cd> -- <col><b>To measure swords with one</b></col>. <cd>See
under <er>Measure</er>, <pos>v. t.</pos></cd> -- <col><b>To put to the
sword</b></col>. <cd>See under <er>Put</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordbill</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"bill`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>A humming bird (<spn>Docimastes ensiferus</spn>) having a very
long, slender bill, exceeding the length of the body of the
bird.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sworded</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"ed</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. AS.
ge<ets>swurdod</ets>.]</ety> <def>Girded with a sword.</def>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sworder</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who uses, or
fights with, a sword; a swordsman; a soldier; a cutthroat.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordfish</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"fish`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A very large oceanic fish
(<spn>Xiphias gladius</spn>), the only representative of the family
<fam>Xiphiidae</fam>. It is highly valued as a food fish. The bones of
the upper jaw are consolidated, and form a long, rigid, swordlike beak;
the dorsal fin is high and without distinct spines; the ventral fins are
absent. The adult is destitute of teeth. It becomes sixteen feet or more
long.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The gar pike.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>The
cutlass fish.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A southern constellation. See
<er>Dorado</er>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Swordfish sucker</b></col> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>, <cd>a
remora (<spn>Remora brachyptera</spn>) which attaches itself to the
swordfish.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordick</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"ick</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>The spotted gunnel (<spn>Muraenoides gunnellus</spn>).</def>
<mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swording</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Slashing with a
sword.</def> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordless</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"less</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Destitute of a
sword.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordman</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Swordmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <def>A swordsman.</def>
<ldquo/Sinewy <xex>swordmen</xex>.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordplay</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"play`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Fencing; a sword
fight.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordplayer</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"play`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A fencer; a
gladiator; one who exhibits his skill in the use of the sword.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sword-shaped</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"-shaped`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Shaped like a sword; ensiform, as the long, flat leaves of the
Iris, cattail, and the like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordsman</ent><br/
<hw>Swords"man</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Swordsmen</plw> <pr>(<?/)</pr>.</plu> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A soldier; a
fighting man.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One skilled of a use of the sword; a professor of
the science of fencing; a fencer.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordsmanship</ent><br/
<hw>Swords"man*ship</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The state of being a
swordsman; skill in the use of the sword.</def>
<rj><au>Cowper.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swordtail</ent><br/
<hw>Sword"tail`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>The limulus.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Any hemipterous
insect of the genus <gen>Uroxiphus</gen>, found upon forest
trees.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swore</ent><br/
<hw>Swore</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp.</pos> of
<er>Swear</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sworn</ent><br/
<hw>Sworn</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>p. p.</pos> of
<er>Swear</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sworn brothers</b></col>, <cd>originally, companions in
arms who took an oath to share together good and bad fortune; hence,
faithful friends.</cd> -- <col><b>Sworn enemies</b></col>,
<cd>determined or irreconcilable enemies.</cd> -- <col><b>Sworn
friends</b></col>, <cd>close friends.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swough</ent><br/
<hw>Swough</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Swoon</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A sound; a groan; a moan; a
sough.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He sigheth with full many a sorry <qex>swough</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A swoon.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark>
<rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swound</ent><br/
<hw>Swound</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Swoon</er>, <pos>v. & n.</pos></def> <mark>[Prov. Eng. or
Archaic]</mark> <rj><au>Shak. Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The landlord stirred<br/
As one awaking from a <qex>swound</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Longfellow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>'Swounds</ent><br/
<hw>'Swounds</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>interj.</pos> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Zounds</er>.]</ety> <def>An exclamation contracted from <xex>God's
wounds</xex>; -- used as an oath.</def> <mark>[Obs. or Archaic]</mark>
<rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swown</ent><br/
<hw>Swown</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. & n.</pos> <def>Swoon.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swum</ent><br/
<hw>Swum</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Swim</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swung</ent><br/
<hw>Swung</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <def><pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> of
<er>Swing</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Swythe</ent><br/
<hw>Swythe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Quickly. See
<er>Swithe</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sy</ent><br/
<hw>Sy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos> <mord>of
<er>See</er></mord>. <def>Saw.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syb</ent><br/
<hw>Syb</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>See <er>Sib</er>.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sybarite</ent><br/
<hw>Syb"a*rite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Sybarita</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/, a city in Italy, noted for the
effeminacy and voluptuousness of its inhabitants; cf. F.
<ets>Sybarite</ets>.]</ety> <def>A person devoted to luxury and
pleasure; a voluptuary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sybaritical</ent><br/
<ent>Sybaritic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syb`a*rit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syb`a*rit"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Sybariticus</ets>, Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to the Sybarites; resembling the
Sybarites; luxurious; wanton; effeminate.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Sybaritic</xex> dinners.<rdquo/ <au>Bp. Warburton.</au>
<ldquo/<xex>Sybaritical</xex> cloistres.<rdquo/ <au>Bp. Hall.</au><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sybaritism</ent><br/
<hw>Syb"a*rit*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Luxuriousness;
effeminacy; wantonness; voluptuousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycamine</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"a*mine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sycaminus</ets>, Gr. <?/; perhaps of Semitic origin.]</ety>
<def>See <er>Sycamore</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycamore</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"a*more</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sycomorus</ets>, Gr. <?/ the fig mulberry; <?/ a fig + <?/ the
black mulberry; or perhaps of Semitic origin: cf. F.
<ets>sycomore</ets>. Cf. <er>Mulberry</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<sd>(a)</sd> <def>A large tree (<spn>Ficus Sycomorus</spn>) allied to
the common fig. It is found in Egypt and Syria, and is the sycamore, or
sycamine, of Scripture.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The American plane tree,
or buttonwood.</def> <sd>(c)</sd> <def>A large European species of maple
(<spn>Acer Pseudo-Platanus</spn>).</def> <altsp>[Written sometimes
<asp>sycomore</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syce</ent><br/
<hw>Syce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Ar.
<ets>s<amac/<ium/s</ets>.]</ety> <def>A groom.</def>
<mark>[India]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycee</ent><br/
<hw>Sy*cee"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Said to be from a
Chinese word, <ets>se-tze</ets> or <ets>se-sze</ets>, meaning, fine
silk, and to be so called because if pure it may be drawn out into fine
threads.]</ety> <def>Silver, pounded into ingots of the shape of a shoe,
and used as currency. The most common weight is about one pound
troy.</def> <mark>[China]</mark> <rj><au>McElrath.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sychnocarpous</ent><br/
<hw>Sych`no*car"pous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ much
or frequent + <?/ fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the
capacity of bearing several successive crops of fruit without perishing;
<as>as, <ex>sychnocarpous</ex> plants</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycite</ent><br/
<hw>Sy"cite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ figlike, fr.
<?/ a fig.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <def>A nodule of flint, or a pebble,
which resembles a fig.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycoceric</ent><br/
<hw>Syc`o*cer"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>Of, pertaining to, or designating, an acid obtained by the
oxidation of sycoceryl alcohol.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycoceryl</ent><br/
<hw>Syc`o*ce"ryl</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a fig +
<?/ wax + <ets>-yl</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A radical, of
the aromatic series, regarded as an essential ingredient of certain
compounds found in the waxy resin of an Australian species of
fig.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1461 --></p>

<p><ent>Sycock</ent><br/
<hw>Sy"cock</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The
missel thrush.</def> <mark>[Prov. Eng.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycones</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sy*co"nes</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/ a fig.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A division of calcareous
sponges.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ They usually resemble a fig, being vase-shaped with a
fringed opening at the summit. The feeding cells are in ampullae
connected with radial tubes in the thickened walls of the
body.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syconus</ent><br/
<ent>Syconium</ent><br/
<mhw>{ \'d8<hw>Sy*co"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, \'d8<hw>Sy*co"nus</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ the
fig.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A collective fleshy fruit, in which
the ovaries are hidden within a hollow receptacle, as in the
fig.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophancy</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phan*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. L.
<ets>sycophantia</ets> deceit, Gr. <?/ false accusation.]</ety> <def>The
character or characteristic of a sycophant.</def> Hence: -<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(a)</sd> <def>False accusation; calumniation; talebearing.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Bp. Hall.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sd>(b)</sd> <def>Obsequious flattery; servility.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The <qex>sycophancy</qex> of A.Philips had prejudiced Mr. Addison
against Pope.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophant</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sycophanta</ets> a slanderer, deceiver, parasite, Gr. <?/ a false
accuser, false adviser, literally, a fig shower; <?/ a fig + <?/ to
show: cf. F. <ets>sycophante</ets>. The reason for the name is not
certainly known. See <er>Phenomenon</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
informer; a talebearer.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/Accusing
<xex>sycophants</xex>, of all men, did best sort to his nature.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Sir P. Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A base parasite; a mean or servile flatterer;
especially, a flatterer of princes and great men.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>sycophant</qex> will everything admire:<br/
Each verse, each sentence, sets his soul on fire.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophant</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phant</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[CF. L.
<ets>sycophantari</ets> to deceive, to trick, Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>To inform against; hence, to calumniate.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sycophanting</qex> and misnaming the work of his
adversary.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To play the sycophant toward; to flatter
obsequiously.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophant</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phant</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To play the
sycophant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophantcy</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phant*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sycophancy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophantical</ent><br/
<ent>Sycophantic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syc`o*phan"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syc`o*phan"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/ slanderous.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to a sycophant; characteristic of a sycophant;
meanly or obsequiously flattering; courting favor by mean adulation;
parasitic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>To be cheated and ruined by a <qex>sycophantical</qex>
parasite.</q> <rj><qau>South.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sycophantic</qex> servants to the King of Spain.</q>
<rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophantish</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phant`ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a
sycophant; obsequiously flattering.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Syc"o*phant`ish*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Sycophantish</qex> satirists that forever humor the
prevailing folly.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophantism</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phant*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sycophancy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophantize</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phant*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To play the
sycophant.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycophantry</ent><br/
<hw>Syc"o*phant*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Sycophancy.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sycosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sy*co"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ a fig.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A pustular eruption
upon the scalp, or the beared part of the face, whether due to ringworm,
acne, or impetigo.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syderolite</ent><br/
<hw>Syd"er*o*lite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A kind of
Bohemian earthenware resembling the Wedgwood ware.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sye</ent><br/
<hw>Sye</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <mark>obs.</mark> <pos>imp.</pos> <mord>of
<er>See</er></mord>. <def>Saw.</def> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syenite</ent><br/
<hw>Sy"e*nite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Syenites</ets> (sc. <ets>lapis</ets>), from <ets>Syene</ets>, Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Orig., a rock composed
of quartz, hornblende, and feldspar, anciently quarried at
<xex>Syene</xex>, in Upper Egypt, and now called
<altname>granite</altname>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>A granular,
crystalline, ingeous rock composed of orthoclase and hornblende, the
latter often replaced or accompanied by pyroxene or mica. Syenite
sometimes contains nephelite (elaeolite) or leucite, and is then called
<altname>nephelite (elaeolite) syenite</altname> or <altname>leucite
syenite</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syenitic</ent><br/
<hw>Sy`e*nit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Written also
<ets>sienitic</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Relating to Syene; <as>as,
<ex>Syenitic</ex> inscriptions</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Relating to, or like, syenite; <as>as,
<ex>syenitic</ex> granite</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syke</ent><br/
<hw>Syke</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. & v.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sike</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syker</ent><br/
<hw>Syk"er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a. & adv.</pos> <def>See
<er>Sicker</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syle</ent><br/
<hw>Syle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sile</er> a
young herring.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A young herring
(<spn>Clupea harengus</spn>).</def> <altsp>[Also written
<asp>sile</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>But our folk call them <qex>syle</qex>, and nought but
<qex>syle</qex>,<br/
And when they're grown, why then we call them herring.</q> <rj><qau>J.
Ingelow.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabarium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syl`la*ba"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Syllabaria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>A syllabary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabary</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"la*ba*ry</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A table of
syllables; more especially, a table of the indivisible syllabic symbols
used in certain languages, as the Japanese and Cherokee, instead of
letters.</def> <rj><au>S. W. Williams.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabe</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"labe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<def>Syllable.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabical</ent><br/
<ent>Syllabic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syl*lab"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syl*lab"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>syllabique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a
syllable or syllables; <as>as, <ex>syllabic</ex> accent</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Consisting of a syllable or syllables; <as>as, a
<ex>syllabic</ex> augment</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>syllabic</xex>
stage of writing.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Earle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabically</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*lab"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a syllabic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabicate</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*lab"i*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Syllabicated</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Syllabicating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <def>To form or divide into
syllables; to syllabify.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabication</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*lab`i*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
forming syllables; the act or method of dividing words into syllables.
See <xex>Guide to Pron.</xex>, <sect/275.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabification</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*lab`i*fi*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Syllabify</er>.]</ety> <def>Same as <er>Syllabication</er>.</def>
<rj><au>Rush.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Syllabification</qex> depends not on mere force, but on
discontinuity of force.</q> <rj><au>H. Sweet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabify</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*lab"i*fy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Syllabified</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Syllabifying</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[L.
<ets>syllaba</ets> syllable + <ets>-fy</ets>.]</ety> <def>To form or
divide into syllables.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabism</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"la*bism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The expressing of
the sounds of a language by syllables, rather than by an alphabet or by
signs for words.</def> <rj><au>I. Taylor (The Alphabet).</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabist</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"la*bist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who forms or
divides words into syllables, or is skilled in doing this.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabize</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"la*bize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
syllabify.</def> <rj><au>Howell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllable</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"la*ble</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>sillable</ets>, OF. <ets>sillabe</ets>, F. <ets>syllabe</ets>, L.
<ets>syllaba</ets>, Gr. <?/ that which is held together, several letters
taken together so as to form one sound, a syllable, fr. <?/ to take
together; <?/ with + <?/ to take; cf. Skr. <ets>labh</ets>,
<ets>rabh</ets>. Cf. <er>Lemma</er>, <er>Dilemma</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>An elementary sound, or a combination of elementary
sounds, uttered together, or with a single effort or impulse of the
voice, and constituting a word or a part of a word. In other terms, it
is a vowel or a diphtong, either by itself or flanked by one or more
consonants, the whole produced by a single impulse or utterance. One of
the liquids, <xex>l</xex>, <xex>m</xex>, <xex>n</xex>, may fill the
place of a vowel in a syllable. Adjoining syllables in a word or phrase
need not to be marked off by a pause, but only by such an abatement and
renewal, or reenforcement, of the stress as to give the feeling of
separate impulses. See <xex>Guide to Pronunciation</xex>,
<sect/275.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>In writing and printing, a part of a word, separated
from the rest, and capable of being pronounced by a single impulse of
the voice. It may or may not correspond to a syllable in the spoken
language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Withouten vice [<it>i. e.</it> mistake] of <qex>syllable</qex> or
letter.</q> <rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A small part of a sentence or discourse; anything
concise or short; a particle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Before any <qex>syllable</qex> of the law of God was written.</q>
<rj><qau>Hooker.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Who dare speak<br/
One <qex>syllable</qex> against him?</q> <rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllable</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"la*ble</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To pronounce the syllables
of; to utter; to articulate.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabub</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"la*bub</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Syllabub</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllabus</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"la*bus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it> E.
<plw>Syllabuses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>, L. <plw>Syllabi</plw>
<pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. the same source as E.
<ets>syllable</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A compendium containing the
heads of a discourse, and the like; an abstract.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>The headnote of a reported case;
the brief statement of the points of law determined prefixed to a
reported case. The opinion controls the syllabus, the latter being
merely explanatory of the former.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllepsis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syl*lep"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>sy`llhpsis</grk> a taking together, from <?/. See
<er>syllable</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld>
<def>A figure of speech by which a word is used in a literal and
metaphorical sense at the same time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The agreement of a verb or
adjective with one, rather than another, of two nouns, with either of
which it might agree in gender, number, etc.; as, <it>rex</it> et regina
<it>beati.</it></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylleptical</ent><br/
<ent>Sylleptic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syl*lep"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syl*lep"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ collective.]</ety>
<def>Of or pertaining to a syllepsis; containing syllepsis.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Syl*lep"tic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllidian</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*lid"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From NL.
<ets>Syllis</ets>, the typical genus.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any
one of numerous species of marine annelids of the family
<fam>Syllidae</fam>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ Many of the species are phosphorescent; others are
remarkable for undergoing strobilation or fission and for their
polymorphism. The egg, in such species, develops into an asexual
individual. When mature, a number of its posterior segments gradually
develop into one or more sexual individuals which finally break away and
swim free in the sea. The males, females, and neuters usually differ
greatly in form and structure.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllogism</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"lo*gism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[OE.
<ets>silogisme</ets>, OF. <ets>silogime</ets>, <ets>sillogisme</ets>, F.
<ets>syllogisme</ets>, L. <ets>syllogismus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>syllogismo`s</grk> a reckoning all together, a reasoning,
syllogism, fr. <grk>syllogi`zesqai</grk> to reckon all together, to
bring at once before the mind, to infer, conclude; <grk>sy`n</grk> with,
together + <grk>logi`zesqai</grk> to reckon, to conclude by reasoning.
See <er>Syn-</er>, and <er>Logistic</er>, <er>Logic</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>The regular logical form of every argument,
consisting of three propositions, of which the first two are called the
<xex>premises</xex>, and the last, the <xex>conclusion</xex>. The
conclusion necessarily follows from the premises; so that, if these are
true, the conclusion must be true, and the argument amounts to
demonstration</def>; <note>as in the following example:</note></p>

<p>Every virtue is laudable; Kindness is a virtue; Therefore kindness is
laudable.<br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note>These propositions are denominated respectively the <xex>major
premise</xex>, the <xex>minor premise</xex>, and the
<xex>conclusion</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ If the premises are not true and the syllogism is
regular, the reasoning is valid, and the conclusion, whether true or
false, is correctly derived.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllogistical</ent><br/
<ent>Syllogistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syl`lo*gis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syl`lo*gis"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>syllogisticus</ets>,
Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>syllogistique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining
to a syllogism; consisting of a syllogism, or of the form of reasoning
by syllogisms; <as>as, <ex>syllogistic</ex> arguments or
reasoning</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllogistically</ent><br/
<hw>Syl`lo*gis"tic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a syllogistic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllogization</ent><br/
<hw>Syl`lo*gi*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A reasoning
by syllogisms.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Harris.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllogize</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"lo*gize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Syllogized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Syllogizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Gr.
<?/: cf. F. <ets>syllogiser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To reason by means of
syllogisms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Men have endeavored . . . to teach boys to <qex>syllogize</qex>,
or frame arguments and refute them, without any real inward knowledge of
the question.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syllogizer</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"lo*gi`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
syllogizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylph</ent><br/
<hw>Sylph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F. <ets>sylphe</ets>,
m., fr. Gr. <?/ a kind of grub, beetle, or moth; -- so called by
Paracelsus.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An imaginary being inhabiting the
air; a fairy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Fig.: A slender, graceful woman.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of several species of
very brilliant South American humming birds, having a very long and
deeply-forked tail; <as>as, the blue-tailed <ex>sylph</ex>
(<spn>Cynanthus cyanurus</spn>)</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylphid</ent><br/
<hw>Sylph"id</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sylphide</ets>, fem. See <er>Sylph</er>.]</ety> <def>A little
sylph; a young or diminutive sylph.</def> <ldquo/The place of the
<xex>sylphid</xex> queen.<rdquo/ <rj><au>J. R. Drake.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ye sylphs and <qex>sylphids</qex>, to your chief give ear,<br/
Fays, fairies, genii, elves, and demons, hear.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylphine</ent><br/
<hw>Sylph"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a
sylph.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylphish</ent><br/
<hw>Sylph"ish</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Sylphlike.</def>
<rj><au>Carlyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylphlike</ent><br/
<hw>Sylph"like`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Like a sylph;
airy; graceful.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Sometimes a dance . . . <br/
Displayed some <qex>sylphlike</qex> figures in its maze.</q>
<rj><qau>Byron.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylva</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syl"va</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sylvae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L. <ets>sylva</ets>, better
<ets>silva</ets>, a wood. See <er>Silva</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Same as <er>Silva</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvan</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"van</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Silvan</er>,
<pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to a sylva;
forestlike; hence, rural; rustic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The traditional memory of a rural and a <qex>sylvan</qex> region .
. . is usually exact as well as tenacious.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Abounding in forests or in trees; woody.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvan</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"van</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>Sylvanus</ets>, better
<ets>Silvanus</ets>. See <er>Silvan</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>A
fabled deity of the wood; a satyr; a faun; sometimes, a
rustic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Her private orchards, walled on every side,<br/
To lawless <qex>sylvans</qex> all access denied.</q>
<rj><qau>Pope.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvan</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"van</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Sylva</ets> +
furfur<ets>an</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A liquid hydrocarbon
obtained together with furfuran (tetrol) by the distillation of pine
wood; -- called also <altname>methyl tetrol</altname>, or
<altname>methyl furfuran</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvanite</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"van*ite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Fr.
Tran<ets>sylvania</ets>, where first found.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>A telluride of gold and silver, <chform>(Au, Ag)Te2</chform>, of a
steel gray, silver white, or brass yellow. It often occurs in implanted
crystals resembling written characters, and hence is called
<altname>graphic tellurium</altname>. H., 1.5-2. Sp.gr.,
7.9-8.3.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvanium</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*va"ni*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., so called
from Tran<ets>sylvania</ets>, where it was first found.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An old name for tellurium.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>silvanium</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvate</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"vate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A
salt of sylvic acid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvatic</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*vat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sylvaticus</ets>, better <ets>silvaticus</ets>. See
<er>Silvan</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety> <def>Sylvan.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvestrian</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*ves"tri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sylvestris</ets>, better <ets>silvestris</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Sylvan.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvic</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"vic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Of,
pertaining to, or resembling, pine or its products; specifically,
designating an acid called also <xex>abeitic acid</xex>, which is the
chief ingredient of common resin (obtained from <spn>Pinus
sylvestris</spn>, and other species).</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvicoline</ent><br/
<hw>Syl*vic"o*line</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sylva</ets>, <ets>silva</ets>, forest + <ets>colere</ets> to
inhabit.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the family
of warblers (<fam>Sylvicolidae</fam>). See <er>Warbler</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylviculture</ent><br/
<hw>Syl"vi*cul`ture</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>sylva</ets>, <ets>silva</ets>, forest + E.
<ets>culture</ets>.]</ety> <def>The cultivation of forest trees for
timber or other purposes; forestry; arboriculture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylviculturist</ent><br/
<hw>Syl`vi*cul"tur*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
cultivates forest trees, especially as a business.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sylvite</ent><br/
<ent>Sylvine</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syl"vine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syl"vite</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[So called from NL. sal digestivus
<ets>sylvii</ets> potassium chloride.]</ety> <fld>(Min.)</fld>
<def>Native potassium chloride.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sym-</ent><br/
<hw>Sym-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <def>See <er>Syn-</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symarr</ent><br/
<ent>Symar</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sy*mar"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sy"marr</hw> }</mhw>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>See <er>Simar</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbal</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"bal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Cimbal</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbiosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sym`bi*o"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>symbi`wsis</grk> a living together, <grk>symbioy^n</grk> to live
together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to live.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld>
<def>The living together in more or less imitative association or even
close union of two dissimilar organisms. In a broad sense the term
includes parasitism, or <col><b>antagonistic symbiosis</b></col>
<it>or</it> <col><b>antipathetic symbiosis</b></col>, in which the
association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms,
but ordinarily it is used of cases where the association is
advantageous, or often necessary, to one or both, and not harmful to
either. When there is bodily union (in extreme cases so close that the
two form practically a single body, as in the union of algae and fungi
to form lichens, and in the inclusion of algae in radiolarians) it is
called <col><b>conjunctive symbiosis</b></col>; if there is no actual
union of the organisms (as in the association of ants with
myrmecophytes), <col><b>disjunctive symbiosis</b></col>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbiotic</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`bi*ot"ic</hw> <pr>(<icr/m`b<icr/*<ocr/t"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <grk>symbiwtiko`s</grk>.]</ety>
<fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to, or characterized by, or living
in, a state of symbiosis.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Sym`bi*ot"ic*al</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> -- <wf>Sym`bi*ot"ic*al*ly</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbol</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"bol</hw> <pr>(s<icr/m"b<ocr/l)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>symbolus</ets>, <ets>symbolum</ets>, Gr. <grk>sy`mbolon</grk> a
sign by which one knows or infers a thing, from <grk>symba`llein</grk>
to throw or put together, to compare; <grk>sy`n</grk> with +
<grk>ba`llein</grk> to throw: cf. F. <ets>symbole</ets>. Cf.
<er>Emblem</er>, <er>Parable</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A visible
sign or representation of an idea; anything which suggests an idea or
quality, or another thing, as by resemblance or by convention; an
emblem; a representation; a type; a figure; <as>as, the lion is the
<ex>symbol</ex> of courage; the lamb is the <ex>symbol</ex> of meekness
or patience</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A <qex>symbol</qex> is a sign included in the idea which it
represents, <it>e. g.</it>, an actual part chosen to represent the
whole, or a lower form or species used as the representative of a higher
in the same kind.</q> <rj><qau>Coleridge.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Any character used to represent a
quantity, an operation, a relation, or an abbreviation.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In crystallography, the <xex>symbol</xex> of a plane is
the numerical expression which defines its position relatively to the
assumed axes.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>An abstract or compendium of
faith or doctrine; a creed, or a summary of the articles of
religion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <ety>[Gr. <?/ contributions.]</ety> <def>That which is
thrown into a common fund; hence, an appointed or accustomed duty.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They do their work in the days of peace . . . and come to pay
their <qex>symbol</qex> in a war or in a plague.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Share; allotment.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The persons who are to be judged . . . shall all appear to receive
their <qex>symbol</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Jer. Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>An abbreviation standing for the
name of an element and consisting of the initial letter of the Latin or
New Latin name, or sometimes of the initial letter with a following one;
<as>as, <chform>C</chform> for carbon, <chform>Na</chform> for sodium
(Natrium), <chform>Fe</chform> for iron (Ferrum), <chform>Sn</chform>
for tin (Stannum), <chform>Sb</chform> for antimony (Stibium), etc.</as>
See the list of names and symbols under <er>Element</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In pure and organic chemistry there are symbols not only
for the elements, but also for their grouping in formulas, radicals, or
residues, as evidenced by their composition, reactions, synthesis, etc.
See the diagram of <cref>Benzene nucleus</cref>, under
<er>Benzene</er>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Emblem; figure; type. See
<er>Emblem</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbol</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"bol</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To symbolize.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Tennyson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolic</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*bol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>symbolique</ets>. See <er>Symbolic</er>, <pos>a.</pos>]</ety>
<fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>See <er>Symbolics</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolical</ent><br/
<ent>Symbolic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sym*bol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sym*bol"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>symbolicus</ets>, Gr.
<grk>symboliko`s</grk>: cf. F. <ets>symbolique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to a symbol or symbols; of the nature of a symbol; exhibiting
or expressing by resemblance or signs; representative; <as>as, the
figure of an eye is <ex>symbolic</ex> of sight and knowledge</as>.</def>
-- <wordforms><wf>Sym*bol"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sym*bol"ic*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The sacrament is a representation of Christ's death by such
<qex>symbolical</qex> actions as he himself appointed.</q> <rj><qau>Jer.
Taylor.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Symbolical delivery</b></col> <fld>(Law)</fld>, <cd>the
delivery of property sold by delivering something else as a symbol,
token, or representative of it.</cd> <au>Bouvier. Chitty.</au> --
<col><b>Symbolical philosophy</b></col>, <cd>the philosophy expressed by
hieroglyphics.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1462 --></p>

<p><ent>Symbolics</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*bol"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The study of
ancient symbols</def>; esp. <fld>(Theol.)</fld>, <def>that branch of
historic theology which treats of creeds and confessions of faith;
symbolism; -- called also <altname>symbolic</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolism</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"bol*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The
act of symbolizing, or the state of being symbolized; <as>as,
<ex>symbolism</ex> in Christian art is the representation of truth,
virtues, vices, etc., by emblematic colors, signs, and
forms</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A system of symbols or representations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The practice of
using symbols, or the system of notation developed thereby.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>A combining together of parts or ingredients.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The science of creeds;
symbolics.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolist</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"bol*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who employs
symbols.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolistical</ent><br/
<ent>Symbolistic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sym`bol*is"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sym`bol*is"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Characterized by the use of
symbols; <as>as, <ex>symbolistic</ex> poetry</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolization</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`bol*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>symbolisation</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act of symbolizing; symbolical
representation.</def> <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolize</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"bol*ize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Symbolized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Symbolizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>symboliser</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To have a resemblance of
qualities or properties; to correspond; to harmonize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The pleasing of color <qex>symbolizeth</qex> with the pleasing of
any single tone to the ear; but the pleasing of order doth
<qex>symbolize</qex> with harmony.</q> <rj><qau>Bacon.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They both <qex>symbolize</qex> in this, that they love to look
upon themselves through multiplying glasses.</q>
<rj><qau>Howell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To hold the same faith; to agree.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The believers in pretended miracles have always previously
<qex>symbolized</qex> with the performers of them.</q> <rj><qau>G. S.
Faber.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To use symbols; to represent ideas
symbolically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolize</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"bol*ize</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To make to agree
in properties or qualities.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To make representative of something; to regard or
treat as symbolic.</def> <ldquo/Some <xex>symbolize</xex> the same from
the mystery of its colors.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir T. Browne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To represent by a symbol or symbols.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbolizer</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"bol*i`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
symbolizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbological</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`bo*log"i*cal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to
a symbology; versed in, or characterized by, symbology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbologist</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*bol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
practices, or who is versed in, symbology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbology</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*bol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Symbol</ets> + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The art of
expressing by symbols.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symbranchii</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sym*bran"chi*i</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a gill.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>An order of slender eel-like fishes having the gill openings
confluent beneath the neck. The pectoral arch is generally attached to
the skull, and the entire margin of the upper jaw is formed by the
premaxillary. Called also <altname>Symbranchia</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symmetral</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"me*tral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Commensurable;
symmetrical.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symmetrian</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*me"tri*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One eminently
studious of symmetry of parts.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Sir P.
Sidney.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symmetric</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*met"ric</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Symmetrical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symmetrical</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*met"ric*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>sym<eacute/trique</ets>. See <er>Symmetry</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Involving or exhibiting symmetry; proportional in parts; having its
parts in due proportion as to dimensions; <as>as, a <ex>symmetrical</ex>
body or building</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Having the organs or parts of one
side corresponding with those of the other; having the parts in two or
more series of organs the same in number; exhibiting a symmetry. See
<er>Symmetry</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Having an equal
number of parts in the successive circles of floral organs; -- said of
flowers.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having a likeness in the form and size
of floral organs of the same kind; regular.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Math.)</fld> <def>Having a common measure;
commensurable.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Having corresponding parts or
relations.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A curve or a plane figure is <xex>symmetrical</xex> with
respect to a given line, and a line, surface, or solid with respect to a
plane, when for each point on one side of the line or plane there is a
corresponding point on the other side, so situated that the line joining
the two corresponding points is perpendicular to the line or plane and
is bisected by it. Two solids are <xex>symmetrical</xex> when they are
so situated with respect to an intervening plane that the several points
of their surfaces thus correspond to each other in position and
distance. In analysis, an expression is <xex>symmetrical</xex> with
respect to several letters when any two of them may change places
without affecting the expression; as, the expression
<mathex>a<exp>2</exp>b + ab<exp>2</exp> + a<exp>2</exp>c +
ac<exp>2</exp> + b<exp>2</exp>c + bc<exp>2</exp></mathex>, is
<xex>symmetrical</xex> with respect to the letters <it>a</it>,
<it>b</it>, <it>c</it>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Sym*met"ric*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos> --
<wf>Sym*met"ric*al*ness</wf>, <pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symmetrician</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`me*tri"cian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Symmetrian</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Holinshed.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symmetrist</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"me*trist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One eminently
studious of symmetry of parts.</def> <rj><au>Sir H.
Wotton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symmetrize</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"me*trize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Symmetrized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Symmetrizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
F. <ets>sym<eacute/triser</ets>.]</ety> <def>To make proportional in its
parts; to reduce to symmetry.</def> <rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symmetry</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"me*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>symmetria</ets>, Gr. <?/; <grk>sy`n</grk> with, together + <?/ a
measure: cf. F. <ets>sym<eacute/trie</ets>. See <er>Syn-</er>, and
<er>Meter</er> rhythm.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A due proportion of the
several parts of a body to each other; adaptation of the form or
dimensions of the several parts of a thing to each other; the union and
conformity of the members of a work to the whole.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>The law of likeness; similarity
of structure; regularity in form and arrangement; orderly and similar
distribution of parts, such that an animal may be divided into parts
which are structurally symmetrical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Bilateral symmetry</xex>, or
<xex>two-sidedness</xex>, in vertebrates, etc., is that in which the
body can be divided into symmetrical halves by a vertical plane passing
through the middle; <xex>radial symmetry</xex>, as in echinoderms, is
that in which the individual parts are arranged symmetrically around a
central axis; <xex>serial symmetry</xex>, or <xex>zonal symmetry</xex>,
as in earthworms, is that in which the segments or metameres of the body
are disposed in a zonal manner one after the other in a longitudinal
axis. This last is sometimes called <xex>metamerism</xex>.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Equality in the
number of parts of the successive circles in a flower.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>Likeness in the form and size of floral organs of the
same kind; regularity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Axis of symmetry</b></col>. <fld>(Geom.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Axis</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Respective symmetry</b></col>,
<cd>that disposition of parts in which only the opposite sides are equal
to each other.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympathetic</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`pa*thet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Sympathy</er>, and cf. <er>Pathetic</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Inclined to sympathy; sympathizing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Far wiser he, whose <qex>sympathetic</qex> mind<br/
Exults in all the good of all mankind.</q>
<rj><qau>Goldsmith.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Produced by, or expressive of, sympathy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Ope the sacred source of <qex>sympathetic</qex> tears.</q>
<rj><qau>Gray.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Produced by
sympathy; -- applied particularly to symptoms or affections. See
<er>Sympathy</er>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>Of or relating to the
sympathetic nervous system or some of its branches; produced by
stimulation on the sympathetic nervious system or some part of it;
<as>as, the <ex>sympathetic</ex> saliva, a modified form of saliva,
produced from some of the salivary glands by stimulation of a
sympathetic nerve fiber</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sympathetic ink</b></col>. <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <cd>See
under <er>Ink</er>.</cd> -- <col><b>Sympathetic nerve</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>any nerve of the sympathetic system; especially,
the axial chain of ganglions and nerves belonging to the sympathetic
system.</cd> -- <col><b>Sympathetic powder</b></col>
<fld>(Alchemy)</fld>, <cd>a kind of powder long supposed to be able to
cure a wound if applied to the weapon that inflicted it, or even to a
portion of the bloody clothes.</cd> <au>Dunglison.</au> --
<col><b>Sympathetic sounds</b></col> <fld>(Physics)</fld>, <cd>sounds
produced from solid bodies by means of vibrations which have been
communicated to them from some other sounding body, by means of the air
or an intervening solid.</cd> -- <col><b>Sympathetic system</b></col>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld>, <cd>a system of nerves and nerve ganglions connected
with the alimentary canal, the vascular system, and the glandular organs
of most vertebrates, and controlling more or less their actions. The
axial part of the system and its principal ganglions and nerves are
situated in the body cavity and form a chain of ganglions on each side
of the vertebral column connected with numerous other ganglions and
nerve plexuses.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympathetical</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`pa*thet"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Sympathetic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympathetically</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`pa*thet"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a sympathetic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympathist</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pa*thist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sympathizes; a sympathizer.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympathize</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pa*thize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Sympathized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Sympathizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[F.
<ets>sympathiser</ets>. See <er>Sympathy</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To have a common feeling, as of bodily pleasure or pain.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The mind will <qex>sympathize</qex> so much with the anguish and
debility of the body, that it will be too distracted to fix itself in
meditation.</q> <rj><qau>Buckminster.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To feel in consequence of what another feels; to be
affected by feelings similar to those of another, in consequence of
knowing the person to be thus affected.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Their countrymen . . . <qex>sympathized</qex> with their heroes in
all their adventures.</q> <rj><qau>Addison.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>To agree; to be in accord; to harmonize.</def>
<rj><au>Dryden.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympathize</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pa*thize</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To experience
together.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <ldquo/This <xex>sympathized</xex> .
. . error.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To ansew to; to correspond to.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Shak.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympathizer</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pa*thi`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
sympathizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympathy</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pa*thy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sympathies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>sympathie</ets>,
L. <ets>sympathia</ets>, Gr. <?/; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ suffering,
passion, fr. <?/, <?/, to suffer. See <er>Syn-</er>, and
<er>Pathos</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Feeling corresponding to that
which another feels; the quality of being affected by the affection of
another, with feelings correspondent in kind, if not in degree;
fellow-feeling.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They saw, but other sight instead -- a crowd<br/
Of ugly serpents! Horror on them fell,<br/
And horrid <qex>sympathy</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An agreement of affections or inclinations, or a
conformity of natural temperament, which causes persons to be pleased,
or in accord, with one another; <as>as, there is perfect
<ex>sympathy</ex> between them</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Kindness of feeling toward one who suffers; pity;
commiseration; compassion.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>I value myself upon <qex>sympathy</qex>, I hate and despise myself
for envy.</q> <rj><qau>Kames.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Physiol. & Med.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>The
reciprocal influence exercised by organs or parts on one another, as
shown in the effects of a diseased condition of one part on another part
or organ, as in the vomiting produced by a tumor of the brain.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The influence of a certain psychological state in one
person in producing a like state in another.</def> <note>In the original
1890 work, sense <it>(b)</it> was described as: <ldquo/That relation
which exists between different persons by which one of them produces in
the others a state or condition like that of himself. This is shown in
the tendency to yawn which a person often feels on seeing another yawn,
or the strong inclination to become hysteric experienced by many women
on seeing another person suffering with hysteria.<rdquo/</note><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source> <source>+PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>A tendency of inanimate things to unite, or to act
on each other; <as>as, the <ex>sympathy</ex> between the loadstone and
iron</as>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <def>Similarity of function, use office, or the
like.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The adverb has most <qex>sympathy</qex> with the verb.</q>
<rj><qau>Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Pity; fellow-feeling; compassion; commiseration;
tenderness; condolence; agreement.</syn> -- <usage><er>Sympathy</er>,
<er>Commiseration</er>. <xex>Sympathy</xex> is literally a
fellow-feeling with others in their varied conditions of joy or of
grief. This term, however, is now more commonly applied to a
fellow-feeling with others under affliction, and then coincides very
nearly with <xex>commiseration</xex>. In this case it is commonly
followed by <xex>for</xex>; <as>as, to feel <ex>sympathy</ex> for a
friend when we see him distressed</as>. The verb <xex>sympathize</xex>
is followed by <xex>with</xex>; <as>as, to <ex>sympathize</ex> with a
friend in his distresses or enjoyments</as>. <ldquo/Every man would be a
distinct species to himself, were there no <xex>sympathy</xex> among
individuals.<rdquo/ <au>South.</au> See <er>Pity</er>.</usage><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Fault,<br/
Acknowledged and deplored, in Adam wrought<br/
<qex>Commiseration</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympetalous</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*pet"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>sym-</ets> + <ets>petal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having
the petals united; gamopetalous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphonic</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*phon"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Symphonious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Relating to, or in the manner of,
symphony; <as>as, the <ex>symphonic</ex> form or style of
composition</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphonious</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*pho"ni*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From
<er>Symphony</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Agreeing in sound; accordant;
harmonious.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Followed with acclamation and the sound<br/
<qex>Symphonious</qex> of ten thousand harps.</q>
<rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Symphonic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphonist</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pho*nist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>symphoniste</ets>.]</ety> <def>A composer of symphonies.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphonize</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pho*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Symphonized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Symphonizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
agree; to be in harmony.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphony</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pho*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Symphonies</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>symphonie</ets>
(cf. It. <ets>sinfonia</ets>), L. <ets>symphonia</ets>, Gr. <?/;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a sound, the voice. See
<er>Phonetic</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A consonance or harmony of
sounds, agreeable to the ear, whether the sounds are vocal or
instrumental, or both.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The trumpets sound,<br/
And warlike <qex>symphony</qex> in heard around.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A stringed instrument formerly in use, somewhat
resembling the virginal.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With harp and pipe and <qex>symphony</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Chaucer.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An elaborate
instrumental composition for a full orchestra, consisting usually, like
the sonata, of three or four contrasted yet inwardly related movements,
as the allegro, the adagio, the minuet and trio, or scherzo, and the
finale in quick time. The term has recently been applied to large
orchestral works in freer form, with arguments or programmes to explain
their meaning, such as the <ldquo/symphonic poems<rdquo/ of Liszt. The
term was formerly applied to any composition for an orchestra, as
overtures, etc., and still earlier, to certain compositions partly
vocal, partly instrumental.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>An instrumental
passage at the beginning or end, or in the course of, a vocal
composition; a prelude, interlude, or postude; a ritornello.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphyla</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sym*phy"la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a clan.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An
order of small apterous insects having an elongated body, with three
pairs of thoracic and about nine pairs of abdominal legs. They are, in
many respects, intermediate between myriapods and true
insects.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphyseal</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*phys"e*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to to symphysis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphyseotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`phy*se*ot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>symphysis pubis</ets> + Gr. <?/ to cut.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>
<def>The operation of dividing the symphysis pubis for the purpose of
facilitating labor; -- formerly called the <xex>Sigualtian
section</xex>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>symphysotomy</asp>.]</altsp> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sym"phy*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Symphyses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
to make to grow together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to cause to grow;
to grow.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>An articulation
formed by intervening cartilage; <as>as, the pubic
<ex>symphysis</ex></as>.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The union or
coalescence of bones; also, the place of union or coalescence; <as>as,
the <ex>symphysis</ex> of the lower jaw</as>. Cf.
<er>Articulation</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphysotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`phy*sot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<def>Symphyseotomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symphytism</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"phy*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ grown
together.]</ety> <def>Coalescence; a growing into one with another
word.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Some of the phrasal adverbs have assumed the form of single words,
by that <qex>symphytism</qex> which naturally attaches these light
elements to each other.</q> <rj><qau>Earle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympiesometer</ent><br/
<hw>Sym`pi*e*som"e*ter</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
compression (fr. <?/ to press together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to
press, squeeze) + <ets>-meter</ets>.]</ety> <def>A sensitive kind of
barometer, in which the pressure of the atmosphere, acting upon a
liquid, as oil, in the lower portion of the instrument, compresses an
elastic gas in the upper part.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- Figure of a sympiesometer, with labeled parts. --> <note><hand/
The column of oil of a lower part <xex>BC</xex> of a glass tube
compresses hydrogen gas in the upper part <xex>AB</xex>, and is thus
measured on the scale <xex>pq</xex> by the position of a surface of the
oil in the tube. The scale <xex>pq</xex> is adjustable, and its index
must be set to the division on the scale <xex>rs</xex> corresponding to
the temperature indicated by the termometer <xex>t</xex>, in order to
correct for the effects of temperature on the gas. It is sensitive, and
convenient for use at sea, but inferior in accuracy to the mercurial
barometer.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symplectic</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*plec"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ plaiting
together, fr. <?/ to plait together.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Plaiting or joining together; -- said of a bone next above the
quadrate in the mandibular suspensorium of many fishes, which unites
together the other bones of the suspensorium.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The symplectic bone.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symploce</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sym"plo*ce</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/ an interweaving, fr. <?/ to twine together; <?/ + <?/ to
twine.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>The repetition of a word or phrase
at the beginning and another at the end of successive clauses; as,
<xex>Justice</xex> came down from heaven to view <xex>the earth</xex>;
<xex>Justice</xex> returned to heaven, and left <xex>the
earth</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1463 --></p>

<p><ent>Sympode</ent><br/
<hw>Sym"pode</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A
sympodium.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympodial</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*po"di*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Composed of superposed branches in such a way as to imitate a
simple axis; <as>as, a <ex>sympodial</ex> stem</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sympodium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sym*po"di*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Sympodia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/, dim. of <?/, <?/, foot.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>An axis or stem produced by dichotomous branching
in which one of the branches is regularly developed at the expense of
the other, as in the grapevine.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symposiac</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*po"si*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>symposiacus</ets>, Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to
compotations and merrymaking; happening where company is drinking
together; <as>as, <ex>symposiac</ex> meetings</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Symposiac</qex> disputations amongst my acquaintance.</q>
<rj><qau>Arbuthnot.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symposiac</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*po"si*ac</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A conference or conversation
of philosophers at a banquet; hence, any similar gathering.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symposiarch</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*po"si*arch</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, <?/;
<?/ a symposium + <?/ to be first, to rule.]</ety> <fld>(Gr.
Antiq.)</fld> <def>The master of a feast.</def> <-- = M.C. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symposiast</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*po"si*ast</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One engaged with
others at a banquet or merrymaking.</def> <rj><au>Sydney
Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symposion</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sym*po"si*on</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>A drinking together; a symposium.</def> <ldquo/Our
<xex>symposion</xex> last night.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Sir W.
Scott.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symposium</ent><br/
<hw>Sym*po"si*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Symposia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<grk>sympo`sion</grk> a drinking party, feast; <grk>sy`n</grk> with +
<grk>po`sis</grk> a drinking. See <er>Syn-</er>, and cf.
<er>Potable</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A drinking together; a merry
feast.</def> <rj><au>T. Warton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A collection of short essays by different authors on
a common topic; -- so called from the appellation given to the
philosophical dialogue by the Greeks.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symptom</ent><br/
<hw>Symp"tom</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>sympt<ocir/me</ets>, Gr. <?/ anything that has befallen one, a
chance, causality, symptom, fr. <?/ to fall together; <grk>sy`n</grk>
with + <?/ to fall; akin to Skr. <ets>pat</ets> to fly, to fall. See
<er>Syn-</er>, and cf. <er>Asymptote</er>, <er>Feather</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Any affection which accompanies
disease; a perceptible change in the body or its functions, which
indicates disease, or the kind or phases of disease; <as>as, the causes
of disease often lie beyond our sight, but we learn their nature by the
<ex>symptoms</ex> exhibited</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Like the sick man, we are expiring with all sorts of good
<qex>symptoms</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Swift.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A sign or token; that which indicates the existence
of something else; <as>as, corruption in elections is a <ex>symptom</ex>
of the decay of public virtue</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Mark; note; sign; token; indication.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symptomatical</ent><br/
<ent>Symptomatic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Symp`tom*at"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Symp`tom*at"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>symptomatique</ets>, Gr. <?/ causal.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to symptoms; happening in concurrence with something; being a
symptom; indicating the existence of something else.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Symptomatic</qex> of a shallow understanding and an unamiable
temper.</q> <rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>According to symptoms; <as>as, a
<ex>symptomatical</ex> classification of diseases</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p>-- <wordforms><wf>Symp`tom*at"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Symptomatology</ent><br/
<hw>Symp`tom*a*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<?/, symptom + <ets>-logy</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>symptomatologie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>The doctrine
of symptoms; that part of the science of medicine which treats of the
symptoms of diseases; semeiology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ It includes <xex>diagnosis</xex>, or the determination
of the disease from its symptoms; and <xex>prognosis</xex>, or the
determination of its probable course and event.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syn-</ent><br/
<hw>Syn-</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with.]</ety>
<def>A prefix meaning <xex>with</xex>, <xex>along with</xex>,
<xex>together</xex>, <xex>at the same time</xex>. <xex>Syn-</xex>
becomes <xex>sym-</xex> before <xex>p</xex>, <xex>b</xex>, and
<xex>m</xex>, and <xex>syl-</xex> before <xex>l</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synacmy</ent><br/
<ent>Synacme</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn*ac"me</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syn*ac"my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
}</mhw> <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. <ets>synacme</ets>. See <er>Syn-</er>,
and <er>Acme</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Synanthesis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syneresis</ent><br/
<ent>Synaeresis</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn*aer"e*sis</hw>, <hw>Syn*er"e*sis</hw> }</mhw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a taking or drawing
together, fr. <?/ to take together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to take,
to grasp. See <er>Syn-</er>, and <er>Heresy</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The union, or drawing together into one
syllable, of two vowels that are ordinarily separated in
syllabification; synecphonesis; -- the opposite of
<xex>diaeresis</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synagogical</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`a*gog"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to a synagogue.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synagogue</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"a*gogue</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F., from L.
<ets>synagoga</ets>, Gr. <?/ a bringing together, an assembly, a
synagogue, fr. <?/ to bring together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to
lead. See <er>Syn-</er>, and <er>Agent</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>A
congregation or assembly of Jews met for the purpose of worship, or the
performance of religious rites.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The building or place appropriated to the religious
worship of the Jews.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>The council of, probably, 120 members among the
Jews, first appointed after the return from the Babylonish captivity; --
called also the <altname>Great Synagogue</altname>, and sometimes,
though erroneously, the <altname>Sanhedrin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A congregation in the early Christian
church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>My brethren, . . . if there come into your <qex>synagogue</qex> a
man with a gold ring.</q> <rj><qau>James ii. 1,2 (Rev.
Ver.).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <def>Any assembly of men.</def> <mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synalepha</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`a*le"pha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. L.
<ets>synaloepha</ets>, Gr. <?/, from <?/ to melt together;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to besmear.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A
contraction of syllables by suppressing some vowel or diphthong at the
end of a word, before another vowel or diphthong; <as>as, <ex>th'
army</ex>, for <ex>the army</ex></as>.</def> <altsp>[Written also
<asp>synaloepha</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synallagmatic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`al*lag*mat"ic</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, from <?/ a
mutual agreement, contract, fr. <?/ to exchange, negotiate with;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to change.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld>
<def>Imposing reciprocal obligations upon the parties; <as>as, a
<ex>synallagmatic</ex> contract</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Bouvier.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synallaxine</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`al*lax"ine</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[From Gr. <?/
to associate with.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Having the outer and
middle toes partially united; -- said of certain birds related to the
creepers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synaloepha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`a*loe"pha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Synalepha</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synangium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*an"gi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Synangia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ + <?/ a
hollow vessel.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The divided part beyond
the pylangium in the aortic trunk of the amphibian heart.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Syn*an"gi*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synantherous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*an"ther*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + anther.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the
stamens united by their anthers; <as>as, <ex>synantherous</ex>
flowers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synanthesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`an*the"sis</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n`<acr/n*th<emac/"s<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with + Gr.
<grk>'a`nqhsis</grk> bloom.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>The
simultaneous maturity of the anthers and stigmas of a blossom.</def>
<rj><au>Gray.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synanthous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*an"thous</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n*<acr/n"th<ucr/s)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[Pref. <ets>syn-</ets> + Gr. <grk>'a`nqos</grk> flower.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having flowers and leaves which appear at the
same time; -- said of certain plants.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synanthrose</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*an"throse"</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n*<acr/n"thr<omac/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[From NL. <ets>Synantherae</ets> the Compositae; Gr.
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <grk>'anqh`ros</grk> blooming.]</ety>
<fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A variety of sugar, isomeric with sucrose, found
in the tubers of the Jerusalem artichoke (<spn>Helianthus
tuberosus</spn>), in the dahlia, and other Compositae.</def><-- ?? not
in Merck I. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synapta</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*ap"ta</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n*<acr/p"t<adot/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>synapto`s</grk> fastened together;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <grk>'a`ptein</grk> to fasten.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A genus of slender, transparent holothurians
which have delicate calcareous anchors attached to the dermal plates.
See <xex>Illustration</xex> in Appendix.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synaptase</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*ap"tase</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ fastened
together + dias<ets>tase</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>A ferment
resembling diastase, found in bitter almonds. Cf. <er>Amygdalin</er>,
and <er>Emulsin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synapticula</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`ap*tic"u*la</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Synapticulae</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
dim. from Gr. <?/ fastened together.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One
of numerous calcareous processes which extend between, and unite, the
adjacent septa of certain corals, especially of the fungian
corals.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synarchy</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"ar*chy</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<adot/r*k<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[Gr. <grk>synarchi`a</grk>, fr. <grk>syna`rchein</grk> to rule
jointly with; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <grk>'a`rchein</grk> to
rule.]</ety> <def>Joint rule or sovereignty.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Stackhouse.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synartesis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`ar*te"sis</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n`<aum/r*t<emac/"s<icr/s)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/ a fastening together, fr. <?/ to
fasten together.]</ety> <def>A fastening or knitting together; the state
of being closely jointed; close union.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Coleridge.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synarthrodia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`ar*thro"di*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Synarthrosis.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Syn`ar*thro"di*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synarthrosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`ar*thro"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Synarthroses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a being jointed together, fr. <?/ to link or joint together;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a joint.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Immovable articulation by close union, as in sutures. It sometimes
includes symphysial articulations also. See the Note under
<er>Articulation</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synastry</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"as*try</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a star.]</ety> <def>Concurrence of starry
position or influence; hence, similarity of condition, fortune, etc., as
prefigured by astrological calculation.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Motley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synaxis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*ax"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/,
fr. <?/ to bring together. See <er>Synagogue</er>.]</ety> <def>A
congregation; also, formerly, the Lord's Supper.</def> <rj><au>Jer.
Taylor.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncarp</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"carp</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.
<ets>syncarpium</ets>. See <er>Syncarpous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>A kind of aggregate fruit in which the ovaries cohere in a solid
mass, with a slender receptacle, as in the magnolia; also, a similar
multiple fruit, as a mulberry.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncarpium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*car"pi*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Syncarpia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Syncarp</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncarpous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*car"pous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + Gr. <?/ a fruit.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Composed of several carpels consolidated into one ovary.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncategorematic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*cat`e*gor`e*mat"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<?/; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a predicate. See <er>Syn-</er>, and
<er>Categorematic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>Not capable of
being used as a term by itself; -- said of words, as an adverb or
preposition.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchondrosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`chon*dro"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Synchondroses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ cartilage.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>An immovable articulation in which the union is
formed by cartilage.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Syn`chon*dro"si*al</wf>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchondrotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`chon*drot"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
union by cartilage + <?/ to cut.]</ety> <fld>(Surg.)</fld>
<def>Symphyseotomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchoresis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`cho*re"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a going.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld>
<def>A concession made for the purpose of retorting with greater
force.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"chro*nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Synchronous</er>.]</ety> <def>Happening at, or belonging to, the
same time; synchronous; simultaneous.</def> <rj><au>Dr. H.
More.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"chro*nal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A synchronal thing or
event.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronical</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*chron"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>synchronique</ets>.]</ety> <def>Happening at the same time;
synchronous.</def> <au>Boyle.</au> --
<wordforms><wf>Syn*chron"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronism</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"chro*nism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr.
<?/ to be contemporary with, from <?/ synchronous. See
<er>Synchronous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The concurrence of events
in time; simultaneousness.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>The tabular arrangement of historical events and
personages, according to their dates.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Paint.)</fld> <def>A representation, in the same
picture, of two or events which occured at different times.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronistic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`chro*nis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or
pertaining to synchronism; arranged according to correspondence in time;
<as>as, <ex>synchronistic</ex> tables</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronization</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`chro*ni*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act of
synchronizing; concurrence of events in respect to time.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronize</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"chro*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Synchronized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. &
vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Synchronizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph>
<ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety> <def>To agree in time; to be
simultaneous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The path of this great empire, through its arch of progress,
<qex>synchronized</qex> with that of Christianity.</q> <rj><qau>De
Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronize</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"chro*nize</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To assign to
the same date or period of time; <as>as, to <ex>synchronize</ex> two
events of Greek and Roman history</as>.</def> <ldquo/Josephus
<xex>synchronizes</xex> Nisan with the Egyptian Pharmus.<rdquo/
<rj><au>W. L. Bevan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To cause to agree in time; <as>as, to
<ex>synchronize</ex> the movements of different machines; to
<ex>synchronize</ex> clocks.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronology</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`chro*nol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + Gr. <?/ time + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Contemporaneous chronology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchronous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"chro*nous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ time. Cf. <er>Chronicle</er>.]</ety>
<def>Happening at the same time; simultaneous.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Syn"chro*nous*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchrony</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"chro*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The concurrence of
events in time; synchronism.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Geological contemporaneity is the same as chronological
<qex>synchrony</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>Huxley.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synchysis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn"chy*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ to confound; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to pour.]</ety>
<def>A derangement or confusion of any kind, as of words in a sentence,
or of humors in the eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Sparkling synchysis</b></col> <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <cd>a
condition in which the vitreous humor is softened and contains sparkling
scales of cholesterin.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synclastic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*clas"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + Gr. <grk>kla^n</grk> to break.]</ety> <fld>(Math.
Physics)</fld> <def>Curved toward the same side in all directions; --
said of surfaces which in all directions around any point bend away from
a tangent plane toward the same side, as the surface of a sphere; --
opposed to anticlastic.</def> <rj><au>Sir W. Thomson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synclinal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*cli"nal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ to
incline together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to incline.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Inclined downward from opposite directions, so as to
meet in a common point or line.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>Formed by strata dipping toward a
common line or plane; <as>as, a <ex>synclinal</ex> trough or valley; a
<ex>synclinal</ex> fold; -- opposed to
<ex>anticlinal</ex>.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ A downward flexure in the case of folded rocks makes a
<xex>synclinal</xex> axis, and the alternating upward flexure an
<xex>anticlinal</xex> axis.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synclinal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*cli"nal</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld> <def>A synclinal
fold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncline</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*cline"</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>A synclinal fold.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synclinical</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*clin"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<def>Synclinal.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synclinorium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`cli*no"ri*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Synclinoria</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ to lay together + <?/ mountain.]</ety> <fld>(Geol.)</fld>
<def>A mountain range owing its origin to the progress of a
geosynclinal, and ending in a catastrophe of displacement and
upturning.</def> <rj><au>Dana.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncopal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"co*pal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
syncope; resembling syncope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncopate</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"co*pate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>Syncopated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Syncopating</conjf>.]</vmorph> <ety>[LL.
<ets>syncopatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>syncopare</ets> to syncopate, to
swoon. See <er>Syncope</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld>
<def>To contract, as a word, by taking one or more letters or syllables
from the middle; <as>as, <ldquo/Gloster<rdquo/ is a <ex>syncopated</ex>
form of <ldquo/Gloucester</as>.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>To commence, as a tone, on an
unaccented part of a measure, and continue it into the following
accented part, so that the accent is driven back upon the weak part and
the rhythm drags.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncopation</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`co*pa"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The act of syncopating; the contraction of a
word by taking one or more letters or syllables from the middle;
syncope.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The act of syncopating; a peculiar
figure of rhythm, or rhythmical alteration, which consists in welding
into one tone the second half of one beat with the first half of the
beat which follows.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncope</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"co*pe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>syncope</ets>, <ets>syncopa</ets>, Gr. <?/ a cutting up, a syncope;
akin to <?/ to beat together, to cut up, cut short, weavy;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to strike, cut.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>An elision or retrenchment of one or more
letters or syllables from the middle of a word; as, <xex>ne'er</xex> for
<xex>never</xex>, <xex>ev'ry</xex> for <xex>every</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>Same as
<er>Syncopation</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A fainting, or swooning. See
<er>Fainting</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>A pause or cessation; suspension.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Revely, and dance, and show,<br/
Suffer a <qex>syncope</qex> and solemn pause.</q>
<rj><qau>Cowper.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncopist</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"co*pist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
syncopates.</def> <rj><au>Addison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncopize</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"co*pize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <def>To
syncopate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncotyledonous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*cot`y*led"on*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + <ets>cotyledonous</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld>
<def>Having united cotyledonous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncretic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*cret"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Uniting and
blending together different systems, as of philosophy, morals, or
religion.</def> <rj><au>Smart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncretism</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"cre*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
to make two parties join against a third: cf. F.
<ets>syncr<eacute/tisme</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Attempted union
of principles or parties irreconcilably at variance with each
other.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>He is plotting a carnal <qex>syncretism</qex>, and attempting the
reconcilement of Christ and Belial.</q> <rj><qau>Baxter.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Syncretism</qex> is opposed to eclecticism in philosophy.</q>
<rj><qau>Krauth-Fleming.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Philol.)</fld> <def>The union or fusion into one of
two or more originally different inflectional forms, as of two
cases.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncretist</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"cre*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>syncr<eacute/tiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who attempts to unite
principles or parties which are irreconcilably at variance;</def>
specifically <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld>, <def>an adherent of George
Calixtus and other Germans of the seventeenth century, who sought to
unite or reconcile the Protestant sects with each other and with the
Roman Catholics, and thus occasioned a long and violent controversy in
the Lutheran church.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncretistic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`cre*tis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Pertaining to, or characterized by, syncretism; <as>as, a
<ex>syncretistic</ex> mixture of the service of Jehovah and the worship
of idols</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to Syncretists.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syncrisis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn"cri*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ a comparison; <?/ together + <?/ to judge.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld>
<def>A figure of speech in which opposite things or persons are
compared.</def> <rj><au>Crabb.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1464 --></p>

<p><ent>Syncytium</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*cy"ti*um</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Syncitia</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/ together
+ <?/ a hollow vessel.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Tissue
in which the cell or partition walls are wholly wanting and the cell
bodies fused together, so that the tissue consists of a continuous mass
of protoplasm in which nuclei are imbedded, as in ordinary striped
muscle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>The ectoderm of a
sponge.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndactyle</ent><br/
<ent>Syndactyl</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn*dac"tyl</hw>, <hw>Syn*dac"tyle</hw> }</mhw> <pr>(?)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <ety>[<ets>Syn-</ets> + Gr. <?/ finger, toe.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool. & Med.)</fld> <def>Having two or more digits wholly or
partly united. See <er>Syndactylism</er>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndactyle</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*dac"tyle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + Gr. <?/ finger, toe: cf. F.
<ets>syndactyle</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any bird having
syndactilous feet.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndactylic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*dac*tyl"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Syndactilous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndactylous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*dac"tyl*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the toes firmly united together for some distance, and
without an intermediate web, as the kingfishers; gressorial.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndesmography</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`des*mog"ra*phy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
band, bond + <ets>-graphy</ets>.]</ety> <def>A description of the
ligaments; syndesmology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndesmology</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`des*mol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
band, bond + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That part of anatomy which
treats of ligaments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndesmosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`des*mo"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Syndesmoses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/ a bond; <?/ together + <?/ a bond, fr. <?/ to bind.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>An articulation formed by means of
ligaments.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndetical</ent><br/
<ent>Syndetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn*det"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syn*det"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, from <?/ to bind
together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to bind; cf.
<er>Asyndetic</er>.]</ety> <def>Connecting; conjunctive; <as>as,
<ex>syndetic</ex> words or connectives; <ex>syndetic</ex> references in
a dictionary.</as></def> -- <wordforms><wf>Syn*det"ic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>With the <qex>syndetic</qex> juxtaposition of distinct members,
the article is not often repeated.</q> <rj><qau>C. J. Grece (Trans.
Maetzner's Gram.).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"dic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>syndictus</ets>, Gr. <?/ helping in a court of justice, advocate;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ justice, akin to <?/ to show: cf. F.
<ets>syndic</ets>. See <er>Teach</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An
officer of government, invested with different powers in different
countries; a magistrate.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>An agent of a corporation, or of
any body of men engaged in a business enterprise; an advocate or patron;
an assignee.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In France, <xex>syndics</xex> are appointed by the
creditors of a bankrupt to manage the property. Almost all the companies
in Paris, the university, and the like, have their <xex>syndics</xex>.
The university of Cambridge, Eng., has its <xex>syndics</xex>, who are
chosen from the senate to transact special business, such as the
regulation of fees, the framing of laws, etc.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Civil Law)</fld> <def>One appointed to manage an
estate, essentially as a trustee, under English law.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndical</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"dic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Consisting of, or pert. to, a syndic.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining to, or of the nature of,
syndicalism.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndicalism</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"dic*al*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>syndicalisme</ets>.]</ety> <def>The theory, plan, or practice of
trade-union action (originally as advocated and practiced by the French
Conf<eacute/d<eacute/ration G<eacute/n<eacute/rale du Travail) which
aims to abolish the present political and social system by means of the
general strike (as distinguished from the local or sectional strike) and
direct action of whatever kind (as distinguished from action which takes
effect only through the medium of political action) -- <xex>direct
action</xex> including any kind of action that is directly effective,
whether it be a simple strike, a peaceful public demonstration,
sabotage, or revolutionary violence. By the general strike and direct
action syndicalism aims to establish a social system in which the means
and processes of production are in the control of local organizations of
workers, who are manage them for the common good.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndicalist</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"dic*al*ist</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who advocates or
practices syndicalism.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Syn`dic*al*is"tic</wf>
<pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndicate</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"di*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>syndicat</ets>, LL. <ets>syndicatus</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>The office or jurisdiction of a syndic; a council, or body of
syndics.</def> <rj><au>Bp. Burnet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An association of persons officially authorized to
undertake some duty or to negotiate some business; also, an association
of persons who combine to carry out, on their own account, a financial
or industrial project; <as>as, a <ex>syndicate</ex> of bankers formed to
take up and dispose of an entire issue of government
bonds</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>A more or less organized association of criminals
controlling some aspects of criminal activity, in a specific area or
country-wide; -- used loosely as a synonym for <altname>organized
crime</altname> or the <altname>mafia</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Journalism)</fld> <def>a commercial organization
that purchases various journalistic items, such as articles, columns, or
comic strips, from their individual creators, and resells them to
newspapers or other periodicals for simultaneous publication over a wide
area.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndicate</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"di*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[LL.
<ets>syndicatus</ets>, p. p. of <ets>syndicare</ets> to censure.]</ety>
<def>To judge; to censure.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndicate</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"di*cate</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>syndicated</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>syndicating</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>To combine or form into, or manage as, a syndicate.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To acquire or control for or by, or to subject to
the management of, a syndicate; <as>as, <ex>syndicated</ex>
newspapers</as>.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Journalism)</fld> <def>to purchase various
journalistic items, such as articles, columns, or comic strips, from
their individual creators, and resell them to numerous periodicals for
simultaneous publication over a wide area; <as>a <ex>syndicated</ex>
columnist</as>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndicate</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"di*cate</hw>, <pos>v. i.</pos> <def>To unite to form a
syndicate.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndication</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`di*ca"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Act or process
of syndicating or forming a syndicate.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndrome</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn"dro*me</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a course, a running.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Concurrence.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Glanvill.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A group of symptoms occurring
together that are characteristic and indicative of some underlying
cause, such as a disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>generally, a pattern of characteristics or behaviors
occurring together, that are sufficiently common and distinctive to
indicate that they are due to a known cause.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syndyasmian</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`dy*as"mi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr.
<grk>syndyasmo`s</grk> a pairing, fr. <grk>syndya`zein</grk> to
pair.]</ety> <def>Pertaining to the state of pairing together sexually;
-- said of animals during periods of procreation and while rearing their
offspring.</def> <rj><au>Morgan.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syne</ent><br/
<hw>Syne</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Since</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Afterwards; since; ago.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or Scot.]</mark> <rj><au>R. of Brunne.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Late, -- as opposed to <xex>soon</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>[Each rogue] shall be discovered either soon or
<qex>syne</qex>.</q> <rj><qau>W. Hamilton (Life of
Wallace).</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syne</ent><br/
<hw>Syne</hw>, <pos>conj.</pos> <def>Since; seeing.</def>
<mark>[Scot.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synecdoche</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*ec"do*che</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n*<ecr/k"d<osl/*k<esl/)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>synecdoche</ets>, Gr.
<grk>synekdochh`</grk>, fr. to receive jointly; <grk>sy`n</grk> with +
<?/ to receive; <?/ out + <?/ to receive.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld>
<def>A figure or trope by which a part of a thing is put for the whole
(as, fifty <xex>sail</xex> for fifty <xex>ships</xex>), or the whole for
a part (as, the smiling <xex>year</xex> for <xex>spring</xex>), the
species for the genus (as, <xex>cutthroat</xex> for
<xex>assassin</xex>), the genus for the species (as, a
<xex>creature</xex> for a <xex>man</xex>), the name of the material for
the thing made, etc.</def> <rj><au>Bain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synecdochical</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`ec*doch"ic*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Expressed by
synecdoche; implying a synecdoche.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Isis is used for Themesis by a <qex>synecdochical</qex> kind of
speech, or by a poetical liberty, in using one for another.</q>
<rj><qau>Drayton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synecdochically</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`ec*doch"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>By
synecdoche.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synechia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*e"chi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ to hold together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to
hold.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A disease of the eye, in which the
iris adheres to the cornea or to the capsule of the crystalline
lens.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synecphonesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*ec`pho*ne"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to utter together.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A
contraction of two syllables into one; synizesis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synedral</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*e"dral</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ sitting
with; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <grk>"e`dra</grk> seat.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Growing on the angles of a stem, as the leaves in
some species of Selaginella.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synentognathi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`en*tog"na*thi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos>
<ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <grk>'ento`s</grk> within +
<grk>gna`qos</grk> jaw.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>An order of
fishes, resembling the Physoclisti, without spines in the dorsal, anal,
and ventral fins. It includes the true flying fishes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synepy</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"e*py</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a word.]</ety> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>The
interjunction, or joining, of words in uttering the clauses of
sentences.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syneresis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*er"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Synaeresis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synergetic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`er*get"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr.
<?/ to work together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <grk>'e`rgon</grk>
work.]</ety> <def>Working together; cooperating; <as>as,
<ex>synergetic</ex> muscles</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synergism</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"er*gism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Synergetic</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Theol.)</fld> <def>The doctrine or
theory, attributed to Melanchthon, that in the regeneration of a human
soul there is a cooperation, or joint agency, on the part both of God
and of man.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 2. Same as synergy, 2. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synergist</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"er*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>synergiste</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who holds the
doctrine of synergism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A remedy which has an action
similar to that of another remedy, and hence increases the efficiency of
that remedy when combined with it.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- 3. (Biochemistry) A chemical compound which exhibits a
synergistic effect on some biochemical or physiological action, in
combination with another compound. [A supertype of def. 2.] --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synergistic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`er*gis"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of
or pertaining to synergism.</def> <ldquo/A <xex>synergistic</xex> view
of regeneration.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Shedd.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Cooperating; synergetic.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synergy</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"er*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/. See
<er>Synergetic</er>.]</ety> <sn>2.</sn> <def>Combined action</def>;
especially <fld>(Med.)</fld>, <def>the combined healthy action of every
organ of a particular system; <as>as, the digestive
<ex>synergy</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An effect of the interaction of the actions of two
agents such that the result of the combined action is greater than
expected as a simple additive combination of the two agents acting
separately; -- also called <altname>synergism.</altname>. Opposite to
<ant>antagonism</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>PJC</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synesis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/
intelligence.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A construction in which
adherence to some element in the sense causes a departure from strict
syntax, as in <ldquo/Philip went down to Samaria and preached Christ
unto <xex>them</xex>.<rdquo/</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syngenesia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`ge*ne"si*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with, together + <?/ generation, birth.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>A Linnaean class of plants in which the stamens
are united by the anthers.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syngenesious</ent><br/
<ent>Syngenesian</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn`ge*ne"sian</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syn`ge*ne"sious</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having the
stamens united by the anthers; of or pertaining to the
Syngenesia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syngenesis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*gen"e*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + <ets>genesis</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>A
theory of generation in which each germ is supposed to contain the germs
of all subsequent generations; -- the opposite of
<contr>epigenesis</contr>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syngnathi</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syng"na*thi</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ jaw.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
suborder of lophobranch fishes which have an elongated snout and lack
the ventral and first dorsal fins. The pipefishes and sea horses are
examples.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Syng"na*thous</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syngraph</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"graph</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>syngrapha</ets>, Gr. <?/; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to
write.]</ety> <fld>(Law)</fld> <def>A writing signed by both or all the
parties to a contract or bond.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synizesis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`i*ze"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ to sit together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to sit.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>An obliteration of the pupil of the
eye.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>A contraction of two syllables
into one; synecphonesis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synneorosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`neo*ro"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Synneuroses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <?/; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a sinew, ligament.]</ety>
<fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Syndesmosis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synocha</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn"o*cha</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ a holding together. See <er>Synechia</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>See <er>Synochus</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synochal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"o*chal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to synocha; like synocha.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synochus</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn"o*chus</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., from Gr.
<?/ joined together.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A continuous
fever.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Synocha</xex> and <xex>synochus</xex> were used as
epithets of two distinct types of fever, but in different senses at
different periods. The same disease is placed under synocha by one
author, under synochus by another.</note> <rj><au>Quain.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synocil</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"o*cil</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + <ets>cil</ets>ium.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A
sense organ found in certain sponges. It consists of several filaments,
each of which arises from a single cell.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synod</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"od</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<ucr/d)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>synodus</ets>, Gr. <grk>sy`nodos</grk> a meeting; <grk>sy`n</grk>
with + <grk>"odo`s</grk> a way; cf. AS. <ets>sino<edh/</ets>,
<ets>seno<edh/</ets>, F. <ets>synode</ets>, both from the Latin.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl. Hist.)</fld> <def>An ecclesiastic council or
meeting to consult on church matters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ <xex>Synods</xex> are of four kinds: 1.
<xex>General</xex>, or <xex>ecumenical</xex>, which are composed of
bishops from different nations; -- commonly called <xex>general
council</xex>. 2. <xex>National</xex>, composed of bishops of one nation
only. 3. <xex>Provincial</xex>, in which the bishops of only one
province meet; -- called also <xex>convocations</xex>. 4.
<xex>Diocesan</xex>, a synod in which the bishop of the diocese or his
representative presides. Among Presbyterians, a <xex>synod</xex> is
composed of several adjoining presbyteries. The members are the
ministers and a ruling elder from each parish.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An assembly or council having civil authority; a
legislative body.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>It hath in solemn <qex>synods</qex> been decreed,<br/
Both by the Syracusians and ourselves,<br/
To admit no traffic to our adverse towns.</q>
<rj><qau>Shak.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Parent of gods and men, propitious Jove!<br/
And you, bright <qex>synod</qex> of the powers above.</q>
<rj><qau>Dryden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>A conjunction of two or more of
the heavenly bodies.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synodal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"od*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>synodalis</ets>: cf. F. <ets>synodal</ets>.]</ety>
<def>Synodical.</def> <rj><au>Milton.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synodal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"od*al</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Ch. of Eng.)</fld>
<def>A tribute in money formerly paid to the bishop or archdeacon, at
the time of his Easter visitation, by every parish priest, now made to
the ecclesiastical commissioners; a procuration.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q><qex>Synodals</qex> are due, of common right, to the bishop
only.</q> <rj><qau>Gibson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A constitution made in a provincial or diocesan
synod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synodical</ent><br/
<ent>Synodic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn*od"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syn*od"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>synodicus</ets>, Gr.
<?/: cf. F. <ets>synodique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Eccl.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to a synod; transacted in, or authorized by, a
synod; <as>as, <ex>synodical</ex> proceedings or forms</as>.</def>
<ldquo/A <xex>synodical</xex> epistle.<rdquo/ <rj><au>Bp.
Stillingfleet.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld> <def>Pertaining to conjunction,
especially to the period between two successive conjunctions; extending
from one conjunction, as of the moon or a planet with the sun, to the
next; <as>as, a <ex>synodical</ex> month (see <ex>Lunar month</ex>,
under <er>Month</er>); the <ex>synodical</ex> revolution of the moon or
a planet.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synodically</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*od"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a synodical manner; in
a synod; by the authority of a synod.</def>
<ldquo/<xex>Synodically</xex> agreed upon.<rdquo/ <rj><au>R.
Nelson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synodist</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"od*ist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>An adherent to a
synod.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These <qex>synodists</qex> thought fit in Latin as yet to veil
their decrees from vulgar eyes.</q> <rj><qau>Fuller.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synoecious</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*oe"cious</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + Gr. <?/ house.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having
stamens and pistil in the same head, or, in mosses, having antheridia
and archegonia on the same receptacle.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synomocy</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*om"o*cy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/
to swear with or together; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to swear.]</ety>
<def>Sworn brotherhood; a society in ancient Greece nearly resembling a
modern political club.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonym</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"o*nym</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n"<osl/*n<icr/m)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Synonyms</plw>
<pr>(s<icr/n"<osl/*n<icr/mz)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[F. <ets>synonyme</ets>,
L. <ets>synonyma</ets>, pl. of <ets>synonymum</ets>, Gr.
<grk>synw`nymon</grk>. See <er>Synonymous</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>One of two or more words (commonly words of the same language)
which are equivalents of each other; one of two or more words which have
very nearly the same signification, and therefore may often be used
interchangeably. See under <er>Synonymous</er>.</def> <altsp>[Written
also <asp>synonyme</asp>.]</altsp><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>All languages tend to clear themselves of <qex>synonyms</qex> as
intellectual culture advances, the superfluous words being taken up and
appropriated by new shades and combinations of thought evolved in the
progress of society.</q> <rj><qau>De Quincey.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>His name has thus become, throughout all civilized countries, a
<qex>synonym</qex> for probity and philanthropy.</q>
<rj><qau>Macaulay.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>In popular literary acceptation, and as employed in special
dictionaries of such words, <qex>synonyms</qex> are words sufficiently
alike in general signification to be liable to be confounded, but yet so
different in special definition as to require to be distinguished.</q>
<rj><au>G. P. Marsh.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>An incorrect or incorrectly applied scientific name,
as a new name applied to a species or genus already properly named, or a
specific name preoccupied by that of another species of the same genus;
-- so used in the system of nomenclature (which see) in which the
correct scientific names of certain natural groups (usually genera,
species, and subspecies) are regarded as determined by
priority.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>One of two or more words corresponding in meaning
but of different languages; a heteronym.</def> <mark>[Rare]</mark><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonyma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*on"y*ma</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n*<ocr/n"<icr/*m<adot/)</pr>,
<pos>n. pl.</pos> <ety>[L.]</ety> <def>Synonyms.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Fuller.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*on"y*mal</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n*<ocr/n"<icr/*m<ait/l)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos> <def>Synonymous.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymally</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*on"y*mal*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>Synonymously.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonyme</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"o*nyme</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Same as
<er>Synonym</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`o*nym"ic</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n`<ocr/*n<icr/m"<icr/k)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <ets>synonymik</ets>. See
<er>Synonymous</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The science, or the
scientific treatment, of synonymous words.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymical</ent><br/
<ent>Synonymic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn`o*nym"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syn`o*nym"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to synonyms,
or synonymic; synonymous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymicon</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`o*nym"i*con</hw>
<pr>(s<icr/n`<ocr/*n<icr/m"<icr/*k<ocr/n)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[NL.]</ety> <def>A dictionary of synonyms.</def> <rj><au>C. J.
Smith.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymist</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*on"y*mist</hw> <pr>(s<icr/n*<ocr/n"<icr/*m<icr/st)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>synonymiste</ets>.]</ety> <def>One who
collects or explains synonyms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymize</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*on"y*mize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp.
& p. p.</pos> <conjf>Synonymized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>Synonymizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <def>To
express by a synonym or synonyms; to give the synonym or synonyms
corresponding to.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>This word <ldquo/fortis<rdquo/ we may <qex>synonymize</qex> after
all these fashions: stout, hardy, valiant, doughty, courageous,
adventurous, brave, bold, daring, intrepid.</q>
<rj><qau>Camden.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*on"y*mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with, together + <?/, <?/, name. See <er>Syn-</er>, and
<er>Name</er>.]</ety> <def>Having the character of a synonym; expressing
the same thing; conveying the same, or approximately the same,
idea.</def> -- <wordforms><wf>Syn*on"y*mous*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These words consist of two propositions, which are not distinct in
sense, but one and the same thing variously expressed; for wisdom and
understanding are <qex>synonymous</qex> words here.</q>
<rj><qau>Tillotson.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Identical; interchangeable. --
<er>Synonymous</er>, <er>Identical</er>. If no words are
<xex>synonymous</xex> except those which are <xex>identical</xex> in use
and meaning, so that the one can in all cases be substituted for the
other, we have scarcely ten such words in our language. But the term
more properly denotes that the words in question approach so near to
each other, that, in many or most cases, they can be used
interchangeably. 1. Words may thus coincide in <xex>certain</xex>
connections, and so be interchanged, when they can not be interchanged
in other connections; thus we may speak either <xex>strength</xex> of
mind or of <xex>force</xex> of mind, but we say the <xex>force</xex>
(not <xex>strength</xex>) of gravitation. 2. Two words may differ
slightly, but this difference may be unimportant to the speaker's
object, so that he may freely interchange them; thus it makes but little
difference, in most cases, whether we speak of a man's having
<xex>secured</xex> his object or having <xex>attained</xex> his object.
For these and other causes we have numerous words which may, in many
cases or connections, be used interchangeably, and these are properly
called <xex>synonyms</xex>. <xex>Synonymous</xex> words <ldquo/are words
which, with great and essential resemblances of meaning, have, at the
same time, small, subordinate, and partial differences, -- these
differences being such as either originally and on the ground of their
etymology inhered in them; or differences which they have by usage
acquired in the eyes of all; or such as, though nearly latent now, they
are capable of receiving at the hands of wise and discreet masters of
the tongue. <xex>Synonyms</xex> are words of like significance in the
main, but with a certain unlikeness as well.<rdquo/
<au>Trench.</au></syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synonymy</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*on"y*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>synonymia</ets>, Gr. <?/ a synonym: cf. F.
<ets>synonymie</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>The quality of being
synonymous; sameness of meaning.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>A system of synonyms.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Rhet.)</fld> <def>A figure by which synonymous
words are used to amplify a discourse.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synopsis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*op"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Synopses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., from Gr. <?/;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with, together + <?/ a sight, view, from the root seen
in E. <ets>optic</ets>.]</ety> <def>A general view, or a collection of
heads or parts so arranged as to exhibit a general view of the whole; an
abstract or summary of a discourse; a syllabus; a conspectus.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>That the reader may see in one view the exactness of the method,
as well as force of the argument, I shall here draw up a short
<qex>synopsis</qex> of this epistle.</q> <rj><qau>Bp.
Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><syn><b>Syn.</b> -- Abridgment; compendium; epitome; abstract;
summary; syllabus; conspectus. See <er>Abridgment</er>.</syn><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synoptical</ent><br/
<ent>Synoptic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn*op"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syn*op"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>synoptique</ets>. See <er>Synopsis</er>.]</ety> <def>Affording a
general view of the whole, or of the principal parts of a thing; <as>as,
a <ex>synoptic</ex> table; a <ex>synoptical</ex> statement of an
argument</as>.</def> <ldquo/The <xex>synoptic</xex> Gospels.<rdquo/
<au>Alford.</au> -- <wordforms><wf>Syn*op"tic*al*ly</wf>,
<pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synoptic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*op"tic</hw>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One of the first three Gospels
of the New Testament. See <er>Synoptist</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synoptist</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*op"tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Any one of the
authors of the three synoptic Gospels, which give a history of our
Lord's life and ministry, in distinction from the writer of John's
Gospel, which gives a fuller record of his teachings.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synosteology</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*os`te*ol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + Gr. <?/ bone + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That part
of anatomy which treats of joints; arthrology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synosteosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*os`te*o"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Synosteoses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL.,
fr. Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ bone.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Union by means of bone; the complete closing up and obliteration of
sutures.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synostosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`os*to"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Synosteosis</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synovia</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*o"vi*a</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., perhaps
fr. Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with + L. <ets>ovum</ets> egg: cf. F.
<ets>synovie</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>A transparent,
viscid, lubricating fluid which contains mucin and secreted by synovial
membranes; synovial fluid.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1465 --></p>

<p><ent>Synovial</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*o"vi*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>synovial</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
synovia; secreting synovia.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Synovial capsule</b></col>, <cd>a closed sac of synovial
membrane situated between the articular surfaces at diarthrodial
joints.</cd> -- <col><b>Synovial fluid</b></col>, <cd>synovia.</cd> --
<col><b>Synovial membrane</b></col>, <cd>the dense and very smooth
connective tissue membrane which secretes synovia and surrounds synovial
capsules and other synovial cavities.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synovitis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn`o*vi"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL. See
<er>Synovia</er>, <er>-itis</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inflammation of the synovial membrane.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synpelmous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*pel"mous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + <?/ the sole of the foot.]</ety> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Having the two main flexor tendons of the toes blended
together.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synsepalous</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*sep"al*ous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + <ets>sepal</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Bot.)</fld> <def>Having
united sepals; gamosepalous.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntactical</ent><br/
<ent>Syntactic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn*tac"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syn*tac"tic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. G. <?/ putting together.
See <er>Syntax</er>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to syntax; according
to the rules of syntax, or construction.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Syn*tac"tic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntax</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"tax</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>syntaxis</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to put together in order;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to put in order; cf. F. <ets>syntaxe</ets>.
See <er>Syn-</er>, and <er>Tactics</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn>
<def>Connected system or order; union of things; a number of things
jointed together; organism.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>They owe no other dependence to the first than what is common to
the whole <qex>syntax</qex> of beings.</q>
<rj><qau>Glanvill.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>That part of grammar which treats of the
construction of sentences; the due arrangement of words in sentences in
their necessary relations, according to established usage in any
language.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntaxis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syn*tax"is</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Syntax.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>B. Jonson.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synteresis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`te*re"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/ preservation, fr. <?/ to preserve; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to
guard.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Prophylaxis.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Metaph.)</fld> <def>Conscience viewed as the
internal repository of the laws of duty.</def>
<rj><au>Whewell.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synteretic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`te*ret"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Preserving health; prophylactic.</def>
<mark>[Obs.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synteretics</ent><br/
<hw>Syn`te*ret"ics</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>That department of medicine which relates to the preservation of
health; prophylaxis.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark><-- = hygeine? --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synthermal</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*ther"mal</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Pref.
<ets>syn-</ets> + <ets>thermal</ets>.]</ety> <def>Having the same degree
of heat.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synthesis</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"the*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Syntheses</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[L., a mixture, properly,
a putting together, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to place or put together;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to place. See <er>Thesis</er>.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <def>Composition, or the putting of two or more things
together, as in compounding medicines.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>The art or process of making a
compound by putting the ingredients together, as contrasted with
<xex>analysis</xex>; thus, water is made by <xex>synthesis</xex> from
hydrogen and oxygen; hence, specifically, the building up of complex
compounds by special reactions, whereby their component radicals are so
grouped that the resulting substances are identical in every respect
with the natural articles when such occur; thus, artificial alcohol,
urea, indigo blue, alizarin, etc., are made by
<xex>synthesis</xex>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Logic)</fld> <def>The combination of separate
elements of thought into a whole, as of simple into complex conceptions,
species into genera, individual propositions into systems; -- the
opposite of <ant>analysis</ant>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Analysis and <qex>synthesis</qex>, though commonly treated as two
different methods, are, if properly understood, only the two necessary
parts of the same method. Each is the relative and correlative of the
other.</q> <rj><qau>Sir W. Hamilton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synthesist</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"the*sist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who employs
synthesis, or who follows synthetic methods.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synthesize</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"the*size</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
combine by synthesis; to unite.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To produce by synthesis; <as>as, to
<ex>synthesize</ex> albumin</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synthetical</ent><br/
<ent>Synthetic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syn*thet"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Syn*thet"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>synth<eacute/tique</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or pertaining
to synthesis; consisting in synthesis or composition; <as>as, the
<ex>synthetic</ex> method of reasoning, as opposed to
<ex>analytical</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Philosophers hasten too much from the analytic to the
<qex>synthetic</qex> method; that is, they draw general conclusions from
too small a number of particular observations and experiments.</q>
<rj><qau>Bolingbroke.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Chem.)</fld> <def>Artificial. Cf.
<er>Synthesis</er>, 2.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Comprising within itself
structural or other characters which are usually found only in two or
more diverse groups; -- said of species, genera, and higher groups. See
the Note under <er>Comprehensive</er>, 3.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Synthetic language</b></col>, <it>or</it>
<col><b>Synthetical language</b></col></mcol>, <cd>an inflectional
language, or one characterized by grammatical endings; -- opposed to
<contr>analytic language</contr>.</cd> <au>R. Morris.</au></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synthetically</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*thet"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a synthetic
manner.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Synthetize</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"the*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr.
<?/.]</ety> <def>To combine; to unite in regular structure.</def>
<mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntomy</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"to*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to
cut short; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to cut.]</ety> <def>Brevity;
conciseness.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntonic</ent><br/
<hw>Syn*ton"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>Of or pert. to syntony; specif., designating, or pert. to, a system
of wireless telegraphy in which the transmitting and receiving apparatus
are in syntony with, and only with, one another.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Syn*ton"ic*al</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> --
<wf>Syn*ton"ic*al*ly</wf>, <pos>adv.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntonin</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"to*nin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/
stretched tight, intense.]</ety> <fld>(Physiol. Chem.)</fld> <def>A
proteid substance (acid albumin) formed from the albuminous matter of
muscle by the action of dilute acids; -- formerly called
<altname>musculin</altname>. See <cref>Acid albumin</cref>, under
<er>Albumin</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntonize</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"to*nize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. &
p. p.</pos> <conjf>-nized</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb.
n.</pos> <conjf>-nizing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[See
<er>Syntony</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld> <def>To adjust or devise
so as to emit or respond to electric oscillations of a certain wave
length; to tune; specif., to put (two or more instruments or systems of
wireless telegraphy) in syntony with each other.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Syn`to*ni*za"tion</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntonizer</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"to*niz`er</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>One that syntonizes; specif., a device consisting essentially of a
variable inductance coil and condenser with a pair of adjustable spark
balls, for attuning the time periods of antennae in wireless telegraphy
(called also <altname><col><b>syntonizing
coil</b></col></altname>).</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syntony</ent><br/
<hw>Syn"to*ny</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. Gr. <?/
agreement. See <er>Syn-</er>; <er>Tone</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Physics)</fld>
<def>State of being adjusted to a certain wave length; agreement or
tuning between the time period of an apparatus emitting electric
oscillations and that of a receiving apparatus, esp. in wireless
telegraphy.</def><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphering</ent><br/
<hw>Sy"pher*ing</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Etymol.
uncertain.]</ety> <fld>(Carp.)</fld> <def>The lapping of chamfered edges
of planks to make a smooth surface, as for a bulkhead.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilide</ent><br/
<hw>Syph"i*lide</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.]</ety>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A cutaneous eruption due to syphilis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilis</ent><br/
<hw>Syph"i*lis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
<ets>Syphilus</ets>, the name of a shepherd in the Latin poem of
Fracastoro, <ldquo/<ets>Syphilus</ets>, sive Morbus Gallicus,<rdquo/
which was published in 1530; Gr. <?/ hog, swine + <?/ dear, loving. The
term was introduced into nosology by Sauvages.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>The pox, or venereal disease; a chronic, specific, infectious
disease, usually communicated by sexual intercourse or by hereditary
transmission, and occurring in three stages known as <xex>primary</xex>,
<xex>secondary</xex>, and <xex>tertiary syphilis</xex>. See under
<er>Primary</er>, <er>Secondary</er>, and <er>Tertiary</er>.</def><-- a
bacterial infection caused by <spn>Treponema pallidum</spn>. Usu.
tretable with penicillin or other beta-lactam antibiotics. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilitic</ent><br/
<hw>Syph`i*lit"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>syphilitique</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining
to syphilis; of the nature of syphilis; affected with syphilis.</def> --
<def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A syphilitic patient.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilitically</ent><br/
<hw>Syph`i*lit"ic*al*ly</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>adv.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>In a syphilitic manner; with venereal
disease.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilization</ent><br/
<hw>Syph`i*li*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Inoculation with the syphilitic virus, especially when employed as
a preventive measure, like vaccination.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilize</ent><br/
<hw>Syph"i*lize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>To inoculate with syphilis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphiloderm</ent><br/
<hw>Syph"i*lo*derm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[See
<er>Syphilis</er>, and <er>Derm</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>A
cutaneous affection due to syphilis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilodermatous</ent><br/
<hw>Syph`i*lo*der"ma*tous</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to the cutaneous manifestations
of syphilis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphiloid</ent><br/
<hw>Syph"i*loid</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Syphil</ets>is + <ets>-oid</ets>.]</ety> <fld>(Med.)</fld>
<def>Resembling syphilis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilologist</ent><br/
<hw>Syph`i*lol"o*gist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One skilled
in syphilology.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphilology</ent><br/
<hw>Syph`i*lol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Syphil</ets>is + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>That branch of
medicine which treats of syphilis.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syphon</ent><br/
<hw>Sy"phon</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Syphon</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syracuse</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"a*cuse</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A red wine of
Italy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syren</ent><br/
<hw>Sy"ren</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>See
<er>Siren</er>.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syriac</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"i*ac</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Syriacus</ets>, from <ets>Syria</ets>: cf. F.
<ets>syriaque</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or pertaining to Syria, or its
language; <as>as, the <ex>Syriac</ex> version of the
Pentateuch</as>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>The language of
Syria; especially, the ancient language of that
country.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syriacism</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"i*a*cism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Syrian idiom; a
Syrianism.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrian</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"i*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>Syrius</ets>: cf. F. <ets>Syrien</ets>.]</ety> <def>Of or
pertaining to Syria; Syriac.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A native
of Syria.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrianism</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"i*an*ism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Syrian idiom,
or a peculiarity of the Syrian language; a Syriacism.</def>
<rj><au>Paley.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syriasm</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"i*asm</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>A Syrian idiom; a
Syrianism; a Syriacism.</def> <rj><au>M. Stuart.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The Scripture Greek is observed to be full of <qex>Syriasms</qex>
and Hebraisms.</q> <rj><qau>Bp. Warburton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syringa</ent><br/
<hw>Sy*rin"ga</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/,
<?/, a shepherd's pipe, tube. Cf. <er>Syringe</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Bot.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>A genus of plants; the lilac.</def>
<sd>(b)</sd> <def>The mock orange; -- popularly so called because its
stems were formerly used as pipestems.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syringe</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"inge</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[F.
<ets>seringue</ets> (cf. Pr. <ets>siringua</ets>, Sp.
<ets>jeringa</ets>, It. <ets>sciringa</ets>, <ets>scilinga</ets>), fg.
Gr. <?/, <?/, a pipe or tube; cf. Skr. <ets>svar</ets> to sound, and E.
<ets>swarum</ets>. Cf. <er>Syringa</er>.]</ety> <def>A kind of small
hand-pump for throwing a stream of liquid, or for purposes of
aspiration. It consists of a small cylindrical barrel and piston, or a
bulb of soft elastic material, with or without valves, and with a nozzle
which is sometimes at the end of a flexible tube; -- used for injecting
animal bodies, cleansing wounds, etc.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Garden syringe</b></col>. <cd>See
<er>Garden</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syringe</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"inge</hw>, <pos>v. t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos>
<conjf>Syringed</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Syringing</conjf> <pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <sn>1.</sn> <def>To
inject by means of a syringe; <as>as, to <ex>syringe</ex> warm water
into a vein</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>To wash and clean by injection from a
syringe.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syringeal</ent><br/
<hw>Sy*rin"ge*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the syrinx; <as>as, the <ex>syringeal</ex>
muscle</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syringin</ent><br/
<hw>Sy*rin"gin</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <fld>(Chem.)</fld>
<def>A glucoside found in the bark of the lilac (<gen>Syringa</gen>) and
extracted as a white crystalline substance; -- formerly called also
<altname>lilacin</altname>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syringocoele</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sy*rin"go*coele</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>
<ety>[<ets>Syrinx</ets> + Gr. <?/ hollow.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The central canal of the spinal cord.</def> <rj><au>B. G.
Wilder.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syringotome</ent><br/
<hw>Sy*rin"go*tome</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>syringotome</ets>. See <er>Syringotomy</er>.]</ety> <fld>(Surg. &
Anat.)</fld> <def>A small blunt-pointed bistoury, -- used in
syringotomy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syringotomy</ent><br/
<hw>Syr`in*got"o*my</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/ a
tube, a hollow sore + <?/ to cut: cf. F. <ets>syringotomie</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Surg.)</fld> <def>The operation of cutting for anal
fistula.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrinx</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"inx</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Syringes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[NL., from Gr. <?/ a
pipe.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>A wind instrument made
of reeds tied together; -- called also <altname>pandean
pipes</altname>.</def><-- pipes of Pan --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat.)</fld> <def>The lower larynx in
birds.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><note><hand/ In birds there are two laringes, an upper or true, but
voiceless, larynx in the usual position behind the tongue, and a lower
one, at or near the junction of the trachea and bronchi, which is the
true organ of the voice.</note><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrma</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syr"ma</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L., fr. Gr. <?/,
fr. <?/ to drag.]</ety> <fld>(Class. Antiq.)</fld> <def>A long dress,
trailing on the floor, worn by tragic actors in Greek and Roman
theaters.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrphian</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"phi*an</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <fld>(Zool.)</fld>
<def>Of or pertaining to the syrphus flies.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>A syrphus fly.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrphus fly</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"phus fly`</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>. <ety>[NL. <ets>Syrphus</ets>, the
generic name, fr. Gr. <?/, <?/, a kind of winged insect.]</ety>
<fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>Any one of numerous species of dipterous flies
of the genus <gen>Syrphus</gen> and allied genera. They are usually
bright-colored, with yellow bands, and hover around plants. The larvae
feed upon plant lice, and are, therefore, very beneficial to
agriculture.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrt</ent><br/
<hw>Syrt</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L. <ets>syrtis</ets> a
sand bank in the sea, Gr. <?/: cf. F. <ets>syrte</ets>.]</ety> <def>A
quicksand; a bog.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Young.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrtic</ent><br/
<hw>Syr"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to a
syrt; resembling syrt, or quicksand.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark> <rj><au>Ed.
Rev.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrtis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Syr"tis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>; <plu><it>pl.</it>
<plw>Syrtes</plw> <pr>(#)</pr>.</plu> <ety>[See <er>Syrt</er>.]</ety>
<def>A quicksand.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Quenched in a boggy <qex>syrtis</qex>, neither sea<br/
Nor good dry land.</q> <rj><qau>Milton.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syrupy</ent><br/
<ent>Syrup</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syr"up</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>, <hw>Syr"up*y</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[See <er>Sirup</er>.]</ety>
<def>Same as <er>Sirup</er>, <er>Sirupy</er>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syssarcosis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sys`sar*co"sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr.
Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to unite by flesh, to cover over with flesh;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/, <?/, flesh.]</ety> <fld>(Anat.)</fld>
<def>The junction of bones by intervening muscles.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systaltic</ent><br/
<hw>Sys*tal"tic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>systalticus</ets> drawing together, Gr. <?/, from <?/ to draw
together. Cf. <er>Sustaltic</er>, <er>Systole</er>.]</ety>
<fld>(Physiol.)</fld> <def>Capable of, or taking place by, alternate
contraction and dilatation; <as>as, the <ex>systaltic</ex> action of the
heart</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systasis</ent><br/
\'d8<hw>Sys"ta*sis</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr.
<?/, fr. <?/ to stand together. See under <er>System</er>.]</ety> <def>A
political union, confederation, or league.</def> <mark>[R.]</mark>
<rj><au>Burke.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>System</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tem</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>systema</ets>, Gr. <?/, fr. <?/ to place together; <grk>sy`n</grk>
with + <?/ to place: cf. F. <ets>syst<egrave/me</ets>. See
<er>Stand</er>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>An assemblage of objects
arranged in regular subordination, or after some distinct method,
usually logical or scientific; a complete whole of objects related by
some common law, principle, or end; a complete exhibition of essential
principles or facts, arranged in a rational dependence or connection; a
regular union of principles or parts forming one entire thing; <as>as, a
<ex>system</ex> of philosophy; a <ex>system</ex> of government; a
<ex>system</ex> of divinity; a <ex>system</ex> of botany or chemistry; a
military <ex>system</ex>; the solar <ex>system</ex>.</as></def> <--
Specifically, a computer system. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>The best way to learn any science, is to begin with a regular
<qex>system</qex>, or a short and plain scheme of that science well
drawn up into a narrow compass.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Hence, the whole scheme of created things regarded
as forming one complete plan of whole; the universe.</def> <ldquo/The
great <xex>system</xex> of the world.<rdquo/
<rj><au>Boyle.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Regular method or order; formal arrangement; plan;
<as>as, to have a <ex>system</ex> in one's business</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Mus.)</fld> <def>The collection of staves which
form a full score. See <er>Score</er>, <pos>n.</pos></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>5.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>An assemblage of parts or organs,
either in animal or plant, essential to the performance of some
particular function or functions which as a rule are of greater
complexity than those manifested by a single organ; <as>as, the
capillary <ex>system</ex>, the muscular <ex>system</ex>, the digestive
<ex>system</ex>, etc.</as>; hence, the whole body as a functional
unity.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>6.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <def>One of the stellate or irregular
clusters of intimately united zooids which are imbedded in, or scattered
over, the surface of the common tissue of many compound
ascidians.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><mcol><col><b>Block system</b></col>, <col><b>Conservative
system</b></col></mcol>, <cd>etc. See under <er>Block</er>,
<er>Conservative</er>, etc.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systematical</ent><br/
<ent>Systematic</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Sys`tem*at"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sys`tem*at"ic*al</hw>
<pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw> <pos>a.</pos> <altsp>[Gr. <?/: cf. F.
<ets>syst<eacute/matique</ets>.]</altsp> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
pertaining to system; consisting in system; methodical; formed with
regular connection and adaptation or subordination of parts to each
other, and to the design of the whole; <as>as, a <ex>systematic</ex>
arrangement of plants or animals; a <ex>systematic</ex> course of
study.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Now we deal much in essays, and unreasonably despise
<qex>systematical</qex> learning; whereas our fathers had a just value
for regularity and systems.</q> <rj><qau>I. Watts.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>A representation of phenomena, in order to answer the purposes of
science, must be <qex>systematic</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Whewell.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>Proceeding according to system, or regular method;
<as>as, a <ex>systematic</ex> writer; <ex>systematic</ex>
benevolence.</as></def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <def>Pertaining to the system of the world;
cosmical.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>These ends may be called cosmical, or <qex>systematical</qex>.</q>
<rj><qau>Boyle.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <fld>(Med.)</fld> <def>Affecting successively the
different parts of the system or set of nervous fibres; <as>as,
<ex>systematic</ex> degeneration</as>.</def> <-- affecting the whole
body, as contrasted with local. --><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Systematic theology</b></col>. <cd>See under
<er>Theology</er>.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systematically</ent><br/
<hw>Sys`tem*at"ic*al*ly</hw>, <pos>adv.</pos> <def>In a systematic
manner; methodically.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systematism</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tem*a*tism</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The reduction
of facts or principles to a system.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systematist</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tem*a*tist</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>syst<eacute/matiste</ets>.]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <def>One who forms a
system, or reduces to system.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <def>One who adheres to a system.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systematization</ent><br/
<hw>Sys`tem*a*ti*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Cf. F.
<ets>syst<eacute/matization</ets>.]</ety> <def>The act or operation of
systematizing.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systematize</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tem*a*tize</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>v. t.</pos>
<vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Systematized</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos> <conjf>Systematizing</conjf>
<pr>(?)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf. F. <ets>syst<eacute/matiser</ets>. Cf.
<er>Systemize</er>.]</ety> <def>To reduce to system or regular method;
to arrange methodically; to methodize; <as>as, to <ex>systematize</ex> a
collection of plants or minerals; to <ex>systematize</ex> one's work; to
<ex>systematize</ex> one's ideas</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Diseases were healed, and buildings erected, before medicine and
architecture were <qex>systematized</qex> into arts.</q>
<rj><qau>Harris.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systematizer</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tem*a*ti`zer</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>One who
systematizes.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><q>Aristotle may be called the <qex>systematizer</qex> of his
master's doctrines.</q> <rj><qau>Harris.</qau></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systematology</ent><br/
<hw>Sys`tem*a*tol"o*gy</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[Gr. <?/,
<?/, system + <ets>-logy</ets>.]</ety> <def>The doctrine of, or a
treatise upon, systems.</def> <rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systemic</ent><br/
<hw>Sys*tem"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Of or
relating to a system; common to a system; <as>as, the <ex>systemic</ex>
circulation of the blood</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Anat. & Physiol.)</fld> <def>Of or pertaining to
the general system, or the body as a whole; <as>as, <ex>systemic</ex>
death, in distinction from <xex>local</xex> death; <ex>systemic</ex>
circulation, in distinction from <xex>pulmonic</xex> circulation;
<ex>systemic</ex> diseases</as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><cs><col><b>Systemic death</b></col>. <cd>See the Note under
<er>Death</er>, <pos>n.</pos>, 1.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systemization</ent><br/
<hw>Sys`tem*i*za"tion</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>The act or
process of systematizing; systematization.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systemize</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tem*ize</hw> <pr>(s<icr/s"t<ecr/m*<imac/z)</pr>, <pos>v.
t.</pos> <vmorph>[<pos>imp. & p. p.</pos> <conjf>Systemized</conjf>
<pr>(s<icr/s"t<ecr/m*<imac/zd)</pr>; <pos>p. pr. & vb. n.</pos>
<conjf>Systemizing</conjf>
<pr>(s<icr/s"t<ecr/m*<imac/`z<icr/ng)</pr>.]</vmorph> <ety>[Cf.
<er>Systematize</er>.]</ety> <def>To reduce to system; to
systematize.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><-- p. 1466 --></p>

<p><ent>Systemizer</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tem*i`zer</hw> <pr>(s<icr/s"t<ecr/m*<imac/`z<etil/r)</pr>,
<pos>n.</pos> <def>One who systematizes, or reduces to system; a
systematizer.</def></p>

<p><ent>Systemless</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tem*less</hw>, <pos>a.</pos> <sn>1.</sn> <def>Being without
system.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Nat. Hist.)</fld> <def>Not agreeing with some
artificial system of classification.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Biol.)</fld> <def>Not having any of the distinct
systems or types of structure, as the radiate, articulate, etc.,
characteristic of organic nature; <as>as, all unicellular organisms are
<ex>systemless</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systole</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"to*le</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <ety>[NL., fr. Gr. <?/,
fr. <?/ to contract; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ to set, place.]</ety>
<sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Gram.)</fld> <def>The shortening of the long
syllable.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Physiol. & Biol.)</fld> <def>The contraction of the
heart and arteries by which the blood is forced onward and the
circulation kept up; also, the contraction of a rhythmically pulsating
contractile vacuole; -- correlative to <contr>diastole</contr>.</def> --
<wordforms><wf>Sys*tol"ic</wf> <pr>(#)</pr>,
<pos>a.</pos></wordforms><br/
[<source>Webster 1913 Suppl.</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systolic</ent><br/
<hw>Sys*tol"ic</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Of or pertaining to
systole, or contraction; contracting; esp., relating to the systole of
the heart; <as>as, <ex>systolic</ex> murmur</as>.</def>
<rj><au>Dunglison.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Systyle</ent><br/
<hw>Sys"tyle</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <ety>[L.
<ets>systylos</ets>, Gr. <grk>sy`n</grk> with columns standing close;
<grk>sy`n</grk> with + <?/ a column: cf. F. <ets>systyle</ets>.]</ety>
<fld>(Arch.)</fld> <def>Having a space equal to two diameters or four
modules between two columns; -- said of a portico or building. See
<er>Intercolumniation</er>.</def> -- <def2><pos>n.</pos> <def>A systyle
temple or other edifice.</def></def2><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sythe</ent><br/
<ent>Syth</ent><br/
<mhw>{ <hw>Syth</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <hw>Sythe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, }</mhw>
<pos>prep., adv., conj. & n.</pos> <def>See <er>Sith</er>,
<er>Sithe</er>.</def> <mark>[Obs.]</mark> <rj><au>Chaucer.</au>
<au>Piers Plowman.</au></rj><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Sythe</ent><br/
<hw>Sythe</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos> <def>Scythe.</def>
<mark>[Obs. or R.]</mark><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syzygial</ent><br/
<hw>Sy*zyg"i*al</hw> <pr>(?)</pr>, <pos>a.</pos> <def>Pertaining to a
syzygy.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><ent>Syzygy</ent><br/
<hw>Syz"y*gy</hw> <pr>(s<icr/z"<icr/*j<ycr/)</pr>, <pos>n.</pos>;
<plu><it>pl.</it> <plw>Syzygies</plw> <pr>(-j<icr/z)</pr>.</plu>
<ety>[L. <ets>syzygia</ets> a joining together, conjunction, Gr.
<grk>syzygi`a</grk>; <grk>sy`n</grk> with + <grk>zeygny`nai</grk> to
join, <grk>zygo`n</grk> yoke: cf. F. <ets>syzygie</ets>. See
<er>Yoke</er>, <pos>n.</pos>]</ety> <sn>1.</sn> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>
<def>The point of an orbit, as of the moon or a planet, at which it is
in conjunction or opposition; -- commonly used in the plural.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>2.</sn> <fld>(Gr. & L. Pros.)</fld> <def>The coupling together of
different feet; <as>as, in Greek verse, an iambic
<ex>syzygy</ex></as>.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>3.</sn> <fld>(Zool.)</fld> <sd>(a)</sd> <def>Any one of the
segments of an arm of a crinoid composed of two joints so closely united
that the line of union is obliterated on the outer, though visible on
the inner, side.</def> <sd>(b)</sd> <def>The immovable union of two
joints of a crinoidal arm.</def><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>

<p><sn>4.</sn> <def>The intimately united and apparently fused condition
of certain low organisms during conjugation.</def></p>

<p><cs><col><b>Line of syzygies</b></col> <fld>(Astron.)</fld>, <cd>the
straight line connecting the earth, the sun, and the moon or a planet,
when the latter is in conjunction or opposition; -- used chiefly of the
moon.</cd></cs><br/
[<source>1913 Webster</source>]</p>


